if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-2145"]="The Jebel es Zubleh is a mountain fifty miles and more in length, and so narrow that its tracery on the map gives it a likeness to a caterpillar crawling from the south to the north. Standing on its red-and-white cliffs, and looking off under the path of the rising sun, one sees only the Desert of Arabia, where the east winds, so hateful to vinegrowers of Jericho, have kept their playgrounds since the beginning. Its feet are well covered by sands tossed from the Euphrates, there to lie, for the mountain is a wall to the pasture-lands of Moab and Ammon on the west—lands which else had been of the desert a part.\n\nThe Arab has impressed his language upon everything south and east of Judea, so, in his tongue, the old Jebel is the parent of numberless wadies which, intersecting the Roman road—now a dim suggestion of what once it was, a dusty path for Syrian pilgrims to and from Mecca—run their furrows, deepening as they go, to pass the torrents of the rainy season into the Jordan, or their last receptacle, the Dead Sea. Out of one of these wadies—or, more particularly, out of that one which rises at the extreme end of the Jebel, and, extending east of north, becomes at length the bed of the Jabbok River—a traveller passed, going to the table-lands of the desert. To this person the attention of the reader is first besought.\n\nJudged by his appearance, he was quite forty-five years old. His beard, once of the deepest black, flowing broadly over his breast, was streaked with white. His face was brown as a parched coffee-berry, and so hidden by a red kufiyeh (as the kerchief of the head is at this day called by the children of the desert) as to be but in part visible. Now and then he raised his eyes, and they were large and dark. He was clad in the flowing garments so universal in the East; but their style may not be described more particularly, for he sat under a miniature tent, and rode a great white dromedary.\n\nIt may be doubted if the people of the West ever overcome the impression made upon them by the first view of a camel equipped and loaded for the desert. Custom, so fatal to other novelties, affects this feeling but little. At the end of long journeys with caravans, after years of residence with the Bedawin, the Western-born, wherever they may be, will stop and wait the passing of the stately brute. The charm is not in the figure, which not even love can make beautiful; nor in the movement, the noiseless stepping, or the broad careen. As is the kindness of the sea to a ship, so that of the desert to its creature. It clothes him with all its mysteries; in such manner, too, that while we are looking at him we are thinking of them: therein is the wonder. The animal which now came out of the wady might well have claimed the customary homage. Its color and height; its breadth of foot; its bulk of body, not fat, but overlaid with muscle; its long, slender neck, of swanlike curvature; the head, wide between the eyes, and tapering to a muzzle which a lady’s bracelet might have almost clasped; its motion, step long and elastic, tread sure and soundless—all certified its Syrian blood, old as the days of Cyrus, and absolutely priceless. There was the usual bridle, covering the forehead with scarlet fringe, and garnishing the throat with pendent brazen chains, each ending with a tinkling silver bell; but to the bridle there was neither rein for the rider nor strap for a driver. The furniture perched on the back was an invention which with any other people than of the East would have made the inventor renowned. It consisted of two wooden boxes, scarce four feet in length, balanced so that one hung at each side; the inner space, softly lined and carpeted, was arranged to allow the master to sit or lie half reclined; over it all was stretched a green awning. Broad back and breast straps, and girths, secured with countless knots and ties, held the device in place. In such manner the ingenious sons of Cush had contrived to make comfortable the sunburnt ways of the wilderness, along which lay their duty as often as their pleasure.\n\nWhen the dromedary lifted itself out of the last break of the wady, the traveller had passed the boundary of El Belka, the ancient Ammon. It was morning-time. Before him was the sun, half curtained in fleecy mist; before him also spread the desert; not the realm of drifting sands, which was farther on, but the region where the herbage began to dwarf; where the surface is strewn with boulders of granite, and gray and brown stones, interspersed with languishing acacias and tufts of camel-grass. The oak, bramble, and arbutus lay behind, as if they had come to a line, looked over into the well-less waste and crouched with fear.\n\nAnd now there was an end of path or road. More than ever the camel seemed insensibly driven; it lengthened and quickened its pace, its head pointed straight towards the horizon; through the wide nostrils it drank the wind in great draughts. The litter swayed, and rose and fell like a boat in the waves. Dried leaves in occasional beds rustled underfoot. Sometimes a perfume like absinthe sweetened all the air. Lark and chat and rock-swallow leaped to wing, and white partridges ran whistling and clucking out of the way. More rarely a fox or a hyena quickened his gallop, to study the intruders at a safe distance. Off to the right rose the hills of the Jebel, the pearl-gray veil resting upon them changing momentarily into a purple which the sun would make matchless a little later. Over their highest peaks a vulture sailed on broad wings into widening circles. But of all these things the tenant under the green tent saw nothing, or, at least, made no sign of recognition. His eyes were fixed and dreamy. The going of the man, like that of the animal, was as one being led.\n\nFor two hours the dromedary swung forward, keeping the trot steadily and the line due east. In that time the traveller never changed his position, nor looked to the right or left. On the desert, distance is not measured by miles or leagues, but by the saat, or hour, and the manzil, or halt: three and a half leagues fill the former, fifteen or twenty-five the latter; but they are the rates for the common camel. A carrier of the genuine Syrian stock can make three leagues easily. At full speed he overtakes the ordinary winds. As one of the results of the rapid advance, the face of the landscape underwent a change. The Jebel stretched along the western horizon, like a pale-blue ribbon. A tell, or hummock of clay and cemented sand, arose here and there. Now and then basaltic stones lifted their round crowns, outposts of the mountain against the forces of the plain; all else, however, was sand, sometimes smooth as the beaten beach, then heaped in rolling ridges; here chopped waves, there long swells. So, too, the condition of the atmosphere changed. The sun, high risen, had drunk his fill of dew and mist, and warmed the breeze that kissed the wanderer under the awning; far and near he was tinting the earth with faint milk-whiteness, and shimmering all the sky.\n\nTwo hours more passed without rest or deviation from the course. Vegetation entirely ceased. The sand, so crusted on the surface that it broke into rattling flakes at every step, held undisputed sway. The Jebel was out of view, and there was no landmark visible. The shadow that before followed had now shifted to the north, and was keeping even race with the objects which cast it; and as there was no sign of halting, the conduct of the traveller became each moment more strange.\n\nNo one, be it remembered, seeks the desert for a pleasure-ground. Life and business traverse it by paths along which the bones of things dead are strewn as so many blazons. Such are the roads from well to well, from pasture to pasture. The heart of the most veteran sheik beats quicker when he finds himself alone in the pathless tracts. So the man with whom we are dealing could not have been in search of pleasure; neither was his manner that of a fugitive; not once did he look behind him. In such situations fear and curiosity are the most common sensations; he was not moved by them. When men are lonely, they stoop to any companionship; the dog becomes a comrade, the horse a friend, and it is no shame to shower them with caresses and speeches of love. The camel received no such token, not a touch, not a word.\n\nExactly at noon the dromedary, of its own will, stopped, and uttered the cry or moan, peculiarly piteous, by which its kind always protest against an overload, and sometimes crave attention and rest. The master thereupon bestirred himself, waking, as it were, from sleep. He threw the curtains of the houdah up, looked at the sun, surveyed the country on every side long and carefully, as if to identify an appointed place. Satisfied with the inspection, he drew a deep breath and nodded, much as to say, “At last, at last!” A moment after, he crossed his hands upon his breast, bowed his head, and prayed silently. The pious duty done, he prepared to dismount. From his throat proceeded the sound heard doubtless by the favorite camels of Job—Ikh! ikh!—the signal to kneel. Slowly the animal obeyed, grunting the while. The rider then put his foot upon the slender neck, and stepped upon the sand.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nThe man as now revealed was of admirable proportions, not so tall as powerful. Loosening the silken rope which held the kufiyeh on his head, he brushed the fringed folds back until his face was bare—a strong face, almost negro in color; yet the low, broad forehead, aquiline nose, the outer corners of the eyes turned slightly upward, the hair profuse, straight, harsh, of metallic lustre, and falling to the shoulder in many plaits, were signs of origin impossible to disguise. So looked the Pharaohs and the later Ptolemies; so looked Mizraim, father of the Egyptian race. He wore the kamis, a white cotton shirt tight-sleeved, open in front, extending to the ankles and embroidered down the collar and breast, over which was thrown a brown woollen cloak, now, as in all probability it was then, called the aba, an outer garment with long skirt and short sleeves, lined inside with stuff of mixed cotton and silk, edged all round with a margin of clouded yellow. His feet were protected by sandals, attached by thongs of soft leather. A sash held the kamis to his waist. What was very noticeable, considering he was alone, and that the desert was the haunt of leopards and lions, and men quite as wild, he carried no arms, not even the crooked stick used for guiding camels; wherefore we may at least infer his errand peaceful, and that he was either uncommonly bold or under extraordinary protection.\n\nThe traveller’s limbs were numb, for the ride had been long and wearisome; so he rubbed his hands and stamped his feet, and walked round the faithful servant, whose lustrous eyes were closing in calm content with the cud he had already found. Often, while making the circuit, he paused, and, shading his eyes with his hands, examined the desert to the extremest verge of vision; and always, when the survey was ended, his face clouded with disappointment, slight, but enough to advise a shrewd spectator that he was there expecting company, if not by appointment; at the same time, the spectator would have been conscious of a sharpening of the curiosity to learn what the business could be that required transaction in a place so far from civilized abode.\n\nHowever disappointed, there could be little doubt of the stranger’s confidence in the coming of the expected company. In token thereof, he went first to the litter, and, from the cot or box opposite the one he had occupied in coming, produced a sponge and a small gurglet of water, with which he washed the eyes, face, and nostrils of the camel; that done, from the same depository he drew a circular cloth, red-and white-striped, a bundle of rods, and a stout cane. The latter, after some manipulation, proved to be a cunning device of lesser joints, one within another, which, when united together, formed a centre pole higher than his head. When the pole was planted, and the rods set around it, he spread the cloth over them, and was literally at home—a home much smaller than the habitations of emir and sheik, yet their counterpart in all other respects. From the litter again he brought a carpet or square rug, and covered the floor of the tent on the side from the sun. That done, he went out, and once more, and with greater care and more eager eyes, swept the encircling country. Except a distant jackal, galloping across the plain, and an eagle flying towards the Gulf of Akaba, the waste below, like the blue above it, was lifeless.\n\nHe turned to the camel, saying low, and in a tongue strange to the desert, “We are far from home, O racer with the swiftest winds—we are far from home, but God is with us. Let us be patient.”\n\nThen he took some beans from a pocket in the saddle, and put them in a bag made to hang below the animal’s nose; and when he saw the relish with which the good servant took to the food, he turned and again scanned the world of sand, dim with the glow of the vertical sun.\n\n“They will come,” he said, calmly. “He that led me is leading them. I will make ready.”\n\nFrom the pouches which lined the interior of the cot, and from a willow basket which was part of its furniture, he brought forth materials for a meal: platters close-woven of the fibres of palms; wine in small gurglets of skin; mutton dried and smoked; stoneless shami, or Syrian pomegranates; dates of El Shelebi, wondrous rich and grown in the nakhil, or palm orchards, of Central Arabia; cheese, like David’s “slices of milk;” and leavened bread from the city bakery—all which he carried and set upon the carpet under the tent. As the final preparation, about the provisions he laid three pieces of silk cloth, used among refined people of the East to cover the knees of guests while at table—a circumstance significant of the number of persons who were to partake of his entertainment—the number he was awaiting.\n\nAll was now ready. He stepped out: lo! in the east a dark speck on the face of the desert. He stood as if rooted to the ground; his eyes dilated; his flesh crept chilly, as if touched by something supernatural. The speck grew; became large as a hand; at length assumed defined proportions. A little later, full into view swung a duplication of his own dromedary, tall and white, and bearing a houdah, the travelling litter of Hindostan. Then the Egyptian crossed his hands upon his breast, and looked to heaven.\n\n“God only is great!” he exclaimed, his eyes full of tears, his soul in awe.\n\nThe stranger drew nigh—at last stopped. Then he, too, seemed just waking. He beheld the kneeling camel, the tent, and the man standing prayerfully at the door. He crossed his hands, bent his head, and prayed silently; after which, in a little while, he stepped from his camel’s neck to the sand, and advanced towards the Egyptian, as did the Egyptian towards him. A moment they looked at each other; then they embraced—that is, each threw his right arm over the other’s shoulder, and the left round the side, placing his chin first upon the left, then upon the right breast.\n\n“Peace be with thee, O servant of the true God!” the stranger said.\n\n“And to thee, O brother of the true faith!—to thee peace and welcome,” the Egyptian replied, with fervor.\n\nThe new-comer was tall and gaunt, with lean face, sunken eyes, white hair and beard, and a complexion between the hue of cinnamon and bronze. He, too, was unarmed. His costume was Hindostani; over the skull-cap a shawl was wound in great folds, forming a turban; his body garments were in the style of the Egyptian’s, except that the aba was shorter, exposing wide flowing breeches gathered at the ankles. In place of sandals, his feet were clad in half-slippers of red leather, pointed at the toes. Save the slippers, the costume from head to foot was of white linen. The air of the man was high, stately, severe. Visvamitra, the greatest of the ascetic heroes of the Iliad of the East, had in him a perfect representative. He might have been called a Life drenched with the wisdom of Brahma—Devotion Incarnate. Only in his eyes was there proof of humanity; when he lifted his face from the Egyptian’s breast, they were glistening with tears.\n\n“God only is great!” he exclaimed, when the embrace was finished.\n\n“And blessed are they that serve him!” the Egyptian answered, wondering at the paraphrase of his own exclamation. “But let us wait,” he added, “let us wait; for see, the other comes yonder!”\n\nThey looked to the north, where, already plain to view, a third camel, of the whiteness of the others, came careening like a ship. They waited, standing together—waited until the new-comer arrived, dismounted, and advanced towards them.\n\n“Peace to you, O my brother!” he said, while embracing the Hindoo.\n\nAnd the Hindoo answered, “God’s will be done!”\n\nThe last comer was all unlike his friends: his frame was slighter; his complexion white; a mass of waving light hair was a perfect crown for his small but beautiful head; the warmth of his dark-blue eyes certified a delicate mind, and a cordial, brave nature. He was bareheaded and unarmed. Under the folds of the Tyrian blanket which he wore with unconscious grace appeared a tunic, short-sleeved and low-necked, gathered to the waist by a band, and reaching nearly to the knee; leaving the neck, arms, and legs bare. Sandals guarded his feet. Fifty years, probably more, had spent themselves upon him, with no other effect, apparently, than to tinge his demeanor with gravity and temper his words with forethought. The physical organization and the brightness of soul were untouched. No need to tell the student from what kindred he was sprung; if he came not himself from the groves of Athene’, his ancestry did.\n\nWhen his arms fell from the Egyptian, the latter said, with a tremulous voice, “The Spirit brought me first; wherefore I know myself chosen to be the servant of my brethren. The tent is set, and the bread is ready for the breaking. Let me perform my office.”\n\nTaking each by the hand, he led them within, and removed their sandals and washed their feet, and he poured water upon their hands, and dried them with napkins.\n\nThen, when he had laved his own hands, he said, “Let us take care of ourselves, brethren, as our service requires, and eat, that we may be strong for what remains of the day’s duty. While we eat, we will each learn who the others are, and whence they come, and how they are called.”\n\nHe took them to the repast, and seated them so that they faced each other. Simultaneously their heads bent forward, their hands crossed upon their breasts, and, speaking together, they said aloud this simple grace:\n\n“Father of all—God!—what we have here is of thee; take our thanks and bless us, that we may continue to do thy will.”\n\nWith the last word they raised their eyes, and looked at each other in wonder. Each had spoken in a language never before heard by the others; yet each understood perfectly what was said. Their souls thrilled with divine emotion; for by the miracle they recognized the Divine Presence.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nTo speak in the style of the period, the meeting just described took place in the year of Rome 747. The month was December, and winter reigned over all the regions east of the Mediterranean. Such as ride upon the desert in this season go not far until smitten with a keen appetite. The company under the little tent were not exceptions to the rule. They were hungry, and ate heartily; and, after the wine, they talked.\n\n“To a wayfarer in a strange land nothing is so sweet as to hear his name on the tongue of a friend,” said the Egyptian, who assumed to be president of the repast. “Before us lie many days of companionship. It is time we knew each other. So, if it be agreeable, he who came last shall be first to speak.”\n\nThen, slowly at first, like one watchful of himself, the Greek began:\n\n“What I have to tell, my brethren, is so strange that I hardly know where to begin or what I may with propriety speak. I do not yet understand myself. The most I am sure of is that I am doing a Master’s will, and that the service is a constant ecstasy. When I think of the purpose I am sent to fulfil, there is in me a joy so inexpressible that I know the will is God’s.”\n\nThe good man paused, unable to proceed, while the others, in sympathy with his feelings, dropped their gaze.\n\n“Far to the west of this,” he began again, “there is a land which may never be forgotten; if only because the world is too much its debtor, and because the indebtedness is for things that bring to men their purest pleasures. I will say nothing of the arts, nothing of philosophy, of eloquence, of poetry, of war: O my brethren, hers is the glory which must shine forever in perfected letters, by which He we go to find and proclaim will be made known to all the earth. The land I speak of is Greece. I am Gaspar, son of Cleanthes the Athenian.\n\n“My people,” he continued, “were given wholly to study, and from them I derived the same passion. It happens that two of our philosophers, the very greatest of the many, teach, one the doctrine of a Soul in every man, and its Immortality; the other the doctrine of One God, infinitely just. From the multitude of subjects about which the schools were disputing, I separated them, as alone worth the labor of solution; for I thought there was a relation between God and the soul as yet unknown. On this theme the mind can reason to a point, a dead, impassable wall; arrived there, all that remains is to stand and cry aloud for help. So I did; but no voice came to me over the wall. In despair, I tore myself from the cities and the schools.”\n\nAt these words a grave smile of approval lighted the gaunt face of the Hindoo.\n\n“In the northern part of my country—in Thessaly,” the Greek proceeded to say, “there is a mountain famous as the home of the gods, where Theus, whom my countrymen believe supreme, has his abode; Olympus is its name. Thither I betook myself. I found a cave in a hill where the mountain, coming from the west, bends to the southeast; there I dwelt, giving myself up to meditation—no, I gave myself up to waiting for what every breath was a prayer—for revelation. Believing in God, invisible yet supreme, I also believed it possible so to yearn for him with all my soul that he would take compassion and give me answer.”\n\n“And he did—he did!” exclaimed the Hindoo, lifting his hands from the silken cloth upon his lap.\n\n“Hear me, brethren,” said the Greek, calming himself with an effort. “The door of my hermitage looks over an arm of the sea, over the Thermaic Gulf. One day I saw a man flung overboard from a ship sailing by. He swam ashore. I received and took care of him. He was a Jew, learned in the history and laws of his people; and from him I came to know that the God of my prayers did indeed exist; and had been for ages their lawmaker, ruler, and king. What was that but the Revelation I dreamed of? My faith had not been fruitless; God answered me!”\n\n“As he does all who cry to him with such faith,” said the Hindoo.\n\n“But, alas!” the Egyptian added, “how few are there wise enough to know when he answers them!”\n\n“That was not all,” the Greek continued. “The man so sent to me told me more. He said the prophets who, in the ages which followed the first revelation, walked and talked with God, declared he would come again. He gave me the names of the prophets, and from the sacred books quoted their very language. He told me, further, that the second coming was at hand—was looked for momentarily in Jerusalem.”\n\nThe Greek paused, and the brightness of his countenance faded.\n\n“It is true,” he said, after a little—“it is true the man told me that as God and the revelation of which he spoke had been for the Jews alone, so it would be again. He that was to come should be King of the Jews. ‘Had he nothing for the rest of the world?’ I asked. ‘No,’ was the answer, given in a proud voice—‘No, we are his chosen people.’ The answer did not crush my hope. Why should such a God limit his love and benefaction to one land, and, as it were, to one family? I set my heart upon knowing. At last I broke through the man’s pride, and found that his fathers had been merely chosen servants to keep the Truth alive, that the world might at last know it and be saved. When the Jew was gone, and I was alone again, I chastened my soul with a new prayer—that I might be permitted to see the King when he was come, and worship him. One night I sat by the door of my cave trying to get nearer the mysteries of my existence, knowing which is to know God; suddenly, on the sea below me, or rather in the darkness that covered its face, I saw a star begin to burn; slowly it arose and drew nigh, and stood over the hill and above my door, so that its light shone full upon me. I fell down, and slept, and in my dream I heard a voice say:\n\n“‘O Gaspar! Thy faith hath conquered! Blessed art thou! With two others, come from the uttermost parts of the earth, thou shalt see Him that is promised, and be a witness for him, and the occasion of testimony in his behalf. In the morning arise, and go meet them, and keep trust in the Spirit that shall guide thee.’\n\n“And in the morning I awoke with the Spirit as a light within me surpassing that of the sun. I put off my hermit’s garb, and dressed myself as of old. From a hiding-place I took the treasure which I had brought from the city. A ship went sailing past. I hailed it, was taken aboard, and landed at Antioch. There I bought the camel and his furniture. Through the gardens and orchards that enamel the banks of the Orontes, I journeyed to Emesa, Damascus, Bostra, and Philadelphia; thence hither. And so, O brethren, you have my story. Let me now listen to you.”\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nThe Egyptian and the Hindoo looked at each other; the former waved his hand; the latter bowed, and began:\n\n“Our brother has spoken well. May my words be as wise.”\n\nHe broke off, reflected a moment, then resumed:\n\n“You may know me, brethren, by the name of Melchior. I speak to you in a language which, if not the oldest in the world, was at least the soonest to be reduced to letters—I mean the Sanscrit of India. I am a Hindoo by birth. My people were the first to walk in the fields of knowledge, first to divide them, first to make them beautiful. Whatever may hereafter befall, the four Vedas must live, for they are the primal fountains of religion and useful intelligence. From them were derived the Upa-Vedas, which, delivered by Brahma, treat of medicine, archery, architecture, music, and the four-and-sixty mechanical arts; the Ved-Angas, revealed by inspired saints, and devoted to astronomy, grammar, prosody, pronunciation, charms and incantations, religious rites and ceremonies; the Up-Angas, written by the sage Vyasa, and given to cosmogony, chronology, and geography; therein also are the Ramayana and the Mahabharata, heroic poems, designed for the perpetuation of our gods and demi-gods. Such, O brethren, are the Great Shastras, or books of sacred ordinances. They are dead to me now; yet through all time they will serve to illustrate the budding genius of my race. They were promises of quick perfection. Ask you why the promises failed? Alas! the books themselves closed all the gates of progress. Under pretext of care for the creature, their authors imposed the fatal principle that a man must not address himself to discovery or invention, as Heaven had provided him all things needful. When that condition became a sacred law, the lamp of Hindoo genius was let down a well, where ever since it has lighted narrow walls and bitter waters.\n\n“These allusions, brethren, are not from pride, as you will understand when I tell you that the Shastras teach a Supreme God called Brahm; also, that the Puranas, or sacred poems of the Up-Angas, tell us of Virtue and Good Works, and of the Soul. So, if my brother will permit the saying”—the speaker bowed deferentially to the Greek—“ages before his people were known, the two great ideas, God and the Soul, had absorbed all the forces of the Hindoo mind. In further explanation let me say that Brahm is taught, by the same sacred books, as a Triad—Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva. Of these, Brahma is said to have been the author of our race; which, in course of creation, he divided into four castes. First, he peopled the worlds below and the heavens above; next, he made the earth ready for terrestrial spirits; then from his mouth proceeded the Brahman caste, nearest in likeness to himself, highest and noblest, sole teachers of the Vedas, which at the same time flowed from his lips in finished state, perfect in all useful knowledge. From his arms next issued the Kshatriya, or warriors; from his breast, the seat of life, came the Vaisya, or producers—shepherds, farmers, merchants; from his foot, in sign of degradation, sprang the Sudra, or serviles, doomed to menial duties for the other classes—serfs, domestics, laborers, artisans. Take notice, further, that the law, so born with them, forbade a man of one caste becoming a member of another; the Brahman could not enter a lower order; if he violated the laws of his own grade, he became an outcast, lost to all but outcasts like himself.”\n\nAt this point, the imagination of the Greek, flashing forward upon all the consequences of such a degradation, overcame his eager attention, and he exclaimed, “In such a state, O brethren, what mighty need of a loving God!”\n\n“Yes,” added the Egyptian, “of a loving God like ours.”\n\nThe brows of the Hindoo knit painfully; when the emotion was spent, he proceeded, in a softened voice.\n\n“I was born a Brahman. My life, consequently, was ordered down to its least act, its last hour. My first draught of nourishment; the giving me my compound name; taking me out the first time to see the sun; investing me with the triple thread by which I became one of the twice-born; my induction into the first order—were all celebrated with sacred texts and rigid ceremonies. I might not walk, eat, drink, or sleep without danger of violating a rule. And the penalty, O brethren, the penalty was to my soul! According to the degrees of omission, my soul went to one of the heavens—Indra’s the lowest, Brahma’s the highest; or it was driven back to become the life of a worm, a fly, a fish, or a brute. The reward for perfect observance was Beatitude, or absorption into the being of Brahm, which was not existence as much as absolute rest.”\n\nThe Hindoo gave himself a moment’s thought; proceeding, he said: “The part of a Brahman’s life called the first order is his student life. When I was ready to enter the second order—that is to say, when I was ready to marry and become a householder—I questioned everything, even Brahm; I was a heretic. From the depths of the well I had discovered a light above, and yearned to go up and see what all it shone upon. At last—ah, with what years of toil!—I stood in the perfect day, and beheld the principle of life, the element of religion, the link between the soul and God—Love!”\n\nThe shrunken face of the good man kindled visibly, and he clasped his hands with force. A silence ensued, during which the others looked at him, the Greek through tears. At length he resumed:\n\n“The happiness of love is in action; its test is what one is willing to do for others. I could not rest. Brahm had filled the world with so much wretchedness. The Sudra appealed to me, so did the countless devotees and victims. The island of Ganga Lagor lies where the sacred waters of the Ganges disappear in the Indian Ocean. Thither I betook myself. In the shade of the temple built there to the sage Kapila, in a union of prayers with the disciples whom the sanctified memory of the holy man keeps around his house, I thought to find rest. But twice every year came pilgrimages of Hindoos seeking the purification of the waters. Their misery strengthened my love. Against its impulse to speak I clenched my jaws; for one word against Brahm or the Triad or the Shastras would doom me; one act of kindness to the outcast Brahmans who now and then dragged themselves to die on the burning sands—a blessing said, a cup of water given—and I became one of them, lost to family, country, privileges, caste. The love conquered! I spoke to the disciples in the temple; they drove me out. I spoke to the pilgrims; they stoned me from the island. On the highways I attempted to preach; my hearers fled from me, or sought my life. In all India, finally, there was not a place in which I could find peace or safety—not even among the outcasts, for, though fallen, they were still believers in Brahm. In my extremity, I looked for a solitude in which to hide from all but God. I followed the Ganges to its source, far up in the Himalayas. When I entered the pass at Hurdwar, where the river, in unstained purity, leaps to its course through the muddy lowlands, I prayed for my race, and thought myself lost to them forever. Through gorges, over cliffs, across glaciers, by peaks that seemed star-high, I made my way to the Lang Tso, a lake of marvellous beauty, asleep at the feet of the Tise Gangri, the Gurla, and the Kailas Parbot, giants which flaunt their crowns of snow everlastingly in the face of the sun. There, in the centre of the earth, where the Indus, Ganges, and Brahmapootra rise to run their different courses; where mankind took up their first abode, and separated to replete the world, leaving Balk, the mother of cities, to attest the great fact; where Nature, gone back to its primeval condition, and secure in its immensities, invites the sage and the exile, with promise of safety to the one and solitude to the other—there I went to abide alone with God, praying, fasting, waiting for death.”\n\nAgain the voice fell, and the bony hands met in a fervent clasp.\n\n“One night I walked by the shores of the lake, and spoke to the listening silence, ‘When will God come and claim his own? Is there to be no redemption?’ Suddenly a light began to glow tremulously out on the water; soon a star arose, and moved towards me, and stood overhead. The brightness stunned me. While I lay upon the ground, I heard a voice of infinite sweetness say, ‘Thy love hath conquered. Blessed art thou, O son of India! The redemption is at hand. With two others, from far quarters of the earth, thou shalt see the Redeemer, and be a witness that he hath come. In the morning arise, and go meet them; and put all thy trust in the Spirit which shall guide thee.’\n\n“And from that time the light has stayed with me; so I knew it was the visible presence of the Spirit. In the morning I started to the world by the way I had come. In a cleft of the mountain I found a stone of vast worth, which I sold in Hurdwar. By Lahore, and Cabool, and Yezd, I came to Ispahan. There I bought the camel, and thence was led to Bagdad, not waiting for caravans. Alone I traveled, fearless, for the Spirit was with me, and is with me yet. What glory is ours, O brethren! We are to see the Redeemer—to speak to him—to worship him! I am done.”\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThe vivacious Greek broke forth in expressions of joy and congratulations; after which the Egyptian said, with characteristic gravity:\n\n“I salute you, my brother. You have suffered much, and I rejoice in your triumph. If you are both pleased to hear me, I will now tell you who I am, and how I came to be called. Wait for me a moment.”\n\nHe went out and tended the camels; coming back, he resumed his seat.\n\n“Your words, brethren, were of the Spirit,” he said, in commencement; “and the Spirit gives me to understand them. You each spoke particularly of your countries; in that there was a great object, which I will explain; but to make the interpretation complete, let me first speak of myself and my people. I am Balthasar the Egyptian.”\n\nThe last words were spoken quietly, but with so much dignity that both listeners bowed to the speaker.\n\n“There are many distinctions I might claim for my race,” he continued; “but I will content myself with one. History began with us. We were the first to perpetuate events by records kept. So we have no traditions; and instead of poetry, we offer you certainty. On the facades of palaces and temples, on obelisks, on the inner walls of tombs, we wrote the names of our kings, and what they did; and to the delicate papyri we intrusted the wisdom of our philosophers and the secrets of our religion—all the secrets but one, whereof I will presently speak. Older than the Vedas of Para-Brahm or the Up-Angas of Vyasa, O Melchior; older than the songs of Homer or the metaphysics of Plato, O my Gaspar; older than the sacred books or kings of the people of China, or those of Siddartha, son of the beautiful Maya; older than the Genesis of Mosche the Hebrew—oldest of human records are the writings of Menes, our first king.” Pausing an instant, he fixed his large eves kindly upon the Greek, saying, “In the youth of Hellas, who, O Gaspar, were the teachers of her teachers?”\n\nThe Greek bowed, smiling.\n\n“By those records,” Balthasar continued, “we know that when the fathers came from the far East, from the region of the birth of the three sacred rivers, from the centre of the earth—the Old Iran of which you spoke, O Melchior—came bringing with them the history of the world before the Flood, and of the Flood itself, as given to the Aryans by the sons of Noah, they taught God, the Creator and the Beginning, and the Soul, deathless as God. When the duty which calls us now is happily done, if you choose to go with me, I will show you the sacred library of our priesthood; among others, the Book of the Dead, in which is the ritual to be observed by the soul after Death has despatched it on its journey to judgment. The ideas—God and the Immortal Soul—were borne to Mizraim over the desert, and by him to the banks of the Nile. They were then in their purity, easy of understanding, as what God intends for our happiness always is; so, also, was the first worship—a song and a prayer natural to a soul joyous, hopeful, and in love with its Maker.”\n\nHere the Greek threw up his hands, exclaiming, “Oh! the light deepens within me!”\n\n“And in me!” said the Hindoo, with equal fervor.\n\nThe Egyptian regarded them benignantly, then went on, saying, “Religion is merely the law which binds man to his Creator: in purity it has but these elements—God, the Soul, and their Mutual Recognition; out of which, when put in practise, spring Worship, Love, and Reward. This law, like all others of divine origin—like that, for instance, which binds the earth to the sun—was perfected in the beginning by its Author. Such, my brothers, was the religion of the first family; such was the religion of our father Mizraim, who could not have been blind to the formula of creation, nowhere so discernible as in the first faith and the earliest worship. Perfection is God; simplicity is perfection. The curse of curses is that men will not let truths like these alone.”\n\nHe stopped, as if considering in what manner to continue.\n\n“Many nations have loved the sweet waters of the Nile,” he said next; “the Ethiopian, the Pali-Putra, the Hebrew, the Assyrian, the Persian, the Macedonian, the Roman—of whom all, except the Hebrew, have at one time or another been its masters. So much coming and going of peoples corrupted the old Mizraimic faith. The Valley of Palms became a Valley of Gods. The Supreme One was divided into eight, each personating a creative principle in nature, with Ammon-Re at the head. Then Isis and Osiris, and their circle, representing water, fire, air, and other forces, were invented. Still the multiplication went on until we had another order, suggested by human qualities, such as strength, knowledge, love, and the like.”\n\n“In all which there was the old folly!” cried the Greek, impulsively. “Only the things out of reach remain as they came to us.”\n\nThe Egyptian bowed, and proceeded:\n\n“Yet a little further, O my brethren, a little further, before I come to myself. What we go to will seem all the holier of comparison with what is and has been. The records show that Mizraim found the Nile in possession of the Ethiopians, who were spread thence through the African desert; a people of rich, fantastic genius, wholly given to the worship of nature. The Poetic Persian sacrificed to the sun, as the completest image of Ormuzd, his God; the devout children of the far East carved their deities out of wood and ivory; but the Ethiopian, without writing, without books, without mechanical faculty of any kind, quieted his soul by the worship of animals, birds, and insects, holding the cat sacred to Re, the bull to Isis, the beetle to Pthah. A long struggle against their rude faith ended in its adoption as the religion of the new empire. Then rose the mighty monuments that cumber the river-bank and the desert—obelisk, labyrinth, pyramid, and tomb of king, blent with tomb of crocodile. Into such deep debasement, O brethren, the sons of the Aryan fell!”\n\nHere, for the first time, the calmness of the Egyptian forsook him: though his countenance remained impassive, his voice gave way.\n\n“Do not too much despise my countrymen,” he began again. “They did not all forget God. I said awhile ago, you may remember, that to papyri we intrusted all the secrets of our religion except one; of that I will now tell you. We had as king once a certain Pharaoh, who lent himself to all manner of changes and additions. To establish the new system, he strove to drive the old entirely out of mind. The Hebrews then dwelt with us as slaves. They clung to their God; and when the persecution became intolerable, they were delivered in a manner never to be forgotten. I speak from the records now. Mosche, himself a Hebrew, came to the palace, and demanded permission for the slaves, then millions in number, to leave the country. The demand was in the name of the Lord God of Israel. Pharaoh refused. Hear what followed. First, all the water, that in the lakes and rivers, like that in the wells and vessels, turned to blood. Yet the monarch refused. Then frogs came up and covered all the land. Still he was firm. Then Mosche threw ashes in the air, and a plague attacked the Egyptians. Next, all the cattle, except of the Hebrews, were struck dead. Locusts devoured the green things of the valley. At noon the day was turned into a darkness so thick that lamps would not burn. Finally, in the night all the first-born of the Egyptians died; not even Pharaoh’s escaped. Then he yielded. But when the Hebrews were gone he followed them with his army. At the last moment the sea was divided, so that the fugitives passed it dry-shod. When the pursuers drove in after them, the waves rushed back and drowned horse, foot, charioteers, and king. You spoke of revelation, my Gaspar—”\n\nThe blue eyes of the Greek sparkled.\n\n“I had the story from the Jew,” he cried. “You confirm it, O Balthasar!”\n\n“Yes, but through me Egypt speaks, not Mosche. I interpret the marbles. The priests of that time wrote in their way what they witnessed, and the revelation has lived. So I come to the one unrecorded secret. In my country, brethren, we have, from the day of the unfortunate Pharaoh, always had two religions—one private, the other public; one of many gods, practised by the people; the other of one God, cherished only by the priesthood. Rejoice with me, O brothers! All the trampling by the many nations, all the harrowing by kings, all the inventions of enemies, all the changes of time, have been in vain. Like a seed under the mountains waiting its hour, the glorious Truth has lived; and this—this is its day!”\n\nThe wasted frame of the Hindoo trembled with delight, and the Greek cried aloud,\n\n“It seems to me the very desert is singing.”\n\nFrom a gurglet of water near-by the Egyptian took a draught, and proceeded:\n\n“I was born at Alexandria, a prince and a priest, and had the education usual to my class. But very early I became discontented. Part of the faith imposed was that after death upon the destruction of the body, the soul at once began its former progression from the lowest up to humanity, the highest and last existence; and that without reference to conduct in the mortal life. When I heard of the Persian’s Realm of Light, his Paradise across the bridge Chinevat, where only the good go, the thought haunted me; insomuch that in the day, as in the night, I brooded over the comparative ideas Eternal Transmigration and Eternal Life in Heaven. If, as my teacher taught, God was just, why was there no distinction between the good and the bad? At length it became clear to me, a certainty, a corollary of the law to which I reduced pure religion, that death was only the point of separation at which the wicked are left or lost, and the faithful rise to a higher life; not the nirvana of Buddha, or the negative rest of Brahma, O Melchior; nor the better condition in hell, which is all of Heaven allowed by the Olympic faith, O Gaspar; but life—life active, joyous, everlasting—LIFE WITH GOD! The discovery led to another inquiry. Why should the Truth be longer kept a secret for the selfish solace of the priesthood? The reason for the suppression was gone. Philosophy had at least brought us toleration. In Egypt we had Rome instead of Rameses. One day, in the Brucheium, the most splendid and crowded quarter of Alexandria, I arose and preached. The East and West contributed to my audience. Students going to the Library, priests from the Serapeion, idlers from the Museum, patrons of the race-course, countrymen from the Rhacotis—a multitude—stopped to hear me. I preached God, the Soul, Right and Wrong, and Heaven, the reward of a virtuous life. You, O Melchior, were stoned; my auditors first wondered, then laughed. I tried again; they pelted me with epigrams, covered my God with ridicule, and darkened my Heaven with mockery. Not to linger needlessly, I fell before them.”\n\nThe Hindoo here drew a long sigh, as he said, “The enemy of man is man, my brother.”\n\nBalthasar lapsed into silence.\n\n“I gave much thought to finding the cause of my failure, and at last succeeded,” he said, upon beginning again. “Up the river, a day’s journey from the city, there is a village of herdsmen and gardeners. I took a boat and went there. In the evening I called the people together, men and women, the poorest of the poor. I preached to them exactly as I had preached in the Brucheium. They did not laugh. Next evening I spoke again, and they believed and rejoiced, and carried the news abroad. At the third meeting a society was formed for prayer. I returned to the city then. Drifting down the river, under the stars, which never seemed so bright and so near, I evolved this lesson: To begin a reform, go not into the places of the great and rich; go rather to those whose cups of happiness are empty—to the poor and humble. And then I laid a plan and devoted my life. As a first step, I secured my vast property, so that the income would be certain, and always at call for the relief of the suffering. From that day, O brethren, I travelled up and down the Nile, in the villages, and to all the tribes, preaching One God, a righteous life, and reward in Heaven. I have done good—it does not become me to say how much. I also know that part of the world to be ripe for the reception of Him we go to find.”\n\nA flush suffused the swarthy cheek of the speaker; but he overcame the feeling, and continued:\n\n“The years so given, O my brothers, were troubled by one thought—When I was gone, what would become of the cause I had started? Was it to end with me? I had dreamed many times of organization as a fitting crown for my work. To hide nothing from you, I had tried to effect it, and failed. Brethren, the world is now in the condition that, to restore the old Mizraimic faith, the reformer must have a more than human sanction; he must not merely come in God’s name, he must have the proofs subject to his word; he must demonstrate all he says, even God. So preoccupied is the mind with myths and systems; so much do false deities crowd every place—earth, air, sky; so have they become of everything a part, that return to the first religion can only be along bloody paths, through fields of persecution; that is to say, the converts must be willing to die rather than recant. And who in this age can carry the faith of men to such a point but God himself? To redeem the race—I do not mean to destroy it—to REDEEM the race, he must make himself once more manifest; HE MUST COME IN PERSON.”\n\nIntense emotion seized the three.\n\n“Are we not going to find him?” exclaimed the Greek.\n\n“You understand why I failed in the attempt to organize,” said the Egyptian, when the spell was past. “I had not the sanction. To know that my work must be lost made me intolerably wretched. I believed in prayer, and to make my appeals pure and strong, like you, my brethren, I went out of the beaten ways, I went where man had not been, where only God was. Above the fifth cataract, above the meeting of rivers in Sennar, up the Bahr el Abiad, into the far unknown of Africa, I went. There, in the morning, a mountain blue as the sky flings a cooling shadow wide over the western desert, and, with its cascades of melted snow, feeds a broad lake nestling at its base on the east. The lake is the mother of the great river. For a year and more the mountain gave me a home. The fruit of the palm fed my body, prayer my spirit. One night I walked in the orchard close by the little sea. ‘The world is dying. When wilt thou come? Why may I not see the redemption, O God?’ So I prayed. The glassy water was sparkling with stars. One of them seemed to leave its place, and rise to the surface, where it became a brilliancy burning to the eyes. Then it moved towards me, and stood over my head, apparently in hand’s reach. I fell down and hid my face. A voice, not of the earth, said, ‘Thy good works have conquered. Blessed art thou, O son of Mizraim! The redemption cometh. With two others, from the remotenesses of the world, thou shalt see the Saviour, and testify for him. In the morning arise, and go meet them. And when ye have all come to the holy city of Jerusalem, ask of the people, Where is he that is born King of the Jews? for we have seen his star in the East and are sent to worship him. Put all thy trust in the Spirit which will guide thee.’\n\n“And the light became an inward illumination not to be doubted, and has stayed with me, a governor and a guide. It led me down the river to Memphis, where I made ready for the desert. I bought my camel, and came hither without rest, by way of Suez and Kufileh, and up through the lands of Moab and Ammon. God is with us, O my brethren!”\n\nHe paused, and thereupon, with a prompting not their own, they all arose, and looked at each other.\n\n“I said there was a purpose in the particularity with which we described our people and their histories,” so the Egyptian proceeded. “He we go to find was called ‘King of the Jews;’ by that name we are bidden to ask for him. But, now that we have met, and heard from each other, we may know him to be the Redeemer, not of the Jews alone, but of all the nations of the earth. The patriarch who survived the Flood had with him three sons, and their families, by whom the world was repeopled. From the old Aryana-Vaejo, the well-remembered Region of Delight in the heart of Asia, they parted. India and the far East received the children of the first; the descendant of the youngest, through the North, streamed into Europe; those of the second overflowed the deserts about the Red Sea, passing into Africa; and though most of the latter are yet dwellers in shifting tents, some of them became builders along the Nile.”\n\nBy a simultaneous impulse the three joined hands.\n\n“Could anything be more divinely ordered?” Balthasar continued. “When we have found the Lord, the brothers, and all the generations that have succeeded them, will kneel to him in homage with us. And when we part to go our separate ways, the world will have learned a new lesson—that Heaven may be won, not by the sword, not by human wisdom, but by Faith, Love, and Good Works.”\n\nThere was silence, broken by sighs and sanctified with tears; for the joy that filled them might not be stayed. It was the unspeakable joy of souls on the shores of the River of Life, resting with the Redeemed in God’s presence.\n\nPresently their hands fell apart, and together they went out of the tent. The desert was still as the sky. The sun was sinking fast. The camels slept.\n\nA little while after, the tent was struck, and, with the remains of the repast, restored to the cot; then the friends mounted, and set out single file, led by the Egyptian. Their course was due west, into the chilly night. The camels swung forward in steady trot, keeping the line and the intervals so exactly that those following seemed to tread in the tracks of the leader. The riders spoke not once.\n\nBy-and-by the moon came up. And as the three tall white figures sped, with soundless tread, through the opalescent light, they appeared like specters flying from hateful shadows. Suddenly, in the air before them, not farther up than a low hill-top flared a lambent flame; as they looked at it, the apparition contracted into a focus of dazzling lustre. Their hearts beat fast; their souls thrilled; and they shouted as with one voice, “The Star! the Star! God is with us!”\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nIn an aperture of the western wall of Jerusalem hang the “oaken valves” called the Bethlehem or Joppa Gate. The area outside of them is one of the notable places of the city. Long before David coveted Zion there was a citadel there. When at last the son of Jesse ousted the Jebusite, and began to build, the site of the citadel became the northwest corner of the new wall, defended by a tower much more imposing than the old one. The location of the gate, however, was not disturbed, for the reasons, most likely, that the roads which met and merged in front of it could not well be transferred to any other point, while the area outside had become a recognized market-place. In Solomon’s day there was great traffic at the locality, shared in by traders from Egypt and the rich dealers from Tyre and Sidon. Nearly three thousand years have passed, and yet a kind of commerce clings to the spot. A pilgrim wanting a pin or a pistol, a cucumber or a camel, a house or a horse, a loan or a lentil, a date or a dragoman, a melon or a man, a dove or a donkey, has only to inquire for the article at the Joppa Gate. Sometimes the scene is quite animated, and then it suggests, What a place the old market must have been in the days of Herod the Builder! And to that period and that market the reader is now to be transferred.\n\nFollowing the Hebrew system, the meeting of the wise men described in the preceding chapters took place in the afternoon of the twenty-fifth day of the third month of the year; that is to say, on the twenty-fifth day of December. The year was the second of the 193d Olympiad, or the 747th of Rome; the sixty-seventh of Herod the Great, and the thirty-fifth of his reign; the fourth before the beginning of the Christian era. The hours of the day, by Judean custom, begin with the sun, the first hour being the first after sunrise; so, to be precise; the market at the Joppa Gate during the first hour of the day stated was in full session, and very lively. The massive valves had been wide open since dawn. Business, always aggressive, had pushed through the arched entrance into a narrow lane and court, which, passing by the walls of the great tower, conducted on into the city. As Jerusalem is in the hill country, the morning air on this occasion was not a little crisp. The rays of the sun, with their promise of warmth, lingered provokingly far up on the battlements and turrets of the great piles about, down from which fell the crooning of pigeons and the whir of the flocks coming and going.\n\nAs a passing acquaintance with the people of the Holy City, strangers as well as residents, will be necessary to an understanding of some of the pages which follow, it will be well to stop at the gate and pass the scene in review. Better opportunity will not offer to get sight of the populace who will afterwhile go forward in a mood very different from that which now possesses them.\n\nThe scene is at first one of utter confusion—confusion of action, sounds, colors, and things. It is especially so in the lane and court. The ground there is paved with broad unshaped flags, from which each cry and jar and hoof-stamp arises to swell the medley that rings and roars up between the solid impending walls. A little mixing with the throng, however, a little familiarity with the business going on, will make analysis possible.\n\nHere stands a donkey, dozing under panniers full of lentils, beans, onions, and cucumbers, brought fresh from the gardens and terraces of Galilee. When not engaged in serving customers, the master, in a voice which only the initiated can understand, cries his stock. Nothing can be simpler than his costume—sandals, and an unbleached, undyed blanket, crossed over one shoulder and girt round the waist. Near-by, and far more imposing and grotesque, though scarcely as patient as the donkey, kneels a camel, raw-boned, rough, and gray, with long shaggy tufts of fox-colored hair under its throat, neck, and body, and a load of boxes and baskets curiously arranged upon an enormous saddle. The owner is an Egyptian, small, lithe, and of a complexion which has borrowed a good deal from the dust of the roads and the sands of the desert. He wears a faded tarbooshe, a loose gown, sleeveless, unbelted, and dropping from the neck to the knee. His feet are bare. The camel, restless under the load, groans and occasionally shows his teeth; but the man paces indifferently to and fro, holding the driving-strap, and all the time advertising his fruits fresh from the orchards of the Kedron—grapes, dates, figs, apples, and pomegranates.\n\nAt the corner where the lane opens out into the court, some women sit with their backs against the gray stones of the wall. Their dress is that common to the humbler classes of the country—a linen frock extending the full length of the person, loosely gathered at the waist, and a veil or wimple broad enough, after covering the head, to wrap the shoulders. Their merchandise is contained in a number of earthen jars, such as are still used in the East for bringing water from the wells, and some leathern bottles. Among the jars and bottles, rolling upon the stony floor, regardless of the crowd and cold, often in danger but never hurt, play half a dozen half-naked children, their brown bodies, jetty eyes, and thick black hair attesting the blood of Israel. Sometimes, from under the wimples, the mothers look up, and in the vernacular modestly bespeak their trade: in the bottles “honey of grapes,” in the jars “strong drink.” Their entreaties are usually lost in the general uproar, and they fare illy against the many competitors: brawny fellows with bare legs, dirty tunics, and long beards, going about with bottles lashed to their backs, and shouting “Honey of wine! Grapes of En-Gedi!” When a customer halts one of them, round comes the bottle, and, upon lifting the thumb from the nozzle, out into the ready cup gushes the deep-red blood of the luscious berry.\n\nScarcely less blatant are the dealers in birds—doves, ducks, and frequently the singing bulbul, or nightingale, most frequently pigeons; and buyers, receiving them from the nets, seldom fail to think of the perilous life of the catchers, bold climbers of the cliffs; now hanging with hand and foot to the face of the crag, now swinging in a basket far down the mountain fissure.\n\nBlent with peddlers of jewelry—sharp men cloaked in scarlet and blue, top-heavy under prodigious white turbans, and fully conscious of the power there is in the lustre of a ribbon and the incisive gleam of gold, whether in bracelet or necklace, or in rings for the finger or the nose—and with peddlers of household utensils, and with dealers in wearing-apparel, and with retailers of unguents for anointing the person, and with hucksters of all articles, fanciful as well as of need, hither and thither, tugging at halters and ropes, now screaming, now coaxing, toil the venders of animals—donkeys, horses, calves, sheep, bleating kids, and awkward camels; animals of every kind except the outlawed swine. All these are there; not singly, as described, but many times repeated; not in one place, but everywhere in the market.\n\nTurning from this scene in the lane and court, this glance at the sellers and their commodities, the reader has need to give attention, in the next place, to visitors and buyers, for which the best studies will be found outside the gates, where the spectacle is quite as varied and animated; indeed, it may be more so, for there are superadded the effects of tent, booth, and sook, greater space, larger crowd, more unqualified freedom, and the glory of the Eastern sunshine.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nLet us take our stand by the gate, just out of the edge of the currents—one flowing in, the other out—and use our eyes and ears awhile.\n\nIn good time! Here come two men of a most noteworthy class.\n\n“Gods! How cold it is!” says one of them, a powerful figure in armor; on his head a brazen helmet, on his body a shining breastplate and skirts of mail. “How cold it is! Dost thou remember, my Caius, that vault in the Comitium at home which the flamens say is the entrance to the lower world? By Pluto! I could stand there this morning, long enough at least to get warm again!”\n\nThe party addressed drops the hood of his military cloak, leaving bare his head and face, and replies, with an ironic smile, “The helmets of the legions which conquered Mark Antony were full of Gallic snow; but thou—ah, my poor friend!—thou hast just come from Egypt, bringing its summer in thy blood.”\n\nAnd with the last word they disappear through the entrance. Though they had been silent, the armor and the sturdy step would have published them Roman soldiers.\n\nFrom the throng a Jew comes next, meager of frame, round-shouldered, and wearing a coarse brown robe; over his eyes and face, and down his back, hangs a mat of long, uncombed hair. He is alone. Those who meet him laugh, if they do not worse; for he is a Nazarite, one of a despised sect which rejects the books of Moses, devotes itself to abhorred vows, and goes unshorn while the vows endure.\n\nAs we watch his retiring figure, suddenly there is a commotion in the crowd, a parting quickly to the right and left, with exclamations sharp and decisive. Then the cause comes—a man, Hebrew in feature and dress. The mantle of snow-white linen, held to his head by cords of yellow silk, flows free over his shoulders; his robe is richly embroidered, a red sash with fringes of gold wraps his waist several times. His demeanor is calm; he even smiles upon those who, with such rude haste, make room for him. A leper? No, he is only a Samaritan. The shrinking crowd, if asked, would say he is a mongrel—an Assyrian—whose touch of the robe is pollution; from whom, consequently, an Israelite, though dying, might not accept life. In fact, the feud is not of blood. When David set his throne here on Mount Zion, with only Judah to support him, the ten tribes betook themselves to Shechem, a city much older, and, at that date, infinitely richer in holy memories. The final union of the tribes did not settle the dispute thus begun. The Samaritans clung to their tabernacle on Gerizim, and, while maintaining its superior sanctity, laughed at the irate doctors in Jerusalem. Time brought no assuagement of the hate. Under Herod, conversion to the faith was open to all the world except the Samaritans; they alone were absolutely and forever shut out from communion with Jews.\n\nAs the Samaritan goes in under the arch of the gate, out come three men so unlike all whom we have yet seen that they fix our gaze, whether we will or not. They are of unusual stature and immense brawn; their eyes are blue, and so fair is their complexion that the blood shines through the skin like blue pencilling; their hair is light and short; their heads, small and round, rest squarely upon necks columnar as the trunks of trees. Woollen tunics, open at the breast, sleeveless and loosely girt, drape their bodies, leaving bare arms and legs of such development that they at once suggest the arena; and when thereto we add their careless, confident, insolent manner, we cease to wonder that the people give them way, and stop after they have passed to look at them again. They are gladiators—wrestlers, runners, boxers, swordsmen; professionals unknown in Judea before the coming of the Roman; fellows who, what time they are not in training, may be seen strolling through the king’s gardens or sitting with the guards at the palace gates; or possibly they are visitors from Cæsarea, Sebaste, or Jericho; in which Herod, more Greek than Jew, and with all a Roman’s love of games and bloody spectacles, has built vast theaters, and now keeps schools of fighting-men, drawn, as is the custom, from the Gallic provinces or the Slavic tribes on the Danube.\n\n“By Bacchus!” says one of them, drawing his clenched hand to his shoulder, “their skulls are not thicker than eggshells.”\n\nThe brutal look which goes with the gesture disgusts us, and we turn happily to something more pleasant.\n\nOpposite us is a fruit-stand. The proprietor has a bald head, a long face, and a nose like the beak of a hawk. He sits upon a carpet spread upon the dust; the wall is at his back; overhead hangs a scant curtain, around him, within hand’s reach and arranged upon little stools, lie osier boxes full of almonds, grapes, figs, and pomegranates. To him now comes one at whom we cannot help looking, though for another reason than that which fixed our eyes upon the gladiators; he is really beautiful—a beautiful Greek. Around his temples, holding the waving hair, is a crown of myrtle, to which still cling the pale flowers and half ripe berries. His tunic, scarlet in color, is of the softest woollen fabric; below the girdle of buff leather, which is clasped in front by a fantastic device of shining gold, the skirt drops to the knee in folds heavy with embroidery of the same royal metal; a scarf, also woollen, and of mixed white and yellow, crosses his throat and falls trailing at his back; his arms and legs, where exposed, are white as ivory, and of the polish impossible except by perfect treatment with bath, oil, brushes, and pincers.\n\nThe dealer, keeping his seat, bends forward, and throws his hands up until they meet in front of him, palm downwards and fingers extended.\n\n“What hast thou, this morning, O son of Paphos?” says the young Greek, looking at the boxes rather than at the Cypriote. “I am hungry. What hast thou for breakfast?”\n\n“Fruits from the Pedius—genuine—such as the singers of Antioch take of mornings to restore the waste of their voices,” the dealer answers, in a querulous nasal tone.\n\n“A fig, but not one of thy best, for the singers of Antioch!” says the Greek. “Thou art a worshiper of Aphrodite, and so am I, as the myrtle I wear proves; therefore I tell thee their voices have the chill of a Caspian wind. Seest thou this girdle?—a gift of the mighty Salome—”\n\n“The king’s sister!” exclaims the Cypriote, with another salaam.\n\n“And of royal taste and divine judgment. And why not? She is more Greek than the king. But—my breakfast! Here is thy money—red coppers of Cyprus. Give me grapes, and—”\n\n“Wilt thou not take the dates also?”\n\n“No, I am not an Arab.”\n\n“Nor figs?”\n\n“That would be to make me a Jew. No, nothing but the grapes. Never waters mixed so sweetly as the blood of the Greek and the blood of the grape.”\n\nThe singer in the grimed and seething market, with all his airs of the court, is a vision not easily shut out of mind by such as see him; as if for the purpose, however, a person follows him challenging all our wonder. He comes up the road slowly, his face towards the ground; at intervals he stops, crosses his hands upon his breast, lengthens his countenance, and turns his eyes towards heaven, as if about to break into prayer. Nowhere, except in Jerusalem, can such a character be found. On his forehead, attached to the band which keeps the mantle in place, projects a leathern case, square in form; another similar case is tied by a thong to the left arm; the borders of his robe are decorated with deep fringe; and by such signs—the phylacteries, the enlarged borders of the garment, and the savor of intense holiness pervading the whole man—we know him to be a Pharisee, one of an organization (in religion a sect, in politics a party) whose bigotry and power will shortly bring the world to grief.\n\nThe densest of the throng outside the gate covers the road leading off to Joppa. Turning from the Pharisee, we are attracted by some parties who, as subjects of study, opportunely separate themselves from the motley crowd. First among them a man of very noble appearance—clear, healthful complexion; bright black eyes; beard long and flowing, and rich with unguents; apparel well-fitting, costly, and suitable for the season. He carries a staff, and wears, suspended by a cord from his neck, a large golden seal. Several servants attend him, some of them with short swords stuck through their sashes; when they address him, it is with the utmost deference. The rest of the party consists of two Arabs of the pure desert stock; thin, wiry men, deeply bronzed, and with hollow cheeks, and eyes of almost evil brightness; on their heads red tarbooshes; over their abas, and wrapping the left shoulder and the body so as to leave the right arm free, brown woollen haicks, or blankets. There is loud chaffering, for the Arabs are leading horses and trying to sell them; and, in their eagerness, they speak in high, shrill voices. The courtly person leaves the talking mostly to his servants; occasionally he answers with much dignity; directly, seeing the Cypriote, he stops and buys some figs. And when the whole party has passed the portal, close after the Pharisee, if we betake ourselves to the dealer in fruits, he will tell, with a wonderful salaam, that the stranger is a Jew, one of the princes of the city, who has travelled, and learned the difference between the common grapes of Syria and those of Cyprus, so surpassingly rich with the dews of the sea.\n\nAnd so, till towards noon, sometimes later, the steady currents of business habitually flow in and out of the Joppa Gate, carrying with them every variety of character; including representatives of all the tribes of Israel, all the sects among whom the ancient faith has been parcelled and refined away, all the religious and social divisions, all the adventurous rabble who, as children of art and ministers of pleasure, riot in the prodigalities of Herod, and all the peoples of note at any time compassed by the Cæsars and their predecessors, especially those dwelling within the circuit of the Mediterranean.\n\nIn other words, Jerusalem, rich in sacred history, richer in connection with sacred prophecies—the Jerusalem of Solomon, in which silver was as stones, and cedars as the sycamores of the vale—had come to be but a copy of Rome, a center of unholy practises, a seat of pagan power. A Jewish king one day put on priestly garments, and went into the Holy of Holies of the first temple to offer incense, and he came out a leper; but in the time of which we are reading, Pompey entered Herod’s temple and the same Holy of Holies, and came out without harm, finding but an empty chamber, and of God not a sign.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\nThe reader is now besought to return to the court described as part of the market at the Joppa Gate. It was the third hour of the day, and many of the people had gone away; yet the press continued without apparent abatement. Of the new-comers, there was a group over by the south wall, consisting of a man, a woman, and a donkey, which requires extended notice.\n\nThe man stood by the animal’s head, holding a leading-strap, and leaning upon a stick which seemed to have been chosen for the double purpose of goad and staff. His dress was like that of the ordinary Jews around him, except that it had an appearance of newness. The mantle dropping from his head, and the robe or frock which clothed his person from neck to heel, were probably the garments he was accustomed to wear to the synagogue on Sabbath days. His features were exposed, and they told of fifty years of life, a surmise confirmed by the gray that streaked his otherwise black beard. He looked around him with the half-curious, half-vacant stare of a stranger and provincial.\n\nThe donkey ate leisurely from an armful of green grass, of which there was an abundance in the market. In its sleepy content, the brute did not admit of disturbance from the bustle and clamor about; no more was it mindful of the woman sitting upon its back in a cushioned pillion. An outer robe of dull woollen stuff completely covered her person, while a white wimple veiled her head and neck. Once in a while, impelled by curiosity to see or hear something passing, she drew the wimple aside, but so slightly that the face remained invisible.\n\nAt length the man was accosted.\n\n“Are you not Joseph of Nazareth?”\n\nThe speaker was standing close by.\n\n“I am so called,” answered Joseph, turning gravely around; “And you—ah, peace be unto you! my friend, Rabbi Samuel!”\n\n“The same give I back to you.” The Rabbi paused, looking at the woman, then added, “To you, and unto your house and all your helpers, be peace.”\n\nWith the last word, he placed one hand upon his breast, and inclined his head to the woman, who, to see him, had by this time withdrawn the wimple enough to show the face of one but a short time out of girlhood. Thereupon the acquaintances grasped right hands, as if to carry them to their lips; at the last moment, however, the clasp was let go, and each kissed his own hand, then put its palm upon his forehead.\n\n“There is so little dust upon your garments,” the Rabbi said, familiarly, “that I infer you passed the night in this city of our fathers.”\n\n“No,” Joseph replied, “as we could only make Bethany before the night came, we stayed in the khan there, and took the road again at daybreak.”\n\n“The journey before you is long, then—not to Joppa, I hope.”\n\n“Only to Bethlehem.”\n\nThe countenance of the Rabbi, theretofore open and friendly, became lowering and sinister, and he cleared his throat with a growl instead of a cough.\n\n“Yes, yes—I see,” he said. “You were born in Bethlehem, and wend thither now, with your daughter, to be counted for taxation, as ordered by Cæsar. The children of Jacob are as the tribes in Egypt were—only they have neither a Moses nor a Joshua. How are the mighty fallen!”\n\nJoseph answered, without change of posture or countenance,\n\n“The woman is not my daughter.”\n\nBut the Rabbi clung to the political idea; and he went on, without noticing the explanation, “What are the Zealots doing down in Galilee?”\n\n“I am a carpenter, and Nazareth is a village,” said Joseph, cautiously. “The street on which my bench stands is not a road leading to any city. Hewing wood and sawing plank leave me no time to take part in the disputes of parties.”\n\n“But you are a Jew,” said the Rabbi, earnestly. “You are a Jew, and of the line of David. It is not possible you can find pleasure in the payment of any tax except the shekel given by ancient custom to Jehovah.”\n\nJoseph held his peace.\n\n“I do not complain,” his friend continued, “of the amount of the tax—a denarius is a trifle. Oh no! The imposition of the tax is the offense. And, besides, what is paying it but submission to tyranny? Tell me, is it true that Judas claims to be the Messiah? You live in the midst of his followers.”\n\n“I have heard his followers say he was the Messiah,” Joseph replied.\n\nAt this point the wimple was drawn aside, and for an instant the whole face of the woman was exposed. The eyes of the Rabbi wandered that way, and he had time to see a countenance of rare beauty, kindled by a look of intense interest; then a blush overspread her cheeks and brow, and the veil was returned to its place.\n\nThe politician forgot his subject.\n\n“Your daughter is comely,” he said, speaking lower.\n\n“She is not my daughter,” Joseph repeated.\n\nThe curiosity of the Rabbi was aroused; seeing which, the Nazarene hastened to say further, “She is the child of Joachim and Anna of Bethlehem, of whom you have at least heard, for they were of great repute—”\n\n“Yes,” remarked the Rabbi, deferentially, “I know them. They were lineally descended from David. I knew them well.”\n\n“Well, they are dead now,” the Nazarene proceeded. “They died in Nazareth. Joachim was not rich, yet he left a house and garden to be divided between his daughters Marian and Mary. This is one of them; and to save her portion of the property, the law required her to marry her next of kin. She is now my wife.”\n\n“And you were—”\n\n“Her uncle.”\n\n“Yes, yes! And as you were both born in Bethlehem, the Roman compels you to take her there with you to be also counted.”\n\nThe Rabbi clasped his hands, and looked indignantly to heaven, exclaiming, “The God of Israel still lives! The vengeance is his!”\n\nWith that he turned and abruptly departed. A stranger near by, observing Joseph’s amazement, said, quietly, “Rabbi Samuel is a zealot. Judas himself is not more fierce.”\n\nJoseph, not wishing to talk with the man, appeared not to hear, and busied himself gathering in a little heap the grass which the donkey had tossed abroad; after which he leaned upon his staff again, and waited.\n\nIn another hour the party passed out the gate, and, turning to the left, took the road into Bethlehem. The descent into the valley of Hinnom was quite broken, garnished here and there with straggling wild olive-trees. Carefully, tenderly, the Nazarene walked by the woman’s side, leading-strap in hand. On their left, reaching to the south and east round Mount Zion, rose the city wall, and on their right the steep prominences which form the western boundary of the valley.\n\nSlowly they passed the Lower Pool of Gihon, out of which the sun was fast driving the lessening shadow of the royal hill; slowly they proceeded, keeping parallel with the aqueduct from the Pools of Solomon, until near the site of the country-house on what is now called the Hill of Evil Counsel; there they began to ascend to the plain of Rephaim. The sun streamed garishly over the stony face of the famous locality, and under its influence Mary, the daughter of Joachim, dropped the wimple entirely, and bared her head. Joseph told the story of the Philistines surprised in their camp there by David. He was tedious in the narrative, speaking with the solemn countenance and lifeless manner of a dull man. She did not always hear him.\n\nWherever on the land men go, and on the sea ships, the face and figure of the Jew are familiar. The physical type of the race has always been the same; yet there have been some individual variations. “Now he was ruddy, and withal of a beautiful countenance, and goodly to look to.” Such was the son of Jesse when brought before Samuel. The fancies of men have been ever since ruled by the description. Poetic license has extended the peculiarities of the ancestor to his notable descendants. So all our ideal Solomons have fair faces, and hair and beard chestnut in the shade, and of the tint of gold in the sun. Such, we are also made believe, were the locks of Absalom the beloved. And, in the absence of authentic history, tradition has dealt no less lovingly by her whom we are now following down to the native city of the ruddy king.\n\nShe was not more than fifteen. Her form, voice, and manner belonged to the period of transition from girlhood. Her face was perfectly oval, her complexion more pale than fair. The nose was faultless; the lips, slightly parted, were full and ripe, giving to the lines of the mouth warmth, tenderness, and trust; the eyes were blue and large, and shaded by drooping lids and long lashes; and, in harmony with all, a flood of golden hair, in the style permitted to Jewish brides, fell unconfined down her back to the pillion on which she sat. The throat and neck had the downy softness sometimes seen which leaves the artist in doubt whether it is an effect of contour or color. To these charms of feature and person were added others more indefinable—an air of purity which only the soul can impart, and of abstraction natural to such as think much of things impalpable. Often, with trembling lips, she raised her eyes to heaven, itself not more deeply blue; often she crossed her hands upon her breast, as in adoration and prayer; often she raised her head like one listening eagerly for a calling voice. Now and then, midst his slow utterances, Joseph turned to look at her, and, catching the expression kindling her face as with light, forgot his theme, and with bowed head, wondering, plodded on.\n\nSo they skirted the great plain, and at length reached the elevation Mar Elias; from which, across a valley, they beheld Bethlehem, the old, old House of Bread, its white walls crowning a ridge, and shining above the brown scumbling of leafless orchards. They paused there, and rested, while Joseph pointed out the places of sacred renown; then they went down into the valley to the well which was the scene of one of the marvellous exploits of David’s strong men. The narrow space was crowded with people and animals. A fear came upon Joseph—a fear lest, if the town were so thronged, there might not be house-room for the gentle Mary. Without delay, he hurried on, past the pillar of stone marking the tomb of Rachel, up the gardened slope, saluting none of the many persons he met on the way, until he stopped before the portal of the khan that then stood outside the village gates, near a junction of roads.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nTo understand thoroughly what happened to the Nazarene at the khan, the reader must be reminded that Eastern inns were different from the inns of the Western world. They were called khans, from the Persian, and, in simplest form, were fenced enclosures, without house or shed, often without a gate or entrance. Their sites were chosen with reference to shade, defence, or water. Such were the inns that sheltered Jacob when he went to seek a wife in Padan-Aram. Their like may been seen at this day in the stopping-places of the desert. On the other hand, some of them, especially those on the roads between great cities, like Jerusalem and Alexandria, were princely establishments, monuments to the piety of the kings who built them. In ordinary, however, they were no more than the house or possession of a sheik, in which, as in headquarters, he swayed his tribe. Lodging the traveller was the least of their uses; they were markets, factories, forts; places of assemblage and residence for merchants and artisans quite as much as places of shelter for belated and wandering wayfarers. Within their walls, all the year round, occurred the multiplied daily transactions of a town.\n\nThe singular management of these hostelries was the feature likely to strike a Western mind with most force. There was no host or hostess; no clerk, cook, or kitchen; a steward at the gate was all the assertion of government or proprietorship anywhere visible. Strangers arriving stayed at will without rendering account. A consequence of the system was that whoever came had to bring his food and culinary outfit with him, or buy them of dealers in the khan. The same rule held good as to his bed and bedding, and forage for his beasts. Water, rest, shelter, and protection were all he looked for from the proprietor, and they were gratuities. The peace of synagogues was sometimes broken by brawling disputants, but that of the khans never. The houses and all their appurtenances were sacred: a well was not more so.\n\nThe khan at Bethlehem, before which Joseph and his wife stopped, was a good specimen of its class, being neither very primitive nor very princely. The building was purely Oriental; that is to say, a quadrangular block of rough stones, one story high, flat-roofed, externally unbroken by a window, and with but one principal entrance—a doorway, which was also a gateway, on the eastern side, or front. The road ran by the door so near that the chalk dust half covered the lintel. A fence of flat rocks, beginning at the northeastern corner of the pile, extended many yards down the slope to a point from whence it swept westwardly to a limestone bluff; making what was in the highest degree essential to a respectable khan—a safe enclosure for animals.\n\nIn a village like Bethlehem, as there was but one sheik, there could not well be more than one khan; and, though born in the place, the Nazarene, from long residence elsewhere, had no claim to hospitality in the town. Moreover, the enumeration for which he was coming might be the work of weeks or months; Roman deputies in the provinces were proverbially slow; and to impose himself and wife for a period so uncertain upon acquaintances or relations was out of the question. So, before he drew nigh the great house, while he was yet climbing the slope, in the steep places toiling to hasten the donkey, the fear that he might not find accommodations in the khan became a painful anxiety; for he found the road thronged with men and boys who, with great ado, were taking their cattle, horses, and camels to and from the valley, some to water, some to the neighboring caves. And when he was come close by, his alarm was not allayed by the discovery of a crowd investing the door of the establishment, while the enclosure adjoining, broad as it was, seemed already full.\n\n“We cannot reach the door,” Joseph said, in his slow way. “Let us stop here, and learn, if we can, what has happened.”\n\nThe wife, without answering, quietly drew the wimple aside. The look of fatigue at first upon her face changed to one of interest. She found herself at the edge of an assemblage that could not be other than a matter of curiosity to her, although it was common enough at the khans on any of the highways which the great caravans were accustomed to traverse. There were men on foot, running hither and thither, talking shrilly and in all the tongues of Syria; men on horseback screaming to men on camels; men struggling doubtfully with fractious cows and frightened sheep; men peddling bread and wine; and among the mass a herd of boys apparently in chase of a herd of dogs. Everybody and everything seemed to be in motion at the same time. Possibly the fair spectator was too weary to be long attracted by the scene; in a little while she sighed, and settled down on the pillion, and, as if in search of peace and rest, or in expectation of some one, looked off to the south, and up to the tall cliffs of the Mount of Paradise, then faintly reddening under the setting sun.\n\nWhile she was thus looking, a man pushed his way out of the press, and, stopping close by the donkey, faced about with an angry brow. The Nazarene spoke to him.\n\n“As I am what I take you to be, good friend—a son of Judah—may I ask the cause of this multitude?”\n\nThe stranger turned fiercely; but, seeing the solemn countenance of Joseph, so in keeping with his deep, slow voice and speech, he raised his hand in half-salutation, and replied,\n\n“Peace be to you, Rabbi! I am a son of Judah, and will answer you. I dwell in Beth-Dagon, which, you know, is in what used to be the land of the tribe of Dan.”\n\n“On the road to Joppa from Modin,” said Joseph.\n\n“Ah, you have been in Beth-Dagon,” the man said, his face softening yet more. “What wanderers we of Judah are! I have been away from the ridge—old Ephrath, as our father Jacob called it—for many years. When the proclamation went abroad requiring all Hebrews to be numbered at the cities of their birth— That is my business here, Rabbi.”\n\nJoseph’s face remained stolid as a mask, while he remarked, “I have come for that also—I and my wife.”\n\nThe stranger glanced at Mary and kept silence. She was looking up at the bald top of Gedor. The sun touched her upturned face, and filled the violet depths of her eyes, and upon her parted lips trembled an aspiration which could not have been to a mortal. For the moment, all the humanity of her beauty seemed refined away: she was as we fancy they are who sit close by the gate in the transfiguring light of Heaven. The Beth-Dagonite saw the original of what, centuries after, came as a vision of genius to Sanzio the divine, and left him immortal.\n\n“Of what was I speaking? Ah! I remember. I was about to say that when I heard of the order to come here, I was angry. Then I thought of the old hill, and the town, and the valley falling away into the depths of Cedron; of the vines and orchards, and fields of grain, unfailing since the days of Boaz and Ruth, of the familiar mountains—Gedor here, Gibeah yonder, Mar Elias there—which, when I was a boy, were the walls of the world to me; and I forgave the tyrants and came—I, and Rachel, my wife, and Deborah and Michal, our roses of Sharon.”\n\nThe man paused again, looking abruptly at Mary, who was now looking at him and listening. Then he said, “Rabbi, will not your wife go to mine? You may see her yonder with the children, under the leaning olive-tree at the bend of the road. I tell you”—he turned to Joseph and spoke positively—“I tell you the khan is full. It is useless to ask at the gate.”\n\nJoseph’s will was slow, like his mind; he hesitated, but at length replied, “The offer is kind. Whether there be room for us or not in the house, we will go see your people. Let me speak to the gate-keeper myself. I will return quickly.”\n\nAnd, putting the leading-strap in the stranger’s hand, he pushed into the stirring crowd.\n\nThe keeper sat on a great cedar block outside the gate. Against the wall behind him leaned a javelin. A dog squatted on the block by his side.\n\n“The peace of Jehovah be with you,” said Joseph, at last confronting the keeper.\n\n“What you give, may you find again; and, when found, be it many times multiplied to you and yours,” returned the watchman, gravely, though without moving.\n\n“I am a Bethlehemite,” said Joseph, in his most deliberate way. “Is there not room for—”\n\n“There is not.”\n\n“You may have heard of me—Joseph of Nazareth. This is the house of my fathers. I am of the line of David.”\n\nThese words held the Nazarene’s hope. If they failed him, further appeal was idle, even that of the offer of many shekels. To be a son of Judah was one thing—in the tribal opinion a great thing; to be of the house of David was yet another; on the tongue of a Hebrew there could be no higher boast. A thousand years and more had passed since the boyish shepherd became the successor of Saul and founded a royal family. Wars, calamities, other kings, and the countless obscuring processes of time had, as respects fortune, lowered his descendants to the common Jewish level; the bread they ate came to them of toil never more humble; yet they had the benefit of history sacredly kept, of which genealogy was the first chapter and the last; they could not become unknown, while, wherever they went In Israel, acquaintance drew after it a respect amounting to reverence.\n\nIf this were so in Jerusalem and elsewhere, certainly one of the sacred line might reasonably rely upon it at the door of the khan of Bethlehem. To say, as Joseph said, “This is the house of my fathers,” was to say the truth most simply and literally; for it was the very house Ruth ruled as the wife of Boaz, the very house in which Jesse and his ten sons, David the youngest, were born, the very house in which Samuel came seeking a king, and found him; the very house which David gave to the son of Barzillai, the friendly Gileadite; the very house in which Jeremiah, by prayer, rescued the remnant of his race flying before the Babylonians.\n\nThe appeal was not without effect. The keeper of the gate slid down from the cedar block, and, laying his hand upon his beard, said, respectfully, “Rabbi, I cannot tell you when this door first opened in welcome to the traveller, but it was more than a thousand years ago; and in all that time there is no known instance of a good man turned away, save when there was no room to rest him in. If it has been so with the stranger, just cause must the steward have who says no to one of the line of David. Wherefore, I salute you again; and, if you care to go with me, I will show you that there is not a lodging-place left in the house; neither in the chambers, nor in the lewens, nor in the court—not even on the roof. May I ask when you came?”\n\n“But now.”\n\nThe keeper smiled.\n\n“‘The stranger that dwelleth with you shall be as one born among you, and thou shalt love him as thyself.’ Is not that the law, Rabbi?”\n\nJoseph was silent.\n\n“If it be the law, can I say to one a long time come, ‘Go thy way; another is here to take thy place?’”\n\nYet Joseph held his peace.\n\n“And, if I said so, to whom would the place belong? See the many that have been waiting, some of them since noon.”\n\n“Who are all these people?” asked Joseph, turning to the crowd. “And why are they here at this time?”\n\n“That which doubtless brought you, Rabbi—the decree of the Cæsar”—the keeper threw an interrogative glance at the Nazarene, then continued—“brought most of those who have lodging in the house. And yesterday the caravan passing from Damascus to Arabia and Lower Egypt arrived. These you see here belong to it—men and camels.”\n\nStill Joseph persisted.\n\n“The court is large,” he said.\n\n“Yes, but it is heaped with cargoes—with bales of silk, and pockets of spices, and goods of every kind.”\n\nThen for a moment the face of the applicant lost its stolidity; the lustreless, staring eyes dropped. With some warmth he next said, “I do not care for myself, but I have with me my wife, and the night is cold—colder on these heights than in Nazareth. She cannot live in the open air. Is there not room in the town?”\n\n“These people”—the keeper waved his hand to the throng before the door—“have all besought the town, and they report its accommodations all engaged.”\n\nAgain Joseph studied the ground, saying, half to himself, “She is so young! if I make her bed on the hill, the frosts will kill her.”\n\nThen he spoke to the keeper again.\n\n“It may be you knew her parents, Joachim and Anna, once of Bethlehem, and, like myself, of the line of David.”\n\n“Yes, I knew them. They were good people. That was in my youth.”\n\nThis time the keeper’s eyes sought the ground in thought. Suddenly he raised his head.\n\n“If I cannot make room for you,” he said, “I cannot turn you away. Rabbi, I will do the best I can for you. How many are of your party?”\n\nJoseph reflected, then replied, “My wife and a friend with his family, from Beth-Dagon, a little town over by Joppa; in all, six of us.”\n\n“Very well. You shall not lie out on the ridge. Bring your people, and hasten; for, when the sun goes down behind the mountain, you know the night comes quickly, and it is nearly there now.”\n\n“I give you the blessing of the houseless traveller; that of the sojourner will follow.”\n\nSo saying, the Nazarene went back joyfully to Mary and the Beth-Dagonite. In a little while the latter brought up his family, the women mounted on donkeys. The wife was matronly, the daughters were images of what she must have been in youth; and as they drew nigh the door, the keeper knew them to be of the humble class.\n\n“This is she of whom I spoke,” said the Nazarene; “and these are our friends.”\n\nMary’s veil was raised.\n\n“Blue eyes and hair of gold,” muttered the steward to himself, seeing but her. “So looked the young king when he went to sing before Saul.”\n\nThen he took the leading-strap from Joseph, and said to Mary, “Peace to you, O daughter of David!” Then to the others, “Peace to you all!” Then to Joseph, “Rabbi, follow me.”\n\nThe party were conducted into a wide passage paved with stone, from which they entered the court of the khan. To a stranger the scene would have been curious; but they noticed the lewens that yawned darkly upon them from all sides, and the court itself, only to remark how crowded they were. By a lane reserved in the stowage of the cargoes, and thence by a passage similar to the one at the entrance, they emerged into the enclosure adjoining the house, and came upon camels, horses, and donkeys, tethered and dozing in close groups; among them were the keepers, men of many lands; and they, too, slept or kept silent watch. They went down the slope of the crowded yard slowly, for the dull carriers of the women had wills of their own. At length they turned into a path running towards the gray limestone bluff overlooking the khan on the west.\n\n“We are going to the cave,” said Joseph, laconically.\n\nThe guide lingered till Mary came to his side.\n\n“The cave to which we are going,” he said to her, “must have been a resort of your ancestor David. From the field below us, and from the well down in the valley, he used to drive his flocks to it for safety; and afterwards, when he was king, he came back to the old house here for rest and health, bringing great trains of animals. The mangers yet remain as they were in his day. Better a bed on the floor where he has slept than one in the court-yard or out by the roadside. Ah, here is the house before the cave!”\n\nThis speech must not be taken as an apology for the lodging offered. There was no need of apology. The place was the best then at disposal. The guests were simple folks, by habits of life easily satisfied. To the Jew of that period, moreover, abode in caverns was a familiar idea, made so by every-day occurrences, and by what he heard of Sabbaths in the synagogues. How much of Jewish history, how many of the many exciting incidents in that history, had transpired in caves! Yet further, these people were Jews of Bethlehem, with whom the idea was especially commonplace; for their locality abounded with caves great and small, some of which had been dwelling-places from the time of the Emim and Horites. No more was there offence to them in the fact that the cavern to which they were being taken had been, or was, a stable. They were the descendants of a race of herdsmen, whose flocks habitually shared both their habitations and wanderings. In keeping with a custom derived from Abraham, the tent of the Bedawin yet shelters his horses and children alike. So they obeyed the keeper cheerfully, and gazed at the house, feeling only a natural curiosity. Everything associated with the history of David was interesting to them.\n\nThe building was low and narrow, projecting but a little from the rock to which it was joined at the rear, and wholly without a window. In its blank front there was a door, swung on enormous hinges, and thickly daubed with ochreous clay. While the wooden bolt of the lock was being pushed back, the women were assisted from their pillions. Upon the opening of the door, the keeper called out,\n\n“Come in!”\n\nThe guests entered, and stared about them. It became apparent immediately that the house was but a mask or covering for the mouth of a natural cave or grotto, probably forty feet long, nine or ten high, and twelve or fifteen in width. The light streamed through the doorway, over an uneven floor, falling upon piles of grain and fodder, and earthenware and household property, occupying the centre of the chamber. Along the sides were mangers, low enough for sheep, and built of stones laid in cement. There were no stalls or partitions of any kind. Dust and chaff yellowed the floor, filled all the crevices and hollows, and thickened the spider-webs, which dropped from the ceiling like bits of dirty linen; otherwise the place was cleanly, and, to appearance, as comfortable as any of the arched lewens of the khan proper. In fact, a cave was the model and first suggestion of the lewen.\n\n“Come in!” said the guide. “These piles upon the floor are for travellers like yourselves. Take what of them you need.”\n\nThen he spoke to Mary.\n\n“Can you rest here?”\n\n“The place is sanctified,” she answered.\n\n“I leave you then. Peace be with you all!”\n\nWhen he was gone, they busied themselves making the cave habitable.\n\n# CHAPTER X.\n\nAt a certain hour in the evening the shouting and stir of the people in and about the khan ceased; at the same time, every Israelite, if not already upon his feet, arose, solemnized his face, looked towards Jerusalem, crossed his hands upon his breast, and prayed; for it was the sacred ninth hour, when sacrifices were offered in the temple on Moriah, and God was supposed to be there. When the hands of the worshippers fell down, the commotion broke forth again; everybody hastened to bread, or to make his pallet. A little later, the lights were put out, and there was silence, and then sleep.\n\nAbout midnight some one on the roof cried out, “What light is that in the sky? Awake, brethren, awake and see!”\n\nThe people, half asleep, sat up and looked; then they became wide-awake, though wonder-struck. And the stir spread to the court below, and into the lewens; soon the entire tenantry of the house and court and enclosure were out gazing at the sky.\n\nAnd this was what they saw. A ray of light, beginning at a height immeasurably beyond the nearest stars, and dropping obliquely to the earth; at its top, a diminishing point; at its base, many furlongs in width; its sides blending softly with the darkness of the night, its core a roseate electrical splendor. The apparition seemed to rest on the nearest mountain southeast of the town, making a pale corona along the line of the summit. The khan was touched luminously, so that those upon the roof saw each other’s faces, all filled with wonder.\n\nSteadily, through minutes, the ray lingered, and then the wonder changed to awe and fear; the timid trembled; the boldest spoke in whispers.\n\n“Saw you ever the like?” asked one.\n\n“It seems just over the mountain there. I cannot tell what it is, nor did I ever see anything like it,” was the answer.\n\n“Can it be that a star has burst and fallen?” asked another, his tongue faltering.\n\n“When a star falls, its light goes out.”\n\n“I have it!” cried one, confidently. “The shepherds have seen a lion, and made fires to keep him from the flocks.”\n\nThe men next the speaker drew a breath of relief, and said, “Yes, that is it! The flocks were grazing in the valley over there to-day.”\n\nA bystander dispelled the comfort.\n\n“No, no! Though all the wood in all the valleys of Judah was brought together in one pile and fired, the blaze would not throw a light so strong and high.”\n\nAfter that there was silence on the house-top, broken but once again while the mystery continued.\n\n“Brethren!” exclaimed a Jew of venerable mien, “what we see is the ladder our father Jacob saw in his dream. Blessed be the Lord God of our fathers!”\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\nA mile and a half, it may be two miles, southeast of Bethlehem, there is a plain separated from the town by an intervening swell of the mountain. Besides being well sheltered from the north winds, the vale was covered with a growth of sycamore, dwarf-oak, and pine trees, while in the glens and ravines adjoining there were thickets of olive and mulberry; all at this season of the year invaluable for the support of sheep, goats, and cattle, of which the wandering flocks consisted.\n\nAt the side farthest from the town, close under a bluff, there was an extensive marah, or sheepcot, ages old. In some long-forgotten foray, the building had been unroofed and almost demolished. The enclosure attached to it remained intact, however, and that was of more importance to the shepherds who drove their charges thither than the house itself. The stone wall around the lot was high as a man’s head, yet not so high but that sometimes a panther or a lion, hungering from the wilderness, leaped boldly in. On the inner side of the wall, and as an additional security against the constant danger, a hedge of the rhamnus had been planted, an invention so successful that now a sparrow could hardly penetrate the overtopping branches, armed as they were with great clusters of thorns hard as spikes.\n\nThe day of the occurrences which occupy the preceding chapters, a number of shepherds, seeking fresh walks for their flocks, led them up to this plain; and from early morning the groves had been made ring with calls, and the blows of axes, the bleating of sheep and goats, the tinkling of bells, the lowing of cattle, and the barking of dogs. When the sun went down, they led the way to the marah, and by nightfall had everything safe in the field; then they kindled a fire down by the gate, partook of their humble supper, and sat down to rest and talk, leaving one on watch.\n\nThere were six of these men, omitting the watchman; and afterwhile they assembled in a group near the fire, some sitting, some lying prone. As they went bareheaded habitually, their hair stood out in thick, coarse, sunburnt shocks; their beard covered their throats, and fell in mats down the breast; mantles of the skin of kids and lambs, with the fleece on, wrapped them from neck to knee, leaving the arms exposed; broad belts girthed the rude garments to their waists; their sandals were of the coarsest quality; from their right shoulders hung scrips containing food and selected stones for slings, with which they were armed; on the ground near each one lay his crook, a symbol of his calling and a weapon of offence.\n\nSuch were the shepherds of Judea! In appearance, rough and savage as the gaunt dogs sitting with them around the blaze; in fact, simple-minded, tender-hearted; effects due, in part, to the primitive life they led, but chiefly to their constant care of things lovable and helpless.\n\nThey rested and talked, and their talk was all about their flocks, a dull theme to the world, yet a theme which was all the world to them. If in narrative they dwelt long upon affairs of trifling moment; if one of them omitted nothing of detail in recounting the loss of a lamb, the relation between him and the unfortunate should be remembered: at birth it became his charge, his to keep all its days, to help over the floods, to carry down the hollows, to name and train; it was to be his companion, his object of thought and interest, the subject of his will; it was to enliven and share his wanderings; in its defense he might be called on to face the lion or robber—to die.\n\nThe great events, such as blotted out nations and changed the mastery of the world, were trifles to them, if perchance they came to their knowledge. Of what Herod was doing in this city or that, building palaces and gymnasia, and indulging forbidden practises, they occasionally heard. As was her habit in those days, Rome did not wait for people slow to inquire about her; she came to them. Over the hills along which he was leading his lagging herd, or in the fastnesses in which he was hiding them, not unfrequently the shepherd was startled by the blare of trumpets, and, peering out, beheld a cohort, sometimes a legion, in march; and when the glittering crests were gone, and the excitement incident to the intrusion over, he bent himself to evolve the meaning of the eagles and gilded globes of the soldiery, and the charm of a life so the opposite of his own.\n\nYet these men, rude and simple as they were, had a knowledge and a wisdom of their own. On Sabbaths they were accustomed to purify themselves, and go up into the synagogues, and sit on the benches farthest from the ark. When the chazzan bore the Torah round, none kissed it with greater zest; when the sheliach read the text, none listened to the interpreter with more absolute faith; and none took away with them more of the elder’s sermon, or gave it more thought afterwards. In a verse of the Shema they found all the learning and all the law of their simple lives—that their Lord was One God, and that they must love him with all their souls. And they loved him, and such was their wisdom, surpassing that of kings.\n\nWhile they talked, and before the first watch was over, one by one the shepherds went to sleep, each lying where he had sat.\n\nThe night, like most nights of the winter season in the hill country, was clear, crisp, and sparkling with stars. There was no wind. The atmosphere seemed never so pure, and the stillness was more than silence; it was a holy hush, a warning that heaven was stooping low to whisper some good thing to the listening earth.\n\nBy the gate, hugging his mantle close, the watchman walked; at times he stopped, attracted by a stir among the sleeping herds, or by a jackal’s cry off on the mountain-side. The midnight was slow coming to him; but at last it came. His task was done; now for the dreamless sleep with which labor blesses its wearied children! He moved towards the fire, but paused; a light was breaking around him, soft and white, like the moon’s. He waited breathlessly. The light deepened; things before invisible came to view; he saw the whole field, and all it sheltered. A chill sharper than that of the frosty air—a chill of fear—smote him. He looked up; the stars were gone; the light was dropping as from a window in the sky; as he looked, it became a splendor; then, in terror, he cried,\n\n“Awake, awake!”\n\nUp sprang the dogs, and, howling, ran away.\n\nThe herds rushed together bewildered.\n\nThe men clambered to their feet, weapons in hand.\n\n“What is it?” they asked, in one voice.\n\n“See!” cried the watchman, “the sky is on fire!”\n\nSuddenly the light became intolerably bright, and they covered their eyes, and dropped upon their knees; then, as their souls shrank with fear, they fell upon their faces blind and fainting, and would have died had not a voice said to them,\n\n“Fear not!”\nAnd they listened.\n\n“Fear not: for behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people.”\n\nThe voice, in sweetness and soothing more than human, and low and clear, penetrated all their being, and filled them with assurance. They rose upon their knees, and, looking worshipfully, beheld in the centre of a great glory the appearance of a man, clad in a robe intensely white; above its shoulders towered the tops of wings shining and folded; a star over its forehead glowed with steady lustre, brilliant as Hesperus; its hands were stretched towards them in blessing; its face was serene and divinely beautiful.\n\nThey had often heard, and, in their simple way, talked, of angels; and they doubted not now, but said, in their hearts, The glory of God is about us, and this is he who of old came to the prophet by the river of Ulai.\n\nDirectly the angel continued:\n\n“For unto you is born this day, in the city of David, a Savior, which is Christ the Lord!”\n\nAgain there was a rest, while the words sank into their minds.\n\n“And this shall be a sign unto you,” the annunciator said next. “Ye shall find the babe, wrapped in swaddling-clothes, lying in a manger.”\n\nThe herald spoke not again; his good tidings were told; yet he stayed awhile. Suddenly the light, of which he seemed the centre, turned roseate and began to tremble; then up, far as the men could see, there was flashing of white wings, and coming and going of radiant forms, and voices as of a multitude chanting in unison,\n\n“Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good-will towards men!”\n\nNot once the praise, but many times.\n\nThen the herald raised his eyes as seeking approval of one far off; his wings stirred, and spread slowly and majestically, on their upper side white as snow, in the shadow vari-tinted, like mother-of-pearl; when they were expanded many cubits beyond his stature, he arose lightly, and, without effort, floated out of view, taking the light up with him. Long after he was gone, down from the sky fell the refrain in measure mellowed by distance, “Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good-will towards men.”\n\nWhen the shepherds came fully to their senses, they stared at each other stupidly, until one of them said, “It was Gabriel, the Lord’s messenger unto men.”\n\nNone answered.\n\n“Christ the Lord is born; said he not so?”\n\nThen another recovered his voice, and replied, “That is what he said.”\n\n“And did he not also say, in the city of David, which is our Bethlehem yonder. And that we should find him a babe in swaddling-clothes?”\n\n“And lying in a manger.”\n\nThe first speaker gazed into the fire thoughtfully, but at length said, like one possessed of a sudden resolve, “There is but one place in Bethlehem where there are mangers; but one, and that is in the cave near the old khan. Brethren, let us go see this thing which has come to pass. The priests and doctors have been a long time looking for the Christ. Now he is born, and the Lord has given us a sign by which to know him. Let us go up and worship him.”\n\n“But the flocks!”\n\n“The Lord will take care of them. Let us make haste.”\n\nThen they all arose and left the marah.\n\nAround the mountain and through the town they passed, and came to the gate of the khan, where there was a man on watch.\n\n“What would you have?” he asked.\n\n“We have seen and heard great things to-night,” they replied.\n\n“Well, we, too, have seen great things, but heard nothing. What did you hear?”\n\n“Let us go down to the cave in the enclosure, that we may be sure; then we will tell you all. Come with us, and see for yourself.”\n\n“It is a fool’s errand.”\n\n“No, the Christ is born.”\n\n“The Christ! How do you know?”\n\n“Let us go and see first.”\n\nThe man laughed scornfully.\n\n“The Christ indeed! How are you to know him?”\n\n“He was born this night, and is now lying in a manger, so we were told; and there is but one place in Bethlehem with mangers.”\n\n“The cave?”\n“Yes. Come with us.”\n\nThey went through the court-yard without notice, although there were some up even then talking about the wonderful light. The door of the cavern was open. A lantern was burning within, and they entered unceremoniously.\n\n“I give you peace,” the watchman said to Joseph and the Beth Dagonite. “Here are people looking for a child born this night, whom they are to know by finding him in swaddling-clothes and lying in a manger.”\n\nFor a moment the face of the stolid Nazarene was moved; turning away, he said, “The child is here.”\n\nThey were led to one of the mangers, and there the child was. The lantern was brought, and the shepherds stood by mute. The little one made no sign; it was as others just born.\n\n“Where is the mother?” asked the watchman.\n\nOne of the women took the baby, and went to Mary, lying near, and put it in her arms. Then the bystanders collected about the two.\n\n“It is the Christ!” said a shepherd, at last.\n\n“The Christ!” they all repeated, falling upon their knees in worship. One of them repeated several times over,\n\n“It is the Lord, and his glory is above the earth and heaven.”\n\nAnd the simple men, never doubting, kissed the hem of the mother’s robe, and with joyful faces departed. In the khan, to all the people aroused and pressing about them, they told their story; and through the town, and all the way back to the marah, they chanted the refrain of the angels, “Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good-will towards men!”\n\nThe story went abroad, confirmed by the light so generally seen; and the next day, and for days thereafter, the cave was visited by curious crowds, of whom some believed, though the greater part laughed and mocked.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\nThe eleventh day after the birth of the child in the cave, about mid-afternoon, the three wise men approached Jerusalem by the road from Shechem. After crossing Brook Cedron, they met many people, of whom none failed to stop and look after them curiously.\n\nJudea was of necessity an international thoroughfare; a narrow ridge, raised, apparently, by the pressure of the desert on the east, and the sea on the west, was all she could claim to be; over the ridge, however, nature had stretched the line of trade between the east and the south; and that was her wealth. In other words, the riches of Jerusalem were the tolls she levied on passing commerce. Nowhere else, consequently, unless in Rome, was there such constant assemblage of so many people of so many different nations; in no other city was a stranger less strange to the residents than within her walls and purlieus. And yet these three men excited the wonder of all whom they met on the way to the gates.\n\nA child belonging to some women sitting by the roadside opposite the Tombs of the Kings saw the party coming; immediately it clapped its hands, and cried, “Look, look! What pretty bells! What big camels!”\n\nThe bells were silver; the camels, as we have seen, were of unusual size and whiteness, and moved with singular stateliness; the trappings told of the desert and of long journeys thereon, and also of ample means in possession of the owners, who sat under the little canopies exactly as they appeared at the rendezvous beyond the Jebel. Yet it was not the bells or the camels, or their furniture, or the demeanor of the riders, that were so wonderful; it was the question put by the man who rode foremost of the three.\n\nThe approach to Jerusalem from the north is across a plain which dips southward, leaving the Damascus Gate in a vale or hollow. The road is narrow, but deeply cut by long use, and in places difficult on account of the cobbles left loose and dry by the washing of the rains. On either side, however, there stretched, in the old time, rich fields and handsome olive-groves, which must, in luxurious growth, have been beautiful, especially to travellers fresh from the wastes of the desert. In this road, the three stopped before the party in front of the Tombs.\n\n“Good people,” said Balthasar, stroking his plaited beard, and bending from his cot, “is not Jerusalem close by?”\n\n“Yes,” answered the woman into whose arms the child had shrunk. “If the trees on yon swell were a little lower you could see the towers on the market-place.”\n\nBalthasar gave the Greek and the Hindoo a look, then asked,\n\n“Where is he that is born King of the Jews?”\n\nThe women gazed at each other without reply.\n\n“You have not heard of him?”\n“No.”\n\n“Well, tell everybody that we have seen his star in the east, and are come to worship him.”\n\nThereupon the friends rode on. Of others they asked the same question, with like result. A large company whom they met going to the Grotto of Jeremiah were so astonished by the inquiry and the appearance of the travellers that they turned about and followed them into the city.\n\nSo much were the three occupied with the idea of their mission that they did not care for the view which presently rose before them in the utmost magnificence: for the village first to receive them on Bezetha; for Mizpah and Olivet, over on their left; for the wall behind the village, with its forty tall and solid towers, superadded partly for strength, partly to gratify the critical taste of the kingly builder; for the same towered wall bending off to the right, with many an angle, and here and there an embattled gate, up to the three great white piles Phasaelus, Mariamne, and Hippicus; for Zion, tallest of the hills, crowned with marble palaces, and never so beautiful; for the glittering terraces of the temple on Moriah, admittedly one of the wonders of the earth; for the regal mountains rimming the sacred city round about until it seemed in the hollow of a mighty bowl.\n\nThey came, at length, to a tower of great height and strength, overlooking the gate which, at that time, answered to the present Damascus Gate, and marked the meeting-place of the three roads from Shechem, Jericho, and Gibeon. A Roman guard kept the passage-way. By this time the people following the camels formed a train sufficient to draw the idlers hanging about the portal; so that when Balthasar stopped to speak to the sentinel, the three became instantly the centre of a close circle eager to hear all that passed.\n\n“I give you peace,” the Egyptian said, in a clear voice.\n\nThe sentinel made no reply.\n\n“We have come great distances in search of one who is born King of the Jews. Can you tell us where he is?”\n\nThe soldier raised the visor of his helmet, and called loudly. From an apartment at the right of the passage an officer appeared.\n\n“Give way,” he cried, to the crowd which now pressed closer in; and as they seemed slow to obey, he advanced twirling his javelin vigorously, now right, now left; and so he gained room.\n\n“What would you?” he asked of Balthasar, speaking in the idiom of the city.\n\nAnd Balthasar answered in the same,\n\n“Where is he that is born King of the Jews?”\n\n“Herod?” asked the officer, confounded.\n\n“Herod’s kingship is from Cæsar; not Herod.”\n\n“There is no other King of the Jews.”\n\n“But we have seen the star of him we seek, and come to worship him.”\n\nThe Roman was perplexed.\n\n“Go farther,” he said, at last. “Go farther. I am not a Jew. Carry the question to the doctors in the Temple, or to Hannas the priest, or, better still, to Herod himself. If there be another King of the Jews, he will find him.”\n\nThereupon he made way for the strangers, and they passed the gate. But, before entering the narrow street, Balthasar lingered to say to his friends, “We are sufficiently proclaimed. By midnight the whole city will have heard of us and of our mission. Let us to the khan now.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\nThat evening, before sunset, some women were washing clothes on the upper step of the flight that led down into the basin of the Pool of Siloam. They knelt each before a broad bowl of earthenware. A girl at the foot of the steps kept them supplied with water, and sang while she filled the jar. The song was cheerful, and no doubt lightened their labor. Occasionally they would sit upon their heels, and look up the slope of Ophel, and round to the summit of what is now the Mount of Offence, then faintly glorified by the dying sun.\n\nWhile they plied their hands, rubbing and wringing the clothes in the bowls, two other women came to them, each with an empty jar upon her shoulder.\n\n“Peace to you,” one of the new-comers said.\n\nThe laborers paused, sat up, wrung the water from their hands, and returned the salutation.\n\n“It is nearly night—time to quit.”\n\n“There is no end to work,” was the reply.\n\n“But there is a time to rest, and—”\n\n“To hear what may be passing,” interposed another.\n\n“What news have you?”\n\n“Then you have not heard?”\n\n“No.”\n“They say the Christ is born,” said the newsmonger, plunging into her story.\n\nIt was curious to see the faces of the laborers brighten with interest; on the other side down came the jars, which, in a moment, were turned into seats for their owners.\n\n“The Christ!” the listeners cried.\n\n“So they say.”\n\n“Who?”\n\n“Everybody; it is common talk.”\n\n“Does anybody believe it?”\n\n“This afternoon three men came across Brook Cedron on the road from Shechem,” the speaker replied, circumstantially, intending to smother doubt. “Each one of them rode a camel spotless white, and larger than any ever before seen in Jerusalem.”\n\nThe eyes and mouths of the auditors opened wide.\n\n“To prove how great and rich the men were,” the narrator continued, “they sat under awnings of silk; the buckles of their saddles were of gold, as was the fringe of their bridles; the bells were of silver, and made real music. Nobody knew them; they looked as if they had come from the ends of the world. Only one of them spoke, and of everybody on the road, even the women and children, he asked this question—‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’ No one gave them answer—no one understood what they meant; so they passed on, leaving behind them this saying: ‘For we have seen his star in the east, and are come to worship him.’ They put the question to the Roman at the gate; and he, no wiser than the simple people on the road, sent them up to Herod.”\n\n“Where are they now?”\n\n“At the khan. Hundreds have been to look at them already, and hundreds more are going.”\n\n“Who are they?”\n\n“Nobody knows. They are said to be Persians—wise men who talk with the stars—prophets, it may be, like Elijah and Jeremiah.”\n\n“What do they mean by King of the Jews?”\n\n“The Christ, and that he is just born.”\n\nOne of the women laughed, and resumed her work, saying, “Well, when I see him I will believe.”\n\nAnother followed her example: “And I—well, when I see him raise the dead, I will believe.”\n\nA third said, quietly, “He has been a long time promised. It will be enough for me to see him heal one leper.”\n\nAnd the party sat talking until the night came, and, with the help of the frosty air, drove them home.\n\nLater in the evening, about the beginning of the first watch, there was an assemblage in the palace on Mount Zion, of probably fifty persons, who never came together except by order of Herod, and then only when he had demanded to know some one or more of the deeper mysteries of the Jewish law and history. It was, in short, a meeting of the teachers of the colleges, of the chief priests, and of the doctors most noted in the city for learning—the leaders of opinion, expounders of the different creeds; princes of the Sadducees; Pharisaic debaters; calm, soft-spoken, stoical philosophers of the Essene socialists.\n\nThe chamber in which the session was held belonged to one of the interior court-yards of the palace, and was quite large and Romanesque. The floor was tessellated with marble blocks; the walls, unbroken by a window, were frescoed in panels of saffron yellow; a divan occupied the centre of the apartment, covered with cushions of bright-yellow cloth, and fashioned in form of the letter U, the opening towards the doorway; in the arch of the divan, or, as it were, in the bend of the letter, there was an immense bronze tripod, curiously inlaid with gold and silver, over which a chandelier dropped from the ceiling, having seven arms, each holding a lighted lamp. The divan and the lamp were purely Jewish.\n\nThe company sat upon the divan after the style of Orientals, in costume singularly uniform, except as to color. They were mostly men advanced in years; immense beards covered their faces; to their large noses were added the effects of large black eyes, deeply shaded by bold brows; their demeanor was grave, dignified, even patriarchal. In brief, their session was that of the Sanhedrim.\n\nHe who sat before the tripod, however, in the place which may be called the head of the divan, having all the rest of his associates on his right and left, and, at the same time, before him, evidently president of the meeting, would have instantly absorbed the attention of a spectator. He had been cast in large mould, but was now shrunken and stooped to ghastliness; his white robe dropped from his shoulders in folds that gave no hint of muscle or anything but an angular skeleton. His hands, half concealed by sleeves of silk, white and crimson striped, were clasped upon his knees. When he spoke, sometimes the first finger of the right hand extended tremulously; he seemed incapable of other gesture. But his head was a splendid dome. A few hairs, whiter than fine-drawn silver, fringed the base; over a broad, full-sphered skull the skin was drawn close, and shone in the light with positive brilliance; the temples were deep hollows, from which the forehead beetled like a wrinkled crag; the eyes were wan and dim; the nose was pinched; and all the lower face was muffed in a beard flowing and venerable as Aaron’s. Such was Hillel the Babylonian! The line of prophets, long extinct in Israel, was now succeeded by a line of scholars, of whom he was first in learning—a prophet in all but the divine inspiration! At the age of one hundred and six, he was still Rector of the Great College.\n\nOn the table before him lay outspread a roll or volume of parchment inscribed with Hebrew characters; behind him, in waiting, stood a page richly habited.\n\nThere had been discussion, but at this moment of introduction the company had reached a conclusion; each one was in an attitude of rest, and the venerable Hillel, without moving, called the page.\n“Hist!”\n\nThe youth advanced respectfully.\n\n“Go tell the king we are ready to give him answer.”\n\nThe boy hurried away.\n\nAfter a time two officers entered and stopped, one on each side the door; after them slowly followed a most striking personage—an old man clad in a purple robe bordered with scarlet, and girt to his waist by a band of gold linked so fine that it was pliable as leather; the latchets of his shoes sparkled with precious stones; a narrow crown wrought in filigree shone outside a tarbooshe of softest crimson plush, which, encasing his head, fell down the neck and shoulders, leaving the throat and neck exposed. Instead of a seal, a dagger dangled from his belt. He walked with a halting step, leaning heavily upon a staff. Not until he reached the opening of the divan, did he pause or look up from the floor; then, as for the first time conscious of the company, and roused by their presence, he raised himself, and looked haughtily round, like one startled and searching for an enemy—so dark, suspicious, and threatening was the glance. Such was Herod the Great—a body broken by diseases, a conscience seared with crimes, a mind magnificently capable, a soul fit for brotherhood with the Cæsars; now seven-and-sixty years old, but guarding his throne with a jealousy never so vigilant, a power never so despotic, and a cruelty never so inexorable.\n\nThere was a general movement on the part of the assemblage—a bending forward in salaam by the more aged, a rising-up by the more courtierly, followed by low genuflections, hands upon the beard or breast.\n\nHis observations taken, Herod moved on until at the tripod opposite the venerable Hillel, who met his cold glance with an inclination of the head, and a slight lifting of the hands.\n\n“The answer!” said the king, with imperious simplicity, addressing Hillel, and planting his staff before him with both hands. “The answer!”\n\nThe eyes of the patriarch glowed mildly, and, raising his head, and looking the inquisitor full in the face, he answered, his associates giving him closest attention,\n\n“With thee, O king, be the peace of God, of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob!”\n\nHis manner was that of invocation; changing it, he resumed:\n\n“Thou hast demanded of us where the Christ should be born.”\n\nThe king bowed, though the evil eyes remained fixed upon the sage’s face.\n\n“That is the question.”\n\n“Then, O king, speaking for myself, and all my brethren here, not one dissenting, I say, in Bethlehem of Judea.”\n\nHillel glanced at the parchment on the tripod; and, pointing with his tremulous finger, continued, “In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet, ‘And thou, Bethlehem, in the land of Judea, art not the least among the princes of Judah; for out of thee shall come a governor that shall rule my people Israel.’”\n\nHerod’s face was troubled, and his eyes fell upon the parchment while he thought. Those beholding him scarcely breathed; they spoke not, nor did he. At length he turned about and left the chamber.\n\n“Brethren,” said Hillel, “we are dismissed.”\n\nThe company then arose, and in groups departed.\n\n“Simeon,” said Hillel again.\n\nA man, quite fifty years old, but in the hearty prime of life, answered and came to him.\n\n“Take up the sacred parchment, my son; roll it tenderly.”\n\nThe order was obeyed.\n\n“Now lend me thy arm; I will to the litter.”\n\nThe strong man stooped; with his withered hands the old one took the offered support, and, rising, moved feebly to the door.\n\nSo departed the famous Rector, and Simeon, his son, who was to be his successor in wisdom, learning, and office.\n\nYet later in the evening the wise men were lying in a lewen of the khan awake. The stones which served them as pillows raised their heads so they could look out of the open arch into the depths of the sky; and as they watched the twinkling of the stars, they thought of the next manifestation. How would it come? What would it be? They were in Jerusalem at last; they had asked at the gate for Him they sought; they had borne witness of his birth; it remained only to find him; and as to that, they placed all trust in the Spirit. Men listening for the voice of God, or waiting a sign from Heaven, cannot sleep.\n\nWhile they were in this condition, a man stepped in under the arch, darkening the lewen.\n\n“Awake!” he said to them; “I bring you a message which will not be put off.”\n\nThey all sat up.\n\n“From whom?” asked the Egyptian.\n\n“Herod the king.”\n\nEach one felt his spirit thrill.\n\n“Are you not the steward of the khan?” Balthasar asked next.\n\n“I am.”\n\n“What would the king with us?”\n\n“His messenger is without; let him answer.”\n\n“Tell him, then, to abide our coming.”\n\n“You were right, O my brother!” said the Greek, when the steward was gone. “The question put to the people on the road, and to the guard at the gate, has given us quick notoriety. I am impatient; let us up quickly.”\n\nThey arose, put on their sandals, girt their mantles about them, and went out.\n\n“I salute you, and give you peace, and pray your pardon; but my master, the king, has sent me to invite you to the palace, where he would have speech with you privately.”\n\nThus the messenger discharged his duty.\n\nA lamp hung in the entrance, and by its light they looked at each other, and knew the Spirit was upon them. Then the Egyptian stepped to the steward, and said, so as not to be heard by the others, “You know where our goods are stored in the court, and where our camels are resting. While we are gone, make all things ready for our departure, if it should be needful.”\n\n“Go your way assured; trust me,” the steward replied.\n\n“The king’s will is our will,” said Balthasar to the messenger. “We will follow you.”\n\nThe streets of the Holy City were narrow then as now, but not so rough and foul; for the great builder, not content with beauty, enforced cleanliness and convenience also. Following their guide, the brethren proceeded without a word. Through the dim starlight, made dimmer by the walls on both sides, sometimes almost lost under bridges connecting the house-tops, out of a low ground they ascended a hill. At last they came to a portal reared across the way. In the light of fires blazing before it in two great braziers, they caught a glimpse of the structure, and also of some guards leaning motionlessly upon their arms. They passed into a building unchallenged. Then by passages and arched halls; through courts, and under colonnades not always lighted; up long flights of stairs, past innumerable cloisters and chambers, they were conducted into a tower of great height. Suddenly the guide halted, and, pointing through an open door, said to them,\n\n“Enter. The king is there.”\n\nThe air of the chamber was heavy with the perfume of sandal-wood, and all the appointments within were effeminately rich. Upon the floor, covering the central space, a tufted rug was spread, and upon that a throne was set. The visitors had but time, however, to catch a confused idea of the place—of carved and gilt ottomans and couches; of fans and jars and musical instruments; of golden candlesticks glittering in their own lights; of walls painted in the style of the voluptuous Grecian school, one look at which had made a Pharisee hide his head with holy horror. Herod, sitting upon the throne to receive them, clad as when at the conference with the doctors and lawyers, claimed all their minds.\n\nAt the edge of the rug, to which they advanced uninvited, they prostrated themselves. The king touched a bell. An attendant came in, and placed three stools before the throne.\n\n“Seat yourselves,” said the monarch, graciously.\n\n“From the North Gate,” he continued, when they were at rest, “I had this afternoon report of the arrival of three strangers, curiously mounted, and appearing as if from far countries. Are you the men?”\n\nThe Egyptian took the sign from the Greek and the Hindoo, and answered, with the profoundest salaam, “Were we other than we are, the mighty Herod, whose fame is as incense to the whole world, would not have sent for us. We may not doubt that we are the strangers.”\n\nHerod acknowledged the speech with a wave of the hand.\n\n“Who are you? Whence do you come?” he asked, adding significantly, “Let each speak for himself.”\n\nIn turn they gave him account, referring simply to the cities and lands of their birth, and the routes by which they came to Jerusalem. Somewhat disappointed, Herod plied them more directly.\n\n“What was the question you put to the officer at the gate?”\n\n“We asked him, Where is he that is born King of the Jews.”\n\n“I see now why the people were so curious. You excite me no less. Is there another King of the Jews?”\n\nThe Egyptian did not blanch.\n\n“There is one newly born.”\n\nAn expression of pain knit the dark face of the monarch, as if his mind were swept by a harrowing recollection.\n\n“Not to me, not to me!” he exclaimed.\n\nPossibly the accusing images of his murdered children flitted before him; recovering from the emotion, whatever it was, he asked, steadily, “Where is the new king?”\n\n“That, O king, is what we would ask.”\n\n“You bring me a wonder—a riddle surpassing any of Solomon’s,” the inquisitor said next. “As you see, I am in the time of life when curiosity is as ungovernable as it was in childhood, when to trifle with it is cruelty. Tell me further, and I will honor you as kings honor each other. Give me all you know about the newly born, and I will join you in the search for him; and when we have found him, I will do what you wish; I will bring him to Jerusalem, and train him in kingcraft; I will use my grace with Cæsar for his promotion and glory. Jealousy shall not come between us, so I swear. But tell me first how, so widely separated by seas and deserts, you all came to hear of him.”\n\n“I will tell you truly, O king.”\n\n“Speak on,” said Herod.\n\nBalthasar raised himself erect, and said, solemnly,\n\n“There is an Almighty God.”\n\nHerod was visibly startled.\n\n“He bade us come hither, promising that we should find the Redeemer of the World; that we should see and worship him, and bear witness that he was come; and, as a sign, we were each given to see a star. His Spirit stayed with us. O king, his Spirit is with us now!”\n\nAn overpowering feeling seized the three. The Greek with difficulty restrained an outcry. Herod’s gaze darted quickly from one to the other; he was more suspicious and dissatisfied than before.\n\n“You are mocking me,” he said. “If not, tell me more. What is to follow the coming of the new king?”\n\n“The salvation of men.”\n\n“From what?”\n\n“Their wickedness.”\n“How?”\n\n“By the divine agencies—Faith, Love, and Good Works.”\n\n“Then”—Herod paused, and from his look no man could have said with what feeling he continued—“you are the heralds of the Christ. Is that all?”\nBalthasar bowed low.\n\n“We are your servants, O king.”\n\nThe monarch touched a bell, and the attendant appeared.\n\n“Bring the gifts,” the master said.\n\nThe attendant went out, but in a little while returned, and, kneeling before the guests, gave to each one an outer robe or mantle of scarlet and blue, and a girdle of gold. They acknowledged the honors with Eastern prostrations.\n\n“A word further,” said Herod, when the ceremony was ended. “To the officer of the gate, and but now to me, you spoke of seeing a star in the east.”\n\n“Yes,” said Balthasar, “his star, the star of the newly born.”\n\n“What time did it appear?”\n\n“When we were bidden come hither.”\n\nHerod arose, signifying the audience was over. Stepping from the throne towards them, he said, with all graciousness,\n\n“If, as I believe, O illustrious men, you are indeed the heralds of the Christ just born, know that I have this night consulted those wisest in things Jewish, and they say with one voice he should be born in Bethlehem of Judea. I say to you, go thither; go and search diligently for the young child; and when you have found him bring me word again, that I may come and worship him. To your going there shall be no let or hindrance. Peace be with you!”\n\nAnd, folding his robe about him, he left the chamber.\n\nDirectly the guide came, and led them back to the street, and thence to the khan, at the portal of which the Greek said, impulsively, “Let us to Bethlehem, O brethren, as the king has advised.”\n\n“Yes,” cried the Hindoo. “The Spirit burns within me.”\n\n“Be it so,” said Balthasar, with equal warmth. “The camels are ready.”\n\nThey gave gifts to the steward, mounted into their saddles, received directions to the Joppa Gate, and departed. At their approach the great valves were unbarred, and they passed out into the open country, taking the road so lately travelled by Joseph and Mary. As they came up out of Hinnom, on the plain of Rephaim, a light appeared, at first wide-spread and faint. Their pulses fluttered fast. The light intensified rapidly; they closed their eyes against its burning brilliance: when they dared look again, lo! the star, perfect as any in the heavens, but low down and moving slowly before them. And they folded their hands, and shouted, and rejoiced with exceeding great joy.\n\n“God is with us! God is with us!” they repeated, in frequent cheer, all the way, until the star, rising out of the valley beyond Mar Elias, stood still over a house up on the slope of the hill near the town.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\nIt was now the beginning of the third watch, and at Bethlehem the morning was breaking over the mountains in the east, but so feebly that it was yet night in the valley. The watchman on the roof of the old khan, shivering in the chilly air, was listening for the first distinguishable sounds with which life, awakening, greets the dawn, when a light came moving up the hill towards the house. He thought it a torch in some one’s hand; next moment he thought it a meteor; the brilliance grew, however, until it became a star. Sore afraid, he cried out, and brought everybody within the walls to the roof. The phenomenon, in eccentric motion, continued to approach; the rocks, trees, and roadway under it shone as in a glare of lightning; directly its brightness became blinding. The more timid of the beholders fell upon their knees, and prayed, with their faces hidden; the boldest, covering their eyes, crouched, and now and then snatched glances fearfully. Afterwhile the khan and everything thereabout lay under the intolerable radiance. Such as dared look beheld the star standing still directly over the house in front of the cave where the Child had been born.\n\nIn the height of this scene, the wise men came up, and at the gate dismounted from their camels, and shouted for admission. When the steward so far mastered his terror as to give them heed, he drew the bars and opened to them. The camels looked spectral in the unnatural light, and, besides their outlandishness, there were in the faces and manner of the three visitors an eagerness and exaltation which still further excited the keeper’s fears and fancy; he fell back, and for a time could not answer the question they put to him.\n\n“Is not this Bethlehem of Judea?”\n\nBut others came, and by their presence gave him assurance.\n\n“No, this is but the khan; the town lies farther on.”\n\n“Is there not here a child newly born?”\n\nThe bystanders turned to each other marvelling, though some of them answered, “Yes, yes.”\n\n“Show us to him!” said the Greek, impatiently.\n\n“Show us to him!” cried Balthasar, breaking through his gravity; “for we have seen his star, even that which ye behold over the house, and are come to worship him.”\n\nThe Hindoo clasped his hands, exclaiming, “God indeed lives! Make haste, make haste! The Savior is found. Blessed, blessed are we above men!”\n\nThe people from the roof came down and followed the strangers as they were taken through the court and out into the enclosure; at sight of the star yet above the cave, though less candescent than before, some turned back afraid; the greater part went on. As the strangers neared the house, the orb arose; when they were at the door, it was high up overhead vanishing; when they entered, it went out lost to sight. And to the witnesses of what then took place came a conviction that there was a divine relation between the star and the strangers, which extended also to at least some of the occupants of the cave. When the door was opened, they crowded in.\n\nThe apartment was lighted by a lantern enough to enable the strangers to find the mother, and the child awake in her lap.\n\n“Is the child thine?” asked Balthasar of Mary.\n\nAnd she who had kept all the things in the least affecting the little one, and pondered them in her heart, held it up in the light, saying,\n\n“He is my son!”\n\nAnd they fell down and worshipped him.\n\nThey saw the child was as other children: about its head was neither nimbus nor material crown; its lips opened not in speech; if it heard their expressions of joy, their invocations, their prayers, it made no sign whatever, but, baby-like, looked longer at the flame in the lantern than at them.\n\nIn a little while they arose, and, returning to the camels, brought gifts of gold, frankincense, and myrrh, and laid them before the child, abating nothing of their worshipful speeches; of which no part is given, for the thoughtful know that the pure worship of the pure heart was then what it is now, and has always been, an inspired song.\n\nAnd this was the Savior they had come so far to find!\n\nYet they worshipped without a doubt.\nWhy?\n\nTheir faith rested upon the signs sent them by him whom we have since come to know as the Father; and they were of the kind to whom his promises were so all-sufficient that they asked nothing about his ways. Few there were who had seen the signs and heard the promises—the Mother and Joseph, the shepherds, and the Three—yet they all believed alike; that is to say, in this period of the plan of salvation, God was all and the Child nothing. But look forward, O reader! A time will come when the signs will all proceed from the Son. Happy they who then believe in him!\n\nLet us wait that period.\n\n# BOOK SECOND\n\n“There is a fire And motion of the soul which will not dwell In its own narrow being, but aspire Beyond the fitting medium of desire; And, but once kindled, quenchless evermore, Preys upon high adventure, nor can tire Of aught but rest.”\nChilde Harold. CHAPTER I\n\nIt is necessary now to carry the reader forward twenty-one years, to the beginning of the administration of Valerius Gratus, the fourth imperial governor of Judea—a period which will be remembered as rent by political agitations in Jerusalem, if, indeed, it be not the precise time of the opening of the final quarrel between the Jew and the Roman.\nIn the interval Judea had been subjected to changes affecting her in many ways, but in nothing so much as her political status. Herod the Great died within one year after the birth of the Child—died so miserably that the Christian world had reason to believe him overtaken by the Divine wrath. Like all great rulers who spend their lives in perfecting the power they create, he dreamed of transmitting his throne and crown—of being the founder of a dynasty. With that intent, he left a will dividing his territories between his three sons, Antipas, Philip, and Archelaus, of whom the last was appointed to succeed to the title. The testament was necessarily referred to Augustus, the emperor, who ratified all its provisions with one exception: he withheld from Archelaus the title of king until he proved his capacity and loyalty; in lieu thereof, he created him ethnarch, and as such permitted him to govern nine years, when, for misconduct and inability to stay the turbulent elements that grew and strengthened around him, he was sent into Gaul as an exile.\n\nCæsar was not content with deposing Archelaus; he struck the people of Jerusalem in a manner that touched their pride, and keenly wounded the sensibilities of the haughty habitues of the Temple. He reduced Judea to a Roman province, and annexed it to the prefecture of Syria. So, instead of a king ruling royally from the palace left by Herod on Mount Zion, the city fell into the hands of an officer of the second grade, an appointee called procurator, who communicated with the court in Rome through the Legate of Syria, residing in Antioch. To make the hurt more painful, the procurator was not permitted to establish himself in Jerusalem; Cæsarea was his seat of government. Most humiliating, however, most exasperating, most studied, Samaria, of all the world the most despised—Samaria was joined to Judea as a part of the same province! What ineffable misery the bigoted Separatists or Pharisees endured at finding themselves elbowed and laughed at in the procurator’s presence in Cæsarea by the devotees of Gerizim!\n\nIn this rain of sorrows, one consolation, and one only, remained to the fallen people: the high-priest occupied the Herodian palace in the market-place, and kept the semblance of a court there. What his authority really was is a matter of easy estimate. Judgment of life and death was retained by the procurator. Justice was administered in the name and according to the decretals of Rome. Yet more significant, the royal house was jointly occupied by the imperial exciseman, and all his corps of assistants, registrars, collectors, publicans, informers, and spies. Still, to the dreamers of liberty to come, there was a certain satisfaction in the fact that the chief ruler in the palace was a Jew. His mere presence there day after day kept them reminded of the covenants and promises of the prophets, and the ages when Jehovah governed the tribes through the sons of Aaron; it was to them a certain sign that he had not abandoned them: so their hopes lived, and served their patience, and helped them wait grimly the son of Judah who was to rule Israel.\n\nJudea had been a Roman province eighty years and more—ample time for the Cæsars to study the idiosyncrasies of the people—time enough, at least, to learn that the Jew, with all his pride, could be quietly governed if his religion were respected. Proceeding upon that policy, the predecessors of Gratus had carefully abstained from interfering with any of the sacred observances of their subjects. But he chose a different course: almost his first official act was to expel Hannas from the high-priesthood, and give the place to Ishmael, son of Fabus.\n\nWhether the act was directed by Augustus, or proceeded from Gratus himself, its impolicy became speedily apparent. The reader shall be spared a chapter on Jewish politics; a few words upon the subject, however, are essential to such as may follow the succeeding narration critically. At this time, leaving origin out of view, there were in Judea the party of the nobles and the Separatist or popular party. Upon Herod’s death, the two united against Archelaus; from temple to palace, from Jerusalem to Rome, they fought him; sometimes with intrigue, sometimes with the actual weapons of war. More than once the holy cloisters on Moriah resounded with the cries of fighting-men. Finally, they drove him into exile. Meantime throughout this struggle the allies had their diverse objects in view. The nobles hated Joazar, the high-priest; the Separatists, on the other hand, were his zealous adherents. When Herod’s settlement went down with Archelaus, Joazar shared the fall. Hannas, the son of Seth, was selected by the nobles to fill the great office; thereupon the allies divided. The induction of the Sethian brought them face to face in fierce hostility.\n\nIn the course of the struggle with the unfortunate ethnarch, the nobles had found it expedient to attach themselves to Rome. Discerning that when the existing settlement was broken up some form of government must needs follow, they suggested the conversion of Judea into a province. The fact furnished the Separatists an additional cause for attack; and, when Samaria was made part of the province, the nobles sank into a minority, with nothing to support them but the imperial court and the prestige of their rank and wealth; yet for fifteen years—down, indeed, to the coming of Valerius Gratus—they managed to maintain themselves in both palace and Temple.\n\nHannas, the idol of his party, had used his power faithfully in the interest of his imperial patron. A Roman garrison held the Tower of Antonia; a Roman guard kept the gates of the palace; a Roman judge dispensed justice civil and criminal; a Roman system of taxation, mercilessly executed, crushed both city and country; daily, hourly, and in a thousand ways, the people were bruised and galled, and taught the difference between a life of independence and a life of subjection; yet Hannas kept them in comparative quiet. Rome had no truer friend; and he made his loss instantly felt. Delivering his vestments to Ishmael, the new appointee, he walked from the courts of the Temple into the councils of the Separatists, and became the head of a new combination, Bethusian and Sethian.\n\nGratus, the procurator, left thus without a party, saw the fires which, in the fifteen years, had sunk into sodden smoke begin to glow with returning life. A month after Ishmael took the office, the Roman found it necessary to visit him in Jerusalem. When from the walls, hooting and hissing him, the Jews beheld his guard enter the north gate of the city and march to the Tower of Antonia, they understood the real purpose of the visit—a full cohort of legionaries was added to the former garrison, and the keys of their yoke could now be tightened with impunity. If the procurator deemed it important to make an example, alas for the first offender!\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nWith the foregoing explanation in mind, the reader is invited to look into one of the gardens of the palace on Mount Zion. The time was noonday in the middle of July, when the heat of summer was at its highest.\n\nThe garden was bounded on every side by buildings, which in places arose two stories, with verandas shading the doors and windows of the lower story, while retreating galleries, guarded by strong balustrades, adorned and protected the upper. Here and there, moreover, the structures fell into what appeared low colonnades, permitting the passage of such winds as chanced to blow, and allowing other parts of the house to be seen, the better to realize its magnitude and beauty. The arrangement of the ground was equally pleasant to the eye. There were walks, and patches of grass and shrubbery, and a few large trees, rare specimens of the palm, grouped with the carob, apricot, and walnut. In all directions the grade sloped gently from the centre, where there was a reservoir, or deep marble basin, broken at intervals by little gates which, when raised, emptied the water into sluices bordering the walks—a cunning device for the rescue of the place from the aridity too prevalent elsewhere in the region.\n\nNot far from the fountain, there was a small pool of clear water nourishing a clump of cane and oleander, such as grow on the Jordan and down by the Dead Sea. Between the clump and the pool, unmindful of the sun shining full upon them in the breathless air, two boys, one about nineteen, the other seventeen, sat engaged in earnest conversation.\n\nThey were both handsome, and, at first glance, would have been pronounced brothers. Both had hair and eyes black; their faces were deeply browned; and, sitting, they seemed of a size proper for the difference in their ages.\n\nThe elder was bareheaded. A loose tunic, dropping to the knees, was his attire complete, except sandals and a light-blue mantle spread under him on the seat. The costume left his arms and legs exposed, and they were brown as the face; nevertheless, a certain grace of manner, refinement of features, and culture of voice decided his rank. The tunic, of softest woollen, gray-tinted, at the neck, sleeves, and edge of the skirt bordered with red, and bound to the waist by a tasselled silken cord, certified him the Roman he was. And if in speech he now and then gazed haughtily at his companion and addressed him as an inferior, he might almost be excused, for he was of a family noble even in Rome—a circumstance which in that age justified any assumption. In the terrible wars between the first Cæsar and his great enemies, a Messala had been the friend of Brutus. After Philippi, without sacrifice of his honor, he and the conqueror became reconciled. Yet later, when Octavius disputed for the empire, Messala supported him. Octavius, as the Emperor Augustus, remembered the service, and showered the family with honors. Among other things, Judea being reduced to a province, he sent the son of his old client or retainer to Jerusalem, charged with the receipt and management of the taxes levied in that region; and in that service the son had since remained, sharing the palace with the high-priest. The youth just described was his son, whose habit it was to carry about with him all too faithfully a remembrance of the relation between his grandfather and the great Romans of his day.\n\nThe associate of the Messala was slighter in form, and his garments were of fine white linen and of the prevalent style in Jerusalem; a cloth covered his head, held by a yellow cord, and arranged so as to fall away from the forehead down low over the back of the neck. An observer skilled in the distinctions of race, and studying his features more than his costume, would have soon discovered him to be of Jewish descent. The forehead of the Roman was high and narrow, his nose sharp and aquiline, while his lips were thin and straight, and his eyes cold and close under the brows. The front of the Israelite, on the other hand, was low and broad; his nose long, with expanded nostrils; his upper lip, slightly shading the lower one, short and curving to the dimpled corners, like a Cupid’s bow; points which, in connection with the round chin, full eyes, and oval cheeks reddened with a wine-like glow, gave his face the softness, strength, and beauty peculiar to his race. The comeliness of the Roman was severe and chaste, that of the Jew rich and voluptuous.\n\n“Did you not say the new procurator is to arrive to-morrow?”\n\nThe question proceeded from the younger of the friends, and was couched in Greek, at the time, singularly enough, the language everywhere prevalent in the politer circles of Judea; having passed from the palace into the camp and college; thence, nobody knew exactly when or how, into the Temple itself, and, for that matter, into precincts of the Temple far beyond the gates and cloisters—precincts of a sanctity intolerable for a Gentile.\n\n“Yes, to-morrow,” Messala answered.\n\n“Who told you?”\n\n“I heard Ishmael, the new governor in the palace—you call him high priest—tell my father so last night. The news had been more credible, I grant you, coming from an Egyptian, who is of a race that has forgotten what truth is, or even from an Idumaean, whose people never knew what truth was; but, to make quite certain, I saw a centurion from the Tower this morning, and he told me preparations were going on for the reception; that the armorers were furbishing the helmets and shields, and regilding the eagles and globes; and that apartments long unused were being cleansed and aired as if for an addition to the garrison—the body-guard, probably, of the great man.”\n\nA perfect idea of the manner in which the answer was given cannot be conveyed, as its fine points continually escape the power behind the pen. The reader’s fancy must come to his aid; and for that he must be reminded that reverence as a quality of the Roman mind was fast breaking down, or, rather, it was becoming unfashionable. The old religion had nearly ceased to be a faith; at most it was a mere habit of thought and expression, cherished principally by the priests who found service in the Temple profitable, and the poets who, in the turn of their verses, could not dispense with the familiar deities: there are singers of this age who are similarly given. As philosophy was taking the place of religion, satire was fast substituting reverence; insomuch that in Latin opinion it was to every speech, even to the little diatribes of conversation, as salt to viands, and aroma to wine. The young Messala, educated in Rome, but lately returned, had caught the habit and manner; the scarce perceptible movement of the outer corner of the lower eyelid, the decided curl of the corresponding nostril, and a languid utterance affected as the best vehicle to convey the idea of general indifference, but more particularly because of the opportunities it afforded for certain rhetorical pauses thought to be of prime importance to enable the listener to take the happy conceit or receive the virus of the stinging epigram. Such a stop occurred in the answer just given, at the end of the allusion to the Egyptian and Idumaean. The color in the Jewish lad’s cheeks deepened, and he may not have heard the rest of the speech, for he remained silent, looking absently into the depths of the pool.\n\n“Our farewell took place in this garden. ‘The peace of the Lord go with you!’—your last words. ‘The gods keep you!’ I said. Do you remember? How many years have passed since then?”\n\n“Five,” answered the Jew, gazing into the water.\n\n“Well, you have reason to be thankful to—whom shall I say? The gods? No matter. You have grown handsome; the Greeks would call you beautiful—happy achievement of the years! If Jupiter would stay content with one Ganymede, what a cup-bearer you would make for the emperor! Tell me, my Judah, how the coming of the procurator is of such interest to you.”\n\nJudah bent his large eyes upon the questioner; the gaze was grave and thoughtful, and caught the Roman’s, and held it while he replied, “Yes, five years. I remember the parting; you went to Rome; I saw you start, and cried, for I love you. The years are gone, and you have come back to me accomplished and princely—I do not jest; and yet—yet—I do wish you were the Messala you went away.”\n\nThe fine nostril of the satirist stirred, and he put on a longer drawl as he said, “No, no; not a Ganymede—an oracle, my Judah. A few lessons from my teacher of rhetoric hard by the Forum—I will give you a letter to him when you become wise enough to accept a suggestion which I am reminded to make you—a little practise of the art of mystery, and Delphi will receive you as Apollo himself. At the sound of your solemn voice, the Pythia will come down to you with her crown. Seriously, O my friend, in what am I not the Messala I went away? I once heard the greatest logician in the world. His subject was Disputation. One saying I remember—‘Understand your antagonist before you answer him.’ Let me understand you.”\n\nThe lad reddened under the cynical look to which he was subjected; yet he replied, firmly, “You have availed yourself, I see, of your opportunities; from your teachers you have brought away much knowledge and many graces. You talk with the ease of a master, yet your speech carries a sting. My Messala, when he went away, had no poison in his nature; not for the world would he have hurt the feelings of a friend.”\n\nThe Roman smiled as if complimented, and raised his patrician head a toss higher.\n\n“O my solemn Judah, we are not at Dodona or Pytho. Drop the oracular, and be plain. Wherein have I hurt you?”\n\nThe other drew a long breath, and said, pulling at the cord about his waist, “In the five years, I, too, have learned somewhat. Hillel may not be the equal of the logician you heard, and Simeon and Shammai are, no doubt, inferior to your master hard by the Forum. Their learning goes not out into forbidden paths; those who sit at their feet arise enriched simply with knowledge of God, the law, and Israel; and the effect is love and reverence for everything that pertains to them. Attendance at the Great College, and study of what I heard there, have taught me that Judea is not as she used to be. I know the space that lies between an independent kingdom and the petty province Judea is. I were meaner, viler, than a Samaritan not to resent the degradation of my country. Ishmael is not lawfully high-priest, and he cannot be while the noble Hannas lives; yet he is a Levite; one of the devoted who for thousands of years have acceptably served the Lord God of our faith and worship. His—”\n\nMessala broke in upon him with a biting laugh.\n\n“Oh, I understand you now. Ishmael, you say, is a usurper, yet to believe an Idumaean sooner than Ishmael is to sting like an adder. By the drunken son of Semele, what it is to be a Jew! All men and things, even heaven and earth, change; but a Jew never. To him there is no backward, no forward; he is what his ancestor was in the beginning. In this sand I draw you a circle—there! Now tell me what more a Jew’s life is? Round and round, Abraham here, Isaac and Jacob yonder, God in the middle. And the circle—by the master of all thunders! the circle is too large. I draw it again—” He stopped, put his thumb upon the ground, and swept the fingers about it. “See, the thumb spot is the Temple, the finger-lines Judea. Outside the little space is there nothing of value? The arts! Herod was a builder; therefore he is accursed. Painting, sculpture! to look upon them is sin. Poetry you make fast to your altars. Except in the synagogue, who of you attempts eloquence? In war all you conquer in the six days you lose on the seventh. Such your life and limit; who shall say no if I laugh at you? Satisfied with the worship of such a people, what is your God to our Roman Jove, who lends us his eagles that we may compass the universe with our arms? Hillel, Simeon, Shammai, Abtalion—what are they to the masters who teach that everything is worth knowing that can be known?”\n\nThe Jew arose, his face much flushed.\n\n“No, no; keep your place, my Judah, keep your place,” Messala cried, extending his hand.\n\n“You mock me.”\n\n“Listen a little further. Directly”—the Roman smiled derisively—“directly Jupiter and his whole family, Greek and Latin, will come to me, as is their habit, and make an end of serious speech. I am mindful of your goodness in walking from the old house of your fathers to welcome me back and renew the love of our childhood—if we can. ‘Go,’ said my teacher, in his last lecture—‘Go, and, to make your lives great, remember Mars reigns and Eros has found his eyes.’ He meant love is nothing, war everything. It is so in Rome. Marriage is the first step to divorce. Virtue is a tradesman’s jewel. Cleopatra, dying, bequeathed her arts, and is avenged; she has a successor in every Roman’s house. The world is going the same way; so, as to our future, down Eros, up Mars! I am to be a soldier; and you, O my Judah, I pity you; what can you be?”\n\nThe Jew moved nearer the pool; Messala’s drawl deepened.\n\n“Yes, I pity you, my fine Judah. From the college to the synagogue; then to the Temple; then—oh, a crowning glory!—the seat in the Sanhedrim. A life without opportunities; the gods help you! But I—”\n\nJudah looked at him in time to see the flush of pride that kindled in his haughty face as he went on.\n\n“But I—ah, the world is not all conquered. The sea has islands unseen. In the north there are nations yet unvisited. The glory of completing Alexander’s march to the Far East remains to some one. See what possibilities lie before a Roman.”\n\nNext instant he resumed his drawl.\n\n“A campaign into Africa; another after the Scythian; then—a legion! Most careers end there; but not mine. I—by Jupiter! what a conception!—I will give up my legion for a prefecture. Think of life in Rome with money—money, wine, women, games—poets at the banquet, intrigues in the court, dice all the year round. Such a rounding of life may be—a fat prefecture, and it is mine. O my Judah, here is Syria! Judea is rich; Antioch a capital for the gods. I will succeed Cyrenius, and you—shall share my fortune.”\n\nThe sophists and rhetoricians who thronged the public resorts of Rome, almost monopolizing the business of teaching her patrician youth, might have approved these sayings of Messala, for they were all in the popular vein; to the young Jew, however, they were new, and unlike the solemn style of discourse and conversation to which he was accustomed. He belonged, moreover, to a race whose laws, modes, and habits of thought forbade satire and humor; very naturally, therefore, he listened to his friend with varying feelings; one moment indignant, then uncertain how to take him. The superior airs assumed had been offensive to him in the beginning; soon they became irritating, and at last an acute smart. Anger lies close by this point in all of us; and that the satirist evoked in another way. To the Jew of the Herodian period patriotism was a savage passion scarcely hidden under his common humor, and so related to his history, religion, and God that it responded instantly to derision of them. Wherefore it is not speaking too strongly to say that Messala’s progress down to the last pause was exquisite torture to his hearer; at that point the latter said, with a forced smile,\n\n“There are a few, I have heard, who can afford to make a jest of their future; you convince me, O my Messala, that I am not one of them.”\n\nThe Roman studied him; then replied, “Why not the truth in a jest as well as a parable? The great Fulvia went fishing the other day; she caught more than all the company besides. They said it was because the barb of her hook was covered with gold.”\n\n“Then you were not merely jesting?”\n\n“My Judah, I see I did not offer you enough,” the Roman answered, quickly, his eyes sparkling. “When I am prefect, with Judea to enrich me, I—will make you high-priest.”\n\nThe Jew turned off angrily.\n\n“Do not leave me,” said Messala.\n\nThe other stopped irresolute.\n\n“Gods, Judah, how hot the sun shines!” cried the patrician, observing his perplexity. “Let us seek a shade.”\n\nJudah answered, coldly,\n\n“We had better part. I wish I had not come. I sought a friend and find a—”\n\n“Roman,” said Messala, quickly.\n\nThe hands of the Jew clenched, but controlling himself again, he started off. Messala arose, and, taking the mantle from the bench, flung it over his shoulder, and followed after; when he gained his side, he put his hand upon his shoulder and walked with him.\n\n“This is the way—my hand thus—we used to walk when we were children. Let us keep it as far as the gate.”\n\nApparently Messala was trying to be serious and kind, though he could not rid his countenance of the habitual satirical expression. Judah permitted the familiarity.\n\n“You are a boy; I am a man; let me talk like one.”\n\nThe complacency of the Roman was superb. Mentor lecturing the young Telemachus could not have been more at ease.\n\n“Do you believe in the Parcae? Ah, I forgot, you are a Sadducee: the Essenes are your sensible people; they believe in the sisters. So do I. How everlastingly the three are in the way of our doing what we please! I sit down scheming. I run paths here and there. Perpol! Just when I am reaching to take the world in hand, I hear behind me the grinding of scissors. I look, and there she is, the accursed Atropos! But, my Judah, why did you get mad when I spoke of succeeding old Cyrenius? You thought I meant to enrich myself plundering your Judea. Suppose so; it is what some Roman will do. Why not I?”\n\nJudah shortened his step.\n\n“There have been strangers in mastery of Judea before the Roman,” he said, with lifted hand. “Where are they, Messala? She has outlived them all. What has been will be again.”\n\nMessala put on his drawl.\n\n“The Parcae have believers outside the Essenes. Welcome, Judah, welcome to the faith!”\n\n“No, Messala, count me not with them. My faith rests on the rock which was the foundation of the faith of my fathers back further than Abraham; on the covenants of the Lord God of Israel.”\n\n“Too much passion, my Judah. How my master would have been shocked had I been guilty of so much heat in his presence! There were other things I had to tell you, but I fear to now.”\n\nWhen they had gone a few yards, the Roman spoke again.\n\n“I think you can hear me now, especially as what I have to say concerns yourself. I would serve you, O handsome as Ganymede; I would serve you with real good-will. I love you—all I can. I told you I meant to be a soldier. Why not you also? Why not you step out of the narrow circle which, as I have shown, is all of noble life your laws and customs allow?”\n\nJudah made no reply.\n\n“Who are the wise men of our day?” Messala continued. “Not they who exhaust their years quarrelling about dead things; about Baals, Joves, and Jehovahs; about philosophies and religions. Give me one great name, O Judah; I care not where you go to find it—to Rome, Egypt, the East, or here in Jerusalem—Pluto take me if it belong not to a man who wrought his fame out of the material furnished him by the present; holding nothing sacred that did not contribute to the end, scorning nothing that did! How was it with Herod? How with the Maccabees? How with the first and second Cæsars? Imitate them. Begin now. At hand see—Rome, as ready to help you as she was the Idumaean Antipater.”\n\nThe Jewish lad trembled with rage; and, as the garden gate was close by, he quickened his steps, eager to escape.\n\n“O Rome, Rome!” he muttered.\n\n“Be wise,” continued Messala. “Give up the follies of Moses and the traditions; see the situation as it is. Dare look the Parcae in the face, and they will tell you, Rome is the world. Ask them of Judea, and they will answer, She is what Rome wills.”\n\nThey were now at the gate. Judah stopped, and took the hand gently from his shoulder, and confronted Messala, tears trembling in his eyes.\n\n“I understand you, because you are a Roman; you cannot understand me—I am an Israelite. You have given me suffering to-day by convincing me that we can never be the friends we have been—never! Here we part. The peace of the God of my fathers abide with you!”\n\nMessala offered him his hand; the Jew walked on through the gateway. When he was gone, the Roman was silent awhile; then he, too, passed through, saying to himself, with a toss of the head,\n\n“Be it so. Eros is dead, Mars reigns!”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nFrom the entrance to the Holy City, equivalent to what is now called St. Stephen’s Gate, a street extended westwardly, on a line parallel with the northern front of the Tower of Antonia, though a square from that famous castle. Keeping the course as far as the Tyropoeon Valley, which it followed a little way south, it turned and again ran west until a short distance beyond what tradition tells us was the Judgment Gate, from whence it broke abruptly south. The traveller or the student familiar with the sacred locality will recognize the thoroughfare described as part of the Via Dolorosa—with Christians of more interest, though of a melancholy kind, than any street in the world. As the purpose in view does not at present require dealing with the whole street, it will be sufficient to point out a house standing in the angle last mentioned as marking the change of direction south, and which, as an important centre of interest, needs somewhat particular description.\n\nThe building fronted north and west, probably four hundred feet each way, and, like most pretentious Eastern structures, was two stories in height, and perfectly quadrangular. The street on the west side was about twelve feet wide, that on the north not more than ten; so that one walking close to the walls, and looking up at them, would have been struck by the rude, unfinished, uninviting, but strong and imposing, appearance they presented; for they were of stone laid in large blocks, undressed—on the outer side, in fact, just as they were taken from the quarry. A critic of this age would have pronounced the house fortelesque in style, except for the windows, with which it was unusually garnished, and the ornate finish of the doorways or gates. The western windows were four in number, the northern only two, all set on the line of the second story in such manner as to overhang the thoroughfares below. The gates were the only breaks of wall externally visible in the first story; and, besides being so thickly riven with iron bolts as to suggest resistance to battering-rams, they were protected by cornices of marble, handsomely executed, and of such bold projection as to assure visitors well informed of the people that the rich man who resided there was a Sadducee in politics and creed.\n\nNot long after the young Jew parted from the Roman at the palace up on the Market-place, he stopped before the western gate of the house described, and knocked. The wicket (a door hung in one of the valves of the gate) was opened to admit him. He stepped in hastily, and failed to acknowledge the low salaam of the porter.\n\nTo get an idea of the interior arrangement of the structure, as well as to see what more befell the youth, we will follow him.\n\nThe passage into which he was admitted appeared not unlike a narrow tunnel with panelled walls and pitted ceiling. There were benches of stone on both sides, stained and polished by long use. Twelve or fifteen steps carried him into a court-yard, oblong north and south, and in every quarter, except the east, bounded by what seemed the fronts of two-story houses; of which the lower floor was divided into lewens, while the upper was terraced and defended by strong balustrading. The servants coming and going along the terraces; the noise of millstones grinding; the garments fluttering from ropes stretched from point to point; the chickens and pigeons in full enjoyment of the place; the goats, cows, donkeys, and horses stabled in the lewens; a massive trough of water, apparently for the common use, declared this court appurtenant to the domestic management of the owner. Eastwardly there was a division wall broken by another passage-way in all respects like the first one.\n\nClearing the second passage, the young man entered a second court, spacious, square, and set with shrubbery and vines, kept fresh and beautiful by water from a basin erected near a porch on the north side. The lewens here were high, airy, and shaded by curtains striped alternate white and red. The arches of the lewens rested on clustered columns. A flight of steps on the south ascended to the terraces of the upper story, over which great awnings were stretched as a defence against the sun. Another stairway reached from the terraces to the roof, the edge of which, all around the square, was defined by a sculptured cornice, and a parapet of burned-clay tiling, sexangular and bright red. In this quarter, moreover, there was everywhere observable a scrupulous neatness, which, allowing no dust in the angles, not even a yellow leaf upon a shrub, contributed quite as much as anything else to the delightful general effect; insomuch that a visitor, breathing the sweet air, knew, in advance of introduction, the refinement of the family he was about calling upon.\n\nA few steps within the second court, the lad turned to the right, and, choosing a walk through the shrubbery, part of which was in flower, passed to the stairway, and ascended to the terrace—a broad pavement of white and brown flags closely laid, and much worn. Making way under the awning to a doorway on the north side, he entered an apartment which the dropping of the screen behind him returned to darkness. Nevertheless, he proceeded, moving over a tiled floor to a divan, upon which he flung himself, face downwards, and lay at rest, his forehead upon his crossed arms.\n\nAbout nightfall a woman came to the door and called; he answered, and she went in.\n\n“Supper is over, and it is night. Is not my son hungry?” she asked.\n\n“No,” he replied.\n\n“Are you sick?”\n\n“I am sleepy.”\n\n“Your mother has asked for you.”\n\n“Where is she?”\n\n“In the summer-house on the roof.”\n\nHe stirred himself, and sat up.\n\n“Very well. Bring me something to eat.”\n\n“What do you want?”\n\n“What you please, Amrah. I am not sick, but indifferent. Life does not seem as pleasant as it did this morning. A new ailment, O my Amrah; and you who know me so well, who never failed me, may think of the things now that answer for food and medicine. Bring me what you choose.”\n\nAmrah’s questions, and the voice in which she put them—low, sympathetic, and solicitous—were significant of an endeared relation between the two. She laid her hand upon his forehead; then, as satisfied, went out, saying, “I will see.”\n\nAfter a while she returned, bearing on a wooden platter a bowl of milk, some thin cakes of white bread broken, a delicate paste of brayed wheat, a bird broiled, and honey and salt. On one end of the platter there was a silver goblet full of wine, on the other a brazen hand-lamp lighted.\n\nThe room was then revealed: its walls smoothly plastered; the ceiling broken by great oaken rafters, brown with rain stains and time; the floor of small diamond-shaped white and blue tiles, very firm and enduring; a few stools with legs carved in imitation of the legs of lions; a divan raised a little above the floor, trimmed with blue cloth, and partially covered by an immense striped woollen blanket or shawl—in brief, a Hebrew bedroom.\n\nThe same light also gave the woman to view. Drawing a stool to the divan, she placed the platter upon it, then knelt close by ready to serve him. Her face was that of a woman of fifty, dark-skinned, dark-eyed, and at the moment softened by a look of tenderness almost maternal. A white turban covered her head, leaving the lobes of the ear exposed, and in them the sign that settled her condition—an orifice bored by a thick awl. She was a slave, of Egyptian origin, to whom not even the sacred fiftieth year could have brought freedom; nor would she have accepted it, for the boy she was attending was her life. She had nursed him through babyhood, tended him as a child, and could not break the service. To her love he could never be a man.\n\nHe spoke but once during the meal.\n\n“You remember, O my Amrah,” he said, “the Messala who used to visit me here days at a time.”\n\n“I remember him.”\n\n“He went to Rome some years ago, and is now back. I called upon him to-day.”\n\nA shudder of disgust seized the lad.\n\n“I knew something had happened,” she said, deeply interested. “I never liked the Messala. Tell me all.”\n\nBut he fell into musing, and to her repeated inquiries only said, “He is much changed, and I shall have nothing more to do with him.”\n\nWhen Amrah took the platter away, he also went out, and up from the terrace to the roof.\n\nThe reader is presumed to know somewhat of the uses of the house-top in the East. In the matter of customs, climate is a lawgiver everywhere. The Syrian summer day drives the seeker of comfort into the darkened lewen; night, however, calls him forth early, and the shadows deepening over the mountain-sides seem veils dimly covering Circean singers; but they are far off, while the roof is close by, and raised above the level of the shimmering plain enough for the visitation of cool airs, and sufficiently above the trees to allure the stars down closer, down at least into brighter shining. So the roof became a resort—became playground, sleeping-chamber, boudoir, rendezvous for the family, place of music, dance, conversation, reverie, and prayer.\n\nThe motive that prompts the decoration, at whatever cost, of interiors in colder climes suggested to the Oriental the embellishment of his house-top. The parapet ordered by Moses became a potter’s triumph; above that, later, arose towers, plain and fantastic; still later, kings and princes crowned their roofs with summer-houses of marble and gold. When the Babylonian hung gardens in the air, extravagance could push the idea no further.\n\nThe lad whom we are following walked slowly across the house-top to a tower built over the northwest corner of the palace. Had he been a stranger, he might have bestowed a glance upon the structure as he drew nigh it, and seen all the dimness permitted—a darkened mass, low, latticed, pillared, and domed. He entered, passing under a half-raised curtain. The interior was all darkness, except that on four sides there were arched openings like doorways, through which the sky, lighted with stars, was visible. In one of the openings, reclining against a cushion from a divan, he saw the figure of a woman, indistinct even in white floating drapery. At the sound of his steps upon the floor, the fan in her hand stopped, glistening where the starlight struck the jewels with which it was sprinkled, and she sat up, and called his name.\n\n“Judah, my son!”\n\n“It is I, mother,” he answered, quickening his approach.\n\nGoing to her, he knelt, and she put her arms around him, and with kisses pressed him to her bosom.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nThe mother resumed her easy position against the cushion, while the son took place on the divan, his head in her lap. Both of them, looking out of the opening, could see a stretch of lower house-tops in the vicinity, a bank of blue-blackness over in the west which they knew to be mountains, and the sky, its shadowy depths brilliant with stars. The city was still. Only the winds stirred.\n\n“Amrah tells me something has happened to you,” she said, caressing his cheek. “When my Judah was a child, I allowed small things to trouble him, but he is now a man. He must not forget”—her voice became very soft—“that one day he is to be my hero.”\n\nShe spoke in the language almost lost in the land, but which a few—and they were always as rich in blood as in possessions—cherished in its purity, that they might be more certainly distinguished from Gentile peoples—the language in which the loved Rebekah and Rachel sang to Benjamin.\n\nThe words appeared to set him thinking anew; after a while, however, he caught the hand with which she fanned him, and said, “Today, O my mother, I have been made to think of many things that never had place in my mind before. Tell me, first, what am I to be?”\n\n“Have I not told you? You are to be my hero.”\n\nHe could not see her face, yet he knew she was in play. He became more serious.\n\n“You are very good, very kind, O my mother. No one will ever love me as you do.”\n\nHe kissed the hand over and over again.\n\n“I think I understand why you would have me put off the question,” he continued. “Thus far my life has belonged to you. How gentle, how sweet your control has been! I wish it could last forever. But that may not be. It is the Lord’s will that I shall one day become owner of myself—a day of separation, and therefore a dreadful day to you. Let us be brave and serious. I will be your hero, but you must put me in the way. You know the law—every son of Israel must have some occupation. I am not exempt, and ask now, shall I tend the herds? or till the soil? or drive the saw? or be a clerk or lawyer? What shall I be? Dear, good mother, help me to an answer.”\n\n“Gamaliel has been lecturing today,” she said, thoughtfully.\n\n“If so, I did not hear him.”\n\n“Then you have been walking with Simeon, who, they tell me, inherits the genius of his family.”\n\n“No, I have not seen him. I have been up on the Market-place, not to the Temple. I visited the young Messala.”\n\nA certain change in his voice attracted the mother’s attention. A presentiment quickened the beating of her heart; the fan became motionless again.\n\n“The Messala!” she said. “What could he say to so trouble you?”\n\n“He is very much changed.”\n\n“You mean he has come back a Roman.”\n“Yes.”\n\n“Roman!” she continued, half to herself. “To all the world the word means master. How long has he been away?”\n\n“Five years.”\n\nShe raised her head, and looked off into the night.\n\n“The airs of the Via Sacra are well enough in the streets of the Egyptian and in Babylon; but in Jerusalem—our Jerusalem—the covenant abides.”\n\nAnd, full of the thought, she settled back into her easy place. He was first to speak.\n\n“What Messala said, my mother, was sharp enough in itself; but, taken with the manner, some of the sayings were intolerable.”\n\n“I think I understand you. Rome, her poets, orators, senators, courtiers, are mad with affectation of what they call satire.”\n\n“I suppose all great peoples are proud,” he went on, scarcely noticing the interruption; “but the pride of that people is unlike all others; in these latter days it is so grown the gods barely escape it.”\n\n“The gods escape!” said the mother, quickly. “More than one Roman has accepted worship as his divine right.”\n\n“Well, Messala always had his share of the disagreeable quality. When he was a child, I have seen him mock strangers whom even Herod condescended to receive with honors; yet he always spared Judea. For the first time, in conversation with me to-day, he trifled with our customs and God. As you would have had me do, I parted with him finally. And now, O my dear mother, I would know with more certainty if there be just ground for the Roman’s contempt. In what am I his inferior? Is ours a lower order of people? Why should I, even in Cæsar’s presence; feel the shrinking of a slave? Tell me especially why, if I have the soul, and so choose, I may not hunt the honors of the world in all its fields? Why may not I take sword and indulge the passion of war? As a poet, why may not I sing of all themes? I can be a worker in metals, a keeper of flocks, a merchant, why not an artist like the Greek? Tell me, O my mother—and this is the sum of my trouble—why may not a son of Israel do all a Roman may?”\n\nThe reader will refer these questions back to the conversation in the Market-place; the mother, listening with all her faculties awake, from something which would have been lost upon one less interested in him—from the connections of the subject, the pointing of the questions, possibly his accent and tone—was not less swift in making the same reference. She sat up, and in a voice quick and sharp as his own, replied, “I see, I see! From association Messala, in boyhood, was almost a Jew; had he remained here, he might have become a proselyte, so much do we all borrow from the influences that ripen our lives; but the years in Rome have been too much for him. I do not wonder at the change; yet”—her voice fell—“he might have dealt tenderly at least with you. It is a hard, cruel nature which in youth can forget its first loves.”\n\nHer hand dropped lightly upon his forehead, and the fingers caught in his hair and lingered there lovingly, while her eyes sought the highest stars in view. Her pride responded to his, not merely in echo, but in the unison of perfect sympathy. She would answer him; at the same time, not for the world would she have had the answer unsatisfactory: an admission of inferiority might weaken his spirit for life. She faltered with misgivings of her own powers.\n\n“What you propose, O my Judah, is not a subject for treatment by a woman. Let me put its consideration off till to-morrow, and I will have the wise Simeon—”\n\n“Do not send me to the Rector,” he said, abruptly.\n\n“I will have him come to us.”\n\n“No, I seek more than information; while he might give me that better than you, O my mother, you can do better by giving me what he cannot—the resolution which is the soul of a man’s soul.”\n\nShe swept the heavens with a rapid glance, trying to compass all the meaning of his questions.\n\n“While craving justice for ourselves, it is never wise to be unjust to others. To deny valor in the enemy we have conquered is to underrate our victory; and if the enemy be strong enough to hold us at bay, much more to conquer us”—she hesitated—“self-respect bids us seek some other explanation of our misfortunes than accusing him of qualities inferior to our own.”\n\nThus, speaking to herself rather than to him, she began:\n\n“Take heart, O my son. The Messala is nobly descended; his family has been illustrious through many generations. In the days of Republican Rome—how far back I cannot tell—they were famous, some as soldiers, some as civilians. I can recall but one consul of the name; their rank was senatorial, and their patronage always sought because they were always rich. Yet if to-day your friend boasted of his ancestry, you might have shamed him by recounting yours. If he referred to the ages through which the line is traceable, or to deeds, rank, or wealth—such allusions, except when great occasion demands them, are tokens of small minds—if he mentioned them in proof of his superiority, then without dread, and standing on each particular, you might have challenged him to a comparison of records.”\n\nTaking a moment’s thought, the mother proceeded:\n\n“One of the ideas of fast hold now is that time has much to do with the nobility of races and families. A Roman boasting his superiority on that account over a son of Israel will always fail when put to the proof. The founding of Rome was his beginning; the very best of them cannot trace their descent beyond that period; few of them pretend to do so; and of such as do, I say not one could make good his claim except by resort to tradition. Messala certainly could not. Let us look now to ourselves. Could we better?”\n\nA little more light would have enabled him to see the pride that diffused itself over her face.\n\n“Let us imagine the Roman putting us to the challenge. I would answer him, neither doubting nor boastful.”\n\nHer voice faltered; a tender thought changed the form of the argument.\n\n“Your father, O my Judah, is at rest with his fathers; yet I remember, as though it were this evening, the day he and I, with many rejoicing friends, went up into the Temple to present you to the Lord. We sacrificed the doves, and to the priest I gave your name, which he wrote in my presence—‘Judah, son of Ithamar, of the House of Hur.’ The name was then carried away, and written in a book of the division of records devoted to the saintly family.\n\n“I cannot tell you when the custom of registration in this mode began. We know it prevailed before the flight from Egypt. I have heard Hillel say Abraham caused the record to be first opened with his own name, and the names of his sons, moved by the promises of the Lord which separated him and them from all other races, and made them the highest and noblest, the very chosen of the earth. The covenant with Jacob was of like effect. ‘In thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed’—so said the angel to Abraham in the place Jehovah-jireh. ‘And the land whereon thou liest, to thee will I give it, and to thy seed’—so the Lord himself said to Jacob asleep at Bethel on the way to Haran. Afterwards the wise men looked forward to a just division of the land of promise; and, that it might be known in the day of partition who were entitled to portions, the Book of Generations was begun. But not for that alone. The promise of a blessing to all the earth through the patriarch reached far into the future. One name was mentioned in connection with the blessing—the benefactor might be the humblest of the chosen family, for the Lord our God knows no distinctions of rank or riches. So, to make the performance clear to men of the generation who were to witness it, and that they might give the glory to whom it belonged, the record was required to be kept with absolute certainty. Has it been so kept?”\n\nThe fan played to and fro, until, becoming impatient, he repeated the question, “Is the record absolutely true?”\n\n“Hillel said it was, and of all who have lived no one was so well-informed upon the subject. Our people have at times been heedless of some parts of the law, but never of this part. The good rector himself has followed the Books of Generations through three periods—from the promises to the opening of the Temple; thence to the Captivity; thence, again, to the present. Once only were the records disturbed, and that was at the end of the second period; but when the nation returned from the long exile, as a first duty to God, Zerubbabel restored the Books, enabling us once more to carry the lines of Jewish descent back unbroken fully two thousand years. And now—”\n\nShe paused as if to allow the hearer to measure the time comprehended in the statement.\n\n“And now,” she continued, “what becomes of the Roman boast of blood enriched by ages? By that test, the sons of Israel watching the herds on old Rephaim yonder are nobler than the noblest of the Marcii.”\n\n“And I, mother—by the Books, who am I?”\n\n“What I have said thus far, my son, had reference to your question. I will answer you. If Messala were here, he might say, as others have said, that the exact trace of your lineage stopped when the Assyrian took Jerusalem, and razed the Temple, with all its precious stores; but you might plead the pious action of Zerubbabel, and retort that all verity in Roman genealogy ended when the barbarians from the West took Rome, and camped six months upon her desolated site. Did the government keep family histories? If so, what became of them in those dreadful days? No, no; there is verity in our Books of Generations; and, following them back to the Captivity, back to the foundation of the first Temple, back to the march from Egypt, we have absolute assurance that you are lineally sprung from Hur, the associate of Joshua. In the matter of descent sanctified by time, is not the honor perfect? Do you care to pursue further? if so, take the Torah, and search the Book of Numbers, and of the seventy-two generations after Adam, you can find the very progenitor of your house.”\n\nThere was silence for a time in the chamber on the roof.\n\n“I thank you, O my mother,” Judah next said, clasping both her hands in his; “I thank you with all my heart. I was right in not having the good rector called in; he could not have satisfied me more than you have. Yet to make a family truly noble, is time alone sufficient?”\n\n“Ah, you forget, you forget; our claim rests not merely upon time; the Lord’s preference is our especial glory.”\n\n“You are speaking of the race, and I, mother, of the family—our family. In the years since Father Abraham, what have they achieved? What have they done? What great things to lift them above the level of their fellows?”\n\nShe hesitated, thinking she might all this time have mistaken his object. The information he sought might have been for more than satisfaction of wounded vanity. Youth is but the painted shell within which, continually growing, lives that wondrous thing the spirit of man, biding its moment of apparition, earlier in some than in others. She trembled under a perception that this might be the supreme moment come to him; that as children at birth reach out their untried hands grasping for shadows, and crying the while, so his spirit might, in temporary blindness, be struggling to take hold of its impalpable future. They to whom a boy comes asking, Who am I, and what am I to be? have need of ever so much care. Each word in answer may prove to the after-life what each finger-touch of the artist is to the clay he is modelling.\n\n“I have a feeling, O my Judah,” she said, patting his cheek with the hand he had been caressing—“I have the feeling that all I have said has been in strife with an antagonist more real than imaginary. If Messala is the enemy, do not leave me to fight him in the dark. Tell me all he said.”\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThe young Israelite proceeded then, and rehearsed his conversation with Messala, dwelling with particularity upon the latter’s speeches in contempt of the Jews, their customs, and much pent round of life.\n\nAfraid to speak the while, the mother listened, discerning the matter plainly. Judah had gone to the palace on the Market-place, allured by love of a playmate whom he thought to find exactly as he had been at the parting years before; a man met him, and, in place of laughter and references to the sports of the past, the man had been full of the future, and talked of glory to be won, and of riches and power. Unconscious of the effect, the visitor had come away hurt in pride, yet touched with a natural ambition; but she, the jealous mother, saw it, and, not knowing the turn the aspiration might take, became at once Jewish in her fear. What if it lured him away from the patriarchal faith? In her view, that consequence was more dreadful than any or all others. She could discover but one way to avert it, and she set about the task, her native power reinforced by love to such degree that her speech took a masculine strength and at times a poet’s fervor.\n\n“There never has been a people,” she began, “who did not think themselves at least equal to any other; never a great nation, my son, that did not believe itself the very superior. When the Roman looks down upon Israel and laughs, he merely repeats the folly of the Egyptian, the Assyrian, and the Macedonian; and as the laugh is against God, the result will be the same.”\n\nHer voice became firmer.\n\n“There is no law by which to determine the superiority of nations; hence the vanity of the claim, and the idleness of disputes about it. A people risen, run their race, and die either of themselves or at the hands of another, who, succeeding to their power, take possession of their place, and upon their monuments write new names; such is history. If I were called upon to symbolize God and man in the simplest form, I would draw a straight line and a circle, and of the line I would say, ‘This is God, for he alone moves forever straightforward,’ and of the circle, ‘This is man—such is his progress.’ I do not mean that there is no difference between the careers of nations; no two are alike. The difference, however, is not, as some say, in the extent of the circle they describe or the space of earth they cover, but in the sphere of their movement, the highest being nearest God.\n\n“To stop here, my son, would be to leave the subject where we began. Let us go on. There are signs by which to measure the height of the circle each nation runs while in its course. By them let us compare the Hebrew and the Roman.\n\n“The simplest of all the signs is the daily life of the people. Of this I will only say, Israel has at times forgotten God, while the Roman never knew him; consequently comparison is not possible.\n\n“Your friend—or your former friend—charged, if I understood you rightly, that we have had no poets, artists, or warriors; by which he meant, I suppose, to deny that we have had great men, the next most certain of the signs. A just consideration of this charge requires a definition at the commencement. A great man, O my boy, is one whose life proves him to have been recognized, if not called, by God. A Persian was used to punish our recreant fathers, and he carried them into captivity; another Persian was selected to restore their children to the Holy Land; greater than either of them, however, was the Macedonian through whom the desolation of Judea and the Temple was avenged. The special distinction of the men was that they were chosen by the Lord, each for a divine purpose; and that they were Gentiles does not lessen their glory. Do not lose sight of this definition while I proceed.\n\n“There is an idea that war is the most noble occupation of men, and that the most exalted greatness is the growth of battle-fields. Because the world has adopted the idea, be not you deceived. That we must worship something is a law which will continue as long as there is anything we cannot understand. The prayer of the barbarian is a wail of fear addressed to Strength, the only divine quality he can clearly conceive; hence his faith in heroes. What is Jove but a Roman hero? The Greeks have their great glory because they were the first to set Mind above Strength. In Athens the orator and philosopher were more revered than the warrior. The charioteer and the swiftest runner are still idols of the arena; yet the immortelles are reserved for the sweetest singer. The birthplace of one poet was contested by seven cities. But was the Hellene the first to deny the old barbaric faith? No. My son, that glory is ours; against brutalism our fathers erected God; in our worship, the wail of fear gave place to the Hosanna and the Psalm. So the Hebrew and the Greek would have carried all humanity forward and upward. But, alas! the government of the world presumes war as an eternal condition; wherefore, over Mind and above God, the Roman has enthroned his Cæsar, the absorbent of all attainable power, the prohibition of any other greatness.\n\n“The sway of the Greek was a flowering time for genius. In return for the liberty it then enjoyed, what a company of thinkers the Mind led forth? There was a glory for every excellence, and a perfection so absolute that in everything but war even the Roman has stooped to imitation. A Greek is now the model of the orators in the Forum; listen, and in every Roman song you will hear the rhythm of the Greek; if a Roman opens his mouth speaking wisely of moralities, or abstractions, or of the mysteries of nature, he is either a plagiarist or the disciple of some school which had a Greek for its founder. In nothing but war, I say again, has Rome a claim to originality. Her games and spectacles are Greek inventions, dashed with blood to gratify the ferocity of her rabble; her religion, if such it may be called, is made up of contributions from the faiths of all other peoples; her most venerated gods are from Olympus—even her Mars, and, for that matter, the Jove she much magnifies. So it happens, O my son, that of the whole world our Israel alone can dispute the superiority of the Greek, and with him contest the palm of original genius.\n\n“To the excellences of other peoples the egotism of a Roman is a blindfold, impenetrable as his breastplate. Oh, the ruthless robbers! Under their trampling the earth trembles like a floor beaten with flails. Along with the rest we are fallen—alas that I should say it to you, my son! They have our highest places, and the holiest, and the end no man can tell; but this I know—they may reduce Judea as an almond broken with hammers, and devour Jerusalem, which is the oil and sweetness thereof; yet the glory of the men of Israel will remain a light in the heavens overhead out of reach: for their history is the history of God, who wrote with their hands, spake with their tongues, and was himself in all the good they did, even the least; who dwelt with them, a Lawgiver on Sinai, a Guide in the wilderness, in war a Captain, in government a King; who once and again pushed back the curtains of the pavilion which is his resting-place, intolerably bright, and, as a man speaking to men, showed them the right, and the way to happiness, and how they should live, and made them promises binding the strength of his Almightiness with covenants sworn to everlastingly. O my son, could it be that they with whom Jehovah thus dwelt, an awful familiar, derived nothing from him?—that in their lives and deeds the common human qualities should not in some degree have been mixed and colored with the divine? that their genius should not have in it, even after the lapse of ages, some little of heaven?”\n\nFor a time the rustling of the fan was all the sound heard in the chamber.\n\n“In the sense which limits art to sculpture and painting, it is true,” she next said, “Israel has had no artists.”\n\nThe admission was made regretfully, for it must be remembered she was a Sadducee, whose faith, unlike that of the Pharisees, permitted a love of the beautiful in every form, and without reference to its origin.\n\n“Still he who would do justice,” she proceeded, “will not forget that the cunning of our hands was bound by the prohibition, ‘Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, or any likeness of anything;’ which the Sopherim wickedly extended beyond its purpose and time. Nor should it be forgotten that long before Dædalus appeared in Attica and with his wooden statues so transformed sculpture as to make possible the schools of Corinth and Ægina, and their ultimate triumphs the Poecile and Capitolium—long before the age of Dædalus, I say, two Israelites, Bezaleel and Aholiab, the master-builders of the first tabernacle, said to have been skilled ‘in all manner of workmanship,’ wrought the cherubim of the mercy-seat above the ark. Of gold beaten, not chiseled, were they; and they were statues in form both human and divine. ‘And they shall stretch forth their wings on high, .... and their faces shall look one to another.’ Who will say they were not beautiful? or that they were not the first statues?”\n\n“Oh, I see now why the Greek outstripped us,” said Judah, intensely interested. “And the ark; accursed be the Babylonians who destroyed it!”\n\n“Nay, Judah, be of faith. It was not destroyed, only lost, hidden away too safely in some cavern of the mountains. One day—Hillel and Shammai both say so—one day, in the Lord’s good time, it will be found and brought forth, and Israel dance before it, singing as of old. And they who look upon the faces of the cherubim then, though they have seen the face of the ivory Minerva, will be ready to kiss the hand of the Jew from love of his genius, asleep through all the thousands of years.”\n\nThe mother, in her eagerness, had risen into something like the rapidity and vehemence of a speech-maker; but now, to recover herself, or to pick up the thread of her thought, she rested awhile.\n\n“You are so good, my mother,” he said, in a grateful way. “And I will never be done saying so. Shammai could not have talked better, nor Hillel. I am a true son of Israel again.”\n\n“Flatterer!” she said. “You do not know that I am but repeating what I heard Hillel say in an argument he had one day in my presence with a sophist from Rome.”\n\n“Well, the hearty words are yours.”\n\nDirectly all her earnestness returned.\n\n“Where was I? Oh yes, I was claiming for our Hebrew fathers the first statues. The trick of the sculptor, Judah, is not all there is of art, any more than art is all there is of greatness. I always think of great men marching down the centuries in groups and goodly companies, separable according to nationalities; here the Indian, there the Egyptian, yonder the Assyrian; above them the music of trumpets and the beauty of banners; and on their right hand and left, as reverent spectators, the generations from the beginning, numberless. As they go, I think of the Greek, saying, ‘Lo! The Hellene leads the way.’ Then the Roman replies, ‘Silence! what was your place is ours now; we have left you behind as dust trodden on.’ And all the time, from the far front back over the line of march, as well as forward into the farthest future, streams a light of which the wranglers know nothing, except that it is forever leading them on—the Light of Revelation! Who are they that carry it? Ah, the old Judean blood! How it leaps at the thought! By the light we know them. Thrice blessed, O our fathers, servants of God, keepers of the covenants! Ye are the leaders of men, the living and the dead. The front is thine; and though every Roman were a Cæsar, ye shall not lose it!”\n\nJudah was deeply stirred.\n\n“Do not stop, I pray you,” he cried. “You give me to hear the sound of timbrels. I wait for Miriam and the women who went after her dancing and singing.”\n\nShe caught his feeling, and, with ready wit, wove it into her speech.\n\n“Very well, my son. If you can hear the timbrel of the prophetess, you can do what I was about to ask; you can use your fancy, and stand with me, as if by the wayside, while the chosen of Israel pass us at the head of the procession. Now they come—the patriarchs first; next the fathers of the tribes. I almost hear the bells of their camels and the lowing of their herds. Who is he that walks alone between the companies? An old man, yet his eye is not dim, nor his natural force abated. He knew the Lord face to face! Warrior, poet, orator, lawgiver, prophet, his greatness is as the sun at morning, its flood of splendor quenching all other lights, even that of the first and noblest of the Cæsars. After him the judges. And then the kings—the son of Jesse, a hero in war, and a singer of songs eternal as that of the sea; and his son, who, passing all other kings in riches and wisdom, and while making the Desert habitable, and in its waste places planting cities, forgot not Jerusalem which the Lord had chosen for his seat on earth. Bend lower, my son! These that come next are the first of their kind, and the last. Their faces are raised, as if they heard a voice in the sky and were listening. Their lives were full of sorrows. Their garments smell of tombs and caverns. Hearken to a woman among them—‘Sing ye to the Lord, for he hath triumphed gloriously!’ Nay, put your forehead in the dust before them! They were tongues of God, his servants, who looked through heaven, and, seeing all the future, wrote what they saw, and left the writing to be proven by time. Kings turned pale as they approached them, and nations trembled at the sound of their voices. The elements waited upon them. In their hands they carried every bounty and every plague. See the Tishbite and his servant Elisha! See the sad son of Hilkiah, and him, the seer of visions, by the river of Chebar! And of the three children of Judah who refused the image of the Babylonian, lo! that one who, in the feast to the thousand lords, so confounded the astrologers. And yonder—O my son, kiss the dust again!—yonder the gentle son of Amoz, from whom the world has its promise of the Messiah to come!”\n\nIn this passage the fan had been kept in rapid play; it stopped now, and her voice sank low.\n\n“You are tired,” she said.\n\n“No,” he replied, “I was listening to a new song of Israel.”\n\nThe mother was still intent upon her purpose, and passed the pleasant speech.\n\n“In such light as I could, my Judah, I have set our great men before you—patriarchs, legislators, warriors, singers, prophets. Turn we to the best of Rome. Against Moses place Cæsar, and Tarquin against David; Sylla against either of the Maccabees; the best of the consuls against the judges; Augustus against Solomon, and you are done: comparison ends there. But think then of the prophets—greatest of the great.”\n\nShe laughed scornfully.\n\n“Pardon me. I was thinking of the soothsayer who warned Caius Julius against the Ides of March, and fancied him looking for the omens of evil which his master despised in the entrails of a chicken. From that picture turn to Elijah sitting on the hill-top on the way to Samaria, amid the smoking bodies of the captains and their fifties, warning the son of Ahab of the wrath of our God. Finally, O my Judah—if such speech be reverent—how shall we judge Jehovah and Jupiter unless it be by what their servants have done in their names? And as for what you shall do—”\n\nShe spoke the latter words slowly, and with a tremulous utterance.\n\n“As for what you shall do, my boy—serve the Lord, the Lord God of Israel, not Rome. For a child of Abraham there is no glory except in the Lord’s ways, and in them there is much glory.”\n\n“I may be a soldier then?” Judah asked.\n\n“Why not? Did not Moses call God a man of war?”\n\nThere was then a long silence in the summer chamber.\n\n“You have my permission,” she said, finally; “if only you serve the Lord instead of Cæsar.”\n\nHe was content with the condition, and by-and-by fell asleep. She arose then, and put the cushion under his head, and, throwing a shawl over him and kissing him tenderly, went away.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nThe good man, like the bad, must die; but, remembering the lesson of our faith, we say of him and the event, “No matter, he will open his eyes in heaven.” Nearest this in life is the waking from healthful sleep to a quick consciousness of happy sights and sounds.\n\nWhen Judah awoke, the sun was up over the mountains; the pigeons were abroad in flocks, filling the air with the gleams of their white wings; and off southeast he beheld the Temple, an apparition of gold in the blue of the sky. These, however, were familiar objects, and they received but a glance; upon the edge of the divan, close by him, a girl scarcely fifteen sat singing to the accompaniment of a nebel, which she rested upon her knee, and touched gracefully. To her he turned listening; and this was what she sang:\n\n“Wake not, but hear me, love! Adrift, adrift on slumber’s sea, Thy spirit call to list to me. Wake not, but hear me, love! A gift from Sleep, the restful king, All happy, happy dreams I bring.\n\n“Wake not, but hear me, love! Of all the world of dreams ’tis thine This once to choose the most divine. So choose, and sleep, my love! But ne’er again in choice be free, Unless, unless—thou dream’st of me.”\n\nShe put the instrument down, and, resting her hands in her lap, waited for him to speak. And as it has become necessary to tell somewhat of her, we will avail ourselves of the chance, and add such particulars of the family into whose privacy we are brought as the reader may wish to know.\n\nThe favors of Herod had left surviving him many persons of vast estate. Where this fortune was joined to undoubted lineal descent from some famous son of one of the tribes, especially Judah, the happy individual was accounted a Prince of Jerusalem—a distinction which sufficed to bring him the homage of his less favored countrymen, and the respect, if nothing more, of the Gentiles with whom business and social circumstance brought him into dealing. Of this class none had won in private or public life a higher regard than the father of the lad whom we have been following. With a remembrance of his nationality which never failed him, he had yet been true to the king, and served him faithfully at home and abroad. Some offices had taken him to Rome, where his conduct attracted the notice of Augustus, who strove without reserve to engage his friendship. In his house, accordingly, were many presents, such as had gratified the vanity of kings—purple togas, ivory chairs, golden pateroe—chiefly valuable on account of the imperial hand which had honorably conferred them. Such a man could not fail to be rich; yet his wealth was not altogether the largess of royal patrons. He had welcomed the law that bound him to some pursuit; and, instead of one, he entered into many. Of the herdsmen watching flocks on the plains and hill-sides, far as old Lebanon, numbers reported to him as their employer; in the cities by the sea, and in those inland, he founded houses of traffic; his ships brought him silver from Spain, whose mines were then the richest known; while his caravans came twice a year from the East, laden with silks and spices. In faith he was a Hebrew, observant of the law and every essential rite; his place in the synagogue and Temple knew him well; he was thoroughly learned in the Scriptures; he delighted in the society of the college-masters, and carried his reverence for Hillel almost to the point of worship. Yet he was in no sense a Separatist; his hospitality took in strangers from every land; the carping Pharisees even accused him of having more than once entertained Samaritans at his table. Had he been a Gentile, and lived, the world might have heard of him as the rival of Herodes Atticus: as it was, he perished at sea some ten years before this second period of our story, in the prime of life, and lamented everywhere in Judea. We are already acquainted with two members of his family—his widow and son; the only other was a daughter—she whom we have seen singing to her brother.\n\nTirzah was her name, and as the two looked at each other, their resemblance was plain. Her features had the regularity of his, and were of the same Jewish type; they had also the charm of childish innocency of expression. Home-life and its trustful love permitted the negligent attire in which she appeared. A chemise buttoned upon the right shoulder, and passing loosely over the breast and back and under the left arm, but half concealed her person above the waist, while it left the arms entirely nude. A girdle caught the folds of the garment, marking the commencement of the skirt. The coiffure was very simple and becoming—a silken cap, Tyrian-dyed; and over that a striped scarf of the same material, beautifully embroidered, and wound about in thin folds so as to show the shape of the head without enlarging it; the whole finished by a tassel dropping from the crown point of the cap. She had rings, ear and finger; anklets and bracelets, all of gold; and around her neck there was a collar of gold, curiously garnished with a network of delicate chains, to which were pendants of pearl. The edges of her eyelids were painted, and the tips of her fingers stained. Her hair fell in two long plaits down her back. A curled lock rested upon each cheek in front of the ear. Altogether it would have been impossible to deny her grace, refinement, and beauty.\n\n“Very pretty, my Tirzah, very pretty!” he said, with animation.\n\n“The song?” she asked.\n\n“Yes—and the singer, too. It has the conceit of a Greek. Where did you get it?”\n\n“You remember the Greek who sang in the theatre last month? They said he used to be a singer at the court for Herod and his sister Salome. He came out just after an exhibition of wrestlers, when the house was full of noise. At his first note everything became so quiet that I heard every word. I got the song from him.”\n\n“But he sang in Greek.”\n“And I in Hebrew.”\n\n“Ah, yes. I am proud of my little sister. Have you another as good?”\n\n“Very many. But let them go now. Amrah sent me to tell you she will bring you your breakfast, and that you need not come down. She should be here by this time. She thinks you sick—that a dreadful accident happened you yesterday. What was it? Tell me, and I will help Amrah doctor you. She knows the cures of the Egyptians, who were always a stupid set; but I have a great many recipes of the Arabs who—”\n\n“Are even more stupid than the Egyptians,” he said, shaking his head.\n\n“Do you think so? Very well, then,” she replied, almost without pause, and putting her hands to her left ear. “We will have nothing to do with any of them. I have here what is much surer and better—the amulet which was given to some of our people—I cannot tell when, it was so far back—by a Persian magician. See, the inscription is almost worn out.”\n\nShe offered him the earring, which he took, looked at, and handed back, laughing.\n\n“If I were dying, Tirzah, I could not use the charm. It is a relic of idolatry, forbidden every believing son and daughter of Abraham. Take it, but do not wear it any more.”\n\n“Forbidden! Not so,” she said. “Our father’s mother wore it I do not know how many Sabbaths in her life. It has cured I do not know how many people—more than three anyhow. It is approved—look, here is the mark of the rabbis.”\n\n“I have no faith in amulets.”\n\nShe raised her eyes to his in astonishment.\n\n“What would Amrah say?”\n\n“Amrah’s father and mother tended sakiyeh for a garden on the Nile.”\n\n“But Gamaliel!”\n\n“He says they are godless inventions of unbelievers and Shechemites.”\n\nTirzah looked at the ring doubtfully.\n\n“What shall I do with it?”\n\n“Wear it, my little sister. It becomes you—it helps make you beautiful, though I think you that without help.”\n\nSatisfied, she returned the amulet to her ear just as Amrah entered the summer chamber, bearing a platter, with wash-bowl, water, and napkins.\n\nNot being a Pharisee, the ablution was short and simple with Judah. The servant then went out, leaving Tirzah to dress his hair. When a lock was disposed to her satisfaction, she would unloose the small metallic mirror which, as was the fashion among her fair countrywomen, she wore at her girdle, and gave it to him, that he might see the triumph, and how handsome it made him. Meanwhile they kept up their conversation.\n\n“What do you think, Tirzah?—I am going away.”\n\nShe dropped her hands with amazement.\n\n“Going away! When? Where? For what?”\n\nHe laughed.\n\n“Three questions, all in a breath! What a body you are!” Next instant he became serious. “You know the law requires me to follow some occupation. Our good father set me an example. Even you would despise me if I spent in idleness the results of his industry and knowledge. I am going to Rome.”\n\n“Oh, I will go with you.”\n\n“You must stay with mother. If both of us leave her she will die.”\n\nThe brightness faded from her face.\n\n“Ah, yes, yes! But—must you go? Here in Jerusalem you can learn all that is needed to be a merchant—if that is what you are thinking of.”\n\n“But that is not what I am thinking of. The law does not require the son to be what the father was.”\n\n“What else can you be?”\n\n“A soldier,” he replied, with a certain pride of voice.\n\nTears came into her eyes.\n\n“You will be killed.”\n\n“If God’s will, be it so. But, Tirzah, the soldiers are not all killed.”\n\nShe threw her arms around his neck, as if to hold him back.\n\n“We are so happy! Stay at home, my brother.”\n\n“Home cannot always be what it is. You yourself will be going away before long.”\n\n“Never!”\n\nHe smiled at her earnestness.\n\n“A prince of Judah, or some other of one of the tribes, will come soon and claim my Tirzah, and ride away with her, to be the light of another house. What will then become of me?”\n\nShe answered with sobs.\n\n“War is a trade,” he continued, more soberly. “To learn it thoroughly, one must go to school, and there is no school like a Roman camp.”\n\n“You would not fight for Rome?” she asked, holding her breath.\n\n“And you—even you hate her. The whole world hates her. In that, O Tirzah, find the reason of the answer I give you— Yes, I will fight for her, if, in return, she will teach me how one day to fight against her.”\n\n“When will you go?”\n\nAmrah’s steps were then heard returning.\n\n“Hist!” he said. “Do not let her know of what I am thinking.”\n\nThe faithful slave came in with breakfast, and placed the waiter holding it upon a stool before them; then, with white napkins upon her arm, she remained to serve them. They dipped their fingers in a bowl of water, and were rinsing them, when a noise arrested their attention. They listened, and distinguished martial music in the street on the north side of the house.\n\n“Soldiers from the Praetorium! I must see them,” he cried, springing from the divan, and running out.\n\nIn a moment more he was leaning over the parapet of tiles which guarded the roof at the extreme northeast corner, so absorbed that he did not notice Tirzah by his side, resting one hand upon his shoulder.\n\nTheir position—the roof being the highest one in the locality—commanded the house-tops eastward as far as the huge irregular Tower of Antonia, which has been already mentioned as a citadel for the garrison and military headquarters for the governor. The street, not more than ten feet wide, was spanned here and there by bridges, open and covered, which, like the roofs along the way, were beginning to be occupied by men, women, and children, called out by the music. The word is used, though it is hardly fitting; what the people heard when they came forth was rather an uproar of trumpets and the shriller litui so delightful to the soldiers.\n\nThe array after a while came into view of the two upon the house of the Hurs. First, a vanguard of the light-armed—mostly slingers and bowmen—marching with wide intervals between their ranks and files; next a body of heavy-armed infantry, bearing large shields, and hastoe longoe, or spears identical with those used in the duels before Ilium; then the musicians; and then an officer riding alone, but followed closely by a guard of cavalry; after them again, a column of infantry also heavy-armed, which, moving in close order, crowded the streets from wall to wall, and appeared to be without end.\n\nThe brawny limbs of the men; the cadenced motion from right to left of the shields; the sparkle of scales, buckles, and breastplates and helms, all perfectly burnished; the plumes nodding above the tall crests; the sway of ensigns and iron-shod spears; the bold, confident step, exactly timed and measured; the demeanor, so grave, yet so watchful; the machine-like unity of the whole moving mass—made an impression upon Judah, but as something felt rather than seen. Two objects fixed his attention—the eagle of the legion first—a gilded effigy perched on a tall shaft, with wings outspread until they met above its head. He knew that, when brought from its chamber in the Tower, it had been received with divine honors.\n\nThe officer riding alone in the midst of the column was the other attraction. His head was bare; otherwise he was in full armor. At his left hip he wore a short sword; in his hand, however, he carried a truncheon, which looked like a roll of white paper. He sat upon a purple cloth instead of a saddle, and that, and a bridle with a forestall of gold and reins of yellow silk broadly fringed at the lower edge, completed the housings of the horse.\n\nWhile the man was yet in the distance, Judah observed that his presence was sufficient to throw the people looking at him into angry excitement. They would lean over the parapets or stand boldly out, and shake their fists at him; they followed him with loud cries, and spit at him as he passed under the bridges; the women even flung their sandals, sometimes with such good effect as to hit him. When he was nearer, the yells became distinguishable—“Robber, tyrant, dog of a Roman! Away with Ishmael! Give us back our Hannas!”\n\nWhen quite near, Judah could see that, as was but natural, the man did not share the indifference so superbly shown by the soldiers; his face was dark and sullen, and the glances he occasionally cast at his persecutors were full of menace; the very timid shrank from them.\n\nNow the lad had heard of the custom, borrowed from a habit of the first Cæsar, by which chief commanders, to indicate their rank, appeared in public with only a laurel vine upon their heads. By that sign he knew this officer—VALERIUS GRATUS, THE NEW PROCURATOR OF JUDEA!\n\nTo say truth now, the Roman under the unprovoked storm had the young Jew’s sympathy; so that when he reached the corner of the house, the latter leaned yet farther over the parapet to see him go by, and in the act rested a hand upon a tile which had been a long time cracked and allowed to go unnoticed. The pressure was strong enough to displace the outer piece, which started to fall. A thrill of horror shot through the youth. He reached out to catch the missile. In appearance the motion was exactly that of one pitching something from him. The effort failed—nay, it served to push the descending fragment farther out over the wall. He shouted with all his might. The soldiers of the guard looked up; so did the great man, and that moment the missile struck him, and he fell from his seat as dead.\n\nThe cohort halted; the guards leaped from their horses, and hastened to cover the chief with their shields. On the other hand, the people who witnessed the affair, never doubting that the blow had been purposely dealt, cheered the lad as he yet stooped in full view over the parapet, transfixed by what he beheld, and by anticipation of the consequences flashed all too plainly upon him.\n\nA mischievous spirit flew with incredible speed from roof to roof along the line of march, seizing the people, and urging them all alike. They laid hands upon the parapets and tore up the tiling and the sunburnt mud of which the house-tops were for the most part made, and with blind fury began to fling them upon the legionaries halted below. A battle then ensued. Discipline, of course, prevailed. The struggle, the slaughter, the skill of one side, the desperation of the other, are alike unnecessary to our story. Let us look rather to the wretched author of it all.\n\nHe arose from the parapet, his face very pale.\n\n“O Tirzah, Tirzah! What will become of us?”\n\nShe had not seen the occurrence below, but was listening to the shouting and watching the mad activity of the people in view on the houses. Something terrible was going on, she knew; but what it was, or the cause, or that she or any of those dear to her were in danger, she did not know.\n\n“What has happened? What does it all mean?” she asked, in sudden alarm.\n\n“I have killed the Roman governor. The tile fell upon him.”\n\nAn unseen hand appeared to sprinkle her face with the dust of ashes—it grew white so instantly. She put her arm around him, and looked wistfully, but without a word, into his eyes. His fears had passed to her, and the sight of them gave him strength.\n\n“I did not do it purposely, Tirzah—it was an accident,” he said, more calmly.\n\n“What will they do?” she asked.\n\nHe looked off over the tumult momentarily deepening in the street and on the roofs, and thought of the sullen countenance of Gratus. If he were not dead, where would his vengeance stop? And if he were dead, to what height of fury would not the violence of the people lash the legionaries? To evade an answer, he peered over the parapet again, just as the guard were assisting the Roman to remount his horse.\n\n“He lives, he lives, Tirzah! Blessed be the Lord God of our fathers!”\n\nWith that outcry, and a brightened countenance, he drew back and replied to her question.\n\n“Be not afraid, Tirzah. I will explain how it happened, and they will remember our father and his services, and not hurt us.”\n\nHe was leading her to the summer-house, when the roof jarred under their feet, and a crash of strong timbers being burst away, followed by a cry of surprise and agony, arose apparently from the court-yard below. He stopped and listened. The cry was repeated; then came a rush of many feet, and voices lifted in rage blent with voices in prayer; and then the screams of women in mortal terror. The soldiers had beaten in the north gate, and were in possession of the house. The terrible sense of being hunted smote him. His first impulse was to fly; but where? Nothing but wings would serve him. Tirzah, her eyes wild with fear, caught his arm.\n\n“O Judah, what does it mean?”\n\nThe servants were being butchered—and his mother! Was not one of the voices he heard hers? With all the will left him, he said, “Stay here, and wait for me, Tirzah. I will go down and see what is the matter, and come back to you.”\n\nHis voice was not steady as he wished. She clung closer to him.\n\nClearer, shriller, no longer a fancy, his mother’s cry arose. He hesitated no longer.\n\n“Come, then, let us go.”\n\nThe terrace or gallery at the foot of the steps was crowded with soldiers. Other soldiers with drawn swords ran in and out of the chambers. At one place a number of women on their knees clung to each other or prayed for mercy. Apart from them, one with torn garments, and long hair streaming over her face, struggled to tear loose from a man all whose strength was tasked to keep his hold. Her cries were shrillest of all; cutting through the clamor, they had risen distinguishably to the roof. To her Judah sprang—his steps were long and swift, almost a winged flight—“Mother, mother!” he shouted. She stretched her hands towards him; but when almost touching them he was seized and forced aside. Then he heard some one say, speaking loudly,\n\n“That is he!”\n\nJudah looked, and saw—Messala.\n\n“What, the assassin—that?” said a tall man, in legionary armor of beautiful finish. “Why, he is but a boy.”\n\n“Gods!” replied Messala, not forgetting his drawl. “A new philosophy! What would Seneca say to the proposition that a man must be old before he can hate enough to kill? You have him; and that is his mother; yonder his sister. You have the whole family.”\n\nFor love of them, Judah forgot his quarrel.\n\n“Help them, O my Messala! Remember our childhood and help them. I—Judah—pray you.”\n\nMessala affected not to hear.\n\n“I cannot be of further use to you,” he said to the officer. “There is richer entertainment in the street. Down Eros, up Mars!”\n\nWith the last words he disappeared. Judah understood him, and, in the bitterness of his soul, prayed to Heaven.\n\n“In the hour of thy vengeance, O Lord,” he said, “be mine the hand to put it upon him!”\n\nBy great exertion, he drew nearer the officer.\n\n“O sir, the woman you hear is my mother. Spare her, spare my sister yonder. God is just, he will give you mercy for mercy.”\n\nThe man appeared to be moved.\n\n“To the Tower with the women!” he shouted, “but do them no harm. I will demand them of you.” Then to those holding Judah, he said, “Get cords, and bind his hands, and take him to the street. His punishment is reserved.”\n\nThe mother was carried away. The little Tirzah, in her home attire, stupefied with fear, went passively with her keepers. Judah gave each of them a last look, and covered his face with his hands, as if to possess himself of the scene fadelessly. He may have shed tears, though no one saw them.\n\nThere took place in him then what may be justly called the wonder of life. The thoughtful reader of these pages has ere this discerned enough to know that the young Jew in disposition was gentle even to womanliness—a result that seldom fails the habit of loving and being loved. The circumstances through which he had come had made no call upon the harsher elements of his nature, if such he had. At times he had felt the stir and impulses of ambition, but they had been like the formless dreams of a child walking by the sea and gazing at the coming and going of stately ships. But now, if we can imagine an idol, sensible of the worship it was accustomed to, dashed suddenly from its altar, and lying amidst the wreck of its little world of love, an idea may be had of what had befallen the young Ben-Hur, and of its effect upon his being. Yet there was no sign, nothing to indicate that he had undergone a change, except that when he raised his head, and held his arms out to be bound, the bend of the Cupid’s bow had vanished from his lips. In that instant he had put off childhood and become a man.\n\nA trumpet sounded in the court-yard. With the cessation of the call, the gallery was cleared of the soldiery; many of whom, as they dared not appear in the ranks with visible plunder in their hands, flung what they had upon the floor, until it was strewn with articles of richest virtu. When Judah descended, the formation was complete, and the officer waiting to see his last order executed.\n\nThe mother, daughter, and entire household were led out of the north gate, the ruins of which choked the passageway. The cries of the domestics, some of whom had been born in the house, were most pitiable. When, finally, the horses and all the dumb tenantry of the place were driven past him, Judah began to comprehend the scope of the procurator’s vengeance. The very structure was devoted. Far as the order was possible of execution, nothing living was to be left within its walls. If in Judea there were others desperate enough to think of assassinating a Roman governor, the story of what befell the princely family of Hur would be a warning to them, while the ruin of the habitation would keep the story alive.\n\nThe officer waited outside while a detail of men temporarily restored the gate.\n\nIn the street the fighting had almost ceased. Upon the houses here and there clouds of dust told where the struggle was yet prolonged. The cohort was, for the most part, standing at rest, its splendor, like its ranks, in nowise diminished. Borne past the point of care for himself, Judah had heart for nothing in view but the prisoners, among whom he looked in vain for his mother and Tirzah.\n\nSuddenly, from the earth where she had been lying, a woman arose and started swiftly back to the gate. Some of the guards reached out to seize her, and a great shout followed their failure. She ran to Judah, and, dropping down, clasped his knees, the coarse black hair powdered with dust veiling her eyes.\n\n“O Amrah, good Amrah,” he said to her, “God help you; I cannot.”\n\nShe could not speak.\n\nHe bent down, and whispered, “Live, Amrah, for Tirzah and my mother. They will come back, and—”\n\nA soldier drew her away; whereupon she sprang up and rushed through the gateway and passage into the vacant court-yard.\n\n“Let her go,” the officer shouted. “We will seal the house, and she will starve.”\n\nThe men resumed their work, and, when it was finished there, passed round to the west side. That gate was also secured, after which the palace of the Hurs was lost to use.\n\nThe cohort at length marched back to the Tower, where the procurator stayed to recover from his hurts and dispose of his prisoners. On the tenth day following, he visited the Market-place.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nNext day a detachment of legionaries went to the desolated palace, and, closing the gates permanently, plastered the corners with wax, and at the sides nailed a notice in Latin:\n\nIn the haughty Roman idea, the sententious announcement was thought sufficient for the purpose—and it was.\n\nThe day after that again, about noon, a decurion with his command of ten horsemen approached Nazareth from the south—that is, from the direction of Jerusalem. The place was then a straggling village, perched on a hill-side, and so insignificant that its one street was little more than a path well beaten by the coming and going of flocks and herds. The great plain of Esdraelon crept close to it on the south, and from the height on the west a view could be had of the shores of the Mediterranean, the region beyond the Jordan, and Hermon. The valley below, and the country on every side, were given to gardens, vineyards, orchards, and pasturage. Groves of palm-trees Orientalized the landscape. The houses, in irregular assemblage, were of the humbler class—square, one-story, flat-roofed, and covered with bright-green vines. The drought that had burned the hills of Judea to a crisp, brown and lifeless, stopped at the boundary-line of Galilee.\n\nA trumpet, sounded when the cavalcade drew near the village, had a magical effect upon the inhabitants. The gates and front doors cast forth groups eager to be the first to catch the meaning of a visitation so unusual.\n\nNazareth, it must be remembered, was not only aside from any great highway, but within the sway of Judas of Gamala; wherefore it should not be hard to imagine the feelings with which the legionaries were received. But when they were up and traversing the street, the duty that occupied them became apparent, and then fear and hatred were lost in curiosity, under the impulse of which the people, knowing there must be a halt at the well in the northeastern part of the town, quit their gates and doors, and closed in after the procession.\n\nA prisoner whom the horsemen were guarding was the object of curiosity. He was afoot, bareheaded, half naked, his hands bound behind him. A thong fixed to his wrists was looped over the neck of a horse. The dust went with the party when in movement, wrapping him in yellow fog, sometimes in a dense cloud. He drooped forward, footsore and faint. The villagers could see he was young.\n\nAt the well the decurion halted, and, with most of the men, dismounted. The prisoner sank down in the dust of the road, stupefied, and asking nothing: apparently he was in the last stage of exhaustion. Seeing, when they came near, that he was but a boy, the villagers would have helped him had they dared.\n\nIn the midst of their perplexity, and while the pitchers were passing among the soldiers, a man was descried coming down the road from Sepphoris. At sight of him a woman cried out, “Look! Yonder comes the carpenter. Now we will hear something.”\n\nThe person spoken of was quite venerable in appearance. Thin white locks fell below the edge of his full turban, and a mass of still whiter beard flowed down the front of his coarse gray gown. He came slowly, for, in addition to his age, he carried some tools—an axe, a saw, and a drawing-knife, all very rude and heavy—and had evidently travelled some distance without rest.\n\nHe stopped close by to survey the assemblage.\n\n“O Rabbi, good Rabbi Joseph!” cried a woman, running to him. “Here is a prisoner; come ask the soldiers about him, that we may know who he is, and what he has done, and what they are going to do with him.”\n\nThe rabbi’s face remained stolid; he glanced at the prisoner, however, and presently went to the officer.\n\n“The peace of the Lord be with you!” he said, with unbending gravity.\n\n“And that of the gods with you,” the decurion replied.\n\n“Are you from Jerusalem?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Your prisoner is young.”\n\n“In years, yes.”\n\n“May I ask what he has done?”\n\n“He is an assassin.”\n\nThe people repeated the word in astonishment, but Rabbi Joseph pursued his inquest.\n\n“Is he a son of Israel?”\n\n“He is a Jew,” said the Roman, dryly.\n\nThe wavering pity of the bystanders came back.\n\n“I know nothing of your tribes, but can speak of his family,” the speaker continued. “You may have heard of a prince of Jerusalem named Hur—Ben-Hur, they called him. He lived in Herod’s day.”\n\n“I have seen him,” Joseph said.\n\n“Well, this is his son.”\n\nExclamations became general, and the decurion hastened to stop them.\n\n“In the streets of Jerusalem, day before yesterday, he nearly killed the noble Gratus by flinging a tile upon his head from the roof of a palace—his father’s, I believe.”\n\nThere was a pause in the conversation during which the Nazarenes gazed at the young Ben-Hur as at a wild beast.\n\n“No.”\n“He is under sentence.”\n\n“Yes—the galleys for life.”\n\n“The Lord help him!” said Joseph, for once moved out of his stolidity.\n\nThereupon a youth who came up with Joseph, but had stood behind him unobserved, laid down an axe he had been carrying, and, going to the great stone standing by the well, took from it a pitcher of water. The action was so quiet that before the guard could interfere, had they been disposed to do so, he was stooping over the prisoner, and offering him drink.\n\nThe hand laid kindly upon his shoulder awoke the unfortunate Judah, and, looking up, he saw a face he never forgot—the face of a boy about his own age, shaded by locks of yellowish bright chestnut hair; a face lighted by dark-blue eyes, at the time so soft, so appealing, so full of love and holy purpose, that they had all the power of command and will. The spirit of the Jew, hardened though it was by days and nights of suffering, and so embittered by wrong that its dreams of revenge took in all the world, melted under the stranger’s look, and became as a child’s. He put his lips to the pitcher, and drank long and deep. Not a word was said to him, nor did he say a word.\n\nWhen the draught was finished, the hand that had been resting upon the sufferer’s shoulder was placed upon his head, and stayed there in the dusty locks time enough to say a blessing; the stranger then returned the pitcher to its place on the stone, and, taking his axe again, went back to Rabbi Joseph. All eyes went with him, the decurion’s as well as those of the villagers.\n\nThis was the end of the scene at the well. When the men had drunk, and the horses, the march was resumed. But the temper of the decurion was not as it had been; he himself raised the prisoner from the dust, and helped him on a horse behind a soldier. The Nazarenes went to their houses—among them Rabbi Joseph and his apprentice.\n\nAnd so, for the first time, Judah and the son of Mary met and parted.\n\n# BOOK THIRD\n\n“Cleopatra.... Our size of sorrow, Proportion’d to our cause, must be as great As that which makes it.— Enter, below, DIOMEDES. How now? is he dead?\n\nDiomedes. His death’s upon him, but not dead.” Antony and Cleopatra (act iv., sc. xiii.). CHAPTER I\n\nThe city of Misenum gave name to the promontory which it crowned, a few miles southwest of Naples. An account of ruins is all that remains of it now; yet in the year of our Lord 24—to which it is desirable to advance the reader—the place was one of the most important on the western coast of Italy.[1]\n\n[1] The Roman government, it will be remembered, had two harbors in which great fleets were constantly kept—Ravenna and Misenum.\n\nIn the year mentioned, a traveller coming to the promontory to regale himself with the view there offered, would have mounted a wall, and, with the city at his back, looked over the bay of Neapolis, as charming then as now; and then, as now, he would have seen the matchless shore, the smoking cone, the sky and waves so softly, deeply blue, Ischia here and Capri yonder; from one to the other and back again, through the purpled air, his gaze would have sported; at last—for the eyes do weary of the beautiful as the palate with sweets—at last it would have dropped upon a spectacle which the modern tourist cannot see—half the reserve navy of Rome astir or at anchor below him. Thus regarded, Misenum was a very proper place for three masters to meet, and at leisure parcel the world among them.\n\nIn the old time, moreover, there was a gateway in the wall at a certain point fronting the sea—an empty gateway forming the outlet of a street which, after the exit, stretched itself, in the form of a broad mole, out many stadia into the waves.\n\nThe watchman on the wall above the gateway was disturbed, one cool September morning, by a party coming down the street in noisy conversation. He gave one look, then settled into his drowse again.\n\nThere were twenty or thirty persons in the party, of whom the greater number were slaves with torches, which flamed little and smoked much, leaving on the air the perfume of the Indian nard. The masters walked in advance arm-in-arm. One of them, apparently fifty years old, slightly bald, and wearing over his scant locks a crown of laurel, seemed, from the attentions paid him, the central object of some affectionate ceremony. They all sported ample togas of white wool broadly bordered with purple. A glance had sufficed the watchman. He knew, without question, they were of high rank, and escorting a friend to ship after a night of festivity. Further explanation will be found in the conversation they carried on.\n\n“No, my Quintus,” said one, speaking to him with the crown, “it is ill of Fortune to take thee from us so soon. Only yesterday thou didst return from the seas beyond the Pillars. Why, thou hast not even got back thy land legs.”\n\n“By Castor! if a man may swear a woman’s oath,” said another, somewhat worse of wine, “let us not lament. Our Quintus is but going to find what he lost last night. Dice on a rolling ship is not dice on shore—eh, Quintus?”\n\n“Abuse not Fortune!” exclaimed a third. “She is not blind or fickle. At Antium, where our Arrius questions her, she answers him with nods, and at sea she abides with him holding the rudder. She takes him from us, but does she not always give him back with a new victory?”\n\n“The Greeks are taking him away,” another broke in. “Let us abuse them, not the gods. In learning to trade they forgot how to fight.”\n\nWith these words, the party passed the gateway, and came upon the mole, with the bay before them beautiful in the morning light. To the veteran sailor the plash of the waves was like a greeting. He drew a long breath, as if the perfume of the water were sweeter than that of the nard, and held his hand aloft.\n\n“My gifts were at Praeneste, not Antium—and see! Wind from the west. Thanks, O Fortune, my mother!” he said, earnestly.\n\nThe friends all repeated the exclamation, and the slaves waved their torches.\n\n“She comes—yonder!” he continued, pointing to a galley outside the mole. “What need has a sailor for other mistress? Is your Lucrece more graceful, my Caius?”\n\nHe gazed at the coming ship, and justified his pride. A white sail was bent to the low mast, and the oars dipped, arose, poised a moment, then dipped again, with wing-like action, and in perfect time.\n\n“Yes, spare the gods,” he said, soberly, his eyes fixed upon the vessel. “They send us opportunities. Ours the fault if we fail. And as for the Greeks, you forget, O my Lentulus, the pirates I am going to punish are Greeks. One victory over them is of more account than a hundred over the Africans.”\n\n“Then thy way is to the Aegean?”\n\nThe sailor’s eyes were full of his ship.\n\n“What grace, what freedom! A bird hath not less care for the fretting of the waves. See!” he said, but almost immediately added, “Thy pardon, my Lentulus. I am going to the Aegean; and as my departure is so near, I will tell the occasion—only keep it under the rose. I would not that you abuse the duumvir when next you meet him. He is my friend. The trade between Greece and Alexandria, as ye may have heard, is hardly inferior to that between Alexandria and Rome. The people in that part of the world forgot to celebrate the Cerealia, and Triptolemus paid them with a harvest not worth the gathering. At all events, the trade is so grown that it will not brook interruption a day. Ye may also have heard of the Chersonesan pirates, nested up in the Euxine; none bolder, by the Bacchae! Yesterday word came to Rome that, with a fleet, they had rowed down the Bosphorus, sunk the galleys off Byzantium and Chalcedon, swept the Propontis, and, still unsated, burst through into the Aegean. The corn-merchants who have ships in the East Mediterranean are frightened. They had audience with the Emperor himself, and from Ravenna there go to-day a hundred galleys, and from Misenum”—he paused as if to pique the curiosity of his friends, and ended with an emphatic—“one.”\n\n“Happy Quintus! We congratulate thee!”\n\n“The preferment forerunneth promotion. We salute thee duumvir; nothing less.”\n\n“Quintus Arrius, the duumvir, hath a better sound than Quintus Arrius, the tribune.”\n\nIn such manner they showered him with congratulations.\n\n“I am glad with the rest,” said the bibulous friend, “very glad; but I must be practical, O my duumvir; and not until I know if promotion will help thee to knowledge of the tesserae will I have an opinion as to whether the gods mean thee ill or good in this—this business.”\n\n“Thanks, many thanks!” Arrius replied, speaking to them collectively. “Had ye but lanterns, I would say ye were augurs. Perpol! I will go further, and show what master diviners ye are! See—and read.”\n\nFrom the folds of his toga he drew a roll of paper, and passed it to them, saying, “Received while at table last night from—Sejanus.”\n\nThe name was already a great one in the Roman world; great, and not so infamous as it afterwards became.\n\n“Sejanus!” they exclaimed, with one voice, closing in to read what the minister had written.\n\n“Sejanus to C. Caecilius Rufus, Duumvir.\n\n“ROME, XIX. Kal. Sept.\n\n“Cæsar hath good report of Quintus Arrius, the tribune. In particular he hath heard of his valor, manifested in the western seas, insomuch that it is his will that the said Quintus be transferred instantly to the East.\n\n“It is our Cæsar’s will, further, that you cause a hundred triremes, of the first class, and full appointment, to be despatched without delay against the pirates who have appeared in the Aegean, and that Quintus be sent to command the fleet so despatched.\n\n“Details are thine, my Caecilius.\n\n“The necessity is urgent, as thou will be advised by the reports enclosed for thy perusal and the information of the said Quintus.\n\nArrius gave little heed to the reading. As the ship drew more plainly out of the perspective, she became more and more an attraction to him. The look with which he watched her was that of an enthusiast. At length he tossed the loosened folds of his toga in the air; in reply to the signal, over the aplustre, or fan-like fixture at the stern of the vessel, a scarlet flag was displayed; while several sailors appeared upon the bulwarks, and swung themselves hand over hand up the ropes to the antenna, or yard, and furled the sail. The bow was put round, and the time of the oars increased one half; so that at racing speed she bore down directly towards him and his friends. He observed the manoeuvring with a perceptible brightening of the eyes. Her instant answer to the rudder, and the steadiness with which she kept her course, were especially noticeable as virtues to be relied upon in action.\n\n“By the Nymphae!” said one of the friends, giving back the roll, “we may not longer say our friend will be great; he is already great. Our love will now have famous things to feed upon. What more hast thou for us?”\n\n“Nothing more,” Arrius replied. “What ye have of the affair is by this time old news in Rome, especially between the palace and the Forum. The duumvir is discreet; what I am to do, where go to find my fleet, he will tell on the ship, where a sealed package is waiting me. If, however, ye have offerings for any of the altars to-day, pray the gods for a friend plying oar and sail somewhere in the direction of Sicily. But she is here, and will come to,” he said, reverting to the vessel. “I have interest in her masters; they will sail and fight with me. It is not an easy thing to lay ship side on a shore like this; so let us judge their training and skill.”\n\n“What, is she new to thee?”\n\n“I never saw her before; and, as yet, I know not if she will bring me one acquaintance.”\n\n“Is that well?”\n\n“It matters but little. We of the sea come to know each other quickly; our loves, like our hates, are born of sudden dangers.”\n\nThe vessel was of the class called naves liburnicae—long, narrow, low in the water, and modelled for speed and quick manoeuvre. The bow was beautiful. A jet of water spun from its foot as she came on, sprinkling all the prow, which rose in graceful curvature twice a man’s stature above the plane of the deck. Upon the bending of the sides were figures of Triton blowing shells. Below the bow, fixed to the keel, and projecting forward under the water-line, was the rostrum, or beak, a device of solid wood, reinforced and armed with iron, in action used as a ram. A stout molding extended from the bow the full length of the ship’s sides, defining the bulwarks, which were tastefully crenelated; below the molding, in three rows, each covered with a cap or shield of bull-hide, were the holes in which the oars were worked—sixty on the right, sixty on the left. In further ornamentation, caducei leaned against the lofty prow. Two immense ropes passing across the bow marked the number of anchors stowed on the foredeck.\n\nThe simplicity of the upper works declared the oars the chief dependence of the crew. A mast, set a little forward of midship, was held by fore and back stays and shrouds fixed to rings on the inner side of the bulwarks. The tackle was that required for the management of one great square sail and the yard to which it was hung. Above the bulwarks the deck was visible.\n\nSave the sailors who had reefed the sail, and yet lingered on the yard, but one man was to be seen by the party on the mole, and he stood by the prow helmeted and with a shield.\n\nThe hundred and twenty oaken blades, kept white and shining by pumice and the constant wash of the waves, rose and fell as if operated by the same hand, and drove the galley forward with a speed rivalling that of a modern steamer.\n\nSo rapidly, and apparently, so rashly, did she come that the landsmen of the tribune’s party were alarmed. Suddenly the man by the prow raised his hand with a peculiar gesture; whereupon all the oars flew up, poised a moment in air, then fell straight down. The water boiled and bubbled about them; the galley shook in every timber, and stopped as if scared. Another gesture of the hand, and again the oars arose, feathered, and fell; but this time those on the right, dropping towards the stern, pushed forward; while those on the left, dropping towards the bow, pulled backwards. Three times the oars thus pushed and pulled against each other. Round to the right the ship swung as upon a pivot; then, caught by the wind, she settled gently broadside to the mole.\n\nThe movement brought the stern to view, with all its garniture—Tritons like those at the bow; name in large raised letters; the rudder at the side; the elevated platform upon which the helmsman sat, a stately figure in full armor, his hand upon the rudder-rope; and the aplustre, high, gilt, carved, and bent over the helmsman like a great runcinate leaf.\n\nIn the midst of the rounding-to, a trumpet was blown brief and shrill, and from the hatchways out poured the marines, all in superb equipment, brazen helms, burnished shields and javelins. While the fighting-men thus went to quarters as for action, the sailors proper climbed the shrouds and perched themselves along the yard. The officers and musicians took their posts. There was no shouting or needless noise. When the oars touched the mole, a bridge was sent out from the helmsman’s deck. Then the tribune turned to his party and said, with a gravity he had not before shown:\n\n“Duty now, O my friends.”\n\nHe took the chaplet from his head and gave it to the dice-player.\n\n“Take thou the myrtle, O favorite of the tesserae!” he said. “If I return, I will seek my sestertii again; if I am not victor, I will not return. Hang the crown in thy atrium.”\n\nTo the company he opened his arms, and they came one by one and received his parting embrace.\n\n“The gods go with thee, O Quintus!” they said.\n\n“Farewell,” he replied.\n\nTo the slaves waving their torches he waved his hand; then he turned to the waiting ship, beautiful with ordered ranks and crested helms, and shields and javelins. As he stepped upon the bridge, the trumpets sounded, and over the aplustre rose the vexillum purpureum, or pennant of a commander of a fleet.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nThe tribune, standing upon the helmsman’s deck with the order of the duumvir open in his hand, spoke to the chief of the rowers.[1]\n\n[1] Called hortator.\n\n“What force hast thou?”\n\n“Of oarsmen, two hundred and fifty-two; ten supernumeraries.\n\n“Making reliefs of—”\n\n“Eighty-four.”\n\n“And thy habit?”\n\n“It has been to take off and put on every two hours.”\n\nThe tribune mused a moment.\n\n“The division is hard, and I will reform it, but not now. The oars may not rest day or night.”\n\nThen to the sailing-master he said,\n\n“The wind is fair. Let the sail help the oars.”\n\nWhen the two thus addressed were gone, he turned to the chief pilot.[2]\n\n[2] Called rector.\n\n“What service hast thou had?”\n\n“Two-and-thirty years.”\n\n“In what seas chiefly?”\n\n“Between our Rome and the East.”\n\n“Thou art the man I would have chosen.”\n\nThe tribune looked at his orders again.\n\n“Past the Camponellan cape, the course will be to Messina. Beyond that, follow the bend of the Calabrian shore till Melito is on thy left, then— Knowest thou the stars that govern in the Ionian Sea?”\n\n“I know them well.”\n\n“Then from Melito course eastward for Cythera. The gods willing, I will not anchor until in the Bay of Antemona. The duty is urgent. I rely upon thee.”\n\nA prudent man was Arrius—prudent, and of the class which, while enriching the altars at Praeneste and Antium, was of opinion, nevertheless, that the favor of the blind goddess depended more upon the votary’s care and judgment than upon his gifts and vows. All night as master of the feast he had sat at table drinking and playing; yet the odor of the sea returned him to the mood of the sailor, and he would not rest until he knew his ship. Knowledge leaves no room for chances. Having begun with the chief of the rowers, the sailing-master, and the pilot, in company with the other officers—the commander of the marines, the keeper of the stores, the master of the machines, the overseer of the kitchen or fires—he passed through the several quarters. Nothing escaped his inspection. When he was through, of the community crowded within the narrow walls he alone knew perfectly all there was of material preparation for the voyage and its possible incidents; and, finding the preparation complete, there was left him but one thing further—thorough knowledge of the personnel of his command. As this was the most delicate and difficult part of his task, requiring much time, he set about it his own way.\n\nAt noon that day the galley was skimming the sea off Paestum. The wind was yet from the west, filling the sail to the master’s content. The watches had been established. On the foredeck the altar had been set and sprinkled with salt and barley, and before it the tribune had offered solemn prayers to Jove and to Neptune and all the Oceanidæ, and, with vows, poured the wine and burned the incense. And now, the better to study his men, he was seated in the great cabin, a very martial figure.\n\nThe cabin, it should be stated, was the central compartment of the galley, in extent quite sixty-five by thirty feet, and lighted by three broad hatchways. A row of stanchions ran from end to end, supporting the roof, and near the centre the mast was visible, all bristling with axes and spears and javelins. To each hatchway there were double stairs descending right and left, with a pivotal arrangement at the top to allow the lower ends to be hitched to the ceiling; and, as these were now raised, the compartment had the appearance of a skylighted hall.\n\nThe reader will understand readily that this was the heart of the ship, the home of all aboard—eating-room, sleeping-chamber, field of exercise, lounging-place off duty—uses made possible by the laws which reduced life there to minute details and a routine relentless as death.\n\nAt the after-end of the cabin there was a platform, reached by several steps. Upon it the chief of the rowers sat; in front of him a sounding-table, upon which, with a gavel, he beat time for the oarsmen; at his right a clepsydra, or water-clock, to measure the reliefs and watches. Above him, on a higher platform, well guarded by gilded railing, the tribune had his quarters, overlooking everything, and furnished with a couch, a table, and a cathedra, or chair, cushioned, and with arms and high back—articles which the imperial dispensation permitted of the utmost elegance.\n\nThus at ease, lounging in the great chair, swaying with the motion of the vessel, the military cloak half draping his tunic, sword in belt, Arrius kept watchful eye over his command, and was as closely watched by them. He saw critically everything in view, but dwelt longest upon the rowers. The reader would doubtless have done the same: only he would have looked with much sympathy, while, as is the habit with masters, the tribune’s mind ran forward of what he saw, inquiring for results.\n\nThe spectacle was simple enough of itself. Along the sides of the cabin, fixed to the ship’s timbers, were what at first appeared to be three rows of benches; a closer view, however, showed them a succession of rising banks, in each of which the second bench was behind and above the first one, and the third above and behind the second. To accommodate the sixty rowers on a side, the space devoted to them permitted nineteen banks separated by intervals of one yard, with a twentieth bank divided so that what would have been its upper seat or bench was directly above the lower seat of the first bank. The arrangement gave each rower when at work ample room, if he timed his movements with those of his associates, the principle being that of soldiers marching with cadenced step in close order. The arrangement also allowed a multiplication of banks, limited only by the length of the galley.\n\nAs to the rowers, those upon the first and second benches sat, while those upon the third, having longer oars to work, were suffered to stand. The oars were loaded with lead in the handles, and near the point of balance hung to pliable thongs, making possible the delicate touch called feathering, but, at the same time, increasing the need of skill, since an eccentric wave might at any moment catch a heedless fellow and hurl him from his seat. Each oar-hole was a vent through which the laborer opposite it had his plenty of sweet air. Light streamed down upon him from the grating which formed the floor of the passage between the deck and the bulwark over his head. In some respects, therefore, the condition of the men might have been much worse. Still, it must not be imagined that there was any pleasantness in their lives. Communication between them was not allowed. Day after day they filled their places without speech; in hours of labor they could not see each other’s faces; their short respites were given to sleep and the snatching of food. They never laughed; no one ever heard one of them sing. What is the use of tongues when a sigh or a groan will tell all men feel while, perforce, they think in silence? Existence with the poor wretches was like a stream under ground sweeping slowly, laboriously on to its outlet, wherever that might chance to be.\n\nO Son of Mary! The sword has now a heart—and thine the glory! So now; but, in the days of which we are writing, for captivity there was drudgery on walls, and in the streets and mines, and the galleys both of war and commerce were insatiable. When Druilius won the first sea-fight for his country, Romans plied the oars, and the glory was to the rower not less than the marine. These benches which now we are trying to see as they were testified to the change come with conquest, and illustrated both the policy and the prowess of Rome. Nearly all the nations had sons there, mostly prisoners of war, chosen for their brawn and endurance. In one place a Briton; before him a Libyan; behind him a Crimean. Elsewhere a Scythian, a Gaul, and a Thebasite. Roman convicts cast down to consort with Goths and Longobardi, Jews, Ethiopians, and barbarians from the shores of Maeotis. Here an Athenian, there a red-haired savage from Hibernia, yonder blue-eyed giants of the Cimbri.\n\nIn the labor of the rowers there was not enough art to give occupation to their minds, rude and simple as they were. The reach forward, the pull, the feathering the blade, the dip, were all there was of it; motions most perfect when most automatic. Even the care forced upon them by the sea outside grew in time to be a thing instinctive rather than of thought. So, as the result of long service, the poor wretches became imbruted—patient, spiritless, obedient—creatures of vast muscle and exhausted intellects, who lived upon recollections generally few but dear, and at last lowered into the semi-conscious alchemic state wherein misery turns to habit, and the soul takes on incredible endurance.\n\nFrom right to left, hour after hour, the tribune, swaying in his easy-chair, turned with thought of everything rather than the wretchedness of the slaves upon the benches. Their motions, precise, and exactly the same on both sides of the vessel, after a while became monotonous; and then he amused himself singling out individuals. With his stylus he made note of objections, thinking, if all went well, he would find among the pirates of whom he was in search better men for the places.\n\nThere was no need of keeping the proper names of the slaves brought to the galleys as to their graves; so, for convenience, they were usually identified by the numerals painted upon the benches to which they were assigned. As the sharp eyes of the great man moved from seat to seat on either hand, they came at last to number sixty, which, as has been said, belonged properly to the last bank on the left-hand side, but, wanting room aft, had been fixed above the first bench of the first bank. There they rested.\n\nThe bench of number sixty was slightly above the level of the platform, and but a few feet away. The light glinting through the grating over his head gave the rower fairly to the tribune’s view—erect, and, like all his fellows, naked, except a cincture about the loins. There were, however, some points in his favor. He was very young, not more than twenty. Furthermore, Arrius was not merely given to dice; he was a connoisseur of men physically, and when ashore indulged a habit of visiting the gymnasia to see and admire the most famous athletae. From some professor, doubtless, he had caught the idea that strength was as much of the quality as the quantity of the muscle, while superiority in performance required a certain mind as well as strength. Having adopted the doctrine, like most men with a hobby, he was always looking for illustrations to support it.\n\nThe reader may well believe that while the tribune, in the search for the perfect, was often called upon to stop and study, he was seldom perfectly satisfied—in fact, very seldom held as long as on this occasion.\n\nIn the beginning of each movement of the oar, the rower’s body and face were brought into profile view from the platform; the movement ended with the body reversed, and in a pushing posture. The grace and ease of the action at first suggested a doubt of the honesty of the effort put forth; but it was speedily dismissed; the firmness with which the oar was held while in the reach forward, its bending under the push, were proofs of the force applied; not that only, they as certainly proved the rower’s art, and put the critic in the great arm-chair in search of the combination of strength and cleverness which was the central idea of his theory.\n\nIn course of the study, Arrius observed the subject’s youth; wholly unconscious of tenderness on that account, he also observed that he seemed of good height, and that his limbs, upper and nether, were singularly perfect. The arms, perhaps, were too long, but the objection was well hidden under a mass of muscle, which, in some movements, swelled and knotted like kinking cords. Every rib in the round body was discernible; yet the leanness was the healthful reduction so strained after in the palaestrae. And altogether there was in the rower’s action a certain harmony which, besides addressing itself to the tribune’s theory, stimulated both his curiosity and general interest.\n\nVery soon he found himself waiting to catch a view of the man’s face in full. The head was shapely, and balanced upon a neck broad at the base, but of exceeding pliancy and grace. The features in profile were of Oriental outline, and of that delicacy of expression which has always been thought a sign of blood and sensitive spirit. With these observations, the tribune’s interest in the subject deepened.\n\n“By the gods,” he said to himself, “the fellow impresses me! He promises well. I will know more of him.”\n\nDirectly the tribune caught the view he wished—the rower turned and looked at him.\n\n“A Jew! and a boy!”\n\nUnder the gaze then fixed steadily upon him, the large eyes of the slave grew larger—the blood surged to his very brows—the blade lingered in his hands. But instantly, with an angry crash, down fell the gavel of the hortator. The rower started, withdrew his face from the inquisitor, and, as if personally chidden, dropped the oar half feathered. When he glanced again at the tribune, he was vastly more astonished—he was met with a kindly smile.\n\nMeantime the galley entered the Straits of Messina, and, skimming past the city of that name, was after a while turned eastward, leaving the cloud over Ætna in the sky astern.\n\nOften as Arrius resumed to his platform in the cabin he returned to study the rower, and he kept saying to himself, “The fellow hath a spirit. A Jew is not a barbarian. I will know more of him.”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe fourth day out, and the Astroea—so the galley was named—speeding through the Ionian Sea. The sky was clear, and the wind blew as if bearing the good-will of all the gods.\n\nAs it was possible to overtake the fleet before reaching the bay east of the island of Cythera, designated for assemblage, Arrius, somewhat impatient, spent much time on deck. He took note diligently of matters pertaining to his ship, and as a rule was well pleased. In the cabin, swinging in the great chair, his thought continually reverted to the rower on number sixty.\n\n“Knowest thou the man just come from yon bench?” he at length asked of the hortator.\n\nA relief was going on at the moment.\n\n“From number sixty?” returned the chief.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe chief looked sharply at the rower then going forward.\n\n“As thou knowest,” he replied “the ship is but a month from the maker’s hand, and the men are as new to me as the ship.”\n\n“He is a Jew,” Arrius remarked, thoughtfully.\n\n“The noble Quintus is shrewd.”\n\n“He is very young,” Arrius continued.\n\n“But our best rower,” said the other. “I have seen his oar bend almost to breaking.”\n\n“Of what disposition is he?”\n\n“He is obedient; further I know not. Once he made request of me.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“He wished me to change him alternately from the right to the left.”\n\n“Did he give a reason?”\n\n“He had observed that the men who are confined to one side become misshapen. He also said that some day of storm or battle there might be sudden need to change him, and he might then be unserviceable.”\n\n“Perpol! The idea is new. What else hast thou observed of him?”\n\n“He is cleanly above his companions.”\n\n“In that he is Roman,” said Arrius, approvingly. “Have you nothing of his history?”\n\n“Not a word.”\n\nThe tribune reflected awhile, and turned to go to his own seat.\n\n“If I should be on deck when his time is up,” he paused to say, “send him to me. Let him come alone.”\n\nAbout two hours later Arrius stood under the aplustre of the galley; in the mood of one who, seeing himself carried swiftly towards an event of mighty import, has nothing to do but wait—the mood in which philosophy vests an even-minded man with the utmost calm, and is ever so serviceable. The pilot sat with a hand upon the rope by which the rudder paddles, one on each side of the vessel, were managed. In the shade of the sail some sailors lay asleep, and up on the yard there was a lookout. Lifting his eyes from the solarium set under the aplustre for reference in keeping the course, Arrius beheld the rower approaching.\n\n“The chief called thee the noble Arrius, and said it was thy will that I should seek thee here. I have come.”\n\nArrius surveyed the figure, tall, sinewy, glistening in the sun, and tinted by the rich red blood within—surveyed it admiringly, and with a thought of the arena; yet the manner was not without effect upon him: there was in the voice a suggestion of life at least partly spent under refining influences; the eyes were clear and open, and more curious than defiant. To the shrewd, demanding, masterful glance bent upon it, the face gave back nothing to mar its youthful comeliness—nothing of accusation or sullenness or menace, only the signs which a great sorrow long borne imprints, as time mellows the surface of pictures. In tacit acknowledgment of the effect, the Roman spoke as an older man to a younger, not as a master to a slave.\n\n“The hortator tells me thou art his best rower.”\n\n“The hortator is very kind,” the rower answered.\n\n“Hast thou seen much service?”\n\n“About three years.”\n\n“At the oars?”\n\n“I cannot recall a day of rest from them.”\n\n“The labor is hard; few men bear it a year without breaking, and thou—thou art but a boy.”\n\n“The noble Arrius forgets that the spirit hath much to do with endurance. By its help the weak sometimes thrive, when the strong perish.”\n\n“From thy speech, thou art a Jew.”\n\n“My ancestors further back than the first Roman were Hebrews.”\n\n“The stubborn pride of thy race is not lost in thee,” said Arrius, observing a flush upon the rower’s face.\n\n“Pride is never so loud as when in chains.”\n\n“What cause hast thou for pride?”\n\n“That I am a Jew.”\nArrius smiled.\n\n“I have not been to Jerusalem,” he said; “but I have heard of its princes. I knew one of them. He was a merchant, and sailed the seas. He was fit to have been a king. Of what degree art thou?”\n\n“I must answer thee from the bench of a galley. I am of the degree of slaves. My father was a prince of Jerusalem, and, as a merchant, he sailed the seas. He was known and honored in the guest-chamber of the great Augustus.”\n\n“His name?”\n\n“Ithamar, of the house of Hur.”\n\nThe tribune raised his hand in astonishment.\n\n“A son of Hur—thou?”\n\nAfter a silence, he asked,\n\n“What brought thee here?”\n\nJudah lowered his head, and his breast labored hard. When his feelings were sufficiently mastered, he looked the tribune in the face, and answered,\n\n“I was accused of attempting to assassinate Valerius Gratus, the procurator.”\n\n“Thou!” cried Arrius, yet more amazed, and retreating a step. “Thou that assassin! All Rome rang with the story. It came to my ship in the river by Lodinum.”\n\nThe two regarded each other silently.\n\n“I thought the family of Hur blotted from the earth,” said Arrius, speaking first.\n\nA flood of tender recollections carried the young man’s pride away; tears shone upon his cheeks.\n\n“Mother—mother! And my little Tirzah! Where are they? O tribune, noble tribune, if thou knowest anything of them”—he clasped his hands in appeal—“tell me all thou knowest. Tell me if they are living—if living, where are they? and in what condition? Oh, I pray thee, tell me!”\n\nHe drew nearer Arrius, so near that his hands touched the cloak where it dropped from the latter’s folded arms.\n\n“The horrible day is three years gone,” he continued—“three years, O tribune, and every hour a whole lifetime of misery—a lifetime in a bottomless pit with death, and no relief but in labor—and in all that time not a word from any one, not a whisper. Oh, if, in being forgotten, we could only forget! If only I could hide from that scene—my sister torn from me, my mother’s last look! I have felt the plague’s breath, and the shock of ships in battle; I have heard the tempest lashing the sea, and laughed, though others prayed: death would have been a riddance. Bend the oar—yes, in the strain of mighty effort trying to escape the haunting of what that day occurred. Think what little will help me. Tell me they are dead, if no more, for happy they cannot be while I am lost. I have heard them call me in the night; I have seen them on the water walking. Oh, never anything so true as my mother’s love! And Tirzah—her breath was as the breath of white lilies. She was the youngest branch of the palm—so fresh, so tender, so graceful, so beautiful! She made my day all morning. She came and went in music. And mine was the hand that laid them low! I—”\n\n“Dost thou admit thy guilt?” asked Arrius, sternly.\n\nThe change that came upon Ben-Hur was wonderful to see, it was so instant and extreme. The voice sharpened; the hands arose tight-clenched; every fibre thrilled; his eyes inflamed.\n\n“Thou hast heard of the God of my fathers,” he said; “of the infinite Jehovah. By his truth and almightiness, and by the love with which he hath followed Israel from the beginning, I swear I am innocent!”\n\nThe tribune was much moved.\n\n“O noble Roman!” continued Ben-Hur, “give me a little faith, and, into my darkness, deeper darkening every day, send a light!”\n\nArrius turned away, and walked the deck.\n\n“Didst thou not have a trial?” he asked, stopping suddenly.\n“No!”\n\nThe Roman raised his head, surprised.\n\n“No trial—no witnesses! Who passed judgment upon thee?”\n\nRomans, it should be remembered, were at no time such lovers of the law and its forms as in the ages of their decay.\n\n“They bound me with cords, and dragged me to a vault in the Tower. I saw no one. No one spoke to me. Next day soldiers took me to the seaside. I have been a galley-slave ever since.”\n\n“What couldst thou have proven?”\n\n“I was a boy, too young to be a conspirator. Gratus was a stranger to me. If I had meant to kill him, that was not the time or the place. He was riding in the midst of a legion, and it was broad day. I could not have escaped. I was of a class most friendly to Rome. My father had been distinguished for his services to the emperor. We had a great estate to lose. Ruin was certain to myself, my mother, my sister. I had no cause for malice, while every consideration—property, family, life, conscience, the Law—to a son of Israel as the breath of his nostrils—would have stayed my hand, though the foul intent had been ever so strong. I was not mad. Death was preferable to shame; and, believe me, I pray, it is so yet.”\n\n“Who was with thee when the blow was struck?”\n\n“I was on the house-top—my father’s house. Tirzah was with me—at my side—the soul of gentleness. Together we leaned over the parapet to see the legion pass. A tile gave way under my hand, and fell upon Gratus. I thought I had killed him. Ah, what horror I felt!”\n\n“Where was thy mother?”\n\n“In her chamber below.”\n\n“What became of her?”\n\nBen-Hur clenched his hands, and drew a breath like a gasp.\n\n“I do not know. I saw them drag her away—that is all I know. Out of the house they drove every living thing, even the dumb cattle, and they sealed the gates. The purpose was that she should not return. I, too, ask for her. Oh for one word! She, at least, was innocent. I can forgive—but I pray thy pardon, noble tribune! A slave like me should not talk of forgiveness or of revenge. I am bound to an oar for life.”\n\nArrius listened intently. He brought all his experience with slaves to his aid. If the feeling shown in this instance were assumed, the acting was perfect; on the other hand, if it were real, the Jew’s innocence might not be doubted; and if he were innocent, with what blind fury the power had been exercised! A whole family blotted out to atone an accident! The thought shocked him.\n\nThere is no wiser providence than that our occupations, however rude or bloody, cannot wear us out morally; that such qualities as justice and mercy, if they really possess us, continue to live on under them, like flowers under the snow. The tribune could be inexorable, else he had not been fit for the usages of his calling; he could also be just; and to excite his sense of wrong was to put him in the way to right the wrong. The crews of the ships in which he served came after a time to speak of him as the good tribune. Shrewd readers will not want a better definition of his character.\n\nIn this instance there were many circumstances certainly in the young man’s favor, and some to be supposed. Possibly Arrius knew Valerius Gratus without loving him. Possibly he had known the elder Hur. In the course of his appeal, Judah had asked him of that; and, as will be noticed, he had made no reply.\n\nFor once the tribune was at loss, and hesitated. His power was ample. He was monarch of the ship. His prepossessions all moved him to mercy. His faith was won. Yet, he said to himself, there was no haste—or, rather, there was haste to Cythera; the best rower could not then be spared; he would wait; he would learn more; he would at least be sure this was the prince Ben-Hur, and that he was of a right disposition. Ordinarily, slaves were liars.\n\n“It is enough,” he said aloud. “Go back to thy place.”\n\nBen-Hur bowed; looked once more into the master’s face, but saw nothing for hope. He turned away slowly, looked back, and said,\n\n“If thou dost think of me again, O tribune, let it not be lost in thy mind that I prayed thee only for word of my people—mother, sister.”\n\nHe moved on.\n\nArrius followed him with admiring eyes.\n\n“Perpol!” he thought. “With teaching, what a man for the arena! What a runner! Ye gods! what an arm for the sword or the cestus!—Stay!” he said aloud.\n\nBen-Hur stopped, and the tribune went to him.\n\n“If thou wert free, what wouldst thou do?”\n\n“The noble Arrius mocks me!” Judah said, with trembling lips.\n\n“No; by the gods, no!”\n\n“Then I will answer gladly. I would give myself to duty the first of life. I would know no other. I would know no rest until my mother and Tirzah were restored to home. I would give every day and hour to their happiness. I would wait upon them; never a slave more faithful. They have lost much, but, by the God of my fathers, I would find them more!”\n\nThe answer was unexpected by the Roman. For a moment he lost his purpose.\n\n“I spoke to thy ambition,” he said, recovering. “If thy mother and sister were dead, or not to be found, what wouldst thou do?”\n\nA distinct pallor overspread Ben-Hur’s face, and he looked over the sea. There was a struggle with some strong feeling; when it was conquered, he turned to the tribune.\n\n“What pursuit would I follow?” he asked.\n“Yes.”\n\n“Tribune, I will tell thee truly. Only the night before the dreadful day of which I have spoken, I obtained permission to be a soldier. I am of the same mind yet; and, as in all the earth there is but one school of war, thither I would go.”\n\n“The palaestra!” exclaimed Arrius.\n\n“No; a Roman camp.”\n\n“But thou must first acquaint thyself with the use of arms.”\n\nNow a master may never safely advise a slave. Arrius saw his indiscretion, and, in a breath, chilled his voice and manner.\n\n“Go now,” he said, “and do not build upon what has passed between us. Perhaps I do but play with thee. Or”—he looked away musingly—“or, if thou dost think of it with any hope, choose between the renown of a gladiator and the service of a soldier. The former may come of the favor of the emperor; there is no reward for thee in the latter. Thou art not a Roman. Go!”\n\nA short while after Ben-Hur was upon his bench again.\n\nA man’s task is always light if his heart is light. Handling the oar did not seem so toilsome to Judah. A hope had come to him, like a singing bird. He could hardly see the visitor or hear its song; that it was there, though, he knew; his feelings told him so. The caution of the tribune—“Perhaps I do but play with thee”—was dismissed often as it recurred to his mind. That he had been called by the great man and asked his story was the bread upon which he fed his hungry spirit. Surely something good would come of it. The light about his bench was clear and bright with promises, and he prayed.\n\n“O God! I am a true son of the Israel thou hast so loved! Help me, I pray thee!”\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nIn the Bay of Antemona, east of Cythera the island, the hundred galleys assembled. There the tribune gave one day to inspection. He sailed then to Naxos, the largest of the Cyclades, midway the coasts of Greece and Asia, like a great stone planted in the centre of a highway, from which he could challenge everything that passed; at the same time, he would be in position to go after the pirates instantly, whether they were in the Ægean or out on the Mediterranean.\n\nAs the fleet, in order, rowed in towards the mountain shores of the island, a galley was descried coming from the north. Arrius went to meet it. She proved to be a transport just from Byzantium, and from her commander he learned the particulars of which he stood in most need.\n\nThe pirates were from all the farther shores of the Euxine. Even Tanais, at the mouth of the river which was supposed to feed Palus Maeotis, was represented among them. Their preparations had been with the greatest secrecy. The first known of them was their appearance off the entrance to the Thracian Bosphorus, followed by the destruction of the fleet in station there. Thence to the outlet of the Hellespont everything afloat had fallen their prey. There were quite sixty galleys in the squadron, all well manned and supplied. A few were biremes, the rest stout triremes. A Greek was in command, and the pilots, said to be familiar with all the Eastern seas, were Greek. The plunder had been incalculable. The panic, consequently, was not on the sea alone; cities, with closed gates, sent their people nightly to the walls. Traffic had almost ceased.\n\nWhere were the pirates now?\n\nTo this question, of most interest to Arrius, he received answer.\n\nAfter sacking Hephaestia, on the island of Lemnos, the enemy had coursed across to the Thessalian group, and, by last account, disappeared in the gulfs between Euboea and Hellas.\n\nSuch were the tidings.\n\nThen the people of the island, drawn to the hill-tops by the rare spectacle of a hundred ships careering in united squadron, beheld the advance division suddenly turn to the north, and the others follow, wheeling upon the same point like cavalry in a column. News of the piratical descent had reached them, and now, watching the white sails until they faded from sight up between Rhene and Syros, the thoughtful among them took comfort, and were grateful. What Rome seized with strong hand she always defended: in return for their taxes, she gave them safety.\n\nThe tribune was more than pleased with the enemy’s movements; he was doubly thankful to Fortune. She had brought swift and sure intelligence, and had lured his foes into the waters where, of all others, destruction was most assured. He knew the havoc one galley could play in a broad sea like the Mediterranean, and the difficulty of finding and overhauling her; he knew, also, how those very circumstances would enhance the service and glory if, at one blow, he could put a finish to the whole piratical array.\n\nIf the reader will take a map of Greece and the Ægean, he will notice the island of Euboea lying along the classic coast like a rampart against Asia, leaving a channel between it and the continent quite a hundred and twenty miles in length, and scarcely an average of eight in width. The inlet on the north had admitted the fleet of Xerxes, and now it received the bold raiders from the Euxine. The towns along the Pelasgic and Meliac gulfs were rich and their plunder seductive. All things considered, therefore, Arrius judged that the robbers might be found somewhere below Thermopylae. Welcoming the chance, he resolved to enclose them north and south, to do which not an hour could be lost; even the fruits and wines and women of Naxos must be left behind. So he sailed away without stop or tack until, a little before nightfall, Mount Ocha was seen upreared against the sky, and the pilot reported the Euboean coast.\n\nAt a signal the fleet rested upon its oars. When the movement was resumed, Arrius led a division of fifty of the galleys, intending to take them up the channel, while another division, equally strong, turned their prows to the outer or seaward side of the island, with orders to make all haste to the upper inlet, and descend sweeping the waters.\n\nTo be sure, neither division was equal in number to the pirates; but each had advantages in compensation, among them, by no means least, a discipline impossible to a lawless horde, however brave. Besides, it was a shrewd count on the tribune’s side, if, peradventure, one should be defeated, the other would find the enemy shattered by his victory, and in condition to be easily overwhelmed.\n\nMeantime Ben-Hur kept his bench, relieved every six hours. The rest in the Bay of Antemona had freshened him, so that the oar was not troublesome, and the chief on the platform found no fault.\n\nPeople, generally, are not aware of the ease of mind there is in knowing where they are, and where they are going. The sensation of being lost is a keen distress; still worse is the feeling one has in driving blindly into unknown places. Custom had dulled the feeling with Ben-Hur, but only measurably. Pulling away hour after hour, sometimes days and nights together, sensible all the time that the galley was gliding swiftly along some of the many tracks of the broad sea, the longing to know where he was, and whither going, was always present with him; but now it seemed quickened by the hope which had come to new life in his breast since the interview with the tribune. The narrower the abiding-place happens to be, the more intense is the longing; and so he found. He seemed to hear every sound of the ship in labor, and listened to each one as if it were a voice come to tell him something; he looked to the grating overhead, and through it into the light of which so small a portion was his, expecting, he knew not what; and many times he caught himself on the point of yielding to the impulse to speak to the chief on the platform, than which no circumstance of battle would have astonished that dignitary more.\n\nIn his long service, by watching the shifting of the meager sunbeams upon the cabin floor when the ship was under way, he had come to know, generally, the quarter into which she was sailing. This, of course, was only of clear days like those good-fortune was sending the tribune. The experience had not failed him in the period succeeding the departure from Cythera. Thinking they were tending towards the old Judean country, he was sensitive to every variation from the course. With a pang, he had observed the sudden change northward which, as has been noticed, took place near Naxos: the cause, however, he could not even conjecture; for it must be remembered that, in common with his fellow-slaves, he knew nothing of the situation, and had no interest in the voyage. His place was at the oar, and he was held there inexorably, whether at anchor or under sail. Once only in three years had he been permitted an outlook from the deck. The occasion we have seen. He had no idea that, following the vessel he was helping drive, there was a great squadron close at hand and in beautiful order; no more did he know the object of which it was in pursuit.\n\nWhen the sun, going down, withdrew his last ray from the cabin, the galley still held northward. Night fell, yet Ben-Hur could discern no change. About that time the smell of incense floated down the gangways from the deck.\n\n“The tribune is at the altar,” he thought. “Can it be we are going into battle?”\n\nHe became observant.\n\nNow he had been in many battles without having seen one. From his bench he had heard them above and about him, until he was familiar with all their notes, almost as a singer with a song. So, too, he had become acquainted with many of the preliminaries of an engagement, of which, with a Roman as well as a Greek, the most invariable was the sacrifice to the gods. The rites were the same as those performed at the beginning of a voyage, and to him, when noticed, they were always an admonition.\n\nA battle, it should be observed, possessed for him and his fellow-slaves of the oar an interest unlike that of the sailor and marine; it came, not of the danger encountered but of the fact that defeat, if survived, might bring an alteration of condition—possibly freedom—at least a change of masters, which might be for the better.\n\nIn good time the lanterns were lighted and hung by the stairs, and the tribune came down from the deck. At his word the marines put on their armor. At his word again, the machines were looked to, and spears, javelins, and arrows, in great sheaves, brought and laid upon the floor, together with jars of inflammable oil, and baskets of cotton balls wound loose like the wicking of candles. And when, finally, Ben-Hur saw the tribune mount his platform and don his armor, and get his helmet and shield out, the meaning of the preparations might not be any longer doubted, and he made ready for the last ignominy of his service.\n\nTo every bench, as a fixture, there was a chain with heavy anklets. These the hortator proceeded to lock upon the oarsmen, going from number to number, leaving no choice but to obey, and, in event of disaster, no possibility of escape.\n\nIn the cabin, then, a silence fell, broken, at first, only by the sough of the oars turning in the leathern cases. Every man upon the benches felt the shame, Ben-Hur more keenly than his companions. He would have put it away at any price. Soon the clanking of the fetters notified him of the progress the chief was making in his round. He would come to him in turn; but would not the tribune interpose for him?\n\nThe thought may be set down to vanity or selfishness, as the reader pleases; it certainly, at that moment, took possession of Ben-Hur. He believed the Roman would interpose; anyhow, the circumstance would test the man’s feelings. If, intent upon the battle, he would but think of him, it would be proof of his opinion formed—proof that he had been tacitly promoted above his associates in misery—such proof as would justify hope.\n\nBen-Hur waited anxiously. The interval seemed like an age. At every turn of the oar he looked towards the tribune, who, his simple preparations made, lay down upon the couch and composed himself to rest; whereupon number sixty chid himself, and laughed grimly, and resolved not to look that way again.\n\nThe hortator approached. Now he was at number one—the rattle of the iron links sounded horribly. At last number sixty! Calm from despair, Ben-Hur held his oar at poise, and gave his foot to the officer. Then the tribune stirred—sat up—beckoned to the chief.\n\nA strong revulsion seized the Jew. From the hortator, the great man glanced at him; and when he dropped his oar all the section of the ship on his side seemed aglow. He heard nothing of what was said; enough that the chain hung idly from its staple in the bench, and that the chief, going to his seat, began to beat the sounding-board. The notes of the gavel were never so like music. With his breast against the leaded handle, he pushed with all his might—pushed until the shaft bent as if about to break.\n\nThe chief went to the tribune, and, smiling, pointed to number sixty.\n\n“What strength!” he said.\n\n“And what spirit!” the tribune answered. “Perpol! He is better without the irons. Put them on him no more.”\n\nSo saying, he stretched himself upon the couch again.\n\nThe ship sailed on hour after hour under the oars in water scarcely rippled by the wind. And the people not on duty slept, Arrius in his place, the marines on the floor.\n\nOnce—twice—Ben-Hur was relieved; but he could not sleep. Three years of night, and through the darkness a sunbeam at last! At sea adrift and lost, and now land! Dead so long, and, lo! the thrill and stir of resurrection. Sleep was not for such an hour. Hope deals with the future; now and the past are but servants that wait on her with impulse and suggestive circumstance. Starting from the favor of the tribune, she carried him forward indefinitely. The wonder is, not that things so purely imaginative as the results she points us to can make us so happy, but that we can receive them as so real. They must be as gorgeous poppies under the influence of which, under the crimson and purple and gold, reason lies down the while, and is not. Sorrows assuaged, home and the fortunes of his house restored; mother and sister in his arms once more—such were the central ideas which made him happier that moment than he had ever been. That he was rushing, as on wings, into horrible battle had, for the time, nothing to do with his thoughts. The things thus in hope were unmixed with doubts—they WERE. Hence his joy so full, so perfect, there was no room in his heart for revenge. Messala, Gratus, Rome, and all the bitter, passionate memories connected with them, were as dead plagues—miasms of the earth above which he floated, far and safe, listening to singing stars.\n\nThe deeper darkness before the dawn was upon the waters, and all things going well with the Astroea, when a man, descending from the deck, walked swiftly to the platform where the tribune slept, and awoke him. Arrius arose, put on his helmet, sword, and shield, and went to the commander of the marines.\n\n“The pirates are close by. Up and ready!” he said, and passed to the stairs, calm, confident, insomuch that one might have thought, “Happy fellow! Apicius has set a feast for him.”\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nEvery soul aboard, even the ship, awoke. Officers went to their quarters. The marines took arms, and were led out, looking in all respects like legionaries. Sheaves of arrows and armfuls of javelins were carried on deck. By the central stairs the oil-tanks and fire-balls were set ready for use. Additional lanterns were lighted. Buckets were filled with water. The rowers in relief assembled under guard in front of the chief. As Providence would have it, Ben-Hur was one of the latter. Overhead he heard the muffled noises of the final preparations—of the sailors furling sail, spreading the nettings, unslinging the machines, and hanging the armor of bull-hide over the side. Presently quiet settled about the galley again; quiet full of vague dread and expectation, which, interpreted, means READY.\n\nAt a signal passed down from the deck, and communicated to the hortator by a petty officer stationed on the stairs, all at once the oars stopped.\n\nWhat did it mean?\n\nOf the hundred and twenty slaves chained to the benches, not one but asked himself the question. They were without incentive. Patriotism, love of honor, sense of duty, brought them no inspiration. They felt the thrill common to men rushed helpless and blind into danger. It may be supposed the dullest of them, poising his oar, thought of all that might happen, yet could promise himself nothing; for victory would but rivet his chains the firmer, while the chances of the ship were his; sinking or on fire, he was doomed to her fate.\n\nOf the situation without they might not ask. And who were the enemy? And what if they were friends, brethren, countrymen? The reader, carrying the suggestion forward, will see the necessity which governed the Roman when, in such emergencies, he locked the hapless wretches to their seats.\n\nThere was little time, however, for such thought with them. A sound like the rowing of galleys astern attracted Ben-Hur, and the Astroea rocked as if in the midst of countering waves. The idea of a fleet at hand broke upon him—a fleet in manoeuvre—forming probably for attack. His blood started with the fancy.\n\nAnother signal came down from the deck. The oars dipped, and the galley started imperceptibly. No sound from without, none from within, yet each man in the cabin instinctively poised himself for a shock; the very ship seemed to catch the sense, and hold its breath, and go crouched tiger-like.\n\nIn such a situation time is inappreciable; so that Ben-Hur could form no judgment of distance gone. At last there was a sound of trumpets on deck, full, clear, long blown. The chief beat the sounding-board until it rang; the rowers reached forward full length, and, deepening the dip of their oars, pulled suddenly with all their united force. The galley, quivering in every timber, answered with a leap. Other trumpets joined in the clamor—all from the rear, none forward—from the latter quarter only a rising sound of voices in tumult heard briefly. There was a mighty blow; the rowers in front of the chief’s platform reeled, some of them fell; the ship bounded back, recovered, and rushed on more irresistibly than before. Shrill and high arose the shrieks of men in terror; over the blare of trumpets, and the grind and crash of the collision, they arose; then under his feet, under the keel, pounding, rumbling, breaking to pieces, drowning, Ben-Hur felt something overridden. The men about him looked at each other afraid. A shout of triumph from the deck—the beak of the Roman had won! But who were they whom the sea had drunk? Of what tongue, from what land were they?\n\nNo pause, no stay! Forward rushed the Astroea; and, as it went, some sailors ran down, and plunging the cotton balls into the oil-tanks, tossed them dripping to comrades at the head of the stairs: fire was to be added to other horrors of the combat.\n\nDirectly the galley heeled over so far that the oarsmen on the uppermost side with difficulty kept their benches. Again the hearty Roman cheer, and with it despairing shrieks. An opposing vessel, caught by the grappling-hooks of the great crane swinging from the prow, was being lifted into the air that it might be dropped and sunk.\n\nThe shouting increased on the right hand and on the left; before, behind, swelled an indescribable clamor. Occasionally there was a crash, followed by sudden peals of fright, telling of other ships ridden down, and their crews drowned in the vortexes.\n\nNor was the fight all on one side. Now and then a Roman in armor was borne down the hatchway, and laid bleeding, sometimes dying, on the floor.\n\nSometimes, also, puffs of smoke, blended with steam, and foul with the scent of roasting human flesh, poured into the cabin, turning the dimming light into yellow murk. Gasping for breath the while, Ben-Hur knew they were passing through the cloud of a ship on fire, and burning up with the rowers chained to the benches.\n\nThe Astroea all this time was in motion. Suddenly she stopped. The oars forward were dashed from the hands of the rowers, and the rowers from their benches. On deck, then, a furious trampling, and on the sides a grinding of ships afoul of each other. For the first time the beating of the gavel was lost in the uproar. Men sank on the floor in fear or looked about seeking a hiding-place. In the midst of the panic a body plunged or was pitched headlong down the hatchway, falling near Ben-Hur. He beheld the half-naked carcass, a mass of hair blackening the face, and under it a shield of bull-hide and wicker-work—a barbarian from the white-skinned nations of the North whom death had robbed of plunder and revenge. How came he there? An iron hand had snatched him from the opposing deck—no, the Astroea had been boarded! The Romans were fighting on their own deck? A chill smote the young Jew: Arrius was hard pressed—he might be defending his own life. If he should be slain! God of Abraham forefend! The hopes and dreams so lately come, were they only hopes and dreams? Mother and sister—house—home—Holy Land—was he not to see them, after all? The tumult thundered above him; he looked around; in the cabin all was confusion—the rowers on the benches paralyzed; men running blindly hither and thither; only the chief on his seat imperturbable, vainly beating the sounding-board, and waiting the orders of the tribune—in the red murk illustrating the matchless discipline which had won the world.\n\nThe example had a good effect upon Ben-Hur. He controlled himself enough to think. Honor and duty bound the Roman to the platform; but what had he to do with such motives then? The bench was a thing to run from; while, if he were to die a slave, who would be the better of the sacrifice? With him living was duty, if not honor. His life belonged to his people. They arose before him never more real: he saw them, their arms outstretched; he heard them imploring him. And he would go to them. He started—stopped. Alas! a Roman judgment held him in doom. While it endured, escape would be profitless. In the wide, wide earth there was no place in which he would be safe from the imperial demand; upon the land none, nor upon the sea. Whereas he required freedom according to the forms of law, so only could he abide in Judea and execute the filial purpose to which he would devote himself: in other land he would not live. Dear God! How he had waited and watched and prayed for such a release! And how it had been delayed! But at last he had seen it in the promise of the tribune. What else the great man’s meaning? And if the benefactor so belated should now be slain! The dead come not back to redeem the pledges of the living. It should not be—Arrius should not die. At least, better perish with him than survive a galley-slave.\n\nOnce more Ben-Hur looked around. Upon the roof of the cabin the battle yet beat; against the sides the hostile vessels yet crushed and grided. On the benches, the slaves struggled to tear loose from their chains, and, finding their efforts vain, howled like madmen; the guards had gone upstairs; discipline was out, panic in. No, the chief kept his chair, unchanged, calm as ever—except the gavel, weaponless. Vainly with his clangor he filled the lulls in the din. Ben-Hur gave him a last look, then broke away—not in flight, but to seek the tribune.\n\nA very short space lay between him and the stairs of the hatchway aft. He took it with a leap, and was half-way up the steps—up far enough to catch a glimpse of the sky blood-red with fire, of the ships alongside, of the sea covered with ships and wrecks, of the fight closed in about the pilot’s quarter, the assailants many, the defenders few—when suddenly his foothold was knocked away, and he pitched backward. The floor, when he reached it, seemed to be lifting itself and breaking to pieces; then, in a twinkling, the whole after-part of the hull broke asunder, and, as if it had all the time been lying in wait, the sea, hissing and foaming, leaped in, and all became darkness and surging water to Ben-Hur.\n\nIt cannot be said that the young Jew helped himself in this stress. Besides his usual strength, he had the indefinite extra force which nature keeps in reserve for just such perils to life; yet the darkness, and the whirl and roar of water, stupefied him. Even the holding his breath was involuntary.\n\nThe influx of the flood tossed him like a log forward into the cabin, where he would have drowned but for the refluence of the sinking motion. As it was, fathoms under the surface the hollow mass vomited him forth, and he arose along with the loosed debris. In the act of rising, he clutched something, and held to it. The time he was under seemed an age longer than it really was; at last he gained the top; with a great gasp he filled his lungs afresh, and, tossing the water from his hair and eyes, climbed higher upon the plank he held, and looked about him.\n\nDeath had pursued him closely under the waves; he found it waiting for him when he was risen—waiting multiform.\n\nSmoke lay upon the sea like a semitransparent fog, through which here and there shone cores of intense brilliance. A quick intelligence told him that they were ships on fire. The battle was yet on; nor could he say who was victor. Within the radius of his vision now and then ships passed, shooting shadows athwart lights. Out of the dun clouds farther on he caught the crash of other ships colliding. The danger, however, was closer at hand. When the Astroea went down, her deck, it will be recollected, held her own crew, and the crews of the two galleys which had attacked her at the same time, all of whom were ingulfed. Many of them came to the surface together, and on the same plank or support of whatever kind continued the combat, begun possibly in the vortex fathoms down. Writhing and twisting in deadly embrace, sometimes striking with sword or javelin, they kept the sea around them in agitation, at one place inky-black, at another aflame with fiery reflections. With their struggles he had nothing to do; they were all his enemies: not one of them but would kill him for the plank upon which he floated. He made haste to get away.\n\nAbout that time he heard oars in quickest movement, and beheld a galley coming down upon him. The tall prow seemed doubly tall, and the red light playing upon its gilt and carving gave it an appearance of snaky life. Under its foot the water churned to flying foam.\n\nHe struck out, pushing the plank, which was very broad and unmanageable. Seconds were precious—half a second might save or lose him. In the crisis of the effort, up from the sea, within arm’s reach, a helmet shot like a gleam of gold. Next came two hands with fingers extended—large hands were they, and strong—their hold once fixed, might not be loosed. Ben-Hur swerved from them appalled. Up rose the helmet and the head it encased—then two arms, which began to beat the water wildly—the head turned back, and gave the face to the light. The mouth gaping wide; the eyes open, but sightless, and the bloodless pallor of a drowning man—never anything more ghastly! Yet he gave a cry of joy at the sight, and as the face was going under again, he caught the sufferer by the chain which passed from the helmet beneath the chin, and drew him to the plank.\n\nThe man was Arrius, the tribune.\n\nFor a while the water foamed and eddied violently about Ben-Hur, taxing all his strength to hold to the support and at the same time keep the Roman’s head above the surface. The galley had passed, leaving the two barely outside the stroke of its oars. Right through the floating men, over heads helmeted as well as heads bare, she drove, in her wake nothing but the sea sparkling with fire. A muffled crash, succeeded by a great outcry, made the rescuer look again from his charge. A certain savage pleasure touched his heart—the Astroea was avenged.\n\nAfter that the battle moved on. Resistance turned to flight. But who were the victors? Ben-Hur was sensible how much his freedom and the life of the tribune depended upon that event. He pushed the plank under the latter until it floated him, after which all his care was to keep him there. The dawn came slowly. He watched its growing hopefully, yet sometimes afraid. Would it bring the Romans or the pirates? If the pirates, his charge was lost.\n\nAt last morning broke in full, the air without a breath. Off to the left he saw the land, too far to think of attempting to make it. Here and there men were adrift like himself. In spots the sea was blackened by charred and sometimes smoking fragments. A galley up a long way was lying to with a torn sail hanging from the tilted yard, and the oars all idle. Still farther away he could discern moving specks, which he thought might be ships in flight or pursuit, or they might be white birds a-wing.\n\nAn hour passed thus. His anxiety increased. If relief came not speedily, Arrius would die. Sometimes he seemed already dead, he lay so still. He took the helmet off, and then, with greater difficulty, the cuirass; the heart he found fluttering. He took hope at the sign, and held on. There was nothing to do but wait, and, after the manner of his people, pray.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nThe throes of recovery from drowning are more painful than the drowning. These Arrius passed through, and, at length, to Ben-Hur’s delight, reached the point of speech.\n\nGradually, from incoherent questions as to where he was, and by whom and how he had been saved, he reverted to the battle. The doubt of the victory stimulated his faculties to full return, a result aided not a little by a long rest—such as could be had on their frail support. After a while he became talkative.\n\n“Our rescue, I see, depends upon the result of the fight. I see also what thou hast done for me. To speak fairly, thou hast saved my life at the risk of thy own. I make the acknowledgment broadly; and, whatever cometh, thou hast my thanks. More than that, if fortune doth but serve me kindly, and we get well out of this peril, I will do thee such favor as becometh a Roman who hath power and opportunity to prove his gratitude. Yet, yet it is to be seen if, with thy good intent, thou hast really done me a kindness; or, rather, speaking to thy good-will”—he hesitated—“I would exact of thee a promise to do me, in a certain event, the greatest favor one man can do another—and of that let me have thy pledge now.”\n\n“If the thing be not forbidden, I will do it,” Ben-Hur replied.\n\nArrius rested again.\n\n“Art thou, indeed, a son of Hur, the Jew?” he next asked.\n\n“It is as I have said.”\n\n“I knew thy father—”\n\nJudah drew himself nearer, for the tribune’s voice was weak—he drew nearer, and listened eagerly—at last he thought to hear of home.\n\n“I knew him, and loved him,” Arrius continued.\n\nThere was another pause, during which something diverted the speaker’s thought.\n\n“It cannot be,” he proceeded, “that thou, a son of his, hast not heard of Cato and Brutus. They were very great men, and never as great as in death. In their dying, they left this law—A Roman may not survive his good-fortune. Art thou listening?”\n“I hear.”\n\n“It is a custom of gentlemen in Rome to wear a ring. There is one on my hand. Take it now.”\n\nHe held the hand to Judah, who did as he asked.\n\n“Now put it on thine own hand.”\n\nBen-Hur did so.\n\n“The trinket hath its uses,” said Arrius next. “I have property and money. I am accounted rich even in Rome. I have no family. Show the ring to my freedman, who hath control in my absence; you will find him in a villa near Misenum. Tell him how it came to thee, and ask anything, or all he may have; he will not refuse the demand. If I live, I will do better by thee. I will make thee free, and restore thee to thy home and people; or thou mayst give thyself to the pursuit that pleaseth thee most. Dost thou hear?”\n\n“I could not choose but hear.”\n\n“Then pledge me. By the gods—”\n\n“Nay, good tribune, I am a Jew.”\n\n“By thy God, then, or in the form most sacred to those of thy faith—pledge me to do what I tell thee now, and as I tell thee; I am waiting, let me have thy promise.”\n\n“Noble Arrius, I am warned by thy manner to expect something of gravest concern. Tell me thy wish first.”\n\n“Wilt thou promise then?”\n\n“That were to give the pledge, and— Blessed be the God of my fathers! yonder cometh a ship!”\n\n“In what direction?”\n\n“From the north.”\n\n“Canst thou tell her nationality by outward signs?”\n\n“No. My service hath been at the oars.”\n\n“Hath she a flag?”\n\n“I cannot see one.”\n\nArrius remained quiet some time, apparently in deep reflection.\n\n“Does the ship hold this way yet?” he at length asked.\n\n“Still this way.”\n\n“Look for the flag now.”\n\n“She hath none.”\n\n“Nor any other sign?”\n\n“She hath a sail set, and is of three banks, and cometh swiftly—that is all I can say of her.”\n\n“A Roman in triumph would have out many flags. She must be an enemy. Hear now,” said Arrius, becoming grave again, “hear, while yet I may speak. If the galley be a pirate, thy life is safe; they may not give thee freedom; they may put thee to the oar again; but they will not kill thee. On the other hand, I—”\n\nThe tribune faltered.\n\n“Perpol!” he continued, resolutely. “I am too old to submit to dishonor. In Rome, let them tell how Quintus Arrius, as became a Roman tribune, went down with his ship in the midst of the foe. This is what I would have thee do. If the galley prove a pirate, push me from the plank and drown me. Dost thou hear? Swear thou wilt do it.”\n\n“I will not swear,” said Ben-Hur, firmly; “neither will I do the deed. The Law, which is to me most binding, O tribune, would make me answerable for thy life. Take back the ring”—he took the seal from his finger—“take it back, and all thy promises of favor in the event of delivery from this peril. The judgment which sent me to the oar for life made me a slave, yet I am not a slave; no more am I thy freedman. I am a son of Israel, and this moment, at least, my own master. Take back the ring.”\n\nArrius remained passive.\n\n“Thou wilt not?” Judah continued. “Not in anger, then, nor in any despite, but to free myself from a hateful obligation, I will give thy gift to the sea. See, O tribune!”\n\nHe tossed the ring away. Arrius heard the splash where it struck and sank, though he did not look.\n\n“Thou hast done a foolish thing,” he said; “foolish for one placed as thou art. I am not dependent upon thee for death. Life is a thread I can break without thy help; and, if I do, what will become of thee? Men determined on death prefer it at the hands of others, for the reason that the soul which Plato giveth us is rebellious at the thought of self-destruction; that is all. If the ship be a pirate, I will escape from the world. My mind is fixed. I am a Roman. Success and honor are all in all. Yet I would have served thee; thou wouldst not. The ring was the only witness of my will available in this situation. We are both lost. I will die regretting the victory and glory wrested from me; thou wilt live to die a little later, mourning the pious duties undone because of this folly. I pity thee.”\n\nBen-Hur saw the consequences of his act more distinctly than before, yet he did not falter.\n\n“In the three years of my servitude, O tribune, thou wert the first to look upon me kindly. No, no! There was another.” The voice dropped, the eyes became humid, and he saw plainly as if it were then before him the face of the boy who helped him to a drink by the old well at Nazareth. “At least,” he proceeded, “thou wert the first to ask me who I was; and if, when I reached out and caught thee, blind and sinking the last time, I, too, had thought of the many ways in which thou couldst be useful to me in my wretchedness, still the act was not all selfish; this I pray you to believe. Moreover, seeing as God giveth me to know, the ends I dream of are to be wrought by fair means alone. As a thing of conscience, I would rather die with thee than be thy slayer. My mind is firmly set as thine; though thou wert to offer me all Rome, O tribune, and it belonged to thee to make the gift good, I would not kill thee. Thy Cato and Brutus were as little children compared to the Hebrew whose law a Jew must obey.”\n\n“But my request. Hast—”\n\n“Thy command would be of more weight, and that would not move me. I have said.”\n\nBoth became silent, waiting.\n\nBen-Hur looked often at the coming ship. Arrius rested with closed eyes, indifferent.\n\n“Art thou sure she is an enemy?” Ben-Hur asked.\n\n“I think so,” was the reply.\n\n“She stops, and puts a boat over the side.”\n\n“Dost thou see her flag?”\n\n“Is there no other sign by which she may be known if Roman?”\n\n“If Roman, she hath a helmet over the mast’s top.”\n\n“Then be of cheer. I see the helmet.”\n\nStill Arrius was not assured.\n\n“The men in the small boat are taking in the people afloat. Pirates are not humane.”\n\n“They may need rowers,” Arrius replied, recurring, possibly, to times when he had made rescues for the purpose.\n\nBen-Hur was very watchful of the actions of the strangers.\n\n“The ship moves off,” he said.\n\n“Whither?”\n\n“Over on our right there is a galley which I take to be deserted. The new-comer heads towards it. Now she is alongside. Now she is sending men aboard.”\n\nThen Arrius opened his eyes and threw off his calm.\n\n“Thank thou thy God,” he said to Ben-Hur, after a look at the galleys, “thank thou thy God, as I do my many gods. A pirate would sink, not save, yon ship. By the act and the helmet on the mast I know a Roman. The victory is mine. Fortune hath not deserted me. We are saved. Wave thy hand—call to them—bring them quickly. I shall be duumvir, and thou! I knew thy father, and loved him. He was a prince indeed. He taught me a Jew was not a barbarian. I will take thee with me. I will make thee my son. Give thy God thanks, and call the sailors. Haste! The pursuit must be kept. Not a robber shall escape. Hasten them!”\n\nJudah raised himself upon the plank, and waved his hand, and called with all his might; at last he drew the attention of the sailors in the small boat, and they were speedily taken up.\n\nArrius was received on the galley with all the honors due a hero so the favorite of Fortune. Upon a couch on the deck he heard the particulars of the conclusion of the fight. When the survivors afloat upon the water were all saved and the prize secured, he spread his flag of commandant anew, and hurried northward to rejoin the fleet and perfect the victory. In due time the fifty vessels coming down the channel closed in upon the fugitive pirates, and crushed them utterly; not one escaped. To swell the tribune’s glory, twenty galleys of the enemy were captured.\n\nUpon his return from the cruise, Arrius had warm welcome on the mole at Misenum. The young man attending him very early attracted the attention of his friends there; and to their questions as to who he was the tribune proceeded in the most affectionate manner to tell the story of his rescue and introduce the stranger, omitting carefully all that pertained to the latter’s previous history. At the end of the narrative, he called Ben-Hur to him, and said, with a hand resting affectionately upon his shoulder,\n\n“Good friends, this is my son and heir, who, as he is to take my property—if it be the will of the gods that I leave any—shall be known to you by my name. I pray you all to love him as you love me.”\n\nSpeedily as opportunity permitted, the adoption was formally perfected. And in such manner the brave Roman kept his faith with Ben-Hur, giving him happy introduction into the imperial world. The month succeeding Arrius’s return, the armilustrium was celebrated with the utmost magnificence in the theater of Scaurus. One side of the structure was taken up with military trophies; among which by far the most conspicuous and most admired were twenty prows, complemented by their corresponding aplustra, cut bodily from as many galleys; and over them, so as to be legible to the eighty thousand spectators in the seats, was this inscription:\n\nTAKEN FROM THE PIRATES IN THE GULF OF EURIPUS, BY QUINTUS ARRIUS, DUUMVIR.\n\n“Alva. Should the monarch prove unjust— And, at this time—\n\nQueen. Then I must wait for justice Until it come; and they are happiest far Whose consciences may calmly wait their right.” Schiller, Don Carlos (act iv., sc. xv.) CHAPTER I\n\nThe month to which we now come is July, the year that of our Lord 29, and the place Antioch, then Queen of the East, and next to Rome the strongest, if not the most populous, city in the world.\n\nThere is an opinion that the extravagance and dissoluteness of the age had their origin in Rome, and spread thence throughout the empire; that the great cities but reflected the manners of their mistress on the Tiber. This may be doubted. The reaction of the conquest would seem to have been upon the morals of the conqueror. In Greece she found a spring of corruption; so also in Egypt; and the student, having exhausted the subject, will close the books assured that the flow of the demoralizing river was from the East westwardly, and that this very city of Antioch, one of the oldest seats of Assyrian power and splendor, was a principal source of the deadly stream.\n\nA transport galley entered the mouth of the river Orontes from the blue waters of the sea. It was in the forenoon. The heat was great, yet all on board who could avail themselves of the privilege were on deck—Ben-Hur among others.\n\nThe five years had brought the young Jew to perfect manhood. Though the robe of white linen in which he was attired somewhat masked his form, his appearance was unusually attractive. For an hour and more he had occupied a seat in the shade of the sail, and in that time several fellow-passengers of his own nationality had tried to engage him in conversation, but without avail. His replies to their questions had been brief, though gravely courteous, and in the Latin tongue. The purity of his speech, his cultivated manners, his reticence, served to stimulate their curiosity the more. Such as observed him closely were struck by an incongruity between his demeanor, which had the ease and grace of a patrician, and certain points of his person. Thus his arms were disproportionately long; and when, to steady himself against the motion of the vessel, he took hold of anything near by, the size of his hands and their evident power compelled remark; so the wonder who and what he was mixed continually with a wish to know the particulars of his life. In other words, his air cannot be better described than as a notice—This man has a story to tell.\n\nThe galley, in coming, had stopped at one of the ports of Cyprus, and picked up a Hebrew of most respectable appearance, quiet, reserved, paternal. Ben-Hur ventured to ask him some questions; the replies won his confidence, and resulted finally in an extended conversation.\n\nIt chanced also that as the galley from Cyprus entered the receiving bay of the Orontes, two other vessels which had been sighted out in the sea met it and passed into the river at the same time; and as they did so both the strangers threw out small flags of brightest yellow. There was much conjecture as to the meaning of the signals. At length a passenger addressed himself to the respectable Hebrew for information upon the subject.\n\n“Yes, I know the meaning of the flags,” he replied; “they do not signify nationality—they are merely marks of ownership.”\n\n“Has the owner many ships?”\n\n“He has.”\n\n“You know him?”\n\n“I have dealt with him.”\n\nThe passengers looked at the speaker as if requesting him to go on. Ben-Hur listened with interest.\n\n“He lives in Antioch,” the Hebrew continued, in his quiet way. “That he is vastly rich has brought him into notice, and the talk about him is not always kind. There used to be in Jerusalem a prince of very ancient family named Hur.”\n\nJudah strove to be composed, yet his heart beat quicker.\n\n“The prince was a merchant, with a genius for business. He set on foot many enterprises, some reaching far East, others West. In the great cities he had branch houses. The one in Antioch was in charge of a man said by some to have been a family servant called Simonides, Greek in name, yet an Israelite. The master was drowned at sea. His business, however, went on, and was scarcely less prosperous. After a while misfortune overtook the family. The prince’s only son, nearly grown, tried to kill the procurator Gratus in one of the streets of Jerusalem. He failed by a narrow chance, and has not since been heard of. In fact, the Roman’s rage took in the whole house—not one of the name was left alive. Their palace was sealed up, and is now a rookery for pigeons; the estate was confiscated; everything that could be traced to the ownership of the Hurs was confiscated. The procurator cured his hurt with a golden salve.”\n\nThe passengers laughed.\n\n“You mean he kept the property,” said one of them.\n\n“They say so,” the Hebrew replied; “I am only telling a story as I received it. And, to go on, Simonides, who had been the prince’s agent here in Antioch, opened trade in a short time on his own account, and in a space incredibly brief became the master merchant of the city. In imitation of his master, he sent caravans to India; and on the sea at present he has galleys enough to make a royal fleet. They say nothing goes amiss with him. His camels do not die, except of old age; his ships never founder; if he throw a chip into the river, it will come back to him gold.”\n\n“How long has he been going on thus?”\n\n“Not ten years.”\n\n“He must have had a good start.”\n\n“Yes, they say the procurator took only the prince’s property ready at hand—his horses, cattle, houses, land, vessels, goods. The money could not be found, though there must have been vast sums of it. What became of it has been an unsolved mystery.”\n\n“Not to me,” said a passenger, with a sneer.\n\n“I understand you,” the Hebrew answered. “Others have had your idea. That it furnished old Simonides his start is a common belief. The procurator is of that opinion—or he has been—for twice in five years he has caught the merchant, and put him to torture.”\n\nJudah gripped the rope he was holding with crushing force.\n\n“It is said,” the narrator continued, “that there is not a sound bone in the man’s body. The last time I saw him he sat in a chair, a shapeless cripple, propped against cushions.”\n\n“So tortured!” exclaimed several listeners in a breath.\n\n“Disease could not have produced such a deformity. Still the suffering made no impression upon him. All he had was his lawfully, and he was making lawful use of it—that was the most they wrung from him. Now, however, he is past persecution. He has a license to trade signed by Tiberius himself.”\n\n“He paid roundly for it, I warrant.”\n\n“These ships are his,” the Hebrew continued, passing the remark. “It is a custom among his sailors to salute each other upon meeting by throwing out yellow flags, sight of which is as much as to say, ‘We have had a fortunate voyage.’”\n\nThe story ended there.\n\nWhen the transport was fairly in the channel of the river, Judah spoke to the Hebrew.\n\n“What was the name of the merchant’s master?”\n\n“Ben-Hur, Prince of Jerusalem.”\n\n“What became of the prince’s family?”\n\n“The boy was sent to the galleys. I may say he is dead. One year is the ordinary limit of life under that sentence. The widow and daughter have not been heard of; those who know what became of them will not speak. They died doubtless in the cells of one of the castles which spot the waysides of Judea.”\n\nJudah walked to the pilot’s quarter. So absorbed was he in thought that he scarcely noticed the shores of the river, which from sea to city were surpassingly beautiful with orchards of all the Syrian fruits and vines, clustered about villas rich as those of Neapolis. No more did he observe the vessels passing in an endless fleet, nor hear the singing and shouting of the sailors, some in labor, some in merriment. The sky was full of sunlight, lying in hazy warmth upon the land and the water; nowhere except over his life was there a shadow.\n\nOnce only he awoke to a momentary interest, and that was when some one pointed out the Grove of Daphne, discernible from a bend in the river.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nWhen the city came into view, the passengers were on deck, eager that nothing of the scene might escape them. The respectable Jew already introduced to the reader was the principal spokesman.\n\n“The river here runs to the west,” he said, in the way of general answer. “I remember when it washed the base of the walls; but as Roman subjects we have lived in peace, and, as always happens in such times, trade has had its will; now the whole river front is taken up with wharves and docks. Yonder”—the speaker pointed southward—“is Mount Casius, or, as these people love to call it, the Mountains of Orontes, looking across to its brother Amnus in the north; and between them lies the Plain of Antioch. Farther on are the Black Mountains, whence the Ducts of the Kings bring the purest water to wash the thirsty streets and people; yet they are forests in wilderness state, dense, and full of birds and beasts.”\n\n“Where is the lake?” one asked.\n\n“Over north there. You can take horse, if you wish to see it—or, better, a boat, for a tributary connects it with the river.”\n\n“The Grove of Daphne!” he said, to a third inquirer. “Nobody can describe it; only beware! It was begun by Apollo, and completed by him. He prefers it to Olympus. People go there for one look—just one—and never come away. They have a saying which tells it all—‘Better be a worm and feed on the mulberries of Daphne than a king’s guest.’”\n\n“Then you advise me to stay away from it?”\n\n“Not I! Go you will. Everybody goes, cynic philosopher, virile boy, women, and priests—all go. So sure am I of what you will do that I assume to advise you. Do not take quarters in the city—that will be loss of time; but go at once to the village in the edge of the grove. The way is through a garden, under the spray of fountains. The lovers of the god and his Penaean maid built the town; and in its porticos and paths and thousand retreats you will find characters and habits and sweets and kinds elsewhere impossible. But the wall of the city! there it is, the masterpiece of Xeraeus, the master of mural architecture.”\n\nAll eyes followed his pointing finger.\n\n“This part was raised by order of the first of the Seleucidæ. Three hundred years have made it part of the rock it rests upon.”\n\nThe defense justified the encomium. High, solid, and with many bold angles, it curved southwardly out of view.\n\n“On the top there are four hundred towers, each a reservoir of water,” the Hebrew continued. “Look now! Over the wall, tall as it is, see in the distance two hills, which you may know as the rival crests of Sulpius. The structure on the farthest one is the citadel, garrisoned all the year round by a Roman legion. Opposite it this way rises the Temple of Jupiter, and under that the front of the legate’s residence—a palace full of offices, and yet a fortress against which a mob would dash harmlessly as a south wind.”\n\nAt this point the sailors began taking in sail, whereupon the Hebrew exclaimed, heartily, “See! you who hate the sea, and you who have vows, get ready your curses and your prayers. The bridge yonder, over which the road to Seleucia is carried, marks the limit of navigation. What the ship unloads for further transit, the camel takes up there. Above the bridge begins the island upon which Calinicus built his new city, connecting it with five great viaducts so solid time has made no impression upon them, nor floods nor earthquakes. Of the main town, my friends, I have only to say you will be happier all your lives for having seen it.”\n\nAs he concluded, the ship turned and made slowly for her wharf under the wall, bringing even more fairly to view the life with which the river at that point was possessed. Finally, the lines were thrown, the oars shipped, and the voyage was done. Then Ben-Hur sought the respectable Hebrew.\n\n“Let me trouble you a moment before saying farewell.”\n\nThe man bowed assent.\n\n“Your story of the merchant has made me curious to see him. You called him Simonides?”\n\n“Yes. He is a Jew with a Greek name.”\n\n“Where is he to be found?”\n\nThe acquaintance gave a sharp look before he answered,\n\n“I may save you mortification. He is not a money-lender.”\n\n“Nor am I a money-borrower,” said Ben-Hur, smiling at the other’s shrewdness.\n\nThe man raised his head and considered an instant.\n\n“One would think,” he then replied, “that the richest merchant in Antioch would have a house for business corresponding to his wealth; but if you would find him in the day, follow the river to yon bridge, under which he quarters in a building that looks like a buttress of the wall. Before the door there is an immense landing, always covered with cargoes come and to go. The fleet that lies moored there is his. You cannot fail to find him.”\n\n“I give you thanks.”\n\n“The peace of our fathers go with you.”\n\n“And with you.”\n\nWith that they separated.\n\nTwo street-porters, loaded with his baggage, received Ben-Hur’s orders upon the wharf.\n\n“To the citadel,” he said; a direction which implied an official military connection.\n\nTwo great streets, cutting each other at right angles, divided the city into quarters. A curious and immense structure, called the Nymphaeum, arose at the foot of the one running north and south. When the porters turned south there, the new-comer, though fresh from Rome, was amazed at the magnificence of the avenue. On the right and left there were palaces, and between them extended indefinitely double colonnades of marble, leaving separate ways for footmen, beasts, and chariots; the whole under shade, and cooled by fountains of incessant flow.\n\nBen-Hur was not in mood to enjoy the spectacle. The story of Simonides haunted him. Arrived at the Omphalus—a monument of four arches wide as the streets, superbly illustrated, and erected to himself by Epiphanes, the eighth of the Seleucidæ—he suddenly changed his mind.\n\n“I will not go to the citadel to-night,” he said to the porters. “Take me to the khan nearest the bridge on the road to Seleucia.”\n\nThe party faced about, and in good time he was deposited in a public house of primitive but ample construction, within stone’s-throw of the bridge under which old Simonides had his quarters. He lay upon the house-top through the night. In his inner mind lived the thought, “Now—now I will hear of home—and mother—and the dear little Tirzah. If they are on earth, I will find them.”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nNext day early, to the neglect of the city, Ben-Hur sought the house of Simonides. Through an embattled gateway he passed to a continuity of wharves; thence up the river midst a busy press, to the Seleucian Bridge, under which he paused to take in the scene.\n\nThere, directly under the bridge, was the merchant’s house, a mass of gray stone, unhewn, referable to no style, looking, as the voyager had described it, like a buttress of the wall against which it leaned. Two immense doors in front communicated with the wharf. Some holes near the top, heavily barred, served as windows. Weeds waved from the crevices, and in places black moss splotched the otherwise bald stones.\n\nThe doors were open. Through one of them business went in; through the other it came out; and there was hurry, hurry in all its movements.\n\nOn the wharf there were piles of goods in every kind of package, and groups of slaves, stripped to the waist, going about in the abandon of labor.\n\nBelow the bridge lay a fleet of galleys, some loading, others unloading. A yellow flag blew out from each masthead. From fleet and wharf, and from ship to ship, the bondmen of traffic passed in clamorous counter-currents.\n\nAbove the bridge, across the river, a wall rose from the water’s edge, over which towered the fanciful cornices and turrets of an imperial palace, covering every foot of the island spoken of in the Hebrew’s description. But, with all its suggestions, Ben-Hur scarcely noticed it. Now, at last, he thought to hear of his people—this, certainly, if Simonides had indeed been his father’s slave. But would the man acknowledge the relation? That would be to give up his riches and the sovereignty of trade so royally witnessed on the wharf and river. And what was of still greater consequence to the merchant, it would be to forego his career in the midst of amazing success, and yield himself voluntarily once more a slave. Simple thought of the demand seemed a monstrous audacity. Stripped of diplomatic address, it was to say, You are my slave; give me all you have, and—yourself.\n\nYet Ben-Hur derived strength for the interview from faith in his rights and the hope uppermost in his heart. If the story to which he was yielding were true, Simonides belonged to him, with all he had. For the wealth, be it said in justice, he cared nothing. When he started to the door determined in mind, it was with a promise to himself—“Let him tell me of mother and Tirzah, and I will give him his freedom without account.”\n\nHe passed boldly into the house.\n\nThe interior was that of a vast depot where, in ordered spaces, and under careful arrangement, goods of every kind were heaped and pent. Though the light was murky and the air stifling, men moved about briskly; and in places he saw workmen with saws and hammers making packages for shipments. Down a path between the piles he walked slowly, wondering if the man of whose genius there were here such abounding proofs could have been his father’s slave? If so, to what class had he belonged? If a Jew, was he the son of a servant? Or was he a debtor or a debtor’s son? Or had he been sentenced and sold for theft? These thoughts, as they passed, in nowise disturbed the growing respect for the merchant of which he was each instant more and more conscious. A peculiarity of our admiration for another is that it is always looking for circumstances to justify itself.\n\nAt length a man approached and spoke to him.\n\n“What would you have?”\n\n“I would see Simonides, the merchant.”\n\n“Will you come this way?”\n\nBy a number of paths left in the stowage, they finally came to a flight of steps; ascending which, he found himself on the roof of the depot, and in front of a structure which cannot be better described than as a lesser stone house built upon another, invisible from the landing below, and out west of the bridge under the open sky. The roof, hemmed in by a low wall, seemed like a terrace, which, to his astonishment, was brilliant with flowers; in the rich surrounding, the house sat squat, a plain square block, unbroken except by a doorway in front. A dustless path led to the door, through a bordering of shrubs of Persian rose in perfect bloom. Breathing a sweet attar-perfume, he followed the guide.\n\nAt the end of a darkened passage within, they stopped before a curtain half parted. The man called out,\n\n“A stranger to see the master.”\n\nA clear voice replied, “In God’s name, let him enter.”\n\nA Roman might have called the apartment into which the visitor was ushered his atrium. The walls were paneled; each panel was comparted like a modern office-desk, and each compartment crowded with labelled folios all filemot with age and use. Between the panels, and above and below them, were borders of wood once white, now tinted like cream, and carved with marvellous intricacy of design. Above a cornice of gilded balls, the ceiling rose in pavilion style until it broke into a shallow dome set with hundreds of panes of violet mica, permitting a flood of light deliciously reposeful. The floor was carpeted with gray rugs so thick that an invading foot fell half buried and soundless.\n\nIn the midlight of the room were two persons—a man resting in a chair high-backed, broad-armed, and lined with pliant cushions; and at his left, leaning against the back of the chair, a girl well forward into womanhood. At sight of them Ben-Hur felt the blood redden his forehead; bowing, as much to recover himself as in respect, he lost the lifting of the hands, and the shiver and shrink with which the sitter caught sight of him—an emotion as swift to go as it had been to come. When he raised his eyes the two were in the same position, except the girl’s hand had fallen and was resting lightly upon the elder’s shoulder; both of them were regarding him fixedly.\n\n“If you are Simonides, the merchant, and a Jew”—Ben-Hur stopped an instant—“then the peace of the God of our father Abraham upon you and—yours.”\n\nThe last word was addressed to the girl.\n\n“I am the Simonides of whom you speak, by birthright a Jew,” the man made answer, in a voice singularly clear. “I am Simonides, and a Jew; and I return you your salutation, with prayer to know who calls upon me.”\n\nBen-Hur looked as he listened, and where the figure of the man should have been in healthful roundness, there was only a formless heap sunk in the depths of the cushions, and covered by a quilted robe of sombre silk. Over the heap shone a head royally proportioned—the ideal head of a statesman and conqueror—a head broad of base and domelike in front, such as Angelo would have modelled for Cæsar. White hair dropped in thin locks over the white brows, deepening the blackness of the eyes shining through them like sullen lights. The face was bloodless, and much puffed with folds, especially under the chin. In other words, the head and face were those of a man who might move the world more readily than the world could move him—a man to be twice twelve times tortured into the shapeless cripple he was, without a groan, much less a confession; a man to yield his life, but never a purpose or a point; a man born in armor, and assailable only through his loves. To him Ben-Hur stretched his hands, open and palm up, as he would offer peace at the same time he asked it.\n\n“I am Judah, son of Ithamar, late head of the House of Hur, and a prince of Jerusalem.”\n\nThe merchant’s right hand lay outside the robe—a long, thin hand, articulate to deformity with suffering. It closed tightly; otherwise there was not the slightest expression of feeling of any kind on his part; nothing to warrant an inference of surprise or interest; nothing but this calm answer,\n\n“The princes of Jerusalem, of the pure blood, are always welcome in my house; you are welcome. Give the young man a seat, Esther.”\n\nThe girl took an ottoman near by, and carried it to Ben-Hur. As she arose from placing the seat, their eyes met.\n\n“The peace of our Lord with you,” she said, modestly. “Be seated and at rest.”\n\nWhen she resumed her place by the chair, she had not divined his purpose. The powers of woman go not so far: if the matter is of finer feeling, such as pity, mercy, sympathy, that she detects; and therein is a difference between her and man which will endure as long as she remains, by nature, alive to such feelings. She was simply sure he brought some wound of life for healing.\n\nBen-Hur did not take the offered seat, but said, deferentially, “I pray the good master Simonides that he will not hold me an intruder. Coming up the river yesterday, I heard he knew my father.”\n\n“I knew the Prince Hur. We were associated in some enterprises lawful to merchants who find profit in lands beyond the sea and the desert. But sit, I pray you—and, Esther, some wine for the young man. Nehemiah speaks of a son of Hur who once ruled the half part of Jerusalem; an old house; very old, by the faith! In the days of Moses and Joshua even some of them found favor in the sight of the Lord, and divided honors with those princes among men. It can hardly be that their descendant, lineally come to us, will refuse a cup of wine-fat of the genuine vine of Sorek, grown on the south hill-sides of Hebron.”\n\nBy the time of the conclusion of this speech, Esther was before Ben-Hur with a silver cup filled from a vase upon a table a little removed from the chair. She offered the drink with downcast face. He touched her hand gently to put it away. Again their eyes met; whereat he noticed that she was small, not nearly to his shoulder in height; but very graceful, and fair and sweet of face, with eyes black and inexpressibly soft. She is kind and pretty, he thought, and looks as Tirzah would were she living. Poor Tirzah! Then he said aloud,\n\n“No, thy father—if he is thy father?”—he paused.\n\n“I am Esther, the daughter of Simonides,” she said, with dignity.\n\n“Then, fair Esther, thy father, when he has heard my further speech, will not think worse of me if yet I am slow to take his wine of famous extract; nor less I hope not to lose grace in thy sight. Stand thou here with me a moment!”\n\nBoth of them, as in common cause, turned to the merchant. “Simonides!” he said, firmly, “my father, at his death, had a trusted servant of thy name, and it has been told me that thou art the man!”\n\nThere was a sudden start of the wrenched limbs under the robe, and the thin hand clenched.\n\n“Esther, Esther!” the man called, sternly; “here, not there, as thou art thy mother’s child and mine—here, not there, I say!”\n\nThe girl looked once from father to visitor; then she replaced the cup upon the table, and went dutifully to the chair. Her countenance sufficiently expressed her wonder and alarm.\n\nSimonides lifted his left hand, and gave it into hers, lying lovingly upon his shoulder, and said, dispassionately, “I have grown old in dealing with men—old before my time. If he who told thee that whereof thou speakest was a friend acquainted with my history, and spoke of it not harshly, he must have persuaded thee that I could not be else than a man distrustful of my kind. The God of Israel help him who, at the end of life, is constrained to acknowledge so much! My loves are few, but they are. One of them is a soul which”—he carried the hand holding his to his lips, in manner unmistakable—“a soul which to this time has been unselfishly mine, and such sweet comfort that, were it taken from me, I would die.”\n\nEsther’s head drooped until her cheek touched his.\n\n“The other love is but a memory; of which I will say further that, like a benison of the Lord, it hath a compass to contain a whole family, if only”—his voice lowered and trembled—“if only I knew where they were.”\n\nBen-Hur’s face suffused, and, advancing a step, he cried, impulsively, “My mother and sister! Oh, it is of them you speak!”\n\nEsther, as if spoken to, raised her head; but Simonides returned to his calm, and answered, coldly, “Hear me to the end. Because I am that I am, and because of the loves of which I have spoken, before I make return to thy demand touching my relations to the Prince Hur, and as something which of right should come first, do thou show me proofs of who thou art. Is thy witness in writing? Or cometh it in person?”\n\nThe demand was plain, and the right of it indisputable. Ben-Hur blushed, clasped his hands, stammered, and turned away at loss. Simonides pressed him.\n\n“The proofs, the proofs, I say! Set them before me—lay them in my hands!”\n\nYet Ben-Hur had no answer. He had not anticipated the requirement; and, now that it was made, to him as never before came the awful fact that the three years in the galley had carried away all the proofs of his identity; mother and sister gone, he did not live in the knowledge of any human being. Many there were acquainted with him, but that was all. Had Quintus Arrius been present, what could he have said more than where he found him, and that he believed the pretender to be the son of Hur? But, as will presently appear in full, the brave Roman sailor was dead. Judah had felt the loneliness before; to the core of life the sense struck him now. He stood, hands clasped, face averted, in stupefaction. Simonides respected his suffering, and waited in silence.\n\n“Master Simonides,” he said, at length, “I can only tell my story; and I will not that unless you stay judgment so long, and with good-will deign to hear me.”\n\n“Speak,” said Simonides, now, indeed, master of the situation—“speak, and I will listen the more willingly that I have not denied you to be the very person you claim yourself.”\n\nBen-Hur proceeded then, and told his life hurriedly, yet with the feeling which is the source of all eloquence; but as we are familiar with it down to his landing at Misenum, in company with Arrius, returned victorious from the Ægean, at that point we will take up the words.\n\n“My benefactor was loved and trusted by the emperor, who heaped him with honorable rewards. The merchants of the East contributed magnificent presents, and he became doubly rich among the rich of Rome. May a Jew forget his religion? or his birthplace, if it were the Holy Land of our fathers? The good man adopted me his son by formal rites of law; and I strove to make him just return: no child was ever more dutiful to father than I to him. He would have had me a scholar; in art, philosophy, rhetoric, oratory, he would have furnished me the most famous teacher. I declined his insistence, because I was a Jew, and could not forget the Lord God, or the glory of the prophets, or the city set on the hills by David and Solomon. Oh, ask you why I accepted any of the benefactions of the Roman? I loved him; next place, I thought with his help, array influences which would enable me one day to unseal the mystery close-locking the fate of my mother and sister; and to these there was yet another motive of which I shall not speak except to say it controlled me so far that I devoted myself to arms, and the acquisition of everything deemed essential to thorough knowledge of the art of war. In the palaestrae and circuses of the city I toiled, and in the camps no less; and in all of them I have a name, but not that of my fathers. The crowns I won—and on the walls of the villa by Misenum there are many of them—all came to me as the son of Arrius, the duumvir. In that relation only am I known among Romans.... In steadfast pursuit of my secret aim, I left Rome for Antioch, intending to accompany the Consul Maxentius in the campaign he is organizing against the Parthians. Master of personal skill in all arms, I seek now the higher knowledge pertaining to the conduct of bodies of men in the field. The consul has admitted me one of his military family. But yesterday, as our ship entered the Orontes, two other ships sailed in with us flying yellow flags. A fellow-passenger and countryman from Cyprus explained that the vessels belonged to Simonides, the master-merchant of Antioch; he told us, also, who the merchant was; his marvellous success in commerce; of his fleets and caravans, and their coming and going; and, not knowing I had interest in the theme beyond my associate listeners, he said Simonides was a Jew, once the servant of the Prince Hur; nor did he conceal the cruelties of Gratus, or the purpose of their infliction.”\n\nAt this allusion Simonides bowed his head, and, as if to help him conceal his feelings and her own deep sympathy, the daughter hid her face on his neck. Directly he raised his eyes, and said, in a clear voice, “I am listening.”\n\n“O good Simonides!” Ben-Hur then said, advancing a step, his whole soul seeking expression, “I see thou art not convinced, and that yet I stand in the shadow of thy distrust.”\n\nThe merchant held his features fixed as marble, and his tongue as still.\n\n“And not less clearly, I see the difficulties of my position,” Ben-Hur continued. “All my Roman connection I can prove; I have only to call upon the consul, now the guest of the governor of the city; but I cannot prove the particulars of thy demand upon me. I cannot prove I am my father’s son. They who could serve me in that—alas! they are dead or lost.”\n\nHe covered his face with his hands; whereupon Esther arose, and, taking the rejected cup to him, said, “The wine is of the country we all so love. Drink, I pray thee!”\n\nThe voice was sweet as that of Rebekah offering drink at the well near Nahor the city; he saw there were tears in her eyes, and he drank, saying, “Daughter of Simonides, thy heart is full of goodness; and merciful art thou to let the stranger share it with thy father. Be thou blessed of our God! I thank thee.”\n\nThen he addressed himself to the merchant again:\n\n“As I have no proof that I am my father’s son, I will withdraw that I demanded of thee, O Simonides, and go hence to trouble you no more; only let me say I did not seek thy return to servitude nor account of thy fortune; in any event, I would have said, as now I say, that all which is product of thy labor and genius is thine; keep it in welcome. I have no need of any part thereof. When the good Quintus, my second father, sailed on the voyage which was his last, he left me his heir, princely rich. If, therefore, thou dost think of me again, be it with remembrance of this question, which, as I do swear by the prophets and Jehovah, thy God and mine, was the chief purpose of my coming here: What dost thou know—what canst thou tell me—of my mother and Tirzah, my sister—she who should be in beauty and grace even as this one, thy sweetness of life, if not thy very life? Oh! what canst thou tell me of them?”\n\nThe tears ran down Esther’s cheeks; but the man was wilful: in a clear voice, he replied,\n\n“I have said I knew the Prince Ben-Hur. I remember hearing of the misfortune which overtook his family. I remember the bitterness with which I heard it. He who wrought such misery to the widow of my friend is the same who, in the same spirit, hath since wrought upon me. I will go further, and say to you, I have made diligent quest concerning the family, but—I have nothing to tell you of them. They are lost.”\n\nBen-Hur uttered a great groan.\n\n“Then—then it is another hope broken!” he said, struggling with his feelings. “I am used to disappointments. I pray you pardon my intrusion; and if I have occasioned you annoyance, forgive it because of my sorrow. I have nothing now to live for but vengeance. Farewell.”\n\nAt the curtain he turned, and said, simply, “I thank you both.”\n\n“Peace go with you,” the merchant said.\n\nEsther could not speak for sobbing.\n\nAnd so he departed.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nScarcely was Ben-Hur gone, when Simonides seemed to wake as from sleep: his countenance flushed; the sullen light of his eyes changed to brightness; and he said, cheerily,\n\n“Esther, ring—quick!”\n\nShe went to the table, and rang a service-bell.\n\nOne of the panels in the wall swung back, exposing a doorway which gave admittance to a man who passed round to the merchant’s front, and saluted him with a half-salaam.\n\n“Malluch, here—nearer—to the chair,” the master said, imperiously. “I have a mission which shall not fail though the sun should. Hearken! A young man is now descending to the store-room—tall, comely, and in the garb of Israel; follow him, his shadow not more faithful; and every night send me report of where he is, what he does, and the company he keeps; and if, without discovery, you overhear his conversations, report them word for word, together with whatever will serve to expose him, his habits, motives, life. Understand you? Go quickly! Stay, Malluch: if he leave the city, go after him—and, mark you, Malluch, be as a friend. If he bespeak you, tell him what you will to the occasion most suited, except that you are in my service, of that, not a word. Haste—make haste!”\n\nThe man saluted as before, and was gone.\n\nThen Simonides rubbed his wan hands together, and laughed.\n\n“What is the day, daughter?” he said, in the midst of the mood. “What is the day? I wish to remember it for happiness come. See, and look for it laughing, and laughing tell me, Esther.”\n\nThe merriment seemed unnatural to her; and, as if to entreat him from it, she answered, sorrowfully, “Woe’s me, father, that I should ever forget this day!”\n\nHis hands fell down the instant, and his chin, dropping upon his breast, lost itself in the muffling folds of flesh composing his lower face.\n\n“True, most true, my daughter!” he said, without looking up. “This is the twentieth day of the fourth month. To-day, five years ago, my Rachel, thy mother, fell down and died. They brought me home broken as thou seest me, and we found her dead of grief. Oh, to me she was a cluster of camphire in the vineyards of En-Gedi! I have gathered my myrrh with my spice. I have eaten my honeycomb with my honey. We laid her away in a lonely place—in a tomb cut in the mountain; no one near her. Yet in the darkness she left me a little light, which the years have increased to a brightness of morning.” He raised his hand and rested it upon his daughter’s head. “Dear Lord, I thank thee that now in my Esther my lost Rachel liveth again!”\n\nDirectly he lifted his head, and said, as with a sudden thought, “Is it not clear day outside?”\n\n“It was, when the young man came in.”\n\n“Then let Abimelech come and take me to the garden, where I can see the river and the ships, and I will tell thee, dear Esther, why but now my mouth filled with laughter, and my tongue with singing, and my spirit was like to a roe or to a young hart upon the mountains of spices.”\n\nIn answer to the bell a servant came, and at her bidding pushed the chair, set on little wheels for the purpose, out of the room to the roof of the lower house, called by him his garden. Out through the roses, and by beds of lesser flowers, all triumphs of careful attendance, but now unnoticed, he was rolled to a position from which he could view the palace-tops over against him on the island, the bridge in lessening perspective to the farther shore, and the river below the bridge crowded with vessels, all swimming amidst the dancing splendors of the early sun upon the rippling water. There the servant left him with Esther.\n\nThe much shouting of laborers, and their beating and pounding, did not disturb him any more than the tramping of people on the bridge floor almost overhead, being as familiar to his ear as the view before him to his eye, and therefore unnoticeable, except as suggestions of profits in promise.\n\nEsther sat on the arm of the chair nursing his hand, and waiting his speech, which came at length in the calm way, the mighty will having carried him back to himself.\n\n“When the young man was speaking, Esther, I observed thee, and thought thou wert won by him.”\n\nHer eyes fell as she replied,\n\n“Speak you of faith, father, I believed him.”\n\n“In thy eyes, then, he is the lost son of the Prince Hur?”\n\n“If he is not—” She hesitated.\n\n“And if he is not, Esther?”\n\n“I have been thy handmaiden, father, since my mother answered the call of the Lord God; by thy side I have heard and seen thee deal in wise ways with all manner of men seeking profit, holy and unholy; and now I say, if indeed the young man be not the prince he claims to be, then before me falsehood never played so well the part of righteous truth.”\n\n“By the glory of Solomon, daughter, thou speakest earnestly. Dost thou believe thy father his father’s servant?”\n\n“I understood him to ask of that as something he had but heard.”\n\nFor a time Simonides’ gaze swam among his swimming ships, though they had no place in his mind.\n\n“Well, thou art a good child, Esther, of genuine Jewish shrewdness, and of years and strength to hear a sorrowful tale. Wherefore give me heed, and I will tell you of myself, and of thy mother, and of many things pertaining to the past not in thy knowledge or thy dreams—things withheld from the persecuting Romans for a hope’s sake, and from thee that thy nature should grow towards the Lord straight as the reed to the sun.... I was born in a tomb in the valley of Hinnom, on the south side of Zion. My father and mother were Hebrew bond-servants, tenders of the fig and olive trees growing, with many vines, in the King’s Garden hard by Siloam; and in my boyhood I helped them. They were of the class bound to serve forever. They sold me to the Prince Hur, then, next to Herod the King, the richest man in Jerusalem. From the garden he transferred me to his storehouse in Alexandria of Egypt, where I came of age. I served him six years, and in the seventh, by the law of Moses, I went free.”\n\nEsther clapped her hands lightly.\n\n“Oh, then, thou art not his father’s servant!”\n\n“Nay, daughter, hear. Now, in those days there were lawyers in the cloisters of the Temple who disputed vehemently, saying the children of servants bound forever took the condition of their parents; but the Prince Hur was a man righteous in all things, and an interpreter of the law after the straitest sect, though not of them. He said I was a Hebrew servant bought, in the true meaning of the great lawgiver, and, by sealed writings, which I yet have, he set me free.”\n\n“And my mother?” Esther asked.\n\n“Thou shalt hear all, Esther; be patient. Before I am through thou shalt see it were easier for me to forget myself than thy mother.... At the end of my service, I came up to Jerusalem to the Passover. My master entertained me. I was in love with him already, and I prayed to be continued in his service. He consented, and I served him yet another seven years, but as a hired son of Israel. In his behalf I had charge of ventures on the sea by ships, and of ventures on land by caravans eastward to Susa and Persepolis, and the lands of silk beyond them. Perilous passages were they, my daughter; but the Lord blessed all I undertook. I brought home vast gains for the prince, and richer knowledge for myself, without which I could not have mastered the charges since fallen to me.... One day I was a guest in his house in Jerusalem. A servant entered with some sliced bread on a platter. She came to me first. It was then I saw thy mother, and loved her, and took her away in my secret heart. After a while a time came when I sought the prince to make her my wife. He told me she was bond-servant forever; but if she wished, he would set her free that I might be gratified. She gave me love for love, but was happy where she was, and refused her freedom. I prayed and besought, going again and again after long intervals. She would be my wife, she all the time said, if I would become her fellow in servitude. Our father Jacob served yet other seven years for his Rachel. Could I not as much for mine? But thy mother said I must become as she, to serve forever. I came away, but went back. Look, Esther, look here.”\n\nHe pulled out the lobe of his left ear.\n\n“See you not the scar of the awl?”\n\n“I see it,” she said; “and, oh, I see how thou didst love my mother!”\n\n“Love her, Esther! She was to me more than the Shulamite to the singing king, fairer, more spotless; a fountain of gardens, a well of living waters, and streams from Lebanon. The master, even as I required him, took me to the judges, and back to his door, and thrust the awl through my ear into the door, and I was his servant forever. So I won my Rachel. And was ever love like mine?”\n\nEsther stooped and kissed him, and they were silent, thinking of the dead.\n\n“My master was drowned at sea, the first sorrow that ever fell upon me,” the merchant continued. “There was mourning in his house, and in mine here in Antioch, my abiding-place at the time. Now, Esther, mark you! When the good prince was lost, I had risen to be his chief steward, with everything of property belonging to him in my management and control. Judge you how much he loved and trusted me! I hastened to Jerusalem to render account to the widow. She continued me in the stewardship. I applied myself with greater diligence. The business prospered, and grew year by year. Ten years passed; then came the blow which you heard the young man tell about—the accident, as he called it, to the Procurator Gratus. The Roman gave it out an attempt to assassinate him. Under that pretext, by leave from Rome, he confiscated to his own use the immense fortune of the widow and children. Nor stopped he there. That there might be no reversal of the judgment, he removed all the parties interested. From that dreadful day to this the family of Hur have been lost. The son, whom I had seen as a child, was sentenced to the galleys. The widow and daughter are supposed to have been buried in some of the many dungeons of Judea, which, once closed upon the doomed, are like sepulchers sealed and locked. They passed from the knowledge of men as utterly as if the sea had swallowed them unseen. We could not hear how they died—nay, not even that they were dead.”\n\nEsther’s eyes were dewy with tears.\n\n“Thy heart is good, Esther, good as thy mother’s was; and I pray it have not the fate of most good hearts—to be trampled upon by the unmerciful and blind. But hearken further. I went up to Jerusalem to give help to my benefactress, and was seized at the gate of the city and carried to the sunken cells of the Tower of Antonia; why, I knew not, until Gratus himself came and demanded of me the moneys of the House of Hur, which he knew, after our Jewish custom of exchange, were subject to my draft in the different marts of the world. He required me to sign to his order. I refused. He had the houses, lands, goods, ships, and movable property of those I served; he had not their moneys. I saw, if I kept favor in the sight of the Lord, I could rebuild their broken fortunes. I refused the tyrant’s demands. He put me to torture; my will held good, and he set me free, nothing gained. I came home and began again, in the name of Simonides of Antioch, instead of the Prince Hur of Jerusalem. Thou knowest, Esther, how I have prospered; that the increase of the millions of the prince in my hands was miraculous; thou knowest how, at the end of three years, while going up to Cæsarea, I was taken and a second time tortured by Gratus to compel a confession that my goods and moneys were subject to his order of confiscation; thou knowest he failed as before. Broken in body, I came home and found my Rachel dead of fear and grief for me. The Lord our God reigned, and I lived. From the emperor himself I bought immunity and license to trade throughout the world. To-day—praised be He who maketh the clouds his chariot and walketh upon the winds!—to-day, Esther, that which was in my hands for stewardship is multiplied into talents sufficient to enrich a Cæsar.”\n\nHe lifted his head proudly; their eyes met; each read the other’s thought. “What shall I with the treasure, Esther?” he asked, without lowering his gaze.\n\n“My father,” she answered, in a low voice, “did not the rightful owner call for it but now?”\n\nStill his look did not fail.\n\n“And thou, my child; shall I leave thee a beggar?”\n\n“Nay, father, am not I, because I am thy child, his bond-servant? And of whom was it written, ‘Strength and honor are her clothing, and she shall rejoice in time to come?’”\n\nA gleam of ineffable love lighted his face as he said, “The Lord hath been good to me in many ways; but thou, Esther, art the sovereign excellence of his favor.”\n\nHe drew her to his breast and kissed her many times.\n\n“Hear now,” he said, with clearer voice—“hear now why I laughed this morning. The young man faced me the apparition of his father in comely youth. My spirit arose to salute him. I felt my trial-days were over and my labors ended. Hardly could I keep from crying out. I longed to take him by the hand and show the balance I had earned, and say, ‘Lo, ’tis all thine! and I am thy servant, ready now to be called away.’ And so I would have done, Esther, so I would have done, but that moment three thoughts rushed to restrain me. I will be sure he is my master’s son—such was the first thought; if he is my master’s son, I will learn somewhat of his nature. Of those born to riches, bethink you, Esther, how many there are in whose hands riches are but breeding curses”—he paused, while his hands clutched, and his voice shrilled with passion—“Esther, consider the pains I endured at the Roman’s hands; nay, not Gratus’s alone: the merciless wretches who did his bidding the first time and the last were Romans, and they all alike laughed to hear me scream. Consider my broken body, and the years I have gone shorn of my stature; consider thy mother yonder in her lonely tomb, crushed of soul as I of body; consider the sorrows of my master’s family if they are living, and the cruelty of their taking-off if they are dead; consider all, and, with Heaven’s love about thee, tell me, daughter, shall not a hair fall or a red drop run in expiation? Tell me not, as the preachers sometimes do—tell me not that vengeance is the Lord’s. Does he not work his will harmfully as well as in love by agencies? Has he not his men of war more numerous than his prophets? Is not his the law, Eye for eye, hand for hand, foot for foot? Oh, in all these years I have dreamed of vengeance, and prayed and provided for it, and gathered patience from the growing of my store, thinking and promising, as the Lord liveth, it will one day buy me punishment of the wrong-doers? And when, speaking of his practise with arms, the young man said it was for a nameless purpose, I named the purpose even as he spoke—vengeance! and that, Esther, that it was—the third thought which held me still and hard while his pleading lasted, and made me laugh when he was gone.”\n\nEsther caressed the faded hands, and said, as if her spirit with his were running forward to results, “He is gone. Will he come again?”\n\n“Ay, Malluch the faithful goes with him, and will bring him back when I am ready.”\n\n“And when will that be, father?”\n\n“Not long, not long. He thinks all his witnesses dead. There is one living who will not fail to know him, if he be indeed my master’s son.”\n\n“His mother?”\n\n“Nay, daughter, I will set the witness before him; till then let us rest the business with the Lord. I am tired. Call Abimelech.”\n\nEsther called the servant, and they returned into the house.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nWhen Ben-Hur sallied from the great warehouse, it was with the thought that another failure was to be added to the many he had already met in the quest for his people; and the idea was depressing exactly in proportion as the objects of his quest were dear to him; it curtained him round about with a sense of utter loneliness on earth, which, more than anything else, serves to eke from a soul cast down its remaining interest in life.\n\nThrough the people, and the piles of goods, he made way to the edge of the landing, and was tempted by the cool shadows darkening the river’s depth. The lazy current seemed to stop and wait for him. In counteraction of the spell, the saying of the voyager flashed into memory—“Better be a worm, and feed upon the mulberries of Daphne, than a king’s guest.” He turned, and walked rapidly down the landing and back to the khan.\n\n“The road to Daphne!” the steward said, surprised at the question Ben-Hur put to him. “You have not been here before? Well, count this the happiest day of your life. You cannot mistake the road. The next street to the left, going south, leads straight to Mount Sulpius, crowned by the altar of Jupiter and the Amphitheater; keep it to the third cross street, known as Herod’s Colonnade; turn to your right there, and hold the way through the old city of Seleucus to the bronze gates of Epiphanes. There the road to Daphne begins—and may the gods keep you!”\n\nA few directions respecting his baggage, and Ben-Hur set out.\n\nThe Colonnade of Herod was easily found; thence to the brazen gates, under a continuous marble portico, he passed with a multitude mixed of people from all the trading nations of the earth.\n\nIt was about the fourth hour of the day when he passed out the gate, and found himself one of a procession apparently interminable, moving to the famous Grove. The road was divided into separate ways for footmen, for men on horses, and men in chariots; and those again into separate ways for outgoers and incomers. The lines of division were guarded by low balustrading, broken by massive pedestals, many of which were surmounted with statuary. Right and left of the road extended margins of sward perfectly kept, relieved at intervals by groups of oak and sycamore trees, and vine-clad summer-houses for the accommodation of the weary, of whom, on the return side, there were always multitudes. The ways of the footmen were paved with red stone, and those of the riders strewn with white sand compactly rolled, but not so solid as to give back an echo to hoof or wheel. The number and variety of fountains at play were amazing, all gifts of visiting kings, and called after them. Out southwest to the gates of the Grove, the magnificent thoroughfare stretched a little over four miles from the city.\n\nIn his wretchedness of feeling, Ben-Hur barely observed the royal liberality which marked the construction of the road. Nor more did he at first notice the crowd going with him. He treated the processional displays with like indifference. To say truth, besides his self-absorption, he had not a little of the complacency of a Roman visiting the provinces fresh from the ceremonies which daily eddied round and round the golden pillar set up by Augustus as the centre of the world. It was not possible for the provinces to offer anything new or superior. He rather availed himself of every opportunity to push forward through the companies in the way, and too slow-going for his impatience. By the time he reached Heracleia, a suburban village intermediate the city and the Grove, he was somewhat spent with exercise, and began to be susceptible of entertainment. Once a pair of goats led by a beautiful woman, woman and goats alike brilliant with ribbons and flowers, attracted his attention. Then he stopped to look at a bull of mighty girth, and snowy white, covered with vines freshly cut, and bearing on its broad back a naked child in a basket, the image of a young Bacchus, squeezing the juice of ripened berries into a goblet, and drinking with libational formulas. As he resumed his walk, he wondered whose altars would be enriched by the offerings. A horse went by with clipped mane, after the fashion of the time, his rider superbly dressed. He smiled to observe the harmony of pride between the man and the brute. Often after that he turned his head at hearing the rumble of wheels and the dull thud of hoofs; unconsciously he was becoming interested in the styles of chariots and charioteers, as they rustled past him going and coming. Nor was it long until he began to make notes of the people around him. He saw they were of all ages, sexes, and conditions, and all in holiday attire. One company was uniformed in white, another in black; some bore flags, some smoking censers; some went slowly, singing hymns; others stepped to the music of flutes and tabrets. If such were the going to Daphne every day in the year, what a wondrous sight Daphne must be! At last there was a clapping of hands, and a burst of joyous cries; following the pointing of many fingers, he looked and saw upon the brow of a hill the templed gate of the consecrated Grove. The hymns swelled to louder strains; the music quickened time; and, borne along by the impulsive current, and sharing the common eagerness, he passed in, and, Romanized in taste as he was, fell to worshiping the place.\n\nRearward of the structure which graced the entrance-way—a purely Grecian pile—he stood upon a broad esplanade paved with polished stone; around him a restless exclamatory multitude, in gayest colors, relieved against the iridescent spray flying crystal-white from fountains; before him, off to the southwest, dustless paths radiated out into a garden, and beyond that into a forest, over which rested a veil of pale-blue vapor. Ben-Hur gazed wistfully, uncertain where to go. A woman that moment exclaimed,\n\n“Beautiful! But where to now?”\n\nHer companion, wearing a chaplet of bays, laughed and answered, “Go to, thou pretty barbarian! The question implies an earthly fear; and did we not agree to leave all such behind in Antioch with the rusty earth? The winds which blow here are respirations of the gods. Let us give ourselves to waftage of the winds.”\n\n“But if we should get lost?”\n\n“O thou timid! No one was ever lost in Daphne, except those on whom her gates close forever.”\n\n“And who are they?” she asked, still fearful.\n\n“Such as have yielded to the charms of the place and chosen it for life and death. Hark! Stand we here, and I will show you of whom I speak.”\n\nUpon the marble pavement there was a scurry of sandalled feet; the crowd opened, and a party of girls rushed about the speaker and his fair friend, and began singing and dancing to the tabrets they themselves touched. The woman, scared, clung to the man, who put an arm about her, and, with kindled face, kept time to the music with the other hand overhead. The hair of the dancers floated free, and their limbs blushed through the robes of gauze which scarcely draped them. Words may not be used to tell of the voluptuousness of the dance. One brief round, and they darted off through the yielding crowd lightly as they had come.\n\n“Now what think you?” cried the man to the woman.\n\n“Who are they?” she asked.\n\n“Devadasi—priestesses devoted to the Temple of Apollo. There is an army of them. They make the chorus in celebrations. This is their home. Sometimes they wander off to other cities, but all they make is brought here to enrich the house of the divine musician. Shall we go now?”\n\nNext minute the two were gone.\n\nBen-Hur took comfort in the assurance that no one was ever lost in Daphne, and he, too, set out—where, he knew not.\n\nA sculpture reared upon a beautiful pedestal in the garden attracted him first. It proved to be the statue of a centaur. An inscription informed the unlearned visitor that it exactly represented Chiron, the beloved of Apollo and Diana, instructed by them in the mysteries of hunting, medicine, music, and prophecy. The inscription also bade the stranger look out at a certain part of the heavens, at a certain hour of the clear night, and he would behold the dead alive among the stars, whither Jupiter had transferred the good genius.\n\nThe wisest of the centaurs continued, nevertheless, in the service of mankind. In his hand he held a scroll, on which, graven in Greek, were paragraphs of a notice:\n\n“O Traveller! “Art thou a stranger?\n\n“I. Hearken to the singing of the brooks, and fear not the rain of the fountains; so will the Naiades learn to love thee.\n\n“II. The invited breezes of Daphne are Zephyrus and Auster; gentle ministers of life, they will gather sweets for thee; when Eurus blows, Diana is elsewhere hunting; when Boreas blusters, go hide, for Apollo is angry.\n\n“III. The shades of the Grove are thine in the day; at night they belong to Pan and his Dryades. Disturb them not.\n\n“IV. Eat of the Lotus by the brooksides sparingly, unless thou wouldst have surcease of memory, which is to become a child of Daphne.\n\n“V. Walk thou round the weaving spider—’tis Arachne at work for Minerva.\n\n“VI. Wouldst thou behold the tears of Daphne, break but a bud from a laurel bough—and die.\n\n“Heed thou! “And stay and be happy.”\n\nBen-Hur left the interpretation of the mystic notice to others fast enclosing him, and turned away as the white bull was led by. The boy sat in the basket, followed by a procession; after them again, the woman with the goats; and behind her the flute and tabret players, and another procession of gift-bringers.\n\n“Whither go they?” asked a bystander.\n\nAnother made answer, “The bull to Father Jove; the goat—”\n\n“Did not Apollo once keep the flocks of Admetus?”\n\n“Ay, the goat to Apollo!”\n\nThe goodness of the reader is again besought in favor of an explanation. A certain facility of accommodation in the matter of religion comes to us after much intercourse with people of a different faith; gradually we attain the truth that every creed is illustrated by good men who are entitled to our respect, but whom we cannot respect without courtesy to their creed. To this point Ben-Hur had arrived. Neither the years in Rome nor those in the galley had made any impression upon his religious faith; he was yet a Jew. In his view, nevertheless, it was not an impiety to look for the beautiful in the Grove of Daphne.\n\nThe remark does not interdict the further saying, if his scruples had been ever so extreme, not improbably he would at this time have smothered them. He was angry; not as the irritable, from chafing of a trifle; nor was his anger like the fool’s, pumped from the wells of nothing, to be dissipated by a reproach or a curse; it was the wrath peculiar to ardent natures rudely awakened by the sudden annihilation of a hope—dream, if you will—in which the choicest happinesses were thought to be certainly in reach. In such case nothing intermediate will carry off the passion—the quarrel is with Fate.\n\nLet us follow the philosophy a little further, and say to ourselves, it were well in such quarrels if Fate were something tangible, to be despatched with a look or a blow, or a speaking personage with whom high words were possible; then the unhappy mortal would not always end the affair by punishing himself.\n\nIn ordinary mood, Ben-Hur would not have come to the Grove alone, or, coming alone, he would have availed himself of his position in the consul’s family, and made provision against wandering idly about, unknowing and unknown; he would have had all the points of interest in mind, and gone to them under guidance, as in the despatch of business; or, wishing to squander days of leisure in the beautiful place, he would have had in hand a letter to the master of it all, whoever he might be. This would have made him a sightseer, like the shouting herd he was accompanying; whereas he had no reverence for the deities of the Grove, nor curiosity; a man in the blindness of bitter disappointment, he was adrift, not waiting for Fate, but seeking it as a desperate challenger.\n\nEvery one has known this condition of mind, though perhaps not all in the same degree; every one will recognize it as the condition in which he has done brave things with apparent serenity; and every one reading will say, Fortunate for Ben-Hur if the folly which now catches him is but a friendly harlequin with whistle and painted cap, and not some Violence with a pointed sword pitiless.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nBen-Hur entered the woods with the processions. He had not interest enough at first to ask where they were going; yet, to relieve him from absolute indifference, he had a vague impression that they were in movement to the temples, which were the central objects of the Grove, supreme in attractions.\n\nPresently, as singers dreamfully play with a flitting chorus, he began repeating to himself, “Better be a worm, and feed on the mulberries of Daphne, than a king’s guest.” Then of the much repetition arose questions importunate of answer. Was life in the Grove so very sweet? Wherein was the charm? Did it lie in some tangled depth of philosophy? Or was it something in fact, something on the surface, discernible to every-day wakeful senses? Every year thousands, forswearing the world, gave themselves to service here. Did they find the charm? And was it sufficient, when found, to induce forgetfulness profound enough to shut out of mind the infinitely diverse things of life? those that sweeten and those that embitter? hopes hovering in the near future as well as sorrows born of the past? If the Grove were so good for them, why should it not be good for him? He was a Jew; could it be that the excellences were for all the world but children of Abraham? Forthwith he bent all his faculties to the task of discovery, unmindful of the singing of the gift-bringers and the quips of his associates.\n\nIn the quest, the sky yielded him nothing; it was blue, very blue, and full of twittering swallows—so was the sky over the city.\n\nFurther on, out of the woods at his right hand, a breeze poured across the road, splashing him with a wave of sweet smells, blent of roses and consuming spices. He stopped, as did others, looking the way the breeze came.\n\n“A garden over there?” he said, to a man at his elbow.\n\n“Rather some priestly ceremony in performance—something to Diana, or Pan, or a deity of the woods.”\n\nThe answer was in his mother tongue. Ben-Hur gave the speaker a surprised look.\n\n“A Hebrew?” he asked him.\n\nThe man replied with a deferential smile,\n\n“I was born within a stone’s-throw of the market-place in Jerusalem.”\n\nBen-Hur was proceeding to further speech, when the crowd surged forward, thrusting him out on the side of the walk next the woods, and carrying the stranger away. The customary gown and staff, a brown cloth on the head tied by a yellow rope, and a strong Judean face to avouch the garments of honest right, remained in the young man’s mind, a kind of summary of the man.\n\nThis took place at a point where a path into the woods began, offering a happy escape from the noisy processions. Ben-Hur availed himself of the offer.\n\nHe walked first into a thicket which, from the road, appeared in a state of nature, close, impenetrable, a nesting-place for wild birds. A few steps, however, gave him to see the master’s hand even there. The shrubs were flowering or fruit-bearing; under the bending branches the ground was pranked with brightest blooms; over them the jasmine stretched its delicate bonds. From lilac and rose, and lily and tulip, from oleander and strawberry-tree, all old friends in the gardens of the valleys about the city of David, the air, lingering or in haste, loaded itself with exhalations day and night; and that nothing might be wanting to the happiness of the nymphs and naiads, down through the flower-lighted shadows of the mass a brook went its course gently, and by many winding ways.\n\nOut of the thicket, as he proceeded, on his right and left, issued the cry of the pigeon and the cooing of turtle-doves; blackbirds waited for him, and bided his coming close; a nightingale kept its place fearless, though he passed in arm’s-length; a quail ran before him at his feet, whistling to the brood she was leading, and as he paused for them to get out of his way, a figure crawled from a bed of honeyed musk brilliant with balls of golden blossoms. Ben-Hur was startled. Had he, indeed, been permitted to see a satyr at home? The creature looked up at him, and showed in its teeth a hooked pruning-knife; he smiled at his own scare, and, lo! the charm was evolved! Peace without fear—peace a universal condition—that it was!\n\nHe sat upon the ground beneath a citron-tree, which spread its gray roots sprawling to receive a branch of the brook. The nest of a titmouse hung close to the bubbling water, and the tiny creature looked out of the door of the nest into his eyes. “Verily, the bird is interpreting to me,” he thought. “It says, ‘I am not afraid of you, for the law of this happy place is Love.’”\n\nThe charm of the Grove seemed plain to him; he was glad, and determined to render himself one of the lost in Daphne. In charge of the flowers and shrubs, and watching the growth of all the dumb excellences everywhere to be seen, could not he, like the man with the pruning-knife in his mouth, forego the days of his troubled life—forego them forgetting and forgotten?\n\nBut by-and-by his Jewish nature began to stir within him.\n\nThe charm might be sufficient for some people. Of what kind were they?\n\nLove is delightful—ah! how pleasant as a successor to wretchedness like his. But was it all there was of life? All?\n\nThere was an unlikeness between him and those who buried themselves contentedly here. They had no duties—they could not have had; but he—\n\n“God of Israel!” he cried aloud, springing to his feet, with burning cheeks—“Mother! Tirzah! Cursed be the moment, cursed the place, in which I yield myself happy in your loss!”\n\nHe hurried away through the thicket, and came to a stream flowing with the volume of a river between banks of masonry, broken at intervals by gated sluiceways. A bridge carried the path he was traversing across the stream; and, standing upon it, he saw other bridges, no two of them alike. Under him the water was lying in a deep pool, clear as a shadow; down a little way it tumbled with a roar over rocks; then there was another pool, and another cascade; and so on, out of view; and bridges and pools and resounding cascades said, plainly as inarticulate things can tell a story, the river was running by permission of a master, exactly as the master would have it, tractable as became a servant of the gods.\n\nForward from the bridge he beheld a landscape of wide valleys and irregular heights, with groves and lakes and fanciful houses linked together by white paths and shining streams. The valleys were spread below, that the river might be poured upon them for refreshment in days of drought, and they were as green carpets figured with beds and fields of flowers, and flecked with flocks of sheep white as balls of snow; and the voices of shepherds following the flocks were heard afar. As if to tell him of the pious inscription of all he beheld, the altars out under the open sky seemed countless, each with a white-gowned figure attending it, while processions in white went slowly hither and thither between them; and the smoke of the altars half-risen hung collected in pale clouds over the devoted places.\n\nHere, there, happy in flight, intoxicated in pause, from object to object, point to point, now in the meadow, now on the heights, now lingering to penetrate the groves and observe the processions, then lost in efforts to pursue the paths and streams which trended mazily into dim perspectives to end finally in— Ah, what might be a fitting end to scene so beautiful! What adequate mysteries were hidden behind an introduction so marvellous! Here and there, the speech was beginning, his gaze wandered, so he could not help the conviction, forced by the view, and as the sum of it all, that there was peace in the air and on the earth, and invitation everywhere to come and lie down here and be at rest.\n\nSuddenly a revelation dawned upon him—the Grove was, in fact, a temple—one far-reaching, wall-less temple!\n\nNever anything like it!\n\nThe architect had not stopped to pother about columns and porticos, proportions or interiors, or any limitation upon the epic he sought to materialize; he had simply made a servant of Nature—art can go no further. So the cunning son of Jupiter and Callisto built the old Arcadia; and in this, as in that, the genius was Greek.\n\nFrom the bridge Ben-Hur went forward into the nearest valley.\n\nHe came to a flock of sheep. The shepherd was a girl, and she beckoned him, “Come!”\n\nFarther on, the path was divided by an altar—a pedestal of black gneiss, capped with a slab of white marble deftly foliated, and on that a brazier of bronze holding a fire. Close by it, a woman, seeing him, waved a wand of willow, and as he passed called him, “Stay!” And the temptation in her smile was that of passionate youth.\n\nOn yet further, he met one of the processions; at its head a troop of little girls, nude except as they were covered with garlands, piped their shrill voices into a song; then a troop of boys, also nude, their bodies deeply sun-browned, came dancing to the song of the girls; behind them the procession, all women, bearing baskets of spices and sweets to the altars—women clad in simple robes, careless of exposure. As he went by they held their hands to him, and said, “Stay, and go with us.” One, a Greek, sang a verse from Anacreon:\n\n“For to-day I take or give; For to-day I drink and live; For to-day I beg or borrow; Who knows about the silent morrow?”\n\nBut he pursued his way indifferent, and came next to a grove luxuriant, in the heart of the vale at the point where it would be most attractive to the observing eye. As it came close to the path he was travelling, there was a seduction in its shade, and through the foliage he caught the shining of what appeared a pretentious statue; so he turned aside, and entered the cool retreat.\n\nThe grass was fresh and clean. The trees did not crowd each other; and they were of every kind native to the East, blended well with strangers adopted from far quarters; here grouped in exclusive companionship palm-trees plumed like queens; there sycamores, overtopping laurels of darker foliage; and evergreen oaks rising verdantly, with cedars vast enough to be kings on Lebanon; and mulberries; and terebinths so beautiful it is not hyperbole to speak of them as blown from the orchards of Paradise.\n\nThe statue proved to be a Daphne of wondrous beauty. Hardly, however, had he time to more than glance at her face: at the base of the pedestal a girl and a youth were lying upon a tiger’s skin asleep in each other’s arms; close by them the implements of their service—his axe and sickle, her basket—flung carelessly upon a heap of fading roses.\n\nThe exposure startled him. Back in the hush of the perfumed thicket he discovered, as he thought, that the charm of the great Grove was peace without fear, and almost yielded to it; now, in this sleep in the day’s broad glare—this sleep at the feet of Daphne—he read a further chapter to which only the vaguest allusion is sufferable. The law of the place was Love, but Love without Law.\n\nAnd this was the sweet peace of Daphne!\n\nThis the life’s end of her ministers!\n\nFor this kings and princes gave of their revenues!\n\nFor this a crafty priesthood subordinated nature—her birds and brooks and lilies, the river, the labor of many hands, the sanctity of altars, the fertile power of the sun!\n\nIt would be pleasant now to record that as Ben-Hur pursued his walk assailed by such reflections, he yielded somewhat to sorrow for the votaries of the great outdoor temple; especially for those who, by personal service, kept it in a state so surpassingly lovely. How they came to the condition was not any longer a mystery; the motive, the influence, the inducement, were before him. Some there were, no doubt, caught by the promise held out to their troubled spirits of endless peace in a consecrated abode, to the beauty of which, if they had not money, they could contribute their labor; this class implied intellect peculiarly subject to hope and fear; but the great body of the faithful could not be classed with such. Apollo’s nets were wide, and their meshes small; and hardly may one tell what all his fishermen landed: this less for that they cannot be described than because they ought not to be. Enough that the mass were of the sybarites of the world, and of the herds in number vaster and in degree lower—devotees of the unmixed sensualism to which the East was almost wholly given. Not to any of the exaltations—not to the singing-god, or his unhappy mistress; not to any philosophy requiring for its enjoyment the calm of retirement, nor to any service for the comfort there is in religion, nor to love in its holier sense—were they abiding their vows. Good reader, why shall not the truth be told here? Why not learn that, at this age, there were in all earth but two peoples capable of exaltations of the kind referred to—those who lived by the law of Moses, and those who lived by the law of Brahma. They alone could have cried you, Better a law without love than a love without law.\n\nBesides that, sympathy is in great degree a result of the mood we are in at the moment: anger forbids the emotion. On the other hand, it is easiest taken on when we are in a state of most absolute self-satisfaction. Ben-Hur walked with a quicker step, holding his head higher; and, while not less sensitive to the delightfulness of all about him, he made his survey with calmer spirit, though sometimes with curling lip; that is to say, he could not so soon forget how nearly he himself had been imposed upon.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nIn front of Ben-Hur there was a forest of cypress-trees, each a column tall and straight as a mast. Venturing into the shady precinct, he heard a trumpet gayly blown, and an instant after saw lying upon the grass close by the countryman whom he had run upon in the road going to the temples. The man arose, and came to him.\n\n“I give you peace again,” he said, pleasantly.\n\n“Thank you,” Ben-Hur replied, then asked, “Go you my way?”\n\n“I am for the stadium, if that is your way.”\n\n“The stadium!”\n\n“Yes. The trumpet you heard but now was a call for the competitors.”\n\n“Good friend,” said Ben-Hur, frankly, “I admit my ignorance of the Grove; and if you will let me be your follower, I will be glad.”\n\n“That will delight me. Hark! I hear the wheels of the chariots. They are taking the track.”\n\nBen-Hur listened a moment, then completed the introduction by laying his hand upon the man’s arm, and saying, “I am the son of Arrius, the duumvir, and thou?”\n\n“I am Malluch, a merchant of Antioch.”\n\n“Well, good Malluch, the trumpet, and the gride of wheels, and the prospect of diversion excite me. I have some skill in the exercises. In the palaestrae of Rome I am not unknown. Let us to the course.”\n\nMalluch lingered to say, quickly, “The duumvir was a Roman, yet I see his son in the garments of a Jew.”\n\n“The noble Arrius was my father by adoption,” Ben-Hur answered.\n\n“Ah! I see, and beg pardon.”\n\nPassing through the belt of forest, they came to a field with a track laid out upon it, in shape and extent exactly like those of the stadia. The course, or track proper, was of soft earth, rolled and sprinkled, and on both sides defined by ropes, stretched loosely upon upright javelins. For the accommodation of spectators, and such as had interests reaching forward of the mere practise, there were several stands shaded by substantial awnings, and provided with seats in rising rows. In one of the stands the two new-comers found places.\n\nBen-Hur counted the chariots as they went by—nine in all.\n\n“I commend the fellows,” he said, with good-will. “Here in the East, I thought they aspired to nothing better than the two; but they are ambitious, and play with royal fours. Let us study their performance.”\n\nEight of the fours passed the stand, some walking, others on the trot, and all unexceptionably handled; then the ninth one came on the gallop. Ben-Hur burst into exclamation.\n\n“I have been in the stables of the emperor, Malluch, but, by our father Abraham of blessed memory! I never saw the like of these.”\n\nThe last four was then sweeping past. All at once they fell into confusion. Some one on the stand uttered a sharp cry. Ben-Hur turned, and saw an old man half-risen from an upper seat, his hands clenched and raised, his eyes fiercely bright, his long white beard fairly quivering. Some of the spectators nearest him began to laugh.\n\n“They should respect his beard at least. Who is he?” asked Ben-Hur.\n\n“A mighty man from the Desert, somewhere beyond Moab, and owner of camels in herds, and horses descended, they say, from the racers of the first Pharaoh—Sheik Ilderim by name and title.”\n\nThus Malluch replied.\n\nThe driver meanwhile exerted himself to quiet the four, but without avail. Each ineffectual effort excited the sheik the more.\n\n“Abaddon seize him!” yelled the patriarch, shrilly. “Run! fly! do you hear, my children?” The question was to his attendants, apparently of the tribe. “Do you hear? They are Desert-born, like yourselves. Catch them—quick!”\n\nThe plunging of the animals increased.\n\n“Accursed Roman!” and the sheik shook his fist at the driver. “Did he not swear he could drive them—swear it by all his brood of bastard Latin gods? Nay, hands off me—off, I say! They should run swift as eagles, and with the temper of hand-bred lambs, he swore. Cursed be he—cursed the mother of liars who calls him son! See them, the priceless! Let him touch one of them with a lash, and”—the rest of the sentence was lost in a furious grinding of his teeth. “To their heads, some of you, and speak them—a word, one is enough, from the tent-song your mothers sang you. Oh, fool, fool that I was to put trust in a Roman!”\n\nSome of the shrewder of the old man’s friends planted themselves between him and the horses. An opportune failure of breath on his part helped the stratagem.\n\nBen-Hur, thinking he comprehended the sheik, sympathized with him. Far more than mere pride of property—more than anxiety for the result of the race—in his view it was within the possible for the patriarch, according to his habits of thought and his ideas of the inestimable, to love such animals with a tenderness akin to the most sensitive passion.\n\nThey were all bright bays, unspotted, perfectly matched, and so proportioned as to seem less than they really were. Delicate ears pointed small heads; the faces were broad and full between the eyes; the nostrils in expansion disclosed membrane so deeply red as to suggest the flashing of flame; the necks were arches, overlaid with fine mane so abundant as to drape the shoulders and breast, while in happy consonance the forelocks were like ravellings of silken veils; between the knees and the fetlocks the legs were flat as an open hand, but above the knees they were rounded with mighty muscles, needful to upbear the shapely close-knit bodies; the hoofs were like cups of polished agate; and in rearing and plunging they whipped the air, and sometimes the earth, with tails glossy-black and thick and long. The sheik spoke of them as the priceless, and it was a good saying.\n\nIn this second and closer look at the horses, Ben-Hur read the story of their relation to their master. They had grown up under his eyes, objects of his special care in the day, his visions of pride in the night, with his family at home in the black tent out on the shadeless bosom of the desert, as his children beloved. That they might win him a triumph over the haughty and hated Roman, the old man had brought his loves to the city, never doubting they would win, if only he could find a trusty expert to take them in hand; not merely one with skill, but of a spirit which their spirits would acknowledge. Unlike the colder people of the West, he could not protest the driver’s inability, and dismiss him civilly; an Arab and a sheik, he had to explode, and rive the air about him with clamor.\n\nBefore the patriarch was done with his expletives, a dozen hands were at the bits of the horses, and their quiet assured. About that time, another chariot appeared upon the track; and, unlike the others, driver, vehicle, and races were precisely as they would be presented in the Circus the day of final trial. For a reason which will presently be more apparent, it is desirable now to give this turnout plainly to the reader.\n\nThere should be no difficulty in understanding the carriage known to us all as the chariot of classical renown. One has but to picture to himself a dray with low wheels and broad axle, surmounted by a box open at the tail end. Such was the primitive pattern. Artistic genius came along in time, and, touching the rude machine, raised it into a thing of beauty—that, for instance, in which Aurora, riding in advance of the dawn, is given to our fancy.\n\nThe jockeys of the ancients, quite as shrewd and ambitious as their successors of the present, called their humblest turnout a two, and their best in grade a four; in the latter, they contested the Olympics and the other festal shows founded in imitation of them.\n\nThe same sharp gamesters preferred to put their horses to the chariot all abreast; and for distinction they termed the two next the pole yoke-steeds, and those on the right and left outside trace-mates. It was their judgment, also, that, by allowing the fullest freedom of action, the greatest speed was attainable; accordingly, the harness resorted to was peculiarly simple; in fact, there was nothing of it save a collar round the animal’s neck, and a trace fixed to the collar, unless the lines and a halter fall within the term. Wanting to hitch up, the masters pinned a narrow wooden yoke, or cross-tree, near the end of the pole, and, by straps passed through rings at the end of the yoke, buckled the latter to the collar. The traces of the yokesteeds they hitched to the axle; those of the trace-mates to the top rim of the chariot-bed. There remained then but the adjustment of the lines, which, judged by the modern devices, was not the least curious part of the method. For this there was a large ring at the forward extremity of the pole; securing the ends to that ring first, they parted the lines so as to give one to each horse, and proceeded to pass them to the driver, slipping them separately through rings on the inner side of the halters at the mouth.\n\nWith this plain generalization in mind, all further desirable knowledge upon the subject can be had by following the incidents of the scene occurring.\n\nThe other contestants had been received in silence; the last comer was more fortunate. While moving towards the stand from which we are viewing the scene, his progress was signalized by loud demonstrations, by clapping of hands and cheers, the effect of which was to centre attention upon him exclusively. His yoke-steeds, it was observed, were black, while the trace-mates were snow-white. In conformity to the exacting canons of Roman taste, they had all four been mutilated; that is to say, their tails had been clipped, and, to complete the barbarity, their shorn manes were divided into knots tied with flaring red and yellow ribbons.\n\nIn advancing, the stranger at length reached a point where the chariot came into view from the stand, and its appearance would of itself have justified the shouting. The wheels were very marvels of construction. Stout bands of burnished bronze reinforced the hubs, otherwise very light; the spokes were sections of ivory tusks, set in with the natural curve outward to perfect the dishing, considered important then as now; bronze tires held the fellies, which were of shining ebony. The axle, in keeping with the wheels, was tipped with heads of snarling tigers done in brass, and the bed was woven of willow wands gilded with gold.\n\nThe coming of the beautiful horses and resplendent chariot drew Ben-Hur to look at the driver with increased interest.\n\nWho was he?\n\nWhen Ben-Hur asked himself the question first, he could not see the man’s face, or even his full figure; yet the air and manner were familiar, and pricked him keenly with a reminder of a period long gone.\n\nWho could it be?\n\nNearer now, and the horses approaching at a trot. From the shouting and the gorgeousness of the turnout, it was thought he might be some official favorite or famous prince. Such an appearance was not inconsistent with exalted rank. Kings often struggled for the crown of leaves which was the prize of victory. Nero and Commodus, it will be remembered, devoted themselves to the chariot. Ben-Hur arose and forced a passage down nearly to the railing in front of the lower seat of the stand. His face was earnest, his manner eager.\n\nAnd directly the whole person of the driver was in view. A companion rode with him, in classic description a Myrtilus, permitted men of high estate indulging their passion for the race-course. Ben-Hur could see only the driver, standing erect in the chariot, with the reins passed several times round his body—a handsome figure, scantily covered by a tunic of light-red cloth; in the right hand a whip; in the other, the arm raised and lightly extended, the four lines. The pose was exceedingly graceful and animated. The cheers and clapping of hands were received with statuesque indifference. Ben-Hur stood transfixed—his instinct and memory had served him faithfully—THE DRIVER WAS MESSALA.\n\nBy the selection of horses, the magnificence of the chariot, the attitude, and display of person—above all, by the expression of the cold, sharp, eagle features, imperialized in his countrymen by sway of the world through so many generations, Ben-Hur knew Messala unchanged, as haughty, confident, and audacious as ever, the same in ambition, cynicism, and mocking insouciance.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\nAs Ben-Hur descended the steps of the stand, an Arab arose upon the last one at the foot, and cried out,\n\n“Men of the East and West—hearken! The good Sheik Ilderim giveth greeting. With four horses, sons of the favorites of Solomon the Wise, he hath come up against the best. Needs he most a mighty man to drive them. Whoso will take them to his satisfaction, to him he promiseth enrichment forever. Here—there—in the city and in the Circuses, and wherever the strong most do congregate, tell ye this his offer. So saith my master, Sheik Ilderim the Generous.”\n\nThe proclamation awakened a great buzz among the people under the awning. By night it would be repeated and discussed in all the sporting circles of Antioch. Ben-Hur, hearing it, stopped and looked hesitatingly from the herald to the sheik. Malluch thought he was about to accept the offer, but was relieved when he presently turned to him, and asked, “Good Malluch, where to now?”\n\nThe worthy replied, with a laugh, “Would you liken yourself to others visiting the Grove for the first time, you will straightway to hear your fortune told.”\n\n“My fortune, said you? Though the suggestion has in it a flavor of unbelief, let us to the goddess at once.”\n\n“Nay, son of Arrius, these Apollonians have a better trick than that. Instead of speech with a Pythia or a Sibyl, they will sell you a plain papyrus leaf, hardly dry from the stalk, and bid you dip it in the water of a certain fountain, when it will show you a verse in which you may hear of your future.”\n\nThe glow of interest departed from Ben-Hur’s face.\n\n“There are people who have no need to vex themselves about their future,” he said, gloomily.\n\n“Then you prefer to go to the temples?”\n\n“The temples are Greek, are they not?”\n\n“They call them Greek.”\n\n“The Hellenes were masters of the beautiful in art; but in architecture they sacrificed variety to unbending beauty. Their temples are all alike. How call you the fountain?”\n“Castalia.”\n\n“Oh! it has repute throughout the world. Let us thither.”\n\nMalluch kept watch on his companion as they went, and saw that for the moment at least his good spirits were out. To the people passing he gave no attention; over the wonders they came upon there were no exclamations; silently, even sullenly, he kept a slow pace.\n\nThe truth was, the sight of Messala had set Ben-Hur to thinking. It seemed scarce an hour ago that the strong hands had torn him from his mother, scarce an hour ago that the Roman had put seal upon the gates of his father’s house. He recounted how, in the hopeless misery of the life—if such it might be called—in the galleys, he had had little else to do, aside from labor, than dream dreams of vengeance, in all of which Messala was the principal. There might be, he used to say to himself, escape for Gratus, but for Messala—never! And to strengthen and harden his resolution, he was accustomed to repeat over and over, Who pointed us out to the persecutors? And when I begged him for help—not for myself—who mocked me, and went away laughing? And always the dream had the same ending. The day I meet him, help me, thou good God of my people!—help me to some fitting special vengeance!\n\nAnd now the meeting was at hand.\n\nPerhaps, if he had found Messala poor and suffering, Ben-Hur’s feeling had been different; but it was not so. He found him more than prosperous; in the prosperity there was a dash and glitter—gleam of sun on gilt of gold.\n\nSo it happened that what Malluch accounted a passing loss of spirit was pondering when the meeting should be, and in what manner he could make it most memorable.\n\nThey turned after a while into an avenue of oaks, where the people were going and coming in groups; footmen here, and horsemen; there women in litters borne slaves; and now and then chariots rolled by thunderously.\n\nAt the end of the avenue the road, by an easy grade, descended into a lowland, where, on the right hand, there was a precipitous facing of gray rock, and on the left an open meadow of vernal freshness. Then they came in view of the famous Fountain of Castalia.\n\nEdging through a company assembled at the point, Ben-Hur beheld a jet of sweet water pouring from the crest of a stone into a basin of black marble, where, after much boiling and foaming, it disappeared as through a funnel.\n\nBy the basin, under a small portico cut in the solid wall, sat a priest, old, bearded, wrinkled, cowled—never being more perfectly eremitish. From the manner of the people present, hardly might one say which was the attraction, the fountain, forever sparkling, or the priest, forever there. He heard, saw, was seen, but never spoke. Occasionally a visitor extended a hand to him with a coin in it. With a cunning twinkle of the eyes, he took the money, and gave the party in exchange a leaf of papyrus.\n\nThe receiver made haste to plunge the papyrus into the basin; then, holding the dripping leaf in the sunlight, he would be rewarded with a versified inscription upon its face; and the fame of the fountain seldom suffered loss by poverty of merit in the poetry. Before Ben-Hur could test the oracle, some other visitors were seen approaching across the meadow, and their appearance piqued the curiosity of the company, his not less than theirs.\n\nHe saw first a camel, very tall and very white, in leading of a driver on horseback. A houdah on the animal, besides being unusually large, was of crimson and gold. Two other horsemen followed the camel with tall spears in hand.\n\n“What a wonderful camel!” said one of the company.\n\n“A prince from afar,” another one suggested.\n\n“More likely a king.”\n\n“If he were on an elephant, I would say he was a king.”\n\nA third man had a very different opinion.\n\n“A camel—and a white camel!” he said, authoritatively. “By Apollo, friends, they who come yonder—you can see there are two of them—are neither kings nor princes; they are women!”\n\nIn the midst of the dispute the strangers arrived.\n\nThe camel seen at hand did not belie his appearance afar. A taller, statelier brute of his kind no traveller at the fountain, though from the remotest parts, had ever beheld. Such great black eyes! such exceedingly fine white hair! feet so contractile when raised, so soundless in planting, so broad when set!—nobody had ever seen the peer of this camel. And how well he became his housing of silk, and all its frippery of gold in fringe and gold in tassel! The tinkling of silver bells went before him, and he moved lightly, as if unknowing of his burden.\n\nBut who were the man and woman under the houdah?\n\nEvery eye saluted them with the inquiry.\n\nIf the former were a prince or a king, the philosophers of the crowd might not deny the impartiality of Time. When they saw the thin, shrunken face buried under an immense turban, the skin of the hue of a mummy, making it impossible to form an idea of his nationality, they were pleased to think the limit of life was for the great as well as the small. They saw about his person nothing so enviable as the shawl which draped him.\n\nThe woman was seated in the manner of the East, amidst veils and laces of surpassing fineness. Above her elbows she wore armlets fashioned like coiled asps, and linked to bracelets at the wrists by strands of gold; otherwise the arms were bare and of singular natural grace, complemented with hands modelled daintily as a child’s. One of the hands rested upon the side of the carriage, showing tapered fingers glittering with rings, and stained at the tips till they blushed like the pink of mother-of-pearl. She wore an open caul upon her head, sprinkled with beads of coral, and strung with coin-pieces called sunlets, some of which were carried across her forehead, while others fell down her back, half-smothered in the mass of her straight blue-black hair, of itself an incomparable ornament, not needing the veil which covered it, except as a protection against sun and dust. From her elevated seat she looked upon the people calmly, pleasantly, and apparently so intent upon studying them as to be unconscious of the interest she herself was exciting; and, what was unusual—nay, in violent contravention of the custom among women of rank in public—she looked at them with an open face.\n\nIt was a fair face to see; quite youthful; in form, oval: complexion not white, like the Greek; nor brunet, like the Roman; nor blond, like the Gaul; but rather the tinting of the sun of the Upper Nile upon a skin of such transparency that the blood shone through it on cheek and brow with nigh the ruddiness of lamplight. The eyes, naturally large, were touched along the lids with the black paint immemorial throughout the East. The lips were slightly parted, disclosing, through their scarlet lake, teeth of glistening whiteness. To all these excellences of countenance the reader is finally besought to superadd the air derived from the pose of a small head, classic in shape, set upon a neck long, drooping, and graceful—the air, we may fancy, happily described by the word queenly.\n\nAs if satisfied with the survey of people and locality, the fair creature spoke to the driver—an Ethiopian of vast brawn, naked to the waist—who led the camel nearer the fountain, and caused it to kneel; after which he received from her hand a cup, and proceeded to fill it at the basin. That instant the sound of wheels and the trampling of horses in rapid motion broke the silence her beauty had imposed, and, with a great outcry, the bystanders parted in every direction, hurrying to get away.\n\n“The Roman has a mind to ride us down. Look out!” Malluch shouted to Ben-Hur, setting him at the same time an example of hasty flight.\n\nThe latter faced to the direction the sounds came from, and beheld Messala in his chariot pushing the four straight at the crowd. This time the view was near and distinct.\n\nThe parting of the company uncovered the camel, which might have been more agile than his kind generally; yet the hoofs were almost upon him, and he resting with closed eyes, chewing the endless cud with such sense of security as long favoritism may be supposed to have bred in him. The Ethiopian wrung his hands afraid. In the houdah, the old man moved to escape; but he was hampered with age, and could not, even in the face of danger, forget the dignity which was plainly his habit. It was too late for the woman to save herself. Ben-Hur stood nearest them, and he called to Messala,\n\n“Hold! Look where thou goest! Back, back!”\n\nThe patrician was laughing in hearty good-humor; and, seeing there was but one chance of rescue, Ben-Hur stepped in, and caught the bits of the left yoke-steed and his mate. “Dog of a Roman! Carest thou so little for life?” he cried, putting forth all his strength. The two horses reared, and drew the others round; the tilting of the pole tilted the chariot; Messala barely escaped a fall, while his complacent Myrtilus rolled back like a clod to the ground. Seeing the peril past, all the bystanders burst into derisive laughter.\n\nThe matchless audacity of the Roman then manifested itself. Loosing the lines from his body, he tossed them to one side, dismounted, walked round the camel, looked at Ben-Hur, and spoke partly to the old man and partly to the woman.\n\n“Pardon, I pray you—I pray you both. I am Messala,” he said; “and, by the old Mother of the earth, I swear I did not see you or your camel! As to these good people—perhaps I trusted too much to my skill. I sought a laugh at them—the laugh is theirs. Good may it do them!”\n\nThe good-natured, careless look and gesture he threw the bystanders accorded well with the speech. To hear what more he had to say, they became quiet. Assured of victory over the body of the offended, he signed his companion to take the chariot to a safer distance, and addressed himself boldly to the woman.\n\n“Thou hast interest in the good man here, whose pardon, if not granted now, I shall seek with the greater diligence hereafter; his daughter, I should say.”\n\nShe made him no reply.\n\n“By Pallas, thou art beautiful! Beware Apollo mistake thee not for his lost love. I wonder what land can boast herself thy mother. Turn not away. A truce! a truce! There is the sun of India in thine eyes; in the corners of thy mouth, Egypt hath set her love-signs. Perpol! Turn not to that slave, fair mistress, before proving merciful to this one. Tell me at least that I am pardoned.”\n\nAt this point she broke in upon him.\n\n“Wilt thou come here?” she asked, smiling, and with gracious bend of the head to Ben-Hur.\n\n“Take the cup and fill it, I pray thee,” she said to the latter. “My father is thirsty.”\n\n“I am thy most willing servant!”\n\nBen-Hur turned about to do the favor, and was face to face with Messala. Their glances met; the Jew’s defiant; the Roman’s sparkling with humor.\n\n“O stranger, beautiful as cruel!” Messala said, waving his hand to her. “If Apollo get thee not, thou shalt see me again. Not knowing thy country, I cannot name a god to commend thee to; so, by all the gods, I will commend thee to—myself!”\n\nSeeing that Myrtilus had the four composed and ready, he returned to the chariot. The woman looked after him as he moved away, and whatever else there was in her look, there was no displeasure. Presently she received the water; her father drank; then she raised the cup to her lips, and, leaning down, gave it to Ben-Hur; never action more graceful and gracious.\n\n“Keep it, we pray of thee! It is full of blessings—all thine!”\n\nImmediately the camel was aroused, and on his feet, and about to go, when the old man called,\n\n“Stand thou here.”\n\nBen-Hur went to him respectfully.\n\n“Thou hast served the stranger well to-day. There is but one God. In his holy name I thank thee. I am Balthasar, the Egyptian. In the Great Orchard of Palms, beyond the village of Daphne, in the shade of the palms, Sheik Ilderim the Generous abideth in his tents, and we are his guests. Seek us there. Thou shalt have welcome sweet with the savor of the grateful.”\n\nBen-Hur was left in wonder at the old man’s clear voice and reverend manner. As he gazed after the two departing, he caught sight of Messala going as he had come, joyous, indifferent, and with a mocking laugh.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nAs a rule, there is no surer way to the dislike of men than to behave well where they have behaved badly. In this instance, happily, Malluch was an exception to the rule. The affair he had just witnessed raised Ben-Hur in his estimation, since he could not deny him courage and address; could he now get some insight into the young man’s history, the results of the day would not be all unprofitable to good master Simonides.\n\nOn the latter point, referring to what he had as yet learned, two facts comprehended it all—the subject of his investigation was a Jew, and the adopted son of a famous Roman. Another conclusion which might be of importance was beginning to formulate itself in the shrewd mind of the emissary; between Messala and the son of the duumvir there was a connection of some kind. But what was it?—and how could it be reduced to assurance? With all his sounding, the ways and means of solution were not at call. In the heat of the perplexity, Ben-Hur himself came to his help. He laid his hand on Malluch’s arm and drew him out of the crowd, which was already going back to its interest in the gray old priest and the mystic fountain.\n\n“Good Malluch,” he said, stopping, “may a man forget his mother?”\n\nThe question was abrupt and without direction, and therefore of the kind which leaves the person addressed in a state of confusion. Malluch looked into Ben-Hur’s face for a hint of meaning, but saw, instead, two bright-red spots, one on each cheek, and in his eyes traces of what might have been repressed tears; then he answered, mechanically, “No!” adding, with fervor, “never;” and a moment after, when he began to recover himself, “If he is an Israelite, never!” And when at length he was completely recovered—“My first lesson in the synagogue was the Shema; my next was the saying of the son of Sirach, ‘Honor thy father with thy whole soul, and forget not the sorrows of thy mother.’”\n\nThe red spots on Ben-Hur’s face deepened.\n\n“The words bring my childhood back again; and, Malluch, they prove you a genuine Jew. I believe I can trust you.”\n\nBen-Hur let go the arm he was holding, and caught the folds of the gown covering his own breast, and pressed them close, as if to smother a pain, or a feeling there as sharp as a pain.\n\n“My father,” he said, “bore a good name, and was not without honor in Jerusalem, where he dwelt. My mother, at his death, was in the prime of womanhood; and it is not enough to say of her she was good and beautiful: in her tongue was the law of kindness, and her works were the praise of all in the gates, and she smiled at days to come. I had a little sister, and she and I were the family, and we were so happy that I, at least, have never seen harm in the saying of the old rabbi, ‘God could not be everywhere, and, therefore, he made mothers.’ One day an accident happened to a Roman in authority as he was riding past our house at the head of a cohort; the legionaries burst the gate and rushed in and seized us. I have not seen my mother or sister since. I cannot say they are dead or living. I do not know what became of them. But, Malluch, the man in the chariot yonder was present at the separation; he gave us over to the captors; he heard my mother’s prayer for her children, and he laughed when they dragged her away. Hardly may one say which graves deepest in memory, love or hate. To-day I knew him afar—and, Malluch—”\n\nHe caught the listener’s arm again.\n\n“And, Malluch, he knows and takes with him now the secret I would give my life for: he could tell if she lives, and where she is, and her condition; if she—no, THEY—much sorrow has made the two as one—if they are dead, he could tell where they died, and of what, and where their bones await my finding.”\n\n“And will he not?”\n\n“No.”\n“Why?”\n\n“I am a Jew, and he is a Roman.”\n\n“But Romans have tongues, and Jews, though ever so despised, have methods to beguile them.”\n\n“For such as he? No; and, besides, the secret is one of state. All my father’s property was confiscated and divided.”\n\nMalluch nodded his head slowly, much as to admit the argument; then he asked anew, “Did he not recognize you?”\n\n“He could not. I was sent to death in life, and have been long since accounted of the dead.”\n\n“I wonder you did not strike him,” said Malluch, yielding to a touch of passion.\n\n“That would have been to put him past serving me forever. I would have had to kill him, and Death, you know, keeps secrets better even than a guilty Roman.”\n\nThe man who, with so much to avenge, could so calmly put such an opportunity aside must be confident of his future or have ready some better design, and Malluch’s interest changed with the thought; it ceased to be that of an emissary in duty bound to another. Ben-Hur was actually asserting a claim upon him for his own sake. In other words, Malluch was preparing to serve him with good heart and from downright admiration.\n\nAfter brief pause, Ben-Hur resumed speaking.\n\n“I would not take his life, good Malluch; against that extreme the possession of the secret is for the present, at least, his safeguard; yet I may punish him, and so you give me help, I will try.”\n\n“He is a Roman,” said Malluch, without hesitation; “and I am of the tribe of Judah. I will help you. If you choose, put me under oath—under the most solemn oath.”\n\n“Give me your hand, that will suffice.”\n\nAs their hands fell apart, Ben-Hur said, with lightened feeling, “That I would charge you with is not difficult, good friend; neither is it dreadful to conscience. Let us move on.”\n\nThey took the road which led to the right across the meadow spoken of in the description of the coming to the fountain. Ben-Hur was first to break the silence.\n\n“Do you know Sheik Ilderim the Generous?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Where is his Orchard of Palms? or, rather, Malluch, how far is it beyond the village of Daphne?”\n\nMalluch was touched by a doubt; he recalled the prettiness of the favor shown him by the woman at the fountain, and wondered if he who had the sorrows of a mother in mind was about to forget them for a lure of love; yet he replied, “The Orchard of Palms lies beyond the village two hours by horse, and one by swift camel.”\n\n“Thank you; and to your knowledge once more. Have the games of which you told me been widely published? and when will they take place?”\n\nThe questions were suggestive; and if they did not restore Malluch his confidence, they at least stimulated his curiosity.\n\n“Oh yes, they will be of ample splendor. The prefect is rich, and could afford to lose his place; yet, as is the way with successful men, his love of riches is nowise diminished; and to gain a friend at court, if nothing more, he must make ado for the Consul Maxentius, who is coming hither to make final preparations for a campaign against the Parthians. The money there is in the preparations the citizens of Antioch know from experience; so they have had permission to join the prefect in the honors intended for the great man. A month ago heralds went to the four quarters to proclaim the opening of the Circus for the celebration. The name of the prefect would be of itself good guarantee of variety and magnificence, particularly throughout the East; but when to his promises Antioch joins hers, all the islands and the cities by the sea stand assured of the extraordinary, and will be here in person or by their most famous professionals. The fees offered are royal.”\n\n“And the Circus—I have heard it is second only to the Maximus.”\n\n“At Rome, you mean. Well, ours seats two hundred thousand people, yours seats seventy-five thousand more; yours is of marble, so is ours; in arrangement they are exactly the same.”\n\n“Are the rules the same?”\n\nMalluch smiled.\n\n“If Antioch dared be original, son of Arrius, Rome would not be the mistress she is. The laws of the Circus Maximus govern except in one particular: there but four chariots may start at once, here all start without reference to number.”\n\n“That is the practise of the Greeks,” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“Yes, Antioch is more Greek than Roman.”\n\n“So then, Malluch, I may choose my own chariot?”\n\n“Your own chariot and horses. There is no restriction upon either.”\n\nWhile replying, Malluch observed the thoughtful look on Ben-Hur’s face give place to one of satisfaction.\n\n“One thing more now, O Malluch. When will the celebration be?”\n\n“Ah! your pardon,” the other answered. “To-morrow—and the next day,” he said, counting aloud, “then, to speak in the Roman style, if the sea-gods be propitious, the consul arrives. Yes, the sixth day from this we have the games.”\n\n“The time is short, Malluch, but it is enough.” The last words were spoken decisively. “By the prophets of our old Israel! I will take to the reins again. Stay! a condition; is there assurance that Messala will be a competitor?”\n\nMalluch saw now the plan, and all its opportunities for the humiliation of the Roman; and he had not been true descendant of Jacob if, with all his interest wakened, he had not rushed to a consideration of the chances. His voice actually trembled as he said, “Have you the practise?”\n\n“Fear not, my friend. The winners in the Circus Maximus have held their crowns these three years at my will. Ask them—ask the best of them—and they will tell you so. In the last great games the emperor himself offered me his patronage if I would take his horses in hand and run them against the entries of the world.”\n\n“But you did not?”\n\nMalluch spoke eagerly.\n\n“I—I am a Jew”—Ben-Hur seemed shrinking within himself as he spoke—“and, though I wear a Roman name, I dared not do professionally a thing to sully my father’s name in the cloisters and courts of the Temple. In the palaestrae I could indulge practise which, if followed into the Circus, would become an abomination; and if I take to the course here, Malluch, I swear it will not be for the prize or the winner’s fee.”\n\n“Hold—swear not so!” cried Malluch. “The fee is ten thousand sestertii—a fortune for life!”\n\n“Not for me, though the prefect trebled it fifty times. Better than that, better than all the imperial revenues from the first year of the first Cæsar—I will make this race to humble my enemy. Vengeance is permitted by the law.”\n\nMalluch smiled and nodded as if saying, “Right, right—trust me a Jew to understand a Jew.”\n\n“The Messala will drive,” he said, directly. “He is committed to the race in many ways—by publication in the streets, and in the baths and theaters, the palace and barracks; and, to fix him past retreat, his name is on the tablets of every young spendthrift in Antioch.”\n\n“In wager, Malluch?”\n\n“Yes, in wager; and every day he comes ostentatiously to practise, as you saw him.”\n\n“Ah! and that is the chariot, and those the horses, with which he will make the race? Thank you, thank you, Malluch! You have served me well already. I am satisfied. Now be my guide to the Orchard of Palms, and give me introduction to Sheik Ilderim the Generous.”\n\n“When?”\n“To-day. His horses may be engaged to-morrow.”\n\n“You like them, then?”\n\nBen-Hur answered with animation,\n\n“I saw them from the stand an instant only, for Messala then drove up, and I might not look at anything else; yet I recognized them as of the blood which is the wonder as well as the glory of the deserts. I never saw the kind before, except in the stables of Cæsar; but once seen, they are always to be known. To-morrow, upon meeting, I will know you, Malluch, though you do not so much as salute me; I will know you by your face, by your form, by your manner; and by the same signs I will know them, and with the same certainty. If all that is said of them be true, and I can bring their spirit under control of mine, I can—”\n\n“Win the sestertii!” said Malluch, laughing.\n\n“No,” answered Ben-Hur, as quickly. “I will do what better becomes a man born to the heritage of Jacob—I will humble mine enemy in a most public place. But,” he added, impatiently, “we are losing time. How can we most quickly reach the tents of the sheik?”\n\nMalluch took a moment for reflection.\n\n“It is best we go straight to the village, which is fortunately near by; if two swift camels are to be had for hire there, we will be on the road but an hour.”\n\n“Let us about it, then.”\n\nThe village was an assemblage of palaces in beautiful gardens, interspersed with khans of princely sort. Dromedaries were happily secured, and upon them the journey to the famous Orchard of Palms was begun.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\nBeyond the village the country was undulating and cultivated; in fact, it was the garden-land of Antioch, with not a foot lost to labor. The steep faces of the hills were terraced; even the hedges were brighter of the trailing vines which, besides the lure of shade, offered passers-by sweet promises of wine to come, and grapes in clustered purple ripeness. Over melon-patches, and through apricot and fig-tree groves, and groves of oranges and limes, the white-washed houses of the farmers were seen; and everywhere Plenty, the smiling daughter of Peace, gave notice by her thousand signs that she was at home, making the generous traveller merry at heart, until he was even disposed to give Rome her dues. Occasionally, also, views were had of Taurus and Lebanon, between which, a separating line of silver, the Orontes placidly pursued its way.\n\nIn course of their journey the friends came to the river, which they followed with the windings of the road, now over bold bluffs, and then into vales, all alike allotted for country-seats, and if the land was in full foliage of oak and sycamore and myrtle, and bay and arbutus, and perfuming jasmine, the river was bright with slanted sunlight, which would have slept where it fell but for ships in endless procession, gliding with the current, tacking for the wind, or bounding under the impulse of oars—some coming, some going, and all suggestive of the sea, and distant peoples, and famous places, and things coveted on account of their rarity. To the fancy there is nothing so winsome as a white sail seaward blown, unless it be a white sail homeward bound, its voyage happily done. And down the shore the friends went continuously till they came to a lake fed by back-water from the river, clear, deep, and without current. An old palm-tree dominated the angle of the inlet; turning to the left at the foot of the tree, Malluch clapped his hands and shouted,\n\n“Look, look! The Orchard of Palms!”\n\nThe scene was nowhere else to be found unless in the favored oases of Arabia or the Ptolemaean farms along the Nile; and to sustain a sensation new as it was delightful, Ben-Hur was admitted into a tract of land apparently without limit and level as a floor. All under foot was fresh grass, in Syria the rarest and most beautiful production of the soil; if he looked up, it was to see the sky paley blue through the groinery of countless date-bearers, very patriarchs of their kind, so numerous and old, and of such mighty girth, so tall, so serried, so wide of branch, each branch so perfect with fronds, plumy and waxlike and brilliant, they seemed enchanters enchanted. Here was the grass coloring the very atmosphere; there the lake, cool and clear, rippling but a few feet under the surface, and helping the trees to their long life in old age. Did the Grove of Daphne excel this one? And the palms, as if they knew Ben-Hur’s thought, and would win him after a way of their own, seemed, as he passed under their arches, to stir and sprinkle him with dewy coolness.\n\nThe road wound in close parallelism with the shore of the lake; and when it carried the travellers down to the water’s edge, there was always on that side a shining expanse limited not far off by the opposite shore, on which, as on this one, no tree but the palm was permitted.\n\n“See that,” said Malluch, pointing to a giant of the place. “Each ring upon its trunk marks a year of its life. Count them from root to branch, and if the sheik tells you the grove was planted before the Seleucidæ were heard of in Antioch, do not doubt him.”\n\nOne may not look at a perfect palm-tree but that, with a subtlety all its own, it assumes a presence for itself, and makes a poet of the beholder. This is the explanation of the honors it has received, beginning with the artists of the first kings, who could find no form in all the earth to serve them so well as a model for the pillars of their palaces and temples; and for the same reason Ben-Hur was moved to say,\n\n“As I saw him at the stand to-day, good Malluch, Sheik Ilderim appeared to be a very common man. The rabbis in Jerusalem would look down upon him, I fear, as a son of a dog of Edom. How came he in possession of the Orchard? And how has he been able to hold it against the greed of Roman governors?”\n\n“If blood derives excellence from time, son of Arrius, then is old Ilderim a man, though he be an uncircumcised Edomite.”\n\nMalluch spoke warmly.\n\n“All his fathers before him were sheiks. One of them—I shall not say when he lived or did the good deed—once helped a king who was being hunted with swords. The story says he loaned him a thousand horsemen, who knew the paths of the wilderness and its hiding-places as shepherds know the scant hills they inhabit with their flocks; and they carried him here and there until the opportunity came, and then with their spears they slew the enemy, and set him upon his throne again. And the king, it is said, remembered the service, and brought the son of the Desert to this place, and bade him set up his tent and bring his family and his herds, for the lake and trees, and all the land from the river to the nearest mountains, were his and his children’s forever. And they have never been disturbed in the possession. The rulers succeeding have found it policy to keep good terms with the tribe, to whom the Lord has given increase of men and horses, and camels and riches, making them masters of many highways between cities; so that it is with them any time they please to say to commerce, ‘Go in peace,’ or ‘Stop,’ and what they say shall be done. Even the prefect in the citadel overlooking Antioch thinks it happy day with him when Ilderim, surnamed the Generous on account of good deeds done unto all manner of men, with his wives and children, and his trains of camels and horses, and his belongings of sheik, moving as our fathers Abraham and Jacob moved, comes up to exchange briefly his bitter wells for the pleasantness you see about us.”\n\n“How is it, then?” said Ben-Hur, who had been listening unmindful of the slow gait of the dromedaries. “I saw the sheik tear his beard while he cursed himself that he had put trust in a Roman. Cæsar, had he heard him, might have said, ‘I like not such a friend as this; put him away.’”\n\n“It would be but shrewd judgment,” Malluch replied, smiling. “Ilderim is not a lover of Rome; he has a grievance. Three years ago the Parthians rode across the road from Bozra to Damascus, and fell upon a caravan laden, among other things, with the incoming tax-returns of a district over that way. They slew every creature taken, which the censors in Rome could have forgiven if the imperial treasure had been spared and forwarded. The farmers of the taxes, being chargeable with the loss, complained to Cæsar, and Cæsar held Herod to payment, and Herod, on his part, seized property of Ilderim, whom he charged with treasonable neglect of duty. The sheik appealed to Cæsar, and Cæsar has made him such answer as might be looked for from the unwinking sphinx. The old man’s heart has been aching sore ever since, and he nurses his wrath, and takes pleasure in its daily growth.”\n\n“He can do nothing, Malluch.”\n\n“Well,” said Malluch, “that involves another explanation, which I will give you, if we can draw nearer. But see!—the hospitality of the sheik begins early—the children are speaking to you.”\n\nThe dromedaries stopped, and Ben-Hur looked down upon some little girls of the Syrian peasant class, who were offering him their baskets filled with dates. The fruit was freshly gathered, and not to be refused; he stooped and took it, and as he did so a man in the tree by which they were halted cried, “Peace to you, and welcome!”\n\nTheir thanks said to the children, the friends moved on at such gait as the animals chose.\n\n“You must know,” Malluch continued, pausing now and then to dispose of a date, “that the merchant Simonides gives me his confidence, and sometimes flatters me by taking me into council; and as I attend him at his house, I have made acquaintance with many of his friends, who, knowing my footing with the host, talk to him freely in my presence. In that way I became somewhat intimate with Sheik Ilderim.”\n\nFor a moment Ben-Hur’s attention wandered. Before his mind’s eye there arose the image, pure, gentle, and appealing, of Esther, the merchant’s daughter. Her dark eyes bright with the peculiar Jewish lustre met his in modest gaze; he heard her step as when she approached him with the wine, and her voice as she tendered him the cup; and he acknowledged to himself again all the sympathy she manifested for him, and manifested so plainly that words were unnecessary, and so sweetly that words would have been but a detraction. The vision was exceeding pleasant, but upon his turning to Malluch, it flew away.\n\n“A few weeks ago,” said Malluch, continuing, “the old Arab called on Simonides, and found me present. I observed he seemed much moved about something, and, in deference, offered to withdraw, but he himself forbade me. ‘As you are an Israelite,’ he said, ‘stay, for I have a strange story to tell.’ The emphasis on the word Israelite excited my curiosity. I remained, and this is in substance his story—I cut it short because we are drawing nigh the tent, and I leave the details to the good man himself. A good many years ago, three men called at Ilderim’s tent out in the wilderness. They were all foreigners, a Hindoo, a Greek, and an Egyptian; and they had come on camels, the largest he had ever seen, and all white. He welcomed them, and gave them rest. Next morning they arose and prayed a prayer new to the sheik—a prayer addressed to God and his son—this with much mystery besides. After breaking fast with him, the Egyptian told who they were, and whence they had come. Each had seen a star, out of which a voice had bidden them go to Jerusalem and ask, Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’ They obeyed. From Jerusalem they were led by a star to Bethlehem, where, in a cave, they found a child newly born, which they fell down and worshipped; and after worshipping it, and giving it costly presents, and bearing witness of what it was, they took to their camels, and fled without pause to the sheik, because if Herod—meaning him surnamed the Great—could lay hands upon them, he would certainly kill them. And, faithful to his habit, the sheik took care of them, and kept them concealed for a year, when they departed, leaving with him gifts of great value, and each going a separate way.”\n\n“It is, indeed, a most wonderful story,” Ben-Hur exclaimed at its conclusion. “What did you say they were to ask at Jerusalem?”\n\n“They were to ask, ‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’”\n\n“Was that all?”\n\n“There was more to the question, but I cannot recall it.”\n\n“And they found the child?”\n\n“Yes, and worshipped him.”\n\n“It is a miracle, Malluch.”\n\n“Ilderim is a grave man, though excitable as all Arabs are. A lie on his tongue is impossible.”\n\nMalluch spoke positively. Thereupon the dromedaries were forgotten, and, quite as unmindful of their riders, they turned off the road to the growing grass.\n\n“Has Ilderim heard nothing more of the three men?” asked Ben-Hur. “What became of them?”\n\n“Ah, yes, that was the cause of his coming to Simonides the day of which I was speaking. Only the night before that day the Egyptian reappeared to him.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Here at the door of the tent to which we are coming.”\n\n“How knew he the man?”\n\n“As you knew the horses to-day—by face and manner.”\n\n“By nothing else?”\n\n“He rode the same great white camel, and gave him the same name—Balthasar, the Egyptian.”\n\n“It is a wonder of the Lord’s!”\n\nBen-Hur spoke with excitement.\n\nAnd Malluch, wondering, asked, “Why so?”\n\n“Balthasar, you said?”\n\n“Yes. Balthasar, the Egyptian.”\n\n“That was the name the old man gave us at the fountain today.”\n\nThen, at the reminder, Malluch became excited.\n\n“It is true,” he said; “and the camel was the same—and you saved the man’s life.”\n\n“And the woman,” said Ben-Hur, like one speaking to himself—“the woman was his daughter.”\n\nHe fell to thinking; and even the reader will say he was having a vision of the woman, and that it was more welcome than that of Esther, if only because it stayed longer with him; but no—\n\n“Tell me again,” he said, presently. “Were the three to ask, ‘Where is he that is to be King of the Jews?’”\n\n“Not exactly. The words were BORN TO BE KING OF THE JEWS. Those were the words as the old sheik caught them first in the desert, and he has ever since been waiting the coming of the king; nor can any one shake his faith that he will come.”\n\n“How—as king?”\n“Yes, and bringing the doom of Rome—so says the sheik.”\n\nBen-Hur kept silent awhile, thinking and trying to control his feelings.\n\n“The old man is one of many millions,” he said, slowly—“one of many millions each with a wrong to avenge; and this strange faith, Malluch, is bread and wine to his hope; for who but a Herod may be King of the Jews while Rome endures? But, following the story, did you hear what Simonides said to him?”\n\n“If Ilderim is a grave man, Simonides is a wise one,” Malluch replied. “I listened, and he said— But hark! Some one comes overtaking us.”\n\nThe noise grew louder, until presently they heard the rumble of wheels mixed with the beating of horse-hoofs—a moment later Sheik Ilderim himself appeared on horseback, followed by a train, among which were the four wine-red Arabs drawing the chariot. The sheik’s chin, in its muffling of long white beard, was drooped upon his breast. Our friends had out-travelled him; but at sight of them he raised his head and spoke kindly.\n\n“Peace to you!—Ah, my friend Malluch! Welcome! And tell me you are not going, but just come; that you have something for me from the good Simonides—may the Lord of his fathers keep him in life for many years to come! Ay, take up the straps, both of you, and follow me. I have bread and leben, or, if you prefer it, arrack, and the flesh of young kid. Come!”\n\nThey followed after him to the door of the tent, in which, when they were dismounted, he stood to receive them, holding a platter with three cups filled with creamy liquor just drawn from a great smoke-stained skin bottle, pendent from the central post.\n\n“Drink,” he said, heartily, “drink, for this is the fear-naught of the tentmen.”\n\nThey each took a cup, and drank till but the foam remained.\n\n“Enter now, in God’s name.”\n\nAnd when they were gone in, Malluch took the sheik aside, and spoke to him privately; after which he went to Ben-Hur and excused himself.\n\n“I have told the sheik about you, and he will give you the trial of his horses in the morning. He is your friend. Having done for you all I can, you must do the rest, and let me return to Antioch. There is one there who has my promise to meet him to-night. I have no choice but to go. I will come back to-morrow prepared, if all goes well in the meantime, to stay with you until the games are over.”\n\nWith blessings given and received, Malluch set out in return.\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\nWhat time the lower horn of a new moon touched the castellated piles on Mount Sulpius, and two thirds of the people of Antioch were out on their house-tops comforting themselves with the night breeze when it blew, and with fans when it failed, Simonides sat in the chair which had come to be a part of him, and from the terrace looked down over the river, and his ships a-swing at their moorings. The wall at his back cast its shadow broadly over the water to the opposite shore. Above him the endless tramp upon the bridge went on. Esther was holding a plate for him containing his frugal supper—some wheaten cakes, light as wafers, some honey, and a bowl of milk, into which he now and then dipped the wafers after dipping them into the honey.\n\n“Malluch is a laggard to-night,” he said, showing where his thoughts were.\n\n“Do you believe he will come?” Esther asked.\n\n“Unless he has taken to the sea or the desert, and is yet following on, he will come.”\n\nSimonides spoke with quiet confidence.\n\n“He may write,” she said.\n\n“Not so, Esther. He would have despatched a letter when he found he could not return, and told me so; because I have not received such a letter, I know he can come, and will.”\n\n“I hope so,” she said, very softly.\n\nSomething in the utterance attracted his attention; it might have been the tone, it might have been the wish. The smallest bird cannot light upon the greatest tree without sending a shock to its most distant fibre; every mind is at times no less sensitive to the most trifling words.\n\n“You wish him to come, Esther?” he asked.\n\n“Yes,” she said, lifting her eyes to his.\n\n“Why? Can you tell me?” he persisted.\n\n“Because”—she hesitated, then began again—“because the young man is—” The stop was full.\n\n“Our master. Is that the word?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And you still think I should not suffer him to go away without telling him to come, if he chooses, and take us—and all we have—all, Esther—the goods, the shekels, the ships, the slaves, and the mighty credit, which is a mantle of cloth of gold and finest silver spun for me by the greatest of the angels of men—Success.”\n\nShe made no answer.\n\n“Does that move you nothing? No?” he said, with the slightest taint of bitterness. “Well, well, I have found, Esther, the worst reality is never unendurable when it comes out from behind the clouds through which we at first see it darkly—never—not even the rack. I suppose it will be so with death. And by that philosophy the slavery to which we are going must afterwhile become sweet. It pleases me even now to think what a favored man our master is. The fortune cost him nothing—not an anxiety, not a drop of sweat, not so much as a thought; it attaches to him undreamed of, and in his youth. And, Esther, let me waste a little vanity with the reflection; he gets what he could not go into the market and buy with all the pelf in a sum—thee, my child, my darling; thou blossom from the tomb of my lost Rachel!”\n\nHe drew her to him, and kissed her twice—once for herself, once for her mother.\n\n“Say not so,” she said, when his hand fell from her neck. “Let us think better of him; he knows what sorrow is, and will set us free.”\n\n“Ah, thy instincts are fine, Esther; and thou knowest I lean upon them in doubtful cases where good or bad is to be pronounced of a person standing before thee as he stood this morning. But—but”—his voice rose and hardened—“these limbs upon which I cannot stand—this body drawn and beaten out of human shape—they are not all I bring him of myself. Oh no, no! I bring him a soul which has triumphed over torture and Roman malice keener than any torture—I bring him a mind which has eyes to see gold at a distance farther than the ships of Solomon sailed, and power to bring it to hand—ay, Esther, into my palm here for the fingers to grip and keep lest it take wings at some other’s word—a mind skilled at scheming”—he stopped and laughed—“Why, Esther, before the new moon which in the courts of the Temple on the Holy Hill they are this moment celebrating passes into its next quartering I could ring the world so as to startle even Cæsar; for know you, child, I have that faculty which is better than any one sense, better than a perfect body, better than courage and will, better than experience, ordinarily the best product of the longest lives—the faculty divinest of men, but which”—he stopped, and laughed again, not bitterly, but with real zest—“but which even the great do not sufficiently account, while with the herd it is a non-existent—the faculty of drawing men to my purpose and holding them faithfully to its achievement, by which, as against things to be done, I multiply myself into hundreds and thousands. So the captains of my ships plough the seas, and bring me honest returns; so Malluch follows the youth, our master, and will”—just then a footstep was heard upon the terrace—“Ha, Esther! said I not so? He is here—and we will have tidings. For thy sake, sweet child—my lily just budded—I pray the Lord God, who has not forgotten his wandering sheep of Israel, that they be good and comforting. Now we will know if he will let thee go with all thy beauty, and me with all my faculties.”\n\nMalluch came to the chair.\n\n“Peace to you, good master,” he said, with a low obeisance—“and to you, Esther, most excellent of daughters.”\n\nHe stood before them deferentially, and the attitude and the address left it difficult to define his relation to them; the one was that of a servant, the other indicated the familiar and friend. On the other side, Simonides, as was his habit in business, after answering the salutation went straight to the subject.\n\n“What of the young man, Malluch?”\n\nThe events of the day were told quietly and in the simplest words, and until he was through there was no interruption; nor did the listener in the chair so much as move a hand during the narration; but for his eyes, wide open and bright, and an occasional long-drawn breath, he might have been accounted an effigy.\n\n“Thank you, thank you, Malluch,” he said, heartily, at the conclusion; “you have done well—no one could have done better. Now what say you of the young man’s nationality?”\n\n“He is an Israelite, good master, and of the tribe of Judah.”\n\n“You are positive?”\n\n“Very positive.”\n\n“He appears to have told you but little of his life.”\n\n“He has somewhere learned to be prudent. I might call him distrustful. He baffled all my attempts upon his confidence until we started from the Castalian fount going to the village of Daphne.”\n\n“A place of abomination! Why went he there?”\n\n“I would say from curiosity, the first motive of the many who go; but, very strangely, he took no interest in the things he saw. Of the Temple, he merely asked if it were Grecian. Good master, the young man has a trouble of mind from which he would hide, and he went to the Grove, I think, as we go to sepulchres with our dead—he went to bury it.”\n\n“That were well, if so,” Simonides said, in a low voice; then louder, “Malluch, the curse of the time is prodigality. The poor make themselves poorer as apes of the rich, and the merely rich carry themselves like princes. Saw you signs of the weakness in the youth? Did he display moneys—coin of Rome or Israel?”\n\n“None, none, good master.”\n\n“Surely, Malluch, where there are so many inducements to folly—so much, I mean, to eat and drink—surely he made you generous offer of some sort. His age, if nothing more, would warrant that much.”\n\n“He neither ate nor drank in my company.”\n\n“In what he said or did, Malluch, could you in anywise detect his master-idea? You know they peep through cracks close enough to stop the wind.”\n\n“Give me to understand you,” said Malluch, in doubt.\n\n“Well, you know we nor speak nor act, much less decide grave questions concerning ourselves, except as we be driven by a motive. In that respect, what made you of him?”\n\n“As to that, Master Simonides, I can answer with much assurance. He is devoted to finding his mother and sister—that first. Then he has a grievance against Rome; and as the Messala of whom I told you had something to do with the wrong, the great present object is to humiliate him. The meeting at the fountain furnished an opportunity, but it was put aside as not sufficiently public.”\n\n“The Messala is influential,” said Simonides, thoughtfully.\n\n“Yes; but the next meeting will be in the Circus.”\n\n“Well—and then?”\n\n“The son of Arrius will win.”\n\n“How know you?”\n\nMalluch smiled.\n\n“I am judging by what he says.”\n\n“Is that all?”\n\n“No; there is a much better sign—his spirit.”\n\n“Ay; but, Malluch, his idea of vengeance—what is its scope? Does he limit it to the few who did him the wrong, or does he take in the many? And more—is his feeling but the vagary of a sensitive boy, or has it the seasoning of suffering manhood to give it endurance? You know, Malluch, the vengeful thought that has root merely in the mind is but a dream of idlest sort which one clear day will dissipate; while revenge the passion is a disease of the heart which climbs up, up to the brain, and feeds itself on both alike.”\n\nIn this question, Simonides for the first time showed signs of feeling; he spoke with rapid utterance, and with clenched hands and the eagerness of a man illustrating the disease he described.\n\n“Good my master,” Malluch replied, “one of my reasons for believing the young man a Jew is the intensity of his hate. It was plain to me he had himself under watch, as was natural, seeing how long he has lived in an atmosphere of Roman jealousy; yet I saw it blaze—once when he wanted to know Ilderim’s feeling towards Rome, and again when I told him the story of the sheik and the wise man, and spoke of the question, ‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’”\n\nSimonides leaned forward quickly.\n\n“Ah, Malluch, his words—give me his words; let me judge the impression the mystery made upon him.”\n\n“He wanted to know the exact words. Were they TO BE or BORN TO BE? It appeared he was struck by a seeming difference in the effect of the two phrases.”\n\nSimonides settled back into his pose of listening judge.\n\n“Then,” said Malluch, “I told him Ilderim’s view of the mystery—that the king would come with the doom of Rome. The young man’s blood rose over his cheeks and forehead, and he said earnestly, ‘Who but a Herod can be king while Rome endures?’”\n\n“Meaning what?”\n\n“That the empire must be destroyed before there could be another rule.”\n\nSimonides gazed for a time at the ships and their shadows slowly swinging together in the river; when he looked up, it was to end the interview.\n\n“Enough, Malluch,” he said. “Get you to eat, and make ready to return to the Orchard of Palms; you must help the young man in his coming trial. Come to me in the morning. I will send a letter to Ilderim.” Then in an undertone, as if to himself, he added, “I may attend the Circus myself.”\n\nWhen Malluch after the customary benediction given and received was gone, Simonides took a deep draught of milk, and seemed refreshed and easy of mind.\n\n“Put the meal down, Esther,” he said; “it is over.”\n\nShe obeyed.\n“Here now.”\n\nShe resumed her place upon the arm of the chair close to him.\n\n“God is good to me, very good,” he said, fervently. “His habit is to move in mystery, yet sometimes he permits us to think we see and understand him. I am old, dear, and must go; but now, in this eleventh hour, when my hope was beginning to die, he sends me this one with a promise, and I am lifted up. I see the way to a great part in a circumstance itself so great that it shall be as a new birth to the whole world. And I see a reason for the gift of my great riches, and the end for which they were designed. Verily, my child, I take hold on life anew.”\n\nEsther nestled closer to him, as if to bring his thoughts from their far-flying.\n\n“The king has been born” he continued, imagining he was still speaking to her, “and he must be near the half of common life. Balthasar says he was a child on his mother’s lap when he saw him, and gave him presents and worship; and Ilderim holds it was twenty-seven years ago last December when Balthasar and his companions came to his tent asking a hiding-place from Herod. Wherefore the coming cannot now be long delayed. To-night—to-morrow it may be. Holy fathers of Israel, what happiness in the thought! I seem to hear the crash of the falling of old walls and the clamor of a universal change—ay, and for the uttermost joy of men, the earth opens to take Rome in, and they look up and laugh and sing that she is not, while we are;” then he laughed at himself. “Why, Esther, heard you ever the like? Surely, I have on me the passion of a singer, the heat of blood and the thrill of Miriam and David. In my thoughts, which should be those of a plain worker in figures and facts, there is a confusion of cymbals clashing and harp-strings loud beaten, and the voices of a multitude standing around a new-risen throne. I will put the thinking by for the present; only, dear, when the king comes he will need money and men, for as he was a child born of woman he will be but a man after all, bound to human ways as you and I are. And for the money he will have need of getters and keepers, and for the men leaders. There, there! See you not a broad road for my walking, and the running of the youth our master?—and at the end of it glory and revenge for us both?—and—and”—he paused, struck with the selfishness of a scheme in which she had no part or good result; then added, kissing her, “And happiness for thy mother’s child.”\n\nShe sat still, saying nothing. Then he remembered the difference in natures, and the law by which we are not permitted always to take delight in the same cause or be equally afraid of the same thing. He remembered she was but a girl.\n\n“Of what are you thinking, Esther?” he said, in his common home-like way. “If the thought have the form of a wish, give it me, little one, while the power remains mine. For power, you know, is a fretful thing, and hath its wings always spread for flight.”\n\nShe answered with a simplicity almost childish,\n\n“Send for him, father. Send for him to-night, and do not let him go into the Circus.”\n\n“Ah!” he said, prolonging the exclamation; and again his eyes fell upon the river, where the shadows were more shadowy than ever, since the moon had sunk far down behind Sulpius, leaving the city to the ineffectual stars. Shall we say it, reader? He was touched by a twinge of jealousy. If she should really love the young master! Oh no! That could not be; she was too young. But the idea had fast grip, and directly held him still and cold. She was sixteen. He knew it well. On the last natal day he had gone with her to the shipyard where there was a launch, and the yellow flag which the galley bore to its bridal with the waves had on it “Esther;” so they celebrated the day together. Yet the fact struck him now with the force of a surprise. There are realizations which come to us all painfully; mostly, however, such as pertain to ourselves; that we are growing old, for instance; and, more terrible, that we must die. Such a one crept into his heart, shadowy as the shadows, yet substantial enough to wring from him a sigh which was almost a groan. It was not sufficient that she should enter upon her young womanhood a servant, but she must carry to her master her affections, the truth and tenderness and delicacy of which he the father so well knew, because to this time they had all been his own undividedly. The fiend whose task it is to torture us with fears and bitter thoughts seldom does his work by halves. In the pang of the moment, the brave old man lost sight of his new scheme, and of the miraculous king its subject. By a mighty effort, however, he controlled himself, and asked, calmly, “Not go into the Circus, Esther? Why, child?”\n\n“It is not a place for a son of Israel, father.”\n\n“Rabbinical, rabbinical, Esther! Is that all?”\n\nThe tone of the inquiry was searching, and went to her heart, which began to beat loudly—so loudly she could not answer. A confusion new and strangely pleasant fell upon her.\n\n“The young man is to have the fortune,” he said, taking her hand, and speaking more tenderly; “he is to have the ships and the shekels—all, Esther, all. Yet I did not feel poor, for thou wert left me, and thy love so like the dead Rachel’s. Tell me, is he to have that too?”\n\nShe bent over him, and laid her cheek against his head.\n\n“Speak, Esther. I will be the stronger of the knowledge. In warning there is strength.”\n\nShe sat up then, and spoke as if she were Truth’s holy self.\n\n“Comfort thee, father. I will never leave thee; though he take my love, I will be thy handmaid ever as now.”\n\nAnd, stooping, she kissed him.\n\n“And more,” she said, continuing: “he is comely in my sight, and the pleading of his voice drew me to him, and I shudder to think of him in danger. Yes, father, I would be more than glad to see him again. Still, the love that is unrequited cannot be perfect love, wherefore I will wait a time, remembering I am thy daughter and my mother’s.”\n\n“A very blessing of the Lord art thou, Esther! A blessing to keep me rich, though all else be lost. And by his holy name and everlasting life, I swear thou shalt not suffer.”\n\nAt his request, a little later, the servant came and rolled the chair into the room, where he sat for a time thinking of the coming of the king, while she went off and slept the sleep of the innocent.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\nThe palace across the river nearly opposite Simonides’ place is said to have been completed by the famous Epiphanes, and was all such a habitation can be imagined; though he was a builder whose taste ran to the immense rather than the classical, now so called—an architectural imitator, in other words, of the Persians instead of the Greeks.\n\nThe wall enclosing the whole island to the waters edge, and built for the double purpose of bulwark against the river and defence against the mob, was said to have rendered the palace unfit for constant occupancy, insomuch that the legates abandoned it and moved to another residence erected for them on the western ridge of Mount Sulpius, under the Temple of Jupiter. Persons were not wanting, however, who flatly denied the bill against the ancient abode. They said, with shrewdness at least, that the real object of the removal of the legates was not a more healthful locality, but the assurance afforded them by the huge barracks, named, according to the prevalent style, citadel, situated just over the way on the eastern ridge of the mount. And the opinion had plausible showing. Among other pertinent things, it was remarked that the palace was kept in perpetual readiness for use; and when a consul, general of the army, king, or visiting potentate of any kind arrived at Antioch, quarters were at once assigned him on the island.\n\nAs we have to do with but one apartment in the old pile, the residue of it is left to the reader’s fancy; and as pleases him, he may go through its gardens, baths, halls, and labyrinth of rooms to the pavilions on the roof, all furnished as became a house of fame in a city which was more nearly Milton’s “gorgeous East” than any other in the world.\n\nAt this age the apartment alluded to would be termed a saloon. It was quite spacious, floored with polished marble slabs, and lighted in the day by skylights in which colored mica served as glass. The walls were broken by Atlantes, no two of which were alike, but all supporting a cornice wrought with arabesques exceedingly intricate in form, and more elegant on account of superadditions of color—blue, green, Tyrian purple, and gold. Around the room ran a continuous divan of Indian silks and wool of Cashmere. The furniture consisted of tables and stools of Egyptian patterns grotesquely carved. We have left Simonides in his chair perfecting his scheme in aid of the miraculous king, whose coming he has decided is so close at hand. Esther is asleep; and now, having crossed the river by the bridge, and made way through the lion-guarded gate and a number of Babylonian halls and courts, let us enter the gilded saloon.\n\nThere are five chandeliers hanging by sliding bronze chains from the ceiling—one in each corner, and in the centre one—enormous pyramids of lighted lamps, illuminating even the demoniac faces of the Atlantes and the complex tracery of the cornice. About the tables, seated or standing, or moving restlessly from one to another, there are probably a hundred persons, whom we must study at least for a moment.\n\nThey are all young, some of them little more than boys. That they are Italians and mostly Romans is past doubt. They all speak Latin in purity, while each one appears in the in-door dress of the great capital on the Tiber; that is, in tunics short of sleeve and skirt, a style of vesture well adapted to the climate of Antioch, and especially comfortable in the too close atmosphere of the saloon. On the divan here and there togas and lacernae lie where they have been carelessly tossed, some of them significantly bordered with purple. On the divan also lie sleepers stretched at ease; whether they were overcome by the heat and fatigue of the sultry day or by Bacchus we will not pause to inquire.\n\nThe hum of voices is loud and incessant. Sometimes there is an explosion of laughter, sometimes a burst of rage or exultation; but over all prevails a sharp, prolonged rattle, at first somewhat confusing to the non-familiar. If we approach the tables, however, the mystery solves itself. The company is at the favorite games, draughts and dice, singly or together, and the rattle is merely of the tesserae, or ivory cubes, loudly shaken, and the moving of the hostes on the checkered boards.\n\nWho are the company?\n\n“Good Flavius,” said a player, holding his piece in suspended movement, “thou seest yon lacerna; that one in front of us on the divan. It is fresh from the shop, and hath a shoulder-buckle of gold broad as a palm.”\n\n“Well,” said Flavius, intent upon his game, “I have seen such before; wherefore thine may not be old, yet, by the girdle of Venus, it is not new! What of it?”\n\n“Nothing. Only I would give it to find a man who knows everything.”\n\n“Ha, ha! For something cheaper, I will find thee here several with purple who will take thy offer. But play.”\n\n“There—check!”\n\n“So, by all the Jupiters! Now, what sayest thou? Again?”\n\n“Be it so.”\n\n“And the wager?”\n\n“A sestertium.”\n\nThen each drew his tablets and stilus and made a memorandum; and, while they were resetting the pieces, Flavius returned to his friend’s remark.\n\n“A man who knows everything! Hercle! the oracles would die. What wouldst thou with such a monster?”\n\n“Answer to one question, my Flavius; then, perpol! I would cut his throat.”\n\n“And the question?”\n\n“I would have him tell me the hour— Hour, said I?—nay, the minute—Maxentius will arrive to-morrow.”\n\n“Good play, good play! I have you! And why the minute?”\n\n“Hast thou ever stood uncovered in the Syrian sun on the quay at which he will land? The fires of the Vesta are not so hot; and, by the Stator of our father Romulus, I would die, if die I must, in Rome. Avernus is here; there, in the square before the Forum, I could stand, and, with my hand raised thus, touch the floor of the gods. Ha, by Venus, my Flavius, thou didst beguile me! I have lost. O Fortune!”\n\n“Again?”\n\n“I must have back my sestertium.”\n\n“Be it so.”\n\nAnd they played again and again; and when day, stealing through the skylights, began to dim the lamps, it found the two in the same places at the same table, still at the game. Like most of the company, they were military attaches of the consul, awaiting his arrival and amusing themselves meantime.\n\nDuring this conversation a party entered the room, and, unnoticed at first, proceeded to the central table. The signs were that they had come from a revel just dismissed. Some of them kept their feet with difficulty. Around the leader’s brow was a chaplet which marked him master of the feast, if not the giver. The wine had made no impression upon him unless to heighten his beauty, which was of the most manly Roman style; he carried his head high raised; the blood flushed his lips and cheeks brightly; his eyes glittered; though the manner in which, shrouded in a toga spotless white and of ample folds, he walked was too nearly imperial for one sober and not a Cæsar. In going to the table, he made room for himself and his followers with little ceremony and no apologies; and when at length he stopped, and looked over it and at the players, they all turned to him, with a shout like a cheer.\n\n“Messala! Messala!” they cried.\n\nThose in distant quarters, hearing the cry, re-echoed it where they were. Instantly there were dissolution of groups, and breaking-up of games, and a general rush towards the centre.\n\nMessala took the demonstration indifferently, and proceeded presently to show the ground of his popularity.\n\n“A health to thee, Drusus, my friend,” he said to the player next at his right; “a health—and thy tablets a moment.”\n\nHe raised the waxen boards, glanced at the memoranda of wagers, and tossed them down.\n\n“Denarii, only denarii—coin of cartmen and butchers!” he said, with a scornful laugh. “By the drunken Semele, to what is Rome coming, when a Cæsar sits o’ nights waiting a turn of fortune to bring him but a beggarly denarius!”\n\nThe scion of the Drusi reddened to his brows, but the bystanders broke in upon his reply by surging closer around the table, and shouting, “The Messala! the Messala!”\n\n“Men of the Tiber,” Messala continued, wresting a box with the dice in it from a hand near-by, “who is he most favored of the gods? A Roman. Who is he lawgiver of the nations? A Roman. Who is he, by sword right, the universal master?”\n\nThe company were of the easily inspired, and the thought was one to which they were born; in a twinkling they snatched the answer from him.\n\n“A Roman, a Roman!” they shouted.\n\n“Yet—yet”—he lingered to catch their ears—“yet there is a better than the best of Rome.”\n\nHe tossed his patrician head and paused, as if to sting them with his sneer.\n\n“Hear ye?” he asked. “There is a better than the best of Rome.”\n\n“Ay—Hercules!” cried one.\n\n“Bacchus!” yelled a satirist.\n\n“Jove—Jove!” thundered the crowd.\n\n“No,” Messala answered, “among men.”\n\n“Name him, name him!” they demanded.\n\n“I will,” he said, the next lull. “He who to the perfection of Rome hath added the perfection of the East; who to the arm of conquest, which is Western, hath also the art needful to the enjoyment of dominion, which is Eastern.”\n\n“Perpol! His best is a Roman, after all,” some one shouted; and there was a great laugh, and long clapping of hands—an admission that Messala had the advantage.\n\n“In the East” he continued, “we have no gods, only Wine, Women, and Fortune, and the greatest of them is Fortune; wherefore our motto, ‘Who dareth what I dare?’—fit for the senate, fit for battle, fittest for him who, seeking the best, challenges the worst.”\n\nHis voice dropped into an easy, familiar tone, but without relaxing the ascendancy he had gained.\n\n“In the great chest up in the citadel I have five talents coin current in the markets, and here are the receipts for them.”\n\nFrom his tunic he drew a roll of paper, and, flinging it on the table, continued, amidst breathless silence, every eye having him in view fixed on his, every ear listening:\n\n“The sum lies there the measure of what I dare. Who of you dares so much! You are silent. Is it too great? I will strike off one talent. What! still silent? Come, then, throw me once for these three talents—only three; for two; for one—one at least—one for the honor of the river by which you were born—Rome East against Rome West!—Orontes the barbarous against Tiber the sacred!”\n\nHe rattled the dice overhead while waiting.\n\n“The Orontes against the Tiber!” he repeated, with an increase of scornful emphasis.\n\nNot a man moved; then he flung the box upon the table and, laughing, took up the receipts.\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! By the Olympian Jove, I know now ye have fortunes to make or to mend; therefore are ye come to Antioch. Ho, Cecilius!”\n\n“Here, Messala!” cried a man behind him; “here am I, perishing in the mob, and begging a drachma to settle with the ragged ferryman. But, Pluto take me! these new ones have not so much as an obolus among them.”\n\nThe sally provoked a burst of laughter, under which the saloon rang and rang again. Messala alone kept his gravity.\n\n“Go, thou,” he said to Cecilius, “to the chamber whence we came, and bid the servants bring the amphorae here, and the cups and goblets. If these our countrymen, looking for fortune, have not purses, by the Syrian Bacchus, I will see if they are not better blessed with stomachs! Haste thee!”\n\nThen he turned to Drusus, with a laugh heard throughout the apartment.\n\n“Ha, ha, my friend! Be thou not offended because I levelled the Cæsar in thee down to the denarii. Thou seest I did but use the name to try these fine fledglings of our old Rome. Come, my Drusus, come!” He took up the box again and rattled the dice merrily. “Here, for what sum thou wilt, let us measure fortunes.”\n\nThe manner was frank, cordial, winsome. Drusus melted in a moment.\n\n“By the Nymphae, yes!” he said, laughing. “I will throw with thee, Messala—for a denarius.”\n\nA very boyish person was looking over the table watching the scene. Suddenly Messala turned to him.\n\n“Who art thou?” he asked.\n\nThe lad drew back.\n\n“Nay, by Castor! and his brother too! I meant not offence. It is a rule among men, in matters other than dice, to keep the record closest when the deal is least. I have need of a clerk. Wilt thou serve me?”\n\nThe young fellow drew his tablets ready to keep the score: the manner was irresistible.\n\n“Hold, Messala, hold!” cried Drusus. “I know not if it be ominous to stay the poised dice with a question; but one occurs to me, and I must ask it though Venus slap me with her girdle.”\n\n“Nay, my Drusus, Venus with her girdle off is Venus in love. To thy question—I will make the throw and hold it against mischance. Thus—”\n\nHe turned the box upon the table and held it firmly over the dice.\n\nAnd Drusus asked, “Did you ever see one Quintus Arrius?”\n\n“The duumvir?”\n“No—his son?”\n\n“I knew not he had a son.”\n\n“Well, it is nothing,” Drusus added, indifferently; “only, my Messala, Pollux was not more like Castor than Arrius is like thee.”\n\nThe remark had the effect of a signal: twenty voices took it up.\n\n“True, true! His eyes—his face,” they cried.\n\n“What!” answered one, disgusted. “Messala is a Roman; Arrius is a Jew.”\n\n“Thou sayest right,” a third exclaimed. “He is a Jew, or Momus lent his mother the wrong mask.”\n\nThere was promise of a dispute; seeing which, Messala interposed. “The wine is not come, my Drusus; and, as thou seest, I have the freckled Pythias as they were dogs in leash. As to Arrius, I will accept thy opinion of him, so thou tell me more about him.”\n\n“Well, be he Jew or Roman—and, by the great god Pan, I say it not in disrespect of thy feelings, my Messala!—this Arrius is handsome and brave and shrewd. The emperor offered him favor and patronage, which he refused. He came up through mystery, and keepeth distance as if he felt himself better or knew himself worse than the rest of us. In the palaestrae he was unmatched; he played with the blue-eyed giants from the Rhine and the hornless bulls of Sarmatia as they were willow wisps. The duumvir left him vastly rich. He has a passion for arms, and thinks of nothing but war. Maxentius admitted him into his family, and he was to have taken ship with us, but we lost him at Ravenna. Nevertheless he arrived safely. We heard of him this morning. Perpol! Instead of coming to the palace or going to the citadel, he dropped his baggage at the khan, and hath disappeared again.”\n\nAt the beginning of the speech Messala listened with polite indifference; as it proceeded, he became more attentive; at the conclusion, he took his hand from the dice-box, and called out, “Ho, my Caius! Dost thou hear?”\n\nA youth at his elbow—his Myrtilus, or comrade, in the day’s chariot practice—answered, much pleased with the attention, “Did I not, my Messala, I were not thy friend.”\n\n“Dost thou remember the man who gave thee the fall to-day?”\n\n“By the love-locks of Bacchus, have I not a bruised shoulder to help me keep it in mind?” and he seconded the words with a shrug that submerged his ears.\n\n“Well, be thou grateful to the Fates—I have found thy enemy. Listen.”\n\nThereupon Messala turned to Drusus.\n\n“Tell us more of him—perpol!—of him who is both Jew and Roman—by Phoebus, a combination to make a Centaur lovely! What garments doth he affect, my Drusus?”\n\n“Those of the Jews.”\n\n“Hearest thou, Caius?” said Messala. “The fellow is young—one; he hath the visage of a Roman—two; he loveth best the garb of a Jew—three; and in the palaestrae fame and fortune come of arms to throw a horse or tilt a chariot, as the necessity may order—four. And, Drusus, help thou my friend again. Doubtless this Arrius hath tricks of language; otherwise he could not so confound himself, to-day a Jew, to-morrow a Roman; but of the rich tongue of Athene—discourseth he in that as well?”\n\n“With such purity, Messala, he might have been a contestant in the Isthmia.”\n\n“Art thou listening, Caius?” said Messala. “The fellow is qualified to salute a woman—for that matter Aristomache herself—in the Greek; and as I keep the count, that is five. What sayest thou?”\n\n“Thou hast found him, my Messala,” Caius answered; “or I am not myself.”\n\n“Thy pardon, Drusus—and pardon of all—for speaking in riddles thus,” Messala said, in his winsome way. “By all the decent gods, I would not strain thy courtesy to the point of breaking, but now help thou me. See!”—he put his hand on the dice-box again, laughing—“See how close I hold the Pythias and their secret! Thou didst speak, I think, of mystery in connection with the coming of the son of Arrius. Tell me of that.”\n\n“’Tis nothing, Messala, nothing,” Drusus replied; “a child’s story. When Arrius, the father, sailed in pursuit of the pirates, he was without wife or family; he returned with a boy—him of whom we speak—and next day adopted him.”\n\n“Adopted him?” Messala repeated. “By the gods, Drusus, thou dost, indeed, interest me! Where did the duumvir find the boy? And who was he?”\n\n“Who shall answer thee that, Messala? who but the young Arrius himself? Perpol! in the fight the duumvir—then but a tribune—lost his galley. A returning vessel found him and one other—all of the crew who survived—afloat upon the same plank. I give you now the story of the rescuers, which hath this excellence at least—it hath never been contradicted. They say, the duumvir’s companion on the plank was a Jew—”\n\n“A Jew!” echoed Messala.\n\n“And a slave.”\n\n“How Drusus? A slave?”\n\n“When the two were lifted to the deck, the duumvir was in his tribune’s armor, and the other in the vesture of a rower.”\n\nMessala rose from leaning against the table.\n\n“A galley”—he checked the debasing word, and looked around, for once in his life at loss. Just then a procession of slaves filed into the room, some with great jars of wine, others with baskets of fruits and confections, others again with cups and flagons, mostly silver. There was inspiration in the sight. Instantly Messala climbed upon a stool.\n\n“Men of the Tiber,” he said, in a clear voice, “let us turn this waiting for our chief into a feast of Bacchus. Whom choose ye for master?”\n\nDrusus arose.\n\n“Who shall be master but the giver of the feast?” he said. “Answer, Romans.”\n\nThey gave their reply in a shout.\n\nMessala took the chaplet from his head, gave it to Drusus, who climbed upon the table, and, in the view of all, solemnly replaced it, making Messala master of the night.\n\n“There came with me into the room,” he said, “some friends just risen from table. That our feast may have the approval of sacred custom, bring hither that one of them most overcome by wine.”\n\nA din of voices answered, “Here he is, here he is!”\n\nAnd from the floor where he had fallen, a youth was brought forward, so effeminately beautiful he might have passed for the drinking-god himself—only the crown would have dropped from his head, and the thyrsus from his hand.\n\n“Lift him upon the table,” the master said.\n\nIt was found he could not sit.\n\n“Help him, Drusus, as the fair Nyone may yet help thee.”\n\nDrusus took the inebriate in his arms.\n\nThen addressing the limp figure, Messala said, amidst profound silence, “O Bacchus! greatest of the gods, be thou propitious to-night. And for myself, and these thy votaries, I vow this chaplet”—and from his head he raised it reverently—“I vow this chaplet to thy altar in the Grove of Daphne.”\n\nHe bowed, replaced the crown upon his locks, then stooped and uncovered the dice, saying, with a laugh, “See, my Drusus, by the ass of Silenus, the denarius is mine!”\n\nThere was a shout that set the floor to quaking, and the grim Atlantes to dancing, and the orgies began.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\nSheik Ilderim was a man of too much importance to go about with a small establishment. He had a reputation to keep with his tribe, such as became a prince and patriarch of the greatest following in all the Desert east of Syria; with the people of the cities he had another reputation, which was that of one of the richest personages not a king in all the East; and, being rich in fact—in money as well as in servants, camels, horses, and flocks of all kinds—he took pleasure in a certain state, which, besides magnifying his dignity with strangers, contributed to his personal pride and comfort. Wherefore the reader must not be misled by the frequent reference to his tent in the Orchard of Palms. He had there really a respectable dowar; that is to say, he had there three large tents—one for himself, one for visitors, one for his favorite wife and her women; and six or eight lesser ones, occupied by his servants and such tribal retainers as he had chosen to bring with him as a body-guard—strong men of approved courage, and skillful with bow, spear, and horses.\n\nTo be sure, his property of whatever kind was in no danger at the Orchard; yet as the habits of a man go with him to town not less than the country, and as it is never wise to slip the bands of discipline, the interior of the dowar was devoted to his cows, camels, goats, and such property in general as might tempt a lion or a thief.\n\nTo do him full justice, Ilderim kept well all the customs of his people, abating none, not even the smallest; in consequence his life at the Orchard was a continuation of his life in the Desert; nor that alone, it was a fair reproduction of the old patriarchal modes—the genuine pastoral life of primitive Israel.\n\nRecurring to the morning the caravan arrived at the Orchard—“Here, plant it here,” he said, stopping his horse, and thrusting a spear into the ground. “Door to the south; the lake before it thus; and these, the children of the Desert, to sit under at the going-down of the sun.”\n\nAt the last words he went to a group of three great palm-trees, and patted one of them as he would have patted his horse’s neck, or the cheek of the child of his love.\n\nWho but the sheik could of right say to the caravan, Halt! or of the tent, Here be it pitched? The spear was wrested from the ground, and over the wound it had riven in the sod the base of the first pillar of the tent was planted, marking the centre of the front door. Then eight others were planted—in all, three rows of pillars, three in a row. Then, at call, the women and children came, and unfolded the canvas from its packing on the camels. Who might do this but the women? Had they not sheared the hair from the brown goats of the flock? and twisted it into thread? and woven the thread into cloth? and stitched the cloth together, making the perfect roof, dark-brown in fact, though in the distance black as the tents of Kedar? And, finally, with what jests and laughter, and pulls altogether, the united following of the sheik stretched the canvas from pillar to pillar, driving the stakes and fastening the cords as they went! And when the walls of open reed matting were put in place—the finishing-touch to the building after the style of the Desert—with what hush of anxiety they waited the good man’s judgment! When he walked in and out, looking at the house in connection with the sun, the trees, and the lake, and said, rubbing his hands with might of heartiness, “Well done! Make the dowar now as ye well know, and to-night we will sweeten the bread with arrack, and the milk with honey, and at every fire there shall be a kid. God with ye! Want of sweet water there shall not be, for the lake is our well; neither shall the bearers of burden hunger, or the least of the flock, for here is green pasture also. God with you all, my children! Go.”\n\nAnd, shouting, the many happy went their ways then to pitch their own habitations. A few remained to arrange the interior for the sheik; and of these the men-servants hung a curtain to the central row of pillars, making two apartments; the one on the right sacred to Ilderim himself, the other sacred to his horses—his jewels of Solomon—which they led in, and with kisses and love-taps set at liberty. Against the middle pillar they then erected the arms-rack, and filled it with javelins and spears, and bows, arrows, and shields; outside of them hanging the master’s sword, modelled after the new moon; and the glitter of its blade rivalled the glitter of the jewels bedded in its grip. Upon one end of the rack they hung the housings of the horses, gay some of them as the livery of a king’s servant, while on the other end they displayed the great man’s wearing apparel—his robes woollen and robes linen, his tunics and trousers, and many colored kerchiefs for the head. Nor did they give over the work until he pronounced it well.\n\nMeantime the women drew out and set up the divan, more indispensable to him than the beard down-flowing over his breast, white as Aaron’s. They put a frame together in shape of three sides of a square, the opening to the door, and covered it with cushions and base curtains, and the cushions with a changeable spread striped brown and yellow; at the corners they placed pillows and bolsters sacked in cloth blue and crimson; then around the divan they laid a margin of carpet, and the inner space they carpeted as well; and when the carpet was carried from the opening of the divan to the door of the tent, their work was done; whereupon they again waited until the master said it was good. Nothing remained then but to bring and fill the jars with water, and hang the skin bottles of arrack ready for the hand—to-morrow the leben. Nor might an Arab see why Ilderim should not be both happy and generous—in his tent by the lake of sweet waters, under the palms of the Orchard of Palms.\n\nSuch was the tent at the door of which we left Ben-Hur.\n\nServants were already waiting the master’s direction. One of them took off his sandals; another unlatched Ben-Hur’s Roman shoes; then the two exchanged their dusty outer garments for fresh ones of white linen.\n\n“Enter—in God’s name, enter, and take thy rest,” said the host, heartily, in the dialect of the Market-place of Jerusalem; forthwith he led the way to the divan.\n\n“I will sit here,” he said next, pointing; “and there the stranger.”\n\nA woman—in the old time she would have been called a handmaid—answered, and dexterously piled the pillows and bolsters as rests for the back; after which they sat upon the side of the divan, while water was brought fresh from the lake, and their feet bathed and dried with napkins.\n\n“We have a saying in the Desert,” Ilderim began, gathering his beard, and combing it with his slender fingers, “that a good appetite is the promise of a long life. Hast thou such?”\n\n“By that rule, good sheik, I will live a hundred years. I am a hungry wolf at thy door,” Ben-Hur replied.\n\n“Well, thou shalt not be sent away like a wolf. I will give thee the best of the flocks.”\n\nIlderim clapped his hands.\n\n“Seek the stranger in the guest-tent, and say I, Ilderim, send him a prayer that his peace may be as incessant as the flowing of waters.”\n\nThe man in waiting bowed.\n\n“Say, also,” Ilderim continued, “that I have returned with another for breaking of bread; and, if Balthasar the wise careth to share the loaf, three may partake of it, and the portion of the birds be none the less.”\n\nThe second servant went away.\n\n“Let us take our rest now.”\n\nThereupon Ilderim settled himself upon the divan, as at this day merchants sit on their rugs in the bazaars of Damascus; and when fairly at rest, he stopped combing his beard, and said, gravely, “That thou art my guest, and hast drunk my leben, and art about to taste my salt, ought not to forbid a question: Who art thou?”\n\n“Sheik Ilderim,” said Ben-Hur, calmly enduring his gaze, “I pray thee not to think me trifling with thy just demand; but was there never a time in thy life when to answer such a question would have been a crime to thyself?”\n\n“By the splendor of Solomon, yes!” Ilderim answered. “Betrayal of self is at times as base as the betrayal of a tribe.”\n\n“Thanks, thanks, good sheik!” Ben-Hur exclaimed.\n\n“Never answer became thee better. Now I know thou dost but seek assurance to justify the trust I have come to ask, and that such assurance is of more interest to thee than the affairs of my poor life.”\n\nThe sheik in his turn bowed, and Ben-Hur hastened to pursue his advantage.\n\n“So it please thee then,” he said, “first, I am not a Roman, as the name given thee as mine implieth.”\n\nIlderim clasped the beard overflowing his breast, and gazed at the speaker with eyes faintly twinkling through the shade of the heavy close-drawn brows.\n\n“In the next place,” Ben-Hur continued, “I am an Israelite of the tribe of Judah.”\n\nThe sheik raised his brows a little.\n\n“Nor that merely. Sheik, I am a Jew with a grievance against Rome compared with which thine is not more than a child’s trouble.”\n\nThe old man combed his beard with nervous haste, and let fall his brows until even the twinkle of the eyes went out.\n\n“Still further: I swear to thee, Sheik Ilderim—I swear by the covenant the Lord made with my fathers—so thou but give me the revenge I seek, the money and the glory of the race shall be thine.”\n\nIlderim’s brows relaxed; his head arose; his face began to beam; and it was almost possible to see the satisfaction taking possession of him.\n\n“Enough!” he said. “If at the roots of thy tongue there is a lie in coil, Solomon himself had not been safe against thee. That thou art not a Roman—that as a Jew thou hast a grievance against Rome, and revenge to compass, I believe; and on that score enough. But as to thy skill. What experience hast thou in racing with chariots? And the horses—canst thou make them creatures of thy will?—to know thee? to come at call? to go, if thou sayest it, to the last extreme of breath and strength? and then, in the perishing moment, out of the depths of thy life thrill them to one exertion the mightiest of all? The gift, my son, is not to every one. Ah, by the splendor of God! I knew a king who governed millions of men, their perfect master, but could not win the respect of a horse. Mark! I speak not of the dull brutes whose round it is to slave for slaves—the debased in blood and image—the dead in spirit; but of such as mine here—the kings of their kind; of a lineage reaching back to the broods of the first Pharaoh; my comrades and friends, dwellers in tents, whom long association with me has brought up to my plane; who to their instincts have added our wits and to their senses joined our souls, until they feel all we know of ambition, love, hate, and contempt; in war, heroes; in trust, faithful as women. Ho, there!”\n\nA servant came forward.\n\n“Let my Arabs come!”\n\nThe man drew aside part of the division curtain of the tent, exposing to view a group of horses, who lingered a moment where they were as if to make certain of the invitation.\n\n“Come!” Ilderim said to them. “Why stand ye there? What have I that is not yours? Come, I say!”\nThey stalked slowly in.\n\n“Son of Israel,” the master said, “thy Moses was a mighty man, but—ha, ha ha!—I must laugh when I think of his allowing thy fathers the plodding ox and the dull, slow-natured ass, and forbidding them property in horses. Ha, ha, ha! Thinkest thou he would have done so had he seen that one—and that—and this?” At the word he laid his hand upon the face of the first to reach him, and patted it with infinite pride and tenderness.\n\n“It is a misjudgment, sheik, a misjudgment,” Ben-Hur said, warmly. “Moses was a warrior as well as a lawgiver beloved by God; and to follow war—ah, what is it but to love all its creatures—these among the rest?”\n\nA head of exquisite turn—with large eyes, soft as a deer’s, and half hidden by the dense forelock, and small ears, sharp-pointed and sloped well forward—approached then quite to his breast, the nostrils open, and the upper lip in motion. “Who are you?” it asked, plainly as ever man spoke. Ben-Hur recognized one of the four racers he had seen on the course, and gave his open hand to the beautiful brute.\n\n“They will tell you, the blasphemers!—may their days shorten as they grow fewer!”—the sheik spoke with the feeling of a man repelling a personal defamation—“they will tell you, I say, that our horses of the best blood are derived from the Nesaean pastures of Persia. God gave the first Arab a measureless waste of sand, with some treeless mountains, and here and there a well of bitter waters; and said to him, ‘Behold thy country!’ And when the poor man complained, the Mighty One pitied him, and said again, ‘Be of cheer! for I will twice bless thee above other men.’ The Arab heard, and gave thanks, and with faith set out to find the blessings. He travelled all the boundaries first, and failed; then he made a path into the desert, and went on and on—and in the heart of the waste there was an island of green very beautiful to see; and in the heart of the island, lo! a herd of camels, and another of horses! He took them joyfully and kept them with care for what they were—best gifts of God. And from that green isle went forth all the horses of the earth; even to the pastures of Nesaea they went; and northward to the dreadful vales perpetually threshed by blasts from the Sea of Chill Winds. Doubt not the story; or if thou dost, may never amulet have charm for an Arab again. Nay, I will give thee proof.”\n\nHe clapped his hands.\n\n“Bring me the records of the tribe,” he said to the servant who responded.\n\nWhile waiting, the sheik played with the horses, patting their cheeks, combing their forelocks with his fingers, giving each one a token of remembrance. Presently six men appeared with chests of cedar reinforced by bands of brass, and hinged and bolted with brass.\n\n“Nay,” said Ilderim, when they were all set down by the divan, “I meant not all of them; only the records of the horses—that one. Open it and take back the others.”\n\nThe chest was opened, disclosing a mass of ivory tablets strung on rings of silver wire; and as the tablets were scarcely thicker than wafers, each ring held several hundreds of them.\n\n“I know,” said Ilderim, taking some of the rings in his hand—“I know with what care and zeal, my son, the scribes of the Temple in the Holy City keep the names of the newly born, that every son of Israel may trace his line of ancestry to its beginning, though it antedate the patriarchs. My fathers—may the recollection of them be green forever!—did not think it sinful to borrow the idea, and apply it to their dumb servants. See these tablets!”\n\nBen-Hur took the rings, and separating the tablets saw they bore rude hieroglyphs in Arabic, burned on the smooth surface by a sharp point of heated metal.\n\n“Canst thou read them, O son of Israel?”\n\n“No. Thou must tell me their meaning.”\n\n“Know thou, then, each tablet records the name of a foal of the pure blood born to my fathers through the hundreds of years passed; and also the names of sire and dam. Take them, and note their age, that thou mayst the more readily believe.”\n\nSome of the tablets were nearly worn away. All were yellow with age.\n\n“In the chest there, I can tell thee now, I have the perfect history; perfect because certified as history seldom is—showing of what stock all these are sprung—this one, and that now supplicating thy notice and caress; and as they come to us here, their sires, even the furthest removed in time, came to my sires, under a tent-roof like this of mine, to eat their measure of barley from the open hand, and be talked to as children; and as children kiss the thanks they have not speech to express. And now, O son of Israel, thou mayst believe my declaration—if I am a lord of the Desert, behold my ministers! Take them from me, and I become as a sick man left by the caravan to die. Thanks to them, age hath not diminished the terror of me on the highways between cities; and it will not while I have strength to go with them. Ha, ha, ha! I could tell thee marvels done by their ancestors. In a favoring time I may do so; for the present, enough that they were never overtaken in retreat; nor, by the sword of Solomon, did they ever fail in pursuit! That, mark you, on the sands and under saddle; but now—I do not know—I am afraid, for they are under yoke the first time, and the conditions of success are so many. They have the pride and the speed and the endurance. If I find them a master, they will win. Son of Israel! so thou art the man, I swear it shall be a happy day that brought thee thither. Of thyself now speak.”\n\n“I know now,” said Ben-Hur, “why it is that in the love of an Arab his horse is next to his children; and I know, also, why the Arab horses are the best in the world; but, good sheik, I would not have you judge me by words alone; for, as you know, all promises of men sometimes fail. Give me the trial first on some plain hereabout, and put the four in my hand to-morrow.”\n\nIlderim’s face beamed again, and he would have spoken.\n\n“A moment, good sheik, a moment!” said Ben-Hur. “Let me say further. From the masters in Rome I learned many lessons, little thinking they would serve me in a time like this. I tell thee these thy sons of the Desert, though they have separately the speed of eagles and the endurance of lions, will fail if they are not trained to run together under the yoke. For bethink thee, sheik, in every four there is one the slowest and one the swiftest; and while the race is always to the slowest, the trouble is always with the swiftest. It was so to-day; the driver could not reduce the best to harmonious action with the poorest. My trial may have no better result; but if so, I will tell thee of it: that I swear. Wherefore, in the same spirit I say, can I get them to run together, moved by my will, the four as one, thou shalt have the sestertii and the crown, and I my revenge. What sayest thou?”\n\nIlderim listened, combing his beard the while. At the end he said, with a laugh, “I think better of thee, son of Israel. We have a saying in the Desert, ‘If you will cook the meal with words, I will promise an ocean of butter.’ Thou shalt have the horses in the morning.”\n\nAt that moment there was a stir at the rear entrance to the tent.\n\n“The supper—it is here! and yonder my friend Balthasar, whom thou shalt know. He hath a story to tell which an Israelite should never tire of hearing.”\n\nAnd to the servants he added,\n\n“Take the records away, and return my jewels to their apartment.”\n\nAnd they did as he ordered.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\nIf the reader will return now to the repast of the wise men at their meeting in the desert, he will understand the preparations for the supper in Ilderim’s tent. The differences were chiefly such as were incident to ampler means and better service.\n\nThree rugs were spread on the carpet within the space so nearly enclosed by the divan; a table not more than a foot in height was brought and set within the same place, and covered with a cloth. Off to one side a portable earthenware oven was established under the presidency of a woman whose duty it was to keep the company in bread, or, more precisely, in hot cakes of flour from the handmills grinding with constant sound in a neighboring tent.\n\nMeanwhile Balthasar was conducted to the divan, where Ilderim and Ben-Hur received him standing. A loose black gown covered his person; his step was feeble, and his whole movement slow and cautious, apparently dependent upon a long staff and the arm of a servant.\n\n“Peace to you, my friend,” said Ilderim, respectfully. “Peace and welcome.”\n\nThe Egyptian raised his head and replied, “And to thee, good sheik—to thee and thine, peace and the blessing of the One God—God the true and loving.”\n\nThe manner was gentle and devout, and impressed Ben-Hur with a feeling of awe; besides which the blessing included in the answering salutation had been partly addressed to him, and while that part was being spoken, the eyes of the aged guest, hollow yet luminous, rested upon his face long enough to stir an emotion new and mysterious, and so strong that he again and again during the repast scanned the much wrinkled and bloodless face for its meaning; but always there was the expression bland, placid, and trustful as a child’s. A little later he found that expression habitual.\n\n“This is he, O Balthasar,” said the sheik, laying his hand on Ben-Hur’s arm, “who will break bread with us this evening.”\n\nThe Egyptian glanced at the young man, and looked again surprised and doubting; seeing which the sheik continued, “I have promised him my horses for trial to-morrow; and if all goes well, he will drive them in the Circus.”\n\nBalthasar continued his gaze.\n\n“He came well recommended,” Ilderim pursued, much puzzled. “You may know him as the son of Arrius, who was a noble Roman sailor, though”—the sheik hesitated, then resumed, with a laugh—“though he declares himself an Israelite of the tribe of Judah; and, by the splendor of God, I believe what he tells me!”\n\nBalthasar could no longer withhold explanation.\n\n“To-day, O most generous sheik, my life was in peril, and would have been lost had not a youth, the counterpart of this one—if, indeed, he be not the very same—intervened when all others fled, and saved me.” Then he addressed Ben-Hur directly, “Art thou not he?”\n\n“I cannot answer so far,” Ben-Hur replied, with modest deference. “I am he who stopped the horses of the insolent Roman when they were rushing upon thy camel at the Fountain of Castalia. Thy daughter left a cup with me.”\n\nFrom the bosom of his tunic he produced the cup, and gave it to Balthasar.\n\nA glow lighted the faded countenance of the Egyptian.\n\n“The Lord sent thee to me at the Fountain to-day,” he said, in a tremulous voice, stretching his hand towards Ben-Hur; “and he sends thee to me now. I give him thanks; and praise him thou, for of his favor I have wherewith to give thee great reward, and I will. The cup is thine; keep it.”\n\nBen-Hur took back the gift, and Balthasar, seeing the inquiry upon Ilderim’s face, related the occurrence at the Fountain.\n\n“What!” said the sheik to Ben-Hur. “Thou saidst nothing of this to me, when better recommendation thou couldst not have brought. Am I not an Arab, and sheik of my tribe of tens of thousands? And is not he my guest? And is it not in my guest-bond that the good or evil thou dost him is good or evil done to me? Whither shouldst thou go for reward but here? And whose the hand to give it but mine?”\n\nHis voice at the end of the speech rose to cutting shrillness.\n\n“Good sheik, spare me, I pray. I came not for reward, great or small; and that I may be acquitted of the thought, I say the help I gave this excellent man would have been given as well to thy humblest servant.”\n\n“But he is my friend, my guest—not my servant; and seest thou not in the difference the favor of Fortune?” Then to Balthasar the sheik subjoined, “Ah, by the splendor of God! I tell thee again he is not a Roman.”\n\nWith that he turned away, and gave attention to the servants, whose preparations for the supper were about complete.\n\nThe reader who recollects the history of Balthasar as given by himself at the meeting in the desert will understand the effect of Ben-Hur’s assertion of disinterestedness upon that worthy. In his devotion to men there had been, it will be remembered, no distinctions; while the redemption which had been promised him in the way of reward—the redemption for which he was waiting—was universal. To him, therefore, the assertion sounded somewhat like an echo of himself. He took a step nearer Ben-Hur, and spoke to him in the childlike way.\n\n“How did the sheik say I should call you? It was a Roman name, I think.”\n\n“Arrius, the son of Arrius.”\n\n“Yet thou art not a Roman?”\n\n“All my people were Jews.”\n\n“Were, saidst thou? Are they not living?”\n\nThe question was subtle as well as simple; but Ilderim saved Ben-Hur from reply.\n\n“Come,” he said to them, “the meal is ready.”\n\nBen-Hur gave his arm to Balthasar, and conducted him to the table, where shortly they were all seated on their rugs Eastern fashion. The lavers were brought them, and they washed and dried their hands; then the sheik made a sign, the servants stopped, and the voice of the Egyptian arose tremulous with holy feeling.\n\n“Father of All—God! What we have is of thee; take our thanks, and bless us, that we may continue to do thy will.”\n\nIt was the grace the good man had said simultaneously with his brethren Gaspar the Greek and Melchior the Hindoo, the utterance in diverse tongues out of which had come the miracle attesting the Divine Presence at the meal in the desert years before.\n\nThe table to which they immediately addressed themselves was, as may be thought, rich in the substantials and delicacies favorite in the East—in cakes hot from the oven, vegetables from the gardens, meats singly, compounds of meats and vegetables, milk of kine, and honey and butter—all eaten or drunk, it should be remarked, without any of the modern accessories—knives, forks, spoons, cups, or plates; and in this part of the repast but little was said, for they were hungry. But when the dessert was in course it was otherwise. They laved their hands again, had the lap-cloths shaken out, and with a renewed table and the sharp edge of their appetites gone they were disposed to talk and listen.\n\nWith such a company—an Arab, a Jew, and an Egyptian, all believers alike in one God—there could be at that age but one subject of conversation; and of the three, which should be speaker but he to whom the Deity had been so nearly a personal appearance, who had seen him in a star, had heard his voice in direction, had been led so far and so miraculously by his Spirit? And of what should he talk but that of which he had been called to testify?\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\nThe shadows cast over the Orchard of Palms by the mountains at set of sun left no sweet margin time of violet sky and drowsing earth between the day and night. The latter came early and swift; and against its glooming in the tent this evening the servants brought four candlesticks of brass, and set them by the corners of the table. To each candlestick there were four branches, and on each branch a lighted silver lamp and a supply cup of olive-oil. In light ample, even brilliant, the group at dessert continued their conversation, speaking in the Syriac dialect, familiar to all peoples in that part of the world.\n\nThe Egyptian told his story of the meeting of the three in the desert, and agreed with the sheik that it was in December, twenty-seven years before, when he and his companions fleeing from Herod arrived at the tent praying shelter. The narrative was heard with intense interest; even the servants lingering when they could to catch its details. Ben-Hur received it as became a man listening to a revelation of deep concern to all humanity, and to none of more concern than the people of Israel. In his mind, as we shall presently see, there was crystallizing an idea which was to change his course of life, if not absorb it absolutely.\n\nAs the recital proceeded, the impression made by Balthasar upon the young Jew increased; at its conclusion, his feeling was too profound to permit a doubt of its truth; indeed, there was nothing left him desirable in the connection but assurances, if such were to be had, pertaining exclusively to the consequences of the amazing event.\n\nAnd now there is wanting an explanation which the very discerning may have heretofore demanded; certainly it can be no longer delayed. Our tale begins, in point of date not less than fact, to trench close upon the opening of the ministry of the Son of Mary, whom we have seen but once since this same Balthasar left him worshipfully in his mother’s lap in the cave by Bethlehem. Henceforth to the end the mysterious Child will be a subject of continual reference; and slowly though surely the current of events with which we are dealing will bring us nearer and nearer to him, until finally we see him a man—we would like, if armed contrariety of opinion would permit it, to add—A MAN WHOM THE WORLD COULD NOT DO WITHOUT. Of this declaration, apparently so simple, a shrewd mind inspired by faith will make much—and in welcome. Before his time, and since, there have been men indispensable to particular people and periods; but his indispensability was to the whole race, and for all time—a respect in which it is unique, solitary, divine.\n\nTo Sheik Ilderim the story was not new. He had heard it from the three wise men together under circumstances which left no room for doubt; he had acted upon it seriously, for the helping a fugitive escape from the anger of the first Herod was dangerous. Now one of the three sat at his table again, a welcome guest and revered friend. Sheik Ilderim certainly believed the story; yet, in the nature of things, its mighty central fact could not come home to him with the force and absorbing effect it came to Ben-Hur. He was an Arab, whose interest in the consequences was but general; on the other hand, Ben-Hur was an Israelite and a Jew, with more than a special interest in—if the solecism can be pardoned—the truth of the fact. He laid hold of the circumstance with a purely Jewish mind.\n\nFrom his cradle, let it be remembered, he had heard of the Messiah; at the colleges he had been made familiar with all that was known of that Being at once the hope, the fear, and the peculiar glory of the chosen people; the prophets from the first to the last of the heroic line foretold him; and the coming had been, and yet was, the theme of endless exposition with the rabbis—in the synagogues, in the schools, in the Temple, of fast-days and feast-days, in public and in private, the national teachers expounded and kept expounding until all the children of Abraham, wherever their lots were cast, bore the Messiah in expectation, and by it literally, and with iron severity, ruled and moulded their lives.\n\nDoubtless, it will be understood from this that there was much argument among the Jews themselves about the Messiah, and so there was; but the disputation was all limited to one point, and one only—when would he come?\n\nDisquisition is for the preacher; whereas the writer is but telling a tale, and that he may not lose his character, the explanation he is making requires notice merely of a point connected with the Messiah about which the unanimity among the chosen people was matter of marvellous astonishment: he was to be, when come, the KING OF THE JEWS—their political King, their Cæsar. By their instrumentality he was to make armed conquest of the earth, and then, for their profit and in the name of God, hold it down forever. On this faith, dear reader, the Pharisees or Separatists—the latter being rather a political term—in the cloisters and around the altars of the Temple, built an edifice of hope far overtopping the dream of the Macedonian. His but covered the earth; theirs covered the earth and filled the skies; that is to say, in their bold, boundless fantasy of blasphemous egotism, God the Almighty was in effect to suffer them for their uses to nail him by the ear to a door in sign of eternal servitude.\n\nReturning directly to Ben-Hur, it is to be observed now that there were two circumstances in his life the result of which had been to keep him in a state comparatively free from the influence and hard effects of the audacious faith of his Separatist countrymen.\n\nIn the first place, his father followed the faith of the Sadducees, who may, in a general way, be termed the Liberals of their time. They had some loose opinions in denial of the soul. They were strict constructionists and rigorous observers of the Law as found in the books of Moses; but they held the vast mass of Rabbinical addenda to those books in derisive contempt. They were unquestionably a sect, yet their religion was more a philosophy than a creed; they did not deny themselves the enjoyments of life, and saw many admirable methods and productions among the Gentile divisions of the race. In politics they were the active opposition of the Separatists. In the natural order of things, these circumstances and conditions, opinions and peculiarities, would have descended to the son as certainly and really as any portion of his father’s estate; and, as we have seen, he was actually in course of acquiring them, when the second saving event overtook him.\n\nUpon a youth of Ben-Hur’s mind and temperament the influence of five years of affluent life in Rome can be appreciated best by recalling that the great city was then, in fact, the meeting-place of the nations—their meeting-place politically and commercially, as well as for the indulgence of pleasure without restraint. Round and round the golden mile-stone in front of the Forum—now in gloom of eclipse, now in unapproachable splendor—flowed all the active currents of humanity. If excellences of manner, refinements of society, attainments of intellect, and glory of achievement made no impression upon him, how could he, as the son of Arrius, pass day after day, through a period so long, from the beautiful villa near Misenum into the receptions of Cæsar, and be wholly uninfluenced by what he saw there of kings, princes, ambassadors, hostages, and delegates, suitors all of them from every known land, waiting humbly the yes or no which was to make or unmake them? As mere assemblages, to be sure, there was nothing to compare with the gatherings at Jerusalem in celebration of the Passover; yet when he sat under the purple velaria of the Circus Maximus one of three hundred and fifty thousand spectators, he must have been visited by the thought that possibly there might be some branches of the family of man worthy divine consideration, if not mercy, though they were of the uncircumcised—some, by their sorrows, and, yet worse, by their hopelessness in the midst of sorrows, fitted for brotherhood in the promises to his countrymen.\n\nThat he should have had such a thought under such circumstances was but natural; we think so much, at least, will be admitted: but when the reflection came to him, and he gave himself up to it, he could not have been blind to a certain distinction. The wretchedness of the masses, and their hopeless condition, had no relation whatever to religion; their murmurs and groans were not against their gods or for want of gods. In the oak-woods of Britain the Druids held their followers; Odin and Freya maintained their godships in Gaul and Germany and among the Hyperboreans; Egypt was satisfied with her crocodiles and Anubis; the Persians were yet devoted to Ormuzd and Ahriman, holding them in equal honor; in hope of the Nirvana, the Hindoos moved on patient as ever in the rayless paths of Brahm; the beautiful Greek mind, in pauses of philosophy, still sang the heroic gods of Homer; while in Rome nothing was so common and cheap as gods. According to whim, the masters of the world, because they were masters, carried their worship and offerings indifferently from altar to altar, delighted in the pandemonium they had erected. Their discontent, if they were discontented, was with the number of gods; for, after borrowing all the divinities of the earth they proceeded to deify their Cæsars, and vote them altars and holy service. No, the unhappy condition was not from religion, but misgovernment and usurpations and countless tyrannies. The Avernus men had been tumbled into, and were praying to be relieved from, was terribly but essentially political. The supplication—everywhere alike, in Lodinum, Alexandria, Athens, Jerusalem—was for a king to conquer with, not a god to worship.\n\nStudying the situation after two thousand years, we can see and say that religiously there was no relief from the universal confusion except some God could prove himself a true God, and a masterful one, and come to the rescue; but the people of the time, even the discerning and philosophical, discovered no hope except in crushing Rome; that done, the relief would follow in restorations and reorganizations; therefore they prayed, conspired, rebelled, fought, and died, drenching the soil to-day with blood, to-morrow with tears—and always with the same result.\n\nIt remains to be said now that Ben-Hur was in agreement with the mass of men of his time not Romans. The five years’ residence in the capital served him with opportunity to see and study the miseries of the subjugated world; and in full belief that the evils which afflicted it were political, and to be cured only by the sword, he was going forth to fit himself for a part in the day of resort to the heroic remedy. By practice of arms he was a perfect soldier; but war has its higher fields, and he who would move successfully in them must know more than to defend with shield and thrust with spear. In those fields the general finds his tasks, the greatest of which is the reduction of the many into one, and that one himself; the consummate captain is a fighting-man armed with an army. This conception entered into the scheme of life to which he was further swayed by the reflection that the vengeance he dreamed of, in connection with his individual wrongs, would be more surely found in some of the ways of war than in any pursuit of peace.\n\nThe feelings with which he listened to Balthasar can be now understood. The story touched two of the most sensitive points of his being so they rang within him. His heart beat fast—and faster still when, searching himself, he found not a doubt either that the recital was true in every particular, or that the Child so miraculously found was the Messiah. Marvelling much that Israel rested so dead to the revelation, and that he had never heard of it before that day, two questions presented themselves to him as centring all it was at that moment further desirable to know:\n\nWhere was the Child then?\n\nAnd what was his mission?\n\nWith apologies for the interruptions, he proceeded to draw out the opinions of Balthasar, who was in nowise loath to speak.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\n“If I could answer you,” Balthasar said, in his simple, earnest, devout way—“oh, if I knew where he is, how quickly I would go to him! The seas should not stay me, nor the mountains.”\n\n“You have tried to find him, then?” asked Ben-Hur.\n\nA smile flitted across the face of the Egyptian.\n\n“The first task I charged myself with after leaving the shelter given me in the desert”—Balthasar cast a grateful look at Ilderim—“was to learn what became of the Child. But a year had passed, and I dared not go up to Judea in person, for Herod still held the throne bloody-minded as ever. In Egypt, upon my return, there were a few friends to believe the wonderful things I told them of what I had seen and heard—a few who rejoiced with me that a Redeemer was born—a few who never tired of the story. Some of them came up for me looking after the Child. They went first to Bethlehem, and found there the khan and the cave; but the steward—he who sat at the gate the night of the birth, and the night we came following the star—was gone. The king had taken him away, and he was no more seen.”\n\n“But they found some proofs, surely,” said Ben-Hur, eagerly.\n\n“Yes, proofs written in blood—a village in mourning; mothers yet crying for their little ones. You must know, when Herod heard of our flight, he sent down and slew the youngest-born of the children of Bethlehem. Not one escaped. The faith of my messengers was confirmed; but they came to me saying the Child was dead, slain with the other innocents.”\n\n“Dead!” exclaimed Ben-Hur, aghast. “Dead, sayest thou?”\n\n“Nay, my son, I did not say so. I said they, my messengers, told me the Child was dead. I did not believe the report then; I do not believe it now.”\n\n“I see—thou hast some special knowledge.”\n\n“Not so, not so,” said Balthasar, dropping his gaze. “The Spirit was to go with us no farther than to the Child. When we came out of the cave, after our presents were given and we had seen the babe, we looked first thing for the star; but it was gone, and we knew we were left to ourselves. The last inspiration of the Holy One—the last I can recall—was that which sent us to Ilderim for safety.”\n\n“Yes,” said the sheik, fingering his beard nervously. “You told me you were sent to me by a Spirit—I remember it.”\n\n“I have no special knowledge,” Balthasar continued, observing the dejection which had fallen upon Ben-Hur; “but, my son, I have given the matter much thought—thought continuing through years, inspired by faith, which, I assure you, calling God for witness, is as strong in me now as in the hour I heard the voice of the Spirit calling me by the shore of the lake. If you will listen, I will tell you why I believe the Child is living.”\n\nBoth Ilderim and Ben-Hur looked assent, and appeared to summon their faculties that they might understand as well as hear. The interest reached the servants, who drew near to the divan, and stood listening. Throughout the tent there was the profoundest silence.\n\n“We three believe in God.”\n\nBalthasar bowed his head as he spoke.\n\n“And he is the Truth,” he resumed. “His word is God. The hills may turn to dust, and the seas be drunk dry by south winds; but his word shall stand, because it is the Truth.”\n\nThe utterance was in a manner inexpressibly solemn.\n\n“The voice, which was his, speaking to me by the lake, said, ‘Blessed art thou, O son of Mizraim! The Redemption cometh. With two others from the remotenesses of the earth, thou shalt see the Savior.’ I have seen the Savior—blessed be his name!—but the Redemption, which was the second part of the promise, is yet to come. Seest thou now? If the Child be dead, there is no agent to bring the Redemption about, and the word is naught, and God—nay, I dare not say it!”\n\nHe threw up both hands in horror.\n\n“The Redemption was the work for which the Child was born; and so long as the promise abides, not even death can separate him from his work until it is fulfilled, or at least in the way of fulfilment. Take you that now as one reason for my belief; then give me further attention.”\n\nThe good man paused.\n\n“Wilt thou not taste the wine? It is at thy hand—see,” said Ilderim, respectfully.\n\nBalthasar drank, and, seeming refreshed, continued:\n\n“The Savior I saw was born of woman, in nature like us, and subject to all our ills—even death. Let that stand as the first proposition. Consider next the work set apart to him. Was it not a performance for which only a man is fitted?—a man wise, firm, discreet—a man, not a child? To become such he had to grow as we grow. Bethink you now of the dangers his life was subject to in the interval—the long interval between childhood and maturity. The existing powers were his enemies; Herod was his enemy; and what would Rome have been? And as for Israel—that he should not be accepted by Israel was the motive for cutting him off. See you now. What better way was there to take care of his life in the helpless growing time than by passing him into obscurity? Wherefore I say to myself, and to my listening faith, which is never moved except by yearning of love—I say he is not dead, but lost; and, his work remaining undone, he will come again. There you have the reasons for my belief. Are they not good?”\n\nIlderim’s small Arab eyes were bright with understanding, and Ben-Hur, lifted from his dejection, said heartily, “I, at least, may not gainsay them. What further, pray?”\n\n“Hast thou not enough, my son? Well,” he began, in calmer tone, “seeing that the reasons were good—more plainly, seeing it was God’s will that the Child should not be found—I settled my faith into the keeping of patience, and took to waiting.” He raised his eyes, full of holy trust, and broke off abstractedly—“I am waiting now. He lives, keeping well his mighty secret. What though I cannot go to him, or name the hill or the vale of his abiding-place? He lives—it may be as the fruit in blossom, it may be as the fruit just ripening; but by the certainty there is in the promise and reason of God, I know he lives.”\n\nA thrill of awe struck Ben-Hur—a thrill which was but the dying of his half-formed doubt.\n\n“Where thinkest thou he is?” he asked, in a low voice, and hesitating, like one who feels upon his lips the pressure of a sacred silence.\n\nBalthasar looked at him kindly, and replied, his mind not entirely freed from its abstraction,\n\n“In my house on the Nile, so close to the river that the passers-by in boats see it and its reflection in the water at the same time—in my house, a few weeks ago, I sat thinking. A man thirty years old, I said to myself, should have his fields of life all ploughed, and his planting well done; for after that it is summer-time, with space scarce enough to ripen his sowing. The Child, I said further, is now twenty-seven—his time to plant must be at hand. I asked myself, as you here asked me, my son, and answered by coming hither, as to a good resting-place close by the land thy fathers had from God. Where else should he appear, if not in Judea? In what city should he begin his work, if not in Jerusalem? Who should be first to receive the blessings he is to bring, if not the children of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; in love, at least, the children of the Lord? If I were bidden go seek him, I would search well the hamlets and villages on the slopes of the mountains of Judea and Galilee falling eastwardly into the valley of the Jordan. He is there now. Standing in a door or on a hill-top, only this evening he saw the sun set one day nearer the time when he himself shall become the light of the world.”\n\nBalthasar ceased, with his hand raised and finger pointing as if at Judea. All the listeners, even the dull servants outside the divan, affected by his fervor, were startled as if by a majestic presence suddenly apparent within the tent. Nor did the sensation die away at once: of those at the table, each sat awhile thinking. The spell was finally broken by Ben-Hur.\n\n“I see, good Balthasar,” he said, “that thou hast been much and strangely favored. I see, also, that thou art a wise man indeed. It is not in my power to tell how grateful I am for the things thou hast told me. I am warned of the coming of great events, and borrow somewhat from thy faith. Complete the obligation, I pray thee, by telling further of the mission of him for whom thou art waiting, and for whom from this night I too shall wait as becomes a believing son of Judah. He is to be a Savior, thou saidst; is he not to be King of the Jews also?”\n\n“My son,” said Balthasar, in his benignant way, “the mission is yet a purpose in the bosom of God. All I think about it is wrung from the words of the Voice in connection with the prayer to which they were in answer. Shall we refer to them again?”\n\n“Thou art the teacher.”\n\n“The cause of my disquiet,” Balthasar began, calmly—“that which made me a preacher in Alexandria and in the villages of the Nile; that which drove me at last into the solitude where the Spirit found me—was the fallen condition of men, occasioned, as I believed, by loss of the knowledge of God. I sorrowed for the sorrows of my kind—not of one class, but all of them. So utterly were they fallen it seemed to me there could be no Redemption unless God himself would make it his work; and I prayed him to come, and that I might see him. ‘Thy good works have conquered. The Redemption cometh; thou shalt see the Savior’—thus the Voice spake; and with the answer I went up to Jerusalem rejoicing. Now, to whom is the Redemption? To all the world. And how shall it be? Strengthen thy faith, my son! Men say, I know, that there will be no happiness until Rome is razed from her hills. That is to say, the ills of the time are not, as I thought them, from ignorance of God, but from the misgovernment of rulers. Do we need to be told that human governments are never for the sake of religion? How many kings have you heard of who were better than their subjects? Oh no, no! The Redemption cannot be for a political purpose—to pull down rulers and powers, and vacate their places merely that others may take and enjoy them. If that were all of it, the wisdom of God would cease to be surpassing. I tell you, though it be but the saying of blind to blind, he that comes is to be a Savior of souls; and the Redemption means God once more on earth, and righteousness, that his stay here may be tolerable to himself.”\n\nDisappointment showed plainly on Ben-Hur’s face—his head drooped; and if he was not convinced, he yet felt himself incapable that moment of disputing the opinion of the Egyptian. Not so Ilderim.\n\n“By the splendor of God!” he cried, impulsively, “the judgment does away with all custom. The ways of the world are fixed, and cannot be changed. There must be a leader in every community clothed with power, else there is no reform.”\n\nBalthasar received the burst gravely.\n\n“Thy wisdom, good sheik, is of the world; and thou dost forget that it is from the ways of the world we are to be redeemed. Man as a subject is the ambition of a king; the soul of a man for its salvation is the desire of a God.”\n\nIlderim, though silenced, shook his head, unwilling to believe. Ben-Hur took up the argument for him.\n\n“Father—I call thee such by permission,” he said—“for whom wert thou required to ask at the gates of Jerusalem?”\n\nThe sheik threw him a grateful look.\n\n“I was to ask of the people,” said Balthasar, quietly, “‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’”\n\n“And you saw him in the cave by Bethlehem?”\n\n“We saw and worshipped him, and gave him presents—Melchior, gold; Gaspar, frankincense; and I, myrrh.”\n\n“When thou dost speak of fact, O father, to hear thee is to believe,” said Ben-Hur; “but in the matter of opinion, I cannot understand the kind of king thou wouldst make of the Child—I cannot separate the ruler from his powers and duties.”\n\n“Son,” said Balthasar, “we have the habit of studying closely the things which chance to lie at our feet, giving but a look at the greater objects in the distance. Thou seest now but the title—KING OF THE JEWS; wilt thou lift thine eyes to the mystery beyond it, the stumbling-block will disappear. Of the title, a word. Thy Israel hath seen better days—days in which God called thy people endearingly his people, and dealt with them through prophets. Now, if in those days he promised them the Savior I saw—promised him as KING OF THE JEWS—the appearance must be according to the promise, if only for the word’s sake. Ah, thou seest the reason of my question at the gate!—thou seest, and I will no more of it, but pass on. It may be, next, thou art regarding the dignity of the Child; if so, bethink thee—what is it to be a successor of Herod?—by the world’s standard of honor, what? Could not God better by his beloved? If thou canst think of the Almighty Father in want of a title, and stooping to borrow the inventions of men, why was I not bidden ask for a Cæsar at once? Oh, for the substance of that whereof we speak, look higher, I pray thee! Ask rather of what he whom we await shall be king; for I do tell, my son, that is the key to the mystery, which no man shall understand without the key.”\n\nBalthasar raised his eyes devoutly.\n\n“There is a kingdom on the earth, though it is not of it—a kingdom of wider bounds than the earth—wider than the sea and the earth, though they were rolled together as finest gold and spread by the beating of hammers. Its existence is a fact as our hearts are facts, and we journey through it from birth to death without seeing it; nor shall any man see it until he hath first known his own soul; for the kingdom is not for him, but for his soul. And in its dominion there is glory such as hath not entered imagination—original, incomparable, impossible of increase.”\n\n“What thou sayest, father, is a riddle to me,” said Ben-Hur. “I never heard of such a kingdom.”\n\n“Nor did I,” said Ilderim.\n\n“And I may not tell more of it,” Balthasar added, humbly dropping his eyes. “What it is, what it is for, how it may be reached, none can know until the Child comes to take possession of it as his own. He brings the key of the viewless gate, which he will open for his beloved, among whom will be all who love him, for of such only the redeemed will be.”\n\nAfter that there was a long silence, which Balthasar accepted as the end of the conversation.\n\n“Good sheik,” he said, in his placid way, “to-morrow or the next day I will go up to the city for a time. My daughter wishes to see the preparations for the games. I will speak further about the time of our going. And, my son, I will see you again. To you both, peace and good-night.”\n\nThey all arose from the table. The sheik and Ben-Hur remained looking after the Egyptian until he was conducted out of the tent.\n\n“Sheik Ilderim,” said Ben-Hur then, “I have heard strange things tonight. Give me leave, I pray, to walk by the lake that I may think of them.”\n\n“Go; and I will come after you.”\n\nThey washed their hands again; after which, at a sign from the master, a servant brought Ben-Hur his shoes, and directly he went out.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII\n\nUp a little way from the dower there was a cluster of palms, which threw its shade half in the water, half on the land. A bulbul sang from the branches a song of invitation. Ben-Hur stopped beneath to listen. At any other time the notes of the bird would have driven thought away; but the story of the Egyptian was a burden of wonder, and he was a laborer carrying it, and, like other laborers, there was to him no music in the sweetest music until mind and body were happily attuned by rest.\n\nThe night was quiet. Not a ripple broke upon the shore. The old stars of the old East were all out, each in its accustomed place; and there was summer everywhere—on land, on lake, in the sky.\n\nBen-Hur’s imagination was heated, his feelings aroused, his will all unsettled.\n\nSo the palms, the sky, the air, seemed to him of the far south zone into which Balthasar had been driven by despair for men; the lake, with its motionless surface, was a suggestion of the Nilotic mother by which the good man stood praying when the Spirit made its radiant appearance. Had all these accessories of the miracle come to Ben-Hur? or had he been transferred to them? And what if the miracle should be repeated—and to him? He feared, yet wished, and even waited for the vision. When at last his feverish mood was cooled, permitting him to become himself, he was able to think.\n\nHis scheme of life has been explained. In all reflection about it heretofore there had been one hiatus which he had not been able to bridge or fill up—one so broad he could see but vaguely to the other side of it. When, finally, he was graduated a captain as well as a soldier, to what object should he address his efforts? Revolution he contemplated, of course; but the processes of revolution have always been the same, and to lead men into them there have always been required, first, a cause or presence to enlist adherents; second, an end, or something as a practical achievement. As a rule he fights well who has wrongs to redress; but vastly better fights he who, with wrongs as a spur, has also steadily before him a glorious result in prospect—a result in which he can discern balm for wounds, compensation for valor, remembrance and gratitude in the event of death.\n\nTo determine the sufficiency of either the cause or the end, it was needful that Ben-Hur should study the adherents to whom he looked when all was ready for action. Very naturally, they were his countrymen. The wrongs of Israel were to every son of Abraham, and each one was a cause vastly holy, vastly inspiring.\n\nAy, the cause was there; but the end—what should it be?\n\nThe hours and days he had given this branch of his scheme were past calculation—all with the same conclusion—a dim, uncertain, general idea of national liberty. Was it sufficient? He could not say no, for that would have been the death of his hope; he shrank from saying yes, because his judgment taught him better. He could not assure himself even that Israel was able single-handed to successfully combat Rome. He knew the resources of that great enemy; he knew her art was superior to her resources. A universal alliance might suffice, but, alas! that was impossible, except—and upon the exception how long and earnestly he had dwelt!—except a hero would come from one of the suffering nations, and by martial successes accomplish a renown to fill the whole earth. What glory to Judea could she prove the Macedonia of the new Alexander! Alas, again! Under the rabbis valor was possible, but not discipline. And then the taunt of Messala in the garden of Herod—“All you conquer in the six days, you lose on the seventh.”\n\nSo it happened he never approached the chasm thinking to surmount it, but he was beaten back; and so incessantly had he failed in the object that he had about given it over, except as a thing of chance. The hero might be discovered in his day, or he might not. God only knew. Such his state of mind, there need be no lingering upon the effect of Malluch’s skeleton recital of the story of Balthasar. He heard it with a bewildering satisfaction—a feeling that here was the solution of the trouble—here was the requisite hero found at last; and he a son of the Lion tribe, and King of the Jews! Behind the hero, lo! the world in arms.\n\nThe king implied a kingdom; he was to be a warrior glorious as David, a ruler wise and magnificent as Solomon; the kingdom was to be a power against which Rome was to dash itself to pieces. There would be colossal war, and the agonies of death and birth—then peace, meaning, of course, Judean dominion forever.\n\nBen-Hur’s heart beat hard as for an instant he had a vision of Jerusalem the capital of the world, and Zion, the site of the throne of the Universal Master.\n\nIt seemed to the enthusiast rare fortune that the man who had seen the king was at the tent to which he was going. He could see him there, and hear him, and learn of him what all he knew of the coming change, especially all he knew of the time of its happening. If it were at hand, the campaign with Maxentius should be abandoned; and he would go and set about organizing and arming the tribes, that Israel might be ready when the great day of the restoration began to break.\n\nNow, as we have seen, from Balthasar himself Ben-Hur had the marvelous story. Was he satisfied?\n\nThere was a shadow upon him deeper than that of the cluster of palms—the shadow of a great uncertainty, which—take note, O reader! which pertained more to the kingdom than the king.\n\n“What of this kingdom? And what is it to be?” Ben-Hur asked himself in thought.\n\nThus early arose the questions which were to follow the Child to his end, and survive him on earth—incomprehensible in his day, a dispute in this—an enigma to all who do not or cannot understand that every man is two in one—a deathless Soul and a mortal Body.\n\n“What is it to be?” he asked.\n\nFor us, O reader, the Child himself has answered; but for Ben-Hur there were only the words of Balthasar, “On the earth, yet not of it—not for men, but for their souls—a dominion, nevertheless, of unimaginable glory.”\n\nWhat wonder the hapless youth found the phrases but the darkening of a riddle?\n\n“The hand of man is not in it,” he said, despairingly. “Nor has the king of such a kingdom use for men; neither toilers, nor councillors, nor soldiers. The earth must die or be made anew, and for government new principles must be discovered—something besides armed hands—something in place of Force. But what?”\n\nAgain, O reader!\n\nThat which we will not see, he could not. The power there is in Love had not yet occurred to any man; much less had one come saying directly that for government and its objects—peace and order—Love is better and mightier than Force.\n\nIn the midst of his reverie a hand was laid upon his shoulder.\n\n“I have a word to say, O son of Arrius,” said Ilderim, stopping by his side—“a word, and then I must return, for the night is going.”\n\n“I give you welcome, sheik.”\n\n“As to the things you have heard but now,” said Ilderim, almost without pause, “take in belief all save that relating to the kind of kingdom the Child will set up when he comes; as to so much keep virgin mind until you hear Simonides the merchant—a good man here in Antioch, to whom I will make you known. The Egyptian gives you coinage of his dreams which are too good for the earth; Simonides is wiser; he will ring you the sayings of your prophets, giving book and page, so you cannot deny that the Child will be King of the Jews in fact—ay, by the splendor of God! a king as Herod was, only better and far more magnificent. And then, see you, we will taste the sweetness of vengeance. I have said. Peace to you!”\n\n“Stay—sheik!”\n\nIf Ilderim heard his call, he did not stay.\n\n“Simonides again!” said Ben-Hur, bitterly. “Simonides here, Simonides there; from this one now, then from that! I am like to be well ridden by my father’s servant, who knows at least to hold fast that which is mine; wherefore he is richer, if indeed he be not wiser, than the Egyptian. By the covenant! it is not to the faithless a man should go to find a faith to keep—and I will not. But, hark! singing—and the voice a woman’s—or an angel’s! It comes this way.”\n\nDown the lake towards the dower came a woman singing. Her voice floated along the hushed water melodious as a flute, and louder growing each instant. Directly the dipping of oars was heard in slow measure; a little later the words were distinguishable—words in purest Greek, best fitted of all the tongues of the day for the expression of passionate grief.\n\nTHE LAMENT. (Egyptian.)\n\nI sigh as I sing for the story land Across the Syrian sea. The odorous winds from the musky sand Were breaths of life to me. They play with the plumes of the whispering palm For me, alas! no more; Nor more does the Nile in the moonlit calm Moan past the Memphian shore.\n\nO Nilus! thou god of my fainting soul! In dreams thou comest to me; And, dreaming, I play with the lotus bowl, And sing old songs to thee; And hear from afar the Memnonian strain, And calls from dear Simbel; And wake to a passion of grief and pain That e’er I said—Farewell!\n\nAt the conclusion of the song the singer was past the cluster of palms. The last word—farewell—floated past Ben-Hur weighted with all the sweet sorrow of parting. The passing of the boat was as the passing of a deeper shadow into the deeper night.\n\nBen-Hur drew a long breath hardly distinguishable from a sigh.\n\n“I know her by the song—the daughter of Balthasar. How beautiful it was! And how beautiful is she!”\n\nHe recalled her large eyes curtained slightly by the drooping lids, the cheeks oval and rosy rich, the lips full and deep with dimpling in the corners, and all the grace of the tall lithe figure.\n\n“How beautiful she is!” he repeated.\n\nAnd his heart made answer by a quickening of its movement.\n\nThen, almost the same instant, another face, younger and quite as beautiful—more childlike and tender, if not so passionate—appeared as if held up to him out of the lake.\n\n“Esther!” he said, smiling. “As I wished, a star has been sent to me.”\n\nHe turned, and passed slowly back to the tent.\n\nHis life had been crowded with griefs and with vengeful preparations—too much crowded for love. Was this the beginning of a happy change?\n\nAnd if the influence went with him into the tent, whose was it? Esther had given him a cup. So had the Egyptian. And both had come to him at the same time under the palms.\n\nWhich?\n\n# BOOK FIFTH\n\n“Only the actions of the just Smell sweet and blossom in the dust.” SHIRLEY.\n\n“And, through the heat of conflict, keeps the law, In calmness made, and sees what he foresaw.” WORDSWORTH. CHAPTER I\n\nThe morning after the bacchanalia in the saloon of the palace, the divan was covered with young patricians. Maxentius might come, and the city throng to receive him; the legion might descend from Mount Sulpius in glory of arms and armor; from Nymphaeum to Omphalus there might be ceremonial splendors to shame the most notable ever before seen or heard of in the gorgeous East; yet would the many continue to sleep ignominiously on the divan where they had fallen or been carelessly tumbled by the indifferent slaves; that they would be able to take part in the reception that day was about as possible as for the lay-figures in the studio of a modern artist to rise and go bonneted and plumed through the one, two, three of a waltz.\n\nNot all, however, who participated in the orgy were in the shameful condition. When dawn began to peer through the skylights of the saloon, Messala arose, and took the chaplet from his head, in sign that the revel was at end; then he gathered his robe about him, gave a last look at the scene, and, without a word, departed for his quarters. Cicero could not have retired with more gravity from a night-long senatorial debate.\n\nThree hours afterwards two couriers entered his room, and from his own hand received each a despatch, sealed and in duplicate, and consisting chiefly of a letter to Valerius Gratus, the procurator, still resident in Cæsarea. The importance attached to the speedy and certain delivery of the paper may be inferred. One courier was to proceed overland, the other by sea; both were to make the utmost haste.\n\nIt is of great concern now that the reader should be fully informed of the contents of the letter thus forwarded, and it is accordingly given:\n\n“Messala to Gratus.\n\n“O my Midas!\n\n“I pray thou take no offense at the address, seeing it is one of love and gratitude, and an admission that thou art most fortunate among men; seeing, also, that thy ears are as they were derived from thy mother, only proportionate to thy matured condition.\n\n“O my Midas!\n\n“I have to relate to thee an astonishing event, which, though as yet somewhat in the field of conjecture, will, I doubt not, justify thy instant consideration.\n\n“Allow me first to revive thy recollection. Remember, a good many years ago, a family of a prince of Jerusalem, incredibly ancient and vastly rich—by name Ben-Hur. If thy memory have a limp or ailment of any kind, there is, if I mistake not, a wound on thy head which may help thee to a revival of the circumstance.\n\n“Next, to arouse thy interest. In punishment of the attempt upon thy life—for dear repose of conscience, may all the gods forbid it should ever prove to have been an accident!—the family were seized and summarily disposed of, and their property confiscated. And inasmuch, O my Midas! as the action had the approval of our Cæsar, who was as just as he was wise—be there flowers upon his altars forever!—there should be no shame in referring to the sums which were realized to us respectively from that source, for which it is not possible I can ever cease to be grateful to thee, certainly not while I continue, as at present, in the uninterrupted enjoyment of the part which fell to me.\n\n“In vindication of thy wisdom—a quality for which, as I am now advised, the son of Gordius, to whom I have boldly likened thee, was never distinguished among men or gods—I recall further that thou didst make disposition of the family of Hur, both of us at the time supposing the plan hit upon to be the most effective possible for the purposes in view, which were silence and delivery over to inevitable but natural death. Thou wilt remember what thou didst with the mother and sister of the malefactor; yet, if now I yield to a desire to learn whether they be living or dead, I know, from knowing the amiability of thy nature, O my Gratus, that thou wilt pardon me as one scarcely less amiable than thyself.\n\n“As more immediately essential to the present business, however, I take the liberty of inviting to thy remembrance that the actual criminal was sent to the galleys a slave for life—so the precept ran; and it may serve to make the event which I am about to relate the more astonishing by saying here that I saw and read the receipt for his body delivered in course to the tribune commanding a galley.\n\n“Thou mayst begin now to give me more especial heed, O my most excellent Phrygian!\n\n“Referring to the limit of life at the oar, the outlaw thus justly disposed of should be dead, or, better speaking, some one of the three thousand Oceanides should have taken him to husband at least five years ago. And if thou wilt excuse a momentary weakness, O most virtuous and tender of men! inasmuch as I loved him in childhood, and also because he was very handsome—I used in much admiration to call him my Ganymede—he ought in right to have fallen into the arms of the most beautiful daughter of the family. Of opinion, however, that he was certainly dead, I have lived quite five years in calm and innocent enjoyment of the fortune for which I am in a degree indebted to him. I make the admission of indebtedness without intending it to diminish my obligation to thee.\n\n“Now I am at the very point of interest.\n\n“Last night, while acting as master of the feast for a party just from Rome—their extreme youth and inexperience appealed to my compassion—I heard a singular story. Maxentius, the consul, as you know, comes to-day to conduct a campaign against the Parthians. Of the ambitious who are to accompany him there is one, a son of the late duumvir Quintus Arrius. I had occasion to inquire about him particularly. When Arrius set out in pursuit of the pirates, whose defeat gained him his final honors, he had no family; when he returned from the expedition, he brought back with him an heir. Now be thou composed as becomes the owner of so many talents in ready sestertii! The son and heir of whom I speak is he whom thou didst send to the galleys—the very Ben-Hur who should have died at his oar five years ago—returned now with fortune and rank, and possibly as a Roman citizen, to— Well, thou art too firmly seated to be alarmed, but I, O my Midas! I am in danger—no need to tell thee of what. Who should know, if thou dost not?\n\n“Sayst thou to all this, tut-tut?\n\n“When Arrius, the father, by adoption, of this apparition from the arms of the most beautiful of the Oceanides (see above my opinion of what she should be), joined battle with the pirates, his vessel was sunk, and but two of all her crew escaped drowning—Arrius himself and this one, his heir.\n\n“The officers who took them from the plank on which they were floating say the associate of the fortunate tribune was a young man who, when lifted to the deck, was in the dress of a galley slave.\n\n“This should be convincing, to say least; but lest thou say tut-tut again, I tell thee, O my Midas! that yesterday, by good chance—I have a vow to Fortune in consequence—I met the mysterious son of Arrius face to face; and I declare now that, though I did not then recognize him, he is the very Ben-Hur who was for years my playmate; the very Ben-Hur who, if he be a man, though of the commonest grade, must this very moment of my writing be thinking of vengeance—for so would I were I he—vengeance not to be satisfied short of life; vengeance for country, mother, sister, self, and—I say it last, though thou mayst think it would be first—for fortune lost.\n\n“By this time, O good my benefactor and friend! my Gratus! in consideration of thy sestertii in peril, their loss being the worst which could befall one of thy high estate—I quit calling thee after the foolish old King of Phrygia—by this time, I say (meaning after having read me so far), I have faith to believe thou hast ceased saying tut-tut, and art ready to think what ought to be done in such emergency.\n\n“It were vulgar to ask thee now what shall be done. Rather let me say I am thy client; or, better yet, thou art my Ulysses whose part it is to give me sound direction.\n\n“And I please myself thinking I see thee when this letter is put into thy hand. I see thee read it once; thy countenance all gravity, and then again with a smile; then, hesitation ended, and thy judgment formed, it is this, or it is that; wisdom like Mercury’s, promptitude like Cæsar’s.\n\n“The sun is now fairly risen. An hour hence two messengers will depart from my door, each with a sealed copy hereof; one of them will go by land, the other by sea, so important do I regard it that thou shouldst be early and particularly informed of the appearance of our enemy in this part of our Roman world.\n\n“I will await thy answer here.\n\n“Ben-Hur’s going and coming will of course be regulated by his master, the consul, who, though he exert himself without rest day and night, cannot get away under a month. Thou knowest what work it is to assemble and provide for an army destined to operate in a desolate, townless country.\n\n“I saw the Jew yesterday in the Grove of Daphne; and if he be not there now, he is certainly in the neighborhood, making it easy for me to keep him in eye. Indeed, wert thou to ask me where he is now, I should say, with the most positive assurance, he is to be found at the old Orchard of Palms, under the tent of the traitor Sheik Ilderim, who cannot long escape our strong hand. Be not surprised if Maxentius, as his first measure, places the Arab on ship for forwarding to Rome.\n\n“I am so particular about the whereabouts of the Jew because it will be important to thee, O illustrious! when thou comest to consider what is to be done; for already I know, and by the knowledge I flatter myself I am growing in wisdom, that in every scheme involving human action there are three elements always to be taken into account—time, place, and agency.\n\n“If thou sayest this is the place, have thou then no hesitancy in trusting the business to thy most loving friend, who would be thy aptest scholar as well.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nAbout the time the couriers departed from Messala’s door with the despatches (it being yet the early morning hour), Ben-Hur entered Ilderim’s tent. He had taken a plunge into the lake, and breakfasted, and appeared now in an under-tunic, sleeveless, and with skirt scarcely reaching to the knee.\n\nThe sheik saluted him from the divan.\n\n“I give thee peace, son of Arrius,” he said, with admiration, for, in truth, he had never seen a more perfect illustration of glowing, powerful, confident manhood. “I give thee peace and good-will. The horses are ready, I am ready. And thou?”\n\n“The peace thou givest me, good sheik, I give thee in return. I thank thee for so much good-will. I am ready.”\n\nIlderim clapped his hands.\n\n“I will have the horses brought. Be seated.”\n\n“Are they yoked?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then suffer me to serve myself,” said Ben-Hur. “It is needful that I make the acquaintance of thy Arabs. I must know them by name, O sheik, that I may speak to them singly; nor less must I know their temper, for they are like men: if bold, the better of scolding; if timid, the better of praise and flattery. Let the servants bring me the harness.”\n\n“And the chariot?” asked the sheik.\n\n“I will let the chariot alone to-day. In its place, let them bring me a fifth horse, if thou hast it; he should be barebacked, and fleet as the others.”\n\nIlderim’s wonder was aroused, and he summoned a servant immediately.\n\n“Bid them bring the harness for the four,” he said—“the harness for the four, and the bridle for Sirius.”\n\nIlderim then arose.\n\n“Sirius is my love, and I am his, O son of Arrius. We have been comrades for twenty years—in tent, in battle, in all stages of the desert we have been comrades. I will show him to you.”\n\nGoing to the division curtain, he held it, while Ben-Hur passed under. The horses came to him in a body. One with a small head, luminous eyes, neck like the segment of a bended bow, and mighty chest, curtained thickly by a profusion of mane soft and wavy as a damsel’s locks, nickered low and gladly at sight of him.\n\n“Good horse,” said the sheik, patting the dark-brown cheek. “Good horse, good-morning.” Turning then to Ben-Hur, he added, “This is Sirius, father of the four here. Mira, the mother, awaits our return, being too precious to be hazarded in a region where there is a stronger hand than mine. And much I doubt,” he laughed as he spoke—“much I doubt, O son of Arrius, if the tribe could endure her absence. She is their glory; they worship her; did she gallop over them, they would laugh. Ten thousand horsemen, sons of the desert, will ask to-day, ‘Have you heard of Mira?’ And to the answer, ‘She is well,’ they will say, ‘God is good! blessed be God!’”\n\n“Mira—Sirius—names of stars, are they not, O sheik?” asked Ben-Hur, going to each of the four, and to the sire, offering his hand.\n\n“And why not?” replied Ilderim. “Wert thou ever abroad on the desert at night?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then thou canst not know how much we Arabs depend upon the stars. We borrow their names in gratitude, and give them in love. My fathers all had their Miras, as I have mine; and these children are stars no less. There, see thou, is Rigel, and there Antares; that one is Atair, and he whom thou goest to now is Aldebaran, the youngest of the brood, but none the worse of that—no, not he! Against the wind he will carry thee till it roar in thy ears like Akaba; and he will go where thou sayest, son of Arrius—ay, by the glory of Solomon! he will take thee to the lion’s jaws, if thou darest so much.”\n\nThe harness was brought. With his own hands Ben-Hur equipped the horses; with his own hands he led them out of the tent, and there attached the reins.\n\n“Bring me Sirius,” he said.\n\nAn Arab could not have better sprung to seat on the courser’s back.\n\n“And now the reins.”\n\nThey were given him, and carefully separated.\n\n“Good sheik,” he said, “I am ready. Let a guide go before me to the field, and send some of thy men with water.”\n\nThere was no trouble at starting. The horses were not afraid. Already there seemed a tacit understanding between them and the new driver, who had performed his part calmly, and with the confidence which always begets confidence. The order of going was precisely that of driving, except that Ben-Hur sat upon Sirius instead of standing in the chariot. Ilderim’s spirit arose. He combed his beard, and smiled with satisfaction as he muttered, “He is not a Roman, no, by the splendor of God!” He followed on foot, the entire tenantry of the dowar—men, women, and children—pouring after him, participants all in his solicitude, if not in his confidence.\n\nThe field, when reached, proved ample and well fitted for the training, which Ben-Hur began immediately by driving the four at first slowly, and in perpendicular lines, and then in wide circles. Advancing a step in the course, he put them next into a trot; again progressing, he pushed into a gallop; at length he contracted the circles, and yet later drove eccentrically here and there, right, left, forward, and without a break. An hour was thus occupied. Slowing the gait to a walk, he drove up to Ilderim.\n\n“The work is done, nothing now but practice,” he said. “I give you joy, Sheik Ilderim, that you have such servants as these. See,” he continued, dismounting and going to the horses, “see, the gloss of their red coats is without spot; they breathe lightly as when I began. I give thee great joy, and it will go hard if”—he turned his flashing eyes upon the old man’s face—“if we have not the victory and our—”\n\nHe stopped, colored, bowed. At the sheik’s side he observed, for the first time, Balthasar, leaning upon his staff, and two women closely veiled. At one of the latter he looked a second time, saying to himself, with a flutter about his heart, “’Tis she—’tis the Egyptian!” Ilderim picked up his broken sentence—\n\n“The victory, and our revenge!” Then he said aloud, “I am not afraid; I am glad. Son of Arrius, thou art the man. Be the end like the beginning, and thou shalt see of what stuff is the lining of the hand of an Arab who is able to give.”\n\n“I thank thee, good sheik,” Ben-Hur returned, modestly. “Let the servants bring drink for the horses.”\n\nWith his own hands he gave the water.\n\nRemounting Sirius, he renewed the training, going as before from walk to trot, from trot to gallop; finally, he pushed the steady racers into the run, gradually quickening it to full speed. The performance then became exciting; and there were applause for the dainty handling of the reins, and admiration for the four, which were the same, whether they flew forward or wheeled in varying curvature. In their action there were unity, power, grace, pleasure, all without effort or sign of labor. The admiration was unmixed with pity or reproach, which would have been as well bestowed upon swallows in their evening flight.\n\nIn the midst of the exercises, and the attention they received from all the bystanders, Malluch came upon the ground, seeking the sheik.\n\n“I have a message for you, O sheik,” he said, availing himself of a moment he supposed favorable for the speech—“a message from Simonides, the merchant.”\n\n“Simonides!” ejaculated the Arab. “Ah! ’tis well. May Abaddon take all his enemies!”\n\n“He bade me give thee first the holy peace of God,” Malluch continued; “and then this despatch, with prayer that thou read it the instant of receipt.”\n\nIlderim, standing in his place, broke the sealing of the package delivered to him, and from a wrapping of fine linen took two letters, which he proceeded to read.\n\n[No. 1.]\n\n“Simonides to Sheik Ilderim.\n\n“O friend!\n\n“Assure thyself first of a place in my inner heart.\n\n“Then—\n\n“There is in thy dowar a youth of fair presence, calling himself the son of Arrius; and such he is by adoption.\n\n“He is very dear to me.\n\n“He hath a wonderful history, which I will tell thee; come thou to-day or to-morrow, that I may tell thee the history, and have thy counsel.\n\n“Meantime, favor all his requests, so they be not against honor. Should there be need of reparation, I am bound to thee for it.\n\n“That I have interest in this youth, keep thou private.\n\n“Remember me to thy other guest. He, his daughter, thyself, and all whom thou mayst choose to be of thy company, must depend upon me at the Circus the day of the games. I have seats already engaged.\n\n“To thee and all thine, peace.\n\n“What should I be, O my friend, but thy friend?\n\n[No. 2.]\n\n“Simonides to Sheik Ilderim.\n\n“O friend!\n\n“Out of the abundance of my experience, I send you a word.\n\n“There is a sign which all persons not Romans, and who have moneys or goods subject to despoilment, accept as warning—that is, the arrival at a seat of power of some high Roman official charged with authority.\n\n“To-day comes the Consul Maxentius.\n\n“Be thou warned!\n\n“Another word of advice.\n\n“A conspiracy, to be of effect against thee, O friend, must include the Herods as parties; thou hast great properties in their dominions.\n\n“Wherefore keep thou watch.\n\n“Send this morning to thy trusty keepers of the roads leading south from Antioch, and bid them search every courier going and coming; if they find private despatches relating to thee or thine affairs, THOU SHOULDST SEE THEM.\n\n“You should have received this yesterday, though it is not too late, if you act promptly.\n\n“If couriers left Antioch this morning, your messengers know the byways, and can get before them with your orders.\n\n“Do not hesitate.\n\n“Burn this after reading.\n\n“O my friend! thy friend,\n\nIlderim read the letters a second time, and refolded them in the linen wrap, and put the package under his girdle.\n\nThe exercises in the field continued but a little longer—in all about two hours. At their conclusion, Ben-Hur brought the four to a walk, and drove to Ilderim.\n\n“With leave, O sheik,” he said, “I will return thy Arabs to the tent, and bring them out again this afternoon.”\n\nIlderim walked to him as he sat on Sirius, and said, “I give them to you, son of Arrius, to do with as you will until after the games. You have done with them in two hours what the Roman—may jackals gnaw his bones fleshless!—could not in as many weeks. We will win—by the splendor of God, we will win!”\n\nAt the tent Ben-Hur remained with the horses while they were being cared for; then, after a plunge in the lake and a cup of arrack with the sheik, whose flow of spirits was royally exuberant, he dressed himself in his Jewish garb again, and walked with Malluch on into the Orchard.\n\nThere was much conversation between the two, not all of it important. One part, however, must not be overlooked. Ben-Hur was speaking.\n\n“I will give you,” he said, “an order for my property stored in the khan this side the river by the Seleucian Bridge. Bring it to me to-day, if you can. And, good Malluch—if I do not overtask you—”\n\nMalluch protested heartily his willingness to be of service.\n\n“Thank you, Malluch, thank you,” said Ben-Hur. “I will take you at your word, remembering that we are brethren of the old tribe, and that the enemy is a Roman. First, then—as you are a man of business, which I much fear Sheik Ilderim is not—”\n\n“Arabs seldom are,” said Malluch, gravely.\n\n“Nay, I do not impeach their shrewdness, Malluch. It is well, however, to look after them. To save all forfeit or hindrance in connection with the race, you would put me perfectly at rest by going to the office of the Circus, and seeing that he has complied with every preliminary rule; and if you can get a copy of the rules, the service may be of great avail to me. I would like to know the colors I am to wear, and particularly the number of the crypt I am to occupy at the starting; if it be next Messala’s on the right or left, it is well; if not, and you can have it changed so as to bring me next the Roman, do so. Have you good memory, Malluch?”\n\n“It has failed me, but never, son of Arrius, where the heart helped it as now.”\n\n“I will venture, then, to charge you with one further service. I saw yesterday that Messala was proud of his chariot, as he might be, for the best of Cæsar’s scarcely surpass it. Can you not make its display an excuse which will enable you to find if it be light or heavy? I would like to have its exact weight and measurements—and, Malluch, though you fail in all else, bring me exactly the height his axle stands above the ground. You understand, Malluch? I do not wish him to have any actual advantage of me. I do not care for his splendor; if I beat him, it will make his fall the harder, and my triumph the more complete. If there are advantages really important, I want them.”\n\n“I see, I see!” said Malluch. “A line dropped from the centre of the axle is what you want.”\n\n“Thou hast it; and be glad, Malluch—it is the last of my commissions. Let us return to the dowar.”\n\nAt the door of the tent they found a servant replenishing the smoke-stained bottles of leben freshly made, and stopped to refresh themselves. Shortly afterwards Malluch returned to the city.\n\nDuring their absence, a messenger well mounted had been despatched with orders as suggested by Simonides. He was an Arab, and carried nothing written.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n“Iras, the daughter of Balthasar, sends me with salutation and a message,” said a servant to Ben-Hur, who was taking his ease in the tent.\n\n“Give me the message.”\n\n“Would it please you to accompany her upon the lake?”\n\n“I will carry the answer myself. Tell her so.”\n\nHis shoes were brought him, and in a few minutes Ben-Hur sallied out to find the fair Egyptian. The shadow of the mountains was creeping over the Orchard of Palms in advance of night. Afar through the trees came the tinkling of sheep bells, the lowing of cattle, and the voices of the herdsmen bringing their charges home. Life at the Orchard, it should be remembered, was in all respects as pastoral as life on the scantier meadows of the desert.\n\nSheik Ilderim had witnessed the exercises of the afternoon, being a repetition of those of the morning; after which he had gone to the city in answer to the invitation of Simonides; he might return in the night; but, considering the immensity of the field to be talked over with his friend, it was hardly possible. Ben-Hur, thus left alone, had seen his horses cared for; cooled and purified himself in the lake; exchanged the field garb for his customary vestments, all white, as became a Sadducean of the pure blood; supped early; and, thanks to the strength of youth, was well recovered from the violent exertion he had undergone.\n\nIt is neither wise nor honest to detract from beauty as a quality. There cannot be a refined soul insensible to its influence. The story of Pygmalion and his statue is as natural as it is poetical. Beauty is of itself a power; and it was now drawing Ben-Hur.\n\nThe Egyptian was to him a wonderfully beautiful woman—beautiful of face, beautiful of form. In his thought she always appeared to him as he saw her at the fountain; and he felt the influence of her voice, sweeter because in tearful expression of gratitude to him, and of her eyes—the large, soft, black, almond-shaped eyes declarative of her race—eyes which looked more than lies in the supremest wealth of words to utter; and recurrences of the thought of her were returns just so frequent of a figure tall, slender, graceful, refined, wrapped in rich and floating drapery, wanting nothing but a fitting mind to make her, like the Shulamite, and in the same sense, terrible as an army with banners. In other words, as she returned to his fancy, the whole passionate Song of Solomon came with her, inspired by her presence. With this sentiment and that feeling, he was going to see if she actually justified them. It was not love that was taking him, but admiration and curiosity, which might be the heralds of love.\n\nThe landing was a simple affair, consisting of a short stairway, and a platform garnished by some lamp-posts; yet at the top of the steps he paused, arrested by what he beheld.\n\nThere was a shallop resting upon the clear water lightly as an egg-shell. An Ethiop—the camel-driver at the Castalian fount—occupied the rower’s place, his blackness intensified by a livery of shining white. All the boat aft was cushioned and carpeted with stuffs brilliant with Tyrian red. On the rudder seat sat the Egyptian herself, sunk in Indian shawls and a very vapor of most delicate veils and scarfs. Her arms were bare to the shoulders; and, not merely faultless in shape, they had the effect of compelling attention to them—their pose, their action, their expression; the hands, the fingers even, seemed endowed with graces and meaning; each was an object of beauty. The shoulders and neck were protected from the evening air by an ample scarf, which yet did not hide them.\n\nIn the glance he gave her, Ben-Hur paid no attention to these details. There was simply an impression made upon him; and, like strong light, it was a sensation, not a thing of sight or enumeration. Thy lips are like a thread of scarlet; thy temples are like a piece of pomegranate within thy locks. Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away; for, lo! the winter is past, the rain is over and gone; the flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing of birds is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in the land—such was the impression she made upon him translated into words.\n\n“Come,” she said, observing him stop, “come, or I shall think you a poor sailor.”\n\nThe red of his cheek deepened. Did she know anything of his life upon the sea? He descended to the platform at once.\n\n“I was afraid,” he said, as he took the vacant seat before her.\n\n“Of what?”\n\n“Of sinking the boat,” he replied, smiling.\n\n“Wait until we are in deeper water,” she said, giving a signal to the black, who dipped the oars, and they were off.\n\nIf love and Ben-Hur were enemies, the latter was never more at mercy. The Egyptian sat where he could not but see her; she, whom he had already engrossed in memory as his ideal of the Shulamite. With her eyes giving light to his, the stars might come out, and he not see them; and so they did. The night might fall with unrelieved darkness everywhere else; her look would make illumination for him. And then, as everybody knows, given youth and such companionship, there is no situation in which the fancy takes such complete control as upon tranquil waters under a calm night sky, warm with summer. It is so easy at such time to glide imperceptibly out of the commonplace into the ideal.\n\n“Give me the rudder,” he said.\n\n“No,” she replied, “that were to reverse the relation. Did I not ask you to ride with me? I am indebted to you, and would begin payment. You may talk and I will listen, or I will talk and you will listen: that choice is yours; but it shall be mine to choose where we go, and the way thither.”\n\n“And where may that be?”\n\n“You are alarmed again.”\n\n“O fair Egyptian, I but asked you the first question of every captive.”\n\n“Call me Egypt.”\n\n“I would rather call you Iras.”\n\n“You may think of me by that name, but call me Egypt.”\n\n“Egypt is a country, and means many people.”\n\n“Yes, yes! And such a country!”\n\n“I see; it is to Egypt we are going.”\n\n“Would we were! I would be so glad.”\n\nShe sighed as she spoke.\n\n“You have no care for me, then,” he said.\n\n“Ah, by that I know you were never there.”\n“I never was.”\n\n“Oh, it is the land where there are no unhappy people, the desired of all the rest of the earth, the mother of all the gods, and therefore supremely blest. There, O son of Arrius, there the happy find increase of happiness, and the wretched, going, drink once of the sweet water of the sacred river, and laugh and sing, rejoicing like children.”\n\n“Are not the very poor with you there as elsewhere?”\n\n“The very poor in Egypt are the very simple in wants and ways,” she replied. “They have no wish beyond enough, and how little that is, a Greek or a Roman cannot know.”\n\n“But I am neither Greek nor Roman.”\n\nShe laughed.\n\n“I have a garden of roses, and in the midst of it is a tree, and its bloom is the richest of all. Whence came it, think you?”\n\n“From Persia, the home of the rose.”\n\n“From India, then.”\n\n“Ah! one of the isles of Greece.”\n\n“I will tell you,” she said: “a traveller found it perishing by the roadside on the plain of Rephaim.”\n\n“Oh, in Judea!”\n\n“I put it in the earth left bare by the receding Nile, and the soft south wind blew over the desert and nursed it, and the sun kissed it in pity; after which it could not else than grow and flourish. I stand in its shade now, and it thanks me with much perfume. As with the roses, so with the men of Israel. Where shall they reach perfection but in Egypt?”\n\n“Moses was but one of millions.”\n\n“Nay, there was a reader of dreams. Will you forget him?”\n\n“The friendly Pharaohs are dead.”\n\n“Ah, yes! The river by which they dwelt sings to them in their tombs; yet the same sun tempers the same air to the same people.”\n\n“Alexandria is but a Roman town.”\n\n“She has but exchanged sceptres. Cæsar took from her that of the sword, and in its place left that of learning. Go with me to the Brucheium, and I will show you the college of nations; to the Serapeion, and see the perfection of architecture; to the Library, and read the immortals; to the theatre, and hear the heroics of the Greeks and Hindoos; to the quay, and count the triumphs of commerce; descend with me into the streets, O son of Arrius, and, when the philosophers have dispersed, and taken with them the masters of all the arts, and all the gods have home their votaries, and nothing remains of the day but its pleasures, you shall hear the stories that have amused men from the beginning, and the songs which will never, never die.”\n\nAs he listened, Ben-Hur was carried back to the night when, in the summer-house in Jerusalem, his mother, in much the same poetry of patriotism, declaimed the departed glories of Israel.\n\n“I see now why you wish to be called Egypt. Will you sing me a song if I call you by that name? I heard you last night.”\n\n“That was a hymn of the Nile,” she answered, “a lament which I sing when I would fancy I smell the breath of the desert, and hear the surge of the dear old river; let me rather give you a piece of the Indian mind. When we get to Alexandria, I will take you to the corner of the street where you can hear it from the daughter of the Ganga, who taught it to me. Kapila, you should know, was one of the most revered of the Hindoo sages.”\n\nThen, as if it were a natural mode of expression, she began the song.\n\n# I.\n\n“Kapila, Kapila, so young and true, I yearn for a glory like thine, And hail thee from battle to ask anew, Can ever thy Valor be mine?\n\n“Kapila sat on his charger dun, A hero never so grave: ‘Who loveth all things hath fear of none, ’Tis love that maketh me brave. A woman gave me her soul one day, The soul of my soul to be alway; Thence came my Valor to me, Go try it—try it—and see.’\n\n# II.\n\n“Kapila, Kapila, so old and gray, The queen is calling for me; But ere I go hence, I wish thou wouldst say, How Wisdom first came to thee.\n\n“Kapila stood in his temple door, A priest in eremite guise: ‘It did not come as men get their lore, ’Tis faith that maketh me wise. A woman gave me her heart one day, The heart of my heart to be alway; Thence came my Wisdom to me, Go try it—try it—and see.’”\n\nBen-Hur had not time to express his thanks for the song before the keel of the boat grated upon the underlying sand, and, next moment, the bow ran upon the shore.\n\n“A quick voyage, O Egypt!” he cried.\n\n“And a briefer stay!” she replied, as, with a strong push, the black sent them shooting into the open water again.\n\n“You will give me the rudder now.”\n\n“Oh no,” said she, laughing. “To you, the chariot; to me, the boat. We are merely at the lake’s end, and the lesson is that I must not sing any more. Having been to Egypt, let us now to the Grove of Daphne.”\n\n“Without a song on the way?” he said, in deprecation.\n\n“Tell me something of the Roman from whom you saved us to-day,” she asked.\n\nThe request struck Ben-Hur unpleasantly.\n\n“I wish this were the Nile,” he said, evasively. “The kings and queens, having slept so long, might come down from their tombs, and ride with us.”\n\n“They were of the colossi, and would sink our boat. The pygmies would be preferable. But tell me of the Roman. He is very wicked, is he not?”\n\n“I cannot say.”\n\n“Is he of noble family, and rich?”\n\n“I cannot speak of his riches.”\n\n“How beautiful his horses were! and the bed of his chariot was gold, and the wheels ivory. And his audacity! The bystanders laughed as he rode away; they, who were so nearly under his wheels!”\n\nShe laughed at the recollection.\n\n“They were rabble,” said Ben-Hur, bitterly.\n\n“He must be one of the monsters who are said to be growing up in Rome—Apollos ravenous as Cerberus. Does he reside in Antioch?”\n\n“He is of the East somewhere.”\n\n“Egypt would suit him better than Syria.”\n\n“Hardly,” Ben-Hur replied. “Cleopatra is dead.”\n\nThat instant the lamps burning before the door of the tent came into view.\n\n“The dowar!” she cried.\n\n“Ah, then, we have not been to Egypt. I have not seen Karnak or Philae or Abydos. This is not the Nile. I have but heard a song of India, and been boating in a dream.”\n\n“Philae—Karnak. Mourn rather that you have not seen the Rameses at Aboo Simbel, looking at which makes it so easy to think of God, the maker of the heavens and earth. Or why should you mourn at all? Let us go on to the river; and if I cannot sing”—she laughed—“because I have said I would not, yet I can tell you stories of Egypt.”\n\n“Go on! Ay, till morning comes, and the evening, and the next morning!” he said, vehemently.\n\n“Of what shall my stories be? Of the mathematicians?”\n“Oh no.”\n\n“Of the philosophers?”\n\n“No, no.”\n\n“Of the magicians and genii?”\n\n“If you will.”\n\n“Of war?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Of love?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I will tell you a cure for love. It is the story of a queen. Listen reverently. The papyrus from which it was taken by the priests of Philae was wrested from the hand of the heroine herself. It is correct in form, and must be true:\n\n“There is no parallelism in human lives.\n\n“No life runs a straight line.\n\n“The most perfect life develops as a circle, and terminates in its beginning, making it impossible to say, This is the commencement, that the end.\n\n“Perfect lives are the treasures of God; of great days he wears them on the ring-finger of his heart hand.”\n\n# II.\n\n“Ne-ne-hofra dwelt in a house close by Essouan, yet closer to the first cataract—so close, indeed, that the sound of the eternal battle waged there between river and rocks was of the place a part.\n\n“She grew in beauty day by day, so that it was said of her, as of the poppies in her father’s garden, What will she not be in the time of blooming?\n\n“Each year of her life was the beginning of a new song more delightful than any of those which went before.\n\n“Child was she of a marriage between the North, bounded by the sea, and the South, bounded by the desert beyond the Luna mountains; and one gave her its passion, the other its genius; so when they beheld her, both laughed, saying, not meanly, ‘She is mine,’ but generously, ‘Ha, ha! she is ours.’\n\n“All excellences in nature contributed to her perfection and rejoiced in her presence. Did she come or go, the birds ruffled their wings in greeting; the unruly winds sank to cooling zephyrs; the white lotus rose from the water’s depth to look at her; the solemn river loitered on its way; the palm-trees, nodding, shook all their plumes; and they seemed to say, this one, I gave her of my grace; that, I gave her of my brightness; the other, I gave her of my purity: and so each as it had a virtue to give.\n\n“At twelve, Ne-ne-hofra was the delight of Essouan; at sixteen, the fame of her beauty was universal; at twenty, there was never a day which did not bring to her door princes of the desert on swift camels, and lords of Egypt in gilded barges; and, going away disconsolate, they reported everywhere, ‘I have seen her, and she is not a woman, but Athor herself.’”\n\n# III.\n\n“Now of the three hundred and thirty successors of good King Menes, eighteen were Ethiopians, of whom Oraetes was one hundred and ten years old. He had reigned seventy-six years. Under him the people thrived, and the land groaned with fatness of plenty. He practised wisdom because, having seen so much, he knew what it was. He dwelt in Memphis, having there his principal palace, his arsenals, and his treasure-house. Frequently he went down to Butos to talk with Latona.\n\n“The wife of the good king died. Too old was she for perfect embalmment; yet he loved her, and mourned as the inconsolable; seeing which, a colchyte presumed one day to speak to him.\n\n“‘O Oraetes, I am astonished that one so wise and great should not know how to cure a sorrow like this.’\n\n“‘Tell me a cure,’ said the king.\n\n“Three times the colchyte kissed the floor, and then he replied, knowing the dead could not hear him, ‘At Essouan lives Ne-ne-hofra, beautiful as Athor the beautiful. Send for her. She has refused all the lords and princes, and I know not how many kings; but who can say no to Oraetes?’”\n\n# IV.\n\n“Ne-ne-hofra descended the Nile in a barge richer than any ever before seen, attended by an army in barges each but a little less fine. All Nubia and Egypt, and a myriad from Libya, and a host of Troglodytes, and not a few Macrobii from beyond the Mountains of the Moon, lined the tented shores to see the cortege pass, wafted by perfumed winds and golden oars.\n\n“Through a dromos of sphinxes and couchant double-winged lions she was borne, and set down before Oraetes sitting on a throne specially erected at the sculptured pylon of the palace. He raised her up, gave her place by his side, clasped the uraeus upon her arm, kissed her, and Ne-ne-hofra was queen of all queens.\n\n“That was not enough for the wise Oraetes; he wanted love, and a queen happy in his love. So he dealt with her tenderly, showing her his possessions, cities, palaces, people; his armies, his ships: and with his own hand he led her through his treasure-house, saying, ‘O. Ne-ne-hofra! but kiss me in love, and they are all thine.’\n\n“And, thinking she could be happy, if she was not then, she kissed him once, twice, thrice—kissed him thrice, his hundred and ten years notwithstanding.\n\n“The first year she was happy, and it was very short; the third year she was wretched, and it was very long; then she was enlightened: that which she thought love of Oraetes was only daze of his power. Well for her had the daze endured! Her spirits deserted her; she had long spells of tears, and her women could not remember when they heard her laugh; of the roses on her cheeks only ashes remained; she languished and faded gradually, but certainly. Some said she was haunted by the Erinnyes for cruelty to a lover; others, that she was stricken by some god envious of Oraetes. Whatever the cause of her decline, the charms of the magicians availed not to restore her, and the prescript of the doctor was equally without virtue. Ne-ne-hofra was given over to die.\n\n“Oraetes chose a crypt for her up in the tombs of the queens; and, calling the master sculptors and painters to Memphis, he set them to work upon designs more elaborate than any even in the great galleries of the dead kings.\n\n“‘O thou beautiful as Athor herself, my queen!’ said the king, whose hundred and thirteen years did not lessen his ardor as a lover, ‘Tell me, I pray, the ailment of which, alas! thou art so certainly perishing before my eyes.’\n\n“‘You will not love me any more if I tell you,’ she said, in doubt and fear.\n\n“‘Not love you! I will love you the more. I swear it, by the genii of Amente! by the eye of Osiris, I swear it! Speak!’ he cried, passionate as a lover, authoritative as a king.\n\n“‘Hear, then,’ she said. ‘There is an anchorite, the oldest and holiest of his class, in a cave near Essouan. His name is Menopha. He was my teacher and guardian. Send for him, O Oraetes, and he will tell you that you seek to know; he will also help you find the cure for my affliction.’\n\n“Oraetes arose rejoicing. He went away in spirit a hundred years younger than when he came.”\n\n# V.\n\n“‘Speak!’ said Oraetes to Menopha, in the palace at Memphis.\n\n“And Menopha replied, ‘Most mighty king, if you were young, I should not answer, because I am yet pleased with life; as it is, I will say the queen, like any other mortal, is paying the penalty of a crime.’\n\n“‘A crime!’ exclaimed Oraetes, angrily.\n\n“Menopha bowed very low.\n\n“‘Yes; to herself.’\n\n“‘I am not in mood for riddles,’ said the king.\n\n“‘What I say is not a riddle, as you shall hear. Ne-ne-hofra grew up under my eyes, and confided every incident of her life to me; among others, that she loved the son of her father’s gardener, Barbec by name.’\n\n“Oraetes’s frown, strangely enough, began to dissipate.\n\n“‘With that love in her heart, O king, she came to you; of that love she is dying.’\n\n“‘Where is the gardener’s son now?’ asked Oraetes.\n\n“‘In Essouan.’\n\n“The king went out and gave two orders. To one oeris he said, ‘Go to Essouan and bring hither a youth named Barbec. You will find him in the garden of the queen’s father;’ to another, ‘Assemble workmen and cattle and tools, and construct for me in Lake Chemmis an island, which, though laden with a temple, a palace, and a garden, and all manner of trees bearing fruit, and all manner of vines, shall nevertheless float about as the winds may blow it. Make the island, and let it be fully furnished by the time the moon begins to wane.’\n\n“Then to the queen he said,\n\n“‘Be of cheer. I know all, and have sent for Barbec.’\n\n“Ne-ne-hofra kissed his hands.\n\n“‘You shall have him to yourself, and he you to himself; nor shall any disturb your loves for a year.’\n\n“She kissed his feet; he raised her, and kissed her in return; and the rose came back to her cheek, the scarlet to her lips, and the laughter to her heart.”\n\n# VI.\n\n“For one year Ne-ne-hofra and Barbec the gardener floated as the winds blew on the island of Chemmis, which became one of the wonders of the world; never a home of love more beautiful; one year, seeing no one and existing for no one but themselves. Then she returned in state to the palace in Memphis.\n\n“‘Now whom lovest thou best?’ asked the king.\n\n“She kissed his cheek and said, ‘Take me back, O good king, for I am cured.’\n\n“Oraetes laughed, none the worse, that moment, of his hundred and fourteen years.\n\n“‘Then it is true, as Menopha said: ha, ha, ha! it is true, the cure of love is love.’\n\n“‘Even so,’ she replied.\n\n“Suddenly his manner changed, and his look became terrible.\n\n“‘I did not find it so,’ he said.\n\n“She shrank affrighted.\n\n“‘Thou guilty!’ he continued. ‘Thy offense to Oraetes the man he forgives; but thy offence to Oraetes the king remains to be punished.’\n\n“She cast herself at his feet.\n\n“‘Hush!’ he cried. ‘Thou art dead!’\n\n“He clapped his hands, and a terrible procession came in—a procession of parachistes, or embalmers, each with some implement or material of his loathsome art.\n\n“The King pointed to Ne-ne-hofra.\n\n“‘She is dead. Do thy work well.’”\n\n# VII.\n\n“Ne-ne-hofra the beautiful, after seventy-two days, was carried to the crypt chosen for her the year before, and laid with her queenly predecessors; yet there was no funeral procession in her honor across the sacred lake.”\n\nAt the conclusion of the story, Ben-Hur was sitting at the Egyptian’s feet, and her hand upon the tiller was covered by his hand.\n\n“Menopha was wrong,” he said.\n\n“How?”\n“Love lives by loving.”\n\n“Then there is no cure for it?”\n\n“Yes. Oraetes found the cure.”\n\n“What was it?”\n\n“Death.”\n\n“You are a good listener, O son of Arrius.”\n\nAnd so with conversation and stories, they whiled the hours away. As they stepped ashore, she said,\n\n“To-morrow we go to the city.”\n\n“But you will be at the games?” he asked.\n\n“Oh yes.”\n\n“I will send you my colors.”\n\nWith that they separated.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nIlderim returned to the dowar next day about the third hour. As he dismounted, a man whom he recognized as of his own tribe came to him and said, “O sheik, I was bidden give thee this package, with request that thou read it at once. If there be answer, I was to wait thy pleasure.”\n\nIlderim gave the package immediate attention. The seal was already broken. The address ran, To Valerius Gratus at Cæsarea.\n\n“Abaddon take him!” growled the sheik, at discovering a letter in Latin.\n\nHad the missive been in Greek or Arabic, he could have read it; as it was, the utmost he could make out was the signature in bold Roman letters—MESSALA—whereat his eyes twinkled.\n\n“Where is the young Jew?” he asked.\n\n“In the field with the horses,” a servant replied.\n\nThe sheik replaced the papyrus in its envelopes, and, tucking the package under his girdle, remounted the horse. That moment a stranger made his appearance, coming, apparently, from the city.\n\n“I am looking for Sheik Ilderim, surnamed the Generous,” the stranger said.\n\nHis language and attire bespoke him a Roman.\n\nWhat he could not read, he yet could speak; so the old Arab answered, with dignity, “I am Sheik Ilderim.”\n\nThe man’s eyes fell; he raised them again, and said, with forced composure, “I heard you had need of a driver for the games.”\n\nIlderim’s lip under the white mustache curled contemptuously.\n\n“Go thy way,” he said. “I have a driver.”\n\nHe turned to ride away, but the man, lingering, spoke again.\n\n“Sheik, I am a lover of horses, and they say you have the most beautiful in the world.”\n\nThe old man was touched; he drew rein, as if on the point of yielding to the flattery, but finally replied, “Not to-day, not to-day; some other time I will show them to you. I am too busy just now.”\n\nHe rode to the field, while the stranger betook himself to town again with a smiling countenance. He had accomplished his mission.\n\nAnd every day thereafter, down to the great day of the games, a man—sometimes two or three men—came to the sheik at the Orchard, pretending to seek an engagement as driver.\n\nIn such manner Messala kept watch over Ben-Hur.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThe sheik waited, well satisfied, until Ben-Hur drew his horses off the field for the forenoon—well satisfied, for he had seen them, after being put through all the other paces, run full speed in such manner that it did not seem there were one the slowest and another the fastest—run in other words, as if the four were one.\n\n“This afternoon, O sheik, I will give Sirius back to you.” Ben-Hur patted the neck of the old horse as he spoke. “I will give him back, and take to the chariot.”\n\n“So soon?” Ilderim asked.\n\n“With such as these, good sheik, one day suffices. They are not afraid; they have a man’s intelligence, and they love the exercise. This one,” he shook a rein over the back of the youngest of the four—“you called him Aldebaran, I believe—is the swiftest; in once round a stadium he would lead the others thrice his length.”\n\nIlderim pulled his beard, and said, with twinkling eyes, “Aldebaran is the swiftest; but what of the slowest?”\n\n“This is he.” Ben-Hur shook the rein over Antares. “This is he: but he will win, for, look you, sheik, he will run his utmost all day—all day; and, as the sun goes down, he will reach his swiftest.”\n\n“Right again,” said Ilderim.\n\n“I have but one fear, O sheik.”\n\nThe sheik became doubly serious.\n\n“In his greed of triumph, a Roman cannot keep honor pure. In the games—all of them, mark you—their tricks are infinite; in chariot racing their knavery extends to everything—from horse to driver, from driver to master. Wherefore, good sheik, look well to all thou hast; from this till the trial is over, let no stranger so much as see the horses. Would you be perfectly safe, do more—keep watch over them with armed hand as well as sleepless eye; then I will have no fear of the end.”\n\nAt the door of the tent they dismounted.\n\n“What you say shall be attended to. By the splendor of God, no hand shall come near them except it belong to one of the faithful. To-night I will set watches. But, son of Arrius”—Ilderim drew forth the package, and opened it slowly, while they walked to the divan and seated themselves—“son of Arrius, see thou here, and help me with thy Latin.”\n\nHe passed the despatch to Ben-Hur.\n\n“There; read—and read aloud, rendering what thou findest into the tongue of thy fathers. Latin is an abomination.”\n\nBen-Hur was in good spirits, and began the reading carelessly. “‘MESSALA TO GRATUS!’” He paused. A premonition drove the blood to his heart. Ilderim observed his agitation.\n\n“Well; I am waiting.”\n\nBen-Hur prayed pardon, and recommenced the paper, which, it is sufficient to say, was one of the duplicates of the letter despatched so carefully to Gratus by Messala the morning after the revel in the palace.\n\nThe paragraphs in the beginning were remarkable only as proof that the writer had not outgrown his habit of mockery; when they were passed, and the reader came to the parts intended to refresh the memory of Gratus, his voice trembled, and twice he stopped to regain his self-control. By a strong effort he continued. “‘I recall further,’” he read, “‘that thou didst make disposition of the family of Hur’”—there the reader again paused and drew a long breath—“‘both of us at the time supposing the plan hit upon to be the most effective possible for the purposes in view, which were silence and delivery over to inevitable but natural death.’”\n\nHere Ben-Hur broke down utterly. The paper fell from his hands, and he covered his face.\n\n“They are dead—dead. I alone am left.”\n\nThe sheik had been a silent, but not unsympathetic, witness of the young man’s suffering; now he arose and said, “Son of Arrius, it is for me to beg thy pardon. Read the paper by thyself. When thou art strong enough to give the rest of it to me, send word, and I will return.”\n\nHe went out of the tent, and nothing in all his life became him better.\n\nBen-Hur flung himself on the divan and gave way to his feelings. When somewhat recovered, he recollected that a portion of the letter remained unread, and, taking it up, he resumed the reading. “Thou wilt remember,” the missive ran, “what thou didst with the mother and sister of the malefactor; yet, if now I yield to a desire to learn if they be living or dead”—Ben-Hur started, and read again, and then again, and at last broke into exclamation. “He does not know they are dead; he does not know it! Blessed be the name of the Lord! there is yet hope.” He finished the sentence, and was strengthened by it, and went on bravely to the end of the letter.\n\n“They are not dead,” he said, after reflection; “they are not dead, or he would have heard of it.”\n\nA second reading, more careful than the first, confirmed him in the opinion. Then he sent for the sheik.\n\n“In coming to your hospitable tent, O sheik,” he said, calmly, when the Arab was seated and they were alone, “it was not in my mind to speak of myself further than to assure you I had sufficient training to be intrusted with your horses. I declined to tell you my history. But the chances which have sent this paper to my hand and given it to me to be read are so strange that I feel bidden to trust you with everything. And I am the more inclined to do so by knowledge here conveyed that we are both of us threatened by the same enemy, against whom it is needful that we make common cause. I will read the letter and give you explanation; after which you will not wonder I was so moved. If you thought me weak or childish, you will then excuse me.”\n\nThe sheik held his peace, listening closely, until Ben-Hur came to the paragraph in which he was particularly mentioned: “‘I saw the Jew yesterday in the Grove of Daphne;’” so ran the part, “‘and if he be not there now, he is certainly in the neighborhood, making it easy for me to keep him in eye. Indeed, wert thou to ask me where he is now, I should say, with the most positive assurance, he is to be found at the old Orchard of Palms.’”\n\n“A—h!” exclaimed Ilderim, in such a tone one might hardly say he was more surprised than angry; at the same time, he clutched his beard.\n\n“‘At the old Orchard of Palms,’” Ben-Hur repeated, “‘under the tent of the traitor Shiek Ilderim.’”\n\n“Traitor!—I?” the old man cried, in his shrillest tone, while lip and beard curled with ire, and on his forehead and neck the veins swelled and beat as they would burst.\n\n“Yet a moment, sheik,” said Ben-Hur, with a deprecatory gesture. “Such is Messala’s opinion of you. Hear his threat.” And he read on—“‘under the tent of the traitor Sheik Ilderim, who cannot long escape our strong hand. Be not surprised if Maxentius, as his first measure, places the Arab on ship for forwarding to Rome.’”\n\n“To Rome! Me—Ilderim—sheik of ten thousand horsemen with spears—me to Rome!”\n\nHe leaped rather than rose to his feet, his arms outstretched, his fingers spread and curved like claws, his eyes glittering like a serpent’s.\n\n“O God!—nay, by all the gods except of Rome!—when shall this insolence end? A freeman am I; free are my people. Must we die slaves? Or, worse, must I live a dog, crawling to a master’s feet? Must I lick his hand, lest he lash me? What is mine is not mine; I am not my own; for breath of body I must be beholden to a Roman. Oh, if I were young again! Oh, could I shake off twenty years—or ten—or five!”\n\nHe ground his teeth and shook his hands overhead; then, under the impulse of another idea, he walked away and back again to Ben-Hur swiftly, and caught his shoulder with a strong grasp.\n\n“If I were as thou, son of Arrius—as young, as strong, as practised in arms; if I had a motive hissing me to revenge—a motive, like thine, great enough to make hate holy— Away with disguise on thy part and on mine! Son of Hur, son of Hur, I say—”\n\nAt that name all the currents of Ben-Hur’s blood stopped; surprised, bewildered, he gazed into the Arab’s eyes, now close to his, and fiercely bright.\n\n“Son of Hur, I say, were I as thou, with half thy wrongs, bearing about with me memories like thine, I would not, I could not, rest.” Never pausing, his words following each other torrent-like, the old man swept on. “To all my grievances, I would add those of the world, and devote myself to vengeance. From land to land I would go firing all mankind. No war for freedom but should find me engaged; no battle against Rome in which I would not bear a part. I would turn Parthian, if I could not better. If men failed me, still I would not give over the effort—ha, ha, ha! By the splendor of God! I would herd with wolves, and make friends of lions and tigers, in hope of marshalling them against the common enemy. I would use every weapon. So my victims were Romans, I would rejoice in slaughter. Quarter I would not ask; quarter I would not give. To the flames everything Roman; to the sword every Roman born. Of nights I would pray the gods, the good and the bad alike, to lend me their special terrors—tempests, drought, heat, cold, and all the nameless poisons they let loose in air, all the thousand things of which men die on sea and on land. Oh, I could not sleep. I—I—”\n\nThe sheik stopped for want of breath, panting, wringing his hands. And, sooth to say, of all the passionate burst Ben-Hur retained but a vague impression wrought by fiery eyes, a piercing voice, and a rage too intense for coherent expression.\n\nFor the first time in years, the desolate youth heard himself addressed by his proper name. One man at least knew him, and acknowledged it without demand of identity; and he an Arab fresh from the desert!\n\nHow came the man by his knowledge? The letter? No. It told the cruelties from which his family had suffered; it told the story of his own misfortunes, but it did not say he was the very victim whose escape from doom was the theme of the heartless narrative. That was the point of explanation he had notified the sheik would follow the reading of the letter. He was pleased, and thrilled with hope restored, yet kept an air of calmness.\n\n“Good sheik, tell me how you came by this letter.”\n\n“My people keep the roads between cities,” Ilderim answered, bluntly. “They took it from a courier.”\n\n“Are they known to be thy people?”\n\n“No. To the world they are robbers, whom it is mine to catch and slay.”\n\n“Again, sheik. You call me son of Hur—my father’s name. I did not think myself known to a person on earth. How came you by the knowledge?”\n\nIlderim hesitated; but, rallying, he answered, “I know you, yet I am not free to tell you more.”\n\n“Some one holds you in restraint?”\n\nThe sheik closed his mouth, and walked away; but, observing Ben-Hur’s disappointment, he came back, and said, “Let us say no more about the matter now. I will go to town; when I return, I may talk to you fully. Give me the letter.”\n\nIlderim rolled the papyrus carefully, restored it to its envelopes, and became once more all energy.\n\n“What sayest thou?” he asked, while waiting for his horse and retinue. “I told what I would do, were I thou, and thou hast made no answer.”\n\n“I intended to answer, sheik, and I will.” Ben-Hur’s countenance and voice changed with the feeling invoked. “All thou hast said, I will do—all at least in the power of a man. I devoted myself to vengeance long ago. Every hour of the five years passed, I have lived with no other thought. I have taken no respite. I have had no pleasures of youth. The blandishments of Rome were not for me. I wanted her to educate me for revenge. I resorted to her most famous masters and professors—not those of rhetoric or philosophy: alas! I had no time for them. The arts essential to a fighting-man were my desire. I associated with gladiators, and with winners of prizes in the Circus; and they were my teachers. The drill-masters in the great camp accepted me as a scholar, and were proud of my attainments in their line. O sheik, I am a soldier; but the things of which I dream require me to be a captain. With that thought, I have taken part in the campaign against the Parthians; when it is over, then, if the Lord spare my life and strength—then”—he raised his clenched hands, and spoke vehemently—“then I will be an enemy Roman-taught in all things; then Rome shall account to me in Roman lives for her ills. You have my answer, sheik.”\n\nIlderim put an arm over his shoulder, and kissed him, saying, passionately, “If thy God favor thee not, son of Hur, it is because he is dead. Take thou this from me—sworn to, if so thy preference run: thou shalt have my hands, and their fulness—men, horses, camels, and the desert for preparation. I swear it! For the present, enough. Thou shalt see or hear from me before night.”\n\nTurning abruptly off, the sheik was speedily on the road to the city.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nThe intercepted letter was conclusive upon a number of points of great interest to Ben-Hur. It had all the effect of a confession that the writer was a party to the putting-away of the family with murderous intent; that he had sanctioned the plan adopted for the purpose; that he had received a portion of the proceeds of the confiscation, and was yet in enjoyment of his part; that he dreaded the unexpected appearance of what he was pleased to call the chief malefactor, and accepted it as a menace; that he contemplated such further action as would secure him in the future, and was ready to do whatever his accomplice in Cæsarea might advise.\n\nAnd, now that the letter had reached the hand of him really its subject, it was notice of danger to come, as well as a confession of guilt. So when Ilderim left the tent, Ben-Hur had much to think about, requiring immediate action. His enemies were as adroit and powerful as any in the East. If they were afraid of him, he had greater reason to be afraid of them. He strove earnestly to reflect upon the situation, but could not; his feelings constantly overwhelmed him. There was a certain qualified pleasure in the assurance that his mother and sister were alive; and it mattered little that the foundation of the assurance was a mere inference. That there was one person who could tell him where they were seemed to his hope so long deferred as if discovery were now close at hand. These were mere causes of feeling; underlying them, it must be confessed he had a superstitious fancy that God was about to make ordination in his behalf, in which event faith whispered him to stand still.\n\nOccasionally, referring to the words of Ilderim, he wondered whence the Arab derived his information about him; not from Malluch certainly; nor from Simonides, whose interests, all adverse, would hold him dumb. Could Messala have been the informant? No, no: disclosure might be dangerous in that quarter. Conjecture was vain; at the same time, often as Ben-Hur was beaten back from the solution, he was consoled with the thought that whoever the person with the knowledge might be, he was a friend, and, being such, would reveal himself in good time. A little more waiting—a little more patience. Possibly the errand of the sheik was to see the worthy; possibly the letter might precipitate a full disclosure.\n\nAnd patient he would have been if only he could have believed Tirzah and his mother were waiting for him under circumstances permitting hope on their part strong as his; if, in other words, conscience had not stung him with accusations respecting them.\n\nTo escape such accusations, he wandered far through the Orchard, pausing now where the date-gatherers were busy, yet not too busy to offer him of their fruit and talk with him; then, under the great trees, to watch the nesting birds, or hear the bees swarming about the berries bursting with honeyed sweetness, and filling all the green and golden spaces with the music of their beating wings.\n\nBy the lake, however, he lingered longest. He might not look upon the water and its sparkling ripples, so like sensuous life, without thinking of the Egyptian and her marvellous beauty, and of floating with her here and there through the night, made brilliant by her songs and stories; he might not forget the charm of her manner, the lightness of her laugh, the flattery of her attention, the warmth of her little hand under his upon the tiller of the boat. From her it was for his thought but a short way to Balthasar, and the strange things of which he had been witness, unaccountable by any law of nature; and from him, again, to the King of the Jews, whom the good man, with such pathos of patience, was holding in holy promise, the distance was even nearer. And there his mind stayed, finding in the mysteries of that personage a satisfaction answering well for the rest he was seeking. Because, it may have been, nothing is so easy as denial of an idea not agreeable to our wishes, he rejected the definition given by Balthasar of the kingdom the king was coming to establish. A kingdom of souls, if not intolerable to his Sadducean faith, seemed to him but an abstraction drawn from the depths of a devotion too fond and dreamy. A kingdom of Judea, on the other hand, was more than comprehensible: such had been, and, if only for that reason, might be again. And it suited his pride to think of a new kingdom broader of domain, richer in power, and of a more unapproachable splendor than the old one; of a new king wiser and mightier than Solomon—a new king under whom, especially, he could find both service and revenge. In that mood he resumed to the dowar.\n\nThe mid-day meal disposed of, still further to occupy himself, Ben-Hur had the chariot rolled out into the sunlight for inspection. The word but poorly conveys the careful study the vehicle underwent. No point or part of it escaped him. With a pleasure which will be better understood hereafter, he saw the pattern was Greek, in his judgment preferable to the Roman in many respects; it was wider between the wheels, and lower and stronger, and the disadvantage of greater weight would be more than compensated by the greater endurance of his Arabs. Speaking generally, the carriage-makers of Rome built for the games almost solely, sacrificing safety to beauty, and durability to grace; while the chariots of Achilles and “the king of men,” designed for war and all its extreme tests, still ruled the tastes of those who met and struggled for the crowns Isthmian and Olympic.\n\nNext he brought the horses, and, hitching them to the chariot, drove to the field of exercise, where, hour after hour, he practised them in movement under the yoke. When he came away in the evening, it was with restored spirit, and a fixed purpose to defer action in the matter of Messala until the race was won or lost. He could not forego the pleasure of meeting his adversary under the eyes of the East; that there might be other competitors seemed not to enter his thought. His confidence in the result was absolute; no doubt of his own skill; and as to the four, they were his full partners in the glorious game.\n\n“Let him look to it, let him look to it! Ha, Antares—Aldebaran! Shall he not, O honest Rigel? and thou, Atair, king among coursers, shall he not beware of us? Ha, ha! good hearts!”\n\nSo in rests he passed from horse to horse, speaking, not as a master, but the senior of as many brethren.\n\nAfter nightfall, Ben-Hur sat by the door of the tent waiting for Ilderim, not yet returned from the city. He was not impatient, or vexed, or doubtful. The sheik would be heard from, at least. Indeed, whether it was from satisfaction with the performance of the four, or the refreshment there is in cold water succeeding bodily exercise, or supper partaken with royal appetite, or the reaction which, as a kindly provision of nature, always follows depression, the young man was in good-humor verging upon elation. He felt himself in the hands of Providence no longer his enemy. At last there was a sound of horse’s feet coming rapidly, and Malluch rode up.\n\n“Son of Arrius,” he said, cheerily, after salutation, “I salute you for Sheik Ilderim, who requests you to mount and go to the city. He is waiting for you.”\n\nBen-Hur asked no questions, but went in where the horses were feeding. Aldebaran came to him, as if offering his service. He played with him lovingly, but passed on, and chose another, not of the four—they were sacred to the race. Very shortly the two were on the road, going swiftly and in silence.\n\nSome distance below the Seleucian Bridge, they crossed the river by a ferry, and, riding far round on the right bank, and recrossing by another ferry, entered the city from the west. The detour was long, but Ben-Hur accepted it as a precaution for which there was good reason.\n\nDown to Simonides’ landing they rode, and in front of the great warehouse, under the bridge, Malluch drew rein.\n\n“We are come,” he said. “Dismount.”\n\nBen-Hur recognized the place.\n\n“Where is the sheik?” he asked.\n\n“Come with me. I will show you.”\n\nA watchman took the horses, and almost before he realized it Ben-Hur stood once more at the door of the house up on the greater one, listening to the response from within—“In God’s name, enter.”\n\nMalluch stopped at the door; Ben-Hur entered alone.\n\nThe room was the same in which he had formerly interviewed Simonides, and it had been in nowise changed, except now, close by the arm-chair, a polished brazen rod, set on a broad wooden pedestal, arose higher than a tall man, holding lamps of silver on sliding arms, half-a-dozen or more in number, and all burning. The light was clear, bringing into view the panelling on the walls, the cornice with its row of gilded balls, and the dome dully tinted with violet mica.\n\nWithin a few steps, Ben-Hur stopped.\n\nThree persons were present, looking at him—Simonides, Ilderim, and Esther.\n\nHe glanced hurriedly from one to another, as if to find answer to the question half formed in his mind, What business can these have with me? He became calm, with every sense on the alert, for the question was succeeded by another, Are they friends or enemies?\n\nAt length, his eyes rested upon Esther.\n\nThe men returned his look kindly; in her face there was something more than kindness—something too spirituel for definition, which yet went to his inner consciousness without definition.\n\nShall it be said, good reader? Back of his gaze there was a comparison in which the Egyptian arose and set herself over against the gentle Jewess; but it lived an instant, and, as is the habit of such comparisons, passed away without a conclusion.\n\n“Son of Hur—”\n\nThe guest turned to the speaker.\n\n“Son of Hur,” said Simonides, repeating the address slowly, and with distinct emphasis, as if to impress all its meaning upon him most interested in understanding it, “take thou the peace of the Lord God of our fathers—take it from me.” He paused, then added, “From me and mine.”\n\nThe speaker sat in his chair; there were the royal head, the bloodless face, the masterful air, under the influence of which visitors forgot the broken limbs and distorted body of the man. The full black eyes gazed out under the white brows steadily, but not sternly. A moment thus, then he crossed his hands upon his breast.\n\nThe action, taken with the salutation, could not be misunderstood, and was not.\n\n“Simonides,” Ben-Hur answered, much moved, “the holy peace you tender is accepted. As son to father, I return it to you. Only let there be perfect understanding between us.”\n\nThus delicately he sought to put aside the submission of the merchant, and, in place of the relation of master and servant, substitute one higher and holier.\n\nSimonides let fall his hands, and, turning to Esther, said, “A seat for the master, daughter.”\n\nShe hastened, and brought a stool, and stood, with suffused face, looking from one to the other—from Ben-Hur to Simonides, from Simonides to Ben-Hur; and they waited, each declining the superiority direction would imply. When at length the pause began to be embarrassing, Ben-Hur advanced, and gently took the stool from her, and, going to the chair, placed it at the merchant’s feet.\n\n“I will sit here,” he said.\n\nHis eyes met hers—an instant only; but both were better of the look. He recognized her gratitude, she his generosity and forbearance.\n\nSimonides bowed his acknowledgment.\n\n“Esther, child, bring me the paper,” he said, with a breath of relief.\n\nShe went to a panel in the wall, opened it, took out a roll of papyri, and brought and gave it to him.\n\n“Thou saidst well, son of Hur,” Simonides began, while unrolling the sheets. “Let us understand each other. In anticipation of the demand—which I would have made hadst thou waived it—I have here a statement covering everything necessary to the understanding required. I could see but two points involved—the property first, and then our relation. The statement is explicit as to both. Will it please thee to read it now?”\n\nBen-Hur received the papers, but glanced at Ilderim.\n\n“Nay,” said Simonides, “the sheik shall not deter thee from reading. The account—such thou wilt find it—is of a nature requiring a witness. In the attesting place at the end thou wilt find, when thou comest to it, the name—Ilderim, Sheik. He knows all. He is thy friend. All he has been to me, that will he be to thee also.”\n\nSimonides looked at the Arab, nodding pleasantly, and the latter gravely returned the nod, saying, “Thou hast said.”\n\nBen-Hur replied, “I know already the excellence of his friendship, and have yet to prove myself worthy of it.” Immediately he continued, “Later, O Simonides, I will read the papers carefully; for the present, do thou take them, and if thou be not too weary, give me their substance.”\n\nSimonides took back the roll.\n\n“Here, Esther, stand by me and receive the sheets, lest they fall into confusion.”\n\nShe took place by his chair, letting her right arm fall lightly across his shoulder, so, when he spoke, the account seemed to have rendition from both of them jointly.\n\n“This,” said Simonides, drawing out the first leaf, “shows the money I had of thy father’s, being the amount saved from the Romans; there was no property saved, only money, and that the robbers would have secured but for our Jewish custom of bills of exchange. The amount saved, being sums I drew from Rome, Alexandria, Damascus, Carthage, Valentia, and elsewhere within the circle of trade, was one hundred and twenty talents Jewish money.”\n\nHe gave the sheet to Esther, and took the next one.\n\n“With that amount—one hundred and twenty talents—I charged myself. Hear now my credits. I use the word, as thou wilt see, with reference rather to the proceeds gained from the use of the money.”\n\nFrom separate sheets he then read footings, which, fractions omitted, were as follows:\n\nBy ships 60 talents ” goods in store 110 ” ” cargoes in transit 75 ” ” camels, horses, etc. 20 ” ” warehouses 10 ” ” bills due\t54 ” ” money on hand and subject to draft\t224 ” —— Total\t553 ”\n\n“To these now, to the five hundred and fifty-three talents gained, add the original capital I had from thy father, and thou hast SIX HUNDRED AND SEVENTY THREE TALENTS!—and all thine—making thee, O son of Hur, the richest subject in the world.”\n\nHe took the papyri from Esther, and, reserving one, rolled them and offered them to Ben-Hur. The pride perceptible in his manner was not offensive; it might have been from a sense of duty well done; it might have been for Ben-Hur without reference to himself.\n\n“And there is nothing,” he added, dropping his voice, but not his eyes—“there is nothing now thou mayst not do.”\n\nThe moment was one of absorbing interest to all present. Simonides crossed his hands upon his breast again; Esther was anxious; Ilderim nervous. A man is never so on trial as in the moment of excessive good-fortune.\n\nTaking the roll, Ben-Hur arose, struggling with emotion.\n\n“All this is to me as a light from heaven, sent to drive away a night which has been so long I feared it would never end, and so dark I had lost the hope of seeing,” he said, with a husky voice. “I give first thanks to the Lord, who has not abandoned me, and my next to thee, O Simonides. Thy faithfulness outweighs the cruelty of others, and redeems our human nature. ‘There is nothing I cannot do:’ be it so. Shall any man in this my hour of such mighty privilege be more generous than I? Serve me as a witness now, Sheik Ilderim. Hear thou my words as I shall speak them—hear and remember. And thou, Esther, good angel of this good man! hear thou also.”\n\nHe stretched his hand with the roll to Simonides.\n\n“The things these papers take into account—all of them: ships, houses, goods, camels, horses, money; the least as well as the greatest—give I back to thee, O Simonides, making them all thine, and sealing them to thee and thine forever.”\n\nEsther smiled through her tears; Ilderim pulled his beard with rapid motion, his eyes glistening like beads of jet. Simonides alone was calm.\n\n“Sealing them to thee and thine forever,” Ben-Hur continued, with better control of himself, “with one exception, and upon one condition.”\n\nThe breath of the listeners waited upon his words.\n\n“The hundred and twenty talents which were my father’s thou shalt return to me.”\n\nIlderim’s countenance brightened.\n\n“And thou shalt join me in search of my mother and sister, holding all thine subject to the expense of discovery, even as I will hold mine.”\n\nSimonides was much affected. Stretching out his hand, he said, “I see thy spirit, son of Hur, and I am grateful to the Lord that he hath sent thee to me such as thou art. If I served well thy father in life, and his memory afterwards, be not afraid of default to thee; yet must I say the exception cannot stand.”\n\nExhibiting, then, the reserved sheet, he continued,\n\n“Thou hast not all the account. Take this and read—read aloud.”\n\nBen-Hur took the supplement, and read it.\n\n“Statement of the servants of Hur, rendered by Simonides, steward of the estate.\n\n1. Amrah, Egyptian, keeping the palace in Jerusalem. 2. Simonides, the steward, in Antioch. 3. Esther, daughter of Simonides.”\n\nNow, in all his thoughts of Simonides, not once had it entered Ben-Hur’s mind that, by the law, a daughter followed the parent’s condition. In all his visions of her, the sweet-faced Esther had figured as the rival of the Egyptian, and an object of possible love. He shrank from the revelation so suddenly brought him, and looked at her blushing; and, blushing, she dropped her eyes before him. Then he said, while the papyrus rolled itself together,\n\n“A man with six hundred talents is indeed rich, and may do what he pleases; but, rarer than the money, more priceless than the property, is the mind which amassed the wealth, and the heart it could not corrupt when amassed. O Simonides—and thou, fair Esther—fear not. Sheik Ilderim here shall be witness that in the same moment ye were declared my servants, that moment I declared ye free; and what I declare, that will I put in writing. Is it not enough? Can I do more?”\n\n“Son of Hur,” said Simonides, “verily thou dost make servitude lightsome. I was wrong; there are some things thou canst not do; thou canst not make us free in law. I am thy servant forever, because I went to the door with thy father one day, and in my ear the awl-marks yet abide.”\n\n“Did my father that?”\n\n“Judge him not,” cried Simonides, quickly. “He accepted me a servant of that class because I prayed him to do so. I never repented the step. It was the price I paid for Rachel, the mother of my child here; for Rachel, who would not be my wife unless I became what she was.”\n\n“Was she a servant forever?”\n\n“Even so.”\n\nBen-Hur walked the floor in pain of impotent wish.\n\n“I was rich before,” he said, stopping suddenly. “I was rich with the gifts of the generous Arrius; now comes this greater fortune, and the mind which achieved it. Is there not a purpose of God in it all? Counsel me, O Simonides! Help me to see the right and do it. Help me to be worthy my name, and what thou art in law to me, that will I be to thee in fact and deed. I will be thy servant forever.”\n\nSimonides’ face actually glowed.\n\n“O son of my dead master! I will do better than help; I will serve thee with all my might of mind and heart. Body, I have not; it perished in thy cause; but with mind and heart I will serve thee. I swear it, by the altar of our God, and the gifts upon the altar! Only make me formally what I have assumed to be.”\n\n“Name it,” said Ben-Hur, eagerly.\n\n“As steward the care of the property will be mine.”\n\n“Count thyself steward now; or wilt thou have it in writing?”\n\n“Thy word simply is enough; it was so with the father, and I will not more from the son. And now, if the understanding be perfect”—Simonides paused.\n\n“It is with me,” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“And thou, daughter of Rachel, speak!” said Simonides, lifting her arm from his shoulder.\n\nEsther, left thus alone, stood a moment abashed, her color coming and going; then she went to Ben-Hur, and said, with a womanliness singularly sweet, “I am not better than my mother was; and, as she is gone, I pray you, O my master, let me care for my father.”\n\nBen-Hur took her hand, and led her back to the chair, saying, “Thou art a good child. Have thy will.”\n\nSimonides replaced her arm upon his neck, and there was silence for a time in the room.\n\nSimonides looked up, none the less a master.\n\n“Esther,” he said, quietly, “the night is going fast; and, lest we become too weary for that which is before us, let the refreshments be brought.”\n\nShe rang a bell. A servant answered with wine and bread, which she bore round.\n\n“The understanding, good my master,” continued Simonides, when all were served, “is not perfect in my sight. Henceforth our lives will run on together like rivers which have met and joined their waters. I think their flowing will be better if every cloud is blown from the sky above them. You left my door the other day with what seemed a denial of the claims which I have just allowed in the broadest terms; but it was not so, indeed it was not. Esther is witness that I recognized you; and that I did not abandon you, let Malluch say.”\n\n“Malluch!” exclaimed Ben-Hur.\n\n“One bound to a chair, like me, must have many hands far-reaching, if he would move the world from which he is so cruelly barred. I have many such, and Malluch is one of the best of them. And, sometimes”—he cast a grateful glance at the sheik—“sometimes I borrow from others good of heart, like Ilderim the Generous—good and brave. Let him say if I either denied or forgot you.”\n\nBen-Hur looked at the Arab.\n\n“This is he, good Ilderim, this is he who told you of me?”\n\nIlderim’s eyes twinkled as he nodded his answer.\n\n“How, O my master,” said Simonides, “may we without trial tell what a man is? I knew you; I saw your father in you; but the kind of man you were I did not know. There are people to whom fortune is a curse in disguise. Were you of them? I sent Malluch to find out for me, and in the service he was my eyes and ears. Do not blame him. He brought me report of you which was all good.”\n\n“I do not,” said Ben-Hur, heartily. “There was wisdom in your goodness.”\n\n“The words are very pleasant to me,” said the merchant, with feeling, “very pleasant. My fear of misunderstanding is laid. Let the rivers run on now as God may give them direction.”\n\nAfter an interval he continued:\n\n“I am compelled now by truth. The weaver sits weaving, and, as the shuttle flies, the cloth increases, and the figures grow, and he dreams dreams meanwhile; so to my hands the fortune grew, and I wondered at the increase, and asked myself about it many times. I could see a care not my own went with the enterprises I set going. The simooms which smote others on the desert jumped over the things which were mine. The storms which heaped the seashore with wrecks did but blow my ships the sooner into port. Strangest of all, I, so dependent upon others, fixed to a place like a dead thing, had never a loss by an agent—never. The elements stooped to serve me, and all my servants, in fact, were faithful.”\n\n“It is very strange,” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“So I said, and kept saying. Finally, O my master, finally I came to be of your opinion—God was in it—and, like you, I asked, What can his purpose be? Intelligence is never wasted; intelligence like God’s never stirs except with design. I have held the question in heart, lo! these many years, watching for an answer. I felt sure, if God were in it, some day, in his own good time, in his own way, he would show me his purpose, making it clear as a whited house upon a hill. And I believe he has done so.”\n\nBen-Hur listened with every faculty intent.\n\n“Many years ago, with my people—thy mother was with me, Esther, beautiful as morning over old Olivet—I sat by the wayside out north of Jerusalem, near the Tombs of the Kings, when three men passed by riding great white camels, such as had never been seen in the Holy City. The men were strangers, and from far countries. The first one stopped and asked me a question. ‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’ As if to allay my wonder, he went on to say, ‘We have seen his star in the east, and have come to worship him.’ I could not understand, but followed them to the Damascus Gate; and of every person they met on the way—of the guard at the Gate, even—they asked the question. All who heard it were amazed like me. In time I forgot the circumstance, though there was much talk of it as a presage of the Messiah. Alas, alas! What children we are, even the wisest! When God walks the earth, his steps are often centuries apart. You have seen Balthasar?”\n\n“And heard him tell his story,” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“A miracle!—a very miracle!” cried Simonides. “As he told it to me, good my master, I seemed to hear the answer I had so long waited; God’s purpose burst upon me. Poor will the King be when he comes—poor and friendless; without following, without armies, without cities or castles; a kingdom to be set up, and Rome reduced and blotted out. See, see, O my master! thou flushed with strength, thou trained to arms, thou burdened with riches; behold the opportunity the Lord hath sent thee! Shall not his purpose be thine? Could a man be born to a more perfect glory?”\n\nSimonides put his whole force in the appeal.\n\n“But the kingdom, the kingdom!” Ben-Hur answered, eagerly. “Balthasar says it is to be of souls.”\n\nThe pride of the Jew was strong in Simonides, and therefore the slightly contemptuous curl of the lip with which he began his reply:\n\n“Balthasar has been a witness of wonderful things—of miracles, O my master; and when he speaks of them, I bow with belief, for they are of sight and sound personal to him. But he is a son of Mizraim, and not even a proselyte. Hardly may he be supposed to have special knowledge by virtue of which we must bow to him in a matter of God’s dealing with our Israel. The prophets had their light from Heaven directly, even as he had his—many to one, and Jehovah the same forever. I must believe the prophets.—Bring me the Torah, Esther.”\n\nHe proceeded without waiting for her.\n\n“May the testimony of a whole people be slighted, my master? Though you travel from Tyre, which is by the sea in the north, to the capital of Edom, which is in the desert south, you will not find a lisper of the Shema, an alms-giver in the Temple, or any one who has ever eaten of the lamb of the Passover, to tell you the kingdom the King is coming to build for us, the children of the covenant, is other than of this world, like our father David’s. Now where got they the faith, ask you! We will see presently.”\n\nEsther here returned, bringing a number of rolls carefully enveloped in dark-brown linen lettered quaintly in gold.\n\n“Keep them, daughter, to give to me as I call for them,” the father said, in the tender voice he always used in speaking to her, and continued his argument:\n\n“It were long, good my master—too long, indeed—for me to repeat to you the names of the holy men who, in the providence of God, succeeded the prophets, only a little less favored than they—the seers who have written and the preachers who have taught since the Captivity; the very wise who borrowed their lights from the lamp of Malachi, the last of his line, and whose great names Hillel and Shammai never tired of repeating in the colleges. Will you ask them of the kingdom? Thus, the Lord of the sheep in the Book of Enoch—who is he? Who but the King of whom we are speaking? A throne is set up for him; he smites the earth, and the other kings are shaken from their thrones, and the scourges of Israel flung into a cavern of fire flaming with pillars of fire. So also the singer of the Psalms of Solomon—‘Behold, O Lord, and raise up to Israel their king, the son of David, at the time thou knowest, O God, to rule Israel, thy children.... And he will bring the peoples of the heathen under his yoke to serve him.... And he shall be a righteous king taught of God, ... for he shall rule all the earth by the word of his mouth forever.’ And last, though not least, hear Ezra, the second Moses, in his visions of the night, and ask him who is the lion with human voice that says to the eagle—which is Rome—‘Thou hast loved liars, and overthrown the cities of the industrious, and razed their walls, though they did thee no harm. Therefore, begone, that the earth may be refreshed, and recover itself, and hope in the justice and piety of him who made her.’ Whereat the eagle was seen no more. Surely, O my master, the testimony of these should be enough! But the way to the fountain’s head is open. Let us go up to it at once.—Some wine, Esther, and then the Torah.”\n\n“Dost thou believe the prophets, master?” he asked, after drinking. “I know thou dost, for of such was the faith of all thy kindred.—Give me, Esther, the book which hath in it the visions of Isaiah.”\n\nHe took one of the rolls which she had unwrapped for him, and read, “‘The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined.... For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder.... Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it, and to establish it with judgment and with justice from henceforth even forever.’—Believest thou the prophets, O my master?—Now, Esther, the word of the Lord that came to Micah.”\n\nShe gave him the roll he asked.\n\n“‘But thou,’” he began reading—“‘but thou, Bethlehem Ephrath, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel.’—This was he, the very child Balthasar saw and worshipped in the cave. Believest thou the prophets, O my master?—Give me, Esther, the words of Jeremiah.”\n\nReceiving that roll, he read as before, “‘Behold, the days come, saith the Lord, that I will raise unto David a righteous branch, and a king shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth. In his days Judah shall be saved, and Israel shall dwell safely.’ As a king he shall reign—as a king, O my master! Believest thou the prophets?—Now, daughter, the roll of the sayings of that son of Judah in whom there was no blemish.”\n\nShe gave him the Book of Daniel.\n\n“Hear, my master,” he said: “‘I saw in the night visions, and behold, one like the Son of man came with the clouds of heaven.... And there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom, that all people, nations, and languages should serve him; his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away, and his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed.’—Believest thou the prophets, O my master?”\n\n“It is enough. I believe,” cried Ben-Hur.\n\n“What then?” asked Simonides. “If the King come poor, will not my master, of his abundance, give him help?”\n\n“Help him? To the last shekel and the last breath. But why speak of his coming poor?”\n\n“Give me, Esther, the word of the Lord as it came to Zechariah,” said Simonides.\n\nShe gave him one of the rolls.\n\n“Hear how the King will enter Jerusalem.” Then he read, “‘Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion.... Behold, thy King cometh unto thee with justice and salvation; lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt, the foal of an ass.’”\n\nBen-Hur looked away.\n\n“What see you, O my master?”\n\n“Rome!” he answered, gloomily—“Rome, and her legions. I have dwelt with them in their camps. I know them.”\n\n“Ah!” said Simonides. “Thou shalt be a master of legions for the King, with millions to choose from.”\n\n“Millions!” cried Ben-Hur.\n\nSimonides sat a moment thinking.\n\n“The question of power should not trouble you,” he next said.\n\nBen-Hur looked at him inquiringly.\n\n“You were seeing the lowly King in the act of coming to his own,” Simonides answered—“seeing him on the right hand, as it were, and on the left the brassy legions of Cæsar, and you were asking, What can he do?”\n\n“It was my very thought.”\n\n“O my master!” Simonides continued. “You do not know how strong our Israel is. You think of him as a sorrowful old man weeping by the rivers of Babylon. But go up to Jerusalem next Passover, and stand on the Xystus or in the Street of Barter, and see him as he is. The promise of the Lord to father Jacob coming out of Padan-Aram was a law under which our people have not ceased multiplying—not even in captivity; they grew under foot of the Egyptian; the clench of the Roman has been but wholesome nurture to them; now they are indeed ‘a nation and a company of nations.’ Nor that only, my master; in fact, to measure the strength of Israel—which is, in fact, measuring what the King can do—you shall not bide solely by the rule of natural increase, but add thereto the other—I mean the spread of the faith, which will carry you to the far and near of the whole known earth. Further, the habit is, I know, to think and speak of Jerusalem as Israel, which may be likened to our finding an embroidered shred, and holding it up as a magisterial robe of Cæsar’s. Jerusalem is but a stone of the Temple, or the heart in the body. Turn from beholding the legions, strong though they be, and count the hosts of the faithful waiting the old alarm, ‘To your tents, O Israel!’—count the many in Persia, children of those who chose not to return with the returning; count the brethren who swarm the marts of Egypt and Farther Africa; count the Hebrew colonists eking profit in the West—in Lodinum and the trade-courts of Spain; count the pure of blood and the proselytes in Greece and in the isles of the sea, and over in Pontus, and here in Antioch, and, for that matter, those of that city lying accursed in the shadow of the unclean walls of Rome herself; count the worshippers of the Lord dwelling in tents along the deserts next us, as well as in the deserts beyond the Nile: and in the regions across the Caspian, and up in the old lands of Gog and Magog even, separate those who annually send gifts to the Holy Temple in acknowledgment of God—separate them, that they may be counted also. And when you have done counting, lo! my master, a census of the sword hands that await you; lo! a kingdom ready fashioned for him who is to do ‘judgment and justice in the whole earth’—in Rome not less than in Zion. Have then the answer, What Israel can do, that can the King.”\n\nThe picture was fervently given.\n\nUpon Ilderim it operated like the blowing of a trumpet. “Oh that I had back my youth!” he cried, starting to his feet.\n\nBen-Hur sat still. The speech, he saw, was an invitation to devote his life and fortune to the mysterious Being who was palpably as much the centre of a great hope with Simonides as with the devout Egyptian. The idea, as we have seen, was not a new one, but had come to him repeatedly; once while listening to Malluch in the Grove of Daphne; afterwards more distinctly while Balthasar was giving his conception of what the kingdom was to be; still later, in the walk through the old Orchard, it had risen almost, if not quite, into a resolve. At such times it had come and gone only an idea, attended with feelings more or less acute. Not so now. A master had it in charge, a master was working it up; already he had exalted it into a cause brilliant with possibilities and infinitely holy. The effect was as if a door theretofore unseen had suddenly opened flooding Ben-Hur with light, and admitting him to a service which had been his one perfect dream—a service reaching far into the future, and rich with the rewards of duty done, and prizes to sweeten and soothe his ambition. One touch more was needed.\n\n“Let us concede all you say, O Simonides,” said Ben-Hur—“that the King will come, and his kingdom be as Solomon’s; say also I am ready to give myself and all I have to him and his cause; yet more, say that I should do as was God’s purpose in the ordering of my life and in your quick amassment of astonishing fortune; then what? Shall we proceed like blind men building? Shall we wait till the King comes? Or until he sends for me? You have age and experience on your side. Answer.”\n\nSimonides answered at once.\n\n“We have no choice; none. This letter”—he produced Messala’s despatch as he spoke—“this letter is the signal for action. The alliance proposed between Messala and Gratus we are not strong enough to resist; we have not the influence at Rome nor the force here. They will kill you if we wait. How merciful they are, look at me and judge.”\n\nHe shuddered at the terrible recollection.\n\n“O good my master,” he continued, recovering himself; “how strong are you—in purpose, I mean?”\n\nBen-Hur did not understand him.\n\n“I remember how pleasant the world was to me in my youth,” Simonides proceeded.\n\n“Yet,” said Ben-Hur, “you were capable of a great sacrifice.”\n\n“Yes; for love.”\n\n“Has not life other motives as strong?”\n\nSimonides shook his head.\n\n“There is ambition.”\n\n“Ambition is forbidden a son of Israel.”\n\n“What, then, of revenge?”\n\nThe spark dropped upon the inflammable passion; the man’s eyes gleamed; his hands shook; he answered, quickly, “Revenge is a Jew’s of right; it is the law.”\n\n“A camel, even a dog, will remember a wrong,” cried Ilderim.\n\nDirectly Simonides picked up the broken thread of his thought.\n\n“There is a work, a work for the King, which should be done in advance of his coming. We may not doubt that Israel is to be his right hand; but, alas! it is a hand of peace, without cunning in war. Of the millions, there is not one trained band, not a captain. The mercenaries of the Herods I do not count, for they are kept to crush us. The condition is as the Roman would have it; his policy has fruited well for his tyranny; but the time of change is at hand, when the shepherd shall put on armor, and take to spear and sword, and the feeding flocks be turned to fighting lions. Some one, my son, must have place next the King at his right hand. Who shall it be if not he who does this work well?”\n\nBen-Hur’s face flushed at the prospect, though he said, “I see; but speak plainly. A deed to be done is one thing; how to do it is another.”\n\nSimonides sipped the wine Esther brought him, and replied,\n\n“The sheik, and thou, my master, shall be principals, each with a part. I will remain here, carrying on as now, and watchful that the spring go not dry. Thou shalt betake thee to Jerusalem, and thence to the wilderness, and begin numbering the fighting-men of Israel, and telling them into tens and hundreds, and choosing captains and training them, and in secret places hoarding arms, for which I shall keep thee supplied. Commencing over in Perea, thou shalt go then to Galilee, whence it is but a step to Jerusalem. In Perea, the desert will be at thy back, and Ilderim in reach of thy hand. He will keep the roads, so that nothing shall pass without thy knowledge. He will help thee in many ways. Until the ripening time no one shall know what is here contracted. Mine is but a servant’s part. I have spoken to Ilderim. What sayest thou?”\n\nBen-Hur looked at the sheik.\n\n“It is as he says, son of Hur,” the Arab responded. “I have given my word, and he is content with it; but thou shalt have my oath, binding me, and the ready hands of my tribe, and whatever serviceable thing I have.”\n\nThe three—Simonides, Ilderim, Esther—gazed at Ben-Hur fixedly.\n\n“Every man,” he answered, at first sadly, “has a cup of pleasure poured for him, and soon or late it comes to his hand, and he tastes and drinks—every man but me. I see, Simonides, and thou, O generous sheik!—I see whither the proposal tends. If I accept, and enter upon the course, farewell peace, and the hopes which cluster around it. The doors I might enter and the gates of quiet life will shut behind me, never to open again, for Rome keeps them all; and her outlawry will follow me, and her hunters; and in the tombs near cities and the dismal caverns of remotest hills, I must eat my crust and take my rest.”\n\nThe speech was broken by a sob. All turned to Esther, who hid her face upon her father’s shoulder.\n\n“I did not think of you, Esther,” said Simonides, gently, for he was himself deeply moved.\n\n“It is well enough, Simonides,” said Ben-Hur. “A man bears a hard doom better, knowing there is pity for him. Let me go on.”\n\nThey gave him ear again.\n\n“I was about to say,” he continued, “I have no choice, but take the part you assign me; and as remaining here is to meet an ignoble death, I will to the work at once.”\n\n“Shall we have writings?” asked Simonides, moved by his habit of business.\n\n“I rest upon your word,” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“And I,” Ilderim answered.\n\nThus simply was effected the treaty which was to alter Ben-Hur’s life. And almost immediately the latter added,\n\n“It is done, then.”\n\n“May the God of Abraham help us!” Simonides exclaimed.\n\n“One word now, my friends,” Ben-Hur said, more cheerfully. “By your leave, I will be my own until after the games. It is not probable Messala will set peril on foot for me until he has given the procurator time to answer him; and that cannot be in less than seven days from the despatch of his letter. The meeting him in the Circus is a pleasure I would buy at whatever risk.”\n\nIlderim, well pleased, assented readily, and Simonides, intent on business, added, “It is well; for look you, my master, the delay will give me time to do you a good part. I understood you to speak of an inheritance derived from Arrius. Is it in property?”\n\n“A villa near Misenum, and houses in Rome.”\n\n“I suggest, then, the sale of the property, and safe deposit of the proceeds. Give me an account of it, and I will have authorities drawn, and despatch an agent on the mission forthwith. We will forestall the imperial robbers at least this once.”\n\n“You shall have the account to-morrow.”\n\n“Then, if there be nothing more, the work of the night is done,” said Simonides.\n\nIlderim combed his beard complacently, saying, “And well done.”\n\n“The bread and wine again, Esther. Sheik Ilderim will make us happy by staying with us till to-morrow, or at his pleasure; and thou, my master—”\n\n“Let the horses be brought,” said Ben-Hur. “I will return to the Orchard. The enemy will not discover me if I go now, and”—he glanced at Ilderim—“the four will be glad to see me.”\n\nAs the day dawned, he and Malluch dismounted at the door of the tent.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nNext night, about the fourth hour, Ben-Hur stood on the terrace of the great warehouse with Esther. Below them, on the landing, there was much running about, and shifting of packages and boxes, and shouting of men, whose figures, stooping, heaving, hauling, looked, in the light of the crackling torches kindled in their aid, like the laboring genii of the fantastic Eastern tales. A galley was being laden for instant departure. Simonides had not yet come from his office, in which, at the last moment, he would deliver to the captain of the vessel instructions to proceed without stop to Ostia, the seaport of Rome, and, after landing a passenger there, continue more leisurely to Valentia, on the coast of Spain.\n\nThe passenger is the agent going to dispose of the estate derived from Arrius the duumvir. When the lines of the vessel are cast off, and she is put about, and her voyage begun, Ben-Hur will be committed irrevocably to the work undertaken the night before. If he is disposed to repent the agreement with Ilderim, a little time is allowed him to give notice and break it off. He is master, and has only to say the word.\n\nSuch may have been the thought at the moment in his mind. He was standing with folded arms, looking upon the scene in the manner of a man debating with himself. Young, handsome, rich, but recently from the patrician circles of Roman society, it is easy to think of the world besetting him with appeals not to give more to onerous duty or ambition attended with outlawry and danger. We can even imagine the arguments with which he was pressed; the hopelessness of contention with Cæsar; the uncertainty veiling everything connected with the King and his coming; the ease, honors, state, purchasable like goods in market; and, strongest of all, the sense newly acquired of home, with friends to make it delightful. Only those who have been wanderers long desolate can know the power there was in the latter appeal.\n\nLet us add now, the world—always cunning enough of itself; always whispering to the weak, Stay, take thine ease; always presenting the sunny side of life—the world was in this instance helped by Ben-Hur’s companion.\n\n“Were you ever at Rome?” he asked.\n\n“No,” Esther replied.\n\n“Would you like to go?”\n\n“I think not.”\n\n“Why?”\n“I am afraid of Rome,” she answered, with a perceptible tremor of the voice.\n\nHe looked at her then—or rather down upon her, for at his side she appeared little more than a child. In the dim light he could not see her face distinctly; even the form was shadowy. But again he was reminded of Tirzah, and a sudden tenderness fell upon him—just so the lost sister stood with him on the house-top the calamitous morning of the accident to Gratus. Poor Tirzah! Where was she now? Esther had the benefit of the feeling evoked. If not his sister, he could never look upon her as his servant; and that she was his servant in fact would make him always the more considerate and gentle towards her.\n\n“I cannot think of Rome,” she continued, recovering her voice, and speaking in her quiet womanly way—“I cannot think of Rome as a city of palaces and temples, and crowded with people; she is to me a monster which has possession of one of the beautiful lands, and lies there luring men to ruin and death—a monster which it is not possible to resist—a ravenous beast gorging with blood. Why—”\n\nShe faltered, looked down, stopped.\n\n“Go on,” said Ben-Hur, reassuringly.\n\nShe drew closer to him, looked up again, and said, “Why must you make her your enemy? Why not rather make peace with her, and be at rest? You have had many ills, and borne them; you have survived the snares laid for you by foes. Sorrow has consumed your youth; is it well to give it the remainder of your days?”\n\nThe girlish face under his eyes seemed to come nearer and get whiter as the pleading went on; he stooped towards it, and asked, softly, “What would you have me do, Esther?”\n\nShe hesitated a moment, then asked, in return, “Is the property near Rome a residence?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“And pretty?”\n\n“It is beautiful—a palace in the midst of gardens and shell-strewn walks; fountains without and within; statuary in the shady nooks; hills around covered with vines, and so high that Neapolis and Vesuvius are in sight, and the sea an expanse of purpling blue dotted with restless sails. Cæsar has a country-seat near-by, but in Rome they say the old Arrian villa is the prettiest.”\n\n“And the life there, is it quiet?”\n\n“There was never a summer day, never a moonlit night, more quiet, save when visitors come. Now that the old owner is gone, and I am here, there is nothing to break its silence—nothing, unless it be the whispering of servants, or the whistling of happy birds, or the noise of fountains at play; it is changeless, except as day by day old flowers fade and fall, and new ones bud and bloom, and the sunlight gives place to the shadow of a passing cloud. The life, Esther, was all too quiet for me. It made me restless by keeping always present a feeling that I, who have so much to do, was dropping into idle habits, and tying myself with silken chains, and after a while—and not a long while either—would end with nothing done.”\n\nShe looked off over the river.\n\n“Why did you ask?” he said.\n\n“Good my master—”\n\n“No, no, Esther—not that. Call me friend—brother, if you will; I am not your master, and will not be. Call me brother.”\n\nHe could not see the flush of pleasure which reddened her face, and the glow of the eyes that went out lost in the void above the river.\n\n“I cannot understand,” she said, “the nature which prefers the life you are going to—a life of—”\n\n“Of violence, and it may be of blood,” he said, completing the sentence.\n\n“Yes,” she added, “the nature which could prefer that life to such as might be in the beautiful villa.”\n\n“Esther, you mistake. There is no preference. Alas! the Roman is not so kind. I am going of necessity. To stay here is to die; and if I go there, the end will be the same—a poisoned cup, a bravo’s blow, or a judge’s sentence obtained by perjury. Messala and the procurator Gratus are rich with plunder of my father’s estate, and it is more important to them to keep their gains now than was their getting in the first instance. A peaceable settlement is out of reach, because of the confession it would imply. And then—then— Ah, Esther, if I could buy them, I do not know that I would. I do not believe peace possible to me; no, not even in the sleepy shade and sweet air of the marble porches of the old villa—no matter who might be there to help me bear the burden of the days, nor by what patience of love she made the effort. Peace is not possible to me while my people are lost, for I must be watchful to find them. If I find them, and they have suffered wrong, shall not the guilty suffer for it? If they are dead by violence, shall the murderers escape? Oh, I could not sleep for dreams! Nor could the holiest love, by any stratagem, lull me to a rest which conscience would not strangle.”\n\n“Is it so bad then?” she asked, her voice tremulous with feeling. “Can nothing, nothing, be done?”\n\nBen-Hur took her hand.\n\n“Do you care so much for me?”\n\n“Yes,” she answered, simply.\n\nThe hand was warm, and in the palm of his it was lost. He felt it tremble. Then the Egyptian came, so the opposite of this little one; so tall, so audacious, with a flattery so cunning, a wit so ready, a beauty so wonderful, a manner so bewitching. He carried the hand to his lips, and gave it back.\n\n“You shall be another Tirzah to me, Esther.”\n\n“Who is Tirzah?”\n\n“The little sister the Roman stole from me, and whom I must find before I can rest or be happy.”\n\nJust then a gleam of light flashed athwart the terrace and fell upon the two; and, looking round, they saw a servant roll Simonides in his chair out of the door. They went to the merchant, and in the after-talk he was principal.\n\nImmediately the lines of the galley were cast off, and she swung round, and, midst the flashing of torches and the shouting of joyous sailors, hurried off to the sea—leaving Ben-Hur committed to the cause of the KING WHO WAS TO COME.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\nThe day before the games, in the afternoon, all Ilderim’s racing property was taken to the city, and put in quarters adjoining the Circus. Along with it the good man carried a great deal of property not of that class; so with servants, retainers mounted and armed, horses in leading, cattle driven, camels laden with baggage, his outgoing from the Orchard was not unlike a tribal migration. The people along the road failed not to laugh at his motley procession; on the other side, it was observed that, with all his irascibility, he was not in the least offended by their rudeness. If he was under surveillance, as he had reason to believe, the informer would describe the semi-barbarous show with which he came up to the races. The Romans would laugh; the city would be amused; but what cared he? Next morning the pageant would be far on the road to the desert, and going with it would be every movable thing of value belonging to the Orchard—everything save such as were essential to the success of his four. He was, in fact, started home; his tents were all folded; the dowar was no more; in twelve hours all would be out of reach, pursue who might. A man is never safer than when he is under the laugh; and the shrewd old Arab knew it.\n\nNeither he nor Ben-Hur overestimated the influence of Messala; it was their opinion, however, that he would not begin active measures against them until after the meeting in the Circus; if defeated there, especially if defeated by Ben-Hur, they might instantly look for the worst he could do; he might not even wait for advices from Gratus. With this view, they shaped their course, and were prepared to betake themselves out of harm’s way. They rode together now in good spirits, calmly confident of success on the morrow.\n\nOn the way, they came upon Malluch in waiting for them. The faithful fellow gave no sign by which it was possible to infer any knowledge on his part of the relationship so recently admitted between Ben-Hur and Simonides, or of the treaty between them and Ilderim. He exchanged salutations as usual, and produced a paper, saying to the sheik, “I have here the notice of the editor of the games, just issued, in which you will find your horses published for the race. You will find in it also the order of exercises. Without waiting, good sheik, I congratulate you upon your victory.”\n\nHe gave the paper over, and, leaving the worthy to master it, turned to Ben-Hur.\n\n“To you also, son of Arrius, my congratulations. There is nothing now to prevent your meeting Messala. Every condition preliminary to the race is complied with. I have the assurance from the editor himself.”\n\n“I thank you, Malluch,” said Ben-Hur.\n\nMalluch proceeded:\n\n“Your color is white, and Messala’s mixed scarlet and gold. The good effects of the choice are visible already. Boys are now hawking white ribbons along the streets; tomorrow every Arab and Jew in the city will wear them. In the Circus you will see the white fairly divide the galleries with the red.”\n\n“The galleries—but not the tribunal over the Porta Pompae.”\n\n“No; the scarlet and gold will rule there. But if we win”—Malluch chuckled with the pleasure of the thought—“if we win, how the dignitaries will tremble! They will bet, of course, according to their scorn of everything not Roman—two, three, five to one on Messala, because he is Roman.” Dropping his voice yet lower, he added, “It ill becomes a Jew of good standing in the Temple to put his money at such a hazard; yet, in confidence, I will have a friend next behind the consul’s seat to accept offers of three to one, or five, or ten—the madness may go to such height. I have put to his order six thousand shekels for the purpose.”\n\n“Nay, Malluch,” said Ben-Hur, “a Roman will wager only in his Roman coin. Suppose you find your friend to-night, and place to his order sestertii in such amount as you choose. And look you, Malluch—let him be instructed to seek wagers with Messala and his supporters; Ilderim’s four against Messala’s.”\n\nMalluch reflected a moment.\n\n“The effect will be to centre interest upon your contest.”\n\n“The very thing I seek, Malluch.”\n\n“I see, I see.”\n\n“Ay, Malluch; would you serve me perfectly, help me to fix the public eye upon our race—Messala’s and mine.”\n\nMalluch spoke quickly—“It can be done.”\n\n“Then let it be done,” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“Enormous wagers offered will answer; if the offers are accepted, all the better.”\n\nMalluch turned his eyes watchfully upon Ben-Hur.\n\n“Shall I not have back the equivalent of his robbery?” said Ben-Hur, partly to himself. “Another opportunity may not come. And if I could break him in fortune as well as in pride! Our father Jacob could take no offence.”\n\nA look of determined will knit his handsome face, giving emphasis to his further speech.\n\n“Yes, it shall be. Hark, Malluch! Stop not in thy offer of sestertii. Advance them to talents, if any there be who dare so high. Five, ten, twenty talents; ay, fifty, so the wager be with Messala himself.”\n\n“It is a mighty sum,” said Malluch. “I must have security.”\n\n“So thou shalt. Go to Simonides, and tell him I wish the matter arranged. Tell him my heart is set on the ruin of my enemy, and that the opportunity hath such excellent promise that I choose such hazards. On our side be the God of our fathers. Go, good Malluch. Let this not slip.”\n\nAnd Malluch, greatly delighted, gave him parting salutation, and started to ride away, but returned presently.\n\n“Your pardon,” he said to Ben-Hur. “There was another matter. I could not get near Messala’s chariot myself, but I had another measure it; and, from his report, its hub stands quite a palm higher from the ground than yours.”\n\n“A palm! So much?” cried Ben-Hur, joyfully.\n\nThen he leaned over to Malluch.\n\n“As thou art a son of Judah, Malluch, and faithful to thy kin, get thee a seat in the gallery over the Gate of Triumph, down close to the balcony in front of the pillars, and watch well when we make the turns there; watch well, for if I have favor at all, I will— Nay, Malluch, let it go unsaid! Only get thee there, and watch well.”\n\nAt that moment a cry burst from Ilderim.\n\n“Ha! By the splendor of God! what is this?”\n\nHe drew near Ben-Hur with a finger pointing on the face of the notice.\n\n“Read,” said Ben-Hur.\n“No; better thou.”\n\nBen-Hur took the paper, which, signed by the prefect of the province as editor, performed the office of a modern programme, giving particularly the several divertisements provided for the occasion. It informed the public that there would be first a procession of extraordinary splendor; that the procession would be succeeded by the customary honors to the god Consus, whereupon the games would begin; running, leaping, wrestling, boxing, each in the order stated. The names of the competitors were given, with their several nationalities and schools of training, the trials in which they had been engaged, the prizes won, and the prizes now offered; under the latter head the sums of money were stated in illuminated letters, telling of the departure of the day when the simple chaplet of pine or laurel was fully enough for the victor, hungering for glory as something better than riches, and content with it.\n\nOver these parts of the programme Ben-Hur sped with rapid eyes. At last he came to the announcement of the race. He read it slowly. Attending lovers of the heroic sports were assured they would certainly be gratified by an Orestean struggle unparalleled in Antioch. The city offered the spectacle in honor of the consul. One hundred thousand sestertii and a crown of laurel were the prizes. Then followed the particulars. The entries were six in all—fours only permitted; and, to further interest in the performance, the competitors would be turned into the course together. Each four then received description.\n\n“I. A four of Lysippus the Corinthian—two grays, a bay, and a black; entered at Alexandria last year, and again at Corinth, where they were winners. Lysippus, driver. Color, yellow.\n\n“II. A four of Messala of Rome—two white, two black; victors of the Circensian as exhibited in the Circus Maximus last year. Messala, driver. Colors, scarlet and gold.\n\n“III. A four of Cleanthes the Athenian—three gray, one bay; winners at the Isthmian last year. Cleanthes, driver. Color, green.\n\n“IV. A four of Dicaeus the Byzantine—two black, one gray, one bay; winners this year at Byzantium. Dicaeus, driver. Color, black.\n\n“V. A four of Admetus the Sidonian—all grays. Thrice entered at Cæsarea, and thrice victors. Admetus, driver. Color, blue.\n\n“VI. A four of Ilderim, sheik of the Desert. All bays; first race. Ben-Hur, a Jew, driver. Color, white.”\n\nWhy that name instead of Arrius?\n\nBen-Hur raised his eyes to Ilderim. He had found the cause of the Arab’s outcry. Both rushed to the same conclusion.\n\nThe hand was the hand of Messala!\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\nEvening was hardly come upon Antioch, when the Omphalus, nearly in the centre of the city, became a troubled fountain from which in every direction, but chiefly down to the Nymphaeum and east and west along the Colonnade of Herod, flowed currents of people, for the time given up to Bacchus and Apollo.\n\nFor such indulgence anything more fitting cannot be imagined than the great roofed streets, which were literally miles on miles of porticos wrought of marble, polished to the last degree of finish, and all gifts to the voluptuous city by princes careless of expenditure where, as in this instance, they thought they were eternizing themselves. Darkness was not permitted anywhere; and the singing, the laughter, the shouting, were incessant, and in compound like the roar of waters dashing through hollow grots, confused by a multitude of echoes.\n\nThe many nationalities represented, though they might have amazed a stranger, were not peculiar to Antioch. Of the various missions of the great empire, one seems to have been the fusion of men and the introduction of strangers to each other; accordingly, whole peoples rose up and went at pleasure, taking with them their costumes, customs, speech, and gods; and where they chose, they stopped, engaged in business, built houses, erected altars, and were what they had been at home.\n\nThere was a peculiarity, however, which could not have failed the notice of a looker-on this night in Antioch. Nearly everybody wore the colors of one or other of the charioteers announced for the morrow’s race. Sometimes it was in form of a scarf, sometimes a badge; often a ribbon or a feather. Whatever the form, it signified merely the wearer’s partiality; thus, green published a friend of Cleanthes the Athenian, and black an adherent of the Byzantine. This was according to a custom, old probably as the day of the race of Orestes—a custom, by the way, worthy of study as a marvel of history, illustrative of the absurd yet appalling extremities to which men frequently suffer their follies to drag them.\n\nThe observer abroad on this occasion, once attracted to the wearing of colors, would have very shortly decided that there were three in predominance—green, white, and the mixed scarlet and gold.\n\nBut let us from the streets to the palace on the island.\n\nThe five great chandeliers in the saloon are freshly lighted. The assemblage is much the same as that already noticed in connection with the place. The divan has its corps of sleepers and burden of garments, and the tables yet resound with the rattle and clash of dice. Yet the greater part of the company are not doing anything. They walk about, or yawn tremendously, or pause as they pass each other to exchange idle nothings. Will the weather be fair to-morrow? Are the preparations for the games complete? Do the laws of the Circus in Antioch differ from the laws of the Circus in Rome? Truth is, the young fellows are suffering from ennui. Their heavy work is done; that is, we would find their tablets, could we look at them, covered with memoranda of wagers—wagers on every contest; on the running, the wrestling, the boxing; on everything but the chariot-race.\n\nGood reader, they cannot find anybody who will hazard so much as a denarius with them against Messala.\n\nThere are no colors in the saloon but his.\n\nNo one thinks of his defeat.\n\nWhy, they say, is he not perfect in his training? Did he not graduate from an imperial lanista? Were not his horses winners at the Circensian in the Circus Maximus? And then—ah, yes! he is a Roman!\n\nIn a corner, at ease on the divan, Messala himself may be seen. Around him, sitting or standing, are his courtierly admirers, plying him with questions. There is, of course, but one topic.\n\nEnter Drusus and Cecilius.\n\n“Ah!” cries the young prince, throwing himself on the divan at Messala’s feet, “Ah, by Bacchus, I am tired!”\n\n“Whither away?” asks Messala.\n\n“Up the street; up to the Omphalus, and beyond—who shall say how far? Rivers of people; never so many in the city before. They say we will see the whole world at the Circus to-morrow.”\n\nMessala laughed scornfully.\n\n“The idiots! Perpol! They never beheld a Circensian with Cæsar for editor. But, my Drusus, what found you?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“O—ah! You forget,” said Cecilius.\n\n“What?” asked Drusus.\n\n“The procession of whites.”\n\n“Mirabile!” cried Drusus, half rising. “We met a faction of whites, and they had a banner. But—ha, ha, ha!”\n\nHe fell back indolently.\n\n“Cruel Drusus—not to go on,” said Messala.\n\n“Scum of the desert were they, my Messala, and garbage-eaters from the Jacob’s Temple in Jerusalem. What had I to do with them!”\n\n“Nay,” said Cecilius, “Drusus is afraid of a laugh, but I am not, my Messala.”\n\n“Speak thou, then.”\n\n“Well, we stopped the faction, and—”\n\n“Offered them a wager,” said Drusus, relenting, and taking the word from the shadow’s mouth. “And—ha, ha, ha!—one fellow with not enough skin on his face to make a worm for a carp stepped forth, and—ha, ha, ha!—said yes. I drew my tablets. ‘Who is your man?’ I asked. ‘Ben-Hur, the Jew,’ said he. Then I: ‘What shall it be? How much?’ He answered, ‘A—a—’ Excuse me, Messala. By Jove’s thunder, I cannot go on for laughter! Ha, ha, ha!”\n\nThe listeners leaned forward.\n\nMessala looked to Cecilius.\n\n“A shekel,” said the latter.\n\n“A shekel! A shekel!”\n\nA burst of scornful laughter ran fast upon the repetition.\n\n“And what did Drusus?” asked Messala.\n\nAn outcry over about the door just then occasioned a rush to that quarter; and, as the noise there continued, and grew louder, even Cecilius betook himself off, pausing only to say, “The noble Drusus, my Messala, put up his tablets and—lost the shekel.”\n\n“A white! A white!”\n\n“Let him come!”\n\n“This way, this way!”\n\nThese and like exclamations filled the saloon, to the stoppage of other speech. The dice-players quit their games; the sleepers awoke, rubbed their eyes, drew their tablets, and hurried to the common centre.\n\n“I offer you—”\n\n“And I—”\n\n“I—”\nThe person so warmly received was the respectable Jew, Ben-Hur’s fellow-voyager from Cyprus. He entered grave, quiet, observant. His robe was spotlessly white; so was the cloth of his turban. Bowing and smiling at the welcome, he moved slowly towards the central table. Arrived there, he drew his robe about him in a stately manner, took seat, and waved his hand. The gleam of a jewel on a finger helped him not a little to the silence which ensued.\n\n“Romans—most noble Romans—I salute you!” he said.\n\n“Easy, by Jupiter! Who is he?” asked Drusus.\n\n“A dog of Israel—Sanballat by name—purveyor for the army; residence, Rome; vastly rich; grown so as a contractor of furnishments which he never furnishes. He spins mischiefs, nevertheless, finer than spiders spin their webs. Come—by the girdle of Venus! let us catch him!”\n\nMessala arose as he spoke, and, with Drusus, joined the mass crowded about the purveyor.\n\n“It came to me on the street,” said that person, producing his tablets, and opening them on the table with an impressive air of business, “that there was great discomfort in the palace because offers on Messala were going without takers. The gods, you know, must have sacrifices; and here am I. You see my color; let us to the matter. Odds first, amounts next. What will you give me?”\n\nThe audacity seemed to stun his hearers.\n\n“Haste!” he said. “I have an engagement with the consul.”\n\nThe spur was effective.\n\n“Two to one,” cried half a dozen in a voice.\n\n“What!” exclaimed the purveyor, astonished. “Only two to one, and yours a Roman!”\n\n“Take three, then.”\n\n“Three say you—only three—and mine but a dog of a Jew! Give me four.”\n\n“Four it is,” said a boy, stung by the taunt.\n\n“Five—give me five,” cried the purveyor, instantly.\n\nA profound stillness fell upon the assemblage.\n\n“The consul—your master and mine—is waiting for me.”\n\nThe inaction became awkward to the many.\n\n“Give me five—for the honor of Rome, five.”\n\n“Five let it be,” said one in answer.\n\nThere was a sharp cheer—a commotion—and Messala himself appeared.\n\n“Five let it be,” he said.\n\nAnd Sanballat smiled, and made ready to write.\n\n“If Cæsar die to-morrow,” he said, “Rome will not be all bereft. There is at least one other with spirit to take his place. Give me six.”\n\n“Six be it,” answered Messala.\n\nThere was another shout louder than the first.\n\n“Six be it,” repeated Messala. “Six to one—the difference between a Roman and a Jew. And, having found it, now, O redemptor of the flesh of swine, let us on. The amount—and quickly. The consul may send for thee, and I will then be bereft.”\n\nSanballat took the laugh against him coolly, and wrote, and offered the writing to Messala.\n\n“Read, read!” everybody demanded.\n\nAnd Messala read:\n\n“Mem.—Chariot-race. Messala of Rome, in wager with Sanballat, also of Rome, says he will beat Ben-Hur, the Jew. Amount of wager, twenty talents. Odds to Sanballat, six to one.\n\n“Witnesses: SANBALLAT.”\n\nThere was no noise, no motion. Each person seemed held in the pose the reading found him. Messala stared at the memorandum, while the eyes which had him in view opened wide, and stared at him. He felt the gaze, and thought rapidly. So lately he stood in the same place, and in the same way hectored the countrymen around him. They would remember it. If he refused to sign, his hero-ship was lost. And sign he could not; he was not worth one hundred talents, nor the fifth part of the sum. Suddenly his mind became a blank; he stood speechless; the color fled his face. An idea at last came to his relief.\n\n“Thou Jew!” he said, “where hast thou twenty talents? Show me.”\n\nSanballat’s provoking smile deepened.\n\n“There,” he replied, offering Messala a paper.\n\n“Read, read!” arose all around.\n\nAgain Messala read:\n\n“AT ANTIOCH, Tammuz 16th day.\n\n“The bearer, Sanballat of Rome, hath now to his order with me fifty talents, coin of Cæsar.\n\n“Fifty talents, fifty talents!” echoed the throng, in amazement.\n\nThen Drusus came to the rescue.\n\n“By Hercules!” he shouted, “the paper lies, and the Jew is a liar. Who but Cæsar hath fifty talents at order? Down with the insolent white!”\n\nThe cry was angry, and it was angrily repeated; yet Sanballat kept his seat, and his smile grew more exasperating the longer he waited. At length Messala spoke.\n\n“Hush! One to one, my countrymen—one to one, for love of our ancient Roman name.”\n\nThe timely action recovered him his ascendancy.\n\n“O thou circumcised dog!” he continued, to Sanballat, “I gave thee six to one, did I not?”\n\n“Yes,” said the Jew, quietly.\n\n“Well, give me now the fixing of the amount.”\n\n“With reserve, if the amount be trifling, have thy will,” answered Sanballat.\n\n“Write, then, five in place of twenty.”\n\n“Hast thou so much?”\n\n“By the mother of the gods, I will show you receipts.”\n\n“Nay, the word of so brave a Roman must pass. Only make the sum even—six make it, and I will write.”\n\n“Write it so.”\n\nAnd forthwith they exchanged writings.\n\nSanballat immediately arose and looked around him, a sneer in place of his smile. No man better than he knew those with whom he was dealing.\n\n“Romans,” he said, “another wager, if you dare! Five talents against five talents that the white will win. I challenge you collectively.”\n\nThey were again surprised.\n\n“What!” he cried, louder. “Shall it be said in the Circus to-morrow that a dog of Israel went into the saloon of the palace full of Roman nobles—among them the scion of a Cæsar—and laid five talents before them in challenge, and they had not the courage to take it up?”\n\nThe sting was unendurable.\n\n“Have done, O insolent!” said Drusus, “write the challenge, and leave it on the table; and to-morrow, if we find thou hast indeed so much money to put at such hopeless hazard, I, Drusus, promise it shall be taken.”\n\nSanballat wrote again, and, rising, said, unmoved as ever, “See, Drusus, I leave the offer with you. When it is signed, send it to me any time before the race begins. I will be found with the consul in a seat over the Porta Pompae. Peace to you; peace to all.”\n\nHe bowed, and departed, careless of the shout of derision with which they pursued him out of the door.\n\nIn the night the story of the prodigious wager flew along the streets and over the city; and Ben-Hur, lying with his four, was told of it, and also that Messala’s whole fortune was on the hazard.\n\nAnd he slept never so soundly.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\nThe Circus at Antioch stood on the south bank of the river, nearly opposite the island, differing in no respect from the plan of such buildings in general.\n\nIn the purest sense, the games were a gift to the public; consequently, everybody was free to attend; and, vast as the holding capacity of the structure was, so fearful were the people, on this occasion, lest there should not be room for them, that, early the day before the opening of the exhibition, they took up all the vacant spaces in the vicinity, where their temporary shelter suggested an army in waiting.\n\nAt midnight the entrances were thrown wide, and the rabble, surging in, occupied the quarters assigned to them, from which nothing less than an earthquake or an army with spears could have dislodged them. They dozed the night away on the benches, and breakfasted there; and there the close of the exercises found them, patient and sight-hungry as in the beginning.\n\nThe better people, their seats secured, began moving towards the Circus about the first hour of the morning, the noble and very rich among them distinguished by litters and retinues of liveried servants.\n\nBy the second hour, the efflux from the city was a stream unbroken and innumerable.\n\nExactly as the gnomon of the official dial up in the citadel pointed the second hour half gone, the legion, in full panoply, and with all its standards on exhibit, descended from Mount Sulpius; and when the rear of the last cohort disappeared in the bridge, Antioch was literally abandoned—not that the Circus could hold the multitude, but that the multitude was gone out to it, nevertheless.\n\nA great concourse on the river shore witnessed the consul come over from the island in a barge of state. As the great man landed, and was received by the legion, the martial show for one brief moment transcended the attraction of the Circus.\n\nAt the third hour, the audience, if such it may be termed, was assembled; at last, a flourish of trumpets called for silence, and instantly the gaze of over a hundred thousand persons was directed towards a pile forming the eastern section of the building.\n\nThere was a basement first, broken in the middle by a broad arched passage, called the Porta Pompae, over which, on an elevated tribunal magnificently decorated with insignia and legionary standards, the consul sat in the place of honor. On both sides of the passage the basement was divided into stalls termed carceres, each protected in front by massive gates swung to statuesque pilasters. Over the stalls next was a cornice crowned by a low balustrade; back of which the seats arose in theatre arrangement, all occupied by a throng of dignitaries superbly attired. The pile extended the width of the Circus, and was flanked on both sides by towers which, besides helping the architects give grace to their work, served the velaria, or purple awnings, stretched between them so as to throw the whole quarter in a shade that became exceedingly grateful as the day advanced.\n\nThis structure, it is now thought, can be made useful in helping the reader to a sufficient understanding of the arrangement of the rest of the interior of the Circus. He has only to fancy himself seated on the tribunal with the consul, facing to the west, where everything is under his eye.\n\nOn the right and left, if he will look, he will see the main entrances, very ample, and guarded by gates hinged to the towers.\n\nDirectly below him is the arena—a level plane of considerable extent, covered with fine white sand. There all the trials will take place except the running.\n\nLooking across this sanded arena westwardly still, there is a pedestal of marble supporting three low conical pillars of gray stone, much carven. Many an eye will hunt for those pillars before the day is done, for they are the first goal, and mark the beginning and end of the race-course. Behind the pedestal, leaving a passage-way and space for an altar, commences a wall ten or twelve feet in breadth and five or six in height, extending thence exactly two hundred yards, or one Olympic stadium. At the farther, or westward, extremity of the wall there is another pedestal, surmounted with pillars which mark the second goal.\n\nThe racers will enter the course on the right of the first goal, and keep the wall all the time to their left. The beginning and ending points of the contest lie, consequently, directly in front of the consul across the arena; and for that reason his seat was admittedly the most desirable in the Circus.\n\nNow if the reader, who is still supposed to be seated on the consular tribunal over the Porta Pompae, will look up from the ground arrangement of the interior, the first point to attract his notice will be the marking of the outer boundary-line of the course—that is, a plain-faced, solid wall, fifteen or twenty feet in height, with a balustrade on its cope, like that over the carceres, or stalls, in the east. This balcony, if followed round the course, will be found broken in three places to allow passages of exit and entrance, two in the north and one in the west; the latter very ornate, and called the Gate of Triumph, because, when all is over, the victors will pass out that way, crowned, and with triumphal escort and ceremonies.\n\nAt the west end the balcony encloses the course in the form of a half circle, and is made to uphold two great galleries.\n\nDirectly behind the balustrade on the coping of the balcony is the first seat, from which ascend the succeeding benches, each higher than the one in front of it; giving to view a spectacle of surpassing interest—the spectacle of a vast space ruddy and glistening with human faces, and rich with varicolored costumes.\n\nThe commonalty occupy quarters over in the west, beginning at the point of termination of an awning, stretched, it would seem, for the accommodation of the better classes exclusively.\n\nHaving thus the whole interior of the Circus under view at the moment of the sounding of the trumpets, let the reader next imagine the multitude seated and sunk to sudden silence, and motionless in its intensity of interest.\n\nOut of the Porta Pompae over in the east rises a sound mixed of voices and instruments harmonized. Presently, forth issues the chorus of the procession with which the celebration begins; the editor and civic authorities of the city, givers of the games, follow in robes and garlands; then the gods, some on platforms borne by men, others in great four-wheel carriages gorgeously decorated; next them, again, the contestants of the day, each in costume exactly as he will run, wrestle, leap, box, or drive.\n\nSlowly crossing the arena, the procession proceeds to make circuit of the course. The display is beautiful and imposing. Approval runs before it in a shout, as the water rises and swells in front of a boat in motion. If the dumb, figured gods make no sign of appreciation of the welcome, the editor and his associates are not so backward.\n\nThe reception of the athletes is even more demonstrative, for there is not a man in the assemblage who has not something in wager upon them, though but a mite or farthing. And it is noticeable, as the classes move by, that the favorites among them are speedily singled out: either their names are loudest in the uproar, or they are more profusely showered with wreaths and garlands tossed to them from the balcony.\n\nIf there is a question as to the popularity with the public of the several games, it is now put to rest. To the splendor of the chariots and the superexcellent beauty of the horses, the charioteers add the personality necessary to perfect the charm of their display. Their tunics, short, sleeveless, and of the finest woollen texture, are of the assigned colors. A horseman accompanies each one of them except Ben-Hur, who, for some reason—possibly distrust—has chosen to go alone; so, too, they are all helmeted but him. As they approach, the spectators stand upon the benches, and there is a sensible deepening of the clamor, in which a sharp listener may detect the shrill piping of women and children; at the same time, the things roseate flying from the balcony thicken into a storm, and, striking the men, drop into the chariot-beds, which are threatened with filling to the tops. Even the horses have a share in the ovation; nor may it be said they are less conscious than their masters of the honors they receive.\n\nVery soon, as with the other contestants, it is made apparent that some of the drivers are more in favor than others; and then the discovery follows that nearly every individual on the benches, women and children as well as men, wears a color, most frequently a ribbon upon the breast or in the hair: now it is green, now yellow, now blue; but, searching the great body carefully, it is manifest that there is a preponderance of white, and scarlet and gold.\n\nIn a modern assemblage called together as this one is, particularly where there are sums at hazard upon the race, a preference would be decided by the qualities or performance of the horses; here, however, nationality was the rule. If the Byzantine and Sidonian found small support, it was because their cities were scarcely represented on the benches. On their side, the Greeks, though very numerous, were divided between the Corinthian and the Athenian, leaving but a scant showing of green and yellow. Messala’s scarlet and gold would have been but little better had not the citizens of Antioch, proverbially a race of courtiers, joined the Romans by adopting the color of their favorite. There were left then the country people, or Syrians, the Jews, and the Arabs; and they, from faith in the blood of the sheik’s four, blent largely with hate of the Romans, whom they desired, above all things, to see beaten and humbled, mounted the white, making the most noisy, and probably the most numerous, faction of all.\n\nAs the charioteers move on in the circuit, the excitement increases; at the second goal, where, especially in the galleries, the white is the ruling color, the people exhaust their flowers and rive the air with screams.\n\n“Messala! Messala!”\n\n“Ben-Hur! Ben-Hur!”\n\nSuch are the cries.\n\nUpon the passage of the procession, the factionists take their seats and resume conversation.\n\n“Ah, by Bacchus! was he not handsome?” exclaims a woman, whose Romanism is betrayed by the colors flying in her hair.\n\n“And how splendid his chariot!” replies a neighbor, of the same proclivities. “It is all ivory and gold. Jupiter grant he wins!”\n\nThe notes on the bench behind them were entirely different.\n\n“A hundred shekels on the Jew!”\n\nThe voice is high and shrill.\n\n“Nay, be thou not rash,” whispers a moderating friend to the speaker. “The children of Jacob are not much given to Gentile sports, which are too often accursed in the sight of the Lord.”\n\n“True, but saw you ever one more cool and assured? And what an arm he has!”\n\n“And what horses!” says a third.\n\n“And for that,” a fourth one adds, “they say he has all the tricks of the Romans.”\n\nA woman completes the eulogium:\n\n“Yes, and he is even handsomer than the Roman.”\n\nThus encouraged, the enthusiast shrieks again, “A hundred shekels on the Jew!”\n\n“Thou fool!” answers an Antiochian, from a bench well forward on the balcony. “Knowest thou not there are fifty talents laid against him, six to one, on Messala? Put up thy shekels, lest Abraham rise and smite thee.”\n\n“Ha, ha! thou ass of Antioch! Cease thy bray. Knowest thou not it was Messala betting on himself?”\n\nSuch the reply.\n\nAnd so ran the controversy, not always good-natured.\n\nWhen at length the march was ended and the Porta Pompae received back the procession, Ben-Hur knew he had his prayer.\n\nThe eyes of the East were upon his contest with Messala.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\nAbout three o’clock, speaking in modern style, the program was concluded except the chariot-race. The editor, wisely considerate of the comfort of the people, chose that time for a recess. At once the vomitoria were thrown open, and all who could hastened to the portico outside where the restaurateurs had their quarters. Those who remained yawned, talked, gossiped, consulted their tablets, and, all distinctions else forgotten, merged into but two classes—the winners, who were happy, and the losers, who were grum and captious.\n\nNow, however, a third class of spectators, composed of citizens who desired only to witness the chariot-race, availed themselves of the recess to come in and take their reserved seats; by so doing they thought to attract the least attention and give the least offence. Among these were Simonides and his party, whose places were in the vicinity of the main entrance on the north side, opposite the consul.\n\nAs the four stout servants carried the merchant in his chair up the aisle, curiosity was much excited. Presently some one called his name. Those about caught it and passed it on along the benches to the west; and there was hurried climbing on seats to get sight of the man about whom common report had coined and put in circulation a romance so mixed of good fortune and bad that the like had never been known or heard of before.\n\nIlderim was also recognized and warmly greeted; but nobody knew Balthasar or the two women who followed him closely veiled.\n\nThe people made way for the party respectfully, and the ushers seated them in easy speaking distance of each other down by the balustrade overlooking the arena. In providence of comfort, they sat upon cushions and had stools for footrests.\n\nThe women were Iras and Esther.\n\nUpon being seated, the latter cast a frightened look over the Circus, and drew the veil closer about her face; while the Egyptian, letting her veil fall upon her shoulders, gave herself to view, and gazed at the scene with the seeming unconsciousness of being stared at, which, in a woman, is usually the result of long social habitude.\n\nThe new-comers generally were yet making their first examination of the great spectacle, beginning with the consul and his attendants, when some workmen ran in and commenced to stretch a chalked rope across the arena from balcony to balcony in front of the pillars of the first goal.\n\nAbout the same time, also, six men came in through the Porta Pompae and took post, one in front of each occupied stall; whereat there was a prolonged hum of voices in every quarter.\n\n“See, see! The green goes to number four on the right; the Athenian is there.”\n\n“And Messala—yes, he is in number two.”\n\n“The Corinthian—”\n\n“Watch the white! See, he crosses over, he stops; number one it is—number one on the left.”\n\n“No, the black stops there, and the white at number two.”\n\n“So it is.”\n\nThese gate-keepers, it should be understood, were dressed in tunics colored like those of the competing charioteers; so, when they took their stations, everybody knew the particular stall in which his favorite was that moment waiting.\n\n“Did you ever see Messala?” the Egyptian asked Esther.\n\nThe Jewess shuddered as she answered no. If not her father’s enemy, the Roman was Ben-Hur’s.\n\n“He is beautiful as Apollo.”\n\nAs Iras spoke, her large eyes brightened and she shook her jeweled fan. Esther looked at her with the thought, “Is he, then, so much handsomer than Ben-Hur?” Next moment she heard Ilderim say to her father, “Yes, his stall is number two on the left of the Porta Pompae;” and, thinking it was of Ben-Hur he spoke, her eyes turned that way. Taking but the briefest glance at the wattled face of the gate, she drew the veil close and muttered a little prayer.\n\nPresently Sanballat came to the party.\n\n“I am just from the stalls, O sheik,” he said, bowing gravely to Ilderim, who began combing his beard, while his eyes glittered with eager inquiry. “The horses are in perfect condition.”\n\nIlderim replied simply, “If they are beaten, I pray it be by some other than Messala.”\n\nTurning then to Simonides, Sanballat drew out a tablet, saying, “I bring you also something of interest. I reported, you will remember, the wager concluded with Messala last night, and stated that I left another which, if taken, was to be delivered to me in writing to-day before the race began. Here it is.”\n\nSimonides took the tablet and read the memorandum carefully.\n\n“Yes,” he said, “their emissary came to ask me if you had so much money with me. Keep the tablet close. If you lose, you know where to come; if you win”—his face knit hard—“if you win—ah, friend, see to it! See the signers escape not; hold them to the last shekel. That is what they would with us.”\n\n“Trust me,” replied the purveyor.\n\n“Will you not sit with us?” asked Simonides.\n\n“You are very good,” the other returned; “but if I leave the consul, young Rome yonder will boil over. Peace to you; peace to all.”\n\nAt length the recess came to an end.\n\nThe trumpeters blew a call at which the absentees rushed back to their places. At the same time, some attendants appeared in the arena, and, climbing upon the division wall, went to an entablature near the second goal at the west end, and placed upon it seven wooden balls; then returning to the first goal, upon an entablature there they set up seven other pieces of wood hewn to represent dolphins.\n\n“What shall they do with the balls and fishes, O sheik?” asked Balthasar.\n\n“Hast thou never attended a race?”\n\n“Never before; and hardly know I why I am here.”\n\n“Well, they are to keep the count. At the end of each round run thou shalt see one ball and one fish taken down.”\n\nThe preparations were now complete, and presently a trumpeter in gaudy uniform arose by the editor, ready to blow the signal of commencement promptly at his order. Straightway the stir of the people and the hum of their conversation died away. Every face near-by, and every face in the lessening perspective, turned to the east, as all eyes settled upon the gates of the six stalls which shut in the competitors.\n\nThe unusual flush upon his face gave proof that even Simonides had caught the universal excitement. Ilderim pulled his beard fast and furious.\n\n“Look now for the Roman,” said the fair Egyptian to Esther, who did not hear her, for, with close-drawn veil and beating heart, she sat watching for Ben-Hur.\n\nThe structure containing the stalls, it should be observed, was in form of the segment of a circle, retired on the right so that its central point was projected forward, and midway the course, on the starting side of the first goal. Every stall, consequently, was equally distant from the starting-line or chalked rope above mentioned.\n\nThe trumpet sounded short and sharp; whereupon the starters, one for each chariot, leaped down from behind the pillars of the goal, ready to give assistance if any of the fours proved unmanageable.\n\nAgain the trumpet blew, and simultaneously the gate-keepers threw the stalls open.\n\nFirst appeared the mounted attendants of the charioteers, five in all, Ben-Hur having rejected the service. The chalked line was lowered to let them pass, then raised again. They were beautifully mounted, yet scarcely observed as they rode forward; for all the time the trampling of eager horses, and the voices of drivers scarcely less eager, were heard behind in the stalls, so that one might not look away an instant from the gaping doors.\n\nThe chalked line up again, the gate-keepers called their men; instantly the ushers on the balcony waved their hands, and shouted with all their strength, “Down! down!”\n\nAs well have whistled to stay a storm.\n\nForth from each stall, like missiles in a volley from so many great guns, rushed the six fours; and up the vast assemblage arose, electrified and irrepressible, and, leaping upon the benches, filled the Circus and the air above it with yells and screams. This was the time for which they had so patiently waited!—this the moment of supreme interest treasured up in talk and dreams since the proclamation of the games!\n\n“He is come—there—look!” cried Iras, pointing to Messala.\n\n“I see him,” answered Esther, looking at Ben-Hur.\n\nThe veil was withdrawn. For an instant the little Jewess was brave. An idea of the joy there is in doing an heroic deed under the eyes of a multitude came to her, and she understood ever after how, at such times, the souls of men, in the frenzy of performance, laugh at death or forget it utterly.\n\nThe competitors were now under view from nearly every part of the Circus, yet the race was not begun; they had first to make the chalked line successfully.\n\nThe line was stretched for the purpose of equalizing the start. If it were dashed upon, discomfiture of man and horses might be apprehended; on the other hand, to approach it timidly was to incur the hazard of being thrown behind in the beginning of the race; and that was certain forfeit of the great advantage always striven for—the position next the division wall on the inner line of the course.\n\nThis trial, its perils and consequences, the spectators knew thoroughly; and if the opinion of old Nestor, uttered that time he handed the reins to his son, were true—\n\n“It is not strength, but art, obtained the prize, And to be swift is less than to be wise”—\n\nall on the benches might well look for warning of the winner to be now given, justifying the interest with which they breathlessly watched for the result.\n\nThe arena swam in a dazzle of light; yet each driver looked first thing for the rope, then for the coveted inner line. So, all six aiming at the same point and speeding furiously, a collision seemed inevitable; nor that merely. What if the editor, at the last moment, dissatisfied with the start, should withhold the signal to drop the rope? Or if he should not give it in time?\n\nThe crossing was about two hundred and fifty feet in width. Quick the eye, steady the hand, unerring the judgment required. If now one look away! or his mind wander! or a rein slip! And what attraction in the ensemble of the thousands over the spreading balcony! Calculating upon the natural impulse to give one glance—just one—in sooth of curiosity or vanity, malice might be there with an artifice; while friendship and love, did they serve the same result, might be as deadly as malice.\n\nThe divine last touch in perfecting the beautiful is animation. Can we accept the saying, then these latter days, so tame in pastime and dull in sports, have scarcely anything to compare to the spectacle offered by the six contestants. Let the reader try to fancy it; let him first look down upon the arena, and see it glistening in its frame of dull-gray granite walls; let him then, in this perfect field, see the chariots, light of wheel, very graceful, and ornate as paint and burnishing can make them—Messala’s rich with ivory and gold; let him see the drivers, erect and statuesque, undisturbed by the motion of the cars, their limbs naked, and fresh and ruddy with the healthful polish of the baths—in their right hands goads, suggestive of torture dreadful to the thought—in their left hands, held in careful separation, and high, that they may not interfere with view of the steeds, the reins passing taut from the fore ends of the carriage-poles; let him see the fours, chosen for beauty as well as speed; let him see them in magnificent action, their masters not more conscious of the situation and all that is asked and hoped from them—their heads tossing, nostrils in play, now distent, now contracted—limbs too dainty for the sand which they touch but to spurn—limbs slender, yet with impact crushing as hammers—every muscle of the rounded bodies instinct with glorious life, swelling, diminishing, justifying the world in taking from them its ultimate measure of force; finally, along with chariots, drivers, horses, let the reader see the accompanying shadows fly; and, with such distinctness as the picture comes, he may share the satisfaction and deeper pleasure of those to whom it was a thrilling fact, not a feeble fancy. Every age has its plenty of sorrows; Heaven help where there are no pleasures!\n\nThe competitors having started each on the shortest line for the position next the wall, yielding would be like giving up the race; and who dared yield? It is not in common nature to change a purpose in mid-career; and the cries of encouragement from the balcony were indistinguishable and indescribable: a roar which had the same effect upon all the drivers.\n\nThe fours neared the rope together. Then the trumpeter by the editor’s side blew a signal vigorously. Twenty feet away it was not heard. Seeing the action, however, the judges dropped the rope, and not an instant too soon, for the hoof of one of Messala’s horses struck it as it fell. Nothing daunted, the Roman shook out his long lash, loosed the reins, leaned forward, and, with a triumphant shout, took the wall.\n\n“Jove with us! Jove with us!” yelled all the Roman faction, in a frenzy of delight.\n\nAs Messala turned in, the bronze lion’s head at the end of his axle caught the fore-leg of the Athenian’s right-hand trace-mate, flinging the brute over against its yoke-fellow. Both staggered, struggled, and lost their headway. The ushers had their will at least in part. The thousands held their breath with horror; only up where the consul sat was there shouting.\n\n“Jove with us!” screamed Drusus, frantically.\n\n“He wins! Jove with us!” answered his associates, seeing Messala speed on.\n\nTablet in hand, Sanballat turned to them; a crash from the course below stopped his speech, and he could not but look that way.\n\nMessala having passed, the Corinthian was the only contestant on the Athenian’s right, and to that side the latter tried to turn his broken four; and then; as ill-fortune would have it, the wheel of the Byzantine, who was next on the left, struck the tail-piece of his chariot, knocking his feet from under him. There was a crash, a scream of rage and fear, and the unfortunate Cleanthes fell under the hoofs of his own steeds: a terrible sight, against which Esther covered her eyes.\n\nOn swept the Corinthian, on the Byzantine, on the Sidonian.\n\nSanballat looked for Ben-Hur, and turned again to Drusus and his coterie.\n\n“A hundred sestertii on the Jew!” he cried.\n\n“Taken!” answered Drusus.\n\n“Another hundred on the Jew!” shouted Sanballat.\n\nNobody appeared to hear him. He called again; the situation below was too absorbing, and they were too busy shouting, “Messala! Messala! Jove with us!”\n\nWhen the Jewess ventured to look again, a party of workmen were removing the horses and broken car; another party were taking off the man himself; and every bench upon which there was a Greek was vocal with execrations and prayers for vengeance. Suddenly she dropped her hands; Ben-Hur, unhurt, was to the front, coursing freely forward along with the Roman! Behind them, in a group, followed the Sidonian, the Corinthian, and the Byzantine.\n\nThe race was on; the souls of the racers were in it; over them bent the myriads.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\nWhen the dash for position began, Ben-Hur, as we have seen, was on the extreme left of the six. For a moment, like the others, he was half blinded by the light in the arena; yet he managed to catch sight of his antagonists and divine their purpose. At Messala, who was more than an antagonist to him, he gave one searching look. The air of passionless hauteur characteristic of the fine patrician face was there as of old, and so was the Italian beauty, which the helmet rather increased; but more—it may have been a jealous fancy, or the effect of the brassy shadow in which the features were at the moment cast, still the Israelite thought he saw the soul of the man as through a glass, darkly: cruel, cunning, desperate; not so excited as determined—a soul in a tension of watchfulness and fierce resolve.\n\nIn a time not longer than was required to turn to his four again, Ben-Hur felt his own resolution harden to a like temper. At whatever cost, at all hazards, he would humble this enemy! Prize, friends, wagers, honor—everything that can be thought of as a possible interest in the race was lost in the one deliberate purpose. Regard for life even should not hold him back. Yet there was no passion, on his part; no blinding rush of heated blood from heart to brain, and back again; no impulse to fling himself upon Fortune: he did not believe in Fortune; far otherwise. He had his plan, and, confiding in himself, he settled to the task never more observant, never more capable. The air about him seemed aglow with a renewed and perfect transparency.\n\nWhen not half-way across the arena, he saw that Messala’s rush would, if there was no collision, and the rope fell, give him the wall; that the rope would fall, he ceased as soon to doubt; and, further, it came to him, a sudden flash-like insight, that Messala knew it was to be let drop at the last moment (prearrangement with the editor could safely reach that point in the contest); and it suggested, what more Roman-like than for the official to lend himself to a countryman who, besides being so popular, had also so much at stake? There could be no other accounting for the confidence with which Messala pushed his four forward the instant his competitors were prudentially checking their fours in front of the obstruction—no other except madness.\n\nIt is one thing to see a necessity and another to act upon it. Ben-Hur yielded the wall for the time.\n\nThe rope fell, and all the fours but his sprang into the course under urgency of voice and lash. He drew head to the right, and, with all the speed of his Arabs, darted across the trails of his opponents, the angle of movement being such as to lose the least time and gain the greatest possible advance. So, while the spectators were shivering at the Athenian’s mishap, and the Sidonian, Byzantine, and Corinthian were striving, with such skill as they possessed, to avoid involvement in the ruin, Ben-Hur swept around and took the course neck and neck with Messala, though on the outside. The marvellous skill shown in making the change thus from the extreme left across to the right without appreciable loss did not fail the sharp eyes upon the benches; the Circus seemed to rock and rock again with prolonged applause. Then Esther clasped her hands in glad surprise; then Sanballat, smiling, offered his hundred sestertii a second time without a taker; and then the Romans began to doubt, thinking Messala might have found an equal, if not a master, and that in an Israelite!\n\nAnd now, racing together side by side, a narrow interval between them, the two neared the second goal.\n\nThe pedestal of the three pillars there, viewed from the west, was a stone wall in the form of a half-circle, around which the course and opposite balcony were bent in exact parallelism. Making this turn was considered in all respects the most telling test of a charioteer; it was, in fact, the very feat in which Oraetes failed. As an involuntary admission of interest on the part of the spectators, a hush fell over all the Circus, so that for the first time in the race the rattle and clang of the cars plunging after the tugging steeds were distinctly heard. Then, it would seem, Messala observed Ben-Hur, and recognized him; and at once the audacity of the man flamed out in an astonishing manner.\n\n“Down Eros, up Mars!” he shouted, whirling his lash with practised hand—“Down Eros, up Mars!” he repeated, and caught the well-doing Arabs of Ben-Hur a cut the like of which they had never known.\n\nThe blow was seen in every quarter, and the amazement was universal. The silence deepened; up on the benches behind the consul the boldest held his breath, waiting for the outcome. Only a moment thus: then, involuntarily, down from the balcony, as thunder falls, burst the indignant cry of the people.\n\nThe four sprang forward affrighted. No hand had ever been laid upon them except in love; they had been nurtured ever so tenderly; and as they grew, their confidence in man became a lesson to men beautiful to see. What should such dainty natures do under such indignity but leap as from death?\n\nForward they sprang as with one impulse, and forward leaped the car. Past question, every experience is serviceable to us. Where got Ben-Hur the large hand and mighty grip which helped him now so well? Where but from the oar with which so long he fought the sea? And what was this spring of the floor under his feet to the dizzy eccentric lurch with which in the old time the trembling ship yielded to the beat of staggering billows, drunk with their power? So he kept his place, and gave the four free rein, and called to them in soothing voice, trying merely to guide them round the dangerous turn; and before the fever of the people began to abate, he had back the mastery. Nor that only: on approaching the first goal, he was again side by side with Messala, bearing with him the sympathy and admiration of every one not a Roman. So clearly was the feeling shown, so vigorous its manifestation, that Messala, with all his boldness, felt it unsafe to trifle further.\n\nAs the cars whirled round the goal, Esther caught sight of Ben-Hur’s face—a little pale, a little higher raised, otherwise calm, even placid.\n\nImmediately a man climbed on the entablature at the west end of the division wall, and took down one of the conical wooden balls. A dolphin on the east entablature was taken down at the same time.\n\nIn like manner, the second ball and second dolphin disappeared.\n\nAnd then the third ball and third dolphin.\n\nThree rounds concluded: still Messala held the inside position; still Ben-Hur moved with him side by side; still the other competitors followed as before. The contest began to have the appearance of one of the double races which became so popular in Rome during the later Cæsarean period—Messala and Ben-Hur in the first, the Corinthian, Sidonian, and Byzantine in the second. Meantime the ushers succeeded in returning the multitude to their seats, though the clamor continued to run the rounds, keeping, as it were, even pace with the rivals in the course below.\n\nIn the fifth round the Sidonian succeeded in getting a place outside Ben-Hur, but lost it directly.\n\nThe sixth round was entered upon without change of relative position.\n\nGradually the speed had been quickened—gradually the blood of the competitors warmed with the work. Men and beasts seemed to know alike that the final crisis was near, bringing the time for the winner to assert himself.\n\nThe interest which from the beginning had centred chiefly in the struggle between the Roman and the Jew, with an intense and general sympathy for the latter, was fast changing to anxiety on his account. On all the benches the spectators bent forward motionless, except as their faces turned following the contestants. Ilderim quitted combing his beard, and Esther forgot her fears.\n\n“A hundred sestertii on the Jew!” cried Sanballat to the Romans under the consul’s awning.\n\nThere was no reply.\n\n“A talent—or five talents, or ten; choose ye!”\n\nHe shook his tablets at them defiantly.\n\n“I will take thy sestertii,” answered a Roman youth, preparing to write.\n\n“Do not so,” interposed a friend.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Messala hath reached his utmost speed. See him lean over his chariot rim, the reins loose as flying ribbons. Look then at the Jew.”\n\nThe first one looked.\n\n“By Hercules!” he replied, his countenance falling. “The dog throws all his weight on the bits. I see, I see! If the gods help not our friend, he will be run away with by the Israelite. No, not yet. Look! Jove with us, Jove with us!”\n\nThe cry, swelled by every Latin tongue, shook the velaria over the consul’s head.\n\nIf it were true that Messala had attained his utmost speed, the effort was with effect; slowly but certainly he was beginning to forge ahead. His horses were running with their heads low down; from the balcony their bodies appeared actually to skim the earth; their nostrils showed blood red in expansion; their eyes seemed straining in their sockets. Certainly the good steeds were doing their best! How long could they keep the pace? It was but the commencement of the sixth round. On they dashed. As they neared the second goal, Ben-Hur turned in behind the Roman’s car.\n\nThe joy of the Messala faction reached its bound: they screamed and howled, and tossed their colors; and Sanballat filled his tablets with wagers of their tendering.\n\nMalluch, in the lower gallery over the Gate of Triumph, found it hard to keep his cheer. He had cherished the vague hint dropped to him by Ben-Hur of something to happen in the turning of the western pillars. It was the fifth round, yet the something had not come; and he had said to himself, the sixth will bring it; but, lo! Ben-Hur was hardly holding a place at the tail of his enemy’s car.\n\nOver in the east end, Simonides’ party held their peace. The merchant’s head was bent low. Ilderim tugged at his beard, and dropped his brows till there was nothing of his eyes but an occasional sparkle of light. Esther scarcely breathed. Iras alone appeared glad.\n\nAlong the home-stretch—sixth round—Messala leading, next him Ben-Hur, and so close it was the old story:\n\n“First flew Eumelus on Pheretian steeds; With those of Tros bold Diomed succeeds; Close on Eumelus’ back they puff the wind, And seem just mounting on his car behind; Full on his neck he feels the sultry breeze, And, hovering o’er, their stretching shadow sees.”\n\nThus to the first goal, and round it. Messala, fearful of losing his place, hugged the stony wall with perilous clasp; a foot to the left, and he had been dashed to pieces; yet, when the turn was finished, no man, looking at the wheel-tracks of the two cars, could have said, here went Messala, there the Jew. They left but one trace behind them.\n\nAs they whirled by, Esther saw Ben-Hur’s face again, and it was whiter than before.\n\nSimonides, shrewder than Esther, said to Ilderim, the moment the rivals turned into the course, “I am no judge, good sheik, if Ben-Hur be not about to execute some design. His face hath that look.”\n\nTo which Ilderim answered, “Saw you how clean they were and fresh? By the splendor of God, friend, they have not been running! But now watch!”\n\nOne ball and one dolphin remained on the entablatures; and all the people drew a long breath, for the beginning of the end was at hand.\n\nFirst, the Sidonian gave the scourge to his four, and, smarting with fear and pain, they dashed desperately forward, promising for a brief time to go to the front. The effort ended in promise. Next, the Byzantine and the Corinthian each made the trial with like result, after which they were practically out of the race. Thereupon, with a readiness perfectly explicable, all the factions except the Romans joined hope in Ben-Hur, and openly indulged their feeling.\n\n“Ben-Hur! Ben-Hur!” they shouted, and the blent voices of the many rolled overwhelmingly against the consular stand.\n\nFrom the benches above him as he passed, the favor descended in fierce injunctions.\n\n“Speed thee, Jew!”\n\n“Take the wall now!”\n\n“On! loose the Arabs! Give them rein and scourge!”\n\n“Let him not have the turn on thee again. Now or never!”\n\nOver the balustrade they stooped low, stretching their hands imploringly to him.\n\nEither he did not hear, or could not do better, for halfway round the course and he was still following; at the second goal even still no change!\n\nAnd now, to make the turn, Messala began to draw in his left-hand steeds, an act which necessarily slackened their speed. His spirit was high; more than one altar was richer of his vows; the Roman genius was still president. On the three pillars only six hundred feet away were fame, increase of fortune, promotions, and a triumph ineffably sweetened by hate, all in store for him! That moment Malluch, in the gallery, saw Ben-Hur lean forward over his Arabs, and give them the reins. Out flew the many-folded lash in his hand; over the backs of the startled steeds it writhed and hissed, and hissed and writhed again and again; and though it fell not, there were both sting and menace in its quick report; and as the man passed thus from quiet to resistless action, his face suffused, his eyes gleaming, along the reins he seemed to flash his will; and instantly not one, but the four as one, answered with a leap that landed them alongside the Roman’s car. Messala, on the perilous edge of the goal, heard, but dared not look to see what the awakening portended. From the people he received no sign. Above the noises of the race there was but one voice, and that was Ben-Hur’s. In the old Aramaic, as the sheik himself, he called to the Arabs,\n\n“On, Atair! On, Rigel! What, Antares! dost thou linger now? Good horse—oho, Aldebaran! I hear them singing in the tents. I hear the children singing and the women—singing of the stars, of Atair, Antares, Rigel, Aldebaran, victory!—and the song will never end. Well done! Home to-morrow, under the black tent—home! On, Antares! The tribe is waiting for us, and the master is waiting! ’Tis done! ’tis done! Ha, ha! We have overthrown the proud. The hand that smote us is in the dust. Ours the glory! Ha, ha!—steady! The work is done—soho! Rest!”\n\nThere had never been anything of the kind more simple; seldom anything so instantaneous.\n\nAt the moment chosen for the dash, Messala was moving in a circle round the goal. To pass him, Ben-Hur had to cross the track, and good strategy required the movement to be in a forward direction; that is, on a like circle limited to the least possible increase. The thousands on the benches understood it all: they saw the signal given—the magnificent response; the four close outside Messala’s outer wheel; Ben-Hur’s inner wheel behind the other’s car—all this they saw. Then they heard a crash loud enough to send a thrill through the Circus, and, quicker than thought, out over the course a spray of shining white and yellow flinders flew. Down on its right side toppled the bed of the Roman’s chariot. There was a rebound as of the axle hitting the hard earth; another and another; then the car went to pieces; and Messala, entangled in the reins, pitched forward headlong.\n\nTo increase the horror of the sight by making death certain, the Sidonian, who had the wall next behind, could not stop or turn out. Into the wreck full speed he drove; then over the Roman, and into the latter’s four, all mad with fear. Presently, out of the turmoil, the fighting of horses, the resound of blows, the murky cloud of dust and sand, he crawled, in time to see the Corinthian and Byzantine go on down the course after Ben-Hur, who had not been an instant delayed.\n\nThe people arose, and leaped upon the benches, and shouted and screamed. Those who looked that way caught glimpses of Messala, now under the trampling of the fours, now under the abandoned cars. He was still; they thought him dead; but far the greater number followed Ben-Hur in his career. They had not seen the cunning touch of the reins by which, turning a little to the left, he caught Messala’s wheel with the iron-shod point of his axle, and crushed it; but they had seen the transformation of the man, and themselves felt the heat and glow of his spirit, the heroic resolution, the maddening energy of action with which, by look, word, and gesture, he so suddenly inspired his Arabs. And such running! It was rather the long leaping of lions in harness; but for the lumbering chariot, it seemed the four were flying. When the Byzantine and Corinthian were halfway down the course, Ben-Hur turned the first goal.\n\n# AND THE RACE WAS WON!\n\nThe consul arose; the people shouted themselves hoarse; the editor came down from his seat, and crowned the victors.\n\nThe fortunate man among the boxers was a low-browed, yellow-haired Saxon, of such brutalized face as to attract a second look from Ben-Hur, who recognized a teacher with whom he himself had been a favorite at Rome. From him the young Jew looked up and beheld Simonides and his party on the balcony. They waved their hands to him. Esther kept her seat; but Iras arose, and gave him a smile and a wave of her fan—favors not the less intoxicating to him because we know, O reader, they would have fallen to Messala had he been the victor.\n\nThe procession was then formed, and, midst the shouting of the multitude which had had its will, passed out of the Gate of Triumph.\n\nAnd the day was over.\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\nBen-Hur tarried across the river with Ilderim; for at midnight, as previously determined, they would take the road which the caravan, then thirty hours out, had pursued.\n\nThe sheik was happy; his offers of gifts had been royal; but Ben-Hur had refused everything, insisting that he was satisfied with the humiliation of his enemy. The generous dispute was long continued.\n\n“Think,” the sheik would say, “what thou hast done for me. In every black tent down to the Akaba and to the ocean, and across to the Euphrates, and beyond to the sea of the Scythians, the renown of my Mira and her children will go; and they who sing of them will magnify me, and forget that I am in the wane of life; and all the spears now masterless will come to me, and my sword-hands multiply past counting. Thou dost not know what it is to have sway of the desert such as will now be mine. I tell thee it will bring tribute incalculable from commerce, and immunity from kings. Ay, by the sword of Solomon! doth my messenger seek favor for me of Cæsar, that will he get. Yet nothing—nothing?”\n\nAnd Ben-Hur would answer,\n\n“Nay, sheik, have I not thy hand and heart? Let thy increase of power and influence inure to the King who comes. Who shall say it was not allowed thee for him? In the work I am going to, I may have great need. Saying no now will leave me to ask of thee with better grace hereafter.”\n\nIn the midst of a controversy of the kind, two messengers arrived—Malluch and one unknown. The former was admitted first.\n\nThe good fellow did not attempt to hide his joy over the event of the day.\n\n“But, coming to that with which I am charged,” he said, “the master Simonides sends me to say that, upon the adjournment of the games, some of the Roman faction made haste to protest against payment of the money prize.”\n\nIlderim started up, crying, in his shrillest tones,\n\n“By the splendor of God! the East shall decide whether the race was fairly won.”\n\n“Nay, good sheik,” said Malluch, “the editor has paid the money.”\n\n“’Tis well.”\n\n“When they said Ben-Hur struck Messala’s wheel, the editor laughed, and reminded them of the blow the Arabs had at the turn of the goal.”\n\n“And what of the Athenian?”\n\n“He is dead.”\n\n“Dead!” cried Ben-Hur.\n\n“Dead!” echoed Ilderim. “What fortune these Roman monsters have! Messala escaped?”\n\n“Escaped—yes, O sheik, with life; but it shall be a burden to him. The physicians say he will live, but never walk again.”\n\nBen-Hur looked silently up to heaven. He had a vision of Messala, chairbound like Simonides, and, like him, going abroad on the shoulders of servants. The good man had abode well; but what would this one with his pride and ambition?\n\n“Simonides bade me say, further,” Malluch continued, “Sanballat is having trouble. Drusus, and those who signed with him, referred the question of paying the five talents they lost to the Consul Maxentius, and he has referred it to Cæsar. Messala also refused his losses, and Sanballat, in imitation of Drusus, went to the consul, where the matter is still in advisement. The better Romans say the protestants shall not be excused; and all the adverse factions join with them. The city rings with the scandal.”\n\n“What says Simonides?” asked Ben-Hur.\n\n“The master laughs, and is well pleased. If the Roman pays, he is ruined; if he refuses to pay, he is dishonored. The imperial policy will decide the matter. To offend the East would be a bad beginning with the Parthians; to offend Sheik Ilderim would be to antagonize the Desert, over which lie all Maxentius’s lines of operation. Wherefore Simonides bade me tell you to have no disquiet; Messala will pay.”\n\nIlderim was at once restored to his good-humor.\n\n“Let us be off now,” he said, rubbing his hands. “The business will do well with Simonides. The glory is ours. I will order the horses.”\n\n“Stay,” said Malluch. “I left a messenger outside. Will you see him?”\n\n“By the splendor of God! I forgot him.”\n\nMalluch retired, and was succeeded by a lad of gentle manners and delicate appearance, who knelt upon one knee, and said, winningly, “Iras, the daughter of Balthasar, well known to good Sheik Ilderim, hath intrusted me with a message to the sheik, who, she saith, will do her great favor so he receive her congratulations on account of the victory of his four.”\n\n“The daughter of my friend is kind,” said Ilderim, with sparkling eyes. “Do thou give her this jewel, in sign of the pleasure I have from her message.”\n\nHe took a ring from his finger as he spoke.\n\n“I will as thou sayest, O sheik,” the lad replied, and continued, “The daughter of the Egyptian charged me further. She prays the good Sheik Ilderim to send word to the youth Ben-Hur that her father hath taken residence for a time in the palace of Idernee, where she will receive the youth after the fourth hour to-morrow. And if, with her congratulations, Sheik Ilderim will accept her gratitude for this other favor done, she will be ever so pleased.”\n\nThe sheik looked at Ben-Hur, whose face was suffused with pleasure.\n\n“What will you?” he asked.\n\n“By your leave, O sheik, I will see the fair Egyptian.”\n\nIlderim laughed, and said, “Shall not a man enjoy his youth?”\n\nThen Ben-Hur answered the messenger.\n\n“Say to her who sent you that I, Ben-Hur, will see her at the palace of Idernee, wherever that may be, to-morrow at noon.”\n\nThe lad arose, and, with silent salute, departed.\n\nAt midnight Ilderim took the road, having arranged to leave a horse and a guide for Ben-Hur, who was to follow him.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\nGoing next day to fill his appointment with Iras, Ben-Hur turned from the Omphalus, which was in the heart of the city, into the Colonnade of Herod, and came shortly to the palace of Idernee.\n\nFrom the street he passed first into a vestibule, on the sides of which were stairways under cover, leading up to a portico. Winged lions sat by the stairs; in the middle there was a gigantic ibis spouting water over the floor; the lions, ibis, walls, and floor were reminders of the Egyptians: everything, even the balustrading of the stairs, was of massive gray stone.\n\nAbove the vestibule, and covering the landing of the steps, arose the portico, a pillared grace, so light, so exquisitely proportioned, it was at that period hardly possible of conception except by a Greek. Of marble snowy white, its effect was that of a lily dropped carelessly upon a great bare rock.\n\nBen-Hur paused in the shade of the portico to admire its tracery and finish, and the purity of its marble; then he passed on into the palace. Ample folding-doors stood open to receive him. The passage into which he first entered was high, but somewhat narrow; red tiling formed the floor, and the walls were tinted to correspond. Yet this plainness was a warning of something beautiful to come.\n\nHe moved on slowly, all his faculties in repose. Presently he would be in the presence of Iras; she was waiting for him; waiting with song and story and badinage, sparkling, fanciful, capricious—with smiles which glorified her glance, and glances which lent voluptuous suggestion to her whisper. She had sent for him the evening of the boat-ride on the lake in the Orchard of Palms; she had sent for him now; and he was going to her in the beautiful palace of Idernee. He was happy and dreamful rather than thoughtless.\n\nThe passage brought him to a closed door, in front of which he paused; and, as he did so, the broad leaves began to open of themselves, without creak or sound of lock or latch, or touch of foot or finger. The singularity was lost in the view that broke upon him.\n\nStanding in the shade of the dull passage, and looking through the doorway, he beheld the atrium of a Roman house, roomy and rich to a fabulous degree of magnificence.\n\nHow large the chamber was cannot be stated, because of the deceit there is in exact proportions; its depth was vista-like, something never to be said of an equal interior. When he stopped to make survey, and looked down upon the floor, he was standing upon the breast of a Leda, represented as caressing a swan; and, looking farther, he saw the whole floor was similarly laid in mosaic pictures of mythological subjects. And there were stools and chairs, each a separate design, and a work of art exquisitely composed, and tables much carven, and here and there couches which were invitations of themselves. The articles of furniture, which stood out from the walls, were duplicated on the floor distinctly as if they floated unrippled water; even the panelling of the walls, the figures upon them in painting and bas-relief, and the fresco of the ceiling were reflected on the floor. The ceiling curved up towards the centre, where there was an opening through which the sunlight poured without hindrance, and the sky, ever so blue, seemed in hand-reach; the impluvium under the opening was guarded by bronzed rails; the gilded pillars supporting the roof at the edges of the opening shone like flame where the sun struck them, and their reflections beneath seemed to stretch to infinite depth. And there were candelabra quaint and curious, and statuary and vases; the whole making an interior that would have befitted well the house on the Palatine Hill which Cicero bought of Crassus, or that other, yet more famous for extravagance, the Tusculan villa of Scaurus.\n\nStill in his dreamful mood, Ben-Hur sauntered about, charmed by all he beheld, and waiting. He did not mind a little delay; when Iras was ready, she would come or send a servant. In every well-regulated Roman house the atrium was the reception chamber for visitors.\n\nTwice, thrice, he made the round. As often he stood under the opening in the roof, and pondered the sky and its azure depth; then, leaning against a pillar, he studied the distribution of light and shade, and its effects; here a veil diminishing objects, there a brilliance exaggerating others; yet nobody came. Time, or rather the passage of time, began at length to impress itself upon him, and he wondered why Iras stayed so long. Again he traced out the figures upon the floor, but not with the satisfaction the first inspection gave him. He paused often to listen: directly impatience blew a little fevered breath upon his spirit; next time it blew stronger and hotter; and at last he woke to a consciousness of the silence which held the house in thrall, and the thought of it made him uneasy and distrustful. Still he put the feeling off with a smile and a promise. “Oh, she is giving the last touch to her eyelids, or she is arranging a chaplet for me; she will come presently, more beautiful of the delay!” He sat down then to admire a candelabrum—a bronze plinth on rollers, filigree on the sides and edges; the post at one end, and on the end opposite it an altar and a female celebrant; the lamp-rests swinging by delicate chains from the extremities of drooping palm-branches; altogether a wonder in its way. But the silence would obtrude itself: he listened even as he looked at the pretty object—he listened, but there was not a sound; the palace was still as a tomb.\n\nThere might be a mistake. No, the messenger had come from the Egyptian, and this was the palace of Idernee. Then he remembered how mysteriously the door had opened so soundlessly, so of itself. He would see!\n\nHe went to the same door. Though he walked ever so lightly the sound of his stepping was loud and harsh, and he shrank from it. He was getting nervous. The cumbrous Roman lock resisted his first effort to raise it; and the second—the blood chilled in his cheeks—he wrenched with all his might: in vain—the door was not even shaken. A sense of danger seized him, and for a moment he stood irresolute.\n\nWho in Antioch had the motive to do him harm?\n\nMessala!\n\nAnd this palace of Idernee? He had seen Egypt in the vestibule, Athens in the snowy portico; but here, in the atrium, was Rome; everything about him betrayed Roman ownership. True, the site was on the great thoroughfare of the city, a very public place in which to do him violence; but for that reason it was more accordant with the audacious genius of his enemy. The atrium underwent a change; with all its elegance and beauty, it was no more than a trap. Apprehension always paints in black.\n\nThe idea irritated Ben-Hur.\n\nThere were many doors on the right and left of the atrium, leading, doubtless, to sleeping-chambers; he tried them, but they were all firmly fastened. Knocking might bring response. Ashamed to make outcry, he betook himself to a couch, and, lying down, tried to reflect.\n\nAll too plainly he was a prisoner; but for what purpose? and by whom?\n\nIf the work were Messala’s! He sat up, looked about, and smiled defiantly. There were weapons in every table. But birds had been starved in golden cages; not so would he—the couches would serve him as battering-rams; and he was strong, and there was such increase of might in rage and despair!\n\nMessala himself could not come. He would never walk again; he was a cripple like Simonides; still he could move others. And where were there not others to be moved by him? Ben-Hur arose, and tried the doors again. Once he called out; the room echoed so that he was startled. With such calmness as he could assume, he made up his mind to wait a time before attempting to break a way out.\n\nIn such a situation the mind has its ebb and flow of disquiet, with intervals of peace between. At length—how long, though, he could not have said—he came to the conclusion that the affair was an accident or mistake. The palace certainly belonged to somebody; it must have care and keeping: and the keeper would come; the evening or the night would bring him. Patience!\n\nSo concluding, he waited.\n\nHalf an hour passed—a much longer period to Ben-Hur—when the door which had admitted him opened and closed noiselessly as before, and without attracting his attention.\n\nThe moment of the occurrence he was sitting at the farther end of the room. A footstep startled him.\n\n“At last she has come!” he thought, with a throb of relief and pleasure, and arose.\n\nThe step was heavy, and accompanied with the gride and clang of coarse sandals. The gilded pillars were between him and the door; he advanced quietly, and leaned against one of them. Presently he heard voices—the voices of men—one of them rough and guttural. What was said he could not understand, as the language was not of the East or South of Europe.\n\nAfter a general survey of the room, the strangers crossed to their left, and were brought into Ben-Hur’s view—two men, one very stout, both tall, and both in short tunics. They had not the air of masters of the house or domestics. Everything they saw appeared wonderful to them; everything they stopped to examine they touched. They were vulgarians. The atrium seemed profaned by their presence. At the same time, their leisurely manner and the assurance with which they proceeded pointed to some right or business; if business, with whom?\n\nWith much jargon they sauntered this way and that, all the time gradually approaching the pillar by which Ben-Hur was standing. Off a little way, where a slanted gleam of the sun fell with a glare upon the mosaic of the floor, there was a statue which attracted their notice. In examining it, they stopped in the light.\n\nThe mystery surrounding his own presence in the palace tended, as we have seen, to make Ben-Hur nervous; so now, when in the tall stout stranger he recognized the Northman whom he had known in Rome, and seen crowned only the day before in the Circus as the winning pugilist; when he saw the man’s face, scarred with the wounds of many battles, and imbruted by ferocious passions; when he surveyed the fellow’s naked limbs, very marvels of exercise and training, and his shoulders of Herculean breadth, a thought of personal danger started a chill along every vein. A sure instinct warned him that the opportunity for murder was too perfect to have come by chance; and here now were the myrmidons, and their business was with him. He turned an anxious eye upon the Northman’s comrade—young, black-eyed, black-haired, and altogether Jewish in appearance; he observed, also, that both the men were in costume exactly such as professionals of their class were in the habit of wearing in the arena. Putting the several circumstances together, Ben-Hur could not be longer in doubt: he had been lured into the palace with design. Out of reach of aid, in this splendid privacy, he was to die!\n\nAt a loss what to do, he gazed from man to man, while there was enacted within him that miracle of mind by which life is passed before us in awful detail, to be looked at by ourselves as if it were another’s; and from the evolvement, from a hidden depth, cast up, as it were, by a hidden hand, he was given to see that he had entered upon a new life, different from the old one in this: whereas, in that, he had been the victim of violences done to him, henceforth he was to be the aggressor. Only yesterday he had found his first victim! To the purely Christian nature the presentation would have brought the weakness of remorse. Not so with Ben-Hur; his spirit had its emotions from the teachings of the first lawgiver, not the last and greatest one. He had dealt punishment, not wrong, to Messala. By permission of the Lord, he had triumphed; and he derived faith from the circumstance—faith the source of all rational strength, especially strength in peril.\n\nNor did the influence stop there. The new life was made appear to him a mission just begun, and holy as the King to come was holy, and certain as the coming of the King was certain—a mission in which force was lawful if only because it was unavoidable. Should he, on the very threshold of such an errand, be afraid?\n\nHe undid the sash around his waist, and, baring his head and casting off his white Jewish gown, stood forth in an undertunic not unlike those of the enemy, and was ready, body and mind. Folding his arms, he placed his back against the pillar, and calmly waited.\n\nThe examination of the statue was brief. Directly the Northman turned, and said something in the unknown tongue; then both looked at Ben-Hur. A few more words, and they advanced towards him.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked, in Latin.\n\nThe Northman fetched a smile which did not relieve his face of its brutalism, and answered,\n\n“Barbarians.”\n\n“This is the palace of Idernee. Whom seek you? Stand and answer.”\n\nThe words were spoken with earnestness. The strangers stopped; and in his turn the Northman asked, “Who are you?”\n\n“A Roman.”\n\nThe giant laid his head back upon his shoulders.\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! I have heard how a god once came from a cow licking a salted stone; but not even a god can make a Roman of a Jew.”\n\nThe laugh over, he spoke to his companion again, and they moved nearer.\n\n“Hold!” said Ben-Hur, quitting the pillar. “One word.”\n\nThey stopped again.\n\n“A word!” replied the Saxon, folding his immense arms across his breast, and relaxing the menace beginning to blacken his face. “A word! Speak.”\n\n“You are Thord the Northman.”\n\nThe giant opened his blue eyes.\n\n“You were lanista in Rome.”\n\n“I was your scholar.”\n\n“No,” said Thord, shaking his head. “By the beard of Irmin, I had never a Jew to make a fighting-man of.”\n\n“But I will prove my saying.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“You came here to kill me.”\n\n“That is true.”\n\n“Then let this man fight me singly, and I will make the proof on his body.”\n\nA gleam of humor shone in the Northman’s face. He spoke to his companion, who made answer; then he replied with the naivete of a diverted child,\n\n“Wait till I say begin.”\n\nBy repeated touches of his foot, he pushed a couch out on the floor, and proceeded leisurely to stretch his burly form upon it; when perfectly at ease, he said, simply, “Now begin.”\n\nWithout ado, Ben-Hur walked to his antagonist.\n\n“Defend thyself,” he said.\n\nThe man, nothing loath, put up his hands.\n\nAs the two thus confronted each other in approved position, there was no discernible inequality between them; on the contrary, they were as like as brothers. To the stranger’s confident smile, Ben-Hur opposed an earnestness which, had his skill been known, would have been accepted fair warning of danger. Both knew the combat was to be mortal.\n\nBen-Hur feinted with his right hand. The stranger warded, slightly advancing his left arm. Ere he could return to guard, Ben-Hur caught him by the wrist in a grip which years at the oar had made terrible as a vise. The surprise was complete, and no time given. To throw himself forward; to push the arm across the man’s throat and over his right shoulder, and turn him left side front; to strike surely with the ready left hand; to strike the bare neck under the ear—were but petty divisions of the same act. No need of a second blow. The myrmidon fell heavily, and without a cry, and lay still.\n\nBen-Hur turned to Thord.\n\n“Ha! What! By the beard of Irmin!” the latter cried, in astonishment, rising to a sitting posture. Then he laughed.\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! I could not have done it better myself.”\n\nHe viewed Ben-Hur coolly from head to foot, and, rising, faced him with undisguised admiration.\n\n“It was my trick—the trick I have practised for ten years in the schools of Rome. You are not a Jew. Who are you?”\n\n“You knew Arrius the duumvir.”\n\n“Quintus Arrius? Yes, he was my patron.”\n\n“He had a son.”\n\n“Yes,” said Thord, his battered features lighting dully, “I knew the boy; he would have made a king gladiator. Cæsar offered him his patronage. I taught him the very trick you played on this one here—a trick impossible except to a hand and arm like mine. It has won me many a crown.”\n\n“I am that son of Arrius.”\n\nThord drew nearer, and viewed him carefully; then his eyes brightened with genuine pleasure, and, laughing, he held out his hand.\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! He told me I would find a Jew here—a Jew—a dog of a Jew—killing whom was serving the gods.”\n\n“Who told you so?” asked Ben-Hur, taking the hand.\n\n“He—Messala—ha, ha, ha!”\n\n“When, Thord?”\n\n“Last night.”\n\n“I thought he was hurt.”\n\n“He will never walk again. On his bed he told me between groans.”\n\nA very vivid portrayal of hate in a few words; and Ben-Hur saw that the Roman, if he lived, would still be capable and dangerous, and follow him unrelentingly. Revenge remained to sweeten the ruined life; therefore the clinging to fortune lost in the wager with Sanballat. Ben-Hur ran the ground over, with a distinct foresight of the many ways in which it would be possible for his enemy to interfere with him in the work he had undertaken for the King who was coming. Why not he resort to the Roman’s methods? The man hired to kill him could be hired to strike back. It was in his power to offer higher wages. The temptation was strong; and, half yielding, he chanced to look down at his late antagonist lying still, with white upturned face, so like himself. A light came to him, and he asked, “Thord, what was Messala to give you for killing me?”\n\n“A thousand sestertii.”\n\n“You shall have them yet; and so you do now what I tell you, I will add three thousand more to the sum.”\n\nThe giant reflected aloud,\n\n“I won five thousand yesterday; from the Roman one—six. Give me four, good Arrius—four more—and I will stand firm for you, though old Thor, my namesake, strike me with his hammer. Make it four, and I will kill the lying patrician, if you say so. I have only to cover his mouth with my hand—thus.”\n\nHe illustrated the process by clapping his hand over his own mouth.\n\n“I see,” said Ben-Hur; “ten thousand sestertii is a fortune. It will enable you to return to Rome, and open a wine-shop near the Great Circus, and live as becomes the first of the lanistae.”\n\nThe very scars on the giant’s face glowed afresh with the pleasure the picture gave him.\n\n“I will make it four thousand,” Ben-Hur continued; “and in what you shall do for the money there will be no blood on your hands, Thord. Hear me now. Did not your friend here look like me?”\n\n“I would have said he was an apple from the same tree.”\n\n“Well, if I put on his tunic, and dress him in these clothes of mine, and you and I go away together, leaving him here, can you not get your sestertii from Messala all the same? You have only to make him believe it me that is dead.”\n\nThord laughed till the tears ran into his mouth.\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! Ten thousand sestertii were never won so easily. And a wine-shop by the Great Circus!—all for a lie without blood in it! Ha, ha, ha! Give me thy hand, O son of Arrius. Get on now, and—ha, ha, ha!—if ever you come to Rome, fail not to ask for the wine-shop of Thord the Northman. By the beard of Irmin, I will give you the best, though I borrow it from Cæsar!”\n\nThey shook hands again; after which the exchange of clothes was effected. It was arranged then that a messenger should go at night to Thord’s lodging-place with the four thousand sestertii. When they were done, the giant knocked at the front door; it opened to him; and, passing out of the atrium, he led Ben-Hur into a room adjoining, where the latter completed his attire from the coarse garments of the dead pugilist. They separated directly in the Omphalus.\n\n“Fail not, O son of Arrius, fail not the wine-shop near the Great Circus! Ha, ha, ha! By the beard of Irmin, there was never fortune gained so cheap. The gods keep you!”\n\nUpon leaving the atrium, Ben-Hur gave a last look at the myrmidon as he lay in the Jewish vestments, and was satisfied. The likeness was striking. If Thord kept faith, the cheat was a secret to endure forever.\n\nAt night, in the house of Simonides, Ben-Hur told the good man all that had taken place in the palace of Idernee; and it was agreed that, after a few days, public inquiry should be set afloat for the discovery of the whereabouts of the son of Arrius. Eventually the matter was to be carried boldly to Maxentius; then, if the mystery came not out, it was concluded that Messala and Gratus would be at rest and happy, and Ben-Hur free to betake himself to Jerusalem, to make search for his lost people.\n\nAt the leave-taking, Simonides sat in his chair out on the terrace overlooking the river, and gave his farewell and the peace of the Lord with the impressment of a father. Esther went with the young man to the head of the steps.\n\n“If I find my mother, Esther, thou shalt go to her at Jerusalem, and be a sister to Tirzah.”\n\nAnd with the words he kissed her.\n\nWas it only a kiss of peace?\n\nHe crossed the river next to the late quarters of Ilderim, where he found the Arab who was to serve him as guide. The horses were brought out.\n\n“This one is thine,” said the Arab.\n\nBen-Hur looked, and, lo! it was Aldebaran, the swiftest and brightest of the sons of Mira, and, next to Sirius, the beloved of the sheik; and he knew the old man’s heart came to him along with the gift.\n\nThe corpse in the atrium was taken up and buried by night; and, as part of Messala’s plan, a courier was sent off to Gratus to make him at rest by the announcement of Ben-Hur’s death—this time past question.\n\nEre long a wine-shop was opened near the Circus Maximus, with inscription over the door:\n\n# BOOK SIXTH\n\n“Is that a Death? and are there two? Is Death that woman’s mate?\n\nHer skin was as white as leprosy, The Nightmare Life-in-Death was she, Who thicks man’s blood with cold.”\n\n# COLERIDGE. CHAPTER I\n\nOur story moves forward now thirty days from the night Ben-Hur left Antioch to go out with Sheik Ilderim into the desert.\n\nA great change has befallen—great at least as respects the fortunes of our hero. Valerius Gratus has been succeeded by Pontius Pilate!\n\nThe removal, it may be remarked, cost Simonides exactly five talents Roman money in hand paid to Sejanus, who was then in height of power as imperial favorite; the object being to help Ben-Hur, by lessening his exposure while in and about Jerusalem attempting discovery of his people. To such pious use the faithful servant put the winnings from Drusus and his associates; all of whom, having paid their wagers, became at once and naturally the enemies of Messala, whose repudiation was yet an unsettled question in Rome.\n\nBrief as the time was, already the Jews knew the change of rulers was not for the better.\n\nThe cohorts sent to relieve the garrison of Antonia made their entry into the city by night; next morning the first sight that greeted the people resident in the neighborhood was the walls of the old Tower decorated with military ensigns, which unfortunately consisted of busts of the emperor mixed with eagles and globes. A multitude, in passion, marched to Cæsarea, where Pilate was lingering, and implored him to remove the detested images. Five days and nights they beset his palace gates; at last he appointed a meeting with them in the Circus. When they were assembled, he encircled them with soldiers; instead of resisting, they offered him their lives, and conquered. He recalled the images and ensigns to Cæsarea, where Gratus, with more consideration, had kept such abominations housed during the eleven years of his reign.\n\nThe worst of men do once in a while vary their wickednesses by good acts; so with Pilate. He ordered an inspection of all the prisons in Judea, and a return of the names of the persons in custody, with a statement of the crimes for which they had been committed. Doubtless, the motive was the one so common with officials just installed—dread of entailed responsibility; the people, however, in thought of the good which might come of the measure, gave him credit, and, for a period, were comforted. The revelations were astonishing. Hundreds of persons were released against whom there were no accusations; many others came to light who had long been accounted dead; yet more amazing, there was opening of dungeons not merely unknown at the time by the people, but actually forgotten by the prison authorities. With one instance of the latter kind we have now to deal; and, strange to say, it occurred in Jerusalem.\n\nThe Tower of Antonia, which will be remembered as occupying two thirds of the sacred area on Mount Moriah, was originally a castle built by the Macedonians. Afterwards, John Hyrcanus erected the castle into a fortress for the defence of the Temple, and in his day it was considered impregnable to assault; but when Herod came with his bolder genius, he strengthened its walls and extended them, leaving a vast pile which included every appurtenance necessary for the stronghold he intended it to be forever; such as offices, barracks, armories, magazines, cisterns, and last, though not least, prisons of all grades. He levelled the solid rock, and tapped it with deep excavations, and built over them; connecting the whole great mass with the Temple by a beautiful colonnade, from the roof of which one could look down over the courts of the sacred structure. In such condition the Tower fell at last out of his hands into those of the Romans, who were quick to see its strength and advantages, and convert it to uses becoming such masters. All through the administration of Gratus it had been a garrisoned citadel and underground prison terrible to revolutionists. Woe when the cohorts poured from its gates to suppress disorder! Woe not less when a Jew passed the same gates going in under arrest!\n\nWith this explanation, we hasten to our story.\n\nThe order of the new procurator requiring a report of the persons in custody was received at the Tower of Antonia, and promptly executed; and two days have gone since the last unfortunate was brought up for examination. The tabulated statement, ready for forwarding, lies on the table of the tribune in command; in five minutes more it will be on the way to Pilate, sojourning in the palace up on Mount Zion.\n\nThe tribune’s office is spacious and cool, and furnished in a style suitable to the dignity of the commandant of a post in every respect so important. Looking in upon him about the seventh hour of the day, the officer appears weary and impatient; when the report is despatched, he will to the roof of the colonnade for air and exercise, and the amusement to be had watching the Jews over in the courts of the Temple. His subordinates and clerks share his impatience.\n\nIn the spell of waiting a man appeared in a doorway leading to an adjoining apartment. He rattled a bunch of keys, each heavy as a hammer, and at once attracted the chief’s attention.\n\n“Ah, Gesius! come in,” the tribune said.\n\nAs the new-comer approached the table behind which the chief sat in an easy-chair, everybody present looked at him, and, observing a certain expression of alarm and mortification on his face, became silent that they might hear what he had to say.\n\n“O tribune!” he began, bending low, “I fear to tell what now I bring you.”\n\n“Another mistake—ha, Gesius?”\n\n“If I could persuade myself it is but a mistake, I would not be afraid.”\n\n“A crime then—or, worse, a breach of duty. Thou mayst laugh at Cæsar, or curse the gods, and live; but if the offence be to the eagles—ah, thou knowest, Gesius—go on!”\n\n“It is now about eight years since Valerius Gratus selected me to be keeper of prisoners here in the Tower,” said the man, deliberately. “I remember the morning I entered upon the duties of my office. There had been a riot the day before, and fighting in the streets. We slew many Jews, and suffered on our side. The affair came, it was said, of an attempt to assassinate Gratus, who had been knocked from his horse by a tile thrown from a roof. I found him sitting where you now sit, O tribune, his head swathed in bandages. He told me of my selection, and gave me these keys, numbered to correspond with the numbers of the cells; they were the badges of my office, he said, and not to be parted with. There was a roll of parchment on the table. Calling me to him, he opened the roll. ‘Here are maps of the cells,’ said he. There were three of them. ‘This one,’ he went on, ‘shows the arrangement of the upper floor; this second one gives you the second floor; and this last is of the lower floor. I give them to you in trust.’ I took them from his hand, and he said, further, ‘Now you have the keys and the maps; go immediately, and acquaint yourself with the whole arrangement; visit each cell, and see to its condition. When anything is needed for the security of a prisoner, order it according to your judgment, for you are the master under me, and no other.’\n\n“I saluted him, and turned to go away; he called me back. ‘Ah, I forgot,’ he said. ‘Give me the map of the third floor.’ I gave it to him, and he spread it upon the table. ‘Here, Gesius,’ he said, ‘see this cell.’ He laid his finger on the one numbered V. ‘There are three men confined in that cell, desperate characters, who by some means got hold of a state secret, and suffer for their curiosity, which’—he looked at me severely—‘in such matters is worse than a crime. Accordingly, they are blind and tongueless, and are placed there for life. They shall have nothing but food and drink, to be given them through a hole, which you will find in the wall covered by a slide. Do you hear, Gesius?’ I made him answer. ‘It is well,’ he continued. ‘One thing more which you shall not forget, or’—he looked at me threateningly—‘The door of their cell—cell number V. on the same floor—this one, Gesius’—he put his finger on the particular cell to impress my memory—‘shall never be opened for any purpose, neither to let one in nor out, not even yourself.’ ‘But if they die?’ I asked. ‘If they die,’ he said, ‘the cell shall be their tomb. They were put there to die, and be lost. The cell is leprous. Do you understand?’ With that he let me go.”\n\nGesius stopped, and from the breast of his tunic drew three parchments, all much yellowed by time and use; selecting one of them, he spread it upon the table before the tribune, saying, simply, “This is the lower floor.”\n\nThe whole company looked at\n\n_________________________________________ | | | Passage | | | |--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--| | | | | | | | V | IV | III | II | I | |_____|______|______|______|______|\n\n“This is exactly, O tribune, as I had it from Gratus. See, there is cell number V.,” said Gesius.\n\n“I see,” the tribune replied. “Go on now. The cell was leprous, he said.”\n\n“I would like to ask you a question,” remarked the keeper, modestly.\n\nThe tribune assented.\n\n“Had I not a right, under the circumstances, to believe the map a true one?”\n\n“What else couldst thou?”\n\n“Well, it is not a true one.”\n\nThe chief looked up surprised.\n\n“It is not a true one,” the keeper repeated. “It shows but five cells upon that floor, while there are six.”\n\n“Six, sayest thou?”\n\n“I will show you the floor as it is—or as I believe it to be.”\n\nUpon a page of his tablets, Gesius drew the following diagram, and gave it to the tribune:\n\n_________________________________________ | | |--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--| | | | | | | | V | IV | III | II | I | |--][---+--------+--------+--------+-------| | VI | |_________________________________________|\n\n“Thou hast done well,” said the tribune, examining the drawing, and thinking the narrative at an end. “I will have the map corrected, or, better, I will have a new one made, and given thee. Come for it in the morning.”\n\nSo saying, he arose.\n\n“But hear me further, O tribune.”\n\n“To-morrow, Gesius, to-morrow.”\n\n“That which I have yet to tell will not wait.”\n\nThe tribune good-naturedly resumed his chair.\n\n“I will hurry,” said the keeper, humbly, “only let me ask another question. Had I not a right to believe Gratus in what he further told me as to the prisoners in cell number V.?”\n\n“Yes, it was thy duty to believe there were three prisoners in the cell—prisoners of state—blind and without tongues.”\n\n“Well,” said the keeper, “that was not true either.”\n\n“No!” said the tribune, with returning interest.\n\n“Hear, and judge for yourself, O tribune. As required, I visited all the cells, beginning with those on the first floor, and ending with those on the lower. The order that the door of number V. should not be opened had been respected; through all the eight years food and drink for three men had been passed through a hole in the wall. I went to the door yesterday, curious to see the wretches who, against all expectation, had lived so long. The locks refused the key. We pulled a little, and the door fell down, rusted from its hinges. Going in, I found but one man, old, blind, tongueless, and naked. His hair dropped in stiffened mats below his waist. His skin was like the parchment there. He held his hands out, and the finger-nails curled and twisted like the claws of a bird. I asked him where his companions were. He shook his head in denial. Thinking to find the others, we searched the cell. The floor was dry; so were the walls. If three men had been shut in there, and two of them had died, at least their bones would have endured.”\n\n“Wherefore thou thinkest—”\n\n“I think, O tribune, there has been but one prisoner there in the eight years.”\n\nThe chief regarded the keeper sharply, and said, “Have a care; thou art more than saying Valerius lied.”\n\nGesius bowed, but said, “He might have been mistaken.”\n\n“No, he was right,” said the tribune, warmly. “By thine own statement he was right. Didst thou not say but now that for eight years food and drink had been furnished three men?”\n\nThe bystanders approved the shrewdness of their chief; yet Gesius did not seem discomfited.\n\n“You have but half the story, O tribune. When you have it all, you will agree with me. You know what I did with the man: that I sent him to the bath, and had him shorn and clothed, and then took him to the gate of the Tower, and bade him go free. I washed my hands of him. To-day he came back, and was brought to me. By signs and tears he at last made me understand he wished to return to his cell, and I so ordered. As they were leading him off, he broke away and kissed my feet, and, by piteous dumb imploration, insisted I should go with him; and I went. The mystery of the three men stayed in my mind. I was not satisfied about it. Now I am glad I yielded to his entreaty.”\n\nThe whole company at this point became very still.\n\n“When we were in the cell again, and the prisoner knew it, he caught my hand eagerly, and led me to a hole like that through which we were accustomed to pass him his food. Though large enough to push your helmet through, it escaped me yesterday. Still holding my hand, he put his face to the hole and gave a beast-like cry. A sound came faintly back. I was astonished, and drew him away, and called out, ‘Ho, here!’ At first there was no answer. I called again, and received back these words, ‘Be thou praised, O Lord!’ Yet more astonishing, O tribune, the voice was a woman’s. And I asked, ‘Who are you?’ and had reply, ‘A woman of Israel, entombed here with her daughter. Help us quickly, or we die.’ I told them to be of cheer, and hurried here to know your will.”\n\nThe tribune arose hastily.\n\n“Thou wert right, Gesius,” he said, “and I see now. The map was a lie, and so was the tale of the three men. There have been better Romans than Valerius Gratus.”\n\n“Yes,” said the keeper. “I gleaned from the prisoner that he had regularly given the women of the food and drink he had received.”\n\n“It is accounted for,” replied the tribune, and observing the countenances of his friends, and reflecting how well it would be to have witnesses, he added, “Let us rescue the women. Come all.”\n\nGesuis was pleased.\n\n“We will have to pierce the wall,” he said. “I found where a door had been, but it was filled solidly with stones and mortar.”\n\nThe tribune stayed to say to a clerk, “Send workmen after me with tools. Make haste; but hold the report, for I see it will have to be corrected.”\n\nIn a short time they were gone.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“A woman of Israel, entombed here with her daughter. Help us quickly, or we die.”\n\nSuch was the reply Gesius, the keeper, had from the cell which appears on his amended map as VI. The reader, when he observed the answer, knew who the unfortunates were, and, doubtless, said to himself, “At last the mother of Ben-Hur, and Tirzah, his sister!”\n\nAnd so it was.\n\nThe morning of their seizure, eight years before, they had been carried to the Tower, where Gratus proposed to put them out of the way. He had chosen the Tower for the purpose as more immediately in his own keeping, and cell VI. because, first, it could be better lost than any other; and, secondly, it was infected with leprosy; for these prisoners were not merely to be put in a safe place, but in a place to die. They were, accordingly, taken down by slaves in the night-time, when there were no witnesses of the deed; then, in completion of the savage task, the same slaves walled up the door, after which they were themselves separated, and sent away never to be heard of more. To save accusation, and, in the event of discovery, to leave himself such justification as might be allowed in a distinction between the infliction of a punishment and the commission of a double murder, Gratus preferred sinking his victims where natural death was certain, though slow. That they might linger along, he selected a convict who had been made blind and tongueless, and sank him in the only connecting cell, there to serve them with food and drink. Under no circumstances could the poor wretch tell the tale or identify either the prisoners or their doomsman. So, with a cunning partly due to Messala, the Roman, under color of punishing a brood of assassins, smoothed a path to confiscation of the estate of the Hurs, of which no portion ever reached the imperial coffers.\n\nAs the last step in the scheme, Gratus summarily removed the old keeper of the prisons; not because he knew what had been done—for he did not—but because, knowing the underground floors as he did, it would be next to impossible to keep the transaction from him. Then, with masterly ingenuity, the procurator had new maps drawn for delivery to a new keeper, with the omission, as we have seen, of cell VI. The instructions given the latter, taken with the omission on the map, accomplished the design—the cell and its unhappy tenants were all alike lost.\n\nWhat may be thought of the life of the mother and daughter during the eight years must have relation to their culture and previous habits. Conditions are pleasant or grievous to us according to our sensibilities. It is not extreme to say, if there was a sudden exit of all men from the world, heaven, as prefigured in the Christian idea, would not be a heaven to the majority; on the other hand, neither would all suffer equally in the so-called Tophet. Cultivation has its balances. As the mind is made intelligent, the capacity of the soul for pure enjoyment is proportionally increased. Well, therefore, if it be saved! If lost, however, alas that it ever had cultivation! its capacity for enjoyment in the one case is the measure of its capacity to suffer in the other. Wherefore repentance must be something more than mere remorse for sins; it comprehends a change of nature befitting heaven.\n\nWe repeat, to form an adequate idea of the suffering endured by the mother of Ben-Hur, the reader must think of her spirit and its sensibilities as much as, if not more than, of the conditions of the immurement; the question being, not what the conditions were, but how she was affected by them. And now we may be permitted to say it was in anticipation of this thought that the scene in the summer-house on the roof of the family palace was given so fully in the beginning of the Second Book of our story. So, too, to be helpful when the inquiry should come up, we ventured the elaborate description of the palace of the Hurs.\n\nIn other words, let the serene, happy, luxurious life in the princely house be recalled and contrasted with this existence in the lower dungeon of the Tower of Antonia; then if the reader, in his effort to realize the misery of the woman, persists in mere reference to conditions physical, he cannot go amiss; as he is a lover of his kind, tender of heart, he will be melted with much sympathy. But will he go further; will he more than sympathize with her; will he share her agony of mind and spirit; will he at least try to measure it—let him recall her as she discoursed to her son of God and nations and heroes; one moment a philosopher, the next a teacher, and all the time a mother.\n\nWould you hurt a man keenest, strike at his self-love; would you hurt a woman worst, aim at her affections.\n\nWith quickened remembrance of these unfortunates—remembrance of them as they were—let us go down and see them as they are.\n\nThe cell VI. was in form as Gesius drew it on his map. Of its dimensions but little idea can be had; enough that it was a roomy, roughened interior, with ledged and broken walls and floor.\n\nIn the beginning, the site of the Macedonian Castle was separated from the site of the Temple by a narrow but deep cliff somewhat in shape of a wedge. The workmen, wishing to hew out a series of chambers, made their entry in the north face of the cleft, and worked in, leaving a ceiling of the natural stone; delving farther, they executed the cells V., IV., III., II., I., with no connection with number VI. except through number V. In like manner, they constructed the passage and stairs to the floor above. The process of the work was precisely that resorted to in carving out the Tombs of the Kings, yet to be seen a short distance north of Jerusalem; only when the cutting was done, cell VI. was enclosed on its outer side by a wall of prodigious stones, in which, for ventilation, narrow apertures were left bevelled like modern port-holes. Herod, when he took hold of the Temple and Tower, put a facing yet more massive upon this outer wall, and shut up all the apertures but one, which yet admitted a little vitalizing air, and a ray of light not nearly strong enough to redeem the room from darkness.\n\nSuch was cell VI.\n\nStartle not now!\n\nThe description of the blind and tongueless wretch just liberated from cell V. may be accepted to break the horror of what is coming.\n\nThe two women are grouped close by the aperture; one is seated, the other is half reclining against her; there is nothing between them and the bare rock. The light, slanting upwards, strikes them with ghastly effect, and we cannot avoid seeing they are without vesture or covering. At the same time we are helped to the knowledge that love is there yet, for the two are in each other’s arms. Riches take wings, comforts vanish, hope withers away, but love stays with us. Love is God.\n\nWhere the two are thus grouped the stony floor is polished shining smooth. Who shall say how much of the eight years they have spent in that space there in front of the aperture, nursing their hope of rescue by that timid yet friendly ray of light? When the brightness came creeping in, they knew it was dawn; when it began to fade, they knew the world was hushing for the night, which could not be anywhere so long and utterly dark as with them. The world! Through that crevice, as if it were broad and high as a king’s gate, they went to the world in thought, and passed the weary time going up and down as spirits go, looking and asking, the one for her son, the other for her brother. On the seas they sought him, and on the islands of the seas; to-day he was in this city, to-morrow in that other; and everywhere, and at all times, he was a flitting sojourner; for, as they lived waiting for him, he lived looking for them. How often their thoughts passed each other in the endless search, his coming, theirs going! It was such sweet flattery for them to say to each other, “While he lives, we shall not be forgotten; as long as he remembers us, there is hope!” The strength one can eke from little, who knows till he has been subjected to the trial?\n\nOur recollections of them in former days enjoin us to be respectful; their sorrows clothe them with sanctity. Without going too near, across the dungeon, we see they have undergone a change of appearance not to be accounted for by time or long confinement. The mother was beautiful as a woman, the daughter beautiful as a child; not even love could say so much now. Their hair is long, unkempt, and strangely white; they make us shrink and shudder with an indefinable repulsion, though the effect may be from an illusory glozing of the light glimmering dismally through the unhealthy murk; or they may be enduring the tortures of hunger and thirst, not having had to eat or drink since their servant, the convict, was taken away—that is, since yesterday.\n\nTirzah, reclining against her mother in half embrace, moans piteously.\n\n“Be quiet, Tirzah. They will come. God is good. We have been mindful of him, and forgotten not to pray at every sounding of the trumpets over in the Temple. The light, you see, is still bright; the sun is standing in the south sky yet, and it is hardly more than the seventh hour. Somebody will come to us. Let us have faith. God is good.”\n\nThus the mother. The words were simple and effective, although, eight years being now to be added to the thirteen she had attained when last we saw her, Tirzah was no longer a child.\n\n“I will try and be strong, mother,” she said. “Your suffering must be as great as mine; and I do so want to live for you and my brother! But my tongue burns, my lips scorch. I wonder where he is, and if he will ever, ever find us!”\n\nThere is something in the voices that strikes us singularly—an unexpected tone, sharp, dry, metallic, unnatural.\n\nThe mother draws the daughter closer to her breast, and says, “I dreamed about him last night, and saw him as plainly, Tirzah, as I see you. We must believe in dreams, you know, because our fathers did. The Lord spoke to them so often in that way. I thought we were in the Women’s Court just before the Gate Beautiful; there were many women with us; and he came and stood in the shade of the Gate, and looked here and there, at this one and that. My heart beat strong. I knew he was looking for us, and stretched my arms to him, and ran, calling him. He heard me and saw me, but he did not know me. In a moment he was gone.”\n\n“Would it not be so, mother, if we were to meet him in fact? We are so changed.”\n\n“It might be so; but—” The mother’s head droops, and her face knits as with a wrench of pain; recovering, however, she goes on—“but we could make ourselves known to him.”\n\nTirzah tossed her arms, and moaned again.\n\n“Water, mother, water, though but a drop.”\n\nThe mother stares around in blank helplessness. She has named God so often, and so often promised in his name, the repetition is beginning to have a mocking effect upon herself. A shadow passes before her dimming the dim light, and she is brought down to think of death as very near, waiting to come in as her faith goes out. Hardly knowing what she does, speaking aimlessly, because speak she must, she says again,\n\n“Patience, Tirzah; they are coming—they are almost here.”\n\nShe thought she heard a sound over by the little trap in the partition-wall through which they held all their actual communication with the world. And she was not mistaken. A moment, and the cry of the convict rang through the cell. Tirzah heard it also; and they both arose, still keeping hold of each other.\n\n“Praised be the Lord forever!” exclaimed the mother, with the fervor of restored faith and hope.\n\n“Ho, there!” they heard next; and then, “Who are you?”\n\nThe voice was strange. What matter? Except from Tirzah, they were the first and only words the mother had heard in eight years. The revulsion was mighty—from death to life—and so instantly!\n\n“A woman of Israel, entombed here with her daughter. Help us quickly, or we die.”\n\n“Be of cheer. I will return.”\n\nThe women sobbed aloud. They were found; help was coming. From wish to wish hope flew as the twittering swallows fly. They were found; they would be released. And restoration would follow—restoration to all they had lost—home, society, property, son and brother! The scanty light glozed them with the glory of day, and, forgetful of pain and thirst and hunger, and of the menace of death, they sank upon the floor and cried, keeping fast hold of each other the while.\n\nAnd this time they had not long to wait. Gesius, the keeper, told his tale methodically, but finished it at last. The tribune was prompt.\n\n“Within there!” he shouted through the trap.\n\n“Here!” said the mother, rising.\n\nDirectly she heard another sound in another place, as of blows on the wall—blows quick, ringing, and delivered with iron tools. She did not speak, nor did Tirzah, but they listened, well knowing the meaning of it all—that a way to liberty was being made for them. So men a long time buried in deep mines hear the coming of rescuers, heralded by thrust of bar and beat of pick, and answer gratefully with heart-throbs, their eyes fixed upon the spot whence the sounds proceed; and they cannot look away, lest the work should cease, and they be returned to despair.\n\nThe arms outside were strong, the hands skillful, the will good. Each instant the blows sounded more plainly; now and then a piece fell with a crash; and liberty came nearer and nearer. Presently the workmen could be heard speaking. Then—O happiness!—through a crevice flashed a red ray of torches. Into the darkness it cut incisive as diamond brilliance, beautiful as if from a spear of the morning.\n\n“It is he, mother, it is he! He has found us at last!” cried Tirzah, with the quickened fancy of youth.\n\nBut the mother answered meekly, “God is good!”\n\nA block fell inside, and another—then a great mass, and the door was open. A man grimed with mortar and stone-dust stepped in, and stopped, holding a torch over his head. Two or three others followed with torches, and stood aside for the tribune to enter.\n\nRespect for women is not all a conventionality, for it is the best proof of their proper nature. The tribune stopped, because they fled from him—not with fear, be it said, but shame; nor yet, O reader, from shame alone! From the obscurity of their partial hiding he heard these words, the saddest, most dreadful, most utterly despairing of the human tongue:\n\n“Come not near us—unclean, unclean!”\n\nThe men flared their torches while they stared at each other.\n\n“Unclean, unclean!” came from the corner again, a slow tremulous wail exceedingly sorrowful. With such a cry we can imagine a spirit vanishing from the gates of Paradise, looking back the while.\n\nSo the widow and mother performed her duty, and in the moment realized that the freedom she had prayed for and dreamed of, fruit of scarlet and gold seen afar, was but an apple of Sodom in the hand.\n\n# SHE AND TIRZAH WERE—LEPERS!\n\nPossibly the reader does not know all the word means. Let him be told it with reference to the Law of that time, only a little modified in this.\n\n“These four are accounted as dead—the blind, the leper, the poor, and the childless.” Thus the Talmud.\n\nThat is, to be a leper was to be treated as dead—to be excluded from the city as a corpse; to be spoken to by the best beloved and most loving only at a distance; to dwell with none but lepers; to be utterly unprivileged; to be denied the rites of the Temple and the synagogue; to go about in rent garments and with covered mouth, except when crying, “Unclean, unclean!” to find home in the wilderness or in abandoned tombs; to become a materialized specter of Hinnom and Gehenna; to be at all times less a living offence to others than a breathing torment to self; afraid to die, yet without hope except in death.\n\nOnce—she might not tell the day or the year, for down in the haunted hell even time was lost—once the mother felt a dry scurf in the palm of her right hand, a trifle which she tried to wash away. It clung to the member pertinaciously; yet she thought but little of the sign till Tirzah complained that she, too, was attacked in the same way. The supply of water was scant, and they denied themselves drink that they might use it as a curative. At length the whole hand was attacked; the skin cracked open, the fingernails loosened from the flesh. There was not much pain withal, chiefly a steadily increasing discomfort. Later their lips began to parch and seam. One day the mother, who was cleanly to godliness, and struggled against the impurities of the dungeon with all ingenuity, thinking the enemy was taking hold on Tirzah’s face, led her to the light, and, looking with the inspiration of a terrible dread, lo! the young girl’s eyebrows were white as snow.\n\nOh, the anguish of that assurance!\n\nThe mother sat awhile speechless, motionless, paralyzed of soul, and capable of but one thought—leprosy, leprosy!\n\nWhen she began to think, mother-like, it was not of herself, but her child, and, mother-like, her natural tenderness turned to courage, and she made ready for the last sacrifice of perfect heroism. She buried her knowledge in her heart; hopeless herself, she redoubled her devotion to Tirzah, and with wonderful ingenuity—wonderful chiefly in its very inexhaustibility—continued to keep the daughter ignorant of what they were beset with, and even hopeful that it was nothing. She repeated her little games, and retold her stories, and invented new ones, and listened with ever so much pleasure to the songs she would have from Tirzah, while on her own wasting lips the psalms of the singing king and their race served to bring soothing of forgetfulness, and keep alive in them both the recollection of the God who would seem to have abandoned them—the world not more lightly or utterly.\n\nSlowly, steadily, with horrible certainty, the disease spread, after a while bleaching their heads white, eating holes in their lips and eyelids, and covering their bodies with scales; then it fell to their throats shrilling their voices, and to their joints, hardening the tissues and cartilages—slowly, and, as the mother well knew, past remedy, it was affecting their lungs and arteries and bones, at each advance making the sufferers more and more loathsome; and so it would continue till death, which might be years before them.\n\nAnother day of dread at length came—the day the mother, under impulsion of duty, at last told Tirzah the name of their ailment; and the two, in agony of despair, prayed that the end might come quickly.\n\nStill, as is the force of habit, these so afflicted grew in time not merely to speak composedly of their disease; they beheld the hideous transformation of their persons as of course, and in despite clung to existence. One tie to earth remained to them; unmindful of their own loneliness, they kept up a certain spirit by talking and dreaming of Ben-Hur. The mother promised reunion with him to the sister, and she to the mother, not doubting, either of them, that he was equally faithful to them, and would be equally happy of the meeting. And with the spinning and respinning of this slender thread they found pleasure, and excused their not dying. In such manner as we have seen, they were solacing themselves the moment Gesius called them, at the end of twelve hours’ fasting and thirst.\n\nThe torches flashed redly through the dungeon, and liberty was come. “God is good,” the widow cried—not for what had been, O reader, but for what was. In thankfulness for present mercy, nothing so becomes us as losing sight of past ills.\n\nThe tribune came directly; then in the corner to which she had fled, suddenly a sense of duty smote the elder of the women, and straightway the awful warning—\n\n“Unclean, unclean!”\n\nAh, the pang the effort to acquit herself of that duty cost the mother! Not all the selfishness of joy over the prospect could keep her blind to the consequences of release, now that it was at hand. The old happy life could never be again. If she went near the house called home, it would be to stop at the gate and cry, “Unclean, unclean!” She must go about with the yearnings of love alive in her breast strong as ever, and more sensitive even, because return in kind could not be. The boy of whom she had so constantly thought, and with all sweet promises such as mothers find their purest delight in, must, at meeting her, stand afar off. If he held out his hands to her, and called “Mother, mother,” for very love of him she must answer, “Unclean, unclean!” And this other child, before whom, in want of other covering, she was spreading her long tangled locks, bleached unnaturally white—ah! that she was she must continue, sole partner of her blasted remainder of life. Yet, O reader, the brave woman accepted the lot, and took up the cry which had been its sign immemorially, and which thenceforward was to be her salutation without change—“Unclean, unclean!”\n\nThe tribune heard it with a tremor, but kept his place.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked.\n\n“Two women dying of hunger and thirst. Yet”—the mother did not falter—“come not near us, nor touch the floor or the wall. Unclean, unclean!”\n\n“Give me thy story, woman—thy name, and when thou wert put here, and by whom, and for what.”\n\n“There was once in this city of Jerusalem a Prince Ben-Hur, the friend of all generous Romans, and who had Cæsar for his friend. I am his widow, and this one with me is his child. How may I tell you for what we were sunk here, when I do not know, unless it was because we were rich? Valerius Gratus can tell you who our enemy was, and when our imprisonment began. I cannot. See to what we have been reduced—oh, see, and have pity!”\n\nThe air was heavy with the pest and the smoke of the torches, yet the Roman called one of the torch-bearers to his side, and wrote the answer nearly word for word. It was terse, and comprehensive, containing at once a history, an accusation, and a prayer. No common person could have made it, and he could not but pity and believe.\n\n“Thou shalt have relief, woman,” he said, closing the tablets. “I will send thee food and drink.”\n\n“And raiment, and purifying water, we pray you, O generous Roman!”\n\n“As thou wilt,” he replied.\n\n“God is good,” said the widow, sobbing. “May his peace abide with you!”\n\n“And, further,” he added, “I cannot see thee again. Make preparation, and to-night I will have thee taken to the gate of the Tower, and set free. Thou knowest the law. Farewell.”\n\nHe spoke to the men, and went out the door.\n\nVery shortly some slaves came to the cell with a large gurglet of water, a basin and napkins, a platter with bread and meat, and some garments of women’s wear; and, setting them down within reach of the prisoners, they ran away.\n\nAbout the middle of the first watch, the two were conducted to the gate, and turned into the street. So the Roman quit himself of them, and in the city of their fathers they were once more free.\n\nUp to the stars, twinkling merrily as of old, they looked; then they asked themselves,\n\n“What next? and where to?”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nAbout the hour Gesius, the keeper, made his appearance before the tribune in the Tower of Antonia, a footman was climbing the eastern face of Mount Olivet. The road was rough and dusty, and vegetation on that side burned brown, for it was the dry season in Judea. Well for the traveller that he had youth and strength, not to speak of the cool, flowing garments with which he was clothed.\n\nHe proceeded slowly, looking often to his right and left; not with the vexed, anxious expression which marks a man going forward uncertain of the way, but rather the air with which one approaches as old acquaintance after a long separation—half of pleasure, half of inquiry; as if he were saying, “I am glad to be with you again; let me see in what you are changed.”\n\nAs he arose higher, he sometimes paused to look behind him over the gradually widening view terminating in the mountains of Moab; but when at length he drew near the summit, he quickened his step, unmindful of fatigue, and hurried on without pause or turning of the face. On the summit—to reach which he bent his steps somewhat right of the beaten path—he came to a dead stop, arrested as if by a strong hand. Then one might have seen his eyes dilate, his cheeks flush, his breath quicken, effects all of one bright sweeping glance at what lay before him.\n\nThe traveller, good reader, was no other than Ben-Hur; the spectacle, Jerusalem.\n\nNot the Holy City of to-day, but the Holy City as left by Herod—the Holy City of the Christ. Beautiful yet, as seen from old Olivet, what must it have been then?\n\nBen-Hur betook him to a stone and sat down, and, stripping his head of the close white handkerchief which served it for covering, made the survey at leisure.\n\nThe same has been done often since by a great variety of persons, under circumstances surpassingly singular—by the son of Vespasian, by the Islamite, by the Crusader, conquerors all of them; by many a pilgrim from the great New World, which waited discovery nearly fifteen hundred years after the time of our story; but of the multitude probably not one has taken that view with sensations more keenly poignant, more sadly sweet, more proudly bitter, than Ben-Hur. He was stirred by recollections of his countrymen, their triumphs and vicissitudes, their history the history of God. The city was of their building, at once a lasting testimony of their crimes and devotion, their weakness and genius, their religion and their irreligion. Though he had seen Rome to familiarity, he was gratified. The sight filled a measure of pride which would have made him drunk with vainglory but for the thought, princely as the property was, it did not any longer belong to his countrymen; the worship in the Temple was by permission of strangers; the hill where David dwelt was a marbled cheat—an office in which the chosen of the Lord were wrung and wrung for taxes, and scourged for very deathlessness of faith. These, however, were pleasures and griefs of patriotism common to every Jew of the period; in addition, Ben-Hur brought with him a personal history which would not out of mind for other consideration whatever, which the spectacle served only to freshen and vivify.\n\nA country of hills changes but little; where the hills are of rock, it changes not at all. The scene Ben-Hur beheld is the same now, except as respects the city. The failure is in the handiwork of man alone.\n\nThe sun dealt more kindly by the west side of Olivet than by the east, and men were certainly more loving towards it. The vines with which it was partially clad, and the sprinkling of trees, chiefly figs and old wild olives, were comparatively green. Down to the dry bed of the Cedron the verdure extended, a refreshment to the vision; there Olivet ceased and Moriah began—a wall of bluff boldness, white as snow, founded by Solomon, completed by Herod. Up, up the wall the eye climbed course by course of the ponderous rocks composing it—up to Solomon’s Porch, which was as the pedestal of the monument, the hill being the plinth. Lingering there a moment, the eye resumed its climbing, going next to the Gentiles’ Court, then to the Israelites’ Court, then to the Women’s Court, then to the Court of the Priests, each a pillared tier of white marble, one above the other in terraced retrocession; over them all a crown of crowns infinitely sacred, infinitely beautiful, majestic in proportions, effulgent with beaten gold—lo! the Tent, the Tabernacle, the Holy of Holies. The Ark was not there, but Jehovah was—in the faith of every child of Israel he was there a personal Presence. As a temple, as a monument, there was nowhere anything of man’s building to approach that superlative apparition. Now, not a stone of it remains above another. Who shall rebuild that building? When shall the rebuilding be begun? So asks every pilgrim who has stood where Ben-Hur was—he asks, knowing the answer is in the bosom of God, whose secrets are not least marvellous in their well-keeping. And then the third question, What of him who foretold the ruin which has so certainly befallen? God? Or man of God? Or—enough that the question is for us to answer.\n\nAnd still Ben-Hur’s eyes climbed on and up—up over the roof of the Temple, to the hill Zion, consecrated to sacred memories, inseparable from the anointed kings. He knew the Cheesemonger’s Valley dipped deep down between Moriah and Zion; that it was spanned by the Xystus; that there were gardens and palaces in its depths; but over them all his thoughts soared with his vision to the great grouping on the royal hill—the house of Caiaphas, the Central Synagogue, the Roman Praetorium, Hippicus the eternal, and the sad but mighty cenotaphs Phasaelus and Mariamne—all relieved against Gareb, purpling in the distance. And when midst them he singled out the palace of Herod, what could he but think of the King Who Was Coming, to whom he was himself devoted, whose path he had undertaken to smooth, whose empty hands he dreamed of filling? And forward ran his fancy to the day the new King should come to claim his own and take possession of it—of Moriah and its Temple; of Zion and its towers and palaces; of Antonia, frowning darkly there just to the right of the Temple; of the new unwalled city of Bezetha; of the millions of Israel to assemble with palm-branches and banners, to sing rejoicing because the Lord had conquered and given them the world.\n\nMen speak of dreaming as if it were a phenomenon of night and sleep. They should know better. All results achieved by us are self-promised, and all self-promises are made in dreams awake. Dreaming is the relief of labor, the wine that sustains us in act. We learn to love labor, not for itself, but for the opportunity it furnishes for dreaming, which is the great under-monotone of real life, unheard, unnoticed, because of its constancy. Living is dreaming. Only in the grave are there no dreams. Let no one smile at Ben-Hur for doing that which he himself would have done at that time and place under the same circumstances.\n\nThe sun stooped low in its course. Awhile the flaring disk seemed to perch itself on the far summit of the mountains in the west, brazening all the sky above the city, and rimming the walls and towers with the brightness of gold. Then it disappeared as with a plunge. The quiet turned Ben-Hur’s thought homeward. There was a point in the sky a little north of the peerless front of the Holy of Holies upon which he fixed his gaze: under it, straight as a leadline would have dropped, lay his father’s house, if yet the house endured.\n\nThe mellowing influences of the evening mellowed his feelings, and, putting his ambitions aside, he thought of the duty that was bringing him to Jerusalem.\n\nOut in the desert while with Ilderim, looking for strong places and acquainting himself with it generally, as a soldier studies a country in which he has projected a campaign, a messenger came one evening with the news that Gratus was removed, and Pontius Pilate sent to take his place.\n\nMessala was disabled and believed him dead; Gratus was powerless and gone; why should Ben-Hur longer defer the search for his mother and sister? There was nothing to fear now. If he could not himself see into the prisons of Judea, he could examine them with the eyes of others. If the lost were found, Pilate could have no motive in holding them in custody—none, at least, which could not be overcome by purchase. If found, he would carry them to a place of safety, and then, in calmer mind, his conscience at rest, this one first duty done, he could give himself more entirely to the King Who Was Coming. He resolved at once. That night he counselled with Ilderim, and obtained his assent. Three Arabs came with him to Jericho, where he left them and the horses, and proceeded alone and on foot. Malluch was to meet him in Jerusalem.\n\nBen-Hur’s scheme, be it observed, was as yet a generality.\n\nIn view of the future, it was advisable to keep himself in hiding from the authorities, particularly the Romans. Malluch was shrewd and trusty; the very man to charge with the conduct of the investigation.\n\nWhere to begin was the first point. He had no clear idea about it. His wish was to commence with the Tower of Antonia. Tradition not of long standing planted the gloomy pile over a labyrinth of prison-cells, which, more even than the strong garrison, kept it a terror to the Jewish fancy. A burial, such as his people had been subjected to, might be possible there. Besides, in such a strait, the natural inclination is to start search at the place where the loss occurred, and he could not forget that his last sight of the loved ones was as the guard pushed them along the street in the direction to the Tower. If they were not there now, but had been, some record of the fact must remain, a clew which had only to be followed faithfully to the end.\n\nUnder this inclination, moreover, there was a hope which he could not forego. From Simonides he knew Amrah, the Egyptian nurse, was living. It will be remembered, doubtless, that the faithful creature, the morning the calamity overtook the Hurs, broke from the guard and ran back into the palace, where, along with other chattels, she had been sealed up. During the years following, Simonides kept her supplied; so she was there now, sole occupant of the great house, which, with all his offers, Gratus had not been able to sell. The story of its rightful owners sufficed to secure the property from strangers, whether purchasers or mere occupants. People going to and fro passed it with whispers. Its reputation was that of a haunted house; derived probably from the infrequent glimpses of poor old Amrah, sometimes on the roof, sometimes in a latticed window. Certainly no more constant spirit ever abided than she; nor was there ever a tenement so shunned and fitted for ghostly habitation. Now, if he could get to her, Ben-Hur fancied she could help him to knowledge which, though faint, might yet be serviceable. Anyhow, sight of her in that place, so endeared by recollection, would be to him a pleasure next to finding the objects of his solicitude.\n\nSo, first of all things, he would go to the old house, and look for Amrah.\n\nThus resolved, he arose shortly after the going-down of the sun, and began descent of the Mount by the road which, from the summit, bends a little north of east. Down nearly at the foot, close by the bed of the Cedron, he came to the intersection with the road leading south to the village of Siloam and the pool of that name. There he fell in with a herdsman driving some sheep to market. He spoke to the man, and joined him, and in his company passed by Gethsemane on into the city through the Fish Gate.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nIt was dark when, parting with the drover inside the gate, Ben-Hur turned into a narrow lane leading to the south. A few of the people whom he met saluted him. The bouldering of the pavement was rough. The houses on both sides were low, dark, and cheerless; the doors all closed: from the roofs, occasionally, he heard women crooning to children. The loneliness of his situation, the night, the uncertainty cloaking the object of his coming, all affected him cheerlessly. With feelings sinking lower and lower, he came directly to the deep reservoir now known as the Pool of Bethesda, in which the water reflected the over-pending sky. Looking up, he beheld the northern wall of the Tower of Antonia, a black frowning heap reared into the dim steel-gray sky. He halted as if challenged by a threatening sentinel.\n\nThe Tower stood up so high, and seemed so vast, resting apparently upon foundations so sure, that he was constrained to acknowledge its strength. If his mother were there in living burial, what could he do for her? By the strong hand, nothing. An army might beat the stony face with ballista and ram, and be laughed at. Against him alone, the gigantic southeast turret looked down in the self-containment of a hill. And he thought, cunning is so easily baffled; and God, always the last resort of the helpless—God is sometimes so slow to act!\n\nIn doubt and misgiving, he turned into the street in front of the Tower, and followed it slowly on to the west.\n\nOver in Bezetha he knew there was a khan, where it was his intention to seek lodging while in the city; but just now he could not resist the impulse to go home. His heart drew him that way.\n\nThe old formal salutation which he received from the few people who passed him had never sounded so pleasantly. Presently, all the eastern sky began to silver and shine, and objects before invisible in the west—chiefly the tall towers on Mount Zion—emerged as from a shadowy depth, and put on spectral distinctness, floating, as it were, above the yawning blackness of the valley below, very castles in the air.\n\nHe came, at length, to his father’s house.\n\nOf those who read this page, some there will be to divine his feelings without prompting. They are such as had happy homes in their youth, no matter how far that may have been back in time—homes which are now the starting-points of all recollection; paradises from which they went forth in tears, and which they would now return to, if they could, as little children; places of laughter and singing, and associations dearer than any or all the triumphs of after-life.\n\nAt the gate on the north side of the old house Ben-Hur stopped. In the corners the wax used in the sealing-up was still plainly seen, and across the valves was the board with the inscription—\n\nNobody had gone in or out the gate since the dreadful day of the separation. Should he knock as of old? It was useless, he knew; yet he could not resist the temptation. Amrah might hear, and look out of one of the windows on that side. Taking a stone, he mounted the broad stone step, and tapped three times. A dull echo replied. He tried again, louder than before; and again, pausing each time to listen. The silence was mocking. Retiring into the street, he watched the windows; but they, too, were lifeless. The parapet on the roof was defined sharply against the brightening sky; nothing could have stirred upon it unseen by him, and nothing did stir.\n\nFrom the north side he passed to the west, where there were four windows which he watched long and anxiously, but with as little effect. At times his heart swelled with impotent wishes; at others, he trembled at the deceptions of his own fancy. Amrah made no sign—not even a ghost stirred.\n\nSilently, then, he stole round to the south. There, too, the gate was sealed and inscribed. The mellow splendor of the August moon, pouring over the crest of Olivet, since termed the Mount of Offence, brought the lettering boldly out; and he read, and was filled with rage. All he could do was to wrench the board from its nailing, and hurl it into the ditch. Then he sat upon the step, and prayed for the New King, and that his coming might be hastened. As his blood cooled, insensibly he yielded to the fatigue of long travel in the summer heat, and sank down lower, and, at last, slept.\n\nAbout that time two women came down the street from the direction of the Tower of Antonia, approaching the palace of the Hurs. They advanced stealthily, with timid steps, pausing often to listen. At the corner of the rugged pile, one said to the other, in a low voice,\n\n“This is it, Tirzah!”\n\nAnd Tirzah, after a look, caught her mother’s hand, and leaned upon her heavily, sobbing, but silent.\n\n“Let us go on, my child, because”—the mother hesitated and trembled; then, with an effort to be calm, continued—“because when morning comes they will put us out of the gate of the city to—return no more.”\n\nTirzah sank almost to the stones.\n\n“Ah, yes!” she said, between sobs; “I forgot. I had the feeling of going home. But we are lepers, and have no homes; we belong to the dead!”\n\nThe mother stooped and raised her tenderly, saying, “We have nothing to fear. Let us go on.”\n\nIndeed, lifting their empty hands, they could have run upon a legion and put it to flight.\n\nAnd, creeping in close to the rough wall, they glided on, like two ghosts, till they came to the gate, before which they also paused. Seeing the board, they stepped upon the stone in the scarce cold tracks of Ben-Hur, and read the inscription—“This is the Property of the Emperor.”\n\nThen the mother clasped her hands, and, with upraised eyes, moaned in unutterable anguish.\n\n“What now, mother? You scare me!”\n\nAnd the answer was, presently, “Oh, Tirzah, the poor are dead! He is dead!”\n\n“Who, mother?”\n\n“Your brother! They took everything from him—everything—even this house!”\n\n“Poor!” said Tirzah, vacantly.\n\n“He will never be able to help us.”\n\n“And then, mother?”\n\n“To-morrow—to-morrow, my child, we must find a seat by the wayside, and beg alms as the lepers do; beg, or—”\n\nTirzah leaned upon her again, and said, whispering, “Let us—let us die!”\n\n“No!” the mother said, firmly. “The Lord has appointed our times, and we are believers in the Lord. We will wait on him even in this. Come away!”\n\nShe caught Tirzah’s hand as she spoke, and hastened to the west corner of the house, keeping close to the wall. No one being in sight there, they kept on to the next corner, and shrank from the moonlight, which lay exceedingly bright over the whole south front, and along a part of the street. The mother’s will was strong. Casting one look back and up to the windows on the west side, she stepped out into the light, drawing Tirzah after her; and the extent of their affliction was then to be seen—on their lips and cheeks, in their bleared eyes, in their cracked hands; especially in the long, snaky locks, stiff with loathsome ichor, and, like their eyebrows, ghastly white. Nor was it possible to have told which was mother, which daughter; both alike seemed witch-like old.\n\n“Hist!” said the mother. “There is some one lying upon the step—a man. Let us go round him.”\n\nThey crossed to the opposite side of the street quickly, and, in the shade there, moved on till before the gate, where they stopped.\n\n“He is asleep, Tirzah!”\n\nThe man was very still.\n\n“Stay here, and I will try the gate.”\n\nSo saying, the mother stole noiselessly across, and ventured to touch the wicket; she never knew if it yielded, for that moment the man sighed, and, turning restlessly, shifted the handkerchief on his head in such manner that the face was left upturned and fair in the broad moonlight. She looked down at it and started; then looked again, stooping a little, and arose and clasped her hands and raised her eyes to heaven in mute appeal. An instant so, and she ran back to Tirzah.\n\n“As the Lord liveth, the man is my son—thy brother!” she said, in an awe-inspiring whisper.\n\n“My brother?—Judah?”\n\nThe mother caught her hand eagerly.\n\n“Come!” she said, in the same enforced whisper, “let us look at him together—once more—only once—then help thou thy servants, Lord!”\n\nThey crossed the street hand in hand ghostly-quick, ghostly-still. When their shadows fell upon him, they stopped. One of his hands was lying out upon the step palm up. Tirzah fell upon her knees, and would have kissed it; but the mother drew her back.\n\n“Not for thy life; not for thy life! Unclean, unclean!” she whispered.\n\nTirzah shrank from him, as if he were the leprous one.\n\nBen-Hur was handsome as the manly are. His cheeks and forehead were swarthy from exposure to the desert sun and air; yet under the light mustache the lips were red, and the teeth shone white, and the soft beard did not hide the full roundness of chin and throat. How beautiful he appeared to the mother’s eyes! How mightily she yearned to put her arms about him, and take his head upon her bosom and kiss him, as had been her wont in his happy childhood! Where got she the strength to resist the impulse? From her love, O, reader!—her mother-love, which, if thou wilt observe well, hath this unlikeness to any other love: tender to the object, it can be infinitely tyrannical to itself, and thence all its power of self-sacrifice. Not for restoration to health and fortune, not for any blessing of life, not for life itself, would she have left her leprous kiss upon his cheek! Yet touch him she must; in that instant of finding him she must renounce him forever! How bitter, bitter hard it was, let some other mother say! She knelt down, and, crawling to his feet, touched the sole of one of his sandals with her lips, yellow though it was with the dust of the street—and touched it again and again; and her very soul was in the kisses.\n\nHe stirred, and tossed his hand. They moved back, but heard him mutter in his dream,\n\n“Mother! Amrah! Where is—”\n\nHe fell off into the deep sleep.\n\nTirzah stared wistfully. The mother put her face in the dust, struggling to suppress a sob so deep and strong it seemed her heart was bursting. Almost she wished he might waken.\n\nHe had asked for her; she was not forgotten; in his sleep he was thinking of her. Was it not enough?\n\nPresently mother beckoned to Tirzah, and they arose, and taking one more look, as if to print his image past fading, hand in hand they recrossed the street. Back in the shade of the wall there, they retired and knelt, looking at him, waiting for him to wake—waiting some revelation, they knew not what. Nobody has yet given us a measure for the patience of a love like theirs.\n\nBy-and-by, the sleep being yet upon him, another woman appeared at the corner of the palace. The two in the shade saw her plainly in the light; a small figure, much bent, dark-skinned, gray-haired, dressed neatly in servant’s garb, and carrying a basket full of vegetables.\n\nAt sight of the man upon the step the new-comer stopped; then, as if decided, she walked on—very lightly as she drew near the sleeper. Passing round him, she went to the gate, slid the wicket latch easily to one side, and put her hand in the opening. One of the broad boards in the left valve swung ajar without noise. She put the basket through, and was about to follow, when, yielding to curiosity, she lingered to have one look at the stranger whose face was below her in open view.\n\nThe spectators across the street heard a low exclamation, and saw the woman rub her eyes as if to renew their power, bend closer down, clasp her hands, gaze wildly around, look at the sleeper, stoop and raise the outlying hand, and kiss it fondly—that which they wished so mightily to do, but dared not.\n\nAwakened by the action, Ben-Hur instinctively withdrew the hand; as he did so, his eyes met the woman’s.\n\n“Amrah! O Amrah, is it thou?” he said.\n\nThe good heart made no answer in words, but fell upon his neck, crying for joy.\n\nGently he put her arms away, and lifting the dark face wet with tears, kissed it, his joy only a little less than hers. Then those across the way heard him say,\n\n“Mother—Tirzah—O Amrah, tell me of them! Speak, speak, I pray thee!”\n\nAmrah only cried afresh.\n\n“Thou has seen them, Amrah. Thou knowest where they are; tell me they are at home.”\n\nTirzah moved, but her mother, divining her purpose, caught her and whispered, “Do not go—not for life. Unclean, unclean!”\n\nHer love was in tyrannical mood. Though both their hearts broke, he should not become what they were; and she conquered.\n\nMeantime, Amrah, so entreated, only wept the more.\n\n“Wert thou going in?” he asked, presently, seeing the board swung back. “Come, then. I will go with thee.” He arose as he spoke. “The Romans—be the curse of the Lord upon them!—the Romans lied. The house is mine. Rise, Amrah, and let us go in.” A moment and they were gone, leaving the two in the shade to behold the gate staring blankly at them—the gate which they might not ever enter more. They nestled together in the dust.\n\nThey had done their duty.\n\nTheir love was proven.\n\nNext morning they were found, and driven out the city with stones.\n\n“Begone! Ye are of the dead; go to the dead!”\n\nWith the doom ringing in their ears, they went forth.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nNowadays travellers in the Holy Land looking for the famous place with the beautiful name, the King’s Garden, descend the bed of the Cedron or the curve of Gihon and Hinnom as far as the old well En-rogel, take a drink of the sweet living water, and stop, having reached the limit of the interesting in that direction. They look at the great stones with which the well is curbed, ask its depth, smile at the primitive mode of drawing the purling treasure, and waste some pity on the ragged wretch who presides over it; then, facing about, they are enraptured with the mounts Moriah and Zion, both of which slope towards them from the north, one terminating in Ophel, the other in what used to be the site of the city of David. In the background, up far in the sky, the garniture of the sacred places is visible: here the Haram, with its graceful dome; yonder the stalward remains of Hippicus, defiant even in ruins. When that view has been enjoyed, and is sufficiently impressed upon the memory, the travellers glance at the Mount of Offence standing in rugged stateliness at their right hand, and then at the Hill of Evil Counsel over on the left, in which, if they be well up in Scriptural history and in the traditions rabbinical and monkish, they will find a certain interest not to be overcome by superstitious horror.\n\nIt were long to tell all the points of interest grouped around that hill; for the present purpose, enough that its feet are planted in the veritable orthodox Hell of the moderns—the Hell of brimstone and fire—in the old nomenclature Gehenna; and that now, as in the days of Christ, its bluff face opposite the city on the south and southeast is seamed and pitted with tombs which have been immemorially the dwelling-places of lepers, not singly, but collectively. There they set up their government and established their society; there they founded a city and dwelt by themselves, avoided as the accursed of God.\n\nThe second morning after the incidents of the preceding chapter, Amrah drew near the well En-rogel, and seated herself upon a stone. One familiar with Jerusalem, looking at her, would have said she was the favorite servant of some well-to-do family. She brought with her a water-jar and a basket, the contents of the latter covered with a snow-white napkin. Placing them on the ground at her side, she loosened the shawl which fell from her head, knit her fingers together in her lap, and gazed demurely up to where the hill drops steeply down into Aceldama and the Potter’s Field.\n\nIt was very early, and she was the first to arrive at the well. Soon, however, a man came bringing a rope and a leathern bucket. Saluting the little dark-faced woman, he undid the rope, fixed it to the bucket, and waited customers. Others who chose to do so might draw water for themselves, he was a professional in the business, and would fill the largest jar the stoutest woman could carry for a gerah.\n\nAmrah sat still, and had nothing to say. Seeing the jar, the man asked after a while if she wished it filled; she answered him civilly, “Not now;” whereupon he gave her no more attention. When the dawn was fairly defined over Olivet, his patrons began to arrive, and he had all he could do to attend to them. All the time she kept her seat, looking intently up at the hill.\n\nThe sun made its appearance, yet she sat watching and waiting; and while she thus waits, let us see what her purpose is.\n\nHer custom had been to go to market after nightfall. Stealing out unobserved, she would seek the shops in the Tyropoeon, or those over by the Fish Gate in the east, make her purchases of meat and vegetables, and return and shut herself up again.\n\nThe pleasure she derived from the presence of Ben-Hur in the old house once more may be imagined. She had nothing to tell him of her mistress or Tirzah—nothing. He would have had her move to a place not so lonesome; she refused. She would have had him take his own room again, which was just as he had left it; but the danger of discovery was too great, and he wished above all things to avoid inquiry. He would come and see her often as possible. Coming in the night, he would also go away in the night. She was compelled to be satisfied, and at once occupied herself contriving ways to make him happy. That he was a man now did not occur to her; nor did it enter her mind that he might have put by or lost his boyish tastes; to please him, she thought to go on her old round of services. He used to be fond of confections; she remembered the things in that line which delighted him most, and resolved to make them, and have a supply always ready when he came. Could anything be happier? So next night, earlier than usual, she stole out with her basket, and went over to the Fish Gate Market. Wandering about, seeking the best honey, she chanced to hear a man telling a story.\n\nWhat the story was the reader can arrive at with sufficient certainty when told that the narrator was one of the men who had held torches for the commandant of the Tower of Antonia when, down in cell VI., the Hurs were found. The particulars of the finding were all told, and she heard them, with the names of the prisoners, and the widow’s account of herself.\n\nThe feelings with which Amrah listened to the recital were such as became the devoted creature she was. She made her purchases, and returned home in a dream. What a happiness she had in store for her boy! She had found his mother!\n\nShe put the basket away, now laughing, now crying. Suddenly she stopped and thought. It would kill him to be told that his mother and Tirzah were lepers. He would go through the awful city over on the Hill of Evil Counsel—into each infected tomb he would go without rest, asking for them, and the disease would catch him, and their fate would be his. She wrung her hands. What should she do?\n\nLike many a one before her, and many a one since, she derived inspiration, if not wisdom, from her affection, and came to a singular conclusion.\n\nThe lepers, she knew, were accustomed of mornings to come down from their sepulchral abodes in the hill, and take a supply of water for the day from the well En-rogel. Bringing their jars, they would set them on the ground and wait, standing afar until they were filled. To that the mistress and Tirzah must come; for the law was inexorable, and admitted no distinction. A rich leper was no better than a poor one.\n\nSo Amrah decided not to speak to Ben-Hur of the story she had heard, but go alone to the well and wait. Hunger and thirst would drive the unfortunates thither, and she believed she could recognize them at sight; if not, they might recognize her.\n\nMeantime Ben-Hur came, and they talked much. To-morrow Malluch would arrive; then the search should be immediately begun. He was impatient to be about it. To amuse himself he would visit the sacred places in the vicinity. The secret, we may be sure, weighed heavily on the woman, but she held her peace.\n\nWhen he was gone she busied herself in the preparation of things good to eat, applying her utmost skill to the work. At the approach of day, as signalled by the stars, she filled the basket, selected a jar, and took the road to En-rogel, going out by the Fish Gate which was earliest open, and arriving as we have seen.\n\nShortly after sunrise, when business at the well was most pressing, and the drawer of water most hurried; when, in fact, half a dozen buckets were in use at the same time, everybody making haste to get away before the cool of the morning melted into the heat of the day, the tenantry of the hill began to appear and move about the doors of their tombs. Somewhat later they were discernible in groups, of which not a few were children so young that they suggested the holiest relation. Numbers came momentarily around the turn of the bluff—women with jars upon their shoulders, old and very feeble men hobbling along on staffs and crutches. Some leaned upon the shoulders of others; a few—the utterly helpless—lay, like heaps of rags, upon litters. Even that community of superlative sorrow had its love-light to make life endurable and attractive. Distance softened without entirely veiling the misery of the outcasts.\n\nFrom her seat by the well Amrah kept watch upon the spectral groups. She scarcely moved. More than once she imagined she saw those she sought. That they were there upon the hill she had no doubt; that they must come down and near she knew; when the people at the well were all served they would come.\n\nNow, quite at the base of the bluff there was a tomb which had more than once attracted Amrah by its wide gaping. A stone of large dimensions stood near its mouth. The sun looked into it through the hottest hours of the day, and altogether it seemed uninhabitable by anything living, unless, perchance, by some wild dogs returning from scavenger duty down in Gehenna. Thence, however, and greatly to her surprise, the patient Egyptian beheld two women come, one half supporting, half leading, the other. They were both white-haired; both looked old; but their garments were not rent, and they gazed about them as if the locality were new. The witness below thought she even saw them shrink terrified at the spectacle offered by the hideous assemblage of which they found themselves part. Slight reasons, certainly, to make her heart beat faster, and draw her attention to them exclusively; but so they did.\n\nThe two remained by the stone awhile; then they moved slowly, painfully, and with much fear towards the well, whereat several voices were raised to stop them; yet they kept on. The drawer of water picked up some pebbles, and made ready to drive them back. The company cursed them. The greater company on the hill shouted shrilly, “Unclean, unclean!”\n\n“Surely,” thought Amrah of the two, as they kept coming—“surely, they are strangers to the usage of lepers.”\n\nShe arose, and went to meet them, taking the basket and jar. The alarm at the well immediately subsided.\n\n“What a fool,” said one, laughing, “what a fool to give good bread to the dead in that way!”\n\n“And to think of her coming so far!” said another. “I would at least make them meet me at the gate.”\n\nAmrah, with better impulse, proceeded. If she should be mistaken! Her heart arose into her throat. And the farther she went the more doubtful and confused she became. Four or five yards from where they stood waiting for her she stopped.\n\nThat the mistress she loved! whose hand she had so often kissed in gratitude! whose image of matronly loveliness she had treasured in memory so faithfully! And that the Tirzah she had nursed through babyhood! whose pains she had soothed, whose sports she had shared! that the smiling, sweet-faced, songful Tirzah, the light of the great house, the promised blessing of her old age! Her mistress, her darling—they? The soul of the woman sickened at the sight.\n\n“These are old women,” she said to herself. “I never saw them before. I will go back.”\n\nShe turned away.\n\n“Amrah,” said one of the lepers.\n\nThe Egyptian dropped the jar, and looked back, trembling.\n\n“Who called me?” she asked.\n\n“Amrah.”\n\nThe servant’s wondering eyes settled upon the speaker’s face.\n\n“Who are you?” she cried.\n\n“We are they you are seeking.”\n\nAmrah fell upon her knees.\n\n“O my mistress, my mistress! As I have made your God my God, be he praised that he has led me to you!”\n\nAnd upon her knees the poor overwhelmed creature began moving forward.\n\n“Stay, Amrah! Come not nearer. Unclean, unclean!”\n\nThe words sufficed. Amrah fell upon her face, sobbing so loud the people at the well heard her. Suddenly she arose upon her knees again.\n\n“O my mistress, where is Tirzah?”\n\n“Here I am, Amrah, here! Will you not bring me a little water?”\n\nThe habit of the servant renewed itself. Putting back the coarse hair fallen over her face, Amrah arose and went to the basket and uncovered it.\n\n“See,” she said, “here are bread and meat.”\n\nShe would have spread the napkin upon the ground, but the mistress spoke again,\n\n“Do not so, Amrah. Those yonder may stone you, and refuse us drink. Leave the basket with me. Take up the jar and fill it, and bring it here. We will carry them to the tomb with us. For this day you will then have rendered all the service that is lawful. Haste, Amrah.”\n\nThe people under whose eyes all this had passed made way for the servant, and even helped her fill the jar, so piteous was the grief her countenance showed.\n\n“Who are they?” a woman asked.\n\nAmrah meekly answered, “They used to be good to me.”\n\nRaising the jar upon her shoulder, she hurried back. In forgetfulness, she would have gone to them, but the cry “Unclean, unclean! Beware!” arrested her. Placing the water by the basket, she stepped back, and stood off a little way.\n\n“Thank you, Amrah,” said the mistress, taking the articles into possession. “This is very good of you.”\n\n“Is there nothing more I can do?” asked Amrah.\n\nThe mother’s hand was upon the jar, and she was fevered with thirst; yet she paused, and rising, said firmly, “Yes, I know that Judah has come home. I saw him at the gate night before last asleep on the step. I saw you wake him.”\n\nAmrah clasped her hands.\n\n“O my mistress! You saw it, and did not come!”\n\n“That would have been to kill him. I can never take him in my arms again. I can never kiss him more. O Amrah, Amrah, you love him, I know!”\n\n“Yes,” said the true heart, bursting into tears again, and kneeling. “I would die for him.”\n\n“Prove to me what you say, Amrah.”\n\n“I am ready.”\n\n“Then you shall not tell him where we are or that you have seen us—only that, Amrah.”\n\n“But he is looking for you. He has come from afar to find you.”\n\n“He must not find us. He shall not become what we are. Hear, Amrah. You shall serve us as you have this day. You shall bring us the little we need—not long now—not long. You shall come every morning and evening thus, and—and”—the voice trembled, the strong will almost broke down—“and you shall tell us of him, Amrah; but to him you shall say nothing of us. Hear you?”\n\n“Oh, it will be so hard to hear him speak of you, and see him going about looking for you—to see all his love, and not tell him so much as that you are alive!”\n\n“Can you tell him we are well, Amrah?”\n\nThe servant bowed her head in her arms.\n\n“No,” the mistress continued; “wherefore to be silent altogether. Go now, and come this evening. We will look for you. Till then, farewell.”\n\n“The burden will be heavy, O my mistress, and hard to bear,” said Amrah, falling upon her face.\n\n“How much harder would it be to see him as we are,” the mother answered as she gave the basket to Tirzah. “Come again this evening,” she repeated, taking up the water, and starting for the tomb.\n\nAmrah waited kneeling until they had disappeared; then she took the road sorrowfully home.\n\nIn the evening she returned; and thereafter it became her custom to serve them in the morning and evening, so that they wanted for nothing needful. The tomb, though ever so stony and desolate, was less cheerless than the cell in the Tower had been. Daylight gilded its door, and it was in the beautiful world. Then, one can wait death with so much more faith out under the open sky.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nThe morning of the first day of the seventh month—Tishri in the Hebrew, October in English—Ben-Hur arose from his couch in the khan ill satisfied with the whole world.\n\nLittle time had been lost in consultation upon the arrival of Malluch. The latter began the search at the Tower of Antonia, and began it boldly, by a direct inquiry of the tribune commanding. He gave the officer a history of the Hurs, and all the particulars of the accident to Gratus, describing the affair as wholly without criminality. The object of the quest now, he said, was if any of the unhappy family were discovered alive to carry a petition to the feet of Cæsar, praying restitution of the estate and return to their civil rights. Such a petition, he had no doubt, would result in an investigation by the imperial order, a proceeding of which the friends of the family had no fear.\n\nIn reply the tribune stated circumstantially the discovery of the women in the Tower, and permitted a reading of the memorandum he had taken of their account of themselves; when leave to copy it was prayed, he even permitted that.\n\nMalluch thereupon hurried to Ben-Hur.\n\nIt were useless to attempt description of the effect the terrible story had upon the young man. The pain was not relieved by tears or passionate outcries; it was too deep for any expression. He sat still a long time, with pallid face and laboring heart. Now and then, as if to show the thoughts which were most poignant, he muttered,\n\n“Lepers, lepers! They—my mother and Tirzah—they lepers! How long, how long, O Lord!”\n\nOne moment he was torn by a virtuous rage of sorrow, next by a longing for vengeance which, it must be admitted, was scarcely less virtuous.\n\nAt length he arose.\n\n“I must look for them. They may be dying.”\n\n“Where will you look?” asked Malluch.\n\n“There is but one place for them to go.”\n\nMalluch interposed, and finally prevailed so far as to have the management of the further attempt intrusted to him. Together they went to the gate over on the side opposite the Hill of Evil Counsel, immemorially the lepers’ begging-ground. There they stayed all day, giving alms, asking for the two women, and offering rich rewards for their discovery. So they did in repetition day after day through the remainder of the fifth month, and all the sixth. There was diligent scouring of the dread city on the hill by lepers to whom the rewards offered were mighty incentives, for they were only dead in law. Over and over again the gaping tomb down by the well was invaded, and its tenants subjected to inquiry; but they kept their secret fast. The result was failure. And now, the morning of the first day of the seventh month, the extent of the additional information gained was that not long before two leprous women had been stoned from the Fish Gate by the authorities. A little pressing of the clew, together with some shrewd comparison of dates, led to the sad assurance that the sufferers were the Hurs, and left the old questions darker than ever. Where were they? And what had become of them?\n\n“It was not enough that my people should be made lepers,” said the son, over and over again, with what intensity of bitterness the reader may imagine; “that was not enough. Oh no! They must be stoned from their native city! My mother is dead! she has wandered to the wilderness! she is dead! Tirzah is dead! I alone am left. And for what? How long, O God, thou Lord God of my fathers, how long shall this Rome endure?”\n\nAngry, hopeless, vengeful, he entered the court of the khan, and found it crowded with people come in during the night. While he ate his breakfast, he listened to some of them. To one party he was specially attracted. They were mostly young, stout, active, hardy men, in manner and speech provincial. In their look, the certain indefinable air, the pose of the head, glance of the eye, there was a spirit which did not, as a rule, belong to the outward seeming of the lower orders of Jerusalem; the spirit thought by some to be a peculiarity of life in mountainous districts, but which may be more surely traced to a life of healthful freedom. In a short time he ascertained they were Galileans, in the city for various purposes, but chiefly to take part in the Feast of Trumpets, set for that day. They became to him at once objects of interest, as hailing from the region in which he hoped to find readiest support in the work he was shortly to set about.\n\nWhile observing them, his mind running ahead in thought of achievements possible to a legion of such spirits disciplined after the severe Roman style, a man came into the court, his face much flushed, his eyes bright with excitement.\n\n“Why are you here?” he said to the Galileans. “The rabbis and elders are going from the Temple to see Pilate. Come, make haste, and let us go with them.”\n\nThey surrounded him in a moment.\n\n“To see Pilate! For what?”\n\n“They have discovered a conspiracy. Pilate’s new aqueduct is to be paid for with money of the Temple.”\n\n“What, with the sacred treasure?”\n\nThey repeated the question to each other with flashing eyes.\n\n“It is Corban—money of God. Let him touch a shekel of it if he dare!”\n\n“Come,” cried the messenger. “The procession is by this time across the bridge. The whole city is pouring after. We may be needed. Make haste!”\n\nAs if the thought and the act were one, there was quick putting away of useless garments, and the party stood forth bareheaded, and in the short sleeveless under-tunics they were used to wearing as reapers in the field and boatmen on the lake—the garb in which they climbed the hills following the herds, and plucked the ripened vintage, careless of the sun. Lingering only to tighten their girdles, they said, “We are ready.”\n\nThen Ben-Hur spoke to them.\n\n“Men of Galilee,” he said, “I am a son of Judah. Will you take me in your company?”\n\n“We may have to fight,” they replied.\n\n“Oh, then, I will not be first to run away!”\n\nThey took the retort in good humor, and the messenger said, “You seem stout enough. Come along.”\n\nBen-Hur put off his outer garments.\n\n“You think there may be fighting?” he asked, quietly, as he tightened his girdle.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“With whom?”\n\n“The guard.”\n\n“Legionaries?”\n\n“Whom else can a Roman trust?”\n\n“What have you to fight with?”\n\nThey looked at him silently.\n\n“Well,” he continued, “we will have to do the best we can; but had we not better choose a leader? The legionaries always have one, and so are able to act with one mind.”\n\nThe Galileans stared more curiously, as if the idea were new to them.\n\n“Let us at least agree to stay together,” he said. “Now I am ready, if you are.”\n\n“Yes, let us go.”\n\nThe khan, it should not be forgotten, was in Bezetha, the new town; and to get to the Praetorium, as the Romans resonantly styled the palace of Herod on Mount Zion, the party had to cross the lowlands north and west of the Temple. By streets—if they may be so called—trending north and south, with intersections hardly up to the dignity of alleys, they passed rapidly round the Akra district to the Tower of Mariamne, from which the way was short to the grand gate of the walled heights. In going, they overtook, or were overtaken by, people like themselves stirred to wrath by news of the proposed desecration. When, at length, they reached the gate of the Praetorium, the procession of elders and rabbis had passed in with a great following, leaving a greater crowd clamoring outside.\n\nA centurion kept the entrance with a guard drawn up full armed under the beautiful marble battlements. The sun struck the soldiers fervidly on helm and shield; but they kept their ranks indifferent alike to its dazzle and to the mouthings of the rabble. Through the open bronze gates a current of citizens poured in, while a much lesser one poured out.\n\n“What is going on?” one of the Galileans asked an outcomer.\n\n“Nothing,” was the reply. “The rabbis are before the door of the palace asking to see Pilate. He has refused to come out. They have sent one to tell him they will not go away till he has heard them. They are waiting.”\n\n“Let us go in,” said Ben-Hur, in his quiet way, seeing what his companions probably did not, that there was not only a disagreement between the suitors and the governor, but an issue joined, and a serious question as to who should have his will.\n\nInside the gate there was a row of trees in leaf, with seats under them. The people, whether going or coming, carefully avoided the shade cast gratefully upon the white, clean-swept pavement; for, strange as it may seem, a rabbinical ordinance, alleged to have been derived from the law, permitted no green thing to be grown within the walls of Jerusalem. Even the wise king, it was said, wanting a garden for his Egyptian bride, was constrained to found it down in the meeting-place of the valleys above En-rogel.\n\nThrough the tree-tops shone the outer fronts of the palace. Turning to the right, the party proceeded a short distance to a spacious square, on the west side of which stood the residence of the governor. An excited multitude filled the square. Every face was directed towards a portico built over a broad doorway which was closed. Under the portico there was another array of legionaries.\n\nThe throng was so close the friends could not well have advanced if such had been their desire; they remained therefore in the rear, observers of what was going on. About the portico they could see the high turbans of the rabbis, whose impatience communicated at times to the mass behind them; a cry was frequent to the effect “Pilate, if thou be a governor, come forth, come forth!”\n\nOnce a man coming out pushed through the crowd, his face red with anger.\n\n“Israel is of no account here,” he said, in a loud voice. “On this holy ground we are no better than dogs of Rome.”\n\n“Will he not come out, think you?”\n\n“Come? Has he not thrice refused?”\n\n“What will the rabbis do?”\n\n“As at Cæsarea—camp here till he gives them ear.”\n\n“He will not dare touch the treasure, will he?” asked one of the Galileans.\n\n“Who can say? Did not a Roman profane the Holy of Holies? Is there anything sacred from Romans?”\n\nAn hour passed, and though Pilate deigned them no answer, the rabbis and crowd remained. Noon came, bringing a shower from the west, but no change in the situation, except that the multitude was larger and much noisier, and the feeling more decidedly angry. The shouting was almost continuous, Come forth, come forth! The cry was sometimes with disrespectful variations. Meanwhile Ben-Hur held his Galilean friends together. He judged the pride of the Roman would eventually get the better of his discretion, and that the end could not be far off. Pilate was but waiting for the people to furnish him an excuse for resort to violence.\n\nAnd at last the end came. In the midst of the assemblage there was heard the sound of blows, succeeded instantly by yells of pain and rage, and a most furious commotion. The venerable men in front of the portico faced about aghast. The common people in the rear at first pushed forward; in the centre, the effort was to get out; and for a short time the pressure of opposing forces was terrible. A thousand voices made inquiry, raised all at once; as no one had time to answer, the surprise speedily became a panic.\n\nBen-Hur kept his senses.\n\n“You cannot see?” he said to one of the Galileans.\n\n“No.”\n“I will raise you up.”\n\nHe caught the man about the middle, and lifted him bodily.\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“I see now,” said the man. “There are some armed with clubs, and they are beating the people. They are dressed like Jews.”\n\n“Who are they?”\n\n“Romans, as the Lord liveth! Romans in disguise. Their clubs fly like flails! There, I saw a rabbi struck down—an old man! They spare nobody!”\n\nBen-Hur let the man down.\n\n“Men of Galilee,” he said, “it is a trick of Pilate’s. Now, will you do what I say, we will get even with the club-men.”\n\nThe Galilean spirit arose.\n\n“Yes, yes!” they answered.\n\n“Let us go back to the trees by the gate, and we may find the planting of Herod, though unlawful, has some good in it after all. Come!”\n\nThey ran back all of them fast as they could; and, by throwing their united weight upon the limbs, tore them from the trunks. In a brief time they, too, were armed. Returning, at the corner of the square they met the crowd rushing madly for the gate. Behind, the clamor continued—a medley of shrieks, groans, and execrations.\n\n“To the wall!” Ben-Hur shouted. “To the wall!—and let the herd go by!”\n\nSo, clinging to the masonry at their right hand, they escaped the might of the rush, and little by little made headway until, at last, the square was reached.\n\n“Keep together now, and follow me!”\n\nBy this time Ben-Hur’s leadership was perfect; and as he pushed into the seething mob his party closed after him in a body. And when the Romans, clubbing the people and making merry as they struck them down, came hand to hand with the Galileans, lithe of limb, eager for the fray, and equally armed, they were in turn surprised. Then the shouting was close and fierce; the crash of sticks rapid and deadly; the advance furious as hate could make it. No one performed his part as well as Ben-Hur, whose training served him admirably; for, not merely he knew to strike and guard; his long arm, perfect action, and incomparable strength helped him, also, to success in every encounter. He was at the same time fighting-man and leader. The club he wielded was of goodly length and weighty, so he had need to strike a man but once. He seemed, moreover, to have eyes for each combat of his friends, and the faculty of being at the right moment exactly where he was most needed. In his fighting cry there were inspiration for his party and alarm for his enemies. Thus surprised and equally matched, the Romans at first retired, but finally turned their backs and fled to the portico. The impetuous Galileans would have pursued them to the steps, but Ben-Hur wisely restrained them.\n\n“Stay, my men!” he said. “The centurion yonder is coming with the guard. They have swords and shields; we cannot fight them. We have done well; let us get back and out of the gate while we may.”\n\nThey obeyed him, though slowly; for they had frequently to step over their countrymen lying where they had been felled; some writhing and groaning, some praying help, others mute as the dead. But the fallen were not all Jews. In that there was consolation.\n\nThe centurion shouted to them as they went off; Ben-Hur laughed at him, and replied in his own tongue, “If we are dogs of Israel, you are jackals of Rome. Remain here, and we will come again.”\n\nThe Galileans cheered, and laughing went on.\n\nOutside the gate there was a multitude the like of which Ben-Hur had never seen, not even in the circus at Antioch. The house-tops, the streets, the slope of the hill, appeared densely covered with people wailing and praying. The air was filled with their cries and imprecations.\n\nThe party were permitted to pass without challenge by the outer guard. But hardly were they out before the centurion in charge at the portico appeared, and in the gateway called to Ben-Hur,\n\n“Ho, insolent! Art thou a Roman or a Jew?”\n\nBen-Hur answered, “I am a son of Judah, born here. What wouldst thou with me?”\n\n“Stay and fight.”\n\n“Singly?”\n\n“As thou wilt!”\n\nBen-Hur laughed derisively.\n\n“O brave Roman! Worthy son of the bastard Roman Jove! I have no arms.”\n\n“Thou shalt have mine,” the centurion answered. “I will borrow of the guard here.”\n\nThe people in hearing of the colloquy became silent; and from them the hush spread afar. But lately Ben-Hur had beaten a Roman under the eyes of Antioch and the Farther East; now, could he beat another one under the eyes of Jerusalem, the honor might be vastly profitable to the cause of the New King. He did not hesitate. Going frankly to the centurion, he said, “I am willing. Lend me thy sword and shield.”\n\n“And the helm and breastplate?” asked the Roman.\n\n“Keep them. They might not fit me.”\n\nThe arms were as frankly delivered, and directly the centurion was ready. All this time the soldiers in rank close by the gate never moved; they simply listened. As to the multitude, only when the combatants advanced to begin the fight the question sped from mouth to mouth, “Who is he?” And no one knew.\n\nNow the Roman supremacy in arms lay in three things—submission to discipline, the legionary formation of battle, and a peculiar use of the short sword. In combat, they never struck or cut; from first to last they thrust—they advanced thrusting, they retired thrusting; and generally their aim was at the foeman’s face. All this was well known to Ben-Hur. As they were about to engage he said,\n\n“I told thee I was a son of Judah; but I did not tell that I am lanista-taught. Defend thyself!”\n\nAt the last word Ben-Hur closed with his antagonist. A moment, standing foot to foot, they glared at each other over the rims of their embossed shields; then the Roman pushed forward and feinted an under-thrust. The Jew laughed at him. A thrust at the face followed. The Jew stepped lightly to the left; quick as the thrust was, the step was quicker. Under the lifted arm of the foe he slid his shield, advancing it until the sword and sword-arm were both caught on its upper surface; another step, this time forward and left, and the man’s whole right side was offered to the point. The centurion fell heavily on his breast, clanging the pavement, and Ben-Hur had won. With his foot upon his enemy’s back, he raised his shield overhead after a gladiatorial custom, and saluted the imperturbable soldiers by the gate.\n\nWhen the people realized the victory they behaved like mad. On the houses far as the Xystus, fast as the word could fly, they waved their shawls and handkerchiefs and shouted; and if he had consented, the Galileans would have carried Ben-Hur off upon their shoulders.\n\nTo a petty officer who then advanced from the gate he said, “Thy comrade died like a soldier. I leave him undespoiled. Only his sword and shield are mine.”\n\nWith that, he walked away. Off a little he spoke to the Galileans.\n\n“Brethren, you have behaved well. Let us now separate, lest we be pursued. Meet me to-night at the khan in Bethany. I have something to propose to you of great interest to Israel.”\n\n“Who are you?” they asked him.\n\n“A son of Judah,” he answered, simply.\n\nA throng eager to see him surged around the party.\n\n“Will you come to Bethany?” he asked.\n\n“Yes, we will come.”\n\n“Then bring with you this sword and shield that I may know you.”\n\nPushing brusquely through the increasing crowd, he speedily disappeared.\n\nAt the instance of Pilate, the people went up from the city, and carried off their dead and wounded, and there was much mourning for them; but the grief was greatly lightened by the victory of the unknown champion, who was everywhere sought, and by every one extolled. The fainting spirit of the nation was revived by the brave deed; insomuch that in the streets and up in the Temple even, amidst the solemnities of the feast, old tales of the Maccabees were told again, and thousands shook their heads whispering wisely,\n\n“A little longer, only a little longer, brethren, and Israel will come to her own. Let there be faith in the Lord, and patience.”\n\nIn such manner Ben-Hur obtained hold on Galilee, and paved the way to greater services in the cause of the King Who Was Coming.\n\nAnd with what result we shall see.\n\n# BOOK SEVENTH\n\n“And, waking, I beheld her there Sea-dreaming in the moted air, A siren lithe and debonair, With wristlets woven of scarlet weeds, And oblong lucent amber beads Of sea-kelp shining in her hair.”\n\n# THOMAS BAILEY ALDRICH. CHAPTER I\n\nThe meeting took place in the khan of Bethany as appointed. Thence Ben-Hur went with the Galileans into their country, where his exploits up in the old Market-place gave him fame and influence. Before the winter was gone he raised three legions, and organized them after the Roman pattern. He could have had as many more, for the martial spirit of that gallant people never slept. The proceeding, however, required careful guarding as against both Rome and Herod Antipas. Contenting himself for the present with the three, he strove to train and educate them for systematic action. For that purpose he carried the officers over into the lava-beds of Trachonitis, and taught them the use of arms, particularly the javelin and sword, and the manoeuvering peculiar to the legionary formation; after which he sent them home as teachers. And soon the training became a pastime of the people.\n\nAs may be thought, the task called for patience, skill, zeal, faith, and devotion on his part—qualities into which the power of inspiring others in matters of difficulty is always resolvable; and never man possessed them in greater degree or used them to better effect. How he labored! And with utter denial of self! Yet withal he would have failed but for the support he had from Simonides, who furnished him arms and money, and from Ilderim, who kept watch and brought him supplies. And still he would have failed but for the genius of the Galileans.\n\nUnder that name were comprehended the four tribes—Asher, Zebulon, Issachar, and Naphthali—and the districts originally set apart to them. The Jew born in sight of the Temple despised these brethren of the north; but the Talmud itself has said, “The Galilean loves honor, and the Jew money.”\n\nHating Rome fervidly as they loved their own country, in every revolt they were first in the field and last to leave it. One hundred and fifty thousand Galilean youths perished in the final war with Rome. For the great festal days, they went up to Jerusalem marching and camping like armies; yet they were liberal in sentiment, and even tolerant to heathenism. In Herod’s beautiful cities, which were Roman in all things, in Sepphoris and Tiberias especially, they took pride, and in the building them gave loyal support. They had for fellow-citizens men from the outside world everywhere, and lived in peace with them. To the glory of the Hebrew name they contributed poets like the singer of the Song of Songs and prophets like Hosea.\n\nUpon such a people, so quick, so proud, so brave, so devoted, so imaginative, a tale like that of the coming of the King was all-powerful. That he was coming to put Rome down would have been sufficient to enlist them in the scheme proposed by Ben-Hur; but when, besides, they were assured he was to rule the world, more mighty than Cæsar, more magnificent than Solomon, and that the rule was to last forever, the appeal was irresistible, and they vowed themselves to the cause body and soul. They asked Ben-Hur his authority for the sayings, and he quoted the prophets, and told them of Balthasar in waiting over in Antioch; and they were satisfied, for it was the old much-loved legend of the Messiah, familiar to them almost as the name of the Lord; the long-cherished dream with a time fixed for its realization. The King was not merely coming now; he was at hand.\n\nSo with Ben-Hur the winter months rolled by, and spring came, with gladdening showers blown over from the summering sea in the west; and by that time so earnestly and successfully had he toiled that he could say to himself and his followers, “Let the good King come. He has only to tell us where he will have his throne set up. We have the sword-hands to keep it for him.”\n\nAnd in all his dealings with the many men they knew him only as a son of Judah, and by that name.\n\nOne evening, over in Trachonitis, Ben-Hur was sitting with some of his Galileans at the mouth of the cave in which he quartered, when an Arab courier rode to him, and delivered a letter. Breaking the package, he read,\n\n“Jerusalem, Nisan IV.\n\n“A prophet has appeared who men say is Elias. He has been in the wilderness for years, and to our eyes he is a prophet; and such also is his speech, the burden of which is of one much greater than himself, who, he says, is to come presently, and for whom he is now waiting on the eastern shore of the River Jordan. I have been to see and hear him, and the one he is waiting for is certainly the King you are awaiting. Come and judge for yourself.\n\n“All Jerusalem is going out to the prophet, and with many people else the shore on which he abides is like Mount Olivet in the last days of the Passover.\n\nBen-Hur’s face flushed with joy.\n\n“By this word, O my friends,” he said—“by this word, our waiting is at end. The herald of the King has appeared and announced him.”\n\nUpon hearing the letter read, they also rejoiced at the promise it held out.\n\n“Get ready now,” he added, “and in the morning set your faces homeward; when arrived there, send word to those under you, and bid them be ready to assemble as I may direct. For myself and you, I will go see if the King be indeed at hand, and send you report. Let us, in the meantime, live in the pleasure of the promise.”\n\nGoing into the cave, he addressed a letter to Ilderim, and another to Simonides, giving notice of the news received, and of his purpose to go up immediately to Jerusalem. The letters he despatched by swift messengers. When night fell, and the stars of direction came out, he mounted, and with an Arab guide set out for the Jordan, intending to strike the track of the caravans between Rabbath-Ammon and Damascus.\n\nThe guide was sure, and Aldebaran swift; so by midnight the two were out of the lava fastness speeding southward.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nIt was Ben-Hur’s purpose to turn aside at the break of day, and find a safe place in which to rest; but the dawn overtook him while out in the Desert, and he kept on, the guide promising to bring him afterwhile to a vale shut in by great rocks, where there were a spring, some mulberry-trees, and herbage in plenty for the horses.\n\nAs he rode thinking of the wondrous events so soon to happen, and of the changes they were to bring about in the affairs of men and nations, the guide, ever on the alert, called attention to an appearance of strangers behind them. Everywhere around the Desert stretched away in waves of sand, slowly yellowing in the growing light, and without any green thing visible. Over on the left, but still far off, a range of low mountains extended, apparently interminable. In the vacancy of such a waste an object in motion could not long continue a mystery.\n\n“It is a camel with riders,” the guide said, directly.\n\n“Are there others behind?” said Ben-Hur.\n\n“It is alone. No, there is a man on horseback—the driver, probably.”\n\nA little later Ben-Hur himself could see the camel was white and unusually large, reminding him of the wonderful animal he had seen bring Balthasar and Iras to the fountain in the Grove of Daphne. There could be no other like it. Thinking then of the fair Egyptian, insensibly his gait became slower, and at length fell into the merest loiter, until finally he could discern a curtained houdah, and two persons seated within it. If they were Balthasar and Iras! Should he make himself known to them? But it could not be: this was the Desert—and they were alone. But while he debated the question the long swinging stride of the camel brought its riders up to him. He heard the ringing of the tiny bells, and beheld the rich housings which had been so attractive to the crowd at the Castalian fount. He beheld also the Ethiopian, always attendant upon the Egyptians. The tall brute stopped close by his horse, and Ben-Hur, looking up, lo! Iras herself under the raised curtain looking down at him, her great swimming eyes bright with astonishment and inquiry!\n\n“The blessing of the true God upon you!” said Balthasar, in his tremulous voice.\n\n“And to thee and thine be the peace of the Lord,” Ben-Hur replied.\n\n“My eyes are weak with years,” said Balthasar; “but they approve you that son of Hur whom lately I knew an honored guest in the tent of Ilderim the Generous.”\n\n“And thou art that Balthasar, the wise Egyptian, whose speech concerning certain holy things in expectation is having so much to do with the finding me in this waste place. What dost thou here?”\n\n“He is never alone who is where God is—and God is everywhere,” Balthasar answered, gravely; “but in the sense of your asking, there is a caravan short way behind us going to Alexandria; and as it is to pass through Jerusalem, I thought best to avail myself of its company as far as the Holy City, whither I am journeying. This morning, however, in discontent with its slow movement—slower because of a Roman cohort in attendance upon it—we rose early, and ventured thus far in advance. As to robbers along the way, we are not afraid, for I have here a signet of Sheik Ilderim; against beasts of prey, God is our sufficient trust.”\n\nBen-Hur bowed and said, “The good sheik’s signet is a safeguard wherever the wilderness extends, and the lion shall be swift that overtakes this king of his kind.”\n\nHe patted the neck of the camel as he spoke.\n\n“Yet,” said Iras, with a smile which was not lost upon the youth, whose eyes, it must be admitted, had several times turned to her during the interchange of speeches with the elder—“Yet even he would be better if his fast were broken. Kings have hunger and headaches. If you be, indeed, the Ben-Hur of whom my father has spoken, and whom it was my pleasure to have known as well, you will be happy, I am sure, to show us some near path to living water, that with its sparkle we may grace a morning’s meal in the Desert.”\n\nBen-Hur, nothing loath, hastened to answer.\n\n“Fair Egyptian, I give you sympathy. Can you bear suffering a little longer, we will find the spring you ask for, and I promise that its draught shall be as sweet and cooling as that of the more famous Castalia. With leave, we will make haste.”\n\n“I give you the blessing of the thirsty,” she replied; “and offer you in return a bit of bread from the city ovens, dipped in fresh butter from the dewy meadows of Damascus.”\n\n“A most rare favor! Let us go on.”\n\nSo saying, Ben-Hur rode forward with the guide, one of the inconveniences of travelling with camels being that it is necessarily an interdiction of polite conversation.\n\nAfterwhile the party came to a shallow wady, down which, turning to the right hand, the guide led them. The bed of the cut was somewhat soft from recent rains, and quite bold in its descent. Momentarily, however, it widened; and erelong the sides became bluffs ribbed with rocks much scarred by floods rushing to lower depths ahead. Finally, from a narrow passage, the travellers entered a spreading vale which was very delightful; but come upon suddenly from the yellow, unrelieved, verdureless plain, it had the effect of a freshly discovered Paradise. The water-channels winding here and there, definable by crisp white shingling, appeared like threads tangled among islands green with grasses and fringed with reeds. Up from the final depths of the valley of the Jordan some venturous oleanders had crept, and with their large bloom now starred the sunken place. One palm-tree arose in royal assertion. The bases of the boundary-walls were cloaked with clambering vines, and under a leaning cliff over on the left the mulberry grove had planted itself, proclaiming the spring which the party were seeking. And thither the guide conducted them, careless of whistling partridges and lesser birds of brighter hues roused whirring from the reedy coverts.\n\nThe water started from a crack in the cliff which some loving hand had enlarged into an arched cavity. Graven over it in bold Hebraic letters was the word GOD. The graver had no doubt drunk there, and tarried many days, and given thanks in that durable form. From the arch the stream ran merrily over a flag spotted with bright moss, and leaped into a pool glassy clear; thence it stole away between grassy banks, nursing the trees before it vanished in the thirsty sand. A few narrow paths were noticeable about the margin of the pool; otherwise the space around was untrodden turf, at sight of which the guide was assured of rest free from intrusion by men. The horses were presently turned loose, and from the kneeling camel the Ethiopian assisted Balthasar and Iras; whereupon the old man, turning his face to the east, crossed his hands reverently upon his breast and prayed.\n\n“Bring me a cup,” Iras said, with some impatience.\n\nFrom the houdah the slave brought her a crystal goblet; then she said to Ben-Hur,\n\n“I will be your servant at the fountain.”\n\nThey walked to the pool together. He would have dipped the water for her, but she refused his offer, and kneeling, held the cup to be filled by the stream itself; nor yet content, when it was cooled and overrunning, she tendered him the first draught.\n\n“No,” he said, putting the graceful hand aside, and seeing only the large eyes half hidden beneath the arches of the upraised brows, “be the service mine, I pray.”\n\nShe persisted in having her way.\n\n“In my country, O son of Hur, we have a saying, ‘Better a cupbearer to the fortunate than minister to a king.’”\n\n“Fortunate!” he said.\n\nThere were both surprise and inquiry in the tone of his voice and in his look, and she said quickly,\n\n“The gods give us success as a sign by which we may know them on our side. Were you not winner in the Circus?”\n\nHis cheeks began to flush.\n\n“That was one sign. There is another. In a combat with swords you slew a Roman.”\n\nThe flush deepened—not so much for the triumphs themselves as the flattery there was in the thought that she had followed his career with interest. A moment, and the pleasure was succeeded by a reflection. The combat, he knew, was matter of report throughout the East; but the name of the victor had been committed to a very few—Malluch, Ilderim, and Simonides. Could they have made a confidante of the woman? So with wonder and gratification he was confused; and seeing it, she arose and said, holding the cup over the pool,\n\n“O gods of Egypt! I give thanks for a hero discovered—thanks that the victim in the Palace of Idernee was not my king of men. And so, O holy gods, I pour and drink.”\n\nPart of the contents of the cup she returned to the stream, the rest she drank. When she took the crystal from her lips, she laughed at him.\n\n“O son of Hur, is it a fashion of the very brave to be so easily overcome by a woman? Take the cup now, and see if you cannot find a happy word in it for me.”\n\nHe took the cup, and stooped to refill it.\n\n“A son of Israel has no gods whom he can libate,” he said, playing with the water to hide his amazement, now greater than before. What more did the Egyptian know about him? Had she been told of his relations with Simonides? And there was the treaty with Ilderim—had she knowledge of that also? He was struck with mistrust. Somebody had betrayed his secrets, and they were serious. And, besides, he was going to Jerusalem, just then of all the world the place where such intelligence possessed by an enemy might be most dangerous to him, his associates, and the cause. But was she an enemy? It is well for us that, while writing is slow, thought is instantaneous. When the cup was fairly cooled, he filled it and arose, saying, with indifference well affected,\n\n“Most fair, were I an Egyptian or a Greek or a Roman, I would say”—he raised the goblet overhead as he spoke—“O ye better gods! I give thanks that there are yet left to the world, despite its wrongs and sufferings, the charm of beauty and the solace of love, and I drink to her who best represents them—to Iras, loveliest of the daughters of the Nile!”\n\nShe laid her hand softly upon his shoulder.\n\n“You have offended against the law. The gods you have drunk to are false gods. Why shall I not tell the rabbis on you?”\n\n“Oh!” he replied, laughing, “that is very little to tell for one who knows so much else that is really important.”\n\n“I will go further—I will go to the little Jewess who makes the roses grow and the shadows flame in the house of the great merchant over in Antioch. To the rabbis I will accuse you of impenitence; to her—”\n\n“Well, to her?”\n\n“I will repeat what you have said to me under the lifted cup, with the gods for witnesses.”\n\nHe was still a moment, as if waiting for the Egyptian to go on. With quickened fancy he saw Esther at her father’s side listening to the despatches he had forwarded—sometimes reading them. In her presence he had told Simonides the story of the affair in the Palace of Idernee. She and Iras were acquainted; this one was shrewd and worldly; the other was simple and affectionate, and therefore easily won. Simonides could not have broken faith—nor Ilderim—for if not held by honor, there was no one, unless it might be himself, to whom the consequences of exposure were more serious and certain. Could Esther have been the Egyptian’s informant? He did not accuse her; yet a suspicion was sown with the thought, and suspicions, as we all know, are weeds of the mind which grow of themselves, and most rapidly when least wanted. Before he could answer the allusion to the little Jewess, Balthasar came to the pool.\n\n“We are greatly indebted to you, son of Hur,” he said, in his grave manner. “This vale is very beautiful; the grass, the trees, the shade, invite us to stay and rest, and the spring here has the sparkle of diamonds in motion, and sings to me of a loving God. It is not enough to thank you for the enjoyment we find; come sit with us, and taste our bread.”\n\n“Suffer me first to serve you.”\n\nWith that Ben-Hur filled the goblet, and gave it to Balthasar, who lifted his eyes in thanksgiving.\n\nImmediately the slave brought napkins; and after laving their hands and drying them, the three seated themselves in Eastern style under the tent which years before had served the Wise Men at the meeting in the Desert. And they ate heartily of the good things taken from the camel’s pack.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe tent was cosily pitched beneath a tree where the gurgle of the stream was constantly in ear. Overhead the broad leaves hung motionless on their stems; the delicate reed-stalks off in the pearly haze stood up arrowy-straight; occasionally a home-returning bee shot humming athwart the shade, and a partridge creeping from the sedge drank, whistled to his mate, and ran away. The restfulness of the vale, the freshness of the air, the garden beauty, the Sabbath stillness, seemed to have affected the spirits of the elder Egyptian; his voice, gestures, and whole manner were unusually gentle; and often as he bent his eyes upon Ben-Hur conversing with Iras, they softened with pity.\n\n“When we overtook you, son of Hur,” he said, at the conclusion of the repast, “it seemed your face was also turned towards Jerusalem. May I ask, without offence, if you are going so far?”\n\n“I am going to the Holy City.”\n\n“For the great need I have to spare myself prolonged toil, I will further ask you, Is there a shorter road than that by Rabbath-Ammon?”\n\n“A rougher route, but shorter, lies by Gerasa and Rabbath-Gilead. It is the one I design taking.”\n\n“I am impatient,” said Balthasar. “Latterly my sleep has been visited by dreams—or rather by the same dream in repetition. A voice—it is nothing more—comes and tells me, ‘Haste—arise! He whom thou hast so long awaited is at hand.’”\n\n“You mean he that is to be King of the Jews?” Ben-Hur asked, gazing at the Egyptian in wonder.\n\n“Even so.”\n“Then you have heard nothing of him?”\n\n“Nothing, except the words of the voice in the dream.”\n\n“Here, then, are tidings to make you glad as they made me.”\n\nFrom his gown Ben-Hur drew the letter received from Malluch. The hand the Egyptian held out trembled violently. He read aloud, and as he read his feelings increased; the limp veins in his neck swelled and throbbed. At the conclusion he raised his suffused eyes in thanksgiving and prayer. He asked no questions, yet had no doubts.\n\n“Thou hast been very good to me, O God,” he said. “Give me, I pray thee, to see the Saviour again, and worship him, and thy servant will be ready to go in peace.”\n\nThe words, the manner, the singular personality of the simple prayer, touched Ben-Hur with a sensation new and abiding. God never seemed so actual and so near by; it was as if he were there bending over them or sitting at their side—a Friend whose favors were to be had by the most unceremonious asking—a Father to whom all his children were alike in love—Father, not more of the Jew than of the Gentile—the Universal Father, who needed no intermediates, no rabbis, no priests, no teachers. The idea that such a God might send mankind a Saviour instead of a king appeared to Ben-Hur in a light not merely new, but so plain that he could almost discern both the greater want of such a gift and its greater consistency with the nature of such a Deity. So he could not resist asking,\n\n“Now that he has come, O Balthasar, you still think he is to be a Saviour, and not a king?”\n\nBalthasar gave him a look thoughtful as it was tender.\n\n“How shall I understand you?” he asked, in return. “The Spirit, which was the Star that was my guide of old, has not appeared to me since I met you in the tent of the good sheik; that is to say, I have not seen or heard it as formerly. I believe the voice that spoke to me in my dreams was it; but other than that I have no revelation.”\n\n“I will recall the difference between us,” said Ben-Hur, with deference. “You were of opinion that he would be a king, but not as Cæsar is; you thought his sovereignty would be spiritual, not of the world.”\n\n“Oh yes,” the Egyptian answered; “and I am of the same opinion now. I see the divergence in our faith. You are going to meet a king of men, I a Saviour of souls.”\n\nHe paused with the look often seen when people are struggling, with introverted effort, to disentangle a thought which is either too high for quick discernment or too subtle for simple expression.\n\n“Let me try, O son of Hur,” he said, directly, “and help you to a clear understanding of my belief; then it may be, seeing how the spiritual kingdom I expect him to set up can be more excellent in every sense than anything of mere Cæsarean splendor, you will better understand the reason of the interest I take in the mysterious person we are going to welcome.\n\n“I cannot tell you when the idea of a Soul in every man had its origin. Most likely the first parents brought it with them out of the garden in which they had their first dwelling. We all do know, however, that it has never perished entirely out of mind. By some peoples it was lost, but not by all; in some ages it dulled and faded, in others it was overwhelmed with doubts; but, in great goodness, God kept sending us at intervals mighty intellects to argue it back to faith and hope.\n\n“Why should there be a Soul in every man? Look, O son of Hur—for one moment look at the necessity of such a device. To lie down and die, and be no more—no more forever—time never was when man wished for such an end; nor has the man ever been who did not in his heart promise himself something better. The monuments of the nations are all protests against nothingness after death; so are statues and inscriptions; so is history. The greatest of our Egyptian kings had his effigy cut-out of a hill of solid rock. Day after day he went with a host in chariots to see the work; at last it was finished, never effigy so grand, so enduring: it looked like him—the features were his, faithful even in expression. Now may we not think of him saying in that moment of pride, ‘Let Death come; there is an after-life for me!’ He had his wish. The statue is there yet.\n\n“But what is the after-life he thus secured? Only a recollection by men—a glory unsubstantial as moonshine on the brow of the great bust; a story in stone—nothing more. Meantime what has become of the king? There is an embalmed body up in the royal tombs which once was his—an effigy not so fair to look at as the other out in the Desert. But where, O son of Hur, where is the king himself? Is he fallen into nothingness? Two thousand years have gone since he was a man alive as you and I are. Was his last breath the end of him?\n\n“To say yes would be to accuse God; let us rather accept his better plan of attaining life after death for us—actual life, I mean—the something more than a place in mortal memory; life with going and coming, with sensation, with knowledge, with power and all appreciation; life eternal in term though it may be with changes of condition.\n\n“Ask you what God’s plan is? The gift of a Soul to each of us at birth, with this simple law—there shall be no immortality except through the Soul. In that law see the necessity of which I spoke.\n\n“Let us turn from the necessity now. A word as to the pleasure there is in the thought of a Soul in each of us. In the first place, it robs death of its terrors by making dying a change for the better, and burial but the planting of a seed from which there will spring a new life. In the next place, behold me as I am—weak, weary, old, shrunken in body, and graceless; look at my wrinkled face, think of my failing senses, listen to my shrilled voice. Ah! what happiness to me in the promise that when the tomb opens, as soon it will, to receive the worn-out husk I call myself, the now viewless doors of the universe, which is but the palace of God, will swing wide ajar to receive me, a liberated immortal Soul!\n\n“I would I could tell the ecstasy there must be in that life to come! Do not say I know nothing about it. This much I know, and it is enough for me—the being a Soul implies conditions of divine superiority. In such a being there is no dust, nor any gross thing; it must be finer than air, more impalpable than light, purer than essence—it is life in absolute purity.\n\n“What now, O son of Hur? Knowing so much, shall I dispute with myself or you about the unnecessaries—about the form of my soul? Or where it is to abide? Or whether it eats and drinks? Or is winged, or wears this or that? No. It is more becoming to trust in God. The beautiful in this world is all from his hand declaring the perfection of taste; he is the author of all form; he clothes the lily, he colors the rose, he distils the dew-drop, he makes the music of nature; in a word, he organized us for this life, and imposed its conditions; and they are such guaranty to me that, trustful as a little child, I leave to him the organization of my Soul, and every arrangement for the life after death. I know he loves me.”\n\nThe good man stopped and drank, and the hand carrying the cup to his lips trembled; and both Iras and Ben-Hur shared his emotion and remained silent. Upon the latter a light was breaking. He was beginning to see, as never before, that there might be a spiritual kingdom of more import to men than any earthly empire; and that after all a Saviour would indeed be a more godly gift than the greatest king.\n\n“I might ask you now,” said Balthasar, continuing, “whether this human life, so troubled and brief, is preferable to the perfect and everlasting life designed for the Soul? But take the question, and think of it for yourself, formulating thus: Supposing both to be equally happy, is one hour more desirable than one year? From that then advance to the final inquiry, what are threescore and ten years on earth to all eternity with God? By-and-by, son of Hur, thinking in such manner, you will be filled with the meaning of the fact I present you next, to me the most amazing of all events, and in its effects the most sorrowful; it is that the very idea of life as a Soul is a light almost gone out in the world. Here and there, to be sure, a philosopher may be found who will talk to you of a Soul, likening it to a principle; but because philosophers take nothing upon faith, they will not go the length of admitting a Soul to be a being, and on that account its purpose is compressed darkness to them.\n\n“Everything animate has a mind measurable by its wants. Is there to you no meaning in the singularity that power in full degree to speculate upon the future was given to man alone? By the sign as I see it, God meant to make us know ourselves created for another and a better life, such being in fact the greatest need of our nature. But, alas! into what a habit the nations have fallen! They live for the day, as if the present were the all in all, and go about saying, ‘There is no to-morrow after death; or if there be, since we know nothing about it, be it a care unto itself.’ So when Death calls them, ‘Come,’ they may not enter into enjoyment of the glorious after-life because of their unfitness. That is to say, the ultimate happiness of man was everlasting life in the society of God. Alas, O son of Hur, that I should say it! but as well yon sleeping camel constant in such society as the holiest priests this day serving the highest altars in the most renowned temples. So much are men given to this lower earthly life! So nearly have they forgotten that other which is to come!\n\n“See now, I pray you, that which is to be saved to us.\n\n“For my part, speaking with the holiness of truth, I would not give one hour of life as a Soul for a thousand years of life as a man.”\n\nHere the Egyptian seemed to become unconscious of companionship and fall away into abstraction.\n\n“This life has its problems,” he said, “and there are men who spend their days trying to solve them; but what are they to the problems of the hereafter? What is there like knowing God? Not a scroll of the mysteries, but the mysteries themselves would for that hour at least lie before me revealed; even the innermost and most awful—the power which now we shrink from thought of—which rimmed the void with shores, and lighted the darkness, and out of nothing appointed the universe. All places would be opened. I would be filled with divine knowledge; I would see all glories, taste all delights; I would revel in being. And if, at the end of the hour, it should please God to tell me, ‘I take thee into my service forever,’ the furthest limit of desire would be passed; after which the attainable ambitions of life, and its joys of whatever kind, would not be so much as the tinkling of little bells.”\n\nBalthasar paused as if to recover from very ecstasy of feeling; and to Ben-Hur it seemed the speech had been the delivery of a Soul speaking for itself.\n\n“I pray pardon, son of Hur,” the good man continued, with a bow the gravity of which was relieved by the tender look that followed it, “I meant to leave the life of a Soul, its conditions, pleasures, superiority, to your own reflection and finding out. The joy of the thought has betrayed me into much speech. I set out to show, though ever so faintly, the reason of my faith. It grieves me that words are so weak. But help yourself to truth. Consider first the excellence of the existence which was reserved for us after death, and give heed to the feelings and impulses the thought is sure to awaken in you—heed them, I say, because they are your own Soul astir, doing what it can to urge you in the right way. Consider next that the afterlife has become so obscured as to justify calling it a lost light. If you find it, rejoice, O son of Hur—rejoice as I do, though in beggary of words. For then, besides the great gift which is to be saved to us, you will have found the need of a Saviour so infinitely greater than the need of a king; and he we are going to meet will not longer hold place in your hope a warrior with a sword or a monarch with a crown.\n\n“A practical question presents itself—How shall we know him at sight? If you continue in your belief as to his character—that he is to be a king as Herod was—of course you will keep on until you meet a man clothed in purple and with a sceptre. On the other hand, he I look for will be one poor, humble, undistinguished—a man in appearance as other men; and the sign by which I will know him will be never so simple. He will offer to show me and all mankind the way to the eternal life; the beautiful pure Life of the Soul.”\n\nThe company sat a moment in silence which was broken by Balthasar.\n\n“Let us arise now,” he said—“let us arise and set forward again. What I have said has caused a return of impatience to see him who is ever in my thought; and if I seem to hurry you, O son of Hur—and you, my daughter—be that my excuse.”\n\nAt his signal the slave brought them wine in a skin bottle; and they poured and drank, and shaking the lap-cloths out arose.\n\nWhile the slave restored the tent and wares to the box under the houdah, and the Arab brought up the horses, the three principals laved themselves in the pool.\n\nIn a little while they were retracing their steps back through the wady, intending to overtake the caravan if it had passed them by.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nThe caravan, stretched out upon the Desert, was very picturesque; in motion, however, it was like a lazy serpent. By-and-by its stubborn dragging became intolerably irksome to Balthasar, patient as he was; so, at his suggestion, the party determined to go on by themselves.\n\nIf the reader be young, or if he has yet a sympathetic recollection of the romanticisms of his youth, he will relish the pleasure with which Ben-Hur, riding near the camel of the Egyptians, gave a last look at the head of the straggling column almost out of sight on the shimmering plain.\n\nTo be definite as may be, and perfectly confidential, Ben-Hur found a certain charm in Iras’s presence. If she looked down upon him from her high place, he made haste to get near her; if she spoke to him, his heart beat out of its usual time. The desire to be agreeable to her was a constant impulse. Objects on the way, though ever so common, became interesting the moment she called attention to them; a black swallow in the air pursued by her pointing finger went off in a halo; if a bit of quartz or a flake of mica was seen to sparkle in the drab sand under kissing of the sun, at a word he turned aside and brought it to her; and if she threw it away in disappointment, far from thinking of the trouble he had been put to, he was sorry it proved so worthless, and kept a lookout for something better—a ruby, perchance a diamond. So the purple of the far mountains became intensely deep and rich if she distinguished it with an exclamation of praise; and when, now and then, the curtain of the houdah fell down, it seemed a sudden dulness had dropped from the sky bedraggling all the landscape. Thus disposed, yielding to the sweet influence, what shall save him from the dangers there are in days of the close companionship with the fair Egyptian incident to the solitary journey they were entered upon?\n\nFor that there is no logic in love, nor the least mathematical element, it is simply natural that she shall fashion the result who has the wielding of the influence.\n\nTo quicken the conclusion, there were signs, too, that she well knew the influence she was exercising over him. From some place under hand she had since morning drawn a caul of golden coins, and adjusted it so the gleaming strings fell over her forehead and upon her cheeks, blending lustrously with the flowing of her blue-black hair. From the same safe deposit she had also produced articles of jewelry—rings for finger and ear, bracelets, a necklace of pearls—also, a shawl embroidered with threads of fine gold—the effect of all which she softened with a scarf of Indian lace skillfully folded about her throat and shoulders. And so arrayed, she plied Ben-Hur with countless coquetries of speech and manner; showering him with smiles; laughing in flute-like tremolo—and all the while following him with glances, now melting-tender, now sparkling-bright. By such play Antony was weaned from his glory; yet she who wrought his ruin was really not half so beautiful as this her countrywoman.\n\nAnd so to them the nooning came, and the evening.\n\nThe sun at its going down behind a spur of the old Bashan, left the party halted by a pool of clear water of the rains out in the Abilene Desert. There the tent was pitched, the supper eaten, and preparations made for the night.\n\nThe second watch was Ben-Hur’s; and he was standing, spear in hand, within arm-reach of the dozing camel, looking awhile at the stars, then over the veiled land. The stillness was intense; only after long spells a warm breath of wind would sough past, but without disturbing him, for yet in thought he entertained the Egyptian, recounting her charms, and sometimes debating how she came by his secrets, the uses she might make of them, and the course he should pursue with her. And through all the debate Love stood off but a little way—a strong temptation, the stronger of a gleam of policy behind. At the very moment he was most inclined to yield to the allurement, a hand very fair even in the moonless gloaming was laid softly upon his shoulder. The touch thrilled him; he started, turned—and she was there.\n\n“I thought you asleep,” he said, presently.\n\n“Sleep is for old people and little children, and I came out to look at my friends, the stars in the south—those now holding the curtains of midnight over the Nile. But confess yourself surprised!”\n\nHe took the hand which had fallen from his shoulder, and said, “Well, was it by an enemy?”\n\n“Oh no! To be an enemy is to hate, and hating is a sickness which Isis will not suffer to come near me. She kissed me, you should know, on the heart when I was a child.”\n\n“Your speech does not sound in the least like your father’s. Are you not of his faith?”\n\n“I might have been”—and she laughed low—“I might have been had I seen what he has. I may be when I get old like him. There should be no religion for youth, only poetry and philosophy; and no poetry except such as is the inspiration of wine and mirth and love, and no philosophy that does not nod excuse for follies which cannot outlive a season. My father’s God is too awful for me. I failed to find him in the Grove of Daphne. He was never heard of as present in the atria of Rome. But, son of Hur, I have a wish.”\n\n“A wish! Where is he who could say it no?”\n\n“I will try you.”\n\n“Tell it then.”\n\n“It is very simple. I wish to help you.”\n\nShe drew closer as she spoke.\n\nHe laughed, and replied, lightly, “O Egypt!—I came near saying dear Egypt!—does not the sphinx abide in your country?”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“You are one of its riddles. Be merciful, and give me a little clew to help me understand you. In what do I need help? And how can you help me?”\n\nShe took her hand from him, and, turning to the camel, spoke to it endearingly, and patted its monstrous head as it were a thing of beauty.\n\n“O thou last and swiftest and stateliest of the herds of Job! Sometimes thou, too, goest stumbling, because the way is rough and stony and the burden grievous. How is it thou knowest the kind intent by a word; and always makest answer gratefully, though the help offered is from a woman? I will kiss thee, thou royal brute!”—she stooped and touched its broad forehead with her lips, saying immediately, “because in thy intelligence there is no suspicion!”\n\nAnd Ben-Hur, restraining himself, said calmly, “The reproach has not failed its mark, O Egypt! I seem to say thee no; may it not be because I am under seal of honor, and by my silence cover the lives and fortunes of others?”\n\n“May be!” she said, quickly. “It is so.”\n\nHe shrank a step, and asked, his voice sharp with amazement, “What all knowest thou?”\n\nShe answered, after a laugh,\n\n“Why do men deny that the senses of women are sharper than theirs? Your face has been under my eyes all day. I had but to look at it to see you bore some weight in mind; and to find the weight, what had I to do more than recall your debates with my father? Son of Hur!”—she lowered her voice with singular dexterity, and, going nearer, spoke so her breath was warm upon his cheek—“son of Hur! he thou art going to find is to be King of the Jews, is he not?”\n\nHis heart beat fast and hard.\n\n“A King of the Jews like Herod, only greater,” she continued.\n\nHe looked away—into the night, up to the stars; then his eyes met hers, and lingered there; and her breath was on his lips, so near was she.\n\n“Since morning,” she said, further, “we have been having visions. Now if I tell you mine, will you serve me as well? What! silent still?”\n\nShe pushed his hand away, and turned as if to go; but he caught her, and said, eagerly, “Stay—stay and speak!”\n\nShe went back, and with her hand upon his shoulder, leaned against him; and he put his arm around her, and drew her close, very close; and in the caress was the promise she asked.\n\n“Speak, and tell me thy visions, O Egypt, dear Egypt! A prophet—nay, not the Tishbite, not even the Lawgiver—could have refused an asking of thine. I am at thy will. Be merciful—merciful, I pray.”\n\nThe entreaty passed apparently unheard, for looking up and nestling in his embrace, she said, slowly, “The vision which followed me was of magnificent war—war on land and sea—with clashing of arms and rush of armies, as if Cæsar and Pompey were come again, and Octavius and Antony. A cloud of dust and ashes arose and covered the world, and Rome was not any more; all dominion returned to the East; out of the cloud issued another race of heroes; and there were vaster satrapies and brighter crowns for giving away than were ever known. And, son of Hur, while the vision was passing, and after it was gone, I kept asking myself, ‘What shall he not have who served the King earliest and best?’”\n\nAgain Ben-Hur recoiled. The question was the very question which had been with him all day. Presently he fancied he had the clew he wanted.\n\n“So,” he said, “I have you now. The satrapies and crowns are the things to which you would help me. I see, I see! And there never was such queen as you would be, so shrewd, so beautiful, so royal—never! But, alas, dear Egypt! by the vision as you show it me the prizes are all of war, and you are but a woman, though Isis did kiss you on the heart. And crowns are starry gifts beyond your power of help, unless, indeed, you have a way to them more certain than that of the sword. If so, O Egypt, Egypt, show it me, and I will walk in it, if only for your sake.”\n\nShe removed his arm, and said, “Spread your cloak upon the sand—here, so I can rest against the camel. I will sit, and tell you a story which came down the Nile to Alexandria, where I had it.”\n\nHe did as she said, first planting the spear in the ground near by.\n\n“And what shall I do?” he said, ruefully, when she was seated. “In Alexandria is it customary for the listeners to sit or stand?”\n\nFrom the comfortable place against the old domestic she answered, laughing, “The audiences of story-tellers are wilful, and sometimes they do as they please.”\n\nWithout more ado he stretched himself upon the sand, and put her arm about his neck.\n\n“I am ready,” he said.\n\nAnd directly she began:\n\n# HOW THE BEAUTIFUL CAME TO THE EARTH.\n\n“You must know, in the first place, that Isis was—and, for that matter, she may yet be—the most beautiful of deities; and Osiris, her husband, though wise and powerful, was sometimes stung with jealousy of her, for only in their loves are the gods like mortals.\n\n“The palace of the Divine Wife was of silver, crowning the tallest mountain in the moon, and thence she passed often to the sun, in the heart of which, a source of eternal light, Osiris kept his palace of gold too shining for men to look at.\n\n“One time—there are no days with the gods—while she was full pleasantly with him on the roof of the golden palace, she chanced to look, and afar, just on the line of the universe, saw Indra passing with an army of simians, all borne upon the backs of flying eagles. He, the Friend of Living Things—so with much love is Indra called—was returning from his final war with the hideous Rakshakas—returning victorious; and in his suite were Rama, the hero, and Sita, his bride, who, next to Isis herself, was the very most beautiful. And Isis arose, and took off her girdle of stars, and waved it to Sita—to Sita, mind you—waved it in glad salute. And instantly, between the marching host and the two on the golden roof, a something as of night fell, and shut out the view; but it was not night—only the frown of Osiris.\n\n“It happened the subject of his speech that moment was such as none else than they could think of; and he arose, and said, majestically, ‘Get thee home. I will do the work myself. To make a perfectly happy being I do not need thy help. Get thee gone.’\n\n“Now Isis had eyes large as those of the white cow which in the temple eats sweet grasses from the hands of the faithful even while they say their prayers; and her eyes were the color of the cows, and quite as tender. And she too arose and said, smiling as she spoke, so her look was little more than the glow of the moon in the hazy harvest-month, ‘Farewell, good my lord. You will call me presently, I know; for without me you cannot make the perfectly happy creature of which you were thinking, any more’—and she stopped to laugh, knowing well the truth of the saying—‘any more, my lord, than you yourself can be perfectly happy without me.’\n\n“‘We will see,’ he said.\n\n“And she went her way, and took her needles and her chair, and on the roof of the silver palace sat watching and knitting.\n\n“And the will of Osiris, at labor in his mighty breast, was as the sound of the mills of all the other gods grinding at once, so loud that the near stars rattled like seeds in a parched pod; and some dropped out and were lost. And while the sound kept on she waited and knit; nor lost she ever a stitch the while.\n\n“Soon a spot appeared in the space over towards the sun; and it grew until it was great as the moon, and then she knew a world was intended; but when, growing and growing, at last it cast her planet in the shade, all save the little point lighted by her presence, she knew how very angry he was; yet she knit away, assured that the end would be as she had said.\n\n“And so came the earth, at first but a cold gray mass hanging listless in the hollow void. Later she saw it separate into divisions; here a plain, there a mountain, yonder a sea, all as yet without a sparkle. And then, by a river-bank, something moved; and she stopped her knitting for wonder. The something arose, and lifted its hands to the sun in sign of knowledge whence it had its being. And this First Man was beautiful to see. And about him were the creations we call nature—the grass, the trees, birds, beasts, even the insects and reptiles.\n\n“And for a time the man went about happy in his life: it was easy to see how happy he was. And in the lull of the sound of the laboring will Isis heard a scornful laugh, and presently the words, blown across from the sun,\n\n“‘Thy help, indeed! Behold a creature perfectly happy!’\n\n“And Isis fell to knitting again, for she was patient as Osiris was strong; and if he could work, she could wait; and wait she did, knowing that mere life is not enough to keep anything content.\n\n“And sure enough. Not long until the Divine Wife could see a change in the man. He grew listless, and kept to one place prone by the river, and looked up but seldom, and then always with a moody face. Interest was dying in him. And when she made sure of it, even while she was saying to herself, ‘The creature is sick of his being,’ there was a roar of the creative will at work again, and in a twinkling the earth, theretofore all a thing of coldest gray, flamed with colors; the mountains swam in purple, the plains bearing grass and trees turned green, the sea blue, and the clouds varied infinitely.”\n\nAnd the man sprang up and clapped his hands, for he was cured and happy again.\n\n“And Isis smiled, and knit away, saying to herself, ‘It was well thought, and will do a little while; but mere beauty in a world is not enough for such a being. My lord must try again.’\n\n“With the last word, the thunder of the will at work shook the moon, and, looking, Isis dropped her knitting and clapped her hands; for theretofore everything on the earth but the man had been fixed to a given place; now all living, and much that was not living, received the gift of Motion. The birds took to wing joyously; beasts great and small went about, each in its way; the trees shook their verdurous branches, nodding to the enamoured winds; the rivers ran to the seas, and the seas tossed in their beds and rolled in crested waves, and with surging and ebbing painted the shores with glistening foam; and over all the clouds floated like sailed ships unanchored.\n\n“And the man rose up happy as a child; whereat Osiris was pleased, so that he shouted, ‘Ha, ha! See how well I am doing without thee!’\n\n“The good wife took up her work, and answered ever so quietly, ‘It was well thought, my lord—ever so well thought—and will serve awhile.’\n\n“And as before, so again. The sight of things in motion became to the man as of course. The birds in flight, the rivers running, the seas in tumult of action, ceased to amuse him, and he pined again even worse.\n\n“And Isis waited, saying to herself, ‘Poor creature! He is more wretched than ever.’\n\n“And, as if he heard the thought, Osiris stirred, and the noise of his will shook the universe; the sun in its central seat alone stood firm. And Isis looked, but saw no change; then while she was smiling, assured that her lord’s last invention was sped, suddenly the creature arose, and seemed to listen; and his face brightened, and he clapped his hands for joy, for Sounds were heard the first time on earth—sounds dissonant, sounds harmonious. The winds murmured in the trees; the birds sang, each kind a song of its own, or chattered in speech; the rivulets running to the rivers became so many harpers with harps of silver strings all tinkling together; and the rivers running to the seas surged on in solemn accord, while the seas beat the land to a tune of thunder. There was music, music everywhere, and all the time; so the man could not but be happy.\n\n“Then Isis mused, thinking how well, how wondrous well, her lord was doing; but presently she shook her head: Color, Motion, Sound—and she repeated them slowly—there was no element else of beauty except Form and Light, and to them the earth had been born. Now, indeed, Osiris was done; and if the creature should again fall off into wretchedness, her help must be asked; and her fingers flew—two, three, five, even ten stitches she took at once.\n\n“And the man was happy a long time—longer than ever before; it seemed, indeed, he would never tire again. But Isis knew better; and she waited and waited, nor minded the many laughs flung at her from the sun; she waited and waited, and at last saw signs of the end. Sounds became familiar to him, and in their range, from the chirruping of the cricket under the roses to the roar of the seas and the bellow of the clouds in storm, there was not anything unusual. And he pined and sickened, and sought his place of moping by the river, and at last fell down motionless.\n\n“Then Isis in pity spoke.\n“‘My lord,’ she said, ‘the creature is dying.’\n\n“But Osiris, though seeing it all, held his peace; he could do no more.\n\n“‘Shall I help him?’ she asked.\n\n“Osiris was too proud to speak.\n\n“Then Isis took the last stitch in her knitting, and gathering her work in a roll of brilliance flung it off—flung it so it fell close to the man. And he, hearing the sound of the fall so near by, looked up, and lo! a Woman—the First Woman—was stooping to help him! She reached a hand to him; he caught it and arose; and nevermore was miserable, but evermore happy.”\n\n“Such, O son of Hur! is the genesis of the beautiful, as they tell it on the Nile.”\n\nShe paused.\n\n“A pretty invention, and cunning,” he said, directly; “but it is imperfect. What did Osiris afterwards?”\n\n“Oh yes,” she replied. “He called the Divine Wife back to the sun, and they went on all pleasantly together, each helping the other.”\n\n“And shall I not do as the first man?”\n\nHe carried the hand resting upon his neck to his lips. “In love—in love!” he said.\n\nHis head dropped softly into her lap.\n\n“You will find the King,” she said, placing her other hand caressingly upon his head. “You will go on and find the King and serve him. With your sword you will earn his richest gifts; and his best soldier will be my hero.”\n\nHe turned his face, and saw hers close above. In all the sky there was that moment nothing so bright to him as her eyes, enshadowed though they were. Presently he sat up, and put his arms about her, and kissed her passionately, saying, “O Egypt, Egypt! If the King has crowns in gift, one shall be mine; and I will bring it and put it here over the place my lips have marked. You shall be a queen—my queen—no one more beautiful! And we will be ever, ever so happy!”\n\n“And you will tell me everything, and let me help you in all?” she said, kissing him in return.\n\nThe question chilled his fervor.\n\n“Is it not enough that I love you?” he asked.\n\n“Perfect love means perfect faith,” she replied. “But never mind—you will know me better.”\n\nShe took her hand from him and arose.\n\n“You are cruel,” he said.\n\nMoving away, she stopped by the camel, and touched its front face with her lips.\n\n“O thou noblest of thy kind!—that, because there is no suspicion in thy love.”\n\nAn instant, and she was gone.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThe third day of the journey the party nooned by the river Jabbok, where there were a hundred or more men, mostly of Peraea, resting themselves and their beasts. Hardly had they dismounted, before a man came to them with a pitcher of water and a bowl, and offered them drink; as they received the attention with much courtesy, he said, looking at the camel, “I am returning from the Jordan, where just now there are many people from distant parts, travelling as you are, illustrious friend; but they had none of them the equal of your servant here. A very noble animal. May I ask of what breed he is sprung?”\n\nBalthasar answered, and sought his rest; but Ben-Hur, more curious, took up the remark.\n\n“At what place on the river are the people?” he asked.\n\n“At Bethabara.”\n\n“It used to be a lonesome ford,” said Ben-Hur. “I cannot understand how it can have become of such interest.”\n\n“I see,” the stranger replied; “you, too, are from abroad, and have not heard the good tidings.”\n\n“What tidings?”\n\n“Well, a man has appeared out of the wilderness—a very holy man—with his mouth full of strange words, which take hold of all who hear them. He calls himself John the Nazarite, son of Zacharias, and says he is the messenger sent before the Messiah.”\n\nEven Iras listened closely while the man continued:\n\n“They say of this John that he has spent his life from childhood in a cave down by En-Gedi, praying and living more strictly than the Essenes. Crowds go to hear him preach. I went to hear him with the rest.”\n\n“Have all these, your friends, been there?”\n\n“Most of them are going; a few are coming away.”\n\n“What does he preach?”\n\n“A new doctrine—one never before taught in Israel, as all say. He calls it repentance and baptism. The rabbis do not know what to make of him; nor do we. Some have asked him if he is the Christ, others if he is Elias; but to them all he has the answer, ‘I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness, Make straight the way of the Lord!’”\n\nAt this point the man was called away by his friends; as he was going, Balthasar spoke.\n\n“Good stranger!” he said, tremulously, “tell us if we shall find the preacher at the place you left him.”\n\n“Yes, at Bethabara.”\n\n“Who should this Nazarite be?” said Ben-Hur to Iras, “if not the herald of our King?”\n\nIn so short a time he had come to regard the daughter as more interested in the mysterious personage he was looking for than the aged father! Nevertheless, the latter with a positive glow in his sunken eyes half arose, and said,\n\n“Let us make haste. I am not tired.”\n\nThey turned away to help the slave.\n\nThere was little conversation between the three at the stopping-place for the night west of Ramoth-Gilead.\n\n“Let us arise early, son of Hur,” said the old man. “The Saviour may come, and we not there.”\n\n“The King cannot be far behind his herald,” Iras whispered, as she prepared to take her place on the camel.\n\n“To-morrow we will see!” Ben-Hur replied, kissing her hand.\n\nNext day about the third hour, out of the pass through which, skirting the base of Mount Gilead, they had journeyed since leaving Ramoth, the party came upon the barren steppe east of the sacred river. Opposite them they saw the upper limit of the old palm lands of Jericho, stretching off to the hill-country of Judea. Ben-Hur’s blood ran quickly, for he knew the ford was close at hand.\n\n“Content you, good Balthasar,” he said; “we are almost there.”\n\nThe driver quickened the camel’s pace. Soon they caught sight of booths and tents and tethered animals; and then of the river, and a multitude collected down close by the bank, and yet another multitude on the western shore. Knowing that the preacher was preaching, they made greater haste; yet, as they were drawing near, suddenly there was a commotion in the mass, and it began to break up and disperse.\n\nThey were too late!\n\n“Let us stay here,” said Ben-Hur to Balthasar, who was wringing his hands. “The Nazarite may come this way.”\n\nThe people were too intent upon what they had heard, and too busy in discussion, to notice the new-comers. When some hundreds were gone by, and it seemed the opportunity to so much as see the Nazarite was lost to the latter, up the river not far away they beheld a person coming towards them of such singular appearance they forgot all else.\n\nOutwardly the man was rude and uncouth, even savage. Over a thin, gaunt visage of the hue of brown parchment, over his shoulders and down his back below the middle, in witch-like locks, fell a covering of sun-scorched hair. His eyes were burning-bright. All his right side was naked, and of the color of his face, and quite as meagre; a shirt of the coarsest camel’s-hair—coarse as Bedouin tent-cloth—clothed the rest of his person to the knees, being gathered at the waist by a broad girdle of untanned leather. His feet were bare. A scrip, also of untanned leather, was fastened to the girdle. He used a knotted staff to help him forward. His movement was quick, decided, and strangely watchful. Every little while he tossed the unruly hair from his eyes, and peered round as if searching for somebody.\n\nThe fair Egyptian surveyed the son of the Desert with surprise, not to say disgust. Presently, raising the curtain of the houdah, she spoke to Ben-Hur, who sat his horse near by.\n\n“Is that the herald of thy King?”\n\n“It is the Nazarite,” he replied, without looking up.\n\nIn truth, he was himself more than disappointed. Despite his familiarity with the ascetic colonists in En-Gedi—their dress, their indifference to all worldly opinion, their constancy to vows which gave them over to every imaginable suffering of body, and separated them from others of their kind as absolutely as if they had not been born like them—and notwithstanding he had been notified on the way to look for a Nazarite whose simple description of himself was a Voice from the Wilderness—still Ben-Hur’s dream of the King who was to be so great and do so much had colored all his thought of him, so that he never doubted to find in the forerunner some sign or token of the goodliness and royalty he was announcing. Gazing at the savage figure before him, the long trains of courtiers whom he had been used to see in the thermae and imperial corridors at Rome arose before him, forcing a comparison. Shocked, shamed, bewildered, he could only answer,\n\n“It is the Nazarite.”\n\nWith Balthasar it was very different. The ways of God, he knew, were not as men would have them. He had seen the Saviour a child in a manger, and was prepared by his faith for the rude and simple in connection with the Divine reappearance. So he kept his seat, his hands crossed upon his breast, his lips moving in prayer. He was not expecting a king.\n\nIn this time of such interest to the new-comers, and in which they were so differently moved, another man had been sitting by himself on a stone at the edge of the river, thinking yet, probably, of the sermon he had been hearing. Now, however, he arose, and walked slowly up from the shore, in a course to take him across the line the Nazarite was pursuing and bring him near the camel.\n\nAnd the two—the preacher and the stranger—kept on until they came, the former within twenty yards of the animal, the latter within ten feet. Then the preacher stopped, and flung the hair from his eyes, looked at the stranger, threw his hands up as a signal to all the people in sight; and they also stopped, each in the pose of a listener; and when the hush was perfect, slowly the staff in the Nazarite’s right hand came down and pointed to the stranger.\n\nAll those who before were but listeners became watchers also.\n\nAt the same instant, under the same impulse, Balthasar and Ben-Hur fixed their gaze upon the man pointed out, and both took the same impression, only in different degree. He was moving slowly towards them in a clear space a little to their front, a form slightly above the average in stature, and slender, even delicate. His action was calm and deliberate, like that habitual to men much given to serious thought upon grave subjects; and it well became his costume, which was an undergarment full-sleeved and reaching to the ankles, and an outer robe called the talith; on his left arm he carried the usual handkerchief for the head, the red fillet swinging loose down his side. Except the fillet and a narrow border of blue at the lower edge of the talith, his attire was of linen yellowed with dust and road stains. Possibly the exception should be extended to the tassels, which were blue and white, as prescribed by law for rabbis. His sandals were of the simplest kind. He was without scrip or girdle or staff.\n\nThese points of appearance, however, the three beholders observed briefly, and rather as accessories to the head and face of the man, which—especially the latter—were the real sources of the spell they caught in common with all who stood looking at him.\n\nThe head was open to the cloudless light, except as it was draped with hair long and slightly waved, and parted in the middle, and auburn in tint, with a tendency to reddish golden where most strongly touched by the sun. Under a broad, low forehead, under black well arched brows, beamed eyes dark-blue and large, and softened to exceeding tenderness by lashes of the great length sometimes seen on children, but seldom, if ever, on men. As to the other features, it would have been difficult to decide whether they were Greek or Jewish. The delicacy of the nostrils and mouth was unusual to the latter type; and when it was taken into account with the gentleness of the eyes, the pallor of the complexion, the fine texture of the hair, and the softness of the beard, which fell in waves over his throat to his breast, never a soldier but would have laughed at him in encounter, never a woman who would not have confided in him at sight, never a child that would not, with quick instinct, have given him its hand and whole artless trust; nor might any one have said he was not beautiful.\n\nThe features, it should be further said, were ruled by a certain expression which, as the viewer chose, might with equal correctness have been called the effect of intelligence, love, pity, or sorrow; though, in better speech, it was a blending of them all—a look easy to fancy as the mark of a sinless soul doomed to the sight and understanding of the utter sinfulness of those among whom it was passing; yet withal no one could have observed the face with a thought of weakness in the man; so, at least, would not they who know that the qualities mentioned—love, sorrow, pity—are the results of a consciousness of strength to bear suffering oftener than strength to do; such has been the might of martyrs and devotees and the myriads written down in saintly calendars. And such, indeed, was the air of this one.\n\nSlowly he drew near—nearer the three.\n\nNow Ben-Hur, mounted and spear in hand, was an object to claim the glance of a king; yet the eyes of the man approaching were all the time raised above him—and not to Iras, whose loveliness has been so often remarked, but to Balthasar, the old and unserviceable.\n\nThe hush was profound.\n\nPresently the Nazarite, still pointing with his staff, cried, in a loud voice,\n\n“Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world!”\n\nThe many standing still, arrested by the action of the speaker, and listening for what might follow, were struck with awe by words so strange and past their understanding; upon Balthasar they were overpowering. He was there to see once more the Redeemer of men. The faith which had brought him the singular privileges of the time long gone abode yet in his heart; and if now it gave him a power of vision above that of his fellows—a power to see and know him for whom he was looking—better than calling the power a miracle, let it be thought of as the faculty of a soul not yet entirely released from the divine relations to which it had been formerly admitted, or as the fitting reward of a life in that age so without examples of holiness—a life itself a miracle. The ideal of his faith was before him, perfect in face, form, dress, action, age; and he was in its view, and the view was recognition. Ah, now if something should happen to identify the stranger beyond all doubt!\n\nAnd that was what did happen.\n\nExactly at the fitting moment, as if to assure the trembling Egyptian, the Nazarite repeated the outcry,\n\n“Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world!”\n\nBalthasar fell upon his knees. For him there was no need of explanation; and as if the Nazarite knew it, he turned to those more immediately about him staring in wonder, and continued:\n\n“This is he of whom I said, After me cometh a man which is preferred before me, for he was before me. And I knew him not: but that he should be manifest to Israel, therefore am I come baptizing with water. I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove, and it abode upon him. And I knew him not: but he that sent me to baptize with water, the same said unto me, Upon whom thou shalt see the Spirit descending and remaining on him, the same is he which baptizeth with the Holy Ghost. And I saw and bare record, that this”—he paused, his staff still pointing at the stranger in the white garments, as if to give a more absolute certainty to both his words and the conclusions intended—“I bare record, THAT THIS IS THE SON OF GOD!”\n\n“It is he, it is he!” Balthasar cried, with upraised tearful eyes. Next moment he sank down insensible.\n\nIn this time, it should be remembered, Ben-Hur was studying the face of the stranger, though with an interest entirely different. He was not insensible to its purity of feature, and its thoughtfulness, tenderness, humility, and holiness; but just then there was room in his mind for but one thought—Who is this man? And what? Messiah or king? Never was apparition more unroyal. Nay, looking at that calm, benignant countenance, the very idea of war and conquest, and lust of dominion, smote him like a profanation. He said, as if speaking to his own heart, Balthasar must be right and Simonides wrong. This man has not come to rebuild the throne of Solomon; he has neither the nature nor the genius of Herod; king he may be, but not of another and greater than Rome.\n\nIt should be understood now that this was not a conclusion with Ben-Hur, but an impression merely; and while it was forming, while yet he gazed at the wonderful countenance, his memory began to throe and struggle. “Surely,” he said to himself, “I have seen the man; but where and when?” That the look, so calm, so pitiful, so loving, had somewhere in a past time beamed upon him as that moment it was beaming upon Balthasar became an assurance. Faintly at first, at last a clear light, a burst of sunshine, the scene by the well at Nazareth what time the Roman guard was dragging him to the galleys returned, and all his being thrilled. Those hands had helped him when he was perishing. The face was one of the pictures he had carried in mind ever since. In the effusion of feeling excited, the explanation of the preacher was lost by him, all but the last words—words so marvellous that the world yet rings with them:\n\n“—this is the SON OF GOD!”\n\nBen-Hur leaped from his horse to render homage to his benefactor; but Iras cried to him, “Help, son of Hur, help, or my father will die!”\n\nHe stopped, looked back, then hurried to her assistance. She gave him a cup; and leaving the slave to bring the camel to its knees, he ran to the river for water. The stranger was gone when he came back.\n\nAt last Balthasar was restored to consciousness. Stretching forth his hands, he asked, feebly, “Where is he?”\n\n“Who?” asked Iras.\n\nAn intense instant interest shone upon the good man’s face, as if a last wish had been gratified, and he answered,\n\n“He—the Redeemer—the Son of God, whom I have seen again.”\n\n“Believest thou so?” Iras asked in a low voice of Ben-Hur.\n\n“The time is full of wonders; let us wait,” was all he said.\n\nAnd next day while the three were listening to him, the Nazarite broke off in mid-speech, saying reverently, “Behold the Lamb of God!”\n\nLooking to where he pointed, they beheld the stranger again. As Ben-Hur surveyed the slender figure, and holy beautiful countenance compassionate to sadness, a new idea broke upon him.\n\n“Balthasar is right—so is Simonides. May not the Redeemer be a king also?”\n\nAnd he asked one at his side, “Who is the man walking yonder?”\n\nThe other laughed mockingly, and replied,\n\n“He is the son of a carpenter over in Nazareth.”\n\n# BOOK EIGHTH\n\n“Who could resist? Who in this universe? She did so breathe ambrosia, so immerse My fine existence in a golden clime. She took me like a child of suckling-time, And cradled me in roses. Thus condemn’d, The current of my former life was stemm’d, And to this arbitrary queen of sense I bow’d a tranced vassal.”—KEATS, Endymion.\n\n“I am the resurrection and the life.” CHAPTER I\n\n“Esther—Esther! Speak to the servant below that he may bring me a cup of water.”\n\n“Would you not rather have wine, father?”\n\n“Let him bring both.”\n\nThis was in the summer-house upon the roof of the old palace of the Hurs in Jerusalem. From the parapet overlooking the court-yard Esther called to a man in waiting there; at the same moment another man-servant came up the steps and saluted respectfully.\n\n“A package for the master,” he said, giving her a letter enclosed in linen cloth, tied and sealed.\n\nFor the satisfaction of the reader, we stop to say that it is the twenty-first day of March, nearly three years after the annunciation of the Christ at Bethabara.\n\nIn the meanwhile, Malluch, acting for Ben-Hur, who could not longer endure the emptiness and decay of his father’s house, had bought it from Pontius Pilate; and, in process of repair, gates, courts, lewens, stairways, terraces, rooms, and roof had been cleansed and thoroughly restored; not only was there no reminder left of the tragic circumstances so ruinous to the family, but the refurnishment was in a style richer than before. At every point, indeed, a visitor was met by evidences of the higher tastes acquired by the young proprietor during his years of residence in the villa by Misenum and in the Roman capital.\n\nNow it should not be inferred from this explanation that Ben-Hur had publicly assumed ownership of the property. In his opinion, the hour for that was not yet come. Neither had he yet taken his proper name. Passing the time in the labors of preparation in Galilee, he waited patiently the action of the Nazarene, who became daily more and more a mystery to him, and by prodigies done, often before his eyes, kept him in a state of anxious doubt both as to his character and mission. Occasionally he came up to the Holy City, stopping at the paternal house; always, however, as a stranger and a guest.\n\nThese visits of Ben-Hur, it should also be observed, were for more than mere rest from labor. Balthasar and Iras made their home in the palace; and the charm of the daughter was still upon him with all its original freshness, while the father, though feebler in body, held him an unflagging listener to speeches of astonishing power, urging the divinity of the wandering miracle-worker of whom they were all so expectant.\n\nAs to Simonides and Esther, they had arrived from Antioch only a few days before this their reappearance—a wearisome journey to the merchant, borne, as he had been, in a palanquin swung between two camels, which, in their careening, did not always keep the same step. But now that he was come, the good man, it seemed, could not see enough of his native land. He delighted in the perch upon the roof, and spent most of his day hours there seated in an arm-chair, the duplicate of that one kept for him in the cabinet over the store-house by the Orontes. In the shade of the summer-house he could drink fully of the inspiring air lying lightly upon the familiar hills; he could better watch the sun rise, run its course, and set as it used to in the far-gone, not a habit lost; and with Esther by him it was so much easier up there close to the sky, to bring back the other Esther, his love in youth, his wife, dearer growing with the passage of years. And yet he was not unmindful of business. Every day a messenger brought him a despatch from Sanballat, in charge of the big commerce behind; and every day a despatch left him for Sanballat with directions of such minuteness of detail as to exclude all judgment save his own, and all chances except those the Almighty has refused to submit to the most mindful of men.\n\nAs Esther started in return to the summer-house, the sunlight fell softly upon the dustless roof, showing her a woman now—small, graceful in form, of regular features, rosy with youth and health, bright with intelligence, beautiful with the outshining of a devoted nature—a woman to be loved because loving was a habit of life irrepressible with her.\n\nShe looked at the package as she turned, paused, looked at it a second time more closely than at first; and the blood rose reddening her cheeks—the seal was Ben-Hur’s. With quickened steps she hastened on.\n\nSimonides held the package a moment while he also inspected the seal. Breaking it open, he gave her the roll it contained.\n\n“Read,” he said.\n\nHis eyes were upon her as he spoke, and instantly a troubled expression fell upon his own face.\n\n“You know who it is from, I see, Esther.”\n\n“Yes—from—our master.”\n\nThough the manner was halting, she met his gaze with modest sincerity. Slowly his chin sank into the roll of flesh puffed out under it like a cushion.\n\n“You love him, Esther,” he said, quietly.\n\n“Yes,” she answered.\n\n“Have you thought well of what you do?”\n\n“I have tried not to think of him, father, except as the master to whom I am dutifully bound. The effort has not helped me to strength.”\n\n“A good girl, a good girl, even as thy mother was,” he said, dropping into reverie, from which she roused him by unrolling the paper.\n\n“The Lord forgive me, but—but thy love might not have been vainly given had I kept fast hold of all I had, as I might have done—such power is there in money!”\n\n“It would have been worse for me had you done so, father; for then I had been unworthy a look from him, and without pride in you. Shall I not read now?”\n\n“In a moment,” he said. “Let me, for your sake, my child, show you the worst. Seeing it with me may make it less terrible to you. His love, Esther, is all bestowed.”\n\n“I know it,” she said, calmly.\n\n“The Egyptian has him in her net,” he continued. “She has the cunning of her race, with beauty to help her—much beauty, great cunning; but, like her race again, no heart. The daughter who despises her father will bring her husband to grief.”\n\n“Does she that?”\n\nSimonides went on:\n\n“Balthasar is a wise man who has been wonderfully favored for a Gentile, and his faith becomes him; yet she makes a jest of it. I heard her say, speaking of him yesterday, ‘The follies of youth are excusable; nothing is admirable in the aged except wisdom, and when that goes from them, they should die.’ A cruel speech, fit for a Roman. I applied it to myself, knowing a feebleness like her father’s will come to me also—nay, it is not far off. But you, Esther, will never say of me—no, never—‘It were better he were dead.’ No, your mother was a daughter of Judah.”\n\nWith half-formed tears, she kissed him, and said, “I am my mother’s child.”\n\n“Yes, and my daughter—my daughter, who is to me all the Temple was to Solomon.”\n\nAfter a silence, he laid his hand upon her shoulder, and resumed: “When he has taken the Egyptian to wife, Esther, he will think of you with repentance and much calling of the spirit; for at last he will awake to find himself but the minister of her bad ambition. Rome is the centre of all her dreams. To her he is the son of Arrius the duumvir, not the son of Hur, Prince of Jerusalem.”\n\nEsther made no attempt to conceal the effect of these words.\n\n“Save him, father! It is not too late!” she said, entreatingly.\n\nHe answered, with a dubious smile, “A man drowning may be saved; not so a man in love.”\n\n“But you have influence with him. He is alone in the world. Show him his danger. Tell him what a woman she is.”\n\n“That might save him from her. Would it give him to you, Esther? No,” and his brows fell darkly over his eyes. “I am a servant, as my fathers were for generations; yet I could not say to him, ‘Lo, master, my daughter! She is fairer than the Egyptian, and loves thee better!’ I have caught too much from years of liberty and direction. The words would blister my tongue. The stones upon the old hills yonder would turn in their beds for shame when I go out to them. No, by the patriarchs, Esther, I would rather lay us both with your mother to sleep as she sleeps!”\n\nA blush burned Esther’s whole face.\n\n“I did not mean you to tell him so, father. I was concerned for him alone—for his happiness, not mine. Because I have dared love him, I shall keep myself worthy his respect; so only can I excuse my folly. Let me read his letter now.”\n\n“Yes, read it.”\n\nShe began at once, in haste to conclude the distasteful subject.\n\n“Nisan, 8th day.\n\n“On the road from Galilee to Jerusalem.\n\n“The Nazarene is on the way also. With him, though without his knowledge, I am bringing a full legion of mine. A second legion follows. The Passover will excuse the multitude. He said upon setting out, ‘We will go up to Jerusalem, and all things that are written by the prophets concerning me shall be accomplished.’\n\n“Our waiting draws to an end.\n\n“In haste.\n\n“Peace to thee, Simonides.\n\nEsther returned the letter to her father, while a choking sensation gathered in her throat. There was not a word in the missive for her—not even in the salutation had she a share—and it would have been so easy to have written “and to thine, peace.” For the first time in her life she felt the smart of a jealous sting.\n\n“The eighth day,” said Simonides, “the eighth day; and this, Esther, this is the—”\n\n“The ninth,” she replied.\n\n“Ah, then, they may be in Bethany now.”\n\n“And possibly we may see him to-night,” she added, pleased into momentary forgetfulness.\n\n“It may be, it may be! To-morrow is the Feast of Unleavened Bread, and he may wish to celebrate it; so may the Nazarene; and we may see him—we may see both of them, Esther.”\n\nAt this point the servant appeared with the wine and water. Esther helped her father, and in the midst of the service Iras came upon the roof.\n\nTo the Jewess the Egyptian never appeared so very, very beautiful as at that moment. Her gauzy garments fluttered about her like a little cloud of mist; her forehead, neck, and arms glittered with the massive jewelry so affected by her people. Her countenance was suffused with pleasure. She moved with buoyant steps, and self-conscious, though without affectation. Esther at the sight shrank within herself, and nestled closer to her father.\n\n“Peace to you, Simonides, and to the pretty Esther peace,” said Iras, inclining her head to the latter. “You remind me, good master—if I may say it without offence-you remind me of the priests in Persia who climb their temples at the decline of day to send prayers after the departing sun. Is there anything in the worship you do not know, let me call my father. He is Magian-bred.”\n\n“Fair Egyptian,” the merchant replied, nodding with grave politeness, “your father is a good man who would not be offended if he knew I told you his Persian lore is the least part of his wisdom.”\n\nIras’s lip curled slightly.\n\n“To speak like a philosopher, as you invite me,” she said, “the least part always implies a greater. Let me ask what you esteem the greater part of the rare quality you are pleased to attribute to him.”\n\nSimonides turned upon her somewhat sternly.\n\n“Pure wisdom always directs itself towards God; the purest wisdom is knowledge of God; and no man of my acquaintance has it in higher degree, or makes it more manifest in speech and act, than the good Balthasar.”\n\nTo end the parley, he raised the cup and drank.\n\nThe Egyptian turned to Esther a little testily.\n\n“A man who has millions in store, and fleets of ships at sea, cannot discern in what simple women like us find amusement. Let us leave him. By the wall yonder we can talk.”\n\nThey went to the parapet then, stopping at the place where, years before, Ben-Hur loosed the broken tile upon the head of Gratus.\n\n“You have not been to Rome?” Iras began, toying the while with one of her unclasped bracelets.\n\n“No,” said Esther, demurely.\n\n“Have you not wished to go?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Ah, how little there has been of your life!”\n\nThe sigh that succeeded the exclamation could not have been more piteously expressive had the loss been the Egyptian’s own. Next moment her laugh might have been heard in the street below; and she said “Oh, oh, my pretty simpleton! The half-fledged birds nested in the ear of the great bust out on the Memphian sands know nearly as much as you.”\n\nThen, seeing Esther’s confusion, she changed her manner, and said in a confiding tone, “You must not take offence. Oh no! I was playing. Let me kiss the hurt, and tell you what I would not to any other—not if Simbel himself asked it of me, offering a lotus-cup of the spray of the Nile!”\n\nAnother laugh, masking excellently the look she turned sharply upon the Jewess, and she said, “The King is coming.”\n\nEsther gazed at her in innocent surprise.\n\n“The Nazarene,” Iras continued—“he whom our fathers have been talking about so much, whom Ben-Hur has been serving and toiling for so long”—her voice dropped several tones lower—“the Nazarene will be here to-morrow, and Ben-Hur to-night.”\n\nEsther struggled to maintain her composure, but failed: her eyes fell, the tell-tale blood surged to her cheek and forehead, and she was saved sight of the triumphant smile that passed, like a gleam, over the face of the Egyptian.\n\n“See, here is his promise.”\n\nAnd from her girdle she took a roll.\n\n“Rejoice with me, O my friend! He will be here tonight! On the Tiber there is a house, a royal property, which he has pledged to me; and to be its mistress is to be—”\n\nA sound of some one walking swiftly along the street below interrupted the speech, and she leaned over the parapet to see. Then she drew back, and cried, with hands clasped above her head, “Now blessed be Isis! ’Tis he—Ben-Hur himself! That he should appear while I had such thought of him! There are no gods if it be not a good omen. Put your arms about me, Esther—and a kiss!”\n\nThe Jewess looked up. Upon each cheek there was a glow; her eyes sparkled with a light more nearly of anger than ever her nature emitted before. Her gentleness had been too roughly overridden. It was not enough for her to be forbidden more than fugitive dreams of the man she loved; a boastful rival must tell her in confidence of her better success, and of the brilliant promises which were its rewards. Of her, the servant of a servant, there had been no hint of remembrance; this other could show his letter, leaving her to imagine all it breathed. So she said,\n\n“Dost thou love him so much, then, or Rome so much better?”\n\nThe Egyptian drew back a step; then she bent her haughty head quite near her questioner.\n\n“What is he to thee, daughter of Simonides?”\n\nEsther, all thrilling, began, “He is my—”\n\nA thought blasting as lightning stayed the words: she paled, trembled, recovered, and answered,\n\n“He is my father’s friend.”\n\nHer tongue had refused to admit her servile condition.\n\nIras laughed more lightly than before.\n\n“Not more than that?” she said. “Ah, by the lover-gods of Egypt, thou mayst keep thy kisses—keep them. Thou hast taught me but now that there are others vastly more estimable waiting me here in Judea; and”—she turned away, looking back over her shoulder—“I will go get them. Peace to thee.”\n\nEsther saw her disappear down the steps, when, putting her hands over her face, she burst into tears so they ran scalding through her fingers—tears of shame and choking passion. And, to deepen the paroxysm to her even temper so strange, up with a new meaning of withering force rose her father’s words—“Thy love might not have been vainly given had I kept fast hold of all I had, as I might have done.”\n\nAnd all the stars were out, burning low above the city and the dark wall of mountains about it, before she recovered enough to go back to the summer-house, and in silence take her accustomed place at her father’s side, humbly waiting his pleasure. To such duty it seemed her youth, if not her life, must be given. And, let the truth be said, now that the pang was spent, she went not unwillingly back to the duty.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nAn hour or thereabouts after the scene upon the roof, Balthasar and Simonides, the latter attended by Esther, met in the great chamber of the palace; and while they were talking, Ben-Hur and Iras came in together.\n\nThe young Jew, advancing in front of his companion, walked first to Balthasar, and saluted him, and received his reply; then he turned to Simonides, but paused at sight of Esther.\n\nIt is not often we have hearts roomy enough for more than one of the absorbing passions at the same time; in its blaze the others may continue to live, but only as lesser lights. So with Ben-Hur, much study of possibilities, indulgence of hopes and dreams, influences born of the condition of his country, influences more direct—that of Iras, for example—had made him in the broadest worldly sense ambitious; and as he had given the passion place, allowing it to become a rule, and finally an imperious governor, the resolves and impulses of former days faded imperceptibly out of being, and at last almost out of recollection. It is at best so easy to forget our youth; in his case it was but natural that his own sufferings and the mystery darkening the fate of his family should move him less and less as, in hope at least, he approached nearer and nearer the goals which occupied all his visions. Only let us not judge him too harshly.\n\nHe paused in surprise at seeing Esther a woman now, and so beautiful; and as he stood looking at her a still voice reminded him of broken vows and duties undone: almost his old self returned.\n\nFor an instant he was startled; but recovering, he went to Esther, and said, “Peace to thee, sweet Esther—peace; and thou, Simonides”—he looked to the merchant as he spoke—“the blessing of the Lord be thine, if only because thou hast been a good father to the fatherless.”\n\nEsther heard him with downcast face; Simonides answered,\n\n“I repeat the welcome of the good Balthasar, son of Hur—welcome to thy father’s house; and sit, and tell us of thy travels, and of thy work, and of the wonderful Nazarene—who he is, and what. If thou art not at ease here, who shall be? Sit, I pray—there, between us, that we may all hear.”\n\nEsther stepped out quickly and brought a covered stool, and set it for him.\n\n“Thanks,” he said to her, gratefully.\n\nWhen seated, after some other conversation, he addressed himself to the men.\n\n“I have come to tell you of the Nazarene.”\n\nThe two became instantly attentive.\n\n“For many days now I have followed him with such watchfulness as one may give another upon whom he is waiting so anxiously. I have seen him under all circumstances said to be trials and tests of men; and while I am certain he is a man as I am, not less certain am I that he is something more.”\n\n“What more?” asked Simonides.\n\n“I will tell you—”\n\nSome one coming into the room interrupted him; he turned, and arose with extended hands.\n\n“Amrah! Dear old Amrah!” he cried.\n\nShe came forward; and they, seeing the joy in her face, thought not once how wrinkled and tawny it was. She knelt at his feet, clasped his knees, and kissed his hands over and over; and when he could he put the lank gray hair from her cheeks, and kissed them, saying, “Good Amrah, have you nothing, nothing of them—not a word—not one little sign?”\n\nThen she broke into sobbing which made him answer plainer even than the spoken word.\n\n“God’s will has been done,” he next said, solemnly, in a tone to make each listener know he had no hope more of finding his people. In his eyes there were tears which he would not have them see, because he was a man.\n\nWhen he could again, he took seat, and said, “Come, sit by me, Amrah—here. No? then at my feet; for I have much to say to these good friends of a wonderful man come into the world.”\nBut she went off, and stooping with her back to the wall, joined her hands before her knees, content, they all thought, with seeing him. Then Ben-Hur, bowing to the old men, began again:\n\n“I fear to answer the question asked me about the Nazarene without first telling you some of the things I have seen him do; and to that I am the more inclined, my friends, because to-morrow he will come to the city, and go up into the Temple, which he calls his father’s house, where, it is further said, he will proclaim himself. So, whether you are right, O Balthasar, or you, Simonides, we and Israel shall know to-morrow.”\n\nBalthasar rubbed his hands tremulously together, and asked, “Where shall I go to see him?”\n\n“The pressure of the crowd will be very great. Better, I think, that you all go upon the roof above the cloisters—say upon the Porch of Solomon.”\n\n“No,” said Ben-Hur, “my friends will require me, perhaps, in the procession.”\n\n“Procession!” exclaimed Simonides. “Does he travel in state?”\n\nBen-Hur saw the argument in mind.\n\n“He brings twelve men with him, fishermen, tillers of the soil, one a publican, all of the humbler class; and he and they make their journeys on foot, careless of wind, cold, rain, or sun. Seeing them stop by the wayside at nightfall to break bread or lie down to sleep, I have been reminded of a party of shepherds going back to their flocks from market, not of nobles and kings. Only when he lifts the corners of his handkerchief to look at some one or shake the dust from his head, I am made known he is their teacher as well as their companion—their superior not less than their friend.\n\n“You are shrewd men,” Ben-Hur resumed, after a pause. “You know what creatures of certain master motives we are, and that it has become little less than a law of our nature to spend life in eager pursuit of certain objects; now, appealing to that law as something by which we may know ourselves, what would you say of a man who could be rich by making gold of the stones under his feet, yet is poor of choice?”\n\n“The Greeks would call him a philosopher,” said Iras.\n\n“Nay, daughter,” said Balthasar, “the philosophers had never the power to do such thing.”\n\n“How know you this man has?”\n\nBen-Hur answered quickly, “I saw him turn water into wine.”\n\n“Very strange, very strange,” said Simonides; “but it is not so strange to me as that he should prefer to live poor when he could be so rich. Is he so poor?”\n\n“He owns nothing, and envies nobody his owning. He pities the rich. But passing that, what would you say to see a man multiply seven loaves and two fishes, all his store, into enough to feed five thousand people, and have full baskets over? That I saw the Nazarene do.”\n\n“You saw it?” exclaimed Simonides.\n\n“Ay, and ate of the bread and fish.”\n\n“More marvellous still,” Ben-Hur continued, “what would you say of a man in whom there is such healing virtue that the sick have but to touch the hem of his garment to be cured, or cry to him afar? That, too, I witnessed, not once, but many times. As we came out of Jericho two blind men by the wayside called to the Nazarene, and he touched their eyes, and they saw. So they brought a palsied man to him, and he said merely, ‘Go unto thy house,’ and the man went away well. What say you to these things?”\n\nThe merchant had no answer.\n\n“Think you now, as I have heard others argue, that what I have told you are tricks of jugglery? Let me answer by recalling greater things which I have seen him do. Look first to that curse of God—comfortless, as you all know, except by death—leprosy.”\n\nAt these words Amrah dropped her hands to the floor, and in her eagerness to hear him half arose.\n\n“What would you say,” said Ben-Hur, with increased earnestness—“what would you say to have seen that I now tell you? A leper came to the Nazarene while I was with him down in Galilee, and said, ‘Lord, if thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.’ He heard the cry, and touched the outcast with his hand, saying, ‘Be thou clean;’ and forthwith the man was himself again, healthful as any of us who beheld the cure, and we were a multitude.”\n\nHere Amrah arose, and with her gaunt fingers held the wiry locks from her eyes. The brain of the poor creature had long since gone to heart, and she was troubled to follow the speech.\n\n“Then, again,” said Ben-Hur, without stop, “ten lepers came to him one day in a body, and falling at his feet, called out—I saw and heard it all—called out, ‘Master, Master, have mercy upon us!’ He told them, ‘Go, show yourselves to the priest, as the law requires; and before you are come there ye shall be healed.’”\n\n“And were they?”\n\n“Yes. On the road going their infirmity left them, so that there was nothing to remind us of it except their polluted clothes.”\n\n“Such thing was never heard before—never in all Israel!” said Simonides, in undertone.\n\nAnd then, while he was speaking, Amrah turned away, and walked noiselessly to the door, and went out; and none of the company saw her go.\n\n“The thoughts stirred by such things done under my eyes I leave you to imagine,” said Ben-Hur, continuing; “but my doubts, my misgivings, my amazement, were not yet at the full. The people of Galilee are, as you know, impetuous and rash; after years of waiting their swords burned their hands; nothing would do them but action. ‘He is slow to declare himself; let us force him,’ they cried to me. And I too became impatient. If he is to be king, why not now? The legions are ready. So as he was once teaching by the seaside we would have crowned him whether or not; but he disappeared, and was next seen on a ship departing from the shore. Good Simonides, the desires that make other men mad—riches, power, even kingships offered out of great love by a great people—move this one not at all. What say you?”\n\nThe merchant’s chin was low upon his breast; raising his head, he replied, resolutely, “The Lord liveth, and so do the words of the prophets. Time is in the green yet; let to-morrow answer.”\n\n“Be it so,” said Balthasar, smiling.\n\nAnd Ben-Hur said, “Be it so.” Then he went on: “But I have not yet done. From these things, not too great to be above suspicion by such as did not see them in performance as I did, let me carry you now to others infinitely greater, acknowledged since the world began to be past the power of man. Tell me, has any one to your knowledge ever reached out and taken from Death what Death has made his own? Who ever gave again the breath of a life lost? Who but—”\n\n“God!” said Balthasar, reverently.\n\nBen-Hur bowed.\n\n“O wise Egyptian! I may not refuse the name you lend me. What would you—or you, Simonides—what would you either or both have said had you seen as I did, a man, with few words and no ceremony, without effort more than a mother’s when she speaks to wake her child asleep, undo the work of Death? It was down at Nain. We were about going into the gate, when a company came out bearing a dead man. The Nazarene stopped to let the train pass. There was a woman among them crying. I saw his face soften with pity. He spoke to her, then went and touched the bier, and said to him who lay upon it dressed for burial, ‘Young man, I say unto thee, Arise!’ And instantly the dead sat up and talked.”\n\n“God only is so great,” said Balthasar to Simonides.\n\n“Mark you,” Ben-Hur proceeded, “I do but tell you things of which I was a witness, together with a cloud of other men. On the way hither I saw another act still more mighty. In Bethany there was a man named Lazarus, who died and was buried; and after he had lain four days in a tomb, shut in by a great stone, the Nazarene was shown to the place. Upon rolling the stone away, we beheld the man lying inside bound and rotting. There were many people standing by, and we all heard what the Nazarene said, for he spoke in a loud voice: ‘Lazarus, come forth!’ I cannot tell you my feelings when in answer, as it were, the man arose and came out to us with all his cerements about him. ‘Loose him,’ said the Nazarene next, ‘loose him, and let him go.’ And when the napkin was taken from the face of the resurrected, lo, my friends! the blood ran anew through the wasted body, and he was exactly as he had been in life before the sickness that took him off. He lives yet, and is hourly seen and spoken to. You may go see him to-morrow. And now, as nothing more is needed for the purpose, I ask you that which I came to ask, it being but a repetition of what you asked me, O Simonides, What more than a man is this Nazarene?”\n\nThe question was put solemnly, and long after midnight the company sat and debated it; Simonides being yet unwilling to give up his understanding of the sayings of the prophets, and Ben-Hur contending that the elder disputants were both right—that the Nazarene was the Redeemer, as claimed by Balthasar, and also the destined king the merchant would have.\n\n“To-morrow we will see. Peace to you all.”\n\nSo saying, Ben-Hur took his leave, intending to return to Bethany.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe first person to go out of the city upon the opening of the Sheep’s Gate next morning was Amrah, basket on arm. No questions were asked her by the keepers, since the morning itself had not been more regular in coming than she; they knew her somebody’s faithful servant, and that was enough for them.\n\nDown the eastern valley she took her way. The side of Olivet, darkly green, was spotted with white tents recently put up by people attending the feasts; the hour, however, was too early for the strangers to be abroad; still, had it not been so, no one would have troubled her. Past Gethsemane; past the tombs at the meeting of the Bethany roads; past the sepulchral village of Siloam she went. Occasionally the decrepit little body staggered; once she sat down to get her breath; rising shortly, she struggled on with renewed haste. The great rocks on either hand, if they had had ears, might have heard her mutter to herself; could they have seen, it would have been to observe how frequently she looked up over the Mount, reproving the dawn for its promptness; if it had been possible for them to gossip, not improbably they would have said to each other, “Our friend is in a hurry this morning; the mouths she goes to feed must be very hungry.”\n\nWhen at last she reached the King’s Garden she slackened her gait; for then the grim city of the lepers was in view, extending far round the pitted south hill of Hinnom.\n\nAs the reader must by this time have surmised, she was going to her mistress, whose tomb, it will be remembered, overlooked the well En-Rogel.\n\nEarly as it was, the unhappy woman was up and sitting outside, leaving Tirzah asleep within. The course of the malady had been terribly swift in the three years. Conscious of her appearance, with the refined instincts of her nature, she kept her whole person habitually covered. Seldom as possible she permitted even Tirzah to see her.\n\nThis morning she was taking the air with bared head, knowing there was no one to be shocked by the exposure. The light was not full, but enough to show the ravages to which she had been subject. Her hair was snow-white and unmanageably coarse, falling over her back and shoulders like so much silver wire. The eyelids, the lips, the nostrils, the flesh of the cheeks, were either gone or reduced to fetid rawness. The neck was a mass of ash-colored scales. One hand lay outside the folds of her habit rigid as that of a skeleton; the nails had been eaten away; the joints of the fingers, if not bare to the bone, were swollen knots crusted with red secretion. Head, face, neck, and hand indicated all too plainly the condition of the whole body. Seeing her thus, it was easy to understand how the once fair widow of the princely Hur had been able to maintain her incognito so well through such a period of years.\n\nWhen the sun would gild the crest of Olivet and the Mount of Offence with light sharper and more brilliant in that old land than in the West, she knew Amrah would come, first to the well, then to a stone midway the well and the foot of the hill on which she had her abode, and that the good servant would there deposit the food she carried in the basket, and fill the water-jar afresh for the day. Of her former plentitude of happiness, that brief visit was all that remained to the unfortunate. She could then ask about her son, and be told of his welfare, with such bits of news concerning him as the messenger could glean. Usually the information was meagre enough, yet comforting; at times she heard he was at home; then she would issue from her dreary cell at break of day, and sit till noon, and from noon to set of sun, a motionless figure draped in white, looking, statue-like, invariably to one point—over the Temple to the spot under the rounded sky where the old house stood, dear in memory, and dearer because he was there. Nothing else was left her. Tirzah she counted of the dead; and as for herself, she simply waited the end, knowing every hour of life was an hour of dying—happily, of painless dying.\n\nThe things of nature about the hill to keep her sensitive to the world’s attractions were wretchedly scant; beasts and birds avoided the place as if they knew its history and present use; every green thing perished in its first season; the winds warred upon the shrubs and venturous grasses, leaving to drought such as they could not uproot. Look where she would, the view was made depressingly suggestive by tombs—tombs above her, tombs below, tombs opposite her own tomb—all now freshly whitened in warning to visiting pilgrims. In the sky—clear, fair, inviting—one would think she might have found some relief to her ache of mind; but, alas! in making the beautiful elsewhere the sun served her never so unfriendly—it did but disclose her growing hideousness. But for the sun she would not have been the horror she was to herself, nor been waked so cruelly from dreams of Tirzah as she used to be. The gift of seeing can be sometimes a dreadful curse.\n\nDoes one ask why she did not make an end to her sufferings?\n\n# THE LAW FORBADE HER!\n\nA Gentile may smile at the answer; but so will not a son of Israel.\n\nWhile she sat there peopling the dusky solitude with thoughts even more cheerless, suddenly a woman came up the hill staggering and spent with exertion.\n\nThe widow arose hastily, and covering her head, cried, in a voice unnaturally harsh, “Unclean, unclean!”\n\nIn a moment, heedless of the notice, Amrah was at her feet. All the long-pent love of the simple creature burst forth: with tears and passionate exclamations she kissed her mistress’s garments, and for a while the latter strove to escape from her; then, seeing she could not, she waited till the violence of the paroxysm was over.\n\n“What have you done, Amrah?” she said. “Is it by such disobedience you prove your love for us? Wicked woman! You are lost; and he—your master—you can never, never go back to him.”\n\nAmrah grovelled sobbing in the dust.\n\n“The ban of the Law is upon you, too; you cannot return to Jerusalem. What will become of us? Who will bring us bread? O wicked, wicked Amrah! We are all, all undone alike!”\n\n“Mercy, mercy!” Amrah answered from the ground.\n\n“You should have been merciful to yourself, and by so doing been most merciful to us. Now where can we fly? There is no one to help us. O false servant! The wrath of the Lord was already too heavy upon us.”\n\nHere Tirzah, awakened by the noise, appeared at the door of the tomb. The pen shrinks from the picture she presented. In the half-clad apparition, patched with scales, lividly seamed, nearly blind, its limbs and extremities swollen to grotesque largeness, familiar eyes however sharpened by love could not have recognized the creature of childish grace and purity we first beheld her.\n\n“Is it Amrah, mother?”\n\nThe servant tried to crawl to her also.\n\n“Stay, Amrah!” the widow cried, imperiously. “I forbid you touching her. Rise, and get you gone before any at the well see you here. Nay, I forgot—it is too late! You must remain now and share our doom. Rise, I say!”\n\nAmrah rose to her knees, and said, brokenly and with clasped hands, “O good mistress! I am not false—I am not wicked. I bring you good tidings.”\n\n“Of Judah?” and as she spoke, the widow half withdrew the cloth from her head.\n\n“There is a wonderful man,” Amrah continued, “who has power to cure you. He speaks a word, and the sick are made well, and even the dead come to life. I have come to take you to him.”\n\n“Poor Amrah!” said Tirzah, compassionately.\n\n“No,” cried Amrah, detecting the doubt underlying the expression—“no, as the Lord lives, even the Lord of Israel, my God as well as yours, I speak the truth. Go with me, I pray, and lose no time. This morning he will pass by on his way to the city. See! the day is at hand. Take the food here—eat, and let us go.”\n\nThe mother listened eagerly. Not unlikely she had heard of the wonderful man, for by this time his fame had penetrated every nook in the land.\n\n“Who is he?” she asked.\n\n“A Nazarene.”\n\n“Who told you about him?”\n\n“Judah.”\n\n“Judah told you? Is he at home?”\n\n“He came last night.”\n\nThe widow, trying to still the beating of her heart, was silent awhile.\n\n“Did Judah send you to tell us this?” she next asked.\n\n“No. He believes you dead.”\n\n“There was a prophet once who cured a leper,” the mother said thoughtfully to Tirzah; “but he had his power from God.” Then addressing Amrah, she asked, “How does my son know this man so possessed?”\n\n“He was travelling with him, and heard the lepers call, and saw them go away well. First there was one man; then there were ten; and they were all made whole.”\n\nThe elder listener was silent again. The skeleton hand shook. We may believe she was struggling to give the story the sanction of faith, which is always an absolutist in demand, and that it was with her as with the men of the day, eye-witnesses of what was done by the Christ, as well as the myriads who have succeeded them. She did not question the performance, for her own son was the witness testifying through the servant; but she strove to comprehend the power by which work so astonishing could be done by a man. Well enough to make inquiry as to the fact; to comprehend the power, on the other hand, it is first necessary to comprehend God; and he who waits for that will die waiting. With her, however, the hesitation was brief. To Tirzah she said,\n\n“This must be the Messiah!”\n\nShe spoke not coldly, like one reasoning a doubt away, but as a woman of Israel familiar with the promises of God to her race—a woman of understanding, ready to be glad over the least sign of the realization of the promises.\n\n“There was a time when Jerusalem and all Judea were filled with a story that he was born. I remember it. By this time he should be a man. It must be—it is he. Yes,” she said to Amrah, “we will go with you. Bring the water which you will find in the tomb in a jar, and set the food for us. We will eat and be gone.”\n\nThe breakfast, partaken under excitement, was soon despatched, and the three women set out on their extraordinary journey. As Tirzah had caught the confident spirit of the others, there was but one fear that troubled the party. Bethany, Amrah said, was the town the man was coming from; now from that to Jerusalem there were three roads, or rather paths—one over the first summit of Olivet, a second at its base, a third between the second summit and the Mount of Offence. The three were not far apart; far enough, however, to make it possible for the unfortunates to miss the Nazarene if they failed the one he chose to come by.\n\nA little questioning satisfied the mother that Amrah knew nothing of the country beyond the Cedron, and even less of the intentions of the man they were going to see, if they could. She discerned, also, that both Amrah and Tirzah—the one from confirmed habits of servitude, the other from natural dependency—looked to her for guidance; and she accepted the charge.\n\n“We will go first to Bethphage,” she said to them. “There, if the Lord favor us, we may learn what else to do.”\n\nThey descended the hill to Tophet and the King’s Garden, and paused in the deep trail furrowed through them by centuries of wayfaring.\n\n“I am afraid of the road,” the matron said. “Better that we keep to the country among the rocks and trees. This is feast-day, and on the hill-sides yonder I see signs of a great multitude in attendance. By going across the Mount of Offence here we may avoid them.”\n\nTirzah had been walking with great difficulty; upon hearing this her heart began to fail her.\n\n“The mount is steep, mother; I cannot climb it.”\n\n“Remember, we are going to find health and life. See, my child, how the day brightens around us! And yonder are women coming this way to the well. They will stone us if we stay here. Come, be strong this once.”\n\nThus the mother, not less tortured herself, sought to inspire the daughter; and Amrah came to her aid. To this time the latter had not touched the persons of the afflicted, nor they her; now, in disregard of consequences as well as of command, the faithful creature went to Tirzah, and put her arm over her shoulder, and whispered, “Lean on me. I am strong, though I am old; and it is but a little way off. There—now we can go.”\n\nThe face of the hill they essayed to cross was somewhat broken with pits, and ruins of old structures; but when at last they stood upon the top to rest, and looked at the spectacle presented them over in the northwest—at the Temple and its courtly terraces, at Zion, at the enduring towers white beetling into the sky beyond—the mother was strengthened with a love of life for life’s sake.\n\n“Look, Tirzah,” she said—“look at the plates of gold on the Gate Beautiful. How they give back the flames of the sun, brightness for brightness! Do you remember we used to go up there? Will it not be pleasant to do so again? And think—home is but a little way off. I can almost see it over the roof of the Holy of Holies; and Judah will be there to receive us!”\n\nFrom the side of the middle summit garnished green with myrtle and olive trees, they saw, upon looking that way next, thin columns of smoke rising lightly and straight up into the pulseless morning, each a warning of restless pilgrims astir, and of the flight of the pitiless hours, and the need of haste.\n\nThough the good servant toiled faithfully to lighten the labor in descending the hill-side, not sparing herself in the least, the girl moaned at every step; sometimes in extremity of anguish she cried out. Upon reaching the road—that is, the road between the Mount of Offence and the middle or second summit of Olivet—she fell down exhausted.\n\n“Go on with Amrah, mother, and leave me here,” she said, faintly.\n\n“No, no, Tirzah. What would the gain be to me if I were healed and you not? When Judah asks for you, as he will, what would I have to say to him were I to leave you?”\n\n“Tell him I loved him.”\n\nThe elder leper arose from bending over the fainting sufferer, and gazed about her with that sensation of hope perishing which is more nearly like annihilation of the soul than anything else. The supremest joy of the thought of cure was inseparable from Tirzah, who was not too old to forget, in the happiness of healthful life to come, the years of misery by which she had been so reduced in body and broken in spirit. Even as the brave woman was about leaving the venture they were engaged in to the determination of God, she saw a man on foot coming rapidly up the road from the east.\n\n“Courage, Tirzah! Be of cheer,” she said. “Yonder I know is one to tell us of the Nazarene.”\n\nAmrah helped the girl to a sitting posture, and supported her while the man advanced.\n\n“In your goodness, mother, you forget what we are. The stranger will go around us; his best gift to us will be a curse, if not a stone.”\n\n“We will see.”\n\nThere was no other answer to be given, since the mother was too well and sadly acquainted with the treatment outcasts of the class to which she belonged were accustomed to at the hands of her countrymen.\n\nAs has been said, the road at the edge of which the group was posted was little more than a worn path or trail, winding crookedly through tumuli of limestone. If the stranger kept it, he must meet them face to face; and he did so, until near enough to hear the cry she was bound to give. Then, uncovering her head, a further demand of the law, she shouted shrilly,\n\n“Unclean, unclean!”\n\nTo her surprise, the man came steadily on.\n\n“What would you have?” he asked, stopping opposite them not four yards off.\n\n“Thou seest us. Have a care,” the mother said, with dignity.\n\n“Woman, I am the courier of him who speaketh but once to such as thou and they are healed. I am not afraid.”\n\n“The Nazarene?”\n\n“The Messiah,” he said.\n\n“Is it true that he cometh to the city to-day?”\n\n“He is now at Bethphage.”\n\n“On what road, master?”\n\n“This one.”\n\nShe clasped her hands, and looked up thankfully.\n\n“For whom takest thou him?” the man asked, with pity.\n\n“The Son of God,” she replied.\n\n“Stay thou here then; or, as there is a multitude with him, take thy stand by the rock yonder, the white one under the tree; and as he goeth by fail not to call to him; call, and fear not. If thy faith but equal thy knowledge, he will hear thee though all the heavens thunder. I go to tell Israel, assembled in and about the city, that he is at hand, and to make ready to receive him. Peace to thee and thine, woman.”\n\nThe stranger moved on.\n\n“Did you hear, Tirzah? Did you hear? The Nazarene is on the road, on this one, and he will hear us. Once more, my child—oh, only once! and let us to the rock. It is but a step.”\n\nThus encouraged Tirzah took Amrah’s hand and arose; but as they were going, Amrah said, “Stay; the man is returning.” And they waited for him.\n\n“I pray your grace, woman,” he said, upon overtaking them. “Remembering that the sun will be hot before the Nazarene arrives, and that the city is near by to give me refreshment should I need it, I thought this water would do thee better than it will me. Take it and be of good cheer. Call to him as he passes.”\n\nHe followed the words by offering her a gourd full of water, such as foot-travellers sometimes carried with them in their journeys across the hills; and instead of placing the gift on the ground for her to take up when he was at a safe distance, he gave it into her hand.\n\n“Art thou a Jew?” she asked, surprised.\n\n“I am that, and better; I am a disciple of the Christ who teacheth daily by word and example this thing which I have done unto you. The world hath long known the word charity without understanding it. Again I say peace and good cheer to thee and thine.”\n\nHe went on, and they went slowly to the rock he had pointed out to them, high as their heads, and scarcely thirty yards from the road on the right. Standing in front of it, the mother satisfied herself they could be seen and heard plainly by passers-by whose notice they desired to attract. There they cast themselves under the tree in its shade, and drank of the gourd, and rested refreshed. Ere long Tirzah slept, and fearing to disturb her, the others held their peace.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nDuring the third hour the road in front of the resting-place of the lepers became gradually more and more frequented by people going in the direction of Bethphage and Bethany; now, however, about the commencement of the fourth hour, a great crowd appeared over the crest of Olivet, and as it defiled down the road thousands in number, the two watchers noticed with wonder that every one in it carried a palm-branch freshly cut. As they sat absorbed by the novelty, the noise of another multitude approaching from the east drew their eyes that way. Then the mother awoke Tirzah.\n\n“What is the meaning of it all?” the latter asked.\n\n“He is coming,” answered the mother. “These we see are from the city going to meet him; those we hear in the east are his friends bearing him company; and it will not be strange if the processions meet here before us.\n\n“I fear, if they do, we cannot be heard.”\n\nThe same thought was in the elder’s mind.\n\n“Amrah,” she asked, “when Judah spoke of the healing of the ten, in what words did he say they called to the Nazarene?”\n\n“Either they said, ‘Lord, have mercy upon us,’ or ‘Master, have mercy.’”\n\n“Only that?”\n\n“No more that I heard.”\n\n“Yet it was enough,” the mother added, to herself.\n\n“Yes,” said Amrah, “Judah said he saw them go away well.”\n\nMeantime the people in the east came up slowly. When at length the foremost of them were in sight, the gaze of the lepers fixed upon a man riding in the midst of what seemed a chosen company which sang and danced about him in extravagance of joy. The rider was bareheaded and clad all in white. When he was in distance to be more clearly observed, these, looking anxiously, saw an olive-hued face shaded by long chestnut hair slightly sunburned and parted in the middle. He looked neither to the right nor left. In the noisy abandon of his followers he appeared to have no part; nor did their favor disturb him in the least, or raise him out of the profound melancholy into which, as his countenance showed, he was plunged. The sun beat upon the back of his head, and lighting up the floating hair gave it a delicate likeness to a golden nimbus. Behind him the irregular procession, pouring forward with continuous singing and shouting, extended out of view. There was no need of any one to tell the lepers that this was he—the wonderful Nazarene!\n\n“He is here, Tirzah,” the mother said; “he is here. Come, my child.”\n\nAs she spoke she glided in front of the white rock and fell upon her knees.\n\nDirectly the daughter and servant were by her side. Then at sight of the procession in the west, the thousands from the city halted, and began to wave their green branches, shouting, or rather chanting (for it was all in one voice),\n\n“Blessed is the King of Israel that cometh in the name of the Lord!”\n\nAnd all the thousands who were of the rider’s company, both those near and those afar, replied so the air shook with the sound, which was as a great wind threshing the side of the hill. Amidst the din, the cries of the poor lepers were not more than the twittering of dazed sparrows.\n\nThe moment of the meeting of the hosts was come, and with it the opportunity the sufferers were seeking; if not taken, it would be lost forever, and they would be lost as well.\n\n“Nearer, my child—let us get nearer. He cannot hear us,” said the mother.\n\nShe arose, and staggered forward. Her ghastly hands were up, and she screamed with horrible shrillness. The people saw her—saw her hideous face, and stopped awe-struck—an effect for which extreme human misery, visible as in this instance, is as potent as majesty in purple and gold. Tirzah, behind her a little way, fell down too faint and frightened to follow farther.\n\n“The lepers! the lepers!”\n\n“Stone them!”\n\n“The accursed of God! Kill them!”\n\nThese, with other yells of like import, broke in upon the hosannas of the part of the multitude too far removed to see and understand the cause of the interruption. Some there were, however, near by familiar with the nature of the man to whom the unfortunates were appealing—some who, by long intercourse with him, had caught somewhat of his divine compassion: they gazed at him, and were silent while, in fair view, he rode up and stopped in front of the woman. She also beheld his face—calm, pitiful, and of exceeding beauty, the large eyes tender with benignant purpose.\n\nAnd this was the colloquy that ensued:\n\n“O Master, Master! Thou seest our need; thou canst make us clean. Have mercy upon us—mercy!”\n\n“Believest thou I am able to do this?” he asked.\n\n“Thou art he of whom the prophets spake—thou art the Messiah!” she replied.\n\nHis eyes grew radiant, his manner confident.\n\n“Woman,” he said, “great is thy faith; be it unto thee even as thou wilt.”\n\nHe lingered an instant after, apparently unconscious of the presence of the throng—an instant—then he rode away.\n\nTo the heart divinely original, yet so human in all the better elements of humanity, going with sure prevision to a death of all the inventions of men the foulest and most cruel, breathing even then in the forecast shadow of the awful event, and still as hungry and thirsty for love and faith as in the beginning, how precious and ineffably soothing the farewell exclamation of the grateful woman:\n\n“To God in the highest, glory! Blessed, thrice blessed, the Son whom he hath given us!”\n\nImmediately both the hosts, that from the city and that from Bethphage, closed around him with their joyous demonstrations, with hosannas and waving of palms, and so he passed from the lepers forever. Covering her head, the elder hastened to Tirzah, and folded her in her arms, crying, “Daughter, look up! I have his promise; he is indeed the Messiah. We are saved—saved!” And the two remained kneeling while the procession, slowly going, disappeared over the mount. When the noise of its singing afar was a sound scarcely heard the miracle began.\n\nThere was first in the hearts of the lepers a freshening of the blood; then it flowed faster and stronger, thrilling their wasted bodies with an infinitely sweet sense of painless healing. Each felt the scourge going from her; their strength revived; they were returning to be themselves. Directly, as if to make the purification complete, from body to spirit the quickening ran, exalting them to a very fervor of ecstasy. The power possessing them to this good end was most nearly that of a draught of swift and happy effect; yet it was unlike and superior in that its healing and cleansing were absolute, and not merely a delicious consciousness while in progress, but the planting, growing, and maturing all at once of a recollection so singular and so holy that the simple thought of it should be of itself ever after a formless yet perfect thanksgiving.\n\nTo this transformation—for such it may be called quite as properly as a cure—there was a witness other than Amrah. The reader will remember the constancy with which Ben-Hur had followed the Nazarene throughout his wanderings; and now, recalling the conversation of the night before, there will be little surprise at learning that the young Jew was present when the leprous woman appeared in the path of the pilgrims. He heard her prayer, and saw her disfigured face; he heard the answer also, and was not so accustomed to incidents of the kind, frequent as they had been, as to have lost interest in them. Had such thing been possible with him, still the bitter disputation always excited by the simplest display of the Master’s curative gift would have sufficed to keep his curiosity alive. Besides that, if not above it as an incentive, his hope to satisfy himself upon the vexed question of the mission of the mysterious man was still upon him strong as in the beginning; we might indeed say even stronger, because of a belief that now quickly, before the sun went down, the man himself would make all known by public proclamation. At the close of the scene, consequently, Ben-Hur had withdrawn from the procession, and seated himself upon a stone to wait its passage.\n\nFrom his place he nodded recognition to many of the people—Galileans in his league, carrying short swords under their long abbas. After a little a swarthy Arab came up leading two horses; at a sign from Ben-Hur he also drew out.\n\n“Stay here,” the young master said, when all were gone by, even the laggards. “I wish to be at the city early, and Aldebaran must do me service.”\n\nHe stroked the broad forehead of the horse, now in his prime of strength and beauty, then crossed the road towards the two women.\n\nThey were to him, it should be borne in mind, strangers in whom he felt interest only as they were subjects of a superhuman experiment, the result of which might possibly help him to solution of the mystery that had so long engaged him. As he proceeded, he glanced casually at the figure of the little woman over by the white rock, standing there, her face hidden in her hands.\n\n“As the Lord liveth, it is Amrah!” he said to himself.\n\nHe hurried on, and passing by the mother and daughter, still without recognizing them, he stopped before the servant.\n\n“Amrah,” he said to her, “Amrah, what do you here?”\n\nShe rushed forward, and fell upon her knees before him, blinded by her tears, nigh speechless with contending joy and fear.\n\n“O master, master! Thy God and mine, how good he is!”\n\nThe knowledge we gain from much sympathy with others passing through trials is but vaguely understood; strangely enough, it enables us, among other things, to merge our identity into theirs often so completely that their sorrows and their delights become our own. So poor Amrah, aloof and hiding her face, knew the transformation the lepers were undergoing without a word spoken to her—knew it, and shared all their feeling to the full. Her countenance, her words, her whole manner, betrayed her condition; and with swift presentiment he connected it with the women he had just passed: he felt her presence there at that time was in some way associated with them, and turned hastily as they arose to their feet. His heart stood still, he became rooted in his tracks—dumb past outcry—awe-struck.\n\nThe woman he had seen before the Nazarene was standing with her hands clasped and eyes streaming, looking towards heaven. The mere transformation would have been a sufficient surprise; but it was the least of the causes of his emotion. Could he be mistaken? Never was there in life a stranger so like his mother; and like her as she was the day the Roman snatched her from him. There was but one difference to mar the identity—the hair of this person was a little streaked with gray; yet that was not impossible of reconcilement, since the intelligence which had directed the miracle might have taken into consideration the natural effects of the passage of years. And who was it by her side, if not Tirzah?—fair, beautiful, perfect, more mature, but in all other respects exactly the same in appearance as when she looked with him over the parapet the morning of the accident to Gratus. He had given them over as dead, and time had accustomed him to the bereavement; he had not ceased mourning for them, yet, as something distinguishable, they had simply dropped out of his plans and dreams. Scarcely believing his senses, he laid his hand upon the servant’s head, and asked, tremulously,\n\n“Amrah, Amrah—my mother! Tirzah! tell me if I see aright.”\n\n“Speak to them, O master, speak to them!” she said.\n\nHe waited no longer, but ran, with outstretched arms, crying, “Mother! mother! Tirzah! Here I am!”\n\nThey heard his call, and with a cry as loving started to meet him. Suddenly the mother stopped, drew back, and uttered the old alarm,\n\n“Stay, Judah, my son; come not nearer. Unclean, unclean!”\n\nThe utterance was not from habit, grown since the dread disease struck her, as much as fear; and the fear was but another form of the ever-thoughtful maternal love. Though they were healed in person, the taint of the scourge might be in their garments ready for communication. He had no such thought. They were before him; he had called them, they had answered. Who or what should keep them from him now? Next moment the three, so long separated, were mingling their tears in each other’s arms.\n\nThe first ecstasy over, the mother said, “In this happiness, O my children, let us not be ungrateful. Let us begin life anew by acknowledgment of him to whom we are all so indebted.”\n\nThey fell upon their knees, Amrah with the rest; and the prayer of the elder outspoken was as a psalm.\n\nTirzah repeated it word for word; so did Ben-Hur, but not with the same clear mind and questionless faith; for when they were risen, he asked,\n\n“In Nazareth, where the man was born, mother, they call him the son of a carpenter. What is he?”\n\nHer eyes rested upon him with all their old tenderness, and she answered as she had answered the Nazarene himself—\n\n“He is the Messiah.”\n\n“And whence has he his power?”\n\n“We may know by the use he makes of it. Can you tell me any ill he has done?”\n\n“No.”\n“By that sign then I answer, He has his power from God.”\n\nIt is not an easy thing to shake off in a moment the expectations nurtured through years until they have become essentially a part of us; and though Ben-Hur asked himself what the vanities of the world were to such a one, his ambition was obdurate and would not down. He persisted as men do yet every day in measuring the Christ by himself. How much better if we measured ourselves by the Christ!\n\nNaturally, the mother was the first to think of the cares of life.\n\n“What shall we do now, my son? Where shall we go?”\n\nThen Ben-Hur, recalled to duty, observed how completely every trace of the scourge had disappeared from his restored people; that each had back her perfection of person; that, as with Naaman when he came up out of the water, their flesh had come again like unto the flesh of a little child; and he took off his cloak, and threw it over Tirzah.\n\n“Take it,” he said, smiling; “the eye of the stranger would have shunned you before, now it shall not offend you.”\n\nThe act exposed a sword belted to his side.\n\n“Is it a time of war?” asked the mother, anxiously.\n\n“No.”\n“Why, then, are you armed?”\n\n“It may be necessary to defend the Nazarene.”\n\nThus Ben-Hur evaded the whole truth.\n\n“Has he enemies? Who are they?”\n\n“Alas, mother, they are not all Romans!”\n\n“Is he not of Israel, and a man of peace?”\n\n“There was never one more so; but in the opinion of the rabbis and teachers he is guilty of a great crime.”\n\n“What crime?”\n\n“In his eyes the uncircumcised Gentile is as worthy favor as a Jew of the strictest habit. He preaches a new dispensation.”\n\nThe mother was silent, and they moved to the shade of the tree by the rock. Calming his impatience to have them home again and hear their story, he showed them the necessity of obedience to the law governing in cases like theirs, and in conclusion called the Arab, bidding him take the horses to the gate by Bethesda and await him there; whereupon they set out by the way of the Mount of Offence. The return was very different from the coming; they walked rapidly and with ease, and in good time reached a tomb newly made near that of Absalom, overlooking the depths of Cedron. Finding it unoccupied, the women took possession, while he went on hastily to make the preparations required for their new condition.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nBen-Hur pitched two tents out on the Upper Cedron east a short space of the Tombs of the Kings, and furnished them with every comfort at his command; and thither, without loss of time, he conducted his mother and sister, to remain until the examining priest could certify their perfect cleansing.\n\nIn course of the duty, the young man had subjected himself to such serious defilement as to debar him from participation in the ceremonies of the great feast, then near at hand. He could not enter the least sacred of the courts of the Temple. Of necessity, not less than choice, therefore, he stayed at the tents with his beloved people. There was a great deal to hear from them, and a great deal to tell them of himself.\n\nStories such as theirs—sad experiences extending through a lapse of years, sufferings of body, acuter sufferings of mind—are usually long in the telling, the incidents seldom following each other in threaded connection. He listened to the narrative and all they told him, with outward patience masking inward feeling. In fact, his hatred of Rome and Romans reached a higher mark than ever; his desire for vengeance became a thirst which attempts at reflection only intensified. In the almost savage bitterness of his humor many mad impulses took hold of him. The opportunities of the highways presented themselves with singular force of temptation; he thought seriously of insurrection in Galilee; even the sea, ordinarily a retrospective horror to him, stretched itself map-like before his fancy, laced and interlaced with lines of passage crowded with imperial plunder and imperial travellers; but the better judgment matured in calmer hours was happily too firmly fixed to be supplanted by present passion however strong. Each mental venture in reach of new expedients brought him back to the old conclusion—that there could be no sound success except in a war involving all Israel in solid union; and all musing upon the subject, all inquiry, all hope, ended where they began—in the Nazarene and his purposes.\n\nAt odd moments the excited schemer found a pleasure in fashioning a speech for that person:\n\n“Hear, O Israel! I am he, the promised of God, born King of the Jews—come to you with the dominion spoken of by the prophets. Rise now, and lay hold on the world!”\n\nWould the Nazarene but speak these few words, what a tumult would follow! How many mouths performing the office of trumpets would take them up and blow them abroad for the massing of armies!\n\nWould he speak them?\n\nAnd eager to begin the work, and answering in the worldly way, Ben-Hur lost sight of the double nature of the man, and of the other possibility, that the divine in him might transcend the human. In the miracle of which Tirzah and his mother were the witnesses even more nearly than himself, he saw and set apart and dwelt upon a power ample enough to raise and support a Jewish crown over the wrecks of the Italian, and more than ample to remodel society, and convert mankind into one purified happy family; and when that work was done, could any one say the peace which might then be ordered without hindrance was not a mission worthy a son of God? Could any one then deny the Redeemership of the Christ? And discarding all consideration of political consequences, what unspeakable personal glory there would then be to him as a man? It was not in the nature of any mere mortal to refuse such a career.\n\nMeantime down the Cedron, and in towards Bezetha, especially on the roadsides quite up to the Damascus Gate, the country filled rapidly with all kinds of temporary shelters for pilgrims to the Passover. Ben-Hur visited the strangers, and talked with them; and returning to his tents, he was each time more and more astonished at the vastness of their numbers. And when he further discovered that every part of the world was represented among them—cities upon both shores of the Mediterranean far off as the Pillars of the West, river-towns in distant India, provinces in northernmost Europe; and that, though they frequently saluted him with tongues unacquainted with a syllable of the old Hebrew of the fathers, these representatives had all the same object—celebration of the notable feast—an idea tinged mistily with superstitious fancy forced itself upon him. Might he not after all have misunderstood the Nazarene? Might not that person by patient waiting be covering silent preparation, and proving his fitness for the glorious task before him? How much better this time for the movement than that other when, by Gennesaret, the Galileans would have forced assumption of the crown? Then the support would have been limited to a few thousands; now his proclamation would be responded to by millions—who could say how many? Pursuing this theory to its conclusions, Ben-Hur moved amidst brilliant promises, and glowed with the thought that the melancholy man, under gentle seeming and wondrous self-denial, was in fact carrying in disguise the subtlety of a politician and the genius of a soldier.\n\nSeveral times also, in the meanwhile, low-set, brawny men, bareheaded and black-bearded, came and asked for Ben-Hur at the tent; his interviews with them were always apart; and to his mother’s question who they were he answered,\n\n“Some good friends of mine from Galilee.”\n\nThrough them he kept informed of the movements of the Nazarene, and of the schemes of the Nazarene’s enemies, Rabbinical and Roman. That the good man’s life was in danger, he knew; but that there were any bold enough to attempt to take it at that time, he could not believe. It seemed too securely intrenched in a great fame and an assured popularity. The very vastness of the attendance in and about the city brought with it a seeming guaranty of safety. And yet, to say truth, Ben-Hur’s confidence rested most certainly upon the miraculous power of the Christ. Pondering the subject in the purely human view, that the master of such authority over life and death, used so frequently for the good of others, would not exert it in care of himself was simply as much past belief as it was past understanding.\n\nNor should it be forgotten that all these were incidents of occurrence between the twenty-first day of March—counting by the modern calendar—and the twenty-fifth. The evening of the latter day Ben-Hur yielded to his impatience, and rode to the city, leaving behind him a promise to return in the night.\n\nThe horse was fresh, and choosing his own gait, sped swiftly. The eyes of the clambering vines winked at the rider from the garden fences on the way; there was nothing else to see him, nor child nor woman nor man. Through the rocky float in the hollows of the road the agate hoofs drummed, ringing like cups of steel; but without notice from any stranger. In the houses passed there were no tenants; the fires by the tent-doors were out; the road was deserted; for this was the first Passover eve, and the hour “between the evenings” when the visiting millions crowded the city, and the slaughter of lambs in offering reeked the fore-courts of the Temple, and the priests in ordered lines caught the flowing blood and carried it swiftly to the dripping altars—when all was haste and hurry, racing with the stars fast coming with the signal after which the roasting and the eating and the singing might go on, but not the preparation more.\n\nThrough the great northern gate the rider rode, and lo! Jerusalem before the fall, in ripeness of glory, illuminated for the Lord.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nBen-Hur alighted at the gate of the khan from which the three Wise Men more than thirty years before departed, going down to Bethlehem. There, in keeping of his Arab followers, he left the horse, and shortly after was at the wicket of his father’s house, and in a yet briefer space in the great chamber. He called for Malluch first; that worthy being out, he sent a salutation to his friends the merchant and the Egyptian. They were being carried abroad to see the celebration. The latter, he was informed, was very feeble, and in a state of deep dejection.\n\nYoung people of that time who were supposed hardly to know their own hearts indulged the habit of politic indirection quite as much as young people in the same condition indulge it in this time; so when Ben-Hur inquired for the good Balthasar, and with grave courtesy desired to know if he would be pleased to see him, he really addressed the daughter a notice of his arrival. While the servant was answering for the elder, the curtain of the doorway was drawn aside, and the younger Egyptian came in, and walked—or floated, upborne in a white cloud of the gauzy raiment she so loved and lived in—to the centre of the chamber, where the light cast by lamps from the seven-armed brazen stick planted upon the floor was the strongest. With her there was no fear of light.\n\nThe servant left the two alone.\n\nIn the excitement occasioned by the events of the few days past Ben-Hur had scarcely given a thought to the fair Egyptian. If she came to his mind at all, it was merely as a briefest pleasure, a suggestion of a delight which could wait for him, and was waiting.\n\nBut now the influence of the woman revived with all its force the instant Ben-Hur beheld her. He advanced to her eagerly, but stopped and gazed. Such a change he had never seen!\n\nTheretofore she had been a lover studious to win him—in manner all warmth, each glance an admission, each action an avowal. She had showered him with incense of flattery. While he was present, she had impressed him with her admiration; going away, he carried the impression with him to remain a delicious expectancy hastening his return. It was for him the painted eyelids drooped lowest over the lustrous almond eyes; for him the love-stories caught from the professionals abounding in the streets of Alexandria were repeated with emphasis and lavishment of poetry; for him endless exclamations of sympathy, and smiles, and little privileges with hand and hair and cheek and lips, and songs of the Nile, and displays of jewelry, and subtleties of lace in veils and scarfs, and other subtleties not less exquisite in flosses of Indian silk. The idea, old as the oldest of peoples, that beauty is the reward of the hero had never such realism as she contrived for his pleasure; insomuch that he could not doubt he was her hero; she avouched it in a thousand artful ways as natural with her as her beauty—winsome ways reserved, it would seem, by the passionate genius of old Egypt for its daughters.\n\nSuch the Egyptian had been to Ben-Hur from the night of the boat-ride on the lake in the Orchard of Palms. But now!\n\nElsewhere in this volume the reader may have observed a term of somewhat indefinite meaning used reverently in a sacred connection; we repeat it now with a general application. There are few persons who have not a double nature, the real and the acquired; the latter a kind of addendum resulting from education, which in time often perfects it into a part of the being as unquestionable as the first. Leaving the thought to the thoughtful, we proceed to say that now the real nature of the Egyptian made itself manifest.\n\nIt was not possible for her to have received a stranger with repulsion more incisive; yet she was apparently as passionless as a statue, only the small head was a little tilted, the nostrils a little drawn, and the sensuous lower lip pushed the upper the least bit out of its natural curvature.\n\nShe was the first to speak.\n\n“Your coming is timely, O son of Hur,” she said, in a voice sharply distinct. “I wish to thank you for hospitality; after to-morrow I may not have the opportunity to do so.”\n\nBen-Hur bowed slightly without taking his eyes from her.\n\n“I have heard of a custom which the dice-players observe with good result among themselves,” she continued. “When the game is over, they refer to their tablets and cast up their accounts; then they libate the gods and put a crown upon the happy winner. We have had a game—it has lasted through many days and nights. Why, now that it is at an end, shall not we see to which the chaplet belongs?”\n\nYet very watchful, Ben-Hur answered, lightly, “A man may not balk a woman bent on having her way.”\n\n“Tell me,” she continued, inclining her head, and permitting the sneer to become positive—“tell me, O prince of Jerusalem, where is he, that son of the carpenter of Nazareth, and son not less of God, from whom so lately such mighty things were expected?”\n\nHe waved his hand impatiently, and replied, “I am not his keeper.”\n\nThe beautiful head sank forward yet lower.\n\n“Has he broken Rome to pieces?”\n\nAgain, but with anger, Ben-Hur raised his hand in deprecation.\n\n“Where has he seated his capital?” she proceeded. “Cannot I go see his throne and its lions of bronze? And his palace—he raised the dead; and to such a one, what is it to raise a golden house? He has but to stamp his foot and say the word, and the house is, pillared like Karnak, and wanting nothing.”\n\nThere was by this time slight ground left to believe her playing; the questions were offensive, and her manner pointed with unfriendliness; seeing which, he on his side became more wary, and said, with good humor, “O Egypt, let us wait another day, even another week, for him, the lions, and the palace.”\n\nShe went on without noticing the suggestion.\n\n“And how is it I see you in that garb? Such is not the habit of governors in India or vice-kings elsewhere. I saw the satrap of Teheran once, and he wore a turban of silk and a cloak of cloth of gold, and the hilt and scabbard of his sword made me dizzy with their splendor of precious stones. I thought Osiris had lent him a glory from the sun. I fear you have not entered upon your kingdom—the kingdom I was to share with you.”\n\n“The daughter of my wise guest is kinder than she imagines herself; she is teaching me that Isis may kiss a heart without making it better.”\n\nBen-Hur spoke with cold courtesy, and Iras, after playing with the pendent solitaire of her necklace of coins, rejoined, “For a Jew, the son of Hur is clever. I saw your dreaming Cæsar make his entry into Jerusalem. You told us he would that day proclaim himself King of the Jews from the steps of the Temple. I beheld the procession descend the mountain bringing him. I heard their singing. They were beautiful with palms in motion. I looked everywhere among them for a figure with a promise of royalty—a horseman in purple, a chariot with a driver in shining brass, a stately warrior behind an orbed shield, rivalling his spear in stature. I looked for his guard. It would have been pleasant to have seen a prince of Jerusalem and a cohort of the legions of Galilee.”\n\nShe flung her listener a glance of provoking disdain, then laughed heartily, as if the ludicrousness of the picture in her mind were too strong for contempt.\n\n“Instead of a Sesostris returning in triumph or a Cæsar helmed and sworded—ha, ha, ha!—I saw a man with a woman’s face and hair, riding an ass’s colt, and in tears. The King! the Son of God! the Redeemer of the world! Ha, ha, ha!”\n\nIn spite of himself, Ben-Hur winced.\n\n“I did not quit my place, O prince of Jerusalem,” she said, before he could recover. “I did not laugh. I said to myself, ‘Wait. In the Temple he will glorify himself as becomes a hero about to take possession of the world.’ I saw him enter the Gate of Shushan and the Court of the Women. I saw him stop and stand before the Gate Beautiful. There were people with me on the porch and in the courts, and on the cloisters and on the steps of the three sides of the Temple there were other people—I will say a million of people, all waiting breathlessly to hear his proclamation. The pillars were not more still than we. Ha, ha, ha! I fancied I heard the axles of the mighty Roman machine begin to crack. Ha, ha, ha! O prince, by the soul of Solomon, your King of the World drew his gown about him and walked away, and out by the farthest gate, nor opened his mouth to say a word; and—the Roman machine is running yet!”\n\nIn simple homage to a hope that instant lost—a hope which, as it began to fall and while it was falling, he unconsciously followed with a parting look down to its disappearance—Ben-Hur lowered his eyes.\n\nAt no previous time, whether when Balthasar was plying him with arguments, or when miracles were being done before his face, had the disputed nature of the Nazarene been so plainly set before him. The best way, after all, to reach an understanding of the divine is by study of the human. In the things superior to men we may always look to find God. So with the picture given by the Egyptian of the scene when the Nazarene turned from the Gate Beautiful; its central theme was an act utterly beyond performance by a man under control of merely human inspirations. A parable to a parable-loving people, it taught what the Christ had so often asserted—that his mission was not political. There was not much more time for thought of all this than that allowed for a common respiration; yet the idea took fast hold of Ben-Hur, and in the same instant he followed his hope of vengeance out of sight, and the man with the woman’s face and hair, and in tears, came near to him—near enough to leave something of his spirit behind.\n\n“Daughter of Balthasar,” he said, with dignity, “if this be the game of which you spoke to me, take the chaplet—I accord it yours. Only let us make an end of words. That you have a purpose I am sure. To it, I pray, and I will answer you; then let us go our several ways, and forget we ever met. Say on; I will listen, but not to more of that which you have given me.”\n\nShe regarded him intently a moment, as if determining what to do—possibly she might have been measuring his will—then she said, coldly, “You have my leave—go.”\n\n“Peace to you,” he responded, and walked away.\n\nAs he was about passing out of the door, she called to him.\n\n“A word.”\n\nHe stopped where he was, and looked back.\n\n“Consider all I know about you.”\n\n“O most fair Egyptian,” he said, returning, “what all do you know about me?”\n\nShe looked at him absently.\n\n“You are more of a Roman, son of Hur, then any of your Hebrew brethren.”\n\n“Am I so unlike my countrymen?” he asked, indifferently.\n\n“The demi-gods are all Roman now,” she rejoined.\n\n“And therefore you will tell me what more you know about me?”\n\n“The likeness is not lost upon me. It might induce me to save you.”\n\n“Save me!”\n\nThe pink-stained fingers toyed daintily with the lustrous pendant at the throat, and her voice was exceeding low and soft; only a tapping on the floor with her silken sandal admonished him to have a care.\n\n“There was a Jew, an escaped galley-slave, who killed a man in the Palace of Idernee,” she began, slowly.\n\nBen-Hur was startled.\n\n“The same Jew slew a Roman soldier before the Market-place here in Jerusalem; the same Jew has three trained legions from Galilee to seize the Roman governor to-night; the same Jew has alliances perfected for war upon Rome, and Ilderim the Sheik is one of his partners.”\n\nDrawing nearer him, she almost whispered,\n\n“You have lived in Rome. Suppose these things repeated in ears we know of. Ah! you change color.”\n\nHe drew back from her with somewhat of the look which may be imagined upon the face of a man who, thinking to play with a kitten, has run upon a tiger; and she proceeded:\n\n“You are acquainted in the antechamber, and know the Lord Sejanus. Suppose it were told him with the proofs in hand—or without the proofs—that the same Jew is the richest man in the East—nay, in all the empire. The fishes of the Tiber would have fattening other than that they dig out of its ooze, would they not? And while they were feeding—ha! son of Hur!—what splendor there would be on exhibition in the Circus! Amusing the Roman people is a fine art; getting the money to keep them amused is another art even finer; and was there ever an artist the equal of the Lord Sejanus?”\n\nBen-Hur was not too much stirred by the evident baseness of the woman for recollection. Not unfrequently when all the other faculties are numb and failing memory does its offices with the greatest fidelity. The scene at the spring on the way to the Jordan reproduced itself; and he remembered thinking then that Esther had betrayed him, and thinking so now, he said calmly as he could,\n\n“To give you pleasure, daughter of Egypt, I acknowledge your cunning, and that I am at your mercy. It may also please you to hear me acknowledge I have no hope of your favor. I could kill you, but you are a woman. The Desert is open to receive me; and though Rome is a good hunter of men, there she would follow long and far before she caught me, for in its heart there are wildernesses of spears as well as wildernesses of sand, and it is not unlovely to the unconquered Parthian. In the toils as I am—dupe that I have been—yet there is one thing my due: who told you all you know about me? In flight or captivity, dying even, there will be consolation in leaving the traitor the curse of a man who has lived knowing nothing but wretchedness. Who told you all you know about me?”\n\nIt might have been a touch of art, or might have been sincere—that as it may—the expression of the Egyptian’s face became sympathetic.\n\n“There are in my country, O son of Hur,” she said, presently, “workmen who make pictures by gathering vari-colored shells here and there on the sea-shore after storms, and cutting them up, and patching the pieces as inlaying on marble slabs. Can you not see the hint there is in the practice to such as go searching for secrets? Enough that from this person I gathered a handful of little circumstances, and from that other yet another handful, and that afterwhile I put them together, and was happy as a woman can be who has at disposal the fortune and life of a man whom”—she stopped, and beat the floor with her foot, and looked away as if to hide a sudden emotion from him; with an air of even painful resolution she presently finished the sentence—“whom she is at loss what to do with.”\n\n“No, it is not enough,” Ben-Hur said, unmoved by the play—“it is not enough. To-morrow you will determine what to do with me. I may die.”\n\n“True,” she rejoined quickly and with emphasis, “I had something from Sheik Ilderim as he lay with my father in a grove out in the Desert. The night was still, very still, and the walls of the tent, sooth to say, were poor ward against ears outside listening to—birds and beetles flying through the air.”\n\nShe smiled at the conceit, but proceeded:\n\n“Some other things—bits of shell for the picture—I had from—”\n\n“Whom?”\n“The son of Hur himself.”\n\n“Was there no other who contributed?”\n\n“No, not one.”\n\nHur drew a breath of relief, and said, lightly, “Thanks. It were not well to keep the Lord Sejanus waiting for you. The Desert is not so sensitive. Again, O Egypt, peace!”\n\nTo this time he had been standing uncovered; now he took the handkerchief from his arm where it had been hanging, and adjusting it upon his head, turned to depart. But she arrested him; in her eagerness, she even reached a hand to him.\n\n“Stay,” she said.\n\nHe looked back at her, but without taking the hand, though it was very noticeable for its sparkling of jewels; and he knew by her manner that the reserved point of the scene which was so surprising to him was now to come.\n\n“Stay, and do not distrust me, O son of Hur, if I declare I know why the noble Arrius took you for his heir. And, by Isis! by all the gods of Egypt! I swear I tremble to think of you, so brave and generous, under the hand of the remorseless minister. You have left a portion of your youth in the atria of the great capital; consider, as I do, what the Desert will be to you in contrast of life. Oh, I give you pity—pity! And if you but do what I say, I will save you. That, also, I swear, by our holy Isis!”\n\nWords of entreaty and prayer these, poured forth volubly and with earnestness and the mighty sanction of beauty.\n\n“Almost—almost I believe you,” Ben-Hur said, yet hesitatingly, and in a voice low and indistinct; for a doubt remained with him grumbling against the yielding tendency of the man—a good sturdy doubt, such a one as has saved many a life and fortune.\n\n“The perfect life for a woman is to live in love; the greatest happiness for a man is the conquest of himself; and that, O prince, is what I have to ask of you.”\n\nShe spoke rapidly, and with animation; indeed, she had never appeared to him so fascinating.\n\n“You had once a friend,” she continued. “It was in your boyhood. There was a quarrel, and you and he became enemies. He did you wrong. After many years you met him again in the Circus at Antioch.”\n\n“Messala!”\n\n“Yes, Messala. You are his creditor. Forgive the past; admit him to friendship again; restore the fortune he lost in the great wager; rescue him. The six talents are as nothing to you; not so much as a bud lost upon a tree already in full leaf; but to him— Ah, he must go about with a broken body; wherever you meet him he must look up to you from the ground. O Ben-Hur, noble prince! to a Roman descended as he is beggary is the other most odious name for death. Save him from beggary!”\n\nIf the rapidity with which she spoke was a cunning invention to keep him from thinking, either she never knew or else had forgotten that there are convictions which derive nothing from thought, but drop into place without leave or notice. It seemed to him, when at last she paused to have his answer, that he could see Messala himself peering at him over her shoulder; and in its expression the countenance of the Roman was not that of a mendicant or a friend; the sneer was as patrician as ever, and the fine edge of the hauteur as flawless and irritating.\n\n“The appeal has been decided then, and for once a Messala takes nothing. I must go and write it in my book of great occurrences—a judgment by a Roman against a Roman! But did he—did Messala send you to me with this request, O Egypt?”\n\n“He has a noble nature, and judged you by it.”\n\nBen-Hur took the hand upon arm.\n\n“As you know him in such friendly way, fair Egyptian, tell me, would he do for me, there being a reversal of the conditions, that he asks of me? Answer, by Isis! Answer, for the truth’s sake!”\n\nThere was insistence in the touch of his hand, and in his look also.\n\n“Oh!” she began, “he is—”\n\n“A Roman, you were about to say; meaning that I, a Jew, must not determine dues from me to him by any measure of dues from him to me; being a Jew, I must forgive him my winnings because he is a Roman. If you have more to tell me, daughter of Balthasar, speak quickly, quickly; for by the Lord God of Israel, when this heat of blood, hotter waxing, attains its highest, I may not be able longer to see that you are a woman, and beautiful! I may see but the spy of a master the more hateful because the master is a Roman. Say on, and quickly.”\n\nShe threw his hand off and stepped back into the full light, with all the evil of her nature collected in her eyes and voice.\n\n“Thou drinker of lees, feeder upon husks! To think I could love thee, having seen Messala! Such as thou were born to serve him. He would have been satisfied with release of the six talents; but I say to the six thou shalt add twenty—twenty, dost thou hear? The kissings of my little finger which thou hast taken from him, though with my consent, shall be paid for; and that I have followed thee with affection of sympathy, and endured thee so long, enter into the account not less because I was serving him. The merchant here is thy keeper of moneys. If by to-morrow at noon he has not thy order acted upon in favor of my Messala for six-and-twenty talents—mark the sum!—thou shalt settle with the Lord Sejanus. Be wise and—farewell.”\n\nAs she was going to the door, he put himself in her way.\n\n“The old Egypt lives in you,” he said. “Whether you see Messala to-morrow or the next day, here or in Rome, give him this message. Tell him I have back the money, even the six talents, he robbed me of by robbing my father’s estate; tell him I survived the galleys to which he had me sent, and in my strength rejoice in his beggary and dishonor; tell him I think the affliction of body which he has from my hand is the curse of our Lord God of Israel upon him more fit than death for his crimes against the helpless; tell him my mother and sister whom he had sent to a cell in Antonia that they might die of leprosy, are alive and well, thanks to the power of the Nazarene whom you so despise; tell him that, to fill my measure of happiness, they are restored to me, and that I will go hence to their love, and find in it more than compensation for the impure passions which you leave me to take to him; tell him—this for your comfort, O cunning incarnate, as much as his—tell him that when the Lord Sejanus comes to despoil me he will find nothing; for the inheritance I had from the duumvir, including the villa by Misenum, has been sold, and the money from the sale is out of reach, afloat in the marts of the world as bills of exchange; and that this house and the goods and merchandise and the ships and caravans with which Simonides plies his commerce with such princely profits are covered by imperial safeguards—a wise head having found the price of the favor, and the Lord Sejanus preferring a reasonable gain in the way of gift to much gain fished from pools of blood and wrong; tell him if all this were not so, if the money and property were all mine, yet should he not have the least part of it, for when he finds our Jewish bills, and forces them to give up their values, there is yet another resort left me—a deed of gift to Cæsar—so much, O Egypt, I found out in the atria of the great capital; tell him that along with my defiance I do not send him a curse in words, but, as a better expression of my undying hate, I send him one who will prove to him the sum of all curses; and when he looks at you repeating this my message, daughter of Balthasar, his Roman shrewdness will tell him all I mean. Go now—and I will go.”\n\nHe conducted her to the door, and, with ceremonious politeness, held back the curtain while she passed out.\n\n“Peace to you,” he said, as she disappeared.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nWhen Ben-Hur left the guest-chamber, there was not nearly so much life in his action as when he entered it; his steps were slower, and he went along with his head quite upon his breast. Having made discovery that a man with a broken back may yet have a sound brain, he was reflecting upon the discovery.\n\nForasmuch as it is easy after a calamity has befallen to look back and see the proofs of its coming strewn along the way, the thought that he had not even suspected the Egyptian as in Messala’s interest, but had gone blindly on through whole years putting himself and his friends more and more at her mercy, was a sore wound to the young man’s vanity. “I remember,” he said to himself, “she had no word of indignation for the perfidious Roman at the Fountain of Castalia! I remember she extolled him at the boat-ride on the lake in the Orchard of Palms! And, ah!”—he stopped, and beat his left hand violently with his right—“ah! that mystery about the appointment she made with me at the Palace of Idernee is no mystery now!”\n\nThe wound, it should be observed, was to his vanity; and fortunately it is not often that people die of such hurts, or even continue a long time sick. In Ben-Hur’s case, moreover, there was a compensation; for presently he exclaimed aloud, “Praised be the Lord God that the woman took not a more lasting hold on me! I see I did not love her.”\n\nThen, as if he had already parted with not a little of the weight on his mind, he stepped forward more lightly; and, coming to the place on the terrace where one stairway led down to the court-yard below, and another ascended to the roof, he took the latter and began to climb. As he made the last step in the flight he stopped again.\n\n“Can Balthasar have been her partner in the long mask she has been playing? No, no. Hypocrisy seldom goes with wrinkled age like that. Balthasar is a good man.”\n\nWith this decided opinion he stepped upon the roof. There was a full moon overhead, yet the vault of the sky at the moment was lurid with light cast up from the fires burning in the streets and open places of the city, and the chanting and chorusing of the old psalmody of Israel filled it with plaintive harmonies to which he could not but listen. The countless voices bearing the burden seemed to say, “Thus, O son of Judah, we prove our worshipfulness of the Lord God, and our loyalty to the land he gave us. Let a Gideon appear, or a David, or a Maccabaeus, and we are ready.”\n\nThat seemed an introduction; for next he saw the man of Nazareth.\n\nIn certain moods the mind is disposed to mock itself with inapposite fancies.\n\nThe tearful woman-like face of the Christ stayed with him while he crossed the roof to the parapet above the street on the north side of the house, and there was in it no sign of war; but rather as the heavens of calm evenings look peace upon everything, so it looked, provoking the old question, What manner of man is he?\n\nBen-Hur permitted himself one glance over the parapet, then turned and walked mechanically towards the summer-house.\n\n“Let them do their worst,” he said, as he went slowly on. “I will not forgive the Roman. I will not divide my fortune with him, nor will I fly from this city of my fathers. I will call on Galilee first, and here make the fight. By brave deeds I will bring the tribes to our side. He who raised up Moses will find us a leader, if I fail. If not the Nazarene, then some other of the many ready to die for freedom.”\n\nThe interior of the summer-house, when Ben-Hur, slow sauntering, came to it, was murkily lighted. The faintest of shadows lay along the floor from the pillars on the north and west sides. Looking in, he saw the arm-chair usually occupied by Simonides drawn to a spot from which a view of the city over towards the Market-place could be best had.\n\n“The good man is returned. I will speak with him, unless he be asleep.”\n\nHe walked in, and with a quiet step approached the chair. Peering over the high back, he beheld Esther nestled in the seat asleep—a small figure snugged away under her father’s lap-robe. The hair dishevelled fell over her face. Her breathing was low and irregular. Once it was broken by a long sigh, ending in a sob. Something—it might have been the sigh or the loneliness in which he found her—imparted to him the idea that the sleep was a rest from sorrow rather than fatigue. Nature kindly sends such relief to children, and he was used to thinking Esther scarcely more than a child. He put his arms upon the back of the chair, and thought.\n\n“I will not wake her. I have nothing to tell her—nothing unless—unless it be my love.... She is a daughter of Judah, and beautiful, and so unlike the Egyptian; for there it is all vanity, here all truth; there ambition, here duty; there selfishness, here self-sacrifice.... Nay, the question is not do I love her, but does she love me? She was my friend from the beginning. The night on the terrace at Antioch, how child-like she begged me not to make Rome my enemy, and had me tell her of the villa by Misenum, and of the life there! That she should not see I saw her cunning drift I kissed her. Can she have forgotten the kiss! I have not. I love her.... They do not know in the city that I have back my people. I shrank from telling it to the Egyptian; but this little one will rejoice with me over their restoration, and welcome them with love and sweet services of hand and heart. She will be to my mother another daughter; in Tirzah she will find her other self. I would wake her and tell her these things, but—out on the sorceress of Egypt! Of that folly I could not command myself to speak. I will go away, and wait another and a better time. I will wait. Fair Esther, dutiful child, daughter of Judah!”\n\nHe retired silently as he came.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\nThe streets were full of people going and coming, or grouped about the fires roasting meat, and feasting and singing, and happy. The odor of scorching flesh mixed with the odor of cedar-wood aflame and smoking loaded the air; and as this was the occasion when every son of Israel was full brother to every other son of Israel, and hospitality was without bounds, Ben-Hur was saluted at every step, while the groups by the fires insisted, “Stay and partake with us. We are brethren in the love of the Lord.” But with thanks to them he hurried on, intending to take horse at the khan and return to the tents on the Cedron.\n\nTo make the place, it was necessary for him to cross the thoroughfare so soon to receive sorrowful Christian perpetuation. There also the pious celebration was at its height. Looking up the street, he noticed the flames of torches in motion streaming out like pennons; then he observed that the singing ceased where the torches came. His wonder rose to its highest, however, when he became certain that amidst the smoke and dancing sparks he saw the keener sparkling of burnished spear-tips, arguing the presence of Roman soldiers. What were they, the scoffing legionaries, doing in a Jewish religious procession? The circumstance was unheard of, and he stayed to see the meaning of it.\n\nThe moon was shining its best; yet, as if the moon and the torches, and the fires in the street, and the rays streaming from windows and open doors were not enough to make the way clear, some of the processionists carried lighted lanterns; and fancying he discovered a special purpose in the use of such equipments, Ben-Hur stepped into the street so close to the line of march as to bring every one of the company under view while passing. The torches and the lanterns were being borne by servants, each of whom was armed with a bludgeon or a sharpened stave. Their present duty seemed to be to pick out the smoothest paths among the rocks in the street for certain dignitaries among them—elders and priests; rabbis with long beards, heavy brows, and beaked noses; men of the class potential in the councils of Caiaphas and Hannas. Where could they be going? Not to the Temple, certainly, for the route to the sacred house from Zion, whence these appeared to be coming, was by the Xystus. And their business—if peaceful, why the soldiers?\n\nAs the procession began to go by Ben-Hur, his attention was particularly called to three persons walking together. They were well towards the front, and the servants who went before them with lanterns appeared unusually careful in the service. In the person moving on the left of this group he recognized a chief policeman of the Temple; the one on the right was a priest; the middle man was not at first so easily placed, as he walked leaning heavily upon the arms of the others, and carried his head so low upon his breast as to hide his face. His appearance was that of a prisoner not yet recovered from the fright of arrest, or being taken to something dreadful—to torture or death. The dignitaries helping him on the right and left, and the attention they gave him, made it clear that if he were not himself the object moving the party, he was at least in some way connected with the object—a witness or a guide, possibly an informer. So if it could be found who he was the business in hand might be shrewdly guessed. With great assurance, Ben-Hur fell in on the right of the priest, and walked along with him. Now if the man would lift his head! And presently he did so, letting the light of the lanterns strike full in his face, pale, dazed, pinched with dread; the beard roughed; the eyes filmy, sunken, and despairing. In much going about following the Nazarene, Ben-Hur had come to know his disciples as well as the Master; and now, at sight of the dismal countenance, he cried out,\n\n“The ’Scariot!”\n\nSlowly the head of the man turned until his eyes settled upon Ben-Hur, and his lips moved as if he were about to speak; but the priest interfered.\n\n“Who art thou? Begone!” he said to Ben-Hur, pushing him away.\n\nThe young man took the push good-naturedly, and, waiting an opportunity, fell into the procession again. Thus he was carried passively along down the street, through the crowded lowlands between the hill Bezetha and the Castle of Antonia, and on by the Bethesda reservoir to the Sheep Gate. There were people everywhere, and everywhere the people were engaged in sacred observances.\n\nIt being Passover night, the valves of the Gate stood open. The keepers were off somewhere feasting. In front of the procession as it passed out unchallenged was the deep gorge of the Cedron, with Olivet beyond, its dressing of cedar and olive trees darker of the moonlight silvering all the heavens. Two roads met and merged into the street at the gate—one from the northeast, the other from Bethany. Ere Ben-Hur could finish wondering whether he were to go farther, and if so, which road was to be taken, he was led off down into the gorge. And still no hint of the purpose of the midnight march.\n\nDown the gorge and over the bridge at the bottom of it. There was a great clatter on the floor as the crowd, now a straggling rabble, passed over beating and pounding with their clubs and staves. A little farther, and they turned off to the left in the direction of an olive orchard enclosed by a stone wall in view from the road. Ben-Hur knew there was nothing in the place but old gnarled trees, the grass, and a trough hewn out of a rock for the treading of oil after the fashion of the country. While, yet more wonder-struck, he was thinking what could bring such a company at such an hour to a quarter so lonesome, they were all brought to a standstill. Voices called out excitedly in front; a chill sensation ran from man to man; there was a rapid falling-back, and a blind stumbling over each other. The soldiers alone kept their order.\n\nIt took Ben-Hur but a moment to disengage himself from the mob and run forward. There he found a gateway without a gate admitting to the orchard, and he halted to take in the scene.\n\nA man in white clothes, and bareheaded, was standing outside the entrance, his hands crossed before him—a slender, stooping figure, with long hair and thin face—in an attitude of resignation and waiting.\n\nIt was the Nazarene!\n\nBehind him, next the gateway, were the disciples in a group; they were excited, but no man was ever calmer than he. The torchlight beat redly upon him, giving his hair a tint ruddier than was natural to it; yet the expression of the countenance was as usual all gentleness and pity.\n\nOpposite this most unmartial figure stood the rabble, gaping, silent, awed, cowering—ready at a sign of anger from him to break and run. And from him to them—then at Judas, conspicuous in their midst—Ben-Hur looked—one quick glance, and the object of the visit lay open to his understanding. Here was the betrayer, there the betrayed; and these with clubs and staves, and the legionaries, were brought to take him.\n\nA man may not always tell what he will do until the trial is upon him. This was the emergency for which Ben-Hur had been for years preparing. The man to whose security he had devoted himself, and upon whose life he had been building so largely, was in personal peril; yet he stood still. Such contradictions are there in human nature! To say truth, O reader, he was not entirely recovered from the picture of the Christ before the Gate Beautiful as it had been given by the Egyptian; and, besides that, the very calmness with which the mysterious person confronted the mob held him in restraint by suggesting the possession of a power in reserve more than sufficient for the peril. Peace and good-will, and love and non-resistance, had been the burden of the Nazarene’s teaching; would he put his preaching into practice? He was master of life; he could restore it when lost; he could take it at pleasure. What use would he make of the power now? Defend himself? And how? A word—a breath—a thought were sufficient. That there would be some signal exhibition of astonishing force beyond the natural Ben-Hur believed, and in that faith waited. And in all this he was still measuring the Nazarene by himself—by the human standard.\n\nPresently the clear voice of the Christ arose.\n\n“Whom seek ye?”\n\n“Jesus of Nazareth,” the priest replied.\n\n“I am he.”\n\nAt these simplest of words, spoken without passion or alarm, the assailants fell back several steps, the timid among them cowering to the ground; and they might have let him alone and gone away had not Judas walked over to him.\n\n“Hail, master!”\n\nWith this friendly speech, he kissed him.\n\n“Judas,” said the Nazarene, mildly, “betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss? Wherefore art thou come?”\n\nReceiving no reply, the Master spoke to the crowd again.\n\n“Whom seek ye?”\n\n“Jesus of Nazareth.”\n“I have told you that I am he. If, therefore, you seek me, let these go their way.”\n\nAt these words of entreaty the rabbis advanced upon him; and, seeing their intent, some of the disciples for whom he interceded drew nearer; one of them cut off a man’s ear, but without saving the Master from being taken. And yet Ben-Hur stood still! Nay, while the officers were making ready with their ropes the Nazarene was doing his greatest charity—not the greatest in deed, but the very greatest in illustration of his forbearance, so far surpassing that of men.\n\n“Suffer ye thus far,” he said to the wounded man, and healed him with a touch.\n\nBoth friends and enemies were confounded—one side that he could do such a thing, the other that he would do it under the circumstances.\n\n“Surely he will not allow them to bind him!”\n\nThus thought Ben-Hur.\n\n“Put up thy sword into the sheath; the cup which my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?” From the offending follower, the Nazarene turned to his captors. “Are you come out as against a thief, with swords and staves to take me? I was daily with you in the Temple, and you took me not; but this is your hour, and the power of darkness.”\n\nThe posse plucked up courage and closed about him; and when Ben-Hur looked for the faithful they were gone—not one of them remained.\n\nThe crowd about the deserted man seemed very busy with tongue, hand, and foot. Over their heads, between the torch-sticks, through the smoke, sometimes in openings between the restless men, Ben-Hur caught momentary glimpses of the prisoner. Never had anything struck him as so piteous, so unfriended, so forsaken! Yet, he thought, the man could have defended himself—he could have slain his enemies with a breath, but he would not. What was the cup his father had given him to drink? And who was the father to be so obeyed? Mystery upon mystery—not one, but many.\n\nDirectly the mob started in return to the city, the soldiers in the lead. Ben-Hur became anxious; he was not satisfied with himself. Where the torches were in the midst of the rabble he knew the Nazarene was to be found. Suddenly he resolved to see him again. He would ask him one question.\n\nTaking off his long outer garment and the handkerchief from his head, he threw them upon the orchard wall, and started after the posse, which he boldly joined. Through the stragglers he made way, and by littles at length reached the man who carried the ends of the rope with which the prisoner was bound.\n\nThe Nazarene was walking slowly, his head down, his hands bound behind him; the hair fell thickly over his face, and he stooped more than usual; apparently he was oblivious to all going on around him. In advance a few steps were priests and elders talking and occasionally looking back. When, at length, they were all near the bridge in the gorge, Ben-Hur took the rope from the servant who had it, and stepped past him.\n\n“Master, master!” he said, hurriedly, speaking close to the Nazarene’s ear. “Dost thou hear, master? A word—one word. Tell me—”\n\nThe fellow from whom he had taken the rope now claimed it.\n\n“Tell me,” Ben-Hur continued, “goest thou with these of thine own accord?”\n\nThe people were come up now, and in his own ears asking angrily, “Who art thou, man?”\n\n“O master,” Ben-Hur made haste to say, his voice sharp with anxiety, “I am thy friend and lover. Tell me, I pray thee, if I bring rescue, wilt thou accept it?”\n\nThe Nazarene never so much as looked up or allowed the slightest sign of recognition; yet the something which when we are suffering is always telling it to such as look at us, though they be strangers, failed not now. “Let him alone,” it seemed to say; “he has been abandoned by his friends; the world has denied him; in bitterness of spirit, he has taken farewell of men; he is going he knows not where, and he cares not. Let him alone.”\n\nAnd to that Ben-Hur was now driven. A dozen hands were upon him, and from all sides there was shouting, “He is one of them. Bring him along; club him—kill him!”\n\nWith a gust of passion which gave him many times his ordinary force, Ben-Hur raised himself, turned once about with arms outstretched, shook the hands off, and rushed through the circle which was fast hemming him in. The hands snatching at him as he passed tore his garments from his back, so he ran off the road naked; and the gorge, in keeping of the friendly darkness, darker there than elsewhere, received him safe.\n\nReclaiming his handkerchief and outer garments from the orchard wall, he followed back to the city gate; thence he went to the khan, and on the good horse rode to the tents of his people out by the Tombs of the Kings.\n\nAs he rode, he promised himself to see the Nazarene on the morrow—promised it, not knowing that the unfriended man was taken straightway to the house of Hannas to be tried that night.\n\nThe heart the young man carried to his couch beat so heavily he could not sleep; for now clearly his renewed Judean kingdom resolved itself into what it was—only a dream. It is bad enough to see our castles overthrown one after another with an interval between in which to recover from the shock, or at least let the echoes of the fall die away; but when they go altogether—go as ships sink, as houses tumble in earthquakes—the spirits which endure it calmly are made of stuffs sterner than common, and Ben-Hur’s was not of them. Through vistas in the future, he began to catch glimpses of a life serenely beautiful, with a home instead of a palace of state, and Esther its mistress. Again and again through the leaden-footed hours of the night he saw the villa by Misenum, and with his little countrywoman strolled through the garden, and rested in the panelled atrium; overhead the Neapolitan sky, at their feet the sunniest of sun-lands and the bluest of bays.\n\nIn plainest speech, he was entering upon a crisis with which to-morrow and the Nazarene will have everything to do.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nNext morning, about the second hour, two men rode full speed to the doors of Ben-Hur’s tents, and dismounting, asked to see him. He was not yet risen, but gave directions for their admission.\n\n“Peace to you, brethren,” he said, for they were of his Galileans, and trusted officers. “Will you be seated?”\n\n“Nay,” the senior replied, bluntly, “to sit and be at ease is to let the Nazarene die. Rise, son of Judah, and go with us. The judgment has been given. The tree of the cross is already at Golgotha.”\n\nBen-Hur stared at them.\n\n“The cross!” was all he could for the moment say.\n\n“They took him last night, and tried him,” the man continued. “At dawn they led him before Pilate. Twice the Roman denied his guilt; twice he refused to give him over. At last he washed his hands, and said, ‘Be it upon you then;’ and they answered—”\n\n“Who answered?”\n\n“They—the priests and people—‘His blood be upon us and our children.’”\n\n“Holy father Abraham!” cried Ben-Hur; “a Roman kinder to an Israelite than his own kin! And if—ah, if he should indeed be the son of God, what shall ever wash his blood from their children? It must not be—’tis time to fight!”\n\nHis face brightened with resolution, and he clapped his hands.\n\n“The horses—and quickly!” he said to the Arab who answered the signal. “And bid Amrah send me fresh garments, and bring my sword! It is time to die for Israel, my friends. Tarry without till I come.”\n\nHe ate a crust, drank a cup of wine, and was soon upon the road.\n\n“Whither would you go first?” asked the Galilean.\n\n“To collect the legions.”\n\n“Alas!” the man replied, throwing up his hands.\n\n“Why alas?”\n\n“Master”—the man spoke with shame—“master, I and my friend here are all that are faithful. The rest do follow the priests.”\n\n“Seeking what?” and Ben-Hur drew rein.\n\n“To kill him.”\n\n“Not the Nazarene?”\n“You have said it.”\n\nBen-Hur looked slowly from one man to the other. He was hearing again the question of the night before: “The cup my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?” In the ear of the Nazarene he was putting his own question, “If I bring thee rescue, wilt thou accept it?” He was saying to himself, “This death may not be averted. The man has been travelling towards it with full knowledge from the day he began his mission: it is imposed by a will higher than his; whose but the Lord’s! If he is consenting, if he goes to it voluntarily, what shall another do?” Nor less did Ben-Hur see the failure of the scheme he had built upon the fidelity of the Galileans; their desertion, in fact, left nothing more of it. But how singular it should happen that morning of all others! A dread seized him. It was possible his scheming, and labor, and expenditure of treasure might have been but blasphemous contention with God. When he picked up the reins and said, “Let us go, brethren,” all before him was uncertainty. The faculty of resolving quickly, without which one cannot be a hero in the midst of stirring scenes, was numb within him.\n\n“Let us go, brethren; let us to Golgotha.”\n\nThey passed through excited crowds of people going south, like themselves. All the country north of the city seemed aroused and in motion.\n\nHearing that the procession with the condemned might be met with somewhere near the great white towers left by Herod, the three friends rode thither, passing round southeast of Akra. In the valley below the Pool of Hezekiah, passage-way against the multitude became impossible, and they were compelled to dismount, and take shelter behind the corner of a house and wait.\n\nThe waiting was as if they were on a river bank, watching a flood go by, for such the people seemed.\n\nThere are certain chapters in the First Book of this story which were written to give the reader an idea of the composition of the Jewish nationality as it was in the time of Christ. They were also written in anticipation of this hour and scene; so that he who has read them with attention can now see all Ben-Hur saw of the going to the crucifixion—a rare and wonderful sight!\n\nHalf an hour—an hour—the flood surged by Ben-Hur and his companions, within arm’s reach, incessant, undiminished. At the end of that time he could have said, “I have seen all the castes of Jerusalem, all the sects of Judea, all the tribes of Israel, and all the nationalities of earth represented by them.” The Libyan Jew went by, and the Jew of Egypt, and the Jew from the Rhine; in short, Jews from all East countries and all West countries, and all islands within commercial connection; they went by on foot, on horseback, on camels, in litters and chariots, and with an infinite variety of costumes, yet with the same marvellous similitude of features which to-day particularizes the children of Israel, tried as they have been by climates and modes of life; they went by speaking all known tongues, for by that means only were they distinguishable group from group; they went by in haste—eager, anxious, crowding—all to behold one poor Nazarene die, a felon between felons.\n\nThese were the many, but they were not all.\n\nBorne along with the stream were thousands not Jews—thousands hating and despising them—Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Syrians, Africans, Egyptians, Easterns. So that, studying the mass, it seemed the whole world was to be represented, and, in that sense, present at the crucifixion.\n\nThe going was singularly quiet. A hoof-stroke upon a rock, the glide and rattle of revolving wheels, voices in conversation, and now and then a calling voice, were all the sounds heard above the rustle of the mighty movement. Yet was there upon every countenance the look with which men make haste to see some dreadful sight, some sudden wreck, or ruin, or calamity of war. And by such signs Ben-Hur judged that these were the strangers in the city come up to the Passover, who had had no part in the trial of the Nazarene, and might be his friends.\n\nAt length, from the direction of the great towers, Ben-Hur heard, at first faint in the distance, a shouting of many men.\n\n“Hark! they are coming now,” said one of his friends.\n\nThe people in the street halted to hear; but as the cry rang on over their heads, they looked at each other, and in shuddering silence moved along.\n\nThe shouting drew nearer each moment; and the air was already full of it and trembling, when Ben-Hur saw the servants of Simonides coming with their master in his chair, and Esther walking by his side; a covered litter was next behind them.\n\n“Peace to you, O Simonides—and to you, Esther,” said Ben-Hur, meeting them. “If you are for Golgotha, stay until the procession passes; I will then go with you. There is room to turn in by the house here.”\n\nThe merchant’s large head rested heavily upon his breast; rousing himself, he answered, “Speak to Balthasar; his pleasure will be mine. He is in the litter.”\n\nBen-Hur hastened to draw aside the curtain. The Egyptian was lying within, his wan face so pinched as to appear like a dead man’s. The proposal was submitted to him.\n\n“Can we see him?” he inquired, faintly.\n\n“The Nazarene? yes; he must pass within a few feet of us.”\n\n“Dear Lord!” the old man cried, fervently. “Once more, once more! Oh, it is a dreadful day for the world!”\n\nShortly the whole party were in waiting under shelter of the house. They said but little, afraid, probably, to trust their thoughts to each other; everything was uncertain, and nothing so much so as opinions. Balthasar drew himself feebly from the litter, and stood supported by a servant; Esther and Ben-Hur kept Simonides company.\n\nMeantime the flood poured along, if anything, more densely than before; and the shouting came nearer, shrill up in the air, hoarse along the earth, and cruel. At last the procession was up.\n\n“See!” said Ben-Hur, bitterly; “that which cometh now is Jerusalem.”\n\nThe advance was in possession of an army of boys, hooting and screaming, “The King of the Jews! Room, room for the King of the Jews!”\n\nSimonides watched them as they whirled and danced along, like a cloud of summer insects, and said, gravely, “When these come to their inheritance, son of Hur, alas for the city of Solomon!”\n\nA band of legionaries fully armed followed next, marching in sturdy indifference, the glory of burnished brass about them the while.\n\nThen came the NAZARENE!\n\nHe was nearly dead. Every few steps he staggered as if he would fall. A stained gown badly torn hung from his shoulders over a seamless undertunic. His bare feet left red splotches upon the stones. An inscription on a board was tied to his neck. A crown of thorns had been crushed hard down upon his head, making cruel wounds from which streams of blood, now dry and blackened, had run over his face and neck. The long hair, tangled in the thorns, was clotted thick. The skin, where it could be seen, was ghastly white. His hands were tied before him. Back somewhere in the city he had fallen exhausted under the transverse beam of his cross, which, as a condemned person, custom required him to bear to the place of execution; now a countryman carried the burden in his stead. Four soldiers went with him as a guard against the mob, who sometimes, nevertheless, broke through, and struck him with sticks, and spit upon him. Yet no sound escaped him, neither remonstrance nor groan; nor did he look up until he was nearly in front of the house sheltering Ben-Hur and his friends, all of whom were moved with quick compassion. Esther clung to her father; and he, strong of will as he was, trembled. Balthasar fell down speechless. Even Ben-Hur cried out, “O my God! my God!” Then, as if he divined their feelings or heard the exclamation, the Nazarene turned his wan face towards the party, and looked at them each one, so they carried the look in memory through life. They could see he was thinking of them, not himself, and the dying eyes gave them the blessing he was not permitted to speak.\n\n“Where are thy legions, son of Hur?” asked Simonides, aroused.\n\n“Hannas can tell thee better than I.”\n\n“What, faithless?”\n\n“All but these two.”\n“Then all is lost, and this good man must die!”\n\nThe face of the merchant knit convulsively as he spoke, and his head sank upon his breast. He had borne his part in Ben-Hur’s labors well, and he had been inspired by the same hopes, now blown out never to be rekindled.\n\nTwo other men succeeded the Nazarene bearing cross-beams.\n\n“Who are these?” Ben-Hur asked of the Galileans.\n\n“Thieves appointed to die with the Nazarene,” they replied.\n\nNext in the procession stalked a mitred figure clad all in the golden vestments of the high-priest. Policemen from the Temple curtained him round about; and after him, in order, strode the sanhedrim, and a long array of priests, the latter in their plain white garments, overwrapped by abnets of many folds and gorgeous colors.\n\n“The son-in-law of Hannas,” said Ben-Hur, in a low voice.\n\n“Caiaphas! I have seen him,” Simonides replied, adding, after a pause during which he thoughtfully watched the haughty pontiff, “And now am I convinced. With such assurance as proceeds from clear enlightenment of the spirit—with absolute assurance—now know I that he who first goes yonder with the inscription about his neck is what the inscription proclaims him—KING OF THE JEWS. A common man, an impostor, a felon, was never thus waited upon. For look! Here are the nations—Jerusalem, Israel. Here is the ephod, here the blue robe with its fringe, and purple pomegranates, and golden bells, not seen in the street since the day Jaddua went out to meet the Macedonian—proofs all that this Nazarene is King. Would I could rise and go after him!”\n\nBen-Hur listened surprised; and directly, as if himself awakening to his unusual display of feeling, Simonides said, impatiently,\n\n“Speak to Balthasar, I pray you, and let us begone. The vomit of Jerusalem is coming.”\n\nThen Esther spoke.\n\n“I see some women there, and they are weeping. Who are they?”\n\nFollowing the pointing of her hand, the party beheld four women in tears; one of them leaned upon the arm of a man of aspect not unlike the Nazarene’s. Presently Ben-Hur answered,\n\n“The man is the disciple whom the Nazarene loves the best of all; she who leans upon his arm is Mary, the Master’s mother; the others are friendly women of Galilee.”\n\nEsther pursued the mourners with glistening eyes until the multitude received them out of sight.\n\nIt may be the reader will fancy the foregoing snatches of conversation were had in quiet; but it was not so. The talking was, for the most part, like that indulged by people at the seaside under the sound of the surf; for to nothing else can the clamor of this division of the mob be so well likened.\n\nThe demonstration was the forerunner of those in which, scarce thirty years later, under rule of the factions, the Holy City was torn to pieces; it was quite as great in numbers, as fanatical and bloodthirsty; boiled and raved, and had in it exactly the same elements—servants, camel-drivers, marketmen, gate-keepers, gardeners, dealers in fruits and wines, proselytes, and foreigners not proselytes, watchmen and menials from the Temple, thieves, robbers, and the myriad not assignable to any class, but who, on such occasions as this, appeared no one could say whence, hungry and smelling of caves and old tombs—bareheaded wretches with naked arms and legs, hair and beard in uncombed mats, and each with one garment the color of clay; beasts with abysmal mouths, in outcry effective as lions calling each other across desert spaces. Some of them had swords; a greater number flourished spears and javelins; though the weapons of the many were staves and knotted clubs, and slings, for which latter selected stones were stored in scrips, and sometimes in sacks improvised from the foreskirts of their dirty tunics. Among the mass here and there appeared persons of high degree—scribes, elders, rabbis, Pharisees with broad fringing, Sadducees in fine cloaks—serving for the time as prompters and directors. If a throat tired of one cry, they invented another for it; if brassy lungs showed signs of collapse, they set them going again; and yet the clamor, loud and continuous as it was, could have been reduced to a few syllables—King of the Jews! Room for the King of the Jews!—Defiler of the Temple!—Blasphemer of God!—Crucify him, crucify him! And of these cries the last one seemed in greatest favor, because, doubtless, it was more directly expressive of the wish of the mob, and helped to better articulate its hatred of the Nazarene.\n\n“Come,” said Simonides, when Balthasar was ready to proceed—“come, let us forward.”\n\nBen-Hur did not hear the call. The appearance of the part of the procession then passing, its brutality and hunger for life, were reminding him of the Nazarene—his gentleness, and the many charities he had seen him do for suffering men. Suggestions beget suggestions; so he remembered suddenly his own great indebtedness to the man; the time he himself was in the hands of a Roman guard going, as was supposed, to a death as certain and almost as terrible as this one of the cross; the cooling drink he had at the well by Nazareth, and the divine expression of the face of him who gave it; the later goodness, the miracle of Palm-Sunday; and with these recollections, the thought of his present powerlessness to give back help for help or make return in kind stung him keenly, and he accused himself. He had not done all he might; he could have watched with the Galileans, and kept them true and ready; and this—ah! this was the moment to strike! A blow well given now would not merely disperse the mob and set the Nazarene free; it would be a trumpet-call to Israel, and precipitate the long-dreamt-of war for freedom. The opportunity was going; the minutes were bearing it away; and if lost! God of Abraham! Was there nothing to be done—nothing?\n\nThat instant a party of Galileans caught his eye. He rushed through the press and overtook them.\n\n“Follow me,” he said. “I would have speech with you.”\n\nThe men obeyed him, and when they were under shelter of the house, he spoke again:\n\n“You are of those who took my swords, and agreed with me to strike for freedom and the King who was coming. You have the swords now, and now is the time to strike with them. Go, look everywhere, and find our brethren, and tell them to meet me at the tree of the cross making ready for the Nazarene. Haste all of you! Nay, stand not so! The Nazarene is the King, and freedom dies with him.”\n\nThey looked at him respectfully, but did not move.\n\n“Hear you?” he asked.\n\nThen one of them replied,\n\n“Son of Judah”—by that name they knew him—“son of Judah, it is you who are deceived, not we or our brethren who have your swords. The Nazarene is not the King; neither has he the spirit of a king. We were with him when he came into Jerusalem; we saw him in the Temple; he failed himself, and us, and Israel; at the Gate Beautiful he turned his back upon God and refused the throne of David. He is not King, and Galilee is not with him. He shall die the death. But hear you, son of Judah. We have your swords, and we are ready now to draw them and strike for freedom; and so is Galilee. Be it for freedom, O son of Judah, for freedom! and we will meet you at the tree of the cross.”\n\nThe sovereign moment of his life was upon Ben-Hur. Could he have taken the offer and said the word, history might have been other than it is; but then it would have been history ordered by men, not God—something that never was, and never will be. A confusion fell upon him; he knew not how, though afterwards he attributed it to the Nazarene; for when the Nazarene was risen, he understood the death was necessary to faith in the resurrection, without which Christianity would be an empty husk. The confusion, as has been said, left him without the faculty of decision; he stood helpless—wordless even. Covering his face with his hand, he shook with the conflict between his wish, which was what he would have ordered, and the power that was upon him.\n\n“Come; we are waiting for you,” said Simonides, the fourth time.\n\nThereupon he walked mechanically after the chair and the litter. Esther walked with him. Like Balthasar and his friends, the Wise Men, the day they went to the meeting in the desert, he was being led along the way.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\nWhen the party—Balthasar, Simonides, Ben-Hur, Esther, and the two faithful Galileans—reached the place of crucifixion, Ben-Hur was in advance leading them. How they had been able to make way through the great press of excited people, he never knew; no more did he know the road by which they came or the time it took them to come. He had walked in total unconsciousness, neither hearing nor seeing anybody or anything, and without a thought of where he was going, or the ghostliest semblance of a purpose in his mind. In such condition a little child could have done as much as he to prevent the awful crime he was about to witness. The intentions of God are always strange to us; but not more so than the means by which they are wrought out, and at last made plain to our belief.\n\nBen-Hur came to a stop; those following him also stopped. As a curtain rises before an audience, the spell holding him in its sleep-awake rose, and he saw with a clear understanding.\n\nThere was a space upon the top of a low knoll rounded like a skull, and dry, dusty, and without vegetation, except some scrubby hyssop. The boundary of the space was a living wall of men, with men behind struggling, some to look over, others to look through it. An inner wall of Roman soldiery held the dense outer wall rigidly to its place. A centurion kept eye upon the soldiers. Up to the very line so vigilantly guarded Ben-Hur had been led; at the line he now stood, his face to the northwest. The knoll was the old Aramaic Golgotha—in Latin, Calvaria; anglicized, Calvary; translated, The Skull.\n\nOn its slopes, in the low places, on the swells and higher hills, the earth sparkled with a strange enamelling. Look where he would outside the walled space, he saw no patch of brown soil, no rock, no green thing; he saw only thousands of eyes in ruddy faces; off a little way in the perspective only ruddy faces without eyes; off a little farther only a broad, broad circle, which the nearer view instructed him was also of faces. And this was the ensemble of three millions of people; under it three millions of hearts throbbing with passionate interest in what was taking place upon the knoll; indifferent as to the thieves, caring only for the Nazarene, and for him only as he was an object of hate or fear or curiosity—he who loved them all, and was about to die for them.\n\nIn the spectacle of a great assemblage of people there are always the bewilderment and fascination one feels while looking over a stretch of sea in agitation, and never had this one been exceeded; yet Ben-Hur gave it but a passing glance, for that which was going on in the space described would permit no division of his interest.\n\nUp on the knoll so high as to be above the living wall, and visible over the heads of an attending company of notables, conspicuous because of his mitre and vestments and his haughty air, stood the high priest. Up the knoll still higher, up quite to the round summit, so as to be seen far and near, was the Nazarene, stooped and suffering, but silent. The wit among the guard had complemented the crown upon his head by putting a reed in his hand for a sceptre. Clamors blew upon him like blasts—laughter—execrations—sometimes both together indistinguishably. A man—ONLY a man, O reader, would have charged the blasts with the remainder of his love for the race, and let it go forever.\n\nAll the eyes then looking were fixed upon the Nazarene. It may have been pity with which he was moved; whatever the cause, Ben-Hur was conscious of a change in his feelings. A conception of something better than the best of this life—something so much better that it could serve a weak man with strength to endure agonies of spirit as well as of body; something to make death welcome—perhaps another life purer than this one—perhaps the spirit-life which Balthasar held to so fast, began to dawn upon his mind clearer and clearer, bringing to him a certain sense that, after all, the mission of the Nazarene was that of guide across the boundary for such as loved him; across the boundary to where his kingdom was set up and waiting for him. Then, as something borne through the air out of the almost forgotten, he heard again, or seemed to hear, the saying of the Nazarene,\n\nAnd the words repeated themselves over and over, and took form, and the dawn touched them with its light, and filled them with a new meaning. And as men repeat a question to grasp and fix the meaning, he asked, gazing at the figure on the hill fainting under its crown, Who the Resurrection? and who the Life?\n\nthe figure seemed to say—and say it for him; for instantly he was sensible of a peace such as he had never known—the peace which is the end of doubt and mystery, and the beginning of faith and love and clear understanding.\n\nFrom this dreamy state Ben-Hur was aroused by the sound of hammering. On the summit of the knoll he observed then what had escaped him before—some soldiers and workmen preparing the crosses. The holes for planting the trees were ready, and now the transverse beams were being fitted to their places.\n\n“Bid the men make haste,” said the high-priest to the centurion. “These”—and he pointed to the Nazarene—“must be dead by the going-down of the sun, and buried that the land may not be defiled. Such is the Law.”\n\nWith a better mind, a soldier went to the Nazarene and offered him something to drink, but he refused the cup. Then another went to him and took from his neck the board with the inscription upon it, which he nailed to the tree of the cross—and the preparation was complete.\n\n“The crosses are ready,” said the centurion to the pontiff, who received the report with a wave of the hand and the reply,\n\n“Let the blasphemer go first. The Son of God should be able to save himself. We will see.”\n\nThe people to whom the preparation in its several stages was visible, and who to this time had assailed the hill with incessant cries of impatience, permitted a lull which directly became a universal hush. The part of the infliction most shocking, at least to the thought, was reached—the men were to be nailed to their crosses. When for that purpose the soldiers laid their hands upon the Nazarene first, a shudder passed through the great concourse; the most brutalized shrank with dread. Afterwards there were those who said the air suddenly chilled and made them shiver.\n\n“How very still it is!” Esther said, as she put her arm about her father’s neck.\n\nAnd remembering the torture he himself had suffered, he drew her face down upon his breast, and sat trembling.\n\n“Avoid it, Esther, avoid it!” he said. “I know not but all who stand and see it—the innocent as well as the guilty—may be cursed from this hour.”\n\nBalthasar sank upon his knees.\n\n“Son of Hur,” said Simonides, with increasing excitement—“son of Hur, if Jehovah stretch not forth his hand, and quickly, Israel is lost—and we are lost.”\n\nBen-Hur answered, calmly, “I have been in a dream, Simonides, and heard in it why all this should be, and why it should go on. It is the will of the Nazarene—it is God’s will. Let us do as the Egyptian here—let us hold our peace and pray.”\n\nAs he looked up on the knoll again, the words were wafted to him through the awful stillness—\n\nHe bowed reverently as to a person speaking.\n\nUp on the summit meantime the work went on. The guard took the Nazarene’s clothes from him; so that he stood before the millions naked. The stripes of the scourging he had received in the early morning were still bloody upon his back; yet he was laid pitilessly down, and stretched upon the cross—first, the arms upon the transverse beam; the spikes were sharp—a few blows, and they were driven through the tender palms; next, they drew his knees up until the soles of the feet rested flat upon the tree; then they placed one foot upon the other, and one spike fixed both of them fast. The dulled sound of the hammering was heard outside the guarded space; and such as could not hear, yet saw the hammer as it fell, shivered with fear. And withal not a groan, or cry, or word of remonstrance from the sufferer: nothing at which an enemy could laugh; nothing a lover could regret.\n\n“Which way wilt thou have him faced?” asked a soldier, bluntly.\n\n“Towards the Temple,” the pontiff replied. “In dying I would have him see the holy house hath not suffered by him.”\n\nThe workmen put their hands to the cross, and carried it, burden and all, to the place of planting. At a word, they dropped the tree into the hole; and the body of the Nazarene also dropped heavily, and hung by the bleeding hands. Still no cry of pain—only the exclamation divinest of all recorded exclamations,\n\n“Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do.”\n\nThe cross, reared now above all other objects, and standing singly out against the sky, was greeted with a burst of delight; and all who could see and read the writing upon the board over the Nazarene’s head made haste to decipher it. Soon as read, the legend was adopted by them and communicated, and presently the whole mighty concourse was ringing the salutation from side to side, and repeating it with laughter and groans,\n\n“King of the Jews! Hail, King of the Jews!”\n\nThe pontiff, with a clearer idea of the import of the inscription, protested against it, but in vain; so the titled King, looking from the knoll with dying eyes, must have had the city of his fathers at rest below him—she who had so ignominiously cast him out.\n\nThe sun was rising rapidly to noon; the hills bared their brown breasts lovingly to it; the more distant mountains rejoiced in the purple with which it so regally dressed them. In the city, the temples, palaces, towers, pinnacles, and all points of beauty and prominence seemed to lift themselves into the unrivalled brilliance, as if they knew the pride they were giving the many who from time to time turned to look at them. Suddenly a dimness began to fill the sky and cover the earth—at first no more than a scarce perceptible fading of the day; a twilight out of time; an evening gliding in upon the splendors of noon. But it deepened, and directly drew attention; whereat the noise of the shouting and laughter fell off, and men, doubting their senses, gazed at each other curiously: then they looked to the sun again; then at the mountains, getting farther away; at the sky and the near landscape, sinking in shadow; at the hill upon which the tragedy was enacting; and from all these they gazed at each other again, and turned pale, and held their peace.\n\n“It is only a mist or passing cloud,” Simonides said soothingly to Esther, who was alarmed. “It will brighten presently.”\n\nBen-Hur did not think so.\n\n“It is not a mist or a cloud,” he said. “The spirits who live in the air—the prophets and saints—are at work in mercy to themselves and nature. I say to you, O Simonides, truly as God lives, he who hangs yonder is the Son of God.”\n\nAnd leaving Simonides lost in wonder at such a speech from him, he went where Balthasar was kneeling near by, and laid his hand upon the good man’s shoulder.\n\n“O wise Egyptian, hearken! Thou alone wert right—the Nazarene is indeed the Son of God.”\n\nBalthasar drew him down to him, and replied, feebly, “I saw him a child in the manger where he was first laid; it is not strange that I knew him sooner than thou; but oh that I should live to see this day! Would I had died with my brethren! Happy Melchior! Happy, happy Gaspar!”\n\n“Comfort thee!” said Ben-Hur. “Doubtless they too are here.”\n\nThe dimness went on deepening into obscurity, and that into positive darkness, but without deterring the bolder spirits upon the knoll. One after the other the thieves were raised on their crosses, and the crosses planted. The guard was then withdrawn, and the people set free closed in upon the height, and surged up it, like a converging wave. A man might take a look, when a new-comer would push him on, and take his place, to be in turn pushed on—and there were laughter and ribaldry and revilements, all for the Nazarene.\n\n“Ha, ha! If thou be King of the Jews, save thyself,” a soldier shouted.\n\n“Ay,” said a priest, “if he will come down to us now, we will believe in him.”\n\nOthers wagged their heads wisely, saying, “He would destroy the Temple, and rebuild it in three days, but cannot save himself.”\n\nOthers still: “He called himself the Son of God; let us see if God will have him.”\n\nWhat all there is in prejudice no one has ever said. The Nazarene had never harmed the people; far the greater part of them had never seen him except in this his hour of calamity; yet—singular contrariety!—they loaded him with their curses, and gave their sympathy to the thieves.\n\nThe supernatural night, dropped thus from the heavens, affected Esther as it began to affect thousands of others braver and stronger.\n\n“Let us go home,” she prayed—twice, three times—saying, “It is the frown of God, father. What other dreadful things may happen, who can tell? I am afraid.”\n\nSimonides was obstinate. He said little, but was plainly under great excitement. Observing, about the end of the first hour, that the violence of the crowding up on the knoll was somewhat abated, at his suggestion the party advanced to take position nearer the crosses. Ben-Hur gave his arm to Balthasar; yet the Egyptian made the ascent with difficulty. From their new stand, the Nazarene was imperfectly visible, appearing to them not more than a dark suspended figure. They could hear him, however—hear his sighing, which showed an endurance or exhaustion greater than that of his fellow-sufferers; for they filled every lull in the noises with their groans and entreaties.\n\nThe second hour after the suspension passed like the first one. To the Nazarene they were hours of insult, provocation, and slow dying. He spoke but once in the time. Some women came and knelt at the foot of his cross. Among them he recognized his mother with the beloved disciple.\n\n“Woman,” he said, raising his voice, “behold thy son!” And to the disciple, “Behold thy mother!”\n\nThe third hour came, and still the people surged round the hill, held to it by some strange attraction, with which, in probability, the night in midday had much to do. They were quieter than in the preceding hour; yet at intervals they could be heard off in the darkness shouting to each other, multitude calling unto multitude. It was noticeable, also, that coming now to the Nazarene, they approached his cross in silence, took the look in silence, and so departed. This change extended even to the guard, who so shortly before had cast lots for the clothes of the crucified; they stood with their officers a little apart, more watchful of the one convict than of the throngs coming and going. If he but breathed heavily, or tossed his head in a paroxysm of pain, they were instantly on the alert. Most marvellous of all, however, was the altered behavior of the high-priest and his following, the wise men who had assisted him in the trial in the night, and, in the victim’s face, kept place by him with zealous approval. When the darkness began to fall, they began to lose their confidence. There were among them many learned in astronomy, and familiar with the apparitions so terrible in those days to the masses; much of the knowledge was descended to them from their fathers far back; some of it had been brought away at the end of the Captivity; and the necessities of the Temple service kept it all bright. These closed together when the sun commenced to fade before their eyes, and the mountains and hills to recede; they drew together in a group around their pontiff, and debated what they saw. “The moon is at its full,” they said, with truth, “and this cannot be an eclipse.” Then, as no one could answer the question common with them all—as no one could account for the darkness, or for its occurrence at that particular time, in their secret hearts they associated it with the Nazarene, and yielded to an alarm which the long continuance of the phenomenon steadily increased. In their place behind the soldiers, they noted every word and motion of the Nazarene, and hung with fear upon his sighs, and talked in whispers. The man might be the Messiah, and then— But they would wait and see!\n\nIn the meantime Ben-Hur was not once visited by the old spirit. The perfect peace abode with him. He prayed simply that the end might be hastened. He knew the condition of Simonides’ mind—that he was hesitating on the verge of belief. He could see the massive face weighed down by solemn reflection. He noticed him casting inquiring glances at the sun, as seeking the cause of the darkness. Nor did he fail to notice the solicitude with which Esther clung to him, smothering her fears to accommodate his wishes.\n\n“Be not afraid,” he heard him say to her; “but stay and watch with me. Thou mayst live twice the span of my life, and see nothing of human interest equal to this; and there may be revelations more. Let us stay to the close.”\n\nWhen the third hour was about half gone, some men of the rudest class—wretches from the tombs about the city—came and stopped in front of the centre cross.\n\n“This is he, the new King of the Jews,” said one of them.\n\nThe others cried, with laughter, “Hail, all hail, King of the Jews!”\n\nReceiving no reply, they went closer.\n\n“If thou be King of the Jews, or Son of God, come down,” they said, loudly.\n\nAt this, one of the thieves quit groaning, and called to the Nazarene, “Yes, if thou be Christ, save thyself and us.”\n\nThe people laughed and applauded; then, while they were listening for a reply, the other felon was heard to say to the first one, “Dost thou not fear God? We receive the due rewards of our deeds; but this man hath done nothing amiss.”\n\nThe bystanders were astonished; in the midst of the hush which ensued, the second felon spoke again, but this time to the Nazarene:\n\n“Lord,” he said, “remember me when thou comest into thy kingdom.”\n\nSimonides gave a great start. “When thou comest into thy kingdom!” It was the very point of doubt in his mind; the point he had so often debated with Balthasar.\n\n“Didst thou hear?” said Ben-Hur to him. “The kingdom cannot be of this world. Yon witness saith the King is but going to his kingdom; and, in effect, I heard the same in my dream.”\n\n“Hush!” said Simonides, more imperiously than ever before in speech to Ben-Hur. “Hush, I pray thee! If the Nazarene should answer—”\n\nAnd as he spoke the Nazarene did answer, in a clear voice, full of confidence:\n\n“Verily I say unto thee, To-day shalt thou be with me in Paradise!”\n\nSimonides waited to hear if that were all; then he folded his hands and said, “No more, no more, Lord! The darkness is gone; I see with other eyes—even as Balthasar, I see with eyes of perfect faith.”\n\nThe faithful servant had at last his fitting reward. His broken body might never be restored; nor was there riddance of the recollection of his sufferings, or recall of the years embittered by them; but suddenly a new life was shown him, with assurance that it was for him—a new life lying just beyond this one—and its name was Paradise. There he would find the Kingdom of which he had been dreaming, and the King. A perfect peace fell upon him.\n\nOver the way, in front of the cross, however, there were surprise and consternation. The cunning casuists there put the assumption underlying the question and the admission underlying the answer together. For saying through the land that he was the Messiah, they had brought the Nazarene to the cross; and, lo! on the cross, more confidently than ever, he had not only reasserted himself, but promised enjoyment of his Paradise to a malefactor. They trembled at what they were doing. The pontiff, with all his pride, was afraid. Where got the man his confidence except from Truth? And what should the Truth be but God? A very little now would put them all to flight.\n\nThe breathing of the Nazarene grew harder, his sighs became great gasps. Only three hours upon the cross, and he was dying!\n\nThe intelligence was carried from man to man, until every one knew it; and then everything hushed; the breeze faltered and died; a stifling vapor loaded the air; heat was superadded to darkness; nor might any one unknowing the fact have thought that off the hill, out under the overhanging pall, there were three millions of people waiting awe-struck what should happen next—they were so still!\n\nThen there went out through the gloom, over the heads of such as were on the hill within hearing of the dying man, a cry of despair, if not reproach:\n\n“My God! my God! why hast thou forsaken me?”\n\nThe voice startled all who heard it. One it touched uncontrollably.\n\nThe soldiers in coming had brought with them a vessel of wine and water, and set it down a little way from Ben-Hur. With a sponge dipped into the liquor, and put on the end of a stick, they could moisten the tongue of a sufferer at their pleasure. Ben-Hur thought of the draught he had had at the well near Nazareth; an impulse seized him; catching up the sponge, he dipped it into the vessel, and started for the cross.\n\n“Let him be!” the people in the way shouted, angrily. “Let him be!”\n\nWithout minding them, he ran on, and put the sponge to the Nazarene’s lips.\n\nToo late, too late!\n\nThe face then plainly seen by Ben-Hur, bruised and black with blood and dust as it was, lighted nevertheless with a sudden glow; the eyes opened wide, and fixed upon some one visible to them alone in the far heavens; and there were content and relief, even triumph, in the shout the victim gave.\n\n“It is finished! It is finished!”\n\nSo a hero, dying in the doing a great deed, celebrates his success with a last cheer.\n\nThe light in the eyes went out; slowly the crowned head sank upon the laboring breast. Ben-Hur thought the struggle over; but the fainting soul recollected itself, so that he and those around him caught the other and last words, spoken in a low voice, as if to one listening close by:\n\n“Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit.”\n\nA tremor shook the tortured body; there was a scream of fiercest anguish, and the mission and the earthly life were over at once. The heart, with all its love, was broken; for of that, O reader, the man died!\n\nBen-Hur went back to his friends, saying, simply, “It is over; he is dead.”\n\nIn a space incredibly short the multitude was informed of the circumstance. No one repeated it aloud; there was a murmur which spread from the knoll in every direction; a murmur that was little more than a whispering, “He is dead! he is dead!” and that was all. The people had their wish; the Nazarene was dead; yet they stared at each other aghast. His blood was upon them! And while they stood staring at each other, the ground commenced to shake; each man took hold of his neighbor to support himself; in a twinkling the darkness disappeared, and the sun came out; and everybody, as with the same glance, beheld the crosses upon the hill all reeling drunken-like in the earthquake. They beheld all three of them; but the one in the centre was arbitrary; it alone would be seen; and for that it seemed to extend itself upwards, and lift its burden, and swing it to and fro higher and higher in the blue of the sky. And every man among them who had jeered at the Nazarene; every one who had struck him; every one who had voted to crucify him; every one who had marched in the procession from the city; every one who had in his heart wished him dead, and they were as ten to one, felt that he was in some way individually singled out from the many, and that if he would live he must get away quickly as possible from that menace in the sky. They started to run; they ran with all their might; on horseback, and camels, and in chariots they ran, as well as on foot; but then as if it were mad at them for what they had done, and had taken up the cause of the unoffending and friendless dead, the earthquake pursued them, and tossed them about, and flung them down, and terrified them yet more by the horrible noise of great rocks grinding and rending beneath them. They beat their breasts and shrieked with fear. His blood was upon them! The home-bred and the foreign, priest and layman, beggar, Sadducee, Pharisee, were overtaken in the race, and tumbled about indiscriminately. If they called on the Lord, the outraged earth answered for him in fury, and dealt them all alike. It did not even know wherein the high-priest was better than his guilty brethren; overtaking him, it tripped him up also, and smirched the fringing of his robe, and filled the golden bells with sand, and his mouth with dust. He and his people were alike in the one thing at least—the blood of the Nazarene was upon them all!\n\nWhen the sunlight broke upon the crucifixion, the mother of the Nazarene, the disciple, and the faithful women of Galilee, the centurion and his soldiers, and Ben-Hur and his party, were all who remained upon the hill. These had not time to observe the flight of the multitude; they were too loudly called upon to take care of themselves.\n\n“Seat thyself here,” said Ben-Hur to Esther, making a place for her at her father’s feet. “Now cover thine eyes and look not up; but put thy trust in God, and the spirit of yon just man so foully slain.”\n\n“Nay,” said Simonides, reverently, “let us henceforth speak of him as the Christ.”\n\n“Be it so,” said Ben-Hur.\n\nPresently a wave of the earthquake struck the hill. The shrieks of the thieves upon the reeling crosses were terrible to hear. Though giddy with the movements of the ground, Ben-Hur had time to look at Balthasar, and beheld him prostrate and still. He ran to him and called—there was no reply. The good man was dead! Then Ben-Hur remembered to have heard a cry in answer, as it were, to the scream of the Nazarene in his last moment; but he had not looked to see from whom it had proceeded; and ever after he believed the spirit of the Egyptian accompanied that of his Master over the boundary into the kingdom of Paradise. The idea rested not only upon the cry heard, but upon the exceeding fitness of the distinction. If faith were worthy reward in the person of Gaspar, and love in that of Melchior, surely he should have some special meed who through a long life and so excellently illustrated the three virtues in combination—Faith, Love, and Good Works.\n\nThe servants of Balthasar had deserted their master; but when all was over, the two Galileans bore the old man in his litter back to the city.\n\nIt was a sorrowful procession that entered the south gate of the palace of the Hurs about the set of sun that memorable day. About the same hour the body of the Christ was taken down from the cross.\n\nThe remains of Balthasar were carried to the guest-chamber. All the servants hastened weeping to see him; for he had the love of every living thing with which he had in anywise to do; but when they beheld his face, and the smile upon it, they dried their tears, saying, “It is well. He is happier this evening than when he went out in the morning.”\n\nBen-Hur would not trust a servant to inform Iras what had befallen her father. He went himself to see her and bring her to the body. He imagined her grief; she would now be alone in the world; it was a time to forgive and pity her. He remembered he had not asked why she was not of the party in the morning, or where she was; he remembered he had not thought of her; and, from shame, he was ready to make any amends, the more so as he was about to plunge her into such acute grief.\n\nHe shook the curtains of her door; and though he heard the ringing of the little bells echoing within, he had no response; he called her name, and again he called—still no answer. He drew the curtain aside and went into the room; she was not there. He ascended hastily to the roof in search of her; nor was she there. He questioned the servants; none of them had seen her during the day. After a long quest everywhere through the house, Ben-Hur returned to the guest-chamber, and took the place by the dead which should have been hers; and he bethought him there how merciful the Christ had been to his aged servant. At the gate of the kingdom of Paradise happily the afflictions of this life, even its desertions, are left behind and forgotten by those who go in and rest.\n\nWhen the gloom of the burial was nigh gone, on the ninth day after the healing, the law being fulfilled, Ben-Hur brought his mother and Tirzah home; and from that day, in that house the most sacred names possible of utterance by men were always coupled worshipfully together,\n\n————\nAbout five years after the crucifixion, Esther, the wife of Ben-Hur, sat in her room in the beautiful villa by Misenum. It was noon, with a warm Italian sun making summer for the roses and vines outside. Everything in the apartment was Roman, except that Esther wore the garments of a Jewish matron. Tirzah and two children at play upon a lion skin on the floor were her companions; and one had only to observe how carefully she watched them to know that the little ones were hers.\n\nTime had treated her generously. She was more than ever beautiful, and in becoming mistress of the villa, she had realized one of her cherished dreams.\n\nIn the midst of this simple, home-like scene, a servant appeared in the doorway, and spoke to her.\n\n“A woman in the atrium to speak with the mistress.”\n\n“Let her come. I will receive her here.”\n\nPresently the stranger entered. At sight of her the Jewess arose, and was about to speak; then she hesitated, changed color, and finally drew back, saying, “I have known you, good woman. You are—”\n\n“I was Iras, the daughter of Balthasar.”\n\nEsther conquered her surprise, and bade the servant bring the Egyptian a seat.\n\n“No,” said Iras, coldly. “I will retire directly.”\n\nThe two gazed at each other. We know what Esther presented—a beautiful woman, a happy mother, a contented wife. On the other side, it was very plain that fortune had not dealt so gently with her former rival. The tall figure remained with some of its grace; but an evil life had tainted the whole person. The face was coarse; the large eyes were red and pursed beneath the lower lids; there was no color in her cheeks. The lips were cynical and hard, and general neglect was leading rapidly to premature old age. Her attire was ill chosen and draggled. The mud of the road clung to her sandals. Iras broke the painful silence.\n\n“These are thy children?”\n\nEsther looked at them, and smiled.\n\n“Yes. Will you not speak to them?”\n\n“I would scare them,” Iras replied. Then she drew closer to Esther, and seeing her shrink, said, “Be not afraid. Give thy husband a message for me. Tell him his enemy is dead, and that for the much misery he brought me I slew him.”\n\n“His enemy!”\n\n“The Messala. Further, tell thy husband that for the harm I sought to do him I have been punished until even he would pity me.”\n\nTears arose in Esther’s eyes, and she was about to speak.\n\n“Nay,” said Iras, “I do not want pity or tears. Tell him, finally, I have found that to be a Roman is to be a brute. Farewell.”\n\nShe moved to go. Esther followed her.\n\n“Stay, and see my husband. He has no feeling against you. He sought for you everywhere. He will be your friend. I will be your friend. We are Christians.”\n\nThe other was firm.\n\n“No; I am what I am of choice. It will be over shortly.”\n\n“But”—Esther hesitated—“have we nothing you would wish; nothing to—to—”\n\nThe countenance of the Egyptian softened; something like a smile played about her lips. She looked at the children upon the floor.\n\n“There is something,” she said.\n\nEsther followed her eyes, and with quick perception answered, “It is yours.”\n\nIras went to them, and knelt on the lion’s skin, and kissed them both. Rising slowly, she looked at them; then passed to the door and out of it without a parting word. She walked rapidly, and was gone before Esther could decide what to do.\n\nBen-Hur, when he was told of the visit, knew certainly what he had long surmised—that on the day of the crucifixion Iras had deserted her father for Messala. Nevertheless, he set out immediately and hunted for her vainly; they never saw her more, or heard of her. The blue bay, with all its laughing under the sun, has yet its dark secrets. Had it a tongue, it might tell us of the Egyptian.\n\nSimonides lived to be a very old man. In the tenth year of Nero’s reign, he gave up the business so long centred in the warehouse at Antioch. To the last he kept a clear head and a good heart, and was successful.\n\nOne evening, in the year named, he sat in his arm-chair on the terrace of the warehouse. Ben-Hur and Esther, and their three children, were with him. The last of the ships swung at mooring in the current of the river; all the rest had been sold. In the long interval between this and the day of the crucifixion but one sorrow had befallen them: that was when the mother of Ben-Hur died; and then and now their grief would have been greater but for their Christian faith.\n\nThe ship spoken of had arrived only the day before, bringing intelligence of the persecution of Christians begun by Nero in Rome, and the party on the terrace were talking of the news when Malluch, who was still in their service, approached and delivered a package to Ben-Hur.\n\n“Who brings this?” the latter asked, after reading.\n\n“An Arab.”\n“Where is he?”\n\n“He left immediately.”\n\n“Listen,” said Ben-Hur to Simonides.\n\nHe read then the following letter:\n\n“I, Ilderim, the son of Ilderim the Generous, and sheik of the tribe of Ilderim, to Judah, son of Hur.\n\n“Know, O friend of my father’s, how my father loved you. Read what is herewith sent, and you will know. His will is my will; therefore what he gave is thine.\n\n“All the Parthians took from him in the great battle in which they slew him I have retaken—this writing, with other things, and vengeance, and all the brood of that Mira who in his time was mother of so many stars.\n\n“Peace be to you and all yours.\n\n“This voice out of the desert is the voice of\n\n“Ilderim, Shiek.”\n\nBen-Hur next unrolled a scrap of papyrus yellow as a withered mulberry leaf. It required the daintiest handling. Proceeding, he read:\n\n“Ilderim, surnamed the Generous, sheik of the tribe of Ilderim, to the son who succeeds me.\n\n“All I have, O son, shall be thine in the day of thy succession, except that property by Antioch known as the Orchard of Palms; and it shall be to the son of Hur who brought us such glory in the Circus—to him and his forever.\n\n“Dishonor not thy father. ILDERIM THE GENEROUS, Sheik.”\n\n“What say you?” asked Ben-Hur, of Simonides.\n\nEsther took the papers pleased, and read them to herself. Simonides remained silent. His eyes were upon the ship; but he was thinking. At length he spoke.\n\n“Son of Hur,” he said, gravely, “the Lord has been good to you in these later years. You have much to be thankful for. Is it not time to decide finally the meaning of the gift of the great fortune now all in your hand, and growing?”\n\n“I decided that long ago. The fortune was meant for the service of the Giver; not a part, Simonides, but all of it. The question with me has been, How can I make it most useful in his cause? And of that tell me, I pray you.”\n\nSimonides answered,\n\n“The great sums you have given to the Church here in Antioch, I am witness to. Now, instantly almost with this gift of the generous sheik’s, comes the news of the persecution of the brethren in Rome. It is the opening of a new field. The light must not go out in the capital.”\n\n“Tell me how I can keep it alive.”\n\n“I will tell you. The Romans, even this Nero, hold two things sacred—I know of no others they so hold—they are the ashes of the dead and all places of burial. If you cannot build temples for the worship of the Lord above ground, then build them below the ground; and to keep them from profanation, carry to them the bodies of all who die in the faith.”\n\nBen-Hur arose excitedly.\n\n“It is a great idea,” he said. “I will not wait to begin it. Time forbids waiting. The ship that brought the news of the suffering of our brethren shall take me to Rome. I will sail to-morrow.”\n\nHe turned to Malluch.\n\n“Get the ship ready, Malluch, and be thou ready to go with me.”\n\n“It is well,” said Simonides.\n\n“And thou, Esther, what sayest thou?” asked Ben-Hur.\n\nEsther came to his side, and put her hand on his arm, and answered,\n\n“So wilt thou best serve the Christ. O my husband, let me not hinder, but go with thee and help.”\n\nIf any of my readers, visiting Rome, will make the short journey to the Catacomb of San Calixto, which is more ancient than that of San Sebastiano, he will see what became of the fortune of Ben-Hur, and give him thanks. Out of that vast tomb Christianity issued to supersede the Cæsars.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-2145"]="「제벨 에스 주블레」는 길이가 80킬로미터가 넘는 산으로, 폭이 어찌나 좁은지 지도에 그려진 모습은 마치 남쪽에서 북쪽으로 기어가는 애벌레를 닮았다. 붉고 흰 절벽 위에 서서 떠오르는 해가 지나는 길을 따라 멀리 바라보면, 오직 아라비아 사막만이 눈에 들어온다. 그곳에서는 여리고의 포도 재배자들이 그토록 미워하는 동풍이 태초부터 제 놀이터를 지켜 왔다. 산기슭은 유프라테스에서 날려 온 모래에 두텁게 덮여 있는데, 이 산이 서쪽으로 모압과 암몬의 목초지를 가로막는 성벽 노릇을 하기에 모래는 그곳에 그대로 쌓여 있다. 그렇지 않았다면 그 목초지마저 사막의 일부가 되었을 것이다.\n\n아랍인은 유대 남쪽과 동쪽의 모든 것에 자기 언어의 흔적을 새겨 놓았다. 그래서 그들의 말로 이 오래된 제벨은 수없이 많은 와디의 어머니다. 그 와디들은 로마 가도를 가로지르며—한때 메카를 오가는 시리아 순례자들이 밟던 먼지투성이 길이었으나 지금은 그 옛 모습의 희미한 흔적만 남은 가도다—골을 이루어 흐르고, 갈수록 깊어지다가, 우기의 급류를 요르단강이나 그 마지막 종착지인 사해로 흘려보낸다. 이 와디들 가운데 하나에서—더 정확히 말하면, 제벨의 맨 끝에서 시작하여 북쪽으로 동쪽을 향해 뻗어 가다가 마침내 얍복강의 바닥이 되는 그 와디에서—한 나그네가 빠져나와 사막의 고원지대를 향해 가고 있었다. 독자가 처음으로 주목해야 할 사람이 바로 이 인물이다.\n\n겉모습으로 미루어 그는 마흔다섯 살쯤 되어 보였다. 한때 더없이 새까맣던 그의 수염은 가슴 위로 넓게 흘러내렸는데, 이제는 군데군데 희끗희끗했다. 그의 얼굴은 바싹 마른 커피 열매처럼 갈색이었고, 붉은 쿠피예에 가려져 일부만 보일 뿐이었다.\n* 쿠피예: 오늘날 사막의 자손들이 머릿수건을 부르는 이름.\n\n이따금 그가 눈을 들면, 크고 검은 눈이 드러났다. 그는 동방에서 누구나 입는 헐렁한 옷을 걸치고 있었으나, 그 차림새를 더 자세히 묘사하기는 어렵다. 그가 작은 천막 아래 앉아 거대한 흰 단봉낙타를 타고 있었기 때문이다.\n서방 사람들이 사막을 가기 위해 채비와 짐을 갖춘 낙타를 처음 보았을 때 받은 인상에서 과연 헤어날 수 있을지 의심스럽다. 다른 새로운 것들에는 그토록 치명적인 익숙함도 이 감정만큼은 좀처럼 어쩌지 못한다. 대상(隊商)과 함께 긴 여행을 마친 뒤에도, 베두인족과 더불어 여러 해를 산 뒤에도, 서방 태생의 사람들은 어디에 있든 걸음을 멈추고 그 위엄 있는 짐승이 지나가기를 기다린다. 그 매력은 생김새에 있지 않다. 그 생김새는 사랑으로도 아름답게 만들 수 없다. 또한 그 움직임에 있는 것도 아니니, 소리 없는 발걸음이나 폭넓게 기우뚱거리는 걸음새도 아니다. 바다가 배에게 베푸는 친절이 그러하듯, 사막은 제 피조물에게 그런 친절을 베푼다. 사막은 낙타에게 제 온갖 신비를 입혀 준다. 게다가 우리가 낙타를 바라보는 동안 그 신비를 떠올리게 만드니, 바로 거기에 경이가 있다. 지금 와디에서 빠져나온 그 짐승은 으레 바치는 경의를 받을 만했다. 그 빛깔과 키, 넓은 발, 살이 찐 것이 아니라 근육으로 뒤덮인 몸집, 백조처럼 휘어진 길고 가느다란 목, 두 눈 사이는 넓고 부인의 팔찌로도 거의 감쌀 만큼 가늘어지는 주둥이를 가진 머리, 그리고 길고 탄력 있는 보폭에 확실하고도 소리 없는 발걸음—이 모든 것이 키루스 시대만큼이나 오래된, 그리고 그야말로 값을 매길 수 없는 시리아 혈통임을 증명하고 있었다. 여느 것과 같은 굴레가 이마를 진홍빛 술로 덮고, 목에는 늘어뜨린 놋쇠 사슬을 장식했는데, 사슬 끝마다 딸랑거리는 은방울이 달려 있었다. 그러나 그 굴레에는 타는 이를 위한 고삐도, 모는 이를 위한 가죽끈도 없었다. 등에 얹힌 장구(裝具)는 동방 사람이 아닌 다른 민족이었다면 그 고안자를 유명하게 만들었을 발명품이었다. 그것은 길이가 채 1.2미터도 안 되는 나무 상자 두 개로 이루어져, 양옆에 하나씩 매달려 균형을 이루었다. 안쪽 공간은 부드럽게 안을 대고 양탄자를 깔아, 주인이 앉거나 반쯤 기대어 누울 수 있게 마련되어 있었다. 그 위로는 초록빛 차일이 드리워졌다. 폭넓은 등띠와 가슴띠와 뱃대끈이 수없이 많은 매듭과 끈으로 단단히 묶여 그 장치를 제자리에 붙들고 있었다. 이런 식으로 쿠시의 슬기로운 자손들은 의무에 따라서든 즐거움에 따라서든 자주 지나야 하는 햇볕 따가운 광야의 길을 편안하게 만들어 냈던 것이다.\n\n단봉낙타가 와디의 마지막 갈래에서 몸을 끌어올렸을 때, 나그네는 옛 암몬 땅인 엘 벨카의 경계를 넘어선 뒤였다. 아침나절이었다. 그의 앞에는 보드라운 안개에 반쯤 가린 해가 떠 있었고, 그의 앞에는 또한 사막이 펼쳐져 있었다. 흩날리는 모래의 세계는 아니었으니, 그것은 더 멀리 있었다. 그가 있는 곳은 풀들이 작아지기 시작하는 지역으로, 땅 위에는 화강암 바위와 잿빛, 갈색 돌멩이가 흩어져 있고, 그 사이로 시들어 가는 아카시아와 낙타풀 더미가 듬성듬성 박혀 있었다. 떡갈나무와 가시덤불과 소귀나무는 마치 어떤 경계선에 다다라, 물 한 점 없는 황야를 넘겨다보고는 두려움에 웅크리기라도 한 듯, 뒤쪽에 남아 있었다.\n\n이제는 오솔길도 큰길도 끝이 났다. 낙타는 그 어느 때보다 보이지 않는 무엇에 이끌리는 듯했다. 걸음을 길고 빠르게 옮기며, 머리는 곧장 지평선을 향했다. 넓은 콧구멍으로는 바람을 크게 들이켰다. 가마는 흔들리며, 물결 위의 배처럼 오르내렸다. 군데군데 마른 잎이 쌓인 자리에서는 발밑으로 바스락 소리가 났다. 때로는 압생트 같은 향기가 온 공기를 달콤하게 적셨다. 종다리와 딱새와 바위제비가 날개를 치며 날아오르고, 흰 자고새들이 휘파람 같은 소리를 내고 꼬꼬댁거리며 길에서 달아났다. 더 드물게는 여우나 하이에나가 안전한 거리에서 침입자를 살피려고 발걸음을 재촉했다. 오른편으로는 제벨의 언덕들이 솟아 있었고, 그 위에 드리운 진줏빛 잿빛 너울은 시시각각 자줏빛으로 바뀌어 갔으니, 조금 뒤면 해가 그 빛을 견줄 데 없이 곱게 물들일 터였다. 가장 높은 봉우리 위로는 독수리 한 마리가 너른 날개를 펴고 점점 넓어지는 원을 그리며 날았다. 그러나 초록빛 천막 아래 든 사람은 이 모든 것을 하나도 보지 못했거나, 적어도 알아차렸다는 기색을 보이지 않았다. 그의 눈은 한곳에 못 박힌 채 꿈꾸는 듯했다. 짐승이 그러하듯, 그 사람의 가는 모습 또한 누군가에게 이끌려 가는 것만 같았다.\n\n두 시간 동안 단봉낙타는 앞으로 흔들흔들 나아가며, 한결같이 종종걸음을 유지하고 정동(正東)으로 길을 잡았다. 그동안 나그네는 한 번도 자세를 바꾸지 않았고, 좌우를 돌아보지도 않았다. 사막에서 거리는 마일이나 리그로 재는 것이 아니라, 사트, 곧 시간과 만질, 곧 멈춤으로 잰다. 한 사트는 3.5리그를, 한 만질은 15리그에서 25리그를 채운다. 그러나 이것은 보통 낙타의 속도다. 진짜 시리아 혈통의 운반용 낙타라면 3리그쯤은 거뜬히 갈 수 있다. 전속력을 내면 여느 바람도 따라잡는다. 빠르게 나아간 결과의 하나로, 풍경의 모습이 바뀌었다. 제벨은 옅은 푸른빛 리본처럼 서쪽 지평선을 따라 뻗어 있었다. 텔, 곧 진흙과 굳은 모래로 된 둔덕이 여기저기 솟아 있었다. 이따금 현무암 돌들이 둥근 봉우리를 들어 올렸으니, 평원의 세력에 맞선 산의 전초였다. 그러나 그 밖의 모든 것은 모래였으니, 어떤 곳은 다져진 해변처럼 매끄럽고, 어떤 곳은 굽이치는 능선처럼 쌓여 있었으며, 여기는 자잘하게 부서진 물결, 저기는 길게 너울지는 물결이었다. 대기의 상태 또한 그러하게 바뀌었다. 높이 떠오른 해는 이슬과 안개를 실컷 들이마셨고, 차일 아래 나그네에게 입 맞추는 산들바람을 데웠다. 멀리서나 가까이서나 해는 대지를 옅은 젖빛으로 물들이며, 온 하늘을 어른어른 빛나게 했다.\n\n쉬지도 않고 길을 벗어나지도 않은 채 두 시간이 더 흘렀다. 초목은 완전히 자취를 감추었다. 표면이 어찌나 단단하게 굳었는지 한 걸음 디딜 때마다 덜그럭거리는 조각으로 부서지는 모래가 다툼 없이 그 땅을 다스리고 있었다. 제벨은 시야에서 사라졌고, 눈에 보이는 어떤 이정표도 없었다. 앞서 뒤따르던 그림자는 이제 북쪽으로 옮겨 가, 그것을 드리운 물체와 나란히 발맞추어 가고 있었다. 그런데도 멈출 기색이 전혀 없으니, 나그네의 행동은 순간순간 더욱 기이해져 갔다.\n\n기억해 두어야 할 것은, 누구도 사막을 놀이터 삼아 찾지는 않는다는 사실이다. 삶과 생업은 죽은 것들의 뼈가 수많은 문장(紋章)처럼 흩어진 길을 따라 사막을 가로지른다. 우물에서 우물로, 목초지에서 목초지로 이어지는 길이 그러하다. 길 없는 황무지에 홀로 있게 되면 아무리 노련한 족장이라도 가슴이 더 빨리 뛴다. 그러므로 우리가 지금 다루고 있는 이 사람도 즐거움을 찾고 있었을 리는 없다. 그렇다고 그 태도가 도망자의 것도 아니었으니, 그는 단 한 번도 뒤를 돌아보지 않았다. 그런 상황에서는 두려움과 호기심이 가장 흔한 감각이건만, 그는 그런 것에 흔들리지 않았다. 사람은 외로워지면 어떤 길동무에게도 몸을 굽히게 마련이라, 개가 동무가 되고 말이 벗이 되며, 그것들에게 쓰다듬음과 사랑의 말을 쏟아붓는 것도 부끄러운 일이 아니다. 그러나 낙타는 그런 표시를 받지 못했으니, 손길 한 번도, 말 한마디도 없었다.\n\n정오가 되자 단봉낙타는 제 뜻대로 걸음을 멈추고, 그 종족이 짐이 너무 무거울 때 늘 내는, 때로는 보살핌과 휴식을 갈구할 때 내는, 유달리 애처로운 울음, 곧 신음 소리를 냈다. 그러자 주인은 마치 잠에서 깨어나듯 몸을 추슬렀다. 그는 후다의 휘장을 걷어 올리고, 해를 바라보고는, 약속된 장소를 가려내기라도 하려는 듯 사방의 지형을 오래도록 꼼꼼히 살폈다. 살피기를 마치고 흡족해진 그는 깊은 숨을 들이쉬고 고개를 끄덕였으니, \"마침내, 마침내 왔구나!\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. 잠시 후 그는 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 고개를 숙여 말없이 기도했다. 경건한 의무를 마친 그는 내릴 채비를 했다. 그의 목구멍에서 욥의 사랑받던 낙타들도 분명 들었을 그 소리가 흘러나왔다—\"이크! 이크!\"—꿇어앉으라는 신호였다. 짐승은 내내 끙끙거리며 천천히 그 말에 따랐다. 그러자 탄 이는 가느다란 목에 발을 디디고, 모래 위로 내려섰다.\n* 후다: 낙타 등에 얹는 덮개 달린 가마.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n이제 모습을 드러낸 그 사람은 더없이 균형 잡힌 체격으로, 키가 큰 것이 아니라 다부진 편이었다. 쿠피예를 머리에 붙들어 매던 비단끈을 풀고, 술 달린 천 자락을 뒤로 젖혀 얼굴을 온전히 드러냈다—그것은 흑인에 가까운 빛깔의 강인한 얼굴이었으나, 낮고 넓은 이마, 매부리코, 바깥쪽으로 살짝 치켜 올라간 눈꼬리, 금속의 윤기가 도는 곧고 뻣뻣한 숱 많은 머리카락이 여러 갈래로 땋여 어깨까지 흘러내린 모습은 도무지 감출 수 없는 혈통의 표징이었다. 파라오들과 후대의 프톨레마이오스들이 이러한 모습이었고, 이집트 민족의 시조인 미스라임이 이러한 모습이었다. 그는 카미스를 입고 있었으니, 소매가 좁고 앞이 트인 흰 무명 셔츠로 발목까지 내려오고 깃과 가슴께에 수가 놓인 옷이었으며, 그 위에는 갈색 모직 망토를 둘렀는데, 지금도 그러하고 그때도 십중팔구 그러했듯이 아바라고 부르는 옷이었다. 아바는 자락이 길고 소매가 짧은 겉옷으로, 안쪽에는 무명과 비단을 섞어 짠 천을 대고, 가장자리는 빙 둘러 흐릿한 노란빛으로 둘러쳐져 있었다. 그의 발은 부드러운 가죽끈으로 동여맨 샌들이 감싸고 있었다. 허리띠가 카미스를 허리에 붙들어 매었다. 그가 홀로인 데다, 사막은 표범과 사자, 그리고 그것들 못지않게 사나운 인간들이 출몰하는 곳임을 생각하면 무척 눈에 띄는 점은, 그가 아무런 무기도 지니지 않았다는 것이었으니, 낙타를 모는 데 쓰는 굽은 막대기조차 없었다. 그러므로 우리는 적어도 그의 볼일이 평화로운 것임을, 그리고 그가 보기 드물게 담대하거나 아니면 어떤 비범한 보호 아래 있음을 미루어 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n나그네의 팔다리는 마비되어 있었으니, 여정이 길고도 고단했던 까닭이다. 그래서 그는 두 손을 비비고 발을 굴렀으며, 충직한 종 둘레를 거닐었다. 낙타는 이미 되새김할 풀을 찾아내고는 흡족한 마음에 빛나는 눈을 지그시 감고 있었다. 그렇게 도는 동안 그는 자주 걸음을 멈추고, 손으로 눈 위에 그늘을 지어 시야가 닿는 가장 먼 끝까지 사막을 살폈다. 그리고 살피기를 마칠 때마다 그의 얼굴에는 실망의 그늘이 드리웠으니, 비록 옅었으나 눈썰미 있는 자라면 그가 약속 때문은 아닐지라도 누군가의 일행을 기다리고 있음을 알아챌 만했다. 동시에 그 구경꾼은 문명의 거처에서 이토록 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 처리해야 할 볼일이 대체 무엇일지 알고 싶은 호기심이 한층 날카로워짐을 느꼈을 것이다.\n\n아무리 실망했다 한들, 기다리던 일행이 오리라는 이 낯선 이의 확신에는 의심의 여지가 거의 없었다. 그 증표로, 그는 먼저 가마로 가서, 올 때 자신이 들어앉았던 칸의 맞은편 칸에서 해면과 작은 가죽 물병을 꺼내, 낙타의 눈과 얼굴과 콧구멍을 씻어 주었다. 그 일을 마치자 같은 곳에서 붉고 흰 줄무늬가 든 둥근 천 한 장과 막대기 한 묶음과 굵은 지팡이 하나를 끄집어냈다. 그 지팡이는 손을 좀 놀려 보니, 작은 마디들이 하나씩 다른 마디 속에 들어가도록 만든 정교한 장치임이 드러났는데, 한데 이으니 그의 키보다 높은 중심 기둥이 되었다. 기둥을 세우고 그 둘레에 막대기들을 박은 뒤, 그는 그 위에 천을 펼쳤다. 이로써 그는 그야말로 제집을 마련한 셈이었으니—에미르나 셰이크의 거처보다는 훨씬 작았으나, 다른 모든 점에서는 그것의 축소판이었다. 다시 가마에서 그는 양탄자, 곧 네모난 깔개를 꺼내 천막 안 해를 등진 쪽 바닥에 깔았다. 그 일을 마치자 그는 다시 밖으로 나가, 한층 정성스럽고 한층 간절한 눈길로 사방의 땅을 둘러보았다. 평원을 가로질러 내달리는 멀리의 재칼 한 마리와 아카바만을 향해 날아가는 독수리 한 마리를 빼면, 위의 푸른 하늘이 그러하듯 아래의 황야 또한 생명이 없었다.\n\n그는 낙타에게 돌아서서, 사막에는 낯선 언어로 나직이 말했다. \"우리는 집에서 멀리 왔구나, 오 가장 빠른 바람과 겨루는 자여—우리는 집에서 멀리 왔으나, 하느님이 우리와 함께 계시느니라. 참고 견디자꾸나.\"\n\n이윽고 그는 안장 주머니에서 콩 몇 줌을 꺼내, 짐승의 코 밑에 매달도록 만든 자루에 담았다. 그리고 충직한 종이 그 먹이를 맛나게 먹는 모습을 보고는, 다시 돌아서서 수직으로 내리쬐는 해의 빛에 어른거리는 모래의 세계를 또 한 번 살폈다.\n\n\"그들은 올 것이다.\" 그는 차분히 말했다. \"나를 이끄신 분이 그들도 이끄시느니라. 채비를 해 두어야겠다.\"\n\n칸의 안쪽을 두른 주머니들과, 거기 갖추어진 버들 바구니에서 그는 식사 재료를 꺼냈다. 종려나무 섬유를 촘촘히 엮은 접시들, 작은 가죽 병에 든 포도주, 말려 훈제한 양고기, 씨 없는 샤미, 곧 시리아 석류, 중앙아라비아의 나킬, 곧 종려 과수원에서 자라는 놀랍도록 달콤한 엘 셸레비 대추야자, 다윗의 \"젖과 같은 치즈\" 같은 치즈, 그리고 도시 빵집에서 구운 발효빵—이 모든 것을 그는 천막 아래 양탄자 위로 날라 차려 놓았다. 마지막 채비로, 그는 음식 둘레에 비단 천 세 장을 깔았으니, 동방의 교양 있는 사람들이 식탁에 앉은 손님의 무릎을 덮는 데 쓰는 것이었다—그가 베푸는 자리에 참여할 사람의 수를 말해 주는 정황이요, 그가 기다리고 있는 인원수였다.\n\n이제 모든 것이 갖추어졌다. 그가 밖으로 나서니, 보라! 동쪽 사막의 얼굴 위에 검은 점 하나가 있었다. 그는 땅에 뿌리박힌 듯 우뚝 섰고, 두 눈은 휘둥그레졌으며, 무언가 초자연적인 것에 닿기라도 한 듯 살갗에 서늘한 소름이 돋았다. 점은 자라났다. 손바닥만 하게 커지더니, 마침내 또렷한 형체를 갖추었다. 조금 뒤, 그 자신의 단봉낙타를 빼닮은 것이 온전히 모습을 드러내며 다가왔으니, 키가 크고 흰 데다, 힌두스탄의 여행용 가마인 후다를 얹고 있었다. 그러자 이집트인은 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 하늘을 우러렀다.\n\n\"오직 하느님만이 위대하시도다!\" 그는 두 눈에 눈물이 그득하고 영혼은 경외에 사로잡힌 채 외쳤다.\n\n낯선 이가 가까이 다가오더니—마침내 멈추어 섰다. 그러자 그 또한 막 잠에서 깨어나는 듯했다. 그는 꿇어앉은 낙타와 천막과, 그 문간에 기도하는 자세로 선 사람을 바라보았다. 그는 두 손을 포개고 고개를 숙여 말없이 기도했다. 그러고는 이내 낙타의 목을 디뎌 모래 위로 내려서서, 이집트인을 향해 나아갔고, 이집트인 또한 그를 향해 나아갔다. 잠시 두 사람은 서로를 바라보았다. 그런 다음 그들은 끌어안았다—곧, 저마다 오른팔을 상대의 어깨 위로 두르고 왼팔로 옆구리를 감싸, 턱을 먼저 왼쪽 가슴에, 다음으로 오른쪽 가슴에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 오 참하느님의 종이여!\" 낯선 이가 말했다.\n\n\"그대에게도 그러하기를, 오 참신앙의 형제여!—그대에게 평화와 환영이 있기를.\" 이집트인이 뜨겁게 화답했다.\n\n새로 온 이는 키가 크고 수척했으니, 마른 얼굴에 움푹 꺼진 눈, 흰 머리와 수염, 그리고 계피와 청동의 중간쯤 되는 낯빛을 하고 있었다. 그 또한 무기를 지니지 않았다. 옷차림은 힌두스탄풍이었으니, 챙 없는 모자 위로 숄을 큼직하게 여러 겹 둘러 터번을 이루었고, 몸에 걸친 옷은 이집트인의 것과 같은 양식이었으되, 다만 아바가 더 짧아 발목께에서 졸라맨 폭 넓은 통바지가 드러나 보였다. 샌들 대신 그의 발은 발끝이 뾰족한 붉은 가죽 반신을 신고 있었다. 그 신을 빼면 머리에서 발끝까지 옷차림은 온통 흰 아마였다. 그 사람의 분위기는 고결하고 위엄 있으며 준엄했다. 동방의 일리아드에서 가장 위대한 고행 영웅인 비스바미트라가 그에게서 완벽한 화신을 얻은 셈이었다. 그는 브라마의 지혜로 흠뻑 젖은 생명이라, 헌신의 화신이라 불릴 만했다. 오직 그의 두 눈에만 인간다움의 증거가 있었으니, 이집트인의 가슴에서 얼굴을 들었을 때 그 눈은 눈물로 반짝이고 있었다.\n\n\"오직 하느님만이 위대하시도다!\" 그는 포옹을 마치고 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 그분을 섬기는 자들에게 복이 있도다!\" 이집트인이 답하면서, 자신의 외침을 고쳐 옮긴 그 말에 놀랐다. \"그러나 기다립시다.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"기다립시다. 보시오, 또 한 분이 저기 오고 있소이다!\"\n\n그들은 북쪽을 바라보았다. 그곳에는 이미 또렷이 눈에 들어오는, 다른 낙타들처럼 새하얀 세 번째 낙타가 한 척의 배처럼 기우뚱거리며 다가오고 있었다. 그들은 함께 서서 기다렸다—새로 온 이가 다다라 내려서서 그들을 향해 나아올 때까지 기다렸다.\n\n\"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 오 나의 형제여!\" 그는 힌두인을 끌어안으며 말했다.\n\n힌두인이 답했다. \"하느님의 뜻이 이루어지이다!\"\n\n마지막에 온 이는 두 벗과는 사뭇 달랐다. 체구가 더 가늘었고, 낯빛이 희었으며, 물결치는 옅은 빛깔의 머리채가 작지만 아름다운 머리에 더없이 어울리는 관을 이루고 있었다. 짙푸른 두 눈의 온기는 섬세한 마음씨와, 따뜻하고 용감한 천성을 일러 주었다. 그는 맨머리에 무기도 지니지 않았다. 무심한 우아함으로 두른 티레산 모포 자락 아래로는, 소매가 짧고 목깃이 낮으며 허리에 띠를 둘러 무릎께까지 닿는 튜닉이 드러났으니, 목과 팔과 다리는 맨살이었다. 샌들이 그의 발을 지켰다. 쉰 해, 아마도 그 이상의 세월이 그에게 흘러갔으되, 겉으로 드러나는 자취라곤 그 거동에 무게를 더하고 그 말에 사려를 깃들인 것뿐인 듯했다. 몸의 됨됨이도, 영혼의 광채도 손상되지 않았다. 그가 어떤 겨레에서 났는지는 글 읽는 이에게 굳이 일러 줄 필요가 없으니, 그 자신은 아테네의 숲에서 오지 않았더라도 그의 조상은 그러했던 것이다.\n\n그의 팔이 이집트인에게서 풀렸을 때, 이집트인이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"성령께서 나를 먼저 이끄셨소이다. 그러니 나는 내가 형제들을 섬기는 종으로 뽑혔음을 아오. 천막은 세워졌고, 빵은 떼기에 알맞게 마련되어 있소. 내가 내 소임을 다하게 해 주시오.\"\n\n그는 저마다의 손을 잡고 그들을 안으로 이끌어, 그들의 샌들을 벗기고 발을 씻겼으며, 손 위에 물을 부어 주고 수건으로 닦아 주었다.\n\n그런 다음, 자기 손마저 씻고 나서 그가 말했다. \"형제들이여, 우리 소임이 그러하니 몸을 잘 돌봅시다. 그리고 먹읍시다. 그래야 오늘 남은 의무를 감당할 힘을 얻을 것이오. 먹는 동안, 우리 각자가 다른 이들이 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지, 어떻게 불리는지 알아 둡시다.\"\n\n그는 그들을 음식 앞으로 데려가, 서로 마주 보도록 앉혔다. 동시에 그들의 머리가 앞으로 숙여지고, 두 손이 가슴 위에 포개졌으며, 그들은 한목소리로 이 소박한 감사의 기도를 소리 내어 올렸다.\n\n\"만물의 아버지이신—하느님!—여기 있는 것은 모두 당신의 것이옵니다. 우리의 감사를 받으시고 우리를 축복하시어, 우리가 당신의 뜻을 계속 행하게 하옵소서.\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 그들은 눈을 들어, 놀라움 속에 서로를 바라보았다. 저마다 다른 이들이 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 언어로 말했건만, 저마다 그 말의 뜻을 완벽하게 알아들었던 것이다. 그들의 영혼은 거룩한 감격으로 떨렸으니, 그 기적으로 말미암아 그들은 하느님의 임재를 깨달았기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n시대의 어법으로 말하자면, 방금 묘사한 만남은 로마력 747년에 일어난 일이다. 때는 12월이었고, 지중해 동쪽의 온 지역에 겨울이 군림하고 있었다. 이 계절에 사막을 가는 사람은 멀리 가지 않아 곧 날카로운 허기에 사로잡힌다. 작은 천막 아래 모인 일행도 그 법칙에서 예외는 아니었다. 그들은 배가 고팠고, 마음껏 먹었다. 그리고 포도주를 마신 뒤에는 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n\"낯선 땅의 나그네에게 친구의 입에서 제 이름을 듣는 것만큼 달콤한 일은 없습니다.\" 그 식사의 좌장을 자처한 이집트인이 말했다. \"우리 앞에는 함께할 많은 날이 놓여 있습니다. 이제 서로를 알 때가 되었습니다. 그러니 괜찮으시다면, 가장 늦게 오신 분이 가장 먼저 말씀하시지요.\"\n\n그러자, 처음에는 천천히, 마치 스스로를 조심스레 살피는 사람처럼, 그리스인이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"형제들이여, 제가 들려드릴 이야기는 너무도 기이하여, 어디서부터 시작해야 할지, 무엇을 마땅히 말씀드려야 할지조차 모르겠습니다. 저 자신도 아직 저를 이해하지 못합니다. 제가 가장 확신하는 것은, 제가 한 주인의 뜻을 행하고 있다는 것, 그리고 그 섬김이 끊임없는 황홀이라는 것입니다. 제가 이루도록 보내심을 받은 그 목적을 생각하면, 제 안에는 형언할 수 없는 기쁨이 차오르니, 그 뜻이 하느님의 것임을 압니다.\"\n\n이 선한 사람은 더 나아가지 못하고 말을 멈추었고, 다른 이들은 그의 마음에 공감하여 시선을 떨구었다.\n\n\"이곳에서 멀리 서쪽으로,\" 그가 다시 입을 열었다. \"결코 잊혀서는 안 될 땅이 하나 있습니다. 세상이 그 땅에 진 빚이 너무도 크고, 더구나 그 빚이 사람에게 가장 순수한 즐거움을 안겨 주는 것들에 대한 빚이기 때문입니다. 저는 예술에 대해서도, 철학이나 웅변이나 시나 전쟁에 대해서도 아무 말 하지 않으렵니다. 아, 형제들이여, 그 땅의 영광은 완성된 문자 속에 영원히 빛나야 할 것이니, 우리가 찾아가 선포하려는 그분이 바로 그 문자를 통해 온 땅에 알려질 것입니다. 제가 말하는 땅은 그리스입니다. 저는 아테네 사람 클레안테스의 아들 가스파르입니다.\n\n\"제 민족은,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"온전히 학문에 몸 바친 사람들이었고, 저 또한 그들에게서 같은 열정을 물려받았습니다. 마침 우리의 철학자 가운데 둘이, 그 많은 이들 중에서도 가장 위대한 두 사람이, 하나는 모든 사람 안에 깃든 영혼과 그 불멸을 가르치고, 다른 하나는 무한히 의로우신 한 분 하느님의 가르침을 폈습니다. 여러 학파가 다투던 수많은 주제 가운데, 저는 그 둘을 따로 떼어 냈으니, 풀이의 수고를 들일 가치가 있는 것은 오직 그것뿐이라 여겼기 때문입니다. 저는 하느님과 영혼 사이에 아직 알려지지 않은 어떤 관계가 있다고 생각했습니다. 이 주제에 관하여 사람의 정신은 한 지점까지, 죽은 듯 넘을 수 없는 벽까지 헤아려 갈 수 있습니다. 거기에 이르면, 남은 것이라고는 그저 서서 도움을 큰 소리로 부르짖는 일뿐입니다. 저도 그리하였습니다. 그러나 그 벽 너머로 제게 들려오는 목소리는 없었습니다. 절망에 빠진 저는 도시와 학파에서 저 자신을 떼어 냈습니다.\"\n\n이 말에 힌두인의 야윈 얼굴에 진지한 동의의 미소가 어렸다.\n\n\"제 나라의 북쪽, 테살리아에,\" 그리스인이 이어 말했다. \"신들의 거처로 이름난 산이 하나 있습니다. 제 동포들이 최고로 받드는 테우스가 머무는 곳이지요. 올림포스라는 이름의 산입니다. 저는 그곳으로 갔습니다. 서쪽에서 뻗어 와 남동쪽으로 굽어지는 자리의 한 언덕에서 동굴을 찾아냈습니다. 거기서 저는 명상에 몸을 맡기며 살았습니다. 아니, 숨 쉬는 매순간이 기도였던 것, 곧 계시를 기다리는 일에 저 자신을 맡겼습니다. 보이지 않으나 지극히 높으신 하느님을 믿었기에, 저는 또한 온 영혼을 다해 그분을 사모하면, 그분께서 가엾이 여겨 제게 응답해 주시리라 믿었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 응답하셨군요, 응답하셨어요!\" 힌두인이 무릎 위 비단 천에서 두 손을 들어 올리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"제 말을 들어 주십시오, 형제들이여.\" 그리스인이 애써 마음을 가라앉히며 말했다. \"제 은둔처의 문은 바다의 한 갈래, 테르마이만을 굽어보고 있었습니다. 어느 날 저는 지나가던 배에서 한 사람이 바다로 던져지는 것을 보았습니다. 그는 헤엄쳐 뭍에 올랐습니다. 저는 그를 받아들여 돌보았습니다. 그는 유대인으로, 제 민족의 역사와 율법에 밝은 사람이었습니다. 그에게서 저는 제 기도의 하느님이 정녕 존재하시며, 여러 시대에 걸쳐 그들의 율법 제정자요 통치자요 왕이셨음을 알게 되었습니다. 그것이 바로 제가 꿈꾸던 계시가 아니고 무엇이겠습니까? 제 믿음은 헛되지 않았으니, 하느님께서 제게 응답하신 것입니다!\"\n\n\"그런 믿음으로 그분께 부르짖는 모든 이에게 그러하시듯이 말입니다.\" 힌두인이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만, 아아!\" 이집트인이 덧붙였다. \"그분이 언제 응답하시는지 알 만큼 지혜로운 사람이 얼마나 적은지요!\"\n\n\"그게 다가 아니었습니다.\" 그리스인이 말을 이었다. \"제게 보내진 그 사람은 더 많은 것을 일러 주었습니다. 그는 첫 계시 이후의 여러 시대에 하느님과 함께 걸으며 말씀을 나눈 예언자들이, 그분께서 다시 오시리라 선언했다고 했습니다. 그는 제게 예언자들의 이름을 일러 주었고, 거룩한 책에서 그들의 말을 그대로 인용해 주었습니다. 또한 그는 그 두 번째 오심이 가까웠다고, 예루살렘에서 곧 닥칠 일로 손꼽아 기다려지고 있다고 했습니다.\"\n\n그리스인이 말을 멈추자, 그의 얼굴에 어렸던 빛이 사그라들었다.\n\n\"사실입니다.\" 잠시 후 그가 말했다. \"그 사람이 제게 이렇게 말한 것은 사실입니다. 하느님과, 그분이 말한 계시가 오직 유대인만을 위한 것이었듯이, 다시 오실 때에도 그러하리라고 말입니다. 오시기로 한 그분은 유대인의 왕이 되시리라는 것이었습니다. '나머지 세상을 위해서는 아무것도 없습니까?' 제가 물었습니다. '없소.' 자랑스러운 목소리로 돌아온 대답이었습니다. '없소, 우리는 그분의 택하신 백성이오.' 그 대답도 제 희망을 꺾지는 못했습니다. 어찌하여 그런 하느님께서 당신의 사랑과 은혜를 한 땅에, 말하자면 한 가족에게만 한정하시겠습니까? 저는 알아내리라 마음먹었습니다. 마침내 저는 그 사람의 자긍심을 뚫고 들어가, 그의 조상들이 단지 진리를 살아 있게 지키도록 택함받은 종에 지나지 않았으며, 그리하여 세상이 마침내 그것을 알고 구원받게 하려 함이었음을 알아냈습니다. 그 유대인이 떠나고 다시 홀로 남았을 때, 저는 새로운 기도로 제 영혼을 다잡았습니다. 그 왕이 오셨을 때 그분을 뵙고 경배하도록 허락해 달라는 기도였습니다. 어느 밤, 저는 동굴 문가에 앉아 제 존재의 신비에, 곧 그것을 아는 것이 하느님을 아는 것인 그 신비에 더 가까이 다가가려 애쓰고 있었습니다. 그때 갑자기 제 아래 바다 위에서, 아니 그 얼굴을 덮은 어둠 속에서, 별 하나가 타오르기 시작하는 것을 보았습니다. 그 별은 천천히 떠올라 다가오더니, 언덕 위, 제 문 위에 멈춰 그 빛이 저를 가득 비추었습니다. 저는 엎드렸고, 잠이 들었으며, 꿈속에서 한 목소리를 들었습니다.\n\n\"'오, 가스파르여! 너의 믿음이 이겼도다! 복되도다 너여! 땅끝에서 온 다른 두 사람과 더불어, 너는 약속된 그분을 뵙고, 그분의 증인이 되며, 그분을 위한 증언의 계기가 되리라. 아침에 일어나, 그들을 만나러 가고, 너를 인도할 영을 끝까지 믿어라.'\n\n\"그리고 아침에 깨어났을 때, 그 영이 제 안에서 해보다도 더 밝은 빛으로 빛나고 있었습니다. 저는 은둔자의 옷을 벗고, 옛날처럼 차려입었습니다. 숨겨 둔 곳에서 도시에서 가져온 보물을 꺼냈습니다. 배 한 척이 지나갔습니다. 저는 그 배를 불러 올라탔고, 안티오크에 내렸습니다. 거기서 저는 낙타와 그 장구를 샀습니다. 오론테스 강기슭을 아름답게 수놓은 정원과 과수원을 지나, 저는 에메사와 다마스쿠스, 보스라와 필라델피아로 여행했고, 거기서 이곳으로 왔습니다. 그리하여, 오 형제들이여, 이것이 제 이야기입니다. 이제 두 분의 이야기를 듣고자 합니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n이집트인과 힌두인이 서로를 바라보았다. 앞의 사람이 손을 들어 보이자, 뒤의 사람이 고개를 숙이고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"우리 형제는 훌륭하게 말씀하셨소. 내 말도 그만큼 슬기롭기를 바라오.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 다시 이었다.\n\n\"형제들이여, 나를 멜키오르라는 이름으로 알아 주시오. 나는 지금 여러분에게 한 언어로 말하고 있는데, 이 언어는 세상에서 가장 오래된 것은 아닐지라도 적어도 가장 먼저 문자로 정리된 언어요. 인도의 산스크리트어를 두고 하는 말이오. 나는 태생이 힌두인이오. 내 민족은 지식의 들판을 처음으로 거닌 자들이며, 그것을 처음으로 나누고, 처음으로 아름답게 가꾼 자들이오. 앞으로 무슨 일이 닥치든, 네 권의 베다는 살아남아야 하오. 그것은 종교와 유익한 지혜의 원천이기 때문이오. 그로부터 우파베다가 비롯되었으니, 브라마가 전한 이 가르침은 의술과 궁술과 건축과 음악, 그리고 예순네 가지 기계 기예를 다루오. 또한 영감을 받은 성인들이 계시한 베단가가 있어 천문과 문법과 운율과 발음, 주문과 주술, 종교적 제의와 의식을 다루오. 그리고 현자 비야사가 쓴 우팡가가 있어 우주 발생과 연대기와 지리를 담았으며, 그 안에는 우리의 신들과 반신(半神)들을 길이 전하기 위해 지어진 영웅시 라마야나와 마하바라타도 들어 있소. 형제들이여, 이것들이 바로 위대한 샤스트라, 곧 거룩한 율법의 책들이오. 이제 그것들은 내게 죽은 것이 되었으나, 길이길이 내 민족의 움트는 천재성을 증언하는 데 쓰일 것이오. 그것들은 빠른 완성을 약속한 책이었소. 그 약속이 어찌하여 어그러졌는지 묻겠소? 아! 그 책들 자체가 진보의 모든 문을 닫아 버렸소. 인간을 돌본다는 구실로, 그 저자들은 하늘이 인간에게 필요한 모든 것을 마련해 주었으니 인간은 발견이나 발명에 마음을 두어서는 안 된다는 치명적인 원리를 강요했소. 그 조건이 거룩한 율법이 되었을 때, 힌두 천재성의 등불은 우물 속으로 내려졌고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 좁은 벽과 쓰디쓴 물만을 비추어 왔소.\n\n\"형제들이여, 이런 이야기는 자랑에서 나온 것이 아니오. 샤스트라가 브람이라 불리는 지고(至高)의 신을 가르친다는 것을 말씀드리면 이해하실 것이오. 또한 우팡가의 거룩한 시편인 푸라나는 우리에게 덕과 선행, 그리고 영혼에 관하여 일러 주오. 그러니, 내 형제가 허락하신다면\"—그가 그리스인을 향해 공손히 고개를 숙였다—\"그의 민족이 알려지기 여러 시대 전에, 신과 영혼이라는 두 가지 위대한 관념이 이미 힌두인의 정신을 온통 사로잡고 있었소. 좀 더 설명하자면, 같은 거룩한 책들은 브람을 삼위(三位), 곧 브라마와 비슈누와 시바로 가르치오. 그 가운데 브라마는 우리 종족의 창시자로 일컬어지며, 창조의 과정에서 우리를 네 계급으로 나누었소. 먼저 그는 아래의 세계와 위의 하늘에 거주민을 두었고, 다음으로 땅을 지상의 영들이 살 수 있도록 마련했소. 그러고 나서 그의 입에서 브라만 계급이 나왔으니, 그를 가장 닮았으며 가장 높고 가장 고귀하고, 베다를 가르치는 유일한 자들이오. 그 베다 또한 같은 입술에서 완성된 모습으로, 온갖 유익한 지식을 두루 갖춘 채 흘러나왔소. 다음으로 그의 팔에서는 크샤트리아, 곧 전사들이 나왔고, 생명의 자리인 그의 가슴에서는 바이샤, 곧 생산자들—목자와 농부와 상인—이 나왔으며, 천함의 표시로 그의 발에서는 수드라, 곧 종복들이 솟아났으니, 이들은 다른 계급들을 위한 비천한 일에 매인 자들—농노와 하인과 노동자와 장인—이오. 더 유념할 것은, 그렇게 그들과 함께 생겨난 율법이 한 계급의 사람이 다른 계급의 일원이 되는 것을 금했다는 점이오. 브라만은 더 낮은 계급으로 들어갈 수 없으며, 만일 자기 계급의 율법을 어기면 추방자가 되어 자기 같은 추방자들 말고는 모두에게 버림받소.\"\n\n이 대목에 이르러, 그리스인의 상상력은 그러한 추락이 가져올 온갖 결과를 향해 번뜩 내달려, 열렬히 기울이던 주의를 압도했다. 그가 외쳤다. \"형제들이여, 그런 처지에서 사랑의 신이 얼마나 절실히 필요하겠소!\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 이집트인이 덧붙였다. \"우리의 신과 같은 사랑의 신이 말이오.\"\n\n힌두인의 이마가 괴롭게 찌푸려졌다. 그 감정이 가라앉자, 그는 부드러워진 목소리로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 브라만으로 태어났소. 그리하여 내 삶은 가장 사소한 행동, 마지막 시간에 이르기까지 모두 정해져 있었소. 처음으로 받은 한 모금의 양식, 합성된 이름을 받는 일, 처음으로 밖으로 나가 해를 보는 일, 세 겹의 실을 두름으로써 두 번 태어난 자의 하나가 되는 일, 첫째 계급으로 들어가는 입문—이 모두가 거룩한 경문과 엄격한 의식으로 기려졌소. 나는 걷거나 먹거나 마시거나 잠을 잘 때조차 규율을 어길 위험 없이는 그리할 수 없었소. 그리고 그 벌은, 형제들이여, 그 벌은 내 영혼에 미치는 것이었소! 어김의 정도에 따라 내 영혼은 여러 하늘 가운데 하나로 갔으니—가장 낮은 곳은 인드라의 하늘이요, 가장 높은 곳은 브라마의 하늘이오—혹은 다시 쫓겨나 벌레나 파리나 물고기나 짐승의 생명이 되었소. 완벽하게 지킨 데 대한 보상은 지복(至福), 곧 브람의 존재 안으로 흡수되는 것이었으니, 그것은 존재라기보다 차라리 절대적인 안식이었소.\"\n\n힌두인은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말을 이었다. \"브라만의 삶에서 첫째 계급이라 불리는 부분은 학생의 삶이오. 내가 둘째 계급으로 들어갈 채비가 되었을 때—다시 말해 혼인하여 가장이 될 채비가 되었을 때—나는 모든 것을, 심지어 브람까지도 의심했소. 나는 이단자였소. 우물 깊은 곳에서 나는 위쪽의 빛을 발견했고, 올라가서 그 빛이 무엇을 비추는지 보고 싶어 갈망했소. 마침내—아, 얼마나 오랜 세월의 수고 끝이었던가!—나는 완전한 대낮 속에 서서, 생명의 원리이며 종교의 본질이며 영혼과 신을 잇는 고리인 것을 보았소. 바로 사랑이었소!\"\n\n선한 이의 야윈 얼굴이 눈에 띄게 환해졌고, 그는 두 손을 힘주어 맞잡았다. 침묵이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 다른 이들은 그를 바라보았고, 그리스인은 눈물 너머로 그를 보았다. 이윽고 그가 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"사랑의 행복은 행함에 있고, 그 시험은 남을 위해 무엇을 기꺼이 하려느냐에 있소. 나는 쉴 수가 없었소. 브람은 세상을 그토록 많은 비참함으로 채워 놓았소. 수드라가 내게 호소했고, 헤아릴 수 없는 신도들과 희생자들도 그러했소. 강가 라고르 섬은 갠지스의 거룩한 물이 인도양으로 사라지는 곳에 있소. 나는 그곳으로 갔소. 거기 현자 카필라를 기려 세운 사원의 그늘에서, 그 거룩한 분의 성스러운 기억이 그의 집 둘레에 머물게 한 제자들과 기도를 함께하며, 나는 안식을 찾으리라 여겼소. 그러나 해마다 두 차례씩 힌두인들의 순례가 이어졌으니, 그 물의 정화를 구하는 자들이었소. 그들의 비참함이 내 사랑을 더 굳세게 했소. 말하려는 충동에 맞서 나는 이를 악물었소. 브람이나 삼위나 샤스트라에 거스르는 한마디면 나는 파멸할 터였고, 이따금 타는 모래밭으로 기어 나와 죽어 가는 추방된 브라만들에게 베푸는 단 한 번의 친절—축복의 말 한마디, 물 한 잔—이면 나도 그들 가운데 하나가 되어 가족과 나라와 특권과 계급을 잃을 터였소. 사랑이 이겼소! 나는 사원의 제자들에게 말했고, 그들은 나를 내쫓았소. 나는 순례자들에게 말했고, 그들은 나를 돌로 쳐 섬에서 몰아냈소. 큰길에서 나는 설교를 하려 했으나, 듣던 이들은 내게서 달아나거나 내 목숨을 노렸소. 끝내 온 인도에는 내가 평안이나 안전을 찾을 곳이 한 군데도 없었소—추방자들 사이에서조차 그러했으니, 그들은 비록 타락했어도 여전히 브람의 신자들이었소. 막다른 처지에서 나는 신 말고는 모든 것으로부터 몸을 숨길 고독을 찾았소. 나는 갠지스를 거슬러 그 발원지까지, 저 멀리 히말라야 높은 곳까지 따라갔소. 더럽혀지지 않은 순수함을 지닌 강물이 진흙투성이 저지대를 향해 그 물길로 뛰어드는 하르드와르의 협곡으로 들어섰을 때, 나는 내 민족을 위해 기도했고, 그들로부터 영영 멀어졌다고 여겼소. 골짜기를 지나고 절벽을 넘고 빙하를 건너고 별처럼 높아 보이는 봉우리들을 거쳐, 나는 랑 초에 이르렀으니, 그것은 더없이 아름다운 호수로, 티세 강그리와 구를라와 카일라스 파르보트의 발치에서 잠들어 있었소. 이 거인들은 해를 마주하고 영원토록 눈의 왕관을 뽐내고 있소. 그곳, 인더스와 갠지스와 브라마푸트라가 솟아올라 저마다 다른 물길로 흘러가는 곳, 인류가 처음으로 거처를 정했다가 세상을 채우려 흩어진 곳, 도시들의 어머니인 발크를 남겨 그 위대한 사실을 증언하게 한 곳, 자연이 태초의 모습으로 돌아가 그 광대함 속에 안전히 자리하여, 현자에게는 안전을 약속하고 추방자에게는 고독을 약속하며 그들을 부르는 곳—그곳으로 나는 가서 신과 더불어 홀로 머물며, 기도하고 금식하며 죽음을 기다렸소.\"\n\n다시 목소리가 잦아들었고, 앙상한 두 손이 간절히 맞잡혔다.\n\n\"어느 날 밤 나는 호숫가를 걸으며, 귀 기울이는 정적을 향해 말했소. '하느님은 언제 오셔서 당신의 것을 거두실까? 구원은 끝내 없는 것인가?' 그때 갑자기 물 위로 한 줄기 빛이 떨리며 빛나기 시작했소. 이내 별 하나가 떠올라 내게로 다가오더니 머리 위에 멈추었소. 그 밝음에 나는 정신이 아득해졌소. 내가 땅에 엎드려 있는 동안, 한없이 감미로운 목소리가 들려왔소. '네 사랑이 이겼노라. 인도의 아들이여, 너는 복되도다! 구원이 가까이 왔노라. 너는 땅의 먼 곳에서 온 다른 두 사람과 더불어 구세주를 보게 되리니, 그분이 오셨음을 증언하는 자가 되리라. 아침에 일어나 그들을 맞으러 가라. 그리고 너를 인도할 영(靈)께 너의 온 믿음을 두어라.'\n\n\"그때부터 그 빛이 나와 함께 머물렀으니, 나는 그것이 그 영의 보이는 임재임을 알았소. 아침에 나는 왔던 길을 따라 세상으로 출발했소. 산의 갈라진 틈에서 나는 값이 어마어마한 보석 하나를 찾아내어, 하르드와르에서 그것을 팔았소. 라호르와 카불과 야즈드를 거쳐 나는 이스파한에 이르렀소. 그곳에서 낙타를 샀고, 거기서 다시 바그다드로 인도되었으니, 대상(隊商)을 기다리지도 않았소. 나는 홀로, 두려움 없이 길을 갔으니, 그 영이 나와 함께 있었고 지금도 함께 있기 때문이오. 형제들이여, 우리의 영광이 얼마나 큰가! 우리는 구세주를 보게 되었으니—그분께 말씀드리고—그분께 경배하게 되었소! 내 이야기는 다 마쳤소.\"\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n활기 넘치는 그리스인은 기쁨과 축하의 말을 쏟아냈다. 그 뒤를 이어 이집트인이 특유의 진중함으로 말했다.\n\n\"형제여, 그대에게 인사를 드립니다. 그대는 많은 고난을 겪었고, 나는 그대의 승리를 기뻐합니다. 두 분이 기꺼이 내 말을 들어 주신다면, 이제 내가 누구이며 어떻게 부름을 받았는지를 말씀드리겠습니다. 잠시만 기다려 주십시오.\"\n\n그는 밖으로 나가 낙타들을 돌보았다. 돌아온 그는 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"형제들이여, 그대들의 말은 성령에게서 나온 것이었습니다.\" 그가 말문을 열었다. \"그리고 그 성령이 나로 하여금 그 말을 이해하게 해 줍니다. 그대들은 저마다 자기 나라를 특별히 이야기하셨습니다. 거기에는 커다란 뜻이 담겨 있으니, 내가 그것을 풀어 드리겠습니다. 그러나 그 풀이를 온전케 하려면, 먼저 나 자신과 내 민족에 대해 말씀드리겠습니다. 나는 이집트인 발타사르입니다.\"\n\n마지막 말은 차분히 나왔으나 그 위엄이 어찌나 컸던지, 듣고 있던 두 사람은 그에게 머리를 숙였다.\n\n\"내 민족을 위해 내세울 만한 영예는 많습니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그러나 그중 하나로 만족하겠습니다. 역사는 우리에게서 비롯되었습니다. 우리는 기록을 남겨 사건을 후세에 전한 최초의 민족입니다. 그러니 우리에게는 전설이 없으며, 우리는 시(詩) 대신 확실함을 그대들에게 바칩니다. 궁전과 신전의 정면에, 오벨리스크에, 무덤의 안벽에, 우리는 우리 왕들의 이름과 그들이 한 일을 새겼습니다. 그리고 섬세한 파피루스에는 우리 철학자들의 지혜와 우리 종교의 비밀을 맡겼습니다. 다만 한 가지 비밀은 예외였으니, 그것은 곧 말씀드리겠습니다. 멜키오르여, 그것은 파라브라흐마의 베다나 비아사의 우팡가스보다 오래되었으며, 나의 가스파르여, 호메로스의 노래나 플라톤의 형이상학보다 오래되었고, 중국 백성의 거룩한 책이나 왕들의 기록보다, 아름다운 마야의 아들 싯다르타의 기록보다 오래되었으며, 히브리인 모셰의 창세기보다 오래되었습니다. 인간이 남긴 가장 오래된 기록은 우리의 첫 왕 메네스의 글입니다.\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추고, 크고 검은 눈을 그리스인에게 다정히 고정한 채 말했다. \"헬라스의 젊은 시절에, 오 가스파르여, 그 스승들의 스승은 누구였습니까?\"\n\n그리스인은 미소 지으며 머리를 숙였다.\n\n\"그 기록들로 우리는 압니다.\" 발타사르가 말을 이었다. \"조상들이 머나먼 동방에서, 세 거룩한 강이 발원하는 곳에서, 대지의 한복판에서—멜키오르여, 그대가 말한 그 옛 이란에서—왔다는 것을. 그들은 노아의 아들들이 아리아인에게 전해 준 대홍수 이전 세계의 역사와 대홍수 자체의 역사를 가지고 와서, 창조주이자 시초이신 하느님과, 하느님처럼 죽지 않는 영혼을 가르쳤습니다. 지금 우리를 부르는 의무를 무사히 마친 뒤, 그대들이 나와 함께 가기를 원한다면, 나는 우리 사제단의 거룩한 서고를 보여 드리겠습니다. 그중에는 사자의 서(書)도 있으니, 그것은 죽음이 영혼을 심판의 길로 떠나보낸 뒤 영혼이 지켜야 할 의식을 담고 있습니다. 하느님과 불멸의 영혼이라는 그 사상은 사막을 건너 미즈라임에게 전해졌고, 그를 통해 나일강 기슭에 이르렀습니다. 그때 그 사상은 순수한 상태였고, 이해하기 쉬웠으니, 우리의 행복을 위한 하느님의 뜻이 늘 그러하듯 그러했습니다. 최초의 예배 또한 그러했으니, 기쁘고 희망에 차 자기 창조주를 사랑하는 영혼에게 자연스레 우러나는 노래 한 가락, 기도 한 마디였습니다.\"\n\n여기서 그리스인이 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"오! 내 안의 빛이 깊어집니다!\"\n\n\"내 안에서도!\" 힌두인도 똑같은 열정으로 말했다.\n\n이집트인은 그들을 인자하게 바라본 다음, 말을 이어 갔다. \"종교란 다만 사람을 그 창조주에게 묶어 주는 법(法)입니다. 순수한 상태에서는 오직 이 요소들만을 갖습니다—하느님, 영혼, 그리고 둘 사이의 서로 알아봄. 그것이 실천에 옮겨질 때 예배와 사랑과 보상이 솟아납니다. 이 법은, 신적인 기원을 지닌 다른 모든 법과 마찬가지로—이를테면 대지를 태양에 묶어 두는 법처럼—그 창조자에 의해 태초에 완성되었습니다. 형제들이여, 최초의 가족이 지닌 종교가 바로 그러했고, 우리 조상 미즈라임의 종교가 그러했습니다. 그는 창조의 형식에 결코 눈멀 수 없었으니, 그 형식은 최초의 신앙과 가장 이른 예배에서만큼 또렷이 드러나는 곳이 없습니다. 완전함은 곧 하느님이요, 단순함은 곧 완전함입니다. 저주 중의 저주는, 사람들이 이러한 진리들을 그대로 내버려 두지 않는다는 것입니다.\"\n\n그는 어떻게 말을 이어 갈지 헤아리듯 멈추었다.\n\n\"많은 민족이 나일강의 단물을 사랑했습니다.\" 그가 다음으로 말했다. \"에티오피아인, 팔리푸트라인, 히브리인, 아시리아인, 페르시아인, 마케도니아인, 로마인—히브리인을 빼고는 이들 모두가 한때 그 강의 주인이었습니다. 이토록 많은 민족이 오고 가니 오래된 미즈라임 신앙은 더럽혀졌습니다. 종려나무의 골짜기는 신들의 골짜기가 되었습니다. 지존하신 한 분이 여덟으로 나뉘었으니, 저마다 자연의 창조 원리 하나씩을 의인화하고, 그 우두머리에는 암몬레가 섰습니다. 그러고는 물과 불과 공기와 그 밖의 힘들을 나타내는 이시스와 오시리스와 그 무리가 만들어졌습니다. 그래도 증식은 멈추지 않아, 마침내 힘과 지식과 사랑 따위의 인간적 자질에서 비롯된 또 다른 무리가 생겨났습니다.\"\n\n\"그 모든 것에 그 오래된 어리석음이 깃들어 있었군요!\" 그리스인이 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"손이 닿지 않는 것들만이 우리에게 온 그대로 남아 있습니다.\"\n\n이집트인은 머리를 숙이고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"조금만 더, 오 형제들이여, 조금만 더 가겠습니다, 나 자신에 이르기 전에. 우리가 가서 만날 것은, 지금 있는 것과 지금까지 있었던 것에 견주어 한층 더 거룩해 보일 것입니다. 기록에 따르면 미즈라임은 에티오피아인이 나일강을 차지하고 있는 것을 발견했으니, 그들은 그곳에서부터 아프리카 사막 전역으로 퍼져 있었습니다. 풍요롭고 환상적인 천재성을 지닌 민족으로, 오로지 자연 숭배에 몸을 바친 자들이었습니다. 시적인 페르시아인은 자기 신 오르무즈드의 가장 온전한 형상으로서 태양에 제물을 바쳤고, 머나먼 동방의 경건한 자녀들은 나무와 상아로 자기 신들을 깎아 냈습니다. 그러나 에티오피아인은 글도, 책도, 어떤 종류의 기계적 재능도 없이, 짐승과 새와 벌레를 숭배하여 제 영혼을 달랬으니, 고양이를 레에게, 황소를 이시스에게, 풍뎅이를 프타에게 바쳐 거룩히 여겼습니다. 그들의 거친 신앙에 맞선 오랜 투쟁은 그것을 새 제국의 종교로 받아들이는 것으로 끝났습니다. 그리하여 강기슭과 사막을 가득 메운 거대한 기념물들이 솟아올랐으니—오벨리스크와 미궁과 피라미드와 왕의 무덤이, 악어의 무덤과 뒤섞여 들어섰습니다. 형제들이여, 아리아인의 자손들이 그토록 깊은 타락에 떨어졌던 것입니다!\"\n\n여기서 처음으로 이집트인의 침착함이 그를 떠났다. 그의 표정은 흔들림이 없었으나, 그 목소리가 무너졌다.\n\n\"내 동포를 너무 멸시하지는 말아 주십시오.\" 그가 다시 말문을 열었다. \"그들이 모두 하느님을 잊은 것은 아니었습니다. 조금 전에 내가 말씀드린 것을 기억하실 텐데, 한 가지를 빼고는 우리 종교의 모든 비밀을 파피루스에 맡겼다고 했습니다. 이제 그 한 가지를 말씀드리겠습니다. 우리에게는 한때 어떤 파라오가 왕으로 있었으니, 그는 온갖 변화와 첨가에 몸을 바쳤습니다. 새로운 체계를 세우려고, 그는 옛것을 사람들의 마음에서 완전히 몰아내려 애썼습니다. 그때 히브리인들은 노예로서 우리와 함께 살고 있었습니다. 그들은 자기네 하느님께 매달렸으며, 박해가 견딜 수 없게 되었을 때, 결코 잊지 못할 방식으로 구원을 받았습니다. 나는 이제 기록에서 말씀드립니다. 모셰는 스스로 히브리인이었는데, 궁전에 와서, 그때 수백만에 이르던 노예들이 그 나라를 떠나도록 허락해 달라고 요구했습니다. 그 요구는 이스라엘의 주 하느님의 이름으로 한 것이었습니다. 파라오는 거절했습니다. 그다음에 무슨 일이 따랐는지 들어 보십시오. 먼저, 모든 물이, 호수와 강의 물이며 우물과 그릇의 물이, 피로 변했습니다. 그래도 군주는 거절했습니다. 그러자 개구리가 올라와 온 땅을 뒤덮었습니다. 그래도 그는 굳건했습니다. 그러자 모셰가 재를 공중에 뿌리니, 역병이 이집트인들을 덮쳤습니다. 다음으로, 히브리인의 것을 빼고는 모든 가축이 쓰러져 죽었습니다. 메뚜기가 골짜기의 푸른 것들을 갉아먹었습니다. 한낮에 대낮이 어둠으로 바뀌니, 그 어둠이 어찌나 짙던지 등불도 타지 못했습니다. 마침내 밤이 되자 이집트인의 맏이가 모두 죽었으니, 파라오의 맏이조차 화를 면하지 못했습니다. 그제야 그는 굴복했습니다. 그러나 히브리인들이 떠나자, 그는 군대를 이끌고 그들을 뒤쫓았습니다. 마지막 순간에 바다가 갈라져, 도망치는 자들이 마른 발로 그것을 건넜습니다. 뒤쫓던 자들이 그들을 따라 몰려들자, 물결이 다시 밀려와 말과 보병과 전차병과 왕을 모두 빠뜨려 죽였습니다. 가스파르여, 그대가 계시를 말씀하셨지요—\"\n\n그리스인의 파란 눈이 반짝였다.\n\n\"나는 그 이야기를 유대인에게서 들었습니다.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"오 발타사르여, 그대가 그것을 확인해 주는군요!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 그러나 나를 통해 말하는 것은 이집트이지 모셰가 아닙니다. 나는 대리석을 풀이하는 것입니다. 그 시대의 사제들이 자기들 방식으로 자기들이 목격한 것을 적었고, 그리하여 그 계시가 살아남았습니다. 그래서 나는 기록되지 않은 그 한 가지 비밀에 이릅니다. 형제들이여, 내 나라에서는 그 불행한 파라오의 시대부터 늘 두 가지 종교를 가져 왔습니다—하나는 사사로운 것이요, 다른 하나는 공적인 것이며, 하나는 많은 신을 섬겨 백성이 따르는 것이요, 다른 하나는 한 분 하느님을 섬겨 오직 사제단만이 소중히 간직한 것입니다. 형제들이여, 나와 함께 기뻐하십시오! 그 많은 민족의 짓밟음도, 왕들의 괴롭힘도, 원수들의 온갖 술책도, 세월의 온갖 변화도, 모두 헛되었습니다. 산 밑에서 제 때를 기다리는 씨앗처럼, 영광스러운 진리는 살아남았으니, 바로 이날이—이날이 그 진리의 날입니다!\"\n\n힌두인의 야윈 몸이 기쁨으로 떨렸고, 그리스인은 소리 높여 외쳤다.\n\n\"내게는 바로 저 사막이 노래하는 것만 같습니다.\"\n\n이집트인은 가까이 있는 물주전자에서 한 모금 마시고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 알렉산드리아에서 왕자이자 사제로 태어나, 내 계급에 으레 주어지는 교육을 받았습니다. 그러나 아주 일찍부터 나는 불만을 품게 되었습니다. 강요된 신앙의 한 부분은, 죽음 뒤 육신이 무너지면 영혼이 곧장 가장 낮은 데서부터 가장 높고 마지막 존재인 인간에 이르기까지 그 옛 진보를 다시 시작한다는 것이었으며, 그것이 죽을 목숨의 삶에서 한 행실과는 아무 관계가 없다는 것이었습니다. 페르시아인의 빛의 나라, 곧 오직 선한 자만이 가는 친바트 다리 너머의 낙원에 대해 들었을 때, 그 생각이 나를 사로잡았습니다. 그리하여 낮에도 밤에도, 나는 영원한 윤회와 천국에서의 영원한 삶이라는, 서로 견주어지는 두 사상을 곱씹었습니다. 내 스승이 가르친 대로 하느님이 의로우시다면, 어찌하여 선한 자와 악한 자 사이에 아무런 구별이 없는가? 마침내 내게는 분명해졌으니, 확실해졌으니, 내가 순수한 종교를 귀결시킨 그 법의 따름정리로서, 죽음이란 다만 갈라지는 지점일 뿐이라는 것이었습니다. 그 지점에서 악한 자는 버려지거나 길을 잃고, 신실한 자는 더 높은 삶으로 올라갑니다. 멜키오르여, 그것은 부처의 열반도, 브라흐마의 소극적인 안식도 아니요, 가스파르여, 올림포스 신앙이 허락하는 천국의 전부인 지옥 속의 더 나은 처지도 아니라, 삶입니다—활기차고 기쁘고 영원한 삶, 하느님과 함께하는 삶입니다! 이 발견이 또 다른 물음으로 이어졌습니다. 어찌하여 그 진리를 사제단의 이기적인 위안을 위해 더 오래 비밀로 간직해야 하는가? 그것을 감추어야 할 이유는 사라졌습니다. 철학이 적어도 우리에게 관용을 가져다주었습니다. 이집트에는 람세스 대신 로마가 있었습니다. 어느 날, 알렉산드리아에서 가장 화려하고 붐비는 구역인 브루케이온에서, 나는 일어서서 설교했습니다. 동방과 서방이 내 청중을 이루었습니다. 도서관으로 가는 학생들, 세라페이온에서 온 사제들, 무세이온에서 나온 한량들, 경기장의 단골들, 라코티스에서 온 시골 사람들—한 무리가 멈춰 서서 내 말을 들었습니다. 나는 하느님과 영혼과 옳고 그름과, 덕 있는 삶의 보상인 천국을 설교했습니다. 멜키오르여, 그대는 돌에 맞았지만, 내 청중은 처음에는 놀라더니 이내 비웃었습니다. 나는 다시 시도했고, 그들은 경구로 나를 두들기고, 내 하느님을 조롱으로 뒤덮고, 내 천국을 비웃음으로 어둡게 했습니다. 쓸데없이 길게 말하지 않겠으니, 나는 그들 앞에서 쓰러졌습니다.\"\n\n여기서 힌두인이 긴 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"사람의 원수는 사람입니다, 형제여.\"\n\n발타사르는 침묵에 잠겼다.\n\n\"나는 내 실패의 원인을 찾는 데 많은 생각을 쏟았고, 마침내 찾아냈습니다.\" 그가 다시 말문을 열며 말했다. \"강을 거슬러, 도시에서 하룻길 떨어진 곳에, 목자와 정원사들의 마을이 하나 있습니다. 나는 배를 타고 그곳에 갔습니다. 저녁에 나는 사람들을 불러 모았으니, 남자와 여자, 가난한 자 중에서도 가장 가난한 자들이었습니다. 나는 브루케이온에서 설교했던 것과 똑같이 그들에게 설교했습니다. 그들은 비웃지 않았습니다. 이튿날 저녁 나는 다시 말했고, 그들은 믿고 기뻐하며, 그 소식을 멀리 퍼뜨렸습니다. 세 번째 모임에서는 기도를 위한 모임이 만들어졌습니다. 그제야 나는 도시로 돌아왔습니다. 일찍이 그토록 밝고 가깝게 보인 적 없던 별들 아래, 강을 따라 떠내려가며, 나는 이런 가르침을 얻었습니다. 개혁을 시작하려거든, 위대하고 부유한 자들의 자리로 가지 말라. 차라리 행복의 잔이 비어 있는 자들에게로 가라—가난하고 비천한 자들에게로. 그러고 나서 나는 계획을 세우고 내 삶을 바쳤습니다. 첫걸음으로, 나는 내 막대한 재산을 확보하여, 그 수입이 확실하고 고통받는 자들을 구제하는 데 언제든 쓰일 수 있게 했습니다. 그날부터, 형제들이여, 나는 나일강을 오르내리며, 마을마다, 모든 부족을 찾아가, 한 분 하느님과, 의로운 삶과, 천국에서의 보상을 설교했습니다. 나는 선한 일을 했습니다—얼마나 많이 했는지를 내 입으로 말하는 것은 어울리지 않습니다. 또한 나는 세상의 그 부분이 우리가 찾아 나선 그분을 맞이할 만큼 무르익었음을 압니다.\"\n\n설교자의 거무스름한 뺨에 홍조가 번졌으나, 그는 그 감정을 누르고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그렇게 바쳐진 세월은, 오 형제들이여, 한 가지 생각으로 괴로웠습니다—내가 떠나고 나면, 내가 시작한 그 대의는 어찌 될 것인가? 그것이 나와 함께 끝나야 하는가? 나는 내 일에 걸맞은 면류관으로서의 조직을 여러 번 꿈꾸었습니다. 그대들에게 아무것도 숨기지 않겠으니, 나는 그것을 이루려 애썼으나 실패했습니다. 형제들이여, 세상은 지금 이런 형편에 있습니다. 옛 미즈라임 신앙을 되살리려면, 개혁자는 인간을 넘어서는 권능을 가져야 합니다. 그는 단지 하느님의 이름으로 와서는 안 되며, 자기 말에 종속된 증거를 지녀야 하고, 자기가 말하는 모든 것을, 심지어 하느님까지도 입증해 보여야 합니다. 사람들의 마음은 신화와 체계에 어찌나 사로잡혀 있는지, 거짓 신들이 모든 자리—땅과 공기와 하늘—를 어찌나 가득 메우고 있는지, 그것들이 만물의 한 부분이 되어 버렸으니, 최초의 종교로 돌아가는 길은 오직 피로 물든 길을 따라, 박해의 들판을 지나야만 갈 수 있습니다. 다시 말해, 개종자들은 신앙을 버리느니 차라리 죽기를 마다하지 않아야 합니다. 그런데 이 시대에 사람들의 신앙을 그러한 경지까지 이끌 수 있는 이가 하느님 자신 말고 누가 있겠습니까? 인류를 구속하려면—나는 인류를 멸하려는 것이 아닙니다—인류를 구속하려면, 그분은 다시 한번 스스로를 드러내셔야 합니다. 그분은 몸소 오셔야 합니다.\"\n\n세 사람을 격렬한 감정이 사로잡았다.\n\n\"우리가 그분을 찾으러 가는 것이 아닙니까?\" 그리스인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 조직을 이루려는 시도에서 왜 실패했는지를 이제 그대들은 이해할 것입니다.\" 그 황홀경이 가시자 이집트인이 말했다. \"내게는 권능이 없었습니다. 내 일이 사라질 수밖에 없음을 안다는 것이 나를 견딜 수 없이 비참하게 만들었습니다. 나는 기도를 믿었고, 내 호소를 순수하고 강하게 하려고, 형제들이여 그대들처럼, 나는 사람이 다닌 길을 벗어났습니다. 나는 사람이 가 본 적 없는 곳, 오직 하느님만 계신 곳으로 갔습니다. 다섯 번째 폭포 너머로, 센나르에서 강들이 만나는 곳 너머로, 바르 엘 아비아드를 거슬러, 아프리카의 머나먼 미지의 땅으로, 나는 갔습니다. 그곳에서는, 아침이 되면, 하늘처럼 푸른 산 하나가 서쪽 사막 위로 시원한 그늘을 넓게 드리우고, 녹은 눈이 폭포를 이루어, 그 동쪽 기슭에 깃든 너른 호수 하나를 먹입니다. 그 호수가 그 큰 강의 어머니입니다. 일 년 남짓 그 산이 내게 보금자리가 되어 주었습니다. 종려나무 열매가 내 몸을 먹였고, 기도가 내 영혼을 먹였습니다. 어느 날 밤, 나는 그 작은 바다 가까이의 과수원을 거닐었습니다. '세상이 죽어 갑니다. 그대는 언제 오시렵니까? 어찌하여 나는 그 구속을 보지 못합니까, 오 하느님?' 나는 그렇게 기도했습니다. 유리 같은 물이 별들로 반짝이고 있었습니다. 그중 하나가 제 자리를 떠나 수면 위로 떠오르는 듯하더니, 거기서 눈을 태울 듯한 광채가 되었습니다. 그러고는 내게로 다가와 내 머리 위에 멈추었으니, 보아하니 손이 닿을 만큼 가까웠습니다. 나는 엎드려 얼굴을 가렸습니다. 이 땅의 것이 아닌 목소리가 말했습니다. '너의 선한 일이 이겼노라. 복되도다, 오 미즈라임의 아들이여! 구속이 오느니라. 세상의 머나먼 곳에서 온 다른 두 사람과 함께, 너는 구세주를 보고, 그를 위해 증언하리라. 아침이 되거든 일어나, 그들을 맞으러 가라. 그리고 너희가 모두 거룩한 도성 예루살렘에 이르거든, 백성에게 물으라, 유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐? 우리가 동방에서 그의 별을 보았고, 그에게 경배하러 보내심을 받았노라, 하라. 너를 인도할 성령께 모든 믿음을 두라.'\n\n\"그리고 그 빛은 의심할 수 없는 내면의 빛이 되어, 다스리는 자요 인도하는 자로서 내게 머물러 왔습니다. 그 빛이 나를 강을 따라 멤피스로 이끌었고, 그곳에서 나는 사막을 건널 채비를 했습니다. 나는 내 낙타를 샀고, 쉬지 않고, 수에즈와 쿠필레를 거쳐, 모압과 암몬의 땅을 거슬러 올라 이곳까지 왔습니다. 하느님이 우리와 함께 계십니다, 오 형제들이여!\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추었고, 그러자 그들 자신의 것이 아닌 어떤 이끄심에 따라, 세 사람은 모두 일어나 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"우리가 우리 민족과 그 역사를 그토록 자세히 묘사한 데에는 한 가지 뜻이 있다고 말씀드렸습니다.\" 이집트인이 그렇게 말을 이었다. \"우리가 찾으러 가는 그분은 '유대인의 왕'이라 불렸으니, 우리는 그 이름으로 그분을 물으라는 명을 받았습니다. 그러나 이제 우리가 만나, 서로에게서 들었으니, 우리는 그분이 유대인만의 구세주가 아니라, 땅 위 모든 민족의 구세주이심을 알 수 있습니다. 대홍수에서 살아남은 족장은 세 아들과 그들의 가족을 거느렸고, 그들로 말미암아 세상에 다시 사람이 가득 찼습니다. 아시아의 심장부에 있는, 잘 기억되는 환희의 땅, 그 옛 아리아나바에조에서 그들은 갈라졌습니다. 인도와 머나먼 동방은 첫째의 자손을 받아들였고, 막내의 자손은 북쪽을 거쳐 유럽으로 흘러들었으며, 둘째의 자손은 홍해 주변의 사막으로 넘쳐흘러 아프리카로 들어갔습니다. 비록 그 둘째의 자손 대부분은 아직도 옮겨 다니는 천막에서 사는 자들이지만, 그중 일부는 나일강을 따라 건설하는 자가 되었습니다.\"\n\n동시에 솟구친 충동에 따라 세 사람은 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"이보다 더 신의 뜻에 따라 마련된 일이 어디 있겠습니까?\" 발타사르가 말을 이었다. \"우리가 주님을 찾으면, 그 형제들과, 그들의 뒤를 이은 모든 세대가, 우리와 더불어 그분께 무릎 꿇어 경배할 것입니다. 그리고 우리가 헤어져 저마다 제 길로 갈 때, 세상은 새로운 가르침을 배우게 될 것입니다—천국은 칼로도, 인간의 지혜로도 아니라, 믿음과 사랑과 선한 일로 얻을 수 있다는 가르침을.\"\n\n침묵이 흘렀으니, 한숨으로 깨어지고 눈물로 거룩해진 침묵이었다. 그들을 가득 채운 기쁨을 막을 수 없었기 때문이다. 그것은 생명의 강 기슭에 선 영혼들이, 구속받은 자들과 더불어 하느님의 앞에서 쉬며 누리는, 형언할 수 없는 기쁨이었다.\n\n이윽고 그들의 손이 풀어지고, 그들은 함께 천막 밖으로 나갔다. 사막은 하늘처럼 고요했다. 해가 빠르게 지고 있었다. 낙타들은 잠들어 있었다.\n\n조금 뒤에 천막이 걷히고, 남은 음식과 함께 가마에 다시 실렸다. 그러고 나서 벗들은 올라타, 이집트인을 앞세우고 한 줄로 길을 떠났다. 그들이 잡은 길은 정서(正西)였으니, 쌀쌀한 밤 속으로 들어가는 길이었다. 낙타들은 한결같은 종종걸음으로 앞으로 흔들흔들 나아가며, 줄과 간격을 어찌나 정확히 지키는지, 뒤따르는 자들이 앞선 자의 발자국을 그대로 밟는 것만 같았다. 탄 이들은 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n이윽고 달이 떠올랐다. 그리고 키 큰 흰 형체 셋이 유백색 빛 속을 소리 없는 걸음으로 내달릴 때, 그들은 가증스러운 그림자에서 달아나는 유령들처럼 보였다. 문득, 그들 앞 허공에서, 낮은 언덕 꼭대기보다 높지 않은 곳에서 너울거리는 불꽃이 타올랐다. 그들이 그것을 바라보자, 그 환영이 눈부신 광채의 한 점으로 오므라들었다. 그들의 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었고, 그들의 영혼이 떨렸다. 그리고 그들은 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"별이다! 별이다! 하느님이 우리와 함께 계신다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n예루살렘 서쪽 성벽의 한 어귀에는 베들레헴 문 또는 욥바 문이라 불리는 \"떡갈나무 문짝\"이 걸려 있다. 그 바깥의 일대는 이 도시에서 손꼽히는 명소다. 다윗이 시온을 탐내기 훨씬 전부터 그곳에는 성채가 있었다. 마침내 이새의 아들이 여부스 사람을 몰아내고 성을 쌓기 시작했을 때, 그 성채가 있던 자리는 새 성벽의 북서쪽 모퉁이가 되었고, 옛 망루보다 훨씬 위풍당당한 탑이 그곳을 지켰다. 그러나 문의 위치만은 건드리지 않았으니, 그 앞에서 만나 하나로 합쳐지는 길들을 다른 자리로 옮기기 어려웠던 데다, 바깥 일대가 이미 알아주는 장터로 자리 잡았기 때문일 것이다. 솔로몬 시대에는 이곳에 큰 거래가 오갔고, 이집트의 상인들과 티레와 시돈의 부유한 장사꾼들도 한몫 끼었다. 그로부터 거의 삼천 년이 흘렀건만, 여전히 일종의 상업이 이 자리에 매여 있다. 순례자가 핀이나 권총을, 오이나 낙타를, 집이나 말을, 돈이나 렌즈콩을, 대추야자나 통역을, 멜론이나 일꾼을, 비둘기나 나귀를 구하려거든, 욥바 문에서 그 물건을 찾기만 하면 된다. 때로는 그 풍경이 자못 활기차서, 헤롯 대왕의 건설 시대에는 이 옛 장터가 얼마나 대단한 곳이었을지 짐작하게 한다. 이제 독자를 그 시대와 그 장터로 옮겨 가려 한다.\n\n히브리 사람의 셈법을 따르면, 앞 장들에서 묘사한 현자들의 만남은 그해 셋째 달 스무닷새 날 오후에 일어났다. 곧 십이월 스무닷새 날이다. 그해는 제193회 올림피아드의 두 번째 해, 곧 로마 건국 747년이며, 헤롯 대왕의 예순일곱 번째 해이자 그 치세의 서른다섯 번째 해요, 기독교 기원이 시작되기 네 해 전이었다. 유대의 관습으로는 하루의 시간을 해와 더불어 헤아려, 첫째 시는 해 뜬 뒤의 첫 시간이다. 그러니 정확히 말하면, 말한 그날 첫째 시 동안 욥바 문의 장터는 한창 무르익어 몹시 흥성거렸다. 육중한 문짝은 동틀 녘부터 활짝 열려 있었다. 늘 거침없는 장사판은 아치형 입구를 지나 좁은 골목과 마당으로 밀고 들어가, 거대한 탑의 벽을 끼고 돌아 도시 안으로 이어졌다. 예루살렘은 산악 지대에 자리한지라, 이날 아침 공기는 적잖이 쌀쌀했다. 온기를 약속하는 햇살은 주위에 솟은 거대한 건물들의 흉벽과 작은 탑 위에 약 올리듯 한참을 머물러 있었고, 그 위에서는 비둘기들의 구구거리는 소리와 무리지어 오가는 새 떼의 날갯짓 소리가 흘러내렸다.\n\n이어지는 몇 쪽을 이해하려면 거룩한 도시의 사람들과, 거주민뿐 아니라 나그네들과도 얼마간 안면을 트는 일이 필요할 터이니, 잠시 문 앞에 멈추어 이 풍경을 찬찬히 살펴보는 것이 좋겠다. 머지않아 지금과는 사뭇 다른 심정으로 나아가게 될 이 군중을 볼 기회는 이보다 더 좋게 오지 않을 것이다.\n\n그 광경은 처음에는 더없는 혼란일 뿐이다. 움직임의 혼란, 소리의 혼란, 빛깔의 혼란, 물건의 혼란이다. 골목과 마당이 특히 그러하다. 그곳 바닥에는 모양을 다듬지 않은 넓적한 돌들이 깔려 있어, 온갖 외침과 덜그럭 소리와 발굽 구르는 소리가 그 위에서 솟아올라, 묵직하게 드리운 벽들 사이로 울리고 으르렁대는 뒤범벅에 보태진다. 그러나 무리에 얼마간 섞이고 그곳에서 오가는 거래에 얼마간 익숙해지면, 비로소 그 갈래를 가려낼 수 있다.\n\n이쪽에는 나귀 한 마리가 갈릴리의 정원과 비탈밭에서 갓 따 온 렌즈콩과 강낭콩, 양파, 오이를 가득 실은 광주리 아래서 졸고 있다. 손님을 맞지 않을 때면 주인은 아는 자만이 알아들을 수 있는 목소리로 제 물건을 외친다. 그 차림새보다 더 간소한 것은 없으니, 샌들 한 켤레에다, 표백도 물도 들이지 않은 담요 한 장을 한쪽 어깨에 걸쳐 허리에 둘러맸을 뿐이다. 그 가까이에는, 나귀만큼 진득하지는 못하나 훨씬 위압적이고 기괴한 낙타 한 마리가 꿇어앉아 있다. 뼈가 앙상하고 거칠며 잿빛인 그 짐승은 목과 멱과 몸통 아래로 여우 빛깔의 길고 텁수룩한 털 다발이 늘어졌고, 거대한 안장 위에는 상자와 바구니 짐이 묘하게 얹혀 있다. 주인은 이집트 사람으로, 자그마하고 날렵하며, 길의 먼지와 사막의 모래를 적잖이 빌려 온 듯한 낯빛이다. 그는 빛바랜 타르부시를 쓰고, 소매도 허리띠도 없이 목에서 무릎까지 흘러내리는 헐렁한 겉옷을 걸쳤다. 그의 발은 맨발이다. 낙타는 짐 밑에서 가만있지 못하고 끙끙거리며 이따금 이빨을 드러내지만, 사내는 모는 끈을 쥔 채 무심히 이리저리 거닐며, 키드론의 과수원에서 갓 따 온 과일을 줄곧 외쳐 댄다. 포도, 대추야자, 무화과, 사과, 석류다.\n\n골목이 마당으로 트이는 모퉁이에는 여인 몇이 잿빛 성벽 돌에 등을 기대고 앉아 있다. 그들의 옷은 이 나라의 천한 계층이 흔히 입는 차림으로, 발끝까지 닿는 아마포 옷자락을 허리에서 헐겁게 여미고, 머리를 덮고도 어깨까지 두를 만큼 넓은 너울 혹은 두건을 썼다. 그들의 상품은 동방에서 지금도 우물물을 길어 오는 데 쓰는 여러 질그릇 항아리와 가죽 부대 몇에 담겨 있다. 그 항아리와 부대 사이로, 돌바닥을 구르며, 군중도 추위도 아랑곳없이, 늘 위태롭되 한 번도 다친 적 없이, 반쯤 헐벗은 아이 대여섯이 뛰논다. 갈색 몸뚱이와 새까만 눈동자, 숱 많은 검은 머리칼이 이스라엘의 혈통을 증언한다. 이따금 두건 아래로 어머니들이 고개를 들어, 그 고장 말로 다소곳이 제 장사를 권한다. 부대에는 \"포도꿀\"이요, 항아리에는 \"독한 술\"이다. 그들의 권하는 소리는 흔히 두루 울리는 소란에 묻혀 버리고, 수많은 경쟁자들 틈에서 형편이 옹색하다. 다리를 드러내고 더러운 웃옷을 걸친 수염 긴 억센 사내들이 부대를 등에 동여매고 돌아다니며 \"포도주 꿀이오! 엔게디의 포도라오!\" 하고 외쳐 댄다. 손님이 그중 하나를 멈춰 세우면, 사내는 부대를 휙 돌려 주둥이에서 엄지를 떼고, 그러면 달큰한 열매의 짙붉은 피가 받쳐 든 잔으로 콸콸 쏟아진다.\n\n새 장수들의 외침도 그에 못지않게 요란하다. 비둘기, 오리, 더러는 노래하는 불불 곧 밤꾀꼬리, 그리고 가장 흔하게는 집비둘기다. 그물에서 새를 건네받는 손님들은, 절벽을 대담히 기어오르는 사냥꾼들의 위험천만한 삶을 떠올리지 않을 수 없다. 그들은 손발로 바위 벼랑에 매달리기도 하고, 광주리에 실려 산골짜기 틈으로 까마득히 내려가기도 한다.\n\n장신구 행상들이 한데 어울려 있다. 진홍과 푸른빛 외투를 걸치고 엄청나게 큰 흰 터번 아래 머리가 무거운 영악한 사내들로, 리본의 윤기와 금붙이의 날카로운 광채에 깃든 위력을 익히 아는 자들이다. 팔찌든 목걸이든, 손가락이나 코에 끼우는 고리든 매한가지다. 그 사이로 살림 그릇 행상이며, 입을 옷을 파는 장수며, 몸에 바르는 향유를 파는 자며, 긴요한 것이든 멋 부리는 것이든 온갖 물건을 파는 잡상인들이 섞여 있다. 그리고 이리저리, 고삐와 밧줄을 잡아당기며, 때로는 악을 쓰고 때로는 어르며, 짐승 장수들이 애를 쓴다. 나귀, 말, 송아지, 양, 매에 우는 새끼 염소, 그리고 어기적거리는 낙타들이다. 금기시된 돼지만 빼고 온갖 짐승이 다 있다. 이 모든 것이 거기 있으니, 묘사한 대로 하나씩만이 아니라 몇 번이고 거듭되고, 한자리에만이 아니라 장터 어디에나 펼쳐져 있다.\n\n골목과 마당의 이 풍경에서, 파는 자들과 그 물건들에 던진 이 눈길에서 돌아서서, 독자는 이제 찾아온 이들과 사는 이들에게 주의를 기울일 필요가 있다. 그 모습은 문밖에서 가장 잘 살필 수 있으니, 그곳의 광경 또한 못지않게 다채롭고 활기차며, 아니 어쩌면 더할는지도 모른다. 거기에는 천막과 노점과 시장이 빚어내는 정취, 더 넓은 공간, 더 큰 무리, 거리낌 없는 자유, 그리고 동방 햇살의 영광이 더해지기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n성문 곁에 자리를 잡고, 안으로 흘러드는 물결과 밖으로 빠져나가는 물결, 그 두 흐름의 가장자리에서 비켜선 채로 잠시 눈과 귀를 써 보기로 하자.\n\n마침 알맞은 때다! 저기 가장 눈여겨볼 만한 부류에 속한 두 사내가 다가온다.\n\n\"신들이여! 어찌 이리 춥단 말인가!\" 그중 한 사람이 말한다. 갑옷을 두른 다부진 체구에, 머리에는 놋쇠 투구를, 몸에는 번쩍이는 흉갑과 사슬 미늘을 늘어뜨리고 있다. \"어찌 이리 춥단 말인가! 카이우스, 자네 기억하는가, 고향의 코미티움에 있던 그 지하 묘소 말이네. 신관들이 지하 세계로 드는 입구라고 하던 곳 말일세. 플루토 신께 맹세코! 오늘 아침이라면 나는 거기 서 있을 수 있겠네. 적어도 다시 몸이 덥혀질 때까지는 말이야!\"\n\n말을 건네받은 쪽은 군용 외투의 두건을 내려 머리와 얼굴을 드러내고는, 비웃는 듯한 미소를 띤 채 대꾸한다. \"마르쿠스 안토니우스를 무찌른 군단의 투구에는 갈리아의 눈이 가득 차 있었지. 한데 자네는—아, 가엾은 친구여!—이제 막 이집트에서 왔으니, 그 여름을 핏속에 담아 온 게로군.\"\n\n그리고 그 마지막 말과 함께 두 사람은 성문 안으로 사라진다. 입을 다물고 있었다 해도, 그 갑옷과 단단한 걸음걸이만으로 그들이 로마 병사임을 알 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n뒤이어 무리 속에서 한 유대인이 나온다. 야윈 몸에 어깨가 굽었고, 거친 갈색 겉옷을 걸쳤다. 빗질하지 않은 긴 머리카락이 헝클어진 채로 두 눈과 얼굴을 덮고 등줄기까지 흘러내려 있다. 그는 홀로다. 그를 마주치는 자들은 더 험한 짓을 하지 않으면 웃음을 터뜨린다. 그가 나실인이기 때문이다. 모세의 율법서를 배척하고, 가증스러운 서원에 몸을 바치며, 그 서원이 지속되는 동안 머리를 깎지 않는, 멸시받는 종파의 일원이다.\n\n그가 물러가는 모습을 지켜보는데, 별안간 군중 속에 소란이 일고, 좌우로 빠르게 길이 갈라지면서 날카롭고 단호한 외침이 터진다. 이윽고 그 까닭이 모습을 드러낸다—이목구비와 차림새가 모두 히브리인인 한 사내다. 눈처럼 흰 아마포 망토를 노란 비단 끈으로 머리에 동여매어 두 어깨 위로 자유로이 흘러내렸고, 겉옷에는 호화로운 자수가 놓였으며, 금술이 달린 붉은 띠가 허리를 몇 겹으로 감싸고 있다. 그의 태도는 차분하다. 그토록 무례한 다급함으로 자기에게 길을 비켜 주는 자들에게 미소까지 짓는다. 나병 환자인가? 아니다, 그는 그저 사마리아인일 뿐이다. 몸을 사리며 물러서는 군중에게 물었다면, 그를 일러 잡종이라고—아시리아인이라고—할 것이니, 그자의 옷자락이 스치기만 해도 더러움이 된다고, 따라서 이스라엘인이라면 죽어 가는 처지라도 그자에게서 목숨을 받을 수 없다고 할 것이다. 사실 이 반목은 핏줄에서 비롯된 것이 아니다. 다윗이 오직 유다 지파의 지지만 받으며 이곳 시온 산에 왕좌를 세웠을 때, 나머지 열 지파는 세겜으로 떠나갔다. 세겜은 훨씬 오래된 도시였고, 그 무렵에는 거룩한 기억으로 비할 데 없이 풍요로웠다. 끝내 지파들이 다시 하나로 합쳐졌어도, 그렇게 시작된 다툼은 가라앉지 않았다. 사마리아인들은 그리심 산의 성막에 매달려, 그 우월한 신성함을 주장하면서 예루살렘의 성난 율법학자들을 비웃었다. 세월이 흘러도 그 증오는 누그러지지 않았다. 헤롯 치하에서는 사마리아인을 제외한 온 세상에 신앙으로의 개종이 열려 있었다. 오직 그들만이 유대인과의 교류에서 절대적으로, 또 영원히 배척당했다.\n\n사마리아인이 성문의 아치 밑으로 들어가는데, 우리가 지금껏 본 그 누구와도 닮은 데가 없어 우리의 시선을 좋든 싫든 붙들어 매는 세 사내가 나온다. 그들은 키가 유달리 크고 근육이 어마어마하다. 두 눈은 푸르고, 살결이 어찌나 흰지 피가 살갗 너머로 푸르스름한 줄을 그은 듯 비쳐 보인다. 머리카락은 옅은 빛깔에 짧게 깎였고, 작고 둥근 머리는 나무 줄기처럼 굵은 목 위에 반듯이 얹혀 있다. 가슴께가 트이고 소매가 없으며 헐겁게 졸라맨 모직 튜닉이 그들의 몸을 두르고 있는데, 드러난 팔다리가 어찌나 발달했는지 보는 즉시 투기장을 떠올리게 한다. 게다가 거기에 그 무심하고 거만하고 안하무인인 태도까지 더하니, 사람들이 그들에게 길을 내주고 그들이 지나간 뒤에도 발길을 멈춰 다시 쳐다보는 것이 조금도 이상하지 않다. 그들은 검투사다—씨름꾼이요, 달리기꾼이요, 권투꾼이요, 검객이다. 로마인이 들어오기 전까지 유대에서는 알려지지 않았던 직업 무사들이다. 훈련을 하지 않을 때면 왕의 정원을 거닐거나 궁전 문간에서 호위병과 함께 앉아 있는 모습이 보이는 자들이다. 어쩌면 카이사레아나 세바스테, 혹은 여리고에서 온 손님일 수도 있는데, 유대인이라기보다 그리스인에 가깝고 로마인 못지않게 경기와 피비린내 나는 구경거리를 즐기는 헤롯은 그 도시들에 거대한 극장을 지었으며, 지금은 관례대로 갈리아 속주나 다뉴브 강가의 슬라브 부족에서 끌어모은 격투사들의 양성소까지 두고 있다.\n\n\"바쿠스 신께 맹세코!\" 그중 하나가 움켜쥔 주먹을 제 어깨까지 끌어올리며 말한다. \"저것들 두개골은 달걀 껍데기보다 두껍지도 않더군.\"\n\n그 몸짓에 어린 잔인한 표정이 우리를 역겹게 하니, 우리는 기꺼이 좀 더 즐거운 무언가로 눈을 돌린다.\n\n맞은편에는 과일 좌판이 있다. 주인은 머리가 벗어졌고, 얼굴이 길며, 코는 매부리처럼 휘었다. 그는 먼지 위에 깔아 둔 양탄자에 앉아 있다. 등 뒤에는 성벽이 있고, 머리 위로는 빈약한 차일이 드리워졌으며, 둘레에는 손이 닿는 거리의 작은 걸상마다 아몬드와 포도와 무화과와 석류가 가득 담긴 버들고리 상자가 놓여 있다. 이제 그에게 한 사람이 다가오는데, 우리는 보지 않을 수 없다. 다만 검투사들에게 시선을 붙들렸던 것과는 다른 까닭에서다. 그는 정말로 아름답다—아름다운 그리스인이다. 관자놀이 둘레에는 물결치는 머리카락을 붙드는 도금양 화관을 둘렀는데, 거기에는 아직도 옅은 빛깔의 꽃과 덜 익은 열매가 매달려 있다. 진홍빛 튜닉은 더없이 부드러운 모직물로 지어졌고, 앞쪽이 번쩍이는 황금의 기묘한 장식으로 채워진 담황색 가죽띠 아래로 자락이 무릎까지 늘어지는데, 그 주름은 똑같은 왕가의 금속으로 놓인 자수로 묵직하다. 역시 모직인 흰색과 노란색이 뒤섞인 목도리가 그의 목을 가로질러 등 뒤로 늘어뜨려져 있다. 드러난 팔다리는 상아처럼 희고, 목욕과 기름과 솔과 족집게로 완벽하게 다듬지 않고는 도무지 낼 수 없는 광택을 띠고 있다.\n\n좌판 주인은 자리에 앉은 채로 몸을 앞으로 굽히고, 두 손을 들어 손바닥을 아래로 향하고 손가락을 편 채 제 앞에서 맞붙인다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에는 무얼 가져왔느냐, 파포스의 아들이여?\" 젊은 그리스인이 키프로스 사람보다 상자들 쪽을 바라보며 말한다. \"배가 고프구나. 아침거리로 무엇이 있느냐?\"\n\n\"페디우스 강가에서 난 과일이올시다—진짜배기로요—안티오크의 가수들이 상한 목청을 되살리려고 아침마다 드는 것이지요.\" 좌판 주인이 칭얼대는 콧소리로 대답한다.\n\n\"무화과나 줘 보거라, 안티오크 가수들에게는 네 으뜸가는 것이 아니어도 좋다!\" 그리스인이 말한다. \"너는 아프로디테를 섬기는 자요, 내가 두른 이 도금양이 증명하듯 나 또한 그러하다. 그러니 일러 두건대, 그자들 목소리에는 카스피 해의 바람 같은 한기가 서려 있느니라. 이 띠가 보이느냐?—드높으신 살로메께서 내리신 선물이니라—\"\n\n\"왕의 누이 되시는 분 아니십니까!\" 키프로스 사람이 다시 한번 절을 하며 외친다.\n\n\"왕가의 안목과 신묘한 판단력을 지니신 분이지. 어찌 안 그렇겠느냐? 그분은 왕보다도 더 그리스인다우시니라. 한데—내 아침이 먼저다! 여기 네 삯이 있다—키프로스의 붉은 동전이니라. 포도를 다오, 그리고—\"\n\n\"대추야자도 함께 드시지 않으렵니까?\"\n\n\"아니다, 나는 아랍인이 아니야.\"\n\n\"무화과는요?\"\n\n\"그건 나를 유대인으로 만드는 짓이지. 아니다, 포도 말고는 아무것도 필요 없다. 그리스인의 피와 포도의 피만큼 달콤하게 섞이는 물은 어디에도 없느니라.\"\n\n때 묻고 들끓는 장터에 선 그 가수는 온통 궁정의 멋을 부린 모습이라, 한번 본 사람의 머릿속에서 쉬이 지워지지 않는 광경이다. 그런데 마치 일부러 그러기라도 하듯, 우리의 온갖 경이를 자아내는 한 인물이 그의 뒤를 따른다. 그는 얼굴을 땅으로 향한 채 천천히 길을 올라온다. 이따금 걸음을 멈추고 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고는, 얼굴을 길게 늘이고 두 눈을 하늘로 돌리니, 마치 곧 기도라도 터뜨릴 것만 같다. 이런 부류는 예루살렘 말고는 어디서도 찾아볼 수 없다. 그의 이마에는, 망토를 제자리에 붙드는 띠에 매달린 네모난 가죽 갑이 튀어나와 있고, 또 하나의 비슷한 갑이 왼팔에 가죽끈으로 묶여 있다. 겉옷 가장자리는 깊은 술로 장식되어 있다. 이런 표지들—성구함, 늘어뜨린 옷자락 단, 그리고 사람 전체에 배어 있는 짙은 거룩함의 냄새—로 우리는 그가 바리새인임을 안다. 한 조직(종교로는 종파요, 정치로는 당파다)의 일원으로, 그 편협함과 권력은 머지않아 온 세상을 비탄에 빠뜨릴 것이다.\n\n성문 밖 무리 가운데 가장 빽빽한 인파는 욥바로 갈라져 나가는 길을 뒤덮고 있다. 바리새인에게서 눈을 돌리니, 잡다한 군중 속에서 마침맞게 따로 떨어져 나와 살펴보기 좋은 한 무리가 우리의 시선을 끈다. 그 가운데 으뜸은 더없이 고귀한 풍모를 지닌 사내다—맑고 건강한 살결, 빛나는 검은 두 눈, 향유로 윤이 나게 길게 흘러내린 수염, 몸에 잘 맞고 값지며 계절에 알맞은 의복. 그는 지팡이를 들었고, 목에서 끈으로 늘어뜨린 커다란 황금 인장을 차고 있다. 여러 하인이 그를 모시는데, 더러는 띠에 짧은 검을 꽂고 있다. 그들이 그에게 말을 건넬 때는 더없이 깍듯하다. 무리의 나머지는 순수한 사막 혈통의 아랍인 둘이다. 야위고 다부진 사내들로, 살갗은 짙게 그을렸고, 두 볼은 움푹 패었으며, 두 눈은 거의 사악하다 싶을 만큼 형형하게 빛난다. 머리에는 붉은 타르부시를 썼고, 아바 위에 갈색 모직 하이크, 곧 담요를 왼쪽 어깨와 몸에 둘러 오른팔만 자유롭게 드러내 놓았다. 시끌벅적한 흥정이 벌어지고 있으니, 아랍인들이 말을 끌고 와 팔려 들기 때문이다. 그들은 다급한 마음에 높고 날카로운 목소리로 떠들어 댄다. 고귀한 풍모의 사내는 말하는 일을 거의 하인들에게 맡기고, 이따금 자기가 위엄 있게 대꾸할 뿐이다. 곧장 키프로스 사람을 보더니 걸음을 멈추고 무화과를 좀 산다. 그리고 이 무리가 모두 바리새인의 뒤를 바짝 따라 성문을 지나간 뒤, 우리가 그 과일 장수에게 가 본다면, 그는 놀랍도록 정중한 절과 함께 일러 줄 것이다. 그 낯선 이는 유대인으로, 이 도시의 귀인 가운데 하나이며, 여러 곳을 여행하여 시리아의 흔한 포도와 바다의 이슬을 머금어 비할 데 없이 풍미가 깊은 키프로스의 포도가 어떻게 다른지를 알게 된 사람이라고.\n\n이렇게 정오 무렵까지, 때로는 그보다 늦게까지, 한결같은 장사의 물결이 욥바 성문으로 으레 들고 나며, 그 흐름과 더불어 온갖 부류의 인물을 실어 나른다. 이스라엘 모든 지파의 대표자들, 그 안에서 옛 신앙이 잘게 나뉘고 닳아 없어진 온갖 종파, 온갖 종교적·사회적 갈래, 예술의 자식이요 쾌락의 종복으로서 헤롯의 방탕에 놀아나는 온갖 모험적인 무리, 그리고 카이사르들과 그 선왕들이 어느 때고 거느렸던 이름난 모든 민족, 특히 지중해 둘레에 자리 잡고 사는 이들까지 모두 그 안에 들어 있다.\n\n다시 말해, 거룩한 역사로 풍요롭고 거룩한 예언과의 인연으로 더욱 풍요로운 예루살렘—은이 돌처럼 흔하고 백향목이 골짜기의 뽕나무처럼 흔했던 솔로몬의 예루살렘—은 이제 로마의 사본에 지나지 않게 되었으니, 부정한 행습의 중심지요, 이교 권력의 본거지가 되었다. 한 유대 왕이 어느 날 제사장의 옷을 입고 첫 성전의 지성소에 들어가 향을 피우려 했다가 나병 환자가 되어 나왔다. 그러나 우리가 지금 읽고 있는 시대에는, 폼페이우스가 헤롯의 성전과 바로 그 지성소에 들어갔다가 아무 해도 입지 않고 나왔으니, 거기서 그가 본 것이라고는 텅 빈 방뿐이요, 하느님의 흔적은 어디에도 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n이제 독자에게 청하노니, 욥바 문 시장의 일부로 묘사된 그 안마당으로 돌아가 주기를 바란다. 그날 세 번째 시각이었고, 많은 사람들이 이미 떠났으나, 북적임은 눈에 띄게 줄어드는 기색 없이 이어지고 있었다. 새로 온 이들 가운데 남쪽 벽 곁에 한 무리가 있었으니, 한 남자와 한 여자와 한 마리 나귀로 이루어진 그들에 대해서는 좀 더 자세히 살펴볼 필요가 있다.\n\n남자는 짐승의 머리맡에 서서 고삐를 쥐고, 막대기에 기대어 있었는데, 그 막대기는 채찍과 지팡이라는 두 가지 쓰임을 위해 고른 듯했다. 그의 옷차림은 주위에 있는 여느 유대인의 것과 다름없었으나, 새것처럼 보이는 점만은 달랐다. 머리에서부터 늘어뜨린 외투와, 목에서 발꿈치까지 몸을 감싼 겉옷은 아마도 안식일에 회당에 갈 때 즐겨 입던 옷이었을 것이다. 그의 얼굴은 가려지지 않았는데, 그 얼굴은 쉰 해의 세월을 말해 주었고, 검은 수염에 줄줄이 섞인 흰빛이 그 짐작을 뒷받침했다. 그는 낯선 시골 사람 특유의, 반쯤은 호기심 어리고 반쯤은 멍한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n나귀는 시장에 넘쳐 나는 푸른 풀 한 아름을 느긋하게 뜯고 있었다. 졸린 듯 흡족한 그 짐승은 주위의 소란과 떠들썩함에도 흔들리지 않았고, 푹신한 안장에 올라앉은 여자에게도 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 칙칙한 모직 천으로 지은 겉옷이 그 여자의 몸을 온통 감쌌고, 흰 머릿수건이 머리와 목을 가렸다. 이따금 무언가 지나가는 것을 보거나 들으려는 호기심에 이끌려 그 머릿수건을 옆으로 젖혔으나, 얼굴이 드러나지 않을 만큼 아주 살짝이었다.\n\n마침내 누군가 그 남자에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"나사렛의 요셉이 아니십니까?\"\n\n말을 건 이는 바로 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"그렇게들 부르지요.\" 요셉이 점잖게 몸을 돌리며 대답했다. \"그리고 당신은—아, 평안하시기를! 내 친구 사무엘 랍비여!\"\n\n\"그 인사를 그대로 당신께 돌려 드립니다.\" 랍비는 잠시 멈추어 여자를 바라보고는 덧붙였다. \"당신과, 당신의 집안과, 모든 일꾼들에게 평안이 있기를.\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 그는 한 손을 가슴에 얹고 여자에게 고개를 숙였다. 그를 보려고 여자는 그새 머릿수건을 젖혔으니, 소녀 시절을 벗어난 지 얼마 되지 않은 한 사람의 얼굴이 드러났다. 그러자 두 지인은 오른손을 마주 잡아, 마치 그 손을 입술로 가져가려는 듯했다. 그러나 마지막 순간에 손을 놓고, 저마다 제 손에 입을 맞춘 다음, 그 손바닥을 제 이마에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"당신 옷에 먼지가 거의 묻어 있지 않으니,\" 랍비가 허물없이 말했다. \"우리 조상의 이 도성에서 하룻밤을 묵으신 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 요셉이 대답했다. \"밤이 오기 전에 베다니까지밖에 갈 수 없어, 그곳 객사에서 묵고, 동틀 녘에 다시 길을 나섰습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 앞길이 머시겠군요—설마 욥바까지는 아니시겠지요.\"\n\n\"베들레헴까지만 갑니다.\"\n\n그때까지 열려 있고 다정하던 랍비의 낯빛이 어두워지고 험상궂어졌으며, 그는 기침 대신 으르렁거리는 소리로 목청을 가다듬었다.\n\n\"그렇지, 그래—알겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 베들레헴에서 태어났으니, 카이사르의 명에 따라 호적에 오르려고 딸과 함께 그리로 가는 길이로군요. 야곱의 자손이 이집트에 있던 지파들과 같은 처지가 되었으니—다만 모세도 없고 여호수아도 없을 따름이지요. 어찌 그리 강하던 자들이 쓰러졌단 말입니까!\"\n\n요셉은 자세도 낯빛도 바꾸지 않고 대답했다.\n\n\"이 사람은 내 딸이 아닙니다.\"\n\n그러나 랍비는 정치적인 생각에 매달려, 그 해명에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다. \"갈릴리 아래쪽의 열심당원들은 무얼 하고 있습니까?\"\n\n\"나는 목수이고, 나사렛은 마을일 뿐입니다.\" 요셉이 조심스럽게 말했다. \"내 작업대가 놓인 거리는 어느 도시로도 통하지 않는 길입니다. 나무를 깎고 널빤지를 켜는 일에 매여, 당파의 다툼에 끼어들 겨를이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그러나 당신은 유대인이오.\" 랍비가 간곡하게 말했다. \"당신은 유대인이고, 다윗의 혈통이지요. 옛 관습에 따라 여호와께 바치는 세겔 말고는, 어떤 세금을 내는 데서도 기쁨을 느낄 리 없을 것이오.\"\n\n요셉은 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"내가 불평하는 건,\" 그 친구가 말을 이었다. \"세금의 액수가 아니오—데나리온 한 닢이야 하찮은 것이지요. 오, 아니오! 세금을 매긴다는 것 자체가 모욕이오. 게다가 그것을 내는 일이 폭정에 복종하는 것이 아니고 무엇이겠소? 말해 보시오, 유다가 자기가 메시아라고 주장한다는 게 사실이오? 당신은 그를 따르는 자들 한가운데서 살고 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그를 따르는 자들이 그가 메시아라고 말하는 것은 들었습니다.\" 요셉이 대답했다.\n\n이때 머릿수건이 옆으로 젖혀지면서, 한순간 여자의 얼굴이 온전히 드러났다. 랍비의 눈길이 그쪽으로 흘러갔고, 그는 깊은 관심의 빛으로 환히 밝아진, 보기 드문 아름다움을 지닌 얼굴을 볼 수 있었다. 그러더니 붉은 기운이 그녀의 뺨과 이마에 번졌고, 너울은 다시 제자리로 돌아갔다.\n\n정치를 논하던 자는 화제를 잊었다.\n\n\"따님이 곱군요.\" 그가 목소리를 낮추어 말했다.\n\n\"내 딸이 아닙니다.\" 요셉이 거듭 말했다.\n\n랍비의 호기심이 일었다. 그것을 알아챈 나사렛 사람은 서둘러 말을 보탰다. \"이 사람은 베들레헴의 요아킴과 안나의 딸입니다. 그분들은 명망이 높았으니, 적어도 들어는 보셨을 겁니다—\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 랍비가 정중하게 말했다. \"내가 그분들을 알지요. 다윗에게서 곧바로 이어진 혈통이었소. 잘 알고 있었소.\"\n\n\"그런데 이제 두 분 다 세상을 떠났습니다.\" 나사렛 사람이 말을 이었다. \"나사렛에서 돌아가셨지요. 요아킴은 넉넉하지는 않았으나, 두 딸 마리안과 마리아에게 나누어 줄 집 한 채와 정원을 남겼습니다. 이 사람이 그 가운데 하나인데, 제 몫의 재산을 지키려면 율법에 따라 가장 가까운 친족과 혼인해야 했습니다. 그래서 지금은 내 아내가 되었지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은—\"\n\n\"그녀의 숙부였습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 그렇소! 그리고 두 분 다 베들레헴에서 태어났으니, 로마가 호적에 함께 오르도록 당신더러 그녀를 데려가라고 강요하는 것이로군요.\"\n\n랍비는 두 손을 맞잡고 분개하여 하늘을 우러르며 외쳤다. \"이스라엘의 하느님은 아직 살아 계신다! 복수는 그분의 것이다!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 몸을 돌려 불쑥 떠나 버렸다. 가까이 있던 한 낯선 사람이 요셉의 놀란 기색을 보고 조용히 말했다. \"사무엘 랍비는 열심당원이지요. 유다라 한들 저보다 더 사납지는 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n요셉은 그 사람과 이야기를 나누고 싶지 않아 못 들은 체하며, 나귀가 흩뜨려 놓은 풀을 작은 무더기로 그러모으는 일에 몰두했다. 그러고는 다시 지팡이에 몸을 기대고 기다렸다.\n\n또 한 시간이 지나 그 일행은 성문을 빠져나가, 왼쪽으로 돌아 베들레헴으로 가는 길로 접어들었다. 힌놈 골짜기로 내려가는 비탈은 제법 험했고, 군데군데 듬성듬성 들올리브나무가 박혀 있었다. 나사렛 사람은 고삐를 손에 쥐고, 조심스럽고도 정성스럽게 여자 곁에서 걸었다. 그들의 왼쪽으로는 남쪽과 동쪽으로 시온산을 휘감으며 성벽이 솟아 있었고, 오른쪽으로는 골짜기의 서쪽 경계를 이루는 가파른 벼랑들이 있었다.\n\n그들은 천천히 기혼 아래 못을 지나쳤으니, 그 못 위에 드리웠던 왕의 언덕 그림자를 해가 빠르게 몰아내며 줄여 가고 있었다. 그들은 천천히 나아가, 솔로몬 못에서 흘러오는 수로와 나란히 길을 잡았고, 마침내 오늘날 흉계의 언덕이라 불리는 곳의 별장 터 가까이에 이르렀다. 거기서부터 그들은 르바임 평원으로 오르기 시작했다. 해가 그 이름난 고장의 돌투성이 땅 위로 눈부시게 쏟아졌고, 그 기운에 못 이겨 요아킴의 딸 마리아는 머릿수건을 아예 내리고 머리를 드러냈다. 요셉은 다윗이 그곳 진지에서 블레셋 사람들을 기습한 이야기를 들려주었다. 그는 둔한 사람 특유의 엄숙한 낯빛과 생기 없는 태도로 지루하게 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 그녀는 그 말을 늘 듣고 있지는 않았다.\n\n땅 위로 사람들이 가는 곳마다, 바다 위로 배들이 가는 곳마다, 유대인의 얼굴과 모습은 낯익다. 그 민족의 신체적 전형은 언제나 한결같았으나, 더러 저마다의 차이가 있었다. \"그는 혈색이 좋고, 게다가 용모가 아름다워 보기에 훌륭하였더라.\" 사무엘 앞에 끌려 나왔을 때의 이새의 아들이 그러했다. 그 뒤로 사람들의 상상은 이 묘사에 매여 왔다. 시적 자유는 그 조상의 특징을 그의 이름난 후손들에게까지 펼쳐 놓았다. 그리하여 우리가 마음에 그리는 모든 솔로몬은 고운 얼굴에, 그늘에서는 밤빛이고 햇빛에서는 금빛을 띠는 머리칼과 수염을 지녔다. 우리는 또한 사랑받던 압살롬의 머리채도 그러했다고 믿게 되었다. 그리고 믿을 만한 역사가 없는 가운데, 전승은 우리가 지금 그 혈색 좋은 왕의 고향 도시로 따라가고 있는 그녀를 못지않게 사랑스럽게 그려 왔다.\n\n그녀는 열다섯을 넘지 않았다. 그 자태와 목소리와 거동은 소녀 시절에서 막 벗어나려는 시기의 것이었다. 얼굴은 완벽한 달걀형이었고, 살빛은 고운 흰빛이라기보다 창백한 편이었다. 코는 흠잡을 데가 없었다. 살짝 벌어진 입술은 도톰하고 무르익어, 입가의 선에 따스함과 부드러움과 미더움을 안겨 주었다. 눈은 푸르고 컸으며, 내리깔린 눈꺼풀과 긴 속눈썹이 그늘을 드리웠다. 그리고 이 모든 것과 어우러져, 유대인 신부에게 허락된 방식대로 풀어 내린 금빛 머리채가, 그녀가 앉은 안장까지 등을 타고 흘러내렸다. 목과 목덜미에는 이따금 보이는 솜털 같은 부드러움이 깃들어 있어, 화가로 하여금 그것이 윤곽이 빚어낸 것인지 빛깔이 빚어낸 것인지 가늠하기 어렵게 했다. 이러한 이목구비와 몸매의 매력에 더하여, 더 형언하기 어려운 다른 것들이 있었으니—오직 영혼만이 줄 수 있는 순결의 기운과, 만질 수 없는 것들을 깊이 생각하는 이에게 자연스레 깃드는 초연함이었다. 자주, 떨리는 입술로, 그녀는 하늘만큼이나 깊게 푸른 두 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러렀다. 자주 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개었으니, 흠모와 기도를 드리는 듯했다. 자주 머리를 들었으니, 부르는 목소리를 간절히 귀 기울여 듣는 사람 같았다. 이따금 느릿느릿 이야기를 늘어놓던 중에 요셉이 돌아서서 그녀를 바라보다가, 빛이 비치듯 그 얼굴을 환히 밝히는 표정을 마주치고는, 제 이야기의 줄거리를 잊은 채 고개를 숙이고 의아해하며 터벅터벅 걸었다.\n\n그렇게 그들은 너른 평원을 끼고 돌아, 마침내 마르 엘리아스의 높은 곳에 이르렀다. 거기서 그들은 골짜기 너머로 베들레헴을 바라보았으니, 오래고 오랜 빵의 집이었다. 그 흰 벽이 능선 위에 왕관처럼 얹혀, 잎이 진 과수원의 갈색 칠 너머로 빛나고 있었다. 그들은 거기서 걸음을 멈추고 쉬는 동안, 요셉이 거룩한 사연을 지닌 곳들을 가리켜 보였다. 그러고는 골짜기로 내려가 우물에 이르렀으니, 그곳은 다윗의 용사들이 벌인 놀라운 무용담 가운데 하나의 무대였다. 그 좁은 자리는 사람과 짐승으로 북적였다. 요셉에게 두려움이 밀려왔다—이렇게 마을이 붐빈다면, 어진 마리아가 머물 방이 없을지도 모른다는 두려움이었다. 그는 지체 없이 서둘러, 라헬의 무덤을 표시하는 돌기둥을 지나, 정원이 딸린 비탈을 올라, 길에서 마주친 숱한 사람들 가운데 누구에게도 인사하지 않은 채, 마침내 그때 마을 성문 밖, 길이 갈라지는 곳 가까이에 서 있던 객사의 정문 앞에 다다라서야 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n나사렛 사람이 객사에서 겪은 일을 온전히 이해하려면, 동방의 여관이 서방 세계의 여관과는 달랐다는 사실을 독자에게 일러 둘 필요가 있다. 그것들은 페르시아어에서 온 말로 칸이라 불렸으며, 가장 단순한 형태로는 집도 헛간도 없이, 흔히 문이나 출입구조차 없는 울타리만 두른 마당이었다. 그 자리는 그늘이나 방어, 또는 물을 기준으로 정해졌다. 야곱이 밧단아람으로 아내를 구하러 갔을 때 그를 재워 준 여관도 이러한 것이었다. 그와 같은 것을 오늘날에도 사막의 쉼터에서 볼 수 있다. 한편 그 가운데 더러는, 특히 예루살렘과 알렉산드리아 같은 큰 도시를 잇는 길가에 선 것들은 왕후(王侯)의 시설이었으니, 그것을 세운 왕들의 경건함을 기리는 기념물이었다. 그러나 보통은 한 족장의 집이나 소유에 지나지 않았으며, 그는 그곳을 본거지 삼아 자기 부족을 다스렸다. 나그네를 재우는 것은 그것의 쓰임새 가운데 가장 작은 것이었다. 그것들은 시장이요, 작업장이요, 요새였으니, 늦어 떠도는 나그네를 위한 피난처인 만큼이나 상인과 장인이 모이고 머무는 곳이기도 했다. 그 담장 안에서는 일 년 내내 한 도시의 갖가지 일상적 거래가 날마다 거듭되었다.\n\n이러한 숙소들의 유별난 운영 방식이야말로 서방 사람의 마음을 가장 강하게 칠 만한 특징이었다. 주인 남자도 주인 여자도 없었고, 사무원도 요리사도 부엌도 없었다. 문간에 앉은 관리인 하나가 어디서든 눈에 띄는 다스림이나 소유의 유일한 표시였다. 도착한 나그네는 셈을 치르지 않고도 마음껏 머물렀다. 이러한 제도의 한 결과로, 오는 사람은 누구나 제 먹을 것과 취사 도구를 가지고 와야 했으며, 그렇지 않으면 칸 안의 장사꾼에게서 사야 했다. 잠자리와 침구, 짐승의 먹이에 대해서도 같은 규칙이 적용되었다. 물과 쉼, 거처와 보호, 이것이 그가 주인에게 바라는 전부였으며, 그것들은 거저 주어지는 것이었다. 회당의 평화는 이따금 다투는 자들의 싸움질로 깨지기도 했으나, 칸의 평화는 결코 그렇지 않았다. 그 집들과 거기 딸린 모든 것은 신성했으니, 우물도 그보다 더 신성하지는 않았다.\n\n요셉과 그 아내가 그 앞에 멈춰 선 베들레헴의 칸은 그 부류의 좋은 본보기로, 지나치게 원시적이지도 않고 지나치게 호화롭지도 않았다. 그 건물은 순전히 동방식이었으니, 곧 거친 돌을 네모지게 쌓아 올린 단층 건물로, 지붕은 평평하고, 바깥쪽은 창 하나 없이 막혔으며, 주된 출입구는 단 하나뿐이었다. 동쪽 면, 곧 앞쪽에 난 문 하나가 그것으로, 출입문이자 대문이기도 했다. 길은 그 문 바로 곁을 지나는지라 백악 가루가 문 상인방을 절반쯤 덮고 있었다. 납작한 바위로 쌓은 울타리가 건물 북동쪽 모퉁이에서 시작하여 비탈을 따라 여러 야드 아래로 뻗어 내려가다가 한 지점에서 서쪽으로 휘어 석회암 벼랑에 닿았으니, 이는 번듯한 칸에 더없이 요긴한 것, 곧 짐승을 가두는 안전한 우리를 이루고 있었다.\n\n베들레헴 같은 마을에는 족장이 하나뿐이었으니, 칸도 하나 넘게 있을 리 없었다. 그리고 나사렛 사람은 비록 그곳 태생이었으나 오랫동안 다른 곳에 살았기에, 그 고을에서 묵을 권리를 주장할 처지가 못 되었다. 더구나 그가 응하러 온 호적 등록은 몇 주가 걸릴지 몇 달이 걸릴지 알 수 없는 일이었으니, 속주에 파견된 로마 관리들의 굼뜸은 익히 소문난 바였다. 그토록 기약 없는 동안 아내와 함께 아는 이나 친척에게 신세를 진다는 것은 생각할 수도 없었다. 그래서 그는 그 큰 집에 다가가기도 전에, 아직 비탈을 오르며 가파른 곳에서 나귀를 재촉하느라 애쓰는 동안, 칸에서 잠자리를 얻지 못할지도 모른다는 두려움이 괴로운 근심이 되었다. 길에는 어른들과 사내아이들이 큰 소동을 벌이며 소와 말과 낙타를 골짜기로 몰고 가고 몰고 오는 무리로 붐비고 있었으니, 더러는 물을 먹이러, 더러는 가까운 동굴로 가는 것이었다. 그리고 가까이 다가갔을 때, 그의 불안은 가시지 않았다. 한 무리가 그 시설의 문을 에워싸고 있었고, 거기 딸린 우리는 그토록 넓은데도 벌써 가득 차 보였던 것이다.\n\n\"문 앞까지 갈 수가 없구려.\" 요셉이 그 느릿한 말투로 말했다. \"여기서 멈추고, 무슨 일이 있었는지 알 수 있으면 알아봅시다.\"\n\n아내는 대답 없이 조용히 너울을 옆으로 걷었다. 처음에는 그 얼굴에 피로한 기색이 떠 있었으나, 이내 흥미 어린 빛으로 바뀌었다. 그녀는 자기가 호기심을 끌 수밖에 없는 한 무리의 가장자리에 서 있음을 깨달았다. 큰 대상들이 으레 오가는 어느 큰길의 칸에서나 흔히 볼 수 있는 광경이었는데도 그러했다. 사람들이 걸어서 이리저리 뛰어다니며 시리아의 온갖 말로 날카롭게 떠들어 댔고, 말 탄 자들은 낙타 탄 자들에게 소리쳤다. 사람들은 고집 센 소와 겁먹은 양과 미덥지 못한 씨름을 벌이고 있었으며, 빵과 포도주를 파는 자들도 있었다. 그 무리 가운데에는 한 떼의 개를 쫓는 듯한 한 떼의 사내아이들도 있었다. 모든 사람과 모든 것이 한꺼번에 움직이는 것 같았다. 어쩌면 그 아름다운 구경꾼은 너무 지쳐 그 광경에 오래 마음을 빼앗기지 못했는지도 모른다. 잠시 후 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고 안장 뒤 받침에 몸을 가라앉히더니, 평안과 쉼을 찾으려는 듯, 또는 누군가를 기다리는 듯, 남쪽을 멀리 바라보며 낙원산의 높은 벼랑을 올려다보았다. 그 벼랑은 지는 해 아래 어렴풋이 붉어지고 있었다.\n\n그녀가 그렇게 바라보고 있을 때, 한 사내가 무리를 헤치고 나오더니 나귀 곁에 바싹 멈춰 서서 성난 이맛살을 하고 돌아섰다. 나사렛 사람이 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"제가 당신을 알아본 대로라면, 선한 벗이여 — 유다의 아들이시라면 — 이 무리의 까닭을 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n낯선 사내는 사납게 돌아섰다. 그러나 요셉의 엄숙한 얼굴, 깊고 느린 그의 목소리와 말투에 그토록 어울리는 그 얼굴을 보고는, 손을 들어 반쯤 인사하는 시늉을 하며 대답했다.\n\n\"당신에게 평화가 있기를, 랍비여! 나는 유다의 아들이니, 답해 드리리다. 나는 벳다곤에 사는데, 아시다시피 그곳은 단 지파의 땅이던 곳에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"모딘에서 욥바로 가는 길에 있는 곳이군요.\" 요셉이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 벳다곤에 가 보셨군요.\" 사내가 말했다. 그 얼굴이 한층 더 부드러워졌다. \"우리 유다 사람들은 참으로 떠도는 무리지요! 나는 그 능선 — 우리 조상 야곱이 옛 에브랏이라 부른 그곳 — 을 여러 해 동안 떠나 있었습니다. 모든 히브리 사람더러 제 태어난 고을에서 호적에 오르라는 칙령이 내려졌을 때 — 내가 여기 온 까닭이 바로 그것입니다, 랍비여.\"\n\n요셉의 얼굴은 가면처럼 무덤덤한 채로, 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"나도 그 때문에 왔소 — 나와 내 아내가 말이오.\"\n\n낯선 사내는 마리아를 흘끗 보고는 잠잠해졌다. 그녀는 게돌의 민둥 꼭대기를 올려다보고 있었다. 해가 위로 향한 그 얼굴에 닿아 보랏빛 깊은 눈동자를 가득 채웠고, 살짝 벌어진 입술 위에는 사람의 것이라고는 할 수 없는 어떤 갈망이 떨고 있었다. 그 순간, 그녀의 아름다움이 지닌 모든 인간적인 것이 정화되어 사라진 듯했으니, 그녀는 우리가 천국의 변모하는 빛 속에서 문 가까이 앉아 있다고 상상하는 이들과도 같았다. 벳다곤 사내는 수백 년 뒤 신과도 같은 산치오에게 천재의 환상으로 찾아와 그를 불멸케 한 바로 그 원형을 보았던 것이다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 이야기를 하고 있었지요? 아! 생각났습니다. 여기로 오라는 명령을 들었을 때 나는 화가 났다고 말하려던 참이었지요. 그러다가 그 오래된 언덕과 마을과, 기드론의 깊은 골짜기로 떨어져 내려가는 골짜기를 떠올렸습니다. 보아스와 룻의 시절 이래로 끊긴 적 없는 포도밭과 과수원과 곡식밭을 떠올렸고, 낯익은 산들 — 여기 게돌, 저편 기브아, 저쪽 마르 엘리야 — 을 떠올렸지요. 어릴 적 내게는 그 산들이 세상의 담벼락이었습니다. 그러자 나는 폭군들을 용서하고 왔습니다 — 나와, 내 아내 라헬과, 우리의 샤론의 장미인 드보라와 미갈이 함께 말이지요.\"\n\n사내는 다시 말을 멈추고, 문득 마리아를 바라보았다. 그녀는 이제 그를 바라보며 귀 기울이고 있었다. 그러더니 그가 말했다. \"랍비여, 당신의 아내가 내 아내에게로 가지 않겠습니까? 저기 길이 꺾이는 곳, 비스듬히 기운 올리브나무 아래에서 아이들과 함께 있는 내 아내를 보실 수 있을 겁니다. 내 말하건대\" — 그는 요셉을 향해 단호하게 말했다 — \"내 말하건대 칸은 가득 찼습니다. 문간에서 물어봐야 소용없습니다.\"\n\n요셉의 결심은 그 마음처럼 느렸다. 그는 망설였으나, 마침내 대답했다. \"친절한 제안이오. 집 안에 우리가 들 자리가 있든 없든, 우리는 당신 식구들에게 가 보겠소. 문지기에게는 내가 직접 말해 보리다. 곧 돌아오겠소.\"\n\n그리고 그는 이끄는 줄을 낯선 사내의 손에 쥐여 주고, 들썩이는 무리 속으로 헤치고 들어갔다.\n\n문지기는 문밖의 커다란 삼나무 토막 위에 앉아 있었다. 그 뒤의 벽에는 투창 하나가 기대 세워져 있었다. 개 한 마리가 그 곁의 토막 위에 웅크리고 있었다.\n\n\"여호와의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를.\" 마침내 문지기와 마주 선 요셉이 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 베푸는 것을 다시 찾으시기를. 그리고 찾거든 그것이 당신과 당신의 식구에게 몇 곱절로 불어나기를.\" 야경꾼은 움직이지도 않은 채 엄숙하게 답했다.\n\n\"나는 베들레헴 사람이오.\" 요셉이 더없이 신중한 말투로 말했다. \"혹시 자리가 없겠소—\"\n\n\"없습니다.\"\n\n\"내 이야기를 들으셨을지도 모르겠소 — 나사렛의 요셉이오. 이곳은 내 조상의 집이오. 나는 다윗의 혈통이오.\"\n\n이 말에 나사렛 사람의 희망이 걸려 있었다. 이 말이 그를 저버린다면, 더 이상의 청은 헛될 터였으니, 세겔을 많이 내겠다는 제안조차 그러할 것이었다. 유다의 아들이라는 것은 한 가지 일이었으니 — 부족의 견해로는 큰일이었다. 다윗의 가문이라는 것은 또 다른 일이었으니, 히브리 사람의 입에서 그보다 더 높은 자랑은 있을 수 없었다. 소년 목동이 사울의 뒤를 이어 왕가를 세운 지 천 년이 넘게 지났다. 전쟁과 재난과 다른 왕들, 그리고 시간의 헤아릴 수 없이 흐릿하게 하는 과정이, 운수로 말하자면, 그 후손들을 여느 유대인의 수준으로 끌어내렸다. 그들이 먹는 빵은 더없이 비천한 수고로 얻은 것이었다. 그러나 그들에게는 거룩하게 지켜져 온 역사의 혜택이 있었으니, 족보가 그 첫 장이자 마지막 장이었다. 그들은 결코 알려지지 않은 존재가 될 수 없었으니, 이스라엘 어디를 가든 그들과 아는 사이가 되면 그 뒤에는 경배에 가까운 존경이 따랐다.\n\n예루살렘에서도 다른 곳에서도 그러했다면, 그 거룩한 혈통의 한 사람이 베들레헴 칸의 문 앞에서 그것에 기대를 거는 것도 분명 무리는 아니었다. 요셉이 말한 대로 \"이곳은 내 조상의 집이오\" 한 것은, 더없이 단순하고 글자 그대로 진실을 말한 것이었다. 그것은 룻이 보아스의 아내로서 다스리던 바로 그 집이요, 이새와 그 열 아들이, 막내 다윗이 태어난 바로 그 집이요, 사무엘이 왕을 찾아와 그를 찾아낸 바로 그 집이요, 다윗이 친절한 길르앗 사람 바실래의 아들에게 준 바로 그 집이요, 예레미야가 기도로써 바벨론 사람들 앞에서 도망치던 제 겨레의 남은 자들을 구해 낸 바로 그 집이었다.\n\n그 호소는 효력이 없지 않았다. 문지기는 삼나무 토막에서 미끄러져 내려와, 제 수염에 손을 얹으며 공손히 말했다. \"랍비여, 이 문이 처음 나그네를 맞아 열린 때가 언제인지는 말씀드릴 수 없으나, 천 년도 더 된 일입니다. 그 오랜 세월 동안, 재워 줄 자리가 없을 때 말고는, 선한 사람이 쫓겨났다는 예는 알려진 바 없습니다. 낯선 이에게도 그러했다면, 다윗의 혈통인 분에게 안 된다고 하는 관리인에게는 마땅한 까닭이 있어야 할 것입니다. 그러므로 다시 한번 당신께 인사를 올립니다. 그리고 저와 함께 가 보시겠다면, 이 집에 묵을 자리가 한 군데도 남지 않았음을 보여 드리겠습니다. 방에도, 회랑에도, 안뜰에도 없으며 — 지붕 위에도 없습니다. 언제 오셨는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"방금 막.\"\n\n문지기는 미소 지었다.\n\n\"'너희와 함께 거하는 나그네를 너희 중에서 난 자같이 여기며 너 자신처럼 그를 사랑하라.' 그것이 율법이 아닙니까, 랍비여?\"\n\n요셉은 잠잠했다.\n\n\"그것이 율법이라면, 오래전에 온 사람에게 '네 갈 길을 가거라. 여기 네 자리를 차지할 다른 이가 있다'고 말할 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n그래도 요셉은 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 내가 그렇게 말한다면, 그 자리는 누구의 것이 되겠습니까? 더러는 정오부터 기다려 온 저 많은 사람들을 보십시오.\"\n\n\"이 사람들은 다 누구요?\" 요셉이 무리 쪽을 돌아보며 물었다. \"그리고 어찌하여 이때에 여기 모여 있소?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 당신을 데려온 것 — 카이사르의 칙령이지요.\" 문지기는 나사렛 사람에게 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던지고는 말을 이었다. \"이 집에 묵고 있는 사람들 대부분도 그 때문에 왔습니다. 그리고 어제는 다마스쿠스에서 아라비아와 하(下)이집트로 가는 대상이 도착했지요. 여기 보이는 이들은 거기 딸린 사람들입니다 — 사람과 낙타 말이지요.\"\n\n그래도 요셉은 끈질겼다.\n\n\"안뜰이 넓지 않소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요. 하지만 화물이 가득 쌓여 있습니다 — 비단 꾸러미며, 향료 자루며, 온갖 물품들로 말이지요.\"\n\n그러자 한순간 청하는 이의 얼굴에서 그 무덤덤함이 사라졌다. 빛 없이 멍하니 응시하던 눈이 아래로 떨어졌다. 그는 다소 격해진 어조로 다음과 같이 말했다. \"내 한 몸은 상관없소. 그러나 나에게는 아내가 함께 있고, 밤은 차갑소 — 이 높은 곳은 나사렛보다도 더 차갑소. 그 사람은 한데서는 지낼 수 없소. 그 마을에는 자리가 없겠소?\"\n\n\"이 사람들이\" — 문지기는 문 앞의 무리를 향해 손을 휘저었다 — \"모두 그 마을에 청해 보았으나, 묵을 자리가 죄다 찼다고 합니다.\"\n\n요셉은 다시 땅을 들여다보며, 반쯤 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"그 사람은 저토록 젊은데! 그 사람의 잠자리를 언덕에 마련한다면, 서리가 그 사람을 죽일 것이오.\"\n\n그러더니 그는 문지기에게 다시 말했다.\n\n\"혹시 당신은 그 사람의 부모를 알았을지도 모르겠소. 요아킴과 안나라고, 한때 베들레헴에 살았으며, 나처럼 다윗의 혈통이오.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나는 그분들을 알았습니다. 선한 분들이었지요. 내 젊은 시절의 일입니다.\"\n\n이번에는 문지기의 눈이 생각에 잠겨 땅을 향했다. 문득 그가 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"당신께 자리를 마련해 드릴 수는 없으나,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신을 돌려보낼 수도 없습니다. 랍비여, 내가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 드리리다. 당신 일행은 몇 분이나 됩니까?\"\n\n요셉은 잠시 생각하고 대답했다. \"내 아내와, 욥바 너머 작은 고을 벳다곤에서 온 한 벗과 그 식구요. 모두 합쳐 여섯이오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 능선에서 한데 잠을 자게 하지는 않겠습니다. 당신 식구들을 데려오시되, 서두르십시오. 해가 산 뒤로 넘어가면 밤이 금세 온다는 것을 아시지 않습니까. 이제 거의 그 무렵입니다.\"\n\n\"집 없는 나그네의 축복을 당신께 드리오. 머무는 자의 축복도 뒤따를 것이오.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나사렛 사람은 기쁘게 마리아와 벳다곤 사내에게로 돌아갔다. 잠시 후 사내가 제 식구를 데려왔으니, 여인들은 나귀에 타고 있었다. 그 아내는 기품 있는 부인이었고, 딸들은 그녀가 젊었을 적 모습 그대로였다. 그들이 문에 가까이 다가오자, 문지기는 그들이 비천한 계층임을 알아보았다.\n\n\"이 사람이 내가 말한 그 사람이오.\" 나사렛 사람이 말했다. \"그리고 이들은 우리의 벗들이오.\"\n\n마리아의 너울이 걷혔다.\n\n\"푸른 눈에 금빛 머리카락이라.\" 관리인은 그녀만을 보며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"사울 앞에서 노래하러 갔던 그 젊은 왕도 저런 모습이었지.\"\n\n그러더니 그는 요셉에게서 이끄는 줄을 받아 들고, 마리아에게 말했다. \"평화가 있기를, 오 다윗의 딸이여!\" 그러고는 다른 이들에게, \"모두에게 평화가 있기를!\" 그러고는 요셉에게, \"랍비여, 나를 따라오십시오.\"\n\n일행은 돌로 포장된 넓은 통로로 안내되었고, 거기서 칸의 안뜰로 들어섰다. 낯선 이에게는 그 광경이 신기했을 테지만, 그들은 사방에서 어둡게 입을 벌리고 있는 회랑과 안뜰 자체를 보고도, 그저 얼마나 붐비는지를 알아챘을 뿐이었다. 화물을 쌓아 두고 남겨 둔 좁은 길을 지나, 그리고 입구의 것과 비슷한 통로를 거쳐, 그들은 집에 딸린 우리로 나왔다. 그곳에는 낙타와 말과 나귀가 빽빽이 무리 지어 매인 채 졸고 있었다. 그 사이에는 여러 고장에서 온 짐승지기들이 있었으니, 그들 또한 잠들었거나 말없이 지켜보고 있었다. 그들은 붐비는 마당의 비탈을 천천히 내려갔으니, 여인들을 태운 굼뜬 짐승들에게도 제 고집이 있었던 까닭이다. 마침내 그들은 칸의 서쪽을 내려다보는 잿빛 석회암 벼랑을 향해 난 오솔길로 접어들었다.\n\n\"우리는 동굴로 가고 있소.\" 요셉이 짧게 말했다.\n\n안내자는 마리아가 제 곁에 올 때까지 걸음을 늦추었다.\n\n\"우리가 가는 그 동굴은,\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"당신 조상 다윗이 즐겨 찾던 곳이었음에 틀림없습니다. 우리 아래 있는 들판에서, 그리고 골짜기 아래의 우물에서, 그분은 양 떼를 안전을 위해 그 동굴로 몰곤 했지요. 그리고 훗날 왕이 되어서는, 휴식과 건강을 위해 이 옛집으로 돌아오면서 짐승들을 큰 무리로 거느리고 왔습니다. 여물통은 그분의 시절 그대로 아직 남아 있습니다. 안뜰이나 길가의 잠자리보다는, 그분이 주무시던 바닥의 잠자리가 더 낫지요. 아, 여기 동굴 앞의 집입니다!\"\n\n이 말은 그들에게 마련해 준 잠자리에 대한 변명으로 받아들여서는 안 된다. 변명할 까닭이 없었던 것이다. 그곳은 그때 내어 줄 수 있는 가장 좋은 자리였다. 손님들은 소박한 사람들이라, 생활 습관으로 보아 쉽게 만족하는 이들이었다. 더구나 그 시대의 유대인에게 동굴에서 머무는 것은 친숙한 관념이었으니, 날마다 일어나는 일들과 회당에서 안식일에 들은 이야기들이 그것을 그렇게 만들었다. 유대 역사의 얼마나 많은 부분이, 그 역사에서 손에 땀을 쥐게 하는 그 많은 사건들 가운데 얼마나 많은 것이 동굴에서 일어났던가! 게다가 이들은 베들레헴의 유대인이었으니, 그들에게 그 관념은 더욱 예사로운 것이었다. 그들의 고장에는 크고 작은 동굴이 가득했으며, 그 가운데 더러는 에밈과 호리 사람들의 시절부터 거처로 쓰여 온 것들이었다. 그들이 안내받고 있는 그 동굴이 한때 마구간이었거나 지금도 마구간이라는 사실 또한 그들에게는 거슬릴 것이 없었다. 그들은 목자들의 겨레의 후손이었으니, 그들의 가축은 으레 그들의 거처와 떠돌이살이를 함께 나누었다. 아브라함에게서 물려받은 관습에 따라, 베두인의 천막은 지금도 말과 아이를 똑같이 한지붕 아래 재운다. 그래서 그들은 즐거이 문지기를 따랐고, 그저 자연스러운 호기심만을 느끼며 그 집을 바라보았다. 다윗의 역사와 얽힌 것이라면 무엇이든 그들에게는 흥미로웠다.\n\n그 건물은 낮고 좁았으며, 뒤편으로 맞붙은 바위에서 조금만 튀어나와 있었고, 창이라고는 도무지 없었다. 그 휑한 앞면에는 문 하나가 거대한 돌쩌귀에 매달려 있었으며, 황토 진흙이 두텁게 발려 있었다. 자물쇠의 나무 빗장이 뒤로 밀리는 동안, 여인들은 안장 뒤 받침에서 내려지는 것을 거들어 받았다. 문이 열리자 문지기가 외쳤다.\n\n\"들어오십시오!\"\n\n손님들은 들어가 사방을 둘러보았다. 그 집이 천연 동굴, 곧 동혈(洞穴)의 입구를 가린 가면 또는 덮개에 지나지 않음이 곧바로 드러났다. 그 동굴은 길이가 아마 12미터쯤, 높이가 3미터 남짓, 너비가 4미터에서 5미터쯤 되어 보였다. 빛이 문간을 통해 고르지 못한 바닥 위로 흘러들어, 방 한가운데를 차지한 곡식과 꼴 더미, 질그릇과 살림살이 위에 떨어졌다. 양옆을 따라서는 여물통이 있었으니, 양이 닿을 만큼 나직하게, 시멘트로 돌을 쌓아 지은 것이었다. 칸막이나 마구간 칸 따위는 어디에도 없었다. 먼지와 검불이 바닥을 누렇게 물들이고 온갖 틈과 우묵한 곳을 가득 메웠으며, 천장에서 더러운 아마포 조각처럼 드리워진 거미줄을 두텁게 했다. 그 밖에는 그곳은 깨끗했으며, 보기에는 칸 본채의 어느 아치형 회랑 못지않게 편안했다. 사실 동굴이야말로 회랑의 본보기이자 그 첫 발상이었다.\n\n\"들어오십시오!\" 안내자가 말했다. \"바닥에 쌓인 이것들은 당신들 같은 나그네를 위한 것입니다. 필요한 만큼 가져다 쓰십시오.\"\n\n그러더니 그는 마리아에게 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 쉴 만하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이곳은 거룩한 곳이군요.\" 그녀가 답했다.\n\n\"그러면 저는 물러가겠습니다. 모두에게 평화가 있기를!\"\n\n그가 떠나자, 그들은 동굴을 지낼 만한 곳으로 만드느라 분주히 움직였다.\n\n# CHAPTER X.\n\n저녁 어느 시각이 되자, 객사 안팎에서 사람들이 떠들고 부산하던 소리가 그쳤다. 그와 동시에 이스라엘 사람들은 아직 서 있지 않은 자라면 모두 일어나, 얼굴을 엄숙히 하고, 예루살렘 쪽을 바라보며, 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 기도를 올렸다. 이때가 거룩한 제구시(第九時), 곧 모리아 산의 성전에서 제물을 바치는 시각이요, 하느님이 그곳에 계시다고 여겨지는 시각이었기 때문이다. 기도하던 이들의 손이 내려오자, 다시 소란이 일었다. 저마다 서둘러 빵을 먹거나 잠자리를 폈다. 조금 뒤에는 불이 꺼지고, 정적이 깃들었으며, 이윽고 잠이 찾아왔다.\n\n자정 무렵, 지붕 위에 있던 누군가가 외쳤다. \"하늘에 저 빛은 무엇인가? 일어나라, 형제들이여, 일어나서 보아라!\"\n\n반쯤 잠든 사람들이 몸을 일으켜 바라보았다. 곧 그들은 경이에 사로잡힌 채 말짱히 깨어났다. 그 술렁임은 아래 안뜰로, 그리고 회랑으로 번져 갔다. 머지않아 집과 안뜰과 울타리 안에 머물던 모든 사람이 밖으로 나와 하늘을 우러러보았다.\n\n그들이 본 것은 이러했다. 한 줄기 빛이 가장 가까운 별보다도 헤아릴 수 없이 높은 곳에서 시작되어 비스듬히 땅으로 떨어지고 있었다. 꼭대기는 한 점으로 줄어들었고, 밑동은 너비가 수백 미터에 이르렀다. 그 가장자리는 밤의 어둠과 부드럽게 어우러졌고, 그 중심은 장밋빛으로 번쩍이는 광채였다. 그 빛은 마을 남동쪽의 가장 가까운 산 위에 머무는 듯하여, 산마루를 따라 희미한 광환(光環)을 드리웠다. 객사도 그 빛에 닿아 환해진 까닭에, 지붕 위에 있던 이들은 서로의 얼굴을 볼 수 있었으니, 그 얼굴마다 경이로 가득했다.\n\n빛은 몇 분에 걸쳐 한결같이 머물렀고, 그러자 경이는 외경과 두려움으로 바뀌었다. 겁 많은 자들은 떨었고, 가장 대담한 자들도 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"이런 것을 본 적이 있는가?\" 하고 한 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"저 산 바로 위에 있는 것 같소. 무엇인지 알 수 없고, 나도 저런 것은 본 적이 없소.\" 하는 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"별이 터져서 떨어진 것이 아닐까?\" 하고 또 한 사람이 물었으나, 그 말끝은 떨렸다.\n\n\"별이 떨어질 때는 그 빛이 꺼지는 법이오.\"\n\n\"알겠소!\" 하고 한 사람이 자신 있게 외쳤다. \"목자들이 사자를 보고, 양 떼를 지키려고 불을 피운 것이오.\"\n\n말한 이의 곁에 있던 사람들이 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"그렇소, 바로 그것이오! 오늘 양 떼가 저쪽 골짜기에서 풀을 뜯고 있었소.\"\n\n곁에 있던 한 사람이 그 위안을 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"아니오, 아니오! 유다의 온 골짜기에 있는 나무를 모조리 한 더미에 모아 불을 지른다 해도, 그 불길이 저토록 강하고 높은 빛을 던지지는 못할 것이오.\"\n\n그 뒤로 지붕 위에는 정적이 흘렀고, 그 신비가 이어지는 동안 단 한 번 더 깨졌을 뿐이다.\n\n\"형제들이여!\" 하고 위엄 있는 풍모의 한 유대인이 외쳤다. \"우리가 보는 것은 우리 조상 야곱이 꿈에서 본 사다리요. 우리 조상의 주 하느님께 복이 있을지어다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\n베들레헴에서 남동쪽으로 2.5킬로미터, 어쩌면 3킬로미터쯤 떨어진 곳에, 산의 솟아오른 능선을 사이에 두고 마을과 갈라진 평원이 하나 있다. 북풍을 잘 막아 주는 데다, 이 골짜기는 무화과나무와 떡갈나무와 소나무가 자라 우거져 있었고, 인접한 협곡과 골에는 올리브나무와 뽕나무 덤불이 빽빽했다. 모두 이 계절에는 떠도는 양 떼와 염소 떼와 소 떼를 먹이는 데 더없이 귀한 것들이었다.\n\n마을에서 가장 멀리 떨어진 쪽, 벼랑 바로 아래에는 아득한 옛날부터 있어 온 널따란 마라, 곧 양 우리가 자리하고 있었다. 오래전 잊힌 어느 약탈 때에 지붕이 날아가고 거의 허물어진 건물이었다. 그러나 거기 딸린 울타리만큼은 온전히 남아 있었고, 가축을 몰고 그리로 오는 목자들에게는 그 울타리가 집 자체보다 더 요긴했다. 둘레를 두른 돌담은 사람 키만큼 높았으나, 그래도 광야에서 굶주려 온 표범이나 사자가 이따금 대담하게 뛰어들 만큼은 낮았다. 담 안쪽에는 그 끊임없는 위험에 대비한 또 하나의 방비로 갈매나무 울타리를 심어 두었는데, 그 고안이 어찌나 잘 들어맞았는지, 못처럼 단단한 가시가 큼직한 송이를 이룬 채 빽빽이 솟은 가지들 사이로는 이제 참새 한 마리조차 비집고 들기 어려웠다.\n\n앞 장들에서 다룬 일이 벌어진 그날, 양 떼에게 새 풀밭을 찾아 주려는 목자 몇이 가축을 이 평원으로 몰고 올라왔다. 이른 아침부터 숲은 부르는 소리와 도끼질 소리, 양과 염소의 울음소리, 방울 딸랑이는 소리, 소의 음매 소리, 개 짖는 소리로 울려 퍼졌다. 해가 지자 그들은 마라로 길을 잡았고, 어둠이 내릴 무렵에는 모든 것을 우리 안에 안전히 들여놓았다. 그런 다음 문 옆에 불을 피우고, 변변찮은 저녁을 나눠 먹은 뒤, 한 사람에게 망을 맡기고는 둘러앉아 쉬며 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n망보는 이를 빼면 그들은 모두 여섯이었다. 얼마 지나지 않아 불 가까이에 무리를 이루어 모였으니, 더러는 앉고 더러는 엎드려 누웠다. 늘 맨머리로 다니는 까닭에 머리카락은 햇볕에 그을린 채 거칠고 두껍게 곤두서 있었다. 수염은 목을 덮고 가슴까지 덥수룩하게 흘러내렸다. 새끼 염소와 새끼 양의 가죽으로 만든, 털이 그대로 달린 망토가 목에서 무릎까지 그들을 감쌌으나 팔은 드러나 있었다. 그 투박한 옷을 넓은 띠로 허리에 둘러 동였다. 신은 가장 거친 종류의 샌들이었다. 오른쪽 어깨에는 먹을 것과, 그들이 무기로 삼은 무릿매에 쓸 골라 둔 돌멩이를 담은 자루가 매달려 있었다. 저마다 옆 땅바닥에는 지팡이가 놓여 있었으니, 그것은 그들 소명의 표징이자 공격의 무기였다.\n\n이런 자들이 유대의 목자였다! 겉모습은 불가에 함께 둘러앉은 야윈 개들처럼 거칠고 사나웠으나, 실은 마음이 단순하고 정이 깊었다. 그것은 한편으로는 그들이 살아온 원시적인 삶에서 비롯된 것이었으나, 무엇보다도 사랑스럽고 가냘픈 것들을 끊임없이 돌보아 온 데서 온 것이었다.\n\n그들은 쉬며 이야기를 나누었는데, 그 이야기는 온통 양 떼에 관한 것이었다. 세상 사람들에게는 따분한 화제였으나, 그들에게는 그것이 곧 온 세상이었다. 그들이 이야기 가운데 하찮은 일에 오래도록 머무른다면, 또 그들 가운데 누가 새끼 양 한 마리를 잃은 사연을 낱낱이 빠짐없이 늘어놓는다면, 그와 그 가엾은 짐승 사이의 인연을 떠올려 보아야 한다. 양은 태어나면서부터 그가 맡은 것이 되어, 평생토록 그가 지켜야 할 것이 되고, 물을 건널 때 거들어 주어야 하고, 움푹한 골을 내려갈 때 안아 주어야 하며, 이름을 지어 주고 길들여야 할 것이 되었다. 양은 그의 길동무가 되고, 그의 생각과 관심의 대상이 되며, 그의 뜻을 받드는 존재가 되었다. 양은 그의 떠도는 길을 생기 있게 하고 함께 나누었다. 그것을 지키기 위해서라면 그는 사자나 도적과 맞서야 할지도 모르고, 죽어야 할지도 몰랐다.\n\n민족을 지워 버리고 세상의 패권을 뒤바꾸는 큰 사건들도, 행여 그들의 귀에 들어온다 한들 그들에게는 사소한 일이었다. 헤롯이 이 도시 저 도시에서 무엇을 하는지, 궁전과 경기장을 짓고 금지된 짓에 빠져 있다는 소식을 그들은 이따금 들었다. 그 시절의 습성대로 로마는 자기에 대해 굼뜨게 묻는 사람들을 기다려 주지 않았다. 로마가 먼저 그들에게 찾아왔다. 더디게 따라오는 가축 떼를 몰고 가는 언덕에서든, 그것들을 숨겨 둔 외진 산골에서든, 목자는 적잖이 나팔 소리에 깜짝 놀라곤 했다. 내다보면 보병 대대가, 때로는 군단 하나가 행군해 가고 있었다. 그러다 번쩍이는 투구 장식이 사라지고, 그 침입이 불러일으킨 동요가 가라앉으면, 그는 군대의 독수리 기와 도금한 구슬 장식이 무엇을 뜻하는지, 또 제 삶과는 그토록 정반대인 그 삶의 매력이 무엇인지 곰곰이 헤아려 보는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이 사람들은 투박하고 단순하기는 해도 나름의 지식과 지혜를 지니고 있었다. 안식일이면 그들은 으레 몸을 정결히 하고 회당에 올라가, 궤에서 가장 멀리 떨어진 자리에 앉았다. 차잔이 토라를 받들고 돌 때, 그것에 더없이 열렬히 입 맞추는 이가 그들이었다. 셸리아흐가 본문을 읽을 때, 더없이 굳은 믿음으로 해석하는 자의 말에 귀 기울이는 이가 그들이었다. 또 장로의 설교를 누구보다 많이 가슴에 담아 가, 나중에 누구보다 깊이 곱씹는 이가 그들이었다. 셰마의 한 구절 속에서 그들은 단순한 삶에 필요한 모든 가르침과 모든 율법을 찾아냈다. 곧 자기들의 주님은 한 분 하나님이시며, 온 마음을 다해 그분을 사랑해야 한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그들은 그분을 사랑했으니, 그 지혜야말로 왕들의 지혜를 뛰어넘는 것이었다.\n* 차잔: 회당에서 예배를 돕는 봉사자. 셸리아흐: 회중을 대표해 경문을 낭독하는 사람. 셰마: \"이스라엘아 들으라, 우리 하나님 여호와는 오직 유일한 여호와시니\"로 시작하는 유대교의 신앙 고백.\n\n이야기를 나누는 동안, 첫 야경이 채 끝나기도 전에 목자들은 하나둘 잠이 들었으니, 저마다 앉았던 그 자리에 누웠다.\n\n그 밤은 산악 지대의 겨울철 밤이 대개 그러하듯, 맑고 쌀쌀하고 별빛으로 반짝였다. 바람 한 점 없었다. 대기가 그토록 깨끗했던 적이 없는 듯했고, 그 고요함은 단순한 적막 이상이었다. 그것은 거룩한 정적이었으니, 하늘이 귀 기울이는 땅에 무언가 좋은 소식을 속삭이려고 나직이 몸을 굽히고 있다는 예고였다.\n\n문 옆에서 망보는 이는 망토를 바싹 여미고 걸었다. 이따금 그는 잠든 가축 떼 사이의 부스럭거림이나, 산비탈 저편에서 들려오는 자칼의 울음소리에 끌려 걸음을 멈추곤 했다. 한밤중은 더디게 다가왔으나, 마침내 찾아왔다. 그의 일은 끝났다. 이제 고된 노동이 지친 자녀들에게 베푸는 꿈 없는 잠을 누릴 차례였다! 그는 불 쪽으로 발을 옮기다 멈칫했다. 달빛처럼 부드럽고 흰 빛이 그의 둘레로 퍼지고 있었다. 그는 숨을 죽이고 기다렸다. 빛은 점점 짙어졌다. 조금 전까지 보이지 않던 것들이 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 들판 전체와, 그 안에 깃든 모든 것을 보았다. 서리 내린 공기보다 더 날카로운 한기가, 두려움의 한기가 그를 엄습했다. 그가 위를 올려다보니, 별들이 사라지고 없었다. 빛은 하늘의 창에서 쏟아져 내리는 듯했다. 바라보는 사이 그것은 찬란한 광휘로 변했다. 그러자 그는 공포에 질려 외쳤다.\n\n\"일어나, 일어나!\"\n\n개들이 벌떡 일어나 짖어 대며 달아났다.\n\n가축 떼는 어쩔 줄 모른 채 한데 뒤엉켰다.\n\n사람들은 무기를 손에 쥐고 비틀거리며 일어섰다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 그들이 한목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"봐!\" 망보는 이가 외쳤다. \"하늘이 불타고 있어!\"\n\n별안간 빛이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 밝아지자, 그들은 두 눈을 가리고 무릎을 꿇었다. 그러다 영혼이 두려움에 움츠러들며 그들은 앞을 못 보고 정신을 잃은 채 얼굴을 땅에 묻고 엎드렸으니, 한 음성이 그들에게 말하지 않았더라면 죽고 말았을 것이다.\n\"두려워하지 말라!\"\n\n그러자 그들은 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"두려워하지 말라. 보라, 내가 온 백성에게 미칠 큰 기쁨의 좋은 소식을 너희에게 전하노라.\"\n\n그 음성은 사람의 것이라 하기에는 너무도 감미롭고 위로가 깃든, 나직하고 또렷한 음성으로, 그들의 온 존재에 스며들어 그들을 확신으로 채웠다. 그들이 무릎을 짚고 일어나 경배하는 마음으로 우러러보니, 거대한 영광 한가운데에 사람의 형상이 나타나 있었다. 더없이 새하얀 옷을 걸쳤고, 그 어깨 위로는 빛나며 접힌 날개의 끝이 우뚝 솟아 있었다. 이마 위의 별 하나가 헤스페로스처럼 찬란하게 변함없는 광채로 빛났다. 두 손은 축복을 내리듯 그들을 향해 뻗쳐 있었고, 그 얼굴은 평온하고 거룩할 만큼 아름다웠다.\n* 헤스페로스: 그리스 신화에서 저녁별, 곧 금성을 이르는 이름.\n\n그들은 천사에 관해 자주 들어 왔고, 그들 나름의 소박한 방식으로 이야기도 해 왔다. 그래서 이제 그들은 의심하지 않고, 마음속으로 말했다. 하나님의 영광이 우리를 둘러쌌으니, 이는 옛적에 울라이 강가에서 예언자에게 나타나셨던 그분이로다.\n\n곧이어 천사가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오늘 다윗의 동네에 너희를 위하여 구주가 나셨으니, 곧 그리스도 주시니라!\"\n\n다시 한동안 침묵이 흘렀으니, 그동안 그 말이 그들의 마음속에 가라앉았다.\n\n\"너희가 이것으로 표징을 삼을지니라.\" 알리는 이가 다음으로 말했다. \"너희가 강보에 싸여 구유에 누인 아기를 보리라.\"\n\n알리는 이는 다시 말하지 않았다. 그의 좋은 소식은 다 전해졌다. 그래도 그는 잠시 머물렀다. 별안간 그가 중심에 자리한 듯하던 빛이 장밋빛으로 물들며 떨리기 시작했다. 그러더니 위쪽으로, 사람들이 볼 수 있는 한 멀리까지 흰 날개가 번득이고, 눈부신 형상들이 오가며, 한목소리로 노래하는 무리의 음성이 울렸다.\n\n\"지극히 높은 곳에서는 하나님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 그가 기뻐하신 사람들 중에 평화로다!\"\n\n한 번이 아니라 여러 번 거듭하여 찬송했다.\n\n이윽고 알리는 이가 멀리 있는 누군가의 허락을 구하듯 눈을 들었다. 그의 날개가 움직이더니 천천히 위엄 있게 펼쳐졌으니, 윗면은 눈처럼 희고 그늘진 쪽은 진주조개처럼 갖가지 빛깔로 어른거렸다. 날개가 그의 키보다 몇 큐빗이나 더 넓게 펼쳐지자, 그는 가뿐히 떠올라 힘들이는 기색 없이 시야 밖으로 떠가며, 그 빛을 함께 거두어 갔다. 그가 사라진 한참 뒤까지도, 거리에 부드럽게 녹아든 가락이 하늘에서 떨어져 내렸다. \"지극히 높은 곳에서는 하나님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 그가 기뻐하신 사람들 중에 평화로다.\"\n* 큐빗: 팔꿈치에서 가운뎃손가락 끝까지의 길이로, 약 45센티미터에 해당하는 옛 척도.\n\n목자들이 완전히 정신을 차렸을 때, 그들은 한 사람이 입을 열기까지 서로 멍하니 바라보았다. \"사람들에게 보내신 주님의 사자, 가브리엘이었어.\"\n\n아무도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"그리스도 주께서 나셨다고, 그렇게 말씀하지 않았나?\"\n\n그러자 다른 이가 목소리를 되찾고 대답했다. \"그렇게 말씀하셨지.\"\n\n\"또 다윗의 동네라고, 곧 저쪽 우리 베들레헴이라고 말씀하지 않았나? 그리고 우리가 강보에 싸인 아기를 보게 되리라고?\"\n\n\"구유에 누인 아기를 말이지.\"\n\n처음 말한 이가 생각에 잠겨 불을 들여다보다가, 마침내 문득 결심이라도 한 듯이 말했다. \"베들레헴에서 구유가 있는 곳은 단 한 군데뿐이야. 단 한 군데, 옛 여관 가까이에 있는 동굴이지. 형제들이여, 가서 이 일어난 일을 봅시다. 제사장과 학자들은 오래도록 그리스도를 기다려 왔소. 이제 그분이 나셨고, 주께서 우리에게 그분을 알아볼 표징을 주셨소. 올라가서 그분께 경배합시다.\"\n\n\"하지만 양 떼는!\"\n\n\"주께서 돌보아 주실 거요. 서두릅시다.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 모두 일어나 마라를 떠났다.\n\n산을 돌아 마을을 가로질러 그들은 여관 문에 이르렀는데, 거기에는 망보는 사람이 하나 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 우리는 엄청난 일을 보고 들었소.\" 그들이 대답했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 우리도 굉장한 것을 보긴 했으나 들은 것은 없소. 당신들은 무엇을 들었소?\"\n\n\"확인할 수 있도록 울타리 안의 동굴로 내려가 봅시다. 그러면 모두 이야기해 드리리다. 우리와 함께 가서 직접 보시오.\"\n\n\"바보 같은 헛걸음이오.\"\n\n\"아니오, 그리스도께서 나셨소.\"\n\n\"그리스도라니! 어떻게 아오?\"\n\n\"먼저 가서 봅시다.\"\n\n그 사람이 비웃듯 웃었다.\n\n\"그리스도라니, 참! 당신들이 그분을 어떻게 알아보겠소?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 나셨고, 지금 구유에 누여 계신다고 우리가 들었소. 그리고 베들레헴에서 구유가 있는 곳은 단 한 군데뿐이오.\"\n\"그 동굴 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 우리와 함께 갑시다.\"\n\n그들은 아무 눈길도 끌지 않고 안뜰을 가로질러 갔다. 그때까지도 그 놀라운 빛에 대해 이야기하며 깨어 있는 사람이 더러 있었으나 마찬가지였다. 동굴 문은 열려 있었다. 안에는 등불이 타고 있었고, 그들은 거리낌 없이 들어섰다.\n\n\"평안하시오.\" 망보는 이가 요셉과 벳다곤 사람에게 말했다. \"여기 오늘 밤에 태어난 아기를 찾는 사람들이 있소. 강보에 싸여 구유에 누인 것으로 그 아기를 알아보게 되리라 하오.\"\n\n한순간 그 무덤덤한 나사렛 사람의 얼굴이 흔들렸다. 그는 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"아기는 여기 있소.\"\n\n그들은 구유 가운데 하나로 이끌렸고, 거기에 아기가 있었다. 등불을 가져왔고, 목자들은 말없이 곁에 섰다. 아기는 아무런 기척도 없었으니, 갓 태어난 다른 아기들과 다름없었다.\n\n\"어머니는 어디 계시오?\" 망보는 이가 물었다.\n\n여인들 가운데 하나가 아기를 받아, 가까이 누워 있는 마리아에게 가서 그 품에 안겨 주었다. 그러자 둘러섰던 이들이 두 사람 곁으로 모여들었다.\n\n\"그리스도시다!\" 마침내 한 목자가 말했다.\n\n\"그리스도시다!\" 그들 모두 되뇌며 무릎을 꿇고 경배했다. 그들 가운데 하나가 여러 번 거듭하여 말했다.\n\n\"주님이시다. 그분의 영광이 땅과 하늘 위에 있도다.\"\n\n그리고 그 순박한 사람들은 조금도 의심하지 않고 어머니의 옷자락에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 떠났다. 여관에서 그들은 잠을 깨어 둘레로 몰려드는 모든 사람에게 자기들의 이야기를 들려주었다. 그리고 마을을 지나, 마라로 돌아가는 내내 그들은 천사들의 노랫가락을 읊조렸다. \"지극히 높은 곳에서는 하나님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 그가 기뻐하신 사람들 중에 평화로다!\"\n\n그 이야기는 널리 퍼졌으니, 그토록 많은 이가 본 빛이 그것을 뒷받침했다. 이튿날, 그리고 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 그 동굴은 호기심에 찬 무리의 발길이 이어졌는데, 더러는 믿었으나 대부분은 비웃고 조롱했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\n동굴에서 아기가 태어난 지 열하루째 되는 날, 오후가 절반쯤 기울었을 무렵, 세 명의 동방박사가 세겜에서 이어지는 길을 따라 예루살렘으로 다가왔다. 기드론 시내를 건넌 뒤로 그들은 수많은 사람과 마주쳤는데, 그 누구도 걸음을 멈추고 호기심 어린 눈으로 그들을 돌아보지 않는 자가 없었다.\n\n유대는 그 지형의 필연으로 국제적인 통로였다. 동쪽 사막의 압력과 서쪽 바다의 압력에 떠밀려 솟아오른 듯한 좁은 산등성이, 그것이 유대가 내세울 수 있는 전부였다. 그러나 그 산등성이를 넘어 자연은 동방과 남방을 잇는 교역의 길을 펼쳐 놓았으니, 그것이 곧 유대의 재산이었다. 다시 말해, 예루살렘의 부는 지나가는 상업에 매기는 통행세였다. 그 결과 로마를 제외하면 그 어디에도 그토록 많은 나라의 그토록 많은 사람이 끊임없이 모여드는 곳은 없었으며, 그 성벽과 변두리 안에서만큼 낯선 이가 주민에게 덜 낯설게 여겨지는 도시도 없었다. 그런데도 이 세 사람만은 성문으로 가는 길에서 마주치는 모든 이의 경탄을 자아냈다.\n\n왕들의 무덤 맞은편 길가에 앉은 어느 아낙들의 아이 하나가 일행이 다가오는 것을 보았다. 아이는 곧장 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다. \"봐, 봐! 정말 예쁜 방울이야! 정말 커다란 낙타야!\"\n\n방울은 은으로 되어 있었다. 낙타들은 앞서 보았듯 유난히 크고 희었으며, 보기 드문 위엄을 갖추고 움직였다. 그 장구는 사막과 그 위에서의 긴 여정을, 또한 작은 차일 아래 앉은 주인들이 넉넉한 재력을 지녔음을 말해 주었으니, 그들은 제벨 너머의 약속 장소에서 보았던 모습 그대로였다. 그러나 그토록 경이로운 것은 방울도 낙타도, 그 장비도, 탄 이들의 풍모도 아니었다. 그것은 세 사람 가운데 맨 앞에 탄 자가 던진 물음이었다.\n\n북쪽에서 예루살렘으로 들어가는 길은 남쪽으로 기울어진 평원을 가로지르는데, 그 평원은 다마스쿠스 성문을 골짜기, 곧 우묵한 자리에 남겨 두고 있다. 길은 좁으나 오랜 통행으로 깊이 패었고, 빗물에 씻겨 헐겁고 메마르게 남은 자갈 탓에 군데군데 다니기 힘들었다. 그러나 양옆으로는 옛적에 기름진 들판과 보기 좋은 올리브 숲이 펼쳐져 있었으니, 그 무성한 자람은 분명 아름다웠을 것이며, 특히 사막의 황무지에서 갓 빠져나온 나그네들의 눈에는 더욱 그러했을 것이다. 이 길에서 세 사람은 무덤 앞의 일행 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"선한 이들이여.\" 발타사르가 땋은 수염을 쓰다듬으며 가마에서 몸을 굽혀 말했다. \"예루살렘이 가까이 있지 않소?\"\n\n\"네.\" 아이가 품으로 파고든 아낙이 대답했다. \"저 언덕의 나무들이 조금만 더 낮았어도 장터의 탑들이 보였을 거예요.\"\n\n발타사르는 그리스인과 힌두인을 바라보더니 물었다.\n\n\"유대인의 왕으로 나신 분이 어디에 계시오?\"\n\n아낙들은 대답 없이 서로 마주 보기만 했다.\n\n\"그분에 대해 들은 바가 없소?\"\n\n\"없습니다.\"\n\"그렇다면 모든 이에게 전하시오. 우리가 동방에서 그분의 별을 보고 그분께 경배하러 왔노라고.\"\n\n그러고서 세 사람은 길을 이어 갔다. 다른 이들에게도 같은 물음을 던졌으나, 결과는 한가지였다. 예레미야 동굴로 가던 한 무리는 그 물음과 나그네들의 모습에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 발길을 돌려 그들을 따라 성안으로 들어왔다.\n\n세 사람은 자신들의 사명에 대한 생각에 어찌나 골똘했던지, 이윽고 더없이 장려하게 눈앞에 솟아오른 광경에는 마음을 두지 않았다. 베제다에서 맨 처음 그들을 맞이한 마을에도, 왼편 저쪽의 미스바와 올리벳에도, 마을 뒤편으로 마흔 개의 높고 견고한 탑이 늘어선 성벽에도—그 탑들은 일부는 방비를 위해, 일부는 왕다운 건설자의 까다로운 안목을 흡족케 하려고 덧세운 것이었다—그리고 같은 탑들이 박힌 성벽이 수없이 많은 모퉁이를 이루며 오른편으로 휘어 가다가, 군데군데 성가퀴를 두른 문을 거쳐 파사엘루스, 마리암네, 히피쿠스라는 세 채의 거대한 흰 건축물에 이르는 광경에도, 대리석 궁전으로 꼭대기가 빛나며 이제껏 본 적 없이 아름다운, 언덕들 가운데 가장 높은 시온에도, 두말할 것 없이 세상의 경이 가운데 하나인, 모리아 위 성전의 반짝이는 단들에도, 거룩한 도시가 마치 거대한 그릇의 우묵한 바닥에 담긴 듯 보이도록 사방을 두른 위풍당당한 산들에도, 그들은 마음을 두지 않았다.\n\n그들은 마침내 매우 높고 견고한 탑 하나에 이르렀다. 그 탑은 당시 오늘날의 다마스쿠스 성문에 해당하던 성문을 내려다보며, 세겜과 여리고와 기브온에서 오는 세 갈래 길이 만나는 자리를 표시하고 있었다. 로마 수비대가 통로를 지켰다. 이때쯤이면 낙타를 따르던 사람들이 성문 어귀에서 어슬렁거리던 한량들까지 끌어들일 만큼 긴 행렬을 이루었으니, 발타사르가 보초에게 말을 걸려고 멈추었을 때, 세 사람은 곧장 오가는 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않으려는 빽빽한 무리의 한가운데에 놓이게 되었다.\n\n\"그대에게 평화를 비오.\" 이집트인이 또렷한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n보초는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"우리는 유대인의 왕으로 나신 분을 찾아 먼 길을 왔소. 그분이 어디 계신지 말해 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n병사는 투구의 면갑을 들어 올리고 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 통로 오른편의 한 방에서 장교가 나타났다.\n\n\"비켜라.\" 그가 이제 더 바싹 밀려드는 군중을 향해 외쳤다. 그들이 더디게 따르는 듯하자, 그는 투창을 좌우로 힘차게 휘두르며 앞으로 나아가, 그렇게 자리를 확보했다.\n\n\"무엇을 바라오?\" 그가 그 도시의 말씨로 발타사르에게 물었다.\n\n발타사르도 같은 말씨로 대답했다.\n\n\"유대인의 왕으로 나신 분이 어디 계시오?\"\n\n\"헤롯 말이오?\" 장교가 어리둥절하여 물었다.\n\n\"헤롯의 왕권은 카이사르에게서 나온 것, 헤롯이 아니오.\"\n\n\"유대인의 왕은 그분 말고 따로 없소.\"\n\n\"그러나 우리는 우리가 찾는 분의 별을 보았고, 그분께 경배하러 왔소.\"\n\n로마인은 난처해졌다.\n\n\"더 가 보시오.\" 그가 마침내 말했다. \"더 가 보시오. 나는 유대인이 아니오. 그 물음일랑 성전의 학자들이나, 제사장 한나스에게, 아니 차라리 헤롯에게 직접 가져가시오. 만약 다른 유대인의 왕이 있다면, 그가 찾아낼 것이오.\"\n\n그러고서 그는 이방인들에게 길을 터 주었고, 그들은 성문을 지났다. 그러나 좁은 거리로 들어서기에 앞서, 발타사르는 잠시 머물러 벗들에게 말했다. \"우리는 충분히 알려졌소. 자정이면 온 도시가 우리와 우리의 사명에 대해 듣게 될 것이오. 이제 객사로 갑시다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\n그날 저녁, 해가 지기 전이었다. 몇몇 여인이 실로암 못의 분지로 내려가는 계단의 맨 윗단에서 빨래를 하고 있었다. 저마다 넓은 질그릇 대야 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다. 계단 아래쪽에 있던 한 소녀가 그들에게 물을 길어다 주었는데, 항아리를 채우며 노래를 불렀다. 노래는 흥겨웠고, 그들의 수고를 한결 가볍게 덜어 주는 것이 분명했다. 이따금 그들은 발뒤꿈치에 엉덩이를 올려 앉고는 오펠의 비탈을 올려다보거나, 지금은 멸시의 산이라 불리는 봉우리 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸으니, 저무는 해가 그 봉우리를 어렴풋이 곱게 물들이고 있었다.\n\n그들이 대야 속의 빨래를 비비고 짜며 손을 놀리고 있을 때, 다른 두 여인이 저마다 빈 항아리를 어깨에 인 채 그들에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"평안하기를.\" 새로 온 여인 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n빨래하던 여인들은 손을 멈추고 몸을 일으켜 손에서 물을 짜내고는 인사에 답했다.\n\n\"이제 거의 밤이 다 됐네. 일을 그만둘 때야.\"\n\n\"일에는 끝이 없는 법이지.\" 하는 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"하지만 쉴 때가 있고, 또—\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 들을 때도 있지.\" 다른 여인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"무슨 소식이라도 있나?\"\n\n\"그럼 아직 못 들었어?\"\n\"못 들었어.\"\n\n\"그리스도가 태어났다고들 해.\" 소식을 전하는 여인이 곧장 이야기 속으로 뛰어들며 말했다.\n\n빨래하던 여인들의 얼굴이 호기심으로 환해지는 모습이 흥미로웠다. 다른 편에서는 항아리가 내려졌고, 그것은 이내 주인들의 걸상이 되었다.\n\n\"그리스도라고!\" 듣던 여인들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇게들 말해.\"\n\n\"누가?\"\n\n\"다들 그래. 흔히 떠도는 이야기야.\"\n\n\"믿는 사람이 있긴 한가?\"\n\n\"오늘 오후에 세 사람이 세겜에서 오는 길로 기드론 시내를 건너왔어.\" 말하는 여인은 의심을 잠재울 셈으로 조목조목 자세히 늘어놓았다. \"저마다 티끌 하나 없이 새하얀 낙타를 탔는데, 예루살렘에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 큰 낙타였다지.\"\n\n듣던 여인들의 눈과 입이 크게 벌어졌다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 얼마나 위대하고 부유한지를 보여 주듯,\" 이야기를 전하는 여인이 말을 이었다. \"비단 차일 아래 앉아 있었고, 안장의 죔쇠는 금이었으며, 굴레의 술도 금이었어. 방울은 은으로 되어 진짜 음악 소리를 냈다지. 아무도 그들을 알아보지 못했어. 마치 세상 끝에서 온 사람들 같았대. 그 가운데 한 사람만 입을 열었는데, 길에서 만나는 누구에게나, 여자나 아이에게까지 이렇게 물었대. '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?' 아무도 대답해 주지 못했어. 무슨 뜻인지 아무도 이해하지 못했으니까. 그래서 그들은 이 말을 남기고 지나갔어. '우리가 동방에서 그의 별을 보고 그에게 경배하러 왔노라.' 성문에 있는 로마 사람에게도 그 질문을 던졌는데, 그 사람도 길에서 만난 순박한 백성보다 나을 게 없어서, 그들을 헤롯에게로 올려보냈대.\"\n\n\"그 사람들 지금 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"여관에 있어. 벌써 수백 명이 그들을 보러 다녀갔고, 또 수백 명이 가는 중이야.\"\n\n\"그들이 누구야?\"\n\n\"아무도 몰라. 페르시아 사람들이라고들 해. 별을 보고 이야기하는 현자들이래. 어쩌면 엘리야나 예레미야 같은 예언자일지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"유대인의 왕이라는 게 무슨 뜻이지?\"\n\n\"그리스도 말이야. 그리고 그분이 막 태어나셨다는 거지.\"\n\n여인 가운데 하나가 웃음을 터뜨리고 다시 일을 시작하며 말했다. \"그럼, 내 눈으로 보면 믿겠지.\"\n\n다른 여인도 그를 따라 했다. \"나도 그래. 그분이 죽은 자를 일으키시는 걸 보면, 그땐 믿겠어.\"\n\n세 번째 여인이 조용히 말했다. \"그분이 오신다는 약속은 오래전부터 있었지. 나는 그분이 문둥병자 하나라도 고치시는 걸 보면 그것으로 족해.\"\n\n그리고 일행은 밤이 올 때까지 이야기를 나누다가, 차가운 밤공기에 떠밀려 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n그날 저녁 늦게, 초경(初更)이 시작될 무렵, 시온산 궁전에 쉰 명쯤 되는 사람들이 모여 있었다. 그들은 헤롯의 명령이 아니고서는 결코 한자리에 모이는 법이 없었으니, 그 명령이란 그가 유대 율법과 역사의 깊은 신비 가운데 하나나 둘쯤을 알고자 할 때만 내려지는 것이었다. 요컨대 그것은 학교의 교사들과 대제사장들, 그리고 도성에서 학식으로 가장 이름난 학자들의 모임이었다. 여론을 이끄는 지도자들, 갖가지 교리를 풀이하는 자들, 사두개파의 우두머리들, 바리새파의 논객들, 그리고 차분하고 부드러운 말씨에 금욕적인 에세네 공동체의 철학자들이 모인 자리였다.\n\n회의가 열린 방은 궁전 안뜰 가운데 하나에 딸린 것으로, 꽤 넓고 로마풍이었다. 바닥은 대리석 조각으로 모자이크가 놓여 있었고, 창 하나 없이 이어진 벽은 사프란빛 노랑 화판(畵板)으로 프레스코가 그려져 있었다. 방 한가운데에는 긴 의자가 놓여 있었는데, 밝은 노란빛 천을 씌운 방석으로 덮여 있었고, 알파벳 U자 모양으로, 그 트인 쪽이 출입문을 향하도록 만들어져 있었다. 의자가 둥글게 휘어진 안쪽, 말하자면 그 글자가 구부러진 자리에는 금과 은으로 정교하게 상감(象嵌)을 놓은 거대한 청동 삼각대가 있었고, 그 위로는 천장에서 촛대가 드리워져 있었으니, 일곱 개의 가지마다 불 켜진 등잔을 하나씩 받쳐 들고 있었다. 그 긴 의자와 등잔은 순전히 유대식이었다.\n\n사람들은 동방인의 방식대로 긴 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 빛깔만 다를 뿐 차림새가 유난히 한결같았다. 대부분 나이가 지긋한 사람들이었다. 거대한 수염이 그들의 얼굴을 뒤덮었고, 큼직한 코에 더하여, 짙게 그늘진 굵은 눈썹 아래 큼직한 검은 눈이 자리하고 있었다. 그들의 태도는 진중하고 위엄 있었으며, 가히 족장다웠다. 요컨대 그들의 회합은 산헤드린의 회합이었다.\n\n그러나 삼각대 앞, 긴 의자의 머리라 할 만한 자리에 앉아, 다른 모든 동료를 좌우에, 동시에 자기 앞에 두고 있는 자, 분명 이 모임의 의장인 그 사람은 보는 이의 눈길을 단번에 사로잡았을 것이다. 그는 큰 틀로 빚어진 사람이었으나, 이제는 오싹할 만큼 쪼그라들고 굽어 있었다. 흰 겉옷이 어깨에서 흘러내리며 주름을 이루었으나, 그 주름은 근육은커녕 모난 뼈대 말고는 아무것도 짐작하게 하지 못했다. 희고 진홍빛 줄무늬가 있는 비단 소매에 반쯤 가려진 그의 두 손은 무릎 위에 모여 있었다. 말을 할 때면, 이따금 오른손 집게손가락이 떨리며 뻗어 나왔다. 그 밖의 몸짓은 할 수 없는 듯했다. 그러나 그의 머리는 빛나는 둥근 지붕이었다. 곱게 뽑은 은보다 더 흰 머리카락 몇 가닥이 그 밑동을 둘러쌌고, 넓고 둥글게 부푼 두개골 위로 살갗이 팽팽히 당겨져 빛 속에서 또렷한 광채로 빛났다. 관자놀이는 깊이 패었고, 거기서 이마가 주름진 바위처럼 불거져 나왔다. 두 눈은 흐릿하고 희미했으며, 코는 야위었고, 아래 얼굴은 온통 아론의 것처럼 흘러내리는 거룩한 수염에 감싸여 있었다. 이가 바로 바빌로니아 사람 힐렐이었다! 이스라엘에서 오래전에 끊긴 예언자의 계보는 이제 학자들의 계보로 이어졌으니, 그가 그 가운데 학식으로 으뜸이었다. 신의 영감만 없을 뿐, 그는 다른 모든 면에서 예언자였다! 백여섯 살의 나이에도 그는 여전히 대학교의 학장이었다.\n\n그의 앞 탁자에는 히브리 글자가 적힌 양피지 두루마리 한 권이 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 뒤에는 화려하게 차려입은 시동이 시중을 들려고 서 있었다.\n\n토론이 있었으나, 우리가 이 자리에 들어선 이 순간에 사람들은 이미 결론에 다다라 있었다. 저마다 쉬는 자세를 취하고 있었고, 존엄한 힐렐은 움직이지도 않은 채 시동을 불렀다.\n\n\"이리 오너라!\"\n소년이 공손히 다가왔다.\n\n\"가서 왕께 우리가 답을 드릴 준비가 되었다고 아뢰어라.\"\n\n소년이 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n얼마 뒤 두 명의 관리가 들어와 문 양옆에 하나씩 멈춰 섰다. 그들 뒤로 더없이 인상적인 한 인물이 천천히 따라 들어왔다. 진홍빛으로 가장자리를 두른 자줏빛 겉옷을 입은 노인이었으니, 가죽처럼 부드러울 만큼 가늘게 엮은 금띠로 허리를 둘렀고, 신발 끈에는 보석이 반짝였다. 가장 부드러운 진홍빛 우단으로 된 타르부시 위로 가는 줄세공 왕관이 빛났는데, 그 타르부시가 머리를 감싸고 목과 어깨로 흘러내리면서도 목과 멱은 드러내 놓고 있었다. 인장(印章) 대신 단검이 그의 허리띠에 매달려 있었다. 그는 절뚝거리는 걸음으로, 지팡이에 무겁게 기대어 걸었다. 긴 의자가 트인 곳에 이르기까지 그는 멈추지도, 바닥에서 눈을 들지도 않았다. 그러다 마치 처음으로 사람들의 존재를 의식하고 그 존재에 자극을 받기라도 한 듯, 그는 몸을 일으켜 거만하게 둘러보았으니, 마치 깜짝 놀라 적을 찾는 사람 같았다. 그만큼 그 눈길은 어둡고 의심에 차 있었으며 위협적이었다. 이가 바로 헤롯 대왕이었으니, 질병으로 무너진 몸, 죄악으로 지져진 양심, 웅장하리만치 유능한 정신, 카이사르들과 형제 될 만한 영혼을 지닌 자였다. 이제 예순일곱 살이었으나, 일찍이 그토록 빈틈없이 경계한 적 없는 시기심으로, 일찍이 그토록 전제적이었던 적 없는 권력으로, 일찍이 그토록 무자비했던 적 없는 잔인함으로 자기 왕좌를 지키고 있었다.\n\n모인 사람들 사이에서 일제히 움직임이 일었다. 더 나이 든 자들은 몸을 굽혀 절을 했고, 더 신하다운 자들은 자리에서 일어나 곧이어 깊이 무릎을 굽히고 손을 수염이나 가슴에 얹었다.\n\n살펴보기를 마친 헤롯은 앞으로 나아가, 존엄한 힐렐의 맞은편 삼각대 앞에 이르렀다. 힐렐은 그 차가운 눈길을 머리를 숙이고 두 손을 살짝 들어 맞이했다.\n\n\"답을 다오!\" 왕이 거만하리만치 간결하게, 힐렐에게 말하며 두 손으로 지팡이를 제 앞에 짚었다. \"답을 다오!\"\n\n장로의 두 눈이 부드럽게 빛났다. 그는 머리를 들어 묻는 자의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 답했고, 그의 동료들은 그에게 가장 깊은 주의를 기울였다.\n\n\"오, 왕이시여, 하느님의, 아브라함과 이삭과 야곱의 평안이 임금님과 함께하기를!\"\n\n그의 태도는 기원을 올리는 것이었다. 그는 태도를 바꾸어 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"임금님께서는 그리스도가 어디서 태어나야 하는지를 저희에게 물으셨습니다.\"\n\n왕이 고개를 숙였으나, 그 사악한 눈은 현자의 얼굴에 고정된 채였다.\n\n\"그것이 질문이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 오 왕이시여, 저 자신과 여기 모인 제 모든 형제를 대신하여, 한 사람도 이의가 없으니, 저는 말씀드립니다. 유대 베들레헴이옵니다.\"\n\n힐렐은 삼각대 위의 양피지를 흘긋 보고는, 떨리는 손가락으로 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"유대 베들레헴이니, 예언자가 이렇게 기록하였기 때문입니다. '또 유대 땅 베들레헴아, 너는 유대 고을 중에서 가장 작지 아니하도다. 네게서 한 다스리는 자가 나와서 내 백성 이스라엘을 다스리리라.'\"\n\n헤롯의 얼굴에는 근심이 어렸고, 그가 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안 그의 눈은 양피지에 떨어졌다. 그를 지켜보는 자들은 거의 숨도 쉬지 못했다. 그들도 입을 열지 않았고, 그도 입을 열지 않았다. 마침내 그는 몸을 돌려 방을 떠났다.\n\n\"형제들이여,\" 힐렐이 말했다. \"우리는 물러나도 좋소.\"\n\n그러자 사람들이 일어나, 무리를 지어 떠나갔다.\n\n\"시므온.\" 힐렐이 다시 불렀다.\n\n쉰은 족히 되었으나 한창때의 정정함을 지닌 한 사람이 대답하며 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"거룩한 양피지를 거두어라, 내 아들아. 정성스레 말아 두어라.\"\n\n명령대로 따랐다.\n\n\"이제 내게 네 팔을 빌려다오. 가마로 가야겠다.\"\n\n건장한 사내가 몸을 굽혔다. 노인은 시든 손으로 내미는 부축을 받아 몸을 일으키고는, 힘없이 문으로 걸어갔다.\n\n이리하여 그 이름난 학장은 떠났고, 지혜와 학식과 직위에서 그의 후계자가 될 그의 아들 시므온도 함께 떠났다.\n\n그날 저녁 더 늦게, 현자들은 여관의 한 레웬에 누워 잠들지 못하고 있었다. 베개 삼은 돌멩이가 그들의 머리를 받쳐 주어, 트인 아치 너머로 하늘 깊은 곳을 내다볼 수 있었다. 그들은 별들의 반짝임을 바라보며 다음 계시를 생각했다. 그것은 어떻게 올 것인가? 어떤 것일까? 그들은 마침내 예루살렘에 와 있었다. 성문에서 그들이 찾는 분에 대해 물었고, 그분의 탄생을 증언했다. 이제 남은 일은 그분을 찾는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 그 일에 관해서는 모든 것을 성령께 맡겼다. 하느님의 음성에 귀 기울이거나 하늘에서 내려올 표징을 기다리는 사람은 잠을 이루지 못하는 법이다.\n* 레웬: 여관 안뜰을 향해 트인, 지붕 덮인 거처.\n\n그들이 이런 상태에 있을 때, 한 사람이 아치 아래로 들어서며 레웬을 어둡게 만들었다.\n\n\"일어나시오!\" 그가 그들에게 말했다. \"미룰 수 없는 전갈을 가져왔소이다.\"\n\n그들 모두 몸을 일으켜 앉았다.\n\n\"누구에게서 온 것이오?\" 이집트인이 물었다.\n\n\"헤롯 왕에게서요.\"\n\n저마다 마음에 전율이 흐르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"당신은 이 여관의 관리인이 아니오?\" 발타사르가 이어 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"왕께서 무슨 일로 우리를 찾으시오?\"\n\n\"왕의 사자가 밖에 있으니, 그가 답할 것이외다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그에게, 우리가 나갈 때까지 기다리라 이르시오.\"\n\n\"형제여, 그대가 옳았소!\" 관리인이 가고 나자 그리스인이 말했다. \"길에서 백성에게, 또 성문에서 경비병에게 던진 그 질문이 우리를 단번에 유명하게 만들었구려. 나는 마음이 급하오. 어서 일어납시다.\"\n\n그들은 일어나 신을 신고, 외투를 몸에 둘러 단단히 여미고는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"인사를 드리며 평안을 빕니다. 그리고 용서를 청합니다. 제 주인이신 왕께서 저를 보내, 여러분을 궁전으로 모셔 오라 하셨습니다. 거기서 여러분과 은밀히 말씀을 나누고자 하십니다.\"\n\n이렇게 사자는 제 임무를 다했다.\n\n입구에는 등잔 하나가 걸려 있었고, 그 불빛으로 그들은 서로를 바라보며 성령이 자기들 위에 임하셨음을 알았다. 그러자 이집트인이 관리인에게 다가가, 다른 이들에게 들리지 않게 말했다. \"당신은 우리 짐이 안뜰 어디에 보관되어 있는지, 우리 낙타가 어디서 쉬고 있는지 알 것이오. 우리가 떠나 있는 동안, 만일 필요하게 되거든 우리가 떠날 채비를 모두 갖추어 두시오.\"\n\n\"안심하고 가십시오. 저를 믿으시면 됩니다.\" 관리인이 답했다.\n\n\"왕의 뜻이 곧 우리의 뜻이오.\" 발타사르가 사자에게 말했다. \"당신을 따르겠소.\"\n\n거룩한 도성의 거리는 그때도 지금처럼 좁았으나, 그토록 거칠거나 더럽지는 않았다. 위대한 건설자가 아름다움에 만족하지 않고 청결함과 편리함까지도 강제했기 때문이다. 길잡이를 따라 형제들은 한마디 말도 없이 나아갔다. 양쪽 벽 때문에 한층 더 어둑해진 어슴푸레한 별빛 속을, 때로는 지붕과 지붕을 잇는 다리 아래로 거의 자취를 감추다시피 하면서, 그들은 낮은 땅에서 언덕으로 올라갔다. 마침내 그들은 길을 가로질러 세운 대문에 이르렀다. 그 앞 두 개의 큰 화로에서 타오르는 불빛으로, 그들은 그 건물의 모습과 함께, 무기에 꼼짝없이 기댄 채 서 있는 몇몇 경비병의 모습을 얼핏 보았다. 그들은 아무런 제지도 받지 않고 건물 안으로 들어갔다. 그러고는 복도와 아치형 회랑을 지나, 안뜰을 가로지르고, 늘 환히 밝혀져 있지만은 않은 줄기둥 아래를 지나며, 긴 계단을 여러 차례 올라, 수없이 많은 주랑과 방을 거쳐, 매우 높은 탑으로 인도되었다. 갑자기 길잡이가 걸음을 멈추고, 열린 문 너머를 가리키며 그들에게 말했다.\n\n\"드십시오. 왕께서 거기 계십니다.\"\n\n방 안의 공기는 백단향 내음으로 무거웠고, 안의 온갖 세간은 여인의 것처럼 사치스러웠다. 바닥의 한가운데를 덮어 보풀이 인 양탄자가 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 위에 왕좌가 놓여 있었다. 그러나 방문객들에게는 그곳에 대한 어렴풋한 인상을 잡을 틈밖에 없었다. 새기고 도금한 안락의자와 긴 의자들, 부채와 항아리와 악기들, 제 불빛에 반짝이는 황금 촛대들, 그리고 관능적인 그리스 화풍으로 그려진 벽들이었으니, 그 벽을 한 번 본 어느 바리새인은 거룩한 두려움에 머리를 감싸 쥐었을 정도였다. 그들을 맞이하려 왕좌에 앉아 있는 헤롯은, 학자들과 율법학자들과 회의할 때와 같은 차림으로, 그들의 온 마음을 사로잡았다.\n\n청하지도 않았으나 그들이 다가선 양탄자 가장자리에서 그들은 엎드려 절했다. 왕이 종을 울렸다. 시종 하나가 들어와 왕좌 앞에 걸상 셋을 놓았다.\n\n\"앉으시오.\" 군주가 너그럽게 말했다.\n\n\"북문에서,\" 그들이 자리에 앉자 그가 말을 이었다. \"오늘 오후에 보고를 받았소. 세 명의 이방인이 도착했는데, 기이하게 짐승을 타고, 마치 먼 나라에서 온 듯하다고 하더이다. 그대들이 그 사람들이오?\"\n\n이집트인은 그리스인과 힌두인의 눈짓을 받고, 더없이 깊은 절을 올리며 답했다. \"저희가 지금의 저희와 다른 자들이었다면, 그 명성이 온 세상에 향처럼 퍼져 있는 위대하신 헤롯께서 저희를 부르지 않으셨을 것입니다. 저희가 그 이방인들임을 의심할 수 없겠나이다.\"\n\n헤롯은 손을 한 번 저어 그 말을 받아들였다.\n\n\"그대들은 누구요? 어디서 왔소?\" 그가 물으며 의미심장하게 덧붙였다. \"각자 자기 이야기를 하시오.\"\n\n그들은 차례로 그에게 사정을 아뢰며, 자기들이 태어난 도시와 나라, 그리고 예루살렘에 이른 경로를 간단히 들려주었다. 다소 실망한 헤롯은 더 직접적으로 그들을 다그쳤다.\n\n\"그대들이 성문의 관리에게 던진 질문이 무엇이었소?\"\n\n\"유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐고 물었습니다.\"\n\n\"이제 알겠소, 백성이 왜 그토록 궁금해했는지. 그대들은 나도 못지않게 흥분시키는구려. 또 다른 유대인의 왕이 있단 말이오?\"\n\n이집트인은 낯빛 하나 변하지 않았다.\n\n\"갓 태어나신 분이 한 분 계십니다.\"\n\n군주의 어두운 얼굴에 고통의 기색이 어렸으니, 마치 그의 마음이 어떤 쓰라린 기억에 휩쓸리기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"내게는 아니지, 내게는 아니야!\" 그가 부르짖었다.\n\n어쩌면 그가 죽인 제 자식들의 책망하는 모습이 그의 앞을 스쳐 지나갔는지도 몰랐다. 그 감정이 무엇이었든 거기서 벗어난 그는 차분하게 물었다. \"그 새로운 왕이 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"오 왕이시여, 그것이 바로 저희가 여쭙고 싶은 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그대들은 내게 경이를 가져왔구려. 솔로몬의 어떤 수수께끼보다도 뛰어난 수수께끼를.\" 묻는 자가 이어 말했다. \"보다시피 나는 호기심이 어린 시절처럼 걷잡을 수 없게 되는 나이에 이르렀으니, 그것을 가지고 장난치는 것은 곧 잔인한 일이오. 더 말해 주시오. 그러면 나는 왕이 왕을 예우하듯 그대들을 예우하겠소. 그 갓 태어난 이에 관해 그대들이 아는 모든 것을 내게 일러 주시오. 그러면 나도 그대들과 함께 그를 찾는 일에 나서겠소. 그를 찾거든, 나는 그대들이 바라는 대로 하겠소. 그를 예루살렘으로 데려와 제왕의 도를 가르치겠고, 카이사르에 대한 내 은총을 써서 그의 영달과 영광을 도모하겠소. 우리 사이에 시기심이 끼어들지 못하게 하리니, 내 맹세하오. 그러나 먼저 말해 주시오. 바다와 사막으로 그토록 멀리 떨어져 있는 그대들이 어찌하여 모두 그의 소식을 듣게 되었는지를.\"\n\n\"진실로 말씀드리겠나이다, 오 왕이시여.\"\n\n\"말해 보시오.\" 헤롯이 말했다.\n\n발타사르가 몸을 곧추세우고 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"전능하신 하느님이 계십니다.\"\n\n헤롯이 눈에 띄게 흠칫했다.\n\n\"그분께서 저희에게 이리로 오라 명하시며, 세상의 구원자를 만나게 되리라 약속하셨습니다. 그분을 뵙고 경배하며, 그분이 오셨음을 증언하게 되리라 하셨습니다. 그리고 그 표징으로, 저희는 저마다 별 하나를 보도록 허락받았습니다. 그분의 영이 저희와 함께 머무셨습니다. 오 왕이시여, 그분의 영이 지금도 저희와 함께 계십니다!\"\n\n압도하는 감정이 세 사람을 사로잡았다. 그리스인은 가까스로 터져 나오려는 외침을 참았다. 헤롯의 눈길이 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 재빠르게 옮겨 다녔으니, 그는 전보다 더 의심스럽고 못마땅해했다.\n\n\"그대들은 나를 조롱하고 있구려.\" 그가 말했다. \"그게 아니라면, 더 말해 보시오. 그 새로운 왕이 오면 그 뒤에 무슨 일이 일어나오?\"\n\n\"사람들의 구원입니다.\"\n\n\"무엇으로부터?\"\n\n\"그들의 사악함으로부터입니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\"신의 권능, 곧 믿음과 사랑과 선행으로써입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면\"—헤롯이 말을 멈췄으니, 그가 어떤 심정으로 말을 이었는지는 그 표정으로 누구도 알 수 없었을 것이다—\"그대들은 그리스도의 선구자들이로구려. 그것이 전부요?\"\n\n발타사르가 깊이 머리를 숙였다.\n\n\"저희는 임금님의 종들이옵니다, 오 왕이시여.\"\n\n군주가 종을 울리자 시종이 나타났다.\n\n\"예물을 가져오너라.\" 주인이 말했다.\n시종이 나갔다가 잠시 뒤 돌아와, 손님들 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 저마다에게 진홍빛과 푸른빛의 겉옷, 곧 외투 하나와 황금 띠 하나를 바쳤다. 그들은 동방식으로 엎드려 절하며 그 영예를 받아들였다.\n\n\"한마디 더 묻겠소.\" 의식이 끝나자 헤롯이 말했다. \"그대들은 성문의 관리에게, 또 방금 내게, 동방에서 별 하나를 보았다고 했소.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 발타사르가 말했다. \"그분의 별, 갓 태어나신 분의 별입니다.\"\n\n\"그것이 언제 나타났소?\"\n\n\"저희가 이리로 오라는 분부를 받았을 때입니다.\"\n\n헤롯이 일어서서 알현이 끝났음을 알렸다. 왕좌에서 그들 쪽으로 내려서며, 그는 더없이 너그럽게 말했다.\n\n\"오 빛나는 분들이여, 내가 믿는 대로 그대들이 참으로 갓 태어난 그리스도의 선구자라면, 알아 두시오. 나는 오늘 밤 유대의 일에 가장 밝은 자들에게 물었고, 그들은 한목소리로 그가 유대 베들레헴에서 태어날 것이라 하였소. 그대들에게 이르노니, 그리로 가시오. 가서 그 어린아이를 부지런히 찾으시오. 그리고 찾거든 내게 다시 기별해 주시오. 나도 가서 그를 경배할 수 있도록 말이오. 그대들이 가는 길에 어떤 방해나 거리낌도 없을 것이오. 평안이 그대들과 함께하기를!\"\n\n그러고는 겉옷을 몸에 여미며 방을 떠났다.\n\n곧이어 길잡이가 와서 그들을 거리로, 다시 여관으로 데려다주었으니, 그 대문에서 그리스인이 충동적으로 말했다. \"베들레헴으로 갑시다, 오 형제들이여, 왕이 권한 대로.\"\n\n\"그럽시다.\" 힌두인이 외쳤다. \"제 안에서 성령이 불타오릅니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 합시다.\" 발타사르도 똑같이 뜨겁게 말했다. \"낙타는 준비되어 있소.\"\n\n그들은 관리인에게 예물을 주고 안장에 올라, 욥바 문으로 가는 길을 안내받아 떠났다. 그들이 다가가자 거대한 문짝이 빗장이 풀려 열렸고, 그들은 들판으로 빠져나가, 조금 전에 요셉과 마리아가 지나간 그 길로 접어들었다. 그들이 힌놈 골짜기에서 르바임 평원으로 올라설 때, 빛 하나가 나타났으니, 처음에는 넓게 퍼져 희미했다. 그들의 맥박이 빠르게 뛰었다. 빛은 급속히 강렬해졌고, 그들은 그 타는 듯한 광채에 눈을 감았다. 다시 감히 바라보았을 때, 보라! 하늘의 어떤 별과도 다름없이 완전한 그 별이, 낮게 드리워져 그들 앞에서 천천히 움직이고 있었다. 그러자 그들은 두 손을 모으고 환호하며, 더없이 큰 기쁨으로 즐거워했다.\n\n\"하느님께서 우리와 함께하신다! 하느님께서 우리와 함께하신다!\" 그들은 가는 내내 거듭 환호하며 외쳤으니, 마침내 그 별이 마르 엘리야스 너머 골짜기에서 솟아올라, 그 고을 가까운 언덕 비탈 위에 있는 한 집 위에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\n지금은 셋째 야경이 시작될 무렵이었고, 베들레헴에서는 동쪽 산 위로 아침이 밝아 오고 있었으나, 그 빛이 너무 희미하여 골짜기는 아직 밤이었다. 낡은 객사 지붕 위의 파수꾼은 쌀쌀한 공기에 몸을 떨며, 깨어난 생명이 새벽을 맞이하며 내는 첫 소리들을 가려들으려 귀 기울이고 있었다. 그때 한 줄기 빛이 언덕을 따라 집을 향해 움직여 올라왔다. 처음에 그는 누군가의 손에 들린 횃불인 줄 알았다. 다음 순간에는 유성인가 싶었다. 그러나 그 광채는 점점 커져 마침내 별이 되었다. 두려움에 사로잡힌 그는 소리를 질러, 담장 안의 모든 사람을 지붕 위로 불러 모았다. 그 빛은 변덕스러운 움직임으로 계속 다가왔다. 그 아래의 바위와 나무와 길은 번개가 번쩍이는 듯 환하게 빛났다. 이윽고 그 밝기는 눈을 멀게 할 지경이 되었다. 보는 이들 가운데 겁 많은 자들은 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 가린 채 기도했다. 가장 담대한 자들도 눈을 가린 채 웅크리고, 이따금 두려운 마음으로 흘끔흘끔 곁눈질했다. 얼마 뒤 객사와 그 주변의 모든 것이 견디기 힘든 광채 아래 놓였다. 감히 쳐다본 자들은, 그 아이가 태어난 동굴 앞 집 바로 위에 별이 멈춰 서 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n이 광경이 절정에 이르렀을 때, 동방박사들이 올라와 문 앞에서 낙타에서 내리고는 들여보내 달라고 외쳤다. 객사지기가 두려움을 가까스로 다스리고 그들에게 귀를 기울이자, 그는 빗장을 풀고 문을 열어 주었다. 그 기이한 빛 속에서 낙타들은 유령처럼 보였고, 그 낯선 생김새에 더하여 세 방문객의 얼굴과 거동에는 간절함과 환희가 서려 있어, 객사지기의 두려움과 망상을 한층 부추겼다. 그는 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 한동안 그들이 던지는 물음에 대답하지 못했다.\n\n\"여기가 유대 베들레헴이 아니오?\"\n\n그러나 다른 이들이 와서, 그들이 곁에 있는 것만으로 그에게 확신을 주었다.\n\n\"아니오, 여기는 객사일 뿐이고, 마을은 더 가야 있소.\"\n\n\"여기 갓 태어난 아이가 있지 않소?\"\n\n곁에 선 사람들은 놀라며 서로를 돌아보았으나, 그 가운데 몇몇은 \"예, 예.\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"그분에게로 우리를 안내해 주시오!\" 그리스 사람이 애타게 말했다.\n\n\"그분에게로 우리를 안내해 주시오!\" 발타사르가 그 위엄을 깨뜨리며 소리쳤다. \"우리는 그분의 별을, 바로 그대들이 저 집 위에서 보고 있는 저 별을 보았으며, 그분을 경배하러 왔소.\"\n\n힌두 사람이 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤다. \"하느님은 참으로 살아 계시는도다! 서두르시오, 서두르시오! 구세주를 찾았소. 우리는 복되도다, 사람들 가운데 복되도다!\"\n\n지붕에 있던 사람들이 내려와, 낯선 이들이 안뜰을 지나 울안으로 안내되어 갈 때 그 뒤를 따랐다. 동굴 위에 아직 떠 있는 별을 보고는, 비록 전보다 광채가 덜했으나, 몇몇은 두려워 돌아섰다. 그러나 대부분은 그대로 따라갔다. 낯선 이들이 집에 가까이 다가가자 그 별빛이 솟아올랐다. 그들이 문 앞에 이르렀을 때 별은 머리 위 높이 떠올라 사라져 가고 있었고, 그들이 안으로 들어서자 별은 자취도 없이 꺼져 버렸다. 그리고 그때 일어난 일을 지켜본 이들에게는, 별과 낯선 이들 사이에 신성한 관계가 있으며, 그 관계가 적어도 동굴 안의 몇몇 사람에게까지 미친다는 확신이 들었다. 문이 열리자 그들은 안으로 몰려 들어갔다.\n\n방 안은 등불 하나로 밝혀져 있었는데, 그 빛만으로도 낯선 이들이 어머니를, 그리고 그 무릎 위에서 깨어 있는 아이를 찾기에는 넉넉했다.\n\n\"이 아이가 당신의 아이입니까?\" 발타사르가 마리아에게 물었다.\n\n그러자 그 어린것에 관한 사소한 일까지 모두 마음속에 간직하고 곰곰이 되새겨 온 그녀는, 아이를 빛 속으로 들어 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"이 아이는 제 아들입니다!\"\n\n그러자 그들은 엎드려 아이를 경배했다.\n\n그들은 아이가 다른 아이들과 다름없음을 보았다. 그 머리 둘레에는 후광도, 형체 있는 면류관도 없었다. 그 입술은 말을 하려고 열리지 않았다. 그들의 기쁨에 찬 외침과 간구와 기도를 들었다 하더라도, 아이는 아무런 기색도 보이지 않았고, 아기답게 그들보다 등불의 불꽃을 더 오래 바라볼 뿐이었다.\n\n얼마 뒤 그들은 일어나, 낙타들에게로 돌아가서 황금과 유향과 몰약의 예물을 가져와 아이 앞에 바쳤으며, 경배의 말도 조금도 줄이지 않았다. 그 말은 여기에 한마디도 옮기지 않으니, 사려 깊은 이라면 순결한 마음의 순결한 경배가 그때나 지금이나, 또 예부터 늘 그러했듯, 영감에 찬 노래임을 알기 때문이다.\n\n그리고 이 아이가 바로 그들이 그토록 먼 길을 찾아온 구세주였다!\n\n그런데도 그들은 한 점 의심 없이 경배했다. 어찌하여?\n\n어찌하여?\n그들의 믿음은, 우리가 훗날 아버지로 알게 된 그분이 보내신 표징 위에 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그들은 그분의 약속만으로 모든 것이 충족되어, 그분의 방식에 대해서는 아무것도 묻지 않는 그런 부류였다. 그 표징을 보고 그 약속을 들은 이는 몇 되지 않았다. 어머니와 요셉, 목자들, 그리고 세 사람뿐이었다. 그런데도 그들은 모두 한결같이 믿었다. 다시 말해, 구원 계획의 이 시기에는 하느님이 전부였고 아이는 아무것도 아니었다. 그러나 앞을 내다보라, 오 독자여! 모든 표징이 아들에게서 나올 때가 오리라. 그때에 그분을 믿는 자들은 복되도다!\n\n그 시기를 기다려 보자.\n\n# BOOK SECOND\n\n“영혼에는 불길과 약동이 있어 제 비좁은 존재에 머무르려 하지 않고 욕망의 합당한 한계 너머를 갈망하며, 한번 타오르면 영영 꺼지지 않고 드높은 모험을 먹이로 삼아, 안식 말고는 그 무엇에도 지치지 않는다.” 차일드 해럴드. 제1장\n\n이제 독자를 이십일 년 뒤로, 유대의 네 번째 제국 총독인 발레리우스 그라투스가 통치를 시작하던 무렵으로 데려갈 필요가 있다—그 시기는 예루살렘이 정치적 동요로 갈가리 찢긴 때로 기억될 것이니, 실로 그때가 유대인과 로마인 사이에 마지막 다툼이 벌어지기 시작한 바로 그 시점이 아니라면 말이다. 그동안 유대는 여러 면에서 영향을 받는 변화를 겪었으나, 그 무엇도 정치적 지위만큼 크게 흔들린 것은 없었다. 헤롯 대왕은 그 아기가 태어난 지 일 년이 채 못 되어 죽었으니, 어찌나 비참하게 죽었던지 그리스도교 세계가 그를 신의 진노에 사로잡힌 자로 믿을 만한 까닭이 되었다. 자기가 세운 권력을 완성하는 데 평생을 바친 모든 위대한 통치자가 그러하듯, 그 역시 제 왕좌와 왕관을 물려주는 일을, 곧 한 왕조의 시조가 되는 일을 꿈꾸었다. 그런 뜻으로 그는 유언을 남겨 자기 영토를 세 아들, 안티파스와 빌립과 아르켈라오스에게 나누었으며, 그 가운데 마지막 아들에게 왕위를 잇도록 정해 두었다. 그 유언은 마땅히 황제 아우구스투스에게 회부되었고, 황제는 단 한 가지를 빼고는 모든 조항을 비준했다. 아르켈라오스에게는 그 능력과 충성을 입증할 때까지 왕의 칭호를 주지 않았다. 그 대신 그를 분봉왕으로 삼아 그 자격으로 구 년을 다스리게 했는데, 끝내 제 주위에서 자라나 거세지는 소요의 무리를 가라앉히지 못한 무능과 실정 탓에 갈리아로 유배되고 말았다.\n\n카이사르는 아르켈라오스를 폐위하는 것으로 만족하지 않았다. 그는 예루살렘 백성의 자존심을 건드리는 방식으로 그들을 내리쳤고, 성전을 드나드는 오만한 무리의 감정에 날카로운 상처를 입혔다. 그는 유대를 로마의 한 속주로 격하시켜 시리아 총독부에 병합했다. 그리하여 헤롯이 시온산에 남긴 궁전에서 왕답게 다스리는 왕 대신, 그 도성은 이등급 관리, 곧 총독이라 불리는 임명직의 손에 떨어졌으니, 그는 안티오크에 머무는 시리아 총독을 거쳐 로마의 궁정과 연락을 주고받았다. 그 상처를 더욱 쓰라리게 하려는 듯, 총독은 예루살렘에 자리 잡는 것조차 허락받지 못했다. 카이사레아가 그의 통치 거점이었다. 그러나 가장 굴욕스럽고, 가장 분통 터지며, 가장 의도적이었던 것은, 온 세상에서 가장 멸시받는 사마리아가, 바로 그 사마리아가 같은 속주의 일부로서 유대에 붙여진 일이었다! 카이사레아에서 총독 앞에 선 채 그리심산을 섬기는 무리에게 떠밀리고 비웃음을 당하는 처지를 깨달았을 때, 완고한 분리주의자들, 곧 바리새인들이 견뎌야 했던 그 형언할 수 없는 비참함이란!\n\n이 슬픔의 빗줄기 속에서도 몰락한 백성에게는 단 하나, 오직 하나의 위안이 남아 있었다. 대제사장이 장터에 자리한 헤롯의 궁전을 차지하여 그곳에 궁정의 시늉을 유지하고 있었던 것이다. 그의 권한이 실제로 무엇이었는지는 가늠하기 쉬운 일이다. 생사를 가르는 재판권은 총독이 쥐고 있었다. 정의는 로마의 이름으로, 로마의 법령에 따라 집행되었다. 더욱 의미심장하게도, 그 왕가의 궁전은 황제의 징세관과 그 휘하의 온갖 보좌관, 등기관, 징수관, 세리, 밀고자, 첩자 들이 함께 차지하고 있었다. 그럼에도 장차 올 자유를 꿈꾸는 자들에게는 그 궁전의 으뜸가는 통치자가 유대인이라는 사실에 어떤 흡족함이 있었다. 그가 날마다 그곳에 있다는 사실만으로도 그들은 예언자들의 언약과 약속을, 그리고 여호와께서 아론의 자손을 통해 지파들을 다스리시던 시대를 떠올렸다. 그것은 그들에게 여호와께서 자기들을 버리지 않으셨다는 확실한 표징이었다. 그리하여 그들의 희망은 살아남아 그들의 인내를 떠받쳤고, 이스라엘을 다스릴 유다의 아들을 굳세게 기다리도록 도왔다.\n\n유대가 로마의 속주가 된 지 팔십 년 남짓 되었으니, 카이사르들이 그 백성의 기질을 연구하기에 넉넉한 시간이었고, 적어도 유대인이 그 온갖 자존심에도 불구하고 제 종교만 존중받으면 조용히 다스려질 수 있다는 사실을 배우기에는 충분한 시간이었다. 그 방침에 따라 그라투스의 전임자들은 백성의 신성한 의례 어느 하나에도 끼어들기를 조심스레 삼갔다. 그러나 그는 다른 길을 택했다. 그의 거의 첫 공무가 한나스를 대제사장직에서 쫓아내고 그 자리를 파부스의 아들 이스마엘에게 넘긴 일이었다.\n\n그 일이 아우구스투스의 지시였는지 아니면 그라투스 자신에게서 비롯된 것인지는 몰라도, 그 정략의 어리석음은 이내 분명하게 드러났다. 독자에게 유대 정치를 다룬 한 장(章)을 늘어놓는 수고는 면하게 해 주겠으나, 이 주제에 관한 몇 마디만은 뒤이은 이야기를 꼼꼼히 좇으려는 이에게 반드시 필요하다. 이 무렵 그 기원은 접어 두더라도, 유대에는 귀족당과 분리주의자, 곧 민중당이 있었다. 헤롯이 죽자 두 당파는 손잡고 아르켈라오스에게 맞섰다. 성전에서 궁전으로, 예루살렘에서 로마로, 그들은 때로는 음모로, 때로는 실제 전쟁의 무기로 그와 싸웠다. 모리아산의 거룩한 회랑이 싸우는 자들의 함성으로 울린 일도 한두 번이 아니었다. 마침내 그들은 그를 유배지로 내몰았다. 그러는 동안 이 다툼 내내 두 동맹자는 저마다 다른 목적을 품고 있었다. 귀족들은 대제사장 요아자르를 미워했으나, 분리주의자들은 도리어 그를 열렬히 따르는 무리였다. 헤롯의 체제가 아르켈라오스와 함께 무너지자, 요아자르도 그 추락을 함께 겪었다. 셋의 아들 한나스가 귀족들에 의해 그 큰 직책을 채울 자로 뽑혔으니, 그로 인해 동맹은 갈라졌다. 셋 가문 사람의 취임은 두 당파를 격렬한 적의 속에 마주 서게 만들었다.\n\n그 불운한 분봉왕과의 다툼이 진행되는 동안, 귀족들은 로마에 붙는 편이 유리하다는 것을 알아차렸다. 기존 체제가 무너지면 어떤 형태로든 통치 기구가 뒤따라야 함을 간파한 그들은 유대를 속주로 전환할 것을 제안했다. 이 사실은 분리주의자들에게 또 하나의 공격 빌미가 되었으며, 사마리아가 속주의 일부로 편입되자 귀족들은 소수파로 가라앉아, 황제의 궁정과 제 신분과 재산의 위세 말고는 그들을 떠받칠 것이 아무것도 없게 되었다. 그러면서도 십오 년 동안, 실로 발레리우스 그라투스가 올 때까지 그들은 궁전과 성전 양쪽에서 제 자리를 지켜 냈다.\n\n제 당파의 우상이었던 한나스는 자기 황제 후원자의 이익을 위해 그 권력을 충실히 써 왔다. 로마 수비대가 안토니아 탑을 지켰고, 로마 경비병이 궁전 문을 지켰으며, 로마 재판관이 민사와 형사의 정의를 베풀었고, 무자비하게 집행되는 로마식 조세 제도가 도성과 시골을 함께 짓눌렀다. 날마다, 시시각각, 천 가지 방식으로 백성은 멍들고 쓰라림을 당하며 독립된 삶과 예속된 삶의 차이를 배웠으나, 한나스는 그들을 그런대로 잠잠하게 붙들어 두었다. 로마에는 그보다 더 진실한 벗이 없었으며, 그의 상실은 즉시 체감되었다. 새 임명자 이스마엘에게 제 제의를 넘긴 그는 성전의 뜰에서 곧장 분리주의자들의 회의로 걸어 들어가, 베투스 가문과 셋 가문이 결합한 새로운 연합의 우두머리가 되었다.\n\n이리하여 의지할 당파 없이 남겨진 총독 그라투스는, 십오 년 동안 눅눅한 연기로 사그라들었던 불길이 되살아나는 생기로 다시 타오르기 시작하는 것을 보았다. 이스마엘이 직책을 맡은 지 한 달 만에 로마인은 예루살렘으로 그를 찾아갈 필요를 느꼈다. 유대인들이 성벽에서 그에게 야유와 휘파람을 퍼붓는 가운데, 그의 경비대가 도성의 북문으로 들어와 안토니아 탑으로 행진하는 것을 보았을 때, 그들은 그 방문의 참뜻을 깨달았다. 로마 군단병 한 개 대대가 종전의 수비대에 더해졌으니, 이제 그들에게 씌운 멍에의 빗장은 거리낌 없이 죄어들 수 있게 된 것이다. 만일 총독이 본보기를 세우는 것을 중요하게 여겼다면, 첫 번째 위반자에게 화가 있으리라!\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n앞의 설명을 마음에 새긴 채, 이제 독자를 시온 산 궁전의 한 정원으로 이끌고자 한다. 때는 칠월 한복판의 정오 무렵으로, 여름의 더위가 절정에 이른 시각이었다.\n\n정원은 사방이 건물로 둘러싸여 있었는데, 어떤 곳은 이층으로 솟아, 아래층의 문과 창에는 차양을 드리운 베란다가 그늘을 드리우고, 위층에는 튼튼한 난간으로 보호된 안쪽으로 물러난 회랑이 장식과 보호를 겸했다. 게다가 곳곳에서 그 구조물들은 낮은 주랑처럼 트여, 어쩌다 부는 바람이 지나가도록 하고, 집의 다른 부분들이 눈에 들어와 그 웅장함과 아름다움을 한결 또렷이 느끼게 했다. 땅의 배치 또한 그에 못지않게 보기 좋았다. 산책로가 있고, 잔디밭과 관목 무더기가 있으며, 큰 나무 몇 그루가 있었으니, 보기 드문 종려나무들이 캐럽나무, 살구나무, 호두나무와 어울려 무리를 이루었다. 사방으로 땅은 한가운데서 완만하게 비탈져 내려갔고, 그 한가운데에는 저수조, 곧 깊은 대리석 수반이 있어, 군데군데 작은 수문으로 끊겨 있었다. 그 수문을 들어 올리면 물이 산책로를 따라 난 수로로 흘러들었으니, 이는 그 지역 다른 곳에 너무도 흔한 메마름에서 이곳을 구해 내려는 교묘한 장치였다.\n\n분수에서 멀지 않은 곳에는 맑은 물이 고인 작은 못이 있어, 갈대와 협죽도 무리를 길러 냈으니, 요르단 강가나 사해 기슭에 자라는 그런 것들이었다. 그 무리와 못 사이에, 숨 막히는 대기 속에서 해가 온통 내리쬐는 것도 아랑곳하지 않고, 두 소년이 앉아 진지한 대화를 나누고 있었다. 하나는 열아홉 살쯤, 다른 하나는 열일곱 살쯤 되어 보였다.\n\n두 사람 다 잘생겼으니, 언뜻 보면 형제라 했을 법했다. 둘 다 머리칼과 눈이 검고, 얼굴은 짙게 그을렸으며, 앉은 모습은 나이 차이에 걸맞은 몸집으로 보였다.\n\n손위 소년은 맨머리였다. 무릎까지 내려오는 헐렁한 튜닉이 그가 걸친 옷의 전부였으니, 샌들과 의자 위 제 밑에 깔린 옅은 푸른 망토를 빼면 그러했다. 그 차림새는 팔다리를 드러냈고, 그것들은 얼굴처럼 갈색이었다. 그런데도 어딘가 우아한 몸가짐, 세련된 이목구비, 잘 다듬어진 목소리가 그의 신분을 말해 주었다. 더없이 부드러운 모직으로 지은 잿빛 튜닉은 목과 소매와 치맛단을 붉은빛으로 둘렀고, 술이 달린 비단 끈으로 허리를 동였으니, 그가 로마인임을 증명하고 있었다. 그리고 그가 말 중에 이따금 동무를 거만하게 바라보며 아랫사람 다루듯 대했더라도, 그것은 거의 용서받을 만했다. 그는 로마에서조차 귀족인 가문 출신이었으니, 이는 그 시대에는 어떤 자만이든 정당화하는 사정이었다. 첫 카이사르와 그의 위대한 적들 사이에 벌어진 끔찍한 전쟁에서, 한 메살라가 브루투스의 벗이었다. 필리피 전투 뒤, 그는 명예를 저버리지 않고도 승자와 화해했다. 그러나 그 뒤 옥타비우스가 제위를 다투었을 때, 메살라는 그를 지지했다. 옥타비우스는 아우구스투스 황제가 되어 그 봉사를 잊지 않고, 그 가문에 영예를 듬뿍 내렸다. 다른 무엇보다도, 유대가 한 속주로 격하되자, 그는 옛 식객 혹은 가신의 아들을 예루살렘으로 보내, 그 지역에서 거두는 세금의 수납과 관리를 맡겼다. 그 직무를 맡은 채 아들은 그 뒤로도 머물러, 대제사장과 더불어 궁전을 함께 썼다. 방금 묘사한 젊은이가 바로 그 아들이었으니, 그는 제 할아버지와 당대의 위대한 로마인들 사이의 관계에 대한 기억을 너무도 충실하게 늘 지니고 다니는 것이 버릇이었다.\n\n메살라의 동무는 몸매가 더 가냘팠고, 그의 옷은 고운 흰 아마포로 지었으니 예루살렘에 두루 퍼진 양식이었다. 천 한 폭이 그의 머리를 덮어, 노란 끈으로 동이고, 이마에서 물러나 목덜미 아래로 길게 흘러내리도록 매만져져 있었다. 인종의 구별에 밝은 관찰자가 그의 차림보다 이목구비를 더 눈여겨보았다면, 곧 그가 유대 혈통임을 알아차렸을 것이다. 로마인의 이마는 높고 좁았으며, 코는 날카롭고 매부리졌고, 입술은 얇고 곧았으며, 눈은 차갑고 눈썹 아래 바짝 붙어 있었다. 반면 이스라엘인의 이마는 낮고 넓었다. 코는 길고 콧방울이 벌어졌으며, 윗입술은 아랫입술을 살짝 덮으며 짧고 보조개 진 입꼬리로 굽이쳐, 마치 큐피드의 활 같았다. 둥근 턱, 그득한 눈, 포도주 같은 홍조가 어린 달걀형 뺨과 더불어, 이런 점들이 그의 얼굴에 그 민족 특유의 부드러움과 힘과 아름다움을 주었다. 로마인의 잘생김은 엄격하고 정결했으나, 유대인의 그것은 풍성하고 관능적이었다.\n\n\"새 총독이 내일 도착한다고 하지 않았나?\"\n\n이 물음은 두 벗 가운데 손아래에게서 나왔으며, 그리스어로 되어 있었으니, 당시로서는 참으로 묘하게도 그리스어가 유대의 점잖은 사회 어디에서나 두루 쓰이는 말이었던 것이다. 그 말은 궁전에서 군영과 학교로 옮겨 갔고, 그곳에서, 언제 어떻게인지는 아무도 정확히 모르나, 성전 자체로까지 스며들었으며, 따지고 보면 문과 회랑 너머 멀리, 이방인에게는 들어가는 것조차 견딜 수 없는 신성한 구역들에까지 파고들었다.\n\n\"그래, 내일이지.\" 메살라가 대답했다.\n\n\"누구한테 들었나?\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 이스마엘이—궁전의 새 총독 말이야, 너희는 대제사장이라 부르지—우리 아버지께 그리 말하는 걸 들었어. 그야 이집트인한테서 나온 소식이라면 더 믿을 만했겠지, 진실이 무엇인지 잊어버린 종족이니까. 아니면 이두매인한테서라면, 그 백성은 진실이 무엇인지 애초에 알지도 못했으니까. 하지만 아주 확실히 해 두려고, 오늘 아침에 망루에서 온 백인대장을 만났는데, 그가 말하길 영접 채비가 한창이라더군. 무기 만드는 자들이 투구와 방패를 닦고, 독수리 깃발과 구슬에 금박을 다시 입히고 있으며, 오래 쓰지 않던 방들을 마치 수비대에 인원이 더해질 것처럼—아마도 그 거물의 호위대겠지—청소하고 바람을 쐬고 있다더군.\"\n\n그 대답이 어떤 태도로 나왔는지 그 완전한 모습을 전할 수는 없으니, 그 미묘한 점들이 펜 뒤에 숨은 힘에서 끊임없이 빠져나가는 까닭이다. 독자는 상상으로 거들어야 하며, 그러기 위해서는 경외심이 로마인의 정신에서 한 자질로서 빠르게 무너지고 있었음을, 아니 오히려 시대에 뒤떨어진 것이 되어 가고 있었음을 떠올려야 한다. 옛 종교는 거의 신앙이기를 그쳤으니, 기껏해야 사고와 표현의 한낱 습관일 뿐이었다. 그것을 주로 소중히 여긴 자들은 성전 봉사를 짭짤한 벌이로 여긴 제사장들, 그리고 시구를 짓는 데 친숙한 신들을 빼놓을 수 없었던 시인들이었으니, 이 시대에도 그와 비슷한 가인들이 있다. 철학이 종교의 자리를 차지하면서, 풍자가 빠르게 경외심을 대신해 갔다. 그리하여 라틴인의 견해로는 그것이 모든 연설에, 심지어 대화 중의 자잘한 독설에까지, 마치 음식에 소금과 같고 포도주에 향과 같은 것이 되었다. 로마에서 교육받고 얼마 전에야 돌아온 젊은 메살라는 그 습관과 태도를 익히고 있었으니, 아래 눈꺼풀 바깥 끝의 좀처럼 알아채기 힘든 움직임, 그에 호응하는 콧방울의 뚜렷한 일그러짐, 그리고 전반적인 무관심을 전하는 데 가장 좋은 수단으로 꾸며 낸 나른한 말투가 그러했다. 그러나 그 나른한 말투를 더욱 즐긴 까닭은, 무엇보다 그것이 어떤 수사적 멈춤의 기회를 주기 때문이었으니, 그 멈춤은 듣는 이가 그 절묘한 기지를 받아들이거나 따끔한 경구의 독을 받게 하는 데 무엇보다 중요하다고 여겨졌다. 방금 한 대답에서도 그런 멈춤이 있었으니, 이집트인과 이두매인을 빗댄 말 끝에서였다. 유대 소년의 두 뺨에 핏기가 짙어졌고, 그는 나머지 말을 듣지 못했을지도 모른다. 그는 잠잠한 채, 못의 깊은 곳을 멍하니 들여다보고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"우리가 작별한 곳이 바로 이 정원이었지. '주님의 평화가 너와 함께하기를!'—그게 네 마지막 말이었어. '신들께서 너를 지켜 주시기를!' 하고 내가 말했고. 기억하나? 그 뒤로 몇 해가 흘렀지?\"\n\n\"다섯 해.\" 유대 소년이 물을 들여다보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 감사할 만하지—누구한테라고 해야 하나? 신들한테? 아무래도 좋아. 너는 잘생겨졌어. 그리스인이라면 아름답다 했을 거다—세월이 빚어낸 행복한 성취지! 유피테르가 가니메데 하나로 만족한다면, 너야말로 황제의 술시중을 들기에 얼마나 좋을까! 말해 보게, 나의 유다, 어째서 총독의 도착이 너한테 그토록 큰 관심사인지를.\"\n\n유다는 그 큰 눈을 묻는 이에게 돌렸다. 그 눈길은 진지하고 사려 깊었으며, 로마인의 눈길을 붙들어 대답하는 동안 놓지 않았다. \"그래, 다섯 해지. 나는 그 작별을 기억해. 너는 로마로 갔고, 나는 네가 떠나는 걸 지켜보며 울었지, 너를 사랑하니까. 세월이 흘러, 너는 빼어나고 기품 있게 다듬어져 내게 돌아왔어—농담이 아니야. 그런데도—그런데도—나는 네가 떠나갈 때의 그 메살라였으면 하고 바라게 되는구나.\"\n\n풍자가의 고운 콧방울이 움찔하더니, 그는 한층 길게 늘어진 말투로 말했다. \"아니, 아니지. 가니메데가 아니라—신탁이지, 나의 유다. 포룸 바로 곁에 있는 내 수사학 스승한테서 몇 차례 배우기만 하면—네가 내 권유를 받아들일 만큼 슬기로워지면 그분께 보내는 편지를 한 통 써 주마, 마침 그런 권유가 떠올랐으니—신비술을 조금 익히기만 하면, 델포이가 너를 아폴론 그 자신으로 맞이할 거다. 네 엄숙한 목소리를 듣고, 무녀가 관을 쓰고 너한테 내려올 테니까. 진지하게 묻겠는데, 오, 나의 벗이여, 내가 떠나갈 때의 메살라가 아닌 점이 무엇이냐? 나는 언젠가 세상에서 가장 위대한 논리학자의 강의를 들었어. 그 주제는 논쟁이었지. 한마디가 기억나는군—'대답하기 전에 네 상대를 이해하라.' 너를 이해하게 해 다오.\"\n\n소년은 그에게 쏟아지는 냉소 어린 눈길에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그래도 그는 단호하게 대답했다. \"보아하니 너는 네 기회를 잘 활용했구나. 스승들한테서 많은 지식과 숱한 우아함을 가져왔어. 너는 대가처럼 거침없이 말하지만, 네 말에는 가시가 박혀 있어. 떠나갈 때의 내 메살라한테는 천성에 독이 없었어. 세상 무엇을 준다 해도 그는 벗의 마음을 다치게 하지 않았을 거다.\"\n\n로마인은 칭찬이라도 받은 듯 미소 짓고는, 귀족다운 고개를 한층 더 치켜올렸다.\n\n\"오, 나의 엄숙한 유다여, 우리는 도도나에도 피토에도 있지 않아. 신탁 같은 말은 그만두고, 알아듣게 말해. 내가 너를 어디서 다치게 했단 말이냐?\"\n\n상대는 길게 숨을 들이쉬고는, 허리에 두른 끈을 잡아당기며 말했다. \"지난 다섯 해 동안, 나도 무언가를 배웠어. 힐렐이 네가 들었다는 그 논리학자만 못할지도 모르고, 시므온과 샴마이도 포룸 곁의 네 스승보다 못할 게 분명하지. 그분들의 학문은 금지된 길로 나아가지 않아. 그분들 발치에 앉는 자들은 그저 하느님과 율법과 이스라엘에 대한 지식으로 풍성해져 일어서지. 그리고 그 결과는 그것들과 관련된 모든 것에 대한 사랑과 경외야. 대학에 다니며 그곳에서 들은 것을 공부하면서, 나는 유대가 옛날의 유대가 아님을 깨달았어. 나는 독립된 왕국과 보잘것없는 속주 사이에 놓인 거리를 알아. 유대가 바로 그 속주지. 내 나라가 격하된 것을 분히 여기지 않는다면, 나는 사마리아인보다도 비천하고 야비한 자일 거다. 이스마엘은 합법적인 대제사장이 아니야. 고귀한 한나스가 살아 있는 한 그럴 수 없지. 그래도 그는 레위인이야. 수천 년 동안 우리 신앙과 예배의 주 하느님을 가납받을 만하게 섬겨 온 헌신된 자들 가운데 하나라고. 그의—\"\n\n메살라가 신랄한 웃음으로 그의 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"오, 이제 알겠다. 이스마엘은 찬탈자라면서도, 이스마엘보다 이두매인을 먼저 믿으면 독사처럼 쏘아붙이는군. 세멜레의 그 술 취한 아들에 두고, 유대인이라는 게 도대체 뭐란 말이냐! 모든 사람과 사물이, 심지어 하늘과 땅까지 변하건만, 유대인만은 결코 변하지 않아. 그한테는 뒤도 앞도 없어. 그는 태초에 제 조상이었던 그대로지. 이 모래 위에 너한테 원을 하나 그려 주마—자, 여기! 이제 말해 봐, 유대인의 삶이 이것보다 더한 게 뭐가 있는지. 돌고 또 돌고, 여기엔 아브라함, 저기엔 이삭과 야곱, 한가운데엔 하느님. 그런데 이 원은—온갖 천둥의 주인에 두고! 이 원은 너무 크군. 다시 그려 주지—\" 그는 말을 멈추고, 엄지손가락을 땅에 대고는, 그 둘레로 손가락들을 휘둘렀다. \"보게, 엄지 자국은 성전이고, 손가락 선은 유대야. 이 작은 자리 바깥에는 가치 있는 게 아무것도 없나? 예술! 헤롯은 건축가였지. 그래서 저주받았어. 회화, 조각! 그것들을 보는 건 죄지. 시는 너희 제단에 매여 있고. 회당을 빼면, 너희 가운데 누가 웅변을 시도하나? 전쟁에서는 엿새 동안 정복한 것을 이렛날에 죄다 잃어버리지. 그게 너희 삶이고 한계야. 내가 너희를 비웃는다 한들 누가 안 된다 하겠나? 그런 백성의 예배에 만족하다니, 너희 하느님이 우리 로마의 유피테르께 견주면 뭐란 말이냐? 그분은 우리한테 독수리 깃발을 빌려주시어, 우리가 우주를 우리 팔에 거머쥐게 하시는데. 힐렐, 시므온, 샴마이, 압탈리온—알 수 있는 모든 것은 알 가치가 있다고 가르치는 대가들에 견주면, 그들이 다 무엇이냐?\"\n\n유대인은 일어섰고, 그의 얼굴은 몹시 상기되었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 자리에 그대로 있게, 나의 유다, 그대로 있어.\" 메살라가 손을 내밀며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네가 나를 조롱하는구나.\"\n\n\"조금만 더 들어 봐. 곧\"—로마인은 비웃듯 미소 지었다—\"곧 유피테르와 그 온 가족이, 그리스 신이든 라틴 신이든, 늘 그러듯 내게로 와서, 진지한 이야기를 끝장낼 테니까. 너희 조상의 옛집에서 여기까지 걸어와 내 귀향을 반기고 우리 어린 시절의 정을 새롭게 하려 한—할 수 있다면 말이지—네 호의를 나는 마음에 새기고 있어. '가거라.' 내 스승이 마지막 강의에서 그러셨지—'가서, 네 삶을 위대하게 만들려거든, 마르스가 다스리고 에로스가 제 눈을 되찾았음을 기억하라.' 그분이 뜻한 바는, 사랑은 아무것도 아니고 전쟁이 전부라는 거였어. 로마에서는 그래. 결혼은 이혼으로 가는 첫걸음이지. 정절은 장사꾼의 보석이고. 죽어 가던 클레오파트라는 제 기예를 물려주고 원수를 갚았어. 로마인의 집집마다 그 후계자가 있으니까. 세상이 같은 길을 가고 있어. 그러니 우리 앞날을 두고는, 에로스는 물리치고, 마르스를 받들자! 나는 군인이 될 거다. 그리고 너는, 오, 나의 유다, 나는 네가 가엾어. 너는 무엇이 될 수 있겠나?\"\n\n유대인은 못 가까이로 다가섰다. 메살라의 말투는 더욱 늘어졌다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 네가 가엾어, 나의 잘난 유다. 대학에서 회당으로, 그다음엔 성전으로, 그다음엔—오, 더없는 영광이지!—산헤드린의 한자리로. 기회라곤 없는 삶이야. 신들께서 너를 도우시기를! 하지만 나는—\"\n\n유다가 그를 바라본 것은 마침, 그가 말을 이어 가는 동안 그 거만한 얼굴에 타오르는 자긍심의 홍조를 볼 수 있을 때였다.\n\n\"하지만 나는—아, 세상은 다 정복된 게 아니야. 바다에는 아직 보지 못한 섬들이 있어. 북쪽에는 아직 가 보지 못한 민족들이 있고. 알렉산드로스의 동방 원정을 완성하는 영광은 누군가의 몫으로 남아 있지. 보게, 한 로마인 앞에 어떤 가능성들이 놓여 있는지를.\"\n\n다음 순간 그는 다시 나른한 말투로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"아프리카 원정, 그다음엔 스키타이 원정, 그다음엔—군단 지휘! 대개의 경력은 거기서 끝나지. 하지만 내 것은 아니야. 나는—유피테르에 두고! 이 얼마나 멋진 구상인가!—나는 내 군단을 내놓고 태수직을 얻을 거다. 돈을 가지고 로마에서 사는 삶을 생각해 봐—돈, 포도주, 여인, 경기—연회의 시인들, 궁정의 음모, 일 년 내내 굴리는 주사위. 그렇게 삶을 마무리하는 게 가능할지도 몰라—기름진 태수직, 그게 내 것이 되는 거지. 오, 나의 유다, 여기에 시리아가 있어! 유대는 부유하고, 안티오크는 신들에게 어울리는 수도야. 나는 키레니우스의 뒤를 이을 거고, 너는—내 재산을 함께 나누게 될 거다.\"\n\n로마의 공공장소마다 떼 지어 몰려, 귀족 자제 가르치는 일을 거의 독차지하다시피 한 궤변가와 수사학자들이라면 메살라의 이런 말을 옳게 여겼을 것이다. 그것들이 모두 세태에 영합하는 결이었으니 말이다. 그러나 젊은 유대인에게는 그것들이 낯설었고, 그가 익숙해 온 엄숙한 담론과 대화의 양식과는 사뭇 달랐다. 게다가 그는 율법과 사고방식과 습관이 풍자와 익살을 금하는 한 민족에 속해 있었다. 따라서 그가 갖가지 심정으로 벗의 말에 귀 기울인 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었으니, 한순간은 분개하다가, 다음 순간은 그를 어찌 받아들여야 할지 갈피를 못 잡았다. 그가 꾸며 보인 우월한 태도는 처음부터 거슬렸으나, 곧 짜증스러워졌고, 마침내 날카로운 아픔이 되었다. 우리 누구에게나 이 지점 바로 곁에 분노가 도사리고 있으니, 풍자가는 또 다른 길로 그 분노를 불러일으켰다. 헤롯 시대의 유대인에게 애국심은 평소의 익살 밑에 좀처럼 감추어지지 않는 사나운 정념이었으며, 그의 역사와 종교와 하느님과 너무도 깊이 얽혀 있어, 그것들에 대한 조롱에 즉각 반응했다. 그러므로 메살라가 마지막 멈춤에 이르기까지 늘어놓은 말이 듣는 이에게 더없는 고문이었다고 해도, 지나친 말은 아니다. 그 지점에서 후자가 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 듣기로 제 앞날을 두고 농담을 할 형편이 되는 자들이 더러 있다더군. 너는 내게 확신시켜 주는구나, 오, 나의 메살라, 내가 그런 자들 가운데 하나가 아님을.\"\n\n로마인은 그를 찬찬히 살피더니 대답했다. \"비유 못지않게 농담 속에도 진실이 담길 수 있지 않은가? 위대한 풀비아가 며칠 전에 낚시를 갔는데, 다른 일행을 다 합친 것보다 더 많이 잡았다네. 사람들이 말하길, 그건 그 여자 낚싯바늘 미늘에 금이 입혀져 있었기 때문이라더군.\"\n\n\"그럼 너는 그저 농담만 한 게 아니었나?\"\n\n\"나의 유다, 보아하니 내가 너한테 충분히 내놓지 않았구나.\" 로마인이 눈을 반짝이며 재빨리 대답했다. \"내가 태수가 되어 유대로 부를 일구게 되면, 나는—너를 대제사장으로 만들어 주마.\"\n\n유대인은 화가 나서 돌아섰다.\n\n\"날 떠나지 말게.\" 메살라가 말했다.\n\n상대는 망설이며 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"신들이여, 유다, 햇볕이 어찌 이리 뜨거운가!\" 그의 당혹스러움을 알아챈 귀족이 외쳤다. \"그늘을 찾아보세.\"\n\n유다가 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n\"우리는 갈라서는 게 낫겠어. 내가 오지 말았어야 했어. 나는 벗을 찾아왔다가, 한 사람의—을 보게 되었구나.\"\n\n\"로마인을.\" 메살라가 재빨리 말했다.\n\n유대인의 두 손이 움켜쥐어졌으나, 그는 다시금 자신을 다스리며 발걸음을 떼었다. 메살라가 일어서, 의자에서 망토를 집어 어깨에 걸치고 뒤따라갔다. 그의 곁에 이르자, 그는 손을 그의 어깨에 얹고 나란히 걸었다.\n\n\"이렇게—내 손을 이렇게 얹고—우리가 어렸을 때 함께 걷곤 했지. 문까지는 이대로 가세.\"\n\n겉보기에 메살라는 진지하고 다정해지려 애쓰는 듯했으나, 그 얼굴에서 몸에 밴 풍자의 표정을 떨쳐 내지는 못했다. 유다는 그 허물없음을 받아 주었다.\n\n\"너는 소년이고, 나는 어른이지. 어른답게 말하게 해 다오.\"\n\n로마인의 자족감은 빼어났다. 어린 텔레마코스를 가르치는 멘토르라 해도 그보다 더 느긋할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"너는 파르카이를 믿나? 아, 깜박했군, 너는 사두개인이지. 에세네파가 너희 가운데 분별 있는 자들이고, 그들은 그 세 자매를 믿어. 나도 믿지. 그 셋이 우리가 하고 싶은 대로 하는 걸 얼마나 끈질기게 가로막는지! 나는 앉아서 궁리하지. 이리저리 길을 내달리고. 페르폴! 내가 막 세상을 손에 거머쥐려고 손을 뻗는 바로 그 순간, 등 뒤에서 가위 가는 소리가 들려. 돌아보면, 거기 그 여자가 있지, 저주받을 아트로포스가! 그런데, 나의 유다, 내가 늙은 키레니우스의 뒤를 잇겠다고 말했을 때 너는 어째서 화를 냈나? 너는 내가 네 유대를 약탈해 부자가 되려는 줄 알았겠지. 그렇다 치자. 어차피 어떤 로마인은 할 일이야. 그게 왜 내가 아니어야 하나?\"\n\n유다가 걸음을 늦추었다.\n\n\"로마인 이전에도 유대를 다스린 이방인들이 있었어.\" 그가 손을 들며 말했다. \"그들은 어디 있나, 메살라? 유대는 그들을 모두 살아남았어. 이미 있었던 일은 다시 있게 될 거다.\"\n\n메살라는 나른한 말투를 띠었다.\n\n\"파르카이를 믿는 자들이 에세네파 바깥에도 있군. 환영하네, 유다, 그 신앙에 들어선 것을 환영해!\"\n\n\"아니야, 메살라, 나를 그들과 같이 치지 말게. 내 신앙은 아브라함보다도 더 멀리 거슬러 올라가 내 조상들의 신앙의 기초가 된 그 반석 위에 서 있어. 이스라엘의 주 하느님의 언약들 위에 말이야.\"\n\n\"정념이 너무 과하군, 나의 유다. 내가 스승 앞에서 그토록 열을 올렸다면 그분이 얼마나 충격을 받으셨을까! 너한테 해 줄 말이 더 있었는데, 이제는 그러기가 두렵구나.\"\n\n몇 발짝을 더 가서, 로마인이 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이제는 내 말을 들을 수 있을 것 같군, 특히 내가 할 말이 너 자신에 관한 것이니 말이야. 나는 너를 돕고 싶어, 오, 가니메데처럼 잘생긴 이여. 나는 진심 어린 선의로 너를 돕고 싶어. 나는 너를 사랑해—내가 할 수 있는 한 다. 내가 군인이 되려 한다고 말했지. 너라고 안 될 게 뭐냐? 내가 보여 주었듯이, 너희 율법과 관습이 허락하는 고귀한 삶의 전부인 그 좁은 원에서, 너라고 한 발짝 벗어나지 못할 게 뭐냐?\"\n\n유다는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"우리 시대의 슬기로운 자들이 누구냐?\" 메살라가 말을 이었다. \"죽은 것들을 두고—바알이니 유피테르니 여호와니 하는 것들을 두고, 철학이니 종교니 하는 것들을 두고 다투느라 세월을 다 써 버리는 자들이 아니지. 위대한 이름 하나만 대 봐, 오, 유다. 그걸 찾으러 어디로 가든 나는 상관없어—로마로든, 이집트로든, 동방으로든, 아니면 여기 예루살렘에서든—만일 그 이름이 현재가 그에게 내준 재료로 제 명성을 빚어낸 자의 것이 아니라면 플루토가 나를 잡아가도 좋아. 목적에 보탬이 안 되는 것은 무엇도 신성하게 여기지 않고, 보탬이 되는 것은 무엇도 업신여기지 않은 자 말이야! 헤롯은 어땠나? 마카베오 가문은 어땠고? 첫째와 둘째 카이사르는 어땠나? 그들을 본받게. 지금 시작하라고. 가까이 보게—로마가, 이두매인 안티파테르를 도왔듯이, 너를 도울 채비가 되어 있어.\"\n\n유대 소년은 분노로 떨었다. 정원 문이 바로 곁에 있었으므로, 그는 어서 벗어나려는 마음에 걸음을 재촉했다.\n\n\"오, 로마여, 로마여!\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"슬기로워지게.\" 메살라가 말을 이었다. \"모세의 어리석은 짓들과 전통들을 버리고, 상황을 있는 그대로 보라고. 감히 파르카이의 얼굴을 마주 보면, 그들이 너한테 말해 줄 거다, 로마가 세상이라고. 그들에게 유대를 물어보면, 그들이 대답할 거야, 유대는 로마가 원하는 대로 되는 것이라고.\"\n\n그들은 이제 문 앞에 이르렀다. 유다는 멈춰 서서, 제 어깨에 얹힌 손을 부드럽게 떼어 내고, 메살라와 마주 섰으니, 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁했다.\n\n\"나는 너를 이해해, 네가 로마인이니까. 너는 나를 이해하지 못해—나는 이스라엘인이니까. 너는 오늘 내게 괴로움을 안겨 주었어, 우리가 결코 예전 같은 벗이 될 수 없음을—결코!—내게 확신시킴으로써 말이야. 여기서 우리는 갈라서네. 내 조상들의 하느님의 평화가 너와 함께 머물기를!\"\n\n메살라가 그에게 손을 내밀었으나, 유대인은 문간을 지나 걸어가 버렸다. 그가 가 버리자, 로마인은 한동안 잠잠히 있었다. 그러더니 그 또한 고개를 한 번 젖히며, 혼잣말로 중얼거리며 문간을 지났다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그렇게 되라지. 에로스는 죽었고, 마르스가 다스린다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n성스러운 도시로 들어가는 입구, 곧 오늘날 스데반 문이라 불리는 곳에서, 거리 하나가 서쪽으로 뻗어 있었으니, 안토니아 탑의 북쪽 정면과 나란한 선을 그으면서도 그 유명한 성채와는 한 블록 떨어져 있었다. 이 길은 티로포에온 골짜기에 이를 때까지 그 방향을 지키다가, 골짜기를 따라 잠시 남쪽으로 내려간 뒤, 다시 방향을 틀어 서쪽으로 달리다가, 전승에 따르면 재판의 문이라 일컬어지는 곳을 조금 지난 지점에서 돌연 남쪽으로 꺾였다. 이 성스러운 장소에 익숙한 나그네나 학자라면, 지금 묘사한 큰길이 비아 돌로로사의 한 부분임을 알아볼 것이다. 그것은 세상의 그 어떤 거리보다도 그리스도교인들에게 더 깊은 관심을 불러일으키는, 그러나 애잔한 종류의 관심을 자아내는 길이다. 지금의 목적이 이 거리 전체를 다룰 것을 요구하지는 않으므로, 마지막으로 언급한 모퉁이, 곧 길이 남쪽으로 방향을 바꾸는 그 자리에 서 있는 한 집을 가리키는 것으로 충분할 것이다. 그 집은 중요한 관심의 중심으로서, 다소 세밀한 묘사가 필요하다.\n\n그 건물은 북쪽과 서쪽을 향해 서 있었는데, 아마 각 면이 120미터쯤 되었을 것이며, 화려함을 뽐내는 동방의 건축물 대부분이 그러하듯 이층 높이에 완벽한 사각형을 이루고 있었다. 서쪽 거리는 폭이 3.5미터쯤 되었고, 북쪽 거리는 3미터를 넘지 않았다. 그래서 누군가 그 벽에 바싹 붙어 걸으며 올려다보았다면, 거칠고 미완성이며 정나미 떨어지는, 그러면서도 강건하고 위압적인 그 외관에 깊은 인상을 받았을 것이다. 벽은 큰 덩어리로 쌓아 올린 돌로 되어 있었으니, 다듬지 않은 채로—사실 바깥쪽은 채석장에서 막 떼어 낸 그대로였다. 이 시대의 비평가라면 그 집을 요새풍이라 단언했을 것이다. 다만 유난히 많이 장식된 창문들과, 출입문 곧 대문의 화려한 마무리만은 예외였다. 서쪽 창문은 넷이었고 북쪽 창문은 둘뿐이었는데, 모두 이층의 선을 따라 자리 잡아 아래의 큰길 위로 내밀어진 모양새였다. 대문은 바깥에서 보이는 일층 벽의 유일한 틈이었다. 게다가 그것은 공성추도 견딜 만큼 두꺼운 쇠빗장으로 촘촘히 박혀 있었을 뿐 아니라, 솜씨 좋게 다듬은 대리석 처마 장식으로 보호되었으니, 그 돌출이 어찌나 대담했던지, 그곳 사람들의 사정에 밝은 방문객이라면 거기 사는 부자가 정치와 신조에서 사두개파임을 능히 짐작할 만했다.\n\n젊은 유대인이 시장 위편의 궁전에서 로마인과 헤어진 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 그는 앞서 묘사한 집의 서쪽 대문 앞에 멈춰 서서 문을 두드렸다. 쪽문이—대문의 한쪽 문짝에 달린 작은 문이—열려 그를 들였다. 그는 황급히 안으로 들어서며, 문지기의 깊은 절에 답례도 하지 않았다.\n\n이 건물의 내부 구조에 대한 짐작을 얻기 위해서도, 또 그 젊은이에게 무슨 일이 더 일어나는지 보기 위해서도, 우리는 그를 따라가 보기로 한다.\n\n그가 들어선 통로는 벽에 널을 댄, 천장이 움푹움푹 팬 좁은 굴과 그리 다르지 않아 보였다. 양쪽에는 돌 벤치가 놓여 있었는데, 오랜 사용으로 얼룩지고 반들반들했다. 열두어 단의 계단을 지나자 그는 안마당에 이르렀다. 그 마당은 남북으로 긴 직사각형이었고, 동쪽을 제외한 사면이 이층집의 정면처럼 보이는 것들로 둘러싸여 있었다. 그 아래층은 르웬으로 나뉘어 있었고, 위층은 테라스로 되어 튼튼한 난간으로 보호되었다. 테라스를 오가는 하인들, 맷돌 가는 소리, 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝까지 매단 줄에 펄럭이는 빨래, 그곳을 마음껏 누리는 닭과 비둘기, 르웬에 마구간을 들인 염소와 소와 나귀와 말, 그리고 모두가 함께 쓰는 듯한 큼직한 물구유—이 모든 것이 이 마당이 주인의 살림살이에 딸린 곳임을 일러 주었다. 동쪽에는 칸막이 벽이 있었는데, 처음 것과 모든 면에서 똑같은 또 하나의 통로가 그 벽을 가르고 있었다.\n\n두 번째 통로를 지나, 젊은이는 두 번째 마당으로 들어섰다. 넓고 네모반듯한 그곳에는 관목과 덩굴이 심겨 있었으며, 북쪽 현관 가까이 만들어진 수반에서 흘러나오는 물 덕분에 싱그럽고 아름답게 가꾸어져 있었다. 이곳의 르웬은 높고 바람이 잘 통했으며, 흰빛과 붉은빛이 번갈아 줄지어진 휘장으로 그늘져 있었다. 르웬의 아치는 한데 묶인 기둥들 위에 얹혀 있었다. 남쪽의 계단은 위층 테라스로 이어졌는데, 그 위에는 햇볕을 막기 위한 커다란 차일이 펼쳐져 있었다. 또 다른 계단은 테라스에서 지붕까지 닿아 있었으니, 그 지붕의 가장자리는 네모난 마당을 빙 둘러 가며 조각된 처마 장식과, 육각형의 새빨간 구운 점토 기와로 된 난간으로 또렷이 구분되어 있었다. 더욱이 이곳에는 어디서나 빈틈없는 정갈함이 눈에 띄었으니, 구석마다 먼지 한 점, 관목에 누런 잎새 하나 허락하지 않는 그 정갈함은 다른 무엇 못지않게 전체의 흐뭇한 인상에 보탬이 되었다. 그리하여 방문객은 그 향긋한 공기를 들이마시는 것만으로도, 소개를 받기에 앞서 자신이 찾아온 집안의 품격을 미리 알 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n두 번째 마당으로 몇 걸음 들어선 소년은 오른쪽으로 돌아, 일부는 꽃이 핀 관목 사이의 길을 골라 걸어 계단으로 다가가, 테라스로 올라섰다. 그곳은 희고 갈색인 판석을 촘촘히 깔아 만든 넓은 포석으로, 많이 닳아 있었다. 차일 아래로 길을 잡아 북쪽의 한 출입구에 이른 그는, 한 방으로 들어섰는데, 등 뒤로 가리개가 떨어지자 방은 다시 어둠에 잠겼다. 그런데도 그는 멈추지 않고, 타일을 깐 바닥을 가로질러 긴 의자로 가더니, 얼굴을 아래로 한 채 몸을 던지고는, 팔짱 위에 이마를 얹고 가만히 누웠다.\n\n해 질 무렵 한 여자가 문으로 와서 그를 불렀다. 그가 대답하자 여자가 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"저녁 식사도 끝나고 밤이 되었습니다. 도련님은 시장하지 않으십니까?\" 여자가 물었다.\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"어디 편찮으십니까?\"\n\n\"졸리는구나.\"\n\n\"마님께서 도련님을 찾으셨습니다.\"\n\n\"어디 계시는데?\"\n\n\"지붕 위 정자에 계십니다.\"\n\n그가 몸을 추스르며 일어나 앉았다.\n\n\"알았다. 먹을 것을 좀 가져다오.\"\n\n\"무엇을 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"네 마음대로, 암라. 아픈 건 아니고 그저 만사가 시들할 뿐이다. 삶이 오늘 아침만큼 즐겁게 느껴지지 않는구나. 새로운 병이로구나, 오 나의 암라야. 나를 그토록 잘 알고, 한 번도 나를 저버린 적 없는 너라면, 이제 음식이자 약이 될 만한 것을 떠올릴 수 있겠지. 네가 고르는 대로 가져다오.\"\n\n암라가 던지는 물음들과, 그것을 묻는 목소리는—낮고, 다정하고, 근심 어린 그 목소리는—둘 사이의 애틋한 정을 일러 주는 것이었다. 여자는 그의 이마에 손을 얹어 보았다. 그러고는 안심한 듯 밖으로 나가며 말했다. \"찾아보겠습니다.\"\n\n잠시 후 여자가 돌아왔는데, 나무 쟁반에 우유 한 그릇, 잘게 뜯은 흰 빵 몇 조각, 곱게 빻은 밀로 만든 부드러운 반죽, 구운 새 한 마리, 그리고 꿀과 소금을 받쳐 들고 왔다. 쟁반 한쪽 끝에는 포도주가 가득 든 은잔이, 다른 한쪽 끝에는 불을 밝힌 놋쇠 손등잔이 놓여 있었다.\n\n그러자 방의 모습이 드러났다. 매끄럽게 회칠한 벽, 빗물 자국과 세월에 갈색이 된 굵은 떡갈나무 서까래들로 끊긴 천장, 작은 마름모꼴의 희고 푸른 타일을 깐, 아주 단단하고 오래갈 듯한 바닥, 사자 다리를 본떠 깎은 다리가 달린 걸상 몇 개, 바닥에서 조금 올려 만들어 푸른 천으로 가장자리를 두르고 줄무늬가 든 커다란 모직 담요인지 숄로 일부를 덮은 긴 의자—한마디로 히브리식 침실이었다.\n\n같은 불빛이 그 여자의 모습도 드러내 주었다. 여자는 걸상 하나를 긴 의자 곁으로 끌어다 그 위에 쟁반을 올려놓더니, 그를 받들 채비를 하고 바로 옆에 무릎을 꿇었다. 그 얼굴은 쉰쯤 된 여자의 얼굴로, 가무잡잡한 피부에 검은 눈을 지녔으며, 그 순간만큼은 거의 어머니와 같은 다정함이 어려 부드러워져 있었다. 흰 터번이 머리를 덮고 귓불을 드러냈는데, 거기에는 그 신분을 결정짓는 표시가 있었으니—굵은 송곳으로 뚫은 구멍이었다. 그 여자는 이집트 태생의 노예로, 거룩한 오십 년째 해가 와도 자유를 얻을 수 없는 처지였다. 아니, 설령 자유가 주어진다 해도 받아들이지 않았을 것이다. 그가 보살피는 그 소년이 곧 그 여자의 삶이었기 때문이다. 여자는 그가 갓난아이일 때 젖을 먹였고, 어린 시절 내내 보살폈으니, 그 시중을 끊을 수가 없었다. 그 여자의 사랑 속에서 그는 결코 어른이 될 수 없었다.\n\n그는 식사를 하는 동안 단 한 번 입을 열었다.\n\n\"기억하느냐, 오 나의 암라야,\" 그가 말했다. \"한때 여기 와서 며칠씩 머물다 가곤 했던 메살라 말이다.\"\n\n\"기억합니다.\"\n\n\"몇 해 전에 로마로 갔다가 이제 돌아왔다. 오늘 그를 찾아갔지.\"\n\n소년은 진저리치는 듯 부르르 떨었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었구나 싶었습니다.\" 여자가 깊은 관심을 보이며 말했다. \"저는 한 번도 그 메살라가 좋은 적이 없었습니다. 다 말씀해 보십시오.\"\n\n그러나 그는 깊은 생각에 잠겼고, 여자가 거듭 묻자 다만 이렇게만 말했다. \"그는 많이 변했다. 다시는 그와 상관하지 않을 것이다.\"\n\n암라가 쟁반을 치우자, 그 또한 밖으로 나가, 테라스에서 지붕 위로 올라갔다.\n\n독자는 동방에서 지붕을 어떻게 쓰는지 어느 정도 알고 있으리라 짐작한다. 관습에 관한 한, 어디서나 기후가 입법자다. 시리아의 여름 한낮은 안락을 찾는 이를 어둑한 르웬으로 몰아넣지만, 밤은 그를 일찌감치 불러내며, 산비탈 위로 짙어지는 그림자는 키르케 같은 노래꾼들을 어슴푸레 가려 주는 너울처럼 보인다. 그러나 그 노래꾼들은 멀리 있는 반면, 지붕은 가까이 있어, 어른거리는 평원의 수면보다 충분히 높이 솟아 서늘한 바람이 찾아들 만하고, 나무들보다 충분히 높아 별들을 더 가까이, 적어도 더 밝게 빛나도록 끌어내릴 만하다. 그리하여 지붕은 안식처가 되었다—놀이터, 침실, 규방, 가족의 만남터, 음악과 춤과 담소와 사색과 기도의 자리가 되었다.\n\n더 추운 지방에서 비용을 아끼지 않고 실내를 꾸미게 만드는 그 동기가, 동방인에게는 지붕을 치장하라고 일렀다. 모세가 명한 난간은 옹기장이의 자랑거리가 되었고, 그 위로는 뒷날 평범한 것이며 기이한 것이며 갖가지 망루가 솟아올랐다. 더 뒤에는 왕과 제후가 대리석과 황금으로 지은 정자로 제 지붕을 장식했다. 바빌론 사람이 공중에 정원을 매달았을 때, 사치는 그 생각을 더 멀리 밀고 갈 수 없을 만큼 극에 달했다.\n\n우리가 뒤따르는 소년은 지붕 위를 천천히 가로질러, 궁전의 북서쪽 모퉁이 위에 세워진 망루로 갔다. 만일 그가 낯선 이였다면, 다가가면서 그 건물에 한 번쯤 눈길을 던지고, 어둠이 허락하는 모든 것을—어둑한 덩어리, 낮고, 격자창을 내고, 기둥을 세우고, 둥근 지붕을 인 그 모습을 보았을 것이다. 그는 반쯤 걷어 올린 휘장 아래를 지나 안으로 들어섰다. 안은 온통 어둠뿐이었는데, 다만 사면에 출입구처럼 아치를 이룬 트인 곳들이 있어, 별빛 어린 하늘이 그리로 내다보였다. 그 트인 곳 가운데 하나에, 긴 의자의 방석에 기대어 누운 한 여인의 모습이 보였으니, 하얗게 나부끼는 옷자락에 가려 흐릿했다. 바닥을 밟는 그의 발소리에, 여인의 손에 들린 부채가 멈추었고, 별빛이 거기 박힌 보석에 부딪쳐 반짝였다. 여인은 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 그의 이름을 불렀다.\n\n\"유다, 내 아들아!\"\n\n\"저예요, 어머니.\" 그가 걸음을 재촉하며 대답했다.\n\n그에게로 가서 그가 무릎을 꿇자, 여인은 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안고, 입맞춤하며 가슴에 끌어안았다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n어머니는 다시 방석에 기대어 편안한 자세를 취했고, 아들은 긴 의자에 누워 어머니의 무릎에 머리를 뉘었다. 두 사람은 열린 곳으로 내다보며 가까운 곳에 늘어선 낮은 지붕들과, 서쪽으로 그들이 산이라고 알고 있는 검푸른 띠와, 그 그늘진 깊이마다 별빛으로 찬란한 하늘을 볼 수 있었다. 도시는 고요했다. 오직 바람만이 일렁였다.\n\n\"암라가 네게 무슨 일이 있었다고 하더구나.\" 어머니는 아들의 뺨을 어루만지며 말했다. \"내 유다가 어렸을 때는 사소한 일에도 마음 졸이게 두었다만, 이제 너는 어른이 되었지. 잊어서는 안 돼.\" 그 목소리가 무척 부드러워졌다. \"언젠가 너는 내 영웅이 되어야 한다는 것을.\"\n\n어머니는 이 땅에서 거의 잊혀 가는 언어로 말했다. 그러나 소수의 사람들은—그들은 언제나 재산만큼이나 혈통에서도 풍요로운 자들이었다—이방 민족과 더 확실히 구별되기 위해 그 언어를 순수하게 간직해 왔으니, 사랑받던 리브가와 라헬이 베냐민에게 노래 불러 주던 바로 그 언어였다.\n\n그 말이 아들을 다시 생각에 잠기게 한 듯했다. 그러나 잠시 후 그는 자기를 부쳐 주던 어머니의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"어머니, 오늘 저는 지금껏 한 번도 마음에 둔 적 없는 여러 가지를 생각하게 되었습니다. 먼저 말씀해 주세요. 저는 무엇이 되어야 합니까?\"\n\n\"이미 말하지 않았느냐? 너는 내 영웅이 되어야 한다.\"\n\n그는 어머니의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었으나, 어머니가 농담하고 있음을 알았다. 그는 더 진지해졌다.\n\n\"어머니는 정말 선하고 자애로우십니다. 어머니만큼 저를 사랑해 줄 사람은 없을 거예요.\"\n\n그는 그 손에 거듭거듭 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"왜 어머니가 그 물음을 미루려 하시는지 알 것 같습니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"지금까지 제 삶은 어머니의 것이었지요. 어머니의 보살핌은 얼마나 다정하고 얼마나 달콤했는지요! 그것이 영원히 이어졌으면 좋겠습니다. 그러나 그럴 수는 없겠지요. 언젠가 제가 제 자신의 주인이 되는 것이 주님의 뜻이니까요. 그것은 헤어짐의 날이요, 그래서 어머니에게는 두려운 날이기도 합니다. 우리 용감하고 진지해집시다. 저는 어머니의 영웅이 되겠습니다. 그러나 어머니가 저를 그 길로 인도해 주셔야 합니다. 어머니도 율법을 아시지요. 이스라엘의 아들이라면 누구나 어떤 생업을 가져야 한다는 것을요. 저도 예외가 아니니, 이제 묻겠습니다. 저는 가축을 칠까요? 아니면 땅을 일굴까요? 톱질을 할까요? 서기나 율법학자가 될까요? 저는 무엇이 되어야 합니까? 자애롭고 선하신 어머니, 제가 답을 찾도록 도와주세요.\"\n\n\"오늘 가말리엘이 강론을 했더구나.\" 어머니가 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"그랬다면 저는 듣지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 시므온과 함께 거닐었겠지. 사람들 말로는 그가 가문의 천재성을 물려받았다고 하던데.\"\n\n\"아니요, 그도 만나지 못했습니다. 저는 성전이 아니라 시장에 다녀왔습니다. 젊은 메살라를 만났지요.\"\n\n그 목소리에 어떤 변화가 어머니의 주의를 끌었다. 어떤 예감이 가슴의 박동을 빠르게 했고, 부채가 다시 멈추었다.\n\n\"메살라라니!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"그 애가 무슨 말을 했기에 너를 그토록 괴롭게 했단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"그는 무척 변했습니다.\"\n\n\"로마인이 되어 돌아왔다는 말이로구나.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"로마인이라!\" 어머니는 반쯤 혼잣말로 말을 이었다. \"온 세상에 그 말은 주인을 뜻하지. 떠난 지 얼마나 되었느냐?\"\n\n\"오 년입니다.\"\n\n어머니는 고개를 들어 밤하늘 저편을 바라보았다.\n\n\"비아 사크라의 풍조는 이집트인의 거리나 바빌론에서는 그럭저럭 통하겠지. 그러나 예루살렘에서는, 우리의 예루살렘에서는, 언약이 변함없이 머무는 법이다.\"\n\n그리고 그 생각으로 가득 차, 어머니는 다시 편안한 자리로 몸을 기댔다. 먼저 입을 연 것은 아들이었다.\n\n\"메살라가 한 말은, 어머니, 그 자체로도 충분히 모질었습니다. 그러나 그 태도까지 더하면, 어떤 말들은 참을 수 없는 것이었지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 알 것 같구나. 로마는, 그 시인이며 웅변가며 원로원 의원이며 궁정인들까지, 풍자라 부르는 것을 부려대는 허세에 미쳐 있지.\"\n\n\"위대한 민족은 모두 자긍심이 강한 법이겠지요.\" 아들은 어머니의 말을 거의 알아차리지 못한 채 말을 이어 갔다. \"그러나 그 민족의 자긍심은 다른 어느 것과도 다릅니다. 요즘 들어 어찌나 부풀어 올랐는지 신들조차 그것을 가까스로 면할 지경이지요.\"\n\n\"신들이 면한다고!\" 어머니가 재빨리 말했다. \"한둘 아닌 로마인이 숭배를 제 신성한 권리로 받아들였느니라.\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 메살라도 늘 그 불쾌한 기질을 제 몫만큼은 지니고 있었습니다. 어린아이였을 때도 헤롯조차 영예로이 맞아들이던 이방인을 조롱하는 것을 본 적이 있습니다. 그래도 그는 늘 유대만큼은 봐주었지요. 오늘 저와 이야기하면서 처음으로 그는 우리의 풍습과 하느님을 가볍게 비웃었습니다. 어머니가 바라셨을 대로, 저는 그와 영영 갈라섰습니다. 그리고 이제, 사랑하는 어머니, 저는 그 로마인의 멸시에 정말로 정당한 근거가 있는지 더 확실히 알고 싶습니다. 제가 무엇이 그보다 못하단 말입니까? 우리가 더 낮은 부류의 사람들입니까? 어찌하여 저는, 카이사르 앞에서조차, 노예 같은 위축을 느껴야 합니까? 무엇보다도 말씀해 주세요. 만약 제게 그럴 영혼이 있고 그러기를 택한다면, 어찌하여 저는 세상의 모든 영역에서 영예를 좇아서는 안 됩니까? 어찌하여 저는 칼을 들고 전쟁의 열정을 마음껏 펼쳐서는 안 됩니까? 시인으로서 어찌하여 모든 주제를 노래해서는 안 됩니까? 저는 금속을 다루는 장인도, 양 떼를 기르는 자도, 상인도 될 수 있는데, 어찌하여 그리스인처럼 예술가는 못 됩니까? 말씀해 주세요, 어머니. 이것이 제 괴로움의 전부입니다. 어찌하여 이스라엘의 아들은 로마인이 할 수 있는 모든 것을 할 수 없는 겁니까?\"\n\n독자는 이 물음들을 시장에서의 그 대화로 거슬러 짚어 볼 것이다. 어머니는 온 신경을 곤두세워 듣고 있었기에, 아들에게 그만큼 마음을 쏟지 않는 이라면 놓쳤을 무언가로부터—그 주제의 연결고리, 물음이 겨냥하는 바, 어쩌면 그 억양과 어조로부터—똑같은 대조를 떠올리는 데 결코 더디지 않았다. 어머니는 몸을 일으켜, 아들 못지않게 빠르고 날카로운 목소리로 대답했다. \"알겠다, 알겠어! 메살라는 어린 시절에 교제로 거의 유대인이나 다름없었지. 그가 여기 남았더라면 개종자가 되었을지도 모른다. 우리는 모두 우리 삶을 무르익게 하는 영향으로부터 그토록 많은 것을 빌려 오니까. 그러나 로마에서 보낸 세월이 그에게는 너무 과했던 게야. 그 변화가 놀랍지는 않다만.\" 어머니의 목소리가 낮아졌다. \"그래도 적어도 너에게만은 다정하게 굴 수 있었을 텐데. 젊은 시절에 첫사랑을 잊을 수 있는 것은 모질고 잔인한 천성이지.\"\n\n어머니의 손이 아들의 이마 위로 가볍게 내려앉았고, 손가락이 머리카락에 얽혀 사랑스럽게 머무는 동안, 그 눈은 시야에 들어오는 가장 높은 별들을 찾았다. 어머니의 자긍심은 아들의 자긍심에 화답했으니, 단지 메아리로서가 아니라 완벽한 공감의 한목소리로였다. 어머니는 아들에게 답해 줄 것이었다. 그러면서도 무슨 일이 있어도 그 답이 흡족하지 못한 것이 되기를 바라지 않았다. 못하다는 인정은 평생토록 아들의 기상을 약하게 할 수도 있었다. 어머니는 자기 능력에 대한 불안으로 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"네가 내놓은 물음은, 유다야, 여자가 다룰 만한 주제가 아니다. 그 고찰은 내일로 미루게 해다오. 그러면 내가 지혜로운 시므온을 불러서—\"\n\n\"저를 학장님께 보내지 마세요.\" 아들이 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"그분을 우리에게 오시게 하마.\"\n\n\"아니요, 저는 지식 이상의 것을 구합니다. 그분이 저보다 그것을 더 잘 주실 수는 있겠지요, 어머니. 그러나 어머니는 그분이 줄 수 없는 것을 주심으로써 더 잘하실 수 있습니다. 바로 사람의 영혼의 영혼인 결의 말입니다.\"\n\n어머니는 아들의 물음이 지닌 온 뜻을 헤아려 보려는 듯, 빠른 눈길로 하늘을 훑었다.\n\n\"우리 자신을 위해 정의를 갈구하면서도, 남에게 부당하게 구는 것은 결코 현명한 일이 아니다. 우리가 정복한 적의 용맹을 부정하는 것은 우리의 승리를 깎아내리는 것이지. 그리고 적이 우리를 막아낼 만큼, 하물며 우리를 정복할 만큼 강하다면,\" 어머니가 머뭇거렸다. \"자존심은 우리의 불운을 두고, 적이 우리보다 못한 자질을 지녔다고 탓하기보다는 다른 설명을 찾으라 명하는 법이다.\"\n\n이렇게, 아들에게라기보다 자기 자신에게 말하듯, 어머니는 입을 열었다.\n\n\"마음을 굳게 먹어라, 아들아. 메살라는 고귀한 혈통을 타고났다. 그 가문은 여러 세대에 걸쳐 명망이 높았지. 공화정 로마 시절에는—얼마나 옛날까지 거슬러 가는지 나도 알 수 없다만—그들은 명성을 떨쳤으니, 더러는 군인으로, 더러는 정치가로 그러했다. 그 이름으로 집정관이 된 이는 한 사람밖에 떠오르지 않는구나. 그들의 신분은 원로원급이었고, 늘 부유했기에 그 후원은 언제나 사람들이 구하는 바였다. 그러나 오늘 네 친구가 제 조상을 자랑했다면, 너는 네 조상을 헤아려 보임으로써 그를 부끄럽게 할 수 있었을 게다. 그가 그 가계를 거슬러 짚을 수 있는 세월이나, 업적이나, 신분이나, 부를 들먹였다면—그런 언급은, 큰일이 요구할 때를 빼면, 옹졸한 마음의 표지이거니와—만약 그가 그것을 제 우월함의 증거로 내세웠다면, 그때 너는 두려움 없이, 하나하나에 버티고 서서, 그에게 기록을 견주어 보자고 도전할 수 있었을 게다.\"\n\n잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 어머니는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"요즘 굳게 자리 잡은 생각 하나는, 세월이 종족과 가문의 고귀함과 큰 관계가 있다는 것이다. 그 점에서 이스라엘의 아들보다 우월하다고 뽐내는 로마인은 막상 증명에 나서면 언제나 무너지고 말 게다. 로마의 건국이 그들의 시작이었지. 그들 중 가장 빼어난 자라도 그 시기 너머로 제 가계를 거슬러 짚을 수 없거니와, 그러는 척하는 자도 드물다. 그러는 자가 있다 해도, 단언컨대 전승에 기대지 않고서는 누구도 그 주장을 입증하지 못할 게다. 메살라도 분명 그러지 못한다. 이제 우리 자신을 보자꾸나. 우리는 더 잘할 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n빛이 조금만 더 밝았다면, 아들은 어머니의 얼굴에 번지는 자긍심을 볼 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n\"로마인이 우리에게 도전해 온다고 상상해 보자. 나는 그에게 답하리라, 의심하지도 뽐내지도 않으면서.\"\n\n어머니의 목소리가 흔들렸다. 어떤 다정한 생각이 그 논지의 형태를 바꾸어 놓았다.\n\n\"네 아버지는, 유다야, 그 조상들과 함께 안식하고 계신다. 그래도 나는, 마치 오늘 저녁 일인 듯, 그날을 기억하는구나. 그분과 내가, 기뻐하는 여러 벗들과 함께, 너를 주님께 바치러 성전에 올라갔던 날을. 우리는 비둘기를 제물로 바쳤고, 나는 제사장에게 네 이름을 일러주었지. 그는 내 앞에서 그것을 적었다. '후르 가문 이타마르의 아들 유다'라고. 그러고 나서 그 이름은 옮겨져, 거룩한 가문에 바쳐진 기록의 분책에 적혔단다.\"\n\n\"이런 식의 등록 관습이 언제 시작되었는지는 나도 말해 줄 수 없구나. 우리는 그것이 이집트 탈출 이전부터 행해졌음을 알고 있다. 힐렐의 말로는, 아브라함이 처음으로 자신의 이름과 그 아들들의 이름으로 그 기록을 열게 했다는구나. 그를 다른 모든 종족으로부터 갈라내어 가장 높고 가장 고귀한, 그야말로 땅의 택함받은 자로 삼으신 주님의 약속에 감동하여서 말이다. 야곱과 맺은 언약도 같은 효력을 지녔지. '네 씨로 말미암아 땅의 모든 민족이 복을 받으리라.' 여호와 이레라는 곳에서 천사가 아브라함에게 그리 말씀하셨다. '네가 누워 있는 땅을 내가 너와 네 씨에게 주리라.' 하란으로 가는 길에 벧엘에서 잠든 야곱에게 주님 친히 그리 말씀하셨다. 그 후에 현인들은 약속의 땅을 공정하게 나눌 날을 내다보았다. 그리고 분배의 날에 누가 몫을 받을 자격이 있는지 알 수 있도록, 족보의 책이 시작되었지. 그러나 그 때문만은 아니었다. 그 조상을 통해 온 땅에 복을 내리겠다는 약속은 먼 미래에까지 미쳤다. 그 복과 관련하여 한 이름이 거론되었으니—은인은 택함받은 가문에서 가장 비천한 자일 수도 있었다. 우리 주 하느님은 신분이나 재물의 차별을 모르시니까. 그래서 그 일을 목격할 세대의 사람들에게 그 성취가 분명히 드러나도록, 또 그들이 마땅한 이에게 영광을 돌릴 수 있도록, 그 기록은 절대적인 확실성으로 보존되어야 했다. 과연 그렇게 보존되어 왔겠느냐?\"\n\n부채가 이리저리 오갔다. 마침내 참다못해 아들이 물음을 되풀이했다. \"그 기록은 절대적으로 참된 것입니까?\"\n\n\"힐렐은 그렇다고 했다. 그리고 살아온 모든 이 가운데 그 주제에 그만큼 정통한 자는 없었지. 우리 민족은 때때로 율법의 어떤 부분에는 무심했어도, 이 부분만은 한 번도 그러지 않았다. 그 선한 학장 자신이 족보의 책을 세 시기에 걸쳐 따라가 보았단다—약속에서 성전의 봉헌까지, 거기서 다시 포로 시기까지, 거기서 또 현재까지. 단 한 번 그 기록이 어지러워진 적이 있었으니, 그것은 두 번째 시기의 끝이었다. 그러나 민족이 그 긴 유배에서 돌아왔을 때, 하느님께 바치는 첫 의무로서, 스룹바벨이 그 책들을 복원했지. 그리하여 우리는 다시 한번 유대 혈통의 계보를 끊김 없이 온전히 이천 년 전까지 거슬러 짚을 수 있게 되었다. 그리고 이제는—\"\n\n어머니는 그 진술에 담긴 세월의 크기를 듣는 이가 헤아려 보게 하려는 듯 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"그리고 이제,\" 어머니가 말을 이었다. \"세월로 풍요로워진 혈통이라는 로마인의 자랑은 어찌 되겠느냐? 그 잣대로 보면, 저 너머 옛 르바임에서 가축을 지키는 이스라엘의 아들들이 마르키우스 가문의 가장 고귀한 자보다 더 고귀하니라.\"\n\n\"그러면 저는, 어머니, 그 책들에 따르면 저는 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"지금까지 내가 한 말은, 아들아, 네 물음과 관련된 것이었다. 이제 네게 답하마. 메살라가 여기 있다면, 그는 남들이 그러했듯 이렇게 말할지도 모른다. 아시리아인이 예루살렘을 점령하고 성전을 그 온갖 귀한 보물과 함께 헐어 버렸을 때 네 가계의 정확한 흔적도 끊겼다고. 그러나 너는 스룹바벨의 경건한 행적을 내세우며, 서방의 야만족이 로마를 점령하고 그 폐허가 된 터에 여섯 달 동안 진을 쳤을 때 로마 족보의 모든 진실도 끝났다고 되받아칠 수 있다. 그 정부가 가문의 역사를 보존했더냐? 그랬다 한들 그 끔찍한 시절에 그것들이 어찌 되었겠느냐? 아니다, 아니야. 우리의 족보의 책에는 진실이 있다. 그리고 그것을 거슬러 포로 시기까지, 첫 성전의 건립까지, 이집트로부터의 행군까지 따라가면, 너는 여호수아의 동료였던 후르의 직계 후손이라는 절대적인 확신을 얻게 된다. 세월로 신성해진 가계라는 점에서, 그 영예가 완벽하지 않으냐? 더 따라가 보고 싶으냐? 그렇다면 토라를 들고 민수기를 살펴보아라. 그러면 아담 이후 일흔두 세대 가운데서 네 가문의 바로 그 시조를 찾아낼 수 있을 게다.\"\n\n지붕 위 방에는 한동안 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 어머니.\" 유다가 이윽고 두 손으로 어머니의 두 손을 감싸쥐며 말했다. \"진심으로 고맙습니다. 선한 학장님을 부르지 않은 것은 옳은 일이었습니다. 그분도 어머니보다 더 저를 만족시켜 주지는 못했을 거예요. 그러나 한 가문을 참으로 고귀하게 만드는 데, 세월만으로 충분합니까?\"\n\n\"아, 너는 잊고 있구나, 잊고 있어. 우리의 권리는 단지 세월에만 기대지 않는다. 주님의 택하심이 바로 우리의 각별한 영광이니라.\"\n\n\"어머니는 종족을 말씀하고 계시지만, 저는, 어머니, 가문을 말하는 겁니다. 우리 가문 말입니다. 아버지 아브라함 이후의 세월 동안 그들은 무엇을 이루었습니까? 무엇을 했습니까? 그들을 동족 위로 높여 줄 어떤 위대한 일을 했단 말입니까?\"\n\n어머니는 머뭇거렸다. 이 내내 자기가 아들의 뜻을 잘못 짚어 온 것은 아닌지 생각하면서였다. 아들이 구하는 정보는 상처받은 자존심을 달래기 위한 것 이상일 수도 있었다. 젊음이란 그저 색칠한 껍데기에 지나지 않으니, 그 안에서는 사람의 영혼이라는 저 경이로운 것이 끊임없이 자라며 그 출현의 순간을 기다리고 있다. 어떤 이에게는 그 순간이 더 일찍 오기도 한다. 어머니는 어쩌면 그 지고한 순간이 아들에게 찾아온 것일지도 모른다는 깨달음에 몸을 떨었다. 갓 태어난 아이가 시험해 본 적 없는 손을 내밀어 그림자를 움켜쥐려 하며 우는 것처럼, 아들의 영혼도 일시적인 눈멂 속에서 그 손에 잡히지 않는 미래를 붙들려 애쓰고 있는 것인지도 몰랐다. 소년이 찾아와 나는 누구이며 무엇이 되어야 하느냐고 묻는 이는, 그 무엇보다 큰 보살핌이 필요하다. 답하는 한 마디 한 마디는, 예술가가 빚는 점토에 그의 손길 하나하나가 그러하듯, 훗날의 삶에 그대로 새겨질 것이기 때문이다.\n\n\"내게 한 가지 느낌이 드는구나, 유다야.\" 어머니는 아들이 어루만지던 그 손으로 아들의 뺨을 다독이며 말했다. \"내가 지금껏 한 모든 말이, 상상 속이라기보다 더 실재하는 어떤 적수와 다투어 온 것이라는 느낌이 든다. 메살라가 그 적이라면, 나 혼자 어둠 속에서 그와 싸우게 두지 마라. 그가 한 말을 모두 들려다오.\"\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n그리하여 젊은 이스라엘인은 이야기를 이어 가, 메살라와 나눈 대화를 되짚었다. 특히 유대인과 그들의 관습, 그리고 갇혀 사는 삶을 멸시하던 메살라의 말들을 낱낱이 들춰냈다.\n\n어머니는 말없이 귀를 기울이며, 사정을 또렷이 헤아렸다. 유다는 시장 광장의 궁전을 찾아갔다. 여러 해 전 헤어질 때 그대로일 것이라 믿었던 어린 시절 동무를 향한 그리움에 이끌려서였다. 그러나 그를 맞이한 사내는, 웃음도 옛 놀이에 대한 이야기도 없이, 오로지 앞날만을 떠들며 손에 넣을 영광과 부와 권력을 늘어놓았다. 자기가 어떤 영향을 끼쳤는지도 모른 채, 그 손님은 자존심에 상처를 입었으면서도 한편으로는 자연스러운 야망에 마음이 흔들린 채 돌아왔다. 그러나 질투심 깊은 어머니는 그것을 알아보았고, 그 동경이 어디로 향할지 알 수 없었기에, 곧장 두려움에 사로잡힌 유대인이 되었다. 만일 그것이 아이를 조상 대대로 이어 온 신앙에서 멀어지게 한다면? 그녀가 보기에 그런 결과는 다른 어떤 일보다도, 모든 일을 합친 것보다도 더 끔찍했다. 그것을 막을 길은 단 하나뿐이라, 그녀는 그 일에 나섰다. 사랑이 타고난 힘을 북돋워 주었기에, 그녀의 말투에는 사내다운 힘이 실리고 때로는 시인의 열정마저 깃들었다.\n\n\"제 스스로를 적어도 어느 민족과 견주어 못하지 않다고 여기지 않은 백성은 한 번도 없었단다.\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. \"아들아, 스스로를 가장 뛰어나다고 믿지 않은 위대한 나라도 한 번도 없었어. 로마인이 이스라엘을 깔보고 비웃을 때, 그는 그저 이집트인과 아시리아인과 마케도니아인의 어리석음을 되풀이하고 있을 뿐이야. 그리고 그 비웃음이 하느님을 향한 것인 이상, 그 끝도 똑같으리라.\"\n\n그녀의 목소리가 더욱 굳어졌다.\n\n\"민족의 우열을 가릴 법칙 따위는 없단다. 그러니 그런 주장은 헛되고, 그것을 두고 다투는 일도 부질없지. 한 백성이 일어나, 제 길을 달리고, 스스로 멸하거나 다른 자의 손에 멸하지. 그 다른 자는 그들의 권세를 이어받아 그 자리를 차지하고, 그들의 비석 위에 새 이름을 새기는 거야. 역사란 그런 것이지. 만일 내가 하느님과 인간을 가장 단순한 형상으로 그려 보라는 부탁을 받는다면, 나는 직선 하나와 원 하나를 그릴 거야. 그리고 직선을 두고 말하겠지. '이것은 하느님이다. 오직 그분만이 영원히 곧장 앞으로 나아가시기 때문이다.' 또 원을 두고 말하겠지. '이것은 인간이다. 그의 나아감이란 이러하다.' 민족들의 행로에 아무 차이가 없다는 뜻은 아니야. 똑같은 둘은 없으니까. 그러나 그 차이는, 어떤 이들이 말하듯, 그들이 그리는 원의 크기나 그들이 덮은 땅의 넓이에 있는 것이 아니라, 그들의 움직임이 머무는 권역에 있단다. 가장 높은 것이 하느님께 가장 가까운 거야.\n\n\"아들아, 여기서 멈춘다면 처음 자리에 그대로 주제를 두고 마는 셈이지. 더 나아가 보자. 각 민족이 제 길을 달리는 동안 그려 내는 원의 높이를 잴 수 있는 표징이 있단다. 그것으로 히브리인과 로마인을 견주어 보자.\n\n\"모든 표징 가운데 가장 단순한 것은 백성의 일상이야. 이것에 대해서는 한마디만 하마. 이스라엘은 때때로 하느님을 잊은 적은 있으나, 로마인은 그분을 안 적이 한 번도 없었어. 그러니 견주는 일 자체가 가당치 않지.\n\n\"네 친구가, 아니 옛 친구가, 내가 너의 말을 옳게 알아들었다면, 우리에게는 시인도 예술가도 전사도 없었다고 꾸짖었다지. 내 생각엔 우리에게 위대한 인물이 없었다고 부정하려는 뜻이었을 거야. 위대한 인물이란, 그다음으로 가장 확실한 표징이거든. 이 비난을 올바로 헤아리려면 먼저 정의부터 내려야 해. 얘야, 위대한 인물이란 그 삶으로써 하느님께 부름받지는 않았더라도 적어도 인정받았음이 드러나는 사람을 말한단다. 한 페르시아인은 우리의 신앙을 저버린 조상들을 벌하는 데 쓰여, 그들을 포로로 끌고 갔지. 또 한 페르시아인은 그 자손들을 거룩한 땅으로 되돌리도록 뽑혔어. 그러나 그 둘보다 더 위대한 자는 마케도니아인이었으니, 그를 통해 유대와 성전이 당한 황폐의 원수가 갚아졌단다. 그 사람들의 남다른 점은, 저마다 거룩한 목적을 위해 주님께 뽑혔다는 데 있어. 그들이 이방인이었다는 사실이 그 영광을 깎아내리지는 않지. 내가 말을 이어 가는 동안 이 정의를 잊지 말거라.\n\n\"전쟁이야말로 인간의 가장 고귀한 일이며, 가장 드높은 위대함은 전쟁터에서 자라난다는 생각이 있단다. 세상이 그 생각을 받아들였다 해서, 너만은 속지 말거라. 우리가 무언가를 섬겨야 한다는 것은, 우리가 이해할 수 없는 무언가가 있는 한 영원히 이어질 법칙이야. 야만인의 기도란 힘을 향해 올리는 두려움의 울부짖음이지. 힘이야말로 그가 또렷이 떠올릴 수 있는 유일한 신성한 속성이거든. 그래서 그는 영웅을 믿는 거야. 유피테르가 로마의 영웅이 아니라면 무엇이겠니? 그리스인이 큰 영광을 누리는 까닭은, 그들이 처음으로 정신을 힘 위에 두었기 때문이지. 아테네에서는 웅변가와 철학자가 전사보다 더 존경받았어. 전차를 모는 자와 가장 빠른 주자는 여전히 경기장의 우상이지만, 영생의 화관은 가장 감미로운 노래꾼을 위해 남겨져 있단다. 어느 시인의 태어난 곳을 두고 일곱 도시가 다투었지. 그러나 헬라스인이 옛 야만의 신앙을 처음으로 부정한 자들이었을까? 아니야. 아들아, 그 영광은 우리의 것이란다. 야만성에 맞서 우리 조상들은 하느님을 세웠지. 우리의 예배에서는 두려움의 울부짖음이 호산나와 시편에 자리를 내주었어. 그렇게 히브리인과 그리스인은 온 인류를 앞으로, 위로 이끌어 갈 수도 있었지. 그러나, 아아! 세상의 통치란 전쟁을 영원한 조건으로 전제한단다. 그래서 로마인은 정신 위에, 또 하느님 위에 제 카이사르를 왕좌에 앉혔어. 손에 넣을 수 있는 모든 권력을 빨아들이고, 다른 어떤 위대함도 금하는 자를 말이야.\n\n\"그리스인의 통치는 천재가 꽃피던 시절이었어. 그때 누리던 자유에 보답하여, 정신은 얼마나 많은 사상가들을 이끌어 냈던가? 어떤 탁월함에도 저마다 영광이 있었고, 그 완벽함이 어찌나 절대적이었던지 전쟁만 빼고는 로마인조차 모방하려고 몸을 굽혔단다. 오늘날 그리스인은 포룸의 웅변가들이 본받는 본보기야. 귀 기울여 보렴, 어떤 로마의 노래에서든 그리스의 운율이 들릴 테니. 로마인이 입을 열어 도덕이나 추상이나 자연의 신비를 슬기롭게 논한다면, 그는 표절자이거나 아니면 그리스인을 시조로 둔 어느 학파의 제자일 뿐이야. 다시 말하지만, 전쟁 말고는 로마가 독창성을 내세울 만한 것이 아무것도 없단다. 그들의 경기와 구경거리는 그리스의 발명품인데, 천한 무리의 잔인함을 채워 주려고 거기에 피를 끼얹은 것이지. 그들의 종교는, 그것을 종교라 부를 수 있다면, 다른 모든 백성의 신앙에서 끌어모은 것들로 이루어졌어. 그들이 가장 떠받드는 신들은 올림포스에서 온 것들이야. 그들의 마르스도 그렇고, 게다가 그들이 그토록 떠받드는 유피테르도 마찬가지지. 그러니, 아들아, 온 세상에서 오직 우리 이스라엘만이 그리스인의 우월함에 맞설 수 있고, 그와 더불어 독창적 천재의 영예를 다툴 수 있는 거란다.\n\n\"다른 백성들의 탁월함 앞에서 로마인의 자만심은 눈가리개야. 제 가슴받이만큼이나 뚫을 수 없는 눈가리개지. 오, 무자비한 약탈자들이여! 그들이 짓밟으면 땅은 도리깨로 두들기는 마룻바닥처럼 떨린단다. 다른 이들과 더불어 우리도 쓰러졌지. 아아, 아들아, 너에게 이런 말을 해야 하다니! 그들은 우리의 가장 높은 자리, 가장 거룩한 자리를 차지했고, 그 끝이 어떠할지는 아무도 말할 수 없어. 그러나 이것만은 안단다. 그들이 유대를 망치로 깨뜨린 편도처럼 부수고, 그 기름과 단맛인 예루살렘을 삼킬 수는 있을 거야. 그래도 이스라엘 사람들의 영광은 손 닿지 않는 머리 위 하늘에서 하나의 빛으로 남으리라. 그들의 역사는 곧 하느님의 역사이기 때문이지. 하느님은 그들의 손으로 쓰셨고, 그들의 혀로 말씀하셨으며, 그들이 행한 모든 선한 일, 아무리 작은 것에도 친히 깃드셨단다. 하느님은 그들과 함께 사셨어. 시나이에서는 율법을 주시는 분으로, 광야에서는 인도자로, 전쟁에서는 장수로, 통치에서는 왕으로 말이야. 그분은 견딜 수 없이 환하게 빛나는, 당신의 안식처인 장막의 휘장을 거듭 젖히시고, 사람이 사람에게 말하듯 그들에게 옳음을, 행복에 이르는 길을, 어떻게 살아야 하는지를 보여 주셨지. 또한 영원토록 맹세한 언약으로 당신 전능하심의 힘을 묶어, 그들에게 약속을 베푸셨단다. 아들아, 여호와께서 이처럼 두려운 벗으로 함께 사신 그 사람들이, 그분에게서 아무것도 얻지 못했을 수 있겠니? 그들의 삶과 행적에서 흔한 인간적 자질이 어느 정도라도 신성한 것과 섞이고 물들지 않았을 수 있겠니? 그들의 천재가, 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에도, 그 안에 하늘의 기운을 조금도 지니지 않았을 수 있겠니?\"\n\n한동안 방 안에 들리는 소리라고는 부채가 사각거리는 소리뿐이었다.\n\n\"예술을 조각과 회화에만 한정하는 뜻으로 본다면,\" 그녀가 다시 말했다. \"이스라엘에 예술가가 없었다는 말은 참이란다.\"\n\n이 고백은 아쉬운 마음으로 나온 것이었다. 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 그녀가 사두개인이었다는 점이다. 사두개인의 신앙은 바리새인과 달리, 그 기원을 따지지 않고 온갖 형태의 아름다움을 사랑하는 일을 허락했다.\n\n\"그래도 공정을 기하려는 사람이라면,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"'너를 위하여 새긴 우상을 만들지 말고, 어떤 형상도 만들지 말라'는 금령에 우리 손의 솜씨가 묶여 있었음을 잊지 않을 거야. 소페림이 그 금령을 본래의 뜻과 때를 넘어 사악하게 늘려 놓았지. 또한 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 다이달로스가 아티카에 나타나 그의 나무 조각상으로 조각을 송두리째 바꾸어 놓아 코린토스와 아이기나의 유파, 그리고 그 마지막 승리인 포이킬레와 카피톨리움을 가능케 하기 훨씬 전에, 다시 말하지만 다이달로스의 시대보다 훨씬 전에, 두 이스라엘인 브살렐과 오홀리압이 있었다는 거야. 첫 성막을 지은 장인들이었지. '온갖 일에 능숙하다'고 일컬어진 그들은 궤 위 속죄소의 그룹들을 만들었어. 끌로 깎은 것이 아니라 두드려 편 금으로 빚은 것이었고, 그것들은 인간의 형상이자 신의 형상을 한 조각상이었지. '그들이 날개를 높이 펴서…… 그 얼굴을 서로 마주 보게 하라.' 누가 그것들을 아름답지 않다고 하겠니? 또 그것들이 최초의 조각상이 아니라고 누가 하겠니?\"\n\n\"아, 이제야 그리스인이 우리를 앞지른 까닭을 알겠어요.\" 유다가 깊이 빠져들며 말했다. \"그리고 그 궤, 그것을 부순 바빌론인들에게 저주가 있기를!\"\n\n\"아니다, 유다, 믿음을 지니거라. 그것은 부서진 게 아니라 그저 잃어버린 것이야. 산속 어느 동굴에 너무도 안전하게 숨겨졌을 뿐이지. 언젠가, 힐렐도 샴마이도 그리 말한단다, 언젠가 주님의 좋으신 때에 그것이 발견되어 끌어내어질 거야. 그러면 이스라엘은 그 앞에서 옛날처럼 노래하며 춤추겠지. 그때 그룹들의 얼굴을 우러르는 자들은, 비록 상아로 빚은 미네르바의 얼굴을 본 적이 있더라도, 수천 년 내내 잠들어 있던 유대인의 천재를 사랑하여 기꺼이 그의 손에 입 맞추려 들 거야.\"\n\n어머니는 열정에 휩쓸려 어느덧 웅변가처럼 빠르고 격렬하게 말하고 있었다. 그러나 이제 마음을 가다듬고, 혹은 생각의 실마리를 다시 잡으려고, 잠시 쉬었다.\n\n\"어머니는 참 훌륭하세요.\" 유다가 고마운 마음을 담아 말했다. \"이 말은 아무리 해도 모자라요. 샴마이도 이보다 더 잘 말하지 못했을 테고, 힐렐도 그랬을 거예요. 저는 다시 이스라엘의 참된 아들이 되었어요.\"\n\n\"아첨꾼 같으니!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이것이 그저 힐렐의 말을 되풀이한 것뿐임을 너는 모르는구나. 어느 날 내 앞에서 로마에서 온 궤변가와 논쟁할 때 힐렐이 한 말을 옮긴 것뿐이란다.\"\n\n\"그래도 그 진심 어린 말씀은 어머니의 것이에요.\"\n\n곧 그녀의 진지함이 모두 되살아났다.\n\n\"내가 어디까지 했지? 아, 그래, 우리 히브리 조상들에게 최초의 조각상이 있었다고 주장하던 참이었지. 유다야, 조각가의 솜씨가 예술의 전부는 아니란다. 예술이 위대함의 전부가 아닌 것과 마찬가지지. 나는 늘 위대한 인물들이 무리를 지어, 훌륭한 일행을 이루어 세기를 따라 행진해 내려오는 모습을 떠올리곤 해. 민족별로 나뉘어, 여기는 인도인, 저기는 이집트인, 저 너머는 아시리아인. 그들 위로는 나팔 소리와 깃발의 아름다움이 어우러지고, 그들 좌우에는 태초부터 이어 온 헤아릴 수 없는 세대가 경건한 구경꾼으로 늘어서 있지. 그들이 나아갈 때, 나는 그리스인이 이렇게 말하는 모습을 떠올린단다. '보라! 헬라스인이 앞장서 간다.' 그러면 로마인이 대꾸하지. '조용하라! 너희 자리였던 곳이 이제 우리 것이다. 우리는 너희를 밟힌 먼지처럼 뒤에 남겨 두었다.' 그리고 그 내내, 저 멀리 앞쪽에서 행렬을 거슬러, 또한 저 머나먼 미래를 향해 앞으로, 한 줄기 빛이 흘러나온단다. 다투는 자들은 그것이 자기들을 영원히 이끌어 간다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르는 빛, 곧 계시의 빛이야! 그것을 받들어 가는 자들은 누구겠니? 아, 그 오랜 유대의 핏줄이지! 그 생각만으로도 어찌나 가슴이 뛰는지! 그 빛으로 우리는 그들을 알아본단다. 세 곱절로 복되어라, 오 우리 조상들이여, 하느님의 종이여, 언약을 지키는 자들이여! 그대들은 산 자와 죽은 자, 모든 사람의 지도자다. 맨 앞은 그대들의 것이니, 로마인이 저마다 카이사르일지라도, 그대들은 그 자리를 잃지 않으리라!\"\n\n유다는 깊이 마음이 흔들렸다.\n\n\"제발 멈추지 마세요.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"어머니 덕분에 소고 소리가 들리는 것 같아요. 저는 미리암과 그 뒤를 따라 춤추고 노래하던 여인들을 기다리고 있어요.\"\n\n그녀는 아들의 마음을 알아채고, 재치 있게 그것을 제 말 속에 엮어 넣었다.\n\n\"좋아, 아들아. 네가 그 여예언자의 소고 소리를 들을 수 있다면, 내가 막 청하려던 일도 할 수 있을 거야. 상상력을 펼쳐 나와 함께, 마치 길가에 선 듯 서 보렴. 행렬의 맨 앞에서 이스라엘의 선택받은 자들이 우리 곁을 지나가는 동안에 말이야. 자, 이제 그들이 온다. 족장들이 먼저고, 다음은 지파들의 조상들이지. 그들의 낙타 방울 소리와 가축 떼의 울음소리가 들리는 듯하구나. 무리들 사이를 홀로 걷는 저이는 누구일까? 늙은이지만, 그 눈은 흐려지지 않았고 타고난 기운도 쇠하지 않았어. 그는 주님을 얼굴과 얼굴을 맞대고 뵈었지! 전사이자 시인이자 웅변가요 입법자요 예언자인 그의 위대함은 아침의 해와 같아서, 그 쏟아지는 광채가 다른 모든 빛을, 카이사르 가운데 으뜸가고 가장 고귀한 자의 빛마저 꺼뜨려 버린단다. 그 뒤로는 사사들이지. 그다음은 왕들이야. 이새의 아들은 전쟁의 영웅이자 바다의 노래처럼 영원한 노래를 부르던 가인이었지. 그의 아들은 부와 지혜에서 다른 모든 왕을 능가했고, 사막을 사람 살 만한 곳으로 만들며 그 황무지에 도시들을 세우면서도, 주님께서 땅 위의 보좌로 택하신 예루살렘을 잊지 않았단다. 더 낮게 몸을 굽히렴, 아들아! 다음에 오는 이들은 그 부류의 처음이자 마지막인 자들이야. 그들의 얼굴은 들려 있으니, 마치 하늘에서 들려오는 음성을 듣고 귀 기울이는 듯하구나. 그들의 삶은 슬픔으로 가득했지. 그들의 옷에서는 무덤과 동굴의 냄새가 난단다. 그들 가운데 한 여인의 목소리를 들어 보렴. '주님을 찬송하라, 그분이 영광스럽게 승리하셨도다!' 아니, 그들 앞에서는 이마를 흙에 대거라! 그들은 하느님의 혀였고, 그분의 종이었으니, 하늘을 꿰뚫어 보고 모든 미래를 보아 그 본 것을 기록하였으며, 그 기록을 시간이 입증하도록 남겼단다. 왕들도 그들 앞에 다가가면 새파랗게 질렸고, 민족들도 그들의 목소리에 떨었지. 천지만물이 그들을 받들었어. 그들은 온갖 은혜와 온갖 재앙을 손에 쥐고 다녔단다. 저 디셉 사람과 그의 종 엘리사를 보렴! 힐기야의 슬픈 아들을 보고, 또 그발 강가에서 환상을 본 자를 보렴! 그리고 바빌론의 우상을 거부한 유대의 세 아이 가운데, 보라, 천 명의 귀족이 모인 잔치에서 점성가들을 그토록 당황케 한 그 한 사람을 보렴. 그리고 저기, 오 아들아, 다시 흙에 입 맞추어라! 저기 아모스의 온유한 아들이 온다. 세상이 그를 통해 장차 오실 메시아의 약속을 받았단다!\"\n\n이 대목에서 부채는 빠르게 움직였다. 이제 그것이 멈추고, 그녀의 목소리는 낮게 가라앉았다.\n\n\"피곤하구나.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"저는 이스라엘의 새 노래를 듣고 있었어요.\"\n\n어머니는 여전히 제 뜻에 골몰해 있어, 그 다정한 말을 흘려보냈다.\n\n\"내 힘닿는 데까지, 유다야, 나는 우리의 위대한 인물들을 네 앞에 펼쳐 보였단다. 족장들, 입법자들, 전사들, 가인들, 예언자들을 말이야. 이제 로마 최고의 인물들에게로 돌아가 보자. 모세 앞에 카이사르를, 다윗 앞에 타르퀴니우스를, 마카베오 형제 중 누구 앞에든 술라를, 사사들 앞에 가장 뛰어난 집정관들을, 솔로몬 앞에 아우구스투스를 세워 보면, 그것으로 끝이야. 견줌은 거기서 멎지. 그러나 그다음, 위대한 자들 중에서도 가장 위대한 예언자들을 떠올려 보렴.\"\n\n그녀는 비웃듯 웃었다.\n\n\"미안하구나. 카이우스 율리우스에게 3월의 이데스를 조심하라 경고한 점쟁이를 떠올렸던 거야. 그가 자기 주인은 거들떠보지도 않던 흉조를 닭의 내장에서 찾는 모습이 떠올랐지. 그 그림에서 눈을 돌려, 사마리아로 가는 길목 산꼭대기에 앉은 엘리야를 보렴. 장수들과 그 쉰 명씩의 무리가 연기를 내며 타 죽은 시신들 한가운데서, 아합의 아들에게 우리 하느님의 진노를 경고하던 모습을 말이야. 끝으로, 오 나의 유다야, 이런 말이 불경하지 않다면, 여호와와 유피테르를 그 종들이 그 이름으로 행한 일이 아니고서야 무엇으로 견주겠니? 그리고 네가 무엇을 해야 하느냐 하면……\"\n\n그녀는 마지막 말을 천천히, 떨리는 목소리로 내뱉었다.\n\n\"네가 무엇을 해야 하느냐 하면, 얘야, 주님을 섬기거라. 이스라엘의 주 하느님을 섬기되, 로마를 섬기지는 말거라. 아브라함의 자손에게 영광이란 오직 주님의 길에만 있고, 그 길에는 큰 영광이 있단다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는 군인이 되어도 되나요?\" 유다가 물었다.\n\n\"안 될 게 무어냐? 모세도 하느님을 전쟁의 용사라 부르지 않았더냐?\"\n\n그러고 나서 여름 방 안에는 긴 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"내가 허락하마.\" 마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"오직 카이사르 대신 주님을 섬긴다면 말이다.\"\n\n그는 그 조건에 만족했고, 이윽고 잠이 들었다. 그러자 그녀는 일어나 아들의 머리 밑에 방석을 받쳐 주고, 숄을 덮어 준 뒤, 다정하게 입 맞추고 자리를 떴다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n선한 사람도 악한 사람과 마찬가지로 죽음을 맞아야 한다. 그러나 우리 신앙의 가르침을 떠올리며, 우리는 그와 그의 죽음을 두고 이렇게 말한다. \"괜찮다. 그는 하늘나라에서 눈을 뜰 테니.\" 살아 있는 동안 이에 가장 가까운 것은, 건강한 잠에서 깨어나 행복한 광경과 소리를 이내 또렷이 의식하는 순간이다.\n\n유다가 깨어났을 때, 해는 산 위로 솟아 있었다. 비둘기들이 떼 지어 날며 흰 날개의 번득임으로 하늘을 가득 채웠고, 남동쪽으로는 성전이, 푸른 하늘 속에 황금빛 환영처럼 떠 있는 것이 보였다. 그러나 이는 모두 낯익은 것들이라 흘낏 눈길만 받았을 뿐이다. 그의 곁, 긴 의자 가장자리에는 열다섯도 채 안 되어 보이는 한 소녀가 무릎에 네벨을 받쳐 들고 우아하게 줄을 뜯으며 노래하고 있었다. 그는 귀를 기울이며 그쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그녀가 부르는 노래는 이러했다.\n노래.\n\n\"깨지 마, 다만 들어 줘, 그대여! 졸음의 바다에 떠밀려 가는 그대, 그대의 넋을 불러 내 말을 듣게 하라. 깨지 마, 다만 들어 줘, 그대여! 쉼을 주는 왕, 잠의 선물로, 온통 행복한, 행복한 꿈을 내가 가져다주노라.\n\n깨지 마, 다만 들어 줘, 그대여! 온 세상 꿈 가운데 가장 거룩한 것을 이번 한 번 그대가 고를 수 있으니. 그러니 고르고, 잠들라, 내 사랑! 허나 다시는 마음대로 고를 수 없으리, 그대가, 그대가 나를 꿈꾸지 않는 한.\"\n\n그녀는 악기를 내려놓고, 두 손을 무릎에 얹은 채 그가 입을 열기를 기다렸다. 이제 그녀에 관해 얼마간 이야기해야 할 차례가 되었으니, 우리는 이 기회를 빌려, 우리가 그 사사로운 영역까지 들어서게 된 이 가문에 대해 독자가 알고 싶어 할 만한 사실들을 덧붙여 두기로 한다.\n\n헤롯의 총애는 그가 죽은 뒤에도 막대한 재산을 지닌 사람들을 여럿 남겼다. 이 재산이 어느 지파, 특히 유다 지파의 이름난 후손이라는 의심할 바 없는 직계 혈통과 결합된 경우, 그 행운의 인물은 예루살렘의 왕자로 여겨졌다. 이 칭호 하나만으로도 그는 자기보다 덜 혜택받은 동포들의 존경을 받기에 충분했고, 사업과 사교의 형편상 거래하게 되는 이방인들에게서도, 그 이상은 아니더라도, 적어도 존중은 받았다. 이런 부류 가운데 우리가 따라온 소년의 아버지만큼 사적으로나 공적으로나 높은 존경을 얻은 이는 없었다. 한 번도 잊은 적 없는 제 민족에 대한 기억을 간직하면서도, 그는 왕에게 충실하여 안팎으로 그를 성실히 섬겼다. 몇몇 직무 때문에 그는 로마에 가게 되었고, 거기서 그의 처신은 아우구스투스의 눈길을 끌었다. 아우구스투스는 거리낌 없이 그의 우정을 얻으려 애썼다. 그리하여 그의 집에는 왕들의 허영을 흡족하게 했을 법한 선물이 많았다. 자줏빛 토가, 상아 의자, 황금 술잔 따위가 그러했는데, 그 값어치는 주로 그것들을 영예롭게 하사한 황제의 손에 있었다. 이런 사람이 부유하지 않을 리 없었다. 그러나 그의 재산이 전부 왕실 후견인의 하사품이었던 것은 아니다. 그는 어떤 생업에 종사하도록 의무 지우는 율법을 기꺼이 받아들였고, 하나가 아니라 여러 가지 일에 손을 댔다. 멀리 옛 레바논에 이르기까지 평원과 산비탈에서 가축을 치는 목자들 가운데 수많은 이가 그를 고용주로 삼아 보고를 올렸다. 바닷가 도시들과 내륙 도시들에 그는 상관(商館)을 세웠다. 그의 배들은 당시 알려진 가장 풍부한 광산이 있던 에스파냐에서 은을 실어 왔고, 그의 대상(隊商)들은 해마다 두 차례 동방에서 비단과 향료를 가득 싣고 왔다. 신앙으로 보면 그는 히브리인이었으니, 율법과 모든 본질적 의식을 지켰다. 회당과 성전에서 그의 자리는 그를 잘 알았고, 그는 성경에 두루 통달했으며, 율법 학자들과 어울리기를 즐겼고, 힐렐에 대한 존경은 거의 숭배에 가까웠다. 그러면서도 그는 결코 분리주의자가 아니었으니, 그의 환대는 온 나라에서 온 나그네들을 품었다. 트집 잡기 좋아하는 바리새인들은 그가 한두 번 이상 사마리아인을 제 식탁에 맞아들였다고 비난하기까지 했다. 만약 그가 이방인이었고 또 살아 있었다면, 세상은 그를 헤로데스 아티쿠스의 맞수로 알았을지도 모른다. 그러나 실제로는 우리 이야기의 이 두 번째 시기보다 십 년쯤 전, 한창나이에 바다에서 목숨을 잃었고, 유대 전역에서 애도를 받았다. 우리는 이미 그 가족 가운데 두 사람을 알고 있다. 그의 미망인과 아들이다. 나머지 단 한 사람은 딸이었으니, 바로 우리가 오빠에게 노래 불러 주는 모습을 본 그 소녀다.\n\n그녀의 이름은 티르자였다. 둘이 서로를 바라보니, 그 닮음이 뚜렷했다. 그녀의 이목구비도 오빠의 것처럼 반듯했고, 같은 유대인의 모습이었다. 또한 어린아이다운 순진한 표정의 매력도 지니고 있었다. 가정생활과 그 미덥고 다정한 사랑이 있었기에, 그녀는 그처럼 무심한 차림으로 나타날 수 있었다. 오른쪽 어깨에 단추를 채우고 가슴과 등을 헐겁게 지나 왼팔 아래로 흐르는 속옷은 허리 위쪽 몸매를 반쯤만 가렸고, 두 팔은 온전히 드러나 있었다. 띠 하나가 옷의 주름을 잡아 치마가 시작되는 자리를 표시했다. 머리 모양은 매우 단순하면서도 어울렸으니, 티레의 물감으로 물들인 비단 모자였고, 그 위에는 같은 천으로 된 줄무늬 스카프가 아름답게 수놓아져 있었다. 그것은 머리 모양을 더 크게 만들지 않으면서도 그대로 드러내도록 얇은 주름으로 둘러 감겨 있었으며, 모자 꼭대기에서 늘어진 술 하나로 마무리되어 있었다. 그녀는 귀에도 손가락에도 반지를 꼈고, 발찌와 팔찌는 모두 금이었다. 목에는 금 목걸이를 둘렀는데, 섬세한 사슬의 그물로 정교하게 꾸미고 거기에 진주 장식을 늘어뜨린 것이었다. 눈꺼풀 가장자리에는 화장을 했고, 손끝은 물들여 놓았다. 머리카락은 두 갈래로 길게 땋아 등 뒤로 늘어뜨렸다. 곱슬머리 한 가닥이 양쪽 뺨, 귀 앞쪽에 드리워져 있었다. 무엇으로 보나 그녀의 우아함과 기품과 아름다움은 부정할 수 없었다.\n\n\"아주 예쁘구나, 티르자, 정말 예뻐!\" 그가 활기차게 말했다.\n\n\"노래가요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 노래도 부르는 이도. 그리스인의 멋이 있구나. 어디서 배웠니?\"\n\n\"지난달 극장에서 노래한 그리스인 기억하세요? 사람들 말로는 그가 헤롯과 그 누이 살로메의 궁정에서 가수였다더군요. 씨름 시합이 막 끝나서 객석이 떠들썩할 때 그가 나왔어요. 그가 첫 음을 내자 모든 것이 어찌나 조용해졌던지, 저는 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었어요. 그에게서 이 노래를 배웠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그는 그리스어로 불렀잖아.\" \"그리고 저는 히브리어로 부르고요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지. 내 어린 누이가 자랑스럽구나. 이만큼 좋은 노래가 또 있느냐?\"\n\n\"아주 많아요. 하지만 지금은 그만두기로 해요. 암라가 오빠한테 아침을 가져다줄 테니 내려올 것 없다고 전하래요. 지금쯤이면 올 텐데. 암라는 오빠가 아프다고 생각해요. 어제 끔찍한 사고를 당했다고요. 무슨 일이었어요? 말해 줘요, 그러면 제가 암라를 도와 오빠를 치료할게요. 암라는 이집트인들의 처방을 알아요. 그 사람들은 늘 멍청한 무리였지만요. 그리고 저는 아랍인들의 처방도 많이 알아요. 그 사람들은—\"\n\n\"이집트인들보다 한층 더 멍청하지.\" 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요? 그럼 좋아요.\" 그녀가 거의 쉴 틈도 없이 대답하며 두 손을 왼쪽 귀에 가져갔다. \"그 어느 쪽도 거들떠보지 않기로 해요. 저한테 훨씬 더 확실하고 좋은 게 있어요. 우리 백성 가운데 누군가가 페르시아 마법사한테서 받은 부적이에요. 언제 받았는지는 모르겠어요, 너무 오래전 일이라. 보세요, 새겨진 글자가 거의 닳아 버렸어요.\"\n\n그녀가 귀고리를 그에게 내밀자, 그는 그것을 받아 들여다보고는 웃으며 돌려주었다.\n\n\"내가 죽어 가는 처지라 해도, 티르자, 나는 그 부적을 쓰지 않을 거다. 그건 우상숭배의 유물이라, 아브라함의 모든 믿는 자손에게 금지된 것이다. 받아 두되, 다시는 차지 말거라.\"\n\n\"금지라뇨! 그렇지 않아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"우리 할머니께서 평생 셀 수 없이 많은 안식일에 이걸 차셨어요. 이것이 몇 사람을 낫게 했는지도 모를 정도예요. 어쨌든 셋은 넘어요. 인정받은 거예요. 보세요, 여기 랍비들의 표시가 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"나는 부적 따위 믿지 않는다.\"\n\n그녀는 놀라서 그에게 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"암라가 뭐라고 하겠어요?\"\n\n\"암라의 아버지와 어머니는 나일강가 어느 정원에서 물 긷는 두레박을 돌보았던 사람들이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 가말리엘은요!\"\n\n\"그분 말씀으로는 그건 불신자들과 세겜 사람들이 만들어 낸 불경한 발명품이라더구나.\"\n\n티르자는 미심쩍은 눈으로 반지를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이걸 어쩌면 좋아요?\"\n\n\"차거라, 내 어린 누이야. 너한테 잘 어울린다. 그게 너를 더 아름답게 해 주지. 하기야 너는 그런 것 없이도 아름답다만.\"\n\n마음이 놓인 그녀는 암라가 대야와 물과 수건을 얹은 쟁반을 들고 여름 방으로 막 들어설 때, 부적을 다시 제 귀에 달았다.\n\n유다는 바리새인이 아닌지라, 손 씻는 의식은 짧고 단순했다. 그러고 나서 하인이 나가자, 티르자가 남아 그의 머리를 매만졌다. 머리 한 가닥을 마음에 들게 손질하고 나면, 그녀는 그 고운 동포 여인들의 유행대로 허리띠에 차고 다니던 작은 쇠거울을 풀어 그에게 건넸다. 그가 그 솜씨를 보고, 그것이 자기를 얼마나 멋지게 만들어 주는지 알게 하려는 것이었다. 그러는 동안 둘은 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"네 생각은 어떠냐, 티르자? 나는 떠날 거다.\"\n\n그녀는 놀라서 두 손을 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"떠난다고요! 언제요? 어디로요? 무엇 때문에요?\"\n\n그가 웃었다.\n\n\"한 숨에 질문이 셋이로구나! 너란 애는 어쩜 이러니!\" 다음 순간 그는 진지해졌다. \"율법이 나더러 어떤 생업에 종사하라고 요구하는 거 너도 알지. 우리 어지신 아버지께서 본을 보여 주셨고. 내가 아버지의 근면과 지혜가 거둔 결실을 게으름으로 흘려보낸다면, 너조차도 나를 경멸할 거다. 나는 로마로 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"아, 저도 함께 갈래요.\"\n\n\"너는 어머니 곁에 있어야지. 우리 둘 다 떠나면 어머니는 돌아가실 거다.\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴에서 밝은 빛이 사라졌다.\n\n\"아, 그래요, 그래요! 하지만 꼭 가셔야 해요? 여기 예루살렘에서도 상인이 되는 데 필요한 건 다 배울 수 있잖아요. 오빠가 생각하는 게 그거라면요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 생각하는 건 그게 아니다. 율법이 아들더러 아버지와 같은 사람이 되라고 요구하는 건 아니거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 무엇이 될 수 있는데요?\"\n\n\"군인.\" 그가 어딘가 자랑스러운 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고였다.\n\n\"오빠는 죽고 말 거예요.\"\n\n\"하느님의 뜻이라면, 그렇게 되겠지. 하지만 티르자, 군인이 다 죽는 건 아니다.\"\n\n그녀는 그를 붙잡아 두려는 듯 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았다.\n\n\"우리는 이토록 행복한데! 집에 있어요, 오빠.\"\n\n\"집이 언제까지나 지금 같을 수는 없어. 너 자신도 머지않아 떠나게 될 거다.\"\n\n\"절대로요!\"\n\n그는 그녀의 간절함에 미소 지었다.\n\n\"유다 지파의 왕자나, 아니면 다른 지파의 누군가가 곧 와서 내 티르자를 제 짝이라 청하고는, 너를 태워 떠나 또 다른 집의 빛으로 삼겠지. 그럼 나는 어떻게 되겠니?\"\n\n그녀는 흐느낌으로 대답했다.\n\n\"전쟁은 하나의 직업이다.\" 그가 좀 더 차분하게 말을 이었다. \"그것을 제대로 배우려면 학교에 가야 하는데, 로마 군영만 한 학교가 없거든.\"\n\n\"오빠가 로마를 위해 싸우진 않을 거죠?\" 그녀가 숨을 죽이며 물었다.\n\n\"너도, 너조차도 로마를 미워하지. 온 세상이 로마를 미워해. 바로 거기에, 오 티르자, 내가 너한테 주는 대답의 까닭이 있단다. 그래, 나는 로마를 위해 싸울 거다. 만약 그 대가로 로마가 나에게 언젠가 로마와 맞서 싸우는 법을 가르쳐 준다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"언제 떠나실 거예요?\"\n\n그때 돌아오는 암라의 발소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 무슨 생각을 하는지 암라가 알게 하지 마라.\"\n\n충직한 하녀가 아침 식사를 들고 들어와, 그것을 받친 쟁반을 그들 앞 걸상 위에 놓았다. 그러고는 흰 수건을 팔에 걸친 채 시중들려고 남았다. 그들이 물그릇에 손가락을 담가 헹구고 있을 때, 어떤 소리가 그들의 주의를 끌었다. 귀를 기울이니, 집 북쪽 거리에서 군악 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"프라이토리움에서 온 병사들이다! 봐야겠어.\" 그가 외치며 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 밖으로 뛰어나갔다.\n\n다음 순간 그는 지붕의 북동쪽 맨 끝 모서리를 두르고 있는 기와 난간에 몸을 기대고 있었으니, 어찌나 정신이 팔렸던지 티르자가 곁에 와 한 손을 그의 어깨에 얹은 것도 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n그들이 선 자리, 곧 그 일대에서 가장 높은 지붕에서는 동쪽으로 집들의 지붕이 한눈에 내려다보였고, 멀리 안토니아 탑의 거대하고 들쭉날쭉한 모습까지 보였다. 그 탑은 앞서 말했듯이 수비대의 성채이자 총독의 군 사령부였다. 너비가 채 3미터도 안 되는 그 거리에는 군데군데 다리가 걸쳐 있었는데, 트인 것도 있고 지붕 덮인 것도 있었다. 길가의 지붕들과 마찬가지로 그 다리들도 음악 소리에 불려 나온 남자와 여자와 아이들로 차기 시작했다. 음악이라는 말을 썼지만, 그리 어울리는 말은 아니다. 사람들이 나왔을 때 들은 것은 차라리 나팔의 요란한 소리와, 병사들이 그토록 좋아하는 더 날카로운 리투우스 소리의 소란이었다.\n\n이윽고 행렬이 후르 집안의 두 사람 눈에 들어왔다. 맨 앞은 경무장한 전위대로, 대부분 투석병과 궁수였는데, 열과 오 사이를 넓게 벌리고 행진했다. 다음은 중무장한 보병 부대로, 큰 방패와 하스타 롱가, 곧 일리움 앞 결투에서 쓰이던 것과 똑같은 창을 들고 있었다. 그다음은 악사들, 그다음은 홀로 말을 탄 장교 하나였는데, 기병대 호위가 바짝 뒤따랐다. 그 뒤에는 다시 역시 중무장한 보병의 종대가 이어졌는데, 빽빽한 대열로 움직이며 벽에서 벽까지 거리를 가득 메웠고, 끝이 없어 보였다.\n\n병사들의 우람한 팔다리, 좌우로 박자 맞춰 움직이는 방패, 완벽하게 닦인 비늘 갑옷과 죔쇠와 흉갑과 투구의 번쩍임, 높은 투구 장식 위로 까딱이는 깃털, 흔들리는 군기와 쇠 박은 창, 정확히 박자 맞춘 대담하고 자신만만한 걸음걸이, 그토록 엄숙하면서도 그토록 빈틈없는 태도, 움직이는 무리 전체의 기계 같은 일사불란함, 이 모든 것이 유다에게 인상을 남겼으나, 그것은 본 것이라기보다 느낀 것에 가까웠다. 두 가지가 그의 눈길을 붙들었다. 첫째는 군단의 독수리였다. 그것은 긴 장대 위에 앉은 도금된 형상으로, 두 날개를 펴서 머리 위에서 서로 맞닿게 한 것이었다. 그는 그것이 탑 안의 보관실에서 나올 때면 신에게나 바칠 예우를 받았다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n종대 한가운데서 홀로 말을 탄 장교가 또 하나의 볼거리였다. 그는 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않았으나, 그 밖에는 완전 무장을 하고 있었다. 왼쪽 허리에는 짧은 검을 찼는데, 손에는 흰 종이 두루마리처럼 보이는 지휘봉을 들고 있었다. 그는 안장 대신 자줏빛 천 위에 앉아 있었고, 그것과, 앞머리를 금으로 장식하고 아래 가장자리에 넓게 술을 단 노란 비단 고삐를 단 굴레가 그 말의 마구를 이루고 있었다.\n\n그 사람이 아직 멀리 있을 때부터, 유다는 그의 등장만으로도 그를 바라보던 사람들이 성난 흥분에 휩싸이는 것을 알아차렸다. 그들은 난간 너머로 몸을 내밀거나 대담하게 앞으로 나서서 그를 향해 주먹을 흔들었고, 큰 소리로 외치며 그를 뒤쫓았으며, 그가 다리 밑을 지날 때면 침을 뱉었다. 여자들은 신발까지 내던졌는데, 더러는 제대로 명중시킬 만큼 솜씨가 좋았다. 그가 더 가까이 다가오자, 외침이 알아들을 만해졌다. \"강도, 폭군, 로마의 개! 이스마엘은 물러가라! 우리 한나스를 돌려다오!\"\n\n아주 가까워지자, 유다는 당연한 일이지만, 그 사람이 병사들처럼 그토록 당당하게 드러내는 무관심을 함께하지 못함을 볼 수 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 어둡고 시무룩했고, 이따금 박해자들에게 던지는 눈길은 위협으로 가득 차 있어, 겁 많은 자들은 그 앞에서 움츠러들었다.\n\n그런데 소년은 첫 카이사르의 습관에서 비롯된 한 관례를 들어 알고 있었다. 그 관례에 따르면 총사령관들은 제 지위를 나타내려고 머리에 월계관만 쓰고 공식 석상에 나타났다. 그 표시로 그는 이 장교가 누구인지 알아보았다. 발레리우스 그라투스, 유대의 새 총독이었다!\n\n이제 솔직히 말하자면, 까닭 없이 쏟아지는 그 폭풍 아래 놓인 그 로마인은 젊은 유대인의 동정을 샀다. 그래서 그가 집 모서리에 이르렀을 때, 유다는 그가 지나가는 모습을 보려고 난간 너머로 한층 더 몸을 내밀었고, 그러는 김에 오래전부터 금이 가 있었으나 눈여겨보지 않고 내버려 둔 기와 한 장에 손을 짚었다. 그 누름이 충분히 강했던지 바깥쪽 조각이 밀려나며 떨어지기 시작했다. 전율과 같은 공포가 소년을 꿰뚫었다. 그는 떨어지는 그것을 붙잡으려고 손을 뻗었다. 그 동작이 겉보기에는 영락없이 무언가를 자기에게서 내던지는 모습이었다. 애는 허사로 돌아갔고, 아니, 오히려 떨어지는 조각을 벽 너머로 더 멀리 밀어내고 말았다. 그는 있는 힘을 다해 소리쳤다. 호위 병사들이 위를 올려다보았고, 그 높은 사람도 그러했는데, 바로 그 순간 그 조각이 그를 때렸고, 그는 죽은 듯 자리에서 떨어졌다.\n\n대대가 멈춰 섰다. 호위병들이 말에서 뛰어내려 방패로 사령관을 덮으려고 서둘렀다. 한편, 그 일을 목격한 사람들은 그 일격이 고의로 가해진 것이라 조금도 의심하지 않고, 난간 너머로 몸을 굽힌 채 자기가 본 광경에, 그리고 너무도 또렷이 떠오르는 결과의 예감에 못 박혀 있는 소년에게 환호를 보냈다.\n\n행진 노선을 따라 지붕에서 지붕으로 믿기 어려운 속도로 짓궂은 기운이 날아가, 사람들을 사로잡고 너 나 할 것 없이 부추겼다. 그들은 난간에 손을 대어 기와와, 지붕을 대부분 이루고 있던 햇볕에 구운 진흙을 뜯어내, 눈먼 광기로 아래 멈춰 선 군단병들 위로 내던지기 시작했다. 그리하여 싸움이 벌어졌다. 물론 규율이 이겼다. 그 다툼, 그 살육, 한편의 솜씨, 다른 편의 필사적임은 모두 우리 이야기에 필요치 않다. 차라리 이 모든 일을 빚어낸 그 가련한 장본인에게 눈길을 돌리기로 하자.\n\n그는 난간에서 몸을 일으켰으니, 얼굴이 몹시 창백했다.\n\n\"오 티르자, 티르자! 우리는 어떻게 되는 거지?\"\n\n그녀는 아래에서 벌어진 일을 보지 못한 채, 외치는 소리에 귀를 기울이고 눈에 보이는 지붕 위 사람들의 미친 듯한 움직임을 지켜보고 있었다. 무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지고 있다는 것은 알았으나, 그것이 무엇인지, 그 까닭이 무엇인지, 혹은 자기나 자기에게 소중한 누군가가 위험에 처했다는 것은 알지 못했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요? 이게 다 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 그녀가 갑작스러운 놀라움에 물었다.\n\n\"내가 로마 총독을 죽이고 말았다. 기와가 그를 덮쳤어.\"\n\n보이지 않는 손이 그녀의 얼굴에 잿가루를 뿌린 듯, 얼굴이 그토록 순식간에 하얗게 질렸다. 그녀는 한 팔로 그를 끌어안고, 말없이 그저 애절하게 그의 눈을 들여다보았다. 그의 두려움이 그녀에게 옮아갔고, 그 두려움을 보자 그는 도리어 힘을 얻었다.\n\n\"일부러 그런 게 아니다, 티르자. 사고였어.\" 그가 좀 더 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"저들이 어떻게 할까요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n그는 거리와 지붕 위로 점점 거세지는 소란을 잠깐 내다보며, 그라투스의 시무룩하던 얼굴을 떠올렸다. 그가 죽지 않았다면, 그 복수가 어디서 멈출 것인가? 그리고 그가 죽었다면, 사람들의 폭력이 군단병들을 어느 정도의 광란으로 몰아갈 것인가? 대답을 피하려고, 그는 다시 난간 너머를 엿보았으니, 마침 호위병들이 그 로마인을 부축해 말에 다시 태우고 있었다.\n\n\"살아 있다, 살아 있어, 티르자! 우리 조상의 주 하느님께 찬미를!\"\n\n그렇게 외치며, 그는 환해진 얼굴로 몸을 물려 그녀의 질문에 답했다.\n\n\"두려워 마라, 티르자. 어쩌다 그리 됐는지 내가 설명하면, 저들도 우리 아버지와 그 공로를 기억하고 우리를 해치지 않을 거다.\"\n\n그가 그녀를 여름 별채로 데려가려 할 때, 발밑에서 지붕이 흔들렸다. 굵은 들보들이 부서져 나가는 요란한 소리에 이어, 놀라움과 고통이 뒤섞인 비명이 분명 아래 안뜰 쪽에서 솟아올랐다. 그는 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다. 비명이 거듭되었다. 그러더니 많은 발소리가 한꺼번에 몰려들고, 분노로 높아진 목소리가 기도하는 목소리와 뒤섞였으며, 이어서 죽음의 공포에 사로잡힌 여인들의 비명이 들렸다. 병사들이 북쪽 문을 부수고 들이쳐 집을 점령한 것이었다. 사냥당하고 있다는 무서운 느낌이 그를 후려쳤다. 그의 첫 충동은 달아나는 것이었으나, 어디로? 날개가 아니고서는 소용없을 일이었다. 티르자가 두려움으로 눈이 휘둥그레져서 그의 팔을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"오 유다, 이게 무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n하인들이 살육당하고 있었다. 그리고 어머니가! 그가 들은 목소리 가운데 하나가 어머니의 것이 아니던가? 남은 의지를 다 짜내어 그가 말했다. \"여기 있어라, 그리고 나를 기다려, 티르자. 내가 내려가서 무슨 일인지 보고 너에게 돌아오마.\"\n\n그의 목소리는 그가 바라는 만큼 굳건하지 못했다. 그녀는 그에게 더욱 바싹 매달렸다.\n\n더 또렷하고, 더 날카롭게, 이제는 착각이 아닌, 어머니의 외침이 솟아올랐다. 그는 더는 망설이지 않았다.\n\n\"그럼 가자, 함께 가자.\"\n\n층계 아래 테라스, 곧 회랑은 병사들로 붐볐다. 다른 병사들은 칼을 빼 들고 방마다 들락거렸다. 한쪽에서는 여러 여인이 무릎을 꿇고 서로 부둥켜안거나 자비를 빌고 있었다. 그들과 떨어진 곳에서, 옷이 찢기고 긴 머리카락이 얼굴을 덮어 흘러내린 한 여인이 한 남자에게서 벗어나려고 몸부림치고 있었는데, 그 남자는 붙든 손을 놓지 않으려고 온 힘을 쏟고 있었다. 그녀의 외침은 그 가운데서도 가장 날카로워, 그 소란을 뚫고 지붕에까지 또렷이 솟아올랐던 것이다. 그녀를 향해 유다가 달려갔으니, 그의 걸음은 길고 빨라, 거의 날아가는 듯했다. \"어머니, 어머니!\" 그가 외쳤다. 그녀가 두 손을 그에게 뻗었으나, 그가 거의 그 손에 닿으려는 순간 그는 붙들려 한쪽으로 떼밀렸다. 그때 누군가 큰 소리로 말하는 것이 들렸다.\n\n\"저자가 바로 그놈이다!\"\n\n유다가 바라보니, 메살라였다.\n\n\"뭐라고, 저 암살자가, 저게?\" 아름답게 마무리된 군단병 갑옷을 입은 키 큰 사람이 말했다. \"원, 한낱 소년에 지나지 않는데.\"\n\n\"신들이시여!\" 메살라가 그 느릿한 말투를 잊지 않고 대꾸했다. \"참 새로운 철학이로군! 사람이 죽일 만큼 미워하려면 먼저 늙어야 한다는 주장에 세네카라면 뭐라고 하겠나? 자네는 저놈을 잡았고, 저것이 그 어미요, 저쪽이 누이일세. 자네는 온 가족을 다 잡은 거야.\"\n\n그들을 사랑하는 마음에, 유다는 제 원한을 잊었다.\n\n\"저들을 도와다오, 오 내 메살라여! 우리 어린 시절을 기억하고 저들을 도와다오. 나, 유다가, 너에게 비노라.\"\n\n메살라는 못 들은 체했다.\n\n\"내가 자네한테 더 도움이 될 일은 없겠군.\" 그가 장교에게 말했다. \"거리에 더 푸짐한 구경거리가 있으니. 에로스는 내려가고, 마르스는 올라오라!\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 그는 사라졌다. 유다는 그의 말뜻을 알아들었고, 영혼의 쓰라림 속에서 하늘에 기도했다.\n\n\"당신의 복수의 때에, 오 주여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것을 그자에게 내릴 손이 제 손이 되게 하소서!\"\n\n그는 안간힘을 써서 장교에게 더 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n\"오 나리, 당신이 듣고 있는 저 여인은 제 어머니입니다. 어머니를 살려 주십시오, 저쪽 제 누이를 살려 주십시오. 하느님은 공정하시니, 자비에는 자비로 갚아 주실 것입니다.\"\n\n그 사람은 마음이 움직인 듯했다.\n\n\"여자들은 탑으로 데려가라!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"다만 해치지는 마라. 내가 너희에게 그들을 따로 요구할 테니.\" 그러고 나서 유다를 붙들고 있는 자들에게 말했다. \"밧줄을 가져와 그 손을 묶고, 거리로 끌고 가라. 이놈의 형벌은 따로 정해 두마.\"\n\n어머니는 끌려갔다. 어린 티르자는 집에서 입던 차림 그대로, 두려움에 얼이 빠져 붙잡은 자들에게 순순히 따라갔다. 유다는 두 사람을 마지막으로 한 번씩 바라보고는, 그 광경을 영영 지워지지 않게 제 안에 담아 두려는 듯, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그는 어쩌면 눈물을 흘렸을지도 모른다, 비록 본 사람은 아무도 없었지만.\n\n그때 그의 안에서, 마땅히 삶의 경이라 부를 만한 일이 일어났다. 이 글을 사려 깊게 읽어 온 독자라면 이미, 그 젊은 유대인의 성품이 여자다울 만큼 온순했다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 보았을 것이다. 그것은 사랑하고 사랑받는 습관에서 좀처럼 어긋나지 않는 결과다. 그가 거쳐 온 형편은, 설령 그에게 더 거친 요소가 있었다 한들, 그것을 불러내지 않았다. 때때로 그는 야망의 들썩임과 충동을 느꼈으나, 그것은 바닷가를 거닐며 위엄 있는 배들이 오가는 모습을 바라보는 아이의 형체 없는 꿈과 같았다. 그러나 이제, 익숙히 받던 숭배를 의식하던 한 우상이 별안간 제단에서 내동댕이쳐져, 무너져 버린 제 작은 사랑의 세계 한가운데 나뒹구는 모습을 상상할 수 있다면, 젊은 벤허에게 닥친 일과 그것이 그의 존재에 미친 영향을 짐작할 수 있을 것이다. 그러나 아무 기색도, 그가 어떤 변화를 겪었음을 드러내는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 다만 그가 고개를 들어 묶이도록 두 팔을 내밀었을 때, 그의 입술에서 큐피드의 활처럼 휘었던 선이 사라졌을 뿐이다. 그 순간 그는 어린 시절을 벗어 버리고 한 사내가 되었다.\n\n안뜰에서 나팔 소리가 울렸다. 그 신호가 그치자, 회랑에서 병사들이 물러갔다. 그들 가운데 많은 이가 약탈물을 손에 든 채로는 감히 대열에 나설 수 없었던지라, 손에 쥔 것을 바닥에 내던졌고, 마침내 바닥은 더없이 값진 골동품으로 어지러이 흩어졌다. 유다가 내려왔을 때, 대형은 이미 갖추어져 있었고, 장교는 자기의 마지막 명령이 이행되는 것을 지켜보려 기다리고 있었다.\n\n어머니와 딸과 온 집안 식구가 북쪽 문 밖으로 끌려 나갔는데, 그 부서진 잔해가 통로를 가로막고 있었다. 하인들의 울부짖음은, 그 가운데 더러는 이 집에서 태어난 이들이라, 더없이 가여웠다. 마침내 말들과 이 집의 말 못 하는 짐승들까지 모조리 그의 앞으로 몰려 지나가자, 유다는 총독의 복수가 어디까지 미치는지 헤아리기 시작했다. 건물 자체가 멸망에 바쳐진 것이었다. 명령을 이행할 수 있는 한, 그 담장 안에 살아 있는 것은 아무것도 남기지 않으려는 것이었다. 만약 유대에 로마 총독을 암살할 생각을 품을 만큼 무모한 자가 또 있다면, 후르의 명문가에 닥친 일의 이야기가 그들에게 경고가 될 터였고, 그 집의 폐허는 그 이야기를 두고두고 살아 있게 할 터였다.\n\n한 무리의 병사가 임시로 문을 다시 세우는 동안, 장교는 밖에서 기다렸다.\n\n거리에서는 싸움이 거의 그쳐 있었다. 집들 위 여기저기 솟은 먼지구름이 아직 다툼이 이어지는 곳을 알려 주었다. 대대는 대부분 쉬어 자세로 서 있었는데, 그 위용도 그 대열도 조금도 줄지 않았다. 제 한 몸을 걱정하는 단계는 이미 지나친 터라, 유다는 눈앞의 그 무엇에도 마음이 가지 않고 오직 붙잡힌 사람들에게만 마음이 쏠렸으나, 그 가운데서 어머니와 티르자를 찾으려 해도 헛일이었다.\n\n별안간, 누워 있던 땅에서 한 여인이 일어나 빠르게 문 쪽으로 되달려 갔다. 호위병 몇이 그녀를 붙잡으려 손을 뻗었으나, 그것이 헛수고로 돌아가자 커다란 함성이 뒤따랐다. 그녀는 유다에게 달려와 주저앉으며 그의 무릎을 끌어안았으니, 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 거친 검은 머리카락이 그녀의 눈을 가렸다.\n\n\"오 암라, 어진 암라.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"하느님께서 너를 도우시기를. 나는 그럴 수 없구나.\"\n\n그녀는 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n그가 몸을 굽혀 속삭였다. \"살아라, 암라, 티르자와 어머니를 위해. 두 사람은 돌아올 거다, 그리고—\"\n\n한 병사가 그녀를 끌어냈다. 그러자 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 문과 통로를 지나 텅 빈 안뜰 안으로 달려 들어갔다.\n\n\"내버려 둬라.\" 장교가 외쳤다. \"집을 봉인할 테니, 저 여자는 굶어 죽을 거다.\"\n\n병사들은 다시 일을 시작했고, 그곳 일이 끝나자 서쪽으로 돌아갔다. 그 문 또한 단단히 봉했으니, 그로써 후르의 저택은 더는 쓸 수 없게 되었다.\n\n대대는 마침내 탑으로 돌아갔고, 총독은 그곳에 머물며 상처를 추스르고 포로들을 처분했다. 그로부터 열흘째 되는 날, 그는 시장에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n이튿날 군단병 한 무리가 황폐해진 저택으로 가서 대문을 영구히 봉쇄하고, 모서리마다 밀랍을 발랐으며, 그 곁에 라틴어로 쓴 고시문을 박아 두었다.\n\n“이것은 황제의 소유물이다.”\n오만한 로마인의 사고로는, 이 간결한 선언만으로도 충분하다고 여겨졌다. 과연 그러했다.\n\n그 다음 날, 정오 무렵에, 부관 한 사람이 열 명의 기병을 거느리고 남쪽에서, 곧 예루살렘 방향에서 나사렛으로 다가왔다. 그곳은 그 무렵 산비탈에 걸터앉은 띄엄띄엄한 마을로, 어찌나 보잘것없는지 단 하나뿐인 거리도 양 떼와 소 떼가 오가며 다져 놓은 오솔길이나 다름없었다. 거대한 에스드라엘론 평원이 남쪽으로 바짝 다가와 있었고, 서쪽 높은 곳에서는 지중해 연안과 요르단 너머의 땅, 그리고 헤르몬산을 바라볼 수 있었다. 아래쪽 골짜기와 사방의 들녘은 텃밭과 포도밭, 과수원과 목초지로 채워져 있었다. 종려나무 숲이 풍경에 동방의 정취를 더했다. 들쭉날쭉 모여 선 집들은 비천한 부류에 속하는 것들로, 네모반듯하고 단층이며 지붕이 평평했고, 산뜻한 초록빛 덩굴로 덮여 있었다. 유대의 언덕을 바싹 메마르게 태워 갈색의 죽은 빛으로 만든 가뭄도 갈릴리의 경계선에서 멈추었다.\n\n행렬이 마을에 가까워졌을 때 울린 나팔 소리는 주민들에게 마법 같은 효력을 발휘했다. 대문과 현관에서는 이토록 보기 드문 방문의 뜻을 누구보다 먼저 알아채려고 안달하는 무리가 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n기억해 두어야 할 것은, 나사렛이 어떤 큰길에서도 비켜나 있었을 뿐 아니라, 가말라의 유다가 세력을 떨치는 영역 안에 있었다는 사실이다. 그러니 군단병이 어떤 심정으로 맞아들여졌을지는 어렵지 않게 짐작할 수 있다. 그러나 그들이 거리를 따라 올라가는 동안 그들에게 맡겨진 임무가 드러나자, 두려움과 미움은 호기심 속에 묻혀 버렸다. 그 충동에 이끌린 사람들은, 마을 동북쪽 우물가에서 멈추어 서리라는 것을 알고는, 대문과 현관을 버리고 행렬 뒤로 모여들었다.\n\n호기심의 대상은 기병들이 호송하는 죄수였다. 그는 맨발에 맨머리였고, 반쯤 헐벗은 채 두 손이 등 뒤로 묶여 있었다. 손목에 매인 가죽끈은 말의 목에 고리처럼 걸려 있었다. 행렬이 움직일 때면 먼지가 일어 그를 누런 안개로, 때로는 짙은 구름으로 감쌌다. 그는 발이 부르트고 기진하여 앞으로 축 늘어졌다. 마을 사람들은 그가 젊다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n우물가에서 부관은 걸음을 멈추고, 대부분의 부하와 더불어 말에서 내렸다. 죄수는 길바닥의 먼지 속에 주저앉았는데, 넋이 나간 채 아무것도 청하지 않았다. 보아하니 탈진의 마지막 단계에 이른 모양이었다. 가까이 다가가 그가 한낱 소년에 지나지 않음을 알아본 마을 사람들은, 용기만 있었다면 그를 거들었을 것이다.\n\n그들이 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 가운데, 그리고 물 단지가 병사들 사이를 도는 동안, 한 사내가 세포리스 쪽 길을 따라 내려오는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 그를 보자 한 여인이 외쳤다. “저기 봐요! 목수가 오고 있어요. 이제 무슨 얘기를 들을 수 있겠네요.”\n\n그 사람은 보기에 자못 위엄이 있었다. 풍성한 터번 가장자리 아래로 가느다란 흰 머리카락이 흘러내렸고, 그보다 더 새하얀 수염이 거친 잿빛 옷자락 앞으로 폭넓게 늘어져 있었다. 그는 천천히 걸어왔는데, 나이 탓도 있었지만, 도끼와 톱과 끌 따위의 연장을, 그것도 하나같이 투박하고 묵직한 것들을 지고 있었기 때문이며, 또한 쉬지도 않고 먼 길을 온 기색이 역력했다.\n\n그는 가까운 데서 걸음을 멈추고 모여 선 사람들을 살펴보았다.\n\n“오, 랍비님, 선량하신 요셉 랍비님!” 한 여인이 그에게 달려가며 외쳤다. “여기 죄수가 있어요. 와서 병사들에게 좀 물어봐 주세요. 저 사람이 누구인지, 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지, 그리고 저 사람을 어떻게 할 셈인지 우리도 알 수 있게요.”\n\n랍비의 얼굴은 무덤덤한 채였다. 그래도 그는 죄수를 한번 흘끗 보고는, 이내 그 장교에게로 갔다.\n\n“주님의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를!” 그가 굽힘 없는 위엄으로 말했다.\n\n“그리고 신들의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를.” 부관이 대답했다.\n\n“예루살렘에서 오시는 길이오?”\n\n“그렇소.”\n\n“당신네 죄수가 젊군요.”\n\n“나이로는, 그렇소.”\n\n“저 사람이 무슨 짓을 했는지 물어도 되겠소?”\n\n“암살을 꾀한 자요.”\n\n사람들은 놀라움에 그 말을 되뇌었으나, 요셉 랍비는 캐묻기를 이어 갔다.\n\n“이스라엘의 자손이오?”\n\n“유대인이오.” 로마인이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다.\n\n흔들리던 구경꾼들의 연민이 되살아났다.\n\n“나는 당신네 지파에 대해서는 아는 바가 없으나, 저자의 집안은 말할 수 있소.” 그 사람이 말을 이었다. “예루살렘의 후르라는 한 귀인에 대해 들어 보았을지 모르겠소. 사람들은 그를 벤허라고 불렀소. 그는 헤롯 시대에 살았소.”\n\n“그를 본 적이 있소.” 요셉이 말했다.\n\n“음, 이자가 바로 그의 아들이오.”\n\n여기저기서 탄성이 터져 나오자, 부관은 서둘러 그것을 잠재웠다.\n\n“그저께, 예루살렘 거리에서, 이자는 어느 저택의 지붕에서, 아마 제 아비의 저택이었을 텐데, 기왓장을 던져 고귀하신 그라투스 각하의 머리를 맞혀 하마터면 죽일 뻔했소.”\n\n대화가 잠시 끊긴 사이, 나사렛 사람들은 마치 사나운 짐승을 보듯 어린 벤허를 바라보았다.\n\n“아니오.” “그는 선고를 받았소.”\n\n“아니오.”\n“선고는 받았소?”\n\n“그렇소. 종신토록 갤리선의 노를 젓게 되었소.”\n\n“주님께서 저 아이를 도우시기를!” 요셉이 말했는데, 평소의 무덤덤함에서 벗어난 것은 이번 한 번뿐이었다.\n그때 요셉과 함께 왔으나 그 뒤에 가려져 눈에 띄지 않았던 한 젊은이가, 들고 있던 도끼를 내려놓고는, 우물 곁에 놓인 큰 돌로 가서 물 단지를 집어 들었다. 그 동작이 어찌나 조용했던지, 호위병들이 막으려 했다 하더라도, 미처 손쓰기도 전에 그는 이미 죄수에게 몸을 굽혀 마실 물을 건네고 있었다.\n\n다정하게 어깨에 얹힌 손이 가엾은 유다를 일깨웠다. 고개를 들어 그는 결코 잊지 못할 얼굴을, 곧 제 또래쯤 되어 보이는 소년의 얼굴을 보았다. 누르스름하게 빛나는 밤색 머리카락이 그 얼굴에 그늘을 드리우고 있었다. 그 얼굴은 짙푸른 두 눈으로 빛나고 있었는데, 그 눈은 그 순간 어찌나 부드럽고, 어찌나 호소하는 듯하며, 사랑과 거룩한 뜻으로 가득 차 있던지, 명령하고 다스리는 온갖 힘을 다 지니고 있었다. 여러 날 밤낮의 고통으로 굳어지고, 온 세상을 복수의 꿈으로 채울 만큼 부당함에 사무쳤던 유다의 마음도 그 낯선 이의 눈길 아래 녹아내려, 어린아이의 마음과 같이 되었다. 그는 단지에 입술을 대고 길고 깊게 물을 들이켰다. 누구도 그에게 한마디 하지 않았고, 그 또한 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n물을 다 마시자, 고통받는 이의 어깨에 얹혀 있던 손이 그의 머리 위로 옮겨가, 축복을 빌 만한 동안 먼지 낀 머리카락 위에 머물렀다. 그러고 나서 그 낯선 이는 단지를 돌 위 제자리에 돌려놓고, 다시 도끼를 집어 들어 요셉 랍비에게로 돌아갔다. 모든 눈길이 그를 좇았으니, 마을 사람들의 눈길은 물론 부관의 눈길까지도 그러했다.\n\n이것이 우물가 장면의 끝이었다. 사람들이 물을 마시고 말들도 물을 마시자, 행군이 다시 시작되었다. 그러나 부관의 마음은 전과 같지 않았다. 그는 손수 죄수를 먼지 속에서 일으켜, 한 병사 뒤에 태워 주었다. 나사렛 사람들은 저마다 집으로 돌아갔는데, 그 가운데에는 요셉 랍비와 그의 도제도 있었다.\n\n이리하여, 처음으로, 유다와 마리아의 아들은 만났다가 헤어졌다.\n\n# BOOK THIRD\n\n\"클레오파트라…… 우리 슬픔의 크기는, 그 원인에 걸맞게, 그것을 자아낸 까닭만큼 커야 마땅하리라.— 아래쪽에서, 디오메데스 등장. 어찌 되었느냐? 그가 죽었느냐?\n\n디오메데스. 죽음이 그에게 내렸으되, 아직 숨은 붙어 있나이다.\" 안토니우스와 클레오파트라 (제4막, 제13장).\n\n미세눔 시는 나폴리에서 남서쪽으로 몇 마일 떨어진 곳, 자신이 꼭대기에 자리한 곶에 그 이름을 주었다. 지금은 폐허에 관한 기록만이 남아 있을 뿐이다. 그러나 우리 주님의 해로 24년—독자를 이제 그 시점으로 데려가고자 한다—에는, 이 고장이 이탈리아 서해안에서 가장 중요한 곳 가운데 하나였다.[1]\n\n[1] 기억해 두어야 할 것은, 로마 정부에 대함대를 늘 주둔시키던 두 항구가 있었으니, 라벤나와 미세눔이 그것이다.\n\n앞서 말한 해에, 그곳에서 펼쳐지는 경치를 즐기러 이 곶을 찾은 나그네라면, 성벽 위에 올라 도시를 등진 채 네아폴리스 만을 굽어보았을 것이다. 그 만은 지금이나 그때나 매혹적이었다. 그리고 그때나 지금이나 그는 견줄 데 없는 해안, 연기를 내뿜는 원뿔 봉우리, 더없이 부드럽고 깊푸른 하늘과 물결을 보았을 것이며, 이쪽에는 이스키아, 저쪽에는 카프리를 보았을 것이다. 자줏빛으로 물든 대기를 가르며, 그의 눈길은 이쪽에서 저쪽으로, 다시 저쪽에서 이쪽으로 즐거이 노닐었으리라. 마침내—눈도 혀가 단것에 물리듯 아름다움에 물리는 법이니—마침내 그 눈길은, 오늘날의 여행자는 볼 수 없는 광경에 떨어졌을 것이다. 로마 예비 함대의 절반이 그의 발아래에서 움직이거나 닻을 내리고 있었던 것이다. 이렇게 보면, 미세눔은 세 통치자가 만나 한가로이 세계를 저들끼리 나눠 갖기에 더없이 알맞은 곳이었다.\n\n게다가 옛날에는, 바다를 마주한 성벽의 어느 한 지점에 성문이 하나 있었다. 그것은 한 거리의 출구를 이루는 텅 빈 성문으로, 그 거리는 문을 빠져나간 뒤 넓은 방파제 모양으로 여러 스타디온이나 물결 속으로 뻗어 나갔다.\n\n성문 위 성벽의 보초는, 어느 선선한 9월 아침, 시끌벅적 이야기를 나누며 거리를 내려오는 한 무리에 잠을 방해받았다. 그는 한 번 흘끗 보고는, 다시 졸음 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n그 무리에는 스무 명이나 서른 명쯤 되는 사람들이 있었는데, 그 대다수는 횃불을 든 노예들이었다. 횃불은 불꽃은 작고 연기는 자욱하여, 인도 감송의 향기를 공기 중에 남겼다. 주인들은 팔짱을 끼고 앞장서 걸었다. 그중 한 사람은 쉰 살쯤 되어 보였고, 머리가 약간 벗어졌으며, 성긴 머리칼 위에 월계관을 쓰고 있었는데, 사람들이 그에게 기울이는 정성으로 보아 어떤 다정한 의례의 중심인물인 듯했다. 그들은 모두 자줏빛으로 넓게 가장자리를 두른 흰 양모 토가를 넉넉히 걸치고 있었다. 보초에게는 한 번 흘끗 보는 것으로 충분했다. 그는 묻지 않고도, 그들이 높은 신분이며, 밤새 잔치를 벌인 뒤 한 벗을 배까지 배웅하고 있음을 알았다. 더 자세한 설명은 그들이 나누는 대화 속에서 찾을 수 있을 것이다.\n\n\"안 되네, 나의 퀸투스.\" 한 사람이 월계관을 쓴 이에게 말을 건넸다. \"이리도 빨리 자네를 우리에게서 데려가다니, 운명의 여신도 야박하구먼. 바로 어제야 자네는 헤라클레스의 기둥 너머 바다에서 돌아오지 않았나. 어허, 자네는 아직 뭍에서 걷는 다리도 되찾지 못했네.\"\n\n\"카스토르께 맹세코! 사내가 계집의 맹세를 입에 담아도 된다면 말일세.\" 또 한 사람이 술기운이 좀 더 도는 채로 말했다. \"한탄일랑 그만두세. 우리 퀸투스는 다만 어젯밤에 잃은 것을 되찾으러 가는 것뿐이니. 흔들리는 배 위의 주사위는 뭍의 주사위와 다르지—그렇지 않나, 퀸투스?\"\n\n\"운명의 여신을 욕되게 하지 말게!\" 세 번째 사람이 외쳤다. \"그분은 눈멀지도, 변덕스럽지도 않으시네. 안티움에서 우리 아리우스가 그분께 여쭈면, 그분은 고개를 끄덕여 답하시고, 바다에서는 그가 키를 잡을 때 늘 그와 함께하시지. 그분이 그를 우리에게서 데려가시지만, 언제나 새 승리와 함께 되돌려 주시지 않던가?\"\n\n\"그를 데려가는 건 그리스인들일세.\" 또 다른 이가 끼어들었다. \"신들이 아니라 그자들을 욕하세. 그자들은 장사하는 법을 배우다가 싸우는 법을 잊어버렸으니.\"\n\n이런 말과 함께, 일행은 성문을 지나 방파제에 들어섰다. 그들 앞에는 아침 빛 속에 아름다운 만이 펼쳐져 있었다. 노련한 뱃사람에게 물결이 철썩이는 소리는 인사처럼 들렸다. 그는, 마치 물의 향기가 감송의 향기보다 더 달콤하기라도 한 듯, 길게 숨을 들이쉬고는, 한 손을 높이 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"내 제물은 안티움이 아니라 프라이네스테에 두었지—그리고 보게! 서쪽에서 바람이 부네. 고맙습니다, 오 운명의 여신이여, 나의 어머니여!\" 그가 간곡하게 말했다.\n\n벗들은 모두 그 외침을 따라 했고, 노예들은 횃불을 흔들었다.\n\n\"그녀가 온다—저기일세!\" 그가 방파제 바깥의 갤리선을 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"뱃사람에게 어찌 다른 정인이 필요하겠나? 자네의 루크레티아가 저보다 우아한가, 나의 카이우스?\"\n\n그는 다가오는 배를 바라보며, 자신의 자부심이 옳음을 증명했다. 흰 돛이 낮은 돛대에 매여 있었고, 노들은 잠겼다가, 솟아올랐다가, 한순간 멈추었다가, 다시 잠겼으니, 날갯짓 같은 동작으로 완벽한 박자를 맞추고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 신들을 욕되게 하지 말게.\" 그가, 눈길을 배에 고정한 채, 차분하게 말했다. \"그분들은 우리에게 기회를 보내 주시지. 우리가 실패한다면 그건 우리 탓일세. 그리고 그리스인들로 말하자면, 자네는 잊고 있군, 오 나의 렌툴루스, 내가 벌하러 가는 해적들이 바로 그리스인들이라는 걸 말일세. 그들을 한 번 이기는 것이 아프리카인들을 백 번 이기는 것보다 값지다네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네의 뱃길은 에게 해로 향하는가?\"\n\n뱃사람의 두 눈에는 자기 배가 가득 차 있었다.\n\n\"저 우아함, 저 자유로움이라니! 새도 물결의 일렁임을 저보다 더 마음 쓰지는 않을 걸세. 보게!\" 그가 말했으나, 거의 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"용서하게, 나의 렌툴루스. 나는 에게 해로 가네. 떠날 때가 이리도 가까우니, 그 사연을 말해 주겠네—다만 비밀로 해 두게. 다음에 두움비르를 만나거든 그를 욕하지 않았으면 하네. 그는 내 벗이니. 그리스와 알렉산드리아 사이의 교역은, 자네들도 들었겠지만, 알렉산드리아와 로마 사이의 교역에 못지않다네. 그 지방 사람들이 케레알리아 제전 올리기를 잊었더니, 트립톨레모스가 거둘 가치도 없는 수확으로 그들에게 갚아 주었다지. 어쨌거나 그 교역이 어찌나 커졌는지, 단 하루의 중단도 견디지 못한다네. 자네들도 케르소네소스 해적들 이야기를 들었을 걸세, 에욱시네 바다에 둥지를 튼 자들 말이야. 바카이께 맹세코, 그보다 대담한 자들이 없지! 어제 로마에 소식이 들어왔네, 그자들이 함대를 거느리고 보스포루스를 노 저어 내려와, 비잔티움과 칼케돈 앞바다의 갤리선들을 침몰시키고, 프로폰티스를 휩쓸고, 그래도 성에 차지 않아 에게 해로 뚫고 들어왔다고 말일세. 동지중해에 배를 둔 곡물 상인들이 겁에 질렸지. 그들이 황제를 직접 알현했고, 오늘 라벤나에서 백 척의 갤리선이 떠나고, 미세눔에서는\"—그는 마치 벗들의 호기심을 돋우려는 듯 말을 멈추었다가, 힘주어 끝맺었다—\"한 척이 떠나네.\"\n\n\"복된 퀸투스로다! 우리가 자네를 축하하네!\"\n\n\"이번 발탁은 승진의 전조일세. 우리는 자네를 두움비르라 부르겠네. 그 이하로는 안 되지.\"\n\n\"두움비르 퀸투스 아리우스가, 호민관 퀸투스 아리우스보다 듣기에 좋군.\"\n\n이런 식으로 그들은 그에게 축하를 쏟아부었다.\n\n\"나도 다른 이들 못지않게 기쁘네.\" 술 좋아하는 벗이 말했다. \"참으로 기쁘네. 하지만 나는 현실적이어야겠네, 오 나의 두움비르. 승진이 자네가 주사위 눈을 아는 데 도움이 될지 알기 전까지는, 이번—이번 일에서 신들이 자네에게 이로움을 뜻하시는지 해로움을 뜻하시는지 내 견해를 내놓지 않겠네.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 정말 고맙네!\" 아리우스가 그들 전체를 향해 대답했다. \"자네들에게 등불만 있다면, 자네들을 점복관이라 부를 텐데. 페르폴! 더 나아가, 자네들이 얼마나 빼어난 예언자인지 보여 주겠네! 보게—그리고 읽어 보게.\"\n\n그는 토가의 주름 속에서 종이 두루마리를 꺼내 그들에게 건네며 말했다. \"어젯밤 식탁에 앉아 있을 때 받은 것일세—세야누스에게서.\"\n\n그 이름은 이미 로마 세계에서 대단한 이름이었으니, 대단하되, 훗날 그가 얻게 될 만큼 악명 높지는 않았다.\n\n\"세야누스라니!\" 그들이 한목소리로 외치며, 그 대신(大臣)이 써 보낸 것을 읽으려고 바싹 다가들었다.\n\n\"세야누스가 두움비르 가이우스 카이킬리우스 루푸스에게.\n\n로마, 9월 초하루 전 열아흐레.\n\n황제께서 호민관 퀸투스 아리우스에 대해 좋은 보고를 받으셨소. 특히 서쪽 바다에서 드러난 그의 용맹을 들으셨으니, 그 정도가 어찌나 대단한지, 앞서 말한 퀸투스를 즉시 동방으로 전임시키는 것이 황제의 뜻이시오.\n\n또한 우리 황제의 뜻은, 그대가 일급 삼단노선 백 척을 완전한 장비를 갖추어 지체 없이 에게 해에 나타난 해적들을 치도록 파견하고, 퀸투스를 그리 파견된 함대의 지휘관으로 보내라는 것이오.\n\n세부 사항은 그대에게 맡기오, 나의 카이킬리우스.\n\n이 일은 시급하니, 그대가 살펴보고 또 앞서 말한 퀸투스에게 알려 주도록 동봉한 보고서들로 알게 될 것이오.\n세야누스.\"\n\n아리우스는 그 낭독에 별 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 배가 원근의 풍경에서 더욱 또렷이 떠오를수록, 그에게는 점점 더 매혹의 대상이 되어 갔다. 그가 배를 지켜보는 눈길은 열광하는 사람의 눈길이었다. 마침내 그는 풀어진 토가 자락을 허공에 흔들어 올렸다. 그 신호에 답하여, 배의 고물에 달린 부챗살 모양의 장식, 곧 아플루스트레 위로 진홍빛 기가 펼쳐졌다. 그러는 사이 몇몇 뱃사람이 뱃전에 나타나, 밧줄을 손으로 번갈아 잡으며 활대, 곧 가로돛대까지 기어올라 돛을 걷었다. 이물이 방향을 틀고, 노 젓는 박자가 반절 빨라졌다. 그리하여 배는 질주하는 속도로 그와 그의 벗들을 향해 곧장 다가왔다. 그는 그 조선술을 지켜보며 눈에 띄게 눈빛이 밝아졌다. 키에 대한 배의 즉각적인 반응과, 항로를 지키는 한결같음은, 실전에서 믿고 의지할 만한 미덕으로서 특히 눈여겨볼 만했다.\n\n\"님파이께 맹세코!\" 한 벗이 두루마리를 돌려주며 말했다. \"우리는 더 이상 우리 벗이 위대해질 것이라 말할 수 없네. 그는 이미 위대하니. 이제 우리의 사랑은 자랑스러운 일들을 양식으로 삼게 되겠군. 우리에게 더 들려줄 것이 있나?\"\n\n\"더는 없네.\" 아리우스가 대답했다. \"이 일에 대해 자네들이 아는 것은 이미 로마에서는 묵은 소식일 걸세, 특히 궁궐과 광장 사이에서는 말이야. 두움비르는 신중한 사람이니, 내가 무엇을 해야 하는지, 어디로 가서 함대를 찾아야 하는지는 배 위에서 일러 줄 걸세, 그곳에 밀봉된 꾸러미가 나를 기다리고 있으니. 하지만 자네들이 오늘 어느 제단에든 바칠 제물이 있거든, 시칠리아 방향 어딘가에서 노와 돛을 부리고 있을 한 벗을 위해 신들께 빌어 주게. 그런데 그녀가 여기 왔으니, 곧 댈 걸세.\" 그가 다시 배 쪽으로 눈을 돌리며 말했다. \"나는 그 배의 부리는 자들에게 관심이 있네. 그들은 나와 함께 항해하고 함께 싸울 테니. 이런 해안에 배의 옆구리를 대는 것은 쉬운 일이 아닐세. 그러니 그들의 훈련과 솜씨를 가늠해 보세.\"\n\n\"무어라, 그 배가 자네에게는 처음인가?\"\n\n\"전에 본 적이 없네. 그리고 아직은, 저 배가 내게 아는 이를 하나라도 데려다줄지 알 수 없네.\"\n\n\"그래도 괜찮은가?\"\n\n\"별것 아닐세. 우리 바다 사람들은 서로를 빨리 알게 되지. 우리의 사랑도, 미움과 마찬가지로, 갑작스러운 위험에서 태어나니까.\"\n\n그 배는 나베스 리부르니카이라 불리는 종류로, 길고, 좁고, 물에 낮게 잠겨, 속도와 날쌘 조작에 맞게 만들어진 것이었다. 이물이 아름다웠다. 배가 다가올 때 그 발치에서 물줄기가 솟아올라 이물 전체에 흩뿌렸는데, 이물은 갑판 면에서 사람 키의 두 배 높이로 우아하게 굽어 솟아 있었다. 굽은 옆면 위에는 소라고둥을 부는 트리톤 상이 새겨져 있었다. 이물 아래, 용골에 박혀 수면 아래로 앞으로 튀어나온 것은 충각, 곧 부리였으니, 단단한 나무로 만들어 쇠로 보강하고 무장한 장치로, 실전에서 들이받는 데 쓰였다. 굵직한 쇠시리가 이물에서 배 옆구리 끝까지 뻗어 뱃전의 윤곽을 이루었는데, 그 뱃전은 운치 있게 총안 모양으로 새겨져 있었다. 그 쇠시리 아래로는, 세 줄로, 저마다 황소 가죽 덮개나 방패로 가린 채, 노를 부리는 구멍들이 있었으니—오른쪽에 예순, 왼쪽에 예순이었다. 더한 장식으로, 카두케우스 지팡이들이 높은 이물에 기대어 있었다. 이물을 가로지르는 두 가닥의 거대한 밧줄은, 앞갑판에 갈무리된 닻의 수를 일러 주었다.\n\n윗부분 구조물이 단순한 것을 보면, 노가 선원들의 주된 의지처임을 알 수 있었다. 배 한가운데보다 조금 앞쪽에 세운 돛대는, 뱃전 안쪽의 고리에 매단 앞뒤 버팀줄과 옆 버팀줄로 지탱되었다. 삭구는 커다란 사각돛 하나와 그것을 매단 활대를 다루는 데 필요한 것이었다. 뱃전 위로는 갑판이 보였다.\n\n돛을 걷고도 활대에 여태 머물러 있던 뱃사람들 말고는, 방파제의 일행 눈에 띄는 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었으니, 그는 투구를 쓰고 방패를 든 채 이물 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n백스무 자루의 떡갈나무 노깃은, 부석과 끊임없는 물결의 씻김으로 희고 반들반들하게 닦인 채, 마치 같은 손이 부리기라도 하는 듯 오르내리며, 갤리선을 오늘날의 증기선에 견줄 만한 속도로 앞으로 몰아 나갔다.\n\n배가 어찌나 빠르게, 그리고 보기에 어찌나 무모하게 다가오는지, 호민관 일행의 뭍사람들은 겁에 질렸다. 갑자기 이물 곁의 사내가 독특한 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올렸다. 그러자 모든 노가 솟구쳐, 한순간 공중에 멈추었다가, 곧장 아래로 떨어졌다. 노 둘레에서 물이 끓어오르고 거품이 일었다. 갤리선은 모든 널빤지가 다 흔들리도록 떨더니, 겁먹은 듯 멈추어 섰다. 손의 또 다른 몸짓에, 노들이 다시 솟아올라, 깃처럼 눕혔다가, 떨어졌다. 그러나 이번에는 오른쪽 노들이 고물 쪽으로 떨어지며 앞으로 밀고, 왼쪽 노들은 이물 쪽으로 떨어지며 뒤로 당겼다. 노들은 이렇게 세 번 서로 맞밀고 맞당겼다. 배는 마치 굴대를 중심으로 돌듯 오른쪽으로 빙 돌았다. 그러더니 바람을 받아, 옆구리를 방파제 쪽으로 부드럽게 돌려 댔다.\n\n그 움직임에 고물이 그 온갖 치장과 함께 드러났다—이물의 것과 같은 트리톤 상들, 크게 도드라진 글자로 새긴 이름, 옆쪽의 키, 키잡이가 앉은 높직한 단(壇)—그 위에는 완전 무장을 한 위엄 있는 사람이 키 줄에 손을 얹고 있었다—그리고 아플루스트레가, 높이 솟아 도금되고 새김질된 채, 커다란 톱니 모양 잎새처럼 키잡이 위로 굽어 있었다.\n\n방향을 돌리는 와중에, 나팔이 짧고 날카롭게 울리더니, 승강구에서 해병들이 쏟아져 나왔다. 모두 더없이 훌륭한 차림으로, 놋쇠 투구를 쓰고, 반들반들한 방패와 투창을 들고 있었다. 이렇게 전투원들이 실전에라도 임하듯 전투 위치로 가는 동안, 본래의 뱃사람들은 옆 버팀줄을 타고 올라 활대를 따라 자리를 잡고 앉았다. 장교들과 악사들도 제자리를 잡았다. 고함도, 쓸데없는 소리도 없었다. 노가 방파제에 닿자, 키잡이의 갑판에서 다리가 뻗어 나왔다. 그러자 호민관은 일행을 향해 돌아서서, 전에 보이지 않던 진중함으로 말했다.\n\n\"이제 임무가 기다리네, 오 나의 벗들이여.\"\n\n그는 머리에서 화관을 벗어 주사위꾼에게 주었다.\n\n\"이 도금양 관을 받게, 오 주사위 눈의 총아여!\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 돌아온다면, 내 세스테르티우스를 다시 찾으러 오겠네. 내가 승리하지 못한다면, 돌아오지 않을 걸세. 이 관을 자네 안뜰에 걸어 두게.\"\n\n그는 일행을 향해 두 팔을 벌렸고, 그들은 한 사람씩 다가와 그의 작별의 포옹을 받았다.\n\n\"신들이 자네와 함께하시기를, 오 퀸투스여!\" 그들이 말했다.\n\n\"잘들 있게.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n횃불을 흔드는 노예들에게 그는 손을 흔들어 주었다. 그러고는 가지런한 대오와 깃 장식 투구들, 방패와 투창으로 아름다운, 기다리는 배 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 그가 다리에 발을 디디자, 나팔이 울리고, 아플루스트레 위로 벡실룸 푸르푸레움, 곧 함대 지휘관의 깃발이 올랐다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n호민관은 키잡이 갑판에 서서 펼쳐 든 두 집정관의 명령서를 손에 쥔 채, 노잡이들의 우두머리에게 말했다.[1]\n\n[1] 호르타토르라 불린다.\n\n\"네가 거느린 인원이 얼마나 되느냐?\"\n\n\"노잡이는 이백쉰둘이고, 예비 인원이 열입니다.\"\n\n\"교대조로 치면—\"\n\n\"여든넷입니다.\"\n\n\"교대 방식은 어떠하냐?\"\n\n\"두 시간마다 내리고 올리는 것이 관례였습니다.\"\n\n호민관은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그 배분은 고되다. 내가 고치겠으나, 지금은 아니다. 노는 밤낮으로 쉬어서는 안 된다.\"\n\n이어 그는 항해장에게 말했다.\n\n\"바람이 좋다. 돛으로 노를 돕도록 하라.\"\n\n이렇게 일러둔 두 사람이 물러가자, 그는 수석 도선사에게 몸을 돌렸다.[2]\n\n[2] 렉토르라 불린다.\n\n\"너는 얼마나 복무했느냐?\"\n\n\"서른두 해입니다.\"\n\n\"주로 어느 바다에서였느냐?\"\n\n\"우리 로마와 동방 사이에서였습니다.\"\n\n\"네가 바로 내가 골랐을 만한 사람이로구나.\"\n\n호민관은 다시 명령서를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"캄파넬라 곶을 지나면 항로는 메시나로 향한다. 그다음에는 멜리토가 왼편에 놓일 때까지 칼라브리아 해안의 굽이를 따라가라. 그러고 나서는— 이오니아해를 다스리는 별들을 아느냐?\"\n\n\"잘 압니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 멜리토에서 동쪽으로 키테라를 향해 항로를 잡아라. 신들이 허락하신다면, 나는 안테모나만에 이르기까지 닻을 내리지 않겠다. 이 임무는 화급하다. 너를 믿겠다.\"\n\n아리우스는 신중한 사람이었다. 신중하면서도, 프라이네스테와 안티움의 제단을 살찌우는 부류에 속했으나, 그러면서도 눈먼 여신의 은총은 봉헌자의 예물이나 서원보다 그의 주의와 판단에 더 달려 있다고 여기는 부류였다. 밤새도록 연회의 주인으로서 그는 식탁에 앉아 술을 마시고 노름을 했다. 그러나 바다의 냄새가 그를 다시 뱃사람의 기분으로 되돌려 놓았으니, 그는 제 배를 속속들이 알기 전에는 쉬려 하지 않았다. 앎은 우연이 끼어들 자리를 남기지 않는다. 노잡이의 우두머리와 항해장과 도선사로 시작하여, 다른 장교들—해병대 지휘관, 군수품 관리인, 기계 담당관, 주방 곧 화덕을 맡은 감독관—과 더불어 그는 여러 구역을 두루 거쳤다. 어느 것도 그의 점검을 비켜 가지 못했다. 점검을 마쳤을 때, 그 좁은 벽 안에 빽빽이 들어찬 무리 가운데 오직 그만이 이번 항해와 일어날 법한 사고에 대비한 물자의 준비를 빈틈없이 다 알고 있었다. 그리고 준비가 완벽함을 확인한 그에게는 단 한 가지 일만이 더 남아 있었으니, 그것은 제 휘하 인원에 대한 철저한 파악이었다. 이것은 그의 임무 가운데 가장 미묘하고 까다로운 부분으로, 많은 시간을 요구했으므로, 그는 제 방식대로 그 일에 착수했다.\n\n그날 정오, 갤리선은 파이스툼 앞바다를 스치듯 미끄러져 갔다. 바람은 여전히 서쪽에서 불어와, 항해장이 흡족할 만큼 돛을 채웠다. 당직이 정해졌다. 앞갑판에는 제단이 세워지고 소금과 보리가 뿌려졌으며, 그 앞에서 호민관은 유피테르와 넵투누스와 모든 오케아니데스에게 엄숙히 기도를 올리고, 서원과 함께 포도주를 붓고 향을 사르었다. 이제 부하들을 더 잘 살피려고, 그는 큰 선실에 앉아 있었으니, 자못 군인다운 모습이었다.\n\n말해 두건대, 그 선실은 갤리선의 한가운데 칸으로, 넓이는 족히 가로 스무 미터에 세로 아홉 미터에 이르렀고, 세 개의 넓은 승강구로 빛이 들었다. 한 줄로 늘어선 받침 기둥이 끝에서 끝까지 이어져 지붕을 떠받쳤고, 한가운데쯤에는 돛대가 보였는데, 도끼와 창과 투창으로 빽빽이 둘러싸여 있었다. 승강구마다 좌우로 내려가는 두 갈래 계단이 있었고, 위쪽에는 회전 장치가 달려 아래쪽 끝을 천장에 걸어 둘 수 있게 되어 있었다. 그리고 지금은 그것들이 들어 올려져 있어, 그 칸은 마치 채광창이 달린 큰 방처럼 보였다.\n\n독자는 이곳이 배의 심장이요, 배에 탄 모든 이의 거처였음을 쉽게 이해할 것이다. 식당이자 침실이요, 운동장이자 비번일 때 쉬는 자리이기도 했다. 이 모든 쓰임은 그곳의 삶을 자질구레한 세목과 죽음처럼 가차 없는 일과로 줄여 놓은 규율 덕분에 가능했다.\n\n선실 뒤쪽 끝에는 단이 하나 있어, 몇 개의 계단을 밟고 올라가게 되어 있었다. 그 위에 노잡이들의 우두머리가 앉았다. 그 앞에는 울림판이 놓여, 그것을 나무망치로 두드려 노잡이들에게 박자를 맞추어 주었다. 오른편에는 클렙시드라, 곧 물시계가 있어 교대조와 당직 시간을 쟀다. 그 위로는 더 높은 단에, 금박을 입힌 난간으로 든든히 둘러싸인 곳에 호민관의 거처가 있어, 모든 것을 내려다볼 수 있었다. 그곳에는 긴 의자와 탁자, 그리고 카테드라, 곧 푹신한 방석을 깐 팔걸이와 높은 등받이가 있는 의자가 갖추어져 있었으니, 모두 황제의 특전이 허락하는 더없이 우아한 물건들이었다.\n\n이렇게 편안히, 큰 의자에 기대어 배의 흔들림에 따라 몸을 흔들며, 군용 망토로 튜닉을 반쯤 두르고 허리띠에 칼을 찬 채, 아리우스는 제 휘하를 빈틈없이 지켜보았고, 그들 또한 그를 못지않게 지켜보았다. 그는 눈에 띄는 모든 것을 비판적으로 살폈으나, 노잡이들에게 가장 오래 눈길을 머물렀다. 독자라도 분명 그리했을 것이다. 다만 독자는 자못 측은한 마음으로 바라보았겠으나, 주인된 자들이 흔히 그러하듯, 호민관의 마음은 눈앞에 보이는 것을 앞질러 결과를 따져 묻고 있었다.\n\n광경 자체는 자못 단순했다. 선실 양쪽 옆으로, 배의 들보에 붙박여, 처음에는 세 줄의 의자처럼 보이는 것이 있었다. 그러나 가까이 살펴보니, 그것은 차례로 높아지는 단의 연속이었다. 각 단에서 둘째 의자는 첫째 의자보다 뒤쪽이자 위쪽에 있었고, 셋째 의자는 둘째 의자보다 위쪽이자 뒤쪽에 있었다. 한쪽에 예순 명의 노잡이를 두기 위해, 그들에게 할당된 공간은 일 미터 간격으로 떨어진 열아홉 단을 허용했고, 거기에 스무째 단이 나뉘어 들어가, 그 윗자리가 될 자리는 첫 단의 아랫자리 바로 위에 놓였다. 이런 배치는 노잡이마다 일할 때 넉넉한 자리를 주었으니, 다만 제 움직임을 동료들의 움직임에 맞추기만 하면 되었다. 그 원리는 병사들이 발맞추어 빽빽한 대열로 행진하는 것과 같았다. 이런 배치는 또한 단의 수를 늘릴 수 있게 했으니, 그 한계는 오직 갤리선의 길이뿐이었다.\n\n노잡이들로 말하면, 첫째와 둘째 의자에 앉은 자들은 앉아 있었으나, 셋째 의자에 앉은 자들은 더 긴 노를 다루어야 했으므로 서 있도록 허락되었다. 노는 손잡이에 납이 채워져 있었고, 균형점 가까이에 휘어지는 가죽끈으로 매달려 있어, 페더링이라 불리는 섬세한 솜씨가 가능했다. 그러나 그와 동시에 솜씨의 필요는 더 커졌으니, 어쩌다 비뚜로 치는 물결이 부주의한 자를 덮쳐 그를 제 자리에서 내동댕이칠 수도 있었기 때문이다. 노 구멍 하나하나는 통풍구가 되어, 그 맞은편의 일꾼에게 신선한 공기를 넉넉히 들여보냈다. 그의 머리 위 갑판과 뱃전 사이 통로의 바닥을 이루는 살창으로부터 빛이 그에게 흘러내렸다. 그러므로 어떤 점에서는 그들의 처지가 훨씬 더 나쁠 수도 있었으리라. 그렇다고 그들의 삶에 무슨 즐거움이 있었으리라 여겨서는 안 된다. 그들 사이의 대화는 허락되지 않았다. 날이면 날마다 그들은 말없이 제 자리를 채웠다. 일하는 동안에는 서로의 얼굴조차 볼 수 없었다. 짧은 휴식은 잠과 음식을 허겁지겁 삼키는 데에 바쳐졌다. 그들은 결코 웃지 않았다. 그들 가운데 누군가 노래하는 것을 들은 이는 아무도 없었다. 한숨이나 신음 한 번이면 사람의 마음에 든 모든 것을 다 일러 주는데, 게다가 어쩔 수 없이 침묵 속에 생각해야 한다면, 혀가 무슨 소용이겠는가? 그 가엾은 이들에게 삶이란, 그 출구가 어디든 간에, 더디고 고되게 그리로 흘러가는 땅 밑의 물줄기 같은 것이었다.\n\n오, 마리아의 아들이여! 이제 칼에는 심장이 있고—그 영광은 당신의 것입니다! 지금은 그러하다. 그러나 우리가 쓰고 있는 그 시절에는, 포로된 자에게 성벽에서의 고역과 거리와 광산에서의 노역이 있었고, 전쟁과 상업의 갤리선은 둘 다 만족을 모르는 아가리였다. 드루일리우스가 제 나라를 위해 첫 해전을 거두었을 때에는, 로마인이 노를 저었고, 영광은 해병 못지않게 노잡이에게 돌아갔다. 지금 우리가 본래 모습 그대로 보려 애쓰는 이 의자들은 정복과 더불어 찾아온 변화를 증언했고, 로마의 정책과 무용을 아울러 보여 주었다. 거의 모든 민족이 그곳에 제 아들들을 두고 있었으니, 대부분 전쟁 포로로, 억센 몸과 인내력을 보고 골라 온 자들이었다. 한 자리에는 브리튼 사람이, 그 앞에는 리비아 사람이, 그 뒤에는 크림 사람이 있었다. 다른 곳에는 스키타이 사람과 갈리아 사람과 테베 사람이 있었다. 로마의 죄수들이 끌려 내려와 고트족과 롱고바르디족, 유대인, 에티오피아인, 그리고 마이오티스 해안에서 온 야만인들과 한데 어울렸다. 여기에는 아테네 사람이, 저기에는 히베르니아에서 온 붉은 머리의 야만인이, 저쪽에는 푸른 눈의 킴브리 거인들이 있었다.\n\n노잡이들의 노동에는 그들의 마음을 붙들어 둘 만한 솜씨랄 것이 거의 없었으니, 그들이 거칠고 단순한 만큼이나 그러했다. 앞으로 뻗기, 당기기, 노깃을 누이기, 물에 담그기—그것이 전부였고, 그 동작들은 가장 기계적일 때 가장 완벽했다. 바깥 바다가 그들에게 강요하는 주의조차 시간이 흐르면서 생각의 산물이 아니라 본능에 가까운 것이 되어 갔다. 그리하여 오랜 복무의 결과로, 그 가엾은 이들은 짐승처럼 되어 갔으니—참을성 있고, 기백 없고, 순종하는—거대한 근육과 메마른 지성을 지닌 피조물이 되어, 대개 몇 안 되나 소중한 추억으로 살아갔고, 마침내 비참함이 습관으로 바뀌고 영혼이 믿기 어려운 인내를 걸치게 되는 반쯤 깬 연금술의 상태로 가라앉아 갔다.\n\n오른쪽에서 왼쪽으로, 시간이 흐르고 또 흐르는 동안, 호민관은 안락의자에 앉아 몸을 흔들며, 의자에 앉은 노예들의 비참함보다는 차라리 다른 온갖 것을 생각하며 시선을 돌렸다. 그들의 동작은 정확했고, 배의 양쪽이 똑같았으니, 얼마 지나자 단조로워졌다. 그러자 그는 한 사람씩 골라내며 심심풀이를 했다. 첨필로 흠을 적어 가며, 만사가 잘 풀린다면 자기가 찾고 있는 해적들 가운데 이 자리들에 어울리는 더 나은 자들을 찾아낼 수 있으리라 생각했다.\n\n무덤에 끌려가듯 갤리선에 끌려온 노예들의 본디 이름을 간직해 둘 필요는 없었다. 그러므로 편의상 그들은 보통 배정된 의자에 칠해진 번호로 식별되었다. 그 위대한 사내의 날카로운 눈이 양쪽으로 자리에서 자리로 옮겨 가다가, 마침내 육십 번에 이르렀다. 그것은 앞서 말했듯이 본래 왼쪽 맨 끝 단에 속하는 것이었으나, 뒤쪽에 자리가 없어 첫 단의 첫 의자 위에 박혀 있었다. 그의 눈은 거기에 머물렀다.\n\n육십 번의 의자는 단의 높이보다 약간 위에 있었고, 불과 몇 걸음 떨어져 있을 뿐이었다. 머리 위 살창으로 비스듬히 새어 드는 빛이 그 노잡이를 호민관의 눈앞에 또렷이 드러냈다—허리에 두른 띠 하나를 빼고는, 다른 동료들과 마찬가지로 벌거벗은, 꼿꼿한 모습이었다. 그러나 그에게는 몇 가지 유리한 점이 있었다. 그는 무척 젊었으니, 스무 살을 넘지 않았다. 게다가 아리우스는 노름에만 빠진 사람이 아니었다. 그는 신체에 관한 한 사람을 보는 안목이 있는 자로, 뭍에 있을 때면 체육관을 찾아가 가장 이름난 운동선수들을 보고 감탄하는 버릇이 있었다. 어느 전문가에게서였는지, 힘이란 근육의 양 못지않게 질에 달려 있고, 동작의 우월함에는 힘과 더불어 어떤 정신이 필요하다는 생각을 얻은 것이 분명했다. 한 가지 신념을 받아들인 그는, 무엇에 빠진 사람들이 흔히 그러하듯, 늘 그것을 뒷받침할 본보기를 찾고 있었다.\n\n완벽함을 찾는 동안 호민관은 자주 멈추어 살펴야 했으나, 좀처럼 흡족한 적이 없었음을—사실 이번처럼 오래 붙들린 적은 거의 없었음을—독자는 능히 믿을 것이다.\n\n노를 한 번 젓기 시작할 때마다, 노잡이의 몸과 얼굴은 단에서 옆모습으로 보였다. 그 동작은 몸이 뒤로 젖혀지며 밀어내는 자세로 끝났다. 그 동작의 우아함과 수월함은 처음에는 그가 쏟는 노력이 참된 것인지 의심을 자아냈으나, 그 의심은 곧 떨쳐졌다. 앞으로 뻗을 때 노를 단단히 쥐는 모습과 밀 때 노가 휘어지는 모습은 그가 쏟는 힘의 증거였다. 그뿐만 아니라 그것은 노잡이의 솜씨 또한 분명히 증명했고, 큰 안락의자에 앉은 비평가로 하여금 제 이론의 핵심을 이루는 힘과 영민함의 결합을 찾아 나서게 했다.\n\n살펴보는 동안, 아리우스는 그 대상이 젊다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그렇다고 그 때문에 측은한 마음이 든 것은 전혀 아니었다. 그는 또한 그의 키가 훤칠하며, 위아래 팔다리가 유달리 완벽하다는 것을 알아차렸다. 팔은 어쩌면 지나치게 길었으나, 그 흠은 우람한 근육 더미 아래 잘 가려져 있었으니, 어떤 동작에서는 그 근육이 뒤틀린 밧줄처럼 부풀어 오르고 매듭졌다. 둥근 몸통의 갈비뼈가 하나하나 드러났다. 그러나 그 야윔은 팔라이스트라에서 그토록 애써 추구하는 건강한 군더더기 빼기였다. 그리고 그 노잡이의 동작에는 무엇보다 어떤 조화가 깃들어 있어, 호민관의 이론에 부합할 뿐 아니라 그의 호기심과 두루 관심을 함께 자극했다.\n\n이내 그는 그 사내의 얼굴을 정면으로 보고 싶어 기다리는 자신을 발견했다. 머리는 균형이 잡혀 있었고, 밑동이 넓되 더없이 유연하고 우아한 목 위에 얹혀 있었다. 옆모습으로 본 이목구비는 동방의 윤곽을 지녔고, 늘 혈통과 예민한 정신의 표시로 여겨져 온 그 섬세한 표정을 띠고 있었다. 이런 관찰과 더불어, 그 대상에 대한 호민관의 관심은 한층 깊어졌다.\n\n\"신들에 맹세코,\" 그는 속으로 말했다. \"저 녀석이 나를 사로잡는구나! 자못 가망이 있어. 저 녀석을 더 알아보아야겠다.\"\n\n곧 호민관은 바라던 광경을 붙잡았다—노잡이가 몸을 돌려 그를 바라본 것이다.\n\n\"유대인이로구나! 게다가 소년이야!\"\n\n그때 자신에게 줄곧 고정된 그 시선 아래, 노예의 큰 눈은 더욱 커졌고—피가 이마까지 솟구쳤으며—노깃이 손안에서 머뭇거렸다. 그러나 곧장, 성난 소리를 내며 호르타토르의 나무망치가 내리쳐졌다. 노잡이는 흠칫하더니, 심문자에게서 얼굴을 거두고, 마치 제가 직접 꾸지람을 들은 듯, 반쯤 누이던 노를 놓쳐 버렸다. 다시 호민관을 흘끗 보았을 때, 그는 훨씬 더 놀랐으니—다정한 미소와 마주쳤던 것이다.\n\n그러는 사이 갤리선은 메시나 해협으로 들어섰고, 그 이름을 지닌 도시를 스치듯 지나, 얼마 뒤 동쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌렸으니, 에트나 위에 드리운 구름을 뒤편 하늘에 남겨 두고 갔다.\n\n선실의 단으로 돌아갈 때마다 아리우스는 그 노잡이를 다시 살피러 갔고, 거듭 속으로 되뇌었다. \"저 녀석에게는 기백이 있어. 유대인은 야만인이 아니다. 저 녀석을 더 알아보아야겠다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n출항한 지 나흘째, 아스트로이아호—갤리선의 이름이 그러했다—는 이오니아해를 가르며 나아가고 있었다. 하늘은 맑았고, 바람은 마치 모든 신들의 호의를 실어 나르듯 불었다.\n\n함대가 집결지로 정해진 키테라섬 동쪽 만에 닿기 전에 따라잡을 수 있겠다는 생각에, 아리우스는 다소 조급해진 마음으로 갑판에서 많은 시간을 보냈다. 그는 자기 배에 관한 일들을 부지런히 살폈고, 대체로 흡족해했다. 선실에서 커다란 의자에 몸을 흔들며 앉아 있을 때면, 그의 생각은 끊임없이 육십 번 노잡이에게로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"방금 저 자리에서 물러난 사내를 아느냐?\" 그는 마침내 호르타토르에게 물었다.\n\n마침 교대가 이루어지던 참이었다.\n\n\"육십 번 자리 말씀이십니까?\" 노잡이 우두머리가 되물었다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n우두머리는 앞으로 걸어 나가는 노잡이를 날카롭게 바라보았다.\n\n\"아시다시피,\" 그가 대답했다. \"이 배는 만들어진 지 한 달밖에 안 되었습니다. 그러니 노잡이들도 배만큼이나 제게 낯섭니다.\"\n\n\"저자는 유대인이다.\" 아리우스가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다.\n\n\"고귀하신 퀸투스님은 예리하십니다.\"\n\n\"아주 젊더구나.\" 아리우스가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하나 우리 배에서 가장 뛰어난 노잡이입니다.\" 우두머리가 말했다. \"노가 거의 부러질 만큼 휘는 것을 본 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"성품은 어떠하냐?\"\n\n\"순종적입니다. 그 밖에는 아는 바가 없습니다. 언젠가 제게 한 가지 청을 한 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 청이냐?\"\n\n\"저를 오른쪽과 왼쪽으로 번갈아 옮겨 달라고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"이유를 말하더냐?\"\n\n\"한쪽에만 매여 있는 자들은 몸이 비뚤어진다는 것을 알아챘다고 했습니다. 또 폭풍이나 전투가 벌어지는 날에는 갑자기 자리를 바꿔야 할 일이 생길 수 있는데, 그때 제구실을 못 하게 될지도 모른다고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"페르폴! 새로운 생각이로구나. 그 밖에 무엇을 보았느냐?\"\n\n\"동료들보다 몸가짐이 깨끗합니다.\"\n\n\"그 점은 로마인답구나.\" 아리우스가 흡족한 듯 말했다. \"저자의 내력에 관해서는 아는 것이 없느냐?\"\n\n\"한마디도 없습니다.\"\n\n호민관은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 제자리로 돌아가려고 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"저자의 차례가 끝났을 때 내가 갑판에 있거든,\" 그가 걸음을 멈추고 말했다. \"내게 보내라. 혼자 오게 하라.\"\n\n두 시간쯤 지난 뒤, 아리우스는 갤리선의 고물 장식 아래 서 있었다. 자신이 거대한 사건을 향해 빠르게 실려 가고 있음을 알면서도 그저 기다리는 수밖에 없는 사람의 심정이었으니—철학이 마음 고른 사람에게 더없는 평온을 입혀 주며, 언제나 그토록 쓸모 있게 작용하는 바로 그 심정이었다. 키잡이는 배 양옆에 하나씩 달린 키 노를 다루는 밧줄에 손을 얹고 앉아 있었다. 돛 그늘에는 선원 몇이 잠들어 누워 있었고, 활대 위에는 망보는 자가 올라가 있었다. 항로를 지키는 데 참고하려고 고물 장식 아래 놓아둔 해시계에서 눈을 들었을 때, 아리우스는 노잡이가 다가오는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"우두머리가 당신을 고귀한 아리우스님이라 부르며, 제가 여기로 당신을 찾아오는 것이 당신의 뜻이라고 했습니다. 그래서 왔습니다.\"\n\n아리우스는 그 모습을 찬찬히 살폈다. 키가 크고 근육이 탄탄하며, 햇빛에 번들거리고, 안에서 도는 진한 붉은 피로 물든 몸이었다—감탄하며 살폈고, 검투장을 떠올리며 살폈다. 그러나 그 태도는 그에게 영향을 미치지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 목소리에는 적어도 일부나마 사람을 다듬는 환경 속에서 보낸 삶의 자취가 배어 있었다. 두 눈은 맑고 트여 있었으며, 반항보다는 호기심이 어려 있었다. 예리하고 따져 묻는, 위엄에 찬 시선이 쏟아졌으나, 그 얼굴은 젊은 미모를 흐릴 만한 것을 조금도 내비치지 않았다—고발도, 음울함도, 위협도 없이, 다만 오래 견뎌 온 큰 슬픔이 새겨 놓은 흔적뿐이었으니, 세월이 그림의 표면을 부드럽게 무르익히듯 한 그것이었다. 그 영향을 말없이 인정하며, 로마인은 주인이 노예에게 하듯이 아니라 손위 사람이 손아래 사람에게 하듯이 말했다.\n\n\"호르타토르가 말하기를 네가 그의 가장 뛰어난 노잡이라더구나.\"\n\n\"호르타토르가 매우 친절하십니다.\" 노잡이가 대답했다.\n\n\"오래 복무했느냐?\"\n\n\"삼 년쯤 됩니다.\"\n\n\"노를 저으며?\"\n\n\"노에서 쉰 날은 단 하루도 떠올릴 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"고된 노릇이다. 일 년을 버티고도 부서지지 않는 자가 드물거늘, 너는—너는 아직 소년이 아니냐.\"\n\n\"고귀한 아리우스님께서는 견딤에 정신이 큰 몫을 한다는 것을 잊으셨습니다. 그 힘을 빌려 약한 자가 때로 살아남고, 강한 자가 도리어 스러지기도 합니다.\"\n\n\"말투를 보니 너는 유대인이로구나.\"\n\n\"제 조상은 첫 로마인보다 더 거슬러 올라가 히브리인이었습니다.\"\n\n\"네 종족의 고집스러운 긍지가 네게서도 사라지지 않았구나.\" 아리우스가 노잡이의 얼굴에 떠오른 홍조를 보며 말했다.\n\n\"긍지는 사슬에 묶였을 때 가장 소리 높이 우는 법입니다.\"\n\n\"네가 긍지를 가질 까닭이 무엇이냐?\"\n\n\"제가 유대인이라는 것입니다.\"\n\n아리우스가 미소 지었다.\n\"나는 예루살렘에 가 본 적이 없다.\" 그가 말했다. \"허나 그곳의 제후들에 관해서는 들었다. 그중 한 사람을 알고 있었지. 그는 상인이었고, 바다를 누볐다. 왕이 될 만한 인물이었다. 너는 어느 신분이냐?\"\n\n\"갤리선의 노잡이 자리에서 답해 드려야겠군요. 저는 노예의 신분입니다. 제 아버지는 예루살렘의 제후였고, 상인으로서 바다를 누볐습니다. 그분은 위대한 아우구스투스의 영빈관에서도 이름이 알려지고 존경받던 분이었습니다.\"\n\n\"이름이?\"\n\n\"이타마르, 후르 가문의 사람입니다.\"\n\n호민관은 놀라움에 손을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이—너란 말이냐?\"\n\n잠시 침묵한 뒤, 그가 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 여기까지 오게 되었느냐?\"\n\n유다는 고개를 떨구었고, 그의 가슴은 거칠게 들썩였다. 감정을 충분히 다스리고 나서, 그는 호민관의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 총독 발레리우스 그라투스를 암살하려 했다는 혐의를 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"네가!\" 아리우스가 더욱 놀라 외치며 한 걸음 물러섰다. \"네가 그 암살범이라고! 온 로마가 그 이야기로 떠들썩했다. 로디눔 곁의 강에 있던 내 배에까지 전해졌지.\"\n\n두 사람은 말없이 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나는 후르 가문이 이 땅에서 깡그리 사라진 줄로 알았다.\" 아리우스가 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n다정한 추억의 물결이 그 젊은이의 긍지를 휩쓸어 갔다. 두 뺨에 눈물이 빛났다.\n\n\"어머니—어머니! 그리고 제 어린 티르자! 그들은 어디에 있습니까? 오, 호민관님, 고귀한 호민관님, 그들에 대해 무엇이든 아신다면\"—그는 애원하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다—\"아시는 것을 모두 말씀해 주십시오. 그들이 살아 있는지, 살아 있다면 어디에 있는지, 어떤 처지인지 말씀해 주십시오. 오, 제발 말씀해 주십시오!\"\n\n그는 아리우스에게 다가섰으니, 어찌나 가까웠던지 그의 두 손이 팔짱을 낀 호민관의 어깨에서 흘러내린 망토에 닿을 정도였다.\n\n\"그 끔찍한 날이 삼 년이나 지났습니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"삼 년입니다, 호민관님, 그 한 시간 한 시간이 통째로 고통의 한평생이었습니다—죽음과 더불어 밑도 끝도 없는 구덩이에 갇힌 한평생, 노동 말고는 아무 위안도 없는 한평생이었습니다—그 모든 세월 동안 누구에게서도 한마디 소식이, 한 자락 속삭임조차 없었습니다. 오, 잊히는 것으로 우리도 잊을 수만 있다면! 그 장면에서—누이가 제게서 떼어 내지고, 어머니의 마지막 눈길이 보이던 그 장면에서 숨을 수만 있다면! 저는 역병의 입김도 맡아 보았고, 전투에서 배가 충돌하는 충격도 겪었습니다. 폭풍이 바다를 후려치는 소리를 듣고도, 남들은 기도할 때 저는 웃었습니다. 죽음이라면 차라리 벗어남이었을 테니까요. 노를 휘게 하고—그렇습니다, 그 안간힘 속에서 그날 일어난 일이 따라다니며 괴롭히는 것에서 벗어나려 했습니다. 얼마나 작은 것이라도 저를 도울 수 있을지 생각해 보십시오. 더는 바라지 않으니, 그들이 죽었다고만 말씀해 주십시오. 제가 사라진 동안 그들이 행복할 수는 없을 테니까요. 밤이면 그들이 저를 부르는 소리를 들었습니다. 물 위를 걸어오는 그들을 보았습니다. 오, 제 어머니의 사랑만큼 참된 것은 결코 없었습니다! 그리고 티르자—그 애의 숨결은 흰 백합의 숨결 같았습니다. 그 애는 종려나무의 가장 어린 가지였습니다—그토록 싱그럽고, 그토록 보드랍고, 그토록 우아하고, 그토록 아름다웠습니다! 그 애는 제 하루를 온통 아침으로 만들어 주었습니다. 그 애는 음악처럼 오고 갔습니다. 그런데 그들을 쓰러뜨린 것이 바로 제 손이었습니다! 제가—\"\n\n\"네 죄를 인정하느냐?\" 아리우스가 엄하게 물었다.\n\n벤허에게 일어난 변화는 보기에 놀라웠으니, 그토록 순식간이었고 그토록 극단적이었다. 목소리가 날카로워졌다. 두 손이 꽉 쥐어진 채 올라갔다. 온몸의 섬유가 떨렸다. 두 눈이 불타올랐다.\n\n\"당신은 제 조상의 하느님에 관해 들으셨을 것입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"무한하신 여호와에 관해 말입니다. 그분의 진리와 전능하심으로, 그리고 그분이 태초부터 이스라엘을 좇으신 그 사랑으로 맹세하건대, 저는 무죄입니다!\"\n\n호민관은 크게 마음이 움직였다.\n\n\"오, 고귀한 로마인이시여!\" 벤허가 말을 이었다. \"제게 작은 믿음이라도 베푸시어, 날마다 더 짙어지는 제 어둠 속으로 한 줄기 빛을 보내 주십시오!\"\n\n아리우스는 몸을 돌려 갑판을 거닐었다.\n\n\"너는 재판을 받지 못했느냐?\" 그가 문득 걸음을 멈추고 물었다.\n\n\"못 했습니다!\"\n로마인은 놀라 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"재판도—증인도 없이! 누가 네게 판결을 내렸느냐?\"\n\n기억해 둘 것은, 로마인은 그 어느 때에도 쇠퇴해 가던 시대만큼 법과 그 형식을 사랑한 적이 없었다는 사실이다.\n\n\"그들은 저를 밧줄로 묶어 탑 안의 지하 감옥으로 끌고 갔습니다. 저는 아무도 보지 못했습니다. 누구도 제게 말을 걸지 않았습니다. 이튿날 병사들이 저를 바닷가로 데려갔습니다. 그 뒤로 저는 줄곧 갤리선의 노예였습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇을 입증할 수 있었겠느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 소년이었습니다. 음모를 꾸미기에는 너무 어렸습니다. 그라투스는 제게 낯선 사람이었습니다. 제가 그를 죽이려 했다 한들, 그때 그 자리는 적당하지 않았습니다. 그는 군단 한가운데서 말을 타고 있었고, 환한 대낮이었습니다. 저는 달아날 수도 없었을 것입니다. 저는 로마에 가장 우호적인 계층이었습니다. 제 아버지는 황제께 바친 공로로 명성을 떨친 분이었습니다. 우리에게는 잃을 수 있는 큰 재산이 있었습니다. 저 자신과 어머니와 누이에게는 파멸이 확실했습니다. 저는 악의를 품을 까닭이 없었고, 온갖 것이—재산, 가문, 목숨, 양심, 그리고 이스라엘의 아들에게는 콧김과도 같은 율법이—설령 그 더러운 뜻이 아무리 강했다 한들 제 손을 막았을 것입니다. 저는 미치지 않았습니다. 수치보다는 죽음이 나았고, 부디 믿어 주십시오, 지금도 그러합니다.\"\n\n\"그 일격이 가해졌을 때 누가 너와 함께 있었느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 지붕 위에 있었습니다—제 아버지의 집 지붕이었습니다. 티르자가 저와 함께 있었습니다—제 곁에 말입니다—온유함 그 자체였지요. 우리는 함께 난간에 기대어 군단이 지나가는 것을 내려다보고 있었습니다. 제 손 밑에서 기와 한 장이 빠져나가 그라투스 위로 떨어졌습니다. 저는 그를 죽인 줄 알았습니다. 아, 제가 느낀 그 공포라니!\"\n\n\"네 어머니는 어디에 계셨느냐?\"\n\n\"아래층 방에 계셨습니다.\"\n\n\"어머니는 어찌 되었느냐?\"\n\n벤허는 두 손을 꽉 쥐고, 숨을 헐떡이듯 들이쉬었다.\n\n\"모릅니다. 그들이 어머니를 끌고 가는 것을 보았을 뿐, 그것이 제가 아는 전부입니다. 그들은 집 안의 살아 있는 모든 것을, 말 못 하는 가축까지도 몰아내고, 대문을 봉인했습니다. 어머니가 돌아오지 못하게 하려는 것이었습니다. 저 역시 어머니의 소식을 구합니다. 오, 한마디만이라도! 적어도 그분은 죄가 없었습니다. 저는 용서할 수 있습니다—하나 용서를 빕니다, 고귀한 호민관님! 저 같은 노예가 용서나 복수를 입에 담아서는 안 되겠지요. 저는 평생 노에 매인 몸이니까요.\"\n\n아리우스는 골똘히 귀를 기울였다. 노예들을 다뤄 온 모든 경험을 끌어모았다. 만일 이번 경우에 드러난 감정이 꾸며 낸 것이라면, 그 연기는 완벽했다. 반대로 그것이 진실이라면, 유대인의 무죄는 의심할 수 없을 터였다. 그리고 그가 무죄라면, 권력이 얼마나 눈먼 광기로 휘둘러졌단 말인가! 한낱 우연한 사고를 갚으려고 한 가문 전체를 깡그리 지워 버리다니! 그 생각에 그는 충격을 받았다.\n\n우리의 생업이 아무리 거칠거나 피비린내 나는 것이라 해도 우리를 도덕적으로 닳아 없애지 못한다는 것, 정의와 자비 같은 자질이 진정 우리 안에 깃들어 있다면 그것들이 눈 아래 피어 있는 꽃처럼 그 밑에서 계속 살아간다는 것보다 더 지혜로운 섭리는 없다. 호민관은 가차 없을 수도 있었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그의 직분이 요구하는 관례에 맞지 않았을 것이다. 그는 또한 공정할 수도 있었다. 그러니 그의 불의에 대한 감각을 일깨우는 것은 곧 그를 그 불의를 바로잡는 길로 들어서게 하는 것이었다. 그가 복무한 배들의 선원들은 얼마 뒤 그를 가리켜 선량한 호민관이라 일컫게 되었다. 예리한 독자라면 그의 성품에 대해 이보다 나은 정의를 바라지 않을 것이다.\n\n이번 경우에는 분명 그 젊은이에게 유리한 정황이 많았고, 짐작으로 헤아릴 만한 것도 몇 가지 있었다. 어쩌면 아리우스는 발레리우스 그라투스를 알면서도 좋아하지 않았는지 모른다. 어쩌면 그는 늙은 후르를 알고 있었는지도 모른다. 유다는 호소하는 가운데 그에게 그 점을 물었으나, 눈여겨보았다시피 그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n이번만큼은 호민관도 어쩔 줄을 몰라 망설였다. 그의 권한은 충분했다. 그는 이 배의 군주였다. 그의 모든 선입견이 그를 자비 쪽으로 이끌었다. 그의 믿음은 이미 얻어졌다. 그럼에도 그는 마음속으로 생각했다. 서두를 일은 아니다—아니, 오히려 키테라로 가는 데에는 서둘러야 한다. 그러니 지금은 가장 뛰어난 노잡이를 내줄 수 없다. 기다리리라. 더 알아보리라. 적어도 이자가 정녕 후르 가문의 제후인지, 또 올바른 성품을 지녔는지 확인하리라. 노예란 으레 거짓말쟁이였으니까.\n\n\"이만하면 되었다.\" 그가 소리 내어 말했다. \"네 자리로 돌아가라.\"\n\n벤허는 고개를 숙였다. 주인의 얼굴을 다시 한번 들여다보았으나, 희망을 줄 만한 것은 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그는 천천히 몸을 돌렸다가, 다시 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"호민관님께서 저를 다시 떠올리실 때가 있거든, 제가 오직 제 식구들—어머니와 누이의 소식만을 청했음을 마음에서 잊지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n그는 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n아리우스는 감탄 어린 눈으로 그를 뒤따라 바라보았다.\n\n\"페르폴!\" 그는 속으로 생각했다. \"가르치기만 한다면, 검투장에 얼마나 어울리는 사내인가! 얼마나 잘 달리는가! 신들이여! 검이나 케스투스를 쥐기에 얼마나 좋은 팔인가!—멈춰라!\" 그가 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n벤허가 걸음을 멈추자, 호민관이 그에게로 다가갔다.\n\n\"네가 자유의 몸이라면 무엇을 하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"고귀한 아리우스님께서 저를 놀리시는군요!\" 유다가 입술을 떨며 말했다.\n\n\"아니다. 신들에게 맹세코, 아니다!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 기꺼이 대답하겠습니다. 저는 삶의 무엇보다 먼저 의무에 저를 바치겠습니다. 다른 것은 알지 않겠습니다. 어머니와 티르자를 집으로 되찾아 올 때까지는 안식을 모르겠습니다. 그들의 행복을 위해 하루하루, 한 시간 한 시간을 다 바치겠습니다. 그들을 받들겠습니다. 그보다 더 충실한 종은 없을 것입니다. 그들은 많은 것을 잃었으나, 제 조상의 하느님께 맹세코, 저는 그들에게 더 많은 것을 찾아 주겠습니다!\"\n\n그 대답은 로마인이 예상하지 못한 것이었다. 한순간 그는 자신의 의도를 잃어버렸다.\n\n\"나는 네 야망을 두고 물은 것이다.\" 그가 마음을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"만일 네 어머니와 누이가 죽었거나 찾을 수 없게 되었다면, 너는 무엇을 하겠느냐?\"\n\n벤허의 얼굴에 또렷한 창백함이 번졌고, 그는 바다 너머를 바라보았다. 어떤 강한 감정과의 싸움이 있었다. 그것을 이겨 내고 나서, 그는 호민관에게로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"제가 어떤 길을 좇겠느냐는 말씀이신지요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\"호민관님, 솔직히 말씀드리겠습니다. 제가 말씀드린 그 끔찍한 날 바로 전날 밤에, 저는 군인이 되도 좋다는 허락을 받았습니다. 지금도 같은 마음입니다. 그리고 온 땅에 전쟁을 배우는 학교는 오직 하나뿐이니, 저는 그곳으로 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"팔라이스트라 말이냐!\" 아리우스가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 로마의 진영입니다.\"\n\n\"그러나 너는 먼저 무기 다루는 법부터 익혀야 한다.\"\n\n그런데 주인이 노예에게 충고하는 것은 결코 안전한 일이 아니다. 아리우스는 자신의 경솔함을 깨닫고, 단숨에 목소리와 태도를 차갑게 굳혔다.\n\n\"이제 가거라.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리 사이에 오간 말을 두고 무엇을 쌓아 올리지는 말아라. 어쩌면 나는 그저 너를 가지고 노는 것일 뿐인지도 모른다. 아니면\"—그는 생각에 잠긴 듯 시선을 돌렸다—\"아니면, 네가 그것을 두고 조금이라도 희망을 품는다면, 검투사의 명성과 군인의 복무 가운데 하나를 골라라. 앞의 것은 황제의 총애에서 나올 수 있다. 뒤의 것에는 네게 돌아갈 보상이 없다. 너는 로마인이 아니니까. 가거라!\"\n\n얼마 지나지 않아 벤허는 다시 자기 자리에 앉아 있었다.\n\n마음이 가벼우면 사람의 일이란 언제나 수월한 법이다. 노를 다루는 것이 유다에게 그리 고되게 느껴지지 않았다. 한 가닥 희망이, 노래하는 새처럼 그에게 찾아왔다. 그는 그 손님을 거의 보지도 못했고 그 노래를 듣지도 못했다. 그러나 그것이 거기 있다는 것만은 알았다. 그의 느낌이 그렇게 일러 주었다. \"어쩌면 나는 그저 너를 가지고 노는 것일 뿐인지도 모른다\"라던 호민관의 경고는 떠오를 때마다 번번이 떨쳐 버렸다. 그 위대한 사람에게 불려 가 자기 이야기를 청해 받았다는 사실, 그것이 그의 굶주린 영혼을 먹이는 빵이었다. 분명 거기서 무언가 좋은 일이 나오리라. 그의 자리를 둘러싼 빛은 약속으로 맑고 환했으니, 그는 기도했다.\n\n\"오, 하느님! 저는 당신이 그토록 사랑하신 이스라엘의 참된 아들입니다! 비오니, 저를 도와주소서!\"\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n키테라섬 동쪽 안테모나만에 백 척의 갤리선이 집결했다. 그곳에서 호민관은 하루를 점검에 바쳤다. 그런 다음 그는 그리스와 아시아 해안 한가운데, 마치 큰길 복판에 박아 놓은 거대한 돌처럼 자리한 키클라데스 제도에서 가장 큰 섬 낙소스로 배를 몰았다. 그곳에서라면 지나는 모든 것을 검문할 수 있었고, 동시에 해적들이 에게해에 있든 지중해 바깥에 나가 있든 즉시 그들을 뒤쫓을 수 있는 위치를 차지하게 되는 셈이었다.\n\n함대가 대열을 이루어 섬의 산악 해안을 향해 노를 저어 들어가고 있을 때, 북쪽에서 다가오는 갤리선 한 척이 눈에 띄었다. 아리우스는 그 배를 맞으러 나갔다. 알고 보니 비잔티움에서 막 떠나온 수송선이었고, 그 선장에게서 그는 가장 필요로 하던 자세한 소식을 들을 수 있었다.\n\n해적들은 흑해의 더 먼 모든 해안에서 모여든 자들이었다. 팔루스 마이오티스에 물을 댄다고 여겨지던 강어귀의 타나이스조차도 그들 가운데 끼어 있었다. 그들의 준비는 더할 나위 없이 은밀하게 이루어졌다. 그들의 존재가 처음 알려진 것은 트라키아 보스포루스 입구 앞바다에 그 모습이 나타나면서였고, 곧이어 그곳에 주둔하던 함대가 격파되었다. 그곳에서 헬레스폰토스 출구에 이르기까지 물 위에 떠 있던 모든 것이 그들의 먹이가 되었다. 그 선단에는 갤리선이 족히 예순 척은 되었고, 하나같이 사람과 물자를 잘 갖추고 있었다. 몇 척은 이단노선이고, 나머지는 튼튼한 삼단노선이었다. 지휘는 한 그리스인이 맡았고, 동방의 모든 바다에 훤하다는 키잡이들 또한 그리스인이었다. 약탈한 재물은 헤아릴 수 없었다. 그러니 공포는 바다에만 머물지 않았다. 도시들은 성문을 닫아걸고 밤마다 백성을 성벽으로 내보냈다. 교역은 거의 끊기다시피 했다.\n\n그렇다면 해적들은 지금 어디에 있는가?\n\n아리우스에게 가장 중요한 이 물음에 그는 답을 얻었다.\n\n적은 렘노스섬의 헤파이스티아를 약탈한 뒤 테살리아 군도로 건너갔고, 마지막으로 들어온 소식에 따르면 에우보이아와 헬라스 사이의 만으로 자취를 감추었다고 했다.\n\n이런 소식이었다.\n\n그러자 백 척의 배가 하나의 선단을 이루어 질주하는 보기 드문 광경에 이끌려 산꼭대기로 모여든 섬사람들은, 선두 분대가 갑자기 북쪽으로 방향을 트는 모습과 다른 배들이 그 뒤를 따라 마치 종대의 기병처럼 같은 지점에서 선회하는 모습을 지켜보았다. 해적의 습격 소식은 그들에게도 닿아 있었고, 이제 레네와 시로스 사이로 그 흰 돛이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 바라보던 사려 깊은 이들은 위안을 얻고 고마워했다. 로마는 강한 손으로 움켜쥔 것을 언제나 지켜 냈다. 세금을 받는 대신 그들에게 안전을 베풀었던 것이다.\n\n호민관은 적의 움직임에 만족하는 정도가 아니었다. 그는 운명의 여신에게 거듭 감사했다. 여신은 빠르고 확실한 정보를 가져다주었고, 적들을 다른 어느 곳보다도 파멸이 가장 확실하게 보장된 바다로 꾀어들였다. 그는 갤리선 한 척이 지중해처럼 너른 바다에서 얼마나 큰 화를 입힐 수 있는지, 또 그것을 찾아내고 따라잡기가 얼마나 어려운지 알고 있었다. 그는 또한 단 한 번의 일격으로 해적 무리 전체를 끝장낼 수만 있다면, 바로 그러한 사정이 그 공훈과 영광을 얼마나 드높여 줄지도 알고 있었다.\n\n독자가 그리스와 에게해의 지도를 펼쳐 본다면, 에우보이아섬이 마치 아시아를 막는 성벽처럼 고전적 해안을 따라 길게 누워 있는 모습을 보게 될 것이다. 그 섬과 대륙 사이에는 길이가 족히 백이십 마일에 이르고 너비는 평균 팔 마일이 채 못 되는 해협이 나 있다. 북쪽 어귀는 일찍이 크세르크세스의 함대를 받아들인 적이 있었고, 이제 흑해에서 온 대담한 침략자들을 받아들였다. 펠라스기아만과 멜리아만을 따라 늘어선 도시들은 부유했고, 그곳에서 거둘 약탈물은 사람을 흘릴 만했다. 그러므로 모든 정황을 헤아린 끝에 아리우스는 도적들이 테르모필레 아래 어딘가에 있으리라고 판단했다. 그 기회를 반기며 그는 그들을 남북에서 에워싸기로 결심했고, 그러자면 한시도 지체할 수 없었다. 낙소스의 과일도 포도주도 여인들도 뒤에 남겨 두어야 했다. 그리하여 그는 멈추지도 침로를 바꾸지도 않고 배를 몰아, 마침내 해 질 무렵 조금 못 미쳐 오카산이 하늘을 배경으로 솟아오른 것이 보였고 키잡이가 에우보이아 해안이라고 알려 왔다.\n\n신호가 떨어지자 함대는 노를 멈추고 쉬었다. 다시 움직이기 시작하자 아리우스는 갤리선 쉰 척의 분대를 이끌고 해협을 거슬러 올라가려 했고, 그에 못지않게 강한 또 한 분대는 뱃머리를 섬의 바깥쪽, 곧 바다 쪽으로 돌려, 위쪽 어귀까지 한껏 서둘러 가서 물길을 쓸어내리며 내려오라는 명령을 받았다.\n\n물론 어느 분대도 수에서는 해적에 미치지 못했다. 그러나 저마다 그것을 메울 이점이 있었으니, 그 가운데 결코 작지 않은 것이 아무리 용맹할지언정 무법한 무리에게는 불가능한 군기였다. 게다가 만에 하나 한쪽이 패한다 해도 다른 한쪽은 승리로 만신창이가 된 적을 만나 손쉽게 제압할 형편이 되리라는 것이, 호민관 편에서 보면 빈틈없는 계산이었다.\n\n그동안 벤허는 자기 자리를 지키며 여섯 시간마다 교대했다. 안테모나만에서의 휴식이 그를 새로 기운 차리게 해 준 덕에 노 젓는 일도 그리 힘들지 않았고, 단상의 우두머리도 트집 잡을 데가 없었다.\n\n사람들은 대개 자신이 어디에 있고 어디로 가는지를 아는 데서 오는 마음의 편안함을 알지 못한다. 길을 잃었다는 느낌은 견디기 힘든 고통이다. 그보다 더 나쁜 것은 알지 못하는 곳으로 눈먼 채 내몰리는 느낌이다. 벤허에게는 그런 느낌이 습관으로 무뎌져 있었으나, 어느 정도까지만 그러했다. 시간이 흐르고 흐르도록, 때로는 밤낮을 연달아 노를 저으면서, 갤리선이 너른 바다의 수많은 항로 가운데 어느 하나를 따라 빠르게 미끄러져 가고 있음을 줄곧 의식하던 그에게, 자신이 어디에 있고 어디로 가는지 알고 싶은 갈망은 늘 함께 있었다. 그러나 이제 그 갈망은 호민관과 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 그의 가슴속에 새로 살아난 희망에 떠밀려 한결 날카로워진 듯했다. 머무는 곳이 좁으면 좁을수록 그 갈망은 더 강렬해지는 법이니, 그도 그러했다. 그는 힘겹게 나아가는 배의 온갖 소리를 하나하나 듣는 듯했고, 그 소리 하나하나가 자기에게 무언가를 일러 주러 온 목소리이기라도 한 듯 귀를 기울였다. 그는 머리 위의 격자창을 올려다보고, 그 사이로 새어 드는, 자기 몫이라고는 그토록 적은 빛 속을 들여다보며, 무엇인지도 모를 무엇을 기다렸다. 그러다 여러 번, 단상의 우두머리에게 말을 걸고 싶은 충동에 굴복하려는 자신을 깨닫고 멈칫했다. 전투의 어떤 정황도 그런 일만큼 그 위엄 있는 자를 놀라게 하지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n오랜 복역 동안 그는, 배가 움직일 때 선실 바닥에 어른거리는 가느다란 햇살이 옮겨 가는 것을 지켜봄으로써, 배가 대체로 어느 방향으로 항해하는지를 가늠하게 되었다. 물론 이것은 운명의 여신이 호민관에게 보내 주고 있는 것과 같은 맑은 날에나 가능한 일이었다. 키테라를 떠난 뒤 이어진 동안에도 그 경험은 그를 저버리지 않았다. 그들이 옛 유대 땅을 향해 가고 있다고 여기던 그는 침로의 변화라면 무엇 하나에도 예민했다. 앞서 본 대로 낙소스 가까이에서 일어난 그 갑작스러운 북향에 그는 가슴이 저리도록 마음 졸였다. 그러나 그 까닭은 짐작조차 할 수 없었다. 기억해 둘 것은, 그가 동료 노예들과 마찬가지로 형편을 전혀 모르고 항해에는 아무 관심도 없었다는 사실이다. 그의 자리는 노 옆이었고, 닻을 내리든 돛을 펴든 그는 거기에 가차 없이 붙들려 있었다. 삼 년 동안 단 한 번 그는 갑판에서 바깥을 내다보도록 허락받았을 뿐이다. 그 경우는 우리가 이미 보았다. 그는 자신이 함께 몰아가는 배의 뒤를 따라 거대한 선단이 바로 곁에서 아름다운 대열을 이루며 따라오고 있다는 것을 짐작도 못 했다. 그 선단이 무엇을 쫓고 있는지는 더더욱 알지 못했다.\n\n해가 기울며 선실에서 마지막 빛줄기를 거두어 갈 때에도 갤리선은 여전히 북쪽으로 나아가고 있었다. 밤이 내렸으나 벤허는 아무런 변화도 알아챌 수 없었다. 그 무렵 갑판에서 통로를 따라 향 냄새가 흘러 내려왔다.\n\n\"호민관이 제단 앞에 있구나.\" 그는 생각했다. \"혹시 우리가 전투에 들어가는 것일까?\"\n\n그는 주의를 곤두세웠다.\n\n지금껏 그는 숱한 전투를 겪었으나 단 한 번도 그것을 본 적은 없었다. 자기 자리에서 그는 머리 위와 사방에서 벌어지는 전투 소리를 들었고, 마침내 그 온갖 가락에, 마치 가수가 노래에 익숙해지듯 훤해졌다. 그렇게 그는 교전에 앞서는 여러 절차에도 익숙해졌는데, 로마인이든 그리스인이든 그 가운데 가장 변함없는 것이 신들에게 바치는 제사였다. 그 의식은 항해를 시작할 때 치르는 것과 같았으니, 그것을 알아챌 때면 그에게는 늘 하나의 경고였다.\n\n짚어 둘 것은, 전투란 그와 노 젓는 동료 노예들에게는 선원이나 해병과는 다른 의미를 지녔다는 점이다. 그것은 맞닥뜨릴 위험에서 오는 것이 아니라, 패하더라도 살아남으면 처지가 바뀔 수도 있다는—어쩌면 자유를, 적어도 더 나아질지도 모를 주인의 교체를 가져올 수도 있다는 사실에서 오는 것이었다.\n\n때가 되자 등불이 켜져 계단 곁에 매달렸고, 호민관이 갑판에서 내려왔다. 그의 말 한마디에 해병들이 갑옷을 걸쳤다. 다시 그의 말 한마디에 투석기들이 점검되었고, 창과 투창과 화살이 큼직한 다발로 운반되어 바닥에 놓였다. 함께 인화성 기름 항아리와 양초 심지처럼 느슨하게 감은 솜뭉치를 담은 바구니도 놓였다. 그리고 마침내 벤허가 호민관이 단상에 올라 갑옷을 입고 투구와 방패를 꺼내는 것을 보았을 때, 이 준비의 뜻은 더 이상 의심할 수 없게 되었고, 그는 복역에서 마지막으로 겪을 치욕에 대비했다.\n\n자리마다 붙박이로 무거운 발고리가 달린 사슬이 하나씩 매여 있었다. 호르타토르는 번호를 따라 차례차례 옮겨 가며 노잡이들에게 이것을 채워, 따르는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없게 하고, 재앙이 닥치더라도 달아날 길을 없애 버렸다.\n\n그러자 선실에는 침묵이 내려앉았고, 처음에는 가죽집 안에서 돌아가는 노의 삐걱거리는 소리만이 그 침묵을 깰 뿐이었다. 자리에 앉은 모든 이가 그 수치를 느꼈고, 벤허는 누구보다도 사무치게 느꼈다. 그는 무슨 값을 치러서라도 그것을 떨쳐 버리고 싶었다. 이내 절그럭거리는 족쇄 소리가, 우두머리가 한 바퀴 도는 걸음이 어디까지 이르렀는지 그에게 알려 주었다. 차례가 되면 그자가 자기에게도 올 터였다. 그러나 호민관이 그를 위해 나서 주지는 않을까?\n\n이 생각은 독자가 마음대로 허영이든 이기심이든 부를 수 있을 것이다. 어쨌든 그 순간 그것은 벤허를 사로잡았다. 그는 로마인이 나서 주리라고 믿었다. 어찌 됐든 이 정황은 그 사람의 속마음을 시험해 볼 일이었다. 전투에 골몰해 있으면서도 자기를 떠올려 준다면, 그것은 그가 품은 짐작이 옳다는 증거가—비참 속의 동료들보다 자기가 은연중에 높여졌다는 증거가, 희망을 품어도 좋다고 정당화해 줄 증거가 될 터였다.\n\n벤허는 애타게 기다렸다. 그 동안이 한 시대처럼 길게 느껴졌다. 노를 한 번 저을 때마다 그는 호민관 쪽을 바라보았다. 호민관은 그 간단한 준비를 마치고 침상에 누워 쉴 채비를 했다. 그러자 육십 번은 스스로를 꾸짖고 쓴웃음을 지으며, 다시는 그쪽을 보지 않으리라 마음먹었다.\n\n호르타토르가 다가왔다. 이제 그자는 일 번에 이르렀다. 쇠고리가 절그럭거리는 소리가 끔찍하게 울렸다. 마침내 육십 번이었다! 절망에서 비롯한 평정으로 벤허는 노를 멈추어 놓고 그 관리에게 발을 내밀었다. 그때 호민관이 움직이더니—몸을 일으켜—우두머리를 손짓으로 불렀다.\n\n유대인은 격한 반전에 사로잡혔다. 호르타토르에게서 그 위대한 사람이 그에게로 눈길을 옮겼다. 그가 노를 놓자 그쪽 배의 그 구역 전체가 환히 빛나는 듯했다. 무슨 말이 오갔는지 그는 하나도 듣지 못했다. 자기 자리의 죔쇠에 사슬이 부질없이 늘어진 채 매달려 있다는 것, 그리고 우두머리가 자기 자리로 돌아가 박자판을 두드리기 시작했다는 것으로 충분했다. 그 망치 소리가 그토록 음악처럼 들린 적은 없었다. 가슴을 납으로 묵직한 손잡이에 대고 그는 온 힘을 다해 밀었다. 노깃이 곧 부러질 듯 휘어질 때까지 밀어붙였다.\n\n우두머리가 호민관에게 가서 빙긋이 웃으며 육십 번을 가리켰다.\n\n\"이런 힘이!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 이런 기개라니!\" 호민관이 대답했다. \"맙소사! 저자는 쇠고리가 없는 편이 낫겠다. 다시는 채우지 마라.\"\n\n그렇게 말하고 그는 다시 침상에 몸을 뉘었다.\n\n배는 시간이 가고 또 가도록 바람에 잔물결조차 거의 일지 않는 물 위를 노에 의지해 나아갔다. 그리고 근무가 없는 자들은 잠을 잤다. 아리우스는 제자리에서, 해병들은 바닥에서.\n\n한 번—두 번—벤허는 교대했다. 그러나 그는 잠들 수 없었다. 삼 년에 걸친 밤, 그 어둠을 뚫고 마침내 한 줄기 햇살이! 바다 위에 떠돌며 길을 잃었더니, 이제 뭍이! 그토록 오래 죽어 있었더니, 보라, 부활의 전율과 설렘이! 잠은 이런 때를 위한 것이 아니었다. 희망은 미래를 다룬다. 지금과 과거는 충동과 암시적 정황으로 그 희망을 받드는 종일 뿐이다. 호민관의 호의에서 출발하여, 희망은 그를 한없이 앞으로 실어 갔다. 놀라운 것은, 희망이 우리에게 가리켜 보이는 결과처럼 순전히 상상에 지나지 않는 것들이 우리를 그토록 행복하게 만들 수 있다는 사실이 아니라, 우리가 그것들을 그토록 실제처럼 받아들일 수 있다는 사실이다. 그것들은 진홍빛과 자줏빛과 황금빛 아래, 그 영향에 잠겨 이성이 잠시 누워 사라져 버리게 만드는 화려한 양귀비꽃 같은 것임에 틀림없다. 슬픔이 가시고, 집과 가문의 운수가 되살아나고, 어머니와 누이가 다시금 그의 품에 안기는 것—이런 것들이 그 순간 그를 그 어느 때보다 행복하게 만든 핵심의 상념들이었다. 자신이 날개를 단 듯 끔찍한 전투를 향해 달려가고 있다는 사실은 그때만큼은 그의 생각과 아무런 상관이 없었다. 이렇게 희망 속에 품은 것들에는 의심이 섞여 있지 않았다. 그것들은 정말로 있었다. 그러기에 그 기쁨은 그토록 가득하고 그토록 완전하여, 그의 가슴속에는 복수가 들어설 자리가 없었다. 메살라, 그라투스, 로마, 그리고 그들에게 얽힌 쓰라리고 격한 온갖 기억은 죽어 버린 역병 같은 것이었다—그가 멀리, 안전하게 그 위에 떠올라 노래하는 별들의 소리에 귀 기울이는, 대지의 독기 같은 것이었다.\n\n동트기 전의 더 짙은 어둠이 물 위에 깔려 있었고, 아스트로이아호의 모든 것이 순조로웠다. 그때 한 사내가 갑판에서 내려와 호민관이 잠든 단상으로 잰걸음으로 다가가 그를 깨웠다. 아리우스는 일어나 투구와 검과 방패를 갖추고 해병 지휘관에게로 갔다.\n\n\"해적이 가까이 있다. 일어나 채비하라!\" 그가 말하고는 계단으로 향했다. 어찌나 침착하고 자신만만한지, 누군가는 이렇게 생각했을 법했다. \"복도 많은 사람이로군! 아피키우스가 잔칫상을 차려 놓았나 보다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n배 안에 있던 모든 영혼이, 아니 배까지도 깨어났다. 장교들은 저마다 위치로 갔다. 해병들은 무장을 갖추고 끌려 나왔는데, 그 모습이 어느 모로 보나 군단병과 다름없었다. 화살 다발과 투창 묶음이 갑판으로 운반되었다. 가운데 계단 옆에는 기름통과 화구(火球)가 쓸 수 있도록 준비되었다. 등잔이 더 밝혀졌다. 양동이마다 물이 채워졌다. 교대 차례의 노잡이들은 감시병에 둘러싸여 노예감독 앞에 모였다. 천우신조로, 벤허도 그 가운데 하나였다. 머리 위에서 그는 막바지 준비의 둔탁한 소리를 들었다. 선원들이 돛을 접고, 그물을 펼치고, 기계를 풀어 놓고, 황소 가죽 갑옷을 뱃전에 늘어뜨리는 소리였다. 이윽고 갤리선 둘레에 다시 고요가 내려앉았다. 막연한 공포와 기대로 가득 찬 고요였으니, 그 뜻을 풀이하자면 곧 준비 완료였다.\n\n갑판에서 신호가 내려와, 계단에 배치된 하급 장교를 거쳐 노예감독에게 전해지자, 별안간 모든 노가 멈추었다.\n\n무슨 뜻인가?\n\n긴 의자에 사슬로 묶인 백스무 명의 노예 가운데, 그 물음을 스스로에게 던지지 않은 자는 하나도 없었다. 그들에게는 어떤 자극도 없었다. 애국심도, 명예욕도, 의무감도, 그들에게 아무런 영감을 주지 못했다. 그들은 속수무책으로 눈먼 채 위험 속으로 떠밀려 가는 사람들이 흔히 느끼는 전율을 느꼈다. 그 가운데 가장 둔한 자라 해도, 노를 매만지며, 앞으로 닥칠 모든 일을 떠올리되 그 무엇도 스스로에게 약속하지 못했으리라. 승리한들 그의 사슬은 더욱 단단히 죄어질 뿐이요, 배의 운명이 곧 그의 운명이었으니, 가라앉든 불타든 그는 배와 함께할 수밖에 없는 처지였다.\n\n바깥 사정이야 그들이 물을 수도 없었다. 적은 또 누구인가? 만일 그들이 벗이요, 형제요, 동포라면 어찌할 것인가? 독자가 이 생각을 한 걸음 더 밀고 나가 보면, 로마인이 그런 위급한 때에 이 가엾은 자들을 자리에 묶어 두던 그 불가피함을 헤아릴 수 있을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그들에게 그런 생각에 잠길 겨를은 거의 없었다. 고물 쪽에서 갤리선이 노 젓는 듯한 소리가 벤허의 귀를 끌었고, 아스트로이아호는 사방에서 부딪쳐 오는 물결 한가운데 든 것처럼 흔들렸다. 함대가 가까이 있다는 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다. 진형을 펼치는 함대, 어쩌면 공격을 위해 대오를 짜는 함대였다. 그 상상에 그의 피가 뛰기 시작했다.\n\n또 하나의 신호가 갑판에서 내려왔다. 노가 물에 잠기고, 갤리선은 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 천천히 움직이기 시작했다. 밖에서도 안에서도 아무 소리 없었으나, 선실 안의 사람들은 저마다 본능적으로 충격에 대비해 몸을 가다듬었다. 배마저 그 기운을 알아챈 듯, 숨을 죽이고, 호랑이처럼 몸을 웅크린 채 나아가는 것 같았다.\n\n이런 상황에서는 시간이 가늠되지 않는 법이라, 벤허는 얼마나 멀리 왔는지 도무지 판단할 수 없었다. 마침내 갑판에서 나팔 소리가 났다. 가득 차고, 또렷하고, 길게 불어 내는 소리였다. 노예감독은 울림판이 쩌렁쩌렁 울릴 때까지 두드렸다. 노잡이들은 몸을 한껏 앞으로 뻗어, 노를 더 깊이 박고는, 일제히 온 힘을 모아 별안간 잡아당겼다. 갤리선은 모든 목재가 떨리도록 진동하며 한 번 도약으로 응답했다. 다른 나팔들이 그 소란에 합세했다. 모두 뒤쪽에서였고, 앞쪽에서는 하나도 없었다. 앞쪽에서는 다만 아우성치는 목소리가 잠시 솟아올랐을 뿐이었다. 거대한 충격이 일었다. 노예감독의 단상 앞에 있던 노잡이들이 비틀거렸고, 더러는 쓰러졌다. 배는 뒤로 튕겼다가, 자세를 되찾고, 이전보다 더 거역할 수 없는 기세로 돌진했다. 공포에 질린 사람들의 비명이 날카롭고 높게 솟아올랐다. 나팔 소리 너머로, 충돌의 갈리고 부서지는 소리 너머로 그것이 솟아올랐다. 그러더니 그의 발밑에서, 용골 아래에서, 짓이기고, 우르릉거리고, 산산이 부서지고, 가라앉으며, 벤허는 무언가가 짓밟혀 넘어가는 것을 느꼈다. 그를 둘러싼 사람들이 두려워하며 서로를 바라보았다. 갑판에서 승리의 함성이 울렸다. 로마의 충각이 이긴 것이다! 그러나 바다가 삼켜 버린 자들은 누구였던가? 어느 말을 쓰고, 어느 땅에서 온 자들이던가?\n\n멈춤도 없고, 머무름도 없었다! 아스트로이아호는 앞으로 돌진했다. 나아가는 동안, 몇몇 선원이 아래로 달려 내려와, 솜뭉치를 기름통에 담갔다가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 그것을 계단 머리의 동료들에게 던져 올렸다. 전투의 다른 온갖 끔찍함에 불까지 더해질 참이었다.\n\n곧 갤리선이 너무 기울어, 가장 높은 쪽의 노잡이들은 자리를 지키기조차 힘들었다. 다시 우렁찬 로마의 환호가 울렸고, 그와 더불어 절망에 찬 비명이 솟았다. 이물에서 휘둘러진 거대한 기중기의 갈고리에 걸린 적함 하나가, 떨어뜨려 가라앉히려는 듯 공중으로 들어 올려지고 있었다.\n\n오른편에서도 왼편에서도 함성이 커져 갔다. 앞에서도 뒤에서도 형언할 수 없는 소란이 부풀어 올랐다. 이따금 우지끈하는 소리에 뒤이어 별안간 공포의 비명이 터졌으니, 다른 배들이 들이받혀 그 선원들이 소용돌이에 빠져 죽었음을 알리는 소리였다.\n\n싸움이 한쪽으로만 흐른 것도 아니었다. 이따금 갑옷 입은 로마인이 승강구 아래로 실려 내려와, 피를 흘리며, 때로는 죽어 가며, 바닥에 뉘어졌다.\n\n또 이따금, 증기와 뒤섞인 연기가, 사람 살이 구워지는 역겨운 냄새를 풍기며 선실 안으로 쏟아져 들어, 어슴푸레해지던 빛을 누런 어둠으로 바꾸어 놓았다. 그동안 숨을 헐떡이며, 벤허는 자기들이 불타는 배의 연기구름을 지나고 있음을, 긴 의자에 사슬로 묶인 노잡이들과 함께 타 버린 배의 연기임을 알았다.\n\n아스트로이아호는 그 내내 움직이고 있었다. 그러더니 별안간 멈추었다. 앞쪽의 노가 노잡이들의 손에서 튕겨 나갔고, 노잡이들은 자리에서 튕겨 나갔다. 그때 갑판에서는 격렬한 발구르는 소리가 났고, 뱃전에서는 서로 부딪친 배들이 갈리는 소리가 났다. 처음으로 망치 두드리는 소리가 그 아우성 속에 묻혀 들리지 않았다. 사람들은 두려움에 바닥에 주저앉거나, 숨을 곳을 찾아 사방을 둘러보았다. 그 공황 한가운데로, 한 몸뚱이가 승강구 아래로 곤두박질치거나 내던져져, 벤허 가까이에 떨어졌다. 그는 반쯤 벌거벗은 그 시체를 보았다. 얼굴을 검게 뒤덮은 머리털 다발, 그 아래 황소 가죽과 버들가지로 엮은 방패가 있었다. 죽음이 약탈과 복수를 앗아 간, 북방의 흰 살갗을 가진 민족에서 온 야만인이었다. 어떻게 그가 여기 들어왔는가? 무쇠 같은 손이 그를 적함 갑판에서 낚아챈 것이다. 아니, 아스트로이아호가 적에게 점령당한 것이다! 로마인들이 제 갑판에서 싸우고 있는 것인가? 한기가 젊은 유대인을 덮쳤다. 아리우스가 곤경에 처해 있다, 어쩌면 제 목숨을 지키려 싸우고 있으리라. 만일 그가 살해된다면! 아브라함의 하나님, 막아 주소서! 그토록 얼마 전에야 찾아온 희망과 꿈이, 한낱 희망과 꿈에 지나지 않았던가? 어머니와 누이를, 집을, 고향을, 거룩한 땅을, 그는 끝내 보지 못한단 말인가? 소란이 머리 위에서 천둥처럼 울렸다. 그는 둘러보았다. 선실 안은 온통 혼란이었다. 긴 의자의 노잡이들은 마비된 채였고, 사람들은 눈먼 듯 이리저리 내달렸다. 오직 노예감독만이 제자리에 흔들림 없이 앉아, 부질없이 울림판을 두드리며 호민관의 명령을 기다리고 있었다. 붉은 어둠 속에서, 세계를 정복한 그 비길 데 없는 규율을 몸소 보여 주면서.\n\n그 본보기는 벤허에게 좋은 영향을 미쳤다. 그는 생각할 수 있을 만큼 마음을 다잡았다. 명예와 의무가 저 로마인을 단상에 붙들어 두는 것이나, 그때 자기가 그런 동기와 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가? 긴 의자는 도망쳐야 할 자리였다. 게다가 노예로 죽는다면, 그 희생으로 누가 덕을 보겠는가? 그에게는 명예는 아닐지언정 사는 것이 곧 의무였다. 그의 목숨은 그의 백성의 것이었다. 그들이 그 어느 때보다 생생하게 그의 앞에 떠올랐다. 그는 그들을, 뻗은 두 팔을 보았다. 그를 향해 애원하는 그들의 목소리를 들었다. 그리고 그는 그들에게 가리라. 그는 몸을 일으켰다가 멈추었다. 아아! 로마의 판결이 그를 형벌 속에 붙들어 매고 있었다. 그것이 유효한 한, 탈출은 헛수고일 뿐이었다. 넓디넓은 땅 어디에도 황제의 추궁에서 그가 안전할 곳은 없었다. 뭍에도 없고, 바다에도 없었다. 그가 법의 절차에 따른 자유를 얻어야만, 그래야만 유대에 머물러 제 한 몸 바치려는 효성의 뜻을 이룰 수 있었다. 다른 땅에서라면 그는 살지 않으리라. 사랑하는 하나님이여! 그가 그런 해방을 얼마나 기다리고, 지켜보고, 기도해 왔던가! 그런데 그것이 어찌나 더디었던가! 그러나 마침내 그는 호민관의 약속 속에서 그것을 보았다. 그 위대한 사람의 말이 달리 무슨 뜻이었겠는가? 그런데 그토록 뒤늦게 나타난 은인이 이제 살해된다면! 죽은 자는 산 자에게 한 언약을 갚으러 돌아오지 못한다. 그래서는 안 된다, 아리우스가 죽어서는 안 된다. 적어도, 갤리선 노예로 살아남느니 그와 함께 죽는 편이 나았다.\n\n벤허는 다시 한번 둘러보았다. 선실 지붕 위에서는 아직도 전투가 휘몰아쳤다. 뱃전에는 아직도 적함들이 부딪치고 갈렸다. 긴 의자에서 노예들은 사슬을 뜯어내려고 몸부림치다가, 헛수고임을 알고는 미친 사람처럼 울부짖었다. 감시병들은 위로 올라갔고, 규율은 사라지고, 공황이 들어찼다. 아니, 노예감독만은 의자를 지키며, 한결같이, 여느 때처럼 침착했다. 다만 망치 말고는 아무 무기가 없었다. 그는 부질없이 그 쇳소리로 소란이 잦아드는 틈을 메웠다. 벤허는 그에게 마지막 눈길을 던지고는, 박차고 나섰다. 도망치려는 것이 아니라, 호민관을 찾으려는 것이었다.\n\n그와 고물 쪽 승강구 계단 사이에는 아주 짧은 거리가 놓여 있었다. 그는 단숨에 도약해 그것을 가로질러, 계단을 반쯤 올랐다. 불빛으로 핏빛이 된 하늘과, 나란히 붙은 배들과, 배와 잔해로 뒤덮인 바다와, 키잡이 자리 둘레에 몰린 싸움을 흘끗 볼 만큼은 올랐다. 공격하는 자는 많고, 지키는 자는 적었다. 그때 별안간 발 디딘 곳이 떨어져 나가, 그는 뒤로 곤두박질쳤다. 바닥에 닿았을 때, 그것은 스스로 들썩이며 산산이 부서지는 듯했다. 그러더니, 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 선체의 고물 쪽 전부가 쪼개지면서, 마치 줄곧 노리고 있었던 듯, 바다가 쉭쉭거리고 거품을 일으키며 뛰어들었고, 벤허에게는 모든 것이 어둠과 솟구치는 물로 변했다.\n\n이 절박한 순간에 젊은 유대인이 스스로를 구했다고는 말할 수 없다. 평소의 힘에 더하여, 그에게는 자연이 바로 이런 목숨의 위기를 위해 따로 간직해 둔 그 막연한 여분의 힘이 있었다. 그런데도 어둠과, 휘몰아치고 포효하는 물이 그를 멍하게 만들었다. 숨을 참는 것조차 저도 모르게 한 일이었다.\n\n밀려드는 홍수는 그를 통나무처럼 선실 앞쪽으로 내던졌으니, 가라앉는 움직임이 만든 역류가 아니었다면 그는 거기서 빠져 죽었으리라. 하지만 실제로는, 수면 아래 여러 길 깊은 곳에서 그 텅 빈 덩어리가 그를 토해 냈고, 그는 풀려난 잔해와 함께 떠올랐다. 떠오르는 와중에 그는 무언가를 움켜쥐고, 거기 매달렸다. 그가 물밑에 잠겨 있던 시간은 실제보다 한 시대만큼이나 더 길게 느껴졌다. 마침내 그는 수면 위에 이르렀다. 크게 숨을 들이켜 폐를 다시 채우고는, 머리와 눈에서 물을 털어 내며, 붙잡은 널빤지 위로 더 높이 올라타 사방을 둘러보았다.\n\n죽음은 물결 아래에서 그를 바싹 뒤쫓았다. 떠올라 보니, 죽음은 그를 기다리고 있었다. 여러 모습으로 기다리고 있었다.\n\n연기가 반쯤 비치는 안개처럼 바다 위에 깔려 있었고, 그 사이로 군데군데 강렬하게 빛나는 불덩이가 비쳤다. 재빠른 분별로 그는 그것들이 불타는 배임을 알아차렸다. 전투는 아직 끝나지 않았으니, 누가 이겼는지 그는 말할 수 없었다. 그의 시야 안으로 이따금 배들이 지나가며, 불빛을 가로질러 그림자를 던졌다. 저 멀리 거무스름한 구름 속에서, 그는 다른 배들이 충돌하는 소리를 들었다. 그러나 위험은 더 가까이 있었다. 아스트로이아호가 가라앉을 때, 기억하다시피, 그 갑판에는 자기 배의 선원들과, 동시에 그 배를 공격한 두 갤리선의 선원들이 함께 있었으니, 그들 모두가 물에 휩쓸렸다. 그들 가운데 다수가 한꺼번에 수면으로 떠올라, 같은 널빤지나 그 무엇이든 붙들 것 위에서, 어쩌면 여러 길 아래 소용돌이 속에서 시작했을 싸움을 이어 갔다. 죽음의 포옹으로 뒤엉켜 몸부림치며, 때로는 칼이나 투창으로 내리치며, 그들은 둘레의 바다를 들끓게 했으니, 한 곳은 먹물처럼 까맣고, 다른 곳은 불의 반사로 활활 타올랐다. 그들의 몸부림과 그는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그들은 모두 그의 적이었다. 그가 떠 있는 널빤지를 차지하려고 그를 죽이려 들지 않을 자가 하나도 없었다. 그는 서둘러 멀어졌다.\n\n그 무렵 그는 더없이 빠르게 움직이는 노 소리를 듣고, 갤리선 한 척이 자기를 향해 내려오는 것을 보았다. 우뚝 솟은 이물은 갑절로 높아 보였고, 그 도금과 조각에 어리는 붉은 빛이 그것을 마치 살아 있는 뱀처럼 보이게 했다. 그 밑에서 물은 휘저어져 날리는 거품으로 변했다.\n\n그는 헤엄쳐 나아가며, 몹시 넓어 다루기 힘든 널빤지를 밀었다. 한순간이 귀했으니, 반 초가 그를 살리거나 잃게 할 수 있었다. 그 안간힘을 쓰던 고비에, 팔 닿을 만한 거리의 바다 속에서, 투구 하나가 금빛 섬광처럼 솟아올랐다. 이어서 손가락을 쫙 편 두 손이 나왔다. 크고 억센 손이었으니, 한번 붙들면 풀리지 않을 듯했다. 벤허는 질겁하여 거기서 비켜났다. 투구와 그것이 감싼 머리가 솟아올랐다. 이어 두 팔이, 미친 듯이 물을 두드리기 시작했다. 그 머리가 뒤로 젖혀지며, 얼굴을 빛 쪽으로 돌렸다. 입은 크게 벌어졌고, 두 눈은 떴으나 아무것도 보지 못했으며, 물에 빠져 죽어 가는 자의 핏기 없는 창백함이 어려 있었다. 그보다 더 섬뜩한 것은 없으리라! 그러나 그는 그 모습을 보고 기쁨의 외침을 토했으니, 그 얼굴이 다시 가라앉으려 할 때, 그는 투구에서 턱 밑으로 이어진 사슬을 잡아, 그 가엾은 사람을 널빤지 쪽으로 끌어당겼다.\n\n그 사람은 호민관 아리우스였다.\n\n한동안 물이 벤허 둘레에서 사납게 거품을 일으키며 소용돌이쳐, 떠 있는 것에 매달리는 동시에 로마인의 머리를 수면 위로 떠받치자니 그의 온 힘이 다 들었다. 갤리선은 지나갔으니, 두 사람은 그 노가 닿는 거리에서 가까스로 벗어나 있었다. 떠 있는 사람들 한가운데로, 투구 쓴 머리든 맨머리든 가리지 않고 넘어, 배는 돌진했고, 그 자취에는 불꽃으로 반짝이는 바다밖에 남지 않았다. 둔탁한 우지끈 소리에 이어 거대한 비명이 터지자, 구해 낸 이는 자기가 떠맡은 자에게서 눈을 들어 다시 바라보았다. 어떤 야성의 쾌감이 그의 마음을 스쳤다. 아스트로이아호의 원수가 갚아진 것이다.\n\n그 뒤로 전투는 멀어져 갔다. 저항은 도주로 바뀌었다. 그러나 승자는 누구였던가? 자기의 자유와 호민관의 목숨이 그 결과에 얼마나 달려 있는지 벤허는 절실히 느꼈다. 그는 널빤지를 아리우스 아래로 밀어 넣어 그를 떠받치게 한 뒤로는, 오로지 그를 거기 붙들어 두는 데에만 마음을 썼다. 새벽이 더디게 왔다. 그는 희망을 품고 그것이 밝아 오는 것을 지켜보았으나, 때로는 두려워하기도 했다. 그것이 로마인을 데려올 것인가, 아니면 해적을 데려올 것인가? 해적이라면, 그가 떠맡은 사람은 끝장이었다.\n\n마침내 아침이 활짝 밝았고, 바람 한 점 없었다. 왼편 저쪽으로 그는 뭍을 보았으나, 너무 멀어 거기까지 가 볼 엄두는 나지 않았다. 여기저기에 그 자신처럼 떠도는 사람들이 있었다. 군데군데 바다는 까맣게 탄, 때로는 연기 나는 잔해로 거뭇거뭇했다. 멀찍이 떨어진 갤리선 한 척이, 기울어진 활대에서 찢긴 돛을 늘어뜨린 채 노를 모두 멈추고 떠 있었다. 더 멀리에서는 움직이는 점들을 알아볼 수 있었는데, 도주하거나 추격하는 배일 수도 있고, 날개를 펼친 흰 새일 수도 있겠다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n그렇게 한 시간이 흘렀다. 그의 불안은 커져 갔다. 구원이 속히 오지 않으면, 아리우스는 죽으리라. 때때로 그는 어찌나 미동도 없이 누워 있던지 이미 죽은 듯했다. 그는 투구를 벗겨 내고, 이어 더 힘겹게 흉갑을 벗겼다. 심장은 가냘프게 떨고 있었다. 그는 그 기색에 희망을 얻어, 버텼다. 기다리는 것 말고는, 그리고 제 백성의 방식대로 기도하는 것 말고는, 할 수 있는 일이 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n물에 빠진 자가 다시 살아나는 고통은 익사 그 자체보다 더 괴롭다. 아리우스는 그 고통을 모두 겪었고, 마침내 벤허가 기뻐할 만큼 말을 할 수 있는 지경에 이르렀다.\n\n차츰, 자기가 어디에 있는지, 누가 어떻게 자신을 구했는지 두서없이 묻던 데서 시작하여, 그는 다시 그 해전으로 생각을 돌렸다. 승패에 대한 의구심이 그의 정신을 온전히 되살리도록 자극했고, 그 허약한 받침대 위에서나마 얻을 수 있는 오랜 휴식이 그 회복을 적지 않게 도왔다. 얼마 뒤 그는 말이 많아졌다.\n\n\"우리가 구조될지는 그 싸움의 결과에 달려 있는 모양이군. 자네가 나를 위해 무엇을 했는지도 알겠네. 솔직히 말해, 자네는 제 목숨을 걸고 내 목숨을 구했어. 나는 이것을 거리낌 없이 인정하네. 어떤 일이 닥치든 자네는 내 고마움을 받을 자격이 있어. 그뿐 아니라, 운명이 내게 자비를 베풀어 우리가 이 위험에서 무사히 벗어난다면, 나는 감사를 증명할 힘과 기회를 가진 로마인답게 자네에게 호의를 베풀겠네. 그러나, 그러나 자네의 선의가 정말로 내게 친절을 베푼 것인지는 아직 두고 보아야 할 일이야. 아니, 자네의 선의에 기대어 말하건대\"—그가 머뭇거렸다—\"나는 자네에게 어떤 경우에, 한 사람이 다른 사람에게 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 호의를 베풀어 달라는 약속을 받아 두고 싶네. 지금 그 맹세를 내게 다오.\"\n\n\"금지된 일이 아니라면 그렇게 하겠습니다.\" 벤허가 대답했다.\n\n아리우스는 다시 숨을 돌렸다.\n\n\"자네는 정말로 유대인 후르의 아들인가?\" 그가 다음으로 물었다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 그대로입니다.\"\n\n\"나는 자네 아버지를 알았네—\"\n\n유다는 더 가까이 몸을 끌어당겼다. 호민관의 목소리가 약했기 때문이다. 그는 더 가까이 다가가, 간절히 귀를 기울였다. 마침내 고향 소식을 들으리라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n\"나는 그를 알았고, 사랑했지.\" 아리우스가 말을 이었다.\n\n또 한 번의 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 무엇인가가 말하는 이의 생각을 다른 데로 돌려놓았다.\n\n\"자네가 그의 아들인데 카토와 브루투스를 들어 보지 못했을 리는 없지.\" 그가 계속했다. \"그들은 참으로 위대한 사람들이었고, 죽음에 임해서만큼 위대했던 적은 없었어. 그들은 죽으면서 이런 법을 남겼지—로마인은 제 행운보다 오래 살아서는 안 된다. 듣고 있나?\"\n\n\"듣고 있습니다.\"\n\"로마의 신사들은 반지를 끼는 관습이 있네. 내 손에도 하나 있지. 지금 이것을 받게.\"\n\n그는 유다에게 손을 내밀었고, 유다는 그가 청한 대로 했다.\n\n\"이제 자네 손에 끼게.\"\n\n벤허는 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"그 패물은 쓸모가 있어.\" 아리우스가 다음으로 말했다. \"내게는 재산과 돈이 있네. 로마에서도 부자로 꼽히지. 가족은 없어. 내가 없을 때 모든 것을 관리하는 내 해방노예에게 이 반지를 보이게. 미세눔 근처의 별장에 가면 그를 찾을 수 있을 거야. 이것이 어떻게 자네 손에 들어왔는지 말하고, 그가 가진 것이라면 무엇이든, 아니 전부라도 청하게. 그는 그 요구를 거절하지 않을 걸세. 내가 살아 있다면 자네에게 더 잘해 주겠네. 자네를 자유의 몸으로 만들어 고향과 동족에게 돌려보내 주지. 아니면 자네가 가장 좋아하는 일에 몸을 바칠 수도 있어. 듣고 있나?\"\n\n\"듣지 않을 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 내게 맹세하게. 신들의 이름으로—\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 훌륭하신 호민관님, 저는 유대인입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네의 하느님께 걸고, 아니면 자네 신앙을 가진 이들에게 가장 거룩한 형식으로—내가 지금 자네에게 이르는 일을, 내가 이르는 대로 하겠다고 맹세하게. 나는 기다리고 있네. 자네의 약속을 다오.\"\n\n\"고귀하신 아리우스님, 당신의 태도로 보아 무언가 더없이 중대한 일을 기대하라는 경고를 받은 듯합니다. 먼저 당신의 바람을 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그러면 약속하겠나?\"\n\n\"그건 맹세를 하는 셈이 되고— 제 조상의 하느님께 영광을! 저기 배 한 척이 옵니다!\"\n\n\"어느 쪽에서?\"\n\n\"북쪽에서입니다.\"\n\n\"겉모습으로 그 국적을 알 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 저는 노 젓는 일만 했으니까요.\"\n\n\"깃발은 있나?\"\n\n\"보이지 않습니다.\"\n\n아리우스는 한동안 잠자코 있었으니, 보아하니 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다.\n\n\"그 배가 아직도 이쪽으로 오나?\" 그가 마침내 물었다.\n\n\"여전히 이쪽으로 옵니다.\"\n\n\"이제 깃발을 살펴보게.\"\n\n\"깃발이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"다른 표지도 없나?\"\n\n\"돛을 하나 올렸고, 노가 삼단이며, 빠르게 다가옵니다—제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은 그뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"승전한 로마 배라면 깃발을 여러 개 내걸었을 텐데. 적임에 틀림없어. 자, 들어 보게.\" 아리우스가 다시 진지해지며 말했다. \"내가 아직 말할 수 있을 때 들어 두게. 저 갤리선이 해적선이라면 자네 목숨은 안전하네. 자유는 주지 않을 테고, 다시 노를 잡게 하겠지만, 죽이지는 않을 거야. 반면에 나는—\"\n\n호민관이 말을 멈칫거렸다.\n\n\"맹세코!\" 그가 단호하게 계속했다. \"나는 치욕에 굴복하기에는 너무 늙었네. 로마에서는 사람들이 이렇게 말하게 하라. 퀸투스 아리우스는 로마 호민관답게 적들 한가운데서 자기 배와 함께 가라앉았다고. 이것이 내가 자네에게 바라는 일이야. 저 갤리선이 해적선으로 밝혀지면, 나를 널빤지에서 밀어 떨어뜨려 빠뜨려라. 듣고 있나? 그렇게 하겠다고 맹세하게.\"\n\n\"맹세하지 않겠습니다.\" 벤허가 단호하게 말했다. \"그 일도 하지 않겠습니다. 제게 가장 큰 구속력을 지닌 율법은, 오 호민관님, 당신의 목숨에 대해 저를 책임지게 합니다. 반지를 도로 가져가십시오\"—그는 손가락에서 인장 반지를 빼냈다—\"이것을 도로 가져가시고, 이 위험에서 벗어났을 때 베풀겠다는 모든 호의의 약속도 거두십시오. 저를 종신토록 노에 매단 그 판결은 저를 노예로 만들었으나, 저는 노예가 아닙니다. 당신의 해방노예도 아닙니다. 저는 이스라엘의 아들이며, 적어도 지금 이 순간만큼은 제 자신의 주인입니다. 반지를 도로 가져가십시오.\"\n\n아리우스는 가만히 있었다.\n\n\"가져가지 않으시겠습니까?\" 유다가 계속했다. \"그러면 노여움에서도, 어떤 멸시에서도 아니라, 다만 가증스러운 의무에서 저를 풀어 놓기 위해, 당신의 선물을 바다에 던지겠습니다. 보십시오, 오 호민관님!\"\n\n그는 반지를 던져 버렸다. 아리우스는 보지 않았으나 반지가 떨어져 가라앉는 물소리를 들었다.\n\n\"자네는 어리석은 짓을 했어.\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 같은 처지에 놓인 자에게는 어리석은 짓이지. 나는 죽는 데 자네에게 기댈 필요가 없네. 목숨이란 자네 도움 없이도 끊을 수 있는 한 가닥 실이야. 그렇게 하면 자네는 어찌 되겠나? 죽기로 작정한 사람은 남의 손에 죽기를 더 좋아하는데, 까닭인즉 플라톤이 우리에게 일러 준 그 영혼이 자기 파괴라는 생각에 거역하기 때문이야. 그뿐이지. 저 배가 해적선이라면 나는 이 세상을 떠나겠네. 내 마음은 굳었어. 나는 로마인이야. 성공과 명예가 전부지. 그래도 나는 자네를 도우려 했건만, 자네가 마다했어. 그 반지는 이 처지에서 내 뜻을 증언할 수 있는 유일한 것이었네. 우리 둘 다 잃고 말았어. 나는 내게서 빼앗긴 승리와 영광을 한탄하며 죽을 것이고, 자네는 조금 더 살다가, 이 어리석음 때문에 다하지 못한 경건한 의무들을 슬퍼하며 죽겠지. 나는 자네가 가엾네.\"\n\n벤허는 자기 행동의 결과를 전보다 더 또렷이 보았으나, 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n\"제가 종살이한 삼 년 동안, 오 호민관님, 당신은 저를 친절하게 바라본 첫 번째 사람이었습니다. 아니, 아닙니다! 또 한 사람이 있었지요.\" 목소리가 잦아들고, 눈가가 촉촉해졌다. 그는 나사렛의 옛 우물가에서 자신에게 물을 마시게 해 준 그 소년의 얼굴을, 마치 지금 눈앞에 있는 듯 또렷이 보았다. \"적어도,\" 그가 계속했다. \"당신은 제가 누구인지를 물어 준 첫 번째 사람이었습니다. 그리고 제가 손을 뻗어, 눈이 멀어 마지막으로 가라앉는 당신을 붙잡았을 때, 비록 저 또한 제 비참한 처지에서 당신이 여러 모로 제게 쓸모가 있으리라는 생각을 했다 해도, 그 행동이 온통 이기적이기만 했던 것은 아닙니다. 이것만은 믿어 주시기를 빕니다. 게다가, 하느님께서 제게 알게 해 주시는 바로는, 제가 꿈꾸는 목적은 오직 정당한 수단으로만 이루어져야 합니다. 양심의 문제로서, 저는 당신을 죽이는 자가 되느니 차라리 당신과 함께 죽기를 택하겠습니다. 제 마음은 당신만큼이나 굳게 정해져 있습니다. 비록 당신이 제게 로마 전부를 주겠다 하고, 그것을 주는 일이 당신 손에 달려 있다 해도, 오 호민관님, 저는 당신을 죽이지 않겠습니다. 당신의 카토와 브루투스도, 유대인이 반드시 따라야 할 율법을 남긴 그 히브리 사람에 비하면 어린아이에 지나지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 부탁은. 자네는—\"\n\n\"당신의 명령이라면 더 무게가 있겠지요. 하지만 그것도 저를 움직이지는 못합니다. 저는 말했습니다.\"\n\n두 사람 다 잠잠해진 채, 기다렸다.\n\n벤허는 다가오는 배를 자주 바라보았다. 아리우스는 눈을 감은 채 무심히 쉬고 있었다.\n\n\"저 배가 적이라고 확신하십니까?\" 벤허가 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같네.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"배가 멈추고, 작은 보트 한 척을 뱃전 너머로 내립니다.\"\n\n\"깃발이 보이나?\"\n\n\"로마 배라면 알아볼 다른 표지는 없습니까?\"\n\n\"로마 배라면 돛대 꼭대기에 투구가 얹혀 있네.\"\n\n\"그러면 기운을 내십시오. 투구가 보입니다.\"\n\n그래도 아리우스는 안심하지 못했다.\n\n\"작은 보트에 탄 자들이 물에 떠 있는 사람들을 거두어들이고 있습니다. 해적은 자비롭지 않은데요.\"\n\n\"노 저을 자가 필요한지도 모르지.\" 아리우스가 대답했다. 어쩌면 그 자신이 그런 목적으로 사람들을 구한 적이 있던 시절을 떠올렸는지도 몰랐다.\n\n벤허는 낯선 자들의 움직임을 유심히 지켜보았다.\n\n\"배가 떠나갑니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"어디로?\"\n\n\"우리 오른편에 갤리선이 한 척 있는데, 버려진 것 같습니다. 새로 온 배가 그쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌립니다. 이제 그 옆에 붙었습니다. 이제 사람들을 그 배에 올려 보냅니다.\"\n\n그러자 아리우스가 눈을 뜨고 평정을 떨쳐 냈다.\n\n\"자네의 하느님께 감사하게.\" 갤리선들을 바라본 뒤 그가 벤허에게 말했다. \"내가 내 여러 신들께 감사하듯, 자네도 자네 하느님께 감사하게. 해적이라면 저 배를 구하기는커녕 가라앉혔을 거야. 저 행동과 돛대 위의 투구로 보아 로마 배임을 알겠네. 승리는 내 것이야. 운명이 나를 저버리지 않았어. 우리는 살았네. 손을 흔들게—저들을 부르게—어서 데려오게. 나는 두움비르가 될 것이고, 자네는! 나는 자네 아버지를 알았고, 사랑했네. 그는 참으로 군주다운 사람이었어. 유대인이 야만인이 아니라는 것을 내게 가르쳐 준 사람이지. 자네를 데려가겠네. 자네를 내 아들로 삼겠어. 자네 하느님께 감사하고, 선원들을 부르게. 서둘러! 추격을 늦춰서는 안 돼. 도적은 한 놈도 빠져나가게 해서는 안 되네. 저들을 재촉하게!\"\n\n유다는 널빤지 위로 몸을 일으켜, 손을 흔들며 있는 힘껏 소리쳤다. 마침내 작은 보트에 탄 선원들의 주의를 끌었고, 두 사람은 곧 끌어올려졌다.\n\n아리우스는 운명의 그토록 큰 총애를 받은 영웅에게 합당한 온갖 예우를 받으며 갤리선에 맞아들여졌다. 그는 갑판의 침상에 누워 싸움이 어떻게 결판났는지 그 자세한 내막을 들었다. 물에 떠 있던 생존자들이 모두 구조되고 노획물도 확보되자, 그는 다시 사령관의 깃발을 올리고, 함대와 다시 합류해 승리를 완성하기 위해 북쪽으로 서둘러 갔다. 때맞추어 해협을 따라 내려온 쉰 척의 배가 달아나는 해적들을 에워싸 그들을 철저히 무찔렀으니, 한 놈도 빠져나가지 못했다. 호민관의 영광을 더하기 위해, 적의 갤리선 스무 척이 나포되었다.\n\n원정에서 돌아온 아리우스는 미세눔의 방파제에서 따뜻한 환영을 받았다. 그를 수행한 젊은이는 일찍부터 그곳 벗들의 눈길을 끌었다. 그가 누구냐는 물음에, 호민관은 더없이 다정한 태도로 자신이 구조된 이야기를 들려주고 그 낯선 젊은이를 소개했으니, 그 젊은이의 지난 내력에 관한 것은 조심스럽게 모두 빼놓았다. 이야기를 마치자 그는 벤허를 곁으로 불러, 한 손을 그의 어깨에 다정하게 얹고 말했다.\n\n\"좋은 벗들이여, 이 사람은 내 아들이자 상속자요. 그가 내 재산을 물려받을 터이니—신들의 뜻에 따라 내가 무엇이든 남기게 된다면 말이오—여러분은 그를 내 이름으로 알아 주시오. 부디 여러분 모두가 나를 사랑하듯 그를 사랑해 주기를 바라오.\"\n\n기회가 허락하는 대로 서둘러 입양이 정식으로 완결되었다. 이런 식으로 그 용감한 로마인은 벤허에게 한 약속을 지켜, 그를 황실의 세계로 행복하게 이끌어 주었다. 아리우스가 돌아온 다음 달, 스카우루스 극장에서 아르밀루스트리움이 더없이 성대하게 거행되었다. 그 건물의 한쪽은 군사 전리품으로 가득 찼는데, 그 가운데 단연 가장 눈에 띄고 가장 찬탄을 받은 것은 스무 개의 뱃머리와 거기 딸린 고물 장식이었으니, 그만큼 많은 갤리선에서 통째로 잘라 낸 것이었다. 그리고 그 위에는, 좌석에 앉은 팔만 관중이 읽을 수 있도록, 이런 글귀가 새겨져 있었다.\n\n에우리포스만에서 해적들로부터 노획함, 두움비르 퀸투스 아리우스.\n\n알바. 만약 군주가 부당하다면— 그리고, 이런 때에—\n\n제 양심이 권리를 차분히 기다릴 수 있는 자들이니.”\n실러, 「돈 카를로스」 (4막 15장)\n\n우리가 이제 다다른 달은 7월, 해는 주후 29년, 그리고 장소는 안티오크다. 당시 동방의 여왕이라 불리던 그 도시는, 인구가 가장 많지는 않았을지언정 로마 다음으로 막강한 도시였다.\n\n그 시대의 사치와 방탕이 로마에서 비롯되어 거기서 온 제국으로 퍼져 나갔으며, 대도시들은 다만 티베르 강가에 군림하는 여주인의 풍속을 비추었을 뿐이라는 견해가 있다. 그러나 이는 의심해 볼 만하다. 정복의 반작용은 도리어 정복자의 도덕을 향했던 것으로 보인다. 로마는 그리스에서 부패의 샘을 발견했고, 이집트에서도 마찬가지였다. 이 문제를 끝까지 파고든 연구자라면, 도덕을 타락시키는 강물의 흐름이 동방에서 서방으로 향했음을, 그리고 아시리아의 권력과 영화를 떨친 가장 오래된 본거지 가운데 하나인 바로 이 안티오크가 그 치명적인 물줄기의 주된 원천이었음을 확신하며 책을 덮을 것이다.\n\n수송용 갤리선 한 척이 푸른 바다를 떠나 오론테스강 어귀로 들어섰다. 오전나절이었다. 더위가 심했으나, 그 특권을 누릴 수 있는 자들은 모두 갑판에 나와 있었으니, 벤허도 그 가운데 하나였다.\n\n다섯 해가 흐르는 동안 그 젊은 유대인은 완연한 장년에 이르렀다. 몸에 걸친 흰 아마포 겉옷이 그의 몸매를 다소 가렸으나, 그의 모습은 유달리 매력적이었다. 한 시간 넘도록 그는 돛의 그늘에 자리를 잡고 앉아 있었는데, 그동안 같은 민족의 동승객 몇이 그에게 말을 붙여 보려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그들의 물음에 그가 내놓는 대답은 짧았지만 무게 있게 정중했고, 그것도 라틴어였다. 정갈한 말씨와 세련된 몸가짐, 그리고 과묵함이 도리어 그들의 호기심을 더욱 자극했다. 그를 가까이서 지켜본 자들은 그의 거동과 몸의 어떤 특징 사이에서 어긋남을 보고 놀랐다. 그의 거동에는 귀족의 여유와 우아함이 배어 있었던 것이다. 이를테면 그의 두 팔은 균형이 맞지 않을 만큼 길었다. 또 배가 흔들릴 때 몸을 가누려고 가까운 무엇을 붙잡으면, 그 손의 크기와 분명한 힘이 사람들의 눈길을 끌었다. 그리하여 그가 누구이며 어떤 사람인가 하는 궁금증이, 그의 삶을 자세히 알고 싶다는 바람과 끊임없이 뒤섞였다. 달리 말하면, 그의 분위기는 이렇게밖에 더 잘 표현할 수 없다. 이 사람에게는 들려줄 이야기가 있다.\n\n그 갤리선은 오는 길에 키프로스의 한 항구에 들러, 더없이 점잖아 보이는 히브리인 하나를 태웠다. 조용하고 말수가 적으며 자상한 사람이었다. 벤허는 용기를 내어 그에게 몇 가지를 물었고, 그의 대답이 신뢰를 얻어 마침내 긴 대화로 이어졌다.\n\n마침 키프로스를 떠난 그 갤리선이 오론테스강 어귀의 만으로 들어설 때, 바다 멀리서 발견되었던 다른 두 척의 배가 그 배를 만나 같은 시각에 강으로 들어섰다. 두 낯선 배는 강에 들어서면서 더없이 밝은 노란 깃발을 작게 내걸었다. 그 신호가 무엇을 뜻하는지를 두고 여러 추측이 오갔다. 마침내 한 승객이 그 점잖은 히브리인에게 그 일에 대해 알려 달라고 청했다.\n\n“그렇소, 나는 저 깃발의 뜻을 알고 있소.” 그가 대답했다. “저것은 국적을 나타내는 게 아니라, 그저 소유를 표시하는 것이오.”\n\n“그 주인이 배를 많이 가지고 있소?”\n\n“그렇소.”\n\n“그를 아시오?”\n\n“그와 거래해 본 적이 있소.”\n\n승객들은 어서 이야기를 이어 가라는 듯 그 사람을 바라보았다. 벤허도 흥미롭게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n“그는 안티오크에 사오.” 히브리인이 차분한 어조로 말을 이었다. “엄청난 부자라서 세상의 주목을 받게 되었는데, 그를 둘러싼 소문이 늘 호의적이지만은 않소. 예전에 예루살렘에 후르라는 매우 유서 깊은 가문의 영주가 있었소.”\n\n유다는 평정을 지키려 애썼으나, 가슴이 더욱 빨리 뛰었다.\n\n“그 영주는 상인이었는데, 사업에 천부적인 재능이 있었소. 그는 수많은 사업을 벌였으니, 어떤 것은 멀리 동방까지, 어떤 것은 서방까지 뻗어 나갔소. 큰 도시마다 그는 지점을 두었소. 안티오크의 지점은 어떤 이들의 말에 따르면 한때 그 집안의 종이었다는 시모니데스라는 사람이 맡고 있었는데, 이름은 그리스식이나 이스라엘 사람이었소. 그 주인은 바다에서 빠져 죽었소. 하지만 그의 사업은 계속되었고, 번창함이 조금도 덜하지 않았소. 얼마 뒤 그 가문에 불행이 닥쳤소. 영주의 외아들은 거의 다 자란 나이였는데, 예루살렘의 어느 거리에서 총독 그라투스를 죽이려 했소. 아슬아슬하게 실패했고, 그 뒤로는 소식이 끊겼소. 사실 그 로마인의 분노는 온 집안을 휩쓸었으니, 그 이름을 가진 자는 하나도 살아남지 못했소. 그들의 저택은 봉인되어 지금은 비둘기 떼의 둥지가 되었고, 재산은 몰수되었소. 후르 가문의 소유로 추적될 수 있는 것은 모조리 몰수되었소. 그 총독은 황금 고약으로 제 상처를 치료한 셈이오.”\n\n승객들이 웃었다.\n\n“재산을 가로챘다는 말이군요.” 그들 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n“그렇게들 말하오.” 히브리인이 대답했다. “나는 그저 들은 대로 이야기를 전할 뿐이오. 이야기를 마저 하자면, 여기 안티오크에서 영주의 대리인이었던 시모니데스는 머지않아 제 이름으로 장사를 시작했고, 믿기 어려울 만큼 짧은 동안에 이 도시 제일가는 상인이 되었소. 그는 옛 주인을 본받아 인도로 대상을 보냈고, 지금 바다에는 왕실 함대를 이룰 만큼 많은 갤리선을 거느리고 있소. 그에게는 어긋나는 일이 하나도 없다고들 하오. 그의 낙타는 늙어 죽는 일 말고는 죽는 법이 없고, 그의 배는 결코 가라앉지 않으며, 그가 강에 나뭇조각 하나를 던지면 황금이 되어 돌아온다 하오.”\n\n“그가 그렇게 잘나간 지 얼마나 되었소?”\n\n“십 년이 채 안 되오.”\n\n“출발이 든든했던 모양이군요.”\n\n“그렇소, 사람들 말로는 총독이 가져간 것은 영주가 손에 쥐고 있던 재산뿐이었다 하오. 말과 가축, 집과 땅, 배와 물품 따위 말이오. 돈은 끝내 찾아내지 못했는데, 분명 막대한 액수가 있었을 거요. 그것이 어찌 되었는지는 풀리지 않은 수수께끼였소.”\n\n“나한테는 수수께끼가 아니오.” 한 승객이 비웃듯 말했다.\n\n“무슨 뜻인지 알겠소.” 히브리인이 대답했다. “다른 이들도 그대와 같은 생각을 했소. 그것이 시모니데스 노인의 밑천이 되었다는 것이 흔한 믿음이오. 총독도 그렇게 여기오. 아니, 적어도 그렇게 여겨 왔소. 다섯 해 사이에 두 차례나 그 상인을 붙잡아 고문했으니 말이오.”\n\n유다는 잡고 있던 밧줄을 으스러뜨릴 듯한 힘으로 움켜쥐었다.\n\n“듣기로는,” 이야기꾼이 말을 이었다. “그 사람 몸에는 성한 뼈가 하나도 없다 하오. 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때 그는 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 쿠션에 기댄 채 형체도 알아볼 수 없는 불구가 되어 있었소.”\n\n“그토록 고문을 당하다니!” 여럿이 한꺼번에 탄식했다.\n\n“병으로는 그런 기형이 생겨날 수 없소. 그래도 그 고통은 그에게 아무런 영향을 주지 못했소. 그가 가진 모든 것은 합법적으로 얻은 것이며, 그것을 합법적으로 쓰고 있을 뿐이라는 것, 그들이 그에게서 짜낸 것은 그게 고작이었소. 그러나 이제 그는 박해를 넘어선 사람이 되었소. 티베리우스가 직접 서명한 교역 허가증을 가지고 있으니 말이오.”\n\n“그 대가로 어마어마하게 치렀겠지요.”\n\n“이 배들이 그의 것이오.” 히브리인은 그 말을 흘려듣고 이야기를 이었다. “그의 뱃사람들 사이에는 서로 마주치면 노란 깃발을 내걸어 인사하는 관습이 있소. 그 깃발을 본다는 것은 ‘우리는 순조로운 항해를 마쳤다’고 말하는 것과 같소.”\n\n이야기는 거기서 끝났다.\n\n수송선이 강의 물길에 완전히 들어섰을 때, 유다가 그 히브리인에게 물었다.\n\n“그 상인의 주인이라는 사람의 이름이 무엇이었소?”\n\n“예루살렘의 영주 벤허였소.”\n\n“그 영주의 가족은 어찌 되었소?”\n\n“아들은 갤리선으로 보내졌소. 죽었다고 봐도 좋소. 그 형벌 아래에서는 한 해가 목숨의 보통 한도니까. 아내와 딸은 소식이 끊겼소. 그들이 어찌 되었는지 아는 자들은 입을 열려 하지 않소. 분명 유대의 길가에 점점이 박힌 어느 성채의 지하 감방에서 죽었을 거요.”\n\n유다는 키잡이가 있는 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그는 생각에 깊이 잠긴 나머지, 바다에서 도시에 이르기까지 더없이 아름다운 강기슭을 거의 알아차리지 못했다. 그 기슭에는 시리아의 온갖 과일나무와 포도밭이 어우러져, 네아폴리스의 별장만큼이나 호화로운 별장들을 에워싸고 있었다. 끝없는 함대를 이루며 지나는 배들도, 일하느라 혹은 흥에 겨워 부르는 뱃사람들의 노랫소리와 외침도 그의 눈과 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 하늘은 햇빛으로 가득 차, 흐릿한 온기가 땅과 물 위에 내려앉았다. 그의 삶 위를 빼고는 어디에도 그림자가 없었다.\n\n단 한 번 그는 잠깐의 흥미에 깨어났는데, 누군가가 강이 굽이도는 자리에서 보이는 다프네 숲을 가리켰을 때였다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n도시가 시야에 들어오자, 승객들은 갑판 위로 나와 그 광경의 어느 하나도 놓치지 않으려고 마음을 졸였다. 앞서 독자에게 소개된 점잖은 유대인이 주된 이야기꾼이었다.\n\n\"여기서 강은 서쪽으로 흐릅니다.\" 그가 모두를 향한 대답처럼 말했다. \"예전엔 이 강물이 성벽 발치를 씻고 흘렀던 걸 기억합니다. 그러나 로마의 신민으로서 우리는 평화롭게 살아왔고, 그런 시절이면 으레 그렇듯 교역이 제멋대로 번성했지요. 이제 강가는 온통 부두와 선창으로 들어찼습니다. 저기\"—말하는 이가 남쪽을 가리켰다—\"카시우스 산이 있는데, 이곳 사람들은 즐겨 오론테스 산이라 부르며, 북쪽에 있는 형제 격인 암누스 산과 마주 보고 있습니다. 그 둘 사이에 안티오크 평원이 펼쳐져 있지요. 더 멀리에는 검은 산들이 있는데, 그곳에서 '왕들의 수로'가 가장 맑은 물을 끌어와 목마른 거리와 사람들을 적셔 줍니다. 그래도 그 산들은 황야 그대로의 숲이어서, 빽빽하고 새와 짐승으로 가득하답니다.\"\n\n\"호수는 어디 있습니까?\" 한 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"저기 북쪽에 있습니다. 보고 싶으시면 말을 타셔도 되고—아니면 배가 더 낫지요. 지류 하나가 그 호수를 강과 이어 주니까요.\"\n\n\"다프네 숲 말입니까!\" 그가 세 번째 묻는 이에게 말했다. \"그건 누구도 묘사할 수가 없습니다. 다만 조심하십시오! 아폴론이 그것을 시작하여 손수 완성했지요. 그분은 그곳을 올림포스보다 더 좋아한답니다. 사람들은 한 번 보러 갔다가—단 한 번이지요—다시는 떠나오지 못합니다. 그곳을 다 말해 주는 속담이 있지요—'왕의 손님이 되느니 차라리 벌레가 되어 다프네의 뽕나무를 먹는 게 낫다.'\"\n\n\"그렇다면 거길 멀리하라고 충고하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"천만에요! 가실 겁니다. 누구나 다 갑니다. 냉소적인 철학자도, 혈기 넘치는 젊은이도, 여자들도, 사제들도—모두 다 갑니다. 당신이 어찌하실지 확신하기에, 제가 충고를 드리는 겁니다. 도성 안에 거처를 잡지 마십시오—그건 시간 낭비예요. 곧장 숲 가장자리에 있는 마을로 가십시오. 가는 길은 정원을 지나고, 분수의 물보라 아래를 거치게 됩니다. 그 신과 그의 페네이오스 처녀를 사랑하는 이들이 그 마을을 지었지요. 그 회랑과 오솔길, 그리고 수많은 은밀한 곳에서 당신은 다른 데서는 도무지 찾을 수 없는 사람들과 풍습과 즐거움과 온갖 부류를 만나게 될 겁니다. 그런데 저 성벽을 보십시오! 저기 있군요, 성벽 건축의 대가 크세라이오스의 걸작입니다.\"\n\n모두의 눈이 그가 가리키는 손끝을 따라갔다.\n\n\"이 부분은 셀레우코스 왕조의 초대 왕이 명하여 쌓은 것입니다. 삼백 년 세월이 흐르는 동안, 그것은 받치고 선 바위와 한 몸이 되어 버렸지요.\"\n\n그 방벽은 그 찬사에 걸맞았다. 높고 견고하며, 대담하게 꺾인 모서리가 숱하게 있어, 남쪽으로 굽이치며 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n\"꼭대기에는 사백 개의 망루가 있고, 그 하나하나가 물 저장고랍니다.\" 히브리인이 말을 이었다. \"자, 보십시오! 저렇게 높은 성벽 너머로, 멀리 두 언덕이 보이지요. 술피우스의 쌍둥이 봉우리라 아실 겁니다. 가장 먼 봉우리 위에 있는 건물이 성채인데, 일 년 내내 로마 군단이 주둔합니다. 그 맞은편 이쪽으로 솟은 것이 유피테르 신전이고, 그 아래가 총독 관저의 정면이지요—온갖 관청으로 가득한 궁전이면서도, 폭도가 부딪혀 봐야 남풍처럼 무력하게 흩어질 뿐인 요새랍니다.\"\n\n이때 선원들이 돛을 거두기 시작하자, 히브리인이 진심을 담아 외쳤다. \"보십시오! 바다를 미워하시는 분들과 서원을 세우신 분들, 이제 저주의 말과 기도를 준비하십시오. 저기 보이는 다리, 셀레우키아로 가는 길이 지나는 저 다리가 항해의 끝을 알리는 표지입니다. 배가 더 멀리 보내려고 부려 놓은 짐을, 저기서 낙타가 넘겨받지요. 저 다리 위로는 섬이 시작되는데, 칼리니쿠스가 그 위에 새 도시를 세우고 다섯 개의 거대한 수도교로 이어 놓았답니다. 어찌나 견고한지 세월도, 홍수도, 지진도 아무런 흔적을 남기지 못했지요. 본 도성에 대해서는, 벗들이여, 그저 이 말만 하렵니다. 그것을 본 까닭에 여러분은 평생토록 더 행복하실 거라고요.\"\n\n그가 말을 맺자, 배는 방향을 틀어 성벽 아래 부두를 향해 천천히 나아갔다. 그러면서 그 지점의 강을 가득 메운 삶의 모습이 한층 또렷이 드러났다. 마침내 밧줄이 던져지고, 노가 거두어지고, 항해가 끝났다. 그러자 벤허는 그 점잖은 히브리인을 찾았다.\n\n\"작별을 고하기 전에 잠시 폐를 끼치겠습니다.\"\n\n남자가 머리를 숙여 응했다.\n\n\"그 상인 이야기를 듣고 나니 그를 만나 보고 싶어졌습니다. 시모니데스라고 부르셨지요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그리스식 이름을 가진 유대인이지요.\"\n\n\"어디로 가면 만날 수 있습니까?\"\n\n그가 대답하기 전에 날카로운 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"낭패를 면하시도록 미리 말씀드리지요. 그는 돈을 빌려주는 사람이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"저 또한 돈을 빌리려는 사람이 아닙니다.\" 벤허가 상대의 눈치 빠름에 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n남자가 고개를 들고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"안티오크에서 가장 부유한 상인이라면, 으레 그 재산에 걸맞은 사업장을 가졌으리라 여기시겠지요.\" 그가 이윽고 대답했다. \"그러나 낮에 그를 찾으시려거든, 강을 따라 저 다리까지 가십시오. 그 아래에 그가 거처하는데, 성벽의 버팀벽처럼 보이는 건물이랍니다. 문 앞에는 어마어마한 선창이 있고, 오가는 화물로 늘 뒤덮여 있지요. 거기 매여 있는 선단이 모두 그의 것입니다. 틀림없이 찾으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"우리 조상들의 평화가 그대와 함께하기를.\"\n\n\"그대와도 함께하기를.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 둘은 헤어졌다.\n\n거리의 짐꾼 둘이 그의 짐을 짊어지고, 부두에서 벤허의 지시를 받았다.\n\n\"성채로.\" 그가 말했다. 군의 공적 직무와 관련이 있음을 내비치는 지시였다.\n\n직각으로 서로 엇갈리는 두 개의 큰길이 도시를 네 구역으로 나누고 있었다. 님파이움이라 불리는 기이하고도 거대한 건축물이 남북으로 뻗은 길의 어귀에 솟아 있었다. 짐꾼들이 거기서 남쪽으로 돌아서자, 로마에서 막 왔는데도 새로 온 이는 그 대로의 웅장함에 놀라움을 금치 못했다. 좌우로는 궁전들이 늘어섰고, 그 사이로 대리석 이중 회랑이 끝없이 뻗어, 보행자와 짐승과 전차가 다닐 길을 따로따로 내고 있었다. 그 모두가 그늘 아래 있었고, 쉼 없이 솟는 분수가 그곳을 시원하게 식혀 주었다.\n\n벤허는 그 광경을 즐길 기분이 아니었다. 시모니데스의 이야기가 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다. 옴팔로스에 이르렀을 때—거리만큼이나 폭이 넓고, 빼어나게 장식된 네 개의 아치로 된 기념물로, 셀레우코스 왕조의 여덟 번째 왕 에피파네스가 자기 자신을 기려 세운 것이었다—그는 문득 마음을 바꾸었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤엔 성채로 가지 않겠다.\" 그가 짐꾼들에게 말했다. \"셀레우키아로 가는 길에서 다리에 가장 가까운 칸으로 데려다다오.\"\n\n일행은 방향을 돌렸고, 이윽고 그는 소박하나 너른 구조의 객사에 짐을 풀었다. 늙은 시모니데스가 거처하는 다리에서 돌을 던지면 닿을 거리였다. 그는 밤새도록 옥상에 누워 있었다. 그의 마음 깊은 곳에는 이런 생각이 살아 있었다. \"이제—이제 집 소식을 듣겠구나—어머니와—사랑스러운 어린 티르자의 소식을. 그들이 이 땅에 살아 있다면, 내가 반드시 찾아내리라.\"\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n바로 이튿날 아침, 벤허는 도시 구경도 뒤로 미룬 채 시모니데스의 집을 찾았다. 그는 성가퀴를 두른 성문을 지나 죽 늘어선 부두로 들어섰고, 거기서 분주히 북적이는 인파 사이로 강을 거슬러 올라가 셀레우키아 다리에 이르렀다. 그는 그 다리 아래에서 걸음을 멈추고 주위 광경을 살펴보았다.\n\n그곳, 바로 다리 밑에 상인의 집이 있었다. 다듬지 않은 잿빛 돌을 쌓아 올린 덩어리로, 어떤 양식에도 들어맞지 않았고, 뱃사람이 묘사했던 그대로 자신이 기댄 성벽의 버팀벽처럼 보였다. 정면에 난 두 개의 거대한 문이 부두로 통했다. 꼭대기 가까이 난 몇 개의 구멍은 굵은 창살로 단단히 막혀 창문 노릇을 했다. 틈새마다 잡초가 흔들거렸고, 군데군데 검은 이끼가 헐벗은 돌을 얼룩지게 했다.\n\n문은 열려 있었다. 한쪽 문으로는 거래가 들어가고, 다른 쪽 문으로는 거래가 나왔으니, 그 모든 움직임에는 바삐, 바삐 서두르는 기색이 가득했다.\n\n부두에는 온갖 모양으로 꾸린 화물 더미가 쌓여 있었고, 허리까지 벗은 노예들이 무리를 지어, 일에 몰두한 채 분주히 오갔다.\n\n다리 아래에는 갤리선 선단이 떠 있었으니, 어떤 배는 짐을 싣고, 어떤 배는 짐을 내렸다. 돛대 꼭대기마다 노란 깃발이 나부꼈다. 선단과 부두 사이에서, 그리고 배에서 배로, 거래에 매인 종들이 시끌벅적하게 마주 흐르는 물결처럼 오갔다.\n\n다리 위쪽으로, 강 건너편에는 물가에서부터 성벽이 솟아 있었고, 그 너머로 황제의 궁전이 화려한 처마 장식과 작은 탑들을 우뚝 세우고 있었으니, 그 히브리인이 묘사했던 섬을 한 치도 남김없이 뒤덮고 있었다. 그러나 그 모든 암시에도 불구하고, 벤허는 그것을 거의 눈여겨보지 않았다. 이제야, 마침내 그는 자기 가족의 소식을 들을 수 있으리라 생각했다. 시모니데스가 정말로 자기 아버지의 종이었다면 분명 그러할 것이었다. 그러나 그 사람이 그 관계를 인정하려 할까? 그것은 자신의 재물과, 부두와 강에서 그토록 왕처럼 위세를 드러내는 거래의 지배권을 내놓는 일이었다. 게다가 상인에게 더욱 중대한 것은, 그토록 놀라운 성공의 한복판에서 자신의 출세를 포기하고, 스스로 다시 한 번 종이 되기를 자청하는 일이었다. 그런 요구를 떠올리는 것만으로도 터무니없는 만용처럼 여겨졌다. 외교적 수사를 벗겨 내면, 그것은 곧 이렇게 말하는 셈이었다. 당신은 내 종이오. 당신이 가진 모든 것을 내게 주시오, 그리고—당신 자신까지도.\n\n그래도 벤허는 자신의 권리에 대한 믿음과, 가슴속에 가장 크게 자리한 희망에서 이 면담에 임할 힘을 얻었다. 그가 마음을 내맡긴 그 이야기가 사실이라면, 시모니데스는 그가 가진 모든 것과 더불어 벤허에게 속한 사람이었다. 그러나 공정하게 말하건대, 그 재물 따위는 벤허에게 아무런 관심거리도 아니었다. 마음을 굳히고 문으로 향할 때, 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 다짐했다. \"그가 어머니와 티르자의 소식을 들려준다면, 나는 따지지 않고 그에게 자유를 주리라.\"\n\n그는 당당하게 집 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n내부는 거대한 창고였으니, 정연하게 구획된 공간마다 온갖 종류의 물건이 빈틈없이 쌓여 갇혀 있었다. 빛은 어둑하고 공기는 숨 막힐 듯했으나, 사람들은 활기차게 움직였다. 군데군데 일꾼들이 톱과 망치를 들고 선적용 짐짝을 짜고 있는 것이 보였다. 그는 화물 더미 사이로 난 길을 따라 천천히 걸으며, 여기 이토록 넘치는 천재성의 증거를 남긴 사람이 과연 자기 아버지의 종이었을 수 있을까 의아해했다. 만일 그렇다면, 그는 어떤 부류에 속했을까? 유대인이라면, 종의 아들이었을까? 아니면 빚진 자, 혹은 빚진 자의 아들이었을까? 아니면 도둑질로 판결을 받아 팔린 것일까? 이런 생각들이 스쳐 갔으나, 그 어느 것도 순간순간 더욱 또렷이 느껴지는 상인에 대한 존경심을 조금도 흔들지 못했다. 우리가 누군가를 흠모할 때 보이는 한 가지 특징은, 그 흠모를 정당화해 줄 정황을 늘 찾는다는 것이다.\n\n마침내 한 사람이 다가와 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 오셨습니까?\"\n\n\"시모니데스 상인을 뵙고 싶소.\"\n\n\"이쪽으로 오시겠습니까?\"\n\n쌓인 화물 사이로 난 여러 길을 지나, 그들은 마침내 한 줄로 늘어선 계단에 이르렀다. 그 계단을 올라가니 벤허는 창고 지붕 위에 서 있었고, 그 앞에는 한 건물이 있었다. 그것을 가장 잘 묘사하자면, 또 다른 석조 건물 위에 세운 작은 돌집이라 할 수 있었으니, 아래쪽 부두에서는 보이지 않았고, 다리 서쪽으로 탁 트인 하늘 아래 있었다. 낮은 담으로 둘린 그 지붕은 테라스 같았으며, 놀랍게도 갖가지 꽃으로 화사했다. 그 풍요로운 둘레 한가운데, 그 집은 납작하게 앉아 있었으니, 정면의 문간 말고는 어디 하나 틈 없는 밋밋한 네모난 덩어리였다. 먼지 한 점 없는 길이 활짝 핀 페르시아 장미 덤불 사이로 문까지 이어졌다. 달콤한 장미 향유의 향기를 맡으며, 벤허는 안내자를 뒤따랐다.\n\n안으로 들어가 어둑한 통로 끝에 이르자, 그들은 반쯤 열린 휘장 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그 사람이 소리쳐 알렸다.\n\n\"낯선 분이 주인님을 뵈러 오셨습니다.\"\n\n맑은 목소리가 대답했다. \"하느님의 이름으로, 들라 하라.\"\n\n로마인이라면 손님이 안내받아 든 그 방을 아트리움이라 불렀을 것이다. 벽에는 널빤지가 둘려 있었고, 널빤지마다 오늘날의 사무용 책상처럼 칸칸이 나뉘었으며, 칸마다 이름표를 붙인 두루마리들이 빽빽이 들어차 있었는데, 모두 세월과 손때에 낡아 누렇게 변해 있었다. 널빤지들 사이와 그 위아래로는 한때 희었으나 이제는 크림빛으로 물든 나무 가장자리 장식이 둘려 있었으니, 놀랍도록 정교한 무늬로 새겨져 있었다. 도금한 둥근 장식을 두른 돌림띠 위로 천장은 정자 모양으로 솟아오르다가, 보랏빛 운모 수백 장을 끼운 얕은 둥근 지붕으로 트였으니, 더없이 편안한 빛이 쏟아져 내렸다. 바닥에는 잿빛 양탄자가 어찌나 두툼하게 깔렸는지, 발을 들여놓으면 절반쯤 파묻혀 소리조차 나지 않았다.\n\n방의 은은한 빛 가운데 두 사람이 있었다. 한 사람은 등받이가 높고 팔걸이가 넓으며 폭신한 방석을 댄 의자에 기대앉은 남자였고, 그 왼편에서 의자 등받이에 기댄 채 서 있는 이는 한창 여인으로 무르익은 처녀였다. 그들을 보는 순간 벤허는 이마로 피가 솟구쳐 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 절을 했는데, 예의를 차리려는 것 못지않게 마음을 가다듬으려는 까닭이었다. 그래서 그는 앉아 있던 사람이 그를 발견하고 두 손을 치켜드는 것도, 흠칫 떨며 움츠리는 것도 놓치고 말았으니, 그것은 솟구쳐 오른 만큼이나 재빨리 스러진 감정이었다. 그가 눈을 들었을 때 두 사람은 똑같은 자세 그대로였으나, 다만 처녀의 손은 내려와 노인의 어깨 위에 가만히 얹혀 있었다. 두 사람 다 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 시모니데스 상인이며, 유대인이시라면\"—벤허는 잠시 말을 멈추었다—\"우리 조상 아브라함의 하느님의 평화가 당신께, 그리고—당신 곁의 분께 깃들기를 빕니다.\"\n\n마지막 말은 처녀를 향한 것이었다.\n\n\"내가 바로 당신이 말한 시모니데스요. 태생으로 유대인이오.\" 그 사람이 유달리 맑은 목소리로 대답했다. \"나는 시모니데스이며, 유대인이오. 당신의 인사를 그대로 돌려드리며, 나를 찾아온 분이 누구신지 알고자 청하오.\"\n\n벤허는 들으면서 살펴보았다. 그 사람의 몸이 건강하게 둥글어야 할 자리에는, 그저 형체 없는 살덩어리가 방석 깊숙이 가라앉아, 빛바랜 비단 누비옷에 덮여 있을 뿐이었다. 그 살덩어리 위로 왕처럼 균형 잡힌 머리가 빛났으니, 정치가와 정복자에게 어울릴 이상적인 머리, 바탕이 넓고 앞이마가 둥근, 미켈란젤로가 카이사르를 빚는다면 본떴을 법한 머리였다. 흰 눈썹 위로 흰 머리칼이 가는 가닥으로 드리워, 그 사이로 음울한 등불처럼 빛나는 눈동자의 검은빛을 더욱 짙게 했다. 얼굴은 핏기가 없었고, 주름이 잔뜩 잡혀 부어 있었으니, 특히 턱 아래가 그러했다. 다시 말해, 그 머리와 얼굴은 세상이 그를 움직이기보다 그가 세상을 더 쉽게 움직일 만한 사람의 것이었다. 스물네 번을 다시 고문당해 지금의 일그러진 불구가 되도록 신음 한 번 내지 않았을, 자백은 더더욱 하지 않았을 사람, 목숨은 내놓을지언정 뜻이나 신념은 결코 굽히지 않을 사람, 갑옷을 입고 태어나 오직 사랑을 통해서만 공략당할 수 있는 사람의 것이었다. 그런 사람에게 벤허는 두 손을 내밀었으니, 손바닥을 펴 위로 향한 채, 평화를 청하는 동시에 평화를 건네듯 했다.\n\n\"나는 유다, 이다말의 아들이며, 후르 가문의 옛 가장이자, 예루살렘의 왕자입니다.\"\n\n상인의 오른손은 옷 밖에 놓여 있었으니, 길고 가는 손이었고, 고통으로 마디마디 흉하게 비틀려 있었다. 그 손이 꽉 쥐어졌다. 그 밖에 그의 편에서는 어떠한 감정의 표시도 조금도 없었다. 놀라움이나 관심을 짐작케 할 만한 것은 아무것도 없었으니, 다만 이 차분한 대답뿐이었다.\n\n\"순수한 혈통의 예루살렘 왕자들은 언제나 내 집에서 환영받소. 당신을 환영하오. 에스더야, 이 젊은이에게 자리를 내드려라.\"\n\n처녀는 가까이 있던 등받이 없는 의자를 가져다 벤허에게 놓아주었다. 자리를 놓고 몸을 일으키다가, 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다.\n\n\"우리 주님의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를.\" 그녀가 조심스레 말했다. \"앉으셔서 편히 쉬십시오.\"\n\n그녀가 다시 의자 곁 제자리로 돌아갔을 때, 그녀는 그가 무엇을 하려는지 헤아리지 못했다. 여인의 힘은 거기까지 미치지 못한다. 다만 그것이 연민이나 자비, 동정 같은 더 섬세한 감정이라면, 그녀는 그것을 알아챈다. 바로 거기에 여인과 남자의 차이가 있으니, 그 차이는 여인이 천성적으로 그런 감정에 민감하게 살아 있는 한 영원히 지속될 것이다. 그녀는 그저 그가 치유받아야 할 삶의 상처를 안고 왔다고 확신했을 뿐이었다.\n\n벤허는 권한 자리에 앉지 않고, 정중하게 말했다. \"부디 어지신 시모니데스 어른께서 저를 불청객으로 여기지 않으셨으면 합니다. 어제 강을 거슬러 올라오면서, 어른께서 제 아버지를 아셨다는 말을 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 후르 왕자를 알았소. 우리는 바다와 사막 너머의 땅에서 이익을 얻는 상인들에게 허락된 몇몇 사업에서 동업한 사이였소. 그러나 앉으시오, 부탁이오—그리고 에스더야, 이 젊은이에게 포도주를 좀 내드려라. 느헤미야서에 한때 예루살렘의 절반을 다스렸던 후르의 아들 이야기가 나오지. 오래된 가문이오. 참으로, 정말 오래되었소! 모세와 여호수아의 시대에도 그들 가운데 몇몇은 주님의 눈에 들어, 사람들 가운데 으뜸가는 왕자들과 더불어 영예를 나누었소. 우리에게 곧장 핏줄로 이어 내려온 그 후손이, 헤브론 남쪽 산비탈에서 자란 소렉의 진짜 포도로 빚은 진한 포도주 한 잔을 마다할 리는 없을 것이오.\"\n\n이 말이 끝날 무렵, 에스더는 의자에서 조금 떨어진 탁자 위 항아리에서 가득 따른 은잔을 들고 벤허 앞에 와 있었다. 그녀는 고개를 숙인 채 그 잔을 권했다. 그는 가만히 그녀의 손을 만지며 그것을 물렸다. 다시 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다. 그제야 그는 그녀가 자그마하여 자기 어깨에도 채 닿지 못할 만큼 작다는 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 그녀는 매우 우아했고, 얼굴은 곱고 사랑스러웠으며, 눈은 검고 이루 말할 수 없이 부드러웠다. 이 처녀는 마음씨가 곱고 예쁘구나, 그는 생각했다. 티르자가 살아 있다면 꼭 이런 모습이리라. 가엾은 티르자! 그러고 나서 그는 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"아니오, 당신의 아버지가—이분이 당신의 아버지시라면 말이오만?\"—그는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"저는 에스더, 시모니데스의 딸입니다.\" 그녀가 기품 있게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 어여쁜 에스더여, 당신의 아버지께서 내 다음 말을 들으신 뒤에는, 내가 그 이름난 포도주를 선뜻 받지 않는다 해도 나를 더 나쁘게 여기지 않으실 것이오. 또한 당신 눈에 비친 내 모습도 흠을 잃지 않기를 바라오. 잠시만 나와 함께 여기 서 있어 주오!\"\n\n두 사람은 마치 한뜻이 된 듯, 상인을 향해 돌아섰다. \"시모니데스!\" 그가 단호하게 말했다. \"내 아버지는 돌아가실 때 당신과 같은 이름을 가진, 믿음직한 종을 두셨소. 그리고 나는 당신이 바로 그 사람이라고 들었소!\"\n\n옷 아래 비틀린 팔다리가 별안간 움찔했고, 가는 손이 꽉 움켜쥐어졌다.\n\n\"에스더, 에스더야!\" 그 사람이 엄하게 불렀다. \"여기다, 거기가 아니라. 네가 네 어미와 내 자식이라면—여기다, 거기가 아니라, 내 말 알아듣겠느냐!\"\n\n처녀는 아버지에게서 손님에게로 한 번 눈길을 옮겼다. 그러고는 잔을 탁자에 다시 내려놓고, 순순히 의자 쪽으로 갔다. 그녀의 얼굴은 놀라움과 두려움을 고스란히 드러냈다.\n\n시모니데스는 왼손을 들어, 그의 어깨 위에 다정히 얹혀 있던 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 차분하게 말했다. \"나는 사람들을 상대하며 늙어 버렸소—제 나이보다 일찍 늙었소. 만일 당신에게 그 이야기를 들려준 자가 내 내력을 잘 아는 친구였고, 그것을 모질게 말하지 않았다면, 그는 분명 내가 같은 인간을 믿지 못하는 사람일 수밖에 없다고 당신을 설득했을 것이오. 인생의 끝에 이르러 그만큼을 인정하지 않을 수 없는 자에게 이스라엘의 하느님께서 도움을 베푸시기를! 내 사랑은 몇 안 되지만, 분명히 있소. 그중 하나는 한 영혼이니\"—그는 자신의 손을 쥔 그 손을 입술로 가져갔으니, 그 뜻은 의심할 여지가 없었다—\"지금까지 사심 없이 내 것이 되어 준 영혼이며, 너무도 달콤한 위안이라, 그것을 내게서 빼앗긴다면 나는 죽고 말 것이오.\"\n\n에스더의 고개가 숙여져, 마침내 그 뺨이 아버지의 뺨에 닿았다.\n\n\"또 다른 사랑은 한낱 기억일 뿐이오. 그에 대해 더 말하자면, 주님의 축복처럼, 그것은 온 가족을 품을 만큼 넓은 둘레를 지녔으니, 다만\"—그의 목소리가 낮아지고 떨렸다—\"다만 내가 그들이 어디 있는지 알기만 한다면 말이오.\"\n\n벤허의 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐다. 그는 한 걸음 다가서며 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"내 어머니와 누이! 오, 당신이 말하는 것이 바로 그들이군요!\"\n\n에스더는 마치 자신에게 한 말이라도 되는 듯 고개를 들었다. 그러나 시모니데스는 다시 차분함을 되찾아, 차갑게 대답했다. \"내 말을 끝까지 들으시오. 나는 나일 수밖에 없고, 또 앞서 말한 그 사랑들 때문에, 후르 왕자와 나의 관계를 묻는 당신의 요구에 답하기 전에, 마땅히 먼저 와야 할 것으로서, 당신이 누구인지 그 증거를 내게 보이시오. 당신의 증거는 글로 된 것이오? 아니면 직접 사람이 와서 증언하오?\"\n\n그 요구는 분명했고, 그 정당함은 따질 여지가 없었다. 벤허는 얼굴을 붉히고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 말을 더듬으며, 어쩔 줄 몰라 고개를 돌렸다. 시모니데스는 그를 다그쳤다.\n\n\"증거, 증거를 보이라 했소! 내 앞에 펼쳐 보이시오—내 손에 쥐여 주시오!\"\n\n그러나 벤허에게는 답할 것이 없었다. 그는 그런 요구를 예상하지 못했다. 그리고 막상 그 요구를 받고 나자, 갤리선에서 보낸 삼 년이 자기 신원을 입증할 모든 증거를 앗아 갔다는 끔찍한 사실이 일찍이 없던 무게로 다가왔다. 어머니와 누이가 사라진 지금, 그는 자신의 존재를 아는 단 한 사람도 없이 살아가고 있었다. 그를 아는 자야 많았으나, 그뿐이었다. 퀸투스 아리우스가 있었던들, 그를 어디서 발견했으며 그가 후르의 아들이라 믿는다는 것 말고 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있었으랴? 그러나 곧 자세히 밝혀지겠지만, 그 용감한 로마 해군 장교는 죽고 없었다. 유다는 전에도 그 외로움을 느낀 적이 있었으나, 이제 그 감각은 목숨의 핵심까지 꿰뚫었다. 그는 두 손을 맞잡고 얼굴을 돌린 채, 망연자실하여 서 있었다. 시모니데스는 그의 고통을 존중하여, 말없이 기다렸다.\n\n\"시모니데스 어른,\" 그가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"저는 그저 제 이야기를 들려드릴 수 있을 뿐입니다. 그러니 그동안만이라도 판단을 미루시고, 너그러이 제 말을 들어 주시지 않는다면, 저는 그 이야기조차 하지 않으렵니다.\"\n\n\"말하시오.\" 이제는 정말로 상황을 장악한 시모니데스가 말했다. \"말하시오. 나는 당신이 스스로 주장하는 바로 그 사람임을 부정하지 않았으니, 더욱 기꺼이 들으리다.\"\n\n그리하여 벤허는 이야기를 이어 갔으니, 자신의 삶을 서둘러, 그러나 온갖 웅변의 원천이 되는 그 감정을 담아 들려주었다. 그러나 우리는 그가 에게해에서 승리하고 돌아온 아리우스와 함께 미세눔에 상륙하는 대목까지는 이미 알고 있으므로, 그 지점에서부터 그의 말을 옮기기로 하자.\n\n\"제 은인은 황제의 사랑과 신임을 받아, 황제께서 명예로운 상을 그에게 듬뿍 내리셨습니다. 동방의 상인들은 굉장한 선물을 바쳤고, 그리하여 그는 로마의 부자들 가운데서도 곱절로 부유해졌습니다. 유대인이 제 종교를 잊을 수 있겠습니까? 그것이 우리 조상의 거룩한 땅이라면, 제 출생지를 잊을 수 있겠습니까? 그 어진 분은 정식 법적 절차를 밟아 저를 양자로 삼으셨고, 저는 그에 합당한 보답을 하려 애썼습니다. 어떤 자식도 제가 그분께 한 것만큼 아버지에게 효성스럽지는 못했을 것입니다. 그분은 저를 학자로 만들고자 하셨습니다. 미술, 철학, 수사학, 웅변술에서 가장 이름난 스승을 붙여 주려 하셨습니다. 저는 그분의 권유를 사양했으니, 제가 유대인이라, 주 하느님이나 예언자들의 영광이나, 다윗과 솔로몬이 언덕 위에 세운 도성을 잊을 수 없었기 때문입니다. 오, 그러면서 어찌하여 제가 그 로마인의 은혜를 받아들였느냐 물으십니까? 저는 그분을 사랑했습니다. 다음으로, 그분의 도움이 있으면, 언젠가 어머니와 누이의 운명을 단단히 봉인한 그 수수께끼를 풀어낼 영향력을 갖추게 되리라 여겼습니다. 그리고 여기에 또 하나의 동기가 있었으나, 그에 대해서는 말하지 않겠습니다. 다만 그것이 저를 어찌나 사로잡았던지, 저는 무예에, 그리고 전쟁술을 철저히 익히는 데 꼭 필요하다 여겨지는 모든 것을 갖추는 데 전념했다는 것만 말씀드리겠습니다. 도시의 연무장과 원형 경기장에서 저는 단련했고, 진영에서도 못지않게 단련했습니다. 그 모든 곳에서 저는 이름을 얻었으나, 그것은 제 조상의 이름이 아니었습니다. 제가 거머쥔 영예의 관들은—미세눔의 별장 벽에는 그것들이 여럿 걸려 있습니다—모두 두움비르 아리우스의 아들로서 제게 온 것입니다. 로마인들 사이에서 저는 오직 그 관계로만 알려져 있습니다…… 제 은밀한 목적을 한결같이 좇으며, 저는 로마를 떠나 안티오크로 왔으니, 막센티우스 집정관이 파르티아를 상대로 준비하는 원정에 동행할 작정이었습니다. 모든 무기를 다루는 개인의 기량에는 통달했으니, 이제 저는 전장에서 군대를 지휘하는 일에 관한 더 높은 지식을 구합니다. 집정관은 저를 그의 군사 막료의 한 사람으로 받아 주셨습니다. 그런데 어제, 우리 배가 오론테스강으로 들어설 때, 다른 두 척의 배가 노란 깃발을 달고 우리와 함께 들어왔습니다. 키프로스에서 온 같은 배의 동승객이자 동향 사람이 설명하기를, 그 배들이 안티오크의 대상인 시모니데스의 것이라 했습니다. 그는 또한 그 상인이 누구인지도 들려주었으니, 그가 거둔 놀라운 상업적 성공, 그의 선단과 대상(隊商), 그리고 그것들이 오가는 모습을 이야기했습니다. 그리고 제가 그 이야기에 함께 듣는 다른 이들보다 더 깊은 관심이 있는 줄도 모른 채, 그는 시모니데스가 유대인이며, 한때 후르 왕자의 종이었다고 말했습니다. 또한 그는 그라투스의 잔혹함도, 그것을 가한 목적도 숨기지 않았습니다.\"\n\n이 말이 나오자 시모니데스는 고개를 숙였고, 마치 그가 제 감정을 감추는 것을 돕기라도 하려는 듯, 또 제 깊은 동정심을 감추려는 듯, 딸은 그의 목에 얼굴을 묻었다. 이내 그는 눈을 들고, 맑은 목소리로 말했다. \"듣고 있소.\"\n\n\"오, 어지신 시모니데스 어른이여!\" 그러자 벤허는 한 걸음 다가서며, 온 영혼이 표현을 갈구하는 듯 말했다. \"당신께서 아직 납득하지 못하셨고, 제가 여전히 당신의 불신이라는 그늘 속에 서 있음을 압니다.\"\n\n상인은 대리석처럼 표정을 굳히고, 혀도 그대로 놀리지 않았다.\n\n\"그리고 제 처지의 어려움 또한 똑똑히 압니다.\" 벤허가 말을 이었다. \"저의 로마 쪽 관계는 모두 증명할 수 있습니다. 지금 도시 총독의 손님으로 와 있는 집정관을 부르기만 하면 됩니다. 그러나 저에 대한 당신의 요구, 그 자세한 부분은 증명할 수가 없습니다. 제가 제 아버지의 아들임을 증명할 수가 없습니다. 그 일에서 저를 도와줄 수 있던 이들은—아아! 그들은 죽었거나 사라졌습니다.\"\n\n그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러자 에스더가 일어나, 물렸던 잔을 그에게 가져가 말했다. \"이 포도주는 우리 모두 그토록 사랑하는 고향의 것입니다. 부디 드십시오!\"\n\n그 목소리는 나홀 성 가까운 우물에서 마실 것을 권하던 리브가의 목소리만큼이나 달콤했다. 그는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고인 것을 보았고, 그래서 잔을 마시며 말했다. \"시모니데스의 따님이여, 당신의 마음은 선함으로 가득하군요. 그리고 낯선 이가 당신 아버지와 더불어 그 선함을 나누게 해 주시니, 당신은 자비로운 분이십니다. 우리 하느님의 축복이 당신께 깃들기를! 고맙습니다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 다시 상인을 향해 말했다.\n\n\"제가 제 아버지의 아들이라는 증거가 없으니, 시모니데스 어른이여, 저는 당신께 요구했던 것을 거두고, 더 이상 당신을 괴롭히지 않으려 이곳을 떠나겠습니다. 다만 이 말씀만은 드리겠습니다. 저는 당신을 다시 종으로 되돌리려 한 것도, 당신 재산의 셈을 따지려 한 것도 아닙니다. 어떤 경우라도 저는, 지금 말씀드리는 그대로, 당신의 노고와 천재성이 낳은 모든 것은 당신의 것이라 말했을 것입니다. 부디 마음 놓고 간직하십시오. 저는 그 가운데 어느 것도 필요치 않습니다. 어진 퀸투스, 제 둘째 아버지께서 마지막이 된 그 항해에 오르실 때, 그분은 저를 상속자로 삼으셨으니, 저는 왕자처럼 부유합니다. 그러니 혹여 다시 저를 떠올리신다면, 부디 이 물음과 함께 기억해 주십시오. 예언자들과 당신의 하느님이자 나의 하느님이신 여호와를 두고 맹세하건대, 이것이야말로 제가 이곳에 온 으뜸가는 목적이었습니다. 당신은 무엇을 아십니까—제게 무엇을 들려주실 수 있습니까—제 어머니와, 제 누이 티르자에 대하여 말입니다. 그 애는 살아 있다면, 당신 삶의 달콤함이자, 어쩌면 당신 목숨 그 자체인 이분만큼이나 아름답고 우아할 것입니다. 오! 그들에 대해 당신은 제게 무엇을 들려주실 수 있습니까?\"\n\n에스더의 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그러나 그 사람은 고집스러웠으니, 맑은 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 벤허 왕자를 알았다고 말했소. 그의 가문에 닥친 불행을 들었던 것도 기억하오. 그것을 듣고 얼마나 비통했는지도 기억하오. 내 벗의 미망인에게 그토록 큰 비참을 안긴 자가, 바로 그 뒤에 같은 마음으로 내게도 손을 댄 자요. 더 말하리다. 나는 그 가족에 관하여 부지런히 수소문했으나—그들에 대해 당신에게 들려줄 것이 아무것도 없소. 그들은 사라졌소.\"\n\n벤허는 깊은 신음을 토했다.\n\n\"그렇다면—그렇다면 또 하나의 희망이 꺾인 것이군요!\" 그가 감정과 싸우며 말했다. \"저는 실망에는 익숙합니다. 제가 끼어든 것을 부디 용서하십시오. 그리고 제가 당신께 폐를 끼쳤다면, 제 슬픔을 보아 용서해 주십시오. 이제 제게는 복수 말고는 살아갈 이유가 없습니다. 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n휘장 앞에서 그는 돌아서서, 그저 이렇게 말했다. \"두 분께 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"평화가 당신과 함께하기를.\" 상인이 말했다.\n\n에스더는 흐느끼느라 말을 할 수 없었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n벤허가 떠나기 무섭게, 시모니데스는 잠에서 깨어나기라도 한 듯했다. 그의 얼굴에는 붉은 기운이 돌았고, 침울하던 눈빛은 환하게 바뀌었다. 그는 활기차게 말했다.\n\n\"에스더, 종을 쳐라—어서!\"\n\n그녀는 탁자로 가서 호출용 종을 울렸다.\n\n벽의 널빤지 하나가 뒤로 젖혀지며 출입구가 드러났고, 거기로 한 사내가 들어와 상인의 앞으로 돌아와서는 반쯤 절하는 인사로 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"말루크, 이리로—더 가까이—의자 곁으로.\" 주인이 명령하듯 말했다. \"해가 진다 해도 실패해서는 안 될 임무가 있다. 잘 들어라! 한 젊은이가 지금 창고로 내려가고 있다—키가 크고, 잘생겼으며, 이스라엘 사람의 차림을 했다. 그를 뒤따르되, 그림자도 그보다 더 충실하지 못할 만큼 따라붙어라. 그리고 밤마다 그가 어디 있는지, 무엇을 하는지, 누구와 어울리는지 내게 보고하라. 만일 들키지 않고 그의 대화를 엿들을 수 있다면, 한 마디도 빠짐없이 그대로 전하고, 그를 드러내 줄 만한 것—그의 습관, 동기, 생활—은 무엇이든 함께 알려라. 알아들었느냐? 어서 가라! 잠깐, 말루크. 그가 도시를 떠나거든, 그를 따라가라—그리고 명심해라, 말루크, 벗처럼 굴어라. 그가 네게 말을 걸거든, 그때그때 가장 알맞은 대로 무엇이든 말하되, 다만 네가 내 밑에서 일한다는 것만은—그것만은 한 마디도 입에 담지 마라. 서둘러라—어서 서둘러!\"\n\n사내는 아까처럼 절하고는 사라졌다.\n\n그러자 시모니데스는 핏기 없는 두 손을 비비며 웃었다.\n\n\"오늘이 며칠이냐, 딸아?\" 그는 그 들뜬 기분 속에서 물었다. \"오늘이 며칠이지? 찾아온 행복을 잊지 않으려고 그러는 게다. 자, 웃으며 헤아려 보고, 웃으며 내게 말해 다오, 에스더.\"\n\n그 즐거워하는 모습이 그녀에게는 부자연스러워 보였다. 마치 그를 그 기분에서 끌어내려는 듯, 그녀는 슬프게 대답했다. \"아, 슬프옵니다, 아버지. 제가 어찌 이날을 잊을 수 있겠어요!\"\n\n그 순간 그의 두 손이 떨어졌고, 턱은 가슴 위로 내려앉아 그의 아래 얼굴을 이룬 두툼한 살주름 속에 파묻혔다.\n\n\"옳다, 참으로 옳은 말이다, 내 딸아!\" 그는 고개를 들지 않은 채 말했다. \"오늘은 넷째 달 스무날이다. 오늘로 다섯 해 전, 네 어미 라헬이 쓰러져 숨을 거두었다. 사람들이 지금 네가 보는 이 부서진 몸으로 나를 집에 데려왔을 때, 우리는 그녀가 비탄으로 죽은 것을 알았다. 오, 내게 그녀는 엔게디 포도원의 고벨화 송이였다! 나는 향료와 함께 몰약을 거두었고, 꿀과 함께 송이꿀을 먹었다. 우리는 그녀를 외딴 곳에 뉘었다—산에 깎아 만든 무덤에, 곁에 아무도 없는 곳에. 그러나 어둠 속에서 그녀는 내게 작은 빛 하나를 남겼고, 그 빛은 세월이 흐르며 아침 햇살처럼 밝아졌다.\" 그는 손을 들어 딸의 머리 위에 얹었다. \"주여, 이제 제 에스더 안에서 잃었던 라헬이 다시 살아 있으니, 감사하나이다!\"\n\n그는 곧 고개를 들고, 문득 떠오른 생각인 듯 말했다. \"바깥은 환한 대낮이 아니냐?\"\n\n\"그랬어요, 그 젊은이가 들어왔을 때는요.\"\n\n\"그러면 아비멜렉을 불러 나를 정원으로 데려가게 하여라. 거기서 강과 배들을 볼 수 있으니. 그리고 들려주마, 사랑하는 에스더야, 어찌하여 방금 내 입이 웃음으로 가득 차고, 내 혀가 노래로 차올랐으며, 내 영혼이 노루처럼, 향기로운 산 위의 어린 사슴처럼 뛰놀았는지를.\"\n\n종소리에 응답하여 하인 하나가 왔다. 그녀의 분부에 따라 그는 그 일을 위해 작은 바퀴를 단 의자를 밀어, 방을 나와 아래층 집의 지붕으로 옮겼다. 그곳을 그는 자신의 정원이라 불렀다. 장미꽃 사이로, 그리고 정성스러운 보살핌의 결실인—그러나 지금은 눈길도 받지 못하는—작은 꽃밭들을 지나, 그는 한자리로 밀려갔다. 그곳에서는 맞은편 섬 위에 늘어선 궁전 지붕들과, 점점 작아지는 원근으로 건너편 기슭까지 뻗은 다리와, 다리 아래 배들로 빼곡한 강이 한눈에 들어왔다. 그 배들은 모두 잔물결 위로 이른 햇살이 춤추는 눈부신 빛 가운데 떠 있었다. 하인은 그를 에스더와 함께 그곳에 남겨 두고 떠났다.\n\n일꾼들의 요란한 고함과 두드리고 찧는 소리도, 거의 머리 위라 할 다리 바닥을 오가는 사람들의 발소리만큼이나 그를 어지럽히지 못했다. 그 소리는 눈앞의 풍경이 그의 눈에 익숙한 만큼이나 그의 귀에 익숙했으니, 약속된 이익을 일러 주는 신호로서가 아니고서는 알아차려지지도 않았다.\n\n에스더는 의자 팔걸이에 걸터앉아 그의 손을 어루만지며, 그가 입을 열기를 기다렸다. 마침내 그 말은 차분하게 흘러나왔으니, 강인한 의지가 그를 다시 제 모습으로 돌려놓았던 것이다.\n\n\"그 젊은이가 말하고 있을 때, 에스더, 내가 너를 지켜보았다. 그리고 네가 그에게 마음이 끌렸다고 생각했다.\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 내리뜨며 대답했다.\n\n\"믿음을 두고 말씀하시는 거라면, 아버지, 저는 그를 믿었어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 네 눈에 그는 후르 공의 잃어버린 아들이더냐?\"\n\n\"만일 그가 아니라면—\" 그녀는 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"그가 아니라면 어떻다는 게냐, 에스더?\"\n\n\"제 어머니가 주 하느님의 부르심에 응하신 뒤로, 아버지, 저는 아버지의 시녀였습니다. 아버지 곁에서 저는, 거룩하든 거룩하지 않든 이익을 좇는 온갖 사람들을 아버지가 지혜롭게 다루시는 것을 듣고 보았어요. 이제 저는 말씀드립니다. 만일 그 젊은이가 정녕 자기가 말하는 그 공자가 아니라면, 일찍이 제 앞에서 거짓이 그토록 의로운 진실의 역할을 잘 해낸 적은 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"솔로몬의 영광에 두고, 딸아, 너는 진지하게 말하는구나. 네 아버지가 그의 아버지의 종이었다고 믿느냐?\"\n\n\"그저 들은 이야기로서 그것을 물으시는 것이라고 저는 이해했어요.\"\n\n한동안 시모니데스의 눈길은 떠 있는 배들 사이를 헤맸으나, 그 배들은 그의 마음속에 자리하지 못했다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 착한 아이다, 에스더. 진정한 유대인의 영민함을 지녔고, 슬픈 이야기를 들을 만한 나이와 굳셈을 갖추었다. 그러니 내 말에 귀를 기울이거라. 나 자신에 대해, 네 어머니에 대해, 그리고 네가 알지도 못하고 꿈꾸지도 못한 지난날의 많은 일들에 대해 일러 주마—희망을 위하여 박해하는 로마인에게 숨겨 두었고, 또한 네게는 숨겼으니, 네 천성이 갈대가 해를 향해 곧게 자라듯 주를 향해 곧게 자라기를 바랐기 때문이다…… 나는 시온 남쪽, 힌놈 골짜기의 한 무덤에서 태어났다. 내 아버지와 어머니는 히브리인 노예로, 실로암 가까이 왕의 정원에서 자라는 무화과나무와 올리브나무, 그리고 수많은 포도나무를 돌보는 사람들이었다. 어린 시절 나는 그들을 도왔다. 그들은 영원히 종으로 매인 부류였다. 그들은 나를 후르 공에게 팔았는데, 그분은 그때 헤롯 왕 다음으로 예루살렘에서 가장 부유한 사람이었다. 그 정원에서 그분은 나를 이집트 알렉산드리아에 있는 자신의 창고로 옮겼고, 거기서 나는 성년이 되었다. 나는 그분을 여섯 해 동안 섬겼고, 일곱째 해에는 모세의 율법에 따라 자유의 몸이 되었다.\"\n\n에스더는 가볍게 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n\"오, 그러면 아버지는 그분 아버지의 종이 아니시군요!\"\n\n\"아니다, 딸아, 들어 보아라. 그 시절 성전 회랑에는 격렬히 논쟁하는 율법학자들이 있었다. 그들은 영원히 매인 종의 자식은 그 부모의 신세를 그대로 물려받는다고 말했다. 그러나 후르 공은 모든 일에 의로운 분이었고, 비록 그 무리에 속하지는 않았으나 가장 엄격한 학파를 따르는 율법 해석자였다. 그분은 내가 위대한 율법 제정자의 참된 뜻에 따라 사들인 히브리인 종이라 말씀하시고는, 내가 아직도 간직하고 있는 봉인된 문서로써 나를 자유롭게 해 주셨다.\"\n\n\"그러면 제 어머니는요?\" 에스더가 물었다.\n\n\"다 듣게 될 게다, 에스더. 참고 기다려라. 이야기를 마치기 전에, 내가 네 어머니를 잊느니 차라리 나 자신을 잊는 편이 더 쉬웠으리라는 걸 너도 알게 될 게다…… 종살이를 마친 뒤, 나는 유월절을 맞아 예루살렘으로 올라갔다. 주인께서는 나를 손님으로 맞아 주셨다. 나는 이미 그분을 사랑하고 있었기에, 계속 그분을 섬기게 해 달라고 청했다. 그분이 허락하여, 나는 또 일곱 해를 더 그분을 섬겼으나, 이번에는 품삯을 받는 이스라엘의 아들로서였다. 그분을 위하여 나는 배로 바다를 건너는 사업과, 대상을 거느리고 육로로 동쪽 수사와 페르세폴리스, 그 너머 비단의 땅까지 가는 사업을 맡았다. 위험천만한 길이었다, 내 딸아. 그러나 주께서는 내가 손댄 모든 일에 복을 주셨다. 나는 공에게 막대한 이익을 안겨 드렸고, 나 자신에게는 더 값진 지식을 가져왔으니, 그것이 없었다면 그 뒤로 내게 맡겨진 일들을 감당하지 못했을 게다…… 어느 날 나는 예루살렘에 있는 그분의 집에서 손님으로 머물고 있었다. 한 하녀가 저며 놓은 빵을 쟁반에 담아 들고 들어왔다. 그녀가 먼저 내게로 왔다. 그때 나는 네 어머니를 보았고, 그녀를 사랑하게 되었으며, 마음 깊은 곳에 그녀를 품었다. 얼마 뒤 한 때가 와서, 나는 공을 찾아가 그녀를 아내로 삼게 해 달라고 청했다. 그분은 그녀가 영원히 매인 종이라 하시면서도, 그녀가 원한다면 나를 흡족하게 하고자 자유롭게 해 주겠다고 하셨다. 그녀는 사랑에 사랑으로 응했으나, 자기가 있는 곳에서 행복하다며 자유를 마다했다. 나는 빌고 또 간청하며, 긴 세월을 두고 거듭 그녀를 찾아갔다. 내가 종살이의 동무가 되어 준다면 아내가 되겠노라고, 그녀는 한결같이 말했다. 우리 조상 야곱도 라헬을 얻으려고 또 일곱 해를 섬겼다. 내 라헬을 위해서라고 그만큼 못할쏘냐? 그러나 네 어머니는 내가 그녀처럼 영원히 종으로 섬기는 몸이 되어야 한다고 말했다. 나는 그곳을 떠났지만, 다시 돌아갔다. 보아라, 에스더, 여기를 보아라.\"\n\n그는 자신의 왼쪽 귓불을 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"송곳 자국이 보이지 않느냐?\"\n\n\"보여요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 오, 아버지가 제 어머니를 얼마나 사랑하셨는지 알겠어요!\"\n\n\"사랑했고말고, 에스더! 그녀는 내게 노래하는 임금에게 술람미 여인이 그러했던 것보다 더한 존재였다. 더 아름답고, 더 흠 없는, 동산의 샘이요, 생수의 우물이며, 레바논에서 흘러내리는 시내였다. 주인께서는 내가 청한 대로 나를 재판관들에게 데려갔다가 다시 그분의 문으로 데려와, 송곳으로 내 귀를 꿰뚫어 문에 박으셨고, 그리하여 나는 영원히 그분의 종이 되었다. 그렇게 나는 내 라헬을 얻었다. 일찍이 내 사랑과 같은 사랑이 또 있었더냐?\"\n\n에스더는 몸을 숙여 그에게 입을 맞추었고, 두 사람은 죽은 이를 생각하며 말없이 있었다.\n\n\"주인께서는 바다에서 익사하셨다. 내게 닥친 첫 번째 슬픔이었다.\" 상인은 말을 이었다. \"그분의 집에도, 그리고 그 무렵 내가 머물던 이곳 안티오크 내 집에도 애도가 가득했다. 자, 에스더, 잘 들어라! 그 어진 공이 세상을 떠나셨을 때, 나는 그분의 수석 집사 자리에 올라, 그분께 속한 모든 재산이 내 관리와 통제 아래 있었다. 그분이 나를 얼마나 사랑하고 믿으셨는지 헤아려 보아라! 나는 미망인께 회계를 보고하려고 서둘러 예루살렘으로 갔다. 그분은 나를 그대로 집사 자리에 두셨다. 나는 더욱 부지런히 일에 매달렸다. 사업은 번창했고, 해마다 자라났다. 열 해가 흘렀다. 그러던 어느 날, 네가 그 젊은이의 입으로 들은 바로 그 일격이 닥쳤다—그가 사고라고 부른, 총독 그라투스에게 일어난 그 일이었다. 그 로마인은 그것을 자기를 암살하려던 기도라고 떠벌렸다. 그 구실로, 로마의 허락을 받아, 그는 미망인과 그 자녀들의 막대한 재산을 몰수하여 제 것으로 삼았다. 거기서 멈추지도 않았다. 판결이 뒤집히는 일이 없도록, 그는 이해 당사자를 모조리 없애 버렸다. 그 끔찍한 날부터 오늘까지 후르 가문은 자취를 감추었다. 어릴 적 내가 보았던 그 아들은 갤리선 노예로 선고받았다. 미망인과 딸은 유대의 수많은 지하 감옥 어딘가에 갇혔으리라 짐작될 뿐이다. 그곳은 한번 죄인 위로 닫히면 봉인되고 잠긴 무덤과 다를 바 없다. 그들은, 바다가 아무도 모르게 삼켜 버리기라도 한 듯, 사람들의 기억에서 완전히 사라졌다. 우리는 그들이 어떻게 죽었는지 들을 수 없었다—아니, 죽었다는 것조차 알 수 없었다.\"\n\n에스더의 눈에는 이슬 같은 눈물이 맺혔다.\n\n\"네 마음은 착하다, 에스더. 네 어머니의 마음이 그러했듯 착하다. 다만 그 마음이 대개의 착한 마음이 맞는 운명—무자비하고 눈먼 자들에게 짓밟히는 운명—을 겪지 않기를 나는 기도한다. 그러나 더 들어라. 나는 은인을 도우려고 예루살렘으로 올라갔다가, 성문에서 붙잡혀 안토니아 요새의 지하 감방으로 끌려갔다. 까닭은 몰랐으나, 마침내 그라투스가 직접 와서 후르 가문의 돈을 내게 요구했다. 우리 유대인의 환전 관습에 따라 그 돈이 세계 곳곳의 시장에서 내 어음으로 인출될 수 있음을 그는 알고 있었던 것이다. 그는 자기 명령서에 서명하라고 요구했다. 나는 거절했다. 그는 내가 섬기던 이들의 집과 땅과 재물과 배와 동산은 손에 넣었으나, 그들의 돈만은 손에 넣지 못했다. 나는 보았다. 내가 주 보시기에 은총을 잃지 않는다면, 무너진 그들의 재산을 다시 일으킬 수 있으리라는 것을. 나는 그 폭군의 요구를 거절했다. 그는 나를 고문했다. 그러나 내 의지는 굳건했고, 그는 아무것도 얻지 못한 채 나를 풀어 주었다. 나는 집으로 돌아와, 예루살렘의 후르 공이 아니라 안티오크의 시모니데스라는 이름으로 다시 시작했다. 너도 알다시피, 에스더, 나는 번창했다. 내 손안에서 공의 수백만 재산이 불어난 것은 기적과도 같았다. 너도 알다시피, 세 해가 지나 카이사레아로 올라가던 길에 나는 또다시 붙잡혀, 내 재물과 돈이 그의 몰수 명령에 종속된다는 자백을 받아 내려는 그라투스에게 두 번째로 고문당했다. 너도 알다시피, 그는 전과 마찬가지로 실패했다. 몸이 부서진 채 집으로 돌아와 보니, 내 라헬이 나에 대한 두려움과 비탄으로 죽어 있었다. 우리 주 하느님께서 다스리셨고, 나는 살아남았다. 나는 황제에게서 직접 면책권과 온 세계에서 교역할 허가를 사들였다. 오늘날—구름을 수레로 삼고 바람 위를 거니시는 그분을 찬양할지어다!—오늘날, 에스더, 집사로서 내 손에 맡겨졌던 그것이 불어나, 카이사르를 부유하게 만들고도 남을 만한 달란트가 되었다.\"\n\n그는 자랑스레 고개를 들었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 서로의 생각을 읽었다. \"이 재물을 어찌하면 좋겠느냐, 에스더?\" 그는 시선을 거두지 않은 채 물었다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 그녀는 나직한 목소리로 대답했다. \"정당한 주인이 방금 그것을 찾으러 오지 않았나요?\"\n\n그래도 그의 눈길은 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n\"그러면 너는, 내 아이야, 내가 너를 거지로 남겨 두어야 하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아버지. 제가 아버지의 자식이기에, 저 또한 그분의 매인 종이 아닌가요? 그리고 누구에 대하여 이렇게 기록되었나요. '힘과 위엄이 그의 옷이며, 그가 장차 올 날에 기뻐하리라'고요?\"\n\n이루 말할 수 없는 사랑의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 밝혔다. 그가 말했다. \"주께서는 여러 가지로 내게 선하셨다. 그러나 너, 에스더, 너는 그 은총 가운데 으뜸가는 보배다.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안고 여러 번 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"이제 들어라.\" 그는 한결 또렷해진 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 오늘 아침 어찌하여 웃었는지를 이제 들어라. 그 젊은이는 잘생긴 젊은 시절의 제 아버지 모습 그대로 내 앞에 나타났다. 내 영혼은 그를 맞으려고 일어섰다. 나는 내 시련의 날들이 끝났고 내 노고도 다했음을 느꼈다. 소리쳐 부르짖지 않기가 어려울 지경이었다. 나는 그의 손을 잡고, 내가 벌어 놓은 잔고를 보이며 이렇게 말하고 싶었다. '보십시오, 이 모든 것이 당신 것입니다! 그리고 저는 당신의 종이니, 이제 언제든 부르심을 받을 채비가 되어 있습니다.' 그렇게 했을 게다, 에스더, 정말 그렇게 했을 게다. 그러나 그 순간 세 가지 생각이 밀려와 나를 붙들었다. 나는 그가 내 주인의 아들임을 확실히 해야겠다—이것이 첫 번째 생각이었다. 그가 내 주인의 아들이라면, 나는 그의 됨됨이를 어느 정도 알아 두어야겠다. 부유하게 태어난 자들 가운데, 헤아려 보아라, 에스더, 그 손안에서 재물이 한낱 저주를 길러 내는 자가 얼마나 많은지를\"—그는 말을 멈추었고, 그 두 손은 움켜쥐어졌으며, 그 목소리는 격정으로 날카로워졌다—\"에스더, 내가 로마인의 손에 당한 고통을 생각해 보아라. 아니, 그라투스 혼자가 아니다. 처음에도 마지막에도 그의 명령을 수행한 그 무자비한 악당들도 로마인이었고, 그들은 하나같이 내 비명을 듣고 웃었다. 부서진 내 몸과, 키마저 줄어든 채 보낸 세월을 생각해 보아라. 외딴 무덤에 누운 네 어머니가, 내가 몸이 으스러진 만큼 영혼이 으스러졌음을 생각해 보아라. 내 주인의 가족이 살아 있다면 그들의 슬픔을, 죽었다면 그들이 죽임당한 잔혹함을 생각해 보아라. 이 모든 것을 생각하고, 하늘의 사랑이 네 둘레에 깃든 채로, 내게 말해 보아라, 딸아. 그 속죄로 머리카락 하나 떨어지거나 붉은 핏방울 하나 흐르지 않아도 좋겠느냐? 설교자들이 더러 그러듯—복수는 주의 것이라고는 내게 말하지 마라. 그분은 사랑으로뿐 아니라 도구를 통해 해롭게도 당신의 뜻을 이루지 않으시더냐? 그분께는 예언자보다 더 많은 군사가 있지 않더냐? 눈에는 눈, 손에는 손, 발에는 발이라는 것이 그분의 율법이 아니더냐? 오, 이 모든 세월 동안 나는 복수를 꿈꾸었고, 그것을 위해 기도하며 대비했고, 내 재물이 불어나는 데서 인내를 길어 올렸다. 주께서 살아 계심같이, 이것이 언젠가 죄지은 자들에게 형벌을 사다 주리라고, 그렇게 생각하고 다짐하면서 말이다. 그리고 그 젊은이가 자기 무예 수련을 두고 이름 붙일 수 없는 목적을 위한 것이라고 말했을 때, 나는 그가 말하는 바로 그 순간에 그 목적에 이름을 붙였다—복수! 그리고 그것, 에스더, 바로 그것이—그가 호소하는 동안 나를 잠잠하고 굳세게 붙들어 두었으며, 그가 떠난 뒤 나를 웃게 만든 세 번째 생각이었다.\"\n\n에스더는 그 시든 두 손을 어루만지며, 마치 자기 영혼이 그의 영혼과 함께 그 결말을 향해 달려가기라도 하듯 말했다. \"그가 떠났어요. 다시 올까요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 충실한 말루크가 그와 함께 가니, 내가 준비되면 그를 데려올 게다.\"\n\n\"그게 언제일까요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"머지않다, 머지않았다. 그는 제 모든 증인이 죽었다고 여긴다. 그러나 그가 정녕 내 주인의 아들이라면, 그를 알아보는 데 결코 실패하지 않을 산 증인이 하나 있다.\"\n\n\"그의 어머니인가요?\"\n\n\"아니다, 딸아. 나는 그 증인을 그 앞에 세울 것이다. 그때까지는 이 일을 주께 맡기고 쉬도록 하자. 나는 피곤하구나. 아비멜렉을 불러라.\"\n\n에스더는 하인을 불렀고, 그들은 집 안으로 돌아갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n벤허가 그 거대한 창고를 박차고 나왔을 때, 그의 머릿속에는 가족을 찾는 일에서 이미 숱하게 맛본 실패에 또 하나의 실패가 더해지리라는 생각뿐이었다. 그 생각은 찾고자 하는 대상이 그에게 소중한 만큼이나 정확히 그를 짓눌렀다. 그 생각은 그를 이 땅에서 더없이 외롭다는 느낌으로 에워쌌으니, 그 무엇보다도 그것은 풀이 죽은 영혼에게 삶에 대한 마지막 미련마저 쥐어짜 내게 마련이었다.\n\n그는 사람들 사이로, 또 쌓아 올린 짐짝들 사이로 길을 헤쳐 부두 끝까지 나아갔다. 강의 깊은 곳을 어둑하게 물들이는 서늘한 그늘이 그를 유혹했다. 나른한 물살은 멈춰 서서 그를 기다리는 것만 같았다. 그 마법에 맞서려는 듯, 그 뱃사람의 말이 문득 기억 속에 떠올랐다—\"임금의 손님이 되느니, 차라리 한 마리 벌레가 되어 다프네의 뽕나무 열매를 먹는 편이 낫다.\" 그는 몸을 돌려, 부두를 빠르게 걸어 내려와 다시 여관으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"다프네로 가는 길 말입니까!\" 벤허가 던진 물음에 놀라며 여관 지배인이 말했다. \"전에 이곳에 와 보신 적이 없습니까? 그렇다면 오늘을 평생 가장 행복한 날로 치십시오. 길은 결코 잘못 들 수가 없습니다. 남쪽으로 가다 보면 왼편으로 다음 거리가 나오는데, 그 길이 술피우스산으로 곧장 이어집니다. 그 산꼭대기에는 유피테르의 제단과 원형극장이 있지요. 그 길을 따라 세 번째 교차로까지 가십시오. 헤롯의 주랑(柱廊)이라 불리는 곳입니다. 거기서 오른쪽으로 꺾어, 셀레우코스의 옛 도시를 가로질러 에피파네스의 청동 문까지 가시면 됩니다. 거기서 다프네로 가는 길이 시작됩니다—부디 신들의 가호가 함께하기를!\"\n\n짐에 관한 몇 가지 지시를 남기고, 벤허는 길을 나섰다.\n\n헤롯의 주랑은 쉽게 찾을 수 있었다. 거기서부터 청동 문까지, 끊임없이 이어진 대리석 주랑 아래로, 그는 이 땅의 온갖 교역하는 나라에서 모여든 사람들 무리에 섞여 지나갔다.\n\n그가 문을 나선 것은 하루의 네 번째 시각쯤이었는데, 그는 어느새 끝이 보이지 않는 행렬의 한 사람이 되어 그 유명한 숲을 향해 나아가고 있었다. 길은 걷는 이를 위한 길, 말 탄 이를 위한 길, 전차를 탄 이를 위한 길로 갈라져 있었고, 그것들은 다시 나가는 이와 들어오는 이를 위한 길로 나뉘어 있었다. 그 경계선은 낮은 난간으로 지켜져 있었는데, 그 난간은 육중한 받침대들로 끊겨 있었고, 그 받침대 위에는 조각상을 얹은 것이 많았다. 길 좌우로는 완벽하게 손질된 잔디밭이 펼쳐졌고, 군데군데 떡갈나무와 무화과나무 무리가 그 단조로움을 덜어 주었으며, 지친 이들을 위한 포도 덩굴 우거진 정자들도 있었으니, 돌아오는 쪽 길에는 그런 이들이 늘 무리 지어 있었다. 걷는 이의 길은 붉은 돌로 포장되었고, 말 탄 이의 길은 단단히 다진 흰 모래가 뿌려져 있었는데, 발굽이나 바퀴 소리가 메아리치지 않을 만큼은 부드러웠다. 솟구치는 분수의 수효와 그 갖가지 모양은 놀라울 따름이었으니, 모두가 이곳을 찾았던 임금들이 바친 선물이라 그들의 이름을 따서 불렀다. 남서쪽으로 숲의 문까지, 그 웅장한 대로는 도시에서 6킬로미터 남짓 뻗어 있었다.\n\n비참한 심정에 빠져 있던 벤허는 그 길의 건설에 새겨진 왕다운 베풂을 거의 눈여겨보지 않았다. 처음에는 자기와 함께 가는 군중도 마찬가지로 알아차리지 못했다. 행렬이 펼치는 볼거리도 똑같이 무심하게 지나쳤다. 사실을 말하자면, 자기 생각에 빠져 있는 데다, 그는 로마인 특유의 우월감을 적잖이 지니고 있었으니, 곧 아우구스투스가 세계의 중심으로 세운 황금 기둥 둘레로 날마다 소용돌이치는 의식들을 막 보고 떠나온 자가 속주를 찾았을 때 갖는 그 우월감이었다. 속주가 무언가 새롭거나 더 뛰어난 것을 내놓을 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 그는 오히려 기회 있을 때마다 길에 늘어선 무리를 헤치고 앞으로 나아가려 했으니, 그 발걸음이 조급한 그에게는 너무 더뎠던 까닭이다. 도시와 숲 사이에 있는 변두리 마을 헤라클레이아에 다다랐을 무렵, 그는 걷느라 다소 지쳐 있었고, 비로소 구경거리에 마음이 끌리기 시작했다. 한번은 아름다운 여인이 끌고 가는 염소 두 마리가 그의 눈길을 끌었는데, 여인이나 염소나 모두 리본과 꽃으로 화려하게 꾸며져 있었다. 그러고는 걸음을 멈추고, 허리둘레가 어마어마하고 눈처럼 새하얀 황소를 바라보았으니, 그 황소는 막 베어 낸 포도 덩굴로 뒤덮여 있었고, 넓은 등에는 바구니에 담긴 벌거벗은 어린아이를 태우고 있었다. 그 아이는 어린 바쿠스의 형상으로, 잘 익은 열매의 즙을 술잔에 짜 넣고는 헌주(獻酒)의 격식을 읊으며 마시고 있었다. 다시 걸음을 옮기면서, 그는 그 제물들로 어느 신의 제단이 풍성해질지 궁금해했다. 그때의 풍습대로 갈기를 짧게 깎은 말 한 마리가 지나갔는데, 그 말을 탄 이는 더없이 화려한 차림이었다. 그는 사람과 짐승 사이에 어우러진 그 거만함의 조화를 보고 미소 지었다. 그 뒤로도 그는 자주 바퀴 구르는 소리와 둔탁한 발굽 소리에 고개를 돌렸으니, 자기도 모르게 그의 곁을 스쳐 가고 오는 전차와 마부들의 갖가지 모양에 흥미를 느끼기 시작한 것이다. 곧이어 그는 주위 사람들도 눈여겨보기 시작했다. 그들은 나이도 성별도 신분도 갖가지였으나, 모두 명절 차림이었다. 한 무리는 흰옷으로, 다른 무리는 검은옷으로 갖춰 입었다. 어떤 이는 깃발을 들었고, 어떤 이는 연기 나는 향로를 들었다. 어떤 이는 찬가를 부르며 천천히 걸었고, 다른 이는 피리와 작은북 소리에 발을 맞추었다. 일 년 내내 날마다 이렇게 다프네로 가는 행렬이 이어진다면, 다프네는 얼마나 경이로운 곳이란 말인가! 마침내 손뼉 치는 소리와 기쁨에 찬 환호성이 터져 나왔다. 수많은 손가락이 가리키는 곳을 따라 바라보니, 언덕 마루 위로 신성한 숲의 신전 같은 문이 보였다. 찬가는 더욱 우렁찬 가락으로 부풀어 올랐고, 음악은 박자를 빨리했다. 그는 그 충동적인 물결에 떠밀려 가며, 모두가 품은 열망을 함께 나누는 가운데 안으로 들어섰으니, 취향은 로마에 물든 자였건만 그 또한 그곳을 경배하기 시작했다.\n\n들머리를 꾸미는 건축물—순수한 그리스 양식의 건물—뒤편에서, 그는 윤이 나는 돌로 포장된 널찍한 광장에 섰다. 그의 둘레에는 더없이 화사한 빛깔로 차려입은 군중이 끊임없이 환호성을 올리고 있었으니, 분수에서 수정처럼 새하얗게 흩날리는 무지갯빛 물보라가 그 뒤로 그들을 돋보이게 했다. 그의 앞에는, 남서쪽으로 멀리, 먼지 한 점 없는 오솔길들이 정원으로 뻗어 나갔고, 그 너머로는 숲으로 이어졌으며, 그 위로는 옅은 푸른빛 안개의 너울이 드리워 있었다. 벤허는 어디로 가야 할지 망설이며 애틋한 눈길로 바라보았다. 그때 한 여인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"아름다워라! 그런데 이제 어디로 가지?\"\n\n월계수 화관을 쓴 동행이 웃으며 대답했다. \"이런, 어여쁜 야만인 같으니! 그 물음에는 속세의 두려움이 배어 있구려. 그 녹슨 흙먼지와 함께 그런 두려움일랑 모조리 안티오크에 두고 오기로 하지 않았소? 여기 부는 바람은 신들의 숨결이라오. 우리 그 바람이 부는 대로 몸을 맡깁시다.\"\n\n\"그래도 길을 잃으면 어쩌지요?\"\n\n\"오, 겁 많은 이여! 다프네에서 길을 잃은 사람은 아무도 없소. 다프네의 문이 영영 닫혀 버린 자들이라면 모를까.\"\n\n\"그게 누군데요?\" 그녀가 여전히 두려워하며 물었다.\n\n\"이곳의 매혹에 굴복하여, 여기서 살다 죽기로 작정한 자들이오. 들어 보시오! 여기 가만히 서 있으면, 내가 말하는 이들이 누구인지 보여 주리다.\"\n\n대리석 포장 위로 샌들 신은 발들이 종종거리는 소리가 났다. 군중이 갈라지더니, 한 무리의 처녀들이 말하던 이와 그의 어여쁜 벗을 둘러싸고 몰려와, 저희가 직접 두드리는 작은북에 맞추어 노래하고 춤추기 시작했다. 여인은 겁에 질려 사내에게 매달렸고, 사내는 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안은 채, 상기된 얼굴로 다른 한 손을 머리 위로 들어 음악에 박자를 맞추었다. 춤추는 이들의 머리칼은 풀어 헤쳐져 휘날렸고, 그 사지는 거의 가리지도 못하는 얇은 비단옷 사이로 발그레하게 비쳤다. 그 춤의 관능을 말로는 다 옮길 수 없다. 한 차례 짧게 돌고 나서, 그들은 처음 왔던 것처럼 가볍게 갈라지는 군중 사이로 날래게 사라졌다.\n\n\"자, 어떻소?\" 사내가 여인에게 외쳤다.\n\n\"저들은 누구지요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"데바다시라오—아폴로 신전에 바쳐진 무녀들이지. 그 수가 한 부대를 이룬다오. 축제 때 합창대를 이루는 이들이오. 이곳이 그들의 집이지. 때로는 다른 도시로 떠돌기도 하지만, 그들이 버는 것은 모조리 이곳으로 가져와 그 신성한 악사의 집을 풍성하게 한다오. 이제 가 볼까?\"\n\n다음 순간 두 사람은 사라졌다.\n\n벤허는 다프네에서 길을 잃은 사람은 아무도 없다는 그 장담에 위안을 얻고, 그 또한 길을 나섰다—어디로 가는지는 그 자신도 알지 못했다.\n\n정원의 아름다운 받침대 위에 세워진 한 조각상이 먼저 그를 끌었다. 그것은 켄타우로스의 상이었다. 새겨진 글귀가 무지한 방문객에게 일러 주기를, 그것은 아폴로와 디아나의 사랑을 받은 키론을 그대로 본뜬 것으로, 키론은 그 두 신에게서 사냥과 의술과 음악과 예언의 신비를 전수받았다고 했다. 그 글귀는 또한 나그네더러 맑은 밤 어느 시각에 하늘의 어느 자리를 바라보라 일렀으니, 그러면 별들 사이에 살아 있는 죽은 자를 보게 되리라 했다. 유피테르가 그 선량한 정령을 그곳으로 옮겨 놓았다는 것이다.\n\n그럼에도 켄타우로스 가운데 가장 슬기로운 이 존재는 여전히 인류를 섬기고 있었다. 그는 손에 두루마리 하나를 들고 있었는데, 거기에는 그리스어로 새겨진 안내문의 여러 구절이 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"오, 나그네여!\n그대는 이방인인가?\n\n하나. 시냇물의 노래에 귀 기울이고, 분수의 빗줄기를 두려워 말라. 그리하면 물의 정령 나이아데스가 그대를 사랑하게 되리라.\n\n둘. 다프네가 청한 산들바람은 제피로스와 아우스테르라. 생명을 돌보는 다정한 사자(使者)이니, 그대를 위해 향기를 모아 주리라. 에우로스가 불 때면 디아나는 어디 다른 곳에서 사냥하는 것이요, 보레아스가 거세게 몰아칠 때면 몸을 숨기라. 아폴로가 노한 것이니.\n\n셋. 숲의 그늘은 낮에는 그대의 것이나, 밤에는 판과 그의 드리아데스의 것이라. 그들을 어지럽히지 말라.\n\n넷. 시냇가의 연꽃은 조금만 먹으라. 기억을 잃고 싶지 않다면 말이다. 기억을 잃는 것은 다프네의 자식이 되는 것이니.\n\n다섯. 베를 짜는 거미는 돌아서 지나가라—저것은 미네르바를 위해 일하는 아라크네라.\n\n여섯. 다프네의 눈물을 보고 싶다면, 월계수 가지에서 봉오리 하나만 꺾어라—그리고 죽으라.\n\n명심하라!\n그리고 머물러 행복하라.\"\n\n벤허는 그 신비로운 안내문의 풀이를 어느새 자기를 에워싸 오는 다른 이들에게 맡기고, 흰 황소가 끌려오는 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 사내아이는 바구니에 앉아 있었고, 그 뒤로 행렬이 따랐다. 그 뒤로는 다시 염소를 끄는 여인이, 그녀 뒤로는 피리와 작은북을 부는 이들이, 또 그 뒤로는 제물을 바치러 가는 또 다른 행렬이 이어졌다.\n\n\"저들은 어디로 가는 거요?\" 한 구경꾼이 물었다.\n\n다른 이가 대답했다. \"황소는 아버지 유피테르께로, 염소는—\"\n\n\"아폴로가 한때 아드메토스의 가축을 친 적이 있지 않은가?\"\n\n\"옳거니, 염소는 아폴로께로!\"\n\n여기서 다시 독자의 너그러움에 기대어 한 가지 설명을 청한다. 신앙이 다른 사람들과 오래 어울리다 보면, 종교 문제에서 어느 정도 타협하는 여유가 생기게 마련이다. 그리하여 우리는 차츰 한 가지 진리에 이르게 되니, 어느 신조든 우리의 존경을 받을 만한 선량한 이들로 빛난다는 것, 그리고 그 신조에 예의를 갖추지 않고서는 그들을 존경할 수 없다는 것이다. 벤허는 바로 이 경지에 이르러 있었다. 로마에서 보낸 세월도, 갤리선에서 보낸 세월도 그의 종교적 신앙에는 아무런 자취를 남기지 못했으니, 그는 여전히 유대인이었다. 그럼에도 그가 보기에, 다프네의 숲에서 아름다운 것을 찾는 일은 불경이 아니었다.\n\n이 말은 또 한 가지 말을 막지 않는다. 곧, 설령 그의 양심의 거리낌이 아무리 극단적이었다 하더라도, 이때만큼은 그것을 억눌렀을 법하다는 것이다. 그는 노여워하고 있었다. 사소한 일에 속이 긁혀 짜증을 부리는 사람의 노여움도 아니요, 아무것도 없는 우물에서 길어 올린 듯하여 한마디 꾸짖음이나 욕설로 흩어져 버리는 어리석은 자의 노여움도 아니었다. 그것은 가장 값진 행복들이 분명히 손에 닿는 곳에 있다 여겼던 희망—굳이 말하자면 꿈—이 느닷없이 무너지는 바람에 거칠게 깨어난, 열정적인 천성에 고유한 분노였다. 이런 경우에는 그 어떤 어중간한 것도 그 격정을 거두어 가지 못한다—그 다툼의 상대는 운명이기 때문이다.\n\n이 철리(哲理)를 조금만 더 좇아, 우리 스스로에게 이렇게 말해 두자. 그런 다툼에서, 운명이 무언가 만질 수 있는 것이어서 한 번의 눈길이나 한 번의 일격으로 처치할 수 있다거나, 또는 거친 말을 주고받을 수 있는 말 상대라도 된다면 다행이련만. 그렇다면 그 가엾은 인간은 끝내 제 자신을 벌하는 것으로 일을 마무리 짓지는 않을 텐데 말이다.\n\n여느 때 같았으면 벤허는 홀로 숲을 찾지 않았을 것이요, 설령 홀로 왔더라도 집정관 집안에서의 제 지위를 이용하여, 아는 이 하나 없이 알려지지도 않은 채 하릴없이 헤매고 다니지 않도록 채비를 갖추었을 것이다. 그는 볼 만한 곳들을 모조리 머릿속에 새겨 두고, 마치 업무를 처리하듯 안내를 받아 그곳들을 찾아갔을 것이다. 또는 그 아름다운 곳에서 한가로운 며칠을 흥청망청 보내고 싶었다면, 그 모든 것을 다스리는 주인이 누구든 그에게 보내는 소개장을 손에 쥐고 있었을 것이다. 그랬다면 그도 자기가 함께 따라온 저 떠들썩한 무리처럼 한낱 구경꾼이 되었으리라. 그러나 그는 숲의 신들에게 경의도 없었고 호기심도 없었다. 쓰라린 실망에 눈먼 사내로서, 그는 정처 없이 떠도는 신세였으니, 운명을 기다리는 것이 아니라 절박한 도전자가 되어 운명을 찾아 헤매고 있었다.\n\n이런 마음의 상태를 누구나 한 번쯤 겪어 보았을 것이다. 그 정도가 모두 같지는 않을지라도 말이다. 누구나 그것을, 겉으로는 태연한 척하면서 용감한 일을 해치웠던 그 마음 상태로 알아볼 것이다. 그리고 이 글을 읽는 누구나 이렇게 말할 것이다. 지금 벤허를 사로잡은 그 어리석음이, 호각을 불고 알록달록한 모자를 쓴 정다운 어릿광대일 뿐이요, 무자비하게 뾰족한 칼을 든 어떤 폭력이 아니라면, 벤허에게는 다행한 일이라고.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n벤허는 행렬과 함께 숲으로 들어섰다. 처음에는 그들이 어디로 가는지 물어볼 만큼의 관심조차 없었다. 그러나 완전한 무관심에서 그를 건져낸 것은, 일행이 숲의 중심을 이루며 무엇보다 강한 매력을 지닌 신전들을 향해 움직이고 있다는 막연한 짐작이었다.\n\n이윽고, 가수들이 스쳐 지나가는 합창을 꿈결처럼 가지고 노닐듯이, 그는 혼잣말로 되뇌기 시작했다. \"왕의 손님이 되느니, 차라리 한 마리 벌레가 되어 다프네의 뽕나무 열매를 먹는 편이 낫다.\" 그러다 거듭 되뇐 끝에, 답을 졸라대는 물음들이 떠올랐다. 이 숲의 삶이 그토록 달콤하단 말인가? 그 매력은 어디에 있는가? 어떤 철학의 뒤엉킨 심연 속에 깃들어 있는가? 아니면 사실 그것은 무언가, 표면에 드러나 있어 날마다 깨어 있는 감각이면 누구나 알아챌 수 있는 무언가인가? 해마다 수천 명이 세상을 버리고 이곳의 봉사에 몸을 바쳤다. 그들은 그 매력을 찾아냈을까? 그리고 찾아냈을 때, 그것은 삶의 무한히 다양한 것들을 마음에서 몰아내기에 충분할 만큼 깊은 망각을 불러올 수 있었을까? 삶을 달콤하게 하는 것들과 쓰디쓰게 하는 것들, 가까운 미래에 어른거리는 희망과 지난날에서 태어난 슬픔까지를? 이 숲이 그들에게 그토록 좋은 것이라면, 어찌하여 그에게는 좋지 않을 것인가? 그는 유대인이었다. 그 빼어남이 아브라함의 자손만 빼고 온 세상을 위한 것일 수도 있단 말인가? 그는 곧 선물을 가져가는 무리의 노래도 동료들의 농담도 아랑곳하지 않고, 온 정신을 발견의 과업에 쏟았다.\n\n그 탐구에서, 하늘은 그에게 아무것도 내주지 않았다. 하늘은 푸르렀다, 아주 푸르렀고, 재잘대는 제비들로 가득했다. 도시 위의 하늘도 그러했다.\n\n조금 더 나아가자, 오른편 숲에서 산들바람이 길을 가로질러 쏟아져 와, 장미와 타오르는 향료가 어우러진 달콤한 향기의 물결로 그를 적셨다. 그는 다른 이들이 그러듯 걸음을 멈추고, 바람이 불어오는 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"저쪽에 정원이라도 있소?\" 그는 곁에 선 사내에게 물었다.\n\n\"정원이라기보다는 어떤 사제의 의식을 치르고 있는 것이겠지요. 디아나나 판, 아니면 숲의 어느 신에게 바치는 것 말입니다.\"\n\n대답은 그의 모국어였다. 벤허는 말을 건넨 자에게 놀란 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"히브리 사람이오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n사내는 공손한 미소로 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 예루살렘 장터에서 돌 던지면 닿을 만큼 가까운 곳에서 태어났습니다.\"\n\n벤허가 말을 더 이으려는 참에, 군중이 앞으로 밀려들며 그를 길 가장자리 숲 쪽으로 떠밀고, 그 낯선 이를 휩쓸어 갔다. 흔히 입는 겉옷과 지팡이, 노란 끈으로 동여맨 갈색 머릿수건, 그리고 정직한 신분의 옷차림임을 증명하는 굳센 유대인의 얼굴이, 그 사내의 일종의 요약처럼 젊은이의 마음에 남았다.\n\n이 일은 숲으로 드는 오솔길이 시작되는 지점에서 일어났으니, 떠들썩한 행렬에서 반갑게 벗어날 길을 열어 주었다. 벤허는 그 기회를 놓치지 않았다.\n\n그는 먼저 덤불 속으로 걸어 들어갔는데, 길에서 보았을 때 그곳은 자연 그대로의 상태인 듯, 빽빽하고 뚫고 들어갈 수 없으며 들새들의 보금자리처럼 보였다. 그러나 몇 걸음 들어서자, 거기에도 주인의 손길이 닿아 있음을 알 수 있었다. 관목들은 꽃을 피우거나 열매를 맺고 있었다. 휘어진 가지 아래로 땅은 더없이 환한 꽃송이들로 단장되어 있고, 그 위로 재스민이 가녀린 덩굴을 뻗었다. 라일락과 장미, 백합과 튤립, 협죽도와 딸기나무에서, 다윗의 도성을 둘러싼 골짜기의 정원에서 일찍이 정든 모든 벗들에게서, 대기는 머무르며 혹은 서두르며 밤낮으로 향기를 가득 실었다. 그리고 님프와 물의 정령들의 기쁨에 모자람이 없도록, 꽃빛으로 환한 그늘 사이로 시냇물 하나가 부드럽게, 수없이 굽이지며 제 길을 흘러갔다.\n\n그가 나아가는 동안 덤불에서, 좌우로 비둘기의 울음과 멧비둘기의 구구거림이 새어 나왔다. 검은지빠귀들이 그를 기다리며 가까이 다가올 때까지 자리를 지켰고, 나이팅게일은 그가 팔 길이만큼 가까이 지나가도 두려움 없이 제자리에 머물렀으며, 메추라기 한 마리가 데리고 가던 새끼들에게 휘파람 소리를 내며 그의 발치 앞을 달려갔다. 그가 새끼들이 길을 비키도록 멈춰 섰을 때, 황금빛 꽃송이가 환히 빛나는 달콤한 사향풀 더미에서 한 형체가 기어 나왔다. 벤허는 흠칫 놀랐다. 정말로 그는 사티로스가 제 집에 있는 모습을 보게 된 것일까? 그 형체는 그를 올려다보았고, 이빨 사이에 갈고리진 전지용 칼을 물고 있었다. 그는 제 놀람에 빙긋 웃었다. 그리고 보라, 그 매력이 풀려나왔다! 두려움 없는 평화, 온 세상에 두루 미친 평화, 바로 그것이었다!\n\n그는 한 그루 시트론나무 아래 땅에 앉았다. 그 나무는 시냇물 한 줄기를 받아들이려는 듯 잿빛 뿌리를 사방으로 뻗고 있었다. 보글거리는 물가에 박새의 둥지가 매달려 있고, 그 작은 것이 둥지 문에서 그의 눈을 들여다보았다. \"참으로, 이 새가 나에게 풀어 일러 주는구나.\" 그는 생각했다. \"이렇게 말하고 있지. '나는 너를 두려워하지 않는다. 이 행복한 곳의 법은 사랑이니까.'\"\n\n숲의 매력이 그에게 분명해진 듯했다. 그는 기뻤고, 다프네에서 길 잃은 자들 가운데 하나가 되기로 마음먹었다. 꽃과 관목을 돌보고, 곳곳에서 볼 수 있는 말 못 하는 모든 빼어남이 자라나는 것을 지켜보며, 입에 전지용 칼을 문 저 사내처럼, 그 또한 고난에 찬 나날을 버릴 수 있지 않을까? 잊으면서, 또 잊히면서 그것을 버릴 수 있지 않을까?\n\n그러나 이윽고 그의 안에서 유대인의 천성이 꿈틀거리기 시작했다.\n\n그 매력은 어떤 이들에게는 충분할지도 몰랐다. 그들은 어떤 부류의 사람들인가?\n\n사랑은 즐겁다, 아! 그 같은 비참함의 뒤를 잇는 것으로서 얼마나 흐뭇한가. 그러나 그것이 삶의 전부였을까? 전부였을까?\n\n그와, 이곳에 만족스레 제 몸을 파묻는 자들 사이에는 다름이 있었다. 그들에게는 의무가 없었다, 있을 수도 없었다. 그러나 그에게는—\n\n\"이스라엘의 하느님!\" 그는 두 뺨이 달아오른 채 벌떡 일어서며 소리쳤다. \"어머니! 티르자! 두 분을 잃고도 이 몸이 행복에 젖는 이 순간을, 이 자리를 저주하나이다!\"\n\n그는 덤불을 헤치고 서둘러 빠져나가, 돌로 쌓은 둑 사이로 강만 한 물줄기를 이루며 흐르는 시내에 이르렀다. 둑은 군데군데 수문이 달린 갑문으로 끊겨 있었다. 그가 걸어가던 길을 다리 하나가 시내 위로 건네주었는데, 그 위에 서서 그는 다른 다리들을 보았으니, 어느 둘도 같은 것이 없었다. 발밑의 물은 그림자처럼 맑게 깊은 웅덩이를 이루어 고여 있었다. 조금 아래로 가면 바위 위로 굉음을 내며 쏟아져 내렸고, 그러고는 또 다른 웅덩이와 또 다른 폭포가 이어졌다. 그렇게 시야 밖으로까지 계속되었다. 다리와 웅덩이와 메아리치는 폭포들은, 말 못 하는 것들이 이야기를 들려줄 수 있는 한 더없이 또렷하게, 이 강이 한 주인의 허락 아래, 주인이 바라는 그대로, 신들의 종에 걸맞게 고분고분히 흐른다고 일러 주었다.\n\n다리에서 앞을 보니, 너른 골짜기들과 들쭉날쭉한 높은 지대가 펼쳐진 풍경이 눈에 들어왔다. 그 사이로 작은 숲과 호수와 기묘한 집들이 흰 오솔길과 반짝이는 시내로 이어져 있었다. 골짜기들은 아래쪽에 펼쳐져 있어, 가뭄이 든 날 강물이 그곳에 부어져 생기를 줄 수 있게 되어 있었다. 골짜기들은 꽃밭과 꽃들판으로 무늬진 초록 양탄자 같았고, 눈송이처럼 흰 양 떼가 점점이 박혀 있었다. 양 떼를 따르는 목자들의 소리가 멀리서 들려왔다. 마치 그가 바라보는 모든 것에 깃든 경건한 헌사를 일러 주기라도 하듯, 탁 트인 하늘 아래 제단들은 헤아릴 수 없이 많아 보였고, 제단마다 흰 옷을 입은 형체가 시중을 들었으며, 흰 옷의 행렬들이 그 사이를 천천히 이리저리 오갔다. 절반쯤 피어오른 제단의 연기는 그 정성 어린 자리들 위에 옅은 구름으로 한데 엉겨 떠 있었다.\n\n여기서, 저기서, 날아오를 때는 행복하고 멈출 때는 도취되어, 이 사물에서 저 사물로, 이 지점에서 저 지점으로, 어느 때는 풀밭에서, 어느 때는 높은 곳에서, 어느 때는 머뭇거리며 숲속을 들여다보고 행렬을 살피다가, 또 어느 때는 어슴푸레한 원경 속으로 미로처럼 굽어들어 끝내 다다르는 그 길과 시내를 좇느라 정신을 잃었으니— 아, 이토록 아름다운 풍경에 어울리는 끝이란 어떤 것일까! 이토록 경이로운 서막 뒤에는 어떤 합당한 신비가 감추어져 있을까! 여기서, 저기서, 무언의 말이 시작되고 있었고, 그의 시선은 떠돌았으니, 그는 그 풍경이 강요하는 확신을, 그 모든 것의 결론으로서, 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 곧 대기에도 땅에도 평화가 깃들어 있고, 어디서나 와서 여기 누워 쉬라는 청함이 있다는 확신이었다.\n\n문득 한 깨달음이 그에게 밝아 왔다. 이 숲이야말로 실은 신전이었다. 끝없이 뻗어 나간, 벽 없는 하나의 신전이었던 것이다!\n\n이런 것은 일찍이 없었다!\n\n그 건축가는 기둥과 주랑, 비례나 내부, 또는 자신이 구현하려는 서사시에 얽매일 그 어떤 제약을 두고 골머리를 앓지 않았다. 그는 그저 자연을 종으로 삼았을 뿐이다. 예술은 그보다 더 멀리 나아갈 수 없다. 유피테르와 칼리스토의 영리한 아들이 옛 아르카디아를 지은 것도 그러했으니, 그때나 이때나 그 천재성은 그리스의 것이었다.\n\n벤허는 다리에서 가장 가까운 골짜기로 나아갔다.\n\n그는 양 떼에 이르렀다. 목자는 한 소녀였는데, 그를 향해 손짓했다. \"오세요!\"\n\n좀 더 나아가자, 길은 제단 하나로 갈라져 있었다. 검은 편마암 받침대 위에 솜씨 좋게 잎무늬를 새긴 흰 대리석 판을 얹고, 그 위에 불을 담은 청동 화로를 놓은 것이었다. 그 가까이에서 한 여인이 그를 보고 버드나무 가지를 흔들며, 그가 지나갈 때 불렀다. \"머무세요!\" 그 미소에 담긴 유혹은 정열에 찬 젊음의 것이었다.\n\n또 더 나아가, 그는 행렬 하나와 마주쳤다. 맨 앞에서는 화환만 둘렀을 뿐 벌거벗은 어린 소녀들의 무리가 째지는 목소리로 노래를 불었다. 이어서 역시 벌거벗은, 햇볕에 짙게 그을린 몸의 소년들 무리가 소녀들의 노래에 맞춰 춤추며 다가왔다. 그 뒤로 행렬을 이룬 것은 모두 여인들이었으니, 향료와 단것을 담은 바구니를 제단으로 나르고 있었다. 소박한 옷을 걸친 여인들은 몸이 드러나는 것에 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그가 지나갈 때 그들은 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"머물러서 우리와 함께 가요.\" 그중 하나, 그리스 여인이 아나크레온의 시구를 노래했다.\n\n\"오늘 나는 받고 또 주노라. 오늘 나는 마시고 또 사노라. 오늘 나는 빌고 또 꾸노라. 말 없는 내일을 그 누가 알리?\"\n\n그러나 그는 무심히 제 길을 갔고, 이윽고 골짜기 한복판, 살피는 눈에 가장 매혹적으로 다가올 만한 지점에 이른 무성한 작은 숲에 다다랐다. 그 숲이 그가 가던 길에 바싹 닿아 있어, 그 그늘에는 끌어당기는 힘이 있었고, 잎새들 사이로 그는 거창한 조각상으로 보이는 무언가가 빛나는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 옆길로 들어, 그 시원한 은신처로 발을 들였다.\n\n풀은 싱그럽고 깨끗했다. 나무들은 서로 비좁게 들어차 있지 않았고, 동방 토박이 온갖 수종이 먼 고장에서 들여온 낯선 나무들과 잘 어우러져 있었다. 여기에는 여왕처럼 깃을 단 야자나무들이 저희끼리 무리 지어 있었고, 저기에는 더 짙은 잎의 월계수를 굽어보는 무화과나무들이 있었으며, 푸르게 솟아오른 상록 떡갈나무들과, 레바논의 왕이라 할 만큼 거대한 삼나무들, 그리고 뽕나무들과, 낙원의 과수원에서 날려 왔다 해도 과장이 아닐 만큼 아름다운 테레빈나무들이 있었다.\n\n그 조각상은 놀랍도록 아름다운 다프네였다. 그러나 그가 그 얼굴을 흘끗 본 것 이상의 겨를도 가지기 전에, 받침대 아래에서 한 소녀와 한 젊은이가 호랑이 가죽 위에 서로 껴안은 채 잠들어 있는 것이 눈에 들어왔다. 그들 곁에는 일에 쓰는 연장들이—그의 도끼와 낫, 그녀의 바구니가—시들어 가는 장미 더미 위에 아무렇게나 내던져져 있었다.\n\n그 드러난 광경에 그는 흠칫 놀랐다. 향기 어린 덤불의 고요 속에서 그는, 자기 딴에는, 이 큰 숲의 매력이 두려움 없는 평화임을 깨달았고, 하마터면 거기에 굴복할 뻔했다. 그런데 이제, 한낮의 환한 빛 속에서의 이 잠, 다프네의 발치에서의 이 잠 속에서, 그는 더없이 어렴풋한 암시로밖에 입에 담을 수 없는 또 하나의 장(章)을 읽어 냈다. 이곳의 법은 사랑이었으나, 율법 없는 사랑이었다.\n\n그런데 이것이 다프네의 그 달콤한 평화였다!\n\n이것이 그 시중꾼들의 삶의 끝이었다!\n\n이것을 위해 왕과 제후들이 제 세입을 바쳤다!\n\n이것을 위해 교활한 사제 무리가 자연을—그 새들과 시내와 백합, 강, 수많은 손길의 노고, 제단의 거룩함, 햇빛의 기름진 힘을—종으로 굴복시켰다!\n\n벤허가 그런 사색에 시달리며 걸음을 이어 가는 동안, 이 거대한 야외 신전의 신도들을 향한, 특히 몸소 봉사하여 이 신전을 그토록 빼어나게 아름다운 상태로 지켜 온 자들을 향한 슬픔에 다소 마음이 기울었다고 적을 수 있다면 흐뭇한 일이리라. 그들이 어쩌다 그 처지에 이르렀는지는 더 이상 신비가 아니었으니, 그 동기와 영향과 유혹이 그의 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있었다. 그중에는, 의심할 바 없이, 거룩히 봉헌된 처소에서의 끝없는 평화라는 약속에 시달린 영혼이 사로잡힌 자들도 있었다. 그 처소의 아름다움에, 돈이 없으면 노동으로나마 보탤 수 있었던 것이다. 이 부류는 유달리 희망과 두려움에 휘둘리기 쉬운 지성을 지녔음을 뜻했다. 그러나 그 신도들의 큰 무리는 그런 자들과 한데 묶을 수 없었다. 아폴론의 그물은 넓고 그 코는 촘촘했으니, 그 모든 어부들이 무엇을 끌어올렸는지 좀처럼 가려 말하기 어려웠다. 이는 그것을 묘사할 수 없어서라기보다, 묘사해서는 안 되기 때문이었다. 그 대다수가 세상의 향락주의자들이었고, 수에서는 더욱 방대하고 그 정도에서는 더욱 저열한 무리, 곧 동방이 거의 송두리째 빠져든 순전한 관능주의의 신봉자들이었다는 것만으로 충분하다. 어떤 드높은 경지에도—노래하는 신에게도, 그 불행한 연인에게도, 그 즐김을 위해 은둔의 고요를 요구하는 어떤 철학에도, 종교가 주는 위안을 위한 어떤 봉사에도, 더 거룩한 뜻에서의 사랑에도—그들은 제 서원을 두지 않았다. 선량한 독자여, 어찌하여 여기서 진실을 말하지 않으랴? 어찌하여 이를 깨닫지 않으랴. 이 시대에 온 땅을 통틀어 앞서 말한 종류의 드높은 경지에 이를 수 있는 백성은 오직 둘뿐이었으니, 모세의 율법으로 사는 자들과 브라마의 법으로 사는 자들이었다. 그들만이 그대를 향해 이렇게 외칠 수 있었으리라. 율법 없는 사랑보다는 사랑 없는 율법이 낫다고.\n\n게다가 동정심이란 대개 우리가 그 순간에 처한 기분의 결과다. 분노는 그 감정을 가로막는다. 반면에 동정심은 우리가 더없이 완전한 자기만족의 상태에 있을 때 가장 쉽게 일어난다. 벤허는 한층 빠른 걸음으로 걸으며 고개를 더 높이 쳐들었다. 그리고 주위의 모든 흐뭇함에 여전히 민감하면서도, 더 차분한 마음으로 둘러보았으니, 다만 이따금 입술을 비틀곤 했다. 다시 말해, 그는 자기 자신이 얼마나 그 속임수에 넘어갈 뻔했는지를 그리 쉬이 잊을 수가 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n벤허 앞에는 사이프러스 숲이 펼쳐져 있었으니, 나무마다 돛대처럼 곧고 키 큰 기둥이었다. 그늘진 영역으로 들어서던 그는 흥겹게 울리는 나팔 소리를 듣더니, 곧이어 신전으로 가는 길에서 마주쳤던 그 시골 사람이 풀밭에 바로 가까이 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 그 사내는 일어나 그에게로 다가왔다.\n\n“다시 평화를 빕니다.” 그가 상냥하게 말했다.\n\n“고맙소.” 벤허가 대답하고는 물었다. “나와 같은 길로 가시오?”\n\n“경기장으로 가는 길입니다. 당신도 그쪽이라면 말이지요.”\n\n“경기장이라!”\n\n“그렇습니다. 방금 들으신 나팔은 경기자들을 부르는 신호였습니다.”\n\n“좋은 벗이여,” 벤허가 솔직하게 말했다. “나는 이 숲을 잘 모른다는 것을 고백하오. 그대를 길잡이로 삼게 해 준다면 기쁘겠소.”\n\n“그것이야말로 제겐 기쁨이지요. 들어 보십시오! 전차 바퀴 소리가 들립니다. 주로(走路)로 들어서고 있군요.”\n\n벤허는 잠시 귀를 기울이더니, 그 사내의 팔에 손을 얹으며 소개를 마무리했다. “나는 두움비르 아리우스의 아들이오. 그대는?”\n* 두움비르: 고대 로마의 두 사람으로 이루어진 관직.\n\n“저는 말루크라고 하며, 안티오크의 상인입니다.”\n\n“좋소, 착한 말루크여. 나팔 소리며, 삐걱대는 바퀴 소리며, 즐거운 구경거리를 기대하니 마음이 들뜨는구려. 나도 운동에는 제법 솜씨가 있소. 로마의 팔라이스트라에서는 이름이 알려지지 않은 것도 아니라오. 자, 주로로 갑시다.”\n\n말루크는 머뭇거리며 얼른 말했다. “두움비르는 로마 사람인데, 그 아들은 유대인의 옷을 입고 있군요.”\n\n“고귀한 아리우스는 양아버지이시오.” 벤허가 대답했다.\n\n“아! 그렇군요. 용서를 빕니다.”\n\n숲의 띠를 지나자, 두 사람은 주로가 마련된 들판에 이르렀다. 그 모양과 크기는 경기장의 그것과 똑같았다. 주로, 곧 본래의 달리는 길은 부드러운 흙을 다지고 물을 뿌려 놓은 것이었고, 양쪽에는 곧추세운 창에 느슨히 매단 밧줄로 경계가 그어져 있었다. 구경꾼들과, 단순한 연습 이상의 관심을 품은 자들을 위해, 든든한 차일을 드리운 관람대가 여럿 마련되어 있었으니, 자리는 층층이 올라가는 줄로 갖춰져 있었다. 그 관람대 가운데 하나에서 두 새내기는 자리를 잡았다.\n\n벤허는 전차들이 지나가는 대로 그 수를 헤아렸다—모두 아홉이었다.\n\n“저 사람들은 칭찬받을 만하군.” 그가 호의를 담아 말했다. “여기 동방에서는 둘 이상은 바라지도 않는 줄 알았는데, 야심을 품고 왕가에나 어울릴 네 마리짜리를 다루고 있구려. 저들의 솜씨를 살펴봅시다.”\n\n네 마리짜리 가운데 여덟은 관람대를 지나갔는데, 더러는 걷고 더러는 종종걸음을 쳤으며, 하나같이 나무랄 데 없이 다루어지고 있었다. 그러더니 아홉 번째 것이 질주해 왔다. 벤허는 절로 탄성을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“말루크, 나는 황제의 마구간에도 들어가 보았지만, 복되신 우리 조상 아브라함을 두고 맹세하건대! 저런 것들은 본 적이 없소.”\n\n마지막 네 마리가 그때 휙 지나가고 있었다. 별안간 그것들이 어지럽게 흐트러졌다. 관람대에서 누군가 날카롭게 소리쳤다. 벤허가 돌아보니, 한 노인이 윗자리에서 반쯤 일어서 있었다. 두 손은 움켜쥔 채 치켜들었고, 눈은 사납게 번뜩였으며, 길고 흰 수염은 그야말로 부들부들 떨고 있었다. 그에게 가까이 있던 구경꾼들 가운데 몇이 웃기 시작했다.\n\n“적어도 저 수염만은 존중해야 할 텐데. 저 사람은 누구요?” 벤허가 물었다.\n\n“사막에서 온 위세 등등한 분이지요. 모압 너머 어딘가에서 왔는데, 낙타를 떼로 거느리고, 듣자 하니 첫 파라오의 경주마에서 내려온 혈통의 말들을 가진 분이랍니다—이름과 직함은 일데림 족장이지요.”\n\n말루크는 그렇게 대답했다.\n\n그러는 동안 마부는 네 마리를 진정시키려 애썼으나 아무 소용이 없었다. 헛된 노력이 거듭될 때마다 족장은 더욱 격앙되었다.\n\n“아바돈이 저놈을 잡아가기를!” 노족장이 새된 소리로 고함쳤다. “달려라! 날아가라! 듣고 있느냐, 내 아이들아?” 그 물음은 제 부족의 일원인 듯한 시종들을 향한 것이었다. “듣고 있느냐? 저것들은 너희와 마찬가지로 사막에서 태어났다. 잡아라—어서!”\n\n짐승들의 날뜀이 더해 갔다.\n\n“저주받을 로마 놈!” 족장이 마부를 향해 주먹을 휘둘렀다. “저놈이 몰 수 있다고 맹세하지 않았더냐—제 잡종 라틴 신들을 모조리 걸고 맹세하지 않았더냐? 아니, 내게서 손 떼라—떼라니까! 독수리처럼 빠르게 달리되, 손으로 기른 양처럼 순한 성미라고 저놈이 맹세했다. 저주받을 놈—저놈을 자식이라 부르는 거짓말쟁이의 어미마저 저주받기를! 저것들을 보라, 값을 매길 수 없는 것들을! 저놈이 채찍으로 한 마리라도 건드리기만 한다면, 그러면”—나머지 말은 격렬한 이갈이 속에 묻혀 버렸다. “너희 가운데 몇은 저것들의 머리로 가서, 말을 걸어라—한마디면 된다, 너희 어미가 들려주던 천막의 노래에서 한 구절이면 충분하다. 오, 어리석구나, 로마 놈을 믿다니 내가 어리석었다!”\n\n노인의 벗들 가운데 좀 더 약삭빠른 자들이 그와 말 사이에 끼어들어 섰다. 마침맞게 노인의 숨이 가빠진 것이 그 꾀를 도왔다.\n\n벤허는 족장의 심정을 헤아렸다고 여기며, 그에게 공감했다. 단순한 소유물에 대한 자랑을 훨씬 넘어서—경주 결과에 대한 근심을 훨씬 넘어서—그가 보기에, 노족장이 제 사고방식과 값을 매길 수 없는 것에 대한 관념에 따라, 그토록 예민한 격정에 버금가는 애틋함으로 그런 짐승들을 사랑하는 것은 충분히 있을 법한 일이었다.\n\n그것들은 모두 빛나는 밤색이었고, 얼룩 하나 없었으며, 완벽하게 짝지어졌고, 균형이 어찌나 잘 잡혔는지 실제보다 작아 보일 정도였다. 가냘픈 귀는 작은 머리 위로 쫑긋했고, 얼굴은 두 눈 사이가 넓고 도톰했다. 콧구멍은 벌어지면 그 속의 막이 어찌나 짙붉은지 불길이 번뜩이는 것 같았다. 목은 활처럼 휘었고, 그 위에 얹힌 고운 갈기는 어찌나 풍성한지 어깨와 가슴을 휘장처럼 덮었으며, 거기에 어울리게 앞머리 갈기는 비단 너울을 풀어 놓은 실오라기 같았다. 무릎과 발목 사이 다리는 펼친 손바닥처럼 납작했으나, 무릎 위로는 우람한 근육으로 둥글게 솟아 있었으니, 잘 빠지고 야무진 몸을 떠받치는 데 그 근육이 꼭 필요했다. 발굽은 잘 닦은 마노 잔 같았고, 몸을 일으키고 날뛸 때면 윤기 흐르는 새까맣고 굵고 긴 꼬리로 허공을, 때로는 땅을 후려쳤다. 족장이 그것들을 값을 매길 수 없는 것이라 부른 것은 옳은 말이었다.\n\n말들을 이렇게 두 번째로 더 가까이 들여다보면서, 벤허는 그것들과 주인 사이의 사연을 읽어 냈다. 그것들은 그의 눈길 아래 자랐으니, 낮에는 그가 각별히 보살피는 대상이었고, 밤에는 그가 자랑스레 꿈꾸는 환영이었다. 그늘 한 점 없는 사막의 품에 친 검은 천막에서 가족과 더불어 살아온 그에게, 그것들은 사랑하는 자식과도 같았다. 거만하고 미운 로마 놈을 누르고 승리를 안겨 줄 수만 있다면, 노인은 제 사랑하는 것들을 도시로 데려왔으니, 믿을 만한 전문가의 손에 맡기기만 한다면 반드시 이기리라는 것을 한 번도 의심하지 않았다. 한낱 솜씨만 지닌 자가 아니라, 그것들의 기개가 알아볼 만한 기개를 지닌 자라야 했다. 그는 서방의 더 냉정한 사람들과는 달리, 마부의 무능을 점잖게 따져 묻고 정중히 내보낼 수가 없었다. 아랍인이자 족장인 그는 폭발할 수밖에 없었고, 고함으로 제 주위의 공기를 갈가리 찢어 놓을 수밖에 없었다.\n\n노족장이 그 욕설을 다 쏟아 내기도 전에, 열 몇 개의 손이 말들의 재갈에 달라붙어 그것들을 가라앉혔다. 그 무렵 또 다른 전차 하나가 주로에 나타났다. 그것은 다른 것들과 달리, 마부도, 수레도, 말도 결승전 당일 원형 경기장에 나설 모습 그대로였다. 곧 분명해질 까닭으로, 이제 이 한 벌을 독자에게 또렷이 그려 보이는 것이 바람직하다.\n\n우리 모두 고전의 명성으로 익히 아는 그 전차를 이해하는 데에는 아무런 어려움도 없을 것이다. 바퀴가 낮고 굴대가 넓은 짐수레를 떠올리되, 그 위에 뒤쪽이 트인 상자가 얹힌 모습을 그려 보기만 하면 된다. 그것이 원시적인 본보기였다. 때가 이르러 예술적 재능이 나타나, 이 투박한 기계를 손보아 아름다운 물건으로 끌어올렸다—이를테면, 새벽에 앞서 달리는 오로라가 타고 있다고 우리 상상이 그리는 그런 것 말이다.\n\n옛 사람들의 마부들은, 오늘날의 후예들 못지않게 약삭빠르고 야심만만하여, 가장 소박한 한 벌을 둘이라 불렀고, 등급이 가장 높은 것을 넷이라 불렀다. 후자로 그들은 올림픽 경기와, 그것을 본떠 세운 다른 축제 경기들을 겨루었다.\n\n같은 영리한 도박꾼들은 말들을 전차에 모두 가로로 나란히 매는 것을 좋아했다. 그래서 구별하기 위해, 채에 가장 가까운 둘을 멍에말이라 일컫고, 좌우 바깥쪽의 것들을 곁말이라 일컬었다. 또한 그들의 판단으로는, 행동의 자유를 한껏 허락하면 가장 빠른 속도를 얻을 수 있었다. 그리하여 쓰이는 마구는 유달리 단순했으니, 사실 짐승의 목에 두른 멍에띠와, 그 멍에띠에 매단 끄는 줄 말고는 아무것도 없었다—고삐와 굴레까지 그 안에 넣지 않는다면 말이다. 매어 달려고 할 때면, 주인들은 좁은 나무 멍에, 곧 가로목을 채 끝 가까이에 꽂고, 멍에 끝의 고리에 끈을 꿰어 그것을 멍에띠에 죄어 맸다. 멍에말의 끄는 줄은 굴대에 매고, 곁말의 끄는 줄은 전차 바닥의 윗테에 맸다. 그러고 나면 남은 것은 고삐를 잡는 일뿐인데, 이는 현대의 장치에 견주어 보면 그 방식 가운데 가장 묘한 부분이라 할 만했다. 이를 위해 채의 앞쪽 끝에 큰 고리 하나가 달려 있었다. 먼저 고삐의 끝을 그 고리에 매고, 고삐를 갈라 말마다 하나씩 가게 한 다음, 입가의 굴레 안쪽에 달린 고리에 하나하나 따로 꿰어서 마부에게 넘겼다.\n\n이 간략한 설명을 마음에 새겨 두면, 이 주제에 관해 더 알고 싶은 것은 모두 지금 벌어지는 장면의 사건들을 좇음으로써 얻을 수 있다.\n\n다른 경기자들은 잠잠한 가운데 맞아들여졌지만, 마지막에 온 자는 더 운이 좋았다. 우리가 이 장면을 바라보고 있는 관람대 쪽으로 나아갈 때, 그의 행차는 요란한 표시로—박수와 환호로 알려졌으니, 그 결과 사람들의 시선이 오로지 그에게로 쏠렸다. 그의 멍에말은 검고, 곁말은 눈처럼 희다는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 까다로운 로마 취향의 규범에 따라, 네 마리 모두 손질을 당했으니, 다시 말해 꼬리는 잘렸고, 야만을 마무리하려는 듯 깎인 갈기는 매듭으로 갈라, 화려한 빨강과 노랑 리본으로 묶여 있었다.\n\n나아가면서 이방인은 마침내 전차가 관람대에서 보이는 지점에 이르렀는데, 그 모습만으로도 환호는 마땅한 것이었다. 바퀴는 그야말로 만듦새의 경이였다. 윤내어 광낸 청동의 든든한 테가 바퀴통을 보강했으니, 그러지 않았다면 바퀴통은 무척 가벼웠을 것이다. 바퀴살은 상아 엄니의 토막으로, 본디 휜 모양을 바깥으로 향하게 끼워 넣어 바퀴의 곡면을 완성했으니, 이는 예나 지금이나 중요하게 여겨지는 것이었다. 청동 테가 빛나는 흑단으로 된 바퀴 둘레를 붙들었다. 굴대는 바퀴와 어울리게, 놋쇠로 빚은 으르렁대는 호랑이 머리로 양 끝을 장식했고, 바닥은 금박을 입힌 버드나무 가지로 엮었다.\n\n아름다운 말들과 눈부신 전차가 다가오자, 벤허는 더욱 큰 관심을 품고 마부를 바라보았다.\n\n그는 누구인가?\n\n벤허가 처음 그 물음을 스스로에게 던졌을 때, 그는 마부의 얼굴은커녕 온전한 모습조차 볼 수 없었다. 그런데도 그 분위기와 거동이 낯익어, 오래전 지나간 한 시절을 날카롭게 일깨우며 그를 찔렀다.\n\n대체 누구일까?\n\n이제 더 가까워졌고, 말들은 종종걸음으로 다가오고 있었다. 환호와 화려한 한 벌로 미루어, 그는 어떤 권세 있는 자의 총신이거나 이름난 왕자일지도 모른다고 여겨졌다. 그런 모습은 드높은 신분과 어긋나지 않았다. 왕들도 승리의 상인 월계관을 얻으려 종종 다투었다. 기억하다시피, 네로와 콤모두스도 전차 경주에 몸을 바쳤다. 벤허는 일어나, 관람대 맨 아래 자리 앞 난간 가까이까지 헤치고 내려갔다. 그의 얼굴은 진지했고, 그 태도는 간절했다.\n\n그러더니 곧 마부의 온전한 모습이 시야에 들어왔다. 한 동행이 그와 함께 타고 있었으니, 고전의 표현으로 미르틸루스라 하여, 경주에 대한 열정을 만끽하는 고귀한 신분의 사람들에게 허락된 자였다. 벤허의 눈에는 오직 마부만이 보였다. 그는 전차 안에 꼿꼿이 서서, 고삐를 제 몸에 여러 번 둘러 감고 있었다—옅은 붉은 천의 짧은 옷으로 간신히 가린 늠름한 모습이었다. 오른손에는 채찍을 들었고, 다른 손은 들어 가볍게 뻗은 채 네 가닥 고삐를 잡고 있었다. 그 자세는 더없이 우아하고 생기 넘쳤다. 환호와 박수도 그는 조각상 같은 무심함으로 받아넘겼다. 벤허는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 섰다—그의 직감과 기억은 그를 충실히 도왔다—그 마부는 메살라였다.\n\n말의 선택, 전차의 호화로움, 자세, 그리고 몸을 드러내는 자태로—무엇보다 차갑고 날카로운 독수리 같은 이목구비의 표정으로, 그 표정은 수많은 세대에 걸쳐 세계를 호령하며 그의 동족에게 제왕의 기품을 새겨 놓은 것이었으니, 벤허는 메살라가 변함없음을 알아보았다. 여전히 거만하고, 자신만만하고, 대담하며, 야심과 냉소와 비웃는 듯한 태평함에서도 한결같았다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n벤허가 관람대 계단을 내려설 때, 한 아랍인이 맨 아래 계단에 올라서서 외쳤다.\n\n\"동방과 서방의 사람들이여, 들으시오! 선량하신 일데림 족장께서 인사를 전하시오. 지혜로운 솔로몬이 아끼던 명마의 후손인 네 마리 말을 데리고, 그분은 최고를 상대하러 나오셨소. 그분께 가장 필요한 것은 그 말들을 몰 수 있는 강건한 사람이오. 그분이 흡족하실 만큼 말들을 몰아 줄 자에게는, 그분이 평생토록 부를 약속하시오. 여기서나 저기서나, 도성에서나 경기장에서나, 강한 자들이 모이는 곳이라면 어디서든 그분의 이 제안을 전하시오. 이것이 나의 주인이신 너그러운 일데림 족장의 말씀이오.\"\n\n이 포고는 차일 아래 모인 사람들 사이에 큰 술렁임을 일으켰다. 밤이 되면 안티오크의 모든 경기 애호가 모임에서 되풀이되고 논의될 것이었다. 그 말을 들은 벤허는 걸음을 멈추고, 전령에게서 족장에게로 망설이듯 눈길을 옮겼다. 말루크는 그가 제안을 받아들이려는 줄 알았으나, 이내 벤허가 자기에게 돌아서며 묻자 안심했다. \"여보게 말루크, 이제 어디로 가지?\"\n\n선량한 사내는 웃으며 대답했다. \"처음으로 이 숲을 찾는 다른 사람들을 본받으시려거든, 곧장 점괘를 들으러 가셔야지요.\"\n\n\"점괘라고 했나? 그 제안에 믿음 없는 자의 냄새가 풍기긴 하네만, 어쨌든 당장 여신에게로 가세나.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아리우스의 아드님, 이곳 아폴로 신전 사람들은 그보다 더 그럴듯한 재주를 부립니다. 피티아나 시빌에게 말을 듣게 하는 대신, 줄기에서 막 잘라내어 아직 마르지도 않은 밋밋한 파피루스 잎 한 장을 팔지요. 그러고는 어느 샘의 물에 담그라고 합니다. 그러면 그 위에 시 한 구절이 떠올라, 거기서 자기 미래를 들을 수 있다는 겁니다.\"\n\n벤허의 얼굴에서 흥미의 빛이 사라졌다.\n\n\"세상에는 자기 미래를 두고 마음 졸일 필요가 없는 사람도 있는 법이지.\" 그가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 신전 쪽으로 가시는 편이 낫겠습니까?\"\n\n\"신전들은 그리스식 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그리스식이라고들 부르지요.\"\n\n\"헬레네족은 예술에서 아름다움의 대가였지. 하지만 건축에서는 다양함을 굽힐 줄 모르는 아름다움에 희생시켰어. 그들의 신전은 죄다 똑같다네. 그 샘은 뭐라 부르나?\"\n\n\"카스탈리아라고 합니다.\"\n\"오! 온 세상에 이름난 곳이지. 그리로 가세.\"\n\n말루크는 가는 동안 길동무를 살폈고, 적어도 지금만큼은 그의 좋던 기분이 가셨음을 알아차렸다. 지나가는 사람들에게 그는 눈길도 주지 않았다. 마주치는 신기한 것들 앞에서도 탄성 한 번 내지 않았다. 그는 말없이, 시무룩하게까지, 느린 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n사실 메살라를 본 것이 벤허를 깊은 생각에 빠뜨린 것이었다. 그 억센 손들이 자기를 어머니에게서 떼어내던 것이 한 시간도 채 지나지 않은 듯했고, 그 로마인이 아버지의 집 대문에 봉인을 찍던 것도 한 시간이 채 안 된 듯했다. 그는 떠올렸다. 노예선에서의—그런 것을 삶이라 부를 수 있다면—그 희망 없는 비참 속에서, 노동을 제외하면 복수의 꿈을 꾸는 일밖에 달리 할 게 없었고, 그 모든 꿈에서 메살라가 주역이었음을. 그는 자신에게 늘 말하곤 했다. 그라투스는 빠져나갈 수도 있겠지만, 메살라만은 결코 안 된다고. 그리고 결심을 다지고 단단히 하려고, 그는 거듭거듭 되뇌곤 했다. 박해자들에게 우리를 가리켜 보인 자가 누구였던가? 내가 그에게 도움을 청했을 때—나 자신을 위해서가 아니었거늘—나를 비웃고 껄껄 웃으며 가 버린 자가 누구였던가? 그리고 그 꿈은 언제나 똑같이 끝났다. 그를 만나는 날, 저를 도우소서, 제 백성의 선하신 하느님이여!—저에게 합당하고도 각별한 복수를 베풀어 주소서!\n\n그리고 이제 그 만남이 코앞에 닥쳐 있었다.\n\n만일 그가 메살라를 가난하고 고통스러운 처지로 마주쳤다면, 벤허의 심정은 달랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇지가 않았다. 그는 메살라가 번창한 정도를 넘어선 모습으로 만났다. 그 번영에는 화려한 기세와 번쩍임이 깃들어 있었으니, 황금의 도금 위에 쏟아지는 햇빛 같은 광채였다.\n\n그래서 말루크가 한때 가신 기분쯤으로 여긴 것은, 사실 그 만남이 언제일지, 그리고 어떻게 하면 가장 잊지 못할 것으로 만들 수 있을지를 곱씹는 것이었다.\n\n이윽고 그들은 떡갈나무가 늘어선 가로수 길로 접어들었다. 그곳에서는 사람들이 무리 지어 오가고 있었다. 여기엔 도보로 가는 이들과 말 탄 이들이, 저기엔 노예들이 메는 가마에 탄 여인들이 있었고, 이따금 전차가 우렁찬 소리를 내며 굴러갔다.\n\n가로수 길 끝에서 길은 완만한 비탈을 이루며 저지대로 내려갔다. 그곳 오른편에는 잿빛 바위가 깎아지른 듯 솟아 있었고, 왼편에는 봄기운으로 싱그러운 풀밭이 트여 있었다. 그러고 나서 그들의 눈앞에 그 이름난 카스탈리아 샘이 들어왔다.\n\n그 자리에 모인 무리를 비집고 들어선 벤허는, 한 줄기 단물이 돌마루에서 검은 대리석 수반으로 쏟아져 내리는 것을 보았다. 물은 한참을 끓어오르고 거품을 일으키다가, 마치 깔때기로 빠지듯 사라졌다.\n\n수반 곁, 단단한 바위벽을 파서 만든 작은 주랑 아래에는 한 사제가 앉아 있었다. 늙고, 수염이 무성하고, 주름지고, 두건을 쓴—이보다 더 완벽한 은수자(隱修者)는 없을 듯했다. 그곳에 모인 사람들의 태도로 보아, 끊임없이 반짝이는 샘과 언제나 그 자리에 있는 사제 중 어느 쪽이 사람을 끄는지 가려내기 어려울 정도였다. 그는 듣고, 보고, 보임을 당했으나, 결코 입을 열지 않았다. 이따금 방문객이 동전을 쥔 손을 그에게 내밀었다. 그러면 그는 약삭빠르게 눈을 반짝이며 돈을 받고, 그 대가로 파피루스 잎 한 장을 건넸다.\n\n그것을 받은 자는 서둘러 파피루스를 수반에 담갔다. 그런 다음 물방울이 떨어지는 잎을 햇빛에 들면, 그 표면에 운(韻)을 맞춘 글귀가 떠올라 보상이 되었다. 그리고 그 시의 빈약함 때문에 샘의 명성이 손상되는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 벤허가 미처 그 신탁을 시험해 보기도 전에, 풀밭 저편으로 다른 방문객들이 다가오는 것이 보였고, 그들의 모습은 무리의 호기심을 자극했으니, 벤허의 호기심도 다른 사람들 못지않았다.\n\n그가 맨 처음 본 것은, 매우 키가 크고 새하얀 낙타였다. 말을 탄 마부가 낙타를 끌고 있었다. 낙타 등에 얹힌 가마는 유달리 큰 데다 진홍빛과 황금빛으로 꾸며져 있었다. 다른 기마병 둘이 긴 창을 손에 들고 낙타 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"정말 놀라운 낙타로군!\" 무리 중 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"먼 곳에서 온 왕자야.\" 또 하나가 짐작해 말했다.\n\n\"왕이라는 편이 더 그럴듯하지.\"\n\n\"코끼리를 탔다면 왕이라고 했겠네만.\"\n\n세 번째 사내는 사뭇 다른 의견이었다.\n\n\"낙타로군—그것도 흰 낙타라니!\" 그가 단정 짓듯 말했다. \"아폴로께 맹세코, 친구들, 저기 오는 자들은—보다시피 둘이지—왕도 아니고 왕자도 아닐세. 여인들이야!\"\n\n이 다툼이 한창일 때 그 낯선 이들이 당도했다.\n\n가까이서 본 낙타는 멀리서 본 모습을 배반하지 않았다. 더 키 크고 더 당당한 낙타는, 아무리 먼 곳에서 온 나그네라도 이 샘에서 본 적이 없을 것이었다. 저토록 크고 검은 눈이라니! 저토록 곱고 흰 털이라니! 들어 올리면 그토록 오므라들고, 디디면 그토록 소리 없고, 디딘 자리는 그토록 넓은 발이라니!—이 낙타에 견줄 만한 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 게다가 비단 안장과, 술과 매듭마다 황금으로 치장한 그 온갖 화려함이 얼마나 잘 어울리던지! 은방울 딸랑이는 소리가 그 앞에 앞서갔고, 낙타는 제 짐을 모르기라도 하듯 가뿐히 움직였다.\n\n그런데 가마 안의 남자와 여자는 누구인가?\n\n모든 이의 눈이 그런 물음을 던지며 그들에게 인사를 보냈다.\n\n만일 그 남자가 왕자나 왕이라면, 무리 가운데 철학자들도 세월의 무차별함을 부인하지 못할 것이었다. 거대한 터번 아래 파묻힌 야위고 쪼그라든 얼굴, 미라 같은 빛깔이어서 그 출신지를 짐작조차 할 수 없는 살갗을 보았을 때, 그들은 위대한 자에게나 미천한 자에게나 똑같이 목숨의 한계가 있다는 사실을 떠올리며 흐뭇해했다. 그의 몸에서 부러워할 만한 것이라곤, 그를 감싼 숄밖에 없었다.\n\n여자는 동방의 방식대로, 더없이 고운 너울과 레이스에 둘러싸여 앉아 있었다. 팔꿈치 위로는 똬리를 튼 독사 모양의 팔찌를 두르고, 황금 줄로 손목의 팔찌와 이어 놓았다. 그 밖에 두 팔은 드러나 있었는데, 보기 드물게 천연의 우아함을 지녔고, 아이의 손처럼 앙증맞게 빚어진 손이 그것을 돋보이게 했다. 한 손은 가마 옆을 짚고 있었으니, 가느다랗게 빠진 손가락은 반지로 반짝였고, 손끝은 진주모(眞珠母)의 분홍빛처럼 발그레하게 물들어 있었다. 그녀는 머리에 트인 망사 머리쓰개를 썼는데, 거기엔 산호 구슬이 점점이 박히고 '해님'이라 불리는 동전 장식들이 꿰여 있었다. 그중 몇몇은 이마를 가로질러 걸쳐 있었고, 다른 것들은 등 뒤로 흘러내려, 곧게 뻗은 검푸른 머리채 더미에 반쯤 묻혀 있었다. 그 머리채만으로도 견줄 데 없는 장식이어서, 그것을 덮은 너울은 햇빛과 먼지를 막는 용도가 아니라면 필요 없을 정도였다. 높이 앉은 자리에서 그녀는 사람들을 차분하고 다정하게 내려다보았는데, 사람들을 살펴보는 데 어찌나 골몰했던지 정작 자기가 불러일으키는 관심은 깨닫지 못하는 듯했다. 그리고 흔치 않은 일로—아니, 공공장소에서 지체 높은 여인들의 관례를 거세게 거스르는 일로—그녀는 얼굴을 가리지 않고 그들을 바라보았다.\n\n그것은 보기에 아름다운 얼굴이었다. 무척 앳되고, 갸름한 모양이었다. 살빛은 그리스 여인처럼 희지도, 로마 여인처럼 가무잡잡하지도, 갈리아 여인처럼 금발에 어울리는 빛도 아니었다. 오히려 나일강 상류의 햇볕이 물들인 빛깔이었으되, 그 살갗이 어찌나 투명한지 피가 그 너머로 비쳐, 뺨과 이마가 등불빛 같은 발그레함으로 빛났다. 본디 큰 두 눈은 눈꺼풀을 따라, 온 동방에서 예부터 써 온 검은 화장먹으로 단장되어 있었다. 입술은 살짝 벌어져, 그 진홍빛 호수 사이로 눈부시게 흰 이가 드러났다. 이 모든 빼어난 용모에, 끝으로 독자는 한 가지를 더 보태어 주기를 청한다. 고전적인 모양으로 빚어진 작은 머리가, 길고 살풋 숙어진 우아한 목 위에 얹힌 데서 우러나는 분위기를—굳이 말하자면, '여왕다운'이라는 말로 다행히 표현될 그 분위기를.\n\n사람들과 주위 풍경을 다 살펴본 데 만족했는지, 그 고운 여인은 마부에게 말을 건넸다. 마부는 허리까지 벗은, 우람한 근육의 에티오피아인이었다. 그가 낙타를 샘 가까이 끌어다 무릎 꿇게 한 다음, 여인의 손에서 잔을 받아 들고 수반에서 물을 채우러 갔다. 바로 그 순간, 그녀의 아름다움이 자아낸 정적을 깨뜨리며, 바퀴 소리와 빠르게 내달리는 말발굽 소리가 터져 나왔다. 그러자 큰 외침과 함께 구경꾼들이 사방으로 흩어지며, 피하려 허둥지둥 내달렸다.\n\n\"저 로마인이 우리를 깔아뭉갤 작정입니다. 조심하십시오!\" 말루크가 벤허에게 소리치며, 동시에 황급히 달아나는 본보기를 보였다.\n\n벤허는 소리 나는 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸고, 메살라가 전차를 몰며 네 마리 말을 곧장 군중을 향해 몰아붙이는 것을 보았다. 이번에는 가깝고 또렷한 광경이었다.\n\n무리가 흩어지자 낙타가 드러났다. 그 낙타는 보통 제 종족보다 더 민첩했을지도 모른다. 그러나 말발굽은 이미 그 코앞에 닥쳤건만, 낙타는 눈을 감은 채 쉬며 끝없는 되새김질을 하고 있었으니, 오랜 총애가 길러 주었으리라 짐작되는 안도감에 잠겨 있었다. 에티오피아인은 겁에 질려 두 손을 비비 꼬았다. 가마 안의 노인은 피하려고 움직였으나, 늙은 몸에 거동이 거북했고, 위험을 앞두고서도 분명 몸에 밴 그 위엄을 잊을 수 없었다. 여자가 스스로를 구하기엔 너무 늦었다. 벤허가 그들과 가장 가까이 서 있었고, 그가 메살라에게 외쳤다.\n\n\"멈춰라! 어디로 가는지 보아라! 물러서, 물러서라!\"\n\n그 귀족은 흥겨운 호탕함으로 웃고 있었다. 구할 길이 단 하나뿐임을 안 벤허는 안으로 뛰어들어, 왼쪽 멍에의 말과 그 짝의 재갈을 움켜잡았다. \"로마의 개야! 목숨이 그리도 하찮으냐?\" 그가 온 힘을 다하며 외쳤다. 두 마리 말이 앞발을 들고 곧추서며 나머지 말들을 빙 끌어돌렸고, 끌채가 기울자 전차도 기울었다. 메살라는 가까스로 나뒹구는 것을 면했으나, 그의 우쭐대던 미르틸로스는 흙덩이처럼 땅바닥으로 굴러떨어졌다. 위험이 지나간 것을 보자, 구경꾼들이 모두 비웃는 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n그러자 로마인의 견줄 데 없는 뻔뻔함이 그 진면목을 드러냈다. 그는 몸에 감긴 고삐를 풀어 한쪽으로 던지고, 전차에서 내려, 낙타를 빙 돌아 걸으며 벤허를 바라보고는, 일부는 노인에게 일부는 여자에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"용서를 비오—두 분 모두에게 비오. 나는 메살라요.\" 그가 말했다. \"대지의 옛 어머니께 맹세코, 그대도 그대의 낙타도 보지 못했소이다! 저 선량한 사람들로 말하자면—아마 내 솜씨를 너무 믿었나 보오. 저들을 한바탕 웃음거리로 삼으려 했더니, 웃음은 저들 차지가 되었구려. 저들에게 좋은 일이 되기를!\"\n\n그가 구경꾼들에게 던진 사람 좋고 무심한 표정과 몸짓은 그 말과 잘 어울렸다. 그가 또 무슨 말을 하려는지 들으려고, 사람들은 조용해졌다. 봉변당한 무리를 제압했다고 확신한 그는, 일행에게 전차를 더 안전한 거리로 물리라는 신호를 보내고는, 대담하게 여자에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"그대는 여기 이 선량한 분을 마음에 두고 계신가 보오. 지금 그분의 용서를 받지 못한다면, 앞으로 더욱 부지런히 구하리다. 그대의 아버님이라 해야겠군.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"팔라스께 맹세코, 그대는 아름답소! 아폴로가 그대를 잃어버린 연인으로 착각하지 않도록 조심하시오. 어느 땅이 그대의 어머니라 뽐낼 수 있을지 궁금하구려. 돌아서지 마시오. 휴전이오! 휴전! 그대의 두 눈에는 인도의 태양이 깃들어 있고, 입가에는 이집트가 사랑의 표식을 새겨 놓았소. 페르폴! 고운 마님이여, 이 사람에게 자비를 베풀기 전에는 저 노예에게로 돌아서지 마시오. 적어도 내가 용서받았다는 말만이라도 해 주시오.\"\n\n이 대목에서 그녀가 그의 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"이리로 와 주시겠어요?\" 그녀가 미소 지으며, 벤허에게 우아하게 고개를 숙이며 물었다.\n\n\"잔을 받아 채워 주세요, 부탁드려요.\" 그녀가 벤허에게 말했다. \"제 아버지가 목말라하십니다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그대의 종이 되겠습니다!\"\n\n벤허가 그 부탁을 들어주려 몸을 돌리니, 메살라와 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다. 두 사람의 눈길이 마주쳤다. 유대인의 눈길은 도전적이었고, 로마인의 눈길은 익살로 반짝였다.\n\n\"오, 아름답되 매정한 이방인이여!\" 메살라가 그녀에게 손을 흔들며 말했다. \"아폴로가 그대를 데려가지 않는다면, 그대는 나를 다시 보게 되리라. 그대의 나라를 알지 못하니, 그대를 어느 신에게 맡겨 달라 할 수가 없구려. 그러니 모든 신께 맹세코, 나는 그대를 나 자신에게 맡기리다!\"\n\n미르틸로스가 네 마리 말을 가지런히 추슬러 채비해 놓은 것을 본 그는 전차로 돌아갔다. 여자는 그가 멀어져 가는 모습을 바라보았는데, 그 눈길에 무엇이 담겨 있든 못마땅함은 없었다. 이윽고 그녀는 물을 건네받았다. 아버지가 마셨다. 그런 다음 그녀는 잔을 제 입술로 가져갔다가, 몸을 숙여 벤허에게 건넸으니, 그보다 더 우아하고 품위 있는 동작은 없었다.\n\n\"부디 그것을 간직하세요! 그것은 축복으로 가득하답니다—모두 당신의 것이에요!\"\n\n곧 낙타가 일으켜져 발을 딛고 막 떠나려 할 때, 노인이 불렀다.\n\n\"여기 좀 서 보게.\"\n\n벤허가 공손히 그에게로 갔다.\n\n\"그대는 오늘 이 이방인을 잘 도와주었네. 하느님은 오직 한 분이시지. 그분의 거룩한 이름으로 그대에게 감사하네. 나는 이집트인 발타사르일세. 다프네 마을 너머, 종려나무 대정원의 종려나무 그늘에, 너그러운 일데림 족장께서 천막을 치고 머무시는데, 우리는 그분의 손님일세. 그곳으로 우리를 찾아오게. 감사하는 자의 향취가 깃든 달가운 환대를 받게 될 걸세.\"\n\n벤허는 노인의 맑은 목소리와 존경스러운 태도에 감탄하며 남겨졌다. 떠나가는 두 사람을 바라보던 그는, 메살라가 올 때와 마찬가지로, 즐겁고 무심하게, 비웃는 웃음을 띤 채 가 버리는 모습을 언뜻 보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n무릇 남들이 잘못 행동한 자리에서 홀로 바르게 처신하는 것만큼 사람들의 미움을 사기 좋은 길은 없다. 그러나 이 경우에는 다행히도 말루크가 그 통례에서 벗어난 사람이었다. 그가 방금 목격한 사건은 그의 눈에 벤허를 한층 높여 놓았으니, 그 젊은이의 용기와 수완을 부인할 수 없었기 때문이다. 만약 이제 그 젊은이의 내력까지 어느 정도 들여다볼 수 있다면, 오늘 하루의 성과는 어진 주인 시모니데스에게도 결코 헛되지만은 않을 터였다.\n\n뒤엣것에 관하여, 그가 지금까지 알아낸 바를 두고 말하자면, 두 가지 사실이 그 전부를 아울렀다. 그가 살피고 있는 대상은 유대인이라는 것, 그리고 이름난 어느 로마인의 양자라는 것이었다. 또 하나, 중요할지도 모를 결론이 그 영민한 밀정의 머릿속에서 차츰 형태를 갖추기 시작했다. 메살라와 그 두 집정관 출신의 아들 사이에는 어떤 종류의 연결이 있었다. 그러나 그것은 무엇인가? 그리고 어떻게 그것을 확실한 것으로 좁혀 낼 수 있을까? 아무리 더듬어 보아도 해답에 이르는 방도와 수단은 떠오르지 않았다. 그 곤혹의 열기 속에서, 벤허 자신이 그를 도우러 나섰다. 그는 말루크의 팔에 손을 얹고 그를 군중 속에서 끌어냈다. 군중은 어느새 다시 잿빛 늙은 사제와 신비로운 샘으로 관심을 돌리고 있었다.\n\n\"착한 말루크,\" 그가 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"사람이 제 어머니를 잊을 수 있겠소?\"\n\n그 물음은 느닷없고 또 어디로 향하는지도 알 수 없는 것이어서, 듣는 이를 어리둥절하게 만드는 종류였다. 말루크는 그 뜻을 짚어 보려고 벤허의 얼굴을 들여다보았으나, 그 대신 양 볼에 하나씩 돋은 새빨간 반점과, 그 두 눈 속에 억눌린 눈물의 흔적인 듯한 것을 보았다. 그러자 그는 기계적으로 \"없습니다!\" 하고 답했고, 거기에 열을 띠어 \"결코 없습니다.\" 하고 덧붙였다. 잠시 후 정신을 추스르기 시작하면서 \"그가 이스라엘 사람이라면, 결코 없습니다!\" 하고 말했다. 그리고 마침내 완전히 마음을 가다듬고는 이렇게 말했다. \"회당에서 제가 처음 배운 가르침은 셰마였고, 그다음은 시락의 아들이 한 말이었습니다. '온 영혼을 다하여 네 아비를 공경하고, 네 어미의 슬픔을 잊지 말라.'\"\n\n벤허의 얼굴에 돋은 붉은 반점이 더욱 짙어졌다.\n\n\"그 말이 내 어린 시절을 다시 불러오는구려. 그리고 말루크, 그 말은 그대가 참된 유대인임을 증명하오. 내가 그대를 믿어도 되리라 생각하오.\"\n\n벤허는 붙잡고 있던 팔을 놓고, 제 가슴을 덮은 옷자락을 움켜쥐어 바싹 눌렀으니, 마치 그곳의 아픔을, 혹은 아픔만큼이나 날카로운 어떤 감정을 짓누르려는 듯했다.\n\n\"내 아버지는,\" 그가 말했다. \"좋은 이름을 지니셨고, 사시던 예루살렘에서도 명예를 잃지 않은 분이셨소. 어머니는 아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 한창나이의 여인이셨소. 그분을 두고 그저 착하고 아름다웠다고 말하는 것만으로는 부족하오. 그분의 혀에는 인자함의 법도가 깃들어 있었고, 그분의 행실은 성문 앞 모든 이의 칭송이었으며, 그분은 다가올 날들을 향해 미소 지으셨소. 내게는 어린 누이가 하나 있었으니, 그 아이와 나, 그렇게가 우리 식구였고, 우리는 어찌나 행복했던지, 적어도 나는 옛 랍비의 말씀에서 그릇된 데를 본 적이 없소. '하나님은 모든 곳에 계실 수 없으셨고, 그리하여 어머니를 만드셨다.' 어느 날 권세를 쥔 어느 로마인이 한 대대를 거느리고 우리 집 앞을 지나가다가 사고를 당했소. 군단병들이 문을 부수고 들이닥쳐 우리를 붙잡아 갔소. 그 뒤로 나는 어머니도 누이도 다시 보지 못했소. 두 사람이 죽었는지 살았는지조차 말할 수 없소. 그들이 어찌 되었는지 나는 모르오. 그러나 말루크, 저기 전차에 탄 저 사람이 그 생이별의 자리에 있었소. 그가 우리를 붙잡는 자들에게 넘겼고, 제 자식들을 위한 어머니의 기도를 들었으며, 그들이 어머니를 끌고 갈 때 웃었소. 사랑과 미움, 그 어느 쪽이 기억에 더 깊이 새겨지는지는 좀처럼 가리기 어렵소. 오늘 나는 멀리서 그를 알아보았소. 그리고 말루크—\"\n\n그는 다시 듣는 이의 팔을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"그리고 말루크, 그는 내가 목숨을 바쳐서라도 알고 싶은 그 비밀을 알고 있고, 지금 그것을 품고 다니고 있소. 그는 어머니가 살아 계신지, 어디에 계신지, 어떤 처지인지 말해 줄 수 있소. 만약 그분이—아니, 두 사람이—너무도 큰 슬픔이 둘을 하나로 만들었으니—만약 두 사람이 죽었다면, 그는 어디서 죽었는지, 무엇 때문에 죽었는지, 그 유골이 내 손길을 어디서 기다리고 있는지 말해 줄 수 있소.\"\n\"한데 그가 말해 주지 않으리란 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"나는 유대인이고, 그는 로마인이니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 로마인에게도 혀가 있고, 유대인은 아무리 멸시받는다 해도 그 혀를 꾀어내는 방도를 지니고 있지요.\"\n\n\"그자 같은 인간을 상대로? 아니오. 게다가 그 비밀은 국가의 일이오. 내 아버지의 재산은 죄다 몰수되어 나뉘었소.\"\n\n말루크는 그 논리를 인정하듯 천천히 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 다시 물었다. \"그가 당신을 알아보지 못했소?\"\n\n\"알아볼 수 없었소. 나는 산 채로 죽음의 길로 보내졌고, 오래전부터 죽은 자로 헤아려졌으니까.\"\n\n\"그자를 쳐 버리지 않은 게 놀랍군요.\" 말루크가 한 가닥 격정에 못 이겨 말했다.\n\n\"그것은 그자가 영영 나를 도울 수 없게 만드는 일이 되었을 거요. 나는 그를 죽일 수밖에 없었을 텐데, 그대도 알다시피, 죽음이란 죄 지은 로마인보다도 비밀을 더 잘 지키는 법이오.\"\n\n복수해야 할 것이 그토록 많으면서도 그런 기회를 그처럼 차분히 물리칠 수 있는 사람이라면, 제 앞날에 확신이 있거나 더 나은 계획을 마련해 두었음이 분명했다. 그 생각과 더불어 말루크의 관심도 달라졌다. 그것은 더 이상 다른 이에게 매인 의무 때문에 움직이는 밀정의 관심이 아니었다. 벤허는 이제 제 자신을 위하여 그에게 요구할 권리를 내세우고 있었던 것이다. 다시 말해, 말루크는 진심에서, 그리고 꾸밈없는 감탄에서 우러나 그를 섬길 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n잠시 말이 끊겼다가, 벤허가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그의 목숨을 빼앗지는 않겠소, 착한 말루크. 그 극단에 이르지 않게 막아 주는 것이, 적어도 지금으로서는, 그가 쥔 비밀이오. 그러나 나는 그를 벌할 수는 있을 것이오. 그러니 그대가 나를 돕는다면, 나는 한번 해 보겠소.\"\n\n\"그는 로마인이고,\" 말루크가 망설임 없이 말했다. \"나는 유다 지파 사람입니다. 내 당신을 돕겠습니다. 원하신다면 나를 맹세에 묶으십시오—가장 엄숙한 맹세에.\"\n\n\"그대의 손을 주시오, 그것으로 족하오.\"\n\n두 사람의 손이 떨어지자, 벤허가 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 맡기려는 일은 어렵지 않소, 좋은 친구여. 양심을 두렵게 하는 일도 아니오. 자, 가십시다.\"\n\n두 사람은 샘에 이르는 길을 묘사할 때 말했던 그 풀밭을 가로질러 오른쪽으로 난 길로 접어들었다. 침묵을 먼저 깬 것은 벤허였다.\n\n\"너그러운 일데림 족장을 아시오?\"\n\n\"압니다.\"\n\n\"그의 종려나무 정원은 어디에 있소? 아니, 말루크, 다프네 마을에서 얼마나 더 가야 하오?\"\n\n말루크는 한 가닥 의심에 마음이 걸렸다. 그는 샘가에서 그 여인이 자기에게 보여 준 고운 호의를 떠올리며, 어머니의 슬픔을 가슴에 품은 이 사람이 사랑의 미끼에 그것을 잊으려는 것은 아닌가 의아해했다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"종려나무 정원은 마을에서 말로 두 시간, 빠른 낙타로는 한 시간 떨어진 곳에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙소. 그러면 다시 한번 그대의 식견에 기대겠소. 그대가 말해 준 그 경기는 널리 알려졌소? 그리고 언제 열리오?\"\n\n그 물음들에는 무언가 암시하는 바가 있었다. 그것이 말루크의 신뢰를 되돌려 주지는 못했더라도, 적어도 그의 호기심만은 부추겼다.\n\n\"오, 그렇습니다. 그야말로 굉장한 광경이 될 겁니다. 총독은 부유하니, 자리를 잃는다 해도 견딜 만하지요. 그런데도 성공한 자들이 으레 그렇듯, 재물에 대한 그의 사랑은 조금도 줄지 않았습니다. 그러니 궁정에 친구 하나라도 얻으려면, 다른 게 아무것도 없더라도, 그는 막센티우스 집정관을 위해 떠들썩한 잔치를 벌여야 합니다. 그 집정관은 파르티아인을 칠 원정의 마지막 준비를 하러 이리로 오고 있지요. 그런 준비에 큰돈이 있다는 것을 안티오크 시민들은 경험으로 알고 있습니다. 그래서 그들은 이 위대한 인물에게 바칠 영예에 총독과 함께 끼어들 허락을 받았지요. 한 달 전에 전령들이 사방으로 나가 이 축전을 위한 원형경기장의 개장을 선포했습니다. 총독의 이름만으로도 다채로움과 호화로움을 충분히 보증할 터인데, 특히 동방 전역에서 그러합니다. 그러나 그의 약속에 안티오크가 제 약속을 보태니, 바다에 면한 모든 섬과 도시가 예사롭지 않은 볼거리를 확신하고, 몸소 오거나 가장 이름난 전문 선수들을 보낼 겁니다. 내거는 상금이 왕에게나 어울릴 만하지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 경기장—듣자니 막시무스 다음간다더군.\"\n\n\"로마의 것 말씀이시군요. 그렇소, 우리 경기장은 이십만 명을 앉히고, 그쪽 것은 거기에 칠만오천 명을 더 앉히지요. 그쪽이 대리석이듯, 우리 것도 대리석이고, 구조는 둘이 똑같습니다.\"\n\n\"규칙도 같소?\"\n\n말루크가 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n\"안티오크가 감히 독창성을 부린다면, 아리우스의 아들이여, 로마는 지금과 같은 여주인이 못 되었을 겁니다. 막시무스 경기장의 규칙이 한 가지만 빼고 그대로 통합니다. 그곳에서는 한 번에 전차 넷만 출발할 수 있으나, 여기서는 수에 상관없이 모두 함께 출발하지요.\"\n\n\"그것은 그리스인들의 방식이오.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 안티오크는 로마보다 그리스에 더 가깝습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 말루크, 나는 내 전차를 골라도 되겠구려?\"\n\n\"당신의 전차와 말이지요. 어느 쪽에도 아무 제한이 없습니다.\"\n\n답하면서 말루크는 벤허의 얼굴에서 생각에 잠긴 빛이 흡족한 빛으로 바뀌는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"이제 한 가지만 더 묻겠소, 오 말루크. 축전은 언제 열리오?\"\n\n\"아! 용서하십시오.\" 상대가 답했다. \"내일—그리고 그다음 날,\" 그가 소리 내어 헤아렸다. \"그러고는, 로마식으로 말하자면, 바다의 신들이 어여삐 여기신다면, 집정관이 도착합니다. 그렇소, 오늘부터 엿새째 되는 날에 경기가 열립니다.\"\n\n\"시간이 짧구려, 말루크. 그러나 그것으로 충분하오.\" 마지막 말은 단호하게 떨어졌다. \"우리 옛 이스라엘의 선지자들을 두고 맹세하건대! 나는 다시 고삐를 잡겠소. 잠깐! 한 가지 조건이 있소. 메살라가 출전하리라는 확실한 보장이 있소?\"\n\n말루크는 이제 그 계획을, 그리고 그 로마인에게 굴욕을 안길 온갖 기회를 알아차렸다. 만약 그가, 그토록 관심이 깨어났는데도 승산을 가늠하는 일에 곧장 뛰어들지 않았다면, 그는 야곱의 참된 후손이 아니었으리라. 그의 목소리는 실로 떨렸다. \"솜씨가 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"두려워 마시오, 친구여. 막시무스 경기장의 승자들은 지난 삼 년 동안 내 뜻에 따라 그 월계관을 차지해 왔소. 그들에게 물어보시오—그 가운데 가장 뛰어난 자에게 물어보시오—그러면 그렇다고 말해 줄 것이오. 지난번 대경기에서는 황제께서 친히, 내가 그의 말들을 맡아 온 세상의 출전마들을 상대로 몰아 보겠다면 나를 후원하겠다고 하셨소.\"\n\n\"한데 몰지 않으셨군요?\"\n\n말루크가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"나는—나는 유대인이오.\" 벤허는 그렇게 말하면서 제 안으로 움츠러드는 듯했다. \"그리고 비록 로마식 이름을 지녔으나, 나는 성전의 회랑과 뜰에서 내 아버지의 이름을 더럽힐 일을 직업으로 삼을 수는 없었소. 연무장에서라면 그런 연습에 몸을 맡길 수 있으나, 그것을 경기장까지 끌고 들어가면 가증한 일이 되고 마오. 그러니 내가 여기서 경주에 나선다면, 말루크, 맹세코 그것은 상이나 승자의 상금을 위한 일이 아닐 것이오.\"\n\n\"잠깐—그런 맹세는 마십시오!\" 말루크가 소리쳤다. \"그 상금은 일만 세스테르티우스—평생을 누릴 재산입니다!\"\n\n\"내게는 아니오, 총독이 그것을 쉰 곱절로 세 배 불린다 해도. 그보다 나은 것, 첫 카이사르의 첫해부터 거두어들인 모든 황실 세입보다 나은 것—나는 이 경주로 내 원수를 무릎 꿇리겠소. 복수는 율법이 허락하는 바이오.\"\n\n말루크는 빙긋 웃으며 고개를 끄덕였으니, 마치 \"옳소, 옳소—유대인을 알아보는 데는 이 유대인을 믿으시오.\" 하고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n\"메살라는 몰 겁니다.\" 그가 곧바로 말했다. \"그는 여러모로 이미 경주에 매여 있습니다—거리와 목욕탕과 극장, 궁전과 병영에 두루 공표된 데다, 물러설 길 없게 못 박으려는 듯, 그의 이름은 안티오크의 모든 젊은 난봉꾼의 서판에 올라 있습니다.\"\n\n\"내기에 말이오, 말루크?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 내기에 말입니다. 그리고 그는 당신이 본 대로 날마다 보란 듯이 연습하러 나옵니다.\"\n\n\"아! 그러면 저것이 그가 경주에 쓸 전차이고, 저것들이 그 말이오? 고맙소, 고맙소, 말루크! 그대는 이미 나를 잘 도와주었소. 나는 만족하오. 이제 종려나무 정원으로 나를 안내하고, 너그러운 일데림 족장에게 나를 소개해 주시오.\"\n\"언제 말입니까?\"\n\n\"오늘이오. 그의 말들은 내일이면 다른 데 잡힐지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 말들이 마음에 드신 거군요?\"\n\n벤허가 활기를 띠어 답했다.\n\n\"관람석에서 한순간 보았을 뿐이오. 그때 메살라가 전차를 몰고 나타나, 나는 다른 데로 눈을 돌릴 수가 없었으니까. 그러나 나는 그것들이 사막의 경이이자 영광인 그 혈통임을 알아보았소. 나는 그런 종류를 카이사르의 마구간 말고는 본 적이 없으나, 한번 보면 언제든 알아볼 수 있소. 내일 만나면 나는 그대를 알아볼 것이오, 말루크. 그대가 내게 인사 한 번 건네지 않더라도. 나는 그대의 얼굴로, 모습으로, 거동으로 그대를 알아볼 것이오. 그리고 똑같은 표지로 그 말들을 알아볼 것이며, 똑같은 확신으로 그리할 것이오. 그것들에 관해 떠도는 말이 모두 사실이고, 내가 그 기백을 내 손아귀에 넣을 수 있다면, 나는—\"\n\n\"세스테르티우스를 따시겠지요!\" 말루크가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 벤허가 그만큼 빠르게 답했다. \"나는 야곱의 유산을 물려받은 사람에게 더 어울리는 일을 하겠소—나는 더없이 많은 사람이 보는 자리에서 내 원수를 무릎 꿇리겠소. 그러나,\" 그가 조바심을 내며 덧붙였다. \"우리는 시간을 허비하고 있소. 어떻게 하면 족장의 천막에 가장 빨리 닿을 수 있겠소?\"\n\n말루크는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"곧장 마을로 가는 것이 상책입니다. 다행히 가까이 있으니까요. 거기서 빠른 낙타 두 마리를 빌릴 수 있다면, 한 시간이면 길에 오를 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 어서 그리합시다.\"\n\n그 마을은 아름다운 정원에 둘러싸인 궁궐들이 모여 이룬 곳으로, 그 사이에 왕후에게나 어울릴 법한 숙소들이 점점이 박혀 있었다. 다행히 단봉낙타들을 구할 수 있었고, 그것들에 올라 이름난 종려나무 정원으로 가는 여정이 시작되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\n마을 너머의 땅은 기복이 완만하고 잘 일구어져 있었다. 사실 그곳은 안티오크의 정원이라 할 만한 땅으로, 손길이 닿지 않은 한 뼘의 땅도 없었다. 가파른 언덕의 비탈은 계단식으로 일구어져 있었고, 울타리마저도 기어오르는 포도 덩굴로 더욱 화사했으니, 그 덩굴은 그늘의 유혹과 더불어 지나는 이에게 앞으로 빚어질 포도주와, 송이송이 자줏빛으로 무르익을 포도의 달콤한 약속을 건네주었다. 멜론밭 너머로, 살구나무와 무화과나무 숲을 지나고, 오렌지나무와 라임나무 숲을 지나며, 농부들의 흰 회칠한 집들이 보였다. 곳곳에서 풍요는, 평화의 미소 짓는 딸인 그 풍요는 수천 가지 표시로 제가 이곳에 깃들어 있음을 알렸으니, 마음 너그러운 나그네의 심사를 흐뭇하게 하여, 마침내 로마에게 마땅한 몫을 인정해 줄 마음이 들게 할 정도였다. 이따금 타우루스와 레바논의 풍경도 눈에 들어왔는데, 그 사이로는 은빛 분계선처럼 오론테스강이 잔잔히 제 길을 따라 흐르고 있었다.\n\n여행하는 동안 두 사람은 강에 이르렀고, 길의 굽이를 따라 강줄기를 좇았다. 때로는 가파른 절벽 위로, 때로는 골짜기 안으로 길이 이어졌는데, 그 골짜기들은 하나같이 시골 별장 터로 나뉘어 있었다. 땅이 떡갈나무와 무화과나무와 도금양과 월계수와 소귀나무와 향내 나는 재스민의 무성한 잎으로 뒤덮여 있다면, 강은 비스듬히 비치는 햇살로 환했으니, 그 햇살은 떨어진 자리에서 그대로 잠들 법도 했지만, 끝없이 줄지어 미끄러져 가는 배들이 그것을 깨웠다. 물살을 타고 흐르는 배, 바람을 받으려 방향을 트는 배, 노의 힘으로 솟구치듯 나아가는 배—오는 것도 있고 가는 것도 있었으니, 하나같이 바다와 머나먼 백성들과 이름난 곳들과 그 진귀함 때문에 탐내는 물건들을 떠올리게 했다. 상상에 가장 마음을 사로잡는 것은 바다를 향해 부풀어 가는 흰 돛이거니와, 그에 견줄 것이 있다면 항해를 무사히 마치고 고향으로 돌아오는 흰 돛뿐이다. 두 사람은 강기슭을 따라 줄곧 내려가다가, 마침내 강물이 거슬러 흘러들어 이루어진 호수에 이르렀으니, 맑고 깊으며 물살이 없었다. 늙은 야자나무 한 그루가 후미의 모퉁이를 굽어보고 있었다. 그 나무 아래서 왼쪽으로 돌아서며, 말루크는 두 손을 마주치고 소리쳤다.\n\n\"보시오, 저것을 보시오! 야자나무 동산이오!\"\n\n이런 풍경은 아라비아의 축복받은 오아시스나 나일강을 따라 펼쳐진 프톨레마이오스 왕조의 농원이 아니고서는 어디서도 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그리고 그 황홀함은 새로운 만큼이나 즐거운 것이어서, 벤허는 끝이 없어 보이고 마룻바닥처럼 평평한 한 자락의 땅으로 들어섰다. 발밑은 온통 싱그러운 풀이었으니, 시리아에서 풀이란 가장 보기 드물고 가장 아름다운 땅의 산물이었다. 위를 올려다보면, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 대추야자나무가 이룬 천장의 갈빗대 사이로 옅푸른 하늘이 보였다. 그 나무들은 제 종족의 진정한 족장이라 할 만큼 수도 많고 늙었으며, 둘레가 어찌나 굵고 키가 어찌나 크며 빽빽하고 가지가 넓게 뻗었던지, 그 가지마다 깃털 같고 밀랍 같고 눈부신 잎이 더없이 완벽하게 달려 있어, 마치 마법에 걸린 마법사들 같았다. 여기서는 풀이 대기마저 물들이고 있었고, 저기서는 호수가 시원하고 맑게, 수면 바로 몇 자 아래에서 잔물결을 일으키며, 나무들이 늙어서도 오래 살도록 거들고 있었다. 다프네의 숲인들 이곳을 능가할 수 있었으랴? 그리고 야자나무들은 마치 벤허의 마음을 알아채고 제 나름의 방식으로 그를 사로잡으려는 듯, 그가 그 아치 아래를 지날 때 흔들리며 이슬 머금은 서늘함을 그에게 뿌려 주는 것 같았다.\n\n길은 호숫가와 바짝 나란히 굽이쳐 이어졌다. 길이 나그네들을 물가까지 데려갈 때면, 그쪽으로는 언제나 빛나는 너른 수면이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 멀지 않은 맞은편 기슭이 그 끝을 가르고 있었다. 그 기슭에도 이쪽과 마찬가지로 야자나무 말고는 어떤 나무도 허락되지 않았다.\n\n\"저것을 보시오.\" 말루크가 그곳의 거목 한 그루를 가리키며 말했다. \"줄기에 새겨진 고리 하나하나가 그 나무가 산 한 해를 나타내오. 뿌리에서 가지까지 그것을 세어 보시오. 셀레우코스 왕조가 안티오크에 이름을 알리기 전에 이 동산이 심어졌다고 족장이 말하거든, 의심하지 마시오.\"\n\n완벽한 야자나무를 바라보면, 그 나무는 제 나름의 미묘한 힘으로 스스로 하나의 존재감을 띠며, 보는 이를 시인으로 만든다. 이것이 바로 그 나무가 받아 온 영예의 까닭이니, 그 영예는 최초의 왕들을 섬긴 예술가들에게서 비롯되었다. 그들은 온 땅을 통틀어 궁전과 신전의 기둥에 본보기로 삼기에 야자나무만큼 합당한 형태를 찾지 못했던 것이다. 그리고 같은 까닭에서 벤허는 이렇게 말하고 싶어졌다.\n\n\"오늘 경주장에서 본 바로는, 말루크여, 일데림 족장은 지극히 평범한 사람으로 보였소. 예루살렘의 랍비들이라면, 안타깝지만, 그를 에돔의 개의 자식이라며 깔보겠지요. 그가 어떻게 이 동산을 차지하게 된 거요? 그리고 로마 총독들의 탐욕에 맞서 어떻게 이것을 지켜 올 수 있었던 거요?\"\n\n\"혈통이 세월에서 그 탁월함을 얻는 것이라면, 아리우스의 아들이여, 늙은 일데림은 비록 할례받지 않은 에돔 사람일지언정 진정한 사내요.\"\n\n말루크는 열을 띠어 말했다.\n\n\"그에 앞선 조상들이 모두 족장이었소. 그중 한 분은—언제 사셨는지, 어떤 선행을 하셨는지는 말하지 않겠소—칼날에 쫓기던 어느 왕을 도운 적이 있었소. 전해 오는 이야기로는, 그분이 그 왕에게 기병 천 명을 빌려주었다 하오. 그들은 양치기들이 제 가축과 더불어 사는 메마른 언덕을 훤히 알듯이, 광야의 길과 그 숨을 곳들을 훤히 알고 있었소. 그들은 기회가 올 때까지 왕을 이리저리 데리고 다녔고, 마침내 창으로 적을 무찔러 그를 다시 왕좌에 앉혔소. 전하는 말로는, 그 왕이 그 공을 기억하여 이 사막의 아들을 이곳으로 데려와, 천막을 치고 가족과 가축 떼를 데려오라 일렀다 하오. 호수와 나무들, 그리고 강에서 가장 가까운 산에 이르는 온 땅이 그와 그 자손들의 것으로 영원히 주어졌기 때문이오. 그리고 그들은 그 소유를 한 번도 방해받지 않았소. 뒤를 이은 통치자들은 그 부족과 좋은 관계를 유지하는 것이 상책임을 알게 되었으니, 주께서 그 부족에게 사람과 말과 낙타와 재물을 더하여 주시어, 도시와 도시를 잇는 수많은 길의 주인으로 삼으셨기 때문이오. 그리하여 그들은 언제든 마음만 먹으면 교역을 향해 '편히 가라' 하거나 '멈추라' 할 수 있고, 그들이 말한 바는 그대로 이루어지오. 안티오크를 굽어보는 성채의 지방관조차도, 일데림이—온갖 부류의 사람들에게 베푼 선행 덕에 '관대한 자'라 불리는 그 일데림이—제 아내들과 자식들, 낙타와 말의 행렬과 족장의 살림붙이를 거느리고, 우리 조상 아브라함과 야곱이 옮겨 다녔듯이 옮겨 와서, 그 쓰디쓴 우물을 잠시 그대가 우리 둘레에서 보는 이 즐거움과 맞바꾸려 올라오는 날을, 제게 복된 날이라 여긴다오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어찌 된 일이오?\" 단봉낙타들의 느린 걸음에는 아랑곳없이 귀를 기울이고 있던 벤허가 말했다. \"나는 그 족장이 로마인을 믿었던 자신을 저주하며 수염을 쥐어뜯는 것을 보았소. 만일 카이사르가 그 말을 들었다면, '나는 이런 자를 벗으로 삼고 싶지 않다. 그를 내쳐라' 하고 말했을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"그것은 빈틈없는 판단일 따름이오.\" 말루크가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"일데림은 로마를 사랑하는 자가 아니오. 그에게는 원한이 있소. 삼 년 전, 파르티아인들이 보스라에서 다마스쿠스로 가는 길을 가로질러 달려와, 여러 짐 가운데서도 그쪽 한 지방에서 거두어들인 세금을 싣고 오던 대상을 덮쳤소. 그들은 붙잡은 자를 하나도 남김없이 죽였소. 로마의 감찰관들도 황실의 재물이 무사히 보내졌더라면 그것은 용서했을 거요. 손실의 책임을 진 세금 도급업자들이 카이사르에게 하소연했고, 카이사르는 헤롯에게 변상을 물렸으며, 헤롯은 그 나름대로 일데림의 재산을 몰수했소. 그에게 직무를 게을리한 반역의 죄를 씌운 것이오. 족장은 카이사르에게 탄원했으나, 카이사르는 눈 한 번 깜박이지 않는 스핑크스에게서나 들을 법한 대답을 그에게 주었소. 그 후로 노인의 가슴은 줄곧 쓰리게 아팠고, 그는 제 분노를 키우며 날마다 그것이 자라는 데서 즐거움을 찾고 있소.\"\n\n\"그가 할 수 있는 일은 없소, 말루크.\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 말루크가 말했다. \"거기에는 또 다른 설명이 따르는데, 우리가 더 가까이 다가설 수 있으면 그것을 들려드리리다. 한데 보시오!—족장의 환대가 일찌감치 시작되었소—아이들이 그대에게 말을 걸고 있소.\"\n\n단봉낙타들이 멈추었고, 벤허는 시리아 농민 계급의 어린 소녀들을 내려다보았다. 그들은 대추야자 열매를 가득 담은 바구니를 그에게 내밀고 있었다. 갓 따 온 열매라 마다할 수 없었다. 그가 몸을 굽혀 그것을 받았는데, 그렇게 하는 사이 그들이 멈추어 선 나무 위에 있던 한 사내가 외쳤다. \"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 어서 오시오!\"\n\n아이들에게 고맙다는 인사를 건넨 뒤, 두 사람은 짐승들이 고르는 걸음대로 다시 나아갔다.\n\n\"알아 두셔야 할 것은,\" 말루크가 이따금 멈추어 대추야자를 입에 넣으며 말을 이었다. \"상인 시모니데스가 내게 신임을 베풀어, 때로는 나를 의논 자리에 끌어들이는 영광을 주기도 한다는 거요. 그의 집에서 그를 시중들다 보니, 나는 그의 벗을 많이 알게 되었소. 그들은 내가 주인과 가까운 사이임을 알기에, 내가 있는 자리에서도 그에게 거리낌 없이 이야기하오. 그런 식으로 나는 일데림 족장과도 어느 정도 가까워졌소.\"\n\n잠시 벤허의 마음이 딴 데로 흘렀다. 그의 마음의 눈앞에는 상인의 딸 에스더의 모습이 떠올랐으니, 순수하고 부드러우며 사람의 마음을 끄는 모습이었다. 유대인 특유의 광채로 빛나는 그녀의 검은 눈이 다소곳한 눈길로 그의 눈을 마주했고, 그녀가 포도주를 들고 그에게 다가올 때의 발소리와, 잔을 건네던 그녀의 목소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 그는 그녀가 자기에게 보였던 그 모든 공감을, 말이 필요 없을 만큼 분명하게, 또 말이 오히려 흠이 되었을 만큼 곱게 드러내 보였던 그 공감을 다시금 마음속으로 인정했다. 그 환영은 더없이 즐거웠으나, 그가 말루크에게로 고개를 돌리자 이내 날아가 버렸다.\n\n\"몇 주 전에,\" 말루크가 말을 이었다. \"그 늙은 아랍인이 시모니데스를 찾아왔는데, 마침 내가 그 자리에 있었소. 보아하니 그가 무언가로 몹시 심란해하는 듯하여, 나는 예를 차려 물러가겠노라 했으나, 그가 손수 나를 말렸소. '그대가 이스라엘 사람이니,' 그가 말했소, '머물러 주시오. 내게 들려줄 이상한 이야기가 하나 있소.' 이스라엘 사람이라는 말에 힘을 준 것이 내 호기심을 돋우었소. 나는 머물렀고, 그가 들려준 이야기의 요지가 이렇소—우리가 천막에 가까워지고 있으니 짧게 줄이고, 자세한 내막은 그 어른께 맡기겠소. 여러 해 전, 세 사람이 광야에 있는 일데림의 천막을 찾아왔소. 모두 이방인이었으니, 한 사람은 힌두인, 한 사람은 그리스인, 한 사람은 이집트인이었소. 그들은 낙타를 타고 왔는데, 그가 여태껏 본 가운데 가장 큰 낙타들이었고, 하나같이 흰색이었소. 그는 그들을 맞이하여 쉬게 했소. 이튿날 아침 그들은 일어나 족장에게는 낯선 기도를 올렸소—하느님과 그 아들에게 드리는 기도였는데—그 밖에도 신비로운 것이 많았소. 그와 함께 아침을 든 뒤, 이집트인이 자기들이 누구이며 어디서 왔는지를 밝혔소. 세 사람은 저마다 별을 하나씩 보았고, 그 별에서 들려온 목소리가 그들에게 예루살렘으로 가서 '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?' 하고 물으라 명했다는 것이오. 그들은 그 명에 따랐소. 예루살렘에서 그들은 별의 인도를 받아 베들레헴으로 갔고, 거기 한 동굴에서 갓 태어난 아기를 찾아내어, 엎드려 경배했소. 그리고 그 아기를 경배하고, 값진 예물을 바치며, 그 아기가 어떤 분인지 증언한 뒤, 낙타에 올라 쉬지 않고 족장에게로 달아났소. 헤롯이—'대왕'이라 불리는 그 헤롯 말이오—그들에게 손을 뻗칠 수 있게 되면 틀림없이 그들을 죽일 것이기 때문이었소. 그리하여 제 습관에 충실하게, 족장은 그들을 돌보아 일 년 동안 숨겨 주었고, 그들은 그에게 매우 값진 선물을 남긴 채 떠나가, 저마다 다른 길을 갔소.\"\n\n\"참으로 더없이 놀라운 이야기로군요.\" 이야기가 끝나자 벤허가 외쳤다. \"예루살렘에서 무엇을 물으라 했다고 하셨소?\"\n\n\"'유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?' 하고 묻기로 되어 있었소.\"\n\n\"그게 전부였소?\"\n\n\"질문에는 더 있었으나, 그것은 기억나지 않소.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 아기를 찾아냈소?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그리고 그를 경배했소.\"\n\n\"그것은 기적이오, 말루크.\"\n\n\"일데림은 진중한 사람이오. 비록 모든 아랍인이 그렇듯 격하기는 하지만 말이오. 그의 혀에 거짓말이 오르는 일은 있을 수 없소.\"\n\n말루크는 단호하게 말했다. 그 바람에 단봉낙타들은 잊히고 말았으니, 그놈들도 제 타는 이들에게는 아랑곳없이 길을 벗어나 자라는 풀밭으로 향했다.\n\n\"일데림은 그 세 사람의 소식을 그 뒤로 더 듣지 못했소?\" 벤허가 물었다. \"그들은 어떻게 되었소?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇소, 바로 그것이 내가 말한 그날 그가 시모니데스를 찾아온 까닭이었소. 바로 그 전날 밤, 그 이집트인이 그에게 다시 나타났던 거요.\"\n\n\"어디에서요?\"\n\n\"바로 여기, 우리가 다가가고 있는 그 천막의 문 앞에서요.\"\n\n\"그가 어떻게 그 사람을 알아보았소?\"\n\n\"오늘 그대가 그 말들을 알아보았듯이—얼굴과 몸가짐으로 알아보았소.\"\n\n\"그 밖에 다른 것은 없었소?\"\n\n\"그는 똑같은 큰 흰 낙타를 타고 있었고, 똑같은 이름을 댔소—이집트인 발타사르라고.\"\n\n\"그것은 주님의 경이로다!\"\n\n벤허가 흥분하여 말했다.\n\n그러자 말루크가 의아해하며 물었다. \"어찌 그렇소?\"\n\n\"발타사르라 하셨소?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 이집트인 발타사르요.\"\n\n\"그것은 오늘 그 노인이 샘터에서 우리에게 댄 이름이오.\"\n\n그 말에 일깨워져, 이번에는 말루크가 흥분했다.\n\n\"정말이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"낙타도 똑같았소—그리고 그대가 그 사람의 목숨을 구했소.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 여인은,\" 벤허가 혼잣말하듯 말했다. \"그 여인은 그의 딸이었소.\"\n\n그는 생각에 잠겼다. 독자조차도 그가 그 여인의 환영을 보고 있었다고, 그리고 그것이 에스더의 환영보다 더 반가운 것이었다고 말할 법하다. 적어도 그것이 더 오래 그에게 머물렀다는 까닭만으로도. 하지만 그렇지 않았으니—\n\n\"다시 말해 주시오.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다. \"그 세 사람이 '유대인의 왕이 되실 이가 어디 계시냐?' 하고 물어야 했던 거요?\"\n\n\"정확히 그것은 아니오. 그 말은 '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이'였소. 늙은 족장이 사막에서 처음 들은 말이 바로 그것이었고, 그 후로 그는 그 왕이 오기를 줄곧 기다리고 있소. 또한 그 왕이 오리라는 그의 믿음은 누구도 흔들 수 없소.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그리고 로마에 멸망을 가져오면서 온다는 거요—족장은 그렇게 말하오.\"\n\n벤허는 한동안 잠자코 있으면서, 생각에 잠긴 채 제 감정을 다스리려 애썼다.\n\n\"그 노인은 수백만 사람 가운데 하나요.\" 그가 천천히 말했다. \"갚아야 할 원한을 저마다 품은 수백만 사람 가운데 하나란 말이오. 그리고 이 기이한 믿음은, 말루크여, 그의 희망에 빵이요 포도주요. 로마가 버티고 있는 한, 헤롯이 아니고서 누가 유대인의 왕이 될 수 있겠소? 한데, 이야기를 이어 가자면, 시모니데스가 그에게 무어라 했는지 들으셨소?\"\n\n\"일데림이 진중한 사람이라면, 시모니데스는 지혜로운 사람이오.\" 말루크가 대답했다. \"나는 귀를 기울였고, 그는 이렇게 말했소— 한데 들어 보시오! 누군가 우리를 따라잡으며 오고 있소.\"\n\n소리가 점점 커지더니, 이윽고 그들은 말발굽 소리에 뒤섞인 바퀴 구르는 소리를 들었다—그리고 한순간 뒤, 일데림 족장이 몸소 말을 타고 나타났고, 그 뒤로 행렬이 따랐는데, 그 가운데에는 전차를 끄는 포도주빛 아랍 말 네 마리가 있었다. 족장의 턱은 길고 흰 수염에 파묻힌 채 가슴 위로 떨구어져 있었다. 우리의 두 벗이 그를 앞질러 와 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그들을 보자 그는 고개를 들고 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"그대들에게 평화가 있기를!—아, 내 벗 말루크여! 어서 오시오! 그대가 떠나는 길이 아니라 막 도착한 길이라고, 그리고 그 어진 시모니데스에게서 내게 전할 무언가를 가져왔노라고 말해 주시오—그분의 조상의 주께서 그분을 앞으로도 오래오래 살게 해 주시기를! 자, 두 사람 다 짐끈을 챙겨 나를 따라오시오. 내게 빵과 레벤이 있고, 그게 싫거든 아라크와 어린 염소 고기도 있소. 자, 오시오!\"\n\n그들은 그를 따라 천막 문에 이르렀고, 거기서 낙타에서 내리자, 그는 그들을 맞으려 서 있었으니, 손에는 잔 세 개가 담긴 쟁반을 들고 있었다. 잔에는 가운데 기둥에 매달린, 연기에 그을린 커다란 가죽 부대에서 막 따라 낸 크림빛 음료가 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"마시오.\" 그가 진심으로 말했다. \"마시오, 이것은 천막에 사는 자들의 두려움을 모르게 하는 술이오.\"\n\n그들은 저마다 잔을 들어, 거품만 남을 때까지 마셨다.\n\n\"이제 하느님의 이름으로 들어오시오.\"\n\n그리고 그들이 안으로 들어서자, 말루크는 족장을 한쪽으로 데려가 은밀히 이야기를 나누었다. 그러고 나서 그는 벤허에게로 가 양해를 구했다.\n\n\"내가 족장에게 그대에 대해 이야기해 두었으니, 그가 아침에 그대에게 자기 말들을 시험해 볼 기회를 줄 거요. 그는 그대의 벗이오. 내가 그대를 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 했으니, 나머지는 그대가 해내야 하오. 나는 안티오크로 돌아가게 해 주시오. 그곳에 오늘 밤 만나기로 약속한 사람이 하나 있소. 가지 않을 도리가 없소. 그동안 만사가 잘되면, 나는 내일 채비를 갖추고 돌아와 경기가 끝날 때까지 그대와 함께 머무르겠소.\"\n\n축복을 주고받은 뒤, 말루크는 돌아가는 길에 올랐다.\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\n초승달의 아래쪽 뿔이 술피우스 산 위의 성채 같은 돌무더기에 닿을 무렵, 안티오크 주민의 삼분의 이가 지붕 위로 나와, 바람이 불면 밤바람으로, 바람이 잦아들면 부채로 더위를 달래고 있을 때, 시모니데스는 이제 그의 몸의 일부가 되다시피 한 의자에 앉아, 테라스에서 강과, 정박지에 매여 흔들거리는 자신의 배들을 내려다보고 있었다. 등 뒤의 벽은 그 그림자를 물 위로 넓게 드리워 맞은편 기슭까지 뻗게 했다. 그의 머리 위로는 다리 위를 끝없이 오가는 발소리가 이어졌다. 에스더는 그의 검소한 저녁상을 담은 접시를 들고 있었다. 살갗처럼 얇은 밀전병 몇 개와 꿀, 그리고 우유 한 사발이었는데, 그는 이따금 전병을 꿀에 적신 다음 우유에 담갔다.\n\n\"말루크가 오늘 밤은 굼뜨구나.\" 그는 자신의 생각이 어디에 있는지 드러내며 말했다.\n\n\"그가 올 거라고 믿으세요?\" 에스더가 물었다.\n\n\"바다나 사막으로 들어가서 아직 뒤를 좇고 있는 게 아니라면, 올 게다.\"\n\n시모니데스는 조용한 확신을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"편지를 보낼지도 모르지요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지 않다, 에스더. 돌아올 수 없다는 걸 알았다면 편지를 부쳐서 내게 그렇게 알렸을 게다. 그런 편지를 받지 못했으니, 나는 그가 올 수 있고 또 올 것임을 안다.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요.\" 그녀가 아주 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n그 말투에 담긴 무언가가 그의 주의를 끌었다. 어조 때문이었을 수도 있고, 그 바람 때문이었을 수도 있다. 아무리 작은 새라도 아무리 큰 나무에 내려앉으면 그 가장 먼 가지 끝까지 떨림을 전한다. 어떤 마음도 때로는 가장 하찮은 말에 그에 못지않게 예민해진다.\n\n\"그가 오기를 바라느냐, 에스더?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"왜냐? 말해 줄 수 있겠느냐?\" 그가 캐물었다.\n\n\"그건요\"—그녀는 머뭇거리다가 다시 입을 열었다—\"그 젊은이가—\"\n말은 거기서 뚝 끊겼다.\n\n\"우리 주인이라서. 그 말이 하고 싶은 게냐?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그런데도 너는, 그가 원한다면 와서 우리를—그리고 우리가 가진 모든 것을—전부, 에스더—재물도, 은화도, 배도, 노예도, 그리고 사람의 천사들 가운데 으뜸가는 자, 곧 성공이 나를 위해 금실과 더없이 고운 은실로 자아 준 외투와도 같은 막대한 신용까지—데려가라고 일러 주지 않은 채 그를 떠나보내서는 안 된다고 여전히 생각하느냐.\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"그것이 네 마음을 조금도 움직이지 못하느냐? 못해?\" 그가 희미한 씁쓸함을 담아 말했다. \"글쎄, 글쎄, 에스더, 나는 깨달았다. 처음에는 어슴푸레하게밖에 보이지 않던 그 구름을 뚫고 나오기만 하면, 가장 끔찍한 현실도 결코 견디지 못할 것이 아니라는 걸—결코 그렇지 않다는 걸—고문대조차 그렇다는 걸. 죽음도 그러하리라 생각한다. 그런 이치라면, 우리가 향해 가는 노예 신세도 머지않아 달콤해질 게다. 지금도 나는 우리 주인이 얼마나 복받은 사람인지 생각하면 흐뭇하구나. 그 재산은 그에게 아무 대가도 치르게 하지 않았다—근심 하나, 땀 한 방울, 생각 한 조각조차도. 그것은 꿈에도 그려 보지 못한 채, 젊은 나이에 그에게 들러붙는다. 그리고 에스더, 이런 생각으로 약간의 허영을 부려 보게 해 다오. 그는 온 재물을 다 합쳐도 시장에 나가 살 수 없는 것을 얻는다—바로 너를, 내 아이, 내 사랑을. 내가 잃은 라헬의 무덤에서 피어난 꽃이여!\"\n\n그는 그녀를 끌어당겨 두 번 입 맞추었다—한 번은 그녀를 위해, 한 번은 그녀의 어머니를 위해.\n\n\"그런 말씀 마세요.\" 그의 손이 그녀의 목에서 떨어지자 그녀가 말했다. \"그를 더 좋게 생각해요. 그는 슬픔이 무엇인지 아니, 우리를 풀어 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"아, 에스더, 너의 직감은 빼어나구나. 그리고 너도 알다시피, 오늘 아침 그가 내 앞에 섰던 것처럼 누군가가 앞에 섰을 때, 그가 좋은 사람인지 나쁜 사람인지 가려야 하는 미심쩍은 경우에 나는 너의 직감에 기댄다. 그러나—그러나\"—그의 목소리가 높아지고 굳어졌다—\"내가 그 위에 설 수도 없는 이 두 다리—사람의 형상에서 비틀리고 두들겨 망가진 이 몸뚱이—이것만이 내가 그에게 바치는 나의 전부가 아니다. 오, 아니다, 아니야! 나는 그에게 고문과, 어떤 고문보다도 더 매서운 로마의 악의를 이겨 낸 영혼을 바친다—나는 그에게, 솔로몬의 배들이 항해한 거리보다 더 멀리 떨어진 황금을 볼 수 있는 눈과, 그것을 손안에 가져올 힘을 지닌—그래, 에스더, 누군가 다른 이의 말에 날개를 달고 날아가 버리지 않도록 손가락으로 움켜쥐어 붙잡을 수 있게 바로 여기 내 손바닥 안으로 가져올 힘을 지닌—책략에 능한 정신을 바친다\"—그는 멈추고 웃었다—\"이것 봐라, 에스더, 지금 이 순간 거룩한 언덕의 성전 뜰에서 사람들이 경축하고 있는 저 초승달이 다음 사분에 들어서기도 전에, 나는 카이사르조차 깜짝 놀라게 할 만큼 온 세상을 울려 댈 수 있다. 알아 두어라, 얘야, 나에게는 어떤 감각 하나보다도 나은, 완벽한 육체보다도 나은, 용기와 의지보다도 나은, 흔히 가장 긴 생애의 가장 좋은 산물인 경험보다도 나은—사람에게 가장 신성한 능력이 있다. 그러나\"—그는 멈추고 다시 웃었다, 씁쓸하게가 아니라 참된 흥취로—\"그러나 위대한 자들조차 충분히 헤아리지 못하고, 무리들에게는 아예 있지도 않은 것으로 치는 그 능력—사람들을 내 뜻으로 끌어들여 그 성취에 충실히 매어 두는 능력, 그리하여 해야 할 일들을 두고 나 자신을 수백 수천으로 불려 내는 능력 말이다. 그래서 내 배의 선장들은 바다를 갈며 내게 정직한 이문을 가져온다. 그래서 말루크는 그 젊은이, 우리 주인을 좇고 있고, 또 그리하여\"—바로 그때 테라스 위에서 발소리가 들렸다—\"하, 에스더! 내가 그렇게 말하지 않았느냐? 그가 왔다—이제 우리는 소식을 듣게 될 게다. 사랑스러운 아이야, 너를 위하여—이제 막 봉오리를 맺은 내 백합아—나는 이스라엘의 길 잃은 양들을 잊지 않으신 주 하느님께, 그 소식이 좋고 위안이 되기를 빈다. 이제 우리는 알게 되리라, 그가 너를 그 온갖 아름다움과 함께, 나를 그 온갖 능력과 함께 놓아줄지를.\"\n\n말루크가 의자 곁으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"평안하시기를, 어지신 주인님.\" 그는 깊이 머리를 숙이며 말했다—\"그리고 에스더, 더없이 훌륭한 따님께도 평안을.\"\n\n그는 공손하게 두 사람 앞에 섰는데, 그 태도와 인사말은 그와 그들의 관계를 가늠하기 어렵게 했다. 하나는 종의 것이었고, 다른 하나는 친밀한 벗을 가리켰다. 한편 시모니데스는 사업에서 늘 그러하듯 인사에 답한 뒤 곧장 본론으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"그 젊은이는 어떻더냐, 말루크?\"\n\n그날 하루의 일들이 조용히, 더없이 단순한 말로 전해졌고, 그가 다 마칠 때까지 끼어드는 일은 없었다. 의자에 앉은 청자는 이야기가 이어지는 동안 손 하나 까딱하지 않았다. 크게 뜨여 반짝이는 눈과 이따금 길게 들이쉬는 숨이 없었더라면, 그는 한갓 인형으로 여겨졌을 것이다.\n\n\"고맙다, 고맙구나, 말루크.\" 이야기가 끝나자 그가 진심으로 말했다. \"참 잘했다—누구도 그보다 잘하지는 못했을 게다. 자, 그 젊은이의 출신에 대해서는 무어라 말하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"그는 이스라엘 사람입니다, 어지신 주인님. 그것도 유다 지파입니다.\"\n\n\"확신하느냐?\"\n\n\"아주 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"제 삶에 대해서는 네게 거의 말하지 않은 모양이구나.\"\n\n\"어디선가 신중함을 익힌 자입니다. 의심이 많다고 해도 좋겠습니다. 카스탈리아 샘에서 다프네 마을로 떠날 때까지, 그의 속내를 끌어내려는 제 온갖 시도를 다 막아 냈습니다.\"\n\n\"가증스러운 곳이로구나! 그가 왜 그리로 갔단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"거기 가는 많은 이들의 첫째 동기인 호기심에서라고 말하겠습니다만, 참으로 이상하게도, 그는 자기가 본 것들에 도무지 흥미를 보이지 않았습니다. 신전에 대해서는 그저 그것이 그리스식인지만 물었습니다. 어지신 주인님, 그 젊은이는 감추고 싶은 마음의 괴로움을 지니고 있습니다. 제 생각에 그가 그 숲으로 간 것은, 우리가 죽은 이를 데리고 무덤으로 가듯—그것을 묻으러 간 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 잘된 일이지.\" 시모니데스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다가, 더 크게 말했다. \"말루크, 이 시대의 저주는 방탕이다. 가난한 자들은 부자를 흉내 내며 더욱 가난해지고, 그저 부유할 뿐인 자들은 군주처럼 행세한다. 그 젊은이에게서 그런 나약함의 기색을 보았느냐? 돈을 내보였더냐—로마 화폐든 이스라엘 화폐든?\"\n\n\"전혀, 전혀 없었습니다, 어지신 주인님.\"\n\n\"하지만 말루크, 어리석음으로 이끄는 유혹이 그토록 많은 곳에서—내 말은, 먹고 마실 것이 그토록 많은 곳에서—그가 분명 네게 무언가 선심을 베풀려 했을 텐데. 다른 건 몰라도 그 나이라면 그쯤은 능히 했을 게다.\"\n\n\"그는 제 앞에서 먹지도 마시지도 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 한 말이나 행동에서, 말루크, 그의 마음을 지배하는 생각을 조금이라도 엿보았느냐? 너도 알다시피, 그런 생각은 바람도 못 새어들 만큼 좁은 틈으로도 새어 나오는 법이다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 알아듣게 해 주십시오.\" 말루크가 미심쩍어하며 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 너도 알다시피, 우리는 어떤 동기에 떠밀리지 않고서는 말하지도 행동하지도 않으며, 더더욱 자신에 관한 중대한 일을 결정하지도 않는다. 그런 점에서, 그를 어떻게 보았느냐?\"\n\n\"그 점이라면, 시모니데스 주인님, 저는 상당한 확신을 가지고 답할 수 있습니다. 그는 어머니와 누이를 찾는 데 골몰해 있습니다—그것이 첫째입니다. 다음으로 그는 로마에 원한을 품고 있습니다. 그리고 제가 말씀드린 그 메살라가 그 부당한 일에 무언가 관여했던 까닭에, 지금의 큰 목표는 그를 욕보이는 것입니다. 샘가에서의 만남이 그 기회를 마련해 주었지만, 그는 그것이 충분히 공개적이지 않다고 미루어 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 메살라는 세력이 있다.\" 시모니데스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그러나 다음 만남은 원형 경기장에서 이루어질 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서—그다음은?\"\n\n\"아리우스의 아들이 이길 것입니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 아느냐?\"\n\n말루크가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그가 하는 말로 미루어 판단합니다.\"\n\n\"그게 전부냐?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 훨씬 더 좋은 징표가 있습니다—그의 기개입니다.\"\n\n\"그래. 그러나 말루크, 그의 복수심—그 범위는 어디까지더냐? 그는 그것을 자기에게 해를 끼친 몇 사람에게로 한정하느냐, 아니면 많은 이들까지 끌어들이느냐? 그리고 한 가지 더—그의 감정은 그저 예민한 소년의 변덕일 뿐이냐, 아니면 그것을 오래 견디게 할 만한, 고통받은 장년의 무게가 배어 있느냐? 알다시피, 말루크, 한갓 머릿속에만 뿌리내린 복수심은 맑은 날 하루면 흩어져 버리는 더없이 부질없는 꿈일 뿐이다. 그러나 정념으로서의 복수는 위로, 위로 뇌까지 기어올라 마음과 뇌 양쪽을 똑같이 파먹는 심장의 병이다.\"\n\n이 물음에서 시모니데스는 처음으로 감정의 기색을 드러냈다. 그는 빠른 말투로, 두 주먹을 움켜쥐고, 자신이 묘사하는 그 병을 몸소 보여 주는 사람처럼 열렬하게 말했다.\n\n\"어지신 주인님.\" 말루크가 대답했다. \"제가 그 젊은이를 유대인이라고 믿는 이유 가운데 하나가 바로 그 증오의 강렬함입니다. 그가 그토록 오래 로마의 시기 어린 분위기 속에서 살아온 것을 생각하면 당연한 일이지만, 그는 스스로를 단속하고 있는 것이 제게도 분명히 보였습니다. 그런데도 그것이 활활 타오르는 것을 보았습니다—한 번은 그가 일데림이 로마에 대해 어떤 감정을 품고 있는지 알고 싶어 했을 때, 또 한 번은 제가 그 족장과 현자의 이야기를 들려주며 '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?'라는 물음을 입에 올렸을 때였습니다.\"\n\n시모니데스가 재빨리 몸을 앞으로 기울였다.\n\n\"아, 말루크, 그의 말을—그가 한 말을 그대로 들려다오. 그 신비가 그에게 어떤 인상을 남겼는지 내가 가늠해 보게.\"\n\n\"그는 정확한 말을 알고 싶어 했습니다. '왕이 되실'이었느냐, 아니면 '왕으로 나신'이었느냐고요. 그 두 표현이 자아내는 효과에 어떤 차이가 있는 듯한 것에 그가 마음이 끌린 듯했습니다.\"\n\n시모니데스는 다시 귀 기울이는 판관의 자세로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 말루크가 말했다. \"저는 그에게 그 신비에 대한 일데림의 견해를 들려주었습니다—그 왕이 로마의 멸망을 가지고 오리라는 것을요. 그러자 그 젊은이의 피가 뺨과 이마 위로 솟구치더니, 그가 간절하게 말했습니다. '로마가 존속하는 한, 헤롯 같은 자가 아니고서야 누가 왕이 될 수 있겠소?'\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"또 다른 통치가 들어서려면 그 제국이 먼저 무너져야 한다는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n시모니데스는 한동안 강 위의 배들과, 그 그림자들이 강물 위에서 천천히 함께 흔들리는 모습을 바라보았다. 그가 눈을 들었을 때는 면담을 끝맺으려는 것이었다.\n\n\"그만하면 됐다, 말루크.\" 그가 말했다. \"가서 요기하고, 종려나무 과수원으로 돌아갈 채비를 하여라. 너는 다가오는 시합에서 그 젊은이를 도와야 한다. 아침에 내게 오너라. 일데림에게 편지를 보내겠다.\" 그러더니 마치 혼잣말처럼 낮은 소리로 덧붙였다. \"내가 직접 경기장에 갈지도 모르겠구나.\"\n\n말루크가 으레 하는 축복의 말을 주고받은 뒤 떠나자, 시모니데스는 우유를 깊이 들이켜고는, 기운이 나고 마음이 편해진 듯했다.\n\n\"상을 내려놓으렴, 에스더.\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 끝났다.\"\n\n그녀가 그대로 따랐다.\n\n\"자, 이리 오너라.\"\n그녀는 그의 곁, 의자 팔걸이 위의 제자리로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"하느님은 내게 선하시다, 참으로 선하시다.\" 그가 뜨겁게 말했다. \"그분은 늘 신비 속에서 움직이시지만, 때로는 우리가 그분을 보고 깨닫는다고 여기도록 허락하신다. 나는 늙었고, 얘야, 떠나야 한다. 그러나 이제, 내 희망이 사그라지기 시작하던 이 마지막 시각에, 그분은 약속을 지닌 이 사람을 내게 보내 주셨고, 나는 다시 일으켜 세워졌다. 나는 그 자체로 너무나 위대하여 온 세상에 새로운 탄생과도 같을 어떤 사태에서 큰 몫을 맡을 길을 본다. 그리고 나는 내 막대한 재물이 주어진 까닭과, 그것이 마련된 목적을 본다. 진실로, 내 아이야, 나는 삶을 새로이 붙드는구나.\"\n\n에스더는 그의 생각을 멀리 날아간 데서 끌어오려는 듯, 그에게 더 바싹 파고들었다.\n\n\"그 왕은 이미 태어났다.\" 그는 여전히 그녀에게 말하고 있다고 여기며 이었다. \"그리고 그는 보통 사람 일생의 절반쯤에 이르렀을 게다. 발타사르는 그를 보았을 때 그가 어머니 무릎에 안긴 아기였으며, 자기가 선물을 바치고 경배했다고 말한다. 그리고 일데림은, 발타사르와 그 동행들이 헤롯을 피해 숨을 곳을 청하며 자기 천막으로 찾아온 것이 지난 십이월로 스물일곱 해 전이었다고 한다. 그러니 그분의 오심은 이제 오래 미루어질 수 없다. 오늘 밤일 수도—내일일 수도 있다. 이스라엘의 거룩한 조상들이여, 그 생각만으로도 얼마나 큰 행복인가! 나는 낡은 성벽이 무너지는 굉음과, 온 세상이 뒤바뀌는 함성을 듣는 듯하다—그래, 그리고 사람들의 더없는 기쁨을 위하여, 땅이 입을 벌려 로마를 삼키고, 사람들이 우러러보며 웃고 노래하리니, 로마는 없어졌으나 우리는 살아 있다고.\" 그러더니 그는 자신을 두고 웃었다. \"이것 봐라, 에스더, 너 이런 말을 들어 본 적 있느냐? 분명 내게 가인(歌人)의 정념이, 미리암과 다윗의 뜨거운 피와 전율이 깃들어 있구나. 숫자와 사실을 다루는 한낱 일꾼의 생각이어야 마땅한 내 머릿속에, 심벌즈가 맞부딪치고 하프 줄이 요란하게 튕겨지며, 새로 일어선 왕좌를 에워싼 군중의 목소리가 뒤섞여 들끓는구나. 그 생각은 당분간 접어 두마. 다만, 얘야, 그 왕이 오시면 그분께는 돈과 사람이 필요하리니, 그분은 여인에게서 난 아이였던 만큼 결국은 한 사람일 뿐이요, 너나 나처럼 사람의 길에 매여 있을 것이기 때문이다. 그리고 돈을 위해서는 그것을 벌고 지키는 자들이, 사람을 위해서는 그들을 이끄는 자들이 필요하실 게다. 자, 자! 너는 내가 걸어갈 너른 길과, 우리 주인인 그 젊은이가 달려갈 길이 보이지 않느냐?—그리고 그 끝에 우리 둘 모두를 위한 영광과 복수가 있지 않느냐?—그리고—그리고\"—그는 그녀에게는 아무 몫도, 아무 좋은 결과도 없는 계획의 이기심에 문득 마음이 찔려 말을 멈추었다. 그러더니 그녀에게 입 맞추며 덧붙였다. \"그리고 네 어머니의 아이를 위한 행복이 있지 않느냐.\"\n\n그녀는 아무 말 없이 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그제야 그는 천성의 차이와, 우리가 늘 같은 일로 똑같이 기뻐하거나 같은 것을 똑같이 두려워하도록 허락되지는 않는다는 법칙을 떠올렸다. 그는 그녀가 한낱 소녀에 지나지 않음을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"무슨 생각을 하고 있느냐, 에스더?\" 그가 평소의 다정한 집안 말투로 말했다. \"그 생각이 바람의 모양을 하고 있거든, 내게 힘이 남아 있는 동안 그것을 내게 다오, 우리 아가. 알다시피 힘이란 안달하는 것이어서, 늘 날아갈 채비로 날개를 펴고 있는 법이니까.\"\n\n그녀는 거의 어린아이처럼 꾸밈없이 대답했다.\n\n\"그를 부르세요, 아버지. 오늘 밤 그를 부르셔서, 경기장에 들어가지 못하게 하세요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 그 감탄사를 길게 늘이며 말했다. 그러고는 다시 그의 눈이 강 위로 떨어졌다. 달이 술피우스 산 너머로 멀리 가라앉아, 도시를 무력한 별들에게 내맡긴 까닭에, 그곳의 그림자는 그 어느 때보다 어슴푸레했다. 독자여, 이렇게 말해도 될까? 그는 한 가닥 질투의 아픔에 사로잡혔다. 만일 그녀가 정말로 그 젊은 주인을 사랑한다면! 오, 아니다! 그럴 리 없다. 그녀는 너무 어리다. 그러나 그 생각은 단단히 움켜쥐고 놓지 않더니, 곧장 그를 가만히, 차갑게 만들었다. 그녀는 열여섯 살이었다. 그는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다. 지난 생일에 그는 그녀와 함께 진수식이 있는 조선소에 갔는데, 그 갤리선이 파도와의 혼례를 위해 내건 노란 깃발에는 \"에스더\"라고 적혀 있었다. 그렇게 두 사람은 그날을 함께 기렸다. 그런데도 그 사실이 이제 와 놀라움의 충격으로 그를 쳤다. 우리 모두에게는 고통스럽게 다가오는 깨달음이 있다. 그러나 대개는 우리 자신에 관한 것들이다. 이를테면 우리가 늙어 간다는 것, 그리고 더욱 끔찍하게는, 우리가 죽어야 한다는 것이다. 그런 깨달음 하나가, 그림자처럼 어슴푸레하면서도 거의 신음에 가까운 한숨을 그에게서 쥐어짜낼 만큼은 또렷하게, 그의 가슴속으로 스며들었다. 그녀가 종의 신세로 젊은 여인의 시절에 들어서는 것만으로도 모자라, 그녀는 그 주인에게 자신의 애정마저 바쳐야 했으니, 그 애정의 진실함과 다정함과 섬세함을 아비인 그는 너무도 잘 알고 있었다. 그때까지 그것이 온전히 그만의 것이었기 때문이다. 두려움과 쓰라린 생각으로 우리를 괴롭히는 것이 임무인 그 악귀는 좀처럼 제 일을 어중간하게 하는 법이 없다. 그 순간의 아픔 속에서, 용감한 노인은 자신의 새로운 계획도, 그 주인공인 기적의 왕도 시야에서 놓쳐 버렸다. 그러나 그는 안간힘을 다하여 스스로를 다스리고는, 차분하게 물었다. \"경기장에 들어가지 말라고, 에스더? 왜냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"거기는 이스라엘의 아들이 갈 곳이 아니에요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"율법학자 같구나, 율법학자 같아, 에스더! 그게 전부냐?\"\n\n그 물음의 어조는 속을 캐는 것이어서, 그녀의 가슴에 가닿았다. 그녀의 가슴이 요란하게 뛰기 시작했다—너무도 요란하여 그녀는 대답할 수가 없었다. 새롭고 기이하게도 달콤한 혼란이 그녀에게 내려앉았다.\n\n\"그 재산은 그 젊은이에게 돌아갈 게다.\" 그가 그녀의 손을 잡고 한층 다정하게 말했다. \"배도, 은화도 그에게 돌아갈 게다—전부, 에스더, 전부 다. 그런데도 나는 가난하다고 느끼지 않았다. 네가 내게 남겨졌고, 죽은 라헬을 그토록 닮은 네 사랑이 있었기 때문이다. 말해 다오, 그것마저 그에게 돌아가는 것이냐?\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 몸을 숙여, 그의 머리에 제 뺨을 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"말하여라, 에스더. 알면 나는 더 강해질 게다. 미리 알면 거기에 힘이 있는 법이다.\"\n\n그러자 그녀는 몸을 일으켜, 마치 자신이 진리의 거룩한 화신이기라도 한 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"마음 놓으세요, 아버지. 저는 결코 아버지를 떠나지 않을 거예요. 그가 제 사랑을 가져간다 해도, 저는 지금처럼 영원히 아버지의 여종이겠어요.\"\n\n그러고는 몸을 숙여 그에게 입 맞추었다.\n\n\"그리고 한 가지 더 말씀드릴게요.\" 그녀가 이어 말했다. \"그는 제 눈에 잘생겨 보이고, 그 호소하는 목소리가 저를 그에게로 끌어당겼어요. 그래서 저는 그가 위험에 처할 것을 생각하면 몸서리가 쳐져요. 네, 아버지, 저는 그를 다시 보면 더없이 기쁠 거예요. 그렇지만 보답받지 못하는 사랑은 온전한 사랑일 수 없으니, 저는 제가 아버지의 딸이요 어머니의 딸임을 잊지 않고, 한동안 기다리겠어요.\"\n\n\"너는 참으로 주님의 축복이로구나, 에스더! 다른 모든 것을 잃어도 나를 부유하게 지켜 줄 축복이다. 그분의 거룩한 이름과 영원한 생명을 두고 맹세하노니, 너는 결코 고통받지 않을 게다.\"\n\n조금 뒤 그의 청에 따라 하인이 와서 의자를 방 안으로 밀어 넣었다. 그곳에서 그는 한동안 그 왕의 오심을 생각하며 앉아 있었고, 그녀는 자리를 떠나 순결한 자의 잠을 잤다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\n강 건너편, 시모니데스의 집과 거의 마주 보는 자리에 선 그 궁전은 이름난 에피파네스가 완공했다고 전해지며, 그런 거처에 대해 상상할 수 있는 모든 것을 갖춘 곳이었다. 다만 그는 이른바 고전적인 것보다 거대한 것을 좋아하는 취향의 건축가였다—다시 말해 그리스인이 아니라 페르시아인을 모방한 건축가였던 것이다.\n\n섬 전체를 물가까지 에워싼 성벽은 강물을 막는 보루이자 폭도를 막는 방벽이라는 이중의 목적으로 세워졌는데, 바로 그 때문에 이 궁전이 늘 거처하기에는 적합하지 못하다는 말이 있었다. 그래서 총독들은 이곳을 버리고, 유피테르 신전 아래 술피우스 산의 서쪽 능선에 그들을 위해 지어진 다른 관저로 옮겨 갔다. 그러나 이 오래된 거처를 향한 그 비난을 딱 잘라 부정하는 사람도 적지 않았다. 적어도 그들은 약삭빠르게도, 총독들이 옮겨 간 진짜 이유는 더 쾌적한 자리를 찾아서가 아니라, 그 산 동쪽 능선의 바로 맞은편에 자리 잡은, 당시의 일반적인 명칭에 따라 성채라 불린 거대한 병영이 그들에게 안전을 보장해 주었기 때문이라고 말했다. 이 견해는 그럴듯한 근거가 있었다. 다른 적절한 정황 가운데서도, 이 궁전이 언제든 쓸 수 있도록 늘 채비되어 있다는 점이 거론되었다. 집정관이든 군 총사령관이든 왕이든, 또는 어떤 부류의 방문하는 권력자든 안티오크에 도착하면, 곧장 섬에 거처가 배정되었던 것이다.\n\n우리가 이 오래된 건물에서 다룰 곳은 단 하나의 방뿐이니, 나머지는 독자의 상상에 맡긴다. 독자는 마음 내키는 대로 그 정원과 욕탕과 회랑과 미궁 같은 방들을 거쳐 지붕 위의 정자까지 거닐어도 좋다. 그 모든 것은 밀턴이 말한 \"화려한 동방\"에 세상 어느 곳보다 가까운 도시에서, 명성 있는 저택에 어울리게 꾸며져 있었으니 말이다.\n\n오늘날이라면 우리가 이야기하려는 그 방을 응접실이라 부를 것이다. 꽤 넓은 방으로, 윤이 나는 대리석 판으로 바닥이 깔려 있었고, 낮에는 채색 운모를 유리 대신 끼운 천창으로 빛이 들었다. 벽면은 아틀라스 상으로 군데군데 끊겼는데, 어느 둘도 같은 것이 없었으나 모두가 처마 장식을 떠받치고 있었으며, 그 장식은 형태가 더없이 정교한 아라베스크 무늬로 짜여, 푸른색, 초록색, 티레의 자주색, 금색으로 색을 덧입혀 한층 우아했다. 방을 빙 둘러 인도산 비단과 캐시미어 양털로 만든 긴 의자가 끊임없이 이어졌다. 가구는 기괴하게 새겨진 이집트풍의 탁자와 걸상으로 이루어져 있었다. 우리는 시모니데스를 그의 의자에 남겨 두고 왔다. 그는 그 기적의 왕—그가 이미 임박했다고 단정한 그 왕—을 돕기 위한 계획을 다듬고 있었다. 에스더는 잠들어 있다. 이제 다리를 건너 강을 넘고, 사자가 지키는 문과 여러 바빌론풍 회랑과 안뜰을 지났으니, 그 황금빛 응접실로 들어가 보자.\n\n천장에서 미끄러지는 청동 사슬에 매달린 샹들리에가 다섯 개 있었으니—네 귀퉁이에 하나씩, 그리고 한가운데 하나—불을 밝힌 등잔의 거대한 피라미드가 아틀라스 상들의 악귀 같은 얼굴과 처마 장식의 복잡한 무늬마저 환히 비추었다. 탁자 둘레에는 앉거나 선 채로, 또는 이 탁자에서 저 탁자로 쉴 새 없이 옮겨 다니는 사람이 백 명쯤은 되었으니, 우리는 적어도 잠깐이나마 이들을 살펴보아야 한다.\n\n그들은 모두 젊었고, 더러는 소년이나 다름없었다. 그들이 이탈리아인이며 대부분 로마인이라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 모두가 순수한 라틴어를 썼고, 저마다 티베르 강가의 그 위대한 수도에서 입는 실내복 차림이었다. 곧 소매와 자락이 짧은 튜닉이었으니, 안티오크의 기후에 잘 어울리는 옷차림이었고, 특히 응접실의 너무 답답한 공기 속에서는 한결 편안했다. 긴 의자 여기저기에는 토가와 라케르나가 아무렇게나 던져진 채 놓여 있었으니, 더러는 자주색으로 의미심장하게 단을 두른 것이었다. 또한 긴 의자에는 편안히 몸을 뻗고 잠든 자들도 있었다. 그들이 무더운 날의 더위와 피로에 쓰러진 것인지, 아니면 바쿠스에게 쓰러진 것인지는 굳이 따지지 않겠다.\n\n말소리의 웅성거림이 요란하고 끊임없었다. 때로는 웃음이 터지고, 때로는 분노나 환희가 폭발했다. 그러나 그 모든 것 위로 날카롭고 길게 이어지는 달그락 소리가 우세하게 깔려 있었으니, 익숙지 않은 사람에게는 처음엔 다소 어리둥절한 소리였다. 그러나 우리가 탁자에 다가가면 그 수수께끼는 절로 풀린다. 사람들은 즐겨 하는 놀이, 곧 장기와 주사위 놀이에 빠져 있었으니, 따로 하기도 하고 함께 하기도 했다. 그 달그락 소리는 다만 테세라, 곧 상아 주사위를 요란하게 흔드는 소리와, 바둑판 같은 판 위에서 말을 옮기는 소리였던 것이다.\n\n이 무리는 누구인가?\n\n\"이보게 플라비우스,\" 한 사람이 말을 든 채 움직임을 멈추고 말했다. \"저기 저 라케르나가 보이나. 우리 앞 긴 의자에 놓인 저것 말일세. 갓 가게에서 나온 새것인데, 어깨에 단 황금 버클이 손바닥만큼이나 넓다네.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 플라비우스가 자기 놀이에 골몰한 채 말했다. \"나도 전에 그런 걸 본 적이 있지. 그러니 자네 것이 오래된 건 아닐 테지만, 베누스의 허리띠를 두고 말하건대, 새것도 아니야! 그래서 어쩌자는 건가?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아닐세. 다만 모든 걸 다 아는 사람을 찾아낼 수 있다면 저걸 주겠다는 거지.\"\n\n\"하하! 그보다 싼값으로, 자네 제안을 받아들일 자주색 단을 두른 자들을 여기서 여럿 찾아 주겠네. 어서 두기나 하게.\"\n\n\"자—장군이야!\"\n\n\"이런, 온 유피테르 신을 두고! 자, 어떤가? 한 판 더?\"\n\n\"그러세.\"\n\n\"내기는?\"\n\n\"세스테르티움 하나.\"\n\n그러자 둘은 저마다 서판과 첨필을 꺼내 기록했다. 말을 다시 늘어놓는 동안, 플라비우스가 친구의 말로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"모든 걸 다 아는 사람이라! 헤르쿨레스를 두고! 그런 자가 있다면 신탁들도 죽어 버리겠군. 그런 괴물을 데려다 뭘 하려고?\"\n\n\"한 가지 물음에 답을 듣자는 걸세, 플라비우스. 그러고 나선, 맹세코! 그놈 멱을 따 버려야지.\"\n\n\"그 물음이란?\"\n\n\"막센티우스가 내일 도착할 그 시각을—시각이라 했나?—아니, 그 분(分)까지 일러 달라 하겠네.\"\n\n\"잘 두는군, 잘 둬! 자넬 잡았네! 그런데 왜 분까지 알려는 건가?\"\n\n\"자넨 그가 상륙할 그 부두에서 시리아의 햇볕 아래 맨머리로 서 본 적이 있나? 베스타의 불도 그처럼 뜨겁진 않을 걸세. 우리 선조 로물루스의 수호신을 두고 말하건대, 죽어야 한다면 나는 로마에서 죽겠네. 여기가 바로 아베르누스야. 로마 광장의 포룸 앞 그 자리에 서서, 이렇게 손을 들면 신들의 마루를 만질 수도 있었지. 아, 베누스를 두고, 플라비우스, 자네 날 속였군! 졌네. 오, 포르투나여!\"\n\n\"한 판 더?\"\n\n\"내 세스테르티움을 도로 찾아야겠네.\"\n\n\"그러세.\"\n\n그렇게 그들은 거듭거듭 놀이를 했다. 천창으로 새어 든 햇빛이 등잔불을 흐리기 시작했을 때에도, 두 사람은 같은 탁자 같은 자리에서 여전히 놀이에 빠져 있었다. 무리 가운데 대부분이 그러하듯, 그들도 집정관의 군 막료로서 그의 도착을 기다리며 그동안 이렇게 시간을 보내고 있었던 것이다.\n\n이 대화가 오가는 동안 한 무리가 방에 들어와, 처음엔 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않은 채 한가운데 탁자로 나아갔다. 정황으로 보아 그들은 방금 파한 연회에서 온 듯했다. 더러는 발을 가누기조차 힘들어했다. 그 무리를 이끄는 자의 이마에는 화관이 둘려 있었으니, 그가 연회를 베푼 자는 아니라 해도 연회의 좌장임을 나타냈다. 술은 그에게 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않았으니, 도리어 그의 아름다움을 한층 돋우었을 뿐이었다. 그것은 더없이 사내다운 로마풍의 아름다움이었다. 그는 고개를 높이 쳐들고 있었고, 입술과 뺨에는 피가 환하게 올라 있었으며, 두 눈은 빛났다. 다만 티 하나 없이 새하얗고 자락이 넉넉한 토가를 두른 채 걷는 그 몸가짐은, 술에 취하지도 않고 카이사르도 아닌 자치고는 지나치게 황제다웠다. 탁자로 가면서 그는 거의 격식도 사과도 없이 자기와 추종자들을 위해 자리를 텄다. 마침내 멈춰 서서 탁자 너머 놀이하는 자들을 둘러보자, 그들은 모두 그를 향해 환호 같은 함성을 질렀다.\n\n\"메살라! 메살라!\" 그들이 외쳤다.\n\n멀리 떨어진 자리에 있던 자들도 그 외침을 듣고는 제자리에서 그 소리를 되받아 메아리쳤다. 순식간에 무리가 흩어지고 놀이가 중단되었으며, 모두가 한가운데로 우르르 몰려들었다.\n\n메살라는 이 소동을 무심하게 받아들이고는, 이내 자기 인기의 근원을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"자네의 건강을 위하여, 드루수스, 내 친구여,\" 그가 바로 오른편에서 놀이하던 자에게 말했다. \"건강을 위하여—그리고 자네 서판을 잠깐 빌리세.\"\n\n그는 밀랍 판을 들어 올려 내기 기록을 흘끗 보고는 도로 내던졌다.\n\n\"데나리우스, 고작 데나리우스—짐꾼과 푸줏간 놈들이나 쓰는 돈이로군!\" 그가 비웃으며 말했다. \"취한 세멜레를 두고, 로마가 대체 어찌 되려고 이러는가. 카이사르쯤 되는 자가 밤새 앉아 고작 거지 같은 데나리우스 한 닢을 가져다줄 운수의 변덕이나 기다리고 있다니!\"\n\n드루수스 가문의 자손은 이마까지 벌게졌으나, 그가 미처 대꾸하기도 전에 둘러선 자들이 탁자 둘레로 더 바짝 밀려들며 끼어들어 외쳤다. \"메살라다! 메살라야!\"\n\n\"티베르의 사내들이여,\" 메살라가 가까이 있던 누군가의 손에서 주사위가 든 통을 빼앗으며 말을 이었다. \"신들의 사랑을 가장 많이 받는 자가 누구인가? 로마인이다. 만방에 법을 내리는 자가 누구인가? 로마인이다. 칼의 권리로 만인의 주인이 된 자는 누구인가?\"\n\n이 무리는 쉽게 흥분하는 자들이었고, 그 생각은 그들이 날 때부터 품어 온 것이었다. 순식간에 그들은 그의 입에서 답을 채어 갔다.\n\n\"로마인이다, 로마인이야!\" 그들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"하지만—하지만\"—그는 그들의 귀를 사로잡으려 뜸을 들였다—\"하지만 로마 최고의 자보다 더 나은 자가 있다.\"\n\n그는 귀족다운 머리를 젖히고 멈추었으니, 마치 그 비웃음으로 그들을 쏘아붙이려는 듯했다.\n\n\"들리는가?\" 그가 물었다. \"로마 최고의 자보다 더 나은 자가 있다.\"\n\n\"오라—헤르쿨레스다!\" 한 사람이 외쳤다.\n\n\"바쿠스다!\" 한 익살꾼이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"유피테르—유피테르다!\" 무리가 우레처럼 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니,\" 메살라가 답했다. \"사람들 가운데서 말이다.\"\n\n\"이름을 대라, 이름을 대!\" 그들이 다그쳤다.\n\n\"대지,\" 그가 다음 잠잠한 틈에 말했다. \"로마의 완벽함에 동방의 완벽함을 더한 자, 서방의 것인 정복의 팔에다 동방의 것인 지배를 누리는 데 필요한 기술까지 갖춘 자다.\"\n\n\"맹세코! 결국 그 최고의 자도 로마인이로군.\" 누군가가 외쳤다. 그러자 큰 웃음이 터지고 손뼉 소리가 길게 이어졌으니, 메살라가 한 수 위라는 것을 인정한 것이었다.\n\n\"동방에는,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"우리에게 신이 없다. 오직 술과 여자와 운수뿐이며, 그 가운데 가장 위대한 것이 운수다. 그래서 우리의 좌우명이 '누가 감히 내가 하는 일을 하겠는가?'라네. 원로원에도 어울리고, 전쟁에도 어울리며, 최고를 좇으면서 최악에 도전하는 자에게 가장 어울리는 말이지.\"\n\n그의 목소리가 편안하고 친근한 어조로 낮아졌으나, 그가 얻은 우위는 조금도 누그러지지 않았다.\n\n\"성채 위 그 큰 궤짝에 시장에서 통용되는 돈으로 다섯 탈렌트가 들어 있고, 여기 그 영수증이 있다.\"\n\n그는 튜닉에서 종이 두루마리를 꺼내 탁자 위에 던지고는, 숨죽인 침묵 속에서 말을 이었으니, 모든 눈이 그에게 쏠렸고 모든 귀가 그에게 기울었다.\n\n\"저기 놓인 그 액수가 바로 내가 감히 거는 만큼의 척도다. 너희 가운데 누가 그만큼 감히 걸겠는가! 잠잠하구나. 너무 큰가? 그럼 한 탈렌트를 깎지. 뭐라고! 그래도 잠잠한가? 자, 그럼 이 세 탈렌트를 두고 한 번 던져 보아라—고작 셋이다. 둘이라도, 하나라도—적어도 하나라도—너희가 태어난 그 강의 명예를 위해 하나라도—동방의 로마가 서방의 로마에 맞서서! 야만의 오론테스가 신성한 티베르에 맞서서!\"\n\n그는 기다리는 동안 머리 위로 주사위를 달그락거렸다.\n\n\"오론테스가 티베르에 맞서서!\" 그가 비웃음을 한층 더해 거듭 외쳤다.\n\n아무도 움직이지 않았다. 그러자 그는 통을 탁자 위에 내던지고 웃으며 영수증을 도로 집어 들었다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하! 올림포스의 유피테르를 두고, 이제 알겠다. 너희가 재산을 모으거나 메우러 온 자들이라는 걸. 그래서 안티오크에 온 게로군. 호, 케킬리우스!\"\n\n\"여기 있네, 메살라!\" 그의 뒤에서 한 사람이 외쳤다. \"여기 있네. 군중 틈에서 죽을 지경이라 누더기 입은 뱃사공에게 치를 드라크마 한 닢을 구걸하던 참이지. 한데, 플루토가 날 잡아가도 좋아! 이 새내기들은 오볼루스 한 닢도 가진 게 없다네.\"\n\n이 익살에 웃음이 터져, 응접실이 울리고 또 울렸다. 오직 메살라만이 진지함을 지켰다.\n\n\"자네는 가게,\" 그가 케킬리우스에게 말했다. \"우리가 온 그 방으로 가서, 하인들더러 암포라들을, 잔과 술잔들을 이리로 가져오라 이르게. 재산을 찾아 나선 우리 동포들에게 지갑이 없다면, 시리아의 바쿠스를 두고, 위장이라도 좀 더 잘 갖추었는지 어디 보세! 어서 가게!\"\n\n그러고는 드루수스에게 돌아서며, 방 안에 다 들리도록 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"하, 하, 내 친구여! 자네 안의 카이사르를 데나리우스 수준으로 끌어내렸다 해서 노여워 말게. 보다시피 나는 다만 그 이름을 빌려 우리 오랜 로마의 이 곱상한 풋내기들을 시험해 본 것뿐이라네. 자, 내 드루수스여, 자!\" 그는 다시 통을 집어 들어 흥겹게 주사위를 달그락거렸다. \"자, 자네가 원하는 액수만큼, 우리 운수를 한번 겨뤄 보세.\"\n\n그 태도는 솔직하고 정겹고 사람을 사로잡았다. 드루수스는 이내 마음이 누그러졌다.\n\n\"님프들을 두고, 좋아!\" 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"자네와 던져 보겠네, 메살라—데나리우스 한 닢을 걸고.\"\n\n매우 앳된 자가 탁자 너머로 그 광경을 지켜보고 있었다. 별안간 메살라가 그를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"너는 누구냐?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n소년은 움찔 물러섰다.\n\n\"아니, 카스토르를 두고! 또 그의 형제까지 두고! 기분 상하게 하려던 게 아니다. 사내들 사이에는, 주사위 말고 다른 일에서도, 거래가 작을수록 기록을 더 꼼꼼히 챙기는 법이라네. 내겐 서기가 필요하다. 나를 위해 일해 주겠느냐?\"\n\n젊은이는 점수를 적으려고 서판을 꺼내 들었으니, 그 태도는 거부할 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n\"잠깐, 메살라, 잠깐!\" 드루수스가 외쳤다. \"겨눈 주사위를 물음으로 멈추는 게 불길한 일인지는 모르겠으나, 한 가지 떠오르는 게 있어서, 베누스가 그 허리띠로 날 후려치더라도 묻지 않을 수 없네.\"\n\n\"아니, 내 드루수스여, 허리띠를 푼 베누스는 사랑에 빠진 베누스라네. 자네 물음에 답하지—나는 던지되 변고에 대비해 그대로 눌러 두겠네. 이렇게—\"\n\n그는 통을 탁자 위에 뒤집고는 주사위 위에 단단히 눌러 잡았다.\n\n그러자 드루수스가 물었다. \"퀸투스 아리우스라는 자를 본 적 있나?\"\n\n\"그 집정관 말인가?\"\n\n\"그에게 아들이 있는 줄은 몰랐네.\"\n\n\"그에게 아들이 있는 줄은 몰랐는데.\"\n\"뭐, 별것 아니네,\" 드루수스가 무심하게 덧붙였다. \"다만, 내 메살라여, 폴룩스가 카스토르를 닮은 것보다 그 아리우스가 자넬 더 닮았다는 것뿐이지.\"\n\n이 말은 신호 같은 효과를 냈으니, 스무 명의 목소리가 그것을 받아 외쳤다.\n\n\"맞아, 맞아! 그 눈매며—그 얼굴이며.\" 그들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 소리!\" 한 사람이 못마땅한 듯 답했다. \"메살라는 로마인이고, 아리우스는 유대인이야.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 옳아,\" 또 한 사람이 외쳤다. \"그자는 유대인이지. 아니면 모무스가 그 어미에게 엉뚱한 가면을 빌려 준 게로군.\"\n\n다툼이 벌어질 낌새가 보이자, 그것을 알아챈 메살라가 끼어들었다. \"술이 아직 오지 않았네, 내 드루수스여. 그리고 보다시피 나는 이 주근깨투성이 피티아스들을 끈에 묶인 개처럼 붙들고 있지. 아리우스에 대해서는, 그에 관해 더 일러 주기만 하면 자네 의견을 받아들이겠네.\"\n\n\"음, 유대인이든 로마인이든—그리고 위대한 신 판을 두고, 자네 기분을 상하게 하려고 하는 말이 아닐세, 내 메살라여!—이 아리우스란 자는 잘생기고 용감하고 영리하네. 황제가 총애와 후원을 베풀려 했으나 그는 마다했지. 그는 알 수 없는 내력을 거쳐 떠올랐고, 마치 자신이 우리보다 낫다고 느끼거나 자신이 우리보다 못하다는 걸 아는 듯 거리를 두고 지낸다네. 격투장에서는 당할 자가 없었지. 라인강에서 온 푸른 눈의 거인들과 사르마티아의 뿔 없는 황소 같은 자들을 마치 버들가지 다루듯 가지고 놀았어. 집정관이 그에게 막대한 재산을 남겼지. 그는 무예에 열정을 품고 있어 오직 전쟁만 생각한다네. 막센티우스가 그를 자기 식솔로 받아들였고, 그는 우리와 함께 배를 타기로 되어 있었으나, 우리는 라벤나에서 그를 놓쳤지. 그래도 그는 무사히 도착했네. 오늘 아침 그의 소식을 들었거든. 맹세코! 그런데 궁전으로 오거나 성채로 가는 대신, 그는 짐을 여관에 부려 놓고 다시 종적을 감췄다네.\"\n\n말이 시작될 무렵 메살라는 점잖은 무심함으로 듣고 있었다. 말이 이어질수록 그는 더 귀를 기울였고, 끝에 이르자 주사위 통에서 손을 떼고 외쳤다. \"호, 내 가이우스! 듣고 있나?\"\n\n그의 곁에 있던 한 젊은이—그날의 전차 연습에서 그의 미르틸루스, 곧 동료였던 자가—그 관심에 무척 기뻐하며 답했다. \"안 듣고 있었다면, 내 메살라여, 내가 자네 친구가 아니겠지.\"\n\n\"오늘 자넬 넘어뜨린 그 사내를 기억하나?\"\n\n\"바쿠스의 머리타래를 두고, 그걸 잊지 않게 도와줄 멍든 어깨가 여기 있지 않은가?\" 그러더니 그는 두 귀가 파묻힐 만큼 어깨를 으쓱하며 그 말에 힘을 보탰다.\n\n\"음, 운명의 여신들에게 감사하게나—내가 자네의 원수를 찾아냈으니. 들어 보게.\"\n\n그러고서 메살라는 드루수스에게 돌아섰다.\n\n\"그에 대해 더 일러 주게—맹세코!—유대인이면서 로마인이기도 한 그자 말일세—포이보스를 두고, 켄타우로스를 사랑스럽게 만들 만한 조합이로군! 그자는 어떤 옷을 즐겨 입나, 내 드루수스여?\"\n\n\"유대인의 옷일세.\"\n\n\"듣고 있나, 가이우스?\" 메살라가 말했다. \"그자는 젊다—하나. 로마인의 얼굴을 지녔다—둘. 유대인의 옷차림을 가장 좋아한다—셋. 격투장에서는 말을 넘어뜨리거나, 필요하면 전차를 부리는 무예로 명성과 재물을 얻는다—넷. 그리고 드루수스, 내 친구를 다시 한번 도와주게. 분명 이 아리우스는 말솜씨에 재주가 있을 거야. 그렇지 않고서야 어떻게 오늘은 유대인, 내일은 로마인으로 자기를 그토록 뒤섞을 수 있겠나. 한데 아테나의 풍부한 언어는—그것도 능란하게 구사하나?\"\n\n\"어찌나 순수한지, 메살라, 이스트미아 경기에 출전해도 손색이 없을 정도라네.\"\n\n\"듣고 있나, 가이우스?\" 메살라가 말했다. \"그자는 그리스어로 여인에게—심지어 아리스토마케 같은 여인에게도—인사를 건넬 자격이 있단 말이지. 내 셈으로는 그게 다섯이다. 어떤가?\"\n\n\"자네가 그자를 찾아냈네, 내 메살라여,\" 가이우스가 답했다. \"아니면 내가 내가 아닐세.\"\n\n\"용서하게, 드루수스—그리고 모두 용서하게—이렇게 수수께끼처럼 말한 것을,\" 메살라가 사람을 사로잡는 그 투로 말했다. \"점잖은 모든 신을 두고, 자네 호의를 끊어질 지경까지 잡아당기고 싶지는 않으나, 이제 나를 도와주게. 보게!\"—그는 다시 주사위 통에 손을 얹고 웃었다—\"보게, 내가 이 피티아스들과 그 비밀을 얼마나 단단히 붙들고 있는지! 자네는 아까 아리우스의 아들이 온 일과 관련해 알 수 없는 내력 운운했지. 그것에 대해 일러 주게.\"\n\n\"별것 아닐세, 메살라, 별것 아니야,\" 드루수스가 답했다. \"어린애 이야기 같은 것이지. 아비 아리우스가 해적을 쫓아 출항했을 때 그에게는 아내도 식솔도 없었네. 그런데 한 소년을 데리고 돌아왔으니—바로 우리가 말하는 그자였지—그러고는 이튿날 그를 양자로 들였다네.\"\n\n\"양자로 들였다고?\" 메살라가 되뇌었다. \"신들을 두고, 드루수스, 정말로 흥미롭군! 그 집정관이 어디서 그 소년을 찾아냈나? 또 그가 누구였나?\"\n\n\"그걸 누가 답해 줄 수 있겠나, 메살라? 젊은 아리우스 자신 말고는 아무도 못 하지. 맹세코! 그 싸움에서 집정관은—그때는 호민관에 불과했지만—자기 갤리선을 잃었네. 돌아오던 배가 그와 또 한 사람을—살아남은 선원이라곤 그 둘뿐이었지—같은 널빤지에 떠 있는 채로 건져 냈다네. 이제 자네에게 구조한 자들의 이야기를 들려주지. 이 이야기는 적어도 한 가지 장점이 있으니—한 번도 반박된 적이 없다는 점이야. 그들 말로는, 널빤지 위에 있던 집정관의 동행이 유대인이었다네—\"\n\n\"유대인이라고!\" 메살라가 되받았다.\n\n\"게다가 노예였지.\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 드루수스? 노예라고?\"\n\n\"두 사람을 갑판으로 끌어올렸을 때, 집정관은 호민관의 갑옷을 입고 있었고, 다른 자는 노 젓는 자의 옷차림이었다네.\"\n\n메살라는 탁자에 기대고 있던 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"갤리선이라\"—그는 그 천박한 말을 멈추고 둘레를 둘러보았으니, 평생 처음으로 어쩔 줄 모르는 모습이었다. 바로 그때 노예들의 행렬이 줄지어 방으로 들어왔다. 더러는 큰 술 항아리를 들고, 더러는 과일과 과자가 담긴 바구니를 들고, 또 더러는 거의가 은으로 된 잔과 병을 들고 있었다. 그 광경에 영감이 깃들어 있었다. 메살라는 즉시 걸상 위로 올라섰다.\n\n\"티베르의 사내들이여,\" 그가 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. \"우리 총사령관을 기다리는 이 시간을 바쿠스의 잔치로 바꾸자. 누구를 좌장으로 뽑겠는가?\"\n\n드루수스가 일어섰다.\n\n\"잔치를 베푼 자 말고 누가 좌장이 되겠는가?\" 그가 말했다. \"답하라, 로마인들이여.\"\n\n그들은 함성으로 답했다.\n\n메살라는 자기 머리에서 화관을 벗어 드루수스에게 건넸고, 드루수스는 탁자 위로 올라가 모두가 보는 앞에서 엄숙하게 그것을 도로 씌워, 메살라를 그 밤의 좌장으로 삼았다.\n\n\"나와 함께 이 방에 들어온 자들 가운데,\" 그가 말했다. \"방금 식탁에서 일어난 친구들이 있다. 우리 잔치가 신성한 관습의 인정을 받도록, 그들 가운데 술에 가장 취한 자를 이리로 데려오라.\"\n\n여러 목소리가 떠들썩하게 답했다. \"여기 있다, 여기 있어!\"\n\n그러고는 쓰러져 있던 바닥에서 한 젊은이가 끌려 나왔으니, 어찌나 여인처럼 곱게 아름다운지 술의 신 그 자체로 보아도 될 정도였다—다만 머리에서는 관이 떨어지고, 손에서는 지팡이가 떨어질 뿐이었다.\n\n\"그를 탁자 위에 올려라,\" 좌장이 말했다.\n\n그가 앉아 있지조차 못한다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"그를 부축하게, 드루수스. 아름다운 니오네가 언젠가 자네를 부축해 줄 것처럼.\"\n\n드루수스는 그 취한 자를 두 팔로 안았다.\n\n그러자 메살라는 그 축 늘어진 형상을 향해, 깊은 침묵 속에서 말했다. \"오, 바쿠스여! 신들 가운데 가장 위대한 분이시여, 오늘 밤 우리에게 은혜를 베푸소서. 그리고 저 자신과, 당신의 이 추종자들을 위하여, 이 화관을 바치나이다\"—그러고는 머리에서 화관을 경건하게 들어 올렸다—\"이 화관을 다프네 숲의 당신 제단에 바치기로 맹세하나이다.\"\n\n그는 머리를 숙이고 화관을 다시 머리타래에 얹은 뒤, 몸을 굽혀 주사위를 드러내며 웃으며 말했다. \"보게, 내 드루수스여, 실레노스의 나귀를 두고, 데나리우스는 내 것일세!\"\n\n바닥을 흔들고 험상궂은 아틀라스 상들을 춤추게 할 만한 함성이 터졌고, 그렇게 광란의 향연이 시작되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\n일더림 족장은 하찮은 살림살이를 거느리고 다니기에는 너무도 위세 있는 인물이었다. 그는 자기 부족 사이에서 지켜야 할 명성이 있었으니, 시리아 동쪽 사막을 통틀어 가장 큰 무리를 이끄는 군주이자 가부장에게 어울리는 명성이었다. 도시 사람들 사이에서는 또 다른 명성을 누렸으니, 동방 전역에서 왕이 아닌 자들 가운데 가장 부유한 인물 중 하나라는 명성이었다. 그리고 사실 그는 부자였다. 돈은 물론이요 하인과 낙타와 말, 온갖 종류의 가축에 이르기까지 풍족했기에, 그는 어떤 위풍을 누리는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다. 그 위풍은 낯선 이들 앞에서 그의 품격을 드높이는 한편, 개인적인 자긍심과 안락에도 보탬이 되었다. 그러므로 독자는 종려 과수원에 있던 그의 천막을 자주 들먹인다 하여 오해해서는 안 된다. 그곳에 그는 실로 번듯한 다와르를 두고 있었다. 다시 말해 큰 천막 세 채가 있었으니, 하나는 그 자신을 위한 것, 하나는 손님을 위한 것, 하나는 그가 가장 아끼는 아내와 그 시녀들을 위한 것이었다. 그리고 작은 천막 예닐곱 채가 있어, 그의 하인들과 호위병으로 삼아 데려온 부족의 신하들이 머물렀으니, 그들은 용기를 인정받은 건장한 사내들로 활과 창과 말 다루는 솜씨가 뛰어났다.\n\n* 다와르: 천막들을 둥글게 둘러 친 아랍의 야영지.\n물론 그의 재산은 어떤 종류든 과수원에서 위험에 처할 일이 없었다. 그러나 사람의 습관이란 시골에서뿐 아니라 도시에서도 그를 따라다니는 법이요, 또 규율의 끈을 늦추는 것은 결코 현명하지 못한 일이기에, 다와르의 안쪽은 그의 소와 낙타와 염소, 그리고 사자나 도둑을 꾈 만한 온갖 재물에 내어 주었다.\n\n일더림을 공정하게 평하자면, 그는 자기 백성의 모든 관습을 잘 지켰으니, 그 무엇도, 가장 작은 것조차도 빠뜨리지 않았다. 그 결과 과수원에서의 그의 삶은 사막에서의 삶이 그대로 이어진 것이었다. 그뿐 아니라 그것은 옛 가부장적 방식을 고스란히 재현한 것이었으니, 곧 원시 이스라엘의 진정한 목가적 삶이었다.\n\n대상이 과수원에 다다른 그날 아침으로 돌아가 보면—\"여기, 여기에 세워라.\" 그는 말을 멈추고 땅에 창을 꽂으며 말했다. \"문은 남쪽으로, 호수는 그 앞에 이렇게 두고, 그리고 이것들, 사막의 자식들은 해가 질 때 그 그늘에 앉을 수 있도록.\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 그는 우람한 종려나무 세 그루가 모여 선 곳으로 가서, 마치 자기 말의 목덜미나 사랑하는 자식의 뺨을 어루만지듯 그 가운데 한 그루를 토닥였다.\n\n대상을 향해 \"멈추어라!\" 하고, 천막을 두고 \"여기에 세워라!\" 하고 떳떳이 말할 수 있는 자가 족장 말고 또 누가 있겠는가? 창이 땅에서 뽑히고, 창이 흙에 낸 상처 위로 천막 첫 기둥의 밑동이 세워져, 정문의 한가운데를 표시했다. 그런 다음 여덟 개가 더 세워졌으니, 모두 세 줄에 한 줄마다 셋씩이었다. 그러자 부름에 따라 여인들과 아이들이 와서, 낙타 등에 꾸려 둔 천을 풀었다. 여인들 말고 누가 이 일을 하겠는가? 그들이 가축 떼의 갈색 염소에게서 털을 깎지 않았던가? 그것을 꼬아 실을 만들지 않았던가? 그 실을 짜서 천을 만들지 않았던가? 그 천을 한데 꿰매어, 사실은 짙은 갈색이지만 멀리서 보면 게달의 천막처럼 새까만, 완벽한 지붕을 만들지 않았던가? 그리고 마침내, 족장의 한데 모인 무리가 얼마나 우스갯소리와 웃음을 주고받으며 다 함께 잡아당겨, 천을 기둥에서 기둥으로 팽팽히 펼치고, 말뚝을 박고 줄을 동여매며 나아갔던가! 그리고 사막의 방식대로 건물의 마무리 손질로서 갈대를 엮어 만든 성긴 벽이 제자리에 세워졌을 때, 그들은 얼마나 조마조마하게 숨죽이며 그 어진 분의 판단을 기다렸던가! 그가 안팎을 거닐며 해와 나무와 호수에 견주어 그 집을 살펴보고는, 진심에서 우러난 힘으로 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"잘했다! 이제 너희가 잘 아는 대로 다와르를 차려라. 오늘 밤에는 아라크로 빵을 달게 하고 꿀로 우유를 달게 하며, 화롯불마다 새끼 염소 한 마리씩 구울 것이다. 하느님이 너희와 함께하시기를! 단물 모자랄 일은 없을 터이니 이 호수가 우리의 우물이요, 짐 나르는 짐승도 가장 작은 가축도 굶주리지 않을 터이니 여기 푸른 목초지 또한 있음이라. 하느님이 너희 모두와 함께하시기를, 내 자식들아! 가거라.\"\n\n* 아라크: 대추야자 등으로 빚은 동방의 독한 술.\n그러자 외치며 많은 이들이 저마다 제 거처를 세우러 흩어져 갔다. 몇 사람은 남아 족장을 위해 안을 정돈했다. 그중 사내종들은 가운데 줄의 기둥에 휘장을 드리워 두 칸을 만들었으니, 오른쪽은 일더림 자신을 위한 신성한 곳이요, 다른 한쪽은 그의 말들을 위한 신성한 곳이었다. 그 말들, 곧 그의 솔로몬의 보석들을 끌고 들어와, 입 맞추고 사랑스럽게 토닥이며 풀어 놓았다. 그런 다음 가운데 기둥에 무기걸이를 세우고, 투창과 창과 활과 화살과 방패로 채웠다. 그 바깥에는 초승달을 본떠 만든 주인의 칼을 걸었는데, 그 칼날의 번득임은 손잡이에 박힌 보석의 번득임과 어금지금했다. 걸이 한쪽 끝에는 말의 안장 덮개를 걸었으니, 그중 더러는 임금의 종이 입는 제복처럼 화려했고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 이 위대한 인물의 의복을 펼쳐 놓았으니, 모직 겉옷과 아마포 겉옷, 그의 윗옷과 바지, 그리고 머리에 두르는 갖가지 빛깔의 두건이었다. 그리고 그가 잘되었다고 말하기 전까지는 일손을 멈추지 않았다.\n\n그동안 여인들은 디반을 꺼내어 차렸으니, 이는 그에게 가슴 위로 흘러내린, 아론의 것처럼 새하얀 수염보다 더 없어서는 안 될 물건이었다. 그들은 정사각형의 세 변 모양으로 틀을 짜 맞추되 그 트인 쪽을 문으로 향하게 하고, 그 위를 방석과 늘어뜨린 휘장으로 덮었으며, 방석은 갈색과 노란색 줄무늬의 빛깔 변하는 깔개로 덮었다. 모퉁이에는 파란색과 진홍색 천에 채워 넣은 베개와 받침을 놓았다. 그런 다음 디반 둘레에 양탄자로 가장자리를 깔고, 안쪽 공간에도 양탄자를 깔았다. 그리고 양탄자가 디반의 트인 쪽에서 천막의 문까지 이어지자, 그들의 일은 끝났다. 그러자 그들은 또다시 주인이 잘되었다고 말하기를 기다렸다. 그러고 나서는 항아리를 가져와 물로 채우고, 아라크가 든 가죽 부대를 손 닿는 곳에 걸어 두는 일만 남았으니—레벤은 내일이었다. 단물 호숫가, 종려 과수원의 종려나무 아래 제 천막에서 일더림이 어찌 행복하고 너그럽지 않을 수 있겠는지, 아랍인이라면 그 까닭을 알 길이 없을 것이다.\n* 레벤: 응유로 만든 아랍의 발효 우유.\n\n우리가 벤허를 남겨 두고 떠난 그 문 앞의 천막이 바로 이러했다.\n\n하인들은 이미 주인의 분부를 기다리고 있었다. 한 사람은 그의 샌들을 벗기고, 다른 한 사람은 벤허의 로마식 신발 끈을 풀었다. 그런 다음 두 사람은 먼지투성이 겉옷을 벗고 산뜻한 흰 아마포 옷으로 갈아입었다.\n\n\"드시오—하느님의 이름으로, 들어와 쉬시오.\" 주인은 예루살렘 장터의 사투리로 진심을 담아 말하고는, 곧 디반으로 앞장서 갔다.\n\n\"나는 여기 앉겠소.\" 그가 다음으로 가리키며 말했다. \"그리고 손님은 거기에.\"\n\n옛날 같으면 시녀라 불렸을 한 여인이 그 말에 응하여, 등을 기댈 수 있도록 베개와 받침을 솜씨 좋게 쌓았다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 디반 가장자리에 앉았고, 그동안 호수에서 갓 길어 온 물로 그들의 발을 씻겨 수건으로 닦아 주었다.\n\n\"우리 사막에 이런 말이 있소.\" 일더림이 수염을 그러모아 가느다란 손가락으로 빗질하며 말문을 열었다. \"왕성한 식욕은 장수의 약속이라고. 그대는 그런 식욕이 있소?\"\n\n\"그 법칙대로라면, 어진 족장이여, 나는 백 년은 살겠습니다. 나는 그대의 문 앞에 선 굶주린 늑대입니다.\" 벤허가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그대를 늑대처럼 쫓아 보내지는 않으리다. 내 가축 중에 가장 좋은 것을 그대에게 주겠소.\"\n\n일더림이 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n\"손님 천막에 있는 그분을 찾아가, 나 일더림이 그분의 평안이 흐르는 물처럼 끊이지 않기를 비는 기도를 보낸다고 전하라.\"\n\n대기하던 사내가 절을 했다.\n\n\"또 이렇게 전하라.\" 일더림이 말을 이었다. \"내가 빵을 떼어 나눌 또 한 사람과 함께 돌아왔으니, 지혜로운 발타사르께서 빵을 나누고자 하신다면 셋이 함께할 수 있으며, 새들에게 돌아갈 몫도 조금도 줄지 않을 것이라고.\"\n\n두 번째 하인이 물러갔다.\n\n\"이제 우리도 쉽시다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 일더림은 디반 위에 자리를 잡았으니, 오늘날 다마스쿠스의 시장에서 상인들이 깔개 위에 앉는 모양 그대로였다. 그리고 제대로 편안해지자, 그는 수염 빗질을 멈추고 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그대가 내 손님이며, 내 레벤을 마셨고, 이제 내 소금을 맛보려 한다 하여, 한 가지 물음마저 막을 까닭은 없을 것이오. 그대는 누구요?\"\n\n\"일더림 족장이여,\" 벤허는 그의 눈길을 차분히 견디며 말했다. \"그대의 정당한 요구를 제가 가벼이 여긴다고는 생각지 말아 주십시오. 그러나 그대의 삶에도, 그런 물음에 답하는 것이 자기 자신에게 죄가 되었을 때가 한 번도 없었습니까?\"\n\n\"솔로몬의 영광을 두고 맹세컨대, 있었소!\" 일더림이 대답했다. \"자기 자신을 저버리는 것은 때로 부족을 저버리는 것만큼이나 비열한 일이지.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다, 어진 족장이여!\" 벤허가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그보다 그대에게 더 잘 어울리는 대답은 없었습니다. 이제 저는 압니다. 그대는 제가 청하러 온 신뢰를 정당화할 확신을 구할 뿐이며, 그런 확신이 제 보잘것없는 삶의 사정보다 그대에게 더 관심거리라는 것을.\"\n\n이번에는 족장이 고개를 숙였고, 벤허는 서둘러 그 기세를 몰아갔다.\n\n\"그러면, 괜찮으시다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"먼저, 저는 그대에게 제 이름으로 전해진 그 이름이 뜻하는 바와 달리 로마인이 아닙니다.\"\n\n일더림은 가슴 위로 흘러넘치는 수염을 움켜쥐고, 짙게 맞붙인 눈썹 그늘 사이로 희미하게 반짝이는 눈으로 말하는 이를 바라보았다.\n\n\"다음으로,\" 벤허가 말을 이었다. \"저는 유다 지파의 이스라엘 사람입니다.\"\n\n족장이 눈썹을 조금 치켜올렸다.\n\n\"그뿐이 아닙니다. 족장이여, 저는 로마에 원한을 품은 유대인이니, 그대의 원한 따위는 그것에 견주면 아이의 투정에 지나지 않습니다.\"\n\n노인은 초조한 손길로 수염을 빗다가, 눈의 반짝임마저 사라질 때까지 눈썹을 내리깔았다.\n\n\"더 나아가, 저는 그대에게 맹세합니다, 일더림 족장이여—주께서 제 조상들과 맺으신 언약을 두고 맹세하건대—그대가 제게 제가 구하는 복수만 허락해 주신다면, 그 종족의 돈과 영광은 그대의 것이 될 것입니다.\"\n\n일더림의 눈썹이 풀렸다. 그의 고개가 들렸다. 그의 얼굴이 빛나기 시작했고, 만족이 그를 사로잡아 가는 모습이 거의 눈에 보일 지경이었다.\n\n\"되었소!\" 그가 말했다. \"그대의 혀뿌리에 거짓이 똬리를 틀고 있다면, 솔로몬 자신도 그대 앞에서는 무사하지 못했을 것이오. 그대가 로마인이 아니라는 것, 유대인으로서 로마에 원한을 품고 갚아야 할 복수가 있다는 것을 나는 믿소. 그 점에 대해서는 그만하면 되었소. 그러나 그대의 솜씨로 말하면. 그대는 전차 경주에 어떤 경험이 있소? 그리고 말들—그대는 그것들을 그대 뜻대로 부릴 수 있소?—그대를 알아보게 할 수 있소? 부르면 오게 하고, 그대가 말하면 숨과 힘이 다하는 마지막 끝까지 달리게 할 수 있소? 그리고 사그라드는 그 순간, 그대 생명의 밑바닥에서 그것들을 떨치게 하여 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 힘찬 한 번의 분발로 이끌 수 있소? 그 재능은, 내 아들이여, 누구에게나 주어지는 것이 아니오. 아, 하느님의 영광을 두고! 나는 수백만 백성을 다스리며 그들의 완벽한 주인이었으나 말 한 마리의 존경은 얻지 못한 임금을 알고 있소. 새겨들으시오! 나는 종을 위해 종노릇하는 게 일과인 둔한 짐승들—피와 모습이 천하고 정신이 죽은 것들을 말하는 게 아니오. 여기 내 것들 같은 것들—제 종족의 왕들이며, 첫 파라오의 무리에까지 거슬러 올라가는 혈통을 지닌 것들을 말하는 거요. 그것들은 내 동지요 벗이며, 천막에서 함께 사는 것들이니, 나와 오래 어울리며 내 경지에까지 끌어올려졌소. 그것들은 제 본능에 우리의 슬기를 보태고 제 감각에 우리의 혼을 이어, 마침내 우리가 아는 모든 야망과 사랑과 미움과 경멸을 느끼게 되었소. 전쟁에서는 영웅이요, 믿고 맡기면 여인처럼 충직하오. 어이, 거기!\"\n\n하인 하나가 앞으로 나왔다.\n\n\"내 아랍 말들을 들이라!\"\n\n사내가 천막을 가르는 휘장의 한쪽을 젖히자, 한 무리의 말이 드러났다. 말들은 마치 그 초대가 분명한지 확인하려는 듯 잠시 제자리에 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"오너라!\" 일더림이 그들에게 말했다. \"왜 거기 서 있느냐? 내가 가진 것 가운데 너희 것이 아닌 게 무엇이 있느냐? 오너라, 어서!\"\n\n그것들은 천천히 위풍 있게 걸어 들어왔다.\n\"이스라엘의 아들이여,\" 주인이 말했다. \"그대의 모세는 위대한 인물이었으나—하, 하, 하!—그가 그대의 조상들에게 터벅터벅 걷는 소와 둔하고 느려 빠진 나귀는 허락하면서 말은 가지지 못하게 한 것을 생각하면 나는 웃지 않을 수 없소. 하, 하, 하! 만일 그가 저것—그리고 저것—그리고 이것을 보았더라도 그리했으리라고 그대는 생각하오?\" 이 말과 함께 그는 제게 가장 먼저 다가온 말의 얼굴에 손을 얹고, 더없는 자랑과 다정함으로 토닥였다.\n\n\"그것은 그릇된 판단입니다, 족장이여, 그릇된 판단입니다.\" 벤허가 뜨겁게 말했다. \"모세는 하느님의 사랑을 받은 율법 수여자였을 뿐 아니라 전사이기도 했습니다. 전쟁을 따른다는 것이—아, 그것이 그 모든 피조물을, 이것들도 그 가운데 하나로 사랑하는 것 말고 무엇이겠습니까?\"\n\n그러자 더없이 빼어나게 생긴 머리 하나가—사슴처럼 부드럽고 큰 눈에, 짙은 앞갈기에 반쯤 가렸으며, 작고 뾰족하게 솟아 앞으로 잘 기울어진 귀를 가진 머리가—그의 가슴께까지 바짝 다가왔으니, 콧구멍을 벌리고 윗입술을 달싹였다. \"당신은 누구요?\" 그것은 사람이 말하듯 또렷이 물었다. 벤허는 그것이 경주로에서 본 네 마리 경주마 가운데 하나임을 알아보고, 그 아름다운 짐승에게 펼친 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"그들은 그대에게 말할 거요, 그 신성모독자들이!—날이 줄어들수록 그 목숨도 짧아지기를!\"—족장은 제 자신에 대한 명예훼손을 물리치는 사람의 감정으로 말했다—\"그자들은 그대에게, 내 말하건대, 우리의 가장 좋은 혈통의 말들이 페르시아의 네사이아 목초지에서 비롯되었다고 말할 거요. 하느님께서는 첫 아랍인에게 끝없는 모래 황무지와 나무 한 그루 없는 산 몇 개, 여기저기 쓴물 솟는 우물을 주시고는 그에게 말씀하셨소. '보라, 네 나라다!' 그리고 그 가엾은 자가 푸념하자, 전능하신 분께서 그를 가엾이 여겨 다시 말씀하셨소. '기운을 내어라! 내가 너를 다른 사람들보다 두 배로 복되게 하리라.' 아랍인은 그 말씀을 듣고 감사드린 뒤, 믿음을 품고 그 복을 찾아 떠났소. 그는 먼저 모든 변두리를 두루 다녔으나 헛수고였소. 그러자 사막으로 길을 내어 가고 또 가니—그 황무지 한가운데에 보기에 더없이 아름다운 푸른 섬이 있었고, 그 섬 한가운데에, 보라! 한 무리의 낙타와 또 한 무리의 말이 있었소! 그는 그것들을 기쁘게 거두어, 하느님의 가장 좋은 선물답게 정성껏 길렀소. 그리고 그 푸른 섬에서 땅 위의 모든 말이 나갔으니, 네사이아의 목초지까지 갔고, 북쪽으로는 차디찬 바람의 바다에서 불어오는 돌풍에 끊임없이 두들겨 맞는 무서운 골짜기까지 갔소. 이 이야기를 의심하지 마시오. 만일 의심한다면, 다시는 어떤 부적도 아랍인에게 효험을 부리지 못하기를. 아니, 내가 그대에게 증거를 보여 주리다.\"\n\n그가 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n\"부족의 기록을 가져오너라.\" 그는 응답한 하인에게 말했다.\n\n기다리는 동안 족장은 말들과 어울리며, 그것들의 뺨을 토닥이고 손가락으로 앞갈기를 빗기며, 저마다에게 기억의 표시를 주었다. 이윽고 여섯 사람이 놋쇠 띠로 보강하고 놋쇠 돌쩌귀와 빗장을 단 삼나무 궤를 들고 나타났다.\n\n\"아니,\" 그것들이 모두 디반 곁에 놓이자 일더림이 말했다. \"전부를 말한 게 아니었다. 말들의 기록만, 저것 하나만. 그것을 열고 나머지는 도로 가져가라.\"\n\n궤가 열리자, 은실 고리에 꿴 한 무더기의 상아 판이 드러났다. 판은 살갗처럼 얇아서, 고리 하나마다 수백 개씩 꿰여 있었다.\n\n\"나는 알고 있소.\" 일더림이 고리 몇 개를 손에 쥐며 말했다. \"내 아들이여, 거룩한 도성 성전의 서기들이 얼마나 정성과 열의를 다해 갓 태어난 이들의 이름을 기록하는지를. 그리하여 이스라엘의 모든 아들은, 비록 가부장들보다 앞선 시대라 하더라도, 제 혈통을 그 시작까지 거슬러 짚을 수 있소. 내 조상들은—그분들에 대한 기억이 영원히 푸르기를!—그 발상을 빌려 말 못 하는 자기네 종들에게 적용하는 것을 죄로 여기지 않았소. 이 판들을 보시오!\"\n\n벤허가 고리를 받아 판들을 떼어 보니, 거기에는 달군 쇠끝으로 매끄러운 표면에 지져 새긴 거친 아랍 문자가 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"그대는 이것을 읽을 수 있소, 오 이스라엘의 아들이여?\"\n\n\"아니요. 그 뜻을 제게 말씀해 주셔야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 알아 두시오. 이 판은 저마다, 지나온 수백 년 동안 내 조상들에게 태어난 순혈종 망아지의 이름을 기록한 것이며, 그 아비와 어미의 이름도 함께 적혀 있소. 그것들을 받아 그 나이를 살펴보시오. 그러면 그대가 더 쉽게 믿게 될 것이오.\"\n\n판들 가운데 더러는 거의 닳아 없어진 것이었다. 모두가 세월에 누렇게 바래 있었다.\n\n\"저기 저 궤 안에는, 이제 그대에게 말해 줄 수 있거니와, 완벽한 내력이 들어 있소. 역사가 좀처럼 그러하지 못할 만큼 확실히 입증되었기에 완벽한 것이니—이것들이 모두 어떤 혈통에서 솟아났는지를—이것이, 그리고 지금 그대의 눈길과 손길을 청하는 저것이 말이오. 그리고 그것들이 여기 우리에게 오듯, 시간상 가장 멀리 떨어진 것까지도 그 아비들이 내 아비들에게, 이 내 천막 같은 천막 지붕 아래로 와서, 펼친 손에서 제 몫의 보리를 먹고, 자식인 양 말 건넴을 받았으며, 자식이 그러하듯 말로 다하지 못하는 고마움에 입맞춤을 했소. 그러니 이제, 오 이스라엘의 아들이여, 그대는 내 선언을 믿어도 좋소—내가 사막의 주인이라면, 보라, 여기 내 신하들이오! 그것들을 내게서 앗아 가면, 나는 대상에게 버림받아 죽어 가는 병자와 같아지오. 그것들 덕분에 나이를 먹어도 도시와 도시를 잇는 길에서 내가 일으키는 두려움이 줄지 않았으며, 내가 그것들과 함께 갈 힘이 있는 한 줄지 않을 것이오. 하, 하, 하! 그것들 조상이 해낸 놀라운 일들을 그대에게 들려줄 수도 있소. 때가 좋으면 그리하리다. 지금으로서는, 그것들이 퇴각하다 따라잡힌 적이 없으며, 솔로몬의 칼을 두고, 추격에 실패한 적도 없다는 것만으로 충분하오! 새겨 두시오, 그것은 모래 위에서, 안장을 얹고서 한 일이오. 그러나 이제—나는 잘 모르겠소—두렵소. 그것들이 처음으로 멍에를 메는 것이며, 성공의 조건이 너무도 많기 때문이오. 그것들에게는 긍지와 빠름과 끈기가 있소. 내가 그것들에게 주인을 찾아 준다면, 그것들은 이기리다. 이스라엘의 아들이여! 그러니 그대가 그 사람이라면, 맹세컨대 그대를 이곳으로 데려온 날은 복된 날이 될 것이오. 이제 그대 자신에 대해 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"이제 알겠습니다.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"어찌하여 아랍인의 사랑에서 그의 말이 자식 다음의 자리를 차지하는지를. 그리고 어찌하여 아랍 말이 세상에서 가장 뛰어난지도 알겠습니다. 그러나 어진 족장이여, 말만으로 저를 판단하지는 말아 주십시오. 아시다시피 사람의 모든 약속이란 때로 어긋나기도 하니까요. 먼저 이 근처 어느 평지에서 제게 시험을 허락하시고, 내일 그 네 마리를 제 손에 맡겨 주십시오.\"\n\n일더림의 얼굴이 다시 빛났고, 그가 막 입을 열려 했다.\n\n\"잠깐, 어진 족장이여, 잠깐만!\" 벤허가 말했다. \"한마디 더 하게 해 주십시오. 저는 로마의 스승들에게서 많은 가르침을 배웠으나, 그것이 이런 때에 제게 도움이 되리라고는 거의 생각지 못했습니다. 말씀드리건대, 사막의 이 아들들은 비록 따로따로는 독수리의 빠름과 사자의 끈기를 지녔다 하더라도, 멍에를 메고 함께 달리도록 훈련받지 못하면 실패할 것입니다. 생각해 보십시오, 족장이여, 네 마리마다 가장 느린 한 마리와 가장 빠른 한 마리가 있게 마련입니다. 그리고 경주는 언제나 가장 느린 놈에 좌우되지만, 말썽은 언제나 가장 빠른 놈에게서 비롯됩니다. 오늘이 그러했습니다. 마부는 가장 좋은 놈을 가장 못한 놈과 조화롭게 움직이도록 누그러뜨리지 못했습니다. 제 시험도 더 나은 결과를 내지 못할지 모릅니다. 그러나 만일 그렇다면, 저는 그대에게 그 사실을 말씀드리겠습니다. 그것을 맹세합니다. 그러므로 같은 마음으로 말씀드리거니와, 제가 그것들을 제 뜻에 따라 움직여 네 마리를 하나처럼 함께 달리게 할 수 있다면, 그대는 그 세스테르티우스와 영예를 얻고, 저는 제 복수를 얻을 것입니다. 어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n* 세스테르티우스: 고대 로마의 화폐 단위.\n일더림은 그동안 수염을 빗으며 귀를 기울였다. 끝에 가서 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"그대를 더 좋게 보게 되었소, 이스라엘의 아들이여. 우리 사막에 이런 말이 있소. '그대가 말로 식사를 짓겠다면, 나는 버터 한 바다를 약속하리라.' 아침이면 그대는 그 말들을 얻을 것이오.\"\n\n그 순간 천막 뒤쪽 입구에 인기척이 일었다.\n\n\"저녁이오—여기 왔구려! 그리고 저기 내 벗 발타사르, 그대가 알게 될 분이오. 그분에게는 이스라엘 사람이라면 결코 듣기에 싫증 내지 않을 이야기가 있소.\"\n\n그리고 그는 하인들에게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"기록을 치우고, 내 보석들을 그 처소로 돌려보내라.\"\n\n그러자 그들은 분부대로 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\n이제 독자가 사막에서 만난 현자들의 식사 장면으로 되돌아간다면, 일데림의 천막에서 차려진 저녁상의 준비가 어떠했는지 이해하게 될 것이다. 그 차이라야 주로 더 넉넉한 재력과 더 나은 시중에서 비롯된 것뿐이었다.\n\n디반이 거의 둘러싼 공간 안쪽 양탄자 위에 깔개 세 장이 펼쳐졌다. 높이가 한 자도 채 안 되는 식탁 하나가 운반되어 같은 자리에 놓이고, 그 위에 보가 덮였다. 한쪽으로는 들고 다닐 수 있는 흙 화덕이 자리 잡았는데, 그것을 맡은 여인의 임무는 일행에게 빵을, 더 정확히 말하면 가까운 천막에서 끊임없이 소리를 내며 돌아가는 맷돌로 빻은 밀가루로 구운 뜨거운 떡을 끊이지 않게 대는 일이었다.\n\n그러는 사이 발타사르가 디반으로 인도되었고, 일데림과 벤허는 일어서서 그를 맞이했다. 헐렁한 검은 가운이 그의 몸을 감싸고 있었다. 그의 걸음은 힘이 없었고, 온몸의 움직임은 느리고 조심스러웠으니, 보아하니 긴 지팡이와 하인의 팔에 기대고 있었다.\n\n\"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 나의 벗이여.\" 일데림이 정중하게 말했다. \"평화와 함께 그대를 환영하오.\"\n\n이집트인은 고개를 들고 대답했다. \"그대에게도, 어진 족장이여, 그대와 그대의 식구들에게도 평화와 유일하신 하느님의 축복이 있기를. 참되고 사랑이 넘치는 하느님의 축복이.\"\n\n그 태도는 부드럽고 경건하여 벤허에게 외경의 감정을 불러일으켰다. 게다가 화답하는 인사 속에 담긴 축복이 일부는 벤허 자신에게 향한 것이었으니, 그 부분이 발음되는 동안 노쇠한 손님의 움푹 꺼졌으되 형형한 두 눈이 그의 얼굴 위에 머물렀고, 그 머무름은 새롭고도 신비로운 감정을 일깨우기에 충분히 길었다. 그 감정이 어찌나 강했던지, 그는 식사 내내 거듭하여 주름이 깊이 패고 핏기 없는 그 얼굴을 살피며 그 의미를 헤아리려 했다. 그러나 그 표정은 언제나 어린아이처럼 온화하고 평온하며 미더웠다. 조금 뒤에 그는 그것이 늘 그 얼굴에 배어 있는 표정임을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"오 발타사르여, 바로 이 사람이오.\" 족장이 벤허의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"오늘 저녁 우리와 함께 빵을 뗄 사람이지요.\"\n\n이집트인은 청년을 흘낏 보더니, 다시 놀라움과 의심에 찬 눈길로 그를 바라보았다. 그것을 본 족장이 말을 이었다. \"내일 시험 삼아 내 말들을 그에게 맡기기로 약속했소. 모든 일이 잘 풀리면, 그가 원형경기장에서 내 말들을 몰 것이오.\"\n\n발타사르는 계속 그를 응시했다.\n\n\"이 사람은 든든한 추천을 받고 왔소.\" 일데림은 몹시 어리둥절해하며 말을 이었다. \"고귀한 로마 선장이었던 아리우스의 아들로 그대는 알아도 좋소만, 그래도—\" 족장은 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 웃으며 다시 말했다. \"그래도 본인은 자기가 유다 지파의 이스라엘 사람이라고 우긴다오. 그리고 하느님의 영광에 걸고, 나는 그가 하는 말을 믿소!\"\n\n발타사르는 더는 설명을 미룰 수 없었다.\n\n\"오 더없이 너그러운 족장이여, 오늘 내 목숨이 위태로워, 한 젊은이가, 이 사람과 똑같이 생긴, 아니 어쩌면 바로 이 사람일지도 모를 그 젊은이가, 다른 모든 이가 달아날 때 나서서 나를 구해 주지 않았더라면 나는 목숨을 잃었을 것이오.\" 그러고는 벤허를 향해 곧장 말했다. \"그대가 바로 그 사람이 아니오?\"\n\n\"거기까지는 대답드릴 수 없습니다.\" 벤허가 겸손하고 정중하게 대답했다. \"저는 카스탈리아 샘에서 무례한 로마인의 말들이 어르신의 낙타로 돌진할 때 그 말들을 멈춰 세운 사람입니다. 어르신의 따님이 제게 잔을 하나 맡기셨지요.\"\n\n그는 윗옷 품에서 잔을 꺼내 발타사르에게 건넸다.\n\n이집트인의 시든 얼굴에 환한 빛이 번졌다.\n\n\"주님께서 오늘 그 샘에서 그대를 내게 보내셨소.\" 그는 떨리는 목소리로 벤허를 향해 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"그리고 이제 다시 그대를 내게 보내셨구려. 나는 그분께 감사드리오. 그대도 그분을 찬미하시오. 그분의 은총으로 나는 그대에게 큰 보답을 할 수 있는 것을 지녔으니, 반드시 그리하겠소. 이 잔은 그대의 것이오. 간직하시오.\"\n\n벤허는 그 선물을 도로 받았고, 발타사르는 일데림의 얼굴에 떠오른 궁금증을 보고 샘에서 있었던 일을 들려주었다.\n\n\"뭐라!\" 족장이 벤허에게 말했다. \"그대는 내게 이 일을 한마디도 하지 않았구려. 이보다 더 나은 추천장을 가져올 수도 없었을 텐데. 나는 아랍인이요, 수만 명에 이르는 부족의 족장이 아니오? 그리고 이분은 내 손님이 아니오? 또한 내 손님에게 그대가 베푼 선이나 악은 곧 내게 베푼 선이나 악이라는 것이, 내 손님을 지키는 맹세에 들어 있지 않소? 보답을 받으러 그대가 어디로 가야 한단 말이오, 여기가 아니면? 그리고 그 보답을 줄 손이 내 손이 아니면 누구의 손이겠소?\"\n\n말끝에 이르러 그의 목소리는 살을 에는 듯한 날카로운 음으로 높아졌다.\n\n\"어진 족장이시여, 부디 저를 봐주십시오. 저는 크든 작든 보답을 바라고 온 것이 아닙니다. 그런 생각을 품지 않았음을 밝히고자 말씀드리거니와, 제가 이 훌륭한 분께 베푼 도움은 어르신의 가장 미천한 하인에게라도 똑같이 베풀었을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"하나 이분은 내 벗이요, 내 손님이지, 내 하인이 아니오. 그대는 그 차이에서 운명의 은총을 보지 못한단 말이오?\" 그러고는 족장이 발타사르에게 덧붙였다. \"아, 하느님의 영광에 걸고! 다시 말하거니와 이 사람은 로마인이 아니오.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 돌아서서, 저녁상 준비가 거의 끝나가는 하인들에게 주의를 기울였다.\n\n사막에서 만났을 때 발타사르가 직접 들려준 그의 내력을 기억하는 독자라면, 벤허가 사심 없음을 밝힌 말이 이 덕망 높은 노인에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지 이해할 것이다. 기억하다시피, 그가 사람들에게 바친 헌신에는 어떠한 차별도 없었다. 한편 보답으로 그에게 약속된 구원은, 곧 그가 기다려 온 그 구원은, 만인을 위한 것이었다. 그러므로 그에게 그 말은 마치 자기 자신의 메아리처럼 들렸다. 그는 벤허에게 한 걸음 더 다가가, 어린아이 같은 말투로 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"족장이 그대를 무어라 부르라 하셨던가? 로마식 이름이었던 것 같소만.\"\n\n\"아리우스, 아리우스의 아들입니다.\"\n\n\"하나 그대는 로마인이 아니지 않소?\"\n\n\"제 핏줄은 모두 유대인이었습니다.\"\n\n\"이었다, 라고 했소? 지금은 살아 있지 않단 말이오?\"\n\n그 물음은 단순하면서도 미묘했다. 그러나 일데림이 벤허를 대답하지 않아도 되게 구해 주었다.\n\n\"자, 오시오.\" 그가 두 사람에게 말했다. \"식사가 준비되었소.\"\n\n벤허는 발타사르에게 팔을 내어 그를 식탁으로 인도했고, 이내 모두가 동방식으로 깔개 위에 앉았다. 물그릇이 들려 나오자, 그들은 손을 씻고 물기를 닦았다. 그런 다음 족장이 신호를 보내니 하인들이 멈췄고, 거룩한 감정에 떨리는 이집트인의 목소리가 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n\"만물의 아버지, 하느님! 우리가 가진 것은 모두 당신에게서 온 것입니다. 우리의 감사를 받으시고, 우리를 축복하시어 우리가 당신의 뜻을 계속 행하게 하소서.\"\n\n그것은 이 어진 노인이 그리스인 가스파르, 힌두인 멜키오르 형제와 함께 동시에 올렸던 그 감사 기도였으니, 갖가지 다른 언어로 터져 나온 그 기도에서 여러 해 전 사막의 식사 자리에 신성한 임재가 함께했음을 증언하는 기적이 일어났던 것이다.\n\n그들이 곧바로 손을 댄 식탁에는, 짐작할 수 있듯이, 동방에서 즐겨 먹는 든든한 음식과 진미가 풍성히 차려져 있었다. 화덕에서 갓 구운 떡, 텃밭에서 거둔 푸성귀, 고기만 따로 차린 것, 고기와 푸성귀를 섞은 것, 소젖, 그리고 꿀과 버터—이 모든 것을, 짚고 넘어가야 할 점이거니와, 칼이나 포크, 숟가락, 잔, 접시 따위 오늘날의 기물 하나 없이 먹고 마셨다. 식사의 이 대목에서는 별 말이 오가지 않았으니, 그들이 시장했기 때문이다. 그러나 후식이 나올 무렵에는 사정이 달라졌다. 그들은 다시 손을 씻고, 무릎 위에 펴 두었던 천을 털어 내었으며, 새로 차린 상과 무뎌진 입맛으로 이제는 이야기를 나누고 귀를 기울일 마음이 되었다.\n\n아랍인과 유대인과 이집트인, 모두가 한 분이신 하느님을 똑같이 믿는 이런 일행에게, 그 시절 화제라야 단 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 셋 가운데 누가 그것을 이야기할 사람으로 신께서 그토록 가까이 몸소 나타나신 분, 별 속에서 그분을 뵙고, 그분의 음성으로 인도하심을 들었으며, 그분의 영에 이끌려 그토록 멀리 그토록 기적처럼 인도받은 그 사람만 하겠는가? 또한 그가 증언하라 부름받았던 그 일이 아니면, 무엇을 이야기하겠는가?\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\n해 질 무렵 산들이 종려나무 과수원 위로 드리운 그림자는, 낮과 밤 사이에 으레 깃들기 마련인 보랏빛 하늘과 졸음에 잠긴 대지의 그 달콤한 짬을 남겨 두지 않았다. 밤은 이르고도 빠르게 찾아왔다. 그래서 오늘 저녁, 천막 안에 어둠이 스며드는 것에 맞서, 하인들은 놋쇠 촛대 네 개를 들고 와 탁자의 네 귀퉁이에 놓았다. 촛대마다 가지가 넷이었고, 가지마다 불을 밝힌 은등잔 하나와 올리브 기름을 담은 보충용 잔이 얹혀 있었다. 넉넉하다 못해 환한 불빛 속에서, 후식을 들던 무리는 이야기를 이어 갔으니, 그 일대 모든 민족에게 익숙한 시리아 방언으로 나누는 말이었다.\n\n이집트인은 사막에서 셋이 만났던 이야기를 들려주었고, 그 일이 스물일곱 해 전 십이월, 헤롯에게서 달아나던 자신과 두 동행이 이 천막에 이르러 묵을 곳을 청했을 때였다는 데에 족장과 뜻을 같이했다. 그 이야기는 깊은 관심 속에 청취되었으니, 하인들조차 틈만 나면 그 세세한 대목을 놓치지 않으려 머뭇거렸다. 벤허는 온 인류에게, 그중에서도 이스라엘 백성에게 누구보다 깊이 관계되는 계시에 귀 기울이는 사람답게 그 이야기를 받아들였다. 곧 보게 되겠지만, 그의 마음속에서는 하나의 생각이 결정(結晶)을 이루어 가고 있었으니, 그것은 그의 삶의 행로를 바꾸어 놓을, 어쩌면 그 행로를 송두리째 삼켜 버릴 생각이었다.\n\n이야기가 이어지는 동안, 발타사르가 젊은 유대인에게 새긴 인상은 더욱 깊어졌다. 이야기가 끝날 무렵 그의 감정은 너무도 깊어 그 진실을 한 점 의심할 여지가 없었다. 실로 이 일과 관련하여 그에게 더 바랄 것이 있다면, 만일 그런 것을 얻을 수 있다면, 오직 그 놀라운 사건의 결과에만 관계된 확언뿐이었다.\n\n이제 한 가지 설명이 필요하니, 어쩌면 눈 밝은 이들은 이미 그것을 요구해 왔을지도 모른다. 더는 미룰 수 없는 것임에 틀림없다. 우리 이야기는 시점으로 보나 사실로 보나, 마리아의 아들이 그 사역을 시작하는 무렵에 바싹 다가서기 시작한다. 그 아이는 다름 아닌 이 발타사르가 베들레헴 곁 동굴에서 어머니의 무릎에 앉은 그를 경배하며 떠난 뒤로, 우리가 단 한 번밖에 보지 못한 그 아이다. 이제부터 끝까지 이 신비로운 아이는 끊임없이 언급될 주제가 될 것이다. 그리고 우리가 다루는 사건의 흐름은 더디지만 확실하게 우리를 그에게 점점 더 가까이 데려가, 마침내 우리는 한 사람이 된 그를 보게 될 것이다—그리고, 만일 첨예하게 맞서는 견해들이 허락만 한다면, 이렇게 덧붙이고 싶다—세상이 없어서는 안 될 한 사람을. 겉보기에 이토록 단순한 이 선언을, 믿음으로 깨우친 영민한 마음은 크게 받아들일 것이며—그것도 기꺼이 받아들일 것이다. 그가 오기 전에도, 그 뒤에도, 특정한 백성과 시대에 없어서는 안 될 사람들은 있었다. 그러나 그의 없어서는 안 될 자리는 온 인류를 향한 것이었고, 모든 시대를 위한 것이었으니—이 점에서 그것은 유례없고, 홀로 우뚝하며, 신성하다.\n\n일데림 족장에게 그 이야기는 새로운 것이 아니었다. 그는 의심할 여지를 남기지 않는 정황 속에서 세 현인이 함께 있을 때 그 이야기를 들었다. 또 그는 그 이야기에 따라 진지하게 행동했으니, 첫 헤롯의 노여움을 피해 달아나는 도망자를 돕는 일은 위험했기 때문이다. 이제 그 셋 가운데 하나가 다시 그의 식탁에 앉아, 반가운 손님이자 존경하는 벗이 되었다. 일데림 족장은 분명 그 이야기를 믿었다. 그러나 사물의 이치상, 그 이야기의 강력한 핵심 사실은 벤허에게 다가온 것만큼의 힘과 가슴을 사로잡는 효력으로 그에게 다가오지는 못했다. 그는 아랍인이었고, 그 결과에 대한 관심은 그저 일반적인 것에 지나지 않았다. 반면에 벤허는 이스라엘 사람이요 유대인이었으니, 그 사실의 진실됨에—이런 어법의 모순을 용서받을 수 있다면—특별한 관심 이상의 것을 품고 있었다. 그는 순전히 유대인다운 마음으로 그 정황을 붙들었다.\n\n기억해 두어야 할 것은, 그가 요람에서부터 메시아 이야기를 들으며 자랐다는 사실이다. 학당에서 그는 선택받은 백성의 희망이자 두려움이며 또한 그들 고유의 영광인 그 존재에 관해 알려진 모든 것을 익혔다. 영웅들의 계보 가운데 처음부터 끝까지 모든 예언자가 그를 예언했다. 그의 오심은 랍비들에게 끝없는 해설의 주제였고 지금도 그러했다—회당에서, 학교에서, 성전에서, 금식일과 절기마다, 공적인 자리에서나 사적인 자리에서나, 민족의 스승들은 해설하고 또 해설하기를 그치지 않았다. 그리하여 아브라함의 모든 자손은, 그들의 운명이 어디에 내던져졌든, 메시아를 기다림 속에 품었고, 그 기다림에 따라 글자 그대로, 쇠처럼 엄격하게 그들의 삶을 다스리고 빚어 나갔다.\n\n이로 미루어 보아 유대인들 사이에도 메시아를 둘러싼 논쟁이 많았으리라는 점은 의심의 여지가 없으며, 실제로도 그러했다. 그러나 그 논쟁은 오직 한 가지, 단 한 가지 점에 국한되어 있었다—그가 언제 올 것인가?\n\n논설은 설교자의 몫이다. 반면에 글쓴이는 그저 이야기를 들려줄 따름이니, 제 본분을 잃지 않으려면, 지금 하고 있는 이 설명에서는 메시아와 관련된 한 가지 점만을 짚으면 된다. 그 점에 관한 한 선택받은 백성 사이의 일치는 그야말로 경이로운 놀라움이었다. 곧 그가 오면 유대인의 왕이 되리라는 것—그들의 정치적 왕, 그들의 카이사르가 되리라는 것이었다. 그를 통해 그는 땅을 무력으로 정복하고, 이어 그들의 이익을 위해, 하느님의 이름으로 영원히 그것을 다스리리라는 것이었다. 친애하는 독자여, 바리새인, 곧 분리주의자들은—후자는 오히려 정치적인 명칭이지만—성전의 회랑에서, 제단 둘레에서, 이 믿음 위에 마케도니아인의 꿈을 훌쩍 넘어서는 희망의 전당을 세웠다. 그의 꿈은 땅을 덮는 데 그쳤으나, 그들의 꿈은 땅을 덮고 하늘까지 채웠다. 다시 말해, 그들의 대담하고 끝 모를, 신성을 모독하는 자만의 환상 속에서, 전능하신 하느님은 사실상 그들의 쓰임에 맞게, 영원한 종살이의 표징으로 자신의 귀를 문에 못 박히도록 내맡기시는 셈이었다.\n\n곧장 벤허에게로 돌아오면, 이제 그의 삶에 두 가지 정황이 있었음을 짚어 두어야겠다. 그 결과로 그는 동족인 분리주의자들의 그 무모한 믿음이 끼치는 영향과 모진 작용에서 비교적 자유로운 상태로 머물러 있었다.\n\n첫째로, 그의 아버지는 사두개인의 믿음을 따랐는데, 이들은 대체로 그 시대의 자유주의자라 일컬을 만했다. 그들은 영혼을 부정하는 다소 느슨한 견해를 지니고 있었다. 그들은 모세 오경에 담긴 율법을 엄격하게 해석하고 철저하게 지켰으나, 그 책들에 덧붙은 방대한 랍비의 부록들은 비웃듯 경멸하며 물리쳤다. 그들은 의심할 바 없이 하나의 분파였으나, 그들의 종교는 신조라기보다 차라리 철학에 가까웠다. 그들은 삶의 즐거움을 스스로 마다하지 않았고, 인류 가운데 이방인 무리에게서도 훌륭한 방식과 산물을 적지 않게 보았다. 정치에서 그들은 분리주의자들의 능동적인 반대 세력이었다. 사물의 자연스러운 이치대로라면, 이러한 정황과 처지, 견해와 특성은 아버지의 재산 어느 한 부분 못지않게 확실하고도 실제로 아들에게 물려졌을 것이다. 그리고 우리가 보았듯, 그는 실제로 그것들을 물려받아 가는 과정에 있었으니, 두 번째 구원의 사건이 그를 덮쳤을 때였다.\n\n벤허와 같은 정신과 기질을 지닌 젊은이에게 로마에서 보낸 다섯 해의 풍요로운 삶이 끼친 영향은, 그 위대한 도시가 당시 실로 만국이 모여드는 자리였음을 떠올릴 때 가장 잘 헤아릴 수 있다—정치적으로나 상업적으로나 모여드는 자리였고, 거리낌 없이 쾌락에 탐닉하는 자리이기도 했다. 포룸 앞의 황금 이정표를 빙빙 돌아—때로는 일식의 어둠에 잠기고, 때로는 다가설 수 없는 광휘에 휩싸인 그 이정표를—인류의 온갖 활기찬 물결이 흘렀다. 처신의 탁월함, 사교의 세련됨, 지성의 성취, 업적의 영광이 그에게 아무 인상도 남기지 않았다면, 어찌 아리우스의 아들인 그가 미세눔 근처의 아름다운 별장에서 카이사르의 접견실로 날마다, 그토록 오랜 세월 동안 드나들면서, 거기서 본 왕과 제후, 사절과 인질과 대표들—모두가 알려진 온 땅에서 온 청원자로서, 자신을 세워 줄지 무너뜨릴지를 가를 가부의 한마디를 비굴하게 기다리던 이들—에게 조금도 영향받지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 물론 단순한 군집으로 치자면, 유월절을 기념하여 예루살렘에 모여드는 무리에 견줄 것은 없었다. 그러나 그가 키르쿠스 막시무스의 자줏빛 차일 아래 삼십오만 관중 가운데 하나로 앉아 있을 때, 그에게는 이런 생각이 찾아들었음에 틀림없다. 어쩌면 인류 가운데에는, 비록 할례받지 못한 자들일지라도, 자비까지는 아니더라도 신의 헤아림을 받을 만한 갈래들이 있을지 모른다는 생각—그들의 슬픔으로 인해, 그리고 더 나쁘게는 슬픔 한가운데서의 그 절망으로 인해, 자기 동족에게 주어진 약속 안에서 형제 됨을 누릴 자격이 있는 이들이 있을지 모른다는 생각이었다.\n\n그러한 정황에서 그가 그러한 생각을 품었다는 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다. 적어도 그 정도는 인정되리라고 우리는 본다. 그러나 그 성찰이 그에게 찾아들어 그가 거기에 자신을 내맡겼을 때, 그는 어떤 분명한 차이를 못 보았을 리 없다. 그 무리의 비참함과 그들의 절망적인 처지는 종교와는 아무런 관련이 없었다. 그들의 웅성거림과 신음은 그들의 신들을 거스르는 것도, 신이 없음을 한탄하는 것도 아니었다. 브리튼의 떡갈나무 숲에서는 드루이드들이 그 추종자들을 거느렸다. 오딘과 프레야는 갈리아와 게르마니아에서, 그리고 극북의 히페르보레아인들 사이에서 그 신성을 지켰다. 이집트는 제 악어들과 아누비스로 흡족해했다. 페르시아인은 여전히 오르무즈드와 아리만에게 헌신하며 둘을 똑같이 받들었다. 힌두인들은 열반을 바라며 한결같이 참을성 있게 브라흐마의 빛 없는 길을 걸어갔다. 아름다운 그리스의 정신은 철학의 막간에도 여전히 호메로스의 영웅 신들을 노래했다. 한편 로마에서는 신들만큼 흔하고 값싼 것이 없었다. 세상의 주인들은, 자기네가 주인이라는 이유로, 변덕에 따라 제단에서 제단으로 거리낌 없이 그 숭배와 제물을 옮겨 다니며, 자기들이 세운 그 아수라장을 즐겼다. 그들의 불만이란, 만일 그들에게 불만이 있었다면, 신들의 수효에 대한 것이었다. 그들은 땅 위의 온갖 신성을 빌려 온 뒤, 자기네 카이사르들마저 신격화하여 그들에게 제단과 성스러운 예배를 바치기로 의결하기에 이르렀으니 말이다. 그렇다, 그 불행한 처지는 종교에서 비롯된 것이 아니라, 그릇된 통치와 찬탈, 그리고 헤아릴 수 없는 폭정에서 비롯된 것이었다. 사람들이 곤두박질쳐 떨어져 거기서 구해 달라고 빌고 있던 그 아베르누스는, 끔찍하기는 했으나 본질적으로 정치적인 것이었다. 그 간구는—로디눔에서나 알렉산드리아에서나 아테네에서나 예루살렘에서나 한결같이—함께 싸워 정복할 왕을 향한 것이었지, 경배할 신을 향한 것이 아니었다.\n\n이천 년이 지난 지금 그 상황을 살펴보면, 우리는 이렇게 보고 말할 수 있다. 종교적으로 그 보편적 혼란에서 벗어날 길은, 어떤 신이 스스로 참된 신임을, 그것도 위엄 있는 신임을 입증하고 구하러 오는 것 말고는 없었다고. 그러나 그 시대의 사람들은, 눈 밝고 사색에 깊은 이들조차도, 로마를 짓밟는 것 말고는 어떤 희망도 찾지 못했다. 그 일만 이루어지면 회복과 재편이 뒤따라 구원이 오리라 여겼다. 그리하여 그들은 기도하고, 음모를 꾸미고, 반란을 일으키고, 싸우고, 죽어 갔으니, 오늘은 피로 땅을 적시고 내일은 눈물로 적셨다—그리고 늘 똑같은 결말을 맞았다.\n\n이제 남은 말은, 벤허가 로마인이 아닌 그 시대 대다수 사람들과 뜻을 같이했다는 것이다. 수도에서 보낸 다섯 해는 그에게 정복당한 세상의 비참함을 보고 헤아릴 기회를 주었다. 그리고 그 세상을 괴롭히는 악이 정치적인 것이며 오직 칼로써만 고쳐질 수 있다고 굳게 믿은 그는, 그 영웅적 처방에 호소하는 날에 한몫을 맡을 수 있도록 스스로를 단련하러 나서던 참이었다. 무예를 익혀 그는 완벽한 군인이었다. 그러나 전쟁에는 더 높은 영역이 있으니, 거기서 성공적으로 움직이려는 자는 방패로 막고 창으로 찌르는 것 이상을 알아야 한다. 그 영역에서 장수는 제 임무를 찾으니, 그 가운데 가장 큰 것은 많은 이를 하나로, 그 하나를 곧 자신으로 모으는 일이다. 더없이 완숙한 지휘관이란 한 군대로 무장한 한 사람의 전사다. 이러한 구상이 그의 삶의 계획 속으로 들어왔고, 그를 거기로 더욱 기울게 한 것은 이런 성찰이었다. 곧 자신이 당한 개인적인 부당함과 관련하여 그가 꿈꾸던 복수는, 평화로운 어떤 길을 좇기보다 전쟁의 어떤 길에서 더 확실하게 찾으리라는 생각이었다.\n\n그가 어떤 심정으로 발타사르의 말에 귀 기울였는지는 이제 이해할 수 있다. 그 이야기는 그의 존재에서 가장 예민한 두 지점을 건드렸기에 그의 안에서 울려 퍼졌다. 그의 심장은 빠르게 뛰었다—스스로를 살펴, 그 이야기가 낱낱이 모두 참되다는 것도, 그토록 기적적으로 발견된 그 아이가 메시아라는 것도 의심할 여지가 없음을 알았을 때, 더욱더 빠르게 뛰었다. 이스라엘이 그 계시에 그토록 죽은 듯 무심하다는 것에, 그리고 자신이 그날 이전에는 그것을 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없다는 것에 크게 놀라며, 두 가지 물음이 그에게 떠올랐으니, 그 순간 그가 더 알고 싶은 모든 것이 거기에 집약되어 있었다.\n\n그 아이는 그때 어디에 있는가?\n\n그리고 그의 사명은 무엇인가?\n\n자꾸 끼어드는 것을 사과하며, 그는 발타사르의 견해를 끌어내기 시작했고, 발타사르는 결코 입을 떼기를 마다하지 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\n\"내가 그대에게 답할 수 있다면,\" 발타사르는 그 소박하고 진실하며 경건한 어조로 말했다. \"오, 그가 어디 있는지 안다면, 나는 얼마나 빨리 그에게 달려가겠는가! 바다도 나를 막지 못하고, 산도 막지 못할 것이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그를 찾으려 해 보셨군요?\" 벤허가 물었다.\n\n이집트인의 얼굴에 미소가 스쳤다.\n\n\"사막에서 내게 주어진 안식처를 떠난 뒤 처음으로 스스로 떠맡은 일은\"—발타사르는 일데림에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다—\"그 아기가 어찌 되었는지 알아내는 것이었소. 하지만 한 해가 지났고, 나는 감히 직접 유대 땅으로 올라갈 수 없었소. 헤롯이 여전히 옥좌에 앉아 예나 다름없이 잔혹한 마음을 품고 있었기 때문이오. 이집트로 돌아오니, 내가 보고 들은 놀라운 일들을 믿어 주는 벗이 몇 있었소. 구세주가 태어났음을 나와 함께 기뻐한 이들, 그 이야기에 결코 싫증 내지 않은 이들이 몇 있었던 거요. 그들 가운데 몇이 나를 위해 그 아기를 찾아 나섰소. 그들은 먼저 베들레헴으로 가서 그 여관과 동굴을 찾아냈소. 그러나 그 청지기는—탄생의 밤에, 그리고 우리가 별을 따라온 그 밤에 문가에 앉아 있던 그 사람은—사라지고 없었소. 왕이 그를 데려갔고, 그 뒤로 그는 다시 보이지 않았소.\"\n\n\"하지만 무언가 증거는 분명히 찾았겠지요.\" 벤허가 간절하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 피로 쓰인 증거였소. 슬픔에 잠긴 마을, 아직도 제 어린것을 위해 우는 어머니들 말이오. 그대도 알아야 하오. 헤롯은 우리가 달아났다는 소식을 듣고는 사람을 내려보내 베들레헴 아이들 가운데 가장 어린 것들을 죽였소. 단 하나도 살아남지 못했소. 내 사자들의 믿음은 확인되었소. 그러나 그들은 내게 와서 아기도 다른 무고한 아이들과 함께 죽임을 당했다고 말했소.\"\n\n\"죽다니!\" 벤허가 깜짝 놀라 외쳤다. \"죽었다고 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 내 아들아, 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았소. 내 사자들이 아기가 죽었다고 말했다고 한 것이오. 나는 그때도 그 소식을 믿지 않았고, 지금도 믿지 않소.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다. 어르신께는 무언가 특별한 앎이 있으시군요.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않소, 그렇지 않소.\" 발타사르가 시선을 떨구며 말했다. \"성령은 그 아기에게 이르기까지만 우리와 함께하셨소. 우리가 예물을 바치고 아기를 본 다음 동굴에서 나왔을 때, 우리는 무엇보다 먼저 그 별을 찾았소. 그러나 별은 사라지고 없었고, 우리는 이제 우리 자신에게 맡겨졌음을 알았소. 거룩하신 분의 마지막 영감은—내가 기억할 수 있는 마지막 영감은—안전을 위해 우리를 일데림에게 보낸 그것이었소.\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 족장이 신경질적으로 수염을 만지작거리며 말했다. \"그대는 어떤 성령에 이끌려 내게 왔다고 했지요. 나는 그것을 기억하오.\"\n\n\"내게 특별한 앎이 있는 것은 아니오.\" 발타사르는 벤허에게 내려앉은 낙담을 살피며 말을 이었다. \"하지만, 내 아들아, 나는 이 일을 두고 많은 생각을 해 왔소. 여러 해에 걸쳐 이어진 생각이었고, 믿음에서 우러난 것이었소. 하느님을 증인 삼아 말하건대, 그 믿음은 호숫가에서 나를 부르는 성령의 음성을 들었던 그 순간만큼이나 지금도 내 안에 굳건하오. 들어 주겠다면, 내가 왜 그 아기가 살아 있다고 믿는지 그대에게 말해 주리다.\"\n\n일데림과 벤허는 둘 다 그러라는 뜻을 내비치고, 듣기만 할 뿐 아니라 이해하려고 온 정신을 그러모으는 듯했다. 그 관심은 하인들에게까지 미쳐, 그들은 자리 가까이 다가와 서서 귀를 기울였다. 천막 안에는 더없이 깊은 침묵이 감돌았다.\n\n\"우리 세 사람은 하느님을 믿소.\"\n\n발타사르는 그렇게 말하며 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"그리고 그분은 진리이시오.\" 그가 다시 말을 이었다. \"그분의 말씀은 곧 하느님이시오. 산은 티끌로 변하고, 바다는 남풍에 말라붙을 수 있소. 그러나 그분의 말씀은 굳건히 서리니, 그것이 곧 진리이기 때문이오.\"\n\n그 말투는 이루 말할 수 없이 장엄했다.\n\n\"호숫가에서 내게 말씀하신 그 음성, 곧 그분의 음성은 이렇게 일렀소. '복되도다, 미스라임의 아들이여! 구속이 다가오느니라. 땅의 먼 곳에서 온 다른 두 사람과 더불어, 너는 구세주를 보리라.' 나는 구세주를 보았소—그 이름을 찬미할지어다!—하지만 약속의 두 번째 부분이었던 구속은 아직 오지 않았소. 이제 알겠소? 만일 그 아기가 죽었다면, 구속을 이룰 자가 없는 것이고, 그러면 그 말씀은 헛것이며, 하느님께서는—아니, 감히 말할 수 없소!\"\n\n그는 두려움에 두 손을 번쩍 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"구속이야말로 그 아기가 태어난 까닭이오. 그러니 그 약속이 살아 있는 한, 죽음조차도 그 일이 이루어질 때까지, 아니면 적어도 이루어지는 길에 들어설 때까지는 그를 그 일에서 떼어 놓을 수 없소. 이제 그것을 내 믿음의 한 가지 이유로 받아들이시오. 그런 다음 좀 더 귀를 기울여 주시오.\"\n\n그 선한 사람은 잠시 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"포도주를 들지 않겠소? 그대 손 가까이 있소, 보시오.\" 일데림이 정중하게 말했다.\n\n발타사르는 포도주를 마시고, 기운을 차린 듯 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내가 본 구세주는 여인에게서 태어났으니, 본성은 우리와 같고, 우리가 겪는 온갖 병고에 매여 있었소. 죽음에까지도. 그것을 첫 번째 명제로 삼읍시다. 다음으로 그에게 따로 정해진 일을 헤아려 보시오. 그것은 오직 어른에게나 어울리는 일이 아니었소? 지혜롭고, 굳세고, 분별 있는 사람—아이가 아니라 어른 말이오. 그런 사람이 되려면 그는 우리가 자라듯 자라야 했소. 그동안 그의 목숨이 처했던 위험들을 이제 생각해 보시오. 어린 시절과 성년 사이의 그 긴 세월 동안의 위험 말이오. 당대의 권세들이 그의 적이었소. 헤롯이 그의 적이었고, 로마는 또 어떠했겠소? 그리고 이스라엘로 말하자면—이스라엘이 그를 받아들이지 않으리라는 것, 바로 그것이 그를 끊어 버리려는 동기였소. 이제 알겠소. 무력하게 자라는 시절에 그의 목숨을 지키는 데, 그를 무명 속으로 숨겨 두는 것보다 더 나은 길이 어디 있었겠소? 그래서 나는 스스로에게, 그리고 사랑의 갈망이 아니고서는 결코 움직이지 않는 내 귀 기울이는 믿음에게 말하오—그는 죽은 것이 아니라 잠겨 있을 뿐이며, 그의 일이 이루어지지 않은 채 남아 있으니 다시 오리라고. 자, 이것이 내 믿음의 이유들이오. 그것들이 옳지 않소?\"\n\n일데림의 작은 아라비아인 눈이 깨달음으로 빛났고, 벤허는 낙담에서 벗어나 진심으로 말했다. \"적어도 저는 그것을 반박할 수 없습니다. 청컨대, 그다음은요?\"\n\n\"이만하면 충분하지 않소, 내 아들아? 그래도,\" 그는 한결 차분한 어조로 시작했다. \"그 이유들이 옳다는 것을—더 분명히 말하면, 그 아기가 발견되지 않는 것이 하느님의 뜻임을 알고서, 나는 내 믿음을 인내의 손에 맡기고 기다리기 시작했소.\" 그는 거룩한 신뢰로 가득한 두 눈을 들어 올리더니, 넋을 놓은 듯 말을 끊었다. \"나는 지금 기다리고 있소. 그는 살아 있고, 그 위대한 비밀을 잘 간직하고 있소. 내가 그에게 갈 수 없다 한들, 그가 머무는 언덕이나 골짜기의 이름을 댈 수 없다 한들 어떻소? 그는 살아 있소—어쩌면 꽃핀 열매처럼, 어쩌면 막 익어 가는 열매처럼. 그러나 하느님의 약속과 섭리 속에 있는 확실함으로 말미암아, 나는 그가 살아 있음을 아오.\"\n\n벤허는 경외감의 전율에 사로잡혔다—그것은 다만 어렴풋이 일었던 의심이 사그라드는 전율이었다.\n\n\"그가 어디 있다고 생각하십니까?\" 그는 낮은 목소리로, 입술 위에 거룩한 침묵의 무게를 느끼는 사람처럼 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n발타사르는 그를 다정하게 바라보고, 아직 넋을 다 추스르지 못한 마음으로 대답했다.\n\n\"나일강 가에 있는 내 집, 강에 어찌나 가까운지 배를 타고 지나는 사람들이 그 집과 물에 비친 그림자를 한꺼번에 볼 수 있는 내 집에서, 몇 주 전에 나는 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있었소. 나는 스스로에게 말했소. 서른 살 된 사람이라면 제 삶의 밭을 죄다 갈아 두고 씨앗도 잘 심어 두었어야 한다고. 그 뒤로는 여름철이니, 뿌린 것을 익힐 만한 시간이 빠듯하기 때문이오. 또 나는 말했소. 그 아기는 이제 스물일곱이니, 씨 뿌릴 때가 가까웠을 것이라고. 나는 스스로에게 물었소. 내 아들아, 그대가 여기서 내게 물은 것처럼 말이오. 그리고 그대 조상들이 하느님께로부터 받은 땅 가까이 있는 좋은 안식처로 여겨 이곳으로 옴으로써 그 물음에 답했소. 유대 땅이 아니라면 그가 어디서 나타나겠소? 예루살렘이 아니라면 어느 도시에서 그의 일을 시작하겠소? 아브라함과 이삭과 야곱의 자손, 적어도 사랑에 있어서는 주님의 자녀인 그들이 아니라면, 그가 가져올 축복을 맨 먼저 받을 자가 누구겠소? 만일 내가 그를 찾아 나서라는 명을 받는다면, 나는 동쪽으로 요르단 골짜기를 향해 내리뻗은 유대와 갈릴리 산기슭의 작은 마을과 동네들을 샅샅이 뒤지겠소. 그는 지금 거기 있소. 어느 문가나 언덕마루에 서서, 바로 오늘 저녁에도 그는 자신이 세상의 빛이 될 그때에 하루 더 가까워진 해가 지는 것을 보았을 것이오.\"\n\n발타사르는 마치 유대 땅을 가리키듯 손을 들어 손가락을 뻗은 채 말을 그쳤다. 듣고 있던 모든 이가, 자리 밖에 있던 둔한 하인들마저도 그의 열정에 사로잡혀, 천막 안에 갑자기 어떤 장엄한 존재가 나타나기라도 한 듯 흠칫 놀랐다. 그 느낌은 곧장 가시지 않았다. 자리에 앉은 이들은 저마다 한동안 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 마침내 벤허가 그 주문을 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"알겠습니다, 선한 발타사르 어르신.\" 그가 말했다. \"어르신께서는 참으로 크고 기이한 은총을 입으셨군요. 또한 어르신께서 진실로 지혜로운 분이심도 알겠습니다. 어르신께서 들려주신 말씀에 제가 얼마나 감사하는지 이루 다 말할 수 없습니다. 저는 큰일이 다가오고 있음을 일깨움받았고, 어르신의 믿음을 얼마간 빌렸습니다. 청컨대, 어르신께서 기다리시는 그분, 그리고 오늘 밤부터 저 또한 유대의 믿는 아들답게 기다릴 그분의 사명에 대해 더 말씀해 주셔서 이 은혜를 마저 채워 주십시오. 그분은 구세주가 되시리라 하셨는데, 또한 유대인의 왕도 되시지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"내 아들아,\" 발타사르가 그 자애로운 어조로 말했다. \"그 사명은 아직 하느님의 품 안에 있는 뜻이오. 내가 그에 관해 생각하는 모든 것은, 그 음성의 말씀과 그 말씀이 답으로 주어진 기도를 견주어 짜낸 것이오. 그것들을 다시 짚어 볼까?\"\n\n\"어르신께서 가르치시는 분이십니다.\"\n\n\"내 불안의 원인은—\" 발타사르가 차분히 시작했다. \"내가 알렉산드리아와 나일강의 마을들에서 설교자가 되게 한 것, 마침내 나를 고독 속으로 몰아넣어 그곳에서 성령이 나를 찾게 한 것은, 사람들의 타락한 처지였소. 내가 믿기로 그것은 하느님을 아는 앎을 잃었기 때문이었소. 나는 내 동족의 슬픔을 슬퍼했소—한 계층만이 아니라 그들 모두의 슬픔을. 그들은 어찌나 철저히 타락했던지, 하느님 자신이 손수 그 일을 떠맡지 않으시는 한 구속이란 있을 수 없으리라 여겨졌소. 그래서 나는 그분께 오시기를, 그리고 내가 그분을 뵐 수 있기를 기도했소. '네 선한 행실이 이겼느니라. 구속이 다가오느니라. 너는 구세주를 보리라.' 그 음성은 이렇게 말씀하셨소. 그 응답을 안고 나는 기뻐하며 예루살렘으로 올라갔소. 자, 그 구속은 누구를 위한 것이오? 온 세상을 위한 것이오. 그러면 어떻게 이루어지겠소? 그대의 믿음을 굳건히 하시오, 내 아들아! 나도 아오, 사람들은 로마가 그 언덕들에서 헐려 없어지기 전에는 행복이 없으리라고 말하오. 다시 말해, 이 시대의 병폐는 내가 여겼던 것처럼 하느님을 모르는 데서 오는 것이 아니라, 통치자들의 그릇된 다스림에서 온다는 거요. 인간의 정부가 결코 종교를 위한 것이 아님을 굳이 누가 일러 줘야 하겠소? 그대는 제 백성보다 나은 왕을 몇이나 들어 보았소? 오, 아니오, 아니오! 구속은 정치적 목적을 위한 것일 수 없소—통치자와 권세를 끌어내리고, 단지 다른 자들이 그 자리를 차지하여 누리게 하려고 자리를 비우는 것일 수 없소. 만일 그게 전부라면, 하느님의 지혜는 더 이상 견줄 데 없는 것이기를 그칠 것이오. 내 그대에게 이르노니, 비록 이것이 눈먼 자가 눈먼 자에게 하는 말일지라도, 오시는 그분은 영혼의 구세주가 되실 것이오. 그리고 구속이란 하느님께서 다시 한 번 이 땅에 오심을 뜻하고, 의로움을 뜻하니, 그분이 여기 머무심을 그분 스스로 견디실 만하게 하기 위함이오.\"\n\n벤허의 얼굴에는 실망이 또렷이 드러났다—그의 고개가 떨구어졌다. 설득되지는 않았더라도, 그는 그 순간 이집트인의 견해에 맞서 논박할 기력이 없음을 느꼈다. 일데림은 그렇지 않았다.\n\n\"하느님의 영광에 두고 말하건대!\" 그가 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"그 판단은 모든 관습을 뒤엎는 것이오. 세상의 이치는 정해져 있어 바뀔 수 없소. 어느 공동체에나 권력을 입은 지도자가 있어야 하오. 그렇지 않으면 개혁이란 없소.\"\n\n발타사르는 그 격한 말을 진중하게 받았다.\n\n\"선한 족장이여, 그대의 지혜는 세상의 것이오. 그리고 그대는 우리가 구속받아야 할 바가 바로 세상의 이치임을 잊고 있소. 사람을 신민으로 삼는 것은 왕의 야심이오. 사람의 영혼을 그 구원으로 이끄는 것은 하느님의 바람이오.\"\n\n일데림은 입을 다물었으나, 믿으려 하지 않고 고개를 저었다. 벤허가 그를 대신해 그 논의를 이어받았다.\n\n\"아버지여—허락하신 대로 그렇게 부르겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"어르신께서는 예루살렘 성문에서 누구를 찾으라는 명을 받으셨습니까?\"\n\n족장이 그에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"나는 사람들에게 물으라는 명을 받았소.\" 발타사르가 조용히 말했다. \"'유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?'고 말이오.\"\n\n\"그리고 베들레헴 곁 동굴에서 그를 보셨고요?\"\n\n\"우리는 그를 보고 경배하고, 그에게 예물을 드렸소—멜키오르는 황금을, 가스파르는 유향을, 그리고 나는 몰약을 드렸소.\"\n\n\"어르신께서 사실을 말씀하실 때는, 듣는 것이 곧 믿는 것입니다, 아버지여.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"하지만 견해의 문제에서는, 어르신께서 그 아기를 어떤 종류의 왕으로 삼으려 하시는지 저로서는 이해할 수가 없습니다. 저는 그 통치자를 그의 권세와 의무에서 떼어 놓고 생각할 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"아들아,\" 발타사르가 말했다. \"우리는 우연히 제 발치에 놓인 것들은 가까이 들여다보면서, 멀리 있는 더 큰 것들에는 흘끗 눈길만 던지는 버릇이 있소. 그대는 지금 그 칭호만을—유대인의 왕이라는 것만을 보고 있소. 그러나 그 너머의 신비로 눈을 들어 올린다면, 그 걸림돌은 사라질 것이오. 그 칭호에 대해 한마디 하리다. 그대의 이스라엘은 더 나은 시절을 보았소—하느님께서 그대의 백성을 사랑스레 자신의 백성이라 부르시고, 예언자들을 통해 그들을 다루시던 시절을 말이오. 자, 만일 그 시절에 그분이 그들에게 내가 본 구세주를 약속하셨다면—유대인의 왕으로 약속하셨다면—그 나타남은 오직 그 말씀을 위해서라도 그 약속에 따른 것이어야 하오. 아, 그대는 내가 성문에서 던진 그 물음의 까닭을 알겠구려!—그대가 알았으니 나는 더 말하지 않고 넘어가겠소. 다음으로, 그대는 어쩌면 그 아기의 위엄을 헤아리고 있을지도 모르겠소. 그렇다면 생각해 보시오—헤롯의 후계자가 된다는 것이 무엇이오? 세상의 영예 기준으로 보면, 그것이 무엇이오? 하느님께서 당신의 사랑하는 이를 그보다 더 낫게 하실 수 없으셨겠소? 만일 그대가 전능하신 아버지께서 칭호가 아쉬워 몸을 굽혀 인간의 발명품을 빌리신다고 생각할 수 있다면, 어찌하여 나는 곧장 카이사르를 찾으라는 명을 받지 않았겠소? 오, 우리가 말하는 것의 본질을 위해서라도, 청컨대 더 높은 곳을 보시오! 차라리 우리가 기다리는 그분이 무엇의 왕이 되실지를 물으시오. 내 아들아, 그것이 바로 그 신비의 열쇠이며, 어떤 사람도 그 열쇠 없이는 그것을 이해하지 못하리라고 나는 말하오.\"\n\n발타사르는 경건하게 두 눈을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"이 땅 위에 한 왕국이 있소, 비록 이 땅에 속한 것은 아니지만 말이오—땅보다 더 넓은 경계를 지닌 왕국, 바다와 땅을 한데 말아 가장 고운 황금처럼 만들어 망치질로 두드려 펼친다 한들 그보다 더 넓은 왕국이오. 그 존재는 우리 마음이 사실인 것처럼 하나의 사실이오. 우리는 태어나서 죽기까지 그것을 지나가면서도 그것을 보지 못하오. 또한 어떤 사람도 먼저 제 영혼을 알기 전에는 그것을 보지 못하리니, 그 왕국은 그를 위한 것이 아니라 그의 영혼을 위한 것이기 때문이오. 그리고 그 다스림 안에는 상상에도 들어 본 적 없는 영광이 있으니—본디 그러하고, 견줄 데 없으며, 더는 보탤 수 없는 영광이오.\"\n\n\"어르신께서 하시는 말씀은 제게 수수께끼입니다, 아버지여.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"저는 그런 왕국에 대해 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"나도 들어 본 적이 없소.\" 일데림이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나도 그에 대해 더는 말할 수 없소.\" 발타사르가 겸손히 눈을 떨구며 덧붙였다. \"그것이 무엇인지, 무엇을 위한 것인지, 어떻게 거기에 이를 수 있는지는, 그 아기가 그것을 자신의 것으로 차지하러 올 때까지 아무도 알 수 없소. 그는 보이지 않는 문의 열쇠를 가져오니, 자신의 사랑하는 이들을 위해 그 문을 열어 줄 것이오. 그들 가운데에는 그를 사랑하는 모든 이가 있으리니, 오직 그러한 이들만이 구속받은 자가 될 것이기 때문이오.\"\n\n그 뒤로 긴 침묵이 흘렀고, 발타사르는 그것을 대화가 끝났다는 뜻으로 받아들였다.\n\n\"선한 족장이여,\" 그가 그 평온한 어조로 말했다. \"내일이나 모레쯤 나는 한동안 도시로 올라가려 하오. 내 딸이 경기 준비하는 모습을 보고 싶어 하오. 우리가 떠날 때에 대해서는 다시 이야기하리다. 그리고 내 아들아, 그대를 다시 보겠소. 두 분께, 평안과 안녕히 주무시기를 비오.\"\n\n그들은 모두 자리에서 일어났다. 족장과 벤허는 이집트인이 천막 밖으로 안내되어 나갈 때까지 그 뒷모습을 바라보며 남아 있었다.\n\n\"일데림 족장이여,\" 그제야 벤허가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 저는 기이한 이야기들을 들었습니다. 청컨대, 그것들을 곰곰이 생각할 수 있도록 호숫가를 거닐게 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"가시오. 나도 뒤따라 가리다.\"\n\n그들은 다시 손을 씻었다. 그러고 나서 주인의 신호에 하인이 벤허에게 신을 가져다주었고, 그는 곧장 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII\n\n둑에서 조금 위쪽에 야자나무 한 무리가 있어, 그 그늘을 반쯤은 물 위에, 반쯤은 뭍에 드리우고 있었다. 가지에서는 불불새 한 마리가 부르는 듯한 노래를 불렀다. 벤허는 그 아래 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다. 다른 때였다면 그 새의 가락이 온갖 상념을 몰아냈으리라. 그러나 이집트인의 이야기는 경이로 가득한 짐이었고, 그는 그 짐을 진 일꾼이었다. 그리고 여느 일꾼이 그러하듯, 그에게는 마음과 몸이 휴식으로 흐뭇하게 어우러지기 전까지는 그 어떤 감미로운 음악도 음악이 아니었다.\n\n밤은 고요했다. 물가에는 잔물결 하나 일지 않았다. 옛 동방의 오래된 별들이 저마다 제자리에 떠올라 있었다. 그리고 온 세상에 여름이 깃들어 있었으니—뭍에도, 호수에도, 하늘에도.\n\n벤허의 상상은 달아올랐고, 그의 감정은 들끓었으며, 그의 의지는 온통 흔들렸다.\n\n그리하여 야자나무도, 하늘도, 대기도 그에게는 사람에 대한 절망에 떠밀려 발타사르가 흘러든 저 머나먼 남쪽 지대의 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그 잔잔한 수면을 지닌 호수는, 성령이 빛나는 모습으로 나타났을 때 그 선한 사람이 곁에 서서 기도하던 나일의 어머니 강을 떠올리게 했다. 그 기적에 딸린 이 모든 정경이 벤허에게 찾아온 것인가? 아니면 그가 그 정경 속으로 옮겨진 것인가? 그리고 만일 그 기적이 되풀이된다면—그것도 그에게 일어난다면? 그는 두려워하면서도 바랐고, 그 환영을 기다리기까지 했다. 마침내 그 열에 들뜬 마음이 가라앉아 제정신으로 돌아오게 되었을 때, 비로소 그는 생각할 수 있었다.\n\n그가 세운 삶의 계획은 이미 설명한 바 있다. 지금까지 그것을 곱씹을 때마다 그가 끝내 잇거나 메울 수 없었던 하나의 틈이 있었으니—어찌나 넓은지 건너편이 어렴풋이 보일 뿐이었다. 마침내 그가 군인일 뿐만 아니라 지휘관으로까지 다듬어졌을 때, 그는 어떤 목표를 향해 자기의 힘을 쏟아야 하는가? 물론 그는 혁명을 꿈꾸고 있었다. 그러나 혁명의 과정이란 예나 지금이나 한결같으니, 사람들을 그 속으로 이끌려면 언제나 먼저 추종자를 모을 명분 혹은 구심점이 필요하고, 다음으로는 목적, 곧 실제로 이룰 무언가가 필요했다. 대체로 바로잡을 부당함을 지닌 자는 잘 싸운다. 그러나 부당함을 채찍으로 삼는 동시에, 눈앞에 영광스러운 결과를 한결같이 그려 보는 자—상처를 어루만질 위안과, 용맹에 대한 보상과, 죽음을 맞을 때의 추억과 감사를 그 결과 속에서 읽어 낼 수 있는 자야말로 한층 더 잘 싸운다.\n\n명분이든 목적이든 그것이 충분한지를 가늠하려면, 벤허는 모든 준비가 갖추어져 행동에 나설 때 의지하게 될 추종자들을 살펴보아야 했다. 지극히 당연하게도, 그들은 그의 동족이었다. 이스라엘의 부당함은 아브라함의 모든 자손에게 닥친 일이었고, 저마다 그것은 더없이 거룩하고 더없이 가슴을 뒤흔드는 명분이었다.\n\n그렇다, 명분은 거기 있었다. 그러나 목적은—그것은 무엇이어야 하는가?\n\n이 계획의 이 갈래에 그가 바친 시간과 나날은 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많았으나—그 모두가 똑같은 결론에 이르렀으니—민족의 자유라는 흐릿하고 막연하고 두루뭉술한 관념이었다. 그것으로 충분한가? 그는 아니라고 말할 수 없었으니, 그리 말하면 그의 희망은 죽고 말 터였다. 또한 그는 그렇다고 말하기를 주저했으니, 그의 판단이 더 나은 것을 가르쳐 주었기 때문이다. 이스라엘이 홀로 로마와 맞서 싸워 이겨 낼 수 있으리라는 것조차 그는 스스로 장담할 수 없었다. 그는 그 거대한 적의 자원을 알았고, 그 자원보다 그 기술이 더 뛰어남을 알았다. 온 세상이 동맹을 맺는다면 충분하겠으나, 아아! 그것은 불가능했다—다만 한 가지 경우는 예외였다. 그리고 그는 그 예외를 두고 얼마나 오래, 얼마나 간절히 생각에 잠겼던가!—고난받는 민족들 가운데 하나에서 한 영웅이 나타나, 전쟁의 승리로 온 땅을 가득 채울 명성을 이룩하는 경우만은 예외였다. 유대가 새로운 알렉산드로스의 마케도니아임을 증명할 수 있다면, 그 영광이 얼마나 크겠는가! 그러나 아아, 다시금! 랍비들 아래서는 용맹은 가능했으나 규율은 가능하지 않았다. 그리고 헤롯의 정원에서 메살라가 던진 비웃음—\"네가 엿새 동안 정복한 것은 이레째에 모조리 잃는다.\"\n\n그리하여 그는 그 틈을 뛰어넘으리라 마음먹고 다가설 때마다 번번이 물러나야 했다. 그리고 그 목표에서 어찌나 끊임없이 좌절했던지, 그는 그것을 거의 운에 맡길 일로 여기고 단념하다시피 했다. 그 영웅이 자기 시대에 나타날 수도, 나타나지 않을 수도 있었다. 오직 하느님만이 아셨다. 그의 마음이 이러했으니, 발타사르의 이야기를 말루크가 뼈대만 간추려 들려준 것이 그에게 미친 영향에 대해서는 굳이 길게 머무를 필요가 없다. 그는 어리둥절할 만큼 흡족한 마음으로 그 이야기를 들었다—여기에 그 고민의 해답이 있다는 느낌, 그토록 필요했던 영웅이 마침내 여기서 발견되었다는 느낌이었다. 더구나 그는 사자 지파의 아들이요, 유대인의 왕이었다! 그 영웅의 뒤에는, 보라! 무장한 온 세상이 있었다.\n\n왕이라 하면 곧 왕국을 뜻했다. 그는 다윗처럼 영광된 전사가 될 것이요, 솔로몬처럼 지혜롭고 위풍당당한 통치자가 될 것이었다. 그 왕국은 로마가 부딪쳐 산산조각 날 하나의 권능이 될 것이었다. 거대한 전쟁이 있을 것이요, 죽음의 고통과 탄생의 진통이 따를 것이며—그런 다음에는 평화, 곧 두말할 것 없이 유대의 영원한 지배가 올 것이었다.\n\n벤허의 가슴은 세차게 뛰었으니, 한순간 그는 세계의 수도가 된 예루살렘과, 만유의 주재자의 보좌가 놓인 시온의 환영을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n이 열광에 빠진 사람에게는, 그 왕을 직접 본 사람이 바로 자기가 찾아가는 천막에 있다는 것이 더없는 행운으로 여겨졌다. 거기서 그를 만나고, 그의 말을 듣고, 다가올 변혁에 관하여 그가 아는 모든 것을, 특히 그 일이 일어날 시기에 관하여 그가 아는 모든 것을 알아낼 수 있을 터였다. 그것이 임박했다면, 막센티우스와의 원정은 포기할 것이며, 그는 가서 지파들을 조직하고 무장시키는 일에 착수하여, 회복의 위대한 날이 동터 오기 시작할 때 이스라엘이 채비를 갖추고 있도록 할 것이었다.\n\n그런데 우리가 이미 보았듯이, 벤허는 발타사르 본인에게서 그 놀라운 이야기를 들었다. 그는 만족했는가?\n\n그의 위에는 야자나무 무리의 그늘보다 더 짙은 그늘이 드리워 있었으니—커다란 불확실성의 그늘이었다. 그리고 그것은—오, 독자여, 새겨 두라!—그것은 왕보다도 왕국에 더 깊이 닿아 있는 그늘이었다.\n\n\"이 왕국이란 어떤 것인가? 또 그것은 무엇이 될 것인가?\" 벤허는 마음속으로 자신에게 물었다.\n\n이렇게 일찍부터, 그 아기를 그의 마지막까지 따라다니고 또 그가 떠난 뒤에도 이 땅에 남게 될 물음들이 솟아났다—그의 시대에는 헤아릴 수 없었고, 우리 시대에는 논쟁거리이며—사람이란 누구나 하나 속에 둘, 곧 죽지 않는 영혼과 죽어야 할 육체임을 깨닫지 못하거나 깨달을 수 없는 모든 이에게는 수수께끼인 물음들이었다.\n\n\"그것은 무엇이 될 것인가?\" 그는 물었다.\n\n오, 독자여, 우리에게는 그 아기 자신이 답해 주었다. 그러나 벤허에게는 오직 발타사르의 말만이 있었을 뿐이니, \"이 땅 위에 있되 이 땅에 속하지 않는 것—사람들을 위함이 아니라 그들의 영혼을 위한 것—그러면서도 상상할 수 없는 영광의 지배\"라는 말이었다.\n\n이 가엾은 젊은이가 그 말들을 한낱 수수께끼를 더 어둡게 만드는 것으로 받아들였다 한들 무엇이 이상하랴?\n\n\"거기에는 사람의 손이 들어설 자리가 없다.\" 그는 절망하여 말했다. \"그런 왕국의 왕에게는 사람이 필요치 않다. 일꾼도, 의논할 신하도, 군사도 필요치 않다. 땅은 멸하거나 새로 지어져야 하고, 다스림에는 새로운 원리가 발견되어야 한다—무장한 손이 아닌 무언가, 힘을 대신할 무언가가. 그런데 그것이 무엇인가?\"\n\n다시금, 오 독자여!\n\n우리가 보려 하지 않는 것을 그는 볼 수 없었다. 사랑 속에 깃든 권능은 아직 어떤 사람의 머릿속에도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 하물며 다스림과 그 목적—평화와 질서—을 위해서는 사랑이 힘보다 더 낫고 더 강하다고 곧바로 말하러 온 이는 더더욱 없었다.\n\n그가 한창 상념에 잠겨 있을 때, 한 손이 그의 어깨 위에 얹혔다.\n\n\"한마디 할 말이 있네, 오, 아리우스의 아들이여.\" 일데림이 그의 곁에 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"한마디만 하고 나는 돌아가야 하네. 밤이 깊어 가니 말일세.\"\n\n\"잘 오셨습니다, 족장님.\"\n\n\"방금 자네가 들은 것들에 대해 말하자면,\" 일데림이 거의 쉬지도 않고 말을 이었다. \"그 아기가 와서 세울 왕국이 어떤 것인가에 관한 부분만 빼고는 모두 믿어 두게나. 그 부분만큼은 시모니데스 상인의 말을 들을 때까지 마음을 비워 두게—여기 안티오크의 선량한 사람이니, 내가 자네에게 소개해 주겠네. 이집트인은 자네에게 제 꿈을 주조하여 내미는데, 그 꿈이란 이 땅에 두기에는 너무 좋은 것일세. 시모니데스는 더 지혜로워, 자네 예언자들의 말씀을 책과 쪽수까지 짚어 가며 또렷이 울려 줄 것이니, 그 아기가 실제로 유대인의 왕이 되리라는 것을—그렇다, 하느님의 광휘에 걸고! 헤롯이 그러했던 것 같은 왕이로되, 다만 더 훌륭하고 훨씬 더 위풍당당한 왕이 되리라는 것을 자네는 부인하지 못할 걸세. 그러고 나면, 두고 보게나, 우리는 복수의 단맛을 맛보게 될 걸세. 할 말은 다 했네. 평안하시게!\"\n\n\"잠깐만요—족장님!\"\n\n일데림이 그 부름을 들었다 해도, 그는 멈추지 않았다.\n\n\"또 시모니데스라!\" 벤허는 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"여기서도 시모니데스, 저기서도 시모니데스. 이쪽에서도 그 사람, 저쪽에서도 그 사람이라니! 나는 아무래도 내 아버지의 종에게 단단히 휘둘리게 생겼구나. 적어도 내 것을 단단히 움켜쥘 줄은 아는 사람이니, 그래서 그가 이집트인보다 더 부유한 것이리라—설령 더 지혜롭지는 않다 하더라도 말이다. 언약에 걸고! 사람이 지킬 만한 믿음을 찾으러 갈 곳은 믿음 없는 자에게가 아니다—나는 가지 않겠다. 그런데, 가만히! 노래로구나—그것도 여인의 목소리—아니면 천사의 목소리인가! 이쪽으로 다가오는구나.\"\n\n호수를 따라 둑 쪽으로 노래를 부르며 한 여인이 다가오고 있었다. 그녀의 목소리는 고요한 물 위를 피리처럼 가락 고르게 떠다니며, 시시각각 점점 커져 갔다. 곧이어 노가 느린 박자로 물에 잠겼다 나오는 소리가 들려왔다. 조금 뒤에는 노랫말이 또렷이 들렸으니—당대의 모든 언어 가운데 격정에 찬 슬픔을 나타내기에 가장 알맞은, 더없이 순수한 그리스어로 된 노랫말이었다.\n\n비탄의 노래. (이집트의 노래.)\n\n나는 노래하며 한숨짓네, 저 옛이야기의 땅을 그리며 시리아의 바다 건너편 그 땅을. 사향내 어린 모래에서 불어오던 향기로운 바람은 내게 생명의 숨결이었네. 바람은 속삭이는 야자나무의 깃털을 어루만지건만 아아, 내게는 이제 없네. 달빛 어린 고요 속에 나일강이 멤피스의 기슭을 지나며 흐느끼던 일도 이제 없네.\n\n오, 닐루스여! 스러져 가는 내 영혼의 신이여! 꿈속에서 그대 내게 찾아오니, 나는 꿈을 꾸며 연꽃 잔을 가지고 놀고, 그대에게 옛 노래를 불러 주네. 멀리서 멤논의 가락을 들으며, 그리운 심벨의 부름을 들으며, 잠에서 깨어나 슬픔과 아픔에 사무치네— 내가 일찍이 작별을 고했음에!\n\n노래가 끝났을 때 노래하던 이는 이미 야자나무 무리를 지나간 뒤였다. 마지막 한마디—작별—이 이별의 온갖 감미로운 슬픔을 가득 싣고 벤허의 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다. 배가 지나가는 것은 마치 더 짙은 그늘 하나가 더 깊은 밤 속으로 지나가는 것과도 같았다.\n\n벤허는 한숨과 거의 분간할 수 없는 긴 숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"노래를 듣고 그녀임을 알겠구나—발타사르의 딸이다. 그 노래는 어찌 그리 아름다웠던가! 또 그녀는 어찌 그리 아름다운가!\"\n\n그는 살짝 내리깐 눈꺼풀에 가려진 그녀의 큰 눈을, 갸름하고 발그레하니 윤기 도는 뺨을, 도톰하고 깊으며 입꼬리에 보조개가 옴폭 패는 입술을, 그리고 늘씬하고 나긋한 자태의 온갖 우아함을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"그녀는 어찌 그리 아름다운가!\" 그는 거듭 말했다.\n\n그러자 그의 심장이 한층 빨라진 고동으로 화답했다.\n\n그러다가 거의 같은 순간에, 또 다른 얼굴 하나가—더 앳되면서도 그에 못지않게 아름답고—그토록 격정적이지는 않더라도 더 어린아이답고 다정한 얼굴이, 마치 호수 밖에서 그에게 들어 올려진 듯 떠올랐다.\n\n\"에스델!\" 그는 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 바라던 대로, 별 하나가 내게 보내졌구나.\"\n\n그는 몸을 돌려 천천히 천막으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n그의 삶은 슬픔과 복수의 준비로 빼곡했다—사랑이 들어설 틈조차 없을 만큼 빼곡했다. 이것이 행복한 변화의 시작인가?\n\n그리고 그 영향이 그와 더불어 천막 안으로 들어갔다면, 그것은 누구의 것이었던가? 에스델이 그에게 잔을 건넸다. 이집트 여인도 그러했다. 그리고 두 사람 모두 야자나무 아래서 같은 시각에 그에게 찾아왔다.\n\n어느 쪽인가?\n\n# BOOK FIFTH\n\n셜리.\n“다툼의 열기 속에서도 율법을 지키며,\n\n고요함 속에 이루어, 미리 본 바를 끝내 보느니라.”\n워즈워스.\n\n궁전 연회장에서 흥청거린 다음 날 아침, 긴 의자는 젊은 귀족들로 뒤덮여 있었다. 막센티우스가 오고, 도시가 그를 맞으러 몰려들지도 모른다. 군단이 무기와 갑옷의 위세를 떨치며 술피우스산에서 내려올지도 모른다. 님파이움에서 옴팔로스까지, 일찍이 이 화려한 동방에서 보거나 들은 그 어떤 광경도 무색하게 할 의식의 장관이 펼쳐질지도 모른다. 그래도 이들 가운데 많은 수는 자기가 쓰러진 자리, 혹은 무심한 노예들이 아무렇게나 굴려 놓은 그 긴 의자 위에서 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 계속 잠들어 있을 터였다. 그들이 그날 영접 행렬에 참여하기를 바라는 것은, 현대 화가의 화실에 놓인 인형이 일어나 모자를 쓰고 깃털을 꽂은 채 왈츠의 하나둘셋 박자를 밟으며 걸어가기를 바라는 것이나 다름없었다.\n\n그러나 그 난잡한 잔치에 끼었던 자가 모두 그토록 수치스러운 꼴은 아니었다. 새벽빛이 연회장 천창으로 스며들기 시작하자, 메살라가 몸을 일으켜 머리에서 화환을 벗었으니, 흥청거림이 끝났다는 표시였다. 그러고는 옷자락을 여미고, 그 자리를 마지막으로 한 번 돌아본 뒤, 한마디 말도 없이 제 거처로 떠났다. 키케로라도 밤새 이어진 원로원 토론에서 그보다 더 무게 있게 물러나지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n세 시간 뒤, 두 명의 전령이 그의 방으로 들어와, 각각 그의 손에서 봉인된 사본 하나씩을 받았다. 그것은 주로 아직 카이사레아에 머물고 있는 총독 발레리우스 그라투스에게 보내는 편지였다. 그 문서를 빠르고 확실하게 전달하는 일에 얼마나 큰 비중이 실려 있었는지는 미루어 짐작할 만하다. 한 전령은 육로로, 다른 한 전령은 바닷길로 가게 되어 있었고, 둘 다 최대한 서둘러야 했다.\n\n이제 독자가 그렇게 보내진 편지의 내용을 빠짐없이 알아 두는 것이 매우 중요하다. 그래서 여기에 그 전문을 싣는다.\n“안티오크, 7월 초하루 열이틀 전.\n\n“메살라가 그라투스에게.\n\n“오, 나의 미다스여!\n\n“이렇게 부르는 것을 부디 언짢게 여기지 마시오. 이는 사랑과 감사의 부름이며, 그대가 사람들 가운데 가장 복받은 자임을 인정하는 것이외다. 또한 그대의 두 귀가 어머니에게서 물려받은 그대로이되, 다만 그대의 무르익은 형편에 걸맞게 자랐음을 인정하는 것이기도 하오.\n\n“오, 나의 미다스여!\n\n“그대에게 놀라운 사건 하나를 전하려 하오. 아직은 다소 추측의 영역에 머물러 있으나, 그대가 당장 깊이 헤아려 마땅한 일임을 나는 의심치 않소.\n\n“먼저 그대의 기억을 되살리게 해 주시오. 여러 해 전, 예루살렘의 어느 귀족 가문을 기억하시오. 믿기 어려울 만큼 유서 깊고 어마어마하게 부유했던 가문, 그 이름은 벤허였소. 만일 그대의 기억에 어떤 절뚝거림이나 병통이 있다면, 내가 잘못 안 것이 아닌 한, 그 일을 되살리는 데 도움이 될 상처 하나가 그대의 머리에 남아 있을 것이오.\n\n“다음으로 그대의 흥미를 일깨워 보겠소. 그대의 목숨을 노린 시도에 대한 징벌로—양심의 안식을 위하여, 그것이 한낱 사고였음이 끝내 밝혀지는 일이 없도록 모든 신들이 막아 주시기를!—그 가문은 붙잡혀 즉각 처리되었고, 그 재산은 몰수되었소. 그리고, 오 나의 미다스여, 그 조치가 슬기로우면서도 공정하셨던 우리 카이사르의 재가를 받았으니—그분의 제단에 영원토록 꽃이 피어나기를!—그 일에서 우리가 각자 거둬들인 액수를 언급하는 데에는 아무런 부끄러움이 없을 것이오. 나로서는 그 일로 그대에게 감사하기를 영영 멈출 수 없으리니, 적어도 지금처럼 내 몫으로 떨어진 것을 끊임없이 누리는 동안에는 분명히 그러할 것이오.\n\n“그대의 지혜를 변호하건대—내가 지금 듣기로는, 내가 그대를 대담하게 견준 저 고르디우스의 아들은 사람들 사이에서도 신들 사이에서도 결코 그런 자질로 이름난 적이 없었다 하오—나는 그대가 허 가문을 어떻게 처분했는지 다시금 떠올리오. 그때 우리 둘 다 그 방책이 우리가 노린 목적, 곧 침묵과 피할 길 없되 자연스러운 죽음으로 넘겨 버리는 데 가장 효과적이라 여겼지요. 그대가 그 죄인의 어미와 누이를 어떻게 했는지 그대는 기억할 것이오. 허나 지금 내가 그들이 살았는지 죽었는지 알고 싶은 마음에 굴복한다 해도, 오 나의 그라투스여, 그대의 온화한 천성을 아는 터라, 그대 못지않게 온화하다 할 만한 이 사람을 그대가 너그러이 용서하리라 믿소.\n\n“그러나 지금의 일에 더 직결되는 것으로서, 나는 그 진짜 죄인이 종신 노예로 갤리선에 보내졌음을 그대의 기억에 불러일으키는 무례를 무릅쓰겠소. 명령이 그러했지요. 그리고 내가 이제 전하려는 사건을 한층 더 놀랍게 만들기 위하여 여기서 말해 두건대, 그자의 몸이 절차에 따라 어느 갤리선을 지휘하는 호민관에게 인도되었다는 영수증을 내가 직접 보고 읽었소.\n\n“이제 그대는 내게 좀 더 각별한 주의를 기울이기 시작해도 좋소, 오 더없이 훌륭한 프리기아 사람이여!\n\n“노 젓는 자의 수명의 한계로 미루어 볼 때, 그렇게 정당하게 처리된 무법자는 이미 죽었어야 마땅하오. 더 잘 말하자면, 삼천의 오케아니데스 가운데 하나가 적어도 다섯 해 전에 그를 지아비로 삼았어야 했소. 그리고, 오 사람들 가운데 가장 덕스럽고 다정한 이여, 한순간의 약한 마음을 부디 너그러이 봐주시오—내가 어린 시절 그를 사랑했고, 또한 그가 무척 잘생긴 까닭에—나는 크게 흠모하며 그를 내 가니메데스라 부르곤 했소—그는 마땅히 그 가문의 가장 아름다운 딸의 품에 안겼어야 옳았소. 그러나 그가 분명 죽었으리라 여겨, 나는 어느 정도 그의 덕에 얻은 재산을 다섯 해 동안 고요하고 떳떳하게 누리며 살아왔소. 내가 빚을 졌음을 인정하되, 그렇다고 그대에 대한 내 의무를 줄이려는 뜻은 아니오.\n\n“이제 바로 그 흥미로운 대목에 이르렀소.\n\n“간밤에, 로마에서 갓 온 한 무리를 위해 내가 연회의 주관자 노릇을 하던 차에—그들의 지나친 젊음과 미숙함이 내 측은지심을 자아냈소—나는 기이한 이야기 하나를 들었소. 그대도 알다시피, 집정관 막센티우스가 오늘 와서 파르티아인을 칠 원정을 지휘할 것이오. 그를 따를 야심가들 가운데 하나가 고(故) 이두정관 퀸투스 아리우스의 아들이오. 나는 그에 대해 특히 자세히 물어볼 일이 있었소. 아리우스가 해적을 추격하러 나섰을 때—그 해적들을 무찌른 일로 그는 마지막 영예를 얻었소—그에게는 가족이 없었소. 그러나 원정에서 돌아왔을 때, 그는 상속자를 하나 데려왔소. 자, 그 많은 세스테르티우스를 현금으로 가진 자답게 부디 침착하시오! 내가 말하는 그 아들이자 상속자가, 바로 그대가 갤리선으로 보낸 자—다섯 해 전에 자기 노 옆에서 죽었어야 마땅한 바로 그 벤허란 말이오—이제 재산과 신분을 갖추고, 어쩌면 로마 시민이 되어 돌아온 것이오, 그리하여— 흠, 그대야 자리가 워낙 굳건하니 놀랄 것이 없겠으나, 나는, 오 나의 미다스여, 나는 위태롭소—무엇 때문인지는 굳이 그대에게 말할 필요가 없을 것이오. 그대가 모른다면 누가 알겠소?\n\n“이 모든 것에 대해 그대는 ‘쯧쯧’이라 하겠소?\n\n“아리우스가, 그러니까 가장 아름다운 오케아니데스의 품에서 나타난 이 환영의 양아버지인 그자가(그녀가 어떠해야 했는지에 대한 내 견해는 위를 보시오) 해적과 맞붙어 싸웠을 때, 그의 배는 가라앉았고, 온 선원 가운데 단 둘만이 익사를 면했소—아리우스 본인과 그의 상속자인 이 한 사람이오.\n\n“그들이 떠다니던 널빤지에서 그 둘을 건져 올린 장교들의 말로는, 운 좋은 호민관의 동료는 갑판에 끌어올렸을 때 갤리선 노예의 차림을 한 젊은이였다 하오.\n\n“이쯤이면 적어도 설득력이 있을 것이오. 그러나 그대가 또다시 ‘쯧쯧’ 할까 하여 말하는데, 오 나의 미다스여, 어제, 다행스러운 우연으로—그 덕에 나는 운명의 여신에게 한 가지 서원을 했소—나는 그 수수께끼 같은 아리우스의 아들과 얼굴을 맞대고 마주쳤소. 그리고 이제 분명히 말하건대, 비록 그때는 알아보지 못했으나 그자는 여러 해 동안 나의 놀이 동무였던 바로 그 벤허였소. 만약 그가 사내라면, 비록 가장 천한 부류일지라도, 내가 이 글을 쓰는 바로 이 순간에도 틀림없이 복수를 골똘히 생각하고 있을 그 벤허란 말이오—내가 그였다면 분명 그러했을 테니—목숨을 거두기 전에는 결코 채워지지 않을 복수, 조국을 위한, 어미를 위한, 누이를 위한, 자기 자신을 위한, 그리고—맨 마지막에 말하지만 그대는 첫째라 여길지도 모를—잃어버린 재산을 위한 복수를 말이오.\n\n“이쯤이면, 오 나의 선한 은인이자 벗인 그라투스여, 위태로워진 그대의 세스테르티우스를 생각해 보면—그것을 잃는 것이야말로 그대처럼 높은 신분을 가진 자에게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 일일 테니—나는 그대를 저 어리석은 프리기아의 늙은 왕에 빗대 부르기를 그만두겠소—이쯤이면, 다시 말해(여기까지 나를 읽고 나면) 그대가 ‘쯧쯧’ 소리를 멈추고, 이런 위급한 상황에서 무엇을 해야 할지 헤아릴 채비를 마쳤으리라 나는 믿어 의심치 않소.\n\n“이제 그대에게 어찌해야 하느냐고 묻는 것은 천박한 일일 것이오. 차라리 내가 그대의 의뢰인이라 말하겠소. 아니, 더 낫게 말하자면, 그대는 나에게 옳은 길을 일러 주는 것이 소임인 나의 오디세우스요.\n\n“이 편지가 그대의 손에 쥐어질 때의 그대를 떠올리며 나는 즐거워하오. 그대가 그것을 한 번 읽는 것을 나는 보오. 그대의 얼굴이 온통 진지함으로 가득하다가, 다시 한 번 읽을 때는 미소를 띠는 것을 보오. 그러고는 망설임이 끝나고 판단이 서면, 이리하라, 혹은 저리하라 하겠지요. 메르쿠리우스 같은 지혜와 카이사르 같은 결단으로 말이오.\n\n“이제 해가 제법 솟았소. 한 시간 뒤면 두 전령이 각자 이것의 봉인된 사본을 들고 내 문을 나설 것이오. 하나는 육로로, 다른 하나는 바닷길로 갈 것이니, 우리의 이 로마 세계 한구석에 우리의 적이 나타났음을 그대가 일찍, 그리고 각별히 알아야 한다고 내가 그만큼 중대하게 여기는 까닭이오.\n\n“나는 여기서 그대의 답을 기다리겠소.\n\n“벤허의 오고 감은 물론 그의 주인인 집정관이 좌우할 것이오. 집정관은 밤낮없이 쉬지 않고 애를 쓴다 해도 한 달 안에는 떠날 수 없을 것이오. 황량하고 도시 하나 없는 땅에서 작전을 펼칠 군대를 모으고 채비를 갖추는 일이 얼마나 큰 작업인지는 그대도 알 것이오.\n\n“나는 어제 다프네 숲에서 그 유대인을 보았소. 그가 지금 거기에 없다 해도, 분명 그 근방에 있어 내가 그를 눈에 담아 두기 쉬울 것이오. 정녕 그대가 지금 그가 어디 있느냐고 내게 묻는다면, 나는 더없이 확신에 차 말하겠소. 그는 저 오래된 종려 과수원에서, 우리의 강한 손길을 오래 피할 수 없을 반역자 셰이크 일데림의 천막 아래에 있다고 말이오. 막센티우스가 첫 조치로 그 아랍인을 배에 실어 로마로 보낸다 한들 놀라지 마시오.\n\n“내가 그 유대인의 행방을 이토록 자세히 따지는 까닭은, 오 빛나는 이여, 그대가 무엇을 해야 할지 헤아릴 때 그것이 그대에게 긴요할 것이기 때문이오. 이미 나는 알고 있고, 그 앎으로써 내 지혜가 자라고 있다고 자부하건대, 사람의 행위가 얽힌 모든 책략에는 언제나 고려해야 할 세 가지 요소가 있소—때와 장소와 수단이 그것이오.\n\n“만약 그대가 여기가 바로 그 장소라 한다면, 그 일을 그대를 더없이 아끼는 벗에게 맡기는 데 조금도 주저하지 마시오. 그 벗은 또한 그대의 가장 영민한 제자가 될 것이오.\n메살라.”\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n메살라의 집 앞에서 급사들이 전갈을 들고 떠날 무렵—아직 이른 아침이었다—벤허는 일데림의 천막으로 들어섰다. 그는 호수에 몸을 담갔다가 아침을 먹은 뒤였고, 지금은 소매 없는 속옷 차림에 자락이 무릎에도 닿지 않는 옷을 걸치고 있었다.\n\n족장은 긴 의자에 앉아 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"그대에게 평안이 있기를, 아리우스의 아들이여.\" 그는 감탄을 담아 말했다. 사실 그는 이토록 완벽하게 빛나고 강건하며 자신만만한 사내다움의 본보기를 본 적이 없었다. \"그대에게 평안과 호의를 베푸노라. 말들도 준비되었고, 나도 준비되었다. 그대는 어떤가?\"\n\n\"그대가 베푼 평안을, 어진 족장이여, 나도 그대에게 돌려드리겠소. 그토록 큰 호의에 감사하오. 나도 준비되었소.\"\n\n일데림이 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n\"말들을 데려오게 하겠다. 앉으라.\"\n\n\"멍에는 메웠소?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 내 손으로 직접 하게 해 주시오.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"내가 그대의 아랍 말들과 친해질 필요가 있소. 한 마리 한 마리에게 말을 걸 수 있도록 이름으로 알아 두어야 하오, 족장이여. 또한 그 못지않게 그들의 성미도 알아야 하니, 말도 사람과 같기 때문이오. 대담한 말이면 꾸짖는 편이 낫고, 겁이 많은 말이면 칭찬하고 추어 주는 편이 낫소. 하인들에게 마구를 가져오라 해 주시오.\"\n\n\"전차는?\" 족장이 물었다.\n\n\"오늘은 전차를 그냥 두겠소. 그 대신, 가지고 있다면 다섯째 말을 데려오게 해 주시오. 안장을 얹지 않은 채, 다른 말들 못지않게 날쌘 놈이어야 하오.\"\n\n일데림의 호기심이 일었고, 그는 곧장 하인을 불렀다.\n\n\"네 마리분 마구를 가져오라 일러라.\" 그가 말했다. \"네 마리분 마구와, 시리우스의 굴레를 말이다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 일데림이 일어섰다.\n\n\"시리우스는 나의 사랑이요, 나는 그의 사랑이라네, 아리우스의 아들이여. 우리는 스무 해 동안 동지였지—천막에서, 전투에서, 사막의 온갖 고비마다 우리는 동지였다네. 그를 그대에게 보여 주겠다.\"\n\n칸막이 휘장으로 가더니 그는 그것을 들어 올렸고, 벤허는 그 아래로 지나갔다. 말들이 한꺼번에 그에게 다가왔다. 그중 한 마리는 작은 머리에 빛나는 눈, 휘어진 활의 한 도막 같은 목, 그리고 처녀의 머릿단처럼 부드럽고 물결치는 갈기가 두텁게 드리운 강건한 가슴을 지녔는데, 그를 보자 나직하고 반가운 울음소리를 냈다.\n\n\"착한 말이다.\" 족장은 짙은 갈색 뺨을 토닥이며 말했다. \"착한 말, 잘 잤느냐.\" 그러고는 벤허를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"이놈이 시리우스, 여기 네 마리의 아비라네. 어미인 미라는 우리가 돌아오기를 기다리고 있으니, 너무 귀해서 나보다 강한 손이 있는 곳에 위험을 무릅쓰게 할 수 없기 때문이지. 그리고 나는 의심스럽다네.\" 그는 말하면서 웃었다. \"참으로 의심스럽다네, 아리우스의 아들이여, 부족이 그녀의 부재를 견딜 수 있을지 말이야. 그녀는 그들의 자랑이요, 그들은 그녀를 떠받든다네. 그녀가 그들 위로 내달린다 해도 그들은 웃을 걸세. 오늘 만 명의 기마병, 사막의 아들들이 물을 거야. '미라의 소식을 들었는가?' 그리고 '잘 있다네'라는 대답에 그들은 '하느님은 선하시도다! 하느님께 복이 있기를!' 하고 말할 거라네.\"\n\n\"미라—시리우스—별 이름들이지요, 그렇지 않소, 족장이여?\" 벤허가 네 마리 말과 그 아비에게 차례로 다가가 손을 내밀며 물었다.\n\n\"아니 될 것이 무엇인가?\" 일데림이 대답했다. \"그대는 밤에 사막에 나가 본 적이 있는가?\"\n\n\"없소.\"\n\n\"그러면 우리 아랍인이 별에 얼마나 기대고 사는지 모를 걸세. 우리는 고마운 마음으로 그 이름을 빌려다가, 사랑으로 그것을 붙여 준다네. 내 선조들도 모두 저마다의 미라를 두었으니, 내가 나의 미라를 둔 것과 같고, 이 자식들 또한 별이나 다름없지. 저기, 보게나, 저놈이 리겔이고, 저놈이 안타레스라네. 저것은 아타이르, 그리고 그대가 지금 다가가는 놈은 알데바란, 한배에서 가장 어린 놈이지만, 그렇다고 못한 것은 아니야—아니, 결코 아니지! 바람을 거슬러도 그놈은 그대 귀에 아카바처럼 울부짖을 때까지 그대를 실어 갈 걸세. 그리고 그대가 이르는 곳이면 어디든 가리라, 아리우스의 아들이여—그렇다네, 솔로몬의 영광에 걸고! 그대가 그만한 용기만 있다면, 그놈은 그대를 사자의 아가리로도 데려갈 걸세.\"\n\n마구가 들려 왔다. 벤허는 제 손으로 말들에게 마구를 채웠고, 제 손으로 그들을 천막 밖으로 끌어내어 거기서 고삐를 매었다.\n\n\"시리우스를 데려와 주시오.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n아랍인이라도 그보다 더 능숙하게 준마의 등에 뛰어오르지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"이제 고삐를 주시오.\"\n\n고삐가 그에게 건네졌고, 그는 조심스레 그것들을 갈라 잡았다.\n\n\"어진 족장이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 준비되었소. 길잡이 하나를 앞세워 벌판으로 보내 주시고, 그대의 사람 몇을 시켜 물을 가져오게 해 주시오.\"\n\n출발에는 아무런 어려움이 없었다. 말들은 두려워하지 않았다. 벌써 그들과 새 마부 사이에는 무언의 이해가 자리 잡은 듯했다. 마부는 침착하게, 언제나 신뢰를 낳는 그 신뢰로 제 몫을 해낸 터였다. 나아가는 방식은 벤허가 전차에 서는 대신 시리우스에 올라탔다는 것만 다를 뿐, 전차를 모는 것과 똑같았다. 일데림의 기운이 솟구쳤다. 그는 수염을 빗어 내리며 흡족하게 미소 짓고는 중얼거렸다. \"저자는 로마인이 아니야, 아니지, 하느님의 광휘에 걸고!\" 그는 걸어서 뒤를 따랐고, 두와르의 온 식솔들—남자와 여자와 아이들—이 그의 뒤로 쏟아져 나오며, 신뢰까지는 아니어도 그 노심초사만큼은 다 함께 나누었다.\n\n벌판에 이르고 보니, 그곳은 넓고도 훈련에 알맞은 곳이었다. 벤허는 곧장 훈련을 시작하여, 처음에는 네 마리를 천천히, 일직선으로 몰았다가, 이어 넓은 원을 그리며 몰았다. 한 단계 더 나아가 그는 다음으로 그들을 속보로 몰았고, 다시 한 걸음 나아가 구보로 밀어붙였다. 마침내 그는 원을 좁혔고, 더 나중에는 이리저리, 오른쪽으로, 왼쪽으로, 앞으로, 끊임없이 변화무쌍하게 몰았다. 그렇게 한 시간이 흘렀다. 걸음을 늦추어 보통 걸음으로 만든 뒤, 그는 일데림에게로 몰고 갔다.\n\n\"훈련은 끝났소. 이제 연습만 남았소.\" 그가 말했다. \"기쁘게 여기시오, 일데림 족장이여, 그대가 이런 종복들을 거느렸으니. 보시오.\" 그는 말에서 내려 말들에게 다가가며 말을 이었다. \"보시오, 붉은 털의 윤기에는 티 하나 없소. 내가 시작했을 때처럼 숨을 가볍게 쉬고 있소. 그대에게 크나큰 기쁨을 안기겠소. 그리고 어려운 일이 있다면 다만—\" 그는 번득이는 눈을 노인의 얼굴로 돌렸다. \"—우리가 승리를 거두지 못하고 우리의—\"\n\n그는 멈추었고, 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 숙였다. 그제야 그는 족장의 곁에, 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 발타사르와, 베일을 깊이 두른 두 여인이 있는 것을 처음으로 알아차렸다. 그 가운데 한 여인을 그는 다시 한번 바라보며, 가슴이 두근거리는 가운데 속으로 말했다. \"그녀다—이집트 여인이다!\" 일데림이 끊긴 그의 말을 이어받았다.\n\n\"승리, 그리고 우리의 복수 말이지!\" 그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"나는 두렵지 않다. 나는 기쁘다. 아리우스의 아들이여, 그대야말로 적임자다. 끝맺음이 시작과 같다면, 그대는 베풀 줄 아는 아랍인의 손바닥 안쪽이 어떤 것으로 채워져 있는지 보게 될 것이다.\"\n\n\"감사하오, 어진 족장이여.\" 벤허는 겸손하게 대답했다. \"하인들에게 말들이 마실 물을 가져오게 해 주시오.\"\n\n그는 제 손으로 물을 먹였다.\n\n다시 시리우스에 올라타고 그는 훈련을 새로 시작하여, 앞서처럼 보통 걸음에서 속보로, 속보에서 구보로 나아갔다. 마침내 그는 한결같은 준마들을 질주로 밀어붙이며, 차츰 속도를 최고로 끌어올렸다. 그러자 그 광경은 흥미진진해졌다. 고삐를 다루는 섬세한 솜씨에 갈채가 쏟아졌고, 앞으로 날아가든 갖가지 곡선으로 돌아가든 한결같은 네 마리에 감탄이 일었다. 그들의 움직임에는 통일과 힘과 우아함과 즐거움이 있었으니, 그 모두가 애쓰는 기색이나 노고의 흔적 하나 없었다. 그 감탄에는 연민도 책망도 섞이지 않았으니, 그런 것은 차라리 저녁 하늘을 나는 제비들에게나 바칠 것이었다.\n\n이 훈련이 한창이고 둘러선 모든 이가 그것에 마음을 빼앗긴 그때, 말루크가 족장을 찾아 그 자리에 나타났다.\n\n\"그대에게 전할 말이 있소, 족장이여.\" 그는 말을 꺼내기에 알맞다 여긴 순간을 틈타 말했다. \"상인 시모니데스가 보낸 전갈이오.\"\n\n\"시모니데스라!\" 아랍인이 외쳤다. \"아! 잘되었구나. 아바돈이 그의 적들을 모조리 잡아가기를!\"\n\n\"그분께서는 먼저 하느님의 거룩한 평안을 그대에게 전하라 하셨소.\" 말루크가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 이 전갈을 드리니, 받는 즉시 읽으시기를 청하셨소.\"\n\n일데림은 제자리에 선 채, 건네받은 꾸러미의 봉인을 뜯고, 고운 아마포 싸개에서 편지 두 통을 꺼내어 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n[첫째 편지]\n\n\"시모니데스가 일데림 족장에게.\n\n오, 벗이여!\n\n먼저 그대가 내 마음 깊은 곳에 한자리를 차지하고 있음을 믿으시오.\n\n그리고—\n\n그대의 두와르에 풍채가 훌륭한 한 젊은이가 있으니, 스스로를 아리우스의 아들이라 부르오. 양자로서 과연 그러하오.\n\n그는 내게 더없이 소중한 사람이오.\n\n그에게는 놀라운 내력이 있어, 내가 그대에게 들려주려 하오. 오늘이든 내일이든 내게 오시오, 그 내력을 들려주고 그대의 조언을 구하고자 하오.\n\n그동안에는 그의 모든 청을 들어주시오, 다만 명예에 어긋나지 않는 한에서요. 보상이 필요할 일이 생기거든, 그것은 내가 그대에게 갚을 책임이 있소.\n\n내가 이 젊은이에게 관심을 두고 있다는 것은, 그대만 알고 비밀로 해 주시오.\n\n그대의 다른 손님에게도 안부를 전해 주시오. 그와 그의 딸, 그대 자신, 그리고 그대가 일행으로 택할 모든 이는 경기가 열리는 날 원형경기장에서 나를 믿고 의지하시오. 좌석은 이미 잡아 두었소.\n\n그대와 그대의 모든 사람에게 평안이 있기를.\n\n오, 나의 벗이여, 내가 그대의 벗이 아니고 무엇이겠소?\n시모니데스.\"\n\n[둘째 편지]\n\n\"시모니데스가 일데림 족장에게.\n\n오, 벗이여!\n\n내 풍부한 경험에서 한마디 보내오.\n\n로마인이 아니면서 재물이나 물건을 약탈당할 처지에 있는 모든 사람이 경고로 받아들이는 징조가 하나 있으니, 곧 권력의 자리에 권한을 위임받은 어느 고위 로마 관리가 도착하는 일이오.\n\n오늘 막센티우스 집정관이 오오.\n\n경계하시오!\n\n또 한 가지 충고가 있소.\n\n그대를 노린 음모가 효력을 거두려면, 오, 벗이여, 반드시 헤롯 일족이 끼어 있어야 하오. 그대는 그들의 영지에 막대한 재산을 두고 있기 때문이오.\n\n그러니 경계를 늦추지 마시오.\n\n오늘 아침 안티오크에서 남쪽으로 뻗은 길들을 지키는 그대의 믿음직한 자들에게 사람을 보내, 오가는 모든 급사를 샅샅이 살피라 이르시오. 그대나 그대의 일에 관한 사사로운 전갈을 그들이 찾아내거든, 그대가 그것을 보아야 하오.\n\n그대는 이것을 어제 받았어야 했으나, 신속히 움직인다면 아직 늦지는 않았소.\n\n오늘 아침 안티오크에서 급사들이 떠났더라도, 그대의 전령들은 샛길을 알 터이니, 그대의 명령을 들고 그들을 앞지를 수 있소.\n\n망설이지 마시오.\n\n읽은 뒤에는 이것을 태우시오.\n\n오, 나의 벗이여! 그대의 벗,\n\n시모니데스.\"\n일데림은 편지를 다시 한번 읽고, 아마포 싸개에 도로 접어 넣은 뒤, 그 꾸러미를 허리띠 밑에 끼웠다.\n\n벌판에서의 훈련은 잠시 더 이어졌으니—통틀어 두 시간쯤이었다. 끝낼 무렵 벤허는 네 마리를 보통 걸음으로 늦추어 일데림에게로 몰고 갔다.\n\n\"허락하신다면, 족장이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"그대의 아랍 말들을 천막으로 돌려보내고, 오늘 오후에 다시 끌어내겠소.\"\n\n일데림은 시리우스에 올라앉은 그에게 걸어와 말했다. \"그들을 그대에게 맡기겠다, 아리우스의 아들이여. 경기가 끝날 때까지 그대 뜻대로 다루라. 그대는 그 로마인—그놈의 뼈를 자칼들이 살점 하나 없이 갉아 먹기를!—이 그만한 주(週)를 들여도 못 한 일을 두 시간 만에 해냈다. 우리는 이길 것이다—하느님의 광휘에 걸고, 우리는 이길 것이다!\"\n\n천막에 이르러 벤허는 말들이 보살핌을 받는 동안 곁에 머물렀다. 그런 뒤 호수에 몸을 담그고, 기운이 임금처럼 한껏 넘쳐흐르는 족장과 더불어 아라크 한 잔을 들고는, 다시 유대인의 옷차림으로 갈아입고서 말루크와 함께 과수원 안으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n두 사람 사이에는 많은 이야기가 오갔으나, 모두가 중요한 것은 아니었다. 그러나 그 가운데 한 대목만은 놓쳐서는 안 된다. 벤허가 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"그대에게 증서를 하나 드리겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"셀레우키아 다리 이쪽, 강가 여관에 보관해 둔 내 물건에 대한 증서요. 가능하다면 오늘 내게 가져다주시오. 그리고, 어진 말루크여—내가 그대에게 너무 무거운 짐을 지우는 것이 아니라면—\"\n\n말루크는 기꺼이 봉사하겠노라고 진심으로 다짐했다.\n\n\"고맙소, 말루크, 고맙소.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"그 말을 믿고 그대에게 부탁하겠소. 우리가 같은 옛 부족의 형제이고, 적은 로마인임을 떠올리면서 말이오. 그러면 먼저—그대는 사업하는 사람이니, 일데림 족장은 그렇지 못한 듯해 몹시 걱정스럽소만—\"\n\n\"아랍인은 좀처럼 그렇지 못하지요.\" 말루크가 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그들의 영민함을 탓하는 것은 아니오, 말루크. 그래도 그들을 살펴 주는 편이 좋소. 경주와 관련하여 위약금이나 차질이 생길 일을 모두 막으려면, 그대가 원형경기장 사무소에 가서 그가 모든 예비 규정을 따랐는지 확인해 준다면 나는 더없이 마음을 놓을 수 있겠소. 그리고 규정 사본을 한 부 얻어 줄 수 있다면, 그 도움은 내게 크게 쓸모가 있을 것이오. 내가 어떤 색깔을 입어야 하는지, 그리고 특히 출발할 때 내가 차지할 칸이 몇 번인지 알고 싶소. 그것이 메살라의 칸 오른쪽이나 왼쪽 바로 옆이라면 좋겠고, 그렇지 않다면, 그대가 그것을 바꾸어 나를 그 로마인 바로 옆에 오게 할 수 있다면 그리해 주시오. 그대는 기억력이 좋소, 말루크?\"\n\n\"예전엔 흐려진 적도 있으나, 아리우스의 아들이여, 지금처럼 마음이 받쳐 줄 때는 결코 그렇지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그대에게 한 가지 더 부탁해 보겠소. 어제 보니 메살라가 제 전차를 자랑스러워하더이다. 그럴 만도 했소, 카이사르의 최고급 전차로도 그것을 넘어서기 어려울 테니. 그 자랑거리를 구실 삼아 그것이 가벼운지 무거운지 알아낼 수 없겠소? 그 정확한 무게와 치수를 알고 싶소—그리고, 말루크, 다른 것은 다 놓치더라도, 그 차축이 땅 위로 얼마나 높이 서 있는지만은 정확히 알아 와 주시오. 알겠소, 말루크? 나는 그가 나보다 실제로 유리한 점을 가지는 것을 원치 않소. 그의 호화로움에는 관심 없소. 내가 그를 이긴다면, 그의 몰락은 더욱 호되고, 나의 승리는 더욱 완전해질 것이오. 정말로 중요한 이점들이 있다면, 그것을 나도 갖고 싶소.\"\n\n\"알겠소, 알겠소!\" 말루크가 말했다. \"차축 한가운데서 내려뜨린 수직선, 그것이 그대가 원하는 것이군요.\"\n\n\"바로 그것이오. 그리고 기뻐하시오, 말루크—이것이 내 마지막 부탁이오. 두와르로 돌아갑시다.\"\n\n천막 문 앞에 이르니 한 하인이 갓 만든 레벤을 연기에 그을린 병에 채우고 있어, 그들은 걸음을 멈추고 목을 축였다. 얼마 뒤 말루크는 도시로 돌아갔다.\n\n그들이 자리를 비운 사이, 시모니데스가 일러 준 대로 명령을 받은 사자 하나가 좋은 말에 올라 떠난 뒤였다. 그는 아랍인이었고, 문서로 쓴 것은 아무것도 지니지 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n“발타사르의 딸 이라스 님이 인사와 함께 전언을 전하라 하셨습니다.” 천막에서 한가로이 쉬고 있던 벤허에게 하인이 말했다.\n\n“전언을 말해 보아라.”\n\n“호수에 함께 나가 주시겠는지 여쭈라 하셨습니다.”\n\n“답은 내가 직접 전하겠다. 그렇게 일러라.”\n\n신발을 가져오게 하고, 벤허는 몇 분 뒤 아름다운 이집트 여인을 찾아 나섰다. 산그늘이 밤보다 앞서 종려나무 동산 위로 기어들고 있었다. 멀리 나무들 사이로 양 떼의 방울 소리, 소들의 울음소리, 그리고 가축을 집으로 몰아오는 목동들의 목소리가 딸랑딸랑 들려왔다. 기억해 둘 것은, 이 동산의 삶이 모든 면에서 사막의 메마른 목초지에서의 삶 못지않게 목가적이었다는 사실이다.\n\n일데림 족장은 오후의 훈련을 지켜보았는데, 그것은 아침에 했던 것을 되풀이한 것이었다. 그 뒤 그는 시모니데스의 초대에 응하여 도시로 떠났다. 밤에 돌아올 수도 있었으나, 벗과 나눌 이야기가 워낙 방대한 것을 생각하면 그러기는 어려웠다. 그리하여 홀로 남은 벤허는 말들을 돌보게 하고, 호수에서 몸을 식히고 씻은 뒤, 순수한 혈통의 사두개인답게 들판에서 입던 옷을 평소의 온통 흰 옷으로 갈아입고, 일찍 저녁을 먹었다. 그리고 젊음의 힘 덕분에, 격렬하게 치른 노고에서 거뜬히 회복되어 있었다.\n\n아름다움을 하나의 성질로 보아 깎아내리는 것은 슬기롭지도 정직하지도 않은 일이다. 그 영향에 무감각한 세련된 영혼이란 있을 수 없다. 피그말리온과 그의 조각상 이야기는 시적인 만큼이나 자연스럽다. 아름다움은 그 자체로 하나의 힘이다. 그리고 그 힘이 지금 벤허를 끌어당기고 있었다.\n\n이집트 여인은 그에게 놀랍도록 아름다운 여자였으니, 얼굴도 아름답고 자태도 아름다웠다. 그의 생각 속에서 그녀는 언제나 샘가에서 보았던 모습 그대로 나타났다. 그는 그녀의 목소리가 풍기는 힘을 느꼈으니, 자신에게 눈물 어린 감사를 표하던 까닭에 그 목소리는 더욱 감미로웠다. 또한 그녀의 눈이 풍기는 힘도 느꼈으니, 그 종족을 드러내는 크고 부드러운 검은 아몬드 모양의 눈은 어떤 풍성한 말로도 다 담을 수 없는 것을 말하고 있었다. 그녀에 대한 생각이 거듭 떠오를 때마다, 크고 가냘프며 우아하고 세련된, 화려하고 하늘하늘 흘러내리는 옷자락에 감싸인 그 자태가 그만큼 자주 되살아났다. 그 자태에 모자란 것은 오직 어울리는 마음씨 하나뿐이었으니, 그것만 갖추면 그녀는 술람미 여인처럼, 그리고 그와 똑같은 뜻에서, 깃발을 든 군대처럼 두려운 존재가 되었을 것이다. 다시 말해, 그녀가 그의 공상 속으로 되돌아올 때면, 솔로몬의 정열적인 노래 전부가 그녀와 함께, 그녀의 존재에 고무되어 따라왔다. 이런 감정과 이런 느낌을 품은 채, 그는 그녀가 과연 그것을 정당화해 주는지 보러 가는 길이었다. 그를 데려가는 것은 사랑이 아니라, 사랑의 선구자일지도 모를 찬탄과 호기심이었다.\n\n선착장은 짧은 계단과 등잔 기둥 몇 개로 꾸며진 단(壇)으로 이루어진, 단출한 것이었다. 그런데도 계단 꼭대기에서 그는 눈에 들어온 광경에 사로잡혀 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n맑은 물 위에 작은 거룻배 한 척이 달걀 껍데기처럼 가볍게 떠 있었다. 카스탈리아 샘가에서 보았던 그 낙타몰이꾼 에티오피아인이 노 젓는 자리를 차지하고 있었는데, 번쩍이는 흰 제복 때문에 그 검은 빛깔이 한층 짙어 보였다. 배의 고물 쪽은 온통 티로스 진홍빛으로 눈부신 천을 덧대어 방석과 양탄자를 깔아 두었다. 키잡이 자리에는 이집트 여인이 몸소 앉아, 인도산 숄과 더없이 섬세한 너울과 목도리의 안개 속에 묻혀 있었다. 그녀의 두 팔은 어깨까지 드러나 있었는데, 그저 흠 없는 모양일 뿐 아니라, 그 자태와 그 움직임과 그 표정으로 시선을 사로잡는 힘이 있었다. 손도, 심지어 손가락마저도 우아함과 의미를 부여받은 듯했다. 그 하나하나가 아름다움의 대상이었다. 어깨와 목은 넉넉한 목도리로 저녁 공기를 막았으나, 그렇다고 그것을 가리지는 못했다.\n\n그녀에게 던진 눈길 속에서 벤허는 이런 세세한 것들에 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그저 하나의 인상이 그에게 새겨졌을 뿐이었다. 강한 빛이 그러하듯, 그것은 하나의 감각이었지, 보거나 헤아릴 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 네 입술은 홍색 실 같고, 네 머리채 속 두 뺨은 석류 한 쪽 같구나. 일어나라, 나의 사랑, 나의 어여쁜 자여, 어서 오라. 보라! 겨울도 지나고, 비도 그쳐 갔으며, 땅에는 꽃이 피고, 새들의 노래할 때가 이르렀으니, 산비둘기의 소리가 우리 땅에 들리는구나. 그녀가 그에게 새긴 인상은 말로 옮기면 그러했다.\n\n“오세요.” 그가 멈춰 선 것을 보고 그녀가 말했다. “오세요, 그러지 않으면 당신을 형편없는 뱃사람으로 여기겠어요.”\n\n그의 뺨에 붉은 기운이 짙어졌다. 그녀가 그의 바다 위에서의 삶을 무엇이든 알고 있는 걸까? 그는 곧장 단으로 내려섰다.\n\n“두려웠소.” 그녀 앞의 빈자리에 앉으며 그가 말했다.\n\n“무엇이요?”\n\n“배를 가라앉힐까 봐.” 그가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n“더 깊은 물에 닿을 때까지 기다리세요.” 그녀는 그렇게 말하며 흑인에게 신호를 보냈고, 그가 노를 담그자 배는 출발했다.\n\n사랑과 벤허가 서로 적이었다면, 벤허는 그 어느 때보다 사랑의 처분에 내맡겨져 있었다. 이집트 여인은 그가 보지 않을 수 없는 자리에 앉아 있었다. 이미 기억 속에서 그가 술람미 여인의 이상으로 몰두해 둔 바로 그 여인이었다. 그녀의 눈이 그의 눈에 빛을 주니, 별들이 떠오른들 그는 보지 못했을 것이다. 실제로 별들이 떠올랐다. 다른 모든 곳에는 가시지 않는 어둠과 함께 밤이 내릴지 몰라도, 그녀의 눈길이 그를 위해 빛을 밝혀 줄 터였다. 그리고 누구나 알다시피, 젊음과 이런 동행이 주어졌을 때, 여름의 온기를 머금은 고요한 밤하늘 아래 잔잔한 물 위만큼 공상이 온전히 사람을 사로잡는 상황은 없다. 그런 때면 평범함에서 이상(理想)으로 자기도 모르게 미끄러져 들기란 그토록 쉬운 일이다.\n\n“키를 내게 주시오.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아니에요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “그러면 관계가 뒤바뀌어요. 제가 함께 타자고 청하지 않았나요? 저는 당신께 빚이 있고, 갚기 시작하려는 거예요. 당신이 말하고 제가 듣거나, 제가 말하고 당신이 듣거나, 그 선택은 당신 몫이에요. 하지만 우리가 어디로 가는지, 또 그리로 가는 길은 제가 정할 거예요.”\n\n“그럼 그곳이 어디요?”\n\n“또 놀라시는군요.”\n\n“오, 아름다운 이집트 여인이여, 나는 그저 모든 포로가 던지는 첫 질문을 했을 뿐이오.”\n\n“저를 이집트라고 부르세요.”\n\n“차라리 이라스라고 부르고 싶소.”\n\n“그 이름으로 저를 생각하셔도 좋아요, 하지만 부르기는 이집트라고 하세요.”\n\n“이집트는 한 나라이고, 수많은 백성을 뜻하는데.”\n\n“그래요, 그래요! 그것도 그런 나라죠!”\n\n“알겠소. 우리가 가는 곳이 이집트로군.”\n\n“그랬으면 좋겠네요! 그러면 정말 기쁠 텐데.”\n\n그녀는 말하며 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n“그럼 당신은 내게 마음을 쓰지 않는구려.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아, 그 말로 당신이 거기 한 번도 가 본 적 없다는 걸 알겠어요.”\n\n“한 번도 가 본 적 없소.”\n“오, 그곳은 불행한 사람이 하나도 없는 땅, 세상 모든 다른 곳이 부러워하는 땅, 모든 신의 어머니, 그래서 더없이 복받은 땅이에요. 거기서는, 오 아리우스의 아들이여, 행복한 자는 행복이 더해지고, 비참한 자는 거기 가서 거룩한 강의 단물을 한 번 마시기만 하면 아이처럼 기뻐하며 웃고 노래해요.”\n\n“거기라고 더없이 가난한 자들이 다른 곳처럼 없는 건 아니지 않소?”\n\n“이집트에서 더없이 가난한 자들은 바라는 것도 사는 모양도 더없이 소박한 사람들이에요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “그들은 충분한 것 이상을 바라지 않는데, 그게 얼마나 작은 것인지는 그리스인이나 로마인은 알 수 없어요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 그리스인도 로마인도 아니오.”\n\n그녀가 웃었다.\n\n“제게 장미 정원이 하나 있는데, 그 한가운데 나무 한 그루가 있고, 그 꽃이 모든 것 가운데 가장 화사해요. 그게 어디서 왔을 것 같으세요?”\n\n“장미의 고향, 페르시아에서.”\n“아니에요.”\n\n“그럼 인도에서.”\n\n“아니에요.”\n“아! 그리스의 어느 섬에서.”\n\n“말씀드리죠.” 그녀가 말했다. “한 나그네가 르바임 평원의 길가에서 시들어 가는 그것을 발견했어요.”\n\n“오, 유대 땅에서!”\n\n“저는 나일강이 물러나며 드러난 맨땅에 그것을 심었고, 보드라운 남풍이 사막 너머로 불어와 그것을 보살피고, 해가 가엾이 여겨 입 맞추었어요. 그러니 어찌 자라고 무성해지지 않을 수 있었겠어요. 저는 이제 그 그늘에 서 있고, 그것은 짙은 향기로 제게 감사해요. 장미가 그러하듯, 이스라엘 사람들도 그래요. 이집트가 아니면 어디서 그들이 완성에 이르겠어요?”\n\n“모세는 수백만 가운데 하나였을 뿐이오.”\n\n“아니요, 꿈을 풀이하는 자가 있었지요. 그분을 잊으시려고요?”\n\n“우호적인 파라오들은 죽었소.”\n\n“아, 그래요! 그들이 곁에 두고 살던 강이 그들의 무덤에서 그들에게 노래해 줘요. 그래도 같은 해가 같은 공기를 같은 백성에게 데워 주죠.”\n\n“알렉산드리아는 로마의 도시일 뿐이오.”\n\n“그저 왕홀(王笏)을 바꿨을 뿐이에요. 카이사르가 그 도시에서 칼의 왕홀을 거두고, 그 자리에 학문의 왕홀을 남겼죠. 저와 함께 브루케이움으로 가요, 그러면 만국의 학당을 보여 드릴게요. 세라페이온으로 가서 건축의 완성을 보고, 도서관으로 가서 불멸의 작가들을 읽고, 극장으로 가서 그리스인과 힌두인의 영웅담을 듣고, 부두로 가서 상업의 승리를 헤아려요. 저와 함께 거리로 내려가요, 오 아리우스의 아들이여. 그리고 철학자들이 흩어지며 온갖 기예의 대가들을 데려가고, 모든 신이 자기 숭배자들을 거느려 돌아가고, 그날에 남은 것이라곤 그 즐거움뿐일 때, 당신은 태초부터 사람들을 즐겁게 해 온 이야기들과, 영영 죽지 않을 노래들을 듣게 될 거예요.”\n\n그 말을 들으며, 벤허는 예루살렘의 정자에서 어머니가 이와 거의 같은 애국의 시정(詩情)으로 사라진 이스라엘의 영광을 읊던 그 밤으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n“이제 알겠소, 당신이 어째서 이집트라고 불리고 싶어 하는지. 내가 그 이름으로 부르면 노래를 한 곡 불러 주겠소? 어젯밤에 당신 노래를 들었소.”\n\n“그건 나일강의 찬가였어요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “사막의 숨결을 맡고, 그리운 옛 강의 물결 소리를 듣는다고 상상하고 싶을 때 부르는 만가(輓歌)지요. 차라리 인도의 마음을 담은 한 곡을 들려 드릴게요. 알렉산드리아에 가면, 거리 모퉁이로 당신을 데려가, 제게 그 노래를 가르쳐 준 갠지스의 딸에게서 그것을 들을 수 있게 해 드리죠. 카필라는, 아셔야 할 텐데, 힌두 현자들 가운데 가장 존경받던 분이었어요.”\n\n그러고는 마치 그것이 본디의 표현 방식인 양, 그녀는 노래를 시작했다.\n카필라\n\n# I.\n\n하나\n“카필라, 카필라, 그토록 젊고 참된 이여, 나는 그대 같은 영광을 갈망하여, 싸움터에서 그대를 불러 다시 묻노니, 그대의 용기가 언젠가 내 것이 될 수 있으리까?\n\n“카필라는 잿빛 군마에 올라앉아, 그토록 진지한 영웅은 없었으니, ‘모든 것을 사랑하는 자는 두려울 게 없으니, 나를 용감케 하는 것은 사랑이라. 어느 날 한 여인이 내게 제 영혼을 주어, 영영 내 영혼의 영혼이 되었으니, 거기서 내 용기가 내게 왔노라, 가서 시험해 보라—시험해 보라—그리고 보라.’\n\n# II.\n\n“카필라, 카필라, 그토록 늙고 백발이 된 이여, 왕비께서 나를 부르고 계시나, 이곳을 떠나기 전에, 부디 그대가 말해 주오, 지혜가 어떻게 처음 그대에게 왔는지를.\n\n“카필라는 제 신전 문간에 서서, 은수자(隱修者)의 차림을 한 사제로서, ‘그것은 사람이 학문을 얻듯 온 것이 아니니, 나를 슬기롭게 하는 것은 믿음이라. 어느 날 한 여인이 내게 제 마음을 주어, 영영 내 마음의 마음이 되었으니, 거기서 내 지혜가 내게 왔노라, 가서 시험해 보라—시험해 보라—그리고 보라.’”\n\n벤허가 미처 노래에 고마움을 표하기도 전에, 배의 용골이 밑에 깔린 모래에 긁히더니, 이내 뱃머리가 기슭에 닿았다.\n\n“참으로 빠른 항해였소, 오 이집트여!” 그가 외쳤다.\n\n“그리고 더없이 짧은 머무름이었죠!” 그녀가 대답하자, 흑인이 힘껏 밀어 배를 다시 트인 물로 쏘아 보냈다.\n\n“이제 키를 내게 주겠소.”\n\n“오, 안 돼요.” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. “당신에겐 전차, 제겐 배예요. 우리는 그저 호수 끝에 닿았을 뿐이고, 교훈인즉슨 제가 더는 노래해선 안 된다는 거예요. 이집트에 다녀왔으니, 이제 다프네 숲으로 가요.”\n\n“가는 길에 노래도 없이?” 그가 아쉬운 듯 말했다.\n\n“오늘 우리를 구해 주신 그 로마인 이야기나 들려주세요.” 그녀가 청했다.\n\n그 청은 벤허의 마음을 언짢게 했다.\n\n“여기가 나일강이었으면.” 그가 말을 돌리며 말했다. “그토록 오래 잠들어 있던 왕들과 왕비들이 무덤에서 내려와 우리와 함께 배를 탈 텐데.”\n\n“그들은 거상(巨像)이라 우리 배를 가라앉힐 거예요. 차라리 난쟁이가 나을 텐데. 하지만 그 로마인 이야기를 해 주세요. 그 사람, 무척 사악하지 않나요?”\n\n“뭐라 말할 수 없소.”\n\n“귀족 가문에 부자인가요?”\n\n“그 사람의 재산에 대해서는 말할 수 없소.”\n\n“그 사람의 말들은 어찌나 아름답던지! 전차의 바닥은 황금이고, 바퀴는 상아였어요. 그리고 그 대담함이라니! 그가 달려가 버릴 때 구경꾼들이 웃었어요. 하마터면 그 바퀴에 깔릴 뻔했던 그들이 말이에요!”\n\n그녀는 그 일을 떠올리며 웃었다.\n\n“그자들은 천한 무리였소.” 벤허가 쓰디쓰게 말했다.\n\n“그는 로마에서 자라고 있다는 그 괴물들 가운데 하나임에 틀림없어요. 케르베로스처럼 게걸스러운 아폴론들 말이에요. 그 사람이 안티오크에 사나요?”\n\n“동방 어딘가 출신이오.”\n\n“그라면 시리아보다 이집트가 더 어울리겠어요.”\n\n“글쎄.” 벤허가 대답했다. “클레오파트라는 죽었소.”\n\n그 순간 천막 문 앞에 켜 둔 등잔불이 시야에 들어왔다.\n\n“다와르예요!” 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n“아, 그럼 우리는 이집트에 가지 못했군요. 나는 카르나크도 필라이도 아비도스도 보지 못했소. 여기는 나일강이 아니오. 나는 그저 인도의 노래 한 곡을 들었고, 꿈속에서 배를 탔을 뿐이오.”\n\n“필라이—카르나크. 차라리 당신이 아부심벨의 람세스를 보지 못한 것을 슬퍼하세요. 그것을 바라보면 하늘과 땅을 지으신 하느님을 떠올리기가 그토록 쉬워져요. 아니, 어째서 슬퍼해야 하나요? 강으로 계속 나아가요. 제가 노래는 못 하더라도”—그녀가 웃었다—“하지 않겠다고 말했으니까요, 그래도 이집트 이야기는 들려드릴 수 있어요.”\n\n“계속하시오! 그래, 아침이 오고, 저녁이 오고, 다음 날 아침이 올 때까지!” 그가 격하게 말했다.\n\n“제 이야기는 무엇에 대한 게 좋을까요? 수학자들?”\n\n“오, 아니오.”\n\n“철학자들?”\n\n“아니, 아니오.”\n“마법사와 정령들?”\n\n“그게 좋다면.”\n\n“전쟁?”\n\n“좋소.”\n\n“사랑?”\n\n“좋소.”\n\n“사랑을 고치는 처방을 들려드릴게요. 어느 왕비의 이야기예요. 경건히 들으세요. 이것을 적은 파피루스는 필라이의 사제들이 바로 그 여주인공의 손에서 빼앗아 온 것이에요. 형식이 올바르니, 틀림없는 사실일 거예요.\n네네호프라\n\n하나\n“인간의 삶에는 나란한 평행이란 없다.\n\n“어떤 삶도 곧은 선을 그리지 않는다.\n\n“가장 완전한 삶은 원으로 펼쳐져, 그 시작에서 끝나니, 여기가 시작이고 저기가 끝이라고 말할 수 없게 한다.\n\n“완전한 삶은 하느님의 보물이다. 위대한 날에 그분은 그것들을 자기 심장 쪽 손의 약손가락에 끼신다.\n\n# II.\n\n“네네호프라는 에수안 가까이, 그러나 첫 번째 폭포에 더 가까운 집에 살았다. 어찌나 가까웠던지, 거기서 강과 바위 사이에 영원히 벌어지는 싸움의 소리가 그 집의 일부였다.\n\n“그녀는 날마다 아름답게 자라나, 그녀를 두고 마치 아버지의 정원에 핀 양귀비를 두고 하듯 이런 말이 오갔다. 활짝 피어날 때면 그녀가 어떤 모습이 될까?\n\n“그녀의 한 해 한 해가, 앞서간 그 어떤 것보다 더 황홀한 새 노래의 시작이었다.\n\n“그녀는 바다에 막힌 북녘과, 루나산맥 너머 사막에 막힌 남녘 사이의 혼인에서 난 아이였다. 하나는 그녀에게 제 정열을 주고, 다른 하나는 제 천재를 주었다. 그래서 둘은 그녀를 바라보며, 옹졸하게 ‘저 아이는 내 것’이라 하지 않고, 너그럽게 ‘하, 하! 저 아이는 우리 것’이라 하며 웃었다.\n\n“자연의 온갖 빼어남이 그녀의 완성에 이바지하고 그녀의 존재를 기뻐했다. 그녀가 오고 가면 새들은 인사하며 날개를 떨었고, 사나운 바람은 가라앉아 서늘한 미풍이 되었으며, 흰 연꽃은 그녀를 보려고 물 깊은 곳에서 떠올랐고, 엄숙한 강은 제 갈 길에서 머뭇거렸으며, 종려나무는 고개를 끄덕이며 제 깃털을 다 흔들었다. 그것들은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. 이것은, 내가 그녀에게 내 우아함을 주었고, 저것은, 내가 그녀에게 내 광채를 주었고, 또 다른 것은, 내가 그녀에게 내 순결을 주었노라. 그리하여 저마다 줄 미덕이 있는 만큼 주었다.\n\n“열두 살에 네네호프라는 에수안의 기쁨이었고, 열여섯 살에는 그 아름다움의 명성이 두루 퍼졌으며, 스무 살에는 빠른 낙타를 탄 사막의 군주들과 황금 거룻배를 탄 이집트의 영주들이 그녀의 문 앞에 찾아오지 않는 날이 없었다. 그들은 낙담하여 돌아가서는 어디서나 이렇게 전했다. ‘나는 그녀를 보았는데, 그녀는 여자가 아니라 아토르 여신 그 자체였다.’\n\n# III.\n\n“선왕 메네스의 삼백서른 후계자 가운데 열여덟은 에티오피아인이었고, 그 가운데 오라이테스는 백열 살이었다. 그는 칠십육 년을 다스렸다. 그의 치세에 백성은 번성했고, 땅은 풍요의 기름짐으로 신음했다. 그는 슬기를 행했으니, 그토록 많은 것을 보았기에 슬기가 무엇인지 알았던 것이다. 그는 멤피스에 살며, 그곳에 자신의 으뜸가는 궁전과 병기고와 보물 창고를 두었다. 그는 자주 부토스로 내려가 라토나와 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n“선왕의 왕비가 죽었다. 그녀는 너무 늙어 완전한 방부 처리를 할 수 없었으나, 그래도 왕은 그녀를 사랑하여 위로받을 길 없는 사람처럼 슬퍼했다. 그것을 본 한 콜키테가 어느 날 감히 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n“‘오 오라이테스시여, 그토록 슬기롭고 위대하신 분이 이런 슬픔을 고치는 법을 모르시다니 저는 놀랍습니다.’\n\n“‘그 고치는 법을 말해 보아라.’ 왕이 말했다.\n\n“콜키테는 세 번 바닥에 입 맞춘 뒤, 죽은 이가 자기 말을 들을 수 없음을 알고 이렇게 대답했다. ‘에수안에 네네호프라가 사는데, 아름다운 아토르 여신처럼 아름답습니다. 그녀를 부르소서. 그녀는 모든 영주와 군주를, 또 얼마나 많은 왕인지 모를 이들을 물리쳤으나, 누가 오라이테스께 ‘아니오’라고 할 수 있겠습니까?’\n\n# IV.\n\n“네네호프라는 일찍이 본 적 없는 화려한 거룻배를 타고 나일강을 내려왔고, 저마다 조금 덜 화려한 거룻배에 탄 군세가 그녀를 호위했다. 누비아와 이집트 전역, 그리고 리비아에서 온 무수한 사람들, 트로글로디테스 무리, 또 달의 산맥 너머에서 온 적지 않은 마크로비이가 천막을 친 강기슭에 늘어서서, 향기로운 바람과 황금 노에 실려 지나가는 행렬을 구경했다.\n\n“스핑크스와 두 날개를 가진 채 엎드린 사자들이 늘어선 참배로를 지나 그녀가 옮겨져, 궁전의 조각된 탑문 앞에 특별히 세워진 옥좌에 앉은 오라이테스 앞에 내려졌다. 그는 그녀를 일으켜 제 곁에 자리를 주고, 그녀의 팔에 우라이우스를 채우고, 입 맞추니, 네네호프라는 모든 왕비 위의 왕비가 되었다.\n\n“그것으로는 슬기로운 오라이테스에게 충분치 않았다. 그는 사랑을, 그리고 자기 사랑 속에서 행복한 왕비를 원했다. 그래서 그는 그녀를 다정하게 대하며 자기 소유를, 도시들과 궁전들과 백성을, 자기 군대와 배들을 보여 주었고, 손수 그녀를 보물 창고로 이끌며 말했다. ‘오 네네호프라여! 사랑으로 내게 입 맞추기만 하면, 이 모든 것이 다 네 것이다.’\n\n“그래서 지금은 아니더라도 행복해질 수 있으리라 생각하며, 그녀는 그에게 한 번, 두 번, 세 번 입 맞추었으니—백열 살이라는 그의 나이에도 아랑곳없이 세 번 입 맞추었다.\n\n“첫해에 그녀는 행복했고, 그 해는 무척 짧았다. 셋째 해에 그녀는 비참했고, 그 해는 무척 길었다. 그러다 그녀는 깨달았다. 오라이테스를 향한 사랑이라 여겼던 것이 그저 그의 권세에 눈멀었던 것일 뿐임을. 그 눈멂이 그대로 이어졌더라면 차라리 다행이었을 것을! 기력이 그녀를 떠났고, 오랫동안 눈물에 잠겼으며, 그녀의 시녀들은 마지막으로 그녀의 웃음소리를 들은 때가 언제인지 떠올리지 못했다. 두 뺨의 장미는 잿빛만 남았다. 그녀는 차츰, 그러나 분명하게 시들고 야위어 갔다. 어떤 이는 그녀가 한 연인에게 모질게 군 탓에 복수의 여신들에게 시달리는 것이라 했고, 또 어떤 이는 오라이테스를 시샘하는 어느 신에게 화를 입은 것이라 했다. 그녀가 쇠하는 까닭이 무엇이든, 마법사들의 부적도 그녀를 되살리지 못했고, 의원의 처방 또한 똑같이 아무 효험이 없었다. 네네호프라는 죽음에 내맡겨졌다.\n\n“오라이테스는 왕비들의 무덤 위쪽에 그녀를 위한 묘실을 골랐다. 그리고 으뜸가는 조각가와 화가들을 멤피스로 불러, 죽은 왕들의 그 거대한 회랑에 있는 것보다도 더 공들인 도안에 매달리게 했다.\n\n“‘오 아토르 여신만큼이나 아름다운 그대, 나의 왕비여!’ 백열세 살이 되어서도 연인으로서의 열정이 사그라지지 않은 왕이 말했다. ‘부디 내게 말해 주오, 아아! 그대가 그토록 분명히 내 눈앞에서 스러져 가게 하는 그 병이 무엇인지를.’\n\n“‘말씀드리면 더는 저를 사랑하지 않으실 거예요.’ 그녀가 의심과 두려움에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n“‘너를 사랑하지 않다니! 나는 더욱더 사랑할 것이다. 아멘테의 정령들에 걸고 맹세하노라! 오시리스의 눈에 걸고 맹세하노라! 말하라!’ 그가 연인처럼 정열적으로, 왕처럼 위엄 있게 외쳤다.\n\n“‘그럼 들으세요.’ 그녀가 말했다. ‘에수안 가까운 어느 동굴에, 그 무리 가운데 가장 늙고 가장 거룩한 은수자가 살고 있어요. 이름은 메노파예요. 그분은 제 스승이자 보호자였어요. 그분을 부르세요, 오 오라이테스시여, 그러면 당신이 알고자 하는 것을 말씀드릴 거예요. 또 제 병을 고칠 방법을 찾도록 도와주실 거예요.’\n\n“오라이테스는 기뻐하며 일어섰다. 올 때보다 영혼이 백 년은 젊어진 채 그는 떠났다.\n\n# V.\n\n“‘말하라!’ 멤피스 궁전에서 오라이테스가 메노파에게 말했다.\n\n“그러자 메노파가 대답했다. ‘더없이 강대하신 왕이시여, 만일 당신이 젊으셨다면 저는 대답하지 않았을 것입니다. 저는 아직 삶을 즐기고 있으니까요. 사정이 이러하니, 저는 말씀드리겠습니다. 왕비께서는 다른 모든 인간과 마찬가지로 어느 죄의 대가를 치르고 계십니다.’\n\n“‘죄라니!’ 오라이테스가 노하여 외쳤다.\n\n“메노파는 아주 낮게 절했다.\n\n“‘그렇습니다. 자기 자신에게 지은 죄지요.’\n\n“‘나는 수수께끼를 들을 기분이 아니다.’ 왕이 말했다.\n\n“‘제가 드리는 말씀은 수수께끼가 아닙니다, 들으시면 아실 겁니다. 네네호프라는 제 눈앞에서 자라났고, 자기 삶의 일을 하나하나 제게 털어놓았습니다. 그 가운데, 자기 아버지의 정원사 아들 바르베크라는 자를 사랑했다는 것도 있었지요.’\n\n“오라이테스의 찌푸린 얼굴이, 참으로 이상하게도, 풀리기 시작했다.\n\n“‘그 사랑을 가슴에 품고서, 오 왕이시여, 그녀는 당신께 왔습니다. 바로 그 사랑 때문에 그녀는 죽어 가고 있습니다.’\n\n“‘그 정원사의 아들은 지금 어디 있느냐?’ 오라이테스가 물었다.\n\n“‘에수안에 있습니다.’\n\n“왕은 밖으로 나가 두 가지 명령을 내렸다. 한 오에리스에게 그는 말했다. ‘에수안으로 가서 바르베크라는 청년을 데려오너라. 왕비의 아버지의 정원에서 그를 찾을 것이다.’ 또 다른 자에게는 이렇게 일렀다. ‘일꾼과 가축과 연장을 모아, 켐미스 호수에 나를 위해 섬을 하나 만들어라. 신전과 궁전과 정원, 갖가지 열매 맺는 나무와 갖가지 포도 덩굴을 실었으되, 그러면서도 바람이 부는 대로 떠다닐 수 있는 섬이라야 한다. 그 섬을 만들고, 달이 이지러지기 시작할 때까지 온전히 갖추어 두어라.’\n\n“그러고는 왕비에게 말했다.\n\n“‘기운을 내라. 나는 모든 것을 알며, 바르베크를 부르러 보냈다.’\n\n“네네호프라는 그의 손에 입 맞추었다.\n\n“‘너는 그를 너만의 것으로 갖고, 그는 너를 자기만의 것으로 가질 것이다. 한 해 동안 누구도 너희 사랑을 방해하지 못하리라.’\n\n“그녀는 그의 발에 입 맞추었다. 그는 그녀를 일으켜 답례로 입 맞추었다. 그러자 두 뺨에 장미가 돌아오고, 입술에 진홍빛이 돌아오고, 가슴에 웃음이 돌아왔다.\n\n# VI.\n\n“한 해 동안 네네호프라와 정원사 바르베크는 바람 부는 대로 켐미스의 섬을 떠다녔으니, 그 섬은 세상의 경이 가운데 하나가 되었다. 그보다 더 아름다운 사랑의 보금자리는 일찍이 없었다. 한 해 동안, 아무도 보지 않고 오직 자기들끼리만을 위해 존재하며 살았다. 그러고는 그녀가 위풍당당하게 멤피스의 궁전으로 돌아왔다.\n\n“‘이제 너는 누구를 가장 사랑하느냐?’ 왕이 물었다.\n\n“그녀는 그의 뺨에 입 맞추며 말했다. ‘저를 다시 거두소서, 오 선하신 왕이여, 저는 다 나았습니다.’\n\n“오라이테스는 웃었으니, 그 순간 백열네 살이라 해서 조금도 못할 것이 없었다.\n\n“‘그럼 메노파가 말한 대로 사실이로구나. 하, 하, 하! 사실이야, 사랑의 약은 사랑이라더니.’\n\n“‘그러하옵니다.’ 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“돌연 그의 태도가 바뀌더니, 그 표정이 무시무시해졌다.\n\n“‘나는 그렇지 않았다.’ 그가 말했다.\n\n“그녀는 겁에 질려 움츠러들었다.\n\n“‘너는 죄인이다!’ 그가 말을 이었다. ‘인간 오라이테스에 대한 네 죄는 그가 용서한다. 그러나 왕 오라이테스에 대한 네 죄는 아직 벌해야 할 것으로 남아 있다.’\n\n“그녀는 그의 발치에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n“‘조용히!’ 그가 외쳤다. ‘너는 죽었다!’\n\n“그가 두 손을 마주치자, 무시무시한 행렬이 들어왔다—파라키스테스, 곧 방부 처리하는 자들의 행렬이었으니, 저마다 그 역겨운 기술의 어떤 연장이나 재료를 들고 있었다.\n\n“왕은 네네호프라를 가리켰다.\n\n“‘저 여자는 죽었다. 너희 일을 잘 해내라.’\n\n# VII.\n\n“아름다운 네네호프라는 일흔두 날 뒤, 한 해 전에 그녀를 위해 골라 둔 묘실로 옮겨져, 그녀에 앞선 왕비들과 나란히 뉘었다. 그러나 그녀를 기리며 거룩한 호수를 건너는 장례 행렬은 없었다.”\n\n이야기가 끝났을 때, 벤허는 이집트 여인의 발치에 앉아 있었고, 키를 잡은 그녀의 손은 그의 손에 덮여 있었다.\n“메노파가 틀렸소.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“어째서요?”\n\n“사랑은 사랑함으로써 살아 있는 거요.”\n\n“그럼 그것에는 약이 없나요?”\n\n“있소. 오라이테스가 그 약을 찾아냈소.”\n\n“그게 무엇이었죠?”\n\n“죽음이오.”\n\n“당신은 잘 들어 주는 분이군요, 오 아리우스의 아들이여.”\n\n그렇게 대화와 이야기로 그들은 시간을 흘려보냈다. 기슭에 내려서며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“내일 우리는 도시로 가요.”\n\n“하지만 경기에는 오시겠지요?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“오, 그럼요.”\n\n“내 색깔을 보내 드리겠소.”\n\n그 말과 함께 그들은 헤어졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n일데림은 이튿날 제3시경에 두아르로 돌아왔다. 그가 말에서 내리자, 제 부족 사람임을 알아본 한 남자가 다가와 말했다. \"오, 족장이시여, 이 꾸러미를 전하라는 분부를 받았습니다. 즉시 읽어 주시기를 청하셨습니다. 답신이 있으시면, 분부가 내릴 때까지 기다리라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n일데림은 곧바로 꾸러미를 살폈다. 봉인은 이미 뜯겨 있었다. 겉봉의 수신인은 이러했다. 카이사레아의 발레리우스 그라투스에게.\n\n\"아바돈이 저놈을 데려가기를!\" 라틴어로 쓰인 편지를 발견하고 족장은 으르렁거리듯 말했다.\n\n만약 그 편지가 그리스어나 아랍어로 쓰였다면 그는 읽을 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 형편이 이러하니, 그가 알아볼 수 있는 것이라곤 굵직한 로마 글자로 쓰인 서명—메살라—뿐이었고, 그 순간 그의 눈이 번득였다.\n\n\"그 젊은 유대인은 어디 있느냐?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"말들과 함께 들판에 있습니다.\" 하인 하나가 대답했다.\n\n족장은 파피루스를 다시 봉투 속에 넣고, 그 꾸러미를 허리띠 안에 찔러 넣은 뒤, 다시 말에 올랐다. 바로 그때 낯선 사람 하나가 모습을 드러냈는데, 보아하니 도시 쪽에서 오는 길이었다.\n\n\"관대한 자라 불리는 일데림 족장을 찾고 있습니다.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다.\n\n그의 말투와 차림새는 그가 로마인임을 드러냈다.\n\n읽지는 못해도 말은 할 줄 알았으므로, 늙은 아랍인은 위엄을 갖추어 대답했다. \"내가 일데림 족장이오.\"\n\n남자의 시선이 아래로 떨어졌다가 다시 들렸다. 그는 애써 침착을 꾸미며 말했다. \"경주에 쓸 마부가 필요하시다는 말을 들었습니다.\"\n\n하얀 콧수염 아래로 일데림의 입술이 경멸하듯 비틀렸다.\n\n\"네 갈 길이나 가거라.\" 그가 말했다. \"마부는 이미 있다.\"\n\n그는 말을 돌려 떠나려 했으나, 그 남자는 머뭇거리며 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"족장이시여, 저는 말을 사랑하는 사람입니다. 세상에서 가장 아름다운 말들을 가지고 계시다고들 하더군요.\"\n\n늙은이의 마음이 움직였다. 그는 그 아첨에 넘어가려는 듯 고삐를 당겼으나, 끝내 이렇게 대답했다. \"오늘은 안 되네, 오늘은 안 돼. 다른 날 보여 주겠네. 지금은 너무 바빠서 말이야.\"\n\n그는 들판으로 말을 몰았고, 그동안 낯선 사람은 흐뭇한 얼굴로 다시 도시를 향해 발길을 돌렸다. 그는 제 임무를 완수한 것이었다.\n\n그 뒤로 날마다, 경주가 열리는 대망의 날에 이르기까지, 한 사람이—때로는 두세 사람이—마부 자리를 구하는 척하며 그 과수원의 족장을 찾아왔다.\n\n이런 식으로 메살라는 벤허를 감시하고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n족장은 흡족한 마음으로 기다렸다. 벤허가 오전 훈련을 마치고 말들을 경기장에서 끌어낼 때까지—흡족하게, 그도 그럴 것이 그는 말들이 온갖 걸음새를 다 거친 끝에 전속력으로 달리는 모습을 지켜보았는데, 그 가운데 가장 느린 놈도 가장 빠른 놈도 따로 있는 것 같지 않을 만큼—다시 말해, 네 마리가 마치 한 마리처럼 달렸던 것이다.\n\n\"오늘 오후에, 족장이시여, 시리우스를 돌려드리겠습니다.\" 벤허가 말하면서 늙은 말의 목을 토닥였다. \"이놈을 돌려드리고, 전차에 매달겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그리 빨리?\" 일데림이 물었다.\n\n\"이런 말들이라면, 족장이시여, 하루면 충분합니다. 이놈들은 두려워하지 않습니다. 사람의 지혜를 지녔고, 훈련을 좋아합니다. 이놈은,\" 그는 네 마리 가운데 가장 어린 놈의 등 위로 고삐를 흔들었다—\"알데바란이라고 부르셨던 것 같은데—가장 빠릅니다. 경기장을 한 바퀴 돌면 제 몸길이의 세 배만큼 다른 놈들을 앞지를 겁니다.\"\n\n일데림은 수염을 쓰다듬으며, 눈을 반짝이며 말했다. \"알데바란이 가장 빠르다면, 가장 느린 놈은 어느 놈인가?\"\n\n\"이놈입니다.\" 벤허가 안타레스의 등 위로 고삐를 흔들었다. \"이놈입니다. 하지만 이놈이 이길 겁니다. 보십시오, 족장이시여, 이놈은 온종일 제 힘을 다해 달릴 겁니다—온종일 말입니다. 그리고 해가 질 무렵이면 가장 빠른 속도에 이를 겁니다.\"\n\n\"또 맞는 말이군.\" 일데림이 말했다.\n\n\"한 가지 두려운 것이 있습니다, 족장이시여.\"\n\n족장은 갑절로 진지해졌다.\n\n\"승리에 눈이 먼 로마인은 명예를 깨끗이 지키지 못합니다. 경기에서는—모든 경기에서, 명심하십시오—그들의 술책이 끝이 없습니다. 전차 경주에서는 그 간계가 온갖 것에 미칩니다—말에서 마부에게로, 마부에서 주인에게로 말입니다. 그러니, 족장이시여, 가지신 모든 것을 잘 지키십시오. 지금부터 시합이 끝날 때까지, 낯선 자가 말들을 눈으로 보는 것조차 허락하지 마십시오. 완전히 안전하기를 바라신다면, 그 이상을 하십시오—잠들지 않는 눈만이 아니라 무장한 손으로 지키십시오. 그러면 저는 결과를 두려워하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n천막 문 앞에서 그들은 말에서 내렸다.\n\n\"자네 말대로 하겠네. 하느님의 영광을 두고 맹세컨대, 충성스러운 자들의 손이 아니고서는 어떤 손도 말들 가까이 오지 못할 것일세. 오늘 밤 보초를 세우겠네. 그건 그렇고, 아리우스의 아들이여\"—일데림은 꾸러미를 꺼내 천천히 펼쳤다. 그들이 긴 의자로 걸어가 자리에 앉는 동안이었다—\"아리우스의 아들이여, 여기 좀 보고, 자네의 라틴어로 나를 도와주게.\"\n\n그는 그 문서를 벤허에게 건넸다.\n\n\"자, 읽어 보게—소리 내어 읽되, 거기 적힌 것을 자네 조상의 말로 옮겨 주게. 라틴어는 끔찍한 것이거든.\"\n\n벤허는 기분이 좋았던 터라, 무심히 읽기 시작했다. \"'메살라가 그라투스에게!'\" 그는 멈추었다. 어떤 예감이 피를 가슴으로 몰아붙였다. 일데림이 그의 동요를 알아차렸다.\n\n\"자, 기다리고 있네.\"\n\n벤허는 용서를 구하고, 다시 문서를 읽기 시작했다. 그 문서는 궁전에서 향연이 벌어진 다음 날 아침, 메살라가 그토록 조심스럽게 그라투스에게 보낸 편지의 사본 가운데 하나였다는 것만 밝혀 두면 충분하다.\n\n서두의 몇 단락은 글쓴이가 조롱하는 버릇을 아직 버리지 못했음을 보여 주는 증거로서만 주목할 만했다. 그 부분을 지나, 읽는 이가 그라투스의 기억을 되살리려는 대목에 이르자, 그의 목소리는 떨렸고, 두 번이나 멈추어 마음을 가다듬어야 했다. 그는 안간힘을 다해 계속 읽었다. \"'또 한 가지 떠오르는 것은,'\" 그가 읽었다. \"'그대가 후르 가문을 처리한 일이오'\"—여기서 읽는 이는 다시 멈추어 긴 숨을 들이쉬었다—\"'그때 우리 둘 다, 우리가 떠올린 그 방법이 목적을 이루는 데 가장 효과적이라 여겼소. 그 목적이란 침묵, 그리고 피할 수 없으나 자연스러운 죽음에 그들을 넘기는 것이었소.'\"\n\n여기서 벤허는 완전히 무너졌다. 문서가 그의 손에서 떨어졌고, 그는 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"그들은 죽었구나—죽었어. 나 혼자만 남았어.\"\n\n족장은 말없이, 그러나 동정 어린 마음으로 젊은이의 고통을 지켜보고 있었다. 이제 그는 일어서서 말했다. \"아리우스의 아들이여, 이번엔 내가 자네에게 용서를 구해야겠네. 그 문서는 자네 혼자 읽게. 남은 부분을 나에게 들려줄 만큼 마음이 추슬러지면, 기별을 주게. 그러면 돌아오겠네.\"\n\n그는 천막을 나갔다. 그의 평생에 그보다 더 어울리는 행동은 없었다.\n\n벤허는 긴 의자에 몸을 던지고 감정에 자신을 내맡겼다. 어느 정도 마음을 추스르자, 편지의 한 부분을 아직 읽지 않은 것이 떠올랐다. 그는 그것을 집어 들고 다시 읽기 시작했다. \"그대도 기억할 것이오,\" 편지는 이어졌다. \"그 죄인의 어미와 누이를 어찌 처리했는지를. 그렇지만, 지금 내가 그들이 살았는지 죽었는지 알고 싶은 마음에 굴복한다면\"—벤허는 흠칫하여, 다시 읽고, 또다시 읽다가, 마침내 탄성을 터뜨렸다. \"이자는 그들이 죽은 줄 모르는구나. 모르고 있어! 주님의 이름에 복이 있기를! 아직 희망이 있어.\" 그는 그 문장을 끝까지 읽었고, 그것에 힘을 얻어, 용기를 내어 편지의 끝까지 읽어 나갔다.\n\n\"그들은 죽지 않았어,\" 그는 생각에 잠긴 뒤 말했다. \"그들은 죽지 않았어. 안 그랬다면 이자가 그 소식을 들었을 테지.\"\n\n처음보다 더 신중하게 두 번째로 읽고 나니, 그 생각이 굳어졌다. 그러고 나서 그는 족장을 불러오게 했다.\n\n\"환대해 주시는 천막에 들어오면서, 족장이시여,\" 아랍인이 자리에 앉고 둘만 남게 되자, 그는 차분히 말했다. \"저는 제 말들을 맡길 만큼 충분한 훈련을 받았다는 것을 보여드리는 것 말고는, 제 자신에 대해 더 말씀드릴 생각이 없었습니다. 제 내력을 말씀드리기를 사양했지요. 그러나 이 문서를 제 손에 들어오게 하고 제가 읽도록 한 우연이 너무도 기이하여, 모든 것을 당신께 털어놓아야겠다는 마음이 듭니다. 더욱이 이 편지가 알려 준 바, 우리 둘이 같은 적에게 위협받고 있으며, 그자에 맞서 우리가 힘을 합쳐야 한다는 사실을 알았기에, 더욱 그러고 싶어집니다. 편지를 읽고 설명을 드리겠습니다. 그러고 나면 제가 왜 그토록 흔들렸는지 의아해하지 않으실 겁니다. 저를 나약하다거나 어린아이 같다고 여기셨다면, 그때 양해해 주실 겁니다.\"\n\n족장은 입을 다물고 귀를 기울여 듣다가, 벤허가 자신이 특별히 언급된 단락에 이르자 더욱 그러했다. \"'어제 다프네 숲에서 그 유대인을 보았소.'\" 그 부분은 이렇게 이어졌다. \"'그리고 지금 거기에 없다 해도, 분명 그 근처에 있을 터이니, 내가 그를 눈여겨 살피기는 쉬운 일이오. 실로, 그대가 나에게 지금 그가 어디 있느냐고 묻는다면, 나는 더없는 확신을 가지고, 그가 옛 종려나무 과수원에 있을 거라고 말하겠소.'\"\n\n\"아—!\" 일데림이 외쳤다. 놀랐다기보다 화가 났다고 하기 어려운 어조였다. 동시에 그는 수염을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"'옛 종려나무 과수원에,'\" 벤허가 거듭 읽었다. \"'배신자 일데림 족장의 천막 아래에 말이오.'\"\n\n\"배신자라고!—내가?\" 노인이 더없이 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. 입술과 수염이 분노로 일그러지고, 이마와 목에는 핏줄이 터질 듯 부풀어 올라 펄떡였다.\n\n\"잠시만 더, 족장이시여,\" 벤허가 말리는 몸짓으로 말했다. \"이것은 당신에 대한 메살라의 생각입니다. 그의 위협을 들어 보십시오.\" 그러고 그는 계속 읽었다—\"'배신자 일데림 족장의 천막 아래에 있으니, 그자도 우리의 강한 손을 오래 피하지는 못할 것이오. 막센티우스가 그 첫 조치로 그 아랍인을 배에 태워 로마로 보낸다 해도 놀라지 마시오.'\"\n\n\"로마로! 나를—이 일데림을—창을 든 기마병 일만 명의 족장인 나를—로마로 보낸다고!\"\n\n그는 일어선다기보다 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다. 두 팔을 쭉 뻗고, 손가락을 발톱처럼 펴서 구부리고, 두 눈은 뱀의 그것처럼 번득였다.\n\n\"오 하느님!—아니, 로마의 신들을 뺀 모든 신을 두고 맹세컨대!—이 오만함이 언제나 끝이 날 것인가? 나는 자유인이다. 내 백성도 자유롭다. 우리가 노예로 죽어야 한단 말인가? 아니, 그보다 더 못하게, 내가 주인의 발치에 기어가는 개로 살아야 한단 말인가? 매를 맞지 않으려고 그자의 손을 핥아야 한단 말인가? 내 것이 내 것이 아니고, 나는 내 것이 아니구나. 몸뚱이의 숨결조차 로마인에게 빚져야 하다니. 오, 내가 다시 젊어질 수만 있다면! 오, 스무 해를—아니 열 해를—아니 다섯 해라도 떨쳐 버릴 수 있다면!\"\n\n그는 이를 갈며 머리 위로 두 손을 흔들었다. 그러더니 또 다른 생각에 사로잡혀, 성큼성큼 걸어갔다가 다시 벤허에게로 돌아와, 억센 손아귀로 그의 어깨를 붙들었다.\n\n\"내가 자네 같다면, 아리우스의 아들이여—그토록 젊고, 그토록 강하고, 무예에 그토록 단련되어 있다면. 복수를 부추기는 동기가 있다면—자네의 것처럼, 증오를 거룩하게 만들 만큼 큰 동기가 있다면— 자네도 나도 위장은 그만두세! 후르의 아들, 후르의 아들이여, 내가 말하노니—\"\n\n그 이름에 벤허의 온몸의 피가 멎었다. 놀랍고 어리둥절하여, 그는 이제 제 얼굴에 바싹 다가온, 사납게 번득이는 아랍인의 두 눈을 응시했다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이여, 내가 말하노니, 내가 자네 같다면, 자네가 당한 부당함의 절반만이라도 당했다면, 자네 같은 기억을 안고 다닌다면, 나는 결코 가만히 있지 않을 것이며, 있을 수도 없을 것이네.\" 노인은 한 번도 멈추지 않고, 말들이 격류처럼 잇따라 쏟아져 나오며 거침없이 이어 갔다. \"내 모든 원한에다 온 세상의 원한을 보태어, 복수에 나 자신을 바치겠네. 이 땅에서 저 땅으로 다니며 온 인류에 불을 지르겠네. 자유를 위한 전쟁에는 빠짐없이 끼어들겠고, 로마에 맞선 싸움에는 어느 것에든 한몫 거들겠네. 달리 나을 길이 없다면 파르티아인이라도 되겠네. 사람들이 나를 저버린다 해도, 나는 그 노력을 그만두지 않을 것이네—하, 하, 하! 하느님의 영광을 두고 맹세컨대! 나는 이리 떼와 무리를 이루고, 사자와 호랑이를 벗 삼아, 공동의 적에 맞서 그것들을 부릴 수 있기를 바라겠네. 나는 모든 무기를 쓸 것이네. 내 희생자가 로마인이기만 하다면, 나는 살육 속에서 기뻐하겠네. 자비를 구하지도 않을 것이며, 자비를 베풀지도 않을 것이네. 로마의 것이라면 무엇이든 불길 속에 던지고, 로마에서 태어난 자라면 누구든 칼에 부치겠네. 밤이면 신들에게, 선한 신이든 악한 신이든 가리지 않고 기도하여, 그들의 특별한 공포를 빌려 달라 하겠네—폭풍, 가뭄, 더위, 추위, 그리고 그들이 공중에 풀어놓는 이름 없는 온갖 독, 사람들이 바다와 뭍에서 죽어 나가는 그 수천 가지 것들을 말이네. 오, 나는 잠을 이룰 수 없을 것이네. 나는—나는—\"\n\n족장은 숨이 차서 멈추었다. 헐떡이며 두 손을 쥐어짰다. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그 격정의 폭발 가운데 벤허에게 남은 것은 불타는 눈, 꿰뚫는 목소리, 조리 있게 풀어내기에는 너무도 격렬한 분노가 빚어낸 어렴풋한 인상뿐이었다.\n\n여러 해 만에 처음으로, 외로운 젊은이는 자신이 제 본명으로 불리는 것을 들었다. 적어도 한 사람은 그를 알아보았고, 신분을 캐묻지도 않고 그것을 인정했다. 더구나 그자는 사막에서 갓 나온 아랍인이었다!\n\n그자는 어떻게 그것을 알게 되었을까? 편지를 통해서? 아니다. 편지는 그의 가족이 당한 잔혹함을 말해 주었고, 그 자신의 불행을 이야기해 주었으나, 그 무정한 이야기의 주제가 된, 파멸에서 벗어난 바로 그 희생자가 그라는 말은 하지 않았다. 바로 그 점이, 편지를 다 읽은 뒤에 이어질 것이라고 그가 족장에게 알려 두었던 설명할 대목이었다. 그는 기뻤고, 되살아난 희망에 가슴이 떨렸으나, 짐짓 침착한 태도를 지켰다.\n\n\"족장이시여, 이 편지를 어떻게 손에 넣으셨는지 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"내 사람들이 도시와 도시 사이의 길을 지킨다네,\" 일데림이 퉁명스럽게 대답했다. \"전령에게서 빼앗은 것일세.\"\n\n\"그들이 당신의 사람들로 알려져 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아닐세. 세상이 보기에 그들은 도둑이고, 내가 잡아서 죽여야 할 자들이지.\"\n\n\"다시 여쭙겠습니다, 족장이시여. 당신은 저를 후르의 아들이라 부르셨습니다—제 아버지의 이름이지요. 저는 이 세상 누구도 저를 알지 못한다고 여겼습니다. 어떻게 그것을 아셨습니까?\"\n\n일데림은 머뭇거렸다. 그러나 마음을 다잡고 대답했다. \"나는 자네를 아네. 그렇지만 그 이상은 자유로이 말할 수가 없네.\"\n\n\"누군가가 당신을 묶어 두고 있습니까?\"\n\n족장은 입을 닫고 걸어가 버렸다. 그러나 벤허의 실망한 기색을 보고는 다시 돌아와 말했다. \"그 일은 지금 더 말하지 마세. 나는 시내로 가겠네. 돌아오면 자네에게 다 이야기할 수 있을지도 모르지. 편지를 주게.\"\n\n일데림은 파피루스를 조심스럽게 말아 봉투에 도로 넣고, 다시금 온통 활기에 넘쳤다.\n\n\"자네는 어찌 말하려는가?\" 그는 말과 수행원들을 기다리며 물었다. \"내가 자네라면 어찌하겠다고 말했건만, 자네는 아무 대답이 없었네.\"\n\n\"대답할 생각이었습니다, 족장이시여. 그리고 대답하겠습니다.\" 벤허의 얼굴과 목소리는 솟구치는 감정과 더불어 달라졌다. \"당신이 말씀하신 모든 것을 저는 하겠습니다—적어도 한 사람의 힘이 미치는 한 모두 다 말입니다. 저는 오래전에 복수에 제 자신을 바쳤습니다. 지나간 다섯 해의 매 순간을, 저는 오로지 그 생각만으로 살아왔습니다. 한순간도 쉬지 않았습니다. 젊음의 즐거움이라고는 누리지 못했습니다. 로마의 온갖 유혹도 저에게는 아무것도 아니었습니다. 저는 로마가 저를 복수를 위해 길러 주기를 바랐습니다. 저는 로마의 가장 이름난 스승과 교사들을 찾아갔습니다—수사학이나 철학을 가르치는 자들이 아니라 말입니다. 아아! 그런 것에는 시간이 없었습니다. 싸우는 자에게 꼭 필요한 기예가 제가 바라는 것이었지요. 저는 검투사들과, 원형 경기장에서 상을 탄 자들과 어울렸고, 그들이 제 스승이었습니다. 대규모 병영의 훈련 교관들은 저를 제자로 받아들였고, 그들의 분야에서 제가 이룬 성취를 자랑스러워했습니다. 오 족장이시여, 저는 군인입니다. 그러나 제가 꿈꾸는 것들은 제가 지휘관이 되기를 요구합니다. 그 생각으로, 저는 파르티아인에 맞선 원정에 참가했습니다. 그것이 끝나면, 그때, 주님께서 제 목숨과 힘을 살려 주신다면—그때\"—그는 움켜쥔 두 손을 들어 올리며 격렬하게 말했다—\"그때 저는 로마가 가르친 모든 것으로 무장한 적이 될 것입니다. 그때 로마는 저에게 그 죄악을 로마인의 목숨으로 갚을 것입니다. 이것이 제 대답입니다, 족장이시여.\"\n\n일데림은 그의 어깨에 한 팔을 두르고 입을 맞추며, 격정에 차서 말했다. \"자네의 하느님이 자네를 돌보지 않는다면, 후르의 아들이여, 그것은 그분이 죽었기 때문일 것이네. 내게서 이것을 받아 두게—자네가 원한다면 맹세코 하리니, 자네는 내 두 손과 그 손이 가득 채운 것을 가지게 될 걸세—사람과 말과 낙타와, 그리고 준비를 위한 이 사막을 말이네. 내 맹세하노라! 지금은 이것으로 충분하네. 밤이 되기 전에 자네는 나를 보거나 내 기별을 듣게 될 걸세.\"\n\n족장은 불쑥 돌아서더니, 어느새 시내로 가는 길에 올라 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n가로챈 편지는 벤허에게 큰 관심사인 여러 사항을 명백히 밝혀 주었다. 그것은 한낱 자백이나 다름없었다. 편지를 쓴 자가 살의를 품고 그 일가를 처치하는 데 가담했다는 것, 그 목적을 위해 채택된 계획을 승인했다는 것, 몰수한 재산의 일부를 받아 지금도 그 몫을 누리고 있다는 것, 그가 즐겨 일컫는 이른바 주범의 예기치 못한 출현을 두려워하며 그것을 위협으로 받아들였다는 것, 앞으로 자신을 안전하게 지켜 줄 다음 행동을 궁리하고 있으며, 카이사레아의 공범이 권하는 일이라면 무엇이든 할 준비가 되어 있다는 것—이 모든 것을 자백한 셈이었다.\n\n그리고 이제 그 편지가 정작 그 글의 진짜 대상인 사람의 손에 들어왔으니, 그것은 죄의 고백인 동시에 다가올 위험을 알리는 통지였다. 그래서 일데림이 천막을 나갔을 때, 벤허에게는 곰곰이 생각해야 할 일이 많았고, 그 일에는 즉각적인 행동이 필요했다. 그의 적들은 동방의 누구 못지않게 교활하고 강력했다. 그들이 그를 두려워한다면, 그가 그들을 두려워할 까닭은 더욱 컸다. 그는 처지를 진지하게 헤아려 보려 애썼으나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 감정이 끊임없이 그를 압도했기 때문이다. 어머니와 누이가 살아 있다는 확신에는 어떤 절제된 기쁨이 있었다. 그 확신의 근거가 한낱 추론에 불과하다는 것은 그다지 중요하지 않았다. 그들이 어디에 있는지 일러 줄 사람이 하나 있다는 사실은, 그토록 오래 미뤄진 그의 희망에는 마치 발견이 이제 코앞에 다가온 것처럼 여겨졌다. 이것들은 한낱 감정의 원인일 뿐이었다. 그 밑바닥에는, 고백건대, 하느님이 자신을 위해 무언가를 마련해 주시려 한다는 미신 같은 상상이 깔려 있었고, 그런 경우라면 가만히 기다리라고 신앙이 그에게 속삭이는 것이었다.\n\n이따금 일데림의 말을 떠올리며, 그는 그 아랍인이 자신에 관한 정보를 어디서 얻었는지 궁금해했다. 멀룩에게서 얻은 것은 분명 아니었고, 시모니데스에게서도 아니었으니, 그는 이해관계가 온통 상반되어 입을 다물 수밖에 없는 사람이었다. 메살라가 정보를 준 자일 수 있을까? 아니, 아니다. 그쪽에서는 발설이 위험할 수 있었다. 추측은 부질없었다. 그러나 벤허는 해답에서 번번이 물러서면서도, 그 지식을 가진 사람이 누구든 친구일 것이며, 친구라면 때가 되면 스스로 모습을 드러내리라는 생각에 위안을 얻었다. 조금만 더 기다리면 되었다. 조금만 더 참으면 되었다. 어쩌면 족장의 볼일은 그 가치 있는 사람을 만나는 일인지도 몰랐고, 어쩌면 그 편지가 모든 것의 완전한 폭로를 앞당길지도 몰랐다.\n\n티르자와 어머니가, 자신만큼이나 강한 희망을 품을 만한 처지에서 그를 기다리고 있다고 믿을 수만 있었다면, 다시 말해 그들을 두고 양심이 그를 가책으로 찌르지만 않았다면, 그는 능히 참을 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그런 가책에서 벗어나려고 그는 과수원을 멀리 헤매고 다녔다. 대추야자를 따는 일꾼들이 바삐 일하는 곳에 잠시 멈추었으나, 그들은 그에게 열매를 권하고 이야기를 나눌 만큼은 한가했다. 그러다 큰 나무 아래에서 둥지를 트는 새들을 바라보거나, 꿀같이 단 즙으로 터질 듯한 열매 주위로 벌들이 떼 지어 모여드는 소리를, 그리고 푸르고 황금빛으로 물든 온 공간을 날갯짓 소리로 채우는 소리를 듣곤 했다.\n\n그러나 호숫가에서 그는 가장 오래 머물렀다. 관능적인 생명처럼 반짝이는 잔물결이 일렁이는 물을 바라보면서, 그 이집트 여인과 그녀의 경이로운 아름다움을, 그리고 그녀의 노래와 이야기로 찬란하게 빛나던 밤에 그녀와 함께 여기저기 떠다니던 일을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀의 몸짓이 지닌 매력을, 가벼운 웃음소리를, 자신에게 쏟던 관심의 달콤함을, 배의 키 위에 얹은 자신의 손 아래로 느껴지던 그녀의 작은 손의 온기를 잊을 수 없었다. 그녀에게서 발타사르에게로 생각이 옮겨 가는 것은 그저 짧은 걸음에 지나지 않았고, 자연의 어떤 법칙으로도 설명할 수 없는, 그가 목격한 기이한 일들로 이어지는 것 또한 마찬가지였다. 그리고 거기서 다시, 그 선량한 노인이 그토록 애틋한 인내로 거룩한 약속 속에 붙들고 있는 유대인의 왕에게로 이어지는 거리는 더욱 가까웠다. 그리고 그의 마음은 거기에 머물렀으니, 그 인물의 신비 속에서 그가 찾던 안식에 잘 답해 주는 만족을 얻었기 때문이다. 어쩌면 우리의 바람에 맞지 않는 생각을 부정하는 것만큼 쉬운 일은 없기에, 그는 그 왕이 세우러 온다는 나라에 대해 발타사르가 내린 정의를 거부했다. 영혼의 나라란, 사두개인다운 그의 신앙에 견딜 수 없는 것까지는 아니더라도, 너무도 애틋하고 몽상적인 헌신의 깊은 곳에서 끌어낸 한낱 관념에 불과하게 여겨졌다. 반면에 유대의 나라란 충분히 이해할 만한 것이었다. 그런 나라는 일찍이 존재했고, 다만 그 이유만으로도 다시 존재할 수 있었다. 옛 나라보다 영토가 더 넓고, 권세가 더 풍부하며, 더 범접할 수 없는 영화를 누리는 새로운 나라를 떠올리는 것, 솔로몬보다 더 지혜롭고 더 강성한 새 왕을, 무엇보다도 자신이 봉직할 곳과 복수할 길을 함께 찾을 수 있는 새 왕을 떠올리는 것은 그의 자존심에 맞아떨어졌다. 그런 심정으로 그는 천막촌으로 돌아왔다.\n\n점심을 마친 뒤에도 무언가에 더 몰두하려고, 벤허는 점검을 위해 전차를 햇빛 속으로 끌어내게 했다. 점검이라는 말로는 그 수레가 받은 세심한 연구를 제대로 전하지 못한다. 그것의 어떤 점도, 어떤 부분도 그의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다. 뒤에 가서 더 잘 이해될 어떤 기쁨을 느끼며, 그는 그것이 그리스식 양식임을 알아보았으니, 그의 판단으로는 여러 면에서 로마식보다 나았다. 그것은 바퀴 사이가 더 넓고, 더 낮고 더 튼튼했으며, 무게가 더 나간다는 불리함은 자신의 아랍 말들이 지닌 더 큰 지구력으로 충분히 메워질 터였다. 대체로 말해, 로마의 수레 장인들은 거의 오로지 경기를 위해서만 전차를 만들어, 아름다움을 위해 안전을 희생하고 우아함을 위해 내구성을 버렸다. 반면에 전쟁과 그 온갖 극한의 시험을 위해 고안된 아킬레우스와 \"인간들의 왕\"의 전차는, 이스트미아와 올림피아의 월계관을 두고 맞붙어 겨루는 자들의 취향을 여전히 지배하고 있었다.\n\n다음으로 그는 말들을 끌어내어 전차에 매고는, 운동장으로 몰고 가, 멍에를 메운 채 움직이는 연습을 몇 시간이고 시켰다. 저녁에 그곳을 떠날 때, 그는 기운을 되찾았고, 경주에서 이기든 지든 그 결과가 날 때까지 메살라에 관한 일의 행동은 미루기로 마음을 굳혔다. 그는 동방의 눈앞에서 적수와 맞서는 즐거움을 포기할 수 없었다. 다른 경쟁자들이 있을 수 있다는 생각은 그의 머릿속에 들어오지도 않는 듯했다. 결과에 대한 그의 확신은 절대적이었으니, 자신의 기량에 대한 의심은 조금도 없었다. 그리고 네 마리 말로 말하자면, 그들은 그 영광스러운 경기에서 그의 온전한 동반자였다.\n\n\"그자더러 정신 차리라고 해, 정신 차리라고! 하, 안타레스, 알데바란! 그렇지 않으냐, 오 정직한 리겔아? 그리고 너, 준마들의 왕 아타이르야, 그자가 우리를 경계해야 하지 않겠느냐? 하, 하! 착한 녀석들!\"\n\n그렇게 그는 짬짬이 말에서 말로 옮겨 다니며 말을 건넸으니, 주인으로서가 아니라 여러 형제 가운데 맏이로서였다.\n\n날이 저문 뒤, 벤허는 아직 도시에서 돌아오지 않은 일데림을 기다리며 천막 문가에 앉아 있었다. 그는 조급하지도, 짜증스럽지도, 미심쩍어하지도 않았다. 적어도 족장에게서는 소식이 올 터였다. 사실, 네 마리 말의 성능에 대한 만족 때문이든, 몸을 쓴 뒤 찬물이 주는 상쾌함 때문이든, 임금 같은 식욕으로 든 저녁 식사 때문이든, 자연의 다정한 섭리로 침체 뒤에 늘 따라오는 반작용 때문이든, 그 젊은이는 의기양양함에 가까운 좋은 기분에 젖어 있었다. 그는 자신이 더는 적이 아닌 섭리의 손안에 있다고 느꼈다. 마침내 말발굽이 빠르게 다가오는 소리가 났고, 멀룩이 말을 타고 다가왔다.\n\n\"아리우스의 아들이여,\" 그가 인사를 건넨 뒤 쾌활하게 말했다. \"일데림 족장을 대신해 인사드립니다. 족장께서는 그대가 말을 타고 도시로 와 주기를 청하십니다. 그대를 기다리고 계십니다.\"\n\n벤허는 아무것도 묻지 않고, 말들이 먹이를 먹고 있는 곳으로 들어갔다. 알데바란이 그에게 다가왔으니, 마치 제 봉사를 자청하는 것 같았다. 그는 그 말을 다정하게 어루만지다가 지나쳐, 네 마리 가운데 들지 않은 다른 말을 골랐다. 그 넷은 경주를 위해 신성하게 남겨 둔 것이었다. 곧 두 사람은 길에 올라, 빠르게, 그리고 말없이 나아갔다.\n\n셀레우키아 다리에서 얼마쯤 아래에서 그들은 나룻배로 강을 건넌 다음, 오른편 강기슭을 멀리 돌아가, 또 다른 나룻배로 다시 건너서, 서쪽에서 도시로 들어섰다. 우회로는 길었으나, 벤허는 그것을 충분한 까닭이 있는 예방책으로 받아들였다.\n\n그들은 시모니데스의 부두까지 내려가, 다리 밑 큰 창고 앞에서 멀룩이 고삐를 당겼다.\n\n\"다 왔습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"내리시지요.\"\n\n벤허는 그곳을 알아보았다.\n\n\"족장은 어디 계시오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"저와 함께 가시지요. 제가 보여 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n야경꾼이 말들을 받아 갔고, 미처 깨닫기도 전에 벤허는 큰 건물 위에 얹힌 그 집의 문 앞에 다시 서서, 안에서 들려오는 대답에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. \"하느님의 이름으로, 어서 드십시오.\"\n\n말루크는 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 벤허는 혼자 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n그 방은 전에 시모니데스를 만났던 바로 그 방이었고, 어디 하나 달라진 데가 없었다. 다만 안락의자 가까이에, 넓은 나무 받침대 위에 윤이 나는 놋쇠 막대가 키 큰 사람보다 높게 솟아, 미끄러지는 팔에 은 등잔을 여섯 개 남짓 매달고 있었으며, 모두 불을 밝히고 있었다. 그 빛은 맑아서, 벽의 판자 장식과 도금한 구슬이 한 줄로 늘어선 처마 돌림띠와 보랏빛 운모로 은은하게 물든 둥근 천장까지 한눈에 드러나게 했다.\n\n몇 걸음 채 못 가서, 벤허는 멈춰 섰다.\n\n세 사람이 그곳에 있었으니, 모두 그를 바라보고 있었다. 시모니데스와 일데림과 에스더였다.\n\n그는 마음속에 반쯤 떠오른 물음의 답을 찾기라도 하려는 듯, 한 사람씩 차례로 황급히 눈길을 옮겼다. 이들이 내게 무슨 볼일이 있단 말인가? 그는 곧 마음을 가라앉히고 온 감각을 곤두세웠으니, 그 물음 뒤에 또 다른 물음이 이어졌기 때문이다. 이들은 벗인가, 적인가?\n\n마침내 그의 눈길이 에스더에게 머물렀다.\n\n두 사내는 다정하게 그의 눈길을 받아 주었다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴에는 다정함 이상의 무엇이 있었으니, 정의 내리기에는 너무도 영적인 그것은 정의되지 않은 채로 그의 내면 깊은 의식에까지 닿았다.\n\n말해도 좋을까, 선량한 독자여? 그 눈길 뒤편에서는 한 비교가 일어나, 이집트 여인이 떠올라 그 온화한 유대 처녀와 맞세워졌다. 그러나 그것은 한순간 살아 있다가, 그런 비교가 으레 그러하듯, 어떤 결론도 없이 스러졌다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이여—\"\n\n손님은 말하는 이를 향해 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이여.\" 시모니데스는 그 부름을 천천히, 또렷한 강세를 실어 되풀이했으니, 마치 그 말의 온 뜻을, 그것을 알아듣는 데 가장 마음 쓰는 이의 가슴에 새겨 넣으려는 듯했다. \"우리 조상들의 주 하느님의 평화를 받으시오. 나로부터 그것을 받으시오.\" 그는 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"나와 내 사람들로부터.\"\n\n말하는 이는 의자에 앉아 있었다. 그 위엄 있는 머리와 핏기 없는 얼굴과 당당한 기품 아래에서, 찾아온 이들은 이 사내의 부서진 사지와 뒤틀린 몸을 잊곤 했다. 새까만 두 눈은 하얀 눈썹 아래에서 흔들림 없이, 그러나 매섭지 않게 바라보고 있었다. 그렇게 한순간이 흐른 뒤, 그는 두 손을 가슴 위에 포갰다.\n\n그 인사와 더불어 행해진 그 동작은 오해될 수 없는 것이었고, 또한 오해되지 않았다.\n\n\"시모니데스,\" 벤허가 깊이 감동하여 대답했다. \"그대가 건네는 거룩한 평화를 받겠소. 아들이 아버지에게 하듯, 그것을 그대에게 되돌리겠소. 다만 우리 사이에 온전한 이해가 있게 합시다.\"\n\n그는 이토록 섬세하게, 상인의 복종을 물리치고, 주인과 종이라는 관계 대신 그보다 더 높고 더 거룩한 관계를 세우려 했다.\n\n시모니데스는 두 손을 내려놓고, 에스더를 향해 말했다. \"주인님께 앉을 자리를, 딸아.\"\n\n그녀는 서둘러 등받이 없는 의자를 가져왔으나, 얼굴이 발갛게 물든 채 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라보며 서 있었다. 벤허에게서 시모니데스에게로, 시모니데스에게서 벤허에게로. 두 사람은 자리를 권하는 일이 내포할 우위를 서로 사양하며 기다렸다. 마침내 그 침묵이 거북해지기 시작하자, 벤허가 앞으로 나서서 그녀에게서 부드럽게 의자를 받아 들고, 의자 쪽으로 가서 상인의 발치에 놓았다.\n\n\"나는 여기 앉겠소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그의 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주쳤다. 단 한순간이었으나, 그 눈길로 두 사람 모두 마음이 한결 나아졌다. 그는 그녀의 고마움을, 그녀는 그의 너그러움과 너른 아량을 알아보았다.\n\n시모니데스가 고개를 숙여 고마움을 표했다.\n\n\"에스더, 얘야, 그 문서를 가져오너라.\" 그가 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 벽의 판자 한 곳으로 가서 그것을 열고, 파피루스 두루마리 하나를 꺼내, 그에게 가져다주었다.\n\n\"그대 말이 옳소, 후르의 아들이여.\" 시모니데스는 종이를 펼치며 입을 열었다. \"우리 서로를 이해하도록 합시다. 그대가 요구하지 않더라도 내가 먼저 내놓았을 그 요구를 미리 헤아려, 여기 우리에게 필요한 이해에 빠짐없이 닿는 명세를 마련해 두었소. 내가 보기에 얽힌 문제는 두 가지뿐이었으니, 첫째는 재산이요, 다음은 우리의 관계요. 이 명세는 그 둘 모두에 대해 분명하오. 지금 그것을 읽어 보시겠소?\"\n\n벤허는 종이를 받아 들었으나, 일데림을 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"아니,\" 시모니데스가 말했다. \"족장이 읽기를 막지는 않을 것이오. 이 계산서는—그대가 보면 알겠지만—증인이 필요한 성격의 것이오. 그대가 그곳에 이르면, 끝의 증명란에서 '일데림, 족장'이라는 이름을 보게 될 것이오. 그는 모든 것을 알고 있소. 그는 그대의 벗이오. 그가 내게 그러했던 모든 것을, 그대에게도 그리할 것이오.\"\n\n시모니데스는 아랍인을 바라보며 다정하게 고개를 끄덕였고, 상대는 진중하게 그 끄덕임을 되돌리며 말했다. \"그대 말 그대로요.\"\n\n벤허가 대답했다. \"나는 이미 그의 우정이 얼마나 훌륭한지 알고 있으며, 아직 그럴 만한 사람임을 스스로 증명해야 할 처지요.\" 그는 곧바로 말을 이었다. \"시모니데스, 나중에 이 문서를 꼼꼼히 읽겠소. 지금은 그대가 그것을 받아 두었다가, 너무 지치지 않았다면 그 요지를 들려주시오.\"\n\n시모니데스는 두루마리를 도로 받았다.\n\n\"자, 에스더, 내 곁에 서서 이 종이들을 받아 다오. 흐트러지지 않도록.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 의자 곁에 자리를 잡고, 오른팔을 그의 어깨 위에 가볍게 올렸다. 그리하여 그가 말할 때, 그 계산서는 두 사람이 함께 풀어내는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"이것은,\" 시모니데스가 첫 장을 빼내며 말했다. \"내가 그대 부친에게서 받은 돈을 보여 주는 것으로, 로마인들로부터 건져 낸 액수요. 건진 재산은 없었고 오직 돈뿐이었는데, 우리 유대인의 환어음 관습이 아니었다면 그 돈마저 강도들이 차지했을 것이오. 건져 낸 액수는, 내가 로마와 알렉산드리아와 다마스쿠스와 카르타고와 발렌티아와 그 밖에 교역망 안의 여러 곳에서 거두어들인 금액으로, 유대 화폐로 백이십 달란트였소.\"\n\n그는 그 장을 에스더에게 건네고, 다음 장을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"그 액수, 곧 백이십 달란트를 내 책임 아래 두었소. 이제 내가 거둔 수익을 들으시오. 그대도 알게 되겠지만, 나는 이 말을 그 돈을 굴려 얻은 이익을 가리키는 뜻으로 쓰는 것이오.\"\n\n그러고는 따로따로 된 장들에서 합계를 읽어 내려갔으니, 끝수를 버리면 다음과 같았다.\n\n선박으로 60달란트, 창고에 쌓인 물품으로 110달란트, 운송 중인 화물로 75달란트, 낙타와 말 따위로 20달란트, 창고 건물로 10달란트, 받을 어음으로 54달란트, 수중에 있고 언제든 인출할 수 있는 돈으로 224달란트—총계 553달란트.\n\n\"이제 이것에, 거두어들인 오백쉰세 달란트에, 그대 부친에게서 받은 처음의 자본을 더하면, 그대에게는 육백일흔세 달란트가 있게 되오! 그리고 모두 그대의 것이니—후르의 아들이여, 그대는 이로써 세상에서 가장 부유한 신민이 되는 것이오.\"\n\n그는 에스더에게서 파피루스를 받아 들고, 한 장을 따로 남겨 둔 채 나머지를 둘둘 말아 벤허에게 내밀었다. 그의 태도에 어린 자랑스러움은 거슬리지 않았으니, 그것은 임무를 잘 완수했다는 마음에서 비롯된 것일 수도 있었고, 자기 자신과는 상관없이 오로지 벤허를 위한 것일 수도 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 이제는,\" 그는 목소리는 낮추되 눈길은 거두지 않으며 덧붙였다. \"이제는 그대가 못할 일이 아무것도 없소.\"\n\n그 순간은 그곳에 있는 모든 이에게 마음을 사로잡는 순간이었다. 시모니데스는 다시 두 손을 가슴 위에 포갰고, 에스더는 마음을 졸였으며, 일데림은 안절부절못했다. 사람은 지나친 행운의 순간만큼 호되게 시험받는 때가 없다.\n\n벤허는 두루마리를 받아 들고 일어섰으니, 북받치는 감정과 싸우고 있었다.\n\n\"이 모든 것은 내게 하늘에서 보내 준 빛과 같소. 너무도 길어서 결코 끝나지 않으리라 두려워했던 밤, 너무도 어두워서 다시 보리라는 희망마저 잃었던 밤을 몰아내려고 보내 주신 빛이오.\" 그가 잠긴 목소리로 말했다. \"먼저 나를 버리지 않으신 주님께 감사드리고, 그다음으로 그대에게 감사드리오, 시모니데스. 그대의 충심은 다른 이들의 잔인함을 무겁게 누르고, 우리 인간 본성을 구원하오. '내가 못할 일이 아무것도 없다.' 그럼 그렇게 하리다. 이토록 큰 특권이 주어진 이 시각에, 나보다 더 너그러운 자가 어디 있겠소? 이제 내 증인이 되어 주시오, 일데림 족장. 내가 말하는 대로 들으시오—듣고 기억해 주시오. 그리고 그대, 에스더, 이 선량한 분의 선한 천사여! 그대도 들으시오.\"\n\n그는 두루마리를 든 손을 시모니데스에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"이 문서가 셈해 놓은 것들을—그 모두를, 선박이며 가옥이며 물품이며 낙타며 말이며 돈이며, 가장 작은 것에서 가장 큰 것까지—그대에게 도로 돌려주겠소, 시모니데스. 그 모두를 그대의 것으로 삼아, 그대와 그대의 사람들에게 영원히 봉인하겠소.\"\n\n에스더는 눈물 사이로 미소 지었고, 일데림은 빠른 손짓으로 수염을 잡아당겼으니, 그 두 눈은 흑옥 구슬처럼 반짝였다. 오직 시모니데스만이 평온했다.\n\n\"그대와 그대의 사람들에게 영원히 봉인하겠소.\" 벤허는 한결 마음을 추스르고 말을 이었다. \"단 하나의 예외와, 단 하나의 조건을 두고서.\"\n\n듣는 이들의 숨결이 그의 말에 매달렸다.\n\n\"내 부친의 것이었던 백이십 달란트는 그대가 내게 돌려주시오.\"\n\n일데림의 얼굴이 환해졌다.\n\n\"그리고 그대는 나와 함께 내 어머니와 누이를 찾는 일에 나서야 하오. 내가 내 몫을 그 수색 비용에 내놓듯, 그대도 그대의 모든 것을 그 비용에 내놓을 셈치고서 말이오.\"\n\n시모니데스는 크게 감동했다. 그는 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"그대의 마음을 알겠소, 후르의 아들이여. 주님께서 그대를 지금 이 모습 그대로 내게 보내 주신 것을 감사하오. 내가 살아 계실 적의 그대 부친을 잘 섬겼고 그 뒤로도 그 기억을 잘 받들었다면, 그대에게 소홀할까는 염려치 마시오. 허나 한 가지 말해 둘 것은, 그 예외는 받아들일 수 없다는 것이오.\"\n\n그러고는 따로 남겨 두었던 장을 내보이며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그대는 아직 계산서를 다 보지 못했소. 이것을 받아 읽으시오—소리 내어 읽으시오.\"\n\n벤허는 그 부속 문서를 받아 읽었다.\n\n\"후르가의 종복 명세, 후르가 재산의 청지기 시모니데스가 작성함.\n\n1. 암라, 이집트인, 예루살렘의 저택을 지킴. 2. 시모니데스, 청지기, 안티오크에 있음. 3. 에스더, 시모니데스의 딸.\"\n\n그런데 시모니데스를 두고 생각할 때, 벤허의 머릿속에는 율법에 따라 딸이 부모의 신분을 잇는다는 사실이 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그녀를 마음속에 그릴 때면, 그 다정한 얼굴의 에스더는 늘 이집트 여인의 맞수로, 그리고 사랑할 수도 있을 대상으로 떠올랐다. 그는 그토록 느닷없이 닥쳐온 사실 앞에서 움츠러들며, 얼굴을 붉히는 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉힌 채 그의 앞에서 눈을 떨구었다. 그러고서 그는, 파피루스가 저절로 둘둘 말리는 동안 말했다.\n\n\"육백 달란트를 지닌 사람이라면 참으로 부유하고, 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 할 수 있을 것이오. 그러나 그 돈보다 더 드물고 그 재산보다 더 값을 매길 수 없는 것은, 그 부를 쌓아 올린 정신이며, 쌓아 올린 뒤에도 그 부가 더럽힐 수 없었던 마음이오. 시모니데스—그리고 그대, 어여쁜 에스더—두려워 마시오. 여기 일데림 족장이 증인이 되어 줄 것이니, 그대들이 내 종복이라 선언된 바로 그 순간, 그 순간에 나는 그대들이 자유롭다고 선언하는 바요. 그리고 내가 선언하는 것을 문서로도 남기겠소. 이것으로 충분치 않소? 내가 더 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"후르의 아들이여,\" 시모니데스가 말했다. \"참으로 그대는 종살이를 가볍게 만들어 주는구려. 내가 틀렸소. 그대가 못할 일이 더러 있으니, 그대는 율법상으로 우리를 자유롭게 만들 수는 없소. 나는 영원히 그대의 종이오. 어느 날 그대 부친과 함께 문으로 갔던 까닭이며, 내 귀에는 아직도 송곳 자국이 남아 있기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"내 부친이 그리하셨단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그분을 탓하지 마시오.\" 시모니데스가 황급히 외쳤다. \"내가 간청하였기에 그분께서 나를 그런 부류의 종으로 받아 주신 것이오. 나는 그 일을 한 번도 후회한 적이 없소. 그것은 라헬을, 여기 내 자식의 어미를 얻기 위해 내가 치른 값이었소. 자기와 같은 처지가 되지 않으면 내 아내가 되려 하지 않았던 그 라헬을 위해서 말이오.\"\n\n\"그분도 영원한 종이었소?\"\n\n\"그러하였소.\"\n\n벤허는 이루지 못할 소망의 아픔 속에서 방 안을 거닐었다.\n\n\"나는 전부터 부유했소.\" 그가 문득 걸음을 멈추고 말했다. \"너그러운 아리우스가 주신 선물로 부유했고, 이제는 이 더 큰 재산과, 그것을 이루어 낸 정신까지 더해졌소. 이 모든 일에 하느님의 뜻이 있는 것이 아니겠소? 내게 조언해 주시오, 시모니데스! 옳은 것을 보고 행하도록 도와주시오. 내 이름에 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 되도록 도와주시오. 그리고 그대가 율법상으로 내게 어떤 존재이든, 나도 그대에게 사실과 행실로 그러한 존재가 되겠소. 나는 영원히 그대의 종이 되겠소.\"\n\n시모니데스의 얼굴이 정말로 환히 빛났다.\n\n\"오, 돌아가신 내 주인의 아드님이여! 나는 돕는 것보다 더한 일을 하겠소. 내 온 마음과 정신을 다해 그대를 섬기겠소. 몸은 내게 없으니, 그것은 그대 부친의 일로 망가졌소. 허나 마음과 정신으로 그대를 섬기겠소. 우리 하느님의 제단과 그 위의 제물을 두고 맹세하오! 다만 내가 자처해 온 것을 정식으로 내게 맡겨만 주시오.\"\n\n\"그것을 말해 보시오.\" 벤허가 간절히 말했다.\n\n\"청지기로서 재산을 돌보는 일이 내 몫이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"지금부터 그대를 청지기로 여기겠소. 아니면 문서로 받기를 원하오?\"\n\n\"그대의 말 한마디면 충분하오. 부친께도 그러하였으니, 아드님께 더 바라지는 않겠소. 자, 이제 이해가 온전하다면—\" 시모니데스는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"내게는 온전하오.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 너, 라헬의 딸아, 말해 보아라!\" 시모니데스가 자기 어깨에서 그녀의 팔을 들어 내리며 말했다.\n\n그리하여 홀로 남겨진 에스더는 한순간 머뭇거리며 서 있었으니, 얼굴빛이 붉어졌다 가셨다 했다. 이윽고 그녀는 벤허에게 다가가, 유달리 고운 여인다움으로 말했다. \"저는 제 어머니보다 나은 사람이 아닙니다. 어머니가 가셨으니, 청하옵건대, 주인님, 제가 제 아버지를 돌보게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n벤허는 그녀의 손을 잡고 의자로 도로 이끌며 말했다. \"그대는 착한 아이로구려. 그대 뜻대로 하시오.\"\n\n시모니데스는 그녀의 팔을 다시 제 목에 올려놓았고, 방 안에는 한동안 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n시모니데스가 눈을 들었으니, 그래도 여전히 주인의 풍모였다.\n\n\"에스더야.\" 그가 조용히 말했다. \"밤이 빠르게 깊어 가는구나. 앞에 놓인 일을 감당하기에 너무 지쳐 버리지 않도록, 요기할 것을 가져오너라.\"\n\n그녀가 종을 울렸다. 하인이 포도주와 빵을 내왔고, 그녀가 그것을 돌렸다.\n\n\"좋은 나의 주인이시여.\" 모두에게 음식이 돌아가자 시모니데스가 말을 이었다. \"제가 보기에 우리 사이의 이해는 아직 온전치 못합니다. 이제부터 우리의 삶은 만나서 서로의 물줄기를 합친 강물처럼 함께 흘러갈 것입니다. 그 흐름 위의 하늘에서 구름 한 점까지 모두 걷힌다면, 그 흐름은 더욱 나아질 것이라 생각합니다. 도련님은 며칠 전 제 문을 떠나실 때, 제가 방금 가장 폭넓은 말로 인정한 그 권리를 거부당하기라도 한 것처럼 보였습니다. 그러나 결코 그런 것이 아니었습니다, 정말이지 그렇지 않았습니다. 제가 도련님을 알아보았다는 것은 에스더가 증인이요, 제가 도련님을 저버리지 않았다는 것은 말루크가 말해 줄 것입니다.\"\n\n\"말루크라니!\" 벤허가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"저처럼 의자에 묶인 몸이라면, 그토록 모질게 가로막힌 세상을 움직이려 할 때 멀리까지 뻗는 손이 여럿 있어야 합니다. 저에게는 그런 손이 많고, 말루크는 그 가운데 가장 뛰어난 자의 하나입니다. 그리고 때로는\"—그는 족장에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다—\"때로는 너그러운 일데림처럼 마음이 어진 다른 분들에게서 도움을 빌리기도 합니다. 어질고도 용감한 분이지요. 제가 도련님을 거부했는지 잊었는지, 저분에게 물어보십시오.\"\n\n벤허가 아랍인을 바라보았다.\n\n\"이분이로군요, 좋은 일데림이시여. 이분이 저에 관해 당신께 말씀드린 분이로군요?\"\n\n일데림의 두 눈이 반짝였고, 그는 고개를 끄덕여 답했다.\n\n\"오, 나의 주인이시여.\" 시모니데스가 말했다. \"시험해 보지 않고서 어찌 한 사람이 어떤 인물인지 알 수 있겠습니까? 저는 도련님을 알았고, 도련님 안에서 부친을 보았습니다. 그러나 도련님이 어떤 부류의 사람인지는 알지 못했습니다. 어떤 이들에게 행운은 변장한 저주입니다. 도련님이 그런 부류였습니까? 저는 그것을 알아내고자 말루크를 보냈고, 그 일을 하는 동안 그는 저의 눈이요 귀였습니다. 그를 탓하지 마십시오. 그가 가져온 도련님에 관한 보고는 한결같이 좋은 것이었습니다.\"\n\n\"탓하지 않습니다.\" 벤허가 진심으로 말했다. \"당신의 선의에는 지혜가 깃들어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 말씀이 제게는 참으로 기쁩니다.\" 상인이 감격하여 말했다. \"참으로 기쁩니다. 오해에 대한 두려움이 가셨습니다. 이제 하느님께서 인도하시는 대로 강물이 흘러가게 하십시오.\"\n\n잠시 사이를 둔 뒤 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 저는 진실에 떠밀려 말씀드리겠습니다. 직조공이 앉아서 베를 짜면, 북이 날아갈수록 천이 늘어나고 무늬가 자라나며, 그 사이 그는 꿈을 꿉니다. 그처럼 제 손에서 재산이 불어났고, 저는 그 불어남에 놀라 여러 번 스스로에게 까닭을 물었습니다. 제가 벌인 사업마다 제 것이 아닌 어떤 보살핌이 함께함을 볼 수 있었습니다. 사막에서 남들을 후려친 모래폭풍은 제 것을 뛰어넘어 갔습니다. 해안을 난파선으로 뒤덮은 폭풍우는 오히려 제 배들을 더 빨리 항구로 몰아넣을 뿐이었습니다. 무엇보다 기이한 것은, 그토록 남들에게 의지하며 죽은 물건처럼 한자리에 붙박인 제가, 대리인 때문에 손해를 본 적이 단 한 번도 없었다는 것입니다, 단 한 번도. 천지만물이 몸을 굽혀 저를 섬겼고, 사실 제 모든 종이 충실했습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 기이한 일이군요.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"저도 그렇게 말했고, 거듭 그렇게 말했습니다. 마침내, 오, 나의 주인이시여, 마침내 저는 도련님과 같은 생각에 이르렀습니다. 하느님께서 그 안에 계셨던 것입니다. 그리고 도련님처럼 저도 물었습니다, 그분의 뜻이 무엇일 수 있는가? 지혜는 결코 헛되이 쓰이지 않습니다. 하느님의 지혜 같은 것은 뜻 없이 움직이는 법이 없습니다. 보십시오, 저는 이 여러 해 동안 그 물음을 가슴에 품고 답을 기다려 왔습니다. 만약 하느님께서 그 안에 계신다면, 언젠가 그분의 좋은 때에, 그분의 방식으로, 언덕 위에 회칠한 집처럼 환하게 드러내며 그 뜻을 제게 보여 주시리라 확신했습니다. 그리고 저는 그분께서 그리하셨다고 믿습니다.\"\n\n벤허는 온 정신을 모아 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"여러 해 전, 제 식솔과 함께—네 어머니도 나와 함께였단다, 에스더야, 옛 올리벳 위로 떠오르는 아침처럼 아름다웠지—저는 예루살렘 북쪽 바깥, 왕들의 무덤 가까운 길가에 앉아 있었습니다. 그때 세 사람이 거대한 흰 낙타를 타고 지나갔는데, 거룩한 도성에서 한 번도 본 적 없는 낙타였습니다. 그들은 낯선 이들로, 먼 나라에서 온 사람들이었습니다. 첫 번째 사람이 멈추어 서서 제게 물었습니다. '유대인의 왕으로 나신 분이 어디 계십니까?' 그러고는 제 놀라움을 달래기라도 하려는 듯 이렇게 덧붙였습니다. '우리는 동방에서 그분의 별을 보고, 그분께 경배하러 왔습니다.' 저는 영문을 알 수 없었으나 다마스쿠스 문까지 그들을 따라갔습니다. 그들은 길에서 마주치는 사람마다—심지어 문을 지키는 보초에게까지—그 물음을 던졌습니다. 그것을 들은 모든 이가 저처럼 놀랐습니다. 그 일은 메시아의 전조라며 말이 많았으나, 세월이 흐르며 저는 그 일을 잊었습니다. 아아, 아아! 가장 지혜로운 자라 한들 우리는 얼마나 어린아이 같은지요! 하느님께서 이 땅을 걸으실 때, 그분의 발걸음은 흔히 수백 년씩 떨어져 있습니다. 도련님은 발타사르를 만나셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그분이 자기 이야기를 들려주는 것을 들었습니다.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"기적입니다! 그야말로 기적입니다!\" 시모니데스가 외쳤다. \"좋은 나의 주인이시여, 그분이 그 이야기를 들려주실 때, 저는 그토록 오래 기다려 온 그 답을 듣는 것만 같았습니다. 하느님의 뜻이 제게 활짝 열렸습니다. 그 왕이 오실 때 그분은 가난할 것입니다—가난하고 벗도 없이, 따르는 무리도 없고, 군대도 없고, 도성도 성채도 없이 말입니다. 세워야 할 왕국이 있고, 로마는 무너뜨려 자취를 지워야 합니다. 보십시오, 보십시오, 오, 나의 주인이시여! 도련님은 힘이 넘치고, 무예로 단련되었으며, 재물을 가득 짊어지셨습니다. 주께서 도련님께 보내신 이 기회를 보십시오! 그분의 뜻이 곧 도련님의 뜻이 되어서는 안 되겠습니까? 사람이 이보다 더 완전한 영광을 위해 태어날 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n시모니데스는 그 호소에 온 힘을 쏟아부었다.\n\n\"하지만 그 왕국, 그 왕국 말입니다!\" 벤허가 간절하게 답했다. \"발타사르는 그것이 영혼의 왕국이 될 것이라 말합니다.\"\n\n시모니데스 안에는 유대인의 자긍심이 강하게 살아 있었으니, 그가 답을 시작하며 입술이 살짝 비웃음으로 일그러진 것도 그 때문이었다.\n\n\"발타사르는 놀라운 일들의 증인이었습니다—기적의 증인 말입니다, 오, 나의 주인이시여. 그가 그 일들을 말할 때 저는 믿음으로 머리를 숙입니다. 그것은 그가 몸소 보고 들은 것이기 때문입니다. 그러나 그는 미스라임의 자손이요, 개종자조차 아닙니다. 하느님께서 우리 이스라엘을 다루시는 일에 관하여 우리가 그에게 머리를 숙여야 할 만큼, 그가 특별한 지식을 지녔다고 보기는 어렵습니다. 예언자들은 그가 그러했듯 하늘에서 직접 빛을 받았습니다—여럿이 하나에게서, 그리고 여호와께서는 영원토록 한결같으십니다. 저는 예언자들을 믿어야 합니다.—토라를 가져오너라, 에스더야.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 기다리지 않고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"한 민족 전체의 증언이 가벼이 여겨질 수 있겠습니까, 나의 주인이시여? 북쪽 바닷가에 있는 티레에서 남쪽 사막에 있는 에돔의 수도까지 두루 다니신다 해도, 셰마를 읊조리는 자, 성전에서 자선을 베푸는 자, 유월절 어린양을 먹어 본 자 가운데, 그 왕이 우리, 곧 언약의 자손들을 위해 세우러 오시는 왕국이 우리 조상 다윗의 왕국처럼 이 세상의 것이 아니라고 말할 사람을 단 하나도 찾지 못할 것입니다. 그렇다면 그들이 그 믿음을 어디서 얻었는지 물으시겠지요! 곧 알게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n그때 에스더가 돌아왔는데, 짙은 갈색 아마포에 정성껏 싸여 금빛으로 예스럽게 글자가 새겨진 두루마리를 여러 개 들고 왔다.\n\n\"잘 간수하고 있다가, 얘야, 내가 청할 때 하나씩 다오.\" 아버지가 그녀에게 말할 때면 늘 쓰던 다정한 목소리로 말하고는, 그 논변을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"좋은 나의 주인이시여, 하느님의 섭리 가운데 예언자들의 뒤를 이은 거룩한 사람들의 이름을 도련님께 일일이 되풀이하기에는 너무 길고—참으로 너무 길 것입니다—그들은 예언자들보다 조금 덜 은총을 입었을 뿐입니다. 바빌론 유수 이후 글을 쓴 선견자들과 가르침을 베푼 설교자들, 그리고 자기 계보의 마지막인 말라기의 등불에서 빛을 빌려 온 더없이 지혜로운 자들, 힐렐과 샴마이가 학당에서 그 위대한 이름들을 입에 올리기를 결코 싫증 내지 않던 그들 말입니다. 도련님은 그들에게 그 왕국에 관하여 물으시겠습니까? 자, 에녹서에 나오는 양들의 주님—그는 누구입니까? 우리가 말하고 있는 그 왕이 아니면 누구이겠습니까? 그를 위해 보좌가 세워지고, 그가 땅을 치니 다른 왕들이 그 보좌에서 떨려 나며, 이스라엘을 매질하던 자들은 불기둥으로 타오르는 불의 동굴 속으로 던져집니다. 솔로몬의 시편을 노래한 자도 마찬가지입니다—'보소서, 오 주여, 이스라엘에게 그들의 왕을, 다윗의 아들을 일으켜 세우소서, 당신께서 아시는 그때에, 오 하느님이시여, 당신의 자녀인 이스라엘을 다스리게 하소서.…… 그리고 그는 이방의 백성들을 그의 멍에 아래 끌어들여 그를 섬기게 하리이다.…… 그리고 그는 하느님께 가르침을 받은 의로운 왕이 되리니, …… 그는 그 입의 말씀으로 온 땅을 영원토록 다스릴 것이기 때문이니이다.' 그리고 마지막으로, 그러나 결코 못하지 않은, 제2의 모세인 에스라가 밤의 환상 속에서 한 말을 들으십시오. 그에게 물어보십시오, 독수리—이는 곧 로마입니다—에게 이렇게 말하는, 사람의 목소리를 가진 사자가 누구인지를. '너는 거짓말쟁이들을 사랑하였고, 부지런한 자들의 도성을 뒤엎었으며, 그들이 네게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았건만 그 성벽을 허물었다. 그러므로 물러가라, 땅이 새로워지고 스스로 회복하며, 그를 지으신 분의 공의와 경건을 바라게 하라.' 그러자 그 독수리는 더는 보이지 않았습니다. 참으로, 오, 나의 주인이시여, 이들의 증언만으로도 넉넉할 것입니다! 그러나 샘의 발원지로 가는 길은 열려 있습니다. 곧바로 그리로 올라가 봅시다.—포도주를 좀 다오, 에스더야. 그러고 나서 토라를 다오.\"\n\n\"도련님은 예언자들을 믿으십니까, 주인이시여?\" 그는 포도주를 마신 뒤 물었다. \"믿으시리라는 것을 압니다. 도련님의 온 일가가 그러한 믿음을 지녔으니까요.—에스더야, 이사야의 환상이 담긴 책을 다오.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 펴서 풀어 준 두루마리 가운데 하나를 받아 들고 읽었다. \"'어둠 속을 걷던 백성이 큰 빛을 보았고, 죽음의 그늘진 땅에 거하던 자들 위에 빛이 비치었도다.…… 우리에게 한 아기가 태어났고, 우리에게 한 아들이 주어졌으니, 그 정권이 그의 어깨에 있으리로다.…… 그 정권과 평강의 더함이 끝이 없을 것이며, 다윗의 보좌와 그의 나라 위에 군림하여, 이제부터 영원토록 공평과 정의로 그것을 굳게 세우리로다.'—예언자들을 믿으십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?—이제, 에스더야, 미가에게 임한 주님의 말씀을 다오.\"\n\n그녀가 그가 청한 두루마리를 건넸다.\n\n\"'그러나 너'.\" 그가 읽기 시작했다. \"'그러나 너 베들레헴 에브라다야, 너는 유다의 수많은 무리 가운데 작을지라도, 이스라엘을 다스릴 자가 너에게서 내게로 나오리라.'—이가 바로 그분, 발타사르가 동굴에서 보고 경배한 바로 그 아기입니다. 예언자들을 믿으십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?—에스더야, 예레미야의 말씀을 다오.\"\n\n그 두루마리를 받아 그는 앞서처럼 읽었다. \"'보라, 날이 이르리니, 주께서 말씀하시되, 내가 다윗에게 한 의로운 가지를 일으키리라, 한 왕이 다스려 형통하며, 세상에서 공평과 정의를 행하리라. 그의 날에 유다가 구원을 받고, 이스라엘이 평안히 거하리라.' 그분은 왕으로서 다스릴 것입니다—왕으로서 말입니다, 오, 나의 주인이시여! 예언자들을 믿으십니까?—이제, 얘야, 흠 없던 유다의 그 아들이 한 말이 담긴 두루마리를 다오.\"\n\n그녀가 그에게 다니엘서를 건넸다.\n\n\"들으십시오, 나의 주인이시여.\" 그가 말했다. \"'내가 밤의 환상 가운데 보았는데, 보라, 인자 같은 이가 하늘 구름을 타고 오더라.…… 그에게 권세와 영광과 나라가 주어져, 모든 백성과 나라와 언어가 그를 섬기게 하니, 그의 권세는 영원한 권세라 사라지지 아니할 것이요, 그의 나라는 결코 멸망하지 아니하리라.'—예언자들을 믿으십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?\"\n\n\"충분합니다. 저는 믿습니다.\" 벤허가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면 어떻게 하시겠습니까?\" 시모니데스가 물었다. \"만약 그 왕이 가난하게 오신다면, 나의 주인은 그 풍족함으로 그분을 도와드리지 않으시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그분을 돕는다고요? 마지막 한 푼까지, 마지막 숨까지 바칠 것입니다. 그런데 어찌하여 그분이 가난하게 오신다고 말하십니까?\"\n\n\"에스더야, 스가랴에게 임한 주님의 말씀을 다오.\" 시모니데스가 말했다.\n\n그녀가 그에게 두루마리 하나를 건넸다.\n\n\"그 왕이 어떻게 예루살렘에 들어오실지 들으십시오.\" 그러고는 그가 읽었다. \"'크게 기뻐할지어다, 오 시온의 딸아.…… 보라, 네 왕이 공의와 구원을 가지고 네게 임하시나니, 그는 겸손하여 나귀를 타시며, 나귀의 새끼인 어린 나귀를 타셨도다.'\"\n\n벤허가 시선을 돌렸다.\n\n\"무엇을 보십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?\"\n\n\"로마입니다!\" 그가 침울하게 답했다. \"로마와 그 군단들 말입니다. 저는 그들의 진영에서 그들과 함께 지냈습니다. 저는 그들을 압니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 시모니데스가 말했다. \"도련님은 그 왕을 위한 군단의 주인이 되실 것입니다, 수백만 가운데서 골라 거느리면서 말입니다.\"\n\n\"수백만이라고요!\" 벤허가 외쳤다.\n\n시모니데스는 잠시 앉아 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"힘의 문제는 도련님을 괴롭히지 않을 것입니다.\" 그가 이어 말했다.\n\n벤허가 의아한 듯 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"도련님은 그 겸손한 왕이 자기 백성에게 임하시는 모습을 보고 계셨습니다.\" 시모니데스가 답했다. \"이를테면 오른편에는 그분을, 왼편에는 카이사르의 놋쇠 같은 군단을 두고 보면서, 이렇게 물으셨겠지요, 그분이 무엇을 하실 수 있겠는가?\"\n\n\"바로 제 생각이 그것이었습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 나의 주인이시여!\" 시모니데스가 말을 이었다. \"도련님은 우리 이스라엘이 얼마나 강한지 모르십니다. 도련님은 그를 바빌론 강가에서 우는 슬픈 노인쯤으로 여기십니다. 그러나 다음 유월절에 예루살렘으로 올라가, 크시스토스나 흥정의 거리에 서서, 그가 어떤 모습인지 보십시오. 파단아람을 떠나오던 우리 조상 야곱에게 주신 주님의 약속은, 우리 백성이 번성하기를 그치지 않게 한 율법이었습니다—심지어 유수 중에도 그러했습니다. 그들은 이집트인의 발밑에서 자라났고, 로마인의 움켜쥠도 그들에게는 오히려 건강한 양분이었습니다. 이제 그들은 참으로 '한 민족이요 많은 민족의 무리'입니다. 그뿐이 아닙니다, 나의 주인이시여. 사실 이스라엘의 힘을 가늠하는 일은—이는 곧 그 왕이 무엇을 하실 수 있는지를 가늠하는 일입니다—자연스러운 번성의 잣대에만 의지해서는 안 되고, 거기에 또 다른 것을 더해야 합니다. 곧 믿음의 퍼짐을 말합니다. 그것은 도련님을 알려진 온 땅의 멀고 가까운 곳으로 데려갈 것입니다. 더 나아가, 사람들이 예루살렘을 이스라엘이라 여기고 말하는 버릇이 있음을 압니다. 그러나 이는 마치 수놓인 자투리 천 한 조각을 발견하고는 그것을 카이사르의 위엄 있는 옷이라며 치켜드는 것과 같습니다. 예루살렘은 성전의 돌 하나에 지나지 않고, 몸속의 심장에 지나지 않습니다. 강하기는 하지만 그 군단들을 바라보던 눈을 돌려, '네 장막으로 돌아가라, 오 이스라엘아!' 하는 옛 외침을 기다리는 신실한 무리를 헤아려 보십시오.—돌아오는 자들과 함께 돌아오기를 택하지 않은 자들의 자손인, 페르시아의 수많은 이들을 헤아려 보십시오. 이집트와 그 너머 아프리카의 저자에 들끓는 형제들을 헤아려 보십시오. 서쪽에서—로디눔과 에스파냐의 교역 마당에서—이문을 챙기는 히브리 이주민들을 헤아려 보십시오. 그리스와 바다의 섬들, 저 멀리 폰토스, 그리고 이곳 안티오크에서, 또 그런 까닭에 로마 자신의 부정한 성벽 그늘 아래 저주받아 누운 그 도성의 사람들 가운데, 순수한 혈통의 자들과 개종자들을 헤아려 보십시오. 우리와 인접한 사막을 따라 장막에 거하는 주님의 경배자들을, 나일강 너머 사막에 거하는 자들과 더불어 헤아려 보십시오. 카스피해 건너편 지역, 그리고 곡과 마곡의 옛 땅에 이르기까지, 하느님을 인정하는 표시로 해마다 거룩한 성전에 예물을 보내는 자들을 가려내십시오—그들도 헤아릴 수 있도록 가려내십시오. 그리고 헤아리기를 마치면, 보십시오, 나의 주인이시여, 도련님을 기다리는 칼을 쥔 손들의 호적이 드러날 것입니다. 보십시오, '온 땅에서 공평과 정의를'—시온에서 못지않게 로마에서도—행하실 그분을 위해 이미 빚어진 왕국이 드러날 것입니다. 그러니 답을 가지십시오, 이스라엘이 할 수 있는 것, 그것을 그 왕도 하실 수 있습니다.\"\n\n그 그림은 열렬하게 그려졌다.\n\n일데림에게는 그것이 나팔 소리처럼 작용했다. \"오, 내 젊음을 되찾을 수만 있다면!\" 그가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다.\n\n벤허는 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그 말은 자기 삶과 재산을 그 신비로운 존재에게 바치라는 권유임을 그는 알아차렸다. 그 존재는 경건한 이집트인에게 그러하듯 시모니데스에게도 분명 위대한 희망의 중심이었다. 우리가 보아 왔듯이, 그 생각은 새로운 것이 아니라 그에게 거듭 찾아왔던 것이다. 한 번은 다프네 숲에서 말루크의 말을 들을 때였고, 그 뒤에는 발타사르가 그 왕국이 어떤 것이 될지 자기 생각을 펼칠 때 더욱 또렷하게 찾아왔으며, 그보다 더 뒤, 옛 과수원을 거닐 때는 거의, 어쩌면 완전히, 하나의 결심으로까지 솟아오른 적도 있었다. 그러나 그럴 때마다 그것은 다만 하나의 생각으로서 오갔을 뿐이며, 더하거나 덜한 절실한 감정을 거느리고 있었다. 그러나 지금은 달랐다. 한 주인이 그 일을 떠맡았고, 한 주인이 그것을 빚어 가고 있었다. 이미 그는 그것을 무수한 가능성으로 찬란하고 한없이 거룩한 하나의 대의로 끌어올렸던 것이다. 그 효과는 마치 그때껏 보이지 않던 문이 갑자기 열려, 벤허에게 빛이 쏟아져 들고, 그가 한결같이 꿈꾸어 온 그 완전한 일에 그를 들어서게 하는 것과 같았다—먼 미래까지 뻗어 가고, 다한 의무의 보상으로 풍성하며, 그의 야망을 달래고 어루만질 상급으로 가득한 일이었다. 한 번의 손길만 더 필요했다.\n\n\"당신이 하신 모든 말씀을 인정하기로 합시다, 오, 시모니데스.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"그 왕이 오시고, 그 왕국이 솔로몬의 왕국과 같으리라는 것을. 또한 제가 저 자신과 제가 가진 모든 것을 그분과 그분의 대의에 바칠 각오가 되어 있다고 합시다. 더 나아가, 제 삶을 그렇게 빚으시고 당신의 놀라운 재산을 그토록 빠르게 쌓게 하신 것이 하느님의 뜻이었던 대로 제가 행해야 한다고 합시다. 그렇다면 어떻게 하지요? 우리는 눈먼 자들이 집을 짓듯 나아가야 합니까? 그 왕이 오실 때까지 기다려야 합니까? 아니면 그분이 저를 부르실 때까지요? 당신께는 연륜과 경험이 있습니다. 답해 주십시오.\"\n\n시모니데스가 곧바로 답했다.\n\n\"우리에게 선택의 여지는 없습니다, 전혀 없습니다. 이 편지가\"—그는 말하면서 메살라의 급보를 꺼냈다—\"이 편지가 행동에 나서라는 신호입니다. 메살라와 그라투스 사이에 제안된 동맹에 맞서기에 우리는 그만큼 강하지 못합니다. 로마에 영향력도 없고, 이곳에 무력도 없습니다. 우리가 기다린다면 그들은 도련님을 죽일 것입니다. 그들이 얼마나 자비로운 자들인지, 저를 보고 헤아리십시오.\"\n\n그는 그 끔찍한 기억에 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"오, 좋은 나의 주인이시여.\" 그는 마음을 추스르며 말을 이었다. \"도련님은 얼마나 강하십니까—제 말은, 의지에 있어서 말입니다.\"\n\n벤허는 그의 말을 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n\"저는 젊은 시절 이 세상이 제게 얼마나 즐거웠는지 기억합니다.\" 시모니데스가 이어 말했다.\n\n\"그런데도.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"당신은 위대한 희생을 감당할 수 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 사랑을 위해서 말입니다.\"\n\n\"삶에는 그만큼 강한 다른 동기가 없습니까?\"\n\n시모니데스가 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"야망이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"야망은 이스라엘의 자손에게 금지되어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 복수는 어떻습니까?\"\n\n그 불꽃이 불붙기 쉬운 열정 위로 떨어졌다. 그의 두 눈이 번득였고, 두 손이 떨렸다. 그가 재빨리 답했다. \"복수는 유대인의 당연한 권리입니다. 그것은 율법입니다.\"\n\n\"낙타도, 개조차도, 당한 해코지를 기억합니다.\" 일데림이 외쳤다.\n\n곧이어 시모니데스는 끊어졌던 생각의 실타래를 다시 집어 들었다.\n\n\"한 가지 일이 있습니다, 그 왕을 위한 일이, 그분이 오시기에 앞서 마쳐 두어야 할 일이 말입니다. 이스라엘이 그분의 오른팔이 되리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그러나, 아아! 그것은 평화의 손이라, 싸움의 솜씨가 없습니다. 그 수백만 가운데 훈련된 부대 하나, 지휘관 하나 없습니다. 헤롯 일가의 용병들은 헤아리지 않습니다. 그들은 우리를 짓밟기 위해 길러진 자들이니까요. 그 형편은 로마인이 바라는 그대로입니다. 그의 정책은 그의 폭정을 위해 좋은 열매를 맺었습니다. 그러나 변화의 때가 가까이 왔으니, 목자가 갑옷을 두르고 창과 칼을 잡으며, 풀을 뜯던 양 떼가 싸우는 사자로 바뀌는 때 말입니다. 누군가는, 내 아들아, 그 왕의 오른편에서 그분 곁의 자리를 차지해야 합니다. 이 일을 잘 해내는 자가 아니라면 누가 그 자리에 앉겠습니까?\"\n\n벤허의 얼굴이 그 전망에 붉어졌으나, 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"알겠습니다. 그러나 분명하게 말씀해 주십시오. 해야 할 일이 있다는 것은 하나요, 그것을 어떻게 하느냐는 또 다른 일입니다.\"\n\n시모니데스는 에스더가 가져온 포도주를 한 모금 마시고 답했다.\n\n\"족장과 도련님이, 나의 주인이시여, 각기 한몫을 맡은 주역이 되실 것입니다. 저는 이곳에 남아 지금처럼 일을 꾸려 가며, 그 샘이 마르지 않도록 살피겠습니다. 도련님은 예루살렘으로, 거기서 다시 광야로 가셔서, 이스라엘의 싸울 수 있는 자들을 헤아리기 시작하고, 그들을 열 명 단위, 백 명 단위로 셈하며, 지휘관을 뽑아 훈련시키고, 은밀한 곳에 무기를 쌓아 두십시오. 그 무기는 제가 계속 대어 드리겠습니다. 페레아에서 시작하여, 그다음 갈릴리로 가십시오. 거기서 예루살렘까지는 한 걸음에 지나지 않습니다. 페레아에서는 사막이 도련님의 등 뒤에 있고, 일데림이 도련님의 손이 닿는 거리에 있을 것입니다. 그는 길을 지킬 것이니, 도련님이 모르는 채로 지나갈 것은 아무것도 없을 것입니다. 그는 여러모로 도련님을 도울 것입니다. 무르익을 때가 오기까지는 여기서 맺은 약조를 아무도 모를 것입니다. 제 몫은 다만 종의 몫일 뿐입니다. 저는 일데림과 이야기를 나누었습니다. 도련님은 어떻게 말씀하시겠습니까?\"\n\n벤허가 족장을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그의 말 그대로다, 후르의 아들이여.\" 아랍인이 답했다. \"나는 이미 내 말을 주었고, 그는 그것으로 흡족해한다. 그러나 그대에게는 나를 묶는 맹세를 주겠다. 내 부족의 늘 준비된 손들과, 내게 쓸모 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 함께 말이다.\"\n\n세 사람—시모니데스, 일데림, 에스더—이 벤허를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"사람은 누구나.\" 그가 처음에는 슬프게 답했다. \"자기 몫으로 부어진 즐거움의 잔을 가지고 있어, 이르든 늦든 그것이 그의 손에 닿으면 맛보고 마십니다—저만 빼고 누구나 그렇습니다. 알겠습니다, 시모니데스, 그리고 그대, 오, 너그러운 족장이시여!—저는 이 제안이 어디로 향하는지 알겠습니다. 제가 받아들여 그 길에 들어선다면, 평화도, 그것을 둘러싼 온갖 희망도 안녕입니다. 제가 들어설 수 있었을 문들, 평온한 삶으로 통하는 그 문들은 제 뒤에서 닫혀 다시는 열리지 않을 것입니다. 로마가 그 모든 문을 쥐고 있기 때문입니다. 로마의 추방령이 저를 따라다닐 것이고, 로마의 사냥꾼들도 그러할 것입니다. 그리고 도성 가까운 무덤 속과 가장 외진 언덕의 음산한 동굴 속에서, 저는 제 빵 부스러기를 먹고 쉬어야 할 것입니다.\"\n\n그 말이 흐느낌에 끊겼다. 모두 에스더에게로 고개를 돌렸으니, 그녀가 아버지의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 있었다.\n\n\"내 너를 미처 생각하지 못했구나, 에스더야.\" 시모니데스가 부드럽게 말했으니, 그 자신이 깊이 마음이 움직인 까닭이었다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 시모니데스.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"사람은 자기를 가엾이 여기는 마음이 있음을 알면, 모진 운명도 더 잘 견디는 법입니다. 계속하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n그들이 다시 그에게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"제가 말하려던 것은.\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"저에게 선택의 여지가 없으니, 당신이 제게 맡기는 몫을 받겠다는 것입니다. 그리고 이곳에 남는 것이 곧 비천한 죽음을 맞는 일이니, 저는 곧바로 그 일에 착수하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 문서를 작성할까요?\" 시모니데스가 사업가다운 버릇에 이끌려 물었다.\n\n\"저는 당신의 말씀에 의지하겠습니다.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"저 또한 그렇습니다.\" 일데림이 답했다.\n\n이렇게 단출하게, 벤허의 삶을 바꾸어 놓을 그 맹약이 맺어졌다. 그리고 거의 곧바로 벤허가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그러면 다 된 것입니다.\"\n\n\"아브라함의 하느님께서 우리를 도우시기를!\" 시모니데스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이제 한마디만, 벗들이여.\" 벤허가 한결 밝게 말했다. \"허락해 주신다면, 경기가 끝날 때까지는 제 몸을 제 뜻대로 두겠습니다. 메살라가 자기 편지에 총독이 답할 시간을 주기 전에 저를 위태롭게 할 일을 꾸미지는 않을 것 같습니다. 그 답은 그가 편지를 보낸 지 이레 안에는 오지 못할 것입니다. 원형경기장에서 그를 만나는 것은, 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 사고 싶은 즐거움입니다.\"\n\n일데림은 크게 흡족하여 선뜻 동의했고, 사업에 골몰한 시모니데스가 덧붙였다. \"잘 생각하셨습니다. 보십시오, 나의 주인이시여, 그 미룸은 제가 도련님께 한 가지 좋은 일을 해 드릴 시간을 줄 것입니다. 제가 알기로 도련님은 아리우스에게서 물려받은 유산을 말씀하셨지요. 그것이 재산으로 되어 있습니까?\"\n\n\"미세눔 근처의 별장과, 로마에 있는 집들입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 저는 그 재산을 팔아, 그 대금을 안전하게 맡겨 두기를 권합니다. 제게 그 명세를 주시면, 위임장을 작성하고 곧바로 대리인을 그 일에 보내겠습니다. 적어도 이번 한 번은 제국의 도적들보다 한발 앞서겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내일 명세를 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면, 더 할 말이 없다면, 오늘 밤의 일은 다 마쳤습니다.\" 시모니데스가 말했다.\n\n일데림은 흡족하게 수염을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"그것도 잘 마쳤지요.\"\n\n\"빵과 포도주를 다시 다오, 에스더야. 일데림 족장께서 내일까지, 혹은 마음 내키시는 만큼 우리와 함께 머물러 주신다면 우리는 기쁠 것입니다. 그리고 도련님은—\"\n\n\"말을 끌어오게 해 주십시오.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"저는 과수원으로 돌아가겠습니다. 지금 가면 적이 저를 알아채지 못할 것이고, 그리고\"—그는 일데림을 흘긋 보았다—\"그 네 마리도 저를 보면 반가워할 것입니다.\"\n\n날이 밝아 올 무렵, 그와 말루크는 천막 문 앞에서 말에서 내렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n이튿날 밤, 네 번째 시각 무렵, 벤허는 거대한 창고의 옥상 테라스에 에스더와 함께 서 있었다. 그들 아래, 부두에서는 분주한 움직임이 한창이어서, 짐짝과 상자가 옮겨지고, 사람들이 고함을 질렀다. 허리를 구부리고, 들어 올리고, 끌어당기는 그들의 모습은, 그 일을 거들려고 피워 놓은 타닥거리는 횃불 빛 속에서, 동방의 환상적인 이야기에 나오는 일하는 정령들처럼 보였다. 갤리선 한 척이 당장 떠날 채비로 짐을 싣고 있었다. 시모니데스는 아직 사무실에서 나오지 않았으니, 마지막 순간에 그 배의 선장에게 로마의 항구인 오스티아까지 쉬지 말고 나아가, 거기서 승객 한 사람을 내려놓은 뒤, 더 느긋하게 에스파냐 해안의 발렌티아까지 계속 가라는 지시를 내릴 참이었다.\n\n그 승객은 두움비르 아리우스에게서 물려받은 재산을 처분하러 가는 대리인이다. 배의 밧줄이 풀리고, 뱃머리가 돌려져, 항해가 시작되면, 벤허는 전날 밤에 떠맡은 일에 돌이킬 수 없이 매이게 된다. 만약 일데림과 맺은 약속을 후회할 마음이 있다면, 그것을 알리고 깨뜨릴 약간의 시간이 그에게 허락되어 있다. 그가 주인이니, 한마디 말만 하면 그만이다.\n\n아마도 그 순간 그의 마음속에 든 생각이 그러했을 것이다. 그는 팔짱을 낀 채, 자기 자신과 논쟁하는 사람의 태도로 그 광경을 바라보며 서 있었다. 젊고, 잘생기고, 부유하며, 최근까지 로마 사회의 귀족 계층에 속했던 사람이니, 세상이 그를 둘러싸고, 무법과 위험이 뒤따르는 짐스러운 의무나 야망에 더 이상 자신을 바치지 말라고 호소하는 모습을 떠올리기란 어렵지 않다. 그를 짓누른 논리마저 우리는 상상할 수 있다. 카이사르와 다투어 보았자 가망이 없다는 것, 왕과 그의 강림에 얽힌 모든 것을 가리고 있는 불확실함, 시장에서 물건을 사듯 살 수 있는 안락과 명예와 지위, 그리고 무엇보다도 가장 강력한 것, 새로이 얻은 가정에 대한 감각, 그것을 즐겁게 해 줄 벗들. 오랫동안 황량하게 떠돈 자만이 그 마지막 호소에 깃든 힘을 알 수 있으리라.\n\n이제 덧붙이자면, 세상은—언제나 제 스스로 충분히 교활하고, 언제나 약한 자에게 머물러라, 편히 쉬어라 하고 속삭이며, 언제나 삶의 양지바른 면을 내보이는 세상은—이 경우 벤허의 길동무의 도움까지 받았다.\n\"로마에 가 본 적이 있소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 에스더가 대답했다.\n\n\"가 보고 싶소?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않은 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"저는 로마가 두려워요.\" 그녀가 또렷이 떨리는 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n그러자 그는 그녀를 바라보았다. 아니, 그녀를 내려다보았다고 해야 옳으니, 그의 곁에서 그녀는 어린아이나 다름없어 보였기 때문이다. 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 또렷이 볼 수 없었다. 그 모습조차 그림자 같았다. 그러나 다시금 그는 티르자를 떠올렸고, 문득 부드러운 정이 그에게 밀려왔다. 그라투스에게 사고가 일어났던 그 재앙의 아침에, 잃어버린 누이가 바로 그렇게 지붕 위에서 그와 함께 서 있었던 것이다. 가엾은 티르자! 그녀는 지금 어디에 있는가? 에스더는 그렇게 불러일으켜진 그 감정의 덕을 보았다. 누이가 아니라 해도, 그는 결코 그녀를 자기 종으로 볼 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 실제로 그의 종이라는 사실은, 그가 늘 그녀에게 더욱 사려 깊고 다정하게 대하도록 만들 것이었다.\n\n\"저는 로마를 생각할 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 목소리를 가다듬고, 조용하고 여인다운 말투로 이어 갔다. \"저는 로마를 궁전과 신전이 가득하고 사람들이 북적이는 도시로는 생각할 수가 없어요. 제게 로마는 아름다운 땅 하나를 차지하고 들어앉아, 거기서 사람들을 파멸과 죽음으로 꾀어내는 괴물이에요. 도저히 맞설 수 없는 괴물, 피를 게걸스레 빨아먹는 굶주린 짐승이지요. 그런데 왜—\"\n\n그녀는 말을 더듬다가, 시선을 떨구고, 멈추었다.\n\n\"계속하시오.\" 벤허가 안심시키듯 말했다.\n\n그녀는 그에게 더 가까이 다가서서, 다시 고개를 들고 말했다. \"어째서 로마를 적으로 삼으셔야 하나요? 차라리 로마와 화평을 맺고 편히 쉬시는 것이 어떻겠어요? 당신은 숱한 고난을 겪고도 견뎌 내셨어요. 원수들이 놓은 덫에서도 살아남으셨고요. 슬픔이 당신의 젊음을 다 삼켜 버렸는데, 남은 날들마저 거기에 내주는 것이 옳은 일인가요?\"\n\n호소가 이어질수록, 그의 눈 아래 어린 소녀 같은 얼굴은 더 가까이 다가오며 더 창백해지는 듯했다. 그는 그 얼굴을 향해 몸을 숙이고 부드럽게 물었다. \"내가 어찌하기를 바라오, 에스더?\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 머뭇거리더니, 도리어 이렇게 물었다. \"로마 근처의 그 재산은 살 만한 집인가요?\"\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그리고 아름다운가요?\"\n\n\"아름답소. 정원과 조개껍데기 깔린 산책로 한가운데 자리 잡은 궁전이지. 안에도 밖에도 분수가 있고, 그늘진 구석마다 조각상이 서 있소. 둘레의 언덕들은 포도 덩굴로 뒤덮여 있고, 어찌나 높은지 네아폴리스와 베수비우스가 다 보인다오. 바다는 자줏빛으로 물든 푸른 너른 들판처럼 펼쳐지고, 그 위로 쉼 없이 움직이는 돛들이 점점이 박혀 있소. 카이사르도 근처에 시골 별장을 두고 있지만, 로마 사람들은 옛 아리우스 별장이 가장 아름답다고들 하오.\"\n\n\"그러면 그곳의 삶은 평온한가요?\"\n\n\"손님이 찾아올 때를 빼면, 그보다 더 평온한 여름날도, 그보다 더 평온한 달밤도 없었소. 이제는 옛 주인도 떠나고 나도 이곳에 와 있으니, 그 고요를 깨뜨릴 것은 아무것도 없소. 종들의 속삭임이나, 행복한 새들의 지저귐이나, 분수의 노니는 소리가 아니라면 말이오. 그것은 변함이 없소. 다만 날마다 옛 꽃들이 시들어 떨어지고 새 꽃들이 움트고 피어나며, 햇살이 지나가는 구름 그림자에 자리를 내주는 것 말고는. 에스더, 그 삶은 내게는 너무 평온했소. 할 일이 그토록 많은 내가 게으른 습관에 빠져들고, 비단 사슬로 나 자신을 옭아매며, 얼마 지나지 않아—그것도 그리 긴 세월이 아니라—아무것도 이룬 것 없이 끝나 버리리라는 느낌을 끊임없이 일깨우며 나를 불안하게 만들었소.\"\n\n그녀는 강 건너편으로 시선을 돌렸다.\n\n\"왜 물어본 거요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"주인님—\"\n\n\"아니, 아니오, 에스더, 그렇게 부르지 마시오. 친구라고 부르시오. 원한다면 오라비라 불러도 좋소. 나는 당신의 주인이 아니고, 주인이 되지도 않을 것이오. 오라비라 부르시오.\"\n\n기쁨으로 그녀의 얼굴을 붉게 물들인 홍조도, 강물 위 허공 속으로 사라져 간 두 눈의 빛도, 그는 보지 못했다.\n\n\"저는 이해할 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 택하시려는 그런 삶을—그런—\"\n\n\"폭력의 삶, 어쩌면 피의 삶이겠지.\" 그가 그녀의 말을 마무리 지었다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그 아름다운 별장에서 누릴 수 있는 삶보다 그런 삶을 더 좋아하실 수 있는 그런 본성을요.\"\n\n\"에스더, 당신이 잘못 알고 있소. 좋아하고 말고가 아니오. 아아! 로마인은 그렇게 너그럽지 않다오. 나는 어쩔 수 없이 가는 것이오. 여기 머무는 것은 곧 죽는 것이고, 거기로 간다 해도 끝은 마찬가지일 것이오. 독을 탄 잔, 자객의 칼, 아니면 위증으로 얻어 낸 재판관의 판결이겠지. 메살라와 충독 그라투스는 내 아버지의 재산을 약탈해 부유해졌으니, 그들에게는 처음 그것을 손에 넣었을 때보다 지금 그 이득을 지키는 것이 더 중요한 일이오. 화평한 합의란 손에 닿지 않는 것이니, 그것은 곧 자기들의 죄를 인정하는 일이 되기 때문이오. 그리고 또한—또한—아, 에스더, 설령 내가 그들을 돈으로 살 수 있다 해도, 그러고 싶을지 모르겠소. 나는 내게 평화가 가능하다고 믿지 않소. 그렇소, 옛 별장의 대리석 회랑의 졸음 겨운 그늘과 향긋한 공기 속에서도 그렇지 못할 것이오. 거기서 누가 내 곁에 있어 날들의 짐을 함께 져 주든, 그녀가 얼마나 사랑의 인내로 그 일에 힘쓰든 말이오. 내 식구들이 사라진 채로 있는 한, 내게 평화란 가능하지 않소. 그들을 찾아내기 위해 나는 늘 경계하고 있어야 하기 때문이오. 만약 내가 그들을 찾아냈는데 그들이 부당한 일을 당했다면, 죄지은 자가 그 대가를 치러야 마땅하지 않겠소? 만약 그들이 폭력으로 죽임을 당했다면, 살인자들이 빠져나가도 되겠소? 오, 나는 꿈자리가 사나워 잠을 이룰 수 없을 것이오! 아무리 거룩한 사랑이라도, 어떤 술수를 부린들, 양심이 졸라 죽이지 않을 안식으로 나를 달랠 수는 없을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그렇게나 나쁜 건가요?\" 그녀가 감정으로 떨리는 목소리로 물었다. \"아무것도, 정말 아무것도 할 수 없는 건가요?\"\n\n벤허는 그녀의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"나를 그토록 생각해 주는 거요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 꾸밈없이 대답했다.\n\n그 손은 따뜻했고, 그의 손바닥 안에서 자취를 감추었다. 그는 그 손이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. 그때 이 작은 여인과 그토록 대조적인 그 이집트 여인이 떠올랐다. 그토록 키가 크고, 그토록 대담하며, 그토록 교묘한 아첨과 그토록 재빠른 재치와 그토록 놀라운 아름다움과 그토록 사람을 호리는 태도를 지닌 여인이. 그는 그 손을 들어 입술에 가져다 댔다가, 다시 돌려주었다.\n\n\"당신은 내게 또 하나의 티르자가 되어 줄 거요, 에스더.\"\n\n\"티르자가 누구예요?\"\n\n\"로마인이 내게서 훔쳐 간 어린 누이요. 내가 쉬거나 행복해지기 전에 반드시 찾아내야 할 누이지.\"\n\n바로 그때 한 줄기 빛이 테라스를 가로질러 번쩍이며 두 사람 위로 떨어졌다. 돌아보니, 한 종이 의자에 앉은 시모니데스를 밀어 문밖으로 내오고 있었다. 그들은 그 상인에게로 갔고, 뒤이은 대화에서는 그가 주역이었다.\n\n곧이어 갤리선의 밧줄이 풀리고, 배가 빙 돌아섰다. 그리고 횃불의 번쩍임과 즐거워하는 뱃사람들의 고함 속에서, 배는 바다를 향해 서둘러 떠나갔다. 벤허를 장차 오실 왕의 대의에 매어 둔 채로.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\n경기 전날 오후, 일데림의 경주용 가축은 모두 도시로 옮겨져 원형경기장에 딸린 마구간에 들었다. 그 일행과 더불어 이 선량한 사람은 경주와는 무관한 재산도 잔뜩 거느리고 갔다. 하인과 종자들은 말을 타고 무장했으며, 끌려가는 말, 몰려가는 가축, 짐을 실은 낙타까지 어우러진 그의 과수원 행렬은 부족 전체가 이주하는 모습과 다르지 않았다. 길가의 사람들은 그 잡다한 행렬을 보고 웃어 댔으나, 다른 한편으로는 그토록 성마른 그가 그들의 무례에 조금도 언짢아하지 않는다는 사실이 눈에 띄었다. 그가 짐작할 만한 이유가 있듯이 만일 감시를 받고 있다면, 밀고자는 그가 경주를 향해 올라온 그 반쯤 야만스러운 행렬을 보고할 것이다. 로마인들은 비웃을 테고, 도시는 즐거워할 테지만, 그가 무엇을 마음에 두랴? 이튿날 아침이면 그 행렬은 사막으로 가는 길을 멀리 나아가 있을 테고, 함께 가는 것은 과수원에 속한 값나가는 물건 일체—오로지 그의 네 마리 말의 승리에 꼭 필요한 것만 빼고 모조리—였다. 사실 그는 이미 귀향길에 오른 셈이었다. 천막은 모두 접혔고, 다와르는 더 이상 없었다. 열두 시간이 지나면 누가 쫓아오든 모든 것이 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있을 터였다. 사람은 비웃음을 받고 있을 때만큼 안전한 때가 없는 법이니, 이 약삭빠른 늙은 아랍인은 그것을 알고 있었다.\n\n* 다와르: 천막들을 둥글게 둘러친 아랍인의 야영지.\n그도 벤허도 메살라의 영향력을 과대평가하지는 않았다. 다만 두 사람의 생각은, 그가 원형경기장에서의 대결이 끝나기 전에는 그들을 상대로 본격적인 조치를 시작하지 않으리라는 것이었다. 그곳에서 패한다면, 특히 벤허에게 패한다면, 그가 할 수 있는 최악의 짓을 당장 각오해야 할 것이다. 그는 그라투스의 지시를 기다리지도 않을지 모른다. 이런 관점에서 그들은 행로를 짰고, 위험을 벗어날 채비를 갖추어 두었다. 이제 두 사람은 좋은 기분으로 나란히 말을 타고 가며, 내일의 승리를 차분히 확신했다.\n\n가는 길에 그들은 자기들을 기다리고 있던 말루크와 마주쳤다. 충직한 그는 벤허와 시모니데스 사이에 얼마 전 인정된 관계나, 그들과 일데림 사이의 협약을 알고 있다고 짐작할 만한 기색을 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그는 여느 때처럼 인사를 나누고는 종이 한 장을 꺼내며 족장에게 말했다. \"방금 발표된 경기 주최자의 공고문을 가지고 왔습니다. 여기에 어르신의 말들이 경주에 출전하는 것으로 실려 있고, 경기 순서도 적혀 있습니다. 미리 기다릴 것도 없이, 어르신, 어르신의 승리를 축하드립니다.\"\n\n그는 종이를 건네주고, 그것을 찬찬히 살피도록 그 어른에게 맡긴 채 벤허에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"아리우스의 아들이신 당신께도 축하드립니다. 이제 메살라와 맞붙는 것을 막을 것은 아무것도 없습니다. 경주에 앞선 모든 조건이 충족되었습니다. 주최자 본인에게서 직접 확약을 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 말루크.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n말루크는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"당신의 색은 흰색이고, 메살라의 색은 진홍과 황금이 섞인 색입니다. 그 선택의 효과는 벌써 눈에 보입니다. 지금 아이들이 거리에서 흰 리본을 외치며 팔고 있고, 내일이면 도시의 아랍인과 유대인이 모두 그것을 두를 겁니다. 원형경기장에서는 흰색이 붉은색과 관람석을 엇비슷하게 나누어 차지하는 광경을 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"관람석은 그렇겠지—하지만 폼파이 문 위의 귀빈석은 아닐 테지.\"\n\n\"네, 그곳은 진홍과 황금이 다스릴 겁니다. 하지만 우리가 이긴다면\"—말루크는 그 생각의 즐거움에 빙긋 웃었다—\"우리가 이긴다면, 그 고관들이 얼마나 떨겠습니까! 그들은 물론 로마 아닌 모든 것을 멸시하는 만큼 판돈을 걸 겁니다—메살라가 로마인이라는 이유로 그에게 두 배, 세 배, 다섯 배씩 말입니다.\" 목소리를 한층 더 낮추며 그는 덧붙였다. \"성전에서 신망 높은 유대인이 그런 위험에 돈을 거는 것은 어울리지 않는 일이지요. 그래도 은밀히 말씀드리면, 집정관 자리 바로 뒤에 친구를 하나 두어 세 배, 다섯 배, 열 배의 제안을 받게 할 작정입니다—광기가 그렇게까지 치솟을지도 모릅니다. 그 목적으로 그에게 육천 세겔을 맡겨 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"아닐세, 말루크.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"로마인은 오직 로마 화폐로만 내기를 걸 걸세. 오늘 밤에 자네의 친구를 찾아가, 자네가 원하는 만큼의 세스테르티우스를 그에게 맡기게. 그리고 잘 듣게, 말루크—그에게 메살라와 그 지지자들과 내기를 하도록 일러 두게. 일데림의 네 마리 말이 메살라의 네 마리 말에 맞서는 것으로 말일세.\"\n\n말루크는 잠시 생각했다.\n\n\"그렇게 하면 당신의 대결에 관심이 집중되는 효과가 있겠군요.\"\n\n\"내가 노리는 바로 그것일세, 말루크.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 말루크. 자네가 나를 완벽히 섬길 생각이라면, 대중의 눈을 우리 경주에—메살라와 나의 경주에 못 박도록 도와주게.\"\n\n말루크가 재빨리 말했다. \"할 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그렇게 하게.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"엄청난 판돈을 제안하면 효과가 있을 겁니다. 그 제안이 받아들여진다면 더욱 좋고요.\"\n\n말루크는 경계하는 눈빛으로 벤허를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그가 빼앗아 간 것만큼 되찾아야 하지 않겠나?\" 벤허가 반쯤 혼잣말로 말했다. \"이런 기회가 다시 오지 않을지도 모르네. 그의 자존심뿐 아니라 재산까지 무너뜨릴 수 있다면! 우리 조상 야곱도 노여워하지 않으실 걸세.\"\n\n굳은 의지의 빛이 그의 잘생긴 얼굴에 어리며, 뒤이은 말에 무게를 실었다.\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 하겠네. 잘 듣게, 말루크! 세스테르티우스로 제안하는 데서 그치지 말게. 감히 그렇게 높이 거는 자가 있거든 탈렌트로 올리게. 다섯, 열, 스무 탈렌트, 아니 쉰까지라도—메살라 본인과의 내기라면 말일세.\"\n\n\"엄청난 금액입니다.\" 말루크가 말했다. \"담보가 있어야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그리될 걸세. 시모니데스에게 가서 내가 이 일을 처리해 주기를 바란다고 전하게. 내 마음이 적의 파멸에 쏠려 있으며, 이번 기회가 더없이 좋은 전망을 품고 있어 이런 위험을 택한다고 전하게. 우리 편에는 우리 조상의 하나님이 계시네. 가게, 충직한 말루크. 이 일을 놓치지 말게.\"\n\n크게 기뻐한 말루크는 작별 인사를 하고 말을 몰아 떠나려다가 이내 되돌아왔다.\n\n\"송구합니다.\" 그가 벤허에게 말했다. \"한 가지 더 있었습니다. 저로서는 메살라의 전차에 가까이 갈 수 없었으나, 다른 사람을 시켜 그것을 재 보게 했습니다. 그 보고에 따르면, 그 전차의 바퀴통이 당신 것보다 땅에서 한 뼘쯤 더 높이 서 있다고 합니다.\"\n\n\"한 뼘이라고? 그렇게나?\" 벤허가 기뻐하며 외쳤다.\n\n그러더니 그는 말루크에게로 몸을 기울였다.\n\n\"자네가 유다의 아들이고 동족에게 충실하다면, 말루크, 개선문 위 관람석에서 기둥 앞 발코니에 바싹 가까운 자리를 잡고, 우리가 거기서 회전할 때 잘 지켜보게. 잘 지켜보게. 내게 조금이라도 운이 따른다면, 나는— 아닐세, 말루크, 말하지 않은 채로 두겠네! 그저 거기 자리를 잡고, 잘 지켜보게.\"\n\n그 순간 일데림의 입에서 외침이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"하! 하나님의 영광에 맹세코! 이게 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n그는 공고문의 한 대목을 손가락으로 가리키며 벤허에게 다가갔다.\n\"읽어 보게.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 자네가 읽는 편이 낫겠네.\"\n\n벤허는 종이를 받아 들었다. 그것은 주최자인 속주 장관의 서명이 담긴, 오늘날의 식순과 같은 구실을 하는 문서로, 이번 행사를 위해 마련된 여러 여흥을 자세히 일러 주고 있었다. 거기에는 먼저 더없이 화려한 행진이 있을 것이며, 그 행진에 뒤이어 신 콘수스에게 바치는 관례의 의식이 거행되고, 그런 다음 경기가 시작된다고 적혀 있었다. 달리기, 뛰기, 씨름, 권투가 적힌 순서대로 이어진다고 했다. 출전자들의 이름이 저마다의 국적과 훈련받은 학교, 그들이 참가했던 시합, 거머쥔 상, 그리고 이번에 걸린 상과 더불어 실려 있었다. 마지막 항목에는 상금의 액수가 화려한 글자로 적혀 있어, 영광을 부보다 나은 것으로 갈망하며 그것에 만족했던 승자에게 소박한 솔잎 관이나 월계관만으로 족했던 시절이 이미 지나갔음을 일러 주었다.\n\n이런 부분들을 벤허는 빠른 눈길로 훑어 내려갔다. 마침내 그는 경주에 관한 발표에 이르렀다. 그것은 천천히 읽었다. 영웅적 경기를 사랑하여 모여든 이들은, 안티오크에서 일찍이 없던 오레스테스 같은 격돌에 반드시 흡족하리라는 약속을 받았다. 도시가 집정관을 기리어 이 볼거리를 마련한 것이었다. 십만 세스테르티우스와 월계관이 상으로 걸렸다. 이어서 세부 사항이 따랐다. 출전은 모두 여섯으로, 네 마리 전차만 허락되었다. 그리고 경기의 흥미를 더하기 위해, 경쟁자들은 한꺼번에 출발선에 세워질 것이었다. 그런 다음 네 마리 말 각각에 대한 설명이 이어졌다.\n\n\"제1번. 코린토스 사람 리시포스의 사두마차—회색 둘, 적갈색 하나, 검은색 하나. 지난해 알렉산드리아에서 출전했고, 코린토스에서 다시 출전하여 우승했다. 마부는 리시포스. 색은 노란색.\n\n\"제2번. 로마 사람 메살라의 사두마차—흰색 둘, 검은색 둘. 지난해 마시무스 원형경기장에서 펼쳐진 키르켄세스의 우승마. 마부는 메살라. 색은 진홍과 황금.\n\n\"제3번. 아테네 사람 클레안테스의 사두마차—회색 셋, 적갈색 하나. 지난해 이스트미아에서 우승했다. 마부는 클레안테스. 색은 초록색.\n\n\"제4번. 비잔티움 사람 디카이오스의 사두마차—검은색 둘, 회색 하나, 적갈색 하나. 올해 비잔티움에서 우승했다. 마부는 디카이오스. 색은 검은색.\n\n\"제5번. 시돈 사람 아드메토스의 사두마차—모두 회색. 카이사레아에서 세 차례 출전하여 세 번 모두 우승했다. 마부는 아드메토스. 색은 파란색.\n\n\"제6번. 사막의 족장 일데림의 사두마차. 모두 적갈색. 첫 경주. 마부는 유대인 벤허. 색은 흰색.\"\n마부는 유대인 벤허!\n\n어찌하여 아리우스 대신 그 이름인가?\n\n벤허는 눈을 들어 일데림을 바라보았다. 그는 그 아랍인이 외친 까닭을 알아차렸다. 두 사람은 똑같은 결론으로 치달았다.\n\n그 손은 메살라의 손이었다!\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\n저녁이 안티오크에 막 내려앉자, 도시의 거의 한복판에 있는 옴팔로스는 사방으로 흘러넘치는 어수선한 샘이 되었다. 그 물줄기는 무엇보다 님파이움 쪽으로, 또 동서로 헤롯의 주랑(柱廊)을 따라 사람들의 흐름이 되어 쏟아졌으니, 그 시각 사람들은 바쿠스와 아폴로에게 몸을 내맡기고 있었다.\n\n그런 방종에 이보다 더 어울리는 곳은 상상하기 어려우니, 지붕을 인 그 거대한 거리는 말 그대로 몇 킬로미터에 걸쳐 이어지는 대리석 주랑이었고, 마지막 한 점까지 닦아 광을 낸 그것은 모두 씀씀이에 개의치 않는 군주들이 음락(淫樂)의 도시에 바친 선물이었다. 이 경우처럼, 그들은 그렇게 함으로써 제 이름을 영원히 새긴다고 믿었던 것이다. 어디에도 어둠은 허락되지 않았다. 노랫소리와 웃음소리와 외침이 끊이지 않았고, 한데 뒤섞여 텅 빈 동굴을 때리며 흐르는 물의 포효처럼, 수많은 메아리가 어지러이 얽혀들었다.\n\n거기에 모인 온갖 민족은 낯선 이방인을 놀라게 했을지언정, 안티오크에만 있는 풍경은 아니었다. 이 거대한 제국이 떠맡은 여러 사명 가운데 하나는 사람들을 뒤섞고 낯선 이들을 서로에게 소개하는 것이었던 듯하다. 그리하여 온 민족이 일어나 제 마음 가는 대로 옮겨 다녔고, 제 의복과 풍습과 언어와 신들을 함께 가지고 다녔다. 그들은 마음에 드는 곳에서 멈추어, 생업에 종사하고, 집을 짓고, 제단을 세웠으니, 고향에서 살던 모습 그대로였다.\n\n그러나 그날 밤 안티오크에서 구경꾼이라면 누구도 놓칠 수 없는 한 가지 특이한 점이 있었다. 거의 모든 사람이 이튿날 경주에 나설 어느 한 전차 기수의 색깔을 몸에 두르고 있었던 것이다. 어떤 이는 목도리로, 어떤 이는 휘장으로, 또 흔히는 리본이나 깃털로 그것을 드러냈다. 형태가 무엇이든 그것은 단지 두른 자의 편애를 뜻할 뿐이었다. 초록은 아테네 사람 클레안테스의 벗임을 알렸고, 검정은 비잔티움 사람의 지지자임을 알렸다. 이는 아마도 오레스테스가 경주를 벌이던 시절만큼이나 오래된 관습에 따른 것이었다. 그것은 역사의 경이로서 연구할 만한 관습이니, 사람들이 제 어리석음에 끌려 얼마나 우스꽝스럽고도 끔찍한 극단에까지 자주 빠져드는지를 잘 보여 준다.\n\n그날 밖으로 나선 관찰자라면, 한번 그 색깔에 마음이 끌린 순간 곧바로 세 가지 색깔이 우세하다는 것을 알아챘을 것이다. 초록과 흰색, 그리고 진홍과 황금이 뒤섞인 색이었다.\n\n그러나 이제 거리를 떠나 섬에 있는 궁전으로 가 보자.\n\n홀의 거대한 샹들리에 다섯 개에 갓 불이 밝혀졌다. 모인 사람들은 앞서 이 장소와 관련하여 살펴본 바로 그 무리와 크게 다르지 않았다. 긴 의자에는 잠든 자들과 벗어 놓은 옷가지가 그득했고, 탁자에서는 여전히 주사위가 달그락거리고 부딪치는 소리가 울렸다. 그러나 무리의 대부분은 아무것도 하지 않고 있었다. 그들은 이리저리 거닐거나, 입이 찢어지게 하품하거나, 서로 스쳐 지나가다 멈추어 시답잖은 말을 주고받았다. 내일 날씨는 좋을까? 경기 준비는 다 되었을까? 안티오크 원형 경기장의 규칙은 로마의 그것과 다를까? 사실 이 젊은이들은 권태에 시달리고 있었다. 힘든 일은 이미 끝나 있었다. 다시 말해, 그들의 서판을 들여다볼 수 있다면 거기에 내기 메모가 빼곡히 적혀 있는 것을 보았으리라. 모든 시합에 건 내기였다. 달리기와 씨름과 권투에—전차 경주만 빼고 모든 것에 건 내기였다.\n그런데 어찌하여 전차 경주에는 걸지 않았는가?\n\n선량한 독자여, 그들은 메살라를 상대로 단 1데나리우스라도 걸려는 사람을 단 한 명도 찾을 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n홀 안에는 그의 색깔 말고는 다른 색깔이 없었다.\n\n누구도 그의 패배를 생각하지 않았다.\n\n그들은 말했다. 그가 어찌 완벽하게 훈련되지 않았겠는가? 그가 황제의 검투사 양성소를 졸업하지 않았던가? 그의 말들이 막시무스 원형 경기장의 키르켄세스 경주에서 우승하지 않았던가? 게다가—아, 그렇다! 그는 로마인이 아닌가!\n\n긴 의자 한 귀퉁이에 편히 기대앉은 메살라 자신의 모습이 보였다. 그를 둘러싸고 앉거나 선 채로, 아첨하는 숭배자들이 그에게 질문을 퍼붓고 있었다. 화제는 물론 단 하나뿐이었다.\n\n드루수스와 케킬리우스가 들어왔다.\n\n\"아!\" 젊은 귀공자는 메살라의 발치에 있는 긴 의자에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. \"아, 바쿠스에게 맹세코, 지쳤다!\"\n\n\"어디를 다녀왔나?\" 메살라가 물었다.\n\n\"거리를 따라 올라갔다가, 옴팔로스까지, 그 너머까지—얼마나 멀리 갔는지 누가 알겠나? 사람의 강물이었다. 일찍이 이 도시에서 그렇게 많은 사람을 본 적이 없다. 내일 원형 경기장에서 온 세상을 다 보게 될 거라더군.\"\n\n메살라는 비웃듯 껄껄 웃었다.\n\n\"멍청이들! 페르폴(맙소사)! 그들은 카이사르가 주최하는 키르켄세스를 본 적이 없는 거야. 그런데 드루수스, 뭘 좀 보았나?\"\n\n\"아무것도.\"\n\n\"오—아! 자네 잊었군.\" 케킬리우스가 말했다.\n\n\"무엇을?\" 드루수스가 물었다.\n\n\"흰색의 행렬 말일세.\"\n\n\"미라빌레(놀라운 일이지)!\" 드루수스가 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다. \"우리는 흰색 패거리를 만났는데, 그들에게 깃발이 있었지. 그런데—하, 하, 하!\"\n\n그는 나른하게 다시 몸을 뉘었다.\n\n\"잔인하군, 드루수스, 말을 끝까지 안 하다니.\" 메살라가 말했다.\n\n\"사막의 쓰레기들이었네, 메살라. 예루살렘 야곱의 성전에서 온 음식 찌꺼기나 먹는 자들이지. 내가 그자들과 무슨 상관이 있겠나!\"\n\n\"아니,\" 케킬리우스가 말했다. \"드루수스는 웃음거리가 될까 두려워하지만, 나는 그렇지 않네, 메살라.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네가 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"좋아, 우리는 그 패거리를 멈춰 세우고는—\"\n\n\"내기를 걸자고 했지.\" 드루수스가 마음이 누그러져, 그림자 같은 친구의 입에서 말을 가로채며 끼어들었다. \"그랬더니—하, 하, 하!—잉어 미끼로 쓸 지렁이 한 마리 만들 가죽도 얼굴에 없는 녀석 하나가 나서더니—하, 하, 하!—그러겠다고 하는 거야. 내가 서판을 꺼냈지. '네 선수가 누구냐?' 하고 물었더니, '유대인 벤허'라더군. 그래서 내가 '얼마를 걸겠느냐? 액수는?' 하고 묻자, 그가 '한—한—' 미안하네, 메살라. 유피테르의 천둥에 맹세코, 웃음 때문에 더는 못 잇겠어! 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n듣는 이들이 몸을 앞으로 기울였다.\n\n메살라가 케킬리우스를 보았다.\n\n\"세겔 한 닢.\" 케킬리우스가 말했다.\n\n\"세겔 한 닢! 세겔 한 닢이라니!\"\n\n그 말이 되풀이되자마자 비웃는 웃음이 와르르 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"그래서 드루수스는 어떻게 했나?\" 메살라가 물었다.\n\n바로 그때 문 쪽에서 떠들썩한 소리가 일어나, 사람들이 그쪽으로 우르르 몰려갔다. 그곳의 소음이 계속되며 점점 더 커지자, 케킬리우스마저 자리를 떴는데, 떠나면서 이 말만 남겼다. \"고결한 드루수스는, 메살라, 서판을 도로 넣고는—세겔 한 닢을 잃었네.\"\n\n\"흰색이다! 흰색이야!\"\n\n\"이리 오라고 해!\"\n\n\"이쪽이오, 이쪽!\"\n\n이런 외침들이 홀을 가득 채워 다른 말은 끊기고 말았다. 주사위 놀이꾼들은 게임을 멈추었고, 잠자던 자들은 깨어나 눈을 비비고 서판을 꺼내 들고는 사람들이 모인 한복판으로 서둘러 달려갔다.\n\n\"나는 당신에게 제안하노니—\"\n\n\"나는—\"\n\n그토록 열렬한 환영을 받은 사람은 다름 아닌, 키프로스에서 벤허와 같은 배를 탔던 점잖은 유대인이었다. 그는 진지하고 조용하며 주의 깊은 태도로 들어섰다. 그의 겉옷은 티 한 점 없이 희었고, 터번의 천 또한 그러했다. 환대에 고개를 숙이고 미소 지으며, 그는 천천히 가운데 탁자로 다가갔다. 그곳에 이르자 그는 위엄 있는 몸짓으로 겉옷을 여미고 자리에 앉아 손을 흔들었다. 손가락에서 반짝이는 보석 하나가, 뒤이어 찾아온 침묵을 끌어내는 데 적잖이 한몫했다.\n\n\"로마인 여러분—더없이 고귀한 로마인 여러분, 인사드립니다!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 유피테르에게 맹세코! 저자는 누구인가?\" 드루수스가 물었다.\n\n\"이스라엘의 개일세—이름은 산발랏이라 하지. 군대 납품업자고, 거주지는 로마, 엄청난 부자라네. 결코 납품하지도 않는 군수품 계약자로 그렇게 부자가 되었지. 그래도 거미가 거미줄을 치는 것보다 더 정교하게 못된 술수를 꾸미는 자야. 자—베누스의 허리띠에 맹세코! 저자를 한번 사로잡아 보세!\"\n\n메살라는 이렇게 말하며 일어나, 드루수스와 함께 납품업자를 둘러싼 무리 속으로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"거리에서 들은 이야기인데,\" 그 사람은 서판을 꺼내어 인상적일 만큼 사무적인 태도로 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓으며 말했다. \"메살라에게 건 제안에 응하는 사람이 없어서 궁전 안이 무척 불편한 분위기라더군요. 아시다시피 신들은 제물을 받으셔야 하는 법, 그래서 제가 여기 있는 겁니다. 제 색깔이 보이시지요. 자, 본론으로 들어갑시다. 먼저 배당률, 그다음에 액수입니다. 제게 얼마를 거시겠습니까?\"\n\n그 대담함에 듣는 이들은 어안이 벙벙해진 듯했다.\n\n\"서두르시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 집정관과 약속이 있소.\"\n\n그 채찍질이 효과가 있었다.\n\n\"이 대 일!\" 대여섯 명이 한목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 납품업자가 놀라 소리쳤다. \"겨우 이 대 일이라니, 그것도 여러분 쪽이 로마인인데!\"\n\n\"그럼 삼 대 일로 하지.\"\n\n\"삼이라고 하셨소—겨우 삼이라니—게다가 내 쪽은 유대인 개에 불과한데! 사 대 일을 주시오.\"\n\n\"사 대 일이다.\" 그 비웃음에 발끈한 소년 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"오—오 대 일을 주시오.\" 납품업자가 즉시 외쳤다.\n\n깊은 정적이 모인 사람들 위에 내려앉았다.\n\n\"집정관께서—당신들과 나의 주인이신 그분이—나를 기다리고 계시오.\"\n\n여럿이 머뭇거리는 모양새가 어색해지기 시작했다.\n\n\"오 대 일을 주시오—로마의 명예를 위해, 오 대 일을.\"\n\n\"오 대 일로 하지.\" 한 사람이 대답하며 말했다.\n\n날카로운 환호가 터지고—소동이 일더니—메살라 자신이 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"오 대 일로 하지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러자 산발랏은 미소 지으며 적을 채비를 했다.\n\n\"내일 카이사르께서 돌아가신다 해도,\" 그가 말했다. \"로마가 텅 비지는 않겠군요. 적어도 그분의 자리를 메울 기개를 지닌 분이 또 하나는 있으니까요. 육 대 일을 주시오.\"\n\n\"육 대 일이다.\" 메살라가 대답했다.\n\n처음보다 더 큰 함성이 또 한 번 일었다.\n\n\"육 대 일이다.\" 메살라가 되풀이했다. \"육 대 일—로마인과 유대인의 차이가 그만큼이지. 자, 이제 그것을 찾았으니, 오, 돼지고기를 속량하는 자여, 어서 진행하자. 액수다—어서. 집정관께서 너를 부르러 사람을 보낼지도 모르니, 그러면 나야말로 텅 비고 말 테니까.\"\n\n산발랏은 자신을 겨냥한 그 웃음을 태연히 받아넘기고는, 적은 다음 그 글을 메살라에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"읽어라, 읽어!\" 모두가 요구했다.\n\n그리하여 메살라가 읽었다.\n\n\"각서—전차 경주. 로마의 메살라는, 역시 로마의 산발랏과 내기를 하여, 유대인 벤허를 이기겠다고 말한다. 내기 액수, 이십 탈렌트. 산발랏에게 주는 배당률, 육 대 일.\n\n증인: 산발랏.\"\n\n아무 소리도, 아무 움직임도 없었다. 저마다 글을 다 읽었을 때의 자세 그대로 굳어 버린 듯했다. 메살라는 그 각서를 뚫어지게 보았고, 그를 지켜보던 눈들은 휘둥그레져서 그를 응시했다. 그는 그 시선을 느끼며 빠르게 머리를 굴렸다. 바로 얼마 전에 그는 같은 자리에 서서, 같은 방식으로 주위의 동향인들을 윽박질렀던 것이다. 그들은 그것을 기억할 것이다. 서명을 거부한다면 그의 영웅 자리는 사라진다. 그렇다고 서명할 수도 없었다. 그는 백 탈렌트는커녕 그 오분의 일도 가진 사람이 아니었다. 갑자기 그의 머릿속이 텅 비었다. 그는 말없이 서 있었고, 얼굴에서 핏기가 가셨다. 마침내 한 가지 생각이 떠올라 그를 구원했다.\n\n\"유대인 너!\" 그가 말했다. \"이십 탈렌트가 어디에 있느냐? 내게 보여라.\"\n\n산발랏의 약 올리는 미소가 더 깊어졌다.\n\n\"여기 있소.\" 그가 메살라에게 종이 한 장을 내밀며 대답했다.\n\n\"읽어라, 읽어!\" 사방에서 소리가 일었다.\n\n메살라가 다시 읽었다.\n\n\"안티오크에서, 담무스 16일.\n\n이 증서를 지닌 자, 로마의 산발랏은 지금 나에게 카이사르의 화폐로 오십 탈렌트를 청구할 권리가 있다.\n\n시모니데스.\"\n\"오십 탈렌트, 오십 탈렌트라니!\" 무리가 경악하여 따라 외쳤다.\n\n그때 드루수스가 구원에 나섰다.\n\n\"헤르쿨레스에게 맹세코!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"그 종이는 거짓말이고, 저 유대인은 거짓말쟁이다. 카이사르 말고 누가 오십 탈렌트를 청구할 권리가 있단 말인가? 저 건방진 흰색을 끌어내려라!\"\n\n그 외침은 분노에 차 있었고, 분노에 차서 되풀이되었다. 그러나 산발랏은 자리를 지켰고, 오래 기다릴수록 그의 미소는 더욱 사람의 속을 긁었다. 마침내 메살라가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"조용히! 일 대 일이다, 동향인들이여—일 대 일로, 우리 유서 깊은 로마라는 이름을 위하여.\"\n\n그 시의적절한 처사가 그에게 우위를 되찾아 주었다.\n\n\"오, 할례받은 개여!\" 그가 산발랏을 향해 말을 이었다. \"내가 너에게 육 대 일을 주었지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 유대인이 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 이제 액수를 정하는 것은 나에게 맡겨라.\"\n\n\"액수가 보잘것없는 경우에 한해서라면, 마음대로 하시오.\" 산발랏이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 적어라, 이십 대신 오 탈렌트라고.\"\n\n\"그만한 돈은 있소?\"\n\n\"신들의 어머니께 맹세코, 영수증을 보여 주마.\"\n\n\"아니오, 그토록 용감한 로마인의 말이라면 통하는 법이지요. 다만 액수를 떨어지게만 하시오—육으로 하면 적겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇게 적어라.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 곧바로 글을 교환했다.\n\n산발랏은 즉시 일어나 주위를 둘러보았는데, 미소 대신 냉소를 띠고 있었다. 자신이 상대하는 자들을 그보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다.\n\n\"로마인 여러분,\" 그가 말했다. \"또 하나 내기를 하실까요, 만약 용기가 있다면! 흰색이 이긴다는 데 오 탈렌트 대 오 탈렌트로. 여러분 모두에게 도전합니다.\"\n\n그들은 또다시 놀랐다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 그가 더 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이스라엘의 개 한 마리가 로마 귀족들로 가득 찬 궁전 홀에 들어와—그 가운데에는 카이사르의 후손도 있는데—그들 앞에 오 탈렌트를 내놓으며 도전했건만, 그들에게는 그것을 받아들일 용기조차 없더라고, 내일 원형 경기장에서 이런 말이 떠돌게 해도 좋단 말입니까?\"\n\n그 가시는 견딜 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n\"그만하라, 오, 건방진 자여!\" 드루수스가 말했다. \"그 도전을 적어 탁자 위에 두고 가라. 그러면 내일, 네가 정말로 그토록 가망 없는 모험에 걸 만한 돈을 가졌음이 확인된다면, 나 드루수스가 그것을 받아들이겠다고 약속하마.\"\n\n산발랏은 다시 글을 적고는, 일어서며 여전히 흔들림 없이 말했다. \"보시오, 드루수스, 이 제안을 당신에게 남겨 두겠소. 서명이 끝나거든, 경주가 시작되기 전 언제든 내게 보내시오. 나는 포르타 폼파이 위쪽 좌석에서 집정관과 함께 있을 것이오. 평안하시기를. 모두 평안하시기를.\"\n\n그는 고개를 숙이고 떠났는데, 사람들이 문밖까지 그를 쫓으며 퍼붓는 조롱의 함성에는 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n밤사이 그 엄청난 내기 이야기는 거리를 따라 도시 전역으로 퍼져 나갔다. 자신의 네 마리 말과 함께 누워 있던 벤허도 그 소식을 전해 들었고, 메살라의 전 재산이 그 모험에 걸려 있다는 것도 알게 되었다.\n\n그리고 그는 그 어느 때보다 깊이 잠들었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\n안티오크의 원형경기장은 강 남쪽 기슭, 섬과 거의 마주 보는 자리에 서 있었으니, 그 설계는 이런 종류의 건물이 으레 따르는 양식과 조금도 다르지 않았다.\n\n가장 순수한 의미에서 이 경기는 대중에게 베푸는 선물이었다. 따라서 누구나 자유로이 구경할 수 있었다. 건물이 받아들일 수 있는 규모가 그토록 방대했음에도, 이번만큼은 사람들이 자기 자리가 없을까 두려워한 나머지, 흥행이 열리기 하루 전 이른 시각부터 그 일대의 빈터를 죄다 차지해 버렸다. 그곳에 임시로 친 거처들은 마치 출진을 기다리는 군대를 떠올리게 했다.\n\n자정이 되자 입구가 활짝 열렸고, 밀려든 군중은 자기들에게 배정된 구역을 차지했으니, 지진이나 창을 든 군대가 아니고서는 그들을 그곳에서 몰아낼 수 없었다. 그들은 의자에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸며 밤을 새우고 거기서 아침을 먹었다. 행사가 끝날 때까지도 그들은 그 자리에 그대로 있었으니, 처음과 다름없이 끈기 있고 구경에 굶주린 모습이었다.\n\n신분이 높은 사람들은 자리를 확보해 둔 터라, 아침 첫 시각쯤에야 경기장을 향해 움직이기 시작했다. 그 가운데 귀족과 부호 들은 가마와 제복을 갖춰 입은 종자들의 행렬로 한눈에 구별되었다.\n\n둘째 시각이 되자, 도시에서 흘러나오는 인파는 끊이지 않고 헤아릴 수 없는 물줄기를 이루었다.\n\n성채 위에 세워진 공식 해시계의 바늘이 둘째 시각의 절반이 지났음을 가리키는 바로 그 순간, 군단이 완전 무장을 하고 군기를 모두 내건 채 술피우스산에서 내려왔다. 마지막 보병대의 후미가 다리 위로 사라지자, 안티오크는 그야말로 텅 비고 말았다. 경기장이 그 많은 무리를 다 담을 수 있어서가 아니라, 그래도 그 무리가 모조리 경기장으로 빠져나갔기 때문이다.\n\n강기슭에 모인 큰 무리가, 집정관이 성대한 거룻배를 타고 섬에서 건너오는 광경을 지켜보았다. 그 위인이 뭍에 올라 군단의 영접을 받자, 그 군사적 위용은 잠시나마 경기장의 매력마저 능가했다.\n\n셋째 시각이 되자 관중이라 부를 만한 사람들이 모두 모였다. 마침내 나팔 소리가 한 차례 울려 정숙을 명하니, 그 즉시 십만이 넘는 사람들의 시선이 건물 동쪽 구역을 이루는 한 건축물로 쏠렸다.\n\n그 아래쪽에는 먼저 기단이 있었는데, 가운데가 폭넓은 아치형 통로로 갈라져 있었고 그것을 포르타 폼파이라 불렀다. 그 위로는 휘장과 군단 깃발로 호화롭게 꾸민 높직한 단상이 있어, 집정관이 영예로운 자리에 앉아 있었다. 통로 양쪽으로는 기단이 칸칸이 나뉘어 카르케레스라 불리는 마구간을 이루었는데, 칸마다 앞쪽이 조각상 같은 벽기둥에 걸린 육중한 문으로 막혀 있었다. 그 마구간 위로는 처마 돌림띠가 있고, 그 위에 낮은 난간이 얹혀 있었다. 난간 뒤로는 극장처럼 자리가 층층이 올라가 있었으니, 그 모두를 더없이 화려하게 차려입은 고관들의 무리가 차지하고 있었다. 이 건축물은 경기장의 너비만큼 뻗어 있었고, 양옆에는 탑이 솟아 있었다. 그 탑들은 설계자가 작품에 우아함을 더하는 데 보탬이 되었을 뿐 아니라, 그 사이에 펼쳐 친 벨라리아, 곧 자줏빛 차일을 떠받치는 구실도 했다. 그리하여 그 일대를 온통 그늘로 덮으니, 날이 깊어 갈수록 그 그늘이 더없이 고맙게 여겨졌다.\n\n이 건축물을 설명해 두면, 경기장 안쪽의 나머지 배치를 독자가 충분히 이해하는 데 보탬이 될 것이다. 독자는 다만 자신이 집정관과 함께 단상에 앉아 서쪽을 바라보고 있다고 상상하기만 하면 된다. 그러면 모든 것이 그의 눈 아래에 펼쳐진다.\n\n좌우를 둘러보면, 매우 넓은 정문들이 보이는데, 그 문들은 탑에 경첩으로 달린 문짝으로 지켜지고 있다.\n\n그의 바로 아래에는 경기장이 있다—곱고 흰 모래로 덮인, 제법 넓은 평지다. 달리기를 제외한 모든 경기가 그곳에서 치러질 것이다.\n\n모래 깔린 이 경기장 너머로 여전히 서쪽을 바라보면, 대리석 받침대 하나가 있어 정교하게 새겨진 잿빛 돌 원뿔 기둥 세 개를 떠받치고 있다. 날이 저물기 전에 수많은 눈이 그 기둥을 찾아 헤맬 것이니, 그것이 바로 첫 번째 반환점이요, 경주로의 시작이자 끝을 표시하기 때문이다. 받침대 뒤로는 통로와 제단을 놓을 공간을 남겨 두고, 폭이 3, 4미터에 높이가 1.5미터에서 2미터쯤 되는 벽이 시작되어, 거기서부터 정확히 약 180미터, 곧 올림픽 경기장 하나 길이만큼 뻗어 있다. 그 벽의 저쪽 끝, 곧 서쪽 끝에는 또 하나의 받침대가 있어, 두 번째 반환점을 표시하는 기둥들을 떠받치고 있다.\n\n경주마들은 첫 번째 반환점 오른쪽으로 들어와, 내내 그 벽을 왼편에 두고 달릴 것이다. 따라서 경기의 시작점과 끝점은 경기장을 사이에 두고 집정관 정면에 놓여 있다. 그런 까닭에 그의 자리가 경기장에서 가장 바람직한 자리로 인정받았던 것이다.\n\n이제 여전히 포르타 폼파이 위 집정관 단상에 앉아 있다고 여겨지는 독자가 안쪽의 바닥 배치에서 눈을 들어 올린다면, 가장 먼저 눈에 들어오는 것은 경주로 바깥 경계선의 표시일 것이다—곧 표면이 밋밋하고 단단한 벽으로, 높이가 4, 5미터쯤 되며, 그 마루에는 동쪽 카르케레스, 곧 마구간 위에 있는 것과 같은 난간이 둘려 있다. 이 발코니는 경주로를 따라 빙 돌아가면 세 군데가 끊겨 있어 드나드는 통로 구실을 하는데, 둘은 북쪽에, 하나는 서쪽에 있다. 서쪽 것은 매우 화려하게 꾸며져 있어 개선문이라 불린다. 모든 것이 끝나면 승자들이 월계관을 쓰고 개선 행렬과 의식을 거느린 채 그곳으로 나가기 때문이다.\n\n서쪽 끝에서 발코니는 반원 모양으로 경주로를 에워싸며, 두 개의 커다란 관람석을 떠받치도록 만들어졌다.\n\n발코니 마루의 난간 바로 뒤에 첫 번째 자리가 있고, 거기서부터 이어지는 의자들이 층층이 올라가는데, 저마다 앞자리보다 한 단씩 높다. 그리하여 더없이 흥미로운 광경이 한눈에 펼쳐진다—드넓은 공간이 사람의 얼굴들로 발갛게 빛나고, 갖가지 빛깔의 의상으로 화려하게 물든 광경이다.\n\n평민들은 서쪽 구역을 차지하는데, 차일이 끝나는 지점에서부터 시작된다. 그 차일은 오로지 상류층을 위해서만 펼쳐 친 듯했다.\n\n이렇게 나팔이 울리는 순간 경기장 안쪽 전체를 한눈에 두었으니, 이제 독자는 그 무리가 자리에 앉아 별안간 침묵에 잠긴 채, 흥미에 사로잡혀 미동도 없는 모습을 떠올려 보라.\n\n동쪽 포르타 폼파이에서 사람의 목소리와 악기 소리가 어우러진 가락이 솟아오른다. 이윽고 축전을 여는 행렬의 합창대가 모습을 드러낸다. 경기를 베푸는 자인 흥행 주최자와 도시의 관헌들이 예복에 화관을 쓰고 그 뒤를 따른다. 다음으로는 신들이 나오는데, 더러는 사람이 짊어진 단 위에, 더러는 호화롭게 장식한 큼직한 네 바퀴 수레에 실려 있다. 그다음으로 다시, 그날의 출전자들이 저마다 달리거나 씨름하거나 뛰거나 권투하거나 전차를 몰 때 그대로의 차림으로 나온다.\n\n행렬은 경기장을 천천히 가로질러, 경주로를 한 바퀴 도는 데로 나아간다. 그 행렬은 아름답고도 위풍당당하다. 마치 움직이는 배 앞에서 물이 솟아 부풀어 오르듯, 함성이 그 앞을 앞질러 달려간다. 형상으로 빚어진 말 없는 신들이야 그 환영에 고마워하는 기색을 보이지 않더라도, 흥행 주최자와 그 동료들은 그처럼 무덤덤하지만은 않다.\n\n선수들에 대한 환대는 한층 더 요란하다. 그도 그럴 것이, 비록 한 푼이나 반 푼일망정, 그들에게 무언가를 걸지 않은 사람이 모인 무리 가운데 하나도 없기 때문이다. 그리고 각 부류가 지나갈 때면, 그 가운데 인기 있는 자들이 금세 가려진다는 점이 눈에 띈다. 함성 속에서 그들의 이름이 가장 크게 울리거나, 발코니에서 던지는 화환과 화관이 그들에게 더욱 풍성하게 쏟아진다.\n\n여러 경기 가운데 어느 것이 대중에게 인기 있느냐는 물음이 있었다면, 이제 그 답이 났다. 전차의 찬란함과 말들의 빼어난 아름다움에 더하여, 전차몰이꾼들은 그 볼거리의 매력을 완성하는 데 필요한 개성을 보탠다. 그들의 튜닉은 짧고 소매가 없으며 가장 고운 모직으로 짠 것인데, 저마다 배정받은 빛깔로 되어 있다. 벤허를 제외한 저마다에게는 기수 한 사람씩이 딸려 있었으니, 벤허는 무슨 까닭에선지—어쩌면 의심 때문에—혼자 가기를 택했다. 마찬가지로 그를 빼고는 모두 투구를 쓰고 있었다. 그들이 다가오자 구경꾼들은 의자 위에 올라섰고, 함성은 눈에 띄게 깊어졌다. 귀가 밝은 자라면 그 속에서 여인과 아이 들의 새된 휘파람 같은 외침을 가려낼 수 있었다. 동시에 발코니에서 날아오는 장밋빛 물건들이 폭풍처럼 짙어져, 사내들을 때리고는 전차 칸 안으로 떨어졌으니, 칸은 곧 가득 채워질 듯했다. 말들조차 그 갈채에 한몫을 받았다. 그 말들이 자신들이 받는 영예를 주인보다 못하게 느낀다고는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n이내, 다른 출전자들과 마찬가지로, 전차몰이꾼 가운데 더러는 다른 자들보다 더 인기 있다는 사실이 드러났다. 그러자 의자에 앉은 거의 모든 사람이, 여인과 아이도 사내와 마찬가지로, 빛깔 하나씩을 걸치고 있다는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 흔히는 가슴이나 머리에 단 리본이었다. 어떤 것은 초록, 어떤 것은 노랑, 어떤 것은 파랑이었다. 그러나 그 큰 무리를 꼼꼼히 살펴보면, 흰빛과 진홍과 금빛이 우세하다는 것이 분명했다.\n\n이처럼 모여든 현대의 집회였다면, 더구나 경주에 돈이 걸린 자리였다면, 누구를 편드느냐는 말의 됨됨이나 그 솜씨로 정해졌을 것이다. 그러나 이곳에서는 민족이 그 기준이었다. 비잔티움 사람과 시돈 사람이 거의 지지를 받지 못한 것은, 그들의 도시가 의자에 변변히 대표되지 않았기 때문이다. 한편 그리스인은 수가 매우 많았으나 코린토스 편과 아테네 편으로 갈라져, 초록과 노랑은 빈약하게 보일 뿐이었다. 메살라의 진홍과 금빛도, 으레 아첨꾼의 족속이라 일컬어지는 안티오크 시민들이 자기들이 좋아하는 자의 빛깔을 받아들여 로마인 편에 가담하지 않았더라면, 그보다 별로 나을 것이 없었을 것이다. 그러고 나면 시골 사람들, 곧 시리아인과 유대인과 아랍인이 남았다. 그들은 족장의 네 마리 말의 혈통에 대한 믿음에, 무엇보다도 로마인이 패하고 굴욕당하는 꼴을 보고 싶어 하는 미움이 크게 뒤섞여, 흰빛을 택했다. 그리하여 가장 떠들썩하고, 아마도 가장 수가 많은 무리를 이루었다.\n\n전차몰이꾼들이 한 바퀴를 돌며 나아가자, 흥분은 더욱 높아졌다. 특히 관람석에서 흰빛이 으뜸가는 빛깔인 두 번째 반환점에 이르자, 사람들은 꽃을 다 쏟아붓고 비명으로 허공을 갈랐다.\n\n\"메살라! 메살라!\"\n\n\"벤허! 벤허!\"\n\n이런 외침들이었다.\n\n행렬이 지나가자 편을 가른 자들은 자리에 앉아 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"아, 바쿠스 신께 맹세코! 그이가 잘생기지 않았어?\" 머리에 휘날리는 빛깔로 로마 편임이 드러나는 한 여인이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"게다가 그이 전차가 얼마나 멋진지!\" 같은 성향을 지닌 옆 사람이 대꾸했다. \"온통 상아와 황금이잖아. 유피테르께서 그이를 이기게 해 주시기를!\"\n\n그들 뒤쪽 의자에서 흘러나오는 가락은 전혀 달랐다.\n\n\"유대인에게 백 세겔!\"\n\n높고 새된 목소리였다.\n\n\"아니, 그렇게 경솔하지 말게.\" 함께 말리는 친구가 그자에게 속삭였다. \"야곱의 자손은 이방인의 경기에 그다지 끼어들지 않는 법이지. 주님 보시기에 너무도 자주 저주받은 것이니 말일세.\"\n\n\"맞는 말일세. 하지만 저보다 더 침착하고 자신만만한 자를 본 적이 있나? 게다가 저 팔뚝 좀 보게!\"\n\n\"그리고 저 말들은 또 어떻고!\" 세 번째 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 말일세,\" 네 번째 사람이 덧붙였다. \"사람들 말로는 저자가 로마인들의 온갖 솜씨를 죄다 익혔다더군.\"\n\n한 여인이 그 칭송을 마무리지었다.\n\n\"그래요, 게다가 저이는 그 로마인보다도 잘생겼다고요.\"\n\n이렇게 부추김을 받자, 그 열렬한 자는 다시 비명을 질렀다. \"유대인에게 백 세겔!\"\n\n\"이런 바보 같으니!\" 발코니 앞쪽 의자에 앉은 한 안티오크 사람이 대꾸했다. \"메살라에게 일 대 육으로 오십 탈란트가 걸린 것을 모른단 말인가? 자네 세겔이나 거두어들이게. 아브라함이 일어나 자네를 치기 전에 말일세.\"\n\n\"하, 하! 이 안티오크의 멍청이야! 그 울음소리 좀 그치게. 그게 메살라가 제 자신에게 건 돈이라는 걸 모른단 말인가?\"\n\n이런 대꾸였다.\n\n이렇게 논쟁은 이어졌으니, 늘 좋게만 흘러가지는 않았다.\n\n마침내 행진이 끝나고 포르타 폼파이가 행렬을 도로 받아들였을 때, 벤허는 자신의 기도가 이루어졌음을 알았다.\n\n동방의 눈길이 메살라와 겨루는 그의 대결에 쏠려 있었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\n현대식으로 말하자면 오후 세 시쯤, 전차 경주를 제외한 순서는 모두 끝났다. 흥행 책임자는 관중의 편안함을 슬기롭게 헤아려 이때를 휴식 시간으로 잡았다. 곧바로 출입구가 활짝 열렸고, 나갈 수 있는 사람은 모두 음식 장수들이 자리 잡은 바깥 회랑으로 서둘러 나갔다. 남은 사람들은 하품을 하고, 이야기를 나누고, 잡담을 떨고, 자기 서판을 들여다보았으니, 다른 모든 구별은 잊은 채 오직 두 부류로 나뉘었다—만족스러운 승자와, 뚱하고 까다로운 패자로.\n\n그런데 이제 제삼의 관중, 곧 오직 전차 경주만을 보고 싶어 하는 시민들이 이 휴식 시간을 틈타 들어와 예약된 자리를 차지했다. 그렇게 함으로써 그들은 가장 적은 눈길을 끌고 가장 적은 거슬림을 주리라 여겼다. 그 가운데 시모니데스와 그 일행이 있었으니, 그들의 자리는 집정관 맞은편, 북쪽 정문 가까이였다.\n\n건장한 하인 넷이 의자에 앉은 상인을 들고 통로를 올라가자, 호기심이 크게 일었다. 이윽고 누군가 그의 이름을 불렀다. 둘레에 있던 사람들이 그 소리를 받아 서쪽 의자들을 따라 옮겨 전했다. 사람들은 자리 위로 황급히 기어올라, 일찍이 들어 본 적도 본 적도 없을 만큼 행운과 불행이 뒤섞인 풍문이 만들어져 떠돌게 한 그 사내를 보려 했다.\n\n일데림 또한 알아보고 따뜻한 인사를 받았다. 그러나 발타사르나, 그를 바싹 따라가며 너울로 얼굴을 가린 두 여인은 아무도 몰라보았다.\n\n사람들은 정중히 일행에게 길을 내주었고, 안내인들은 경기장을 내려다보는 난간 곁에, 서로 편히 말을 주고받을 만한 거리에 자리를 마련해 주었다. 편안함을 헤아려 그들은 방석 위에 앉았고 발받침으로 걸상을 두었다.\n\n두 여인은 이라스와 에스더였다.\n\n자리에 앉자 에스더는 경기장을 한 차례 겁먹은 눈길로 둘러보고, 너울을 얼굴에 더 바싹 끌어당겼다. 한편 이집트 여인은 너울을 어깨로 떨어뜨려 제 모습을 드러내고는, 남들이 자기를 쳐다본다는 것을 의식하지 못하는 듯한 태도로 광경을 바라보았으니, 여인에게 그런 태도란 대개 오랜 사교 생활에서 몸에 밴 것이었다.\n\n새로 들어온 사람들은 대체로 아직 그 거대한 구경거리를 처음 살펴보는 중이었으니, 집정관과 그 시종들부터 보기 시작하던 차에, 일꾼 몇이 뛰어들어 와 첫 번째 반환점 기둥 앞을 가로질러 발코니에서 발코니까지 분필 칠한 줄을 치기 시작했다.\n\n거의 같은 무렵, 여섯 사내가 폼파이 문을 통해 들어와, 사용 중인 칸막이 자리 앞마다 하나씩 늘어섰다. 그러자 사방에서 길게 끄는 목소리의 웅성거림이 일었다.\n\n\"봐, 봐! 초록이 오른쪽 네 번째로 가는군. 아테네 사람이 저기 있어.\"\n\n\"그리고 메살라—그래, 그는 두 번째에 있어.\"\n\n\"코린토스 사람은—\"\n\n\"흰색을 봐! 봐라, 그가 건너간다, 멈춘다. 첫 번째다—왼쪽 첫 번째야.\"\n\n\"아니, 검은색이 거기 멈추고, 흰색은 두 번째야.\"\n\n\"그렇군.\"\n\n알아 두어야 할 것은, 이 문지기들이 경쟁하는 전차 몰이꾼들과 같은 색의 짧은 옷을 입고 있었다는 점이다. 그래서 그들이 제자리에 서면, 모두가 자기가 미는 선수가 그 순간 어느 칸막이에서 기다리고 있는지 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"메살라를 본 적이 있느냐?\" 이집트 여인이 에스더에게 물었다.\n\n유대 여인은 몸서리치며 아니라고 답했다. 그 로마인이 제 아버지의 원수는 아닐지언정, 벤허의 원수였다.\n\n\"그는 아폴론처럼 아름답단다.\"\n\n이라스가 말할 때, 그 큰 눈이 빛났고 보석으로 장식한 부채를 흔들었다. 에스더는 \"그렇다면 그가 벤허보다 그토록 잘생겼단 말인가?\" 하고 생각하며 그녀를 바라보았다. 다음 순간 일데림이 제 아버지에게 \"그렇소, 그의 칸은 폼파이 문 왼쪽으로 두 번째요\" 하고 말하는 것을 들었다. 벤허를 두고 하는 말이라 여긴 에스더는 그쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다. 엮어 짠 문의 얼굴을 잠깐 흘낏 보고는, 너울을 바싹 끌어당기며 짧은 기도를 중얼거렸다.\n\n이윽고 산발라트가 일행에게로 왔다.\n\n\"방금 칸막이에서 오는 길입니다, 족장님.\" 그는 일데림에게 정중히 고개를 숙이며 말했고, 족장은 수염을 빗어 내리기 시작했으니, 그 눈은 간절한 물음으로 반짝였다. \"말들은 더없이 좋은 상태입니다.\"\n\n일데림은 짤막하게 대답했다. \"그것들이 진다면, 부디 메살라 아닌 다른 자에게 지기를 비오.\"\n\n이어 시모니데스를 향해 산발라트는 서판을 꺼내며 말했다. \"당신에게도 흥미로운 것을 가져왔습니다. 기억하시겠지만, 어젯밤 메살라와 맺은 내기를 보고드렸고, 또 다른 내기 하나를 남겨 두었는데 그것이 받아들여진다면 경주가 시작되기 전 오늘 안에 서면으로 제게 전해지기로 했다고 말씀드렸지요. 여기 있습니다.\"\n\n시모니데스는 서판을 받아 그 적힌 내용을 꼼꼼히 읽었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들의 심부름꾼이 와서 자네가 그만한 돈을 내게 맡겨 두었는지 물었네. 그 서판을 잘 간수하게. 진다면 어디로 와야 할지 알 테고, 이긴다면\"—그의 얼굴이 단단히 굳었다—\"이긴다면—아, 친구, 잘 살피게! 서명한 자들이 빠져나가지 못하게 하게. 마지막 세겔까지 붙들어 두게. 그자들이 우리에게 하려던 짓이 바로 그것이니.\"\n\n\"믿으십시오.\" 조달인이 대답했다.\n\n\"우리와 함께 앉지 않겠나?\" 시모니데스가 물었다.\n\n\"참으로 고마우신 말씀입니다.\" 그가 답했다. \"허나 제가 집정관 곁을 떠나면 저쪽 젊은 로마가 끓어 넘칠 겁니다. 평안하시기를. 모두에게 평안이 있기를.\"\n\n마침내 휴식 시간이 끝났다.\n\n나팔수들이 신호를 불자 자리를 비웠던 이들이 제자리로 달려 돌아왔다. 동시에 시중꾼 몇이 경기장에 나타나, 가운데 칸막이 벽 위로 기어올라, 서쪽 끝 두 번째 반환점 가까이의 벽 장식대로 가서 그 위에 나무 공 일곱 개를 올려놓았다. 그러고는 첫 번째 반환점으로 돌아와, 그곳 장식대 위에 돌고래 모양으로 깎은 나무 일곱 개를 또 세워 놓았다.\n\n\"저 공과 물고기로 무엇을 하려는 것이오, 족장?\" 발타사르가 물었다.\n\n\"경주를 한 번도 본 적이 없소?\"\n\n\"한 번도 없소. 게다가 내가 왜 여기 와 있는지조차 잘 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"음, 저것들은 횟수를 세는 데 쓰이오. 한 바퀴 돌 때마다 공 하나와 물고기 하나씩 내려놓는 것을 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n이제 준비가 모두 끝났고, 이윽고 화려한 옷차림의 나팔수 하나가 흥행 책임자 곁에 일어나, 그 명령이 떨어지면 곧장 개시 신호를 불 채비를 갖추었다. 즉시 사람들의 술렁임과 이야기의 웅성거림이 잦아들었다. 가까이 있는 얼굴마다, 점점 작아져 가는 시야 속 얼굴마다 동쪽으로 돌아섰으니, 모든 눈이 경쟁자들을 가둔 여섯 칸막이의 문에 박혔다.\n\n얼굴에 떠오른 여느 때와 다른 홍조가, 시모니데스조차 그 보편적 흥분에 사로잡혔음을 증명했다. 일데림은 수염을 빠르고 거세게 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"이제 로마인을 보아라.\" 어여쁜 이집트 여인이 에스더에게 말했으나, 에스더는 듣지 못했으니, 너울을 바싹 끌어당기고 가슴을 뛰게 하며 벤허를 지켜보느라 여념이 없었다.\n\n알아 두어야 할 것은, 칸막이가 늘어선 건물이 원의 한 조각 모양을 하고 있었다는 점이다. 오른쪽으로 물러나 그 중심점이 앞으로 튀어나오게 하여, 첫 번째 반환점의 출발 쪽 주로 중간쯤에 자리하게 했다. 따라서 모든 칸막이는 출발선, 곧 앞서 말한 분필 칠한 줄에서 똑같은 거리에 있었다.\n\n나팔이 짧고 날카롭게 울렸다. 그러자 출발 도우미들이, 전차마다 하나씩 배정된 그들이, 반환점 기둥 뒤에서 뛰어내려, 어느 사두마차든 다루기 힘들어지면 도울 채비를 갖추었다.\n\n다시 나팔이 울렸고, 동시에 문지기들이 칸막이를 활짝 열어젖혔다.\n\n먼저 전차 몰이꾼들의 기마 시중꾼들이 나타났으니, 모두 다섯이었다. 벤허는 그 시중을 마다했던 것이다. 분필 줄을 내려 그들을 지나가게 하고는 다시 올렸다. 그들은 아름다운 말을 타고 있었으나, 앞으로 나아가는 동안 거의 눈길을 끌지 못했다. 그동안 내내 칸막이 안에서 안달하는 말들의 발 구르는 소리와, 그에 못지않게 안달하는 몰이꾼들의 목소리가 들려왔으니, 누구도 잠시라도 그 떡 벌어진 문에서 눈을 뗄 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n분필 줄이 다시 올라가자, 문지기들이 자기 선수를 불렀다. 즉시 발코니의 안내인들이 손을 흔들며 있는 힘껏 외쳤다. \"내려라! 내려라!\"\n\n폭풍을 멈추라고 휘파람을 부는 편이 차라리 나았을 것이다.\n\n여섯 사두마차가 각 칸막이에서, 마치 수많은 거포에서 일제히 쏘아 올린 포탄처럼 쏟아져 나왔다. 그러자 거대한 군중이 일어나, 전율하며 걷잡을 수 없이, 의자 위로 뛰어올라 경기장과 그 위 허공을 고함과 비명으로 가득 채웠다. 바로 이때를 위해 그들은 그토록 참을성 있게 기다려 온 것이다!—경기가 선포된 뒤로 이야기와 꿈속에 간직해 온 더없는 흥분의 순간이었다!\n\n\"그가 왔어—저기—봐!\" 이라스가 메살라를 가리키며 외쳤다.\n\n\"보고 있어요.\" 에스더가 벤허를 바라보며 답했다.\n\n너울이 걷혔다. 한순간 이 작은 유대 여인은 용감했다. 군중의 눈앞에서 영웅적인 일을 해내는 데 깃든 기쁨이 어떤 것인지 그녀에게 떠올랐고, 그런 순간에 사람의 영혼이 어떻게 실행의 광란 속에서 죽음을 비웃거나 아예 잊어버리는지를 이후로 줄곧 깨닫게 되었다.\n\n이제 경쟁자들은 경기장 거의 모든 곳에서 보였으나, 경주는 아직 시작되지 않았다. 그들은 먼저 분필 줄을 무사히 넘어야 했다.\n\n그 줄은 출발을 고르게 하려고 쳐 둔 것이었다. 거기에 달려들었다가는 사람과 말이 낭패를 볼 수 있었다. 반면, 머뭇거리며 다가가는 것은 경주 첫머리에 뒤처질 위험을 무릅쓰는 일이었으니, 그것은 늘 다투어 차지하려는 큰 이점—주로 안쪽 선, 곧 칸막이 벽에 바싹 붙은 자리—을 확실히 잃는 것을 뜻했다.\n\n이 시험과 그 위험과 결과를, 관중은 속속들이 알고 있었다. 만약 늙은 네스토르가 아들에게 고삐를 건넬 그때 했던 말이 참이라면—\n\n\"상을 얻은 것은 힘이 아니라 기술이며,\n빠른 것은 슬기로운 것만 못하다\"—\n\n의자에 앉은 모두가 이제 승자를 알리는 경고가 주어지기를 기대해 봄 직했으니, 그들이 숨죽이며 결과를 지켜보는 그 관심도 마땅한 것이었다.\n\n경기장은 눈부신 빛 속에 일렁였다. 그러나 몰이꾼마다 가장 먼저 줄을 살피고, 다음으로 그토록 탐내는 안쪽 선을 살폈다. 그리하여 여섯 모두 같은 지점을 노리며 맹렬히 내달리니, 충돌은 피할 수 없어 보였다. 그뿐만이 아니었다. 만약 흥행 책임자가 마지막 순간에 출발이 마뜩잖아 줄을 떨어뜨리라는 신호를 내지 않는다면? 혹은 제때 내지 못한다면?\n\n그 교차 지점은 폭이 약 75미터였다. 빠른 눈, 흔들림 없는 손, 어김없는 판단이 요구되었다. 이제 누구든 한눈을 판다면! 혹은 마음이 흐트러진다면! 혹은 고삐가 미끄러진다면! 그리고 펼쳐진 발코니 위 수천 명의 무리에는 어떤 끌림이 있던가! 한 번—단 한 번만이라도—호기심에서든 허영에서든 시선을 던지고 싶은 자연스러운 충동을 계산에 넣고, 악의가 거기에 책략을 부려 도사릴 수도 있었다. 한편 우정과 사랑도, 같은 결과를 빚는다면, 악의 못지않게 치명적일 수 있었다.\n\n아름다움을 완성하는 신적인 마지막 손길은 생동감이다. 이 말을 받아들일 수 있다면, 오락은 그토록 맥없고 운동은 그토록 따분한 이 후세에는, 여섯 경쟁자가 보여 준 그 광경에 견줄 만한 것이 거의 없다. 독자는 그것을 상상해 보라. 먼저 경기장을 내려다보아, 칙칙한 잿빛 화강암 벽으로 둘러싸인 채 반짝이는 그곳을 보라. 그다음 이 완벽한 무대 위에서, 바퀴 가볍고 더없이 우아하며 칠과 광택이 낼 수 있는 한껏 장식된 전차들을—메살라의 것은 상아와 황금으로 호화로운 그 전차들을 보라. 몰이꾼들이 꼿꼿이, 조각상처럼, 수레의 흔들림에도 흔들리지 않고 선 모습을 보라. 그 팔다리는 벌거벗었고, 목욕으로 건강하게 윤이 나 싱싱하고 불그레했다—오른손에는 생각만으로도 끔찍한 고문을 떠올리게 하는 채찍을, 왼손에는 말들을 보는 데 방해가 되지 않도록 조심스레 떼어 높이 든 채, 수레 채의 앞 끝에서 팽팽히 뻗어 온 고삐를 쥐고 있었다. 빠르기 못지않게 아름다움으로 골라 뽑은 사두마들을 보라. 그것들이 장엄하게 내달리는 모습을 보라. 그 주인들조차 처지와, 자기들에게 요구되고 바라는 모든 것을 그보다 더 의식하지는 못할 만큼이었다. 머리를 치켜들고, 콧구멍을 벌름거려 넓혔다 좁혔다 하고, 디디는 모래마저도 뿌리치듯 차 버릴 만큼 그 다리가 너무도 곱고—가늘면서도 부딪치면 망치처럼 으스러뜨리는 다리였다. 둥근 몸의 근육 하나하나가 영광스러운 생명으로 가득 차 부풀었다 줄어들었다 하니, 세상이 그것들에게서 힘의 궁극적 잣대를 가져가는 것도 마땅했다. 끝으로, 전차와 몰이꾼과 말과 더불어, 그 곁을 따라 나는 그림자들을 독자는 보라. 그리하여 그 그림이 또렷이 떠오르는 만큼, 독자는 그것이 가냘픈 공상이 아니라 가슴 떨리는 사실이었던 이들의 만족과 더 깊은 기쁨을 함께 나눌 수 있으리라. 어느 시대에나 슬픔은 넘쳐 난다. 기쁨이 없는 곳에는 하늘이 도와주시기를!\n\n경쟁자들은 저마다 벽에 바싹 붙은 자리를 향해 최단 선을 따라 출발했으니, 양보하는 것은 경주를 포기하는 것과 같았다. 한데 누가 감히 양보하겠는가? 길 한복판에서 마음먹은 바를 바꾸는 것은 사람의 본성에 없는 일이다. 발코니에서 쏟아지는 격려의 외침은 알아들을 수도, 말로 옮길 수도 없었다. 그것은 모든 몰이꾼에게 똑같은 효과를 미치는 함성이었다.\n\n사두마차들이 다 함께 줄에 다가섰다. 그러자 흥행 책임자 곁의 나팔수가 힘차게 신호를 불었다. 6미터 떨어진 곳에서는 들리지 않았다. 그러나 그 동작을 본 심판들이 줄을 떨어뜨렸으니, 조금도 이르지 않은 때였다. 메살라의 말 한 마리의 발굽이 떨어지는 줄을 차고 지나갔던 것이다. 조금도 겁먹지 않고 로마인은 긴 채찍을 휘둘러 풀고, 고삐를 늦추고, 앞으로 몸을 기울이며, 의기양양한 외침과 함께 벽 쪽을 차지했다.\n\n\"제우스가 우리와 함께하신다! 제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 모든 로마 패거리가 미칠 듯한 기쁨으로 외쳤다.\n\n메살라가 안으로 돌아드는 순간, 그 굴대 끝의 청동 사자 머리가 아테네 선수의 오른쪽 곁말 앞다리를 걸어, 그 짐승을 멍에 짝에게로 내동댕이쳤다. 둘 다 비틀거리고 버둥거리다 달리던 기세를 잃었다. 안내인들의 뜻이 적어도 얼마쯤은 이루어진 셈이었다. 수천 명이 공포에 숨을 죽였다. 오직 집정관이 앉은 저 위에서만 함성이 일었다.\n\n\"제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 드루수스가 미친 듯이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"그가 이긴다! 제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 메살라가 내달리는 것을 보고 그 일당이 화답했다.\n\n서판을 손에 든 채 산발라트가 그들에게로 돌아섰다. 그러나 아래 주로에서 들려온 충돌 소리가 그의 말을 막았으니, 그도 그쪽을 보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n메살라가 지나가 버린 뒤, 아테네 선수의 오른쪽에는 코린토스 선수만 남아 있었고, 그쪽으로 아테네 선수는 망가진 사두마를 돌리려 했다. 그러자 운이 나쁘게도, 왼쪽으로 그 옆에 있던 비잔티움 선수의 바퀴가 그의 전차 뒤꽁무니를 들이받아 그의 발밑을 휩쓸어 버렸다. 우지끈하는 소리, 분노와 두려움의 비명이 일었고, 불운한 클레안테스는 제 말들의 발굽 아래로 쓰러졌으니, 에스더가 두 눈을 가린 그 끔찍한 광경이었다.\n\n코린토스 선수가 내달렸고, 비잔티움 선수가, 시돈 선수가 내달렸다.\n\n산발라트는 벤허를 살피고는, 다시 드루수스와 그 패거리를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"유대인에게 백 세스테르티우스!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"받겠소!\" 드루수스가 답했다.\n\n\"유대인에게 또 백!\" 산발라트가 소리쳤다.\n\n아무도 그의 말을 듣는 것 같지 않았다. 그가 다시 외쳤다. 아래 상황이 너무도 사람을 사로잡았고, 그들은 \"메살라! 메살라! 제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 하고 외치느라 너무 바빴다.\n\n유대 여인이 다시 보기로 마음을 다잡았을 때, 일꾼 한 무리는 말들과 부서진 수레를 치우고 있었고, 또 다른 무리는 사람 자체를 들어 내고 있었다. 그리고 그리스 사람이 앉은 의자마다 저주와 복수를 비는 기도가 울려 퍼졌다. 문득 그녀가 두 손을 떨구었다. 다친 데 없는 벤허가 앞쪽에서, 로마인과 나란히 거침없이 앞으로 내달리고 있었던 것이다! 그들 뒤로는 시돈 선수와 코린토스 선수와 비잔티움 선수가 한 무리를 이루어 따라왔다.\n\n경주가 진행되고 있었다. 경주자들의 영혼이 그 안에 들어 있었다. 그들 위로 수만 명이 몸을 굽혀 내려다보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\n위치 다툼이 시작되었을 때, 우리가 이미 보았듯이 벤허는 여섯 명 가운데 맨 왼쪽에 있었다. 다른 이들처럼 그도 한순간 경기장의 빛에 반쯤 눈이 멀었으나, 그래도 그는 가까스로 적수들의 모습을 포착하고 그들의 속셈을 헤아렸다. 단순한 적수 이상인 메살라에게 그는 한 번 꿰뚫는 시선을 던졌다. 그 우아한 귀족의 얼굴에 깃든 무정하고 오만한 분위기는 예전 그대로였고, 투구가 오히려 돋보이게 하는 이탈리아풍의 아름다움도 여전했다. 그러나 그 이상이었으니—질투에서 비롯된 착각이었을 수도, 그 순간 이목구비 위에 드리운 놋빛 그림자의 효과였을 수도 있으나, 그래도 이 이스라엘 사람은 마치 유리를 통해 어렴풋이 보듯 그자의 영혼을 들여다보았다고 여겼다. 잔인하고, 교활하고, 필사적인 영혼. 흥분했다기보다는 단단히 결심한—경계와 사나운 결의로 팽팽히 당겨진 영혼이었다.\n\n다시 자기의 네 마리에게로 눈을 돌리는 데 걸리는 시간도 채 안 되어, 벤허는 자기 자신의 결심이 그와 똑같은 강도로 단단해지는 것을 느꼈다. 어떤 대가를 치르더라도, 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰더라도, 이 원수를 무릎 꿇리리라! 상금이며, 친구들이며, 내기며, 명예며—경주에서 가질 법한 온갖 관심사가 그 하나의 단호한 목적 속에 모조리 사라졌다. 목숨에 대한 미련조차 그를 붙들지 못할 터였다. 그러나 그에게 격정은 없었다. 심장에서 머리로, 다시 심장으로 치닫는 달뜬 피의 눈먼 격류도 없었고, 운명에 제 몸을 내던지려는 충동도 없었다. 그는 운명을 믿지 않았으니, 오히려 그 반대였다. 그에게는 계획이 있었고, 자기 자신을 믿으며 그 어느 때보다 빈틈없이, 그 어느 때보다 능숙하게 일에 임할 채비를 갖추었다. 그를 둘러싼 공기마저 새롭고도 완벽한 투명함으로 빛나는 듯했다.\n\n경기장을 가로질러 절반도 채 못 갔을 때, 그는 깨달았다. 충돌만 없고 밧줄이 떨어지기만 하면, 메살라의 돌진이 그에게 안쪽 벽을 내줄 것임을. 밧줄이 떨어지리라는 것은 곧 의심할 여지가 없었다. 게다가 번개처럼 갑작스러운 통찰이 그에게 찾아왔으니, 그것은 메살라가 밧줄이 마지막 순간에 떨어지도록 되어 있음을 알고 있다는 사실이었다(주최자와의 사전 모의가 경기에서 그 정도 지점까지는 충분히 미칠 수 있었다). 그리고 그것은 또 한 가지를 일깨워 주었다. 관리가 동족에게, 그것도 그토록 인기가 높을 뿐 아니라 걸린 것도 그토록 많은 동족에게 한몫 거들어 주는 것보다 더 로마인다운 일이 어디 있겠는가? 경쟁자들이 장애물 앞에서 신중하게 자기의 네 마리를 제어하고 있는 바로 그 순간 메살라가 자기 네 마리를 앞으로 밀어붙이는 그 자신감을 달리 설명할 길은 없었다—광기가 아니고서는 달리 설명할 수 없었다.\n\n필연을 알아보는 것과 그에 따라 행동하는 것은 별개의 일이다. 벤허는 일단 벽을 양보했다.\n\n밧줄이 떨어지자, 그의 네 마리를 뺀 모든 네 마리가 고함과 채찍의 재촉을 받으며 경주로로 튀어 나갔다. 그는 머리를 오른쪽으로 돌려, 아랍 말들의 온 속력을 다해 적수들의 진로를 가로질러 내달렸다. 그 움직임의 각도는 시간을 가장 적게 잃고 가능한 한 가장 크게 전진하도록 맞춰져 있었다. 그리하여 관중이 아테네 사람의 사고에 몸서리치고, 시돈 사람과 비잔티움 사람과 코린토스 사람이 저마다의 솜씨로 그 난파에 휘말리지 않으려 안간힘을 쓰는 동안, 벤허는 휘돌아 나와 비록 바깥쪽이긴 했으나 메살라와 나란히 어깨를 견주며 경주로에 들어섰다. 맨 왼쪽에서 오른쪽까지 이렇게 별다른 손실 없이 자리를 바꾸는 데 보인 놀라운 솜씨는 관람석의 날카로운 눈들을 놓치지 않았다. 경기장은 오래도록 이어지는 박수갈채에 흔들리고 또 흔들리는 듯했다. 그러자 에스더는 기쁜 놀라움에 두 손을 맞잡았고, 산발랏은 미소 지으며 다시 한번 100세스테르티우스를 걸었으나 받는 자가 없었으며, 로마인들은 의심을 품기 시작했으니, 메살라가 자기의 스승은 아닐지언정 호적수를 만난 것은 아닌가, 그것도 이스라엘 사람 중에서!\n\n이제 둘은 좁은 간격을 사이에 두고 나란히 어깨를 견주며 달려, 두 번째 반환점에 다가갔다.\n\n거기 세워진 세 기둥의 받침대는 서쪽에서 보면 반원 모양의 돌벽으로, 경주로와 맞은편 발코니가 그 둘레를 정확히 평행으로 휘감고 있었다. 이 반환점을 도는 것은 모든 면에서 전차 모는 자의 가장 결정적인 시험으로 여겨졌으니, 사실 오라이테스가 실패한 것이 바로 이 묘기였다. 관중이 무심결에 드러내는 관심의 표시인 양, 온 경기장에 정적이 내려앉았다. 그리하여 경주가 시작된 이래 처음으로, 안간힘을 다하는 말들을 뒤쫓아 돌진하는 전차들의 덜그럭거리고 쟁그랑거리는 소리가 또렷이 들려왔다. 그때, 보아하니 메살라가 벤허를 알아본 모양이었다. 누구인지 알아차린 것이다. 그러자 그자의 대담함이 단번에 놀라운 방식으로 불타올랐다.\n\n\"에로스는 내려가고, 마르스는 올라오라!\" 그는 노련한 손놀림으로 채찍을 휘두르며 외쳤다—\"에로스는 내려가고, 마르스는 올라오라!\" 그는 거듭 외치며, 잘 달리고 있던 벤허의 아랍 말들에게 그들이 일찍이 겪어 본 적 없는 채찍질을 가했다.\n\n그 일격은 사방에서 목격되었고, 놀라움은 누구에게나 한결같았다. 정적은 더욱 깊어졌다. 집정관 뒤편 관람석 위에서는 가장 담대한 자조차 숨을 죽인 채 결과를 기다렸다. 그러나 그것도 한순간이었다. 곧 천둥이 떨어지듯, 발코니에서 백성의 분노에 찬 외침이 무심결에 터져 나왔다.\n\n네 마리는 겁에 질려 앞으로 튀어 나갔다. 이제껏 사랑의 손길 말고는 누구의 손도 닿은 적이 없는 말들이었다. 더없이 다정하게 길러졌고, 자라면서 사람에게 보이는 그 신뢰는 사람들이 보기에도 아름다운 본보기가 되었다. 그토록 곱게 자란 천성이 그런 모욕 앞에서 죽음에서 달아나듯 펄쩍 뛰는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴?\n\n마치 한 가지 충동에 사로잡힌 듯 네 마리가 앞으로 솟구쳤고, 전차도 앞으로 튀어 나갔다. 두말할 것도 없이, 모든 경험은 우리에게 쓸모가 있다. 지금 그토록 큰 도움이 된 벤허의 그 큰 손과 억센 악력은 어디서 얻은 것인가? 그토록 오랜 세월 바다와 싸우던 그 노가 아니고서 어디겠는가? 그리고 발밑 바닥의 이 들썩임이, 비틀거리는 물결의 힘에 취해 떨리던 배가 그 너울의 박동에 못 이겨 어지러이 제멋대로 휘청거리던 옛 시절의 그것에 비하면 무엇이겠는가? 그리하여 그는 제자리를 지키며 네 마리에게 고삐를 늦추어 주고, 다독이는 목소리로 그들을 불렀으니, 그저 그들을 위험한 반환점 둘레로 이끌고자 할 뿐이었다. 그리고 백성의 열기가 가라앉기도 전에, 그는 다시 통제권을 되찾았다. 그뿐이 아니었다. 첫 번째 반환점에 다가가면서 그는 다시 메살라와 어깨를 나란히 했으니, 로마인이 아닌 모든 이의 공감과 찬탄을 한 몸에 받으면서였다. 그 감정이 어찌나 또렷이 드러났던지, 그 표출이 어찌나 격렬했던지, 메살라는 그 모든 대담함에도 더는 함부로 굴기가 위험하다고 느꼈다.\n\n전차들이 반환점을 휘돌아 나갈 때, 에스더는 벤허의 얼굴을 흘끗 보았다. 조금 창백하고, 조금 더 높이 쳐들었으되, 그 밖에는 차분한, 심지어 평온한 얼굴이었다.\n\n곧바로 한 사람이 분리벽 서쪽 끝의 처마 장식 위로 올라가, 원뿔 모양의 나무 공 하나를 내렸다. 동쪽 처마 장식의 돌고래 하나도 같은 때에 내려졌다.\n\n같은 방식으로, 두 번째 공과 두 번째 돌고래가 사라졌다.\n\n그러고는 세 번째 공과 세 번째 돌고래가 사라졌다.\n\n세 바퀴를 마쳤다. 여전히 메살라가 안쪽 자리를 지켰고, 여전히 벤허가 그와 어깨를 나란히 하며 움직였으며, 여전히 다른 경쟁자들이 전과 같이 뒤따랐다. 경주는 카이사르 후기 시절 로마에서 그토록 인기를 끌었던 이중 경주 가운데 하나와 같은 양상을 띠기 시작했다—첫 번째 무리에는 메살라와 벤허가, 두 번째 무리에는 코린토스 사람과 시돈 사람과 비잔티움 사람이 있었다. 그동안 안내인들은 군중을 자리로 되돌려 앉히는 데 성공했으나, 함성은 계속 경주로를 돌며, 마치 저 아래 경주로의 맞수들과 발맞추기라도 하듯 이어졌다.\n\n다섯 번째 바퀴에서 시돈 사람이 벤허의 바깥쪽 자리를 차지하는 데 성공했으나, 곧 그 자리를 잃었다.\n\n여섯 번째 바퀴는 서로의 위치에 변화 없이 시작되었다.\n\n속력은 차츰 빨라졌고—경쟁자들의 피는 차츰 그 분투로 달아올랐다. 사람도 짐승도 한결같이, 마지막 위기가 가까웠음을, 승자가 자기를 내세울 때가 다가오고 있음을 아는 듯했다.\n\n처음부터 주로 로마인과 유대인의 다툼에 쏠려 있던, 그러면서 후자에게 강렬하고도 두루 퍼진 공감을 보내던 관심은, 이제 빠르게 그를 위한 근심으로 바뀌어 갔다. 모든 관람석에서 관중은 꼼짝 않고 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채, 다만 얼굴만 경쟁자들을 따라 돌렸다. 일데림은 수염 빗질을 멈추었고, 에스더는 두려움도 잊었다.\n\n\"유대인에게 100세스테르티우스!\" 산발랏이 집정관의 차일 아래 로마인들을 향해 외쳤다.\n\n아무런 대답이 없었다.\n\n\"한 탈렌트—아니 다섯 탈렌트, 아니 열 탈렌트라도, 골라 보시오!\"\n\n그는 도전하듯 그들에게 서판을 흔들어 보였다.\n\n\"내가 그대의 세스테르티우스를 받겠소.\" 한 로마 청년이 적을 채비를 하며 대답했다.\n\n\"그러지 마시게.\" 한 친구가 가로막았다.\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"메살라는 이미 제 속력을 다했네. 보게, 전차 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 고삐를 날리는 리본처럼 늦추어 잡고 있지 않은가. 그럼 이제 저 유대인을 보게.\"\n\n처음의 청년이 바라보았다.\n\n\"헤라클레스에 맹세코!\" 그는 낯빛이 어두워지며 대답했다. \"저 개 같은 자가 재갈에 제 온 무게를 싣고 있구나. 알겠다, 알겠어! 신들이 우리 벗을 돕지 않으면, 저자는 이스라엘 사람에게 추월당하고 말 게다. 아니, 아직은 아니다. 보게! 유피테르께서 우리와 함께하시기를, 유피테르께서 우리와 함께하시기를!\"\n\n라틴어를 쓰는 모든 입에서 부풀어 오른 그 외침이, 집정관의 머리 위 차일을 뒤흔들었다.\n\n메살라가 정말로 제 속력을 다한 것이 사실이라면, 그 분투에는 효과가 있었다. 더디지만 분명히 그는 앞서 나가기 시작했다. 그의 말들은 머리를 낮게 숙인 채 달렸고, 발코니에서 보면 그 몸뚱이가 실제로 땅을 스치는 듯했으며, 벌름거리는 콧구멍은 핏빛으로 붉었고, 두 눈은 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯 부릅떠져 있었다. 분명 그 훌륭한 말들은 온 힘을 다하고 있었다! 그러나 그 속력을 얼마나 오래 유지할 수 있을 것인가? 이제 겨우 여섯 번째 바퀴의 시작이었다. 그들은 계속 내달렸다. 두 번째 반환점에 다가가면서, 벤허는 로마인의 전차 뒤로 돌아섰다.\n\n메살라 편 사람들의 환희는 극에 달했다. 그들은 비명을 지르고 울부짖으며 자기네 깃발을 흔들어 댔고, 산발랏은 그들이 내미는 내기로 서판을 가득 채웠다.\n\n개선문 위쪽 아래 회랑에 있던 말루크는 환호를 이어 가기가 힘들었다. 그는 서쪽 기둥을 도는 길목에서 무슨 일이 벌어지리라던, 벤허가 그에게 흘린 막연한 암시를 가슴에 품어 왔던 것이다. 어느덧 다섯 번째 바퀴가 되었으나 그 무엇은 아직 오지 않았다. 그는 여섯 번째 바퀴가 그것을 가져오리라고 스스로 다독였으나, 보라! 벤허는 원수의 전차 꽁무니에서 자리를 겨우 지키고 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n저편 동쪽 끝에서, 시모니데스 일행은 입을 다물고 있었다. 상인은 고개를 깊이 떨구었다. 일데림은 수염을 잡아당기며 눈썹을 내리깔아, 이따금 한 줄기 빛이 반짝이는 것 말고는 두 눈이 도무지 보이지 않았다. 에스더는 숨도 제대로 쉬지 못했다. 오직 이라스만이 기뻐 보였다.\n\n홈스트레치를 따라—여섯 번째 바퀴—메살라가 앞서고, 그다음에 벤허, 그것도 어찌나 바짝 붙었던지 그것은 그 옛 이야기 그대로였다.\n\n\"맨 앞은 페레스의 준마를 몰며 에우멜로스가 날았고, 트로스의 말들로 용맹한 디오메데스가 그 뒤를 이었네. 에우멜로스의 등에 바짝 붙어 그들은 바람을 내뿜으니, 금방이라도 뒤에서 그의 전차에 올라탈 듯하구나. 그의 목덜미에 후끈한 바람이 그대로 끼쳐 오고, 머리 위를 맴도는 그들의 길게 뻗은 그림자가 보이네.\"\n\n이렇게 첫 번째 반환점까지, 그리고 그것을 돌았다. 메살라는 자리를 잃을까 두려워, 아슬아슬하게 돌벽을 끌어안았다. 왼쪽으로 한 발만 빗나갔어도 그는 산산조각이 났으리라. 그러나 반환을 마쳤을 때, 두 전차의 바퀴 자국을 본 그 누구도 여기는 메살라가, 저기는 유대인이 지나갔다고 말할 수 없었으리라. 그들은 단 하나의 자국만을 뒤에 남겼다.\n\n그들이 휙 지나갈 때, 에스더는 다시 벤허의 얼굴을 보았는데, 그것은 전보다 더 창백했다.\n\n에스더보다 영민한 시모니데스는, 맞수들이 경주로로 접어드는 그 순간 일데림에게 말했다. \"내 눈이 틀리지 않다면, 선량한 족장이여, 벤허가 무언가 계책을 펼치려는 참이오. 그 얼굴에 그런 기색이 있소.\"\n\n이에 일데림이 대답했다. \"저 말들이 얼마나 멀쩡하고 생생한지 보았소? 하느님의 영광에 맹세코, 친구여, 저놈들은 아직 달린 것도 아니오! 자, 이제 두고 보시오!\"\n\n처마 장식 위에는 공 하나와 돌고래 하나만이 남았다. 그리고 모든 백성이 길게 숨을 들이쉬었으니, 마지막의 시작이 임박했기 때문이다.\n\n먼저, 시돈 사람이 자기 네 마리에게 채찍을 휘둘렀고, 두려움과 고통에 따끔거리던 말들은 필사적으로 앞으로 내달려, 잠시 동안 선두로 나설 듯이 보였다. 그러나 그 분투는 가능성에 그치고 말았다. 다음으로 비잔티움 사람과 코린토스 사람이 저마다 시도했으나 결과는 같았고, 그 뒤로 그들은 사실상 경주에서 밀려났다. 그러자 충분히 이해할 만한 거리낌 없음으로, 로마인을 뺀 모든 편이 벤허에게 희망을 모았고, 드러내 놓고 그 감정을 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"벤허! 벤허!\" 그들은 외쳤고, 수많은 사람의 어우러진 목소리가 집정관석을 향해 압도하듯 굽이쳐 갔다.\n\n그가 지나갈 때, 그의 머리 위 관람석에서는 호의가 격렬한 당부의 말이 되어 쏟아져 내렸다.\n\n\"서둘러라, 유대인이여!\"\n\n\"이제 안쪽 벽을 차지해라!\"\n\n\"가라! 아랍 말들을 풀어 줘라! 고삐도 채찍도!\"\n\n\"저자에게 다시 반환점을 빼앗기지 마라. 지금이 아니면 영영 안 된다!\"\n\n그들은 난간 너머로 몸을 낮게 숙이고, 그를 향해 애원하듯 두 손을 뻗었다.\n\n그가 듣지 못한 것인지, 아니면 그 이상 어쩔 수 없었던 것인지, 경주로를 절반이나 돌도록 그는 여전히 뒤따르고 있었다. 두 번째 반환점에 이르러서도 여전히 아무런 변화가 없었다!\n\n그리고 이제, 반환점을 돌고자 메살라가 왼쪽 말들을 안으로 당기기 시작했으니, 이는 필연적으로 그들의 속력을 늦추는 행위였다. 그의 기세는 드높았다. 한둘이 아닌 제단이 그의 봉헌으로 풍성해졌고, 로마의 수호신은 여전히 그를 주재하고 있었다. 600피트밖에 떨어지지 않은 세 기둥 위에는 명성과, 재산의 증식과, 승진과, 증오로 형언할 수 없이 달콤해진 승리가, 모두 그를 위해 마련되어 있었다! 바로 그 순간, 회랑에 있던 말루크는 벤허가 자기의 아랍 말들 위로 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 고삐를 늦추어 주는 것을 보았다. 그의 손에서 여러 겹으로 접힌 채찍이 튀어 나가, 놀란 말들의 등 위로 꿈틀거리며 쉭쉭 소리를 내고, 다시 쉭쉭거리며 거듭 꿈틀거렸다. 그것이 살에 닿지는 않았으나, 그 잽싼 울림에는 따끔함과 위협이 함께 담겨 있었다. 그리고 그 사람이 이렇게 고요함에서 거스를 수 없는 행동으로 옮겨 갈 때, 그의 얼굴은 붉게 달아오르고 두 눈은 번득이며, 고삐를 따라 자기의 의지를 번개처럼 내쏘는 듯했다. 그러자 한 마리가 아니라 네 마리가 한 몸처럼, 단번에 도약하여 로마인의 전차와 나란히 들어섰다. 반환점의 아슬아슬한 가장자리에 있던 메살라는 그 소리를 들었으나, 그 깨어남이 무엇을 예고하는지 감히 돌아볼 엄두를 내지 못했다. 백성에게서 그는 아무런 신호도 받지 못했다. 경주의 온갖 소음 위로 오직 하나의 목소리만이 들렸으니, 그것은 벤허의 목소리였다. 그 옛 아람어로, 마치 족장이 그러하듯 그는 아랍 말들에게 외쳤다.\n\n\"가자, 알타이르! 가자, 리겔! 뭐냐, 안타레스! 이제 와서 머뭇거리느냐? 착하지—이랴, 알데바란! 천막에서 그들이 노래하는 소리가 들린다. 아이들이 노래하고 여인들이 노래하는 소리가 들린다—별들의 노래, 알타이르와 안타레스와 리겔과 알데바란의 노래, 승리의 노래!—그리고 그 노래는 영영 그치지 않으리라. 잘했다! 내일이면 집으로, 검은 천막 아래 집으로—집으로 간다! 가자, 안타레스! 부족이 우리를 기다리고, 주인이 우리를 기다린다! 해냈다! 해냈어! 하, 하! 우리가 저 오만한 자를 쓰러뜨렸다. 우리를 친 그 손이 흙먼지 속에 처박혔다. 영광은 우리 것이다! 하, 하!—천천히! 일은 끝났다—워워! 쉬어라!\"\n\n이보다 더 단순한 일은 일찍이 없었고, 이토록 순식간에 벌어진 일도 좀처럼 없었다.\n\n돌진을 위해 택한 그 순간, 메살라는 반환점 둘레로 원을 그리며 돌고 있었다. 그를 추월하려면 벤허는 진로를 가로질러야 했고, 좋은 전략은 그 움직임을 앞으로 향하게 할 것을 요구했다. 다시 말해, 늘어남이 가능한 한 가장 적은, 비슷한 원을 그려야 했다. 관람석의 수천 명이 그 모든 것을 알아차렸다. 그들은 신호가 떨어지는 것을—그 장엄한 응답을, 메살라의 바깥 바퀴 바로 옆에 바짝 붙은 네 마리를, 메살라의 전차 뒤편에 놓인 벤허의 안쪽 바퀴를—그 모든 것을 보았다. 그러더니 경기장에 전율을 일으킬 만큼 요란한 충돌음이 들렸고, 생각보다 빠르게 경주로 위로 번쩍이는 흰빛과 노란빛 파편이 물보라처럼 흩날렸다. 로마인의 전차 차대가 오른쪽으로 기우뚱 쓰러졌다. 차축이 단단한 땅에 부딪히는 듯한 반동이 일었고, 또 한 번, 또 한 번 그러더니, 전차는 산산조각으로 부서졌다. 그리고 고삐에 뒤엉킨 메살라가 머리부터 앞으로 곤두박질쳤다.\n\n죽음을 확실하게 만들어 그 광경의 참혹함을 더하기라도 하듯, 바로 뒤 안쪽 벽을 차지하고 있던 시돈 사람은 멈추지도 비켜 가지도 못했다. 그는 전속력으로 그 잔해 속으로 돌진했고, 이어 로마인을 타고 넘어, 두려움에 미쳐 날뛰는 그자의 네 마리 속으로 처박혔다. 이윽고 그 아수라장에서, 말들이 뒤엉켜 몸부림치고, 부딪치는 소리가 울려 퍼지고, 흙먼지와 모래가 자욱이 피어오르는 가운데에서, 그는 기어 나왔다. 코린토스 사람과 비잔티움 사람이 한순간도 지체하지 않은 벤허를 뒤쫓아 경주로를 따라 내달리는 것을 때맞춰 보면서였다.\n\n백성이 일어나, 관람석 위로 뛰어올라, 고함치고 비명을 질렀다. 그쪽을 바라본 자들은 메살라를 흘끗 보았으니, 어느 때는 네 마리의 짓밟힘 아래, 어느 때는 버려진 전차들 아래 깔린 모습이었다. 그는 미동도 없었다. 사람들은 그가 죽었다고 여겼다. 그러나 훨씬 더 많은 수는 질주하는 벤허를 좇았다. 그들은 벤허가 고삐를 살짝 왼쪽으로 트는 그 교묘한 손놀림으로, 쇠를 두른 차축 끝으로 메살라의 바퀴를 걸어 부숴 버린 것은 보지 못했다. 그러나 그 사람의 변모는 보았고, 그가 눈빛과 말과 몸짓으로 그토록 갑작스레 자기의 아랍 말들에게 불어넣은 그 영혼의 열기와 광채를, 그 영웅적인 결의를, 그 미칠 듯한 행동의 기운을 스스로도 느꼈다. 그리고 그 질주라니! 그것은 차라리 멍에를 멘 사자들의 길고 긴 도약과도 같았다. 굼뜬 전차만 아니었다면 네 마리는 날고 있는 것만 같았다. 비잔티움 사람과 코린토스 사람이 경주로의 절반쯤에 이르렀을 때, 벤허는 첫 번째 반환점을 돌았다.\n\n# AND THE RACE WAS WON!\n\n경주는 이렇게 끝났다!\n집정관이 일어섰다. 백성은 목이 쉬도록 외쳤다. 주최자는 자기 자리에서 내려와 승자들에게 관을 씌웠다.\n\n권투 시합에서 운 좋게 이긴 자는 이마가 낮고 머리칼이 노란 색슨 사람으로, 그 짐승 같은 얼굴이 어찌나 험상궂은지 벤허가 두 번 돌아보게 만들었으니, 그는 자기가 로마에서 총애받던 시절의 한 스승을 알아보았다. 그에게서 눈을 들어 젊은 유대인은 발코니 위의 시모니데스 일행을 바라보았다. 그들은 그를 향해 손을 흔들었다. 에스더는 자리를 지켰으나, 이라스는 일어서서 그에게 미소를 보내고 부채를 흔들어 보였다—그 호의는, 독자여, 메살라가 승자였다면 그에게로 돌아갔으리라는 것을 우리가 안다 한들, 그렇다고 그에게 덜 도취적인 것은 아니었다.\n\n이윽고 행렬이 이루어졌고, 제 뜻을 다 이룬 군중의 함성 한가운데를 지나, 개선문 밖으로 빠져나갔다.\n\n그리고 그날은 저물었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\n벤허는 일데림과 함께 강 건너에 머물렀다. 자정이 되면, 미리 정한 대로, 서른 시간 전에 떠난 대상(隊商)이 밟아 간 길을 따라나설 것이기 때문이었다.\n\n족장은 행복했다. 그가 내놓은 선물은 임금에게 어울릴 만큼 후했으나, 벤허는 모든 것을 사양하며, 원수를 욕보인 것만으로 만족한다고 거듭 말했다. 너그러운 실랑이는 오래 이어졌다.\n\n\"생각해 보게.\" 족장이 말했다. \"자네가 나를 위해 무슨 일을 했는지를. 아카바와 대양에 이르기까지, 또 유프라테스를 건너 그 너머 스키타이인의 바다에 이르기까지, 검은 천막마다 나의 미라와 그 새끼들의 명성이 퍼져 나갈 걸세. 그들을 노래하는 자들이 나를 드높여, 내가 인생의 황혼에 접어들었음을 잊을 걸세. 이제 주인 없는 모든 창들이 나에게로 모여들 것이고, 내 칼잡이들은 셀 수 없을 만큼 불어날 걸세. 자네는 이제 내 것이 될 그런 사막의 지배가 어떤 것인지 모르네. 그것이 교역에서 헤아릴 수 없는 공물을 가져다주고, 왕들로부터의 면제를 안겨 주리라고 내 장담하지. 그렇고말고, 솔로몬의 칼을 두고 맹세하건대! 내 사자(使者)가 카이사르에게 나를 위한 호의를 구한다면, 그것마저 얻을 걸세. 그런데도 아무것도, 아무것도 받지 않겠다는 건가?\"\n\n그러면 벤허는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 족장님. 제가 족장님의 손과 마음을 얻지 않았습니까? 족장님의 늘어난 권세와 영향력은 장차 오실 왕을 위해 쓰이게 하십시오. 그것이 그분을 위해 족장님께 허락된 것이 아니라고 누가 말하겠습니까? 제가 이제부터 하려는 일에서, 저는 큰 도움이 필요할지도 모릅니다. 지금 사양해 두면, 훗날 더 떳떳하게 족장님께 청할 수 있을 것입니다.\"\n\n이런 다툼이 한창일 때, 두 사람의 사자가 도착했다. 말루크와 알지 못하는 한 사람이었다. 먼저 말루크가 안으로 들었다.\n\n이 선량한 사내는 그날의 사건을 두고 솟구치는 기쁨을 숨기려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 제게 맡겨진 일로 말씀드리자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"시모니데스 어른께서 저를 보내어 전하시기를, 경기가 끝나자 로마 일파의 몇몇이 서둘러 상금 지급에 항의를 제기했다고 합니다.\"\n\n일데림이 벌떡 일어나, 더없이 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"하느님의 영광을 두고! 그 경주가 정정당당하게 이긴 것인지는 동방이 판가름할 것이다.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 족장님.\" 말루크가 말했다. \"주최자가 이미 상금을 지급했습니다.\"\n\n\"그것 참 잘되었군.\"\n\n\"벤허가 메살라의 바퀴를 들이받았다고 그들이 말하자, 주최자는 웃으며 반환점에서 아랍 말들이 당한 충돌을 그들에게 일깨워 주었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 아테네 사람은 어찌 되었나?\"\n\n\"죽었습니다.\"\n\n\"죽다니!\" 벤허가 외쳤다.\n\n\"죽다니!\" 일데림이 따라 외쳤다. \"이 로마의 괴물들은 무슨 운을 타고났단 말인가! 메살라는 살아남았느냐?\"\n\n\"살아남았습니다. 그렇습니다, 족장님, 목숨만은 부지했습니다. 그러나 그것이 그에게는 짐이 될 것입니다. 의원들은 그가 살기는 하겠으나 다시는 걷지 못하리라고 합니다.\"\n\n벤허는 말없이 하늘을 우러러보았다. 그의 눈앞에는 시모니데스처럼 의자에 묶인 채, 그처럼 하인들의 어깨에 실려 바깥을 다니는 메살라의 모습이 떠올랐다. 그 선량한 사람은 그 처지를 잘 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 자존심과 야망으로 가득한 이 사람은 어떻게 견딜 것인가?\n\n\"시모니데스 어른께서 더 전하라 하셨습니다.\" 말루크가 말을 이었다. \"산발랏이 곤경에 처했습니다. 드루수스와 그와 함께 서명한 자들은 자기들이 잃은 다섯 탈란트를 지급하는 문제를 집정관 막센티우스에게 넘겼고, 막센티우스는 그것을 카이사르에게 넘겼습니다. 메살라 역시 자기 패전금 지급을 거부했고, 산발랏은 드루수스를 본받아 집정관에게 갔으나, 그 문제는 아직 검토 중입니다. 점잖은 로마인들은 항의한 자들을 봐줘서는 안 된다고 말하며, 적대하는 모든 일파가 그들과 한편이 되고 있습니다. 도시가 그 추문으로 떠들썩합니다.\"\n\n\"시모니데스 어른은 뭐라 하시던가?\" 벤허가 물었다.\n\n\"어른께서는 웃으시며 무척 흡족해하십니다. 로마인이 돈을 치르면 그는 파산이요, 치르기를 거부하면 그는 불명예입니다. 황제의 정책이 그 문제를 판가름할 것입니다. 동방을 노엽게 하는 것은 파르티아인과의 관계에서 좋지 못한 시작이 될 것이요, 일데림 족장님을 노엽게 하는 것은 사막을 적으로 돌리는 일이니, 막센티우스의 모든 군사 작전선이 그 사막을 가로지르고 있습니다. 그러므로 시모니데스 어른께서는 조금도 마음 졸이지 말라 전하라 하셨습니다. 메살라는 돈을 치를 것입니다.\"\n\n일데림은 곧바로 명랑한 기분을 되찾았다.\n\n\"이제 그만 떠나세.\" 그가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"그 일은 시모니데스가 잘 처리할 걸세. 영광은 우리 것이지. 내 말들을 준비시키겠네.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 말루크가 말했다. \"제가 밖에 사자 하나를 남겨 두었습니다. 만나 보시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"하느님의 영광을 두고! 내가 그를 깜빡 잊었구먼.\"\n\n말루크가 물러가고, 그 뒤를 이어 점잖은 몸가짐에 고운 용모를 지닌 한 소년이 들어와, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 상냥하게 말했다.\n\"발타사르의 따님이시며, 선량한 일데림 족장님께서 잘 아시는 이라스 님께서 저에게 족장님께 전할 말씀을 맡기셨습니다. 따님께서 말씀하시기를, 족장님께서 사두마차의 승리를 두고 따님의 축하를 받아 주신다면 따님에게 크나큰 은혜를 베푸시는 것이라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"내 벗의 딸은 친절하구나.\" 일데림이 눈을 반짝이며 말했다. \"이 보석을 그 아가씨에게 전해 다오. 그 말에 내가 느낀 기쁨의 표시로다.\"\n\n그는 이렇게 말하며 손가락에서 반지를 빼냈다.\n\n\"분부대로 하겠습니다, 족장님.\" 소년이 대답하고는 말을 이었다. \"이집트 여인의 따님께서 저에게 한 가지 더 당부하셨습니다. 따님께서는 선량한 일데림 족장님께서 청년 벤허에게 전갈을 보내 주시기를 청하십니다. 따님의 아버님께서 한동안 이데르네 궁전에 거처를 정하셨으니, 따님께서 내일 넷째 시가 지나서 그 청년을 맞이하시겠다는 것입니다. 그리고 따님의 축하와 더불어, 베풀어 주신 이 또 다른 은혜에 대한 감사를 일데림 족장님께서 받아 주신다면, 따님께서는 더없이 기뻐하실 것입니다.\"\n\n족장은 기쁨으로 얼굴이 환해진 벤허를 바라보았다.\n\n\"어찌하겠나?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"허락해 주신다면, 족장님, 저는 그 아리따운 이집트 여인을 만나 보겠습니다.\"\n\n일데림이 웃으며 말했다. \"사내가 어찌 제 젊음을 누리지 않을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n그러자 벤허가 사자에게 대답했다.\n\n\"너를 보낸 분께 전하여라. 나, 벤허는 이데르네 궁전이 어디에 있든, 내일 정오에 그곳에서 그분을 뵙겠노라고.\"\n\n소년이 일어나, 말없이 인사를 올리고 떠났다.\n\n자정이 되자 일데림이 길에 올랐다. 자기를 뒤따라올 벤허를 위해 말 한 필과 길잡이 한 사람을 남겨 두기로 해 둔 터였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\n이튿날 이라스와의 약속을 지키러 가는 길에, 벤허는 도시 한복판에 있는 옴팔로스에서 헤롯의 주랑(柱廊)으로 접어들어, 이내 이데르네 궁에 다다랐다.\n\n거리에서 그는 먼저 현관으로 들어섰는데, 그 양옆에는 지붕이 덮인 계단이 있어 위쪽 주랑 현관으로 이어졌다. 계단 곁에는 날개 달린 사자들이 앉아 있었고, 한가운데에는 거대한 따오기 상이 바닥 위로 물을 뿜고 있었다. 사자, 따오기, 벽, 바닥은 모두 이집트를 떠올리게 했으니, 계단의 난간조차도 무엇 하나 빠짐없이 육중한 잿빛 돌로 되어 있었다.\n\n현관 위, 계단 층계참을 덮으며 주랑 현관이 솟아 있었다. 그것은 기둥으로 이루어진 우아한 구조물로, 어찌나 가볍고 어찌나 절묘하게 균형이 잡혀 있는지, 그 시대에는 그리스인이 아니고서는 좀처럼 떠올리기조차 어려운 것이었다. 눈처럼 새하얀 대리석으로 된 그것은, 마치 거대한 헐벗은 바위 위에 무심히 떨어진 백합 한 송이 같은 인상을 주었다.\n\n벤허는 주랑 현관의 그늘에 멈춰 서서 그 무늬와 마감, 그리고 대리석의 순백을 감상했다. 그러고 나서 그는 궁 안으로 들어섰다. 널찍한 접이문이 활짝 열린 채 그를 맞이했다. 그가 처음 들어선 통로는 높았으나 다소 좁았다. 바닥은 붉은 타일로 깔려 있었고, 벽도 그에 맞추어 같은 빛으로 물들여져 있었다. 그러나 이 소박함은 곧 다가올 어떤 아름다움을 예고하는 것이었다.\n\n그는 천천히 걸음을 옮겼고, 온갖 감각은 차분히 가라앉아 있었다. 머지않아 그는 이라스 앞에 서게 될 터였다. 그녀가 그를 기다리고 있었다. 노래와 이야기와 우스갯소리로, 반짝이고 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그녀의 눈길을 빛내는 미소와, 그 속삭임에 관능의 여운을 더해 주는 눈길로. 종려나무 과수원의 호수에서 뱃놀이를 하던 그날 저녁에도 그녀는 그를 불렀고, 지금도 그를 불렀다. 그리하여 그는 아름다운 이데르네 궁으로 그녀를 찾아가고 있었다. 그는 생각이 없다기보다, 행복하고 꿈결 같은 기분이었다.\n\n통로는 그를 닫힌 문 앞으로 데려갔고, 그는 그 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그러자 그 넓은 문짝들이 삐걱 소리도, 자물쇠나 빗장 소리도, 발이나 손가락의 닿음도 없이 저절로 열리기 시작했다. 그 기이함은 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경 속에 묻혀 버렸다.\n\n침침한 통로의 그늘에 서서 문간 너머를 바라보며, 그는 로마식 저택의 아트리움을 보았다. 널찍하고, 호화롭기가 그야말로 꿈결 같은 장려함에 이르러 있었다.\n\n그 방이 얼마나 컸는지는 말할 수 없으니, 정확한 비례에는 사람을 속이는 데가 있기 때문이다. 그 깊이는 끝없이 펼쳐진 풍경 같았으니, 같은 넓이의 실내를 두고는 결코 할 수 없는 말이었다. 그가 걸음을 멈추고 둘러보며 바닥을 내려다보았을 때, 그는 백조를 어루만지는 모습으로 그려진 레다의 가슴 위에 서 있었다. 그리고 더 멀리 보니, 바닥 전체가 똑같이 신화를 소재로 한 모자이크 그림으로 깔려 있었다. 또 걸상과 의자가 있었는데, 저마다 다른 도안에, 절묘하게 짜인 예술품이었다. 정교하게 새긴 탁자들이 있었고, 여기저기에는 그 자체로 사람을 청하는 듯한 긴 의자들이 놓여 있었다. 벽에서 도드라져 나온 가구들은, 마치 잔물결 하나 없는 물 위에 떠 있기라도 한 듯, 바닥에 또렷이 한 번 더 비쳤다. 벽의 판벽, 그 위에 그려지고 돋을새김된 형상들, 천장의 벽화까지도 바닥에 그대로 비쳤다. 천장은 한가운데를 향해 휘어 올라갔고, 그곳에 난 구멍으로는 햇빛이 거침없이 쏟아져 들어왔으며, 더없이 푸른 하늘은 손에 닿을 듯했다. 그 구멍 아래의 임플루비움은 청동 난간으로 둘러쳐져 있었다. 구멍 가장자리에서 지붕을 떠받친 금빛 기둥들은 햇빛이 닿는 곳마다 불꽃처럼 빛났고, 그 아래 비친 모습은 끝없는 깊이로 뻗어 내려가는 듯했다. 진기하고 기묘한 촛대들이 있었고, 조각상과 꽃병들이 있었다. 그 모든 것이 어우러져, 키케로가 크라수스에게서 사들인 팔라티누스 언덕의 저택에도, 또는 사치로 그보다 더 이름난 스카우루스의 투스쿨룸 별장에도 능히 어울릴 만한 실내를 이루고 있었다.\n\n여전히 꿈결 같은 기분에 잠긴 채, 벤허는 눈에 보이는 모든 것에 매혹되어 거닐며 기다렸다. 조금 늦어지는 것쯤은 개의치 않았다. 이라스가 채비를 마치면, 그녀가 오거나 하인을 보낼 터였다. 잘 갖추어진 로마식 저택이라면 어디서나 아트리움은 손님을 맞이하는 방이었다.\n\n그는 두 번, 세 번 방을 빙 돌았다. 그때마다 지붕의 구멍 아래 서서, 하늘과 그 짙푸른 깊이를 헤아렸다. 그러고는 기둥에 몸을 기대어, 빛과 그늘이 어떻게 나뉘는지, 그 효과가 어떠한지 살폈다. 여기서는 베일처럼 사물을 작아 보이게 하고, 저기서는 광채로 다른 것들을 부풀려 보이게 했다. 그런데도 아무도 오지 않았다. 시간이, 아니 그보다도 시간의 흐름이 마침내 그에게 와닿기 시작했고, 그는 이라스가 왜 이리 오래 머무는지 의아해졌다. 그는 다시 바닥의 형상들을 더듬어 보았으나, 처음 보았을 때의 만족감은 없었다. 그는 자주 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다. 곧 조바심이 그의 마음에 약간 달뜬 입김을 불어넣었고, 다음번에는 더 세고 더 뜨겁게 불었다. 그러다 마침내 그는 저택을 옭아맨 정적을 깨닫게 되었고, 그 생각이 그를 불안하고 미심쩍게 만들었다. 그래도 그는 미소와 다짐으로 그 느낌을 밀어냈다. \"아, 그녀가 눈가에 마지막 단장을 하고 있거나, 나를 위해 화관을 엮고 있는 게로군. 곧 올 거야, 늦어진 만큼 더 아름다워져서!\" 그러고 나서 그는 자리에 앉아 촛대 하나를 감상했다. 굴림대 위에 얹힌 청동 받침대로, 옆면과 가장자리에 가는 세공이 새겨져 있었다. 한쪽 끝에는 기둥이, 그 맞은편 끝에는 제단과 여자 사제가 있었다. 늘어진 종려나무 가지의 끝마다 가느다란 사슬로 등잔 받침이 매달려 흔들렸다. 그것은 그 나름으로 더없는 경이였다. 그러나 정적이 자꾸만 끼어들었다. 그는 그 어여쁜 물건을 바라보면서도 귀를 기울였다. 귀를 기울였으나, 아무 소리도 없었다. 궁은 무덤처럼 고요했다.\n\n무언가 착오가 있었을 수도 있었다. 아니, 이집트 여인이 보낸 사람이 왔었고, 이곳은 이데르네 궁이었다. 그러자 그는 그 문이 얼마나 신비롭게, 소리도 없이, 저절로 열렸던가를 떠올렸다. 어디 한번 보자!\n\n그는 같은 문으로 갔다. 아무리 가볍게 걸어도 그 발소리는 크고 거슬렸고, 그는 그 소리에 움찔했다. 그는 신경이 곤두서고 있었다. 육중한 로마식 자물쇠는 그가 처음 들어 올리려는 시도를 버텨 냈다. 두 번째에는—그의 뺨에서 피가 싸늘하게 식었다—그가 온 힘을 다해 비틀어 보았으나, 헛일이었다. 문은 흔들리지도 않았다. 위험에 대한 감각이 그를 사로잡았고, 그는 한순간 어찌할 바를 모르고 서 있었다.\n\n안티오크에서 그를 해치려는 동기를 가진 자가 누구인가?\n\n메살라!\n\n그렇다면 이 이데르네 궁은? 그는 현관에서 이집트를 보았고, 새하얀 주랑 현관에서 아테네를 보았다. 그러나 이곳, 아트리움에서는 로마가 있었다. 그를 둘러싼 모든 것이 로마인의 소유임을 드러냈다. 과연 그 자리는 도시의 큰 길목에 있어, 그에게 폭력을 휘두르기에는 지나치게 사람들의 눈에 띄는 곳이었다. 그러나 바로 그렇기에, 그것은 그의 적이 지닌 대담무쌍한 기질에 더욱 들어맞았다. 아트리움은 모습을 바꾸었다. 그 모든 우아함과 아름다움에도, 그것은 함정에 지나지 않았다. 불안은 언제나 모든 것을 검게 칠한다.\n\n그 생각이 벤허를 격앙시켰다.\n\n아트리움의 좌우에는 문이 여럿 있었는데, 틀림없이 침실로 통하는 것이었다. 그는 그것들을 밀어 보았으나, 모두 단단히 잠겨 있었다. 두드리면 응답이 올지도 몰랐다. 소리 지르기가 부끄러워, 그는 긴 의자로 가서 누운 채 곰곰이 생각해 보려 했다.\n\n너무도 분명하게, 그는 갇힌 몸이었다. 그러나 무엇 때문에? 그리고 누구의 손에?\n\n만일 이것이 메살라의 짓이라면! 그는 일어나 앉아 사방을 둘러보고는, 도전하듯 미소 지었다. 탁자마다 무기가 있었다. 새들이 황금 새장 속에서 굶어 죽기도 했다지만, 그는 그렇게 되지 않을 터였다. 긴 의자들이 그에게는 성문을 부수는 망치가 되어 줄 것이었다. 그는 힘이 셌고, 분노와 절망 속에서 그 힘은 한껏 부풀어 올랐다!\n\n메살라가 직접 올 수는 없었다. 그는 두 번 다시 걷지 못할 것이었다. 시모니데스처럼 불구가 된 몸이었다. 그래도 그는 다른 자들을 움직일 수 있었다. 그가 움직일 수 있는 자들이 어디엔들 없겠는가? 벤허는 일어나 다시 문들을 밀어 보았다. 한번은 소리쳐 불렀는데, 방이 어찌나 메아리치는지 그 자신이 흠칫 놀랐다. 그는 애써 침착함을 가장하며, 빠져나갈 길을 부수어 내기 전에 한동안 기다려 보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그런 처지에 놓이면 마음은 불안의 밀물과 썰물을 겪으며, 그 사이사이에 평온의 틈이 끼어든다. 마침내—그것이 얼마나 오래였는지는 그 자신도 말할 수 없었으나—그는 이 일이 우연이거나 착오라는 결론에 이르렀다. 이 궁은 분명 누군가의 것이었고, 보살피고 지키는 사람이 있을 터였다. 그 관리인이 올 것이었다. 저녁이나 밤이 그를 데려올 것이었다. 인내하자!\n\n그렇게 결론을 내리고, 그는 기다렸다.\n\n반 시간이 흘렀다—벤허에게는 훨씬 더 긴 시간이었다—그때 그를 들였던 문이 전과 같이 소리 없이 열렸다 닫혔으나, 그의 주의를 끌지는 못했다.\n\n그 일이 일어난 순간, 그는 방의 가장 먼 끝에 앉아 있었다. 발소리에 그는 흠칫했다.\n\n\"드디어 그녀가 왔구나!\" 그는 안도와 기쁨에 가슴이 뛰며 그렇게 생각하고 일어났다.\n\n발걸음은 무거웠고, 거친 샌들이 긁히고 부딪치는 소리가 따랐다. 금빛 기둥들이 그와 문 사이를 가로막고 있었다. 그는 조용히 다가가 그중 하나에 몸을 기댔다. 이내 그는 목소리를 들었다—남자들의 목소리였고, 그중 하나는 거칠고 목구멍에서 나오는 소리였다. 무슨 말인지는 알아들을 수 없었으니, 그것은 동방의 말도, 남유럽의 말도 아니었다.\n\n방을 두루 둘러본 뒤, 낯선 자들은 왼쪽으로 가로질러 가더니, 벤허의 시야에 들어왔다. 두 남자였는데, 하나는 몹시 건장했고, 둘 다 키가 컸으며, 둘 다 짧은 튜닉을 걸치고 있었다. 그들은 이 집의 주인이나 하인의 분위기가 아니었다. 그들 눈에는 보이는 것마다 다 신기한 모양이었고, 멈춰 서서 살펴보는 것마다 죄다 손을 댔다. 그들은 천박한 자들이었다. 그들이 있는 것만으로도 아트리움이 더럽혀지는 것 같았다. 그러면서도 그 느긋한 태도와 거침없는 거동은 무언가 권리나 볼일이 있음을 가리켰다. 볼일이라면, 누구와의 볼일인가?\n\n그들은 알아들을 수 없는 말을 잔뜩 지껄이며 이리저리 거닐었고, 그러는 내내 벤허가 서 있는 기둥 쪽으로 조금씩 다가왔다. 조금 떨어진 곳, 비스듬히 비쳐 든 햇살이 바닥의 모자이크 위에 환하게 떨어진 자리에, 그들의 눈길을 끄는 조각상 하나가 있었다. 그것을 살피느라, 그들은 빛 속에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n이미 보았듯이, 이 궁에 자신이 있게 된 까닭을 둘러싼 수수께끼는 벤허의 신경을 곤두서게 했다. 그러던 차에, 키가 크고 건장한 낯선 자가 로마에서 알고 지내던, 그리고 바로 전날 원형 경기장에서 우승한 권투 선수로 월계관을 쓴 것을 보았던 바로 그 북방인임을 알아보았을 때, 그 사내의 얼굴이 숱한 싸움의 상처로 흉터투성이가 되고 사나운 격정으로 짐승처럼 거칠어진 것을 보았을 때, 그 사내의 드러난 사지가 그야말로 단련과 훈련의 경이이며 그 어깨가 헤라클레스처럼 떡 벌어진 것을 살펴보았을 때, 자신에게 닥친 위험에 대한 생각이 그의 온 핏줄에 한기를 일으켰다. 확실한 본능이 그에게 경고했으니, 살인을 위한 이 기회가 우연히 찾아왔다고 하기에는 너무도 완벽하다는 것이었다. 그리고 지금 여기 그 하수인들이 있었고, 그들의 볼일은 그를 상대로 한 것이었다. 그는 불안한 눈길을 북방인의 동료에게로 돌렸다. 젊고, 눈도 머리카락도 검었으며, 생김새가 영락없이 유대인이었다. 그는 또한 두 사내 모두 그들 부류의 직업인들이 경기장에서 즐겨 입던 차림 그대로임을 알아챘다. 여러 정황을 한데 꿰어 맞추자, 벤허는 더는 의심할 수 없었다. 그는 어떤 계략에 걸려 이 궁으로 꾀어 들여진 것이었다. 도움의 손길이 닿지 않는 곳, 이 으리으리한 밀실에서, 그는 죽게 될 운명이었다!\n\n어찌해야 할지 몰라, 그는 이 사내에게서 저 사내에게로 시선을 옮겼다. 그러는 사이 그의 안에서는 마음의 그 기적이 일어났으니, 그로 인해 삶이 무시무시할 만큼 세세하게 우리 앞에 펼쳐져, 마치 남의 것인 양 스스로 들여다보게 되는 것이었다. 그리고 그 펼쳐짐으로부터, 어떤 숨은 깊이로부터, 마치 보이지 않는 손에 의해 던져 올려지듯, 그는 자신이 새로운 삶에 들어섰음을 보게 되었다. 그 삶이 옛 삶과 다른 점은 이것이었다. 옛 삶에서 그는 자신에게 가해진 폭력의 희생자였으나, 이제부터 그는 공격하는 자가 되리라는 것이었다. 바로 어제 그는 자신의 첫 희생자를 보지 않았던가! 순전히 그리스도교적인 천성이라면 이 깨달음이 회한이라는 나약함을 가져왔을 것이다. 벤허는 그렇지 않았다. 그의 영혼은 마지막의, 가장 위대한 율법 수여자가 아니라, 첫 번째 율법 수여자의 가르침에서 그 감정을 길어 왔다. 그는 메살라에게 잘못이 아니라 응징을 내린 것이었다. 주님의 허락으로 그는 승리했다. 그리고 그는 그 사실에서 믿음을 길어 냈다—믿음이야말로 모든 이성적인 힘의 원천이요, 특히 위기 속에서의 힘의 원천이었다.\n\n그 영향은 거기서 그치지 않았다. 그 새로운 삶은 그에게 이제 막 시작된 사명으로 비쳤으니, 장차 오실 왕이 거룩한 만큼 거룩하고, 그 왕의 오심이 확실한 만큼 확실한 사명이었다—힘을 쓰는 것이 다만 피할 수 없는 일이라는 이유만으로도 정당한 사명이었다. 그런 사명의 문턱에서, 그가 두려워해야 한단 말인가?\n\n그는 허리에 두른 띠를 풀고, 머리를 드러내고, 새하얀 유대식 겉옷을 벗어 던졌다. 그러자 적들의 것과 그리 다르지 않은 속튜닉 차림으로 나서게 되었고, 그는 몸도 마음도 만반의 채비가 되었다. 팔짱을 끼고, 그는 기둥에 등을 댄 채, 차분히 기다렸다.\n\n조각상을 살피는 일은 오래가지 않았다. 곧 북방인이 돌아서서 알 수 없는 말로 무언가를 말했다. 그러자 둘 다 벤허를 바라보았다. 몇 마디를 더 주고받더니, 그들은 그를 향해 다가왔다.\n\n\"너희는 누구냐?\" 그가 라틴어로 물었다.\n\n북방인이 미소를 지었으나, 그 얼굴에서 야수성을 걷어내지는 못했다. 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"야만인이다.\"\n\n\"이곳은 이데르네 궁이다. 누구를 찾느냐? 멈춰 서서 대답하라.\"\n\n그 말은 진지하게 던져졌다. 낯선 자들이 멈춰 섰다. 그리고 이번에는 북방인이 물었다. \"너는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"로마인이다.\"\n\n거인은 어깨 위로 고개를 젖혔다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하! 옛날에 신 하나가 소금 바른 돌을 핥는 암소에게서 나왔다는 얘기는 들었다만, 아무리 신이라도 유대인을 로마인으로 만들 수는 없지.\"\n\n웃음이 끝나자 그는 다시 동료에게 무어라 말했고, 그들은 더 가까이 다가왔다.\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 벤허가 기둥에서 떨어져 나오며 말했다. \"한마디만 하자.\"\n\n그들이 다시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"한마디라!\" 색슨인이 그 거대한 두 팔을 가슴께에 모아 끼고, 얼굴에 거뭇거뭇 떠오르던 위협을 누그러뜨리며 대꾸했다. \"한마디라! 말해 봐라.\"\n\n\"너는 북방인 토르드다.\"\n\n거인이 푸른 눈을 크게 떴다.\n\n“넌 로마에서 검투사 교관이었지.”\n\n\"나는 너의 제자였다.\"\n\n\"아니지.\" 토르드가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"이르민의 수염을 두고 맹세컨대, 나는 유대인을 데려다 싸움꾼으로 만든 적이 단 한 번도 없다.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 말이 참임을 증명해 보이지.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"너는 나를 죽이러 여기 왔다.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 이 사내가 나와 단둘이 겨루게 하라. 그러면 내가 그자의 몸으로 증명해 보이겠다.\"\n\n북방인의 얼굴에 한 가닥 익살이 번졌다. 그는 동료에게 무어라 말했고, 동료가 대답하자, 그는 흥에 겨운 어린아이 같은 천진함으로 응답했다.\n\n\"내가 시작하라고 할 때까지 기다려라.\"\n\n그는 발끝으로 거듭 밀어, 긴 의자 하나를 바닥 가운데로 밀어내고는, 느긋하게 그 우람한 몸을 그 위에 뉘었다. 더없이 편안한 자세를 잡고 나서, 그는 간단히 말했다. \"자, 시작해라.\"\n\n벤허는 군말 없이 자신의 적수에게로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"막아 봐라.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n사내는 마다하지 않고 두 손을 들어 올렸다.\n\n이렇게 둘이 격투의 정석대로 자세를 잡고 마주 섰을 때, 둘 사이에는 이렇다 할 우열의 차이가 보이지 않았다. 오히려 그들은 형제처럼 닮아 있었다. 낯선 자의 자신만만한 미소에 맞서, 벤허는 진지함을 드러냈으니, 만일 그의 솜씨가 알려졌더라면 그 진지함은 위험을 알리는 정당한 경고로 받아들여졌을 것이다. 둘 다 이 싸움이 목숨을 건 것임을 알고 있었다.\n\n벤허가 오른손으로 거짓 동작을 했다. 낯선 자가 왼팔을 살짝 내밀며 막았다. 그가 미처 방어 자세로 돌아오기도 전에, 벤허가 그의 손목을 움켜잡았으니, 여러 해를 노에 매달려 살아온 손아귀가 만들어 낸 그 악력은 바이스처럼 무시무시했다. 기습은 완벽했고, 빈틈이라곤 주지 않았다. 몸을 앞으로 던지는 것, 사내의 팔을 그 목을 가로질러 오른쪽 어깨 위로 밀어붙이며 그를 왼쪽 옆으로 돌려세우는 것, 준비된 왼손으로 어김없이 내리치는 것, 귀밑의 드러난 목을 후려치는 것—이 모든 것은 한 동작의 자잘한 마디에 지나지 않았다. 두 번째 일격은 필요 없었다. 하수인은 비명 한 번 없이 묵직하게 쓰러져, 꼼짝 않고 누웠다.\n\n벤허가 토르드에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"하! 이게 뭐냐! 이르민의 수염을 두고!\" 토르드가 깜짝 놀라 소리치며 일어나 앉았다. 그러더니 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하! 나라도 저보다 더 잘하지는 못했겠다.\"\n\n그는 벤허를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 차분히 훑어보더니, 일어서서 감탄을 숨기지 않은 채 그를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"저건 내 기술이다—내가 로마의 도장에서 십 년 동안 갈고닦은 기술이야. 너는 유대인이 아니다. 너는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"너는 이인 집정관 아리우스를 알았지.\"\n\n\"퀸투스 아리우스? 그래, 그분은 내 후원자였다.\"\n\n\"그분에게는 아들이 있었다.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 토르드가 말했고, 두들겨 맞은 그의 얼굴이 흐릿하게나마 환해졌다. \"그 젊은이를 나도 알았지. 그는 검투사의 왕이 되고도 남았을 거다. 카이사르께서 친히 후원하겠다고 하셨지. 내가 그에게 바로 네가 이자에게 쓴 그 기술을 가르쳤다—나 같은 손과 팔이 아니고서는 도저히 불가능한 기술이야. 그 기술로 나는 숱한 월계관을 따냈지.\"\n\n\"내가 바로 그 아리우스의 아들이다.\"\n\n토르드가 더 가까이 다가와 그를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그러더니 그의 눈이 진정한 기쁨으로 빛났고, 그는 웃으며 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하! 그자가 나더러 여기서 유대인을 만나게 될 거라 했지—유대인을—유대인 개새끼를—그놈을 죽이는 게 신들을 섬기는 일이라고 했어.\"\n\n\"누가 그렇게 말하더냐?\" 벤허가 그 손을 잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"그자가—메살라가—하, 하, 하!\"\n\n\"언제 그랬느냐, 토르드?\"\n\n\"어젯밤이다.\"\n\n\"나는 그가 다친 줄로 알았는데.\"\n\n\"그자는 두 번 다시 걷지 못할 거다. 침상에 누워, 신음 사이사이로 내게 말하더군.\"\n\n몇 마디 말로 증오를 더없이 생생하게 그려 낸 셈이었다. 그리고 벤허는 보았다. 그 로마인이 살아남는다면 여전히 능력 있고 위험한 자로서, 끈질기게 자신을 쫓아오리라는 것을. 무너진 삶에 단맛을 더해 줄 복수가 아직 남아 있었으니, 그래서 산발랏과의 내기에서 잃은 재산에 그토록 매달리는 것이었다. 벤허는 사태를 곰곰이 짚어 보며, 자신이 장차 오실 왕을 위해 떠맡은 일에서 그의 적이 그를 방해할 수 있는 갖가지 길을 또렷이 내다보았다. 그라고 로마인의 방식을 쓰지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가? 그를 죽이라고 고용된 자라면, 거꾸로 되받아치라고도 고용할 수 있었다. 더 높은 삯을 내미는 것은 그의 능력 안에 있었다. 유혹은 강했다. 그리고 거기에 반쯤 넘어가던 차에, 그는 우연히 좀 전의 적수를 내려다보게 되었으니, 그자는 하얗게 위를 향한 얼굴로 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었고, 그 모습이 자신과 어찌나 닮았던지. 한 줄기 빛이 그에게 떠올랐고, 그가 물었다. \"토르드, 메살라가 나를 죽이는 대가로 너에게 얼마를 주기로 했느냐?\"\n\n\"천 세스테르티우스다.\"\n\n\"그건 그대로 받게 될 거다. 그리고 네가 지금 내 말대로 한다면, 거기에 삼천을 더 얹어 주겠다.\"\n\n거인이 소리 내어 셈을 했다.\n\n\"어제 오천을 벌었고, 로마인 그자에게서—육천이지. 사천을 다오, 착한 아리우스—사천을 더 주면—내 이름과 같은 늙은 토르 신이 망치로 나를 내려친다 해도, 내 너를 위해 굳건히 서겠다. 사천으로 하자. 네가 시키기만 한다면 그 거짓말쟁이 귀족 놈도 죽여 주마. 그저 그 입을 손으로 틀어막기만 하면 돼—이렇게.\"\n\n그는 제 입을 손으로 틀어막아 보이며 그 방법을 시연했다.\n\n\"알겠다.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"만 세스테르티우스라면 한밑천이지. 그 돈이면 로마로 돌아가, 대전차 경기장 근처에 술집을 열고, 검투사 교관 중에서도 으뜸가는 자답게 살 수 있을 거다.\"\n\n그 그림이 거인에게 안겨 준 기쁨에, 그의 얼굴에 난 흉터들마저 새삼 화끈 달아올랐다.\n\n\"사천으로 해 주마.\" 벤허가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그 돈을 받고 네가 할 일에는, 토르드, 네 손에 피 한 방울 묻지 않을 거다. 자, 들어 봐라. 여기 네 동료가 나와 닮지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"같은 나무에서 딴 사과라고 해도 믿겠더라.\"\n\n\"좋다. 내가 그자의 튜닉을 입고, 그자에게는 내 이 옷을 입혀, 너와 내가 그자를 여기 남겨 두고 함께 떠난다면, 그래도 너는 메살라에게서 그 세스테르티우스를 똑같이 받아 낼 수 있지 않겠느냐? 죽은 게 나라고 그자가 믿게 만들기만 하면 되는 거다.\"\n\n토르드는 눈물이 입가로 흘러내리도록 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하! 만 세스테르티우스를 이렇게 쉽게 번 적은 없었지. 게다가 대전차 경기장 옆에 술집까지!—그것도 피 한 방울 안 묻은 거짓말 하나로! 하, 하, 하! 손을 다오, 오 아리우스의 아들이여. 자, 이제 어서 가거라, 그리고—하, 하, 하!—언제고 로마에 오거든, 북방인 토르드의 술집을 꼭 물어보아라. 이르민의 수염을 두고, 카이사르에게서 빌려서라도 너에게 제일 좋은 걸 내주마!\"\n\n그들은 다시 손을 맞잡았다. 그러고 나서 옷을 바꿔 입는 일이 이루어졌다. 그런 다음, 밤에 심부름꾼을 토르드의 거처로 보내 사천 세스테르티우스를 전하기로 약속이 정해졌다. 일을 다 마치자, 거인은 정문을 두드렸다. 문이 그에게 열렸다. 그리고 아트리움을 나서며, 그는 벤허를 옆방으로 데려갔고, 거기서 벤허는 죽은 권투 선수의 거친 옷가지로 차림을 마저 갖추었다. 그들은 곧장 옴팔로스에서 헤어졌다.\n\n\"잊지 마라, 오 아리우스의 아들이여, 대전차 경기장 옆 그 술집을 잊지 마라! 하, 하, 하! 이르민의 수염을 두고, 이렇게 헐값에 거머쥔 행운은 다시없을 게다. 신들이 너를 지켜 주시기를!\"\n\n아트리움을 나서며, 벤허는 유대식 옷을 입고 누워 있는 그 하수인을 마지막으로 한 번 바라보았고, 흡족했다. 닮은 모습이 놀라울 정도였다. 토르드가 신의를 지킨다면, 그 속임수는 영영 비밀로 묻힐 것이었다.\n\n밤이 되어, 시모니데스의 집에서 벤허는 그 선한 사람에게 이데르네 궁에서 일어난 일을 모두 들려주었다. 그리고 며칠 뒤에 아리우스의 아들의 행방을 찾는 공개 수색을 흘려보내기로 의견이 모아졌다. 결국 그 일은 막센티우스에게까지 대담하게 가져갈 것이었다. 그렇게 해도 수수께끼가 풀리지 않으면, 메살라와 그라투스는 마음을 놓고 흡족해할 것이고, 벤허는 자유로이 예루살렘으로 떠나 잃어버린 가족을 찾아 나설 수 있으리라는 결론이었다.\n\n작별할 때, 시모니데스는 강이 내려다보이는 테라스에 의자를 내놓고 앉아, 아버지 같은 간곡함으로 작별 인사와 주님의 평안을 빌어 주었다. 에스더가 그 젊은이를 따라 계단 어귀까지 나왔다.\n\n\"내가 어머니를 찾게 되면, 에스더, 그대는 예루살렘으로 어머니에게 가서, 티르자에게 자매가 되어 주오.\"\n\n그러고는 그 말과 함께 그가 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그것은 다만 평안의 입맞춤이었을까?\n\n이튿날 그는 강을 건너 일데림이 머물던 곳으로 갔고, 거기서 자신의 길잡이가 되어 줄 아랍인을 만났다. 말들이 끌려 나왔다.\n\n\"이놈은 당신 것입니다.\" 아랍인이 말했다.\n\n벤허가 보니, 아, 그것은 미라의 아들들 가운데 가장 빠르고 가장 빛나는 알데바란이었으니, 시리우스 다음으로 족장이 사랑하던 말이었다. 그리고 그는 알았다, 이 선물과 더불어 그 노인의 마음까지 자신에게 건너왔음을.\n\n아트리움의 시신은 밤중에 들려 나가 묻혔다. 그리고 메살라의 계획의 일부로, 벤허의 죽음을—이번에는 의심할 여지 없는 죽음을—알려 그라투스를 안심시키기 위해 한 전령이 그에게로 떠났다.\n\n머지않아 대전차 경기장 근처에 술집 하나가 문을 열었고, 그 문 위에는 이런 글귀가 적혀 있었다.\n북방인 토르드.\n\n# BOOK SIXTH\n\n“저것은 죽음인가? 그리고 둘이 있는가?\n저 여인이 죽음의 짝인가?\n\n그 살갗은 나병처럼 희었으니,\n‘삶 속의 죽음’이라는 악몽이 바로 그 여인이라,\n\n# COLERIDGE. CHAPTER I\n\n사람의 피를 차갑게 엉기게 하는 자였다.”\n콜리지.\n\n우리 이야기는 이제 벤허가 안티오크를 떠나 일데림 족장과 함께 사막으로 나간 그 밤으로부터 서른 날을 앞으로 나아간다.\n\n커다란 변화가 일어났다. 적어도 우리 주인공의 운명에 관한 한 큰 변화였다. 발레리우스 그라투스의 후임으로 본디오 빌라도가 부임한 것이다!\n이 교체에는, 한마디 덧붙이자면, 시모니데스가 당시 황제의 총신으로서 권세의 정점에 있던 세야누스에게 직접 건넨 로마 화폐로 정확히 다섯 달란트의 비용이 들었다. 그 목적은 벤허가 예루살렘 안팎에서 가족을 찾으려 애쓰는 동안 노출을 줄여 그를 돕는 데 있었다. 충직한 종은 드루수스와 그 일당에게서 딴 돈을 이처럼 경건한 용도에 썼다. 내기에 진 그들은 빚을 갚자마자 당연하게도 일제히 메살라의 적이 되었으니, 메살라가 빚을 떼어먹은 일은 로마에서 아직도 결말이 나지 않은 문제로 남아 있었다.\n\n짧은 시간이었으나 유대인들은 이미 통치자의 교체가 더 나은 쪽으로 이루어진 것이 아님을 알았다.\n\n안토니아 요새의 수비대를 교대하러 파견된 군대는 밤을 틈타 도성으로 들어왔다. 이튿날 아침, 그 근처에 사는 사람들의 눈에 가장 먼저 들어온 광경은 옛 요새의 성벽을 장식한 군기였는데, 불행하게도 그것은 독수리와 구체에 황제의 흉상이 뒤섞인 표지였다. 격분한 군중이 빌라도가 머물고 있는 가이사랴로 행진하여, 그 가증스러운 형상들을 치워 달라고 간청했다. 닷새 밤낮을 그들은 그의 궁전 문 앞에 죽치고 늘어섰다. 마침내 그는 원형 경기장에서 그들을 만나기로 약속했다. 그들이 모이자 그는 병사들로 그들을 에워쌌다. 그러나 그들은 저항하는 대신 제 목숨을 내놓았고, 그리하여 이겼다. 그는 그 형상과 군기를 가이사랴로 다시 거두어 갔으니, 그곳은 그라투스가 좀 더 사려 깊게 그 혐오스러운 물건들을 십일 년 통치 기간 내내 가두어 두었던 곳이었다.\n\n아무리 악한 자라도 이따금은 선행으로 제 악행에 변화를 주는 법이니, 빌라도도 그러했다. 그는 유대 지방의 모든 감옥을 점검하라 명하고, 구금된 자들의 이름과 그들이 갇힌 죄목을 적은 보고를 올리게 했다. 그 동기는 분명, 갓 부임한 관리에게 흔한 것—떠넘겨진 책임에 대한 두려움이었다. 그러나 백성은 그 조치에서 비롯될 선을 헤아려 그를 좋게 여겼고, 한동안 위안을 얻었다. 드러난 사실들은 놀라웠다. 아무런 고발도 없던 수백 명이 풀려났고, 오래전에 죽은 줄로만 알았던 많은 이들이 햇빛을 보게 되었다. 더욱 놀라운 것은, 당시 백성에게 알려지지 않았을 뿐 아니라 감옥 당국조차 까맣게 잊고 있던 지하 감방들이 열렸다는 사실이다. 그런 후자의 사례 가운데 하나를 우리는 지금 다루려 한다. 그리고 기이하게도, 그 일은 예루살렘에서 일어났다.\n\n안토니아 요새는 모리아산의 성스러운 구역 가운데 삼분의 이를 차지하고 있던 것으로 기억될 터인데, 본디 마케도니아 사람들이 세운 성채였다. 훗날 요한 히르카누스가 그 성채를 성전 방어를 위한 요새로 키웠으니, 그의 시대에는 어떤 공격에도 함락되지 않으리라 여겨졌다. 그러나 헤롯이 더 대담한 기질을 품고 등장하여 성벽을 보강하고 넓혀, 거대한 건축물을 남겼다. 그것은 그가 영원토록 요새로 삼고자 한 시설에 꼭 필요한 모든 부속을 갖추고 있었으니, 곧 집무실, 막사, 무기고, 창고, 저수조, 그리고 마지막이되 결코 사소하지 않은 온갖 등급의 감옥이 그러했다. 그는 단단한 바위를 평평하게 깎고, 깊이 파 들어가 굴을 뚫었으며, 그 위에 건물을 올렸다. 그리고 그 거대한 덩어리 전체를 아름다운 주랑(柱廊)으로 성전과 이었으니, 그 주랑의 지붕에서는 성전 건물의 안뜰들을 내려다볼 수 있었다. 이러한 상태로 요새는 마침내 그의 손에서 로마인의 손으로 넘어갔다. 그들은 그 견고함과 이점을 재빨리 알아보았고, 그런 주인에게 어울리는 용도로 그것을 바꾸어 놓았다. 그라투스의 통치 내내 그곳은 수비대가 주둔한 성채였고, 혁명가들에게 두려운 지하 감옥이었다. 무리를 이룬 군대가 소요를 진압하러 그 문에서 쏟아져 나올 때 화 있을진저! 유대인이 체포되어 그 같은 문을 지나 안으로 끌려들어 갈 때 그에 못지않게 화 있을진저!\n\n이쯤 설명해 두고, 우리는 서둘러 이야기로 나아간다.\n\n구금된 자들의 보고를 요구한 새 총독의 명령은 안토니아 요새에 전달되었고, 곧바로 이행되었다. 마지막 불행한 자가 심문을 위해 끌려 올라온 지 이틀이 지났다. 발송 준비를 마친 그 표로 정리된 보고서는 지휘를 맡은 호민관의 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다. 앞으로 오 분이면 그것은 시온산 위의 궁전에 머물고 있는 빌라도에게로 떠날 참이었다.\n\n호민관의 집무실은 널찍하고 시원했으며, 여러모로 그토록 중요한 초소 사령관의 위엄에 걸맞은 양식으로 꾸며져 있었다. 그날 일곱째 시각쯤 그를 들여다보면, 그 장교는 지치고 초조해 보였다. 보고서가 발송되고 나면 그는 주랑의 지붕으로 올라가 바람을 쐬고 운동을 하며, 저편 성전 안뜰의 유대인들을 구경하는 재미를 누릴 작정이었다. 그의 부하들과 서기들도 그 초조함을 함께 나누고 있었다.\n\n기다림에 사로잡혀 있을 때, 한 사내가 옆방으로 통하는 문간에 나타났다. 그는 저마다 망치만큼 묵직한 열쇠 꾸러미를 절그럭거리며, 단번에 책임자의 주의를 끌었다.\n\n“아, 게시우스! 들어오게.” 호민관이 말했다.\n\n새로 들어온 자가 책임자가 안락의자에 앉아 있는 탁자 뒤편으로 다가가자, 그 자리에 있던 모두가 그를 바라보았다. 그리고 그의 얼굴에 어린 일종의 놀라움과 굴욕의 표정을 알아채고는, 그가 무슨 말을 하려는지 들으려 입을 다물었다.\n\n“오, 호민관님!” 그는 허리를 깊이 숙이며 입을 열었다. “지금 제가 가져온 소식을 어떻게 말씀드려야 할지 두렵습니다.”\n\n“또 무슨 착오인가, 게시우스?”\n\n“그저 착오일 뿐이라고 스스로를 납득시킬 수 있다면, 저는 두렵지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 범죄인가. 아니면, 더 나쁘게는 직무 위반인가. 자네는 카이사르를 비웃거나 신들을 저주하고도 살아남을 수 있네. 그러나 그 죄가 독수리 군기에 닿는 것이라면—아, 자네도 알지 않나, 게시우스—말해 보게!”\n\n“발레리우스 그라투스가 저를 이 요새의 감옥지기로 뽑은 지 이제 여덟 해쯤 되었습니다.” 사내는 차분하게 말했다. “제가 직무를 시작하던 그날 아침이 기억납니다. 그 전날 폭동이 있었고, 거리에서 싸움이 벌어졌습니다. 우리는 많은 유대인을 죽였고, 우리 쪽도 손실을 입었습니다. 그 일은 그라투스를 암살하려던 시도에서 비롯되었다고들 했는데, 그분은 지붕에서 날아온 기와에 맞아 말에서 떨어진 터였습니다. 저는 그분이 지금 호민관님께서 앉아 계신 바로 그 자리에 머리에 붕대를 감고 앉아 계신 것을 보았습니다. 그분은 저를 뽑았다고 일러 주시고, 감방 번호와 맞추어 번호가 매겨진 이 열쇠들을 주셨습니다. 이것이 제 직무의 표지이니 결코 손에서 놓아서는 안 된다고 하셨지요. 탁자 위에는 양피지 두루마리가 있었습니다. 저를 가까이 부르신 그분은 그 두루마리를 펼치셨습니다. ‘여기 감방들의 지도가 있다’고 하셨습니다. 지도는 세 장이었습니다. ‘이것은’ 하고 그분이 말을 이으셨습니다. ‘위층의 배치를 보여 준다. 이 둘째 것은 둘째 층을, 이 마지막 것은 아래층을 알려 준다. 이것들을 너에게 맡긴다.’ 제가 그분 손에서 그것들을 받자, 그분은 다시 말씀하셨습니다. ‘이제 너에게 열쇠와 지도가 있으니, 곧장 가서 전체 배치를 익혀 두어라. 감방마다 들러 그 상태를 살펴라. 죄수의 보안을 위해 필요한 것이 있거든 네 판단에 따라 조치하라. 너는 내 밑에서 주인이며, 그 밖에 다른 자는 없으니.’\n\n“저는 그분께 경례를 올리고 물러나려 돌아섰습니다. 그분이 저를 다시 부르셨습니다. ‘아, 깜박했구나.’ 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. ‘셋째 층의 지도를 다오.’ 제가 그것을 드리자, 그분은 탁자 위에 그것을 펼치셨습니다. ‘게시우스, 여기다.’ 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. ‘이 감방을 보아라.’ 그분은 5번이라 매겨진 감방에 손가락을 짚으셨습니다. ‘저 감방에는 세 사람이 갇혀 있다. 흉악한 자들로, 어떤 경로로 나라의 기밀을 손에 넣었고, 그 호기심의 대가를 치르고 있는 것이다. 그 호기심이란’—그분은 저를 매섭게 바라보셨습니다—‘그런 일에 관해서라면 죄보다도 더 나쁜 것이다. 그래서 그들은 눈이 멀고 혀가 잘린 채 그곳에 종신토록 두어졌다. 그들에게는 먹을 것과 마실 것 외에는 아무것도 주지 않을 것이며, 그것은 벽에 난 구멍—덧문으로 가려진 그 구멍을 너는 보게 될 것이다—을 통해 넣어 줄 것이다. 알아들었느냐, 게시우스?’ 저는 그분께 대답을 올렸습니다. ‘좋다.’ 하고 그분이 말을 이으셨습니다. ‘한 가지 더, 너는 결코 잊어서는 안 될 것이 있다. 잊는다면’—그분은 저를 위협하듯 바라보셨습니다—‘그들의 감방 문—같은 층의 5번 감방—바로 이것이다, 게시우스’—그분은 제 기억에 새기려 그 특정한 감방에 손가락을 짚으셨습니다—‘어떤 목적으로도 결코 열려서는 안 된다. 누구를 들이려고도, 내보내려고도 안 되며, 너 자신도 마찬가지다.’ ‘그러나 그들이 죽으면 어찌합니까?’ 제가 여쭈었습니다. ‘그들이 죽으면’ 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. ‘그 감방이 그들의 무덤이 될 것이다. 그들은 죽어 사라지도록 그곳에 두어졌다. 그 감방은 나병이 든 곳이다. 알아들었느냐?’ 그 말씀과 함께 그분은 저를 보내셨습니다.”\n게시우스는 말을 멈추고, 겉옷 가슴께에서 양피지 세 장을 꺼냈는데, 모두 세월과 손때로 누렇게 바래 있었다. 그중 하나를 골라 호민관 앞 탁자 위에 펼치며, 그는 간단히 말했다. “이것이 아래층입니다.”\n\n사람들 모두가 그 지도를 바라보았다.\n__________\n\n| |\n| 통로 |\n\n| |\n|--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--|\n\n| | | | | |\n| 5 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n\n|_|__|__|__|___|\n“이것이, 오 호민관님, 제가 그라투스에게서 받은 그대로입니다. 보십시오, 5번 감방이 있습니다.” 게시우스가 말했다.\n\n“보이는군.” 호민관이 대답했다. “이제 계속하게. 그가 그 감방이 나병이 든 곳이라 했다지.”\n“한 가지 여쭙고 싶습니다.” 감옥지기가 조심스레 말했다.\n\n호민관이 그러라고 했다.\n\n“그런 상황에서, 저로서는 그 지도를 참된 것이라 믿을 권리가 있지 않았겠습니까?”\n“달리 어찌하겠는가?”\n\n“그런데, 그것은 참된 것이 아닙니다.”\n\n책임자가 놀라 고개를 들었다.\n\n“그것은 참된 것이 아닙니다.” 감옥지기가 거듭 말했다. “그것은 그 층에 감방이 다섯뿐이라 보여 주지만, 실은 여섯입니다.”\n“여섯이라고 했나?”\n\n“그 층을 있는 그대로—제가 그러하다고 믿는 그대로 보여 드리겠습니다.”\n게시우스는 서판의 한 면에 다음과 같은 도면을 그려 호민관에게 건넸다.\n\n__________\n| |\n\n|--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--|\n\n| | | | | |\n| 5 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n\n|--][---+--------+--------+--------+-------|\n| 6 |\n\n|__________|\n“수고했네.” 호민관이 그 그림을 살피며, 이야기가 끝났으리라 여기고 말했다. “지도를 바로잡게 하겠네. 아니, 더 나은 방법으로, 새로 하나 만들어 자네에게 주겠네. 아침에 받으러 오게.”\n\n이렇게 말하며 그가 일어섰다.\n\n“그러나 더 들어 주십시오, 오 호민관님.”\n\n“내일 하세, 게시우스, 내일.”\n\n“제가 아직 말씀드리지 못한 것은 기다릴 수 없는 일입니다.”\n\n호민관이 너그럽게 다시 의자에 앉았다.\n\n“서두르겠습니다.” 감옥지기가 겸손하게 말했다. “다만 한 가지 더 여쭙겠습니다. 5번 감방의 죄수들에 관하여 그라투스가 제게 더 일러 준 말을, 제가 믿을 권리가 있지 않았겠습니까?”\n\n“그렇네. 그 감방에 세 죄수가 있다고—나라의 죄수로서 눈멀고 혀 없는 자들이라고 믿는 것이 자네의 의무였네.”\n\n“그런데,” 감옥지기가 말했다. “그것 또한 사실이 아니었습니다.”\n\n“아니라고!” 호민관이 다시 흥미를 느끼며 말했다.\n\n“들어 보시고 친히 판단하십시오, 오 호민관님. 명령받은 대로 저는 모든 감방을 둘러보았으니, 첫 층의 것들에서 시작하여 아래층의 것들로 마쳤습니다. 5번 감방의 문은 결코 열어서는 안 된다는 명령은 지켜졌습니다. 여덟 해 내내 세 사람 몫의 먹을 것과 마실 것이 벽의 구멍을 통해 들어갔습니다. 어제 저는 그 문으로 갔습니다. 어떤 예상도 뒤엎고 그토록 오래 살아남은 가련한 자들을 보고 싶은 호기심에서였습니다. 자물쇠가 열쇠를 받지 않았습니다. 우리가 조금 잡아당기자 문은 경첩에서 녹슬어 떨어져 버렸습니다. 안으로 들어가 보니, 단 한 사람뿐이었습니다. 늙고, 눈멀고, 혀가 없고, 벌거벗은 자였습니다. 그의 머리카락은 굳어 엉긴 채 허리 아래로 늘어져 있었습니다. 그의 살갗은 저기 저 양피지 같았습니다. 그가 두 손을 내밀었는데, 손톱은 새의 발톱처럼 말려 비틀려 있었습니다. 저는 그의 동료들이 어디 있느냐고 물었습니다. 그는 고개를 저어 없다고 했습니다. 나머지를 찾을까 하여 우리는 감방을 뒤졌습니다. 바닥은 말라 있었고, 벽도 그러했습니다. 세 사람이 그곳에 갇혔고 그중 둘이 죽었다면, 적어도 그들의 뼈는 남아 있었을 것입니다.”\n\n“그래서 자네 생각은—”\n\n“제 생각으로는, 오 호민관님, 여덟 해 동안 그곳에는 단 한 명의 죄수만 있었습니다.”\n\n책임자가 감옥지기를 날카롭게 바라보며 말했다. “말조심하게. 자네는 발레리우스가 거짓을 말했다는 것 이상을 말하고 있네.”\n\n게시우스는 고개를 숙였으나, 이렇게 말했다. “그분이 착오를 하셨을 수도 있습니다.”\n\n“아니, 그는 옳았네.” 호민관이 열을 띠며 말했다. “자네 자신의 진술로 보아 그는 옳았어. 방금 자네가 여덟 해 동안 세 사람 몫의 먹을 것과 마실 것이 들어갔다고 하지 않았나?”\n\n곁에 있던 자들이 책임자의 명민함을 칭찬했다. 그러나 게시우스는 당황한 기색이 없었다.\n\n“호민관님은 아직 이야기의 절반밖에 듣지 못하셨습니다. 다 들으시면 제 말에 동의하실 겁니다. 제가 그 사내를 어찌했는지 아실 겁니다. 저는 그를 목욕탕에 보내고, 머리를 깎고 옷을 입힌 다음, 요새의 문으로 데려가 자유로이 가라 했습니다. 그렇게 그에게서 손을 씻은 것이지요. 오늘 그가 돌아왔고, 제게로 끌려왔습니다. 손짓과 눈물로 그는 마침내 제게, 제 감방으로 돌아가고 싶다는 뜻을 알렸고, 저는 그리하라 명했습니다. 그를 데려가려는데, 그가 뿌리치고 제 발에 입을 맞추더니, 애처롭게 말 못 하는 간청으로 저더러 함께 가 달라 졸랐습니다. 그래서 저는 갔습니다. 세 사람의 수수께끼가 제 마음에 남아 있었거든요. 저는 그것이 못내 미심쩍었습니다. 이제 저는 그의 애원에 응한 것을 기쁘게 여깁니다.”\n\n이 대목에서 사람들 모두가 매우 조용해졌다.\n\n“우리가 다시 감방 안에 들었고, 죄수가 그것을 알아채자, 그는 제 손을 간절히 붙잡고 우리가 그에게 음식을 넣어 주곤 하던 그런 구멍으로 저를 이끌었습니다. 투구를 밀어 넣을 만큼 컸는데도, 어제는 제 눈을 비껴갔습니다. 여전히 제 손을 붙든 채, 그는 얼굴을 구멍에 대고 짐승 같은 소리를 질렀습니다. 희미하게 무슨 소리가 되돌아왔습니다. 저는 놀라 그를 떼어 놓고 외쳤습니다. ‘여보시오, 거기!’ 처음엔 아무 대답이 없었습니다. 다시 외치자, 이런 말이 돌아왔습니다. ‘주여, 찬양받으소서!’ 더욱 놀라운 것은, 오 호민관님, 그 목소리가 여인의 것이었다는 사실입니다. 제가 물었습니다. ‘당신은 누구요?’ 그러자 이런 대답이 돌아왔습니다. ‘이스라엘의 한 여인으로, 딸과 함께 이곳에 산 채로 묻혀 있습니다. 어서 도와주십시오, 그러지 않으면 우리는 죽습니다.’ 저는 그들에게 기운을 내라 이르고, 호민관님의 뜻을 알고자 이리로 서둘러 왔습니다.”\n\n호민관이 황급히 일어섰다.\n\n“자네가 옳았네, 게시우스.” 그가 말했다. “이제 알겠군. 그 지도는 거짓이었고, 세 사람 이야기도 그러했네. 발레리우스 그라투스보다 나은 로마인들이 있었던 것이지.”\n\n“그렇습니다.” 감옥지기가 말했다. “저는 그 죄수에게서, 그가 받은 먹을 것과 마실 것을 그 여인들에게 꼬박꼬박 나누어 주었다는 것을 알아냈습니다.”\n\n“이제 설명이 되는군.” 호민관이 대답했다. 그리고 친구들의 얼굴을 살피며, 증인을 두는 것이 얼마나 좋을지 헤아리고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. “그 여인들을 구해 내세. 모두 따라오게.”\n\n게시우스는 흡족해했다.\n\n“벽을 뚫어야 할 것입니다.” 그가 말했다. “문이 있던 자리를 찾았는데, 돌과 모르타르로 빈틈없이 메워져 있었습니다.”\n\n호민관은 머무르며 한 서기에게 일렀다. “일꾼들을 연장과 함께 내 뒤로 보내게. 서둘러라. 그러나 보고서는 붙들어 두게. 보아하니 바로잡아야 할 듯하니.”\n\n얼마 지나지 않아 그들은 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“이스라엘의 한 여인이 딸과 함께 이곳에 갇혀 있습니다. 어서 도와주십시오. 그러지 않으면 우리는 죽습니다.”\n\n간수 게시우스가 새로 고쳐 그린 지도에 6번으로 표시된 감방에서 들려온 대답이 바로 이것이었다. 독자는 이 대답을 보는 순간 그 불행한 이들이 누구인지 알아차렸을 것이며, 틀림없이 속으로 이렇게 중얼거렸을 것이다. “마침내 벤허의 어머니와 누이동생 티르자로구나!”\n\n과연 그러했다.\n\n여드레 해 전, 그들이 붙잡힌 그날 아침에 두 사람은 탑으로 끌려갔고, 그라투스는 그곳에서 그들을 영영 없애 버릴 작정이었다. 그가 이 일을 위해 탑을 고른 것은 그곳이 자기 손아귀 안에 가장 가까이 있었기 때문이며, 6번 감방을 고른 것은 첫째로 그곳이 다른 어떤 곳보다 잊히기 쉬웠기 때문이고, 둘째로 그곳이 나병에 오염되어 있었기 때문이다. 이 죄수들은 그저 안전한 곳에 가두기 위함이 아니라, 죽으라고 가두는 것이었으니 말이다. 그리하여 두 사람은 그 일을 목격할 사람이 아무도 없는 한밤중에 노예들의 손에 끌려 내려갔다. 그 야만스러운 일을 마무리하기 위해 그 노예들은 문을 벽돌로 막아 버렸고, 그러고 나서 그들 자신은 뿔뿔이 갈라져 멀리 보내져 다시는 소식이 들리지 않았다. 고발을 면하기 위해, 그리고 만에 하나 발각될 경우 형벌을 내린 것과 두 사람을 죽인 것 사이의 구별 속에서 자신에게 허락될 만한 정당화의 구실을 남겨 두기 위해, 그라투스는 비록 더디기는 하나 자연사가 확실한 곳에 희생자들을 가라앉히는 편을 택했다. 그들이 더 오래 목숨을 부지하도록, 그는 눈이 멀고 혀가 잘린 죄수 하나를 골라 그들과 통하는 단 하나의 감방에 가두어, 그가 그들에게 먹을 것과 마실 것을 가져다주게 했다. 그 불쌍한 자는 어떤 경우에도 이 일을 발설하거나, 죄수들이 누구인지 또 누가 그들을 죽음에 몰아넣었는지 알릴 수가 없었다. 이렇게 하여, 얼마쯤은 로마인 메살라의 머리에서 나온 술책으로, 한 무리의 자객을 처벌한다는 구실 아래, 후르 가문의 재산을 몰수하는 길이 매끄럽게 닦였으니, 그 재산 가운데 단 한 몫도 황실 금고에 들어간 일이 없었다.\n\n계획의 마지막 단계로, 그라투스는 옛 간수를 별안간 자리에서 내쫓았다. 그가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 알았기 때문은 아니었으니—그는 알지 못했다—다만 지하층을 그토록 훤히 꿰고 있는 그에게 이 거래를 숨기기란 거의 불가능했기 때문이다. 그런 다음, 그 충독은 능란한 솜씨로 새 간수에게 넘겨줄 새 지도를 그리게 하면서, 우리가 이미 보았듯이 6번 감방을 빠뜨렸다. 새 간수에게 내려진 지시와 지도에서의 누락이 한데 어우러져 그 계략은 이루어졌으니—감방도 그 가엾은 거주자들도 모조리 똑같이 잊혀 버린 것이다.\n\n여드레 해 동안 그 어머니와 딸의 삶이 어떠했는지는 그들의 교양과 지난날의 습관에 비추어 생각해야 한다. 우리에게 어떤 처지가 즐거운가 괴로운가는 우리의 감수성에 달려 있다. 만일 세상에서 모든 사람이 별안간 떠나간다면, 그리스도교의 관념 속에 그려진 천국이라 한들 대다수에게는 천국이 아닐 것이며, 다른 한편으로 이른바 토페트에서도 누구나 똑같이 고통받지는 않으리라고 말한다 해서 그것이 지나친 말은 아니다. 교양에는 그 나름의 저울이 있다. 정신이 총명해질수록 영혼이 순수한 기쁨을 누리는 능력도 그에 비례하여 커진다. 그러니 그것이 구원받는다면 얼마나 좋은 일인가! 그러나 잃어버린다면, 아아, 교양을 지녔던 것이 오히려 한이 되리라! 한쪽 경우에 누리는 능력이 곧 다른 쪽 경우에 고통받는 능력의 척도가 되는 까닭이다. 그러므로 회개란 죄에 대한 단순한 뉘우침 이상의 것이어야 하니, 그것은 천국에 어울리는 본성의 변화를 아우르는 것이다.\n\n다시 한번 말하건대, 벤허의 어머니가 견뎌 낸 고통을 제대로 그려 보려면, 독자는 갇혀 있던 처지의 조건 못지않게, 아니 그보다 더, 그 영혼과 그 감수성을 헤아려야 한다. 문제는 그 조건이 어떠했는가가 아니라, 그것이 그녀에게 어떻게 작용했는가에 있기 때문이다. 이제 우리는, 가문의 궁전 옥상 정자에서 벌어진 장면을 우리 이야기 둘째 권 첫머리에서 그토록 자세히 그려 둔 것이 바로 이 생각을 미리 내다보았기 때문이라고 밝혀도 좋을 것이다. 마찬가지로, 이런 물음이 떠오를 때 도움이 되도록, 우리는 후르 가문의 궁전을 공들여 묘사하는 일도 마다하지 않았다.\n\n다시 말해, 그 고귀한 집에서 누리던 평온하고 행복하며 호사스러운 삶을 떠올려, 안토니아 탑의 아래쪽 토굴에서 영위하는 이 생존과 견주어 보라. 그런 다음 독자가 그 여인의 비참함을 실감하려는 노력 속에서 한낱 물리적 조건만을 끝까지 들먹인다 해도 그것이 그릇된 일은 아니다. 그가 동족을 사랑하는 이요 마음이 여린 이라면 깊은 연민에 가슴이 녹아내릴 것이다. 그러나 그가 거기서 더 나아가려 한다면, 그녀에게 연민을 느끼는 것 이상을 하려 한다면, 그녀의 마음과 영혼의 고뇌를 함께 나누려 한다면, 적어도 그것을 가늠해 보려 한다면—그녀가 아들에게 하느님과 민족들과 영웅들에 관해 들려주던 모습을, 한순간은 철학자였다가 다음 순간은 교사였으며 내내 어머니였던 그 모습을 떠올리게 하라.\n\n남자에게 가장 날카로운 상처를 입히려거든 그의 자존심을 치라. 여자에게 가장 모진 상처를 입히려거든 그녀의 정을 겨누라.\n\n이 불행한 이들에 대한 기억을 새로이 떠올렸으니—그들이 예전에 어떠했는지를 떠올렸으니—이제 아래로 내려가 그들이 지금 어떠한지를 보기로 하자.\n\n6번 감방은 게시우스가 지도에 그린 그대로의 모양이었다. 그 크기는 짐작하기 어려우나, 거칠고 널찍한 내부에 턱이 지고 부서진 벽과 바닥이 있었다는 것으로 족하다.\n\n애초에 마케도니아 성채가 들어선 자리는 성전이 들어선 자리와 좁지만 깊은 절벽으로 나뉘어 있었으니, 그 모양은 다소 쐐기를 닮아 있었다. 일꾼들은 여러 개의 방을 깎아 내고자 그 갈라진 틈의 북쪽 면에 입구를 내어 안쪽으로 파 들어가면서, 천장은 자연석 그대로 남겨 두었다. 더 깊이 파 들어가 5번, 4번, 3번, 2번, 1번 감방을 만들었는데, 이것들은 5번을 거치지 않고서는 6번과 통하는 길이 없었다. 같은 방식으로 그들은 위층으로 이어지는 통로와 계단을 만들었다. 이 작업의 과정은 예루살렘 북쪽으로 조금 떨어진 곳에서 지금도 볼 수 있는 왕들의 무덤을 깎아 내던 그 방식과 똑같았다. 다만 깎는 일이 끝났을 때 6번 감방은 그 바깥쪽이 엄청나게 큰 돌들로 쌓은 벽으로 둘러막혔는데, 그 벽에는 환기를 위해 오늘날의 포안처럼 비스듬히 깎인 좁은 틈이 남겨졌다. 헤롯은 성전과 탑에 손을 댈 적에 이 바깥벽 위에 한층 더 육중한 외장을 입히고, 그 틈을 모두 막아 버리되 단 하나만 남겨 두었으니, 그 틈으로는 그래도 목숨을 부지할 만한 공기와, 그 방을 어둠에서 건져 내기에는 어림없이 약한 한 줄기 빛이 들어왔다.\n\n이것이 6번 감방이었다.\n\n지금 너무 놀라지는 말라!\n\n5번 감방에서 막 풀려난 그 눈멀고 혀 잘린 가엾은 자에 대한 묘사를, 이제 닥쳐올 끔찍한 광경의 충격을 누그러뜨리는 데 받아들여도 좋으리라.\n\n두 여인은 그 틈 가까이에 함께 모여 있다. 하나는 앉아 있고, 다른 하나는 그녀에게 반쯤 기대어 누워 있으며, 그들과 맨바위 사이에는 아무것도 없다. 비스듬히 위로 비쳐 드는 빛이 그들을 소름 끼치게 비추는데, 우리는 그들에게 옷도 덮을 것도 없다는 사실을 보지 않을 수 없다. 동시에 우리는 그곳에 아직 사랑이 있음을 알게 되니, 두 사람이 서로를 끌어안고 있기 때문이다. 재물은 날개 돋쳐 날아가고 안락은 사라지며 희망은 시들어 가지만, 사랑은 우리 곁에 머문다. 사랑은 하느님이다.\n\n두 사람이 이렇게 모여 있는 자리의 돌바닥은 반들반들 윤이 나도록 닳아 있다. 그 틈에서 들어오는 수줍지만 정겨운 빛에 기대어 구조의 희망을 키우며, 여드레 해 가운데 얼마를 그 틈 앞의 그 자리에서 보냈는지 누가 말할 수 있으랴? 환한 빛이 슬며시 기어들면 그들은 동이 텄음을 알았고, 그 빛이 스러지기 시작하면 세상이 밤을 맞아 잠잠해짐을 알았으니, 그 밤은 어디에서도 그들에게만큼 그토록 길고 그토록 완전히 어둡지는 않았으리라. 세상이여! 마치 그 틈이 임금의 성문처럼 넓고 높기라도 한 듯, 그들은 그 틈을 지나 마음으로 세상에 나아가, 영혼이 떠돌듯 오르내리며 그 고단한 시간을 보냈으니, 보고 또 물으며, 한 사람은 제 아들을, 다른 한 사람은 제 오빠를 찾았다. 바다 위에서도 그를 찾았고 바다의 섬들에서도 그를 찾았으며, 오늘은 그가 이 도시에, 내일은 저 다른 도시에 있었으니, 어디에서나 또 언제나 그는 잠깐 머물다 떠나는 나그네였다. 그들이 그를 기다리며 살았듯이, 그도 그들을 찾으며 살았기 때문이다. 그 끝없는 찾음 속에서 그들의 생각이 얼마나 자주 엇갈렸던가—그는 오고 그들은 가니! “그가 살아 있는 한 우리는 잊히지 않으리라. 그가 우리를 기억하는 한 희망이 있으리라!” 하고 서로에게 말하는 것은 그들에게 얼마나 달콤한 위안이었던가! 시련을 겪어 보기 전에야, 사람이 그토록 보잘것없는 것에서 얼마만큼의 힘을 짜낼 수 있는지 누가 알겠는가?\n\n지난날의 그들에 대한 우리의 기억은 우리에게 경의를 갖추라 명한다. 그들의 슬픔이 그들에게 거룩함을 입히고 있다. 토굴을 가로질러 너무 가까이 다가가지 않더라도, 우리는 그들이 세월이나 오랜 감금만으로는 설명할 수 없는 외모의 변화를 겪었음을 알 수 있다. 어머니는 여인으로서 아름다웠고 딸은 아이로서 아름다웠으나, 이제는 사랑조차도 그렇게 말할 수 없다. 그들의 머리카락은 길고 헝클어졌으며 기이하게도 새하얗다. 그 모습은 우리를 형언할 수 없는 혐오로 움츠리고 떨게 하나, 그 느낌은 어쩌면 병든 어둠을 음산하게 가물거리며 비추는 빛이 만들어 낸 착시 탓일지도 모른다. 또 어쩌면 그들은 굶주림과 목마름의 고통을 견디고 있는지도 모르니, 그들을 섬기던 죄수가 끌려간 뒤로—다시 말해 어제부터—먹지도 마시지도 못했기 때문이다.\n\n티르자는 어머니에게 반쯤 안긴 채 기대어 가엾게 신음했다.\n\n“조용히 하렴, 티르자. 그들이 올 거야. 하느님은 선하시단다. 우리는 그분을 잊지 않았고, 성전에서 나팔이 울릴 때마다 기도하기를 잊지 않았어. 보렴, 빛이 아직 환하구나. 해가 아직 남쪽 하늘에 떠 있으니, 일곱째 시가 겨우 지났을 뿐이야. 누군가 우리에게 올 거야. 믿음을 가지자. 하느님은 선하시단다.”\n\n이렇게 어머니가 말했다. 그 말은 소박하면서도 힘이 있었다. 우리가 마지막으로 그녀를 보았을 때 열세 살이던 티르자에게 이제 여덟 해를 더해야 했으니, 그녀는 더 이상 아이가 아니었다.\n\n“힘을 내 볼게요, 어머니.” 그녀가 말했다. “어머니의 고통도 제 고통만큼이나 크실 텐데, 저는 어머니와 오빠를 위해 살고 싶어요. 그런데 혀가 타들어 가고 입술이 바싹 마르네요. 오빠가 어디에 있을까, 그리고 언젠가, 정말 언젠가 우리를 찾아낼 수 있을까 궁금해요!”\n\n그 목소리에는 우리를 묘하게 사로잡는 무엇이 있다—뜻밖의 음색, 날카롭고 메마르며 쇠붙이 같고 부자연스러운 음색이다.\n\n어머니는 딸을 제 가슴으로 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. “어젯밤에 그 애 꿈을 꾸었단다. 티르자, 지금 너를 보는 것만큼이나 또렷이 그 애를 보았어. 우리는 꿈을 믿어야 한단다, 알지? 우리 조상들이 그러셨으니까. 주님께서는 그렇게 자주 그들에게 말씀하셨지. 우리가 아름다운 문 바로 앞 여인의 뜰에 있는 것 같았어. 우리와 함께 많은 여인들이 있었지. 그러자 그 애가 와서 그 문 그늘에 서서, 이쪽저쪽을, 이 사람 저 사람을 둘러보더구나. 내 가슴이 세차게 뛰었어. 그 애가 우리를 찾고 있다는 걸 알았으니까. 나는 그 애에게 두 팔을 뻗고, 그 애를 부르며 달려갔지. 그 애가 내 소리를 듣고 나를 보았지만, 나를 알아보지 못하더구나. 한순간에 그 애는 사라져 버렸어.”\n\n“정말로 그 애를 만난다 해도 그렇지 않을까요, 어머니? 우리는 너무나 변해 버렸잖아요.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지. 하지만—” 어머니의 머리가 수그러지고 그 얼굴이 고통에 비틀리듯 일그러졌다. 그러나 곧 추스르고는 말을 이었다. “하지만 우리가 우리 자신을 그 애에게 알릴 수는 있을 거야.”\n\n티르자는 두 팔을 내저으며 다시 신음했다.\n\n“물을, 어머니, 물을, 단 한 방울이라도.”\n\n어머니는 망연한 무력감에 사로잡혀 사방을 둘러본다. 그녀는 하느님의 이름을 너무도 자주 불렀고 그 이름으로 너무도 자주 약속했기에, 그 되풀이가 이제 그녀 자신에게 조롱처럼 느껴지기 시작한다. 그림자 하나가 그 어슴푸레한 빛을 더욱 흐리며 그녀 앞을 스쳐 지나가니, 그녀는 죽음이 아주 가까이 와서, 그녀의 믿음이 꺼져 갈 적에 들어오려고 기다리고 있음을 생각하게 된다. 자신이 무엇을 하는지도 거의 알지 못한 채, 그저 말을 해야 하기에 두서없이 그녀는 다시 말한다.\n\n“참으렴, 티르자. 그들이 오고 있어—거의 다 왔단다.”\n\n칸막이벽에 난 작은 쪽문—그들이 세상과 실제로 통하는 모든 일을 그것을 통해 해 온—쪽에서 무슨 소리가 들린 것 같았다. 그리고 그녀의 생각은 틀리지 않았다. 한순간, 그 죄수의 외침이 감방을 울렸다. 티르자도 그 소리를 들었고, 두 사람은 여전히 서로를 붙든 채 일어섰다.\n\n“주님께 영원토록 찬미를!” 어머니가 되살아난 믿음과 희망의 열렬함으로 외쳤다.\n\n“여보시오, 거기!” 다음에 그들이 들은 것은 이것이었고, 이어서, “당신들은 누구요?”\n\n낯선 목소리였다. 그것이 무슨 상관이랴? 티르자를 빼고는, 그것은 여드레 해 동안 어머니가 들은 첫 번째이자 유일한 말이었다. 그 격변은 엄청났으니—죽음에서 삶으로, 그것도 그토록 순식간에!\n\n“이스라엘의 한 여인이 딸과 함께 이곳에 갇혀 있습니다. 어서 도와주십시오. 그러지 않으면 우리는 죽습니다.”\n\n“기운을 내시오. 내 다시 돌아오리다.”\n\n여인들은 소리 내어 흐느꼈다. 그들은 발견되었고, 도움이 오고 있었다. 바람에서 바람으로, 희망은 지저귀는 제비처럼 날아다녔다. 그들은 발견되었으니, 풀려나리라. 그리고 회복이 뒤따르리라—그들이 잃은 모든 것의 회복, 집과 사회와 재산과 아들과 오빠의 회복이! 그 보잘것없는 빛이 그들을 대낮의 영광으로 입혔으니, 고통과 목마름과 굶주림과 죽음의 위협마저 잊은 채, 그들은 내내 서로를 꼭 붙든 채로 바닥에 주저앉아 울었다.\n\n그리고 이번에는 오래 기다릴 것도 없었다. 간수 게시우스가 차근차근 자기 이야기를 늘어놓았으나, 마침내 그것을 끝냈다. 그 호민관은 지체하지 않았다.\n\n“거기 안에 있는 이들이여!” 그가 쪽문 너머로 외쳤다.\n\n“여기요!” 어머니가 일어서며 말했다.\n\n곧이어 그녀는 다른 곳에서 또 다른 소리를 들었으니, 마치 벽을 두드리는 소리 같았다—재빠르고 쟁쟁하며 쇠 연장으로 내리치는 타격 소리였다. 그녀도 티르자도 말하지 않았으나, 둘은 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 잘 알기에 귀를 기울였다—자유로 이어지는 길이 그들을 위해 뚫리고 있다는 것을. 깊은 광산에 오래 묻혀 있던 사람들이 쇠지레를 찔러 넣고 곡괭이를 내리치는 소리로 미리 알려지는 구조자들의 다가옴을 듣고, 그 소리가 나는 자리에 눈을 못 박은 채 심장의 고동으로 고마이 화답하는 것처럼, 그리고 작업이 멈추어 다시 절망 속으로 돌려보내질까 봐 차마 눈을 돌리지 못하는 것처럼, 그들도 그러했다.\n\n바깥의 팔들은 억세었고, 손들은 능란했으며, 의지는 굳건했다. 매 순간 그 타격 소리가 더욱 또렷이 울려왔다. 이따금 돌 한 조각이 우지끈 떨어졌고, 자유는 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다. 이윽고 일꾼들이 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 그러자—오, 행복이여!—한 틈새로 횃불의 붉은 빛줄기가 번뜩였다. 그것은 다이아몬드의 광채처럼 예리하게, 아침의 창에서 쏟아진 듯 아름답게 어둠을 갈랐다.\n\n“그예요, 어머니, 그예요! 마침내 우리를 찾아냈어요!” 티르자가 젊음 특유의 들뜬 상상으로 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 어머니는 온순하게 대답했다. “하느님은 선하시단다!”\n\n안쪽에서 돌덩이 하나가 떨어지고, 또 하나가 떨어지더니—이윽고 커다란 덩어리가 떨어지고, 문이 열렸다. 회반죽과 돌가루를 뒤집어쓴 사내 하나가 안으로 들어서서, 머리 위로 횃불을 치켜든 채 걸음을 멈추었다. 두엇이 횃불을 들고 뒤따라 들어와, 그 호민관이 들어올 수 있도록 옆으로 비켜섰다.\n\n여인에 대한 존중은 한낱 관습만은 아니니, 그것은 그들의 올바른 본성을 보여 주는 가장 좋은 증거이기 때문이다. 그 호민관이 걸음을 멈춘 것은, 그들이 그에게서 달아났기 때문이다—말해 두건대, 두려움 탓이 아니라 부끄러움 탓이었다. 아니, 독자여, 부끄러움 탓만도 아니었다! 어슴푸레 반쯤 몸을 숨긴 그 자리에서 그는 이런 말을 들었으니, 사람의 혀가 내뱉을 수 있는 가장 슬프고, 가장 끔찍하며, 가장 철저히 절망적인 말이었다.\n\n“우리에게 가까이 오지 마세요—부정합니다, 부정합니다!”\n\n사내들은 서로를 멀거니 바라보면서 횃불을 흔들어 댔다.\n\n“부정합니다, 부정합니다!” 그 구석에서 다시금 그 소리가 들려왔으니, 더없이 슬픈, 느릿하고 떨리는 통곡이었다. 그런 외침과 함께라면, 우리는 한 영혼이 낙원의 문에서 사라지면서 내내 뒤를 돌아보는 모습을 떠올릴 수 있으리라.\n\n이렇게 하여 그 과부이자 어머니는 제 의무를 다했고, 그 순간 그녀가 기도하고 꿈꾸던 자유가, 멀리서 보이던 진홍빛과 황금빛의 열매가 손에 쥐고 보니 한낱 소돔의 사과에 지나지 않음을 깨달았다.\n\n# SHE AND TIRZAH WERE—LEPERS!\n\n그녀와 티르자는—나병 환자였던 것이다!\n아마도 독자는 그 말이 지닌 뜻을 다 알지 못할 것이다. 당시의 율법에 비추어, 오늘날 조금 달라졌을 뿐인 그 뜻을 일러 주기로 하자.\n\n“이 넷은 죽은 자로 여겨진다—눈먼 자, 나병 환자, 가난한 자, 자식 없는 자.” 탈무드에 이렇게 적혀 있다.\n\n다시 말해, 나병 환자가 된다는 것은 죽은 자처럼 다루어지는 것이었다—주검처럼 도시 밖으로 내쳐지는 것이요, 가장 사랑하고 가장 사랑하는 이들조차 오직 멀리서만 말을 건넬 수 있는 것이요, 나병 환자 외에는 그 누구와도 함께 살 수 없는 것이요, 모든 권리를 철저히 빼앗기는 것이요, 성전과 회당의 예식을 거부당하는 것이요, “부정합니다, 부정합니다!” 하고 외칠 때를 빼고는 찢어진 옷차림에 입을 가린 채 돌아다니는 것이요, 광야나 버려진 무덤에서 거처를 찾는 것이요, 힌놈과 게헨나의 형상화된 망령이 되는 것이요, 늘 남에게 살아 있는 모욕거리라기보다 자기 자신에게 숨 쉬는 고통거리가 되는 것이요, 죽음을 두려워하면서도 죽음 외에는 아무런 희망이 없는 것이었다.\n\n언젠가—그날도 그해도 그녀는 말할 수 없었으니, 그 귀신 들린 지옥 아래에서는 시간마저 잃어버렸기에—언젠가 어머니는 오른손 바닥에서 마른 딱지를 느꼈으니, 그것은 그녀가 씻어 내려 한 하찮은 것이었다. 그것은 그 손에 끈질기게 달라붙었으나, 그녀는 티르자도 똑같은 식으로 침범당했다고 호소할 때까지 그 징조를 대수롭지 않게 여겼다. 물은 모자랐으나, 그들은 그것을 치료에 쓰고자 마실 것을 스스로 삼갔다. 마침내 손 전체가 침범당했으니, 살갗이 갈라져 터지고 손톱이 살에서 떨어져 나갔다. 그렇다 해도 아픔은 그리 크지 않았으니, 주로 꾸준히 더해 가는 불편함뿐이었다. 그 뒤 그들의 입술이 바싹 마르고 갈라지기 시작했다. 어느 날, 청결함이 곧 경건함이라 여기며 온갖 슬기를 다해 토굴의 더러움과 맞서 싸우던 어머니가, 그 적이 티르자의 얼굴을 사로잡고 있다고 여겨 그녀를 빛 쪽으로 이끌고는, 끔찍한 두려움이 불어넣은 직감으로 들여다보니, 보라! 그 어린 소녀의 눈썹이 눈처럼 새하얗게 세어 있었다.\n\n오, 그 확인이 주는 고뇌여!\n\n어머니는 한동안 말도 없이 미동도 없이 앉아 있었으니, 영혼이 마비된 채 단 하나의 생각만을 떠올릴 수 있었다—나병이다, 나병이다!\n\n그녀가 생각하기 시작했을 때, 어머니답게도 그것은 자기 자신이 아니라 제 아이에 관한 것이었으며, 어머니답게도 그녀의 타고난 다정함은 용기로 바뀌어, 그녀는 완벽한 영웅적 자기희생의 마지막 제물을 바칠 채비를 했다. 그녀는 자신이 안 것을 가슴속에 묻었다. 스스로는 희망이 없으면서도 티르자에 대한 헌신을 곱절로 더했으며, 놀라운 슬기로—주로 그 끝없는 마르지 않음에서 놀라운 슬기로—그들에게 들이닥친 것이 무엇인지 딸이 알지 못하게, 나아가 그것이 아무것도 아니리라는 희망마저 품게 계속 지켜 주었다. 그녀는 자신의 자그마한 놀이를 되풀이하고, 이야기를 되풀이해 들려주고, 새 이야기를 지어내고, 티르자에게 청해 듣는 노래에 더없는 기쁨으로 귀를 기울였다. 한편 자신의 시들어 가는 입술 위에서는 노래하는 임금과 그들 겨레의 시편이 망각의 위안을 가져다주고, 그들 둘 안에 그들을 버린 듯한 하느님—세상도 그보다 더 가벼이 또 철저히 버리지는 못했을 그 하느님—에 대한 기억을 살아 있게 했다.\n\n천천히, 꾸준히, 끔찍한 확실함으로 그 병은 퍼져 나가, 얼마 뒤 그들의 머리를 새하얗게 표백하고, 입술과 눈꺼풀에 구멍을 파먹고, 그들의 몸을 비늘로 뒤덮었다. 그러고는 그들의 목구멍으로 내려가 목소리를 날카롭게 만들고, 관절로 내려가 조직과 연골을 굳게 만들었다—천천히, 그리고 어머니가 잘 알고 있었듯이 치유할 길 없이, 그것은 그들의 폐와 동맥과 뼈를 침범해, 한 걸음씩 나아갈 때마다 그 고통받는 이들을 더욱더 흉측하게 만들었다. 그리고 그것은 죽음에 이르기까지 그렇게 계속될 터였으니, 그 죽음은 여러 해 뒤에나 올지도 몰랐다.\n\n마침내 또 다른 두려운 날이 왔다—의무에 떠밀린 어머니가 끝내 티르자에게 그들 병의 이름을 일러 준 그날이었다. 그리고 두 사람은 절망의 고뇌 속에서, 그 끝이 어서 오기를 빌었다.\n\n그래도, 습관의 힘이란 그러하기에, 이토록 시달린 그들은 이윽고 제 병에 대해 차분히 말하게 되었을 뿐 아니라, 제 몸이 흉측하게 변해 가는 것을 당연한 일로 바라보면서도 그에 맞서 끈질기게 삶에 매달렸다. 그들에게는 이 땅과 잇는 끈 하나가 남아 있었다. 자신들의 외로움은 아랑곳하지 않고, 벤허에 관해 이야기하고 꿈꾸며 어떤 기개를 지켜 갔다. 어머니는 누이동생에게 그와의 재회를 약속했고, 누이동생은 어머니에게 그것을 약속했으니, 둘 다 그가 자기들에게 똑같이 충실하며 그 만남을 똑같이 기뻐하리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다. 그리고 이 가느다란 실을 잣고 또 다시 자으면서 그들은 기쁨을 찾았고, 죽지 않고 있는 것에 대한 핑계로 삼았다. 우리가 본 것과 같은 식으로, 게시우스가 그들을 불렀던 그 순간에도, 열두 시간의 단식과 갈증 끝에 그들은 그렇게 스스로를 위로하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n횃불이 토굴을 가로질러 붉게 번뜩였고, 자유가 다가왔다. “하느님은 선하시다.” 과부가 외쳤으니—독자여, 지나간 일 때문이 아니라 지금 있는 일 때문이었다. 지금의 자비에 감사함에 있어, 지난날의 불행을 잊어버리는 것만큼 우리에게 어울리는 일은 없다.\n\n그 호민관이 곧장 다가왔다. 그러자 그녀가 달아나 숨은 구석에서, 별안간 의무감이 그 나이 든 여인을 때렸으니, 곧바로 그 무시무시한 경고가 터져 나왔다—\n\n“부정합니다, 부정합니다!”\n\n아아, 그 의무를 다하려는 노력이 어머니에게 안긴 고통이여! 풀려나리라는 전망에 들뜬 그 어떤 이기적 기쁨도, 이제 그것이 손에 잡히려는 마당에 풀려남이 가져올 결과를 그녀가 보지 못하도록 눈멀게 할 수는 없었다. 옛날의 행복한 삶은 결코 다시 올 수 없었다. 그녀가 집이라 부르던 그 집 가까이 간다 해도, 그것은 대문 앞에 멈추어 서서 “부정합니다, 부정합니다!” 하고 외치기 위함이리라. 그녀는 가슴속에 사랑의 그리움을 여전히 변함없이 강하게, 아니 도리어 더 예민하게 살아 있는 채로 돌아다녀야 했으니, 같은 사랑으로 되갚음을 받을 수 없는 까닭이었다. 그녀가 그토록 끊임없이 생각해 온 아이, 어머니들이 가장 순수한 기쁨을 찾는 그 온갖 달콤한 약속들과 함께 생각해 온 그 아이는, 그녀를 만날 때 멀찍이 떨어져 서야만 했다. 그가 그녀에게 두 손을 내밀며 “어머니, 어머니” 하고 부른다 해도, 바로 그를 사랑하기에 그녀는 “부정합니다, 부정합니다!” 하고 대답해야 했다. 그리고 달리 덮을 것이 없어 부자연스럽게 새하얗게 센 그 길고 헝클어진 머리카락을 펼쳐 가려 주고 있는 이 다른 아이—아아! 그 아이도 그렇게 남아 있어야 했으니, 그녀의 망가진 남은 삶의 유일한 동반자였다. 그러나 독자여, 그 용감한 여인은 그 운명을 받아들였고, 까마득한 옛날부터 그 표시였으며 그때부터 변함없이 그녀의 인사가 될 그 외침을 입에 담았다—“부정합니다, 부정합니다!”\n\n그 호민관은 그 소리를 듣고 몸을 떨었으나, 제자리를 지켰다.\n\n“당신들은 누구요?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“굶주림과 목마름으로 죽어 가는 두 여인입니다. 하지만”—어머니는 머뭇거리지 않았다—“우리에게 가까이 오지 마시고, 바닥이나 벽도 만지지 마세요. 부정합니다, 부정합니다!”\n\n“당신의 이야기를 내게 들려주시오, 여인이여—당신의 이름과, 언제 이곳에 갇혔으며, 누구의 손에, 무슨 까닭으로 갇혔는지.”\n\n“언젠가 이 예루살렘 도시에 후르 가문의 한 영주가 있었으니, 그는 모든 너그러운 로마인의 벗이요 카이사르를 벗으로 둔 사람이었습니다. 저는 그의 과부요, 저와 함께 있는 이는 그의 아이입니다. 우리가 무슨 까닭으로 이곳에 가라앉혀졌는지 제가 어찌 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니까, 제 자신이 모르는데 말입니다. 다만 우리가 부유했던 탓이 아니라면요. 누가 우리의 원수였으며 우리의 갇힘이 언제 시작되었는지는 발레리우스 그라투스가 말해 줄 수 있을 것입니다. 저는 모릅니다. 우리가 어떤 꼴로 전락했는지 보십시오—오, 보시고, 가엾이 여겨 주십시오!”\n\n공기는 역병과 횃불 연기로 무거웠으나, 그 로마인은 횃불을 든 자 하나를 제 곁으로 불러, 그 대답을 거의 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 받아 적었다. 그것은 간결하면서도 두루 갖추어, 역사와 고발과 탄원을 한꺼번에 담고 있었다. 여느 평범한 사람이 할 수 있는 말이 아니었으니, 그는 가엾이 여기고 믿지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“구제를 받을 것이오, 여인이여.” 그가 서판을 덮으며 말했다. “당신에게 먹을 것과 마실 것을 보내 주겠소.”\n\n“그리고 옷과, 몸을 정결케 할 물도, 부디 베풀어 주소서, 오 너그러운 로마인이여!”\n\n“당신 뜻대로 하리다.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n“하느님은 선하시다.” 과부가 흐느끼며 말했다. “그분의 평안이 당신과 함께 머물기를!”\n\n“그리고, 한 가지 더,” 그가 덧붙였다. “나는 당신을 다시 볼 수 없소. 채비를 하시오. 오늘 밤 당신을 탑의 문까지 데려가 풀어 주게 하리다. 당신은 율법을 알고 있을 것이오. 잘 있으시오.”\n\n그는 사내들에게 말을 건네고는 문밖으로 나갔다.\n\n머잖아 노예 몇이 감방으로 와서, 물이 가득 든 커다란 항아리와 대야와 수건, 빵과 고기를 담은 접시, 그리고 여인이 입을 옷가지를 가져왔다. 그것들을 죄수들의 손이 닿는 곳에 내려놓고는 그들은 달아났다.\n\n첫 번째 경(更)의 한복판쯤에, 두 사람은 그 문까지 안내되어 거리로 내보내졌다. 이렇게 하여 그 로마인은 그들에게서 손을 떼었고, 조상들의 도시에서 그들은 다시 한번 자유로운 몸이 되었다.\n\n그들은 예전처럼 즐거이 반짝이는 별들을 우러러보았다. 그러고는 스스로에게 물었다.\n\n“이제 어떻게 하나? 어디로 가야 하나?”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n「안토니아 탑」에서 간수 게시우스가 호민관 앞에 모습을 드러낸 그 무렵, 한 도보 여행자가 올리브 산의 동쪽 비탈을 오르고 있었다. 길은 험하고 먼지투성이였으며, 그쪽 비탈의 초목은 누렇게 타들어 가 있었으니, 유대는 건기였던 것이다. 그 나그네에게는 젊음과 힘이 있었으니 다행이었고, 게다가 시원하게 흘러내리는 옷을 걸치고 있었음은 두말할 나위도 없다.\n\n그는 천천히 나아가며 좌우를 자주 둘러보았다. 길을 확신하지 못한 채 앞으로 가는 사람에게서 드러나는 짜증스럽고 불안한 표정이 아니라, 오랜 이별 끝에 옛 친구에게 다가가는 사람의 분위기였다. 절반은 기쁨이요, 절반은 캐묻는 마음이었으니, 마치 \"다시 너와 함께하게 되어 기쁘다, 네가 어떻게 변했는지 어디 보자\" 하고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n더 높이 올라가면서 그는 이따금 걸음을 멈추고 뒤를 돌아보았으니, 점점 넓어지다 마침내 모압의 산들에서 끝나는 풍경을 바라보았다. 그러나 마침내 정상 가까이에 이르자 그는 피로도 잊은 채 걸음을 재촉했고, 멈추지도 얼굴을 돌리지도 않은 채 서둘러 나아갔다. 정상에서는—그곳에 닿기 위해 그는 다져진 길에서 다소 오른쪽으로 발걸음을 틀었다—마치 억센 손에 붙들리기라도 한 듯 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그러자 그의 눈동자가 커지고, 두 뺨이 붉어지며, 숨결이 빨라지는 것을 누군가는 보았을 법했으니, 이 모두가 앞에 펼쳐진 광경을 한 번에 환히 훑어본 데서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n선량한 독자여, 그 나그네는 다름 아닌 벤허였다. 그 광경은 예루살렘이었다.\n\n오늘날의 거룩한 도시가 아니라, 헤롯이 남겨 놓은 거룩한 도시, 곧 그리스도의 거룩한 도시였다. 오래된 올리브 산에서 바라본 지금도 아름답거늘, 그때는 과연 어떠했겠는가?\n\n벤허는 바위 하나로 다가가 앉았다. 그러고는 머리를 덮어 주던 흰 천을 벗고, 느긋하게 그 광경을 살폈다.\n\n그 뒤로 이와 똑같은 일을 더없이 기이한 상황 속에서 갖가지 사람들이 자주 행했다. 베스파시아누스의 아들이, 이슬람교도가, 십자군이 그러했으니, 모두가 정복자였다. 우리 이야기의 시대로부터 천오백 년 가까이 지나서야 발견될 그 위대한 신세계에서 온 수많은 순례자들도 그러했다. 그러나 그 무리 가운데 벤허보다 더 사무치게 가슴 아프고, 더 슬프도록 달콤하며, 더 자랑스럽도록 쓰라린 감정으로 그 풍경을 바라본 이는 아마 하나도 없었을 것이다. 그는 제 동족의 추억에, 그들의 승리와 흥망에, 곧 하느님의 역사이기도 한 그들의 역사에 마음이 흔들렸다. 그 도시는 그들이 세운 것으로, 그들의 죄악과 헌신, 약함과 천재성, 신앙과 불신앙을 한꺼번에 길이 증언하는 곳이었다. 그는 로마를 익숙해질 만큼 보아 왔건만, 그래도 흐뭇했다. 그 광경은 그를 자긍심으로 가득 채워, 헛된 영광에 취하게 만들었을 법도 했다. 다만 한 가지 생각이 그를 붙들었으니, 그토록 왕가에 어울리는 재산이건만 그것이 더는 제 동족의 것이 아니라는 사실이었다. 성전에서 드리는 예배는 이방인의 허락에 따른 것이요, 다윗이 살던 언덕은 대리석으로 꾸민 기만이었으니, 그곳은 주께서 택하신 백성이 세금 때문에 쥐어짜이고 또 쥐어짜이며, 죽음으로도 꺾이지 않는 신앙 때문에 채찍질당하는 관청이 되어 있었다. 그러나 이런 것들은 그 시대 모든 유대인이 함께 느끼던 애국의 기쁨이요 슬픔이었다. 거기에 더하여 벤허는 그 무엇으로도 마음에서 지울 수 없는 개인의 사연을 품고 있었으니, 그 광경은 다만 그 사연을 더 새롭고 생생하게 되살려 줄 뿐이었다.\n\n언덕의 고장은 거의 변하지 않는다. 언덕이 바위로 이루어진 곳이라면 조금도 변하지 않는다. 벤허가 바라본 풍경은 도시를 빼면 지금도 그대로다. 무너진 것은 오직 사람의 솜씨뿐이다.\n\n해는 올리브 산의 동쪽보다 서쪽을 더 다정하게 대해 주었고, 사람들 또한 분명 그쪽을 더 사랑했다. 산을 군데군데 덮은 포도나무와 듬성듬성 박힌 나무들, 주로 무화과나무와 늙은 야생 올리브나무는 그래도 푸른 편이었다. 그 푸르름은 메마른 케드론 골짜기의 바닥까지 뻗어 내려가, 보는 눈을 시원하게 했다. 그곳에서 올리브 산이 끝나고 모리아 산이 시작되었으니, 눈처럼 흰 절벽 같은 성벽이 우뚝 솟아 있었다. 솔로몬이 기초를 놓고 헤롯이 완성한 것이었다. 눈길은 그 성벽을 이루는 육중한 바위들을 한 켜 한 켜 짚으며 위로, 위로 기어올라 솔로몬 행각에 이르렀으니, 언덕이 주춧돌이라면 그 행각은 기념비의 받침대였다. 그곳에서 잠시 머물던 눈길은 다시 오르기 시작하여, 다음으로 이방인의 뜰로, 그다음으로 이스라엘인의 뜰로, 그다음으로 여인의 뜰로, 그다음으로 제사장의 뜰로 옮겨 갔으니, 저마다 흰 대리석 기둥이 늘어선 한 층이 되어, 계단 모양으로 뒤로 물러나며 차례차례 위로 솟아 있었다. 그 모두의 위에는 더없이 거룩하고 더없이 아름다우며, 그 비례가 장엄하고, 두드려 편 금으로 눈부신 으뜸가는 면류관이 얹혀 있었으니, 보라, 곧 천막이요, 성막이요, 지성소였다. 언약궤는 그곳에 없었으나 여호와는 그곳에 계셨다. 이스라엘의 모든 자손의 믿음 속에서 그분은 그곳에 친히 임재하신 분이었다. 성전으로 보나 기념비로 보나, 사람의 손으로 세운 것 가운데 그 더없이 빼어난 모습에 견줄 만한 것은 어디에도 없었다. 이제는 그 돌 하나도 다른 돌 위에 남아 있지 않다. 누가 그 건물을 다시 세우겠는가? 그 재건은 언제 시작되겠는가? 벤허가 섰던 그 자리에 서 본 순례자라면 누구나 이렇게 묻는다. 그러면서도 그 답이 하느님의 품속에 있음을 안다. 그분의 비밀은 그 잘 간직됨에서 더없이 놀랍다. 그리고 세 번째 물음이 따른다. 그토록 분명히 닥쳐온 그 멸망을 미리 일러 준 이는 과연 누구인가? 하느님이신가? 하느님의 사람인가? 아니면—그 물음은 우리가 답할 몫이라는 것으로 충분하다.\n\n그래도 벤허의 눈길은 위로, 위로 기어올라—성전 지붕을 넘어, 거룩한 추억에 바쳐진, 기름 부음 받은 왕들과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 시온 언덕에 이르렀다. 그는 알고 있었다. 치즈장수 골짜기가 모리아와 시온 사이로 깊이 파여 내려가 있고, 그 위로 크시스토스 다리가 걸쳐 있으며, 그 골짜기 깊은 곳에는 정원과 궁전이 있다는 것을. 그러나 그 모든 것을 넘어 그의 생각은 그의 시선과 더불어 왕가의 언덕 위에 모여 선 큰 무리로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 가야바의 집, 중앙 회당, 로마 총독 관저, 영원한 히피쿠스 탑, 그리고 슬프되 위풍당당한 기념탑 파사엘루스와 마리암네—이 모두가 멀리서 자줏빛으로 물들어 가는 가렙 언덕을 배경으로 또렷이 드러나 있었다. 그 가운데서 헤롯 궁전을 가려내었을 때, 그가 어찌 장차 오실 왕을 떠올리지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 그 자신이 몸 바쳐 섬기는 분, 그 길을 평탄케 하기로 떠맡은 분, 그 빈손을 채워 드리는 꿈을 꾸어 온 분이었다. 그리고 그의 상상은 앞으로 내달려, 새 왕이 자기 것을 되찾으러 와서 그것을 차지할 그날에 다다랐다—모리아와 그 성전을, 시온과 그 망루와 궁전들을, 성전 바로 오른편에서 음울하게 노려보고 있는 안토니아를, 성벽 없는 새 도시 베제다를, 그리고 종려 가지와 깃발을 들고 모여들어 주께서 정복하시고 그들에게 세상을 주셨다며 기뻐 노래할 수백만 이스라엘 백성을 차지할 그날이었다.\n\n사람들은 꿈꾸는 일을 마치 밤과 잠의 현상인 양 말한다. 그들은 더 잘 알아야 한다. 우리가 이룬 온갖 성과는 스스로에게 한 약속에서 비롯되며, 그 모든 자기 약속은 깨어 있는 채 꾸는 꿈속에서 맺어진다. 꿈은 노고의 위안이요, 행동 속에서 우리를 떠받치는 포도주다. 우리는 노동을 사랑하기를 배우되, 노동 그 자체 때문이 아니라 그것이 베풀어 주는 꿈꿀 기회 때문에 사랑한다. 그 꿈이야말로 실제 삶의 밑바닥을 흐르는 거대한 저음이니, 한결같기에 들리지도 않고 알아차려지지도 않는다. 산다는 것은 꿈꾸는 것이다. 오직 무덤 속에만 꿈이 없다. 그러므로 벤허가 그런 꿈을 꾸었다고 누구도 비웃지 말라. 같은 시간 같은 장소 같은 처지였다면 비웃는 자 자신도 그리했을 것이다.\n\n해는 제 길을 따라 낮게 기울었다. 한동안 활활 타오르는 둥근 해는 서쪽 산의 먼 봉우리에 걸터앉은 듯 보였으니, 도시 위의 온 하늘을 놋빛으로 물들이고, 성벽과 망루를 금빛으로 둘러쳤다. 그러더니 곤두박질치듯 사라져 버렸다. 그 고요함이 벤허의 생각을 집으로 돌려놓았다. 지성소의 비할 데 없는 정면에서 조금 북쪽 하늘에 한 점이 있었으니, 그는 그곳에 시선을 박았다. 그 아래로, 다림줄을 곧장 떨어뜨린 듯 그 자리에, 아버지의 집이 있었다. 그 집이 아직 남아 있다면 말이다.\n\n저녁의 부드러운 기운이 그의 감정을 누그러뜨렸다. 그는 야망을 한쪽으로 밀어 두고, 자신을 예루살렘으로 이끈 의무를 생각했다.\n\n사막 한가운데서 일데림과 함께 있을 때, 마치 군인이 작전을 펼칠 고장을 살피듯 그는 요충지를 찾고 그 일대를 두루 익히고 있었다. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 한 전령이 와서 그라투스가 물러나고 그 자리에 본디오 빌라도가 부임했다는 소식을 전했다.\n\n메살라는 불구가 되었으니 그는 메살라가 죽은 줄로 여겼고, 그라투스는 힘을 잃고 떠났다. 그러니 벤허가 어머니와 누이를 찾는 일을 더 미룰 까닭이 무엇이겠는가? 이제는 두려워할 것이 없었다. 그 자신이 유대의 감옥들을 직접 들여다볼 수 없다면, 다른 이들의 눈으로 살필 수 있었다. 잃어버린 이들을 찾아낸다면, 빌라도가 그들을 가두어 둘 동기는 없을 것이었다. 적어도 돈으로 풀지 못할 동기는 없었다. 찾아내기만 하면 안전한 곳으로 데려갈 것이요, 그런 다음에는 더 평온한 마음으로, 양심의 짐을 내려놓고, 이 첫 번째 의무를 다한 뒤에, 장차 오실 왕께 자신을 더 온전히 바칠 수 있을 것이었다. 그는 곧장 마음을 정했다. 그날 밤 그는 일데림과 의논하여 그의 동의를 얻었다. 아랍인 셋이 그와 함께 여리고까지 왔고, 그는 그곳에 그들과 말들을 남겨 둔 채 홀로 걸어서 길을 떠났다. 말루크가 예루살렘에서 그를 만나기로 되어 있었다.\n\n여기서 밝혀 둘 것은, 벤허의 계획이 아직은 막연한 윤곽에 지나지 않았다는 점이다.\n\n앞날을 생각하면, 당국의 눈, 특히 로마인의 눈을 피해 몸을 숨기는 것이 마땅했다. 말루크는 영민하고 믿음직했으니, 바로 이 조사를 맡기기에 알맞은 사람이었다.\n\n어디서 시작할 것인가가 첫 번째 문제였다. 그는 그 점에 대해 뚜렷한 생각이 없었다. 그의 바람은 안토니아 탑에서부터 시작하는 것이었다. 그리 오래되지 않은 전승에 따르면, 그 음침한 건물 더미는 미궁 같은 감방들 위에 세워져 있었으니, 그 감방들은 강력한 수비대보다도 더 유대인의 상상 속에 공포로 자리 잡고 있었다. 그의 가족이 당했던 것과 같은 생매장이 그곳에서라면 있을 법한 일이었다. 게다가 그런 막막한 처지에서는 잃어버린 그 자리에서 수색을 시작하는 것이 자연스러운 마음이었으니, 그는 사랑하는 이들을 마지막으로 본 모습을 잊을 수 없었다. 경비병들이 그들을 탑 쪽 거리로 떠밀고 가던 광경이었다. 만약 그들이 지금은 그곳에 없더라도 한때 있었다면, 그 사실의 어떤 기록은 남아 있을 터이니, 충실히 끝까지 따라가기만 하면 될 단서였다.\n\n더욱이 이 마음 밑바닥에는 그가 차마 버릴 수 없는 희망 하나가 있었다. 시모니데스에게서 그는 이집트인 유모 암라가 살아 있다는 것을 알았다. 의심할 바 없이 기억하겠지만, 그 충직한 여인은 허 가문에 재앙이 닥치던 그날 아침 경비병에게서 벗어나 궁전 안으로 도로 뛰어 들어갔고, 그곳에서 다른 가재도구들과 함께 봉인되어 갇혔다. 그 뒤 여러 해 동안 시모니데스가 그녀에게 필요한 것을 대 주었으니, 그래서 그녀는 지금도 그곳에 있었다. 그라투스가 온갖 제안을 내놓고도 끝내 팔지 못한 그 큰 집의 유일한 거주자였다. 그 정당한 주인들의 사연이 알려진 탓에, 매수하려는 자든 그저 들어와 살려는 자든, 낯선 사람들로부터 그 재산은 안전했다. 오가는 사람들은 그 집을 지나칠 때면 수군거렸다. 그 집은 귀신 들린 집이라는 평판이 나 있었으니, 아마도 가엾은 늙은 암라의 모습이 드문드문 눈에 띄었기 때문일 것이다. 어떤 때는 지붕 위에서, 어떤 때는 격자창 안에서였다. 분명 그녀보다 더 한결같이 머무른 혼령은 일찍이 없었고, 그토록 사람들이 피하면서도 유령의 거처로 딱 어울리는 집 또한 없었다. 이제 그녀에게 다가갈 수만 있다면, 벤허는 그녀가 자신에게 비록 희미할지언정 쓸모 있을 지식을 줄 수 있으리라 여겼다. 어쨌든 추억으로 그토록 정든 그곳에서 그녀를 보는 것은, 그가 애타게 찾는 이들을 찾아내는 일에 버금가는 기쁨이 될 터였다.\n\n그리하여 무엇보다 먼저 그는 옛집으로 가서 암라를 찾아볼 작정이었다.\n\n이렇게 마음을 정한 그는 해가 진 직후에 일어나, 정상에서 동쪽보다 조금 북쪽으로 굽어지는 길을 따라 산을 내려가기 시작했다. 거의 산기슭에 이르러, 케드론 골짜기 바닥 가까이에서 그는 남쪽으로 실로암 마을과 그 이름을 딴 못으로 이어지는 길과 만나는 갈림길에 다다랐다. 그곳에서 그는 양 떼를 몰아 장터로 가는 목자와 마주쳤다. 그는 그 사람에게 말을 건네 함께 어울렸고, 그와 동행하여 겟세마네를 지나 어문(魚門)을 통해 도시로 들어갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n벤허가 성문 안에서 가축 몰이꾼과 헤어져, 남쪽으로 난 좁은 골목으로 접어들었을 때는 이미 날이 저물어 있었다. 마주치는 사람들 중 몇몇이 그에게 인사를 건넸다. 포석은 울퉁불퉁했다. 양쪽으로 늘어선 집들은 낮고 어둡고 적막했으며, 문이란 문은 모두 닫혀 있었다. 지붕에서는 이따금 여인들이 아이들을 달래며 흥얼거리는 소리가 들려왔다. 그가 처한 처지의 외로움, 밤, 그리고 그가 이곳에 온 목적을 휘감은 불확실함이 모두 그의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 마음이 점점 더 가라앉는 채로, 그는 오늘날 베데스다 못이라 불리는 깊은 저수지에 곧장 다다랐다. 그곳에는 머리 위로 드리운 하늘이 물에 비치고 있었다. 위를 올려다보니, 안토니아 탑의 북쪽 성벽이 보였는데, 검고 음산한 덩어리가 어슴푸레한 강철빛 잿빛 하늘로 솟아 있었다. 그는 위협하는 보초에게 가로막히기라도 한 듯 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n그 탑은 어찌나 높이 솟았고 어찌나 거대해 보였는지, 그리고 더없이 든든한 기초 위에 떡 버티고 선 듯하여, 그는 그 견고함을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 어머니가 그 안에 산 채로 갇혀 있다면, 그가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가? 힘으로는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 한 부대의 군대가 투석기와 공성퇴로 그 돌의 얼굴을 두드린다 해도 비웃음만 살 것이다. 그 거대한 남동쪽 망루는 그 혼자만을 상대로, 산처럼 태연자약하게 내려다보고 있었다. 그리고 그는 생각했다. 꾀란 이토록 쉽게 좌절되는구나. 그리고 언제나 무력한 자들의 마지막 의지처인 하느님—그 하느님께서는 때로 이토록 더디게 움직이시는구나!\n\n의혹과 불안 속에서, 그는 탑 앞으로 난 거리로 접어들어 천천히 서쪽으로 그 길을 따라갔다.\n\n베제다 너머에는 카라반사라이가 하나 있어, 이 도시에 머무는 동안 그곳에서 묵을 작정이었다. 그러나 지금 이 순간만큼은 집으로 가고 싶은 충동을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그의 마음이 그를 그쪽으로 끌어당겼다.\n\n곁을 지나는 몇 안 되는 사람들에게서 받은 그 오래되고 격식 있는 인사가 이토록 정겹게 들린 적이 없었다. 이윽고 동쪽 하늘 전체가 은빛으로 물들며 빛나기 시작했고, 그때까지 서쪽에서 보이지 않던 것들이—주로 시온산의 높은 탑들이—마치 어둑한 심연에서 솟아오르듯 드러나, 유령 같은 또렷함을 입은 채, 발치의 골짜기에 입을 벌린 검은 어둠 위로 둥둥 떠올라, 그야말로 허공의 성채를 이루었다.\n\n그는 마침내 아버지의 집에 다다랐다.\n\n이 글을 읽는 이들 가운데에는 굳이 일러 주지 않아도 그의 심정을 헤아릴 사람이 있을 것이다. 어린 시절 행복한 가정을 가졌던 이들이 그러하니—그 시절이 시간상 아무리 멀리 떨어져 있다 해도—그 가정은 이제 모든 회상의 출발점이 되는 곳이요, 눈물을 흘리며 떠나왔으나 할 수만 있다면 다시 어린아이가 되어 돌아가고 싶은 낙원이며, 웃음과 노래의 자리요, 훗날의 그 어떤 승리보다, 아니 그 모든 승리를 합한 것보다 더 소중한 추억이 깃든 곳이다.\n\n벤허는 오래된 집의 북쪽에 난 대문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다. 봉인할 때 쓴 밀랍이 모서리마다 아직 또렷이 남아 있었고, 문짝을 가로질러 다음과 같은 글귀가 적힌 판자가 붙어 있었다—\n\"이 재산은 황제의 소유다.\"\n\n그 끔찍한 이별의 날 이후로, 누구도 그 대문을 드나든 적이 없었다. 옛날처럼 두드려 볼까? 부질없는 짓임을 그도 알았다. 그러나 그 유혹을 떨칠 수가 없었다. 암라가 듣고서 그쪽 창문 하나로 내다볼지도 몰랐다. 돌멩이 하나를 집어 들고, 그는 너른 돌계단을 올라가 세 번 두드렸다. 둔탁한 메아리가 답했다. 다시, 아까보다 더 크게 두드려 보았다. 그리고 또 한 번, 그때마다 귀를 기울이려 잠시 멈추었다. 정적이 그를 조롱하고 있었다. 거리로 물러나 창문들을 살폈으나, 그것들 또한 생기가 없었다. 지붕의 난간이 밝아 오는 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 윤곽을 드러냈으니, 그 위에서 무언가 움직였다면 그의 눈을 피할 수 없었을 텐데, 아무것도 움직이지 않았다.\n\n북쪽에서 서쪽으로 옮겨 가니 그곳에는 창문이 넷 있었고, 그는 그것들을 오래도록 애타게 살폈으나, 역시 아무 소용이 없었다. 어떤 때는 가슴이 부질없는 소망으로 부풀어 올랐고, 또 어떤 때는 제 공상이 빚어내는 속임수에 몸을 떨었다. 암라는 아무 기척도 보이지 않았으니—유령 하나조차 움직이지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 그는 소리 없이 남쪽으로 돌아 들어갔다. 그곳에서도 대문은 봉인되고 글귀가 적혀 있었다. 훗날 죄악의 산이라 불리게 된 올리벳 마루 너머로 쏟아져 내리는 팔월 달의 부드러운 광채가 그 글자들을 또렷이 드러냈다. 그는 그것을 읽고, 분노에 사로잡혔다. 그가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 못 박힌 판자를 비틀어 뜯어내, 도랑에 내던지는 것뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 계단에 앉아, 새로운 왕을 위해 기도하며, 그분의 오심이 앞당겨지기를 빌었다. 피가 식어 가면서, 그는 어느새 여름 더위 속 긴 여행의 피로에 굴복하여 점점 더 낮게 가라앉다가, 마침내 잠이 들었다.\n\n그 무렵, 두 여인이 안토니아 탑 쪽에서 거리를 따라 내려와, 벤허 가문의 저택으로 다가오고 있었다. 그들은 겁먹은 걸음으로 몰래 나아가며, 귀를 기울이려 자주 멈추어 섰다. 그 울퉁불퉁한 건물의 모퉁이에서, 한 여인이 다른 여인에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"여기로구나, 티르자!\"\n\n티르자는 한번 바라보고는 어머니의 손을 잡고서, 흐느끼면서도 소리는 내지 않은 채, 어머니에게 무겁게 기대었다.\n\n\"가자꾸나, 얘야, 왜냐하면\"—어머니는 머뭇거리며 떨었다. 그러더니 애써 차분해지려 하며 말을 이었다—\"왜냐하면 아침이 오면 저들이 우리를 성문 밖으로 내쫓을 테고, 그러면 다시는 돌아오지 못할 테니까.\"\n\n티르자는 거의 돌바닥에 주저앉을 뻔했다.\n\n\"아, 그래요!\" 그녀가 흐느끼는 사이사이 말했다. \"잊고 있었어요. 집으로 돌아가는 기분이 들었거든요. 하지만 우리는 나병 환자고, 집도 없어요. 우리는 죽은 자들의 무리에 속한걸요!\"\n\n어머니는 몸을 숙여 다정하게 그녀를 일으키며 말했다. \"두려워할 것 없다. 가자꾸나.\"\n\n실로, 빈손을 쳐들기만 해도 그들은 한 군단을 향해 달려들어 그것을 패주시킬 수도 있었으리라.\n\n그리고 거친 벽에 바싹 붙어, 그들은 두 유령처럼 미끄러져 나아가다가, 대문에 이르자 그 앞에서 또한 걸음을 멈추었다. 판자를 보고서, 그들은 아직 채 식지도 않은 벤허의 발자국 위로 그 돌을 밟고 올라서서, 그 글귀를 읽었다—\"이 재산은 황제의 소유다.\"\n\n그러자 어머니는 두 손을 맞잡고, 눈을 치켜뜬 채, 형언할 수 없는 비통함 속에서 신음했다.\n\n\"왜 그러세요, 어머니? 무서워요!\"\n\n이윽고 돌아온 대답은 이러했다. \"오, 티르자, 가엾은 그 애가 죽었구나! 그 애가 죽었어!\"\n\n\"누가요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"네 오라비 말이다! 저들이 그 애한테서 모든 것을 빼앗았어—모든 것을—이 집까지도!\"\n\n\"가엾어라!\" 티르자가 멍하니 말했다.\n\n\"이제 그 애는 우리를 도와줄 수 없게 되었구나.\"\n\n\"그러면, 어머니?\"\n\n\"내일—내일이면, 얘야, 우리는 길가에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 나병 환자들이 그러하듯 동냥을 해야 한다. 동냥을 하든가, 아니면—\"\n\n티르자는 다시 어머니에게 기대어, 속삭이듯 말했다. \"우리—우리 차라리 죽어요!\"\n\n\"안 된다!\" 어머니가 단호하게 말했다. \"주님께서 우리의 때를 정해 놓으셨고, 우리는 주님을 믿는 사람들이다. 이런 일에서도 우리는 주님을 기다릴 것이다. 어서 가자!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그녀는 티르자의 손을 잡고서, 벽에 바싹 붙은 채 집의 서쪽 모퉁이로 서둘러 갔다. 그곳에 아무도 보이지 않자, 그들은 다음 모퉁이까지 계속 나아갔고, 남쪽 정면 전체와 거리 일부에 유난히 환하게 깔린 달빛을 피해 몸을 움츠렸다. 어머니의 의지는 굳세었다. 그녀는 서쪽의 창문들을 한 번 뒤돌아 올려다보고는, 티르자를 끌어당기며 빛 속으로 발을 내디뎠다. 그러자 그들이 받은 재앙의 깊이가 비로소 드러났으니—입술과 뺨에, 짓무른 눈에, 갈라진 손에, 무엇보다 길고 뱀처럼 늘어진 머리채에 그것이 드러났다. 그 머리채는 역겨운 진물로 뻣뻣했고, 눈썹과 마찬가지로 소름 끼치도록 하얗게 세어 있었다. 또한 누가 어머니이고 누가 딸인지조차 분간할 수 없었으니, 둘 다 마녀처럼 늙어 보였다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"계단에 누가 누워 있구나—남자다. 돌아서 가자.\"\n\n그들은 재빨리 거리 건너편으로 가서, 그 그늘 속을 따라 나아가다가, 대문 앞에 이르자 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"잠들었구나, 티르자!\"\n\n남자는 미동도 없었다.\n\n\"여기 있거라, 내가 대문을 열어 보마.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 어머니는 소리 없이 건너가, 쪽문을 만져 보려 했다. 그 문이 열렸는지 아닌지는 그녀도 끝내 알지 못했으니, 바로 그 순간 남자가 한숨을 쉬며 몸을 뒤척였고, 머리에 둘렀던 수건이 흘러내려 얼굴이 위를 향한 채 너른 달빛 속에 환히 드러났기 때문이다. 그녀는 그 얼굴을 내려다보고 흠칫 놀랐다. 그러고는 몸을 조금 숙여 다시 보더니, 몸을 일으켜 두 손을 맞잡고, 말없이 호소하듯 하늘로 눈을 들었다. 그렇게 한순간을 보내고, 그녀는 티르자에게 달려 돌아갔다.\n\n\"살아 계신 주님께 맹세코, 저 사람은 내 아들이다—네 오라비야!\" 그녀가 경외에 찬 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n\"제 오라비요?—유다요?\"\n\n어머니가 다급히 그녀의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"가자!\" 그녀가 똑같이 억누른 속삭임으로 말했다. \"함께 그 애를 보자꾸나—한 번 더—단 한 번만—그런 다음 주님, 당신의 종들을 도와주소서!\"\n\n그들은 손을 맞잡고 거리를 건넜으니, 유령처럼 재빠르고 유령처럼 고요했다. 그들의 그림자가 그에게 드리워지자, 그들은 걸음을 멈추었다. 그의 한 손이 손바닥을 위로 한 채 계단에 놓여 있었다. 티르자는 무릎을 꿇고 그 손에 입을 맞추려 했으나, 어머니가 그녀를 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"목숨이 아깝거든 안 된다. 절대 안 돼! 부정하다, 우리는 부정해!\" 그녀가 속삭였다.\n\n티르자는 마치 그가 나병 환자이기라도 한 듯 그에게서 몸을 움츠렸다.\n\n벤허는 사내다운 사람들이 그러하듯 준수했다. 사막의 햇볕과 바람에 노출되어 뺨과 이마는 가무잡잡했으나, 옅은 콧수염 아래 입술은 붉었고, 이는 희게 빛났으며, 부드러운 턱수염도 턱과 목의 둥근 윤곽을 다 가리지는 못했다. 어머니의 눈에 그가 얼마나 아름다워 보였던가! 행복했던 어린 시절에 늘 그러했듯, 그를 두 팔로 끌어안고 그 머리를 가슴에 품고 입 맞추고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 강렬했던가! 그 충동을 이겨 낼 힘을 그녀는 어디서 얻었던가? 그녀의 사랑에서다, 오 독자여!—그 어머니의 사랑에서다. 잘 살펴보면, 이 사랑에는 다른 어떤 사랑과도 닮지 않은 점이 있으니, 사랑하는 대상에게는 더없이 다정하면서도, 자기 자신에게는 한없이 가혹할 수 있다는 점이요, 바로 거기서 모든 자기희생의 힘이 솟아난다. 건강과 재산을 되찾기 위해서도, 인생의 그 어떤 축복을 위해서도, 생명 그 자체를 위해서도, 그녀는 결코 그의 뺨에 나병에 걸린 입맞춤을 남기지 않으리라! 그러나 그를 만지지 않을 수는 없었다. 그를 찾아낸 바로 그 순간에, 그녀는 영원히 그를 단념해야만 했던 것이다! 그것이 얼마나 쓰디쓰고 쓰디쓰게 힘든 일이었는지는, 다른 어느 어머니에게 말하게 하라! 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고, 그의 발치로 기어가, 그의 신발 한 짝 바닥에 입술을 댔다. 비록 거리의 먼지로 누렇게 더럽혀져 있었으나—그리고 거듭거듭 입을 맞추었으니, 그 입맞춤에 그녀의 온 영혼이 담겨 있었다.\n\n그가 몸을 뒤척이며 손을 휘저었다. 그들은 뒤로 물러났으나, 그가 꿈결에 중얼거리는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"어머니! 암라! 어디—\"\n\n그는 다시 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다.\n\n티르자는 애틋하게 바라보았다. 어머니는 얼굴을 먼지 속에 파묻고, 가슴이 터질 것만 같은 그 깊고 거센 흐느낌을 억누르려 안간힘을 썼다. 그녀는 차라리 그가 깨어나기를 바랄 뻔했다.\n\n그가 그녀를 찾았으니, 그녀는 잊히지 않은 것이다. 잠결에도 그는 그녀를 생각하고 있었다. 이것으로 충분하지 않은가?\n\n이윽고 어머니가 티르자에게 손짓했다. 그들은 몸을 일으켜, 그 모습을 영원히 지워지지 않도록 마음에 새기려는 듯 한 번 더 바라보고는, 손을 맞잡고 거리를 다시 건넜다. 그곳 벽의 그늘로 돌아가, 그들은 물러나 무릎을 꿇은 채, 그를 바라보며, 그가 깨어나기를 기다렸다—무엇인지도 모를 어떤 계시를 기다렸다. 그들의 것과 같은 사랑의 인내심을 잴 척도를, 아직 누구도 우리에게 일러 준 적이 없다.\n\n이윽고, 그가 아직 잠들어 있는 동안, 또 다른 여인이 저택 모퉁이에 나타났다. 그늘에 있던 두 사람은 빛 속에서 그녀를 또렷이 보았다. 자그마하고, 허리가 많이 굽고, 피부가 거무스름하고, 머리가 희끗희끗한, 하인의 옷차림을 단정하게 갖춘, 채소가 가득 든 바구니를 든 여인이었다.\n\n계단 위의 남자를 보고서 그 새로 온 여인은 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러더니 마음을 정한 듯, 잠든 이에게 다가가면서 더없이 가벼운 걸음으로 계속 나아갔다. 그를 빙 돌아, 그녀는 대문으로 가서, 쪽문 빗장을 손쉽게 한쪽으로 밀고, 그 틈으로 손을 넣었다. 왼쪽 문짝의 너른 판자 하나가 소리 없이 빠끔히 열렸다. 그녀는 바구니를 그 안으로 들이밀고 자신도 뒤따라 들어가려다가, 호기심에 못 이겨, 발치에 얼굴을 훤히 드러낸 채 누운 낯선 이를 한 번 보려고 머뭇거렸다.\n\n거리 건너편의 구경꾼들은 낮은 탄성을 들었고, 그 여인이 눈의 힘을 되살리려는 듯 두 눈을 비비고, 더 가까이 몸을 숙이고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 사방을 미친 듯이 둘러보고, 잠든 이를 바라보고, 몸을 굽혀 밖으로 뻗은 손을 들어 올려, 애틋하게 입 맞추는 것을 보았다—그들이 그토록 간절히 하고 싶었으나 감히 하지 못한 바로 그 일이었다.\n\n그 행동에 잠이 깬 벤허는 본능적으로 손을 거두어들였다. 그러면서 그의 눈이 그 여인의 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"암라! 오 암라, 정말 너냐?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그 착한 마음씨는 말로 답하지 않고, 그의 목덜미에 엎드려 기쁨에 겨워 울었다.\n\n그는 가만히 그녀의 팔을 풀고, 눈물에 젖은 거무스름한 얼굴을 들어 올려 거기에 입 맞추었으니, 그의 기쁨은 그녀의 기쁨에 조금 못 미칠 뿐이었다. 그러자 길 건너편의 두 사람은 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"어머니—티르자—오 암라, 그분들 소식을 말해 다오! 말해 봐, 어서 말해 봐, 제발!\"\n\n암라는 그저 새삼스레 울기만 했다.\n\n\"네가 그분들을 뵈었구나, 암라. 어디 계신지 너는 알고 있어. 집에 계신다고 말해 다오.\"\n\n티르자가 움직였으나, 어머니는 그 속셈을 알아채고 그녀를 붙잡으며 속삭였다. \"가지 마라—목숨이 아깝거든. 부정하다, 우리는 부정해!\"\n\n그녀의 사랑은 가혹한 기세에 사로잡혀 있었다. 두 사람의 가슴이 다 미어진다 해도, 그가 자신들과 같은 신세가 되어서는 안 되었다. 그리하여 그녀는 이겨 냈다.\n\n그러는 동안, 그토록 애원을 받은 암라는 그저 더욱 흐느끼기만 했다.\n\n\"들어가는 길이었느냐?\" 그가 이윽고, 판자가 젖혀진 것을 보고 물었다. \"그럼 가자. 나도 너와 함께 들어가마.\" 그는 말하면서 몸을 일으켰다. \"로마인들—주님의 저주가 그들에게 내리기를!—로마인들이 거짓말을 한 것이다. 이 집은 내 것이야. 일어나라, 암라, 어서 들어가자.\" 잠시 후 그들은 사라졌고, 그늘 속의 두 사람만 남겨져, 자신들을 향해 멍하니 입을 벌린 대문을 바라보았다—다시는 결코 들어갈 수 없는 그 대문을. 그들은 먼지 속에서 서로 몸을 맞대고 웅크렸다.\n\n그들은 자신들의 도리를 다했다.\n\n그들의 사랑은 증명되었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그들은 발각되어, 돌팔매질을 받으며 도시 밖으로 쫓겨났다.\n\n\"꺼져라! 너희는 죽은 자들의 무리니, 죽은 자들에게로 가라!\"\n\n그 선고가 귓전에 울리는 가운데, 그들은 밖으로 나아갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n「왕의 정원」이라는 아름다운 이름을 지닌 그 유명한 장소를 찾는 오늘날의 성지 순례자들은 기드론 골짜기의 바닥을 따라가거나, 기혼과 힌놈의 굽이를 거슬러 옛 우물 엔로겔까지 내려간다. 거기서 달콤한 생수를 한 모금 마시고는 걸음을 멈추니, 그 방향에서 흥미를 끄는 것은 그것이 끝이다. 그들은 우물을 두른 커다란 돌을 바라보고, 그 깊이를 물으며, 졸졸 흐르는 보물을 길어 올리는 원시적인 방식에 미소 짓고, 그것을 지키는 누더기 차림의 가엾은 사내에게 약간의 연민을 흘린다. 그런 다음 몸을 돌려, 북쪽에서 그들을 향해 비탈져 내려오는 모리아산과 시온산에 황홀해하니, 하나는 오벨에서 끝나고 다른 하나는 한때 다윗 성의 터였던 곳에서 끝난다. 그 너머, 저 멀리 하늘 높이에는 성스러운 곳들의 장식이 보인다. 여기에는 우아한 둥근 지붕을 인 하람이 있고, 저기에는 폐허가 되어서도 당당한, 히피쿠스의 굳건한 잔해가 있다. 그 풍경을 충분히 즐기고 기억에 또렷이 새기고 나면, 순례자들은 오른편에 험준한 위엄으로 서 있는 죄악의 산을 한 번 보고, 이어 왼편 너머에 있는 악한 모의의 언덕으로 눈길을 옮긴다. 성서의 역사와 랍비 및 수도사들의 전승에 밝은 이라면, 그 언덕에서 미신적인 공포로도 이겨 낼 수 없는 어떤 흥미를 발견하게 될 것이다.\n\n그 언덕을 둘러싸고 무리 지은 흥미로운 지점들을 모두 이야기하자면 너무 길어진다. 지금의 목적으로는, 그 언덕의 발치가 현대인들의 진짜 정통 지옥—유황과 불의 지옥, 옛 이름으로는 게헨나—에 박혀 있다는 것, 그리고 그리스도 시대에도 그러했듯 지금도, 남쪽과 남동쪽으로 도시와 마주한 그 가파른 면이 무덤들로 갈라지고 패여 있다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하다. 그 무덤들은 아득한 옛날부터 나병 환자들의 거처였으니, 하나씩이 아니라 무리를 지어 살았다. 그곳에서 그들은 자기네 통치를 세우고 사회를 이루었으며, 그곳에 도시를 세우고 저희끼리 살았으니, 하느님의 저주받은 자로서 사람들의 외면을 받았다.\n\n앞 장의 사건들이 있은 지 이틀째 되는 날 아침, 암라는 엔로겔 우물 가까이 다가가, 돌 위에 앉았다. 예루살렘을 잘 아는 이가 그녀를 보았다면, 어느 넉넉한 집안의 사랑받는 하녀라고 했을 것이다. 그녀는 물항아리와 바구니를 가져왔는데, 바구니 안의 것은 눈처럼 흰 보자기로 덮여 있었다. 그것들을 곁의 땅에 내려놓은 뒤, 머리에서 흘러내린 숄을 풀고, 두 손을 무릎 위에 깍지 낀 채, 언덕이 아켈다마와 토기장이의 밭으로 가파르게 떨어지는 그곳을 다소곳이 올려다보았다.\n\n아직 무척 이른 시각이라, 그녀가 우물에 처음 도착한 사람이었다. 그러나 곧 한 사내가 밧줄과 가죽 두레박을 가지고 왔다. 그는 자그마하고 가무잡잡한 얼굴의 여인에게 인사한 뒤, 밧줄을 풀어 두레박에 매고는 손님을 기다렸다. 원하는 이는 스스로 물을 길어도 좋았지만, 그는 그 일을 직업으로 삼은 자였으니, 게라 한 닢이면 아무리 건장한 여인이 들고 갈 수 있는 가장 큰 항아리라도 채워 주었다.\n\n암라는 가만히 앉아, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 사내는 항아리를 보고는 잠시 뒤 채워 주기를 바라느냐고 물었다. 그녀가 정중하게 \"지금은 됐어요\" 하고 대답하자, 그는 더 이상 그녀에게 마음을 두지 않았다. 새벽빛이 올리벳 위로 또렷이 드러나자 그의 손님들이 모여들기 시작했고, 그는 그들을 챙기느라 손이 모자랄 지경이었다. 그동안 그녀는 줄곧 제자리를 지키며, 언덕 위를 뚫어지게 올려다보았다.\n\n해가 모습을 드러냈건만, 그녀는 여전히 앉아 지켜보며 기다렸다. 그녀가 이렇게 기다리는 동안, 그녀의 목적이 무엇인지 살펴보기로 하자.\n\n그녀는 해가 진 뒤에 장을 보러 가는 것이 버릇이었다. 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 몰래 빠져나가, 티로포에온 골짜기의 가게들이나 동쪽 어문 근처의 가게들을 찾아, 고기와 채소를 사서, 돌아와 다시 안에 틀어박혔다.\n\n벤허가 옛집에 다시 와 있다는 사실에서 그녀가 얻은 기쁨은 가히 짐작할 만하다. 그녀에게는 여주인이나 티르자에 관해 그에게 들려줄 것이 아무것도 없었다. 정말 아무것도. 그는 그토록 외롭지 않은 곳으로 그녀가 옮겨 가기를 바랐지만, 그녀는 거절했다. 그녀는 그가 떠날 때 그대로 남아 있는 자기 방을 다시 쓰기를 바랐지만, 발각될 위험이 너무 컸고, 그는 무엇보다 사람들의 캐물음을 피하고 싶어 했다. 그는 되도록 자주 그녀를 보러 오기로 했다. 밤에 와서 밤에 떠나기로 했다. 그녀는 그것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었고, 곧장 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 방법을 궁리하는 데 몰두했다. 그가 이제 어엿한 사내가 되었다는 생각은 그녀의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 그가 소년 시절의 취향을 버렸거나 잃었을지도 모른다는 생각 역시 들지 않았다. 그를 기쁘게 하려고, 그녀는 옛날에 하던 시중의 순서를 그대로 이어 갈 작정이었다. 그는 단것을 좋아했었다. 그녀는 그가 가장 좋아하던 종류를 떠올려, 그것을 만들어 그가 올 때마다 늘 준비해 두기로 마음먹었다. 이보다 더 행복한 일이 있을까? 그래서 이튿날 밤, 여느 때보다 일찍, 그녀는 바구니를 들고 몰래 나가 어문 시장으로 갔다. 가장 좋은 꿀을 찾아 이리저리 거니는데, 우연히 한 사내가 이야기를 들려주는 것을 듣게 되었다.\n\n그 이야기가 무엇이었는지는, 그 이야기꾼이 안토니아 탑의 사령관을 위해 횃불을 들었던 자들 가운데 하나, 곧 지하 제6호 감방에서 후르 집안 사람들이 발견되었을 때 그 자리에 있던 사람이었다고 말해 두면, 독자는 충분히 확실하게 짐작할 수 있을 것이다. 발견의 모든 경위가 낱낱이 이야기되었고, 그녀는 그것을 들었으니, 죄수들의 이름과 그 미망인이 스스로에 관해 한 진술까지 들었다.\n\n이 이야기를 들으며 암라가 느낀 감정은, 그녀가 본래 그러한 헌신적인 사람이었던 만큼 마땅한 것이었다. 그녀는 장을 다 보고, 꿈을 꾸는 듯한 마음으로 집으로 돌아왔다. 자기 도련님을 위해 얼마나 큰 행복을 마련해 두었는가! 그녀는 그의 어머니를 찾아낸 것이다!\n\n그녀는 웃다가 울다가 하면서 바구니를 치웠다. 그러다 문득 멈춰 서서 생각에 잠겼다. 어머니와 티르자가 나병 환자가 되었다는 말을 들으면 그는 죽고 말 것이다. 그는 악한 모의의 언덕 위에 있는 그 무서운 도시를 헤매며, 감염된 무덤마다 쉬지 않고 들어가 그들을 찾아 헤맬 것이고, 그러면 병이 그에게 옮아, 그들의 운명이 곧 그의 운명이 될 것이다. 그녀는 두 손을 비틀어 쥐었다. 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?\n\n그녀보다 앞선 많은 이들이 그러했고 그녀 이후의 많은 이들이 그러하듯, 그녀는 지혜는 아닐지언정 영감만은 자신의 애정에서 얻어, 하나의 기묘한 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n그녀가 알기로, 나병 환자들은 아침이면 언덕의 무덤 거처에서 내려와, 엔로겔 우물에서 하루치 물을 길어 가는 것이 습관이었다. 항아리를 가져와 땅에 내려놓고는, 물이 채워질 때까지 멀찍이 서서 기다렸다. 여주인과 티르자도 거기에 와야 했으니, 율법은 가차 없어 아무런 차별도 두지 않았기 때문이다. 부유한 나병 환자라 해서 가난한 자보다 나을 것이 없었다.\n\n그리하여 암라는 자기가 들은 이야기를 벤허에게 말하지 않고, 혼자 우물로 가서 기다리기로 결심했다. 굶주림과 목마름이 그 가엾은 이들을 그리로 내몰 것이고, 그녀는 그들을 보면 알아볼 수 있으리라 믿었다. 설령 알아보지 못하더라도, 그들이 그녀를 알아볼지도 몰랐다.\n\n그러는 사이 벤허가 와서, 둘은 많은 이야기를 나누었다. 내일이면 말루크가 도착할 터였다. 그러면 곧바로 수색을 시작할 참이었다. 그는 그 일을 시작하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그동안 무료함을 달래려고 그는 인근의 성스러운 곳들을 둘러볼 작정이었다. 비밀이 그 여인을 무겁게 짓눌렀으리라는 것은 충분히 짐작할 만한 일이지만, 그녀는 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n그가 떠나자 그녀는 먹기 좋은 음식을 마련하느라 바삐 움직이며, 가진 솜씨를 다해 그 일에 매달렸다. 별들이 알려 주는 대로 날이 밝아 올 무렵, 그녀는 바구니를 채우고 항아리 하나를 골라, 가장 먼저 열리는 어문으로 빠져나가 엔로겔로 가는 길에 올랐으니, 우리가 본 그대로 그곳에 다다랐다.\n\n해가 뜨고 얼마 지나지 않아, 우물의 일이 가장 분주하고 물 긷는 사내가 가장 부산할 때, 사실 여섯 개나 되는 두레박이 한꺼번에 쓰이고 모두들 아침의 서늘함이 한낮의 더위로 녹아들기 전에 자리를 뜨려고 서두를 때, 언덕의 주민들이 모습을 드러내며 자기네 무덤 문 앞을 오가기 시작했다. 좀 더 시간이 지나자 그들은 무리를 이루어 분간되었는데, 그 가운데 적지 않은 수가 가장 거룩한 관계를 떠올리게 할 만큼 어린아이들이었다. 가파른 면의 모퉁이를 돌아 잇따라 사람들이 나타났으니, 어깨에 항아리를 인 여인들이며, 지팡이와 목발에 의지해 비척비척 걸어오는 늙고 몹시 쇠약한 사내들이었다. 더러는 다른 이의 어깨에 기댔고, 몇몇은—아주 속수무책인 자들은—넝마 더미처럼 들것 위에 누워 있었다. 더없이 극심한 슬픔으로 이루어진 그 공동체에조차, 삶을 견딜 만하고 끌리는 것으로 만들어 주는 사랑의 빛은 있었다. 거리가 그 버림받은 이들의 비참함을 온전히 가리지는 못했으되 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸다.\n\n우물가의 자리에서 암라는 그 유령 같은 무리를 지켜보았다. 그녀는 거의 움직이지 않았다. 한 번 이상, 그녀는 자기가 찾는 이들을 본 것 같은 생각이 들었다. 그들이 언덕 위에 있다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었고, 그들이 내려와 가까이 와야 한다는 것도 그녀는 알았다. 우물의 사람들이 모두 물을 받고 나면, 그들이 올 터였다.\n\n그런데 가파른 면의 맨 밑자락에, 입을 크게 벌리고 있어 한 번 이상 암라의 눈길을 끌었던 무덤이 하나 있었다. 그 입구 가까이에는 커다란 돌 하나가 서 있었다. 해는 하루 중 가장 뜨거운 시간 내내 그 안을 들여다보았고, 무엇 하나 살아 있는 것이 깃들 만한 곳으로는 도무지 보이지 않았으니, 어쩌면 게헨나 밑에서 청소부 노릇을 마치고 돌아온 들개들이나 들어갈 만했다. 그런데도 그곳에서, 참을성 있는 이집트 여인은 무척 놀랍게도, 두 여인이 나오는 것을 보았으니, 하나가 다른 하나를 반쯤 부축하고 반쯤 이끌고 있었다. 둘 다 머리가 하얗게 셌고, 둘 다 늙어 보였다. 그러나 그들의 옷은 찢겨 있지 않았고, 마치 그곳이 낯선 곳이기라도 한 듯 사방을 둘러보았다. 아래에서 지켜보던 이는, 자기들도 그 한 자락이 되어 버린 그 끔찍한 무리의 광경에 그들이 겁에 질려 움츠러드는 것까지 본 듯한 생각이 들었다. 그녀의 심장이 더 빨리 뛰고 오로지 그들에게만 마음이 쏠리게 할 만한 까닭으로는 분명 보잘것없었으나, 어쨌든 그러했다.\n\n두 사람은 잠시 돌 곁에 머물렀다. 그러더니 천천히, 고통스럽게, 무척 두려워하며 우물 쪽으로 움직였는데, 그러자 여러 목소리가 그들을 멈추게 하려고 높아졌다. 그래도 그들은 계속 다가왔다. 물 긷던 사내가 조약돌 몇 개를 집어 들어 그들을 쫓아 보낼 채비를 했다. 사람들은 그들에게 저주를 퍼부었다. 언덕 위의 더 큰 무리는 날카롭게 소리쳤다. \"부정하다, 부정하다!\"\n\n\"틀림없이,\" 그들이 계속 다가오는 것을 보며 암라는 두 사람을 두고 생각했다. \"틀림없이, 저들은 나병 환자의 관습을 모르는 사람들이야.\"\n\n그녀는 일어나, 바구니와 항아리를 들고 그들을 맞으러 갔다. 우물가의 소동은 곧 가라앉았다.\n\n\"어리석기도 하지,\" 한 사람이 웃으며 말했다. \"저렇게 죽은 자들에게 좋은 빵을 주다니, 어리석기도 하지!\"\n\n\"그것도 저렇게 멀리까지 오다니!\" 또 다른 사람이 말했다. \"나라면 적어도 문간에서 만나게 하겠어.\"\n\n암라는 더 갸륵한 마음으로 나아갔다. 혹시 잘못 본 것이라면! 그녀의 심장이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐다. 그리고 다가갈수록 그녀는 더욱 미심쩍고 어지러워졌다. 그들이 그녀를 기다리며 서 있는 곳에서 너덧 걸음 떨어진 데서 그녀는 멈춰 섰다.\n\n저 사람이 자기가 사랑하던 여주인이라니! 감사한 마음에 그토록 자주 손에 입 맞추던 그분이! 그토록 신실하게 기억 속에 간직해 온 그 부인다운 사랑스러운 모습의 주인이! 그리고 저 사람이 자기가 갓난아기 때부터 길러 온 티르자라니! 아픔을 달래 주고, 놀이를 함께 나누던 그 아이가! 미소 짓고, 사랑스러운 얼굴에, 노래하던 티르자, 그 큰 집의 빛이요, 자기 노년에 약속된 축복이었던 그 아이가! 자기 여주인, 자기 귀염둥이가—저들이라니? 그 광경에 여인의 영혼은 메스꺼워졌다.\n\n\"이들은 늙은 여인들이야,\" 그녀는 속으로 말했다. \"나는 전에 이들을 본 적이 없어. 돌아가야지.\"\n\n그녀는 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"암라,\" 나병 환자 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n이집트 여인은 항아리를 떨어뜨리고, 떨면서 뒤를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"누가 나를 불렀어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"암라.\"\n\n하녀의 어리둥절한 눈이 말하는 이의 얼굴에 머물렀다.\n\n\"당신들은 누구예요?\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리가 바로 네가 찾는 사람들이다.\"\n\n암라는 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"오, 마님, 마님! 마님의 하느님을 제 하느님으로 삼았듯이, 저를 마님께로 인도하신 그분께 찬양이 있기를!\"\n\n그러고는 무릎을 꿇은 채, 그 가엾고 벅찬 사람은 앞으로 다가가기 시작했다.\n\n\"멈춰라, 암라! 더 가까이 오지 마라. 부정하다, 부정하다!\"\n\n그 말로 충분했다. 암라는 엎드려, 우물가의 사람들이 들을 만큼 큰 소리로 흐느꼈다. 그러다 갑자기 다시 무릎을 세웠다.\n\n\"오, 마님, 티르자 아가씨는 어디 계세요?\"\n\n\"여기 있어, 암라, 여기! 물을 조금만 가져다주지 않겠니?\"\n\n하녀의 몸에 밴 버릇이 되살아났다. 얼굴 위로 흘러내린 거친 머리칼을 쓸어 올리고, 암라는 일어나 바구니로 가서 보자기를 걷었다.\n\n\"보세요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여기 빵과 고기가 있어요.\"\n\n그녀는 보자기를 땅에 펼치려 했으나, 여주인이 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그러지 마라, 암라. 저기 있는 자들이 너에게 돌을 던지고, 우리에게 물을 주지 않을지도 모른다. 바구니는 우리 곁에 두어라. 항아리를 들고 가서 물을 채워 이리로 가져오너라. 우리가 그것들을 무덤으로 가지고 가마. 그러면 오늘은 네가 율법이 허락하는 모든 시중을 다 든 셈이 된다. 서둘러라, 암라.\"\n\n이 모든 일이 눈앞에서 벌어진 사람들은 하녀에게 길을 비켜 주었고, 항아리 채우는 것까지 거들어 주었으니, 그녀의 얼굴에 드러난 슬픔이 그토록 가엾었던 것이다.\n\n\"저들은 누구요?\" 한 여인이 물었다.\n\n암라는 다소곳이 대답했다. \"저에게 잘해 주시던 분들이에요.\"\n\n항아리를 어깨에 들어 올리고, 그녀는 서둘러 돌아왔다. 무심결에 그녀는 그들에게로 가려 했으나, \"부정하다, 부정하다! 조심해!\" 하는 외침이 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다. 물을 바구니 곁에 내려놓고, 그녀는 뒤로 물러나, 조금 떨어진 곳에 섰다.\n\n\"고맙다, 암라,\" 여주인이 그것들을 거두어들이며 말했다. \"참으로 갸륵하구나.\"\n\n\"제가 더 할 수 있는 일은 없을까요?\" 암라가 물었다.\n\n어머니의 손은 항아리 위에 놓여 있었고, 그녀는 갈증으로 애가 탔다. 그런데도 그녀는 멈칫하더니, 몸을 일으켜 단호하게 말했다. \"있다. 나는 유다가 집에 돌아온 것을 안다. 그저께 밤, 문간 계단에서 잠든 그 애를 보았다. 네가 그 애를 깨우는 것도 보았다.\"\n\n암라는 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"오, 마님! 그것을 보시고도, 나오지 않으셨어요!\"\n\n\"그랬다면 그 애를 죽이는 일이 되었겠지. 나는 다시는 그 애를 품에 안을 수 없다. 다시는 그 애에게 입 맞출 수 없어. 오, 암라, 암라, 네가 그 애를 사랑하는 걸 나는 안다!\"\n\n\"네,\" 그 진실한 마음은 다시 눈물을 터뜨리며 무릎을 꿇은 채 말했다. \"그분을 위해서라면 저는 죽을 수도 있어요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 사실임을 내게 증명해 보여라, 암라.\"\n\n\"준비되어 있어요.\"\n\n\"그러면 우리가 어디 있는지, 우리를 보았다는 것을 그 애에게 말하지 마라. 그것만이다, 암라.\"\n\n\"하지만 도련님은 마님을 찾고 계세요. 마님을 찾으려고 먼 곳에서 오셨어요.\"\n\n\"그 애는 우리를 찾아서는 안 된다. 그 애가 우리처럼 되어서는 안 돼. 들어라, 암라. 너는 오늘 그러했듯이 우리를 섬기게 될 것이다. 우리에게 필요한 얼마 안 되는 것을 가져다주어라—이제 오래지 않을 거다—오래지 않아. 너는 이렇게 아침저녁으로 와서, 그리고—그리고\"—목소리가 떨렸고, 굳센 의지가 거의 무너질 뻔했다—\"그리고 우리에게 그 애의 소식을 전해 다오, 암라. 그러나 그 애에게는 우리에 관해 아무 말도 하지 마라. 알아들었느냐?\"\n\n\"오, 도련님이 마님 이야기를 하시는 것을 듣고, 마님을 찾아 헤매시는 것을 보면서—그 모든 사랑을 보면서도, 마님이 살아 계시다는 것조차 말씀드리지 못한다니, 너무도 견디기 힘들 거예요!\"\n\n\"우리가 잘 지낸다고 그 애에게 말할 수 있겠느냐, 암라?\"\n\n하녀는 두 팔에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"그럴 수 없지,\" 여주인이 말을 이었다. \"그러니 아예 아무 말도 하지 마라. 이제 가거라, 그리고 오늘 저녁에 오너라. 우리가 너를 기다리마. 그때까지, 잘 가거라.\"\n\n\"그 짐은 무거울 거예요, 오, 마님, 견디기 힘들 거예요,\" 암라가 엎드리며 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 이런 꼴이 된 것을 그 애가 보는 것은 얼마나 더 힘들겠느냐,\" 어머니는 바구니를 티르자에게 건네며 대답했다. \"오늘 저녁에 다시 오너라,\" 그녀는 거듭 말하며, 물을 들고 무덤을 향해 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n암라는 그들이 사라질 때까지 무릎을 꿇은 채 기다렸다. 그러고는 슬픈 마음으로 집으로 가는 길에 올랐다.\n\n저녁에 그녀는 다시 왔다. 그 뒤로 아침저녁으로 그들을 섬기는 것이 그녀의 일과가 되었으니, 그리하여 그들은 꼭 필요한 것에 부족함이 없었다. 무덤은 아무리 돌투성이에 황량하다 해도, 탑의 감방보다는 덜 음울했다. 햇빛이 그 문을 금빛으로 물들였고, 그곳은 아름다운 세상 안에 있었다. 그리고 탁 트인 하늘 아래에서는, 그만큼 더 큰 믿음으로 죽음을 기다릴 수 있는 법이다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n일곱째 달 초하루 아침—히브리어로는 티슈리, 영어로는 시월—벤허는 객사의 침상에서 일어났으나, 온 세상이 못마땅하기만 했다.\n\n말루크가 도착했을 때 의논에 허비한 시간은 거의 없었다. 그는 안토니아 탑에서 수색을 시작했는데, 그 첫걸음이 대담하게도 지휘관인 호민관에게 직접 묻는 것이었다. 그는 그 장교에게 후르 가문의 내력과 그라투스가 당한 사고의 자초지종을 들려주며, 그 일에는 아무런 범죄성이 없었음을 설명했다. 이제 이 수색의 목적은, 그 불행한 가족 가운데 누구라도 살아 있는 이가 발견되거든 카이사르의 발 앞에 청원서를 올려 재산의 반환과 시민권의 회복을 간청하려는 데 있다고 그는 말했다. 그러한 청원은 황제의 명령에 따른 조사로 이어질 것이 분명하나, 그 가족의 벗들은 그러한 절차를 조금도 두려워하지 않는다고 했다.\n\n이에 호민관은 탑에서 두 여인을 발견한 정황을 소상히 진술하고, 그들이 스스로에 관해 진술한 내용을 적어 둔 비망록을 읽도록 허락했다. 그 사본을 베끼게 해 달라고 청하자, 호민관은 그것마저 허락해 주었다.\n\n말루크는 곧장 벤허에게 달려갔다.\n\n그 끔찍한 이야기가 이 젊은이에게 미친 충격을 묘사하려는 시도는 부질없다. 그 고통은 눈물로도 격정의 절규로도 가시지 않았으니, 어떤 표현으로도 담아낼 수 없을 만큼 깊었다. 그는 창백한 얼굴과 짓눌린 가슴으로 오랫동안 가만히 앉아 있었다. 이따금, 가장 사무치는 생각이 무엇인지 드러내기라도 하듯, 그는 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"나병환자라니, 나병환자라니! 그분이—내 어머니와 티르자가—나병환자라니! 언제까지입니까, 언제까지입니까, 주여!\"\n\n한순간 그는 의로운 슬픔의 분노에 찢겼다가, 다음 순간에는 복수의 갈망에 사로잡혔으니, 솔직히 말해 그 복수의 갈망 또한 슬픔 못지않게 의로운 것이었다.\n\n마침내 그는 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"그들을 찾아야 한다. 죽어 가고 있을지도 모른다.\"\n\n\"어디서 찾으시렵니까?\" 말루크가 물었다.\n\n\"그들이 갈 곳은 단 한 군데뿐이다.\"\n\n말루크가 끼어들었고, 마침내 이후의 시도를 자신이 맡아 처리하게 해 달라는 청을 관철시켰다. 두 사람은 함께 악한 의논의 언덕 맞은편, 아득한 옛적부터 나병환자들의 구걸터였던 성문으로 갔다. 그곳에서 그들은 온종일 머물며 적선을 베풀고, 두 여인의 행방을 묻고, 그들을 찾아 주는 이에게 후한 상을 내걸었다. 그렇게 그들은 다섯째 달의 남은 날들과 여섯째 달 내내 날마다 거듭하여 그 일을 했다. 내건 상이 강력한 유인이 되어, 언덕 위의 그 두려운 도시를 나병환자들이 부지런히 샅샅이 뒤졌으니, 그들은 법으로만 죽은 자들이었기 때문이다. 우물가 아래 입을 벌린 무덤도 거듭거듭 들어가 살펴, 그 안에 든 자들에게 캐물었으나, 두 사람은 자신들의 비밀을 굳게 지켰다. 그 결과는 실패였다. 그리고 이제, 일곱째 달 초하루 아침에 추가로 얻은 정보라고는, 얼마 전 두 나병환자 여인이 관헌에 의해 어문에서 돌팔매질을 당해 쫓겨났다는 것뿐이었다. 그 실마리를 조금 캐고 날짜를 빈틈없이 견주어 보니, 그 고난을 당한 이들이 후르 가문 사람들이라는 슬픈 확신에 이르렀고, 해묵은 의문은 그 어느 때보다 어두워졌다. 그들은 어디에 있는가? 또 그들은 어떻게 되었는가?\n\n\"내 가족이 나병환자가 된 것으로도 모자랐단 말인가.\" 아들은 거듭거듭 말했는데, 그 쓰라림이 얼마나 격렬했는지는 독자의 상상에 맡긴다. \"그것으로도 모자랐어. 오, 아니지! 그들을 고향 도시에서 돌로 쳐서 내쫓아야만 했어! 내 어머니는 돌아가셨다! 광야를 헤매다 돌아가셨어! 돌아가셨어! 티르자도 죽었어! 나만 홀로 남았다. 그런데 무엇을 위해서인가? 언제까지입니까, 오 하느님, 내 조상의 주 하느님이여, 언제까지 이 로마가 버티겠습니까?\"\n\n분노에 차고, 절망에 빠지고, 복수심에 불타며 그는 객사의 안뜰로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 밤사이 들어온 사람들로 붐비고 있었다. 그는 아침을 먹으며 그들 가운데 몇몇의 이야기에 귀를 기울였다. 한 무리에게 그는 유독 마음이 끌렸다. 그들은 대개 젊고, 건장하고, 활기차고, 다부진 사내들로, 거동과 말씨가 시골풍이었다. 그들의 눈빛, 무어라 꼬집어 말할 수 없는 어떤 기운, 머리를 두는 자세, 눈을 굴리는 모습에는, 으레 예루살렘 하층민의 겉모습에 깃들지 않는 어떤 기상이 서려 있었다. 이는 어떤 이들이 산악 지대 생활의 특색이라 여기는 기상이나, 더 확실히는 건강하고 자유로운 삶에서 비롯된 것이라 할 수 있다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 그들이 갈릴리 사람들임을 알아냈으니, 여러 가지 볼일로 도시에 와 있었으나 주된 목적은 바로 그날로 정해진 나팔절에 참여하려는 것이었다. 그들은 단번에 그의 관심거리가 되었으니, 그가 머지않아 착수하려는 일에서 가장 손쉽게 지지를 얻으리라 바라던 바로 그 지방에서 온 이들이었기 때문이다.\n\n그들을 살피며, 그의 마음은 그러한 기상을 지닌 자들로 이루어진 군단을 엄혹한 로마식으로 훈련시킨다면 능히 이룰 수 있을 업적을 앞질러 그리고 있었는데, 그때 한 사내가 안뜰로 들어왔으니, 얼굴은 잔뜩 상기되고 두 눈은 흥분으로 번득이고 있었다.\n\n\"여기서 무얼 하고 있는 거요?\" 그가 갈릴리 사람들에게 말했다. \"랍비들과 장로들이 성전에서 빌라도를 보러 가고 있소. 자, 서두르시오, 우리도 그들과 함께 갑시다.\"\n\n그들이 순식간에 그를 에워쌌다.\n\n\"빌라도를 보러? 무엇 때문에?\"\n\n\"음모가 발각됐소. 빌라도가 새로 짓는 수로를 성전 돈으로 치르려 한다오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 신성한 보물로?\"\n\n그들은 번득이는 눈으로 서로에게 그 물음을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그것은 코르반—하느님의 돈이오. 그가 감히 한 세겔이라도 손댄다면 어디 두고 보자!\"\n\n\"갑시다.\" 전령이 외쳤다. \"행렬은 지금쯤 다리를 건넜을 거요. 온 도시가 그 뒤를 따라 쏟아져 나오고 있소. 우리가 필요할지도 모르오. 서두릅시다!\"\n\n생각과 행동이 하나이기라도 하듯, 거추장스러운 옷들이 잽싸게 벗어 던져졌고, 그 무리는 맨머리에, 들판에서 곡식을 거두는 일꾼으로서, 또 호수에서 뱃사공으로서 늘 걸치던 짧은 민소매 속옷 차림으로 나섰다. 그것은 그들이 가축 떼를 따라 언덕을 오르고, 햇볕에도 아랑곳없이 익은 포도를 따던 차림이었다. 허리띠를 단단히 죄느라 잠시 머뭇거렸을 뿐, 그들은 말했다. \"준비됐소.\"\n\n그러자 벤허가 그들에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"갈릴리 사람들이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 유다의 아들이오. 그대들의 무리에 나를 끼워 주겠소?\"\n\n\"싸워야 할지도 모르오.\" 그들이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇다면 나는 결코 먼저 달아나지 않겠소!\"\n\n그들은 그 응수를 흔쾌히 받아들였고, 전령이 말했다. \"보아하니 어지간히 다부지구려. 함께 갑시다.\"\n\n벤허는 겉옷을 벗었다.\n\n\"싸움이 벌어질지도 모른다고 생각하오?\" 그가 허리띠를 죄며 조용히 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"누구와?\"\n\n\"수비대와.\"\n\n\"군단병과?\"\n\n\"로마인이 그 밖에 누구를 믿을 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"무엇으로 싸울 작정이오?\"\n\n그들은 말없이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"음.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"우리는 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해야 할 거요. 그래도 우두머리를 하나 정하는 게 낫지 않겠소? 군단병들은 늘 우두머리가 있어서 한마음으로 움직일 수 있는 거요.\"\n\n갈릴리 사람들은, 처음 듣는 발상이기라도 한 듯, 더욱 궁금한 눈길로 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"적어도 함께 뭉쳐 있기로 합시다.\" 그가 말했다. \"자, 그대들이 준비됐다면 나도 준비됐소.\"\n\n\"좋소, 갑시다.\"\n\n잊지 말아야 할 것은, 그 객사가 새 시가지인 베제타에 있었다는 사실이다. 그래서 로마인들이 시온산 위의 헤롯 궁전을 거창하게 일컫던 프라이토리움에 이르려면, 그 무리는 성전 북쪽과 서쪽의 저지대를 가로질러야 했다. 남북으로 뻗은 거리들을—그렇게 부를 수 있다면—골목이라 할 위엄도 못 되는 교차로들과 함께 지나며, 그들은 아크라 지구를 빠르게 돌아 마리암네 탑에 이르렀으니, 거기서부터 성벽으로 둘러싸인 높은 곳의 웅장한 성문까지는 길이 짧았다. 가는 길에 그들은 자신들처럼 신성모독의 소식에 분노가 끓어오른 사람들을 앞지르기도 하고, 그들에게 추월당하기도 했다. 마침내 프라이토리움 성문에 다다랐을 때, 장로들과 랍비들의 행렬은 이미 큰 무리를 거느리고 안으로 들어간 뒤였고, 그보다 더 큰 군중이 밖에서 떠들썩하게 아우성치고 있었다.\n\n백인대장이 아름다운 대리석 흉벽 아래 완전 무장한 채 도열한 수비대를 거느리고 입구를 지키고 있었다. 햇살이 병사들의 투구와 방패에 뜨겁게 내리쬐었으나, 그들은 그 눈부신 빛에도, 천한 무리의 떠벌림에도 한결같이 무심한 채 대오를 지켰다. 활짝 열린 청동 문으로 시민들의 물결이 안으로 쏟아져 들어갔고, 그보다 훨씬 적은 물결이 밖으로 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 갈릴리 사람 하나가 나오는 이에게 물었다.\n\n\"아무 일도 없소.\" 대답이 돌아왔다. \"랍비들이 궁전 문 앞에서 빌라도를 만나게 해 달라고 청하고 있소. 그가 나오기를 거부했소. 그래서 한 사람을 보내, 그가 자기들의 말을 들어줄 때까지 물러가지 않겠다고 전했소. 지금 기다리는 중이오.\"\n\n\"들어갑시다.\" 벤허가 특유의 조용한 어조로 말했다. 그는 자기 동료들이 미처 보지 못한 것을 보았으니, 청원하는 자들과 총독 사이에 의견 차이가 있을 뿐 아니라, 쟁점이 맞부딪쳤고, 누구의 뜻이 관철될 것인가 하는 심각한 문제가 걸려 있다는 것이었다.\n\n성문 안쪽에는 잎이 무성한 나무들이 줄지어 서 있고, 그 아래 의자들이 놓여 있었다. 들어가는 이든 나오는 이든, 사람들은 희고 깨끗이 쓸린 포석 위에 고맙게도 드리워진 그늘을 애써 피했다. 기이하게 들릴지 모르나, 율법에서 비롯되었다고 일컬어지는 랍비들의 율례가 예루살렘 성벽 안에서는 어떤 푸른 것도 기르지 못하게 했기 때문이다. 전하는 바로는, 저 지혜로운 왕조차도 이집트인 신부를 위해 정원을 만들고 싶었으나, 엔로겔 위쪽 골짜기들이 만나는 자리 아래에 그것을 두지 않을 수 없었다고 한다.\n\n나무 우듬지 사이로 궁전의 바깥 정면이 빛났다. 오른쪽으로 돌아 그 무리는 잠시 나아가 널찍한 광장에 이르렀으니, 그 서쪽에 총독의 거처가 서 있었다. 흥분한 군중이 광장을 가득 메우고 있었다. 모든 얼굴이 닫힌 넓은 출입문 위에 세워진 주랑을 향하고 있었다. 그 주랑 아래에는 또 한 무리의 군단병이 도열해 있었다.\n\n군중이 어찌나 빽빽한지 그 벗들은 설령 그러고 싶었더라도 앞으로 나아가기 어려웠을 것이다. 그래서 그들은 뒤쪽에 남아 벌어지는 일을 지켜보았다. 주랑 둘레로 랍비들의 높다란 터번이 보였는데, 그들의 조바심이 이따금 뒤에 늘어선 무리에게 옮아갔다. \"빌라도여, 그대가 총독이라면 나오라, 나오라!\" 하는 외침이 거듭 터져 나왔다.\n\n한번은 밖으로 나온 사내가 분노로 얼굴이 벌게진 채 군중을 헤치고 지나갔다.\n\n\"여기서는 이스라엘이 아무것도 아니로구나.\" 그가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"이 거룩한 땅에서 우리는 로마의 개만도 못한 신세다.\"\n\n\"그가 나올 것 같지 않소?\"\n\n\"나오다니? 세 번이나 거절하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"랍비들은 어떻게 할 작정이오?\"\n\n\"카이사레아에서처럼—그가 귀를 기울일 때까지 여기서 진을 칠 거요.\"\n\n\"설마 그가 감히 보물에 손대지는 않겠지요?\" 갈릴리 사람 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"누가 알겠소? 로마인이 지성소를 더럽힌 적이 있지 않소? 로마인에게 거룩한 것이 무엇 하나라도 있겠소?\"\n\n한 시간이 지났으나, 빌라도가 그들에게 아무런 대답도 내려 주지 않았는데도 랍비들과 군중은 그대로 남아 있었다. 정오가 되자 서쪽에서 소나기가 몰려왔으나, 상황에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 다만 군중이 더 불어나고 훨씬 더 시끄러워졌으며, 그 감정이 한결 단호하게 분노로 기울었을 뿐이었다. 나오라, 나오라! 하는 고함이 거의 끊이지 않았다. 그 외침에는 때때로 불경한 변주가 섞였다. 그동안 벤허는 갈릴리 동지들을 한데 묶어 두었다. 그는 로마인의 자존심이 끝내 그 분별을 이기리라, 그리고 그 끝이 멀지 않으리라 판단했다. 빌라도는 백성이 자신에게 폭력에 호소할 구실을 줄 때까지 기다리고 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n마침내 그 끝이 왔다. 무리 한복판에서 매질하는 소리가 들렸고, 곧바로 고통과 분노의 비명, 그리고 더없이 격렬한 소동이 뒤따랐다. 주랑 앞에 선 노인들이 아연실색하여 돌아섰다. 뒤편의 보통 사람들은 처음에 앞으로 밀고 나갔다. 한복판에서는 빠져나가려는 안간힘이 일었다. 잠시 동안 맞부딪치는 두 힘의 압력이 끔찍했다. 일천의 목소리가 한꺼번에 일어나 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 아무도 대답할 겨를이 없으니, 놀라움은 삽시간에 공황으로 번졌다.\n\n벤허는 침착함을 잃지 않았다.\n\n\"안 보이오?\" 그가 갈릴리 사람 하나에게 말했다.\n\"안 보이오.\"\n\n\"내가 들어 올려 주겠소.\"\n\n그는 그 사내의 허리를 붙들어 번쩍 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"이제 보이오.\" 사내가 말했다. \"몽둥이로 무장한 자들이 있는데, 사람들을 두들겨 패고 있소. 유대인처럼 차려입었소.\"\n\n\"그들이 누구요?\"\n\n\"로마인이오, 주께서 살아 계심을 두고 맹세하건대! 변장한 로마인이오. 그들의 몽둥이가 도리깨처럼 휘날리고 있소! 저기, 랍비 하나가 쓰러지는 걸 보았소—노인인데! 그들은 아무도 봐주지 않소!\"\n\n벤허는 사내를 내려놓았다.\n\n\"갈릴리 사람들이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"이것은 빌라도의 술책이오. 자, 그대들이 내가 말하는 대로 한다면, 우리는 그 몽둥이꾼들에게 앙갚음할 수 있소.\"\n\n갈릴리 사람의 기상이 솟구쳤다.\n\n\"좋소, 좋소!\" 그들이 대답했다.\n\n\"성문 옆 나무들이 있는 데로 돌아갑시다. 헤롯이 율법을 어기고 심은 그 나무들이 결국 쓸모가 좀 있다는 걸 알게 될지도 모르오. 갑시다!\"\n\n그들 모두는 있는 힘껏 달려 되돌아갔다. 그리고 가지에 힘을 합쳐 매달려 몸무게를 실어, 줄기에서 가지를 뜯어냈다. 잠깐 사이에 그들 또한 무장을 갖추었다. 되돌아가는 길에, 광장 모퉁이에서 그들은 미친 듯이 성문으로 몰려오는 군중과 마주쳤다. 뒤에서는 비명과 신음과 저주가 뒤섞인 아우성이 계속 이어지고 있었다.\n\n\"벽으로!\" 벤허가 외쳤다. \"벽으로!—그리고 저 떼거리는 지나가게 두시오!\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 오른편 석벽에 바싹 붙어 밀려드는 군중의 힘을 피했고, 조금씩 앞으로 나아가 마침내 광장에 이르렀다.\n\n\"이제 함께 뭉쳐 나를 따르시오!\"\n\n이 무렵 벤허의 지휘는 완벽했다. 그가 들끓는 군중 속으로 밀고 들어가자, 그의 무리가 한 덩어리로 그 뒤를 따라 좁혀 들었다. 그리고 사람들을 몽둥이로 후려치며 쳐 쓰러뜨리는 것을 즐기던 로마인들이, 팔다리가 날렵하고 싸움에 굶주린 데다 똑같이 무장한 갈릴리 사람들과 맞붙게 되자, 이번에는 그들이 놀랄 차례였다. 그러자 함성이 바싹 다가들며 사나워졌고, 막대기 부딪치는 소리가 빠르고 치명적으로 울렸으며, 진격은 증오가 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 맹렬한 것이었다. 누구도 벤허만큼 제 몫을 해내지 못했으니, 그의 훈련이 그를 훌륭히 도왔다. 그는 단지 치고 막을 줄 아는 것만이 아니라, 긴 팔과 완벽한 동작과 견줄 데 없는 힘이 또한 매번의 격돌에서 그를 승리로 이끌었기 때문이다. 그는 싸우는 자이자 동시에 우두머리였다. 그가 휘두르는 몽둥이는 길이가 넉넉하고 묵직해서, 한 사내를 단 한 번 후려치는 것으로 충분했다. 게다가 그는 동지들의 격투 하나하나에까지 눈길이 닿는 듯했고, 가장 필요한 바로 그 순간 정확히 그 자리에 있는 재간을 지닌 듯했다. 그의 싸움터 외침에는 제 무리에게는 격려가, 적에게는 두려움이 담겨 있었다. 그리하여 불시에 당하고 힘으로도 대등하게 맞붙은 로마인들은 처음에는 물러서다가, 끝내 등을 돌려 주랑으로 달아났다. 혈기 넘치는 갈릴리 사람들은 그들을 계단까지 뒤쫓으려 했으나, 벤허가 슬기롭게 그들을 말렸다.\n\n\"멈추시오, 형제들!\" 그가 말했다. \"저기 백인대장이 수비대를 거느리고 오고 있소. 저들은 칼과 방패를 지녔으니, 우리는 그들과 싸울 수 없소. 우리는 잘 해냈소. 할 수 있을 때 되돌아가 성문 밖으로 빠져나갑시다.\"\n\n그들은, 비록 더디기는 했으나, 그의 말에 따랐다. 쓰러진 자리에 그대로 누운 동포들을 자주 넘어가야 했기 때문이다. 더러는 몸부림치며 신음했고, 더러는 도움을 구하며 빌었으며, 또 더러는 죽은 듯 말이 없었다. 그러나 쓰러진 자들이 모두 유대인은 아니었다. 거기에 위안이 있었다.\n\n백인대장이 그들이 떠나는 동안 그들에게 소리쳤다. 벤허는 그를 비웃으며 제 나라 말로 응수했다. \"우리가 이스라엘의 개라면, 그대들은 로마의 자칼이오. 여기 남아 있으시오, 우리가 다시 오겠소.\"\n\n갈릴리 사람들은 환호하며 웃음을 터뜨리고는 가던 길을 갔다.\n\n성문 밖에는 벤허가 일찍이, 안티오크의 원형 경기장에서조차 본 적 없는 엄청난 군중이 모여 있었다. 지붕들, 거리들, 언덕의 비탈이 통곡하며 기도하는 사람들로 빽빽이 뒤덮인 듯했다. 그들의 외침과 저주가 대기를 가득 채웠다.\n\n그 무리는 바깥 수비대의 제지 없이 통과할 수 있었다. 그러나 그들이 막 빠져나오자마자, 주랑을 지키던 백인대장이 나타나 성문에서 벤허에게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이봐, 건방진 놈! 너는 로마인이냐 유대인이냐?\"\n\n벤허가 대답했다. \"나는 유다의 아들이며, 여기서 태어났다. 나에게 무슨 볼일이냐?\"\n\n\"남아서 싸워라.\"\n\n\"일대일로?\"\n\n\"네 좋을 대로!\"\n\n벤허는 비웃듯 웃었다.\n\n\"오, 용감한 로마인이여! 사생아 로마 신 유피테르의 합당한 아들이로구나! 나는 무기가 없다.\"\n\n\"내 것을 주마.\" 백인대장이 대답했다. \"여기 수비대에게서 빌리겠다.\"\n\n그 문답을 듣고 있던 사람들이 조용해졌고, 그 침묵이 그들에게서 멀리 번져 나갔다. 바로 얼마 전 벤허는 안티오크와 동방 저편의 눈앞에서 로마인 하나를 꺾었다. 이제 예루살렘의 눈앞에서 또 한 사람을 꺾을 수 있다면, 그 영예는 새 왕의 대업에 더없이 유익할 터였다. 그는 망설이지 않았다. 거리낌 없이 백인대장에게 다가가며 그가 말했다. \"기꺼이 하겠다. 그대의 칼과 방패를 빌려다오.\"\n\n\"투구와 흉갑도?\" 로마인이 물었다.\n\n\"그건 그냥 둬라. 내게 맞지 않을 테니.\"\n\n무기는 그만큼이나 거리낌 없이 건네졌고, 곧 백인대장도 채비를 갖추었다. 그동안 내내 성문 가까이 도열한 병사들은 꿈쩍도 하지 않았으니, 그저 듣고 있을 뿐이었다. 군중으로 말하자면, 두 결투자가 싸움을 시작하려 나아갔을 때에야 비로소 \"저자는 누구냐?\" 하는 물음이 이 입에서 저 입으로 번졌다. 그러나 아무도 알지 못했다.\n\n자, 무기를 다루는 데 있어 로마인의 우월함은 세 가지에 있었다—규율에 대한 복종, 군단의 전투 대형, 그리고 단검의 독특한 사용법이었다. 싸움에서 그들은 결코 후려치거나 베지 않았으니, 처음부터 끝까지 찔렀다. 찌르며 나아가고 찌르며 물러섰다. 그리고 대개 그들의 겨냥은 적의 얼굴이었다. 이 모든 것을 벤허는 잘 알고 있었다. 그들이 맞붙으려 할 때 그가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 유다의 아들이라고 그대에게 말했지. 그러나 내가 검투사 사범에게 배웠다는 말은 하지 않았다. 막아 봐라!\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 벤허는 적수에게 달려들었다. 한순간, 두 사람은 발과 발을 맞댄 채 돋을새김한 방패의 테두리 너머로 서로를 노려보았다. 그러더니 로마인이 앞으로 밀고 들며 낮은 찌르기를 거짓 동작으로 시도했다. 유대인은 그를 비웃었다. 얼굴을 향한 찌르기가 뒤따랐다. 유대인은 가볍게 왼쪽으로 발을 옮겼다. 찌르기가 빨랐으나, 그 발놀림이 더 빨랐다. 적이 치켜든 팔 아래로 그는 방패를 밀어 넣어, 칼과 칼을 쥔 팔이 둘 다 그 윗면에 걸릴 때까지 들이밀었다. 또 한 걸음, 이번에는 앞으로, 그리고 왼쪽으로 내딛자, 그 사내의 오른쪽 옆구리 전체가 칼끝에 드러났다. 백인대장은 가슴을 짓찧으며 포석을 쟁그랑 울리며 무겁게 쓰러졌고, 벤허가 이겼다. 그는 적의 등을 발로 디딘 채, 검투사의 관습에 따라 방패를 머리 위로 치켜들고는, 성문 옆에 미동도 없는 병사들에게 경례를 보냈다.\n\n사람들이 그 승리를 깨닫자 미친 듯이 날뛰었다. 크시스토스에 이르기까지 멀리 지붕마다, 말이 날아갈 수 있는 한 빠르게, 그들은 숄과 손수건을 흔들며 환호했다. 그리고 만약 그가 허락했더라면, 갈릴리 사람들은 벤허를 어깨에 메고 갔으리라.\n\n그때 성문에서 나선 하급 장교에게 그가 말했다. \"그대의 동료는 군인답게 죽었다. 나는 그를 약탈하지 않은 채 남겨 둔다. 다만 그의 칼과 방패는 내 것이다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 걸어 나갔다. 조금 떨어진 곳에서 그는 갈릴리 사람들에게 말했다.\n\n\"형제들이여, 그대들은 잘 싸웠소. 이제 헤어집시다, 추격당하지 않도록 말이오. 오늘 밤 베다니의 객사에서 나를 만나 주시오. 이스라엘에 크게 이로운 일을 그대들에게 제안하고 싶소.\"\n\n\"당신은 누구요?\" 그들이 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"유다의 아들이오.\" 그가 간단히 대답했다.\n\n그를 보려고 안달하는 무리가 그 일행 둘레로 밀려들었다.\n\n\"베다니로 오겠소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 가겠소.\"\n\n\"그러면 이 칼과 방패를 가지고 오시오, 그래야 내가 그대들을 알아볼 수 있소.\"\n\n점점 불어나는 군중을 거칠게 헤치며, 그는 이내 자취를 감추었다.\n\n빌라도의 분부에 따라, 사람들은 도시에서 올라가 자기네 죽은 자와 다친 자를 거두어 갔고, 그들을 두고 통곡이 컸다. 그러나 그 슬픔은 알려지지 않은 투사의 승리로 크게 가벼워졌으니, 그 투사는 사방에서 수소문되었고 누구에게나 칭송받았다. 그 용맹한 행적으로 풀 죽었던 민족의 기상이 되살아났다. 그리하여 거리에서도, 또 성전 위에서도, 절기의 엄숙한 의식 한가운데서마저, 마카베오 시대의 옛이야기가 다시금 입에 올랐고, 수천의 사람들이 고개를 끄덕이며 슬기롭게 속삭였다.\n\n\"조금만 더, 형제들이여, 아주 조금만 더 기다리면, 이스라엘은 제 권리를 되찾을 것이오. 주를 믿고, 참읍시다.\"\n\n이런 식으로 벤허는 갈릴리에 발판을 마련했고, 장차 오실 왕의 대업에 더 큰 봉사를 바칠 길을 닦았다.\n\n그리하여 그 결과가 어떠했는지는 차차 보게 될 것이다.\n\n# BOOK SEVENTH\n\n“그리고 잠에서 깨어나, 나는 그곳에 있는 그녀를 보았네\n티끌 어린 대기 속에서 바다를 꿈꾸는 그녀를,\n\n# THOMAS BAILEY ALDRICH. CHAPTER I\n\n날렵하고 우아한 한 세이렌을,\n진홍빛 해초로 엮은 팔찌를 두르고,\n\n머리카락에는 바닷말의 길쭉하고 투명한\n호박빛 구슬을 빛내고 있는 그녀를.”\n\n토머스 베일리 올드리치.\n약속한 대로 만남은 베다니의 카라반사라이에서 이루어졌다. 그곳에서 벤허는 갈릴리 사람들과 함께 그들의 고향으로 들어갔으니, 옛 장터에서 떨친 그의 공적이 그에게 명성과 영향력을 안겨 주었다. 겨울이 다 가기 전에 그는 세 개의 군단을 일으켜 로마식으로 편성했다. 마음만 먹었다면 그보다 더 많이 거느릴 수도 있었으니, 그 용맹한 백성의 상무 정신은 결코 잠드는 법이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 일은 로마와 헤롯 안티파스 양쪽을 경계하며 조심스럽게 진행해야 했다. 그는 당분간 세 군단으로 만족하면서, 그들을 조직적인 행동에 맞게 훈련하고 가르치는 데 힘을 쏟았다. 그 목적을 위해 그는 장교들을 트라코니티스의 용암 지대로 데려가 무기 다루는 법, 특히 투창과 칼 쓰는 법, 그리고 군단 대형 특유의 기동을 가르쳤다. 그런 다음 교관으로 삼아 고향으로 돌려보냈다. 머지않아 그 훈련은 백성의 놀이가 되었다.\n\n짐작할 수 있듯이, 이 과업은 그에게 인내와 기술, 열성과 믿음과 헌신을 요구했다—어려운 일에서 남을 분발하게 만드는 힘은 언제나 이러한 자질로 환원되는 법인데, 일찍이 그 누구도 그보다 더 큰 정도로 이를 지녔거나 더 훌륭하게 발휘한 적이 없었다. 그가 얼마나 애를 썼던가! 그것도 자기를 철저히 버린 채로! 그러나 그 모든 노력에도 불구하고, 무기와 자금을 대 준 시모니데스와 망을 보며 보급품을 가져다준 일데림의 지원이 없었다면 그는 실패하고 말았을 것이다. 그리고 갈릴리 사람들의 천성이 없었더라도 여전히 실패했을 것이다.\n그 이름 아래에는 네 지파—아셀, 스불론, 잇사갈, 납달리—와 본래 그들에게 나뉘어 떨어진 지역들이 포괄되었다. 성전이 보이는 곳에서 태어난 유대인은 이 북방의 형제들을 멸시했다. 그러나 탈무드조차 이렇게 말했다. “갈릴리 사람은 명예를 사랑하고, 유대인은 돈을 사랑한다.”\n\n자기 고향을 사랑하는 만큼 로마를 격렬히 미워했기에, 그들은 어떤 반란에서든 가장 먼저 싸움터에 나서고 가장 나중에 떠났다. 로마와의 마지막 전쟁에서 갈릴리 젊은이 십오만 명이 목숨을 잃었다. 큰 축제일이면 그들은 군대처럼 행군하고 진을 치며 예루살렘으로 올라갔다. 그러면서도 그들은 정서가 너그러웠고, 심지어 이교도에게도 관대했다. 모든 면에서 로마식이던 헤롯의 아름다운 도시들, 특히 세포리스와 티베리아스를 자랑스러워했고, 그것을 짓는 데 충실히 힘을 보탰다. 그들은 바깥세상 곳곳에서 온 사람들을 동료 시민으로 두고 그들과 평화롭게 살았다. 히브리라는 이름의 영광을 위해 그들은 아가를 노래한 시인 같은 이들과 호세아 같은 예언자들을 배출했다.\n이처럼 재빠르고, 자긍심 높고, 용감하고, 헌신적이며, 상상력 풍부한 백성에게, 왕의 도래에 관한 이야기는 더없이 강력했다. 그가 로마를 무너뜨리러 온다는 것만으로도 벤허가 내놓은 계획에 그들을 끌어들이기에 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 그뿐만 아니라, 그가 카이사르보다 더 강하고 솔로몬보다 더 장엄하게 온 세상을 다스릴 것이며 그 통치가 영원히 이어지리라는 확언까지 들었으니, 그 호소는 거역할 수 없는 것이 되어, 그들은 몸과 영혼을 다해 그 대의에 스스로를 바쳤다. 그들은 벤허에게 그런 말들의 근거를 물었고, 그는 예언자들의 말을 인용하며 안티오크에서 기다리고 있는 발타사르에 대해 일러 주었다. 그러자 그들은 흡족해했으니, 그것은 메시아에 관한 오래되고 사랑받는 전설로, 그들에게는 주님의 이름만큼이나 친숙한 것이요, 실현될 때까지 정해진, 오래도록 품어 온 꿈이었기 때문이다. 이제 왕은 단지 오는 정도가 아니라, 바로 코앞에 와 있었다.\n\n이렇게 벤허에게 겨울 몇 달이 흘러갔고, 서쪽 여름 바다에서 불어온 반가운 소나기와 함께 봄이 찾아왔다. 그 무렵 그는 너무도 진지하고 성공적으로 애쓴 끝에, 스스로에게도 추종자들에게도 이렇게 말할 수 있었다. “선하신 왕이여, 오십시오. 어디에 왕좌를 세우려 하시는지 우리에게 말씀만 하시면 됩니다. 그것을 지켜 드릴 검의 손길은 우리에게 있습니다.”\n\n그리고 그 수많은 사람과의 모든 거래에서, 그들은 그를 오직 유다의 아들로만, 그 이름으로만 알고 있었다.\n\n어느 저녁, 트라코니티스에서 벤허는 자신이 머무는 동굴 어귀에 갈릴리 사람 몇과 함께 앉아 있었는데, 한 아랍인 전령이 말을 타고 그에게 다가와 편지 한 통을 건넸다. 봉함을 뜯고 그는 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n“예루살렘, 니산월 사일.\n\n“한 예언자가 나타났는데, 사람들은 그가 엘리야라고 합니다. 그는 여러 해 동안 광야에 있었으니, 우리 눈에도 그는 예언자입니다. 그의 말씀 또한 그러하여, 그 핵심은 자기보다 훨씬 위대한 한 분에 관한 것입니다. 그분이 곧 오시리라고 그는 말하며, 지금 요르단강 동편 기슭에서 그분을 기다리고 있습니다. 나는 그를 보고 그 말을 들으러 다녀왔는데, 그가 기다리는 그분은 분명 그대가 기다리는 왕입니다. 직접 와서 판단해 보십시오.\n\n“온 예루살렘이 그 예언자에게로 몰려가고 있으며, 그 밖에도 수많은 사람과 더불어, 그가 머무는 기슭은 마치 유월절 마지막 날들의 올리브산과 같습니다.\n“말루크.”\n\n벤허의 얼굴이 기쁨으로 붉게 달아올랐다.\n\n“오, 나의 벗들이여, 이 소식으로,” 그가 말했다—“이 소식으로 우리의 기다림은 끝이 났소. 왕의 선구자가 나타나 그분을 알렸소.”\n\n편지를 읽어 주는 것을 듣자, 그들 또한 그것이 품은 약속에 기뻐했다.\n\n“이제 채비를 하시오,” 그가 덧붙였다. “그리고 아침이 되면 고향으로 발걸음을 돌리시오. 거기 이르거든, 휘하의 사람들에게 기별을 보내, 내가 지시하는 대로 모일 수 있도록 준비하라 이르시오. 나와 그대들을 위하여, 나는 가서 왕이 정말로 가까이 오셨는지 살펴보고, 그대들에게 보고를 보내겠소. 그동안 우리, 그 약속의 기쁨 속에 살아갑시다.”\n\n동굴로 들어간 그는 일데림에게 편지 한 통을, 시모니데스에게 또 한 통을 써서, 받은 소식과 곧장 예루살렘으로 올라가려는 자신의 뜻을 알렸다. 그 편지들은 빠른 전령들 편에 부쳤다. 밤이 깃들고 방향을 일러 주는 별들이 떠오르자, 그는 말에 올라 아랍인 길잡이와 함께 요르단강을 향해 길을 떠났으니, 라바스암몬과 다마스쿠스 사이를 잇는 대상로를 타려는 작정이었다.\n\n길잡이는 미덥고 알데바란은 빨랐다. 그리하여 자정 무렵 두 사람은 용암 요새를 벗어나 남쪽으로 내달리고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n날이 밝으면 길을 벗어나 쉴 만한 안전한 곳을 찾는 것이 벤허의 생각이었다. 그러나 새벽은 그가 아직 사막 한가운데 있을 때 그를 따라잡았고, 그는 그대로 길을 이어 갔다. 안내인은 큰 바위들에 둘러싸인 골짜기로 곧 데려다주겠노라 약속했는데, 그곳에는 샘이 있고, 뽕나무가 몇 그루 있으며, 말들이 먹을 풀이 넉넉하다고 했다.\n\n곧 일어날 그 놀라운 일들과, 그 일들이 사람과 나라들의 운명에 가져올 변화를 생각하며 말을 몰고 가던 그에게, 늘 경계를 늦추지 않던 안내인이 뒤편에 낯선 자들이 나타났음을 일러 주었다. 사방으로 사막이 모래 물결을 이루며 멀리 뻗어 있었고, 그 물결은 밝아 오는 빛 속에서 서서히 누렇게 물들었으며, 푸른 것이라곤 하나도 보이지 않았다. 왼편으로는 아직 멀긴 했으나 낮은 산줄기가 이어졌는데, 보아하니 끝이 없는 듯했다. 그처럼 텅 빈 황무지에서 움직이는 물체가 오래도록 수수께끼로 남을 수는 없었다.\n\n\"사람들을 태운 낙타입니다.\" 안내인이 곧바로 말했다.\n\n\"그 뒤에 다른 자들은 없느냐?\" 벤허가 물었다.\n\n\"한 마리뿐입니다. 아니, 말을 탄 사람이 하나 있군요—몰이꾼인 듯합니다.\"\n\n조금 뒤에는 벤허 자신도 그 낙타가 희고 유난히 크다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었으니, 다프네 숲의 샘으로 발타사르와 이라스를 데려오던 그 놀라운 짐승이 떠올랐다. 그런 낙타가 또 있을 리 없었다. 그러자 그 아리따운 이집트 여인이 생각나, 자기도 모르게 걸음이 느려지더니, 마침내는 거의 어슬렁거리는 지경에 이르렀고, 끝내는 휘장을 드리운 가마와 그 안에 앉은 두 사람을 분간할 수 있었다. 만일 발타사르와 이라스라면! 자신을 그들에게 알려야 할까? 그러나 그럴 리는 없었다. 이곳은 사막이었고—그들은 단둘이었다. 그러나 그가 이 물음을 두고 망설이는 사이, 낙타의 길고 흔들거리는 걸음이 탄 이들을 그에게로 데려왔다. 그는 작은 방울들이 딸랑거리는 소리를 들었고, 카스탈리아 샘가의 군중을 그토록 끌어당기던 화려한 장구를 보았다. 또한 늘 이집트인들을 시중드는 에티오피아인도 보였다. 그 키 큰 짐승은 그의 말 바로 곁에 멈춰 섰고, 벤허가 고개를 들어 올려다보니, 이런! 이라스가 걷어 올린 휘장 아래에서 그를 내려다보고 있었으니, 크고 촉촉한 두 눈이 놀라움과 궁금증으로 빛나고 있었다!\n\n\"참된 하느님의 축복이 그대에게 있기를!\" 발타사르가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그대와 그대의 사람들에게도 주님의 평화가 깃들기를.\" 벤허가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 눈은 나이가 들어 흐릿하오.\" 발타사르가 말했다. \"그러나 이 눈도 그대가, 얼마 전 내가 너그러운 일데림의 천막에서 귀한 손님으로 뵌 후르의 아들임을 알아보는구려.\"\n\n\"그러시면 어르신은 그 발타사르, 곧 지혜로운 이집트인이시군요. 기다려 마지않는 어떤 거룩한 일들에 관한 어르신의 말씀이, 저를 이 황무지에서 만나게 한 일과 그토록 깊이 얽혀 있습니다. 어르신은 여기서 무얼 하고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"하느님이 계신 곳에 있는 자는 결코 혼자가 아니라오—그리고 하느님은 어디에나 계시지요.\" 발타사르가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그러나 그대가 묻는 뜻으로 말하자면, 우리 뒤로 멀지 않은 곳에 알렉산드리아로 가는 대상이 하나 있소. 그 대상이 예루살렘을 지나가기에, 내가 가는 성도(聖都)까지는 그 일행에 끼는 것이 좋겠다 싶었소. 그런데 오늘 아침, 그 더딘 걸음에—거기 딸린 로마 보병대 때문에 더더욱 더뎠지요—마음이 답답해, 우리는 일찍 일어나 이렇게 앞질러 나섰다오. 길의 도둑들이라면 두렵지 않으니, 내게 일데림 족장의 인장이 있기 때문이오. 들짐승들에 대해서는 하느님이 우리의 넉넉한 의지가 되어 주신다오.\"\n\n벤허는 고개를 숙이고 말했다. \"그 어진 족장의 인장은 황야가 뻗은 곳이라면 어디서나 안전을 지켜 줄 것입니다. 또한 이 짐승, 그 종족의 왕을 따라잡을 사자라면 무던히도 날쌔야 할 것입니다.\"\n\n그는 이렇게 말하며 낙타의 목을 토닥였다.\n\n\"그렇지만요,\" 이라스가 미소를 띠며 말했는데, 그 미소는 젊은이의 눈길을 놓치지 않았으니, 솔직히 말해 그의 눈은 노인과 말을 주고받는 동안 몇 번이나 그 여인에게로 향했던 것이다. \"그렇지만 이 짐승도 끼니를 채운다면 한결 나아질 거예요. 왕에게도 시장기와 두통은 있는 법이지요. 그대가 정녕 제 아버지께서 말씀하신 그 벤허이고, 저 또한 알게 되어 기뻤던 바로 그분이라면, 사막에서 아침상을 차릴 수 있도록 그 반짝이는 물이 솟는 가까운 샘으로 우리를 데려다주시는 일이 분명 그대에게도 기쁨이 되리라 믿어요.\"\n\n벤허는 마다하지 않고 서둘러 대답했다.\n\n\"아리따운 이집트 여인이시여, 그 딱한 사정에 마음이 갑니다. 조금만 더 견디실 수 있다면 찾으시는 샘을 우리가 찾아낼 것이고, 그 한 모금이 저 이름난 카스탈리아의 물만큼이나 달고 시원하리라 약속드립니다. 허락하신다면, 어서 서두르지요.\"\n\n\"목마른 자의 축복을 그대에게 드리지요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그 답례로는 도시의 화덕에서 구운 빵 한 조각을, 이슬 맺힌 다마스쿠스 목초지에서 짜낸 신선한 버터에 적셔 드리겠어요.\"\n\n\"더없이 귀한 호의로군요! 자, 갑시다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 벤허는 안내인과 더불어 앞으로 말을 몰았다. 낙타와 함께 다니는 일의 불편함 가운데 하나는, 정중한 대화를 어쩔 수 없이 가로막는다는 데 있었다.\n\n얼마 뒤 일행은 얕은 와디에 이르렀고, 안내인은 오른쪽으로 꺾어 그 골을 따라 그들을 이끌었다. 갈라진 바닥은 얼마 전 내린 비로 다소 무른 데다, 가파르게 내리쏠렸다. 그러나 이내 폭이 넓어졌고, 머지않아 양옆은 절벽을 이루었으니, 앞쪽 더 낮은 곳으로 쏟아져 내리던 급류에 깊이 패인 바위들이 갈빗대처럼 박혀 있었다. 마침내 좁은 통로를 빠져나오자 나그네들은 넓게 펼쳐진 골짜기로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 무척이나 아름다웠다. 그러나 누렇고 단조롭고 푸른 기운이라곤 없는 평원에서 갑작스레 마주친 터라, 마치 새로 발견한 낙원과도 같은 느낌을 주었다. 여기저기 굽이치는 물줄기는 바삭바삭한 흰 자갈로 분간할 수 있었는데, 풀빛으로 푸르고 갈대로 가장자리를 두른 섬들 사이에 엉킨 실들처럼 보였다. 요르단 골짜기의 깊디깊은 바닥에서부터 겁 없는 협죽도가 몇 그루 기어올라, 이제 그 큼직한 꽃송이로 이 움푹한 곳을 별처럼 수놓고 있었다. 종려나무 한 그루가 왕처럼 당당히 솟아 있었다. 경계를 이루는 벼랑의 밑동은 기어오르는 덩굴들로 뒤덮여 있었고, 왼편으로 비스듬히 기운 절벽 아래에는 뽕나무 숲이 자리를 잡아, 일행이 찾던 그 샘이 거기 있음을 알려 주었다. 안내인은 그곳으로 그들을 이끌었으니, 갈대 우거진 덤불에서 푸드덕 날아오르며 휘파람 같은 소리를 내는 자고새들이나, 더 고운 빛깔의 작은 새들에는 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n물은 절벽의 갈라진 틈에서 솟아났는데, 어느 사랑 깊은 손길이 그것을 아치 모양의 우묵한 공간으로 넓혀 놓았다. 그 위에는 굵직한 히브리 글자로 \"하느님\"이라는 말이 새겨져 있었다. 그것을 새긴 이는 분명 거기서 물을 마셨고, 여러 날 머물렀으며, 그 변치 않는 형태로 감사를 드렸으리라. 아치에서 흘러나온 물줄기는 산뜻한 이끼가 점점이 박힌 넓적한 돌 위를 경쾌하게 지나, 거울처럼 맑은 웅덩이로 뛰어들었다. 거기서 다시 풀 우거진 둑 사이로 슬며시 빠져나가, 목마른 모래 속으로 자취를 감추기 전에 나무들을 길러 냈다. 웅덩이 가장자리로는 좁다란 길이 몇 가닥 보였으나, 그 밖의 둘레는 사람의 발길이 닿지 않은 잔디밭이었다. 그 모습을 본 안내인은 사람의 방해를 받지 않고 쉴 수 있으리라 확신했다. 말들은 곧 풀어 놓았고, 꿇어앉은 낙타에서 에티오피아인이 발타사르와 이라스를 내려 주었다. 그러자 노인은 동쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 두 손을 경건하게 가슴 위에 포개어 기도했다.\n\n\"잔을 가져오너라.\" 이라스가 다소 조급한 기색으로 말했다.\n\n종이 가마에서 수정 잔을 가져오자, 그녀는 벤허에게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 샘가에서 그대의 시중을 들겠어요.\"\n\n두 사람은 함께 웅덩이로 걸어갔다. 그가 물을 떠 주려 했으나 그녀는 그 호의를 마다하고, 무릎을 꿇은 채 물줄기 그 자체가 잔을 채우도록 받쳐 들었다. 그것으로도 모자라, 물이 차고 시원해져 넘쳐흐르자 그녀는 첫 모금을 그에게 권했다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 그는 그 우아한 손을 옆으로 밀어내며 말했는데, 치켜올린 눈썹의 활 아래 반쯤 가려진 그 큰 두 눈만이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. \"부디 그 시중은 제 몫이 되게 해 주시지요.\"\n\n그녀는 한사코 자기 뜻을 굽히지 않았다.\n\n\"제 나라에는, 오 후르의 아들이여, 이런 말이 있답니다. '왕을 섬기는 신하가 되느니 행운아의 술잔을 받드는 자가 되라'고요.\"\n\n\"행운아라니요!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그 목소리에도 그 표정에도 놀라움과 궁금증이 함께 어려 있었으니, 그녀가 얼른 말했다.\n\n\"신들은 우리에게 성공을 내려, 그것을 표징 삼아 그분들이 우리 편임을 알게 하시지요. 그대는 원형경기장에서 승리한 분이 아니던가요?\"\n\n그의 두 뺨이 붉어지기 시작했다.\n\n\"그것이 하나의 표징이지요. 또 하나가 있어요. 칼을 맞댄 싸움에서 그대는 로마인을 베어 죽이셨지요.\"\n\n홍조가 더욱 짙어졌다—그 승리들 자체 때문이라기보다, 그녀가 자신의 행적을 관심 있게 좇아왔다는 생각에 깃든 그 은근한 추어줌 때문이었다. 잠시 뒤, 그 기쁨은 한 가닥 생각에 자리를 내주었다. 그 싸움은 동방 전역에 소문이 퍼졌음을 그도 알고 있었다. 그러나 승자의 이름은 극히 몇몇 사람—말루크, 일데림, 시모니데스에게만 맡겨져 있었다. 그들이 이 여인에게 속을 털어놓았을까? 그리하여 그는 의아함과 흐뭇함이 뒤섞여 어쩔 줄 몰랐다. 그것을 알아챈 그녀가 자리에서 일어나, 잔을 웅덩이 위로 받쳐 들고 말했다.\n\n\"오 이집트의 신들이시여! 한 영웅을 찾아낸 것에 감사드립니다—이데르네 궁에서 희생된 자가 제 사내들의 왕이 아니었음에 감사드립니다. 하여, 오 거룩한 신들이시여, 저는 이렇게 따라 마십니다.\"\n\n그녀는 잔에 든 것을 일부는 다시 샘물로 돌려보내고, 나머지는 마셨다. 입술에서 수정 잔을 떼며, 그녀는 그를 보고 웃었다.\n\n\"오, 후르의 아들이여, 한낱 여인에게 이토록 쉽게 무너지는 것이 참으로 용감한 자의 풍습인가요? 자, 이제 잔을 받으세요. 거기서 저를 위한 즐거운 한마디를 찾아낼 수 있는지 보세요.\"\n\n그는 잔을 받아 들고, 다시 채우려 몸을 숙였다.\n\n\"이스라엘의 아들에게는 헌주를 바칠 신이 없습니다.\" 그는 이렇게 말하며, 전보다 한층 커진 놀라움을 감추려 물장난을 쳤다. 이 이집트 여인은 자신에 관해 그밖에 또 무엇을 알고 있는 것일까? 시모니데스와의 관계도 들었을까? 게다가 일데림과의 약조가 있었는데, 그것까지 알고 있는 것일까? 그는 불신에 사로잡혔다. 누군가 그의 비밀을 누설한 것이고, 그 비밀은 결코 가벼운 것이 아니었다. 더구나 그는 지금 예루살렘으로 가는 길이었으니, 적이 그런 정보를 쥐고 있을 때 자신과 동지들과 대의에 가장 위험할 수 있는 곳이 바로 그곳이었다. 그러나 그녀가 과연 적일까? 글쓰기는 더디나 생각은 순간이라는 것이 우리에게는 다행한 일이다. 잔이 충분히 식자 그는 잔을 채우고 일어서며, 짐짓 무심한 척 말했다.\n\n\"더없이 아리따운 분이여, 제가 이집트인이거나 그리스인이거나 로마인이었다면 이렇게 말했겠지요.\" 그는 말하면서 잔을 머리 위로 들어 올렸다. \"오, 그대들 선한 신들이여! 이 세상이 온갖 부조리와 고통을 겪으면서도, 아직 아름다움의 매력과 사랑의 위안이 남아 있음에 감사드리나이다. 그리하여 나는 그것들을 가장 잘 드러내는 이를 위해 마시노라. 나일강의 딸들 가운데 가장 사랑스러운 이라스를 위하여!\"\n\n그녀는 그의 어깨 위에 살며시 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"당신은 율법을 어겼어요. 당신이 위해 마신 신들은 거짓 신들이에요. 제가 랍비들에게 당신을 일러바치면 어떡하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"오!\" 그는 웃으며 대답했다. \"정말로 중요한 다른 일들을 그토록 많이 아시는 분에게는, 그것쯤은 일러바칠 거리도 못 되지요.\"\n\n\"한 걸음 더 나아가겠어요. 안티오크 건너편, 그 위대한 상인의 집에서 장미를 피우고 그림자를 불타오르게 하는 작은 유대인 처녀에게 가겠어요. 랍비들에게는 당신이 뉘우칠 줄 모른다고 고발하고, 그녀에게는……\"\n\n\"그래, 그녀에게는?\"\n\n\"잔을 들어 올린 채, 신들을 증인 삼아 저에게 하신 말씀을 그대로 되풀이해 주겠어요.\"\n\n그는 잠시 가만히 있었다. 마치 이집트 여인이 말을 잇기를 기다리는 듯했다. 빨라진 상상 속에서 그는 아버지 곁에서 자신이 보낸 전갈에 귀 기울이는 에스더를, 때로는 그것을 읽는 에스더를 떠올렸다. 그녀가 있는 자리에서 그는 시모니데스에게 이데르네 궁에서 벌어진 사건의 전말을 들려준 적이 있었다. 그녀와 이라스는 서로 아는 사이였다. 이쪽은 영리하고 세상물정에 밝았고, 저쪽은 순박하고 정이 많아 그만큼 쉽게 마음을 얻을 수 있었다. 시모니데스가 신의를 저버렸을 리 없고, 일데림도 마찬가지였다. 명예에 매여 있지 않더라도, 비밀이 드러났을 때의 결과가 더 무겁고 확실하게 닥칠 사람은 자신 말고는 아무도 없었으니 말이다. 에스더가 이집트 여인에게 알려준 사람일 수 있을까? 그는 그녀를 탓하지는 않았다. 그러나 그 생각과 더불어 의심의 씨가 뿌려졌고, 누구나 알다시피 의심이란 마음속에서 절로 자라나는 잡초이며, 가장 원치 않을 때 가장 빠르게 자라는 법이다. 그가 작은 유대인 처녀에 대한 암시에 미처 대답하기도 전에, 발타사르가 샘가로 다가왔다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이여, 우리는 그대에게 큰 신세를 졌소.\" 그는 진중한 태도로 말했다. \"이 골짜기는 참으로 아름답구려. 풀과 나무와 그늘이 우리에게 머물러 쉬어 가라 권하고, 여기 이 샘은 움직이는 다이아몬드처럼 반짝이며 사랑이 넘치는 하느님을 내게 노래해 주는구려. 우리가 누리는 이 즐거움에 대해 그저 감사하다는 말만으로는 부족하오. 와서 우리와 함께 앉아 우리의 빵을 맛보시오.\"\n\n\"먼저 제가 모시게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n그러면서 벤허는 잔을 채워 발타사르에게 건넸고, 발타사르는 감사의 뜻으로 눈을 들어 올렸다.\n\n곧 종이 수건을 가져왔다. 세 사람은 손을 씻고 닦은 뒤, 동방식으로 천막 아래 자리를 잡았으니, 그것은 여러 해 전 사막에서 만났던 동방박사들을 받들어 모셨던 바로 그 천막이었다. 그리고 그들은 낙타의 짐에서 꺼낸 좋은 음식들을 마음껏 먹었다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n천막은 시냇물 소리가 끊임없이 귀에 닿는 나무 아래 아늑하게 쳐져 있었다. 머리 위에서는 넓은 잎사귀들이 줄기에 매달린 채 미동도 없었고, 진줏빛 아지랑이 너머 가녀린 갈대 줄기들은 화살처럼 곧게 서 있었다. 이따금 집으로 돌아가는 벌 한 마리가 윙윙거리며 그늘을 가로질러 쏜살같이 지나갔고, 사초 사이에서 기어 나온 자고새 한 마리가 물을 마시고는 짝을 향해 휘파람을 불고 달아났다. 골짜기의 안온함, 공기의 신선함, 정원의 아름다움, 안식일 같은 고요—이 모든 것이 나이 든 이집트인의 마음을 움직인 듯했다. 그의 목소리며 몸짓이며 태도가 유난히 부드러웠다. 그리고 이라스와 이야기를 나누는 벤허에게 눈길을 보낼 때면, 그 눈빛은 자주 연민으로 누그러졌다.\n\n\"우리가 그대를 따라잡았을 때, 후르의 아들이여,\" 식사가 끝날 무렵 그가 말했다. \"그대의 얼굴 또한 예루살렘을 향하고 있는 듯했네. 무례가 아니라면, 그토록 먼 곳까지 가는 길인지 물어도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"성도(聖都)로 가는 길입니다.\"\n\n\"긴 여정의 수고를 덜어야 할 큰 사정이 있어 한 가지 더 묻겠네. 랍밧암몬을 거치는 길보다 더 가까운 길이 있는가?\"\n\n\"거친 길이긴 하나 더 가까운 길이 게라사와 랍밧길르앗을 지나 있습니다. 제가 가려는 길이 바로 그 길입니다.\"\n\n\"마음이 조급하다네,\" 발타사르가 말했다. \"요사이 잠을 자면 꿈이 찾아드네—아니, 같은 꿈이 거듭 찾아든다고 해야겠지. 어떤 목소리가—그저 목소리일 뿐이네—찾아와 이르더군. '서둘러라, 일어나라! 네가 그토록 오래 기다려 온 분이 가까이 와 계신다.'\"\n\n\"유대인의 왕이 되실 분을 말씀하시는 겁니까?\" 벤허가 놀라움에 차서 이집트인을 바라보며 물었다.\n\"바로 그러하네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그분에 관해 아무 소식도 듣지 못하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아무것도 듣지 못했네. 꿈속 목소리의 말 외에는.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 여기, 제가 그러했듯 어른께도 기쁨을 안겨 드릴 소식이 있습니다.\"\n\n벤허는 자기 옷에서 말루크에게서 받은 편지를 꺼냈다. 이집트인이 내민 손이 심하게 떨렸다. 그는 소리 내어 읽었고, 읽어 가면서 감정이 북받쳤다. 목의 늘어진 핏줄이 부풀어 올라 고동쳤다. 다 읽고 나자 그는 눈물 어린 눈을 들어 감사와 기도를 올렸다. 그는 아무것도 묻지 않았으나, 아무런 의심도 없었다.\n\n\"주여, 당신은 제게 참으로 자비로우셨나이다,\" 그가 말했다. \"비오니, 구주를 다시 뵙고 그분을 경배하게 하소서. 그리하면 당신의 종은 평안히 떠날 채비가 되겠나이다.\"\n\n그 말, 그 태도, 그 소박한 기도의 남다른 인격이 벤허에게 새롭고도 오래 남을 느낌을 안겨 주었다. 하느님이 이토록 실재하고 가까이 계신 듯 여겨진 적은 일찍이 없었다. 마치 그분이 그곳에서 그들 위로 몸을 굽히고 계시거나 곁에 앉아 계신 것만 같았다—더없이 격식 없는 청만으로도 은혜를 베푸시는 벗, 모든 자식을 똑같이 사랑하시는 아버지, 유대인의 아버지인 동시에 이방인의 아버지인, 중개자도 랍비도 사제도 교사도 필요로 하지 않으시는 만민의 아버지. 그런 하느님이 인류에게 왕이 아니라 구주를 보내실지도 모른다는 생각이, 단지 새로울 뿐 아니라 너무도 분명하게 벤허에게 다가왔다. 그래서 그는 그러한 선물에 대한 더 큰 갈망과, 그러한 신성에 그것이 더 잘 어울린다는 사실을 거의 또렷이 분별할 수 있을 정도였다. 그리하여 그는 묻지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"이제 그분이 오셨는데도, 발타사르 어른, 어른께서는 여전히 그분이 왕이 아니라 구주가 되실 거라고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n발타사르는 다정하면서도 사려 깊은 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그대의 말을 어찌 알아들어야 할까?\" 그가 되물었다. \"옛적 나의 인도자였던 별, 곧 그 영(靈)은 내가 그 어진 족장의 천막에서 그대를 만난 이래로 다시는 내게 나타나지 않았네. 다시 말해, 예전처럼 그것을 보거나 듣지 못했다는 말일세. 꿈속에서 내게 이른 그 목소리가 바로 그것이라고 나는 믿네. 허나 그 외에는 어떤 계시도 받지 못했네.\"\n\n\"우리 사이의 차이를 다시 짚어 보겠습니다,\" 벤허가 정중하게 말했다. \"어른께서는 그분이 왕이 되시되 카이사르 같은 왕은 아닐 거라고 보셨지요. 그 통치권이 세속의 것이 아니라 영적인 것이리라 여기셨습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇다네,\" 이집트인이 대답했다. \"그리고 지금도 같은 생각일세. 우리 믿음이 어디서 갈라지는지 나는 보네. 그대는 사람들의 왕을 만나러 가는 것이고, 나는 영혼들의 구주를 만나러 가는 것이지.\"\n\n그는 사람들이 너무 높아 얼른 분별하기 어렵거나 너무 미묘해 단순히 표현하기 어려운 생각을 안으로 더듬어 풀어내려 애쓸 때 흔히 보이는 그런 표정으로 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"내가 한번 애써 보겠네, 후르의 아들이여,\" 그가 곧바로 말했다. \"그대가 내 믿음을 분명히 깨닫도록 도와주겠네. 그러면 내가 그분이 세우시리라 기대하는 영적 왕국이 어떤 의미로 보아도 한낱 카이사르식 화려함보다 더 뛰어날 수 있음을 알게 되어, 우리가 맞이하러 가는 그 신비로운 분에게 내가 품은 관심의 까닭을 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이네.\n\n\"사람마다 영혼이 깃들어 있다는 생각이 언제 비롯되었는지는 나도 말할 수 없네. 아마도 최초의 부모가 처음 거하던 동산에서 그것을 지니고 나왔을 게야. 그러나 그 생각이 사람의 마음에서 완전히 사라진 적은 한 번도 없다는 것을 우리 모두 알고 있네. 어떤 민족은 그것을 잃었으나 모든 민족이 그런 것은 아니었네. 어떤 시대에는 그것이 흐려지고 바래었으며, 어떤 시대에는 의심에 휩싸였네. 그러나 큰 자비로, 하느님은 때마다 우리에게 위대한 지성을 보내시어 그것을 논하여 다시 믿음과 소망으로 이끄셨네.\n\n\"어찌하여 사람마다 영혼이 깃들어 있어야 하는가? 보게나, 후르의 아들이여—그러한 장치가 어찌하여 필요한지 잠시만 들여다보게. 누워 죽어 더는 존재하지 않는 것—영영 사라지는 것—사람이 그런 종말을 바란 때는 일찍이 없었네. 또한 마음속으로 더 나은 무언가를 스스로 약속하지 않은 사람도 없었네. 여러 민족의 기념비는 모두 죽음 뒤의 허무에 대한 항거일세. 조각상과 비문도 그러하고, 역사 또한 그러하네. 우리 이집트의 가장 위대한 왕은 단단한 바위 언덕을 깎아 자기 형상을 만들게 했네. 날마다 그는 수많은 전차를 거느리고 그 작업을 보러 갔지. 마침내 그것이 완성되었으니, 그토록 웅장하고 그토록 오래갈 형상은 일찍이 없었네. 그것은 그를 빼닮았고—이목구비가 그의 것이었으며, 표정마저 충실했네. 자, 그 자랑스러운 순간에 그가 이렇게 말했으리라고 생각해 볼 수 있지 않겠나. '죽음이여 오라. 내게는 사후의 삶이 있도다!' 그는 소원을 이루었네. 그 조각상은 지금도 그곳에 있으니.\n\n\"그러나 그가 그렇게 얻은 사후의 삶이란 무엇인가? 그저 사람들의 기억일 뿐—그 거대한 흉상의 이마에 비친 달빛처럼 실체 없는 영광일 뿐. 돌에 새겨진 이야기—그 이상은 아무것도 아니네. 그동안 왕 자신은 어찌 되었는가? 왕릉에는 한때 그의 것이었던, 방부 처리된 몸뚱이 하나가 있네—사막에 있는 저 형상만큼 보기 좋지도 않은 형상이지. 그러나, 후르의 아들이여, 왕 자신은 어디에 있는가? 그는 허무 속으로 떨어졌는가? 그가 그대와 나처럼 살아 있는 사람이었던 때로부터 이천 년이 흘렀네. 그의 마지막 숨이 곧 그의 끝이었는가?\n\n\"그렇다고 말하는 것은 하느님을 탓하는 일이 되리니, 차라리 우리에게 사후의 삶—내 말은 실제의 삶—을 얻게 하시려는 그분의 더 나은 계획을 받아들이세. 죽을 자들의 기억 속 한자리 그 이상의 것, 오감과 가고 옴이 있고, 지식이 있고, 능력과 온갖 향유가 있는 삶, 비록 상태의 변화는 있을지언정 그 기한은 영원한 삶 말일세.\n\n\"하느님의 계획이 무엇이냐고 묻는가? 태어날 때 우리 각자에게 영혼을 주시되, 이 한 가지 단순한 법을 두신 것일세—영혼을 통하지 않고는 어떤 불멸도 없으리라는 법. 그 법 안에 내가 말한 그 필요가 있네.\n\n\"이제 그 필요에서 눈을 돌려 보세. 우리 각자에게 영혼이 깃들어 있다는 생각 속에 있는 기쁨에 관해 한마디 하겠네. 우선 그것은 죽음에서 그 공포를 앗아 가네. 죽는 것을 더 나은 쪽으로의 변화로, 묻히는 것을 새 생명이 움터 나올 씨앗을 심는 일로 만들기 때문이지. 다음으로, 지금 내 모습을 보게나—약하고, 지치고, 늙고, 몸은 쪼그라들었으며, 볼품없네. 내 주름진 얼굴을 보게, 쇠해 가는 내 감각을 생각해 보게, 내 새된 목소리에 귀 기울여 보게. 아! 무덤이 곧 열려, 내가 나라고 부르는 이 닳아 버린 껍질을 받아들일 때, 곧 하느님의 궁전에 다름 아닌 우주의, 지금은 보이지 않는 문이 활짝 열려 해방된 불멸의 영혼인 나를 맞아들이리라는 약속, 그것이 내게 주는 행복이 어떠하겠는가!\n\n\"다가올 그 삶 속에 있을 황홀을 내가 다 말할 수 있다면 좋으련만! 내가 그에 관해 아무것도 모른다고는 말하지 말게. 이만큼은 내가 아네. 그리고 내게는 그것으로 충분하네—영혼이 된다는 것은 신성한 우월함의 조건을 함의한다는 것을. 그러한 존재 안에는 티끌 한 점도, 어떤 거친 것도 없네. 그것은 공기보다 더 미세하고, 빛보다 더 만질 수 없으며, 정수보다 더 순수할 것일세—그것은 더없이 순수한 삶이라네.\n\n\"그렇다면, 후르의 아들이여, 이만큼 알고 있으면서 굳이 그 불필요한 것들을 두고 나 자신과, 또는 그대와 다투어야 하겠는가—내 영혼의 형태를 두고? 또는 그것이 어디에 머물 것인지를 두고? 또는 그것이 먹고 마시는지를 두고? 또는 날개가 있는지, 이런저런 것을 걸치는지를 두고? 아닐세. 하느님을 믿는 것이 더 마땅하네. 이 세상의 아름다운 것은 모두 그분의 손에서 나와 그 취향의 완전함을 드러내네. 그분은 모든 형태의 창조자이시며, 백합을 입히시고, 장미를 물들이시고, 이슬방울을 빚으시고, 자연의 음악을 지으시네. 한마디로, 그분은 이 삶을 위해 우리를 빚으시고 그 조건을 정하셨으니, 그것이 내게는 너무도 든든한 보증이라, 나는 어린아이처럼 믿음을 품고 내 영혼의 빚음과 사후의 삶을 위한 온갖 마련을 그분께 맡기네. 나는 그분이 나를 사랑하심을 아네.\"\n\n이 어진 사람은 말을 멈추고 물을 마셨는데, 잔을 입으로 가져가는 손이 떨렸다. 이라스와 벤허도 그의 감동을 함께 나누며 잠자코 있었다. 벤허에게는 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐 들고 있었다. 그는 일찍이 없었던 방식으로 깨닫기 시작했다. 어떤 지상의 제국보다도 사람들에게 더 중대한 영적 왕국이 있을 수 있으며, 결국 구주야말로 가장 위대한 왕보다 참으로 더 신성한 선물이리라는 것을.\n\n\"이제 그대에게 물어볼 수도 있겠네,\" 발타사르가 말을 이었다. \"이토록 고달프고 짧은 인간의 삶이, 영혼을 위해 마련된 완전하고 영원한 삶보다 더 나은가를 말일세. 허나 그 물음을 받아 들고, 이렇게 정리하여 스스로 생각해 보게. 두 삶이 똑같이 행복하다고 가정할 때, 한 시간이 한 해보다 더 바람직한가? 거기서 다시 마지막 물음으로 나아가 보게. 지상의 일흔 해가 하느님과 함께하는 영원에 비하면 무엇이겠는가? 머지않아, 후르의 아들이여, 그런 식으로 생각하다 보면, 내가 다음으로 제시하는 사실의 의미로 그대 마음이 가득 차게 될 걸세. 그것은 내게 모든 사건 가운데 가장 놀라운 것이요, 그 영향에서 가장 슬픈 것이네. 곧 영혼으로서의 삶이라는 생각 자체가 세상에서 거의 꺼져 버린 빛이라는 사실 말일세. 물론 여기저기서, 영혼을 두고 그것을 하나의 원리에 빗대어 그대에게 이야기할 철학자를 더러 만날 수도 있겠지. 그러나 철학자들은 어떤 것도 믿음으로 받아들이지 않기에, 영혼을 하나의 존재라고 인정하는 데까지는 나아가지 않을 것이네. 그런 까닭에 그들에게는 영혼의 목적이 빽빽한 어둠일 뿐이지.\n\n\"숨 쉬는 모든 것은 제 필요만큼 헤아릴 수 있는 마음을 지니고 있네. 미래를 온전히 헤아릴 능력이 오직 사람에게만 주어졌다는 그 남다름이 그대에게는 아무 의미도 없는가? 내가 보기에 그 표징으로, 하느님은 우리가 또 다른 더 나은 삶을 위해 창조되었음을 알게 하려 하셨네. 사실 그것이야말로 우리 본성의 가장 큰 필요이기 때문이지. 허나, 아아! 여러 민족이 얼마나 한심한 버릇에 빠져들었는가! 그들은 마치 현재가 전부인 양 하루하루만 살며, 이렇게 떠들고 다니네. '죽음 뒤에 내일은 없다. 설령 있다 한들 우리가 그에 관해 아무것도 모르니, 그것은 그것대로 두라.' 그리하여 죽음이 그들에게 '오라'고 부를 때, 그들은 그 영광스러운 사후의 삶을 누리는 데 들지 못하네. 거기에 합당하지 못한 까닭이지. 다시 말해, 사람의 궁극의 행복은 하느님과 더불어 사는 영원한 삶이었네. 아아, 후르의 아들이여, 이런 말을 해야 하다니! 허나 오늘날 가장 이름난 신전에서 가장 높은 제단을 섬기는 더없이 거룩한 사제들이나, 저기 잠든 낙타나, 그러한 사귐에 한결같기는 매한가지일세. 사람들은 그토록 이 낮은 지상의 삶에 매여 있네! 다가올 저 삶을 그토록 거의 다 잊어버렸네!\n\n\"이제, 청컨대, 우리에게 구원되어야 할 것이 무엇인지 보게나.\n\n\"나로 말하자면, 진리의 거룩함을 걸고 말하건대, 영혼으로서의 한 시간을 사람으로서의 천 년과도 바꾸지 않겠네.\"\n\n여기서 이집트인은 함께 있는 이들을 잊은 듯, 깊은 사색 속으로 빠져드는 것 같았다.\n\n\"이 삶에도 문제들이 있네,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그것들을 풀어 보려 평생을 바치는 사람들도 있지. 허나 그것이 내세의 문제들에 비하면 무엇이겠는가? 하느님을 아는 것에 견줄 만한 것이 어디 있겠나? 신비의 두루마리가 아니라 신비 그 자체가, 적어도 그 한 시간만은 내 앞에 드러나 놓이게 될 것이네. 가장 깊고 가장 두려운 것마저도—지금 우리가 생각만으로도 움츠러드는 그 권능, 곧 허공에 기슭을 둘러치고, 어둠을 밝히고, 무에서 우주를 지으신 그 권능마저도. 모든 곳이 열리리라. 나는 신성한 지식으로 가득 차고, 온갖 영광을 보며, 온갖 기쁨을 맛보고, 존재함을 마음껏 누릴 것이네. 그리고 만일 그 한 시간이 끝날 무렵, 하느님이 기꺼이 내게 '내가 너를 영원히 내 종으로 삼겠노라' 하고 이르신다면, 욕망의 가장 먼 끝마저 넘어서게 되리니, 그 뒤로는 삶에서 이룰 수 있는 야망도, 어떤 종류의 즐거움도, 자그마한 방울들이 짤랑이는 소리만도 못하게 될 것이네.\"\n\n발타사르는 감정의 황홀에서 헤어나려는 듯 말을 멈추었다. 벤허에게는 그 말이 한 영혼이 스스로를 대변하여 전한 것처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n\"부디 용서하게, 후르의 아들이여,\" 어진 사람이 말을 이으며, 그 무게를 뒤따르는 다정한 눈빛으로 누그러뜨린 채 고개를 숙였다. \"나는 영혼의 삶과 그 조건, 그 기쁨, 그 우월함을 그대 자신의 성찰과 깨달음에 맡길 작정이었네. 그 생각이 주는 기쁨에 그만 끌려 말이 너무 많아졌구려. 나는 내 믿음의 까닭을, 아무리 희미할지라도, 보여 주려 했네. 말이 이토록 약한 것이 안타깝네. 허나 진리는 그대 스스로 거두게나. 먼저 죽음 뒤에 우리를 위해 마련된 그 존재의 빼어남을 헤아려 보고, 그 생각이 그대 안에 불러일으킬 것이 분명한 감정과 충동에 마음을 기울이게—거기에 마음을 기울이라는 말일세. 그것들이야말로 그대 자신의 영혼이 깨어나, 그대를 옳은 길로 다그치려 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하고 있는 것이니까. 다음으로 사후의 삶이 너무도 흐려져 잃어버린 빛이라 불러도 마땅할 지경에 이르렀음을 헤아려 보게. 만일 그것을 찾거든 기뻐하게, 후르의 아들이여—내가 그러하듯 기뻐하게, 비록 말이 이토록 궁색할지라도. 그러면 우리에게 구원되어야 할 그 큰 선물 외에도, 그대는 왕에 대한 필요보다 한없이 더 큰 구주에 대한 필요를 발견하게 될 것이며, 우리가 만나러 가는 그분은 더 이상 그대의 소망 속에서 칼을 든 전사나 왕관을 쓴 군주의 자리를 차지하지 않게 될 것이네.\n\n\"한 가지 실제적인 물음이 떠오르네—우리가 어찌 그분을 한눈에 알아보겠는가? 만일 그대가 그분의 성격에 대한 믿음을 고수한다면—그분이 헤롯처럼 왕이 되시리라는 믿음 말일세—물론 그대는 자줏빛 옷을 걸치고 왕홀을 든 사람을 만날 때까지 계속 나아갈 것이네. 반면에 내가 찾는 분은 가난하고, 비천하고, 두드러지지 않은 분—겉모습은 다른 사람들과 다름없는 사람일 것이네. 그리고 내가 그분을 알아볼 표징은 더없이 단순할 것이네. 그분은 나와 온 인류에게 영원한 삶에 이르는 길을, 곧 영혼의 아름답고 순수한 삶을 보여 주려 하실 것이네.\"\n\n일행은 잠시 말없이 앉아 있었고, 그 침묵을 발타사르가 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"이제 일어나세,\" 그가 말했다. \"일어나 다시 길을 나서세. 내가 한 말이 늘 내 마음속에 있는 그분을 뵙고 싶은 조바심을 되살려 놓았네. 그러니 내가 그대를 재촉하는 듯 보이거든, 후르의 아들이여—그리고 내 딸이여—그것을 내 변명으로 삼아 주게.\"\n\n그의 신호에 종이 가죽 부대에 담은 포도주를 가져왔다. 그들은 그것을 따라 마시고, 무릎에 덮었던 천을 털어 내며 일어섰다.\n\n종이 천막과 그릇들을 후다 밑의 상자에 도로 챙겨 넣고, 아랍인이 말들을 끌어 오는 동안, 세 주인공은 못에서 몸을 씻었다.\n\n이윽고 그들은 와디를 거슬러 발걸음을 되짚어 가고 있었으니, 대상이 자기들을 지나쳐 갔다면 따라잡으려는 셈이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n사막 위로 길게 늘어선 대상(隊商)의 모습은 무척이나 그림 같았다. 그러나 움직일 때는 게으른 뱀과 다름없었다. 발타사르는 참을성이 많은 사람이었지만, 그 고집스러운 굼뜬 걸음은 머지않아 견디기 힘들 만큼 따분해졌다. 그리하여 그의 제안에 따라 일행은 자기들끼리 먼저 나아가기로 했다.\n\n만약 독자가 젊은이라면, 또는 젊은 날의 낭만에 대한 다정한 추억을 아직 간직하고 있다면, 이집트인들의 낙타 가까이에서 말을 몰던 벤허가 가물거리는 평원 위로 거의 시야에서 사라져 가는, 더디게 늘어선 행렬의 선두를 마지막으로 돌아보며 느낀 그 즐거움을 함께 음미할 수 있으리라.\n\n되도록 분명하게, 또 솔직하게 말하자면, 벤허는 이라스가 곁에 있다는 사실에서 어떤 매혹을 느꼈다. 그녀가 높은 자리에서 그를 내려다보면, 그는 서둘러 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그녀가 그에게 말을 건네면, 그의 심장은 여느 때의 박자를 잃었다. 그녀의 마음에 들고 싶은 욕망은 끊임없는 충동이었다. 길가의 사물들은 아무리 흔한 것이라도 그녀가 눈길을 주는 순간 흥미로워졌다. 그녀가 가리키는 손가락을 좇아 날아가는 검은 제비 한 마리는 후광을 두르고 사라졌다. 햇살의 입맞춤을 받아 칙칙한 모래 속에서 석영이나 운모 조각이 반짝이는 것이 보이면, 그는 한마디 말에 길을 비켜 가 그것을 주워 그녀에게 가져다주었다. 그녀가 실망하여 그것을 던져 버려도, 그는 자기가 겪은 수고는 조금도 생각지 않고 다만 그것이 그토록 보잘것없는 것이었음을 안타까워하며, 더 나은 것 — 어쩌면 루비나 다이아몬드 — 을 찾아 두리번거렸다. 그래서 그녀가 감탄의 외침으로 알아보면 먼 산들의 자줏빛도 한층 깊고 짙어졌다. 그리고 이따금 가마의 휘장이 내려오면, 갑작스러운 침침함이 하늘에서 떨어져 온 풍경을 흐려 놓는 것만 같았다. 이렇게 마음이 기울고, 그 달콤한 영향에 굴복한 채라면, 두 사람이 들어선 이 외로운 여정에 따르는, 아름다운 이집트 여인과의 가까운 동행이 며칠씩 이어지는 동안 그를 그 위험에서 무엇이 구해 낼 수 있겠는가?\n\n사랑에는 논리가 없고 수학적 요소라곤 조금도 없으니, 영향력을 휘두르는 자가 그 결과를 빚어내는 것은 그저 자연스러운 일이다.\n\n이 결말을 재촉하기라도 하듯, 그녀가 자신이 그에게 미치는 영향을 잘 알고 있다는 징표 또한 있었다. 그녀는 아침나절부터 어딘가 손 가까운 곳에서 금화로 엮은 머리그물을 꺼내, 반짝이는 줄이 이마와 두 뺨 위로 흘러내려 그녀의 검푸른 머리채와 윤기 있게 어우러지도록 매만져 두었다. 같은 안전한 보관처에서 그녀는 또 장신구들을 꺼냈으니 — 손가락과 귀에 끼우는 반지, 팔찌, 진주 목걸이 — 또한 고운 금실로 수놓은 숄이 있었다. 그 효과를 그녀는 목과 어깨에 솜씨 있게 두른 인도산 레이스 스카프로 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸다. 그렇게 차려입고서 그녀는 말과 몸짓으로 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 교태를 벤허에게 부렸다. 미소를 쏟아붓고, 피리 같은 떨림으로 웃었다 — 그러면서 줄곧 그를 눈길로 좇았으니, 그 눈빛은 어느 때는 녹아내릴 듯 부드럽고, 어느 때는 반짝반짝 빛났다. 이런 농간으로 안토니우스는 자기 영광에서 떨어져 나갔다. 그러나 그를 파멸로 이끈 여인은 사실 이 동향(同鄕) 여인의 절반만큼도 아름답지 못했다.\n\n이리하여 그들에게 한낮이 왔고, 또 저녁이 왔다.\n\n해가 오래된 바산의 한 산자락 뒤로 넘어갈 무렵, 일행은 아빌레네 사막 들판에 빗물이 고여 맑게 된 못 곁에 멈추었다. 거기에 천막을 치고, 저녁을 먹고, 밤을 보낼 채비를 했다.\n\n두 번째 불침번은 벤허의 차례였다. 그는 창을 손에 쥐고, 졸고 있는 낙타에서 팔이 닿을 만한 거리에 서서, 한동안 별들을 바라보다가, 다시 너울에 덮인 듯한 대지를 둘러보았다. 적막은 깊었다. 다만 긴 사이를 두고 따스한 바람이 한숨처럼 스쳐 지나갈 뿐이었으나, 그것도 그를 흩뜨리지는 못했다. 아직도 그의 생각은 이집트 여인을 붙들고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀의 매력을 헤아리고, 때로는 그녀가 어떻게 자기 비밀을 알게 되었는지, 그것을 어디에 쓸지, 또 그녀를 어떻게 대해야 할지를 따져 보았다. 그러한 궁리가 이어지는 내내 사랑은 조금 떨어진 곳에 서 있었으니 — 강한 유혹이었고, 그 뒤에 어른거리는 책략의 빛 때문에 더욱 강했다. 바로 그 미혹에 굴복하고 싶은 마음이 가장 컸던 순간, 달 없는 어스름 속에서도 무척 고운 손 하나가 살며시 그의 어깨에 얹혔다. 그 손길에 그는 전율했다. 깜짝 놀라 몸을 돌리니 — 그녀가 거기에 있었다.\n\n\"잠든 줄 알았는데요.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다.\n\n\"잠은 늙은이와 어린아이의 것이지요. 저는 제 벗들을 보러 나왔답니다, 남쪽 하늘의 별들 말이에요 — 지금 나일강 위로 한밤의 휘장을 드리우고 있는 별들 말이에요. 하지만 놀랐다고 인정하세요!\"\n\n그는 자기 어깨에서 떨어진 그 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"그래요, 그것이 적의 짓이었던가요?\"\n\n\"아니에요! 적이 된다는 건 미워하는 것이고, 미움은 병이에요. 이시스 여신께서는 그 병이 제게 가까이 오는 것을 두고 보지 않으세요. 아셔야 할 것은, 제가 어렸을 때 여신께서 제 가슴에 입을 맞춰 주셨다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"당신의 말투는 아버님과는 조금도 닮지 않았군요. 당신은 아버님과 같은 신앙을 갖고 있지 않은가요?\"\n\n\"그랬을지도 모르지요\" — 그녀는 나직이 웃었다 — \"아버지가 본 것을 제가 보았더라면 그랬을지도 몰라요. 아버지처럼 늙으면 그렇게 될지도 모르고요. 젊은이에게는 종교가 있어선 안 돼요, 오직 시와 철학만 있으면 되지요. 그리고 시라면 술과 환락과 사랑에서 우러나온 것 외에는 안 되고, 철학이라면 한 철도 못 넘기는 어리석음에 변명을 끄덕여 주지 않는 것이라면 안 되지요. 아버지의 하느님은 제게는 너무 두려운 분이에요. 저는 다프네 숲에서 그분을 찾지 못했어요. 로마의 안뜰에 그분이 계신다는 말도 들어 본 적이 없고요. 하지만, 후르의 아들이여, 제게는 소원이 하나 있어요.\"\n\n\"소원이라고요! 그 소원에 안 된다고 말할 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n\"한번 시험해 보지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"아주 간단한 거예요. 저는 당신을 돕고 싶어요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 더 가까이 다가왔다.\n\n그는 웃으며 가볍게 대꾸했다. \"오, 이집트여! — 사랑하는 이집트라고 할 뻔했군! — 당신네 나라에는 스핑크스가 살지 않소?\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"당신은 그 수수께끼들 가운데 하나요. 자비를 베풀어, 당신을 이해할 수 있도록 작은 실마리라도 주시오. 내가 무엇에서 도움이 필요하단 말이오? 그리고 당신이 어떻게 나를 도울 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n그녀는 그에게서 손을 거두더니, 낙타 쪽으로 돌아서서 다정하게 말을 걸고, 그 거대한 머리가 아름다운 것이라도 되는 양 쓰다듬었다.\n\n\"오, 욥의 무리 가운데 가장 마지막에 나고 가장 빠르며 가장 위엄 있는 자여! 때로는 너 또한 비틀거리며 가는구나, 길은 험하고 돌투성이고 짐은 무거우니. 어찌하여 너는 한마디 말에 따뜻한 뜻을 알아채고, 또 그 도움이 여인에게서 나온 것일지라도 늘 고맙게 응답하느냐? 내 너에게 입 맞추리라, 너 고귀한 짐승아!\" — 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그 넓은 이마에 입술을 대고는, 곧바로 말했다. \"네 슬기에는 의심이라는 것이 없으니까!\"\n\n그러자 벤허는 스스로를 억누르며 차분히 말했다. \"그 책망이 과녁을 빗나가지는 않았소, 오 이집트여! 내가 당신에게 안 된다고 말하는 것처럼 보인다면, 그것은 내가 명예의 봉인 아래 있고, 내 침묵으로 다른 이들의 목숨과 운명을 지키고 있기 때문은 아니겠소?\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요!\" 그녀가 재빨리 말했다. \"정말 그래요.\"\n\n그는 한 걸음 물러서며, 놀라움으로 날카로워진 목소리로 물었다. \"당신은 대체 무엇을 다 알고 있는 거요?\"\n\n그녀는 한바탕 웃고 나서 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 사내들은 여인의 감각이 자기들보다 더 예리하다는 것을 부정할까요? 당신 얼굴은 온종일 제 눈앞에 있었어요. 저는 그저 그것을 바라보기만 하면 당신이 마음에 어떤 무게를 지고 있다는 걸 알 수 있었지요. 그리고 그 무게가 무엇인지 알려면, 당신이 제 아버지와 나눈 토론을 떠올리는 것 말고 무얼 더 해야 했겠어요? 후르의 아들이여!\" — 그녀는 남다른 솜씨로 목소리를 낮추고, 더 가까이 다가가, 자기 숨결이 그의 뺨에 따뜻하게 닿도록 속삭였다 — \"후르의 아들이여! 당신이 찾으러 가는 그분은 유대인의 왕이 되실 분이지요, 그렇지 않아요?\"\n\n그의 심장이 빠르고 세차게 뛰었다.\n\n\"헤롯 같은 유대인의 왕, 다만 그보다 더 위대한 분이지요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다.\n\n그는 눈길을 돌려 — 밤 속을, 별들을 올려다보았다. 그러다 그의 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주쳐, 거기에 머물렀다. 그녀의 숨결이 그의 입술에 닿았으니, 그만큼 그녀는 가까이 있었다.\n\n\"아침나절부터\" 하고 그녀가 다시 말했다. \"우리는 환상을 보고 있었지요. 자, 제가 제 환상을 당신에게 말해 준다면, 당신도 제게 그만큼 해 주시겠어요? 어머! 아직도 말이 없으시군요?\"\n\n그녀는 그의 손을 밀쳐 내고, 가려는 듯 돌아섰다. 그러나 그가 그녀를 붙잡으며 간절히 말했다. \"있어 줘요 — 있어 주고 말해 주오!\"\n\n그녀는 돌아와, 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 그에게 기댔다. 그는 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안고, 가까이, 아주 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그 포옹 속에 그녀가 구한 약속이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"말해 주오, 당신의 환상을 들려주오, 오 이집트여, 사랑하는 이집트여! 예언자라도 — 아니, 디셉 사람도, 율법을 준 그분조차도 — 당신의 청을 거절할 수는 없었을 거요. 나는 당신 뜻대로 하겠소. 자비를 베푸시오 — 자비를, 비오.\"\n\n그 애원은 들은 척 만 척 지나간 듯, 그녀는 그를 올려다보며 그의 품에 파고든 채 천천히 말했다. \"저를 따라온 환상은 어마어마한 전쟁이었어요 — 뭍과 바다에서 벌어지는 전쟁 — 병장기가 부딪치고 군대가 밀려드는, 마치 카이사르와 폼페이우스가 다시 살아 돌아오고, 옥타비아누스와 안토니우스가 되돌아온 것 같았어요. 먼지와 잿더미의 구름이 일어나 온 세상을 뒤덮었고, 로마는 더 이상 없었지요. 모든 지배권이 동방으로 돌아왔어요. 그 구름 속에서 또 다른 영웅의 족속이 나왔고, 일찍이 알려진 적 없던 더 광대한 태수령(太守領)과 더 빛나는 왕관들이 나눠 주기 위해 마련되어 있었어요. 그리고 후르의 아들이여, 그 환상이 지나가는 동안에도, 또 그것이 사라진 뒤에도, 저는 줄곧 자신에게 물었답니다. '왕을 가장 먼저, 가장 훌륭히 섬긴 자가 무엇인들 얻지 못하랴?'\"\n\n벤허는 또다시 움찔했다. 그 물음이야말로 온종일 그를 따라다닌 바로 그 물음이었다. 이윽고 그는 원하던 실마리를 잡았다고 여겼다.\n\n\"그렇군.\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 알겠소. 태수령과 왕관, 그것이 당신이 나를 돕고 싶다는 그것이로군. 알겠소, 알겠어! 그리고 당신만큼 그토록 영리하고, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 위엄 있는 왕비는 일찍이 없었을 거요 — 결코! 하지만, 아아, 사랑하는 이집트여! 당신이 내게 보여 준 그 환상에서 상(賞)들은 모두 전쟁에서 나오는 것이고, 당신은 비록 이시스 여신께서 가슴에 입을 맞춰 주셨다 해도 한낱 여인일 뿐이오. 왕관이란 당신의 힘으로는 도울 수 없는 별처럼 먼 선물이오, 칼보다 더 확실한 길이 정말로 당신에게 있지 않다면 말이오. 만약 그렇다면, 오 이집트여, 이집트여, 그 길을 내게 보여 주오, 그러면 오직 당신을 위해서라도 나는 그 길을 걷겠소.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 팔을 풀고 말했다. \"당신의 외투를 모래 위에 펼쳐 줘요 — 여기, 제가 낙타에 기댈 수 있도록요. 저는 앉아서, 나일강을 따라 알렉산드리아까지 내려온 이야기를 들려 드릴게요. 저는 거기서 그 이야기를 들었거든요.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 말한 대로 했다. 먼저 창을 가까운 땅에 꽂아 두었다.\n\n\"그럼 나는 무얼 해야 하오?\" 그녀가 자리에 앉자 그가 처량하게 물었다. \"알렉산드리아에서는 이야기를 듣는 사람이 앉는 것이 관례요, 아니면 서 있는 것이 관례요?\"\n\n그 늙은 가축에 편안히 기댄 자리에서 그녀가 웃으며 대답했다. \"이야기꾼의 청중은 제멋대로지요, 때로는 마음 내키는 대로 한답니다.\"\n\n그는 더 군말 없이 모래 위에 몸을 뉘고, 그녀의 팔을 자기 목에 둘렀다.\n\n\"준비됐소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러자 그녀가 곧바로 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n# HOW THE BEAUTIFUL CAME TO THE EARTH.\n\n아름다움이 어떻게 땅에 왔는가.\n\"우선 아셔야 할 것은, 이시스 여신은 — 그리고 그건 지금도 그럴지 모르지만 — 신들 가운데 가장 아름다운 분이었다는 거예요. 그리고 그녀의 남편 오시리스는 지혜롭고 강대했지만, 때로는 그녀에 대한 질투에 마음이 찔렸지요. 신들도 사랑에 있어서만큼은 인간과 같으니까요.\n\n신성한 아내의 궁전은 은으로 지어졌고, 달에서 가장 높은 산을 왕관처럼 이고 있었어요. 그녀는 거기서 자주 해로 건너갔는데, 그 한가운데에는 영원한 빛의 원천이 있어, 오시리스가 황금 궁전을 두고 있었지요. 너무 눈부셔 사람은 차마 바라볼 수 없는 궁전이었답니다.\n\n한번은 — 신들에게는 날(日)이라는 게 없지요 — 그녀가 황금 궁전 지붕 위에서 그와 더없이 즐겁게 지내고 있을 때, 우연히 시선을 돌리다가, 저 멀리 우주의 경계선 바로 위에서 인드라가 원숭이들의 군대를 거느리고 지나가는 것을 보았어요. 모두 날아다니는 독수리들의 등에 실려 있었지요. 그분, 살아 있는 것들의 벗 — 깊은 사랑을 담아 인드라를 그렇게 부르지요 — 은 끔찍한 락샤사들과의 마지막 전쟁에서 돌아오는 길이었어요, 승리를 거두고 돌아오는 길이었지요. 그 행렬에는 영웅 라마와, 그의 신부 시타가 있었는데, 시타는 이시스 여신 다음으로 가장 아름다웠어요. 이시스는 일어나 별들로 엮은 허리띠를 풀어, 시타에게 흔들어 보였지요 — 시타에게요, 잘 들으세요 — 반가운 인사로 흔들어 보였답니다. 그러자 즉시 행군하는 무리와 황금 지붕 위의 두 신 사이로, 마치 밤 같은 무언가가 내려와 그 광경을 가려 버렸어요. 그러나 그것은 밤이 아니었어요 — 그저 오시리스의 찌푸린 얼굴이었지요.\n\n마침 그 순간 그가 하던 이야기는 그들 둘 말고는 아무도 떠올릴 수 없는 그런 것이었어요. 그는 일어나 위엄 있게 말했지요. '집으로 돌아가라. 그 일은 내가 직접 하겠다. 완전히 행복한 존재를 만드는 데 그대의 도움은 필요 없다. 어서 가라.'\n\n그런데 이시스는 흰 암소처럼 큰 눈을 지녔어요. 신전에서 그 암소는 신도들이 기도를 올리는 동안에도 그들의 손에서 달콤한 풀을 받아먹지요. 그녀의 눈은 그 암소의 눈 빛깔이었고, 꼭 그만큼 부드러웠어요. 그녀 또한 일어나, 말하면서 미소를 지었는데, 그 표정은 안개 낀 추수의 달밤에 비치는 달빛보다 조금 더 환할 뿐이었지요. '안녕히 계세요, 다정한 나의 주여. 당신은 머지않아 저를 부르실 거예요, 저는 알아요. 저 없이는 당신이 생각하시던 완전히 행복한 피조물을 만드실 수 없으니까요, 더 이상은' — 그녀는 그 말이 참임을 잘 알기에 웃으려 잠시 멈추었어요 — '더 이상은요, 나의 주여, 당신 자신이 저 없이는 완전히 행복할 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로요.'\n\n'두고 보면 알겠지.' 그가 말했어요.\n\n그래서 그녀는 제 길을 갔고, 뜨개바늘과 의자를 가져다, 은 궁전 지붕 위에 앉아 지켜보며 뜨개질을 했지요.\n\n그리고 오시리스의 의지가 그 위대한 가슴속에서 일하는 소리는, 다른 모든 신들의 방앗간이 한꺼번에 돌아가며 가는 소리 같았어요. 어찌나 컸던지 가까운 별들이 마른 꼬투리 속 씨앗처럼 달그락거렸고, 더러는 떨어져 나가 사라졌지요. 그 소리가 이어지는 동안 그녀는 기다리며 뜨개질을 했어요. 그러면서도 코 하나 놓치는 법이 없었답니다.\n\n이윽고 해 쪽으로 향한 공간에 점 하나가 나타났어요. 그것은 달만큼 커질 때까지 자라났고, 그제야 그녀는 하나의 세계가 의도되고 있음을 알았지요. 그러나 그것이 자라고 또 자라, 마침내 그녀의 별을 그늘 속에 던져 넣고, 오직 그녀가 있어 빛나는 작은 점만 남기자, 그녀는 그가 얼마나 노했는지를 알았어요. 그래도 그녀는 자기가 말한 대로 끝이 나리라 확신하며 뜨개질을 계속했지요.\n\n이렇게 땅이 생겨났어요, 처음에는 그저 텅 빈 허공에 무기력하게 매달린 차갑고 잿빛인 덩어리였지요. 나중에 그녀는 그것이 여러 갈래로 나뉘는 것을 보았어요. 여기는 평원, 저기는 산, 그 너머는 바다, 그러나 아직 반짝임이라곤 하나도 없었지요. 그러다가 강기슭에서 무언가가 움직였어요. 그녀는 놀라워 뜨개질을 멈추었지요. 그 무언가가 일어나, 자기 존재가 어디서 비롯되었는지를 아는 표시로 두 손을 해를 향해 들어 올렸어요. 이 첫 사람은 보기에 아름다웠어요. 그리고 그의 둘레에는 우리가 자연이라 부르는 창조물들이 있었지요 — 풀, 나무, 새, 짐승, 심지어 곤충과 파충류까지요.\n\n한동안 그 사람은 자기 삶을 누리며 행복하게 돌아다녔어요. 그가 얼마나 행복한지는 한눈에 알 수 있었지요. 그리고 일하는 의지의 소리가 잦아든 틈에 이시스는 비웃는 웃음소리를, 곧이어 해 너머에서 불어오는 말소리를 들었어요.\n\n'그대의 도움이라고, 흥! 보라, 완전히 행복한 피조물이로다!'\n\n그러자 이시스는 다시 뜨개질에 빠져들었어요. 오시리스가 강한 만큼 그녀는 참을성이 많았으니까요. 그가 일할 수 있다면, 그녀는 기다릴 수 있었지요. 그리고 그녀는 기다렸어요, 그저 사는 것만으로는 무엇도 만족시킬 수 없음을 알기에.\n\n그리고 정말 그랬지요. 오래지 않아 신성한 아내는 그 사람에게 변화가 일어나는 것을 볼 수 있었어요. 그는 무기력해졌고, 강가 한자리에 엎드려 있기만 하면서, 좀처럼 눈을 들지 않았고, 든다 해도 늘 침울한 얼굴이었지요. 그의 마음에서 흥미가 죽어 가고 있었어요. 그녀가 그것을 확신하고, '이 피조물이 자기 존재에 싫증이 났구나' 하고 혼잣말을 하던 바로 그때, 창조의 의지가 다시 일하는 굉음이 울렸어요. 그러자 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 그때까지 온통 가장 차가운 잿빛이던 땅이 색채로 타올랐지요. 산들은 자줏빛으로 일렁이고, 풀과 나무를 인 평원은 초록으로 변하고, 바다는 푸르게, 구름은 끝없이 변화무쌍하게 물들었어요.\"\n\n그러자 그 사람은 벌떡 일어나 손뼉을 쳤어요, 다시 나아 행복해졌으니까요.\n\n\"이시스는 미소를 지으며 뜨개질을 계속했어요, 혼잣말을 하면서요. '잘 생각하셨어, 한동안은 효험이 있겠지. 하지만 세계의 한낱 아름다움만으로는 저런 존재에게 충분치 않아. 나의 주는 다시 한번 시도하셔야 할 거야.'\n\n그 마지막 말과 함께, 일하는 의지의 천둥소리가 달을 뒤흔들었어요. 이시스가 바라보다가 뜨개질을 떨어뜨리고 손뼉을 쳤지요. 그때까지 땅 위의 모든 것은 사람 말고는 정해진 자리에 붙박여 있었는데, 이제 살아 있는 모든 것이, 또 살아 있지 않은 많은 것까지 운동이라는 선물을 받았으니까요. 새들은 기쁘게 날개를 폈고, 크고 작은 짐승들이 저마다 제 방식으로 돌아다녔어요. 나무들은 푸르른 가지를 흔들며 그것을 사랑하는 바람에게 고개를 끄덕였지요. 강물은 바다로 흘러갔고, 바다는 제 자리에서 뒤척이며 마루를 인 물결로 굽이쳤고, 밀려왔다 밀려가며 반짝이는 거품으로 해안을 물들였어요. 그리고 그 모든 것 위로 구름이 닻을 내리지 않은 돛단배처럼 떠다녔지요.\n\n그러자 그 사람은 어린아이처럼 행복하게 일어났어요. 그것을 보고 오시리스는 흡족하여 외쳤지요. '하하! 보아라, 그대 없이도 내가 얼마나 잘 해내고 있는지를!'\n\n착한 아내는 일감을 집어 들고, 더없이 조용히 대답했어요. '잘 생각하셨어요, 나의 주여 — 참으로 잘 생각하셨어요 — 한동안은 쓸모가 있을 거예요.'\n\n그리고 전과 같이, 또 같은 일이 일어났지요. 움직이는 것들의 광경은 그 사람에게 당연한 것이 되어 버렸어요. 나는 새도, 흐르는 강도, 활동의 소용돌이 속 바다도 더 이상 그를 즐겁게 하지 못했고, 그는 다시, 전보다 더 심하게 시들어 갔지요.\n\n이시스는 기다리며 혼잣말을 했어요. '딱한 것! 그 어느 때보다 비참하구나.'\n\n그러자 마치 그 생각을 듣기라도 한 듯, 오시리스가 움직였고, 그 의지의 소리가 우주를 뒤흔들었어요. 오직 한가운데 자리에 앉은 해만이 굳건히 서 있었지요. 이시스가 바라보았지만 아무런 변화도 보지 못했어요. 그러다가 그녀가 미소를 짓고, 주의 마지막 발명이 끝났다고 확신하던 그때, 갑자기 그 피조물이 일어나, 귀를 기울이는 듯했어요. 그의 얼굴이 환해지더니, 기뻐서 손뼉을 쳤지요. 땅 위에 처음으로 소리가 들렸으니까요 — 어긋나는 소리, 어우러지는 소리. 바람은 나무들 사이에서 속삭였고, 새들은 노래했어요, 저마다 제 종류의 노래를, 아니면 말처럼 재잘거렸지요. 강으로 흘러드는 시냇물들은 은빛 줄을 매단 수많은 하프 연주자가 되어 다 함께 딸랑거렸고, 바다로 흘러드는 강들은 엄숙한 화음을 이루며 밀려갔고, 그동안 바다는 천둥의 가락으로 뭍을 두드렸어요. 음악이, 음악이 어디에나 있었고, 언제나 있었지요. 그러니 그 사람은 행복하지 않을 수 없었답니다.\n\n그러자 이시스는 골똘히 생각했어요, 나의 주가 얼마나 잘, 얼마나 놀랍도록 잘 해내고 있는지를. 그러나 이윽고 그녀는 고개를 가로저었지요. 색채, 운동, 소리 — 그녀는 그것들을 천천히 되뇌었어요 — 형상과 빛 말고는 아름다움의 요소가 더 없었고, 그 둘을 위해 땅이 태어났던 거예요. 이제야말로 오시리스는 다 한 셈이었지요. 그리고 만약 그 피조물이 또다시 비참함에 빠진다면, 그녀의 도움을 청해야 할 터였어요. 그녀의 손가락이 날아다녔지요 — 한 번에 두 코, 세 코, 다섯 코, 심지어 열 코까지 떴어요.\n\n그리고 그 사람은 오랫동안 행복했어요 — 그 어느 때보다도 오래요. 정말이지 그는 다시는 싫증 내지 않을 것만 같았지요. 그러나 이시스는 더 잘 알았어요. 그녀는 기다리고 또 기다렸지요, 해 쪽에서 던져 오는 숱한 웃음에도 아랑곳하지 않고요. 기다리고 또 기다리던 끝에 마침내 끝이 다가오는 징조를 보았어요. 소리들이 그에게 익숙해졌고, 장미꽃 아래 귀뚜라미의 울음에서부터 바다의 포효와 폭풍 속 구름의 으르렁거림에 이르기까지, 그 범위 안에 새로운 것이라곤 하나도 없었지요. 그는 시들고 병들어, 강가의 침울한 자리를 찾았고, 마침내 꼼짝없이 쓰러졌어요.\n그러자 이시스가 가엾이 여겨 입을 열었어요.\n\n'나의 주여,' 그녀가 말했지요. '저 피조물이 죽어 가고 있어요.'\n\n그러나 오시리스는 그 모든 것을 보면서도 잠자코 있었어요. 그는 더 이상 할 수 있는 것이 없었으니까요.\n\n'제가 그를 도울까요?' 그녀가 물었어요.\n\n오시리스는 너무 자존심이 강해 입을 열지 않았지요.\n\n그러자 이시스는 뜨개질의 마지막 코를 떴어요. 그리고 자기 일감을 눈부신 두루마리로 거두어 던졌지요 — 그것이 그 사람 가까이에 떨어지도록 던졌어요. 그러자 그는, 그토록 가까이에서 떨어지는 소리를 듣고 눈을 들었지요. 그런데 보세요! 한 여인이 — 첫 여인이 — 몸을 굽혀 그를 돕고 있었어요! 그녀가 그에게 손을 내밀자, 그는 그것을 잡고 일어났어요. 그리고 다시는 비참해지지 않았고, 영원토록 행복했답니다.\"\n\n\"후르의 아들이여! 이것이 바로 아름다움의 기원이에요, 나일강에서 전해 오는 이야기로는 그렇답니다.\"\n\n그녀는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"예쁜 이야기로군, 게다가 영리하기도 하고.\" 그가 곧바로 말했다. \"하지만 미완성이오. 오시리스는 그 뒤에 어떻게 했소?\"\n\n\"아, 그래요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그는 신성한 아내를 다시 해로 불러들였고, 둘은 서로를 도우며 더없이 즐겁게 함께 지냈답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 나도 그 첫 사람처럼 해야 하지 않겠소?\"\n\n그는 자기 목에 얹힌 그녀의 손을 입술로 가져갔다. \"사랑 속에서 — 사랑 속에서!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그의 머리가 그녀의 무릎 위로 부드럽게 떨어졌다.\n\n\"당신은 왕을 찾아낼 거예요.\" 그녀가 다른 손을 그의 머리에 다정하게 얹으며 말했다. \"당신은 나아가 왕을 찾아 그를 섬길 거예요. 당신의 칼로 그분의 가장 값진 선물들을 얻을 것이고, 그분의 가장 훌륭한 병사가 바로 나의 영웅일 거예요.\"\n\n그는 얼굴을 돌렸고, 자기 위로 가까이 있는 그녀의 얼굴을 보았다. 온 하늘에서 그 순간 그에게 그녀의 두 눈만큼 빛나는 것은 없었다, 그늘에 가려져 있었는데도. 이윽고 그는 일어나 앉아, 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸고 격정에 차서 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"오 이집트여, 이집트여! 왕이 나눠 줄 왕관을 가지고 있다면, 그중 하나는 내 것이 될 거요. 나는 그것을 가져다, 내 입술이 표를 새긴 바로 여기에 얹어 주겠소. 당신은 왕비가 될 거요 — 나의 왕비가 — 그보다 더 아름다운 이는 없으리! 그리고 우리는 언제나, 언제까지나 더없이 행복할 거요!\"\n\n\"그러면 당신은 제게 모든 것을 말해 주고, 제가 무엇이든 당신을 돕게 해 주실 거예요?\" 그녀가 답으로 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n그 물음이 그의 열정을 식혀 버렸다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 사랑하는 것으로 충분치 않소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"완전한 사랑은 완전한 믿음을 뜻하지요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 괜찮아요 — 당신은 저를 더 잘 알게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게서 손을 거두고 일어섰다.\n\n\"당신은 잔인하군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n물러서던 그녀가 낙타 곁에 멈추어, 그 낯짝 앞쪽에 입술을 댔다.\n\n\"오, 네 종족 가운데 가장 고귀한 자여! — 네 사랑에는 의심이라곤 없으니까.\"\n\n그러고는 한순간, 그녀는 사라졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n여행 사흘째 되던 날, 일행은 얍복강 가에서 한낮의 휴식을 가졌다. 그곳에는 대부분 페레아 출신인 백 명이 넘는 사람들이 자기 몸과 짐승들을 쉬게 하고 있었다. 그들이 낙타에서 내리기가 무섭게 한 남자가 물 항아리와 대접을 들고 다가와 마실 것을 권했다. 그들이 정중하게 그 호의를 받자, 남자는 낙타를 바라보며 말했다. \"저는 요르단강에서 돌아오는 길입니다. 지금 그곳에는 먼 곳에서 온 사람들이 많은데, 빛나는 벗이여, 그들도 당신처럼 길을 가는 이들이지요. 그러나 그들 가운데 누구도 여기 당신의 종만 한 짐승은 없었습니다. 참으로 고귀한 짐승이군요. 어떤 혈통에서 났는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n발타사르가 대답하고는 쉬려고 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 더 호기심이 동한 벤허가 그 말을 이어받았다.\n\n\"강의 어느 곳에 사람들이 모여 있습니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"베다바라입니다.\"\n\n\"거기는 본래 인적 드문 나루터였는데요.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"어떻게 그토록 사람들의 관심을 끄는 곳이 되었는지 이해가 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 낯선 남자가 대답했다. \"당신도 외지에서 온 분이라 그 좋은 소식을 듣지 못했군요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소식 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그게, 한 사람이 광야에서 나타났습니다. 매우 거룩한 사람인데, 입에는 듣는 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡는 기이한 말이 가득합니다. 자기를 나사렛 사람 요한, 사가랴의 아들이라 부르며, 자기는 메시아보다 앞서 보내심을 받은 사자라고 말합니다.\"\n\n이라스조차 귀를 기울이는 가운데 남자는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"사람들이 이 요한에 대해 말하기를, 그는 어린 시절부터 엔게디 아래쪽 동굴에서 평생을 보내며 에세네파보다 더 엄격하게 기도하며 살았다고 합니다. 무리가 그의 설교를 들으러 갑니다. 저도 다른 사람들과 함께 들으러 갔지요.\"\n\n\"여기 당신의 벗들도 모두 그곳에 다녀왔습니까?\"\n\n\"대부분은 가는 길이고, 더러는 돌아오는 길입니다.\"\n\n\"그가 무엇을 설교합니까?\"\n\n\"새로운 교리입니다. 다들 말하기를, 이스라엘에서 일찍이 가르친 적이 없는 것이라 합니다. 그는 그것을 회개와 세례라 부릅니다. 랍비들도 그를 어찌 보아야 할지 모르고, 우리도 모릅니다. 어떤 이들은 그에게 그가 그리스도냐고 물었고, 또 어떤 이들은 그가 엘리야냐고 물었습니다. 그러나 그는 그들 모두에게 이렇게 대답합니다. '나는 광야에서 외치는 자의 소리니, 주의 길을 곧게 하라!'\"\n\n이 대목에서 남자는 벗들에게 불려 갔다. 그가 떠나려 할 때 발타사르가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"착한 나그네여!\" 그가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"당신이 떠나온 그곳에 가면 그 설교자를 만날 수 있을지 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"네, 베다바라에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"이 나사렛 사람이 우리의 왕을 알리는 전령이 아니라면 도대체 누구겠습니까?\" 벤허가 이라스에게 말했다.\n\n이토록 짧은 시간에 그는 딸이 늙은 아버지보다 자기가 찾고 있는 그 신비로운 인물에게 더 관심이 많다고 여기게 되었다. 그런데도 아버지는 움푹 꺼진 두 눈에 또렷한 빛을 띠며 몸을 반쯤 일으키고 말했다.\n\n\"서두릅시다. 나는 피곤하지 않소.\"\n\n그들은 몸을 돌려 노인이 일어서도록 거들었다.\n\n라못 길르앗 서쪽에서 하룻밤을 묵으려 멈춘 곳에서 세 사람 사이에는 별다른 대화가 없었다.\n\n\"일찍 일어납시다, 후르의 아들이여.\" 노인이 말했다. \"구세주께서 오시는데 우리가 그곳에 없을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"왕은 전령의 뒤를 멀리 떨어져 따라오실 리 없어요.\" 이라스가 낙타에 오를 채비를 하며 속삭였다.\n\n\"내일이면 알게 되겠지요!\" 벤허가 그녀의 손에 입맞추며 대답했다.\n\n이튿날 제삼시쯤, 라못을 떠난 뒤로 길르앗산 기슭을 끼고 지나온 산길을 빠져나오자, 일행은 거룩한 강 동쪽의 메마른 초원에 다다랐다. 맞은편으로는 여리고의 오래된 종려나무 땅의 위쪽 경계가 보였는데, 그것은 유대의 구릉지대까지 뻗어 있었다. 벤허는 나루터가 가까이 있음을 알았기에 피가 빠르게 뛰었다.\n\n\"마음을 놓으시오, 착한 발타사르.\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 거의 다 왔습니다.\"\n\n낙타 모는 자가 낙타의 걸음을 재촉했다. 곧 그들은 임시 막사와 천막, 매어 둔 짐승들을 보았다. 그러고는 강과, 강기슭 가까이 모여든 한 무리, 그리고 서쪽 기슭의 또 다른 무리를 보았다. 설교자가 설교하고 있음을 알고 그들은 더욱 서둘렀다. 그러나 그들이 가까이 다가가는 사이에, 갑자기 그 무리에 동요가 일더니 흩어지며 헤어지기 시작했다.\n\n너무 늦었던 것이다!\n\n\"여기 머무릅시다.\" 벤허가 두 손을 비틀고 있는 발타사르에게 말했다. \"나사렛 사람이 이쪽으로 올지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n사람들은 자기들이 들은 것에 너무 골몰해 있었고, 논의에 너무 바빠 새로 온 이들을 알아채지 못했다. 수백 명이 지나가고, 이제 나사렛 사람을 보는 일조차 이 일행에게는 놓쳐 버린 기회처럼 여겨질 무렵, 강 위쪽 멀지 않은 곳에서 그들을 향해 다가오는 한 사람이 보였는데, 그 모습이 어찌나 기이한지 그들은 다른 모든 것을 잊었다.\n\n겉모습으로 보아 그 사람은 거칠고 투박했으며, 야만스럽기까지 했다. 갈색 양피지 빛깔의 야위고 수척한 얼굴 위로, 어깨와 등을 타고 허리 아래까지, 마법사 같은 텁수룩한 머리채로, 햇볕에 그을린 머리카락이 덮개처럼 늘어져 있었다. 두 눈은 불타듯 빛났다. 오른쪽 몸은 온통 드러나 있었는데, 얼굴과 같은 빛깔이었고 똑같이 비쩍 말라 있었다. 베두인의 천막 천처럼 거친, 가장 거친 낙타털로 지은 옷이 무릎까지 그의 나머지 몸을 가렸고, 허리에는 무두질하지 않은 가죽으로 된 넓은 띠를 둘렀다. 발은 맨발이었다. 역시 무두질하지 않은 가죽으로 된 자루가 띠에 매여 있었다. 그는 마디진 지팡이를 짚어 걸음을 도왔다. 그의 움직임은 빠르고 단호하며 기이하리만치 경계하는 듯했다. 이따금 그는 흐트러진 머리카락을 눈에서 떨쳐 내고는, 누군가를 찾기라도 하는 듯 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n아리따운 이집트 여인은 놀라움을, 아니 혐오에 가까운 마음으로 그 사막의 아들을 바라보았다. 이윽고 그녀는 가마의 휘장을 들어 올리고, 가까이 말을 타고 있는 벤허에게 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 당신 왕의 전령인가요?\"\n\n\"나사렛 사람입니다.\" 그가 올려다보지도 않고 대답했다.\n\n사실 그 자신도 실망 이상의 감정을 느끼고 있었다. 엔게디의 금욕적인 정착민들에 대해 잘 알고 있었음에도—그들의 복장, 세상의 모든 평판에 대한 무관심, 상상할 수 있는 온갖 육신의 고통에 몸을 내맡기고 동족의 다른 이들과는 마치 같은 모양으로 태어나지 않은 양 철저히 갈라서게 만드는 그 서원에 대한 한결같음을 알고 있었음에도—그리고 오는 길에 자신을 광야의 소리라고 소박하게 일컫는 나사렛 사람을 찾으라는 일러둠을 받았음에도—그토록 위대하고 그토록 많은 일을 하실 왕에 대한 벤허의 꿈은 그에 관한 모든 생각을 물들여 놓았기에, 그는 그 선구자에게서 그가 알리는 분의 훌륭함과 왕다움을 보여 주는 어떤 표징이나 징표를 찾으리라고 의심치 않았다. 눈앞의 그 거친 모습을 바라보자, 로마의 온천장과 황궁 회랑에서 늘 보아 온 신하들의 긴 행렬이 떠올라 자꾸만 견주게 되었다. 충격을 받고, 부끄럽고, 어리둥절한 그는 이렇게밖에 대답할 수 없었다.\n\n\"나사렛 사람입니다.\"\n\n발타사르에게는 사정이 사뭇 달랐다. 그는 하느님의 길이 사람들이 바라는 대로가 아님을 알고 있었다. 그는 구세주가 구유에 누인 아기였을 때 보았고, 그 신성한 재림과 관련하여 거칠고 소박한 것을 자신의 믿음으로 미리 받아들일 채비가 되어 있었다. 그래서 그는 자리에 앉은 채 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 입술로 기도를 읊조렸다. 그는 왕을 기대하고 있지 않았다.\n\n새로 온 이들에게 이토록 흥미로운, 그리고 그들이 저마다 다르게 마음이 움직이던 이 무렵, 또 한 사람이 강가의 돌 위에 홀로 앉아, 아마도 자기가 들은 설교를 여전히 곱씹으며 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 이제 그가 일어나 기슭에서 천천히 걸어 올라왔는데, 그 길은 나사렛 사람이 가는 방향을 가로질러 그를 낙타 가까이로 데려올 진로였다.\n\n그리고 그 두 사람—설교자와 낯선 이—은 계속 나아가, 마침내 앞사람은 짐승에게서 스무 걸음 안쪽에, 뒷사람은 열 발짝 안쪽에 이르렀다. 그러자 설교자가 걸음을 멈추고 눈에서 머리카락을 떨쳐 내고는, 낯선 이를 바라보며, 보이는 모든 사람에게 신호를 보내듯 두 손을 치켜들었다. 그러자 사람들도 저마다 귀 기울이는 자세로 걸음을 멈추었다. 그리고 정적이 완전해지자, 나사렛 사람의 오른손에 든 지팡이가 천천히 내려와 낯선 이를 가리켰다.\n\n조금 전까지 그저 듣기만 하던 모든 이들이 이제 지켜보는 자들도 되었다.\n\n같은 순간, 같은 충동에 이끌려 발타사르와 벤허는 지목된 그 사람에게 시선을 못 박았고, 둘 다 같은 인상을, 다만 그 정도만 다르게 받았다. 그는 그들의 조금 앞쪽 트인 공간에서 천천히 그들을 향해 다가오고 있었는데, 키는 보통보다 조금 크고, 호리호리하며, 가냘프기까지 한 몸이었다. 그의 거동은 차분하고 신중했으니, 진중한 문제를 두고 깊이 사색하는 데 익숙한 사람들에게 몸에 밴 그런 태도였다. 그것은 그의 복장과도 잘 어울렸는데, 그 옷은 소매가 길고 발목까지 닿는 속옷과, 탈리트라 불리는 겉옷이었다. 그의 왼팔에는 머리에 두르는 흔한 수건이 걸려 있었고, 붉은 끈이 옆구리로 느슨하게 늘어져 있었다. 그 끈과 탈리트 아랫단의 좁은 파란 가두리를 빼면, 그의 옷은 먼지와 길의 얼룩으로 누렇게 바랜 아마포였다. 어쩌면 그 예외에는 술도 더해야 할 터인데, 그것은 랍비를 위해 율법이 정한 대로 파랑과 하양이었다. 그의 샌들은 더없이 소박한 것이었다. 그는 자루도 띠도 지팡이도 지니지 않았다.\n\n그러나 세 구경꾼은 이런 외양의 세부들을 잠깐만 살폈을 뿐, 오히려 그것을 그 사람의 머리와 얼굴에 딸린 부속물쯤으로 여겼다. 그 머리와 얼굴이—특히 얼굴이—그를 바라보고 선 모든 이들과 더불어 그들이 함께 사로잡힌 그 마력의 진짜 원천이었던 것이다.\n\n그 머리는 구름 한 점 없는 빛에 활짝 드러나 있었으나, 길고 살짝 물결치며 한가운데서 갈라진 적갈색 머리카락이 그것을 덮고 있었는데, 햇볕이 가장 세게 닿은 곳은 불그스레한 금빛을 띠는 경향이 있었다. 넓고 낮은 이마 아래, 검고 잘 휘어진 눈썹 아래로, 짙푸르고 큰 두 눈이 빛났으니, 아이들에게서나 이따금 볼 수 있을 뿐 어른에게서는 좀처럼, 아니 거의 볼 수 없는 그 긴 속눈썹 덕에 더없이 부드러워져 있었다. 다른 이목구비로 말하면, 그것이 그리스적인지 유대적인지 판단하기 어려웠을 것이다. 콧방울과 입의 섬세함은 유대인의 모습에서는 드문 것이었다. 그리고 그 눈의 온화함, 안색의 창백함, 머리카락의 고운 결, 그리고 목을 타고 가슴까지 물결치며 흘러내린 수염의 부드러움까지 함께 헤아리면, 어떤 병사도 맞붙어 싸우는 자리에서 그를 비웃지 않을 수 없었을 것이요, 어떤 여인도 한눈에 그를 믿지 않을 수 없었을 것이며, 어떤 아이도 재빠른 본능으로 그에게 제 손을 내밀어 천진한 신뢰를 송두리째 맡기지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 또한 누구도 그가 아름답지 않다고는 말하지 못했으리라.\n\n덧붙여 말해 두자면, 그 이목구비는 어떤 표정에 다스려지고 있었으니, 보는 이에 따라 지성이라 불러도, 사랑이라 불러도, 연민이라 불러도, 슬픔이라 불러도 똑같이 옳았을 표정이었다. 그러나 더 나은 말로 하자면 그것은 그 모두가 한데 어우러진 것이었다—죄 없는 영혼이 자기가 지나가는 무리의 더할 나위 없는 죄악을 보고 깨달을 운명에 처한 듯한, 그런 표정으로 떠올리기 쉬운 모습이었다. 그러면서도 그 누구도 그 얼굴을 보며 그 사람에게 나약함이 있다고는 생각하지 못했을 것이다. 적어도, 앞서 말한 그 자질들—사랑, 슬픔, 연민—이 무언가를 행할 힘보다는 고난을 견딜 힘을 의식하는 데서 비롯되는 경우가 더 많다는 것을 아는 이들은 그러지 않았으리라. 순교자들과 신앙의 헌신자들, 그리고 성인의 명부에 적힌 무수한 이들의 위력이 그러했던 것이다. 그리고 이 사람의 분위기 또한 참으로 그러했다.\n\n그는 천천히 다가왔다—세 사람에게 더 가까이.\n\n그런데 말을 타고 창을 든 벤허는 왕의 눈길을 끌 만한 대상이었다. 그러나 다가오는 사람의 눈은 줄곧 그의 위쪽으로 들려 있었으니—그 아름다움이 그토록 자주 언급된 이라스에게가 아니라, 늙고 쓸모없어진 발타사르에게 향해 있었다.\n\n정적이 깊었다.\n\n이윽고 나사렛 사람이 여전히 지팡이로 가리키며 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"보라, 세상 죄를 지고 가는 하느님의 어린양이로다!\"\n\n말하는 이의 동작에 붙들려 멈춰 선 채 다음에 무슨 일이 이어질까 귀 기울이던 많은 이들은, 너무도 기이하고 자기네 이해를 넘어선 그 말에 경외에 사로잡혔다. 발타사르에게 그 말은 압도적이었다. 그는 사람들의 구속자를 다시 한번 보기 위해 그곳에 있었던 것이다. 오래전 그에게 그 보기 드문 특권을 가져다준 그 믿음이 여전히 그의 가슴에 머물러 있었다. 그리고 만약 그 믿음이 이제 그에게 동료들의 것을 뛰어넘는 봄의 능력을—자기가 찾고 있던 분을 보고 알아보는 능력을—주었다면, 그 능력을 기적이라 부르기보다는, 일찍이 받아들여진 신성한 관계에서 아직 온전히 풀려나지 않은 영혼의 기능이라 여기거나, 혹은 거룩함의 본보기가 그토록 없던 시대에 그 자체가 하나의 기적인 한 생애에 주어진 합당한 보답이라 여기는 편이 나을 것이다. 그 믿음의 이상이 그의 눈앞에 있었으니, 얼굴도, 모습도, 옷차림도, 거동도, 나이도 완벽했다. 그리고 그는 그 이상의 시야 안에 있었고, 그 시선은 알아봄이었다. 아, 이제 무언가 일이 일어나 이 낯선 이를 한 점 의심도 없이 확인해 준다면!\n\n그리고 바로 그 일이 일어났다.\n\n꼭 알맞은 순간에, 떨고 있는 이집트인을 안심시키려는 듯, 나사렛 사람이 그 외침을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"보라, 세상 죄를 지고 가는 하느님의 어린양이로다!\"\n\n발타사르가 무릎을 꿇었다. 그에게는 더 이상의 설명이 필요 없었다. 그리고 나사렛 사람도 그것을 안다는 듯, 자기 가까이서 놀라움에 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고 바라보는 이들을 향해 돌아서서 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이분은 내가 전에 말하기를, 내 뒤에 오는 사람이 나보다 앞선 것은 나보다 먼저 계셨음이라 한 바로 그분이라. 나도 그를 알지 못하였으나, 그가 이스라엘에 나타나시게 하려고 내가 와서 물로 세례를 베푸노라. 나는 성령이 하늘로부터 비둘기같이 내려와 그 위에 머무는 것을 보았노라. 나도 그를 알지 못하였으나, 나를 보내어 물로 세례를 베풀게 하신 그분이 내게 말씀하시기를, 성령이 내려서 누구 위에 머무는 것을 보거든 그가 곧 성령으로 세례를 베푸는 이라 하셨느니라. 내가 보고 증언하노니, 이는\"—그가 말을 멈추었으나, 지팡이는 여전히 흰옷 입은 낯선 이를 가리킨 채였으니, 자기 말과 거기에 담긴 결론 모두에 더 절대적인 확실함을 부여하려는 듯했다—\"내가 증언하노니, 이는 하느님의 아들이시니라!\"\n\n\"그분이다, 그분이야!\" 발타사르가 눈물 어린 눈을 치켜뜨며 외쳤다. 다음 순간 그는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다.\n\n기억해 둘 것은, 이 무렵 벤허도 그 낯선 이의 얼굴을 살피고 있었다는 사실이다. 다만 그 관심은 전혀 다른 것이었다. 그도 그 이목구비의 깨끗함과, 그 사색적인 면모, 부드러움, 겸손함, 거룩함에 무감각하지는 않았다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그의 마음에는 단 하나의 생각만이 들어설 자리가 있었다—이 사람은 누구인가? 그리고 무엇인가? 메시아인가, 왕인가? 이토록 왕답지 않은 형상은 일찍이 없었다. 아니, 그 차분하고 자애로운 얼굴을 바라보자, 전쟁과 정복, 지배를 향한 탐욕이라는 생각 자체가 신성모독처럼 그를 후려쳤다. 그는 마치 자기 마음에 대고 말하듯 이렇게 되뇌었다. 발타사르가 옳고 시모니데스가 틀린 것이 분명하다. 이 사람은 솔로몬의 왕좌를 다시 세우러 온 것이 아니다. 그에게는 헤롯의 본성도, 헤롯의 수완도 없다. 왕일지는 모르나, 로마보다 또 다른 더 위대한 나라의 왕은 아니다.\n\n이제 알아 두어야 할 것은, 이것이 벤허에게 하나의 결론이 아니라 그저 인상일 뿐이었다는 점이다. 그리고 그 인상이 형성되어 가는 동안, 그가 여전히 그 경이로운 얼굴을 응시하는 동안, 그의 기억이 꿈틀거리며 몸부림치기 시작했다. \"분명히,\" 그는 속으로 말했다. \"나는 저 사람을 본 적이 있다. 그런데 어디서, 언제였던가?\" 그토록 차분하고, 그토록 가엾이 여기고, 그토록 사랑이 가득한 그 눈길이, 지금 발타사르에게 비치는 것처럼 지난 어느 때 어딘가에서 자기에게도 비쳤었다는 것이 하나의 확신으로 다가왔다. 처음에는 희미하게, 마침내는 또렷한 빛으로, 햇살이 터지듯, 로마 호위병이 그를 갤리선으로 끌고 가던 그때 나사렛 우물가의 그 장면이 되살아났고, 그의 온 존재가 전율했다. 그 손이 그가 스러져 가던 때에 그를 도와주었던 것이다. 그 얼굴은 그가 그 후로 줄곧 마음에 품어 온 그림들 가운데 하나였다. 북받쳐 오른 감정의 격류 속에서, 그는 설교자의 말을 놓치고 말았으니, 다만 마지막 말만은—세상이 아직도 그 울림을 간직하고 있는 그토록 놀라운 그 말만은 들었다.\n\n\"—이는 하느님의 아들이시니라!\"\n\n벤허는 자기 은인에게 경의를 표하려고 말에서 뛰어내렸다. 그러나 이라스가 그에게 외쳤다. \"도와줘요, 후르의 아들이여, 도와줘요. 안 그러면 아버지가 돌아가시겠어요!\"\n\n그는 멈춰 서서 돌아보고는, 서둘러 그녀를 도우러 갔다. 그녀가 그에게 잔을 건넸다. 그리고 종에게 낙타를 꿇어앉히는 일을 맡긴 채, 그는 물을 뜨러 강으로 달려갔다. 그가 돌아왔을 때 그 낯선 이는 떠나고 없었다.\n\n마침내 발타사르가 의식을 되찾았다. 두 손을 앞으로 뻗으며 그는 힘없이 물었다. \"그분은 어디 계시오?\"\n\n\"누구 말씀이세요?\" 이라스가 물었다.\n\n마치 마지막 소원이 이루어지기라도 한 듯, 그 선량한 노인의 얼굴에 강렬하고도 순간적인 관심이 빛났다. 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"그분 말이오—구속자—내가 다시 뵌 하느님의 아들 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 믿으세요?\" 이라스가 낮은 목소리로 벤허에게 물었다.\n\n\"이 시대는 경이로 가득하오. 기다려 봅시다.\" 그가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n그리고 이튿날 세 사람이 그의 말을 듣고 있을 때, 나사렛 사람이 말을 하다 말고 멈추더니 경건하게 말했다. \"보라, 하느님의 어린양이로다!\"\n\n그가 가리키는 곳을 보자, 그들은 그 낯선 이를 다시 보았다. 벤허가 그 호리호리한 모습과, 슬픔에 잠길 만큼 가엾이 여기는 거룩하고 아름다운 얼굴을 살피는 사이, 새로운 생각 하나가 그에게 떠올랐다.\n\n\"발타사르가 옳다—시모니데스도 옳다. 구속자가 왕이기도 하지 말란 법이 어디 있는가?\"\n\n그리고 그는 곁에 있는 한 사람에게 물었다. \"저기 걸어가는 사람이 누구입니까?\"\n\n상대는 비웃듯 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"저 너머 나사렛에 사는 목수의 아들이지요.\"\n\n# BOOK EIGHTH\n\n“누가 능히 거역할 수 있으랴? 이 우주에 그 누가?\n그녀는 신의 음식을 내쉬듯 숨 쉬며, 나의 고귀한 존재를\n\n황금빛 하늘에 그토록 깊이 잠기게 하였다.\n\n그녀는 나를 젖먹이 어린아이처럼 안아\n장미꽃 요람에 뉘었다. 이리하여 정죄받아,\n\n내 지난 삶의 물줄기는 막히고 말았으니,\n\n이 제멋대로인 감각의 여왕에게\n\n나는 황홀에 사로잡힌 신하로서 머리를 조아렸다.”—키츠, 「엔디미온」.\n\n“나는 부활이요 생명이니.”\n“에스더야—에스더야! 아래에 있는 하인에게 일러 물 한 잔만 가져오라고 하여라.”\n\n“포도주가 낫지 않으시겠어요, 아버지?”\n“둘 다 가져오라고 하려무나.”\n\n이곳은 예루살렘에 있는 후르 가문의 옛 저택, 그 지붕 위에 마련된 정자였다. 안마당이 내려다보이는 난간에서 에스더는 그곳에 대기하고 있던 한 사람을 불렀다. 바로 그때 또 다른 하인 하나가 계단을 올라와 공손히 인사했다.\n“주인님께 온 소포입니다.” 그는 아마포에 싸여 끈으로 묶이고 봉인된 편지 하나를 그녀에게 건네며 말했다.\n\n독자의 이해를 돕기 위해 잠시 멈추어 말해 두자면, 지금은 3월 21일로, 베다바라에서 그리스도가 선포된 지 거의 3년이 지난 때다.\n그동안 아버지의 집이 텅 비고 허물어져 가는 모습을 더는 견딜 수 없었던 벤허를 대신해 말루크가 본디오 빌라도에게서 그 집을 사들였다. 그리고 수리를 하는 과정에서 대문과 안마당, 회랑과 계단, 테라스와 방과 지붕까지 모두 깨끗이 손질되고 철저히 복구되었다. 가문을 파멸로 몰아넣은 그 비극적 사연을 떠올리게 하는 자취가 하나도 남지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 새로 갖춘 살림은 예전보다도 더 호화로운 양식이었다. 실로 어디를 둘러보든, 찾아온 이는 젊은 주인이 미세눔의 별장과 로마의 수도에서 여러 해 머무는 동안 익힌 한층 높은 안목의 증거와 마주치게 되었다.\n\n그렇다고 이 설명에서 벤허가 그 재산의 소유권을 공공연히 떠맡았다고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 그의 생각으로는 아직 그럴 때가 오지 않았다. 또한 그는 아직 자신의 본래 이름을 쓰지도 않았다. 갈릴리에서 준비의 노고로 시간을 보내며, 그는 나사렛 사람의 움직임을 끈기 있게 기다렸다. 그 사람은 날이 갈수록 그에게 더욱더 수수께끼가 되어 갔으니, 흔히 그의 눈앞에서 행해지는 이적들로 말미암아 그의 본성과 사명 양쪽 모두에 대해 그를 불안한 의심의 상태에 머물게 했다. 그는 이따금 거룩한 도성으로 올라와 아버지의 집에 묵었으나, 언제나 한낱 낯선 손님으로서였다.\n\n벤허의 이런 방문이 단지 노동에서 벗어나 쉬기 위한 것만은 아니었음도 일러두어야겠다. 발타사르와 이라스가 저택에 거처를 두고 있었던 것이다. 그 딸의 매력은 처음 그대로의 신선함을 간직한 채 여전히 그를 사로잡고 있었고, 한편 아버지는 몸은 더 쇠약해졌으나, 그들 모두가 그토록 기다리는 저 떠도는 기적의 행자(行者)가 신성을 지녔음을 역설하는, 놀라운 힘이 깃든 그 이야기에 그를 지칠 줄 모르는 청자로 붙들어 두었다.\n시모니데스와 에스더로 말하자면, 이번에 이렇게 다시 모습을 보이기 불과 며칠 전 안디옥에서 막 도착한 참이었다. 그것은 상인에게 고된 여정이었으니, 그는 두 마리 낙타 사이에 매달린 가마에 실려 왔는데, 낙타들은 이리저리 기우뚱거리며 늘 발걸음을 맞추지는 못했다. 그러나 막상 와 보니, 이 선량한 사람은 고향 땅을 아무리 보아도 성에 차지 않는 듯했다. 그는 지붕 위 높은 자리를 즐겨, 하루의 낮 시간 대부분을 그곳에서 안락의자에 앉아 보냈는데, 그것은 오론테스 강가 창고 위 사실(私室)에 마련된 그 의자와 똑같은 것이었다. 정자의 그늘에서 그는 낯익은 언덕들 위에 가볍게 깔린, 영감을 불어넣는 공기를 한껏 들이마실 수 있었다. 그곳에서는 해가 떠올라 제 길을 달려 저무는 모습을, 지난날 그러했던 대로 더 잘 바라볼 수 있었으니, 한 가지 습관도 잃지 않았다. 그리고 에스더가 곁에 있으면, 하늘 가까운 그 높은 곳에서는 또 다른 에스더, 곧 젊은 날의 연인이요 그의 아내이며 세월이 흐를수록 더욱 소중해지는 그 사람을 떠올리기가 한결 수월했다. 그러면서도 그는 일을 소홀히 하지 않았다. 날마다 전령 하나가 뒤에 남겨 둔 큰 상업을 맡아보는 산발랏에게서 전갈을 가져왔고, 날마다 전갈 하나가 그에게서 산발랏에게로 떠났는데, 그 지시는 세세하기 이를 데 없어 그 자신의 판단 말고는 다른 어떤 판단도, 그리고 전능하신 분이 가장 사려 깊은 사람에게조차 맡기기를 거부하신 우연 말고는 다른 어떤 우연도 끼어들 여지가 없었다.\n\n에스더가 정자로 돌아서려는데, 햇빛이 티끌 하나 없는 지붕 위로 부드럽게 내려앉아 그녀의 모습을 비추었다. 이제 그녀는 어엿한 여인이었다—몸집은 작으나 자태가 우아하고, 이목구비가 단정하며, 젊음과 건강으로 발그레하고, 지성으로 빛나며, 헌신적인 천성이 밖으로 비쳐 나와 아름다운—사랑하는 일이 도무지 억누를 수 없는 삶의 습관이었기에 누구에게나 사랑받을 만한 여인이었다.\n\n돌아서며 그녀는 그 소포를 보았다. 걸음을 멈추고 처음보다 더 가까이 다시 한번 들여다보았다. 그러자 피가 솟구쳐 두 뺨을 붉게 물들였다—봉인이 벤허의 것이었다. 그녀는 발걸음을 재촉해 서둘러 걸어갔다.\n\n시모니데스는 그 역시 봉인을 살피느라 소포를 잠시 손에 들고 있었다. 그것을 뜯어, 안에 든 두루마리를 그녀에게 건넸다.\n\n“읽어 보아라.” 그가 말했다.\n\n그가 말하는 동안 그의 눈은 그녀에게 가 있었는데, 이내 그의 얼굴에도 근심스러운 빛이 어렸다.\n\n“누구한테서 온 것인지 알고 있구나, 에스더. 보아하니 그렇구나.”\n\n“네—저—주인님한테서요.”\n\n말투는 더듬거렸으나, 그녀는 정숙한 진솔함으로 그의 시선을 마주했다. 그의 턱이 천천히 그 아래 방석처럼 부풀어 오른 살의 두툼한 주름 속으로 묻혔다.\n\n“너는 그를 사랑하는구나, 에스더.” 그가 조용히 말했다.\n\n“네.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“네가 하는 일에 대해 깊이 생각해 보았느냐?”\n\n“그분을 생각하지 않으려고 애썼어요, 아버지. 제가 도리로써 매여 있는 주인 이상으로는요. 하지만 그 애씀이 저를 강하게 만들어 주지는 못했어요.”\n\n“착한 아이로구나, 착한 아이야, 꼭 네 어미 같구나.” 그가 이렇게 말하며 회상에 잠겼는데, 그녀가 종이를 펼치는 바람에 그는 거기서 깨어났다.\n\n“주님께서 나를 용서하시기를. 하지만—하지만 내가 가졌던 모든 것을, 능히 그럴 수 있었던 대로 단단히 붙들고 있었더라면 네 사랑이 헛되이 바쳐지지는 않았을지도 모른다—돈에는 그만한 힘이 있는 법이니!”\n\n“그러셨다면 저로서는 더 나빴을 거예요, 아버지. 그랬다면 저는 그분의 눈길 한 번 받을 자격도 없었을 테고, 아버지를 자랑스러워할 수도 없었을 테니까요. 이제 읽어도 될까요?”\n\n“잠깐만.” 그가 말했다. “내 아이야, 너를 위해 가장 나쁜 것을 먼저 보여 주마. 나와 함께 그것을 보면 네게 덜 끔찍해질지도 모르겠구나. 그의 사랑은, 에스더, 이미 온전히 다른 데 바쳐졌단다.”\n\n“알고 있어요.” 그녀가 차분히 말했다.\n\n“그 이집트 여자가 그를 제 그물에 가두어 놓았다.” 그가 말을 이었다. “그 여자는 제 핏줄다운 교활함을 지녔고, 거기에 미모까지 더했으니—대단한 미모에, 대단한 교활함이지. 하지만 또한 제 핏줄답게 가슴은 없는 여자다. 제 아비를 업신여기는 딸은 제 남편을 비탄에 빠뜨리고 말 것이다.”\n\n“그 여자가 그래요?”\n\n시모니데스가 말을 이었다.\n\n“발타사르는 슬기로운 사람으로, 이방인치고는 놀랍도록 은총을 입었고, 그 신앙이 그에게 잘 어울린다. 그런데도 그 여자는 그것을 비웃음거리로 삼는구나. 어제 그 여자가 아비 이야기를 하며 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. ‘젊은 날의 어리석음은 용서받을 만하지요. 늙은이에게 칭송할 만한 것이라곤 지혜밖에 없는데, 그것마저 떠나 버리면 죽는 편이 마땅해요.’ 잔인한 말이다, 로마인에게나 어울릴 말이지. 나는 그 말을 내게도 적용해 보았다. 그 아비 같은 쇠약함이 내게도 닥쳐오리라는 것을 알기에—아니, 그것은 멀지 않았다. 하지만 너는, 에스더야, 결코 나를 두고 그런 말을 하지 않겠지—그래, 결코—‘차라리 돌아가시는 편이 나아.’ 그래, 네 어미는 유다의 딸이었다.”\n\n눈물이 반쯤 고인 채 그녀는 그에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. “저는 제 어머니의 자식이에요.”\n\n“그래, 그리고 내 딸이지—내 딸, 솔로몬에게 성전이 그러했듯이 내게 전부인 내 딸.”\n\n잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그는 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹고 다시 말을 이었다. “그가 그 이집트 여자를 아내로 맞이하고 나면, 에스더야, 그는 후회하며 너를 떠올리고 영혼으로 너를 부르게 될 것이다. 마침내 그는 깨어나, 자신이 한낱 그 여자의 못된 야망을 받드는 종에 지나지 않음을 알게 될 테니까. 로마야말로 그 여자의 온갖 꿈의 중심이다. 그 여자에게 그는 예루살렘의 군주 후르의 아들이 아니라, 두 집정관 가운데 하나였던 아리우스의 아들일 뿐이다.”\n\n에스더는 이 말이 미친 영향을 굳이 감추려 들지 않았다.\n\n“그분을 구해 주세요, 아버지! 아직 너무 늦지 않았어요!” 그녀가 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n그는 미심쩍은 미소를 지으며 대답했다. “물에 빠진 사람은 구할 수 있어도, 사랑에 빠진 사람은 그렇지 못한 법이란다.”\n\n“하지만 아버지는 그분에게 영향력이 있으시잖아요. 그분은 이 세상에 홀로예요. 그분께 위험을 일깨워 주세요. 그 여자가 어떤 여자인지 말씀해 주세요.”\n\n“그러면 그를 그 여자한테서 구할 수는 있을지도 모르지. 그렇다고 그를 네게 줄 수 있겠느냐, 에스더? 아니다.” 그러면서 그의 눈썹이 어둡게 두 눈 위로 내려앉았다. “나는 종이다, 내 조상들이 여러 대에 걸쳐 그러했듯이. 그러니 그에게 ‘보십시오, 주인님, 제 딸입니다! 이 아이는 그 이집트 여자보다 더 곱고, 당신을 더 깊이 사랑합니다!’ 하고 말할 수는 없다. 나는 자유와 결정권을 누린 세월에서 너무 많은 것을 물려받았다. 그런 말은 내 혀를 데게 할 것이다. 내가 저기 저 오래된 언덕들로 나아가면, 그 위의 돌들이 부끄러움에 제자리에서 돌아누울 것이다. 아니다, 조상들의 이름을 걸고, 에스더야, 차라리 나는 우리 둘을 네 어미 곁에, 그가 잠든 그 잠 속에 함께 뉘이고 말겠다!”\n\n에스더의 온 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐다.\n\n“그렇게 말씀해 달라는 뜻이 아니었어요, 아버지. 저는 그분만을 생각했어요—제 행복이 아니라 그분의 행복을요. 제가 감히 그분을 사랑한 이상, 저는 그분의 존경을 받을 만한 사람으로 저를 지킬 거예요. 그래야만 제 어리석음을 변명할 수 있으니까요. 이제 그분의 편지를 읽게 해 주세요.”\n\n“그래, 읽어 보아라.”\n\n그녀는 이 달갑잖은 이야기를 끝내고 싶은 마음에 곧장 읽기 시작했다.\n\n“니산월 여드렛날.\n\n“갈릴리에서 예루살렘으로 가는 길에서.\n\n“나사렛 사람도 같은 길을 가고 있습니다. 나는 그가 모르게, 내 군단 하나를 온전히 거느리고 그와 함께 가고 있습니다. 또 다른 군단 하나가 뒤따르고 있습니다. 유월절이 그 많은 무리를 너끈히 변명해 줄 것입니다. 그는 길을 나서며 이렇게 말했습니다. ‘우리가 예루살렘으로 올라가노니, 선지자들로 나를 두고 기록한 모든 것이 다 이루어지리라.’\n\n“우리의 기다림이 끝을 향해 가고 있습니다.\n\n“총총.\n\n“그대에게 평화가 있기를, 시모니데스.\n\n“벤허.”\n에스더는 편지를 아버지에게 돌려주었는데, 그러는 사이 목구멍에 무언가 메는 것이 차올랐다. 그 편지에는 그녀를 위한 말이 한마디도 없었다—인사말 속에서조차 그녀의 몫은 없었으니—“그대의 가족에게도 평화가 있기를” 한 줄 적기가 그토록 쉬웠을 텐데도. 평생 처음으로 그녀는 질투의 따끔한 가시를 느꼈다.\n\n“여드렛날이라.” 시모니데스가 말했다. “여드렛날이라. 그러면, 에스더야, 오늘이—”\n\n“아흐렛날이에요.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“아, 그렇다면 지금쯤 베다니에 와 있겠구나.”\n\n“그러면 어쩌면 오늘 밤에 그분을 뵐 수도 있겠어요.” 그녀는 잠시 모든 것을 잊고 기뻐하며 덧붙였다.\n\n“그럴지도, 그럴지도 모르지! 내일이 무교절(無酵節)이니, 그가 그것을 지키고 싶어 할지도 모른다. 나사렛 사람도 그러할 테고. 그러면 우리는 그를 볼 수 있을 게다—우리는 둘 다 볼 수 있을 게야, 에스더.”\n\n이때 하인이 포도주와 물을 가지고 나타났다. 에스더가 아버지의 시중을 드는데, 그 와중에 이라스가 지붕 위로 올라왔다.\n\n이 유대 처녀가 보기에 그 이집트 여자가 그 순간만큼 그토록, 그토록 아름다워 보인 적은 없었다. 얇은 비단옷이 자그마한 안개구름처럼 그녀의 몸을 감싸고 나풀거렸다. 이마와 목과 두 팔은 그 민족이 즐겨 다는 묵직한 패물로 번쩍였다. 그녀의 얼굴은 기쁨으로 가득 차 있었다. 그녀는 들뜬 걸음으로, 스스로를 의식하면서도 꾸밈은 없이 움직였다. 그 모습에 에스더는 제 속으로 움츠러들며 아버지에게 더 바짝 붙었다.\n\n“그대에게 평화가 있기를, 시모니데스. 그리고 어여쁜 에스더에게도 평화를.” 이라스가 에스더에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “선량한 어르신, 무례가 아니라면 말씀드리건대—어르신을 뵈니 페르시아의 사제들이 떠오르는군요. 그들은 날이 저물 무렵 신전 위로 올라가, 떠나가는 해를 뒤쫓아 기도를 보내지요. 그 예배에 대해 모르시는 것이 있거든, 제 아버지를 부르게 해 주세요. 그분은 마기교(敎)의 가르침을 받고 자라셨으니까요.”\n\n“아리따운 이집트 처녀여,” 상인은 진중한 예의로 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. “그대의 아버지는 선량한 분으로, 내가 그대에게 그분의 페르시아 학문은 그분 지혜의 가장 작은 부분일 뿐이라고 말했다는 것을 아셔도 결코 언짢아하지 않으실 게요.”\n\n이라스의 입술이 살짝 비틀어졌다.\n\n“어르신께서 그리 하라 하시니 철학자처럼 말해 보자면,” 그녀가 말했다. “가장 작은 부분이 있다는 것은 언제나 더 큰 부분이 있다는 뜻이지요. 어르신께서 그분께 기꺼이 돌리시는 그 드문 자질 가운데, 더 큰 부분이라 여기시는 것이 무엇인지 여쭤도 될까요?”\n\n시모니데스는 다소 엄한 얼굴로 그녀를 돌아보았다.\n\n“순수한 지혜는 언제나 하느님을 향한다오. 가장 순수한 지혜는 하느님을 아는 것이지. 그리고 내가 아는 사람 가운데 그것을 선량한 발타사르보다 더 높이 지닌 이도, 말과 행실로 그것을 더 뚜렷이 드러내는 이도 없소.”\n\n입씨름을 끝내려고 그는 잔을 들어 마셨다.\n\n이집트 여자는 다소 짜증스럽게 에스더 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n“창고에 수백만 금을 쌓아 두고 바다에 함대를 띄워 둔 사람은, 우리 같은 순박한 여인들이 무엇에서 재미를 찾는지 알아차리지 못하는 법이지. 우리는 어르신을 두고 갑시다. 저기 담장 옆에 가면 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠어요.”\n\n그리하여 그들은 난간으로 갔는데, 여러 해 전 벤허가 깨진 기와 조각을 그라투스의 머리 위로 떨어뜨린 바로 그 자리에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n“로마에 가 본 적 없나요?” 이라스가 끌러 둔 팔찌 하나를 만지작거리며 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“없어요.” 에스더가 얌전히 말했다.\n\n“가 보고 싶지도 않았나요?”\n\n“네.”\n\n“아, 그대의 삶에는 어쩌면 그토록 가진 것이 없을까!”\n\n그 탄식 뒤에 이어진 한숨은, 잃은 것이 이집트 여자 자신의 것이었다 해도 그보다 더 가련하게 들릴 수 없었을 것이다. 다음 순간 그녀의 웃음소리는 아래 거리에서도 들릴 만했다. 그러고는 그녀가 말했다. “오, 오, 가엾은 내 숙맥! 멤피스의 모래밭에 솟은 저 거대한 흉상의 귓속에 둥지를 튼, 깃도 채 나지 않은 새 새끼들도 그대만큼은 알 거예요.”\n\n그러다 에스더가 당황하는 것을 보고는 태도를 바꾸어, 속을 터놓는 어조로 말했다. “언짢게 여기면 안 돼요. 오, 그러지 마요! 장난친 거예요. 내가 입맞춤으로 그 아픈 데를 달래 주고, 다른 누구에게도—심벨 신이 친히 청하며 나일강 물보라를 담은 연꽃 잔을 내민다 해도—하지 않을 이야기를 들려줄게요!”\n\n또 한 번 웃음을 터뜨려, 유대 처녀에게 날카롭게 던진 눈길을 감쪽같이 가리고는, 그녀가 말했다. “왕이 오신답니다.”\n\n에스더는 순진한 놀라움으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n“나사렛 사람 말이에요.” 이라스가 말을 이었다. “우리 아버지들이 그토록 많이 입에 올리던 그 사람, 벤허가 그토록 오래 섬기고 애써 온 그 사람.” 그녀의 목소리가 몇 음 더 낮아졌다. “나사렛 사람은 내일 이곳에 올 거예요. 그리고 벤허는 오늘 밤에요.”\n\n에스더는 침착함을 지키려 애썼으나 그러지 못했다. 그녀의 눈이 내리깔리고, 마음을 들켜 버린 피가 두 뺨과 이마로 솟구쳤다. 그래서 그녀는 이집트 여자의 얼굴 위로 한 줄기 빛처럼 스쳐 간 그 의기양양한 미소를 보지 못했다.\n\n“보세요, 여기 그분의 약속이 있어요.”\n\n그러고는 허리띠에서 두루마리 하나를 꺼냈다.\n\n“나와 함께 기뻐해 줘요, 오 나의 벗이여! 그분은 오늘 밤 이곳에 올 거예요! 티베르 강가에 집이 한 채 있는데, 왕가의 재산이지요. 그분이 그것을 내게 약속하셨어요. 그 집의 안주인이 된다는 것은 곧—”\n\n아래 거리를 누군가 빠른 걸음으로 지나는 소리가 그 말을 끊었고, 그녀는 보려고 난간 너머로 몸을 기울였다. 그러다 다시 물러서며, 두 손을 머리 위로 맞잡고 외쳤다. “이제 이시스 여신께 축복 있으라! 그분이에요—벤허 본인이에요! 내가 그분을 이토록 생각하고 있는데 그분이 나타나다니! 이것이 길조가 아니라면 신은 없는 거예요. 나를 안아 줘요, 에스더—그리고 입맞춤도!”\n\n유대 처녀가 고개를 들었다. 두 뺨에는 저마다 발그레한 기운이 어렸고, 그녀의 눈은 일찍이 그 천성이 내뿜은 적 없던, 분노에 가까운 빛으로 번득였다. 그녀의 온순함이 너무도 거칠게 짓밟힌 것이었다. 사랑하는 사람을 두고 덧없는 꿈조차 마음껏 꾸지 못하도록 금지당한 것만으로도 모자란데, 으스대는 연적이 자신의 더 큰 성공을, 그리고 그 보답으로 받은 빛나는 약속들을 은밀히 일러 주기까지 한 것이다. 종의 종에 지나지 않는 그녀에게는 기억해 주는 기색 한 점 없었건만, 이 다른 여자는 제 편지를 보여 줄 수 있었고, 그 편지에 깃든 모든 것을 그녀더러 상상하게 내버려 두었다. 그래서 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그대는 그분을 그토록 사랑하나요, 아니면 로마를 그보다 더 사랑하나요?”\n\n이집트 여자는 한 걸음 물러섰다. 그러더니 거만한 고개를 숙여 질문한 이에게 바짝 들이댔다.\n\n“그분이 그대에게 무엇이지요, 시모니데스의 딸이여?”\n\n에스더는 온몸을 떨며 입을 열었다. “그분은 제—”\n\n번개처럼 모든 것을 태워 버릴 듯한 한 생각이 그 말을 멈춰 세웠다. 그녀는 핏기를 잃고 떨다가, 다시 정신을 차려 대답했다.\n\n“그분은 제 아버지의 벗이에요.”\n\n그녀의 혀가 자신의 종된 처지를 인정하기를 거부한 것이다.\n\n이라스는 아까보다 더 가볍게 웃었다.\n\n“그뿐이에요?” 그녀가 말했다. “아, 이집트의 사랑의 신들을 걸고, 그대의 입맞춤은 그대가 간직해도 좋아요—간직해 둬요. 그대는 방금, 여기 유대 땅에서 나를 기다리는, 비할 데 없이 더 값진 다른 이들이 있다는 것을 일러 주었으니까요. 그리고”—그녀는 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 돌아섰다—“나는 가서 그 입맞춤들을 받겠어요. 그대에게 평화가 있기를.”\n\n에스더는 그녀가 계단 아래로 사라지는 것을 지켜보다가, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 눈물이 손가락 사이로 뜨겁게 흘러내렸다—수치와, 목이 메도록 북받치는 격정의 눈물이었다. 그리고 그토록 차분하던 그녀의 성정에는 이상하리만치 그 발작을 더욱 깊게 하느라, 아버지의 말이 사람을 시들게 하는 새로운 힘의 의미를 띠고 되살아났다—“내가 가졌던 모든 것을, 능히 그럴 수 있었던 대로 단단히 붙들고 있었더라면 네 사랑이 헛되이 바쳐지지는 않았을지도 모른다.”\n\n그녀가 가까스로 마음을 추슬러 정자로 돌아가, 말없이 아버지 곁 제 자리에 앉아 그의 분부를 겸허히 기다릴 수 있게 되었을 무렵에는, 도성과 그것을 에워싼 어두운 산줄기 위로 별들이 죄다 나와 낮게 타오르고 있었다. 그런 도리에 그녀의 젊음이, 아니 어쩌면 그녀의 삶이 바쳐져야 할 것만 같았다. 그리고 진실을 말하자면, 이제 아픔이 가신 터라, 그녀는 그 도리로 돌아가는 것이 그리 내키지 않는 일만은 아니었다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n지붕 위에서 있었던 장면이 끝나고 한 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 발타사르와 시모니데스가 궁전의 큰 방에서 만났다. 시모니데스 곁에는 에스더가 시중을 들고 있었다. 두 사람이 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 벤허와 이라스가 함께 들어왔다.\n\n젊은 유대인은 길동무보다 앞서 나아가, 먼저 발타사르에게 다가가 인사를 건네고 답례를 받았다. 그런 다음 시모니데스에게로 몸을 돌렸으나, 에스더를 보고는 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n우리 마음은 한꺼번에 두 가지 넘는 정념을 한껏 품을 만큼 넉넉하지 못할 때가 많다. 어느 하나가 활활 타오르면 나머지는 살아남을지언정 더 작은 불빛으로 머물 뿐이다. 벤허도 그러했다. 온갖 가능성을 거듭 따져 보고, 희망과 꿈에 마음을 내맡기고, 조국의 처지에서 비롯된 영향과 더 직접적인 영향—이를테면 이라스가 끼친 영향—을 받으며, 그는 세속의 가장 넓은 의미에서 야심가가 되어 갔다. 그 정념에게 자리를 내주어 그것이 하나의 법칙이 되도록, 마침내 거역할 수 없는 지배자가 되도록 내버려 두는 사이, 지난날의 결의와 충동은 알게 모르게 스러져 가다가 끝내 거의 기억에서조차 사라졌다. 누구든 제 젊은 날을 잊기란 너무도 쉬운 법이다. 그의 경우, 자신을 둘러싼 모든 환상을 채우는 목표에 적어도 희망 속에서나마 점점 더 가까이 다가갈수록, 제가 겪은 고통과 가족의 운명을 어둠으로 덮은 그 수수께끼가 점점 덜 마음을 흔든 것도 그저 자연스러운 일이었다. 다만 그를 너무 가혹하게 판단하지는 말자.\n\n그는 에스더가 이제 어엿한 여인이 되었고, 또 그토록 아름다워진 것을 보고 놀라 걸음을 멈췄다. 그렇게 서서 그녀를 바라보고 있노라니, 어떤 고요한 목소리가 깨어진 맹세와 다하지 못한 의무를 일깨워 주었다. 거의 옛 자신이 되돌아온 듯했다.\n\n한순간 그는 흠칫했으나, 곧 마음을 가다듬고 에스더에게 다가가 말했다. \"그대에게 평안이 있기를, 어여쁜 에스더여—평안이 있기를. 그리고 그대, 시모니데스여\"—그는 말하면서 상인을 바라보았다—\"주님의 축복이 그대에게 함께하기를. 다른 까닭이 없더라도, 그대가 아비 없는 자에게 좋은 아비가 되어 주었으니 말이오.\"\n\n에스더는 고개를 떨군 채 그 말을 들었다. 시모니데스가 대답했다.\n\n\"훌륭한 발타사르께서 베푸신 환대를 나도 거듭 전하리다, 후르의 아들이여—그대 아버지의 집에 온 것을 환영하오. 자, 앉아서 그대의 여행과 그대가 한 일, 그리고 그 놀라운 나사렛 사람에 대해 들려주오—그가 누구이며 무엇인지를. 그대가 여기서 편안하지 못하다면, 누가 편안하겠소? 부디 앉으시오—거기, 우리 사이에 앉아 우리 모두가 들을 수 있게 말이오.\"\n\n에스더가 잰걸음으로 나가 덮개 씌운 걸상을 가져와 그를 위해 놓아 주었다.\n\n\"고맙소.\" 그가 그녀에게 고마운 마음으로 말했다.\n\n자리에 앉은 그는 다른 이야기를 조금 나눈 뒤, 두 남자를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"나는 나사렛 사람에 대해 말씀드리려고 왔습니다.\"\n\n두 사람은 곧바로 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"여러 날 동안 나는 그를 따라다녔습니다. 누군가를 그토록 애타게 기다리는 사람이 그 대상에게 기울일 만한 그런 주의를 다해서 말입니다. 사람을 시험하고 떠본다고들 하는 온갖 상황에서 그를 지켜보았습니다. 그가 나와 같은 사람이라는 것은 분명하지만, 동시에 그가 그 이상의 무언가라는 것 또한 못지않게 분명합니다.\"\n\n\"무엇 이상이란 말이오?\" 시모니데스가 물었다.\n\n\"말씀드리지요—\"\n\n누군가 방으로 들어오는 바람에 그의 말이 끊겼다. 그가 몸을 돌리며 두 손을 내밀고 일어섰다.\n\n\"암라! 정다운 암라 할멈!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n그녀가 앞으로 나왔다. 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 기쁨을 본 사람들은, 그 얼굴이 얼마나 주름지고 거무튀튀한지는 한 번도 생각하지 않았다. 그녀는 그의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 그의 무릎을 끌어안았으며, 그의 두 손에 거듭 입을 맞추었다. 그는 짬을 내어 그녀의 뺨에서 가느다란 잿빛 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기고, 그 뺨에 입 맞추며 말했다. \"착한 암라여, 그들에 대해 아무것도, 정말 아무것도 모르는가—단 한마디도—아주 작은 기별 하나도?\"\n\n그러자 그녀는 흐느낌을 터뜨렸으니, 그것은 어떤 말보다도 더 분명하게 그에게 대답해 주었다.\n\n\"하나님의 뜻이 이루어졌구나.\" 이어 그가 엄숙하게 말했으니, 그 어조는 듣는 이마다 그가 이제 제 가족을 찾을 희망을 더는 품지 않음을 알게 했다. 그의 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었으나, 그는 사내였으므로 남들에게 그것을 보이려 하지 않았다.\n\n다시 추스를 수 있게 되자, 그는 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"자, 내 곁에 앉게, 암라여—여기에. 싫은가? 그럼 내 발치에라도 앉게. 세상에 오신 한 놀라운 분에 대해 이 좋은 벗들에게 들려줄 말이 많으니 말일세.\" 그러나 그녀는 자리를 떠나, 벽에 등을 대고 웅크려 앉아 무릎 앞에 두 손을 모았다. 모두가 보기에, 그녀는 그를 바라보는 것만으로도 만족하는 듯했다. 그러자 벤허는 두 노인에게 고개를 숙여 보이고 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나사렛 사람에 대해 받은 물음에 답하기 전에, 내가 그가 하는 일을 직접 본 몇 가지를 먼저 말씀드리지 않으면 두려울 것 같습니다. 더욱 그러고 싶은 까닭은, 벗들이여, 내일 그가 도성으로 와서 그가 제 아버지의 집이라 부르는 성전으로 올라갈 것이기 때문입니다. 게다가 거기서 그가 스스로를 드러내 보이리라는 말도 있습니다. 그러니 발타사르여, 그대가 옳든, 시모니데스여, 그대가 옳든, 우리와 이스라엘은 내일이면 알게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n발타사르가 두 손을 떨리는 듯 비비며 물었다. \"그를 보려면 어디로 가야 하겠소?\"\n\n\"군중에 밀려 몹시 혼잡할 것입니다. 회랑 위 지붕으로—이를테면 솔로몬 행각으로 다들 올라가시는 편이 나으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 벤허가 말했다. \"내 벗들이 어쩌면 행렬에서 나를 필요로 할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"행렬이라니!\" 시모니데스가 소리쳤다. \"그가 위엄을 갖추어 다닌단 말이오?\"\n\n벤허는 그 마음속에 깃든 속뜻을 알아챘다.\n\n\"그는 열두 사람을 데리고 다닙니다. 어부와 농부, 그 가운데 하나는 세리이며, 모두 비천한 계층의 사람들입니다. 그와 그들은 바람도 추위도 비도 햇볕도 아랑곳하지 않고 걸어서 길을 갑니다. 해 질 녘 길가에 멈추어 빵을 떼거나 누워 잠을 청하는 그들을 보고 있으면, 나는 귀족이나 왕이 아니라 장에서 제 양 떼에게로 돌아가는 한 무리의 목자들을 떠올리곤 합니다. 다만 그가 누군가를 보려고, 혹은 머리에서 먼지를 털려고 머릿수건 자락을 들어 올릴 때에야, 나는 그가 그들의 동무일 뿐 아니라 스승이며—벗인 동시에 그들보다 윗사람임을 깨닫게 됩니다.\n\n\"두 분은 명민한 분들이십니다.\" 벤허가 잠시 멈추었다가 다시 말을 이었다. \"우리가 어떤 으뜸가는 동기에 사로잡힌 존재인지, 또 어떤 목적을 열심히 좇으며 한평생을 보내는 것이 우리 본성의 법칙이나 다름없이 되었다는 것을 두 분은 아십니다. 그러니 우리 자신을 가늠할 잣대로서 그 법칙에 호소하건대, 발밑의 돌을 황금으로 바꾸어 부자가 될 수 있는데도 스스로 가난을 택한 사람을 두고 무어라 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그리스 사람이라면 철학자라 부르겠지요.\" 이라스가 말했다.\n\n\"아니다, 딸아.\" 발타사르가 말했다. \"철학자들은 그런 일을 할 힘을 가진 적이 없었느니라.\"\n\n\"이 사람이 그런 힘을 지녔다는 것을 어찌 아시는지요?\"\n\n벤허가 곧바로 대답했다. \"나는 그가 물을 포도주로 바꾸는 것을 보았습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 기이하고, 참으로 기이하오.\" 시모니데스가 말했다. \"허나 내게는 그것이 그토록 부유해질 수 있는데도 가난하게 살기를 택했다는 사실만큼 기이하지는 않소. 그가 그토록 가난하단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그는 아무것도 가진 것이 없고, 누가 무엇을 가졌든 부러워하지 않습니다. 그는 도리어 부자를 가엾이 여깁니다. 그런데 그 일은 그만두고라도, 한 사람이 빵 일곱 덩이와 물고기 두 마리, 곧 가진 것 전부를 불려서 오천 명을 먹이고도 광주리에 가득 남기는 것을 보았다면 무어라 하시겠습니까? 나는 나사렛 사람이 그렇게 하는 것을 보았습니다.\"\n\n\"그대가 그것을 보았단 말이오?\" 시모니데스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 게다가 그 빵과 물고기를 먹기도 했습니다.\"\n\n\"더욱 놀라운 일은,\" 벤허가 말을 이었다. \"병자가 그저 그 옷자락에 손을 대기만 해도, 혹은 멀리서 그에게 부르짖기만 해도 낫게 하는 그런 치유의 힘을 지닌 사람을 두고는 무어라 하시겠습니까? 그것 또한 나는 한 번이 아니라 여러 번 목격했습니다. 우리가 여리고를 나설 때 길가의 눈먼 두 사람이 나사렛 사람을 불렀는데, 그가 그들의 눈에 손을 대니 그들이 보게 되었습니다. 또 사람들이 중풍 든 자를 그에게 데려오자, 그는 그저 '네 집으로 가라'고만 했는데, 그 사람은 멀쩡해져서 떠나갔습니다. 이런 일들을 두고 무어라 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n상인은 아무런 대답이 없었다.\n\n\"이제 두 분께서는, 다른 이들이 따지는 것을 내가 들었던 대로, 내가 들려드린 일들이 요술쟁이의 속임수라 여기십니까? 그렇다면 내가 본 더 큰 일들을 떠올려 답해 드리겠습니다. 먼저 하나님의 저주, 곧 두 분 모두 아시다시피 죽음 말고는 달랠 길이 없는 그 나병을 보십시오.\"\n\n이 말에 암라가 두 손을 바닥에 내려놓고는, 그의 말을 듣고 싶은 간절함에 반쯤 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"무어라 하시겠습니까,\" 벤허가 한층 진지하게 말했다. \"내가 이제 들려드리는 것을 직접 보셨다면 무어라 하시겠습니까? 내가 갈릴리에 그와 함께 내려가 있을 때, 한 나병환자가 나사렛 사람에게 와서 말했습니다. '주여, 원하시면 저를 깨끗하게 하실 수 있나이다.' 그가 그 부르짖음을 듣고 손으로 그 버림받은 자를 어루만지며 말했습니다. '깨끗함을 받으라.' 그러자 그 사람은 곧바로 본래의 자신으로 돌아와, 그 치유를 지켜본 우리 가운데 누구 못지않게 건강해졌습니다. 그리고 우리는 한 무리의 군중이었습니다.\"\n\n이때 암라가 몸을 일으켜, 앙상한 손가락으로 눈을 가린 뻣뻣한 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겼다. 이 가엾은 여인의 머릿속 생각은 이미 오래전에 가슴으로 옮겨 가 버린 터라, 그녀는 그 이야기를 따라가기가 힘겨웠다.\n\n\"그리고 또,\" 벤허가 멈추지 않고 말했다. \"어느 날 나병환자 열 사람이 한꺼번에 그에게 와서 발치에 엎드려 부르짖었습니다—나는 그 모든 것을 보고 들었습니다—부르짖기를, '선생님, 선생님, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!' 그가 그들에게 일렀습니다. '가서 율법이 정한 대로 제사장에게 너희 몸을 보이라. 거기에 이르기 전에 너희가 나으리라.'\"\n\n\"그래서 나았소?\"\n\n\"네. 가는 길에 그들의 병이 떠나가, 더럽혀진 옷 말고는 그 병을 떠올리게 할 것이 아무것도 남지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그런 일은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없소—온 이스라엘을 통틀어 한 번도 없었소!\" 시모니데스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그가 말하는 동안, 암라는 몸을 돌려 소리 없이 문으로 걸어가 밖으로 나갔다. 그러나 그녀가 나가는 것을 일행 가운데 누구도 보지 못했다.\n\n\"내 눈앞에서 벌어진 그런 일들이 불러일으킨 생각이 어떠했을지는 두 분의 상상에 맡기겠습니다.\" 벤허가 말을 이었다. \"허나 나의 의혹과 불안과 경탄은 아직 그 끝에 이르지 않았습니다. 두 분도 아시다시피 갈릴리 사람들은 성미가 급하고 무모합니다. 여러 해를 기다린 끝에 그들의 손은 칼자루를 쥐고 싶어 달아올랐고, 행동 말고는 아무것도 그들을 만족시키지 못했습니다. '그분은 좀처럼 스스로를 드러내지 않으니, 우리가 밀어붙입시다.' 그들이 내게 외쳤습니다. 나 또한 조바심이 났습니다. 그가 왕이 되려 한다면 어찌하여 지금이 아닌가? 군단은 준비되어 있는데. 그래서 그가 한번 바닷가에서 가르치고 있을 때, 우리는 그가 원하든 원치 않든 그에게 왕관을 씌우려 했습니다. 그러나 그는 사라졌고, 이내 기슭을 떠나는 배 위에서 모습을 보였습니다. 훌륭한 시모니데스여, 다른 사람들을 미치게 하는 욕망들—부와 권력, 심지어 위대한 백성이 큰 사랑으로 바치는 왕위마저도—이 사람만은 조금도 움직이지 못합니다. 무어라 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n상인의 턱은 가슴께로 낮게 떨구어져 있었다. 그가 고개를 들고 단호하게 대답했다. \"주께서 살아 계시고, 예언자들의 말씀 또한 살아 있소. 때는 아직 무르익지 않았으니, 내일이 답하게 합시다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 합시다.\" 발타사르가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n벤허도 \"그렇게 합시다\" 하고 말했다. 그런 다음 그가 말을 이었다. \"허나 아직 끝나지 않았습니다. 나처럼 그 행함을 직접 보지 못한 이라면 의심해 봄 직도 한, 그러나 의심하기에는 너무도 큰 이 일들로부터, 이제 두 분을 한없이 더 큰 일들로 이끌고자 합니다. 세상이 시작된 이래로 사람의 힘을 넘어선 것이라 누구나 인정해 온 일들 말입니다. 말씀해 보십시오, 두 분이 아시는 한, 죽음이 제 것으로 삼은 것을 손을 뻗어 죽음에게서 도로 빼앗은 사람이 일찍이 있었습니까? 잃어버린 생명에 다시 숨을 불어넣은 이가 누구입니까? 오직—\"\n\n\"하나님이시지요!\" 발타사르가 경건하게 말했다.\n\n벤허가 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"오, 지혜로운 이집트인이여! 그대가 일러 주신 그 이름을 나도 마다하지 못하겠습니다. 두 분께서는—아니, 시모니데스여, 그대는—내가 본 것을, 곧 한 사람이 몇 마디 말뿐 아무 의식도 없이, 잠든 아이를 깨우려 말 건네는 어머니의 수고보다도 더 힘들이지 않고 죽음이 한 일을 되돌려 놓는 것을 보셨다면, 둘 중 하나든 두 분 다든 무어라 하셨겠습니까? 그것은 나인에서였습니다. 우리가 막 성문으로 들어가려 할 때, 한 무리가 죽은 사람을 메고 나왔습니다. 나사렛 사람은 그 행렬이 지나가도록 멈춰 섰습니다. 그들 가운데 한 여인이 울고 있었습니다. 그의 얼굴이 연민으로 부드러워지는 것을 나는 보았습니다. 그가 그녀에게 말을 건넨 뒤, 다가가 상여에 손을 대고, 장례 차림으로 그 위에 누운 자에게 말했습니다. '청년아, 내가 네게 이르노니, 일어나라!' 그러자 곧바로 죽었던 자가 일어나 앉아 말을 했습니다.\"\n\n\"오직 하나님만이 그토록 위대하시오.\" 발타사르가 시모니데스에게 말했다.\n\n\"새겨들으십시오.\" 벤허가 계속했다. \"나는 다른 수많은 사람들과 함께 내가 목격한 일들만을 말씀드리는 것입니다. 이리로 오는 길에 나는 더욱 강력한 또 한 가지 일을 보았습니다. 베다니에 나사로라는 사람이 있었는데, 그가 죽어 묻혔습니다. 그가 큰 돌로 막힌 무덤 속에 나흘 동안이나 누워 있은 뒤, 나사렛 사람이 그곳으로 안내되었습니다. 돌을 굴려 치우자, 우리는 그 사람이 안에서 묶인 채 썩어 가며 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다. 곁에 서 있는 사람이 많았는데, 나사렛 사람이 큰 소리로 말했으므로 우리 모두가 그 말을 들었습니다. '나사로야, 나오너라!' 그 부름에 답하기라도 하듯 그 사람이 온몸에 수의를 두른 채 일어나 우리에게로 걸어 나왔을 때, 내 심정이 어떠했는지는 차마 다 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 나사렛 사람이 다음으로 말했습니다. '풀어 주라. 풀어 주어 가게 하라.' 그리고 그 되살아난 자의 얼굴에서 수건을 벗기니, 보십시오, 벗들이여! 시들어 버린 몸으로 피가 새로이 흘렀고, 그는 그를 데려간 병이 들기 전 살아 있을 때와 꼭 같았습니다. 그는 지금도 살아 있어, 시시각각 사람들이 그를 보고 그와 더불어 말합니다. 두 분도 내일 가서 그를 보실 수 있습니다. 자, 이제 이 목적을 위해 더 필요한 것이 없으니, 내가 여쭈러 온 것을 여쭙겠습니다. 그것은 시모니데스여, 그대가 내게 물으신 것을 되풀이하는 것에 지나지 않습니다. 이 나사렛 사람은 사람 이상의 무엇입니까?\"\n\n그 물음은 엄숙하게 던져졌고, 자정이 한참 지나도록 일행은 앉아서 그것을 두고 토론했다. 시모니데스는 여전히 예언자들의 말씀에 대한 제 나름의 이해를 버리려 하지 않았고, 벤허는 두 연로한 논객이 둘 다 옳다고—발타사르가 주장하는 대로 나사렛 사람이 구속자이며, 동시에 상인이 바라는 정해진 왕이기도 하다고 우겼다.\n\n\"내일이면 알게 되겠지요. 두 분 모두에게 평안이 있기를.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 벤허는 작별을 고했으니, 베다니로 돌아갈 작정이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n이튿날 아침, 양의 문이 열리자 가장 먼저 성을 나선 사람은 팔에 바구니를 건 암라였다. 문지기들은 그녀에게 아무것도 묻지 않았으니, 아침이 어김없이 찾아오듯 그녀 또한 어김없이 찾아왔기 때문이다. 그들은 그녀가 누군가의 충실한 하인이라는 것을 알았고, 그것으로 충분했다.\n\n그녀는 동쪽 골짜기로 길을 잡았다. 짙푸른 올리벳 산비탈에는 명절을 지키러 온 사람들이 최근에 세운 흰 천막들이 점점이 박혀 있었다. 그러나 이방인들이 나돌아 다니기에는 너무 이른 시각이었다. 설령 그렇지 않았더라도 아무도 그녀를 성가시게 하지 않았을 것이다. 그녀는 겟세마네를 지나고, 베다니로 가는 길이 만나는 곳의 무덤들을 지나고, 무덤이 많은 마을 실로암을 지나갔다. 이따금 노쇠한 작은 몸이 비틀거렸다. 한 번은 숨을 고르려고 주저앉았다가, 곧 다시 일어나 새로운 조급함으로 발걸음을 재촉했다. 길 양옆의 큰 바위들에 귀가 있었다면, 그녀가 혼자 중얼거리는 소리를 들었을지도 모른다. 눈이 있었다면, 그녀가 얼마나 자주 산 너머를 올려다보며 새벽이 너무 일찍 온 것을 나무라는지 보았을 것이다. 만약 바위들이 수다를 떨 수 있었다면, 서로 이렇게 말했을지도 모른다. \"우리 친구가 오늘 아침엔 급한가 보군. 먹여 살릴 입들이 어지간히 굶주린 모양이야.\"\n\n마침내 왕의 정원에 이르자 그녀는 걸음을 늦추었다. 이제 한놈의 골짜기 남쪽 언덕을 멀리 두르고 펼쳐진 음산한 나병 환자들의 도시가 눈에 들어왔기 때문이다.\n\n독자도 이쯤이면 짐작했겠지만, 그녀는 자기 여주인을 찾아가는 길이었다. 기억하겠지만, 그 여주인의 무덤은 엔로겔 우물을 내려다보는 자리에 있었다.\n\n이른 시각이었으나, 그 불행한 여인은 일어나 밖에 앉아 있었고, 안에는 티르자가 잠들어 있었다. 지난 삼 년 동안 병세는 무섭도록 빠르게 진행되었다. 자기 모습을 의식한 그녀는 본디 섬세한 천성으로, 늘 온몸을 가린 채 지냈다. 티르자에게조차도 되도록 자기 모습을 보이지 않았다.\n\n이날 아침 그녀는 맨머리로 바람을 쐬고 있었으니, 그 드러난 모습에 충격받을 사람이 아무도 없음을 알았기 때문이다. 빛은 충분치 않았으나, 그녀가 겪어 온 참혹한 변화를 드러내기에는 모자라지 않았다. 머리카락은 눈처럼 희고 손쓸 수 없이 거칠어, 수많은 은실처럼 등과 어깨로 흘러내렸다. 눈꺼풀과 입술과 콧구멍과 두 뺨의 살은 사라졌거나, 악취 나는 짓무른 살로 변해 있었다. 목은 잿빛 비늘 덩어리였다. 한쪽 손은 옷자락 밖으로 나와 해골처럼 뻣뻣하게 놓여 있었다. 손톱은 다 파먹혔고, 손가락 마디는 뼈까지 드러나지는 않았더라도, 붉은 분비물로 딱지가 앉은 부어오른 혹이었다. 머리와 얼굴과 목과 손은 온몸의 상태가 어떠한지를 너무도 또렷이 일러 주었다. 이러한 모습을 보면, 한때 어여뻤던 후르 가문의 미망인이 그토록 여러 해 동안 어떻게 그렇게 잘 정체를 숨길 수 있었는지 쉬이 이해할 수 있었다.\n\n해가 그 옛 땅에서 서방보다 한결 날카롭고 눈부신 빛으로 올리벳 산마루와 멸시의 산마루를 금빛으로 물들일 무렵이면, 그녀는 암라가 오리라는 것을 알았다. 먼저 우물로, 다음에는 우물과 그녀가 거처하는 언덕 기슭 중간의 돌로 와서, 그 착한 하인이 바구니에 담아 온 음식을 그곳에 내려놓고 그날 쓸 물항아리를 새로 채워 두리라는 것을. 한때 넘치도록 누렸던 행복 가운데, 이 불행한 여인에게 남은 것은 그 짧은 방문뿐이었다. 그때면 그녀는 아들의 소식을 물을 수 있었고, 그가 잘 지낸다는 말과, 심부름꾼이 주워 모을 수 있는 만큼의 소식을 들을 수 있었다. 대개 그 소식은 매우 빈약했으나, 그래도 위안이 되었다. 때때로 그녀는 아들이 집에 와 있다는 말을 들었다. 그러면 그녀는 동틀 녘에 음산한 굴에서 나와 정오까지, 다시 정오부터 해질녘까지, 흰옷을 두른 채 미동도 없이 앉아, 마치 조각상처럼 늘 한 곳만을 바라보았다. 성전 너머, 둥근 하늘 아래, 기억 속에 정겨운, 그리고 아들이 그곳에 있기에 더욱 정겨운 옛집이 서 있던 그 자리를. 그녀에게 남은 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 없었다. 티르자는 죽은 자로 쳤다. 그리고 자기 자신으로 말하자면, 삶의 매 순간이 죽어 가는 순간임을, 다행히도 고통 없이 죽어 가는 순간임을 알면서, 그저 끝을 기다릴 뿐이었다.\n\n언덕 둘레에서 그녀를 세상의 매력에 마음 쓰게 할 만한 자연의 것들은 비참하리만치 모자랐다. 짐승과 새들은 마치 그 내력과 지금의 쓰임새를 알기라도 하듯 그곳을 피했다. 푸른 것은 무엇이든 첫 철에 시들어 버렸다. 바람은 관목과 위태로이 돋은 풀들과 싸워, 뽑아내지 못한 것은 가뭄에 내맡겼다. 어디를 바라보든, 무덤들이 그 풍경을 우울하게 물들였다. 그녀의 위에도 무덤, 아래에도 무덤, 그녀의 무덤 맞은편에도 무덤이었으니, 이제 모두 순례객들에게 경고하느라 새로 하얗게 칠해져 있었다. 맑고 고와 마음을 끄는 하늘에서는 마음의 아픔을 덜어 줄 무언가를 찾을 수 있으리라 여길 법도 했다. 그러나 아아, 다른 곳을 아름답게 만들면서도 해는 그녀에게는 결코 그토록 매정하게 굴었으니, 다만 점점 더해 가는 그녀의 흉측함을 드러낼 뿐이었다. 해만 없었더라면, 그녀는 스스로에게 그토록 끔찍한 존재가 되지도 않았을 것이며, 예전처럼 티르자의 꿈에서 그토록 잔인하게 깨어나지도 않았을 것이다. 본다는 재능이 때로는 무서운 저주가 될 수도 있는 것이다.\n\n어찌하여 그녀가 자기 고통을 끝내지 않았는지 묻는 이가 있을까?\n율법이 그것을 금했기 때문이다!\n\n# THE LAW FORBADE HER!\n\n이방인이라면 이 대답에 웃을지도 모른다. 그러나 이스라엘의 자손이라면 결코 웃지 않으리라.\n\n그녀가 그곳에 앉아, 한층 더 음울한 생각들로 그 어스레한 적막을 채우고 있을 때, 갑자기 한 여인이 비틀거리며, 안간힘을 다 쓴 듯 지친 모습으로 언덕을 올라왔다.\n\n미망인은 황급히 일어나, 머리를 가리고는, 부자연스레 거친 목소리로 외쳤다. \"부정한 몸이다, 부정한 몸이야!\"\n\n그러나 암라는 그 경고에 아랑곳없이 한순간에 그녀의 발치에 엎드렸다. 그 순박한 여인이 오래 가두어 둔 사랑이 한꺼번에 터져 나왔다. 그녀는 눈물과 격정 어린 부르짖음으로 여주인의 옷에 입을 맞추었고, 여주인은 한동안 그녀에게서 벗어나려 애썼으나, 그럴 수 없음을 깨닫고는 그 격발의 격정이 가라앉기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"무슨 짓을 한 거냐, 암라?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 불순종으로 우리를 향한 네 사랑을 증명하겠다는 거냐? 어리석은 여인 같으니! 너는 이제 끝이다. 그리고 그분, 네 주인에게로 너는 결코, 결코 돌아갈 수 없게 되었어.\"\n\n암라는 흐느끼며 흙바닥에 엎드렸다.\n\n\"율법의 금령이 이제 네게도 내린 거야. 너는 예루살렘으로 돌아갈 수 없어. 우리는 어찌 되겠느냐? 누가 우리에게 빵을 가져다주겠어? 오, 어리석고 어리석은 암라야! 우리는 모두, 모두 똑같이 끝장나고 말았구나!\"\n\n\"용서를, 용서를 해 주세요!\" 암라가 땅바닥에서 대답했다.\n\n\"너 자신에게 자비를 베풀었어야지. 그랬다면 우리에게 가장 큰 자비를 베푸는 것이 되었을 텐데. 이제 우리가 어디로 달아나겠느냐? 우리를 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없어. 오, 못난 하인아! 주님의 진노가 이미 우리에게 너무도 무겁게 내려 있거늘.\"\n\n이때 그 소리에 잠을 깬 티르자가 무덤 입구에 나타났다. 붓으로는 그녀가 드러낸 모습을 차마 그릴 수가 없다. 반쯤 옷을 걸친 그 허깨비 같은 모습은 비늘로 덮이고, 검푸르게 갈라지고, 거의 앞을 보지 못했으며, 팔다리와 손발은 흉측하게 커다랗도록 부어 있었으니, 사랑으로 아무리 눈이 밝아진들 낯익은 눈으로도, 우리가 처음 보았던 그 어린아이다운 우아함과 순결함을 지닌 존재를 알아볼 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"암라예요, 어머니?\"\n\n하인은 그녀에게도 기어가려 했다.\n\n\"멈춰라, 암라!\" 미망인이 위엄 있게 외쳤다. \"저 아이를 만지는 것을 금한다. 일어나서, 우물가의 누구든 너를 보기 전에 어서 가거라. 아니, 잊고 있었구나. 이제 너무 늦었어! 너는 이제 남아서 우리의 운명을 함께 나눌 수밖에 없다. 일어나라니까!\"\n\n암라가 무릎을 꿇고 일어나, 두 손을 맞잡은 채 더듬거리며 말했다. \"오, 착하신 마님! 저는 거짓되지도, 사악하지도 않습니다. 좋은 소식을 가져왔어요.\"\n\n\"유다 소식이냐?\" 그녀가 말하며, 머리에서 천을 반쯤 걷어 냈다.\n\n\"놀라운 분이 있는데,\" 암라가 말을 이었다. \"마님을 고칠 능력이 있답니다. 그분이 한마디 말씀을 하시면, 병든 자가 낫고, 죽은 자까지도 살아난답니다. 제가 마님을 그분에게 모셔 가려고 왔어요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 암라야!\" 티르자가 가엾이 여기며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요,\" 암라가 그 말 아래 깔린 의심을 알아채고 외쳤다. \"아니에요, 주님께서, 곧 이스라엘의 주님이시며 마님의 하느님이자 제 하느님이신 그분께서 살아 계심을 두고, 저는 진실을 말하고 있어요. 저와 함께 가세요, 부디, 머뭇거리지 마시고요. 오늘 아침 그분이 성으로 가시는 길에 이리로 지나가십니다. 보세요! 날이 밝아 옵니다. 여기 음식을 드세요. 잡수시고, 어서 가십시다.\"\n\n어머니는 귀를 곤두세우고 들었다. 그녀도 그 놀라운 사람의 소문을 들었을 법했으니, 이미 그의 명성이 온 땅 구석구석까지 스며들었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그가 누구냐?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"나사렛 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"누가 그에 대해 말해 주더냐?\"\n\n\"유다 도련님이요.\"\n\n\"유다가 네게 말했다고? 그 아이가 집에 있느냐?\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 오셨어요.\"\n\n미망인은 두근거리는 가슴을 가라앉히려 애쓰며 한동안 말이 없었다.\n\n\"유다가 우리에게 이것을 알리라고 너를 보냈느냐?\" 그녀가 이윽고 물었다.\n\n\"아니에요. 도련님은 마님이 돌아가신 줄로 알고 계세요.\"\n\n\"옛적에 나병 환자를 고친 예언자가 있었지.\" 어머니가 생각에 잠겨 티르자에게 말했다. \"하지만 그는 하느님께로부터 그 능력을 받았던 거란다.\" 그러고는 암라를 향해 물었다. \"내 아들은 그 사람이 그런 능력을 지녔다는 것을 어찌 알았다더냐?\"\n\n\"그분과 함께 길을 가시다가, 나병 환자들이 부르짖는 소리를 듣고, 그들이 다 나아 떠나가는 것을 보셨답니다. 처음엔 한 사람이었고, 다음엔 열 사람이었는데, 모두 온전해졌답니다.\"\n\n연배 든 이는 다시 입을 다물었다. 해골 같은 손이 떨렸다. 그녀가 그 이야기에 믿음의 인정을 부여하려고 애쓰고 있었다고 우리는 믿어도 좋으리라. 믿음이란 언제나 절대적인 것을 요구하는 법이다. 그녀도 그 시대 사람들, 곧 그리스도가 행한 일을 직접 목격한 자들이나, 그 뒤를 이은 수많은 무리와 다를 바 없었으리라. 그녀는 그 일이 행해졌다는 것을 의심하지는 않았으니, 다름 아닌 자기 아들이 하인을 통해 증언하는 증인이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀는 한낱 사람이 어떻게 그토록 놀라운 일을 해낼 수 있는지, 그 능력의 정체를 헤아리려 애썼다. 사실 여부를 캐묻는 것은 좋은 일이다. 그러나 그 능력을 헤아리려면, 먼저 하느님을 헤아려야 하는 법이니, 그것을 기다리는 자는 기다리다가 죽고 말 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 망설임은 잠시였다. 그녀가 티르자에게 말했다.\n\n\"이분은 틀림없이 메시아일 거야!\"\n\n그녀는 의심을 논리로 떨쳐 내려는 사람처럼 차갑게 말한 것이 아니라, 자기 민족을 향한 하느님의 약속에 익숙한 이스라엘의 여인으로서, 그 약속이 이루어지는 가장 작은 표징에도 기꺼이 기뻐할 준비가 된, 깨우친 여인으로서 말했다.\n\n\"한때 예루살렘과 온 유대가 그분이 태어났다는 이야기로 가득 찼던 때가 있었지. 나는 기억한단다. 지금쯤이면 그분도 어른이 되셨을 거야. 틀림없어. 바로 그분이야. 그래,\" 그녀가 암라에게 말했다. \"우리가 너와 함께 가마. 무덤 안에 항아리에 든 물을 가져오고, 우리에게 음식을 차려 주거라. 먹고 나서 떠나자꾸나.\"\n\n흥분 속에 든 아침 식사는 이내 끝났고, 세 여인은 그 예사롭지 않은 여정에 올랐다. 티르자도 다른 두 사람의 자신에 찬 마음을 받아들였으니, 일행을 괴롭히는 두려움은 단 하나뿐이었다. 암라의 말로는 그 사람이 베다니에서 오고 있다는 것이었다. 그런데 그곳에서 예루살렘으로 가는 길은 셋, 더 정확히 말하면 세 갈래의 작은 길이 있었다. 하나는 올리벳의 첫째 봉우리를 넘는 길이요, 둘째는 그 기슭을 지나는 길이며, 셋째는 둘째 봉우리와 멸시의 산 사이로 난 길이었다. 세 길은 서로 멀지 않았으나, 그 불행한 이들이 나사렛 사람이 택해 오는 길을 놓치면 그를 만나지 못할 만큼은 떨어져 있었다.\n\n조금 캐물어 본 어머니는 암라가 키드론 너머의 땅에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 것을, 그리고 그들이 만나려는 그 사람의 뜻에 대해서는 더더욱 모른다는 것을 알게 되었다. 또한 그녀는 암라와 티르자 둘 다, 하나는 몸에 밴 종살이 습성 탓에, 다른 하나는 타고난 의존심 탓에, 자신에게 길잡이 노릇을 기대하고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그래서 그 책임을 떠맡았다.\n\n\"우리는 먼저 벳바게로 가자.\" 그녀가 그들에게 말했다. \"거기서 주님께서 우리를 도우시면, 그다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 알게 될 거다.\"\n\n그들은 언덕을 내려가 도벳과 왕의 정원에 이르러, 수백 년 동안 길손들이 밟아 깊이 파인 자취 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"나는 저 길이 두렵구나.\" 부인이 말했다. \"차라리 바위와 나무들 사이로 들판을 따라가는 편이 낫겠어. 오늘은 명절날이라, 저 산비탈에 큰 무리가 모여든 기색이 보이는구나. 여기 멸시의 산을 가로질러 가면 그들을 피할 수 있을 게다.\"\n\n티르자는 무척 힘겹게 걷고 있었다. 이 말을 듣자 그녀의 마음이 약해지기 시작했다.\n\n\"산이 가파른걸요, 어머니. 저는 못 오르겠어요.\"\n\n\"명심하렴, 우리는 건강과 생명을 찾으러 가는 길이란다. 보아라, 얘야, 우리 둘레로 날이 얼마나 환히 밝아 오는지! 그리고 저기 여인들이 우물로 이리 오고 있구나. 우리가 여기 머물면 저들이 우리에게 돌을 던질 거야. 자, 이번 한 번만 굳세어지렴.\"\n\n이렇게 어머니는, 자기 자신도 못지않게 괴로우면서도 딸에게 용기를 북돋우려 했고, 암라가 그녀를 거들었다. 이때까지 암라는 병든 두 사람의 몸에 손을 대지 않았고, 그들도 암라에게 손을 대지 않았다. 그러나 이제 그 충실한 여인은 그 결과도, 명령도 아랑곳하지 않고 티르자에게 다가가, 그 어깨에 팔을 두르고 속삭였다. \"제게 기대세요. 저는 늙었어도 튼튼해요. 그리고 이제 얼마 안 남았어요. 자, 이제 갈 수 있어요.\"\n\n그들이 넘으려는 언덕 비탈은 구덩이며 옛 건물의 잔해로 다소 험했다. 그러나 마침내 꼭대기에 올라 쉬며, 북서쪽 너머로 펼쳐진 광경을 바라보았을 때, 곧 성전과 그 화려한 층계를, 시온을, 그리고 그 너머 하늘로 희게 솟아오른 든든한 탑들을 바라보았을 때, 어머니는 삶 그 자체를 향한 사랑으로 새 힘을 얻었다.\n\n\"보아라, 티르자,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아름다운 문에 입힌 금판들을 보렴. 저것들이 햇살을 어떻게 되비추는지, 빛에 빛으로 화답하는구나! 우리가 저기 올라가곤 했던 것을 기억하느냐? 다시 그리할 수 있다면 얼마나 즐겁겠니? 그리고 생각해 보렴, 집이 바로 코앞이야. 지성소 지붕 너머로 거의 보일 듯하구나. 그리고 유다가 거기서 우리를 맞아 줄 게다!\"\n\n도금양과 올리브 나무로 푸르게 단장한 가운데 봉우리 쪽에서, 그들이 그쪽을 바라보자, 맥박 없이 고요한 아침 공기 속으로 가느다란 연기 기둥들이 가볍고 곧게 피어오르고 있었으니, 저마다 분주히 움직이는 순례객들과, 무정하게 흘러가는 시간과, 서둘러야 할 까닭을 일러 주는 경고였다.\n\n착한 하인이 언덕 비탈을 내려가는 수고를 덜어 주려고 자신을 조금도 아끼지 않고 충실히 애썼으나, 소녀는 걸음마다 신음했고, 때로는 견딜 수 없는 고통에 부르짖었다. 길에, 곧 멸시의 산과 올리벳의 가운데 봉우리, 곧 둘째 봉우리 사이로 난 길에 다다르자, 그녀는 기진하여 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"어머니, 암라와 함께 가세요. 저는 여기 두고요.\" 그녀가 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 안 된다, 티르자. 내가 낫고 네가 낫지 못한다면, 그게 내게 무슨 소용이 있겠니? 유다가, 틀림없이 그럴 텐데, 너를 찾을 때, 내가 너를 두고 왔다면 그 아이에게 무슨 말을 하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 도련님을 사랑했다고 전해 주세요.\"\n\n연배 든 나병 환자는 까무러친 가엾은 딸 위로 굽혔던 몸을 일으켜, 무엇보다 영혼이 사멸되는 것에 가장 가까운 그 절망의 느낌으로 사방을 둘러보았다. 치유의 생각이 주는 더없는 기쁨은 티르자와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 것이었으니, 그 아이는 앞으로 누릴 건강한 삶의 행복 속에서 몸이 그토록 쇠하고 마음이 그토록 꺾인 그 비참했던 세월을 잊기에 너무 나이 든 것도 아니었다. 그 의연한 여인이 자신들이 나선 그 모험을 하느님의 뜻에 막 내맡기려던 바로 그때, 그녀는 한 남자가 동쪽에서 길을 따라 빠르게 걸어 올라오는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"용기를 내렴, 티르자! 기운을 내거라.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저기 오는 이가 나사렛 사람에 대해 우리에게 일러 줄 사람인 게 틀림없구나.\"\n\n암라가 소녀를 부축해 앉히고, 사내가 다가오는 동안 그녀를 받쳐 주었다.\n\n\"어머니, 어머니는 마음이 고우셔서 우리가 어떤 처지인지 잊으시는군요. 저 나그네는 우리를 빙 둘러 갈 거예요. 그가 우리에게 베풀 가장 큰 선물이라야 저주일 테고, 아니면 돌멩이겠지요.\"\n\n\"두고 보자꾸나.\"\n\n이 밖에 달리 해 줄 대답은 없었으니, 어머니는 자기가 속한 그 부류의 버림받은 자들이 동족의 손에서 으레 받아 온 대접을 너무도 잘, 그리고 슬프게도 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯, 이 무리가 자리 잡은 길가는, 석회암 둔덕들 사이로 구불구불 뻗은, 닳고 닳은 오솔길, 곧 자취에 지나지 않았다. 나그네가 그 길을 계속 간다면, 그들과 얼굴을 마주칠 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 그는 그렇게 다가왔으니, 그녀가 마땅히 외쳐야 할 소리를 들을 만큼 가까워질 때까지였다. 그러자 그녀는 율법이 또한 요구하는 대로 머리를 드러내고, 날카롭게 외쳤다.\n\n\"부정한 몸이다, 부정한 몸이야!\"\n\n그녀가 놀랍게도, 사내는 망설임 없이 다가왔다.\n\n\"무엇을 원하시오?\" 그가 그들 맞은편 네 걸음도 채 안 되는 곳에 멈춰 서서 물었다.\n\n\"우리를 보고 계시오. 조심하시오.\" 어머니가 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"여인이여, 나는 그대 같은 이에게 단 한마디만 하시면 그들이 낫게 되는 분의 전령이오. 나는 두렵지 않소.\"\n\n\"나사렛 사람 말이오?\"\n\n\"메시아요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 오늘 성으로 오신다는 게 참말이오?\"\n\n\"그분은 지금 벳바게에 계시오.\"\n\n\"어느 길로 오신단 말이오, 선생?\"\n\n\"이 길이오.\"\n\n그녀는 두 손을 맞잡고, 감사하며 위를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"그대는 그분을 누구로 여기시오?\" 사내가 가엾이 여기며 물었다.\n\n\"하느님의 아들이지요.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 여기 머물러 계시오. 아니면, 그분과 함께 무리가 있으니, 저기 저 바위 곁에, 나무 아래 저 흰 바위 곁에 서 계시오. 그리고 그분이 지나가시거든 잊지 말고 그분을 부르시오. 부르되, 두려워 마시오. 그대의 믿음이 그대의 앎과 같기만 하다면, 온 하늘이 천둥 친다 해도 그분은 그대의 소리를 들으실 거요. 나는 성 안팎에 모인 이스라엘에게 그분이 가까이 오셨음을 알리고, 그분을 맞을 채비를 시키러 가오. 그대와 그대의 식구들에게 평화가 있기를, 여인이여.\"\n\n나그네는 길을 떠났다.\n\n\"들었느냐, 티르자? 들었어? 나사렛 사람이 이 길로, 바로 이 길로 오고 계신다는구나. 그리고 그분이 우리 소리를 들으실 거래. 한 번만 더, 얘야, 오, 단 한 번만 더! 저 바위로 가자꾸나. 한 걸음이면 된다.\"\n\n이렇게 용기를 얻은 티르자는 암라의 손을 잡고 일어섰다. 그러나 그들이 막 가려 할 때, 암라가 말했다. \"잠깐만요. 그 사람이 되돌아오고 있어요.\" 그래서 그들은 그를 기다렸다.\n\n\"그대의 양해를 구하오, 여인이여.\" 그가 그들을 따라잡고 말했다. \"나사렛 사람이 오시기 전에 해가 뜨거워질 것이고, 내게 마실 것이 필요하면 성이 가까이 있어 마련해 주리라는 생각이 들어, 이 물이 내게보다 그대에게 더 요긴하겠다 싶었소. 받으시고 기운을 내시오. 그분이 지나가시거든 부르시오.\"\n\n그는 말끝에 호리병박에 가득 담은 물을 그녀에게 내밀었으니, 길손들이 더러 산을 넘는 여정에 지니고 다니는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 안전한 거리로 물러난 뒤 그녀가 집어 들도록 땅바닥에 놓아두는 대신, 그것을 그녀의 손에 직접 건네주었다.\n\n\"그대는 유대인이오?\" 그녀가 놀라며 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그 이상이오. 나는 그리스도의 제자요. 그분은 내가 그대에게 행한 이 일을 날마다 말씀과 본보기로 가르치시오. 세상은 오래도록 자비라는 말을 알면서도 그 뜻을 깨닫지 못했소. 다시 한번 말하니, 그대와 그대의 식구들에게 평화와 기쁨이 있기를.\"\n\n그는 계속 길을 갔고, 그들은 그가 가리켜 준 바위로 천천히 갔으니, 그것은 그들의 키만큼 높았고, 오른편 길에서 채 서른 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 있었다. 그 앞에 선 어머니는, 자기들이 눈길을 끌고자 하는 길손들에게 또렷이 보이고 들릴 수 있음을 확인하고 흡족해했다. 그곳, 나무 그늘 아래 그들은 몸을 누이고, 호리병박의 물을 마시며, 기운을 되찾아 쉬었다. 이윽고 티르자가 잠들었고, 다른 이들은 그녀를 깨울까 저어하여 입을 다물었다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n셋째 시간 동안 나병환자들이 쉬던 곳 앞의 길에는 베다니와 벳바게 쪽으로 가는 사람들이 차츰 늘어 갔다. 그러나 넷째 시간이 시작될 무렵, 올리벳 산마루 위로 거대한 무리가 나타났다. 수천 명이 길을 따라 줄지어 내려오는데, 두 사람은 무리 가운데 누구나 갓 잘라 낸 종려나무 가지를 손에 들고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 두 사람이 이 새로운 광경에 넋을 잃고 앉아 있을 때, 동쪽에서 다가오는 또 다른 무리의 소리가 그들의 눈길을 그쪽으로 끌었다. 그러자 어머니가 티르자를 깨웠다.\n\n\"이게 다 무슨 일이에요?\" 딸이 물었다.\n\n\"그분이 오신단다.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"우리가 보는 저들은 그분을 맞으러 성에서 나온 사람들이고, 동쪽에서 소리가 들리는 저들은 그분을 모시고 오는 동무들이란다. 두 행렬이 우리 앞에서 만나게 되더라도 이상할 게 없겠지.\"\n\n\"그리되면, 우리 소리가 들리지 않을까 봐 두려워요.\"\n\n같은 생각이 어머니의 마음속에도 들어 있었다.\n\n\"암라야.\" 어머니가 물었다. \"유다가 열 사람을 고친 이야기를 했을 때, 그들이 나사렛 사람을 어떤 말로 불렀다더냐?\"\n\n\"'주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서' 아니면 '선생님, 불쌍히 여기소서' 둘 중 하나였습니다.\"\n\n\"그것뿐이더냐?\"\n\n\"제가 들은 건 그게 다입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그것으로 충분했겠지.\" 어머니가 혼잣말처럼 덧붙였다.\n\n\"네.\" 암라가 말했다. \"유다는 그들이 멀쩡해져서 떠나는 걸 보았다고 했습니다.\"\n\n그러는 동안 동쪽의 사람들은 천천히 다가왔다. 마침내 맨 앞에 선 자들이 시야에 들어왔을 때, 나병환자들의 시선은 한 무리 가운데서 말을 탄 한 사람에게 못 박혔다. 무리는 마치 가려 뽑힌 듯 보였고, 그를 둘러싸고 넘치는 기쁨으로 노래하고 춤추었다. 말 탄 이는 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않았고, 온몸에 흰옷을 걸치고 있었다. 그가 더 또렷이 볼 만한 거리에 이르렀을 때, 이들은 애타게 바라보다가, 올리브빛 얼굴이 길고 밤색인 머리카락에 가려진 것을 보았다. 그 머리카락은 햇볕에 살짝 그을렸고 한가운데서 가르마를 타고 있었다. 그는 좌로도 우로도 돌아보지 않았다. 떠들썩하게 들떠 있는 추종자들의 모습에 그는 아무런 몫도 없는 듯했다. 그들의 환호도 그를 조금도 흔들지 못했고, 그 얼굴에 드러나듯 그가 빠져 있는 깊은 우수에서 그를 끌어올리지도 못했다. 해가 그의 뒤통수를 내리쬐어 흩날리는 머리카락을 빛나게 하니, 그것이 황금빛 후광과 어렴풋이 닮아 보였다. 그의 뒤로는 들쭉날쭉한 행렬이 끊임없이 노래하고 외치며 앞으로 쏟아져 나오는데, 끝이 보이지 않을 만큼 길게 뻗어 있었다. 나병환자들에게 이가 바로 그분이라고, 그 놀라운 나사렛 사람이라고 일러 줄 사람은 아무도 필요 없었다.\n\n\"그분이 여기 오셨다, 티르자야.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"그분이 오셨어. 가자, 얘야.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 어머니는 흰 바위 앞으로 미끄러지듯 나아가 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n곧바로 딸과 하녀도 그 곁에 섰다. 그때 서쪽에서 오는 행렬을 보고, 성에서 나온 수천 명이 걸음을 멈추고는 푸른 가지를 흔들며 외쳤다. 아니, 차라리 노래했다고 해야 옳았으니, 모두가 한목소리였기 때문이다.\n\n\"주의 이름으로 오시는 이스라엘의 왕이여 찬송하리로다!\"\n\n그러자 말 탄 이의 무리에 속한 수천 명이, 가까이 있는 자도 멀리 있는 자도 모두 화답하니, 그 소리에 공기가 진동했다. 그것은 마치 산허리를 후려치는 큰 바람과도 같았다. 그 소란 속에서 가련한 나병환자들의 외침은 정신이 멍해진 참새들의 지저귐만도 못했다.\n\n두 무리가 만나는 순간이 다가왔으니, 그와 더불어 고통받는 자들이 찾던 기회도 다가왔다. 잡지 못하면 영영 사라질 기회였고, 그들 또한 함께 사라질 터였다.\n\n\"더 가까이, 얘야. 더 가까이 가자. 그분이 우리 소리를 듣지 못하시는구나.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n어머니는 일어나 비틀비틀 앞으로 나아갔다. 그 소름 끼치는 두 손을 치켜들고, 어머니는 끔찍하리만큼 날카롭게 비명을 질렀다. 사람들이 그녀를 보았다. 그 흉측한 얼굴을 보고는 두려움에 사로잡혀 걸음을 멈추었다. 이 경우처럼 눈에 보이는 극한의 인간적 비참은, 자줏빛과 황금으로 치장한 위엄에 못지않은 힘을 지니는 법이다. 티르자는 어머니 뒤로 조금 떨어진 자리에서 너무 기진하고 겁에 질려 더는 따라가지 못한 채 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"나병환자다! 나병환자야!\"\n\n\"돌로 쳐라!\"\n\n\"하느님의 저주받은 것들이다! 죽여라!\"\n\n이런 외침과 비슷한 뜻의 다른 고함들이, 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어 소동의 원인을 보지도 알지도 못하는 무리 일부의 호산나 사이로 끼어들었다. 그러나 가까이 있는 자들 가운데 더러는, 이 불행한 이들이 호소하는 그 사람의 본성을 잘 아는 자들이 있었다. 그와 오래 사귀면서 그의 신성한 연민을 얼마간 받아 지닌 자들이었다. 그들은 그를 바라보며 잠잠했고, 그동안 그는 환히 보이는 곳에서 말을 몰아 그 여인 앞에 와 멈추었다. 여인도 그의 얼굴을 보았다. 고요하고, 자비로우며, 더없이 아름다운 얼굴이었고, 그 큰 눈은 너그러운 뜻으로 부드러웠다.\n\n이어서 다음과 같은 대화가 오갔다.\n\n\"오 선생님, 선생님! 저희의 곤궁을 보고 계시지요. 선생님은 저희를 깨끗하게 하실 수 있습니다. 저희를 불쌍히 여기소서, 불쌍히 여기소서!\"\n\n\"내가 이 일을 할 수 있다고 믿느냐?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"선생님은 예언자들이 말한 바로 그분이십니다. 선생님은 메시아이십니다!\" 여인이 대답했다.\n\n그의 눈이 빛나고, 그 태도가 확신에 찼다.\n\n\"여인아.\" 그가 말했다. \"네 믿음이 크도다. 네 뜻대로 이루어질지어다.\"\n\n그는 그 뒤로도 한순간 머뭇거렸는데, 군중이 거기 있다는 것을 알아차리지 못하는 듯했다. 한순간이었다. 그러고는 말을 몰아 떠났다.\n\n신성하게 독창적이면서도 인간성의 더 나은 요소들에서는 더없이 인간다운 그 마음, 사람이 고안한 가장 더럽고 잔혹한 죽음을 향해 확실한 예지로 나아가는 그 마음, 그 무서운 사건의 미리 드리운 그림자 속에서 이미 숨 쉬면서도 처음과 다름없이 사랑과 믿음에 굶주리고 목마른 그 마음에게, 감사하는 여인의 작별 외침은 얼마나 귀하고 이루 말할 수 없이 위안이 되었을까.\n\n\"지극히 높은 곳에 계신 하느님께 영광을! 그분이 우리에게 주신 아드님께 복이, 세 곱절의 복이 있을지어다!\"\n\n곧바로 두 무리가, 성에서 온 무리와 벳바게에서 온 무리가 기쁨에 찬 환호와 호산나와 종려나무 흔들기로 그를 에워쌌고, 그렇게 그는 나병환자들에게서 영영 멀어졌다. 어머니는 머리를 가리고 서둘러 티르자에게 달려가, 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 외쳤다. \"딸아, 고개를 들어라! 나는 그분의 약속을 받았다. 그분은 참으로 메시아시다. 우리는 구원받았다. 구원받았어!\" 두 사람은 무릎을 꿇은 채 그대로 있었고, 그동안 행렬은 천천히 가다가 산 너머로 사라졌다. 그 노랫소리가 멀리서 가까스로 들릴 만큼 잦아들었을 때 기적이 시작되었다.\n\n먼저 나병환자들의 가슴속에서 피가 새로워졌다. 이어 피가 더 빠르고 힘차게 흐르며, 쇠약해진 몸을 통증 없는 치유의 한없이 달콤한 감각으로 떨리게 했다. 저마다 채찍 같은 병이 제 몸에서 빠져나가는 것을 느꼈다. 기운이 되살아났다. 그들은 본래의 자신으로 돌아가고 있었다. 곧, 정화를 완전하게 하려는 듯, 그 생기가 몸에서 정신으로 번져 그들을 황홀경의 열기로까지 끌어올렸다. 이 선한 목적을 위해 그들을 사로잡은 힘은 빠르고 행복한 효력을 지닌 한 모금의 음료에 가장 가까웠다. 그러나 그것과는 다르고 또 그보다 뛰어났으니, 그 치유와 정화가 절대적이었기 때문이고, 진행되는 동안의 황홀한 의식에 그치지 않고, 단지 그것을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그 뒤로 언제까지나 형태 없으되 온전한 감사가 되리만큼 유별나고 거룩한 기억을 한꺼번에 심고 키우고 무르익게 했기 때문이다.\n\n이 변화에는, 그것은 치유라고 부르는 것만큼이나 마땅하게 변화라고 부를 수 있는데, 암라 말고도 또 다른 증인이 있었다. 독자는 벤허가 나사렛 사람의 유랑길을 줄곧 한결같이 따라다닌 것을 기억할 것이다. 이제 전날 밤의 대화를 떠올린다면, 나병에 걸린 여인이 순례자들의 길에 나타났을 때 그 젊은 유대인이 그 자리에 있었다는 사실을 알게 되어도 그리 놀라지 않을 것이다. 그는 여인의 기도를 들었고, 그 일그러진 얼굴을 보았다. 그 대답도 들었는데, 그런 일이 잦았다고는 해도, 그런 종류의 사건에 익숙해져 흥미를 잃을 만큼은 아니었다. 설령 그가 그럴 수 있었다 해도, 선생의 치유 능력을 가장 단순하게 드러낼 때마다 늘 일어나는 신랄한 논쟁만으로도 그의 호기심을 살려 두기에 충분했을 것이다. 게다가, 그 동기로서 그에 못지않게, 아니 어쩌면 그 이상으로, 이 신비로운 사람의 사명이라는 골치 아픈 문제를 스스로 풀어 보려는 희망이 처음과 다름없이 강하게 그를 붙들고 있었다. 사실 더 강해졌다고도 할 수 있었으니, 이제 곧, 해가 지기 전에, 그 사람이 공개 선포로써 모든 것을 알리리라는 믿음 때문이었다. 그리하여 그 장면이 끝나자 벤허는 행렬에서 빠져나와 돌 위에 앉아 행렬이 지나가기를 기다렸다.\n\n그는 제자리에서 많은 사람들에게 알아본다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 긴 아바 자락 아래 짧은 칼을 감춘, 그와 한패인 갈릴리 사람들이었다. 잠시 후 거무스름한 아랍인 하나가 말 두 필을 끌고 다가왔다. 벤허의 신호에 그도 길에서 물러났다.\n\n\"여기서 기다려라.\" 모두가, 심지어 뒤처진 자들까지 다 지나간 뒤에 젊은 주인이 말했다. \"나는 일찍 성에 가야 하니, 알데바란이 내게 수고를 해 주어야겠다.\"\n\n그는 이제 한창 힘과 아름다움이 무르익은 말의 넓은 이마를 쓰다듬고는, 두 여인을 향해 길을 건넜다.\n\n명심해 둘 것은, 그에게 이들은 낯선 사람들이었고, 오직 초인적인 실험의 대상으로서만 관심이 가는 존재였다는 점이다. 그 실험의 결과가 어쩌면 그를 오래도록 사로잡아 온 신비의 해답으로 이끌어 줄지도 모르는 까닭이었다. 걸어가면서 그는 흰 바위 곁에 서 있는 작은 여인의 모습을 무심코 흘긋 보았다. 여인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 그곳에 서 있었다.\n\n\"주께서 살아 계심을 두고 맹세컨대, 저건 암라가 아닌가!\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n그는 걸음을 재촉했고, 어머니와 딸을 지나치면서도 여전히 그들을 알아보지 못한 채, 하녀 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"암라.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"암라, 여기서 무얼 하는 게냐?\"\n\n암라는 앞으로 달려 나와 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다. 눈물에 앞이 가려지고, 뒤엉킨 기쁨과 두려움에 거의 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"오 도련님, 도련님! 도련님의 하느님이자 저의 하느님이, 얼마나 선하신지요!\"\n\n남들이 시련을 겪는 것을 깊이 공감하면서 얻는 깨달음은 어렴풋이밖에 이해되지 못한다. 묘하게도 그 깨달음은, 다른 무엇보다도, 우리의 자아를 그들의 자아에 녹여 넣어 그들의 슬픔과 기쁨이 곧 우리 것이 되게 할 만큼 자주 우리를 그들과 온전히 하나로 만든다. 그래서 가련한 암라는, 멀찍이 떨어져 얼굴을 가리고 있으면서도, 한마디 말을 듣지 않고도 나병환자들이 겪는 변화를 알았다. 알았을 뿐 아니라 그들의 모든 감정을 남김없이 함께 나누었다. 그녀의 표정, 그녀의 말, 그녀의 온 태도가 그 상태를 드러냈다. 그러자 벤허는 재빠른 예감으로 그것을 방금 지나쳐 온 여인들과 연결 지었다. 그녀가 그때 거기 있는 것이 어떤 식으로든 그들과 관련되어 있음을 느낀 그는, 그들이 몸을 일으키자 황급히 돌아보았다. 그의 심장이 멎었고, 그는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 굳어졌다. 소리조차 지르지 못한 채, 두려움에 사로잡혔다.\n\n그가 나사렛 사람 앞에서 보았던 그 여인이 두 손을 맞잡고 눈물을 흘리며 하늘을 향해 바라보며 서 있었다. 그 변화만으로도 충분히 놀랄 일이었을 것이다. 그러나 그것은 그의 감정을 일으킨 원인 중 가장 작은 것이었다. 그가 잘못 본 것일까? 살아오면서 이토록 그의 어머니를 닮은 낯선 사람은 없었다. 게다가 로마인이 어머니를 그에게서 앗아 가던 그날의 모습 그대로 닮아 있었다. 그 동일함을 흐리는 차이는 단 하나뿐이었으니, 이 사람의 머리카락이 군데군데 희끗하다는 것이었다. 그러나 그것도 들어맞지 않는 것은 아니었으니, 기적을 주관한 그 지혜가 세월의 흐름이 가져오는 자연스러운 결과를 헤아렸을 수도 있는 까닭이었다. 그리고 그 곁에 있는 이는, 티르자가 아니라면 누구란 말인가? 곱고 아름답고 흠 없으며, 더 성숙해졌으되, 그 밖의 모든 점에서는 그라투스에게 사고가 일어나던 그날 아침 그와 함께 난간 너머를 내려다보던 때와 똑같은 모습이었다. 그는 그들을 죽은 사람으로 단념했고, 세월은 그를 그 상실에 익숙해지게 했다. 그들을 위한 애도를 그친 것은 아니었으나, 또렷이 구분되는 무엇으로서의 그들은 그저 그의 계획과 꿈에서 떨어져 나가 있었다. 자신의 감각을 좀처럼 믿지 못한 채, 그는 하녀의 머리에 손을 얹고 떨리는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"암라, 암라야. 내 어머니! 티르자! 내가 제대로 보고 있는 건지 말해 다오.\"\n\n\"저분들께 말씀하세요, 오 도련님, 저분들께 말씀하세요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그는 더 기다리지 않고, 두 팔을 벌린 채 달려가며 외쳤다. \"어머니! 어머니! 티르자! 저예요!\"\n\n그들은 그의 부름을 듣고, 못지않게 다정한 외침과 함께 그를 맞으러 나섰다. 그런데 갑자기 어머니가 걸음을 멈추고 뒤로 물러서며, 옛 경고를 외쳤다.\n\n\"멈춰라, 유다, 내 아들아. 더 가까이 오지 마라. 부정하다, 부정해!\"\n\n그 외침은 그 무서운 병이 어머니를 덮친 뒤로 몸에 밴 습관에서 나온 것이라기보다는 두려움에서 나온 것이었다. 그리고 그 두려움은 언제나 사려 깊은 모성애의 또 다른 형태일 뿐이었다. 비록 몸은 나았어도, 그 채찍 같은 병의 독기가 옷에 남아 옮길 채비를 하고 있을지도 몰랐다. 그에게는 그런 생각이 없었다. 그들이 그의 앞에 있었다. 그가 그들을 불렀고, 그들이 대답했다. 이제 누가, 무엇이 그들을 그에게서 떼어 놓을 수 있단 말인가? 다음 순간, 그토록 오래 떨어져 있던 세 사람은 서로의 품에 안겨 눈물을 뒤섞고 있었다.\n\n첫 황홀경이 지나가자 어머니가 말했다. \"이 행복 속에서, 오 내 자식들아, 우리 감사할 줄 모르는 자가 되지는 말자. 우리 모두가 그토록 큰 빚을 진 그분을 받들며 새 삶을 시작하자.\"\n\n그들은 무릎을 꿇었고, 암라도 함께 꿇었다. 소리 높여 올리는 어머니의 기도는 한 편의 시편과도 같았다.\n\n티르자가 한마디 한마디 그대로 따라 했다. 벤허도 그리했으나, 같은 맑은 마음과 의심 없는 믿음으로 한 것은 아니었다. 다들 일어났을 때 그가 물은 까닭이다.\n\n\"그 사람이 태어난 나사렛에서는, 어머니, 사람들이 그를 목수의 아들이라고 부릅니다. 그는 누구입니까?\"\n\n어머니의 눈길이 옛날 그대로의 다정함을 가득 담아 그에게 머물렀고, 어머니는 나사렛 사람에게 대답했던 그대로 대답했다.\n\n\"그분은 메시아시다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 힘은 어디서 나온 것입니까?\"\n\n\"그분이 그 힘을 어떻게 쓰는가를 보면 알 수 있다. 그분이 행한 악을 하나라도 말할 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\"없습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 표징으로 답하마. 그분은 그 힘을 하느님에게서 받으셨다.\"\n\n여러 해 동안 길러 온 기대가 마침내 우리의 본질적인 일부가 되어 버렸을 때, 그것을 한순간에 떨쳐 버리기란 쉬운 일이 아니다. 벤허는 그런 분에게 세상의 헛된 것들이 다 무엇이겠느냐고 스스로 물어보았지만, 그의 야망은 완고하여 수그러들려 하지 않았다. 그는 오늘날에도 사람들이 날마다 그러하듯, 그리스도를 자신의 잣대로 재기를 고집했다. 우리가 우리 자신을 그리스도의 잣대로 잰다면 얼마나 더 나을 것인가!\n\n자연히 살림 걱정을 먼저 떠올린 것은 어머니였다.\n\n\"이제 우리 어떻게 하면 좋겠느냐, 아들아? 어디로 가야 하지?\"\n\n그러자 의무를 다시 떠올린 벤허는, 회복된 식구들에게서 그 채찍 같은 병의 흔적이 얼마나 깨끗이 사라졌는지를 살펴보았다. 저마다 몸의 온전함을 되찾았고, 나아만이 물에서 올라왔을 때처럼 그들의 살갗이 어린아이의 살갗처럼 다시 돌아와 있었다. 그는 자기 겉옷을 벗어 티르자에게 둘러 주었다.\n\n\"받으세요.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"전에는 낯선 사람의 눈이 누나를 피했지만, 이제는 누나를 욕보이지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n이 동작에 옆구리에 찬 칼이 드러났다.\n\n\"전쟁 때라도 되었느냐?\" 어머니가 걱정스레 물었다.\n\"아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 어찌하여 무기를 지녔느냐?\"\n\n\"나사렛 사람을 지켜야 할지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n이렇게 벤허는 온전한 진실을 비껴갔다.\n\n\"그분에게 적이 있느냐? 그들이 누구냐?\"\n\n\"아아, 어머니, 그들이 다 로마인은 아닙니다!\"\n\n\"그분은 이스라엘 사람이며 평화의 사람이 아니냐?\"\n\n\"그보다 더한 사람은 없었지요. 그러나 랍비들과 율법학자들의 생각으로는, 그분이 큰 죄를 지은 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 죄냐?\"\n\n\"그분의 눈에는 할례받지 않은 이방인도 가장 엄격한 율법을 지키는 유대인 못지않게 은혜를 받을 만합니다. 그분은 새로운 가르침을 설파하십니다.\"\n\n어머니는 잠잠했고, 그들은 바위 곁 나무 그늘로 자리를 옮겼다. 그들을 어서 집으로 데려가 그동안의 이야기를 듣고 싶은 조바심을 가라앉히고, 그는 그들과 같은 처지에 적용되는 율법에 따라야 할 필요성을 일러 주었다. 끝으로 아랍인을 불러, 말들을 베데스다 곁 성문으로 끌고 가 거기서 자기를 기다리라고 일렀다. 그러고서 그들은 멸시의 산 쪽 길로 출발했다. 돌아가는 길은 올 때와는 사뭇 달랐다. 그들은 빠르고 가뿐하게 걸었고, 알맞은 때에 압살롬의 무덤 가까이 새로 만든 무덤에 이르렀는데, 그곳은 기드론의 깊은 골짜기를 내려다보고 있었다. 비어 있는 것을 알고 여인들이 그곳을 차지했고, 그동안 그는 그들의 새로운 처지에 필요한 채비를 갖추려고 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n벤허는 키드론강 상류, 왕들의 무덤에서 동쪽으로 조금 떨어진 곳에 천막 두 개를 치고, 그가 마련할 수 있는 온갖 편의를 갖추어 놓았다. 그리고 한시도 지체하지 않고 어머니와 누이를 그리로 데려가, 검사를 맡은 제사장이 두 사람이 완전히 정결해졌음을 증명해 줄 때까지 머물게 했다.\n\n이 의무를 치르는 동안 젊은이는 큰 명절의 의식에 참여하지 못할 만큼 심각한 부정(不淨)을 스스로 뒤집어쓰고 말았으니, 명절은 이제 코앞에 다가와 있었다. 그는 성전의 가장 덜 거룩한 뜰에조차 들어갈 수 없었다. 그러므로 그는 선택이라기보다 어쩔 수 없이, 사랑하는 가족과 함께 천막에 머물렀다. 그들에게서 들을 이야기도 많았고, 자신에 대해 들려줄 이야기도 많았다.\n\n그들이 겪은 것과 같은 사연—여러 해에 걸친 슬픈 경험, 육신의 고통, 그보다 더 날카로운 마음의 고통—은 이야기하는 데 으레 오랜 시간이 걸리고, 사건들이 실에 꿰이듯 차례로 이어지는 일도 드물다. 그는 그 이야기와 그들이 들려주는 모든 말에 귀를 기울이며, 겉으로는 인내심을 가장하여 속의 감정을 가렸다. 사실 로마와 로마인을 향한 그의 증오는 그 어느 때보다 높은 곳에 다다라 있었다. 복수를 향한 그의 열망은 갈증이 되어, 곰곰이 생각해 보려는 시도가 도리어 그것을 더욱 거세게 만들었다. 거의 야수와 같은 쓰라린 마음속에서 수많은 광기 어린 충동이 그를 사로잡았다. 큰길이 주는 기회들이 유달리 강한 유혹의 힘으로 그 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 갈릴리에서의 봉기를 진지하게 생각했다. 평소에는 떠올리기만 해도 끔찍하던 바다조차 그의 상상 앞에 지도처럼 펼쳐졌으니, 황제의 약탈품과 황제의 길손들로 붐비는 항로가 거기에 얼기설기 그려져 있었다. 그러나 더 차분한 시간에 무르익은 그의 더 나은 판단은 다행히도 너무나 단단히 자리 잡고 있어서, 아무리 강렬한 지금의 격정으로도 그것을 밀어낼 수 없었다. 새로운 방책을 향한 그의 모든 정신적 모험은 그를 옛 결론으로 되돌려 놓았다—온 이스라엘이 굳게 하나로 뭉친 전쟁이 아니고서는 온전한 성공이 있을 수 없다는 것이었다. 그리고 이 문제를 둘러싼 모든 사색, 모든 탐구, 모든 희망은 시작한 자리에서 끝났으니—나사렛 사람과 그의 목적에서였다.\n\n흥분한 이 책략가는 이따금 그 사람을 위한 연설을 지어 보는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다.\n\n\"들으라, 오 이스라엘이여! 내가 바로 그다. 하느님께서 약속하신 자, 나면서부터 유대인의 왕인 자—예언자들이 말한 그 권세를 가지고 너희에게 왔노라. 이제 일어나, 세상을 거머쥐어라!\"\n\n나사렛 사람이 이 몇 마디만 말한다면, 얼마나 큰 소용돌이가 뒤따를 것인가! 나팔 노릇을 하는 입이 얼마나 많이 그 말을 받아 사방으로 불어, 군대를 불러 모을 것인가!\n\n그가 과연 그 말을 할 것인가?\n\n그리고 그 일을 어서 시작하고 싶은 마음에, 세속의 방식으로 헤아리느라 벤허는 그 사람의 이중적 본성과, 그 안의 신성(神性)이 인간성을 뛰어넘을 수 있다는 또 다른 가능성을 놓치고 말았다. 자신보다 오히려 더 가까이서 티르자와 어머니가 목격한 그 기적 속에서, 그는 한 가지 권능을 보고 따로 떼어 거기에 마음을 쏟았다. 이탈리아 왕관의 잔해 위에 유대인의 왕관을 세우고 떠받치기에 충분하고도 남는, 사회를 다시 빚고 인류를 정결한 하나의 행복한 가족으로 바꾸어 놓기에도 차고 넘치는 권능이었다. 그리고 그 일이 이루어지고 나면, 그때 거침없이 베풀어질 평화가 하느님의 아들에게 합당한 사명이 아니라고 말할 자가 있겠는가? 그때 그리스도의 구속자 되심을 부인할 자가 있겠는가? 정치적 결과에 대한 온갖 고려를 다 제쳐 두더라도, 한 인간으로서 그에게 돌아올 개인적 영광은 또 얼마나 형언할 수 없을 것인가? 그런 앞길을 마다하는 것은 한낱 인간의 본성에 있을 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n그러는 동안 키드론을 따라 내려가며, 또 베세다 쪽으로 들어가는 길, 특히 다마스쿠스 문에 이르기까지 길가에는 유월절 순례자들을 위한 온갖 임시 거처가 빠르게 들어찼다. 벤허는 그 나그네들을 찾아가 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 천막으로 돌아올 때마다 그들의 어마어마한 수에 갈수록 더 놀랐다. 그뿐 아니라 그들 가운데 세계의 모든 곳이 다 대표되어 있음을—지중해 양쪽 기슭의 도시들로, 멀리 서쪽 끝의 헤라클레스 기둥에 이르는 곳까지, 머나먼 인도의 강변 마을들, 유럽 최북단의 속주들까지—그리고 그들이 더러는 조상들의 옛 히브리어 한 음절도 모르는 혀로 그에게 인사를 건네면서도, 이 대표자들이 모두 같은 목적, 곧 그 이름난 명절을 기념하려는 한 가지 목적을 가지고 있음을 알게 되었을 때, 미신적인 환상으로 어렴풋이 물든 한 가지 생각이 그에게 밀려들었다. 어쩌면 자신이 결국 나사렛 사람을 잘못 이해한 것이 아닐까? 그 사람이 참고 기다림으로써 조용한 준비를 감추고 있는 것은 아닐까? 자기 앞에 놓인 영광스러운 과업에 자신이 합당함을 증명하고 있는 것은 아닐까? 갈릴리 사람들이 게네사렛 호숫가에서 그에게 왕관을 억지로 씌우려 했던 그때보다, 지금이 그 운동에 얼마나 더 좋은 때인가? 그때라면 지지하는 무리가 고작 몇천에 그쳤겠지만, 지금은 그의 선포에 수백만이 호응할 터이니—누가 그 수를 헤아릴 수 있겠는가? 이 가설을 그 결론까지 좇아가며, 벤허는 눈부신 약속들 사이를 거닐었다. 그 우울한 사람이 온화한 겉모습과 놀라운 자기 절제 아래, 실은 정치가의 빈틈없음과 군인의 천재성을 변장하여 품고 다니는 것이라는 생각에 가슴이 뜨거워졌다.\n\n또한 그러는 동안 몇 차례, 키가 작고 우람한 사내들이—머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않고 검은 수염을 기른 자들이—천막으로 찾아와 벤허를 찾았다. 그들과의 면담은 늘 따로 이루어졌다. 그들이 누구냐는 어머니의 물음에 그는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"갈릴리에서 온 제 좋은 친구들입니다.\"\n\n그들을 통해 그는 나사렛 사람의 동향과, 율법학자와 로마인을 비롯한 나사렛 사람의 적들이 꾸미는 음모를 계속 전해 들었다. 그 선한 사람의 목숨이 위태롭다는 것은 그도 알고 있었다. 그러나 이 시점에 감히 그 목숨을 노릴 만큼 대담한 자가 있으리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 그 목숨은 드높은 명성과 확고한 인기 속에 너무도 단단히 보호받고 있는 듯했다. 도성 안팎에 몰려든 군중의 그 방대함 자체가 안전을 보증해 주는 것 같았다. 그렇지만 진실을 말하자면, 벤허의 확신이 가장 확실하게 기대고 있는 것은 그리스도의 기적적인 권능이었다. 순전히 인간적인 관점에서 이 문제를 곰곰이 헤아려 볼 때, 생사를 다스리는 그런 권세를 지닌 분이—그 권세를 그토록 자주 남을 위해 써 온 분이—정작 자신을 지키는 데는 쓰지 않으리라는 것은 이해할 수 없는 만큼이나 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n또한 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 이 모든 일이 현대의 달력으로 셈하여 삼월 이십일일에서 이십오일 사이에 일어난 사건이라는 사실이다. 이십오일 저녁, 벤허는 조바심을 이기지 못하고 말을 타고 도성으로 향했으니, 밤중에 돌아오겠다는 약속을 뒤에 남긴 채였다.\n\n말은 기운이 넘쳤고, 제 걸음을 골라 잡아 쏜살같이 달렸다. 길가 정원 울타리에서는 기어오른 덩굴의 눈들이 말 탄 이를 향해 눈짓했다. 그를 지켜보는 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 없었으니, 아이도 여자도 남자도 없었다. 길 우묵한 곳에 깔린 돌 위로 마노 같은 발굽이 둥둥 울리며, 강철 잔이 부딪치듯 쩌렁거렸다. 그러나 어떤 나그네도 그것을 눈여겨보지 않았다. 지나치는 집집마다 사람이 없었고, 천막 문 앞의 불은 꺼져 있었다. 길은 텅 비어 있었다. 이날이 첫 유월절 전야였기 때문이다. \"저녁과 저녁 사이\"라 불리는 그 시각, 찾아온 수백만이 도성을 가득 메우고, 제물로 바칠 어린양을 잡는 피비린내가 성전 앞뜰에 진동하며, 제사장들이 줄지어 서서 흘러나오는 피를 받아 뚝뚝 떨어지는 제단으로 재빨리 날라 가던 때였다. 모든 것이 서두름과 부산함이었으니, 신호와 함께 빠르게 떠오르는 별들과 다투어 달음질치고 있었다. 그 신호가 떨어진 뒤라야 굽고 먹고 노래하는 일은 이어질 수 있어도, 준비는 더 이상 할 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n말 탄 이는 큰 북쪽 문을 지나 안으로 들어섰다. 그러자 보라! 멸망을 앞둔 예루살렘이, 무르익은 영광 속에서, 주님을 위해 환히 밝혀진 채 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n벤허는 카라반사라이의 문 앞에서 말에서 내렸다. 삼십여 년 전 세 명의 동방박사가 베들레헴으로 내려가려고 떠난 바로 그 문이었다. 그는 아랍인 수행원들에게 말을 맡기고, 곧 아버지 집의 쪽문에 이르렀으며, 그보다 더 짧은 사이에 큰 방에 들어섰다. 그는 먼저 말루크를 찾았으나, 그 충직한 사람은 자리에 없었다. 그래서 그는 벗인 상인과 이집트인에게 안부 인사를 전하게 했다. 두 사람은 축제 구경을 위해 밖으로 실려 나간 참이었다. 후자는, 그가 전해 들은 바로는, 몹시 쇠약하고 깊은 실의에 빠져 있다고 했다.\n\n당시의 젊은이들은 제 마음조차 잘 모를 것이라 여겨졌지만, 실은 오늘날 같은 처지의 젊은이들이 그러하듯 빙 둘러 에두르는 술책을 곧잘 부렸다. 그래서 벤허가 선량한 발타사르의 안부를 묻고, 정중한 예를 갖추어 자기를 만나 줄 뜻이 있는지 알고자 했을 때, 그는 사실 그 딸에게 자기가 왔다는 기별을 보낸 셈이었다. 하인이 어른을 대신해 대답하는 동안, 문간의 휘장이 옆으로 젖혀지고 젊은 이집트 여자가 들어와—아니, 그녀가 그토록 좋아하며 그 속에서 살아가는 얇은 비단옷의 흰 구름에 떠받쳐 떠가듯이—방 한가운데로 걸어갔다. 그곳은 바닥에 세워진 일곱 가지 놋쇠 촛대의 등잔에서 쏟아지는 빛이 가장 강한 자리였다. 그녀에게 빛은 두려움의 대상이 아니었다.\n\n하인은 두 사람만 남겨 두고 물러갔다.\n\n지난 며칠간의 사건들이 불러일으킨 흥분 속에서 벤허는 그 어여쁜 이집트 여자를 거의 떠올리지 못했다. 설령 마음에 떠오른다 해도, 그것은 한순간의 가벼운 즐거움, 자기를 기다려 줄 수 있으며 또 기다리고 있는 어떤 기쁨의 암시일 뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 이제 벤허가 그녀를 본 순간, 그 여자의 영향력은 온 힘을 되찾아 되살아났다. 그는 반가운 마음에 다가서다가, 멈춰 서서 바라보았다. 그토록 큰 변화는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다!\n\n그때까지 그녀는 그의 마음을 얻으려고 애쓰는 연인이었으니, 몸짓 하나하나가 온통 따스함이요, 눈길 하나하나가 마음을 내보이는 고백이며, 행동 하나하나가 사랑의 맹세였다. 그녀는 아첨의 향기를 그에게 뿌려 댔다. 그가 곁에 있을 때면 그녀는 자신의 흠모로 그를 사로잡았다. 그가 떠나갈 때면 그 인상을 가슴에 품고 가게 하여, 그것이 그의 귀환을 재촉하는 달콤한 기대로 남게 했다. 윤기 흐르는 살구씨 같은 눈 위로 화장한 눈꺼풀을 가장 낮게 드리운 것도 그를 위해서였고, 알렉산드리아의 거리에 넘쳐나는 이야기꾼들에게서 주워들은 사랑 이야기를 힘주어, 또 시정(詩情)을 아낌없이 쏟아부으며 거듭 들려준 것도 그를 위해서였다. 그를 위해 끝없는 공감의 탄성과 미소, 손과 머리카락과 뺨과 입술로 베푸는 자잘한 호의, 나일강의 노래, 보석의 자랑, 베일과 스카프에 담긴 레이스의 정교함, 그에 못지않게 빼어난 인도 명주실 보풀의 또 다른 정교함이 있었다. 가장 오래된 민족만큼이나 오래된 생각, 곧 아름다움은 영웅에게 주어지는 보상이라는 그 생각이, 그녀가 그의 즐거움을 위해 꾸며 낸 만큼 생생한 현실로 다가온 적은 없었다. 그리하여 그는 자기가 그녀의 영웅임을 의심할 수 없었으니, 그녀는 제 아름다움처럼 자연스러운 천 가지 교묘한 방식으로 그것을 단언했다—옛 이집트의 정열적인 천재가 제 딸들을 위해 따로 간직해 둔 듯한, 사람을 호리는 그 방식들로.\n\n야자 동산의 호숫가에서 배를 타던 그날 밤 이후로, 그 이집트 여자는 벤허에게 그런 존재였다. 그러나 지금은!\n\n이 책의 다른 대목에서 독자는 거룩한 맥락에서 경건하게 쓰인, 다소 막연한 뜻의 어떤 말을 보았을 것이다. 우리는 이제 그것을 일반적인 의미로 다시 쓰려 한다. 이중의 본성, 곧 타고난 본성과 후천적인 본성을 지니지 않은 사람은 거의 없다. 후자는 교육에서 비롯된 일종의 덧붙임이지만, 세월이 흐르면 흔히 그것이 다듬어져 전자만큼이나 의심할 수 없는 존재의 일부가 된다. 이 생각은 사색을 즐기는 이들에게 맡겨 두고, 우리는 다만 이제 그 이집트 여자의 타고난 본성이 모습을 드러냈음을 말해 두려 한다.\n\n그녀가 낯선 이를 더 날카로운 혐오로 맞이하기란 불가능했을 것이다. 그러면서도 그녀는 겉보기에 조각상처럼 무정해 보였으니, 다만 작은 머리를 조금 갸웃하고, 콧구멍을 살짝 오므렸으며, 관능적인 아랫입술이 윗입술을 본래의 곡선에서 아주 조금 밀어낼 뿐이었다.\n\n먼저 입을 연 것은 그녀였다.\n\n\"오, 후르의 아들이여, 마침맞게 오셨군요.\" 그녀는 또렷하고 날카로운 목소리로 말했다. \"베풀어 주신 환대에 감사하고 싶어요. 내일이 지나면 그럴 기회가 없을지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n벤허는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 가볍게 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"주사위 노름꾼들이 저희끼리 좋은 결과를 보려고 지키는 풍습이 있다고 들었어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"노름이 끝나면 그들은 서판을 들여다보며 셈을 맞추지요. 그러고는 신들에게 술을 따라 바치고, 운 좋은 승자에게 화관을 씌워 줘요. 우리도 한 판 노름을 했어요—여러 날 밤낮에 걸쳐 이어진 노름이었지요. 자, 이제 그것이 끝났으니, 화관이 누구의 것인지 가려 보지 않으시겠어요?\"\n\n여전히 빈틈없이 경계하면서도 벤허는 가볍게 대답했다. \"제 뜻을 굽히지 않으려는 여자를 막을 수 있는 남자는 없지요.\"\n\n\"말해 보세요.\" 그녀는 고개를 기울이며, 비웃음이 또렷이 드러나도록 내버려 두고 말을 이었다—\"말해 보세요, 오, 예루살렘의 왕자여, 그가 어디 있나요? 그 나사렛 목수의 아들, 또한 못지않게 하느님의 아들이라던, 얼마 전까지 그토록 엄청난 일을 기대받던 그 사람 말이에요.\"\n\n그는 짜증스럽게 손을 내저으며 대꾸했다. \"나는 그의 파수꾼이 아니오.\"\n\n아름다운 머리가 더욱 앞으로 수그러들었다.\n\n\"그가 로마를 산산조각 냈던가요?\"\n\n다시, 그러나 이번에는 노여움을 담아, 벤허는 그만두라는 듯 손을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"그는 어디에 도읍을 정했나요?\" 그녀가 계속했다. \"가서 그의 옥좌와 청동 사자들을 구경할 수는 없을까요? 그리고 그의 궁전은요—그는 죽은 자를 살려 냈다지요. 그런 사람에게 황금 집 하나 짓는 게 무슨 대수겠어요? 발을 한 번 구르며 한마디 하기만 하면, 카르나크처럼 기둥이 늘어선 집이 부족함 없이 들어서겠지요.\"\n\n이쯤 되니 그녀가 농담을 하고 있다고 믿을 만한 근거는 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 그 물음들은 모욕적이었고, 태도에는 적의가 날카롭게 서려 있었다. 그것을 알아챈 그는 그 나름대로 더욱 경계하며, 좋은 낯빛으로 말했다. \"오, 이집트여, 그와 사자들과 궁전을 위해서라면, 하루만 더, 아니 한 주일만 더 기다려 봅시다.\"\n\n그녀는 그 제안을 못 들은 척 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그런데 어찌하여 당신은 그런 차림새인가요? 그건 인도의 총독이나 다른 곳의 부왕(副王)이 하는 차림이 아니지요. 나는 한 번 테헤란의 태수를 본 적이 있는데, 그는 비단 터번을 두르고 금실로 짠 천의 망토를 걸쳤으며, 검의 자루와 칼집은 보석의 광채로 내 눈을 어지럽게 했어요. 나는 오시리스가 해에서 광휘를 빌려 그에게 준 줄 알았답니다. 아무래도 당신은 아직 당신의 왕국에—나와 함께 나누기로 했던 그 왕국에 들어서지 못한 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"내 슬기로운 손님의 따님은 자기가 생각하는 것보다 친절하시군요. 이시스가 마음에 입을 맞춘다 해서 그 마음이 더 나아지는 건 아님을 내게 가르쳐 주고 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n벤허가 차가운 예의를 갖추어 말하자, 이라스는 동전 목걸이에 매달린 외알 보석을 만지작거리다가 받아쳤다. \"유대인치고는, 후르의 아들은 영리하군요. 나는 당신의 꿈꾸는 카이사르가 예루살렘에 입성하는 걸 보았어요. 당신은 그날 그가 성전 계단에서 유대인의 왕임을 선포하리라 했지요. 나는 행렬이 그를 모시고 산을 내려오는 걸 지켜보았어요. 그들이 노래하는 걸 들었지요. 그들은 종려나무 가지를 흔들며 아름다웠어요. 나는 그 무리 가운데서 왕다운 기상을 약속하는 인물을 찾아 사방을 둘러보았어요—자줏빛 옷을 입은 기마병, 번쩍이는 놋쇠 갑옷의 마부가 모는 전차, 둥근 방패 뒤에 선, 키가 제 창에 견줄 만한 당당한 무사를요. 나는 그의 호위대를 찾았어요. 예루살렘의 왕자와 갈릴리 군단의 한 보병대를 보았다면 기뻤을 텐데요.\"\n\n그녀는 듣는 이에게 사람을 약 올리는 멸시의 눈길을 던지고는, 마음속 그림의 우스꽝스러움이 경멸을 누를 만큼 강렬했다는 듯, 한바탕 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n\"개선하여 돌아오는 세소스트리스나 투구를 쓰고 칼을 찬 카이사르 대신에—하, 하, 하!—나는 여자의 얼굴과 머리를 한 사내가 나귀 새끼를 타고 눈물 흘리는 걸 보았어요. 그 왕! 하느님의 아들! 세상의 구원자! 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n벤허는 자기도 모르게 움찔했다.\n\n\"나는 자리를 뜨지 않았어요, 오, 예루살렘의 왕자여.\" 그가 미처 정신을 추스르기도 전에 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 웃지 않았어요. 나는 속으로 말했지요. '기다려라. 성전에서 그는 세상을 차지하려는 영웅답게 자기를 영광스럽게 드러낼 것이다.' 나는 그가 수산 문과 여인의 뜰로 들어가는 걸 보았어요. 그가 아름다운 문 앞에 멈춰 서는 걸 보았지요. 회랑과 뜰에는 나와 함께 있던 사람들이 있었고, 성전 세 면의 주랑과 계단에는 또 다른 사람들이 있었어요—백만 명은 되었다고 해 두지요. 모두 숨죽이며 그의 선포를 들으려 기다리고 있었어요. 기둥들도 우리보다 더 고요하진 못했어요. 하, 하, 하! 나는 그 거대한 로마 기계의 굴대가 삐걱거리기 시작하는 소리를 들은 것만 같았어요. 하, 하, 하! 오, 왕자여, 솔로몬의 영혼을 두고 맹세컨대, 당신의 세상의 왕은 옷자락을 추슬러 걸어가 버렸어요. 가장 먼 문으로 나가 버렸지요. 한마디 하려고 입을 열지도 않았어요. 그리고—로마 기계는 아직도 잘만 돌아가고 있답니다!\"\n\n그 순간 잃어버린 한 가닥 희망—무너지기 시작하여 무너져 가는 동안, 그가 무심코 그 사라짐을 끝까지 작별의 눈길로 좇았던 그 희망—에 대한 순수한 경의로, 벤허는 눈을 내리깔았다.\n\n발타사르가 그에게 논증을 펼치던 때든, 그의 눈앞에서 기적이 일어나던 때든, 그 어느 때에도 나사렛 사람의 논란 많은 본성이 이토록 또렷하게 그의 앞에 펼쳐진 적은 없었다. 따지고 보면 신적인 것을 이해하는 가장 좋은 길은 인간적인 것을 살피는 데 있다. 사람보다 뛰어난 것들 속에서 우리는 언제나 하느님을 찾아볼 수 있다. 나사렛 사람이 아름다운 문에서 돌아서던 장면을 그 이집트 여자가 그려 낸 그림도 그러했다. 그 중심 주제는 한낱 인간의 영감에 좌우되는 사람으로서는 도저히 해낼 수 없는 행동이었다. 비유를 좋아하는 백성에게 던진 한 편의 비유로서, 그것은 그리스도가 그토록 자주 단언한 바—자기의 사명은 정치적인 것이 아니라는 것을 가르쳐 주었다. 이 모든 것을 생각할 시간은 보통의 한 호흡에 허락된 만큼밖에 되지 않았다. 그런데도 그 생각은 벤허를 단단히 사로잡았고, 같은 순간에 그는 복수의 희망이 시야에서 사라지는 것을 끝까지 좇았으며, 여자의 얼굴과 머리를 하고 눈물 흘리던 그 사람이 그에게 가까이 다가왔다—제 영혼의 무언가를 그에게 남기고 갈 만큼 가까이.\n\n\"발타사르의 따님이여.\" 그가 위엄을 갖추어 말했다. \"이것이 당신이 내게 말한 그 노름이라면, 화관을 가지시오—그대의 것임을 인정하리다. 다만 이제 말은 그만둡시다. 당신에게 어떤 목적이 있음을 나는 확신하오. 부디 그 본론으로 들어가시오, 그러면 답하리다. 그런 다음 우리는 각자의 길로 가서, 서로 만났던 일을 잊읍시다. 말하시오. 들어 드리리다. 다만 당신이 지금까지 내게 들려준 그런 말은 더 이상 듣지 않겠소.\"\n\n그녀는 무엇을 할지 작정하려는 듯—어쩌면 그의 의지를 가늠하고 있었는지도 모른다—잠시 그를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 차갑게 말했다. \"허락하지요—가세요.\"\n\n\"그대에게 평안이 있기를.\" 그는 그렇게 답하고 걸어 나갔다.\n\n문을 막 나서려는데, 그녀가 그를 불러 세웠다.\n\n\"한마디만.\"\n\n그는 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 돌아보았다.\n\n\"내가 당신에 대해 아는 모든 걸 헤아려 보세요.\"\n\n\"오, 더없이 아리따운 이집트 여인이여.\" 그는 되돌아오며 말했다. \"당신이 나에 대해 안다는 그 모든 게 대체 무엇이오?\"\n\n그녀는 멍하니 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은, 후르의 아들이여, 당신의 어느 히브리 형제보다도 로마인에 가까운 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"내가 그렇게도 내 동족과 다르단 말이오?\" 그가 무심히 물었다.\n\n\"반신반인(半神半人)은 이제 모두 로마인이지요.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"그래서 당신은 나에 대해 더 아는 걸 내게 말해 주려는 거요?\"\n\n\"그 닮음이 내 눈을 비껴가지는 않았어요. 그것이 어쩌면 당신을 구해 주고 싶은 마음을 내게 일으킬지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"날 구한다고!\"\n\n연분홍빛으로 물든 손가락이 목에 걸린 윤기 흐르는 장식을 곱게 만지작거렸고, 그녀의 목소리는 더없이 낮고 부드러웠다. 다만 비단 신발로 바닥을 톡톡 두드리는 소리만이 조심하라고 그에게 일러 주었다.\n\n\"한 유대인이 있었어요. 갤리선에서 도망친 노예였는데, 이데르네이 궁전에서 한 사내를 죽였지요.\" 그녀가 천천히 말문을 열었다.\n\n벤허는 흠칫했다.\n\n\"같은 그 유대인이 여기 예루살렘의 시장 앞에서 로마 병사 하나를 죽였어요. 같은 그 유대인이 오늘 밤 로마 총독을 사로잡으려고 갈릴리에서 훈련된 세 개 군단을 데려왔지요. 같은 그 유대인이 로마와 싸우려고 동맹을 완벽하게 맺어 두었는데, 일데림 족장이 그 동맹자 가운데 하나예요.\"\n\n그에게 더 가까이 다가서며, 그녀는 거의 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 로마에서 살아 보았지요. 이 일들이 우리 둘 다 아는 어떤 귀에 들어간다고 해 봐요. 아! 당신, 안색이 변하는군요.\"\n\n그는 그녀에게서 물러섰으니, 그 얼굴에는 새끼 고양이와 장난치려다 호랑이에게 달려든 사람의 얼굴에 떠올랐을 법한 빛이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"당신은 궁정 대기실 사람들과 안면이 있고, 세야누스 공도 알지요. 증거를 손에 쥐고서—아니, 증거 없이라도—그 같은 유대인이 동방에서 가장 부유한 사람이라고, 아니 온 제국에서 가장 부유한 사람이라고 그에게 일러바친다고 해 봐요. 티베르강의 물고기들은 강바닥 진흙에서 파내는 것 말고 다른 살찌울 거리를 얻게 되지 않겠어요? 그리고 그것들이 배를 불리는 동안—하! 후르의 아들이여!—원형경기장에는 얼마나 화려한 구경거리가 펼쳐지겠어요! 로마 백성을 즐겁게 하는 건 훌륭한 기술이지요. 그들을 계속 즐겁게 해 줄 돈을 마련하는 건 그보다 더 훌륭한 기술이고요. 한데 세야누스 공에 견줄 만한 예술가가 일찍이 있었던가요?\"\n\n벤허는 그 여자의 명백한 비열함에 너무 마음이 흔들린 나머지 도리어 기억을 잃을 지경은 아니었다. 다른 모든 능력이 마비되어 스러져 갈 때에도, 흔히 기억은 더없이 충실하게 제 소임을 다하는 법이다. 요르단으로 가는 길의 샘가 장면이 되살아났다. 그때 그는 에스델이 자기를 배신했다고 생각했음을 떠올렸고, 지금도 그렇게 생각하며, 될 수 있는 한 침착하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 즐겁게 해 드리고자, 이집트의 따님이여, 나는 당신의 교활함을 인정하고, 내가 당신의 처분에 달려 있음을 인정하리다. 또한 당신의 호의 따위는 바라지 않음을 내가 인정하는 것을 듣는 것도 당신을 즐겁게 할지 모르겠소. 나는 당신을 죽일 수도 있으나, 당신은 여자요. 사막은 나를 받아들이려 열려 있소. 비록 로마가 사람을 잘 사냥하는 자라 하나, 나를 붙잡기까지는 거기서 오래도록 멀리 쫓아야 할 것이오. 그 사막의 한복판에는 모래의 광야뿐 아니라 창의 광야도 있고, 정복되지 않은 파르티아인에게 그곳은 그리 정 떨어지는 곳도 아니니 말이오. 이렇게 덫에 걸려—얼마나 어리석은 멍청이였던가—그래도 내게 마땅히 주어져야 할 것이 하나 있소. 나에 대해 당신이 아는 그 모든 걸 누가 말해 주었소? 도망치든 사로잡히든, 설령 죽는다 해도, 비참함 말고는 아무것도 모르고 살아온 사내의 저주를 그 배신자에게 남기는 것이 위안이 될 거요. 나에 대해 당신이 아는 그 모든 걸 누가 말해 주었소?\"\n\n기교의 한 자락이었는지, 아니면 진심이었는지—그야 어쨌든—그 이집트 여자의 얼굴에는 공감의 빛이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"내 나라에는요, 오, 후르의 아들이여.\" 그녀가 이윽고 말했다. \"폭풍이 지나간 뒤 바닷가 여기저기서 빛깔 고운 조개껍데기를 주워 모아 그걸 잘라서, 그 조각들을 대리석판에 박아 넣어 그림을 만드는 장인들이 있어요. 비밀을 캐러 다니는 이들에게 그 솜씨가 주는 암시가 보이지 않으세요? 이 사람에게서 자잘한 사정 한 줌을 모으고, 저 사람에게서 또 한 줌을 모아서, 한참 뒤에 그것들을 짜 맞춘 끝에, 한 사내의 재산과 목숨을 마음대로 할 수 있게 된 여자가 누릴 수 있는 만큼 행복했다고 해 두면 충분하겠지요. 그 사내를—\" 그녀는 말을 멈추고 발로 바닥을 두드리며, 별안간 솟구친 감정을 그에게 감추려는 듯 눈길을 돌렸다. 이윽고 고통스럽다 할 만한 결연한 태도로 그 문장을 끝맺었다. \"—그 사내를 어찌해야 할지 몰라 막막한 여자가요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그것으로는 충분치 않소.\" 벤허는 그 연극에 흔들리지 않고 말했다—\"충분치 않소. 내일 당신은 나를 어찌할지 정하겠지. 나는 죽을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 그녀는 재빨리, 힘주어 받아쳤다. \"일데림 족장에게서 무언가를 얻었지요. 그가 사막의 한 숲에서 내 아버지와 함께 누워 있을 때였어요. 밤은 고요했어요, 아주 고요했지요. 그리고, 솔직히 말하면, 천막의 벽이란 게 밖에서 엿듣는 귀를 막는 데는 변변치 못한 방패더군요—공중을 날아다니는 새와 풍뎅이의 귀 말이에요.\"\n\n그녀는 제 익살에 미소 짓고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"또 다른 것들—그림에 박을 조개껍데기 몇 조각은—이런 이에게서 얻었지요—\"\n\"누구한테서?\"\n\n\"바로 후르의 아들 자신에게서요.\"\n\n\"달리 거든 사람은 없었소?\"\n\n\"없어요, 하나도.\"\n\n후르는 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 가볍게 말했다. \"고맙소. 세야누스 공을 기다리게 해서야 당신에게 좋지 않겠지. 사막은 그렇게 까다롭지 않다오. 다시 한번, 오, 이집트여, 평안하시오!\"\n\n이때까지 그는 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않은 채 서 있었다. 이제 그는 팔에 걸쳐 두었던 머릿수건을 집어 머리에 매만져 두르고는, 떠나려고 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 그녀가 그를 붙잡았다. 다급한 마음에 그녀는 그에게 손까지 내밀었다.\n\n\"가지 마세요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀를 돌아보았으나, 보석으로 눈부시게 빛나 유난히 눈에 띄는 그 손은 잡지 않았다. 그리고 그녀의 태도로 미루어, 자기를 그토록 놀라게 한 이 장면의 아껴 둔 핵심이 이제 나오리라는 것을 그는 알았다.\n\n\"가지 말고, 나를 의심하지 마세요, 오, 후르의 아들이여. 고귀한 아리우스가 어찌하여 당신을 상속자로 삼았는지 내가 안다고 말한다 해도요. 그리고, 이시스를 두고! 이집트의 모든 신을 두고 맹세컨대! 그토록 용감하고 너그러운 당신이 그 무자비한 대신의 손아귀에 들어간다고 생각하면 나는 몸이 떨려요. 당신은 위대한 수도의 안마당에 청춘의 한 자락을 두고 왔지요. 나처럼 헤아려 보세요. 그런 삶에 견주어 사막이 당신에게 어떤 것일지를요. 오, 당신이 가여워요—가여워! 당신이 내 말대로만 하면, 내가 당신을 구해 드리겠어요. 그것도, 우리의 거룩한 이시스를 두고 맹세하지요!\"\n\n이 모든 애원과 간청의 말이 거침없이, 진지하게, 그리고 아름다움이라는 강력한 권위에 실려 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n\"하마터면—하마터면 당신을 믿을 뻔했소.\" 벤허는 그래도 망설이며, 낮고 또렷하지 않은 목소리로 말했다. 그의 안에는 사내의 누그러지려는 성향에 맞서 으르렁대는 의심이 남아 있었기 때문이다—수많은 목숨과 재산을 구해 낸, 든든하고 굳건한 그런 의심이었다.\n\n\"여자에게 완전한 삶은 사랑 속에 사는 것이고, 남자에게 가장 큰 행복은 자기 자신을 이기는 것이지요. 그리고, 오, 왕자여, 그것이 바로 내가 당신에게 청하려는 것이랍니다.\"\n\n그녀는 빠르게, 생기 넘치게 말했다. 정말이지 그녀가 그토록 매혹적으로 보인 적은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"당신에게는 한때 벗이 있었지요.\" 그녀가 이어 갔다. \"당신의 소년 시절이었어요. 다툼이 있었고, 당신과 그는 원수가 되었지요. 그가 당신에게 못된 짓을 했어요. 여러 해 뒤, 당신은 안티오크의 원형경기장에서 그를 다시 만났지요.\"\n\n\"메살라!\"\n\n\"그래요, 메살라. 당신은 그의 채권자예요. 지난 일을 용서하세요. 그를 다시 벗으로 받아들이세요. 그가 그 큰 내기에서 잃은 재산을 되돌려 주세요. 그를 구해 주세요. 당신에게 여섯 탈렌트는 아무것도 아니에요. 이미 잎이 무성한 나무에서 봉오리 하나 잃는 것만도 못하지요. 하지만 그에게는—아, 그는 부서진 몸을 이끌고 살아가야 해요. 당신을 만나는 곳마다 그는 땅바닥에서 당신을 우러러보아야 하지요. 오, 벤허, 고귀한 왕자여! 그처럼 혈통 있는 로마인에게 거지 신세란 죽음의 또 다른, 가장 끔찍한 이름이에요. 그를 거지 신세에서 구해 주세요!\"\n\n만일 그녀가 그토록 빠르게 말한 것이 그를 생각하지 못하게 하려는 교묘한 술책이었다면, 그녀는 생각에서 비롯되지 않고 허락도 기별도 없이 제자리에 떨어지는 어떤 확신이 있다는 것을 아예 몰랐거나, 아니면 잊고 있었던 셈이다. 마침내 그녀가 그의 대답을 들으려고 말을 멈추었을 때, 그에게는 메살라 자신이 그녀의 어깨 너머로 자기를 들여다보는 것이 보이는 듯했다. 그리고 그 표정에 어린 로마인의 얼굴은 구걸하는 자의 것도, 벗의 것도 아니었다. 그 비웃음은 여전히 귀족다웠고, 거만함의 그 날카로운 날은 흠 하나 없이 사람을 거슬렸다.\n\n\"그렇다면 청은 판가름이 났고, 메살라가 단 한 번 아무것도 얻지 못하게 되었군. 가서 내 큰일들의 기록부에 적어 두어야겠소—로마인에 맞서 로마인이 내린 판결이라고! 한데 그가—메살라가 이 청을 가지고 당신을 내게 보냈소, 오, 이집트여?\"\n\n\"그는 고귀한 천성을 지녔기에, 당신도 그러하리라 여겼지요.\"\n\n벤허는 그녀의 팔에 얹힌 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"당신이 그를 그토록 다정한 사이로 안다 하니, 어여쁜 이집트 여인이여, 말해 주시오. 처지가 뒤바뀌어, 그가 내게 청하는 것을 내가 그에게 청한다면, 그가 나를 위해 그리해 주겠소? 이시스를 두고 답하시오! 진실을 위해 답하시오!\"\n\n그의 손길에도, 눈길에도 다그침이 서려 있었다.\n\n\"오!\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. \"그는—\"\n\n\"로마인이다, 그렇게 말하려던 거지. 곧 나, 유대인은 그가 내게 진 빚의 잣대로 내가 그에게 받을 빚을 가늠해서는 안 된다는 거요. 유대인이니, 나는 그가 로마인이라는 이유로 내가 딴 것을 그에게 용서해 주어야 한다는 거지. 더 할 말이 있거든, 발타사르의 따님이여, 어서, 어서 말하시오. 이스라엘의 주 하느님을 두고 맹세컨대, 이 끓는 피가 더욱 뜨거워져 가장 높은 곳에 이르면, 나는 더 이상 당신이 여자이며 아름답다는 것을 보지 못할지도 모르오! 다만 한 주인의 첩자만이 보일 뿐이며, 그 주인이 로마인이기에 더욱 가증스러울 따름이오. 말하시오, 그것도 어서.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 손을 뿌리치고 환한 빛 속으로 물러서며, 제 본성의 온갖 악을 두 눈과 목소리에 모았다.\n\n\"이 찌꺼기나 들이켜고 쭉정이나 먹는 자야! 메살라를 보고서도 내가 너를 사랑할 수 있었으리라 생각하다니! 너 같은 자는 그를 섬기려고 태어난 것이다. 그는 여섯 탈렌트를 풀어 주는 것으로 만족했을 테지만, 나는 말한다. 그 여섯에 스무 탈렌트를 더하라—스물, 듣고 있느냐? 비록 내 동의로 그에게서 가져갔다 하나, 네가 가져간 내 새끼손가락의 입맞춤도 값을 치러야 한다. 또한 내가 공감의 애정으로 너를 좇으며 그토록 오래 너를 견뎌 온 것도, 그를 섬기느라 그리한 것이라 해서 셈에서 빼지 못한다. 여기 이 상인이 네 돈을 맡아 둔 자이니라. 내일 정오까지 그가 내 메살라에게 스물여섯 탈렌트—그 액수를 똑똑히 새겨라!—를 치르라는 네 명령을 실행하지 않는다면, 너는 세야누스 공과 셈을 치르게 될 것이다. 슬기롭게 굴어라, 그리고—잘 있거라.\"\n\n그녀가 문으로 가려 하자, 그가 그 앞을 가로막았다.\n\n\"옛 이집트가 당신 안에 살아 있구려.\" 그가 말했다. \"내일이든 모레든, 여기서든 로마에서든 메살라를 만나거든, 그에게 이 전갈을 전하시오. 그가 내 아버지의 재산을 강탈하여 내게서 빼앗은 돈을, 그 여섯 탈렌트마저 도로 찾았다고 그에게 전하시오. 그가 나를 보낸 갤리선에서 살아남아, 이제 내 힘으로 그의 거지 신세와 불명예를 기뻐한다고 전하시오. 내 손으로 그에게 입힌 몸의 고통이, 그가 무력한 자들에게 저지른 죄에 대해 죽음보다 더 합당한, 이스라엘의 주 하느님께서 그에게 내리신 저주라고 내가 여긴다고 전하시오. 그가 나병으로 죽으라고 안토니아의 감방에 보냈던 내 어머니와 누이가, 당신이 그토록 업신여기는 나사렛 사람의 권능 덕분에 살아 건강하다고 전하시오. 내 행복을 가득 채우려고 그들이 내게 돌아왔으며, 나는 이제 그들의 사랑을 향해 떠나, 당신이 내게 그에게로 가져가라고 떠넘긴 그 더러운 정욕에 대한 보상보다 더한 것을 그 사랑 속에서 찾으리라고 전하시오. 그에게 전하시오—오, 교활함의 화신이여, 이건 그를 위해서만큼이나 당신을 위한 위안이오—세야누스 공이 나를 약탈하러 와도 아무것도 찾지 못하리라고 전하시오. 미세눔의 별장을 비롯해 그 두움비르에게서 물려받은 유산은 이미 팔아넘겼고, 그 판 돈은 환어음이 되어 세상의 시장들을 떠다니며 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있으니 말이오. 그리고 이 집과 재물과 상품, 시모니데스가 그토록 어마어마한 이문을 남기며 장사를 부리는 배와 대상(隊商)들은 황제의 보호를 받고 있다고 전하시오—슬기로운 머리가 그 호의의 값을 알아냈고, 세야누스 공은 피와 불의의 웅덩이에서 낚아 올린 큰 이익보다 선물의 형식으로 들어오는 적당한 이득을 더 좋아하니 말이오. 설령 이 모든 게 그렇지 않다 해도, 그 돈과 재산이 모조리 내 것이라 해도, 그는 그 가운데 티끌만큼도 가지지 못하리라고 전하시오. 그가 우리의 유대 어음들을 찾아내어 억지로 그 값을 토해 내게 한다 해도, 내게는 아직 마지막 수단이 하나 남아 있으니—카이사르께 바치는 증여 증서가 그것이오—이만큼은, 오, 이집트여, 내가 위대한 수도의 안마당에서 알아낸 것이라오. 내 도전과 더불어 말로 된 저주는 보내지 않으나, 사그라지지 않는 내 증오를 더 잘 드러내는 표시로, 그에게 온갖 저주의 총합임을 증명해 줄 사람 하나를 보낸다고 전하시오. 그리고 발타사르의 따님이여, 당신이 이 전갈을 되풀이하는 것을 그가 바라볼 때, 그 로마인의 약삭빠름이 내 말뜻을 모조리 그에게 일러 줄 것이오. 이제 가시오—나도 가리다.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 문까지 바래다주었고, 격식을 갖춘 정중함으로 그녀가 나갈 때까지 휘장을 걷어 잡고 있었다.\n\n\"그대에게 평안이 있기를.\" 그녀가 사라질 때, 그가 말했다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n벤허가 객실을 나설 때, 그의 거동에는 들어갈 때만큼의 활기가 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 걸음은 느려졌고, 고개는 가슴께까지 푹 떨군 채였다. 등이 부러진 사람이라도 멀쩡한 머리는 지닐 수 있음을 깨달은 그는, 그 깨달음을 곱씹고 있었다.\n\n재앙이 닥친 뒤에 돌아보면 그것이 다가오던 흔적이 길 위에 흩뿌려져 있었음을 알아채기란 쉬운 법이다. 그러나 그는 이집트 여인이 메살라의 편이라는 것을 의심조차 하지 않았고, 여러 해 동안 눈먼 채로 나아가며 자기 자신과 벗들을 점점 더 그녀의 손아귀에 내맡겨 왔으니, 그 생각은 이 젊은이의 자존심에 아픈 상처가 되었다. \"이제 와 떠올려 보니,\" 그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"그녀는 카스탈리아의 샘에서 그 신의 없는 로마인을 두고 한마디 분노도 내비치지 않았다! 떠올려 보니, 종려나무 정원의 호수에서 뱃놀이를 할 때 그녀는 오히려 그를 추켜세웠다! 그리고, 아!\" 그는 걸음을 멈추고 오른손으로 왼손을 사납게 내리쳤다. \"아! 이데르네 궁전에서 나와 약속을 잡은 그 수수께끼도 이제는 더 이상 수수께끼가 아니다!\"\n\n여기서 눈여겨봐야 할 것은, 그 상처가 자존심에 난 것이라는 사실이다. 다행히도 사람이 그런 상처로 죽는 일은 드물고, 오래도록 앓아눕는 일조차 흔하지 않다. 더욱이 벤허의 경우에는 보상이 따랐으니, 그가 곧 소리 내어 외쳤기 때문이다. \"주 하느님께 찬미를 드리노라, 그 여인이 나를 더 깊이 사로잡지 못한 것을! 나는 그녀를 사랑하지 않았음을 알겠다.\"\n\n그러더니, 마음을 짓누르던 무게를 적잖이 덜어 낸 듯, 그의 발걸음은 한결 가벼워졌다. 테라스에서 한쪽 계단은 아래 안뜰로 내려가고 다른 한쪽은 지붕으로 올라가는 곳에 이르자, 그는 후자를 택해 오르기 시작했다. 그러다 마지막 계단을 밟으며 그는 다시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"발타사르가 그녀가 그토록 오래 써 온 가면극의 동반자였을 수 있을까? 아니, 아니다. 위선은 그렇게 주름진 노년과는 좀처럼 어울리지 않는다. 발타사르는 선한 사람이다.\"\n\n이렇게 마음을 정하고 그는 지붕 위로 올라섰다. 머리 위에는 보름달이 떠 있었으나, 바로 그 순간 하늘의 둥근 천장은 도시의 거리와 빈터에서 타오르는 불빛이 솟아올라 불그레하게 물들어 있었고, 이스라엘의 오래된 시편을 노래하고 합창하는 소리가 구슬픈 가락으로 그 하늘을 가득 채우니, 그는 귀 기울이지 않을 수 없었다. 그 가락을 떠받치는 수없이 많은 목소리들은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"유다의 아들이여, 이렇게 우리는 주 하느님을 향한 우리의 경배와, 그분이 주신 이 땅에 대한 우리의 충성을 증명하나이다. 기드온이 나타나든, 다윗이 나타나든, 마카베오가 나타나든, 우리는 준비되어 있나이다.\"\n\n그것은 서막처럼 들렸으니, 다음 순간 그는 나사렛 사람을 떠올렸다.\n\n어떤 기분에 잠겨 있을 때면 마음은 어울리지 않는 환상으로 스스로를 희롱하기도 한다.\n\n집의 북쪽, 거리 위로 솟은 난간을 향해 지붕을 가로질러 가는 동안, 눈물 어린 여인 같은 그리스도의 얼굴이 그에게서 떠나지 않았다. 그 얼굴에는 싸움의 기색이라곤 없었다. 오히려 고요한 저녁의 하늘이 만물에 평화를 비추듯, 그 얼굴이 그렇게 보였으니, 해묵은 물음을 다시 불러일으켰다. 저 사람은 대체 어떤 부류의 사람인가?\n\n벤허는 난간 너머로 한 번 눈길을 던지더니, 몸을 돌려 정자를 향해 무심히 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"마음껏 해 보라지.\" 그는 천천히 걸으며 말했다. \"나는 그 로마인을 용서하지 않으리라. 그와 내 재산을 나누지도 않을 것이며, 조상들의 이 도시에서 달아나지도 않으리라. 먼저 갈릴리에 호소하여 이곳에서 싸움을 벌이리라. 용감한 행동으로 여러 지파를 우리 편으로 끌어오리라. 모세를 일으키신 분께서, 내가 실패한다면 우리에게 또 다른 지도자를 보내 주시리라. 나사렛 사람이 아니라면, 자유를 위해 죽기를 마다하지 않는 수많은 이들 가운데 누군가가 나타나리라.\"\n\n벤허가 느릿느릿 거닐다 정자에 이르렀을 때, 그 안은 어슴푸레하게 밝혀져 있었다. 북쪽과 서쪽 기둥들이 던지는 희미하기 짝이 없는 그림자가 바닥을 따라 누워 있었다. 안을 들여다보니, 평소 시모니데스가 차지하던 안락의자가 시장 쪽으로 도시를 가장 잘 내려다볼 수 있는 자리로 끌어당겨져 있었다.\n\n\"그 선한 어른이 돌아오셨군. 잠들지 않으셨다면 말씀을 나눠야겠다.\"\n\n그는 안으로 들어가 조용한 걸음으로 의자에 다가갔다. 높은 등받이 너머로 들여다보니, 에스더가 잠든 채 의자에 파묻혀 있었다. 아버지의 무릎담요 아래 포근히 몸을 웅크린 작은 모습이었다. 헝클어진 머리카락이 얼굴 위로 흘러내렸다. 그녀의 숨결은 낮고 고르지 않았다. 한번은 긴 한숨에 끊어졌다가 흐느낌으로 끝나기도 했다. 무언가가—그 한숨이었는지, 그녀가 홀로 있는 그 외로움이었는지—그에게 그 잠이 고단함에서 오는 휴식이라기보다 슬픔에서 벗어난 쉼이라는 생각을 일깨웠다. 자연은 이런 위안을 아이들에게 너그러이 베푸는 법이고, 그는 에스더를 아이나 다름없다고 늘 여겨 왔다. 그는 의자 등받이에 두 팔을 얹고 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"깨우지 말아야겠다. 그녀에게 들려줄 이야기가 없으니—없다, 만약 그것이—만약 그것이 내 사랑이 아니라면…… 그녀는 유다의 딸이고, 아름다우며, 그 이집트 여인과는 어찌나 다른가. 거기에는 온통 허영뿐이지만, 여기에는 온통 진실이 있다. 거기에는 야심이, 여기에는 책무가 있다. 거기에는 이기심이, 여기에는 자기희생이 있다…… 아니, 물음은 내가 그녀를 사랑하느냐가 아니라, 그녀가 나를 사랑하느냐다. 그녀는 처음부터 내 벗이었다. 안티오크의 테라스에서 보낸 그날 밤, 그녀는 얼마나 아이처럼 로마를 적으로 삼지 말라고 내게 간청하며, 미세눔의 별장과 그곳의 삶에 대해 들려 달라고 졸랐던가! 나는 그녀의 그 영리한 속내를 알아챘다는 것을 들키지 않으려고 입을 맞추었다. 그녀가 그 입맞춤을 잊었을 리 있을까! 나는 잊지 않았다. 나는 그녀를 사랑한다…… 도시 사람들은 내가 가족을 되찾았다는 것을 모른다. 그것을 그 이집트 여인에게 말하기는 꺼려졌다. 그러나 이 어린 사람은 그들이 돌아온 것을 나와 함께 기뻐할 것이고, 사랑과 손과 마음의 다정한 보살핌으로 그들을 맞이할 것이다. 그녀는 내 어머니에게 또 한 명의 딸이 되어 줄 것이고, 티르자는 그녀에게서 또 하나의 자기 자신을 찾으리라. 그녀를 깨워 이 이야기들을 들려주고 싶지만—그 이집트의 마녀는 꺼지라지! 그 어리석은 일에 대해서는 도무지 입을 뗄 마음이 들지 않는구나. 나는 물러가, 더 나은 다른 때를 기다리리라. 기다리겠다. 아리따운 에스더, 효성스러운 아이, 유다의 딸이여!\"\n\n그는 들어올 때처럼 소리 없이 물러갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n거리는 오가는 사람들로 가득했고, 더러는 불가에 모여 고기를 굽고, 잔치를 벌이고, 노래하며 즐거워하고 있었다. 살이 타는 냄새가 향나무 장작이 타며 피우는 연기 냄새와 뒤섞여 공기를 채웠다. 이날만큼은 이스라엘의 모든 아들이 다른 모든 이스라엘의 아들과 한 형제가 되고, 환대에는 끝이 없었으므로, 벤허는 걸음을 옮길 때마다 인사를 받았고, 불가에 모인 사람들은 한사코 권했다. \"머물러 우리와 함께 드시오. 우리는 주님의 사랑 안에서 형제이외다.\" 그러나 그는 고맙다는 말을 남기고 발걸음을 재촉했으니, 객사에서 말을 타고 케드론 가의 천막으로 돌아갈 작정이었다.\n\n그곳에 이르자면, 머지않아 그리스도교에 슬픈 기념으로 남게 될 거리를 가로질러야 했다. 그곳에서도 경건한 축제가 절정에 이르러 있었다. 거리 위쪽을 바라보던 그는 횃불의 불길이 깃발처럼 펄럭이며 흘러나오는 것을 알아차렸다. 이어 횃불이 닿는 곳마다 노래가 그친다는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그의 놀라움이 절정에 달한 것은, 연기와 춤추는 불티 속에서 잘 닦인 창끝이 더 날카롭게 번득이는 것을 분명히 보았을 때였으니, 그것은 로마 병사들이 있다는 증거였다. 저 비웃기 좋아하는 군단병들이 유대인의 종교 행렬 속에서 무엇을 하고 있단 말인가? 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 일이라, 그는 그 까닭을 알아보려고 멈춰 섰다.\n\n달이 한껏 밝게 빛나고 있었다. 그런데도 달과 횃불과 거리의 불, 창과 열린 문에서 흘러나오는 빛만으로는 길을 밝히기에 모자라기라도 한 듯, 행렬에 낀 몇몇은 불 켠 등을 들고 있었다. 그런 장비를 쓰는 데 무언가 특별한 목적이 있으리라 짐작한 벤허는, 지나가는 일행을 하나하나 살펴볼 수 있을 만큼 행렬의 줄에 바싹 붙어 거리로 들어섰다. 횃불과 등은 하인들이 들고 있었고, 그들은 저마다 몽둥이나 끝을 뾰족하게 깎은 막대로 무장하고 있었다. 그들의 지금 임무는 일행 가운데 몇몇 높은 이들—장로와 제사장들, 긴 수염에 굵은 눈썹과 매부리코를 한 랍비들, 가야바와 한나스의 의회에서 세력을 떨치는 부류의 사람들—을 위해 거리의 바위 사이로 가장 평탄한 길을 골라 주는 것인 듯했다. 그들은 어디로 가는 것일까? 성전은 분명 아니었으니, 이들이 떠나온 듯한 시온에서 성전으로 가는 길은 크시스투스를 거치기 때문이다. 그리고 그들의 볼일이—평화로운 것이라면, 어찌하여 병사들이 있단 말인가?\n\n행렬이 벤허 앞을 지나가기 시작할 때, 함께 걷는 세 사람에게 특히 그의 눈길이 쏠렸다. 그들은 앞쪽에 가까이 서 있었고, 등을 들고 그들 앞에 선 하인들은 시중을 드는 데 유난히 조심스러워 보였다. 이 무리의 왼편에서 움직이는 사람에게서 그는 성전의 경비 책임자를 알아보았다. 오른편 사람은 제사장이었다. 가운데 사람은 처음에는 그리 쉽게 가려낼 수 없었으니, 그는 양옆 사람의 팔에 무겁게 기대어 걸으며, 얼굴을 감추려는 듯 머리를 가슴께로 푹 숙이고 있었다. 그 모습은 체포의 공포에서 아직 헤어나지 못한 죄수, 혹은 무언가 끔찍한 것—고문이나 죽음—으로 끌려가는 자의 것이었다. 좌우에서 그를 부축하는 높은 이들과 그에게 쏟는 관심으로 미루어, 설령 그 자신이 이 무리를 움직이게 한 목표가 아니더라도, 적어도 그 목표와 어떤 식으로든 얽혀 있음이—증인이거나 길잡이, 어쩌면 밀고자임이—분명했다. 그러니 그가 누구인지만 알아낸다면 지금 벌어지는 일을 영리하게 짐작할 수 있을 터였다. 벤허는 한껏 대담하게 제사장의 오른편에 끼어들어 그와 나란히 걸었다. 이제 저 사람이 머리를 들어 주기만 한다면! 이윽고 그가 머리를 들어, 등불의 빛이 그의 얼굴에 정면으로 비치게 했다. 창백하고 멍하니 두려움에 일그러진 얼굴, 헝클어진 수염, 흐릿하고 움푹 들어가 절망에 잠긴 두 눈이었다. 나사렛 사람을 따라 두루 다니는 동안 벤허는 그 스승만큼이나 제자들도 알게 되었던 터라, 그 침울한 얼굴을 보자 그는 외쳤다.\n\n\"가룟 사람이로구나!\"\n\n천천히 그 사람의 머리가 돌아가더니, 그 두 눈이 벤허에게 머물렀고, 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 입술이 움직였다. 그러나 제사장이 가로막았다.\n\n\"너는 누구냐? 썩 물러가라!\" 제사장은 그를 밀쳐 내며 벤허에게 말했다.\n\n젊은이는 그 밀침을 너그럽게 받아넘기고, 기회를 엿보다가 다시 행렬에 끼어들었다. 그렇게 그는 거리를 따라 떠밀리듯 끌려 내려가, 베세다 언덕과 안토니아 요새 사이에 붐비는 저지대를 지나, 베데스다 저수지를 거쳐 양 문에 이르렀다. 어디에나 사람들이 있었고, 어디에서나 사람들은 거룩한 의식에 몰두해 있었다.\n\n유월절 밤이었으므로, 성문의 두 짝은 열려 있었다. 문지기들은 어디론가 잔치를 즐기러 가고 없었다. 행렬이 아무런 제지도 받지 않고 빠져나갈 때, 그 앞에는 케드론의 깊은 골짜기가 펼쳐졌고, 그 너머로는 올리벳이 솟아 있었다. 향나무와 올리브나무로 단장한 그 산은, 온 하늘을 은빛으로 물들이는 달빛 속에서 더욱 어둡게 보였다. 성문에서 두 길이 만나 거리로 합쳐졌으니, 하나는 북동쪽에서, 다른 하나는 베다니에서 오는 길이었다. 더 멀리 가야 하는지, 만일 그렇다면 어느 길로 가야 하는지 벤허가 미처 다 헤아리기도 전에, 그는 골짜기 아래로 이끌려 내려갔다. 그런데도 이 한밤중 행군의 목적에 대해서는 여전히 아무런 실마리도 없었다.\n\n골짜기를 따라 내려가, 그 바닥의 다리를 건넜다. 이제 흩어진 군중이 된 무리가 몽둥이와 막대로 두드리고 짓찧으며 건너가니, 다리 바닥에서 요란한 소리가 났다. 조금 더 가서 그들은 길에서 보이는, 돌담으로 둘러싸인 올리브 과수원 쪽으로 왼편으로 꺾어 들었다. 벤허는 그곳에 늙고 옹이진 나무들과 풀, 그리고 그 고장의 방식대로 기름을 짜기 위해 바위를 파서 만든 통 말고는 아무것도 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 무엇이 이런 무리를 이런 시각에 이토록 적막한 곳으로 끌고 왔을까 하고 더욱 놀라 생각에 잠겨 있는데, 일행이 모두 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 앞쪽에서 사람들이 흥분하여 외치는 소리가 들렸다. 오싹한 느낌이 이 사람에게서 저 사람에게로 번졌다. 황급히 물러서며 서로 부딪쳐 비틀거렸다. 오직 병사들만이 대오를 지켰다.\n\n벤허가 무리에서 빠져나와 앞으로 달려 나가는 데에는 한순간이면 충분했다. 그곳에서 그는 문짝 없는 문간이 과수원으로 통하는 것을 발견하고, 광경을 가늠하려고 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n흰옷을 입고 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않은 한 사람이 입구 밖에 서서 두 손을 앞으로 모으고 있었다. 호리호리하고 구부정한 몸매에, 긴 머리와 야윈 얼굴을 한, 체념과 기다림의 자세였다.\n\n나사렛 사람이었다!\n\n그의 뒤, 문간 가까이에는 제자들이 무리 지어 있었다. 그들은 흥분해 있었으나, 그보다 더 차분한 사람은 일찍이 없었다. 횃불의 빛이 그에게 붉게 쏟아져, 그의 머리카락에 본래의 빛보다 더 불그스름한 빛을 입혔다. 그러나 그 얼굴의 표정은 여느 때와 다름없이 온통 온화함과 연민이었다.\n\n이 더없이 비전투적인 모습 맞은편에는 군중이 입을 벌린 채 말없이, 경외에 잠겨 움츠리며 서 있었으니, 그에게서 노여움의 기색이라도 비치면 곧 흩어져 달아날 채비였다. 그에게서 그들에게로—이어 그들 한가운데 두드러져 보이는 유다에게로—벤허는 눈길을 돌렸다. 한 번의 재빠른 일별에, 이 방문의 목적이 그의 이해 앞에 환히 드러났다. 여기 배신자가 있고, 저기 배신당한 이가 있었다. 그리고 몽둥이와 막대를 든 이자들과 군단병들은 그를 잡으러 온 것이었다.\n\n사람은 시련이 닥치기 전까지는 자기가 무엇을 할지 늘 알지는 못한다. 이것이야말로 벤허가 오랜 세월 대비해 온 바로 그 위급한 순간이었다. 자신이 그 안전을 위해 헌신해 왔고, 그 목숨 위에 그토록 많은 것을 쌓아 올려 온 그 사람이 몸소 위험에 처했건만, 그는 가만히 서 있었다. 사람의 본성에는 이러한 모순이 있는 것이다! 사실대로 말하자면, 오 독자여, 그는 이집트 여인이 들려준, 아름다운 문 앞에 선 그리스도의 그림에서 아직 완전히 헤어나지 못한 상태였다. 게다가 그 신비로운 인물이 군중을 마주하는 그 침착함 자체가, 이 위험을 감당하고도 남을 힘을 비축하고 있음을 시사함으로써 그를 자제하게 했다. 평화와 선의, 사랑과 무저항이 나사렛 사람이 가르친 핵심이었다. 그는 자신의 설교를 실천에 옮길 것인가? 그는 생명의 주인이었다. 그는 잃어버린 생명을 되살릴 수 있었고, 마음대로 거두어들일 수도 있었다. 이제 그 힘을 어떻게 쓸 것인가? 자신을 지킬 것인가? 그러면 어떻게? 한마디 말로—한 번의 숨으로—한 가닥 생각만으로도 충분했다. 자연을 뛰어넘는 놀라운 힘의 신호가 어떤 식으로든 나타나리라고 벤허는 믿었고, 그 믿음 속에서 기다렸다. 그리고 이 모든 동안에도 그는 여전히 나사렛 사람을 자기 자신의 잣대로—인간의 기준으로 재고 있었다.\n\n이윽고 그리스도의 맑은 목소리가 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n\"너희가 누구를 찾느냐?\"\n\n\"나사렛 예수요.\" 제사장이 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 그로다.\"\n\n격정도 놀람도 없이 내뱉은 이 더없이 단순한 말에, 습격자들은 몇 걸음 물러섰고, 그중 겁 많은 자들은 땅바닥에 움츠렸다. 유다가 그에게로 걸어가지만 않았더라면, 그들은 그를 내버려 두고 떠나갔을지도 모른다.\n\n\"안녕하시옵니까, 선생님!\"\n\n이 다정한 인사와 함께 그는 그에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"유다야,\" 나사렛 사람이 부드럽게 말했다. \"네가 입맞춤으로 인자를 파느냐? 무엇 하러 왔느냐?\"\n\n대답이 없자, 스승은 다시 군중에게 말했다.\n\"너희가 누구를 찾느냐?\"\n\n\"나사렛 예수요.\"\n\n\"내가 그라고 너희에게 일렀느니라. 그러니 너희가 나를 찾거든, 이 사람들은 제 갈 길로 가게 두라.\"\n\n이 간청의 말에 랍비들이 그에게 다가들었다. 그 의도를 알아챈, 그가 두둔해 준 제자 몇이 더 가까이 다가들었다. 그중 하나가 한 사람의 귀를 베었으나, 그렇다고 스승이 붙잡히는 것을 막지는 못했다. 그런데도 벤허는 가만히 서 있었다! 아니, 관헌들이 밧줄을 준비하는 동안, 나사렛 사람은 그의 가장 큰 자비를 베풀고 있었다—행위로 따져 가장 큰 것이 아니라, 사람의 그것을 그토록 뛰어넘는 그의 인내를 보여 주는 데에 더없이 큰 자비였다.\n\n\"이것까지 참으라.\" 그는 다친 사람에게 이렇게 말하고, 손길 한 번으로 그를 고쳤다.\n\n벗도 적도 모두 어리둥절했으니, 한쪽은 그가 그런 일을 할 수 있다는 데에, 다른 쪽은 그가 이런 상황에서 그렇게 하려 한다는 데에 그러했다.\n\n\"분명 그는 저들이 자기를 결박하도록 내버려 두지는 않으리라!\"\n\n벤허는 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"네 칼을 칼집에 도로 꽂아라. 아버지께서 내게 주신 잔을 내가 마시지 않겠느냐?\" 나사렛 사람은 잘못을 저지른 추종자에게서 자신을 사로잡으러 온 자들에게로 몸을 돌렸다. \"너희가 강도를 잡으려는 것처럼 칼과 막대를 들고 나를 잡으러 나왔느냐? 내가 날마다 성전에서 너희와 함께 있었으되 너희가 나를 잡지 아니하였도다. 그러나 이는 너희 때요, 어둠의 권세로다.\"\n\n무리는 용기를 내어 그를 에워쌌다. 그리고 벤허가 충직한 이들을 찾았을 때, 그들은 사라지고 없었다—한 사람도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n버림받은 사람을 둘러싼 군중은 혀와 손과 발을 몹시 부산스레 놀리는 듯했다. 그들의 머리 너머로, 횃불 막대 사이로, 연기 사이로, 때로는 들썩이는 사람들 사이의 틈으로, 벤허는 죄수의 모습을 순간순간 흘긋 보았다. 그토록 가엾고, 그토록 벗 없고, 그토록 버림받은 것은 일찍이 그의 마음을 친 적이 없었다! 그러나 그는 생각했다. 저 사람은 자신을 지킬 수 있었으리라—숨 한 번으로 적들을 죽일 수 있었으련만, 그러려 하지 않았다. 그의 아버지가 그에게 마시라고 준 잔이란 무엇인가? 그리고 그토록 순종해야 할 아버지란 누구인가? 신비 위에 신비라—하나가 아니라, 여럿이었다.\n\n이내 무리는 병사들을 앞세우고 도성으로 돌아가기 시작했다. 벤허는 마음이 불안해졌다. 그는 자신이 만족스럽지 않았다. 무리 한가운데 횃불이 있는 곳에 나사렛 사람이 있으리라는 것을 그는 알았다. 문득 그는 그를 다시 보기로 결심했다. 그에게 한 가지를 물어보리라.\n\n그는 긴 겉옷과 머리에 쓴 두건을 벗어 과수원 담장 위에 던져 놓고, 무리를 뒤쫓아 대담하게 그 속에 끼어들었다. 뒤처진 자들 사이를 헤치고, 조금씩조금씩 나아가 마침내 죄수를 묶은 밧줄 끝을 쥔 사람에게 이르렀다.\n\n나사렛 사람은 머리를 떨군 채 두 손을 등 뒤로 묶이고 천천히 걷고 있었다. 머리카락이 그의 얼굴 위로 수북이 흘러내렸고, 그는 여느 때보다 더 구부정했다. 그는 주위에서 벌어지는 일을 모두 잊은 듯 보였다. 몇 걸음 앞에서는 제사장들과 장로들이 이야기를 나누며 이따금 뒤를 돌아보았다. 마침내 그들이 모두 골짜기의 다리 가까이에 이르렀을 때, 벤허는 밧줄을 쥔 하인에게서 그것을 받아 들고 그를 지나쳐 갔다.\n\n\"선생님, 선생님!\" 그는 나사렛 사람의 귀에 바싹 대고 황급히 말했다. \"제 말이 들리십니까, 선생님? 한마디만—한마디만 하여 주십시오. 제게 말씀해 주십시오—\"\n\n밧줄을 그에게서 넘겨받았던 그 사내가 이제 그것을 도로 내놓으라 했다.\n\n\"말씀해 주십시오.\" 벤허는 계속했다. \"스스로 원하셔서 이들과 함께 가시는 것입니까?\"\n\n이제 사람들이 다가들어, 그의 귓전에다 성난 목소리로 물었다. \"이자야, 너는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"오 선생님,\" 벤허는 불안에 날카로워진 목소리로 다급히 말했다. \"저는 당신의 벗이요 당신을 사랑하는 자입니다. 비오니 말씀해 주십시오, 제가 구원을 가져온다면, 받아들이시겠습니까?\"\n\n나사렛 사람은 고개를 들어 보기는커녕 알아본다는 기색을 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그러나 우리가 고통받을 때, 비록 낯선 이라 할지라도 우리를 바라보는 자에게 늘 그것을 일러 주는 그 무언가가, 이번에도 어김없이 전해졌다. \"그를 내버려 두라.\" 그것은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"그는 벗들에게 버림받았다. 세상은 그를 부인했다. 마음의 쓰라림 속에서 그는 사람들에게 작별을 고했다. 그는 자기가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 가고 있으며, 마음 쓰지도 않는다. 그를 내버려 두라.\"\n\n그리고 이제 벤허는 그리로 떠밀렸다. 열 개도 넘는 손이 그를 붙들었고, 사방에서 외치는 소리가 들렸다. \"이자도 그 패거리다. 끌고 오라. 몽둥이로 쳐라—죽여 버려라!\"\n\n벤허는 평소의 몇 배에 달하는 힘을 솟구치게 하는 격정으로 몸을 일으켜, 두 팔을 활짝 뻗고 한 번 빙 돌며 붙든 손들을 떨쳐 냈다. 그러고는 빠르게 자기를 에워싸 오는 원을 뚫고 내달렸다. 그가 지나갈 때 그를 움켜쥐려던 손들이 등에서 옷을 찢어 냈으므로, 그는 발가벗은 채 길에서 달아났다. 그리고 다른 어느 곳보다 더 어두운, 정다운 어둠이 깃든 골짜기가 그를 무사히 받아들였다.\n\n그는 과수원 담장에서 두건과 겉옷을 되찾아 입고, 도성 문까지 되짚어 갔다. 거기서 객사로 가, 좋은 말을 타고 왕들의 무덤 곁에 있는 제 식구들의 천막으로 달려갔다.\n\n말을 달리며 그는 이튿날 나사렛 사람을 보리라고 스스로에게 다짐했다—다짐하면서도, 그 벗 없는 사람이 그날 밤 재판을 받으러 곧장 한나스의 집으로 끌려갔다는 것은 알지 못했다.\n\n침상에 누운 젊은이의 가슴은 어찌나 무겁게 뛰는지 잠을 이룰 수 없었다. 이제 그가 되살리려던 유대 왕국이 본래 무엇이었는지 분명해졌기 때문이다—한낱 꿈에 지나지 않았다. 우리의 성채가 충격에서 회복할, 적어도 무너지는 메아리가 잦아들 만한 사이를 두고 하나씩 차례로 쓰러지는 것을 보는 일도 충분히 괴롭다. 그러나 그것들이 한꺼번에 무너질 때—배가 가라앉듯, 집이 지진에 무너지듯 무너질 때—그것을 차분히 견디는 영혼은 여느 사람보다 더 굳센 재료로 빚어진 법이거니와, 벤허의 영혼은 그런 부류가 아니었다. 미래로 트인 길목들 사이로, 그는 고요히 아름다운 삶을 어렴풋이 보기 시작했으니, 국정의 궁전 대신 가정이 있고, 에스더가 그 안주인인 삶이었다. 납덩이처럼 더디 흐르는 밤의 시간을 지나는 동안, 그는 거듭거듭 미세눔 곁의 별장을 보았고, 어린 동향 여인과 함께 정원을 거닐었으며, 널을 댄 안뜰에서 쉬었다. 머리 위에는 나폴리의 하늘이, 발치에는 더없이 볕 좋은 땅과 더없이 푸른 만이 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n가장 쉬운 말로 하자면, 그는 어떤 위기로 접어들고 있었으니, 그 위기와는 이튿날과 나사렛 사람이 모든 것을 좌우하게 될 터였다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n아침나절 두 시간쯤 되었을 때, 두 사내가 전속력으로 말을 달려 벤허의 천막 앞에 이르렀다. 그들은 말에서 내려 그를 만나게 해 달라고 청했다. 그는 아직 일어나지 않았으나, 들이라고 일렀다.\n\n\"평안하시오, 형제들.\" 그가 말했다. 그들은 그의 갈릴리 사람들로, 믿음직한 부하들이었다. \"앉으시겠소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 연장자가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다. \"이렇게 편히 앉아 있다가는 나사렛 사람이 죽고 맙니다. 일어나십시오, 유다의 아들이여, 우리와 함께 가십시오. 판결이 내려졌습니다. 십자가의 나무는 벌써 골고다에 세워졌습니다.\"\n\n벤허가 그들을 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"십자가라니!\" 그 순간 그가 할 수 있는 말은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n\"어젯밤 그를 잡아다 재판에 부쳤습니다.\" 사내가 말을 이었다. \"날이 밝자 그들은 그를 빌라도 앞으로 끌고 갔습니다. 그 로마인은 두 번이나 그의 죄를 부인했고, 두 번이나 그를 넘겨주기를 거부했습니다. 마침내 그는 손을 씻으며 말했습니다. '그렇다면 너희가 책임지라.' 그러자 그들이 대답했습니다—\"\n\n\"누가 대답했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그들이—제사장들과 백성이—'그 피를 우리와 우리 자손에게 돌리라'고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"거룩하신 조상 아브라함이여!\" 벤허가 부르짖었다. \"로마인이 자기네 동족보다 이스라엘 사람에게 더 자비롭다니! 그리고 만일—아, 만일 그가 정말 하나님의 아들이라면, 무엇이 그 피를 그들의 자손에게서 씻어 줄 수 있겠는가? 이래서는 안 된다—이제 싸울 때다!\"\n\n그의 얼굴이 결의로 환해졌고, 그는 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n\"말을—빨리!\" 신호에 응해 나온 아랍인에게 그가 말했다. \"그리고 암라에게 깨끗한 옷을 보내고 내 검을 가져오라고 일러라! 이스라엘을 위해 죽을 때가 왔소, 벗들이여. 내가 나올 때까지 밖에서 기다리시오.\"\n\n그는 빵 한 조각을 먹고 포도주 한 잔을 마신 뒤, 곧 길에 나섰다.\n\n\"어디로 먼저 가시렵니까?\" 갈릴리 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"군단을 모아야지.\"\n\n\"아아!\" 사내가 두 손을 치켜들며 대답했다.\n\n\"어찌 아아라 하는가?\"\n\n\"주인님\"—사내가 부끄러운 듯 말했다—\"주인님, 저와 여기 이 벗만이 충실한 자들의 전부입니다. 나머지는 제사장들을 따르고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇을 노리고?\" 벤허가 고삐를 당겼다.\n\n\"그를 죽이려는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나사렛 사람을 죽인단 말이 아니겠지?\" \"네 입으로 말했다.\"\n\n\"바로 그를 말합니다.\"\n벤허는 두 사내를 천천히 번갈아 바라보았다. 그는 어젯밤의 그 물음을 다시 듣고 있었다. \"아버지께서 내게 주신 잔을 내가 마시지 않겠느냐?\" 그는 나사렛 사람의 귀에 자신의 물음을 던지고 있었다. \"내가 당신을 구해 드린다면, 당신은 그것을 받아들이시겠습니까?\" 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"이 죽음은 막을 수 없을지도 모른다. 이 사람은 사명을 시작한 날부터 모든 것을 알면서도 그 죽음을 향해 걸어왔다. 그것은 그 자신보다 높은 뜻에 의해 정해진 것이니, 주님의 뜻이 아니고 누구의 뜻이겠는가! 그가 동의한다면, 그가 스스로 그리로 나아간다면, 다른 누가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?\" 그러면서도 벤허는 갈릴리 사람들의 충성 위에 세웠던 계획이 무너지는 것을 똑똑히 보았다. 사실 그들의 이탈로 그 계획에는 아무것도 남지 않았다. 그러나 하필이면 다른 날도 아닌 바로 그날 아침에 이런 일이 일어나다니, 이 얼마나 기이한가! 두려움이 그를 사로잡았다. 그의 책략과 노고와 재물의 소모가 어쩌면 하나님을 거스르는 불경한 다툼에 지나지 않았을지도 몰랐다. 그가 고삐를 집어 들고 \"갑시다, 형제들\" 하고 말했을 때, 그의 앞에 놓인 모든 것은 불확실했다. 들끓는 장면 한복판에서 영웅이 되려면 없어서는 안 될, 빠르게 결단하는 능력이 그의 안에서 마비되어 있었다.\n\n\"갑시다, 형제들. 골고다로 갑시다.\"\n\n그들은 자신들처럼 남쪽으로 가는, 들뜬 인파를 헤치고 나아갔다. 도성 북쪽의 온 고장이 깨어나 움직이는 듯했다.\n\n사형수를 끌고 가는 행렬을 헤롯이 남긴 거대한 흰 망루 근처 어디쯤에서 마주칠 수 있으리라는 말을 듣고, 세 벗은 아크라의 남동쪽을 돌아 그리로 말을 달렸다. 히스기야 못 아래 골짜기에서는 군중을 거슬러 나아가기가 불가능해졌으므로, 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 말에서 내려 어느 집 모퉁이 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 기다려야 했다.\n\n기다림은 마치 강둑에 서서 홍수가 지나가는 것을 지켜보는 것과도 같았으니, 사람들이 꼭 그렇게 보였던 것이다.\n\n이 이야기의 제1부에는 그리스도 시대의 유대 민족 구성에 관해 독자에게 어떤 그림을 보여 주려고 쓴 몇몇 장이 있다. 그 장들은 또한 바로 이 시각과 이 장면을 내다보며 쓴 것이기도 하다. 그래서 그것을 주의 깊게 읽은 사람이라면 이제 벤허가 본 십자가형으로 가는 행렬을 모두 볼 수 있을 것이니—그것은 보기 드물고 놀라운 광경이었다!\n\n반 시간—한 시간—홍수는 벤허와 그 일행 곁을, 팔이 닿을 만한 거리에서, 끊임없이, 줄어들지 않고 밀려갔다. 그 시간이 다할 무렵 그는 이렇게 말할 수 있었을 것이다. \"나는 예루살렘의 모든 계급을, 유대의 모든 분파를, 이스라엘의 모든 지파를, 그리고 그들이 대표하는 땅 위의 모든 민족을 보았다.\" 리비아의 유대인이 지나갔고, 이집트의 유대인이, 라인강 가의 유대인이 지나갔다. 한마디로 모든 동방 나라와 모든 서방 나라, 그리고 교역으로 이어진 모든 섬에서 온 유대인들이었다. 그들은 걸어서, 말을 타고, 낙타를 타고, 가마와 마차에 실려, 헤아릴 수 없이 다양한 의복을 걸치고 지나갔으나, 풍토와 생활 방식으로 그토록 시달려 왔으면서도 오늘날까지 이스라엘 자손을 두드러지게 하는 그 놀라운 용모의 닮음을 한결같이 지니고 있었다. 그들은 알려진 모든 언어를 말하며 지나갔으니, 오직 그 수단으로만 무리와 무리를 가려낼 수 있었던 것이다. 그들은 황급히 지나갔다—애타게, 조마조마하게, 서로 밀치며—하나같이 가련한 나사렛 사람 하나가 죄인들 틈에 끼여 죄인으로 죽는 것을 보려고.\n\n이들이 다수였으나, 전부는 아니었다.\n\n그 물결에 실려 가는 이들 가운데는 유대인이 아닌 수천 명이—그들을 미워하고 멸시하는 수천 명이—있었으니, 그리스인, 로마인, 아랍인, 시리아인, 아프리카인, 이집트인, 동방 사람들이었다. 그리하여 그 무리를 살펴보면, 마치 온 세상이 거기 대표되어, 그런 의미에서 십자가형 자리에 함께한 듯이 보였다.\n\n그 행렬은 유달리 조용했다. 바위에 부딪히는 말발굽 소리, 돌아가는 바퀴가 미끄러지고 덜그럭거리는 소리, 이야기하는 목소리, 그리고 이따금 외치는 소리, 이런 것들이 그 거대한 움직임의 술렁임 위로 들리는 소리의 전부였다. 그런데도 사람들의 얼굴마다 어떤 무서운 광경을, 갑작스러운 파선이나 폐허나 전쟁의 참화 같은 것을 황급히 보러 갈 때의 그런 표정이 어려 있었다. 그런 기색으로 미루어 벤허는 이들이 유월절을 지키러 도성에 올라온 이방 사람들이며, 나사렛 사람의 재판에는 끼지 않았으니 그의 벗이 될 수도 있는 자들이라고 판단했다.\n\n마침내 거대한 망루 쪽에서, 벤허는 처음에는 멀리서 희미하게, 수많은 사람의 외침을 들었다.\n\n\"들으십시오! 이제 오고 있습니다.\" 그의 벗 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n거리의 사람들은 그 소리를 들으려고 걸음을 멈췄다. 그러나 그 외침이 머리 위로 울려 퍼지자, 그들은 서로 마주 보고는 몸서리치는 침묵 속에 다시 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n외침은 순간순간 가까워졌다. 어느새 대기가 그 소리로 가득 차 떨릴 무렵, 벤허는 시모니데스의 종들이 가마에 주인을 모시고 오는 것을 보았으니, 에스더가 그 곁에서 걷고 있었다. 그 바로 뒤에는 휘장을 두른 가마가 따라왔다.\n\n\"평안하시오, 시모니데스여—그대도 평안하시오, 에스더.\" 그들을 맞으며 벤허가 말했다. \"골고다로 가시는 길이라면, 행렬이 지나갈 때까지 기다리십시오. 그러면 함께 가겠습니다. 여기 이 집 옆으로 들어가 돌아설 만한 자리가 있습니다.\"\n\n상인의 큰 머리가 가슴 위로 무겁게 떨구어져 있었다. 그는 몸을 추스르며 대답했다. \"발타사르에게 말해 보게. 그분의 뜻이 곧 내 뜻일세. 그분은 저 가마 안에 계시네.\"\n\n벤허는 서둘러 휘장을 젖혔다. 이집트인이 그 안에 누워 있었는데, 핼쑥한 얼굴이 어찌나 야위었는지 죽은 사람처럼 보였다. 그에게 제안이 전해졌다.\n\n\"우리가 그를 볼 수 있겠소?\" 그가 가냘프게 물었다.\n\n\"나사렛 사람 말씀입니까? 그렇습니다. 그는 우리에게서 몇 걸음 안 되는 곳을 지나갈 것입니다.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 주님이시여!\" 노인이 간절하게 부르짖었다. \"다시 한 번, 다시 한 번! 오, 세상에 무서운 날이로다!\"\n\n이내 온 일행은 그 집 그늘 아래에서 기다리게 되었다. 그들은 말을 거의 하지 않았으니, 아마도 서로에게 제 생각을 털어놓기가 두려웠으리라. 모든 것이 불확실했고, 그 무엇보다도 견해가 그러했다. 발타사르는 가마에서 힘없이 몸을 일으켜, 종의 부축을 받으며 섰다. 에스더와 벤허는 시모니데스 곁을 지켰다.\n\n그러는 동안에도 홍수는 도리어 전보다 더 빽빽하게 밀려갔다. 외침은 더 가까워져, 공중에서는 날카롭게, 땅을 따라서는 거칠게, 잔인하게 들려왔다. 마침내 행렬이 다가왔다.\n\n\"보십시오!\" 벤허가 쓰라리게 말했다. \"이제 오는 저것이 예루살렘입니다.\"\n\n행렬의 선두는 한 무리의 소년들이 차지하고 있었으니, 그들은 \"유대인의 왕이다! 길을 비켜라, 유대인의 왕에게 길을 비켜라!\" 하고 야유하며 소리쳐 댔다.\n\n시모니데스는 여름 벌레 떼처럼 빙빙 돌며 춤추듯 지나가는 그들을 지켜보다가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"허의 아들이여, 저 아이들이 제 유산을 물려받을 때면, 아아, 솔로몬의 도성이 가엾어질 걸세!\"\n\n다음으로 완전 무장한 한 떼의 군단병들이 따라왔으니, 그들은 우직한 무관심 속에 행진했고, 그동안 광나는 놋쇠의 광채가 그들을 두르고 있었다.\n\n그러고 나서 나사렛 사람이 왔다!\n\n그는 거의 죽어 가고 있었다. 몇 걸음마다 그는 쓰러질 듯 비틀거렸다. 심하게 찢긴 얼룩진 겉옷이 솔기 없는 속옷 위로 어깨에서 흘러내려 있었다. 그의 맨발은 돌마다 붉은 자국을 남겼다. 죄패를 적은 판자 하나가 그의 목에 매달려 있었다. 가시관이 그의 머리에 모질게 눌려 박혀 잔혹한 상처를 냈고, 거기서 흘러내린 피가 이제는 말라 검게 엉긴 채 그의 얼굴과 목을 덮고 있었다. 가시에 엉킨 긴 머리카락은 두텁게 들러붙어 있었다. 살갗은 보이는 곳마다 소름 끼치도록 희었다. 그의 두 손은 앞으로 묶여 있었다. 도성 어딘가 뒤편에서 그는 십자가의 가로 막대 아래 지쳐 쓰러졌으니, 사형수로서 관습에 따라 그것을 형장까지 메고 가야 했던 것이다. 이제는 한 시골 사람이 그를 대신해 그 짐을 지고 있었다. 병사 넷이 그를 따르며 군중을 막았으나, 그래도 군중은 이따금 뚫고 들어와 막대기로 그를 치고 침을 뱉었다. 그런데도 그에게서는 아무 소리도 새어 나오지 않았으니, 항변도 신음도 없었다. 그는 벤허와 그 벗들이 몸을 숨긴 집 거의 앞에 이르기까지 눈을 들지 않았는데, 그들은 모두 순식간에 연민에 사로잡혔다. 에스더는 아버지에게 매달렸고, 의지가 굳센 그도 떨었다. 발타사르는 말없이 주저앉았다. 벤허마저 \"오 나의 하나님! 나의 하나님!\" 하고 부르짖었다. 그러자 마치 그들의 심정을 알아챘거나 그 외침을 들은 듯이, 나사렛 사람은 핼쑥한 얼굴을 그 일행 쪽으로 돌려 그들을 하나하나 바라보았으니, 그들은 평생토록 그 눈길을 기억 속에 간직했다. 그가 자신이 아니라 그들을 생각하고 있음을 그들은 볼 수 있었고, 죽어 가는 그 눈은 그가 입으로 말할 수 없는 축복을 그들에게 내려 주었다.\n\"그대의 군단은 어디 있는가, 허의 아들이여?\" 시모니데스가 정신을 차리고 물었다.\n\n\"한나스가 저보다 더 잘 일러 드릴 것입니다.\"\n\n\"무엇이라, 배신하였다는 말인가?\"\n\n\"이 두 사람을 빼고는 모두.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 모든 것이 끝났구나. 그리고 이 선한 사람은 죽어야 하는구나!\"\n\n상인은 말하면서 얼굴을 발작하듯 일그러뜨렸고, 그의 머리는 가슴 위로 떨구어졌다. 그는 벤허의 노고에 맡은 몫을 잘 감당해 왔고, 같은 희망에 고무되어 있었으나, 그 희망은 이제 다시는 되살아날 수 없도록 꺼져 버렸다.\n\n다른 두 사람이 가로 막대를 메고 나사렛 사람의 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"이들은 누구요?\" 벤허가 갈릴리 사람들에게 물었다.\n\n\"나사렛 사람과 함께 죽도록 정해진 도둑들입니다.\" 그들이 대답했다.\n\n행렬에서 그다음으로는 대제사장의 금빛 예복을 온전히 갖춰 입고 관을 쓴 인물이 위풍당당하게 걸어왔다. 성전의 경비병들이 그를 둘러싸 가렸고, 그 뒤로는 산헤드린이 차례로 성큼성큼 걸었으며, 길게 늘어선 제사장들이 따랐으니, 이들은 수수한 흰옷 위에 주름이 많고 화려한 빛깔의 띠를 두르고 있었다.\n\n\"한나스의 사위입니다.\" 벤허가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"가야바로구나! 나는 그를 본 적이 있네.\" 시모니데스가 대답하고는, 거만한 대제사장을 사색에 잠겨 지켜보더니 잠시 뒤 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"이제 나는 확신하네. 영혼의 맑은 깨우침에서 비롯된 그런 확신으로—절대적인 확신으로—이제 나는 아노라, 저 앞에서 목에 죄패를 걸고 먼저 가는 이가 바로 그 죄패가 선포하는 그자임을—유대인의 왕임을. 한낱 평범한 사람이, 사기꾼이, 죄인이 이렇게 떠받들리는 일은 결코 없었네. 보게나! 여기 만방이—예루살렘이, 이스라엘이 있네. 여기 에봇이, 여기 단을 두르고 자줏빛 석류와 금방울을 단 청색 겉옷이 있으니, 얏두아가 마케도니아 사람을 맞으러 나간 그날 이후로 거리에서 본 적 없는 것일세—이 모든 것이 이 나사렛 사람이 왕임을 증명하는 것이라네. 일어나 그를 따라갈 수만 있다면!\"\n\n벤허는 놀라며 귀를 기울였다. 그러자 곧 시모니데스는 마치 제 유별난 감정의 발로를 스스로 깨달은 듯, 조바심을 내며 말했다.\n\n\"부디 발타사르에게 말씀하시게. 어서 떠나세. 예루살렘의 게운 찌꺼기가 오고 있네.\"\n\n그때 에스더가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"저기 여인들이 보이는데, 울고 있어요. 누구인가요?\"\n\n그녀가 가리키는 손길을 따라가니, 일행은 눈물을 흘리는 네 여인을 보았다. 그 가운데 하나는 나사렛 사람과 닮은 데가 있는 어떤 남자의 팔에 기대어 있었다. 이내 벤허가 대답했다.\n\n\"저 남자는 나사렛 사람이 누구보다 가장 사랑하는 제자입니다. 그의 팔에 기댄 여인은 마리아로, 그분의 어머니입니다. 나머지는 갈릴리의 다정한 여인들입니다.\"\n\n에스더는 빛나는 눈으로 그 슬퍼하는 이들을 좇았으나, 군중이 이내 그들을 시야에서 거두어 가 버렸다.\n\n독자는 앞서 띄엄띄엄 오간 대화들이 조용한 가운데 이루어졌으리라 여길지 모르나, 그렇지 않았다. 그 이야기는 대개 사람들이 파도 소리 아래 바닷가에서 주고받는 것과 같았으니, 이 무리의 외침은 다른 무엇에도 그토록 잘 견줄 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n그 소동은 그로부터 채 삼십 년도 지나지 않아 당파들의 지배 아래 거룩한 도성이 갈가리 찢기게 될 때의 그것들을 미리 보여 주는 것이었다. 그것은 머릿수도 그에 못지않았고, 그만큼이나 광신적이고 피에 굶주려 있었으며, 끓어오르고 미쳐 날뛰었고, 그 안에 똑같은 무리가 들어 있었다—종들, 낙타몰이꾼, 장사치, 문지기, 정원사, 과일과 포도주 장수, 개종자들, 개종하지 않은 이방인들, 성전의 파수꾼과 하인들, 도둑들, 강도들, 그리고 어느 부류로도 헤아릴 수 없으나 이런 때면 어디서 나타났는지 아무도 말할 수 없게 모습을 드러내는, 동굴과 오래된 무덤 냄새를 풍기며 굶주린 무수한 자들이었다—맨머리에 팔다리를 드러내고, 머리와 수염은 빗질하지 않아 엉겨 붙고, 저마다 진흙 빛깔의 옷 한 벌만 걸친 가련한 자들이었으니, 사막 너머로 서로를 부르는 사자들처럼 그 외침이 우렁찬, 입이 아득히 벌어진 짐승들이었다. 그들 가운데 더러는 검을 지녔고, 더 많은 자가 창과 투창을 휘둘렀으나, 대다수의 무기는 막대기와 옹이진 몽둥이와 무릿매였으니, 무릿매에 쓸 골라낸 돌은 전대에, 더러는 더러운 속옷 자락으로 즉석에서 만든 자루에 담아 두었다. 그 무리 가운데 여기저기 높은 신분의 인물들이 보였으니—서기관, 장로, 랍비, 술을 넓게 단 바리새인, 고운 망토를 걸친 사두개인이었다—이들은 한동안 부추기는 자요 이끄는 자 노릇을 하고 있었다. 한 목구멍이 한 외침에 지치면, 그들은 그를 위해 새 외침을 지어냈다. 놋쇠 같던 허파가 주저앉을 기색을 보이면, 그들은 그것을 다시 돌아가게 했다. 그러면서도 그토록 크고 끊임없던 그 소동은 몇 마디 음절로 간추릴 수 있었으니—유대인의 왕! 유대인의 왕에게 길을 비켜라!—성전을 더럽힌 자!—하나님을 모독한 자!—그를 십자가에 못 박아라, 십자가에 못 박아라! 이런 외침들 가운데 마지막 것이 가장 인기를 끄는 듯했으니, 의심할 바 없이 그것이 군중의 바람을 가장 직접적으로 드러냈고, 나사렛 사람을 향한 증오를 더 또렷이 표현하는 데 보탬이 되었기 때문이다.\n\n\"갑시다.\" 발타사르가 떠날 채비를 마치자 시모니데스가 말했다. \"갑시다, 어서 나아갑시다.\"\n\n벤허는 그 부름을 듣지 못했다. 그때 지나가던 행렬의 그 부분이 보여 주는 모습이, 그 잔인함과 목숨을 향한 굶주림이, 그에게 나사렛 사람을 떠올리게 했던 것이다—그의 온유함과, 고통받는 사람들에게 베푸는 것을 그가 숱하게 보았던 그 많은 자비를. 떠오름은 또 다른 떠오름을 낳았다. 그리하여 그는 문득 그 사람에게 진 자신의 크나큰 빚을 기억해 냈다. 그 자신이 로마 호위병의 손에 붙들려, 짐작하건대 이 십자가의 죽음만큼이나 확실하고 거의 그만큼이나 끔찍한 죽음을 향해 끌려가던 때를, 나사렛 곁의 우물가에서 마셨던 시원한 물을, 그리고 그것을 건네준 이의 얼굴에 어린 거룩한 표정을, 그 뒤의 선함을, 종려주일의 기적을. 이런 기억과 더불어, 도움에 도움으로 갚거나 같은 식으로 보답할 힘이 지금 자신에게 없다는 생각이 그를 매섭게 찔렀고, 그는 스스로를 책망했다. 그는 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다하지 않았다. 갈릴리 사람들과 함께 망을 보며 그들을 충실하고 채비된 채로 지킬 수 있었을 텐데. 그리고 이때야말로—아! 이때야말로 칠 순간이었다! 지금 제대로 한 방을 먹인다면 군중을 흩뜨리고 나사렛 사람을 풀어 주는 데 그치지 않을 것이었다. 그것은 이스라엘을 향한 나팔 소리가 되어, 오래도록 꿈꿔 온 자유의 전쟁을 앞당길 것이었다. 기회가 가고 있었다. 시간이 그것을 실어 가고 있었다. 그러니 만일 놓친다면! 아브라함의 하나님이여! 정녕 할 수 있는 일이 아무것도—아무것도 없단 말인가?\n\n바로 그 순간 한 무리의 갈릴리 사람들이 그의 눈에 띄었다. 그는 인파를 뚫고 달려가 그들을 따라잡았다.\n\n\"나를 따라오시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그대들과 할 말이 있소.\"\n\n사람들이 그를 따랐고, 그들이 집 그늘 아래 이르자 그는 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그대들은 내 검을 받고, 자유를 위해, 그리고 오시리라던 왕을 위해 떨쳐 일어서기로 나와 약조한 이들이오. 그대들에게 지금 그 검이 있고, 지금이 바로 그것으로 칠 때요. 가서, 사방을 살펴 우리 형제들을 찾고, 나사렛 사람을 위해 채비하고 있는 십자가 나무 곁에서 나를 만나자고 이르시오. 모두 서두르시오! 아니, 그렇게 서 있지 마시오! 나사렛 사람이 왕이오. 그리고 그가 죽으면 자유도 죽는 것이오.\"\n\n그들은 그를 정중하게 바라보았으나, 움직이지 않았다.\n\n\"내 말이 들리지 않소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n그러자 그들 가운데 하나가 대답했다.\n\n\"유다의 아들이여\"—그들은 그 이름으로 그를 알고 있었다—\"유다의 아들이여, 속고 있는 것은 바로 당신이지, 당신의 검을 지닌 우리나 우리 형제들이 아닙니다. 나사렛 사람은 왕이 아닙니다. 그에게는 왕의 기개도 없습니다. 우리는 그가 예루살렘에 들어올 때 함께 있었고, 성전에서 그를 보았습니다. 그는 자신을, 우리를, 이스라엘을 저버렸습니다. 미문에서 그는 하나님께 등을 돌리고 다윗의 왕좌를 마다했습니다. 그는 왕이 아니며, 갈릴리도 그와 함께하지 않습니다. 그는 죽음을 맞을 것입니다. 그러나 들으십시오, 유다의 아들이여. 우리에게는 당신의 검이 있고, 우리는 지금이라도 그것을 뽑아 자유를 위해 칠 채비가 되어 있습니다. 갈릴리도 그러합니다. 자유를 위해서라면, 오 유다의 아들이여, 자유를 위해서라면! 우리는 십자가 나무 곁에서 당신을 만나겠습니다.\"\n\n벤허의 생애에서 가장 결정적인 순간이 그에게 닥쳐 있었다. 그가 그 제안을 받아들여 한마디만 했더라면, 역사는 지금과 달라졌을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇게 되었다면 그것은 하나님이 아니라 사람이 정한 역사였을 것이니—결코 있었던 적 없고, 앞으로도 결코 없을 것이었다. 혼란이 그를 덮쳤다. 그 까닭을 그는 알지 못했으나, 훗날 그는 그것을 나사렛 사람의 탓으로 돌렸다. 나사렛 사람이 부활했을 때, 그는 그 죽음이 부활에 대한 믿음을 위해 꼭 필요했음을, 그 믿음이 없다면 기독교란 속이 빈 껍데기에 지나지 않으리라는 것을 깨달았기 때문이다. 앞서 말했듯, 그 혼란은 그에게서 결단의 능력을 앗아 갔다. 그는 무력하게—말조차 잃은 채 서 있었다. 그는 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 자신이 명하고자 했던 바람과, 자신을 짓누르는 힘 사이의 다툼으로 떨었다.\n\n\"갑시다, 우리가 기다리고 있소.\" 시모니데스가 네 번째로 말했다.\n\n그러자 그는 기계처럼 가마와 들것을 뒤따라 걸었다. 에스더가 그와 함께 걸었다. 발타사르와 그의 벗들인 동방 박사들이 사막의 만남으로 가던 그날처럼, 그 역시 그 길로 이끌려 가고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\n일행이—발타사르, 시모니데스, 벤허, 에스더, 그리고 두 충직한 갈릴리 사람이—처형장에 다다랐을 때, 벤허가 앞장서서 그들을 이끌고 있었다. 흥분한 군중의 엄청난 인파를 어떻게 헤치고 나아갈 수 있었는지, 그는 끝내 알지 못했다. 어느 길로 왔는지도, 오는 데 얼마나 걸렸는지도 알지 못했다. 그는 아무것도 의식하지 못한 채 걸었으니, 누구의 말도 듣지 못했고, 무엇도 보지 못했으며, 어디로 가는지 생각조차 없었고, 마음속에 어떤 목적의 희미한 그림자조차 떠올리지 못했다. 이런 상태에서라면, 그가 곧 목격하게 될 끔찍한 범죄를 막는 일에는 어린아이라도 그만큼은 할 수 있었으리라. 하느님의 뜻은 언제나 우리에게 낯설다. 그러나 그 뜻이 이루어지는 방편, 그리하여 마침내 우리의 믿음 앞에 분명히 드러나는 그 방편 또한 그에 못지않게 낯설다.\n\n벤허는 걸음을 멈추었다. 그를 뒤따르던 이들도 멈추어 섰다. 관객 앞에서 막이 오르듯, 그를 자다 깬 듯한 상태에 붙들어 두던 주문이 걷히고, 그는 또렷한 깨달음으로 바라보았다.\n\n해골처럼 둥글게 솟은 나지막한 언덕 꼭대기에 빈터가 있었다. 메마르고 먼지투성이에 풀 한 포기 없었으니, 다만 우슬초 덤불 몇 무더기가 있을 뿐이었다. 그 빈터의 경계는 사람들로 이루어진 살아 있는 벽이었고, 그 뒤로 또 사람들이 안간힘을 쓰고 있었으니, 더러는 넘겨다보려고, 더러는 그 틈으로 들여다보려고 애썼다. 안쪽으로는 로마 군병들의 벽이 그 빽빽한 바깥 벽을 제자리에 단단히 붙들어 두었다. 백부장 하나가 군병들을 지켜보았다. 벤허는 그토록 삼엄하게 지켜지는 경계선 바로 앞까지 인도되어, 이제 그 선에 서서 북서쪽을 향하고 있었다. 그 언덕은 옛 아람말로 골고다였으니—라틴말로는 칼바리아, 영어로는 캘버리, 풀이하면 해골이다.\n\n그 비탈에서, 낮은 자리에서, 융기한 곳과 더 높은 언덕들에서, 대지는 기이한 칠보처럼 반짝였다. 벽으로 둘러쳐진 빈터 바깥의 어디를 바라보아도, 그는 갈색 흙 한 조각도, 바위 하나도, 푸른 것 하나도 보지 못했다. 그가 본 것은 오직 불그레한 얼굴들 속의 수천 개의 눈동자뿐이었고, 조금 떨어진 곳에서는 눈동자 없는 불그레한 얼굴들뿐이었으며, 거기서 조금 더 떨어진 곳에서는 넓고도 넓은 둥근 띠뿐이었으니, 가까이서 보면 그것 또한 얼굴들임을 알 수 있었다. 그리고 이것이 곧 삼백만 사람이 한데 모인 광경이었다. 그 아래에서는 삼백만 개의 심장이 언덕 위에서 벌어지는 일에 격정 어린 관심으로 두근거리고 있었다. 강도들에게는 무심한 채, 오직 그 나사렛 사람만을 마음에 두었으니, 그것도 오로지 그가 미움이나 두려움이나 호기심의 대상인 까닭이었다—그들 모두를 사랑하고, 그들을 위해 곧 죽으려 하는 바로 그 사람을.\n\n큰 무리의 사람들이 모인 광경에는, 일렁이는 바다의 펼쳐진 면을 바라볼 때 느끼는 그 어리둥절함과 매혹이 언제나 깃들어 있는 법이며, 이번만큼 그것을 능가한 적은 없었다. 그러나 벤허는 그것에 스쳐 가는 눈길 한 번만을 던졌을 뿐이니, 앞서 말한 빈터에서 벌어지고 있는 일이 그의 관심을 다른 데로 나누는 것을 허락하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n언덕 위, 살아 있는 벽보다 높이 솟아 둘러선 명사들의 무리 머리 위로 보이는 곳, 주교관과 제의(祭衣)와 거만한 태도로 단연 눈에 띄는 자리에 대제사장이 서 있었다. 언덕을 더 높이 올라가, 둥근 정상 바로 그곳에 이르러, 멀리서도 가까이서도 보이는 자리에 그 나사렛 사람이 있었으니, 구부정하니 고통받으면서도 잠잠했다. 경비병 가운데 한 익살꾼이 그의 머리에 씌운 가시관을 보태어, 그의 손에 갈대 하나를 홀(笏) 삼아 쥐여 주었다. 외침이 돌풍처럼 그에게 몰아쳤다—웃음소리—저주—때로는 둘이 한데 뒤엉켜 분간할 수 없었다. 한 사람이—한낱 사람이라면, 오 독자여, 그 돌풍에 인류를 향한 사랑의 남은 몫까지 모조리 실어, 영영 흘려보냈으리라.\n\n그때 바라보던 모든 눈은 그 나사렛 사람에게 못 박혀 있었다. 그를 움직인 것은 어쩌면 연민이었는지도 모른다. 그 까닭이 무엇이든, 벤허는 제 마음속에 일어나는 변화를 의식했다. 이 삶의 가장 좋은 것보다 더 나은 무엇—연약한 사람에게 육신의 고통뿐 아니라 영혼의 고통까지 견딜 힘을 줄 만큼 그토록 더 나은 무엇, 죽음을 기꺼이 맞이하게 만드는 무엇—어쩌면 이 삶보다 더 순결한 또 다른 삶, 어쩌면 발타사르가 그토록 굳게 붙들었던 영혼의 삶에 대한 깨달음이 그의 마음속에 점점 또렷이 밝아 오기 시작하더니, 결국 그 나사렛 사람의 사명이 자기를 사랑하는 이들을 경계 너머로 인도하는 것이었다는 어떤 확신을 그에게 안겨 주었다—그의 나라가 세워져 그를 기다리고 있는 그 경계 너머로 말이다. 그러자 거의 잊혔던 것 가운데서 공기를 타고 실려 오듯, 그는 그 나사렛 사람의 말을 다시 들었으니, 적어도 들은 것만 같았다.\n\n\"나는 부활이요 생명이니.\"\n그 말이 거듭거듭 되풀이되어 형태를 갖추고, 새벽빛이 그것을 어루만져 새로운 뜻으로 채웠다. 그리하여 사람들이 뜻을 붙들어 새기려고 물음을 되묻듯, 그는 가시관 아래 기진해 가는 언덕 위의 형상을 바라보며 물었다. 부활은 누구인가? 그리고 생명은 누구인가?\n\n\"나니라.\"\n그 형상이 그렇게 말하는 듯했다—그를 위해 말하는 듯했다. 그 순간 그는 일찍이 알지 못했던 평안을 느꼈으니—의심과 신비의 끝이며 믿음과 사랑과 또렷한 깨달음의 시작인 그 평안이었다.\n\n이 꿈결 같은 상태에서 벤허는 망치질 소리에 깨어났다. 그때 그는 언덕 정상에서 미처 보지 못했던 것을 알아차렸다—군병 몇과 일꾼들이 십자가를 마련하고 있었던 것이다. 나무를 박을 구덩이는 이미 준비되어 있었고, 이제 가로대를 제자리에 맞추는 중이었다.\n\n\"사람들에게 서두르라 일러라.\" 대제사장이 백부장에게 말했다. \"이자들은\"—그러며 그는 나사렛 사람을 가리켰다—\"해가 지기 전에 죽어야 하고, 이 땅이 더럽혀지지 않도록 묻혀야 한다. 율법이 그러하다.\"\n\n한 군병이 갸륵한 마음으로 나사렛 사람에게 다가가 마실 것을 권했으나, 그는 그 잔을 물리쳤다. 그러자 다른 군병이 다가가 그의 목에서 죄패가 적힌 판을 떼어, 그것을 십자가 나무에 못으로 박았다—그리하여 채비가 끝났다.\n\n\"십자가가 준비되었습니다.\" 백부장이 제사장에게 말하자, 제사장은 손을 한 번 내저으며 그 보고를 받고 대답했다.\n\n\"저 신성모독자부터 먼저 처리하라. 하느님의 아들이라면 제 한 몸쯤은 구할 수 있으렷다. 어디 두고 보자.\"\n\n채비가 단계마다 눈에 보이던 사람들, 그때껏 그치지 않는 조바심의 외침으로 언덕을 들볶던 사람들이 잠잠해지더니, 그 잠잠함이 곧장 온통의 침묵으로 번졌다. 적어도 생각하기에 가장 충격적인 형벌의 대목에 이른 것이었다—사람들이 십자가에 못 박히게 된 것이다. 그 일을 위해 군병들이 먼저 나사렛 사람에게 손을 댔을 때, 거대한 군중 사이로 전율이 지나갔다. 가장 모질게 무뎌진 자들마저 두려움에 움츠러들었다. 나중에 더러는 별안간 공기가 차가워져 몸이 떨렸다고 말하기도 했다.\n\n\"어쩜 이리도 고요할까요!\" 에스더가 아버지의 목에 팔을 두르며 말했다.\n\n그러자 그는 자신이 겪었던 고문을 떠올리며 딸의 얼굴을 제 가슴 위로 끌어당기고는, 떨면서 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"보지 마라, 에스더, 보지 마라!\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 서서 이 일을 보는 모든 이가—죄 있는 자뿐 아니라 죄 없는 자까지도—이 시각부터 저주받을지 누가 알겠느냐.\"\n\n발타사르는 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이여,\" 시모니데스가 점점 격해지며 말했다—\"후르의 아들이여, 여호와께서 손을 뻗으시지 않는다면, 그것도 어서 뻗으시지 않는다면, 이스라엘은 끝장이오—우리도 끝장이오.\"\n\n벤허가 차분히 대답했다. \"나는 꿈을 꾸었소, 시모니데스. 그 꿈속에서 어찌하여 이 모든 일이 일어나야 하는지, 어찌하여 이대로 진행되어야 하는지 들었소. 이것은 저 나사렛 사람의 뜻이오—하느님의 뜻이오. 여기 이 이집트 사람처럼 합시다—잠잠히 기도합시다.\"\n\n그가 다시 언덕 위를 올려다보았을 때, 그 두려운 고요를 뚫고 말씀이 그에게 실려 왔다.\n\"나는 부활이요 생명이니.\"\n\n그는 곁에서 말하는 사람을 대하듯 경건히 머리를 숙였다.\n\n그동안 정상에서는 일이 진행되었다. 경비병들이 나사렛 사람의 옷을 벗기니, 그는 수백만 사람 앞에 벌거벗은 채로 섰다. 이른 아침에 받은 채찍질 자국이 아직도 그의 등에 핏자국으로 남아 있었다. 그런데도 그는 무자비하게 눕혀져 십자가 위에 펼쳐졌다—먼저 두 팔이 가로대 위에 놓였다. 못은 날카로웠다—몇 번 두드리자, 못은 그 여린 손바닥을 꿰뚫었다. 다음으로 그들은 발바닥이 나무에 평평하게 닿도록 그의 무릎을 끌어올렸다. 그러고는 한쪽 발을 다른 발 위에 포개고, 못 하나로 두 발을 한꺼번에 단단히 박았다. 둔탁한 망치질 소리가 지켜지는 빈터 밖으로 새어 나왔고, 그 소리를 듣지 못하는 이들도 망치가 떨어지는 모습을 보고는 두려움에 떨었다. 그러면서도 그 고통받는 이에게서는 신음 한 번, 외침 한 번, 항변의 말 한마디도 없었으니, 원수가 비웃을 만한 것도 없었고, 사랑하는 이가 안타까워할 만한 것도 없었다.\n\n\"이자를 어느 쪽으로 향하게 할까요?\" 한 군병이 무뚝뚝하게 물었다.\n\n\"성전 쪽으로.\" 제사장이 대답했다. \"죽어 가면서 거룩한 집이 자기 때문에 해를 입지 않았음을 보게 하리라.\"\n\n일꾼들이 십자가에 손을 대어, 짐과 함께 통째로 박을 자리로 옮겼다. 한마디 신호에 그들은 나무를 구덩이에 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 나사렛 사람의 몸 또한 묵직하게 떨어지면서, 피 흐르는 두 손에 매달렸다. 그래도 고통의 외침은 없었다—다만 기록된 모든 탄성 가운데 가장 거룩한 탄성이 흘러나왔을 뿐이다.\n\n\"아버지, 저들을 용서하소서. 저들은 자기가 하는 일을 알지 못하나이다.\"\n\n이제 다른 모든 것 위로 높이 세워져 하늘을 배경으로 홀로 우뚝 선 십자가는 환호의 폭발로 맞이되었다. 나사렛 사람의 머리 위 판자에 적힌 글을 보고 읽을 수 있는 이들은 모두 서둘러 그것을 풀이했다. 읽자마자 그 글귀는 받아들여져 입에서 입으로 전해졌고, 곧 그 거대한 군중 전체가 이쪽저쪽에서 인사말을 울려 대며 웃음과 신음 속에 그것을 되뇌었다.\n\n\"유대인의 왕이여! 만세, 유대인의 왕이여!\"\n\n그 글귀의 의미를 더 분명히 알아챈 제사장이 그에 항의했으나, 부질없었다. 그리하여 왕이라 불린 그분은 죽어 가는 눈으로 언덕에서 바라보며, 자기를 그토록 욕되게 내쫓았던 조상들의 도성이 자기 발치 아래 잠잠히 놓여 있는 것을 틀림없이 보았으리라.\n\n해는 빠르게 정오를 향해 떠올랐다. 언덕들은 그 갈색 가슴을 정겹게 해에게 드러냈고, 더 멀리 있는 산들은 해가 그토록 왕답게 입혀 준 자줏빛에 기뻐했다. 도성에서는 성전과 궁전과 탑과 첨탑, 그리고 아름답고 두드러진 모든 지점이 그 견줄 데 없는 광채 속으로 스스로를 들어 올리는 듯했으니, 마치 이따금 그것들을 돌아보는 많은 이에게 자기가 안겨 주는 긍지를 알고 있기라도 한 듯했다. 그런데 별안간 어둑함이 하늘을 채우고 대지를 덮기 시작했다—처음에는 좀처럼 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 낮이 희미해지는 정도였으니, 때아닌 어스름, 정오의 광휘 위로 슬며시 미끄러져 드는 저녁이었다. 그러나 그것은 점점 짙어져 곧장 주의를 끌었다. 그러자 외침과 웃음의 소란이 잦아들었고, 사람들은 제 감각을 의심하며 야릇하게 서로를 바라보았다. 그러고는 다시 해를 올려다보았고, 점점 멀어져 가는 산들을 보았으며, 그늘에 잠겨 드는 하늘과 가까운 들녘을 보았고, 비극이 펼쳐지고 있는 언덕을 보았다. 그러고는 이 모든 것에서 다시 서로를 바라보고는, 얼굴이 새파래져 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"안개나 지나가는 구름일 뿐이란다.\" 시모니데스가 겁에 질린 에스더를 달래며 말했다. \"곧 다시 밝아질 게다.\"\n\n벤허는 그렇게 생각하지 않았다.\n\n\"안개나 구름이 아닙니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"공중에 사는 영들이—예언자들과 성도들이—자기 자신과 자연을 위해 자비롭게 일하고 있는 것입니다. 그대에게 이르노니, 오 시모니데스여, 하느님이 살아 계심이 진실인 것만큼이나 진실로, 저기 매달린 분은 하느님의 아들입니다.\"\n\n그러고는 그런 말을 한 그에게 놀라 어쩔 줄 모르는 시모니데스를 두고, 그는 발타사르가 가까이서 무릎 꿇고 있는 곳으로 가, 그 선한 사람의 어깨에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"오 슬기로운 이집트 사람이여, 들으시오! 그대만이 옳았소—저 나사렛 사람은 참으로 하느님의 아들이오.\"\n\n발타사르는 그를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기고는 힘없이 대답했다. \"나는 그가 처음 눕혀진 구유 속의 갓난아기일 적에 그를 보았다네. 그러니 내가 자네보다 먼저 그를 알아본 것이 이상할 게 없지. 하지만 아, 이 날을 보도록 내가 살아 있다니! 차라리 내 형제들과 함께 죽었더라면! 복된 멜키오르여! 복되고 복된 가스파르여!\"\n\n\"위안을 삼으시오!\" 벤허가 말했다. \"분명 그들도 여기 함께 있을 것이오.\"\n\n어둑함은 점점 짙어져 어슴푸레함이 되고, 그것이 다시 완연한 어둠이 되었으나, 언덕 위의 더 대담한 자들을 막지는 못했다. 강도들이 차례로 제 십자가에 들어 올려지고, 십자가가 박혔다. 그러자 경비가 거두어지고, 풀려난 사람들이 그 높은 곳을 향해 밀려들어, 한곳으로 모이는 물결처럼 그 위로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 한 사람이 한 번 들여다볼라치면, 새로 온 자가 그를 밀쳐 내고 그 자리를 차지했다가 다시 떠밀리곤 했다—그리고 웃음과 음담과 욕설이 있었으니, 모두 나사렛 사람을 향한 것이었다.\n\n\"하, 하! 네가 유대인의 왕이거든, 네 한 몸이나 구해 보아라.\" 한 군병이 외쳤다.\n\n\"옳소,\" 한 제사장이 말했다. \"저자가 지금 우리에게 내려온다면, 우리도 그를 믿어 주마.\"\n\n또 다른 자들은 짐짓 똑똑한 척 머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"성전을 헐고 사흘 만에 다시 짓겠다더니, 제 한 몸도 못 구하는구나.\"\n\n또 다른 자들은 이렇게 말했다. \"자기를 하느님의 아들이라 일컬었으니, 하느님이 그를 거두시는지 어디 보자.\"\n\n편견 속에 무엇이 다 들어 있는지는 아무도 말한 적이 없다. 그 나사렛 사람은 이 사람들을 결코 해친 적이 없었다. 그들 가운데 대부분은 이 재난의 시각이 아니고서는 그를 본 적조차 없었다. 그런데도—기이한 모순이여!—그들은 그에게 저주를 퍼붓고, 강도들에게 동정을 보냈다.\n\n하늘에서 그렇게 떨어진 초자연의 밤은, 그것이 더 용감하고 강한 수천 사람에게 영향을 미치기 시작했듯이, 에스더에게도 영향을 미쳤다.\n\n\"집으로 가요.\" 그녀가 두 번, 세 번 애원했다. \"이건 하느님의 진노예요, 아버지. 또 무슨 무서운 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠어요? 저는 무서워요.\"\n\n시모니데스는 고집을 부렸다. 그는 말은 거의 없었으나, 분명 큰 흥분에 사로잡혀 있었다. 첫 시각이 끝나갈 무렵, 언덕 위로 밀려드는 사람들의 기세가 다소 누그러진 것을 보고는, 그의 제안에 따라 일행은 십자가에 더 가까운 자리로 나아갔다. 벤허가 발타사르에게 팔을 내주었으나, 그 이집트 사람은 어렵게야 오를 수 있었다. 새로 잡은 자리에서는 나사렛 사람이 흐릿하게 보였으니, 그들에게는 매달린 어두운 형상에 지나지 않았다. 그래도 그들은 그의 소리를 들을 수 있었으니—그의 한숨 소리를 들을 수 있었는데, 그 한숨은 함께 고통받는 자들보다 더 큰 인내, 혹은 더 큰 탈진을 드러냈다. 그 두 사람은 소음이 잦아드는 틈마다 신음과 애원으로 채웠기 때문이다.\n\n십자가에 매달린 지 두 시간째도 첫 시간처럼 지나갔다. 나사렛 사람에게 그 시간은 모욕과 도발과 더딘 죽음의 시간이었다. 그는 그 사이에 단 한 번 입을 열었다. 몇몇 여인이 와서 그의 십자가 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다. 그들 가운데서 그는 자기 어머니와 사랑하는 제자를 알아보았다.\n\n\"여인이여,\" 그가 목소리를 높여 말했다. \"당신의 아들을 보소서!\" 그리고 제자에게는 \"네 어머니를 보아라!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n세 시간째가 되었으나, 사람들은 여전히 언덕 둘레로 밀려들었으니, 어떤 기이한 이끌림에 붙들려 있었고, 그 이끌림에는 십중팔구 한낮의 밤이 큰 몫을 했을 것이다. 그들은 앞선 시각보다 잠잠했다. 그래도 이따금 어둠 속 멀리서 서로 외쳐 대는 소리가 들렸으니, 무리가 무리를 부르는 소리였다. 또한 눈에 띈 것은, 이제 나사렛 사람에게 다가올 때면 사람들이 잠잠히 그의 십자가에 이르러, 잠잠히 바라보고는, 그렇게 잠잠히 떠나갔다는 점이다. 이 변화는 경비병들에게까지 미쳤으니, 그토록 조금 전에 처형된 자의 옷을 두고 제비를 뽑던 자들이었다. 그들은 장교들과 함께 조금 떨어져 서서, 오가는 군중보다 그 한 죄수를 더 눈여겨 지켰다. 그가 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬거나 고통의 발작으로 머리를 젖히기만 해도, 그들은 즉시 경계 태세에 들어갔다. 그러나 무엇보다 놀라운 것은 대제사장과 그를 따르는 자들, 곧 밤의 재판에서 그를 거들고 희생자의 면전에서 열성껏 동조하며 그 곁을 지켰던 현자들의 달라진 행동이었다. 어둠이 내리기 시작하자, 그들은 자신감을 잃기 시작했다. 그들 가운데는 천문학에 밝아, 그 시절 대중에게 그토록 무서웠던 천체 현상에 익숙한 자가 많았다. 그 지식의 상당 부분은 먼 조상에게서 물려받은 것이었고, 일부는 포로 생활이 끝날 무렵 가지고 나온 것이었으며, 성전 봉사의 필요가 그 모든 지식을 늘 또렷하게 지켜 주었다. 이들은 해가 자기네 눈앞에서 희미해지고 산과 언덕이 물러가기 시작하자 한데 모여들었다. 그들은 제사장을 둘러싸고 무리 지어, 자기네가 본 것을 두고 논의했다. \"달이 만월이니,\" 그들은 사실대로 말했다. \"이것은 일식일 수 없다.\" 그러고는, 그들 모두에게 공통된 그 물음에 아무도 답할 수 없자—아무도 그 어둠을, 또는 하필 그 특정한 시각에 일어난 그 어둠을 설명할 수 없자, 그들은 속마음으로 그것을 나사렛 사람과 연결 지었고, 그 현상이 오래 이어질수록 점점 커지는 불안에 굴복했다. 군병들 뒤 제자리에서 그들은 나사렛 사람의 말 한마디 몸짓 하나하나를 살피며, 그의 한숨에 두려워 매달리고, 소곤소곤 말을 나누었다. 저 사람이 메시아일지도 모르고, 그렇다면— 그러나 그들은 두고 보기로 했다!\n\n그동안 벤허에게는 그 옛날의 영(靈)이 한 번도 찾아오지 않았다. 그 완전한 평안이 그에게 머물러 있었다. 그는 다만 그 끝이 어서 앞당겨지기를 간구했다. 그는 시모니데스의 마음 상태를 알고 있었으니—그가 믿음의 문턱에서 망설이고 있음을 알았다. 그 묵직한 얼굴이 엄숙한 사색에 짓눌려 있는 것이 그의 눈에 보였다. 그가 어둠의 원인을 찾기라도 하듯 해를 향해 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던지는 것이 그의 눈에 띄었다. 또한 에스더가 제 두려움을 억누르고 아버지의 뜻에 맞추며 그에게 매달리는 그 애틋함도 그는 놓치지 않았다.\n\n\"두려워 말아라.\" 그가 딸에게 말하는 것을 그는 들었다. \"다만 머물러 나와 함께 지켜보아라. 너는 내 평생의 두 곱절을 산다 해도, 이만한 인간사를 보지는 못할 것이다. 그리고 더 많은 계시가 있을지도 모른다. 끝까지 머물자꾸나.\"\n\n세 시간째가 절반쯤 지났을 때, 가장 천한 부류의 사람들 몇이—도성 둘레 무덤가에서 나온 비참한 자들이—와서 한가운데 십자가 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"이자가 바로 그자다, 유대인의 새 왕이라지.\" 그들 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n다른 자들이 웃으며 외쳤다. \"만세, 만만세, 유대인의 왕이시여!\"\n\n아무 대답이 없자, 그들은 더 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n\"네가 유대인의 왕이거나 하느님의 아들이거든, 어디 내려와 보아라.\" 그들이 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n이 말에, 강도 가운데 하나가 신음을 그치고 나사렛 사람에게 외쳤다. \"그래, 네가 그리스도거든, 너 자신과 우리를 구원해 보아라.\"\n\n사람들이 웃으며 박수를 쳤다. 그러고는 그들이 대답을 들으려고 귀를 기울이는데, 다른 죄수가 첫째 죄수에게 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. \"너는 하느님이 두렵지도 않으냐? 우리는 우리가 행한 일에 마땅한 갚음을 받는 것이지만, 이분은 아무런 잘못도 하지 않으셨다.\"\n\n곁에 선 자들이 놀랐다. 뒤이은 침묵 가운데 둘째 죄수가 다시 입을 열었으나, 이번에는 나사렛 사람을 향해서였다.\n\n\"주여,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신의 나라에 들어가실 때 저를 기억하소서.\"\n\n시모니데스가 크게 움찔했다. \"당신의 나라에 들어가실 때!\" 그것은 바로 그의 마음속 의심의 핵심이었으니, 그가 발타사르와 그토록 자주 논쟁했던 바로 그 지점이었다.\n\n\"들었소?\" 벤허가 그에게 말했다. \"그 나라는 이 세상에 속한 것일 수 없소. 저 증인이 말하기를, 왕은 다만 자기 나라로 가는 길이라 하오. 그리고 사실, 나도 내 꿈속에서 똑같은 것을 들었소.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 시모니데스가 일찍이 벤허에게 한 어떤 말보다도 단호하게 말했다. \"쉿, 제발! 만일 저 나사렛 사람이 대답한다면—\"\n\n그가 말하는 사이에 그 나사렛 사람이 정말로 대답했으니, 확신이 가득한 또렷한 목소리였다.\n\n\"진실로 네게 이르노니, 오늘 네가 나와 함께 낙원에 있으리라!\"\n\n시모니데스는 그것이 전부인지 잠시 기다렸다. 그러고는 두 손을 모으고 말했다. \"더는 필요 없습니다, 더는, 주여! 어둠이 걷혔습니다. 저는 다른 눈으로 봅니다—발타사르처럼, 저는 완전한 믿음의 눈으로 봅니다.\"\n\n그 충직한 종이 마침내 합당한 보상을 받았다. 그의 망가진 몸은 결코 회복되지 못할 것이며, 그가 겪은 고초의 기억에서 벗어날 길도, 그 고초로 쓰라렸던 세월을 되돌릴 길도 없었다. 그러나 별안간 그에게 새로운 삶이 보였으니, 그것이 그를 위한 것이라는 확신과 더불어였다—이 삶 바로 너머에 놓인 새로운 삶, 그 이름은 낙원이었다. 거기서 그는 그토록 꿈꾸어 온 그 나라와 그 왕을 만나리라. 완전한 평안이 그에게 임했다.\n\n그러나 길 건너편, 십자가 앞에서는 놀람과 경악이 일었다. 거기 교활한 궤변가들은 그 물음의 밑바탕에 깔린 전제와 그 대답의 밑바탕에 깔린 시인(是認)을 한데 짜 맞추었다. 그가 메시아라고 온 땅에 떠들고 다닌 까닭에 그들은 나사렛 사람을 십자가에 매달았던 것이다. 그런데, 보라! 십자가 위에서 그는 그 어느 때보다 확신에 차서 자기를 다시 내세웠을 뿐 아니라, 한 죄인에게 자기 낙원을 누리게 해 주겠다고 약속한 것이다. 그들은 자기가 하고 있는 일에 몸을 떨었다. 그토록 거만하던 제사장도 두려워졌다. 저 사람이 그 확신을 진리가 아니고서야 어디서 얻었겠는가? 그리고 진리란 곧 하느님이 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 이제 아주 작은 것 하나면 그들 모두를 달아나게 하기에 족할 터였다.\n\n나사렛 사람의 숨결이 점점 거칠어지더니, 그의 한숨이 큰 헐떡임으로 변했다. 십자가에 매달린 지 겨우 세 시간 만에, 그가 죽어 가고 있었다!\n\n그 소식이 이 사람에게서 저 사람에게로 전해져, 마침내 모두가 그것을 알게 되었다. 그러자 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다. 미풍이 비틀거리다 잦아들었다. 숨 막히는 수증기가 공기를 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 어둠에 열기까지 더해졌다. 그 사실을 모르는 자라면 누구도, 저 언덕 너머 드리운 장막 아래에 삼백만 사람이 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질까 외경에 사로잡혀 기다리고 있다고는 생각하지 못했으리라—그토록 그들은 잠잠했다!\n\n그러자 어둠을 뚫고, 죽어 가는 사람의 목소리가 들리는 거리에 있던 자들의 머리 위로, 절망의, 어쩌면 책망의 외침이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"나의 하느님, 나의 하느님, 어찌하여 저를 버리셨나이까?\"\n\n그 목소리는 들은 모든 이를 소스라치게 했다. 한 사람은 걷잡을 수 없이 그것에 사로잡혔다.\n\n군병들은 올 때 포도주와 물을 담은 그릇을 가지고 와, 벤허에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에 내려놓았다. 그 액체에 적신 해면을 막대 끝에 꽂으면, 고통받는 자의 혀를 제 마음대로 적셔 줄 수 있었다. 벤허는 나사렛 부근 우물에서 자기가 마셨던 한 모금을 떠올렸다. 어떤 충동이 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 해면을 집어 들어 그릇에 적시고는 십자가를 향해 달려갔다.\n\n\"내버려 두어라!\" 길에 선 사람들이 성난 목소리로 외쳤다. \"내버려 두어!\"\n\n그는 아랑곳하지 않고 달려가, 그 해면을 나사렛 사람의 입술에 가져다 댔다.\n\n너무 늦었다, 너무 늦었다!\n\n그때 벤허에게 또렷이 보인 그 얼굴은, 피와 먼지로 멍들고 검게 그을렸으나, 그래도 별안간 환한 빛으로 밝아졌다. 두 눈이 활짝 열려, 오직 그들만이 볼 수 있는 누군가를 저 먼 하늘에서 지그시 바라보았다. 그리고 그 희생자가 내지른 외침에는 만족과 안도가, 심지어 승리가 깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"다 이루었다! 다 이루었다!\"\n\n위대한 일을 이루다가 죽어 가는 영웅이 마지막 환호로 제 성취를 기리듯이.\n\n그 두 눈의 빛이 꺼져 갔다. 가시관 쓴 머리가 천천히 가쁜 가슴 위로 떨어졌다. 벤허는 그 싸움이 끝났다고 생각했다. 그러나 기진해 가던 영혼이 정신을 가다듬어, 그와 그 둘레의 사람들은 마치 가까이서 듣는 이에게 건네듯 나직한 목소리로 말하는 또 한마디, 마지막 말을 들었다.\n\n\"아버지, 제 영혼을 아버지 손에 맡기나이다.\"\n\n전율이 그 고문당한 몸을 뒤흔들었다. 가장 사나운 고통의 비명이 울렸고, 그 사명과 지상의 삶이 일순간에 끝났다. 그 온갖 사랑을 품은 심장이 터졌으니, 오 독자여, 그 때문에 그분은 죽으신 것이다!\n\n벤허는 친구들에게 돌아가, 그저 이렇게 말했다. \"끝났습니다. 그분은 돌아가셨습니다.\"\n\n믿기 어려울 만큼 짧은 사이에 군중에게 그 사실이 알려졌다. 누구도 그것을 큰 소리로 되뇌지 않았다. 언덕에서 사방으로 번져 가는 웅성거림이 있었을 뿐이니, 소곤거림에 지나지 않는 웅성거림이었다. \"그가 죽었다! 그가 죽었어!\" 그것이 전부였다. 사람들은 제 바라던 바를 이루었다. 그 나사렛 사람이 죽은 것이다. 그러나 그들은 아연실색하여 서로를 빤히 바라보았다. 그의 피가 그들 위에 떨어진 것이다! 그렇게 서로를 응시하며 서 있는데, 땅이 흔들리기 시작했다. 저마다 몸을 가누려고 곁 사람을 붙들었다. 눈 깜짝할 새 어둠이 사라지고 해가 나왔다. 그리고 모두가 한순간에 같은 눈길로 보기라도 하듯, 언덕 위 십자가들이 지진에 술 취한 듯 죄다 휘청거리는 것을 보았다. 그들은 셋을 다 보았으나, 한가운데 것이 유독 두드러졌다. 그것만이 보였다. 그리고 그것은 위로 스스로를 뻗어, 제 짐을 들어 올리고, 푸른 하늘 속에서 점점 더 높이 그것을 이리저리 흔드는 듯했다. 그리고 그들 가운데 나사렛 사람을 조롱했던 자, 그를 때렸던 자, 그를 십자가에 못 박자고 표결했던 자, 도성에서부터 행렬을 지어 걸어왔던 자, 마음속으로 그가 죽기를 바랐던 자—이들이 열에 아홉이었다—모두가 자기가 어떤 식으로든 그 많은 무리 가운데서 따로 지목되었다고, 그래서 살려거든 저 하늘의 위협으로부터 되도록 빨리 달아나야 한다고 느꼈다. 그들은 달리기 시작했다. 그들은 있는 힘을 다해 달렸다. 말을 타고, 낙타를 타고, 전차를 타고, 또 두 발로 달렸다. 그러나 그러자 지진은, 마치 그들이 저지른 일에 노하여 죄 없고 의지할 데 없는 죽은 이의 편을 들기라도 한 듯, 그들을 뒤쫓아 이리저리 내던지고 거꾸러뜨렸으며, 발아래에서 거대한 바위들이 갈리고 찢기는 무시무시한 굉음으로 그들을 한층 더 떨게 했다. 그들은 제 가슴을 치며 두려움에 비명을 질렀다. 그의 피가 그들 위에 떨어진 것이다! 본토박이든 이방인이든, 제사장이든 평신도든, 거지든, 사두개인이든, 바리새인이든, 달아나다 따라잡혀 분별없이 나뒹굴었다. 그들이 주를 부르면, 능욕당한 대지가 그를 대신해 격노로 답하며 그들 모두를 한결같이 다루었다. 대지는 대제사장이 그 죄 많은 형제들보다 어디가 나은지조차 알지 못했다. 그를 따라잡아 그 또한 거꾸러뜨리고, 그 옷자락에 흙칠을 하고, 금방울 속을 모래로 채우고, 그 입을 먼지로 채웠다. 그와 그의 백성은 적어도 한 가지에서는 똑같았으니—그 나사렛 사람의 피가 그들 모두 위에 떨어진 것이다!\n\n햇빛이 처형장에 비쳐 들었을 때, 나사렛 사람의 어머니와 제자와 갈릴리의 충실한 여인들, 백부장과 그의 군병들, 그리고 벤허와 그 일행만이 언덕 위에 남아 있었다. 이들에게는 군중의 도피를 살필 겨를이 없었으니, 제 한 몸 건사하기에도 너무나 다급하게 내몰렸던 것이다.\n\n\"여기 앉아라.\" 벤허가 에스더에게 아버지의 발치에 자리를 만들어 주며 말했다. \"자, 두 눈을 가리고 위를 보지 마라. 다만 하느님과, 저토록 끔찍하게 죽임당한 의로운 분의 영을 믿어라.\"\n\n\"아닙니다,\" 시모니데스가 경건하게 말했다. \"이제부터 그분을 그리스도라 부릅시다.\"\n\n\"그러지요.\" 벤허가 말했다.\n\n이윽고 지진의 물결이 언덕을 쳤다. 휘청대는 십자가 위 강도들의 비명은 차마 듣기 끔찍했다. 땅의 움직임에 어지러우면서도, 벤허는 발타사르를 살필 겨를이 있었으니, 그가 엎어진 채 미동도 없는 것을 보았다. 그는 그에게 달려가 불렀다—아무 대답이 없었다. 그 선한 사람이 죽은 것이다! 그제야 벤허는, 나사렛 사람이 마지막 순간에 지른 비명에 화답하듯 외침 하나가 들렸던 것을 떠올렸다. 그러나 그것이 누구에게서 나온 것인지 살펴보지는 않았던 터라, 그 뒤로 줄곧 그는 그 이집트 사람의 영이 그 주님의 영과 함께 경계를 넘어 낙원의 나라로 들어갔다고 믿었다. 그 생각은 들었던 그 외침에 근거했을 뿐 아니라, 그러한 영예가 그에게 더없이 합당하다는 데에도 근거했다. 믿음이 가스파르라는 인물 안에서 보상받을 만하고, 사랑이 멜키오르라는 인물 안에서 보상받을 만하다면, 긴 일생을 통하여 믿음과 사랑과 선행이라는 세 가지 덕을 하나로 어우러 그토록 훌륭하게 보여 준 그가 어떤 특별한 갚음을 받는 것은 마땅한 일이었다.\n\n발타사르의 종들은 주인을 버리고 달아났다. 그러나 모든 것이 끝나자, 두 갈릴리 사람이 그 노인을 가마에 실어 도성으로 옮겼다.\n\n그 잊을 수 없는 날 해 질 무렵, 후르 가의 궁전 남문으로 들어선 것은 슬픔에 잠긴 행렬이었다. 그와 거의 같은 시각에 그리스도의 몸이 십자가에서 내려졌다.\n\n발타사르의 유해는 객실로 옮겨졌다. 모든 종이 울며 그를 보러 달려왔으니, 그는 자기가 어떤 식으로든 관계 맺은 살아 있는 모든 것의 사랑을 받았기 때문이다. 그러나 그들이 그의 얼굴과 그 위에 어린 미소를 보고는, 눈물을 거두며 말했다. \"잘된 일이다. 그분은 아침에 나가실 때보다 이 저녁에 더 행복하시구나.\"\n\n벤허는 아버지에게 닥친 일을 이라스에게 알리는 일을 종에게 맡기려 하지 않았다. 그는 몸소 그녀를 만나러 가, 그녀를 그 유해 곁으로 데려오려 했다. 그는 그녀의 슬픔을 헤아려 보았다. 이제 그녀는 세상에 홀로 남을 것이었다. 용서하고 가엾이 여길 때였다. 그는 자기가 아침에 어찌하여 그녀가 일행에 없었는지, 그녀가 어디 있는지 묻지 않았던 것을 떠올렸다. 그녀를 생각조차 하지 않았던 것을 떠올렸다. 그래서 부끄러움에, 그는 어떤 식으로든 보상할 마음이 들었으니, 그녀를 그토록 사무치는 슬픔에 빠뜨리려는 참이었기에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n그는 그녀 방의 휘장을 흔들었다. 안에서 작은 방울들이 울리는 소리가 들렸으나, 아무 응답이 없었다. 그는 그녀의 이름을 불렀고, 다시 불렀다—여전히 대답이 없었다. 그는 휘장을 걷고 방 안으로 들어갔다. 그녀는 거기 없었다. 그는 그녀를 찾아 황급히 지붕으로 올라갔다. 거기에도 그녀는 없었다. 그는 종들에게 물었다. 그날 하루 종일 그녀를 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 집 안 곳곳을 오래도록 찾아 헤맨 끝에, 벤허는 객실로 돌아와, 마땅히 그녀의 자리였어야 할 그 죽은 이의 곁자리에 앉았다. 그러고는 거기서 그리스도께서 그 늙은 종에게 얼마나 자비로우셨는가를 생각했다. 낙원의 나라 문턱에서는, 다행히도 이 삶의 고난이, 심지어 그 버림받음마저도, 그 안으로 들어가 안식하는 이들에게는 뒤에 남겨져 잊히는 것이다.\n\n매장의 침통함이 거의 가셨을 무렵, 치유가 있은 지 아흐레째 되는 날, 율법이 다 이루어진 뒤에, 벤허는 어머니와 티르자를 집으로 데려왔다. 그리고 그날부터 그 집에서는 사람의 입으로 일컬을 수 있는 가장 거룩한 이름이 언제나 경배 속에 나란히 짝지어 불렸으니,\n성부 하느님과 성자 그리스도였다.\n\n처형이 있은 지 다섯 해쯤 뒤, 벤허의 아내 에스더는 미세눔 곁의 아름다운 별장 안 제 방에 앉아 있었다. 정오였고, 따스한 이탈리아의 해가 바깥의 장미와 포도덩굴에 여름을 펼쳐 주고 있었다. 그 방의 모든 것이 로마풍이었으나, 다만 에스더가 유대 부인의 옷을 걸치고 있는 것만은 달랐다. 티르자와, 바닥의 사자 가죽 위에서 놀고 있는 두 아이가 그녀의 벗이었다. 그녀가 얼마나 정성스레 그들을 지켜보는지 보기만 해도, 그 어린것들이 그녀의 아이임을 알 수 있었다.\n\n세월은 그녀에게 너그러웠다. 그녀는 어느 때보다 아름다웠고, 이 별장의 안주인이 됨으로써 그녀는 오래 품어 온 꿈 하나를 이룬 것이었다.\n\n이 소박하고 단란한 정경 한가운데로 한 종이 문간에 나타나 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"안마당에 한 여인이 와서 안주인을 뵙겠다 합니다.\"\n\n\"들라 하라. 여기서 맞이하겠다.\"\n\n이윽고 그 낯선 여인이 들어왔다. 그녀를 보자 유대 여인은 일어서서 막 말을 건네려 했다. 그러다 머뭇거리며 낯빛이 변하더니, 마침내 뒤로 물러서며 말했다. \"당신을 알 듯합니다, 부인. 당신은—\"\n\n\"저는 발타사르의 딸 이라스였습니다.\"\n\n에스더는 놀라움을 가라앉히고, 종에게 그 이집트 여인의 자리를 가져오라 일렀다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 이라스가 쌀쌀하게 말했다. \"곧 물러갈 겁니다.\"\n\n두 여인은 서로를 바라보았다. 에스더가 어떤 모습인지는 우리가 안다—아름다운 여인, 행복한 어머니, 만족한 아내였다. 다른 한쪽은, 운명이 한때의 연적에게는 그토록 너그럽지 못했음이 너무도 분명했다. 그 큰 키에는 우아함이 얼마간 남아 있었으나, 사악한 삶이 온몸을 더럽혀 놓았다. 얼굴은 거칠었고, 큰 두 눈은 붉었으며 아래 눈꺼풀 밑이 부어 있었다. 두 뺨에는 핏기가 없었다. 입술은 냉소적이고 굳어 있었으며, 전반적인 방치가 빠르게 때 이른 노쇠로 이끌고 있었다. 차림새는 형편없이 골라 입은 데다 너절했다. 길의 진흙이 그녀의 샌들에 들러붙어 있었다. 이라스가 그 괴로운 침묵을 깼다.\n\n\"이 아이들이 당신 아이들인가요?\"\n\n에스더는 아이들을 바라보며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"네. 이 아이들에게 말을 건네지 않겠어요?\"\n\n\"제가 아이들을 겁먹게 할 거예요.\" 이라스가 대답했다. 그러고는 에스더에게 더 가까이 다가갔다가, 그녀가 움찔하는 것을 보고 말했다. \"두려워 마세요. 당신 남편에게 내 말을 전해 주세요. 그의 원수가 죽었고, 그가 내게 안긴 그 숱한 비참함의 갚음으로 내가 그를 죽였다고.\"\n\n\"그의 원수라니요!\"\n\n\"메살라 말이에요. 또한 당신 남편에게 전해 주세요. 그에게 해를 끼치려 했던 그 죄로, 나는 그조차 가엾이 여길 만큼 벌을 받아 왔다고.\"\n\n에스더의 눈에 눈물이 고였고, 그녀는 막 입을 열려 했다.\n\n\"아니,\" 이라스가 말했다. \"나는 동정도 눈물도 바라지 않아요. 마지막으로 그에게 전해 주세요. 로마인이 된다는 것은 곧 짐승이 된다는 것임을 내가 깨달았다고. 안녕히.\"\n\n그녀는 가려고 몸을 움직였다. 에스더가 그녀를 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"머물러 제 남편을 만나세요. 그는 당신에게 아무런 악감정이 없어요. 그는 당신을 사방으로 찾았어요. 그가 당신의 벗이 되어 줄 거예요. 저도 당신의 벗이 되겠어요. 우리는 그리스도인이에요.\"\n\n상대는 단호했다.\n\n\"아니에요. 나는 스스로 택해 지금의 내가 된 거예요. 곧 다 끝날 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만\"—에스더가 머뭇거렸다—\"우리에게 당신이 바라는 것이, 무언가 그—그—\"\n\n이집트 여인의 표정이 누그러졌다. 미소 비슷한 것이 그녀의 입가에 어렸다. 그녀는 바닥의 아이들을 바라보았다.\n\n\"한 가지 있어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n에스더가 그 눈길을 좇고는, 재빠른 헤아림으로 대답했다. \"그건 당신 거예요.\"\n\n이라스는 아이들에게 다가가, 사자 가죽 위에 무릎을 꿇고 둘 다에게 입을 맞추었다. 천천히 일어서서 그녀는 아이들을 바라보았다. 그러고는 작별의 말 한마디 없이 문 쪽으로 가 밖으로 나섰다. 그녀는 빠르게 걸어, 에스더가 어찌할지 미처 정하기도 전에 가 버리고 없었다.\n\n벤허는 그 방문에 대해 전해 들었을 때, 자기가 오래도록 짐작해 온 것을 확실히 알게 되었다—처형의 날에 이라스가 메살라를 위해 제 아버지를 버렸다는 것을. 그런데도 그는 곧장 길을 나서 그녀를 찾았으나 헛수고였다. 그들은 다시는 그녀를 보지도, 그녀의 소식을 듣지도 못했다. 그 푸른 만(灣)은, 햇빛 아래 온통 웃음 짓고 있으면서도, 여전히 어두운 비밀들을 품고 있다. 만일 그것에 혀가 있다면, 그 이집트 여인의 이야기를 우리에게 들려줄 수 있으리라.\n\n시모니데스는 아주 늙도록 살았다. 네로 치세 열째 해에, 그는 안디옥의 창고에 그토록 오래 자리 잡고 있던 사업에서 손을 뗐다. 마지막까지 그는 맑은 머리와 선한 마음을 지녔고, 성공을 거두었다.\n\n말한 그 해의 어느 저녁, 그는 창고 옥상에 안락의자를 놓고 앉아 있었다. 벤허와 에스더, 그리고 그들의 세 아이가 그와 함께 있었다. 마지막 남은 배 한 척이 강물의 흐름 속에 매여 일렁이고 있었다. 나머지는 모두 팔려 나간 뒤였다. 처형의 날부터 이때에 이르는 긴 세월 동안 그들에게 닥친 슬픔은 단 하나였으니, 벤허의 어머니가 세상을 떠난 일이었다. 그때나 지금이나 그들의 그리스도 신앙이 아니었다면 그 슬픔은 한층 더 컸으리라.\n\n방금 말한 그 배는 바로 전날에야 도착하여, 네로가 로마에서 시작한 그리스도인 박해의 소식을 가져왔다. 옥상의 일행이 그 소식을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있는데, 아직도 그들을 섬기고 있던 말루크가 다가와 벤허에게 꾸러미 하나를 전했다.\n\n\"누가 이걸 가져왔는가?\" 벤허가 읽고 나서 물었다.\n\n\"한 아랍 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"그는 어디 있는가?\"\n\n\"곧바로 떠났습니다.\"\n\n\"들어 보시오.\" 벤허가 시모니데스에게 말했다.\n\n그러고는 다음 편지를 읽었다.\n\"나, 너그러운 일데림의 아들 일데림이며 일데림 부족의 족장인 내가, 후르의 아들 유다에게.\n\n알아 두시오, 오 내 아버지의 벗이여, 내 아버지가 그대를 얼마나 사랑하셨는지를. 여기 함께 보내는 글을 읽으면 알게 될 것이오. 아버지의 뜻이 곧 내 뜻이니, 그러므로 아버지가 주신 것은 그대의 것이오.\n\n파르티아 사람들이 내 아버지를 살해한 그 큰 전투에서 그분에게서 빼앗아 간 모든 것을 나는 되찾았소—이 글과, 다른 것들과, 복수와, 그리고 그 시절 그토록 많은 별의 어미였던 그 미라의 온 핏줄까지.\n\n그대와 그대의 모든 이에게 평안이 있기를.\n\n사막에서 들려오는 이 목소리는\n\n일데림 족장의 목소리요.\"\n\n벤허는 다음으로, 시든 뽕잎처럼 누런 파피루스 조각을 펼쳤다. 그것은 더없이 조심스러운 손길을 요했다. 그는 이어서 읽어 나갔다.\n\n\"너그러운 이라 불리는, 일데림 부족의 족장 일데림이, 나를 이을 아들에게.\n\n내가 가진 모든 것은, 오 아들아, 네가 뒤를 잇는 날에 네 것이 되리라. 다만 안디옥 곁에 있는 종려나무 과수원이라 알려진 그 재산만은 예외이니, 그것은 원형 경기장에서 우리에게 그토록 큰 영광을 안겨 준 후르의 아들의 것이 되리라—그와 그의 자손에게 영원히.\n\n네 아버지를 욕되게 하지 말라. 너그러운 일데림 족장.\"\n\n\"어찌 생각하시오?\" 벤허가 시모니데스에게 물었다.\n\n에스더는 그 글들을 기뻐하며 받아, 혼자 속으로 읽었다. 시모니데스는 잠잠히 있었다. 그의 눈은 배를 향하고 있었으나, 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 이윽고 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"후르의 아들이여,\" 그가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"주께서는 이 근래의 세월에 그대에게 자애로우셨소. 그대는 감사할 일이 많소. 이제 그대 손안에 다 들어와 자라고 있는 그 큰 재물의 선물이 무엇을 뜻하는지를 마침내 결정할 때가 되지 않았소?\"\n\n\"나는 오래전에 그것을 결정했소. 그 재물은 주신 분을 섬기는 데에 쓰라고 주어진 것이오. 그 일부가 아니라, 시모니데스, 그 전부 말이오. 나의 물음은 다만, 어찌하면 그것을 그분의 대의에 가장 쓸모 있게 만들 수 있을까 하는 것이었소. 그 점을 내게 일러 주시오, 청하건대.\"\n\n시모니데스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그대가 여기 안디옥의 교회에 내놓은 큰돈을, 나는 똑똑히 보아 왔소. 그런데 이제, 너그러운 족장의 이 선물과 거의 때를 같이하여, 로마의 형제들이 박해받는다는 소식이 왔소. 이것은 새로운 일터가 열리는 것이오. 수도에서 빛이 꺼져서는 안 되오.\"\n\n\"어찌하면 그 빛을 살려 둘 수 있는지 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"내 일러 주리다. 로마인들은, 이 네로조차도, 두 가지를 거룩히 여기오—그들이 그토록 거룩히 여기는 다른 것을 나는 알지 못하오—그것은 죽은 자의 유골과 모든 매장지요. 만일 그대가 주를 예배할 성전을 땅 위에 지을 수 없거든, 땅 밑에 지으시오. 그리고 그곳이 더럽혀지지 않도록, 신앙 안에서 죽은 모든 이의 시신을 그리로 옮기시오.\"\n\n벤허가 흥분하여 일어섰다.\n\n\"위대한 생각이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 일을 시작하는 데에 지체하지 않겠소. 시간이 지체를 허락하지 않소. 우리 형제들의 고난을 전해 준 그 배가 나를 로마로 데려갈 것이오. 나는 내일 떠나겠소.\"\n\n그는 말루크에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"배를 준비하게, 말루크. 그리고 자네도 나와 함께 갈 채비를 하게.\"\n\n\"잘된 일이오.\" 시모니데스가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그대, 에스더는, 어찌 생각하오?\" 벤허가 물었다.\n\n에스더가 그의 곁으로 와 그의 팔에 손을 얹고 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇게 하셔야 그리스도를 가장 잘 섬기시는 거예요. 오 여보, 제가 막지 않게 해 주세요. 그저 당신과 함께 가서 돕게 해 주세요.\"\n\n내 독자 가운데 누구라도 로마를 찾거든, 산 세바스티아노보다 더 오래된 산 칼리스토 카타콤으로 잠깐 발걸음을 옮겨 보라. 그러면 그는 벤허의 재물이 어찌 되었는지를 보게 될 것이며, 그에게 감사를 드릴 것이다. 그 광대한 무덤에서 그리스도교가 솟아 나와, 카이사르들의 자리를 대신했던 것이다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-2145"]=[[["The Jebel es Zubleh is a mountain fifty miles and more in length, and so narrow that its tracery on the map gives it a likeness to a caterpillar crawling from the south to the north.", "「제벨 에스 주블레」는 길이가 80킬로미터가 넘는 산으로, 폭이 어찌나 좁은지 지도에 그려진 모습은 마치 남쪽에서 북쪽으로 기어가는 애벌레를 닮았다."], ["Standing on its red-and-white cliffs, and looking off under the path of the rising sun, one sees only the Desert of Arabia, where the east winds, so hateful to vinegrowers of Jericho, have kept their playgrounds since the beginning.", "붉고 흰 절벽 위에 서서 떠오르는 해가 지나는 길을 따라 멀리 바라보면, 오직 아라비아 사막만이 눈에 들어온다. 그곳에서는 여리고의 포도 재배자들이 그토록 미워하는 동풍이 태초부터 제 놀이터를 지켜 왔다."], ["Its feet are well covered by sands tossed from the Euphrates, there to lie, for the mountain is a wall to the pasture-lands of Moab and Ammon on the west—lands which else had been of the desert a part.", "산기슭은 유프라테스에서 날려 온 모래에 두텁게 덮여 있는데, 이 산이 서쪽으로 모압과 암몬의 목초지를 가로막는 성벽 노릇을 하기에 모래는 그곳에 그대로 쌓여 있다. 그렇지 않았다면 그 목초지마저 사막의 일부가 되었을 것이다."]], [["The Arab has impressed his language upon everything south and east of Judea, so, in his tongue, the old Jebel is the parent of numberless wadies which, intersecting the Roman road—now a dim suggestion of what once it was, a dusty path for Syrian pilgrims to and from Mecca—run their furrows, deepening as they go, to pass the torrents of the rainy season into the Jordan, or their last receptacle, the Dead Sea.", "아랍인은 유대 남쪽과 동쪽의 모든 것에 자기 언어의 흔적을 새겨 놓았다. 그래서 그들의 말로 이 오래된 제벨은 수없이 많은 와디의 어머니다. 그 와디들은 로마 가도를 가로지르며—한때 메카를 오가는 시리아 순례자들이 밟던 먼지투성이 길이었으나 지금은 그 옛 모습의 희미한 흔적만 남은 가도다—골을 이루어 흐르고, 갈수록 깊어지다가, 우기의 급류를 요르단강이나 그 마지막 종착지인 사해로 흘려보낸다."], ["Out of one of these wadies—or, more particularly, out of that one which rises at the extreme end of the Jebel, and, extending east of north, becomes at length the bed of the Jabbok River—a traveller passed, going to the table-lands of the desert.", "이 와디들 가운데 하나에서—더 정확히 말하면, 제벨의 맨 끝에서 시작하여 북쪽으로 동쪽을 향해 뻗어 가다가 마침내 얍복강의 바닥이 되는 그 와디에서—한 나그네가 빠져나와 사막의 고원지대를 향해 가고 있었다."], ["To this person the attention of the reader is first besought.", "독자가 처음으로 주목해야 할 사람이 바로 이 인물이다."]], [["Judged by his appearance, he was quite forty-five years old. His beard, once of the deepest black, flowing broadly over his breast, was streaked with white.", "겉모습으로 미루어 그는 마흔다섯 살쯤 되어 보였다."], ["His face was brown as a parched coffee-berry, and so hidden by a red kufiyeh (as the kerchief of the head is at this day called by the children of the desert) as to be but in part visible.", "한때 더없이 새까맣던 그의 수염은 가슴 위로 넓게 흘러내렸는데, 이제는 군데군데 희끗희끗했다."], ["Now and then he raised his eyes, and they were large and dark. He was clad in the flowing garments so universal in the East; but their style may not be described more particularly, for he sat under a miniature tent, and rode a great white dromedary.", "그의 얼굴은 바싹 마른 커피 열매처럼 갈색이었고, 붉은 쿠피예에 가려져 일부만 보일 뿐이었다. * 쿠피예: 오늘날 사막의 자손들이 머릿수건을 부르는 이름."]], [["It may be doubted if the people of the West ever overcome the impression made upon them by the first view of a camel equipped and loaded for the desert.", "이따금 그가 눈을 들면, 크고 검은 눈이 드러났다. 그는 동방에서 누구나 입는 헐렁한 옷을 걸치고 있었으나, 그 차림새를 더 자세히 묘사하기는 어렵다."], ["Custom, so fatal to other novelties, affects this feeling but little.", "그가 작은 천막 아래 앉아 거대한 흰 단봉낙타를 타고 있었기 때문이다."], ["At the end of long journeys with caravans, after years of residence with the Bedawin, the Western-born, wherever they may be, will stop and wait the passing of the stately brute.", "서방 사람들이 사막을 가기 위해 채비와 짐을 갖춘 낙타를 처음 보았을 때 받은 인상에서 과연 헤어날 수 있을지 의심스럽다. 다른 새로운 것들에는 그토록 치명적인 익숙함도 이 감정만큼은 좀처럼 어쩌지 못한다."], ["The charm is not in the figure, which not even love can make beautiful; nor in the movement, the noiseless stepping, or the broad careen.", "대상(隊商)과 함께 긴 여행을 마친 뒤에도, 베두인족과 더불어 여러 해를 산 뒤에도, 서방 태생의 사람들은 어디에 있든 걸음을 멈추고 그 위엄 있는 짐승이 지나가기를 기다린다."], ["As is the kindness of the sea to a ship, so that of the desert to its creature.", "그 매력은 생김새에 있지 않다. 그 생김새는 사랑으로도 아름답게 만들 수 없다."], ["It clothes him with all its mysteries; in such manner, too, that while we are looking at him we are thinking of them: therein is the wonder.", "또한 그 움직임에 있는 것도 아니니, 소리 없는 발걸음이나 폭넓게 기우뚱거리는 걸음새도 아니다. 바다가 배에게 베푸는 친절이 그러하듯, 사막은 제 피조물에게 그런 친절을 베푼다. 사막은 낙타에게 제 온갖 신비를 입혀 준다."], ["The animal which now came out of the wady might well have claimed the customary homage.", "게다가 우리가 낙타를 바라보는 동안 그 신비를 떠올리게 만드니, 바로 거기에 경이가 있다."], ["Its color and height; its breadth of foot; its bulk of body, not fat, but overlaid with muscle; its long, slender neck, of swanlike curvature; the head, wide between the eyes, and tapering to a muzzle which a lady’s bracelet might have almost clasped; its motion, step long and elastic, tread sure and soundless—all certified its Syrian blood, old as the days of Cyrus, and absolutely priceless.", "지금 와디에서 빠져나온 그 짐승은 으레 바치는 경의를 받을 만했다. 그 빛깔과 키, 넓은 발, 살이 찐 것이 아니라 근육으로 뒤덮인 몸집, 백조처럼 휘어진 길고 가느다란 목, 두 눈 사이는 넓고 부인의 팔찌로도 거의 감쌀 만큼 가늘어지는 주둥이를 가진 머리, 그리고 길고 탄력 있는 보폭에 확실하고도 소리 없는 발걸음—이 모든 것이 키루스 시대만큼이나 오래된, 그리고 그야말로 값을 매길 수 없는 시리아 혈통임을 증명하고 있었다."], ["There was the usual bridle, covering the forehead with scarlet fringe, and garnishing the throat with pendent brazen chains, each ending with a tinkling silver bell; but to the bridle there was neither rein for the rider nor strap for a driver.", "여느 것과 같은 굴레가 이마를 진홍빛 술로 덮고, 목에는 늘어뜨린 놋쇠 사슬을 장식했는데, 사슬 끝마다 딸랑거리는 은방울이 달려 있었다. 그러나 그 굴레에는 타는 이를 위한 고삐도, 모는 이를 위한 가죽끈도 없었다."], ["The furniture perched on the back was an invention which with any other people than of the East would have made the inventor renowned.", "등에 얹힌 장구(裝具)는 동방 사람이 아닌 다른 민족이었다면 그 고안자를 유명하게 만들었을 발명품이었다."], ["It consisted of two wooden boxes, scarce four feet in length, balanced so that one hung at each side; the inner space, softly lined and carpeted, was arranged to allow the master to sit or lie half reclined; over it all was stretched a green awning.", "그것은 길이가 채 1.2미터도 안 되는 나무 상자 두 개로 이루어져, 양옆에 하나씩 매달려 균형을 이루었다. 안쪽 공간은 부드럽게 안을 대고 양탄자를 깔아, 주인이 앉거나 반쯤 기대어 누울 수 있게 마련되어 있었다. 그 위로는 초록빛 차일이 드리워졌다."], ["Broad back and breast straps, and girths, secured with countless knots and ties, held the device in place.", "폭넓은 등띠와 가슴띠와 뱃대끈이 수없이 많은 매듭과 끈으로 단단히 묶여 그 장치를 제자리에 붙들고 있었다."], ["In such manner the ingenious sons of Cush had contrived to make comfortable the sunburnt ways of the wilderness, along which lay their duty as often as their pleasure.", "이런 식으로 쿠시의 슬기로운 자손들은 의무에 따라서든 즐거움에 따라서든 자주 지나야 하는 햇볕 따가운 광야의 길을 편안하게 만들어 냈던 것이다."]], [["When the dromedary lifted itself out of the last break of the wady, the traveller had passed the boundary of El Belka, the ancient Ammon.", "단봉낙타가 와디의 마지막 갈래에서 몸을 끌어올렸을 때, 나그네는 옛 암몬 땅인 엘 벨카의 경계를 넘어선 뒤였다."], ["It was morning-time.", "아침나절이었다."], ["Before him was the sun, half curtained in fleecy mist; before him also spread the desert; not the realm of drifting sands, which was farther on, but the region where the herbage began to dwarf; where the surface is strewn with boulders of granite, and gray and brown stones, interspersed with languishing acacias and tufts of camel-grass.", "그의 앞에는 보드라운 안개에 반쯤 가린 해가 떠 있었고, 그의 앞에는 또한 사막이 펼쳐져 있었다. 흩날리는 모래의 세계는 아니었으니, 그것은 더 멀리 있었다. 그가 있는 곳은 풀들이 작아지기 시작하는 지역으로, 땅 위에는 화강암 바위와 잿빛, 갈색 돌멩이가 흩어져 있고, 그 사이로 시들어 가는 아카시아와 낙타풀 더미가 듬성듬성 박혀 있었다."], ["The oak, bramble, and arbutus lay behind, as if they had come to a line, looked over into the well-less waste and crouched with fear.", "떡갈나무와 가시덤불과 소귀나무는 마치 어떤 경계선에 다다라, 물 한 점 없는 황야를 넘겨다보고는 두려움에 웅크리기라도 한 듯, 뒤쪽에 남아 있었다."]], [["And now there was an end of path or road.", "이제는 오솔길도 큰길도 끝이 났다."], ["More than ever the camel seemed insensibly driven; it lengthened and quickened its pace, its head pointed straight towards the horizon; through the wide nostrils it drank the wind in great draughts.", "낙타는 그 어느 때보다 보이지 않는 무엇에 이끌리는 듯했다. 걸음을 길고 빠르게 옮기며, 머리는 곧장 지평선을 향했다. 넓은 콧구멍으로는 바람을 크게 들이켰다."], ["The litter swayed, and rose and fell like a boat in the waves. Dried leaves in occasional beds rustled underfoot.", "가마는 흔들리며, 물결 위의 배처럼 오르내렸다. 군데군데 마른 잎이 쌓인 자리에서는 발밑으로 바스락 소리가 났다."], ["Sometimes a perfume like absinthe sweetened all the air.", "때로는 압생트 같은 향기가 온 공기를 달콤하게 적셨다."], ["Lark and chat and rock-swallow leaped to wing, and white partridges ran whistling and clucking out of the way.", "종다리와 딱새와 바위제비가 날개를 치며 날아오르고, 흰 자고새들이 휘파람 같은 소리를 내고 꼬꼬댁거리며 길에서 달아났다."], ["More rarely a fox or a hyena quickened his gallop, to study the intruders at a safe distance.", "더 드물게는 여우나 하이에나가 안전한 거리에서 침입자를 살피려고 발걸음을 재촉했다."], ["Off to the right rose the hills of the Jebel, the pearl-gray veil resting upon them changing momentarily into a purple which the sun would make matchless a little later.", "오른편으로는 제벨의 언덕들이 솟아 있었고, 그 위에 드리운 진줏빛 잿빛 너울은 시시각각 자줏빛으로 바뀌어 갔으니, 조금 뒤면 해가 그 빛을 견줄 데 없이 곱게 물들일 터였다."], ["Over their highest peaks a vulture sailed on broad wings into widening circles.", "가장 높은 봉우리 위로는 독수리 한 마리가 너른 날개를 펴고 점점 넓어지는 원을 그리며 날았다."], ["But of all these things the tenant under the green tent saw nothing, or, at least, made no sign of recognition.", "그러나 초록빛 천막 아래 든 사람은 이 모든 것을 하나도 보지 못했거나, 적어도 알아차렸다는 기색을 보이지 않았다."], ["His eyes were fixed and dreamy.", "그의 눈은 한곳에 못 박힌 채 꿈꾸는 듯했다."], ["The going of the man, like that of the animal, was as one being led.", "짐승이 그러하듯, 그 사람의 가는 모습 또한 누군가에게 이끌려 가는 것만 같았다."]], [["For two hours the dromedary swung forward, keeping the trot steadily and the line due east.", "두 시간 동안 단봉낙타는 앞으로 흔들흔들 나아가며, 한결같이 종종걸음을 유지하고 정동(正東)으로 길을 잡았다."], ["In that time the traveller never changed his position, nor looked to the right or left.", "그동안 나그네는 한 번도 자세를 바꾸지 않았고, 좌우를 돌아보지도 않았다."], ["On the desert, distance is not measured by miles or leagues, but by the saat, or hour, and the manzil, or halt: three and a half leagues fill the former, fifteen or twenty-five the latter; but they are the rates for the common camel.", "사막에서 거리는 마일이나 리그로 재는 것이 아니라, 사트, 곧 시간과 만질, 곧 멈춤으로 잰다. 한 사트는 3.5리그를, 한 만질은 15리그에서 25리그를 채운다. 그러나 이것은 보통 낙타의 속도다."], ["A carrier of the genuine Syrian stock can make three leagues easily.", "진짜 시리아 혈통의 운반용 낙타라면 3리그쯤은 거뜬히 갈 수 있다."], ["At full speed he overtakes the ordinary winds.", "전속력을 내면 여느 바람도 따라잡는다."], ["As one of the results of the rapid advance, the face of the landscape underwent a change.", "빠르게 나아간 결과의 하나로, 풍경의 모습이 바뀌었다."], ["The Jebel stretched along the western horizon, like a pale-blue ribbon.", "제벨은 옅은 푸른빛 리본처럼 서쪽 지평선을 따라 뻗어 있었다."], ["A tell, or hummock of clay and cemented sand, arose here and there.", "텔, 곧 진흙과 굳은 모래로 된 둔덕이 여기저기 솟아 있었다."], ["Now and then basaltic stones lifted their round crowns, outposts of the mountain against the forces of the plain; all else, however, was sand, sometimes smooth as the beaten beach, then heaped in rolling ridges; here chopped waves, there long swells.", "이따금 현무암 돌들이 둥근 봉우리를 들어 올렸으니, 평원의 세력에 맞선 산의 전초였다. 그러나 그 밖의 모든 것은 모래였으니, 어떤 곳은 다져진 해변처럼 매끄럽고, 어떤 곳은 굽이치는 능선처럼 쌓여 있었으며, 여기는 자잘하게 부서진 물결, 저기는 길게 너울지는 물결이었다."], ["So, too, the condition of the atmosphere changed.", "대기의 상태 또한 그러하게 바뀌었다."], ["The sun, high risen, had drunk his fill of dew and mist, and warmed the breeze that kissed the wanderer under the awning; far and near he was tinting the earth with faint milk-whiteness, and shimmering all the sky.", "높이 떠오른 해는 이슬과 안개를 실컷 들이마셨고, 차일 아래 나그네에게 입 맞추는 산들바람을 데웠다. 멀리서나 가까이서나 해는 대지를 옅은 젖빛으로 물들이며, 온 하늘을 어른어른 빛나게 했다."]], [["Two hours more passed without rest or deviation from the course.", "쉬지도 않고 길을 벗어나지도 않은 채 두 시간이 더 흘렀다."], ["Vegetation entirely ceased.", "초목은 완전히 자취를 감추었다."], ["The sand, so crusted on the surface that it broke into rattling flakes at every step, held undisputed sway.", "표면이 어찌나 단단하게 굳었는지 한 걸음 디딜 때마다 덜그럭거리는 조각으로 부서지는 모래가 다툼 없이 그 땅을 다스리고 있었다."], ["The Jebel was out of view, and there was no landmark visible.", "제벨은 시야에서 사라졌고, 눈에 보이는 어떤 이정표도 없었다."], ["The shadow that before followed had now shifted to the north, and was keeping even race with the objects which cast it; and as there was no sign of halting, the conduct of the traveller became each moment more strange.", "앞서 뒤따르던 그림자는 이제 북쪽으로 옮겨 가, 그것을 드리운 물체와 나란히 발맞추어 가고 있었다. 그런데도 멈출 기색이 전혀 없으니, 나그네의 행동은 순간순간 더욱 기이해져 갔다."]], [["No one, be it remembered, seeks the desert for a pleasure-ground.", "기억해 두어야 할 것은, 누구도 사막을 놀이터 삼아 찾지는 않는다는 사실이다."], ["Life and business traverse it by paths along which the bones of things dead are strewn as so many blazons.", "삶과 생업은 죽은 것들의 뼈가 수많은 문장(紋章)처럼 흩어진 길을 따라 사막을 가로지른다."], ["Such are the roads from well to well, from pasture to pasture.", "우물에서 우물로, 목초지에서 목초지로 이어지는 길이 그러하다."], ["The heart of the most veteran sheik beats quicker when he finds himself alone in the pathless tracts.", "길 없는 황무지에 홀로 있게 되면 아무리 노련한 족장이라도 가슴이 더 빨리 뛴다."], ["So the man with whom we are dealing could not have been in search of pleasure; neither was his manner that of a fugitive; not once did he look behind him.", "그러므로 우리가 지금 다루고 있는 이 사람도 즐거움을 찾고 있었을 리는 없다. 그렇다고 그 태도가 도망자의 것도 아니었으니, 그는 단 한 번도 뒤를 돌아보지 않았다."], ["In such situations fear and curiosity are the most common sensations; he was not moved by them.", "그런 상황에서는 두려움과 호기심이 가장 흔한 감각이건만, 그는 그런 것에 흔들리지 않았다."], ["When men are lonely, they stoop to any companionship; the dog becomes a comrade, the horse a friend, and it is no shame to shower them with caresses and speeches of love.", "사람은 외로워지면 어떤 길동무에게도 몸을 굽히게 마련이라, 개가 동무가 되고 말이 벗이 되며, 그것들에게 쓰다듬음과 사랑의 말을 쏟아붓는 것도 부끄러운 일이 아니다."], ["The camel received no such token, not a touch, not a word.", "그러나 낙타는 그런 표시를 받지 못했으니, 손길 한 번도, 말 한마디도 없었다."]], [["Exactly at noon the dromedary, of its own will, stopped, and uttered the cry or moan, peculiarly piteous, by which its kind always protest against an overload, and sometimes crave attention and rest.", "정오가 되자 단봉낙타는 제 뜻대로 걸음을 멈추고, 그 종족이 짐이 너무 무거울 때 늘 내는, 때로는 보살핌과 휴식을 갈구할 때 내는, 유달리 애처로운 울음, 곧 신음 소리를 냈다."], ["The master thereupon bestirred himself, waking, as it were, from sleep.", "그러자 주인은 마치 잠에서 깨어나듯 몸을 추슬렀다."], ["He threw the curtains of the houdah up, looked at the sun, surveyed the country on every side long and carefully, as if to identify an appointed place.", "그는 후다의 휘장을 걷어 올리고, 해를 바라보고는, 약속된 장소를 가려내기라도 하려는 듯 사방의 지형을 오래도록 꼼꼼히 살폈다."], ["Satisfied with the inspection, he drew a deep breath and nodded, much as to say, “At last, at last!”", "살피기를 마치고 흡족해진 그는 깊은 숨을 들이쉬고 고개를 끄덕였으니, \"마침내, 마침내 왔구나!\""], ["A moment after, he crossed his hands upon his breast, bowed his head, and prayed silently.", "하고 말하는 듯했다. 잠시 후 그는 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 고개를 숙여 말없이 기도했다."], ["The pious duty done, he prepared to dismount.", "경건한 의무를 마친 그는 내릴 채비를 했다."], ["From his throat proceeded the sound heard doubtless by the favorite camels of Job—Ikh!", "그의 목구멍에서 욥의 사랑받던 낙타들도 분명 들었을 그 소리가 흘러나왔다—\"이크!"], ["ikh!—the signal to kneel.", "이크!\"—꿇어앉으라는 신호였다."], ["Slowly the animal obeyed, grunting the while.", "짐승은 내내 끙끙거리며 천천히 그 말에 따랐다."], ["The rider then put his foot upon the slender neck, and stepped upon the sand.", "그러자 탄 이는 가느다란 목에 발을 디디고, 모래 위로 내려섰다. * 후다: 낙타 등에 얹는 덮개 달린 가마."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["The man as now revealed was of admirable proportions, not so tall as powerful.", "이제 모습을 드러낸 그 사람은 더없이 균형 잡힌 체격으로, 키가 큰 것이 아니라 다부진 편이었다."], ["Loosening the silken rope which held the kufiyeh on his head, he brushed the fringed folds back until his face was bare—a strong face, almost negro in color; yet the low, broad forehead, aquiline nose, the outer corners of the eyes turned slightly upward, the hair profuse, straight, harsh, of metallic lustre, and falling to the shoulder in many plaits, were signs of origin impossible to disguise.", "쿠피예를 머리에 붙들어 매던 비단끈을 풀고, 술 달린 천 자락을 뒤로 젖혀 얼굴을 온전히 드러냈다—그것은 흑인에 가까운 빛깔의 강인한 얼굴이었으나, 낮고 넓은 이마, 매부리코, 바깥쪽으로 살짝 치켜 올라간 눈꼬리, 금속의 윤기가 도는 곧고 뻣뻣한 숱 많은 머리카락이 여러 갈래로 땋여 어깨까지 흘러내린 모습은 도무지 감출 수 없는 혈통의 표징이었다."], ["So looked the Pharaohs and the later Ptolemies; so looked Mizraim, father of the Egyptian race.", "파라오들과 후대의 프톨레마이오스들이 이러한 모습이었고, 이집트 민족의 시조인 미스라임이 이러한 모습이었다."], ["He wore the kamis, a white cotton shirt tight-sleeved, open in front, extending to the ankles and embroidered down the collar and breast, over which was thrown a brown woollen cloak, now, as in all probability it was then, called the aba, an outer garment with long skirt and short sleeves, lined inside with stuff of mixed cotton and silk, edged all round with a margin of clouded yellow.", "그는 카미스를 입고 있었으니, 소매가 좁고 앞이 트인 흰 무명 셔츠로 발목까지 내려오고 깃과 가슴께에 수가 놓인 옷이었으며, 그 위에는 갈색 모직 망토를 둘렀는데, 지금도 그러하고 그때도 십중팔구 그러했듯이 아바라고 부르는 옷이었다. 아바는 자락이 길고 소매가 짧은 겉옷으로, 안쪽에는 무명과 비단을 섞어 짠 천을 대고, 가장자리는 빙 둘러 흐릿한 노란빛으로 둘러쳐져 있었다."], ["His feet were protected by sandals, attached by thongs of soft leather.", "그의 발은 부드러운 가죽끈으로 동여맨 샌들이 감싸고 있었다."], ["A sash held the kamis to his waist.", "허리띠가 카미스를 허리에 붙들어 매었다."], ["What was very noticeable, considering he was alone, and that the desert was the haunt of leopards and lions, and men quite as wild, he carried no arms, not even the crooked stick used for guiding camels; wherefore we may at least infer his errand peaceful, and that he was either uncommonly bold or under extraordinary protection.", "그가 홀로인 데다, 사막은 표범과 사자, 그리고 그것들 못지않게 사나운 인간들이 출몰하는 곳임을 생각하면 무척 눈에 띄는 점은, 그가 아무런 무기도 지니지 않았다는 것이었으니, 낙타를 모는 데 쓰는 굽은 막대기조차 없었다. 그러므로 우리는 적어도 그의 볼일이 평화로운 것임을, 그리고 그가 보기 드물게 담대하거나 아니면 어떤 비범한 보호 아래 있음을 미루어 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["The traveller’s limbs were numb, for the ride had been long and wearisome; so he rubbed his hands and stamped his feet, and walked round the faithful servant, whose lustrous eyes were closing in calm content with the cud he had already found.", "나그네의 팔다리는 마비되어 있었으니, 여정이 길고도 고단했던 까닭이다. 그래서 그는 두 손을 비비고 발을 굴렀으며, 충직한 종 둘레를 거닐었다. 낙타는 이미 되새김할 풀을 찾아내고는 흡족한 마음에 빛나는 눈을 지그시 감고 있었다."], ["Often, while making the circuit, he paused, and, shading his eyes with his hands, examined the desert to the extremest verge of vision; and always, when the survey was ended, his face clouded with disappointment, slight, but enough to advise a shrewd spectator that he was there expecting company, if not by appointment; at the same time, the spectator would have been conscious of a sharpening of the curiosity to learn what the business could be that required transaction in a place so far from civilized abode.", "그렇게 도는 동안 그는 자주 걸음을 멈추고, 손으로 눈 위에 그늘을 지어 시야가 닿는 가장 먼 끝까지 사막을 살폈다. 그리고 살피기를 마칠 때마다 그의 얼굴에는 실망의 그늘이 드리웠으니, 비록 옅었으나 눈썰미 있는 자라면 그가 약속 때문은 아닐지라도 누군가의 일행을 기다리고 있음을 알아챌 만했다. 동시에 그 구경꾼은 문명의 거처에서 이토록 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 처리해야 할 볼일이 대체 무엇일지 알고 싶은 호기심이 한층 날카로워짐을 느꼈을 것이다."]], [["However disappointed, there could be little doubt of the stranger’s confidence in the coming of the expected company.", "아무리 실망했다 한들, 기다리던 일행이 오리라는 이 낯선 이의 확신에는 의심의 여지가 거의 없었다."], ["In token thereof, he went first to the litter, and, from the cot or box opposite the one he had occupied in coming, produced a sponge and a small gurglet of water, with which he washed the eyes, face, and nostrils of the camel; that done, from the same depository he drew a circular cloth, red-and white-striped, a bundle of rods, and a stout cane.", "그 증표로, 그는 먼저 가마로 가서, 올 때 자신이 들어앉았던 칸의 맞은편 칸에서 해면과 작은 가죽 물병을 꺼내, 낙타의 눈과 얼굴과 콧구멍을 씻어 주었다. 그 일을 마치자 같은 곳에서 붉고 흰 줄무늬가 든 둥근 천 한 장과 막대기 한 묶음과 굵은 지팡이 하나를 끄집어냈다."], ["The latter, after some manipulation, proved to be a cunning device of lesser joints, one within another, which, when united together, formed a centre pole higher than his head.", "그 지팡이는 손을 좀 놀려 보니, 작은 마디들이 하나씩 다른 마디 속에 들어가도록 만든 정교한 장치임이 드러났는데, 한데 이으니 그의 키보다 높은 중심 기둥이 되었다."], ["When the pole was planted, and the rods set around it, he spread the cloth over them, and was literally at home—a home much smaller than the habitations of emir and sheik, yet their counterpart in all other respects.", "기둥을 세우고 그 둘레에 막대기들을 박은 뒤, 그는 그 위에 천을 펼쳤다. 이로써 그는 그야말로 제집을 마련한 셈이었으니—에미르나 셰이크의 거처보다는 훨씬 작았으나, 다른 모든 점에서는 그것의 축소판이었다."], ["From the litter again he brought a carpet or square rug, and covered the floor of the tent on the side from the sun.", "다시 가마에서 그는 양탄자, 곧 네모난 깔개를 꺼내 천막 안 해를 등진 쪽 바닥에 깔았다."], ["That done, he went out, and once more, and with greater care and more eager eyes, swept the encircling country.", "그 일을 마치자 그는 다시 밖으로 나가, 한층 정성스럽고 한층 간절한 눈길로 사방의 땅을 둘러보았다."], ["Except a distant jackal, galloping across the plain, and an eagle flying towards the Gulf of Akaba, the waste below, like the blue above it, was lifeless.", "평원을 가로질러 내달리는 멀리의 재칼 한 마리와 아카바만을 향해 날아가는 독수리 한 마리를 빼면, 위의 푸른 하늘이 그러하듯 아래의 황야 또한 생명이 없었다."]], [["He turned to the camel, saying low, and in a tongue strange to the desert, “We are far from home, O racer with the swiftest winds—we are far from home, but God is with us.", "그는 낙타에게 돌아서서, 사막에는 낯선 언어로 나직이 말했다. \"우리는 집에서 멀리 왔구나, 오 가장 빠른 바람과 겨루는 자여—우리는 집에서 멀리 왔으나, 하느님이 우리와 함께 계시느니라."], ["Let us be patient.”", "참고 견디자꾸나.\""]], [["Then he took some beans from a pocket in the saddle, and put them in a bag made to hang below the animal’s nose; and when he saw the relish with which the good servant took to the food, he turned and again scanned the world of sand, dim with the glow of the vertical sun.", "이윽고 그는 안장 주머니에서 콩 몇 줌을 꺼내, 짐승의 코 밑에 매달도록 만든 자루에 담았다. 그리고 충직한 종이 그 먹이를 맛나게 먹는 모습을 보고는, 다시 돌아서서 수직으로 내리쬐는 해의 빛에 어른거리는 모래의 세계를 또 한 번 살폈다."]], [["“They will come,”", "\"그들은 올 것이다.\""], ["he said, calmly.", "그는 차분히 말했다."], ["“He that led me is leading them.", "\"나를 이끄신 분이 그들도 이끄시느니라."], ["I will make ready.”", "채비를 해 두어야겠다.\""]], [["From the pouches which lined the interior of the cot, and from a willow basket which was part of its furniture, he brought forth materials for a meal: platters close-woven of the fibres of palms; wine in small gurglets of skin; mutton dried and smoked; stoneless shami, or Syrian pomegranates; dates of El Shelebi, wondrous rich and grown in the nakhil, or palm orchards, of Central Arabia; cheese, like David’s “slices of milk;”", "칸의 안쪽을 두른 주머니들과, 거기 갖추어진 버들 바구니에서 그는 식사 재료를 꺼냈다. 종려나무 섬유를 촘촘히 엮은 접시들, 작은 가죽 병에 든 포도주, 말려 훈제한 양고기, 씨 없는 샤미, 곧 시리아 석류, 중앙아라비아의 나킬, 곧 종려 과수원에서 자라는 놀랍도록 달콤한 엘 셸레비 대추야자, 다윗의 \"젖과 같은 치즈\""], ["and leavened bread from the city bakery—all which he carried and set upon the carpet under the tent.", "같은 치즈, 그리고 도시 빵집에서 구운 발효빵—이 모든 것을 그는 천막 아래 양탄자 위로 날라 차려 놓았다."], ["As the final preparation, about the provisions he laid three pieces of silk cloth, used among refined people of the East to cover the knees of guests while at table—a circumstance significant of the number of persons who were to partake of his entertainment—the number he was awaiting.", "마지막 채비로, 그는 음식 둘레에 비단 천 세 장을 깔았으니, 동방의 교양 있는 사람들이 식탁에 앉은 손님의 무릎을 덮는 데 쓰는 것이었다—그가 베푸는 자리에 참여할 사람의 수를 말해 주는 정황이요, 그가 기다리고 있는 인원수였다."]], [["All was now ready.", "이제 모든 것이 갖추어졌다."], ["He stepped out: lo! in the east a dark speck on the face of the desert.", "그가 밖으로 나서니, 보라! 동쪽 사막의 얼굴 위에 검은 점 하나가 있었다."], ["He stood as if rooted to the ground; his eyes dilated; his flesh crept chilly, as if touched by something supernatural.", "그는 땅에 뿌리박힌 듯 우뚝 섰고, 두 눈은 휘둥그레졌으며, 무언가 초자연적인 것에 닿기라도 한 듯 살갗에 서늘한 소름이 돋았다."], ["The speck grew; became large as a hand; at length assumed defined proportions.", "점은 자라났다. 손바닥만 하게 커지더니, 마침내 또렷한 형체를 갖추었다."], ["A little later, full into view swung a duplication of his own dromedary, tall and white, and bearing a houdah, the travelling litter of Hindostan.", "조금 뒤, 그 자신의 단봉낙타를 빼닮은 것이 온전히 모습을 드러내며 다가왔으니, 키가 크고 흰 데다, 힌두스탄의 여행용 가마인 후다를 얹고 있었다."], ["Then the Egyptian crossed his hands upon his breast, and looked to heaven.", "그러자 이집트인은 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 하늘을 우러렀다."]], [["“God only is great!”", "\"오직 하느님만이 위대하시도다!\""], ["he exclaimed, his eyes full of tears, his soul in awe.", "그는 두 눈에 눈물이 그득하고 영혼은 경외에 사로잡힌 채 외쳤다."]], [["The stranger drew nigh—at last stopped.", "낯선 이가 가까이 다가오더니—마침내 멈추어 섰다."], ["Then he, too, seemed just waking.", "그러자 그 또한 막 잠에서 깨어나는 듯했다."], ["He beheld the kneeling camel, the tent, and the man standing prayerfully at the door.", "그는 꿇어앉은 낙타와 천막과, 그 문간에 기도하는 자세로 선 사람을 바라보았다."], ["He crossed his hands, bent his head, and prayed silently; after which, in a little while, he stepped from his camel’s neck to the sand, and advanced towards the Egyptian, as did the Egyptian towards him.", "그는 두 손을 포개고 고개를 숙여 말없이 기도했다. 그러고는 이내 낙타의 목을 디뎌 모래 위로 내려서서, 이집트인을 향해 나아갔고, 이집트인 또한 그를 향해 나아갔다."], ["A moment they looked at each other; then they embraced—that is, each threw his right arm over the other’s shoulder, and the left round the side, placing his chin first upon the left, then upon the right breast.", "잠시 두 사람은 서로를 바라보았다. 그런 다음 그들은 끌어안았다—곧, 저마다 오른팔을 상대의 어깨 위로 두르고 왼팔로 옆구리를 감싸, 턱을 먼저 왼쪽 가슴에, 다음으로 오른쪽 가슴에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Peace be with thee, O servant of the true God!”", "\"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 오 참하느님의 종이여!\""], ["the stranger said.", "낯선 이가 말했다."]], [["“And to thee, O brother of the true faith!—to thee peace and welcome,”", "\"그대에게도 그러하기를, 오 참신앙의 형제여!—그대에게 평화와 환영이 있기를.\""], ["the Egyptian replied, with fervor.", "이집트인이 뜨겁게 화답했다."]], [["The new-comer was tall and gaunt, with lean face, sunken eyes, white hair and beard, and a complexion between the hue of cinnamon and bronze.", "새로 온 이는 키가 크고 수척했으니, 마른 얼굴에 움푹 꺼진 눈, 흰 머리와 수염, 그리고 계피와 청동의 중간쯤 되는 낯빛을 하고 있었다."], ["He, too, was unarmed. His costume was Hindostani; over the skull-cap a shawl was wound in great folds, forming a turban; his body garments were in the style of the Egyptian’s, except that the aba was shorter, exposing wide flowing breeches gathered at the ankles.", "그 또한 무기를 지니지 않았다. 옷차림은 힌두스탄풍이었으니, 챙 없는 모자 위로 숄을 큼직하게 여러 겹 둘러 터번을 이루었고, 몸에 걸친 옷은 이집트인의 것과 같은 양식이었으되, 다만 아바가 더 짧아 발목께에서 졸라맨 폭 넓은 통바지가 드러나 보였다."], ["In place of sandals, his feet were clad in half-slippers of red leather, pointed at the toes.", "샌들 대신 그의 발은 발끝이 뾰족한 붉은 가죽 반신을 신고 있었다."], ["Save the slippers, the costume from head to foot was of white linen.", "그 신을 빼면 머리에서 발끝까지 옷차림은 온통 흰 아마였다."], ["The air of the man was high, stately, severe.", "그 사람의 분위기는 고결하고 위엄 있으며 준엄했다."], ["Visvamitra, the greatest of the ascetic heroes of the Iliad of the East, had in him a perfect representative.", "동방의 일리아드에서 가장 위대한 고행 영웅인 비스바미트라가 그에게서 완벽한 화신을 얻은 셈이었다."], ["He might have been called a Life drenched with the wisdom of Brahma—Devotion Incarnate.", "그는 브라마의 지혜로 흠뻑 젖은 생명이라, 헌신의 화신이라 불릴 만했다."], ["Only in his eyes was there proof of humanity; when he lifted his face from the Egyptian’s breast, they were glistening with tears.", "오직 그의 두 눈에만 인간다움의 증거가 있었으니, 이집트인의 가슴에서 얼굴을 들었을 때 그 눈은 눈물로 반짝이고 있었다."]], [["“God only is great!” he exclaimed, when the embrace was finished.", "\"오직 하느님만이 위대하시도다!\" 그는 포옹을 마치고 외쳤다."]], [["“And blessed are they that serve him!”", "\"그리고 그분을 섬기는 자들에게 복이 있도다!\""], ["the Egyptian answered, wondering at the paraphrase of his own exclamation.", "이집트인이 답하면서, 자신의 외침을 고쳐 옮긴 그 말에 놀랐다. \"그러나 기다립시다.\""], ["“But let us wait,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added, “let us wait; for see, the other comes yonder!”", "\"기다립시다. 보시오, 또 한 분이 저기 오고 있소이다!\""]], [["They looked to the north, where, already plain to view, a third camel, of the whiteness of the others, came careening like a ship.", "그들은 북쪽을 바라보았다. 그곳에는 이미 또렷이 눈에 들어오는, 다른 낙타들처럼 새하얀 세 번째 낙타가 한 척의 배처럼 기우뚱거리며 다가오고 있었다."], ["They waited, standing together—waited until the new-comer arrived, dismounted, and advanced towards them.", "그들은 함께 서서 기다렸다—새로 온 이가 다다라 내려서서 그들을 향해 나아올 때까지 기다렸다."]], [["“Peace to you, O my brother!” he said, while embracing the Hindoo.", "\"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 오 나의 형제여!\" 그는 힌두인을 끌어안으며 말했다."]], [["And the Hindoo answered, “God’s will be done!”", "힌두인이 답했다. \"하느님의 뜻이 이루어지이다!\""]], [["The last comer was all unlike his friends: his frame was slighter; his complexion white; a mass of waving light hair was a perfect crown for his small but beautiful head; the warmth of his dark-blue eyes certified a delicate mind, and a cordial, brave nature.", "마지막에 온 이는 두 벗과는 사뭇 달랐다. 체구가 더 가늘었고, 낯빛이 희었으며, 물결치는 옅은 빛깔의 머리채가 작지만 아름다운 머리에 더없이 어울리는 관을 이루고 있었다. 짙푸른 두 눈의 온기는 섬세한 마음씨와, 따뜻하고 용감한 천성을 일러 주었다."], ["He was bareheaded and unarmed. Under the folds of the Tyrian blanket which he wore with unconscious grace appeared a tunic, short-sleeved and low-necked, gathered to the waist by a band, and reaching nearly to the knee; leaving the neck, arms, and legs bare.", "그는 맨머리에 무기도 지니지 않았다. 무심한 우아함으로 두른 티레산 모포 자락 아래로는, 소매가 짧고 목깃이 낮으며 허리에 띠를 둘러 무릎께까지 닿는 튜닉이 드러났으니, 목과 팔과 다리는 맨살이었다."], ["Sandals guarded his feet.", "샌들이 그의 발을 지켰다."], ["Fifty years, probably more, had spent themselves upon him, with no other effect, apparently, than to tinge his demeanor with gravity and temper his words with forethought.", "쉰 해, 아마도 그 이상의 세월이 그에게 흘러갔으되, 겉으로 드러나는 자취라곤 그 거동에 무게를 더하고 그 말에 사려를 깃들인 것뿐인 듯했다."], ["The physical organization and the brightness of soul were untouched. No need to tell the student from what kindred he was sprung; if he came not himself from the groves of Athene’, his ancestry did.", "몸의 됨됨이도, 영혼의 광채도 손상되지 않았다. 그가 어떤 겨레에서 났는지는 글 읽는 이에게 굳이 일러 줄 필요가 없으니, 그 자신은 아테네의 숲에서 오지 않았더라도 그의 조상은 그러했던 것이다."]], [["When his arms fell from the Egyptian, the latter said, with a tremulous voice, “The Spirit brought me first; wherefore I know myself chosen to be the servant of my brethren.", "그의 팔이 이집트인에게서 풀렸을 때, 이집트인이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"성령께서 나를 먼저 이끄셨소이다. 그러니 나는 내가 형제들을 섬기는 종으로 뽑혔음을 아오."], ["The tent is set, and the bread is ready for the breaking.", "천막은 세워졌고, 빵은 떼기에 알맞게 마련되어 있소."], ["Let me perform my office.”", "내가 내 소임을 다하게 해 주시오.\""]], [["Taking each by the hand, he led them within, and removed their sandals and washed their feet, and he poured water upon their hands, and dried them with napkins.", "그는 저마다의 손을 잡고 그들을 안으로 이끌어, 그들의 샌들을 벗기고 발을 씻겼으며, 손 위에 물을 부어 주고 수건으로 닦아 주었다."]], [["Then, when he had laved his own hands, he said, “Let us take care of ourselves, brethren, as our service requires, and eat, that we may be strong for what remains of the day’s duty.", "그런 다음, 자기 손마저 씻고 나서 그가 말했다. \"형제들이여, 우리 소임이 그러하니 몸을 잘 돌봅시다. 그리고 먹읍시다."], ["While we eat, we will each learn who the others are, and whence they come, and how they are called.”", "그래야 오늘 남은 의무를 감당할 힘을 얻을 것이오. 먹는 동안, 우리 각자가 다른 이들이 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지, 어떻게 불리는지 알아 둡시다.\""]], [["He took them to the repast, and seated them so that they faced each other.", "그는 그들을 음식 앞으로 데려가, 서로 마주 보도록 앉혔다."], ["Simultaneously their heads bent forward, their hands crossed upon their breasts, and, speaking together, they said aloud this simple grace:", "동시에 그들의 머리가 앞으로 숙여지고, 두 손이 가슴 위에 포개졌으며, 그들은 한목소리로 이 소박한 감사의 기도를 소리 내어 올렸다."]], [["“Father of all—God!—what we have here is of thee; take our thanks and bless us, that we may continue to do thy will.”", "\"만물의 아버지이신—하느님!—여기 있는 것은 모두 당신의 것이옵니다. 우리의 감사를 받으시고 우리를 축복하시어, 우리가 당신의 뜻을 계속 행하게 하옵소서.\""]], [["With the last word they raised their eyes, and looked at each other in wonder.", "마지막 말과 함께 그들은 눈을 들어, 놀라움 속에 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Each had spoken in a language never before heard by the others; yet each understood perfectly what was said.", "저마다 다른 이들이 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 언어로 말했건만, 저마다 그 말의 뜻을 완벽하게 알아들었던 것이다."], ["Their souls thrilled with divine emotion; for by the miracle they recognized the Divine Presence.", "그들의 영혼은 거룩한 감격으로 떨렸으니, 그 기적으로 말미암아 그들은 하느님의 임재를 깨달았기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["To speak in the style of the period, the meeting just described took place in the year of Rome 747.", "시대의 어법으로 말하자면, 방금 묘사한 만남은 로마력 747년에 일어난 일이다."], ["The month was December, and winter reigned over all the regions east of the Mediterranean.", "때는 12월이었고, 지중해 동쪽의 온 지역에 겨울이 군림하고 있었다."], ["Such as ride upon the desert in this season go not far until smitten with a keen appetite.", "이 계절에 사막을 가는 사람은 멀리 가지 않아 곧 날카로운 허기에 사로잡힌다."], ["The company under the little tent were not exceptions to the rule.", "작은 천막 아래 모인 일행도 그 법칙에서 예외는 아니었다."], ["They were hungry, and ate heartily; and, after the wine, they talked.", "그들은 배가 고팠고, 마음껏 먹었다. 그리고 포도주를 마신 뒤에는 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["“To a wayfarer in a strange land nothing is so sweet as to hear his name on the tongue of a friend,”", "\"낯선 땅의 나그네에게 친구의 입에서 제 이름을 듣는 것만큼 달콤한 일은 없습니다.\""], ["said the Egyptian, who assumed to be president of the repast.", "그 식사의 좌장을 자처한 이집트인이 말했다."], ["“Before us lie many days of companionship.", "\"우리 앞에는 함께할 많은 날이 놓여 있습니다."], ["It is time we knew each other.", "이제 서로를 알 때가 되었습니다."], ["So, if it be agreeable, he who came last shall be first to speak.”", "그러니 괜찮으시다면, 가장 늦게 오신 분이 가장 먼저 말씀하시지요.\""]], [["Then, slowly at first, like one watchful of himself, the Greek began:", "그러자, 처음에는 천천히, 마치 스스로를 조심스레 살피는 사람처럼, 그리스인이 입을 열었다."]], [["“What I have to tell, my brethren, is so strange that I hardly know where to begin or what I may with propriety speak.", "\"형제들이여, 제가 들려드릴 이야기는 너무도 기이하여, 어디서부터 시작해야 할지, 무엇을 마땅히 말씀드려야 할지조차 모르겠습니다."], ["I do not yet understand myself.", "저 자신도 아직 저를 이해하지 못합니다."], ["The most I am sure of is that I am doing a Master’s will, and that the service is a constant ecstasy.", "제가 가장 확신하는 것은, 제가 한 주인의 뜻을 행하고 있다는 것, 그리고 그 섬김이 끊임없는 황홀이라는 것입니다."], ["When I think of the purpose I am sent to fulfil, there is in me a joy so inexpressible that I know the will is God’s.”", "제가 이루도록 보내심을 받은 그 목적을 생각하면, 제 안에는 형언할 수 없는 기쁨이 차오르니, 그 뜻이 하느님의 것임을 압니다.\""]], [["The good man paused, unable to proceed, while the others, in sympathy with his feelings, dropped their gaze.", "이 선한 사람은 더 나아가지 못하고 말을 멈추었고, 다른 이들은 그의 마음에 공감하여 시선을 떨구었다."]], [["“Far to the west of this,”", "\"이곳에서 멀리 서쪽으로,\""], ["he began again, “there is a land which may never be forgotten; if only because the world is too much its debtor, and because the indebtedness is for things that bring to men their purest pleasures.", "그가 다시 입을 열었다. \"결코 잊혀서는 안 될 땅이 하나 있습니다. 세상이 그 땅에 진 빚이 너무도 크고, 더구나 그 빚이 사람에게 가장 순수한 즐거움을 안겨 주는 것들에 대한 빚이기 때문입니다."], ["I will say nothing of the arts, nothing of philosophy, of eloquence, of poetry, of war: O my brethren, hers is the glory which must shine forever in perfected letters, by which He we go to find and proclaim will be made known to all the earth.", "저는 예술에 대해서도, 철학이나 웅변이나 시나 전쟁에 대해서도 아무 말 하지 않으렵니다. 아, 형제들이여, 그 땅의 영광은 완성된 문자 속에 영원히 빛나야 할 것이니, 우리가 찾아가 선포하려는 그분이 바로 그 문자를 통해 온 땅에 알려질 것입니다."], ["The land I speak of is Greece.", "제가 말하는 땅은 그리스입니다."], ["I am Gaspar, son of Cleanthes the Athenian.", "저는 아테네 사람 클레안테스의 아들 가스파르입니다."]], [["“My people,”", "\"제 민족은,\""], ["he continued, “were given wholly to study, and from them I derived the same passion.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"온전히 학문에 몸 바친 사람들이었고, 저 또한 그들에게서 같은 열정을 물려받았습니다."], ["It happens that two of our philosophers, the very greatest of the many, teach, one the doctrine of a Soul in every man, and its Immortality; the other the doctrine of One God, infinitely just.", "마침 우리의 철학자 가운데 둘이, 그 많은 이들 중에서도 가장 위대한 두 사람이, 하나는 모든 사람 안에 깃든 영혼과 그 불멸을 가르치고, 다른 하나는 무한히 의로우신 한 분 하느님의 가르침을 폈습니다."], ["From the multitude of subjects about which the schools were disputing, I separated them, as alone worth the labor of solution; for I thought there was a relation between God and the soul as yet unknown.", "여러 학파가 다투던 수많은 주제 가운데, 저는 그 둘을 따로 떼어 냈으니, 풀이의 수고를 들일 가치가 있는 것은 오직 그것뿐이라 여겼기 때문입니다. 저는 하느님과 영혼 사이에 아직 알려지지 않은 어떤 관계가 있다고 생각했습니다."], ["On this theme the mind can reason to a point, a dead, impassable wall; arrived there, all that remains is to stand and cry aloud for help.", "이 주제에 관하여 사람의 정신은 한 지점까지, 죽은 듯 넘을 수 없는 벽까지 헤아려 갈 수 있습니다. 거기에 이르면, 남은 것이라고는 그저 서서 도움을 큰 소리로 부르짖는 일뿐입니다. 저도 그리하였습니다."], ["So I did; but no voice came to me over the wall.", "그러나 그 벽 너머로 제게 들려오는 목소리는 없었습니다."], ["In despair, I tore myself from the cities and the schools.”", "절망에 빠진 저는 도시와 학파에서 저 자신을 떼어 냈습니다.\""]], [["At these words a grave smile of approval lighted the gaunt face of the Hindoo.", "이 말에 힌두인의 야윈 얼굴에 진지한 동의의 미소가 어렸다."]], [["“In the northern part of my country—in Thessaly,”", "\"제 나라의 북쪽, 테살리아에,\" 그리스인이 이어 말했다."], ["the Greek proceeded to say, “there is a mountain famous as the home of the gods, where Theus, whom my countrymen believe supreme, has his abode; Olympus is its name.", "\"신들의 거처로 이름난 산이 하나 있습니다. 제 동포들이 최고로 받드는 테우스가 머무는 곳이지요. 올림포스라는 이름의 산입니다."], ["Thither I betook myself.", "저는 그곳으로 갔습니다."], ["I found a cave in a hill where the mountain, coming from the west, bends to the southeast; there I dwelt, giving myself up to meditation—no, I gave myself up to waiting for what every breath was a prayer—for revelation.", "서쪽에서 뻗어 와 남동쪽으로 굽어지는 자리의 한 언덕에서 동굴을 찾아냈습니다. 거기서 저는 명상에 몸을 맡기며 살았습니다. 아니, 숨 쉬는 매순간이 기도였던 것, 곧 계시를 기다리는 일에 저 자신을 맡겼습니다."], ["Believing in God, invisible yet supreme, I also believed it possible so to yearn for him with all my soul that he would take compassion and give me answer.”", "보이지 않으나 지극히 높으신 하느님을 믿었기에, 저는 또한 온 영혼을 다해 그분을 사모하면, 그분께서 가엾이 여겨 제게 응답해 주시리라 믿었습니다.\""]], [["“And he did—he did!” exclaimed the Hindoo, lifting his hands from the silken cloth upon his lap.", "\"그래서 응답하셨군요, 응답하셨어요!\" 힌두인이 무릎 위 비단 천에서 두 손을 들어 올리며 외쳤다."]], [["“Hear me, brethren,” said the Greek, calming himself with an effort.", "\"제 말을 들어 주십시오, 형제들이여.\" 그리스인이 애써 마음을 가라앉히며 말했다."], ["“The door of my hermitage looks over an arm of the sea, over the Thermaic Gulf.", "\"제 은둔처의 문은 바다의 한 갈래, 테르마이만을 굽어보고 있었습니다."], ["One day I saw a man flung overboard from a ship sailing by.", "어느 날 저는 지나가던 배에서 한 사람이 바다로 던져지는 것을 보았습니다."], ["He swam ashore. I received and took care of him.", "그는 헤엄쳐 뭍에 올랐습니다. 저는 그를 받아들여 돌보았습니다."], ["He was a Jew, learned in the history and laws of his people; and from him I came to know that the God of my prayers did indeed exist; and had been for ages their lawmaker, ruler, and king.", "그는 유대인으로, 제 민족의 역사와 율법에 밝은 사람이었습니다. 그에게서 저는 제 기도의 하느님이 정녕 존재하시며, 여러 시대에 걸쳐 그들의 율법 제정자요 통치자요 왕이셨음을 알게 되었습니다."], ["What was that but the Revelation I dreamed of?", "그것이 바로 제가 꿈꾸던 계시가 아니고 무엇이겠습니까?"], ["My faith had not been fruitless; God answered me!”", "제 믿음은 헛되지 않았으니, 하느님께서 제게 응답하신 것입니다!\""]], [["“As he does all who cry to him with such faith,”", "\"그런 믿음으로 그분께 부르짖는 모든 이에게 그러하시듯이 말입니다.\""], ["said the Hindoo.", "힌두인이 말했다."]], [["“But, alas!”", "\"하지만, 아아!\""], ["the Egyptian added, “how few are there wise enough to know when he answers them!”", "이집트인이 덧붙였다. \"그분이 언제 응답하시는지 알 만큼 지혜로운 사람이 얼마나 적은지요!\""]], [["“That was not all,”", "\"그게 다가 아니었습니다.\""], ["the Greek continued.", "그리스인이 말을 이었다."], ["“The man so sent to me told me more. He said the prophets who, in the ages which followed the first revelation, walked and talked with God, declared he would come again.", "\"제게 보내진 그 사람은 더 많은 것을 일러 주었습니다. 그는 첫 계시 이후의 여러 시대에 하느님과 함께 걸으며 말씀을 나눈 예언자들이, 그분께서 다시 오시리라 선언했다고 했습니다."], ["He gave me the names of the prophets, and from the sacred books quoted their very language.", "그는 제게 예언자들의 이름을 일러 주었고, 거룩한 책에서 그들의 말을 그대로 인용해 주었습니다."], ["He told me, further, that the second coming was at hand—was looked for momentarily in Jerusalem.”", "또한 그는 그 두 번째 오심이 가까웠다고, 예루살렘에서 곧 닥칠 일로 손꼽아 기다려지고 있다고 했습니다.\""]], [["The Greek paused, and the brightness of his countenance faded.", "그리스인이 말을 멈추자, 그의 얼굴에 어렸던 빛이 사그라들었다."]], [["“It is true,”", "\"사실입니다.\""], ["he said, after a little—“it is true the man told me that as God and the revelation of which he spoke had been for the Jews alone, so it would be again.", "잠시 후 그가 말했다. \"그 사람이 제게 이렇게 말한 것은 사실입니다. 하느님과, 그분이 말한 계시가 오직 유대인만을 위한 것이었듯이, 다시 오실 때에도 그러하리라고 말입니다."], ["He that was to come should be King of the Jews.", "오시기로 한 그분은 유대인의 왕이 되시리라는 것이었습니다."], ["‘Had he nothing for the rest of the world?’ I asked.", "'나머지 세상을 위해서는 아무것도 없습니까?' 제가 물었습니다. '없소.'"], ["‘No,’ was the answer, given in a proud voice—‘No, we are his chosen people.’ The answer did not crush my hope.", "자랑스러운 목소리로 돌아온 대답이었습니다. '없소, 우리는 그분의 택하신 백성이오.' 그 대답도 제 희망을 꺾지는 못했습니다."], ["Why should such a God limit his love and benefaction to one land, and, as it were, to one family?", "어찌하여 그런 하느님께서 당신의 사랑과 은혜를 한 땅에, 말하자면 한 가족에게만 한정하시겠습니까?"], ["I set my heart upon knowing.", "저는 알아내리라 마음먹었습니다."], ["At last I broke through the man’s pride, and found that his fathers had been merely chosen servants to keep the Truth alive, that the world might at last know it and be saved.", "마침내 저는 그 사람의 자긍심을 뚫고 들어가, 그의 조상들이 단지 진리를 살아 있게 지키도록 택함받은 종에 지나지 않았으며, 그리하여 세상이 마침내 그것을 알고 구원받게 하려 함이었음을 알아냈습니다."], ["When the Jew was gone, and I was alone again, I chastened my soul with a new prayer—that I might be permitted to see the King when he was come, and worship him.", "그 유대인이 떠나고 다시 홀로 남았을 때, 저는 새로운 기도로 제 영혼을 다잡았습니다. 그 왕이 오셨을 때 그분을 뵙고 경배하도록 허락해 달라는 기도였습니다."], ["One night I sat by the door of my cave trying to get nearer the mysteries of my existence, knowing which is to know God; suddenly, on the sea below me, or rather in the darkness that covered its face, I saw a star begin to burn; slowly it arose and drew nigh, and stood over the hill and above my door, so that its light shone full upon me.", "어느 밤, 저는 동굴 문가에 앉아 제 존재의 신비에, 곧 그것을 아는 것이 하느님을 아는 것인 그 신비에 더 가까이 다가가려 애쓰고 있었습니다. 그때 갑자기 제 아래 바다 위에서, 아니 그 얼굴을 덮은 어둠 속에서, 별 하나가 타오르기 시작하는 것을 보았습니다. 그 별은 천천히 떠올라 다가오더니, 언덕 위, 제 문 위에 멈춰 그 빛이 저를 가득 비추었습니다."], ["I fell down, and slept, and in my dream I heard a voice say:", "저는 엎드렸고, 잠이 들었으며, 꿈속에서 한 목소리를 들었습니다."]], [["“‘O Gaspar! Thy faith hath conquered!", "\"'오, 가스파르여! 너의 믿음이 이겼도다!"], ["Blessed art thou!", "복되도다 너여!"], ["With two others, come from the uttermost parts of the earth, thou shalt see Him that is promised, and be a witness for him, and the occasion of testimony in his behalf.", "땅끝에서 온 다른 두 사람과 더불어, 너는 약속된 그분을 뵙고, 그분의 증인이 되며, 그분을 위한 증언의 계기가 되리라."], ["In the morning arise, and go meet them, and keep trust in the Spirit that shall guide thee.’", "아침에 일어나, 그들을 만나러 가고, 너를 인도할 영을 끝까지 믿어라.'"]], [["“And in the morning I awoke with the Spirit as a light within me surpassing that of the sun.", "\"그리고 아침에 깨어났을 때, 그 영이 제 안에서 해보다도 더 밝은 빛으로 빛나고 있었습니다."], ["I put off my hermit’s garb, and dressed myself as of old.", "저는 은둔자의 옷을 벗고, 옛날처럼 차려입었습니다."], ["From a hiding-place I took the treasure which I had brought from the city.", "숨겨 둔 곳에서 도시에서 가져온 보물을 꺼냈습니다."], ["A ship went sailing past.", "배 한 척이 지나갔습니다."], ["I hailed it, was taken aboard, and landed at Antioch.", "저는 그 배를 불러 올라탔고, 안티오크에 내렸습니다."], ["There I bought the camel and his furniture.", "거기서 저는 낙타와 그 장구를 샀습니다."], ["Through the gardens and orchards that enamel the banks of the Orontes, I journeyed to Emesa, Damascus, Bostra, and Philadelphia; thence hither.", "오론테스 강기슭을 아름답게 수놓은 정원과 과수원을 지나, 저는 에메사와 다마스쿠스, 보스라와 필라델피아로 여행했고, 거기서 이곳으로 왔습니다."], ["And so, O brethren, you have my story. Let me now listen to you.”", "그리하여, 오 형제들이여, 이것이 제 이야기입니다. 이제 두 분의 이야기를 듣고자 합니다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["The Egyptian and the Hindoo looked at each other; the former waved his hand; the latter bowed, and began:", "이집트인과 힌두인이 서로를 바라보았다. 앞의 사람이 손을 들어 보이자, 뒤의 사람이 고개를 숙이고 입을 열었다."]], [["“Our brother has spoken well.", "\"우리 형제는 훌륭하게 말씀하셨소."], ["May my words be as wise.”", "내 말도 그만큼 슬기롭기를 바라오.\""]], [["He broke off, reflected a moment, then resumed:", "그는 말을 멈추고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 다시 이었다."]], [["“You may know me, brethren, by the name of Melchior.", "\"형제들이여, 나를 멜키오르라는 이름으로 알아 주시오."], ["I speak to you in a language which, if not the oldest in the world, was at least the soonest to be reduced to letters—I mean the Sanscrit of India.", "나는 지금 여러분에게 한 언어로 말하고 있는데, 이 언어는 세상에서 가장 오래된 것은 아닐지라도 적어도 가장 먼저 문자로 정리된 언어요. 인도의 산스크리트어를 두고 하는 말이오."], ["I am a Hindoo by birth.", "나는 태생이 힌두인이오."], ["My people were the first to walk in the fields of knowledge, first to divide them, first to make them beautiful.", "내 민족은 지식의 들판을 처음으로 거닌 자들이며, 그것을 처음으로 나누고, 처음으로 아름답게 가꾼 자들이오."], ["Whatever may hereafter befall, the four Vedas must live, for they are the primal fountains of religion and useful intelligence.", "앞으로 무슨 일이 닥치든, 네 권의 베다는 살아남아야 하오. 그것은 종교와 유익한 지혜의 원천이기 때문이오."], ["From them were derived the Upa-Vedas, which, delivered by Brahma, treat of medicine, archery, architecture, music, and the four-and-sixty mechanical arts; the Ved-Angas, revealed by inspired saints, and devoted to astronomy, grammar, prosody, pronunciation, charms and incantations, religious rites and ceremonies; the Up-Angas, written by the sage Vyasa, and given to cosmogony, chronology, and geography; therein also are the Ramayana and the Mahabharata, heroic poems, designed for the perpetuation of our gods and demi-gods.", "그로부터 우파베다가 비롯되었으니, 브라마가 전한 이 가르침은 의술과 궁술과 건축과 음악, 그리고 예순네 가지 기계 기예를 다루오. 또한 영감을 받은 성인들이 계시한 베단가가 있어 천문과 문법과 운율과 발음, 주문과 주술, 종교적 제의와 의식을 다루오. 그리고 현자 비야사가 쓴 우팡가가 있어 우주 발생과 연대기와 지리를 담았으며, 그 안에는 우리의 신들과 반신(半神)들을 길이 전하기 위해 지어진 영웅시 라마야나와 마하바라타도 들어 있소."], ["Such, O brethren, are the Great Shastras, or books of sacred ordinances.", "형제들이여, 이것들이 바로 위대한 샤스트라, 곧 거룩한 율법의 책들이오."], ["They are dead to me now; yet through all time they will serve to illustrate the budding genius of my race.", "이제 그것들은 내게 죽은 것이 되었으나, 길이길이 내 민족의 움트는 천재성을 증언하는 데 쓰일 것이오."], ["They were promises of quick perfection.", "그것들은 빠른 완성을 약속한 책이었소."], ["Ask you why the promises failed? Alas!", "그 약속이 어찌하여 어그러졌는지 묻겠소? 아!"], ["the books themselves closed all the gates of progress.", "그 책들 자체가 진보의 모든 문을 닫아 버렸소."], ["Under pretext of care for the creature, their authors imposed the fatal principle that a man must not address himself to discovery or invention, as Heaven had provided him all things needful.", "인간을 돌본다는 구실로, 그 저자들은 하늘이 인간에게 필요한 모든 것을 마련해 주었으니 인간은 발견이나 발명에 마음을 두어서는 안 된다는 치명적인 원리를 강요했소."], ["When that condition became a sacred law, the lamp of Hindoo genius was let down a well, where ever since it has lighted narrow walls and bitter waters.", "그 조건이 거룩한 율법이 되었을 때, 힌두 천재성의 등불은 우물 속으로 내려졌고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 좁은 벽과 쓰디쓴 물만을 비추어 왔소."]], [["“These allusions, brethren, are not from pride, as you will understand when I tell you that the Shastras teach a Supreme God called Brahm; also, that the Puranas, or sacred poems of the Up-Angas, tell us of Virtue and Good Works, and of the Soul.", "\"형제들이여, 이런 이야기는 자랑에서 나온 것이 아니오. 샤스트라가 브람이라 불리는 지고(至高)의 신을 가르친다는 것을 말씀드리면 이해하실 것이오. 또한 우팡가의 거룩한 시편인 푸라나는 우리에게 덕과 선행, 그리고 영혼에 관하여 일러 주오."], ["So, if my brother will permit the saying”—the speaker bowed deferentially to the Greek—“ages before his people were known, the two great ideas, God and the Soul, had absorbed all the forces of the Hindoo mind.", "그러니, 내 형제가 허락하신다면\"—그가 그리스인을 향해 공손히 고개를 숙였다—\"그의 민족이 알려지기 여러 시대 전에, 신과 영혼이라는 두 가지 위대한 관념이 이미 힌두인의 정신을 온통 사로잡고 있었소."], ["In further explanation let me say that Brahm is taught, by the same sacred books, as a Triad—Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva.", "좀 더 설명하자면, 같은 거룩한 책들은 브람을 삼위(三位), 곧 브라마와 비슈누와 시바로 가르치오."], ["Of these, Brahma is said to have been the author of our race; which, in course of creation, he divided into four castes.", "그 가운데 브라마는 우리 종족의 창시자로 일컬어지며, 창조의 과정에서 우리를 네 계급으로 나누었소."], ["First, he peopled the worlds below and the heavens above; next, he made the earth ready for terrestrial spirits; then from his mouth proceeded the Brahman caste, nearest in likeness to himself, highest and noblest, sole teachers of the Vedas, which at the same time flowed from his lips in finished state, perfect in all useful knowledge.", "먼저 그는 아래의 세계와 위의 하늘에 거주민을 두었고, 다음으로 땅을 지상의 영들이 살 수 있도록 마련했소. 그러고 나서 그의 입에서 브라만 계급이 나왔으니, 그를 가장 닮았으며 가장 높고 가장 고귀하고, 베다를 가르치는 유일한 자들이오. 그 베다 또한 같은 입술에서 완성된 모습으로, 온갖 유익한 지식을 두루 갖춘 채 흘러나왔소."], ["From his arms next issued the Kshatriya, or warriors; from his breast, the seat of life, came the Vaisya, or producers—shepherds, farmers, merchants; from his foot, in sign of degradation, sprang the Sudra, or serviles, doomed to menial duties for the other classes—serfs, domestics, laborers, artisans.", "다음으로 그의 팔에서는 크샤트리아, 곧 전사들이 나왔고, 생명의 자리인 그의 가슴에서는 바이샤, 곧 생산자들—목자와 농부와 상인—이 나왔으며, 천함의 표시로 그의 발에서는 수드라, 곧 종복들이 솟아났으니, 이들은 다른 계급들을 위한 비천한 일에 매인 자들—농노와 하인과 노동자와 장인—이오."], ["Take notice, further, that the law, so born with them, forbade a man of one caste becoming a member of another; the Brahman could not enter a lower order; if he violated the laws of his own grade, he became an outcast, lost to all but outcasts like himself.”", "더 유념할 것은, 그렇게 그들과 함께 생겨난 율법이 한 계급의 사람이 다른 계급의 일원이 되는 것을 금했다는 점이오. 브라만은 더 낮은 계급으로 들어갈 수 없으며, 만일 자기 계급의 율법을 어기면 추방자가 되어 자기 같은 추방자들 말고는 모두에게 버림받소.\""]], [["At this point, the imagination of the Greek, flashing forward upon all the consequences of such a degradation, overcame his eager attention, and he exclaimed, “In such a state, O brethren, what mighty need of a loving God!”", "이 대목에 이르러, 그리스인의 상상력은 그러한 추락이 가져올 온갖 결과를 향해 번뜩 내달려, 열렬히 기울이던 주의를 압도했다. 그가 외쳤다. \"형제들이여, 그런 처지에서 사랑의 신이 얼마나 절실히 필요하겠소!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["added the Egyptian, “of a loving God like ours.”", "이집트인이 덧붙였다. \"우리의 신과 같은 사랑의 신이 말이오.\""]], [["The brows of the Hindoo knit painfully; when the emotion was spent, he proceeded, in a softened voice.", "힌두인의 이마가 괴롭게 찌푸려졌다. 그 감정이 가라앉자, 그는 부드러워진 목소리로 말을 이었다."]], [["“I was born a Brahman.", "\"나는 브라만으로 태어났소."], ["My life, consequently, was ordered down to its least act, its last hour.", "그리하여 내 삶은 가장 사소한 행동, 마지막 시간에 이르기까지 모두 정해져 있었소."], ["My first draught of nourishment; the giving me my compound name; taking me out the first time to see the sun; investing me with the triple thread by which I became one of the twice-born; my induction into the first order—were all celebrated with sacred texts and rigid ceremonies.", "처음으로 받은 한 모금의 양식, 합성된 이름을 받는 일, 처음으로 밖으로 나가 해를 보는 일, 세 겹의 실을 두름으로써 두 번 태어난 자의 하나가 되는 일, 첫째 계급으로 들어가는 입문—이 모두가 거룩한 경문과 엄격한 의식으로 기려졌소."], ["I might not walk, eat, drink, or sleep without danger of violating a rule.", "나는 걷거나 먹거나 마시거나 잠을 잘 때조차 규율을 어길 위험 없이는 그리할 수 없었소."], ["And the penalty, O brethren, the penalty was to my soul!", "그리고 그 벌은, 형제들이여, 그 벌은 내 영혼에 미치는 것이었소!"], ["According to the degrees of omission, my soul went to one of the heavens—Indra’s the lowest, Brahma’s the highest; or it was driven back to become the life of a worm, a fly, a fish, or a brute.", "어김의 정도에 따라 내 영혼은 여러 하늘 가운데 하나로 갔으니—가장 낮은 곳은 인드라의 하늘이요, 가장 높은 곳은 브라마의 하늘이오—혹은 다시 쫓겨나 벌레나 파리나 물고기나 짐승의 생명이 되었소."], ["The reward for perfect observance was Beatitude, or absorption into the being of Brahm, which was not existence as much as absolute rest.”", "완벽하게 지킨 데 대한 보상은 지복(至福), 곧 브람의 존재 안으로 흡수되는 것이었으니, 그것은 존재라기보다 차라리 절대적인 안식이었소.\""]], [["The Hindoo gave himself a moment’s thought; proceeding, he said: “The part of a Brahman’s life called the first order is his student life.", "힌두인은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말을 이었다. \"브라만의 삶에서 첫째 계급이라 불리는 부분은 학생의 삶이오."], ["When I was ready to enter the second order—that is to say, when I was ready to marry and become a householder—I questioned everything, even Brahm; I was a heretic.", "내가 둘째 계급으로 들어갈 채비가 되었을 때—다시 말해 혼인하여 가장이 될 채비가 되었을 때—나는 모든 것을, 심지어 브람까지도 의심했소. 나는 이단자였소."], ["From the depths of the well I had discovered a light above, and yearned to go up and see what all it shone upon.", "우물 깊은 곳에서 나는 위쪽의 빛을 발견했고, 올라가서 그 빛이 무엇을 비추는지 보고 싶어 갈망했소."], ["At last—ah, with what years of toil!—I stood in the perfect day, and beheld the principle of life, the element of religion, the link between the soul and God—Love!”", "마침내—아, 얼마나 오랜 세월의 수고 끝이었던가!—나는 완전한 대낮 속에 서서, 생명의 원리이며 종교의 본질이며 영혼과 신을 잇는 고리인 것을 보았소. 바로 사랑이었소!\""]], [["The shrunken face of the good man kindled visibly, and he clasped his hands with force.", "선한 이의 야윈 얼굴이 눈에 띄게 환해졌고, 그는 두 손을 힘주어 맞잡았다."], ["A silence ensued, during which the others looked at him, the Greek through tears.", "침묵이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 다른 이들은 그를 바라보았고, 그리스인은 눈물 너머로 그를 보았다."], ["At length he resumed:", "이윽고 그가 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“The happiness of love is in action; its test is what one is willing to do for others.", "\"사랑의 행복은 행함에 있고, 그 시험은 남을 위해 무엇을 기꺼이 하려느냐에 있소."], ["I could not rest.", "나는 쉴 수가 없었소."], ["Brahm had filled the world with so much wretchedness.", "브람은 세상을 그토록 많은 비참함으로 채워 놓았소."], ["The Sudra appealed to me, so did the countless devotees and victims.", "수드라가 내게 호소했고, 헤아릴 수 없는 신도들과 희생자들도 그러했소."], ["The island of Ganga Lagor lies where the sacred waters of the Ganges disappear in the Indian Ocean.", "강가 라고르 섬은 갠지스의 거룩한 물이 인도양으로 사라지는 곳에 있소."], ["Thither I betook myself.", "나는 그곳으로 갔소."], ["In the shade of the temple built there to the sage Kapila, in a union of prayers with the disciples whom the sanctified memory of the holy man keeps around his house, I thought to find rest.", "거기 현자 카필라를 기려 세운 사원의 그늘에서, 그 거룩한 분의 성스러운 기억이 그의 집 둘레에 머물게 한 제자들과 기도를 함께하며, 나는 안식을 찾으리라 여겼소."], ["But twice every year came pilgrimages of Hindoos seeking the purification of the waters.", "그러나 해마다 두 차례씩 힌두인들의 순례가 이어졌으니, 그 물의 정화를 구하는 자들이었소."], ["Their misery strengthened my love.", "그들의 비참함이 내 사랑을 더 굳세게 했소."], ["Against its impulse to speak I clenched my jaws; for one word against Brahm or the Triad or the Shastras would doom me; one act of kindness to the outcast Brahmans who now and then dragged themselves to die on the burning sands—a blessing said, a cup of water given—and I became one of them, lost to family, country, privileges, caste.", "말하려는 충동에 맞서 나는 이를 악물었소. 브람이나 삼위나 샤스트라에 거스르는 한마디면 나는 파멸할 터였고, 이따금 타는 모래밭으로 기어 나와 죽어 가는 추방된 브라만들에게 베푸는 단 한 번의 친절—축복의 말 한마디, 물 한 잔—이면 나도 그들 가운데 하나가 되어 가족과 나라와 특권과 계급을 잃을 터였소."], ["The love conquered!", "사랑이 이겼소!"], ["I spoke to the disciples in the temple; they drove me out.", "나는 사원의 제자들에게 말했고, 그들은 나를 내쫓았소."], ["I spoke to the pilgrims; they stoned me from the island.", "나는 순례자들에게 말했고, 그들은 나를 돌로 쳐 섬에서 몰아냈소."], ["On the highways I attempted to preach; my hearers fled from me, or sought my life.", "큰길에서 나는 설교를 하려 했으나, 듣던 이들은 내게서 달아나거나 내 목숨을 노렸소."], ["In all India, finally, there was not a place in which I could find peace or safety—not even among the outcasts, for, though fallen, they were still believers in Brahm.", "끝내 온 인도에는 내가 평안이나 안전을 찾을 곳이 한 군데도 없었소—추방자들 사이에서조차 그러했으니, 그들은 비록 타락했어도 여전히 브람의 신자들이었소."], ["In my extremity, I looked for a solitude in which to hide from all but God.", "막다른 처지에서 나는 신 말고는 모든 것으로부터 몸을 숨길 고독을 찾았소."], ["I followed the Ganges to its source, far up in the Himalayas.", "나는 갠지스를 거슬러 그 발원지까지, 저 멀리 히말라야 높은 곳까지 따라갔소."], ["When I entered the pass at Hurdwar, where the river, in unstained purity, leaps to its course through the muddy lowlands, I prayed for my race, and thought myself lost to them forever.", "더럽혀지지 않은 순수함을 지닌 강물이 진흙투성이 저지대를 향해 그 물길로 뛰어드는 하르드와르의 협곡으로 들어섰을 때, 나는 내 민족을 위해 기도했고, 그들로부터 영영 멀어졌다고 여겼소."], ["Through gorges, over cliffs, across glaciers, by peaks that seemed star-high, I made my way to the Lang Tso, a lake of marvellous beauty, asleep at the feet of the Tise Gangri, the Gurla, and the Kailas Parbot, giants which flaunt their crowns of snow everlastingly in the face of the sun.", "골짜기를 지나고 절벽을 넘고 빙하를 건너고 별처럼 높아 보이는 봉우리들을 거쳐, 나는 랑 초에 이르렀으니, 그것은 더없이 아름다운 호수로, 티세 강그리와 구를라와 카일라스 파르보트의 발치에서 잠들어 있었소. 이 거인들은 해를 마주하고 영원토록 눈의 왕관을 뽐내고 있소."], ["There, in the centre of the earth, where the Indus, Ganges, and Brahmapootra rise to run their different courses; where mankind took up their first abode, and separated to replete the world, leaving Balk, the mother of cities, to attest the great fact; where Nature, gone back to its primeval condition, and secure in its immensities, invites the sage and the exile, with promise of safety to the one and solitude to the other—there I went to abide alone with God, praying, fasting, waiting for death.”", "그곳, 인더스와 갠지스와 브라마푸트라가 솟아올라 저마다 다른 물길로 흘러가는 곳, 인류가 처음으로 거처를 정했다가 세상을 채우려 흩어진 곳, 도시들의 어머니인 발크를 남겨 그 위대한 사실을 증언하게 한 곳, 자연이 태초의 모습으로 돌아가 그 광대함 속에 안전히 자리하여, 현자에게는 안전을 약속하고 추방자에게는 고독을 약속하며 그들을 부르는 곳—그곳으로 나는 가서 신과 더불어 홀로 머물며, 기도하고 금식하며 죽음을 기다렸소.\""]], [["Again the voice fell, and the bony hands met in a fervent clasp.", "다시 목소리가 잦아들었고, 앙상한 두 손이 간절히 맞잡혔다."]], [["“One night I walked by the shores of the lake, and spoke to the listening silence, ‘When will God come and claim his own?", "\"어느 날 밤 나는 호숫가를 걸으며, 귀 기울이는 정적을 향해 말했소. '하느님은 언제 오셔서 당신의 것을 거두실까?"], ["Is there to be no redemption?’ Suddenly a light began to glow tremulously out on the water; soon a star arose, and moved towards me, and stood overhead.", "구원은 끝내 없는 것인가?' 그때 갑자기 물 위로 한 줄기 빛이 떨리며 빛나기 시작했소. 이내 별 하나가 떠올라 내게로 다가오더니 머리 위에 멈추었소."], ["The brightness stunned me.", "그 밝음에 나는 정신이 아득해졌소."], ["While I lay upon the ground, I heard a voice of infinite sweetness say, ‘Thy love hath conquered.", "내가 땅에 엎드려 있는 동안, 한없이 감미로운 목소리가 들려왔소. '네 사랑이 이겼노라."], ["Blessed art thou, O son of India!", "인도의 아들이여, 너는 복되도다!"], ["The redemption is at hand.", "구원이 가까이 왔노라."], ["With two others, from far quarters of the earth, thou shalt see the Redeemer, and be a witness that he hath come.", "너는 땅의 먼 곳에서 온 다른 두 사람과 더불어 구세주를 보게 되리니, 그분이 오셨음을 증언하는 자가 되리라."], ["In the morning arise, and go meet them; and put all thy trust in the Spirit which shall guide thee.’", "아침에 일어나 그들을 맞으러 가라. 그리고 너를 인도할 영(靈)께 너의 온 믿음을 두어라.'"]], [["“And from that time the light has stayed with me; so I knew it was the visible presence of the Spirit.", "\"그때부터 그 빛이 나와 함께 머물렀으니, 나는 그것이 그 영의 보이는 임재임을 알았소."], ["In the morning I started to the world by the way I had come.", "아침에 나는 왔던 길을 따라 세상으로 출발했소."], ["In a cleft of the mountain I found a stone of vast worth, which I sold in Hurdwar.", "산의 갈라진 틈에서 나는 값이 어마어마한 보석 하나를 찾아내어, 하르드와르에서 그것을 팔았소."], ["By Lahore, and Cabool, and Yezd, I came to Ispahan.", "라호르와 카불과 야즈드를 거쳐 나는 이스파한에 이르렀소."], ["There I bought the camel, and thence was led to Bagdad, not waiting for caravans.", "그곳에서 낙타를 샀고, 거기서 다시 바그다드로 인도되었으니, 대상(隊商)을 기다리지도 않았소."], ["Alone I traveled, fearless, for the Spirit was with me, and is with me yet.", "나는 홀로, 두려움 없이 길을 갔으니, 그 영이 나와 함께 있었고 지금도 함께 있기 때문이오."], ["What glory is ours, O brethren!", "형제들이여, 우리의 영광이 얼마나 큰가!"], ["We are to see the Redeemer—to speak to him—to worship him! I am done.”", "우리는 구세주를 보게 되었으니—그분께 말씀드리고—그분께 경배하게 되었소! 내 이야기는 다 마쳤소.\""]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["The vivacious Greek broke forth in expressions of joy and congratulations; after which the Egyptian said, with characteristic gravity:", "활기 넘치는 그리스인은 기쁨과 축하의 말을 쏟아냈다. 그 뒤를 이어 이집트인이 특유의 진중함으로 말했다."]], [["“I salute you, my brother.", "\"형제여, 그대에게 인사를 드립니다."], ["You have suffered much, and I rejoice in your triumph.", "그대는 많은 고난을 겪었고, 나는 그대의 승리를 기뻐합니다."], ["If you are both pleased to hear me, I will now tell you who I am, and how I came to be called.", "두 분이 기꺼이 내 말을 들어 주신다면, 이제 내가 누구이며 어떻게 부름을 받았는지를 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Wait for me a moment.”", "잠시만 기다려 주십시오.\""]], [["He went out and tended the camels; coming back, he resumed his seat.", "그는 밖으로 나가 낙타들을 돌보았다. 돌아온 그는 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Your words, brethren, were of the Spirit,”", "\"형제들이여, 그대들의 말은 성령에게서 나온 것이었습니다.\""], ["he said, in commencement; “and the Spirit gives me to understand them.", "그가 말문을 열었다. \"그리고 그 성령이 나로 하여금 그 말을 이해하게 해 줍니다."], ["You each spoke particularly of your countries; in that there was a great object, which I will explain; but to make the interpretation complete, let me first speak of myself and my people.", "그대들은 저마다 자기 나라를 특별히 이야기하셨습니다. 거기에는 커다란 뜻이 담겨 있으니, 내가 그것을 풀어 드리겠습니다. 그러나 그 풀이를 온전케 하려면, 먼저 나 자신과 내 민족에 대해 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["I am Balthasar the Egyptian.”", "나는 이집트인 발타사르입니다.\""]], [["The last words were spoken quietly, but with so much dignity that both listeners bowed to the speaker.", "마지막 말은 차분히 나왔으나 그 위엄이 어찌나 컸던지, 듣고 있던 두 사람은 그에게 머리를 숙였다."]], [["“There are many distinctions I might claim for my race,”", "\"내 민족을 위해 내세울 만한 영예는 많습니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he continued; “but I will content myself with one. History began with us.", "\"그러나 그중 하나로 만족하겠습니다. 역사는 우리에게서 비롯되었습니다."], ["We were the first to perpetuate events by records kept.", "우리는 기록을 남겨 사건을 후세에 전한 최초의 민족입니다."], ["So we have no traditions; and instead of poetry, we offer you certainty.", "그러니 우리에게는 전설이 없으며, 우리는 시(詩) 대신 확실함을 그대들에게 바칩니다."], ["On the facades of palaces and temples, on obelisks, on the inner walls of tombs, we wrote the names of our kings, and what they did; and to the delicate papyri we intrusted the wisdom of our philosophers and the secrets of our religion—all the secrets but one, whereof I will presently speak.", "궁전과 신전의 정면에, 오벨리스크에, 무덤의 안벽에, 우리는 우리 왕들의 이름과 그들이 한 일을 새겼습니다. 그리고 섬세한 파피루스에는 우리 철학자들의 지혜와 우리 종교의 비밀을 맡겼습니다. 다만 한 가지 비밀은 예외였으니, 그것은 곧 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Older than the Vedas of Para-Brahm or the Up-Angas of Vyasa, O Melchior; older than the songs of Homer or the metaphysics of Plato, O my Gaspar; older than the sacred books or kings of the people of China, or those of Siddartha, son of the beautiful Maya; older than the Genesis of Mosche the Hebrew—oldest of human records are the writings of Menes, our first king.”", "멜키오르여, 그것은 파라브라흐마의 베다나 비아사의 우팡가스보다 오래되었으며, 나의 가스파르여, 호메로스의 노래나 플라톤의 형이상학보다 오래되었고, 중국 백성의 거룩한 책이나 왕들의 기록보다, 아름다운 마야의 아들 싯다르타의 기록보다 오래되었으며, 히브리인 모셰의 창세기보다 오래되었습니다. 인간이 남긴 가장 오래된 기록은 우리의 첫 왕 메네스의 글입니다.\""], ["Pausing an instant, he fixed his large eves kindly upon the Greek, saying, “In the youth of Hellas, who, O Gaspar, were the teachers of her teachers?”", "그는 잠시 말을 멈추고, 크고 검은 눈을 그리스인에게 다정히 고정한 채 말했다. \"헬라스의 젊은 시절에, 오 가스파르여, 그 스승들의 스승은 누구였습니까?\""]], [["The Greek bowed, smiling.", "그리스인은 미소 지으며 머리를 숙였다."]], [["“By those records,”", "\"그 기록들로 우리는 압니다.\""], ["Balthasar continued, “we know that when the fathers came from the far East, from the region of the birth of the three sacred rivers, from the centre of the earth—the Old Iran of which you spoke, O Melchior—came bringing with them the history of the world before the Flood, and of the Flood itself, as given to the Aryans by the sons of Noah, they taught God, the Creator and the Beginning, and the Soul, deathless as God.", "발타사르가 말을 이었다. \"조상들이 머나먼 동방에서, 세 거룩한 강이 발원하는 곳에서, 대지의 한복판에서—멜키오르여, 그대가 말한 그 옛 이란에서—왔다는 것을. 그들은 노아의 아들들이 아리아인에게 전해 준 대홍수 이전 세계의 역사와 대홍수 자체의 역사를 가지고 와서, 창조주이자 시초이신 하느님과, 하느님처럼 죽지 않는 영혼을 가르쳤습니다."], ["When the duty which calls us now is happily done, if you choose to go with me, I will show you the sacred library of our priesthood; among others, the Book of the Dead, in which is the ritual to be observed by the soul after Death has despatched it on its journey to judgment.", "지금 우리를 부르는 의무를 무사히 마친 뒤, 그대들이 나와 함께 가기를 원한다면, 나는 우리 사제단의 거룩한 서고를 보여 드리겠습니다. 그중에는 사자의 서(書)도 있으니, 그것은 죽음이 영혼을 심판의 길로 떠나보낸 뒤 영혼이 지켜야 할 의식을 담고 있습니다."], ["The ideas—God and the Immortal Soul—were borne to Mizraim over the desert, and by him to the banks of the Nile.", "하느님과 불멸의 영혼이라는 그 사상은 사막을 건너 미즈라임에게 전해졌고, 그를 통해 나일강 기슭에 이르렀습니다."], ["They were then in their purity, easy of understanding, as what God intends for our happiness always is; so, also, was the first worship—a song and a prayer natural to a soul joyous, hopeful, and in love with its Maker.”", "그때 그 사상은 순수한 상태였고, 이해하기 쉬웠으니, 우리의 행복을 위한 하느님의 뜻이 늘 그러하듯 그러했습니다. 최초의 예배 또한 그러했으니, 기쁘고 희망에 차 자기 창조주를 사랑하는 영혼에게 자연스레 우러나는 노래 한 가락, 기도 한 마디였습니다.\""]], [["Here the Greek threw up his hands, exclaiming, “Oh!", "여기서 그리스인이 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"오!"], ["the light deepens within me!”", "내 안의 빛이 깊어집니다!\""]], [["“And in me!” said the Hindoo, with equal fervor.", "\"내 안에서도!\" 힌두인도 똑같은 열정으로 말했다."]], [["The Egyptian regarded them benignantly, then went on, saying, “Religion is merely the law which binds man to his Creator: in purity it has but these elements—God, the Soul, and their Mutual Recognition; out of which, when put in practise, spring Worship, Love, and Reward.", "이집트인은 그들을 인자하게 바라본 다음, 말을 이어 갔다. \"종교란 다만 사람을 그 창조주에게 묶어 주는 법(法)입니다. 순수한 상태에서는 오직 이 요소들만을 갖습니다—하느님, 영혼, 그리고 둘 사이의 서로 알아봄."], ["This law, like all others of divine origin—like that, for instance, which binds the earth to the sun—was perfected in the beginning by its Author.", "그것이 실천에 옮겨질 때 예배와 사랑과 보상이 솟아납니다. 이 법은, 신적인 기원을 지닌 다른 모든 법과 마찬가지로—이를테면 대지를 태양에 묶어 두는 법처럼—그 창조자에 의해 태초에 완성되었습니다."], ["Such, my brothers, was the religion of the first family; such was the religion of our father Mizraim, who could not have been blind to the formula of creation, nowhere so discernible as in the first faith and the earliest worship.", "형제들이여, 최초의 가족이 지닌 종교가 바로 그러했고, 우리 조상 미즈라임의 종교가 그러했습니다. 그는 창조의 형식에 결코 눈멀 수 없었으니, 그 형식은 최초의 신앙과 가장 이른 예배에서만큼 또렷이 드러나는 곳이 없습니다."], ["Perfection is God; simplicity is perfection.", "완전함은 곧 하느님이요, 단순함은 곧 완전함입니다."], ["The curse of curses is that men will not let truths like these alone.”", "저주 중의 저주는, 사람들이 이러한 진리들을 그대로 내버려 두지 않는다는 것입니다.\""]], [["He stopped, as if considering in what manner to continue.", "그는 어떻게 말을 이어 갈지 헤아리듯 멈추었다."]], [["“Many nations have loved the sweet waters of the Nile,”", "\"많은 민족이 나일강의 단물을 사랑했습니다.\""], ["he said next; “the Ethiopian, the Pali-Putra, the Hebrew, the Assyrian, the Persian, the Macedonian, the Roman—of whom all, except the Hebrew, have at one time or another been its masters.", "그가 다음으로 말했다. \"에티오피아인, 팔리푸트라인, 히브리인, 아시리아인, 페르시아인, 마케도니아인, 로마인—히브리인을 빼고는 이들 모두가 한때 그 강의 주인이었습니다."], ["So much coming and going of peoples corrupted the old Mizraimic faith.", "이토록 많은 민족이 오고 가니 오래된 미즈라임 신앙은 더럽혀졌습니다."], ["The Valley of Palms became a Valley of Gods.", "종려나무의 골짜기는 신들의 골짜기가 되었습니다."], ["The Supreme One was divided into eight, each personating a creative principle in nature, with Ammon-Re at the head.", "지존하신 한 분이 여덟으로 나뉘었으니, 저마다 자연의 창조 원리 하나씩을 의인화하고, 그 우두머리에는 암몬레가 섰습니다."], ["Then Isis and Osiris, and their circle, representing water, fire, air, and other forces, were invented.", "그러고는 물과 불과 공기와 그 밖의 힘들을 나타내는 이시스와 오시리스와 그 무리가 만들어졌습니다."], ["Still the multiplication went on until we had another order, suggested by human qualities, such as strength, knowledge, love, and the like.”", "그래도 증식은 멈추지 않아, 마침내 힘과 지식과 사랑 따위의 인간적 자질에서 비롯된 또 다른 무리가 생겨났습니다.\""]], [["“In all which there was the old folly!”", "\"그 모든 것에 그 오래된 어리석음이 깃들어 있었군요!\""], ["cried the Greek, impulsively.", "그리스인이 충동적으로 외쳤다."], ["“Only the things out of reach remain as they came to us.”", "\"손이 닿지 않는 것들만이 우리에게 온 그대로 남아 있습니다.\""]], [["The Egyptian bowed, and proceeded:", "이집트인은 머리를 숙이고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Yet a little further, O my brethren, a little further, before I come to myself.", "\"조금만 더, 오 형제들이여, 조금만 더 가겠습니다, 나 자신에 이르기 전에."], ["What we go to will seem all the holier of comparison with what is and has been.", "우리가 가서 만날 것은, 지금 있는 것과 지금까지 있었던 것에 견주어 한층 더 거룩해 보일 것입니다."], ["The records show that Mizraim found the Nile in possession of the Ethiopians, who were spread thence through the African desert; a people of rich, fantastic genius, wholly given to the worship of nature.", "기록에 따르면 미즈라임은 에티오피아인이 나일강을 차지하고 있는 것을 발견했으니, 그들은 그곳에서부터 아프리카 사막 전역으로 퍼져 있었습니다. 풍요롭고 환상적인 천재성을 지닌 민족으로, 오로지 자연 숭배에 몸을 바친 자들이었습니다."], ["The Poetic Persian sacrificed to the sun, as the completest image of Ormuzd, his God; the devout children of the far East carved their deities out of wood and ivory; but the Ethiopian, without writing, without books, without mechanical faculty of any kind, quieted his soul by the worship of animals, birds, and insects, holding the cat sacred to Re, the bull to Isis, the beetle to Pthah.", "시적인 페르시아인은 자기 신 오르무즈드의 가장 온전한 형상으로서 태양에 제물을 바쳤고, 머나먼 동방의 경건한 자녀들은 나무와 상아로 자기 신들을 깎아 냈습니다. 그러나 에티오피아인은 글도, 책도, 어떤 종류의 기계적 재능도 없이, 짐승과 새와 벌레를 숭배하여 제 영혼을 달랬으니, 고양이를 레에게, 황소를 이시스에게, 풍뎅이를 프타에게 바쳐 거룩히 여겼습니다."], ["A long struggle against their rude faith ended in its adoption as the religion of the new empire.", "그들의 거친 신앙에 맞선 오랜 투쟁은 그것을 새 제국의 종교로 받아들이는 것으로 끝났습니다."], ["Then rose the mighty monuments that cumber the river-bank and the desert—obelisk, labyrinth, pyramid, and tomb of king, blent with tomb of crocodile.", "그리하여 강기슭과 사막을 가득 메운 거대한 기념물들이 솟아올랐으니—오벨리스크와 미궁과 피라미드와 왕의 무덤이, 악어의 무덤과 뒤섞여 들어섰습니다."], ["Into such deep debasement, O brethren, the sons of the Aryan fell!”", "형제들이여, 아리아인의 자손들이 그토록 깊은 타락에 떨어졌던 것입니다!\""]], [["Here, for the first time, the calmness of the Egyptian forsook him: though his countenance remained impassive, his voice gave way.", "여기서 처음으로 이집트인의 침착함이 그를 떠났다. 그의 표정은 흔들림이 없었으나, 그 목소리가 무너졌다."]], [["“Do not too much despise my countrymen,”", "\"내 동포를 너무 멸시하지는 말아 주십시오.\""], ["he began again. “They did not all forget God.", "그가 다시 말문을 열었다. \"그들이 모두 하느님을 잊은 것은 아니었습니다."], ["I said awhile ago, you may remember, that to papyri we intrusted all the secrets of our religion except one; of that I will now tell you.", "조금 전에 내가 말씀드린 것을 기억하실 텐데, 한 가지를 빼고는 우리 종교의 모든 비밀을 파피루스에 맡겼다고 했습니다. 이제 그 한 가지를 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["We had as king once a certain Pharaoh, who lent himself to all manner of changes and additions.", "우리에게는 한때 어떤 파라오가 왕으로 있었으니, 그는 온갖 변화와 첨가에 몸을 바쳤습니다."], ["To establish the new system, he strove to drive the old entirely out of mind.", "새로운 체계를 세우려고, 그는 옛것을 사람들의 마음에서 완전히 몰아내려 애썼습니다."], ["The Hebrews then dwelt with us as slaves.", "그때 히브리인들은 노예로서 우리와 함께 살고 있었습니다."], ["They clung to their God; and when the persecution became intolerable, they were delivered in a manner never to be forgotten.", "그들은 자기네 하느님께 매달렸으며, 박해가 견딜 수 없게 되었을 때, 결코 잊지 못할 방식으로 구원을 받았습니다."], ["I speak from the records now.", "나는 이제 기록에서 말씀드립니다."], ["Mosche, himself a Hebrew, came to the palace, and demanded permission for the slaves, then millions in number, to leave the country.", "모셰는 스스로 히브리인이었는데, 궁전에 와서, 그때 수백만에 이르던 노예들이 그 나라를 떠나도록 허락해 달라고 요구했습니다."], ["The demand was in the name of the Lord God of Israel.", "그 요구는 이스라엘의 주 하느님의 이름으로 한 것이었습니다."], ["Pharaoh refused.", "파라오는 거절했습니다."], ["Hear what followed. First, all the water, that in the lakes and rivers, like that in the wells and vessels, turned to blood.", "그다음에 무슨 일이 따랐는지 들어 보십시오. 먼저, 모든 물이, 호수와 강의 물이며 우물과 그릇의 물이, 피로 변했습니다."], ["Yet the monarch refused.", "그래도 군주는 거절했습니다."], ["Then frogs came up and covered all the land.", "그러자 개구리가 올라와 온 땅을 뒤덮었습니다."], ["Still he was firm. Then Mosche threw ashes in the air, and a plague attacked the Egyptians.", "그래도 그는 굳건했습니다. 그러자 모셰가 재를 공중에 뿌리니, 역병이 이집트인들을 덮쳤습니다."], ["Next, all the cattle, except of the Hebrews, were struck dead.", "다음으로, 히브리인의 것을 빼고는 모든 가축이 쓰러져 죽었습니다."], ["Locusts devoured the green things of the valley.", "메뚜기가 골짜기의 푸른 것들을 갉아먹었습니다."], ["At noon the day was turned into a darkness so thick that lamps would not burn.", "한낮에 대낮이 어둠으로 바뀌니, 그 어둠이 어찌나 짙던지 등불도 타지 못했습니다."], ["Finally, in the night all the first-born of the Egyptians died; not even Pharaoh’s escaped. Then he yielded.", "마침내 밤이 되자 이집트인의 맏이가 모두 죽었으니, 파라오의 맏이조차 화를 면하지 못했습니다. 그제야 그는 굴복했습니다."], ["But when the Hebrews were gone he followed them with his army.", "그러나 히브리인들이 떠나자, 그는 군대를 이끌고 그들을 뒤쫓았습니다."], ["At the last moment the sea was divided, so that the fugitives passed it dry-shod.", "마지막 순간에 바다가 갈라져, 도망치는 자들이 마른 발로 그것을 건넜습니다."], ["When the pursuers drove in after them, the waves rushed back and drowned horse, foot, charioteers, and king.", "뒤쫓던 자들이 그들을 따라 몰려들자, 물결이 다시 밀려와 말과 보병과 전차병과 왕을 모두 빠뜨려 죽였습니다."], ["You spoke of revelation, my Gaspar—”", "가스파르여, 그대가 계시를 말씀하셨지요—\""]], [["The blue eyes of the Greek sparkled.", "그리스인의 파란 눈이 반짝였다."]], [["“I had the story from the Jew,”", "\"나는 그 이야기를 유대인에게서 들었습니다.\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“You confirm it, O Balthasar!”", "\"오 발타사르여, 그대가 그것을 확인해 주는군요!\""]], [["“Yes, but through me Egypt speaks, not Mosche.", "\"그렇습니다, 그러나 나를 통해 말하는 것은 이집트이지 모셰가 아닙니다."], ["I interpret the marbles.", "나는 대리석을 풀이하는 것입니다."], ["The priests of that time wrote in their way what they witnessed, and the revelation has lived.", "그 시대의 사제들이 자기들 방식으로 자기들이 목격한 것을 적었고, 그리하여 그 계시가 살아남았습니다."], ["So I come to the one unrecorded secret.", "그래서 나는 기록되지 않은 그 한 가지 비밀에 이릅니다."], ["In my country, brethren, we have, from the day of the unfortunate Pharaoh, always had two religions—one private, the other public; one of many gods, practised by the people; the other of one God, cherished only by the priesthood.", "형제들이여, 내 나라에서는 그 불행한 파라오의 시대부터 늘 두 가지 종교를 가져 왔습니다—하나는 사사로운 것이요, 다른 하나는 공적인 것이며, 하나는 많은 신을 섬겨 백성이 따르는 것이요, 다른 하나는 한 분 하느님을 섬겨 오직 사제단만이 소중히 간직한 것입니다."], ["Rejoice with me, O brothers!", "형제들이여, 나와 함께 기뻐하십시오!"], ["All the trampling by the many nations, all the harrowing by kings, all the inventions of enemies, all the changes of time, have been in vain.", "그 많은 민족의 짓밟음도, 왕들의 괴롭힘도, 원수들의 온갖 술책도, 세월의 온갖 변화도, 모두 헛되었습니다."], ["Like a seed under the mountains waiting its hour, the glorious Truth has lived; and this—this is its day!”", "산 밑에서 제 때를 기다리는 씨앗처럼, 영광스러운 진리는 살아남았으니, 바로 이날이—이날이 그 진리의 날입니다!\""]], [["The wasted frame of the Hindoo trembled with delight, and the Greek cried aloud,", "힌두인의 야윈 몸이 기쁨으로 떨렸고, 그리스인은 소리 높여 외쳤다."]], [["“It seems to me the very desert is singing.”", "\"내게는 바로 저 사막이 노래하는 것만 같습니다.\""]], [["From a gurglet of water near-by the Egyptian took a draught, and proceeded:", "이집트인은 가까이 있는 물주전자에서 한 모금 마시고는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I was born at Alexandria, a prince and a priest, and had the education usual to my class.", "\"나는 알렉산드리아에서 왕자이자 사제로 태어나, 내 계급에 으레 주어지는 교육을 받았습니다."], ["But very early I became discontented.", "그러나 아주 일찍부터 나는 불만을 품게 되었습니다."], ["Part of the faith imposed was that after death upon the destruction of the body, the soul at once began its former progression from the lowest up to humanity, the highest and last existence; and that without reference to conduct in the mortal life.", "강요된 신앙의 한 부분은, 죽음 뒤 육신이 무너지면 영혼이 곧장 가장 낮은 데서부터 가장 높고 마지막 존재인 인간에 이르기까지 그 옛 진보를 다시 시작한다는 것이었으며, 그것이 죽을 목숨의 삶에서 한 행실과는 아무 관계가 없다는 것이었습니다."], ["When I heard of the Persian’s Realm of Light, his Paradise across the bridge Chinevat, where only the good go, the thought haunted me; insomuch that in the day, as in the night, I brooded over the comparative ideas Eternal Transmigration and Eternal Life in Heaven.", "페르시아인의 빛의 나라, 곧 오직 선한 자만이 가는 친바트 다리 너머의 낙원에 대해 들었을 때, 그 생각이 나를 사로잡았습니다. 그리하여 낮에도 밤에도, 나는 영원한 윤회와 천국에서의 영원한 삶이라는, 서로 견주어지는 두 사상을 곱씹었습니다."], ["If, as my teacher taught, God was just, why was there no distinction between the good and the bad?", "내 스승이 가르친 대로 하느님이 의로우시다면, 어찌하여 선한 자와 악한 자 사이에 아무런 구별이 없는가?"], ["At length it became clear to me, a certainty, a corollary of the law to which I reduced pure religion, that death was only the point of separation at which the wicked are left or lost, and the faithful rise to a higher life; not the nirvana of Buddha, or the negative rest of Brahma, O Melchior; nor the better condition in hell, which is all of Heaven allowed by the Olympic faith, O Gaspar; but life—life active, joyous, everlasting—LIFE WITH GOD!", "마침내 내게는 분명해졌으니, 확실해졌으니, 내가 순수한 종교를 귀결시킨 그 법의 따름정리로서, 죽음이란 다만 갈라지는 지점일 뿐이라는 것이었습니다. 그 지점에서 악한 자는 버려지거나 길을 잃고, 신실한 자는 더 높은 삶으로 올라갑니다. 멜키오르여, 그것은 부처의 열반도, 브라흐마의 소극적인 안식도 아니요, 가스파르여, 올림포스 신앙이 허락하는 천국의 전부인 지옥 속의 더 나은 처지도 아니라, 삶입니다—활기차고 기쁘고 영원한 삶, 하느님과 함께하는 삶입니다!"], ["The discovery led to another inquiry.", "이 발견이 또 다른 물음으로 이어졌습니다."], ["Why should the Truth be longer kept a secret for the selfish solace of the priesthood?", "어찌하여 그 진리를 사제단의 이기적인 위안을 위해 더 오래 비밀로 간직해야 하는가?"], ["The reason for the suppression was gone.", "그것을 감추어야 할 이유는 사라졌습니다."], ["Philosophy had at least brought us toleration.", "철학이 적어도 우리에게 관용을 가져다주었습니다."], ["In Egypt we had Rome instead of Rameses.", "이집트에는 람세스 대신 로마가 있었습니다."], ["One day, in the Brucheium, the most splendid and crowded quarter of Alexandria, I arose and preached.", "어느 날, 알렉산드리아에서 가장 화려하고 붐비는 구역인 브루케이온에서, 나는 일어서서 설교했습니다."], ["The East and West contributed to my audience.", "동방과 서방이 내 청중을 이루었습니다."], ["Students going to the Library, priests from the Serapeion, idlers from the Museum, patrons of the race-course, countrymen from the Rhacotis—a multitude—stopped to hear me.", "도서관으로 가는 학생들, 세라페이온에서 온 사제들, 무세이온에서 나온 한량들, 경기장의 단골들, 라코티스에서 온 시골 사람들—한 무리가 멈춰 서서 내 말을 들었습니다."], ["I preached God, the Soul, Right and Wrong, and Heaven, the reward of a virtuous life.", "나는 하느님과 영혼과 옳고 그름과, 덕 있는 삶의 보상인 천국을 설교했습니다."], ["You, O Melchior, were stoned; my auditors first wondered, then laughed.", "멜키오르여, 그대는 돌에 맞았지만, 내 청중은 처음에는 놀라더니 이내 비웃었습니다."], ["I tried again; they pelted me with epigrams, covered my God with ridicule, and darkened my Heaven with mockery.", "나는 다시 시도했고, 그들은 경구로 나를 두들기고, 내 하느님을 조롱으로 뒤덮고, 내 천국을 비웃음으로 어둡게 했습니다."], ["Not to linger needlessly, I fell before them.”", "쓸데없이 길게 말하지 않겠으니, 나는 그들 앞에서 쓰러졌습니다.\""]], [["The Hindoo here drew a long sigh, as he said, “The enemy of man is man, my brother.”", "여기서 힌두인이 긴 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"사람의 원수는 사람입니다, 형제여.\""]], [["Balthasar lapsed into silence.", "발타사르는 침묵에 잠겼다."]], [["“I gave much thought to finding the cause of my failure, and at last succeeded,”", "\"나는 내 실패의 원인을 찾는 데 많은 생각을 쏟았고, 마침내 찾아냈습니다.\""], ["he said, upon beginning again.", "그가 다시 말문을 열며 말했다."], ["“Up the river, a day’s journey from the city, there is a village of herdsmen and gardeners.", "\"강을 거슬러, 도시에서 하룻길 떨어진 곳에, 목자와 정원사들의 마을이 하나 있습니다."], ["I took a boat and went there.", "나는 배를 타고 그곳에 갔습니다."], ["In the evening I called the people together, men and women, the poorest of the poor.", "저녁에 나는 사람들을 불러 모았으니, 남자와 여자, 가난한 자 중에서도 가장 가난한 자들이었습니다."], ["I preached to them exactly as I had preached in the Brucheium.", "나는 브루케이온에서 설교했던 것과 똑같이 그들에게 설교했습니다."], ["They did not laugh.", "그들은 비웃지 않았습니다."], ["Next evening I spoke again, and they believed and rejoiced, and carried the news abroad.", "이튿날 저녁 나는 다시 말했고, 그들은 믿고 기뻐하며, 그 소식을 멀리 퍼뜨렸습니다."], ["At the third meeting a society was formed for prayer.", "세 번째 모임에서는 기도를 위한 모임이 만들어졌습니다."], ["I returned to the city then.", "그제야 나는 도시로 돌아왔습니다."], ["Drifting down the river, under the stars, which never seemed so bright and so near, I evolved this lesson: To begin a reform, go not into the places of the great and rich; go rather to those whose cups of happiness are empty—to the poor and humble.", "일찍이 그토록 밝고 가깝게 보인 적 없던 별들 아래, 강을 따라 떠내려가며, 나는 이런 가르침을 얻었습니다. 개혁을 시작하려거든, 위대하고 부유한 자들의 자리로 가지 말라. 차라리 행복의 잔이 비어 있는 자들에게로 가라—가난하고 비천한 자들에게로."], ["And then I laid a plan and devoted my life.", "그러고 나서 나는 계획을 세우고 내 삶을 바쳤습니다."], ["As a first step, I secured my vast property, so that the income would be certain, and always at call for the relief of the suffering.", "첫걸음으로, 나는 내 막대한 재산을 확보하여, 그 수입이 확실하고 고통받는 자들을 구제하는 데 언제든 쓰일 수 있게 했습니다."], ["From that day, O brethren, I travelled up and down the Nile, in the villages, and to all the tribes, preaching One God, a righteous life, and reward in Heaven. I have done good—it does not become me to say how much.", "그날부터, 형제들이여, 나는 나일강을 오르내리며, 마을마다, 모든 부족을 찾아가, 한 분 하느님과, 의로운 삶과, 천국에서의 보상을 설교했습니다. 나는 선한 일을 했습니다—얼마나 많이 했는지를 내 입으로 말하는 것은 어울리지 않습니다."], ["I also know that part of the world to be ripe for the reception of Him we go to find.”", "또한 나는 세상의 그 부분이 우리가 찾아 나선 그분을 맞이할 만큼 무르익었음을 압니다.\""]], [["A flush suffused the swarthy cheek of the speaker; but he overcame the feeling, and continued:", "설교자의 거무스름한 뺨에 홍조가 번졌으나, 그는 그 감정을 누르고 말을 이었다."]], [["“The years so given, O my brothers, were troubled by one thought—When I was gone, what would become of the cause I had started?", "\"그렇게 바쳐진 세월은, 오 형제들이여, 한 가지 생각으로 괴로웠습니다—내가 떠나고 나면, 내가 시작한 그 대의는 어찌 될 것인가?"], ["Was it to end with me? I had dreamed many times of organization as a fitting crown for my work.", "그것이 나와 함께 끝나야 하는가? 나는 내 일에 걸맞은 면류관으로서의 조직을 여러 번 꿈꾸었습니다."], ["To hide nothing from you, I had tried to effect it, and failed.", "그대들에게 아무것도 숨기지 않겠으니, 나는 그것을 이루려 애썼으나 실패했습니다."], ["Brethren, the world is now in the condition that, to restore the old Mizraimic faith, the reformer must have a more than human sanction; he must not merely come in God’s name, he must have the proofs subject to his word; he must demonstrate all he says, even God.", "형제들이여, 세상은 지금 이런 형편에 있습니다. 옛 미즈라임 신앙을 되살리려면, 개혁자는 인간을 넘어서는 권능을 가져야 합니다. 그는 단지 하느님의 이름으로 와서는 안 되며, 자기 말에 종속된 증거를 지녀야 하고, 자기가 말하는 모든 것을, 심지어 하느님까지도 입증해 보여야 합니다."], ["So preoccupied is the mind with myths and systems; so much do false deities crowd every place—earth, air, sky; so have they become of everything a part, that return to the first religion can only be along bloody paths, through fields of persecution; that is to say, the converts must be willing to die rather than recant.", "사람들의 마음은 신화와 체계에 어찌나 사로잡혀 있는지, 거짓 신들이 모든 자리—땅과 공기와 하늘—를 어찌나 가득 메우고 있는지, 그것들이 만물의 한 부분이 되어 버렸으니, 최초의 종교로 돌아가는 길은 오직 피로 물든 길을 따라, 박해의 들판을 지나야만 갈 수 있습니다. 다시 말해, 개종자들은 신앙을 버리느니 차라리 죽기를 마다하지 않아야 합니다."], ["And who in this age can carry the faith of men to such a point but God himself?", "그런데 이 시대에 사람들의 신앙을 그러한 경지까지 이끌 수 있는 이가 하느님 자신 말고 누가 있겠습니까?"], ["To redeem the race—I do not mean to destroy it—to REDEEM the race, he must make himself once more manifest; HE MUST COME IN PERSON.”", "인류를 구속하려면—나는 인류를 멸하려는 것이 아닙니다—인류를 구속하려면, 그분은 다시 한번 스스로를 드러내셔야 합니다. 그분은 몸소 오셔야 합니다.\""]], [["Intense emotion seized the three.", "세 사람을 격렬한 감정이 사로잡았다."]], [["“Are we not going to find him?” exclaimed the Greek.", "\"우리가 그분을 찾으러 가는 것이 아닙니까?\" 그리스인이 외쳤다."]], [["“You understand why I failed in the attempt to organize,”", "\"내가 조직을 이루려는 시도에서 왜 실패했는지를 이제 그대들은 이해할 것입니다.\""], ["said the Egyptian, when the spell was past.", "그 황홀경이 가시자 이집트인이 말했다."], ["“I had not the sanction.", "\"내게는 권능이 없었습니다."], ["To know that my work must be lost made me intolerably wretched.", "내 일이 사라질 수밖에 없음을 안다는 것이 나를 견딜 수 없이 비참하게 만들었습니다."], ["I believed in prayer, and to make my appeals pure and strong, like you, my brethren, I went out of the beaten ways, I went where man had not been, where only God was.", "나는 기도를 믿었고, 내 호소를 순수하고 강하게 하려고, 형제들이여 그대들처럼, 나는 사람이 다닌 길을 벗어났습니다. 나는 사람이 가 본 적 없는 곳, 오직 하느님만 계신 곳으로 갔습니다."], ["Above the fifth cataract, above the meeting of rivers in Sennar, up the Bahr el Abiad, into the far unknown of Africa, I went.", "다섯 번째 폭포 너머로, 센나르에서 강들이 만나는 곳 너머로, 바르 엘 아비아드를 거슬러, 아프리카의 머나먼 미지의 땅으로, 나는 갔습니다."], ["There, in the morning, a mountain blue as the sky flings a cooling shadow wide over the western desert, and, with its cascades of melted snow, feeds a broad lake nestling at its base on the east.", "그곳에서는, 아침이 되면, 하늘처럼 푸른 산 하나가 서쪽 사막 위로 시원한 그늘을 넓게 드리우고, 녹은 눈이 폭포를 이루어, 그 동쪽 기슭에 깃든 너른 호수 하나를 먹입니다."], ["The lake is the mother of the great river.", "그 호수가 그 큰 강의 어머니입니다."], ["For a year and more the mountain gave me a home.", "일 년 남짓 그 산이 내게 보금자리가 되어 주었습니다."], ["The fruit of the palm fed my body, prayer my spirit.", "종려나무 열매가 내 몸을 먹였고, 기도가 내 영혼을 먹였습니다."], ["One night I walked in the orchard close by the little sea.", "어느 날 밤, 나는 그 작은 바다 가까이의 과수원을 거닐었습니다."], ["‘The world is dying.", "'세상이 죽어 갑니다."], ["When wilt thou come?", "그대는 언제 오시렵니까?"], ["Why may I not see the redemption, O God?’ So I prayed.", "어찌하여 나는 그 구속을 보지 못합니까, 오 하느님?' 나는 그렇게 기도했습니다."], ["The glassy water was sparkling with stars.", "유리 같은 물이 별들로 반짝이고 있었습니다."], ["One of them seemed to leave its place, and rise to the surface, where it became a brilliancy burning to the eyes.", "그중 하나가 제 자리를 떠나 수면 위로 떠오르는 듯하더니, 거기서 눈을 태울 듯한 광채가 되었습니다."], ["Then it moved towards me, and stood over my head, apparently in hand’s reach.", "그러고는 내게로 다가와 내 머리 위에 멈추었으니, 보아하니 손이 닿을 만큼 가까웠습니다."], ["I fell down and hid my face.", "나는 엎드려 얼굴을 가렸습니다."], ["A voice, not of the earth, said, ‘Thy good works have conquered.", "이 땅의 것이 아닌 목소리가 말했습니다. '너의 선한 일이 이겼노라."], ["Blessed art thou, O son of Mizraim!", "복되도다, 오 미즈라임의 아들이여!"], ["The redemption cometh.", "구속이 오느니라."], ["With two others, from the remotenesses of the world, thou shalt see the Saviour, and testify for him.", "세상의 머나먼 곳에서 온 다른 두 사람과 함께, 너는 구세주를 보고, 그를 위해 증언하리라."], ["In the morning arise, and go meet them.", "아침이 되거든 일어나, 그들을 맞으러 가라."], ["And when ye have all come to the holy city of Jerusalem, ask of the people, Where is he that is born King of the Jews?", "그리고 너희가 모두 거룩한 도성 예루살렘에 이르거든, 백성에게 물으라, 유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?"], ["for we have seen his star in the East and are sent to worship him.", "우리가 동방에서 그의 별을 보았고, 그에게 경배하러 보내심을 받았노라, 하라."], ["Put all thy trust in the Spirit which will guide thee.’", "너를 인도할 성령께 모든 믿음을 두라.'"]], [["“And the light became an inward illumination not to be doubted, and has stayed with me, a governor and a guide.", "\"그리고 그 빛은 의심할 수 없는 내면의 빛이 되어, 다스리는 자요 인도하는 자로서 내게 머물러 왔습니다."], ["It led me down the river to Memphis, where I made ready for the desert.", "그 빛이 나를 강을 따라 멤피스로 이끌었고, 그곳에서 나는 사막을 건널 채비를 했습니다."], ["I bought my camel, and came hither without rest, by way of Suez and Kufileh, and up through the lands of Moab and Ammon. God is with us, O my brethren!”", "나는 내 낙타를 샀고, 쉬지 않고, 수에즈와 쿠필레를 거쳐, 모압과 암몬의 땅을 거슬러 올라 이곳까지 왔습니다. 하느님이 우리와 함께 계십니다, 오 형제들이여!\""]], [["He paused, and thereupon, with a prompting not their own, they all arose, and looked at each other.", "그는 말을 멈추었고, 그러자 그들 자신의 것이 아닌 어떤 이끄심에 따라, 세 사람은 모두 일어나 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“I said there was a purpose in the particularity with which we described our people and their histories,”", "\"우리가 우리 민족과 그 역사를 그토록 자세히 묘사한 데에는 한 가지 뜻이 있다고 말씀드렸습니다.\""], ["so the Egyptian proceeded.", "이집트인이 그렇게 말을 이었다."], ["“He we go to find was called ‘King of the Jews;’ by that name we are bidden to ask for him.", "\"우리가 찾으러 가는 그분은 '유대인의 왕'이라 불렸으니, 우리는 그 이름으로 그분을 물으라는 명을 받았습니다."], ["But, now that we have met, and heard from each other, we may know him to be the Redeemer, not of the Jews alone, but of all the nations of the earth.", "그러나 이제 우리가 만나, 서로에게서 들었으니, 우리는 그분이 유대인만의 구세주가 아니라, 땅 위 모든 민족의 구세주이심을 알 수 있습니다."], ["The patriarch who survived the Flood had with him three sons, and their families, by whom the world was repeopled.", "대홍수에서 살아남은 족장은 세 아들과 그들의 가족을 거느렸고, 그들로 말미암아 세상에 다시 사람이 가득 찼습니다."], ["From the old Aryana-Vaejo, the well-remembered Region of Delight in the heart of Asia, they parted.", "아시아의 심장부에 있는, 잘 기억되는 환희의 땅, 그 옛 아리아나바에조에서 그들은 갈라졌습니다."], ["India and the far East received the children of the first; the descendant of the youngest, through the North, streamed into Europe; those of the second overflowed the deserts about the Red Sea, passing into Africa; and though most of the latter are yet dwellers in shifting tents, some of them became builders along the Nile.”", "인도와 머나먼 동방은 첫째의 자손을 받아들였고, 막내의 자손은 북쪽을 거쳐 유럽으로 흘러들었으며, 둘째의 자손은 홍해 주변의 사막으로 넘쳐흘러 아프리카로 들어갔습니다. 비록 그 둘째의 자손 대부분은 아직도 옮겨 다니는 천막에서 사는 자들이지만, 그중 일부는 나일강을 따라 건설하는 자가 되었습니다.\""]], [["By a simultaneous impulse the three joined hands.", "동시에 솟구친 충동에 따라 세 사람은 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Could anything be more divinely ordered?”", "\"이보다 더 신의 뜻에 따라 마련된 일이 어디 있겠습니까?\""], ["Balthasar continued.", "발타사르가 말을 이었다."], ["“When we have found the Lord, the brothers, and all the generations that have succeeded them, will kneel to him in homage with us.", "\"우리가 주님을 찾으면, 그 형제들과, 그들의 뒤를 이은 모든 세대가, 우리와 더불어 그분께 무릎 꿇어 경배할 것입니다."], ["And when we part to go our separate ways, the world will have learned a new lesson—that Heaven may be won, not by the sword, not by human wisdom, but by Faith, Love, and Good Works.”", "그리고 우리가 헤어져 저마다 제 길로 갈 때, 세상은 새로운 가르침을 배우게 될 것입니다—천국은 칼로도, 인간의 지혜로도 아니라, 믿음과 사랑과 선한 일로 얻을 수 있다는 가르침을.\""]], [["There was silence, broken by sighs and sanctified with tears; for the joy that filled them might not be stayed.", "침묵이 흘렀으니, 한숨으로 깨어지고 눈물로 거룩해진 침묵이었다. 그들을 가득 채운 기쁨을 막을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["It was the unspeakable joy of souls on the shores of the River of Life, resting with the Redeemed in God’s presence.", "그것은 생명의 강 기슭에 선 영혼들이, 구속받은 자들과 더불어 하느님의 앞에서 쉬며 누리는, 형언할 수 없는 기쁨이었다."]], [["Presently their hands fell apart, and together they went out of the tent.", "이윽고 그들의 손이 풀어지고, 그들은 함께 천막 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The desert was still as the sky.", "사막은 하늘처럼 고요했다."], ["The sun was sinking fast.", "해가 빠르게 지고 있었다."], ["The camels slept.", "낙타들은 잠들어 있었다."]], [["A little while after, the tent was struck, and, with the remains of the repast, restored to the cot; then the friends mounted, and set out single file, led by the Egyptian.", "조금 뒤에 천막이 걷히고, 남은 음식과 함께 가마에 다시 실렸다. 그러고 나서 벗들은 올라타, 이집트인을 앞세우고 한 줄로 길을 떠났다."], ["Their course was due west, into the chilly night.", "그들이 잡은 길은 정서(正西)였으니, 쌀쌀한 밤 속으로 들어가는 길이었다."], ["The camels swung forward in steady trot, keeping the line and the intervals so exactly that those following seemed to tread in the tracks of the leader.", "낙타들은 한결같은 종종걸음으로 앞으로 흔들흔들 나아가며, 줄과 간격을 어찌나 정확히 지키는지, 뒤따르는 자들이 앞선 자의 발자국을 그대로 밟는 것만 같았다."], ["The riders spoke not once.", "탄 이들은 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["By-and-by the moon came up.", "이윽고 달이 떠올랐다."], ["And as the three tall white figures sped, with soundless tread, through the opalescent light, they appeared like specters flying from hateful shadows.", "그리고 키 큰 흰 형체 셋이 유백색 빛 속을 소리 없는 걸음으로 내달릴 때, 그들은 가증스러운 그림자에서 달아나는 유령들처럼 보였다."], ["Suddenly, in the air before them, not farther up than a low hill-top flared a lambent flame; as they looked at it, the apparition contracted into a focus of dazzling lustre.", "문득, 그들 앞 허공에서, 낮은 언덕 꼭대기보다 높지 않은 곳에서 너울거리는 불꽃이 타올랐다. 그들이 그것을 바라보자, 그 환영이 눈부신 광채의 한 점으로 오므라들었다."], ["Their hearts beat fast; their souls thrilled; and they shouted as with one voice, “The Star!", "그들의 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었고, 그들의 영혼이 떨렸다. 그리고 그들은 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"별이다! 별이다!"], ["the Star! God is with us!”", "하느님이 우리와 함께 계신다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["In an aperture of the western wall of Jerusalem hang the “oaken valves” called the Bethlehem or Joppa Gate.", "예루살렘 서쪽 성벽의 한 어귀에는 베들레헴 문 또는 욥바 문이라 불리는 \"떡갈나무 문짝\"이 걸려 있다."], ["The area outside of them is one of the notable places of the city.", "그 바깥의 일대는 이 도시에서 손꼽히는 명소다."], ["Long before David coveted Zion there was a citadel there.", "다윗이 시온을 탐내기 훨씬 전부터 그곳에는 성채가 있었다."], ["When at last the son of Jesse ousted the Jebusite, and began to build, the site of the citadel became the northwest corner of the new wall, defended by a tower much more imposing than the old one.", "마침내 이새의 아들이 여부스 사람을 몰아내고 성을 쌓기 시작했을 때, 그 성채가 있던 자리는 새 성벽의 북서쪽 모퉁이가 되었고, 옛 망루보다 훨씬 위풍당당한 탑이 그곳을 지켰다."], ["The location of the gate, however, was not disturbed, for the reasons, most likely, that the roads which met and merged in front of it could not well be transferred to any other point, while the area outside had become a recognized market-place.", "그러나 문의 위치만은 건드리지 않았으니, 그 앞에서 만나 하나로 합쳐지는 길들을 다른 자리로 옮기기 어려웠던 데다, 바깥 일대가 이미 알아주는 장터로 자리 잡았기 때문일 것이다."], ["In Solomon’s day there was great traffic at the locality, shared in by traders from Egypt and the rich dealers from Tyre and Sidon.", "솔로몬 시대에는 이곳에 큰 거래가 오갔고, 이집트의 상인들과 티레와 시돈의 부유한 장사꾼들도 한몫 끼었다."], ["Nearly three thousand years have passed, and yet a kind of commerce clings to the spot.", "그로부터 거의 삼천 년이 흘렀건만, 여전히 일종의 상업이 이 자리에 매여 있다."], ["A pilgrim wanting a pin or a pistol, a cucumber or a camel, a house or a horse, a loan or a lentil, a date or a dragoman, a melon or a man, a dove or a donkey, has only to inquire for the article at the Joppa Gate.", "순례자가 핀이나 권총을, 오이나 낙타를, 집이나 말을, 돈이나 렌즈콩을, 대추야자나 통역을, 멜론이나 일꾼을, 비둘기나 나귀를 구하려거든, 욥바 문에서 그 물건을 찾기만 하면 된다."], ["Sometimes the scene is quite animated, and then it suggests, What a place the old market must have been in the days of Herod the Builder!", "때로는 그 풍경이 자못 활기차서, 헤롯 대왕의 건설 시대에는 이 옛 장터가 얼마나 대단한 곳이었을지 짐작하게 한다."], ["And to that period and that market the reader is now to be transferred.", "이제 독자를 그 시대와 그 장터로 옮겨 가려 한다."]], [["Following the Hebrew system, the meeting of the wise men described in the preceding chapters took place in the afternoon of the twenty-fifth day of the third month of the year; that is to say, on the twenty-fifth day of December.", "히브리 사람의 셈법을 따르면, 앞 장들에서 묘사한 현자들의 만남은 그해 셋째 달 스무닷새 날 오후에 일어났다. 곧 십이월 스무닷새 날이다."], ["The year was the second of the 193d Olympiad, or the 747th of Rome; the sixty-seventh of Herod the Great, and the thirty-fifth of his reign; the fourth before the beginning of the Christian era.", "그해는 제193회 올림피아드의 두 번째 해, 곧 로마 건국 747년이며, 헤롯 대왕의 예순일곱 번째 해이자 그 치세의 서른다섯 번째 해요, 기독교 기원이 시작되기 네 해 전이었다."], ["The hours of the day, by Judean custom, begin with the sun, the first hour being the first after sunrise; so, to be precise; the market at the Joppa Gate during the first hour of the day stated was in full session, and very lively.", "유대의 관습으로는 하루의 시간을 해와 더불어 헤아려, 첫째 시는 해 뜬 뒤의 첫 시간이다. 그러니 정확히 말하면, 말한 그날 첫째 시 동안 욥바 문의 장터는 한창 무르익어 몹시 흥성거렸다."], ["The massive valves had been wide open since dawn.", "육중한 문짝은 동틀 녘부터 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["Business, always aggressive, had pushed through the arched entrance into a narrow lane and court, which, passing by the walls of the great tower, conducted on into the city.", "늘 거침없는 장사판은 아치형 입구를 지나 좁은 골목과 마당으로 밀고 들어가, 거대한 탑의 벽을 끼고 돌아 도시 안으로 이어졌다."], ["As Jerusalem is in the hill country, the morning air on this occasion was not a little crisp.", "예루살렘은 산악 지대에 자리한지라, 이날 아침 공기는 적잖이 쌀쌀했다."], ["The rays of the sun, with their promise of warmth, lingered provokingly far up on the battlements and turrets of the great piles about, down from which fell the crooning of pigeons and the whir of the flocks coming and going.", "온기를 약속하는 햇살은 주위에 솟은 거대한 건물들의 흉벽과 작은 탑 위에 약 올리듯 한참을 머물러 있었고, 그 위에서는 비둘기들의 구구거리는 소리와 무리지어 오가는 새 떼의 날갯짓 소리가 흘러내렸다."]], [["As a passing acquaintance with the people of the Holy City, strangers as well as residents, will be necessary to an understanding of some of the pages which follow, it will be well to stop at the gate and pass the scene in review.", "이어지는 몇 쪽을 이해하려면 거룩한 도시의 사람들과, 거주민뿐 아니라 나그네들과도 얼마간 안면을 트는 일이 필요할 터이니, 잠시 문 앞에 멈추어 이 풍경을 찬찬히 살펴보는 것이 좋겠다."], ["Better opportunity will not offer to get sight of the populace who will afterwhile go forward in a mood very different from that which now possesses them.", "머지않아 지금과는 사뭇 다른 심정으로 나아가게 될 이 군중을 볼 기회는 이보다 더 좋게 오지 않을 것이다."]], [["The scene is at first one of utter confusion—confusion of action, sounds, colors, and things.", "그 광경은 처음에는 더없는 혼란일 뿐이다. 움직임의 혼란, 소리의 혼란, 빛깔의 혼란, 물건의 혼란이다."], ["It is especially so in the lane and court.", "골목과 마당이 특히 그러하다."], ["The ground there is paved with broad unshaped flags, from which each cry and jar and hoof-stamp arises to swell the medley that rings and roars up between the solid impending walls.", "그곳 바닥에는 모양을 다듬지 않은 넓적한 돌들이 깔려 있어, 온갖 외침과 덜그럭 소리와 발굽 구르는 소리가 그 위에서 솟아올라, 묵직하게 드리운 벽들 사이로 울리고 으르렁대는 뒤범벅에 보태진다."], ["A little mixing with the throng, however, a little familiarity with the business going on, will make analysis possible.", "그러나 무리에 얼마간 섞이고 그곳에서 오가는 거래에 얼마간 익숙해지면, 비로소 그 갈래를 가려낼 수 있다."]], [["Here stands a donkey, dozing under panniers full of lentils, beans, onions, and cucumbers, brought fresh from the gardens and terraces of Galilee.", "이쪽에는 나귀 한 마리가 갈릴리의 정원과 비탈밭에서 갓 따 온 렌즈콩과 강낭콩, 양파, 오이를 가득 실은 광주리 아래서 졸고 있다."], ["When not engaged in serving customers, the master, in a voice which only the initiated can understand, cries his stock.", "손님을 맞지 않을 때면 주인은 아는 자만이 알아들을 수 있는 목소리로 제 물건을 외친다."], ["Nothing can be simpler than his costume—sandals, and an unbleached, undyed blanket, crossed over one shoulder and girt round the waist.", "그 차림새보다 더 간소한 것은 없으니, 샌들 한 켤레에다, 표백도 물도 들이지 않은 담요 한 장을 한쪽 어깨에 걸쳐 허리에 둘러맸을 뿐이다."], ["Near-by, and far more imposing and grotesque, though scarcely as patient as the donkey, kneels a camel, raw-boned, rough, and gray, with long shaggy tufts of fox-colored hair under its throat, neck, and body, and a load of boxes and baskets curiously arranged upon an enormous saddle.", "그 가까이에는, 나귀만큼 진득하지는 못하나 훨씬 위압적이고 기괴한 낙타 한 마리가 꿇어앉아 있다. 뼈가 앙상하고 거칠며 잿빛인 그 짐승은 목과 멱과 몸통 아래로 여우 빛깔의 길고 텁수룩한 털 다발이 늘어졌고, 거대한 안장 위에는 상자와 바구니 짐이 묘하게 얹혀 있다."], ["The owner is an Egyptian, small, lithe, and of a complexion which has borrowed a good deal from the dust of the roads and the sands of the desert.", "주인은 이집트 사람으로, 자그마하고 날렵하며, 길의 먼지와 사막의 모래를 적잖이 빌려 온 듯한 낯빛이다."], ["He wears a faded tarbooshe, a loose gown, sleeveless, unbelted, and dropping from the neck to the knee.", "그는 빛바랜 타르부시를 쓰고, 소매도 허리띠도 없이 목에서 무릎까지 흘러내리는 헐렁한 겉옷을 걸쳤다."], ["His feet are bare.", "그의 발은 맨발이다."], ["The camel, restless under the load, groans and occasionally shows his teeth; but the man paces indifferently to and fro, holding the driving-strap, and all the time advertising his fruits fresh from the orchards of the Kedron—grapes, dates, figs, apples, and pomegranates.", "낙타는 짐 밑에서 가만있지 못하고 끙끙거리며 이따금 이빨을 드러내지만, 사내는 모는 끈을 쥔 채 무심히 이리저리 거닐며, 키드론의 과수원에서 갓 따 온 과일을 줄곧 외쳐 댄다. 포도, 대추야자, 무화과, 사과, 석류다."]], [["At the corner where the lane opens out into the court, some women sit with their backs against the gray stones of the wall.", "골목이 마당으로 트이는 모퉁이에는 여인 몇이 잿빛 성벽 돌에 등을 기대고 앉아 있다."], ["Their dress is that common to the humbler classes of the country—a linen frock extending the full length of the person, loosely gathered at the waist, and a veil or wimple broad enough, after covering the head, to wrap the shoulders.", "그들의 옷은 이 나라의 천한 계층이 흔히 입는 차림으로, 발끝까지 닿는 아마포 옷자락을 허리에서 헐겁게 여미고, 머리를 덮고도 어깨까지 두를 만큼 넓은 너울 혹은 두건을 썼다."], ["Their merchandise is contained in a number of earthen jars, such as are still used in the East for bringing water from the wells, and some leathern bottles.", "그들의 상품은 동방에서 지금도 우물물을 길어 오는 데 쓰는 여러 질그릇 항아리와 가죽 부대 몇에 담겨 있다."], ["Among the jars and bottles, rolling upon the stony floor, regardless of the crowd and cold, often in danger but never hurt, play half a dozen half-naked children, their brown bodies, jetty eyes, and thick black hair attesting the blood of Israel.", "그 항아리와 부대 사이로, 돌바닥을 구르며, 군중도 추위도 아랑곳없이, 늘 위태롭되 한 번도 다친 적 없이, 반쯤 헐벗은 아이 대여섯이 뛰논다. 갈색 몸뚱이와 새까만 눈동자, 숱 많은 검은 머리칼이 이스라엘의 혈통을 증언한다."], ["Sometimes, from under the wimples, the mothers look up, and in the vernacular modestly bespeak their trade: in the bottles “honey of grapes,” in the jars “strong drink.”", "이따금 두건 아래로 어머니들이 고개를 들어, 그 고장 말로 다소곳이 제 장사를 권한다. 부대에는 \"포도꿀\"이요, 항아리에는 \"독한 술\"이다."], ["Their entreaties are usually lost in the general uproar, and they fare illy against the many competitors: brawny fellows with bare legs, dirty tunics, and long beards, going about with bottles lashed to their backs, and shouting “Honey of wine!", "그들의 권하는 소리는 흔히 두루 울리는 소란에 묻혀 버리고, 수많은 경쟁자들 틈에서 형편이 옹색하다. 다리를 드러내고 더러운 웃옷을 걸친 수염 긴 억센 사내들이 부대를 등에 동여매고 돌아다니며 \"포도주 꿀이오! 엔게디의 포도라오!\""], ["Grapes of En-Gedi!”", "하고 외쳐 댄다."], ["When a customer halts one of them, round comes the bottle, and, upon lifting the thumb from the nozzle, out into the ready cup gushes the deep-red blood of the luscious berry.", "손님이 그중 하나를 멈춰 세우면, 사내는 부대를 휙 돌려 주둥이에서 엄지를 떼고, 그러면 달큰한 열매의 짙붉은 피가 받쳐 든 잔으로 콸콸 쏟아진다."]], [["Scarcely less blatant are the dealers in birds—doves, ducks, and frequently the singing bulbul, or nightingale, most frequently pigeons; and buyers, receiving them from the nets, seldom fail to think of the perilous life of the catchers, bold climbers of the cliffs; now hanging with hand and foot to the face of the crag, now swinging in a basket far down the mountain fissure.", "새 장수들의 외침도 그에 못지않게 요란하다. 비둘기, 오리, 더러는 노래하는 불불 곧 밤꾀꼬리, 그리고 가장 흔하게는 집비둘기다. 그물에서 새를 건네받는 손님들은, 절벽을 대담히 기어오르는 사냥꾼들의 위험천만한 삶을 떠올리지 않을 수 없다. 그들은 손발로 바위 벼랑에 매달리기도 하고, 광주리에 실려 산골짜기 틈으로 까마득히 내려가기도 한다."]], [["Blent with peddlers of jewelry—sharp men cloaked in scarlet and blue, top-heavy under prodigious white turbans, and fully conscious of the power there is in the lustre of a ribbon and the incisive gleam of gold, whether in bracelet or necklace, or in rings for the finger or the nose—and with peddlers of household utensils, and with dealers in wearing-apparel, and with retailers of unguents for anointing the person, and with hucksters of all articles, fanciful as well as of need, hither and thither, tugging at halters and ropes, now screaming, now coaxing, toil the venders of animals—donkeys, horses, calves, sheep, bleating kids, and awkward camels; animals of every kind except the outlawed swine. All these are there; not singly, as described, but many times repeated; not in one place, but everywhere in the market.", "장신구 행상들이 한데 어울려 있다. 진홍과 푸른빛 외투를 걸치고 엄청나게 큰 흰 터번 아래 머리가 무거운 영악한 사내들로, 리본의 윤기와 금붙이의 날카로운 광채에 깃든 위력을 익히 아는 자들이다. 팔찌든 목걸이든, 손가락이나 코에 끼우는 고리든 매한가지다. 그 사이로 살림 그릇 행상이며, 입을 옷을 파는 장수며, 몸에 바르는 향유를 파는 자며, 긴요한 것이든 멋 부리는 것이든 온갖 물건을 파는 잡상인들이 섞여 있다. 그리고 이리저리, 고삐와 밧줄을 잡아당기며, 때로는 악을 쓰고 때로는 어르며, 짐승 장수들이 애를 쓴다. 나귀, 말, 송아지, 양, 매에 우는 새끼 염소, 그리고 어기적거리는 낙타들이다. 금기시된 돼지만 빼고 온갖 짐승이 다 있다. 이 모든 것이 거기 있으니, 묘사한 대로 하나씩만이 아니라 몇 번이고 거듭되고, 한자리에만이 아니라 장터 어디에나 펼쳐져 있다."]], [["Turning from this scene in the lane and court, this glance at the sellers and their commodities, the reader has need to give attention, in the next place, to visitors and buyers, for which the best studies will be found outside the gates, where the spectacle is quite as varied and animated; indeed, it may be more so, for there are superadded the effects of tent, booth, and sook, greater space, larger crowd, more unqualified freedom, and the glory of the Eastern sunshine.", "골목과 마당의 이 풍경에서, 파는 자들과 그 물건들에 던진 이 눈길에서 돌아서서, 독자는 이제 찾아온 이들과 사는 이들에게 주의를 기울일 필요가 있다. 그 모습은 문밖에서 가장 잘 살필 수 있으니, 그곳의 광경 또한 못지않게 다채롭고 활기차며, 아니 어쩌면 더할는지도 모른다. 거기에는 천막과 노점과 시장이 빚어내는 정취, 더 넓은 공간, 더 큰 무리, 거리낌 없는 자유, 그리고 동방 햇살의 영광이 더해지기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["Let us take our stand by the gate, just out of the edge of the currents—one flowing in, the other out—and use our eyes and ears awhile.", "성문 곁에 자리를 잡고, 안으로 흘러드는 물결과 밖으로 빠져나가는 물결, 그 두 흐름의 가장자리에서 비켜선 채로 잠시 눈과 귀를 써 보기로 하자."]], [["In good time!", "마침 알맞은 때다!"], ["Here come two men of a most noteworthy class.", "저기 가장 눈여겨볼 만한 부류에 속한 두 사내가 다가온다."]], [["“Gods! How cold it is!”", "\"신들이여! 어찌 이리 춥단 말인가!\""], ["says one of them, a powerful figure in armor; on his head a brazen helmet, on his body a shining breastplate and skirts of mail.", "그중 한 사람이 말한다. 갑옷을 두른 다부진 체구에, 머리에는 놋쇠 투구를, 몸에는 번쩍이는 흉갑과 사슬 미늘을 늘어뜨리고 있다."], ["“How cold it is!", "\"어찌 이리 춥단 말인가!"], ["Dost thou remember, my Caius, that vault in the Comitium at home which the flamens say is the entrance to the lower world?", "카이우스, 자네 기억하는가, 고향의 코미티움에 있던 그 지하 묘소 말이네. 신관들이 지하 세계로 드는 입구라고 하던 곳 말일세. 플루토 신께 맹세코!"], ["By Pluto! I could stand there this morning, long enough at least to get warm again!”", "오늘 아침이라면 나는 거기 서 있을 수 있겠네. 적어도 다시 몸이 덥혀질 때까지는 말이야!\""]], [["The party addressed drops the hood of his military cloak, leaving bare his head and face, and replies, with an ironic smile, “The helmets of the legions which conquered Mark Antony were full of Gallic snow; but thou—ah, my poor friend!—thou hast just come from Egypt, bringing its summer in thy blood.”", "말을 건네받은 쪽은 군용 외투의 두건을 내려 머리와 얼굴을 드러내고는, 비웃는 듯한 미소를 띤 채 대꾸한다. \"마르쿠스 안토니우스를 무찌른 군단의 투구에는 갈리아의 눈이 가득 차 있었지. 한데 자네는—아, 가엾은 친구여!—이제 막 이집트에서 왔으니, 그 여름을 핏속에 담아 온 게로군.\""]], [["And with the last word they disappear through the entrance.", "그리고 그 마지막 말과 함께 두 사람은 성문 안으로 사라진다."], ["Though they had been silent, the armor and the sturdy step would have published them Roman soldiers.", "입을 다물고 있었다 해도, 그 갑옷과 단단한 걸음걸이만으로 그들이 로마 병사임을 알 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["From the throng a Jew comes next, meager of frame, round-shouldered, and wearing a coarse brown robe; over his eyes and face, and down his back, hangs a mat of long, uncombed hair.", "뒤이어 무리 속에서 한 유대인이 나온다. 야윈 몸에 어깨가 굽었고, 거친 갈색 겉옷을 걸쳤다. 빗질하지 않은 긴 머리카락이 헝클어진 채로 두 눈과 얼굴을 덮고 등줄기까지 흘러내려 있다."], ["He is alone.", "그는 홀로다."], ["Those who meet him laugh, if they do not worse; for he is a Nazarite, one of a despised sect which rejects the books of Moses, devotes itself to abhorred vows, and goes unshorn while the vows endure.", "그를 마주치는 자들은 더 험한 짓을 하지 않으면 웃음을 터뜨린다. 그가 나실인이기 때문이다. 모세의 율법서를 배척하고, 가증스러운 서원에 몸을 바치며, 그 서원이 지속되는 동안 머리를 깎지 않는, 멸시받는 종파의 일원이다."]], [["As we watch his retiring figure, suddenly there is a commotion in the crowd, a parting quickly to the right and left, with exclamations sharp and decisive. Then the cause comes—a man, Hebrew in feature and dress.", "그가 물러가는 모습을 지켜보는데, 별안간 군중 속에 소란이 일고, 좌우로 빠르게 길이 갈라지면서 날카롭고 단호한 외침이 터진다. 이윽고 그 까닭이 모습을 드러낸다—이목구비와 차림새가 모두 히브리인인 한 사내다."], ["The mantle of snow-white linen, held to his head by cords of yellow silk, flows free over his shoulders; his robe is richly embroidered, a red sash with fringes of gold wraps his waist several times.", "눈처럼 흰 아마포 망토를 노란 비단 끈으로 머리에 동여매어 두 어깨 위로 자유로이 흘러내렸고, 겉옷에는 호화로운 자수가 놓였으며, 금술이 달린 붉은 띠가 허리를 몇 겹으로 감싸고 있다. 그의 태도는 차분하다."], ["His demeanor is calm; he even smiles upon those who, with such rude haste, make room for him.", "그토록 무례한 다급함으로 자기에게 길을 비켜 주는 자들에게 미소까지 짓는다. 나병 환자인가?"], ["A leper? No, he is only a Samaritan.", "아니다, 그는 그저 사마리아인일 뿐이다."], ["The shrinking crowd, if asked, would say he is a mongrel—an Assyrian—whose touch of the robe is pollution; from whom, consequently, an Israelite, though dying, might not accept life.", "몸을 사리며 물러서는 군중에게 물었다면, 그를 일러 잡종이라고—아시리아인이라고—할 것이니, 그자의 옷자락이 스치기만 해도 더러움이 된다고, 따라서 이스라엘인이라면 죽어 가는 처지라도 그자에게서 목숨을 받을 수 없다고 할 것이다."], ["In fact, the feud is not of blood.", "사실 이 반목은 핏줄에서 비롯된 것이 아니다."], ["When David set his throne here on Mount Zion, with only Judah to support him, the ten tribes betook themselves to Shechem, a city much older, and, at that date, infinitely richer in holy memories.", "다윗이 오직 유다 지파의 지지만 받으며 이곳 시온 산에 왕좌를 세웠을 때, 나머지 열 지파는 세겜으로 떠나갔다. 세겜은 훨씬 오래된 도시였고, 그 무렵에는 거룩한 기억으로 비할 데 없이 풍요로웠다."], ["The final union of the tribes did not settle the dispute thus begun.", "끝내 지파들이 다시 하나로 합쳐졌어도, 그렇게 시작된 다툼은 가라앉지 않았다."], ["The Samaritans clung to their tabernacle on Gerizim, and, while maintaining its superior sanctity, laughed at the irate doctors in Jerusalem.", "사마리아인들은 그리심 산의 성막에 매달려, 그 우월한 신성함을 주장하면서 예루살렘의 성난 율법학자들을 비웃었다."], ["Time brought no assuagement of the hate.", "세월이 흘러도 그 증오는 누그러지지 않았다."], ["Under Herod, conversion to the faith was open to all the world except the Samaritans; they alone were absolutely and forever shut out from communion with Jews.", "헤롯 치하에서는 사마리아인을 제외한 온 세상에 신앙으로의 개종이 열려 있었다. 오직 그들만이 유대인과의 교류에서 절대적으로, 또 영원히 배척당했다."]], [["As the Samaritan goes in under the arch of the gate, out come three men so unlike all whom we have yet seen that they fix our gaze, whether we will or not.", "사마리아인이 성문의 아치 밑으로 들어가는데, 우리가 지금껏 본 그 누구와도 닮은 데가 없어 우리의 시선을 좋든 싫든 붙들어 매는 세 사내가 나온다."], ["They are of unusual stature and immense brawn; their eyes are blue, and so fair is their complexion that the blood shines through the skin like blue pencilling; their hair is light and short; their heads, small and round, rest squarely upon necks columnar as the trunks of trees.", "그들은 키가 유달리 크고 근육이 어마어마하다. 두 눈은 푸르고, 살결이 어찌나 흰지 피가 살갗 너머로 푸르스름한 줄을 그은 듯 비쳐 보인다. 머리카락은 옅은 빛깔에 짧게 깎였고, 작고 둥근 머리는 나무 줄기처럼 굵은 목 위에 반듯이 얹혀 있다."], ["Woollen tunics, open at the breast, sleeveless and loosely girt, drape their bodies, leaving bare arms and legs of such development that they at once suggest the arena; and when thereto we add their careless, confident, insolent manner, we cease to wonder that the people give them way, and stop after they have passed to look at them again.", "가슴께가 트이고 소매가 없으며 헐겁게 졸라맨 모직 튜닉이 그들의 몸을 두르고 있는데, 드러난 팔다리가 어찌나 발달했는지 보는 즉시 투기장을 떠올리게 한다. 게다가 거기에 그 무심하고 거만하고 안하무인인 태도까지 더하니, 사람들이 그들에게 길을 내주고 그들이 지나간 뒤에도 발길을 멈춰 다시 쳐다보는 것이 조금도 이상하지 않다. 그들은 검투사다—씨름꾼이요, 달리기꾼이요, 권투꾼이요, 검객이다."], ["They are gladiators—wrestlers, runners, boxers, swordsmen; professionals unknown in Judea before the coming of the Roman; fellows who, what time they are not in training, may be seen strolling through the king’s gardens or sitting with the guards at the palace gates; or possibly they are visitors from Cæsarea, Sebaste, or Jericho; in which Herod, more Greek than Jew, and with all a Roman’s love of games and bloody spectacles, has built vast theaters, and now keeps schools of fighting-men, drawn, as is the custom, from the Gallic provinces or the Slavic tribes on the Danube.", "로마인이 들어오기 전까지 유대에서는 알려지지 않았던 직업 무사들이다. 훈련을 하지 않을 때면 왕의 정원을 거닐거나 궁전 문간에서 호위병과 함께 앉아 있는 모습이 보이는 자들이다. 어쩌면 카이사레아나 세바스테, 혹은 여리고에서 온 손님일 수도 있는데, 유대인이라기보다 그리스인에 가깝고 로마인 못지않게 경기와 피비린내 나는 구경거리를 즐기는 헤롯은 그 도시들에 거대한 극장을 지었으며, 지금은 관례대로 갈리아 속주나 다뉴브 강가의 슬라브 부족에서 끌어모은 격투사들의 양성소까지 두고 있다."]], [["“By Bacchus!”", "\"바쿠스 신께 맹세코!\""], ["says one of them, drawing his clenched hand to his shoulder, “their skulls are not thicker than eggshells.”", "그중 하나가 움켜쥔 주먹을 제 어깨까지 끌어올리며 말한다. \"저것들 두개골은 달걀 껍데기보다 두껍지도 않더군.\""]], [["The brutal look which goes with the gesture disgusts us, and we turn happily to something more pleasant.", "그 몸짓에 어린 잔인한 표정이 우리를 역겹게 하니, 우리는 기꺼이 좀 더 즐거운 무언가로 눈을 돌린다."]], [["Opposite us is a fruit-stand.", "맞은편에는 과일 좌판이 있다."], ["The proprietor has a bald head, a long face, and a nose like the beak of a hawk.", "주인은 머리가 벗어졌고, 얼굴이 길며, 코는 매부리처럼 휘었다."], ["He sits upon a carpet spread upon the dust; the wall is at his back; overhead hangs a scant curtain, around him, within hand’s reach and arranged upon little stools, lie osier boxes full of almonds, grapes, figs, and pomegranates.", "그는 먼지 위에 깔아 둔 양탄자에 앉아 있다. 등 뒤에는 성벽이 있고, 머리 위로는 빈약한 차일이 드리워졌으며, 둘레에는 손이 닿는 거리의 작은 걸상마다 아몬드와 포도와 무화과와 석류가 가득 담긴 버들고리 상자가 놓여 있다."], ["To him now comes one at whom we cannot help looking, though for another reason than that which fixed our eyes upon the gladiators; he is really beautiful—a beautiful Greek.", "이제 그에게 한 사람이 다가오는데, 우리는 보지 않을 수 없다. 다만 검투사들에게 시선을 붙들렸던 것과는 다른 까닭에서다. 그는 정말로 아름답다—아름다운 그리스인이다."], ["Around his temples, holding the waving hair, is a crown of myrtle, to which still cling the pale flowers and half ripe berries.", "관자놀이 둘레에는 물결치는 머리카락을 붙드는 도금양 화관을 둘렀는데, 거기에는 아직도 옅은 빛깔의 꽃과 덜 익은 열매가 매달려 있다."], ["His tunic, scarlet in color, is of the softest woollen fabric; below the girdle of buff leather, which is clasped in front by a fantastic device of shining gold, the skirt drops to the knee in folds heavy with embroidery of the same royal metal; a scarf, also woollen, and of mixed white and yellow, crosses his throat and falls trailing at his back; his arms and legs, where exposed, are white as ivory, and of the polish impossible except by perfect treatment with bath, oil, brushes, and pincers.", "진홍빛 튜닉은 더없이 부드러운 모직물로 지어졌고, 앞쪽이 번쩍이는 황금의 기묘한 장식으로 채워진 담황색 가죽띠 아래로 자락이 무릎까지 늘어지는데, 그 주름은 똑같은 왕가의 금속으로 놓인 자수로 묵직하다. 역시 모직인 흰색과 노란색이 뒤섞인 목도리가 그의 목을 가로질러 등 뒤로 늘어뜨려져 있다. 드러난 팔다리는 상아처럼 희고, 목욕과 기름과 솔과 족집게로 완벽하게 다듬지 않고는 도무지 낼 수 없는 광택을 띠고 있다."]], [["The dealer, keeping his seat, bends forward, and throws his hands up until they meet in front of him, palm downwards and fingers extended.", "좌판 주인은 자리에 앉은 채로 몸을 앞으로 굽히고, 두 손을 들어 손바닥을 아래로 향하고 손가락을 편 채 제 앞에서 맞붙인다."]], [["“What hast thou, this morning, O son of Paphos?”", "\"오늘 아침에는 무얼 가져왔느냐, 파포스의 아들이여?\""], ["says the young Greek, looking at the boxes rather than at the Cypriote.", "젊은 그리스인이 키프로스 사람보다 상자들 쪽을 바라보며 말한다."], ["“I am hungry.", "\"배가 고프구나."], ["What hast thou for breakfast?”", "아침거리로 무엇이 있느냐?\""]], [["“Fruits from the Pedius—genuine—such as the singers of Antioch take of mornings to restore the waste of their voices,”", "\"페디우스 강가에서 난 과일이올시다—진짜배기로요—안티오크의 가수들이 상한 목청을 되살리려고 아침마다 드는 것이지요.\""], ["the dealer answers, in a querulous nasal tone.", "좌판 주인이 칭얼대는 콧소리로 대답한다."]], [["“A fig, but not one of thy best, for the singers of Antioch!”", "\"무화과나 줘 보거라, 안티오크 가수들에게는 네 으뜸가는 것이 아니어도 좋다!\""], ["says the Greek.", "그리스인이 말한다."], ["“Thou art a worshiper of Aphrodite, and so am I, as the myrtle I wear proves; therefore I tell thee their voices have the chill of a Caspian wind.", "\"너는 아프로디테를 섬기는 자요, 내가 두른 이 도금양이 증명하듯 나 또한 그러하다. 그러니 일러 두건대, 그자들 목소리에는 카스피 해의 바람 같은 한기가 서려 있느니라."], ["Seest thou this girdle?—a gift of the mighty Salome—”", "이 띠가 보이느냐?—드높으신 살로메께서 내리신 선물이니라—\""]], [["“The king’s sister!” exclaims the Cypriote, with another salaam.", "\"왕의 누이 되시는 분 아니십니까!\" 키프로스 사람이 다시 한번 절을 하며 외친다."]], [["“And of royal taste and divine judgment.", "\"왕가의 안목과 신묘한 판단력을 지니신 분이지."], ["And why not?", "어찌 안 그렇겠느냐?"], ["She is more Greek than the king.", "그분은 왕보다도 더 그리스인다우시니라."], ["But—my breakfast!", "한데—내 아침이 먼저다!"], ["Here is thy money—red coppers of Cyprus.", "여기 네 삯이 있다—키프로스의 붉은 동전이니라."], ["Give me grapes, and—”", "포도를 다오, 그리고—\""]], [["“Wilt thou not take the dates also?”", "\"대추야자도 함께 드시지 않으렵니까?\""]], [["“No, I am not an Arab.”", "\"아니다, 나는 아랍인이 아니야.\""]], [["“Nor figs?”", "\"무화과는요?\""]], [["“That would be to make me a Jew.", "\"그건 나를 유대인으로 만드는 짓이지."], ["No, nothing but the grapes. Never waters mixed so sweetly as the blood of the Greek and the blood of the grape.”", "아니다, 포도 말고는 아무것도 필요 없다. 그리스인의 피와 포도의 피만큼 달콤하게 섞이는 물은 어디에도 없느니라.\""]], [["The singer in the grimed and seething market, with all his airs of the court, is a vision not easily shut out of mind by such as see him; as if for the purpose, however, a person follows him challenging all our wonder.", "때 묻고 들끓는 장터에 선 그 가수는 온통 궁정의 멋을 부린 모습이라, 한번 본 사람의 머릿속에서 쉬이 지워지지 않는 광경이다. 그런데 마치 일부러 그러기라도 하듯, 우리의 온갖 경이를 자아내는 한 인물이 그의 뒤를 따른다. 그는 얼굴을 땅으로 향한 채 천천히 길을 올라온다."], ["He comes up the road slowly, his face towards the ground; at intervals he stops, crosses his hands upon his breast, lengthens his countenance, and turns his eyes towards heaven, as if about to break into prayer.", "이따금 걸음을 멈추고 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고는, 얼굴을 길게 늘이고 두 눈을 하늘로 돌리니, 마치 곧 기도라도 터뜨릴 것만 같다. 이런 부류는 예루살렘 말고는 어디서도 찾아볼 수 없다. 그의 이마에는, 망토를 제자리에 붙드는 띠에 매달린 네모난 가죽 갑이 튀어나와 있고, 또 하나의 비슷한 갑이 왼팔에 가죽끈으로 묶여 있다."], ["Nowhere, except in Jerusalem, can such a character be found.", "겉옷 가장자리는 깊은 술로 장식되어 있다."], ["On his forehead, attached to the band which keeps the mantle in place, projects a leathern case, square in form; another similar case is tied by a thong to the left arm; the borders of his robe are decorated with deep fringe; and by such signs—the phylacteries, the enlarged borders of the garment, and the savor of intense holiness pervading the whole man—we know him to be a Pharisee, one of an organization (in religion a sect, in politics a party) whose bigotry and power will shortly bring the world to grief.", "이런 표지들—성구함, 늘어뜨린 옷자락 단, 그리고 사람 전체에 배어 있는 짙은 거룩함의 냄새—로 우리는 그가 바리새인임을 안다. 한 조직(종교로는 종파요, 정치로는 당파다)의 일원으로, 그 편협함과 권력은 머지않아 온 세상을 비탄에 빠뜨릴 것이다."]], [["The densest of the throng outside the gate covers the road leading off to Joppa.", "성문 밖 무리 가운데 가장 빽빽한 인파는 욥바로 갈라져 나가는 길을 뒤덮고 있다."], ["Turning from the Pharisee, we are attracted by some parties who, as subjects of study, opportunely separate themselves from the motley crowd.", "바리새인에게서 눈을 돌리니, 잡다한 군중 속에서 마침맞게 따로 떨어져 나와 살펴보기 좋은 한 무리가 우리의 시선을 끈다."], ["First among them a man of very noble appearance—clear, healthful complexion; bright black eyes; beard long and flowing, and rich with unguents; apparel well-fitting, costly, and suitable for the season.", "그 가운데 으뜸은 더없이 고귀한 풍모를 지닌 사내다—맑고 건강한 살결, 빛나는 검은 두 눈, 향유로 윤이 나게 길게 흘러내린 수염, 몸에 잘 맞고 값지며 계절에 알맞은 의복."], ["He carries a staff, and wears, suspended by a cord from his neck, a large golden seal.", "그는 지팡이를 들었고, 목에서 끈으로 늘어뜨린 커다란 황금 인장을 차고 있다."], ["Several servants attend him, some of them with short swords stuck through their sashes; when they address him, it is with the utmost deference.", "여러 하인이 그를 모시는데, 더러는 띠에 짧은 검을 꽂고 있다. 그들이 그에게 말을 건넬 때는 더없이 깍듯하다."], ["The rest of the party consists of two Arabs of the pure desert stock; thin, wiry men, deeply bronzed, and with hollow cheeks, and eyes of almost evil brightness; on their heads red tarbooshes; over their abas, and wrapping the left shoulder and the body so as to leave the right arm free, brown woollen haicks, or blankets.", "무리의 나머지는 순수한 사막 혈통의 아랍인 둘이다. 야위고 다부진 사내들로, 살갗은 짙게 그을렸고, 두 볼은 움푹 패었으며, 두 눈은 거의 사악하다 싶을 만큼 형형하게 빛난다. 머리에는 붉은 타르부시를 썼고, 아바 위에 갈색 모직 하이크, 곧 담요를 왼쪽 어깨와 몸에 둘러 오른팔만 자유롭게 드러내 놓았다."], ["There is loud chaffering, for the Arabs are leading horses and trying to sell them; and, in their eagerness, they speak in high, shrill voices.", "시끌벅적한 흥정이 벌어지고 있으니, 아랍인들이 말을 끌고 와 팔려 들기 때문이다. 그들은 다급한 마음에 높고 날카로운 목소리로 떠들어 댄다."], ["The courtly person leaves the talking mostly to his servants; occasionally he answers with much dignity; directly, seeing the Cypriote, he stops and buys some figs.", "고귀한 풍모의 사내는 말하는 일을 거의 하인들에게 맡기고, 이따금 자기가 위엄 있게 대꾸할 뿐이다. 곧장 키프로스 사람을 보더니 걸음을 멈추고 무화과를 좀 산다."], ["And when the whole party has passed the portal, close after the Pharisee, if we betake ourselves to the dealer in fruits, he will tell, with a wonderful salaam, that the stranger is a Jew, one of the princes of the city, who has travelled, and learned the difference between the common grapes of Syria and those of Cyprus, so surpassingly rich with the dews of the sea.", "그리고 이 무리가 모두 바리새인의 뒤를 바짝 따라 성문을 지나간 뒤, 우리가 그 과일 장수에게 가 본다면, 그는 놀랍도록 정중한 절과 함께 일러 줄 것이다. 그 낯선 이는 유대인으로, 이 도시의 귀인 가운데 하나이며, 여러 곳을 여행하여 시리아의 흔한 포도와 바다의 이슬을 머금어 비할 데 없이 풍미가 깊은 키프로스의 포도가 어떻게 다른지를 알게 된 사람이라고."]], [["And so, till towards noon, sometimes later, the steady currents of business habitually flow in and out of the Joppa Gate, carrying with them every variety of character; including representatives of all the tribes of Israel, all the sects among whom the ancient faith has been parcelled and refined away, all the religious and social divisions, all the adventurous rabble who, as children of art and ministers of pleasure, riot in the prodigalities of Herod, and all the peoples of note at any time compassed by the Cæsars and their predecessors, especially those dwelling within the circuit of the Mediterranean.", "이렇게 정오 무렵까지, 때로는 그보다 늦게까지, 한결같은 장사의 물결이 욥바 성문으로 으레 들고 나며, 그 흐름과 더불어 온갖 부류의 인물을 실어 나른다. 이스라엘 모든 지파의 대표자들, 그 안에서 옛 신앙이 잘게 나뉘고 닳아 없어진 온갖 종파, 온갖 종교적·사회적 갈래, 예술의 자식이요 쾌락의 종복으로서 헤롯의 방탕에 놀아나는 온갖 모험적인 무리, 그리고 카이사르들과 그 선왕들이 어느 때고 거느렸던 이름난 모든 민족, 특히 지중해 둘레에 자리 잡고 사는 이들까지 모두 그 안에 들어 있다."]], [["In other words, Jerusalem, rich in sacred history, richer in connection with sacred prophecies—the Jerusalem of Solomon, in which silver was as stones, and cedars as the sycamores of the vale—had come to be but a copy of Rome, a center of unholy practises, a seat of pagan power.", "다시 말해, 거룩한 역사로 풍요롭고 거룩한 예언과의 인연으로 더욱 풍요로운 예루살렘—은이 돌처럼 흔하고 백향목이 골짜기의 뽕나무처럼 흔했던 솔로몬의 예루살렘—은 이제 로마의 사본에 지나지 않게 되었으니, 부정한 행습의 중심지요, 이교 권력의 본거지가 되었다."], ["A Jewish king one day put on priestly garments, and went into the Holy of Holies of the first temple to offer incense, and he came out a leper; but in the time of which we are reading, Pompey entered Herod’s temple and the same Holy of Holies, and came out without harm, finding but an empty chamber, and of God not a sign.", "한 유대 왕이 어느 날 제사장의 옷을 입고 첫 성전의 지성소에 들어가 향을 피우려 했다가 나병 환자가 되어 나왔다. 그러나 우리가 지금 읽고 있는 시대에는, 폼페이우스가 헤롯의 성전과 바로 그 지성소에 들어갔다가 아무 해도 입지 않고 나왔으니, 거기서 그가 본 것이라고는 텅 빈 방뿐이요, 하느님의 흔적은 어디에도 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# CHAPTER VIII"]], [["The reader is now besought to return to the court described as part of the market at the Joppa Gate.", "이제 독자에게 청하노니, 욥바 문 시장의 일부로 묘사된 그 안마당으로 돌아가 주기를 바란다."], ["It was the third hour of the day, and many of the people had gone away; yet the press continued without apparent abatement.", "그날 세 번째 시각이었고, 많은 사람들이 이미 떠났으나, 북적임은 눈에 띄게 줄어드는 기색 없이 이어지고 있었다."], ["Of the new-comers, there was a group over by the south wall, consisting of a man, a woman, and a donkey, which requires extended notice.", "새로 온 이들 가운데 남쪽 벽 곁에 한 무리가 있었으니, 한 남자와 한 여자와 한 마리 나귀로 이루어진 그들에 대해서는 좀 더 자세히 살펴볼 필요가 있다."]], [["The man stood by the animal’s head, holding a leading-strap, and leaning upon a stick which seemed to have been chosen for the double purpose of goad and staff.", "남자는 짐승의 머리맡에 서서 고삐를 쥐고, 막대기에 기대어 있었는데, 그 막대기는 채찍과 지팡이라는 두 가지 쓰임을 위해 고른 듯했다."], ["His dress was like that of the ordinary Jews around him, except that it had an appearance of newness.", "그의 옷차림은 주위에 있는 여느 유대인의 것과 다름없었으나, 새것처럼 보이는 점만은 달랐다."], ["The mantle dropping from his head, and the robe or frock which clothed his person from neck to heel, were probably the garments he was accustomed to wear to the synagogue on Sabbath days.", "머리에서부터 늘어뜨린 외투와, 목에서 발꿈치까지 몸을 감싼 겉옷은 아마도 안식일에 회당에 갈 때 즐겨 입던 옷이었을 것이다."], ["His features were exposed, and they told of fifty years of life, a surmise confirmed by the gray that streaked his otherwise black beard.", "그의 얼굴은 가려지지 않았는데, 그 얼굴은 쉰 해의 세월을 말해 주었고, 검은 수염에 줄줄이 섞인 흰빛이 그 짐작을 뒷받침했다."], ["He looked around him with the half-curious, half-vacant stare of a stranger and provincial.", "그는 낯선 시골 사람 특유의, 반쯤은 호기심 어리고 반쯤은 멍한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["The donkey ate leisurely from an armful of green grass, of which there was an abundance in the market.", "나귀는 시장에 넘쳐 나는 푸른 풀 한 아름을 느긋하게 뜯고 있었다."], ["In its sleepy content, the brute did not admit of disturbance from the bustle and clamor about; no more was it mindful of the woman sitting upon its back in a cushioned pillion.", "졸린 듯 흡족한 그 짐승은 주위의 소란과 떠들썩함에도 흔들리지 않았고, 푹신한 안장에 올라앉은 여자에게도 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["An outer robe of dull woollen stuff completely covered her person, while a white wimple veiled her head and neck.", "칙칙한 모직 천으로 지은 겉옷이 그 여자의 몸을 온통 감쌌고, 흰 머릿수건이 머리와 목을 가렸다."], ["Once in a while, impelled by curiosity to see or hear something passing, she drew the wimple aside, but so slightly that the face remained invisible.", "이따금 무언가 지나가는 것을 보거나 들으려는 호기심에 이끌려 그 머릿수건을 옆으로 젖혔으나, 얼굴이 드러나지 않을 만큼 아주 살짝이었다."]], [["At length the man was accosted.", "마침내 누군가 그 남자에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Are you not Joseph of Nazareth?”", "\"나사렛의 요셉이 아니십니까?\""]], [["The speaker was standing close by.", "말을 건 이는 바로 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["“I am so called,”", "\"그렇게들 부르지요.\""], ["answered Joseph, turning gravely around; “And you—ah, peace be unto you!", "요셉이 점잖게 몸을 돌리며 대답했다. \"그리고 당신은—아, 평안하시기를!"], ["my friend, Rabbi Samuel!”", "내 친구 사무엘 랍비여!\""]], [["“The same give I back to you.”", "\"그 인사를 그대로 당신께 돌려 드립니다.\""], ["The Rabbi paused, looking at the woman, then added, “To you, and unto your house and all your helpers, be peace.”", "랍비는 잠시 멈추어 여자를 바라보고는 덧붙였다. \"당신과, 당신의 집안과, 모든 일꾼들에게 평안이 있기를.\""]], [["With the last word, he placed one hand upon his breast, and inclined his head to the woman, who, to see him, had by this time withdrawn the wimple enough to show the face of one but a short time out of girlhood.", "마지막 말과 함께 그는 한 손을 가슴에 얹고 여자에게 고개를 숙였다. 그를 보려고 여자는 그새 머릿수건을 젖혔으니, 소녀 시절을 벗어난 지 얼마 되지 않은 한 사람의 얼굴이 드러났다."], ["Thereupon the acquaintances grasped right hands, as if to carry them to their lips; at the last moment, however, the clasp was let go, and each kissed his own hand, then put its palm upon his forehead.", "그러자 두 지인은 오른손을 마주 잡아, 마치 그 손을 입술로 가져가려는 듯했다. 그러나 마지막 순간에 손을 놓고, 저마다 제 손에 입을 맞춘 다음, 그 손바닥을 제 이마에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“There is so little dust upon your garments,”", "\"당신 옷에 먼지가 거의 묻어 있지 않으니,\""], ["the Rabbi said, familiarly, “that I infer you passed the night in this city of our fathers.”", "랍비가 허물없이 말했다. \"우리 조상의 이 도성에서 하룻밤을 묵으신 모양이군요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["Joseph replied, “as we could only make Bethany before the night came, we stayed in the khan there, and took the road again at daybreak.”", "요셉이 대답했다. \"밤이 오기 전에 베다니까지밖에 갈 수 없어, 그곳 객사에서 묵고, 동틀 녘에 다시 길을 나섰습니다.\""]], [["“The journey before you is long, then—not to Joppa, I hope.”", "\"그렇다면 앞길이 머시겠군요—설마 욥바까지는 아니시겠지요.\""]], [["“Only to Bethlehem.”", "\"베들레헴까지만 갑니다.\""]], [["The countenance of the Rabbi, theretofore open and friendly, became lowering and sinister, and he cleared his throat with a growl instead of a cough.", "그때까지 열려 있고 다정하던 랍비의 낯빛이 어두워지고 험상궂어졌으며, 그는 기침 대신 으르렁거리는 소리로 목청을 가다듬었다."]], [["“Yes, yes—I see,” he said.", "\"그렇지, 그래—알겠습니다.\""], ["“You were born in Bethlehem, and wend thither now, with your daughter, to be counted for taxation, as ordered by Cæsar.", "그가 말했다. \"당신은 베들레헴에서 태어났으니, 카이사르의 명에 따라 호적에 오르려고 딸과 함께 그리로 가는 길이로군요."], ["The children of Jacob are as the tribes in Egypt were—only they have neither a Moses nor a Joshua. How are the mighty fallen!”", "야곱의 자손이 이집트에 있던 지파들과 같은 처지가 되었으니—다만 모세도 없고 여호수아도 없을 따름이지요. 어찌 그리 강하던 자들이 쓰러졌단 말입니까!\""]], [["Joseph answered, without change of posture or countenance,", "요셉은 자세도 낯빛도 바꾸지 않고 대답했다."]], [["“The woman is not my daughter.”", "\"이 사람은 내 딸이 아닙니다.\""]], [["But the Rabbi clung to the political idea; and he went on, without noticing the explanation, “What are the Zealots doing down in Galilee?”", "그러나 랍비는 정치적인 생각에 매달려, 그 해명에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다. \"갈릴리 아래쪽의 열심당원들은 무얼 하고 있습니까?\""]], [["“I am a carpenter, and Nazareth is a village,”", "\"나는 목수이고, 나사렛은 마을일 뿐입니다.\""], ["said Joseph, cautiously.", "요셉이 조심스럽게 말했다."], ["“The street on which my bench stands is not a road leading to any city.", "\"내 작업대가 놓인 거리는 어느 도시로도 통하지 않는 길입니다."], ["Hewing wood and sawing plank leave me no time to take part in the disputes of parties.”", "나무를 깎고 널빤지를 켜는 일에 매여, 당파의 다툼에 끼어들 겨를이 없습니다.\""]], [["“But you are a Jew,”", "\"그러나 당신은 유대인이오.\""], ["said the Rabbi, earnestly.", "랍비가 간곡하게 말했다."], ["“You are a Jew, and of the line of David.", "\"당신은 유대인이고, 다윗의 혈통이지요."], ["It is not possible you can find pleasure in the payment of any tax except the shekel given by ancient custom to Jehovah.”", "옛 관습에 따라 여호와께 바치는 세겔 말고는, 어떤 세금을 내는 데서도 기쁨을 느낄 리 없을 것이오.\""]], [["Joseph held his peace.", "요셉은 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“I do not complain,”", "\"내가 불평하는 건,\""], ["his friend continued, “of the amount of the tax—a denarius is a trifle. Oh no!", "그 친구가 말을 이었다. \"세금의 액수가 아니오—데나리온 한 닢이야 하찮은 것이지요."], ["The imposition of the tax is the offense.", "오, 아니오! 세금을 매긴다는 것 자체가 모욕이오."], ["And, besides, what is paying it but submission to tyranny?", "게다가 그것을 내는 일이 폭정에 복종하는 것이 아니고 무엇이겠소?"], ["Tell me, is it true that Judas claims to be the Messiah?", "말해 보시오, 유다가 자기가 메시아라고 주장한다는 게 사실이오?"], ["You live in the midst of his followers.”", "당신은 그를 따르는 자들 한가운데서 살고 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“I have heard his followers say he was the Messiah,”", "\"그를 따르는 자들이 그가 메시아라고 말하는 것은 들었습니다.\""], ["Joseph replied.", "요셉이 대답했다."]], [["At this point the wimple was drawn aside, and for an instant the whole face of the woman was exposed.", "이때 머릿수건이 옆으로 젖혀지면서, 한순간 여자의 얼굴이 온전히 드러났다."], ["The eyes of the Rabbi wandered that way, and he had time to see a countenance of rare beauty, kindled by a look of intense interest; then a blush overspread her cheeks and brow, and the veil was returned to its place.", "랍비의 눈길이 그쪽으로 흘러갔고, 그는 깊은 관심의 빛으로 환히 밝아진, 보기 드문 아름다움을 지닌 얼굴을 볼 수 있었다. 그러더니 붉은 기운이 그녀의 뺨과 이마에 번졌고, 너울은 다시 제자리로 돌아갔다."]], [["The politician forgot his subject.", "정치를 논하던 자는 화제를 잊었다."]], [["“Your daughter is comely,” he said, speaking lower.", "\"따님이 곱군요.\" 그가 목소리를 낮추어 말했다."]], [["“She is not my daughter,”", "\"내 딸이 아닙니다.\""], ["Joseph repeated.", "요셉이 거듭 말했다."]], [["The curiosity of the Rabbi was aroused; seeing which, the Nazarene hastened to say further, “She is the child of Joachim and Anna of Bethlehem, of whom you have at least heard, for they were of great repute—”", "랍비의 호기심이 일었다. 그것을 알아챈 나사렛 사람은 서둘러 말을 보탰다. \"이 사람은 베들레헴의 요아킴과 안나의 딸입니다. 그분들은 명망이 높았으니, 적어도 들어는 보셨을 겁니다—\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["remarked the Rabbi, deferentially, “I know them.", "랍비가 정중하게 말했다. \"내가 그분들을 알지요."], ["They were lineally descended from David.", "다윗에게서 곧바로 이어진 혈통이었소."], ["I knew them well.”", "잘 알고 있었소.\""]], [["“Well, they are dead now,”", "\"그런데 이제 두 분 다 세상을 떠났습니다.\""], ["the Nazarene proceeded.", "나사렛 사람이 말을 이었다."], ["“They died in Nazareth.", "\"나사렛에서 돌아가셨지요."], ["Joachim was not rich, yet he left a house and garden to be divided between his daughters Marian and Mary.", "요아킴은 넉넉하지는 않았으나, 두 딸 마리안과 마리아에게 나누어 줄 집 한 채와 정원을 남겼습니다."], ["This is one of them; and to save her portion of the property, the law required her to marry her next of kin. She is now my wife.”", "이 사람이 그 가운데 하나인데, 제 몫의 재산을 지키려면 율법에 따라 가장 가까운 친족과 혼인해야 했습니다. 그래서 지금은 내 아내가 되었지요.\""]], [["“And you were—”", "\"그렇다면 당신은—\""]], [["“Her uncle.”", "\"그녀의 숙부였습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes! And as you were both born in Bethlehem, the Roman compels you to take her there with you to be also counted.”", "\"그렇지, 그렇소! 그리고 두 분 다 베들레헴에서 태어났으니, 로마가 호적에 함께 오르도록 당신더러 그녀를 데려가라고 강요하는 것이로군요.\""]], [["The Rabbi clasped his hands, and looked indignantly to heaven, exclaiming, “The God of Israel still lives!", "랍비는 두 손을 맞잡고 분개하여 하늘을 우러르며 외쳤다. \"이스라엘의 하느님은 아직 살아 계신다!"], ["The vengeance is his!”", "복수는 그분의 것이다!\""]], [["With that he turned and abruptly departed.", "그 말과 함께 그는 몸을 돌려 불쑥 떠나 버렸다."], ["A stranger near by, observing Joseph’s amazement, said, quietly, “Rabbi Samuel is a zealot.", "가까이 있던 한 낯선 사람이 요셉의 놀란 기색을 보고 조용히 말했다. \"사무엘 랍비는 열심당원이지요."], ["Judas himself is not more fierce.”", "유다라 한들 저보다 더 사납지는 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["Joseph, not wishing to talk with the man, appeared not to hear, and busied himself gathering in a little heap the grass which the donkey had tossed abroad; after which he leaned upon his staff again, and waited.", "요셉은 그 사람과 이야기를 나누고 싶지 않아 못 들은 체하며, 나귀가 흩뜨려 놓은 풀을 작은 무더기로 그러모으는 일에 몰두했다. 그러고는 다시 지팡이에 몸을 기대고 기다렸다."]], [["In another hour the party passed out the gate, and, turning to the left, took the road into Bethlehem.", "또 한 시간이 지나 그 일행은 성문을 빠져나가, 왼쪽으로 돌아 베들레헴으로 가는 길로 접어들었다."], ["The descent into the valley of Hinnom was quite broken, garnished here and there with straggling wild olive-trees.", "힌놈 골짜기로 내려가는 비탈은 제법 험했고, 군데군데 듬성듬성 들올리브나무가 박혀 있었다."], ["Carefully, tenderly, the Nazarene walked by the woman’s side, leading-strap in hand.", "나사렛 사람은 고삐를 손에 쥐고, 조심스럽고도 정성스럽게 여자 곁에서 걸었다."], ["On their left, reaching to the south and east round Mount Zion, rose the city wall, and on their right the steep prominences which form the western boundary of the valley.", "그들의 왼쪽으로는 남쪽과 동쪽으로 시온산을 휘감으며 성벽이 솟아 있었고, 오른쪽으로는 골짜기의 서쪽 경계를 이루는 가파른 벼랑들이 있었다."]], [["Slowly they passed the Lower Pool of Gihon, out of which the sun was fast driving the lessening shadow of the royal hill; slowly they proceeded, keeping parallel with the aqueduct from the Pools of Solomon, until near the site of the country-house on what is now called the Hill of Evil Counsel; there they began to ascend to the plain of Rephaim.", "그들은 천천히 기혼 아래 못을 지나쳤으니, 그 못 위에 드리웠던 왕의 언덕 그림자를 해가 빠르게 몰아내며 줄여 가고 있었다. 그들은 천천히 나아가, 솔로몬 못에서 흘러오는 수로와 나란히 길을 잡았고, 마침내 오늘날 흉계의 언덕이라 불리는 곳의 별장 터 가까이에 이르렀다. 거기서부터 그들은 르바임 평원으로 오르기 시작했다."], ["The sun streamed garishly over the stony face of the famous locality, and under its influence Mary, the daughter of Joachim, dropped the wimple entirely, and bared her head.", "해가 그 이름난 고장의 돌투성이 땅 위로 눈부시게 쏟아졌고, 그 기운에 못 이겨 요아킴의 딸 마리아는 머릿수건을 아예 내리고 머리를 드러냈다."], ["Joseph told the story of the Philistines surprised in their camp there by David.", "요셉은 다윗이 그곳 진지에서 블레셋 사람들을 기습한 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["He was tedious in the narrative, speaking with the solemn countenance and lifeless manner of a dull man. She did not always hear him.", "그는 둔한 사람 특유의 엄숙한 낯빛과 생기 없는 태도로 지루하게 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 그녀는 그 말을 늘 듣고 있지는 않았다."]], [["Wherever on the land men go, and on the sea ships, the face and figure of the Jew are familiar.", "땅 위로 사람들이 가는 곳마다, 바다 위로 배들이 가는 곳마다, 유대인의 얼굴과 모습은 낯익다."], ["The physical type of the race has always been the same; yet there have been some individual variations.", "그 민족의 신체적 전형은 언제나 한결같았으나, 더러 저마다의 차이가 있었다."], ["“Now he was ruddy, and withal of a beautiful countenance, and goodly to look to.”", "\"그는 혈색이 좋고, 게다가 용모가 아름다워 보기에 훌륭하였더라.\""], ["Such was the son of Jesse when brought before Samuel.", "사무엘 앞에 끌려 나왔을 때의 이새의 아들이 그러했다."], ["The fancies of men have been ever since ruled by the description.", "그 뒤로 사람들의 상상은 이 묘사에 매여 왔다."], ["Poetic license has extended the peculiarities of the ancestor to his notable descendants.", "시적 자유는 그 조상의 특징을 그의 이름난 후손들에게까지 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["So all our ideal Solomons have fair faces, and hair and beard chestnut in the shade, and of the tint of gold in the sun.", "그리하여 우리가 마음에 그리는 모든 솔로몬은 고운 얼굴에, 그늘에서는 밤빛이고 햇빛에서는 금빛을 띠는 머리칼과 수염을 지녔다."], ["Such, we are also made believe, were the locks of Absalom the beloved.", "우리는 또한 사랑받던 압살롬의 머리채도 그러했다고 믿게 되었다."], ["And, in the absence of authentic history, tradition has dealt no less lovingly by her whom we are now following down to the native city of the ruddy king.", "그리고 믿을 만한 역사가 없는 가운데, 전승은 우리가 지금 그 혈색 좋은 왕의 고향 도시로 따라가고 있는 그녀를 못지않게 사랑스럽게 그려 왔다."]], [["She was not more than fifteen.", "그녀는 열다섯을 넘지 않았다."], ["Her form, voice, and manner belonged to the period of transition from girlhood.", "그 자태와 목소리와 거동은 소녀 시절에서 막 벗어나려는 시기의 것이었다."], ["Her face was perfectly oval, her complexion more pale than fair.", "얼굴은 완벽한 달걀형이었고, 살빛은 고운 흰빛이라기보다 창백한 편이었다. 코는 흠잡을 데가 없었다."], ["The nose was faultless; the lips, slightly parted, were full and ripe, giving to the lines of the mouth warmth, tenderness, and trust; the eyes were blue and large, and shaded by drooping lids and long lashes; and, in harmony with all, a flood of golden hair, in the style permitted to Jewish brides, fell unconfined down her back to the pillion on which she sat.", "살짝 벌어진 입술은 도톰하고 무르익어, 입가의 선에 따스함과 부드러움과 미더움을 안겨 주었다. 눈은 푸르고 컸으며, 내리깔린 눈꺼풀과 긴 속눈썹이 그늘을 드리웠다. 그리고 이 모든 것과 어우러져, 유대인 신부에게 허락된 방식대로 풀어 내린 금빛 머리채가, 그녀가 앉은 안장까지 등을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["The throat and neck had the downy softness sometimes seen which leaves the artist in doubt whether it is an effect of contour or color.", "목과 목덜미에는 이따금 보이는 솜털 같은 부드러움이 깃들어 있어, 화가로 하여금 그것이 윤곽이 빚어낸 것인지 빛깔이 빚어낸 것인지 가늠하기 어렵게 했다."], ["To these charms of feature and person were added others more indefinable—an air of purity which only the soul can impart, and of abstraction natural to such as think much of things impalpable.", "이러한 이목구비와 몸매의 매력에 더하여, 더 형언하기 어려운 다른 것들이 있었으니—오직 영혼만이 줄 수 있는 순결의 기운과, 만질 수 없는 것들을 깊이 생각하는 이에게 자연스레 깃드는 초연함이었다."], ["Often, with trembling lips, she raised her eyes to heaven, itself not more deeply blue; often she crossed her hands upon her breast, as in adoration and prayer; often she raised her head like one listening eagerly for a calling voice.", "자주, 떨리는 입술로, 그녀는 하늘만큼이나 깊게 푸른 두 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러렀다. 자주 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개었으니, 흠모와 기도를 드리는 듯했다. 자주 머리를 들었으니, 부르는 목소리를 간절히 귀 기울여 듣는 사람 같았다."], ["Now and then, midst his slow utterances, Joseph turned to look at her, and, catching the expression kindling her face as with light, forgot his theme, and with bowed head, wondering, plodded on.", "이따금 느릿느릿 이야기를 늘어놓던 중에 요셉이 돌아서서 그녀를 바라보다가, 빛이 비치듯 그 얼굴을 환히 밝히는 표정을 마주치고는, 제 이야기의 줄거리를 잊은 채 고개를 숙이고 의아해하며 터벅터벅 걸었다."]], [["So they skirted the great plain, and at length reached the elevation Mar Elias; from which, across a valley, they beheld Bethlehem, the old, old House of Bread, its white walls crowning a ridge, and shining above the brown scumbling of leafless orchards.", "그렇게 그들은 너른 평원을 끼고 돌아, 마침내 마르 엘리아스의 높은 곳에 이르렀다. 거기서 그들은 골짜기 너머로 베들레헴을 바라보았으니, 오래고 오랜 빵의 집이었다. 그 흰 벽이 능선 위에 왕관처럼 얹혀, 잎이 진 과수원의 갈색 칠 너머로 빛나고 있었다."], ["They paused there, and rested, while Joseph pointed out the places of sacred renown; then they went down into the valley to the well which was the scene of one of the marvellous exploits of David’s strong men.", "그들은 거기서 걸음을 멈추고 쉬는 동안, 요셉이 거룩한 사연을 지닌 곳들을 가리켜 보였다. 그러고는 골짜기로 내려가 우물에 이르렀으니, 그곳은 다윗의 용사들이 벌인 놀라운 무용담 가운데 하나의 무대였다."], ["The narrow space was crowded with people and animals.", "그 좁은 자리는 사람과 짐승으로 북적였다."], ["A fear came upon Joseph—a fear lest, if the town were so thronged, there might not be house-room for the gentle Mary.", "요셉에게 두려움이 밀려왔다—이렇게 마을이 붐빈다면, 어진 마리아가 머물 방이 없을지도 모른다는 두려움이었다."], ["Without delay, he hurried on, past the pillar of stone marking the tomb of Rachel, up the gardened slope, saluting none of the many persons he met on the way, until he stopped before the portal of the khan that then stood outside the village gates, near a junction of roads.", "그는 지체 없이 서둘러, 라헬의 무덤을 표시하는 돌기둥을 지나, 정원이 딸린 비탈을 올라, 길에서 마주친 숱한 사람들 가운데 누구에게도 인사하지 않은 채, 마침내 그때 마을 성문 밖, 길이 갈라지는 곳 가까이에 서 있던 객사의 정문 앞에 다다라서야 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# CHAPTER IX"]], [["To understand thoroughly what happened to the Nazarene at the khan, the reader must be reminded that Eastern inns were different from the inns of the Western world.", "나사렛 사람이 객사에서 겪은 일을 온전히 이해하려면, 동방의 여관이 서방 세계의 여관과는 달랐다는 사실을 독자에게 일러 둘 필요가 있다."], ["They were called khans, from the Persian, and, in simplest form, were fenced enclosures, without house or shed, often without a gate or entrance.", "그것들은 페르시아어에서 온 말로 칸이라 불렸으며, 가장 단순한 형태로는 집도 헛간도 없이, 흔히 문이나 출입구조차 없는 울타리만 두른 마당이었다."], ["Their sites were chosen with reference to shade, defence, or water.", "그 자리는 그늘이나 방어, 또는 물을 기준으로 정해졌다."], ["Such were the inns that sheltered Jacob when he went to seek a wife in Padan-Aram.", "야곱이 밧단아람으로 아내를 구하러 갔을 때 그를 재워 준 여관도 이러한 것이었다."], ["Their like may been seen at this day in the stopping-places of the desert.", "그와 같은 것을 오늘날에도 사막의 쉼터에서 볼 수 있다."], ["On the other hand, some of them, especially those on the roads between great cities, like Jerusalem and Alexandria, were princely establishments, monuments to the piety of the kings who built them.", "한편 그 가운데 더러는, 특히 예루살렘과 알렉산드리아 같은 큰 도시를 잇는 길가에 선 것들은 왕후(王侯)의 시설이었으니, 그것을 세운 왕들의 경건함을 기리는 기념물이었다."], ["In ordinary, however, they were no more than the house or possession of a sheik, in which, as in headquarters, he swayed his tribe.", "그러나 보통은 한 족장의 집이나 소유에 지나지 않았으며, 그는 그곳을 본거지 삼아 자기 부족을 다스렸다."], ["Lodging the traveller was the least of their uses; they were markets, factories, forts; places of assemblage and residence for merchants and artisans quite as much as places of shelter for belated and wandering wayfarers.", "나그네를 재우는 것은 그것의 쓰임새 가운데 가장 작은 것이었다. 그것들은 시장이요, 작업장이요, 요새였으니, 늦어 떠도는 나그네를 위한 피난처인 만큼이나 상인과 장인이 모이고 머무는 곳이기도 했다."], ["Within their walls, all the year round, occurred the multiplied daily transactions of a town.", "그 담장 안에서는 일 년 내내 한 도시의 갖가지 일상적 거래가 날마다 거듭되었다."]], [["The singular management of these hostelries was the feature likely to strike a Western mind with most force.", "이러한 숙소들의 유별난 운영 방식이야말로 서방 사람의 마음을 가장 강하게 칠 만한 특징이었다."], ["There was no host or hostess; no clerk, cook, or kitchen; a steward at the gate was all the assertion of government or proprietorship anywhere visible.", "주인 남자도 주인 여자도 없었고, 사무원도 요리사도 부엌도 없었다. 문간에 앉은 관리인 하나가 어디서든 눈에 띄는 다스림이나 소유의 유일한 표시였다."], ["Strangers arriving stayed at will without rendering account.", "도착한 나그네는 셈을 치르지 않고도 마음껏 머물렀다."], ["A consequence of the system was that whoever came had to bring his food and culinary outfit with him, or buy them of dealers in the khan.", "이러한 제도의 한 결과로, 오는 사람은 누구나 제 먹을 것과 취사 도구를 가지고 와야 했으며, 그렇지 않으면 칸 안의 장사꾼에게서 사야 했다."], ["The same rule held good as to his bed and bedding, and forage for his beasts.", "잠자리와 침구, 짐승의 먹이에 대해서도 같은 규칙이 적용되었다."], ["Water, rest, shelter, and protection were all he looked for from the proprietor, and they were gratuities.", "물과 쉼, 거처와 보호, 이것이 그가 주인에게 바라는 전부였으며, 그것들은 거저 주어지는 것이었다."], ["The peace of synagogues was sometimes broken by brawling disputants, but that of the khans never.", "회당의 평화는 이따금 다투는 자들의 싸움질로 깨지기도 했으나, 칸의 평화는 결코 그렇지 않았다."], ["The houses and all their appurtenances were sacred: a well was not more so.", "그 집들과 거기 딸린 모든 것은 신성했으니, 우물도 그보다 더 신성하지는 않았다."]], [["The khan at Bethlehem, before which Joseph and his wife stopped, was a good specimen of its class, being neither very primitive nor very princely.", "요셉과 그 아내가 그 앞에 멈춰 선 베들레헴의 칸은 그 부류의 좋은 본보기로, 지나치게 원시적이지도 않고 지나치게 호화롭지도 않았다."], ["The building was purely Oriental; that is to say, a quadrangular block of rough stones, one story high, flat-roofed, externally unbroken by a window, and with but one principal entrance—a doorway, which was also a gateway, on the eastern side, or front.", "그 건물은 순전히 동방식이었으니, 곧 거친 돌을 네모지게 쌓아 올린 단층 건물로, 지붕은 평평하고, 바깥쪽은 창 하나 없이 막혔으며, 주된 출입구는 단 하나뿐이었다. 동쪽 면, 곧 앞쪽에 난 문 하나가 그것으로, 출입문이자 대문이기도 했다."], ["The road ran by the door so near that the chalk dust half covered the lintel.", "길은 그 문 바로 곁을 지나는지라 백악 가루가 문 상인방을 절반쯤 덮고 있었다."], ["A fence of flat rocks, beginning at the northeastern corner of the pile, extended many yards down the slope to a point from whence it swept westwardly to a limestone bluff; making what was in the highest degree essential to a respectable khan—a safe enclosure for animals.", "납작한 바위로 쌓은 울타리가 건물 북동쪽 모퉁이에서 시작하여 비탈을 따라 여러 야드 아래로 뻗어 내려가다가 한 지점에서 서쪽으로 휘어 석회암 벼랑에 닿았으니, 이는 번듯한 칸에 더없이 요긴한 것, 곧 짐승을 가두는 안전한 우리를 이루고 있었다."]], [["In a village like Bethlehem, as there was but one sheik, there could not well be more than one khan; and, though born in the place, the Nazarene, from long residence elsewhere, had no claim to hospitality in the town.", "베들레헴 같은 마을에는 족장이 하나뿐이었으니, 칸도 하나 넘게 있을 리 없었다. 그리고 나사렛 사람은 비록 그곳 태생이었으나 오랫동안 다른 곳에 살았기에, 그 고을에서 묵을 권리를 주장할 처지가 못 되었다."], ["Moreover, the enumeration for which he was coming might be the work of weeks or months; Roman deputies in the provinces were proverbially slow; and to impose himself and wife for a period so uncertain upon acquaintances or relations was out of the question.", "더구나 그가 응하러 온 호적 등록은 몇 주가 걸릴지 몇 달이 걸릴지 알 수 없는 일이었으니, 속주에 파견된 로마 관리들의 굼뜸은 익히 소문난 바였다. 그토록 기약 없는 동안 아내와 함께 아는 이나 친척에게 신세를 진다는 것은 생각할 수도 없었다."], ["So, before he drew nigh the great house, while he was yet climbing the slope, in the steep places toiling to hasten the donkey, the fear that he might not find accommodations in the khan became a painful anxiety; for he found the road thronged with men and boys who, with great ado, were taking their cattle, horses, and camels to and from the valley, some to water, some to the neighboring caves.", "그래서 그는 그 큰 집에 다가가기도 전에, 아직 비탈을 오르며 가파른 곳에서 나귀를 재촉하느라 애쓰는 동안, 칸에서 잠자리를 얻지 못할지도 모른다는 두려움이 괴로운 근심이 되었다. 길에는 어른들과 사내아이들이 큰 소동을 벌이며 소와 말과 낙타를 골짜기로 몰고 가고 몰고 오는 무리로 붐비고 있었으니, 더러는 물을 먹이러, 더러는 가까운 동굴로 가는 것이었다."], ["And when he was come close by, his alarm was not allayed by the discovery of a crowd investing the door of the establishment, while the enclosure adjoining, broad as it was, seemed already full.", "그리고 가까이 다가갔을 때, 그의 불안은 가시지 않았다. 한 무리가 그 시설의 문을 에워싸고 있었고, 거기 딸린 우리는 그토록 넓은데도 벌써 가득 차 보였던 것이다."]], [["“We cannot reach the door,”", "\"문 앞까지 갈 수가 없구려.\""], ["Joseph said, in his slow way.", "요셉이 그 느릿한 말투로 말했다."], ["“Let us stop here, and learn, if we can, what has happened.”", "\"여기서 멈추고, 무슨 일이 있었는지 알 수 있으면 알아봅시다.\""]], [["The wife, without answering, quietly drew the wimple aside.", "아내는 대답 없이 조용히 너울을 옆으로 걷었다."], ["The look of fatigue at first upon her face changed to one of interest.", "처음에는 그 얼굴에 피로한 기색이 떠 있었으나, 이내 흥미 어린 빛으로 바뀌었다."], ["She found herself at the edge of an assemblage that could not be other than a matter of curiosity to her, although it was common enough at the khans on any of the highways which the great caravans were accustomed to traverse.", "그녀는 자기가 호기심을 끌 수밖에 없는 한 무리의 가장자리에 서 있음을 깨달았다. 큰 대상들이 으레 오가는 어느 큰길의 칸에서나 흔히 볼 수 있는 광경이었는데도 그러했다."], ["There were men on foot, running hither and thither, talking shrilly and in all the tongues of Syria; men on horseback screaming to men on camels; men struggling doubtfully with fractious cows and frightened sheep; men peddling bread and wine; and among the mass a herd of boys apparently in chase of a herd of dogs.", "사람들이 걸어서 이리저리 뛰어다니며 시리아의 온갖 말로 날카롭게 떠들어 댔고, 말 탄 자들은 낙타 탄 자들에게 소리쳤다. 사람들은 고집 센 소와 겁먹은 양과 미덥지 못한 씨름을 벌이고 있었으며, 빵과 포도주를 파는 자들도 있었다. 그 무리 가운데에는 한 떼의 개를 쫓는 듯한 한 떼의 사내아이들도 있었다."], ["Everybody and everything seemed to be in motion at the same time.", "모든 사람과 모든 것이 한꺼번에 움직이는 것 같았다."], ["Possibly the fair spectator was too weary to be long attracted by the scene; in a little while she sighed, and settled down on the pillion, and, as if in search of peace and rest, or in expectation of some one, looked off to the south, and up to the tall cliffs of the Mount of Paradise, then faintly reddening under the setting sun.", "어쩌면 그 아름다운 구경꾼은 너무 지쳐 그 광경에 오래 마음을 빼앗기지 못했는지도 모른다. 잠시 후 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고 안장 뒤 받침에 몸을 가라앉히더니, 평안과 쉼을 찾으려는 듯, 또는 누군가를 기다리는 듯, 남쪽을 멀리 바라보며 낙원산의 높은 벼랑을 올려다보았다. 그 벼랑은 지는 해 아래 어렴풋이 붉어지고 있었다."]], [["While she was thus looking, a man pushed his way out of the press, and, stopping close by the donkey, faced about with an angry brow.", "그녀가 그렇게 바라보고 있을 때, 한 사내가 무리를 헤치고 나오더니 나귀 곁에 바싹 멈춰 서서 성난 이맛살을 하고 돌아섰다."], ["The Nazarene spoke to him.", "나사렛 사람이 그에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“As I am what I take you to be, good friend—a son of Judah—may I ask the cause of this multitude?”", "\"제가 당신을 알아본 대로라면, 선한 벗이여 — 유다의 아들이시라면 — 이 무리의 까닭을 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["The stranger turned fiercely; but, seeing the solemn countenance of Joseph, so in keeping with his deep, slow voice and speech, he raised his hand in half-salutation, and replied,", "낯선 사내는 사납게 돌아섰다. 그러나 요셉의 엄숙한 얼굴, 깊고 느린 그의 목소리와 말투에 그토록 어울리는 그 얼굴을 보고는, 손을 들어 반쯤 인사하는 시늉을 하며 대답했다."]], [["“Peace be to you, Rabbi!", "\"당신에게 평화가 있기를, 랍비여!"], ["I am a son of Judah, and will answer you.", "나는 유다의 아들이니, 답해 드리리다."], ["I dwell in Beth-Dagon, which, you know, is in what used to be the land of the tribe of Dan.”", "나는 벳다곤에 사는데, 아시다시피 그곳은 단 지파의 땅이던 곳에 있습니다.\""]], [["“On the road to Joppa from Modin,”", "\"모딘에서 욥바로 가는 길에 있는 곳이군요.\""], ["said Joseph.", "요셉이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, you have been in Beth-Dagon,”", "\"아, 벳다곤에 가 보셨군요.\""], ["the man said, his face softening yet more.", "사내가 말했다. 그 얼굴이 한층 더 부드러워졌다."], ["“What wanderers we of Judah are!", "\"우리 유다 사람들은 참으로 떠도는 무리지요!"], ["I have been away from the ridge—old Ephrath, as our father Jacob called it—for many years.", "나는 그 능선 — 우리 조상 야곱이 옛 에브랏이라 부른 그곳 — 을 여러 해 동안 떠나 있었습니다."], ["When the proclamation went abroad requiring all Hebrews to be numbered at the cities of their birth— That is my business here, Rabbi.”", "모든 히브리 사람더러 제 태어난 고을에서 호적에 오르라는 칙령이 내려졌을 때 — 내가 여기 온 까닭이 바로 그것입니다, 랍비여.\""]], [["Joseph’s face remained stolid as a mask, while he remarked, “I have come for that also—I and my wife.”", "요셉의 얼굴은 가면처럼 무덤덤한 채로, 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"나도 그 때문에 왔소 — 나와 내 아내가 말이오.\""]], [["The stranger glanced at Mary and kept silence.", "낯선 사내는 마리아를 흘끗 보고는 잠잠해졌다."], ["She was looking up at the bald top of Gedor.", "그녀는 게돌의 민둥 꼭대기를 올려다보고 있었다."], ["The sun touched her upturned face, and filled the violet depths of her eyes, and upon her parted lips trembled an aspiration which could not have been to a mortal.", "해가 위로 향한 그 얼굴에 닿아 보랏빛 깊은 눈동자를 가득 채웠고, 살짝 벌어진 입술 위에는 사람의 것이라고는 할 수 없는 어떤 갈망이 떨고 있었다."], ["For the moment, all the humanity of her beauty seemed refined away: she was as we fancy they are who sit close by the gate in the transfiguring light of Heaven.", "그 순간, 그녀의 아름다움이 지닌 모든 인간적인 것이 정화되어 사라진 듯했으니, 그녀는 우리가 천국의 변모하는 빛 속에서 문 가까이 앉아 있다고 상상하는 이들과도 같았다."], ["The Beth-Dagonite saw the original of what, centuries after, came as a vision of genius to Sanzio the divine, and left him immortal.", "벳다곤 사내는 수백 년 뒤 신과도 같은 산치오에게 천재의 환상으로 찾아와 그를 불멸케 한 바로 그 원형을 보았던 것이다."]], [["“Of what was I speaking? Ah!", "\"내가 무슨 이야기를 하고 있었지요? 아!"], ["I remember.", "생각났습니다."], ["I was about to say that when I heard of the order to come here, I was angry.", "여기로 오라는 명령을 들었을 때 나는 화가 났다고 말하려던 참이었지요. 그러다가 그 오래된 언덕과 마을과, 기드론의 깊은 골짜기로 떨어져 내려가는 골짜기를 떠올렸습니다."], ["Then I thought of the old hill, and the town, and the valley falling away into the depths of Cedron; of the vines and orchards, and fields of grain, unfailing since the days of Boaz and Ruth, of the familiar mountains—Gedor here, Gibeah yonder, Mar Elias there—which, when I was a boy, were the walls of the world to me; and I forgave the tyrants and came—I, and Rachel, my wife, and Deborah and Michal, our roses of Sharon.”", "보아스와 룻의 시절 이래로 끊긴 적 없는 포도밭과 과수원과 곡식밭을 떠올렸고, 낯익은 산들 — 여기 게돌, 저편 기브아, 저쪽 마르 엘리야 — 을 떠올렸지요. 어릴 적 내게는 그 산들이 세상의 담벼락이었습니다. 그러자 나는 폭군들을 용서하고 왔습니다 — 나와, 내 아내 라헬과, 우리의 샤론의 장미인 드보라와 미갈이 함께 말이지요.\""]], [["The man paused again, looking abruptly at Mary, who was now looking at him and listening.", "사내는 다시 말을 멈추고, 문득 마리아를 바라보았다. 그녀는 이제 그를 바라보며 귀 기울이고 있었다. 그러더니 그가 말했다."], ["Then he said, “Rabbi, will not your wife go to mine?", "\"랍비여, 당신의 아내가 내 아내에게로 가지 않겠습니까?"], ["You may see her yonder with the children, under the leaning olive-tree at the bend of the road.", "저기 길이 꺾이는 곳, 비스듬히 기운 올리브나무 아래에서 아이들과 함께 있는 내 아내를 보실 수 있을 겁니다."], ["I tell you”—he turned to Joseph and spoke positively—“I tell you the khan is full.", "내 말하건대\" — 그는 요셉을 향해 단호하게 말했다 — \"내 말하건대 칸은 가득 찼습니다."], ["It is useless to ask at the gate.”", "문간에서 물어봐야 소용없습니다.\""]], [["Joseph’s will was slow, like his mind; he hesitated, but at length replied, “The offer is kind.", "요셉의 결심은 그 마음처럼 느렸다. 그는 망설였으나, 마침내 대답했다. \"친절한 제안이오."], ["Whether there be room for us or not in the house, we will go see your people.", "집 안에 우리가 들 자리가 있든 없든, 우리는 당신 식구들에게 가 보겠소."], ["Let me speak to the gate-keeper myself.", "문지기에게는 내가 직접 말해 보리다."], ["I will return quickly.”", "곧 돌아오겠소.\""]], [["And, putting the leading-strap in the stranger’s hand, he pushed into the stirring crowd.", "그리고 그는 이끄는 줄을 낯선 사내의 손에 쥐여 주고, 들썩이는 무리 속으로 헤치고 들어갔다."]], [["The keeper sat on a great cedar block outside the gate.", "문지기는 문밖의 커다란 삼나무 토막 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["Against the wall behind him leaned a javelin.", "그 뒤의 벽에는 투창 하나가 기대 세워져 있었다."], ["A dog squatted on the block by his side.", "개 한 마리가 그 곁의 토막 위에 웅크리고 있었다."]], [["“The peace of Jehovah be with you,”", "\"여호와의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를.\""], ["said Joseph, at last confronting the keeper.", "마침내 문지기와 마주 선 요셉이 말했다."]], [["“What you give, may you find again; and, when found, be it many times multiplied to you and yours,”", "\"당신이 베푸는 것을 다시 찾으시기를. 그리고 찾거든 그것이 당신과 당신의 식구에게 몇 곱절로 불어나기를.\""], ["returned the watchman, gravely, though without moving.", "야경꾼은 움직이지도 않은 채 엄숙하게 답했다."]], [["“I am a Bethlehemite,”", "\"나는 베들레헴 사람이오.\""], ["said Joseph, in his most deliberate way.", "요셉이 더없이 신중한 말투로 말했다."], ["“Is there not room for—”", "\"혹시 자리가 없겠소—\""]], [["“There is not.”", "\"없습니다.\""]], [["“You may have heard of me—Joseph of Nazareth. This is the house of my fathers.", "\"내 이야기를 들으셨을지도 모르겠소 — 나사렛의 요셉이오. 이곳은 내 조상의 집이오."], ["I am of the line of David.”", "나는 다윗의 혈통이오.\""]], [["These words held the Nazarene’s hope.", "이 말에 나사렛 사람의 희망이 걸려 있었다."], ["If they failed him, further appeal was idle, even that of the offer of many shekels.", "이 말이 그를 저버린다면, 더 이상의 청은 헛될 터였으니, 세겔을 많이 내겠다는 제안조차 그러할 것이었다."], ["To be a son of Judah was one thing—in the tribal opinion a great thing; to be of the house of David was yet another; on the tongue of a Hebrew there could be no higher boast.", "유다의 아들이라는 것은 한 가지 일이었으니 — 부족의 견해로는 큰일이었다. 다윗의 가문이라는 것은 또 다른 일이었으니, 히브리 사람의 입에서 그보다 더 높은 자랑은 있을 수 없었다. 소년 목동이 사울의 뒤를 이어 왕가를 세운 지 천 년이 넘게 지났다."], ["A thousand years and more had passed since the boyish shepherd became the successor of Saul and founded a royal family.", "전쟁과 재난과 다른 왕들, 그리고 시간의 헤아릴 수 없이 흐릿하게 하는 과정이, 운수로 말하자면, 그 후손들을 여느 유대인의 수준으로 끌어내렸다."], ["Wars, calamities, other kings, and the countless obscuring processes of time had, as respects fortune, lowered his descendants to the common Jewish level; the bread they ate came to them of toil never more humble; yet they had the benefit of history sacredly kept, of which genealogy was the first chapter and the last; they could not become unknown, while, wherever they went In Israel, acquaintance drew after it a respect amounting to reverence.", "그들이 먹는 빵은 더없이 비천한 수고로 얻은 것이었다. 그러나 그들에게는 거룩하게 지켜져 온 역사의 혜택이 있었으니, 족보가 그 첫 장이자 마지막 장이었다. 그들은 결코 알려지지 않은 존재가 될 수 없었으니, 이스라엘 어디를 가든 그들과 아는 사이가 되면 그 뒤에는 경배에 가까운 존경이 따랐다."]], [["If this were so in Jerusalem and elsewhere, certainly one of the sacred line might reasonably rely upon it at the door of the khan of Bethlehem.", "예루살렘에서도 다른 곳에서도 그러했다면, 그 거룩한 혈통의 한 사람이 베들레헴 칸의 문 앞에서 그것에 기대를 거는 것도 분명 무리는 아니었다."], ["To say, as Joseph said, “This is the house of my fathers,”", "요셉이 말한 대로 \"이곳은 내 조상의 집이오\""], ["was to say the truth most simply and literally; for it was the very house Ruth ruled as the wife of Boaz, the very house in which Jesse and his ten sons, David the youngest, were born, the very house in which Samuel came seeking a king, and found him; the very house which David gave to the son of Barzillai, the friendly Gileadite; the very house in which Jeremiah, by prayer, rescued the remnant of his race flying before the Babylonians.", "한 것은, 더없이 단순하고 글자 그대로 진실을 말한 것이었다. 그것은 룻이 보아스의 아내로서 다스리던 바로 그 집이요, 이새와 그 열 아들이, 막내 다윗이 태어난 바로 그 집이요, 사무엘이 왕을 찾아와 그를 찾아낸 바로 그 집이요, 다윗이 친절한 길르앗 사람 바실래의 아들에게 준 바로 그 집이요, 예레미야가 기도로써 바벨론 사람들 앞에서 도망치던 제 겨레의 남은 자들을 구해 낸 바로 그 집이었다."]], [["The appeal was not without effect.", "그 호소는 효력이 없지 않았다."], ["The keeper of the gate slid down from the cedar block, and, laying his hand upon his beard, said, respectfully, “Rabbi, I cannot tell you when this door first opened in welcome to the traveller, but it was more than a thousand years ago; and in all that time there is no known instance of a good man turned away, save when there was no room to rest him in.", "문지기는 삼나무 토막에서 미끄러져 내려와, 제 수염에 손을 얹으며 공손히 말했다. \"랍비여, 이 문이 처음 나그네를 맞아 열린 때가 언제인지는 말씀드릴 수 없으나, 천 년도 더 된 일입니다. 그 오랜 세월 동안, 재워 줄 자리가 없을 때 말고는, 선한 사람이 쫓겨났다는 예는 알려진 바 없습니다."], ["If it has been so with the stranger, just cause must the steward have who says no to one of the line of David.", "낯선 이에게도 그러했다면, 다윗의 혈통인 분에게 안 된다고 하는 관리인에게는 마땅한 까닭이 있어야 할 것입니다."], ["Wherefore, I salute you again; and, if you care to go with me, I will show you that there is not a lodging-place left in the house; neither in the chambers, nor in the lewens, nor in the court—not even on the roof.", "그러므로 다시 한번 당신께 인사를 올립니다. 그리고 저와 함께 가 보시겠다면, 이 집에 묵을 자리가 한 군데도 남지 않았음을 보여 드리겠습니다. 방에도, 회랑에도, 안뜰에도 없으며 — 지붕 위에도 없습니다."], ["May I ask when you came?”", "언제 오셨는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“But now.”", "\"방금 막.\""]], [["The keeper smiled.", "문지기는 미소 지었다."]], [["“‘The stranger that dwelleth with you shall be as one born among you, and thou shalt love him as thyself.’ Is not that the law, Rabbi?”", "\"'너희와 함께 거하는 나그네를 너희 중에서 난 자같이 여기며 너 자신처럼 그를 사랑하라.' 그것이 율법이 아닙니까, 랍비여?\""]], [["Joseph was silent.", "요셉은 잠잠했다."]], [["“If it be the law, can I say to one a long time come, ‘Go thy way; another is here to take thy place?’”", "\"그것이 율법이라면, 오래전에 온 사람에게 '네 갈 길을 가거라. 여기 네 자리를 차지할 다른 이가 있다'고 말할 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["Yet Joseph held his peace.", "그래도 요셉은 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“And, if I said so, to whom would the place belong?", "\"그리고 내가 그렇게 말한다면, 그 자리는 누구의 것이 되겠습니까?"], ["See the many that have been waiting, some of them since noon.”", "더러는 정오부터 기다려 온 저 많은 사람들을 보십시오.\""]], [["“Who are all these people?”", "\"이 사람들은 다 누구요?\""], ["asked Joseph, turning to the crowd.", "요셉이 무리 쪽을 돌아보며 물었다."], ["“And why are they here at this time?”", "\"그리고 어찌하여 이때에 여기 모여 있소?\""]], [["“That which doubtless brought you, Rabbi—the decree of the Cæsar”—the keeper threw an interrogative glance at the Nazarene, then continued—“brought most of those who have lodging in the house.", "\"틀림없이 당신을 데려온 것 — 카이사르의 칙령이지요.\" 문지기는 나사렛 사람에게 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던지고는 말을 이었다. \"이 집에 묵고 있는 사람들 대부분도 그 때문에 왔습니다."], ["And yesterday the caravan passing from Damascus to Arabia and Lower Egypt arrived.", "그리고 어제는 다마스쿠스에서 아라비아와 하(下)이집트로 가는 대상이 도착했지요."], ["These you see here belong to it—men and camels.”", "여기 보이는 이들은 거기 딸린 사람들입니다 — 사람과 낙타 말이지요.\""]], [["Still Joseph persisted.", "그래도 요셉은 끈질겼다."]], [["“The court is large,” he said.", "\"안뜰이 넓지 않소.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but it is heaped with cargoes—with bales of silk, and pockets of spices, and goods of every kind.”", "\"그렇지요. 하지만 화물이 가득 쌓여 있습니다 — 비단 꾸러미며, 향료 자루며, 온갖 물품들로 말이지요.\""]], [["Then for a moment the face of the applicant lost its stolidity; the lustreless, staring eyes dropped.", "그러자 한순간 청하는 이의 얼굴에서 그 무덤덤함이 사라졌다. 빛 없이 멍하니 응시하던 눈이 아래로 떨어졌다."], ["With some warmth he next said, “I do not care for myself, but I have with me my wife, and the night is cold—colder on these heights than in Nazareth.", "그는 다소 격해진 어조로 다음과 같이 말했다. \"내 한 몸은 상관없소. 그러나 나에게는 아내가 함께 있고, 밤은 차갑소 — 이 높은 곳은 나사렛보다도 더 차갑소."], ["She cannot live in the open air.", "그 사람은 한데서는 지낼 수 없소."], ["Is there not room in the town?”", "그 마을에는 자리가 없겠소?\""]], [["“These people”—the keeper waved his hand to the throng before the door—“have all besought the town, and they report its accommodations all engaged.”", "\"이 사람들이\" — 문지기는 문 앞의 무리를 향해 손을 휘저었다 — \"모두 그 마을에 청해 보았으나, 묵을 자리가 죄다 찼다고 합니다.\""]], [["Again Joseph studied the ground, saying, half to himself, “She is so young!", "요셉은 다시 땅을 들여다보며, 반쯤 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"그 사람은 저토록 젊은데!"], ["if I make her bed on the hill, the frosts will kill her.”", "그 사람의 잠자리를 언덕에 마련한다면, 서리가 그 사람을 죽일 것이오.\""]], [["Then he spoke to the keeper again.", "그러더니 그는 문지기에게 다시 말했다."]], [["“It may be you knew her parents, Joachim and Anna, once of Bethlehem, and, like myself, of the line of David.”", "\"혹시 당신은 그 사람의 부모를 알았을지도 모르겠소. 요아킴과 안나라고, 한때 베들레헴에 살았으며, 나처럼 다윗의 혈통이오.\""]], [["“Yes, I knew them. They were good people.", "\"그렇습니다, 나는 그분들을 알았습니다. 선한 분들이었지요."], ["That was in my youth.”", "내 젊은 시절의 일입니다.\""]], [["This time the keeper’s eyes sought the ground in thought.", "이번에는 문지기의 눈이 생각에 잠겨 땅을 향했다."], ["Suddenly he raised his head.", "문득 그가 고개를 들었다."]], [["“If I cannot make room for you,”", "\"당신께 자리를 마련해 드릴 수는 없으나,\""], ["he said, “I cannot turn you away.", "그가 말했다. \"당신을 돌려보낼 수도 없습니다."], ["Rabbi, I will do the best I can for you.", "랍비여, 내가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 드리리다."], ["How many are of your party?”", "당신 일행은 몇 분이나 됩니까?\""]], [["Joseph reflected, then replied, “My wife and a friend with his family, from Beth-Dagon, a little town over by Joppa; in all, six of us.”", "요셉은 잠시 생각하고 대답했다. \"내 아내와, 욥바 너머 작은 고을 벳다곤에서 온 한 벗과 그 식구요. 모두 합쳐 여섯이오.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋습니다."], ["You shall not lie out on the ridge.", "능선에서 한데 잠을 자게 하지는 않겠습니다."], ["Bring your people, and hasten; for, when the sun goes down behind the mountain, you know the night comes quickly, and it is nearly there now.”", "당신 식구들을 데려오시되, 서두르십시오. 해가 산 뒤로 넘어가면 밤이 금세 온다는 것을 아시지 않습니까. 이제 거의 그 무렵입니다.\""]], [["“I give you the blessing of the houseless traveller; that of the sojourner will follow.”", "\"집 없는 나그네의 축복을 당신께 드리오. 머무는 자의 축복도 뒤따를 것이오.\""]], [["So saying, the Nazarene went back joyfully to Mary and the Beth-Dagonite.", "이렇게 말하고 나사렛 사람은 기쁘게 마리아와 벳다곤 사내에게로 돌아갔다."], ["In a little while the latter brought up his family, the women mounted on donkeys.", "잠시 후 사내가 제 식구를 데려왔으니, 여인들은 나귀에 타고 있었다."], ["The wife was matronly, the daughters were images of what she must have been in youth; and as they drew nigh the door, the keeper knew them to be of the humble class.", "그 아내는 기품 있는 부인이었고, 딸들은 그녀가 젊었을 적 모습 그대로였다. 그들이 문에 가까이 다가오자, 문지기는 그들이 비천한 계층임을 알아보았다."]], [["“This is she of whom I spoke,”", "\"이 사람이 내가 말한 그 사람이오.\""], ["said the Nazarene; “and these are our friends.”", "나사렛 사람이 말했다. \"그리고 이들은 우리의 벗들이오.\""]], [["Mary’s veil was raised.", "마리아의 너울이 걷혔다."]], [["“Blue eyes and hair of gold,”", "\"푸른 눈에 금빛 머리카락이라.\""], ["muttered the steward to himself, seeing but her.", "관리인은 그녀만을 보며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["“So looked the young king when he went to sing before Saul.”", "\"사울 앞에서 노래하러 갔던 그 젊은 왕도 저런 모습이었지.\""]], [["Then he took the leading-strap from Joseph, and said to Mary, “Peace to you, O daughter of David!”", "그러더니 그는 요셉에게서 이끄는 줄을 받아 들고, 마리아에게 말했다. \"평화가 있기를, 오 다윗의 딸이여!\""], ["Then to the others, “Peace to you all!”", "그러고는 다른 이들에게, \"모두에게 평화가 있기를!\""], ["Then to Joseph, “Rabbi, follow me.”", "그러고는 요셉에게, \"랍비여, 나를 따라오십시오.\""]], [["The party were conducted into a wide passage paved with stone, from which they entered the court of the khan.", "일행은 돌로 포장된 넓은 통로로 안내되었고, 거기서 칸의 안뜰로 들어섰다."], ["To a stranger the scene would have been curious; but they noticed the lewens that yawned darkly upon them from all sides, and the court itself, only to remark how crowded they were.", "낯선 이에게는 그 광경이 신기했을 테지만, 그들은 사방에서 어둡게 입을 벌리고 있는 회랑과 안뜰 자체를 보고도, 그저 얼마나 붐비는지를 알아챘을 뿐이었다."], ["By a lane reserved in the stowage of the cargoes, and thence by a passage similar to the one at the entrance, they emerged into the enclosure adjoining the house, and came upon camels, horses, and donkeys, tethered and dozing in close groups; among them were the keepers, men of many lands; and they, too, slept or kept silent watch.", "화물을 쌓아 두고 남겨 둔 좁은 길을 지나, 그리고 입구의 것과 비슷한 통로를 거쳐, 그들은 집에 딸린 우리로 나왔다. 그곳에는 낙타와 말과 나귀가 빽빽이 무리 지어 매인 채 졸고 있었다. 그 사이에는 여러 고장에서 온 짐승지기들이 있었으니, 그들 또한 잠들었거나 말없이 지켜보고 있었다."], ["They went down the slope of the crowded yard slowly, for the dull carriers of the women had wills of their own.", "그들은 붐비는 마당의 비탈을 천천히 내려갔으니, 여인들을 태운 굼뜬 짐승들에게도 제 고집이 있었던 까닭이다."], ["At length they turned into a path running towards the gray limestone bluff overlooking the khan on the west.", "마침내 그들은 칸의 서쪽을 내려다보는 잿빛 석회암 벼랑을 향해 난 오솔길로 접어들었다."]], [["“We are going to the cave,”", "\"우리는 동굴로 가고 있소.\""], ["said Joseph, laconically.", "요셉이 짧게 말했다."]], [["The guide lingered till Mary came to his side.", "안내자는 마리아가 제 곁에 올 때까지 걸음을 늦추었다."]], [["“The cave to which we are going,”", "\"우리가 가는 그 동굴은,\""], ["he said to her, “must have been a resort of your ancestor David.", "그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"당신 조상 다윗이 즐겨 찾던 곳이었음에 틀림없습니다."], ["From the field below us, and from the well down in the valley, he used to drive his flocks to it for safety; and afterwards, when he was king, he came back to the old house here for rest and health, bringing great trains of animals.", "우리 아래 있는 들판에서, 그리고 골짜기 아래의 우물에서, 그분은 양 떼를 안전을 위해 그 동굴로 몰곤 했지요. 그리고 훗날 왕이 되어서는, 휴식과 건강을 위해 이 옛집으로 돌아오면서 짐승들을 큰 무리로 거느리고 왔습니다."], ["The mangers yet remain as they were in his day.", "여물통은 그분의 시절 그대로 아직 남아 있습니다."], ["Better a bed on the floor where he has slept than one in the court-yard or out by the roadside.", "안뜰이나 길가의 잠자리보다는, 그분이 주무시던 바닥의 잠자리가 더 낫지요."], ["Ah, here is the house before the cave!”", "아, 여기 동굴 앞의 집입니다!\""]], [["This speech must not be taken as an apology for the lodging offered.", "이 말은 그들에게 마련해 준 잠자리에 대한 변명으로 받아들여서는 안 된다."], ["There was no need of apology.", "변명할 까닭이 없었던 것이다."], ["The place was the best then at disposal.", "그곳은 그때 내어 줄 수 있는 가장 좋은 자리였다."], ["The guests were simple folks, by habits of life easily satisfied.", "손님들은 소박한 사람들이라, 생활 습관으로 보아 쉽게 만족하는 이들이었다."], ["To the Jew of that period, moreover, abode in caverns was a familiar idea, made so by every-day occurrences, and by what he heard of Sabbaths in the synagogues.", "더구나 그 시대의 유대인에게 동굴에서 머무는 것은 친숙한 관념이었으니, 날마다 일어나는 일들과 회당에서 안식일에 들은 이야기들이 그것을 그렇게 만들었다."], ["How much of Jewish history, how many of the many exciting incidents in that history, had transpired in caves!", "유대 역사의 얼마나 많은 부분이, 그 역사에서 손에 땀을 쥐게 하는 그 많은 사건들 가운데 얼마나 많은 것이 동굴에서 일어났던가!"], ["Yet further, these people were Jews of Bethlehem, with whom the idea was especially commonplace; for their locality abounded with caves great and small, some of which had been dwelling-places from the time of the Emim and Horites.", "게다가 이들은 베들레헴의 유대인이었으니, 그들에게 그 관념은 더욱 예사로운 것이었다. 그들의 고장에는 크고 작은 동굴이 가득했으며, 그 가운데 더러는 에밈과 호리 사람들의 시절부터 거처로 쓰여 온 것들이었다."], ["No more was there offence to them in the fact that the cavern to which they were being taken had been, or was, a stable.", "그들이 안내받고 있는 그 동굴이 한때 마구간이었거나 지금도 마구간이라는 사실 또한 그들에게는 거슬릴 것이 없었다."], ["They were the descendants of a race of herdsmen, whose flocks habitually shared both their habitations and wanderings.", "그들은 목자들의 겨레의 후손이었으니, 그들의 가축은 으레 그들의 거처와 떠돌이살이를 함께 나누었다."], ["In keeping with a custom derived from Abraham, the tent of the Bedawin yet shelters his horses and children alike.", "아브라함에게서 물려받은 관습에 따라, 베두인의 천막은 지금도 말과 아이를 똑같이 한지붕 아래 재운다."], ["So they obeyed the keeper cheerfully, and gazed at the house, feeling only a natural curiosity.", "그래서 그들은 즐거이 문지기를 따랐고, 그저 자연스러운 호기심만을 느끼며 그 집을 바라보았다."], ["Everything associated with the history of David was interesting to them.", "다윗의 역사와 얽힌 것이라면 무엇이든 그들에게는 흥미로웠다."]], [["The building was low and narrow, projecting but a little from the rock to which it was joined at the rear, and wholly without a window.", "그 건물은 낮고 좁았으며, 뒤편으로 맞붙은 바위에서 조금만 튀어나와 있었고, 창이라고는 도무지 없었다."], ["In its blank front there was a door, swung on enormous hinges, and thickly daubed with ochreous clay.", "그 휑한 앞면에는 문 하나가 거대한 돌쩌귀에 매달려 있었으며, 황토 진흙이 두텁게 발려 있었다."], ["While the wooden bolt of the lock was being pushed back, the women were assisted from their pillions. Upon the opening of the door, the keeper called out,", "자물쇠의 나무 빗장이 뒤로 밀리는 동안, 여인들은 안장 뒤 받침에서 내려지는 것을 거들어 받았다. 문이 열리자 문지기가 외쳤다."]], [["“Come in!”", "\"들어오십시오!\""]], [["The guests entered, and stared about them.", "손님들은 들어가 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["It became apparent immediately that the house was but a mask or covering for the mouth of a natural cave or grotto, probably forty feet long, nine or ten high, and twelve or fifteen in width.", "그 집이 천연 동굴, 곧 동혈(洞穴)의 입구를 가린 가면 또는 덮개에 지나지 않음이 곧바로 드러났다. 그 동굴은 길이가 아마 12미터쯤, 높이가 3미터 남짓, 너비가 4미터에서 5미터쯤 되어 보였다."], ["The light streamed through the doorway, over an uneven floor, falling upon piles of grain and fodder, and earthenware and household property, occupying the centre of the chamber. Along the sides were mangers, low enough for sheep, and built of stones laid in cement.", "빛이 문간을 통해 고르지 못한 바닥 위로 흘러들어, 방 한가운데를 차지한 곡식과 꼴 더미, 질그릇과 살림살이 위에 떨어졌다. 양옆을 따라서는 여물통이 있었으니, 양이 닿을 만큼 나직하게, 시멘트로 돌을 쌓아 지은 것이었다."], ["There were no stalls or partitions of any kind.", "칸막이나 마구간 칸 따위는 어디에도 없었다."], ["Dust and chaff yellowed the floor, filled all the crevices and hollows, and thickened the spider-webs, which dropped from the ceiling like bits of dirty linen; otherwise the place was cleanly, and, to appearance, as comfortable as any of the arched lewens of the khan proper.", "먼지와 검불이 바닥을 누렇게 물들이고 온갖 틈과 우묵한 곳을 가득 메웠으며, 천장에서 더러운 아마포 조각처럼 드리워진 거미줄을 두텁게 했다. 그 밖에는 그곳은 깨끗했으며, 보기에는 칸 본채의 어느 아치형 회랑 못지않게 편안했다."], ["In fact, a cave was the model and first suggestion of the lewen.", "사실 동굴이야말로 회랑의 본보기이자 그 첫 발상이었다."]], [["“Come in!”", "\"들어오십시오!\""], ["said the guide.", "안내자가 말했다."], ["“These piles upon the floor are for travellers like yourselves.", "\"바닥에 쌓인 이것들은 당신들 같은 나그네를 위한 것입니다."], ["Take what of them you need.”", "필요한 만큼 가져다 쓰십시오.\""]], [["Then he spoke to Mary.", "그러더니 그는 마리아에게 말했다."]], [["“Can you rest here?”", "\"여기서 쉴 만하겠습니까?\""]], [["“The place is sanctified,”", "\"이곳은 거룩한 곳이군요.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 답했다."]], [["“I leave you then.", "\"그러면 저는 물러가겠습니다."], ["Peace be with you all!”", "모두에게 평화가 있기를!\""]], [["When he was gone, they busied themselves making the cave habitable.", "그가 떠나자, 그들은 동굴을 지낼 만한 곳으로 만드느라 분주히 움직였다."]], [["# CHAPTER X.", "# CHAPTER X."]], [["At a certain hour in the evening the shouting and stir of the people in and about the khan ceased; at the same time, every Israelite, if not already upon his feet, arose, solemnized his face, looked towards Jerusalem, crossed his hands upon his breast, and prayed; for it was the sacred ninth hour, when sacrifices were offered in the temple on Moriah, and God was supposed to be there.", "저녁 어느 시각이 되자, 객사 안팎에서 사람들이 떠들고 부산하던 소리가 그쳤다. 그와 동시에 이스라엘 사람들은 아직 서 있지 않은 자라면 모두 일어나, 얼굴을 엄숙히 하고, 예루살렘 쪽을 바라보며, 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 기도를 올렸다. 이때가 거룩한 제구시(第九時), 곧 모리아 산의 성전에서 제물을 바치는 시각이요, 하느님이 그곳에 계시다고 여겨지는 시각이었기 때문이다."], ["When the hands of the worshippers fell down, the commotion broke forth again; everybody hastened to bread, or to make his pallet.", "기도하던 이들의 손이 내려오자, 다시 소란이 일었다. 저마다 서둘러 빵을 먹거나 잠자리를 폈다."], ["A little later, the lights were put out, and there was silence, and then sleep.", "조금 뒤에는 불이 꺼지고, 정적이 깃들었으며, 이윽고 잠이 찾아왔다."]], [["About midnight some one on the roof cried out, “What light is that in the sky?", "자정 무렵, 지붕 위에 있던 누군가가 외쳤다. \"하늘에 저 빛은 무엇인가?"], ["Awake, brethren, awake and see!”", "일어나라, 형제들이여, 일어나서 보아라!\""]], [["The people, half asleep, sat up and looked; then they became wide-awake, though wonder-struck.", "반쯤 잠든 사람들이 몸을 일으켜 바라보았다. 곧 그들은 경이에 사로잡힌 채 말짱히 깨어났다."], ["And the stir spread to the court below, and into the lewens; soon the entire tenantry of the house and court and enclosure were out gazing at the sky.", "그 술렁임은 아래 안뜰로, 그리고 회랑으로 번져 갔다. 머지않아 집과 안뜰과 울타리 안에 머물던 모든 사람이 밖으로 나와 하늘을 우러러보았다."]], [["And this was what they saw.", "그들이 본 것은 이러했다."], ["A ray of light, beginning at a height immeasurably beyond the nearest stars, and dropping obliquely to the earth; at its top, a diminishing point; at its base, many furlongs in width; its sides blending softly with the darkness of the night, its core a roseate electrical splendor.", "한 줄기 빛이 가장 가까운 별보다도 헤아릴 수 없이 높은 곳에서 시작되어 비스듬히 땅으로 떨어지고 있었다. 꼭대기는 한 점으로 줄어들었고, 밑동은 너비가 수백 미터에 이르렀다. 그 가장자리는 밤의 어둠과 부드럽게 어우러졌고, 그 중심은 장밋빛으로 번쩍이는 광채였다."], ["The apparition seemed to rest on the nearest mountain southeast of the town, making a pale corona along the line of the summit.", "그 빛은 마을 남동쪽의 가장 가까운 산 위에 머무는 듯하여, 산마루를 따라 희미한 광환(光環)을 드리웠다."], ["The khan was touched luminously, so that those upon the roof saw each other’s faces, all filled with wonder.", "객사도 그 빛에 닿아 환해진 까닭에, 지붕 위에 있던 이들은 서로의 얼굴을 볼 수 있었으니, 그 얼굴마다 경이로 가득했다."]], [["Steadily, through minutes, the ray lingered, and then the wonder changed to awe and fear; the timid trembled; the boldest spoke in whispers.", "빛은 몇 분에 걸쳐 한결같이 머물렀고, 그러자 경이는 외경과 두려움으로 바뀌었다. 겁 많은 자들은 떨었고, 가장 대담한 자들도 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“Saw you ever the like?” asked one.", "\"이런 것을 본 적이 있는가?\" 하고 한 사람이 물었다."]], [["“It seems just over the mountain there.", "\"저 산 바로 위에 있는 것 같소."], ["I cannot tell what it is, nor did I ever see anything like it,” was the answer.", "무엇인지 알 수 없고, 나도 저런 것은 본 적이 없소.\" 하는 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Can it be that a star has burst and fallen?” asked another, his tongue faltering.", "\"별이 터져서 떨어진 것이 아닐까?\" 하고 또 한 사람이 물었으나, 그 말끝은 떨렸다."]], [["“When a star falls, its light goes out.”", "\"별이 떨어질 때는 그 빛이 꺼지는 법이오.\""]], [["“I have it!”", "\"알겠소!\""], ["cried one, confidently. “The shepherds have seen a lion, and made fires to keep him from the flocks.”", "하고 한 사람이 자신 있게 외쳤다. \"목자들이 사자를 보고, 양 떼를 지키려고 불을 피운 것이오.\""]], [["The men next the speaker drew a breath of relief, and said, “Yes, that is it!", "말한 이의 곁에 있던 사람들이 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"그렇소, 바로 그것이오!"], ["The flocks were grazing in the valley over there to-day.”", "오늘 양 떼가 저쪽 골짜기에서 풀을 뜯고 있었소.\""]], [["A bystander dispelled the comfort.", "곁에 있던 한 사람이 그 위안을 깨뜨렸다."]], [["“No, no! Though all the wood in all the valleys of Judah was brought together in one pile and fired, the blaze would not throw a light so strong and high.”", "\"아니오, 아니오! 유다의 온 골짜기에 있는 나무를 모조리 한 더미에 모아 불을 지른다 해도, 그 불길이 저토록 강하고 높은 빛을 던지지는 못할 것이오.\""]], [["After that there was silence on the house-top, broken but once again while the mystery continued.", "그 뒤로 지붕 위에는 정적이 흘렀고, 그 신비가 이어지는 동안 단 한 번 더 깨졌을 뿐이다."]], [["“Brethren!”", "\"형제들이여!\""], ["exclaimed a Jew of venerable mien, “what we see is the ladder our father Jacob saw in his dream.", "하고 위엄 있는 풍모의 한 유대인이 외쳤다. \"우리가 보는 것은 우리 조상 야곱이 꿈에서 본 사다리요."], ["Blessed be the Lord God of our fathers!”", "우리 조상의 주 하느님께 복이 있을지어다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XI", "# CHAPTER XI"]], [["A mile and a half, it may be two miles, southeast of Bethlehem, there is a plain separated from the town by an intervening swell of the mountain.", "베들레헴에서 남동쪽으로 2.5킬로미터, 어쩌면 3킬로미터쯤 떨어진 곳에, 산의 솟아오른 능선을 사이에 두고 마을과 갈라진 평원이 하나 있다."], ["Besides being well sheltered from the north winds, the vale was covered with a growth of sycamore, dwarf-oak, and pine trees, while in the glens and ravines adjoining there were thickets of olive and mulberry; all at this season of the year invaluable for the support of sheep, goats, and cattle, of which the wandering flocks consisted.", "북풍을 잘 막아 주는 데다, 이 골짜기는 무화과나무와 떡갈나무와 소나무가 자라 우거져 있었고, 인접한 협곡과 골에는 올리브나무와 뽕나무 덤불이 빽빽했다. 모두 이 계절에는 떠도는 양 떼와 염소 떼와 소 떼를 먹이는 데 더없이 귀한 것들이었다."]], [["At the side farthest from the town, close under a bluff, there was an extensive marah, or sheepcot, ages old.", "마을에서 가장 멀리 떨어진 쪽, 벼랑 바로 아래에는 아득한 옛날부터 있어 온 널따란 마라, 곧 양 우리가 자리하고 있었다."], ["In some long-forgotten foray, the building had been unroofed and almost demolished.", "오래전 잊힌 어느 약탈 때에 지붕이 날아가고 거의 허물어진 건물이었다."], ["The enclosure attached to it remained intact, however, and that was of more importance to the shepherds who drove their charges thither than the house itself.", "그러나 거기 딸린 울타리만큼은 온전히 남아 있었고, 가축을 몰고 그리로 오는 목자들에게는 그 울타리가 집 자체보다 더 요긴했다."], ["The stone wall around the lot was high as a man’s head, yet not so high but that sometimes a panther or a lion, hungering from the wilderness, leaped boldly in.", "둘레를 두른 돌담은 사람 키만큼 높았으나, 그래도 광야에서 굶주려 온 표범이나 사자가 이따금 대담하게 뛰어들 만큼은 낮았다."], ["On the inner side of the wall, and as an additional security against the constant danger, a hedge of the rhamnus had been planted, an invention so successful that now a sparrow could hardly penetrate the overtopping branches, armed as they were with great clusters of thorns hard as spikes.", "담 안쪽에는 그 끊임없는 위험에 대비한 또 하나의 방비로 갈매나무 울타리를 심어 두었는데, 그 고안이 어찌나 잘 들어맞았는지, 못처럼 단단한 가시가 큼직한 송이를 이룬 채 빽빽이 솟은 가지들 사이로는 이제 참새 한 마리조차 비집고 들기 어려웠다."]], [["The day of the occurrences which occupy the preceding chapters, a number of shepherds, seeking fresh walks for their flocks, led them up to this plain; and from early morning the groves had been made ring with calls, and the blows of axes, the bleating of sheep and goats, the tinkling of bells, the lowing of cattle, and the barking of dogs.", "앞 장들에서 다룬 일이 벌어진 그날, 양 떼에게 새 풀밭을 찾아 주려는 목자 몇이 가축을 이 평원으로 몰고 올라왔다. 이른 아침부터 숲은 부르는 소리와 도끼질 소리, 양과 염소의 울음소리, 방울 딸랑이는 소리, 소의 음매 소리, 개 짖는 소리로 울려 퍼졌다."], ["When the sun went down, they led the way to the marah, and by nightfall had everything safe in the field; then they kindled a fire down by the gate, partook of their humble supper, and sat down to rest and talk, leaving one on watch.", "해가 지자 그들은 마라로 길을 잡았고, 어둠이 내릴 무렵에는 모든 것을 우리 안에 안전히 들여놓았다. 그런 다음 문 옆에 불을 피우고, 변변찮은 저녁을 나눠 먹은 뒤, 한 사람에게 망을 맡기고는 둘러앉아 쉬며 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["There were six of these men, omitting the watchman; and afterwhile they assembled in a group near the fire, some sitting, some lying prone. As they went bareheaded habitually, their hair stood out in thick, coarse, sunburnt shocks; their beard covered their throats, and fell in mats down the breast; mantles of the skin of kids and lambs, with the fleece on, wrapped them from neck to knee, leaving the arms exposed; broad belts girthed the rude garments to their waists; their sandals were of the coarsest quality; from their right shoulders hung scrips containing food and selected stones for slings, with which they were armed; on the ground near each one lay his crook, a symbol of his calling and a weapon of offence.", "망보는 이를 빼면 그들은 모두 여섯이었다. 얼마 지나지 않아 불 가까이에 무리를 이루어 모였으니, 더러는 앉고 더러는 엎드려 누웠다. 늘 맨머리로 다니는 까닭에 머리카락은 햇볕에 그을린 채 거칠고 두껍게 곤두서 있었다. 수염은 목을 덮고 가슴까지 덥수룩하게 흘러내렸다. 새끼 염소와 새끼 양의 가죽으로 만든, 털이 그대로 달린 망토가 목에서 무릎까지 그들을 감쌌으나 팔은 드러나 있었다. 그 투박한 옷을 넓은 띠로 허리에 둘러 동였다. 신은 가장 거친 종류의 샌들이었다. 오른쪽 어깨에는 먹을 것과, 그들이 무기로 삼은 무릿매에 쓸 골라 둔 돌멩이를 담은 자루가 매달려 있었다. 저마다 옆 땅바닥에는 지팡이가 놓여 있었으니, 그것은 그들 소명의 표징이자 공격의 무기였다."]], [["Such were the shepherds of Judea!", "이런 자들이 유대의 목자였다!"], ["In appearance, rough and savage as the gaunt dogs sitting with them around the blaze; in fact, simple-minded, tender-hearted; effects due, in part, to the primitive life they led, but chiefly to their constant care of things lovable and helpless.", "겉모습은 불가에 함께 둘러앉은 야윈 개들처럼 거칠고 사나웠으나, 실은 마음이 단순하고 정이 깊었다. 그것은 한편으로는 그들이 살아온 원시적인 삶에서 비롯된 것이었으나, 무엇보다도 사랑스럽고 가냘픈 것들을 끊임없이 돌보아 온 데서 온 것이었다."]], [["They rested and talked, and their talk was all about their flocks, a dull theme to the world, yet a theme which was all the world to them. If in narrative they dwelt long upon affairs of trifling moment; if one of them omitted nothing of detail in recounting the loss of a lamb, the relation between him and the unfortunate should be remembered: at birth it became his charge, his to keep all its days, to help over the floods, to carry down the hollows, to name and train; it was to be his companion, his object of thought and interest, the subject of his will; it was to enliven and share his wanderings; in its defense he might be called on to face the lion or robber—to die.", "그들은 쉬며 이야기를 나누었는데, 그 이야기는 온통 양 떼에 관한 것이었다. 세상 사람들에게는 따분한 화제였으나, 그들에게는 그것이 곧 온 세상이었다. 그들이 이야기 가운데 하찮은 일에 오래도록 머무른다면, 또 그들 가운데 누가 새끼 양 한 마리를 잃은 사연을 낱낱이 빠짐없이 늘어놓는다면, 그와 그 가엾은 짐승 사이의 인연을 떠올려 보아야 한다. 양은 태어나면서부터 그가 맡은 것이 되어, 평생토록 그가 지켜야 할 것이 되고, 물을 건널 때 거들어 주어야 하고, 움푹한 골을 내려갈 때 안아 주어야 하며, 이름을 지어 주고 길들여야 할 것이 되었다. 양은 그의 길동무가 되고, 그의 생각과 관심의 대상이 되며, 그의 뜻을 받드는 존재가 되었다. 양은 그의 떠도는 길을 생기 있게 하고 함께 나누었다. 그것을 지키기 위해서라면 그는 사자나 도적과 맞서야 할지도 모르고, 죽어야 할지도 몰랐다."]], [["The great events, such as blotted out nations and changed the mastery of the world, were trifles to them, if perchance they came to their knowledge.", "민족을 지워 버리고 세상의 패권을 뒤바꾸는 큰 사건들도, 행여 그들의 귀에 들어온다 한들 그들에게는 사소한 일이었다."], ["Of what Herod was doing in this city or that, building palaces and gymnasia, and indulging forbidden practises, they occasionally heard.", "헤롯이 이 도시 저 도시에서 무엇을 하는지, 궁전과 경기장을 짓고 금지된 짓에 빠져 있다는 소식을 그들은 이따금 들었다."], ["As was her habit in those days, Rome did not wait for people slow to inquire about her; she came to them.", "그 시절의 습성대로 로마는 자기에 대해 굼뜨게 묻는 사람들을 기다려 주지 않았다. 로마가 먼저 그들에게 찾아왔다."], ["Over the hills along which he was leading his lagging herd, or in the fastnesses in which he was hiding them, not unfrequently the shepherd was startled by the blare of trumpets, and, peering out, beheld a cohort, sometimes a legion, in march; and when the glittering crests were gone, and the excitement incident to the intrusion over, he bent himself to evolve the meaning of the eagles and gilded globes of the soldiery, and the charm of a life so the opposite of his own.", "더디게 따라오는 가축 떼를 몰고 가는 언덕에서든, 그것들을 숨겨 둔 외진 산골에서든, 목자는 적잖이 나팔 소리에 깜짝 놀라곤 했다. 내다보면 보병 대대가, 때로는 군단 하나가 행군해 가고 있었다. 그러다 번쩍이는 투구 장식이 사라지고, 그 침입이 불러일으킨 동요가 가라앉으면, 그는 군대의 독수리 기와 도금한 구슬 장식이 무엇을 뜻하는지, 또 제 삶과는 그토록 정반대인 그 삶의 매력이 무엇인지 곰곰이 헤아려 보는 것이었다."]], [["Yet these men, rude and simple as they were, had a knowledge and a wisdom of their own.", "그러나 이 사람들은 투박하고 단순하기는 해도 나름의 지식과 지혜를 지니고 있었다."], ["On Sabbaths they were accustomed to purify themselves, and go up into the synagogues, and sit on the benches farthest from the ark.", "안식일이면 그들은 으레 몸을 정결히 하고 회당에 올라가, 궤에서 가장 멀리 떨어진 자리에 앉았다. 차잔이 토라를 받들고 돌 때, 그것에 더없이 열렬히 입 맞추는 이가 그들이었다."], ["When the chazzan bore the Torah round, none kissed it with greater zest; when the sheliach read the text, none listened to the interpreter with more absolute faith; and none took away with them more of the elder’s sermon, or gave it more thought afterwards.", "셸리아흐가 본문을 읽을 때, 더없이 굳은 믿음으로 해석하는 자의 말에 귀 기울이는 이가 그들이었다. 또 장로의 설교를 누구보다 많이 가슴에 담아 가, 나중에 누구보다 깊이 곱씹는 이가 그들이었다. 셰마의 한 구절 속에서 그들은 단순한 삶에 필요한 모든 가르침과 모든 율법을 찾아냈다."], ["In a verse of the Shema they found all the learning and all the law of their simple lives—that their Lord was One God, and that they must love him with all their souls.", "곧 자기들의 주님은 한 분 하나님이시며, 온 마음을 다해 그분을 사랑해야 한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그들은 그분을 사랑했으니, 그 지혜야말로 왕들의 지혜를 뛰어넘는 것이었다. * 차잔: 회당에서 예배를 돕는 봉사자."], ["And they loved him, and such was their wisdom, surpassing that of kings.", "셸리아흐: 회중을 대표해 경문을 낭독하는 사람. 셰마: \"이스라엘아 들으라, 우리 하나님 여호와는 오직 유일한 여호와시니\"로 시작하는 유대교의 신앙 고백."]], [["While they talked, and before the first watch was over, one by one the shepherds went to sleep, each lying where he had sat.", "이야기를 나누는 동안, 첫 야경이 채 끝나기도 전에 목자들은 하나둘 잠이 들었으니, 저마다 앉았던 그 자리에 누웠다."]], [["The night, like most nights of the winter season in the hill country, was clear, crisp, and sparkling with stars.", "그 밤은 산악 지대의 겨울철 밤이 대개 그러하듯, 맑고 쌀쌀하고 별빛으로 반짝였다. 바람 한 점 없었다."], ["There was no wind. The atmosphere seemed never so pure, and the stillness was more than silence; it was a holy hush, a warning that heaven was stooping low to whisper some good thing to the listening earth.", "대기가 그토록 깨끗했던 적이 없는 듯했고, 그 고요함은 단순한 적막 이상이었다. 그것은 거룩한 정적이었으니, 하늘이 귀 기울이는 땅에 무언가 좋은 소식을 속삭이려고 나직이 몸을 굽히고 있다는 예고였다."]], [["By the gate, hugging his mantle close, the watchman walked; at times he stopped, attracted by a stir among the sleeping herds, or by a jackal’s cry off on the mountain-side.", "문 옆에서 망보는 이는 망토를 바싹 여미고 걸었다. 이따금 그는 잠든 가축 떼 사이의 부스럭거림이나, 산비탈 저편에서 들려오는 자칼의 울음소리에 끌려 걸음을 멈추곤 했다."], ["The midnight was slow coming to him; but at last it came.", "한밤중은 더디게 다가왔으나, 마침내 찾아왔다."], ["His task was done; now for the dreamless sleep with which labor blesses its wearied children!", "그의 일은 끝났다. 이제 고된 노동이 지친 자녀들에게 베푸는 꿈 없는 잠을 누릴 차례였다!"], ["He moved towards the fire, but paused; a light was breaking around him, soft and white, like the moon’s.", "그는 불 쪽으로 발을 옮기다 멈칫했다. 달빛처럼 부드럽고 흰 빛이 그의 둘레로 퍼지고 있었다. 그는 숨을 죽이고 기다렸다."], ["He waited breathlessly.", "빛은 점점 짙어졌다."], ["The light deepened; things before invisible came to view; he saw the whole field, and all it sheltered.", "조금 전까지 보이지 않던 것들이 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 들판 전체와, 그 안에 깃든 모든 것을 보았다."], ["A chill sharper than that of the frosty air—a chill of fear—smote him.", "서리 내린 공기보다 더 날카로운 한기가, 두려움의 한기가 그를 엄습했다. 그가 위를 올려다보니, 별들이 사라지고 없었다."], ["He looked up; the stars were gone; the light was dropping as from a window in the sky; as he looked, it became a splendor; then, in terror, he cried,", "빛은 하늘의 창에서 쏟아져 내리는 듯했다. 바라보는 사이 그것은 찬란한 광휘로 변했다. 그러자 그는 공포에 질려 외쳤다."]], [["“Awake, awake!”", "\"일어나, 일어나!\""]], [["Up sprang the dogs, and, howling, ran away.", "개들이 벌떡 일어나 짖어 대며 달아났다."]], [["The herds rushed together bewildered.", "가축 떼는 어쩔 줄 모른 채 한데 뒤엉켰다."]], [["The men clambered to their feet, weapons in hand.", "사람들은 무기를 손에 쥐고 비틀거리며 일어섰다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이야?\""], ["they asked, in one voice.", "그들이 한목소리로 물었다."]], [["“See!”", "\"봐!\""], ["cried the watchman, “the sky is on fire!”", "망보는 이가 외쳤다. \"하늘이 불타고 있어!\""]], [["Suddenly the light became intolerably bright, and they covered their eyes, and dropped upon their knees; then, as their souls shrank with fear, they fell upon their faces blind and fainting, and would have died had not a voice said to them,", "별안간 빛이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 밝아지자, 그들은 두 눈을 가리고 무릎을 꿇었다. 그러다 영혼이 두려움에 움츠러들며 그들은 앞을 못 보고 정신을 잃은 채 얼굴을 땅에 묻고 엎드렸으니, 한 음성이 그들에게 말하지 않았더라면 죽고 말았을 것이다. \"두려워하지 말라!\""]], [["“Fear not!” And they listened.", "그러자 그들은 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Fear not: for behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people.”", "\"두려워하지 말라. 보라, 내가 온 백성에게 미칠 큰 기쁨의 좋은 소식을 너희에게 전하노라.\""]], [["The voice, in sweetness and soothing more than human, and low and clear, penetrated all their being, and filled them with assurance.", "그 음성은 사람의 것이라 하기에는 너무도 감미롭고 위로가 깃든, 나직하고 또렷한 음성으로, 그들의 온 존재에 스며들어 그들을 확신으로 채웠다. 그들이 무릎을 짚고 일어나 경배하는 마음으로 우러러보니, 거대한 영광 한가운데에 사람의 형상이 나타나 있었다. 더없이 새하얀 옷을 걸쳤고, 그 어깨 위로는 빛나며 접힌 날개의 끝이 우뚝 솟아 있었다."], ["They rose upon their knees, and, looking worshipfully, beheld in the centre of a great glory the appearance of a man, clad in a robe intensely white; above its shoulders towered the tops of wings shining and folded; a star over its forehead glowed with steady lustre, brilliant as Hesperus; its hands were stretched towards them in blessing; its face was serene and divinely beautiful.", "이마 위의 별 하나가 헤스페로스처럼 찬란하게 변함없는 광채로 빛났다. 두 손은 축복을 내리듯 그들을 향해 뻗쳐 있었고, 그 얼굴은 평온하고 거룩할 만큼 아름다웠다. * 헤스페로스: 그리스 신화에서 저녁별, 곧 금성을 이르는 이름."]], [["They had often heard, and, in their simple way, talked, of angels; and they doubted not now, but said, in their hearts, The glory of God is about us, and this is he who of old came to the prophet by the river of Ulai.", "그들은 천사에 관해 자주 들어 왔고, 그들 나름의 소박한 방식으로 이야기도 해 왔다. 그래서 이제 그들은 의심하지 않고, 마음속으로 말했다. 하나님의 영광이 우리를 둘러쌌으니, 이는 옛적에 울라이 강가에서 예언자에게 나타나셨던 그분이로다."]], [["Directly the angel continued:", "곧이어 천사가 말을 이었다."]], [["“For unto you is born this day, in the city of David, a Savior, which is Christ the Lord!”", "\"오늘 다윗의 동네에 너희를 위하여 구주가 나셨으니, 곧 그리스도 주시니라!\""]], [["Again there was a rest, while the words sank into their minds.", "다시 한동안 침묵이 흘렀으니, 그동안 그 말이 그들의 마음속에 가라앉았다."]], [["“And this shall be a sign unto you,”", "\"너희가 이것으로 표징을 삼을지니라.\""], ["the annunciator said next. “Ye shall find the babe, wrapped in swaddling-clothes, lying in a manger.”", "알리는 이가 다음으로 말했다. \"너희가 강보에 싸여 구유에 누인 아기를 보리라.\""]], [["The herald spoke not again; his good tidings were told; yet he stayed awhile.", "알리는 이는 다시 말하지 않았다. 그의 좋은 소식은 다 전해졌다."], ["Suddenly the light, of which he seemed the centre, turned roseate and began to tremble; then up, far as the men could see, there was flashing of white wings, and coming and going of radiant forms, and voices as of a multitude chanting in unison,", "그래도 그는 잠시 머물렀다. 별안간 그가 중심에 자리한 듯하던 빛이 장밋빛으로 물들며 떨리기 시작했다. 그러더니 위쪽으로, 사람들이 볼 수 있는 한 멀리까지 흰 날개가 번득이고, 눈부신 형상들이 오가며, 한목소리로 노래하는 무리의 음성이 울렸다."]], [["“Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good-will towards men!”", "\"지극히 높은 곳에서는 하나님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 그가 기뻐하신 사람들 중에 평화로다!\""]], [["Not once the praise, but many times.", "한 번이 아니라 여러 번 거듭하여 찬송했다."]], [["Then the herald raised his eyes as seeking approval of one far off; his wings stirred, and spread slowly and majestically, on their upper side white as snow, in the shadow vari-tinted, like mother-of-pearl; when they were expanded many cubits beyond his stature, he arose lightly, and, without effort, floated out of view, taking the light up with him.", "이윽고 알리는 이가 멀리 있는 누군가의 허락을 구하듯 눈을 들었다. 그의 날개가 움직이더니 천천히 위엄 있게 펼쳐졌으니, 윗면은 눈처럼 희고 그늘진 쪽은 진주조개처럼 갖가지 빛깔로 어른거렸다. 날개가 그의 키보다 몇 큐빗이나 더 넓게 펼쳐지자, 그는 가뿐히 떠올라 힘들이는 기색 없이 시야 밖으로 떠가며, 그 빛을 함께 거두어 갔다."], ["Long after he was gone, down from the sky fell the refrain in measure mellowed by distance, “Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good-will towards men.”", "그가 사라진 한참 뒤까지도, 거리에 부드럽게 녹아든 가락이 하늘에서 떨어져 내렸다. \"지극히 높은 곳에서는 하나님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 그가 기뻐하신 사람들 중에 평화로다.\" * 큐빗: 팔꿈치에서 가운뎃손가락 끝까지의 길이로, 약 45센티미터에 해당하는 옛 척도."]], [["When the shepherds came fully to their senses, they stared at each other stupidly, until one of them said, “It was Gabriel, the Lord’s messenger unto men.”", "목자들이 완전히 정신을 차렸을 때, 그들은 한 사람이 입을 열기까지 서로 멍하니 바라보았다. \"사람들에게 보내신 주님의 사자, 가브리엘이었어.\""]], [["None answered.", "아무도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Christ the Lord is born; said he not so?”", "\"그리스도 주께서 나셨다고, 그렇게 말씀하지 않았나?\""]], [["Then another recovered his voice, and replied, “That is what he said.”", "그러자 다른 이가 목소리를 되찾고 대답했다. \"그렇게 말씀하셨지.\""]], [["“And did he not also say, in the city of David, which is our Bethlehem yonder.", "\"또 다윗의 동네라고, 곧 저쪽 우리 베들레헴이라고 말씀하지 않았나?"], ["And that we should find him a babe in swaddling-clothes?”", "그리고 우리가 강보에 싸인 아기를 보게 되리라고?\""]], [["“And lying in a manger.”", "\"구유에 누인 아기를 말이지.\""]], [["The first speaker gazed into the fire thoughtfully, but at length said, like one possessed of a sudden resolve, “There is but one place in Bethlehem where there are mangers; but one, and that is in the cave near the old khan.", "처음 말한 이가 생각에 잠겨 불을 들여다보다가, 마침내 문득 결심이라도 한 듯이 말했다. \"베들레헴에서 구유가 있는 곳은 단 한 군데뿐이야. 단 한 군데, 옛 여관 가까이에 있는 동굴이지."], ["Brethren, let us go see this thing which has come to pass.", "형제들이여, 가서 이 일어난 일을 봅시다."], ["The priests and doctors have been a long time looking for the Christ.", "제사장과 학자들은 오래도록 그리스도를 기다려 왔소."], ["Now he is born, and the Lord has given us a sign by which to know him.", "이제 그분이 나셨고, 주께서 우리에게 그분을 알아볼 표징을 주셨소."], ["Let us go up and worship him.”", "올라가서 그분께 경배합시다.\""]], [["“But the flocks!”", "\"하지만 양 떼는!\""]], [["“The Lord will take care of them.", "\"주께서 돌보아 주실 거요."], ["Let us make haste.”", "서두릅시다.\""]], [["Then they all arose and left the marah.", "그리하여 그들은 모두 일어나 마라를 떠났다."]], [["Around the mountain and through the town they passed, and came to the gate of the khan, where there was a man on watch.", "산을 돌아 마을을 가로질러 그들은 여관 문에 이르렀는데, 거기에는 망보는 사람이 하나 있었다."]], [["“What would you have?” he asked.", "\"무슨 일이오?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“We have seen and heard great things to-night,”", "\"오늘 밤 우리는 엄청난 일을 보고 들었소.\""], ["they replied.", "그들이 대답했다."]], [["“Well, we, too, have seen great things, but heard nothing.", "\"글쎄, 우리도 굉장한 것을 보긴 했으나 들은 것은 없소."], ["What did you hear?”", "당신들은 무엇을 들었소?\""]], [["“Let us go down to the cave in the enclosure, that we may be sure; then we will tell you all.", "\"확인할 수 있도록 울타리 안의 동굴로 내려가 봅시다. 그러면 모두 이야기해 드리리다."], ["Come with us, and see for yourself.”", "우리와 함께 가서 직접 보시오.\""]], [["“It is a fool’s errand.”", "\"바보 같은 헛걸음이오.\""]], [["“No, the Christ is born.”", "\"아니오, 그리스도께서 나셨소.\""]], [["“The Christ!", "\"그리스도라니!"], ["How do you know?”", "어떻게 아오?\""]], [["“Let us go and see first.”", "\"먼저 가서 봅시다.\""]], [["The man laughed scornfully.", "그 사람이 비웃듯 웃었다."]], [["“The Christ indeed!", "\"그리스도라니, 참!"], ["How are you to know him?”", "당신들이 그분을 어떻게 알아보겠소?\""]], [["“He was born this night, and is now lying in a manger, so we were told; and there is but one place in Bethlehem with mangers.”", "\"오늘 밤 나셨고, 지금 구유에 누여 계신다고 우리가 들었소. 그리고 베들레헴에서 구유가 있는 곳은 단 한 군데뿐이오.\" \"그 동굴 말이오?\""]], [["“The cave?” “Yes. Come with us.”", "\"그렇소. 우리와 함께 갑시다.\""]], [["They went through the court-yard without notice, although there were some up even then talking about the wonderful light.", "그들은 아무 눈길도 끌지 않고 안뜰을 가로질러 갔다. 그때까지도 그 놀라운 빛에 대해 이야기하며 깨어 있는 사람이 더러 있었으나 마찬가지였다."], ["The door of the cavern was open.", "동굴 문은 열려 있었다."], ["A lantern was burning within, and they entered unceremoniously.", "안에는 등불이 타고 있었고, 그들은 거리낌 없이 들어섰다."]], [["“I give you peace,”", "\"평안하시오.\""], ["the watchman said to Joseph and the Beth Dagonite.", "망보는 이가 요셉과 벳다곤 사람에게 말했다."], ["“Here are people looking for a child born this night, whom they are to know by finding him in swaddling-clothes and lying in a manger.”", "\"여기 오늘 밤에 태어난 아기를 찾는 사람들이 있소. 강보에 싸여 구유에 누인 것으로 그 아기를 알아보게 되리라 하오.\""]], [["For a moment the face of the stolid Nazarene was moved; turning away, he said, “The child is here.”", "한순간 그 무덤덤한 나사렛 사람의 얼굴이 흔들렸다. 그는 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"아기는 여기 있소.\""]], [["They were led to one of the mangers, and there the child was.", "그들은 구유 가운데 하나로 이끌렸고, 거기에 아기가 있었다."], ["The lantern was brought, and the shepherds stood by mute. The little one made no sign; it was as others just born.", "등불을 가져왔고, 목자들은 말없이 곁에 섰다. 아기는 아무런 기척도 없었으니, 갓 태어난 다른 아기들과 다름없었다."]], [["“Where is the mother?”", "\"어머니는 어디 계시오?\""], ["asked the watchman.", "망보는 이가 물었다."]], [["One of the women took the baby, and went to Mary, lying near, and put it in her arms.", "여인들 가운데 하나가 아기를 받아, 가까이 누워 있는 마리아에게 가서 그 품에 안겨 주었다."], ["Then the bystanders collected about the two.", "그러자 둘러섰던 이들이 두 사람 곁으로 모여들었다."]], [["“It is the Christ!”", "\"그리스도시다!\""], ["said a shepherd, at last.", "마침내 한 목자가 말했다."]], [["“The Christ!” they all repeated, falling upon their knees in worship.", "\"그리스도시다!\" 그들 모두 되뇌며 무릎을 꿇고 경배했다."], ["One of them repeated several times over,", "그들 가운데 하나가 여러 번 거듭하여 말했다."]], [["“It is the Lord, and his glory is above the earth and heaven.”", "\"주님이시다. 그분의 영광이 땅과 하늘 위에 있도다.\""]], [["And the simple men, never doubting, kissed the hem of the mother’s robe, and with joyful faces departed.", "그리고 그 순박한 사람들은 조금도 의심하지 않고 어머니의 옷자락에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 떠났다."], ["In the khan, to all the people aroused and pressing about them, they told their story; and through the town, and all the way back to the marah, they chanted the refrain of the angels, “Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good-will towards men!”", "여관에서 그들은 잠을 깨어 둘레로 몰려드는 모든 사람에게 자기들의 이야기를 들려주었다. 그리고 마을을 지나, 마라로 돌아가는 내내 그들은 천사들의 노랫가락을 읊조렸다. \"지극히 높은 곳에서는 하나님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 그가 기뻐하신 사람들 중에 평화로다!\""]], [["The story went abroad, confirmed by the light so generally seen; and the next day, and for days thereafter, the cave was visited by curious crowds, of whom some believed, though the greater part laughed and mocked.", "그 이야기는 널리 퍼졌으니, 그토록 많은 이가 본 빛이 그것을 뒷받침했다. 이튿날, 그리고 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 그 동굴은 호기심에 찬 무리의 발길이 이어졌는데, 더러는 믿었으나 대부분은 비웃고 조롱했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII", "# CHAPTER XII"]], [["The eleventh day after the birth of the child in the cave, about mid-afternoon, the three wise men approached Jerusalem by the road from Shechem.", "동굴에서 아기가 태어난 지 열하루째 되는 날, 오후가 절반쯤 기울었을 무렵, 세 명의 동방박사가 세겜에서 이어지는 길을 따라 예루살렘으로 다가왔다."], ["After crossing Brook Cedron, they met many people, of whom none failed to stop and look after them curiously.", "기드론 시내를 건넌 뒤로 그들은 수많은 사람과 마주쳤는데, 그 누구도 걸음을 멈추고 호기심 어린 눈으로 그들을 돌아보지 않는 자가 없었다."]], [["Judea was of necessity an international thoroughfare; a narrow ridge, raised, apparently, by the pressure of the desert on the east, and the sea on the west, was all she could claim to be; over the ridge, however, nature had stretched the line of trade between the east and the south; and that was her wealth.", "유대는 그 지형의 필연으로 국제적인 통로였다. 동쪽 사막의 압력과 서쪽 바다의 압력에 떠밀려 솟아오른 듯한 좁은 산등성이, 그것이 유대가 내세울 수 있는 전부였다. 그러나 그 산등성이를 넘어 자연은 동방과 남방을 잇는 교역의 길을 펼쳐 놓았으니, 그것이 곧 유대의 재산이었다."], ["In other words, the riches of Jerusalem were the tolls she levied on passing commerce.", "다시 말해, 예루살렘의 부는 지나가는 상업에 매기는 통행세였다."], ["Nowhere else, consequently, unless in Rome, was there such constant assemblage of so many people of so many different nations; in no other city was a stranger less strange to the residents than within her walls and purlieus.", "그 결과 로마를 제외하면 그 어디에도 그토록 많은 나라의 그토록 많은 사람이 끊임없이 모여드는 곳은 없었으며, 그 성벽과 변두리 안에서만큼 낯선 이가 주민에게 덜 낯설게 여겨지는 도시도 없었다."], ["And yet these three men excited the wonder of all whom they met on the way to the gates.", "그런데도 이 세 사람만은 성문으로 가는 길에서 마주치는 모든 이의 경탄을 자아냈다."]], [["A child belonging to some women sitting by the roadside opposite the Tombs of the Kings saw the party coming; immediately it clapped its hands, and cried, “Look, look!", "왕들의 무덤 맞은편 길가에 앉은 어느 아낙들의 아이 하나가 일행이 다가오는 것을 보았다. 아이는 곧장 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다. \"봐, 봐!"], ["What pretty bells!", "정말 예쁜 방울이야!"], ["What big camels!”", "정말 커다란 낙타야!\""]], [["The bells were silver; the camels, as we have seen, were of unusual size and whiteness, and moved with singular stateliness; the trappings told of the desert and of long journeys thereon, and also of ample means in possession of the owners, who sat under the little canopies exactly as they appeared at the rendezvous beyond the Jebel.", "방울은 은으로 되어 있었다. 낙타들은 앞서 보았듯 유난히 크고 희었으며, 보기 드문 위엄을 갖추고 움직였다. 그 장구는 사막과 그 위에서의 긴 여정을, 또한 작은 차일 아래 앉은 주인들이 넉넉한 재력을 지녔음을 말해 주었으니, 그들은 제벨 너머의 약속 장소에서 보았던 모습 그대로였다."], ["Yet it was not the bells or the camels, or their furniture, or the demeanor of the riders, that were so wonderful; it was the question put by the man who rode foremost of the three.", "그러나 그토록 경이로운 것은 방울도 낙타도, 그 장비도, 탄 이들의 풍모도 아니었다. 그것은 세 사람 가운데 맨 앞에 탄 자가 던진 물음이었다."]], [["The approach to Jerusalem from the north is across a plain which dips southward, leaving the Damascus Gate in a vale or hollow.", "북쪽에서 예루살렘으로 들어가는 길은 남쪽으로 기울어진 평원을 가로지르는데, 그 평원은 다마스쿠스 성문을 골짜기, 곧 우묵한 자리에 남겨 두고 있다."], ["The road is narrow, but deeply cut by long use, and in places difficult on account of the cobbles left loose and dry by the washing of the rains.", "길은 좁으나 오랜 통행으로 깊이 패었고, 빗물에 씻겨 헐겁고 메마르게 남은 자갈 탓에 군데군데 다니기 힘들었다."], ["On either side, however, there stretched, in the old time, rich fields and handsome olive-groves, which must, in luxurious growth, have been beautiful, especially to travellers fresh from the wastes of the desert.", "그러나 양옆으로는 옛적에 기름진 들판과 보기 좋은 올리브 숲이 펼쳐져 있었으니, 그 무성한 자람은 분명 아름다웠을 것이며, 특히 사막의 황무지에서 갓 빠져나온 나그네들의 눈에는 더욱 그러했을 것이다."], ["In this road, the three stopped before the party in front of the Tombs.", "이 길에서 세 사람은 무덤 앞의 일행 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Good people,”", "\"선한 이들이여.\""], ["said Balthasar, stroking his plaited beard, and bending from his cot, “is not Jerusalem close by?”", "발타사르가 땋은 수염을 쓰다듬으며 가마에서 몸을 굽혀 말했다. \"예루살렘이 가까이 있지 않소?\""]], [["“Yes,” answered the woman into whose arms the child had shrunk.", "\"네.\" 아이가 품으로 파고든 아낙이 대답했다."], ["“If the trees on yon swell were a little lower you could see the towers on the market-place.”", "\"저 언덕의 나무들이 조금만 더 낮았어도 장터의 탑들이 보였을 거예요.\""]], [["Balthasar gave the Greek and the Hindoo a look, then asked,", "발타사르는 그리스인과 힌두인을 바라보더니 물었다."]], [["“Where is he that is born King of the Jews?”", "\"유대인의 왕으로 나신 분이 어디에 계시오?\""]], [["The women gazed at each other without reply.", "아낙들은 대답 없이 서로 마주 보기만 했다."]], [["“You have not heard of him?” “No.”", "\"그분에 대해 들은 바가 없소?\""]], [["“Well, tell everybody that we have seen his star in the east, and are come to worship him.”", "\"없습니다.\" \"그렇다면 모든 이에게 전하시오. 우리가 동방에서 그분의 별을 보고 그분께 경배하러 왔노라고.\""]], [["Thereupon the friends rode on. Of others they asked the same question, with like result.", "그러고서 세 사람은 길을 이어 갔다. 다른 이들에게도 같은 물음을 던졌으나, 결과는 한가지였다."], ["A large company whom they met going to the Grotto of Jeremiah were so astonished by the inquiry and the appearance of the travellers that they turned about and followed them into the city.", "예레미야 동굴로 가던 한 무리는 그 물음과 나그네들의 모습에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 발길을 돌려 그들을 따라 성안으로 들어왔다."]], [["So much were the three occupied with the idea of their mission that they did not care for the view which presently rose before them in the utmost magnificence: for the village first to receive them on Bezetha; for Mizpah and Olivet, over on their left; for the wall behind the village, with its forty tall and solid towers, superadded partly for strength, partly to gratify the critical taste of the kingly builder; for the same towered wall bending off to the right, with many an angle, and here and there an embattled gate, up to the three great white piles Phasaelus, Mariamne, and Hippicus; for Zion, tallest of the hills, crowned with marble palaces, and never so beautiful; for the glittering terraces of the temple on Moriah, admittedly one of the wonders of the earth; for the regal mountains rimming the sacred city round about until it seemed in the hollow of a mighty bowl.", "세 사람은 자신들의 사명에 대한 생각에 어찌나 골똘했던지, 이윽고 더없이 장려하게 눈앞에 솟아오른 광경에는 마음을 두지 않았다. 베제다에서 맨 처음 그들을 맞이한 마을에도, 왼편 저쪽의 미스바와 올리벳에도, 마을 뒤편으로 마흔 개의 높고 견고한 탑이 늘어선 성벽에도—그 탑들은 일부는 방비를 위해, 일부는 왕다운 건설자의 까다로운 안목을 흡족케 하려고 덧세운 것이었다—그리고 같은 탑들이 박힌 성벽이 수없이 많은 모퉁이를 이루며 오른편으로 휘어 가다가, 군데군데 성가퀴를 두른 문을 거쳐 파사엘루스, 마리암네, 히피쿠스라는 세 채의 거대한 흰 건축물에 이르는 광경에도, 대리석 궁전으로 꼭대기가 빛나며 이제껏 본 적 없이 아름다운, 언덕들 가운데 가장 높은 시온에도, 두말할 것 없이 세상의 경이 가운데 하나인, 모리아 위 성전의 반짝이는 단들에도, 거룩한 도시가 마치 거대한 그릇의 우묵한 바닥에 담긴 듯 보이도록 사방을 두른 위풍당당한 산들에도, 그들은 마음을 두지 않았다."]], [["They came, at length, to a tower of great height and strength, overlooking the gate which, at that time, answered to the present Damascus Gate, and marked the meeting-place of the three roads from Shechem, Jericho, and Gibeon.", "그들은 마침내 매우 높고 견고한 탑 하나에 이르렀다. 그 탑은 당시 오늘날의 다마스쿠스 성문에 해당하던 성문을 내려다보며, 세겜과 여리고와 기브온에서 오는 세 갈래 길이 만나는 자리를 표시하고 있었다."], ["A Roman guard kept the passage-way.", "로마 수비대가 통로를 지켰다."], ["By this time the people following the camels formed a train sufficient to draw the idlers hanging about the portal; so that when Balthasar stopped to speak to the sentinel, the three became instantly the centre of a close circle eager to hear all that passed.", "이때쯤이면 낙타를 따르던 사람들이 성문 어귀에서 어슬렁거리던 한량들까지 끌어들일 만큼 긴 행렬을 이루었으니, 발타사르가 보초에게 말을 걸려고 멈추었을 때, 세 사람은 곧장 오가는 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않으려는 빽빽한 무리의 한가운데에 놓이게 되었다."]], [["“I give you peace,”", "\"그대에게 평화를 비오.\""], ["the Egyptian said, in a clear voice.", "이집트인이 또렷한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["The sentinel made no reply.", "보초는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“We have come great distances in search of one who is born King of the Jews. Can you tell us where he is?”", "\"우리는 유대인의 왕으로 나신 분을 찾아 먼 길을 왔소. 그분이 어디 계신지 말해 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["The soldier raised the visor of his helmet, and called loudly.", "병사는 투구의 면갑을 들어 올리고 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["From an apartment at the right of the passage an officer appeared.", "통로 오른편의 한 방에서 장교가 나타났다."]], [["“Give way,”", "\"비켜라.\""], ["he cried, to the crowd which now pressed closer in; and as they seemed slow to obey, he advanced twirling his javelin vigorously, now right, now left; and so he gained room.", "그가 이제 더 바싹 밀려드는 군중을 향해 외쳤다. 그들이 더디게 따르는 듯하자, 그는 투창을 좌우로 힘차게 휘두르며 앞으로 나아가, 그렇게 자리를 확보했다."]], [["“What would you?”", "\"무엇을 바라오?\""], ["he asked of Balthasar, speaking in the idiom of the city.", "그가 그 도시의 말씨로 발타사르에게 물었다."]], [["And Balthasar answered in the same,", "발타사르도 같은 말씨로 대답했다."]], [["“Where is he that is born King of the Jews?”", "\"유대인의 왕으로 나신 분이 어디 계시오?\""]], [["“Herod?” asked the officer, confounded.", "\"헤롯 말이오?\" 장교가 어리둥절하여 물었다."]], [["“Herod’s kingship is from Cæsar; not Herod.”", "\"헤롯의 왕권은 카이사르에게서 나온 것, 헤롯이 아니오.\""]], [["“There is no other King of the Jews.”", "\"유대인의 왕은 그분 말고 따로 없소.\""]], [["“But we have seen the star of him we seek, and come to worship him.”", "\"그러나 우리는 우리가 찾는 분의 별을 보았고, 그분께 경배하러 왔소.\""]], [["The Roman was perplexed.", "로마인은 난처해졌다."]], [["“Go farther,”", "\"더 가 보시오.\""], ["he said, at last.", "그가 마침내 말했다."], ["“Go farther.", "\"더 가 보시오."], ["I am not a Jew. Carry the question to the doctors in the Temple, or to Hannas the priest, or, better still, to Herod himself.", "나는 유대인이 아니오. 그 물음일랑 성전의 학자들이나, 제사장 한나스에게, 아니 차라리 헤롯에게 직접 가져가시오."], ["If there be another King of the Jews, he will find him.”", "만약 다른 유대인의 왕이 있다면, 그가 찾아낼 것이오.\""]], [["Thereupon he made way for the strangers, and they passed the gate.", "그러고서 그는 이방인들에게 길을 터 주었고, 그들은 성문을 지났다."], ["But, before entering the narrow street, Balthasar lingered to say to his friends, “We are sufficiently proclaimed.", "그러나 좁은 거리로 들어서기에 앞서, 발타사르는 잠시 머물러 벗들에게 말했다. \"우리는 충분히 알려졌소."], ["By midnight the whole city will have heard of us and of our mission.", "자정이면 온 도시가 우리와 우리의 사명에 대해 듣게 될 것이오."], ["Let us to the khan now.”", "이제 객사로 갑시다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XIII", "# CHAPTER XIII"]], [["That evening, before sunset, some women were washing clothes on the upper step of the flight that led down into the basin of the Pool of Siloam.", "그날 저녁, 해가 지기 전이었다. 몇몇 여인이 실로암 못의 분지로 내려가는 계단의 맨 윗단에서 빨래를 하고 있었다."], ["They knelt each before a broad bowl of earthenware.", "저마다 넓은 질그릇 대야 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다."], ["A girl at the foot of the steps kept them supplied with water, and sang while she filled the jar.", "계단 아래쪽에 있던 한 소녀가 그들에게 물을 길어다 주었는데, 항아리를 채우며 노래를 불렀다."], ["The song was cheerful, and no doubt lightened their labor.", "노래는 흥겨웠고, 그들의 수고를 한결 가볍게 덜어 주는 것이 분명했다."], ["Occasionally they would sit upon their heels, and look up the slope of Ophel, and round to the summit of what is now the Mount of Offence, then faintly glorified by the dying sun.", "이따금 그들은 발뒤꿈치에 엉덩이를 올려 앉고는 오펠의 비탈을 올려다보거나, 지금은 멸시의 산이라 불리는 봉우리 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸으니, 저무는 해가 그 봉우리를 어렴풋이 곱게 물들이고 있었다."]], [["While they plied their hands, rubbing and wringing the clothes in the bowls, two other women came to them, each with an empty jar upon her shoulder.", "그들이 대야 속의 빨래를 비비고 짜며 손을 놀리고 있을 때, 다른 두 여인이 저마다 빈 항아리를 어깨에 인 채 그들에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Peace to you,”", "\"평안하기를.\""], ["one of the new-comers said.", "새로 온 여인 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["The laborers paused, sat up, wrung the water from their hands, and returned the salutation.", "빨래하던 여인들은 손을 멈추고 몸을 일으켜 손에서 물을 짜내고는 인사에 답했다."]], [["“It is nearly night—time to quit.”", "\"이제 거의 밤이 다 됐네. 일을 그만둘 때야.\""]], [["“There is no end to work,”", "\"일에는 끝이 없는 법이지.\""], ["was the reply.", "하는 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“But there is a time to rest, and—”", "\"하지만 쉴 때가 있고, 또—\""]], [["“To hear what may be passing,”", "\"무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 들을 때도 있지.\""], ["interposed another.", "다른 여인이 끼어들었다."]], [["“What news have you?”", "\"무슨 소식이라도 있나?\""]], [["“Then you have not heard?”", "\"그럼 아직 못 들었어?\" \"못 들었어.\""]], [["“No.” “They say the Christ is born,”", "\"그리스도가 태어났다고들 해.\""], ["said the newsmonger, plunging into her story.", "소식을 전하는 여인이 곧장 이야기 속으로 뛰어들며 말했다."]], [["It was curious to see the faces of the laborers brighten with interest; on the other side down came the jars, which, in a moment, were turned into seats for their owners.", "빨래하던 여인들의 얼굴이 호기심으로 환해지는 모습이 흥미로웠다. 다른 편에서는 항아리가 내려졌고, 그것은 이내 주인들의 걸상이 되었다."]], [["“The Christ!”", "\"그리스도라고!\""], ["the listeners cried.", "듣던 여인들이 외쳤다."]], [["“So they say.”", "\"그렇게들 말해.\""]], [["“Who?”", "\"누가?\""]], [["“Everybody; it is common talk.”", "\"다들 그래. 흔히 떠도는 이야기야.\""]], [["“Does anybody believe it?”", "\"믿는 사람이 있긴 한가?\""]], [["“This afternoon three men came across Brook Cedron on the road from Shechem,”", "\"오늘 오후에 세 사람이 세겜에서 오는 길로 기드론 시내를 건너왔어.\""], ["the speaker replied, circumstantially, intending to smother doubt.", "말하는 여인은 의심을 잠재울 셈으로 조목조목 자세히 늘어놓았다."], ["“Each one of them rode a camel spotless white, and larger than any ever before seen in Jerusalem.”", "\"저마다 티끌 하나 없이 새하얀 낙타를 탔는데, 예루살렘에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 큰 낙타였다지.\""]], [["The eyes and mouths of the auditors opened wide.", "듣던 여인들의 눈과 입이 크게 벌어졌다."]], [["“To prove how great and rich the men were,” the narrator continued, “they sat under awnings of silk; the buckles of their saddles were of gold, as was the fringe of their bridles; the bells were of silver, and made real music. Nobody knew them; they looked as if they had come from the ends of the world. Only one of them spoke, and of everybody on the road, even the women and children, he asked this question—‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’ No one gave them answer—no one understood what they meant; so they passed on, leaving behind them this saying: ‘For we have seen his star in the east, and are come to worship him.’ They put the question to the Roman at the gate; and he, no wiser than the simple people on the road, sent them up to Herod.”", "\"그 사람들이 얼마나 위대하고 부유한지를 보여 주듯,\" 이야기를 전하는 여인이 말을 이었다. \"비단 차일 아래 앉아 있었고, 안장의 죔쇠는 금이었으며, 굴레의 술도 금이었어. 방울은 은으로 되어 진짜 음악 소리를 냈다지. 아무도 그들을 알아보지 못했어. 마치 세상 끝에서 온 사람들 같았대. 그 가운데 한 사람만 입을 열었는데, 길에서 만나는 누구에게나, 여자나 아이에게까지 이렇게 물었대. '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?' 아무도 대답해 주지 못했어. 무슨 뜻인지 아무도 이해하지 못했으니까. 그래서 그들은 이 말을 남기고 지나갔어. '우리가 동방에서 그의 별을 보고 그에게 경배하러 왔노라.' 성문에 있는 로마 사람에게도 그 질문을 던졌는데, 그 사람도 길에서 만난 순박한 백성보다 나을 게 없어서, 그들을 헤롯에게로 올려보냈대.\""]], [["“Where are they now?”", "\"그 사람들 지금 어디 있어?\""]], [["“At the khan.", "\"여관에 있어."], ["Hundreds have been to look at them already, and hundreds more are going.”", "벌써 수백 명이 그들을 보러 다녀갔고, 또 수백 명이 가는 중이야.\""]], [["“Who are they?”", "\"그들이 누구야?\""]], [["“Nobody knows.", "\"아무도 몰라."], ["They are said to be Persians—wise men who talk with the stars—prophets, it may be, like Elijah and Jeremiah.”", "페르시아 사람들이라고들 해. 별을 보고 이야기하는 현자들이래. 어쩌면 엘리야나 예레미야 같은 예언자일지도 모르지.\""]], [["“What do they mean by King of the Jews?”", "\"유대인의 왕이라는 게 무슨 뜻이지?\""]], [["“The Christ, and that he is just born.”", "\"그리스도 말이야. 그리고 그분이 막 태어나셨다는 거지.\""]], [["One of the women laughed, and resumed her work, saying, “Well, when I see him I will believe.”", "여인 가운데 하나가 웃음을 터뜨리고 다시 일을 시작하며 말했다. \"그럼, 내 눈으로 보면 믿겠지.\""]], [["Another followed her example: “And I—well, when I see him raise the dead, I will believe.”", "다른 여인도 그를 따라 했다. \"나도 그래. 그분이 죽은 자를 일으키시는 걸 보면, 그땐 믿겠어.\""]], [["A third said, quietly, “He has been a long time promised.", "세 번째 여인이 조용히 말했다. \"그분이 오신다는 약속은 오래전부터 있었지."], ["It will be enough for me to see him heal one leper.”", "나는 그분이 문둥병자 하나라도 고치시는 걸 보면 그것으로 족해.\""]], [["And the party sat talking until the night came, and, with the help of the frosty air, drove them home.", "그리고 일행은 밤이 올 때까지 이야기를 나누다가, 차가운 밤공기에 떠밀려 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Later in the evening, about the beginning of the first watch, there was an assemblage in the palace on Mount Zion, of probably fifty persons, who never came together except by order of Herod, and then only when he had demanded to know some one or more of the deeper mysteries of the Jewish law and history.", "그날 저녁 늦게, 초경(初更)이 시작될 무렵, 시온산 궁전에 쉰 명쯤 되는 사람들이 모여 있었다. 그들은 헤롯의 명령이 아니고서는 결코 한자리에 모이는 법이 없었으니, 그 명령이란 그가 유대 율법과 역사의 깊은 신비 가운데 하나나 둘쯤을 알고자 할 때만 내려지는 것이었다."], ["It was, in short, a meeting of the teachers of the colleges, of the chief priests, and of the doctors most noted in the city for learning—the leaders of opinion, expounders of the different creeds; princes of the Sadducees; Pharisaic debaters; calm, soft-spoken, stoical philosophers of the Essene socialists.", "요컨대 그것은 학교의 교사들과 대제사장들, 그리고 도성에서 학식으로 가장 이름난 학자들의 모임이었다. 여론을 이끄는 지도자들, 갖가지 교리를 풀이하는 자들, 사두개파의 우두머리들, 바리새파의 논객들, 그리고 차분하고 부드러운 말씨에 금욕적인 에세네 공동체의 철학자들이 모인 자리였다."]], [["The chamber in which the session was held belonged to one of the interior court-yards of the palace, and was quite large and Romanesque.", "회의가 열린 방은 궁전 안뜰 가운데 하나에 딸린 것으로, 꽤 넓고 로마풍이었다."], ["The floor was tessellated with marble blocks; the walls, unbroken by a window, were frescoed in panels of saffron yellow; a divan occupied the centre of the apartment, covered with cushions of bright-yellow cloth, and fashioned in form of the letter U, the opening towards the doorway; in the arch of the divan, or, as it were, in the bend of the letter, there was an immense bronze tripod, curiously inlaid with gold and silver, over which a chandelier dropped from the ceiling, having seven arms, each holding a lighted lamp.", "바닥은 대리석 조각으로 모자이크가 놓여 있었고, 창 하나 없이 이어진 벽은 사프란빛 노랑 화판(畵板)으로 프레스코가 그려져 있었다. 방 한가운데에는 긴 의자가 놓여 있었는데, 밝은 노란빛 천을 씌운 방석으로 덮여 있었고, 알파벳 U자 모양으로, 그 트인 쪽이 출입문을 향하도록 만들어져 있었다. 의자가 둥글게 휘어진 안쪽, 말하자면 그 글자가 구부러진 자리에는 금과 은으로 정교하게 상감(象嵌)을 놓은 거대한 청동 삼각대가 있었고, 그 위로는 천장에서 촛대가 드리워져 있었으니, 일곱 개의 가지마다 불 켜진 등잔을 하나씩 받쳐 들고 있었다."], ["The divan and the lamp were purely Jewish.", "그 긴 의자와 등잔은 순전히 유대식이었다."]], [["The company sat upon the divan after the style of Orientals, in costume singularly uniform, except as to color.", "사람들은 동방인의 방식대로 긴 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 빛깔만 다를 뿐 차림새가 유난히 한결같았다."], ["They were mostly men advanced in years; immense beards covered their faces; to their large noses were added the effects of large black eyes, deeply shaded by bold brows; their demeanor was grave, dignified, even patriarchal.", "대부분 나이가 지긋한 사람들이었다. 거대한 수염이 그들의 얼굴을 뒤덮었고, 큼직한 코에 더하여, 짙게 그늘진 굵은 눈썹 아래 큼직한 검은 눈이 자리하고 있었다. 그들의 태도는 진중하고 위엄 있었으며, 가히 족장다웠다."], ["In brief, their session was that of the Sanhedrim.", "요컨대 그들의 회합은 산헤드린의 회합이었다."]], [["He who sat before the tripod, however, in the place which may be called the head of the divan, having all the rest of his associates on his right and left, and, at the same time, before him, evidently president of the meeting, would have instantly absorbed the attention of a spectator.", "그러나 삼각대 앞, 긴 의자의 머리라 할 만한 자리에 앉아, 다른 모든 동료를 좌우에, 동시에 자기 앞에 두고 있는 자, 분명 이 모임의 의장인 그 사람은 보는 이의 눈길을 단번에 사로잡았을 것이다."], ["He had been cast in large mould, but was now shrunken and stooped to ghastliness; his white robe dropped from his shoulders in folds that gave no hint of muscle or anything but an angular skeleton.", "그는 큰 틀로 빚어진 사람이었으나, 이제는 오싹할 만큼 쪼그라들고 굽어 있었다. 흰 겉옷이 어깨에서 흘러내리며 주름을 이루었으나, 그 주름은 근육은커녕 모난 뼈대 말고는 아무것도 짐작하게 하지 못했다."], ["His hands, half concealed by sleeves of silk, white and crimson striped, were clasped upon his knees.", "희고 진홍빛 줄무늬가 있는 비단 소매에 반쯤 가려진 그의 두 손은 무릎 위에 모여 있었다."], ["When he spoke, sometimes the first finger of the right hand extended tremulously; he seemed incapable of other gesture.", "말을 할 때면, 이따금 오른손 집게손가락이 떨리며 뻗어 나왔다. 그 밖의 몸짓은 할 수 없는 듯했다."], ["But his head was a splendid dome.", "그러나 그의 머리는 빛나는 둥근 지붕이었다."], ["A few hairs, whiter than fine-drawn silver, fringed the base; over a broad, full-sphered skull the skin was drawn close, and shone in the light with positive brilliance; the temples were deep hollows, from which the forehead beetled like a wrinkled crag; the eyes were wan and dim; the nose was pinched; and all the lower face was muffed in a beard flowing and venerable as Aaron’s.", "곱게 뽑은 은보다 더 흰 머리카락 몇 가닥이 그 밑동을 둘러쌌고, 넓고 둥글게 부푼 두개골 위로 살갗이 팽팽히 당겨져 빛 속에서 또렷한 광채로 빛났다. 관자놀이는 깊이 패었고, 거기서 이마가 주름진 바위처럼 불거져 나왔다. 두 눈은 흐릿하고 희미했으며, 코는 야위었고, 아래 얼굴은 온통 아론의 것처럼 흘러내리는 거룩한 수염에 감싸여 있었다."], ["Such was Hillel the Babylonian!", "이가 바로 바빌로니아 사람 힐렐이었다!"], ["The line of prophets, long extinct in Israel, was now succeeded by a line of scholars, of whom he was first in learning—a prophet in all but the divine inspiration!", "이스라엘에서 오래전에 끊긴 예언자의 계보는 이제 학자들의 계보로 이어졌으니, 그가 그 가운데 학식으로 으뜸이었다. 신의 영감만 없을 뿐, 그는 다른 모든 면에서 예언자였다!"], ["At the age of one hundred and six, he was still Rector of the Great College.", "백여섯 살의 나이에도 그는 여전히 대학교의 학장이었다."]], [["On the table before him lay outspread a roll or volume of parchment inscribed with Hebrew characters; behind him, in waiting, stood a page richly habited.", "그의 앞 탁자에는 히브리 글자가 적힌 양피지 두루마리 한 권이 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 뒤에는 화려하게 차려입은 시동이 시중을 들려고 서 있었다."]], [["There had been discussion, but at this moment of introduction the company had reached a conclusion; each one was in an attitude of rest, and the venerable Hillel, without moving, called the page. “Hist!”", "토론이 있었으나, 우리가 이 자리에 들어선 이 순간에 사람들은 이미 결론에 다다라 있었다. 저마다 쉬는 자세를 취하고 있었고, 존엄한 힐렐은 움직이지도 않은 채 시동을 불렀다."]], [["The youth advanced respectfully.", "\"이리 오너라!\" 소년이 공손히 다가왔다."]], [["“Go tell the king we are ready to give him answer.”", "\"가서 왕께 우리가 답을 드릴 준비가 되었다고 아뢰어라.\""]], [["The boy hurried away.", "소년이 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["After a time two officers entered and stopped, one on each side the door; after them slowly followed a most striking personage—an old man clad in a purple robe bordered with scarlet, and girt to his waist by a band of gold linked so fine that it was pliable as leather; the latchets of his shoes sparkled with precious stones; a narrow crown wrought in filigree shone outside a tarbooshe of softest crimson plush, which, encasing his head, fell down the neck and shoulders, leaving the throat and neck exposed.", "얼마 뒤 두 명의 관리가 들어와 문 양옆에 하나씩 멈춰 섰다. 그들 뒤로 더없이 인상적인 한 인물이 천천히 따라 들어왔다. 진홍빛으로 가장자리를 두른 자줏빛 겉옷을 입은 노인이었으니, 가죽처럼 부드러울 만큼 가늘게 엮은 금띠로 허리를 둘렀고, 신발 끈에는 보석이 반짝였다."], ["Instead of a seal, a dagger dangled from his belt. He walked with a halting step, leaning heavily upon a staff.", "가장 부드러운 진홍빛 우단으로 된 타르부시 위로 가는 줄세공 왕관이 빛났는데, 그 타르부시가 머리를 감싸고 목과 어깨로 흘러내리면서도 목과 멱은 드러내 놓고 있었다. 인장(印章) 대신 단검이 그의 허리띠에 매달려 있었다."], ["Not until he reached the opening of the divan, did he pause or look up from the floor; then, as for the first time conscious of the company, and roused by their presence, he raised himself, and looked haughtily round, like one startled and searching for an enemy—so dark, suspicious, and threatening was the glance.", "그는 절뚝거리는 걸음으로, 지팡이에 무겁게 기대어 걸었다. 긴 의자가 트인 곳에 이르기까지 그는 멈추지도, 바닥에서 눈을 들지도 않았다. 그러다 마치 처음으로 사람들의 존재를 의식하고 그 존재에 자극을 받기라도 한 듯, 그는 몸을 일으켜 거만하게 둘러보았으니, 마치 깜짝 놀라 적을 찾는 사람 같았다."], ["Such was Herod the Great—a body broken by diseases, a conscience seared with crimes, a mind magnificently capable, a soul fit for brotherhood with the Cæsars; now seven-and-sixty years old, but guarding his throne with a jealousy never so vigilant, a power never so despotic, and a cruelty never so inexorable.", "그만큼 그 눈길은 어둡고 의심에 차 있었으며 위협적이었다. 이가 바로 헤롯 대왕이었으니, 질병으로 무너진 몸, 죄악으로 지져진 양심, 웅장하리만치 유능한 정신, 카이사르들과 형제 될 만한 영혼을 지닌 자였다. 이제 예순일곱 살이었으나, 일찍이 그토록 빈틈없이 경계한 적 없는 시기심으로, 일찍이 그토록 전제적이었던 적 없는 권력으로, 일찍이 그토록 무자비했던 적 없는 잔인함으로 자기 왕좌를 지키고 있었다."]], [["There was a general movement on the part of the assemblage—a bending forward in salaam by the more aged, a rising-up by the more courtierly, followed by low genuflections, hands upon the beard or breast.", "모인 사람들 사이에서 일제히 움직임이 일었다. 더 나이 든 자들은 몸을 굽혀 절을 했고, 더 신하다운 자들은 자리에서 일어나 곧이어 깊이 무릎을 굽히고 손을 수염이나 가슴에 얹었다."]], [["His observations taken, Herod moved on until at the tripod opposite the venerable Hillel, who met his cold glance with an inclination of the head, and a slight lifting of the hands.", "살펴보기를 마친 헤롯은 앞으로 나아가, 존엄한 힐렐의 맞은편 삼각대 앞에 이르렀다. 힐렐은 그 차가운 눈길을 머리를 숙이고 두 손을 살짝 들어 맞이했다."]], [["“The answer!”", "\"답을 다오!\""], ["said the king, with imperious simplicity, addressing Hillel, and planting his staff before him with both hands. “The answer!”", "왕이 거만하리만치 간결하게, 힐렐에게 말하며 두 손으로 지팡이를 제 앞에 짚었다. \"답을 다오!\""]], [["The eyes of the patriarch glowed mildly, and, raising his head, and looking the inquisitor full in the face, he answered, his associates giving him closest attention,", "장로의 두 눈이 부드럽게 빛났다. 그는 머리를 들어 묻는 자의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 답했고, 그의 동료들은 그에게 가장 깊은 주의를 기울였다."]], [["“With thee, O king, be the peace of God, of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob!”", "\"오, 왕이시여, 하느님의, 아브라함과 이삭과 야곱의 평안이 임금님과 함께하기를!\""]], [["His manner was that of invocation; changing it, he resumed:", "그의 태도는 기원을 올리는 것이었다. 그는 태도를 바꾸어 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“Thou hast demanded of us where the Christ should be born.”", "\"임금님께서는 그리스도가 어디서 태어나야 하는지를 저희에게 물으셨습니다.\""]], [["The king bowed, though the evil eyes remained fixed upon the sage’s face.", "왕이 고개를 숙였으나, 그 사악한 눈은 현자의 얼굴에 고정된 채였다."]], [["“That is the question.”", "\"그것이 질문이오.\""]], [["“Then, O king, speaking for myself, and all my brethren here, not one dissenting, I say, in Bethlehem of Judea.”", "\"그렇다면, 오 왕이시여, 저 자신과 여기 모인 제 모든 형제를 대신하여, 한 사람도 이의가 없으니, 저는 말씀드립니다. 유대 베들레헴이옵니다.\""]], [["Hillel glanced at the parchment on the tripod; and, pointing with his tremulous finger, continued, “In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet, ‘And thou, Bethlehem, in the land of Judea, art not the least among the princes of Judah; for out of thee shall come a governor that shall rule my people Israel.’”", "힐렐은 삼각대 위의 양피지를 흘긋 보고는, 떨리는 손가락으로 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"유대 베들레헴이니, 예언자가 이렇게 기록하였기 때문입니다. '또 유대 땅 베들레헴아, 너는 유대 고을 중에서 가장 작지 아니하도다. 네게서 한 다스리는 자가 나와서 내 백성 이스라엘을 다스리리라.'\""]], [["Herod’s face was troubled, and his eyes fell upon the parchment while he thought.", "헤롯의 얼굴에는 근심이 어렸고, 그가 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안 그의 눈은 양피지에 떨어졌다."], ["Those beholding him scarcely breathed; they spoke not, nor did he.", "그를 지켜보는 자들은 거의 숨도 쉬지 못했다. 그들도 입을 열지 않았고, 그도 입을 열지 않았다."], ["At length he turned about and left the chamber.", "마침내 그는 몸을 돌려 방을 떠났다."]], [["“Brethren,”", "\"형제들이여,\""], ["said Hillel, “we are dismissed.”", "힐렐이 말했다. \"우리는 물러나도 좋소.\""]], [["The company then arose, and in groups departed.", "그러자 사람들이 일어나, 무리를 지어 떠나갔다."]], [["“Simeon,”", "\"시므온.\""], ["said Hillel again.", "힐렐이 다시 불렀다."]], [["A man, quite fifty years old, but in the hearty prime of life, answered and came to him.", "쉰은 족히 되었으나 한창때의 정정함을 지닌 한 사람이 대답하며 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Take up the sacred parchment, my son; roll it tenderly.”", "\"거룩한 양피지를 거두어라, 내 아들아. 정성스레 말아 두어라.\""]], [["The order was obeyed.", "명령대로 따랐다."]], [["“Now lend me thy arm; I will to the litter.”", "\"이제 내게 네 팔을 빌려다오. 가마로 가야겠다.\""]], [["The strong man stooped; with his withered hands the old one took the offered support, and, rising, moved feebly to the door.", "건장한 사내가 몸을 굽혔다. 노인은 시든 손으로 내미는 부축을 받아 몸을 일으키고는, 힘없이 문으로 걸어갔다."]], [["So departed the famous Rector, and Simeon, his son, who was to be his successor in wisdom, learning, and office.", "이리하여 그 이름난 학장은 떠났고, 지혜와 학식과 직위에서 그의 후계자가 될 그의 아들 시므온도 함께 떠났다."]], [["Yet later in the evening the wise men were lying in a lewen of the khan awake.", "그날 저녁 더 늦게, 현자들은 여관의 한 레웬에 누워 잠들지 못하고 있었다."], ["The stones which served them as pillows raised their heads so they could look out of the open arch into the depths of the sky; and as they watched the twinkling of the stars, they thought of the next manifestation.", "베개 삼은 돌멩이가 그들의 머리를 받쳐 주어, 트인 아치 너머로 하늘 깊은 곳을 내다볼 수 있었다. 그들은 별들의 반짝임을 바라보며 다음 계시를 생각했다. 그것은 어떻게 올 것인가?"], ["How would it come? What would it be?", "어떤 것일까? 그들은 마침내 예루살렘에 와 있었다."], ["They were in Jerusalem at last; they had asked at the gate for Him they sought; they had borne witness of his birth; it remained only to find him; and as to that, they placed all trust in the Spirit.", "성문에서 그들이 찾는 분에 대해 물었고, 그분의 탄생을 증언했다. 이제 남은 일은 그분을 찾는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 그 일에 관해서는 모든 것을 성령께 맡겼다."], ["Men listening for the voice of God, or waiting a sign from Heaven, cannot sleep.", "하느님의 음성에 귀 기울이거나 하늘에서 내려올 표징을 기다리는 사람은 잠을 이루지 못하는 법이다. * 레웬: 여관 안뜰을 향해 트인, 지붕 덮인 거처."]], [["While they were in this condition, a man stepped in under the arch, darkening the lewen.", "그들이 이런 상태에 있을 때, 한 사람이 아치 아래로 들어서며 레웬을 어둡게 만들었다."]], [["“Awake!”", "\"일어나시오!\""], ["he said to them; “I bring you a message which will not be put off.”", "그가 그들에게 말했다. \"미룰 수 없는 전갈을 가져왔소이다.\""]], [["They all sat up.", "그들 모두 몸을 일으켜 앉았다."]], [["“From whom?” asked the Egyptian.", "\"누구에게서 온 것이오?\" 이집트인이 물었다."]], [["“Herod the king.”", "\"헤롯 왕에게서요.\""]], [["Each one felt his spirit thrill.", "저마다 마음에 전율이 흐르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Are you not the steward of the khan?”", "\"당신은 이 여관의 관리인이 아니오?\""], ["Balthasar asked next.", "발타사르가 이어 물었다."]], [["“I am.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“What would the king with us?”", "\"왕께서 무슨 일로 우리를 찾으시오?\""]], [["“His messenger is without; let him answer.”", "\"왕의 사자가 밖에 있으니, 그가 답할 것이외다.\""]], [["“Tell him, then, to abide our coming.”", "\"그러면 그에게, 우리가 나갈 때까지 기다리라 이르시오.\""]], [["“You were right, O my brother!”", "\"형제여, 그대가 옳았소!\""], ["said the Greek, when the steward was gone.", "관리인이 가고 나자 그리스인이 말했다."], ["“The question put to the people on the road, and to the guard at the gate, has given us quick notoriety.", "\"길에서 백성에게, 또 성문에서 경비병에게 던진 그 질문이 우리를 단번에 유명하게 만들었구려."], ["I am impatient; let us up quickly.”", "나는 마음이 급하오. 어서 일어납시다.\""]], [["They arose, put on their sandals, girt their mantles about them, and went out.", "그들은 일어나 신을 신고, 외투를 몸에 둘러 단단히 여미고는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“I salute you, and give you peace, and pray your pardon; but my master, the king, has sent me to invite you to the palace, where he would have speech with you privately.”", "\"인사를 드리며 평안을 빕니다. 그리고 용서를 청합니다. 제 주인이신 왕께서 저를 보내, 여러분을 궁전으로 모셔 오라 하셨습니다. 거기서 여러분과 은밀히 말씀을 나누고자 하십니다.\""]], [["Thus the messenger discharged his duty.", "이렇게 사자는 제 임무를 다했다."]], [["A lamp hung in the entrance, and by its light they looked at each other, and knew the Spirit was upon them.", "입구에는 등잔 하나가 걸려 있었고, 그 불빛으로 그들은 서로를 바라보며 성령이 자기들 위에 임하셨음을 알았다."], ["Then the Egyptian stepped to the steward, and said, so as not to be heard by the others, “You know where our goods are stored in the court, and where our camels are resting.", "그러자 이집트인이 관리인에게 다가가, 다른 이들에게 들리지 않게 말했다. \"당신은 우리 짐이 안뜰 어디에 보관되어 있는지, 우리 낙타가 어디서 쉬고 있는지 알 것이오."], ["While we are gone, make all things ready for our departure, if it should be needful.”", "우리가 떠나 있는 동안, 만일 필요하게 되거든 우리가 떠날 채비를 모두 갖추어 두시오.\""]], [["“Go your way assured; trust me,”", "\"안심하고 가십시오. 저를 믿으시면 됩니다.\""], ["the steward replied.", "관리인이 답했다."]], [["“The king’s will is our will,”", "\"왕의 뜻이 곧 우리의 뜻이오.\""], ["said Balthasar to the messenger.", "발타사르가 사자에게 말했다."], ["“We will follow you.”", "\"당신을 따르겠소.\""]], [["The streets of the Holy City were narrow then as now, but not so rough and foul; for the great builder, not content with beauty, enforced cleanliness and convenience also.", "거룩한 도성의 거리는 그때도 지금처럼 좁았으나, 그토록 거칠거나 더럽지는 않았다. 위대한 건설자가 아름다움에 만족하지 않고 청결함과 편리함까지도 강제했기 때문이다."], ["Following their guide, the brethren proceeded without a word.", "길잡이를 따라 형제들은 한마디 말도 없이 나아갔다."], ["Through the dim starlight, made dimmer by the walls on both sides, sometimes almost lost under bridges connecting the house-tops, out of a low ground they ascended a hill.", "양쪽 벽 때문에 한층 더 어둑해진 어슴푸레한 별빛 속을, 때로는 지붕과 지붕을 잇는 다리 아래로 거의 자취를 감추다시피 하면서, 그들은 낮은 땅에서 언덕으로 올라갔다."], ["At last they came to a portal reared across the way.", "마침내 그들은 길을 가로질러 세운 대문에 이르렀다."], ["In the light of fires blazing before it in two great braziers, they caught a glimpse of the structure, and also of some guards leaning motionlessly upon their arms.", "그 앞 두 개의 큰 화로에서 타오르는 불빛으로, 그들은 그 건물의 모습과 함께, 무기에 꼼짝없이 기댄 채 서 있는 몇몇 경비병의 모습을 얼핏 보았다."], ["They passed into a building unchallenged.", "그들은 아무런 제지도 받지 않고 건물 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Then by passages and arched halls; through courts, and under colonnades not always lighted; up long flights of stairs, past innumerable cloisters and chambers, they were conducted into a tower of great height.", "그러고는 복도와 아치형 회랑을 지나, 안뜰을 가로지르고, 늘 환히 밝혀져 있지만은 않은 줄기둥 아래를 지나며, 긴 계단을 여러 차례 올라, 수없이 많은 주랑과 방을 거쳐, 매우 높은 탑으로 인도되었다."], ["Suddenly the guide halted, and, pointing through an open door, said to them,", "갑자기 길잡이가 걸음을 멈추고, 열린 문 너머를 가리키며 그들에게 말했다."]], [["“Enter.", "\"드십시오."], ["The king is there.”", "왕께서 거기 계십니다.\""]], [["The air of the chamber was heavy with the perfume of sandal-wood, and all the appointments within were effeminately rich.", "방 안의 공기는 백단향 내음으로 무거웠고, 안의 온갖 세간은 여인의 것처럼 사치스러웠다."], ["Upon the floor, covering the central space, a tufted rug was spread, and upon that a throne was set.", "바닥의 한가운데를 덮어 보풀이 인 양탄자가 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 위에 왕좌가 놓여 있었다."], ["The visitors had but time, however, to catch a confused idea of the place—of carved and gilt ottomans and couches; of fans and jars and musical instruments; of golden candlesticks glittering in their own lights; of walls painted in the style of the voluptuous Grecian school, one look at which had made a Pharisee hide his head with holy horror.", "그러나 방문객들에게는 그곳에 대한 어렴풋한 인상을 잡을 틈밖에 없었다. 새기고 도금한 안락의자와 긴 의자들, 부채와 항아리와 악기들, 제 불빛에 반짝이는 황금 촛대들, 그리고 관능적인 그리스 화풍으로 그려진 벽들이었으니, 그 벽을 한 번 본 어느 바리새인은 거룩한 두려움에 머리를 감싸 쥐었을 정도였다."], ["Herod, sitting upon the throne to receive them, clad as when at the conference with the doctors and lawyers, claimed all their minds.", "그들을 맞이하려 왕좌에 앉아 있는 헤롯은, 학자들과 율법학자들과 회의할 때와 같은 차림으로, 그들의 온 마음을 사로잡았다."]], [["At the edge of the rug, to which they advanced uninvited, they prostrated themselves.", "청하지도 않았으나 그들이 다가선 양탄자 가장자리에서 그들은 엎드려 절했다."], ["The king touched a bell.", "왕이 종을 울렸다."], ["An attendant came in, and placed three stools before the throne.", "시종 하나가 들어와 왕좌 앞에 걸상 셋을 놓았다."]], [["“Seat yourselves,”", "\"앉으시오.\""], ["said the monarch, graciously.", "군주가 너그럽게 말했다."]], [["“From the North Gate,”", "\"북문에서,\""], ["he continued, when they were at rest, “I had this afternoon report of the arrival of three strangers, curiously mounted, and appearing as if from far countries.", "그들이 자리에 앉자 그가 말을 이었다. \"오늘 오후에 보고를 받았소. 세 명의 이방인이 도착했는데, 기이하게 짐승을 타고, 마치 먼 나라에서 온 듯하다고 하더이다."], ["Are you the men?”", "그대들이 그 사람들이오?\""]], [["The Egyptian took the sign from the Greek and the Hindoo, and answered, with the profoundest salaam, “Were we other than we are, the mighty Herod, whose fame is as incense to the whole world, would not have sent for us.", "이집트인은 그리스인과 힌두인의 눈짓을 받고, 더없이 깊은 절을 올리며 답했다. \"저희가 지금의 저희와 다른 자들이었다면, 그 명성이 온 세상에 향처럼 퍼져 있는 위대하신 헤롯께서 저희를 부르지 않으셨을 것입니다."], ["We may not doubt that we are the strangers.”", "저희가 그 이방인들임을 의심할 수 없겠나이다.\""]], [["Herod acknowledged the speech with a wave of the hand.", "헤롯은 손을 한 번 저어 그 말을 받아들였다."]], [["“Who are you? Whence do you come?”", "\"그대들은 누구요? 어디서 왔소?\""], ["he asked, adding significantly, “Let each speak for himself.”", "그가 물으며 의미심장하게 덧붙였다. \"각자 자기 이야기를 하시오.\""]], [["In turn they gave him account, referring simply to the cities and lands of their birth, and the routes by which they came to Jerusalem.", "그들은 차례로 그에게 사정을 아뢰며, 자기들이 태어난 도시와 나라, 그리고 예루살렘에 이른 경로를 간단히 들려주었다."], ["Somewhat disappointed, Herod plied them more directly.", "다소 실망한 헤롯은 더 직접적으로 그들을 다그쳤다."]], [["“What was the question you put to the officer at the gate?”", "\"그대들이 성문의 관리에게 던진 질문이 무엇이었소?\""]], [["“We asked him, Where is he that is born King of the Jews.”", "\"유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐고 물었습니다.\""]], [["“I see now why the people were so curious.", "\"이제 알겠소, 백성이 왜 그토록 궁금해했는지."], ["You excite me no less. Is there another King of the Jews?”", "그대들은 나도 못지않게 흥분시키는구려. 또 다른 유대인의 왕이 있단 말이오?\""]], [["The Egyptian did not blanch.", "이집트인은 낯빛 하나 변하지 않았다."]], [["“There is one newly born.”", "\"갓 태어나신 분이 한 분 계십니다.\""]], [["An expression of pain knit the dark face of the monarch, as if his mind were swept by a harrowing recollection.", "군주의 어두운 얼굴에 고통의 기색이 어렸으니, 마치 그의 마음이 어떤 쓰라린 기억에 휩쓸리기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["“Not to me, not to me!”", "\"내게는 아니지, 내게는 아니야!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 부르짖었다."]], [["Possibly the accusing images of his murdered children flitted before him; recovering from the emotion, whatever it was, he asked, steadily, “Where is the new king?”", "어쩌면 그가 죽인 제 자식들의 책망하는 모습이 그의 앞을 스쳐 지나갔는지도 몰랐다. 그 감정이 무엇이었든 거기서 벗어난 그는 차분하게 물었다. \"그 새로운 왕이 어디 있소?\""]], [["“That, O king, is what we would ask.”", "\"오 왕이시여, 그것이 바로 저희가 여쭙고 싶은 것입니다.\""]], [["“You bring me a wonder—a riddle surpassing any of Solomon’s,”", "\"그대들은 내게 경이를 가져왔구려. 솔로몬의 어떤 수수께끼보다도 뛰어난 수수께끼를.\""], ["the inquisitor said next.", "묻는 자가 이어 말했다."], ["“As you see, I am in the time of life when curiosity is as ungovernable as it was in childhood, when to trifle with it is cruelty.", "\"보다시피 나는 호기심이 어린 시절처럼 걷잡을 수 없게 되는 나이에 이르렀으니, 그것을 가지고 장난치는 것은 곧 잔인한 일이오. 더 말해 주시오. 그러면 나는 왕이 왕을 예우하듯 그대들을 예우하겠소."], ["Tell me further, and I will honor you as kings honor each other.", "그 갓 태어난 이에 관해 그대들이 아는 모든 것을 내게 일러 주시오."], ["Give me all you know about the newly born, and I will join you in the search for him; and when we have found him, I will do what you wish; I will bring him to Jerusalem, and train him in kingcraft; I will use my grace with Cæsar for his promotion and glory.", "그러면 나도 그대들과 함께 그를 찾는 일에 나서겠소. 그를 찾거든, 나는 그대들이 바라는 대로 하겠소. 그를 예루살렘으로 데려와 제왕의 도를 가르치겠고, 카이사르에 대한 내 은총을 써서 그의 영달과 영광을 도모하겠소."], ["Jealousy shall not come between us, so I swear.", "우리 사이에 시기심이 끼어들지 못하게 하리니, 내 맹세하오."], ["But tell me first how, so widely separated by seas and deserts, you all came to hear of him.”", "그러나 먼저 말해 주시오. 바다와 사막으로 그토록 멀리 떨어져 있는 그대들이 어찌하여 모두 그의 소식을 듣게 되었는지를.\""]], [["“I will tell you truly, O king.”", "\"진실로 말씀드리겠나이다, 오 왕이시여.\""]], [["“Speak on,”", "\"말해 보시오.\""], ["said Herod.", "헤롯이 말했다."]], [["Balthasar raised himself erect, and said, solemnly,", "발타사르가 몸을 곧추세우고 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“There is an Almighty God.”", "\"전능하신 하느님이 계십니다.\""]], [["Herod was visibly startled.", "헤롯이 눈에 띄게 흠칫했다."]], [["“He bade us come hither, promising that we should find the Redeemer of the World; that we should see and worship him, and bear witness that he was come; and, as a sign, we were each given to see a star.", "\"그분께서 저희에게 이리로 오라 명하시며, 세상의 구원자를 만나게 되리라 약속하셨습니다. 그분을 뵙고 경배하며, 그분이 오셨음을 증언하게 되리라 하셨습니다. 그리고 그 표징으로, 저희는 저마다 별 하나를 보도록 허락받았습니다."], ["His Spirit stayed with us.", "그분의 영이 저희와 함께 머무셨습니다."], ["O king, his Spirit is with us now!”", "오 왕이시여, 그분의 영이 지금도 저희와 함께 계십니다!\""]], [["An overpowering feeling seized the three.", "압도하는 감정이 세 사람을 사로잡았다."], ["The Greek with difficulty restrained an outcry.", "그리스인은 가까스로 터져 나오려는 외침을 참았다."], ["Herod’s gaze darted quickly from one to the other; he was more suspicious and dissatisfied than before.", "헤롯의 눈길이 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 재빠르게 옮겨 다녔으니, 그는 전보다 더 의심스럽고 못마땅해했다."]], [["“You are mocking me,”", "\"그대들은 나를 조롱하고 있구려.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“If not, tell me more.", "\"그게 아니라면, 더 말해 보시오."], ["What is to follow the coming of the new king?”", "그 새로운 왕이 오면 그 뒤에 무슨 일이 일어나오?\""]], [["“The salvation of men.”", "\"사람들의 구원입니다.\""]], [["“From what?”", "\"무엇으로부터?\""]], [["“Their wickedness.” “How?”", "\"그들의 사악함으로부터입니다.\""]], [["“By the divine agencies—Faith, Love, and Good Works.”", "\"어떻게?\" \"신의 권능, 곧 믿음과 사랑과 선행으로써입니다.\""]], [["“Then”—Herod paused, and from his look no man could have said with what feeling he continued—“you are the heralds of the Christ. Is that all?”", "\"그렇다면\"—헤롯이 말을 멈췄으니, 그가 어떤 심정으로 말을 이었는지는 그 표정으로 누구도 알 수 없었을 것이다—\"그대들은 그리스도의 선구자들이로구려."], ["Balthasar bowed low.", "그것이 전부요?\""]], [["“We are your servants, O king.”", "발타사르가 깊이 머리를 숙였다."]], [["The monarch touched a bell, and the attendant appeared.", "\"저희는 임금님의 종들이옵니다, 오 왕이시여.\""]], [["“Bring the gifts,” the master said.", "군주가 종을 울리자 시종이 나타났다."]], [["The attendant went out, but in a little while returned, and, kneeling before the guests, gave to each one an outer robe or mantle of scarlet and blue, and a girdle of gold.", "\"예물을 가져오너라.\" 주인이 말했다. 시종이 나갔다가 잠시 뒤 돌아와, 손님들 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 저마다에게 진홍빛과 푸른빛의 겉옷, 곧 외투 하나와 황금 띠 하나를 바쳤다."], ["They acknowledged the honors with Eastern prostrations.", "그들은 동방식으로 엎드려 절하며 그 영예를 받아들였다."]], [["“A word further,” said Herod, when the ceremony was ended.", "\"한마디 더 묻겠소.\" 의식이 끝나자 헤롯이 말했다."], ["“To the officer of the gate, and but now to me, you spoke of seeing a star in the east.”", "\"그대들은 성문의 관리에게, 또 방금 내게, 동방에서 별 하나를 보았다고 했소.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Balthasar, “his star, the star of the newly born.”", "발타사르가 말했다. \"그분의 별, 갓 태어나신 분의 별입니다.\""]], [["“What time did it appear?”", "\"그것이 언제 나타났소?\""]], [["“When we were bidden come hither.”", "\"저희가 이리로 오라는 분부를 받았을 때입니다.\""]], [["Herod arose, signifying the audience was over.", "헤롯이 일어서서 알현이 끝났음을 알렸다."], ["Stepping from the throne towards them, he said, with all graciousness,", "왕좌에서 그들 쪽으로 내려서며, 그는 더없이 너그럽게 말했다."]], [["“If, as I believe, O illustrious men, you are indeed the heralds of the Christ just born, know that I have this night consulted those wisest in things Jewish, and they say with one voice he should be born in Bethlehem of Judea.", "\"오 빛나는 분들이여, 내가 믿는 대로 그대들이 참으로 갓 태어난 그리스도의 선구자라면, 알아 두시오. 나는 오늘 밤 유대의 일에 가장 밝은 자들에게 물었고, 그들은 한목소리로 그가 유대 베들레헴에서 태어날 것이라 하였소. 그대들에게 이르노니, 그리로 가시오."], ["I say to you, go thither; go and search diligently for the young child; and when you have found him bring me word again, that I may come and worship him.", "가서 그 어린아이를 부지런히 찾으시오. 그리고 찾거든 내게 다시 기별해 주시오. 나도 가서 그를 경배할 수 있도록 말이오."], ["To your going there shall be no let or hindrance.", "그대들이 가는 길에 어떤 방해나 거리낌도 없을 것이오."], ["Peace be with you!”", "평안이 그대들과 함께하기를!\""]], [["And, folding his robe about him, he left the chamber.", "그러고는 겉옷을 몸에 여미며 방을 떠났다."]], [["Directly the guide came, and led them back to the street, and thence to the khan, at the portal of which the Greek said, impulsively, “Let us to Bethlehem, O brethren, as the king has advised.”", "곧이어 길잡이가 와서 그들을 거리로, 다시 여관으로 데려다주었으니, 그 대문에서 그리스인이 충동적으로 말했다. \"베들레헴으로 갑시다, 오 형제들이여, 왕이 권한 대로.\""]], [["“Yes,” cried the Hindoo.", "\"그럽시다.\" 힌두인이 외쳤다."], ["“The Spirit burns within me.”", "\"제 안에서 성령이 불타오릅니다.\""]], [["“Be it so,” said Balthasar, with equal warmth.", "\"그렇게 합시다.\" 발타사르도 똑같이 뜨겁게 말했다."], ["“The camels are ready.”", "\"낙타는 준비되어 있소.\""]], [["They gave gifts to the steward, mounted into their saddles, received directions to the Joppa Gate, and departed.", "그들은 관리인에게 예물을 주고 안장에 올라, 욥바 문으로 가는 길을 안내받아 떠났다."], ["At their approach the great valves were unbarred, and they passed out into the open country, taking the road so lately travelled by Joseph and Mary.", "그들이 다가가자 거대한 문짝이 빗장이 풀려 열렸고, 그들은 들판으로 빠져나가, 조금 전에 요셉과 마리아가 지나간 그 길로 접어들었다."], ["As they came up out of Hinnom, on the plain of Rephaim, a light appeared, at first wide-spread and faint.", "그들이 힌놈 골짜기에서 르바임 평원으로 올라설 때, 빛 하나가 나타났으니, 처음에는 넓게 퍼져 희미했다."], ["Their pulses fluttered fast.", "그들의 맥박이 빠르게 뛰었다."], ["The light intensified rapidly; they closed their eyes against its burning brilliance: when they dared look again, lo!", "빛은 급속히 강렬해졌고, 그들은 그 타는 듯한 광채에 눈을 감았다. 다시 감히 바라보았을 때, 보라!"], ["the star, perfect as any in the heavens, but low down and moving slowly before them.", "하늘의 어떤 별과도 다름없이 완전한 그 별이, 낮게 드리워져 그들 앞에서 천천히 움직이고 있었다."], ["And they folded their hands, and shouted, and rejoiced with exceeding great joy.", "그러자 그들은 두 손을 모으고 환호하며, 더없이 큰 기쁨으로 즐거워했다."]], [["“God is with us! God is with us!”", "\"하느님께서 우리와 함께하신다!"], ["they repeated, in frequent cheer, all the way, until the star, rising out of the valley beyond Mar Elias, stood still over a house up on the slope of the hill near the town.", "하느님께서 우리와 함께하신다!\" 그들은 가는 내내 거듭 환호하며 외쳤으니, 마침내 그 별이 마르 엘리야스 너머 골짜기에서 솟아올라, 그 고을 가까운 언덕 비탈 위에 있는 한 집 위에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV", "# CHAPTER XIV"]], [["It was now the beginning of the third watch, and at Bethlehem the morning was breaking over the mountains in the east, but so feebly that it was yet night in the valley.", "지금은 셋째 야경이 시작될 무렵이었고, 베들레헴에서는 동쪽 산 위로 아침이 밝아 오고 있었으나, 그 빛이 너무 희미하여 골짜기는 아직 밤이었다."], ["The watchman on the roof of the old khan, shivering in the chilly air, was listening for the first distinguishable sounds with which life, awakening, greets the dawn, when a light came moving up the hill towards the house.", "낡은 객사 지붕 위의 파수꾼은 쌀쌀한 공기에 몸을 떨며, 깨어난 생명이 새벽을 맞이하며 내는 첫 소리들을 가려들으려 귀 기울이고 있었다. 그때 한 줄기 빛이 언덕을 따라 집을 향해 움직여 올라왔다."], ["He thought it a torch in some one’s hand; next moment he thought it a meteor; the brilliance grew, however, until it became a star.", "처음에 그는 누군가의 손에 들린 횃불인 줄 알았다. 다음 순간에는 유성인가 싶었다. 그러나 그 광채는 점점 커져 마침내 별이 되었다."], ["Sore afraid, he cried out, and brought everybody within the walls to the roof.", "두려움에 사로잡힌 그는 소리를 질러, 담장 안의 모든 사람을 지붕 위로 불러 모았다."], ["The phenomenon, in eccentric motion, continued to approach; the rocks, trees, and roadway under it shone as in a glare of lightning; directly its brightness became blinding.", "그 빛은 변덕스러운 움직임으로 계속 다가왔다. 그 아래의 바위와 나무와 길은 번개가 번쩍이는 듯 환하게 빛났다. 이윽고 그 밝기는 눈을 멀게 할 지경이 되었다."], ["The more timid of the beholders fell upon their knees, and prayed, with their faces hidden; the boldest, covering their eyes, crouched, and now and then snatched glances fearfully.", "보는 이들 가운데 겁 많은 자들은 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 가린 채 기도했다. 가장 담대한 자들도 눈을 가린 채 웅크리고, 이따금 두려운 마음으로 흘끔흘끔 곁눈질했다."], ["Afterwhile the khan and everything thereabout lay under the intolerable radiance.", "얼마 뒤 객사와 그 주변의 모든 것이 견디기 힘든 광채 아래 놓였다."], ["Such as dared look beheld the star standing still directly over the house in front of the cave where the Child had been born.", "감히 쳐다본 자들은, 그 아이가 태어난 동굴 앞 집 바로 위에 별이 멈춰 서 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["In the height of this scene, the wise men came up, and at the gate dismounted from their camels, and shouted for admission.", "이 광경이 절정에 이르렀을 때, 동방박사들이 올라와 문 앞에서 낙타에서 내리고는 들여보내 달라고 외쳤다."], ["When the steward so far mastered his terror as to give them heed, he drew the bars and opened to them.", "객사지기가 두려움을 가까스로 다스리고 그들에게 귀를 기울이자, 그는 빗장을 풀고 문을 열어 주었다."], ["The camels looked spectral in the unnatural light, and, besides their outlandishness, there were in the faces and manner of the three visitors an eagerness and exaltation which still further excited the keeper’s fears and fancy; he fell back, and for a time could not answer the question they put to him.", "그 기이한 빛 속에서 낙타들은 유령처럼 보였고, 그 낯선 생김새에 더하여 세 방문객의 얼굴과 거동에는 간절함과 환희가 서려 있어, 객사지기의 두려움과 망상을 한층 부추겼다. 그는 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 한동안 그들이 던지는 물음에 대답하지 못했다."]], [["“Is not this Bethlehem of Judea?”", "\"여기가 유대 베들레헴이 아니오?\""]], [["But others came, and by their presence gave him assurance.", "그러나 다른 이들이 와서, 그들이 곁에 있는 것만으로 그에게 확신을 주었다."]], [["“No, this is but the khan; the town lies farther on.”", "\"아니오, 여기는 객사일 뿐이고, 마을은 더 가야 있소.\""]], [["“Is there not here a child newly born?”", "\"여기 갓 태어난 아이가 있지 않소?\""]], [["The bystanders turned to each other marvelling, though some of them answered, “Yes, yes.”", "곁에 선 사람들은 놀라며 서로를 돌아보았으나, 그 가운데 몇몇은 \"예, 예.\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["“Show us to him!” said the Greek, impatiently.", "\"그분에게로 우리를 안내해 주시오!\" 그리스 사람이 애타게 말했다."]], [["“Show us to him!”", "\"그분에게로 우리를 안내해 주시오!\""], ["cried Balthasar, breaking through his gravity; “for we have seen his star, even that which ye behold over the house, and are come to worship him.”", "발타사르가 그 위엄을 깨뜨리며 소리쳤다. \"우리는 그분의 별을, 바로 그대들이 저 집 위에서 보고 있는 저 별을 보았으며, 그분을 경배하러 왔소.\""]], [["The Hindoo clasped his hands, exclaiming, “God indeed lives!", "힌두 사람이 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤다. \"하느님은 참으로 살아 계시는도다!"], ["Make haste, make haste!", "서두르시오, 서두르시오!"], ["The Savior is found.", "구세주를 찾았소."], ["Blessed, blessed are we above men!”", "우리는 복되도다, 사람들 가운데 복되도다!\""]], [["The people from the roof came down and followed the strangers as they were taken through the court and out into the enclosure; at sight of the star yet above the cave, though less candescent than before, some turned back afraid; the greater part went on.", "지붕에 있던 사람들이 내려와, 낯선 이들이 안뜰을 지나 울안으로 안내되어 갈 때 그 뒤를 따랐다. 동굴 위에 아직 떠 있는 별을 보고는, 비록 전보다 광채가 덜했으나, 몇몇은 두려워 돌아섰다. 그러나 대부분은 그대로 따라갔다."], ["As the strangers neared the house, the orb arose; when they were at the door, it was high up overhead vanishing; when they entered, it went out lost to sight.", "낯선 이들이 집에 가까이 다가가자 그 별빛이 솟아올랐다. 그들이 문 앞에 이르렀을 때 별은 머리 위 높이 떠올라 사라져 가고 있었고, 그들이 안으로 들어서자 별은 자취도 없이 꺼져 버렸다."], ["And to the witnesses of what then took place came a conviction that there was a divine relation between the star and the strangers, which extended also to at least some of the occupants of the cave.", "그리고 그때 일어난 일을 지켜본 이들에게는, 별과 낯선 이들 사이에 신성한 관계가 있으며, 그 관계가 적어도 동굴 안의 몇몇 사람에게까지 미친다는 확신이 들었다."], ["When the door was opened, they crowded in.", "문이 열리자 그들은 안으로 몰려 들어갔다."]], [["The apartment was lighted by a lantern enough to enable the strangers to find the mother, and the child awake in her lap.", "방 안은 등불 하나로 밝혀져 있었는데, 그 빛만으로도 낯선 이들이 어머니를, 그리고 그 무릎 위에서 깨어 있는 아이를 찾기에는 넉넉했다."]], [["“Is the child thine?”", "\"이 아이가 당신의 아이입니까?\""], ["asked Balthasar of Mary.", "발타사르가 마리아에게 물었다."]], [["And she who had kept all the things in the least affecting the little one, and pondered them in her heart, held it up in the light, saying,", "그러자 그 어린것에 관한 사소한 일까지 모두 마음속에 간직하고 곰곰이 되새겨 온 그녀는, 아이를 빛 속으로 들어 올리며 말했다."]], [["“He is my son!”", "\"이 아이는 제 아들입니다!\""]], [["And they fell down and worshipped him.", "그러자 그들은 엎드려 아이를 경배했다."]], [["They saw the child was as other children: about its head was neither nimbus nor material crown; its lips opened not in speech; if it heard their expressions of joy, their invocations, their prayers, it made no sign whatever, but, baby-like, looked longer at the flame in the lantern than at them.", "그들은 아이가 다른 아이들과 다름없음을 보았다. 그 머리 둘레에는 후광도, 형체 있는 면류관도 없었다. 그 입술은 말을 하려고 열리지 않았다. 그들의 기쁨에 찬 외침과 간구와 기도를 들었다 하더라도, 아이는 아무런 기색도 보이지 않았고, 아기답게 그들보다 등불의 불꽃을 더 오래 바라볼 뿐이었다."]], [["In a little while they arose, and, returning to the camels, brought gifts of gold, frankincense, and myrrh, and laid them before the child, abating nothing of their worshipful speeches; of which no part is given, for the thoughtful know that the pure worship of the pure heart was then what it is now, and has always been, an inspired song.", "얼마 뒤 그들은 일어나, 낙타들에게로 돌아가서 황금과 유향과 몰약의 예물을 가져와 아이 앞에 바쳤으며, 경배의 말도 조금도 줄이지 않았다. 그 말은 여기에 한마디도 옮기지 않으니, 사려 깊은 이라면 순결한 마음의 순결한 경배가 그때나 지금이나, 또 예부터 늘 그러했듯, 영감에 찬 노래임을 알기 때문이다."]], [["And this was the Savior they had come so far to find!", "그리고 이 아이가 바로 그들이 그토록 먼 길을 찾아온 구세주였다!"]], [["Yet they worshipped without a doubt. Why?", "그런데도 그들은 한 점 의심 없이 경배했다. 어찌하여?"]], [["Their faith rested upon the signs sent them by him whom we have since come to know as the Father; and they were of the kind to whom his promises were so all-sufficient that they asked nothing about his ways.", "어찌하여? 그들의 믿음은, 우리가 훗날 아버지로 알게 된 그분이 보내신 표징 위에 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그들은 그분의 약속만으로 모든 것이 충족되어, 그분의 방식에 대해서는 아무것도 묻지 않는 그런 부류였다."], ["Few there were who had seen the signs and heard the promises—the Mother and Joseph, the shepherds, and the Three—yet they all believed alike; that is to say, in this period of the plan of salvation, God was all and the Child nothing.", "그 표징을 보고 그 약속을 들은 이는 몇 되지 않았다. 어머니와 요셉, 목자들, 그리고 세 사람뿐이었다. 그런데도 그들은 모두 한결같이 믿었다."], ["But look forward, O reader! A time will come when the signs will all proceed from the Son.", "다시 말해, 구원 계획의 이 시기에는 하느님이 전부였고 아이는 아무것도 아니었다. 그러나 앞을 내다보라, 오 독자여!"], ["Happy they who then believe in him!", "모든 표징이 아들에게서 나올 때가 오리라. 그때에 그분을 믿는 자들은 복되도다!"]], [["Let us wait that period.", "그 시기를 기다려 보자."]], [["# BOOK SECOND", "# BOOK SECOND"]], [["“There is a fire And motion of the soul which will not dwell In its own narrow being, but aspire Beyond the fitting medium of desire; And, but once kindled, quenchless evermore, Preys upon high adventure, nor can tire Of aught but rest.”", "“영혼에는 불길과 약동이 있어 제 비좁은 존재에 머무르려 하지 않고 욕망의 합당한 한계 너머를 갈망하며, 한번 타오르면 영영 꺼지지 않고 드높은 모험을 먹이로 삼아, 안식 말고는 그 무엇에도 지치지 않는다.”"], ["Childe Harold. CHAPTER I", "차일드 해럴드. 제1장"]], [["It is necessary now to carry the reader forward twenty-one years, to the beginning of the administration of Valerius Gratus, the fourth imperial governor of Judea—a period which will be remembered as rent by political agitations in Jerusalem, if, indeed, it be not the precise time of the opening of the final quarrel between the Jew and the Roman.", "이제 독자를 이십일 년 뒤로, 유대의 네 번째 제국 총독인 발레리우스 그라투스가 통치를 시작하던 무렵으로 데려갈 필요가 있다—그 시기는 예루살렘이 정치적 동요로 갈가리 찢긴 때로 기억될 것이니, 실로 그때가 유대인과 로마인 사이에 마지막 다툼이 벌어지기 시작한 바로 그 시점이 아니라면 말이다."], ["In the interval Judea had been subjected to changes affecting her in many ways, but in nothing so much as her political status.", "그동안 유대는 여러 면에서 영향을 받는 변화를 겪었으나, 그 무엇도 정치적 지위만큼 크게 흔들린 것은 없었다."], ["Herod the Great died within one year after the birth of the Child—died so miserably that the Christian world had reason to believe him overtaken by the Divine wrath.", "헤롯 대왕은 그 아기가 태어난 지 일 년이 채 못 되어 죽었으니, 어찌나 비참하게 죽었던지 그리스도교 세계가 그를 신의 진노에 사로잡힌 자로 믿을 만한 까닭이 되었다."], ["Like all great rulers who spend their lives in perfecting the power they create, he dreamed of transmitting his throne and crown—of being the founder of a dynasty.", "자기가 세운 권력을 완성하는 데 평생을 바친 모든 위대한 통치자가 그러하듯, 그 역시 제 왕좌와 왕관을 물려주는 일을, 곧 한 왕조의 시조가 되는 일을 꿈꾸었다."], ["With that intent, he left a will dividing his territories between his three sons, Antipas, Philip, and Archelaus, of whom the last was appointed to succeed to the title.", "그런 뜻으로 그는 유언을 남겨 자기 영토를 세 아들, 안티파스와 빌립과 아르켈라오스에게 나누었으며, 그 가운데 마지막 아들에게 왕위를 잇도록 정해 두었다."], ["The testament was necessarily referred to Augustus, the emperor, who ratified all its provisions with one exception: he withheld from Archelaus the title of king until he proved his capacity and loyalty; in lieu thereof, he created him ethnarch, and as such permitted him to govern nine years, when, for misconduct and inability to stay the turbulent elements that grew and strengthened around him, he was sent into Gaul as an exile.", "그 유언은 마땅히 황제 아우구스투스에게 회부되었고, 황제는 단 한 가지를 빼고는 모든 조항을 비준했다. 아르켈라오스에게는 그 능력과 충성을 입증할 때까지 왕의 칭호를 주지 않았다. 그 대신 그를 분봉왕으로 삼아 그 자격으로 구 년을 다스리게 했는데, 끝내 제 주위에서 자라나 거세지는 소요의 무리를 가라앉히지 못한 무능과 실정 탓에 갈리아로 유배되고 말았다."]], [["Cæsar was not content with deposing Archelaus; he struck the people of Jerusalem in a manner that touched their pride, and keenly wounded the sensibilities of the haughty habitues of the Temple.", "카이사르는 아르켈라오스를 폐위하는 것으로 만족하지 않았다. 그는 예루살렘 백성의 자존심을 건드리는 방식으로 그들을 내리쳤고, 성전을 드나드는 오만한 무리의 감정에 날카로운 상처를 입혔다."], ["He reduced Judea to a Roman province, and annexed it to the prefecture of Syria.", "그는 유대를 로마의 한 속주로 격하시켜 시리아 총독부에 병합했다."], ["So, instead of a king ruling royally from the palace left by Herod on Mount Zion, the city fell into the hands of an officer of the second grade, an appointee called procurator, who communicated with the court in Rome through the Legate of Syria, residing in Antioch.", "그리하여 헤롯이 시온산에 남긴 궁전에서 왕답게 다스리는 왕 대신, 그 도성은 이등급 관리, 곧 총독이라 불리는 임명직의 손에 떨어졌으니, 그는 안티오크에 머무는 시리아 총독을 거쳐 로마의 궁정과 연락을 주고받았다."], ["To make the hurt more painful, the procurator was not permitted to establish himself in Jerusalem; Cæsarea was his seat of government.", "그 상처를 더욱 쓰라리게 하려는 듯, 총독은 예루살렘에 자리 잡는 것조차 허락받지 못했다. 카이사레아가 그의 통치 거점이었다."], ["Most humiliating, however, most exasperating, most studied, Samaria, of all the world the most despised—Samaria was joined to Judea as a part of the same province!", "그러나 가장 굴욕스럽고, 가장 분통 터지며, 가장 의도적이었던 것은, 온 세상에서 가장 멸시받는 사마리아가, 바로 그 사마리아가 같은 속주의 일부로서 유대에 붙여진 일이었다!"], ["What ineffable misery the bigoted Separatists or Pharisees endured at finding themselves elbowed and laughed at in the procurator’s presence in Cæsarea by the devotees of Gerizim!", "카이사레아에서 총독 앞에 선 채 그리심산을 섬기는 무리에게 떠밀리고 비웃음을 당하는 처지를 깨달았을 때, 완고한 분리주의자들, 곧 바리새인들이 견뎌야 했던 그 형언할 수 없는 비참함이란!"]], [["In this rain of sorrows, one consolation, and one only, remained to the fallen people: the high-priest occupied the Herodian palace in the market-place, and kept the semblance of a court there.", "이 슬픔의 빗줄기 속에서도 몰락한 백성에게는 단 하나, 오직 하나의 위안이 남아 있었다. 대제사장이 장터에 자리한 헤롯의 궁전을 차지하여 그곳에 궁정의 시늉을 유지하고 있었던 것이다."], ["What his authority really was is a matter of easy estimate.", "그의 권한이 실제로 무엇이었는지는 가늠하기 쉬운 일이다."], ["Judgment of life and death was retained by the procurator.", "생사를 가르는 재판권은 총독이 쥐고 있었다."], ["Justice was administered in the name and according to the decretals of Rome.", "정의는 로마의 이름으로, 로마의 법령에 따라 집행되었다."], ["Yet more significant, the royal house was jointly occupied by the imperial exciseman, and all his corps of assistants, registrars, collectors, publicans, informers, and spies.", "더욱 의미심장하게도, 그 왕가의 궁전은 황제의 징세관과 그 휘하의 온갖 보좌관, 등기관, 징수관, 세리, 밀고자, 첩자 들이 함께 차지하고 있었다."], ["Still, to the dreamers of liberty to come, there was a certain satisfaction in the fact that the chief ruler in the palace was a Jew.", "그럼에도 장차 올 자유를 꿈꾸는 자들에게는 그 궁전의 으뜸가는 통치자가 유대인이라는 사실에 어떤 흡족함이 있었다."], ["His mere presence there day after day kept them reminded of the covenants and promises of the prophets, and the ages when Jehovah governed the tribes through the sons of Aaron; it was to them a certain sign that he had not abandoned them: so their hopes lived, and served their patience, and helped them wait grimly the son of Judah who was to rule Israel.", "그가 날마다 그곳에 있다는 사실만으로도 그들은 예언자들의 언약과 약속을, 그리고 여호와께서 아론의 자손을 통해 지파들을 다스리시던 시대를 떠올렸다. 그것은 그들에게 여호와께서 자기들을 버리지 않으셨다는 확실한 표징이었다. 그리하여 그들의 희망은 살아남아 그들의 인내를 떠받쳤고, 이스라엘을 다스릴 유다의 아들을 굳세게 기다리도록 도왔다."]], [["Judea had been a Roman province eighty years and more—ample time for the Cæsars to study the idiosyncrasies of the people—time enough, at least, to learn that the Jew, with all his pride, could be quietly governed if his religion were respected.", "유대가 로마의 속주가 된 지 팔십 년 남짓 되었으니, 카이사르들이 그 백성의 기질을 연구하기에 넉넉한 시간이었고, 적어도 유대인이 그 온갖 자존심에도 불구하고 제 종교만 존중받으면 조용히 다스려질 수 있다는 사실을 배우기에는 충분한 시간이었다."], ["Proceeding upon that policy, the predecessors of Gratus had carefully abstained from interfering with any of the sacred observances of their subjects.", "그 방침에 따라 그라투스의 전임자들은 백성의 신성한 의례 어느 하나에도 끼어들기를 조심스레 삼갔다. 그러나 그는 다른 길을 택했다."], ["But he chose a different course: almost his first official act was to expel Hannas from the high-priesthood, and give the place to Ishmael, son of Fabus.", "그의 거의 첫 공무가 한나스를 대제사장직에서 쫓아내고 그 자리를 파부스의 아들 이스마엘에게 넘긴 일이었다."]], [["Whether the act was directed by Augustus, or proceeded from Gratus himself, its impolicy became speedily apparent.", "그 일이 아우구스투스의 지시였는지 아니면 그라투스 자신에게서 비롯된 것인지는 몰라도, 그 정략의 어리석음은 이내 분명하게 드러났다."], ["The reader shall be spared a chapter on Jewish politics; a few words upon the subject, however, are essential to such as may follow the succeeding narration critically.", "독자에게 유대 정치를 다룬 한 장(章)을 늘어놓는 수고는 면하게 해 주겠으나, 이 주제에 관한 몇 마디만은 뒤이은 이야기를 꼼꼼히 좇으려는 이에게 반드시 필요하다."], ["At this time, leaving origin out of view, there were in Judea the party of the nobles and the Separatist or popular party.", "이 무렵 그 기원은 접어 두더라도, 유대에는 귀족당과 분리주의자, 곧 민중당이 있었다."], ["Upon Herod’s death, the two united against Archelaus; from temple to palace, from Jerusalem to Rome, they fought him; sometimes with intrigue, sometimes with the actual weapons of war.", "헤롯이 죽자 두 당파는 손잡고 아르켈라오스에게 맞섰다. 성전에서 궁전으로, 예루살렘에서 로마로, 그들은 때로는 음모로, 때로는 실제 전쟁의 무기로 그와 싸웠다."], ["More than once the holy cloisters on Moriah resounded with the cries of fighting-men.", "모리아산의 거룩한 회랑이 싸우는 자들의 함성으로 울린 일도 한두 번이 아니었다."], ["Finally, they drove him into exile.", "마침내 그들은 그를 유배지로 내몰았다."], ["Meantime throughout this struggle the allies had their diverse objects in view.", "그러는 동안 이 다툼 내내 두 동맹자는 저마다 다른 목적을 품고 있었다."], ["The nobles hated Joazar, the high-priest; the Separatists, on the other hand, were his zealous adherents.", "귀족들은 대제사장 요아자르를 미워했으나, 분리주의자들은 도리어 그를 열렬히 따르는 무리였다."], ["When Herod’s settlement went down with Archelaus, Joazar shared the fall.", "헤롯의 체제가 아르켈라오스와 함께 무너지자, 요아자르도 그 추락을 함께 겪었다."], ["Hannas, the son of Seth, was selected by the nobles to fill the great office; thereupon the allies divided.", "셋의 아들 한나스가 귀족들에 의해 그 큰 직책을 채울 자로 뽑혔으니, 그로 인해 동맹은 갈라졌다."], ["The induction of the Sethian brought them face to face in fierce hostility.", "셋 가문 사람의 취임은 두 당파를 격렬한 적의 속에 마주 서게 만들었다."]], [["In the course of the struggle with the unfortunate ethnarch, the nobles had found it expedient to attach themselves to Rome.", "그 불운한 분봉왕과의 다툼이 진행되는 동안, 귀족들은 로마에 붙는 편이 유리하다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Discerning that when the existing settlement was broken up some form of government must needs follow, they suggested the conversion of Judea into a province.", "기존 체제가 무너지면 어떤 형태로든 통치 기구가 뒤따라야 함을 간파한 그들은 유대를 속주로 전환할 것을 제안했다."], ["The fact furnished the Separatists an additional cause for attack; and, when Samaria was made part of the province, the nobles sank into a minority, with nothing to support them but the imperial court and the prestige of their rank and wealth; yet for fifteen years—down, indeed, to the coming of Valerius Gratus—they managed to maintain themselves in both palace and Temple.", "이 사실은 분리주의자들에게 또 하나의 공격 빌미가 되었으며, 사마리아가 속주의 일부로 편입되자 귀족들은 소수파로 가라앉아, 황제의 궁정과 제 신분과 재산의 위세 말고는 그들을 떠받칠 것이 아무것도 없게 되었다. 그러면서도 십오 년 동안, 실로 발레리우스 그라투스가 올 때까지 그들은 궁전과 성전 양쪽에서 제 자리를 지켜 냈다."]], [["Hannas, the idol of his party, had used his power faithfully in the interest of his imperial patron.", "제 당파의 우상이었던 한나스는 자기 황제 후원자의 이익을 위해 그 권력을 충실히 써 왔다."], ["A Roman garrison held the Tower of Antonia; a Roman guard kept the gates of the palace; a Roman judge dispensed justice civil and criminal; a Roman system of taxation, mercilessly executed, crushed both city and country; daily, hourly, and in a thousand ways, the people were bruised and galled, and taught the difference between a life of independence and a life of subjection; yet Hannas kept them in comparative quiet.", "로마 수비대가 안토니아 탑을 지켰고, 로마 경비병이 궁전 문을 지켰으며, 로마 재판관이 민사와 형사의 정의를 베풀었고, 무자비하게 집행되는 로마식 조세 제도가 도성과 시골을 함께 짓눌렀다. 날마다, 시시각각, 천 가지 방식으로 백성은 멍들고 쓰라림을 당하며 독립된 삶과 예속된 삶의 차이를 배웠으나, 한나스는 그들을 그런대로 잠잠하게 붙들어 두었다."], ["Rome had no truer friend; and he made his loss instantly felt.", "로마에는 그보다 더 진실한 벗이 없었으며, 그의 상실은 즉시 체감되었다."], ["Delivering his vestments to Ishmael, the new appointee, he walked from the courts of the Temple into the councils of the Separatists, and became the head of a new combination, Bethusian and Sethian.", "새 임명자 이스마엘에게 제 제의를 넘긴 그는 성전의 뜰에서 곧장 분리주의자들의 회의로 걸어 들어가, 베투스 가문과 셋 가문이 결합한 새로운 연합의 우두머리가 되었다."]], [["Gratus, the procurator, left thus without a party, saw the fires which, in the fifteen years, had sunk into sodden smoke begin to glow with returning life.", "이리하여 의지할 당파 없이 남겨진 총독 그라투스는, 십오 년 동안 눅눅한 연기로 사그라들었던 불길이 되살아나는 생기로 다시 타오르기 시작하는 것을 보았다."], ["A month after Ishmael took the office, the Roman found it necessary to visit him in Jerusalem.", "이스마엘이 직책을 맡은 지 한 달 만에 로마인은 예루살렘으로 그를 찾아갈 필요를 느꼈다."], ["When from the walls, hooting and hissing him, the Jews beheld his guard enter the north gate of the city and march to the Tower of Antonia, they understood the real purpose of the visit—a full cohort of legionaries was added to the former garrison, and the keys of their yoke could now be tightened with impunity.", "유대인들이 성벽에서 그에게 야유와 휘파람을 퍼붓는 가운데, 그의 경비대가 도성의 북문으로 들어와 안토니아 탑으로 행진하는 것을 보았을 때, 그들은 그 방문의 참뜻을 깨달았다. 로마 군단병 한 개 대대가 종전의 수비대에 더해졌으니, 이제 그들에게 씌운 멍에의 빗장은 거리낌 없이 죄어들 수 있게 된 것이다."], ["If the procurator deemed it important to make an example, alas for the first offender!", "만일 총독이 본보기를 세우는 것을 중요하게 여겼다면, 첫 번째 위반자에게 화가 있으리라!"]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["With the foregoing explanation in mind, the reader is invited to look into one of the gardens of the palace on Mount Zion.", "앞의 설명을 마음에 새긴 채, 이제 독자를 시온 산 궁전의 한 정원으로 이끌고자 한다."], ["The time was noonday in the middle of July, when the heat of summer was at its highest.", "때는 칠월 한복판의 정오 무렵으로, 여름의 더위가 절정에 이른 시각이었다."]], [["The garden was bounded on every side by buildings, which in places arose two stories, with verandas shading the doors and windows of the lower story, while retreating galleries, guarded by strong balustrades, adorned and protected the upper.", "정원은 사방이 건물로 둘러싸여 있었는데, 어떤 곳은 이층으로 솟아, 아래층의 문과 창에는 차양을 드리운 베란다가 그늘을 드리우고, 위층에는 튼튼한 난간으로 보호된 안쪽으로 물러난 회랑이 장식과 보호를 겸했다."], ["Here and there, moreover, the structures fell into what appeared low colonnades, permitting the passage of such winds as chanced to blow, and allowing other parts of the house to be seen, the better to realize its magnitude and beauty.", "게다가 곳곳에서 그 구조물들은 낮은 주랑처럼 트여, 어쩌다 부는 바람이 지나가도록 하고, 집의 다른 부분들이 눈에 들어와 그 웅장함과 아름다움을 한결 또렷이 느끼게 했다."], ["The arrangement of the ground was equally pleasant to the eye.", "땅의 배치 또한 그에 못지않게 보기 좋았다."], ["There were walks, and patches of grass and shrubbery, and a few large trees, rare specimens of the palm, grouped with the carob, apricot, and walnut.", "산책로가 있고, 잔디밭과 관목 무더기가 있으며, 큰 나무 몇 그루가 있었으니, 보기 드문 종려나무들이 캐럽나무, 살구나무, 호두나무와 어울려 무리를 이루었다."], ["In all directions the grade sloped gently from the centre, where there was a reservoir, or deep marble basin, broken at intervals by little gates which, when raised, emptied the water into sluices bordering the walks—a cunning device for the rescue of the place from the aridity too prevalent elsewhere in the region.", "사방으로 땅은 한가운데서 완만하게 비탈져 내려갔고, 그 한가운데에는 저수조, 곧 깊은 대리석 수반이 있어, 군데군데 작은 수문으로 끊겨 있었다. 그 수문을 들어 올리면 물이 산책로를 따라 난 수로로 흘러들었으니, 이는 그 지역 다른 곳에 너무도 흔한 메마름에서 이곳을 구해 내려는 교묘한 장치였다."]], [["Not far from the fountain, there was a small pool of clear water nourishing a clump of cane and oleander, such as grow on the Jordan and down by the Dead Sea.", "분수에서 멀지 않은 곳에는 맑은 물이 고인 작은 못이 있어, 갈대와 협죽도 무리를 길러 냈으니, 요르단 강가나 사해 기슭에 자라는 그런 것들이었다."], ["Between the clump and the pool, unmindful of the sun shining full upon them in the breathless air, two boys, one about nineteen, the other seventeen, sat engaged in earnest conversation.", "그 무리와 못 사이에, 숨 막히는 대기 속에서 해가 온통 내리쬐는 것도 아랑곳하지 않고, 두 소년이 앉아 진지한 대화를 나누고 있었다. 하나는 열아홉 살쯤, 다른 하나는 열일곱 살쯤 되어 보였다."]], [["They were both handsome, and, at first glance, would have been pronounced brothers.", "두 사람 다 잘생겼으니, 언뜻 보면 형제라 했을 법했다."], ["Both had hair and eyes black; their faces were deeply browned; and, sitting, they seemed of a size proper for the difference in their ages.", "둘 다 머리칼과 눈이 검고, 얼굴은 짙게 그을렸으며, 앉은 모습은 나이 차이에 걸맞은 몸집으로 보였다."]], [["The elder was bareheaded.", "손위 소년은 맨머리였다."], ["A loose tunic, dropping to the knees, was his attire complete, except sandals and a light-blue mantle spread under him on the seat.", "무릎까지 내려오는 헐렁한 튜닉이 그가 걸친 옷의 전부였으니, 샌들과 의자 위 제 밑에 깔린 옅은 푸른 망토를 빼면 그러했다."], ["The costume left his arms and legs exposed, and they were brown as the face; nevertheless, a certain grace of manner, refinement of features, and culture of voice decided his rank.", "그 차림새는 팔다리를 드러냈고, 그것들은 얼굴처럼 갈색이었다. 그런데도 어딘가 우아한 몸가짐, 세련된 이목구비, 잘 다듬어진 목소리가 그의 신분을 말해 주었다."], ["The tunic, of softest woollen, gray-tinted, at the neck, sleeves, and edge of the skirt bordered with red, and bound to the waist by a tasselled silken cord, certified him the Roman he was.", "더없이 부드러운 모직으로 지은 잿빛 튜닉은 목과 소매와 치맛단을 붉은빛으로 둘렀고, 술이 달린 비단 끈으로 허리를 동였으니, 그가 로마인임을 증명하고 있었다."], ["And if in speech he now and then gazed haughtily at his companion and addressed him as an inferior, he might almost be excused, for he was of a family noble even in Rome—a circumstance which in that age justified any assumption.", "그리고 그가 말 중에 이따금 동무를 거만하게 바라보며 아랫사람 다루듯 대했더라도, 그것은 거의 용서받을 만했다. 그는 로마에서조차 귀족인 가문 출신이었으니, 이는 그 시대에는 어떤 자만이든 정당화하는 사정이었다."], ["In the terrible wars between the first Cæsar and his great enemies, a Messala had been the friend of Brutus.", "첫 카이사르와 그의 위대한 적들 사이에 벌어진 끔찍한 전쟁에서, 한 메살라가 브루투스의 벗이었다."], ["After Philippi, without sacrifice of his honor, he and the conqueror became reconciled.", "필리피 전투 뒤, 그는 명예를 저버리지 않고도 승자와 화해했다."], ["Yet later, when Octavius disputed for the empire, Messala supported him.", "그러나 그 뒤 옥타비우스가 제위를 다투었을 때, 메살라는 그를 지지했다."], ["Octavius, as the Emperor Augustus, remembered the service, and showered the family with honors.", "옥타비우스는 아우구스투스 황제가 되어 그 봉사를 잊지 않고, 그 가문에 영예를 듬뿍 내렸다."], ["Among other things, Judea being reduced to a province, he sent the son of his old client or retainer to Jerusalem, charged with the receipt and management of the taxes levied in that region; and in that service the son had since remained, sharing the palace with the high-priest.", "다른 무엇보다도, 유대가 한 속주로 격하되자, 그는 옛 식객 혹은 가신의 아들을 예루살렘으로 보내, 그 지역에서 거두는 세금의 수납과 관리를 맡겼다. 그 직무를 맡은 채 아들은 그 뒤로도 머물러, 대제사장과 더불어 궁전을 함께 썼다."], ["The youth just described was his son, whose habit it was to carry about with him all too faithfully a remembrance of the relation between his grandfather and the great Romans of his day.", "방금 묘사한 젊은이가 바로 그 아들이었으니, 그는 제 할아버지와 당대의 위대한 로마인들 사이의 관계에 대한 기억을 너무도 충실하게 늘 지니고 다니는 것이 버릇이었다."]], [["The associate of the Messala was slighter in form, and his garments were of fine white linen and of the prevalent style in Jerusalem; a cloth covered his head, held by a yellow cord, and arranged so as to fall away from the forehead down low over the back of the neck.", "메살라의 동무는 몸매가 더 가냘팠고, 그의 옷은 고운 흰 아마포로 지었으니 예루살렘에 두루 퍼진 양식이었다. 천 한 폭이 그의 머리를 덮어, 노란 끈으로 동이고, 이마에서 물러나 목덜미 아래로 길게 흘러내리도록 매만져져 있었다."], ["An observer skilled in the distinctions of race, and studying his features more than his costume, would have soon discovered him to be of Jewish descent.", "인종의 구별에 밝은 관찰자가 그의 차림보다 이목구비를 더 눈여겨보았다면, 곧 그가 유대 혈통임을 알아차렸을 것이다."], ["The forehead of the Roman was high and narrow, his nose sharp and aquiline, while his lips were thin and straight, and his eyes cold and close under the brows.", "로마인의 이마는 높고 좁았으며, 코는 날카롭고 매부리졌고, 입술은 얇고 곧았으며, 눈은 차갑고 눈썹 아래 바짝 붙어 있었다."], ["The front of the Israelite, on the other hand, was low and broad; his nose long, with expanded nostrils; his upper lip, slightly shading the lower one, short and curving to the dimpled corners, like a Cupid’s bow; points which, in connection with the round chin, full eyes, and oval cheeks reddened with a wine-like glow, gave his face the softness, strength, and beauty peculiar to his race.", "반면 이스라엘인의 이마는 낮고 넓었다. 코는 길고 콧방울이 벌어졌으며, 윗입술은 아랫입술을 살짝 덮으며 짧고 보조개 진 입꼬리로 굽이쳐, 마치 큐피드의 활 같았다. 둥근 턱, 그득한 눈, 포도주 같은 홍조가 어린 달걀형 뺨과 더불어, 이런 점들이 그의 얼굴에 그 민족 특유의 부드러움과 힘과 아름다움을 주었다."], ["The comeliness of the Roman was severe and chaste, that of the Jew rich and voluptuous.", "로마인의 잘생김은 엄격하고 정결했으나, 유대인의 그것은 풍성하고 관능적이었다."]], [["“Did you not say the new procurator is to arrive to-morrow?”", "\"새 총독이 내일 도착한다고 하지 않았나?\""]], [["The question proceeded from the younger of the friends, and was couched in Greek, at the time, singularly enough, the language everywhere prevalent in the politer circles of Judea; having passed from the palace into the camp and college; thence, nobody knew exactly when or how, into the Temple itself, and, for that matter, into precincts of the Temple far beyond the gates and cloisters—precincts of a sanctity intolerable for a Gentile.", "이 물음은 두 벗 가운데 손아래에게서 나왔으며, 그리스어로 되어 있었으니, 당시로서는 참으로 묘하게도 그리스어가 유대의 점잖은 사회 어디에서나 두루 쓰이는 말이었던 것이다. 그 말은 궁전에서 군영과 학교로 옮겨 갔고, 그곳에서, 언제 어떻게인지는 아무도 정확히 모르나, 성전 자체로까지 스며들었으며, 따지고 보면 문과 회랑 너머 멀리, 이방인에게는 들어가는 것조차 견딜 수 없는 신성한 구역들에까지 파고들었다."]], [["“Yes, to-morrow,”", "\"그래, 내일이지.\""], ["Messala answered.", "메살라가 대답했다."]], [["“Who told you?”", "\"누구한테 들었나?\""]], [["“I heard Ishmael, the new governor in the palace—you call him high priest—tell my father so last night.", "\"어젯밤에 이스마엘이—궁전의 새 총독 말이야, 너희는 대제사장이라 부르지—우리 아버지께 그리 말하는 걸 들었어. 그야 이집트인한테서 나온 소식이라면 더 믿을 만했겠지, 진실이 무엇인지 잊어버린 종족이니까."], ["The news had been more credible, I grant you, coming from an Egyptian, who is of a race that has forgotten what truth is, or even from an Idumaean, whose people never knew what truth was; but, to make quite certain, I saw a centurion from the Tower this morning, and he told me preparations were going on for the reception; that the armorers were furbishing the helmets and shields, and regilding the eagles and globes; and that apartments long unused were being cleansed and aired as if for an addition to the garrison—the body-guard, probably, of the great man.”", "아니면 이두매인한테서라면, 그 백성은 진실이 무엇인지 애초에 알지도 못했으니까. 하지만 아주 확실히 해 두려고, 오늘 아침에 망루에서 온 백인대장을 만났는데, 그가 말하길 영접 채비가 한창이라더군. 무기 만드는 자들이 투구와 방패를 닦고, 독수리 깃발과 구슬에 금박을 다시 입히고 있으며, 오래 쓰지 않던 방들을 마치 수비대에 인원이 더해질 것처럼—아마도 그 거물의 호위대겠지—청소하고 바람을 쐬고 있다더군.\""]], [["A perfect idea of the manner in which the answer was given cannot be conveyed, as its fine points continually escape the power behind the pen.", "그 대답이 어떤 태도로 나왔는지 그 완전한 모습을 전할 수는 없으니, 그 미묘한 점들이 펜 뒤에 숨은 힘에서 끊임없이 빠져나가는 까닭이다."], ["The reader’s fancy must come to his aid; and for that he must be reminded that reverence as a quality of the Roman mind was fast breaking down, or, rather, it was becoming unfashionable.", "독자는 상상으로 거들어야 하며, 그러기 위해서는 경외심이 로마인의 정신에서 한 자질로서 빠르게 무너지고 있었음을, 아니 오히려 시대에 뒤떨어진 것이 되어 가고 있었음을 떠올려야 한다."], ["The old religion had nearly ceased to be a faith; at most it was a mere habit of thought and expression, cherished principally by the priests who found service in the Temple profitable, and the poets who, in the turn of their verses, could not dispense with the familiar deities: there are singers of this age who are similarly given.", "옛 종교는 거의 신앙이기를 그쳤으니, 기껏해야 사고와 표현의 한낱 습관일 뿐이었다. 그것을 주로 소중히 여긴 자들은 성전 봉사를 짭짤한 벌이로 여긴 제사장들, 그리고 시구를 짓는 데 친숙한 신들을 빼놓을 수 없었던 시인들이었으니, 이 시대에도 그와 비슷한 가인들이 있다."], ["As philosophy was taking the place of religion, satire was fast substituting reverence; insomuch that in Latin opinion it was to every speech, even to the little diatribes of conversation, as salt to viands, and aroma to wine.", "철학이 종교의 자리를 차지하면서, 풍자가 빠르게 경외심을 대신해 갔다. 그리하여 라틴인의 견해로는 그것이 모든 연설에, 심지어 대화 중의 자잘한 독설에까지, 마치 음식에 소금과 같고 포도주에 향과 같은 것이 되었다."], ["The young Messala, educated in Rome, but lately returned, had caught the habit and manner; the scarce perceptible movement of the outer corner of the lower eyelid, the decided curl of the corresponding nostril, and a languid utterance affected as the best vehicle to convey the idea of general indifference, but more particularly because of the opportunities it afforded for certain rhetorical pauses thought to be of prime importance to enable the listener to take the happy conceit or receive the virus of the stinging epigram.", "로마에서 교육받고 얼마 전에야 돌아온 젊은 메살라는 그 습관과 태도를 익히고 있었으니, 아래 눈꺼풀 바깥 끝의 좀처럼 알아채기 힘든 움직임, 그에 호응하는 콧방울의 뚜렷한 일그러짐, 그리고 전반적인 무관심을 전하는 데 가장 좋은 수단으로 꾸며 낸 나른한 말투가 그러했다. 그러나 그 나른한 말투를 더욱 즐긴 까닭은, 무엇보다 그것이 어떤 수사적 멈춤의 기회를 주기 때문이었으니, 그 멈춤은 듣는 이가 그 절묘한 기지를 받아들이거나 따끔한 경구의 독을 받게 하는 데 무엇보다 중요하다고 여겨졌다."], ["Such a stop occurred in the answer just given, at the end of the allusion to the Egyptian and Idumaean.", "방금 한 대답에서도 그런 멈춤이 있었으니, 이집트인과 이두매인을 빗댄 말 끝에서였다."], ["The color in the Jewish lad’s cheeks deepened, and he may not have heard the rest of the speech, for he remained silent, looking absently into the depths of the pool.", "유대 소년의 두 뺨에 핏기가 짙어졌고, 그는 나머지 말을 듣지 못했을지도 모른다. 그는 잠잠한 채, 못의 깊은 곳을 멍하니 들여다보고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Our farewell took place in this garden.", "\"우리가 작별한 곳이 바로 이 정원이었지."], ["‘The peace of the Lord go with you!’—your last words.", "'주님의 평화가 너와 함께하기를!'—그게 네 마지막 말이었어."], ["‘The gods keep you!’ I said.", "'신들께서 너를 지켜 주시기를!'"], ["Do you remember? How many years have passed since then?”", "하고 내가 말했고. 기억하나? 그 뒤로 몇 해가 흘렀지?\""]], [["“Five,” answered the Jew, gazing into the water.", "\"다섯 해.\" 유대 소년이 물을 들여다보며 대답했다."]], [["“Well, you have reason to be thankful to—whom shall I say?", "\"그래, 너는 감사할 만하지—누구한테라고 해야 하나? 신들한테?"], ["The gods? No matter.", "아무래도 좋아."], ["You have grown handsome; the Greeks would call you beautiful—happy achievement of the years!", "너는 잘생겨졌어. 그리스인이라면 아름답다 했을 거다—세월이 빚어낸 행복한 성취지!"], ["If Jupiter would stay content with one Ganymede, what a cup-bearer you would make for the emperor!", "유피테르가 가니메데 하나로 만족한다면, 너야말로 황제의 술시중을 들기에 얼마나 좋을까!"], ["Tell me, my Judah, how the coming of the procurator is of such interest to you.”", "말해 보게, 나의 유다, 어째서 총독의 도착이 너한테 그토록 큰 관심사인지를.\""]], [["Judah bent his large eyes upon the questioner; the gaze was grave and thoughtful, and caught the Roman’s, and held it while he replied, “Yes, five years.", "유다는 그 큰 눈을 묻는 이에게 돌렸다. 그 눈길은 진지하고 사려 깊었으며, 로마인의 눈길을 붙들어 대답하는 동안 놓지 않았다. \"그래, 다섯 해지."], ["I remember the parting; you went to Rome; I saw you start, and cried, for I love you.", "나는 그 작별을 기억해. 너는 로마로 갔고, 나는 네가 떠나는 걸 지켜보며 울었지, 너를 사랑하니까."], ["The years are gone, and you have come back to me accomplished and princely—I do not jest; and yet—yet—I do wish you were the Messala you went away.”", "세월이 흘러, 너는 빼어나고 기품 있게 다듬어져 내게 돌아왔어—농담이 아니야. 그런데도—그런데도—나는 네가 떠나갈 때의 그 메살라였으면 하고 바라게 되는구나.\""]], [["The fine nostril of the satirist stirred, and he put on a longer drawl as he said, “No, no; not a Ganymede—an oracle, my Judah.", "풍자가의 고운 콧방울이 움찔하더니, 그는 한층 길게 늘어진 말투로 말했다. \"아니, 아니지. 가니메데가 아니라—신탁이지, 나의 유다."], ["A few lessons from my teacher of rhetoric hard by the Forum—I will give you a letter to him when you become wise enough to accept a suggestion which I am reminded to make you—a little practise of the art of mystery, and Delphi will receive you as Apollo himself.", "포룸 바로 곁에 있는 내 수사학 스승한테서 몇 차례 배우기만 하면—네가 내 권유를 받아들일 만큼 슬기로워지면 그분께 보내는 편지를 한 통 써 주마, 마침 그런 권유가 떠올랐으니—신비술을 조금 익히기만 하면, 델포이가 너를 아폴론 그 자신으로 맞이할 거다."], ["At the sound of your solemn voice, the Pythia will come down to you with her crown. Seriously, O my friend, in what am I not the Messala I went away?", "네 엄숙한 목소리를 듣고, 무녀가 관을 쓰고 너한테 내려올 테니까. 진지하게 묻겠는데, 오, 나의 벗이여, 내가 떠나갈 때의 메살라가 아닌 점이 무엇이냐?"], ["I once heard the greatest logician in the world.", "나는 언젠가 세상에서 가장 위대한 논리학자의 강의를 들었어."], ["His subject was Disputation.", "그 주제는 논쟁이었지."], ["One saying I remember—‘Understand your antagonist before you answer him.’ Let me understand you.”", "한마디가 기억나는군—'대답하기 전에 네 상대를 이해하라.' 너를 이해하게 해 다오.\""]], [["The lad reddened under the cynical look to which he was subjected; yet he replied, firmly, “You have availed yourself, I see, of your opportunities; from your teachers you have brought away much knowledge and many graces.", "소년은 그에게 쏟아지는 냉소 어린 눈길에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그래도 그는 단호하게 대답했다. \"보아하니 너는 네 기회를 잘 활용했구나."], ["You talk with the ease of a master, yet your speech carries a sting.", "스승들한테서 많은 지식과 숱한 우아함을 가져왔어."], ["My Messala, when he went away, had no poison in his nature; not for the world would he have hurt the feelings of a friend.”", "너는 대가처럼 거침없이 말하지만, 네 말에는 가시가 박혀 있어. 떠나갈 때의 내 메살라한테는 천성에 독이 없었어. 세상 무엇을 준다 해도 그는 벗의 마음을 다치게 하지 않았을 거다.\""]], [["The Roman smiled as if complimented, and raised his patrician head a toss higher.", "로마인은 칭찬이라도 받은 듯 미소 짓고는, 귀족다운 고개를 한층 더 치켜올렸다."]], [["“O my solemn Judah, we are not at Dodona or Pytho.", "\"오, 나의 엄숙한 유다여, 우리는 도도나에도 피토에도 있지 않아."], ["Drop the oracular, and be plain.", "신탁 같은 말은 그만두고, 알아듣게 말해."], ["Wherein have I hurt you?”", "내가 너를 어디서 다치게 했단 말이냐?\""]], [["The other drew a long breath, and said, pulling at the cord about his waist, “In the five years, I, too, have learned somewhat.", "상대는 길게 숨을 들이쉬고는, 허리에 두른 끈을 잡아당기며 말했다. \"지난 다섯 해 동안, 나도 무언가를 배웠어."], ["Hillel may not be the equal of the logician you heard, and Simeon and Shammai are, no doubt, inferior to your master hard by the Forum.", "힐렐이 네가 들었다는 그 논리학자만 못할지도 모르고, 시므온과 샴마이도 포룸 곁의 네 스승보다 못할 게 분명하지."], ["Their learning goes not out into forbidden paths; those who sit at their feet arise enriched simply with knowledge of God, the law, and Israel; and the effect is love and reverence for everything that pertains to them.", "그분들의 학문은 금지된 길로 나아가지 않아. 그분들 발치에 앉는 자들은 그저 하느님과 율법과 이스라엘에 대한 지식으로 풍성해져 일어서지. 그리고 그 결과는 그것들과 관련된 모든 것에 대한 사랑과 경외야."], ["Attendance at the Great College, and study of what I heard there, have taught me that Judea is not as she used to be.", "대학에 다니며 그곳에서 들은 것을 공부하면서, 나는 유대가 옛날의 유대가 아님을 깨달았어."], ["I know the space that lies between an independent kingdom and the petty province Judea is.", "나는 독립된 왕국과 보잘것없는 속주 사이에 놓인 거리를 알아. 유대가 바로 그 속주지."], ["I were meaner, viler, than a Samaritan not to resent the degradation of my country.", "내 나라가 격하된 것을 분히 여기지 않는다면, 나는 사마리아인보다도 비천하고 야비한 자일 거다. 이스마엘은 합법적인 대제사장이 아니야."], ["Ishmael is not lawfully high-priest, and he cannot be while the noble Hannas lives; yet he is a Levite; one of the devoted who for thousands of years have acceptably served the Lord God of our faith and worship. His—”", "고귀한 한나스가 살아 있는 한 그럴 수 없지. 그래도 그는 레위인이야. 수천 년 동안 우리 신앙과 예배의 주 하느님을 가납받을 만하게 섬겨 온 헌신된 자들 가운데 하나라고. 그의—\""]], [["Messala broke in upon him with a biting laugh.", "메살라가 신랄한 웃음으로 그의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“Oh, I understand you now.", "\"오, 이제 알겠다."], ["Ishmael, you say, is a usurper, yet to believe an Idumaean sooner than Ishmael is to sting like an adder. By the drunken son of Semele, what it is to be a Jew!", "이스마엘은 찬탈자라면서도, 이스마엘보다 이두매인을 먼저 믿으면 독사처럼 쏘아붙이는군. 세멜레의 그 술 취한 아들에 두고, 유대인이라는 게 도대체 뭐란 말이냐!"], ["All men and things, even heaven and earth, change; but a Jew never.", "모든 사람과 사물이, 심지어 하늘과 땅까지 변하건만, 유대인만은 결코 변하지 않아."], ["To him there is no backward, no forward; he is what his ancestor was in the beginning.", "그한테는 뒤도 앞도 없어. 그는 태초에 제 조상이었던 그대로지. 이 모래 위에 너한테 원을 하나 그려 주마—자, 여기!"], ["In this sand I draw you a circle—there! Now tell me what more a Jew’s life is?", "이제 말해 봐, 유대인의 삶이 이것보다 더한 게 뭐가 있는지."], ["Round and round, Abraham here, Isaac and Jacob yonder, God in the middle.", "돌고 또 돌고, 여기엔 아브라함, 저기엔 이삭과 야곱, 한가운데엔 하느님."], ["And the circle—by the master of all thunders!", "그런데 이 원은—온갖 천둥의 주인에 두고!"], ["the circle is too large.", "이 원은 너무 크군."], ["I draw it again—”", "다시 그려 주지—\""], ["He stopped, put his thumb upon the ground, and swept the fingers about it.", "그는 말을 멈추고, 엄지손가락을 땅에 대고는, 그 둘레로 손가락들을 휘둘렀다."], ["“See, the thumb spot is the Temple, the finger-lines Judea.", "\"보게, 엄지 자국은 성전이고, 손가락 선은 유대야."], ["Outside the little space is there nothing of value? The arts!", "이 작은 자리 바깥에는 가치 있는 게 아무것도 없나? 예술!"], ["Herod was a builder; therefore he is accursed.", "헤롯은 건축가였지. 그래서 저주받았어."], ["Painting, sculpture!", "회화, 조각!"], ["to look upon them is sin.", "그것들을 보는 건 죄지."], ["Poetry you make fast to your altars.", "시는 너희 제단에 매여 있고."], ["Except in the synagogue, who of you attempts eloquence?", "회당을 빼면, 너희 가운데 누가 웅변을 시도하나?"], ["In war all you conquer in the six days you lose on the seventh.", "전쟁에서는 엿새 동안 정복한 것을 이렛날에 죄다 잃어버리지."], ["Such your life and limit; who shall say no if I laugh at you?", "그게 너희 삶이고 한계야. 내가 너희를 비웃는다 한들 누가 안 된다 하겠나?"], ["Satisfied with the worship of such a people, what is your God to our Roman Jove, who lends us his eagles that we may compass the universe with our arms?", "그런 백성의 예배에 만족하다니, 너희 하느님이 우리 로마의 유피테르께 견주면 뭐란 말이냐? 그분은 우리한테 독수리 깃발을 빌려주시어, 우리가 우주를 우리 팔에 거머쥐게 하시는데."], ["Hillel, Simeon, Shammai, Abtalion—what are they to the masters who teach that everything is worth knowing that can be known?”", "힐렐, 시므온, 샴마이, 압탈리온—알 수 있는 모든 것은 알 가치가 있다고 가르치는 대가들에 견주면, 그들이 다 무엇이냐?\""]], [["The Jew arose, his face much flushed.", "유대인은 일어섰고, 그의 얼굴은 몹시 상기되었다."]], [["“No, no; keep your place, my Judah, keep your place,”", "\"아니, 아니, 자리에 그대로 있게, 나의 유다, 그대로 있어.\""], ["Messala cried, extending his hand.", "메살라가 손을 내밀며 외쳤다."]], [["“You mock me.”", "\"네가 나를 조롱하는구나.\""]], [["“Listen a little further.", "\"조금만 더 들어 봐."], ["Directly”—the Roman smiled derisively—“directly Jupiter and his whole family, Greek and Latin, will come to me, as is their habit, and make an end of serious speech.", "곧\"—로마인은 비웃듯 미소 지었다—\"곧 유피테르와 그 온 가족이, 그리스 신이든 라틴 신이든, 늘 그러듯 내게로 와서, 진지한 이야기를 끝장낼 테니까."], ["I am mindful of your goodness in walking from the old house of your fathers to welcome me back and renew the love of our childhood—if we can.", "너희 조상의 옛집에서 여기까지 걸어와 내 귀향을 반기고 우리 어린 시절의 정을 새롭게 하려 한—할 수 있다면 말이지—네 호의를 나는 마음에 새기고 있어."], ["‘Go,’ said my teacher, in his last lecture—‘Go, and, to make your lives great, remember Mars reigns and Eros has found his eyes.’ He meant love is nothing, war everything.", "'가거라.' 내 스승이 마지막 강의에서 그러셨지—'가서, 네 삶을 위대하게 만들려거든, 마르스가 다스리고 에로스가 제 눈을 되찾았음을 기억하라.' 그분이 뜻한 바는, 사랑은 아무것도 아니고 전쟁이 전부라는 거였어."], ["It is so in Rome.", "로마에서는 그래."], ["Marriage is the first step to divorce.", "결혼은 이혼으로 가는 첫걸음이지."], ["Virtue is a tradesman’s jewel.", "정절은 장사꾼의 보석이고."], ["Cleopatra, dying, bequeathed her arts, and is avenged; she has a successor in every Roman’s house.", "죽어 가던 클레오파트라는 제 기예를 물려주고 원수를 갚았어. 로마인의 집집마다 그 후계자가 있으니까. 세상이 같은 길을 가고 있어."], ["The world is going the same way; so, as to our future, down Eros, up Mars!", "그러니 우리 앞날을 두고는, 에로스는 물리치고, 마르스를 받들자! 나는 군인이 될 거다."], ["I am to be a soldier; and you, O my Judah, I pity you; what can you be?”", "그리고 너는, 오, 나의 유다, 나는 네가 가엾어. 너는 무엇이 될 수 있겠나?\""]], [["The Jew moved nearer the pool; Messala’s drawl deepened.", "유대인은 못 가까이로 다가섰다. 메살라의 말투는 더욱 늘어졌다."]], [["“Yes, I pity you, my fine Judah.", "\"그래, 나는 네가 가엾어, 나의 잘난 유다."], ["From the college to the synagogue; then to the Temple; then—oh, a crowning glory!—the seat in the Sanhedrim.", "대학에서 회당으로, 그다음엔 성전으로, 그다음엔—오, 더없는 영광이지!—산헤드린의 한자리로. 기회라곤 없는 삶이야."], ["A life without opportunities; the gods help you! But I—”", "신들께서 너를 도우시기를! 하지만 나는—\""]], [["Judah looked at him in time to see the flush of pride that kindled in his haughty face as he went on.", "유다가 그를 바라본 것은 마침, 그가 말을 이어 가는 동안 그 거만한 얼굴에 타오르는 자긍심의 홍조를 볼 수 있을 때였다."]], [["“But I—ah, the world is not all conquered.", "\"하지만 나는—아, 세상은 다 정복된 게 아니야."], ["The sea has islands unseen.", "바다에는 아직 보지 못한 섬들이 있어."], ["In the north there are nations yet unvisited.", "북쪽에는 아직 가 보지 못한 민족들이 있고."], ["The glory of completing Alexander’s march to the Far East remains to some one.", "알렉산드로스의 동방 원정을 완성하는 영광은 누군가의 몫으로 남아 있지."], ["See what possibilities lie before a Roman.”", "보게, 한 로마인 앞에 어떤 가능성들이 놓여 있는지를.\""]], [["Next instant he resumed his drawl.", "다음 순간 그는 다시 나른한 말투로 돌아갔다."]], [["“A campaign into Africa; another after the Scythian; then—a legion!", "\"아프리카 원정, 그다음엔 스키타이 원정, 그다음엔—군단 지휘! 대개의 경력은 거기서 끝나지."], ["Most careers end there; but not mine. I—by Jupiter!", "하지만 내 것은 아니야. 나는—유피테르에 두고!"], ["what a conception!—I will give up my legion for a prefecture.", "이 얼마나 멋진 구상인가!—나는 내 군단을 내놓고 태수직을 얻을 거다."], ["Think of life in Rome with money—money, wine, women, games—poets at the banquet, intrigues in the court, dice all the year round.", "돈을 가지고 로마에서 사는 삶을 생각해 봐—돈, 포도주, 여인, 경기—연회의 시인들, 궁정의 음모, 일 년 내내 굴리는 주사위."], ["Such a rounding of life may be—a fat prefecture, and it is mine.", "그렇게 삶을 마무리하는 게 가능할지도 몰라—기름진 태수직, 그게 내 것이 되는 거지."], ["O my Judah, here is Syria!", "오, 나의 유다, 여기에 시리아가 있어!"], ["Judea is rich; Antioch a capital for the gods.", "유대는 부유하고, 안티오크는 신들에게 어울리는 수도야."], ["I will succeed Cyrenius, and you—shall share my fortune.”", "나는 키레니우스의 뒤를 이을 거고, 너는—내 재산을 함께 나누게 될 거다.\""]], [["The sophists and rhetoricians who thronged the public resorts of Rome, almost monopolizing the business of teaching her patrician youth, might have approved these sayings of Messala, for they were all in the popular vein; to the young Jew, however, they were new, and unlike the solemn style of discourse and conversation to which he was accustomed.", "로마의 공공장소마다 떼 지어 몰려, 귀족 자제 가르치는 일을 거의 독차지하다시피 한 궤변가와 수사학자들이라면 메살라의 이런 말을 옳게 여겼을 것이다. 그것들이 모두 세태에 영합하는 결이었으니 말이다. 그러나 젊은 유대인에게는 그것들이 낯설었고, 그가 익숙해 온 엄숙한 담론과 대화의 양식과는 사뭇 달랐다."], ["He belonged, moreover, to a race whose laws, modes, and habits of thought forbade satire and humor; very naturally, therefore, he listened to his friend with varying feelings; one moment indignant, then uncertain how to take him.", "게다가 그는 율법과 사고방식과 습관이 풍자와 익살을 금하는 한 민족에 속해 있었다. 따라서 그가 갖가지 심정으로 벗의 말에 귀 기울인 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었으니, 한순간은 분개하다가, 다음 순간은 그를 어찌 받아들여야 할지 갈피를 못 잡았다."], ["The superior airs assumed had been offensive to him in the beginning; soon they became irritating, and at last an acute smart.", "그가 꾸며 보인 우월한 태도는 처음부터 거슬렸으나, 곧 짜증스러워졌고, 마침내 날카로운 아픔이 되었다."], ["Anger lies close by this point in all of us; and that the satirist evoked in another way.", "우리 누구에게나 이 지점 바로 곁에 분노가 도사리고 있으니, 풍자가는 또 다른 길로 그 분노를 불러일으켰다."], ["To the Jew of the Herodian period patriotism was a savage passion scarcely hidden under his common humor, and so related to his history, religion, and God that it responded instantly to derision of them.", "헤롯 시대의 유대인에게 애국심은 평소의 익살 밑에 좀처럼 감추어지지 않는 사나운 정념이었으며, 그의 역사와 종교와 하느님과 너무도 깊이 얽혀 있어, 그것들에 대한 조롱에 즉각 반응했다."], ["Wherefore it is not speaking too strongly to say that Messala’s progress down to the last pause was exquisite torture to his hearer; at that point the latter said, with a forced smile,", "그러므로 메살라가 마지막 멈춤에 이르기까지 늘어놓은 말이 듣는 이에게 더없는 고문이었다고 해도, 지나친 말은 아니다. 그 지점에서 후자가 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["“There are a few, I have heard, who can afford to make a jest of their future; you convince me, O my Messala, that I am not one of them.”", "\"내가 듣기로 제 앞날을 두고 농담을 할 형편이 되는 자들이 더러 있다더군. 너는 내게 확신시켜 주는구나, 오, 나의 메살라, 내가 그런 자들 가운데 하나가 아님을.\""]], [["The Roman studied him; then replied, “Why not the truth in a jest as well as a parable?", "로마인은 그를 찬찬히 살피더니 대답했다. \"비유 못지않게 농담 속에도 진실이 담길 수 있지 않은가?"], ["The great Fulvia went fishing the other day; she caught more than all the company besides.", "위대한 풀비아가 며칠 전에 낚시를 갔는데, 다른 일행을 다 합친 것보다 더 많이 잡았다네."], ["They said it was because the barb of her hook was covered with gold.”", "사람들이 말하길, 그건 그 여자 낚싯바늘 미늘에 금이 입혀져 있었기 때문이라더군.\""]], [["“Then you were not merely jesting?”", "\"그럼 너는 그저 농담만 한 게 아니었나?\""]], [["“My Judah, I see I did not offer you enough,” the Roman answered, quickly, his eyes sparkling.", "\"나의 유다, 보아하니 내가 너한테 충분히 내놓지 않았구나.\" 로마인이 눈을 반짝이며 재빨리 대답했다."], ["“When I am prefect, with Judea to enrich me, I—will make you high-priest.”", "\"내가 태수가 되어 유대로 부를 일구게 되면, 나는—너를 대제사장으로 만들어 주마.\""]], [["The Jew turned off angrily.", "유대인은 화가 나서 돌아섰다."]], [["“Do not leave me,”", "\"날 떠나지 말게.\""], ["said Messala.", "메살라가 말했다."]], [["The other stopped irresolute.", "상대는 망설이며 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Gods, Judah, how hot the sun shines!”", "\"신들이여, 유다, 햇볕이 어찌 이리 뜨거운가!\""], ["cried the patrician, observing his perplexity.", "그의 당혹스러움을 알아챈 귀족이 외쳤다."], ["“Let us seek a shade.”", "\"그늘을 찾아보세.\""]], [["Judah answered, coldly,", "유다가 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“We had better part.", "\"우리는 갈라서는 게 낫겠어."], ["I wish I had not come. I sought a friend and find a—”", "내가 오지 말았어야 했어. 나는 벗을 찾아왔다가, 한 사람의—을 보게 되었구나.\""]], [["“Roman,” said Messala, quickly.", "\"로마인을.\" 메살라가 재빨리 말했다."]], [["The hands of the Jew clenched, but controlling himself again, he started off.", "유대인의 두 손이 움켜쥐어졌으나, 그는 다시금 자신을 다스리며 발걸음을 떼었다."], ["Messala arose, and, taking the mantle from the bench, flung it over his shoulder, and followed after; when he gained his side, he put his hand upon his shoulder and walked with him.", "메살라가 일어서, 의자에서 망토를 집어 어깨에 걸치고 뒤따라갔다. 그의 곁에 이르자, 그는 손을 그의 어깨에 얹고 나란히 걸었다."]], [["“This is the way—my hand thus—we used to walk when we were children.", "\"이렇게—내 손을 이렇게 얹고—우리가 어렸을 때 함께 걷곤 했지."], ["Let us keep it as far as the gate.”", "문까지는 이대로 가세.\""]], [["Apparently Messala was trying to be serious and kind, though he could not rid his countenance of the habitual satirical expression.", "겉보기에 메살라는 진지하고 다정해지려 애쓰는 듯했으나, 그 얼굴에서 몸에 밴 풍자의 표정을 떨쳐 내지는 못했다."], ["Judah permitted the familiarity.", "유다는 그 허물없음을 받아 주었다."]], [["“You are a boy; I am a man; let me talk like one.”", "\"너는 소년이고, 나는 어른이지. 어른답게 말하게 해 다오.\""]], [["The complacency of the Roman was superb. Mentor lecturing the young Telemachus could not have been more at ease.", "로마인의 자족감은 빼어났다. 어린 텔레마코스를 가르치는 멘토르라 해도 그보다 더 느긋할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Do you believe in the Parcae?", "\"너는 파르카이를 믿나?"], ["Ah, I forgot, you are a Sadducee: the Essenes are your sensible people; they believe in the sisters.", "아, 깜박했군, 너는 사두개인이지. 에세네파가 너희 가운데 분별 있는 자들이고, 그들은 그 세 자매를 믿어."], ["So do I.", "나도 믿지."], ["How everlastingly the three are in the way of our doing what we please!", "그 셋이 우리가 하고 싶은 대로 하는 걸 얼마나 끈질기게 가로막는지!"], ["I sit down scheming.", "나는 앉아서 궁리하지."], ["I run paths here and there. Perpol!", "이리저리 길을 내달리고. 페르폴!"], ["Just when I am reaching to take the world in hand, I hear behind me the grinding of scissors.", "내가 막 세상을 손에 거머쥐려고 손을 뻗는 바로 그 순간, 등 뒤에서 가위 가는 소리가 들려."], ["I look, and there she is, the accursed Atropos!", "돌아보면, 거기 그 여자가 있지, 저주받을 아트로포스가!"], ["But, my Judah, why did you get mad when I spoke of succeeding old Cyrenius?", "그런데, 나의 유다, 내가 늙은 키레니우스의 뒤를 잇겠다고 말했을 때 너는 어째서 화를 냈나?"], ["You thought I meant to enrich myself plundering your Judea.", "너는 내가 네 유대를 약탈해 부자가 되려는 줄 알았겠지. 그렇다 치자."], ["Suppose so; it is what some Roman will do. Why not I?”", "어차피 어떤 로마인은 할 일이야. 그게 왜 내가 아니어야 하나?\""]], [["Judah shortened his step.", "유다가 걸음을 늦추었다."]], [["“There have been strangers in mastery of Judea before the Roman,”", "\"로마인 이전에도 유대를 다스린 이방인들이 있었어.\""], ["he said, with lifted hand.", "그가 손을 들며 말했다."], ["“Where are they, Messala?", "\"그들은 어디 있나, 메살라?"], ["She has outlived them all.", "유대는 그들을 모두 살아남았어."], ["What has been will be again.”", "이미 있었던 일은 다시 있게 될 거다.\""]], [["Messala put on his drawl.", "메살라는 나른한 말투를 띠었다."]], [["“The Parcae have believers outside the Essenes.", "\"파르카이를 믿는 자들이 에세네파 바깥에도 있군."], ["Welcome, Judah, welcome to the faith!”", "환영하네, 유다, 그 신앙에 들어선 것을 환영해!\""]], [["“No, Messala, count me not with them.", "\"아니야, 메살라, 나를 그들과 같이 치지 말게."], ["My faith rests on the rock which was the foundation of the faith of my fathers back further than Abraham; on the covenants of the Lord God of Israel.”", "내 신앙은 아브라함보다도 더 멀리 거슬러 올라가 내 조상들의 신앙의 기초가 된 그 반석 위에 서 있어. 이스라엘의 주 하느님의 언약들 위에 말이야.\""]], [["“Too much passion, my Judah. How my master would have been shocked had I been guilty of so much heat in his presence!", "\"정념이 너무 과하군, 나의 유다. 내가 스승 앞에서 그토록 열을 올렸다면 그분이 얼마나 충격을 받으셨을까!"], ["There were other things I had to tell you, but I fear to now.”", "너한테 해 줄 말이 더 있었는데, 이제는 그러기가 두렵구나.\""]], [["When they had gone a few yards, the Roman spoke again.", "몇 발짝을 더 가서, 로마인이 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“I think you can hear me now, especially as what I have to say concerns yourself.", "\"이제는 내 말을 들을 수 있을 것 같군, 특히 내가 할 말이 너 자신에 관한 것이니 말이야."], ["I would serve you, O handsome as Ganymede; I would serve you with real good-will.", "나는 너를 돕고 싶어, 오, 가니메데처럼 잘생긴 이여. 나는 진심 어린 선의로 너를 돕고 싶어."], ["I love you—all I can. I told you I meant to be a soldier.", "나는 너를 사랑해—내가 할 수 있는 한 다. 내가 군인이 되려 한다고 말했지."], ["Why not you also?", "너라고 안 될 게 뭐냐?"], ["Why not you step out of the narrow circle which, as I have shown, is all of noble life your laws and customs allow?”", "내가 보여 주었듯이, 너희 율법과 관습이 허락하는 고귀한 삶의 전부인 그 좁은 원에서, 너라고 한 발짝 벗어나지 못할 게 뭐냐?\""]], [["Judah made no reply.", "유다는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Who are the wise men of our day?”", "\"우리 시대의 슬기로운 자들이 누구냐?\""], ["Messala continued.", "메살라가 말을 이었다."], ["“Not they who exhaust their years quarrelling about dead things; about Baals, Joves, and Jehovahs; about philosophies and religions.", "\"죽은 것들을 두고—바알이니 유피테르니 여호와니 하는 것들을 두고, 철학이니 종교니 하는 것들을 두고 다투느라 세월을 다 써 버리는 자들이 아니지."], ["Give me one great name, O Judah; I care not where you go to find it—to Rome, Egypt, the East, or here in Jerusalem—Pluto take me if it belong not to a man who wrought his fame out of the material furnished him by the present; holding nothing sacred that did not contribute to the end, scorning nothing that did!", "위대한 이름 하나만 대 봐, 오, 유다. 그걸 찾으러 어디로 가든 나는 상관없어—로마로든, 이집트로든, 동방으로든, 아니면 여기 예루살렘에서든—만일 그 이름이 현재가 그에게 내준 재료로 제 명성을 빚어낸 자의 것이 아니라면 플루토가 나를 잡아가도 좋아. 목적에 보탬이 안 되는 것은 무엇도 신성하게 여기지 않고, 보탬이 되는 것은 무엇도 업신여기지 않은 자 말이야!"], ["How was it with Herod?", "헤롯은 어땠나?"], ["How with the Maccabees?", "마카베오 가문은 어땠고?"], ["How with the first and second Cæsars?", "첫째와 둘째 카이사르는 어땠나?"], ["Imitate them.", "그들을 본받게."], ["Begin now.", "지금 시작하라고."], ["At hand see—Rome, as ready to help you as she was the Idumaean Antipater.”", "가까이 보게—로마가, 이두매인 안티파테르를 도왔듯이, 너를 도울 채비가 되어 있어.\""]], [["The Jewish lad trembled with rage; and, as the garden gate was close by, he quickened his steps, eager to escape.", "유대 소년은 분노로 떨었다. 정원 문이 바로 곁에 있었으므로, 그는 어서 벗어나려는 마음에 걸음을 재촉했다."]], [["“O Rome, Rome!”", "\"오, 로마여, 로마여!\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Be wise,”", "\"슬기로워지게.\""], ["continued Messala.", "메살라가 말을 이었다."], ["“Give up the follies of Moses and the traditions; see the situation as it is.", "\"모세의 어리석은 짓들과 전통들을 버리고, 상황을 있는 그대로 보라고."], ["Dare look the Parcae in the face, and they will tell you, Rome is the world.", "감히 파르카이의 얼굴을 마주 보면, 그들이 너한테 말해 줄 거다, 로마가 세상이라고."], ["Ask them of Judea, and they will answer, She is what Rome wills.”", "그들에게 유대를 물어보면, 그들이 대답할 거야, 유대는 로마가 원하는 대로 되는 것이라고.\""]], [["They were now at the gate.", "그들은 이제 문 앞에 이르렀다."], ["Judah stopped, and took the hand gently from his shoulder, and confronted Messala, tears trembling in his eyes.", "유다는 멈춰 서서, 제 어깨에 얹힌 손을 부드럽게 떼어 내고, 메살라와 마주 섰으니, 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁했다."]], [["“I understand you, because you are a Roman; you cannot understand me—I am an Israelite.", "\"나는 너를 이해해, 네가 로마인이니까. 너는 나를 이해하지 못해—나는 이스라엘인이니까."], ["You have given me suffering to-day by convincing me that we can never be the friends we have been—never!", "너는 오늘 내게 괴로움을 안겨 주었어, 우리가 결코 예전 같은 벗이 될 수 없음을—결코!—내게 확신시킴으로써 말이야."], ["Here we part. The peace of the God of my fathers abide with you!”", "여기서 우리는 갈라서네. 내 조상들의 하느님의 평화가 너와 함께 머물기를!\""]], [["Messala offered him his hand; the Jew walked on through the gateway.", "메살라가 그에게 손을 내밀었으나, 유대인은 문간을 지나 걸어가 버렸다."], ["When he was gone, the Roman was silent awhile; then he, too, passed through, saying to himself, with a toss of the head,", "그가 가 버리자, 로마인은 한동안 잠잠히 있었다. 그러더니 그 또한 고개를 한 번 젖히며, 혼잣말로 중얼거리며 문간을 지났다."]], [["“Be it so. Eros is dead, Mars reigns!”", "\"그렇다면 그렇게 되라지. 에로스는 죽었고, 마르스가 다스린다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["From the entrance to the Holy City, equivalent to what is now called St. Stephen’s Gate, a street extended westwardly, on a line parallel with the northern front of the Tower of Antonia, though a square from that famous castle.", "성스러운 도시로 들어가는 입구, 곧 오늘날 스데반 문이라 불리는 곳에서, 거리 하나가 서쪽으로 뻗어 있었으니, 안토니아 탑의 북쪽 정면과 나란한 선을 그으면서도 그 유명한 성채와는 한 블록 떨어져 있었다."], ["Keeping the course as far as the Tyropoeon Valley, which it followed a little way south, it turned and again ran west until a short distance beyond what tradition tells us was the Judgment Gate, from whence it broke abruptly south.", "이 길은 티로포에온 골짜기에 이를 때까지 그 방향을 지키다가, 골짜기를 따라 잠시 남쪽으로 내려간 뒤, 다시 방향을 틀어 서쪽으로 달리다가, 전승에 따르면 재판의 문이라 일컬어지는 곳을 조금 지난 지점에서 돌연 남쪽으로 꺾였다."], ["The traveller or the student familiar with the sacred locality will recognize the thoroughfare described as part of the Via Dolorosa—with Christians of more interest, though of a melancholy kind, than any street in the world.", "이 성스러운 장소에 익숙한 나그네나 학자라면, 지금 묘사한 큰길이 비아 돌로로사의 한 부분임을 알아볼 것이다. 그것은 세상의 그 어떤 거리보다도 그리스도교인들에게 더 깊은 관심을 불러일으키는, 그러나 애잔한 종류의 관심을 자아내는 길이다."], ["As the purpose in view does not at present require dealing with the whole street, it will be sufficient to point out a house standing in the angle last mentioned as marking the change of direction south, and which, as an important centre of interest, needs somewhat particular description.", "지금의 목적이 이 거리 전체를 다룰 것을 요구하지는 않으므로, 마지막으로 언급한 모퉁이, 곧 길이 남쪽으로 방향을 바꾸는 그 자리에 서 있는 한 집을 가리키는 것으로 충분할 것이다. 그 집은 중요한 관심의 중심으로서, 다소 세밀한 묘사가 필요하다."]], [["The building fronted north and west, probably four hundred feet each way, and, like most pretentious Eastern structures, was two stories in height, and perfectly quadrangular.", "그 건물은 북쪽과 서쪽을 향해 서 있었는데, 아마 각 면이 120미터쯤 되었을 것이며, 화려함을 뽐내는 동방의 건축물 대부분이 그러하듯 이층 높이에 완벽한 사각형을 이루고 있었다."], ["The street on the west side was about twelve feet wide, that on the north not more than ten; so that one walking close to the walls, and looking up at them, would have been struck by the rude, unfinished, uninviting, but strong and imposing, appearance they presented; for they were of stone laid in large blocks, undressed—on the outer side, in fact, just as they were taken from the quarry.", "서쪽 거리는 폭이 3.5미터쯤 되었고, 북쪽 거리는 3미터를 넘지 않았다. 그래서 누군가 그 벽에 바싹 붙어 걸으며 올려다보았다면, 거칠고 미완성이며 정나미 떨어지는, 그러면서도 강건하고 위압적인 그 외관에 깊은 인상을 받았을 것이다. 벽은 큰 덩어리로 쌓아 올린 돌로 되어 있었으니, 다듬지 않은 채로—사실 바깥쪽은 채석장에서 막 떼어 낸 그대로였다."], ["A critic of this age would have pronounced the house fortelesque in style, except for the windows, with which it was unusually garnished, and the ornate finish of the doorways or gates.", "이 시대의 비평가라면 그 집을 요새풍이라 단언했을 것이다. 다만 유난히 많이 장식된 창문들과, 출입문 곧 대문의 화려한 마무리만은 예외였다."], ["The western windows were four in number, the northern only two, all set on the line of the second story in such manner as to overhang the thoroughfares below.", "서쪽 창문은 넷이었고 북쪽 창문은 둘뿐이었는데, 모두 이층의 선을 따라 자리 잡아 아래의 큰길 위로 내밀어진 모양새였다."], ["The gates were the only breaks of wall externally visible in the first story; and, besides being so thickly riven with iron bolts as to suggest resistance to battering-rams, they were protected by cornices of marble, handsomely executed, and of such bold projection as to assure visitors well informed of the people that the rich man who resided there was a Sadducee in politics and creed.", "대문은 바깥에서 보이는 일층 벽의 유일한 틈이었다. 게다가 그것은 공성추도 견딜 만큼 두꺼운 쇠빗장으로 촘촘히 박혀 있었을 뿐 아니라, 솜씨 좋게 다듬은 대리석 처마 장식으로 보호되었으니, 그 돌출이 어찌나 대담했던지, 그곳 사람들의 사정에 밝은 방문객이라면 거기 사는 부자가 정치와 신조에서 사두개파임을 능히 짐작할 만했다."]], [["Not long after the young Jew parted from the Roman at the palace up on the Market-place, he stopped before the western gate of the house described, and knocked.", "젊은 유대인이 시장 위편의 궁전에서 로마인과 헤어진 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 그는 앞서 묘사한 집의 서쪽 대문 앞에 멈춰 서서 문을 두드렸다."], ["The wicket (a door hung in one of the valves of the gate) was opened to admit him.", "쪽문이—대문의 한쪽 문짝에 달린 작은 문이—열려 그를 들였다."], ["He stepped in hastily, and failed to acknowledge the low salaam of the porter.", "그는 황급히 안으로 들어서며, 문지기의 깊은 절에 답례도 하지 않았다."]], [["To get an idea of the interior arrangement of the structure, as well as to see what more befell the youth, we will follow him.", "이 건물의 내부 구조에 대한 짐작을 얻기 위해서도, 또 그 젊은이에게 무슨 일이 더 일어나는지 보기 위해서도, 우리는 그를 따라가 보기로 한다."]], [["The passage into which he was admitted appeared not unlike a narrow tunnel with panelled walls and pitted ceiling.", "그가 들어선 통로는 벽에 널을 댄, 천장이 움푹움푹 팬 좁은 굴과 그리 다르지 않아 보였다."], ["There were benches of stone on both sides, stained and polished by long use.", "양쪽에는 돌 벤치가 놓여 있었는데, 오랜 사용으로 얼룩지고 반들반들했다."], ["Twelve or fifteen steps carried him into a court-yard, oblong north and south, and in every quarter, except the east, bounded by what seemed the fronts of two-story houses; of which the lower floor was divided into lewens, while the upper was terraced and defended by strong balustrading.", "열두어 단의 계단을 지나자 그는 안마당에 이르렀다. 그 마당은 남북으로 긴 직사각형이었고, 동쪽을 제외한 사면이 이층집의 정면처럼 보이는 것들로 둘러싸여 있었다. 그 아래층은 르웬으로 나뉘어 있었고, 위층은 테라스로 되어 튼튼한 난간으로 보호되었다."], ["The servants coming and going along the terraces; the noise of millstones grinding; the garments fluttering from ropes stretched from point to point; the chickens and pigeons in full enjoyment of the place; the goats, cows, donkeys, and horses stabled in the lewens; a massive trough of water, apparently for the common use, declared this court appurtenant to the domestic management of the owner.", "테라스를 오가는 하인들, 맷돌 가는 소리, 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝까지 매단 줄에 펄럭이는 빨래, 그곳을 마음껏 누리는 닭과 비둘기, 르웬에 마구간을 들인 염소와 소와 나귀와 말, 그리고 모두가 함께 쓰는 듯한 큼직한 물구유—이 모든 것이 이 마당이 주인의 살림살이에 딸린 곳임을 일러 주었다."], ["Eastwardly there was a division wall broken by another passage-way in all respects like the first one.", "동쪽에는 칸막이 벽이 있었는데, 처음 것과 모든 면에서 똑같은 또 하나의 통로가 그 벽을 가르고 있었다."]], [["Clearing the second passage, the young man entered a second court, spacious, square, and set with shrubbery and vines, kept fresh and beautiful by water from a basin erected near a porch on the north side.", "두 번째 통로를 지나, 젊은이는 두 번째 마당으로 들어섰다. 넓고 네모반듯한 그곳에는 관목과 덩굴이 심겨 있었으며, 북쪽 현관 가까이 만들어진 수반에서 흘러나오는 물 덕분에 싱그럽고 아름답게 가꾸어져 있었다."], ["The lewens here were high, airy, and shaded by curtains striped alternate white and red.", "이곳의 르웬은 높고 바람이 잘 통했으며, 흰빛과 붉은빛이 번갈아 줄지어진 휘장으로 그늘져 있었다."], ["The arches of the lewens rested on clustered columns.", "르웬의 아치는 한데 묶인 기둥들 위에 얹혀 있었다."], ["A flight of steps on the south ascended to the terraces of the upper story, over which great awnings were stretched as a defence against the sun.", "남쪽의 계단은 위층 테라스로 이어졌는데, 그 위에는 햇볕을 막기 위한 커다란 차일이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["Another stairway reached from the terraces to the roof, the edge of which, all around the square, was defined by a sculptured cornice, and a parapet of burned-clay tiling, sexangular and bright red.", "또 다른 계단은 테라스에서 지붕까지 닿아 있었으니, 그 지붕의 가장자리는 네모난 마당을 빙 둘러 가며 조각된 처마 장식과, 육각형의 새빨간 구운 점토 기와로 된 난간으로 또렷이 구분되어 있었다."], ["In this quarter, moreover, there was everywhere observable a scrupulous neatness, which, allowing no dust in the angles, not even a yellow leaf upon a shrub, contributed quite as much as anything else to the delightful general effect; insomuch that a visitor, breathing the sweet air, knew, in advance of introduction, the refinement of the family he was about calling upon.", "더욱이 이곳에는 어디서나 빈틈없는 정갈함이 눈에 띄었으니, 구석마다 먼지 한 점, 관목에 누런 잎새 하나 허락하지 않는 그 정갈함은 다른 무엇 못지않게 전체의 흐뭇한 인상에 보탬이 되었다. 그리하여 방문객은 그 향긋한 공기를 들이마시는 것만으로도, 소개를 받기에 앞서 자신이 찾아온 집안의 품격을 미리 알 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["A few steps within the second court, the lad turned to the right, and, choosing a walk through the shrubbery, part of which was in flower, passed to the stairway, and ascended to the terrace—a broad pavement of white and brown flags closely laid, and much worn.", "두 번째 마당으로 몇 걸음 들어선 소년은 오른쪽으로 돌아, 일부는 꽃이 핀 관목 사이의 길을 골라 걸어 계단으로 다가가, 테라스로 올라섰다. 그곳은 희고 갈색인 판석을 촘촘히 깔아 만든 넓은 포석으로, 많이 닳아 있었다."], ["Making way under the awning to a doorway on the north side, he entered an apartment which the dropping of the screen behind him returned to darkness.", "차일 아래로 길을 잡아 북쪽의 한 출입구에 이른 그는, 한 방으로 들어섰는데, 등 뒤로 가리개가 떨어지자 방은 다시 어둠에 잠겼다."], ["Nevertheless, he proceeded, moving over a tiled floor to a divan, upon which he flung himself, face downwards, and lay at rest, his forehead upon his crossed arms.", "그런데도 그는 멈추지 않고, 타일을 깐 바닥을 가로질러 긴 의자로 가더니, 얼굴을 아래로 한 채 몸을 던지고는, 팔짱 위에 이마를 얹고 가만히 누웠다."]], [["About nightfall a woman came to the door and called; he answered, and she went in.", "해 질 무렵 한 여자가 문으로 와서 그를 불렀다. 그가 대답하자 여자가 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["“Supper is over, and it is night.", "\"저녁 식사도 끝나고 밤이 되었습니다."], ["Is not my son hungry?”", "도련님은 시장하지 않으십니까?\""], ["she asked.", "여자가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“Are you sick?”", "\"어디 편찮으십니까?\""]], [["“I am sleepy.”", "\"졸리는구나.\""]], [["“Your mother has asked for you.”", "\"마님께서 도련님을 찾으셨습니다.\""]], [["“Where is she?”", "\"어디 계시는데?\""]], [["“In the summer-house on the roof.”", "\"지붕 위 정자에 계십니다.\""]], [["He stirred himself, and sat up.", "그가 몸을 추스르며 일어나 앉았다."]], [["“Very well.", "\"알았다."], ["Bring me something to eat.”", "먹을 것을 좀 가져다오.\""]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무엇을 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["“What you please, Amrah.", "\"네 마음대로, 암라."], ["I am not sick, but indifferent. Life does not seem as pleasant as it did this morning.", "아픈 건 아니고 그저 만사가 시들할 뿐이다. 삶이 오늘 아침만큼 즐겁게 느껴지지 않는구나."], ["A new ailment, O my Amrah; and you who know me so well, who never failed me, may think of the things now that answer for food and medicine.", "새로운 병이로구나, 오 나의 암라야. 나를 그토록 잘 알고, 한 번도 나를 저버린 적 없는 너라면, 이제 음식이자 약이 될 만한 것을 떠올릴 수 있겠지."], ["Bring me what you choose.”", "네가 고르는 대로 가져다오.\""]], [["Amrah’s questions, and the voice in which she put them—low, sympathetic, and solicitous—were significant of an endeared relation between the two.", "암라가 던지는 물음들과, 그것을 묻는 목소리는—낮고, 다정하고, 근심 어린 그 목소리는—둘 사이의 애틋한 정을 일러 주는 것이었다."], ["She laid her hand upon his forehead; then, as satisfied, went out, saying, “I will see.”", "여자는 그의 이마에 손을 얹어 보았다. 그러고는 안심한 듯 밖으로 나가며 말했다. \"찾아보겠습니다.\""]], [["After a while she returned, bearing on a wooden platter a bowl of milk, some thin cakes of white bread broken, a delicate paste of brayed wheat, a bird broiled, and honey and salt.", "잠시 후 여자가 돌아왔는데, 나무 쟁반에 우유 한 그릇, 잘게 뜯은 흰 빵 몇 조각, 곱게 빻은 밀로 만든 부드러운 반죽, 구운 새 한 마리, 그리고 꿀과 소금을 받쳐 들고 왔다."], ["On one end of the platter there was a silver goblet full of wine, on the other a brazen hand-lamp lighted.", "쟁반 한쪽 끝에는 포도주가 가득 든 은잔이, 다른 한쪽 끝에는 불을 밝힌 놋쇠 손등잔이 놓여 있었다."]], [["The room was then revealed: its walls smoothly plastered; the ceiling broken by great oaken rafters, brown with rain stains and time; the floor of small diamond-shaped white and blue tiles, very firm and enduring; a few stools with legs carved in imitation of the legs of lions; a divan raised a little above the floor, trimmed with blue cloth, and partially covered by an immense striped woollen blanket or shawl—in brief, a Hebrew bedroom.", "그러자 방의 모습이 드러났다. 매끄럽게 회칠한 벽, 빗물 자국과 세월에 갈색이 된 굵은 떡갈나무 서까래들로 끊긴 천장, 작은 마름모꼴의 희고 푸른 타일을 깐, 아주 단단하고 오래갈 듯한 바닥, 사자 다리를 본떠 깎은 다리가 달린 걸상 몇 개, 바닥에서 조금 올려 만들어 푸른 천으로 가장자리를 두르고 줄무늬가 든 커다란 모직 담요인지 숄로 일부를 덮은 긴 의자—한마디로 히브리식 침실이었다."]], [["The same light also gave the woman to view.", "같은 불빛이 그 여자의 모습도 드러내 주었다."], ["Drawing a stool to the divan, she placed the platter upon it, then knelt close by ready to serve him.", "여자는 걸상 하나를 긴 의자 곁으로 끌어다 그 위에 쟁반을 올려놓더니, 그를 받들 채비를 하고 바로 옆에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["Her face was that of a woman of fifty, dark-skinned, dark-eyed, and at the moment softened by a look of tenderness almost maternal.", "그 얼굴은 쉰쯤 된 여자의 얼굴로, 가무잡잡한 피부에 검은 눈을 지녔으며, 그 순간만큼은 거의 어머니와 같은 다정함이 어려 부드러워져 있었다."], ["A white turban covered her head, leaving the lobes of the ear exposed, and in them the sign that settled her condition—an orifice bored by a thick awl.", "흰 터번이 머리를 덮고 귓불을 드러냈는데, 거기에는 그 신분을 결정짓는 표시가 있었으니—굵은 송곳으로 뚫은 구멍이었다."], ["She was a slave, of Egyptian origin, to whom not even the sacred fiftieth year could have brought freedom; nor would she have accepted it, for the boy she was attending was her life.", "그 여자는 이집트 태생의 노예로, 거룩한 오십 년째 해가 와도 자유를 얻을 수 없는 처지였다. 아니, 설령 자유가 주어진다 해도 받아들이지 않았을 것이다. 그가 보살피는 그 소년이 곧 그 여자의 삶이었기 때문이다."], ["She had nursed him through babyhood, tended him as a child, and could not break the service.", "여자는 그가 갓난아이일 때 젖을 먹였고, 어린 시절 내내 보살폈으니, 그 시중을 끊을 수가 없었다."], ["To her love he could never be a man.", "그 여자의 사랑 속에서 그는 결코 어른이 될 수 없었다."]], [["He spoke but once during the meal.", "그는 식사를 하는 동안 단 한 번 입을 열었다."]], [["“You remember, O my Amrah,”", "\"기억하느냐, 오 나의 암라야,\""], ["he said, “the Messala who used to visit me here days at a time.”", "그가 말했다. \"한때 여기 와서 며칠씩 머물다 가곤 했던 메살라 말이다.\""]], [["“I remember him.”", "\"기억합니다.\""]], [["“He went to Rome some years ago, and is now back.", "\"몇 해 전에 로마로 갔다가 이제 돌아왔다."], ["I called upon him to-day.”", "오늘 그를 찾아갔지.\""]], [["A shudder of disgust seized the lad.", "소년은 진저리치는 듯 부르르 떨었다."]], [["“I knew something had happened,”", "\"무슨 일이 있었구나 싶었습니다.\""], ["she said, deeply interested.", "여자가 깊은 관심을 보이며 말했다."], ["“I never liked the Messala.", "\"저는 한 번도 그 메살라가 좋은 적이 없었습니다."], ["Tell me all.”", "다 말씀해 보십시오.\""]], [["But he fell into musing, and to her repeated inquiries only said, “He is much changed, and I shall have nothing more to do with him.”", "그러나 그는 깊은 생각에 잠겼고, 여자가 거듭 묻자 다만 이렇게만 말했다. \"그는 많이 변했다. 다시는 그와 상관하지 않을 것이다.\""]], [["When Amrah took the platter away, he also went out, and up from the terrace to the roof.", "암라가 쟁반을 치우자, 그 또한 밖으로 나가, 테라스에서 지붕 위로 올라갔다."]], [["The reader is presumed to know somewhat of the uses of the house-top in the East.", "독자는 동방에서 지붕을 어떻게 쓰는지 어느 정도 알고 있으리라 짐작한다."], ["In the matter of customs, climate is a lawgiver everywhere.", "관습에 관한 한, 어디서나 기후가 입법자다."], ["The Syrian summer day drives the seeker of comfort into the darkened lewen; night, however, calls him forth early, and the shadows deepening over the mountain-sides seem veils dimly covering Circean singers; but they are far off, while the roof is close by, and raised above the level of the shimmering plain enough for the visitation of cool airs, and sufficiently above the trees to allure the stars down closer, down at least into brighter shining.", "시리아의 여름 한낮은 안락을 찾는 이를 어둑한 르웬으로 몰아넣지만, 밤은 그를 일찌감치 불러내며, 산비탈 위로 짙어지는 그림자는 키르케 같은 노래꾼들을 어슴푸레 가려 주는 너울처럼 보인다. 그러나 그 노래꾼들은 멀리 있는 반면, 지붕은 가까이 있어, 어른거리는 평원의 수면보다 충분히 높이 솟아 서늘한 바람이 찾아들 만하고, 나무들보다 충분히 높아 별들을 더 가까이, 적어도 더 밝게 빛나도록 끌어내릴 만하다."], ["So the roof became a resort—became playground, sleeping-chamber, boudoir, rendezvous for the family, place of music, dance, conversation, reverie, and prayer.", "그리하여 지붕은 안식처가 되었다—놀이터, 침실, 규방, 가족의 만남터, 음악과 춤과 담소와 사색과 기도의 자리가 되었다."]], [["The motive that prompts the decoration, at whatever cost, of interiors in colder climes suggested to the Oriental the embellishment of his house-top.", "더 추운 지방에서 비용을 아끼지 않고 실내를 꾸미게 만드는 그 동기가, 동방인에게는 지붕을 치장하라고 일렀다."], ["The parapet ordered by Moses became a potter’s triumph; above that, later, arose towers, plain and fantastic; still later, kings and princes crowned their roofs with summer-houses of marble and gold.", "모세가 명한 난간은 옹기장이의 자랑거리가 되었고, 그 위로는 뒷날 평범한 것이며 기이한 것이며 갖가지 망루가 솟아올랐다. 더 뒤에는 왕과 제후가 대리석과 황금으로 지은 정자로 제 지붕을 장식했다."], ["When the Babylonian hung gardens in the air, extravagance could push the idea no further.", "바빌론 사람이 공중에 정원을 매달았을 때, 사치는 그 생각을 더 멀리 밀고 갈 수 없을 만큼 극에 달했다."]], [["The lad whom we are following walked slowly across the house-top to a tower built over the northwest corner of the palace.", "우리가 뒤따르는 소년은 지붕 위를 천천히 가로질러, 궁전의 북서쪽 모퉁이 위에 세워진 망루로 갔다."], ["Had he been a stranger, he might have bestowed a glance upon the structure as he drew nigh it, and seen all the dimness permitted—a darkened mass, low, latticed, pillared, and domed.", "만일 그가 낯선 이였다면, 다가가면서 그 건물에 한 번쯤 눈길을 던지고, 어둠이 허락하는 모든 것을—어둑한 덩어리, 낮고, 격자창을 내고, 기둥을 세우고, 둥근 지붕을 인 그 모습을 보았을 것이다."], ["He entered, passing under a half-raised curtain.", "그는 반쯤 걷어 올린 휘장 아래를 지나 안으로 들어섰다."], ["The interior was all darkness, except that on four sides there were arched openings like doorways, through which the sky, lighted with stars, was visible.", "안은 온통 어둠뿐이었는데, 다만 사면에 출입구처럼 아치를 이룬 트인 곳들이 있어, 별빛 어린 하늘이 그리로 내다보였다."], ["In one of the openings, reclining against a cushion from a divan, he saw the figure of a woman, indistinct even in white floating drapery.", "그 트인 곳 가운데 하나에, 긴 의자의 방석에 기대어 누운 한 여인의 모습이 보였으니, 하얗게 나부끼는 옷자락에 가려 흐릿했다."], ["At the sound of his steps upon the floor, the fan in her hand stopped, glistening where the starlight struck the jewels with which it was sprinkled, and she sat up, and called his name.", "바닥을 밟는 그의 발소리에, 여인의 손에 들린 부채가 멈추었고, 별빛이 거기 박힌 보석에 부딪쳐 반짝였다. 여인은 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 그의 이름을 불렀다."]], [["“Judah, my son!”", "\"유다, 내 아들아!\""]], [["“It is I, mother,”", "\"저예요, 어머니.\""], ["he answered, quickening his approach.", "그가 걸음을 재촉하며 대답했다."]], [["Going to her, he knelt, and she put her arms around him, and with kisses pressed him to her bosom.", "그에게로 가서 그가 무릎을 꿇자, 여인은 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안고, 입맞춤하며 가슴에 끌어안았다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["The mother resumed her easy position against the cushion, while the son took place on the divan, his head in her lap.", "어머니는 다시 방석에 기대어 편안한 자세를 취했고, 아들은 긴 의자에 누워 어머니의 무릎에 머리를 뉘었다."], ["Both of them, looking out of the opening, could see a stretch of lower house-tops in the vicinity, a bank of blue-blackness over in the west which they knew to be mountains, and the sky, its shadowy depths brilliant with stars.", "두 사람은 열린 곳으로 내다보며 가까운 곳에 늘어선 낮은 지붕들과, 서쪽으로 그들이 산이라고 알고 있는 검푸른 띠와, 그 그늘진 깊이마다 별빛으로 찬란한 하늘을 볼 수 있었다."], ["The city was still.", "도시는 고요했다."], ["Only the winds stirred.", "오직 바람만이 일렁였다."]], [["“Amrah tells me something has happened to you,”", "\"암라가 네게 무슨 일이 있었다고 하더구나.\""], ["she said, caressing his cheek.", "어머니는 아들의 뺨을 어루만지며 말했다."], ["“When my Judah was a child, I allowed small things to trouble him, but he is now a man.", "\"내 유다가 어렸을 때는 사소한 일에도 마음 졸이게 두었다만, 이제 너는 어른이 되었지."], ["He must not forget”—her voice became very soft—“that one day he is to be my hero.”", "잊어서는 안 돼.\" 그 목소리가 무척 부드러워졌다. \"언젠가 너는 내 영웅이 되어야 한다는 것을.\""]], [["She spoke in the language almost lost in the land, but which a few—and they were always as rich in blood as in possessions—cherished in its purity, that they might be more certainly distinguished from Gentile peoples—the language in which the loved Rebekah and Rachel sang to Benjamin.", "어머니는 이 땅에서 거의 잊혀 가는 언어로 말했다. 그러나 소수의 사람들은—그들은 언제나 재산만큼이나 혈통에서도 풍요로운 자들이었다—이방 민족과 더 확실히 구별되기 위해 그 언어를 순수하게 간직해 왔으니, 사랑받던 리브가와 라헬이 베냐민에게 노래 불러 주던 바로 그 언어였다."]], [["The words appeared to set him thinking anew; after a while, however, he caught the hand with which she fanned him, and said, “Today, O my mother, I have been made to think of many things that never had place in my mind before.", "그 말이 아들을 다시 생각에 잠기게 한 듯했다. 그러나 잠시 후 그는 자기를 부쳐 주던 어머니의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"어머니, 오늘 저는 지금껏 한 번도 마음에 둔 적 없는 여러 가지를 생각하게 되었습니다."], ["Tell me, first, what am I to be?”", "먼저 말씀해 주세요. 저는 무엇이 되어야 합니까?\""]], [["“Have I not told you?", "\"이미 말하지 않았느냐?"], ["You are to be my hero.”", "너는 내 영웅이 되어야 한다.\""]], [["He could not see her face, yet he knew she was in play.", "그는 어머니의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었으나, 어머니가 농담하고 있음을 알았다."], ["He became more serious.", "그는 더 진지해졌다."]], [["“You are very good, very kind, O my mother. No one will ever love me as you do.”", "\"어머니는 정말 선하고 자애로우십니다. 어머니만큼 저를 사랑해 줄 사람은 없을 거예요.\""]], [["He kissed the hand over and over again.", "그는 그 손에 거듭거듭 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“I think I understand why you would have me put off the question,”", "\"왜 어머니가 그 물음을 미루려 하시는지 알 것 같습니다.\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“Thus far my life has belonged to you.", "\"지금까지 제 삶은 어머니의 것이었지요."], ["How gentle, how sweet your control has been!", "어머니의 보살핌은 얼마나 다정하고 얼마나 달콤했는지요!"], ["I wish it could last forever.", "그것이 영원히 이어졌으면 좋겠습니다."], ["But that may not be.", "그러나 그럴 수는 없겠지요."], ["It is the Lord’s will that I shall one day become owner of myself—a day of separation, and therefore a dreadful day to you.", "언젠가 제가 제 자신의 주인이 되는 것이 주님의 뜻이니까요. 그것은 헤어짐의 날이요, 그래서 어머니에게는 두려운 날이기도 합니다. 우리 용감하고 진지해집시다."], ["Let us be brave and serious.", "저는 어머니의 영웅이 되겠습니다."], ["I will be your hero, but you must put me in the way.", "그러나 어머니가 저를 그 길로 인도해 주셔야 합니다."], ["You know the law—every son of Israel must have some occupation.", "어머니도 율법을 아시지요. 이스라엘의 아들이라면 누구나 어떤 생업을 가져야 한다는 것을요."], ["I am not exempt, and ask now, shall I tend the herds?", "저도 예외가 아니니, 이제 묻겠습니다. 저는 가축을 칠까요?"], ["or till the soil?", "아니면 땅을 일굴까요?"], ["or drive the saw?", "톱질을 할까요?"], ["or be a clerk or lawyer? What shall I be?", "서기나 율법학자가 될까요? 저는 무엇이 되어야 합니까?"], ["Dear, good mother, help me to an answer.”", "자애롭고 선하신 어머니, 제가 답을 찾도록 도와주세요.\""]], [["“Gamaliel has been lecturing today,”", "\"오늘 가말리엘이 강론을 했더구나.\""], ["she said, thoughtfully.", "어머니가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“If so, I did not hear him.”", "\"그랬다면 저는 듣지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Then you have been walking with Simeon, who, they tell me, inherits the genius of his family.”", "\"그렇다면 시므온과 함께 거닐었겠지. 사람들 말로는 그가 가문의 천재성을 물려받았다고 하던데.\""]], [["“No, I have not seen him. I have been up on the Market-place, not to the Temple.", "\"아니요, 그도 만나지 못했습니다. 저는 성전이 아니라 시장에 다녀왔습니다."], ["I visited the young Messala.”", "젊은 메살라를 만났지요.\""]], [["A certain change in his voice attracted the mother’s attention.", "그 목소리에 어떤 변화가 어머니의 주의를 끌었다."], ["A presentiment quickened the beating of her heart; the fan became motionless again.", "어떤 예감이 가슴의 박동을 빠르게 했고, 부채가 다시 멈추었다."]], [["“The Messala!”", "\"메살라라니!\""], ["she said.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["“What could he say to so trouble you?”", "\"그 애가 무슨 말을 했기에 너를 그토록 괴롭게 했단 말이냐?\""]], [["“He is very much changed.”", "\"그는 무척 변했습니다.\""]], [["“You mean he has come back a Roman.” “Yes.”", "\"로마인이 되어 돌아왔다는 말이로구나.\""]], [["“Roman!”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["she continued, half to herself.", "\"로마인이라!\" 어머니는 반쯤 혼잣말로 말을 이었다."], ["“To all the world the word means master.", "\"온 세상에 그 말은 주인을 뜻하지."], ["How long has he been away?”", "떠난 지 얼마나 되었느냐?\""]], [["“Five years.”", "\"오 년입니다.\""]], [["She raised her head, and looked off into the night.", "어머니는 고개를 들어 밤하늘 저편을 바라보았다."]], [["“The airs of the Via Sacra are well enough in the streets of the Egyptian and in Babylon; but in Jerusalem—our Jerusalem—the covenant abides.”", "\"비아 사크라의 풍조는 이집트인의 거리나 바빌론에서는 그럭저럭 통하겠지. 그러나 예루살렘에서는, 우리의 예루살렘에서는, 언약이 변함없이 머무는 법이다.\""]], [["And, full of the thought, she settled back into her easy place.", "그리고 그 생각으로 가득 차, 어머니는 다시 편안한 자리로 몸을 기댔다."], ["He was first to speak.", "먼저 입을 연 것은 아들이었다."]], [["“What Messala said, my mother, was sharp enough in itself; but, taken with the manner, some of the sayings were intolerable.”", "\"메살라가 한 말은, 어머니, 그 자체로도 충분히 모질었습니다. 그러나 그 태도까지 더하면, 어떤 말들은 참을 수 없는 것이었지요.\""]], [["“I think I understand you.", "\"무슨 말인지 알 것 같구나."], ["Rome, her poets, orators, senators, courtiers, are mad with affectation of what they call satire.”", "로마는, 그 시인이며 웅변가며 원로원 의원이며 궁정인들까지, 풍자라 부르는 것을 부려대는 허세에 미쳐 있지.\""]], [["“I suppose all great peoples are proud,”", "\"위대한 민족은 모두 자긍심이 강한 법이겠지요.\""], ["he went on, scarcely noticing the interruption; “but the pride of that people is unlike all others; in these latter days it is so grown the gods barely escape it.”", "아들은 어머니의 말을 거의 알아차리지 못한 채 말을 이어 갔다. \"그러나 그 민족의 자긍심은 다른 어느 것과도 다릅니다. 요즘 들어 어찌나 부풀어 올랐는지 신들조차 그것을 가까스로 면할 지경이지요.\""]], [["“The gods escape!”", "\"신들이 면한다고!\""], ["said the mother, quickly.", "어머니가 재빨리 말했다."], ["“More than one Roman has accepted worship as his divine right.”", "\"한둘 아닌 로마인이 숭배를 제 신성한 권리로 받아들였느니라.\""]], [["“Well, Messala always had his share of the disagreeable quality.", "\"그렇지요. 메살라도 늘 그 불쾌한 기질을 제 몫만큼은 지니고 있었습니다."], ["When he was a child, I have seen him mock strangers whom even Herod condescended to receive with honors; yet he always spared Judea.", "어린아이였을 때도 헤롯조차 영예로이 맞아들이던 이방인을 조롱하는 것을 본 적이 있습니다. 그래도 그는 늘 유대만큼은 봐주었지요."], ["For the first time, in conversation with me to-day, he trifled with our customs and God.", "오늘 저와 이야기하면서 처음으로 그는 우리의 풍습과 하느님을 가볍게 비웃었습니다."], ["As you would have had me do, I parted with him finally.", "어머니가 바라셨을 대로, 저는 그와 영영 갈라섰습니다."], ["And now, O my dear mother, I would know with more certainty if there be just ground for the Roman’s contempt.", "그리고 이제, 사랑하는 어머니, 저는 그 로마인의 멸시에 정말로 정당한 근거가 있는지 더 확실히 알고 싶습니다."], ["In what am I his inferior?", "제가 무엇이 그보다 못하단 말입니까?"], ["Is ours a lower order of people?", "우리가 더 낮은 부류의 사람들입니까?"], ["Why should I, even in Cæsar’s presence; feel the shrinking of a slave?", "어찌하여 저는, 카이사르 앞에서조차, 노예 같은 위축을 느껴야 합니까?"], ["Tell me especially why, if I have the soul, and so choose, I may not hunt the honors of the world in all its fields?", "무엇보다도 말씀해 주세요. 만약 제게 그럴 영혼이 있고 그러기를 택한다면, 어찌하여 저는 세상의 모든 영역에서 영예를 좇아서는 안 됩니까?"], ["Why may not I take sword and indulge the passion of war?", "어찌하여 저는 칼을 들고 전쟁의 열정을 마음껏 펼쳐서는 안 됩니까?"], ["As a poet, why may not I sing of all themes?", "시인으로서 어찌하여 모든 주제를 노래해서는 안 됩니까?"], ["I can be a worker in metals, a keeper of flocks, a merchant, why not an artist like the Greek?", "저는 금속을 다루는 장인도, 양 떼를 기르는 자도, 상인도 될 수 있는데, 어찌하여 그리스인처럼 예술가는 못 됩니까?"], ["Tell me, O my mother—and this is the sum of my trouble—why may not a son of Israel do all a Roman may?”", "말씀해 주세요, 어머니. 이것이 제 괴로움의 전부입니다. 어찌하여 이스라엘의 아들은 로마인이 할 수 있는 모든 것을 할 수 없는 겁니까?\""]], [["The reader will refer these questions back to the conversation in the Market-place; the mother, listening with all her faculties awake, from something which would have been lost upon one less interested in him—from the connections of the subject, the pointing of the questions, possibly his accent and tone—was not less swift in making the same reference.", "독자는 이 물음들을 시장에서의 그 대화로 거슬러 짚어 볼 것이다. 어머니는 온 신경을 곤두세워 듣고 있었기에, 아들에게 그만큼 마음을 쏟지 않는 이라면 놓쳤을 무언가로부터—그 주제의 연결고리, 물음이 겨냥하는 바, 어쩌면 그 억양과 어조로부터—똑같은 대조를 떠올리는 데 결코 더디지 않았다. 어머니는 몸을 일으켜, 아들 못지않게 빠르고 날카로운 목소리로 대답했다."], ["She sat up, and in a voice quick and sharp as his own, replied, “I see, I see!", "\"알겠다, 알겠어! 메살라는 어린 시절에 교제로 거의 유대인이나 다름없었지."], ["From association Messala, in boyhood, was almost a Jew; had he remained here, he might have become a proselyte, so much do we all borrow from the influences that ripen our lives; but the years in Rome have been too much for him.", "그가 여기 남았더라면 개종자가 되었을지도 모른다. 우리는 모두 우리 삶을 무르익게 하는 영향으로부터 그토록 많은 것을 빌려 오니까. 그러나 로마에서 보낸 세월이 그에게는 너무 과했던 게야."], ["I do not wonder at the change; yet”—her voice fell—“he might have dealt tenderly at least with you.", "그 변화가 놀랍지는 않다만.\" 어머니의 목소리가 낮아졌다. \"그래도 적어도 너에게만은 다정하게 굴 수 있었을 텐데."], ["It is a hard, cruel nature which in youth can forget its first loves.”", "젊은 시절에 첫사랑을 잊을 수 있는 것은 모질고 잔인한 천성이지.\""]], [["Her hand dropped lightly upon his forehead, and the fingers caught in his hair and lingered there lovingly, while her eyes sought the highest stars in view.", "어머니의 손이 아들의 이마 위로 가볍게 내려앉았고, 손가락이 머리카락에 얽혀 사랑스럽게 머무는 동안, 그 눈은 시야에 들어오는 가장 높은 별들을 찾았다."], ["Her pride responded to his, not merely in echo, but in the unison of perfect sympathy.", "어머니의 자긍심은 아들의 자긍심에 화답했으니, 단지 메아리로서가 아니라 완벽한 공감의 한목소리로였다."], ["She would answer him; at the same time, not for the world would she have had the answer unsatisfactory: an admission of inferiority might weaken his spirit for life.", "어머니는 아들에게 답해 줄 것이었다. 그러면서도 무슨 일이 있어도 그 답이 흡족하지 못한 것이 되기를 바라지 않았다. 못하다는 인정은 평생토록 아들의 기상을 약하게 할 수도 있었다."], ["She faltered with misgivings of her own powers.", "어머니는 자기 능력에 대한 불안으로 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“What you propose, O my Judah, is not a subject for treatment by a woman.", "\"네가 내놓은 물음은, 유다야, 여자가 다룰 만한 주제가 아니다."], ["Let me put its consideration off till to-morrow, and I will have the wise Simeon—”", "그 고찰은 내일로 미루게 해다오. 그러면 내가 지혜로운 시므온을 불러서—\""]], [["“Do not send me to the Rector,”", "\"저를 학장님께 보내지 마세요.\""], ["he said, abruptly.", "아들이 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“I will have him come to us.”", "\"그분을 우리에게 오시게 하마.\""]], [["“No, I seek more than information; while he might give me that better than you, O my mother, you can do better by giving me what he cannot—the resolution which is the soul of a man’s soul.”", "\"아니요, 저는 지식 이상의 것을 구합니다. 그분이 저보다 그것을 더 잘 주실 수는 있겠지요, 어머니. 그러나 어머니는 그분이 줄 수 없는 것을 주심으로써 더 잘하실 수 있습니다. 바로 사람의 영혼의 영혼인 결의 말입니다.\""]], [["She swept the heavens with a rapid glance, trying to compass all the meaning of his questions.", "어머니는 아들의 물음이 지닌 온 뜻을 헤아려 보려는 듯, 빠른 눈길로 하늘을 훑었다."]], [["“While craving justice for ourselves, it is never wise to be unjust to others.", "\"우리 자신을 위해 정의를 갈구하면서도, 남에게 부당하게 구는 것은 결코 현명한 일이 아니다. 우리가 정복한 적의 용맹을 부정하는 것은 우리의 승리를 깎아내리는 것이지."], ["To deny valor in the enemy we have conquered is to underrate our victory; and if the enemy be strong enough to hold us at bay, much more to conquer us”—she hesitated—“self-respect bids us seek some other explanation of our misfortunes than accusing him of qualities inferior to our own.”", "그리고 적이 우리를 막아낼 만큼, 하물며 우리를 정복할 만큼 강하다면,\" 어머니가 머뭇거렸다. \"자존심은 우리의 불운을 두고, 적이 우리보다 못한 자질을 지녔다고 탓하기보다는 다른 설명을 찾으라 명하는 법이다.\""]], [["Thus, speaking to herself rather than to him, she began:", "이렇게, 아들에게라기보다 자기 자신에게 말하듯, 어머니는 입을 열었다."]], [["“Take heart, O my son.", "\"마음을 굳게 먹어라, 아들아."], ["The Messala is nobly descended; his family has been illustrious through many generations.", "메살라는 고귀한 혈통을 타고났다. 그 가문은 여러 세대에 걸쳐 명망이 높았지."], ["In the days of Republican Rome—how far back I cannot tell—they were famous, some as soldiers, some as civilians.", "공화정 로마 시절에는—얼마나 옛날까지 거슬러 가는지 나도 알 수 없다만—그들은 명성을 떨쳤으니, 더러는 군인으로, 더러는 정치가로 그러했다."], ["I can recall but one consul of the name; their rank was senatorial, and their patronage always sought because they were always rich.", "그 이름으로 집정관이 된 이는 한 사람밖에 떠오르지 않는구나. 그들의 신분은 원로원급이었고, 늘 부유했기에 그 후원은 언제나 사람들이 구하는 바였다."], ["Yet if to-day your friend boasted of his ancestry, you might have shamed him by recounting yours.", "그러나 오늘 네 친구가 제 조상을 자랑했다면, 너는 네 조상을 헤아려 보임으로써 그를 부끄럽게 할 수 있었을 게다."], ["If he referred to the ages through which the line is traceable, or to deeds, rank, or wealth—such allusions, except when great occasion demands them, are tokens of small minds—if he mentioned them in proof of his superiority, then without dread, and standing on each particular, you might have challenged him to a comparison of records.”", "그가 그 가계를 거슬러 짚을 수 있는 세월이나, 업적이나, 신분이나, 부를 들먹였다면—그런 언급은, 큰일이 요구할 때를 빼면, 옹졸한 마음의 표지이거니와—만약 그가 그것을 제 우월함의 증거로 내세웠다면, 그때 너는 두려움 없이, 하나하나에 버티고 서서, 그에게 기록을 견주어 보자고 도전할 수 있었을 게다.\""]], [["Taking a moment’s thought, the mother proceeded:", "잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 어머니는 말을 이었다."]], [["“One of the ideas of fast hold now is that time has much to do with the nobility of races and families.", "\"요즘 굳게 자리 잡은 생각 하나는, 세월이 종족과 가문의 고귀함과 큰 관계가 있다는 것이다."], ["A Roman boasting his superiority on that account over a son of Israel will always fail when put to the proof.", "그 점에서 이스라엘의 아들보다 우월하다고 뽐내는 로마인은 막상 증명에 나서면 언제나 무너지고 말 게다."], ["The founding of Rome was his beginning; the very best of them cannot trace their descent beyond that period; few of them pretend to do so; and of such as do, I say not one could make good his claim except by resort to tradition.", "로마의 건국이 그들의 시작이었지. 그들 중 가장 빼어난 자라도 그 시기 너머로 제 가계를 거슬러 짚을 수 없거니와, 그러는 척하는 자도 드물다. 그러는 자가 있다 해도, 단언컨대 전승에 기대지 않고서는 누구도 그 주장을 입증하지 못할 게다."], ["Messala certainly could not.", "메살라도 분명 그러지 못한다."], ["Let us look now to ourselves. Could we better?”", "이제 우리 자신을 보자꾸나. 우리는 더 잘할 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["A little more light would have enabled him to see the pride that diffused itself over her face.", "빛이 조금만 더 밝았다면, 아들은 어머니의 얼굴에 번지는 자긍심을 볼 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["“Let us imagine the Roman putting us to the challenge.", "\"로마인이 우리에게 도전해 온다고 상상해 보자."], ["I would answer him, neither doubting nor boastful.”", "나는 그에게 답하리라, 의심하지도 뽐내지도 않으면서.\""]], [["Her voice faltered; a tender thought changed the form of the argument.", "어머니의 목소리가 흔들렸다. 어떤 다정한 생각이 그 논지의 형태를 바꾸어 놓았다."]], [["“Your father, O my Judah, is at rest with his fathers; yet I remember, as though it were this evening, the day he and I, with many rejoicing friends, went up into the Temple to present you to the Lord. We sacrificed the doves, and to the priest I gave your name, which he wrote in my presence—‘Judah, son of Ithamar, of the House of Hur.’ The name was then carried away, and written in a book of the division of records devoted to the saintly family.", "\"네 아버지는, 유다야, 그 조상들과 함께 안식하고 계신다. 그래도 나는, 마치 오늘 저녁 일인 듯, 그날을 기억하는구나. 그분과 내가, 기뻐하는 여러 벗들과 함께, 너를 주님께 바치러 성전에 올라갔던 날을. 우리는 비둘기를 제물로 바쳤고, 나는 제사장에게 네 이름을 일러주었지. 그는 내 앞에서 그것을 적었다. '후르 가문 이타마르의 아들 유다'라고. 그러고 나서 그 이름은 옮겨져, 거룩한 가문에 바쳐진 기록의 분책에 적혔단다.\""]], [["“I cannot tell you when the custom of registration in this mode began.", "\"이런 식의 등록 관습이 언제 시작되었는지는 나도 말해 줄 수 없구나."], ["We know it prevailed before the flight from Egypt.", "우리는 그것이 이집트 탈출 이전부터 행해졌음을 알고 있다."], ["I have heard Hillel say Abraham caused the record to be first opened with his own name, and the names of his sons, moved by the promises of the Lord which separated him and them from all other races, and made them the highest and noblest, the very chosen of the earth.", "힐렐의 말로는, 아브라함이 처음으로 자신의 이름과 그 아들들의 이름으로 그 기록을 열게 했다는구나. 그를 다른 모든 종족으로부터 갈라내어 가장 높고 가장 고귀한, 그야말로 땅의 택함받은 자로 삼으신 주님의 약속에 감동하여서 말이다."], ["The covenant with Jacob was of like effect.", "야곱과 맺은 언약도 같은 효력을 지녔지."], ["‘In thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed’—so said the angel to Abraham in the place Jehovah-jireh.", "'네 씨로 말미암아 땅의 모든 민족이 복을 받으리라.' 여호와 이레라는 곳에서 천사가 아브라함에게 그리 말씀하셨다."], ["‘And the land whereon thou liest, to thee will I give it, and to thy seed’—so the Lord himself said to Jacob asleep at Bethel on the way to Haran.", "'네가 누워 있는 땅을 내가 너와 네 씨에게 주리라.' 하란으로 가는 길에 벧엘에서 잠든 야곱에게 주님 친히 그리 말씀하셨다."], ["Afterwards the wise men looked forward to a just division of the land of promise; and, that it might be known in the day of partition who were entitled to portions, the Book of Generations was begun.", "그 후에 현인들은 약속의 땅을 공정하게 나눌 날을 내다보았다. 그리고 분배의 날에 누가 몫을 받을 자격이 있는지 알 수 있도록, 족보의 책이 시작되었지."], ["But not for that alone. The promise of a blessing to all the earth through the patriarch reached far into the future.", "그러나 그 때문만은 아니었다. 그 조상을 통해 온 땅에 복을 내리겠다는 약속은 먼 미래에까지 미쳤다."], ["One name was mentioned in connection with the blessing—the benefactor might be the humblest of the chosen family, for the Lord our God knows no distinctions of rank or riches.", "그 복과 관련하여 한 이름이 거론되었으니—은인은 택함받은 가문에서 가장 비천한 자일 수도 있었다. 우리 주 하느님은 신분이나 재물의 차별을 모르시니까."], ["So, to make the performance clear to men of the generation who were to witness it, and that they might give the glory to whom it belonged, the record was required to be kept with absolute certainty. Has it been so kept?”", "그래서 그 일을 목격할 세대의 사람들에게 그 성취가 분명히 드러나도록, 또 그들이 마땅한 이에게 영광을 돌릴 수 있도록, 그 기록은 절대적인 확실성으로 보존되어야 했다. 과연 그렇게 보존되어 왔겠느냐?\""]], [["The fan played to and fro, until, becoming impatient, he repeated the question, “Is the record absolutely true?”", "부채가 이리저리 오갔다. 마침내 참다못해 아들이 물음을 되풀이했다. \"그 기록은 절대적으로 참된 것입니까?\""]], [["“Hillel said it was, and of all who have lived no one was so well-informed upon the subject.", "\"힐렐은 그렇다고 했다. 그리고 살아온 모든 이 가운데 그 주제에 그만큼 정통한 자는 없었지."], ["Our people have at times been heedless of some parts of the law, but never of this part.", "우리 민족은 때때로 율법의 어떤 부분에는 무심했어도, 이 부분만은 한 번도 그러지 않았다."], ["The good rector himself has followed the Books of Generations through three periods—from the promises to the opening of the Temple; thence to the Captivity; thence, again, to the present.", "그 선한 학장 자신이 족보의 책을 세 시기에 걸쳐 따라가 보았단다—약속에서 성전의 봉헌까지, 거기서 다시 포로 시기까지, 거기서 또 현재까지."], ["Once only were the records disturbed, and that was at the end of the second period; but when the nation returned from the long exile, as a first duty to God, Zerubbabel restored the Books, enabling us once more to carry the lines of Jewish descent back unbroken fully two thousand years.", "단 한 번 그 기록이 어지러워진 적이 있었으니, 그것은 두 번째 시기의 끝이었다. 그러나 민족이 그 긴 유배에서 돌아왔을 때, 하느님께 바치는 첫 의무로서, 스룹바벨이 그 책들을 복원했지. 그리하여 우리는 다시 한번 유대 혈통의 계보를 끊김 없이 온전히 이천 년 전까지 거슬러 짚을 수 있게 되었다."], ["And now—”", "그리고 이제는—\""]], [["She paused as if to allow the hearer to measure the time comprehended in the statement.", "어머니는 그 진술에 담긴 세월의 크기를 듣는 이가 헤아려 보게 하려는 듯 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"그리고 이제,\""], ["she continued, “what becomes of the Roman boast of blood enriched by ages?", "어머니가 말을 이었다. \"세월로 풍요로워진 혈통이라는 로마인의 자랑은 어찌 되겠느냐?"], ["By that test, the sons of Israel watching the herds on old Rephaim yonder are nobler than the noblest of the Marcii.”", "그 잣대로 보면, 저 너머 옛 르바임에서 가축을 지키는 이스라엘의 아들들이 마르키우스 가문의 가장 고귀한 자보다 더 고귀하니라.\""]], [["“And I, mother—by the Books, who am I?”", "\"그러면 저는, 어머니, 그 책들에 따르면 저는 누구입니까?\""]], [["“What I have said thus far, my son, had reference to your question.", "\"지금까지 내가 한 말은, 아들아, 네 물음과 관련된 것이었다."], ["I will answer you.", "이제 네게 답하마."], ["If Messala were here, he might say, as others have said, that the exact trace of your lineage stopped when the Assyrian took Jerusalem, and razed the Temple, with all its precious stores; but you might plead the pious action of Zerubbabel, and retort that all verity in Roman genealogy ended when the barbarians from the West took Rome, and camped six months upon her desolated site.", "메살라가 여기 있다면, 그는 남들이 그러했듯 이렇게 말할지도 모른다. 아시리아인이 예루살렘을 점령하고 성전을 그 온갖 귀한 보물과 함께 헐어 버렸을 때 네 가계의 정확한 흔적도 끊겼다고. 그러나 너는 스룹바벨의 경건한 행적을 내세우며, 서방의 야만족이 로마를 점령하고 그 폐허가 된 터에 여섯 달 동안 진을 쳤을 때 로마 족보의 모든 진실도 끝났다고 되받아칠 수 있다."], ["Did the government keep family histories?", "그 정부가 가문의 역사를 보존했더냐?"], ["If so, what became of them in those dreadful days?", "그랬다 한들 그 끔찍한 시절에 그것들이 어찌 되었겠느냐?"], ["No, no; there is verity in our Books of Generations; and, following them back to the Captivity, back to the foundation of the first Temple, back to the march from Egypt, we have absolute assurance that you are lineally sprung from Hur, the associate of Joshua.", "아니다, 아니야. 우리의 족보의 책에는 진실이 있다. 그리고 그것을 거슬러 포로 시기까지, 첫 성전의 건립까지, 이집트로부터의 행군까지 따라가면, 너는 여호수아의 동료였던 후르의 직계 후손이라는 절대적인 확신을 얻게 된다."], ["In the matter of descent sanctified by time, is not the honor perfect?", "세월로 신성해진 가계라는 점에서, 그 영예가 완벽하지 않으냐?"], ["Do you care to pursue further?", "더 따라가 보고 싶으냐?"], ["if so, take the Torah, and search the Book of Numbers, and of the seventy-two generations after Adam, you can find the very progenitor of your house.”", "그렇다면 토라를 들고 민수기를 살펴보아라. 그러면 아담 이후 일흔두 세대 가운데서 네 가문의 바로 그 시조를 찾아낼 수 있을 게다.\""]], [["There was silence for a time in the chamber on the roof.", "지붕 위 방에는 한동안 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“I thank you, O my mother,”", "\"고맙습니다, 어머니.\""], ["Judah next said, clasping both her hands in his; “I thank you with all my heart.", "유다가 이윽고 두 손으로 어머니의 두 손을 감싸쥐며 말했다. \"진심으로 고맙습니다."], ["I was right in not having the good rector called in; he could not have satisfied me more than you have.", "선한 학장님을 부르지 않은 것은 옳은 일이었습니다. 그분도 어머니보다 더 저를 만족시켜 주지는 못했을 거예요."], ["Yet to make a family truly noble, is time alone sufficient?”", "그러나 한 가문을 참으로 고귀하게 만드는 데, 세월만으로 충분합니까?\""]], [["“Ah, you forget, you forget; our claim rests not merely upon time; the Lord’s preference is our especial glory.”", "\"아, 너는 잊고 있구나, 잊고 있어. 우리의 권리는 단지 세월에만 기대지 않는다. 주님의 택하심이 바로 우리의 각별한 영광이니라.\""]], [["“You are speaking of the race, and I, mother, of the family—our family.", "\"어머니는 종족을 말씀하고 계시지만, 저는, 어머니, 가문을 말하는 겁니다."], ["In the years since Father Abraham, what have they achieved? What have they done?", "우리 가문 말입니다. 아버지 아브라함 이후의 세월 동안 그들은 무엇을 이루었습니까?"], ["What great things to lift them above the level of their fellows?”", "무엇을 했습니까? 그들을 동족 위로 높여 줄 어떤 위대한 일을 했단 말입니까?\""]], [["She hesitated, thinking she might all this time have mistaken his object.", "어머니는 머뭇거렸다. 이 내내 자기가 아들의 뜻을 잘못 짚어 온 것은 아닌지 생각하면서였다."], ["The information he sought might have been for more than satisfaction of wounded vanity.", "아들이 구하는 정보는 상처받은 자존심을 달래기 위한 것 이상일 수도 있었다."], ["Youth is but the painted shell within which, continually growing, lives that wondrous thing the spirit of man, biding its moment of apparition, earlier in some than in others.", "젊음이란 그저 색칠한 껍데기에 지나지 않으니, 그 안에서는 사람의 영혼이라는 저 경이로운 것이 끊임없이 자라며 그 출현의 순간을 기다리고 있다. 어떤 이에게는 그 순간이 더 일찍 오기도 한다."], ["She trembled under a perception that this might be the supreme moment come to him; that as children at birth reach out their untried hands grasping for shadows, and crying the while, so his spirit might, in temporary blindness, be struggling to take hold of its impalpable future.", "어머니는 어쩌면 그 지고한 순간이 아들에게 찾아온 것일지도 모른다는 깨달음에 몸을 떨었다. 갓 태어난 아이가 시험해 본 적 없는 손을 내밀어 그림자를 움켜쥐려 하며 우는 것처럼, 아들의 영혼도 일시적인 눈멂 속에서 그 손에 잡히지 않는 미래를 붙들려 애쓰고 있는 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["They to whom a boy comes asking, Who am I, and what am I to be? have need of ever so much care.", "소년이 찾아와 나는 누구이며 무엇이 되어야 하느냐고 묻는 이는, 그 무엇보다 큰 보살핌이 필요하다."], ["Each word in answer may prove to the after-life what each finger-touch of the artist is to the clay he is modelling.", "답하는 한 마디 한 마디는, 예술가가 빚는 점토에 그의 손길 하나하나가 그러하듯, 훗날의 삶에 그대로 새겨질 것이기 때문이다."]], [["“I have a feeling, O my Judah,”", "\"내게 한 가지 느낌이 드는구나, 유다야.\""], ["she said, patting his cheek with the hand he had been caressing—“I have the feeling that all I have said has been in strife with an antagonist more real than imaginary.", "어머니는 아들이 어루만지던 그 손으로 아들의 뺨을 다독이며 말했다. \"내가 지금껏 한 모든 말이, 상상 속이라기보다 더 실재하는 어떤 적수와 다투어 온 것이라는 느낌이 든다."], ["If Messala is the enemy, do not leave me to fight him in the dark.", "메살라가 그 적이라면, 나 혼자 어둠 속에서 그와 싸우게 두지 마라."], ["Tell me all he said.”", "그가 한 말을 모두 들려다오.\""]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["The young Israelite proceeded then, and rehearsed his conversation with Messala, dwelling with particularity upon the latter’s speeches in contempt of the Jews, their customs, and much pent round of life.", "그리하여 젊은 이스라엘인은 이야기를 이어 가, 메살라와 나눈 대화를 되짚었다. 특히 유대인과 그들의 관습, 그리고 갇혀 사는 삶을 멸시하던 메살라의 말들을 낱낱이 들춰냈다."]], [["Afraid to speak the while, the mother listened, discerning the matter plainly.", "어머니는 말없이 귀를 기울이며, 사정을 또렷이 헤아렸다."], ["Judah had gone to the palace on the Market-place, allured by love of a playmate whom he thought to find exactly as he had been at the parting years before; a man met him, and, in place of laughter and references to the sports of the past, the man had been full of the future, and talked of glory to be won, and of riches and power.", "유다는 시장 광장의 궁전을 찾아갔다. 여러 해 전 헤어질 때 그대로일 것이라 믿었던 어린 시절 동무를 향한 그리움에 이끌려서였다. 그러나 그를 맞이한 사내는, 웃음도 옛 놀이에 대한 이야기도 없이, 오로지 앞날만을 떠들며 손에 넣을 영광과 부와 권력을 늘어놓았다."], ["Unconscious of the effect, the visitor had come away hurt in pride, yet touched with a natural ambition; but she, the jealous mother, saw it, and, not knowing the turn the aspiration might take, became at once Jewish in her fear. What if it lured him away from the patriarchal faith?", "자기가 어떤 영향을 끼쳤는지도 모른 채, 그 손님은 자존심에 상처를 입었으면서도 한편으로는 자연스러운 야망에 마음이 흔들린 채 돌아왔다. 그러나 질투심 깊은 어머니는 그것을 알아보았고, 그 동경이 어디로 향할지 알 수 없었기에, 곧장 두려움에 사로잡힌 유대인이 되었다."], ["In her view, that consequence was more dreadful than any or all others.", "만일 그것이 아이를 조상 대대로 이어 온 신앙에서 멀어지게 한다면?"], ["She could discover but one way to avert it, and she set about the task, her native power reinforced by love to such degree that her speech took a masculine strength and at times a poet’s fervor.", "그녀가 보기에 그런 결과는 다른 어떤 일보다도, 모든 일을 합친 것보다도 더 끔찍했다. 그것을 막을 길은 단 하나뿐이라, 그녀는 그 일에 나섰다. 사랑이 타고난 힘을 북돋워 주었기에, 그녀의 말투에는 사내다운 힘이 실리고 때로는 시인의 열정마저 깃들었다."]], [["“There never has been a people,” she began, “who did not think themselves at least equal to any other; never a great nation, my son, that did not believe itself the very superior.", "\"제 스스로를 적어도 어느 민족과 견주어 못하지 않다고 여기지 않은 백성은 한 번도 없었단다.\" 그녀가 입을 열었다."], ["When the Roman looks down upon Israel and laughs, he merely repeats the folly of the Egyptian, the Assyrian, and the Macedonian; and as the laugh is against God, the result will be the same.”", "\"아들아, 스스로를 가장 뛰어나다고 믿지 않은 위대한 나라도 한 번도 없었어. 로마인이 이스라엘을 깔보고 비웃을 때, 그는 그저 이집트인과 아시리아인과 마케도니아인의 어리석음을 되풀이하고 있을 뿐이야. 그리고 그 비웃음이 하느님을 향한 것인 이상, 그 끝도 똑같으리라.\""]], [["Her voice became firmer.", "그녀의 목소리가 더욱 굳어졌다."]], [["“There is no law by which to determine the superiority of nations; hence the vanity of the claim, and the idleness of disputes about it. A people risen, run their race, and die either of themselves or at the hands of another, who, succeeding to their power, take possession of their place, and upon their monuments write new names; such is history. If I were called upon to symbolize God and man in the simplest form, I would draw a straight line and a circle, and of the line I would say, ‘This is God, for he alone moves forever straightforward,’ and of the circle, ‘This is man—such is his progress.’ I do not mean that there is no difference between the careers of nations; no two are alike. The difference, however, is not, as some say, in the extent of the circle they describe or the space of earth they cover, but in the sphere of their movement, the highest being nearest God.", "\"민족의 우열을 가릴 법칙 따위는 없단다. 그러니 그런 주장은 헛되고, 그것을 두고 다투는 일도 부질없지. 한 백성이 일어나, 제 길을 달리고, 스스로 멸하거나 다른 자의 손에 멸하지. 그 다른 자는 그들의 권세를 이어받아 그 자리를 차지하고, 그들의 비석 위에 새 이름을 새기는 거야. 역사란 그런 것이지. 만일 내가 하느님과 인간을 가장 단순한 형상으로 그려 보라는 부탁을 받는다면, 나는 직선 하나와 원 하나를 그릴 거야. 그리고 직선을 두고 말하겠지. '이것은 하느님이다. 오직 그분만이 영원히 곧장 앞으로 나아가시기 때문이다.' 또 원을 두고 말하겠지. '이것은 인간이다. 그의 나아감이란 이러하다.' 민족들의 행로에 아무 차이가 없다는 뜻은 아니야. 똑같은 둘은 없으니까. 그러나 그 차이는, 어떤 이들이 말하듯, 그들이 그리는 원의 크기나 그들이 덮은 땅의 넓이에 있는 것이 아니라, 그들의 움직임이 머무는 권역에 있단다. 가장 높은 것이 하느님께 가장 가까운 거야."]], [["“To stop here, my son, would be to leave the subject where we began.", "\"아들아, 여기서 멈춘다면 처음 자리에 그대로 주제를 두고 마는 셈이지."], ["Let us go on.", "더 나아가 보자."], ["There are signs by which to measure the height of the circle each nation runs while in its course.", "각 민족이 제 길을 달리는 동안 그려 내는 원의 높이를 잴 수 있는 표징이 있단다."], ["By them let us compare the Hebrew and the Roman.", "그것으로 히브리인과 로마인을 견주어 보자."]], [["“The simplest of all the signs is the daily life of the people.", "\"모든 표징 가운데 가장 단순한 것은 백성의 일상이야."], ["Of this I will only say, Israel has at times forgotten God, while the Roman never knew him; consequently comparison is not possible.", "이것에 대해서는 한마디만 하마. 이스라엘은 때때로 하느님을 잊은 적은 있으나, 로마인은 그분을 안 적이 한 번도 없었어. 그러니 견주는 일 자체가 가당치 않지."]], [["“Your friend—or your former friend—charged, if I understood you rightly, that we have had no poets, artists, or warriors; by which he meant, I suppose, to deny that we have had great men, the next most certain of the signs.", "\"네 친구가, 아니 옛 친구가, 내가 너의 말을 옳게 알아들었다면, 우리에게는 시인도 예술가도 전사도 없었다고 꾸짖었다지. 내 생각엔 우리에게 위대한 인물이 없었다고 부정하려는 뜻이었을 거야. 위대한 인물이란, 그다음으로 가장 확실한 표징이거든."], ["A just consideration of this charge requires a definition at the commencement. A great man, O my boy, is one whose life proves him to have been recognized, if not called, by God.", "이 비난을 올바로 헤아리려면 먼저 정의부터 내려야 해. 얘야, 위대한 인물이란 그 삶으로써 하느님께 부름받지는 않았더라도 적어도 인정받았음이 드러나는 사람을 말한단다."], ["A Persian was used to punish our recreant fathers, and he carried them into captivity; another Persian was selected to restore their children to the Holy Land; greater than either of them, however, was the Macedonian through whom the desolation of Judea and the Temple was avenged.", "한 페르시아인은 우리의 신앙을 저버린 조상들을 벌하는 데 쓰여, 그들을 포로로 끌고 갔지. 또 한 페르시아인은 그 자손들을 거룩한 땅으로 되돌리도록 뽑혔어. 그러나 그 둘보다 더 위대한 자는 마케도니아인이었으니, 그를 통해 유대와 성전이 당한 황폐의 원수가 갚아졌단다."], ["The special distinction of the men was that they were chosen by the Lord, each for a divine purpose; and that they were Gentiles does not lessen their glory.", "그 사람들의 남다른 점은, 저마다 거룩한 목적을 위해 주님께 뽑혔다는 데 있어. 그들이 이방인이었다는 사실이 그 영광을 깎아내리지는 않지."], ["Do not lose sight of this definition while I proceed.", "내가 말을 이어 가는 동안 이 정의를 잊지 말거라."]], [["“There is an idea that war is the most noble occupation of men, and that the most exalted greatness is the growth of battle-fields.", "\"전쟁이야말로 인간의 가장 고귀한 일이며, 가장 드높은 위대함은 전쟁터에서 자라난다는 생각이 있단다."], ["Because the world has adopted the idea, be not you deceived.", "세상이 그 생각을 받아들였다 해서, 너만은 속지 말거라."], ["That we must worship something is a law which will continue as long as there is anything we cannot understand.", "우리가 무언가를 섬겨야 한다는 것은, 우리가 이해할 수 없는 무언가가 있는 한 영원히 이어질 법칙이야."], ["The prayer of the barbarian is a wail of fear addressed to Strength, the only divine quality he can clearly conceive; hence his faith in heroes.", "야만인의 기도란 힘을 향해 올리는 두려움의 울부짖음이지. 힘이야말로 그가 또렷이 떠올릴 수 있는 유일한 신성한 속성이거든. 그래서 그는 영웅을 믿는 거야."], ["What is Jove but a Roman hero? The Greeks have their great glory because they were the first to set Mind above Strength.", "유피테르가 로마의 영웅이 아니라면 무엇이겠니? 그리스인이 큰 영광을 누리는 까닭은, 그들이 처음으로 정신을 힘 위에 두었기 때문이지."], ["In Athens the orator and philosopher were more revered than the warrior.", "아테네에서는 웅변가와 철학자가 전사보다 더 존경받았어."], ["The charioteer and the swiftest runner are still idols of the arena; yet the immortelles are reserved for the sweetest singer.", "전차를 모는 자와 가장 빠른 주자는 여전히 경기장의 우상이지만, 영생의 화관은 가장 감미로운 노래꾼을 위해 남겨져 있단다."], ["The birthplace of one poet was contested by seven cities.", "어느 시인의 태어난 곳을 두고 일곱 도시가 다투었지."], ["But was the Hellene the first to deny the old barbaric faith? No.", "그러나 헬라스인이 옛 야만의 신앙을 처음으로 부정한 자들이었을까? 아니야."], ["My son, that glory is ours; against brutalism our fathers erected God; in our worship, the wail of fear gave place to the Hosanna and the Psalm.", "아들아, 그 영광은 우리의 것이란다. 야만성에 맞서 우리 조상들은 하느님을 세웠지. 우리의 예배에서는 두려움의 울부짖음이 호산나와 시편에 자리를 내주었어."], ["So the Hebrew and the Greek would have carried all humanity forward and upward. But, alas!", "그렇게 히브리인과 그리스인은 온 인류를 앞으로, 위로 이끌어 갈 수도 있었지. 그러나, 아아!"], ["the government of the world presumes war as an eternal condition; wherefore, over Mind and above God, the Roman has enthroned his Cæsar, the absorbent of all attainable power, the prohibition of any other greatness.", "세상의 통치란 전쟁을 영원한 조건으로 전제한단다. 그래서 로마인은 정신 위에, 또 하느님 위에 제 카이사르를 왕좌에 앉혔어. 손에 넣을 수 있는 모든 권력을 빨아들이고, 다른 어떤 위대함도 금하는 자를 말이야."]], [["“The sway of the Greek was a flowering time for genius.", "\"그리스인의 통치는 천재가 꽃피던 시절이었어."], ["In return for the liberty it then enjoyed, what a company of thinkers the Mind led forth?", "그때 누리던 자유에 보답하여, 정신은 얼마나 많은 사상가들을 이끌어 냈던가?"], ["There was a glory for every excellence, and a perfection so absolute that in everything but war even the Roman has stooped to imitation.", "어떤 탁월함에도 저마다 영광이 있었고, 그 완벽함이 어찌나 절대적이었던지 전쟁만 빼고는 로마인조차 모방하려고 몸을 굽혔단다."], ["A Greek is now the model of the orators in the Forum; listen, and in every Roman song you will hear the rhythm of the Greek; if a Roman opens his mouth speaking wisely of moralities, or abstractions, or of the mysteries of nature, he is either a plagiarist or the disciple of some school which had a Greek for its founder.", "오늘날 그리스인은 포룸의 웅변가들이 본받는 본보기야. 귀 기울여 보렴, 어떤 로마의 노래에서든 그리스의 운율이 들릴 테니. 로마인이 입을 열어 도덕이나 추상이나 자연의 신비를 슬기롭게 논한다면, 그는 표절자이거나 아니면 그리스인을 시조로 둔 어느 학파의 제자일 뿐이야."], ["In nothing but war, I say again, has Rome a claim to originality.", "다시 말하지만, 전쟁 말고는 로마가 독창성을 내세울 만한 것이 아무것도 없단다."], ["Her games and spectacles are Greek inventions, dashed with blood to gratify the ferocity of her rabble; her religion, if such it may be called, is made up of contributions from the faiths of all other peoples; her most venerated gods are from Olympus—even her Mars, and, for that matter, the Jove she much magnifies.", "그들의 경기와 구경거리는 그리스의 발명품인데, 천한 무리의 잔인함을 채워 주려고 거기에 피를 끼얹은 것이지. 그들의 종교는, 그것을 종교라 부를 수 있다면, 다른 모든 백성의 신앙에서 끌어모은 것들로 이루어졌어. 그들이 가장 떠받드는 신들은 올림포스에서 온 것들이야."], ["So it happens, O my son, that of the whole world our Israel alone can dispute the superiority of the Greek, and with him contest the palm of original genius.", "그들의 마르스도 그렇고, 게다가 그들이 그토록 떠받드는 유피테르도 마찬가지지. 그러니, 아들아, 온 세상에서 오직 우리 이스라엘만이 그리스인의 우월함에 맞설 수 있고, 그와 더불어 독창적 천재의 영예를 다툴 수 있는 거란다."]], [["“To the excellences of other peoples the egotism of a Roman is a blindfold, impenetrable as his breastplate.", "\"다른 백성들의 탁월함 앞에서 로마인의 자만심은 눈가리개야. 제 가슴받이만큼이나 뚫을 수 없는 눈가리개지. 오, 무자비한 약탈자들이여!"], ["Oh, the ruthless robbers!", "그들이 짓밟으면 땅은 도리깨로 두들기는 마룻바닥처럼 떨린단다."], ["Under their trampling the earth trembles like a floor beaten with flails.", "다른 이들과 더불어 우리도 쓰러졌지. 아아, 아들아, 너에게 이런 말을 해야 하다니! 그들은 우리의 가장 높은 자리, 가장 거룩한 자리를 차지했고, 그 끝이 어떠할지는 아무도 말할 수 없어."], ["Along with the rest we are fallen—alas that I should say it to you, my son!", "그러나 이것만은 안단다. 그들이 유대를 망치로 깨뜨린 편도처럼 부수고, 그 기름과 단맛인 예루살렘을 삼킬 수는 있을 거야. 그래도 이스라엘 사람들의 영광은 손 닿지 않는 머리 위 하늘에서 하나의 빛으로 남으리라."], ["They have our highest places, and the holiest, and the end no man can tell; but this I know—they may reduce Judea as an almond broken with hammers, and devour Jerusalem, which is the oil and sweetness thereof; yet the glory of the men of Israel will remain a light in the heavens overhead out of reach: for their history is the history of God, who wrote with their hands, spake with their tongues, and was himself in all the good they did, even the least; who dwelt with them, a Lawgiver on Sinai, a Guide in the wilderness, in war a Captain, in government a King; who once and again pushed back the curtains of the pavilion which is his resting-place, intolerably bright, and, as a man speaking to men, showed them the right, and the way to happiness, and how they should live, and made them promises binding the strength of his Almightiness with covenants sworn to everlastingly.", "그들의 역사는 곧 하느님의 역사이기 때문이지. 하느님은 그들의 손으로 쓰셨고, 그들의 혀로 말씀하셨으며, 그들이 행한 모든 선한 일, 아무리 작은 것에도 친히 깃드셨단다. 하느님은 그들과 함께 사셨어."], ["O my son, could it be that they with whom Jehovah thus dwelt, an awful familiar, derived nothing from him?—that in their lives and deeds the common human qualities should not in some degree have been mixed and colored with the divine?", "시나이에서는 율법을 주시는 분으로, 광야에서는 인도자로, 전쟁에서는 장수로, 통치에서는 왕으로 말이야. 그분은 견딜 수 없이 환하게 빛나는, 당신의 안식처인 장막의 휘장을 거듭 젖히시고, 사람이 사람에게 말하듯 그들에게 옳음을, 행복에 이르는 길을, 어떻게 살아야 하는지를 보여 주셨지. 또한 영원토록 맹세한 언약으로 당신 전능하심의 힘을 묶어, 그들에게 약속을 베푸셨단다."], ["that their genius should not have in it, even after the lapse of ages, some little of heaven?”", "아들아, 여호와께서 이처럼 두려운 벗으로 함께 사신 그 사람들이, 그분에게서 아무것도 얻지 못했을 수 있겠니? 그들의 삶과 행적에서 흔한 인간적 자질이 어느 정도라도 신성한 것과 섞이고 물들지 않았을 수 있겠니? 그들의 천재가, 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에도, 그 안에 하늘의 기운을 조금도 지니지 않았을 수 있겠니?\""]], [["For a time the rustling of the fan was all the sound heard in the chamber.", "한동안 방 안에 들리는 소리라고는 부채가 사각거리는 소리뿐이었다."]], [["“In the sense which limits art to sculpture and painting, it is true,”", "\"예술을 조각과 회화에만 한정하는 뜻으로 본다면,\" 그녀가 다시 말했다."], ["she next said, “Israel has had no artists.”", "\"이스라엘에 예술가가 없었다는 말은 참이란다.\""]], [["The admission was made regretfully, for it must be remembered she was a Sadducee, whose faith, unlike that of the Pharisees, permitted a love of the beautiful in every form, and without reference to its origin.", "이 고백은 아쉬운 마음으로 나온 것이었다. 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 그녀가 사두개인이었다는 점이다. 사두개인의 신앙은 바리새인과 달리, 그 기원을 따지지 않고 온갖 형태의 아름다움을 사랑하는 일을 허락했다."]], [["“Still he who would do justice,”", "\"그래도 공정을 기하려는 사람이라면,\""], ["she proceeded, “will not forget that the cunning of our hands was bound by the prohibition, ‘Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, or any likeness of anything;’ which the Sopherim wickedly extended beyond its purpose and time.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"'너를 위하여 새긴 우상을 만들지 말고, 어떤 형상도 만들지 말라'는 금령에 우리 손의 솜씨가 묶여 있었음을 잊지 않을 거야. 소페림이 그 금령을 본래의 뜻과 때를 넘어 사악하게 늘려 놓았지."], ["Nor should it be forgotten that long before Dædalus appeared in Attica and with his wooden statues so transformed sculpture as to make possible the schools of Corinth and Ægina, and their ultimate triumphs the Poecile and Capitolium—long before the age of Dædalus, I say, two Israelites, Bezaleel and Aholiab, the master-builders of the first tabernacle, said to have been skilled ‘in all manner of workmanship,’ wrought the cherubim of the mercy-seat above the ark.", "또한 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 다이달로스가 아티카에 나타나 그의 나무 조각상으로 조각을 송두리째 바꾸어 놓아 코린토스와 아이기나의 유파, 그리고 그 마지막 승리인 포이킬레와 카피톨리움을 가능케 하기 훨씬 전에, 다시 말하지만 다이달로스의 시대보다 훨씬 전에, 두 이스라엘인 브살렐과 오홀리압이 있었다는 거야. 첫 성막을 지은 장인들이었지. '온갖 일에 능숙하다'고 일컬어진 그들은 궤 위 속죄소의 그룹들을 만들었어."], ["Of gold beaten, not chiseled, were they; and they were statues in form both human and divine. ‘And they shall stretch forth their wings on high, ....", "끌로 깎은 것이 아니라 두드려 편 금으로 빚은 것이었고, 그것들은 인간의 형상이자 신의 형상을 한 조각상이었지. '그들이 날개를 높이 펴서……"], ["and their faces shall look one to another.’ Who will say they were not beautiful?", "그 얼굴을 서로 마주 보게 하라.' 누가 그것들을 아름답지 않다고 하겠니?"], ["or that they were not the first statues?”", "또 그것들이 최초의 조각상이 아니라고 누가 하겠니?\""]], [["“Oh, I see now why the Greek outstripped us,”", "\"아, 이제야 그리스인이 우리를 앞지른 까닭을 알겠어요.\""], ["said Judah, intensely interested.", "유다가 깊이 빠져들며 말했다."], ["“And the ark; accursed be the Babylonians who destroyed it!”", "\"그리고 그 궤, 그것을 부순 바빌론인들에게 저주가 있기를!\""]], [["“Nay, Judah, be of faith.", "\"아니다, 유다, 믿음을 지니거라."], ["It was not destroyed, only lost, hidden away too safely in some cavern of the mountains.", "그것은 부서진 게 아니라 그저 잃어버린 것이야. 산속 어느 동굴에 너무도 안전하게 숨겨졌을 뿐이지."], ["One day—Hillel and Shammai both say so—one day, in the Lord’s good time, it will be found and brought forth, and Israel dance before it, singing as of old.", "언젠가, 힐렐도 샴마이도 그리 말한단다, 언젠가 주님의 좋으신 때에 그것이 발견되어 끌어내어질 거야. 그러면 이스라엘은 그 앞에서 옛날처럼 노래하며 춤추겠지."], ["And they who look upon the faces of the cherubim then, though they have seen the face of the ivory Minerva, will be ready to kiss the hand of the Jew from love of his genius, asleep through all the thousands of years.”", "그때 그룹들의 얼굴을 우러르는 자들은, 비록 상아로 빚은 미네르바의 얼굴을 본 적이 있더라도, 수천 년 내내 잠들어 있던 유대인의 천재를 사랑하여 기꺼이 그의 손에 입 맞추려 들 거야.\""]], [["The mother, in her eagerness, had risen into something like the rapidity and vehemence of a speech-maker; but now, to recover herself, or to pick up the thread of her thought, she rested awhile.", "어머니는 열정에 휩쓸려 어느덧 웅변가처럼 빠르고 격렬하게 말하고 있었다. 그러나 이제 마음을 가다듬고, 혹은 생각의 실마리를 다시 잡으려고, 잠시 쉬었다."]], [["“You are so good, my mother,”", "\"어머니는 참 훌륭하세요.\""], ["he said, in a grateful way.", "유다가 고마운 마음을 담아 말했다."], ["“And I will never be done saying so.", "\"이 말은 아무리 해도 모자라요."], ["Shammai could not have talked better, nor Hillel.", "샴마이도 이보다 더 잘 말하지 못했을 테고, 힐렐도 그랬을 거예요."], ["I am a true son of Israel again.”", "저는 다시 이스라엘의 참된 아들이 되었어요.\""]], [["“Flatterer!”", "\"아첨꾼 같으니!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You do not know that I am but repeating what I heard Hillel say in an argument he had one day in my presence with a sophist from Rome.”", "\"이것이 그저 힐렐의 말을 되풀이한 것뿐임을 너는 모르는구나. 어느 날 내 앞에서 로마에서 온 궤변가와 논쟁할 때 힐렐이 한 말을 옮긴 것뿐이란다.\""]], [["“Well, the hearty words are yours.”", "\"그래도 그 진심 어린 말씀은 어머니의 것이에요.\""]], [["Directly all her earnestness returned.", "곧 그녀의 진지함이 모두 되살아났다."]], [["“Where was I? Oh yes, I was claiming for our Hebrew fathers the first statues.", "\"내가 어디까지 했지? 아, 그래, 우리 히브리 조상들에게 최초의 조각상이 있었다고 주장하던 참이었지."], ["The trick of the sculptor, Judah, is not all there is of art, any more than art is all there is of greatness.", "유다야, 조각가의 솜씨가 예술의 전부는 아니란다. 예술이 위대함의 전부가 아닌 것과 마찬가지지. 나는 늘 위대한 인물들이 무리를 지어, 훌륭한 일행을 이루어 세기를 따라 행진해 내려오는 모습을 떠올리곤 해."], ["I always think of great men marching down the centuries in groups and goodly companies, separable according to nationalities; here the Indian, there the Egyptian, yonder the Assyrian; above them the music of trumpets and the beauty of banners; and on their right hand and left, as reverent spectators, the generations from the beginning, numberless.", "민족별로 나뉘어, 여기는 인도인, 저기는 이집트인, 저 너머는 아시리아인. 그들 위로는 나팔 소리와 깃발의 아름다움이 어우러지고, 그들 좌우에는 태초부터 이어 온 헤아릴 수 없는 세대가 경건한 구경꾼으로 늘어서 있지. 그들이 나아갈 때, 나는 그리스인이 이렇게 말하는 모습을 떠올린단다. '보라!"], ["As they go, I think of the Greek, saying, ‘Lo!", "헬라스인이 앞장서 간다.' 그러면 로마인이 대꾸하지."], ["The Hellene leads the way.’ Then the Roman replies, ‘Silence!", "'조용하라! 너희 자리였던 곳이 이제 우리 것이다."], ["what was your place is ours now; we have left you behind as dust trodden on.’ And all the time, from the far front back over the line of march, as well as forward into the farthest future, streams a light of which the wranglers know nothing, except that it is forever leading them on—the Light of Revelation!", "우리는 너희를 밟힌 먼지처럼 뒤에 남겨 두었다.' 그리고 그 내내, 저 멀리 앞쪽에서 행렬을 거슬러, 또한 저 머나먼 미래를 향해 앞으로, 한 줄기 빛이 흘러나온단다. 다투는 자들은 그것이 자기들을 영원히 이끌어 간다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르는 빛, 곧 계시의 빛이야!"], ["Who are they that carry it?", "그것을 받들어 가는 자들은 누구겠니?"], ["Ah, the old Judean blood!", "아, 그 오랜 유대의 핏줄이지!"], ["How it leaps at the thought!", "그 생각만으로도 어찌나 가슴이 뛰는지!"], ["By the light we know them.", "그 빛으로 우리는 그들을 알아본단다."], ["Thrice blessed, O our fathers, servants of God, keepers of the covenants!", "세 곱절로 복되어라, 오 우리 조상들이여, 하느님의 종이여, 언약을 지키는 자들이여!"], ["Ye are the leaders of men, the living and the dead.", "그대들은 산 자와 죽은 자, 모든 사람의 지도자다."], ["The front is thine; and though every Roman were a Cæsar, ye shall not lose it!”", "맨 앞은 그대들의 것이니, 로마인이 저마다 카이사르일지라도, 그대들은 그 자리를 잃지 않으리라!\""]], [["Judah was deeply stirred.", "유다는 깊이 마음이 흔들렸다."]], [["“Do not stop, I pray you,” he cried.", "\"제발 멈추지 마세요.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["“You give me to hear the sound of timbrels.", "\"어머니 덕분에 소고 소리가 들리는 것 같아요."], ["I wait for Miriam and the women who went after her dancing and singing.”", "저는 미리암과 그 뒤를 따라 춤추고 노래하던 여인들을 기다리고 있어요.\""]], [["She caught his feeling, and, with ready wit, wove it into her speech.", "그녀는 아들의 마음을 알아채고, 재치 있게 그것을 제 말 속에 엮어 넣었다."]], [["“Very well, my son.", "\"좋아, 아들아."], ["If you can hear the timbrel of the prophetess, you can do what I was about to ask; you can use your fancy, and stand with me, as if by the wayside, while the chosen of Israel pass us at the head of the procession.", "네가 그 여예언자의 소고 소리를 들을 수 있다면, 내가 막 청하려던 일도 할 수 있을 거야. 상상력을 펼쳐 나와 함께, 마치 길가에 선 듯 서 보렴. 행렬의 맨 앞에서 이스라엘의 선택받은 자들이 우리 곁을 지나가는 동안에 말이야."], ["Now they come—the patriarchs first; next the fathers of the tribes.", "자, 이제 그들이 온다. 족장들이 먼저고, 다음은 지파들의 조상들이지."], ["I almost hear the bells of their camels and the lowing of their herds.", "그들의 낙타 방울 소리와 가축 떼의 울음소리가 들리는 듯하구나."], ["Who is he that walks alone between the companies?", "무리들 사이를 홀로 걷는 저이는 누구일까?"], ["An old man, yet his eye is not dim, nor his natural force abated.", "늙은이지만, 그 눈은 흐려지지 않았고 타고난 기운도 쇠하지 않았어."], ["He knew the Lord face to face!", "그는 주님을 얼굴과 얼굴을 맞대고 뵈었지!"], ["Warrior, poet, orator, lawgiver, prophet, his greatness is as the sun at morning, its flood of splendor quenching all other lights, even that of the first and noblest of the Cæsars.", "전사이자 시인이자 웅변가요 입법자요 예언자인 그의 위대함은 아침의 해와 같아서, 그 쏟아지는 광채가 다른 모든 빛을, 카이사르 가운데 으뜸가고 가장 고귀한 자의 빛마저 꺼뜨려 버린단다."], ["After him the judges.", "그 뒤로는 사사들이지."], ["And then the kings—the son of Jesse, a hero in war, and a singer of songs eternal as that of the sea; and his son, who, passing all other kings in riches and wisdom, and while making the Desert habitable, and in its waste places planting cities, forgot not Jerusalem which the Lord had chosen for his seat on earth.", "그다음은 왕들이야. 이새의 아들은 전쟁의 영웅이자 바다의 노래처럼 영원한 노래를 부르던 가인이었지. 그의 아들은 부와 지혜에서 다른 모든 왕을 능가했고, 사막을 사람 살 만한 곳으로 만들며 그 황무지에 도시들을 세우면서도, 주님께서 땅 위의 보좌로 택하신 예루살렘을 잊지 않았단다."], ["Bend lower, my son! These that come next are the first of their kind, and the last.", "더 낮게 몸을 굽히렴, 아들아! 다음에 오는 이들은 그 부류의 처음이자 마지막인 자들이야."], ["Their faces are raised, as if they heard a voice in the sky and were listening.", "그들의 얼굴은 들려 있으니, 마치 하늘에서 들려오는 음성을 듣고 귀 기울이는 듯하구나."], ["Their lives were full of sorrows.", "그들의 삶은 슬픔으로 가득했지."], ["Their garments smell of tombs and caverns.", "그들의 옷에서는 무덤과 동굴의 냄새가 난단다."], ["Hearken to a woman among them—‘Sing ye to the Lord, for he hath triumphed gloriously!’ Nay, put your forehead in the dust before them!", "그들 가운데 한 여인의 목소리를 들어 보렴. '주님을 찬송하라, 그분이 영광스럽게 승리하셨도다!' 아니, 그들 앞에서는 이마를 흙에 대거라!"], ["They were tongues of God, his servants, who looked through heaven, and, seeing all the future, wrote what they saw, and left the writing to be proven by time.", "그들은 하느님의 혀였고, 그분의 종이었으니, 하늘을 꿰뚫어 보고 모든 미래를 보아 그 본 것을 기록하였으며, 그 기록을 시간이 입증하도록 남겼단다."], ["Kings turned pale as they approached them, and nations trembled at the sound of their voices.", "왕들도 그들 앞에 다가가면 새파랗게 질렸고, 민족들도 그들의 목소리에 떨었지."], ["The elements waited upon them.", "천지만물이 그들을 받들었어."], ["In their hands they carried every bounty and every plague.", "그들은 온갖 은혜와 온갖 재앙을 손에 쥐고 다녔단다."], ["See the Tishbite and his servant Elisha!", "저 디셉 사람과 그의 종 엘리사를 보렴!"], ["See the sad son of Hilkiah, and him, the seer of visions, by the river of Chebar!", "힐기야의 슬픈 아들을 보고, 또 그발 강가에서 환상을 본 자를 보렴!"], ["And of the three children of Judah who refused the image of the Babylonian, lo! that one who, in the feast to the thousand lords, so confounded the astrologers.", "그리고 바빌론의 우상을 거부한 유대의 세 아이 가운데, 보라, 천 명의 귀족이 모인 잔치에서 점성가들을 그토록 당황케 한 그 한 사람을 보렴."], ["And yonder—O my son, kiss the dust again!—yonder the gentle son of Amoz, from whom the world has its promise of the Messiah to come!”", "그리고 저기, 오 아들아, 다시 흙에 입 맞추어라! 저기 아모스의 온유한 아들이 온다. 세상이 그를 통해 장차 오실 메시아의 약속을 받았단다!\""]], [["In this passage the fan had been kept in rapid play; it stopped now, and her voice sank low.", "이 대목에서 부채는 빠르게 움직였다. 이제 그것이 멈추고, 그녀의 목소리는 낮게 가라앉았다."]], [["“You are tired,” she said.", "\"피곤하구나.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["he replied, “I was listening to a new song of Israel.”", "그가 대답했다. \"저는 이스라엘의 새 노래를 듣고 있었어요.\""]], [["The mother was still intent upon her purpose, and passed the pleasant speech.", "어머니는 여전히 제 뜻에 골몰해 있어, 그 다정한 말을 흘려보냈다."]], [["“In such light as I could, my Judah, I have set our great men before you—patriarchs, legislators, warriors, singers, prophets.", "\"내 힘닿는 데까지, 유다야, 나는 우리의 위대한 인물들을 네 앞에 펼쳐 보였단다. 족장들, 입법자들, 전사들, 가인들, 예언자들을 말이야."], ["Turn we to the best of Rome.", "이제 로마 최고의 인물들에게로 돌아가 보자."], ["Against Moses place Cæsar, and Tarquin against David; Sylla against either of the Maccabees; the best of the consuls against the judges; Augustus against Solomon, and you are done: comparison ends there.", "모세 앞에 카이사르를, 다윗 앞에 타르퀴니우스를, 마카베오 형제 중 누구 앞에든 술라를, 사사들 앞에 가장 뛰어난 집정관들을, 솔로몬 앞에 아우구스투스를 세워 보면, 그것으로 끝이야. 견줌은 거기서 멎지."], ["But think then of the prophets—greatest of the great.”", "그러나 그다음, 위대한 자들 중에서도 가장 위대한 예언자들을 떠올려 보렴.\""]], [["She laughed scornfully.", "그녀는 비웃듯 웃었다."]], [["“Pardon me.", "\"미안하구나."], ["I was thinking of the soothsayer who warned Caius Julius against the Ides of March, and fancied him looking for the omens of evil which his master despised in the entrails of a chicken.", "카이우스 율리우스에게 3월의 이데스를 조심하라 경고한 점쟁이를 떠올렸던 거야. 그가 자기 주인은 거들떠보지도 않던 흉조를 닭의 내장에서 찾는 모습이 떠올랐지."], ["From that picture turn to Elijah sitting on the hill-top on the way to Samaria, amid the smoking bodies of the captains and their fifties, warning the son of Ahab of the wrath of our God.", "그 그림에서 눈을 돌려, 사마리아로 가는 길목 산꼭대기에 앉은 엘리야를 보렴. 장수들과 그 쉰 명씩의 무리가 연기를 내며 타 죽은 시신들 한가운데서, 아합의 아들에게 우리 하느님의 진노를 경고하던 모습을 말이야."], ["Finally, O my Judah—if such speech be reverent—how shall we judge Jehovah and Jupiter unless it be by what their servants have done in their names? And as for what you shall do—”", "끝으로, 오 나의 유다야, 이런 말이 불경하지 않다면, 여호와와 유피테르를 그 종들이 그 이름으로 행한 일이 아니고서야 무엇으로 견주겠니? 그리고 네가 무엇을 해야 하느냐 하면……\""]], [["She spoke the latter words slowly, and with a tremulous utterance.", "그녀는 마지막 말을 천천히, 떨리는 목소리로 내뱉었다."]], [["“As for what you shall do, my boy—serve the Lord, the Lord God of Israel, not Rome.", "\"네가 무엇을 해야 하느냐 하면, 얘야, 주님을 섬기거라. 이스라엘의 주 하느님을 섬기되, 로마를 섬기지는 말거라."], ["For a child of Abraham there is no glory except in the Lord’s ways, and in them there is much glory.”", "아브라함의 자손에게 영광이란 오직 주님의 길에만 있고, 그 길에는 큰 영광이 있단다.\""]], [["“I may be a soldier then?”", "\"그럼 저는 군인이 되어도 되나요?\""], ["Judah asked.", "유다가 물었다."]], [["“Why not? Did not Moses call God a man of war?”", "\"안 될 게 무어냐? 모세도 하느님을 전쟁의 용사라 부르지 않았더냐?\""]], [["There was then a long silence in the summer chamber.", "그러고 나서 여름 방 안에는 긴 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“You have my permission,”", "\"내가 허락하마.\""], ["she said, finally; “if only you serve the Lord instead of Cæsar.”", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"오직 카이사르 대신 주님을 섬긴다면 말이다.\""]], [["He was content with the condition, and by-and-by fell asleep.", "그는 그 조건에 만족했고, 이윽고 잠이 들었다."], ["She arose then, and put the cushion under his head, and, throwing a shawl over him and kissing him tenderly, went away.", "그러자 그녀는 일어나 아들의 머리 밑에 방석을 받쳐 주고, 숄을 덮어 준 뒤, 다정하게 입 맞추고 자리를 떴다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["The good man, like the bad, must die; but, remembering the lesson of our faith, we say of him and the event, “No matter, he will open his eyes in heaven.”", "선한 사람도 악한 사람과 마찬가지로 죽음을 맞아야 한다. 그러나 우리 신앙의 가르침을 떠올리며, 우리는 그와 그의 죽음을 두고 이렇게 말한다. \"괜찮다. 그는 하늘나라에서 눈을 뜰 테니.\""], ["Nearest this in life is the waking from healthful sleep to a quick consciousness of happy sights and sounds.", "살아 있는 동안 이에 가장 가까운 것은, 건강한 잠에서 깨어나 행복한 광경과 소리를 이내 또렷이 의식하는 순간이다."]], [["When Judah awoke, the sun was up over the mountains; the pigeons were abroad in flocks, filling the air with the gleams of their white wings; and off southeast he beheld the Temple, an apparition of gold in the blue of the sky.", "유다가 깨어났을 때, 해는 산 위로 솟아 있었다. 비둘기들이 떼 지어 날며 흰 날개의 번득임으로 하늘을 가득 채웠고, 남동쪽으로는 성전이, 푸른 하늘 속에 황금빛 환영처럼 떠 있는 것이 보였다."], ["These, however, were familiar objects, and they received but a glance; upon the edge of the divan, close by him, a girl scarcely fifteen sat singing to the accompaniment of a nebel, which she rested upon her knee, and touched gracefully.", "그러나 이는 모두 낯익은 것들이라 흘낏 눈길만 받았을 뿐이다. 그의 곁, 긴 의자 가장자리에는 열다섯도 채 안 되어 보이는 한 소녀가 무릎에 네벨을 받쳐 들고 우아하게 줄을 뜯으며 노래하고 있었다. 그는 귀를 기울이며 그쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["To her he turned listening; and this was what she sang:", "그녀가 부르는 노래는 이러했다. 노래."]], [["“Wake not, but hear me, love!", "\"깨지 마, 다만 들어 줘, 그대여!"], ["Adrift, adrift on slumber’s sea, Thy spirit call to list to me.", "졸음의 바다에 떠밀려 가는 그대, 그대의 넋을 불러 내 말을 듣게 하라."], ["Wake not, but hear me, love!", "깨지 마, 다만 들어 줘, 그대여!"], ["A gift from Sleep, the restful king, All happy, happy dreams I bring.", "쉼을 주는 왕, 잠의 선물로, 온통 행복한, 행복한 꿈을 내가 가져다주노라."]], [["“Wake not, but hear me, love!", "깨지 마, 다만 들어 줘, 그대여!"], ["Of all the world of dreams ’tis thine This once to choose the most divine.", "온 세상 꿈 가운데 가장 거룩한 것을 이번 한 번 그대가 고를 수 있으니."], ["So choose, and sleep, my love!", "그러니 고르고, 잠들라, 내 사랑!"], ["But ne’er again in choice be free, Unless, unless—thou dream’st of me.”", "허나 다시는 마음대로 고를 수 없으리, 그대가, 그대가 나를 꿈꾸지 않는 한.\""]], [["She put the instrument down, and, resting her hands in her lap, waited for him to speak.", "그녀는 악기를 내려놓고, 두 손을 무릎에 얹은 채 그가 입을 열기를 기다렸다."], ["And as it has become necessary to tell somewhat of her, we will avail ourselves of the chance, and add such particulars of the family into whose privacy we are brought as the reader may wish to know.", "이제 그녀에 관해 얼마간 이야기해야 할 차례가 되었으니, 우리는 이 기회를 빌려, 우리가 그 사사로운 영역까지 들어서게 된 이 가문에 대해 독자가 알고 싶어 할 만한 사실들을 덧붙여 두기로 한다."]], [["The favors of Herod had left surviving him many persons of vast estate.", "헤롯의 총애는 그가 죽은 뒤에도 막대한 재산을 지닌 사람들을 여럿 남겼다."], ["Where this fortune was joined to undoubted lineal descent from some famous son of one of the tribes, especially Judah, the happy individual was accounted a Prince of Jerusalem—a distinction which sufficed to bring him the homage of his less favored countrymen, and the respect, if nothing more, of the Gentiles with whom business and social circumstance brought him into dealing.", "이 재산이 어느 지파, 특히 유다 지파의 이름난 후손이라는 의심할 바 없는 직계 혈통과 결합된 경우, 그 행운의 인물은 예루살렘의 왕자로 여겨졌다. 이 칭호 하나만으로도 그는 자기보다 덜 혜택받은 동포들의 존경을 받기에 충분했고, 사업과 사교의 형편상 거래하게 되는 이방인들에게서도, 그 이상은 아니더라도, 적어도 존중은 받았다."], ["Of this class none had won in private or public life a higher regard than the father of the lad whom we have been following.", "이런 부류 가운데 우리가 따라온 소년의 아버지만큼 사적으로나 공적으로나 높은 존경을 얻은 이는 없었다."], ["With a remembrance of his nationality which never failed him, he had yet been true to the king, and served him faithfully at home and abroad.", "한 번도 잊은 적 없는 제 민족에 대한 기억을 간직하면서도, 그는 왕에게 충실하여 안팎으로 그를 성실히 섬겼다."], ["Some offices had taken him to Rome, where his conduct attracted the notice of Augustus, who strove without reserve to engage his friendship.", "몇몇 직무 때문에 그는 로마에 가게 되었고, 거기서 그의 처신은 아우구스투스의 눈길을 끌었다. 아우구스투스는 거리낌 없이 그의 우정을 얻으려 애썼다."], ["In his house, accordingly, were many presents, such as had gratified the vanity of kings—purple togas, ivory chairs, golden pateroe—chiefly valuable on account of the imperial hand which had honorably conferred them.", "그리하여 그의 집에는 왕들의 허영을 흡족하게 했을 법한 선물이 많았다. 자줏빛 토가, 상아 의자, 황금 술잔 따위가 그러했는데, 그 값어치는 주로 그것들을 영예롭게 하사한 황제의 손에 있었다."], ["Such a man could not fail to be rich; yet his wealth was not altogether the largess of royal patrons.", "이런 사람이 부유하지 않을 리 없었다. 그러나 그의 재산이 전부 왕실 후견인의 하사품이었던 것은 아니다."], ["He had welcomed the law that bound him to some pursuit; and, instead of one, he entered into many.", "그는 어떤 생업에 종사하도록 의무 지우는 율법을 기꺼이 받아들였고, 하나가 아니라 여러 가지 일에 손을 댔다."], ["Of the herdsmen watching flocks on the plains and hill-sides, far as old Lebanon, numbers reported to him as their employer; in the cities by the sea, and in those inland, he founded houses of traffic; his ships brought him silver from Spain, whose mines were then the richest known; while his caravans came twice a year from the East, laden with silks and spices.", "멀리 옛 레바논에 이르기까지 평원과 산비탈에서 가축을 치는 목자들 가운데 수많은 이가 그를 고용주로 삼아 보고를 올렸다. 바닷가 도시들과 내륙 도시들에 그는 상관(商館)을 세웠다. 그의 배들은 당시 알려진 가장 풍부한 광산이 있던 에스파냐에서 은을 실어 왔고, 그의 대상(隊商)들은 해마다 두 차례 동방에서 비단과 향료를 가득 싣고 왔다."], ["In faith he was a Hebrew, observant of the law and every essential rite; his place in the synagogue and Temple knew him well; he was thoroughly learned in the Scriptures; he delighted in the society of the college-masters, and carried his reverence for Hillel almost to the point of worship.", "신앙으로 보면 그는 히브리인이었으니, 율법과 모든 본질적 의식을 지켰다. 회당과 성전에서 그의 자리는 그를 잘 알았고, 그는 성경에 두루 통달했으며, 율법 학자들과 어울리기를 즐겼고, 힐렐에 대한 존경은 거의 숭배에 가까웠다."], ["Yet he was in no sense a Separatist; his hospitality took in strangers from every land; the carping Pharisees even accused him of having more than once entertained Samaritans at his table.", "그러면서도 그는 결코 분리주의자가 아니었으니, 그의 환대는 온 나라에서 온 나그네들을 품었다. 트집 잡기 좋아하는 바리새인들은 그가 한두 번 이상 사마리아인을 제 식탁에 맞아들였다고 비난하기까지 했다."], ["Had he been a Gentile, and lived, the world might have heard of him as the rival of Herodes Atticus: as it was, he perished at sea some ten years before this second period of our story, in the prime of life, and lamented everywhere in Judea.", "만약 그가 이방인이었고 또 살아 있었다면, 세상은 그를 헤로데스 아티쿠스의 맞수로 알았을지도 모른다. 그러나 실제로는 우리 이야기의 이 두 번째 시기보다 십 년쯤 전, 한창나이에 바다에서 목숨을 잃었고, 유대 전역에서 애도를 받았다."], ["We are already acquainted with two members of his family—his widow and son; the only other was a daughter—she whom we have seen singing to her brother.", "우리는 이미 그 가족 가운데 두 사람을 알고 있다. 그의 미망인과 아들이다. 나머지 단 한 사람은 딸이었으니, 바로 우리가 오빠에게 노래 불러 주는 모습을 본 그 소녀다."]], [["Tirzah was her name, and as the two looked at each other, their resemblance was plain.", "그녀의 이름은 티르자였다. 둘이 서로를 바라보니, 그 닮음이 뚜렷했다."], ["Her features had the regularity of his, and were of the same Jewish type; they had also the charm of childish innocency of expression.", "그녀의 이목구비도 오빠의 것처럼 반듯했고, 같은 유대인의 모습이었다. 또한 어린아이다운 순진한 표정의 매력도 지니고 있었다."], ["Home-life and its trustful love permitted the negligent attire in which she appeared.", "가정생활과 그 미덥고 다정한 사랑이 있었기에, 그녀는 그처럼 무심한 차림으로 나타날 수 있었다."], ["A chemise buttoned upon the right shoulder, and passing loosely over the breast and back and under the left arm, but half concealed her person above the waist, while it left the arms entirely nude.", "오른쪽 어깨에 단추를 채우고 가슴과 등을 헐겁게 지나 왼팔 아래로 흐르는 속옷은 허리 위쪽 몸매를 반쯤만 가렸고, 두 팔은 온전히 드러나 있었다."], ["A girdle caught the folds of the garment, marking the commencement of the skirt.", "띠 하나가 옷의 주름을 잡아 치마가 시작되는 자리를 표시했다."], ["The coiffure was very simple and becoming—a silken cap, Tyrian-dyed; and over that a striped scarf of the same material, beautifully embroidered, and wound about in thin folds so as to show the shape of the head without enlarging it; the whole finished by a tassel dropping from the crown point of the cap.", "머리 모양은 매우 단순하면서도 어울렸으니, 티레의 물감으로 물들인 비단 모자였고, 그 위에는 같은 천으로 된 줄무늬 스카프가 아름답게 수놓아져 있었다. 그것은 머리 모양을 더 크게 만들지 않으면서도 그대로 드러내도록 얇은 주름으로 둘러 감겨 있었으며, 모자 꼭대기에서 늘어진 술 하나로 마무리되어 있었다."], ["She had rings, ear and finger; anklets and bracelets, all of gold; and around her neck there was a collar of gold, curiously garnished with a network of delicate chains, to which were pendants of pearl.", "그녀는 귀에도 손가락에도 반지를 꼈고, 발찌와 팔찌는 모두 금이었다. 목에는 금 목걸이를 둘렀는데, 섬세한 사슬의 그물로 정교하게 꾸미고 거기에 진주 장식을 늘어뜨린 것이었다."], ["The edges of her eyelids were painted, and the tips of her fingers stained. Her hair fell in two long plaits down her back.", "눈꺼풀 가장자리에는 화장을 했고, 손끝은 물들여 놓았다. 머리카락은 두 갈래로 길게 땋아 등 뒤로 늘어뜨렸다."], ["A curled lock rested upon each cheek in front of the ear.", "곱슬머리 한 가닥이 양쪽 뺨, 귀 앞쪽에 드리워져 있었다."], ["Altogether it would have been impossible to deny her grace, refinement, and beauty.", "무엇으로 보나 그녀의 우아함과 기품과 아름다움은 부정할 수 없었다."]], [["“Very pretty, my Tirzah, very pretty!”", "\"아주 예쁘구나, 티르자, 정말 예뻐!\""], ["he said, with animation.", "그가 활기차게 말했다."]], [["“The song?”", "\"노래가요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yes—and the singer, too.", "\"그래, 노래도 부르는 이도."], ["It has the conceit of a Greek.", "그리스인의 멋이 있구나."], ["Where did you get it?”", "어디서 배웠니?\""]], [["“You remember the Greek who sang in the theatre last month?", "\"지난달 극장에서 노래한 그리스인 기억하세요?"], ["They said he used to be a singer at the court for Herod and his sister Salome.", "사람들 말로는 그가 헤롯과 그 누이 살로메의 궁정에서 가수였다더군요."], ["He came out just after an exhibition of wrestlers, when the house was full of noise. At his first note everything became so quiet that I heard every word.", "씨름 시합이 막 끝나서 객석이 떠들썩할 때 그가 나왔어요. 그가 첫 음을 내자 모든 것이 어찌나 조용해졌던지, 저는 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었어요."], ["I got the song from him.”", "그에게서 이 노래를 배웠지요.\""]], [["“But he sang in Greek.”", "\"하지만 그는 그리스어로 불렀잖아.\""], ["“And I in Hebrew.”", "\"그리고 저는 히브리어로 부르고요.\""]], [["“Ah, yes. I am proud of my little sister.", "\"아, 그렇지. 내 어린 누이가 자랑스럽구나."], ["Have you another as good?”", "이만큼 좋은 노래가 또 있느냐?\""]], [["“Very many. But let them go now.", "\"아주 많아요. 하지만 지금은 그만두기로 해요."], ["Amrah sent me to tell you she will bring you your breakfast, and that you need not come down.", "암라가 오빠한테 아침을 가져다줄 테니 내려올 것 없다고 전하래요. 지금쯤이면 올 텐데. 암라는 오빠가 아프다고 생각해요."], ["She should be here by this time.", "어제 끔찍한 사고를 당했다고요."], ["She thinks you sick—that a dreadful accident happened you yesterday. What was it?", "무슨 일이었어요? 말해 줘요, 그러면 제가 암라를 도와 오빠를 치료할게요."], ["Tell me, and I will help Amrah doctor you.", "암라는 이집트인들의 처방을 알아요."], ["She knows the cures of the Egyptians, who were always a stupid set; but I have a great many recipes of the Arabs who—”", "그 사람들은 늘 멍청한 무리였지만요. 그리고 저는 아랍인들의 처방도 많이 알아요. 그 사람들은—\""]], [["“Are even more stupid than the Egyptians,”", "\"이집트인들보다 한층 더 멍청하지.\""], ["he said, shaking his head.", "그가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“Do you think so?", "\"그렇게 생각하세요?"], ["Very well, then,”", "그럼 좋아요.\""], ["she replied, almost without pause, and putting her hands to her left ear.", "그녀가 거의 쉴 틈도 없이 대답하며 두 손을 왼쪽 귀에 가져갔다."], ["“We will have nothing to do with any of them.", "\"그 어느 쪽도 거들떠보지 않기로 해요."], ["I have here what is much surer and better—the amulet which was given to some of our people—I cannot tell when, it was so far back—by a Persian magician.", "저한테 훨씬 더 확실하고 좋은 게 있어요. 우리 백성 가운데 누군가가 페르시아 마법사한테서 받은 부적이에요. 언제 받았는지는 모르겠어요, 너무 오래전 일이라."], ["See, the inscription is almost worn out.”", "보세요, 새겨진 글자가 거의 닳아 버렸어요.\""]], [["She offered him the earring, which he took, looked at, and handed back, laughing.", "그녀가 귀고리를 그에게 내밀자, 그는 그것을 받아 들여다보고는 웃으며 돌려주었다."]], [["“If I were dying, Tirzah, I could not use the charm. It is a relic of idolatry, forbidden every believing son and daughter of Abraham.", "\"내가 죽어 가는 처지라 해도, 티르자, 나는 그 부적을 쓰지 않을 거다. 그건 우상숭배의 유물이라, 아브라함의 모든 믿는 자손에게 금지된 것이다."], ["Take it, but do not wear it any more.”", "받아 두되, 다시는 차지 말거라.\""]], [["“Forbidden!", "\"금지라뇨!"], ["Not so,” she said.", "그렇지 않아요.\""], ["“Our father’s mother wore it I do not know how many Sabbaths in her life.", "그녀가 말했다. \"우리 할머니께서 평생 셀 수 없이 많은 안식일에 이걸 차셨어요."], ["It has cured I do not know how many people—more than three anyhow.", "이것이 몇 사람을 낫게 했는지도 모를 정도예요. 어쨌든 셋은 넘어요."], ["It is approved—look, here is the mark of the rabbis.”", "인정받은 거예요. 보세요, 여기 랍비들의 표시가 있잖아요.\""]], [["“I have no faith in amulets.”", "\"나는 부적 따위 믿지 않는다.\""]], [["She raised her eyes to his in astonishment.", "그녀는 놀라서 그에게 눈을 들었다."]], [["“What would Amrah say?”", "\"암라가 뭐라고 하겠어요?\""]], [["“Amrah’s father and mother tended sakiyeh for a garden on the Nile.”", "\"암라의 아버지와 어머니는 나일강가 어느 정원에서 물 긷는 두레박을 돌보았던 사람들이지.\""]], [["“But Gamaliel!”", "\"하지만 가말리엘은요!\""]], [["“He says they are godless inventions of unbelievers and Shechemites.”", "\"그분 말씀으로는 그건 불신자들과 세겜 사람들이 만들어 낸 불경한 발명품이라더구나.\""]], [["Tirzah looked at the ring doubtfully.", "티르자는 미심쩍은 눈으로 반지를 바라보았다."]], [["“What shall I do with it?”", "\"이걸 어쩌면 좋아요?\""]], [["“Wear it, my little sister.", "\"차거라, 내 어린 누이야."], ["It becomes you—it helps make you beautiful, though I think you that without help.”", "너한테 잘 어울린다. 그게 너를 더 아름답게 해 주지. 하기야 너는 그런 것 없이도 아름답다만.\""]], [["Satisfied, she returned the amulet to her ear just as Amrah entered the summer chamber, bearing a platter, with wash-bowl, water, and napkins.", "마음이 놓인 그녀는 암라가 대야와 물과 수건을 얹은 쟁반을 들고 여름 방으로 막 들어설 때, 부적을 다시 제 귀에 달았다."]], [["Not being a Pharisee, the ablution was short and simple with Judah.", "유다는 바리새인이 아닌지라, 손 씻는 의식은 짧고 단순했다."], ["The servant then went out, leaving Tirzah to dress his hair.", "그러고 나서 하인이 나가자, 티르자가 남아 그의 머리를 매만졌다."], ["When a lock was disposed to her satisfaction, she would unloose the small metallic mirror which, as was the fashion among her fair countrywomen, she wore at her girdle, and gave it to him, that he might see the triumph, and how handsome it made him.", "머리 한 가닥을 마음에 들게 손질하고 나면, 그녀는 그 고운 동포 여인들의 유행대로 허리띠에 차고 다니던 작은 쇠거울을 풀어 그에게 건넸다. 그가 그 솜씨를 보고, 그것이 자기를 얼마나 멋지게 만들어 주는지 알게 하려는 것이었다."], ["Meanwhile they kept up their conversation.", "그러는 동안 둘은 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“What do you think, Tirzah?—I am going away.”", "\"네 생각은 어떠냐, 티르자? 나는 떠날 거다.\""]], [["She dropped her hands with amazement.", "그녀는 놀라서 두 손을 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["“Going away! When? Where?", "\"떠난다고요! 언제요? 어디로요?"], ["For what?”", "무엇 때문에요?\""]], [["He laughed.", "그가 웃었다."]], [["“Three questions, all in a breath!", "\"한 숨에 질문이 셋이로구나!"], ["What a body you are!”", "너란 애는 어쩜 이러니!\""], ["Next instant he became serious.", "다음 순간 그는 진지해졌다."], ["“You know the law requires me to follow some occupation.", "\"율법이 나더러 어떤 생업에 종사하라고 요구하는 거 너도 알지."], ["Our good father set me an example.", "우리 어지신 아버지께서 본을 보여 주셨고."], ["Even you would despise me if I spent in idleness the results of his industry and knowledge.", "내가 아버지의 근면과 지혜가 거둔 결실을 게으름으로 흘려보낸다면, 너조차도 나를 경멸할 거다."], ["I am going to Rome.”", "나는 로마로 갈 거야.\""]], [["“Oh, I will go with you.”", "\"아, 저도 함께 갈래요.\""]], [["“You must stay with mother.", "\"너는 어머니 곁에 있어야지."], ["If both of us leave her she will die.”", "우리 둘 다 떠나면 어머니는 돌아가실 거다.\""]], [["The brightness faded from her face.", "그녀의 얼굴에서 밝은 빛이 사라졌다."]], [["“Ah, yes, yes! But—must you go?", "\"아, 그래요, 그래요! 하지만 꼭 가셔야 해요?"], ["Here in Jerusalem you can learn all that is needed to be a merchant—if that is what you are thinking of.”", "여기 예루살렘에서도 상인이 되는 데 필요한 건 다 배울 수 있잖아요. 오빠가 생각하는 게 그거라면요.\""]], [["“But that is not what I am thinking of.", "\"하지만 내가 생각하는 건 그게 아니다."], ["The law does not require the son to be what the father was.”", "율법이 아들더러 아버지와 같은 사람이 되라고 요구하는 건 아니거든.\""]], [["“What else can you be?”", "\"그럼 무엇이 될 수 있는데요?\""]], [["“A soldier,”", "\"군인.\""], ["he replied, with a certain pride of voice.", "그가 어딘가 자랑스러운 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["Tears came into her eyes.", "그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고였다."]], [["“You will be killed.”", "\"오빠는 죽고 말 거예요.\""]], [["“If God’s will, be it so. But, Tirzah, the soldiers are not all killed.”", "\"하느님의 뜻이라면, 그렇게 되겠지. 하지만 티르자, 군인이 다 죽는 건 아니다.\""]], [["She threw her arms around his neck, as if to hold him back.", "그녀는 그를 붙잡아 두려는 듯 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았다."]], [["“We are so happy! Stay at home, my brother.”", "\"우리는 이토록 행복한데! 집에 있어요, 오빠.\""]], [["“Home cannot always be what it is.", "\"집이 언제까지나 지금 같을 수는 없어."], ["You yourself will be going away before long.”", "너 자신도 머지않아 떠나게 될 거다.\""]], [["“Never!”", "\"절대로요!\""]], [["He smiled at her earnestness.", "그는 그녀의 간절함에 미소 지었다."]], [["“A prince of Judah, or some other of one of the tribes, will come soon and claim my Tirzah, and ride away with her, to be the light of another house.", "\"유다 지파의 왕자나, 아니면 다른 지파의 누군가가 곧 와서 내 티르자를 제 짝이라 청하고는, 너를 태워 떠나 또 다른 집의 빛으로 삼겠지."], ["What will then become of me?”", "그럼 나는 어떻게 되겠니?\""]], [["She answered with sobs.", "그녀는 흐느낌으로 대답했다."]], [["“War is a trade,”", "\"전쟁은 하나의 직업이다.\""], ["he continued, more soberly.", "그가 좀 더 차분하게 말을 이었다."], ["“To learn it thoroughly, one must go to school, and there is no school like a Roman camp.”", "\"그것을 제대로 배우려면 학교에 가야 하는데, 로마 군영만 한 학교가 없거든.\""]], [["“You would not fight for Rome?”", "\"오빠가 로마를 위해 싸우진 않을 거죠?\""], ["she asked, holding her breath.", "그녀가 숨을 죽이며 물었다."]], [["“And you—even you hate her.", "\"너도, 너조차도 로마를 미워하지."], ["The whole world hates her.", "온 세상이 로마를 미워해."], ["In that, O Tirzah, find the reason of the answer I give you— Yes, I will fight for her, if, in return, she will teach me how one day to fight against her.”", "바로 거기에, 오 티르자, 내가 너한테 주는 대답의 까닭이 있단다. 그래, 나는 로마를 위해 싸울 거다. 만약 그 대가로 로마가 나에게 언젠가 로마와 맞서 싸우는 법을 가르쳐 준다면 말이야.\""]], [["“When will you go?”", "\"언제 떠나실 거예요?\""]], [["Amrah’s steps were then heard returning.", "그때 돌아오는 암라의 발소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Hist!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Do not let her know of what I am thinking.”", "\"내가 무슨 생각을 하는지 암라가 알게 하지 마라.\""]], [["The faithful slave came in with breakfast, and placed the waiter holding it upon a stool before them; then, with white napkins upon her arm, she remained to serve them.", "충직한 하녀가 아침 식사를 들고 들어와, 그것을 받친 쟁반을 그들 앞 걸상 위에 놓았다. 그러고는 흰 수건을 팔에 걸친 채 시중들려고 남았다."], ["They dipped their fingers in a bowl of water, and were rinsing them, when a noise arrested their attention.", "그들이 물그릇에 손가락을 담가 헹구고 있을 때, 어떤 소리가 그들의 주의를 끌었다."], ["They listened, and distinguished martial music in the street on the north side of the house.", "귀를 기울이니, 집 북쪽 거리에서 군악 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Soldiers from the Praetorium!", "\"프라이토리움에서 온 병사들이다!"], ["I must see them,” he cried, springing from the divan, and running out.", "봐야겠어.\" 그가 외치며 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 밖으로 뛰어나갔다."]], [["In a moment more he was leaning over the parapet of tiles which guarded the roof at the extreme northeast corner, so absorbed that he did not notice Tirzah by his side, resting one hand upon his shoulder.", "다음 순간 그는 지붕의 북동쪽 맨 끝 모서리를 두르고 있는 기와 난간에 몸을 기대고 있었으니, 어찌나 정신이 팔렸던지 티르자가 곁에 와 한 손을 그의 어깨에 얹은 것도 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["Their position—the roof being the highest one in the locality—commanded the house-tops eastward as far as the huge irregular Tower of Antonia, which has been already mentioned as a citadel for the garrison and military headquarters for the governor.", "그들이 선 자리, 곧 그 일대에서 가장 높은 지붕에서는 동쪽으로 집들의 지붕이 한눈에 내려다보였고, 멀리 안토니아 탑의 거대하고 들쭉날쭉한 모습까지 보였다. 그 탑은 앞서 말했듯이 수비대의 성채이자 총독의 군 사령부였다."], ["The street, not more than ten feet wide, was spanned here and there by bridges, open and covered, which, like the roofs along the way, were beginning to be occupied by men, women, and children, called out by the music.", "너비가 채 3미터도 안 되는 그 거리에는 군데군데 다리가 걸쳐 있었는데, 트인 것도 있고 지붕 덮인 것도 있었다. 길가의 지붕들과 마찬가지로 그 다리들도 음악 소리에 불려 나온 남자와 여자와 아이들로 차기 시작했다."], ["The word is used, though it is hardly fitting; what the people heard when they came forth was rather an uproar of trumpets and the shriller litui so delightful to the soldiers.", "음악이라는 말을 썼지만, 그리 어울리는 말은 아니다. 사람들이 나왔을 때 들은 것은 차라리 나팔의 요란한 소리와, 병사들이 그토록 좋아하는 더 날카로운 리투우스 소리의 소란이었다."]], [["The array after a while came into view of the two upon the house of the Hurs.", "이윽고 행렬이 후르 집안의 두 사람 눈에 들어왔다. 맨 앞은 경무장한 전위대로, 대부분 투석병과 궁수였는데, 열과 오 사이를 넓게 벌리고 행진했다."], ["First, a vanguard of the light-armed—mostly slingers and bowmen—marching with wide intervals between their ranks and files; next a body of heavy-armed infantry, bearing large shields, and hastoe longoe, or spears identical with those used in the duels before Ilium; then the musicians; and then an officer riding alone, but followed closely by a guard of cavalry; after them again, a column of infantry also heavy-armed, which, moving in close order, crowded the streets from wall to wall, and appeared to be without end.", "다음은 중무장한 보병 부대로, 큰 방패와 하스타 롱가, 곧 일리움 앞 결투에서 쓰이던 것과 똑같은 창을 들고 있었다. 그다음은 악사들, 그다음은 홀로 말을 탄 장교 하나였는데, 기병대 호위가 바짝 뒤따랐다. 그 뒤에는 다시 역시 중무장한 보병의 종대가 이어졌는데, 빽빽한 대열로 움직이며 벽에서 벽까지 거리를 가득 메웠고, 끝이 없어 보였다."]], [["The brawny limbs of the men; the cadenced motion from right to left of the shields; the sparkle of scales, buckles, and breastplates and helms, all perfectly burnished; the plumes nodding above the tall crests; the sway of ensigns and iron-shod spears; the bold, confident step, exactly timed and measured; the demeanor, so grave, yet so watchful; the machine-like unity of the whole moving mass—made an impression upon Judah, but as something felt rather than seen.", "병사들의 우람한 팔다리, 좌우로 박자 맞춰 움직이는 방패, 완벽하게 닦인 비늘 갑옷과 죔쇠와 흉갑과 투구의 번쩍임, 높은 투구 장식 위로 까딱이는 깃털, 흔들리는 군기와 쇠 박은 창, 정확히 박자 맞춘 대담하고 자신만만한 걸음걸이, 그토록 엄숙하면서도 그토록 빈틈없는 태도, 움직이는 무리 전체의 기계 같은 일사불란함, 이 모든 것이 유다에게 인상을 남겼으나, 그것은 본 것이라기보다 느낀 것에 가까웠다. 두 가지가 그의 눈길을 붙들었다."], ["Two objects fixed his attention—the eagle of the legion first—a gilded effigy perched on a tall shaft, with wings outspread until they met above its head.", "첫째는 군단의 독수리였다. 그것은 긴 장대 위에 앉은 도금된 형상으로, 두 날개를 펴서 머리 위에서 서로 맞닿게 한 것이었다."], ["He knew that, when brought from its chamber in the Tower, it had been received with divine honors.", "그는 그것이 탑 안의 보관실에서 나올 때면 신에게나 바칠 예우를 받았다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["The officer riding alone in the midst of the column was the other attraction.", "종대 한가운데서 홀로 말을 탄 장교가 또 하나의 볼거리였다."], ["His head was bare; otherwise he was in full armor. At his left hip he wore a short sword; in his hand, however, he carried a truncheon, which looked like a roll of white paper.", "그는 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않았으나, 그 밖에는 완전 무장을 하고 있었다. 왼쪽 허리에는 짧은 검을 찼는데, 손에는 흰 종이 두루마리처럼 보이는 지휘봉을 들고 있었다."], ["He sat upon a purple cloth instead of a saddle, and that, and a bridle with a forestall of gold and reins of yellow silk broadly fringed at the lower edge, completed the housings of the horse.", "그는 안장 대신 자줏빛 천 위에 앉아 있었고, 그것과, 앞머리를 금으로 장식하고 아래 가장자리에 넓게 술을 단 노란 비단 고삐를 단 굴레가 그 말의 마구를 이루고 있었다."]], [["While the man was yet in the distance, Judah observed that his presence was sufficient to throw the people looking at him into angry excitement.", "그 사람이 아직 멀리 있을 때부터, 유다는 그의 등장만으로도 그를 바라보던 사람들이 성난 흥분에 휩싸이는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["They would lean over the parapets or stand boldly out, and shake their fists at him; they followed him with loud cries, and spit at him as he passed under the bridges; the women even flung their sandals, sometimes with such good effect as to hit him.", "그들은 난간 너머로 몸을 내밀거나 대담하게 앞으로 나서서 그를 향해 주먹을 흔들었고, 큰 소리로 외치며 그를 뒤쫓았으며, 그가 다리 밑을 지날 때면 침을 뱉었다. 여자들은 신발까지 내던졌는데, 더러는 제대로 명중시킬 만큼 솜씨가 좋았다."], ["When he was nearer, the yells became distinguishable—“Robber, tyrant, dog of a Roman!", "그가 더 가까이 다가오자, 외침이 알아들을 만해졌다. \"강도, 폭군, 로마의 개!"], ["Away with Ishmael!", "이스마엘은 물러가라!"], ["Give us back our Hannas!”", "우리 한나스를 돌려다오!\""]], [["When quite near, Judah could see that, as was but natural, the man did not share the indifference so superbly shown by the soldiers; his face was dark and sullen, and the glances he occasionally cast at his persecutors were full of menace; the very timid shrank from them.", "아주 가까워지자, 유다는 당연한 일이지만, 그 사람이 병사들처럼 그토록 당당하게 드러내는 무관심을 함께하지 못함을 볼 수 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 어둡고 시무룩했고, 이따금 박해자들에게 던지는 눈길은 위협으로 가득 차 있어, 겁 많은 자들은 그 앞에서 움츠러들었다."]], [["Now the lad had heard of the custom, borrowed from a habit of the first Cæsar, by which chief commanders, to indicate their rank, appeared in public with only a laurel vine upon their heads.", "그런데 소년은 첫 카이사르의 습관에서 비롯된 한 관례를 들어 알고 있었다. 그 관례에 따르면 총사령관들은 제 지위를 나타내려고 머리에 월계관만 쓰고 공식 석상에 나타났다."], ["By that sign he knew this officer—VALERIUS GRATUS, THE NEW PROCURATOR OF JUDEA!", "그 표시로 그는 이 장교가 누구인지 알아보았다. 발레리우스 그라투스, 유대의 새 총독이었다!"]], [["To say truth now, the Roman under the unprovoked storm had the young Jew’s sympathy; so that when he reached the corner of the house, the latter leaned yet farther over the parapet to see him go by, and in the act rested a hand upon a tile which had been a long time cracked and allowed to go unnoticed.", "이제 솔직히 말하자면, 까닭 없이 쏟아지는 그 폭풍 아래 놓인 그 로마인은 젊은 유대인의 동정을 샀다. 그래서 그가 집 모서리에 이르렀을 때, 유다는 그가 지나가는 모습을 보려고 난간 너머로 한층 더 몸을 내밀었고, 그러는 김에 오래전부터 금이 가 있었으나 눈여겨보지 않고 내버려 둔 기와 한 장에 손을 짚었다."], ["The pressure was strong enough to displace the outer piece, which started to fall.", "그 누름이 충분히 강했던지 바깥쪽 조각이 밀려나며 떨어지기 시작했다."], ["A thrill of horror shot through the youth.", "전율과 같은 공포가 소년을 꿰뚫었다."], ["He reached out to catch the missile.", "그는 떨어지는 그것을 붙잡으려고 손을 뻗었다."], ["In appearance the motion was exactly that of one pitching something from him.", "그 동작이 겉보기에는 영락없이 무언가를 자기에게서 내던지는 모습이었다."], ["The effort failed—nay, it served to push the descending fragment farther out over the wall.", "애는 허사로 돌아갔고, 아니, 오히려 떨어지는 조각을 벽 너머로 더 멀리 밀어내고 말았다."], ["He shouted with all his might.", "그는 있는 힘을 다해 소리쳤다."], ["The soldiers of the guard looked up; so did the great man, and that moment the missile struck him, and he fell from his seat as dead.", "호위 병사들이 위를 올려다보았고, 그 높은 사람도 그러했는데, 바로 그 순간 그 조각이 그를 때렸고, 그는 죽은 듯 자리에서 떨어졌다."]], [["The cohort halted; the guards leaped from their horses, and hastened to cover the chief with their shields.", "대대가 멈춰 섰다. 호위병들이 말에서 뛰어내려 방패로 사령관을 덮으려고 서둘렀다."], ["On the other hand, the people who witnessed the affair, never doubting that the blow had been purposely dealt, cheered the lad as he yet stooped in full view over the parapet, transfixed by what he beheld, and by anticipation of the consequences flashed all too plainly upon him.", "한편, 그 일을 목격한 사람들은 그 일격이 고의로 가해진 것이라 조금도 의심하지 않고, 난간 너머로 몸을 굽힌 채 자기가 본 광경에, 그리고 너무도 또렷이 떠오르는 결과의 예감에 못 박혀 있는 소년에게 환호를 보냈다."]], [["A mischievous spirit flew with incredible speed from roof to roof along the line of march, seizing the people, and urging them all alike.", "행진 노선을 따라 지붕에서 지붕으로 믿기 어려운 속도로 짓궂은 기운이 날아가, 사람들을 사로잡고 너 나 할 것 없이 부추겼다."], ["They laid hands upon the parapets and tore up the tiling and the sunburnt mud of which the house-tops were for the most part made, and with blind fury began to fling them upon the legionaries halted below.", "그들은 난간에 손을 대어 기와와, 지붕을 대부분 이루고 있던 햇볕에 구운 진흙을 뜯어내, 눈먼 광기로 아래 멈춰 선 군단병들 위로 내던지기 시작했다."], ["A battle then ensued. Discipline, of course, prevailed.", "그리하여 싸움이 벌어졌다. 물론 규율이 이겼다."], ["The struggle, the slaughter, the skill of one side, the desperation of the other, are alike unnecessary to our story. Let us look rather to the wretched author of it all.", "그 다툼, 그 살육, 한편의 솜씨, 다른 편의 필사적임은 모두 우리 이야기에 필요치 않다. 차라리 이 모든 일을 빚어낸 그 가련한 장본인에게 눈길을 돌리기로 하자."]], [["He arose from the parapet, his face very pale.", "그는 난간에서 몸을 일으켰으니, 얼굴이 몹시 창백했다."]], [["“O Tirzah, Tirzah!", "\"오 티르자, 티르자!"], ["What will become of us?”", "우리는 어떻게 되는 거지?\""]], [["She had not seen the occurrence below, but was listening to the shouting and watching the mad activity of the people in view on the houses.", "그녀는 아래에서 벌어진 일을 보지 못한 채, 외치는 소리에 귀를 기울이고 눈에 보이는 지붕 위 사람들의 미친 듯한 움직임을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Something terrible was going on, she knew; but what it was, or the cause, or that she or any of those dear to her were in danger, she did not know.", "무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지고 있다는 것은 알았으나, 그것이 무엇인지, 그 까닭이 무엇인지, 혹은 자기나 자기에게 소중한 누군가가 위험에 처했다는 것은 알지 못했다."]], [["“What has happened?", "\"무슨 일이에요?"], ["What does it all mean?”", "이게 다 무슨 뜻이에요?\""], ["she asked, in sudden alarm.", "그녀가 갑작스러운 놀라움에 물었다."]], [["“I have killed the Roman governor.", "\"내가 로마 총독을 죽이고 말았다."], ["The tile fell upon him.”", "기와가 그를 덮쳤어.\""]], [["An unseen hand appeared to sprinkle her face with the dust of ashes—it grew white so instantly.", "보이지 않는 손이 그녀의 얼굴에 잿가루를 뿌린 듯, 얼굴이 그토록 순식간에 하얗게 질렸다."], ["She put her arm around him, and looked wistfully, but without a word, into his eyes.", "그녀는 한 팔로 그를 끌어안고, 말없이 그저 애절하게 그의 눈을 들여다보았다."], ["His fears had passed to her, and the sight of them gave him strength.", "그의 두려움이 그녀에게 옮아갔고, 그 두려움을 보자 그는 도리어 힘을 얻었다."]], [["“I did not do it purposely, Tirzah—it was an accident,”", "\"일부러 그런 게 아니다, 티르자. 사고였어.\""], ["he said, more calmly.", "그가 좀 더 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“What will they do?”", "\"저들이 어떻게 할까요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["He looked off over the tumult momentarily deepening in the street and on the roofs, and thought of the sullen countenance of Gratus.", "그는 거리와 지붕 위로 점점 거세지는 소란을 잠깐 내다보며, 그라투스의 시무룩하던 얼굴을 떠올렸다."], ["If he were not dead, where would his vengeance stop?", "그가 죽지 않았다면, 그 복수가 어디서 멈출 것인가?"], ["And if he were dead, to what height of fury would not the violence of the people lash the legionaries?", "그리고 그가 죽었다면, 사람들의 폭력이 군단병들을 어느 정도의 광란으로 몰아갈 것인가?"], ["To evade an answer, he peered over the parapet again, just as the guard were assisting the Roman to remount his horse.", "대답을 피하려고, 그는 다시 난간 너머를 엿보았으니, 마침 호위병들이 그 로마인을 부축해 말에 다시 태우고 있었다."]], [["“He lives, he lives, Tirzah!", "\"살아 있다, 살아 있어, 티르자!"], ["Blessed be the Lord God of our fathers!”", "우리 조상의 주 하느님께 찬미를!\""]], [["With that outcry, and a brightened countenance, he drew back and replied to her question.", "그렇게 외치며, 그는 환해진 얼굴로 몸을 물려 그녀의 질문에 답했다."]], [["“Be not afraid, Tirzah.", "\"두려워 마라, 티르자."], ["I will explain how it happened, and they will remember our father and his services, and not hurt us.”", "어쩌다 그리 됐는지 내가 설명하면, 저들도 우리 아버지와 그 공로를 기억하고 우리를 해치지 않을 거다.\""]], [["He was leading her to the summer-house, when the roof jarred under their feet, and a crash of strong timbers being burst away, followed by a cry of surprise and agony, arose apparently from the court-yard below.", "그가 그녀를 여름 별채로 데려가려 할 때, 발밑에서 지붕이 흔들렸다. 굵은 들보들이 부서져 나가는 요란한 소리에 이어, 놀라움과 고통이 뒤섞인 비명이 분명 아래 안뜰 쪽에서 솟아올랐다. 그는 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["He stopped and listened.", "비명이 거듭되었다."], ["The cry was repeated; then came a rush of many feet, and voices lifted in rage blent with voices in prayer; and then the screams of women in mortal terror.", "그러더니 많은 발소리가 한꺼번에 몰려들고, 분노로 높아진 목소리가 기도하는 목소리와 뒤섞였으며, 이어서 죽음의 공포에 사로잡힌 여인들의 비명이 들렸다."], ["The soldiers had beaten in the north gate, and were in possession of the house.", "병사들이 북쪽 문을 부수고 들이쳐 집을 점령한 것이었다."], ["The terrible sense of being hunted smote him.", "사냥당하고 있다는 무서운 느낌이 그를 후려쳤다."], ["His first impulse was to fly; but where?", "그의 첫 충동은 달아나는 것이었으나, 어디로?"], ["Nothing but wings would serve him.", "날개가 아니고서는 소용없을 일이었다."], ["Tirzah, her eyes wild with fear, caught his arm.", "티르자가 두려움으로 눈이 휘둥그레져서 그의 팔을 붙잡았다."]], [["“O Judah, what does it mean?”", "\"오 유다, 이게 무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["The servants were being butchered—and his mother!", "하인들이 살육당하고 있었다. 그리고 어머니가!"], ["Was not one of the voices he heard hers?", "그가 들은 목소리 가운데 하나가 어머니의 것이 아니던가?"], ["With all the will left him, he said, “Stay here, and wait for me, Tirzah.", "남은 의지를 다 짜내어 그가 말했다. \"여기 있어라, 그리고 나를 기다려, 티르자."], ["I will go down and see what is the matter, and come back to you.”", "내가 내려가서 무슨 일인지 보고 너에게 돌아오마.\""]], [["His voice was not steady as he wished.", "그의 목소리는 그가 바라는 만큼 굳건하지 못했다."], ["She clung closer to him.", "그녀는 그에게 더욱 바싹 매달렸다."]], [["Clearer, shriller, no longer a fancy, his mother’s cry arose.", "더 또렷하고, 더 날카롭게, 이제는 착각이 아닌, 어머니의 외침이 솟아올랐다."], ["He hesitated no longer.", "그는 더는 망설이지 않았다."]], [["“Come, then, let us go.”", "\"그럼 가자, 함께 가자.\""]], [["The terrace or gallery at the foot of the steps was crowded with soldiers.", "층계 아래 테라스, 곧 회랑은 병사들로 붐볐다."], ["Other soldiers with drawn swords ran in and out of the chambers.", "다른 병사들은 칼을 빼 들고 방마다 들락거렸다."], ["At one place a number of women on their knees clung to each other or prayed for mercy.", "한쪽에서는 여러 여인이 무릎을 꿇고 서로 부둥켜안거나 자비를 빌고 있었다."], ["Apart from them, one with torn garments, and long hair streaming over her face, struggled to tear loose from a man all whose strength was tasked to keep his hold.", "그들과 떨어진 곳에서, 옷이 찢기고 긴 머리카락이 얼굴을 덮어 흘러내린 한 여인이 한 남자에게서 벗어나려고 몸부림치고 있었는데, 그 남자는 붙든 손을 놓지 않으려고 온 힘을 쏟고 있었다."], ["Her cries were shrillest of all; cutting through the clamor, they had risen distinguishably to the roof.", "그녀의 외침은 그 가운데서도 가장 날카로워, 그 소란을 뚫고 지붕에까지 또렷이 솟아올랐던 것이다."], ["To her Judah sprang—his steps were long and swift, almost a winged flight—“Mother, mother!”", "그녀를 향해 유다가 달려갔으니, 그의 걸음은 길고 빨라, 거의 날아가는 듯했다. \"어머니, 어머니!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["She stretched her hands towards him; but when almost touching them he was seized and forced aside.", "그녀가 두 손을 그에게 뻗었으나, 그가 거의 그 손에 닿으려는 순간 그는 붙들려 한쪽으로 떼밀렸다."], ["Then he heard some one say, speaking loudly,", "그때 누군가 큰 소리로 말하는 것이 들렸다."]], [["“That is he!”", "\"저자가 바로 그놈이다!\""]], [["Judah looked, and saw—Messala.", "유다가 바라보니, 메살라였다."]], [["“What, the assassin—that?”", "\"뭐라고, 저 암살자가, 저게?\""], ["said a tall man, in legionary armor of beautiful finish. “Why, he is but a boy.”", "아름답게 마무리된 군단병 갑옷을 입은 키 큰 사람이 말했다. \"원, 한낱 소년에 지나지 않는데.\""]], [["“Gods!” replied Messala, not forgetting his drawl.", "\"신들이시여!\" 메살라가 그 느릿한 말투를 잊지 않고 대꾸했다."], ["“A new philosophy!", "\"참 새로운 철학이로군!"], ["What would Seneca say to the proposition that a man must be old before he can hate enough to kill?", "사람이 죽일 만큼 미워하려면 먼저 늙어야 한다는 주장에 세네카라면 뭐라고 하겠나?"], ["You have him; and that is his mother; yonder his sister.", "자네는 저놈을 잡았고, 저것이 그 어미요, 저쪽이 누이일세."], ["You have the whole family.”", "자네는 온 가족을 다 잡은 거야.\""]], [["For love of them, Judah forgot his quarrel.", "그들을 사랑하는 마음에, 유다는 제 원한을 잊었다."]], [["“Help them, O my Messala!", "\"저들을 도와다오, 오 내 메살라여!"], ["Remember our childhood and help them.", "우리 어린 시절을 기억하고 저들을 도와다오."], ["I—Judah—pray you.”", "나, 유다가, 너에게 비노라.\""]], [["Messala affected not to hear.", "메살라는 못 들은 체했다."]], [["“I cannot be of further use to you,”", "\"내가 자네한테 더 도움이 될 일은 없겠군.\""], ["he said to the officer.", "그가 장교에게 말했다."], ["“There is richer entertainment in the street. Down Eros, up Mars!”", "\"거리에 더 푸짐한 구경거리가 있으니. 에로스는 내려가고, 마르스는 올라오라!\""]], [["With the last words he disappeared.", "마지막 말과 함께 그는 사라졌다."], ["Judah understood him, and, in the bitterness of his soul, prayed to Heaven.", "유다는 그의 말뜻을 알아들었고, 영혼의 쓰라림 속에서 하늘에 기도했다."]], [["“In the hour of thy vengeance, O Lord,”", "\"당신의 복수의 때에, 오 주여,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “be mine the hand to put it upon him!”", "\"그것을 그자에게 내릴 손이 제 손이 되게 하소서!\""]], [["By great exertion, he drew nearer the officer.", "그는 안간힘을 써서 장교에게 더 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["“O sir, the woman you hear is my mother.", "\"오 나리, 당신이 듣고 있는 저 여인은 제 어머니입니다."], ["Spare her, spare my sister yonder.", "어머니를 살려 주십시오, 저쪽 제 누이를 살려 주십시오."], ["God is just, he will give you mercy for mercy.”", "하느님은 공정하시니, 자비에는 자비로 갚아 주실 것입니다.\""]], [["The man appeared to be moved.", "그 사람은 마음이 움직인 듯했다."]], [["“To the Tower with the women!”", "\"여자들은 탑으로 데려가라!\""], ["he shouted, “but do them no harm.", "그가 외쳤다. \"다만 해치지는 마라."], ["I will demand them of you.”", "내가 너희에게 그들을 따로 요구할 테니.\""], ["Then to those holding Judah, he said, “Get cords, and bind his hands, and take him to the street.", "그러고 나서 유다를 붙들고 있는 자들에게 말했다. \"밧줄을 가져와 그 손을 묶고, 거리로 끌고 가라."], ["His punishment is reserved.”", "이놈의 형벌은 따로 정해 두마.\""]], [["The mother was carried away.", "어머니는 끌려갔다."], ["The little Tirzah, in her home attire, stupefied with fear, went passively with her keepers.", "어린 티르자는 집에서 입던 차림 그대로, 두려움에 얼이 빠져 붙잡은 자들에게 순순히 따라갔다."], ["Judah gave each of them a last look, and covered his face with his hands, as if to possess himself of the scene fadelessly.", "유다는 두 사람을 마지막으로 한 번씩 바라보고는, 그 광경을 영영 지워지지 않게 제 안에 담아 두려는 듯, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["He may have shed tears, though no one saw them.", "그는 어쩌면 눈물을 흘렸을지도 모른다, 비록 본 사람은 아무도 없었지만."]], [["There took place in him then what may be justly called the wonder of life.", "그때 그의 안에서, 마땅히 삶의 경이라 부를 만한 일이 일어났다."], ["The thoughtful reader of these pages has ere this discerned enough to know that the young Jew in disposition was gentle even to womanliness—a result that seldom fails the habit of loving and being loved.", "이 글을 사려 깊게 읽어 온 독자라면 이미, 그 젊은 유대인의 성품이 여자다울 만큼 온순했다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 보았을 것이다. 그것은 사랑하고 사랑받는 습관에서 좀처럼 어긋나지 않는 결과다."], ["The circumstances through which he had come had made no call upon the harsher elements of his nature, if such he had.", "그가 거쳐 온 형편은, 설령 그에게 더 거친 요소가 있었다 한들, 그것을 불러내지 않았다."], ["At times he had felt the stir and impulses of ambition, but they had been like the formless dreams of a child walking by the sea and gazing at the coming and going of stately ships.", "때때로 그는 야망의 들썩임과 충동을 느꼈으나, 그것은 바닷가를 거닐며 위엄 있는 배들이 오가는 모습을 바라보는 아이의 형체 없는 꿈과 같았다."], ["But now, if we can imagine an idol, sensible of the worship it was accustomed to, dashed suddenly from its altar, and lying amidst the wreck of its little world of love, an idea may be had of what had befallen the young Ben-Hur, and of its effect upon his being.", "그러나 이제, 익숙히 받던 숭배를 의식하던 한 우상이 별안간 제단에서 내동댕이쳐져, 무너져 버린 제 작은 사랑의 세계 한가운데 나뒹구는 모습을 상상할 수 있다면, 젊은 벤허에게 닥친 일과 그것이 그의 존재에 미친 영향을 짐작할 수 있을 것이다."], ["Yet there was no sign, nothing to indicate that he had undergone a change, except that when he raised his head, and held his arms out to be bound, the bend of the Cupid’s bow had vanished from his lips.", "그러나 아무 기색도, 그가 어떤 변화를 겪었음을 드러내는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 다만 그가 고개를 들어 묶이도록 두 팔을 내밀었을 때, 그의 입술에서 큐피드의 활처럼 휘었던 선이 사라졌을 뿐이다."], ["In that instant he had put off childhood and become a man.", "그 순간 그는 어린 시절을 벗어 버리고 한 사내가 되었다."]], [["A trumpet sounded in the court-yard.", "안뜰에서 나팔 소리가 울렸다."], ["With the cessation of the call, the gallery was cleared of the soldiery; many of whom, as they dared not appear in the ranks with visible plunder in their hands, flung what they had upon the floor, until it was strewn with articles of richest virtu.", "그 신호가 그치자, 회랑에서 병사들이 물러갔다. 그들 가운데 많은 이가 약탈물을 손에 든 채로는 감히 대열에 나설 수 없었던지라, 손에 쥔 것을 바닥에 내던졌고, 마침내 바닥은 더없이 값진 골동품으로 어지러이 흩어졌다."], ["When Judah descended, the formation was complete, and the officer waiting to see his last order executed.", "유다가 내려왔을 때, 대형은 이미 갖추어져 있었고, 장교는 자기의 마지막 명령이 이행되는 것을 지켜보려 기다리고 있었다."]], [["The mother, daughter, and entire household were led out of the north gate, the ruins of which choked the passageway.", "어머니와 딸과 온 집안 식구가 북쪽 문 밖으로 끌려 나갔는데, 그 부서진 잔해가 통로를 가로막고 있었다."], ["The cries of the domestics, some of whom had been born in the house, were most pitiable.", "하인들의 울부짖음은, 그 가운데 더러는 이 집에서 태어난 이들이라, 더없이 가여웠다."], ["When, finally, the horses and all the dumb tenantry of the place were driven past him, Judah began to comprehend the scope of the procurator’s vengeance.", "마침내 말들과 이 집의 말 못 하는 짐승들까지 모조리 그의 앞으로 몰려 지나가자, 유다는 총독의 복수가 어디까지 미치는지 헤아리기 시작했다."], ["The very structure was devoted.", "건물 자체가 멸망에 바쳐진 것이었다."], ["Far as the order was possible of execution, nothing living was to be left within its walls.", "명령을 이행할 수 있는 한, 그 담장 안에 살아 있는 것은 아무것도 남기지 않으려는 것이었다."], ["If in Judea there were others desperate enough to think of assassinating a Roman governor, the story of what befell the princely family of Hur would be a warning to them, while the ruin of the habitation would keep the story alive.", "만약 유대에 로마 총독을 암살할 생각을 품을 만큼 무모한 자가 또 있다면, 후르의 명문가에 닥친 일의 이야기가 그들에게 경고가 될 터였고, 그 집의 폐허는 그 이야기를 두고두고 살아 있게 할 터였다."]], [["The officer waited outside while a detail of men temporarily restored the gate.", "한 무리의 병사가 임시로 문을 다시 세우는 동안, 장교는 밖에서 기다렸다."]], [["In the street the fighting had almost ceased.", "거리에서는 싸움이 거의 그쳐 있었다."], ["Upon the houses here and there clouds of dust told where the struggle was yet prolonged.", "집들 위 여기저기 솟은 먼지구름이 아직 다툼이 이어지는 곳을 알려 주었다."], ["The cohort was, for the most part, standing at rest, its splendor, like its ranks, in nowise diminished.", "대대는 대부분 쉬어 자세로 서 있었는데, 그 위용도 그 대열도 조금도 줄지 않았다."], ["Borne past the point of care for himself, Judah had heart for nothing in view but the prisoners, among whom he looked in vain for his mother and Tirzah.", "제 한 몸을 걱정하는 단계는 이미 지나친 터라, 유다는 눈앞의 그 무엇에도 마음이 가지 않고 오직 붙잡힌 사람들에게만 마음이 쏠렸으나, 그 가운데서 어머니와 티르자를 찾으려 해도 헛일이었다."]], [["Suddenly, from the earth where she had been lying, a woman arose and started swiftly back to the gate.", "별안간, 누워 있던 땅에서 한 여인이 일어나 빠르게 문 쪽으로 되달려 갔다."], ["Some of the guards reached out to seize her, and a great shout followed their failure.", "호위병 몇이 그녀를 붙잡으려 손을 뻗었으나, 그것이 헛수고로 돌아가자 커다란 함성이 뒤따랐다."], ["She ran to Judah, and, dropping down, clasped his knees, the coarse black hair powdered with dust veiling her eyes.", "그녀는 유다에게 달려와 주저앉으며 그의 무릎을 끌어안았으니, 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 거친 검은 머리카락이 그녀의 눈을 가렸다."]], [["“O Amrah, good Amrah,”", "\"오 암라, 어진 암라.\""], ["he said to her, “God help you; I cannot.”", "그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"하느님께서 너를 도우시기를. 나는 그럴 수 없구나.\""]], [["She could not speak.", "그녀는 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["He bent down, and whispered, “Live, Amrah, for Tirzah and my mother.", "그가 몸을 굽혀 속삭였다. \"살아라, 암라, 티르자와 어머니를 위해."], ["They will come back, and—”", "두 사람은 돌아올 거다, 그리고—\""]], [["A soldier drew her away; whereupon she sprang up and rushed through the gateway and passage into the vacant court-yard.", "한 병사가 그녀를 끌어냈다. 그러자 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 문과 통로를 지나 텅 빈 안뜰 안으로 달려 들어갔다."]], [["“Let her go,” the officer shouted.", "\"내버려 둬라.\" 장교가 외쳤다."], ["“We will seal the house, and she will starve.”", "\"집을 봉인할 테니, 저 여자는 굶어 죽을 거다.\""]], [["The men resumed their work, and, when it was finished there, passed round to the west side.", "병사들은 다시 일을 시작했고, 그곳 일이 끝나자 서쪽으로 돌아갔다."], ["That gate was also secured, after which the palace of the Hurs was lost to use.", "그 문 또한 단단히 봉했으니, 그로써 후르의 저택은 더는 쓸 수 없게 되었다."]], [["The cohort at length marched back to the Tower, where the procurator stayed to recover from his hurts and dispose of his prisoners. On the tenth day following, he visited the Market-place.", "대대는 마침내 탑으로 돌아갔고, 총독은 그곳에 머물며 상처를 추스르고 포로들을 처분했다. 그로부터 열흘째 되는 날, 그는 시장에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["Next day a detachment of legionaries went to the desolated palace, and, closing the gates permanently, plastered the corners with wax, and at the sides nailed a notice in Latin:", "이튿날 군단병 한 무리가 황폐해진 저택으로 가서 대문을 영구히 봉쇄하고, 모서리마다 밀랍을 발랐으며, 그 곁에 라틴어로 쓴 고시문을 박아 두었다."]], [["In the haughty Roman idea, the sententious announcement was thought sufficient for the purpose—and it was.", "“이것은 황제의 소유물이다.” 오만한 로마인의 사고로는, 이 간결한 선언만으로도 충분하다고 여겨졌다. 과연 그러했다."]], [["The day after that again, about noon, a decurion with his command of ten horsemen approached Nazareth from the south—that is, from the direction of Jerusalem.", "그 다음 날, 정오 무렵에, 부관 한 사람이 열 명의 기병을 거느리고 남쪽에서, 곧 예루살렘 방향에서 나사렛으로 다가왔다."], ["The place was then a straggling village, perched on a hill-side, and so insignificant that its one street was little more than a path well beaten by the coming and going of flocks and herds.", "그곳은 그 무렵 산비탈에 걸터앉은 띄엄띄엄한 마을로, 어찌나 보잘것없는지 단 하나뿐인 거리도 양 떼와 소 떼가 오가며 다져 놓은 오솔길이나 다름없었다."], ["The great plain of Esdraelon crept close to it on the south, and from the height on the west a view could be had of the shores of the Mediterranean, the region beyond the Jordan, and Hermon.", "거대한 에스드라엘론 평원이 남쪽으로 바짝 다가와 있었고, 서쪽 높은 곳에서는 지중해 연안과 요르단 너머의 땅, 그리고 헤르몬산을 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["The valley below, and the country on every side, were given to gardens, vineyards, orchards, and pasturage.", "아래쪽 골짜기와 사방의 들녘은 텃밭과 포도밭, 과수원과 목초지로 채워져 있었다."], ["Groves of palm-trees Orientalized the landscape.", "종려나무 숲이 풍경에 동방의 정취를 더했다."], ["The houses, in irregular assemblage, were of the humbler class—square, one-story, flat-roofed, and covered with bright-green vines.", "들쭉날쭉 모여 선 집들은 비천한 부류에 속하는 것들로, 네모반듯하고 단층이며 지붕이 평평했고, 산뜻한 초록빛 덩굴로 덮여 있었다."], ["The drought that had burned the hills of Judea to a crisp, brown and lifeless, stopped at the boundary-line of Galilee.", "유대의 언덕을 바싹 메마르게 태워 갈색의 죽은 빛으로 만든 가뭄도 갈릴리의 경계선에서 멈추었다."]], [["A trumpet, sounded when the cavalcade drew near the village, had a magical effect upon the inhabitants.", "행렬이 마을에 가까워졌을 때 울린 나팔 소리는 주민들에게 마법 같은 효력을 발휘했다."], ["The gates and front doors cast forth groups eager to be the first to catch the meaning of a visitation so unusual.", "대문과 현관에서는 이토록 보기 드문 방문의 뜻을 누구보다 먼저 알아채려고 안달하는 무리가 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["Nazareth, it must be remembered, was not only aside from any great highway, but within the sway of Judas of Gamala; wherefore it should not be hard to imagine the feelings with which the legionaries were received.", "기억해 두어야 할 것은, 나사렛이 어떤 큰길에서도 비켜나 있었을 뿐 아니라, 가말라의 유다가 세력을 떨치는 영역 안에 있었다는 사실이다. 그러니 군단병이 어떤 심정으로 맞아들여졌을지는 어렵지 않게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["But when they were up and traversing the street, the duty that occupied them became apparent, and then fear and hatred were lost in curiosity, under the impulse of which the people, knowing there must be a halt at the well in the northeastern part of the town, quit their gates and doors, and closed in after the procession.", "그러나 그들이 거리를 따라 올라가는 동안 그들에게 맡겨진 임무가 드러나자, 두려움과 미움은 호기심 속에 묻혀 버렸다. 그 충동에 이끌린 사람들은, 마을 동북쪽 우물가에서 멈추어 서리라는 것을 알고는, 대문과 현관을 버리고 행렬 뒤로 모여들었다."]], [["A prisoner whom the horsemen were guarding was the object of curiosity. He was afoot, bareheaded, half naked, his hands bound behind him.", "호기심의 대상은 기병들이 호송하는 죄수였다. 그는 맨발에 맨머리였고, 반쯤 헐벗은 채 두 손이 등 뒤로 묶여 있었다."], ["A thong fixed to his wrists was looped over the neck of a horse.", "손목에 매인 가죽끈은 말의 목에 고리처럼 걸려 있었다."], ["The dust went with the party when in movement, wrapping him in yellow fog, sometimes in a dense cloud. He drooped forward, footsore and faint.", "행렬이 움직일 때면 먼지가 일어 그를 누런 안개로, 때로는 짙은 구름으로 감쌌다. 그는 발이 부르트고 기진하여 앞으로 축 늘어졌다."], ["The villagers could see he was young.", "마을 사람들은 그가 젊다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["At the well the decurion halted, and, with most of the men, dismounted.", "우물가에서 부관은 걸음을 멈추고, 대부분의 부하와 더불어 말에서 내렸다."], ["The prisoner sank down in the dust of the road, stupefied, and asking nothing: apparently he was in the last stage of exhaustion.", "죄수는 길바닥의 먼지 속에 주저앉았는데, 넋이 나간 채 아무것도 청하지 않았다. 보아하니 탈진의 마지막 단계에 이른 모양이었다."], ["Seeing, when they came near, that he was but a boy, the villagers would have helped him had they dared.", "가까이 다가가 그가 한낱 소년에 지나지 않음을 알아본 마을 사람들은, 용기만 있었다면 그를 거들었을 것이다."]], [["In the midst of their perplexity, and while the pitchers were passing among the soldiers, a man was descried coming down the road from Sepphoris.", "그들이 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 가운데, 그리고 물 단지가 병사들 사이를 도는 동안, 한 사내가 세포리스 쪽 길을 따라 내려오는 것이 눈에 띄었다."], ["At sight of him a woman cried out, “Look!", "그를 보자 한 여인이 외쳤다. “저기 봐요!"], ["Yonder comes the carpenter. Now we will hear something.”", "목수가 오고 있어요. 이제 무슨 얘기를 들을 수 있겠네요.”"]], [["The person spoken of was quite venerable in appearance.", "그 사람은 보기에 자못 위엄이 있었다."], ["Thin white locks fell below the edge of his full turban, and a mass of still whiter beard flowed down the front of his coarse gray gown.", "풍성한 터번 가장자리 아래로 가느다란 흰 머리카락이 흘러내렸고, 그보다 더 새하얀 수염이 거친 잿빛 옷자락 앞으로 폭넓게 늘어져 있었다."], ["He came slowly, for, in addition to his age, he carried some tools—an axe, a saw, and a drawing-knife, all very rude and heavy—and had evidently travelled some distance without rest.", "그는 천천히 걸어왔는데, 나이 탓도 있었지만, 도끼와 톱과 끌 따위의 연장을, 그것도 하나같이 투박하고 묵직한 것들을 지고 있었기 때문이며, 또한 쉬지도 않고 먼 길을 온 기색이 역력했다."]], [["He stopped close by to survey the assemblage.", "그는 가까운 데서 걸음을 멈추고 모여 선 사람들을 살펴보았다."]], [["“O Rabbi, good Rabbi Joseph!”", "“오, 랍비님, 선량하신 요셉 랍비님!”"], ["cried a woman, running to him.", "한 여인이 그에게 달려가며 외쳤다."], ["“Here is a prisoner; come ask the soldiers about him, that we may know who he is, and what he has done, and what they are going to do with him.”", "“여기 죄수가 있어요. 와서 병사들에게 좀 물어봐 주세요. 저 사람이 누구인지, 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지, 그리고 저 사람을 어떻게 할 셈인지 우리도 알 수 있게요.”"]], [["The rabbi’s face remained stolid; he glanced at the prisoner, however, and presently went to the officer.", "랍비의 얼굴은 무덤덤한 채였다. 그래도 그는 죄수를 한번 흘끗 보고는, 이내 그 장교에게로 갔다."]], [["“The peace of the Lord be with you!”", "“주님의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를!”"], ["he said, with unbending gravity.", "그가 굽힘 없는 위엄으로 말했다."]], [["“And that of the gods with you,” the decurion replied.", "“그리고 신들의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를.” 부관이 대답했다."]], [["“Are you from Jerusalem?”", "“예루살렘에서 오시는 길이오?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그렇소.”"]], [["“Your prisoner is young.”", "“당신네 죄수가 젊군요.”"]], [["“In years, yes.”", "“나이로는, 그렇소.”"]], [["“May I ask what he has done?”", "“저 사람이 무슨 짓을 했는지 물어도 되겠소?”"]], [["“He is an assassin.”", "“암살을 꾀한 자요.”"]], [["The people repeated the word in astonishment, but Rabbi Joseph pursued his inquest.", "사람들은 놀라움에 그 말을 되뇌었으나, 요셉 랍비는 캐묻기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Is he a son of Israel?”", "“이스라엘의 자손이오?”"]], [["“He is a Jew,”", "“유대인이오.”"], ["said the Roman, dryly.", "로마인이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."]], [["The wavering pity of the bystanders came back.", "흔들리던 구경꾼들의 연민이 되살아났다."]], [["“I know nothing of your tribes, but can speak of his family,”", "“나는 당신네 지파에 대해서는 아는 바가 없으나, 저자의 집안은 말할 수 있소.”"], ["the speaker continued.", "그 사람이 말을 이었다."], ["“You may have heard of a prince of Jerusalem named Hur—Ben-Hur, they called him.", "“예루살렘의 후르라는 한 귀인에 대해 들어 보았을지 모르겠소. 사람들은 그를 벤허라고 불렀소."], ["He lived in Herod’s day.”", "그는 헤롯 시대에 살았소.”"]], [["“I have seen him,”", "“그를 본 적이 있소.”"], ["Joseph said.", "요셉이 말했다."]], [["“Well, this is his son.”", "“음, 이자가 바로 그의 아들이오.”"]], [["Exclamations became general, and the decurion hastened to stop them.", "여기저기서 탄성이 터져 나오자, 부관은 서둘러 그것을 잠재웠다."]], [["“In the streets of Jerusalem, day before yesterday, he nearly killed the noble Gratus by flinging a tile upon his head from the roof of a palace—his father’s, I believe.”", "“그저께, 예루살렘 거리에서, 이자는 어느 저택의 지붕에서, 아마 제 아비의 저택이었을 텐데, 기왓장을 던져 고귀하신 그라투스 각하의 머리를 맞혀 하마터면 죽일 뻔했소.”"]], [["There was a pause in the conversation during which the Nazarenes gazed at the young Ben-Hur as at a wild beast.", "대화가 잠시 끊긴 사이, 나사렛 사람들은 마치 사나운 짐승을 보듯 어린 벤허를 바라보았다."]], [["“No.” “He is under sentence.”", "“아니오.” “그는 선고를 받았소.”"]], [["“Yes—the galleys for life.”", "“아니오.” “선고는 받았소?”"]], [["“The Lord help him!” said Joseph, for once moved out of his stolidity.", "“그렇소. 종신토록 갤리선의 노를 젓게 되었소.”"]], [["Thereupon a youth who came up with Joseph, but had stood behind him unobserved, laid down an axe he had been carrying, and, going to the great stone standing by the well, took from it a pitcher of water.", "“주님께서 저 아이를 도우시기를!” 요셉이 말했는데, 평소의 무덤덤함에서 벗어난 것은 이번 한 번뿐이었다. 그때 요셉과 함께 왔으나 그 뒤에 가려져 눈에 띄지 않았던 한 젊은이가, 들고 있던 도끼를 내려놓고는, 우물 곁에 놓인 큰 돌로 가서 물 단지를 집어 들었다."], ["The action was so quiet that before the guard could interfere, had they been disposed to do so, he was stooping over the prisoner, and offering him drink.", "그 동작이 어찌나 조용했던지, 호위병들이 막으려 했다 하더라도, 미처 손쓰기도 전에 그는 이미 죄수에게 몸을 굽혀 마실 물을 건네고 있었다."]], [["The hand laid kindly upon his shoulder awoke the unfortunate Judah, and, looking up, he saw a face he never forgot—the face of a boy about his own age, shaded by locks of yellowish bright chestnut hair; a face lighted by dark-blue eyes, at the time so soft, so appealing, so full of love and holy purpose, that they had all the power of command and will.", "다정하게 어깨에 얹힌 손이 가엾은 유다를 일깨웠다. 고개를 들어 그는 결코 잊지 못할 얼굴을, 곧 제 또래쯤 되어 보이는 소년의 얼굴을 보았다. 누르스름하게 빛나는 밤색 머리카락이 그 얼굴에 그늘을 드리우고 있었다."], ["The spirit of the Jew, hardened though it was by days and nights of suffering, and so embittered by wrong that its dreams of revenge took in all the world, melted under the stranger’s look, and became as a child’s.", "그 얼굴은 짙푸른 두 눈으로 빛나고 있었는데, 그 눈은 그 순간 어찌나 부드럽고, 어찌나 호소하는 듯하며, 사랑과 거룩한 뜻으로 가득 차 있던지, 명령하고 다스리는 온갖 힘을 다 지니고 있었다. 여러 날 밤낮의 고통으로 굳어지고, 온 세상을 복수의 꿈으로 채울 만큼 부당함에 사무쳤던 유다의 마음도 그 낯선 이의 눈길 아래 녹아내려, 어린아이의 마음과 같이 되었다."], ["He put his lips to the pitcher, and drank long and deep.", "그는 단지에 입술을 대고 길고 깊게 물을 들이켰다."], ["Not a word was said to him, nor did he say a word.", "누구도 그에게 한마디 하지 않았고, 그 또한 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["When the draught was finished, the hand that had been resting upon the sufferer’s shoulder was placed upon his head, and stayed there in the dusty locks time enough to say a blessing; the stranger then returned the pitcher to its place on the stone, and, taking his axe again, went back to Rabbi Joseph.", "물을 다 마시자, 고통받는 이의 어깨에 얹혀 있던 손이 그의 머리 위로 옮겨가, 축복을 빌 만한 동안 먼지 낀 머리카락 위에 머물렀다. 그러고 나서 그 낯선 이는 단지를 돌 위 제자리에 돌려놓고, 다시 도끼를 집어 들어 요셉 랍비에게로 돌아갔다."], ["All eyes went with him, the decurion’s as well as those of the villagers.", "모든 눈길이 그를 좇았으니, 마을 사람들의 눈길은 물론 부관의 눈길까지도 그러했다."]], [["This was the end of the scene at the well.", "이것이 우물가 장면의 끝이었다."], ["When the men had drunk, and the horses, the march was resumed.", "사람들이 물을 마시고 말들도 물을 마시자, 행군이 다시 시작되었다."], ["But the temper of the decurion was not as it had been; he himself raised the prisoner from the dust, and helped him on a horse behind a soldier.", "그러나 부관의 마음은 전과 같지 않았다. 그는 손수 죄수를 먼지 속에서 일으켜, 한 병사 뒤에 태워 주었다."], ["The Nazarenes went to their houses—among them Rabbi Joseph and his apprentice.", "나사렛 사람들은 저마다 집으로 돌아갔는데, 그 가운데에는 요셉 랍비와 그의 도제도 있었다."]], [["And so, for the first time, Judah and the son of Mary met and parted.", "이리하여, 처음으로, 유다와 마리아의 아들은 만났다가 헤어졌다."]], [["# BOOK THIRD", "# BOOK THIRD"]], [["“Cleopatra....", "\"클레오파트라……"], ["Our size of sorrow, Proportion’d to our cause, must be as great As that which makes it.— Enter, below, DIOMEDES. How now?", "우리 슬픔의 크기는, 그 원인에 걸맞게, 그것을 자아낸 까닭만큼 커야 마땅하리라.— 아래쪽에서, 디오메데스 등장. 어찌 되었느냐?"], ["is he dead?", "그가 죽었느냐?"]], [["Diomedes.", "디오메데스."], ["His death’s upon him, but not dead.”", "죽음이 그에게 내렸으되, 아직 숨은 붙어 있나이다.\""], ["Antony and Cleopatra (act iv., sc. xiii.). CHAPTER I", "안토니우스와 클레오파트라 (제4막, 제13장)."]], [["The city of Misenum gave name to the promontory which it crowned, a few miles southwest of Naples.", "미세눔 시는 나폴리에서 남서쪽으로 몇 마일 떨어진 곳, 자신이 꼭대기에 자리한 곶에 그 이름을 주었다."], ["An account of ruins is all that remains of it now; yet in the year of our Lord 24—to which it is desirable to advance the reader—the place was one of the most important on the western coast of Italy.[1]", "지금은 폐허에 관한 기록만이 남아 있을 뿐이다. 그러나 우리 주님의 해로 24년—독자를 이제 그 시점으로 데려가고자 한다—에는, 이 고장이 이탈리아 서해안에서 가장 중요한 곳 가운데 하나였다.[1]"]], [["[1] The Roman government, it will be remembered, had two harbors in which great fleets were constantly kept—Ravenna and Misenum.", "[1] 기억해 두어야 할 것은, 로마 정부에 대함대를 늘 주둔시키던 두 항구가 있었으니, 라벤나와 미세눔이 그것이다."]], [["In the year mentioned, a traveller coming to the promontory to regale himself with the view there offered, would have mounted a wall, and, with the city at his back, looked over the bay of Neapolis, as charming then as now; and then, as now, he would have seen the matchless shore, the smoking cone, the sky and waves so softly, deeply blue, Ischia here and Capri yonder; from one to the other and back again, through the purpled air, his gaze would have sported; at last—for the eyes do weary of the beautiful as the palate with sweets—at last it would have dropped upon a spectacle which the modern tourist cannot see—half the reserve navy of Rome astir or at anchor below him. Thus regarded, Misenum was a very proper place for three masters to meet, and at leisure parcel the world among them.", "앞서 말한 해에, 그곳에서 펼쳐지는 경치를 즐기러 이 곶을 찾은 나그네라면, 성벽 위에 올라 도시를 등진 채 네아폴리스 만을 굽어보았을 것이다. 그 만은 지금이나 그때나 매혹적이었다. 그리고 그때나 지금이나 그는 견줄 데 없는 해안, 연기를 내뿜는 원뿔 봉우리, 더없이 부드럽고 깊푸른 하늘과 물결을 보았을 것이며, 이쪽에는 이스키아, 저쪽에는 카프리를 보았을 것이다. 자줏빛으로 물든 대기를 가르며, 그의 눈길은 이쪽에서 저쪽으로, 다시 저쪽에서 이쪽으로 즐거이 노닐었으리라. 마침내—눈도 혀가 단것에 물리듯 아름다움에 물리는 법이니—마침내 그 눈길은, 오늘날의 여행자는 볼 수 없는 광경에 떨어졌을 것이다. 로마 예비 함대의 절반이 그의 발아래에서 움직이거나 닻을 내리고 있었던 것이다. 이렇게 보면, 미세눔은 세 통치자가 만나 한가로이 세계를 저들끼리 나눠 갖기에 더없이 알맞은 곳이었다."]], [["In the old time, moreover, there was a gateway in the wall at a certain point fronting the sea—an empty gateway forming the outlet of a street which, after the exit, stretched itself, in the form of a broad mole, out many stadia into the waves.", "게다가 옛날에는, 바다를 마주한 성벽의 어느 한 지점에 성문이 하나 있었다. 그것은 한 거리의 출구를 이루는 텅 빈 성문으로, 그 거리는 문을 빠져나간 뒤 넓은 방파제 모양으로 여러 스타디온이나 물결 속으로 뻗어 나갔다."]], [["The watchman on the wall above the gateway was disturbed, one cool September morning, by a party coming down the street in noisy conversation.", "성문 위 성벽의 보초는, 어느 선선한 9월 아침, 시끌벅적 이야기를 나누며 거리를 내려오는 한 무리에 잠을 방해받았다."], ["He gave one look, then settled into his drowse again.", "그는 한 번 흘끗 보고는, 다시 졸음 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["There were twenty or thirty persons in the party, of whom the greater number were slaves with torches, which flamed little and smoked much, leaving on the air the perfume of the Indian nard.", "그 무리에는 스무 명이나 서른 명쯤 되는 사람들이 있었는데, 그 대다수는 횃불을 든 노예들이었다. 횃불은 불꽃은 작고 연기는 자욱하여, 인도 감송의 향기를 공기 중에 남겼다."], ["The masters walked in advance arm-in-arm.", "주인들은 팔짱을 끼고 앞장서 걸었다."], ["One of them, apparently fifty years old, slightly bald, and wearing over his scant locks a crown of laurel, seemed, from the attentions paid him, the central object of some affectionate ceremony.", "그중 한 사람은 쉰 살쯤 되어 보였고, 머리가 약간 벗어졌으며, 성긴 머리칼 위에 월계관을 쓰고 있었는데, 사람들이 그에게 기울이는 정성으로 보아 어떤 다정한 의례의 중심인물인 듯했다."], ["They all sported ample togas of white wool broadly bordered with purple.", "그들은 모두 자줏빛으로 넓게 가장자리를 두른 흰 양모 토가를 넉넉히 걸치고 있었다."], ["A glance had sufficed the watchman.", "보초에게는 한 번 흘끗 보는 것으로 충분했다."], ["He knew, without question, they were of high rank, and escorting a friend to ship after a night of festivity.", "그는 묻지 않고도, 그들이 높은 신분이며, 밤새 잔치를 벌인 뒤 한 벗을 배까지 배웅하고 있음을 알았다."], ["Further explanation will be found in the conversation they carried on.", "더 자세한 설명은 그들이 나누는 대화 속에서 찾을 수 있을 것이다."]], [["“No, my Quintus,”", "\"안 되네, 나의 퀸투스.\""], ["said one, speaking to him with the crown, “it is ill of Fortune to take thee from us so soon.", "한 사람이 월계관을 쓴 이에게 말을 건넸다. \"이리도 빨리 자네를 우리에게서 데려가다니, 운명의 여신도 야박하구먼."], ["Only yesterday thou didst return from the seas beyond the Pillars.", "바로 어제야 자네는 헤라클레스의 기둥 너머 바다에서 돌아오지 않았나."], ["Why, thou hast not even got back thy land legs.”", "어허, 자네는 아직 뭍에서 걷는 다리도 되찾지 못했네.\""]], [["“By Castor!", "\"카스토르께 맹세코!"], ["if a man may swear a woman’s oath,”", "사내가 계집의 맹세를 입에 담아도 된다면 말일세.\""], ["said another, somewhat worse of wine, “let us not lament.", "또 한 사람이 술기운이 좀 더 도는 채로 말했다. \"한탄일랑 그만두세."], ["Our Quintus is but going to find what he lost last night.", "우리 퀸투스는 다만 어젯밤에 잃은 것을 되찾으러 가는 것뿐이니."], ["Dice on a rolling ship is not dice on shore—eh, Quintus?”", "흔들리는 배 위의 주사위는 뭍의 주사위와 다르지—그렇지 않나, 퀸투스?\""]], [["“Abuse not Fortune!”", "\"운명의 여신을 욕되게 하지 말게!\""], ["exclaimed a third.", "세 번째 사람이 외쳤다."], ["“She is not blind or fickle.", "\"그분은 눈멀지도, 변덕스럽지도 않으시네."], ["At Antium, where our Arrius questions her, she answers him with nods, and at sea she abides with him holding the rudder.", "안티움에서 우리 아리우스가 그분께 여쭈면, 그분은 고개를 끄덕여 답하시고, 바다에서는 그가 키를 잡을 때 늘 그와 함께하시지."], ["She takes him from us, but does she not always give him back with a new victory?”", "그분이 그를 우리에게서 데려가시지만, 언제나 새 승리와 함께 되돌려 주시지 않던가?\""]], [["“The Greeks are taking him away,”", "\"그를 데려가는 건 그리스인들일세.\""], ["another broke in. “Let us abuse them, not the gods.", "또 다른 이가 끼어들었다. \"신들이 아니라 그자들을 욕하세."], ["In learning to trade they forgot how to fight.”", "그자들은 장사하는 법을 배우다가 싸우는 법을 잊어버렸으니.\""]], [["With these words, the party passed the gateway, and came upon the mole, with the bay before them beautiful in the morning light.", "이런 말과 함께, 일행은 성문을 지나 방파제에 들어섰다. 그들 앞에는 아침 빛 속에 아름다운 만이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["To the veteran sailor the plash of the waves was like a greeting.", "노련한 뱃사람에게 물결이 철썩이는 소리는 인사처럼 들렸다."], ["He drew a long breath, as if the perfume of the water were sweeter than that of the nard, and held his hand aloft.", "그는, 마치 물의 향기가 감송의 향기보다 더 달콤하기라도 한 듯, 길게 숨을 들이쉬고는, 한 손을 높이 들어 올렸다."]], [["“My gifts were at Praeneste, not Antium—and see!", "\"내 제물은 안티움이 아니라 프라이네스테에 두었지—그리고 보게!"], ["Wind from the west.", "서쪽에서 바람이 부네."], ["Thanks, O Fortune, my mother!”", "고맙습니다, 오 운명의 여신이여, 나의 어머니여!\""], ["he said, earnestly.", "그가 간곡하게 말했다."]], [["The friends all repeated the exclamation, and the slaves waved their torches.", "벗들은 모두 그 외침을 따라 했고, 노예들은 횃불을 흔들었다."]], [["“She comes—yonder!”", "\"그녀가 온다—저기일세!\""], ["he continued, pointing to a galley outside the mole.", "그가 방파제 바깥의 갤리선을 가리키며 말을 이었다."], ["“What need has a sailor for other mistress?", "\"뱃사람에게 어찌 다른 정인이 필요하겠나?"], ["Is your Lucrece more graceful, my Caius?”", "자네의 루크레티아가 저보다 우아한가, 나의 카이우스?\""]], [["He gazed at the coming ship, and justified his pride.", "그는 다가오는 배를 바라보며, 자신의 자부심이 옳음을 증명했다."], ["A white sail was bent to the low mast, and the oars dipped, arose, poised a moment, then dipped again, with wing-like action, and in perfect time.", "흰 돛이 낮은 돛대에 매여 있었고, 노들은 잠겼다가, 솟아올랐다가, 한순간 멈추었다가, 다시 잠겼으니, 날갯짓 같은 동작으로 완벽한 박자를 맞추고 있었다."]], [["“Yes, spare the gods,” he said, soberly, his eyes fixed upon the vessel.", "\"그래, 신들을 욕되게 하지 말게.\" 그가, 눈길을 배에 고정한 채, 차분하게 말했다."], ["“They send us opportunities.", "\"그분들은 우리에게 기회를 보내 주시지."], ["Ours the fault if we fail.", "우리가 실패한다면 그건 우리 탓일세."], ["And as for the Greeks, you forget, O my Lentulus, the pirates I am going to punish are Greeks.", "그리고 그리스인들로 말하자면, 자네는 잊고 있군, 오 나의 렌툴루스, 내가 벌하러 가는 해적들이 바로 그리스인들이라는 걸 말일세."], ["One victory over them is of more account than a hundred over the Africans.”", "그들을 한 번 이기는 것이 아프리카인들을 백 번 이기는 것보다 값지다네.\""]], [["“Then thy way is to the Aegean?”", "\"그렇다면 자네의 뱃길은 에게 해로 향하는가?\""]], [["The sailor’s eyes were full of his ship.", "뱃사람의 두 눈에는 자기 배가 가득 차 있었다."]], [["“What grace, what freedom!", "\"저 우아함, 저 자유로움이라니!"], ["A bird hath not less care for the fretting of the waves. See!”", "새도 물결의 일렁임을 저보다 더 마음 쓰지는 않을 걸세. 보게!\""], ["he said, but almost immediately added, “Thy pardon, my Lentulus.", "그가 말했으나, 거의 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"용서하게, 나의 렌툴루스."], ["I am going to the Aegean; and as my departure is so near, I will tell the occasion—only keep it under the rose.", "나는 에게 해로 가네. 떠날 때가 이리도 가까우니, 그 사연을 말해 주겠네—다만 비밀로 해 두게."], ["I would not that you abuse the duumvir when next you meet him.", "다음에 두움비르를 만나거든 그를 욕하지 않았으면 하네."], ["He is my friend.", "그는 내 벗이니."], ["The trade between Greece and Alexandria, as ye may have heard, is hardly inferior to that between Alexandria and Rome.", "그리스와 알렉산드리아 사이의 교역은, 자네들도 들었겠지만, 알렉산드리아와 로마 사이의 교역에 못지않다네."], ["The people in that part of the world forgot to celebrate the Cerealia, and Triptolemus paid them with a harvest not worth the gathering.", "그 지방 사람들이 케레알리아 제전 올리기를 잊었더니, 트립톨레모스가 거둘 가치도 없는 수확으로 그들에게 갚아 주었다지."], ["At all events, the trade is so grown that it will not brook interruption a day.", "어쨌거나 그 교역이 어찌나 커졌는지, 단 하루의 중단도 견디지 못한다네."], ["Ye may also have heard of the Chersonesan pirates, nested up in the Euxine; none bolder, by the Bacchae!", "자네들도 케르소네소스 해적들 이야기를 들었을 걸세, 에욱시네 바다에 둥지를 튼 자들 말이야."], ["Yesterday word came to Rome that, with a fleet, they had rowed down the Bosphorus, sunk the galleys off Byzantium and Chalcedon, swept the Propontis, and, still unsated, burst through into the Aegean. The corn-merchants who have ships in the East Mediterranean are frightened.", "바카이께 맹세코, 그보다 대담한 자들이 없지! 어제 로마에 소식이 들어왔네, 그자들이 함대를 거느리고 보스포루스를 노 저어 내려와, 비잔티움과 칼케돈 앞바다의 갤리선들을 침몰시키고, 프로폰티스를 휩쓸고, 그래도 성에 차지 않아 에게 해로 뚫고 들어왔다고 말일세."], ["They had audience with the Emperor himself, and from Ravenna there go to-day a hundred galleys, and from Misenum”—he paused as if to pique the curiosity of his friends, and ended with an emphatic—“one.”", "동지중해에 배를 둔 곡물 상인들이 겁에 질렸지. 그들이 황제를 직접 알현했고, 오늘 라벤나에서 백 척의 갤리선이 떠나고, 미세눔에서는\"—그는 마치 벗들의 호기심을 돋우려는 듯 말을 멈추었다가, 힘주어 끝맺었다—\"한 척이 떠나네.\""]], [["“Happy Quintus!", "\"복된 퀸투스로다!"], ["We congratulate thee!”", "우리가 자네를 축하하네!\""]], [["“The preferment forerunneth promotion.", "\"이번 발탁은 승진의 전조일세."], ["We salute thee duumvir; nothing less.”", "우리는 자네를 두움비르라 부르겠네. 그 이하로는 안 되지.\""]], [["“Quintus Arrius, the duumvir, hath a better sound than Quintus Arrius, the tribune.”", "\"두움비르 퀸투스 아리우스가, 호민관 퀸투스 아리우스보다 듣기에 좋군.\""]], [["In such manner they showered him with congratulations.", "이런 식으로 그들은 그에게 축하를 쏟아부었다."]], [["“I am glad with the rest,”", "\"나도 다른 이들 못지않게 기쁘네.\" 술 좋아하는 벗이 말했다."], ["said the bibulous friend, “very glad; but I must be practical, O my duumvir; and not until I know if promotion will help thee to knowledge of the tesserae will I have an opinion as to whether the gods mean thee ill or good in this—this business.”", "\"참으로 기쁘네. 하지만 나는 현실적이어야겠네, 오 나의 두움비르. 승진이 자네가 주사위 눈을 아는 데 도움이 될지 알기 전까지는, 이번—이번 일에서 신들이 자네에게 이로움을 뜻하시는지 해로움을 뜻하시는지 내 견해를 내놓지 않겠네.\""]], [["“Thanks, many thanks!”", "\"고맙네, 정말 고맙네!\""], ["Arrius replied, speaking to them collectively.", "아리우스가 그들 전체를 향해 대답했다."], ["“Had ye but lanterns, I would say ye were augurs. Perpol!", "\"자네들에게 등불만 있다면, 자네들을 점복관이라 부를 텐데. 페르폴!"], ["I will go further, and show what master diviners ye are! See—and read.”", "더 나아가, 자네들이 얼마나 빼어난 예언자인지 보여 주겠네! 보게—그리고 읽어 보게.\""]], [["From the folds of his toga he drew a roll of paper, and passed it to them, saying, “Received while at table last night from—Sejanus.”", "그는 토가의 주름 속에서 종이 두루마리를 꺼내 그들에게 건네며 말했다. \"어젯밤 식탁에 앉아 있을 때 받은 것일세—세야누스에게서.\""]], [["The name was already a great one in the Roman world; great, and not so infamous as it afterwards became.", "그 이름은 이미 로마 세계에서 대단한 이름이었으니, 대단하되, 훗날 그가 얻게 될 만큼 악명 높지는 않았다."]], [["“Sejanus!” they exclaimed, with one voice, closing in to read what the minister had written.", "\"세야누스라니!\" 그들이 한목소리로 외치며, 그 대신(大臣)이 써 보낸 것을 읽으려고 바싹 다가들었다."]], [["“Sejanus to C. Caecilius Rufus, Duumvir.", "\"세야누스가 두움비르 가이우스 카이킬리우스 루푸스에게."]], [["“ROME, XIX. Kal. Sept.", "로마, 9월 초하루 전 열아흐레."]], [["“Cæsar hath good report of Quintus Arrius, the tribune.", "황제께서 호민관 퀸투스 아리우스에 대해 좋은 보고를 받으셨소."], ["In particular he hath heard of his valor, manifested in the western seas, insomuch that it is his will that the said Quintus be transferred instantly to the East.", "특히 서쪽 바다에서 드러난 그의 용맹을 들으셨으니, 그 정도가 어찌나 대단한지, 앞서 말한 퀸투스를 즉시 동방으로 전임시키는 것이 황제의 뜻이시오."]], [["“It is our Cæsar’s will, further, that you cause a hundred triremes, of the first class, and full appointment, to be despatched without delay against the pirates who have appeared in the Aegean, and that Quintus be sent to command the fleet so despatched.", "또한 우리 황제의 뜻은, 그대가 일급 삼단노선 백 척을 완전한 장비를 갖추어 지체 없이 에게 해에 나타난 해적들을 치도록 파견하고, 퀸투스를 그리 파견된 함대의 지휘관으로 보내라는 것이오."]], [["“Details are thine, my Caecilius.", "세부 사항은 그대에게 맡기오, 나의 카이킬리우스."]], [["“The necessity is urgent, as thou will be advised by the reports enclosed for thy perusal and the information of the said Quintus.", "이 일은 시급하니, 그대가 살펴보고 또 앞서 말한 퀸투스에게 알려 주도록 동봉한 보고서들로 알게 될 것이오. 세야누스.\""]], [["Arrius gave little heed to the reading.", "아리우스는 그 낭독에 별 주의를 기울이지 않았다."], ["As the ship drew more plainly out of the perspective, she became more and more an attraction to him.", "배가 원근의 풍경에서 더욱 또렷이 떠오를수록, 그에게는 점점 더 매혹의 대상이 되어 갔다."], ["The look with which he watched her was that of an enthusiast.", "그가 배를 지켜보는 눈길은 열광하는 사람의 눈길이었다."], ["At length he tossed the loosened folds of his toga in the air; in reply to the signal, over the aplustre, or fan-like fixture at the stern of the vessel, a scarlet flag was displayed; while several sailors appeared upon the bulwarks, and swung themselves hand over hand up the ropes to the antenna, or yard, and furled the sail.", "마침내 그는 풀어진 토가 자락을 허공에 흔들어 올렸다. 그 신호에 답하여, 배의 고물에 달린 부챗살 모양의 장식, 곧 아플루스트레 위로 진홍빛 기가 펼쳐졌다. 그러는 사이 몇몇 뱃사람이 뱃전에 나타나, 밧줄을 손으로 번갈아 잡으며 활대, 곧 가로돛대까지 기어올라 돛을 걷었다."], ["The bow was put round, and the time of the oars increased one half; so that at racing speed she bore down directly towards him and his friends.", "이물이 방향을 틀고, 노 젓는 박자가 반절 빨라졌다. 그리하여 배는 질주하는 속도로 그와 그의 벗들을 향해 곧장 다가왔다."], ["He observed the manoeuvring with a perceptible brightening of the eyes.", "그는 그 조선술을 지켜보며 눈에 띄게 눈빛이 밝아졌다."], ["Her instant answer to the rudder, and the steadiness with which she kept her course, were especially noticeable as virtues to be relied upon in action.", "키에 대한 배의 즉각적인 반응과, 항로를 지키는 한결같음은, 실전에서 믿고 의지할 만한 미덕으로서 특히 눈여겨볼 만했다."]], [["“By the Nymphae!”", "\"님파이께 맹세코!\""], ["said one of the friends, giving back the roll, “we may not longer say our friend will be great; he is already great.", "한 벗이 두루마리를 돌려주며 말했다. \"우리는 더 이상 우리 벗이 위대해질 것이라 말할 수 없네. 그는 이미 위대하니."], ["Our love will now have famous things to feed upon.", "이제 우리의 사랑은 자랑스러운 일들을 양식으로 삼게 되겠군."], ["What more hast thou for us?”", "우리에게 더 들려줄 것이 있나?\""]], [["“Nothing more,”", "\"더는 없네.\""], ["Arrius replied.", "아리우스가 대답했다."], ["“What ye have of the affair is by this time old news in Rome, especially between the palace and the Forum.", "\"이 일에 대해 자네들이 아는 것은 이미 로마에서는 묵은 소식일 걸세, 특히 궁궐과 광장 사이에서는 말이야."], ["The duumvir is discreet; what I am to do, where go to find my fleet, he will tell on the ship, where a sealed package is waiting me.", "두움비르는 신중한 사람이니, 내가 무엇을 해야 하는지, 어디로 가서 함대를 찾아야 하는지는 배 위에서 일러 줄 걸세, 그곳에 밀봉된 꾸러미가 나를 기다리고 있으니."], ["If, however, ye have offerings for any of the altars to-day, pray the gods for a friend plying oar and sail somewhere in the direction of Sicily.", "하지만 자네들이 오늘 어느 제단에든 바칠 제물이 있거든, 시칠리아 방향 어딘가에서 노와 돛을 부리고 있을 한 벗을 위해 신들께 빌어 주게."], ["But she is here, and will come to,”", "그런데 그녀가 여기 왔으니, 곧 댈 걸세.\""], ["he said, reverting to the vessel.", "그가 다시 배 쪽으로 눈을 돌리며 말했다."], ["“I have interest in her masters; they will sail and fight with me.", "\"나는 그 배의 부리는 자들에게 관심이 있네. 그들은 나와 함께 항해하고 함께 싸울 테니."], ["It is not an easy thing to lay ship side on a shore like this; so let us judge their training and skill.”", "이런 해안에 배의 옆구리를 대는 것은 쉬운 일이 아닐세. 그러니 그들의 훈련과 솜씨를 가늠해 보세.\""]], [["“What, is she new to thee?”", "\"무어라, 그 배가 자네에게는 처음인가?\""]], [["“I never saw her before; and, as yet, I know not if she will bring me one acquaintance.”", "\"전에 본 적이 없네. 그리고 아직은, 저 배가 내게 아는 이를 하나라도 데려다줄지 알 수 없네.\""]], [["“Is that well?”", "\"그래도 괜찮은가?\""]], [["“It matters but little.", "\"별것 아닐세."], ["We of the sea come to know each other quickly; our loves, like our hates, are born of sudden dangers.”", "우리 바다 사람들은 서로를 빨리 알게 되지. 우리의 사랑도, 미움과 마찬가지로, 갑작스러운 위험에서 태어나니까.\""]], [["The vessel was of the class called naves liburnicae—long, narrow, low in the water, and modelled for speed and quick manoeuvre.", "그 배는 나베스 리부르니카이라 불리는 종류로, 길고, 좁고, 물에 낮게 잠겨, 속도와 날쌘 조작에 맞게 만들어진 것이었다."], ["The bow was beautiful.", "이물이 아름다웠다."], ["A jet of water spun from its foot as she came on, sprinkling all the prow, which rose in graceful curvature twice a man’s stature above the plane of the deck.", "배가 다가올 때 그 발치에서 물줄기가 솟아올라 이물 전체에 흩뿌렸는데, 이물은 갑판 면에서 사람 키의 두 배 높이로 우아하게 굽어 솟아 있었다."], ["Upon the bending of the sides were figures of Triton blowing shells.", "굽은 옆면 위에는 소라고둥을 부는 트리톤 상이 새겨져 있었다."], ["Below the bow, fixed to the keel, and projecting forward under the water-line, was the rostrum, or beak, a device of solid wood, reinforced and armed with iron, in action used as a ram.", "이물 아래, 용골에 박혀 수면 아래로 앞으로 튀어나온 것은 충각, 곧 부리였으니, 단단한 나무로 만들어 쇠로 보강하고 무장한 장치로, 실전에서 들이받는 데 쓰였다."], ["A stout molding extended from the bow the full length of the ship’s sides, defining the bulwarks, which were tastefully crenelated; below the molding, in three rows, each covered with a cap or shield of bull-hide, were the holes in which the oars were worked—sixty on the right, sixty on the left.", "굵직한 쇠시리가 이물에서 배 옆구리 끝까지 뻗어 뱃전의 윤곽을 이루었는데, 그 뱃전은 운치 있게 총안 모양으로 새겨져 있었다. 그 쇠시리 아래로는, 세 줄로, 저마다 황소 가죽 덮개나 방패로 가린 채, 노를 부리는 구멍들이 있었으니—오른쪽에 예순, 왼쪽에 예순이었다."], ["In further ornamentation, caducei leaned against the lofty prow.", "더한 장식으로, 카두케우스 지팡이들이 높은 이물에 기대어 있었다."], ["Two immense ropes passing across the bow marked the number of anchors stowed on the foredeck.", "이물을 가로지르는 두 가닥의 거대한 밧줄은, 앞갑판에 갈무리된 닻의 수를 일러 주었다."]], [["The simplicity of the upper works declared the oars the chief dependence of the crew.", "윗부분 구조물이 단순한 것을 보면, 노가 선원들의 주된 의지처임을 알 수 있었다."], ["A mast, set a little forward of midship, was held by fore and back stays and shrouds fixed to rings on the inner side of the bulwarks.", "배 한가운데보다 조금 앞쪽에 세운 돛대는, 뱃전 안쪽의 고리에 매단 앞뒤 버팀줄과 옆 버팀줄로 지탱되었다."], ["The tackle was that required for the management of one great square sail and the yard to which it was hung.", "삭구는 커다란 사각돛 하나와 그것을 매단 활대를 다루는 데 필요한 것이었다."], ["Above the bulwarks the deck was visible.", "뱃전 위로는 갑판이 보였다."]], [["Save the sailors who had reefed the sail, and yet lingered on the yard, but one man was to be seen by the party on the mole, and he stood by the prow helmeted and with a shield.", "돛을 걷고도 활대에 여태 머물러 있던 뱃사람들 말고는, 방파제의 일행 눈에 띄는 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었으니, 그는 투구를 쓰고 방패를 든 채 이물 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["The hundred and twenty oaken blades, kept white and shining by pumice and the constant wash of the waves, rose and fell as if operated by the same hand, and drove the galley forward with a speed rivalling that of a modern steamer.", "백스무 자루의 떡갈나무 노깃은, 부석과 끊임없는 물결의 씻김으로 희고 반들반들하게 닦인 채, 마치 같은 손이 부리기라도 하는 듯 오르내리며, 갤리선을 오늘날의 증기선에 견줄 만한 속도로 앞으로 몰아 나갔다."]], [["So rapidly, and apparently, so rashly, did she come that the landsmen of the tribune’s party were alarmed.", "배가 어찌나 빠르게, 그리고 보기에 어찌나 무모하게 다가오는지, 호민관 일행의 뭍사람들은 겁에 질렸다."], ["Suddenly the man by the prow raised his hand with a peculiar gesture; whereupon all the oars flew up, poised a moment in air, then fell straight down.", "갑자기 이물 곁의 사내가 독특한 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올렸다. 그러자 모든 노가 솟구쳐, 한순간 공중에 멈추었다가, 곧장 아래로 떨어졌다."], ["The water boiled and bubbled about them; the galley shook in every timber, and stopped as if scared.", "노 둘레에서 물이 끓어오르고 거품이 일었다. 갤리선은 모든 널빤지가 다 흔들리도록 떨더니, 겁먹은 듯 멈추어 섰다."], ["Another gesture of the hand, and again the oars arose, feathered, and fell; but this time those on the right, dropping towards the stern, pushed forward; while those on the left, dropping towards the bow, pulled backwards.", "손의 또 다른 몸짓에, 노들이 다시 솟아올라, 깃처럼 눕혔다가, 떨어졌다. 그러나 이번에는 오른쪽 노들이 고물 쪽으로 떨어지며 앞으로 밀고, 왼쪽 노들은 이물 쪽으로 떨어지며 뒤로 당겼다."], ["Three times the oars thus pushed and pulled against each other.", "노들은 이렇게 세 번 서로 맞밀고 맞당겼다."], ["Round to the right the ship swung as upon a pivot; then, caught by the wind, she settled gently broadside to the mole.", "배는 마치 굴대를 중심으로 돌듯 오른쪽으로 빙 돌았다. 그러더니 바람을 받아, 옆구리를 방파제 쪽으로 부드럽게 돌려 댔다."]], [["The movement brought the stern to view, with all its garniture—Tritons like those at the bow; name in large raised letters; the rudder at the side; the elevated platform upon which the helmsman sat, a stately figure in full armor, his hand upon the rudder-rope; and the aplustre, high, gilt, carved, and bent over the helmsman like a great runcinate leaf.", "그 움직임에 고물이 그 온갖 치장과 함께 드러났다—이물의 것과 같은 트리톤 상들, 크게 도드라진 글자로 새긴 이름, 옆쪽의 키, 키잡이가 앉은 높직한 단(壇)—그 위에는 완전 무장을 한 위엄 있는 사람이 키 줄에 손을 얹고 있었다—그리고 아플루스트레가, 높이 솟아 도금되고 새김질된 채, 커다란 톱니 모양 잎새처럼 키잡이 위로 굽어 있었다."]], [["In the midst of the rounding-to, a trumpet was blown brief and shrill, and from the hatchways out poured the marines, all in superb equipment, brazen helms, burnished shields and javelins.", "방향을 돌리는 와중에, 나팔이 짧고 날카롭게 울리더니, 승강구에서 해병들이 쏟아져 나왔다. 모두 더없이 훌륭한 차림으로, 놋쇠 투구를 쓰고, 반들반들한 방패와 투창을 들고 있었다."], ["While the fighting-men thus went to quarters as for action, the sailors proper climbed the shrouds and perched themselves along the yard.", "이렇게 전투원들이 실전에라도 임하듯 전투 위치로 가는 동안, 본래의 뱃사람들은 옆 버팀줄을 타고 올라 활대를 따라 자리를 잡고 앉았다."], ["The officers and musicians took their posts.", "장교들과 악사들도 제자리를 잡았다."], ["There was no shouting or needless noise.", "고함도, 쓸데없는 소리도 없었다."], ["When the oars touched the mole, a bridge was sent out from the helmsman’s deck.", "노가 방파제에 닿자, 키잡이의 갑판에서 다리가 뻗어 나왔다."], ["Then the tribune turned to his party and said, with a gravity he had not before shown:", "그러자 호민관은 일행을 향해 돌아서서, 전에 보이지 않던 진중함으로 말했다."]], [["“Duty now, O my friends.”", "\"이제 임무가 기다리네, 오 나의 벗들이여.\""]], [["He took the chaplet from his head and gave it to the dice-player.", "그는 머리에서 화관을 벗어 주사위꾼에게 주었다."]], [["“Take thou the myrtle, O favorite of the tesserae!”", "\"이 도금양 관을 받게, 오 주사위 눈의 총아여!\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “If I return, I will seek my sestertii again; if I am not victor, I will not return.", "\"내가 돌아온다면, 내 세스테르티우스를 다시 찾으러 오겠네. 내가 승리하지 못한다면, 돌아오지 않을 걸세."], ["Hang the crown in thy atrium.”", "이 관을 자네 안뜰에 걸어 두게.\""]], [["To the company he opened his arms, and they came one by one and received his parting embrace.", "그는 일행을 향해 두 팔을 벌렸고, 그들은 한 사람씩 다가와 그의 작별의 포옹을 받았다."]], [["“The gods go with thee, O Quintus!”", "\"신들이 자네와 함께하시기를, 오 퀸투스여!\""], ["they said.", "그들이 말했다."]], [["“Farewell,”", "\"잘들 있게.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["To the slaves waving their torches he waved his hand; then he turned to the waiting ship, beautiful with ordered ranks and crested helms, and shields and javelins.", "횃불을 흔드는 노예들에게 그는 손을 흔들어 주었다. 그러고는 가지런한 대오와 깃 장식 투구들, 방패와 투창으로 아름다운, 기다리는 배 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["As he stepped upon the bridge, the trumpets sounded, and over the aplustre rose the vexillum purpureum, or pennant of a commander of a fleet.", "그가 다리에 발을 디디자, 나팔이 울리고, 아플루스트레 위로 벡실룸 푸르푸레움, 곧 함대 지휘관의 깃발이 올랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["The tribune, standing upon the helmsman’s deck with the order of the duumvir open in his hand, spoke to the chief of the rowers.[1]", "호민관은 키잡이 갑판에 서서 펼쳐 든 두 집정관의 명령서를 손에 쥔 채, 노잡이들의 우두머리에게 말했다.[1]"]], [["[1] Called hortator.", "[1] 호르타토르라 불린다."]], [["“What force hast thou?”", "\"네가 거느린 인원이 얼마나 되느냐?\""]], [["“Of oarsmen, two hundred and fifty-two; ten supernumeraries.", "\"노잡이는 이백쉰둘이고, 예비 인원이 열입니다.\""]], [["“Making reliefs of—”", "\"교대조로 치면—\""]], [["“Eighty-four.”", "\"여든넷입니다.\""]], [["“And thy habit?”", "\"교대 방식은 어떠하냐?\""]], [["“It has been to take off and put on every two hours.”", "\"두 시간마다 내리고 올리는 것이 관례였습니다.\""]], [["The tribune mused a moment.", "호민관은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“The division is hard, and I will reform it, but not now.", "\"그 배분은 고되다. 내가 고치겠으나, 지금은 아니다."], ["The oars may not rest day or night.”", "노는 밤낮으로 쉬어서는 안 된다.\""]], [["Then to the sailing-master he said,", "이어 그는 항해장에게 말했다."]], [["“The wind is fair.", "\"바람이 좋다."], ["Let the sail help the oars.”", "돛으로 노를 돕도록 하라.\""]], [["When the two thus addressed were gone, he turned to the chief pilot.[2]", "이렇게 일러둔 두 사람이 물러가자, 그는 수석 도선사에게 몸을 돌렸다.[2]"]], [["[2] Called rector.", "[2] 렉토르라 불린다."]], [["“What service hast thou had?”", "\"너는 얼마나 복무했느냐?\""]], [["“Two-and-thirty years.”", "\"서른두 해입니다.\""]], [["“In what seas chiefly?”", "\"주로 어느 바다에서였느냐?\""]], [["“Between our Rome and the East.”", "\"우리 로마와 동방 사이에서였습니다.\""]], [["“Thou art the man I would have chosen.”", "\"네가 바로 내가 골랐을 만한 사람이로구나.\""]], [["The tribune looked at his orders again.", "호민관은 다시 명령서를 들여다보았다."]], [["“Past the Camponellan cape, the course will be to Messina.", "\"캄파넬라 곶을 지나면 항로는 메시나로 향한다."], ["Beyond that, follow the bend of the Calabrian shore till Melito is on thy left, then— Knowest thou the stars that govern in the Ionian Sea?”", "그다음에는 멜리토가 왼편에 놓일 때까지 칼라브리아 해안의 굽이를 따라가라. 그러고 나서는— 이오니아해를 다스리는 별들을 아느냐?\""]], [["“I know them well.”", "\"잘 압니다.\""]], [["“Then from Melito course eastward for Cythera.", "\"그러면 멜리토에서 동쪽으로 키테라를 향해 항로를 잡아라."], ["The gods willing, I will not anchor until in the Bay of Antemona.", "신들이 허락하신다면, 나는 안테모나만에 이르기까지 닻을 내리지 않겠다."], ["The duty is urgent.", "이 임무는 화급하다."], ["I rely upon thee.”", "너를 믿겠다.\""]], [["A prudent man was Arrius—prudent, and of the class which, while enriching the altars at Praeneste and Antium, was of opinion, nevertheless, that the favor of the blind goddess depended more upon the votary’s care and judgment than upon his gifts and vows.", "아리우스는 신중한 사람이었다. 신중하면서도, 프라이네스테와 안티움의 제단을 살찌우는 부류에 속했으나, 그러면서도 눈먼 여신의 은총은 봉헌자의 예물이나 서원보다 그의 주의와 판단에 더 달려 있다고 여기는 부류였다."], ["All night as master of the feast he had sat at table drinking and playing; yet the odor of the sea returned him to the mood of the sailor, and he would not rest until he knew his ship. Knowledge leaves no room for chances.", "밤새도록 연회의 주인으로서 그는 식탁에 앉아 술을 마시고 노름을 했다. 그러나 바다의 냄새가 그를 다시 뱃사람의 기분으로 되돌려 놓았으니, 그는 제 배를 속속들이 알기 전에는 쉬려 하지 않았다."], ["Having begun with the chief of the rowers, the sailing-master, and the pilot, in company with the other officers—the commander of the marines, the keeper of the stores, the master of the machines, the overseer of the kitchen or fires—he passed through the several quarters.", "앎은 우연이 끼어들 자리를 남기지 않는다. 노잡이의 우두머리와 항해장과 도선사로 시작하여, 다른 장교들—해병대 지휘관, 군수품 관리인, 기계 담당관, 주방 곧 화덕을 맡은 감독관—과 더불어 그는 여러 구역을 두루 거쳤다. 어느 것도 그의 점검을 비켜 가지 못했다."], ["Nothing escaped his inspection. When he was through, of the community crowded within the narrow walls he alone knew perfectly all there was of material preparation for the voyage and its possible incidents; and, finding the preparation complete, there was left him but one thing further—thorough knowledge of the personnel of his command.", "점검을 마쳤을 때, 그 좁은 벽 안에 빽빽이 들어찬 무리 가운데 오직 그만이 이번 항해와 일어날 법한 사고에 대비한 물자의 준비를 빈틈없이 다 알고 있었다. 그리고 준비가 완벽함을 확인한 그에게는 단 한 가지 일만이 더 남아 있었으니, 그것은 제 휘하 인원에 대한 철저한 파악이었다."], ["As this was the most delicate and difficult part of his task, requiring much time, he set about it his own way.", "이것은 그의 임무 가운데 가장 미묘하고 까다로운 부분으로, 많은 시간을 요구했으므로, 그는 제 방식대로 그 일에 착수했다."]], [["At noon that day the galley was skimming the sea off Paestum.", "그날 정오, 갤리선은 파이스툼 앞바다를 스치듯 미끄러져 갔다."], ["The wind was yet from the west, filling the sail to the master’s content. The watches had been established.", "바람은 여전히 서쪽에서 불어와, 항해장이 흡족할 만큼 돛을 채웠다. 당직이 정해졌다."], ["On the foredeck the altar had been set and sprinkled with salt and barley, and before it the tribune had offered solemn prayers to Jove and to Neptune and all the Oceanidæ, and, with vows, poured the wine and burned the incense.", "앞갑판에는 제단이 세워지고 소금과 보리가 뿌려졌으며, 그 앞에서 호민관은 유피테르와 넵투누스와 모든 오케아니데스에게 엄숙히 기도를 올리고, 서원과 함께 포도주를 붓고 향을 사르었다."], ["And now, the better to study his men, he was seated in the great cabin, a very martial figure.", "이제 부하들을 더 잘 살피려고, 그는 큰 선실에 앉아 있었으니, 자못 군인다운 모습이었다."]], [["The cabin, it should be stated, was the central compartment of the galley, in extent quite sixty-five by thirty feet, and lighted by three broad hatchways.", "말해 두건대, 그 선실은 갤리선의 한가운데 칸으로, 넓이는 족히 가로 스무 미터에 세로 아홉 미터에 이르렀고, 세 개의 넓은 승강구로 빛이 들었다."], ["A row of stanchions ran from end to end, supporting the roof, and near the centre the mast was visible, all bristling with axes and spears and javelins.", "한 줄로 늘어선 받침 기둥이 끝에서 끝까지 이어져 지붕을 떠받쳤고, 한가운데쯤에는 돛대가 보였는데, 도끼와 창과 투창으로 빽빽이 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["To each hatchway there were double stairs descending right and left, with a pivotal arrangement at the top to allow the lower ends to be hitched to the ceiling; and, as these were now raised, the compartment had the appearance of a skylighted hall.", "승강구마다 좌우로 내려가는 두 갈래 계단이 있었고, 위쪽에는 회전 장치가 달려 아래쪽 끝을 천장에 걸어 둘 수 있게 되어 있었다. 그리고 지금은 그것들이 들어 올려져 있어, 그 칸은 마치 채광창이 달린 큰 방처럼 보였다."]], [["The reader will understand readily that this was the heart of the ship, the home of all aboard—eating-room, sleeping-chamber, field of exercise, lounging-place off duty—uses made possible by the laws which reduced life there to minute details and a routine relentless as death.", "독자는 이곳이 배의 심장이요, 배에 탄 모든 이의 거처였음을 쉽게 이해할 것이다. 식당이자 침실이요, 운동장이자 비번일 때 쉬는 자리이기도 했다. 이 모든 쓰임은 그곳의 삶을 자질구레한 세목과 죽음처럼 가차 없는 일과로 줄여 놓은 규율 덕분에 가능했다."]], [["At the after-end of the cabin there was a platform, reached by several steps.", "선실 뒤쪽 끝에는 단이 하나 있어, 몇 개의 계단을 밟고 올라가게 되어 있었다."], ["Upon it the chief of the rowers sat; in front of him a sounding-table, upon which, with a gavel, he beat time for the oarsmen; at his right a clepsydra, or water-clock, to measure the reliefs and watches.", "그 위에 노잡이들의 우두머리가 앉았다. 그 앞에는 울림판이 놓여, 그것을 나무망치로 두드려 노잡이들에게 박자를 맞추어 주었다. 오른편에는 클렙시드라, 곧 물시계가 있어 교대조와 당직 시간을 쟀다."], ["Above him, on a higher platform, well guarded by gilded railing, the tribune had his quarters, overlooking everything, and furnished with a couch, a table, and a cathedra, or chair, cushioned, and with arms and high back—articles which the imperial dispensation permitted of the utmost elegance.", "그 위로는 더 높은 단에, 금박을 입힌 난간으로 든든히 둘러싸인 곳에 호민관의 거처가 있어, 모든 것을 내려다볼 수 있었다. 그곳에는 긴 의자와 탁자, 그리고 카테드라, 곧 푹신한 방석을 깐 팔걸이와 높은 등받이가 있는 의자가 갖추어져 있었으니, 모두 황제의 특전이 허락하는 더없이 우아한 물건들이었다."]], [["Thus at ease, lounging in the great chair, swaying with the motion of the vessel, the military cloak half draping his tunic, sword in belt, Arrius kept watchful eye over his command, and was as closely watched by them.", "이렇게 편안히, 큰 의자에 기대어 배의 흔들림에 따라 몸을 흔들며, 군용 망토로 튜닉을 반쯤 두르고 허리띠에 칼을 찬 채, 아리우스는 제 휘하를 빈틈없이 지켜보았고, 그들 또한 그를 못지않게 지켜보았다."], ["He saw critically everything in view, but dwelt longest upon the rowers.", "그는 눈에 띄는 모든 것을 비판적으로 살폈으나, 노잡이들에게 가장 오래 눈길을 머물렀다."], ["The reader would doubtless have done the same: only he would have looked with much sympathy, while, as is the habit with masters, the tribune’s mind ran forward of what he saw, inquiring for results.", "독자라도 분명 그리했을 것이다. 다만 독자는 자못 측은한 마음으로 바라보았겠으나, 주인된 자들이 흔히 그러하듯, 호민관의 마음은 눈앞에 보이는 것을 앞질러 결과를 따져 묻고 있었다."]], [["The spectacle was simple enough of itself.", "광경 자체는 자못 단순했다."], ["Along the sides of the cabin, fixed to the ship’s timbers, were what at first appeared to be three rows of benches; a closer view, however, showed them a succession of rising banks, in each of which the second bench was behind and above the first one, and the third above and behind the second.", "선실 양쪽 옆으로, 배의 들보에 붙박여, 처음에는 세 줄의 의자처럼 보이는 것이 있었다. 그러나 가까이 살펴보니, 그것은 차례로 높아지는 단의 연속이었다. 각 단에서 둘째 의자는 첫째 의자보다 뒤쪽이자 위쪽에 있었고, 셋째 의자는 둘째 의자보다 위쪽이자 뒤쪽에 있었다."], ["To accommodate the sixty rowers on a side, the space devoted to them permitted nineteen banks separated by intervals of one yard, with a twentieth bank divided so that what would have been its upper seat or bench was directly above the lower seat of the first bank.", "한쪽에 예순 명의 노잡이를 두기 위해, 그들에게 할당된 공간은 일 미터 간격으로 떨어진 열아홉 단을 허용했고, 거기에 스무째 단이 나뉘어 들어가, 그 윗자리가 될 자리는 첫 단의 아랫자리 바로 위에 놓였다."], ["The arrangement gave each rower when at work ample room, if he timed his movements with those of his associates, the principle being that of soldiers marching with cadenced step in close order.", "이런 배치는 노잡이마다 일할 때 넉넉한 자리를 주었으니, 다만 제 움직임을 동료들의 움직임에 맞추기만 하면 되었다. 그 원리는 병사들이 발맞추어 빽빽한 대열로 행진하는 것과 같았다."], ["The arrangement also allowed a multiplication of banks, limited only by the length of the galley.", "이런 배치는 또한 단의 수를 늘릴 수 있게 했으니, 그 한계는 오직 갤리선의 길이뿐이었다."]], [["As to the rowers, those upon the first and second benches sat, while those upon the third, having longer oars to work, were suffered to stand.", "노잡이들로 말하면, 첫째와 둘째 의자에 앉은 자들은 앉아 있었으나, 셋째 의자에 앉은 자들은 더 긴 노를 다루어야 했으므로 서 있도록 허락되었다."], ["The oars were loaded with lead in the handles, and near the point of balance hung to pliable thongs, making possible the delicate touch called feathering, but, at the same time, increasing the need of skill, since an eccentric wave might at any moment catch a heedless fellow and hurl him from his seat.", "노는 손잡이에 납이 채워져 있었고, 균형점 가까이에 휘어지는 가죽끈으로 매달려 있어, 페더링이라 불리는 섬세한 솜씨가 가능했다. 그러나 그와 동시에 솜씨의 필요는 더 커졌으니, 어쩌다 비뚜로 치는 물결이 부주의한 자를 덮쳐 그를 제 자리에서 내동댕이칠 수도 있었기 때문이다."], ["Each oar-hole was a vent through which the laborer opposite it had his plenty of sweet air.", "노 구멍 하나하나는 통풍구가 되어, 그 맞은편의 일꾼에게 신선한 공기를 넉넉히 들여보냈다."], ["Light streamed down upon him from the grating which formed the floor of the passage between the deck and the bulwark over his head.", "그의 머리 위 갑판과 뱃전 사이 통로의 바닥을 이루는 살창으로부터 빛이 그에게 흘러내렸다. 그러므로 어떤 점에서는 그들의 처지가 훨씬 더 나쁠 수도 있었으리라."], ["In some respects, therefore, the condition of the men might have been much worse.", "그렇다고 그들의 삶에 무슨 즐거움이 있었으리라 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["Still, it must not be imagined that there was any pleasantness in their lives.", "그들 사이의 대화는 허락되지 않았다. 날이면 날마다 그들은 말없이 제 자리를 채웠다."], ["Communication between them was not allowed.", "일하는 동안에는 서로의 얼굴조차 볼 수 없었다."], ["Day after day they filled their places without speech; in hours of labor they could not see each other’s faces; their short respites were given to sleep and the snatching of food.", "짧은 휴식은 잠과 음식을 허겁지겁 삼키는 데에 바쳐졌다. 그들은 결코 웃지 않았다. 그들 가운데 누군가 노래하는 것을 들은 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["They never laughed; no one ever heard one of them sing. What is the use of tongues when a sigh or a groan will tell all men feel while, perforce, they think in silence?", "한숨이나 신음 한 번이면 사람의 마음에 든 모든 것을 다 일러 주는데, 게다가 어쩔 수 없이 침묵 속에 생각해야 한다면, 혀가 무슨 소용이겠는가?"], ["Existence with the poor wretches was like a stream under ground sweeping slowly, laboriously on to its outlet, wherever that might chance to be.", "그 가엾은 이들에게 삶이란, 그 출구가 어디든 간에, 더디고 고되게 그리로 흘러가는 땅 밑의 물줄기 같은 것이었다."]], [["O Son of Mary! The sword has now a heart—and thine the glory!", "오, 마리아의 아들이여! 이제 칼에는 심장이 있고—그 영광은 당신의 것입니다!"], ["So now; but, in the days of which we are writing, for captivity there was drudgery on walls, and in the streets and mines, and the galleys both of war and commerce were insatiable.", "지금은 그러하다. 그러나 우리가 쓰고 있는 그 시절에는, 포로된 자에게 성벽에서의 고역과 거리와 광산에서의 노역이 있었고, 전쟁과 상업의 갤리선은 둘 다 만족을 모르는 아가리였다."], ["When Druilius won the first sea-fight for his country, Romans plied the oars, and the glory was to the rower not less than the marine.", "드루일리우스가 제 나라를 위해 첫 해전을 거두었을 때에는, 로마인이 노를 저었고, 영광은 해병 못지않게 노잡이에게 돌아갔다."], ["These benches which now we are trying to see as they were testified to the change come with conquest, and illustrated both the policy and the prowess of Rome.", "지금 우리가 본래 모습 그대로 보려 애쓰는 이 의자들은 정복과 더불어 찾아온 변화를 증언했고, 로마의 정책과 무용을 아울러 보여 주었다."], ["Nearly all the nations had sons there, mostly prisoners of war, chosen for their brawn and endurance.", "거의 모든 민족이 그곳에 제 아들들을 두고 있었으니, 대부분 전쟁 포로로, 억센 몸과 인내력을 보고 골라 온 자들이었다."], ["In one place a Briton; before him a Libyan; behind him a Crimean.", "한 자리에는 브리튼 사람이, 그 앞에는 리비아 사람이, 그 뒤에는 크림 사람이 있었다."], ["Elsewhere a Scythian, a Gaul, and a Thebasite.", "다른 곳에는 스키타이 사람과 갈리아 사람과 테베 사람이 있었다."], ["Roman convicts cast down to consort with Goths and Longobardi, Jews, Ethiopians, and barbarians from the shores of Maeotis.", "로마의 죄수들이 끌려 내려와 고트족과 롱고바르디족, 유대인, 에티오피아인, 그리고 마이오티스 해안에서 온 야만인들과 한데 어울렸다."], ["Here an Athenian, there a red-haired savage from Hibernia, yonder blue-eyed giants of the Cimbri.", "여기에는 아테네 사람이, 저기에는 히베르니아에서 온 붉은 머리의 야만인이, 저쪽에는 푸른 눈의 킴브리 거인들이 있었다."]], [["In the labor of the rowers there was not enough art to give occupation to their minds, rude and simple as they were.", "노잡이들의 노동에는 그들의 마음을 붙들어 둘 만한 솜씨랄 것이 거의 없었으니, 그들이 거칠고 단순한 만큼이나 그러했다."], ["The reach forward, the pull, the feathering the blade, the dip, were all there was of it; motions most perfect when most automatic.", "앞으로 뻗기, 당기기, 노깃을 누이기, 물에 담그기—그것이 전부였고, 그 동작들은 가장 기계적일 때 가장 완벽했다."], ["Even the care forced upon them by the sea outside grew in time to be a thing instinctive rather than of thought.", "바깥 바다가 그들에게 강요하는 주의조차 시간이 흐르면서 생각의 산물이 아니라 본능에 가까운 것이 되어 갔다."], ["So, as the result of long service, the poor wretches became imbruted—patient, spiritless, obedient—creatures of vast muscle and exhausted intellects, who lived upon recollections generally few but dear, and at last lowered into the semi-conscious alchemic state wherein misery turns to habit, and the soul takes on incredible endurance.", "그리하여 오랜 복무의 결과로, 그 가엾은 이들은 짐승처럼 되어 갔으니—참을성 있고, 기백 없고, 순종하는—거대한 근육과 메마른 지성을 지닌 피조물이 되어, 대개 몇 안 되나 소중한 추억으로 살아갔고, 마침내 비참함이 습관으로 바뀌고 영혼이 믿기 어려운 인내를 걸치게 되는 반쯤 깬 연금술의 상태로 가라앉아 갔다."]], [["From right to left, hour after hour, the tribune, swaying in his easy-chair, turned with thought of everything rather than the wretchedness of the slaves upon the benches.", "오른쪽에서 왼쪽으로, 시간이 흐르고 또 흐르는 동안, 호민관은 안락의자에 앉아 몸을 흔들며, 의자에 앉은 노예들의 비참함보다는 차라리 다른 온갖 것을 생각하며 시선을 돌렸다."], ["Their motions, precise, and exactly the same on both sides of the vessel, after a while became monotonous; and then he amused himself singling out individuals.", "그들의 동작은 정확했고, 배의 양쪽이 똑같았으니, 얼마 지나자 단조로워졌다. 그러자 그는 한 사람씩 골라내며 심심풀이를 했다."], ["With his stylus he made note of objections, thinking, if all went well, he would find among the pirates of whom he was in search better men for the places.", "첨필로 흠을 적어 가며, 만사가 잘 풀린다면 자기가 찾고 있는 해적들 가운데 이 자리들에 어울리는 더 나은 자들을 찾아낼 수 있으리라 생각했다."]], [["There was no need of keeping the proper names of the slaves brought to the galleys as to their graves; so, for convenience, they were usually identified by the numerals painted upon the benches to which they were assigned.", "무덤에 끌려가듯 갤리선에 끌려온 노예들의 본디 이름을 간직해 둘 필요는 없었다. 그러므로 편의상 그들은 보통 배정된 의자에 칠해진 번호로 식별되었다."], ["As the sharp eyes of the great man moved from seat to seat on either hand, they came at last to number sixty, which, as has been said, belonged properly to the last bank on the left-hand side, but, wanting room aft, had been fixed above the first bench of the first bank.", "그 위대한 사내의 날카로운 눈이 양쪽으로 자리에서 자리로 옮겨 가다가, 마침내 육십 번에 이르렀다. 그것은 앞서 말했듯이 본래 왼쪽 맨 끝 단에 속하는 것이었으나, 뒤쪽에 자리가 없어 첫 단의 첫 의자 위에 박혀 있었다."], ["There they rested.", "그의 눈은 거기에 머물렀다."]], [["The bench of number sixty was slightly above the level of the platform, and but a few feet away.", "육십 번의 의자는 단의 높이보다 약간 위에 있었고, 불과 몇 걸음 떨어져 있을 뿐이었다."], ["The light glinting through the grating over his head gave the rower fairly to the tribune’s view—erect, and, like all his fellows, naked, except a cincture about the loins.", "머리 위 살창으로 비스듬히 새어 드는 빛이 그 노잡이를 호민관의 눈앞에 또렷이 드러냈다—허리에 두른 띠 하나를 빼고는, 다른 동료들과 마찬가지로 벌거벗은, 꼿꼿한 모습이었다."], ["There were, however, some points in his favor.", "그러나 그에게는 몇 가지 유리한 점이 있었다."], ["He was very young, not more than twenty.", "그는 무척 젊었으니, 스무 살을 넘지 않았다."], ["Furthermore, Arrius was not merely given to dice; he was a connoisseur of men physically, and when ashore indulged a habit of visiting the gymnasia to see and admire the most famous athletae.", "게다가 아리우스는 노름에만 빠진 사람이 아니었다. 그는 신체에 관한 한 사람을 보는 안목이 있는 자로, 뭍에 있을 때면 체육관을 찾아가 가장 이름난 운동선수들을 보고 감탄하는 버릇이 있었다."], ["From some professor, doubtless, he had caught the idea that strength was as much of the quality as the quantity of the muscle, while superiority in performance required a certain mind as well as strength.", "어느 전문가에게서였는지, 힘이란 근육의 양 못지않게 질에 달려 있고, 동작의 우월함에는 힘과 더불어 어떤 정신이 필요하다는 생각을 얻은 것이 분명했다."], ["Having adopted the doctrine, like most men with a hobby, he was always looking for illustrations to support it.", "한 가지 신념을 받아들인 그는, 무엇에 빠진 사람들이 흔히 그러하듯, 늘 그것을 뒷받침할 본보기를 찾고 있었다."]], [["The reader may well believe that while the tribune, in the search for the perfect, was often called upon to stop and study, he was seldom perfectly satisfied—in fact, very seldom held as long as on this occasion.", "완벽함을 찾는 동안 호민관은 자주 멈추어 살펴야 했으나, 좀처럼 흡족한 적이 없었음을—사실 이번처럼 오래 붙들린 적은 거의 없었음을—독자는 능히 믿을 것이다."]], [["In the beginning of each movement of the oar, the rower’s body and face were brought into profile view from the platform; the movement ended with the body reversed, and in a pushing posture.", "노를 한 번 젓기 시작할 때마다, 노잡이의 몸과 얼굴은 단에서 옆모습으로 보였다. 그 동작은 몸이 뒤로 젖혀지며 밀어내는 자세로 끝났다."], ["The grace and ease of the action at first suggested a doubt of the honesty of the effort put forth; but it was speedily dismissed; the firmness with which the oar was held while in the reach forward, its bending under the push, were proofs of the force applied; not that only, they as certainly proved the rower’s art, and put the critic in the great arm-chair in search of the combination of strength and cleverness which was the central idea of his theory.", "그 동작의 우아함과 수월함은 처음에는 그가 쏟는 노력이 참된 것인지 의심을 자아냈으나, 그 의심은 곧 떨쳐졌다. 앞으로 뻗을 때 노를 단단히 쥐는 모습과 밀 때 노가 휘어지는 모습은 그가 쏟는 힘의 증거였다. 그뿐만 아니라 그것은 노잡이의 솜씨 또한 분명히 증명했고, 큰 안락의자에 앉은 비평가로 하여금 제 이론의 핵심을 이루는 힘과 영민함의 결합을 찾아 나서게 했다."]], [["In course of the study, Arrius observed the subject’s youth; wholly unconscious of tenderness on that account, he also observed that he seemed of good height, and that his limbs, upper and nether, were singularly perfect.", "살펴보는 동안, 아리우스는 그 대상이 젊다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그렇다고 그 때문에 측은한 마음이 든 것은 전혀 아니었다. 그는 또한 그의 키가 훤칠하며, 위아래 팔다리가 유달리 완벽하다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["The arms, perhaps, were too long, but the objection was well hidden under a mass of muscle, which, in some movements, swelled and knotted like kinking cords.", "팔은 어쩌면 지나치게 길었으나, 그 흠은 우람한 근육 더미 아래 잘 가려져 있었으니, 어떤 동작에서는 그 근육이 뒤틀린 밧줄처럼 부풀어 오르고 매듭졌다."], ["Every rib in the round body was discernible; yet the leanness was the healthful reduction so strained after in the palaestrae.", "둥근 몸통의 갈비뼈가 하나하나 드러났다. 그러나 그 야윔은 팔라이스트라에서 그토록 애써 추구하는 건강한 군더더기 빼기였다."], ["And altogether there was in the rower’s action a certain harmony which, besides addressing itself to the tribune’s theory, stimulated both his curiosity and general interest.", "그리고 그 노잡이의 동작에는 무엇보다 어떤 조화가 깃들어 있어, 호민관의 이론에 부합할 뿐 아니라 그의 호기심과 두루 관심을 함께 자극했다."]], [["Very soon he found himself waiting to catch a view of the man’s face in full.", "이내 그는 그 사내의 얼굴을 정면으로 보고 싶어 기다리는 자신을 발견했다."], ["The head was shapely, and balanced upon a neck broad at the base, but of exceeding pliancy and grace.", "머리는 균형이 잡혀 있었고, 밑동이 넓되 더없이 유연하고 우아한 목 위에 얹혀 있었다."], ["The features in profile were of Oriental outline, and of that delicacy of expression which has always been thought a sign of blood and sensitive spirit.", "옆모습으로 본 이목구비는 동방의 윤곽을 지녔고, 늘 혈통과 예민한 정신의 표시로 여겨져 온 그 섬세한 표정을 띠고 있었다."], ["With these observations, the tribune’s interest in the subject deepened.", "이런 관찰과 더불어, 그 대상에 대한 호민관의 관심은 한층 깊어졌다."]], [["“By the gods,”", "\"신들에 맹세코,\""], ["he said to himself, “the fellow impresses me!", "그는 속으로 말했다. \"저 녀석이 나를 사로잡는구나!"], ["He promises well.", "자못 가망이 있어."], ["I will know more of him.”", "저 녀석을 더 알아보아야겠다.\""]], [["Directly the tribune caught the view he wished—the rower turned and looked at him.", "곧 호민관은 바라던 광경을 붙잡았다—노잡이가 몸을 돌려 그를 바라본 것이다."]], [["“A Jew!", "\"유대인이로구나!"], ["and a boy!”", "게다가 소년이야!\""]], [["Under the gaze then fixed steadily upon him, the large eyes of the slave grew larger—the blood surged to his very brows—the blade lingered in his hands.", "그때 자신에게 줄곧 고정된 그 시선 아래, 노예의 큰 눈은 더욱 커졌고—피가 이마까지 솟구쳤으며—노깃이 손안에서 머뭇거렸다."], ["But instantly, with an angry crash, down fell the gavel of the hortator.", "그러나 곧장, 성난 소리를 내며 호르타토르의 나무망치가 내리쳐졌다."], ["The rower started, withdrew his face from the inquisitor, and, as if personally chidden, dropped the oar half feathered.", "노잡이는 흠칫하더니, 심문자에게서 얼굴을 거두고, 마치 제가 직접 꾸지람을 들은 듯, 반쯤 누이던 노를 놓쳐 버렸다."], ["When he glanced again at the tribune, he was vastly more astonished—he was met with a kindly smile.", "다시 호민관을 흘끗 보았을 때, 그는 훨씬 더 놀랐으니—다정한 미소와 마주쳤던 것이다."]], [["Meantime the galley entered the Straits of Messina, and, skimming past the city of that name, was after a while turned eastward, leaving the cloud over Ætna in the sky astern.", "그러는 사이 갤리선은 메시나 해협으로 들어섰고, 그 이름을 지닌 도시를 스치듯 지나, 얼마 뒤 동쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌렸으니, 에트나 위에 드리운 구름을 뒤편 하늘에 남겨 두고 갔다."]], [["Often as Arrius resumed to his platform in the cabin he returned to study the rower, and he kept saying to himself, “The fellow hath a spirit.", "선실의 단으로 돌아갈 때마다 아리우스는 그 노잡이를 다시 살피러 갔고, 거듭 속으로 되뇌었다. \"저 녀석에게는 기백이 있어."], ["A Jew is not a barbarian.", "유대인은 야만인이 아니다."], ["I will know more of him.”", "저 녀석을 더 알아보아야겠다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["The fourth day out, and the Astroea—so the galley was named—speeding through the Ionian Sea.", "출항한 지 나흘째, 아스트로이아호—갤리선의 이름이 그러했다—는 이오니아해를 가르며 나아가고 있었다."], ["The sky was clear, and the wind blew as if bearing the good-will of all the gods.", "하늘은 맑았고, 바람은 마치 모든 신들의 호의를 실어 나르듯 불었다."]], [["As it was possible to overtake the fleet before reaching the bay east of the island of Cythera, designated for assemblage, Arrius, somewhat impatient, spent much time on deck.", "함대가 집결지로 정해진 키테라섬 동쪽 만에 닿기 전에 따라잡을 수 있겠다는 생각에, 아리우스는 다소 조급해진 마음으로 갑판에서 많은 시간을 보냈다."], ["He took note diligently of matters pertaining to his ship, and as a rule was well pleased.", "그는 자기 배에 관한 일들을 부지런히 살폈고, 대체로 흡족해했다."], ["In the cabin, swinging in the great chair, his thought continually reverted to the rower on number sixty.", "선실에서 커다란 의자에 몸을 흔들며 앉아 있을 때면, 그의 생각은 끊임없이 육십 번 노잡이에게로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“Knowest thou the man just come from yon bench?”", "\"방금 저 자리에서 물러난 사내를 아느냐?\""], ["he at length asked of the hortator.", "그는 마침내 호르타토르에게 물었다."]], [["A relief was going on at the moment.", "마침 교대가 이루어지던 참이었다."]], [["“From number sixty?”", "\"육십 번 자리 말씀이십니까?\""], ["returned the chief.", "노잡이 우두머리가 되물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["The chief looked sharply at the rower then going forward.", "우두머리는 앞으로 걸어 나가는 노잡이를 날카롭게 바라보았다."]], [["“As thou knowest,”", "\"아시다시피,\""], ["he replied “the ship is but a month from the maker’s hand, and the men are as new to me as the ship.”", "그가 대답했다. \"이 배는 만들어진 지 한 달밖에 안 되었습니다. 그러니 노잡이들도 배만큼이나 제게 낯섭니다.\""]], [["“He is a Jew,”", "\"저자는 유대인이다.\""], ["Arrius remarked, thoughtfully.", "아리우스가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다."]], [["“The noble Quintus is shrewd.”", "\"고귀하신 퀸투스님은 예리하십니다.\""]], [["“He is very young,”", "\"아주 젊더구나.\""], ["Arrius continued.", "아리우스가 말을 이었다."]], [["“But our best rower,” said the other.", "\"하나 우리 배에서 가장 뛰어난 노잡이입니다.\" 우두머리가 말했다."], ["“I have seen his oar bend almost to breaking.”", "\"노가 거의 부러질 만큼 휘는 것을 본 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Of what disposition is he?”", "\"성품은 어떠하냐?\""]], [["“He is obedient; further I know not.", "\"순종적입니다. 그 밖에는 아는 바가 없습니다."], ["Once he made request of me.”", "언젠가 제게 한 가지 청을 한 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“For what?”", "\"무슨 청이냐?\""]], [["“He wished me to change him alternately from the right to the left.”", "\"저를 오른쪽과 왼쪽으로 번갈아 옮겨 달라고 했습니다.\""]], [["“Did he give a reason?”", "\"이유를 말하더냐?\""]], [["“He had observed that the men who are confined to one side become misshapen.", "\"한쪽에만 매여 있는 자들은 몸이 비뚤어진다는 것을 알아챘다고 했습니다."], ["He also said that some day of storm or battle there might be sudden need to change him, and he might then be unserviceable.”", "또 폭풍이나 전투가 벌어지는 날에는 갑자기 자리를 바꿔야 할 일이 생길 수 있는데, 그때 제구실을 못 하게 될지도 모른다고 했습니다.\""]], [["“Perpol!", "\"페르폴!"], ["The idea is new. What else hast thou observed of him?”", "새로운 생각이로구나. 그 밖에 무엇을 보았느냐?\""]], [["“He is cleanly above his companions.”", "\"동료들보다 몸가짐이 깨끗합니다.\""]], [["“In that he is Roman,”", "\"그 점은 로마인답구나.\""], ["said Arrius, approvingly.", "아리우스가 흡족한 듯 말했다."], ["“Have you nothing of his history?”", "\"저자의 내력에 관해서는 아는 것이 없느냐?\""]], [["“Not a word.”", "\"한마디도 없습니다.\""]], [["The tribune reflected awhile, and turned to go to his own seat.", "호민관은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 제자리로 돌아가려고 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“If I should be on deck when his time is up,”", "\"저자의 차례가 끝났을 때 내가 갑판에 있거든,\""], ["he paused to say, “send him to me.", "그가 걸음을 멈추고 말했다. \"내게 보내라."], ["Let him come alone.”", "혼자 오게 하라.\""]], [["About two hours later Arrius stood under the aplustre of the galley; in the mood of one who, seeing himself carried swiftly towards an event of mighty import, has nothing to do but wait—the mood in which philosophy vests an even-minded man with the utmost calm, and is ever so serviceable.", "두 시간쯤 지난 뒤, 아리우스는 갤리선의 고물 장식 아래 서 있었다. 자신이 거대한 사건을 향해 빠르게 실려 가고 있음을 알면서도 그저 기다리는 수밖에 없는 사람의 심정이었으니—철학이 마음 고른 사람에게 더없는 평온을 입혀 주며, 언제나 그토록 쓸모 있게 작용하는 바로 그 심정이었다."], ["The pilot sat with a hand upon the rope by which the rudder paddles, one on each side of the vessel, were managed.", "키잡이는 배 양옆에 하나씩 달린 키 노를 다루는 밧줄에 손을 얹고 앉아 있었다."], ["In the shade of the sail some sailors lay asleep, and up on the yard there was a lookout.", "돛 그늘에는 선원 몇이 잠들어 누워 있었고, 활대 위에는 망보는 자가 올라가 있었다."], ["Lifting his eyes from the solarium set under the aplustre for reference in keeping the course, Arrius beheld the rower approaching.", "항로를 지키는 데 참고하려고 고물 장식 아래 놓아둔 해시계에서 눈을 들었을 때, 아리우스는 노잡이가 다가오는 것을 보았다."]], [["“The chief called thee the noble Arrius, and said it was thy will that I should seek thee here.", "\"우두머리가 당신을 고귀한 아리우스님이라 부르며, 제가 여기로 당신을 찾아오는 것이 당신의 뜻이라고 했습니다."], ["I have come.”", "그래서 왔습니다.\""]], [["Arrius surveyed the figure, tall, sinewy, glistening in the sun, and tinted by the rich red blood within—surveyed it admiringly, and with a thought of the arena; yet the manner was not without effect upon him: there was in the voice a suggestion of life at least partly spent under refining influences; the eyes were clear and open, and more curious than defiant.", "아리우스는 그 모습을 찬찬히 살폈다. 키가 크고 근육이 탄탄하며, 햇빛에 번들거리고, 안에서 도는 진한 붉은 피로 물든 몸이었다—감탄하며 살폈고, 검투장을 떠올리며 살폈다. 그러나 그 태도는 그에게 영향을 미치지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 목소리에는 적어도 일부나마 사람을 다듬는 환경 속에서 보낸 삶의 자취가 배어 있었다."], ["To the shrewd, demanding, masterful glance bent upon it, the face gave back nothing to mar its youthful comeliness—nothing of accusation or sullenness or menace, only the signs which a great sorrow long borne imprints, as time mellows the surface of pictures.", "두 눈은 맑고 트여 있었으며, 반항보다는 호기심이 어려 있었다. 예리하고 따져 묻는, 위엄에 찬 시선이 쏟아졌으나, 그 얼굴은 젊은 미모를 흐릴 만한 것을 조금도 내비치지 않았다—고발도, 음울함도, 위협도 없이, 다만 오래 견뎌 온 큰 슬픔이 새겨 놓은 흔적뿐이었으니, 세월이 그림의 표면을 부드럽게 무르익히듯 한 그것이었다."], ["In tacit acknowledgment of the effect, the Roman spoke as an older man to a younger, not as a master to a slave.", "그 영향을 말없이 인정하며, 로마인은 주인이 노예에게 하듯이 아니라 손위 사람이 손아래 사람에게 하듯이 말했다."]], [["“The hortator tells me thou art his best rower.”", "\"호르타토르가 말하기를 네가 그의 가장 뛰어난 노잡이라더구나.\""]], [["“The hortator is very kind,”", "\"호르타토르가 매우 친절하십니다.\""], ["the rower answered.", "노잡이가 대답했다."]], [["“Hast thou seen much service?”", "\"오래 복무했느냐?\""]], [["“About three years.”", "\"삼 년쯤 됩니다.\""]], [["“At the oars?”", "\"노를 저으며?\""]], [["“I cannot recall a day of rest from them.”", "\"노에서 쉰 날은 단 하루도 떠올릴 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“The labor is hard; few men bear it a year without breaking, and thou—thou art but a boy.”", "\"고된 노릇이다. 일 년을 버티고도 부서지지 않는 자가 드물거늘, 너는—너는 아직 소년이 아니냐.\""]], [["“The noble Arrius forgets that the spirit hath much to do with endurance.", "\"고귀한 아리우스님께서는 견딤에 정신이 큰 몫을 한다는 것을 잊으셨습니다."], ["By its help the weak sometimes thrive, when the strong perish.”", "그 힘을 빌려 약한 자가 때로 살아남고, 강한 자가 도리어 스러지기도 합니다.\""]], [["“From thy speech, thou art a Jew.”", "\"말투를 보니 너는 유대인이로구나.\""]], [["“My ancestors further back than the first Roman were Hebrews.”", "\"제 조상은 첫 로마인보다 더 거슬러 올라가 히브리인이었습니다.\""]], [["“The stubborn pride of thy race is not lost in thee,”", "\"네 종족의 고집스러운 긍지가 네게서도 사라지지 않았구나.\""], ["said Arrius, observing a flush upon the rower’s face.", "아리우스가 노잡이의 얼굴에 떠오른 홍조를 보며 말했다."]], [["“Pride is never so loud as when in chains.”", "\"긍지는 사슬에 묶였을 때 가장 소리 높이 우는 법입니다.\""]], [["“What cause hast thou for pride?”", "\"네가 긍지를 가질 까닭이 무엇이냐?\""]], [["“That I am a Jew.” Arrius smiled.", "\"제가 유대인이라는 것입니다.\""]], [["“I have not been to Jerusalem,”", "아리우스가 미소 지었다."], ["he said; “but I have heard of its princes.", "\"나는 예루살렘에 가 본 적이 없다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["I knew one of them. He was a merchant, and sailed the seas.", "\"허나 그곳의 제후들에 관해서는 들었다. 그중 한 사람을 알고 있었지."], ["He was fit to have been a king.", "그는 상인이었고, 바다를 누볐다."], ["Of what degree art thou?”", "왕이 될 만한 인물이었다. 너는 어느 신분이냐?\""]], [["“I must answer thee from the bench of a galley.", "\"갤리선의 노잡이 자리에서 답해 드려야겠군요."], ["I am of the degree of slaves.", "저는 노예의 신분입니다."], ["My father was a prince of Jerusalem, and, as a merchant, he sailed the seas.", "제 아버지는 예루살렘의 제후였고, 상인으로서 바다를 누볐습니다."], ["He was known and honored in the guest-chamber of the great Augustus.”", "그분은 위대한 아우구스투스의 영빈관에서도 이름이 알려지고 존경받던 분이었습니다.\""]], [["“His name?”", "\"이름이?\""]], [["“Ithamar, of the house of Hur.”", "\"이타마르, 후르 가문의 사람입니다.\""]], [["The tribune raised his hand in astonishment.", "호민관은 놀라움에 손을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“A son of Hur—thou?”", "\"후르의 아들이—너란 말이냐?\""]], [["After a silence, he asked,", "잠시 침묵한 뒤, 그가 물었다."]], [["“What brought thee here?”", "\"무슨 일로 여기까지 오게 되었느냐?\""]], [["Judah lowered his head, and his breast labored hard.", "유다는 고개를 떨구었고, 그의 가슴은 거칠게 들썩였다."], ["When his feelings were sufficiently mastered, he looked the tribune in the face, and answered,", "감정을 충분히 다스리고 나서, 그는 호민관의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["“I was accused of attempting to assassinate Valerius Gratus, the procurator.”", "\"저는 총독 발레리우스 그라투스를 암살하려 했다는 혐의를 받았습니다.\""]], [["“Thou!”", "\"네가!\""], ["cried Arrius, yet more amazed, and retreating a step.", "아리우스가 더욱 놀라 외치며 한 걸음 물러섰다."], ["“Thou that assassin!", "\"네가 그 암살범이라고!"], ["All Rome rang with the story.", "온 로마가 그 이야기로 떠들썩했다."], ["It came to my ship in the river by Lodinum.”", "로디눔 곁의 강에 있던 내 배에까지 전해졌지.\""]], [["The two regarded each other silently.", "두 사람은 말없이 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“I thought the family of Hur blotted from the earth,”", "\"나는 후르 가문이 이 땅에서 깡그리 사라진 줄로 알았다.\""], ["said Arrius, speaking first.", "아리우스가 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["A flood of tender recollections carried the young man’s pride away; tears shone upon his cheeks.", "다정한 추억의 물결이 그 젊은이의 긍지를 휩쓸어 갔다. 두 뺨에 눈물이 빛났다."]], [["“Mother—mother!", "\"어머니—어머니!"], ["And my little Tirzah!", "그리고 제 어린 티르자!"], ["Where are they?", "그들은 어디에 있습니까?"], ["O tribune, noble tribune, if thou knowest anything of them”—he clasped his hands in appeal—“tell me all thou knowest.", "오, 호민관님, 고귀한 호민관님, 그들에 대해 무엇이든 아신다면\"—그는 애원하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다—\"아시는 것을 모두 말씀해 주십시오."], ["Tell me if they are living—if living, where are they? and in what condition?", "그들이 살아 있는지, 살아 있다면 어디에 있는지, 어떤 처지인지 말씀해 주십시오."], ["Oh, I pray thee, tell me!”", "오, 제발 말씀해 주십시오!\""]], [["He drew nearer Arrius, so near that his hands touched the cloak where it dropped from the latter’s folded arms.", "그는 아리우스에게 다가섰으니, 어찌나 가까웠던지 그의 두 손이 팔짱을 낀 호민관의 어깨에서 흘러내린 망토에 닿을 정도였다."]], [["“The horrible day is three years gone,”", "\"그 끔찍한 날이 삼 년이나 지났습니다.\""], ["he continued—“three years, O tribune, and every hour a whole lifetime of misery—a lifetime in a bottomless pit with death, and no relief but in labor—and in all that time not a word from any one, not a whisper.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"삼 년입니다, 호민관님, 그 한 시간 한 시간이 통째로 고통의 한평생이었습니다—죽음과 더불어 밑도 끝도 없는 구덩이에 갇힌 한평생, 노동 말고는 아무 위안도 없는 한평생이었습니다—그 모든 세월 동안 누구에게서도 한마디 소식이, 한 자락 속삭임조차 없었습니다."], ["Oh, if, in being forgotten, we could only forget!", "오, 잊히는 것으로 우리도 잊을 수만 있다면!"], ["If only I could hide from that scene—my sister torn from me, my mother’s last look!", "그 장면에서—누이가 제게서 떼어 내지고, 어머니의 마지막 눈길이 보이던 그 장면에서 숨을 수만 있다면!"], ["I have felt the plague’s breath, and the shock of ships in battle; I have heard the tempest lashing the sea, and laughed, though others prayed: death would have been a riddance.", "저는 역병의 입김도 맡아 보았고, 전투에서 배가 충돌하는 충격도 겪었습니다. 폭풍이 바다를 후려치는 소리를 듣고도, 남들은 기도할 때 저는 웃었습니다. 죽음이라면 차라리 벗어남이었을 테니까요."], ["Bend the oar—yes, in the strain of mighty effort trying to escape the haunting of what that day occurred. Think what little will help me.", "노를 휘게 하고—그렇습니다, 그 안간힘 속에서 그날 일어난 일이 따라다니며 괴롭히는 것에서 벗어나려 했습니다. 얼마나 작은 것이라도 저를 도울 수 있을지 생각해 보십시오."], ["Tell me they are dead, if no more, for happy they cannot be while I am lost.", "더는 바라지 않으니, 그들이 죽었다고만 말씀해 주십시오. 제가 사라진 동안 그들이 행복할 수는 없을 테니까요."], ["I have heard them call me in the night; I have seen them on the water walking.", "밤이면 그들이 저를 부르는 소리를 들었습니다. 물 위를 걸어오는 그들을 보았습니다."], ["Oh, never anything so true as my mother’s love!", "오, 제 어머니의 사랑만큼 참된 것은 결코 없었습니다!"], ["And Tirzah—her breath was as the breath of white lilies.", "그리고 티르자—그 애의 숨결은 흰 백합의 숨결 같았습니다."], ["She was the youngest branch of the palm—so fresh, so tender, so graceful, so beautiful! She made my day all morning.", "그 애는 종려나무의 가장 어린 가지였습니다—그토록 싱그럽고, 그토록 보드랍고, 그토록 우아하고, 그토록 아름다웠습니다! 그 애는 제 하루를 온통 아침으로 만들어 주었습니다."], ["She came and went in music.", "그 애는 음악처럼 오고 갔습니다."], ["And mine was the hand that laid them low! I—”", "그런데 그들을 쓰러뜨린 것이 바로 제 손이었습니다! 제가—\""]], [["“Dost thou admit thy guilt?”", "\"네 죄를 인정하느냐?\""], ["asked Arrius, sternly.", "아리우스가 엄하게 물었다."]], [["The change that came upon Ben-Hur was wonderful to see, it was so instant and extreme.", "벤허에게 일어난 변화는 보기에 놀라웠으니, 그토록 순식간이었고 그토록 극단적이었다. 목소리가 날카로워졌다."], ["The voice sharpened; the hands arose tight-clenched; every fibre thrilled; his eyes inflamed.", "두 손이 꽉 쥐어진 채 올라갔다. 온몸의 섬유가 떨렸다. 두 눈이 불타올랐다."]], [["“Thou hast heard of the God of my fathers,”", "\"당신은 제 조상의 하느님에 관해 들으셨을 것입니다.\""], ["he said; “of the infinite Jehovah.", "그가 말했다. \"무한하신 여호와에 관해 말입니다."], ["By his truth and almightiness, and by the love with which he hath followed Israel from the beginning, I swear I am innocent!”", "그분의 진리와 전능하심으로, 그리고 그분이 태초부터 이스라엘을 좇으신 그 사랑으로 맹세하건대, 저는 무죄입니다!\""]], [["The tribune was much moved.", "호민관은 크게 마음이 움직였다."]], [["“O noble Roman!”", "\"오, 고귀한 로마인이시여!\""], ["continued Ben-Hur, “give me a little faith, and, into my darkness, deeper darkening every day, send a light!”", "벤허가 말을 이었다. \"제게 작은 믿음이라도 베푸시어, 날마다 더 짙어지는 제 어둠 속으로 한 줄기 빛을 보내 주십시오!\""]], [["Arrius turned away, and walked the deck.", "아리우스는 몸을 돌려 갑판을 거닐었다."]], [["“Didst thou not have a trial?”", "\"너는 재판을 받지 못했느냐?\""], ["he asked, stopping suddenly. “No!”", "그가 문득 걸음을 멈추고 물었다."]], [["The Roman raised his head, surprised.", "\"못 했습니다!\" 로마인은 놀라 고개를 들었다."]], [["“No trial—no witnesses!", "\"재판도—증인도 없이!"], ["Who passed judgment upon thee?”", "누가 네게 판결을 내렸느냐?\""]], [["Romans, it should be remembered, were at no time such lovers of the law and its forms as in the ages of their decay.", "기억해 둘 것은, 로마인은 그 어느 때에도 쇠퇴해 가던 시대만큼 법과 그 형식을 사랑한 적이 없었다는 사실이다."]], [["“They bound me with cords, and dragged me to a vault in the Tower. I saw no one.", "\"그들은 저를 밧줄로 묶어 탑 안의 지하 감옥으로 끌고 갔습니다. 저는 아무도 보지 못했습니다."], ["No one spoke to me. Next day soldiers took me to the seaside.", "누구도 제게 말을 걸지 않았습니다. 이튿날 병사들이 저를 바닷가로 데려갔습니다."], ["I have been a galley-slave ever since.”", "그 뒤로 저는 줄곧 갤리선의 노예였습니다.\""]], [["“What couldst thou have proven?”", "\"무엇을 입증할 수 있었겠느냐?\""]], [["“I was a boy, too young to be a conspirator.", "\"저는 소년이었습니다. 음모를 꾸미기에는 너무 어렸습니다."], ["Gratus was a stranger to me.", "그라투스는 제게 낯선 사람이었습니다."], ["If I had meant to kill him, that was not the time or the place.", "제가 그를 죽이려 했다 한들, 그때 그 자리는 적당하지 않았습니다."], ["He was riding in the midst of a legion, and it was broad day.", "그는 군단 한가운데서 말을 타고 있었고, 환한 대낮이었습니다."], ["I could not have escaped.", "저는 달아날 수도 없었을 것입니다."], ["I was of a class most friendly to Rome.", "저는 로마에 가장 우호적인 계층이었습니다."], ["My father had been distinguished for his services to the emperor. We had a great estate to lose.", "제 아버지는 황제께 바친 공로로 명성을 떨친 분이었습니다. 우리에게는 잃을 수 있는 큰 재산이 있었습니다."], ["Ruin was certain to myself, my mother, my sister.", "저 자신과 어머니와 누이에게는 파멸이 확실했습니다."], ["I had no cause for malice, while every consideration—property, family, life, conscience, the Law—to a son of Israel as the breath of his nostrils—would have stayed my hand, though the foul intent had been ever so strong. I was not mad.", "저는 악의를 품을 까닭이 없었고, 온갖 것이—재산, 가문, 목숨, 양심, 그리고 이스라엘의 아들에게는 콧김과도 같은 율법이—설령 그 더러운 뜻이 아무리 강했다 한들 제 손을 막았을 것입니다. 저는 미치지 않았습니다."], ["Death was preferable to shame; and, believe me, I pray, it is so yet.”", "수치보다는 죽음이 나았고, 부디 믿어 주십시오, 지금도 그러합니다.\""]], [["“Who was with thee when the blow was struck?”", "\"그 일격이 가해졌을 때 누가 너와 함께 있었느냐?\""]], [["“I was on the house-top—my father’s house.", "\"저는 지붕 위에 있었습니다—제 아버지의 집 지붕이었습니다."], ["Tirzah was with me—at my side—the soul of gentleness.", "티르자가 저와 함께 있었습니다—제 곁에 말입니다—온유함 그 자체였지요."], ["Together we leaned over the parapet to see the legion pass.", "우리는 함께 난간에 기대어 군단이 지나가는 것을 내려다보고 있었습니다."], ["A tile gave way under my hand, and fell upon Gratus.", "제 손 밑에서 기와 한 장이 빠져나가 그라투스 위로 떨어졌습니다."], ["I thought I had killed him.", "저는 그를 죽인 줄 알았습니다."], ["Ah, what horror I felt!”", "아, 제가 느낀 그 공포라니!\""]], [["“Where was thy mother?”", "\"네 어머니는 어디에 계셨느냐?\""]], [["“In her chamber below.”", "\"아래층 방에 계셨습니다.\""]], [["“What became of her?”", "\"어머니는 어찌 되었느냐?\""]], [["Ben-Hur clenched his hands, and drew a breath like a gasp.", "벤허는 두 손을 꽉 쥐고, 숨을 헐떡이듯 들이쉬었다."]], [["“I do not know. I saw them drag her away—that is all I know.", "\"모릅니다. 그들이 어머니를 끌고 가는 것을 보았을 뿐, 그것이 제가 아는 전부입니다."], ["Out of the house they drove every living thing, even the dumb cattle, and they sealed the gates.", "그들은 집 안의 살아 있는 모든 것을, 말 못 하는 가축까지도 몰아내고, 대문을 봉인했습니다."], ["The purpose was that she should not return. I, too, ask for her.", "어머니가 돌아오지 못하게 하려는 것이었습니다. 저 역시 어머니의 소식을 구합니다."], ["Oh for one word!", "오, 한마디만이라도!"], ["She, at least, was innocent.", "적어도 그분은 죄가 없었습니다."], ["I can forgive—but I pray thy pardon, noble tribune!", "저는 용서할 수 있습니다—하나 용서를 빕니다, 고귀한 호민관님!"], ["A slave like me should not talk of forgiveness or of revenge.", "저 같은 노예가 용서나 복수를 입에 담아서는 안 되겠지요."], ["I am bound to an oar for life.”", "저는 평생 노에 매인 몸이니까요.\""]], [["Arrius listened intently. He brought all his experience with slaves to his aid.", "아리우스는 골똘히 귀를 기울였다. 노예들을 다뤄 온 모든 경험을 끌어모았다."], ["If the feeling shown in this instance were assumed, the acting was perfect; on the other hand, if it were real, the Jew’s innocence might not be doubted; and if he were innocent, with what blind fury the power had been exercised!", "만일 이번 경우에 드러난 감정이 꾸며 낸 것이라면, 그 연기는 완벽했다. 반대로 그것이 진실이라면, 유대인의 무죄는 의심할 수 없을 터였다. 그리고 그가 무죄라면, 권력이 얼마나 눈먼 광기로 휘둘러졌단 말인가!"], ["A whole family blotted out to atone an accident!", "한낱 우연한 사고를 갚으려고 한 가문 전체를 깡그리 지워 버리다니!"], ["The thought shocked him.", "그 생각에 그는 충격을 받았다."]], [["There is no wiser providence than that our occupations, however rude or bloody, cannot wear us out morally; that such qualities as justice and mercy, if they really possess us, continue to live on under them, like flowers under the snow.", "우리의 생업이 아무리 거칠거나 피비린내 나는 것이라 해도 우리를 도덕적으로 닳아 없애지 못한다는 것, 정의와 자비 같은 자질이 진정 우리 안에 깃들어 있다면 그것들이 눈 아래 피어 있는 꽃처럼 그 밑에서 계속 살아간다는 것보다 더 지혜로운 섭리는 없다."], ["The tribune could be inexorable, else he had not been fit for the usages of his calling; he could also be just; and to excite his sense of wrong was to put him in the way to right the wrong.", "호민관은 가차 없을 수도 있었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그의 직분이 요구하는 관례에 맞지 않았을 것이다. 그는 또한 공정할 수도 있었다. 그러니 그의 불의에 대한 감각을 일깨우는 것은 곧 그를 그 불의를 바로잡는 길로 들어서게 하는 것이었다."], ["The crews of the ships in which he served came after a time to speak of him as the good tribune.", "그가 복무한 배들의 선원들은 얼마 뒤 그를 가리켜 선량한 호민관이라 일컫게 되었다."], ["Shrewd readers will not want a better definition of his character.", "예리한 독자라면 그의 성품에 대해 이보다 나은 정의를 바라지 않을 것이다."]], [["In this instance there were many circumstances certainly in the young man’s favor, and some to be supposed.", "이번 경우에는 분명 그 젊은이에게 유리한 정황이 많았고, 짐작으로 헤아릴 만한 것도 몇 가지 있었다."], ["Possibly Arrius knew Valerius Gratus without loving him.", "어쩌면 아리우스는 발레리우스 그라투스를 알면서도 좋아하지 않았는지 모른다."], ["Possibly he had known the elder Hur.", "어쩌면 그는 늙은 후르를 알고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["In the course of his appeal, Judah had asked him of that; and, as will be noticed, he had made no reply.", "유다는 호소하는 가운데 그에게 그 점을 물었으나, 눈여겨보았다시피 그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["For once the tribune was at loss, and hesitated.", "이번만큼은 호민관도 어쩔 줄을 몰라 망설였다. 그의 권한은 충분했다."], ["His power was ample.", "그는 이 배의 군주였다."], ["He was monarch of the ship.", "그의 모든 선입견이 그를 자비 쪽으로 이끌었다."], ["His prepossessions all moved him to mercy. His faith was won.", "그의 믿음은 이미 얻어졌다. 그럼에도 그는 마음속으로 생각했다."], ["Yet, he said to himself, there was no haste—or, rather, there was haste to Cythera; the best rower could not then be spared; he would wait; he would learn more; he would at least be sure this was the prince Ben-Hur, and that he was of a right disposition.", "서두를 일은 아니다—아니, 오히려 키테라로 가는 데에는 서둘러야 한다. 그러니 지금은 가장 뛰어난 노잡이를 내줄 수 없다. 기다리리라."], ["Ordinarily, slaves were liars.", "더 알아보리라. 적어도 이자가 정녕 후르 가문의 제후인지, 또 올바른 성품을 지녔는지 확인하리라. 노예란 으레 거짓말쟁이였으니까."]], [["“It is enough,”", "\"이만하면 되었다.\""], ["he said aloud. “Go back to thy place.”", "그가 소리 내어 말했다. \"네 자리로 돌아가라.\""]], [["Ben-Hur bowed; looked once more into the master’s face, but saw nothing for hope.", "벤허는 고개를 숙였다. 주인의 얼굴을 다시 한번 들여다보았으나, 희망을 줄 만한 것은 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["He turned away slowly, looked back, and said,", "그는 천천히 몸을 돌렸다가, 다시 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“If thou dost think of me again, O tribune, let it not be lost in thy mind that I prayed thee only for word of my people—mother, sister.”", "\"호민관님께서 저를 다시 떠올리실 때가 있거든, 제가 오직 제 식구들—어머니와 누이의 소식만을 청했음을 마음에서 잊지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["He moved on.", "그는 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Arrius followed him with admiring eyes.", "아리우스는 감탄 어린 눈으로 그를 뒤따라 바라보았다."]], [["“Perpol!”", "\"페르폴!\""], ["he thought.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["“With teaching, what a man for the arena!", "\"가르치기만 한다면, 검투장에 얼마나 어울리는 사내인가!"], ["What a runner! Ye gods!", "얼마나 잘 달리는가! 신들이여!"], ["what an arm for the sword or the cestus!—Stay!”", "검이나 케스투스를 쥐기에 얼마나 좋은 팔인가!—멈춰라!\""], ["he said aloud.", "그가 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["Ben-Hur stopped, and the tribune went to him.", "벤허가 걸음을 멈추자, 호민관이 그에게로 다가갔다."]], [["“If thou wert free, what wouldst thou do?”", "\"네가 자유의 몸이라면 무엇을 하겠느냐?\""]], [["“The noble Arrius mocks me!” Judah said, with trembling lips.", "\"고귀한 아리우스님께서 저를 놀리시는군요!\" 유다가 입술을 떨며 말했다."]], [["“No; by the gods, no!”", "\"아니다. 신들에게 맹세코, 아니다!\""]], [["“Then I will answer gladly.", "\"그렇다면 기꺼이 대답하겠습니다."], ["I would give myself to duty the first of life.", "저는 삶의 무엇보다 먼저 의무에 저를 바치겠습니다."], ["I would know no other.", "다른 것은 알지 않겠습니다."], ["I would know no rest until my mother and Tirzah were restored to home.", "어머니와 티르자를 집으로 되찾아 올 때까지는 안식을 모르겠습니다."], ["I would give every day and hour to their happiness.", "그들의 행복을 위해 하루하루, 한 시간 한 시간을 다 바치겠습니다."], ["I would wait upon them; never a slave more faithful.", "그들을 받들겠습니다. 그보다 더 충실한 종은 없을 것입니다."], ["They have lost much, but, by the God of my fathers, I would find them more!”", "그들은 많은 것을 잃었으나, 제 조상의 하느님께 맹세코, 저는 그들에게 더 많은 것을 찾아 주겠습니다!\""]], [["The answer was unexpected by the Roman.", "그 대답은 로마인이 예상하지 못한 것이었다."], ["For a moment he lost his purpose.", "한순간 그는 자신의 의도를 잃어버렸다."]], [["“I spoke to thy ambition,”", "\"나는 네 야망을 두고 물은 것이다.\""], ["he said, recovering.", "그가 마음을 가다듬으며 말했다."], ["“If thy mother and sister were dead, or not to be found, what wouldst thou do?”", "\"만일 네 어머니와 누이가 죽었거나 찾을 수 없게 되었다면, 너는 무엇을 하겠느냐?\""]], [["A distinct pallor overspread Ben-Hur’s face, and he looked over the sea.", "벤허의 얼굴에 또렷한 창백함이 번졌고, 그는 바다 너머를 바라보았다."], ["There was a struggle with some strong feeling; when it was conquered, he turned to the tribune.", "어떤 강한 감정과의 싸움이 있었다. 그것을 이겨 내고 나서, 그는 호민관에게로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“What pursuit would I follow?”", "\"제가 어떤 길을 좇겠느냐는 말씀이신지요?\""], ["he asked. “Yes.”", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Tribune, I will tell thee truly.", "\"그렇다.\" \"호민관님, 솔직히 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Only the night before the dreadful day of which I have spoken, I obtained permission to be a soldier.", "제가 말씀드린 그 끔찍한 날 바로 전날 밤에, 저는 군인이 되도 좋다는 허락을 받았습니다."], ["I am of the same mind yet; and, as in all the earth there is but one school of war, thither I would go.”", "지금도 같은 마음입니다. 그리고 온 땅에 전쟁을 배우는 학교는 오직 하나뿐이니, 저는 그곳으로 가겠습니다.\""]], [["“The palaestra!”", "\"팔라이스트라 말이냐!\""], ["exclaimed Arrius.", "아리우스가 소리쳤다."]], [["“No; a Roman camp.”", "\"아닙니다. 로마의 진영입니다.\""]], [["“But thou must first acquaint thyself with the use of arms.”", "\"그러나 너는 먼저 무기 다루는 법부터 익혀야 한다.\""]], [["Now a master may never safely advise a slave.", "그런데 주인이 노예에게 충고하는 것은 결코 안전한 일이 아니다."], ["Arrius saw his indiscretion, and, in a breath, chilled his voice and manner.", "아리우스는 자신의 경솔함을 깨닫고, 단숨에 목소리와 태도를 차갑게 굳혔다."]], [["“Go now,”", "\"이제 가거라.\""], ["he said, “and do not build upon what has passed between us.", "그가 말했다. \"우리 사이에 오간 말을 두고 무엇을 쌓아 올리지는 말아라."], ["Perhaps I do but play with thee. Or”—he looked away musingly—“or, if thou dost think of it with any hope, choose between the renown of a gladiator and the service of a soldier.", "어쩌면 나는 그저 너를 가지고 노는 것일 뿐인지도 모른다. 아니면\"—그는 생각에 잠긴 듯 시선을 돌렸다—\"아니면, 네가 그것을 두고 조금이라도 희망을 품는다면, 검투사의 명성과 군인의 복무 가운데 하나를 골라라."], ["The former may come of the favor of the emperor; there is no reward for thee in the latter.", "앞의 것은 황제의 총애에서 나올 수 있다. 뒤의 것에는 네게 돌아갈 보상이 없다."], ["Thou art not a Roman. Go!”", "너는 로마인이 아니니까. 가거라!\""]], [["A short while after Ben-Hur was upon his bench again.", "얼마 지나지 않아 벤허는 다시 자기 자리에 앉아 있었다."]], [["A man’s task is always light if his heart is light.", "마음이 가벼우면 사람의 일이란 언제나 수월한 법이다."], ["Handling the oar did not seem so toilsome to Judah.", "노를 다루는 것이 유다에게 그리 고되게 느껴지지 않았다."], ["A hope had come to him, like a singing bird.", "한 가닥 희망이, 노래하는 새처럼 그에게 찾아왔다."], ["He could hardly see the visitor or hear its song; that it was there, though, he knew; his feelings told him so.", "그는 그 손님을 거의 보지도 못했고 그 노래를 듣지도 못했다. 그러나 그것이 거기 있다는 것만은 알았다. 그의 느낌이 그렇게 일러 주었다."], ["The caution of the tribune—“Perhaps I do but play with thee”—was dismissed often as it recurred to his mind.", "\"어쩌면 나는 그저 너를 가지고 노는 것일 뿐인지도 모른다\"라던 호민관의 경고는 떠오를 때마다 번번이 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["That he had been called by the great man and asked his story was the bread upon which he fed his hungry spirit.", "그 위대한 사람에게 불려 가 자기 이야기를 청해 받았다는 사실, 그것이 그의 굶주린 영혼을 먹이는 빵이었다."], ["Surely something good would come of it.", "분명 거기서 무언가 좋은 일이 나오리라."], ["The light about his bench was clear and bright with promises, and he prayed.", "그의 자리를 둘러싼 빛은 약속으로 맑고 환했으니, 그는 기도했다."]], [["“O God! I am a true son of the Israel thou hast so loved!", "\"오, 하느님! 저는 당신이 그토록 사랑하신 이스라엘의 참된 아들입니다!"], ["Help me, I pray thee!”", "비오니, 저를 도와주소서!\""]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["In the Bay of Antemona, east of Cythera the island, the hundred galleys assembled.", "키테라섬 동쪽 안테모나만에 백 척의 갤리선이 집결했다."], ["There the tribune gave one day to inspection.", "그곳에서 호민관은 하루를 점검에 바쳤다."], ["He sailed then to Naxos, the largest of the Cyclades, midway the coasts of Greece and Asia, like a great stone planted in the centre of a highway, from which he could challenge everything that passed; at the same time, he would be in position to go after the pirates instantly, whether they were in the Ægean or out on the Mediterranean.", "그런 다음 그는 그리스와 아시아 해안 한가운데, 마치 큰길 복판에 박아 놓은 거대한 돌처럼 자리한 키클라데스 제도에서 가장 큰 섬 낙소스로 배를 몰았다. 그곳에서라면 지나는 모든 것을 검문할 수 있었고, 동시에 해적들이 에게해에 있든 지중해 바깥에 나가 있든 즉시 그들을 뒤쫓을 수 있는 위치를 차지하게 되는 셈이었다."]], [["As the fleet, in order, rowed in towards the mountain shores of the island, a galley was descried coming from the north.", "함대가 대열을 이루어 섬의 산악 해안을 향해 노를 저어 들어가고 있을 때, 북쪽에서 다가오는 갤리선 한 척이 눈에 띄었다."], ["Arrius went to meet it. She proved to be a transport just from Byzantium, and from her commander he learned the particulars of which he stood in most need.", "아리우스는 그 배를 맞으러 나갔다. 알고 보니 비잔티움에서 막 떠나온 수송선이었고, 그 선장에게서 그는 가장 필요로 하던 자세한 소식을 들을 수 있었다."]], [["The pirates were from all the farther shores of the Euxine.", "해적들은 흑해의 더 먼 모든 해안에서 모여든 자들이었다."], ["Even Tanais, at the mouth of the river which was supposed to feed Palus Maeotis, was represented among them.", "팔루스 마이오티스에 물을 댄다고 여겨지던 강어귀의 타나이스조차도 그들 가운데 끼어 있었다."], ["Their preparations had been with the greatest secrecy.", "그들의 준비는 더할 나위 없이 은밀하게 이루어졌다."], ["The first known of them was their appearance off the entrance to the Thracian Bosphorus, followed by the destruction of the fleet in station there.", "그들의 존재가 처음 알려진 것은 트라키아 보스포루스 입구 앞바다에 그 모습이 나타나면서였고, 곧이어 그곳에 주둔하던 함대가 격파되었다."], ["Thence to the outlet of the Hellespont everything afloat had fallen their prey.", "그곳에서 헬레스폰토스 출구에 이르기까지 물 위에 떠 있던 모든 것이 그들의 먹이가 되었다."], ["There were quite sixty galleys in the squadron, all well manned and supplied.", "그 선단에는 갤리선이 족히 예순 척은 되었고, 하나같이 사람과 물자를 잘 갖추고 있었다."], ["A few were biremes, the rest stout triremes.", "몇 척은 이단노선이고, 나머지는 튼튼한 삼단노선이었다."], ["A Greek was in command, and the pilots, said to be familiar with all the Eastern seas, were Greek.", "지휘는 한 그리스인이 맡았고, 동방의 모든 바다에 훤하다는 키잡이들 또한 그리스인이었다."], ["The plunder had been incalculable.", "약탈한 재물은 헤아릴 수 없었다."], ["The panic, consequently, was not on the sea alone; cities, with closed gates, sent their people nightly to the walls.", "그러니 공포는 바다에만 머물지 않았다. 도시들은 성문을 닫아걸고 밤마다 백성을 성벽으로 내보냈다."], ["Traffic had almost ceased.", "교역은 거의 끊기다시피 했다."]], [["Where were the pirates now?", "그렇다면 해적들은 지금 어디에 있는가?"]], [["To this question, of most interest to Arrius, he received answer.", "아리우스에게 가장 중요한 이 물음에 그는 답을 얻었다."]], [["After sacking Hephaestia, on the island of Lemnos, the enemy had coursed across to the Thessalian group, and, by last account, disappeared in the gulfs between Euboea and Hellas.", "적은 렘노스섬의 헤파이스티아를 약탈한 뒤 테살리아 군도로 건너갔고, 마지막으로 들어온 소식에 따르면 에우보이아와 헬라스 사이의 만으로 자취를 감추었다고 했다."]], [["Such were the tidings.", "이런 소식이었다."]], [["Then the people of the island, drawn to the hill-tops by the rare spectacle of a hundred ships careering in united squadron, beheld the advance division suddenly turn to the north, and the others follow, wheeling upon the same point like cavalry in a column.", "그러자 백 척의 배가 하나의 선단을 이루어 질주하는 보기 드문 광경에 이끌려 산꼭대기로 모여든 섬사람들은, 선두 분대가 갑자기 북쪽으로 방향을 트는 모습과 다른 배들이 그 뒤를 따라 마치 종대의 기병처럼 같은 지점에서 선회하는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["News of the piratical descent had reached them, and now, watching the white sails until they faded from sight up between Rhene and Syros, the thoughtful among them took comfort, and were grateful.", "해적의 습격 소식은 그들에게도 닿아 있었고, 이제 레네와 시로스 사이로 그 흰 돛이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 바라보던 사려 깊은 이들은 위안을 얻고 고마워했다."], ["What Rome seized with strong hand she always defended: in return for their taxes, she gave them safety.", "로마는 강한 손으로 움켜쥔 것을 언제나 지켜 냈다. 세금을 받는 대신 그들에게 안전을 베풀었던 것이다."]], [["The tribune was more than pleased with the enemy’s movements; he was doubly thankful to Fortune.", "호민관은 적의 움직임에 만족하는 정도가 아니었다. 그는 운명의 여신에게 거듭 감사했다."], ["She had brought swift and sure intelligence, and had lured his foes into the waters where, of all others, destruction was most assured.", "여신은 빠르고 확실한 정보를 가져다주었고, 적들을 다른 어느 곳보다도 파멸이 가장 확실하게 보장된 바다로 꾀어들였다."], ["He knew the havoc one galley could play in a broad sea like the Mediterranean, and the difficulty of finding and overhauling her; he knew, also, how those very circumstances would enhance the service and glory if, at one blow, he could put a finish to the whole piratical array.", "그는 갤리선 한 척이 지중해처럼 너른 바다에서 얼마나 큰 화를 입힐 수 있는지, 또 그것을 찾아내고 따라잡기가 얼마나 어려운지 알고 있었다. 그는 또한 단 한 번의 일격으로 해적 무리 전체를 끝장낼 수만 있다면, 바로 그러한 사정이 그 공훈과 영광을 얼마나 드높여 줄지도 알고 있었다."]], [["If the reader will take a map of Greece and the Ægean, he will notice the island of Euboea lying along the classic coast like a rampart against Asia, leaving a channel between it and the continent quite a hundred and twenty miles in length, and scarcely an average of eight in width.", "독자가 그리스와 에게해의 지도를 펼쳐 본다면, 에우보이아섬이 마치 아시아를 막는 성벽처럼 고전적 해안을 따라 길게 누워 있는 모습을 보게 될 것이다. 그 섬과 대륙 사이에는 길이가 족히 백이십 마일에 이르고 너비는 평균 팔 마일이 채 못 되는 해협이 나 있다."], ["The inlet on the north had admitted the fleet of Xerxes, and now it received the bold raiders from the Euxine.", "북쪽 어귀는 일찍이 크세르크세스의 함대를 받아들인 적이 있었고, 이제 흑해에서 온 대담한 침략자들을 받아들였다."], ["The towns along the Pelasgic and Meliac gulfs were rich and their plunder seductive.", "펠라스기아만과 멜리아만을 따라 늘어선 도시들은 부유했고, 그곳에서 거둘 약탈물은 사람을 흘릴 만했다."], ["All things considered, therefore, Arrius judged that the robbers might be found somewhere below Thermopylae.", "그러므로 모든 정황을 헤아린 끝에 아리우스는 도적들이 테르모필레 아래 어딘가에 있으리라고 판단했다."], ["Welcoming the chance, he resolved to enclose them north and south, to do which not an hour could be lost; even the fruits and wines and women of Naxos must be left behind.", "그 기회를 반기며 그는 그들을 남북에서 에워싸기로 결심했고, 그러자면 한시도 지체할 수 없었다. 낙소스의 과일도 포도주도 여인들도 뒤에 남겨 두어야 했다."], ["So he sailed away without stop or tack until, a little before nightfall, Mount Ocha was seen upreared against the sky, and the pilot reported the Euboean coast.", "그리하여 그는 멈추지도 침로를 바꾸지도 않고 배를 몰아, 마침내 해 질 무렵 조금 못 미쳐 오카산이 하늘을 배경으로 솟아오른 것이 보였고 키잡이가 에우보이아 해안이라고 알려 왔다."]], [["At a signal the fleet rested upon its oars.", "신호가 떨어지자 함대는 노를 멈추고 쉬었다."], ["When the movement was resumed, Arrius led a division of fifty of the galleys, intending to take them up the channel, while another division, equally strong, turned their prows to the outer or seaward side of the island, with orders to make all haste to the upper inlet, and descend sweeping the waters.", "다시 움직이기 시작하자 아리우스는 갤리선 쉰 척의 분대를 이끌고 해협을 거슬러 올라가려 했고, 그에 못지않게 강한 또 한 분대는 뱃머리를 섬의 바깥쪽, 곧 바다 쪽으로 돌려, 위쪽 어귀까지 한껏 서둘러 가서 물길을 쓸어내리며 내려오라는 명령을 받았다."]], [["To be sure, neither division was equal in number to the pirates; but each had advantages in compensation, among them, by no means least, a discipline impossible to a lawless horde, however brave.", "물론 어느 분대도 수에서는 해적에 미치지 못했다. 그러나 저마다 그것을 메울 이점이 있었으니, 그 가운데 결코 작지 않은 것이 아무리 용맹할지언정 무법한 무리에게는 불가능한 군기였다."], ["Besides, it was a shrewd count on the tribune’s side, if, peradventure, one should be defeated, the other would find the enemy shattered by his victory, and in condition to be easily overwhelmed.", "게다가 만에 하나 한쪽이 패한다 해도 다른 한쪽은 승리로 만신창이가 된 적을 만나 손쉽게 제압할 형편이 되리라는 것이, 호민관 편에서 보면 빈틈없는 계산이었다."]], [["Meantime Ben-Hur kept his bench, relieved every six hours.", "그동안 벤허는 자기 자리를 지키며 여섯 시간마다 교대했다."], ["The rest in the Bay of Antemona had freshened him, so that the oar was not troublesome, and the chief on the platform found no fault.", "안테모나만에서의 휴식이 그를 새로 기운 차리게 해 준 덕에 노 젓는 일도 그리 힘들지 않았고, 단상의 우두머리도 트집 잡을 데가 없었다."]], [["People, generally, are not aware of the ease of mind there is in knowing where they are, and where they are going.", "사람들은 대개 자신이 어디에 있고 어디로 가는지를 아는 데서 오는 마음의 편안함을 알지 못한다."], ["The sensation of being lost is a keen distress; still worse is the feeling one has in driving blindly into unknown places.", "길을 잃었다는 느낌은 견디기 힘든 고통이다. 그보다 더 나쁜 것은 알지 못하는 곳으로 눈먼 채 내몰리는 느낌이다."], ["Custom had dulled the feeling with Ben-Hur, but only measurably.", "벤허에게는 그런 느낌이 습관으로 무뎌져 있었으나, 어느 정도까지만 그러했다."], ["Pulling away hour after hour, sometimes days and nights together, sensible all the time that the galley was gliding swiftly along some of the many tracks of the broad sea, the longing to know where he was, and whither going, was always present with him; but now it seemed quickened by the hope which had come to new life in his breast since the interview with the tribune.", "시간이 흐르고 흐르도록, 때로는 밤낮을 연달아 노를 저으면서, 갤리선이 너른 바다의 수많은 항로 가운데 어느 하나를 따라 빠르게 미끄러져 가고 있음을 줄곧 의식하던 그에게, 자신이 어디에 있고 어디로 가는지 알고 싶은 갈망은 늘 함께 있었다. 그러나 이제 그 갈망은 호민관과 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 그의 가슴속에 새로 살아난 희망에 떠밀려 한결 날카로워진 듯했다. 머무는 곳이 좁으면 좁을수록 그 갈망은 더 강렬해지는 법이니, 그도 그러했다."], ["The narrower the abiding-place happens to be, the more intense is the longing; and so he found.", "그는 힘겹게 나아가는 배의 온갖 소리를 하나하나 듣는 듯했고, 그 소리 하나하나가 자기에게 무언가를 일러 주러 온 목소리이기라도 한 듯 귀를 기울였다."], ["He seemed to hear every sound of the ship in labor, and listened to each one as if it were a voice come to tell him something; he looked to the grating overhead, and through it into the light of which so small a portion was his, expecting, he knew not what; and many times he caught himself on the point of yielding to the impulse to speak to the chief on the platform, than which no circumstance of battle would have astonished that dignitary more.", "그는 머리 위의 격자창을 올려다보고, 그 사이로 새어 드는, 자기 몫이라고는 그토록 적은 빛 속을 들여다보며, 무엇인지도 모를 무엇을 기다렸다. 그러다 여러 번, 단상의 우두머리에게 말을 걸고 싶은 충동에 굴복하려는 자신을 깨닫고 멈칫했다. 전투의 어떤 정황도 그런 일만큼 그 위엄 있는 자를 놀라게 하지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["In his long service, by watching the shifting of the meager sunbeams upon the cabin floor when the ship was under way, he had come to know, generally, the quarter into which she was sailing.", "오랜 복역 동안 그는, 배가 움직일 때 선실 바닥에 어른거리는 가느다란 햇살이 옮겨 가는 것을 지켜봄으로써, 배가 대체로 어느 방향으로 항해하는지를 가늠하게 되었다."], ["This, of course, was only of clear days like those good-fortune was sending the tribune.", "물론 이것은 운명의 여신이 호민관에게 보내 주고 있는 것과 같은 맑은 날에나 가능한 일이었다."], ["The experience had not failed him in the period succeeding the departure from Cythera.", "키테라를 떠난 뒤 이어진 동안에도 그 경험은 그를 저버리지 않았다."], ["Thinking they were tending towards the old Judean country, he was sensitive to every variation from the course.", "그들이 옛 유대 땅을 향해 가고 있다고 여기던 그는 침로의 변화라면 무엇 하나에도 예민했다."], ["With a pang, he had observed the sudden change northward which, as has been noticed, took place near Naxos: the cause, however, he could not even conjecture; for it must be remembered that, in common with his fellow-slaves, he knew nothing of the situation, and had no interest in the voyage.", "앞서 본 대로 낙소스 가까이에서 일어난 그 갑작스러운 북향에 그는 가슴이 저리도록 마음 졸였다. 그러나 그 까닭은 짐작조차 할 수 없었다. 기억해 둘 것은, 그가 동료 노예들과 마찬가지로 형편을 전혀 모르고 항해에는 아무 관심도 없었다는 사실이다."], ["His place was at the oar, and he was held there inexorably, whether at anchor or under sail.", "그의 자리는 노 옆이었고, 닻을 내리든 돛을 펴든 그는 거기에 가차 없이 붙들려 있었다."], ["Once only in three years had he been permitted an outlook from the deck.", "삼 년 동안 단 한 번 그는 갑판에서 바깥을 내다보도록 허락받았을 뿐이다."], ["The occasion we have seen.", "그 경우는 우리가 이미 보았다."], ["He had no idea that, following the vessel he was helping drive, there was a great squadron close at hand and in beautiful order; no more did he know the object of which it was in pursuit.", "그는 자신이 함께 몰아가는 배의 뒤를 따라 거대한 선단이 바로 곁에서 아름다운 대열을 이루며 따라오고 있다는 것을 짐작도 못 했다. 그 선단이 무엇을 쫓고 있는지는 더더욱 알지 못했다."]], [["When the sun, going down, withdrew his last ray from the cabin, the galley still held northward.", "해가 기울며 선실에서 마지막 빛줄기를 거두어 갈 때에도 갤리선은 여전히 북쪽으로 나아가고 있었다."], ["Night fell, yet Ben-Hur could discern no change.", "밤이 내렸으나 벤허는 아무런 변화도 알아챌 수 없었다."], ["About that time the smell of incense floated down the gangways from the deck.", "그 무렵 갑판에서 통로를 따라 향 냄새가 흘러 내려왔다."]], [["“The tribune is at the altar,”", "\"호민관이 제단 앞에 있구나.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“Can it be we are going into battle?”", "\"혹시 우리가 전투에 들어가는 것일까?\""]], [["He became observant.", "그는 주의를 곤두세웠다."]], [["Now he had been in many battles without having seen one.", "지금껏 그는 숱한 전투를 겪었으나 단 한 번도 그것을 본 적은 없었다."], ["From his bench he had heard them above and about him, until he was familiar with all their notes, almost as a singer with a song.", "자기 자리에서 그는 머리 위와 사방에서 벌어지는 전투 소리를 들었고, 마침내 그 온갖 가락에, 마치 가수가 노래에 익숙해지듯 훤해졌다."], ["So, too, he had become acquainted with many of the preliminaries of an engagement, of which, with a Roman as well as a Greek, the most invariable was the sacrifice to the gods.", "그렇게 그는 교전에 앞서는 여러 절차에도 익숙해졌는데, 로마인이든 그리스인이든 그 가운데 가장 변함없는 것이 신들에게 바치는 제사였다."], ["The rites were the same as those performed at the beginning of a voyage, and to him, when noticed, they were always an admonition.", "그 의식은 항해를 시작할 때 치르는 것과 같았으니, 그것을 알아챌 때면 그에게는 늘 하나의 경고였다."]], [["A battle, it should be observed, possessed for him and his fellow-slaves of the oar an interest unlike that of the sailor and marine; it came, not of the danger encountered but of the fact that defeat, if survived, might bring an alteration of condition—possibly freedom—at least a change of masters, which might be for the better.", "짚어 둘 것은, 전투란 그와 노 젓는 동료 노예들에게는 선원이나 해병과는 다른 의미를 지녔다는 점이다. 그것은 맞닥뜨릴 위험에서 오는 것이 아니라, 패하더라도 살아남으면 처지가 바뀔 수도 있다는—어쩌면 자유를, 적어도 더 나아질지도 모를 주인의 교체를 가져올 수도 있다는 사실에서 오는 것이었다."]], [["In good time the lanterns were lighted and hung by the stairs, and the tribune came down from the deck.", "때가 되자 등불이 켜져 계단 곁에 매달렸고, 호민관이 갑판에서 내려왔다."], ["At his word the marines put on their armor.", "그의 말 한마디에 해병들이 갑옷을 걸쳤다."], ["At his word again, the machines were looked to, and spears, javelins, and arrows, in great sheaves, brought and laid upon the floor, together with jars of inflammable oil, and baskets of cotton balls wound loose like the wicking of candles.", "다시 그의 말 한마디에 투석기들이 점검되었고, 창과 투창과 화살이 큼직한 다발로 운반되어 바닥에 놓였다. 함께 인화성 기름 항아리와 양초 심지처럼 느슨하게 감은 솜뭉치를 담은 바구니도 놓였다."], ["And when, finally, Ben-Hur saw the tribune mount his platform and don his armor, and get his helmet and shield out, the meaning of the preparations might not be any longer doubted, and he made ready for the last ignominy of his service.", "그리고 마침내 벤허가 호민관이 단상에 올라 갑옷을 입고 투구와 방패를 꺼내는 것을 보았을 때, 이 준비의 뜻은 더 이상 의심할 수 없게 되었고, 그는 복역에서 마지막으로 겪을 치욕에 대비했다."]], [["To every bench, as a fixture, there was a chain with heavy anklets.", "자리마다 붙박이로 무거운 발고리가 달린 사슬이 하나씩 매여 있었다."], ["These the hortator proceeded to lock upon the oarsmen, going from number to number, leaving no choice but to obey, and, in event of disaster, no possibility of escape.", "호르타토르는 번호를 따라 차례차례 옮겨 가며 노잡이들에게 이것을 채워, 따르는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없게 하고, 재앙이 닥치더라도 달아날 길을 없애 버렸다."]], [["In the cabin, then, a silence fell, broken, at first, only by the sough of the oars turning in the leathern cases.", "그러자 선실에는 침묵이 내려앉았고, 처음에는 가죽집 안에서 돌아가는 노의 삐걱거리는 소리만이 그 침묵을 깰 뿐이었다."], ["Every man upon the benches felt the shame, Ben-Hur more keenly than his companions. He would have put it away at any price.", "자리에 앉은 모든 이가 그 수치를 느꼈고, 벤허는 누구보다도 사무치게 느꼈다. 그는 무슨 값을 치러서라도 그것을 떨쳐 버리고 싶었다."], ["Soon the clanking of the fetters notified him of the progress the chief was making in his round.", "이내 절그럭거리는 족쇄 소리가, 우두머리가 한 바퀴 도는 걸음이 어디까지 이르렀는지 그에게 알려 주었다."], ["He would come to him in turn; but would not the tribune interpose for him?", "차례가 되면 그자가 자기에게도 올 터였다. 그러나 호민관이 그를 위해 나서 주지는 않을까?"]], [["The thought may be set down to vanity or selfishness, as the reader pleases; it certainly, at that moment, took possession of Ben-Hur.", "이 생각은 독자가 마음대로 허영이든 이기심이든 부를 수 있을 것이다. 어쨌든 그 순간 그것은 벤허를 사로잡았다."], ["He believed the Roman would interpose; anyhow, the circumstance would test the man’s feelings.", "그는 로마인이 나서 주리라고 믿었다. 어찌 됐든 이 정황은 그 사람의 속마음을 시험해 볼 일이었다."], ["If, intent upon the battle, he would but think of him, it would be proof of his opinion formed—proof that he had been tacitly promoted above his associates in misery—such proof as would justify hope.", "전투에 골몰해 있으면서도 자기를 떠올려 준다면, 그것은 그가 품은 짐작이 옳다는 증거가—비참 속의 동료들보다 자기가 은연중에 높여졌다는 증거가, 희망을 품어도 좋다고 정당화해 줄 증거가 될 터였다."]], [["Ben-Hur waited anxiously.", "벤허는 애타게 기다렸다."], ["The interval seemed like an age.", "그 동안이 한 시대처럼 길게 느껴졌다."], ["At every turn of the oar he looked towards the tribune, who, his simple preparations made, lay down upon the couch and composed himself to rest; whereupon number sixty chid himself, and laughed grimly, and resolved not to look that way again.", "노를 한 번 저을 때마다 그는 호민관 쪽을 바라보았다. 호민관은 그 간단한 준비를 마치고 침상에 누워 쉴 채비를 했다. 그러자 육십 번은 스스로를 꾸짖고 쓴웃음을 지으며, 다시는 그쪽을 보지 않으리라 마음먹었다."]], [["The hortator approached.", "호르타토르가 다가왔다."], ["Now he was at number one—the rattle of the iron links sounded horribly.", "이제 그자는 일 번에 이르렀다. 쇠고리가 절그럭거리는 소리가 끔찍하게 울렸다."], ["At last number sixty!", "마침내 육십 번이었다!"], ["Calm from despair, Ben-Hur held his oar at poise, and gave his foot to the officer.", "절망에서 비롯한 평정으로 벤허는 노를 멈추어 놓고 그 관리에게 발을 내밀었다."], ["Then the tribune stirred—sat up—beckoned to the chief.", "그때 호민관이 움직이더니—몸을 일으켜—우두머리를 손짓으로 불렀다."]], [["A strong revulsion seized the Jew.", "유대인은 격한 반전에 사로잡혔다."], ["From the hortator, the great man glanced at him; and when he dropped his oar all the section of the ship on his side seemed aglow.", "호르타토르에게서 그 위대한 사람이 그에게로 눈길을 옮겼다. 그가 노를 놓자 그쪽 배의 그 구역 전체가 환히 빛나는 듯했다."], ["He heard nothing of what was said; enough that the chain hung idly from its staple in the bench, and that the chief, going to his seat, began to beat the sounding-board.", "무슨 말이 오갔는지 그는 하나도 듣지 못했다. 자기 자리의 죔쇠에 사슬이 부질없이 늘어진 채 매달려 있다는 것, 그리고 우두머리가 자기 자리로 돌아가 박자판을 두드리기 시작했다는 것으로 충분했다."], ["The notes of the gavel were never so like music.", "그 망치 소리가 그토록 음악처럼 들린 적은 없었다."], ["With his breast against the leaded handle, he pushed with all his might—pushed until the shaft bent as if about to break.", "가슴을 납으로 묵직한 손잡이에 대고 그는 온 힘을 다해 밀었다. 노깃이 곧 부러질 듯 휘어질 때까지 밀어붙였다."]], [["The chief went to the tribune, and, smiling, pointed to number sixty.", "우두머리가 호민관에게 가서 빙긋이 웃으며 육십 번을 가리켰다."]], [["“What strength!” he said.", "\"이런 힘이!\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“And what spirit!”", "\"게다가 이런 기개라니!\""], ["the tribune answered. “Perpol!", "호민관이 대답했다. \"맙소사!"], ["He is better without the irons.", "저자는 쇠고리가 없는 편이 낫겠다."], ["Put them on him no more.”", "다시는 채우지 마라.\""]], [["So saying, he stretched himself upon the couch again.", "그렇게 말하고 그는 다시 침상에 몸을 뉘었다."]], [["The ship sailed on hour after hour under the oars in water scarcely rippled by the wind.", "배는 시간이 가고 또 가도록 바람에 잔물결조차 거의 일지 않는 물 위를 노에 의지해 나아갔다."], ["And the people not on duty slept, Arrius in his place, the marines on the floor.", "그리고 근무가 없는 자들은 잠을 잤다. 아리우스는 제자리에서, 해병들은 바닥에서."]], [["Once—twice—Ben-Hur was relieved; but he could not sleep.", "한 번—두 번—벤허는 교대했다. 그러나 그는 잠들 수 없었다."], ["Three years of night, and through the darkness a sunbeam at last!", "삼 년에 걸친 밤, 그 어둠을 뚫고 마침내 한 줄기 햇살이!"], ["At sea adrift and lost, and now land!", "바다 위에 떠돌며 길을 잃었더니, 이제 뭍이!"], ["Dead so long, and, lo! the thrill and stir of resurrection.", "그토록 오래 죽어 있었더니, 보라, 부활의 전율과 설렘이!"], ["Sleep was not for such an hour.", "잠은 이런 때를 위한 것이 아니었다."], ["Hope deals with the future; now and the past are but servants that wait on her with impulse and suggestive circumstance.", "희망은 미래를 다룬다. 지금과 과거는 충동과 암시적 정황으로 그 희망을 받드는 종일 뿐이다."], ["Starting from the favor of the tribune, she carried him forward indefinitely.", "호민관의 호의에서 출발하여, 희망은 그를 한없이 앞으로 실어 갔다."], ["The wonder is, not that things so purely imaginative as the results she points us to can make us so happy, but that we can receive them as so real.", "놀라운 것은, 희망이 우리에게 가리켜 보이는 결과처럼 순전히 상상에 지나지 않는 것들이 우리를 그토록 행복하게 만들 수 있다는 사실이 아니라, 우리가 그것들을 그토록 실제처럼 받아들일 수 있다는 사실이다."], ["They must be as gorgeous poppies under the influence of which, under the crimson and purple and gold, reason lies down the while, and is not.", "그것들은 진홍빛과 자줏빛과 황금빛 아래, 그 영향에 잠겨 이성이 잠시 누워 사라져 버리게 만드는 화려한 양귀비꽃 같은 것임에 틀림없다."], ["Sorrows assuaged, home and the fortunes of his house restored; mother and sister in his arms once more—such were the central ideas which made him happier that moment than he had ever been.", "슬픔이 가시고, 집과 가문의 운수가 되살아나고, 어머니와 누이가 다시금 그의 품에 안기는 것—이런 것들이 그 순간 그를 그 어느 때보다 행복하게 만든 핵심의 상념들이었다."], ["That he was rushing, as on wings, into horrible battle had, for the time, nothing to do with his thoughts.", "자신이 날개를 단 듯 끔찍한 전투를 향해 달려가고 있다는 사실은 그때만큼은 그의 생각과 아무런 상관이 없었다."], ["The things thus in hope were unmixed with doubts—they WERE.", "이렇게 희망 속에 품은 것들에는 의심이 섞여 있지 않았다. 그것들은 정말로 있었다."], ["Hence his joy so full, so perfect, there was no room in his heart for revenge.", "그러기에 그 기쁨은 그토록 가득하고 그토록 완전하여, 그의 가슴속에는 복수가 들어설 자리가 없었다."], ["Messala, Gratus, Rome, and all the bitter, passionate memories connected with them, were as dead plagues—miasms of the earth above which he floated, far and safe, listening to singing stars.", "메살라, 그라투스, 로마, 그리고 그들에게 얽힌 쓰라리고 격한 온갖 기억은 죽어 버린 역병 같은 것이었다—그가 멀리, 안전하게 그 위에 떠올라 노래하는 별들의 소리에 귀 기울이는, 대지의 독기 같은 것이었다."]], [["The deeper darkness before the dawn was upon the waters, and all things going well with the Astroea, when a man, descending from the deck, walked swiftly to the platform where the tribune slept, and awoke him.", "동트기 전의 더 짙은 어둠이 물 위에 깔려 있었고, 아스트로이아호의 모든 것이 순조로웠다. 그때 한 사내가 갑판에서 내려와 호민관이 잠든 단상으로 잰걸음으로 다가가 그를 깨웠다."], ["Arrius arose, put on his helmet, sword, and shield, and went to the commander of the marines.", "아리우스는 일어나 투구와 검과 방패를 갖추고 해병 지휘관에게로 갔다."]], [["“The pirates are close by.", "\"해적이 가까이 있다."], ["Up and ready!”", "일어나 채비하라!\""], ["he said, and passed to the stairs, calm, confident, insomuch that one might have thought, “Happy fellow!", "그가 말하고는 계단으로 향했다. 어찌나 침착하고 자신만만한지, 누군가는 이렇게 생각했을 법했다. \"복도 많은 사람이로군!"], ["Apicius has set a feast for him.”", "아피키우스가 잔칫상을 차려 놓았나 보다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["Every soul aboard, even the ship, awoke.", "배 안에 있던 모든 영혼이, 아니 배까지도 깨어났다."], ["Officers went to their quarters.", "장교들은 저마다 위치로 갔다."], ["The marines took arms, and were led out, looking in all respects like legionaries.", "해병들은 무장을 갖추고 끌려 나왔는데, 그 모습이 어느 모로 보나 군단병과 다름없었다."], ["Sheaves of arrows and armfuls of javelins were carried on deck.", "화살 다발과 투창 묶음이 갑판으로 운반되었다."], ["By the central stairs the oil-tanks and fire-balls were set ready for use.", "가운데 계단 옆에는 기름통과 화구(火球)가 쓸 수 있도록 준비되었다."], ["Additional lanterns were lighted.", "등잔이 더 밝혀졌다."], ["Buckets were filled with water.", "양동이마다 물이 채워졌다."], ["The rowers in relief assembled under guard in front of the chief.", "교대 차례의 노잡이들은 감시병에 둘러싸여 노예감독 앞에 모였다."], ["As Providence would have it, Ben-Hur was one of the latter.", "천우신조로, 벤허도 그 가운데 하나였다."], ["Overhead he heard the muffled noises of the final preparations—of the sailors furling sail, spreading the nettings, unslinging the machines, and hanging the armor of bull-hide over the side.", "머리 위에서 그는 막바지 준비의 둔탁한 소리를 들었다. 선원들이 돛을 접고, 그물을 펼치고, 기계를 풀어 놓고, 황소 가죽 갑옷을 뱃전에 늘어뜨리는 소리였다."], ["Presently quiet settled about the galley again; quiet full of vague dread and expectation, which, interpreted, means READY.", "이윽고 갤리선 둘레에 다시 고요가 내려앉았다. 막연한 공포와 기대로 가득 찬 고요였으니, 그 뜻을 풀이하자면 곧 준비 완료였다."]], [["At a signal passed down from the deck, and communicated to the hortator by a petty officer stationed on the stairs, all at once the oars stopped.", "갑판에서 신호가 내려와, 계단에 배치된 하급 장교를 거쳐 노예감독에게 전해지자, 별안간 모든 노가 멈추었다."]], [["What did it mean?", "무슨 뜻인가?"]], [["Of the hundred and twenty slaves chained to the benches, not one but asked himself the question.", "긴 의자에 사슬로 묶인 백스무 명의 노예 가운데, 그 물음을 스스로에게 던지지 않은 자는 하나도 없었다."], ["They were without incentive.", "그들에게는 어떤 자극도 없었다."], ["Patriotism, love of honor, sense of duty, brought them no inspiration.", "애국심도, 명예욕도, 의무감도, 그들에게 아무런 영감을 주지 못했다."], ["They felt the thrill common to men rushed helpless and blind into danger.", "그들은 속수무책으로 눈먼 채 위험 속으로 떠밀려 가는 사람들이 흔히 느끼는 전율을 느꼈다."], ["It may be supposed the dullest of them, poising his oar, thought of all that might happen, yet could promise himself nothing; for victory would but rivet his chains the firmer, while the chances of the ship were his; sinking or on fire, he was doomed to her fate.", "그 가운데 가장 둔한 자라 해도, 노를 매만지며, 앞으로 닥칠 모든 일을 떠올리되 그 무엇도 스스로에게 약속하지 못했으리라. 승리한들 그의 사슬은 더욱 단단히 죄어질 뿐이요, 배의 운명이 곧 그의 운명이었으니, 가라앉든 불타든 그는 배와 함께할 수밖에 없는 처지였다."]], [["Of the situation without they might not ask.", "바깥 사정이야 그들이 물을 수도 없었다."], ["And who were the enemy?", "적은 또 누구인가?"], ["And what if they were friends, brethren, countrymen?", "만일 그들이 벗이요, 형제요, 동포라면 어찌할 것인가?"], ["The reader, carrying the suggestion forward, will see the necessity which governed the Roman when, in such emergencies, he locked the hapless wretches to their seats.", "독자가 이 생각을 한 걸음 더 밀고 나가 보면, 로마인이 그런 위급한 때에 이 가엾은 자들을 자리에 묶어 두던 그 불가피함을 헤아릴 수 있을 것이다."]], [["There was little time, however, for such thought with them.", "그러나 그들에게 그런 생각에 잠길 겨를은 거의 없었다."], ["A sound like the rowing of galleys astern attracted Ben-Hur, and the Astroea rocked as if in the midst of countering waves.", "고물 쪽에서 갤리선이 노 젓는 듯한 소리가 벤허의 귀를 끌었고, 아스트로이아호는 사방에서 부딪쳐 오는 물결 한가운데 든 것처럼 흔들렸다."], ["The idea of a fleet at hand broke upon him—a fleet in manoeuvre—forming probably for attack.", "함대가 가까이 있다는 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다. 진형을 펼치는 함대, 어쩌면 공격을 위해 대오를 짜는 함대였다."], ["His blood started with the fancy.", "그 상상에 그의 피가 뛰기 시작했다."]], [["Another signal came down from the deck.", "또 하나의 신호가 갑판에서 내려왔다."], ["The oars dipped, and the galley started imperceptibly.", "노가 물에 잠기고, 갤리선은 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 천천히 움직이기 시작했다."], ["No sound from without, none from within, yet each man in the cabin instinctively poised himself for a shock; the very ship seemed to catch the sense, and hold its breath, and go crouched tiger-like.", "밖에서도 안에서도 아무 소리 없었으나, 선실 안의 사람들은 저마다 본능적으로 충격에 대비해 몸을 가다듬었다. 배마저 그 기운을 알아챈 듯, 숨을 죽이고, 호랑이처럼 몸을 웅크린 채 나아가는 것 같았다."]], [["In such a situation time is inappreciable; so that Ben-Hur could form no judgment of distance gone.", "이런 상황에서는 시간이 가늠되지 않는 법이라, 벤허는 얼마나 멀리 왔는지 도무지 판단할 수 없었다."], ["At last there was a sound of trumpets on deck, full, clear, long blown.", "마침내 갑판에서 나팔 소리가 났다. 가득 차고, 또렷하고, 길게 불어 내는 소리였다."], ["The chief beat the sounding-board until it rang; the rowers reached forward full length, and, deepening the dip of their oars, pulled suddenly with all their united force.", "노예감독은 울림판이 쩌렁쩌렁 울릴 때까지 두드렸다. 노잡이들은 몸을 한껏 앞으로 뻗어, 노를 더 깊이 박고는, 일제히 온 힘을 모아 별안간 잡아당겼다."], ["The galley, quivering in every timber, answered with a leap.", "갤리선은 모든 목재가 떨리도록 진동하며 한 번 도약으로 응답했다."], ["Other trumpets joined in the clamor—all from the rear, none forward—from the latter quarter only a rising sound of voices in tumult heard briefly.", "다른 나팔들이 그 소란에 합세했다. 모두 뒤쪽에서였고, 앞쪽에서는 하나도 없었다. 앞쪽에서는 다만 아우성치는 목소리가 잠시 솟아올랐을 뿐이었다."], ["There was a mighty blow; the rowers in front of the chief’s platform reeled, some of them fell; the ship bounded back, recovered, and rushed on more irresistibly than before.", "거대한 충격이 일었다. 노예감독의 단상 앞에 있던 노잡이들이 비틀거렸고, 더러는 쓰러졌다. 배는 뒤로 튕겼다가, 자세를 되찾고, 이전보다 더 거역할 수 없는 기세로 돌진했다."], ["Shrill and high arose the shrieks of men in terror; over the blare of trumpets, and the grind and crash of the collision, they arose; then under his feet, under the keel, pounding, rumbling, breaking to pieces, drowning, Ben-Hur felt something overridden.", "공포에 질린 사람들의 비명이 날카롭고 높게 솟아올랐다. 나팔 소리 너머로, 충돌의 갈리고 부서지는 소리 너머로 그것이 솟아올랐다. 그러더니 그의 발밑에서, 용골 아래에서, 짓이기고, 우르릉거리고, 산산이 부서지고, 가라앉으며, 벤허는 무언가가 짓밟혀 넘어가는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The men about him looked at each other afraid.", "그를 둘러싼 사람들이 두려워하며 서로를 바라보았다."], ["A shout of triumph from the deck—the beak of the Roman had won!", "갑판에서 승리의 함성이 울렸다. 로마의 충각이 이긴 것이다!"], ["But who were they whom the sea had drunk?", "그러나 바다가 삼켜 버린 자들은 누구였던가?"], ["Of what tongue, from what land were they?", "어느 말을 쓰고, 어느 땅에서 온 자들이던가?"]], [["No pause, no stay!", "멈춤도 없고, 머무름도 없었다!"], ["Forward rushed the Astroea; and, as it went, some sailors ran down, and plunging the cotton balls into the oil-tanks, tossed them dripping to comrades at the head of the stairs: fire was to be added to other horrors of the combat.", "아스트로이아호는 앞으로 돌진했다. 나아가는 동안, 몇몇 선원이 아래로 달려 내려와, 솜뭉치를 기름통에 담갔다가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 그것을 계단 머리의 동료들에게 던져 올렸다. 전투의 다른 온갖 끔찍함에 불까지 더해질 참이었다."]], [["Directly the galley heeled over so far that the oarsmen on the uppermost side with difficulty kept their benches. Again the hearty Roman cheer, and with it despairing shrieks.", "곧 갤리선이 너무 기울어, 가장 높은 쪽의 노잡이들은 자리를 지키기조차 힘들었다. 다시 우렁찬 로마의 환호가 울렸고, 그와 더불어 절망에 찬 비명이 솟았다."], ["An opposing vessel, caught by the grappling-hooks of the great crane swinging from the prow, was being lifted into the air that it might be dropped and sunk.", "이물에서 휘둘러진 거대한 기중기의 갈고리에 걸린 적함 하나가, 떨어뜨려 가라앉히려는 듯 공중으로 들어 올려지고 있었다."]], [["The shouting increased on the right hand and on the left; before, behind, swelled an indescribable clamor.", "오른편에서도 왼편에서도 함성이 커져 갔다. 앞에서도 뒤에서도 형언할 수 없는 소란이 부풀어 올랐다."], ["Occasionally there was a crash, followed by sudden peals of fright, telling of other ships ridden down, and their crews drowned in the vortexes.", "이따금 우지끈하는 소리에 뒤이어 별안간 공포의 비명이 터졌으니, 다른 배들이 들이받혀 그 선원들이 소용돌이에 빠져 죽었음을 알리는 소리였다."]], [["Nor was the fight all on one side.", "싸움이 한쪽으로만 흐른 것도 아니었다."], ["Now and then a Roman in armor was borne down the hatchway, and laid bleeding, sometimes dying, on the floor.", "이따금 갑옷 입은 로마인이 승강구 아래로 실려 내려와, 피를 흘리며, 때로는 죽어 가며, 바닥에 뉘어졌다."]], [["Sometimes, also, puffs of smoke, blended with steam, and foul with the scent of roasting human flesh, poured into the cabin, turning the dimming light into yellow murk.", "또 이따금, 증기와 뒤섞인 연기가, 사람 살이 구워지는 역겨운 냄새를 풍기며 선실 안으로 쏟아져 들어, 어슴푸레해지던 빛을 누런 어둠으로 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["Gasping for breath the while, Ben-Hur knew they were passing through the cloud of a ship on fire, and burning up with the rowers chained to the benches.", "그동안 숨을 헐떡이며, 벤허는 자기들이 불타는 배의 연기구름을 지나고 있음을, 긴 의자에 사슬로 묶인 노잡이들과 함께 타 버린 배의 연기임을 알았다."]], [["The Astroea all this time was in motion.", "아스트로이아호는 그 내내 움직이고 있었다."], ["Suddenly she stopped.", "그러더니 별안간 멈추었다."], ["The oars forward were dashed from the hands of the rowers, and the rowers from their benches.", "앞쪽의 노가 노잡이들의 손에서 튕겨 나갔고, 노잡이들은 자리에서 튕겨 나갔다."], ["On deck, then, a furious trampling, and on the sides a grinding of ships afoul of each other.", "그때 갑판에서는 격렬한 발구르는 소리가 났고, 뱃전에서는 서로 부딪친 배들이 갈리는 소리가 났다."], ["For the first time the beating of the gavel was lost in the uproar.", "처음으로 망치 두드리는 소리가 그 아우성 속에 묻혀 들리지 않았다."], ["Men sank on the floor in fear or looked about seeking a hiding-place.", "사람들은 두려움에 바닥에 주저앉거나, 숨을 곳을 찾아 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["In the midst of the panic a body plunged or was pitched headlong down the hatchway, falling near Ben-Hur.", "그 공황 한가운데로, 한 몸뚱이가 승강구 아래로 곤두박질치거나 내던져져, 벤허 가까이에 떨어졌다. 그는 반쯤 벌거벗은 그 시체를 보았다."], ["He beheld the half-naked carcass, a mass of hair blackening the face, and under it a shield of bull-hide and wicker-work—a barbarian from the white-skinned nations of the North whom death had robbed of plunder and revenge.", "얼굴을 검게 뒤덮은 머리털 다발, 그 아래 황소 가죽과 버들가지로 엮은 방패가 있었다. 죽음이 약탈과 복수를 앗아 간, 북방의 흰 살갗을 가진 민족에서 온 야만인이었다. 어떻게 그가 여기 들어왔는가?"], ["How came he there? An iron hand had snatched him from the opposing deck—no, the Astroea had been boarded!", "무쇠 같은 손이 그를 적함 갑판에서 낚아챈 것이다. 아니, 아스트로이아호가 적에게 점령당한 것이다!"], ["The Romans were fighting on their own deck?", "로마인들이 제 갑판에서 싸우고 있는 것인가?"], ["A chill smote the young Jew: Arrius was hard pressed—he might be defending his own life.", "한기가 젊은 유대인을 덮쳤다. 아리우스가 곤경에 처해 있다, 어쩌면 제 목숨을 지키려 싸우고 있으리라."], ["If he should be slain!", "만일 그가 살해된다면!"], ["God of Abraham forefend!", "아브라함의 하나님, 막아 주소서!"], ["The hopes and dreams so lately come, were they only hopes and dreams?", "그토록 얼마 전에야 찾아온 희망과 꿈이, 한낱 희망과 꿈에 지나지 않았던가? 어머니와 누이를, 집을, 고향을, 거룩한 땅을, 그는 끝내 보지 못한단 말인가?"], ["Mother and sister—house—home—Holy Land—was he not to see them, after all?", "소란이 머리 위에서 천둥처럼 울렸다. 그는 둘러보았다. 선실 안은 온통 혼란이었다."], ["The tumult thundered above him; he looked around; in the cabin all was confusion—the rowers on the benches paralyzed; men running blindly hither and thither; only the chief on his seat imperturbable, vainly beating the sounding-board, and waiting the orders of the tribune—in the red murk illustrating the matchless discipline which had won the world.", "긴 의자의 노잡이들은 마비된 채였고, 사람들은 눈먼 듯 이리저리 내달렸다. 오직 노예감독만이 제자리에 흔들림 없이 앉아, 부질없이 울림판을 두드리며 호민관의 명령을 기다리고 있었다. 붉은 어둠 속에서, 세계를 정복한 그 비길 데 없는 규율을 몸소 보여 주면서."]], [["The example had a good effect upon Ben-Hur.", "그 본보기는 벤허에게 좋은 영향을 미쳤다."], ["He controlled himself enough to think.", "그는 생각할 수 있을 만큼 마음을 다잡았다."], ["Honor and duty bound the Roman to the platform; but what had he to do with such motives then?", "명예와 의무가 저 로마인을 단상에 붙들어 두는 것이나, 그때 자기가 그런 동기와 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가?"], ["The bench was a thing to run from; while, if he were to die a slave, who would be the better of the sacrifice?", "긴 의자는 도망쳐야 할 자리였다. 게다가 노예로 죽는다면, 그 희생으로 누가 덕을 보겠는가?"], ["With him living was duty, if not honor.", "그에게는 명예는 아닐지언정 사는 것이 곧 의무였다."], ["His life belonged to his people.", "그의 목숨은 그의 백성의 것이었다."], ["They arose before him never more real: he saw them, their arms outstretched; he heard them imploring him.", "그들이 그 어느 때보다 생생하게 그의 앞에 떠올랐다. 그는 그들을, 뻗은 두 팔을 보았다."], ["And he would go to them. He started—stopped. Alas!", "그를 향해 애원하는 그들의 목소리를 들었다. 그리고 그는 그들에게 가리라."], ["a Roman judgment held him in doom.", "그는 몸을 일으켰다가 멈추었다. 아아!"], ["While it endured, escape would be profitless.", "로마의 판결이 그를 형벌 속에 붙들어 매고 있었다."], ["In the wide, wide earth there was no place in which he would be safe from the imperial demand; upon the land none, nor upon the sea.", "그것이 유효한 한, 탈출은 헛수고일 뿐이었다. 넓디넓은 땅 어디에도 황제의 추궁에서 그가 안전할 곳은 없었다. 뭍에도 없고, 바다에도 없었다."], ["Whereas he required freedom according to the forms of law, so only could he abide in Judea and execute the filial purpose to which he would devote himself: in other land he would not live.", "그가 법의 절차에 따른 자유를 얻어야만, 그래야만 유대에 머물러 제 한 몸 바치려는 효성의 뜻을 이룰 수 있었다. 다른 땅에서라면 그는 살지 않으리라."], ["Dear God! How he had waited and watched and prayed for such a release!", "사랑하는 하나님이여! 그가 그런 해방을 얼마나 기다리고, 지켜보고, 기도해 왔던가!"], ["And how it had been delayed!", "그런데 그것이 어찌나 더디었던가!"], ["But at last he had seen it in the promise of the tribune.", "그러나 마침내 그는 호민관의 약속 속에서 그것을 보았다."], ["What else the great man’s meaning?", "그 위대한 사람의 말이 달리 무슨 뜻이었겠는가?"], ["And if the benefactor so belated should now be slain!", "그런데 그토록 뒤늦게 나타난 은인이 이제 살해된다면!"], ["The dead come not back to redeem the pledges of the living.", "죽은 자는 산 자에게 한 언약을 갚으러 돌아오지 못한다."], ["It should not be—Arrius should not die.", "그래서는 안 된다, 아리우스가 죽어서는 안 된다."], ["At least, better perish with him than survive a galley-slave.", "적어도, 갤리선 노예로 살아남느니 그와 함께 죽는 편이 나았다."]], [["Once more Ben-Hur looked around.", "벤허는 다시 한번 둘러보았다."], ["Upon the roof of the cabin the battle yet beat; against the sides the hostile vessels yet crushed and grided.", "선실 지붕 위에서는 아직도 전투가 휘몰아쳤다. 뱃전에는 아직도 적함들이 부딪치고 갈렸다."], ["On the benches, the slaves struggled to tear loose from their chains, and, finding their efforts vain, howled like madmen; the guards had gone upstairs; discipline was out, panic in.", "긴 의자에서 노예들은 사슬을 뜯어내려고 몸부림치다가, 헛수고임을 알고는 미친 사람처럼 울부짖었다. 감시병들은 위로 올라갔고, 규율은 사라지고, 공황이 들어찼다."], ["No, the chief kept his chair, unchanged, calm as ever—except the gavel, weaponless.", "아니, 노예감독만은 의자를 지키며, 한결같이, 여느 때처럼 침착했다. 다만 망치 말고는 아무 무기가 없었다."], ["Vainly with his clangor he filled the lulls in the din.", "그는 부질없이 그 쇳소리로 소란이 잦아드는 틈을 메웠다."], ["Ben-Hur gave him a last look, then broke away—not in flight, but to seek the tribune.", "벤허는 그에게 마지막 눈길을 던지고는, 박차고 나섰다. 도망치려는 것이 아니라, 호민관을 찾으려는 것이었다."]], [["A very short space lay between him and the stairs of the hatchway aft.", "그와 고물 쪽 승강구 계단 사이에는 아주 짧은 거리가 놓여 있었다."], ["He took it with a leap, and was half-way up the steps—up far enough to catch a glimpse of the sky blood-red with fire, of the ships alongside, of the sea covered with ships and wrecks, of the fight closed in about the pilot’s quarter, the assailants many, the defenders few—when suddenly his foothold was knocked away, and he pitched backward.", "그는 단숨에 도약해 그것을 가로질러, 계단을 반쯤 올랐다. 불빛으로 핏빛이 된 하늘과, 나란히 붙은 배들과, 배와 잔해로 뒤덮인 바다와, 키잡이 자리 둘레에 몰린 싸움을 흘끗 볼 만큼은 올랐다. 공격하는 자는 많고, 지키는 자는 적었다."], ["The floor, when he reached it, seemed to be lifting itself and breaking to pieces; then, in a twinkling, the whole after-part of the hull broke asunder, and, as if it had all the time been lying in wait, the sea, hissing and foaming, leaped in, and all became darkness and surging water to Ben-Hur.", "그때 별안간 발 디딘 곳이 떨어져 나가, 그는 뒤로 곤두박질쳤다. 바닥에 닿았을 때, 그것은 스스로 들썩이며 산산이 부서지는 듯했다. 그러더니, 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 선체의 고물 쪽 전부가 쪼개지면서, 마치 줄곧 노리고 있었던 듯, 바다가 쉭쉭거리고 거품을 일으키며 뛰어들었고, 벤허에게는 모든 것이 어둠과 솟구치는 물로 변했다."]], [["It cannot be said that the young Jew helped himself in this stress.", "이 절박한 순간에 젊은 유대인이 스스로를 구했다고는 말할 수 없다."], ["Besides his usual strength, he had the indefinite extra force which nature keeps in reserve for just such perils to life; yet the darkness, and the whirl and roar of water, stupefied him.", "평소의 힘에 더하여, 그에게는 자연이 바로 이런 목숨의 위기를 위해 따로 간직해 둔 그 막연한 여분의 힘이 있었다. 그런데도 어둠과, 휘몰아치고 포효하는 물이 그를 멍하게 만들었다."], ["Even the holding his breath was involuntary.", "숨을 참는 것조차 저도 모르게 한 일이었다."]], [["The influx of the flood tossed him like a log forward into the cabin, where he would have drowned but for the refluence of the sinking motion.", "밀려드는 홍수는 그를 통나무처럼 선실 앞쪽으로 내던졌으니, 가라앉는 움직임이 만든 역류가 아니었다면 그는 거기서 빠져 죽었으리라."], ["As it was, fathoms under the surface the hollow mass vomited him forth, and he arose along with the loosed debris.", "하지만 실제로는, 수면 아래 여러 길 깊은 곳에서 그 텅 빈 덩어리가 그를 토해 냈고, 그는 풀려난 잔해와 함께 떠올랐다."], ["In the act of rising, he clutched something, and held to it.", "떠오르는 와중에 그는 무언가를 움켜쥐고, 거기 매달렸다."], ["The time he was under seemed an age longer than it really was; at last he gained the top; with a great gasp he filled his lungs afresh, and, tossing the water from his hair and eyes, climbed higher upon the plank he held, and looked about him.", "그가 물밑에 잠겨 있던 시간은 실제보다 한 시대만큼이나 더 길게 느껴졌다. 마침내 그는 수면 위에 이르렀다. 크게 숨을 들이켜 폐를 다시 채우고는, 머리와 눈에서 물을 털어 내며, 붙잡은 널빤지 위로 더 높이 올라타 사방을 둘러보았다."]], [["Death had pursued him closely under the waves; he found it waiting for him when he was risen—waiting multiform.", "죽음은 물결 아래에서 그를 바싹 뒤쫓았다. 떠올라 보니, 죽음은 그를 기다리고 있었다. 여러 모습으로 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Smoke lay upon the sea like a semitransparent fog, through which here and there shone cores of intense brilliance.", "연기가 반쯤 비치는 안개처럼 바다 위에 깔려 있었고, 그 사이로 군데군데 강렬하게 빛나는 불덩이가 비쳤다."], ["A quick intelligence told him that they were ships on fire.", "재빠른 분별로 그는 그것들이 불타는 배임을 알아차렸다."], ["The battle was yet on; nor could he say who was victor.", "전투는 아직 끝나지 않았으니, 누가 이겼는지 그는 말할 수 없었다."], ["Within the radius of his vision now and then ships passed, shooting shadows athwart lights.", "그의 시야 안으로 이따금 배들이 지나가며, 불빛을 가로질러 그림자를 던졌다."], ["Out of the dun clouds farther on he caught the crash of other ships colliding.", "저 멀리 거무스름한 구름 속에서, 그는 다른 배들이 충돌하는 소리를 들었다."], ["The danger, however, was closer at hand.", "그러나 위험은 더 가까이 있었다."], ["When the Astroea went down, her deck, it will be recollected, held her own crew, and the crews of the two galleys which had attacked her at the same time, all of whom were ingulfed.", "아스트로이아호가 가라앉을 때, 기억하다시피, 그 갑판에는 자기 배의 선원들과, 동시에 그 배를 공격한 두 갤리선의 선원들이 함께 있었으니, 그들 모두가 물에 휩쓸렸다."], ["Many of them came to the surface together, and on the same plank or support of whatever kind continued the combat, begun possibly in the vortex fathoms down.", "그들 가운데 다수가 한꺼번에 수면으로 떠올라, 같은 널빤지나 그 무엇이든 붙들 것 위에서, 어쩌면 여러 길 아래 소용돌이 속에서 시작했을 싸움을 이어 갔다."], ["Writhing and twisting in deadly embrace, sometimes striking with sword or javelin, they kept the sea around them in agitation, at one place inky-black, at another aflame with fiery reflections.", "죽음의 포옹으로 뒤엉켜 몸부림치며, 때로는 칼이나 투창으로 내리치며, 그들은 둘레의 바다를 들끓게 했으니, 한 곳은 먹물처럼 까맣고, 다른 곳은 불의 반사로 활활 타올랐다."], ["With their struggles he had nothing to do; they were all his enemies: not one of them but would kill him for the plank upon which he floated.", "그들의 몸부림과 그는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그들은 모두 그의 적이었다. 그가 떠 있는 널빤지를 차지하려고 그를 죽이려 들지 않을 자가 하나도 없었다."], ["He made haste to get away.", "그는 서둘러 멀어졌다."]], [["About that time he heard oars in quickest movement, and beheld a galley coming down upon him.", "그 무렵 그는 더없이 빠르게 움직이는 노 소리를 듣고, 갤리선 한 척이 자기를 향해 내려오는 것을 보았다."], ["The tall prow seemed doubly tall, and the red light playing upon its gilt and carving gave it an appearance of snaky life.", "우뚝 솟은 이물은 갑절로 높아 보였고, 그 도금과 조각에 어리는 붉은 빛이 그것을 마치 살아 있는 뱀처럼 보이게 했다."], ["Under its foot the water churned to flying foam.", "그 밑에서 물은 휘저어져 날리는 거품으로 변했다."]], [["He struck out, pushing the plank, which was very broad and unmanageable.", "그는 헤엄쳐 나아가며, 몹시 넓어 다루기 힘든 널빤지를 밀었다."], ["Seconds were precious—half a second might save or lose him.", "한순간이 귀했으니, 반 초가 그를 살리거나 잃게 할 수 있었다."], ["In the crisis of the effort, up from the sea, within arm’s reach, a helmet shot like a gleam of gold.", "그 안간힘을 쓰던 고비에, 팔 닿을 만한 거리의 바다 속에서, 투구 하나가 금빛 섬광처럼 솟아올랐다."], ["Next came two hands with fingers extended—large hands were they, and strong—their hold once fixed, might not be loosed.", "이어서 손가락을 쫙 편 두 손이 나왔다. 크고 억센 손이었으니, 한번 붙들면 풀리지 않을 듯했다."], ["Ben-Hur swerved from them appalled.", "벤허는 질겁하여 거기서 비켜났다."], ["Up rose the helmet and the head it encased—then two arms, which began to beat the water wildly—the head turned back, and gave the face to the light.", "투구와 그것이 감싼 머리가 솟아올랐다. 이어 두 팔이, 미친 듯이 물을 두드리기 시작했다. 그 머리가 뒤로 젖혀지며, 얼굴을 빛 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["The mouth gaping wide; the eyes open, but sightless, and the bloodless pallor of a drowning man—never anything more ghastly!", "입은 크게 벌어졌고, 두 눈은 떴으나 아무것도 보지 못했으며, 물에 빠져 죽어 가는 자의 핏기 없는 창백함이 어려 있었다."], ["Yet he gave a cry of joy at the sight, and as the face was going under again, he caught the sufferer by the chain which passed from the helmet beneath the chin, and drew him to the plank.", "그보다 더 섬뜩한 것은 없으리라! 그러나 그는 그 모습을 보고 기쁨의 외침을 토했으니, 그 얼굴이 다시 가라앉으려 할 때, 그는 투구에서 턱 밑으로 이어진 사슬을 잡아, 그 가엾은 사람을 널빤지 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."]], [["The man was Arrius, the tribune.", "그 사람은 호민관 아리우스였다."]], [["For a while the water foamed and eddied violently about Ben-Hur, taxing all his strength to hold to the support and at the same time keep the Roman’s head above the surface.", "한동안 물이 벤허 둘레에서 사납게 거품을 일으키며 소용돌이쳐, 떠 있는 것에 매달리는 동시에 로마인의 머리를 수면 위로 떠받치자니 그의 온 힘이 다 들었다."], ["The galley had passed, leaving the two barely outside the stroke of its oars.", "갤리선은 지나갔으니, 두 사람은 그 노가 닿는 거리에서 가까스로 벗어나 있었다."], ["Right through the floating men, over heads helmeted as well as heads bare, she drove, in her wake nothing but the sea sparkling with fire.", "떠 있는 사람들 한가운데로, 투구 쓴 머리든 맨머리든 가리지 않고 넘어, 배는 돌진했고, 그 자취에는 불꽃으로 반짝이는 바다밖에 남지 않았다."], ["A muffled crash, succeeded by a great outcry, made the rescuer look again from his charge.", "둔탁한 우지끈 소리에 이어 거대한 비명이 터지자, 구해 낸 이는 자기가 떠맡은 자에게서 눈을 들어 다시 바라보았다."], ["A certain savage pleasure touched his heart—the Astroea was avenged.", "어떤 야성의 쾌감이 그의 마음을 스쳤다. 아스트로이아호의 원수가 갚아진 것이다."]], [["After that the battle moved on.", "그 뒤로 전투는 멀어져 갔다."], ["Resistance turned to flight.", "저항은 도주로 바뀌었다."], ["But who were the victors?", "그러나 승자는 누구였던가?"], ["Ben-Hur was sensible how much his freedom and the life of the tribune depended upon that event.", "자기의 자유와 호민관의 목숨이 그 결과에 얼마나 달려 있는지 벤허는 절실히 느꼈다."], ["He pushed the plank under the latter until it floated him, after which all his care was to keep him there.", "그는 널빤지를 아리우스 아래로 밀어 넣어 그를 떠받치게 한 뒤로는, 오로지 그를 거기 붙들어 두는 데에만 마음을 썼다."], ["The dawn came slowly.", "새벽이 더디게 왔다."], ["He watched its growing hopefully, yet sometimes afraid.", "그는 희망을 품고 그것이 밝아 오는 것을 지켜보았으나, 때로는 두려워하기도 했다."], ["Would it bring the Romans or the pirates?", "그것이 로마인을 데려올 것인가, 아니면 해적을 데려올 것인가?"], ["If the pirates, his charge was lost.", "해적이라면, 그가 떠맡은 사람은 끝장이었다."]], [["At last morning broke in full, the air without a breath.", "마침내 아침이 활짝 밝았고, 바람 한 점 없었다."], ["Off to the left he saw the land, too far to think of attempting to make it.", "왼편 저쪽으로 그는 뭍을 보았으나, 너무 멀어 거기까지 가 볼 엄두는 나지 않았다."], ["Here and there men were adrift like himself.", "여기저기에 그 자신처럼 떠도는 사람들이 있었다."], ["In spots the sea was blackened by charred and sometimes smoking fragments.", "군데군데 바다는 까맣게 탄, 때로는 연기 나는 잔해로 거뭇거뭇했다."], ["A galley up a long way was lying to with a torn sail hanging from the tilted yard, and the oars all idle.", "멀찍이 떨어진 갤리선 한 척이, 기울어진 활대에서 찢긴 돛을 늘어뜨린 채 노를 모두 멈추고 떠 있었다."], ["Still farther away he could discern moving specks, which he thought might be ships in flight or pursuit, or they might be white birds a-wing.", "더 멀리에서는 움직이는 점들을 알아볼 수 있었는데, 도주하거나 추격하는 배일 수도 있고, 날개를 펼친 흰 새일 수도 있겠다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["An hour passed thus.", "그렇게 한 시간이 흘렀다."], ["His anxiety increased.", "그의 불안은 커져 갔다."], ["If relief came not speedily, Arrius would die.", "구원이 속히 오지 않으면, 아리우스는 죽으리라."], ["Sometimes he seemed already dead, he lay so still.", "때때로 그는 어찌나 미동도 없이 누워 있던지 이미 죽은 듯했다."], ["He took the helmet off, and then, with greater difficulty, the cuirass; the heart he found fluttering.", "그는 투구를 벗겨 내고, 이어 더 힘겹게 흉갑을 벗겼다. 심장은 가냘프게 떨고 있었다."], ["He took hope at the sign, and held on.", "그는 그 기색에 희망을 얻어, 버텼다."], ["There was nothing to do but wait, and, after the manner of his people, pray.", "기다리는 것 말고는, 그리고 제 백성의 방식대로 기도하는 것 말고는, 할 수 있는 일이 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["The throes of recovery from drowning are more painful than the drowning.", "물에 빠진 자가 다시 살아나는 고통은 익사 그 자체보다 더 괴롭다."], ["These Arrius passed through, and, at length, to Ben-Hur’s delight, reached the point of speech.", "아리우스는 그 고통을 모두 겪었고, 마침내 벤허가 기뻐할 만큼 말을 할 수 있는 지경에 이르렀다."]], [["Gradually, from incoherent questions as to where he was, and by whom and how he had been saved, he reverted to the battle.", "차츰, 자기가 어디에 있는지, 누가 어떻게 자신을 구했는지 두서없이 묻던 데서 시작하여, 그는 다시 그 해전으로 생각을 돌렸다."], ["The doubt of the victory stimulated his faculties to full return, a result aided not a little by a long rest—such as could be had on their frail support.", "승패에 대한 의구심이 그의 정신을 온전히 되살리도록 자극했고, 그 허약한 받침대 위에서나마 얻을 수 있는 오랜 휴식이 그 회복을 적지 않게 도왔다."], ["After a while he became talkative.", "얼마 뒤 그는 말이 많아졌다."]], [["“Our rescue, I see, depends upon the result of the fight.", "\"우리가 구조될지는 그 싸움의 결과에 달려 있는 모양이군."], ["I see also what thou hast done for me.", "자네가 나를 위해 무엇을 했는지도 알겠네."], ["To speak fairly, thou hast saved my life at the risk of thy own.", "솔직히 말해, 자네는 제 목숨을 걸고 내 목숨을 구했어."], ["I make the acknowledgment broadly; and, whatever cometh, thou hast my thanks.", "나는 이것을 거리낌 없이 인정하네. 어떤 일이 닥치든 자네는 내 고마움을 받을 자격이 있어."], ["More than that, if fortune doth but serve me kindly, and we get well out of this peril, I will do thee such favor as becometh a Roman who hath power and opportunity to prove his gratitude.", "그뿐 아니라, 운명이 내게 자비를 베풀어 우리가 이 위험에서 무사히 벗어난다면, 나는 감사를 증명할 힘과 기회를 가진 로마인답게 자네에게 호의를 베풀겠네."], ["Yet, yet it is to be seen if, with thy good intent, thou hast really done me a kindness; or, rather, speaking to thy good-will”—he hesitated—“I would exact of thee a promise to do me, in a certain event, the greatest favor one man can do another—and of that let me have thy pledge now.”", "그러나, 그러나 자네의 선의가 정말로 내게 친절을 베푼 것인지는 아직 두고 보아야 할 일이야. 아니, 자네의 선의에 기대어 말하건대\"—그가 머뭇거렸다—\"나는 자네에게 어떤 경우에, 한 사람이 다른 사람에게 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 호의를 베풀어 달라는 약속을 받아 두고 싶네. 지금 그 맹세를 내게 다오.\""]], [["“If the thing be not forbidden, I will do it,”", "\"금지된 일이 아니라면 그렇게 하겠습니다.\""], ["Ben-Hur replied.", "벤허가 대답했다."]], [["Arrius rested again.", "아리우스는 다시 숨을 돌렸다."]], [["“Art thou, indeed, a son of Hur, the Jew?” he next asked.", "\"자네는 정말로 유대인 후르의 아들인가?\" 그가 다음으로 물었다."]], [["“It is as I have said.”", "\"제가 말씀드린 그대로입니다.\""]], [["“I knew thy father—”", "\"나는 자네 아버지를 알았네—\""]], [["Judah drew himself nearer, for the tribune’s voice was weak—he drew nearer, and listened eagerly—at last he thought to hear of home.", "유다는 더 가까이 몸을 끌어당겼다. 호민관의 목소리가 약했기 때문이다. 그는 더 가까이 다가가, 간절히 귀를 기울였다. 마침내 고향 소식을 들으리라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["“I knew him, and loved him,”", "\"나는 그를 알았고, 사랑했지.\""], ["Arrius continued.", "아리우스가 말을 이었다."]], [["There was another pause, during which something diverted the speaker’s thought.", "또 한 번의 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 무엇인가가 말하는 이의 생각을 다른 데로 돌려놓았다."]], [["“It cannot be,” he proceeded, “that thou, a son of his, hast not heard of Cato and Brutus.", "\"자네가 그의 아들인데 카토와 브루투스를 들어 보지 못했을 리는 없지.\" 그가 계속했다."], ["They were very great men, and never as great as in death.", "\"그들은 참으로 위대한 사람들이었고, 죽음에 임해서만큼 위대했던 적은 없었어."], ["In their dying, they left this law—A Roman may not survive his good-fortune. Art thou listening?”", "그들은 죽으면서 이런 법을 남겼지—로마인은 제 행운보다 오래 살아서는 안 된다."], ["“I hear.”", "듣고 있나?\""]], [["“It is a custom of gentlemen in Rome to wear a ring.", "\"듣고 있습니다.\" \"로마의 신사들은 반지를 끼는 관습이 있네."], ["There is one on my hand. Take it now.”", "내 손에도 하나 있지. 지금 이것을 받게.\""]], [["He held the hand to Judah, who did as he asked.", "그는 유다에게 손을 내밀었고, 유다는 그가 청한 대로 했다."]], [["“Now put it on thine own hand.”", "\"이제 자네 손에 끼게.\""]], [["Ben-Hur did so.", "벤허는 그렇게 했다."]], [["“The trinket hath its uses,” said Arrius next.", "\"그 패물은 쓸모가 있어.\" 아리우스가 다음으로 말했다."], ["“I have property and money.", "\"내게는 재산과 돈이 있네."], ["I am accounted rich even in Rome.", "로마에서도 부자로 꼽히지. 가족은 없어."], ["I have no family. Show the ring to my freedman, who hath control in my absence; you will find him in a villa near Misenum.", "내가 없을 때 모든 것을 관리하는 내 해방노예에게 이 반지를 보이게. 미세눔 근처의 별장에 가면 그를 찾을 수 있을 거야."], ["Tell him how it came to thee, and ask anything, or all he may have; he will not refuse the demand.", "이것이 어떻게 자네 손에 들어왔는지 말하고, 그가 가진 것이라면 무엇이든, 아니 전부라도 청하게."], ["If I live, I will do better by thee.", "그는 그 요구를 거절하지 않을 걸세."], ["I will make thee free, and restore thee to thy home and people; or thou mayst give thyself to the pursuit that pleaseth thee most.", "내가 살아 있다면 자네에게 더 잘해 주겠네. 자네를 자유의 몸으로 만들어 고향과 동족에게 돌려보내 주지. 아니면 자네가 가장 좋아하는 일에 몸을 바칠 수도 있어."], ["Dost thou hear?”", "듣고 있나?\""]], [["“I could not choose but hear.”", "\"듣지 않을 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Then pledge me.", "\"그러면 내게 맹세하게."], ["By the gods—”", "신들의 이름으로—\""]], [["“Nay, good tribune, I am a Jew.”", "\"아닙니다, 훌륭하신 호민관님, 저는 유대인입니다.\""]], [["“By thy God, then, or in the form most sacred to those of thy faith—pledge me to do what I tell thee now, and as I tell thee; I am waiting, let me have thy promise.”", "\"그렇다면 자네의 하느님께 걸고, 아니면 자네 신앙을 가진 이들에게 가장 거룩한 형식으로—내가 지금 자네에게 이르는 일을, 내가 이르는 대로 하겠다고 맹세하게. 나는 기다리고 있네. 자네의 약속을 다오.\""]], [["“Noble Arrius, I am warned by thy manner to expect something of gravest concern.", "\"고귀하신 아리우스님, 당신의 태도로 보아 무언가 더없이 중대한 일을 기대하라는 경고를 받은 듯합니다."], ["Tell me thy wish first.”", "먼저 당신의 바람을 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Wilt thou promise then?”", "\"그러면 약속하겠나?\""]], [["“That were to give the pledge, and— Blessed be the God of my fathers!", "\"그건 맹세를 하는 셈이 되고— 제 조상의 하느님께 영광을!"], ["yonder cometh a ship!”", "저기 배 한 척이 옵니다!\""]], [["“In what direction?”", "\"어느 쪽에서?\""]], [["“From the north.”", "\"북쪽에서입니다.\""]], [["“Canst thou tell her nationality by outward signs?”", "\"겉모습으로 그 국적을 알 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“No. My service hath been at the oars.”", "\"모르겠습니다. 저는 노 젓는 일만 했으니까요.\""]], [["“Hath she a flag?”", "\"깃발은 있나?\""]], [["“I cannot see one.”", "\"보이지 않습니다.\""]], [["Arrius remained quiet some time, apparently in deep reflection.", "아리우스는 한동안 잠자코 있었으니, 보아하니 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다."]], [["“Does the ship hold this way yet?”", "\"그 배가 아직도 이쪽으로 오나?\""], ["he at length asked.", "그가 마침내 물었다."]], [["“Still this way.”", "\"여전히 이쪽으로 옵니다.\""]], [["“Look for the flag now.”", "\"이제 깃발을 살펴보게.\""]], [["“She hath none.”", "\"깃발이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Nor any other sign?”", "\"다른 표지도 없나?\""]], [["“She hath a sail set, and is of three banks, and cometh swiftly—that is all I can say of her.”", "\"돛을 하나 올렸고, 노가 삼단이며, 빠르게 다가옵니다—제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은 그뿐입니다.\""]], [["“A Roman in triumph would have out many flags.", "\"승전한 로마 배라면 깃발을 여러 개 내걸었을 텐데."], ["She must be an enemy. Hear now,”", "적임에 틀림없어. 자, 들어 보게.\""], ["said Arrius, becoming grave again, “hear, while yet I may speak.", "아리우스가 다시 진지해지며 말했다. \"내가 아직 말할 수 있을 때 들어 두게."], ["If the galley be a pirate, thy life is safe; they may not give thee freedom; they may put thee to the oar again; but they will not kill thee.", "저 갤리선이 해적선이라면 자네 목숨은 안전하네. 자유는 주지 않을 테고, 다시 노를 잡게 하겠지만, 죽이지는 않을 거야."], ["On the other hand, I—”", "반면에 나는—\""]], [["The tribune faltered.", "호민관이 말을 멈칫거렸다."]], [["“Perpol!”", "\"맹세코!\""], ["he continued, resolutely.", "그가 단호하게 계속했다."], ["“I am too old to submit to dishonor.", "\"나는 치욕에 굴복하기에는 너무 늙었네."], ["In Rome, let them tell how Quintus Arrius, as became a Roman tribune, went down with his ship in the midst of the foe.", "로마에서는 사람들이 이렇게 말하게 하라. 퀸투스 아리우스는 로마 호민관답게 적들 한가운데서 자기 배와 함께 가라앉았다고."], ["This is what I would have thee do.", "이것이 내가 자네에게 바라는 일이야."], ["If the galley prove a pirate, push me from the plank and drown me.", "저 갤리선이 해적선으로 밝혀지면, 나를 널빤지에서 밀어 떨어뜨려 빠뜨려라."], ["Dost thou hear?", "듣고 있나?"], ["Swear thou wilt do it.”", "그렇게 하겠다고 맹세하게.\""]], [["“I will not swear,”", "\"맹세하지 않겠습니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, firmly; “neither will I do the deed.", "벤허가 단호하게 말했다. \"그 일도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["The Law, which is to me most binding, O tribune, would make me answerable for thy life.", "제게 가장 큰 구속력을 지닌 율법은, 오 호민관님, 당신의 목숨에 대해 저를 책임지게 합니다."], ["Take back the ring”—he took the seal from his finger—“take it back, and all thy promises of favor in the event of delivery from this peril.", "반지를 도로 가져가십시오\"—그는 손가락에서 인장 반지를 빼냈다—\"이것을 도로 가져가시고, 이 위험에서 벗어났을 때 베풀겠다는 모든 호의의 약속도 거두십시오."], ["The judgment which sent me to the oar for life made me a slave, yet I am not a slave; no more am I thy freedman.", "저를 종신토록 노에 매단 그 판결은 저를 노예로 만들었으나, 저는 노예가 아닙니다. 당신의 해방노예도 아닙니다."], ["I am a son of Israel, and this moment, at least, my own master.", "저는 이스라엘의 아들이며, 적어도 지금 이 순간만큼은 제 자신의 주인입니다."], ["Take back the ring.”", "반지를 도로 가져가십시오.\""]], [["Arrius remained passive.", "아리우스는 가만히 있었다."]], [["“Thou wilt not?” Judah continued.", "\"가져가지 않으시겠습니까?\" 유다가 계속했다."], ["“Not in anger, then, nor in any despite, but to free myself from a hateful obligation, I will give thy gift to the sea.", "\"그러면 노여움에서도, 어떤 멸시에서도 아니라, 다만 가증스러운 의무에서 저를 풀어 놓기 위해, 당신의 선물을 바다에 던지겠습니다."], ["See, O tribune!”", "보십시오, 오 호민관님!\""]], [["He tossed the ring away.", "그는 반지를 던져 버렸다."], ["Arrius heard the splash where it struck and sank, though he did not look.", "아리우스는 보지 않았으나 반지가 떨어져 가라앉는 물소리를 들었다."]], [["“Thou hast done a foolish thing,”", "\"자네는 어리석은 짓을 했어.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “foolish for one placed as thou art.", "\"자네 같은 처지에 놓인 자에게는 어리석은 짓이지."], ["I am not dependent upon thee for death.", "나는 죽는 데 자네에게 기댈 필요가 없네."], ["Life is a thread I can break without thy help; and, if I do, what will become of thee?", "목숨이란 자네 도움 없이도 끊을 수 있는 한 가닥 실이야. 그렇게 하면 자네는 어찌 되겠나?"], ["Men determined on death prefer it at the hands of others, for the reason that the soul which Plato giveth us is rebellious at the thought of self-destruction; that is all.", "죽기로 작정한 사람은 남의 손에 죽기를 더 좋아하는데, 까닭인즉 플라톤이 우리에게 일러 준 그 영혼이 자기 파괴라는 생각에 거역하기 때문이야. 그뿐이지."], ["If the ship be a pirate, I will escape from the world.", "저 배가 해적선이라면 나는 이 세상을 떠나겠네."], ["My mind is fixed.", "내 마음은 굳었어."], ["I am a Roman. Success and honor are all in all.", "나는 로마인이야. 성공과 명예가 전부지."], ["Yet I would have served thee; thou wouldst not.", "그래도 나는 자네를 도우려 했건만, 자네가 마다했어."], ["The ring was the only witness of my will available in this situation. We are both lost.", "그 반지는 이 처지에서 내 뜻을 증언할 수 있는 유일한 것이었네. 우리 둘 다 잃고 말았어."], ["I will die regretting the victory and glory wrested from me; thou wilt live to die a little later, mourning the pious duties undone because of this folly. I pity thee.”", "나는 내게서 빼앗긴 승리와 영광을 한탄하며 죽을 것이고, 자네는 조금 더 살다가, 이 어리석음 때문에 다하지 못한 경건한 의무들을 슬퍼하며 죽겠지. 나는 자네가 가엾네.\""]], [["Ben-Hur saw the consequences of his act more distinctly than before, yet he did not falter.", "벤허는 자기 행동의 결과를 전보다 더 또렷이 보았으나, 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["“In the three years of my servitude, O tribune, thou wert the first to look upon me kindly. No, no!", "\"제가 종살이한 삼 년 동안, 오 호민관님, 당신은 저를 친절하게 바라본 첫 번째 사람이었습니다. 아니, 아닙니다!"], ["There was another.”", "또 한 사람이 있었지요.\""], ["The voice dropped, the eyes became humid, and he saw plainly as if it were then before him the face of the boy who helped him to a drink by the old well at Nazareth.", "목소리가 잦아들고, 눈가가 촉촉해졌다. 그는 나사렛의 옛 우물가에서 자신에게 물을 마시게 해 준 그 소년의 얼굴을, 마치 지금 눈앞에 있는 듯 또렷이 보았다. \"적어도,\""], ["“At least,”", "그가 계속했다."], ["he proceeded, “thou wert the first to ask me who I was; and if, when I reached out and caught thee, blind and sinking the last time, I, too, had thought of the many ways in which thou couldst be useful to me in my wretchedness, still the act was not all selfish; this I pray you to believe.", "\"당신은 제가 누구인지를 물어 준 첫 번째 사람이었습니다. 그리고 제가 손을 뻗어, 눈이 멀어 마지막으로 가라앉는 당신을 붙잡았을 때, 비록 저 또한 제 비참한 처지에서 당신이 여러 모로 제게 쓸모가 있으리라는 생각을 했다 해도, 그 행동이 온통 이기적이기만 했던 것은 아닙니다. 이것만은 믿어 주시기를 빕니다."], ["Moreover, seeing as God giveth me to know, the ends I dream of are to be wrought by fair means alone.", "게다가, 하느님께서 제게 알게 해 주시는 바로는, 제가 꿈꾸는 목적은 오직 정당한 수단으로만 이루어져야 합니다."], ["As a thing of conscience, I would rather die with thee than be thy slayer.", "양심의 문제로서, 저는 당신을 죽이는 자가 되느니 차라리 당신과 함께 죽기를 택하겠습니다."], ["My mind is firmly set as thine; though thou wert to offer me all Rome, O tribune, and it belonged to thee to make the gift good, I would not kill thee.", "제 마음은 당신만큼이나 굳게 정해져 있습니다. 비록 당신이 제게 로마 전부를 주겠다 하고, 그것을 주는 일이 당신 손에 달려 있다 해도, 오 호민관님, 저는 당신을 죽이지 않겠습니다."], ["Thy Cato and Brutus were as little children compared to the Hebrew whose law a Jew must obey.”", "당신의 카토와 브루투스도, 유대인이 반드시 따라야 할 율법을 남긴 그 히브리 사람에 비하면 어린아이에 지나지 않습니다.\""]], [["“But my request. Hast—”", "\"하지만 내 부탁은. 자네는—\""]], [["“Thy command would be of more weight, and that would not move me.", "\"당신의 명령이라면 더 무게가 있겠지요. 하지만 그것도 저를 움직이지는 못합니다."], ["I have said.”", "저는 말했습니다.\""]], [["Both became silent, waiting.", "두 사람 다 잠잠해진 채, 기다렸다."]], [["Ben-Hur looked often at the coming ship.", "벤허는 다가오는 배를 자주 바라보았다."], ["Arrius rested with closed eyes, indifferent.", "아리우스는 눈을 감은 채 무심히 쉬고 있었다."]], [["“Art thou sure she is an enemy?”", "\"저 배가 적이라고 확신하십니까?\""], ["Ben-Hur asked.", "벤허가 물었다."]], [["“I think so,”", "\"그런 것 같네.\""], ["was the reply.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“She stops, and puts a boat over the side.”", "\"배가 멈추고, 작은 보트 한 척을 뱃전 너머로 내립니다.\""]], [["“Dost thou see her flag?”", "\"깃발이 보이나?\""]], [["“Is there no other sign by which she may be known if Roman?”", "\"로마 배라면 알아볼 다른 표지는 없습니까?\""]], [["“If Roman, she hath a helmet over the mast’s top.”", "\"로마 배라면 돛대 꼭대기에 투구가 얹혀 있네.\""]], [["“Then be of cheer.", "\"그러면 기운을 내십시오."], ["I see the helmet.”", "투구가 보입니다.\""]], [["Still Arrius was not assured.", "그래도 아리우스는 안심하지 못했다."]], [["“The men in the small boat are taking in the people afloat.", "\"작은 보트에 탄 자들이 물에 떠 있는 사람들을 거두어들이고 있습니다."], ["Pirates are not humane.”", "해적은 자비롭지 않은데요.\""]], [["“They may need rowers,”", "\"노 저을 자가 필요한지도 모르지.\""], ["Arrius replied, recurring, possibly, to times when he had made rescues for the purpose.", "아리우스가 대답했다. 어쩌면 그 자신이 그런 목적으로 사람들을 구한 적이 있던 시절을 떠올렸는지도 몰랐다."]], [["Ben-Hur was very watchful of the actions of the strangers.", "벤허는 낯선 자들의 움직임을 유심히 지켜보았다."]], [["“The ship moves off,” he said.", "\"배가 떠나갑니다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Whither?”", "\"어디로?\""]], [["“Over on our right there is a galley which I take to be deserted.", "\"우리 오른편에 갤리선이 한 척 있는데, 버려진 것 같습니다."], ["The new-comer heads towards it.", "새로 온 배가 그쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌립니다."], ["Now she is alongside.", "이제 그 옆에 붙었습니다."], ["Now she is sending men aboard.”", "이제 사람들을 그 배에 올려 보냅니다.\""]], [["Then Arrius opened his eyes and threw off his calm.", "그러자 아리우스가 눈을 뜨고 평정을 떨쳐 냈다."]], [["“Thank thou thy God,”", "\"자네의 하느님께 감사하게.\""], ["he said to Ben-Hur, after a look at the galleys, “thank thou thy God, as I do my many gods.", "갤리선들을 바라본 뒤 그가 벤허에게 말했다. \"내가 내 여러 신들께 감사하듯, 자네도 자네 하느님께 감사하게."], ["A pirate would sink, not save, yon ship.", "해적이라면 저 배를 구하기는커녕 가라앉혔을 거야."], ["By the act and the helmet on the mast I know a Roman.", "저 행동과 돛대 위의 투구로 보아 로마 배임을 알겠네."], ["The victory is mine.", "승리는 내 것이야."], ["Fortune hath not deserted me.", "운명이 나를 저버리지 않았어."], ["We are saved.", "우리는 살았네."], ["Wave thy hand—call to them—bring them quickly.", "손을 흔들게—저들을 부르게—어서 데려오게."], ["I shall be duumvir, and thou!", "나는 두움비르가 될 것이고, 자네는!"], ["I knew thy father, and loved him.", "나는 자네 아버지를 알았고, 사랑했네."], ["He was a prince indeed.", "그는 참으로 군주다운 사람이었어."], ["He taught me a Jew was not a barbarian. I will take thee with me.", "유대인이 야만인이 아니라는 것을 내게 가르쳐 준 사람이지. 자네를 데려가겠네."], ["I will make thee my son.", "자네를 내 아들로 삼겠어."], ["Give thy God thanks, and call the sailors. Haste!", "자네 하느님께 감사하고, 선원들을 부르게. 서둘러!"], ["The pursuit must be kept. Not a robber shall escape.", "추격을 늦춰서는 안 돼. 도적은 한 놈도 빠져나가게 해서는 안 되네."], ["Hasten them!”", "저들을 재촉하게!\""]], [["Judah raised himself upon the plank, and waved his hand, and called with all his might; at last he drew the attention of the sailors in the small boat, and they were speedily taken up.", "유다는 널빤지 위로 몸을 일으켜, 손을 흔들며 있는 힘껏 소리쳤다. 마침내 작은 보트에 탄 선원들의 주의를 끌었고, 두 사람은 곧 끌어올려졌다."]], [["Arrius was received on the galley with all the honors due a hero so the favorite of Fortune.", "아리우스는 운명의 그토록 큰 총애를 받은 영웅에게 합당한 온갖 예우를 받으며 갤리선에 맞아들여졌다."], ["Upon a couch on the deck he heard the particulars of the conclusion of the fight.", "그는 갑판의 침상에 누워 싸움이 어떻게 결판났는지 그 자세한 내막을 들었다."], ["When the survivors afloat upon the water were all saved and the prize secured, he spread his flag of commandant anew, and hurried northward to rejoin the fleet and perfect the victory.", "물에 떠 있던 생존자들이 모두 구조되고 노획물도 확보되자, 그는 다시 사령관의 깃발을 올리고, 함대와 다시 합류해 승리를 완성하기 위해 북쪽으로 서둘러 갔다."], ["In due time the fifty vessels coming down the channel closed in upon the fugitive pirates, and crushed them utterly; not one escaped.", "때맞추어 해협을 따라 내려온 쉰 척의 배가 달아나는 해적들을 에워싸 그들을 철저히 무찔렀으니, 한 놈도 빠져나가지 못했다."], ["To swell the tribune’s glory, twenty galleys of the enemy were captured.", "호민관의 영광을 더하기 위해, 적의 갤리선 스무 척이 나포되었다."]], [["Upon his return from the cruise, Arrius had warm welcome on the mole at Misenum.", "원정에서 돌아온 아리우스는 미세눔의 방파제에서 따뜻한 환영을 받았다."], ["The young man attending him very early attracted the attention of his friends there; and to their questions as to who he was the tribune proceeded in the most affectionate manner to tell the story of his rescue and introduce the stranger, omitting carefully all that pertained to the latter’s previous history.", "그를 수행한 젊은이는 일찍부터 그곳 벗들의 눈길을 끌었다. 그가 누구냐는 물음에, 호민관은 더없이 다정한 태도로 자신이 구조된 이야기를 들려주고 그 낯선 젊은이를 소개했으니, 그 젊은이의 지난 내력에 관한 것은 조심스럽게 모두 빼놓았다."], ["At the end of the narrative, he called Ben-Hur to him, and said, with a hand resting affectionately upon his shoulder,", "이야기를 마치자 그는 벤허를 곁으로 불러, 한 손을 그의 어깨에 다정하게 얹고 말했다."]], [["“Good friends, this is my son and heir, who, as he is to take my property—if it be the will of the gods that I leave any—shall be known to you by my name.", "\"좋은 벗들이여, 이 사람은 내 아들이자 상속자요. 그가 내 재산을 물려받을 터이니—신들의 뜻에 따라 내가 무엇이든 남기게 된다면 말이오—여러분은 그를 내 이름으로 알아 주시오."], ["I pray you all to love him as you love me.”", "부디 여러분 모두가 나를 사랑하듯 그를 사랑해 주기를 바라오.\""]], [["Speedily as opportunity permitted, the adoption was formally perfected.", "기회가 허락하는 대로 서둘러 입양이 정식으로 완결되었다."], ["And in such manner the brave Roman kept his faith with Ben-Hur, giving him happy introduction into the imperial world.", "이런 식으로 그 용감한 로마인은 벤허에게 한 약속을 지켜, 그를 황실의 세계로 행복하게 이끌어 주었다."], ["The month succeeding Arrius’s return, the armilustrium was celebrated with the utmost magnificence in the theater of Scaurus.", "아리우스가 돌아온 다음 달, 스카우루스 극장에서 아르밀루스트리움이 더없이 성대하게 거행되었다."], ["One side of the structure was taken up with military trophies; among which by far the most conspicuous and most admired were twenty prows, complemented by their corresponding aplustra, cut bodily from as many galleys; and over them, so as to be legible to the eighty thousand spectators in the seats, was this inscription:", "그 건물의 한쪽은 군사 전리품으로 가득 찼는데, 그 가운데 단연 가장 눈에 띄고 가장 찬탄을 받은 것은 스무 개의 뱃머리와 거기 딸린 고물 장식이었으니, 그만큼 많은 갤리선에서 통째로 잘라 낸 것이었다. 그리고 그 위에는, 좌석에 앉은 팔만 관중이 읽을 수 있도록, 이런 글귀가 새겨져 있었다."]], [["TAKEN FROM THE PIRATES IN THE GULF OF EURIPUS, BY QUINTUS ARRIUS, DUUMVIR.", "에우리포스만에서 해적들로부터 노획함, 두움비르 퀸투스 아리우스."]], [["“Alva.", "알바."], ["Should the monarch prove unjust— And, at this time—", "만약 군주가 부당하다면— 그리고, 이런 때에—"]], [["Queen. Then I must wait for justice Until it come; and they are happiest far Whose consciences may calmly wait their right.”", "제 양심이 권리를 차분히 기다릴 수 있는 자들이니.” 실러, 「돈 카를로스」"], ["Schiller, Don Carlos (act iv., sc. xv.) CHAPTER I", "(4막 15장)"]], [["The month to which we now come is July, the year that of our Lord 29, and the place Antioch, then Queen of the East, and next to Rome the strongest, if not the most populous, city in the world.", "우리가 이제 다다른 달은 7월, 해는 주후 29년, 그리고 장소는 안티오크다. 당시 동방의 여왕이라 불리던 그 도시는, 인구가 가장 많지는 않았을지언정 로마 다음으로 막강한 도시였다."]], [["There is an opinion that the extravagance and dissoluteness of the age had their origin in Rome, and spread thence throughout the empire; that the great cities but reflected the manners of their mistress on the Tiber.", "그 시대의 사치와 방탕이 로마에서 비롯되어 거기서 온 제국으로 퍼져 나갔으며, 대도시들은 다만 티베르 강가에 군림하는 여주인의 풍속을 비추었을 뿐이라는 견해가 있다."], ["This may be doubted. The reaction of the conquest would seem to have been upon the morals of the conqueror.", "그러나 이는 의심해 볼 만하다. 정복의 반작용은 도리어 정복자의 도덕을 향했던 것으로 보인다."], ["In Greece she found a spring of corruption; so also in Egypt; and the student, having exhausted the subject, will close the books assured that the flow of the demoralizing river was from the East westwardly, and that this very city of Antioch, one of the oldest seats of Assyrian power and splendor, was a principal source of the deadly stream.", "로마는 그리스에서 부패의 샘을 발견했고, 이집트에서도 마찬가지였다. 이 문제를 끝까지 파고든 연구자라면, 도덕을 타락시키는 강물의 흐름이 동방에서 서방으로 향했음을, 그리고 아시리아의 권력과 영화를 떨친 가장 오래된 본거지 가운데 하나인 바로 이 안티오크가 그 치명적인 물줄기의 주된 원천이었음을 확신하며 책을 덮을 것이다."]], [["A transport galley entered the mouth of the river Orontes from the blue waters of the sea.", "수송용 갤리선 한 척이 푸른 바다를 떠나 오론테스강 어귀로 들어섰다."], ["It was in the forenoon.", "오전나절이었다."], ["The heat was great, yet all on board who could avail themselves of the privilege were on deck—Ben-Hur among others.", "더위가 심했으나, 그 특권을 누릴 수 있는 자들은 모두 갑판에 나와 있었으니, 벤허도 그 가운데 하나였다."]], [["The five years had brought the young Jew to perfect manhood.", "다섯 해가 흐르는 동안 그 젊은 유대인은 완연한 장년에 이르렀다."], ["Though the robe of white linen in which he was attired somewhat masked his form, his appearance was unusually attractive.", "몸에 걸친 흰 아마포 겉옷이 그의 몸매를 다소 가렸으나, 그의 모습은 유달리 매력적이었다."], ["For an hour and more he had occupied a seat in the shade of the sail, and in that time several fellow-passengers of his own nationality had tried to engage him in conversation, but without avail.", "한 시간 넘도록 그는 돛의 그늘에 자리를 잡고 앉아 있었는데, 그동안 같은 민족의 동승객 몇이 그에게 말을 붙여 보려 했으나 헛수고였다."], ["His replies to their questions had been brief, though gravely courteous, and in the Latin tongue.", "그들의 물음에 그가 내놓는 대답은 짧았지만 무게 있게 정중했고, 그것도 라틴어였다."], ["The purity of his speech, his cultivated manners, his reticence, served to stimulate their curiosity the more.", "정갈한 말씨와 세련된 몸가짐, 그리고 과묵함이 도리어 그들의 호기심을 더욱 자극했다."], ["Such as observed him closely were struck by an incongruity between his demeanor, which had the ease and grace of a patrician, and certain points of his person.", "그를 가까이서 지켜본 자들은 그의 거동과 몸의 어떤 특징 사이에서 어긋남을 보고 놀랐다. 그의 거동에는 귀족의 여유와 우아함이 배어 있었던 것이다."], ["Thus his arms were disproportionately long; and when, to steady himself against the motion of the vessel, he took hold of anything near by, the size of his hands and their evident power compelled remark; so the wonder who and what he was mixed continually with a wish to know the particulars of his life.", "이를테면 그의 두 팔은 균형이 맞지 않을 만큼 길었다. 또 배가 흔들릴 때 몸을 가누려고 가까운 무엇을 붙잡으면, 그 손의 크기와 분명한 힘이 사람들의 눈길을 끌었다. 그리하여 그가 누구이며 어떤 사람인가 하는 궁금증이, 그의 삶을 자세히 알고 싶다는 바람과 끊임없이 뒤섞였다."], ["In other words, his air cannot be better described than as a notice—This man has a story to tell.", "달리 말하면, 그의 분위기는 이렇게밖에 더 잘 표현할 수 없다. 이 사람에게는 들려줄 이야기가 있다."]], [["The galley, in coming, had stopped at one of the ports of Cyprus, and picked up a Hebrew of most respectable appearance, quiet, reserved, paternal.", "그 갤리선은 오는 길에 키프로스의 한 항구에 들러, 더없이 점잖아 보이는 히브리인 하나를 태웠다. 조용하고 말수가 적으며 자상한 사람이었다."], ["Ben-Hur ventured to ask him some questions; the replies won his confidence, and resulted finally in an extended conversation.", "벤허는 용기를 내어 그에게 몇 가지를 물었고, 그의 대답이 신뢰를 얻어 마침내 긴 대화로 이어졌다."]], [["It chanced also that as the galley from Cyprus entered the receiving bay of the Orontes, two other vessels which had been sighted out in the sea met it and passed into the river at the same time; and as they did so both the strangers threw out small flags of brightest yellow.", "마침 키프로스를 떠난 그 갤리선이 오론테스강 어귀의 만으로 들어설 때, 바다 멀리서 발견되었던 다른 두 척의 배가 그 배를 만나 같은 시각에 강으로 들어섰다. 두 낯선 배는 강에 들어서면서 더없이 밝은 노란 깃발을 작게 내걸었다."], ["There was much conjecture as to the meaning of the signals.", "그 신호가 무엇을 뜻하는지를 두고 여러 추측이 오갔다."], ["At length a passenger addressed himself to the respectable Hebrew for information upon the subject.", "마침내 한 승객이 그 점잖은 히브리인에게 그 일에 대해 알려 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Yes, I know the meaning of the flags,”", "“그렇소, 나는 저 깃발의 뜻을 알고 있소.”"], ["he replied; “they do not signify nationality—they are merely marks of ownership.”", "그가 대답했다. “저것은 국적을 나타내는 게 아니라, 그저 소유를 표시하는 것이오.”"]], [["“Has the owner many ships?”", "“그 주인이 배를 많이 가지고 있소?”"]], [["“He has.”", "“그렇소.”"]], [["“You know him?”", "“그를 아시오?”"]], [["“I have dealt with him.”", "“그와 거래해 본 적이 있소.”"]], [["The passengers looked at the speaker as if requesting him to go on.", "승객들은 어서 이야기를 이어 가라는 듯 그 사람을 바라보았다."], ["Ben-Hur listened with interest.", "벤허도 흥미롭게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“He lives in Antioch,”", "“그는 안티오크에 사오.”"], ["the Hebrew continued, in his quiet way.", "히브리인이 차분한 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["“That he is vastly rich has brought him into notice, and the talk about him is not always kind.", "“엄청난 부자라서 세상의 주목을 받게 되었는데, 그를 둘러싼 소문이 늘 호의적이지만은 않소."], ["There used to be in Jerusalem a prince of very ancient family named Hur.”", "예전에 예루살렘에 후르라는 매우 유서 깊은 가문의 영주가 있었소.”"]], [["Judah strove to be composed, yet his heart beat quicker.", "유다는 평정을 지키려 애썼으나, 가슴이 더욱 빨리 뛰었다."]], [["“The prince was a merchant, with a genius for business.", "“그 영주는 상인이었는데, 사업에 천부적인 재능이 있었소."], ["He set on foot many enterprises, some reaching far East, others West.", "그는 수많은 사업을 벌였으니, 어떤 것은 멀리 동방까지, 어떤 것은 서방까지 뻗어 나갔소."], ["In the great cities he had branch houses.", "큰 도시마다 그는 지점을 두었소."], ["The one in Antioch was in charge of a man said by some to have been a family servant called Simonides, Greek in name, yet an Israelite.", "안티오크의 지점은 어떤 이들의 말에 따르면 한때 그 집안의 종이었다는 시모니데스라는 사람이 맡고 있었는데, 이름은 그리스식이나 이스라엘 사람이었소."], ["The master was drowned at sea.", "그 주인은 바다에서 빠져 죽었소."], ["His business, however, went on, and was scarcely less prosperous.", "하지만 그의 사업은 계속되었고, 번창함이 조금도 덜하지 않았소."], ["After a while misfortune overtook the family.", "얼마 뒤 그 가문에 불행이 닥쳤소."], ["The prince’s only son, nearly grown, tried to kill the procurator Gratus in one of the streets of Jerusalem.", "영주의 외아들은 거의 다 자란 나이였는데, 예루살렘의 어느 거리에서 총독 그라투스를 죽이려 했소."], ["He failed by a narrow chance, and has not since been heard of.", "아슬아슬하게 실패했고, 그 뒤로는 소식이 끊겼소."], ["In fact, the Roman’s rage took in the whole house—not one of the name was left alive.", "사실 그 로마인의 분노는 온 집안을 휩쓸었으니, 그 이름을 가진 자는 하나도 살아남지 못했소."], ["Their palace was sealed up, and is now a rookery for pigeons; the estate was confiscated; everything that could be traced to the ownership of the Hurs was confiscated.", "그들의 저택은 봉인되어 지금은 비둘기 떼의 둥지가 되었고, 재산은 몰수되었소. 후르 가문의 소유로 추적될 수 있는 것은 모조리 몰수되었소."], ["The procurator cured his hurt with a golden salve.”", "그 총독은 황금 고약으로 제 상처를 치료한 셈이오.”"]], [["The passengers laughed.", "승객들이 웃었다."]], [["“You mean he kept the property,” said one of them.", "“재산을 가로챘다는 말이군요.” 그들 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["“They say so,”", "“그렇게들 말하오.”"], ["the Hebrew replied; “I am only telling a story as I received it.", "히브리인이 대답했다. “나는 그저 들은 대로 이야기를 전할 뿐이오."], ["And, to go on, Simonides, who had been the prince’s agent here in Antioch, opened trade in a short time on his own account, and in a space incredibly brief became the master merchant of the city.", "이야기를 마저 하자면, 여기 안티오크에서 영주의 대리인이었던 시모니데스는 머지않아 제 이름으로 장사를 시작했고, 믿기 어려울 만큼 짧은 동안에 이 도시 제일가는 상인이 되었소."], ["In imitation of his master, he sent caravans to India; and on the sea at present he has galleys enough to make a royal fleet.", "그는 옛 주인을 본받아 인도로 대상을 보냈고, 지금 바다에는 왕실 함대를 이룰 만큼 많은 갤리선을 거느리고 있소."], ["They say nothing goes amiss with him.", "그에게는 어긋나는 일이 하나도 없다고들 하오."], ["His camels do not die, except of old age; his ships never founder; if he throw a chip into the river, it will come back to him gold.”", "그의 낙타는 늙어 죽는 일 말고는 죽는 법이 없고, 그의 배는 결코 가라앉지 않으며, 그가 강에 나뭇조각 하나를 던지면 황금이 되어 돌아온다 하오.”"]], [["“How long has he been going on thus?”", "“그가 그렇게 잘나간 지 얼마나 되었소?”"]], [["“Not ten years.”", "“십 년이 채 안 되오.”"]], [["“He must have had a good start.”", "“출발이 든든했던 모양이군요.”"]], [["“Yes, they say the procurator took only the prince’s property ready at hand—his horses, cattle, houses, land, vessels, goods.", "“그렇소, 사람들 말로는 총독이 가져간 것은 영주가 손에 쥐고 있던 재산뿐이었다 하오. 말과 가축, 집과 땅, 배와 물품 따위 말이오."], ["The money could not be found, though there must have been vast sums of it.", "돈은 끝내 찾아내지 못했는데, 분명 막대한 액수가 있었을 거요."], ["What became of it has been an unsolved mystery.”", "그것이 어찌 되었는지는 풀리지 않은 수수께끼였소.”"]], [["“Not to me,” said a passenger, with a sneer.", "“나한테는 수수께끼가 아니오.” 한 승객이 비웃듯 말했다."]], [["“I understand you,”", "“무슨 뜻인지 알겠소.”"], ["the Hebrew answered.", "히브리인이 대답했다."], ["“Others have had your idea.", "“다른 이들도 그대와 같은 생각을 했소."], ["That it furnished old Simonides his start is a common belief.", "그것이 시모니데스 노인의 밑천이 되었다는 것이 흔한 믿음이오."], ["The procurator is of that opinion—or he has been—for twice in five years he has caught the merchant, and put him to torture.”", "총독도 그렇게 여기오. 아니, 적어도 그렇게 여겨 왔소. 다섯 해 사이에 두 차례나 그 상인을 붙잡아 고문했으니 말이오.”"]], [["Judah gripped the rope he was holding with crushing force.", "유다는 잡고 있던 밧줄을 으스러뜨릴 듯한 힘으로 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“It is said,”", "“듣기로는,”"], ["the narrator continued, “that there is not a sound bone in the man’s body.", "이야기꾼이 말을 이었다. “그 사람 몸에는 성한 뼈가 하나도 없다 하오."], ["The last time I saw him he sat in a chair, a shapeless cripple, propped against cushions.”", "내가 마지막으로 보았을 때 그는 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 쿠션에 기댄 채 형체도 알아볼 수 없는 불구가 되어 있었소.”"]], [["“So tortured!” exclaimed several listeners in a breath.", "“그토록 고문을 당하다니!” 여럿이 한꺼번에 탄식했다."]], [["“Disease could not have produced such a deformity.", "“병으로는 그런 기형이 생겨날 수 없소."], ["Still the suffering made no impression upon him.", "그래도 그 고통은 그에게 아무런 영향을 주지 못했소."], ["All he had was his lawfully, and he was making lawful use of it—that was the most they wrung from him.", "그가 가진 모든 것은 합법적으로 얻은 것이며, 그것을 합법적으로 쓰고 있을 뿐이라는 것, 그들이 그에게서 짜낸 것은 그게 고작이었소."], ["Now, however, he is past persecution.", "그러나 이제 그는 박해를 넘어선 사람이 되었소."], ["He has a license to trade signed by Tiberius himself.”", "티베리우스가 직접 서명한 교역 허가증을 가지고 있으니 말이오.”"]], [["“He paid roundly for it, I warrant.”", "“그 대가로 어마어마하게 치렀겠지요.”"]], [["“These ships are his,”", "“이 배들이 그의 것이오.”"], ["the Hebrew continued, passing the remark.", "히브리인은 그 말을 흘려듣고 이야기를 이었다."], ["“It is a custom among his sailors to salute each other upon meeting by throwing out yellow flags, sight of which is as much as to say, ‘We have had a fortunate voyage.’”", "“그의 뱃사람들 사이에는 서로 마주치면 노란 깃발을 내걸어 인사하는 관습이 있소. 그 깃발을 본다는 것은 ‘우리는 순조로운 항해를 마쳤다’고 말하는 것과 같소.”"]], [["The story ended there.", "이야기는 거기서 끝났다."]], [["When the transport was fairly in the channel of the river, Judah spoke to the Hebrew.", "수송선이 강의 물길에 완전히 들어섰을 때, 유다가 그 히브리인에게 물었다."]], [["“What was the name of the merchant’s master?”", "“그 상인의 주인이라는 사람의 이름이 무엇이었소?”"]], [["“Ben-Hur, Prince of Jerusalem.”", "“예루살렘의 영주 벤허였소.”"]], [["“What became of the prince’s family?”", "“그 영주의 가족은 어찌 되었소?”"]], [["“The boy was sent to the galleys.", "“아들은 갤리선으로 보내졌소."], ["I may say he is dead.", "죽었다고 봐도 좋소."], ["One year is the ordinary limit of life under that sentence.", "그 형벌 아래에서는 한 해가 목숨의 보통 한도니까."], ["The widow and daughter have not been heard of; those who know what became of them will not speak.", "아내와 딸은 소식이 끊겼소. 그들이 어찌 되었는지 아는 자들은 입을 열려 하지 않소."], ["They died doubtless in the cells of one of the castles which spot the waysides of Judea.”", "분명 유대의 길가에 점점이 박힌 어느 성채의 지하 감방에서 죽었을 거요.”"]], [["Judah walked to the pilot’s quarter.", "유다는 키잡이가 있는 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["So absorbed was he in thought that he scarcely noticed the shores of the river, which from sea to city were surpassingly beautiful with orchards of all the Syrian fruits and vines, clustered about villas rich as those of Neapolis.", "그는 생각에 깊이 잠긴 나머지, 바다에서 도시에 이르기까지 더없이 아름다운 강기슭을 거의 알아차리지 못했다. 그 기슭에는 시리아의 온갖 과일나무와 포도밭이 어우러져, 네아폴리스의 별장만큼이나 호화로운 별장들을 에워싸고 있었다."], ["No more did he observe the vessels passing in an endless fleet, nor hear the singing and shouting of the sailors, some in labor, some in merriment.", "끝없는 함대를 이루며 지나는 배들도, 일하느라 혹은 흥에 겨워 부르는 뱃사람들의 노랫소리와 외침도 그의 눈과 귀에 들어오지 않았다."], ["The sky was full of sunlight, lying in hazy warmth upon the land and the water; nowhere except over his life was there a shadow.", "하늘은 햇빛으로 가득 차, 흐릿한 온기가 땅과 물 위에 내려앉았다. 그의 삶 위를 빼고는 어디에도 그림자가 없었다."]], [["Once only he awoke to a momentary interest, and that was when some one pointed out the Grove of Daphne, discernible from a bend in the river.", "단 한 번 그는 잠깐의 흥미에 깨어났는데, 누군가가 강이 굽이도는 자리에서 보이는 다프네 숲을 가리켰을 때였다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["When the city came into view, the passengers were on deck, eager that nothing of the scene might escape them.", "도시가 시야에 들어오자, 승객들은 갑판 위로 나와 그 광경의 어느 하나도 놓치지 않으려고 마음을 졸였다."], ["The respectable Jew already introduced to the reader was the principal spokesman.", "앞서 독자에게 소개된 점잖은 유대인이 주된 이야기꾼이었다."]], [["“The river here runs to the west,”", "\"여기서 강은 서쪽으로 흐릅니다.\""], ["he said, in the way of general answer.", "그가 모두를 향한 대답처럼 말했다."], ["“I remember when it washed the base of the walls; but as Roman subjects we have lived in peace, and, as always happens in such times, trade has had its will; now the whole river front is taken up with wharves and docks.", "\"예전엔 이 강물이 성벽 발치를 씻고 흘렀던 걸 기억합니다. 그러나 로마의 신민으로서 우리는 평화롭게 살아왔고, 그런 시절이면 으레 그렇듯 교역이 제멋대로 번성했지요. 이제 강가는 온통 부두와 선창으로 들어찼습니다."], ["Yonder”—the speaker pointed southward—“is Mount Casius, or, as these people love to call it, the Mountains of Orontes, looking across to its brother Amnus in the north; and between them lies the Plain of Antioch.", "저기\"—말하는 이가 남쪽을 가리켰다—\"카시우스 산이 있는데, 이곳 사람들은 즐겨 오론테스 산이라 부르며, 북쪽에 있는 형제 격인 암누스 산과 마주 보고 있습니다. 그 둘 사이에 안티오크 평원이 펼쳐져 있지요."], ["Farther on are the Black Mountains, whence the Ducts of the Kings bring the purest water to wash the thirsty streets and people; yet they are forests in wilderness state, dense, and full of birds and beasts.”", "더 멀리에는 검은 산들이 있는데, 그곳에서 '왕들의 수로'가 가장 맑은 물을 끌어와 목마른 거리와 사람들을 적셔 줍니다. 그래도 그 산들은 황야 그대로의 숲이어서, 빽빽하고 새와 짐승으로 가득하답니다.\""]], [["“Where is the lake?”", "\"호수는 어디 있습니까?\""], ["one asked.", "한 사람이 물었다."]], [["“Over north there.", "\"저기 북쪽에 있습니다."], ["You can take horse, if you wish to see it—or, better, a boat, for a tributary connects it with the river.”", "보고 싶으시면 말을 타셔도 되고—아니면 배가 더 낫지요. 지류 하나가 그 호수를 강과 이어 주니까요.\""]], [["“The Grove of Daphne!”", "\"다프네 숲 말입니까!\""], ["he said, to a third inquirer.", "그가 세 번째 묻는 이에게 말했다."], ["“Nobody can describe it; only beware!", "\"그건 누구도 묘사할 수가 없습니다. 다만 조심하십시오!"], ["It was begun by Apollo, and completed by him. He prefers it to Olympus.", "아폴론이 그것을 시작하여 손수 완성했지요. 그분은 그곳을 올림포스보다 더 좋아한답니다."], ["People go there for one look—just one—and never come away.", "사람들은 한 번 보러 갔다가—단 한 번이지요—다시는 떠나오지 못합니다."], ["They have a saying which tells it all—‘Better be a worm and feed on the mulberries of Daphne than a king’s guest.’”", "그곳을 다 말해 주는 속담이 있지요—'왕의 손님이 되느니 차라리 벌레가 되어 다프네의 뽕나무를 먹는 게 낫다.'\""]], [["“Then you advise me to stay away from it?”", "\"그렇다면 거길 멀리하라고 충고하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Not I!", "\"천만에요!"], ["Go you will.", "가실 겁니다."], ["Everybody goes, cynic philosopher, virile boy, women, and priests—all go.", "누구나 다 갑니다. 냉소적인 철학자도, 혈기 넘치는 젊은이도, 여자들도, 사제들도—모두 다 갑니다."], ["So sure am I of what you will do that I assume to advise you.", "당신이 어찌하실지 확신하기에, 제가 충고를 드리는 겁니다."], ["Do not take quarters in the city—that will be loss of time; but go at once to the village in the edge of the grove.", "도성 안에 거처를 잡지 마십시오—그건 시간 낭비예요. 곧장 숲 가장자리에 있는 마을로 가십시오."], ["The way is through a garden, under the spray of fountains.", "가는 길은 정원을 지나고, 분수의 물보라 아래를 거치게 됩니다."], ["The lovers of the god and his Penaean maid built the town; and in its porticos and paths and thousand retreats you will find characters and habits and sweets and kinds elsewhere impossible.", "그 신과 그의 페네이오스 처녀를 사랑하는 이들이 그 마을을 지었지요. 그 회랑과 오솔길, 그리고 수많은 은밀한 곳에서 당신은 다른 데서는 도무지 찾을 수 없는 사람들과 풍습과 즐거움과 온갖 부류를 만나게 될 겁니다."], ["But the wall of the city!", "그런데 저 성벽을 보십시오!"], ["there it is, the masterpiece of Xeraeus, the master of mural architecture.”", "저기 있군요, 성벽 건축의 대가 크세라이오스의 걸작입니다.\""]], [["All eyes followed his pointing finger.", "모두의 눈이 그가 가리키는 손끝을 따라갔다."]], [["“This part was raised by order of the first of the Seleucidæ.", "\"이 부분은 셀레우코스 왕조의 초대 왕이 명하여 쌓은 것입니다."], ["Three hundred years have made it part of the rock it rests upon.”", "삼백 년 세월이 흐르는 동안, 그것은 받치고 선 바위와 한 몸이 되어 버렸지요.\""]], [["The defense justified the encomium.", "그 방벽은 그 찬사에 걸맞았다."], ["High, solid, and with many bold angles, it curved southwardly out of view.", "높고 견고하며, 대담하게 꺾인 모서리가 숱하게 있어, 남쪽으로 굽이치며 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["“On the top there are four hundred towers, each a reservoir of water,”", "\"꼭대기에는 사백 개의 망루가 있고, 그 하나하나가 물 저장고랍니다.\""], ["the Hebrew continued. “Look now!", "히브리인이 말을 이었다."], ["Over the wall, tall as it is, see in the distance two hills, which you may know as the rival crests of Sulpius.", "\"자, 보십시오! 저렇게 높은 성벽 너머로, 멀리 두 언덕이 보이지요. 술피우스의 쌍둥이 봉우리라 아실 겁니다."], ["The structure on the farthest one is the citadel, garrisoned all the year round by a Roman legion.", "가장 먼 봉우리 위에 있는 건물이 성채인데, 일 년 내내 로마 군단이 주둔합니다."], ["Opposite it this way rises the Temple of Jupiter, and under that the front of the legate’s residence—a palace full of offices, and yet a fortress against which a mob would dash harmlessly as a south wind.”", "그 맞은편 이쪽으로 솟은 것이 유피테르 신전이고, 그 아래가 총독 관저의 정면이지요—온갖 관청으로 가득한 궁전이면서도, 폭도가 부딪혀 봐야 남풍처럼 무력하게 흩어질 뿐인 요새랍니다.\""]], [["At this point the sailors began taking in sail, whereupon the Hebrew exclaimed, heartily, “See!", "이때 선원들이 돛을 거두기 시작하자, 히브리인이 진심을 담아 외쳤다. \"보십시오!"], ["you who hate the sea, and you who have vows, get ready your curses and your prayers.", "바다를 미워하시는 분들과 서원을 세우신 분들, 이제 저주의 말과 기도를 준비하십시오."], ["The bridge yonder, over which the road to Seleucia is carried, marks the limit of navigation.", "저기 보이는 다리, 셀레우키아로 가는 길이 지나는 저 다리가 항해의 끝을 알리는 표지입니다."], ["What the ship unloads for further transit, the camel takes up there.", "배가 더 멀리 보내려고 부려 놓은 짐을, 저기서 낙타가 넘겨받지요."], ["Above the bridge begins the island upon which Calinicus built his new city, connecting it with five great viaducts so solid time has made no impression upon them, nor floods nor earthquakes.", "저 다리 위로는 섬이 시작되는데, 칼리니쿠스가 그 위에 새 도시를 세우고 다섯 개의 거대한 수도교로 이어 놓았답니다. 어찌나 견고한지 세월도, 홍수도, 지진도 아무런 흔적을 남기지 못했지요."], ["Of the main town, my friends, I have only to say you will be happier all your lives for having seen it.”", "본 도성에 대해서는, 벗들이여, 그저 이 말만 하렵니다. 그것을 본 까닭에 여러분은 평생토록 더 행복하실 거라고요.\""]], [["As he concluded, the ship turned and made slowly for her wharf under the wall, bringing even more fairly to view the life with which the river at that point was possessed.", "그가 말을 맺자, 배는 방향을 틀어 성벽 아래 부두를 향해 천천히 나아갔다. 그러면서 그 지점의 강을 가득 메운 삶의 모습이 한층 또렷이 드러났다."], ["Finally, the lines were thrown, the oars shipped, and the voyage was done.", "마침내 밧줄이 던져지고, 노가 거두어지고, 항해가 끝났다."], ["Then Ben-Hur sought the respectable Hebrew.", "그러자 벤허는 그 점잖은 히브리인을 찾았다."]], [["“Let me trouble you a moment before saying farewell.”", "\"작별을 고하기 전에 잠시 폐를 끼치겠습니다.\""]], [["The man bowed assent.", "남자가 머리를 숙여 응했다."]], [["“Your story of the merchant has made me curious to see him.", "\"그 상인 이야기를 듣고 나니 그를 만나 보고 싶어졌습니다."], ["You called him Simonides?”", "시모니데스라고 부르셨지요?\""]], [["“Yes. He is a Jew with a Greek name.”", "\"그렇습니다. 그리스식 이름을 가진 유대인이지요.\""]], [["“Where is he to be found?”", "\"어디로 가면 만날 수 있습니까?\""]], [["The acquaintance gave a sharp look before he answered,", "그가 대답하기 전에 날카로운 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“I may save you mortification.", "\"낭패를 면하시도록 미리 말씀드리지요."], ["He is not a money-lender.”", "그는 돈을 빌려주는 사람이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Nor am I a money-borrower,” said Ben-Hur, smiling at the other’s shrewdness.", "\"저 또한 돈을 빌리려는 사람이 아닙니다.\" 벤허가 상대의 눈치 빠름에 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["The man raised his head and considered an instant.", "남자가 고개를 들고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“One would think,” he then replied, “that the richest merchant in Antioch would have a house for business corresponding to his wealth; but if you would find him in the day, follow the river to yon bridge, under which he quarters in a building that looks like a buttress of the wall.", "\"안티오크에서 가장 부유한 상인이라면, 으레 그 재산에 걸맞은 사업장을 가졌으리라 여기시겠지요.\" 그가 이윽고 대답했다."], ["Before the door there is an immense landing, always covered with cargoes come and to go.", "\"그러나 낮에 그를 찾으시려거든, 강을 따라 저 다리까지 가십시오. 그 아래에 그가 거처하는데, 성벽의 버팀벽처럼 보이는 건물이랍니다. 문 앞에는 어마어마한 선창이 있고, 오가는 화물로 늘 뒤덮여 있지요."], ["The fleet that lies moored there is his.", "거기 매여 있는 선단이 모두 그의 것입니다."], ["You cannot fail to find him.”", "틀림없이 찾으실 겁니다.\""]], [["“I give you thanks.”", "\"감사드립니다.\""]], [["“The peace of our fathers go with you.”", "\"우리 조상들의 평화가 그대와 함께하기를.\""]], [["“And with you.”", "\"그대와도 함께하기를.\""]], [["With that they separated.", "그 말과 함께 둘은 헤어졌다."]], [["Two street-porters, loaded with his baggage, received Ben-Hur’s orders upon the wharf.", "거리의 짐꾼 둘이 그의 짐을 짊어지고, 부두에서 벤허의 지시를 받았다."]], [["“To the citadel,”", "\"성채로.\""], ["he said; a direction which implied an official military connection.", "그가 말했다. 군의 공적 직무와 관련이 있음을 내비치는 지시였다."]], [["Two great streets, cutting each other at right angles, divided the city into quarters.", "직각으로 서로 엇갈리는 두 개의 큰길이 도시를 네 구역으로 나누고 있었다."], ["A curious and immense structure, called the Nymphaeum, arose at the foot of the one running north and south.", "님파이움이라 불리는 기이하고도 거대한 건축물이 남북으로 뻗은 길의 어귀에 솟아 있었다."], ["When the porters turned south there, the new-comer, though fresh from Rome, was amazed at the magnificence of the avenue.", "짐꾼들이 거기서 남쪽으로 돌아서자, 로마에서 막 왔는데도 새로 온 이는 그 대로의 웅장함에 놀라움을 금치 못했다."], ["On the right and left there were palaces, and between them extended indefinitely double colonnades of marble, leaving separate ways for footmen, beasts, and chariots; the whole under shade, and cooled by fountains of incessant flow.", "좌우로는 궁전들이 늘어섰고, 그 사이로 대리석 이중 회랑이 끝없이 뻗어, 보행자와 짐승과 전차가 다닐 길을 따로따로 내고 있었다. 그 모두가 그늘 아래 있었고, 쉼 없이 솟는 분수가 그곳을 시원하게 식혀 주었다."]], [["Ben-Hur was not in mood to enjoy the spectacle.", "벤허는 그 광경을 즐길 기분이 아니었다."], ["The story of Simonides haunted him.", "시모니데스의 이야기가 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다."], ["Arrived at the Omphalus—a monument of four arches wide as the streets, superbly illustrated, and erected to himself by Epiphanes, the eighth of the Seleucidæ—he suddenly changed his mind.", "옴팔로스에 이르렀을 때—거리만큼이나 폭이 넓고, 빼어나게 장식된 네 개의 아치로 된 기념물로, 셀레우코스 왕조의 여덟 번째 왕 에피파네스가 자기 자신을 기려 세운 것이었다—그는 문득 마음을 바꾸었다."]], [["“I will not go to the citadel to-night,”", "\"오늘 밤엔 성채로 가지 않겠다.\""], ["he said to the porters.", "그가 짐꾼들에게 말했다."], ["“Take me to the khan nearest the bridge on the road to Seleucia.”", "\"셀레우키아로 가는 길에서 다리에 가장 가까운 칸으로 데려다다오.\""]], [["The party faced about, and in good time he was deposited in a public house of primitive but ample construction, within stone’s-throw of the bridge under which old Simonides had his quarters.", "일행은 방향을 돌렸고, 이윽고 그는 소박하나 너른 구조의 객사에 짐을 풀었다. 늙은 시모니데스가 거처하는 다리에서 돌을 던지면 닿을 거리였다. 그는 밤새도록 옥상에 누워 있었다."], ["He lay upon the house-top through the night.", "그의 마음 깊은 곳에는 이런 생각이 살아 있었다."], ["In his inner mind lived the thought, “Now—now I will hear of home—and mother—and the dear little Tirzah. If they are on earth, I will find them.”", "\"이제—이제 집 소식을 듣겠구나—어머니와—사랑스러운 어린 티르자의 소식을. 그들이 이 땅에 살아 있다면, 내가 반드시 찾아내리라.\""]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["Next day early, to the neglect of the city, Ben-Hur sought the house of Simonides.", "바로 이튿날 아침, 벤허는 도시 구경도 뒤로 미룬 채 시모니데스의 집을 찾았다."], ["Through an embattled gateway he passed to a continuity of wharves; thence up the river midst a busy press, to the Seleucian Bridge, under which he paused to take in the scene.", "그는 성가퀴를 두른 성문을 지나 죽 늘어선 부두로 들어섰고, 거기서 분주히 북적이는 인파 사이로 강을 거슬러 올라가 셀레우키아 다리에 이르렀다. 그는 그 다리 아래에서 걸음을 멈추고 주위 광경을 살펴보았다."]], [["There, directly under the bridge, was the merchant’s house, a mass of gray stone, unhewn, referable to no style, looking, as the voyager had described it, like a buttress of the wall against which it leaned.", "그곳, 바로 다리 밑에 상인의 집이 있었다. 다듬지 않은 잿빛 돌을 쌓아 올린 덩어리로, 어떤 양식에도 들어맞지 않았고, 뱃사람이 묘사했던 그대로 자신이 기댄 성벽의 버팀벽처럼 보였다."], ["Two immense doors in front communicated with the wharf.", "정면에 난 두 개의 거대한 문이 부두로 통했다."], ["Some holes near the top, heavily barred, served as windows. Weeds waved from the crevices, and in places black moss splotched the otherwise bald stones.", "꼭대기 가까이 난 몇 개의 구멍은 굵은 창살로 단단히 막혀 창문 노릇을 했다. 틈새마다 잡초가 흔들거렸고, 군데군데 검은 이끼가 헐벗은 돌을 얼룩지게 했다."]], [["The doors were open.", "문은 열려 있었다."], ["Through one of them business went in; through the other it came out; and there was hurry, hurry in all its movements.", "한쪽 문으로는 거래가 들어가고, 다른 쪽 문으로는 거래가 나왔으니, 그 모든 움직임에는 바삐, 바삐 서두르는 기색이 가득했다."]], [["On the wharf there were piles of goods in every kind of package, and groups of slaves, stripped to the waist, going about in the abandon of labor.", "부두에는 온갖 모양으로 꾸린 화물 더미가 쌓여 있었고, 허리까지 벗은 노예들이 무리를 지어, 일에 몰두한 채 분주히 오갔다."]], [["Below the bridge lay a fleet of galleys, some loading, others unloading.", "다리 아래에는 갤리선 선단이 떠 있었으니, 어떤 배는 짐을 싣고, 어떤 배는 짐을 내렸다."], ["A yellow flag blew out from each masthead.", "돛대 꼭대기마다 노란 깃발이 나부꼈다."], ["From fleet and wharf, and from ship to ship, the bondmen of traffic passed in clamorous counter-currents.", "선단과 부두 사이에서, 그리고 배에서 배로, 거래에 매인 종들이 시끌벅적하게 마주 흐르는 물결처럼 오갔다."]], [["Above the bridge, across the river, a wall rose from the water’s edge, over which towered the fanciful cornices and turrets of an imperial palace, covering every foot of the island spoken of in the Hebrew’s description.", "다리 위쪽으로, 강 건너편에는 물가에서부터 성벽이 솟아 있었고, 그 너머로 황제의 궁전이 화려한 처마 장식과 작은 탑들을 우뚝 세우고 있었으니, 그 히브리인이 묘사했던 섬을 한 치도 남김없이 뒤덮고 있었다."], ["But, with all its suggestions, Ben-Hur scarcely noticed it.", "그러나 그 모든 암시에도 불구하고, 벤허는 그것을 거의 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["Now, at last, he thought to hear of his people—this, certainly, if Simonides had indeed been his father’s slave.", "이제야, 마침내 그는 자기 가족의 소식을 들을 수 있으리라 생각했다. 시모니데스가 정말로 자기 아버지의 종이었다면 분명 그러할 것이었다."], ["But would the man acknowledge the relation?", "그러나 그 사람이 그 관계를 인정하려 할까?"], ["That would be to give up his riches and the sovereignty of trade so royally witnessed on the wharf and river.", "그것은 자신의 재물과, 부두와 강에서 그토록 왕처럼 위세를 드러내는 거래의 지배권을 내놓는 일이었다."], ["And what was of still greater consequence to the merchant, it would be to forego his career in the midst of amazing success, and yield himself voluntarily once more a slave.", "게다가 상인에게 더욱 중대한 것은, 그토록 놀라운 성공의 한복판에서 자신의 출세를 포기하고, 스스로 다시 한 번 종이 되기를 자청하는 일이었다. 그런 요구를 떠올리는 것만으로도 터무니없는 만용처럼 여겨졌다."], ["Simple thought of the demand seemed a monstrous audacity.", "외교적 수사를 벗겨 내면, 그것은 곧 이렇게 말하는 셈이었다."], ["Stripped of diplomatic address, it was to say, You are my slave; give me all you have, and—yourself.", "당신은 내 종이오. 당신이 가진 모든 것을 내게 주시오, 그리고—당신 자신까지도."]], [["Yet Ben-Hur derived strength for the interview from faith in his rights and the hope uppermost in his heart.", "그래도 벤허는 자신의 권리에 대한 믿음과, 가슴속에 가장 크게 자리한 희망에서 이 면담에 임할 힘을 얻었다."], ["If the story to which he was yielding were true, Simonides belonged to him, with all he had.", "그가 마음을 내맡긴 그 이야기가 사실이라면, 시모니데스는 그가 가진 모든 것과 더불어 벤허에게 속한 사람이었다."], ["For the wealth, be it said in justice, he cared nothing.", "그러나 공정하게 말하건대, 그 재물 따위는 벤허에게 아무런 관심거리도 아니었다."], ["When he started to the door determined in mind, it was with a promise to himself—“Let him tell me of mother and Tirzah, and I will give him his freedom without account.”", "마음을 굳히고 문으로 향할 때, 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 다짐했다. \"그가 어머니와 티르자의 소식을 들려준다면, 나는 따지지 않고 그에게 자유를 주리라.\""]], [["He passed boldly into the house.", "그는 당당하게 집 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["The interior was that of a vast depot where, in ordered spaces, and under careful arrangement, goods of every kind were heaped and pent.", "내부는 거대한 창고였으니, 정연하게 구획된 공간마다 온갖 종류의 물건이 빈틈없이 쌓여 갇혀 있었다."], ["Though the light was murky and the air stifling, men moved about briskly; and in places he saw workmen with saws and hammers making packages for shipments.", "빛은 어둑하고 공기는 숨 막힐 듯했으나, 사람들은 활기차게 움직였다. 군데군데 일꾼들이 톱과 망치를 들고 선적용 짐짝을 짜고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Down a path between the piles he walked slowly, wondering if the man of whose genius there were here such abounding proofs could have been his father’s slave?", "그는 화물 더미 사이로 난 길을 따라 천천히 걸으며, 여기 이토록 넘치는 천재성의 증거를 남긴 사람이 과연 자기 아버지의 종이었을 수 있을까 의아해했다."], ["If so, to what class had he belonged?", "만일 그렇다면, 그는 어떤 부류에 속했을까?"], ["If a Jew, was he the son of a servant?", "유대인이라면, 종의 아들이었을까?"], ["Or was he a debtor or a debtor’s son?", "아니면 빚진 자, 혹은 빚진 자의 아들이었을까?"], ["Or had he been sentenced and sold for theft?", "아니면 도둑질로 판결을 받아 팔린 것일까?"], ["These thoughts, as they passed, in nowise disturbed the growing respect for the merchant of which he was each instant more and more conscious.", "이런 생각들이 스쳐 갔으나, 그 어느 것도 순간순간 더욱 또렷이 느껴지는 상인에 대한 존경심을 조금도 흔들지 못했다."], ["A peculiarity of our admiration for another is that it is always looking for circumstances to justify itself.", "우리가 누군가를 흠모할 때 보이는 한 가지 특징은, 그 흠모를 정당화해 줄 정황을 늘 찾는다는 것이다."]], [["At length a man approached and spoke to him.", "마침내 한 사람이 다가와 그에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“What would you have?”", "\"무슨 일로 오셨습니까?\""]], [["“I would see Simonides, the merchant.”", "\"시모니데스 상인을 뵙고 싶소.\""]], [["“Will you come this way?”", "\"이쪽으로 오시겠습니까?\""]], [["By a number of paths left in the stowage, they finally came to a flight of steps; ascending which, he found himself on the roof of the depot, and in front of a structure which cannot be better described than as a lesser stone house built upon another, invisible from the landing below, and out west of the bridge under the open sky.", "쌓인 화물 사이로 난 여러 길을 지나, 그들은 마침내 한 줄로 늘어선 계단에 이르렀다. 그 계단을 올라가니 벤허는 창고 지붕 위에 서 있었고, 그 앞에는 한 건물이 있었다. 그것을 가장 잘 묘사하자면, 또 다른 석조 건물 위에 세운 작은 돌집이라 할 수 있었으니, 아래쪽 부두에서는 보이지 않았고, 다리 서쪽으로 탁 트인 하늘 아래 있었다."], ["The roof, hemmed in by a low wall, seemed like a terrace, which, to his astonishment, was brilliant with flowers; in the rich surrounding, the house sat squat, a plain square block, unbroken except by a doorway in front.", "낮은 담으로 둘린 그 지붕은 테라스 같았으며, 놀랍게도 갖가지 꽃으로 화사했다. 그 풍요로운 둘레 한가운데, 그 집은 납작하게 앉아 있었으니, 정면의 문간 말고는 어디 하나 틈 없는 밋밋한 네모난 덩어리였다."], ["A dustless path led to the door, through a bordering of shrubs of Persian rose in perfect bloom.", "먼지 한 점 없는 길이 활짝 핀 페르시아 장미 덤불 사이로 문까지 이어졌다."], ["Breathing a sweet attar-perfume, he followed the guide.", "달콤한 장미 향유의 향기를 맡으며, 벤허는 안내자를 뒤따랐다."]], [["At the end of a darkened passage within, they stopped before a curtain half parted.", "안으로 들어가 어둑한 통로 끝에 이르자, 그들은 반쯤 열린 휘장 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The man called out,", "그 사람이 소리쳐 알렸다."]], [["“A stranger to see the master.”", "\"낯선 분이 주인님을 뵈러 오셨습니다.\""]], [["A clear voice replied, “In God’s name, let him enter.”", "맑은 목소리가 대답했다. \"하느님의 이름으로, 들라 하라.\""]], [["A Roman might have called the apartment into which the visitor was ushered his atrium.", "로마인이라면 손님이 안내받아 든 그 방을 아트리움이라 불렀을 것이다."], ["The walls were paneled; each panel was comparted like a modern office-desk, and each compartment crowded with labelled folios all filemot with age and use.", "벽에는 널빤지가 둘려 있었고, 널빤지마다 오늘날의 사무용 책상처럼 칸칸이 나뉘었으며, 칸마다 이름표를 붙인 두루마리들이 빽빽이 들어차 있었는데, 모두 세월과 손때에 낡아 누렇게 변해 있었다."], ["Between the panels, and above and below them, were borders of wood once white, now tinted like cream, and carved with marvellous intricacy of design.", "널빤지들 사이와 그 위아래로는 한때 희었으나 이제는 크림빛으로 물든 나무 가장자리 장식이 둘려 있었으니, 놀랍도록 정교한 무늬로 새겨져 있었다."], ["Above a cornice of gilded balls, the ceiling rose in pavilion style until it broke into a shallow dome set with hundreds of panes of violet mica, permitting a flood of light deliciously reposeful.", "도금한 둥근 장식을 두른 돌림띠 위로 천장은 정자 모양으로 솟아오르다가, 보랏빛 운모 수백 장을 끼운 얕은 둥근 지붕으로 트였으니, 더없이 편안한 빛이 쏟아져 내렸다."], ["The floor was carpeted with gray rugs so thick that an invading foot fell half buried and soundless.", "바닥에는 잿빛 양탄자가 어찌나 두툼하게 깔렸는지, 발을 들여놓으면 절반쯤 파묻혀 소리조차 나지 않았다."]], [["In the midlight of the room were two persons—a man resting in a chair high-backed, broad-armed, and lined with pliant cushions; and at his left, leaning against the back of the chair, a girl well forward into womanhood.", "방의 은은한 빛 가운데 두 사람이 있었다. 한 사람은 등받이가 높고 팔걸이가 넓으며 폭신한 방석을 댄 의자에 기대앉은 남자였고, 그 왼편에서 의자 등받이에 기댄 채 서 있는 이는 한창 여인으로 무르익은 처녀였다."], ["At sight of them Ben-Hur felt the blood redden his forehead; bowing, as much to recover himself as in respect, he lost the lifting of the hands, and the shiver and shrink with which the sitter caught sight of him—an emotion as swift to go as it had been to come.", "그들을 보는 순간 벤허는 이마로 피가 솟구쳐 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 절을 했는데, 예의를 차리려는 것 못지않게 마음을 가다듬으려는 까닭이었다. 그래서 그는 앉아 있던 사람이 그를 발견하고 두 손을 치켜드는 것도, 흠칫 떨며 움츠리는 것도 놓치고 말았으니, 그것은 솟구쳐 오른 만큼이나 재빨리 스러진 감정이었다."], ["When he raised his eyes the two were in the same position, except the girl’s hand had fallen and was resting lightly upon the elder’s shoulder; both of them were regarding him fixedly.", "그가 눈을 들었을 때 두 사람은 똑같은 자세 그대로였으나, 다만 처녀의 손은 내려와 노인의 어깨 위에 가만히 얹혀 있었다. 두 사람 다 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“If you are Simonides, the merchant, and a Jew”—Ben-Hur stopped an instant—“then the peace of the God of our father Abraham upon you and—yours.”", "\"당신이 시모니데스 상인이며, 유대인이시라면\"—벤허는 잠시 말을 멈추었다—\"우리 조상 아브라함의 하느님의 평화가 당신께, 그리고—당신 곁의 분께 깃들기를 빕니다.\""]], [["The last word was addressed to the girl.", "마지막 말은 처녀를 향한 것이었다."]], [["“I am the Simonides of whom you speak, by birthright a Jew,”", "\"내가 바로 당신이 말한 시모니데스요. 태생으로 유대인이오.\""], ["the man made answer, in a voice singularly clear.", "그 사람이 유달리 맑은 목소리로 대답했다."], ["“I am Simonides, and a Jew; and I return you your salutation, with prayer to know who calls upon me.”", "\"나는 시모니데스이며, 유대인이오. 당신의 인사를 그대로 돌려드리며, 나를 찾아온 분이 누구신지 알고자 청하오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur looked as he listened, and where the figure of the man should have been in healthful roundness, there was only a formless heap sunk in the depths of the cushions, and covered by a quilted robe of sombre silk.", "벤허는 들으면서 살펴보았다. 그 사람의 몸이 건강하게 둥글어야 할 자리에는, 그저 형체 없는 살덩어리가 방석 깊숙이 가라앉아, 빛바랜 비단 누비옷에 덮여 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Over the heap shone a head royally proportioned—the ideal head of a statesman and conqueror—a head broad of base and domelike in front, such as Angelo would have modelled for Cæsar.", "그 살덩어리 위로 왕처럼 균형 잡힌 머리가 빛났으니, 정치가와 정복자에게 어울릴 이상적인 머리, 바탕이 넓고 앞이마가 둥근, 미켈란젤로가 카이사르를 빚는다면 본떴을 법한 머리였다."], ["White hair dropped in thin locks over the white brows, deepening the blackness of the eyes shining through them like sullen lights.", "흰 눈썹 위로 흰 머리칼이 가는 가닥으로 드리워, 그 사이로 음울한 등불처럼 빛나는 눈동자의 검은빛을 더욱 짙게 했다."], ["The face was bloodless, and much puffed with folds, especially under the chin.", "얼굴은 핏기가 없었고, 주름이 잔뜩 잡혀 부어 있었으니, 특히 턱 아래가 그러했다."], ["In other words, the head and face were those of a man who might move the world more readily than the world could move him—a man to be twice twelve times tortured into the shapeless cripple he was, without a groan, much less a confession; a man to yield his life, but never a purpose or a point; a man born in armor, and assailable only through his loves.", "다시 말해, 그 머리와 얼굴은 세상이 그를 움직이기보다 그가 세상을 더 쉽게 움직일 만한 사람의 것이었다. 스물네 번을 다시 고문당해 지금의 일그러진 불구가 되도록 신음 한 번 내지 않았을, 자백은 더더욱 하지 않았을 사람, 목숨은 내놓을지언정 뜻이나 신념은 결코 굽히지 않을 사람, 갑옷을 입고 태어나 오직 사랑을 통해서만 공략당할 수 있는 사람의 것이었다."], ["To him Ben-Hur stretched his hands, open and palm up, as he would offer peace at the same time he asked it.", "그런 사람에게 벤허는 두 손을 내밀었으니, 손바닥을 펴 위로 향한 채, 평화를 청하는 동시에 평화를 건네듯 했다."]], [["“I am Judah, son of Ithamar, late head of the House of Hur, and a prince of Jerusalem.”", "\"나는 유다, 이다말의 아들이며, 후르 가문의 옛 가장이자, 예루살렘의 왕자입니다.\""]], [["The merchant’s right hand lay outside the robe—a long, thin hand, articulate to deformity with suffering.", "상인의 오른손은 옷 밖에 놓여 있었으니, 길고 가는 손이었고, 고통으로 마디마디 흉하게 비틀려 있었다."], ["It closed tightly; otherwise there was not the slightest expression of feeling of any kind on his part; nothing to warrant an inference of surprise or interest; nothing but this calm answer,", "그 손이 꽉 쥐어졌다. 그 밖에 그의 편에서는 어떠한 감정의 표시도 조금도 없었다. 놀라움이나 관심을 짐작케 할 만한 것은 아무것도 없었으니, 다만 이 차분한 대답뿐이었다."]], [["“The princes of Jerusalem, of the pure blood, are always welcome in my house; you are welcome.", "\"순수한 혈통의 예루살렘 왕자들은 언제나 내 집에서 환영받소. 당신을 환영하오."], ["Give the young man a seat, Esther.”", "에스더야, 이 젊은이에게 자리를 내드려라.\""]], [["The girl took an ottoman near by, and carried it to Ben-Hur.", "처녀는 가까이 있던 등받이 없는 의자를 가져다 벤허에게 놓아주었다."], ["As she arose from placing the seat, their eyes met.", "자리를 놓고 몸을 일으키다가, 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다."]], [["“The peace of our Lord with you,”", "\"우리 주님의 평화가 당신과 함께하기를.\""], ["she said, modestly.", "그녀가 조심스레 말했다."], ["“Be seated and at rest.”", "\"앉으셔서 편히 쉬십시오.\""]], [["When she resumed her place by the chair, she had not divined his purpose.", "그녀가 다시 의자 곁 제자리로 돌아갔을 때, 그녀는 그가 무엇을 하려는지 헤아리지 못했다."], ["The powers of woman go not so far: if the matter is of finer feeling, such as pity, mercy, sympathy, that she detects; and therein is a difference between her and man which will endure as long as she remains, by nature, alive to such feelings.", "여인의 힘은 거기까지 미치지 못한다. 다만 그것이 연민이나 자비, 동정 같은 더 섬세한 감정이라면, 그녀는 그것을 알아챈다. 바로 거기에 여인과 남자의 차이가 있으니, 그 차이는 여인이 천성적으로 그런 감정에 민감하게 살아 있는 한 영원히 지속될 것이다."], ["She was simply sure he brought some wound of life for healing.", "그녀는 그저 그가 치유받아야 할 삶의 상처를 안고 왔다고 확신했을 뿐이었다."]], [["Ben-Hur did not take the offered seat, but said, deferentially, “I pray the good master Simonides that he will not hold me an intruder.", "벤허는 권한 자리에 앉지 않고, 정중하게 말했다. \"부디 어지신 시모니데스 어른께서 저를 불청객으로 여기지 않으셨으면 합니다."], ["Coming up the river yesterday, I heard he knew my father.”", "어제 강을 거슬러 올라오면서, 어른께서 제 아버지를 아셨다는 말을 들었습니다.\""]], [["“I knew the Prince Hur.", "\"나는 후르 왕자를 알았소."], ["We were associated in some enterprises lawful to merchants who find profit in lands beyond the sea and the desert. But sit, I pray you—and, Esther, some wine for the young man.", "우리는 바다와 사막 너머의 땅에서 이익을 얻는 상인들에게 허락된 몇몇 사업에서 동업한 사이였소. 그러나 앉으시오, 부탁이오—그리고 에스더야, 이 젊은이에게 포도주를 좀 내드려라."], ["Nehemiah speaks of a son of Hur who once ruled the half part of Jerusalem; an old house; very old, by the faith!", "느헤미야서에 한때 예루살렘의 절반을 다스렸던 후르의 아들 이야기가 나오지. 오래된 가문이오. 참으로, 정말 오래되었소!"], ["In the days of Moses and Joshua even some of them found favor in the sight of the Lord, and divided honors with those princes among men.", "모세와 여호수아의 시대에도 그들 가운데 몇몇은 주님의 눈에 들어, 사람들 가운데 으뜸가는 왕자들과 더불어 영예를 나누었소."], ["It can hardly be that their descendant, lineally come to us, will refuse a cup of wine-fat of the genuine vine of Sorek, grown on the south hill-sides of Hebron.”", "우리에게 곧장 핏줄로 이어 내려온 그 후손이, 헤브론 남쪽 산비탈에서 자란 소렉의 진짜 포도로 빚은 진한 포도주 한 잔을 마다할 리는 없을 것이오.\""]], [["By the time of the conclusion of this speech, Esther was before Ben-Hur with a silver cup filled from a vase upon a table a little removed from the chair.", "이 말이 끝날 무렵, 에스더는 의자에서 조금 떨어진 탁자 위 항아리에서 가득 따른 은잔을 들고 벤허 앞에 와 있었다. 그녀는 고개를 숙인 채 그 잔을 권했다."], ["She offered the drink with downcast face.", "그는 가만히 그녀의 손을 만지며 그것을 물렸다."], ["He touched her hand gently to put it away.", "다시 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다."], ["Again their eyes met; whereat he noticed that she was small, not nearly to his shoulder in height; but very graceful, and fair and sweet of face, with eyes black and inexpressibly soft.", "그제야 그는 그녀가 자그마하여 자기 어깨에도 채 닿지 못할 만큼 작다는 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 그녀는 매우 우아했고, 얼굴은 곱고 사랑스러웠으며, 눈은 검고 이루 말할 수 없이 부드러웠다."], ["She is kind and pretty, he thought, and looks as Tirzah would were she living.", "이 처녀는 마음씨가 곱고 예쁘구나, 그는 생각했다. 티르자가 살아 있다면 꼭 이런 모습이리라. 가엾은 티르자!"], ["Poor Tirzah! Then he said aloud,", "그러고 나서 그는 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["“No, thy father—if he is thy father?”—he paused.", "\"아니오, 당신의 아버지가—이분이 당신의 아버지시라면 말이오만?\"—그는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“I am Esther, the daughter of Simonides,”", "\"저는 에스더, 시모니데스의 딸입니다.\""], ["she said, with dignity.", "그녀가 기품 있게 말했다."]], [["“Then, fair Esther, thy father, when he has heard my further speech, will not think worse of me if yet I am slow to take his wine of famous extract; nor less I hope not to lose grace in thy sight.", "\"그렇다면, 어여쁜 에스더여, 당신의 아버지께서 내 다음 말을 들으신 뒤에는, 내가 그 이름난 포도주를 선뜻 받지 않는다 해도 나를 더 나쁘게 여기지 않으실 것이오. 또한 당신 눈에 비친 내 모습도 흠을 잃지 않기를 바라오."], ["Stand thou here with me a moment!”", "잠시만 나와 함께 여기 서 있어 주오!\""]], [["Both of them, as in common cause, turned to the merchant.", "두 사람은 마치 한뜻이 된 듯, 상인을 향해 돌아섰다."], ["“Simonides!”", "\"시모니데스!\""], ["he said, firmly, “my father, at his death, had a trusted servant of thy name, and it has been told me that thou art the man!”", "그가 단호하게 말했다. \"내 아버지는 돌아가실 때 당신과 같은 이름을 가진, 믿음직한 종을 두셨소. 그리고 나는 당신이 바로 그 사람이라고 들었소!\""]], [["There was a sudden start of the wrenched limbs under the robe, and the thin hand clenched.", "옷 아래 비틀린 팔다리가 별안간 움찔했고, 가는 손이 꽉 움켜쥐어졌다."]], [["“Esther, Esther!”", "\"에스더, 에스더야!\""], ["the man called, sternly; “here, not there, as thou art thy mother’s child and mine—here, not there, I say!”", "그 사람이 엄하게 불렀다. \"여기다, 거기가 아니라. 네가 네 어미와 내 자식이라면—여기다, 거기가 아니라, 내 말 알아듣겠느냐!\""]], [["The girl looked once from father to visitor; then she replaced the cup upon the table, and went dutifully to the chair.", "처녀는 아버지에게서 손님에게로 한 번 눈길을 옮겼다. 그러고는 잔을 탁자에 다시 내려놓고, 순순히 의자 쪽으로 갔다."], ["Her countenance sufficiently expressed her wonder and alarm.", "그녀의 얼굴은 놀라움과 두려움을 고스란히 드러냈다."]], [["Simonides lifted his left hand, and gave it into hers, lying lovingly upon his shoulder, and said, dispassionately, “I have grown old in dealing with men—old before my time.", "시모니데스는 왼손을 들어, 그의 어깨 위에 다정히 얹혀 있던 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 차분하게 말했다. \"나는 사람들을 상대하며 늙어 버렸소—제 나이보다 일찍 늙었소."], ["If he who told thee that whereof thou speakest was a friend acquainted with my history, and spoke of it not harshly, he must have persuaded thee that I could not be else than a man distrustful of my kind.", "만일 당신에게 그 이야기를 들려준 자가 내 내력을 잘 아는 친구였고, 그것을 모질게 말하지 않았다면, 그는 분명 내가 같은 인간을 믿지 못하는 사람일 수밖에 없다고 당신을 설득했을 것이오."], ["The God of Israel help him who, at the end of life, is constrained to acknowledge so much!", "인생의 끝에 이르러 그만큼을 인정하지 않을 수 없는 자에게 이스라엘의 하느님께서 도움을 베푸시기를!"], ["My loves are few, but they are.", "내 사랑은 몇 안 되지만, 분명히 있소."], ["One of them is a soul which”—he carried the hand holding his to his lips, in manner unmistakable—“a soul which to this time has been unselfishly mine, and such sweet comfort that, were it taken from me, I would die.”", "그중 하나는 한 영혼이니\"—그는 자신의 손을 쥔 그 손을 입술로 가져갔으니, 그 뜻은 의심할 여지가 없었다—\"지금까지 사심 없이 내 것이 되어 준 영혼이며, 너무도 달콤한 위안이라, 그것을 내게서 빼앗긴다면 나는 죽고 말 것이오.\""]], [["Esther’s head drooped until her cheek touched his.", "에스더의 고개가 숙여져, 마침내 그 뺨이 아버지의 뺨에 닿았다."]], [["“The other love is but a memory; of which I will say further that, like a benison of the Lord, it hath a compass to contain a whole family, if only”—his voice lowered and trembled—“if only I knew where they were.”", "\"또 다른 사랑은 한낱 기억일 뿐이오. 그에 대해 더 말하자면, 주님의 축복처럼, 그것은 온 가족을 품을 만큼 넓은 둘레를 지녔으니, 다만\"—그의 목소리가 낮아지고 떨렸다—\"다만 내가 그들이 어디 있는지 알기만 한다면 말이오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur’s face suffused, and, advancing a step, he cried, impulsively, “My mother and sister!", "벤허의 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐다. 그는 한 걸음 다가서며 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"내 어머니와 누이!"], ["Oh, it is of them you speak!”", "오, 당신이 말하는 것이 바로 그들이군요!\""]], [["Esther, as if spoken to, raised her head; but Simonides returned to his calm, and answered, coldly, “Hear me to the end.", "에스더는 마치 자신에게 한 말이라도 되는 듯 고개를 들었다. 그러나 시모니데스는 다시 차분함을 되찾아, 차갑게 대답했다."], ["Because I am that I am, and because of the loves of which I have spoken, before I make return to thy demand touching my relations to the Prince Hur, and as something which of right should come first, do thou show me proofs of who thou art.", "\"내 말을 끝까지 들으시오. 나는 나일 수밖에 없고, 또 앞서 말한 그 사랑들 때문에, 후르 왕자와 나의 관계를 묻는 당신의 요구에 답하기 전에, 마땅히 먼저 와야 할 것으로서, 당신이 누구인지 그 증거를 내게 보이시오."], ["Is thy witness in writing?", "당신의 증거는 글로 된 것이오?"], ["Or cometh it in person?”", "아니면 직접 사람이 와서 증언하오?\""]], [["The demand was plain, and the right of it indisputable.", "그 요구는 분명했고, 그 정당함은 따질 여지가 없었다."], ["Ben-Hur blushed, clasped his hands, stammered, and turned away at loss.", "벤허는 얼굴을 붉히고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 말을 더듬으며, 어쩔 줄 몰라 고개를 돌렸다."], ["Simonides pressed him.", "시모니데스는 그를 다그쳤다."]], [["“The proofs, the proofs, I say!", "\"증거, 증거를 보이라 했소!"], ["Set them before me—lay them in my hands!”", "내 앞에 펼쳐 보이시오—내 손에 쥐여 주시오!\""]], [["Yet Ben-Hur had no answer.", "그러나 벤허에게는 답할 것이 없었다."], ["He had not anticipated the requirement; and, now that it was made, to him as never before came the awful fact that the three years in the galley had carried away all the proofs of his identity; mother and sister gone, he did not live in the knowledge of any human being.", "그는 그런 요구를 예상하지 못했다. 그리고 막상 그 요구를 받고 나자, 갤리선에서 보낸 삼 년이 자기 신원을 입증할 모든 증거를 앗아 갔다는 끔찍한 사실이 일찍이 없던 무게로 다가왔다. 어머니와 누이가 사라진 지금, 그는 자신의 존재를 아는 단 한 사람도 없이 살아가고 있었다."], ["Many there were acquainted with him, but that was all.", "그를 아는 자야 많았으나, 그뿐이었다."], ["Had Quintus Arrius been present, what could he have said more than where he found him, and that he believed the pretender to be the son of Hur?", "퀸투스 아리우스가 있었던들, 그를 어디서 발견했으며 그가 후르의 아들이라 믿는다는 것 말고 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있었으랴?"], ["But, as will presently appear in full, the brave Roman sailor was dead.", "그러나 곧 자세히 밝혀지겠지만, 그 용감한 로마 해군 장교는 죽고 없었다."], ["Judah had felt the loneliness before; to the core of life the sense struck him now.", "유다는 전에도 그 외로움을 느낀 적이 있었으나, 이제 그 감각은 목숨의 핵심까지 꿰뚫었다."], ["He stood, hands clasped, face averted, in stupefaction.", "그는 두 손을 맞잡고 얼굴을 돌린 채, 망연자실하여 서 있었다."], ["Simonides respected his suffering, and waited in silence.", "시모니데스는 그의 고통을 존중하여, 말없이 기다렸다."]], [["“Master Simonides,”", "\"시모니데스 어른,\""], ["he said, at length, “I can only tell my story; and I will not that unless you stay judgment so long, and with good-will deign to hear me.”", "그가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"저는 그저 제 이야기를 들려드릴 수 있을 뿐입니다. 그러니 그동안만이라도 판단을 미루시고, 너그러이 제 말을 들어 주시지 않는다면, 저는 그 이야기조차 하지 않으렵니다.\""]], [["“Speak,”", "\"말하시오.\""], ["said Simonides, now, indeed, master of the situation—“speak, and I will listen the more willingly that I have not denied you to be the very person you claim yourself.”", "이제는 정말로 상황을 장악한 시모니데스가 말했다. \"말하시오. 나는 당신이 스스로 주장하는 바로 그 사람임을 부정하지 않았으니, 더욱 기꺼이 들으리다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur proceeded then, and told his life hurriedly, yet with the feeling which is the source of all eloquence; but as we are familiar with it down to his landing at Misenum, in company with Arrius, returned victorious from the Ægean, at that point we will take up the words.", "그리하여 벤허는 이야기를 이어 갔으니, 자신의 삶을 서둘러, 그러나 온갖 웅변의 원천이 되는 그 감정을 담아 들려주었다. 그러나 우리는 그가 에게해에서 승리하고 돌아온 아리우스와 함께 미세눔에 상륙하는 대목까지는 이미 알고 있으므로, 그 지점에서부터 그의 말을 옮기기로 하자."]], [["“My benefactor was loved and trusted by the emperor, who heaped him with honorable rewards.", "\"제 은인은 황제의 사랑과 신임을 받아, 황제께서 명예로운 상을 그에게 듬뿍 내리셨습니다."], ["The merchants of the East contributed magnificent presents, and he became doubly rich among the rich of Rome.", "동방의 상인들은 굉장한 선물을 바쳤고, 그리하여 그는 로마의 부자들 가운데서도 곱절로 부유해졌습니다."], ["May a Jew forget his religion?", "유대인이 제 종교를 잊을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["or his birthplace, if it were the Holy Land of our fathers?", "그것이 우리 조상의 거룩한 땅이라면, 제 출생지를 잊을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["The good man adopted me his son by formal rites of law; and I strove to make him just return: no child was ever more dutiful to father than I to him.", "그 어진 분은 정식 법적 절차를 밟아 저를 양자로 삼으셨고, 저는 그에 합당한 보답을 하려 애썼습니다. 어떤 자식도 제가 그분께 한 것만큼 아버지에게 효성스럽지는 못했을 것입니다."], ["He would have had me a scholar; in art, philosophy, rhetoric, oratory, he would have furnished me the most famous teacher.", "그분은 저를 학자로 만들고자 하셨습니다. 미술, 철학, 수사학, 웅변술에서 가장 이름난 스승을 붙여 주려 하셨습니다."], ["I declined his insistence, because I was a Jew, and could not forget the Lord God, or the glory of the prophets, or the city set on the hills by David and Solomon.", "저는 그분의 권유를 사양했으니, 제가 유대인이라, 주 하느님이나 예언자들의 영광이나, 다윗과 솔로몬이 언덕 위에 세운 도성을 잊을 수 없었기 때문입니다."], ["Oh, ask you why I accepted any of the benefactions of the Roman?", "오, 그러면서 어찌하여 제가 그 로마인의 은혜를 받아들였느냐 물으십니까? 저는 그분을 사랑했습니다."], ["I loved him; next place, I thought with his help, array influences which would enable me one day to unseal the mystery close-locking the fate of my mother and sister; and to these there was yet another motive of which I shall not speak except to say it controlled me so far that I devoted myself to arms, and the acquisition of everything deemed essential to thorough knowledge of the art of war.", "다음으로, 그분의 도움이 있으면, 언젠가 어머니와 누이의 운명을 단단히 봉인한 그 수수께끼를 풀어낼 영향력을 갖추게 되리라 여겼습니다. 그리고 여기에 또 하나의 동기가 있었으나, 그에 대해서는 말하지 않겠습니다. 다만 그것이 저를 어찌나 사로잡았던지, 저는 무예에, 그리고 전쟁술을 철저히 익히는 데 꼭 필요하다 여겨지는 모든 것을 갖추는 데 전념했다는 것만 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["In the palaestrae and circuses of the city I toiled, and in the camps no less; and in all of them I have a name, but not that of my fathers.", "도시의 연무장과 원형 경기장에서 저는 단련했고, 진영에서도 못지않게 단련했습니다. 그 모든 곳에서 저는 이름을 얻었으나, 그것은 제 조상의 이름이 아니었습니다."], ["The crowns I won—and on the walls of the villa by Misenum there are many of them—all came to me as the son of Arrius, the duumvir.", "제가 거머쥔 영예의 관들은—미세눔의 별장 벽에는 그것들이 여럿 걸려 있습니다—모두 두움비르 아리우스의 아들로서 제게 온 것입니다."], ["In that relation only am I known among Romans....", "로마인들 사이에서 저는 오직 그 관계로만 알려져 있습니다……"], ["In steadfast pursuit of my secret aim, I left Rome for Antioch, intending to accompany the Consul Maxentius in the campaign he is organizing against the Parthians.", "제 은밀한 목적을 한결같이 좇으며, 저는 로마를 떠나 안티오크로 왔으니, 막센티우스 집정관이 파르티아를 상대로 준비하는 원정에 동행할 작정이었습니다. 모든 무기를 다루는 개인의 기량에는 통달했으니, 이제 저는 전장에서 군대를 지휘하는 일에 관한 더 높은 지식을 구합니다."], ["Master of personal skill in all arms, I seek now the higher knowledge pertaining to the conduct of bodies of men in the field. The consul has admitted me one of his military family.", "집정관은 저를 그의 군사 막료의 한 사람으로 받아 주셨습니다. 그런데 어제, 우리 배가 오론테스강으로 들어설 때, 다른 두 척의 배가 노란 깃발을 달고 우리와 함께 들어왔습니다."], ["But yesterday, as our ship entered the Orontes, two other ships sailed in with us flying yellow flags.", "키프로스에서 온 같은 배의 동승객이자 동향 사람이 설명하기를, 그 배들이 안티오크의 대상인 시모니데스의 것이라 했습니다."], ["A fellow-passenger and countryman from Cyprus explained that the vessels belonged to Simonides, the master-merchant of Antioch; he told us, also, who the merchant was; his marvellous success in commerce; of his fleets and caravans, and their coming and going; and, not knowing I had interest in the theme beyond my associate listeners, he said Simonides was a Jew, once the servant of the Prince Hur; nor did he conceal the cruelties of Gratus, or the purpose of their infliction.”", "그는 또한 그 상인이 누구인지도 들려주었으니, 그가 거둔 놀라운 상업적 성공, 그의 선단과 대상(隊商), 그리고 그것들이 오가는 모습을 이야기했습니다. 그리고 제가 그 이야기에 함께 듣는 다른 이들보다 더 깊은 관심이 있는 줄도 모른 채, 그는 시모니데스가 유대인이며, 한때 후르 왕자의 종이었다고 말했습니다. 또한 그는 그라투스의 잔혹함도, 그것을 가한 목적도 숨기지 않았습니다.\""]], [["At this allusion Simonides bowed his head, and, as if to help him conceal his feelings and her own deep sympathy, the daughter hid her face on his neck.", "이 말이 나오자 시모니데스는 고개를 숙였고, 마치 그가 제 감정을 감추는 것을 돕기라도 하려는 듯, 또 제 깊은 동정심을 감추려는 듯, 딸은 그의 목에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["Directly he raised his eyes, and said, in a clear voice, “I am listening.”", "이내 그는 눈을 들고, 맑은 목소리로 말했다. \"듣고 있소.\""]], [["“O good Simonides!”", "\"오, 어지신 시모니데스 어른이여!\""], ["Ben-Hur then said, advancing a step, his whole soul seeking expression, “I see thou art not convinced, and that yet I stand in the shadow of thy distrust.”", "그러자 벤허는 한 걸음 다가서며, 온 영혼이 표현을 갈구하는 듯 말했다. \"당신께서 아직 납득하지 못하셨고, 제가 여전히 당신의 불신이라는 그늘 속에 서 있음을 압니다.\""]], [["The merchant held his features fixed as marble, and his tongue as still.", "상인은 대리석처럼 표정을 굳히고, 혀도 그대로 놀리지 않았다."]], [["“And not less clearly, I see the difficulties of my position,”", "\"그리고 제 처지의 어려움 또한 똑똑히 압니다.\""], ["Ben-Hur continued.", "벤허가 말을 이었다."], ["“All my Roman connection I can prove; I have only to call upon the consul, now the guest of the governor of the city; but I cannot prove the particulars of thy demand upon me.", "\"저의 로마 쪽 관계는 모두 증명할 수 있습니다. 지금 도시 총독의 손님으로 와 있는 집정관을 부르기만 하면 됩니다. 그러나 저에 대한 당신의 요구, 그 자세한 부분은 증명할 수가 없습니다."], ["I cannot prove I am my father’s son.", "제가 제 아버지의 아들임을 증명할 수가 없습니다."], ["They who could serve me in that—alas!", "그 일에서 저를 도와줄 수 있던 이들은—아아!"], ["they are dead or lost.”", "그들은 죽었거나 사라졌습니다.\""]], [["He covered his face with his hands; whereupon Esther arose, and, taking the rejected cup to him, said, “The wine is of the country we all so love.", "그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러자 에스더가 일어나, 물렸던 잔을 그에게 가져가 말했다. \"이 포도주는 우리 모두 그토록 사랑하는 고향의 것입니다."], ["Drink, I pray thee!”", "부디 드십시오!\""]], [["The voice was sweet as that of Rebekah offering drink at the well near Nahor the city; he saw there were tears in her eyes, and he drank, saying, “Daughter of Simonides, thy heart is full of goodness; and merciful art thou to let the stranger share it with thy father.", "그 목소리는 나홀 성 가까운 우물에서 마실 것을 권하던 리브가의 목소리만큼이나 달콤했다. 그는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고인 것을 보았고, 그래서 잔을 마시며 말했다. \"시모니데스의 따님이여, 당신의 마음은 선함으로 가득하군요."], ["Be thou blessed of our God!", "그리고 낯선 이가 당신 아버지와 더불어 그 선함을 나누게 해 주시니, 당신은 자비로운 분이십니다. 우리 하느님의 축복이 당신께 깃들기를!"], ["I thank thee.”", "고맙습니다.\""]], [["Then he addressed himself to the merchant again:", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 상인을 향해 말했다."]], [["“As I have no proof that I am my father’s son, I will withdraw that I demanded of thee, O Simonides, and go hence to trouble you no more; only let me say I did not seek thy return to servitude nor account of thy fortune; in any event, I would have said, as now I say, that all which is product of thy labor and genius is thine; keep it in welcome.", "\"제가 제 아버지의 아들이라는 증거가 없으니, 시모니데스 어른이여, 저는 당신께 요구했던 것을 거두고, 더 이상 당신을 괴롭히지 않으려 이곳을 떠나겠습니다. 다만 이 말씀만은 드리겠습니다. 저는 당신을 다시 종으로 되돌리려 한 것도, 당신 재산의 셈을 따지려 한 것도 아닙니다."], ["I have no need of any part thereof.", "어떤 경우라도 저는, 지금 말씀드리는 그대로, 당신의 노고와 천재성이 낳은 모든 것은 당신의 것이라 말했을 것입니다. 부디 마음 놓고 간직하십시오."], ["When the good Quintus, my second father, sailed on the voyage which was his last, he left me his heir, princely rich.", "저는 그 가운데 어느 것도 필요치 않습니다. 어진 퀸투스, 제 둘째 아버지께서 마지막이 된 그 항해에 오르실 때, 그분은 저를 상속자로 삼으셨으니, 저는 왕자처럼 부유합니다. 그러니 혹여 다시 저를 떠올리신다면, 부디 이 물음과 함께 기억해 주십시오."], ["If, therefore, thou dost think of me again, be it with remembrance of this question, which, as I do swear by the prophets and Jehovah, thy God and mine, was the chief purpose of my coming here: What dost thou know—what canst thou tell me—of my mother and Tirzah, my sister—she who should be in beauty and grace even as this one, thy sweetness of life, if not thy very life? Oh!", "예언자들과 당신의 하느님이자 나의 하느님이신 여호와를 두고 맹세하건대, 이것이야말로 제가 이곳에 온 으뜸가는 목적이었습니다. 당신은 무엇을 아십니까—제게 무엇을 들려주실 수 있습니까—제 어머니와, 제 누이 티르자에 대하여 말입니다. 그 애는 살아 있다면, 당신 삶의 달콤함이자, 어쩌면 당신 목숨 그 자체인 이분만큼이나 아름답고 우아할 것입니다. 오!"], ["what canst thou tell me of them?”", "그들에 대해 당신은 제게 무엇을 들려주실 수 있습니까?\""]], [["The tears ran down Esther’s cheeks; but the man was wilful: in a clear voice, he replied,", "에스더의 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그러나 그 사람은 고집스러웠으니, 맑은 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“I have said I knew the Prince Ben-Hur.", "\"나는 벤허 왕자를 알았다고 말했소."], ["I remember hearing of the misfortune which overtook his family.", "그의 가문에 닥친 불행을 들었던 것도 기억하오."], ["I remember the bitterness with which I heard it.", "그것을 듣고 얼마나 비통했는지도 기억하오."], ["He who wrought such misery to the widow of my friend is the same who, in the same spirit, hath since wrought upon me.", "내 벗의 미망인에게 그토록 큰 비참을 안긴 자가, 바로 그 뒤에 같은 마음으로 내게도 손을 댄 자요."], ["I will go further, and say to you, I have made diligent quest concerning the family, but—I have nothing to tell you of them.", "더 말하리다. 나는 그 가족에 관하여 부지런히 수소문했으나—그들에 대해 당신에게 들려줄 것이 아무것도 없소."], ["They are lost.”", "그들은 사라졌소.\""]], [["Ben-Hur uttered a great groan.", "벤허는 깊은 신음을 토했다."]], [["“Then—then it is another hope broken!” he said, struggling with his feelings.", "\"그렇다면—그렇다면 또 하나의 희망이 꺾인 것이군요!\" 그가 감정과 싸우며 말했다."], ["“I am used to disappointments.", "\"저는 실망에는 익숙합니다."], ["I pray you pardon my intrusion; and if I have occasioned you annoyance, forgive it because of my sorrow.", "제가 끼어든 것을 부디 용서하십시오. 그리고 제가 당신께 폐를 끼쳤다면, 제 슬픔을 보아 용서해 주십시오."], ["I have nothing now to live for but vengeance. Farewell.”", "이제 제게는 복수 말고는 살아갈 이유가 없습니다. 안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["At the curtain he turned, and said, simply, “I thank you both.”", "휘장 앞에서 그는 돌아서서, 그저 이렇게 말했다. \"두 분께 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“Peace go with you,” the merchant said.", "\"평화가 당신과 함께하기를.\" 상인이 말했다."]], [["Esther could not speak for sobbing.", "에스더는 흐느끼느라 말을 할 수 없었다."]], [["And so he departed.", "그리하여 그는 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["Scarcely was Ben-Hur gone, when Simonides seemed to wake as from sleep: his countenance flushed; the sullen light of his eyes changed to brightness; and he said, cheerily,", "벤허가 떠나기 무섭게, 시모니데스는 잠에서 깨어나기라도 한 듯했다. 그의 얼굴에는 붉은 기운이 돌았고, 침울하던 눈빛은 환하게 바뀌었다. 그는 활기차게 말했다."]], [["“Esther, ring—quick!”", "\"에스더, 종을 쳐라—어서!\""]], [["She went to the table, and rang a service-bell.", "그녀는 탁자로 가서 호출용 종을 울렸다."]], [["One of the panels in the wall swung back, exposing a doorway which gave admittance to a man who passed round to the merchant’s front, and saluted him with a half-salaam.", "벽의 널빤지 하나가 뒤로 젖혀지며 출입구가 드러났고, 거기로 한 사내가 들어와 상인의 앞으로 돌아와서는 반쯤 절하는 인사로 그를 맞았다."]], [["“Malluch, here—nearer—to the chair,”", "\"말루크, 이리로—더 가까이—의자 곁으로.\" 주인이 명령하듯 말했다."], ["the master said, imperiously.", "\"해가 진다 해도 실패해서는 안 될 임무가 있다."], ["“I have a mission which shall not fail though the sun should. Hearken!", "잘 들어라! 한 젊은이가 지금 창고로 내려가고 있다—키가 크고, 잘생겼으며, 이스라엘 사람의 차림을 했다."], ["A young man is now descending to the store-room—tall, comely, and in the garb of Israel; follow him, his shadow not more faithful; and every night send me report of where he is, what he does, and the company he keeps; and if, without discovery, you overhear his conversations, report them word for word, together with whatever will serve to expose him, his habits, motives, life.", "그를 뒤따르되, 그림자도 그보다 더 충실하지 못할 만큼 따라붙어라. 그리고 밤마다 그가 어디 있는지, 무엇을 하는지, 누구와 어울리는지 내게 보고하라. 만일 들키지 않고 그의 대화를 엿들을 수 있다면, 한 마디도 빠짐없이 그대로 전하고, 그를 드러내 줄 만한 것—그의 습관, 동기, 생활—은 무엇이든 함께 알려라."], ["Understand you?", "알아들었느냐?"], ["Go quickly!", "어서 가라!"], ["Stay, Malluch: if he leave the city, go after him—and, mark you, Malluch, be as a friend.", "잠깐, 말루크. 그가 도시를 떠나거든, 그를 따라가라—그리고 명심해라, 말루크, 벗처럼 굴어라."], ["If he bespeak you, tell him what you will to the occasion most suited, except that you are in my service, of that, not a word.", "그가 네게 말을 걸거든, 그때그때 가장 알맞은 대로 무엇이든 말하되, 다만 네가 내 밑에서 일한다는 것만은—그것만은 한 마디도 입에 담지 마라."], ["Haste—make haste!”", "서둘러라—어서 서둘러!\""]], [["The man saluted as before, and was gone.", "사내는 아까처럼 절하고는 사라졌다."]], [["Then Simonides rubbed his wan hands together, and laughed.", "그러자 시모니데스는 핏기 없는 두 손을 비비며 웃었다."]], [["“What is the day, daughter?”", "\"오늘이 며칠이냐, 딸아?\""], ["he said, in the midst of the mood.", "그는 그 들뜬 기분 속에서 물었다."], ["“What is the day?", "\"오늘이 며칠이지?"], ["I wish to remember it for happiness come.", "찾아온 행복을 잊지 않으려고 그러는 게다."], ["See, and look for it laughing, and laughing tell me, Esther.”", "자, 웃으며 헤아려 보고, 웃으며 내게 말해 다오, 에스더.\""]], [["The merriment seemed unnatural to her; and, as if to entreat him from it, she answered, sorrowfully, “Woe’s me, father, that I should ever forget this day!”", "그 즐거워하는 모습이 그녀에게는 부자연스러워 보였다. 마치 그를 그 기분에서 끌어내려는 듯, 그녀는 슬프게 대답했다. \"아, 슬프옵니다, 아버지. 제가 어찌 이날을 잊을 수 있겠어요!\""]], [["His hands fell down the instant, and his chin, dropping upon his breast, lost itself in the muffling folds of flesh composing his lower face.", "그 순간 그의 두 손이 떨어졌고, 턱은 가슴 위로 내려앉아 그의 아래 얼굴을 이룬 두툼한 살주름 속에 파묻혔다."]], [["“True, most true, my daughter!”", "\"옳다, 참으로 옳은 말이다, 내 딸아!\""], ["he said, without looking up. “This is the twentieth day of the fourth month.", "그는 고개를 들지 않은 채 말했다. \"오늘은 넷째 달 스무날이다."], ["To-day, five years ago, my Rachel, thy mother, fell down and died.", "오늘로 다섯 해 전, 네 어미 라헬이 쓰러져 숨을 거두었다."], ["They brought me home broken as thou seest me, and we found her dead of grief. Oh, to me she was a cluster of camphire in the vineyards of En-Gedi!", "사람들이 지금 네가 보는 이 부서진 몸으로 나를 집에 데려왔을 때, 우리는 그녀가 비탄으로 죽은 것을 알았다. 오, 내게 그녀는 엔게디 포도원의 고벨화 송이였다!"], ["I have gathered my myrrh with my spice. I have eaten my honeycomb with my honey.", "나는 향료와 함께 몰약을 거두었고, 꿀과 함께 송이꿀을 먹었다."], ["We laid her away in a lonely place—in a tomb cut in the mountain; no one near her.", "우리는 그녀를 외딴 곳에 뉘었다—산에 깎아 만든 무덤에, 곁에 아무도 없는 곳에."], ["Yet in the darkness she left me a little light, which the years have increased to a brightness of morning.”", "그러나 어둠 속에서 그녀는 내게 작은 빛 하나를 남겼고, 그 빛은 세월이 흐르며 아침 햇살처럼 밝아졌다.\""], ["He raised his hand and rested it upon his daughter’s head.", "그는 손을 들어 딸의 머리 위에 얹었다."], ["“Dear Lord, I thank thee that now in my Esther my lost Rachel liveth again!”", "\"주여, 이제 제 에스더 안에서 잃었던 라헬이 다시 살아 있으니, 감사하나이다!\""]], [["Directly he lifted his head, and said, as with a sudden thought, “Is it not clear day outside?”", "그는 곧 고개를 들고, 문득 떠오른 생각인 듯 말했다. \"바깥은 환한 대낮이 아니냐?\""]], [["“It was, when the young man came in.”", "\"그랬어요, 그 젊은이가 들어왔을 때는요.\""]], [["“Then let Abimelech come and take me to the garden, where I can see the river and the ships, and I will tell thee, dear Esther, why but now my mouth filled with laughter, and my tongue with singing, and my spirit was like to a roe or to a young hart upon the mountains of spices.”", "\"그러면 아비멜렉을 불러 나를 정원으로 데려가게 하여라. 거기서 강과 배들을 볼 수 있으니. 그리고 들려주마, 사랑하는 에스더야, 어찌하여 방금 내 입이 웃음으로 가득 차고, 내 혀가 노래로 차올랐으며, 내 영혼이 노루처럼, 향기로운 산 위의 어린 사슴처럼 뛰놀았는지를.\""]], [["In answer to the bell a servant came, and at her bidding pushed the chair, set on little wheels for the purpose, out of the room to the roof of the lower house, called by him his garden.", "종소리에 응답하여 하인 하나가 왔다. 그녀의 분부에 따라 그는 그 일을 위해 작은 바퀴를 단 의자를 밀어, 방을 나와 아래층 집의 지붕으로 옮겼다. 그곳을 그는 자신의 정원이라 불렀다."], ["Out through the roses, and by beds of lesser flowers, all triumphs of careful attendance, but now unnoticed, he was rolled to a position from which he could view the palace-tops over against him on the island, the bridge in lessening perspective to the farther shore, and the river below the bridge crowded with vessels, all swimming amidst the dancing splendors of the early sun upon the rippling water.", "장미꽃 사이로, 그리고 정성스러운 보살핌의 결실인—그러나 지금은 눈길도 받지 못하는—작은 꽃밭들을 지나, 그는 한자리로 밀려갔다. 그곳에서는 맞은편 섬 위에 늘어선 궁전 지붕들과, 점점 작아지는 원근으로 건너편 기슭까지 뻗은 다리와, 다리 아래 배들로 빼곡한 강이 한눈에 들어왔다. 그 배들은 모두 잔물결 위로 이른 햇살이 춤추는 눈부신 빛 가운데 떠 있었다."], ["There the servant left him with Esther.", "하인은 그를 에스더와 함께 그곳에 남겨 두고 떠났다."]], [["The much shouting of laborers, and their beating and pounding, did not disturb him any more than the tramping of people on the bridge floor almost overhead, being as familiar to his ear as the view before him to his eye, and therefore unnoticeable, except as suggestions of profits in promise.", "일꾼들의 요란한 고함과 두드리고 찧는 소리도, 거의 머리 위라 할 다리 바닥을 오가는 사람들의 발소리만큼이나 그를 어지럽히지 못했다. 그 소리는 눈앞의 풍경이 그의 눈에 익숙한 만큼이나 그의 귀에 익숙했으니, 약속된 이익을 일러 주는 신호로서가 아니고서는 알아차려지지도 않았다."]], [["Esther sat on the arm of the chair nursing his hand, and waiting his speech, which came at length in the calm way, the mighty will having carried him back to himself.", "에스더는 의자 팔걸이에 걸터앉아 그의 손을 어루만지며, 그가 입을 열기를 기다렸다. 마침내 그 말은 차분하게 흘러나왔으니, 강인한 의지가 그를 다시 제 모습으로 돌려놓았던 것이다."]], [["“When the young man was speaking, Esther, I observed thee, and thought thou wert won by him.”", "\"그 젊은이가 말하고 있을 때, 에스더, 내가 너를 지켜보았다. 그리고 네가 그에게 마음이 끌렸다고 생각했다.\""]], [["Her eyes fell as she replied,", "그녀는 눈을 내리뜨며 대답했다."]], [["“Speak you of faith, father, I believed him.”", "\"믿음을 두고 말씀하시는 거라면, 아버지, 저는 그를 믿었어요.\""]], [["“In thy eyes, then, he is the lost son of the Prince Hur?”", "\"그렇다면 네 눈에 그는 후르 공의 잃어버린 아들이더냐?\""]], [["“If he is not—”", "\"만일 그가 아니라면—\""], ["She hesitated.", "그녀는 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“And if he is not, Esther?”", "\"그가 아니라면 어떻다는 게냐, 에스더?\""]], [["“I have been thy handmaiden, father, since my mother answered the call of the Lord God; by thy side I have heard and seen thee deal in wise ways with all manner of men seeking profit, holy and unholy; and now I say, if indeed the young man be not the prince he claims to be, then before me falsehood never played so well the part of righteous truth.”", "\"제 어머니가 주 하느님의 부르심에 응하신 뒤로, 아버지, 저는 아버지의 시녀였습니다. 아버지 곁에서 저는, 거룩하든 거룩하지 않든 이익을 좇는 온갖 사람들을 아버지가 지혜롭게 다루시는 것을 듣고 보았어요. 이제 저는 말씀드립니다. 만일 그 젊은이가 정녕 자기가 말하는 그 공자가 아니라면, 일찍이 제 앞에서 거짓이 그토록 의로운 진실의 역할을 잘 해낸 적은 없었습니다.\""]], [["“By the glory of Solomon, daughter, thou speakest earnestly.", "\"솔로몬의 영광에 두고, 딸아, 너는 진지하게 말하는구나."], ["Dost thou believe thy father his father’s servant?”", "네 아버지가 그의 아버지의 종이었다고 믿느냐?\""]], [["“I understood him to ask of that as something he had but heard.”", "\"그저 들은 이야기로서 그것을 물으시는 것이라고 저는 이해했어요.\""]], [["For a time Simonides’ gaze swam among his swimming ships, though they had no place in his mind.", "한동안 시모니데스의 눈길은 떠 있는 배들 사이를 헤맸으나, 그 배들은 그의 마음속에 자리하지 못했다."]], [["“Well, thou art a good child, Esther, of genuine Jewish shrewdness, and of years and strength to hear a sorrowful tale.", "\"그래, 너는 착한 아이다, 에스더. 진정한 유대인의 영민함을 지녔고, 슬픈 이야기를 들을 만한 나이와 굳셈을 갖추었다."], ["Wherefore give me heed, and I will tell you of myself, and of thy mother, and of many things pertaining to the past not in thy knowledge or thy dreams—things withheld from the persecuting Romans for a hope’s sake, and from thee that thy nature should grow towards the Lord straight as the reed to the sun....", "그러니 내 말에 귀를 기울이거라. 나 자신에 대해, 네 어머니에 대해, 그리고 네가 알지도 못하고 꿈꾸지도 못한 지난날의 많은 일들에 대해 일러 주마—희망을 위하여 박해하는 로마인에게 숨겨 두었고, 또한 네게는 숨겼으니, 네 천성이 갈대가 해를 향해 곧게 자라듯 주를 향해 곧게 자라기를 바랐기 때문이다……"], ["I was born in a tomb in the valley of Hinnom, on the south side of Zion.", "나는 시온 남쪽, 힌놈 골짜기의 한 무덤에서 태어났다."], ["My father and mother were Hebrew bond-servants, tenders of the fig and olive trees growing, with many vines, in the King’s Garden hard by Siloam; and in my boyhood I helped them.", "내 아버지와 어머니는 히브리인 노예로, 실로암 가까이 왕의 정원에서 자라는 무화과나무와 올리브나무, 그리고 수많은 포도나무를 돌보는 사람들이었다. 어린 시절 나는 그들을 도왔다."], ["They were of the class bound to serve forever.", "그들은 영원히 종으로 매인 부류였다."], ["They sold me to the Prince Hur, then, next to Herod the King, the richest man in Jerusalem.", "그들은 나를 후르 공에게 팔았는데, 그분은 그때 헤롯 왕 다음으로 예루살렘에서 가장 부유한 사람이었다."], ["From the garden he transferred me to his storehouse in Alexandria of Egypt, where I came of age.", "그 정원에서 그분은 나를 이집트 알렉산드리아에 있는 자신의 창고로 옮겼고, 거기서 나는 성년이 되었다."], ["I served him six years, and in the seventh, by the law of Moses, I went free.”", "나는 그분을 여섯 해 동안 섬겼고, 일곱째 해에는 모세의 율법에 따라 자유의 몸이 되었다.\""]], [["Esther clapped her hands lightly.", "에스더는 가볍게 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["“Oh, then, thou art not his father’s servant!”", "\"오, 그러면 아버지는 그분 아버지의 종이 아니시군요!\""]], [["“Nay, daughter, hear.", "\"아니다, 딸아, 들어 보아라."], ["Now, in those days there were lawyers in the cloisters of the Temple who disputed vehemently, saying the children of servants bound forever took the condition of their parents; but the Prince Hur was a man righteous in all things, and an interpreter of the law after the straitest sect, though not of them.", "그 시절 성전 회랑에는 격렬히 논쟁하는 율법학자들이 있었다. 그들은 영원히 매인 종의 자식은 그 부모의 신세를 그대로 물려받는다고 말했다. 그러나 후르 공은 모든 일에 의로운 분이었고, 비록 그 무리에 속하지는 않았으나 가장 엄격한 학파를 따르는 율법 해석자였다."], ["He said I was a Hebrew servant bought, in the true meaning of the great lawgiver, and, by sealed writings, which I yet have, he set me free.”", "그분은 내가 위대한 율법 제정자의 참된 뜻에 따라 사들인 히브리인 종이라 말씀하시고는, 내가 아직도 간직하고 있는 봉인된 문서로써 나를 자유롭게 해 주셨다.\""]], [["“And my mother?”", "\"그러면 제 어머니는요?\""], ["Esther asked.", "에스더가 물었다."]], [["“Thou shalt hear all, Esther; be patient.", "\"다 듣게 될 게다, 에스더. 참고 기다려라."], ["Before I am through thou shalt see it were easier for me to forget myself than thy mother....", "이야기를 마치기 전에, 내가 네 어머니를 잊느니 차라리 나 자신을 잊는 편이 더 쉬웠으리라는 걸 너도 알게 될 게다……"], ["At the end of my service, I came up to Jerusalem to the Passover. My master entertained me.", "종살이를 마친 뒤, 나는 유월절을 맞아 예루살렘으로 올라갔다. 주인께서는 나를 손님으로 맞아 주셨다."], ["I was in love with him already, and I prayed to be continued in his service.", "나는 이미 그분을 사랑하고 있었기에, 계속 그분을 섬기게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He consented, and I served him yet another seven years, but as a hired son of Israel.", "그분이 허락하여, 나는 또 일곱 해를 더 그분을 섬겼으나, 이번에는 품삯을 받는 이스라엘의 아들로서였다."], ["In his behalf I had charge of ventures on the sea by ships, and of ventures on land by caravans eastward to Susa and Persepolis, and the lands of silk beyond them.", "그분을 위하여 나는 배로 바다를 건너는 사업과, 대상을 거느리고 육로로 동쪽 수사와 페르세폴리스, 그 너머 비단의 땅까지 가는 사업을 맡았다."], ["Perilous passages were they, my daughter; but the Lord blessed all I undertook.", "위험천만한 길이었다, 내 딸아. 그러나 주께서는 내가 손댄 모든 일에 복을 주셨다."], ["I brought home vast gains for the prince, and richer knowledge for myself, without which I could not have mastered the charges since fallen to me....", "나는 공에게 막대한 이익을 안겨 드렸고, 나 자신에게는 더 값진 지식을 가져왔으니, 그것이 없었다면 그 뒤로 내게 맡겨진 일들을 감당하지 못했을 게다……"], ["One day I was a guest in his house in Jerusalem.", "어느 날 나는 예루살렘에 있는 그분의 집에서 손님으로 머물고 있었다."], ["A servant entered with some sliced bread on a platter.", "한 하녀가 저며 놓은 빵을 쟁반에 담아 들고 들어왔다."], ["She came to me first.", "그녀가 먼저 내게로 왔다."], ["It was then I saw thy mother, and loved her, and took her away in my secret heart.", "그때 나는 네 어머니를 보았고, 그녀를 사랑하게 되었으며, 마음 깊은 곳에 그녀를 품었다."], ["After a while a time came when I sought the prince to make her my wife.", "얼마 뒤 한 때가 와서, 나는 공을 찾아가 그녀를 아내로 삼게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He told me she was bond-servant forever; but if she wished, he would set her free that I might be gratified.", "그분은 그녀가 영원히 매인 종이라 하시면서도, 그녀가 원한다면 나를 흡족하게 하고자 자유롭게 해 주겠다고 하셨다."], ["She gave me love for love, but was happy where she was, and refused her freedom.", "그녀는 사랑에 사랑으로 응했으나, 자기가 있는 곳에서 행복하다며 자유를 마다했다."], ["I prayed and besought, going again and again after long intervals.", "나는 빌고 또 간청하며, 긴 세월을 두고 거듭 그녀를 찾아갔다."], ["She would be my wife, she all the time said, if I would become her fellow in servitude.", "내가 종살이의 동무가 되어 준다면 아내가 되겠노라고, 그녀는 한결같이 말했다."], ["Our father Jacob served yet other seven years for his Rachel.", "우리 조상 야곱도 라헬을 얻으려고 또 일곱 해를 섬겼다."], ["Could I not as much for mine?", "내 라헬을 위해서라고 그만큼 못할쏘냐?"], ["But thy mother said I must become as she, to serve forever.", "그러나 네 어머니는 내가 그녀처럼 영원히 종으로 섬기는 몸이 되어야 한다고 말했다."], ["I came away, but went back.", "나는 그곳을 떠났지만, 다시 돌아갔다."], ["Look, Esther, look here.”", "보아라, 에스더, 여기를 보아라.\""]], [["He pulled out the lobe of his left ear.", "그는 자신의 왼쪽 귓불을 잡아당겼다."]], [["“See you not the scar of the awl?”", "\"송곳 자국이 보이지 않느냐?\""]], [["“I see it,”", "\"보여요.\""], ["she said; “and, oh, I see how thou didst love my mother!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 오, 아버지가 제 어머니를 얼마나 사랑하셨는지 알겠어요!\""]], [["“Love her, Esther!", "\"사랑했고말고, 에스더!"], ["She was to me more than the Shulamite to the singing king, fairer, more spotless; a fountain of gardens, a well of living waters, and streams from Lebanon.", "그녀는 내게 노래하는 임금에게 술람미 여인이 그러했던 것보다 더한 존재였다. 더 아름답고, 더 흠 없는, 동산의 샘이요, 생수의 우물이며, 레바논에서 흘러내리는 시내였다."], ["The master, even as I required him, took me to the judges, and back to his door, and thrust the awl through my ear into the door, and I was his servant forever. So I won my Rachel.", "주인께서는 내가 청한 대로 나를 재판관들에게 데려갔다가 다시 그분의 문으로 데려와, 송곳으로 내 귀를 꿰뚫어 문에 박으셨고, 그리하여 나는 영원히 그분의 종이 되었다. 그렇게 나는 내 라헬을 얻었다."], ["And was ever love like mine?”", "일찍이 내 사랑과 같은 사랑이 또 있었더냐?\""]], [["Esther stooped and kissed him, and they were silent, thinking of the dead.", "에스더는 몸을 숙여 그에게 입을 맞추었고, 두 사람은 죽은 이를 생각하며 말없이 있었다."]], [["“My master was drowned at sea, the first sorrow that ever fell upon me,”", "\"주인께서는 바다에서 익사하셨다. 내게 닥친 첫 번째 슬픔이었다.\""], ["the merchant continued.", "상인은 말을 이었다."], ["“There was mourning in his house, and in mine here in Antioch, my abiding-place at the time.", "\"그분의 집에도, 그리고 그 무렵 내가 머물던 이곳 안티오크 내 집에도 애도가 가득했다."], ["Now, Esther, mark you!", "자, 에스더, 잘 들어라!"], ["When the good prince was lost, I had risen to be his chief steward, with everything of property belonging to him in my management and control.", "그 어진 공이 세상을 떠나셨을 때, 나는 그분의 수석 집사 자리에 올라, 그분께 속한 모든 재산이 내 관리와 통제 아래 있었다."], ["Judge you how much he loved and trusted me!", "그분이 나를 얼마나 사랑하고 믿으셨는지 헤아려 보아라!"], ["I hastened to Jerusalem to render account to the widow.", "나는 미망인께 회계를 보고하려고 서둘러 예루살렘으로 갔다."], ["She continued me in the stewardship.", "그분은 나를 그대로 집사 자리에 두셨다."], ["I applied myself with greater diligence.", "나는 더욱 부지런히 일에 매달렸다."], ["The business prospered, and grew year by year.", "사업은 번창했고, 해마다 자라났다. 열 해가 흘렀다."], ["Ten years passed; then came the blow which you heard the young man tell about—the accident, as he called it, to the Procurator Gratus.", "그러던 어느 날, 네가 그 젊은이의 입으로 들은 바로 그 일격이 닥쳤다—그가 사고라고 부른, 총독 그라투스에게 일어난 그 일이었다."], ["The Roman gave it out an attempt to assassinate him.", "그 로마인은 그것을 자기를 암살하려던 기도라고 떠벌렸다."], ["Under that pretext, by leave from Rome, he confiscated to his own use the immense fortune of the widow and children.", "그 구실로, 로마의 허락을 받아, 그는 미망인과 그 자녀들의 막대한 재산을 몰수하여 제 것으로 삼았다."], ["Nor stopped he there.", "거기서 멈추지도 않았다."], ["That there might be no reversal of the judgment, he removed all the parties interested.", "판결이 뒤집히는 일이 없도록, 그는 이해 당사자를 모조리 없애 버렸다."], ["From that dreadful day to this the family of Hur have been lost.", "그 끔찍한 날부터 오늘까지 후르 가문은 자취를 감추었다."], ["The son, whom I had seen as a child, was sentenced to the galleys.", "어릴 적 내가 보았던 그 아들은 갤리선 노예로 선고받았다."], ["The widow and daughter are supposed to have been buried in some of the many dungeons of Judea, which, once closed upon the doomed, are like sepulchers sealed and locked.", "미망인과 딸은 유대의 수많은 지하 감옥 어딘가에 갇혔으리라 짐작될 뿐이다. 그곳은 한번 죄인 위로 닫히면 봉인되고 잠긴 무덤과 다를 바 없다."], ["They passed from the knowledge of men as utterly as if the sea had swallowed them unseen.", "그들은, 바다가 아무도 모르게 삼켜 버리기라도 한 듯, 사람들의 기억에서 완전히 사라졌다."], ["We could not hear how they died—nay, not even that they were dead.”", "우리는 그들이 어떻게 죽었는지 들을 수 없었다—아니, 죽었다는 것조차 알 수 없었다.\""]], [["Esther’s eyes were dewy with tears.", "에스더의 눈에는 이슬 같은 눈물이 맺혔다."]], [["“Thy heart is good, Esther, good as thy mother’s was; and I pray it have not the fate of most good hearts—to be trampled upon by the unmerciful and blind.", "\"네 마음은 착하다, 에스더. 네 어머니의 마음이 그러했듯 착하다. 다만 그 마음이 대개의 착한 마음이 맞는 운명—무자비하고 눈먼 자들에게 짓밟히는 운명—을 겪지 않기를 나는 기도한다."], ["But hearken further.", "그러나 더 들어라."], ["I went up to Jerusalem to give help to my benefactress, and was seized at the gate of the city and carried to the sunken cells of the Tower of Antonia; why, I knew not, until Gratus himself came and demanded of me the moneys of the House of Hur, which he knew, after our Jewish custom of exchange, were subject to my draft in the different marts of the world.", "나는 은인을 도우려고 예루살렘으로 올라갔다가, 성문에서 붙잡혀 안토니아 요새의 지하 감방으로 끌려갔다. 까닭은 몰랐으나, 마침내 그라투스가 직접 와서 후르 가문의 돈을 내게 요구했다. 우리 유대인의 환전 관습에 따라 그 돈이 세계 곳곳의 시장에서 내 어음으로 인출될 수 있음을 그는 알고 있었던 것이다."], ["He required me to sign to his order.", "그는 자기 명령서에 서명하라고 요구했다."], ["I refused. He had the houses, lands, goods, ships, and movable property of those I served; he had not their moneys.", "나는 거절했다. 그는 내가 섬기던 이들의 집과 땅과 재물과 배와 동산은 손에 넣었으나, 그들의 돈만은 손에 넣지 못했다."], ["I saw, if I kept favor in the sight of the Lord, I could rebuild their broken fortunes.", "나는 보았다. 내가 주 보시기에 은총을 잃지 않는다면, 무너진 그들의 재산을 다시 일으킬 수 있으리라는 것을."], ["I refused the tyrant’s demands.", "나는 그 폭군의 요구를 거절했다."], ["He put me to torture; my will held good, and he set me free, nothing gained.", "그는 나를 고문했다. 그러나 내 의지는 굳건했고, 그는 아무것도 얻지 못한 채 나를 풀어 주었다."], ["I came home and began again, in the name of Simonides of Antioch, instead of the Prince Hur of Jerusalem.", "나는 집으로 돌아와, 예루살렘의 후르 공이 아니라 안티오크의 시모니데스라는 이름으로 다시 시작했다. 너도 알다시피, 에스더, 나는 번창했다."], ["Thou knowest, Esther, how I have prospered; that the increase of the millions of the prince in my hands was miraculous; thou knowest how, at the end of three years, while going up to Cæsarea, I was taken and a second time tortured by Gratus to compel a confession that my goods and moneys were subject to his order of confiscation; thou knowest he failed as before.", "내 손안에서 공의 수백만 재산이 불어난 것은 기적과도 같았다. 너도 알다시피, 세 해가 지나 카이사레아로 올라가던 길에 나는 또다시 붙잡혀, 내 재물과 돈이 그의 몰수 명령에 종속된다는 자백을 받아 내려는 그라투스에게 두 번째로 고문당했다. 너도 알다시피, 그는 전과 마찬가지로 실패했다."], ["Broken in body, I came home and found my Rachel dead of fear and grief for me.", "몸이 부서진 채 집으로 돌아와 보니, 내 라헬이 나에 대한 두려움과 비탄으로 죽어 있었다."], ["The Lord our God reigned, and I lived. From the emperor himself I bought immunity and license to trade throughout the world.", "우리 주 하느님께서 다스리셨고, 나는 살아남았다. 나는 황제에게서 직접 면책권과 온 세계에서 교역할 허가를 사들였다."], ["To-day—praised be He who maketh the clouds his chariot and walketh upon the winds!—to-day, Esther, that which was in my hands for stewardship is multiplied into talents sufficient to enrich a Cæsar.”", "오늘날—구름을 수레로 삼고 바람 위를 거니시는 그분을 찬양할지어다!—오늘날, 에스더, 집사로서 내 손에 맡겨졌던 그것이 불어나, 카이사르를 부유하게 만들고도 남을 만한 달란트가 되었다.\""]], [["He lifted his head proudly; their eyes met; each read the other’s thought.", "그는 자랑스레 고개를 들었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 서로의 생각을 읽었다."], ["“What shall I with the treasure, Esther?”", "\"이 재물을 어찌하면 좋겠느냐, 에스더?\""], ["he asked, without lowering his gaze.", "그는 시선을 거두지 않은 채 물었다."]], [["“My father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["she answered, in a low voice, “did not the rightful owner call for it but now?”", "그녀는 나직한 목소리로 대답했다. \"정당한 주인이 방금 그것을 찾으러 오지 않았나요?\""]], [["Still his look did not fail.", "그래도 그의 눈길은 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["“And thou, my child; shall I leave thee a beggar?”", "\"그러면 너는, 내 아이야, 내가 너를 거지로 남겨 두어야 하겠느냐?\""]], [["“Nay, father, am not I, because I am thy child, his bond-servant?", "\"아니에요, 아버지. 제가 아버지의 자식이기에, 저 또한 그분의 매인 종이 아닌가요?"], ["And of whom was it written, ‘Strength and honor are her clothing, and she shall rejoice in time to come?’”", "그리고 누구에 대하여 이렇게 기록되었나요. '힘과 위엄이 그의 옷이며, 그가 장차 올 날에 기뻐하리라'고요?\""]], [["A gleam of ineffable love lighted his face as he said, “The Lord hath been good to me in many ways; but thou, Esther, art the sovereign excellence of his favor.”", "이루 말할 수 없는 사랑의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 밝혔다. 그가 말했다. \"주께서는 여러 가지로 내게 선하셨다. 그러나 너, 에스더, 너는 그 은총 가운데 으뜸가는 보배다.\""]], [["He drew her to his breast and kissed her many times.", "그는 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안고 여러 번 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Hear now,”", "\"이제 들어라.\""], ["he said, with clearer voice—“hear now why I laughed this morning.", "그는 한결 또렷해진 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 오늘 아침 어찌하여 웃었는지를 이제 들어라."], ["The young man faced me the apparition of his father in comely youth.", "그 젊은이는 잘생긴 젊은 시절의 제 아버지 모습 그대로 내 앞에 나타났다."], ["My spirit arose to salute him.", "내 영혼은 그를 맞으려고 일어섰다."], ["I felt my trial-days were over and my labors ended.", "나는 내 시련의 날들이 끝났고 내 노고도 다했음을 느꼈다."], ["Hardly could I keep from crying out.", "소리쳐 부르짖지 않기가 어려울 지경이었다."], ["I longed to take him by the hand and show the balance I had earned, and say, ‘Lo, ’tis all thine!", "나는 그의 손을 잡고, 내가 벌어 놓은 잔고를 보이며 이렇게 말하고 싶었다. '보십시오, 이 모든 것이 당신 것입니다!"], ["and I am thy servant, ready now to be called away.’ And so I would have done, Esther, so I would have done, but that moment three thoughts rushed to restrain me.", "그리고 저는 당신의 종이니, 이제 언제든 부르심을 받을 채비가 되어 있습니다.' 그렇게 했을 게다, 에스더, 정말 그렇게 했을 게다. 그러나 그 순간 세 가지 생각이 밀려와 나를 붙들었다."], ["I will be sure he is my master’s son—such was the first thought; if he is my master’s son, I will learn somewhat of his nature.", "나는 그가 내 주인의 아들임을 확실히 해야겠다—이것이 첫 번째 생각이었다. 그가 내 주인의 아들이라면, 나는 그의 됨됨이를 어느 정도 알아 두어야겠다."], ["Of those born to riches, bethink you, Esther, how many there are in whose hands riches are but breeding curses”—he paused, while his hands clutched, and his voice shrilled with passion—“Esther, consider the pains I endured at the Roman’s hands; nay, not Gratus’s alone: the merciless wretches who did his bidding the first time and the last were Romans, and they all alike laughed to hear me scream.", "부유하게 태어난 자들 가운데, 헤아려 보아라, 에스더, 그 손안에서 재물이 한낱 저주를 길러 내는 자가 얼마나 많은지를\"—그는 말을 멈추었고, 그 두 손은 움켜쥐어졌으며, 그 목소리는 격정으로 날카로워졌다—\"에스더, 내가 로마인의 손에 당한 고통을 생각해 보아라. 아니, 그라투스 혼자가 아니다. 처음에도 마지막에도 그의 명령을 수행한 그 무자비한 악당들도 로마인이었고, 그들은 하나같이 내 비명을 듣고 웃었다."], ["Consider my broken body, and the years I have gone shorn of my stature; consider thy mother yonder in her lonely tomb, crushed of soul as I of body; consider the sorrows of my master’s family if they are living, and the cruelty of their taking-off if they are dead; consider all, and, with Heaven’s love about thee, tell me, daughter, shall not a hair fall or a red drop run in expiation?", "부서진 내 몸과, 키마저 줄어든 채 보낸 세월을 생각해 보아라. 외딴 무덤에 누운 네 어머니가, 내가 몸이 으스러진 만큼 영혼이 으스러졌음을 생각해 보아라. 내 주인의 가족이 살아 있다면 그들의 슬픔을, 죽었다면 그들이 죽임당한 잔혹함을 생각해 보아라."], ["Tell me not, as the preachers sometimes do—tell me not that vengeance is the Lord’s.", "이 모든 것을 생각하고, 하늘의 사랑이 네 둘레에 깃든 채로, 내게 말해 보아라, 딸아."], ["Does he not work his will harmfully as well as in love by agencies?", "그 속죄로 머리카락 하나 떨어지거나 붉은 핏방울 하나 흐르지 않아도 좋겠느냐?"], ["Has he not his men of war more numerous than his prophets?", "설교자들이 더러 그러듯—복수는 주의 것이라고는 내게 말하지 마라."], ["Is not his the law, Eye for eye, hand for hand, foot for foot?", "그분은 사랑으로뿐 아니라 도구를 통해 해롭게도 당신의 뜻을 이루지 않으시더냐?"], ["Oh, in all these years I have dreamed of vengeance, and prayed and provided for it, and gathered patience from the growing of my store, thinking and promising, as the Lord liveth, it will one day buy me punishment of the wrong-doers?", "그분께는 예언자보다 더 많은 군사가 있지 않더냐? 눈에는 눈, 손에는 손, 발에는 발이라는 것이 그분의 율법이 아니더냐? 오, 이 모든 세월 동안 나는 복수를 꿈꾸었고, 그것을 위해 기도하며 대비했고, 내 재물이 불어나는 데서 인내를 길어 올렸다."], ["And when, speaking of his practise with arms, the young man said it was for a nameless purpose, I named the purpose even as he spoke—vengeance!", "주께서 살아 계심같이, 이것이 언젠가 죄지은 자들에게 형벌을 사다 주리라고, 그렇게 생각하고 다짐하면서 말이다. 그리고 그 젊은이가 자기 무예 수련을 두고 이름 붙일 수 없는 목적을 위한 것이라고 말했을 때, 나는 그가 말하는 바로 그 순간에 그 목적에 이름을 붙였다—복수!"], ["and that, Esther, that it was—the third thought which held me still and hard while his pleading lasted, and made me laugh when he was gone.”", "그리고 그것, 에스더, 바로 그것이—그가 호소하는 동안 나를 잠잠하고 굳세게 붙들어 두었으며, 그가 떠난 뒤 나를 웃게 만든 세 번째 생각이었다.\""]], [["Esther caressed the faded hands, and said, as if her spirit with his were running forward to results, “He is gone.", "에스더는 그 시든 두 손을 어루만지며, 마치 자기 영혼이 그의 영혼과 함께 그 결말을 향해 달려가기라도 하듯 말했다. \"그가 떠났어요."], ["Will he come again?”", "다시 올까요?\""]], [["“Ay, Malluch the faithful goes with him, and will bring him back when I am ready.”", "\"그럼, 충실한 말루크가 그와 함께 가니, 내가 준비되면 그를 데려올 게다.\""]], [["“And when will that be, father?”", "\"그게 언제일까요, 아버지?\""]], [["“Not long, not long.", "\"머지않다, 머지않았다."], ["He thinks all his witnesses dead.", "그는 제 모든 증인이 죽었다고 여긴다."], ["There is one living who will not fail to know him, if he be indeed my master’s son.”", "그러나 그가 정녕 내 주인의 아들이라면, 그를 알아보는 데 결코 실패하지 않을 산 증인이 하나 있다.\""]], [["“His mother?”", "\"그의 어머니인가요?\""]], [["“Nay, daughter, I will set the witness before him; till then let us rest the business with the Lord.", "\"아니다, 딸아. 나는 그 증인을 그 앞에 세울 것이다. 그때까지는 이 일을 주께 맡기고 쉬도록 하자."], ["I am tired.", "나는 피곤하구나."], ["Call Abimelech.”", "아비멜렉을 불러라.\""]], [["Esther called the servant, and they returned into the house.", "에스더는 하인을 불렀고, 그들은 집 안으로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["When Ben-Hur sallied from the great warehouse, it was with the thought that another failure was to be added to the many he had already met in the quest for his people; and the idea was depressing exactly in proportion as the objects of his quest were dear to him; it curtained him round about with a sense of utter loneliness on earth, which, more than anything else, serves to eke from a soul cast down its remaining interest in life.", "벤허가 그 거대한 창고를 박차고 나왔을 때, 그의 머릿속에는 가족을 찾는 일에서 이미 숱하게 맛본 실패에 또 하나의 실패가 더해지리라는 생각뿐이었다. 그 생각은 찾고자 하는 대상이 그에게 소중한 만큼이나 정확히 그를 짓눌렀다. 그 생각은 그를 이 땅에서 더없이 외롭다는 느낌으로 에워쌌으니, 그 무엇보다도 그것은 풀이 죽은 영혼에게 삶에 대한 마지막 미련마저 쥐어짜 내게 마련이었다."]], [["Through the people, and the piles of goods, he made way to the edge of the landing, and was tempted by the cool shadows darkening the river’s depth.", "그는 사람들 사이로, 또 쌓아 올린 짐짝들 사이로 길을 헤쳐 부두 끝까지 나아갔다. 강의 깊은 곳을 어둑하게 물들이는 서늘한 그늘이 그를 유혹했다."], ["The lazy current seemed to stop and wait for him.", "나른한 물살은 멈춰 서서 그를 기다리는 것만 같았다."], ["In counteraction of the spell, the saying of the voyager flashed into memory—“Better be a worm, and feed upon the mulberries of Daphne, than a king’s guest.”", "그 마법에 맞서려는 듯, 그 뱃사람의 말이 문득 기억 속에 떠올랐다—\"임금의 손님이 되느니, 차라리 한 마리 벌레가 되어 다프네의 뽕나무 열매를 먹는 편이 낫다.\""], ["He turned, and walked rapidly down the landing and back to the khan.", "그는 몸을 돌려, 부두를 빠르게 걸어 내려와 다시 여관으로 돌아갔다."]], [["“The road to Daphne!”", "\"다프네로 가는 길 말입니까!\""], ["the steward said, surprised at the question Ben-Hur put to him.", "벤허가 던진 물음에 놀라며 여관 지배인이 말했다. \"전에 이곳에 와 보신 적이 없습니까? 그렇다면 오늘을 평생 가장 행복한 날로 치십시오."], ["“You have not been here before?", "길은 결코 잘못 들 수가 없습니다."], ["Well, count this the happiest day of your life. You cannot mistake the road.", "남쪽으로 가다 보면 왼편으로 다음 거리가 나오는데, 그 길이 술피우스산으로 곧장 이어집니다. 그 산꼭대기에는 유피테르의 제단과 원형극장이 있지요."], ["The next street to the left, going south, leads straight to Mount Sulpius, crowned by the altar of Jupiter and the Amphitheater; keep it to the third cross street, known as Herod’s Colonnade; turn to your right there, and hold the way through the old city of Seleucus to the bronze gates of Epiphanes.", "그 길을 따라 세 번째 교차로까지 가십시오. 헤롯의 주랑(柱廊)이라 불리는 곳입니다. 거기서 오른쪽으로 꺾어, 셀레우코스의 옛 도시를 가로질러 에피파네스의 청동 문까지 가시면 됩니다."], ["There the road to Daphne begins—and may the gods keep you!”", "거기서 다프네로 가는 길이 시작됩니다—부디 신들의 가호가 함께하기를!\""]], [["A few directions respecting his baggage, and Ben-Hur set out.", "짐에 관한 몇 가지 지시를 남기고, 벤허는 길을 나섰다."]], [["The Colonnade of Herod was easily found; thence to the brazen gates, under a continuous marble portico, he passed with a multitude mixed of people from all the trading nations of the earth.", "헤롯의 주랑은 쉽게 찾을 수 있었다. 거기서부터 청동 문까지, 끊임없이 이어진 대리석 주랑 아래로, 그는 이 땅의 온갖 교역하는 나라에서 모여든 사람들 무리에 섞여 지나갔다."]], [["It was about the fourth hour of the day when he passed out the gate, and found himself one of a procession apparently interminable, moving to the famous Grove.", "그가 문을 나선 것은 하루의 네 번째 시각쯤이었는데, 그는 어느새 끝이 보이지 않는 행렬의 한 사람이 되어 그 유명한 숲을 향해 나아가고 있었다."], ["The road was divided into separate ways for footmen, for men on horses, and men in chariots; and those again into separate ways for outgoers and incomers.", "길은 걷는 이를 위한 길, 말 탄 이를 위한 길, 전차를 탄 이를 위한 길로 갈라져 있었고, 그것들은 다시 나가는 이와 들어오는 이를 위한 길로 나뉘어 있었다."], ["The lines of division were guarded by low balustrading, broken by massive pedestals, many of which were surmounted with statuary.", "그 경계선은 낮은 난간으로 지켜져 있었는데, 그 난간은 육중한 받침대들로 끊겨 있었고, 그 받침대 위에는 조각상을 얹은 것이 많았다."], ["Right and left of the road extended margins of sward perfectly kept, relieved at intervals by groups of oak and sycamore trees, and vine-clad summer-houses for the accommodation of the weary, of whom, on the return side, there were always multitudes.", "길 좌우로는 완벽하게 손질된 잔디밭이 펼쳐졌고, 군데군데 떡갈나무와 무화과나무 무리가 그 단조로움을 덜어 주었으며, 지친 이들을 위한 포도 덩굴 우거진 정자들도 있었으니, 돌아오는 쪽 길에는 그런 이들이 늘 무리 지어 있었다."], ["The ways of the footmen were paved with red stone, and those of the riders strewn with white sand compactly rolled, but not so solid as to give back an echo to hoof or wheel.", "걷는 이의 길은 붉은 돌로 포장되었고, 말 탄 이의 길은 단단히 다진 흰 모래가 뿌려져 있었는데, 발굽이나 바퀴 소리가 메아리치지 않을 만큼은 부드러웠다."], ["The number and variety of fountains at play were amazing, all gifts of visiting kings, and called after them. Out southwest to the gates of the Grove, the magnificent thoroughfare stretched a little over four miles from the city.", "솟구치는 분수의 수효와 그 갖가지 모양은 놀라울 따름이었으니, 모두가 이곳을 찾았던 임금들이 바친 선물이라 그들의 이름을 따서 불렀다. 남서쪽으로 숲의 문까지, 그 웅장한 대로는 도시에서 6킬로미터 남짓 뻗어 있었다."]], [["In his wretchedness of feeling, Ben-Hur barely observed the royal liberality which marked the construction of the road.", "비참한 심정에 빠져 있던 벤허는 그 길의 건설에 새겨진 왕다운 베풂을 거의 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["Nor more did he at first notice the crowd going with him.", "처음에는 자기와 함께 가는 군중도 마찬가지로 알아차리지 못했다."], ["He treated the processional displays with like indifference.", "행렬이 펼치는 볼거리도 똑같이 무심하게 지나쳤다."], ["To say truth, besides his self-absorption, he had not a little of the complacency of a Roman visiting the provinces fresh from the ceremonies which daily eddied round and round the golden pillar set up by Augustus as the centre of the world.", "사실을 말하자면, 자기 생각에 빠져 있는 데다, 그는 로마인 특유의 우월감을 적잖이 지니고 있었으니, 곧 아우구스투스가 세계의 중심으로 세운 황금 기둥 둘레로 날마다 소용돌이치는 의식들을 막 보고 떠나온 자가 속주를 찾았을 때 갖는 그 우월감이었다."], ["It was not possible for the provinces to offer anything new or superior.", "속주가 무언가 새롭거나 더 뛰어난 것을 내놓을 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["He rather availed himself of every opportunity to push forward through the companies in the way, and too slow-going for his impatience.", "그는 오히려 기회 있을 때마다 길에 늘어선 무리를 헤치고 앞으로 나아가려 했으니, 그 발걸음이 조급한 그에게는 너무 더뎠던 까닭이다."], ["By the time he reached Heracleia, a suburban village intermediate the city and the Grove, he was somewhat spent with exercise, and began to be susceptible of entertainment.", "도시와 숲 사이에 있는 변두리 마을 헤라클레이아에 다다랐을 무렵, 그는 걷느라 다소 지쳐 있었고, 비로소 구경거리에 마음이 끌리기 시작했다."], ["Once a pair of goats led by a beautiful woman, woman and goats alike brilliant with ribbons and flowers, attracted his attention.", "한번은 아름다운 여인이 끌고 가는 염소 두 마리가 그의 눈길을 끌었는데, 여인이나 염소나 모두 리본과 꽃으로 화려하게 꾸며져 있었다."], ["Then he stopped to look at a bull of mighty girth, and snowy white, covered with vines freshly cut, and bearing on its broad back a naked child in a basket, the image of a young Bacchus, squeezing the juice of ripened berries into a goblet, and drinking with libational formulas.", "그러고는 걸음을 멈추고, 허리둘레가 어마어마하고 눈처럼 새하얀 황소를 바라보았으니, 그 황소는 막 베어 낸 포도 덩굴로 뒤덮여 있었고, 넓은 등에는 바구니에 담긴 벌거벗은 어린아이를 태우고 있었다. 그 아이는 어린 바쿠스의 형상으로, 잘 익은 열매의 즙을 술잔에 짜 넣고는 헌주(獻酒)의 격식을 읊으며 마시고 있었다."], ["As he resumed his walk, he wondered whose altars would be enriched by the offerings.", "다시 걸음을 옮기면서, 그는 그 제물들로 어느 신의 제단이 풍성해질지 궁금해했다."], ["A horse went by with clipped mane, after the fashion of the time, his rider superbly dressed.", "그때의 풍습대로 갈기를 짧게 깎은 말 한 마리가 지나갔는데, 그 말을 탄 이는 더없이 화려한 차림이었다."], ["He smiled to observe the harmony of pride between the man and the brute.", "그는 사람과 짐승 사이에 어우러진 그 거만함의 조화를 보고 미소 지었다."], ["Often after that he turned his head at hearing the rumble of wheels and the dull thud of hoofs; unconsciously he was becoming interested in the styles of chariots and charioteers, as they rustled past him going and coming.", "그 뒤로도 그는 자주 바퀴 구르는 소리와 둔탁한 발굽 소리에 고개를 돌렸으니, 자기도 모르게 그의 곁을 스쳐 가고 오는 전차와 마부들의 갖가지 모양에 흥미를 느끼기 시작한 것이다. 곧이어 그는 주위 사람들도 눈여겨보기 시작했다."], ["Nor was it long until he began to make notes of the people around him.", "그들은 나이도 성별도 신분도 갖가지였으나, 모두 명절 차림이었다."], ["He saw they were of all ages, sexes, and conditions, and all in holiday attire.", "한 무리는 흰옷으로, 다른 무리는 검은옷으로 갖춰 입었다."], ["One company was uniformed in white, another in black; some bore flags, some smoking censers; some went slowly, singing hymns; others stepped to the music of flutes and tabrets.", "어떤 이는 깃발을 들었고, 어떤 이는 연기 나는 향로를 들었다. 어떤 이는 찬가를 부르며 천천히 걸었고, 다른 이는 피리와 작은북 소리에 발을 맞추었다."], ["If such were the going to Daphne every day in the year, what a wondrous sight Daphne must be!", "일 년 내내 날마다 이렇게 다프네로 가는 행렬이 이어진다면, 다프네는 얼마나 경이로운 곳이란 말인가!"], ["At last there was a clapping of hands, and a burst of joyous cries; following the pointing of many fingers, he looked and saw upon the brow of a hill the templed gate of the consecrated Grove.", "마침내 손뼉 치는 소리와 기쁨에 찬 환호성이 터져 나왔다. 수많은 손가락이 가리키는 곳을 따라 바라보니, 언덕 마루 위로 신성한 숲의 신전 같은 문이 보였다."], ["The hymns swelled to louder strains; the music quickened time; and, borne along by the impulsive current, and sharing the common eagerness, he passed in, and, Romanized in taste as he was, fell to worshiping the place.", "찬가는 더욱 우렁찬 가락으로 부풀어 올랐고, 음악은 박자를 빨리했다. 그는 그 충동적인 물결에 떠밀려 가며, 모두가 품은 열망을 함께 나누는 가운데 안으로 들어섰으니, 취향은 로마에 물든 자였건만 그 또한 그곳을 경배하기 시작했다."]], [["Rearward of the structure which graced the entrance-way—a purely Grecian pile—he stood upon a broad esplanade paved with polished stone; around him a restless exclamatory multitude, in gayest colors, relieved against the iridescent spray flying crystal-white from fountains; before him, off to the southwest, dustless paths radiated out into a garden, and beyond that into a forest, over which rested a veil of pale-blue vapor.", "들머리를 꾸미는 건축물—순수한 그리스 양식의 건물—뒤편에서, 그는 윤이 나는 돌로 포장된 널찍한 광장에 섰다. 그의 둘레에는 더없이 화사한 빛깔로 차려입은 군중이 끊임없이 환호성을 올리고 있었으니, 분수에서 수정처럼 새하얗게 흩날리는 무지갯빛 물보라가 그 뒤로 그들을 돋보이게 했다. 그의 앞에는, 남서쪽으로 멀리, 먼지 한 점 없는 오솔길들이 정원으로 뻗어 나갔고, 그 너머로는 숲으로 이어졌으며, 그 위로는 옅은 푸른빛 안개의 너울이 드리워 있었다."], ["Ben-Hur gazed wistfully, uncertain where to go. A woman that moment exclaimed,", "벤허는 어디로 가야 할지 망설이며 애틋한 눈길로 바라보았다. 그때 한 여인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Beautiful!", "\"아름다워라!"], ["But where to now?”", "그런데 이제 어디로 가지?\""]], [["Her companion, wearing a chaplet of bays, laughed and answered, “Go to, thou pretty barbarian!", "월계수 화관을 쓴 동행이 웃으며 대답했다. \"이런, 어여쁜 야만인 같으니!"], ["The question implies an earthly fear; and did we not agree to leave all such behind in Antioch with the rusty earth?", "그 물음에는 속세의 두려움이 배어 있구려. 그 녹슨 흙먼지와 함께 그런 두려움일랑 모조리 안티오크에 두고 오기로 하지 않았소?"], ["The winds which blow here are respirations of the gods.", "여기 부는 바람은 신들의 숨결이라오."], ["Let us give ourselves to waftage of the winds.”", "우리 그 바람이 부는 대로 몸을 맡깁시다.\""]], [["“But if we should get lost?”", "\"그래도 길을 잃으면 어쩌지요?\""]], [["“O thou timid!", "\"오, 겁 많은 이여!"], ["No one was ever lost in Daphne, except those on whom her gates close forever.”", "다프네에서 길을 잃은 사람은 아무도 없소. 다프네의 문이 영영 닫혀 버린 자들이라면 모를까.\""]], [["“And who are they?”", "\"그게 누군데요?\""], ["she asked, still fearful.", "그녀가 여전히 두려워하며 물었다."]], [["“Such as have yielded to the charms of the place and chosen it for life and death. Hark!", "\"이곳의 매혹에 굴복하여, 여기서 살다 죽기로 작정한 자들이오. 들어 보시오!"], ["Stand we here, and I will show you of whom I speak.”", "여기 가만히 서 있으면, 내가 말하는 이들이 누구인지 보여 주리다.\""]], [["Upon the marble pavement there was a scurry of sandalled feet; the crowd opened, and a party of girls rushed about the speaker and his fair friend, and began singing and dancing to the tabrets they themselves touched.", "대리석 포장 위로 샌들 신은 발들이 종종거리는 소리가 났다. 군중이 갈라지더니, 한 무리의 처녀들이 말하던 이와 그의 어여쁜 벗을 둘러싸고 몰려와, 저희가 직접 두드리는 작은북에 맞추어 노래하고 춤추기 시작했다."], ["The woman, scared, clung to the man, who put an arm about her, and, with kindled face, kept time to the music with the other hand overhead.", "여인은 겁에 질려 사내에게 매달렸고, 사내는 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안은 채, 상기된 얼굴로 다른 한 손을 머리 위로 들어 음악에 박자를 맞추었다."], ["The hair of the dancers floated free, and their limbs blushed through the robes of gauze which scarcely draped them.", "춤추는 이들의 머리칼은 풀어 헤쳐져 휘날렸고, 그 사지는 거의 가리지도 못하는 얇은 비단옷 사이로 발그레하게 비쳤다."], ["Words may not be used to tell of the voluptuousness of the dance.", "그 춤의 관능을 말로는 다 옮길 수 없다."], ["One brief round, and they darted off through the yielding crowd lightly as they had come.", "한 차례 짧게 돌고 나서, 그들은 처음 왔던 것처럼 가볍게 갈라지는 군중 사이로 날래게 사라졌다."]], [["“Now what think you?”", "\"자, 어떻소?\""], ["cried the man to the woman.", "사내가 여인에게 외쳤다."]], [["“Who are they?”", "\"저들은 누구지요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Devadasi—priestesses devoted to the Temple of Apollo.", "\"데바다시라오—아폴로 신전에 바쳐진 무녀들이지."], ["There is an army of them.", "그 수가 한 부대를 이룬다오."], ["They make the chorus in celebrations.", "축제 때 합창대를 이루는 이들이오."], ["This is their home.", "이곳이 그들의 집이지."], ["Sometimes they wander off to other cities, but all they make is brought here to enrich the house of the divine musician.", "때로는 다른 도시로 떠돌기도 하지만, 그들이 버는 것은 모조리 이곳으로 가져와 그 신성한 악사의 집을 풍성하게 한다오."], ["Shall we go now?”", "이제 가 볼까?\""]], [["Next minute the two were gone.", "다음 순간 두 사람은 사라졌다."]], [["Ben-Hur took comfort in the assurance that no one was ever lost in Daphne, and he, too, set out—where, he knew not.", "벤허는 다프네에서 길을 잃은 사람은 아무도 없다는 그 장담에 위안을 얻고, 그 또한 길을 나섰다—어디로 가는지는 그 자신도 알지 못했다."]], [["A sculpture reared upon a beautiful pedestal in the garden attracted him first.", "정원의 아름다운 받침대 위에 세워진 한 조각상이 먼저 그를 끌었다."], ["It proved to be the statue of a centaur.", "그것은 켄타우로스의 상이었다."], ["An inscription informed the unlearned visitor that it exactly represented Chiron, the beloved of Apollo and Diana, instructed by them in the mysteries of hunting, medicine, music, and prophecy.", "새겨진 글귀가 무지한 방문객에게 일러 주기를, 그것은 아폴로와 디아나의 사랑을 받은 키론을 그대로 본뜬 것으로, 키론은 그 두 신에게서 사냥과 의술과 음악과 예언의 신비를 전수받았다고 했다."], ["The inscription also bade the stranger look out at a certain part of the heavens, at a certain hour of the clear night, and he would behold the dead alive among the stars, whither Jupiter had transferred the good genius.", "그 글귀는 또한 나그네더러 맑은 밤 어느 시각에 하늘의 어느 자리를 바라보라 일렀으니, 그러면 별들 사이에 살아 있는 죽은 자를 보게 되리라 했다. 유피테르가 그 선량한 정령을 그곳으로 옮겨 놓았다는 것이다."]], [["The wisest of the centaurs continued, nevertheless, in the service of mankind.", "그럼에도 켄타우로스 가운데 가장 슬기로운 이 존재는 여전히 인류를 섬기고 있었다."], ["In his hand he held a scroll, on which, graven in Greek, were paragraphs of a notice:", "그는 손에 두루마리 하나를 들고 있었는데, 거기에는 그리스어로 새겨진 안내문의 여러 구절이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“O Traveller!", "\"오, 나그네여!"], ["“Art thou a stranger?", "그대는 이방인인가?"]], [["“I.", "하나."], ["Hearken to the singing of the brooks, and fear not the rain of the fountains; so will the Naiades learn to love thee.", "시냇물의 노래에 귀 기울이고, 분수의 빗줄기를 두려워 말라. 그리하면 물의 정령 나이아데스가 그대를 사랑하게 되리라."]], [["“II.", "둘. 다프네가 청한 산들바람은 제피로스와 아우스테르라."], ["The invited breezes of Daphne are Zephyrus and Auster; gentle ministers of life, they will gather sweets for thee; when Eurus blows, Diana is elsewhere hunting; when Boreas blusters, go hide, for Apollo is angry.", "생명을 돌보는 다정한 사자(使者)이니, 그대를 위해 향기를 모아 주리라. 에우로스가 불 때면 디아나는 어디 다른 곳에서 사냥하는 것이요, 보레아스가 거세게 몰아칠 때면 몸을 숨기라. 아폴로가 노한 것이니."]], [["“III.", "셋."], ["The shades of the Grove are thine in the day; at night they belong to Pan and his Dryades. Disturb them not.", "숲의 그늘은 낮에는 그대의 것이나, 밤에는 판과 그의 드리아데스의 것이라. 그들을 어지럽히지 말라."]], [["“IV.", "넷."], ["Eat of the Lotus by the brooksides sparingly, unless thou wouldst have surcease of memory, which is to become a child of Daphne.", "시냇가의 연꽃은 조금만 먹으라. 기억을 잃고 싶지 않다면 말이다. 기억을 잃는 것은 다프네의 자식이 되는 것이니."]], [["“V. Walk thou round the weaving spider—’tis Arachne at work for Minerva.", "다섯. 베를 짜는 거미는 돌아서 지나가라—저것은 미네르바를 위해 일하는 아라크네라."]], [["“VI.", "여섯."], ["Wouldst thou behold the tears of Daphne, break but a bud from a laurel bough—and die.", "다프네의 눈물을 보고 싶다면, 월계수 가지에서 봉오리 하나만 꺾어라—그리고 죽으라."]], [["“Heed thou!", "명심하라!"], ["“And stay and be happy.”", "그리고 머물러 행복하라.\""]], [["Ben-Hur left the interpretation of the mystic notice to others fast enclosing him, and turned away as the white bull was led by.", "벤허는 그 신비로운 안내문의 풀이를 어느새 자기를 에워싸 오는 다른 이들에게 맡기고, 흰 황소가 끌려오는 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["The boy sat in the basket, followed by a procession; after them again, the woman with the goats; and behind her the flute and tabret players, and another procession of gift-bringers.", "사내아이는 바구니에 앉아 있었고, 그 뒤로 행렬이 따랐다. 그 뒤로는 다시 염소를 끄는 여인이, 그녀 뒤로는 피리와 작은북을 부는 이들이, 또 그 뒤로는 제물을 바치러 가는 또 다른 행렬이 이어졌다."]], [["“Whither go they?”", "\"저들은 어디로 가는 거요?\""], ["asked a bystander.", "한 구경꾼이 물었다."]], [["Another made answer, “The bull to Father Jove; the goat—”", "다른 이가 대답했다. \"황소는 아버지 유피테르께로, 염소는—\""]], [["“Did not Apollo once keep the flocks of Admetus?”", "\"아폴로가 한때 아드메토스의 가축을 친 적이 있지 않은가?\""]], [["“Ay, the goat to Apollo!”", "\"옳거니, 염소는 아폴로께로!\""]], [["The goodness of the reader is again besought in favor of an explanation.", "여기서 다시 독자의 너그러움에 기대어 한 가지 설명을 청한다."], ["A certain facility of accommodation in the matter of religion comes to us after much intercourse with people of a different faith; gradually we attain the truth that every creed is illustrated by good men who are entitled to our respect, but whom we cannot respect without courtesy to their creed.", "신앙이 다른 사람들과 오래 어울리다 보면, 종교 문제에서 어느 정도 타협하는 여유가 생기게 마련이다. 그리하여 우리는 차츰 한 가지 진리에 이르게 되니, 어느 신조든 우리의 존경을 받을 만한 선량한 이들로 빛난다는 것, 그리고 그 신조에 예의를 갖추지 않고서는 그들을 존경할 수 없다는 것이다."], ["To this point Ben-Hur had arrived.", "벤허는 바로 이 경지에 이르러 있었다."], ["Neither the years in Rome nor those in the galley had made any impression upon his religious faith; he was yet a Jew.", "로마에서 보낸 세월도, 갤리선에서 보낸 세월도 그의 종교적 신앙에는 아무런 자취를 남기지 못했으니, 그는 여전히 유대인이었다."], ["In his view, nevertheless, it was not an impiety to look for the beautiful in the Grove of Daphne.", "그럼에도 그가 보기에, 다프네의 숲에서 아름다운 것을 찾는 일은 불경이 아니었다."]], [["The remark does not interdict the further saying, if his scruples had been ever so extreme, not improbably he would at this time have smothered them.", "이 말은 또 한 가지 말을 막지 않는다. 곧, 설령 그의 양심의 거리낌이 아무리 극단적이었다 하더라도, 이때만큼은 그것을 억눌렀을 법하다는 것이다."], ["He was angry; not as the irritable, from chafing of a trifle; nor was his anger like the fool’s, pumped from the wells of nothing, to be dissipated by a reproach or a curse; it was the wrath peculiar to ardent natures rudely awakened by the sudden annihilation of a hope—dream, if you will—in which the choicest happinesses were thought to be certainly in reach.", "그는 노여워하고 있었다. 사소한 일에 속이 긁혀 짜증을 부리는 사람의 노여움도 아니요, 아무것도 없는 우물에서 길어 올린 듯하여 한마디 꾸짖음이나 욕설로 흩어져 버리는 어리석은 자의 노여움도 아니었다. 그것은 가장 값진 행복들이 분명히 손에 닿는 곳에 있다 여겼던 희망—굳이 말하자면 꿈—이 느닷없이 무너지는 바람에 거칠게 깨어난, 열정적인 천성에 고유한 분노였다."], ["In such case nothing intermediate will carry off the passion—the quarrel is with Fate.", "이런 경우에는 그 어떤 어중간한 것도 그 격정을 거두어 가지 못한다—그 다툼의 상대는 운명이기 때문이다."]], [["Let us follow the philosophy a little further, and say to ourselves, it were well in such quarrels if Fate were something tangible, to be despatched with a look or a blow, or a speaking personage with whom high words were possible; then the unhappy mortal would not always end the affair by punishing himself.", "이 철리(哲理)를 조금만 더 좇아, 우리 스스로에게 이렇게 말해 두자. 그런 다툼에서, 운명이 무언가 만질 수 있는 것이어서 한 번의 눈길이나 한 번의 일격으로 처치할 수 있다거나, 또는 거친 말을 주고받을 수 있는 말 상대라도 된다면 다행이련만. 그렇다면 그 가엾은 인간은 끝내 제 자신을 벌하는 것으로 일을 마무리 짓지는 않을 텐데 말이다."]], [["In ordinary mood, Ben-Hur would not have come to the Grove alone, or, coming alone, he would have availed himself of his position in the consul’s family, and made provision against wandering idly about, unknowing and unknown; he would have had all the points of interest in mind, and gone to them under guidance, as in the despatch of business; or, wishing to squander days of leisure in the beautiful place, he would have had in hand a letter to the master of it all, whoever he might be.", "여느 때 같았으면 벤허는 홀로 숲을 찾지 않았을 것이요, 설령 홀로 왔더라도 집정관 집안에서의 제 지위를 이용하여, 아는 이 하나 없이 알려지지도 않은 채 하릴없이 헤매고 다니지 않도록 채비를 갖추었을 것이다. 그는 볼 만한 곳들을 모조리 머릿속에 새겨 두고, 마치 업무를 처리하듯 안내를 받아 그곳들을 찾아갔을 것이다. 또는 그 아름다운 곳에서 한가로운 며칠을 흥청망청 보내고 싶었다면, 그 모든 것을 다스리는 주인이 누구든 그에게 보내는 소개장을 손에 쥐고 있었을 것이다."], ["This would have made him a sightseer, like the shouting herd he was accompanying; whereas he had no reverence for the deities of the Grove, nor curiosity; a man in the blindness of bitter disappointment, he was adrift, not waiting for Fate, but seeking it as a desperate challenger.", "그랬다면 그도 자기가 함께 따라온 저 떠들썩한 무리처럼 한낱 구경꾼이 되었으리라. 그러나 그는 숲의 신들에게 경의도 없었고 호기심도 없었다. 쓰라린 실망에 눈먼 사내로서, 그는 정처 없이 떠도는 신세였으니, 운명을 기다리는 것이 아니라 절박한 도전자가 되어 운명을 찾아 헤매고 있었다."]], [["Every one has known this condition of mind, though perhaps not all in the same degree; every one will recognize it as the condition in which he has done brave things with apparent serenity; and every one reading will say, Fortunate for Ben-Hur if the folly which now catches him is but a friendly harlequin with whistle and painted cap, and not some Violence with a pointed sword pitiless.", "이런 마음의 상태를 누구나 한 번쯤 겪어 보았을 것이다. 그 정도가 모두 같지는 않을지라도 말이다. 누구나 그것을, 겉으로는 태연한 척하면서 용감한 일을 해치웠던 그 마음 상태로 알아볼 것이다. 그리고 이 글을 읽는 누구나 이렇게 말할 것이다. 지금 벤허를 사로잡은 그 어리석음이, 호각을 불고 알록달록한 모자를 쓴 정다운 어릿광대일 뿐이요, 무자비하게 뾰족한 칼을 든 어떤 폭력이 아니라면, 벤허에게는 다행한 일이라고."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["Ben-Hur entered the woods with the processions.", "벤허는 행렬과 함께 숲으로 들어섰다."], ["He had not interest enough at first to ask where they were going; yet, to relieve him from absolute indifference, he had a vague impression that they were in movement to the temples, which were the central objects of the Grove, supreme in attractions.", "처음에는 그들이 어디로 가는지 물어볼 만큼의 관심조차 없었다. 그러나 완전한 무관심에서 그를 건져낸 것은, 일행이 숲의 중심을 이루며 무엇보다 강한 매력을 지닌 신전들을 향해 움직이고 있다는 막연한 짐작이었다."]], [["Presently, as singers dreamfully play with a flitting chorus, he began repeating to himself, “Better be a worm, and feed on the mulberries of Daphne, than a king’s guest.”", "이윽고, 가수들이 스쳐 지나가는 합창을 꿈결처럼 가지고 노닐듯이, 그는 혼잣말로 되뇌기 시작했다. \"왕의 손님이 되느니, 차라리 한 마리 벌레가 되어 다프네의 뽕나무 열매를 먹는 편이 낫다.\""], ["Then of the much repetition arose questions importunate of answer.", "그러다 거듭 되뇐 끝에, 답을 졸라대는 물음들이 떠올랐다."], ["Was life in the Grove so very sweet?", "이 숲의 삶이 그토록 달콤하단 말인가?"], ["Wherein was the charm?", "그 매력은 어디에 있는가?"], ["Did it lie in some tangled depth of philosophy?", "어떤 철학의 뒤엉킨 심연 속에 깃들어 있는가?"], ["Or was it something in fact, something on the surface, discernible to every-day wakeful senses?", "아니면 사실 그것은 무언가, 표면에 드러나 있어 날마다 깨어 있는 감각이면 누구나 알아챌 수 있는 무언가인가?"], ["Every year thousands, forswearing the world, gave themselves to service here.", "해마다 수천 명이 세상을 버리고 이곳의 봉사에 몸을 바쳤다."], ["Did they find the charm?", "그들은 그 매력을 찾아냈을까?"], ["And was it sufficient, when found, to induce forgetfulness profound enough to shut out of mind the infinitely diverse things of life?", "그리고 찾아냈을 때, 그것은 삶의 무한히 다양한 것들을 마음에서 몰아내기에 충분할 만큼 깊은 망각을 불러올 수 있었을까?"], ["those that sweeten and those that embitter? hopes hovering in the near future as well as sorrows born of the past?", "삶을 달콤하게 하는 것들과 쓰디쓰게 하는 것들, 가까운 미래에 어른거리는 희망과 지난날에서 태어난 슬픔까지를?"], ["If the Grove were so good for them, why should it not be good for him?", "이 숲이 그들에게 그토록 좋은 것이라면, 어찌하여 그에게는 좋지 않을 것인가?"], ["He was a Jew; could it be that the excellences were for all the world but children of Abraham?", "그는 유대인이었다. 그 빼어남이 아브라함의 자손만 빼고 온 세상을 위한 것일 수도 있단 말인가?"], ["Forthwith he bent all his faculties to the task of discovery, unmindful of the singing of the gift-bringers and the quips of his associates.", "그는 곧 선물을 가져가는 무리의 노래도 동료들의 농담도 아랑곳하지 않고, 온 정신을 발견의 과업에 쏟았다."]], [["In the quest, the sky yielded him nothing; it was blue, very blue, and full of twittering swallows—so was the sky over the city.", "그 탐구에서, 하늘은 그에게 아무것도 내주지 않았다. 하늘은 푸르렀다, 아주 푸르렀고, 재잘대는 제비들로 가득했다. 도시 위의 하늘도 그러했다."]], [["Further on, out of the woods at his right hand, a breeze poured across the road, splashing him with a wave of sweet smells, blent of roses and consuming spices.", "조금 더 나아가자, 오른편 숲에서 산들바람이 길을 가로질러 쏟아져 와, 장미와 타오르는 향료가 어우러진 달콤한 향기의 물결로 그를 적셨다."], ["He stopped, as did others, looking the way the breeze came.", "그는 다른 이들이 그러듯 걸음을 멈추고, 바람이 불어오는 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“A garden over there?”", "\"저쪽에 정원이라도 있소?\""], ["he said, to a man at his elbow.", "그는 곁에 선 사내에게 물었다."]], [["“Rather some priestly ceremony in performance—something to Diana, or Pan, or a deity of the woods.”", "\"정원이라기보다는 어떤 사제의 의식을 치르고 있는 것이겠지요. 디아나나 판, 아니면 숲의 어느 신에게 바치는 것 말입니다.\""]], [["The answer was in his mother tongue. Ben-Hur gave the speaker a surprised look.", "대답은 그의 모국어였다. 벤허는 말을 건넨 자에게 놀란 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“A Hebrew?” he asked him.", "\"히브리 사람이오?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["The man replied with a deferential smile,", "사내는 공손한 미소로 대답했다."]], [["“I was born within a stone’s-throw of the market-place in Jerusalem.”", "\"저는 예루살렘 장터에서 돌 던지면 닿을 만큼 가까운 곳에서 태어났습니다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur was proceeding to further speech, when the crowd surged forward, thrusting him out on the side of the walk next the woods, and carrying the stranger away.", "벤허가 말을 더 이으려는 참에, 군중이 앞으로 밀려들며 그를 길 가장자리 숲 쪽으로 떠밀고, 그 낯선 이를 휩쓸어 갔다."], ["The customary gown and staff, a brown cloth on the head tied by a yellow rope, and a strong Judean face to avouch the garments of honest right, remained in the young man’s mind, a kind of summary of the man.", "흔히 입는 겉옷과 지팡이, 노란 끈으로 동여맨 갈색 머릿수건, 그리고 정직한 신분의 옷차림임을 증명하는 굳센 유대인의 얼굴이, 그 사내의 일종의 요약처럼 젊은이의 마음에 남았다."]], [["This took place at a point where a path into the woods began, offering a happy escape from the noisy processions.", "이 일은 숲으로 드는 오솔길이 시작되는 지점에서 일어났으니, 떠들썩한 행렬에서 반갑게 벗어날 길을 열어 주었다."], ["Ben-Hur availed himself of the offer.", "벤허는 그 기회를 놓치지 않았다."]], [["He walked first into a thicket which, from the road, appeared in a state of nature, close, impenetrable, a nesting-place for wild birds.", "그는 먼저 덤불 속으로 걸어 들어갔는데, 길에서 보았을 때 그곳은 자연 그대로의 상태인 듯, 빽빽하고 뚫고 들어갈 수 없으며 들새들의 보금자리처럼 보였다."], ["A few steps, however, gave him to see the master’s hand even there.", "그러나 몇 걸음 들어서자, 거기에도 주인의 손길이 닿아 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["The shrubs were flowering or fruit-bearing; under the bending branches the ground was pranked with brightest blooms; over them the jasmine stretched its delicate bonds.", "관목들은 꽃을 피우거나 열매를 맺고 있었다. 휘어진 가지 아래로 땅은 더없이 환한 꽃송이들로 단장되어 있고, 그 위로 재스민이 가녀린 덩굴을 뻗었다."], ["From lilac and rose, and lily and tulip, from oleander and strawberry-tree, all old friends in the gardens of the valleys about the city of David, the air, lingering or in haste, loaded itself with exhalations day and night; and that nothing might be wanting to the happiness of the nymphs and naiads, down through the flower-lighted shadows of the mass a brook went its course gently, and by many winding ways.", "라일락과 장미, 백합과 튤립, 협죽도와 딸기나무에서, 다윗의 도성을 둘러싼 골짜기의 정원에서 일찍이 정든 모든 벗들에게서, 대기는 머무르며 혹은 서두르며 밤낮으로 향기를 가득 실었다. 그리고 님프와 물의 정령들의 기쁨에 모자람이 없도록, 꽃빛으로 환한 그늘 사이로 시냇물 하나가 부드럽게, 수없이 굽이지며 제 길을 흘러갔다."]], [["Out of the thicket, as he proceeded, on his right and left, issued the cry of the pigeon and the cooing of turtle-doves; blackbirds waited for him, and bided his coming close; a nightingale kept its place fearless, though he passed in arm’s-length; a quail ran before him at his feet, whistling to the brood she was leading, and as he paused for them to get out of his way, a figure crawled from a bed of honeyed musk brilliant with balls of golden blossoms.", "그가 나아가는 동안 덤불에서, 좌우로 비둘기의 울음과 멧비둘기의 구구거림이 새어 나왔다. 검은지빠귀들이 그를 기다리며 가까이 다가올 때까지 자리를 지켰고, 나이팅게일은 그가 팔 길이만큼 가까이 지나가도 두려움 없이 제자리에 머물렀으며, 메추라기 한 마리가 데리고 가던 새끼들에게 휘파람 소리를 내며 그의 발치 앞을 달려갔다. 그가 새끼들이 길을 비키도록 멈춰 섰을 때, 황금빛 꽃송이가 환히 빛나는 달콤한 사향풀 더미에서 한 형체가 기어 나왔다."], ["Ben-Hur was startled.", "벤허는 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Had he, indeed, been permitted to see a satyr at home?", "정말로 그는 사티로스가 제 집에 있는 모습을 보게 된 것일까?"], ["The creature looked up at him, and showed in its teeth a hooked pruning-knife; he smiled at his own scare, and, lo!", "그 형체는 그를 올려다보았고, 이빨 사이에 갈고리진 전지용 칼을 물고 있었다. 그는 제 놀람에 빙긋 웃었다."], ["the charm was evolved! Peace without fear—peace a universal condition—that it was!", "그리고 보라, 그 매력이 풀려나왔다! 두려움 없는 평화, 온 세상에 두루 미친 평화, 바로 그것이었다!"]], [["He sat upon the ground beneath a citron-tree, which spread its gray roots sprawling to receive a branch of the brook.", "그는 한 그루 시트론나무 아래 땅에 앉았다. 그 나무는 시냇물 한 줄기를 받아들이려는 듯 잿빛 뿌리를 사방으로 뻗고 있었다."], ["The nest of a titmouse hung close to the bubbling water, and the tiny creature looked out of the door of the nest into his eyes.", "보글거리는 물가에 박새의 둥지가 매달려 있고, 그 작은 것이 둥지 문에서 그의 눈을 들여다보았다. \"참으로, 이 새가 나에게 풀어 일러 주는구나.\""], ["“Verily, the bird is interpreting to me,”", "그는 생각했다. \"이렇게 말하고 있지."], ["he thought. “It says, ‘I am not afraid of you, for the law of this happy place is Love.’”", "'나는 너를 두려워하지 않는다. 이 행복한 곳의 법은 사랑이니까.'\""]], [["The charm of the Grove seemed plain to him; he was glad, and determined to render himself one of the lost in Daphne.", "숲의 매력이 그에게 분명해진 듯했다. 그는 기뻤고, 다프네에서 길 잃은 자들 가운데 하나가 되기로 마음먹었다."], ["In charge of the flowers and shrubs, and watching the growth of all the dumb excellences everywhere to be seen, could not he, like the man with the pruning-knife in his mouth, forego the days of his troubled life—forego them forgetting and forgotten?", "꽃과 관목을 돌보고, 곳곳에서 볼 수 있는 말 못 하는 모든 빼어남이 자라나는 것을 지켜보며, 입에 전지용 칼을 문 저 사내처럼, 그 또한 고난에 찬 나날을 버릴 수 있지 않을까? 잊으면서, 또 잊히면서 그것을 버릴 수 있지 않을까?"]], [["But by-and-by his Jewish nature began to stir within him.", "그러나 이윽고 그의 안에서 유대인의 천성이 꿈틀거리기 시작했다."]], [["The charm might be sufficient for some people.", "그 매력은 어떤 이들에게는 충분할지도 몰랐다."], ["Of what kind were they?", "그들은 어떤 부류의 사람들인가?"]], [["Love is delightful—ah!", "사랑은 즐겁다, 아!"], ["how pleasant as a successor to wretchedness like his.", "그 같은 비참함의 뒤를 잇는 것으로서 얼마나 흐뭇한가."], ["But was it all there was of life? All?", "그러나 그것이 삶의 전부였을까? 전부였을까?"]], [["There was an unlikeness between him and those who buried themselves contentedly here.", "그와, 이곳에 만족스레 제 몸을 파묻는 자들 사이에는 다름이 있었다."], ["They had no duties—they could not have had; but he—", "그들에게는 의무가 없었다, 있을 수도 없었다. 그러나 그에게는—"]], [["“God of Israel!”", "\"이스라엘의 하느님!\""], ["he cried aloud, springing to his feet, with burning cheeks—“Mother! Tirzah!", "그는 두 뺨이 달아오른 채 벌떡 일어서며 소리쳤다. \"어머니! 티르자!"], ["Cursed be the moment, cursed the place, in which I yield myself happy in your loss!”", "두 분을 잃고도 이 몸이 행복에 젖는 이 순간을, 이 자리를 저주하나이다!\""]], [["He hurried away through the thicket, and came to a stream flowing with the volume of a river between banks of masonry, broken at intervals by gated sluiceways.", "그는 덤불을 헤치고 서둘러 빠져나가, 돌로 쌓은 둑 사이로 강만 한 물줄기를 이루며 흐르는 시내에 이르렀다. 둑은 군데군데 수문이 달린 갑문으로 끊겨 있었다."], ["A bridge carried the path he was traversing across the stream; and, standing upon it, he saw other bridges, no two of them alike.", "그가 걸어가던 길을 다리 하나가 시내 위로 건네주었는데, 그 위에 서서 그는 다른 다리들을 보았으니, 어느 둘도 같은 것이 없었다. 발밑의 물은 그림자처럼 맑게 깊은 웅덩이를 이루어 고여 있었다."], ["Under him the water was lying in a deep pool, clear as a shadow; down a little way it tumbled with a roar over rocks; then there was another pool, and another cascade; and so on, out of view; and bridges and pools and resounding cascades said, plainly as inarticulate things can tell a story, the river was running by permission of a master, exactly as the master would have it, tractable as became a servant of the gods.", "조금 아래로 가면 바위 위로 굉음을 내며 쏟아져 내렸고, 그러고는 또 다른 웅덩이와 또 다른 폭포가 이어졌다. 그렇게 시야 밖으로까지 계속되었다. 다리와 웅덩이와 메아리치는 폭포들은, 말 못 하는 것들이 이야기를 들려줄 수 있는 한 더없이 또렷하게, 이 강이 한 주인의 허락 아래, 주인이 바라는 그대로, 신들의 종에 걸맞게 고분고분히 흐른다고 일러 주었다."]], [["Forward from the bridge he beheld a landscape of wide valleys and irregular heights, with groves and lakes and fanciful houses linked together by white paths and shining streams.", "다리에서 앞을 보니, 너른 골짜기들과 들쭉날쭉한 높은 지대가 펼쳐진 풍경이 눈에 들어왔다. 그 사이로 작은 숲과 호수와 기묘한 집들이 흰 오솔길과 반짝이는 시내로 이어져 있었다."], ["The valleys were spread below, that the river might be poured upon them for refreshment in days of drought, and they were as green carpets figured with beds and fields of flowers, and flecked with flocks of sheep white as balls of snow; and the voices of shepherds following the flocks were heard afar.", "골짜기들은 아래쪽에 펼쳐져 있어, 가뭄이 든 날 강물이 그곳에 부어져 생기를 줄 수 있게 되어 있었다. 골짜기들은 꽃밭과 꽃들판으로 무늬진 초록 양탄자 같았고, 눈송이처럼 흰 양 떼가 점점이 박혀 있었다. 양 떼를 따르는 목자들의 소리가 멀리서 들려왔다."], ["As if to tell him of the pious inscription of all he beheld, the altars out under the open sky seemed countless, each with a white-gowned figure attending it, while processions in white went slowly hither and thither between them; and the smoke of the altars half-risen hung collected in pale clouds over the devoted places.", "마치 그가 바라보는 모든 것에 깃든 경건한 헌사를 일러 주기라도 하듯, 탁 트인 하늘 아래 제단들은 헤아릴 수 없이 많아 보였고, 제단마다 흰 옷을 입은 형체가 시중을 들었으며, 흰 옷의 행렬들이 그 사이를 천천히 이리저리 오갔다. 절반쯤 피어오른 제단의 연기는 그 정성 어린 자리들 위에 옅은 구름으로 한데 엉겨 떠 있었다."]], [["Here, there, happy in flight, intoxicated in pause, from object to object, point to point, now in the meadow, now on the heights, now lingering to penetrate the groves and observe the processions, then lost in efforts to pursue the paths and streams which trended mazily into dim perspectives to end finally in— Ah, what might be a fitting end to scene so beautiful!", "여기서, 저기서, 날아오를 때는 행복하고 멈출 때는 도취되어, 이 사물에서 저 사물로, 이 지점에서 저 지점으로, 어느 때는 풀밭에서, 어느 때는 높은 곳에서, 어느 때는 머뭇거리며 숲속을 들여다보고 행렬을 살피다가, 또 어느 때는 어슴푸레한 원경 속으로 미로처럼 굽어들어 끝내 다다르는 그 길과 시내를 좇느라 정신을 잃었으니— 아, 이토록 아름다운 풍경에 어울리는 끝이란 어떤 것일까!"], ["What adequate mysteries were hidden behind an introduction so marvellous!", "이토록 경이로운 서막 뒤에는 어떤 합당한 신비가 감추어져 있을까!"], ["Here and there, the speech was beginning, his gaze wandered, so he could not help the conviction, forced by the view, and as the sum of it all, that there was peace in the air and on the earth, and invitation everywhere to come and lie down here and be at rest.", "여기서, 저기서, 무언의 말이 시작되고 있었고, 그의 시선은 떠돌았으니, 그는 그 풍경이 강요하는 확신을, 그 모든 것의 결론으로서, 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 곧 대기에도 땅에도 평화가 깃들어 있고, 어디서나 와서 여기 누워 쉬라는 청함이 있다는 확신이었다."]], [["Suddenly a revelation dawned upon him—the Grove was, in fact, a temple—one far-reaching, wall-less temple!", "문득 한 깨달음이 그에게 밝아 왔다. 이 숲이야말로 실은 신전이었다. 끝없이 뻗어 나간, 벽 없는 하나의 신전이었던 것이다!"]], [["Never anything like it!", "이런 것은 일찍이 없었다!"]], [["The architect had not stopped to pother about columns and porticos, proportions or interiors, or any limitation upon the epic he sought to materialize; he had simply made a servant of Nature—art can go no further.", "그 건축가는 기둥과 주랑, 비례나 내부, 또는 자신이 구현하려는 서사시에 얽매일 그 어떤 제약을 두고 골머리를 앓지 않았다. 그는 그저 자연을 종으로 삼았을 뿐이다. 예술은 그보다 더 멀리 나아갈 수 없다."], ["So the cunning son of Jupiter and Callisto built the old Arcadia; and in this, as in that, the genius was Greek.", "유피테르와 칼리스토의 영리한 아들이 옛 아르카디아를 지은 것도 그러했으니, 그때나 이때나 그 천재성은 그리스의 것이었다."]], [["From the bridge Ben-Hur went forward into the nearest valley.", "벤허는 다리에서 가장 가까운 골짜기로 나아갔다."]], [["He came to a flock of sheep.", "그는 양 떼에 이르렀다."], ["The shepherd was a girl, and she beckoned him, “Come!”", "목자는 한 소녀였는데, 그를 향해 손짓했다. \"오세요!\""]], [["Farther on, the path was divided by an altar—a pedestal of black gneiss, capped with a slab of white marble deftly foliated, and on that a brazier of bronze holding a fire.", "좀 더 나아가자, 길은 제단 하나로 갈라져 있었다. 검은 편마암 받침대 위에 솜씨 좋게 잎무늬를 새긴 흰 대리석 판을 얹고, 그 위에 불을 담은 청동 화로를 놓은 것이었다."], ["Close by it, a woman, seeing him, waved a wand of willow, and as he passed called him, “Stay!”", "그 가까이에서 한 여인이 그를 보고 버드나무 가지를 흔들며, 그가 지나갈 때 불렀다."], ["And the temptation in her smile was that of passionate youth.", "\"머무세요!\" 그 미소에 담긴 유혹은 정열에 찬 젊음의 것이었다."]], [["On yet further, he met one of the processions; at its head a troop of little girls, nude except as they were covered with garlands, piped their shrill voices into a song; then a troop of boys, also nude, their bodies deeply sun-browned, came dancing to the song of the girls; behind them the procession, all women, bearing baskets of spices and sweets to the altars—women clad in simple robes, careless of exposure.", "또 더 나아가, 그는 행렬 하나와 마주쳤다. 맨 앞에서는 화환만 둘렀을 뿐 벌거벗은 어린 소녀들의 무리가 째지는 목소리로 노래를 불었다. 이어서 역시 벌거벗은, 햇볕에 짙게 그을린 몸의 소년들 무리가 소녀들의 노래에 맞춰 춤추며 다가왔다."], ["As he went by they held their hands to him, and said, “Stay, and go with us.”", "그 뒤로 행렬을 이룬 것은 모두 여인들이었으니, 향료와 단것을 담은 바구니를 제단으로 나르고 있었다. 소박한 옷을 걸친 여인들은 몸이 드러나는 것에 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그가 지나갈 때 그들은 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["One, a Greek, sang a verse from Anacreon:", "\"머물러서 우리와 함께 가요.\" 그중 하나, 그리스 여인이 아나크레온의 시구를 노래했다."]], [["“For to-day I take or give; For to-day I drink and live; For to-day I beg or borrow; Who knows about the silent morrow?”", "\"오늘 나는 받고 또 주노라. 오늘 나는 마시고 또 사노라. 오늘 나는 빌고 또 꾸노라. 말 없는 내일을 그 누가 알리?\""]], [["But he pursued his way indifferent, and came next to a grove luxuriant, in the heart of the vale at the point where it would be most attractive to the observing eye.", "그러나 그는 무심히 제 길을 갔고, 이윽고 골짜기 한복판, 살피는 눈에 가장 매혹적으로 다가올 만한 지점에 이른 무성한 작은 숲에 다다랐다."], ["As it came close to the path he was travelling, there was a seduction in its shade, and through the foliage he caught the shining of what appeared a pretentious statue; so he turned aside, and entered the cool retreat.", "그 숲이 그가 가던 길에 바싹 닿아 있어, 그 그늘에는 끌어당기는 힘이 있었고, 잎새들 사이로 그는 거창한 조각상으로 보이는 무언가가 빛나는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 옆길로 들어, 그 시원한 은신처로 발을 들였다."]], [["The grass was fresh and clean.", "풀은 싱그럽고 깨끗했다."], ["The trees did not crowd each other; and they were of every kind native to the East, blended well with strangers adopted from far quarters; here grouped in exclusive companionship palm-trees plumed like queens; there sycamores, overtopping laurels of darker foliage; and evergreen oaks rising verdantly, with cedars vast enough to be kings on Lebanon; and mulberries; and terebinths so beautiful it is not hyperbole to speak of them as blown from the orchards of Paradise.", "나무들은 서로 비좁게 들어차 있지 않았고, 동방 토박이 온갖 수종이 먼 고장에서 들여온 낯선 나무들과 잘 어우러져 있었다. 여기에는 여왕처럼 깃을 단 야자나무들이 저희끼리 무리 지어 있었고, 저기에는 더 짙은 잎의 월계수를 굽어보는 무화과나무들이 있었으며, 푸르게 솟아오른 상록 떡갈나무들과, 레바논의 왕이라 할 만큼 거대한 삼나무들, 그리고 뽕나무들과, 낙원의 과수원에서 날려 왔다 해도 과장이 아닐 만큼 아름다운 테레빈나무들이 있었다."]], [["The statue proved to be a Daphne of wondrous beauty.", "그 조각상은 놀랍도록 아름다운 다프네였다."], ["Hardly, however, had he time to more than glance at her face: at the base of the pedestal a girl and a youth were lying upon a tiger’s skin asleep in each other’s arms; close by them the implements of their service—his axe and sickle, her basket—flung carelessly upon a heap of fading roses.", "그러나 그가 그 얼굴을 흘끗 본 것 이상의 겨를도 가지기 전에, 받침대 아래에서 한 소녀와 한 젊은이가 호랑이 가죽 위에 서로 껴안은 채 잠들어 있는 것이 눈에 들어왔다. 그들 곁에는 일에 쓰는 연장들이—그의 도끼와 낫, 그녀의 바구니가—시들어 가는 장미 더미 위에 아무렇게나 내던져져 있었다."]], [["The exposure startled him.", "그 드러난 광경에 그는 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Back in the hush of the perfumed thicket he discovered, as he thought, that the charm of the great Grove was peace without fear, and almost yielded to it; now, in this sleep in the day’s broad glare—this sleep at the feet of Daphne—he read a further chapter to which only the vaguest allusion is sufferable.", "향기 어린 덤불의 고요 속에서 그는, 자기 딴에는, 이 큰 숲의 매력이 두려움 없는 평화임을 깨달았고, 하마터면 거기에 굴복할 뻔했다. 그런데 이제, 한낮의 환한 빛 속에서의 이 잠, 다프네의 발치에서의 이 잠 속에서, 그는 더없이 어렴풋한 암시로밖에 입에 담을 수 없는 또 하나의 장(章)을 읽어 냈다."], ["The law of the place was Love, but Love without Law.", "이곳의 법은 사랑이었으나, 율법 없는 사랑이었다."]], [["And this was the sweet peace of Daphne!", "그런데 이것이 다프네의 그 달콤한 평화였다!"]], [["This the life’s end of her ministers!", "이것이 그 시중꾼들의 삶의 끝이었다!"]], [["For this kings and princes gave of their revenues!", "이것을 위해 왕과 제후들이 제 세입을 바쳤다!"]], [["For this a crafty priesthood subordinated nature—her birds and brooks and lilies, the river, the labor of many hands, the sanctity of altars, the fertile power of the sun!", "이것을 위해 교활한 사제 무리가 자연을—그 새들과 시내와 백합, 강, 수많은 손길의 노고, 제단의 거룩함, 햇빛의 기름진 힘을—종으로 굴복시켰다!"]], [["It would be pleasant now to record that as Ben-Hur pursued his walk assailed by such reflections, he yielded somewhat to sorrow for the votaries of the great outdoor temple; especially for those who, by personal service, kept it in a state so surpassingly lovely.", "벤허가 그런 사색에 시달리며 걸음을 이어 가는 동안, 이 거대한 야외 신전의 신도들을 향한, 특히 몸소 봉사하여 이 신전을 그토록 빼어나게 아름다운 상태로 지켜 온 자들을 향한 슬픔에 다소 마음이 기울었다고 적을 수 있다면 흐뭇한 일이리라."], ["How they came to the condition was not any longer a mystery; the motive, the influence, the inducement, were before him.", "그들이 어쩌다 그 처지에 이르렀는지는 더 이상 신비가 아니었으니, 그 동기와 영향과 유혹이 그의 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["Some there were, no doubt, caught by the promise held out to their troubled spirits of endless peace in a consecrated abode, to the beauty of which, if they had not money, they could contribute their labor; this class implied intellect peculiarly subject to hope and fear; but the great body of the faithful could not be classed with such.", "그중에는, 의심할 바 없이, 거룩히 봉헌된 처소에서의 끝없는 평화라는 약속에 시달린 영혼이 사로잡힌 자들도 있었다. 그 처소의 아름다움에, 돈이 없으면 노동으로나마 보탤 수 있었던 것이다. 이 부류는 유달리 희망과 두려움에 휘둘리기 쉬운 지성을 지녔음을 뜻했다."], ["Apollo’s nets were wide, and their meshes small; and hardly may one tell what all his fishermen landed: this less for that they cannot be described than because they ought not to be.", "그러나 그 신도들의 큰 무리는 그런 자들과 한데 묶을 수 없었다. 아폴론의 그물은 넓고 그 코는 촘촘했으니, 그 모든 어부들이 무엇을 끌어올렸는지 좀처럼 가려 말하기 어려웠다."], ["Enough that the mass were of the sybarites of the world, and of the herds in number vaster and in degree lower—devotees of the unmixed sensualism to which the East was almost wholly given.", "이는 그것을 묘사할 수 없어서라기보다, 묘사해서는 안 되기 때문이었다. 그 대다수가 세상의 향락주의자들이었고, 수에서는 더욱 방대하고 그 정도에서는 더욱 저열한 무리, 곧 동방이 거의 송두리째 빠져든 순전한 관능주의의 신봉자들이었다는 것만으로 충분하다."], ["Not to any of the exaltations—not to the singing-god, or his unhappy mistress; not to any philosophy requiring for its enjoyment the calm of retirement, nor to any service for the comfort there is in religion, nor to love in its holier sense—were they abiding their vows.", "어떤 드높은 경지에도—노래하는 신에게도, 그 불행한 연인에게도, 그 즐김을 위해 은둔의 고요를 요구하는 어떤 철학에도, 종교가 주는 위안을 위한 어떤 봉사에도, 더 거룩한 뜻에서의 사랑에도—그들은 제 서원을 두지 않았다."], ["Good reader, why shall not the truth be told here?", "선량한 독자여, 어찌하여 여기서 진실을 말하지 않으랴?"], ["Why not learn that, at this age, there were in all earth but two peoples capable of exaltations of the kind referred to—those who lived by the law of Moses, and those who lived by the law of Brahma.", "어찌하여 이를 깨닫지 않으랴. 이 시대에 온 땅을 통틀어 앞서 말한 종류의 드높은 경지에 이를 수 있는 백성은 오직 둘뿐이었으니, 모세의 율법으로 사는 자들과 브라마의 법으로 사는 자들이었다."], ["They alone could have cried you, Better a law without love than a love without law.", "그들만이 그대를 향해 이렇게 외칠 수 있었으리라. 율법 없는 사랑보다는 사랑 없는 율법이 낫다고."]], [["Besides that, sympathy is in great degree a result of the mood we are in at the moment: anger forbids the emotion.", "게다가 동정심이란 대개 우리가 그 순간에 처한 기분의 결과다. 분노는 그 감정을 가로막는다."], ["On the other hand, it is easiest taken on when we are in a state of most absolute self-satisfaction.", "반면에 동정심은 우리가 더없이 완전한 자기만족의 상태에 있을 때 가장 쉽게 일어난다."], ["Ben-Hur walked with a quicker step, holding his head higher; and, while not less sensitive to the delightfulness of all about him, he made his survey with calmer spirit, though sometimes with curling lip; that is to say, he could not so soon forget how nearly he himself had been imposed upon.", "벤허는 한층 빠른 걸음으로 걸으며 고개를 더 높이 쳐들었다. 그리고 주위의 모든 흐뭇함에 여전히 민감하면서도, 더 차분한 마음으로 둘러보았으니, 다만 이따금 입술을 비틀곤 했다. 다시 말해, 그는 자기 자신이 얼마나 그 속임수에 넘어갈 뻔했는지를 그리 쉬이 잊을 수가 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["In front of Ben-Hur there was a forest of cypress-trees, each a column tall and straight as a mast.", "벤허 앞에는 사이프러스 숲이 펼쳐져 있었으니, 나무마다 돛대처럼 곧고 키 큰 기둥이었다."], ["Venturing into the shady precinct, he heard a trumpet gayly blown, and an instant after saw lying upon the grass close by the countryman whom he had run upon in the road going to the temples.", "그늘진 영역으로 들어서던 그는 흥겹게 울리는 나팔 소리를 듣더니, 곧이어 신전으로 가는 길에서 마주쳤던 그 시골 사람이 풀밭에 바로 가까이 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The man arose, and came to him.", "그 사내는 일어나 그에게로 다가왔다."]], [["“I give you peace again,”", "“다시 평화를 빕니다.”"], ["he said, pleasantly.", "그가 상냥하게 말했다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "“고맙소.”"], ["Ben-Hur replied, then asked, “Go you my way?”", "벤허가 대답하고는 물었다. “나와 같은 길로 가시오?”"]], [["“I am for the stadium, if that is your way.”", "“경기장으로 가는 길입니다. 당신도 그쪽이라면 말이지요.”"]], [["“The stadium!”", "“경기장이라!”"]], [["“Yes. The trumpet you heard but now was a call for the competitors.”", "“그렇습니다. 방금 들으신 나팔은 경기자들을 부르는 신호였습니다.”"]], [["“Good friend,”", "“좋은 벗이여,”"], ["said Ben-Hur, frankly, “I admit my ignorance of the Grove; and if you will let me be your follower, I will be glad.”", "벤허가 솔직하게 말했다. “나는 이 숲을 잘 모른다는 것을 고백하오. 그대를 길잡이로 삼게 해 준다면 기쁘겠소.”"]], [["“That will delight me. Hark!", "“그것이야말로 제겐 기쁨이지요."], ["I hear the wheels of the chariots.", "들어 보십시오! 전차 바퀴 소리가 들립니다."], ["They are taking the track.”", "주로(走路)로 들어서고 있군요.”"]], [["Ben-Hur listened a moment, then completed the introduction by laying his hand upon the man’s arm, and saying, “I am the son of Arrius, the duumvir, and thou?”", "벤허는 잠시 귀를 기울이더니, 그 사내의 팔에 손을 얹으며 소개를 마무리했다. “나는 두움비르 아리우스의 아들이오. 그대는?” * 두움비르: 고대 로마의 두 사람으로 이루어진 관직."]], [["“I am Malluch, a merchant of Antioch.”", "“저는 말루크라고 하며, 안티오크의 상인입니다.”"]], [["“Well, good Malluch, the trumpet, and the gride of wheels, and the prospect of diversion excite me.", "“좋소, 착한 말루크여. 나팔 소리며, 삐걱대는 바퀴 소리며, 즐거운 구경거리를 기대하니 마음이 들뜨는구려."], ["I have some skill in the exercises.", "나도 운동에는 제법 솜씨가 있소."], ["In the palaestrae of Rome I am not unknown.", "로마의 팔라이스트라에서는 이름이 알려지지 않은 것도 아니라오."], ["Let us to the course.”", "자, 주로로 갑시다.”"]], [["Malluch lingered to say, quickly, “The duumvir was a Roman, yet I see his son in the garments of a Jew.”", "말루크는 머뭇거리며 얼른 말했다. “두움비르는 로마 사람인데, 그 아들은 유대인의 옷을 입고 있군요.”"]], [["“The noble Arrius was my father by adoption,”", "“고귀한 아리우스는 양아버지이시오.”"], ["Ben-Hur answered.", "벤허가 대답했다."]], [["“Ah! I see, and beg pardon.”", "“아! 그렇군요. 용서를 빕니다.”"]], [["Passing through the belt of forest, they came to a field with a track laid out upon it, in shape and extent exactly like those of the stadia.", "숲의 띠를 지나자, 두 사람은 주로가 마련된 들판에 이르렀다. 그 모양과 크기는 경기장의 그것과 똑같았다."], ["The course, or track proper, was of soft earth, rolled and sprinkled, and on both sides defined by ropes, stretched loosely upon upright javelins.", "주로, 곧 본래의 달리는 길은 부드러운 흙을 다지고 물을 뿌려 놓은 것이었고, 양쪽에는 곧추세운 창에 느슨히 매단 밧줄로 경계가 그어져 있었다."], ["For the accommodation of spectators, and such as had interests reaching forward of the mere practise, there were several stands shaded by substantial awnings, and provided with seats in rising rows.", "구경꾼들과, 단순한 연습 이상의 관심을 품은 자들을 위해, 든든한 차일을 드리운 관람대가 여럿 마련되어 있었으니, 자리는 층층이 올라가는 줄로 갖춰져 있었다."], ["In one of the stands the two new-comers found places.", "그 관람대 가운데 하나에서 두 새내기는 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Ben-Hur counted the chariots as they went by—nine in all.", "벤허는 전차들이 지나가는 대로 그 수를 헤아렸다—모두 아홉이었다."]], [["“I commend the fellows,”", "“저 사람들은 칭찬받을 만하군.”"], ["he said, with good-will.", "그가 호의를 담아 말했다."], ["“Here in the East, I thought they aspired to nothing better than the two; but they are ambitious, and play with royal fours.", "“여기 동방에서는 둘 이상은 바라지도 않는 줄 알았는데, 야심을 품고 왕가에나 어울릴 네 마리짜리를 다루고 있구려."], ["Let us study their performance.”", "저들의 솜씨를 살펴봅시다.”"]], [["Eight of the fours passed the stand, some walking, others on the trot, and all unexceptionably handled; then the ninth one came on the gallop.", "네 마리짜리 가운데 여덟은 관람대를 지나갔는데, 더러는 걷고 더러는 종종걸음을 쳤으며, 하나같이 나무랄 데 없이 다루어지고 있었다. 그러더니 아홉 번째 것이 질주해 왔다."], ["Ben-Hur burst into exclamation.", "벤허는 절로 탄성을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“I have been in the stables of the emperor, Malluch, but, by our father Abraham of blessed memory!", "“말루크, 나는 황제의 마구간에도 들어가 보았지만, 복되신 우리 조상 아브라함을 두고 맹세하건대!"], ["I never saw the like of these.”", "저런 것들은 본 적이 없소.”"]], [["The last four was then sweeping past.", "마지막 네 마리가 그때 휙 지나가고 있었다."], ["All at once they fell into confusion.", "별안간 그것들이 어지럽게 흐트러졌다."], ["Some one on the stand uttered a sharp cry.", "관람대에서 누군가 날카롭게 소리쳤다."], ["Ben-Hur turned, and saw an old man half-risen from an upper seat, his hands clenched and raised, his eyes fiercely bright, his long white beard fairly quivering.", "벤허가 돌아보니, 한 노인이 윗자리에서 반쯤 일어서 있었다. 두 손은 움켜쥔 채 치켜들었고, 눈은 사납게 번뜩였으며, 길고 흰 수염은 그야말로 부들부들 떨고 있었다."], ["Some of the spectators nearest him began to laugh.", "그에게 가까이 있던 구경꾼들 가운데 몇이 웃기 시작했다."]], [["“They should respect his beard at least. Who is he?”", "“적어도 저 수염만은 존중해야 할 텐데. 저 사람은 누구요?”"], ["asked Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 물었다."]], [["“A mighty man from the Desert, somewhere beyond Moab, and owner of camels in herds, and horses descended, they say, from the racers of the first Pharaoh—Sheik Ilderim by name and title.”", "“사막에서 온 위세 등등한 분이지요. 모압 너머 어딘가에서 왔는데, 낙타를 떼로 거느리고, 듣자 하니 첫 파라오의 경주마에서 내려온 혈통의 말들을 가진 분이랍니다—이름과 직함은 일데림 족장이지요.”"]], [["Thus Malluch replied.", "말루크는 그렇게 대답했다."]], [["The driver meanwhile exerted himself to quiet the four, but without avail.", "그러는 동안 마부는 네 마리를 진정시키려 애썼으나 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["Each ineffectual effort excited the sheik the more.", "헛된 노력이 거듭될 때마다 족장은 더욱 격앙되었다."]], [["“Abaddon seize him!”", "“아바돈이 저놈을 잡아가기를!”"], ["yelled the patriarch, shrilly. “Run! fly!", "노족장이 새된 소리로 고함쳤다. “달려라! 날아가라!"], ["do you hear, my children?”", "듣고 있느냐, 내 아이들아?”"], ["The question was to his attendants, apparently of the tribe.", "그 물음은 제 부족의 일원인 듯한 시종들을 향한 것이었다."], ["“Do you hear?", "“듣고 있느냐?"], ["They are Desert-born, like yourselves.", "저것들은 너희와 마찬가지로 사막에서 태어났다."], ["Catch them—quick!”", "잡아라—어서!”"]], [["The plunging of the animals increased.", "짐승들의 날뜀이 더해 갔다."]], [["“Accursed Roman!”", "“저주받을 로마 놈!”"], ["and the sheik shook his fist at the driver.", "족장이 마부를 향해 주먹을 휘둘렀다."], ["“Did he not swear he could drive them—swear it by all his brood of bastard Latin gods?", "“저놈이 몰 수 있다고 맹세하지 않았더냐—제 잡종 라틴 신들을 모조리 걸고 맹세하지 않았더냐?"], ["Nay, hands off me—off, I say!", "아니, 내게서 손 떼라—떼라니까!"], ["They should run swift as eagles, and with the temper of hand-bred lambs, he swore.", "독수리처럼 빠르게 달리되, 손으로 기른 양처럼 순한 성미라고 저놈이 맹세했다."], ["Cursed be he—cursed the mother of liars who calls him son!", "저주받을 놈—저놈을 자식이라 부르는 거짓말쟁이의 어미마저 저주받기를!"], ["See them, the priceless! Let him touch one of them with a lash, and”—the rest of the sentence was lost in a furious grinding of his teeth.", "저것들을 보라, 값을 매길 수 없는 것들을! 저놈이 채찍으로 한 마리라도 건드리기만 한다면, 그러면”—나머지 말은 격렬한 이갈이 속에 묻혀 버렸다."], ["“To their heads, some of you, and speak them—a word, one is enough, from the tent-song your mothers sang you.", "“너희 가운데 몇은 저것들의 머리로 가서, 말을 걸어라—한마디면 된다, 너희 어미가 들려주던 천막의 노래에서 한 구절이면 충분하다."], ["Oh, fool, fool that I was to put trust in a Roman!”", "오, 어리석구나, 로마 놈을 믿다니 내가 어리석었다!”"]], [["Some of the shrewder of the old man’s friends planted themselves between him and the horses.", "노인의 벗들 가운데 좀 더 약삭빠른 자들이 그와 말 사이에 끼어들어 섰다."], ["An opportune failure of breath on his part helped the stratagem.", "마침맞게 노인의 숨이 가빠진 것이 그 꾀를 도왔다."]], [["Ben-Hur, thinking he comprehended the sheik, sympathized with him.", "벤허는 족장의 심정을 헤아렸다고 여기며, 그에게 공감했다."], ["Far more than mere pride of property—more than anxiety for the result of the race—in his view it was within the possible for the patriarch, according to his habits of thought and his ideas of the inestimable, to love such animals with a tenderness akin to the most sensitive passion.", "단순한 소유물에 대한 자랑을 훨씬 넘어서—경주 결과에 대한 근심을 훨씬 넘어서—그가 보기에, 노족장이 제 사고방식과 값을 매길 수 없는 것에 대한 관념에 따라, 그토록 예민한 격정에 버금가는 애틋함으로 그런 짐승들을 사랑하는 것은 충분히 있을 법한 일이었다."]], [["They were all bright bays, unspotted, perfectly matched, and so proportioned as to seem less than they really were.", "그것들은 모두 빛나는 밤색이었고, 얼룩 하나 없었으며, 완벽하게 짝지어졌고, 균형이 어찌나 잘 잡혔는지 실제보다 작아 보일 정도였다. 가냘픈 귀는 작은 머리 위로 쫑긋했고, 얼굴은 두 눈 사이가 넓고 도톰했다. 콧구멍은 벌어지면 그 속의 막이 어찌나 짙붉은지 불길이 번뜩이는 것 같았다."], ["Delicate ears pointed small heads; the faces were broad and full between the eyes; the nostrils in expansion disclosed membrane so deeply red as to suggest the flashing of flame; the necks were arches, overlaid with fine mane so abundant as to drape the shoulders and breast, while in happy consonance the forelocks were like ravellings of silken veils; between the knees and the fetlocks the legs were flat as an open hand, but above the knees they were rounded with mighty muscles, needful to upbear the shapely close-knit bodies; the hoofs were like cups of polished agate; and in rearing and plunging they whipped the air, and sometimes the earth, with tails glossy-black and thick and long.", "목은 활처럼 휘었고, 그 위에 얹힌 고운 갈기는 어찌나 풍성한지 어깨와 가슴을 휘장처럼 덮었으며, 거기에 어울리게 앞머리 갈기는 비단 너울을 풀어 놓은 실오라기 같았다. 무릎과 발목 사이 다리는 펼친 손바닥처럼 납작했으나, 무릎 위로는 우람한 근육으로 둥글게 솟아 있었으니, 잘 빠지고 야무진 몸을 떠받치는 데 그 근육이 꼭 필요했다. 발굽은 잘 닦은 마노 잔 같았고, 몸을 일으키고 날뛸 때면 윤기 흐르는 새까맣고 굵고 긴 꼬리로 허공을, 때로는 땅을 후려쳤다."], ["The sheik spoke of them as the priceless, and it was a good saying.", "족장이 그것들을 값을 매길 수 없는 것이라 부른 것은 옳은 말이었다."]], [["In this second and closer look at the horses, Ben-Hur read the story of their relation to their master.", "말들을 이렇게 두 번째로 더 가까이 들여다보면서, 벤허는 그것들과 주인 사이의 사연을 읽어 냈다."], ["They had grown up under his eyes, objects of his special care in the day, his visions of pride in the night, with his family at home in the black tent out on the shadeless bosom of the desert, as his children beloved.", "그것들은 그의 눈길 아래 자랐으니, 낮에는 그가 각별히 보살피는 대상이었고, 밤에는 그가 자랑스레 꿈꾸는 환영이었다. 그늘 한 점 없는 사막의 품에 친 검은 천막에서 가족과 더불어 살아온 그에게, 그것들은 사랑하는 자식과도 같았다."], ["That they might win him a triumph over the haughty and hated Roman, the old man had brought his loves to the city, never doubting they would win, if only he could find a trusty expert to take them in hand; not merely one with skill, but of a spirit which their spirits would acknowledge.", "거만하고 미운 로마 놈을 누르고 승리를 안겨 줄 수만 있다면, 노인은 제 사랑하는 것들을 도시로 데려왔으니, 믿을 만한 전문가의 손에 맡기기만 한다면 반드시 이기리라는 것을 한 번도 의심하지 않았다. 한낱 솜씨만 지닌 자가 아니라, 그것들의 기개가 알아볼 만한 기개를 지닌 자라야 했다."], ["Unlike the colder people of the West, he could not protest the driver’s inability, and dismiss him civilly; an Arab and a sheik, he had to explode, and rive the air about him with clamor.", "그는 서방의 더 냉정한 사람들과는 달리, 마부의 무능을 점잖게 따져 묻고 정중히 내보낼 수가 없었다. 아랍인이자 족장인 그는 폭발할 수밖에 없었고, 고함으로 제 주위의 공기를 갈가리 찢어 놓을 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Before the patriarch was done with his expletives, a dozen hands were at the bits of the horses, and their quiet assured.", "노족장이 그 욕설을 다 쏟아 내기도 전에, 열 몇 개의 손이 말들의 재갈에 달라붙어 그것들을 가라앉혔다."], ["About that time, another chariot appeared upon the track; and, unlike the others, driver, vehicle, and races were precisely as they would be presented in the Circus the day of final trial.", "그 무렵 또 다른 전차 하나가 주로에 나타났다. 그것은 다른 것들과 달리, 마부도, 수레도, 말도 결승전 당일 원형 경기장에 나설 모습 그대로였다."], ["For a reason which will presently be more apparent, it is desirable now to give this turnout plainly to the reader.", "곧 분명해질 까닭으로, 이제 이 한 벌을 독자에게 또렷이 그려 보이는 것이 바람직하다."]], [["There should be no difficulty in understanding the carriage known to us all as the chariot of classical renown.", "우리 모두 고전의 명성으로 익히 아는 그 전차를 이해하는 데에는 아무런 어려움도 없을 것이다."], ["One has but to picture to himself a dray with low wheels and broad axle, surmounted by a box open at the tail end.", "바퀴가 낮고 굴대가 넓은 짐수레를 떠올리되, 그 위에 뒤쪽이 트인 상자가 얹힌 모습을 그려 보기만 하면 된다."], ["Such was the primitive pattern.", "그것이 원시적인 본보기였다."], ["Artistic genius came along in time, and, touching the rude machine, raised it into a thing of beauty—that, for instance, in which Aurora, riding in advance of the dawn, is given to our fancy.", "때가 이르러 예술적 재능이 나타나, 이 투박한 기계를 손보아 아름다운 물건으로 끌어올렸다—이를테면, 새벽에 앞서 달리는 오로라가 타고 있다고 우리 상상이 그리는 그런 것 말이다."]], [["The jockeys of the ancients, quite as shrewd and ambitious as their successors of the present, called their humblest turnout a two, and their best in grade a four; in the latter, they contested the Olympics and the other festal shows founded in imitation of them.", "옛 사람들의 마부들은, 오늘날의 후예들 못지않게 약삭빠르고 야심만만하여, 가장 소박한 한 벌을 둘이라 불렀고, 등급이 가장 높은 것을 넷이라 불렀다. 후자로 그들은 올림픽 경기와, 그것을 본떠 세운 다른 축제 경기들을 겨루었다."]], [["The same sharp gamesters preferred to put their horses to the chariot all abreast; and for distinction they termed the two next the pole yoke-steeds, and those on the right and left outside trace-mates.", "같은 영리한 도박꾼들은 말들을 전차에 모두 가로로 나란히 매는 것을 좋아했다. 그래서 구별하기 위해, 채에 가장 가까운 둘을 멍에말이라 일컫고, 좌우 바깥쪽의 것들을 곁말이라 일컬었다."], ["It was their judgment, also, that, by allowing the fullest freedom of action, the greatest speed was attainable; accordingly, the harness resorted to was peculiarly simple; in fact, there was nothing of it save a collar round the animal’s neck, and a trace fixed to the collar, unless the lines and a halter fall within the term.", "또한 그들의 판단으로는, 행동의 자유를 한껏 허락하면 가장 빠른 속도를 얻을 수 있었다. 그리하여 쓰이는 마구는 유달리 단순했으니, 사실 짐승의 목에 두른 멍에띠와, 그 멍에띠에 매단 끄는 줄 말고는 아무것도 없었다—고삐와 굴레까지 그 안에 넣지 않는다면 말이다."], ["Wanting to hitch up, the masters pinned a narrow wooden yoke, or cross-tree, near the end of the pole, and, by straps passed through rings at the end of the yoke, buckled the latter to the collar.", "매어 달려고 할 때면, 주인들은 좁은 나무 멍에, 곧 가로목을 채 끝 가까이에 꽂고, 멍에 끝의 고리에 끈을 꿰어 그것을 멍에띠에 죄어 맸다."], ["The traces of the yokesteeds they hitched to the axle; those of the trace-mates to the top rim of the chariot-bed.", "멍에말의 끄는 줄은 굴대에 매고, 곁말의 끄는 줄은 전차 바닥의 윗테에 맸다."], ["There remained then but the adjustment of the lines, which, judged by the modern devices, was not the least curious part of the method.", "그러고 나면 남은 것은 고삐를 잡는 일뿐인데, 이는 현대의 장치에 견주어 보면 그 방식 가운데 가장 묘한 부분이라 할 만했다."], ["For this there was a large ring at the forward extremity of the pole; securing the ends to that ring first, they parted the lines so as to give one to each horse, and proceeded to pass them to the driver, slipping them separately through rings on the inner side of the halters at the mouth.", "이를 위해 채의 앞쪽 끝에 큰 고리 하나가 달려 있었다. 먼저 고삐의 끝을 그 고리에 매고, 고삐를 갈라 말마다 하나씩 가게 한 다음, 입가의 굴레 안쪽에 달린 고리에 하나하나 따로 꿰어서 마부에게 넘겼다."]], [["With this plain generalization in mind, all further desirable knowledge upon the subject can be had by following the incidents of the scene occurring.", "이 간략한 설명을 마음에 새겨 두면, 이 주제에 관해 더 알고 싶은 것은 모두 지금 벌어지는 장면의 사건들을 좇음으로써 얻을 수 있다."]], [["The other contestants had been received in silence; the last comer was more fortunate.", "다른 경기자들은 잠잠한 가운데 맞아들여졌지만, 마지막에 온 자는 더 운이 좋았다."], ["While moving towards the stand from which we are viewing the scene, his progress was signalized by loud demonstrations, by clapping of hands and cheers, the effect of which was to centre attention upon him exclusively.", "우리가 이 장면을 바라보고 있는 관람대 쪽으로 나아갈 때, 그의 행차는 요란한 표시로—박수와 환호로 알려졌으니, 그 결과 사람들의 시선이 오로지 그에게로 쏠렸다."], ["His yoke-steeds, it was observed, were black, while the trace-mates were snow-white.", "그의 멍에말은 검고, 곁말은 눈처럼 희다는 것이 눈에 띄었다."], ["In conformity to the exacting canons of Roman taste, they had all four been mutilated; that is to say, their tails had been clipped, and, to complete the barbarity, their shorn manes were divided into knots tied with flaring red and yellow ribbons.", "까다로운 로마 취향의 규범에 따라, 네 마리 모두 손질을 당했으니, 다시 말해 꼬리는 잘렸고, 야만을 마무리하려는 듯 깎인 갈기는 매듭으로 갈라, 화려한 빨강과 노랑 리본으로 묶여 있었다."]], [["In advancing, the stranger at length reached a point where the chariot came into view from the stand, and its appearance would of itself have justified the shouting.", "나아가면서 이방인은 마침내 전차가 관람대에서 보이는 지점에 이르렀는데, 그 모습만으로도 환호는 마땅한 것이었다."], ["The wheels were very marvels of construction.", "바퀴는 그야말로 만듦새의 경이였다."], ["Stout bands of burnished bronze reinforced the hubs, otherwise very light; the spokes were sections of ivory tusks, set in with the natural curve outward to perfect the dishing, considered important then as now; bronze tires held the fellies, which were of shining ebony.", "윤내어 광낸 청동의 든든한 테가 바퀴통을 보강했으니, 그러지 않았다면 바퀴통은 무척 가벼웠을 것이다. 바퀴살은 상아 엄니의 토막으로, 본디 휜 모양을 바깥으로 향하게 끼워 넣어 바퀴의 곡면을 완성했으니, 이는 예나 지금이나 중요하게 여겨지는 것이었다. 청동 테가 빛나는 흑단으로 된 바퀴 둘레를 붙들었다."], ["The axle, in keeping with the wheels, was tipped with heads of snarling tigers done in brass, and the bed was woven of willow wands gilded with gold.", "굴대는 바퀴와 어울리게, 놋쇠로 빚은 으르렁대는 호랑이 머리로 양 끝을 장식했고, 바닥은 금박을 입힌 버드나무 가지로 엮었다."]], [["The coming of the beautiful horses and resplendent chariot drew Ben-Hur to look at the driver with increased interest.", "아름다운 말들과 눈부신 전차가 다가오자, 벤허는 더욱 큰 관심을 품고 마부를 바라보았다."]], [["Who was he?", "그는 누구인가?"]], [["When Ben-Hur asked himself the question first, he could not see the man’s face, or even his full figure; yet the air and manner were familiar, and pricked him keenly with a reminder of a period long gone.", "벤허가 처음 그 물음을 스스로에게 던졌을 때, 그는 마부의 얼굴은커녕 온전한 모습조차 볼 수 없었다. 그런데도 그 분위기와 거동이 낯익어, 오래전 지나간 한 시절을 날카롭게 일깨우며 그를 찔렀다."]], [["Who could it be?", "대체 누구일까?"]], [["Nearer now, and the horses approaching at a trot.", "이제 더 가까워졌고, 말들은 종종걸음으로 다가오고 있었다."], ["From the shouting and the gorgeousness of the turnout, it was thought he might be some official favorite or famous prince.", "환호와 화려한 한 벌로 미루어, 그는 어떤 권세 있는 자의 총신이거나 이름난 왕자일지도 모른다고 여겨졌다."], ["Such an appearance was not inconsistent with exalted rank.", "그런 모습은 드높은 신분과 어긋나지 않았다."], ["Kings often struggled for the crown of leaves which was the prize of victory.", "왕들도 승리의 상인 월계관을 얻으려 종종 다투었다."], ["Nero and Commodus, it will be remembered, devoted themselves to the chariot.", "기억하다시피, 네로와 콤모두스도 전차 경주에 몸을 바쳤다."], ["Ben-Hur arose and forced a passage down nearly to the railing in front of the lower seat of the stand. His face was earnest, his manner eager.", "벤허는 일어나, 관람대 맨 아래 자리 앞 난간 가까이까지 헤치고 내려갔다. 그의 얼굴은 진지했고, 그 태도는 간절했다."]], [["And directly the whole person of the driver was in view.", "그러더니 곧 마부의 온전한 모습이 시야에 들어왔다."], ["A companion rode with him, in classic description a Myrtilus, permitted men of high estate indulging their passion for the race-course.", "한 동행이 그와 함께 타고 있었으니, 고전의 표현으로 미르틸루스라 하여, 경주에 대한 열정을 만끽하는 고귀한 신분의 사람들에게 허락된 자였다."], ["Ben-Hur could see only the driver, standing erect in the chariot, with the reins passed several times round his body—a handsome figure, scantily covered by a tunic of light-red cloth; in the right hand a whip; in the other, the arm raised and lightly extended, the four lines.", "벤허의 눈에는 오직 마부만이 보였다. 그는 전차 안에 꼿꼿이 서서, 고삐를 제 몸에 여러 번 둘러 감고 있었다—옅은 붉은 천의 짧은 옷으로 간신히 가린 늠름한 모습이었다. 오른손에는 채찍을 들었고, 다른 손은 들어 가볍게 뻗은 채 네 가닥 고삐를 잡고 있었다."], ["The pose was exceedingly graceful and animated.", "그 자세는 더없이 우아하고 생기 넘쳤다."], ["The cheers and clapping of hands were received with statuesque indifference.", "환호와 박수도 그는 조각상 같은 무심함으로 받아넘겼다."], ["Ben-Hur stood transfixed—his instinct and memory had served him faithfully—THE DRIVER WAS MESSALA.", "벤허는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 섰다—그의 직감과 기억은 그를 충실히 도왔다—그 마부는 메살라였다."]], [["By the selection of horses, the magnificence of the chariot, the attitude, and display of person—above all, by the expression of the cold, sharp, eagle features, imperialized in his countrymen by sway of the world through so many generations, Ben-Hur knew Messala unchanged, as haughty, confident, and audacious as ever, the same in ambition, cynicism, and mocking insouciance.", "말의 선택, 전차의 호화로움, 자세, 그리고 몸을 드러내는 자태로—무엇보다 차갑고 날카로운 독수리 같은 이목구비의 표정으로, 그 표정은 수많은 세대에 걸쳐 세계를 호령하며 그의 동족에게 제왕의 기품을 새겨 놓은 것이었으니, 벤허는 메살라가 변함없음을 알아보았다. 여전히 거만하고, 자신만만하고, 대담하며, 야심과 냉소와 비웃는 듯한 태평함에서도 한결같았다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# CHAPTER VIII"]], [["As Ben-Hur descended the steps of the stand, an Arab arose upon the last one at the foot, and cried out,", "벤허가 관람대 계단을 내려설 때, 한 아랍인이 맨 아래 계단에 올라서서 외쳤다."]], [["“Men of the East and West—hearken!", "\"동방과 서방의 사람들이여, 들으시오!"], ["The good Sheik Ilderim giveth greeting.", "선량하신 일데림 족장께서 인사를 전하시오."], ["With four horses, sons of the favorites of Solomon the Wise, he hath come up against the best.", "지혜로운 솔로몬이 아끼던 명마의 후손인 네 마리 말을 데리고, 그분은 최고를 상대하러 나오셨소."], ["Needs he most a mighty man to drive them. Whoso will take them to his satisfaction, to him he promiseth enrichment forever.", "그분께 가장 필요한 것은 그 말들을 몰 수 있는 강건한 사람이오. 그분이 흡족하실 만큼 말들을 몰아 줄 자에게는, 그분이 평생토록 부를 약속하시오."], ["Here—there—in the city and in the Circuses, and wherever the strong most do congregate, tell ye this his offer.", "여기서나 저기서나, 도성에서나 경기장에서나, 강한 자들이 모이는 곳이라면 어디서든 그분의 이 제안을 전하시오."], ["So saith my master, Sheik Ilderim the Generous.”", "이것이 나의 주인이신 너그러운 일데림 족장의 말씀이오.\""]], [["The proclamation awakened a great buzz among the people under the awning.", "이 포고는 차일 아래 모인 사람들 사이에 큰 술렁임을 일으켰다."], ["By night it would be repeated and discussed in all the sporting circles of Antioch.", "밤이 되면 안티오크의 모든 경기 애호가 모임에서 되풀이되고 논의될 것이었다."], ["Ben-Hur, hearing it, stopped and looked hesitatingly from the herald to the sheik.", "그 말을 들은 벤허는 걸음을 멈추고, 전령에게서 족장에게로 망설이듯 눈길을 옮겼다."], ["Malluch thought he was about to accept the offer, but was relieved when he presently turned to him, and asked, “Good Malluch, where to now?”", "말루크는 그가 제안을 받아들이려는 줄 알았으나, 이내 벤허가 자기에게 돌아서며 묻자 안심했다. \"여보게 말루크, 이제 어디로 가지?\""]], [["The worthy replied, with a laugh, “Would you liken yourself to others visiting the Grove for the first time, you will straightway to hear your fortune told.”", "선량한 사내는 웃으며 대답했다. \"처음으로 이 숲을 찾는 다른 사람들을 본받으시려거든, 곧장 점괘를 들으러 가셔야지요.\""]], [["“My fortune, said you?", "\"점괘라고 했나?"], ["Though the suggestion has in it a flavor of unbelief, let us to the goddess at once.”", "그 제안에 믿음 없는 자의 냄새가 풍기긴 하네만, 어쨌든 당장 여신에게로 가세나.\""]], [["“Nay, son of Arrius, these Apollonians have a better trick than that.", "\"아닙니다, 아리우스의 아드님, 이곳 아폴로 신전 사람들은 그보다 더 그럴듯한 재주를 부립니다."], ["Instead of speech with a Pythia or a Sibyl, they will sell you a plain papyrus leaf, hardly dry from the stalk, and bid you dip it in the water of a certain fountain, when it will show you a verse in which you may hear of your future.”", "피티아나 시빌에게 말을 듣게 하는 대신, 줄기에서 막 잘라내어 아직 마르지도 않은 밋밋한 파피루스 잎 한 장을 팔지요. 그러고는 어느 샘의 물에 담그라고 합니다. 그러면 그 위에 시 한 구절이 떠올라, 거기서 자기 미래를 들을 수 있다는 겁니다.\""]], [["The glow of interest departed from Ben-Hur’s face.", "벤허의 얼굴에서 흥미의 빛이 사라졌다."]], [["“There are people who have no need to vex themselves about their future,”", "\"세상에는 자기 미래를 두고 마음 졸일 필요가 없는 사람도 있는 법이지.\""], ["he said, gloomily.", "그가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“Then you prefer to go to the temples?”", "\"그럼 신전 쪽으로 가시는 편이 낫겠습니까?\""]], [["“The temples are Greek, are they not?”", "\"신전들은 그리스식 아닌가?\""]], [["“They call them Greek.”", "\"그리스식이라고들 부르지요.\""]], [["“The Hellenes were masters of the beautiful in art; but in architecture they sacrificed variety to unbending beauty.", "\"헬레네족은 예술에서 아름다움의 대가였지. 하지만 건축에서는 다양함을 굽힐 줄 모르는 아름다움에 희생시켰어."], ["Their temples are all alike.", "그들의 신전은 죄다 똑같다네."], ["How call you the fountain?” “Castalia.”", "그 샘은 뭐라 부르나?\""]], [["“Oh! it has repute throughout the world.", "\"카스탈리아라고 합니다.\" \"오! 온 세상에 이름난 곳이지."], ["Let us thither.”", "그리로 가세.\""]], [["Malluch kept watch on his companion as they went, and saw that for the moment at least his good spirits were out.", "말루크는 가는 동안 길동무를 살폈고, 적어도 지금만큼은 그의 좋던 기분이 가셨음을 알아차렸다."], ["To the people passing he gave no attention; over the wonders they came upon there were no exclamations; silently, even sullenly, he kept a slow pace.", "지나가는 사람들에게 그는 눈길도 주지 않았다. 마주치는 신기한 것들 앞에서도 탄성 한 번 내지 않았다. 그는 말없이, 시무룩하게까지, 느린 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["The truth was, the sight of Messala had set Ben-Hur to thinking.", "사실 메살라를 본 것이 벤허를 깊은 생각에 빠뜨린 것이었다."], ["It seemed scarce an hour ago that the strong hands had torn him from his mother, scarce an hour ago that the Roman had put seal upon the gates of his father’s house.", "그 억센 손들이 자기를 어머니에게서 떼어내던 것이 한 시간도 채 지나지 않은 듯했고, 그 로마인이 아버지의 집 대문에 봉인을 찍던 것도 한 시간이 채 안 된 듯했다."], ["He recounted how, in the hopeless misery of the life—if such it might be called—in the galleys, he had had little else to do, aside from labor, than dream dreams of vengeance, in all of which Messala was the principal.", "그는 떠올렸다. 노예선에서의—그런 것을 삶이라 부를 수 있다면—그 희망 없는 비참 속에서, 노동을 제외하면 복수의 꿈을 꾸는 일밖에 달리 할 게 없었고, 그 모든 꿈에서 메살라가 주역이었음을."], ["There might be, he used to say to himself, escape for Gratus, but for Messala—never!", "그는 자신에게 늘 말하곤 했다. 그라투스는 빠져나갈 수도 있겠지만, 메살라만은 결코 안 된다고."], ["And to strengthen and harden his resolution, he was accustomed to repeat over and over, Who pointed us out to the persecutors?", "그리고 결심을 다지고 단단히 하려고, 그는 거듭거듭 되뇌곤 했다. 박해자들에게 우리를 가리켜 보인 자가 누구였던가?"], ["And when I begged him for help—not for myself—who mocked me, and went away laughing?", "내가 그에게 도움을 청했을 때—나 자신을 위해서가 아니었거늘—나를 비웃고 껄껄 웃으며 가 버린 자가 누구였던가?"], ["And always the dream had the same ending.", "그리고 그 꿈은 언제나 똑같이 끝났다."], ["The day I meet him, help me, thou good God of my people!—help me to some fitting special vengeance!", "그를 만나는 날, 저를 도우소서, 제 백성의 선하신 하느님이여!—저에게 합당하고도 각별한 복수를 베풀어 주소서!"]], [["And now the meeting was at hand.", "그리고 이제 그 만남이 코앞에 닥쳐 있었다."]], [["Perhaps, if he had found Messala poor and suffering, Ben-Hur’s feeling had been different; but it was not so.", "만일 그가 메살라를 가난하고 고통스러운 처지로 마주쳤다면, 벤허의 심정은 달랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇지가 않았다."], ["He found him more than prosperous; in the prosperity there was a dash and glitter—gleam of sun on gilt of gold.", "그는 메살라가 번창한 정도를 넘어선 모습으로 만났다. 그 번영에는 화려한 기세와 번쩍임이 깃들어 있었으니, 황금의 도금 위에 쏟아지는 햇빛 같은 광채였다."]], [["So it happened that what Malluch accounted a passing loss of spirit was pondering when the meeting should be, and in what manner he could make it most memorable.", "그래서 말루크가 한때 가신 기분쯤으로 여긴 것은, 사실 그 만남이 언제일지, 그리고 어떻게 하면 가장 잊지 못할 것으로 만들 수 있을지를 곱씹는 것이었다."]], [["They turned after a while into an avenue of oaks, where the people were going and coming in groups; footmen here, and horsemen; there women in litters borne slaves; and now and then chariots rolled by thunderously.", "이윽고 그들은 떡갈나무가 늘어선 가로수 길로 접어들었다. 그곳에서는 사람들이 무리 지어 오가고 있었다. 여기엔 도보로 가는 이들과 말 탄 이들이, 저기엔 노예들이 메는 가마에 탄 여인들이 있었고, 이따금 전차가 우렁찬 소리를 내며 굴러갔다."]], [["At the end of the avenue the road, by an easy grade, descended into a lowland, where, on the right hand, there was a precipitous facing of gray rock, and on the left an open meadow of vernal freshness.", "가로수 길 끝에서 길은 완만한 비탈을 이루며 저지대로 내려갔다. 그곳 오른편에는 잿빛 바위가 깎아지른 듯 솟아 있었고, 왼편에는 봄기운으로 싱그러운 풀밭이 트여 있었다."], ["Then they came in view of the famous Fountain of Castalia.", "그러고 나서 그들의 눈앞에 그 이름난 카스탈리아 샘이 들어왔다."]], [["Edging through a company assembled at the point, Ben-Hur beheld a jet of sweet water pouring from the crest of a stone into a basin of black marble, where, after much boiling and foaming, it disappeared as through a funnel.", "그 자리에 모인 무리를 비집고 들어선 벤허는, 한 줄기 단물이 돌마루에서 검은 대리석 수반으로 쏟아져 내리는 것을 보았다. 물은 한참을 끓어오르고 거품을 일으키다가, 마치 깔때기로 빠지듯 사라졌다."]], [["By the basin, under a small portico cut in the solid wall, sat a priest, old, bearded, wrinkled, cowled—never being more perfectly eremitish.", "수반 곁, 단단한 바위벽을 파서 만든 작은 주랑 아래에는 한 사제가 앉아 있었다. 늙고, 수염이 무성하고, 주름지고, 두건을 쓴—이보다 더 완벽한 은수자(隱修者)는 없을 듯했다."], ["From the manner of the people present, hardly might one say which was the attraction, the fountain, forever sparkling, or the priest, forever there.", "그곳에 모인 사람들의 태도로 보아, 끊임없이 반짝이는 샘과 언제나 그 자리에 있는 사제 중 어느 쪽이 사람을 끄는지 가려내기 어려울 정도였다."], ["He heard, saw, was seen, but never spoke. Occasionally a visitor extended a hand to him with a coin in it.", "그는 듣고, 보고, 보임을 당했으나, 결코 입을 열지 않았다. 이따금 방문객이 동전을 쥔 손을 그에게 내밀었다."], ["With a cunning twinkle of the eyes, he took the money, and gave the party in exchange a leaf of papyrus.", "그러면 그는 약삭빠르게 눈을 반짝이며 돈을 받고, 그 대가로 파피루스 잎 한 장을 건넸다."]], [["The receiver made haste to plunge the papyrus into the basin; then, holding the dripping leaf in the sunlight, he would be rewarded with a versified inscription upon its face; and the fame of the fountain seldom suffered loss by poverty of merit in the poetry.", "그것을 받은 자는 서둘러 파피루스를 수반에 담갔다. 그런 다음 물방울이 떨어지는 잎을 햇빛에 들면, 그 표면에 운(韻)을 맞춘 글귀가 떠올라 보상이 되었다. 그리고 그 시의 빈약함 때문에 샘의 명성이 손상되는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["Before Ben-Hur could test the oracle, some other visitors were seen approaching across the meadow, and their appearance piqued the curiosity of the company, his not less than theirs.", "벤허가 미처 그 신탁을 시험해 보기도 전에, 풀밭 저편으로 다른 방문객들이 다가오는 것이 보였고, 그들의 모습은 무리의 호기심을 자극했으니, 벤허의 호기심도 다른 사람들 못지않았다."]], [["He saw first a camel, very tall and very white, in leading of a driver on horseback.", "그가 맨 처음 본 것은, 매우 키가 크고 새하얀 낙타였다. 말을 탄 마부가 낙타를 끌고 있었다."], ["A houdah on the animal, besides being unusually large, was of crimson and gold.", "낙타 등에 얹힌 가마는 유달리 큰 데다 진홍빛과 황금빛으로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["Two other horsemen followed the camel with tall spears in hand.", "다른 기마병 둘이 긴 창을 손에 들고 낙타 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“What a wonderful camel!”", "\"정말 놀라운 낙타로군!\""], ["said one of the company.", "무리 중 하나가 말했다."]], [["“A prince from afar,”", "\"먼 곳에서 온 왕자야.\""], ["another one suggested.", "또 하나가 짐작해 말했다."]], [["“More likely a king.”", "\"왕이라는 편이 더 그럴듯하지.\""]], [["“If he were on an elephant, I would say he was a king.”", "\"코끼리를 탔다면 왕이라고 했겠네만.\""]], [["A third man had a very different opinion.", "세 번째 사내는 사뭇 다른 의견이었다."]], [["“A camel—and a white camel!”", "\"낙타로군—그것도 흰 낙타라니!\""], ["he said, authoritatively.", "그가 단정 짓듯 말했다."], ["“By Apollo, friends, they who come yonder—you can see there are two of them—are neither kings nor princes; they are women!”", "\"아폴로께 맹세코, 친구들, 저기 오는 자들은—보다시피 둘이지—왕도 아니고 왕자도 아닐세. 여인들이야!\""]], [["In the midst of the dispute the strangers arrived.", "이 다툼이 한창일 때 그 낯선 이들이 당도했다."]], [["The camel seen at hand did not belie his appearance afar.", "가까이서 본 낙타는 멀리서 본 모습을 배반하지 않았다."], ["A taller, statelier brute of his kind no traveller at the fountain, though from the remotest parts, had ever beheld.", "더 키 크고 더 당당한 낙타는, 아무리 먼 곳에서 온 나그네라도 이 샘에서 본 적이 없을 것이었다."], ["Such great black eyes! such exceedingly fine white hair!", "저토록 크고 검은 눈이라니! 저토록 곱고 흰 털이라니!"], ["feet so contractile when raised, so soundless in planting, so broad when set!—nobody had ever seen the peer of this camel.", "들어 올리면 그토록 오므라들고, 디디면 그토록 소리 없고, 디딘 자리는 그토록 넓은 발이라니!—이 낙타에 견줄 만한 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["And how well he became his housing of silk, and all its frippery of gold in fringe and gold in tassel!", "게다가 비단 안장과, 술과 매듭마다 황금으로 치장한 그 온갖 화려함이 얼마나 잘 어울리던지!"], ["The tinkling of silver bells went before him, and he moved lightly, as if unknowing of his burden.", "은방울 딸랑이는 소리가 그 앞에 앞서갔고, 낙타는 제 짐을 모르기라도 하듯 가뿐히 움직였다."]], [["But who were the man and woman under the houdah?", "그런데 가마 안의 남자와 여자는 누구인가?"]], [["Every eye saluted them with the inquiry.", "모든 이의 눈이 그런 물음을 던지며 그들에게 인사를 보냈다."]], [["If the former were a prince or a king, the philosophers of the crowd might not deny the impartiality of Time.", "만일 그 남자가 왕자나 왕이라면, 무리 가운데 철학자들도 세월의 무차별함을 부인하지 못할 것이었다."], ["When they saw the thin, shrunken face buried under an immense turban, the skin of the hue of a mummy, making it impossible to form an idea of his nationality, they were pleased to think the limit of life was for the great as well as the small.", "거대한 터번 아래 파묻힌 야위고 쪼그라든 얼굴, 미라 같은 빛깔이어서 그 출신지를 짐작조차 할 수 없는 살갗을 보았을 때, 그들은 위대한 자에게나 미천한 자에게나 똑같이 목숨의 한계가 있다는 사실을 떠올리며 흐뭇해했다."], ["They saw about his person nothing so enviable as the shawl which draped him.", "그의 몸에서 부러워할 만한 것이라곤, 그를 감싼 숄밖에 없었다."]], [["The woman was seated in the manner of the East, amidst veils and laces of surpassing fineness.", "여자는 동방의 방식대로, 더없이 고운 너울과 레이스에 둘러싸여 앉아 있었다."], ["Above her elbows she wore armlets fashioned like coiled asps, and linked to bracelets at the wrists by strands of gold; otherwise the arms were bare and of singular natural grace, complemented with hands modelled daintily as a child’s.", "팔꿈치 위로는 똬리를 튼 독사 모양의 팔찌를 두르고, 황금 줄로 손목의 팔찌와 이어 놓았다. 그 밖에 두 팔은 드러나 있었는데, 보기 드물게 천연의 우아함을 지녔고, 아이의 손처럼 앙증맞게 빚어진 손이 그것을 돋보이게 했다."], ["One of the hands rested upon the side of the carriage, showing tapered fingers glittering with rings, and stained at the tips till they blushed like the pink of mother-of-pearl.", "한 손은 가마 옆을 짚고 있었으니, 가느다랗게 빠진 손가락은 반지로 반짝였고, 손끝은 진주모(眞珠母)의 분홍빛처럼 발그레하게 물들어 있었다."], ["She wore an open caul upon her head, sprinkled with beads of coral, and strung with coin-pieces called sunlets, some of which were carried across her forehead, while others fell down her back, half-smothered in the mass of her straight blue-black hair, of itself an incomparable ornament, not needing the veil which covered it, except as a protection against sun and dust.", "그녀는 머리에 트인 망사 머리쓰개를 썼는데, 거기엔 산호 구슬이 점점이 박히고 '해님'이라 불리는 동전 장식들이 꿰여 있었다. 그중 몇몇은 이마를 가로질러 걸쳐 있었고, 다른 것들은 등 뒤로 흘러내려, 곧게 뻗은 검푸른 머리채 더미에 반쯤 묻혀 있었다. 그 머리채만으로도 견줄 데 없는 장식이어서, 그것을 덮은 너울은 햇빛과 먼지를 막는 용도가 아니라면 필요 없을 정도였다."], ["From her elevated seat she looked upon the people calmly, pleasantly, and apparently so intent upon studying them as to be unconscious of the interest she herself was exciting; and, what was unusual—nay, in violent contravention of the custom among women of rank in public—she looked at them with an open face.", "높이 앉은 자리에서 그녀는 사람들을 차분하고 다정하게 내려다보았는데, 사람들을 살펴보는 데 어찌나 골몰했던지 정작 자기가 불러일으키는 관심은 깨닫지 못하는 듯했다. 그리고 흔치 않은 일로—아니, 공공장소에서 지체 높은 여인들의 관례를 거세게 거스르는 일로—그녀는 얼굴을 가리지 않고 그들을 바라보았다."]], [["It was a fair face to see; quite youthful; in form, oval: complexion not white, like the Greek; nor brunet, like the Roman; nor blond, like the Gaul; but rather the tinting of the sun of the Upper Nile upon a skin of such transparency that the blood shone through it on cheek and brow with nigh the ruddiness of lamplight.", "그것은 보기에 아름다운 얼굴이었다. 무척 앳되고, 갸름한 모양이었다. 살빛은 그리스 여인처럼 희지도, 로마 여인처럼 가무잡잡하지도, 갈리아 여인처럼 금발에 어울리는 빛도 아니었다."], ["The eyes, naturally large, were touched along the lids with the black paint immemorial throughout the East.", "오히려 나일강 상류의 햇볕이 물들인 빛깔이었으되, 그 살갗이 어찌나 투명한지 피가 그 너머로 비쳐, 뺨과 이마가 등불빛 같은 발그레함으로 빛났다."], ["The lips were slightly parted, disclosing, through their scarlet lake, teeth of glistening whiteness.", "본디 큰 두 눈은 눈꺼풀을 따라, 온 동방에서 예부터 써 온 검은 화장먹으로 단장되어 있었다."], ["To all these excellences of countenance the reader is finally besought to superadd the air derived from the pose of a small head, classic in shape, set upon a neck long, drooping, and graceful—the air, we may fancy, happily described by the word queenly.", "입술은 살짝 벌어져, 그 진홍빛 호수 사이로 눈부시게 흰 이가 드러났다. 이 모든 빼어난 용모에, 끝으로 독자는 한 가지를 더 보태어 주기를 청한다. 고전적인 모양으로 빚어진 작은 머리가, 길고 살풋 숙어진 우아한 목 위에 얹힌 데서 우러나는 분위기를—굳이 말하자면, '여왕다운'이라는 말로 다행히 표현될 그 분위기를."]], [["As if satisfied with the survey of people and locality, the fair creature spoke to the driver—an Ethiopian of vast brawn, naked to the waist—who led the camel nearer the fountain, and caused it to kneel; after which he received from her hand a cup, and proceeded to fill it at the basin.", "사람들과 주위 풍경을 다 살펴본 데 만족했는지, 그 고운 여인은 마부에게 말을 건넸다. 마부는 허리까지 벗은, 우람한 근육의 에티오피아인이었다. 그가 낙타를 샘 가까이 끌어다 무릎 꿇게 한 다음, 여인의 손에서 잔을 받아 들고 수반에서 물을 채우러 갔다."], ["That instant the sound of wheels and the trampling of horses in rapid motion broke the silence her beauty had imposed, and, with a great outcry, the bystanders parted in every direction, hurrying to get away.", "바로 그 순간, 그녀의 아름다움이 자아낸 정적을 깨뜨리며, 바퀴 소리와 빠르게 내달리는 말발굽 소리가 터져 나왔다. 그러자 큰 외침과 함께 구경꾼들이 사방으로 흩어지며, 피하려 허둥지둥 내달렸다."]], [["“The Roman has a mind to ride us down.", "\"저 로마인이 우리를 깔아뭉갤 작정입니다."], ["Look out!” Malluch shouted to Ben-Hur, setting him at the same time an example of hasty flight.", "조심하십시오!\" 말루크가 벤허에게 소리치며, 동시에 황급히 달아나는 본보기를 보였다."]], [["The latter faced to the direction the sounds came from, and beheld Messala in his chariot pushing the four straight at the crowd.", "벤허는 소리 나는 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸고, 메살라가 전차를 몰며 네 마리 말을 곧장 군중을 향해 몰아붙이는 것을 보았다."], ["This time the view was near and distinct.", "이번에는 가깝고 또렷한 광경이었다."]], [["The parting of the company uncovered the camel, which might have been more agile than his kind generally; yet the hoofs were almost upon him, and he resting with closed eyes, chewing the endless cud with such sense of security as long favoritism may be supposed to have bred in him.", "무리가 흩어지자 낙타가 드러났다. 그 낙타는 보통 제 종족보다 더 민첩했을지도 모른다. 그러나 말발굽은 이미 그 코앞에 닥쳤건만, 낙타는 눈을 감은 채 쉬며 끝없는 되새김질을 하고 있었으니, 오랜 총애가 길러 주었으리라 짐작되는 안도감에 잠겨 있었다."], ["The Ethiopian wrung his hands afraid. In the houdah, the old man moved to escape; but he was hampered with age, and could not, even in the face of danger, forget the dignity which was plainly his habit.", "에티오피아인은 겁에 질려 두 손을 비비 꼬았다. 가마 안의 노인은 피하려고 움직였으나, 늙은 몸에 거동이 거북했고, 위험을 앞두고서도 분명 몸에 밴 그 위엄을 잊을 수 없었다."], ["It was too late for the woman to save herself.", "여자가 스스로를 구하기엔 너무 늦었다."], ["Ben-Hur stood nearest them, and he called to Messala,", "벤허가 그들과 가장 가까이 서 있었고, 그가 메살라에게 외쳤다."]], [["“Hold!", "\"멈춰라!"], ["Look where thou goest! Back, back!”", "어디로 가는지 보아라! 물러서, 물러서라!\""]], [["The patrician was laughing in hearty good-humor; and, seeing there was but one chance of rescue, Ben-Hur stepped in, and caught the bits of the left yoke-steed and his mate.", "그 귀족은 흥겨운 호탕함으로 웃고 있었다. 구할 길이 단 하나뿐임을 안 벤허는 안으로 뛰어들어, 왼쪽 멍에의 말과 그 짝의 재갈을 움켜잡았다."], ["“Dog of a Roman!", "\"로마의 개야!"], ["Carest thou so little for life?”", "목숨이 그리도 하찮으냐?\""], ["he cried, putting forth all his strength.", "그가 온 힘을 다하며 외쳤다."], ["The two horses reared, and drew the others round; the tilting of the pole tilted the chariot; Messala barely escaped a fall, while his complacent Myrtilus rolled back like a clod to the ground.", "두 마리 말이 앞발을 들고 곧추서며 나머지 말들을 빙 끌어돌렸고, 끌채가 기울자 전차도 기울었다. 메살라는 가까스로 나뒹구는 것을 면했으나, 그의 우쭐대던 미르틸로스는 흙덩이처럼 땅바닥으로 굴러떨어졌다."], ["Seeing the peril past, all the bystanders burst into derisive laughter.", "위험이 지나간 것을 보자, 구경꾼들이 모두 비웃는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["The matchless audacity of the Roman then manifested itself.", "그러자 로마인의 견줄 데 없는 뻔뻔함이 그 진면목을 드러냈다."], ["Loosing the lines from his body, he tossed them to one side, dismounted, walked round the camel, looked at Ben-Hur, and spoke partly to the old man and partly to the woman.", "그는 몸에 감긴 고삐를 풀어 한쪽으로 던지고, 전차에서 내려, 낙타를 빙 돌아 걸으며 벤허를 바라보고는, 일부는 노인에게 일부는 여자에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Pardon, I pray you—I pray you both.", "\"용서를 비오—두 분 모두에게 비오."], ["I am Messala,”", "나는 메살라요.\""], ["he said; “and, by the old Mother of the earth, I swear I did not see you or your camel!", "그가 말했다. \"대지의 옛 어머니께 맹세코, 그대도 그대의 낙타도 보지 못했소이다!"], ["As to these good people—perhaps I trusted too much to my skill.", "저 선량한 사람들로 말하자면—아마 내 솜씨를 너무 믿었나 보오."], ["I sought a laugh at them—the laugh is theirs.", "저들을 한바탕 웃음거리로 삼으려 했더니, 웃음은 저들 차지가 되었구려."], ["Good may it do them!”", "저들에게 좋은 일이 되기를!\""]], [["The good-natured, careless look and gesture he threw the bystanders accorded well with the speech.", "그가 구경꾼들에게 던진 사람 좋고 무심한 표정과 몸짓은 그 말과 잘 어울렸다."], ["To hear what more he had to say, they became quiet.", "그가 또 무슨 말을 하려는지 들으려고, 사람들은 조용해졌다."], ["Assured of victory over the body of the offended, he signed his companion to take the chariot to a safer distance, and addressed himself boldly to the woman.", "봉변당한 무리를 제압했다고 확신한 그는, 일행에게 전차를 더 안전한 거리로 물리라는 신호를 보내고는, 대담하게 여자에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Thou hast interest in the good man here, whose pardon, if not granted now, I shall seek with the greater diligence hereafter; his daughter, I should say.”", "\"그대는 여기 이 선량한 분을 마음에 두고 계신가 보오. 지금 그분의 용서를 받지 못한다면, 앞으로 더욱 부지런히 구하리다. 그대의 아버님이라 해야겠군.\""]], [["She made him no reply.", "그녀는 그에게 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“By Pallas, thou art beautiful!", "\"팔라스께 맹세코, 그대는 아름답소!"], ["Beware Apollo mistake thee not for his lost love.", "아폴로가 그대를 잃어버린 연인으로 착각하지 않도록 조심하시오."], ["I wonder what land can boast herself thy mother.", "어느 땅이 그대의 어머니라 뽐낼 수 있을지 궁금하구려."], ["Turn not away. A truce! a truce!", "돌아서지 마시오. 휴전이오! 휴전!"], ["There is the sun of India in thine eyes; in the corners of thy mouth, Egypt hath set her love-signs. Perpol!", "그대의 두 눈에는 인도의 태양이 깃들어 있고, 입가에는 이집트가 사랑의 표식을 새겨 놓았소. 페르폴!"], ["Turn not to that slave, fair mistress, before proving merciful to this one.", "고운 마님이여, 이 사람에게 자비를 베풀기 전에는 저 노예에게로 돌아서지 마시오."], ["Tell me at least that I am pardoned.”", "적어도 내가 용서받았다는 말만이라도 해 주시오.\""]], [["At this point she broke in upon him.", "이 대목에서 그녀가 그의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“Wilt thou come here?”", "\"이리로 와 주시겠어요?\""], ["she asked, smiling, and with gracious bend of the head to Ben-Hur.", "그녀가 미소 지으며, 벤허에게 우아하게 고개를 숙이며 물었다."]], [["“Take the cup and fill it, I pray thee,”", "\"잔을 받아 채워 주세요, 부탁드려요.\""], ["she said to the latter.", "그녀가 벤허에게 말했다."], ["“My father is thirsty.”", "\"제 아버지가 목말라하십니다.\""]], [["“I am thy most willing servant!”", "\"기꺼이 그대의 종이 되겠습니다!\""]], [["Ben-Hur turned about to do the favor, and was face to face with Messala.", "벤허가 그 부탁을 들어주려 몸을 돌리니, 메살라와 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다."], ["Their glances met; the Jew’s defiant; the Roman’s sparkling with humor.", "두 사람의 눈길이 마주쳤다. 유대인의 눈길은 도전적이었고, 로마인의 눈길은 익살로 반짝였다."]], [["“O stranger, beautiful as cruel!”", "\"오, 아름답되 매정한 이방인이여!\""], ["Messala said, waving his hand to her.", "메살라가 그녀에게 손을 흔들며 말했다."], ["“If Apollo get thee not, thou shalt see me again.", "\"아폴로가 그대를 데려가지 않는다면, 그대는 나를 다시 보게 되리라."], ["Not knowing thy country, I cannot name a god to commend thee to; so, by all the gods, I will commend thee to—myself!”", "그대의 나라를 알지 못하니, 그대를 어느 신에게 맡겨 달라 할 수가 없구려. 그러니 모든 신께 맹세코, 나는 그대를 나 자신에게 맡기리다!\""]], [["Seeing that Myrtilus had the four composed and ready, he returned to the chariot.", "미르틸로스가 네 마리 말을 가지런히 추슬러 채비해 놓은 것을 본 그는 전차로 돌아갔다."], ["The woman looked after him as he moved away, and whatever else there was in her look, there was no displeasure.", "여자는 그가 멀어져 가는 모습을 바라보았는데, 그 눈길에 무엇이 담겨 있든 못마땅함은 없었다."], ["Presently she received the water; her father drank; then she raised the cup to her lips, and, leaning down, gave it to Ben-Hur; never action more graceful and gracious.", "이윽고 그녀는 물을 건네받았다. 아버지가 마셨다. 그런 다음 그녀는 잔을 제 입술로 가져갔다가, 몸을 숙여 벤허에게 건넸으니, 그보다 더 우아하고 품위 있는 동작은 없었다."]], [["“Keep it, we pray of thee! It is full of blessings—all thine!”", "\"부디 그것을 간직하세요! 그것은 축복으로 가득하답니다—모두 당신의 것이에요!\""]], [["Immediately the camel was aroused, and on his feet, and about to go, when the old man called,", "곧 낙타가 일으켜져 발을 딛고 막 떠나려 할 때, 노인이 불렀다."]], [["“Stand thou here.”", "\"여기 좀 서 보게.\""]], [["Ben-Hur went to him respectfully.", "벤허가 공손히 그에게로 갔다."]], [["“Thou hast served the stranger well to-day.", "\"그대는 오늘 이 이방인을 잘 도와주었네."], ["There is but one God.", "하느님은 오직 한 분이시지."], ["In his holy name I thank thee.", "그분의 거룩한 이름으로 그대에게 감사하네."], ["I am Balthasar, the Egyptian.", "나는 이집트인 발타사르일세."], ["In the Great Orchard of Palms, beyond the village of Daphne, in the shade of the palms, Sheik Ilderim the Generous abideth in his tents, and we are his guests. Seek us there.", "다프네 마을 너머, 종려나무 대정원의 종려나무 그늘에, 너그러운 일데림 족장께서 천막을 치고 머무시는데, 우리는 그분의 손님일세. 그곳으로 우리를 찾아오게."], ["Thou shalt have welcome sweet with the savor of the grateful.”", "감사하는 자의 향취가 깃든 달가운 환대를 받게 될 걸세.\""]], [["Ben-Hur was left in wonder at the old man’s clear voice and reverend manner.", "벤허는 노인의 맑은 목소리와 존경스러운 태도에 감탄하며 남겨졌다."], ["As he gazed after the two departing, he caught sight of Messala going as he had come, joyous, indifferent, and with a mocking laugh.", "떠나가는 두 사람을 바라보던 그는, 메살라가 올 때와 마찬가지로, 즐겁고 무심하게, 비웃는 웃음을 띤 채 가 버리는 모습을 언뜻 보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# CHAPTER IX"]], [["As a rule, there is no surer way to the dislike of men than to behave well where they have behaved badly.", "무릇 남들이 잘못 행동한 자리에서 홀로 바르게 처신하는 것만큼 사람들의 미움을 사기 좋은 길은 없다."], ["In this instance, happily, Malluch was an exception to the rule.", "그러나 이 경우에는 다행히도 말루크가 그 통례에서 벗어난 사람이었다."], ["The affair he had just witnessed raised Ben-Hur in his estimation, since he could not deny him courage and address; could he now get some insight into the young man’s history, the results of the day would not be all unprofitable to good master Simonides.", "그가 방금 목격한 사건은 그의 눈에 벤허를 한층 높여 놓았으니, 그 젊은이의 용기와 수완을 부인할 수 없었기 때문이다. 만약 이제 그 젊은이의 내력까지 어느 정도 들여다볼 수 있다면, 오늘 하루의 성과는 어진 주인 시모니데스에게도 결코 헛되지만은 않을 터였다."]], [["On the latter point, referring to what he had as yet learned, two facts comprehended it all—the subject of his investigation was a Jew, and the adopted son of a famous Roman.", "뒤엣것에 관하여, 그가 지금까지 알아낸 바를 두고 말하자면, 두 가지 사실이 그 전부를 아울렀다. 그가 살피고 있는 대상은 유대인이라는 것, 그리고 이름난 어느 로마인의 양자라는 것이었다."], ["Another conclusion which might be of importance was beginning to formulate itself in the shrewd mind of the emissary; between Messala and the son of the duumvir there was a connection of some kind.", "또 하나, 중요할지도 모를 결론이 그 영민한 밀정의 머릿속에서 차츰 형태를 갖추기 시작했다. 메살라와 그 두 집정관 출신의 아들 사이에는 어떤 종류의 연결이 있었다. 그러나 그것은 무엇인가?"], ["But what was it?—and how could it be reduced to assurance?", "그리고 어떻게 그것을 확실한 것으로 좁혀 낼 수 있을까?"], ["With all his sounding, the ways and means of solution were not at call.", "아무리 더듬어 보아도 해답에 이르는 방도와 수단은 떠오르지 않았다."], ["In the heat of the perplexity, Ben-Hur himself came to his help.", "그 곤혹의 열기 속에서, 벤허 자신이 그를 도우러 나섰다."], ["He laid his hand on Malluch’s arm and drew him out of the crowd, which was already going back to its interest in the gray old priest and the mystic fountain.", "그는 말루크의 팔에 손을 얹고 그를 군중 속에서 끌어냈다. 군중은 어느새 다시 잿빛 늙은 사제와 신비로운 샘으로 관심을 돌리고 있었다."]], [["“Good Malluch,”", "\"착한 말루크,\""], ["he said, stopping, “may a man forget his mother?”", "그가 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"사람이 제 어머니를 잊을 수 있겠소?\""]], [["The question was abrupt and without direction, and therefore of the kind which leaves the person addressed in a state of confusion.", "그 물음은 느닷없고 또 어디로 향하는지도 알 수 없는 것이어서, 듣는 이를 어리둥절하게 만드는 종류였다."], ["Malluch looked into Ben-Hur’s face for a hint of meaning, but saw, instead, two bright-red spots, one on each cheek, and in his eyes traces of what might have been repressed tears; then he answered, mechanically, “No!”", "말루크는 그 뜻을 짚어 보려고 벤허의 얼굴을 들여다보았으나, 그 대신 양 볼에 하나씩 돋은 새빨간 반점과, 그 두 눈 속에 억눌린 눈물의 흔적인 듯한 것을 보았다. 그러자 그는 기계적으로 \"없습니다!\" 하고 답했고, 거기에 열을 띠어 \"결코 없습니다.\""], ["adding, with fervor, “never;”", "하고 덧붙였다."], ["and a moment after, when he began to recover himself, “If he is an Israelite, never!”", "잠시 후 정신을 추스르기 시작하면서 \"그가 이스라엘 사람이라면, 결코 없습니다!\" 하고 말했다."], ["And when at length he was completely recovered—“My first lesson in the synagogue was the Shema; my next was the saying of the son of Sirach, ‘Honor thy father with thy whole soul, and forget not the sorrows of thy mother.’”", "그리고 마침내 완전히 마음을 가다듬고는 이렇게 말했다. \"회당에서 제가 처음 배운 가르침은 셰마였고, 그다음은 시락의 아들이 한 말이었습니다. '온 영혼을 다하여 네 아비를 공경하고, 네 어미의 슬픔을 잊지 말라.'\""]], [["The red spots on Ben-Hur’s face deepened.", "벤허의 얼굴에 돋은 붉은 반점이 더욱 짙어졌다."]], [["“The words bring my childhood back again; and, Malluch, they prove you a genuine Jew.", "\"그 말이 내 어린 시절을 다시 불러오는구려. 그리고 말루크, 그 말은 그대가 참된 유대인임을 증명하오."], ["I believe I can trust you.”", "내가 그대를 믿어도 되리라 생각하오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur let go the arm he was holding, and caught the folds of the gown covering his own breast, and pressed them close, as if to smother a pain, or a feeling there as sharp as a pain.", "벤허는 붙잡고 있던 팔을 놓고, 제 가슴을 덮은 옷자락을 움켜쥐어 바싹 눌렀으니, 마치 그곳의 아픔을, 혹은 아픔만큼이나 날카로운 어떤 감정을 짓누르려는 듯했다."]], [["“My father,”", "\"내 아버지는,\""], ["he said, “bore a good name, and was not without honor in Jerusalem, where he dwelt.", "그가 말했다. \"좋은 이름을 지니셨고, 사시던 예루살렘에서도 명예를 잃지 않은 분이셨소."], ["My mother, at his death, was in the prime of womanhood; and it is not enough to say of her she was good and beautiful: in her tongue was the law of kindness, and her works were the praise of all in the gates, and she smiled at days to come.", "어머니는 아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 한창나이의 여인이셨소. 그분을 두고 그저 착하고 아름다웠다고 말하는 것만으로는 부족하오. 그분의 혀에는 인자함의 법도가 깃들어 있었고, 그분의 행실은 성문 앞 모든 이의 칭송이었으며, 그분은 다가올 날들을 향해 미소 지으셨소."], ["I had a little sister, and she and I were the family, and we were so happy that I, at least, have never seen harm in the saying of the old rabbi, ‘God could not be everywhere, and, therefore, he made mothers.’ One day an accident happened to a Roman in authority as he was riding past our house at the head of a cohort; the legionaries burst the gate and rushed in and seized us.", "내게는 어린 누이가 하나 있었으니, 그 아이와 나, 그렇게가 우리 식구였고, 우리는 어찌나 행복했던지, 적어도 나는 옛 랍비의 말씀에서 그릇된 데를 본 적이 없소. '하나님은 모든 곳에 계실 수 없으셨고, 그리하여 어머니를 만드셨다.' 어느 날 권세를 쥔 어느 로마인이 한 대대를 거느리고 우리 집 앞을 지나가다가 사고를 당했소."], ["I have not seen my mother or sister since.", "군단병들이 문을 부수고 들이닥쳐 우리를 붙잡아 갔소."], ["I cannot say they are dead or living.", "그 뒤로 나는 어머니도 누이도 다시 보지 못했소."], ["I do not know what became of them.", "두 사람이 죽었는지 살았는지조차 말할 수 없소."], ["But, Malluch, the man in the chariot yonder was present at the separation; he gave us over to the captors; he heard my mother’s prayer for her children, and he laughed when they dragged her away.", "그들이 어찌 되었는지 나는 모르오. 그러나 말루크, 저기 전차에 탄 저 사람이 그 생이별의 자리에 있었소. 그가 우리를 붙잡는 자들에게 넘겼고, 제 자식들을 위한 어머니의 기도를 들었으며, 그들이 어머니를 끌고 갈 때 웃었소."], ["Hardly may one say which graves deepest in memory, love or hate.", "사랑과 미움, 그 어느 쪽이 기억에 더 깊이 새겨지는지는 좀처럼 가리기 어렵소."], ["To-day I knew him afar—and, Malluch—”", "오늘 나는 멀리서 그를 알아보았소. 그리고 말루크—\""]], [["He caught the listener’s arm again.", "그는 다시 듣는 이의 팔을 붙잡았다."]], [["“And, Malluch, he knows and takes with him now the secret I would give my life for: he could tell if she lives, and where she is, and her condition; if she—no, THEY—much sorrow has made the two as one—if they are dead, he could tell where they died, and of what, and where their bones await my finding.”", "\"그리고 말루크, 그는 내가 목숨을 바쳐서라도 알고 싶은 그 비밀을 알고 있고, 지금 그것을 품고 다니고 있소. 그는 어머니가 살아 계신지, 어디에 계신지, 어떤 처지인지 말해 줄 수 있소. 만약 그분이—아니, 두 사람이—너무도 큰 슬픔이 둘을 하나로 만들었으니—만약 두 사람이 죽었다면, 그는 어디서 죽었는지, 무엇 때문에 죽었는지, 그 유골이 내 손길을 어디서 기다리고 있는지 말해 줄 수 있소.\" \"한데 그가 말해 주지 않으리란 거요?\""]], [["“And will he not?”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“No.” “Why?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“I am a Jew, and he is a Roman.”", "\"나는 유대인이고, 그는 로마인이니까.\""]], [["“But Romans have tongues, and Jews, though ever so despised, have methods to beguile them.”", "\"하지만 로마인에게도 혀가 있고, 유대인은 아무리 멸시받는다 해도 그 혀를 꾀어내는 방도를 지니고 있지요.\""]], [["“For such as he? No; and, besides, the secret is one of state.", "\"그자 같은 인간을 상대로? 아니오. 게다가 그 비밀은 국가의 일이오."], ["All my father’s property was confiscated and divided.”", "내 아버지의 재산은 죄다 몰수되어 나뉘었소.\""]], [["Malluch nodded his head slowly, much as to admit the argument; then he asked anew, “Did he not recognize you?”", "말루크는 그 논리를 인정하듯 천천히 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 다시 물었다. \"그가 당신을 알아보지 못했소?\""]], [["“He could not. I was sent to death in life, and have been long since accounted of the dead.”", "\"알아볼 수 없었소. 나는 산 채로 죽음의 길로 보내졌고, 오래전부터 죽은 자로 헤아려졌으니까.\""]], [["“I wonder you did not strike him,”", "\"그자를 쳐 버리지 않은 게 놀랍군요.\""], ["said Malluch, yielding to a touch of passion.", "말루크가 한 가닥 격정에 못 이겨 말했다."]], [["“That would have been to put him past serving me forever.", "\"그것은 그자가 영영 나를 도울 수 없게 만드는 일이 되었을 거요."], ["I would have had to kill him, and Death, you know, keeps secrets better even than a guilty Roman.”", "나는 그를 죽일 수밖에 없었을 텐데, 그대도 알다시피, 죽음이란 죄 지은 로마인보다도 비밀을 더 잘 지키는 법이오.\""]], [["The man who, with so much to avenge, could so calmly put such an opportunity aside must be confident of his future or have ready some better design, and Malluch’s interest changed with the thought; it ceased to be that of an emissary in duty bound to another.", "복수해야 할 것이 그토록 많으면서도 그런 기회를 그처럼 차분히 물리칠 수 있는 사람이라면, 제 앞날에 확신이 있거나 더 나은 계획을 마련해 두었음이 분명했다. 그 생각과 더불어 말루크의 관심도 달라졌다. 그것은 더 이상 다른 이에게 매인 의무 때문에 움직이는 밀정의 관심이 아니었다."], ["Ben-Hur was actually asserting a claim upon him for his own sake.", "벤허는 이제 제 자신을 위하여 그에게 요구할 권리를 내세우고 있었던 것이다."], ["In other words, Malluch was preparing to serve him with good heart and from downright admiration.", "다시 말해, 말루크는 진심에서, 그리고 꾸밈없는 감탄에서 우러나 그를 섬길 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["After brief pause, Ben-Hur resumed speaking.", "잠시 말이 끊겼다가, 벤허가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“I would not take his life, good Malluch; against that extreme the possession of the secret is for the present, at least, his safeguard; yet I may punish him, and so you give me help, I will try.”", "\"그의 목숨을 빼앗지는 않겠소, 착한 말루크. 그 극단에 이르지 않게 막아 주는 것이, 적어도 지금으로서는, 그가 쥔 비밀이오. 그러나 나는 그를 벌할 수는 있을 것이오. 그러니 그대가 나를 돕는다면, 나는 한번 해 보겠소.\""]], [["“He is a Roman,”", "\"그는 로마인이고,\""], ["said Malluch, without hesitation; “and I am of the tribe of Judah.", "말루크가 망설임 없이 말했다. \"나는 유다 지파 사람입니다."], ["I will help you. If you choose, put me under oath—under the most solemn oath.”", "내 당신을 돕겠습니다. 원하신다면 나를 맹세에 묶으십시오—가장 엄숙한 맹세에.\""]], [["“Give me your hand, that will suffice.”", "\"그대의 손을 주시오, 그것으로 족하오.\""]], [["As their hands fell apart, Ben-Hur said, with lightened feeling, “That I would charge you with is not difficult, good friend; neither is it dreadful to conscience.", "두 사람의 손이 떨어지자, 벤허가 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 맡기려는 일은 어렵지 않소, 좋은 친구여. 양심을 두렵게 하는 일도 아니오."], ["Let us move on.”", "자, 가십시다.\""]], [["They took the road which led to the right across the meadow spoken of in the description of the coming to the fountain.", "두 사람은 샘에 이르는 길을 묘사할 때 말했던 그 풀밭을 가로질러 오른쪽으로 난 길로 접어들었다."], ["Ben-Hur was first to break the silence.", "침묵을 먼저 깬 것은 벤허였다."]], [["“Do you know Sheik Ilderim the Generous?”", "\"너그러운 일데림 족장을 아시오?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"압니다.\""]], [["“Where is his Orchard of Palms?", "\"그의 종려나무 정원은 어디에 있소?"], ["or, rather, Malluch, how far is it beyond the village of Daphne?”", "아니, 말루크, 다프네 마을에서 얼마나 더 가야 하오?\""]], [["Malluch was touched by a doubt; he recalled the prettiness of the favor shown him by the woman at the fountain, and wondered if he who had the sorrows of a mother in mind was about to forget them for a lure of love; yet he replied, “The Orchard of Palms lies beyond the village two hours by horse, and one by swift camel.”", "말루크는 한 가닥 의심에 마음이 걸렸다. 그는 샘가에서 그 여인이 자기에게 보여 준 고운 호의를 떠올리며, 어머니의 슬픔을 가슴에 품은 이 사람이 사랑의 미끼에 그것을 잊으려는 것은 아닌가 의아해했다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"종려나무 정원은 마을에서 말로 두 시간, 빠른 낙타로는 한 시간 떨어진 곳에 있습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you; and to your knowledge once more.", "\"고맙소. 그러면 다시 한번 그대의 식견에 기대겠소."], ["Have the games of which you told me been widely published?", "그대가 말해 준 그 경기는 널리 알려졌소?"], ["and when will they take place?”", "그리고 언제 열리오?\""]], [["The questions were suggestive; and if they did not restore Malluch his confidence, they at least stimulated his curiosity.", "그 물음들에는 무언가 암시하는 바가 있었다. 그것이 말루크의 신뢰를 되돌려 주지는 못했더라도, 적어도 그의 호기심만은 부추겼다."]], [["“Oh yes, they will be of ample splendor.", "\"오, 그렇습니다. 그야말로 굉장한 광경이 될 겁니다."], ["The prefect is rich, and could afford to lose his place; yet, as is the way with successful men, his love of riches is nowise diminished; and to gain a friend at court, if nothing more, he must make ado for the Consul Maxentius, who is coming hither to make final preparations for a campaign against the Parthians.", "총독은 부유하니, 자리를 잃는다 해도 견딜 만하지요. 그런데도 성공한 자들이 으레 그렇듯, 재물에 대한 그의 사랑은 조금도 줄지 않았습니다. 그러니 궁정에 친구 하나라도 얻으려면, 다른 게 아무것도 없더라도, 그는 막센티우스 집정관을 위해 떠들썩한 잔치를 벌여야 합니다."], ["The money there is in the preparations the citizens of Antioch know from experience; so they have had permission to join the prefect in the honors intended for the great man.", "그 집정관은 파르티아인을 칠 원정의 마지막 준비를 하러 이리로 오고 있지요. 그런 준비에 큰돈이 있다는 것을 안티오크 시민들은 경험으로 알고 있습니다."], ["A month ago heralds went to the four quarters to proclaim the opening of the Circus for the celebration.", "그래서 그들은 이 위대한 인물에게 바칠 영예에 총독과 함께 끼어들 허락을 받았지요."], ["The name of the prefect would be of itself good guarantee of variety and magnificence, particularly throughout the East; but when to his promises Antioch joins hers, all the islands and the cities by the sea stand assured of the extraordinary, and will be here in person or by their most famous professionals.", "한 달 전에 전령들이 사방으로 나가 이 축전을 위한 원형경기장의 개장을 선포했습니다. 총독의 이름만으로도 다채로움과 호화로움을 충분히 보증할 터인데, 특히 동방 전역에서 그러합니다. 그러나 그의 약속에 안티오크가 제 약속을 보태니, 바다에 면한 모든 섬과 도시가 예사롭지 않은 볼거리를 확신하고, 몸소 오거나 가장 이름난 전문 선수들을 보낼 겁니다."], ["The fees offered are royal.”", "내거는 상금이 왕에게나 어울릴 만하지요.\""]], [["“And the Circus—I have heard it is second only to the Maximus.”", "\"그리고 그 경기장—듣자니 막시무스 다음간다더군.\""]], [["“At Rome, you mean.", "\"로마의 것 말씀이시군요."], ["Well, ours seats two hundred thousand people, yours seats seventy-five thousand more; yours is of marble, so is ours; in arrangement they are exactly the same.”", "그렇소, 우리 경기장은 이십만 명을 앉히고, 그쪽 것은 거기에 칠만오천 명을 더 앉히지요. 그쪽이 대리석이듯, 우리 것도 대리석이고, 구조는 둘이 똑같습니다.\""]], [["“Are the rules the same?”", "\"규칙도 같소?\""]], [["Malluch smiled.", "말루크가 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["“If Antioch dared be original, son of Arrius, Rome would not be the mistress she is.", "\"안티오크가 감히 독창성을 부린다면, 아리우스의 아들이여, 로마는 지금과 같은 여주인이 못 되었을 겁니다."], ["The laws of the Circus Maximus govern except in one particular: there but four chariots may start at once, here all start without reference to number.”", "막시무스 경기장의 규칙이 한 가지만 빼고 그대로 통합니다. 그곳에서는 한 번에 전차 넷만 출발할 수 있으나, 여기서는 수에 상관없이 모두 함께 출발하지요.\""]], [["“That is the practise of the Greeks,”", "\"그것은 그리스인들의 방식이오.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Antioch is more Greek than Roman.”", "\"그렇소, 안티오크는 로마보다 그리스에 더 가깝습니다.\""]], [["“So then, Malluch, I may choose my own chariot?”", "\"그렇다면, 말루크, 나는 내 전차를 골라도 되겠구려?\""]], [["“Your own chariot and horses.", "\"당신의 전차와 말이지요."], ["There is no restriction upon either.”", "어느 쪽에도 아무 제한이 없습니다.\""]], [["While replying, Malluch observed the thoughtful look on Ben-Hur’s face give place to one of satisfaction.", "답하면서 말루크는 벤허의 얼굴에서 생각에 잠긴 빛이 흡족한 빛으로 바뀌는 것을 보았다."]], [["“One thing more now, O Malluch. When will the celebration be?”", "\"이제 한 가지만 더 묻겠소, 오 말루크. 축전은 언제 열리오?\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["your pardon,”", "용서하십시오.\""], ["the other answered.", "상대가 답했다."], ["“To-morrow—and the next day,”", "\"내일—그리고 그다음 날,\""], ["he said, counting aloud, “then, to speak in the Roman style, if the sea-gods be propitious, the consul arrives.", "그가 소리 내어 헤아렸다. \"그러고는, 로마식으로 말하자면, 바다의 신들이 어여삐 여기신다면, 집정관이 도착합니다."], ["Yes, the sixth day from this we have the games.”", "그렇소, 오늘부터 엿새째 되는 날에 경기가 열립니다.\""]], [["“The time is short, Malluch, but it is enough.”", "\"시간이 짧구려, 말루크. 그러나 그것으로 충분하오.\""], ["The last words were spoken decisively. “By the prophets of our old Israel!", "마지막 말은 단호하게 떨어졌다. \"우리 옛 이스라엘의 선지자들을 두고 맹세하건대!"], ["I will take to the reins again. Stay!", "나는 다시 고삐를 잡겠소. 잠깐!"], ["a condition; is there assurance that Messala will be a competitor?”", "한 가지 조건이 있소. 메살라가 출전하리라는 확실한 보장이 있소?\""]], [["Malluch saw now the plan, and all its opportunities for the humiliation of the Roman; and he had not been true descendant of Jacob if, with all his interest wakened, he had not rushed to a consideration of the chances.", "말루크는 이제 그 계획을, 그리고 그 로마인에게 굴욕을 안길 온갖 기회를 알아차렸다. 만약 그가, 그토록 관심이 깨어났는데도 승산을 가늠하는 일에 곧장 뛰어들지 않았다면, 그는 야곱의 참된 후손이 아니었으리라."], ["His voice actually trembled as he said, “Have you the practise?”", "그의 목소리는 실로 떨렸다. \"솜씨가 있으십니까?\""]], [["“Fear not, my friend.", "\"두려워 마시오, 친구여."], ["The winners in the Circus Maximus have held their crowns these three years at my will.", "막시무스 경기장의 승자들은 지난 삼 년 동안 내 뜻에 따라 그 월계관을 차지해 왔소."], ["Ask them—ask the best of them—and they will tell you so. In the last great games the emperor himself offered me his patronage if I would take his horses in hand and run them against the entries of the world.”", "그들에게 물어보시오—그 가운데 가장 뛰어난 자에게 물어보시오—그러면 그렇다고 말해 줄 것이오. 지난번 대경기에서는 황제께서 친히, 내가 그의 말들을 맡아 온 세상의 출전마들을 상대로 몰아 보겠다면 나를 후원하겠다고 하셨소.\""]], [["“But you did not?”", "\"한데 몰지 않으셨군요?\""]], [["Malluch spoke eagerly.", "말루크가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“I—I am a Jew”—Ben-Hur seemed shrinking within himself as he spoke—“and, though I wear a Roman name, I dared not do professionally a thing to sully my father’s name in the cloisters and courts of the Temple.", "\"나는—나는 유대인이오.\" 벤허는 그렇게 말하면서 제 안으로 움츠러드는 듯했다. \"그리고 비록 로마식 이름을 지녔으나, 나는 성전의 회랑과 뜰에서 내 아버지의 이름을 더럽힐 일을 직업으로 삼을 수는 없었소."], ["In the palaestrae I could indulge practise which, if followed into the Circus, would become an abomination; and if I take to the course here, Malluch, I swear it will not be for the prize or the winner’s fee.”", "연무장에서라면 그런 연습에 몸을 맡길 수 있으나, 그것을 경기장까지 끌고 들어가면 가증한 일이 되고 마오. 그러니 내가 여기서 경주에 나선다면, 말루크, 맹세코 그것은 상이나 승자의 상금을 위한 일이 아닐 것이오.\""]], [["“Hold—swear not so!”", "\"잠깐—그런 맹세는 마십시오!\""], ["cried Malluch.", "말루크가 소리쳤다."], ["“The fee is ten thousand sestertii—a fortune for life!”", "\"그 상금은 일만 세스테르티우스—평생을 누릴 재산입니다!\""]], [["“Not for me, though the prefect trebled it fifty times.", "\"내게는 아니오, 총독이 그것을 쉰 곱절로 세 배 불린다 해도."], ["Better than that, better than all the imperial revenues from the first year of the first Cæsar—I will make this race to humble my enemy.", "그보다 나은 것, 첫 카이사르의 첫해부터 거두어들인 모든 황실 세입보다 나은 것—나는 이 경주로 내 원수를 무릎 꿇리겠소."], ["Vengeance is permitted by the law.”", "복수는 율법이 허락하는 바이오.\""]], [["Malluch smiled and nodded as if saying, “Right, right—trust me a Jew to understand a Jew.”", "말루크는 빙긋 웃으며 고개를 끄덕였으니, 마치 \"옳소, 옳소—유대인을 알아보는 데는 이 유대인을 믿으시오.\" 하고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["“The Messala will drive,”", "\"메살라는 몰 겁니다.\""], ["he said, directly.", "그가 곧바로 말했다."], ["“He is committed to the race in many ways—by publication in the streets, and in the baths and theaters, the palace and barracks; and, to fix him past retreat, his name is on the tablets of every young spendthrift in Antioch.”", "\"그는 여러모로 이미 경주에 매여 있습니다—거리와 목욕탕과 극장, 궁전과 병영에 두루 공표된 데다, 물러설 길 없게 못 박으려는 듯, 그의 이름은 안티오크의 모든 젊은 난봉꾼의 서판에 올라 있습니다.\""]], [["“In wager, Malluch?”", "\"내기에 말이오, 말루크?\""]], [["“Yes, in wager; and every day he comes ostentatiously to practise, as you saw him.”", "\"그렇습니다, 내기에 말입니다. 그리고 그는 당신이 본 대로 날마다 보란 듯이 연습하러 나옵니다.\""]], [["“Ah! and that is the chariot, and those the horses, with which he will make the race?", "\"아! 그러면 저것이 그가 경주에 쓸 전차이고, 저것들이 그 말이오?"], ["Thank you, thank you, Malluch!", "고맙소, 고맙소, 말루크!"], ["You have served me well already.", "그대는 이미 나를 잘 도와주었소."], ["I am satisfied.", "나는 만족하오."], ["Now be my guide to the Orchard of Palms, and give me introduction to Sheik Ilderim the Generous.”", "이제 종려나무 정원으로 나를 안내하고, 너그러운 일데림 족장에게 나를 소개해 주시오.\" \"언제 말입니까?\""]], [["“When?”", "\"오늘이오."], ["“To-day. His horses may be engaged to-morrow.”", "그의 말들은 내일이면 다른 데 잡힐지도 모르오.\""]], [["“You like them, then?”", "\"그러면 그 말들이 마음에 드신 거군요?\""]], [["Ben-Hur answered with animation,", "벤허가 활기를 띠어 답했다."]], [["“I saw them from the stand an instant only, for Messala then drove up, and I might not look at anything else; yet I recognized them as of the blood which is the wonder as well as the glory of the deserts.", "\"관람석에서 한순간 보았을 뿐이오. 그때 메살라가 전차를 몰고 나타나, 나는 다른 데로 눈을 돌릴 수가 없었으니까. 그러나 나는 그것들이 사막의 경이이자 영광인 그 혈통임을 알아보았소."], ["I never saw the kind before, except in the stables of Cæsar; but once seen, they are always to be known.", "나는 그런 종류를 카이사르의 마구간 말고는 본 적이 없으나, 한번 보면 언제든 알아볼 수 있소. 내일 만나면 나는 그대를 알아볼 것이오, 말루크."], ["To-morrow, upon meeting, I will know you, Malluch, though you do not so much as salute me; I will know you by your face, by your form, by your manner; and by the same signs I will know them, and with the same certainty.", "그대가 내게 인사 한 번 건네지 않더라도. 나는 그대의 얼굴로, 모습으로, 거동으로 그대를 알아볼 것이오. 그리고 똑같은 표지로 그 말들을 알아볼 것이며, 똑같은 확신으로 그리할 것이오."], ["If all that is said of them be true, and I can bring their spirit under control of mine, I can—”", "그것들에 관해 떠도는 말이 모두 사실이고, 내가 그 기백을 내 손아귀에 넣을 수 있다면, 나는—\""]], [["“Win the sestertii!” said Malluch, laughing.", "\"세스테르티우스를 따시겠지요!\" 말루크가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“No,” answered Ben-Hur, as quickly.", "\"아니오.\" 벤허가 그만큼 빠르게 답했다."], ["“I will do what better becomes a man born to the heritage of Jacob—I will humble mine enemy in a most public place. But,”", "\"나는 야곱의 유산을 물려받은 사람에게 더 어울리는 일을 하겠소—나는 더없이 많은 사람이 보는 자리에서 내 원수를 무릎 꿇리겠소. 그러나,\""], ["he added, impatiently, “we are losing time.", "그가 조바심을 내며 덧붙였다. \"우리는 시간을 허비하고 있소."], ["How can we most quickly reach the tents of the sheik?”", "어떻게 하면 족장의 천막에 가장 빨리 닿을 수 있겠소?\""]], [["Malluch took a moment for reflection.", "말루크는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“It is best we go straight to the village, which is fortunately near by; if two swift camels are to be had for hire there, we will be on the road but an hour.”", "\"곧장 마을로 가는 것이 상책입니다. 다행히 가까이 있으니까요. 거기서 빠른 낙타 두 마리를 빌릴 수 있다면, 한 시간이면 길에 오를 겁니다.\""]], [["“Let us about it, then.”", "\"그럼 어서 그리합시다.\""]], [["The village was an assemblage of palaces in beautiful gardens, interspersed with khans of princely sort.", "그 마을은 아름다운 정원에 둘러싸인 궁궐들이 모여 이룬 곳으로, 그 사이에 왕후에게나 어울릴 법한 숙소들이 점점이 박혀 있었다."], ["Dromedaries were happily secured, and upon them the journey to the famous Orchard of Palms was begun.", "다행히 단봉낙타들을 구할 수 있었고, 그것들에 올라 이름난 종려나무 정원으로 가는 여정이 시작되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# CHAPTER X"]], [["Beyond the village the country was undulating and cultivated; in fact, it was the garden-land of Antioch, with not a foot lost to labor.", "마을 너머의 땅은 기복이 완만하고 잘 일구어져 있었다. 사실 그곳은 안티오크의 정원이라 할 만한 땅으로, 손길이 닿지 않은 한 뼘의 땅도 없었다."], ["The steep faces of the hills were terraced; even the hedges were brighter of the trailing vines which, besides the lure of shade, offered passers-by sweet promises of wine to come, and grapes in clustered purple ripeness.", "가파른 언덕의 비탈은 계단식으로 일구어져 있었고, 울타리마저도 기어오르는 포도 덩굴로 더욱 화사했으니, 그 덩굴은 그늘의 유혹과 더불어 지나는 이에게 앞으로 빚어질 포도주와, 송이송이 자줏빛으로 무르익을 포도의 달콤한 약속을 건네주었다."], ["Over melon-patches, and through apricot and fig-tree groves, and groves of oranges and limes, the white-washed houses of the farmers were seen; and everywhere Plenty, the smiling daughter of Peace, gave notice by her thousand signs that she was at home, making the generous traveller merry at heart, until he was even disposed to give Rome her dues.", "멜론밭 너머로, 살구나무와 무화과나무 숲을 지나고, 오렌지나무와 라임나무 숲을 지나며, 농부들의 흰 회칠한 집들이 보였다. 곳곳에서 풍요는, 평화의 미소 짓는 딸인 그 풍요는 수천 가지 표시로 제가 이곳에 깃들어 있음을 알렸으니, 마음 너그러운 나그네의 심사를 흐뭇하게 하여, 마침내 로마에게 마땅한 몫을 인정해 줄 마음이 들게 할 정도였다."], ["Occasionally, also, views were had of Taurus and Lebanon, between which, a separating line of silver, the Orontes placidly pursued its way.", "이따금 타우루스와 레바논의 풍경도 눈에 들어왔는데, 그 사이로는 은빛 분계선처럼 오론테스강이 잔잔히 제 길을 따라 흐르고 있었다."]], [["In course of their journey the friends came to the river, which they followed with the windings of the road, now over bold bluffs, and then into vales, all alike allotted for country-seats, and if the land was in full foliage of oak and sycamore and myrtle, and bay and arbutus, and perfuming jasmine, the river was bright with slanted sunlight, which would have slept where it fell but for ships in endless procession, gliding with the current, tacking for the wind, or bounding under the impulse of oars—some coming, some going, and all suggestive of the sea, and distant peoples, and famous places, and things coveted on account of their rarity.", "여행하는 동안 두 사람은 강에 이르렀고, 길의 굽이를 따라 강줄기를 좇았다. 때로는 가파른 절벽 위로, 때로는 골짜기 안으로 길이 이어졌는데, 그 골짜기들은 하나같이 시골 별장 터로 나뉘어 있었다. 땅이 떡갈나무와 무화과나무와 도금양과 월계수와 소귀나무와 향내 나는 재스민의 무성한 잎으로 뒤덮여 있다면, 강은 비스듬히 비치는 햇살로 환했으니, 그 햇살은 떨어진 자리에서 그대로 잠들 법도 했지만, 끝없이 줄지어 미끄러져 가는 배들이 그것을 깨웠다."], ["To the fancy there is nothing so winsome as a white sail seaward blown, unless it be a white sail homeward bound, its voyage happily done.", "물살을 타고 흐르는 배, 바람을 받으려 방향을 트는 배, 노의 힘으로 솟구치듯 나아가는 배—오는 것도 있고 가는 것도 있었으니, 하나같이 바다와 머나먼 백성들과 이름난 곳들과 그 진귀함 때문에 탐내는 물건들을 떠올리게 했다."], ["And down the shore the friends went continuously till they came to a lake fed by back-water from the river, clear, deep, and without current.", "상상에 가장 마음을 사로잡는 것은 바다를 향해 부풀어 가는 흰 돛이거니와, 그에 견줄 것이 있다면 항해를 무사히 마치고 고향으로 돌아오는 흰 돛뿐이다."], ["An old palm-tree dominated the angle of the inlet; turning to the left at the foot of the tree, Malluch clapped his hands and shouted,", "두 사람은 강기슭을 따라 줄곧 내려가다가, 마침내 강물이 거슬러 흘러들어 이루어진 호수에 이르렀으니, 맑고 깊으며 물살이 없었다. 늙은 야자나무 한 그루가 후미의 모퉁이를 굽어보고 있었다. 그 나무 아래서 왼쪽으로 돌아서며, 말루크는 두 손을 마주치고 소리쳤다."]], [["“Look, look! The Orchard of Palms!”", "\"보시오, 저것을 보시오! 야자나무 동산이오!\""]], [["The scene was nowhere else to be found unless in the favored oases of Arabia or the Ptolemaean farms along the Nile; and to sustain a sensation new as it was delightful, Ben-Hur was admitted into a tract of land apparently without limit and level as a floor.", "이런 풍경은 아라비아의 축복받은 오아시스나 나일강을 따라 펼쳐진 프톨레마이오스 왕조의 농원이 아니고서는 어디서도 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그리고 그 황홀함은 새로운 만큼이나 즐거운 것이어서, 벤허는 끝이 없어 보이고 마룻바닥처럼 평평한 한 자락의 땅으로 들어섰다."], ["All under foot was fresh grass, in Syria the rarest and most beautiful production of the soil; if he looked up, it was to see the sky paley blue through the groinery of countless date-bearers, very patriarchs of their kind, so numerous and old, and of such mighty girth, so tall, so serried, so wide of branch, each branch so perfect with fronds, plumy and waxlike and brilliant, they seemed enchanters enchanted.", "발밑은 온통 싱그러운 풀이었으니, 시리아에서 풀이란 가장 보기 드물고 가장 아름다운 땅의 산물이었다. 위를 올려다보면, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 대추야자나무가 이룬 천장의 갈빗대 사이로 옅푸른 하늘이 보였다. 그 나무들은 제 종족의 진정한 족장이라 할 만큼 수도 많고 늙었으며, 둘레가 어찌나 굵고 키가 어찌나 크며 빽빽하고 가지가 넓게 뻗었던지, 그 가지마다 깃털 같고 밀랍 같고 눈부신 잎이 더없이 완벽하게 달려 있어, 마치 마법에 걸린 마법사들 같았다."], ["Here was the grass coloring the very atmosphere; there the lake, cool and clear, rippling but a few feet under the surface, and helping the trees to their long life in old age.", "여기서는 풀이 대기마저 물들이고 있었고, 저기서는 호수가 시원하고 맑게, 수면 바로 몇 자 아래에서 잔물결을 일으키며, 나무들이 늙어서도 오래 살도록 거들고 있었다."], ["Did the Grove of Daphne excel this one?", "다프네의 숲인들 이곳을 능가할 수 있었으랴?"], ["And the palms, as if they knew Ben-Hur’s thought, and would win him after a way of their own, seemed, as he passed under their arches, to stir and sprinkle him with dewy coolness.", "그리고 야자나무들은 마치 벤허의 마음을 알아채고 제 나름의 방식으로 그를 사로잡으려는 듯, 그가 그 아치 아래를 지날 때 흔들리며 이슬 머금은 서늘함을 그에게 뿌려 주는 것 같았다."]], [["The road wound in close parallelism with the shore of the lake; and when it carried the travellers down to the water’s edge, there was always on that side a shining expanse limited not far off by the opposite shore, on which, as on this one, no tree but the palm was permitted.", "길은 호숫가와 바짝 나란히 굽이쳐 이어졌다. 길이 나그네들을 물가까지 데려갈 때면, 그쪽으로는 언제나 빛나는 너른 수면이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 멀지 않은 맞은편 기슭이 그 끝을 가르고 있었다. 그 기슭에도 이쪽과 마찬가지로 야자나무 말고는 어떤 나무도 허락되지 않았다."]], [["“See that,” said Malluch, pointing to a giant of the place.", "\"저것을 보시오.\" 말루크가 그곳의 거목 한 그루를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“Each ring upon its trunk marks a year of its life.", "\"줄기에 새겨진 고리 하나하나가 그 나무가 산 한 해를 나타내오."], ["Count them from root to branch, and if the sheik tells you the grove was planted before the Seleucidæ were heard of in Antioch, do not doubt him.”", "뿌리에서 가지까지 그것을 세어 보시오. 셀레우코스 왕조가 안티오크에 이름을 알리기 전에 이 동산이 심어졌다고 족장이 말하거든, 의심하지 마시오.\""]], [["One may not look at a perfect palm-tree but that, with a subtlety all its own, it assumes a presence for itself, and makes a poet of the beholder.", "완벽한 야자나무를 바라보면, 그 나무는 제 나름의 미묘한 힘으로 스스로 하나의 존재감을 띠며, 보는 이를 시인으로 만든다."], ["This is the explanation of the honors it has received, beginning with the artists of the first kings, who could find no form in all the earth to serve them so well as a model for the pillars of their palaces and temples; and for the same reason Ben-Hur was moved to say,", "이것이 바로 그 나무가 받아 온 영예의 까닭이니, 그 영예는 최초의 왕들을 섬긴 예술가들에게서 비롯되었다. 그들은 온 땅을 통틀어 궁전과 신전의 기둥에 본보기로 삼기에 야자나무만큼 합당한 형태를 찾지 못했던 것이다. 그리고 같은 까닭에서 벤허는 이렇게 말하고 싶어졌다."]], [["“As I saw him at the stand to-day, good Malluch, Sheik Ilderim appeared to be a very common man.", "\"오늘 경주장에서 본 바로는, 말루크여, 일데림 족장은 지극히 평범한 사람으로 보였소."], ["The rabbis in Jerusalem would look down upon him, I fear, as a son of a dog of Edom.", "예루살렘의 랍비들이라면, 안타깝지만, 그를 에돔의 개의 자식이라며 깔보겠지요."], ["How came he in possession of the Orchard?", "그가 어떻게 이 동산을 차지하게 된 거요?"], ["And how has he been able to hold it against the greed of Roman governors?”", "그리고 로마 총독들의 탐욕에 맞서 어떻게 이것을 지켜 올 수 있었던 거요?\""]], [["“If blood derives excellence from time, son of Arrius, then is old Ilderim a man, though he be an uncircumcised Edomite.”", "\"혈통이 세월에서 그 탁월함을 얻는 것이라면, 아리우스의 아들이여, 늙은 일데림은 비록 할례받지 않은 에돔 사람일지언정 진정한 사내요.\""]], [["Malluch spoke warmly.", "말루크는 열을 띠어 말했다."]], [["“All his fathers before him were sheiks.", "\"그에 앞선 조상들이 모두 족장이었소."], ["One of them—I shall not say when he lived or did the good deed—once helped a king who was being hunted with swords.", "그중 한 분은—언제 사셨는지, 어떤 선행을 하셨는지는 말하지 않겠소—칼날에 쫓기던 어느 왕을 도운 적이 있었소."], ["The story says he loaned him a thousand horsemen, who knew the paths of the wilderness and its hiding-places as shepherds know the scant hills they inhabit with their flocks; and they carried him here and there until the opportunity came, and then with their spears they slew the enemy, and set him upon his throne again.", "전해 오는 이야기로는, 그분이 그 왕에게 기병 천 명을 빌려주었다 하오. 그들은 양치기들이 제 가축과 더불어 사는 메마른 언덕을 훤히 알듯이, 광야의 길과 그 숨을 곳들을 훤히 알고 있었소. 그들은 기회가 올 때까지 왕을 이리저리 데리고 다녔고, 마침내 창으로 적을 무찔러 그를 다시 왕좌에 앉혔소."], ["And the king, it is said, remembered the service, and brought the son of the Desert to this place, and bade him set up his tent and bring his family and his herds, for the lake and trees, and all the land from the river to the nearest mountains, were his and his children’s forever.", "전하는 말로는, 그 왕이 그 공을 기억하여 이 사막의 아들을 이곳으로 데려와, 천막을 치고 가족과 가축 떼를 데려오라 일렀다 하오. 호수와 나무들, 그리고 강에서 가장 가까운 산에 이르는 온 땅이 그와 그 자손들의 것으로 영원히 주어졌기 때문이오."], ["And they have never been disturbed in the possession.", "그리고 그들은 그 소유를 한 번도 방해받지 않았소."], ["The rulers succeeding have found it policy to keep good terms with the tribe, to whom the Lord has given increase of men and horses, and camels and riches, making them masters of many highways between cities; so that it is with them any time they please to say to commerce, ‘Go in peace,’ or ‘Stop,’ and what they say shall be done.", "뒤를 이은 통치자들은 그 부족과 좋은 관계를 유지하는 것이 상책임을 알게 되었으니, 주께서 그 부족에게 사람과 말과 낙타와 재물을 더하여 주시어, 도시와 도시를 잇는 수많은 길의 주인으로 삼으셨기 때문이오. 그리하여 그들은 언제든 마음만 먹으면 교역을 향해 '편히 가라' 하거나 '멈추라'"], ["Even the prefect in the citadel overlooking Antioch thinks it happy day with him when Ilderim, surnamed the Generous on account of good deeds done unto all manner of men, with his wives and children, and his trains of camels and horses, and his belongings of sheik, moving as our fathers Abraham and Jacob moved, comes up to exchange briefly his bitter wells for the pleasantness you see about us.”", "할 수 있고, 그들이 말한 바는 그대로 이루어지오. 안티오크를 굽어보는 성채의 지방관조차도, 일데림이—온갖 부류의 사람들에게 베푼 선행 덕에 '관대한 자'라 불리는 그 일데림이—제 아내들과 자식들, 낙타와 말의 행렬과 족장의 살림붙이를 거느리고, 우리 조상 아브라함과 야곱이 옮겨 다녔듯이 옮겨 와서, 그 쓰디쓴 우물을 잠시 그대가 우리 둘레에서 보는 이 즐거움과 맞바꾸려 올라오는 날을, 제게 복된 날이라 여긴다오.\""]], [["“How is it, then?” said Ben-Hur, who had been listening unmindful of the slow gait of the dromedaries.", "\"그렇다면 어찌 된 일이오?\" 단봉낙타들의 느린 걸음에는 아랑곳없이 귀를 기울이고 있던 벤허가 말했다."], ["“I saw the sheik tear his beard while he cursed himself that he had put trust in a Roman.", "\"나는 그 족장이 로마인을 믿었던 자신을 저주하며 수염을 쥐어뜯는 것을 보았소."], ["Cæsar, had he heard him, might have said, ‘I like not such a friend as this; put him away.’”", "만일 카이사르가 그 말을 들었다면, '나는 이런 자를 벗으로 삼고 싶지 않다. 그를 내쳐라' 하고 말했을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“It would be but shrewd judgment,”", "\"그것은 빈틈없는 판단일 따름이오.\""], ["Malluch replied, smiling. “Ilderim is not a lover of Rome; he has a grievance.", "말루크가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"일데림은 로마를 사랑하는 자가 아니오."], ["Three years ago the Parthians rode across the road from Bozra to Damascus, and fell upon a caravan laden, among other things, with the incoming tax-returns of a district over that way.", "그에게는 원한이 있소. 삼 년 전, 파르티아인들이 보스라에서 다마스쿠스로 가는 길을 가로질러 달려와, 여러 짐 가운데서도 그쪽 한 지방에서 거두어들인 세금을 싣고 오던 대상을 덮쳤소."], ["They slew every creature taken, which the censors in Rome could have forgiven if the imperial treasure had been spared and forwarded.", "그들은 붙잡은 자를 하나도 남김없이 죽였소. 로마의 감찰관들도 황실의 재물이 무사히 보내졌더라면 그것은 용서했을 거요."], ["The farmers of the taxes, being chargeable with the loss, complained to Cæsar, and Cæsar held Herod to payment, and Herod, on his part, seized property of Ilderim, whom he charged with treasonable neglect of duty.", "손실의 책임을 진 세금 도급업자들이 카이사르에게 하소연했고, 카이사르는 헤롯에게 변상을 물렸으며, 헤롯은 그 나름대로 일데림의 재산을 몰수했소. 그에게 직무를 게을리한 반역의 죄를 씌운 것이오."], ["The sheik appealed to Cæsar, and Cæsar has made him such answer as might be looked for from the unwinking sphinx.", "족장은 카이사르에게 탄원했으나, 카이사르는 눈 한 번 깜박이지 않는 스핑크스에게서나 들을 법한 대답을 그에게 주었소."], ["The old man’s heart has been aching sore ever since, and he nurses his wrath, and takes pleasure in its daily growth.”", "그 후로 노인의 가슴은 줄곧 쓰리게 아팠고, 그는 제 분노를 키우며 날마다 그것이 자라는 데서 즐거움을 찾고 있소.\""]], [["“He can do nothing, Malluch.”", "\"그가 할 수 있는 일은 없소, 말루크.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["said Malluch, “that involves another explanation, which I will give you, if we can draw nearer.", "말루크가 말했다. \"거기에는 또 다른 설명이 따르는데, 우리가 더 가까이 다가설 수 있으면 그것을 들려드리리다."], ["But see!—the hospitality of the sheik begins early—the children are speaking to you.”", "한데 보시오!—족장의 환대가 일찌감치 시작되었소—아이들이 그대에게 말을 걸고 있소.\""]], [["The dromedaries stopped, and Ben-Hur looked down upon some little girls of the Syrian peasant class, who were offering him their baskets filled with dates.", "단봉낙타들이 멈추었고, 벤허는 시리아 농민 계급의 어린 소녀들을 내려다보았다. 그들은 대추야자 열매를 가득 담은 바구니를 그에게 내밀고 있었다."], ["The fruit was freshly gathered, and not to be refused; he stooped and took it, and as he did so a man in the tree by which they were halted cried, “Peace to you, and welcome!”", "갓 따 온 열매라 마다할 수 없었다. 그가 몸을 굽혀 그것을 받았는데, 그렇게 하는 사이 그들이 멈추어 선 나무 위에 있던 한 사내가 외쳤다. \"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 어서 오시오!\""]], [["Their thanks said to the children, the friends moved on at such gait as the animals chose.", "아이들에게 고맙다는 인사를 건넨 뒤, 두 사람은 짐승들이 고르는 걸음대로 다시 나아갔다."]], [["“You must know,”", "\"알아 두셔야 할 것은,\""], ["Malluch continued, pausing now and then to dispose of a date, “that the merchant Simonides gives me his confidence, and sometimes flatters me by taking me into council; and as I attend him at his house, I have made acquaintance with many of his friends, who, knowing my footing with the host, talk to him freely in my presence.", "말루크가 이따금 멈추어 대추야자를 입에 넣으며 말을 이었다. \"상인 시모니데스가 내게 신임을 베풀어, 때로는 나를 의논 자리에 끌어들이는 영광을 주기도 한다는 거요. 그의 집에서 그를 시중들다 보니, 나는 그의 벗을 많이 알게 되었소."], ["In that way I became somewhat intimate with Sheik Ilderim.”", "그들은 내가 주인과 가까운 사이임을 알기에, 내가 있는 자리에서도 그에게 거리낌 없이 이야기하오. 그런 식으로 나는 일데림 족장과도 어느 정도 가까워졌소.\""]], [["For a moment Ben-Hur’s attention wandered.", "잠시 벤허의 마음이 딴 데로 흘렀다."], ["Before his mind’s eye there arose the image, pure, gentle, and appealing, of Esther, the merchant’s daughter.", "그의 마음의 눈앞에는 상인의 딸 에스더의 모습이 떠올랐으니, 순수하고 부드러우며 사람의 마음을 끄는 모습이었다."], ["Her dark eyes bright with the peculiar Jewish lustre met his in modest gaze; he heard her step as when she approached him with the wine, and her voice as she tendered him the cup; and he acknowledged to himself again all the sympathy she manifested for him, and manifested so plainly that words were unnecessary, and so sweetly that words would have been but a detraction.", "유대인 특유의 광채로 빛나는 그녀의 검은 눈이 다소곳한 눈길로 그의 눈을 마주했고, 그녀가 포도주를 들고 그에게 다가올 때의 발소리와, 잔을 건네던 그녀의 목소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 그는 그녀가 자기에게 보였던 그 모든 공감을, 말이 필요 없을 만큼 분명하게, 또 말이 오히려 흠이 되었을 만큼 곱게 드러내 보였던 그 공감을 다시금 마음속으로 인정했다."], ["The vision was exceeding pleasant, but upon his turning to Malluch, it flew away.", "그 환영은 더없이 즐거웠으나, 그가 말루크에게로 고개를 돌리자 이내 날아가 버렸다."]], [["“A few weeks ago,”", "\"몇 주 전에,\""], ["said Malluch, continuing, “the old Arab called on Simonides, and found me present.", "말루크가 말을 이었다. \"그 늙은 아랍인이 시모니데스를 찾아왔는데, 마침 내가 그 자리에 있었소."], ["I observed he seemed much moved about something, and, in deference, offered to withdraw, but he himself forbade me.", "보아하니 그가 무언가로 몹시 심란해하는 듯하여, 나는 예를 차려 물러가겠노라 했으나, 그가 손수 나를 말렸소. '그대가 이스라엘 사람이니,'"], ["‘As you are an Israelite,’ he said, ‘stay, for I have a strange story to tell.’ The emphasis on the word Israelite excited my curiosity.", "그가 말했소, '머물러 주시오. 내게 들려줄 이상한 이야기가 하나 있소.' 이스라엘 사람이라는 말에 힘을 준 것이 내 호기심을 돋우었소."], ["I remained, and this is in substance his story—I cut it short because we are drawing nigh the tent, and I leave the details to the good man himself.", "나는 머물렀고, 그가 들려준 이야기의 요지가 이렇소—우리가 천막에 가까워지고 있으니 짧게 줄이고, 자세한 내막은 그 어른께 맡기겠소."], ["A good many years ago, three men called at Ilderim’s tent out in the wilderness.", "여러 해 전, 세 사람이 광야에 있는 일데림의 천막을 찾아왔소."], ["They were all foreigners, a Hindoo, a Greek, and an Egyptian; and they had come on camels, the largest he had ever seen, and all white.", "모두 이방인이었으니, 한 사람은 힌두인, 한 사람은 그리스인, 한 사람은 이집트인이었소. 그들은 낙타를 타고 왔는데, 그가 여태껏 본 가운데 가장 큰 낙타들이었고, 하나같이 흰색이었소."], ["He welcomed them, and gave them rest.", "그는 그들을 맞이하여 쉬게 했소."], ["Next morning they arose and prayed a prayer new to the sheik—a prayer addressed to God and his son—this with much mystery besides.", "이튿날 아침 그들은 일어나 족장에게는 낯선 기도를 올렸소—하느님과 그 아들에게 드리는 기도였는데—그 밖에도 신비로운 것이 많았소."], ["After breaking fast with him, the Egyptian told who they were, and whence they had come.", "그와 함께 아침을 든 뒤, 이집트인이 자기들이 누구이며 어디서 왔는지를 밝혔소."], ["Each had seen a star, out of which a voice had bidden them go to Jerusalem and ask, Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’ They obeyed.", "세 사람은 저마다 별을 하나씩 보았고, 그 별에서 들려온 목소리가 그들에게 예루살렘으로 가서 '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?' 하고 물으라 명했다는 것이오. 그들은 그 명에 따랐소."], ["From Jerusalem they were led by a star to Bethlehem, where, in a cave, they found a child newly born, which they fell down and worshipped; and after worshipping it, and giving it costly presents, and bearing witness of what it was, they took to their camels, and fled without pause to the sheik, because if Herod—meaning him surnamed the Great—could lay hands upon them, he would certainly kill them.", "예루살렘에서 그들은 별의 인도를 받아 베들레헴으로 갔고, 거기 한 동굴에서 갓 태어난 아기를 찾아내어, 엎드려 경배했소. 그리고 그 아기를 경배하고, 값진 예물을 바치며, 그 아기가 어떤 분인지 증언한 뒤, 낙타에 올라 쉬지 않고 족장에게로 달아났소. 헤롯이—'대왕'이라 불리는 그 헤롯 말이오—그들에게 손을 뻗칠 수 있게 되면 틀림없이 그들을 죽일 것이기 때문이었소."], ["And, faithful to his habit, the sheik took care of them, and kept them concealed for a year, when they departed, leaving with him gifts of great value, and each going a separate way.”", "그리하여 제 습관에 충실하게, 족장은 그들을 돌보아 일 년 동안 숨겨 주었고, 그들은 그에게 매우 값진 선물을 남긴 채 떠나가, 저마다 다른 길을 갔소.\""]], [["“It is, indeed, a most wonderful story,”", "\"참으로 더없이 놀라운 이야기로군요.\""], ["Ben-Hur exclaimed at its conclusion.", "이야기가 끝나자 벤허가 외쳤다."], ["“What did you say they were to ask at Jerusalem?”", "\"예루살렘에서 무엇을 물으라 했다고 하셨소?\""]], [["“They were to ask, ‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’”", "\"'유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?' 하고 묻기로 되어 있었소.\""]], [["“Was that all?”", "\"그게 전부였소?\""]], [["“There was more to the question, but I cannot recall it.”", "\"질문에는 더 있었으나, 그것은 기억나지 않소.\""]], [["“And they found the child?”", "\"그래서 그 아기를 찾아냈소?\""]], [["“Yes, and worshipped him.”", "\"그렇소, 그리고 그를 경배했소.\""]], [["“It is a miracle, Malluch.”", "\"그것은 기적이오, 말루크.\""]], [["“Ilderim is a grave man, though excitable as all Arabs are.", "\"일데림은 진중한 사람이오. 비록 모든 아랍인이 그렇듯 격하기는 하지만 말이오."], ["A lie on his tongue is impossible.”", "그의 혀에 거짓말이 오르는 일은 있을 수 없소.\""]], [["Malluch spoke positively.", "말루크는 단호하게 말했다."], ["Thereupon the dromedaries were forgotten, and, quite as unmindful of their riders, they turned off the road to the growing grass.", "그 바람에 단봉낙타들은 잊히고 말았으니, 그놈들도 제 타는 이들에게는 아랑곳없이 길을 벗어나 자라는 풀밭으로 향했다."]], [["“Has Ilderim heard nothing more of the three men?”", "\"일데림은 그 세 사람의 소식을 그 뒤로 더 듣지 못했소?\""], ["asked Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 물었다."], ["“What became of them?”", "\"그들은 어떻게 되었소?\""]], [["“Ah, yes, that was the cause of his coming to Simonides the day of which I was speaking.", "\"아, 그렇소, 바로 그것이 내가 말한 그날 그가 시모니데스를 찾아온 까닭이었소."], ["Only the night before that day the Egyptian reappeared to him.”", "바로 그 전날 밤, 그 이집트인이 그에게 다시 나타났던 거요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디에서요?\""]], [["“Here at the door of the tent to which we are coming.”", "\"바로 여기, 우리가 다가가고 있는 그 천막의 문 앞에서요.\""]], [["“How knew he the man?”", "\"그가 어떻게 그 사람을 알아보았소?\""]], [["“As you knew the horses to-day—by face and manner.”", "\"오늘 그대가 그 말들을 알아보았듯이—얼굴과 몸가짐으로 알아보았소.\""]], [["“By nothing else?”", "\"그 밖에 다른 것은 없었소?\""]], [["“He rode the same great white camel, and gave him the same name—Balthasar, the Egyptian.”", "\"그는 똑같은 큰 흰 낙타를 타고 있었고, 똑같은 이름을 댔소—이집트인 발타사르라고.\""]], [["“It is a wonder of the Lord’s!”", "\"그것은 주님의 경이로다!\""]], [["Ben-Hur spoke with excitement.", "벤허가 흥분하여 말했다."]], [["And Malluch, wondering, asked, “Why so?”", "그러자 말루크가 의아해하며 물었다. \"어찌 그렇소?\""]], [["“Balthasar, you said?”", "\"발타사르라 하셨소?\""]], [["“Yes. Balthasar, the Egyptian.”", "\"그렇소. 이집트인 발타사르요.\""]], [["“That was the name the old man gave us at the fountain today.”", "\"그것은 오늘 그 노인이 샘터에서 우리에게 댄 이름이오.\""]], [["Then, at the reminder, Malluch became excited.", "그 말에 일깨워져, 이번에는 말루크가 흥분했다."]], [["“It is true,”", "\"정말이오.\""], ["he said; “and the camel was the same—and you saved the man’s life.”", "그가 말했다. \"낙타도 똑같았소—그리고 그대가 그 사람의 목숨을 구했소.\""]], [["“And the woman,”", "\"그리고 그 여인은,\""], ["said Ben-Hur, like one speaking to himself—“the woman was his daughter.”", "벤허가 혼잣말하듯 말했다. \"그 여인은 그의 딸이었소.\""]], [["He fell to thinking; and even the reader will say he was having a vision of the woman, and that it was more welcome than that of Esther, if only because it stayed longer with him; but no—", "그는 생각에 잠겼다. 독자조차도 그가 그 여인의 환영을 보고 있었다고, 그리고 그것이 에스더의 환영보다 더 반가운 것이었다고 말할 법하다. 적어도 그것이 더 오래 그에게 머물렀다는 까닭만으로도. 하지만 그렇지 않았으니—"]], [["“Tell me again,”", "\"다시 말해 주시오.\""], ["he said, presently.", "그가 이윽고 말했다."], ["“Were the three to ask, ‘Where is he that is to be King of the Jews?’”", "\"그 세 사람이 '유대인의 왕이 되실 이가 어디 계시냐?' 하고 물어야 했던 거요?\""]], [["“Not exactly. The words were BORN TO BE KING OF THE JEWS.", "\"정확히 그것은 아니오. 그 말은 '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이'였소."], ["Those were the words as the old sheik caught them first in the desert, and he has ever since been waiting the coming of the king; nor can any one shake his faith that he will come.”", "늙은 족장이 사막에서 처음 들은 말이 바로 그것이었고, 그 후로 그는 그 왕이 오기를 줄곧 기다리고 있소. 또한 그 왕이 오리라는 그의 믿음은 누구도 흔들 수 없소.\""]], [["“How—as king?” “Yes, and bringing the doom of Rome—so says the sheik.”", "\"그렇소, 그리고 로마에 멸망을 가져오면서 온다는 거요—족장은 그렇게 말하오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur kept silent awhile, thinking and trying to control his feelings.", "벤허는 한동안 잠자코 있으면서, 생각에 잠긴 채 제 감정을 다스리려 애썼다."]], [["“The old man is one of many millions,”", "\"그 노인은 수백만 사람 가운데 하나요.\" 그가 천천히 말했다."], ["he said, slowly—“one of many millions each with a wrong to avenge; and this strange faith, Malluch, is bread and wine to his hope; for who but a Herod may be King of the Jews while Rome endures?", "\"갚아야 할 원한을 저마다 품은 수백만 사람 가운데 하나란 말이오. 그리고 이 기이한 믿음은, 말루크여, 그의 희망에 빵이요 포도주요. 로마가 버티고 있는 한, 헤롯이 아니고서 누가 유대인의 왕이 될 수 있겠소?"], ["But, following the story, did you hear what Simonides said to him?”", "한데, 이야기를 이어 가자면, 시모니데스가 그에게 무어라 했는지 들으셨소?\""]], [["“If Ilderim is a grave man, Simonides is a wise one,”", "\"일데림이 진중한 사람이라면, 시모니데스는 지혜로운 사람이오.\""], ["Malluch replied.", "말루크가 대답했다."], ["“I listened, and he said— But hark!", "\"나는 귀를 기울였고, 그는 이렇게 말했소— 한데 들어 보시오!"], ["Some one comes overtaking us.”", "누군가 우리를 따라잡으며 오고 있소.\""]], [["The noise grew louder, until presently they heard the rumble of wheels mixed with the beating of horse-hoofs—a moment later Sheik Ilderim himself appeared on horseback, followed by a train, among which were the four wine-red Arabs drawing the chariot.", "소리가 점점 커지더니, 이윽고 그들은 말발굽 소리에 뒤섞인 바퀴 구르는 소리를 들었다—그리고 한순간 뒤, 일데림 족장이 몸소 말을 타고 나타났고, 그 뒤로 행렬이 따랐는데, 그 가운데에는 전차를 끄는 포도주빛 아랍 말 네 마리가 있었다."], ["The sheik’s chin, in its muffling of long white beard, was drooped upon his breast.", "족장의 턱은 길고 흰 수염에 파묻힌 채 가슴 위로 떨구어져 있었다."], ["Our friends had out-travelled him; but at sight of them he raised his head and spoke kindly.", "우리의 두 벗이 그를 앞질러 와 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그들을 보자 그는 고개를 들고 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Peace to you!—Ah, my friend Malluch!", "\"그대들에게 평화가 있기를!—아, 내 벗 말루크여!"], ["Welcome! And tell me you are not going, but just come; that you have something for me from the good Simonides—may the Lord of his fathers keep him in life for many years to come!", "어서 오시오! 그대가 떠나는 길이 아니라 막 도착한 길이라고, 그리고 그 어진 시모니데스에게서 내게 전할 무언가를 가져왔노라고 말해 주시오—그분의 조상의 주께서 그분을 앞으로도 오래오래 살게 해 주시기를!"], ["Ay, take up the straps, both of you, and follow me.", "자, 두 사람 다 짐끈을 챙겨 나를 따라오시오."], ["I have bread and leben, or, if you prefer it, arrack, and the flesh of young kid. Come!”", "내게 빵과 레벤이 있고, 그게 싫거든 아라크와 어린 염소 고기도 있소. 자, 오시오!\""]], [["They followed after him to the door of the tent, in which, when they were dismounted, he stood to receive them, holding a platter with three cups filled with creamy liquor just drawn from a great smoke-stained skin bottle, pendent from the central post.", "그들은 그를 따라 천막 문에 이르렀고, 거기서 낙타에서 내리자, 그는 그들을 맞으려 서 있었으니, 손에는 잔 세 개가 담긴 쟁반을 들고 있었다. 잔에는 가운데 기둥에 매달린, 연기에 그을린 커다란 가죽 부대에서 막 따라 낸 크림빛 음료가 담겨 있었다."]], [["“Drink,”", "\"마시오.\""], ["he said, heartily, “drink, for this is the fear-naught of the tentmen.”", "그가 진심으로 말했다. \"마시오, 이것은 천막에 사는 자들의 두려움을 모르게 하는 술이오.\""]], [["They each took a cup, and drank till but the foam remained.", "그들은 저마다 잔을 들어, 거품만 남을 때까지 마셨다."]], [["“Enter now, in God’s name.”", "\"이제 하느님의 이름으로 들어오시오.\""]], [["And when they were gone in, Malluch took the sheik aside, and spoke to him privately; after which he went to Ben-Hur and excused himself.", "그리고 그들이 안으로 들어서자, 말루크는 족장을 한쪽으로 데려가 은밀히 이야기를 나누었다. 그러고 나서 그는 벤허에게로 가 양해를 구했다."]], [["“I have told the sheik about you, and he will give you the trial of his horses in the morning.", "\"내가 족장에게 그대에 대해 이야기해 두었으니, 그가 아침에 그대에게 자기 말들을 시험해 볼 기회를 줄 거요."], ["He is your friend.", "그는 그대의 벗이오."], ["Having done for you all I can, you must do the rest, and let me return to Antioch.", "내가 그대를 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 했으니, 나머지는 그대가 해내야 하오."], ["There is one there who has my promise to meet him to-night. I have no choice but to go.", "나는 안티오크로 돌아가게 해 주시오. 그곳에 오늘 밤 만나기로 약속한 사람이 하나 있소."], ["I will come back to-morrow prepared, if all goes well in the meantime, to stay with you until the games are over.”", "가지 않을 도리가 없소. 그동안 만사가 잘되면, 나는 내일 채비를 갖추고 돌아와 경기가 끝날 때까지 그대와 함께 머무르겠소.\""]], [["With blessings given and received, Malluch set out in return.", "축복을 주고받은 뒤, 말루크는 돌아가는 길에 올랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER XI", "# CHAPTER XI"]], [["What time the lower horn of a new moon touched the castellated piles on Mount Sulpius, and two thirds of the people of Antioch were out on their house-tops comforting themselves with the night breeze when it blew, and with fans when it failed, Simonides sat in the chair which had come to be a part of him, and from the terrace looked down over the river, and his ships a-swing at their moorings.", "초승달의 아래쪽 뿔이 술피우스 산 위의 성채 같은 돌무더기에 닿을 무렵, 안티오크 주민의 삼분의 이가 지붕 위로 나와, 바람이 불면 밤바람으로, 바람이 잦아들면 부채로 더위를 달래고 있을 때, 시모니데스는 이제 그의 몸의 일부가 되다시피 한 의자에 앉아, 테라스에서 강과, 정박지에 매여 흔들거리는 자신의 배들을 내려다보고 있었다."], ["The wall at his back cast its shadow broadly over the water to the opposite shore.", "등 뒤의 벽은 그 그림자를 물 위로 넓게 드리워 맞은편 기슭까지 뻗게 했다."], ["Above him the endless tramp upon the bridge went on.", "그의 머리 위로는 다리 위를 끝없이 오가는 발소리가 이어졌다."], ["Esther was holding a plate for him containing his frugal supper—some wheaten cakes, light as wafers, some honey, and a bowl of milk, into which he now and then dipped the wafers after dipping them into the honey.", "에스더는 그의 검소한 저녁상을 담은 접시를 들고 있었다. 살갗처럼 얇은 밀전병 몇 개와 꿀, 그리고 우유 한 사발이었는데, 그는 이따금 전병을 꿀에 적신 다음 우유에 담갔다."]], [["“Malluch is a laggard to-night,”", "\"말루크가 오늘 밤은 굼뜨구나.\""], ["he said, showing where his thoughts were.", "그는 자신의 생각이 어디에 있는지 드러내며 말했다."]], [["“Do you believe he will come?”", "\"그가 올 거라고 믿으세요?\""], ["Esther asked.", "에스더가 물었다."]], [["“Unless he has taken to the sea or the desert, and is yet following on, he will come.”", "\"바다나 사막으로 들어가서 아직 뒤를 좇고 있는 게 아니라면, 올 게다.\""]], [["Simonides spoke with quiet confidence.", "시모니데스는 조용한 확신을 담아 말했다."]], [["“He may write,”", "\"편지를 보낼지도 모르지요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Not so, Esther.", "\"그렇지 않다, 에스더."], ["He would have despatched a letter when he found he could not return, and told me so; because I have not received such a letter, I know he can come, and will.”", "돌아올 수 없다는 걸 알았다면 편지를 부쳐서 내게 그렇게 알렸을 게다. 그런 편지를 받지 못했으니, 나는 그가 올 수 있고 또 올 것임을 안다.\""]], [["“I hope so,”", "\"그러길 바라요.\""], ["she said, very softly.", "그녀가 아주 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["Something in the utterance attracted his attention; it might have been the tone, it might have been the wish.", "그 말투에 담긴 무언가가 그의 주의를 끌었다. 어조 때문이었을 수도 있고, 그 바람 때문이었을 수도 있다."], ["The smallest bird cannot light upon the greatest tree without sending a shock to its most distant fibre; every mind is at times no less sensitive to the most trifling words.", "아무리 작은 새라도 아무리 큰 나무에 내려앉으면 그 가장 먼 가지 끝까지 떨림을 전한다. 어떤 마음도 때로는 가장 하찮은 말에 그에 못지않게 예민해진다."]], [["“You wish him to come, Esther?”", "\"그가 오기를 바라느냐, 에스더?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she said, lifting her eyes to his.", "그녀가 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Why?", "\"왜냐?"], ["Can you tell me?”", "말해 줄 수 있겠느냐?\""], ["he persisted.", "그가 캐물었다."]], [["“Because”—she hesitated, then began again—“because the young man is—”", "\"그건요\"—그녀는 머뭇거리다가 다시 입을 열었다—\"그 젊은이가—\""], ["The stop was full.", "말은 거기서 뚝 끊겼다."]], [["“Our master.", "\"우리 주인이라서."], ["Is that the word?”", "그 말이 하고 싶은 게냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And you still think I should not suffer him to go away without telling him to come, if he chooses, and take us—and all we have—all, Esther—the goods, the shekels, the ships, the slaves, and the mighty credit, which is a mantle of cloth of gold and finest silver spun for me by the greatest of the angels of men—Success.”", "\"그런데도 너는, 그가 원한다면 와서 우리를—그리고 우리가 가진 모든 것을—전부, 에스더—재물도, 은화도, 배도, 노예도, 그리고 사람의 천사들 가운데 으뜸가는 자, 곧 성공이 나를 위해 금실과 더없이 고운 은실로 자아 준 외투와도 같은 막대한 신용까지—데려가라고 일러 주지 않은 채 그를 떠나보내서는 안 된다고 여전히 생각하느냐.\""]], [["She made no answer.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Does that move you nothing? No?” he said, with the slightest taint of bitterness.", "\"그것이 네 마음을 조금도 움직이지 못하느냐? 못해?\" 그가 희미한 씁쓸함을 담아 말했다."], ["“Well, well, I have found, Esther, the worst reality is never unendurable when it comes out from behind the clouds through which we at first see it darkly—never—not even the rack. I suppose it will be so with death.", "\"글쎄, 글쎄, 에스더, 나는 깨달았다. 처음에는 어슴푸레하게밖에 보이지 않던 그 구름을 뚫고 나오기만 하면, 가장 끔찍한 현실도 결코 견디지 못할 것이 아니라는 걸—결코 그렇지 않다는 걸—고문대조차 그렇다는 걸."], ["And by that philosophy the slavery to which we are going must afterwhile become sweet.", "죽음도 그러하리라 생각한다. 그런 이치라면, 우리가 향해 가는 노예 신세도 머지않아 달콤해질 게다."], ["It pleases me even now to think what a favored man our master is.", "지금도 나는 우리 주인이 얼마나 복받은 사람인지 생각하면 흐뭇하구나."], ["The fortune cost him nothing—not an anxiety, not a drop of sweat, not so much as a thought; it attaches to him undreamed of, and in his youth.", "그 재산은 그에게 아무 대가도 치르게 하지 않았다—근심 하나, 땀 한 방울, 생각 한 조각조차도. 그것은 꿈에도 그려 보지 못한 채, 젊은 나이에 그에게 들러붙는다."], ["And, Esther, let me waste a little vanity with the reflection; he gets what he could not go into the market and buy with all the pelf in a sum—thee, my child, my darling; thou blossom from the tomb of my lost Rachel!”", "그리고 에스더, 이런 생각으로 약간의 허영을 부려 보게 해 다오. 그는 온 재물을 다 합쳐도 시장에 나가 살 수 없는 것을 얻는다—바로 너를, 내 아이, 내 사랑을. 내가 잃은 라헬의 무덤에서 피어난 꽃이여!\""]], [["He drew her to him, and kissed her twice—once for herself, once for her mother.", "그는 그녀를 끌어당겨 두 번 입 맞추었다—한 번은 그녀를 위해, 한 번은 그녀의 어머니를 위해."]], [["“Say not so,”", "\"그런 말씀 마세요.\""], ["she said, when his hand fell from her neck.", "그의 손이 그녀의 목에서 떨어지자 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Let us think better of him; he knows what sorrow is, and will set us free.”", "\"그를 더 좋게 생각해요. 그는 슬픔이 무엇인지 아니, 우리를 풀어 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah, thy instincts are fine, Esther; and thou knowest I lean upon them in doubtful cases where good or bad is to be pronounced of a person standing before thee as he stood this morning.", "\"아, 에스더, 너의 직감은 빼어나구나. 그리고 너도 알다시피, 오늘 아침 그가 내 앞에 섰던 것처럼 누군가가 앞에 섰을 때, 그가 좋은 사람인지 나쁜 사람인지 가려야 하는 미심쩍은 경우에 나는 너의 직감에 기댄다."], ["But—but”—his voice rose and hardened—“these limbs upon which I cannot stand—this body drawn and beaten out of human shape—they are not all I bring him of myself. Oh no, no!", "그러나—그러나\"—그의 목소리가 높아지고 굳어졌다—\"내가 그 위에 설 수도 없는 이 두 다리—사람의 형상에서 비틀리고 두들겨 망가진 이 몸뚱이—이것만이 내가 그에게 바치는 나의 전부가 아니다. 오, 아니다, 아니야!"], ["I bring him a soul which has triumphed over torture and Roman malice keener than any torture—I bring him a mind which has eyes to see gold at a distance farther than the ships of Solomon sailed, and power to bring it to hand—ay, Esther, into my palm here for the fingers to grip and keep lest it take wings at some other’s word—a mind skilled at scheming”—he stopped and laughed—“Why, Esther, before the new moon which in the courts of the Temple on the Holy Hill they are this moment celebrating passes into its next quartering I could ring the world so as to startle even Cæsar; for know you, child, I have that faculty which is better than any one sense, better than a perfect body, better than courage and will, better than experience, ordinarily the best product of the longest lives—the faculty divinest of men, but which”—he stopped, and laughed again, not bitterly, but with real zest—“but which even the great do not sufficiently account, while with the herd it is a non-existent—the faculty of drawing men to my purpose and holding them faithfully to its achievement, by which, as against things to be done, I multiply myself into hundreds and thousands.", "나는 그에게 고문과, 어떤 고문보다도 더 매서운 로마의 악의를 이겨 낸 영혼을 바친다—나는 그에게, 솔로몬의 배들이 항해한 거리보다 더 멀리 떨어진 황금을 볼 수 있는 눈과, 그것을 손안에 가져올 힘을 지닌—그래, 에스더, 누군가 다른 이의 말에 날개를 달고 날아가 버리지 않도록 손가락으로 움켜쥐어 붙잡을 수 있게 바로 여기 내 손바닥 안으로 가져올 힘을 지닌—책략에 능한 정신을 바친다\"—그는 멈추고 웃었다—\"이것 봐라, 에스더, 지금 이 순간 거룩한 언덕의 성전 뜰에서 사람들이 경축하고 있는 저 초승달이 다음 사분에 들어서기도 전에, 나는 카이사르조차 깜짝 놀라게 할 만큼 온 세상을 울려 댈 수 있다. 알아 두어라, 얘야, 나에게는 어떤 감각 하나보다도 나은, 완벽한 육체보다도 나은, 용기와 의지보다도 나은, 흔히 가장 긴 생애의 가장 좋은 산물인 경험보다도 나은—사람에게 가장 신성한 능력이 있다. 그러나\"—그는 멈추고 다시 웃었다, 씁쓸하게가 아니라 참된 흥취로—\"그러나 위대한 자들조차 충분히 헤아리지 못하고, 무리들에게는 아예 있지도 않은 것으로 치는 그 능력—사람들을 내 뜻으로 끌어들여 그 성취에 충실히 매어 두는 능력, 그리하여 해야 할 일들을 두고 나 자신을 수백 수천으로 불려 내는 능력 말이다."], ["So the captains of my ships plough the seas, and bring me honest returns; so Malluch follows the youth, our master, and will”—just then a footstep was heard upon the terrace—“Ha, Esther!", "그래서 내 배의 선장들은 바다를 갈며 내게 정직한 이문을 가져온다. 그래서 말루크는 그 젊은이, 우리 주인을 좇고 있고, 또 그리하여\"—바로 그때 테라스 위에서 발소리가 들렸다—\"하, 에스더!"], ["said I not so? He is here—and we will have tidings.", "내가 그렇게 말하지 않았느냐? 그가 왔다—이제 우리는 소식을 듣게 될 게다."], ["For thy sake, sweet child—my lily just budded—I pray the Lord God, who has not forgotten his wandering sheep of Israel, that they be good and comforting.", "사랑스러운 아이야, 너를 위하여—이제 막 봉오리를 맺은 내 백합아—나는 이스라엘의 길 잃은 양들을 잊지 않으신 주 하느님께, 그 소식이 좋고 위안이 되기를 빈다."], ["Now we will know if he will let thee go with all thy beauty, and me with all my faculties.”", "이제 우리는 알게 되리라, 그가 너를 그 온갖 아름다움과 함께, 나를 그 온갖 능력과 함께 놓아줄지를.\""]], [["Malluch came to the chair.", "말루크가 의자 곁으로 다가왔다."]], [["“Peace to you, good master,”", "\"평안하시기를, 어지신 주인님.\""], ["he said, with a low obeisance—“and to you, Esther, most excellent of daughters.”", "그는 깊이 머리를 숙이며 말했다—\"그리고 에스더, 더없이 훌륭한 따님께도 평안을.\""]], [["He stood before them deferentially, and the attitude and the address left it difficult to define his relation to them; the one was that of a servant, the other indicated the familiar and friend.", "그는 공손하게 두 사람 앞에 섰는데, 그 태도와 인사말은 그와 그들의 관계를 가늠하기 어렵게 했다. 하나는 종의 것이었고, 다른 하나는 친밀한 벗을 가리켰다."], ["On the other side, Simonides, as was his habit in business, after answering the salutation went straight to the subject.", "한편 시모니데스는 사업에서 늘 그러하듯 인사에 답한 뒤 곧장 본론으로 들어갔다."]], [["“What of the young man, Malluch?”", "\"그 젊은이는 어떻더냐, 말루크?\""]], [["The events of the day were told quietly and in the simplest words, and until he was through there was no interruption; nor did the listener in the chair so much as move a hand during the narration; but for his eyes, wide open and bright, and an occasional long-drawn breath, he might have been accounted an effigy.", "그날 하루의 일들이 조용히, 더없이 단순한 말로 전해졌고, 그가 다 마칠 때까지 끼어드는 일은 없었다. 의자에 앉은 청자는 이야기가 이어지는 동안 손 하나 까딱하지 않았다. 크게 뜨여 반짝이는 눈과 이따금 길게 들이쉬는 숨이 없었더라면, 그는 한갓 인형으로 여겨졌을 것이다."]], [["“Thank you, thank you, Malluch,”", "\"고맙다, 고맙구나, 말루크.\""], ["he said, heartily, at the conclusion; “you have done well—no one could have done better.", "이야기가 끝나자 그가 진심으로 말했다. \"참 잘했다—누구도 그보다 잘하지는 못했을 게다."], ["Now what say you of the young man’s nationality?”", "자, 그 젊은이의 출신에 대해서는 무어라 말하겠느냐?\""]], [["“He is an Israelite, good master, and of the tribe of Judah.”", "\"그는 이스라엘 사람입니다, 어지신 주인님. 그것도 유다 지파입니다.\""]], [["“You are positive?”", "\"확신하느냐?\""]], [["“Very positive.”", "\"아주 확신합니다.\""]], [["“He appears to have told you but little of his life.”", "\"제 삶에 대해서는 네게 거의 말하지 않은 모양이구나.\""]], [["“He has somewhere learned to be prudent.", "\"어디선가 신중함을 익힌 자입니다."], ["I might call him distrustful.", "의심이 많다고 해도 좋겠습니다."], ["He baffled all my attempts upon his confidence until we started from the Castalian fount going to the village of Daphne.”", "카스탈리아 샘에서 다프네 마을로 떠날 때까지, 그의 속내를 끌어내려는 제 온갖 시도를 다 막아 냈습니다.\""]], [["“A place of abomination! Why went he there?”", "\"가증스러운 곳이로구나! 그가 왜 그리로 갔단 말이냐?\""]], [["“I would say from curiosity, the first motive of the many who go; but, very strangely, he took no interest in the things he saw.", "\"거기 가는 많은 이들의 첫째 동기인 호기심에서라고 말하겠습니다만, 참으로 이상하게도, 그는 자기가 본 것들에 도무지 흥미를 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Of the Temple, he merely asked if it were Grecian.", "신전에 대해서는 그저 그것이 그리스식인지만 물었습니다."], ["Good master, the young man has a trouble of mind from which he would hide, and he went to the Grove, I think, as we go to sepulchres with our dead—he went to bury it.”", "어지신 주인님, 그 젊은이는 감추고 싶은 마음의 괴로움을 지니고 있습니다. 제 생각에 그가 그 숲으로 간 것은, 우리가 죽은 이를 데리고 무덤으로 가듯—그것을 묻으러 간 것입니다.\""]], [["“That were well, if so,”", "\"그렇다면 잘된 일이지.\""], ["Simonides said, in a low voice; then louder, “Malluch, the curse of the time is prodigality.", "시모니데스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다가, 더 크게 말했다. \"말루크, 이 시대의 저주는 방탕이다."], ["The poor make themselves poorer as apes of the rich, and the merely rich carry themselves like princes.", "가난한 자들은 부자를 흉내 내며 더욱 가난해지고, 그저 부유할 뿐인 자들은 군주처럼 행세한다."], ["Saw you signs of the weakness in the youth?", "그 젊은이에게서 그런 나약함의 기색을 보았느냐?"], ["Did he display moneys—coin of Rome or Israel?”", "돈을 내보였더냐—로마 화폐든 이스라엘 화폐든?\""]], [["“None, none, good master.”", "\"전혀, 전혀 없었습니다, 어지신 주인님.\""]], [["“Surely, Malluch, where there are so many inducements to folly—so much, I mean, to eat and drink—surely he made you generous offer of some sort.", "\"하지만 말루크, 어리석음으로 이끄는 유혹이 그토록 많은 곳에서—내 말은, 먹고 마실 것이 그토록 많은 곳에서—그가 분명 네게 무언가 선심을 베풀려 했을 텐데."], ["His age, if nothing more, would warrant that much.”", "다른 건 몰라도 그 나이라면 그쯤은 능히 했을 게다.\""]], [["“He neither ate nor drank in my company.”", "\"그는 제 앞에서 먹지도 마시지도 않았습니다.\""]], [["“In what he said or did, Malluch, could you in anywise detect his master-idea?", "\"그가 한 말이나 행동에서, 말루크, 그의 마음을 지배하는 생각을 조금이라도 엿보았느냐?"], ["You know they peep through cracks close enough to stop the wind.”", "너도 알다시피, 그런 생각은 바람도 못 새어들 만큼 좁은 틈으로도 새어 나오는 법이다.\""]], [["“Give me to understand you,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 알아듣게 해 주십시오.\""], ["said Malluch, in doubt.", "말루크가 미심쩍어하며 말했다."]], [["“Well, you know we nor speak nor act, much less decide grave questions concerning ourselves, except as we be driven by a motive.", "\"그러니까, 너도 알다시피, 우리는 어떤 동기에 떠밀리지 않고서는 말하지도 행동하지도 않으며, 더더욱 자신에 관한 중대한 일을 결정하지도 않는다."], ["In that respect, what made you of him?”", "그런 점에서, 그를 어떻게 보았느냐?\""]], [["“As to that, Master Simonides, I can answer with much assurance.", "\"그 점이라면, 시모니데스 주인님, 저는 상당한 확신을 가지고 답할 수 있습니다."], ["He is devoted to finding his mother and sister—that first.", "그는 어머니와 누이를 찾는 데 골몰해 있습니다—그것이 첫째입니다."], ["Then he has a grievance against Rome; and as the Messala of whom I told you had something to do with the wrong, the great present object is to humiliate him.", "다음으로 그는 로마에 원한을 품고 있습니다. 그리고 제가 말씀드린 그 메살라가 그 부당한 일에 무언가 관여했던 까닭에, 지금의 큰 목표는 그를 욕보이는 것입니다."], ["The meeting at the fountain furnished an opportunity, but it was put aside as not sufficiently public.”", "샘가에서의 만남이 그 기회를 마련해 주었지만, 그는 그것이 충분히 공개적이지 않다고 미루어 두었습니다.\""]], [["“The Messala is influential,”", "\"그 메살라는 세력이 있다.\""], ["said Simonides, thoughtfully.", "시모니데스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“Yes; but the next meeting will be in the Circus.”", "\"그렇습니다. 그러나 다음 만남은 원형 경기장에서 이루어질 것입니다.\""]], [["“Well—and then?”", "\"그래서—그다음은?\""]], [["“The son of Arrius will win.”", "\"아리우스의 아들이 이길 것입니다.\""]], [["“How know you?”", "\"어떻게 아느냐?\""]], [["Malluch smiled.", "말루크가 미소 지었다."]], [["“I am judging by what he says.”", "\"그가 하는 말로 미루어 판단합니다.\""]], [["“Is that all?”", "\"그게 전부냐?\""]], [["“No; there is a much better sign—his spirit.”", "\"아닙니다. 훨씬 더 좋은 징표가 있습니다—그의 기개입니다.\""]], [["“Ay; but, Malluch, his idea of vengeance—what is its scope?", "\"그래. 그러나 말루크, 그의 복수심—그 범위는 어디까지더냐?"], ["Does he limit it to the few who did him the wrong, or does he take in the many?", "그는 그것을 자기에게 해를 끼친 몇 사람에게로 한정하느냐, 아니면 많은 이들까지 끌어들이느냐?"], ["And more—is his feeling but the vagary of a sensitive boy, or has it the seasoning of suffering manhood to give it endurance?", "그리고 한 가지 더—그의 감정은 그저 예민한 소년의 변덕일 뿐이냐, 아니면 그것을 오래 견디게 할 만한, 고통받은 장년의 무게가 배어 있느냐?"], ["You know, Malluch, the vengeful thought that has root merely in the mind is but a dream of idlest sort which one clear day will dissipate; while revenge the passion is a disease of the heart which climbs up, up to the brain, and feeds itself on both alike.”", "알다시피, 말루크, 한갓 머릿속에만 뿌리내린 복수심은 맑은 날 하루면 흩어져 버리는 더없이 부질없는 꿈일 뿐이다. 그러나 정념으로서의 복수는 위로, 위로 뇌까지 기어올라 마음과 뇌 양쪽을 똑같이 파먹는 심장의 병이다.\""]], [["In this question, Simonides for the first time showed signs of feeling; he spoke with rapid utterance, and with clenched hands and the eagerness of a man illustrating the disease he described.", "이 물음에서 시모니데스는 처음으로 감정의 기색을 드러냈다. 그는 빠른 말투로, 두 주먹을 움켜쥐고, 자신이 묘사하는 그 병을 몸소 보여 주는 사람처럼 열렬하게 말했다."]], [["“Good my master,”", "\"어지신 주인님.\""], ["Malluch replied, “one of my reasons for believing the young man a Jew is the intensity of his hate.", "말루크가 대답했다. \"제가 그 젊은이를 유대인이라고 믿는 이유 가운데 하나가 바로 그 증오의 강렬함입니다."], ["It was plain to me he had himself under watch, as was natural, seeing how long he has lived in an atmosphere of Roman jealousy; yet I saw it blaze—once when he wanted to know Ilderim’s feeling towards Rome, and again when I told him the story of the sheik and the wise man, and spoke of the question, ‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’”", "그가 그토록 오래 로마의 시기 어린 분위기 속에서 살아온 것을 생각하면 당연한 일이지만, 그는 스스로를 단속하고 있는 것이 제게도 분명히 보였습니다. 그런데도 그것이 활활 타오르는 것을 보았습니다—한 번은 그가 일데림이 로마에 대해 어떤 감정을 품고 있는지 알고 싶어 했을 때, 또 한 번은 제가 그 족장과 현자의 이야기를 들려주며 '유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?'라는 물음을 입에 올렸을 때였습니다.\""]], [["Simonides leaned forward quickly.", "시모니데스가 재빨리 몸을 앞으로 기울였다."]], [["“Ah, Malluch, his words—give me his words; let me judge the impression the mystery made upon him.”", "\"아, 말루크, 그의 말을—그가 한 말을 그대로 들려다오. 그 신비가 그에게 어떤 인상을 남겼는지 내가 가늠해 보게.\""]], [["“He wanted to know the exact words.", "\"그는 정확한 말을 알고 싶어 했습니다."], ["Were they TO BE or BORN TO BE? It appeared he was struck by a seeming difference in the effect of the two phrases.”", "'왕이 되실'이었느냐, 아니면 '왕으로 나신'이었느냐고요. 그 두 표현이 자아내는 효과에 어떤 차이가 있는 듯한 것에 그가 마음이 끌린 듯했습니다.\""]], [["Simonides settled back into his pose of listening judge.", "시모니데스는 다시 귀 기울이는 판관의 자세로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said Malluch, “I told him Ilderim’s view of the mystery—that the king would come with the doom of Rome.", "말루크가 말했다. \"저는 그에게 그 신비에 대한 일데림의 견해를 들려주었습니다—그 왕이 로마의 멸망을 가지고 오리라는 것을요."], ["The young man’s blood rose over his cheeks and forehead, and he said earnestly, ‘Who but a Herod can be king while Rome endures?’”", "그러자 그 젊은이의 피가 뺨과 이마 위로 솟구치더니, 그가 간절하게 말했습니다. '로마가 존속하는 한, 헤롯 같은 자가 아니고서야 누가 왕이 될 수 있겠소?'\""]], [["“Meaning what?”", "\"무슨 뜻이냐?\""]], [["“That the empire must be destroyed before there could be another rule.”", "\"또 다른 통치가 들어서려면 그 제국이 먼저 무너져야 한다는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["Simonides gazed for a time at the ships and their shadows slowly swinging together in the river; when he looked up, it was to end the interview.", "시모니데스는 한동안 강 위의 배들과, 그 그림자들이 강물 위에서 천천히 함께 흔들리는 모습을 바라보았다. 그가 눈을 들었을 때는 면담을 끝맺으려는 것이었다."]], [["“Enough, Malluch,” he said.", "\"그만하면 됐다, 말루크.\""], ["“Get you to eat, and make ready to return to the Orchard of Palms; you must help the young man in his coming trial.", "그가 말했다. \"가서 요기하고, 종려나무 과수원으로 돌아갈 채비를 하여라. 너는 다가오는 시합에서 그 젊은이를 도와야 한다."], ["Come to me in the morning.", "아침에 내게 오너라."], ["I will send a letter to Ilderim.”", "일데림에게 편지를 보내겠다.\""], ["Then in an undertone, as if to himself, he added, “I may attend the Circus myself.”", "그러더니 마치 혼잣말처럼 낮은 소리로 덧붙였다. \"내가 직접 경기장에 갈지도 모르겠구나.\""]], [["When Malluch after the customary benediction given and received was gone, Simonides took a deep draught of milk, and seemed refreshed and easy of mind.", "말루크가 으레 하는 축복의 말을 주고받은 뒤 떠나자, 시모니데스는 우유를 깊이 들이켜고는, 기운이 나고 마음이 편해진 듯했다."]], [["“Put the meal down, Esther,”", "\"상을 내려놓으렴, 에스더.\""], ["he said; “it is over.”", "그가 말했다. \"이제 끝났다.\""]], [["She obeyed. “Here now.”", "그녀가 그대로 따랐다."]], [["She resumed her place upon the arm of the chair close to him.", "\"자, 이리 오너라.\" 그녀는 그의 곁, 의자 팔걸이 위의 제자리로 돌아왔다."]], [["“God is good to me, very good,”", "\"하느님은 내게 선하시다, 참으로 선하시다.\""], ["he said, fervently.", "그가 뜨겁게 말했다."], ["“His habit is to move in mystery, yet sometimes he permits us to think we see and understand him.", "\"그분은 늘 신비 속에서 움직이시지만, 때로는 우리가 그분을 보고 깨닫는다고 여기도록 허락하신다."], ["I am old, dear, and must go; but now, in this eleventh hour, when my hope was beginning to die, he sends me this one with a promise, and I am lifted up.", "나는 늙었고, 얘야, 떠나야 한다. 그러나 이제, 내 희망이 사그라지기 시작하던 이 마지막 시각에, 그분은 약속을 지닌 이 사람을 내게 보내 주셨고, 나는 다시 일으켜 세워졌다."], ["I see the way to a great part in a circumstance itself so great that it shall be as a new birth to the whole world.", "나는 그 자체로 너무나 위대하여 온 세상에 새로운 탄생과도 같을 어떤 사태에서 큰 몫을 맡을 길을 본다."], ["And I see a reason for the gift of my great riches, and the end for which they were designed.", "그리고 나는 내 막대한 재물이 주어진 까닭과, 그것이 마련된 목적을 본다."], ["Verily, my child, I take hold on life anew.”", "진실로, 내 아이야, 나는 삶을 새로이 붙드는구나.\""]], [["Esther nestled closer to him, as if to bring his thoughts from their far-flying.", "에스더는 그의 생각을 멀리 날아간 데서 끌어오려는 듯, 그에게 더 바싹 파고들었다."]], [["“The king has been born”", "\"그 왕은 이미 태어났다.\""], ["he continued, imagining he was still speaking to her, “and he must be near the half of common life.", "그는 여전히 그녀에게 말하고 있다고 여기며 이었다. \"그리고 그는 보통 사람 일생의 절반쯤에 이르렀을 게다."], ["Balthasar says he was a child on his mother’s lap when he saw him, and gave him presents and worship; and Ilderim holds it was twenty-seven years ago last December when Balthasar and his companions came to his tent asking a hiding-place from Herod.", "발타사르는 그를 보았을 때 그가 어머니 무릎에 안긴 아기였으며, 자기가 선물을 바치고 경배했다고 말한다. 그리고 일데림은, 발타사르와 그 동행들이 헤롯을 피해 숨을 곳을 청하며 자기 천막으로 찾아온 것이 지난 십이월로 스물일곱 해 전이었다고 한다."], ["Wherefore the coming cannot now be long delayed.", "그러니 그분의 오심은 이제 오래 미루어질 수 없다."], ["To-night—to-morrow it may be.", "오늘 밤일 수도—내일일 수도 있다."], ["Holy fathers of Israel, what happiness in the thought!", "이스라엘의 거룩한 조상들이여, 그 생각만으로도 얼마나 큰 행복인가!"], ["I seem to hear the crash of the falling of old walls and the clamor of a universal change—ay, and for the uttermost joy of men, the earth opens to take Rome in, and they look up and laugh and sing that she is not, while we are;”", "나는 낡은 성벽이 무너지는 굉음과, 온 세상이 뒤바뀌는 함성을 듣는 듯하다—그래, 그리고 사람들의 더없는 기쁨을 위하여, 땅이 입을 벌려 로마를 삼키고, 사람들이 우러러보며 웃고 노래하리니, 로마는 없어졌으나 우리는 살아 있다고.\""], ["then he laughed at himself.", "그러더니 그는 자신을 두고 웃었다."], ["“Why, Esther, heard you ever the like? Surely, I have on me the passion of a singer, the heat of blood and the thrill of Miriam and David.", "\"이것 봐라, 에스더, 너 이런 말을 들어 본 적 있느냐? 분명 내게 가인(歌人)의 정념이, 미리암과 다윗의 뜨거운 피와 전율이 깃들어 있구나."], ["In my thoughts, which should be those of a plain worker in figures and facts, there is a confusion of cymbals clashing and harp-strings loud beaten, and the voices of a multitude standing around a new-risen throne.", "숫자와 사실을 다루는 한낱 일꾼의 생각이어야 마땅한 내 머릿속에, 심벌즈가 맞부딪치고 하프 줄이 요란하게 튕겨지며, 새로 일어선 왕좌를 에워싼 군중의 목소리가 뒤섞여 들끓는구나."], ["I will put the thinking by for the present; only, dear, when the king comes he will need money and men, for as he was a child born of woman he will be but a man after all, bound to human ways as you and I are.", "그 생각은 당분간 접어 두마. 다만, 얘야, 그 왕이 오시면 그분께는 돈과 사람이 필요하리니, 그분은 여인에게서 난 아이였던 만큼 결국은 한 사람일 뿐이요, 너나 나처럼 사람의 길에 매여 있을 것이기 때문이다."], ["And for the money he will have need of getters and keepers, and for the men leaders. There, there!", "그리고 돈을 위해서는 그것을 벌고 지키는 자들이, 사람을 위해서는 그들을 이끄는 자들이 필요하실 게다. 자, 자!"], ["See you not a broad road for my walking, and the running of the youth our master?—and at the end of it glory and revenge for us both?—and—and”—he paused, struck with the selfishness of a scheme in which she had no part or good result; then added, kissing her, “And happiness for thy mother’s child.”", "너는 내가 걸어갈 너른 길과, 우리 주인인 그 젊은이가 달려갈 길이 보이지 않느냐?—그리고 그 끝에 우리 둘 모두를 위한 영광과 복수가 있지 않느냐?—그리고—그리고\"—그는 그녀에게는 아무 몫도, 아무 좋은 결과도 없는 계획의 이기심에 문득 마음이 찔려 말을 멈추었다. 그러더니 그녀에게 입 맞추며 덧붙였다. \"그리고 네 어머니의 아이를 위한 행복이 있지 않느냐.\""]], [["She sat still, saying nothing.", "그녀는 아무 말 없이 가만히 앉아 있었다."], ["Then he remembered the difference in natures, and the law by which we are not permitted always to take delight in the same cause or be equally afraid of the same thing. He remembered she was but a girl.", "그제야 그는 천성의 차이와, 우리가 늘 같은 일로 똑같이 기뻐하거나 같은 것을 똑같이 두려워하도록 허락되지는 않는다는 법칙을 떠올렸다. 그는 그녀가 한낱 소녀에 지나지 않음을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Of what are you thinking, Esther?”", "\"무슨 생각을 하고 있느냐, 에스더?\""], ["he said, in his common home-like way.", "그가 평소의 다정한 집안 말투로 말했다."], ["“If the thought have the form of a wish, give it me, little one, while the power remains mine.", "\"그 생각이 바람의 모양을 하고 있거든, 내게 힘이 남아 있는 동안 그것을 내게 다오, 우리 아가."], ["For power, you know, is a fretful thing, and hath its wings always spread for flight.”", "알다시피 힘이란 안달하는 것이어서, 늘 날아갈 채비로 날개를 펴고 있는 법이니까.\""]], [["She answered with a simplicity almost childish,", "그녀는 거의 어린아이처럼 꾸밈없이 대답했다."]], [["“Send for him, father.", "\"그를 부르세요, 아버지."], ["Send for him to-night, and do not let him go into the Circus.”", "오늘 밤 그를 부르셔서, 경기장에 들어가지 못하게 하세요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he said, prolonging the exclamation; and again his eyes fell upon the river, where the shadows were more shadowy than ever, since the moon had sunk far down behind Sulpius, leaving the city to the ineffectual stars.", "그가 그 감탄사를 길게 늘이며 말했다. 그러고는 다시 그의 눈이 강 위로 떨어졌다. 달이 술피우스 산 너머로 멀리 가라앉아, 도시를 무력한 별들에게 내맡긴 까닭에, 그곳의 그림자는 그 어느 때보다 어슴푸레했다."], ["Shall we say it, reader?", "독자여, 이렇게 말해도 될까?"], ["He was touched by a twinge of jealousy.", "그는 한 가닥 질투의 아픔에 사로잡혔다."], ["If she should really love the young master! Oh no!", "만일 그녀가 정말로 그 젊은 주인을 사랑한다면! 오, 아니다!"], ["That could not be; she was too young.", "그럴 리 없다. 그녀는 너무 어리다."], ["But the idea had fast grip, and directly held him still and cold. She was sixteen.", "그러나 그 생각은 단단히 움켜쥐고 놓지 않더니, 곧장 그를 가만히, 차갑게 만들었다."], ["He knew it well.", "그녀는 열여섯 살이었다."], ["On the last natal day he had gone with her to the shipyard where there was a launch, and the yellow flag which the galley bore to its bridal with the waves had on it “Esther;”", "그는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다. 지난 생일에 그는 그녀와 함께 진수식이 있는 조선소에 갔는데, 그 갤리선이 파도와의 혼례를 위해 내건 노란 깃발에는 \"에스더\"라고 적혀 있었다."], ["so they celebrated the day together.", "그렇게 두 사람은 그날을 함께 기렸다."], ["Yet the fact struck him now with the force of a surprise.", "그런데도 그 사실이 이제 와 놀라움의 충격으로 그를 쳤다."], ["There are realizations which come to us all painfully; mostly, however, such as pertain to ourselves; that we are growing old, for instance; and, more terrible, that we must die.", "우리 모두에게는 고통스럽게 다가오는 깨달음이 있다. 그러나 대개는 우리 자신에 관한 것들이다. 이를테면 우리가 늙어 간다는 것, 그리고 더욱 끔찍하게는, 우리가 죽어야 한다는 것이다."], ["Such a one crept into his heart, shadowy as the shadows, yet substantial enough to wring from him a sigh which was almost a groan.", "그런 깨달음 하나가, 그림자처럼 어슴푸레하면서도 거의 신음에 가까운 한숨을 그에게서 쥐어짜낼 만큼은 또렷하게, 그의 가슴속으로 스며들었다."], ["It was not sufficient that she should enter upon her young womanhood a servant, but she must carry to her master her affections, the truth and tenderness and delicacy of which he the father so well knew, because to this time they had all been his own undividedly.", "그녀가 종의 신세로 젊은 여인의 시절에 들어서는 것만으로도 모자라, 그녀는 그 주인에게 자신의 애정마저 바쳐야 했으니, 그 애정의 진실함과 다정함과 섬세함을 아비인 그는 너무도 잘 알고 있었다. 그때까지 그것이 온전히 그만의 것이었기 때문이다."], ["The fiend whose task it is to torture us with fears and bitter thoughts seldom does his work by halves.", "두려움과 쓰라린 생각으로 우리를 괴롭히는 것이 임무인 그 악귀는 좀처럼 제 일을 어중간하게 하는 법이 없다."], ["In the pang of the moment, the brave old man lost sight of his new scheme, and of the miraculous king its subject.", "그 순간의 아픔 속에서, 용감한 노인은 자신의 새로운 계획도, 그 주인공인 기적의 왕도 시야에서 놓쳐 버렸다."], ["By a mighty effort, however, he controlled himself, and asked, calmly, “Not go into the Circus, Esther?", "그러나 그는 안간힘을 다하여 스스로를 다스리고는, 차분하게 물었다. \"경기장에 들어가지 말라고, 에스더?"], ["Why, child?”", "왜냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“It is not a place for a son of Israel, father.”", "\"거기는 이스라엘의 아들이 갈 곳이 아니에요, 아버지.\""]], [["“Rabbinical, rabbinical, Esther!", "\"율법학자 같구나, 율법학자 같아, 에스더!"], ["Is that all?”", "그게 전부냐?\""]], [["The tone of the inquiry was searching, and went to her heart, which began to beat loudly—so loudly she could not answer.", "그 물음의 어조는 속을 캐는 것이어서, 그녀의 가슴에 가닿았다. 그녀의 가슴이 요란하게 뛰기 시작했다—너무도 요란하여 그녀는 대답할 수가 없었다."], ["A confusion new and strangely pleasant fell upon her.", "새롭고 기이하게도 달콤한 혼란이 그녀에게 내려앉았다."]], [["“The young man is to have the fortune,”", "\"그 재산은 그 젊은이에게 돌아갈 게다.\""], ["he said, taking her hand, and speaking more tenderly; “he is to have the ships and the shekels—all, Esther, all.", "그가 그녀의 손을 잡고 한층 다정하게 말했다. \"배도, 은화도 그에게 돌아갈 게다—전부, 에스더, 전부 다."], ["Yet I did not feel poor, for thou wert left me, and thy love so like the dead Rachel’s.", "그런데도 나는 가난하다고 느끼지 않았다. 네가 내게 남겨졌고, 죽은 라헬을 그토록 닮은 네 사랑이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Tell me, is he to have that too?”", "말해 다오, 그것마저 그에게 돌아가는 것이냐?\""]], [["She bent over him, and laid her cheek against his head.", "그녀는 그에게 몸을 숙여, 그의 머리에 제 뺨을 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Speak, Esther.", "\"말하여라, 에스더."], ["I will be the stronger of the knowledge. In warning there is strength.”", "알면 나는 더 강해질 게다. 미리 알면 거기에 힘이 있는 법이다.\""]], [["She sat up then, and spoke as if she were Truth’s holy self.", "그러자 그녀는 몸을 일으켜, 마치 자신이 진리의 거룩한 화신이기라도 한 듯이 말했다."]], [["“Comfort thee, father.", "\"마음 놓으세요, 아버지."], ["I will never leave thee; though he take my love, I will be thy handmaid ever as now.”", "저는 결코 아버지를 떠나지 않을 거예요. 그가 제 사랑을 가져간다 해도, 저는 지금처럼 영원히 아버지의 여종이겠어요.\""]], [["And, stooping, she kissed him.", "그러고는 몸을 숙여 그에게 입 맞추었다."]], [["“And more,” she said, continuing: “he is comely in my sight, and the pleading of his voice drew me to him, and I shudder to think of him in danger.", "\"그리고 한 가지 더 말씀드릴게요.\" 그녀가 이어 말했다."], ["Yes, father, I would be more than glad to see him again.", "\"그는 제 눈에 잘생겨 보이고, 그 호소하는 목소리가 저를 그에게로 끌어당겼어요."], ["Still, the love that is unrequited cannot be perfect love, wherefore I will wait a time, remembering I am thy daughter and my mother’s.”", "그래서 저는 그가 위험에 처할 것을 생각하면 몸서리가 쳐져요. 네, 아버지, 저는 그를 다시 보면 더없이 기쁠 거예요. 그렇지만 보답받지 못하는 사랑은 온전한 사랑일 수 없으니, 저는 제가 아버지의 딸이요 어머니의 딸임을 잊지 않고, 한동안 기다리겠어요.\""]], [["“A very blessing of the Lord art thou, Esther!", "\"너는 참으로 주님의 축복이로구나, 에스더!"], ["A blessing to keep me rich, though all else be lost.", "다른 모든 것을 잃어도 나를 부유하게 지켜 줄 축복이다."], ["And by his holy name and everlasting life, I swear thou shalt not suffer.”", "그분의 거룩한 이름과 영원한 생명을 두고 맹세하노니, 너는 결코 고통받지 않을 게다.\""]], [["At his request, a little later, the servant came and rolled the chair into the room, where he sat for a time thinking of the coming of the king, while she went off and slept the sleep of the innocent.", "조금 뒤 그의 청에 따라 하인이 와서 의자를 방 안으로 밀어 넣었다. 그곳에서 그는 한동안 그 왕의 오심을 생각하며 앉아 있었고, 그녀는 자리를 떠나 순결한 자의 잠을 잤다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII", "# CHAPTER XII"]], [["The palace across the river nearly opposite Simonides’ place is said to have been completed by the famous Epiphanes, and was all such a habitation can be imagined; though he was a builder whose taste ran to the immense rather than the classical, now so called—an architectural imitator, in other words, of the Persians instead of the Greeks.", "강 건너편, 시모니데스의 집과 거의 마주 보는 자리에 선 그 궁전은 이름난 에피파네스가 완공했다고 전해지며, 그런 거처에 대해 상상할 수 있는 모든 것을 갖춘 곳이었다. 다만 그는 이른바 고전적인 것보다 거대한 것을 좋아하는 취향의 건축가였다—다시 말해 그리스인이 아니라 페르시아인을 모방한 건축가였던 것이다."]], [["The wall enclosing the whole island to the waters edge, and built for the double purpose of bulwark against the river and defence against the mob, was said to have rendered the palace unfit for constant occupancy, insomuch that the legates abandoned it and moved to another residence erected for them on the western ridge of Mount Sulpius, under the Temple of Jupiter.", "섬 전체를 물가까지 에워싼 성벽은 강물을 막는 보루이자 폭도를 막는 방벽이라는 이중의 목적으로 세워졌는데, 바로 그 때문에 이 궁전이 늘 거처하기에는 적합하지 못하다는 말이 있었다. 그래서 총독들은 이곳을 버리고, 유피테르 신전 아래 술피우스 산의 서쪽 능선에 그들을 위해 지어진 다른 관저로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Persons were not wanting, however, who flatly denied the bill against the ancient abode.", "그러나 이 오래된 거처를 향한 그 비난을 딱 잘라 부정하는 사람도 적지 않았다."], ["They said, with shrewdness at least, that the real object of the removal of the legates was not a more healthful locality, but the assurance afforded them by the huge barracks, named, according to the prevalent style, citadel, situated just over the way on the eastern ridge of the mount.", "적어도 그들은 약삭빠르게도, 총독들이 옮겨 간 진짜 이유는 더 쾌적한 자리를 찾아서가 아니라, 그 산 동쪽 능선의 바로 맞은편에 자리 잡은, 당시의 일반적인 명칭에 따라 성채라 불린 거대한 병영이 그들에게 안전을 보장해 주었기 때문이라고 말했다."], ["And the opinion had plausible showing.", "이 견해는 그럴듯한 근거가 있었다."], ["Among other pertinent things, it was remarked that the palace was kept in perpetual readiness for use; and when a consul, general of the army, king, or visiting potentate of any kind arrived at Antioch, quarters were at once assigned him on the island.", "다른 적절한 정황 가운데서도, 이 궁전이 언제든 쓸 수 있도록 늘 채비되어 있다는 점이 거론되었다. 집정관이든 군 총사령관이든 왕이든, 또는 어떤 부류의 방문하는 권력자든 안티오크에 도착하면, 곧장 섬에 거처가 배정되었던 것이다."]], [["As we have to do with but one apartment in the old pile, the residue of it is left to the reader’s fancy; and as pleases him, he may go through its gardens, baths, halls, and labyrinth of rooms to the pavilions on the roof, all furnished as became a house of fame in a city which was more nearly Milton’s “gorgeous East”", "우리가 이 오래된 건물에서 다룰 곳은 단 하나의 방뿐이니, 나머지는 독자의 상상에 맡긴다. 독자는 마음 내키는 대로 그 정원과 욕탕과 회랑과 미궁 같은 방들을 거쳐 지붕 위의 정자까지 거닐어도 좋다."], ["than any other in the world.", "그 모든 것은 밀턴이 말한 \"화려한 동방\"에 세상 어느 곳보다 가까운 도시에서, 명성 있는 저택에 어울리게 꾸며져 있었으니 말이다."]], [["At this age the apartment alluded to would be termed a saloon.", "오늘날이라면 우리가 이야기하려는 그 방을 응접실이라 부를 것이다."], ["It was quite spacious, floored with polished marble slabs, and lighted in the day by skylights in which colored mica served as glass.", "꽤 넓은 방으로, 윤이 나는 대리석 판으로 바닥이 깔려 있었고, 낮에는 채색 운모를 유리 대신 끼운 천창으로 빛이 들었다."], ["The walls were broken by Atlantes, no two of which were alike, but all supporting a cornice wrought with arabesques exceedingly intricate in form, and more elegant on account of superadditions of color—blue, green, Tyrian purple, and gold.", "벽면은 아틀라스 상으로 군데군데 끊겼는데, 어느 둘도 같은 것이 없었으나 모두가 처마 장식을 떠받치고 있었으며, 그 장식은 형태가 더없이 정교한 아라베스크 무늬로 짜여, 푸른색, 초록색, 티레의 자주색, 금색으로 색을 덧입혀 한층 우아했다."], ["Around the room ran a continuous divan of Indian silks and wool of Cashmere.", "방을 빙 둘러 인도산 비단과 캐시미어 양털로 만든 긴 의자가 끊임없이 이어졌다."], ["The furniture consisted of tables and stools of Egyptian patterns grotesquely carved.", "가구는 기괴하게 새겨진 이집트풍의 탁자와 걸상으로 이루어져 있었다."], ["We have left Simonides in his chair perfecting his scheme in aid of the miraculous king, whose coming he has decided is so close at hand.", "우리는 시모니데스를 그의 의자에 남겨 두고 왔다. 그는 그 기적의 왕—그가 이미 임박했다고 단정한 그 왕—을 돕기 위한 계획을 다듬고 있었다."], ["Esther is asleep; and now, having crossed the river by the bridge, and made way through the lion-guarded gate and a number of Babylonian halls and courts, let us enter the gilded saloon.", "에스더는 잠들어 있다. 이제 다리를 건너 강을 넘고, 사자가 지키는 문과 여러 바빌론풍 회랑과 안뜰을 지났으니, 그 황금빛 응접실로 들어가 보자."]], [["There are five chandeliers hanging by sliding bronze chains from the ceiling—one in each corner, and in the centre one—enormous pyramids of lighted lamps, illuminating even the demoniac faces of the Atlantes and the complex tracery of the cornice.", "천장에서 미끄러지는 청동 사슬에 매달린 샹들리에가 다섯 개 있었으니—네 귀퉁이에 하나씩, 그리고 한가운데 하나—불을 밝힌 등잔의 거대한 피라미드가 아틀라스 상들의 악귀 같은 얼굴과 처마 장식의 복잡한 무늬마저 환히 비추었다."], ["About the tables, seated or standing, or moving restlessly from one to another, there are probably a hundred persons, whom we must study at least for a moment.", "탁자 둘레에는 앉거나 선 채로, 또는 이 탁자에서 저 탁자로 쉴 새 없이 옮겨 다니는 사람이 백 명쯤은 되었으니, 우리는 적어도 잠깐이나마 이들을 살펴보아야 한다."]], [["They are all young, some of them little more than boys.", "그들은 모두 젊었고, 더러는 소년이나 다름없었다."], ["That they are Italians and mostly Romans is past doubt.", "그들이 이탈리아인이며 대부분 로마인이라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["They all speak Latin in purity, while each one appears in the in-door dress of the great capital on the Tiber; that is, in tunics short of sleeve and skirt, a style of vesture well adapted to the climate of Antioch, and especially comfortable in the too close atmosphere of the saloon.", "모두가 순수한 라틴어를 썼고, 저마다 티베르 강가의 그 위대한 수도에서 입는 실내복 차림이었다. 곧 소매와 자락이 짧은 튜닉이었으니, 안티오크의 기후에 잘 어울리는 옷차림이었고, 특히 응접실의 너무 답답한 공기 속에서는 한결 편안했다."], ["On the divan here and there togas and lacernae lie where they have been carelessly tossed, some of them significantly bordered with purple.", "긴 의자 여기저기에는 토가와 라케르나가 아무렇게나 던져진 채 놓여 있었으니, 더러는 자주색으로 의미심장하게 단을 두른 것이었다."], ["On the divan also lie sleepers stretched at ease; whether they were overcome by the heat and fatigue of the sultry day or by Bacchus we will not pause to inquire.", "또한 긴 의자에는 편안히 몸을 뻗고 잠든 자들도 있었다. 그들이 무더운 날의 더위와 피로에 쓰러진 것인지, 아니면 바쿠스에게 쓰러진 것인지는 굳이 따지지 않겠다."]], [["The hum of voices is loud and incessant.", "말소리의 웅성거림이 요란하고 끊임없었다."], ["Sometimes there is an explosion of laughter, sometimes a burst of rage or exultation; but over all prevails a sharp, prolonged rattle, at first somewhat confusing to the non-familiar.", "때로는 웃음이 터지고, 때로는 분노나 환희가 폭발했다. 그러나 그 모든 것 위로 날카롭고 길게 이어지는 달그락 소리가 우세하게 깔려 있었으니, 익숙지 않은 사람에게는 처음엔 다소 어리둥절한 소리였다."], ["If we approach the tables, however, the mystery solves itself.", "그러나 우리가 탁자에 다가가면 그 수수께끼는 절로 풀린다."], ["The company is at the favorite games, draughts and dice, singly or together, and the rattle is merely of the tesserae, or ivory cubes, loudly shaken, and the moving of the hostes on the checkered boards.", "사람들은 즐겨 하는 놀이, 곧 장기와 주사위 놀이에 빠져 있었으니, 따로 하기도 하고 함께 하기도 했다. 그 달그락 소리는 다만 테세라, 곧 상아 주사위를 요란하게 흔드는 소리와, 바둑판 같은 판 위에서 말을 옮기는 소리였던 것이다."]], [["Who are the company?", "이 무리는 누구인가?"]], [["“Good Flavius,”", "\"이보게 플라비우스,\""], ["said a player, holding his piece in suspended movement, “thou seest yon lacerna; that one in front of us on the divan.", "한 사람이 말을 든 채 움직임을 멈추고 말했다. \"저기 저 라케르나가 보이나. 우리 앞 긴 의자에 놓인 저것 말일세."], ["It is fresh from the shop, and hath a shoulder-buckle of gold broad as a palm.”", "갓 가게에서 나온 새것인데, 어깨에 단 황금 버클이 손바닥만큼이나 넓다네.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Flavius, intent upon his game, “I have seen such before; wherefore thine may not be old, yet, by the girdle of Venus, it is not new!", "플라비우스가 자기 놀이에 골몰한 채 말했다. \"나도 전에 그런 걸 본 적이 있지. 그러니 자네 것이 오래된 건 아닐 테지만, 베누스의 허리띠를 두고 말하건대, 새것도 아니야!"], ["What of it?”", "그래서 어쩌자는 건가?\""]], [["“Nothing. Only I would give it to find a man who knows everything.”", "\"아무것도 아닐세. 다만 모든 걸 다 아는 사람을 찾아낼 수 있다면 저걸 주겠다는 거지.\""]], [["“Ha, ha!", "\"하하!"], ["For something cheaper, I will find thee here several with purple who will take thy offer. But play.”", "그보다 싼값으로, 자네 제안을 받아들일 자주색 단을 두른 자들을 여기서 여럿 찾아 주겠네. 어서 두기나 하게.\""]], [["“There—check!”", "\"자—장군이야!\""]], [["“So, by all the Jupiters!", "\"이런, 온 유피테르 신을 두고!"], ["Now, what sayest thou? Again?”", "자, 어떤가? 한 판 더?\""]], [["“Be it so.”", "\"그러세.\""]], [["“And the wager?”", "\"내기는?\""]], [["“A sestertium.”", "\"세스테르티움 하나.\""]], [["Then each drew his tablets and stilus and made a memorandum; and, while they were resetting the pieces, Flavius returned to his friend’s remark.", "그러자 둘은 저마다 서판과 첨필을 꺼내 기록했다. 말을 다시 늘어놓는 동안, 플라비우스가 친구의 말로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“A man who knows everything! Hercle!", "\"모든 걸 다 아는 사람이라! 헤르쿨레스를 두고!"], ["the oracles would die. What wouldst thou with such a monster?”", "그런 자가 있다면 신탁들도 죽어 버리겠군. 그런 괴물을 데려다 뭘 하려고?\""]], [["“Answer to one question, my Flavius; then, perpol!", "\"한 가지 물음에 답을 듣자는 걸세, 플라비우스. 그러고 나선, 맹세코!"], ["I would cut his throat.”", "그놈 멱을 따 버려야지.\""]], [["“And the question?”", "\"그 물음이란?\""]], [["“I would have him tell me the hour— Hour, said I?—nay, the minute—Maxentius will arrive to-morrow.”", "\"막센티우스가 내일 도착할 그 시각을—시각이라 했나?—아니, 그 분(分)까지 일러 달라 하겠네.\""]], [["“Good play, good play!", "\"잘 두는군, 잘 둬!"], ["I have you!", "자넬 잡았네!"], ["And why the minute?”", "그런데 왜 분까지 알려는 건가?\""]], [["“Hast thou ever stood uncovered in the Syrian sun on the quay at which he will land?", "\"자넨 그가 상륙할 그 부두에서 시리아의 햇볕 아래 맨머리로 서 본 적이 있나?"], ["The fires of the Vesta are not so hot; and, by the Stator of our father Romulus, I would die, if die I must, in Rome.", "베스타의 불도 그처럼 뜨겁진 않을 걸세. 우리 선조 로물루스의 수호신을 두고 말하건대, 죽어야 한다면 나는 로마에서 죽겠네."], ["Avernus is here; there, in the square before the Forum, I could stand, and, with my hand raised thus, touch the floor of the gods.", "여기가 바로 아베르누스야. 로마 광장의 포룸 앞 그 자리에 서서, 이렇게 손을 들면 신들의 마루를 만질 수도 있었지."], ["Ha, by Venus, my Flavius, thou didst beguile me!", "아, 베누스를 두고, 플라비우스, 자네 날 속였군! 졌네."], ["I have lost. O Fortune!”", "오, 포르투나여!\""]], [["“Again?”", "\"한 판 더?\""]], [["“I must have back my sestertium.”", "\"내 세스테르티움을 도로 찾아야겠네.\""]], [["“Be it so.”", "\"그러세.\""]], [["And they played again and again; and when day, stealing through the skylights, began to dim the lamps, it found the two in the same places at the same table, still at the game.", "그렇게 그들은 거듭거듭 놀이를 했다. 천창으로 새어 든 햇빛이 등잔불을 흐리기 시작했을 때에도, 두 사람은 같은 탁자 같은 자리에서 여전히 놀이에 빠져 있었다."], ["Like most of the company, they were military attaches of the consul, awaiting his arrival and amusing themselves meantime.", "무리 가운데 대부분이 그러하듯, 그들도 집정관의 군 막료로서 그의 도착을 기다리며 그동안 이렇게 시간을 보내고 있었던 것이다."]], [["During this conversation a party entered the room, and, unnoticed at first, proceeded to the central table.", "이 대화가 오가는 동안 한 무리가 방에 들어와, 처음엔 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않은 채 한가운데 탁자로 나아갔다."], ["The signs were that they had come from a revel just dismissed.", "정황으로 보아 그들은 방금 파한 연회에서 온 듯했다."], ["Some of them kept their feet with difficulty.", "더러는 발을 가누기조차 힘들어했다."], ["Around the leader’s brow was a chaplet which marked him master of the feast, if not the giver.", "그 무리를 이끄는 자의 이마에는 화관이 둘려 있었으니, 그가 연회를 베푼 자는 아니라 해도 연회의 좌장임을 나타냈다."], ["The wine had made no impression upon him unless to heighten his beauty, which was of the most manly Roman style; he carried his head high raised; the blood flushed his lips and cheeks brightly; his eyes glittered; though the manner in which, shrouded in a toga spotless white and of ample folds, he walked was too nearly imperial for one sober and not a Cæsar.", "술은 그에게 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않았으니, 도리어 그의 아름다움을 한층 돋우었을 뿐이었다. 그것은 더없이 사내다운 로마풍의 아름다움이었다. 그는 고개를 높이 쳐들고 있었고, 입술과 뺨에는 피가 환하게 올라 있었으며, 두 눈은 빛났다."], ["In going to the table, he made room for himself and his followers with little ceremony and no apologies; and when at length he stopped, and looked over it and at the players, they all turned to him, with a shout like a cheer.", "다만 티 하나 없이 새하얗고 자락이 넉넉한 토가를 두른 채 걷는 그 몸가짐은, 술에 취하지도 않고 카이사르도 아닌 자치고는 지나치게 황제다웠다. 탁자로 가면서 그는 거의 격식도 사과도 없이 자기와 추종자들을 위해 자리를 텄다. 마침내 멈춰 서서 탁자 너머 놀이하는 자들을 둘러보자, 그들은 모두 그를 향해 환호 같은 함성을 질렀다."]], [["“Messala! Messala!”", "\"메살라! 메살라!\""], ["they cried.", "그들이 외쳤다."]], [["Those in distant quarters, hearing the cry, re-echoed it where they were. Instantly there were dissolution of groups, and breaking-up of games, and a general rush towards the centre.", "멀리 떨어진 자리에 있던 자들도 그 외침을 듣고는 제자리에서 그 소리를 되받아 메아리쳤다. 순식간에 무리가 흩어지고 놀이가 중단되었으며, 모두가 한가운데로 우르르 몰려들었다."]], [["Messala took the demonstration indifferently, and proceeded presently to show the ground of his popularity.", "메살라는 이 소동을 무심하게 받아들이고는, 이내 자기 인기의 근원을 보여 주었다."]], [["“A health to thee, Drusus, my friend,”", "\"자네의 건강을 위하여, 드루수스, 내 친구여,\""], ["he said to the player next at his right; “a health—and thy tablets a moment.”", "그가 바로 오른편에서 놀이하던 자에게 말했다. \"건강을 위하여—그리고 자네 서판을 잠깐 빌리세.\""]], [["He raised the waxen boards, glanced at the memoranda of wagers, and tossed them down.", "그는 밀랍 판을 들어 올려 내기 기록을 흘끗 보고는 도로 내던졌다."]], [["“Denarii, only denarii—coin of cartmen and butchers!” he said, with a scornful laugh.", "\"데나리우스, 고작 데나리우스—짐꾼과 푸줏간 놈들이나 쓰는 돈이로군!\" 그가 비웃으며 말했다."], ["“By the drunken Semele, to what is Rome coming, when a Cæsar sits o’ nights waiting a turn of fortune to bring him but a beggarly denarius!”", "\"취한 세멜레를 두고, 로마가 대체 어찌 되려고 이러는가. 카이사르쯤 되는 자가 밤새 앉아 고작 거지 같은 데나리우스 한 닢을 가져다줄 운수의 변덕이나 기다리고 있다니!\""]], [["The scion of the Drusi reddened to his brows, but the bystanders broke in upon his reply by surging closer around the table, and shouting, “The Messala!", "드루수스 가문의 자손은 이마까지 벌게졌으나, 그가 미처 대꾸하기도 전에 둘러선 자들이 탁자 둘레로 더 바짝 밀려들며 끼어들어 외쳤다. \"메살라다!"], ["the Messala!”", "메살라야!\""]], [["“Men of the Tiber,”", "\"티베르의 사내들이여,\""], ["Messala continued, wresting a box with the dice in it from a hand near-by, “who is he most favored of the gods?", "메살라가 가까이 있던 누군가의 손에서 주사위가 든 통을 빼앗으며 말을 이었다. \"신들의 사랑을 가장 많이 받는 자가 누구인가?"], ["A Roman.", "로마인이다."], ["Who is he lawgiver of the nations?", "만방에 법을 내리는 자가 누구인가?"], ["A Roman.", "로마인이다."], ["Who is he, by sword right, the universal master?”", "칼의 권리로 만인의 주인이 된 자는 누구인가?\""]], [["The company were of the easily inspired, and the thought was one to which they were born; in a twinkling they snatched the answer from him.", "이 무리는 쉽게 흥분하는 자들이었고, 그 생각은 그들이 날 때부터 품어 온 것이었다. 순식간에 그들은 그의 입에서 답을 채어 갔다."]], [["“A Roman, a Roman!”", "\"로마인이다, 로마인이야!\""], ["they shouted.", "그들이 외쳤다."]], [["“Yet—yet”—he lingered to catch their ears—“yet there is a better than the best of Rome.”", "\"하지만—하지만\"—그는 그들의 귀를 사로잡으려 뜸을 들였다—\"하지만 로마 최고의 자보다 더 나은 자가 있다.\""]], [["He tossed his patrician head and paused, as if to sting them with his sneer.", "그는 귀족다운 머리를 젖히고 멈추었으니, 마치 그 비웃음으로 그들을 쏘아붙이려는 듯했다."]], [["“Hear ye?”", "\"들리는가?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“There is a better than the best of Rome.”", "\"로마 최고의 자보다 더 나은 자가 있다.\""]], [["“Ay—Hercules!”", "\"오라—헤르쿨레스다!\""], ["cried one.", "한 사람이 외쳤다."]], [["“Bacchus!”", "\"바쿠스다!\""], ["yelled a satirist.", "한 익살꾼이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Jove—Jove!” thundered the crowd.", "\"유피테르—유피테르다!\" 무리가 우레처럼 외쳤다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["Messala answered, “among men.”", "메살라가 답했다. \"사람들 가운데서 말이다.\""]], [["“Name him, name him!”", "\"이름을 대라, 이름을 대!\""], ["they demanded.", "그들이 다그쳤다."]], [["“I will,”", "\"대지,\""], ["he said, the next lull. “He who to the perfection of Rome hath added the perfection of the East; who to the arm of conquest, which is Western, hath also the art needful to the enjoyment of dominion, which is Eastern.”", "그가 다음 잠잠한 틈에 말했다. \"로마의 완벽함에 동방의 완벽함을 더한 자, 서방의 것인 정복의 팔에다 동방의 것인 지배를 누리는 데 필요한 기술까지 갖춘 자다.\""]], [["“Perpol!", "\"맹세코!"], ["His best is a Roman, after all,”", "결국 그 최고의 자도 로마인이로군.\""], ["some one shouted; and there was a great laugh, and long clapping of hands—an admission that Messala had the advantage.", "누군가가 외쳤다. 그러자 큰 웃음이 터지고 손뼉 소리가 길게 이어졌으니, 메살라가 한 수 위라는 것을 인정한 것이었다."]], [["“In the East”", "\"동방에는,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"우리에게 신이 없다."], ["he continued, “we have no gods, only Wine, Women, and Fortune, and the greatest of them is Fortune; wherefore our motto, ‘Who dareth what I dare?’—fit for the senate, fit for battle, fittest for him who, seeking the best, challenges the worst.”", "오직 술과 여자와 운수뿐이며, 그 가운데 가장 위대한 것이 운수다. 그래서 우리의 좌우명이 '누가 감히 내가 하는 일을 하겠는가?'라네. 원로원에도 어울리고, 전쟁에도 어울리며, 최고를 좇으면서 최악에 도전하는 자에게 가장 어울리는 말이지.\""]], [["His voice dropped into an easy, familiar tone, but without relaxing the ascendancy he had gained.", "그의 목소리가 편안하고 친근한 어조로 낮아졌으나, 그가 얻은 우위는 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."]], [["“In the great chest up in the citadel I have five talents coin current in the markets, and here are the receipts for them.”", "\"성채 위 그 큰 궤짝에 시장에서 통용되는 돈으로 다섯 탈렌트가 들어 있고, 여기 그 영수증이 있다.\""]], [["From his tunic he drew a roll of paper, and, flinging it on the table, continued, amidst breathless silence, every eye having him in view fixed on his, every ear listening:", "그는 튜닉에서 종이 두루마리를 꺼내 탁자 위에 던지고는, 숨죽인 침묵 속에서 말을 이었으니, 모든 눈이 그에게 쏠렸고 모든 귀가 그에게 기울었다."]], [["“The sum lies there the measure of what I dare.", "\"저기 놓인 그 액수가 바로 내가 감히 거는 만큼의 척도다."], ["Who of you dares so much! You are silent.", "너희 가운데 누가 그만큼 감히 걸겠는가!"], ["Is it too great?", "잠잠하구나. 너무 큰가?"], ["I will strike off one talent. What!", "그럼 한 탈렌트를 깎지. 뭐라고!"], ["still silent?", "그래도 잠잠한가?"], ["Come, then, throw me once for these three talents—only three; for two; for one—one at least—one for the honor of the river by which you were born—Rome East against Rome West!—Orontes the barbarous against Tiber the sacred!”", "자, 그럼 이 세 탈렌트를 두고 한 번 던져 보아라—고작 셋이다. 둘이라도, 하나라도—적어도 하나라도—너희가 태어난 그 강의 명예를 위해 하나라도—동방의 로마가 서방의 로마에 맞서서! 야만의 오론테스가 신성한 티베르에 맞서서!\""]], [["He rattled the dice overhead while waiting.", "그는 기다리는 동안 머리 위로 주사위를 달그락거렸다."]], [["“The Orontes against the Tiber!”", "\"오론테스가 티베르에 맞서서!\""], ["he repeated, with an increase of scornful emphasis.", "그가 비웃음을 한층 더해 거듭 외쳤다."]], [["Not a man moved; then he flung the box upon the table and, laughing, took up the receipts.", "아무도 움직이지 않았다. 그러자 그는 통을 탁자 위에 내던지고 웃으며 영수증을 도로 집어 들었다."]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["By the Olympian Jove, I know now ye have fortunes to make or to mend; therefore are ye come to Antioch.", "올림포스의 유피테르를 두고, 이제 알겠다. 너희가 재산을 모으거나 메우러 온 자들이라는 걸. 그래서 안티오크에 온 게로군."], ["Ho, Cecilius!”", "호, 케킬리우스!\""]], [["“Here, Messala!”", "\"여기 있네, 메살라!\""], ["cried a man behind him; “here am I, perishing in the mob, and begging a drachma to settle with the ragged ferryman.", "그의 뒤에서 한 사람이 외쳤다. \"여기 있네. 군중 틈에서 죽을 지경이라 누더기 입은 뱃사공에게 치를 드라크마 한 닢을 구걸하던 참이지."], ["But, Pluto take me! these new ones have not so much as an obolus among them.”", "한데, 플루토가 날 잡아가도 좋아! 이 새내기들은 오볼루스 한 닢도 가진 게 없다네.\""]], [["The sally provoked a burst of laughter, under which the saloon rang and rang again. Messala alone kept his gravity.", "이 익살에 웃음이 터져, 응접실이 울리고 또 울렸다. 오직 메살라만이 진지함을 지켰다."]], [["“Go, thou,”", "\"자네는 가게,\""], ["he said to Cecilius, “to the chamber whence we came, and bid the servants bring the amphorae here, and the cups and goblets.", "그가 케킬리우스에게 말했다. \"우리가 온 그 방으로 가서, 하인들더러 암포라들을, 잔과 술잔들을 이리로 가져오라 이르게."], ["If these our countrymen, looking for fortune, have not purses, by the Syrian Bacchus, I will see if they are not better blessed with stomachs!", "재산을 찾아 나선 우리 동포들에게 지갑이 없다면, 시리아의 바쿠스를 두고, 위장이라도 좀 더 잘 갖추었는지 어디 보세!"], ["Haste thee!”", "어서 가게!\""]], [["Then he turned to Drusus, with a laugh heard throughout the apartment.", "그러고는 드루수스에게 돌아서며, 방 안에 다 들리도록 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Ha, ha, my friend!", "\"하, 하, 내 친구여!"], ["Be thou not offended because I levelled the Cæsar in thee down to the denarii.", "자네 안의 카이사르를 데나리우스 수준으로 끌어내렸다 해서 노여워 말게."], ["Thou seest I did but use the name to try these fine fledglings of our old Rome.", "보다시피 나는 다만 그 이름을 빌려 우리 오랜 로마의 이 곱상한 풋내기들을 시험해 본 것뿐이라네."], ["Come, my Drusus, come!”", "자, 내 드루수스여, 자!\""], ["He took up the box again and rattled the dice merrily.", "그는 다시 통을 집어 들어 흥겹게 주사위를 달그락거렸다."], ["“Here, for what sum thou wilt, let us measure fortunes.”", "\"자, 자네가 원하는 액수만큼, 우리 운수를 한번 겨뤄 보세.\""]], [["The manner was frank, cordial, winsome.", "그 태도는 솔직하고 정겹고 사람을 사로잡았다."], ["Drusus melted in a moment.", "드루수스는 이내 마음이 누그러졌다."]], [["“By the Nymphae, yes!”", "\"님프들을 두고, 좋아!\""], ["he said, laughing.", "그가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I will throw with thee, Messala—for a denarius.”", "\"자네와 던져 보겠네, 메살라—데나리우스 한 닢을 걸고.\""]], [["A very boyish person was looking over the table watching the scene.", "매우 앳된 자가 탁자 너머로 그 광경을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Suddenly Messala turned to him.", "별안간 메살라가 그를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Who art thou?”", "\"너는 누구냐?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["The lad drew back.", "소년은 움찔 물러섰다."]], [["“Nay, by Castor!", "\"아니, 카스토르를 두고!"], ["and his brother too!", "또 그의 형제까지 두고!"], ["I meant not offence. It is a rule among men, in matters other than dice, to keep the record closest when the deal is least.", "기분 상하게 하려던 게 아니다. 사내들 사이에는, 주사위 말고 다른 일에서도, 거래가 작을수록 기록을 더 꼼꼼히 챙기는 법이라네."], ["I have need of a clerk.", "내겐 서기가 필요하다."], ["Wilt thou serve me?”", "나를 위해 일해 주겠느냐?\""]], [["The young fellow drew his tablets ready to keep the score: the manner was irresistible.", "젊은이는 점수를 적으려고 서판을 꺼내 들었으니, 그 태도는 거부할 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["“Hold, Messala, hold!”", "\"잠깐, 메살라, 잠깐!\""], ["cried Drusus.", "드루수스가 외쳤다."], ["“I know not if it be ominous to stay the poised dice with a question; but one occurs to me, and I must ask it though Venus slap me with her girdle.”", "\"겨눈 주사위를 물음으로 멈추는 게 불길한 일인지는 모르겠으나, 한 가지 떠오르는 게 있어서, 베누스가 그 허리띠로 날 후려치더라도 묻지 않을 수 없네.\""]], [["“Nay, my Drusus, Venus with her girdle off is Venus in love.", "\"아니, 내 드루수스여, 허리띠를 푼 베누스는 사랑에 빠진 베누스라네."], ["To thy question—I will make the throw and hold it against mischance. Thus—”", "자네 물음에 답하지—나는 던지되 변고에 대비해 그대로 눌러 두겠네. 이렇게—\""]], [["He turned the box upon the table and held it firmly over the dice.", "그는 통을 탁자 위에 뒤집고는 주사위 위에 단단히 눌러 잡았다."]], [["And Drusus asked, “Did you ever see one Quintus Arrius?”", "그러자 드루수스가 물었다. \"퀸투스 아리우스라는 자를 본 적 있나?\""]], [["“The duumvir?” “No—his son?”", "\"그 집정관 말인가?\""]], [["“I knew not he had a son.”", "\"그에게 아들이 있는 줄은 몰랐네.\""]], [["“Well, it is nothing,”", "\"그에게 아들이 있는 줄은 몰랐는데.\""], ["Drusus added, indifferently; “only, my Messala, Pollux was not more like Castor than Arrius is like thee.”", "\"뭐, 별것 아니네,\" 드루수스가 무심하게 덧붙였다. \"다만, 내 메살라여, 폴룩스가 카스토르를 닮은 것보다 그 아리우스가 자넬 더 닮았다는 것뿐이지.\""]], [["The remark had the effect of a signal: twenty voices took it up.", "이 말은 신호 같은 효과를 냈으니, 스무 명의 목소리가 그것을 받아 외쳤다."]], [["“True, true!", "\"맞아, 맞아!"], ["His eyes—his face,”", "그 눈매며—그 얼굴이며.\""], ["they cried.", "그들이 외쳤다."]], [["“What!” answered one, disgusted.", "\"무슨 소리!\" 한 사람이 못마땅한 듯 답했다."], ["“Messala is a Roman; Arrius is a Jew.”", "\"메살라는 로마인이고, 아리우스는 유대인이야.\""]], [["“Thou sayest right,”", "\"자네 말이 옳아,\""], ["a third exclaimed.", "또 한 사람이 외쳤다."], ["“He is a Jew, or Momus lent his mother the wrong mask.”", "\"그자는 유대인이지. 아니면 모무스가 그 어미에게 엉뚱한 가면을 빌려 준 게로군.\""]], [["There was promise of a dispute; seeing which, Messala interposed.", "다툼이 벌어질 낌새가 보이자, 그것을 알아챈 메살라가 끼어들었다."], ["“The wine is not come, my Drusus; and, as thou seest, I have the freckled Pythias as they were dogs in leash.", "\"술이 아직 오지 않았네, 내 드루수스여. 그리고 보다시피 나는 이 주근깨투성이 피티아스들을 끈에 묶인 개처럼 붙들고 있지."], ["As to Arrius, I will accept thy opinion of him, so thou tell me more about him.”", "아리우스에 대해서는, 그에 관해 더 일러 주기만 하면 자네 의견을 받아들이겠네.\""]], [["“Well, be he Jew or Roman—and, by the great god Pan, I say it not in disrespect of thy feelings, my Messala!—this Arrius is handsome and brave and shrewd.", "\"음, 유대인이든 로마인이든—그리고 위대한 신 판을 두고, 자네 기분을 상하게 하려고 하는 말이 아닐세, 내 메살라여!—이 아리우스란 자는 잘생기고 용감하고 영리하네."], ["The emperor offered him favor and patronage, which he refused.", "황제가 총애와 후원을 베풀려 했으나 그는 마다했지."], ["He came up through mystery, and keepeth distance as if he felt himself better or knew himself worse than the rest of us.", "그는 알 수 없는 내력을 거쳐 떠올랐고, 마치 자신이 우리보다 낫다고 느끼거나 자신이 우리보다 못하다는 걸 아는 듯 거리를 두고 지낸다네."], ["In the palaestrae he was unmatched; he played with the blue-eyed giants from the Rhine and the hornless bulls of Sarmatia as they were willow wisps.", "격투장에서는 당할 자가 없었지. 라인강에서 온 푸른 눈의 거인들과 사르마티아의 뿔 없는 황소 같은 자들을 마치 버들가지 다루듯 가지고 놀았어."], ["The duumvir left him vastly rich.", "집정관이 그에게 막대한 재산을 남겼지."], ["He has a passion for arms, and thinks of nothing but war.", "그는 무예에 열정을 품고 있어 오직 전쟁만 생각한다네."], ["Maxentius admitted him into his family, and he was to have taken ship with us, but we lost him at Ravenna.", "막센티우스가 그를 자기 식솔로 받아들였고, 그는 우리와 함께 배를 타기로 되어 있었으나, 우리는 라벤나에서 그를 놓쳤지."], ["Nevertheless he arrived safely.", "그래도 그는 무사히 도착했네."], ["We heard of him this morning. Perpol!", "오늘 아침 그의 소식을 들었거든. 맹세코!"], ["Instead of coming to the palace or going to the citadel, he dropped his baggage at the khan, and hath disappeared again.”", "그런데 궁전으로 오거나 성채로 가는 대신, 그는 짐을 여관에 부려 놓고 다시 종적을 감췄다네.\""]], [["At the beginning of the speech Messala listened with polite indifference; as it proceeded, he became more attentive; at the conclusion, he took his hand from the dice-box, and called out, “Ho, my Caius!", "말이 시작될 무렵 메살라는 점잖은 무심함으로 듣고 있었다. 말이 이어질수록 그는 더 귀를 기울였고, 끝에 이르자 주사위 통에서 손을 떼고 외쳤다. \"호, 내 가이우스!"], ["Dost thou hear?”", "듣고 있나?\""]], [["A youth at his elbow—his Myrtilus, or comrade, in the day’s chariot practice—answered, much pleased with the attention, “Did I not, my Messala, I were not thy friend.”", "그의 곁에 있던 한 젊은이—그날의 전차 연습에서 그의 미르틸루스, 곧 동료였던 자가—그 관심에 무척 기뻐하며 답했다. \"안 듣고 있었다면, 내 메살라여, 내가 자네 친구가 아니겠지.\""]], [["“Dost thou remember the man who gave thee the fall to-day?”", "\"오늘 자넬 넘어뜨린 그 사내를 기억하나?\""]], [["“By the love-locks of Bacchus, have I not a bruised shoulder to help me keep it in mind?”", "\"바쿠스의 머리타래를 두고, 그걸 잊지 않게 도와줄 멍든 어깨가 여기 있지 않은가?\""], ["and he seconded the words with a shrug that submerged his ears.", "그러더니 그는 두 귀가 파묻힐 만큼 어깨를 으쓱하며 그 말에 힘을 보탰다."]], [["“Well, be thou grateful to the Fates—I have found thy enemy.", "\"음, 운명의 여신들에게 감사하게나—내가 자네의 원수를 찾아냈으니."], ["Listen.”", "들어 보게.\""]], [["Thereupon Messala turned to Drusus.", "그러고서 메살라는 드루수스에게 돌아섰다."]], [["“Tell us more of him—perpol!—of him who is both Jew and Roman—by Phoebus, a combination to make a Centaur lovely!", "\"그에 대해 더 일러 주게—맹세코!—유대인이면서 로마인이기도 한 그자 말일세—포이보스를 두고, 켄타우로스를 사랑스럽게 만들 만한 조합이로군!"], ["What garments doth he affect, my Drusus?”", "그자는 어떤 옷을 즐겨 입나, 내 드루수스여?\""]], [["“Those of the Jews.”", "\"유대인의 옷일세.\""]], [["“Hearest thou, Caius?”", "\"듣고 있나, 가이우스?\" 메살라가 말했다."], ["said Messala.", "\"그자는 젊다—하나."], ["“The fellow is young—one; he hath the visage of a Roman—two; he loveth best the garb of a Jew—three; and in the palaestrae fame and fortune come of arms to throw a horse or tilt a chariot, as the necessity may order—four.", "로마인의 얼굴을 지녔다—둘. 유대인의 옷차림을 가장 좋아한다—셋. 격투장에서는 말을 넘어뜨리거나, 필요하면 전차를 부리는 무예로 명성과 재물을 얻는다—넷."], ["And, Drusus, help thou my friend again.", "그리고 드루수스, 내 친구를 다시 한번 도와주게."], ["Doubtless this Arrius hath tricks of language; otherwise he could not so confound himself, to-day a Jew, to-morrow a Roman; but of the rich tongue of Athene—discourseth he in that as well?”", "분명 이 아리우스는 말솜씨에 재주가 있을 거야. 그렇지 않고서야 어떻게 오늘은 유대인, 내일은 로마인으로 자기를 그토록 뒤섞을 수 있겠나. 한데 아테나의 풍부한 언어는—그것도 능란하게 구사하나?\""]], [["“With such purity, Messala, he might have been a contestant in the Isthmia.”", "\"어찌나 순수한지, 메살라, 이스트미아 경기에 출전해도 손색이 없을 정도라네.\""]], [["“Art thou listening, Caius?”", "\"듣고 있나, 가이우스?\""], ["said Messala.", "메살라가 말했다."], ["“The fellow is qualified to salute a woman—for that matter Aristomache herself—in the Greek; and as I keep the count, that is five. What sayest thou?”", "\"그자는 그리스어로 여인에게—심지어 아리스토마케 같은 여인에게도—인사를 건넬 자격이 있단 말이지. 내 셈으로는 그게 다섯이다. 어떤가?\""]], [["“Thou hast found him, my Messala,”", "\"자네가 그자를 찾아냈네, 내 메살라여,\""], ["Caius answered; “or I am not myself.”", "가이우스가 답했다. \"아니면 내가 내가 아닐세.\""]], [["“Thy pardon, Drusus—and pardon of all—for speaking in riddles thus,”", "\"용서하게, 드루수스—그리고 모두 용서하게—이렇게 수수께끼처럼 말한 것을,\""], ["Messala said, in his winsome way.", "메살라가 사람을 사로잡는 그 투로 말했다."], ["“By all the decent gods, I would not strain thy courtesy to the point of breaking, but now help thou me.", "\"점잖은 모든 신을 두고, 자네 호의를 끊어질 지경까지 잡아당기고 싶지는 않으나, 이제 나를 도와주게."], ["See!”—he put his hand on the dice-box again, laughing—“See how close I hold the Pythias and their secret!", "보게!\"—그는 다시 주사위 통에 손을 얹고 웃었다—\"보게, 내가 이 피티아스들과 그 비밀을 얼마나 단단히 붙들고 있는지!"], ["Thou didst speak, I think, of mystery in connection with the coming of the son of Arrius. Tell me of that.”", "자네는 아까 아리우스의 아들이 온 일과 관련해 알 수 없는 내력 운운했지. 그것에 대해 일러 주게.\""]], [["“’Tis nothing, Messala, nothing,”", "\"별것 아닐세, 메살라, 별것 아니야,\""], ["Drusus replied; “a child’s story.", "드루수스가 답했다. \"어린애 이야기 같은 것이지."], ["When Arrius, the father, sailed in pursuit of the pirates, he was without wife or family; he returned with a boy—him of whom we speak—and next day adopted him.”", "아비 아리우스가 해적을 쫓아 출항했을 때 그에게는 아내도 식솔도 없었네. 그런데 한 소년을 데리고 돌아왔으니—바로 우리가 말하는 그자였지—그러고는 이튿날 그를 양자로 들였다네.\""]], [["“Adopted him?”", "\"양자로 들였다고?\""], ["Messala repeated.", "메살라가 되뇌었다."], ["“By the gods, Drusus, thou dost, indeed, interest me!", "\"신들을 두고, 드루수스, 정말로 흥미롭군!"], ["Where did the duumvir find the boy?", "그 집정관이 어디서 그 소년을 찾아냈나?"], ["And who was he?”", "또 그가 누구였나?\""]], [["“Who shall answer thee that, Messala?", "\"그걸 누가 답해 줄 수 있겠나, 메살라?"], ["who but the young Arrius himself? Perpol!", "젊은 아리우스 자신 말고는 아무도 못 하지. 맹세코!"], ["in the fight the duumvir—then but a tribune—lost his galley.", "그 싸움에서 집정관은—그때는 호민관에 불과했지만—자기 갤리선을 잃었네."], ["A returning vessel found him and one other—all of the crew who survived—afloat upon the same plank.", "돌아오던 배가 그와 또 한 사람을—살아남은 선원이라곤 그 둘뿐이었지—같은 널빤지에 떠 있는 채로 건져 냈다네."], ["I give you now the story of the rescuers, which hath this excellence at least—it hath never been contradicted.", "이제 자네에게 구조한 자들의 이야기를 들려주지. 이 이야기는 적어도 한 가지 장점이 있으니—한 번도 반박된 적이 없다는 점이야."], ["They say, the duumvir’s companion on the plank was a Jew—”", "그들 말로는, 널빤지 위에 있던 집정관의 동행이 유대인이었다네—\""]], [["“A Jew!” echoed Messala.", "\"유대인이라고!\" 메살라가 되받았다."]], [["“And a slave.”", "\"게다가 노예였지.\""]], [["“How Drusus?", "\"뭐라고, 드루수스?"], ["A slave?”", "노예라고?\""]], [["“When the two were lifted to the deck, the duumvir was in his tribune’s armor, and the other in the vesture of a rower.”", "\"두 사람을 갑판으로 끌어올렸을 때, 집정관은 호민관의 갑옷을 입고 있었고, 다른 자는 노 젓는 자의 옷차림이었다네.\""]], [["Messala rose from leaning against the table.", "메살라는 탁자에 기대고 있던 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“A galley”—he checked the debasing word, and looked around, for once in his life at loss.", "\"갤리선이라\"—그는 그 천박한 말을 멈추고 둘레를 둘러보았으니, 평생 처음으로 어쩔 줄 모르는 모습이었다."], ["Just then a procession of slaves filed into the room, some with great jars of wine, others with baskets of fruits and confections, others again with cups and flagons, mostly silver.", "바로 그때 노예들의 행렬이 줄지어 방으로 들어왔다. 더러는 큰 술 항아리를 들고, 더러는 과일과 과자가 담긴 바구니를 들고, 또 더러는 거의가 은으로 된 잔과 병을 들고 있었다."], ["There was inspiration in the sight.", "그 광경에 영감이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Instantly Messala climbed upon a stool.", "메살라는 즉시 걸상 위로 올라섰다."]], [["“Men of the Tiber,”", "\"티베르의 사내들이여,\""], ["he said, in a clear voice, “let us turn this waiting for our chief into a feast of Bacchus.", "그가 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. \"우리 총사령관을 기다리는 이 시간을 바쿠스의 잔치로 바꾸자."], ["Whom choose ye for master?”", "누구를 좌장으로 뽑겠는가?\""]], [["Drusus arose.", "드루수스가 일어섰다."]], [["“Who shall be master but the giver of the feast?”", "\"잔치를 베푼 자 말고 누가 좌장이 되겠는가?\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Answer, Romans.”", "\"답하라, 로마인들이여.\""]], [["They gave their reply in a shout.", "그들은 함성으로 답했다."]], [["Messala took the chaplet from his head, gave it to Drusus, who climbed upon the table, and, in the view of all, solemnly replaced it, making Messala master of the night.", "메살라는 자기 머리에서 화관을 벗어 드루수스에게 건넸고, 드루수스는 탁자 위로 올라가 모두가 보는 앞에서 엄숙하게 그것을 도로 씌워, 메살라를 그 밤의 좌장으로 삼았다."]], [["“There came with me into the room,”", "\"나와 함께 이 방에 들어온 자들 가운데,\""], ["he said, “some friends just risen from table.", "그가 말했다. \"방금 식탁에서 일어난 친구들이 있다."], ["That our feast may have the approval of sacred custom, bring hither that one of them most overcome by wine.”", "우리 잔치가 신성한 관습의 인정을 받도록, 그들 가운데 술에 가장 취한 자를 이리로 데려오라.\""]], [["A din of voices answered, “Here he is, here he is!”", "여러 목소리가 떠들썩하게 답했다. \"여기 있다, 여기 있어!\""]], [["And from the floor where he had fallen, a youth was brought forward, so effeminately beautiful he might have passed for the drinking-god himself—only the crown would have dropped from his head, and the thyrsus from his hand.", "그러고는 쓰러져 있던 바닥에서 한 젊은이가 끌려 나왔으니, 어찌나 여인처럼 곱게 아름다운지 술의 신 그 자체로 보아도 될 정도였다—다만 머리에서는 관이 떨어지고, 손에서는 지팡이가 떨어질 뿐이었다."]], [["“Lift him upon the table,”", "\"그를 탁자 위에 올려라,\""], ["the master said.", "좌장이 말했다."]], [["It was found he could not sit.", "그가 앉아 있지조차 못한다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["“Help him, Drusus, as the fair Nyone may yet help thee.”", "\"그를 부축하게, 드루수스. 아름다운 니오네가 언젠가 자네를 부축해 줄 것처럼.\""]], [["Drusus took the inebriate in his arms.", "드루수스는 그 취한 자를 두 팔로 안았다."]], [["Then addressing the limp figure, Messala said, amidst profound silence, “O Bacchus!", "그러자 메살라는 그 축 늘어진 형상을 향해, 깊은 침묵 속에서 말했다. \"오, 바쿠스여!"], ["greatest of the gods, be thou propitious to-night.", "신들 가운데 가장 위대한 분이시여, 오늘 밤 우리에게 은혜를 베푸소서."], ["And for myself, and these thy votaries, I vow this chaplet”—and from his head he raised it reverently—“I vow this chaplet to thy altar in the Grove of Daphne.”", "그리고 저 자신과, 당신의 이 추종자들을 위하여, 이 화관을 바치나이다\"—그러고는 머리에서 화관을 경건하게 들어 올렸다—\"이 화관을 다프네 숲의 당신 제단에 바치기로 맹세하나이다.\""]], [["He bowed, replaced the crown upon his locks, then stooped and uncovered the dice, saying, with a laugh, “See, my Drusus, by the ass of Silenus, the denarius is mine!”", "그는 머리를 숙이고 화관을 다시 머리타래에 얹은 뒤, 몸을 굽혀 주사위를 드러내며 웃으며 말했다. \"보게, 내 드루수스여, 실레노스의 나귀를 두고, 데나리우스는 내 것일세!\""]], [["There was a shout that set the floor to quaking, and the grim Atlantes to dancing, and the orgies began.", "바닥을 흔들고 험상궂은 아틀라스 상들을 춤추게 할 만한 함성이 터졌고, 그렇게 광란의 향연이 시작되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII", "# CHAPTER XIII"]], [["Sheik Ilderim was a man of too much importance to go about with a small establishment.", "일더림 족장은 하찮은 살림살이를 거느리고 다니기에는 너무도 위세 있는 인물이었다."], ["He had a reputation to keep with his tribe, such as became a prince and patriarch of the greatest following in all the Desert east of Syria; with the people of the cities he had another reputation, which was that of one of the richest personages not a king in all the East; and, being rich in fact—in money as well as in servants, camels, horses, and flocks of all kinds—he took pleasure in a certain state, which, besides magnifying his dignity with strangers, contributed to his personal pride and comfort.", "그는 자기 부족 사이에서 지켜야 할 명성이 있었으니, 시리아 동쪽 사막을 통틀어 가장 큰 무리를 이끄는 군주이자 가부장에게 어울리는 명성이었다. 도시 사람들 사이에서는 또 다른 명성을 누렸으니, 동방 전역에서 왕이 아닌 자들 가운데 가장 부유한 인물 중 하나라는 명성이었다. 그리고 사실 그는 부자였다."], ["Wherefore the reader must not be misled by the frequent reference to his tent in the Orchard of Palms.", "돈은 물론이요 하인과 낙타와 말, 온갖 종류의 가축에 이르기까지 풍족했기에, 그는 어떤 위풍을 누리는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다. 그 위풍은 낯선 이들 앞에서 그의 품격을 드높이는 한편, 개인적인 자긍심과 안락에도 보탬이 되었다. 그러므로 독자는 종려 과수원에 있던 그의 천막을 자주 들먹인다 하여 오해해서는 안 된다."], ["He had there really a respectable dowar; that is to say, he had there three large tents—one for himself, one for visitors, one for his favorite wife and her women; and six or eight lesser ones, occupied by his servants and such tribal retainers as he had chosen to bring with him as a body-guard—strong men of approved courage, and skillful with bow, spear, and horses.", "그곳에 그는 실로 번듯한 다와르를 두고 있었다. 다시 말해 큰 천막 세 채가 있었으니, 하나는 그 자신을 위한 것, 하나는 손님을 위한 것, 하나는 그가 가장 아끼는 아내와 그 시녀들을 위한 것이었다. 그리고 작은 천막 예닐곱 채가 있어, 그의 하인들과 호위병으로 삼아 데려온 부족의 신하들이 머물렀으니, 그들은 용기를 인정받은 건장한 사내들로 활과 창과 말 다루는 솜씨가 뛰어났다."]], [["To be sure, his property of whatever kind was in no danger at the Orchard; yet as the habits of a man go with him to town not less than the country, and as it is never wise to slip the bands of discipline, the interior of the dowar was devoted to his cows, camels, goats, and such property in general as might tempt a lion or a thief.", "* 다와르: 천막들을 둥글게 둘러 친 아랍의 야영지. 물론 그의 재산은 어떤 종류든 과수원에서 위험에 처할 일이 없었다. 그러나 사람의 습관이란 시골에서뿐 아니라 도시에서도 그를 따라다니는 법이요, 또 규율의 끈을 늦추는 것은 결코 현명하지 못한 일이기에, 다와르의 안쪽은 그의 소와 낙타와 염소, 그리고 사자나 도둑을 꾈 만한 온갖 재물에 내어 주었다."]], [["To do him full justice, Ilderim kept well all the customs of his people, abating none, not even the smallest; in consequence his life at the Orchard was a continuation of his life in the Desert; nor that alone, it was a fair reproduction of the old patriarchal modes—the genuine pastoral life of primitive Israel.", "일더림을 공정하게 평하자면, 그는 자기 백성의 모든 관습을 잘 지켰으니, 그 무엇도, 가장 작은 것조차도 빠뜨리지 않았다. 그 결과 과수원에서의 그의 삶은 사막에서의 삶이 그대로 이어진 것이었다. 그뿐 아니라 그것은 옛 가부장적 방식을 고스란히 재현한 것이었으니, 곧 원시 이스라엘의 진정한 목가적 삶이었다."]], [["Recurring to the morning the caravan arrived at the Orchard—“Here, plant it here,”", "대상이 과수원에 다다른 그날 아침으로 돌아가 보면—\"여기, 여기에 세워라.\""], ["he said, stopping his horse, and thrusting a spear into the ground.", "그는 말을 멈추고 땅에 창을 꽂으며 말했다."], ["“Door to the south; the lake before it thus; and these, the children of the Desert, to sit under at the going-down of the sun.”", "\"문은 남쪽으로, 호수는 그 앞에 이렇게 두고, 그리고 이것들, 사막의 자식들은 해가 질 때 그 그늘에 앉을 수 있도록.\""]], [["At the last words he went to a group of three great palm-trees, and patted one of them as he would have patted his horse’s neck, or the cheek of the child of his love.", "마지막 말과 함께 그는 우람한 종려나무 세 그루가 모여 선 곳으로 가서, 마치 자기 말의 목덜미나 사랑하는 자식의 뺨을 어루만지듯 그 가운데 한 그루를 토닥였다."]], [["Who but the sheik could of right say to the caravan, Halt!", "대상을 향해 \"멈추어라!\" 하고, 천막을 두고 \"여기에 세워라!\""], ["or of the tent, Here be it pitched? The spear was wrested from the ground, and over the wound it had riven in the sod the base of the first pillar of the tent was planted, marking the centre of the front door.", "하고 떳떳이 말할 수 있는 자가 족장 말고 또 누가 있겠는가? 창이 땅에서 뽑히고, 창이 흙에 낸 상처 위로 천막 첫 기둥의 밑동이 세워져, 정문의 한가운데를 표시했다."], ["Then eight others were planted—in all, three rows of pillars, three in a row.", "그런 다음 여덟 개가 더 세워졌으니, 모두 세 줄에 한 줄마다 셋씩이었다."], ["Then, at call, the women and children came, and unfolded the canvas from its packing on the camels.", "그러자 부름에 따라 여인들과 아이들이 와서, 낙타 등에 꾸려 둔 천을 풀었다."], ["Who might do this but the women?", "여인들 말고 누가 이 일을 하겠는가?"], ["Had they not sheared the hair from the brown goats of the flock? and twisted it into thread?", "그들이 가축 떼의 갈색 염소에게서 털을 깎지 않았던가? 그것을 꼬아 실을 만들지 않았던가?"], ["and woven the thread into cloth?", "그 실을 짜서 천을 만들지 않았던가?"], ["and stitched the cloth together, making the perfect roof, dark-brown in fact, though in the distance black as the tents of Kedar?", "그 천을 한데 꿰매어, 사실은 짙은 갈색이지만 멀리서 보면 게달의 천막처럼 새까만, 완벽한 지붕을 만들지 않았던가?"], ["And, finally, with what jests and laughter, and pulls altogether, the united following of the sheik stretched the canvas from pillar to pillar, driving the stakes and fastening the cords as they went!", "그리고 마침내, 족장의 한데 모인 무리가 얼마나 우스갯소리와 웃음을 주고받으며 다 함께 잡아당겨, 천을 기둥에서 기둥으로 팽팽히 펼치고, 말뚝을 박고 줄을 동여매며 나아갔던가!"], ["And when the walls of open reed matting were put in place—the finishing-touch to the building after the style of the Desert—with what hush of anxiety they waited the good man’s judgment!", "그리고 사막의 방식대로 건물의 마무리 손질로서 갈대를 엮어 만든 성긴 벽이 제자리에 세워졌을 때, 그들은 얼마나 조마조마하게 숨죽이며 그 어진 분의 판단을 기다렸던가!"], ["When he walked in and out, looking at the house in connection with the sun, the trees, and the lake, and said, rubbing his hands with might of heartiness, “Well done!", "그가 안팎을 거닐며 해와 나무와 호수에 견주어 그 집을 살펴보고는, 진심에서 우러난 힘으로 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"잘했다! 이제 너희가 잘 아는 대로 다와르를 차려라."], ["Make the dowar now as ye well know, and to-night we will sweeten the bread with arrack, and the milk with honey, and at every fire there shall be a kid. God with ye!", "오늘 밤에는 아라크로 빵을 달게 하고 꿀로 우유를 달게 하며, 화롯불마다 새끼 염소 한 마리씩 구울 것이다. 하느님이 너희와 함께하시기를!"], ["Want of sweet water there shall not be, for the lake is our well; neither shall the bearers of burden hunger, or the least of the flock, for here is green pasture also. God with you all, my children! Go.”", "단물 모자랄 일은 없을 터이니 이 호수가 우리의 우물이요, 짐 나르는 짐승도 가장 작은 가축도 굶주리지 않을 터이니 여기 푸른 목초지 또한 있음이라. 하느님이 너희 모두와 함께하시기를, 내 자식들아! 가거라.\""]], [["And, shouting, the many happy went their ways then to pitch their own habitations.", "* 아라크: 대추야자 등으로 빚은 동방의 독한 술."], ["A few remained to arrange the interior for the sheik; and of these the men-servants hung a curtain to the central row of pillars, making two apartments; the one on the right sacred to Ilderim himself, the other sacred to his horses—his jewels of Solomon—which they led in, and with kisses and love-taps set at liberty.", "그러자 외치며 많은 이들이 저마다 제 거처를 세우러 흩어져 갔다. 몇 사람은 남아 족장을 위해 안을 정돈했다. 그중 사내종들은 가운데 줄의 기둥에 휘장을 드리워 두 칸을 만들었으니, 오른쪽은 일더림 자신을 위한 신성한 곳이요, 다른 한쪽은 그의 말들을 위한 신성한 곳이었다."], ["Against the middle pillar they then erected the arms-rack, and filled it with javelins and spears, and bows, arrows, and shields; outside of them hanging the master’s sword, modelled after the new moon; and the glitter of its blade rivalled the glitter of the jewels bedded in its grip.", "그 말들, 곧 그의 솔로몬의 보석들을 끌고 들어와, 입 맞추고 사랑스럽게 토닥이며 풀어 놓았다. 그런 다음 가운데 기둥에 무기걸이를 세우고, 투창과 창과 활과 화살과 방패로 채웠다. 그 바깥에는 초승달을 본떠 만든 주인의 칼을 걸었는데, 그 칼날의 번득임은 손잡이에 박힌 보석의 번득임과 어금지금했다."], ["Upon one end of the rack they hung the housings of the horses, gay some of them as the livery of a king’s servant, while on the other end they displayed the great man’s wearing apparel—his robes woollen and robes linen, his tunics and trousers, and many colored kerchiefs for the head.", "걸이 한쪽 끝에는 말의 안장 덮개를 걸었으니, 그중 더러는 임금의 종이 입는 제복처럼 화려했고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 이 위대한 인물의 의복을 펼쳐 놓았으니, 모직 겉옷과 아마포 겉옷, 그의 윗옷과 바지, 그리고 머리에 두르는 갖가지 빛깔의 두건이었다."], ["Nor did they give over the work until he pronounced it well.", "그리고 그가 잘되었다고 말하기 전까지는 일손을 멈추지 않았다."]], [["Meantime the women drew out and set up the divan, more indispensable to him than the beard down-flowing over his breast, white as Aaron’s.", "그동안 여인들은 디반을 꺼내어 차렸으니, 이는 그에게 가슴 위로 흘러내린, 아론의 것처럼 새하얀 수염보다 더 없어서는 안 될 물건이었다."], ["They put a frame together in shape of three sides of a square, the opening to the door, and covered it with cushions and base curtains, and the cushions with a changeable spread striped brown and yellow; at the corners they placed pillows and bolsters sacked in cloth blue and crimson; then around the divan they laid a margin of carpet, and the inner space they carpeted as well; and when the carpet was carried from the opening of the divan to the door of the tent, their work was done; whereupon they again waited until the master said it was good.", "그들은 정사각형의 세 변 모양으로 틀을 짜 맞추되 그 트인 쪽을 문으로 향하게 하고, 그 위를 방석과 늘어뜨린 휘장으로 덮었으며, 방석은 갈색과 노란색 줄무늬의 빛깔 변하는 깔개로 덮었다. 모퉁이에는 파란색과 진홍색 천에 채워 넣은 베개와 받침을 놓았다. 그런 다음 디반 둘레에 양탄자로 가장자리를 깔고, 안쪽 공간에도 양탄자를 깔았다."], ["Nothing remained then but to bring and fill the jars with water, and hang the skin bottles of arrack ready for the hand—to-morrow the leben.", "그리고 양탄자가 디반의 트인 쪽에서 천막의 문까지 이어지자, 그들의 일은 끝났다. 그러자 그들은 또다시 주인이 잘되었다고 말하기를 기다렸다."], ["Nor might an Arab see why Ilderim should not be both happy and generous—in his tent by the lake of sweet waters, under the palms of the Orchard of Palms.", "그러고 나서는 항아리를 가져와 물로 채우고, 아라크가 든 가죽 부대를 손 닿는 곳에 걸어 두는 일만 남았으니—레벤은 내일이었다. 단물 호숫가, 종려 과수원의 종려나무 아래 제 천막에서 일더림이 어찌 행복하고 너그럽지 않을 수 있겠는지, 아랍인이라면 그 까닭을 알 길이 없을 것이다. * 레벤: 응유로 만든 아랍의 발효 우유."]], [["Such was the tent at the door of which we left Ben-Hur.", "우리가 벤허를 남겨 두고 떠난 그 문 앞의 천막이 바로 이러했다."]], [["Servants were already waiting the master’s direction.", "하인들은 이미 주인의 분부를 기다리고 있었다."], ["One of them took off his sandals; another unlatched Ben-Hur’s Roman shoes; then the two exchanged their dusty outer garments for fresh ones of white linen.", "한 사람은 그의 샌들을 벗기고, 다른 한 사람은 벤허의 로마식 신발 끈을 풀었다. 그런 다음 두 사람은 먼지투성이 겉옷을 벗고 산뜻한 흰 아마포 옷으로 갈아입었다."]], [["“Enter—in God’s name, enter, and take thy rest,”", "\"드시오—하느님의 이름으로, 들어와 쉬시오.\""], ["said the host, heartily, in the dialect of the Market-place of Jerusalem; forthwith he led the way to the divan.", "주인은 예루살렘 장터의 사투리로 진심을 담아 말하고는, 곧 디반으로 앞장서 갔다."]], [["“I will sit here,”", "\"나는 여기 앉겠소.\""], ["he said next, pointing; “and there the stranger.”", "그가 다음으로 가리키며 말했다. \"그리고 손님은 거기에.\""]], [["A woman—in the old time she would have been called a handmaid—answered, and dexterously piled the pillows and bolsters as rests for the back; after which they sat upon the side of the divan, while water was brought fresh from the lake, and their feet bathed and dried with napkins.", "옛날 같으면 시녀라 불렸을 한 여인이 그 말에 응하여, 등을 기댈 수 있도록 베개와 받침을 솜씨 좋게 쌓았다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 디반 가장자리에 앉았고, 그동안 호수에서 갓 길어 온 물로 그들의 발을 씻겨 수건으로 닦아 주었다."]], [["“We have a saying in the Desert,”", "\"우리 사막에 이런 말이 있소.\""], ["Ilderim began, gathering his beard, and combing it with his slender fingers, “that a good appetite is the promise of a long life.", "일더림이 수염을 그러모아 가느다란 손가락으로 빗질하며 말문을 열었다. \"왕성한 식욕은 장수의 약속이라고."], ["Hast thou such?”", "그대는 그런 식욕이 있소?\""]], [["“By that rule, good sheik, I will live a hundred years.", "\"그 법칙대로라면, 어진 족장이여, 나는 백 년은 살겠습니다."], ["I am a hungry wolf at thy door,”", "나는 그대의 문 앞에 선 굶주린 늑대입니다.\""], ["Ben-Hur replied.", "벤허가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, thou shalt not be sent away like a wolf.", "\"그렇다면 그대를 늑대처럼 쫓아 보내지는 않으리다."], ["I will give thee the best of the flocks.”", "내 가축 중에 가장 좋은 것을 그대에게 주겠소.\""]], [["Ilderim clapped his hands.", "일더림이 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["“Seek the stranger in the guest-tent, and say I, Ilderim, send him a prayer that his peace may be as incessant as the flowing of waters.”", "\"손님 천막에 있는 그분을 찾아가, 나 일더림이 그분의 평안이 흐르는 물처럼 끊이지 않기를 비는 기도를 보낸다고 전하라.\""]], [["The man in waiting bowed.", "대기하던 사내가 절을 했다."]], [["“Say, also,”", "\"또 이렇게 전하라.\""], ["Ilderim continued, “that I have returned with another for breaking of bread; and, if Balthasar the wise careth to share the loaf, three may partake of it, and the portion of the birds be none the less.”", "일더림이 말을 이었다. \"내가 빵을 떼어 나눌 또 한 사람과 함께 돌아왔으니, 지혜로운 발타사르께서 빵을 나누고자 하신다면 셋이 함께할 수 있으며, 새들에게 돌아갈 몫도 조금도 줄지 않을 것이라고.\""]], [["The second servant went away.", "두 번째 하인이 물러갔다."]], [["“Let us take our rest now.”", "\"이제 우리도 쉽시다.\""]], [["Thereupon Ilderim settled himself upon the divan, as at this day merchants sit on their rugs in the bazaars of Damascus; and when fairly at rest, he stopped combing his beard, and said, gravely, “That thou art my guest, and hast drunk my leben, and art about to taste my salt, ought not to forbid a question: Who art thou?”", "그러고 나서 일더림은 디반 위에 자리를 잡았으니, 오늘날 다마스쿠스의 시장에서 상인들이 깔개 위에 앉는 모양 그대로였다. 그리고 제대로 편안해지자, 그는 수염 빗질을 멈추고 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그대가 내 손님이며, 내 레벤을 마셨고, 이제 내 소금을 맛보려 한다 하여, 한 가지 물음마저 막을 까닭은 없을 것이오. 그대는 누구요?\""]], [["“Sheik Ilderim,”", "\"일더림 족장이여,\""], ["said Ben-Hur, calmly enduring his gaze, “I pray thee not to think me trifling with thy just demand; but was there never a time in thy life when to answer such a question would have been a crime to thyself?”", "벤허는 그의 눈길을 차분히 견디며 말했다. \"그대의 정당한 요구를 제가 가벼이 여긴다고는 생각지 말아 주십시오. 그러나 그대의 삶에도, 그런 물음에 답하는 것이 자기 자신에게 죄가 되었을 때가 한 번도 없었습니까?\""]], [["“By the splendor of Solomon, yes!”", "\"솔로몬의 영광을 두고 맹세컨대, 있었소!\""], ["Ilderim answered.", "일더림이 대답했다."], ["“Betrayal of self is at times as base as the betrayal of a tribe.”", "\"자기 자신을 저버리는 것은 때로 부족을 저버리는 것만큼이나 비열한 일이지.\""]], [["“Thanks, thanks, good sheik!” Ben-Hur exclaimed.", "\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다, 어진 족장이여!\" 벤허가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Never answer became thee better.", "\"그보다 그대에게 더 잘 어울리는 대답은 없었습니다."], ["Now I know thou dost but seek assurance to justify the trust I have come to ask, and that such assurance is of more interest to thee than the affairs of my poor life.”", "이제 저는 압니다. 그대는 제가 청하러 온 신뢰를 정당화할 확신을 구할 뿐이며, 그런 확신이 제 보잘것없는 삶의 사정보다 그대에게 더 관심거리라는 것을.\""]], [["The sheik in his turn bowed, and Ben-Hur hastened to pursue his advantage.", "이번에는 족장이 고개를 숙였고, 벤허는 서둘러 그 기세를 몰아갔다."]], [["“So it please thee then,”", "\"그러면, 괜찮으시다면,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “first, I am not a Roman, as the name given thee as mine implieth.”", "\"먼저, 저는 그대에게 제 이름으로 전해진 그 이름이 뜻하는 바와 달리 로마인이 아닙니다.\""]], [["Ilderim clasped the beard overflowing his breast, and gazed at the speaker with eyes faintly twinkling through the shade of the heavy close-drawn brows.", "일더림은 가슴 위로 흘러넘치는 수염을 움켜쥐고, 짙게 맞붙인 눈썹 그늘 사이로 희미하게 반짝이는 눈으로 말하는 이를 바라보았다."]], [["“In the next place,”", "\"다음으로,\""], ["Ben-Hur continued, “I am an Israelite of the tribe of Judah.”", "벤허가 말을 이었다. \"저는 유다 지파의 이스라엘 사람입니다.\""]], [["The sheik raised his brows a little.", "족장이 눈썹을 조금 치켜올렸다."]], [["“Nor that merely.", "\"그뿐이 아닙니다."], ["Sheik, I am a Jew with a grievance against Rome compared with which thine is not more than a child’s trouble.”", "족장이여, 저는 로마에 원한을 품은 유대인이니, 그대의 원한 따위는 그것에 견주면 아이의 투정에 지나지 않습니다.\""]], [["The old man combed his beard with nervous haste, and let fall his brows until even the twinkle of the eyes went out.", "노인은 초조한 손길로 수염을 빗다가, 눈의 반짝임마저 사라질 때까지 눈썹을 내리깔았다."]], [["“Still further: I swear to thee, Sheik Ilderim—I swear by the covenant the Lord made with my fathers—so thou but give me the revenge I seek, the money and the glory of the race shall be thine.”", "\"더 나아가, 저는 그대에게 맹세합니다, 일더림 족장이여—주께서 제 조상들과 맺으신 언약을 두고 맹세하건대—그대가 제게 제가 구하는 복수만 허락해 주신다면, 그 종족의 돈과 영광은 그대의 것이 될 것입니다.\""]], [["Ilderim’s brows relaxed; his head arose; his face began to beam; and it was almost possible to see the satisfaction taking possession of him.", "일더림의 눈썹이 풀렸다. 그의 고개가 들렸다. 그의 얼굴이 빛나기 시작했고, 만족이 그를 사로잡아 가는 모습이 거의 눈에 보일 지경이었다."]], [["“Enough!”", "\"되었소!\""], ["he said. “If at the roots of thy tongue there is a lie in coil, Solomon himself had not been safe against thee.", "그가 말했다. \"그대의 혀뿌리에 거짓이 똬리를 틀고 있다면, 솔로몬 자신도 그대 앞에서는 무사하지 못했을 것이오."], ["That thou art not a Roman—that as a Jew thou hast a grievance against Rome, and revenge to compass, I believe; and on that score enough.", "그대가 로마인이 아니라는 것, 유대인으로서 로마에 원한을 품고 갚아야 할 복수가 있다는 것을 나는 믿소. 그 점에 대해서는 그만하면 되었소."], ["But as to thy skill. What experience hast thou in racing with chariots?", "그러나 그대의 솜씨로 말하면. 그대는 전차 경주에 어떤 경험이 있소?"], ["And the horses—canst thou make them creatures of thy will?—to know thee?", "그리고 말들—그대는 그것들을 그대 뜻대로 부릴 수 있소?—그대를 알아보게 할 수 있소?"], ["to come at call? to go, if thou sayest it, to the last extreme of breath and strength?", "부르면 오게 하고, 그대가 말하면 숨과 힘이 다하는 마지막 끝까지 달리게 할 수 있소?"], ["and then, in the perishing moment, out of the depths of thy life thrill them to one exertion the mightiest of all?", "그리고 사그라드는 그 순간, 그대 생명의 밑바닥에서 그것들을 떨치게 하여 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 힘찬 한 번의 분발로 이끌 수 있소? 그 재능은, 내 아들이여, 누구에게나 주어지는 것이 아니오. 아, 하느님의 영광을 두고!"], ["The gift, my son, is not to every one. Ah, by the splendor of God!", "나는 수백만 백성을 다스리며 그들의 완벽한 주인이었으나 말 한 마리의 존경은 얻지 못한 임금을 알고 있소. 새겨들으시오!"], ["I knew a king who governed millions of men, their perfect master, but could not win the respect of a horse. Mark!", "나는 종을 위해 종노릇하는 게 일과인 둔한 짐승들—피와 모습이 천하고 정신이 죽은 것들을 말하는 게 아니오. 여기 내 것들 같은 것들—제 종족의 왕들이며, 첫 파라오의 무리에까지 거슬러 올라가는 혈통을 지닌 것들을 말하는 거요."], ["I speak not of the dull brutes whose round it is to slave for slaves—the debased in blood and image—the dead in spirit; but of such as mine here—the kings of their kind; of a lineage reaching back to the broods of the first Pharaoh; my comrades and friends, dwellers in tents, whom long association with me has brought up to my plane; who to their instincts have added our wits and to their senses joined our souls, until they feel all we know of ambition, love, hate, and contempt; in war, heroes; in trust, faithful as women.", "그것들은 내 동지요 벗이며, 천막에서 함께 사는 것들이니, 나와 오래 어울리며 내 경지에까지 끌어올려졌소. 그것들은 제 본능에 우리의 슬기를 보태고 제 감각에 우리의 혼을 이어, 마침내 우리가 아는 모든 야망과 사랑과 미움과 경멸을 느끼게 되었소. 전쟁에서는 영웅이요, 믿고 맡기면 여인처럼 충직하오."], ["Ho, there!”", "어이, 거기!\""]], [["A servant came forward.", "하인 하나가 앞으로 나왔다."]], [["“Let my Arabs come!”", "\"내 아랍 말들을 들이라!\""]], [["The man drew aside part of the division curtain of the tent, exposing to view a group of horses, who lingered a moment where they were as if to make certain of the invitation.", "사내가 천막을 가르는 휘장의 한쪽을 젖히자, 한 무리의 말이 드러났다. 말들은 마치 그 초대가 분명한지 확인하려는 듯 잠시 제자리에 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"오너라!\""], ["Ilderim said to them.", "일더림이 그들에게 말했다."], ["“Why stand ye there?", "\"왜 거기 서 있느냐?"], ["What have I that is not yours? Come, I say!”", "내가 가진 것 가운데 너희 것이 아닌 게 무엇이 있느냐?"], ["They stalked slowly in.", "오너라, 어서!\""]], [["“Son of Israel,”", "그것들은 천천히 위풍 있게 걸어 들어왔다."], ["the master said, “thy Moses was a mighty man, but—ha, ha ha!—I must laugh when I think of his allowing thy fathers the plodding ox and the dull, slow-natured ass, and forbidding them property in horses.", "\"이스라엘의 아들이여,\" 주인이 말했다. \"그대의 모세는 위대한 인물이었으나—하, 하, 하!—그가 그대의 조상들에게 터벅터벅 걷는 소와 둔하고 느려 빠진 나귀는 허락하면서 말은 가지지 못하게 한 것을 생각하면 나는 웃지 않을 수 없소."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["Thinkest thou he would have done so had he seen that one—and that—and this?”", "만일 그가 저것—그리고 저것—그리고 이것을 보았더라도 그리했으리라고 그대는 생각하오?\""], ["At the word he laid his hand upon the face of the first to reach him, and patted it with infinite pride and tenderness.", "이 말과 함께 그는 제게 가장 먼저 다가온 말의 얼굴에 손을 얹고, 더없는 자랑과 다정함으로 토닥였다."]], [["“It is a misjudgment, sheik, a misjudgment,”", "\"그것은 그릇된 판단입니다, 족장이여, 그릇된 판단입니다.\""], ["Ben-Hur said, warmly.", "벤허가 뜨겁게 말했다."], ["“Moses was a warrior as well as a lawgiver beloved by God; and to follow war—ah, what is it but to love all its creatures—these among the rest?”", "\"모세는 하느님의 사랑을 받은 율법 수여자였을 뿐 아니라 전사이기도 했습니다. 전쟁을 따른다는 것이—아, 그것이 그 모든 피조물을, 이것들도 그 가운데 하나로 사랑하는 것 말고 무엇이겠습니까?\""]], [["A head of exquisite turn—with large eyes, soft as a deer’s, and half hidden by the dense forelock, and small ears, sharp-pointed and sloped well forward—approached then quite to his breast, the nostrils open, and the upper lip in motion.", "그러자 더없이 빼어나게 생긴 머리 하나가—사슴처럼 부드럽고 큰 눈에, 짙은 앞갈기에 반쯤 가렸으며, 작고 뾰족하게 솟아 앞으로 잘 기울어진 귀를 가진 머리가—그의 가슴께까지 바짝 다가왔으니, 콧구멍을 벌리고 윗입술을 달싹였다."], ["“Who are you?”", "\"당신은 누구요?\""], ["it asked, plainly as ever man spoke.", "그것은 사람이 말하듯 또렷이 물었다."], ["Ben-Hur recognized one of the four racers he had seen on the course, and gave his open hand to the beautiful brute.", "벤허는 그것이 경주로에서 본 네 마리 경주마 가운데 하나임을 알아보고, 그 아름다운 짐승에게 펼친 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“They will tell you, the blasphemers!—may their days shorten as they grow fewer!”—the sheik spoke with the feeling of a man repelling a personal defamation—“they will tell you, I say, that our horses of the best blood are derived from the Nesaean pastures of Persia.", "\"그들은 그대에게 말할 거요, 그 신성모독자들이!—날이 줄어들수록 그 목숨도 짧아지기를!\"—족장은 제 자신에 대한 명예훼손을 물리치는 사람의 감정으로 말했다—\"그자들은 그대에게, 내 말하건대, 우리의 가장 좋은 혈통의 말들이 페르시아의 네사이아 목초지에서 비롯되었다고 말할 거요."], ["God gave the first Arab a measureless waste of sand, with some treeless mountains, and here and there a well of bitter waters; and said to him, ‘Behold thy country!’ And when the poor man complained, the Mighty One pitied him, and said again, ‘Be of cheer!", "하느님께서는 첫 아랍인에게 끝없는 모래 황무지와 나무 한 그루 없는 산 몇 개, 여기저기 쓴물 솟는 우물을 주시고는 그에게 말씀하셨소. '보라, 네 나라다!' 그리고 그 가엾은 자가 푸념하자, 전능하신 분께서 그를 가엾이 여겨 다시 말씀하셨소."], ["for I will twice bless thee above other men.’ The Arab heard, and gave thanks, and with faith set out to find the blessings.", "'기운을 내어라! 내가 너를 다른 사람들보다 두 배로 복되게 하리라.' 아랍인은 그 말씀을 듣고 감사드린 뒤, 믿음을 품고 그 복을 찾아 떠났소."], ["He travelled all the boundaries first, and failed; then he made a path into the desert, and went on and on—and in the heart of the waste there was an island of green very beautiful to see; and in the heart of the island, lo!", "그는 먼저 모든 변두리를 두루 다녔으나 헛수고였소. 그러자 사막으로 길을 내어 가고 또 가니—그 황무지 한가운데에 보기에 더없이 아름다운 푸른 섬이 있었고, 그 섬 한가운데에, 보라!"], ["a herd of camels, and another of horses!", "한 무리의 낙타와 또 한 무리의 말이 있었소!"], ["He took them joyfully and kept them with care for what they were—best gifts of God.", "그는 그것들을 기쁘게 거두어, 하느님의 가장 좋은 선물답게 정성껏 길렀소."], ["And from that green isle went forth all the horses of the earth; even to the pastures of Nesaea they went; and northward to the dreadful vales perpetually threshed by blasts from the Sea of Chill Winds.", "그리고 그 푸른 섬에서 땅 위의 모든 말이 나갔으니, 네사이아의 목초지까지 갔고, 북쪽으로는 차디찬 바람의 바다에서 불어오는 돌풍에 끊임없이 두들겨 맞는 무서운 골짜기까지 갔소. 이 이야기를 의심하지 마시오."], ["Doubt not the story; or if thou dost, may never amulet have charm for an Arab again. Nay, I will give thee proof.”", "만일 의심한다면, 다시는 어떤 부적도 아랍인에게 효험을 부리지 못하기를. 아니, 내가 그대에게 증거를 보여 주리다.\""]], [["He clapped his hands.", "그가 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["“Bring me the records of the tribe,”", "\"부족의 기록을 가져오너라.\""], ["he said to the servant who responded.", "그는 응답한 하인에게 말했다."]], [["While waiting, the sheik played with the horses, patting their cheeks, combing their forelocks with his fingers, giving each one a token of remembrance.", "기다리는 동안 족장은 말들과 어울리며, 그것들의 뺨을 토닥이고 손가락으로 앞갈기를 빗기며, 저마다에게 기억의 표시를 주었다."], ["Presently six men appeared with chests of cedar reinforced by bands of brass, and hinged and bolted with brass.", "이윽고 여섯 사람이 놋쇠 띠로 보강하고 놋쇠 돌쩌귀와 빗장을 단 삼나무 궤를 들고 나타났다."]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Ilderim, when they were all set down by the divan, “I meant not all of them; only the records of the horses—that one.", "그것들이 모두 디반 곁에 놓이자 일더림이 말했다. \"전부를 말한 게 아니었다. 말들의 기록만, 저것 하나만."], ["Open it and take back the others.”", "그것을 열고 나머지는 도로 가져가라.\""]], [["The chest was opened, disclosing a mass of ivory tablets strung on rings of silver wire; and as the tablets were scarcely thicker than wafers, each ring held several hundreds of them.", "궤가 열리자, 은실 고리에 꿴 한 무더기의 상아 판이 드러났다. 판은 살갗처럼 얇아서, 고리 하나마다 수백 개씩 꿰여 있었다."]], [["“I know,”", "\"나는 알고 있소.\""], ["said Ilderim, taking some of the rings in his hand—“I know with what care and zeal, my son, the scribes of the Temple in the Holy City keep the names of the newly born, that every son of Israel may trace his line of ancestry to its beginning, though it antedate the patriarchs.", "일더림이 고리 몇 개를 손에 쥐며 말했다. \"내 아들이여, 거룩한 도성 성전의 서기들이 얼마나 정성과 열의를 다해 갓 태어난 이들의 이름을 기록하는지를. 그리하여 이스라엘의 모든 아들은, 비록 가부장들보다 앞선 시대라 하더라도, 제 혈통을 그 시작까지 거슬러 짚을 수 있소."], ["My fathers—may the recollection of them be green forever!—did not think it sinful to borrow the idea, and apply it to their dumb servants.", "내 조상들은—그분들에 대한 기억이 영원히 푸르기를!—그 발상을 빌려 말 못 하는 자기네 종들에게 적용하는 것을 죄로 여기지 않았소."], ["See these tablets!”", "이 판들을 보시오!\""]], [["Ben-Hur took the rings, and separating the tablets saw they bore rude hieroglyphs in Arabic, burned on the smooth surface by a sharp point of heated metal.", "벤허가 고리를 받아 판들을 떼어 보니, 거기에는 달군 쇠끝으로 매끄러운 표면에 지져 새긴 거친 아랍 문자가 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Canst thou read them, O son of Israel?”", "\"그대는 이것을 읽을 수 있소, 오 이스라엘의 아들이여?\""]], [["“No. Thou must tell me their meaning.”", "\"아니요. 그 뜻을 제게 말씀해 주셔야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Know thou, then, each tablet records the name of a foal of the pure blood born to my fathers through the hundreds of years passed; and also the names of sire and dam.", "\"그러면 알아 두시오. 이 판은 저마다, 지나온 수백 년 동안 내 조상들에게 태어난 순혈종 망아지의 이름을 기록한 것이며, 그 아비와 어미의 이름도 함께 적혀 있소."], ["Take them, and note their age, that thou mayst the more readily believe.”", "그것들을 받아 그 나이를 살펴보시오. 그러면 그대가 더 쉽게 믿게 될 것이오.\""]], [["Some of the tablets were nearly worn away.", "판들 가운데 더러는 거의 닳아 없어진 것이었다."], ["All were yellow with age.", "모두가 세월에 누렇게 바래 있었다."]], [["“In the chest there, I can tell thee now, I have the perfect history; perfect because certified as history seldom is—showing of what stock all these are sprung—this one, and that now supplicating thy notice and caress; and as they come to us here, their sires, even the furthest removed in time, came to my sires, under a tent-roof like this of mine, to eat their measure of barley from the open hand, and be talked to as children; and as children kiss the thanks they have not speech to express.", "\"저기 저 궤 안에는, 이제 그대에게 말해 줄 수 있거니와, 완벽한 내력이 들어 있소. 역사가 좀처럼 그러하지 못할 만큼 확실히 입증되었기에 완벽한 것이니—이것들이 모두 어떤 혈통에서 솟아났는지를—이것이, 그리고 지금 그대의 눈길과 손길을 청하는 저것이 말이오. 그리고 그것들이 여기 우리에게 오듯, 시간상 가장 멀리 떨어진 것까지도 그 아비들이 내 아비들에게, 이 내 천막 같은 천막 지붕 아래로 와서, 펼친 손에서 제 몫의 보리를 먹고, 자식인 양 말 건넴을 받았으며, 자식이 그러하듯 말로 다하지 못하는 고마움에 입맞춤을 했소."], ["And now, O son of Israel, thou mayst believe my declaration—if I am a lord of the Desert, behold my ministers!", "그러니 이제, 오 이스라엘의 아들이여, 그대는 내 선언을 믿어도 좋소—내가 사막의 주인이라면, 보라, 여기 내 신하들이오!"], ["Take them from me, and I become as a sick man left by the caravan to die.", "그것들을 내게서 앗아 가면, 나는 대상에게 버림받아 죽어 가는 병자와 같아지오."], ["Thanks to them, age hath not diminished the terror of me on the highways between cities; and it will not while I have strength to go with them.", "그것들 덕분에 나이를 먹어도 도시와 도시를 잇는 길에서 내가 일으키는 두려움이 줄지 않았으며, 내가 그것들과 함께 갈 힘이 있는 한 줄지 않을 것이오."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["I could tell thee marvels done by their ancestors.", "그것들 조상이 해낸 놀라운 일들을 그대에게 들려줄 수도 있소."], ["In a favoring time I may do so; for the present, enough that they were never overtaken in retreat; nor, by the sword of Solomon, did they ever fail in pursuit!", "때가 좋으면 그리하리다. 지금으로서는, 그것들이 퇴각하다 따라잡힌 적이 없으며, 솔로몬의 칼을 두고, 추격에 실패한 적도 없다는 것만으로 충분하오!"], ["That, mark you, on the sands and under saddle; but now—I do not know—I am afraid, for they are under yoke the first time, and the conditions of success are so many.", "새겨 두시오, 그것은 모래 위에서, 안장을 얹고서 한 일이오. 그러나 이제—나는 잘 모르겠소—두렵소. 그것들이 처음으로 멍에를 메는 것이며, 성공의 조건이 너무도 많기 때문이오."], ["They have the pride and the speed and the endurance. If I find them a master, they will win.", "그것들에게는 긍지와 빠름과 끈기가 있소. 내가 그것들에게 주인을 찾아 준다면, 그것들은 이기리다."], ["Son of Israel!", "이스라엘의 아들이여!"], ["so thou art the man, I swear it shall be a happy day that brought thee thither. Of thyself now speak.”", "그러니 그대가 그 사람이라면, 맹세컨대 그대를 이곳으로 데려온 날은 복된 날이 될 것이오. 이제 그대 자신에 대해 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“I know now,”", "\"이제 알겠습니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, “why it is that in the love of an Arab his horse is next to his children; and I know, also, why the Arab horses are the best in the world; but, good sheik, I would not have you judge me by words alone; for, as you know, all promises of men sometimes fail.", "벤허가 말했다. \"어찌하여 아랍인의 사랑에서 그의 말이 자식 다음의 자리를 차지하는지를. 그리고 어찌하여 아랍 말이 세상에서 가장 뛰어난지도 알겠습니다."], ["Give me the trial first on some plain hereabout, and put the four in my hand to-morrow.”", "그러나 어진 족장이여, 말만으로 저를 판단하지는 말아 주십시오. 아시다시피 사람의 모든 약속이란 때로 어긋나기도 하니까요. 먼저 이 근처 어느 평지에서 제게 시험을 허락하시고, 내일 그 네 마리를 제 손에 맡겨 주십시오.\""]], [["Ilderim’s face beamed again, and he would have spoken.", "일더림의 얼굴이 다시 빛났고, 그가 막 입을 열려 했다."]], [["“A moment, good sheik, a moment!”", "\"잠깐, 어진 족장이여, 잠깐만!\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“Let me say further.", "\"한마디 더 하게 해 주십시오."], ["From the masters in Rome I learned many lessons, little thinking they would serve me in a time like this.", "저는 로마의 스승들에게서 많은 가르침을 배웠으나, 그것이 이런 때에 제게 도움이 되리라고는 거의 생각지 못했습니다."], ["I tell thee these thy sons of the Desert, though they have separately the speed of eagles and the endurance of lions, will fail if they are not trained to run together under the yoke.", "말씀드리건대, 사막의 이 아들들은 비록 따로따로는 독수리의 빠름과 사자의 끈기를 지녔다 하더라도, 멍에를 메고 함께 달리도록 훈련받지 못하면 실패할 것입니다."], ["For bethink thee, sheik, in every four there is one the slowest and one the swiftest; and while the race is always to the slowest, the trouble is always with the swiftest.", "생각해 보십시오, 족장이여, 네 마리마다 가장 느린 한 마리와 가장 빠른 한 마리가 있게 마련입니다. 그리고 경주는 언제나 가장 느린 놈에 좌우되지만, 말썽은 언제나 가장 빠른 놈에게서 비롯됩니다."], ["It was so to-day; the driver could not reduce the best to harmonious action with the poorest.", "오늘이 그러했습니다. 마부는 가장 좋은 놈을 가장 못한 놈과 조화롭게 움직이도록 누그러뜨리지 못했습니다."], ["My trial may have no better result; but if so, I will tell thee of it: that I swear.", "제 시험도 더 나은 결과를 내지 못할지 모릅니다. 그러나 만일 그렇다면, 저는 그대에게 그 사실을 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Wherefore, in the same spirit I say, can I get them to run together, moved by my will, the four as one, thou shalt have the sestertii and the crown, and I my revenge.", "그것을 맹세합니다. 그러므로 같은 마음으로 말씀드리거니와, 제가 그것들을 제 뜻에 따라 움직여 네 마리를 하나처럼 함께 달리게 할 수 있다면, 그대는 그 세스테르티우스와 영예를 얻고, 저는 제 복수를 얻을 것입니다."], ["What sayest thou?”", "어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["Ilderim listened, combing his beard the while.", "* 세스테르티우스: 고대 로마의 화폐 단위. 일더림은 그동안 수염을 빗으며 귀를 기울였다."], ["At the end he said, with a laugh, “I think better of thee, son of Israel.", "끝에 가서 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"그대를 더 좋게 보게 되었소, 이스라엘의 아들이여."], ["We have a saying in the Desert, ‘If you will cook the meal with words, I will promise an ocean of butter.’ Thou shalt have the horses in the morning.”", "우리 사막에 이런 말이 있소. '그대가 말로 식사를 짓겠다면, 나는 버터 한 바다를 약속하리라.' 아침이면 그대는 그 말들을 얻을 것이오.\""]], [["At that moment there was a stir at the rear entrance to the tent.", "그 순간 천막 뒤쪽 입구에 인기척이 일었다."]], [["“The supper—it is here!", "\"저녁이오—여기 왔구려!"], ["and yonder my friend Balthasar, whom thou shalt know.", "그리고 저기 내 벗 발타사르, 그대가 알게 될 분이오."], ["He hath a story to tell which an Israelite should never tire of hearing.”", "그분에게는 이스라엘 사람이라면 결코 듣기에 싫증 내지 않을 이야기가 있소.\""]], [["And to the servants he added,", "그리고 그는 하인들에게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Take the records away, and return my jewels to their apartment.”", "\"기록을 치우고, 내 보석들을 그 처소로 돌려보내라.\""]], [["And they did as he ordered.", "그러자 그들은 분부대로 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV", "# CHAPTER XIV"]], [["If the reader will return now to the repast of the wise men at their meeting in the desert, he will understand the preparations for the supper in Ilderim’s tent.", "이제 독자가 사막에서 만난 현자들의 식사 장면으로 되돌아간다면, 일데림의 천막에서 차려진 저녁상의 준비가 어떠했는지 이해하게 될 것이다."], ["The differences were chiefly such as were incident to ampler means and better service.", "그 차이라야 주로 더 넉넉한 재력과 더 나은 시중에서 비롯된 것뿐이었다."]], [["Three rugs were spread on the carpet within the space so nearly enclosed by the divan; a table not more than a foot in height was brought and set within the same place, and covered with a cloth.", "디반이 거의 둘러싼 공간 안쪽 양탄자 위에 깔개 세 장이 펼쳐졌다. 높이가 한 자도 채 안 되는 식탁 하나가 운반되어 같은 자리에 놓이고, 그 위에 보가 덮였다."], ["Off to one side a portable earthenware oven was established under the presidency of a woman whose duty it was to keep the company in bread, or, more precisely, in hot cakes of flour from the handmills grinding with constant sound in a neighboring tent.", "한쪽으로는 들고 다닐 수 있는 흙 화덕이 자리 잡았는데, 그것을 맡은 여인의 임무는 일행에게 빵을, 더 정확히 말하면 가까운 천막에서 끊임없이 소리를 내며 돌아가는 맷돌로 빻은 밀가루로 구운 뜨거운 떡을 끊이지 않게 대는 일이었다."]], [["Meanwhile Balthasar was conducted to the divan, where Ilderim and Ben-Hur received him standing.", "그러는 사이 발타사르가 디반으로 인도되었고, 일데림과 벤허는 일어서서 그를 맞이했다."], ["A loose black gown covered his person; his step was feeble, and his whole movement slow and cautious, apparently dependent upon a long staff and the arm of a servant.", "헐렁한 검은 가운이 그의 몸을 감싸고 있었다. 그의 걸음은 힘이 없었고, 온몸의 움직임은 느리고 조심스러웠으니, 보아하니 긴 지팡이와 하인의 팔에 기대고 있었다."]], [["“Peace to you, my friend,” said Ilderim, respectfully.", "\"그대에게 평화가 있기를, 나의 벗이여.\" 일데림이 정중하게 말했다."], ["“Peace and welcome.”", "\"평화와 함께 그대를 환영하오.\""]], [["The Egyptian raised his head and replied, “And to thee, good sheik—to thee and thine, peace and the blessing of the One God—God the true and loving.”", "이집트인은 고개를 들고 대답했다. \"그대에게도, 어진 족장이여, 그대와 그대의 식구들에게도 평화와 유일하신 하느님의 축복이 있기를. 참되고 사랑이 넘치는 하느님의 축복이.\""]], [["The manner was gentle and devout, and impressed Ben-Hur with a feeling of awe; besides which the blessing included in the answering salutation had been partly addressed to him, and while that part was being spoken, the eyes of the aged guest, hollow yet luminous, rested upon his face long enough to stir an emotion new and mysterious, and so strong that he again and again during the repast scanned the much wrinkled and bloodless face for its meaning; but always there was the expression bland, placid, and trustful as a child’s.", "그 태도는 부드럽고 경건하여 벤허에게 외경의 감정을 불러일으켰다. 게다가 화답하는 인사 속에 담긴 축복이 일부는 벤허 자신에게 향한 것이었으니, 그 부분이 발음되는 동안 노쇠한 손님의 움푹 꺼졌으되 형형한 두 눈이 그의 얼굴 위에 머물렀고, 그 머무름은 새롭고도 신비로운 감정을 일깨우기에 충분히 길었다. 그 감정이 어찌나 강했던지, 그는 식사 내내 거듭하여 주름이 깊이 패고 핏기 없는 그 얼굴을 살피며 그 의미를 헤아리려 했다."], ["A little later he found that expression habitual.", "그러나 그 표정은 언제나 어린아이처럼 온화하고 평온하며 미더웠다. 조금 뒤에 그는 그것이 늘 그 얼굴에 배어 있는 표정임을 알게 되었다."]], [["“This is he, O Balthasar,”", "\"오 발타사르여, 바로 이 사람이오.\""], ["said the sheik, laying his hand on Ben-Hur’s arm, “who will break bread with us this evening.”", "족장이 벤허의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"오늘 저녁 우리와 함께 빵을 뗄 사람이지요.\""]], [["The Egyptian glanced at the young man, and looked again surprised and doubting; seeing which the sheik continued, “I have promised him my horses for trial to-morrow; and if all goes well, he will drive them in the Circus.”", "이집트인은 청년을 흘낏 보더니, 다시 놀라움과 의심에 찬 눈길로 그를 바라보았다. 그것을 본 족장이 말을 이었다. \"내일 시험 삼아 내 말들을 그에게 맡기기로 약속했소. 모든 일이 잘 풀리면, 그가 원형경기장에서 내 말들을 몰 것이오.\""]], [["Balthasar continued his gaze.", "발타사르는 계속 그를 응시했다."]], [["“He came well recommended,”", "\"이 사람은 든든한 추천을 받고 왔소.\""], ["Ilderim pursued, much puzzled.", "일데림은 몹시 어리둥절해하며 말을 이었다. \"고귀한 로마 선장이었던 아리우스의 아들로 그대는 알아도 좋소만, 그래도—\""], ["“You may know him as the son of Arrius, who was a noble Roman sailor, though”—the sheik hesitated, then resumed, with a laugh—“though he declares himself an Israelite of the tribe of Judah; and, by the splendor of God, I believe what he tells me!”", "족장은 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 웃으며 다시 말했다. \"그래도 본인은 자기가 유다 지파의 이스라엘 사람이라고 우긴다오. 그리고 하느님의 영광에 걸고, 나는 그가 하는 말을 믿소!\""]], [["Balthasar could no longer withhold explanation.", "발타사르는 더는 설명을 미룰 수 없었다."]], [["“To-day, O most generous sheik, my life was in peril, and would have been lost had not a youth, the counterpart of this one—if, indeed, he be not the very same—intervened when all others fled, and saved me.”", "\"오 더없이 너그러운 족장이여, 오늘 내 목숨이 위태로워, 한 젊은이가, 이 사람과 똑같이 생긴, 아니 어쩌면 바로 이 사람일지도 모를 그 젊은이가, 다른 모든 이가 달아날 때 나서서 나를 구해 주지 않았더라면 나는 목숨을 잃었을 것이오.\""], ["Then he addressed Ben-Hur directly, “Art thou not he?”", "그러고는 벤허를 향해 곧장 말했다. \"그대가 바로 그 사람이 아니오?\""]], [["“I cannot answer so far,” Ben-Hur replied, with modest deference.", "\"거기까지는 대답드릴 수 없습니다.\" 벤허가 겸손하고 정중하게 대답했다."], ["“I am he who stopped the horses of the insolent Roman when they were rushing upon thy camel at the Fountain of Castalia. Thy daughter left a cup with me.”", "\"저는 카스탈리아 샘에서 무례한 로마인의 말들이 어르신의 낙타로 돌진할 때 그 말들을 멈춰 세운 사람입니다. 어르신의 따님이 제게 잔을 하나 맡기셨지요.\""]], [["From the bosom of his tunic he produced the cup, and gave it to Balthasar.", "그는 윗옷 품에서 잔을 꺼내 발타사르에게 건넸다."]], [["A glow lighted the faded countenance of the Egyptian.", "이집트인의 시든 얼굴에 환한 빛이 번졌다."]], [["“The Lord sent thee to me at the Fountain to-day,”", "\"주님께서 오늘 그 샘에서 그대를 내게 보내셨소.\""], ["he said, in a tremulous voice, stretching his hand towards Ben-Hur; “and he sends thee to me now.", "그는 떨리는 목소리로 벤허를 향해 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"그리고 이제 다시 그대를 내게 보내셨구려."], ["I give him thanks; and praise him thou, for of his favor I have wherewith to give thee great reward, and I will.", "나는 그분께 감사드리오. 그대도 그분을 찬미하시오. 그분의 은총으로 나는 그대에게 큰 보답을 할 수 있는 것을 지녔으니, 반드시 그리하겠소."], ["The cup is thine; keep it.”", "이 잔은 그대의 것이오. 간직하시오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur took back the gift, and Balthasar, seeing the inquiry upon Ilderim’s face, related the occurrence at the Fountain.", "벤허는 그 선물을 도로 받았고, 발타사르는 일데림의 얼굴에 떠오른 궁금증을 보고 샘에서 있었던 일을 들려주었다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라!\""], ["said the sheik to Ben-Hur.", "족장이 벤허에게 말했다."], ["“Thou saidst nothing of this to me, when better recommendation thou couldst not have brought.", "\"그대는 내게 이 일을 한마디도 하지 않았구려. 이보다 더 나은 추천장을 가져올 수도 없었을 텐데."], ["Am I not an Arab, and sheik of my tribe of tens of thousands?", "나는 아랍인이요, 수만 명에 이르는 부족의 족장이 아니오?"], ["And is not he my guest?", "그리고 이분은 내 손님이 아니오?"], ["And is it not in my guest-bond that the good or evil thou dost him is good or evil done to me?", "또한 내 손님에게 그대가 베푼 선이나 악은 곧 내게 베푼 선이나 악이라는 것이, 내 손님을 지키는 맹세에 들어 있지 않소?"], ["Whither shouldst thou go for reward but here?", "보답을 받으러 그대가 어디로 가야 한단 말이오, 여기가 아니면?"], ["And whose the hand to give it but mine?”", "그리고 그 보답을 줄 손이 내 손이 아니면 누구의 손이겠소?\""]], [["His voice at the end of the speech rose to cutting shrillness.", "말끝에 이르러 그의 목소리는 살을 에는 듯한 날카로운 음으로 높아졌다."]], [["“Good sheik, spare me, I pray.", "\"어진 족장이시여, 부디 저를 봐주십시오."], ["I came not for reward, great or small; and that I may be acquitted of the thought, I say the help I gave this excellent man would have been given as well to thy humblest servant.”", "저는 크든 작든 보답을 바라고 온 것이 아닙니다. 그런 생각을 품지 않았음을 밝히고자 말씀드리거니와, 제가 이 훌륭한 분께 베푼 도움은 어르신의 가장 미천한 하인에게라도 똑같이 베풀었을 것입니다.\""]], [["“But he is my friend, my guest—not my servant; and seest thou not in the difference the favor of Fortune?”", "\"하나 이분은 내 벗이요, 내 손님이지, 내 하인이 아니오. 그대는 그 차이에서 운명의 은총을 보지 못한단 말이오?\""], ["Then to Balthasar the sheik subjoined, “Ah, by the splendor of God!", "그러고는 족장이 발타사르에게 덧붙였다. \"아, 하느님의 영광에 걸고!"], ["I tell thee again he is not a Roman.”", "다시 말하거니와 이 사람은 로마인이 아니오.\""]], [["With that he turned away, and gave attention to the servants, whose preparations for the supper were about complete.", "그 말과 함께 그는 돌아서서, 저녁상 준비가 거의 끝나가는 하인들에게 주의를 기울였다."]], [["The reader who recollects the history of Balthasar as given by himself at the meeting in the desert will understand the effect of Ben-Hur’s assertion of disinterestedness upon that worthy.", "사막에서 만났을 때 발타사르가 직접 들려준 그의 내력을 기억하는 독자라면, 벤허가 사심 없음을 밝힌 말이 이 덕망 높은 노인에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지 이해할 것이다."], ["In his devotion to men there had been, it will be remembered, no distinctions; while the redemption which had been promised him in the way of reward—the redemption for which he was waiting—was universal.", "기억하다시피, 그가 사람들에게 바친 헌신에는 어떠한 차별도 없었다. 한편 보답으로 그에게 약속된 구원은, 곧 그가 기다려 온 그 구원은, 만인을 위한 것이었다."], ["To him, therefore, the assertion sounded somewhat like an echo of himself.", "그러므로 그에게 그 말은 마치 자기 자신의 메아리처럼 들렸다."], ["He took a step nearer Ben-Hur, and spoke to him in the childlike way.", "그는 벤허에게 한 걸음 더 다가가, 어린아이 같은 말투로 그에게 물었다."]], [["“How did the sheik say I should call you?", "\"족장이 그대를 무어라 부르라 하셨던가?"], ["It was a Roman name, I think.”", "로마식 이름이었던 것 같소만.\""]], [["“Arrius, the son of Arrius.”", "\"아리우스, 아리우스의 아들입니다.\""]], [["“Yet thou art not a Roman?”", "\"하나 그대는 로마인이 아니지 않소?\""]], [["“All my people were Jews.”", "\"제 핏줄은 모두 유대인이었습니다.\""]], [["“Were, saidst thou?", "\"이었다, 라고 했소?"], ["Are they not living?”", "지금은 살아 있지 않단 말이오?\""]], [["The question was subtle as well as simple; but Ilderim saved Ben-Hur from reply.", "그 물음은 단순하면서도 미묘했다. 그러나 일데림이 벤허를 대답하지 않아도 되게 구해 주었다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자, 오시오.\""], ["he said to them, “the meal is ready.”", "그가 두 사람에게 말했다. \"식사가 준비되었소.\""]], [["Ben-Hur gave his arm to Balthasar, and conducted him to the table, where shortly they were all seated on their rugs Eastern fashion.", "벤허는 발타사르에게 팔을 내어 그를 식탁으로 인도했고, 이내 모두가 동방식으로 깔개 위에 앉았다."], ["The lavers were brought them, and they washed and dried their hands; then the sheik made a sign, the servants stopped, and the voice of the Egyptian arose tremulous with holy feeling.", "물그릇이 들려 나오자, 그들은 손을 씻고 물기를 닦았다. 그런 다음 족장이 신호를 보내니 하인들이 멈췄고, 거룩한 감정에 떨리는 이집트인의 목소리가 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["“Father of All—God!", "\"만물의 아버지, 하느님!"], ["What we have is of thee; take our thanks, and bless us, that we may continue to do thy will.”", "우리가 가진 것은 모두 당신에게서 온 것입니다. 우리의 감사를 받으시고, 우리를 축복하시어 우리가 당신의 뜻을 계속 행하게 하소서.\""]], [["It was the grace the good man had said simultaneously with his brethren Gaspar the Greek and Melchior the Hindoo, the utterance in diverse tongues out of which had come the miracle attesting the Divine Presence at the meal in the desert years before.", "그것은 이 어진 노인이 그리스인 가스파르, 힌두인 멜키오르 형제와 함께 동시에 올렸던 그 감사 기도였으니, 갖가지 다른 언어로 터져 나온 그 기도에서 여러 해 전 사막의 식사 자리에 신성한 임재가 함께했음을 증언하는 기적이 일어났던 것이다."]], [["The table to which they immediately addressed themselves was, as may be thought, rich in the substantials and delicacies favorite in the East—in cakes hot from the oven, vegetables from the gardens, meats singly, compounds of meats and vegetables, milk of kine, and honey and butter—all eaten or drunk, it should be remarked, without any of the modern accessories—knives, forks, spoons, cups, or plates; and in this part of the repast but little was said, for they were hungry.", "그들이 곧바로 손을 댄 식탁에는, 짐작할 수 있듯이, 동방에서 즐겨 먹는 든든한 음식과 진미가 풍성히 차려져 있었다. 화덕에서 갓 구운 떡, 텃밭에서 거둔 푸성귀, 고기만 따로 차린 것, 고기와 푸성귀를 섞은 것, 소젖, 그리고 꿀과 버터—이 모든 것을, 짚고 넘어가야 할 점이거니와, 칼이나 포크, 숟가락, 잔, 접시 따위 오늘날의 기물 하나 없이 먹고 마셨다. 식사의 이 대목에서는 별 말이 오가지 않았으니, 그들이 시장했기 때문이다."], ["But when the dessert was in course it was otherwise.", "그러나 후식이 나올 무렵에는 사정이 달라졌다."], ["They laved their hands again, had the lap-cloths shaken out, and with a renewed table and the sharp edge of their appetites gone they were disposed to talk and listen.", "그들은 다시 손을 씻고, 무릎 위에 펴 두었던 천을 털어 내었으며, 새로 차린 상과 무뎌진 입맛으로 이제는 이야기를 나누고 귀를 기울일 마음이 되었다."]], [["With such a company—an Arab, a Jew, and an Egyptian, all believers alike in one God—there could be at that age but one subject of conversation; and of the three, which should be speaker but he to whom the Deity had been so nearly a personal appearance, who had seen him in a star, had heard his voice in direction, had been led so far and so miraculously by his Spirit?", "아랍인과 유대인과 이집트인, 모두가 한 분이신 하느님을 똑같이 믿는 이런 일행에게, 그 시절 화제라야 단 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 셋 가운데 누가 그것을 이야기할 사람으로 신께서 그토록 가까이 몸소 나타나신 분, 별 속에서 그분을 뵙고, 그분의 음성으로 인도하심을 들었으며, 그분의 영에 이끌려 그토록 멀리 그토록 기적처럼 인도받은 그 사람만 하겠는가?"], ["And of what should he talk but that of which he had been called to testify?", "또한 그가 증언하라 부름받았던 그 일이 아니면, 무엇을 이야기하겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XV", "# CHAPTER XV"]], [["The shadows cast over the Orchard of Palms by the mountains at set of sun left no sweet margin time of violet sky and drowsing earth between the day and night.", "해 질 무렵 산들이 종려나무 과수원 위로 드리운 그림자는, 낮과 밤 사이에 으레 깃들기 마련인 보랏빛 하늘과 졸음에 잠긴 대지의 그 달콤한 짬을 남겨 두지 않았다."], ["The latter came early and swift; and against its glooming in the tent this evening the servants brought four candlesticks of brass, and set them by the corners of the table.", "밤은 이르고도 빠르게 찾아왔다. 그래서 오늘 저녁, 천막 안에 어둠이 스며드는 것에 맞서, 하인들은 놋쇠 촛대 네 개를 들고 와 탁자의 네 귀퉁이에 놓았다."], ["To each candlestick there were four branches, and on each branch a lighted silver lamp and a supply cup of olive-oil.", "촛대마다 가지가 넷이었고, 가지마다 불을 밝힌 은등잔 하나와 올리브 기름을 담은 보충용 잔이 얹혀 있었다."], ["In light ample, even brilliant, the group at dessert continued their conversation, speaking in the Syriac dialect, familiar to all peoples in that part of the world.", "넉넉하다 못해 환한 불빛 속에서, 후식을 들던 무리는 이야기를 이어 갔으니, 그 일대 모든 민족에게 익숙한 시리아 방언으로 나누는 말이었다."]], [["The Egyptian told his story of the meeting of the three in the desert, and agreed with the sheik that it was in December, twenty-seven years before, when he and his companions fleeing from Herod arrived at the tent praying shelter.", "이집트인은 사막에서 셋이 만났던 이야기를 들려주었고, 그 일이 스물일곱 해 전 십이월, 헤롯에게서 달아나던 자신과 두 동행이 이 천막에 이르러 묵을 곳을 청했을 때였다는 데에 족장과 뜻을 같이했다."], ["The narrative was heard with intense interest; even the servants lingering when they could to catch its details.", "그 이야기는 깊은 관심 속에 청취되었으니, 하인들조차 틈만 나면 그 세세한 대목을 놓치지 않으려 머뭇거렸다."], ["Ben-Hur received it as became a man listening to a revelation of deep concern to all humanity, and to none of more concern than the people of Israel.", "벤허는 온 인류에게, 그중에서도 이스라엘 백성에게 누구보다 깊이 관계되는 계시에 귀 기울이는 사람답게 그 이야기를 받아들였다."], ["In his mind, as we shall presently see, there was crystallizing an idea which was to change his course of life, if not absorb it absolutely.", "곧 보게 되겠지만, 그의 마음속에서는 하나의 생각이 결정(結晶)을 이루어 가고 있었으니, 그것은 그의 삶의 행로를 바꾸어 놓을, 어쩌면 그 행로를 송두리째 삼켜 버릴 생각이었다."]], [["As the recital proceeded, the impression made by Balthasar upon the young Jew increased; at its conclusion, his feeling was too profound to permit a doubt of its truth; indeed, there was nothing left him desirable in the connection but assurances, if such were to be had, pertaining exclusively to the consequences of the amazing event.", "이야기가 이어지는 동안, 발타사르가 젊은 유대인에게 새긴 인상은 더욱 깊어졌다. 이야기가 끝날 무렵 그의 감정은 너무도 깊어 그 진실을 한 점 의심할 여지가 없었다. 실로 이 일과 관련하여 그에게 더 바랄 것이 있다면, 만일 그런 것을 얻을 수 있다면, 오직 그 놀라운 사건의 결과에만 관계된 확언뿐이었다."]], [["And now there is wanting an explanation which the very discerning may have heretofore demanded; certainly it can be no longer delayed.", "이제 한 가지 설명이 필요하니, 어쩌면 눈 밝은 이들은 이미 그것을 요구해 왔을지도 모른다. 더는 미룰 수 없는 것임에 틀림없다."], ["Our tale begins, in point of date not less than fact, to trench close upon the opening of the ministry of the Son of Mary, whom we have seen but once since this same Balthasar left him worshipfully in his mother’s lap in the cave by Bethlehem.", "우리 이야기는 시점으로 보나 사실로 보나, 마리아의 아들이 그 사역을 시작하는 무렵에 바싹 다가서기 시작한다. 그 아이는 다름 아닌 이 발타사르가 베들레헴 곁 동굴에서 어머니의 무릎에 앉은 그를 경배하며 떠난 뒤로, 우리가 단 한 번밖에 보지 못한 그 아이다."], ["Henceforth to the end the mysterious Child will be a subject of continual reference; and slowly though surely the current of events with which we are dealing will bring us nearer and nearer to him, until finally we see him a man—we would like, if armed contrariety of opinion would permit it, to add—A MAN WHOM THE WORLD COULD NOT DO WITHOUT.", "이제부터 끝까지 이 신비로운 아이는 끊임없이 언급될 주제가 될 것이다. 그리고 우리가 다루는 사건의 흐름은 더디지만 확실하게 우리를 그에게 점점 더 가까이 데려가, 마침내 우리는 한 사람이 된 그를 보게 될 것이다—그리고, 만일 첨예하게 맞서는 견해들이 허락만 한다면, 이렇게 덧붙이고 싶다—세상이 없어서는 안 될 한 사람을."], ["Of this declaration, apparently so simple, a shrewd mind inspired by faith will make much—and in welcome.", "겉보기에 이토록 단순한 이 선언을, 믿음으로 깨우친 영민한 마음은 크게 받아들일 것이며—그것도 기꺼이 받아들일 것이다."], ["Before his time, and since, there have been men indispensable to particular people and periods; but his indispensability was to the whole race, and for all time—a respect in which it is unique, solitary, divine.", "그가 오기 전에도, 그 뒤에도, 특정한 백성과 시대에 없어서는 안 될 사람들은 있었다. 그러나 그의 없어서는 안 될 자리는 온 인류를 향한 것이었고, 모든 시대를 위한 것이었으니—이 점에서 그것은 유례없고, 홀로 우뚝하며, 신성하다."]], [["To Sheik Ilderim the story was not new.", "일데림 족장에게 그 이야기는 새로운 것이 아니었다."], ["He had heard it from the three wise men together under circumstances which left no room for doubt; he had acted upon it seriously, for the helping a fugitive escape from the anger of the first Herod was dangerous.", "그는 의심할 여지를 남기지 않는 정황 속에서 세 현인이 함께 있을 때 그 이야기를 들었다. 또 그는 그 이야기에 따라 진지하게 행동했으니, 첫 헤롯의 노여움을 피해 달아나는 도망자를 돕는 일은 위험했기 때문이다."], ["Now one of the three sat at his table again, a welcome guest and revered friend.", "이제 그 셋 가운데 하나가 다시 그의 식탁에 앉아, 반가운 손님이자 존경하는 벗이 되었다."], ["Sheik Ilderim certainly believed the story; yet, in the nature of things, its mighty central fact could not come home to him with the force and absorbing effect it came to Ben-Hur.", "일데림 족장은 분명 그 이야기를 믿었다. 그러나 사물의 이치상, 그 이야기의 강력한 핵심 사실은 벤허에게 다가온 것만큼의 힘과 가슴을 사로잡는 효력으로 그에게 다가오지는 못했다."], ["He was an Arab, whose interest in the consequences was but general; on the other hand, Ben-Hur was an Israelite and a Jew, with more than a special interest in—if the solecism can be pardoned—the truth of the fact.", "그는 아랍인이었고, 그 결과에 대한 관심은 그저 일반적인 것에 지나지 않았다. 반면에 벤허는 이스라엘 사람이요 유대인이었으니, 그 사실의 진실됨에—이런 어법의 모순을 용서받을 수 있다면—특별한 관심 이상의 것을 품고 있었다."], ["He laid hold of the circumstance with a purely Jewish mind.", "그는 순전히 유대인다운 마음으로 그 정황을 붙들었다."]], [["From his cradle, let it be remembered, he had heard of the Messiah; at the colleges he had been made familiar with all that was known of that Being at once the hope, the fear, and the peculiar glory of the chosen people; the prophets from the first to the last of the heroic line foretold him; and the coming had been, and yet was, the theme of endless exposition with the rabbis—in the synagogues, in the schools, in the Temple, of fast-days and feast-days, in public and in private, the national teachers expounded and kept expounding until all the children of Abraham, wherever their lots were cast, bore the Messiah in expectation, and by it literally, and with iron severity, ruled and moulded their lives.", "기억해 두어야 할 것은, 그가 요람에서부터 메시아 이야기를 들으며 자랐다는 사실이다. 학당에서 그는 선택받은 백성의 희망이자 두려움이며 또한 그들 고유의 영광인 그 존재에 관해 알려진 모든 것을 익혔다. 영웅들의 계보 가운데 처음부터 끝까지 모든 예언자가 그를 예언했다. 그의 오심은 랍비들에게 끝없는 해설의 주제였고 지금도 그러했다—회당에서, 학교에서, 성전에서, 금식일과 절기마다, 공적인 자리에서나 사적인 자리에서나, 민족의 스승들은 해설하고 또 해설하기를 그치지 않았다. 그리하여 아브라함의 모든 자손은, 그들의 운명이 어디에 내던져졌든, 메시아를 기다림 속에 품었고, 그 기다림에 따라 글자 그대로, 쇠처럼 엄격하게 그들의 삶을 다스리고 빚어 나갔다."]], [["Doubtless, it will be understood from this that there was much argument among the Jews themselves about the Messiah, and so there was; but the disputation was all limited to one point, and one only—when would he come?", "이로 미루어 보아 유대인들 사이에도 메시아를 둘러싼 논쟁이 많았으리라는 점은 의심의 여지가 없으며, 실제로도 그러했다. 그러나 그 논쟁은 오직 한 가지, 단 한 가지 점에 국한되어 있었다—그가 언제 올 것인가?"]], [["Disquisition is for the preacher; whereas the writer is but telling a tale, and that he may not lose his character, the explanation he is making requires notice merely of a point connected with the Messiah about which the unanimity among the chosen people was matter of marvellous astonishment: he was to be, when come, the KING OF THE JEWS—their political King, their Cæsar.", "논설은 설교자의 몫이다. 반면에 글쓴이는 그저 이야기를 들려줄 따름이니, 제 본분을 잃지 않으려면, 지금 하고 있는 이 설명에서는 메시아와 관련된 한 가지 점만을 짚으면 된다. 그 점에 관한 한 선택받은 백성 사이의 일치는 그야말로 경이로운 놀라움이었다."], ["By their instrumentality he was to make armed conquest of the earth, and then, for their profit and in the name of God, hold it down forever.", "곧 그가 오면 유대인의 왕이 되리라는 것—그들의 정치적 왕, 그들의 카이사르가 되리라는 것이었다."], ["On this faith, dear reader, the Pharisees or Separatists—the latter being rather a political term—in the cloisters and around the altars of the Temple, built an edifice of hope far overtopping the dream of the Macedonian.", "그를 통해 그는 땅을 무력으로 정복하고, 이어 그들의 이익을 위해, 하느님의 이름으로 영원히 그것을 다스리리라는 것이었다. 친애하는 독자여, 바리새인, 곧 분리주의자들은—후자는 오히려 정치적인 명칭이지만—성전의 회랑에서, 제단 둘레에서, 이 믿음 위에 마케도니아인의 꿈을 훌쩍 넘어서는 희망의 전당을 세웠다."], ["His but covered the earth; theirs covered the earth and filled the skies; that is to say, in their bold, boundless fantasy of blasphemous egotism, God the Almighty was in effect to suffer them for their uses to nail him by the ear to a door in sign of eternal servitude.", "그의 꿈은 땅을 덮는 데 그쳤으나, 그들의 꿈은 땅을 덮고 하늘까지 채웠다. 다시 말해, 그들의 대담하고 끝 모를, 신성을 모독하는 자만의 환상 속에서, 전능하신 하느님은 사실상 그들의 쓰임에 맞게, 영원한 종살이의 표징으로 자신의 귀를 문에 못 박히도록 내맡기시는 셈이었다."]], [["Returning directly to Ben-Hur, it is to be observed now that there were two circumstances in his life the result of which had been to keep him in a state comparatively free from the influence and hard effects of the audacious faith of his Separatist countrymen.", "곧장 벤허에게로 돌아오면, 이제 그의 삶에 두 가지 정황이 있었음을 짚어 두어야겠다. 그 결과로 그는 동족인 분리주의자들의 그 무모한 믿음이 끼치는 영향과 모진 작용에서 비교적 자유로운 상태로 머물러 있었다."]], [["In the first place, his father followed the faith of the Sadducees, who may, in a general way, be termed the Liberals of their time.", "첫째로, 그의 아버지는 사두개인의 믿음을 따랐는데, 이들은 대체로 그 시대의 자유주의자라 일컬을 만했다."], ["They had some loose opinions in denial of the soul.", "그들은 영혼을 부정하는 다소 느슨한 견해를 지니고 있었다."], ["They were strict constructionists and rigorous observers of the Law as found in the books of Moses; but they held the vast mass of Rabbinical addenda to those books in derisive contempt.", "그들은 모세 오경에 담긴 율법을 엄격하게 해석하고 철저하게 지켰으나, 그 책들에 덧붙은 방대한 랍비의 부록들은 비웃듯 경멸하며 물리쳤다."], ["They were unquestionably a sect, yet their religion was more a philosophy than a creed; they did not deny themselves the enjoyments of life, and saw many admirable methods and productions among the Gentile divisions of the race.", "그들은 의심할 바 없이 하나의 분파였으나, 그들의 종교는 신조라기보다 차라리 철학에 가까웠다. 그들은 삶의 즐거움을 스스로 마다하지 않았고, 인류 가운데 이방인 무리에게서도 훌륭한 방식과 산물을 적지 않게 보았다."], ["In politics they were the active opposition of the Separatists.", "정치에서 그들은 분리주의자들의 능동적인 반대 세력이었다."], ["In the natural order of things, these circumstances and conditions, opinions and peculiarities, would have descended to the son as certainly and really as any portion of his father’s estate; and, as we have seen, he was actually in course of acquiring them, when the second saving event overtook him.", "사물의 자연스러운 이치대로라면, 이러한 정황과 처지, 견해와 특성은 아버지의 재산 어느 한 부분 못지않게 확실하고도 실제로 아들에게 물려졌을 것이다. 그리고 우리가 보았듯, 그는 실제로 그것들을 물려받아 가는 과정에 있었으니, 두 번째 구원의 사건이 그를 덮쳤을 때였다."]], [["Upon a youth of Ben-Hur’s mind and temperament the influence of five years of affluent life in Rome can be appreciated best by recalling that the great city was then, in fact, the meeting-place of the nations—their meeting-place politically and commercially, as well as for the indulgence of pleasure without restraint.", "벤허와 같은 정신과 기질을 지닌 젊은이에게 로마에서 보낸 다섯 해의 풍요로운 삶이 끼친 영향은, 그 위대한 도시가 당시 실로 만국이 모여드는 자리였음을 떠올릴 때 가장 잘 헤아릴 수 있다—정치적으로나 상업적으로나 모여드는 자리였고, 거리낌 없이 쾌락에 탐닉하는 자리이기도 했다."], ["Round and round the golden mile-stone in front of the Forum—now in gloom of eclipse, now in unapproachable splendor—flowed all the active currents of humanity.", "포룸 앞의 황금 이정표를 빙빙 돌아—때로는 일식의 어둠에 잠기고, 때로는 다가설 수 없는 광휘에 휩싸인 그 이정표를—인류의 온갖 활기찬 물결이 흘렀다."], ["If excellences of manner, refinements of society, attainments of intellect, and glory of achievement made no impression upon him, how could he, as the son of Arrius, pass day after day, through a period so long, from the beautiful villa near Misenum into the receptions of Cæsar, and be wholly uninfluenced by what he saw there of kings, princes, ambassadors, hostages, and delegates, suitors all of them from every known land, waiting humbly the yes or no which was to make or unmake them?", "처신의 탁월함, 사교의 세련됨, 지성의 성취, 업적의 영광이 그에게 아무 인상도 남기지 않았다면, 어찌 아리우스의 아들인 그가 미세눔 근처의 아름다운 별장에서 카이사르의 접견실로 날마다, 그토록 오랜 세월 동안 드나들면서, 거기서 본 왕과 제후, 사절과 인질과 대표들—모두가 알려진 온 땅에서 온 청원자로서, 자신을 세워 줄지 무너뜨릴지를 가를 가부의 한마디를 비굴하게 기다리던 이들—에게 조금도 영향받지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["As mere assemblages, to be sure, there was nothing to compare with the gatherings at Jerusalem in celebration of the Passover; yet when he sat under the purple velaria of the Circus Maximus one of three hundred and fifty thousand spectators, he must have been visited by the thought that possibly there might be some branches of the family of man worthy divine consideration, if not mercy, though they were of the uncircumcised—some, by their sorrows, and, yet worse, by their hopelessness in the midst of sorrows, fitted for brotherhood in the promises to his countrymen.", "물론 단순한 군집으로 치자면, 유월절을 기념하여 예루살렘에 모여드는 무리에 견줄 것은 없었다. 그러나 그가 키르쿠스 막시무스의 자줏빛 차일 아래 삼십오만 관중 가운데 하나로 앉아 있을 때, 그에게는 이런 생각이 찾아들었음에 틀림없다. 어쩌면 인류 가운데에는, 비록 할례받지 못한 자들일지라도, 자비까지는 아니더라도 신의 헤아림을 받을 만한 갈래들이 있을지 모른다는 생각—그들의 슬픔으로 인해, 그리고 더 나쁘게는 슬픔 한가운데서의 그 절망으로 인해, 자기 동족에게 주어진 약속 안에서 형제 됨을 누릴 자격이 있는 이들이 있을지 모른다는 생각이었다."]], [["That he should have had such a thought under such circumstances was but natural; we think so much, at least, will be admitted: but when the reflection came to him, and he gave himself up to it, he could not have been blind to a certain distinction.", "그러한 정황에서 그가 그러한 생각을 품었다는 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다. 적어도 그 정도는 인정되리라고 우리는 본다. 그러나 그 성찰이 그에게 찾아들어 그가 거기에 자신을 내맡겼을 때, 그는 어떤 분명한 차이를 못 보았을 리 없다."], ["The wretchedness of the masses, and their hopeless condition, had no relation whatever to religion; their murmurs and groans were not against their gods or for want of gods.", "그 무리의 비참함과 그들의 절망적인 처지는 종교와는 아무런 관련이 없었다. 그들의 웅성거림과 신음은 그들의 신들을 거스르는 것도, 신이 없음을 한탄하는 것도 아니었다. 브리튼의 떡갈나무 숲에서는 드루이드들이 그 추종자들을 거느렸다."], ["In the oak-woods of Britain the Druids held their followers; Odin and Freya maintained their godships in Gaul and Germany and among the Hyperboreans; Egypt was satisfied with her crocodiles and Anubis; the Persians were yet devoted to Ormuzd and Ahriman, holding them in equal honor; in hope of the Nirvana, the Hindoos moved on patient as ever in the rayless paths of Brahm; the beautiful Greek mind, in pauses of philosophy, still sang the heroic gods of Homer; while in Rome nothing was so common and cheap as gods.", "오딘과 프레야는 갈리아와 게르마니아에서, 그리고 극북의 히페르보레아인들 사이에서 그 신성을 지켰다. 이집트는 제 악어들과 아누비스로 흡족해했다. 페르시아인은 여전히 오르무즈드와 아리만에게 헌신하며 둘을 똑같이 받들었다."], ["According to whim, the masters of the world, because they were masters, carried their worship and offerings indifferently from altar to altar, delighted in the pandemonium they had erected.", "힌두인들은 열반을 바라며 한결같이 참을성 있게 브라흐마의 빛 없는 길을 걸어갔다. 아름다운 그리스의 정신은 철학의 막간에도 여전히 호메로스의 영웅 신들을 노래했다. 한편 로마에서는 신들만큼 흔하고 값싼 것이 없었다."], ["Their discontent, if they were discontented, was with the number of gods; for, after borrowing all the divinities of the earth they proceeded to deify their Cæsars, and vote them altars and holy service.", "세상의 주인들은, 자기네가 주인이라는 이유로, 변덕에 따라 제단에서 제단으로 거리낌 없이 그 숭배와 제물을 옮겨 다니며, 자기들이 세운 그 아수라장을 즐겼다. 그들의 불만이란, 만일 그들에게 불만이 있었다면, 신들의 수효에 대한 것이었다. 그들은 땅 위의 온갖 신성을 빌려 온 뒤, 자기네 카이사르들마저 신격화하여 그들에게 제단과 성스러운 예배를 바치기로 의결하기에 이르렀으니 말이다."], ["No, the unhappy condition was not from religion, but misgovernment and usurpations and countless tyrannies.", "그렇다, 그 불행한 처지는 종교에서 비롯된 것이 아니라, 그릇된 통치와 찬탈, 그리고 헤아릴 수 없는 폭정에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["The Avernus men had been tumbled into, and were praying to be relieved from, was terribly but essentially political.", "사람들이 곤두박질쳐 떨어져 거기서 구해 달라고 빌고 있던 그 아베르누스는, 끔찍하기는 했으나 본질적으로 정치적인 것이었다."], ["The supplication—everywhere alike, in Lodinum, Alexandria, Athens, Jerusalem—was for a king to conquer with, not a god to worship.", "그 간구는—로디눔에서나 알렉산드리아에서나 아테네에서나 예루살렘에서나 한결같이—함께 싸워 정복할 왕을 향한 것이었지, 경배할 신을 향한 것이 아니었다."]], [["Studying the situation after two thousand years, we can see and say that religiously there was no relief from the universal confusion except some God could prove himself a true God, and a masterful one, and come to the rescue; but the people of the time, even the discerning and philosophical, discovered no hope except in crushing Rome; that done, the relief would follow in restorations and reorganizations; therefore they prayed, conspired, rebelled, fought, and died, drenching the soil to-day with blood, to-morrow with tears—and always with the same result.", "이천 년이 지난 지금 그 상황을 살펴보면, 우리는 이렇게 보고 말할 수 있다. 종교적으로 그 보편적 혼란에서 벗어날 길은, 어떤 신이 스스로 참된 신임을, 그것도 위엄 있는 신임을 입증하고 구하러 오는 것 말고는 없었다고. 그러나 그 시대의 사람들은, 눈 밝고 사색에 깊은 이들조차도, 로마를 짓밟는 것 말고는 어떤 희망도 찾지 못했다. 그 일만 이루어지면 회복과 재편이 뒤따라 구원이 오리라 여겼다. 그리하여 그들은 기도하고, 음모를 꾸미고, 반란을 일으키고, 싸우고, 죽어 갔으니, 오늘은 피로 땅을 적시고 내일은 눈물로 적셨다—그리고 늘 똑같은 결말을 맞았다."]], [["It remains to be said now that Ben-Hur was in agreement with the mass of men of his time not Romans.", "이제 남은 말은, 벤허가 로마인이 아닌 그 시대 대다수 사람들과 뜻을 같이했다는 것이다."], ["The five years’ residence in the capital served him with opportunity to see and study the miseries of the subjugated world; and in full belief that the evils which afflicted it were political, and to be cured only by the sword, he was going forth to fit himself for a part in the day of resort to the heroic remedy.", "수도에서 보낸 다섯 해는 그에게 정복당한 세상의 비참함을 보고 헤아릴 기회를 주었다. 그리고 그 세상을 괴롭히는 악이 정치적인 것이며 오직 칼로써만 고쳐질 수 있다고 굳게 믿은 그는, 그 영웅적 처방에 호소하는 날에 한몫을 맡을 수 있도록 스스로를 단련하러 나서던 참이었다."], ["By practice of arms he was a perfect soldier; but war has its higher fields, and he who would move successfully in them must know more than to defend with shield and thrust with spear.", "무예를 익혀 그는 완벽한 군인이었다. 그러나 전쟁에는 더 높은 영역이 있으니, 거기서 성공적으로 움직이려는 자는 방패로 막고 창으로 찌르는 것 이상을 알아야 한다."], ["In those fields the general finds his tasks, the greatest of which is the reduction of the many into one, and that one himself; the consummate captain is a fighting-man armed with an army.", "그 영역에서 장수는 제 임무를 찾으니, 그 가운데 가장 큰 것은 많은 이를 하나로, 그 하나를 곧 자신으로 모으는 일이다. 더없이 완숙한 지휘관이란 한 군대로 무장한 한 사람의 전사다."], ["This conception entered into the scheme of life to which he was further swayed by the reflection that the vengeance he dreamed of, in connection with his individual wrongs, would be more surely found in some of the ways of war than in any pursuit of peace.", "이러한 구상이 그의 삶의 계획 속으로 들어왔고, 그를 거기로 더욱 기울게 한 것은 이런 성찰이었다. 곧 자신이 당한 개인적인 부당함과 관련하여 그가 꿈꾸던 복수는, 평화로운 어떤 길을 좇기보다 전쟁의 어떤 길에서 더 확실하게 찾으리라는 생각이었다."]], [["The feelings with which he listened to Balthasar can be now understood.", "그가 어떤 심정으로 발타사르의 말에 귀 기울였는지는 이제 이해할 수 있다."], ["The story touched two of the most sensitive points of his being so they rang within him.", "그 이야기는 그의 존재에서 가장 예민한 두 지점을 건드렸기에 그의 안에서 울려 퍼졌다."], ["His heart beat fast—and faster still when, searching himself, he found not a doubt either that the recital was true in every particular, or that the Child so miraculously found was the Messiah.", "그의 심장은 빠르게 뛰었다—스스로를 살펴, 그 이야기가 낱낱이 모두 참되다는 것도, 그토록 기적적으로 발견된 그 아이가 메시아라는 것도 의심할 여지가 없음을 알았을 때, 더욱더 빠르게 뛰었다."], ["Marvelling much that Israel rested so dead to the revelation, and that he had never heard of it before that day, two questions presented themselves to him as centring all it was at that moment further desirable to know:", "이스라엘이 그 계시에 그토록 죽은 듯 무심하다는 것에, 그리고 자신이 그날 이전에는 그것을 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없다는 것에 크게 놀라며, 두 가지 물음이 그에게 떠올랐으니, 그 순간 그가 더 알고 싶은 모든 것이 거기에 집약되어 있었다."]], [["Where was the Child then?", "그 아이는 그때 어디에 있는가?"]], [["And what was his mission?", "그리고 그의 사명은 무엇인가?"]], [["With apologies for the interruptions, he proceeded to draw out the opinions of Balthasar, who was in nowise loath to speak.", "자꾸 끼어드는 것을 사과하며, 그는 발타사르의 견해를 끌어내기 시작했고, 발타사르는 결코 입을 떼기를 마다하지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI", "# CHAPTER XVI"]], [["“If I could answer you,”", "\"내가 그대에게 답할 수 있다면,\""], ["Balthasar said, in his simple, earnest, devout way—“oh, if I knew where he is, how quickly I would go to him!", "발타사르는 그 소박하고 진실하며 경건한 어조로 말했다. \"오, 그가 어디 있는지 안다면, 나는 얼마나 빨리 그에게 달려가겠는가!"], ["The seas should not stay me, nor the mountains.”", "바다도 나를 막지 못하고, 산도 막지 못할 것이오.\""]], [["“You have tried to find him, then?”", "\"그렇다면 그를 찾으려 해 보셨군요?\""], ["asked Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 물었다."]], [["A smile flitted across the face of the Egyptian.", "이집트인의 얼굴에 미소가 스쳤다."]], [["“The first task I charged myself with after leaving the shelter given me in the desert”—Balthasar cast a grateful look at Ilderim—“was to learn what became of the Child.", "\"사막에서 내게 주어진 안식처를 떠난 뒤 처음으로 스스로 떠맡은 일은\"—발타사르는 일데림에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다—\"그 아기가 어찌 되었는지 알아내는 것이었소."], ["But a year had passed, and I dared not go up to Judea in person, for Herod still held the throne bloody-minded as ever.", "하지만 한 해가 지났고, 나는 감히 직접 유대 땅으로 올라갈 수 없었소. 헤롯이 여전히 옥좌에 앉아 예나 다름없이 잔혹한 마음을 품고 있었기 때문이오."], ["In Egypt, upon my return, there were a few friends to believe the wonderful things I told them of what I had seen and heard—a few who rejoiced with me that a Redeemer was born—a few who never tired of the story.", "이집트로 돌아오니, 내가 보고 들은 놀라운 일들을 믿어 주는 벗이 몇 있었소. 구세주가 태어났음을 나와 함께 기뻐한 이들, 그 이야기에 결코 싫증 내지 않은 이들이 몇 있었던 거요."], ["Some of them came up for me looking after the Child.", "그들 가운데 몇이 나를 위해 그 아기를 찾아 나섰소."], ["They went first to Bethlehem, and found there the khan and the cave; but the steward—he who sat at the gate the night of the birth, and the night we came following the star—was gone.", "그들은 먼저 베들레헴으로 가서 그 여관과 동굴을 찾아냈소. 그러나 그 청지기는—탄생의 밤에, 그리고 우리가 별을 따라온 그 밤에 문가에 앉아 있던 그 사람은—사라지고 없었소."], ["The king had taken him away, and he was no more seen.”", "왕이 그를 데려갔고, 그 뒤로 그는 다시 보이지 않았소.\""]], [["“But they found some proofs, surely,”", "\"하지만 무언가 증거는 분명히 찾았겠지요.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, eagerly.", "벤허가 간절하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, proofs written in blood—a village in mourning; mothers yet crying for their little ones.", "\"그렇소, 피로 쓰인 증거였소. 슬픔에 잠긴 마을, 아직도 제 어린것을 위해 우는 어머니들 말이오."], ["You must know, when Herod heard of our flight, he sent down and slew the youngest-born of the children of Bethlehem. Not one escaped.", "그대도 알아야 하오. 헤롯은 우리가 달아났다는 소식을 듣고는 사람을 내려보내 베들레헴 아이들 가운데 가장 어린 것들을 죽였소."], ["The faith of my messengers was confirmed; but they came to me saying the Child was dead, slain with the other innocents.”", "단 하나도 살아남지 못했소. 내 사자들의 믿음은 확인되었소. 그러나 그들은 내게 와서 아기도 다른 무고한 아이들과 함께 죽임을 당했다고 말했소.\""]], [["“Dead!” exclaimed Ben-Hur, aghast.", "\"죽다니!\" 벤허가 깜짝 놀라 외쳤다."], ["“Dead, sayest thou?”", "\"죽었다고 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Nay, my son, I did not say so. I said they, my messengers, told me the Child was dead.", "\"아니, 내 아들아, 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았소. 내 사자들이 아기가 죽었다고 말했다고 한 것이오."], ["I did not believe the report then; I do not believe it now.”", "나는 그때도 그 소식을 믿지 않았고, 지금도 믿지 않소.\""]], [["“I see—thou hast some special knowledge.”", "\"알겠습니다. 어르신께는 무언가 특별한 앎이 있으시군요.\""]], [["“Not so, not so,” said Balthasar, dropping his gaze.", "\"그렇지 않소, 그렇지 않소.\" 발타사르가 시선을 떨구며 말했다."], ["“The Spirit was to go with us no farther than to the Child.", "\"성령은 그 아기에게 이르기까지만 우리와 함께하셨소."], ["When we came out of the cave, after our presents were given and we had seen the babe, we looked first thing for the star; but it was gone, and we knew we were left to ourselves.", "우리가 예물을 바치고 아기를 본 다음 동굴에서 나왔을 때, 우리는 무엇보다 먼저 그 별을 찾았소. 그러나 별은 사라지고 없었고, 우리는 이제 우리 자신에게 맡겨졌음을 알았소."], ["The last inspiration of the Holy One—the last I can recall—was that which sent us to Ilderim for safety.”", "거룩하신 분의 마지막 영감은—내가 기억할 수 있는 마지막 영감은—안전을 위해 우리를 일데림에게 보낸 그것이었소.\""]], [["“Yes,” said the sheik, fingering his beard nervously.", "\"그렇소,\" 족장이 신경질적으로 수염을 만지작거리며 말했다."], ["“You told me you were sent to me by a Spirit—I remember it.”", "\"그대는 어떤 성령에 이끌려 내게 왔다고 했지요. 나는 그것을 기억하오.\""]], [["“I have no special knowledge,”", "\"내게 특별한 앎이 있는 것은 아니오.\" 발타사르는 벤허에게 내려앉은 낙담을 살피며 말을 이었다."], ["Balthasar continued, observing the dejection which had fallen upon Ben-Hur; “but, my son, I have given the matter much thought—thought continuing through years, inspired by faith, which, I assure you, calling God for witness, is as strong in me now as in the hour I heard the voice of the Spirit calling me by the shore of the lake.", "\"하지만, 내 아들아, 나는 이 일을 두고 많은 생각을 해 왔소. 여러 해에 걸쳐 이어진 생각이었고, 믿음에서 우러난 것이었소. 하느님을 증인 삼아 말하건대, 그 믿음은 호숫가에서 나를 부르는 성령의 음성을 들었던 그 순간만큼이나 지금도 내 안에 굳건하오."], ["If you will listen, I will tell you why I believe the Child is living.”", "들어 주겠다면, 내가 왜 그 아기가 살아 있다고 믿는지 그대에게 말해 주리다.\""]], [["Both Ilderim and Ben-Hur looked assent, and appeared to summon their faculties that they might understand as well as hear.", "일데림과 벤허는 둘 다 그러라는 뜻을 내비치고, 듣기만 할 뿐 아니라 이해하려고 온 정신을 그러모으는 듯했다."], ["The interest reached the servants, who drew near to the divan, and stood listening.", "그 관심은 하인들에게까지 미쳐, 그들은 자리 가까이 다가와 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["Throughout the tent there was the profoundest silence.", "천막 안에는 더없이 깊은 침묵이 감돌았다."]], [["“We three believe in God.”", "\"우리 세 사람은 하느님을 믿소.\""]], [["Balthasar bowed his head as he spoke.", "발타사르는 그렇게 말하며 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“And he is the Truth,”", "\"그리고 그분은 진리이시오.\""], ["he resumed. “His word is God.", "그가 다시 말을 이었다."], ["The hills may turn to dust, and the seas be drunk dry by south winds; but his word shall stand, because it is the Truth.”", "\"그분의 말씀은 곧 하느님이시오. 산은 티끌로 변하고, 바다는 남풍에 말라붙을 수 있소. 그러나 그분의 말씀은 굳건히 서리니, 그것이 곧 진리이기 때문이오.\""]], [["The utterance was in a manner inexpressibly solemn.", "그 말투는 이루 말할 수 없이 장엄했다."]], [["“The voice, which was his, speaking to me by the lake, said, ‘Blessed art thou, O son of Mizraim!", "\"호숫가에서 내게 말씀하신 그 음성, 곧 그분의 음성은 이렇게 일렀소. '복되도다, 미스라임의 아들이여!"], ["The Redemption cometh.", "구속이 다가오느니라."], ["With two others from the remotenesses of the earth, thou shalt see the Savior.’ I have seen the Savior—blessed be his name!—but the Redemption, which was the second part of the promise, is yet to come.", "땅의 먼 곳에서 온 다른 두 사람과 더불어, 너는 구세주를 보리라.' 나는 구세주를 보았소—그 이름을 찬미할지어다!—하지만 약속의 두 번째 부분이었던 구속은 아직 오지 않았소."], ["Seest thou now?", "이제 알겠소?"], ["If the Child be dead, there is no agent to bring the Redemption about, and the word is naught, and God—nay, I dare not say it!”", "만일 그 아기가 죽었다면, 구속을 이룰 자가 없는 것이고, 그러면 그 말씀은 헛것이며, 하느님께서는—아니, 감히 말할 수 없소!\""]], [["He threw up both hands in horror.", "그는 두려움에 두 손을 번쩍 들어 올렸다."]], [["“The Redemption was the work for which the Child was born; and so long as the promise abides, not even death can separate him from his work until it is fulfilled, or at least in the way of fulfilment.", "\"구속이야말로 그 아기가 태어난 까닭이오. 그러니 그 약속이 살아 있는 한, 죽음조차도 그 일이 이루어질 때까지, 아니면 적어도 이루어지는 길에 들어설 때까지는 그를 그 일에서 떼어 놓을 수 없소."], ["Take you that now as one reason for my belief; then give me further attention.”", "이제 그것을 내 믿음의 한 가지 이유로 받아들이시오. 그런 다음 좀 더 귀를 기울여 주시오.\""]], [["The good man paused.", "그 선한 사람은 잠시 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Wilt thou not taste the wine? It is at thy hand—see,”", "\"포도주를 들지 않겠소? 그대 손 가까이 있소, 보시오.\""], ["said Ilderim, respectfully.", "일데림이 정중하게 말했다."]], [["Balthasar drank, and, seeming refreshed, continued:", "발타사르는 포도주를 마시고, 기운을 차린 듯 말을 이었다."]], [["“The Savior I saw was born of woman, in nature like us, and subject to all our ills—even death.", "\"내가 본 구세주는 여인에게서 태어났으니, 본성은 우리와 같고, 우리가 겪는 온갖 병고에 매여 있었소."], ["Let that stand as the first proposition.", "죽음에까지도. 그것을 첫 번째 명제로 삼읍시다."], ["Consider next the work set apart to him.", "다음으로 그에게 따로 정해진 일을 헤아려 보시오."], ["Was it not a performance for which only a man is fitted?—a man wise, firm, discreet—a man, not a child?", "그것은 오직 어른에게나 어울리는 일이 아니었소? 지혜롭고, 굳세고, 분별 있는 사람—아이가 아니라 어른 말이오."], ["To become such he had to grow as we grow.", "그런 사람이 되려면 그는 우리가 자라듯 자라야 했소."], ["Bethink you now of the dangers his life was subject to in the interval—the long interval between childhood and maturity.", "그동안 그의 목숨이 처했던 위험들을 이제 생각해 보시오. 어린 시절과 성년 사이의 그 긴 세월 동안의 위험 말이오."], ["The existing powers were his enemies; Herod was his enemy; and what would Rome have been?", "당대의 권세들이 그의 적이었소. 헤롯이 그의 적이었고, 로마는 또 어떠했겠소?"], ["And as for Israel—that he should not be accepted by Israel was the motive for cutting him off.", "그리고 이스라엘로 말하자면—이스라엘이 그를 받아들이지 않으리라는 것, 바로 그것이 그를 끊어 버리려는 동기였소."], ["See you now.", "이제 알겠소."], ["What better way was there to take care of his life in the helpless growing time than by passing him into obscurity?", "무력하게 자라는 시절에 그의 목숨을 지키는 데, 그를 무명 속으로 숨겨 두는 것보다 더 나은 길이 어디 있었겠소?"], ["Wherefore I say to myself, and to my listening faith, which is never moved except by yearning of love—I say he is not dead, but lost; and, his work remaining undone, he will come again.", "그래서 나는 스스로에게, 그리고 사랑의 갈망이 아니고서는 결코 움직이지 않는 내 귀 기울이는 믿음에게 말하오—그는 죽은 것이 아니라 잠겨 있을 뿐이며, 그의 일이 이루어지지 않은 채 남아 있으니 다시 오리라고."], ["There you have the reasons for my belief.", "자, 이것이 내 믿음의 이유들이오."], ["Are they not good?”", "그것들이 옳지 않소?\""]], [["Ilderim’s small Arab eyes were bright with understanding, and Ben-Hur, lifted from his dejection, said heartily, “I, at least, may not gainsay them.", "일데림의 작은 아라비아인 눈이 깨달음으로 빛났고, 벤허는 낙담에서 벗어나 진심으로 말했다. \"적어도 저는 그것을 반박할 수 없습니다."], ["What further, pray?”", "청컨대, 그다음은요?\""]], [["“Hast thou not enough, my son? Well,”", "\"이만하면 충분하지 않소, 내 아들아? 그래도,\""], ["he began, in calmer tone, “seeing that the reasons were good—more plainly, seeing it was God’s will that the Child should not be found—I settled my faith into the keeping of patience, and took to waiting.”", "그는 한결 차분한 어조로 시작했다. \"그 이유들이 옳다는 것을—더 분명히 말하면, 그 아기가 발견되지 않는 것이 하느님의 뜻임을 알고서, 나는 내 믿음을 인내의 손에 맡기고 기다리기 시작했소.\""], ["He raised his eyes, full of holy trust, and broke off abstractedly—“I am waiting now.", "그는 거룩한 신뢰로 가득한 두 눈을 들어 올리더니, 넋을 놓은 듯 말을 끊었다. \"나는 지금 기다리고 있소."], ["He lives, keeping well his mighty secret.", "그는 살아 있고, 그 위대한 비밀을 잘 간직하고 있소."], ["What though I cannot go to him, or name the hill or the vale of his abiding-place?", "내가 그에게 갈 수 없다 한들, 그가 머무는 언덕이나 골짜기의 이름을 댈 수 없다 한들 어떻소?"], ["He lives—it may be as the fruit in blossom, it may be as the fruit just ripening; but by the certainty there is in the promise and reason of God, I know he lives.”", "그는 살아 있소—어쩌면 꽃핀 열매처럼, 어쩌면 막 익어 가는 열매처럼. 그러나 하느님의 약속과 섭리 속에 있는 확실함으로 말미암아, 나는 그가 살아 있음을 아오.\""]], [["A thrill of awe struck Ben-Hur—a thrill which was but the dying of his half-formed doubt.", "벤허는 경외감의 전율에 사로잡혔다—그것은 다만 어렴풋이 일었던 의심이 사그라드는 전율이었다."]], [["“Where thinkest thou he is?” he asked, in a low voice, and hesitating, like one who feels upon his lips the pressure of a sacred silence.", "\"그가 어디 있다고 생각하십니까?\" 그는 낮은 목소리로, 입술 위에 거룩한 침묵의 무게를 느끼는 사람처럼 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["Balthasar looked at him kindly, and replied, his mind not entirely freed from its abstraction,", "발타사르는 그를 다정하게 바라보고, 아직 넋을 다 추스르지 못한 마음으로 대답했다."]], [["“In my house on the Nile, so close to the river that the passers-by in boats see it and its reflection in the water at the same time—in my house, a few weeks ago, I sat thinking.", "\"나일강 가에 있는 내 집, 강에 어찌나 가까운지 배를 타고 지나는 사람들이 그 집과 물에 비친 그림자를 한꺼번에 볼 수 있는 내 집에서, 몇 주 전에 나는 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있었소."], ["A man thirty years old, I said to myself, should have his fields of life all ploughed, and his planting well done; for after that it is summer-time, with space scarce enough to ripen his sowing.", "나는 스스로에게 말했소. 서른 살 된 사람이라면 제 삶의 밭을 죄다 갈아 두고 씨앗도 잘 심어 두었어야 한다고. 그 뒤로는 여름철이니, 뿌린 것을 익힐 만한 시간이 빠듯하기 때문이오."], ["The Child, I said further, is now twenty-seven—his time to plant must be at hand.", "또 나는 말했소. 그 아기는 이제 스물일곱이니, 씨 뿌릴 때가 가까웠을 것이라고."], ["I asked myself, as you here asked me, my son, and answered by coming hither, as to a good resting-place close by the land thy fathers had from God.", "나는 스스로에게 물었소. 내 아들아, 그대가 여기서 내게 물은 것처럼 말이오. 그리고 그대 조상들이 하느님께로부터 받은 땅 가까이 있는 좋은 안식처로 여겨 이곳으로 옴으로써 그 물음에 답했소."], ["Where else should he appear, if not in Judea?", "유대 땅이 아니라면 그가 어디서 나타나겠소?"], ["In what city should he begin his work, if not in Jerusalem?", "예루살렘이 아니라면 어느 도시에서 그의 일을 시작하겠소?"], ["Who should be first to receive the blessings he is to bring, if not the children of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; in love, at least, the children of the Lord?", "아브라함과 이삭과 야곱의 자손, 적어도 사랑에 있어서는 주님의 자녀인 그들이 아니라면, 그가 가져올 축복을 맨 먼저 받을 자가 누구겠소?"], ["If I were bidden go seek him, I would search well the hamlets and villages on the slopes of the mountains of Judea and Galilee falling eastwardly into the valley of the Jordan.", "만일 내가 그를 찾아 나서라는 명을 받는다면, 나는 동쪽으로 요르단 골짜기를 향해 내리뻗은 유대와 갈릴리 산기슭의 작은 마을과 동네들을 샅샅이 뒤지겠소."], ["He is there now. Standing in a door or on a hill-top, only this evening he saw the sun set one day nearer the time when he himself shall become the light of the world.”", "그는 지금 거기 있소. 어느 문가나 언덕마루에 서서, 바로 오늘 저녁에도 그는 자신이 세상의 빛이 될 그때에 하루 더 가까워진 해가 지는 것을 보았을 것이오.\""]], [["Balthasar ceased, with his hand raised and finger pointing as if at Judea.", "발타사르는 마치 유대 땅을 가리키듯 손을 들어 손가락을 뻗은 채 말을 그쳤다."], ["All the listeners, even the dull servants outside the divan, affected by his fervor, were startled as if by a majestic presence suddenly apparent within the tent.", "듣고 있던 모든 이가, 자리 밖에 있던 둔한 하인들마저도 그의 열정에 사로잡혀, 천막 안에 갑자기 어떤 장엄한 존재가 나타나기라도 한 듯 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Nor did the sensation die away at once: of those at the table, each sat awhile thinking.", "그 느낌은 곧장 가시지 않았다. 자리에 앉은 이들은 저마다 한동안 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["The spell was finally broken by Ben-Hur.", "마침내 벤허가 그 주문을 깨뜨렸다."]], [["“I see, good Balthasar,”", "\"알겠습니다, 선한 발타사르 어르신.\""], ["he said, “that thou hast been much and strangely favored.", "그가 말했다. \"어르신께서는 참으로 크고 기이한 은총을 입으셨군요."], ["I see, also, that thou art a wise man indeed.", "또한 어르신께서 진실로 지혜로운 분이심도 알겠습니다."], ["It is not in my power to tell how grateful I am for the things thou hast told me.", "어르신께서 들려주신 말씀에 제가 얼마나 감사하는지 이루 다 말할 수 없습니다."], ["I am warned of the coming of great events, and borrow somewhat from thy faith.", "저는 큰일이 다가오고 있음을 일깨움받았고, 어르신의 믿음을 얼마간 빌렸습니다."], ["Complete the obligation, I pray thee, by telling further of the mission of him for whom thou art waiting, and for whom from this night I too shall wait as becomes a believing son of Judah.", "청컨대, 어르신께서 기다리시는 그분, 그리고 오늘 밤부터 저 또한 유대의 믿는 아들답게 기다릴 그분의 사명에 대해 더 말씀해 주셔서 이 은혜를 마저 채워 주십시오."], ["He is to be a Savior, thou saidst; is he not to be King of the Jews also?”", "그분은 구세주가 되시리라 하셨는데, 또한 유대인의 왕도 되시지 않습니까?\""]], [["“My son,”", "\"내 아들아,\""], ["said Balthasar, in his benignant way, “the mission is yet a purpose in the bosom of God.", "발타사르가 그 자애로운 어조로 말했다. \"그 사명은 아직 하느님의 품 안에 있는 뜻이오."], ["All I think about it is wrung from the words of the Voice in connection with the prayer to which they were in answer.", "내가 그에 관해 생각하는 모든 것은, 그 음성의 말씀과 그 말씀이 답으로 주어진 기도를 견주어 짜낸 것이오."], ["Shall we refer to them again?”", "그것들을 다시 짚어 볼까?\""]], [["“Thou art the teacher.”", "\"어르신께서 가르치시는 분이십니다.\""]], [["“The cause of my disquiet,”", "\"내 불안의 원인은—\""], ["Balthasar began, calmly—“that which made me a preacher in Alexandria and in the villages of the Nile; that which drove me at last into the solitude where the Spirit found me—was the fallen condition of men, occasioned, as I believed, by loss of the knowledge of God.", "발타사르가 차분히 시작했다. \"내가 알렉산드리아와 나일강의 마을들에서 설교자가 되게 한 것, 마침내 나를 고독 속으로 몰아넣어 그곳에서 성령이 나를 찾게 한 것은, 사람들의 타락한 처지였소. 내가 믿기로 그것은 하느님을 아는 앎을 잃었기 때문이었소."], ["I sorrowed for the sorrows of my kind—not of one class, but all of them.", "나는 내 동족의 슬픔을 슬퍼했소—한 계층만이 아니라 그들 모두의 슬픔을."], ["So utterly were they fallen it seemed to me there could be no Redemption unless God himself would make it his work; and I prayed him to come, and that I might see him.", "그들은 어찌나 철저히 타락했던지, 하느님 자신이 손수 그 일을 떠맡지 않으시는 한 구속이란 있을 수 없으리라 여겨졌소. 그래서 나는 그분께 오시기를, 그리고 내가 그분을 뵐 수 있기를 기도했소."], ["‘Thy good works have conquered.", "'네 선한 행실이 이겼느니라. 구속이 다가오느니라."], ["The Redemption cometh; thou shalt see the Savior’—thus the Voice spake; and with the answer I went up to Jerusalem rejoicing.", "너는 구세주를 보리라.' 그 음성은 이렇게 말씀하셨소. 그 응답을 안고 나는 기뻐하며 예루살렘으로 올라갔소."], ["Now, to whom is the Redemption?", "자, 그 구속은 누구를 위한 것이오?"], ["To all the world.", "온 세상을 위한 것이오."], ["And how shall it be?", "그러면 어떻게 이루어지겠소?"], ["Strengthen thy faith, my son!", "그대의 믿음을 굳건히 하시오, 내 아들아!"], ["Men say, I know, that there will be no happiness until Rome is razed from her hills.", "나도 아오, 사람들은 로마가 그 언덕들에서 헐려 없어지기 전에는 행복이 없으리라고 말하오."], ["That is to say, the ills of the time are not, as I thought them, from ignorance of God, but from the misgovernment of rulers.", "다시 말해, 이 시대의 병폐는 내가 여겼던 것처럼 하느님을 모르는 데서 오는 것이 아니라, 통치자들의 그릇된 다스림에서 온다는 거요."], ["Do we need to be told that human governments are never for the sake of religion?", "인간의 정부가 결코 종교를 위한 것이 아님을 굳이 누가 일러 줘야 하겠소?"], ["How many kings have you heard of who were better than their subjects? Oh no, no!", "그대는 제 백성보다 나은 왕을 몇이나 들어 보았소? 오, 아니오, 아니오!"], ["The Redemption cannot be for a political purpose—to pull down rulers and powers, and vacate their places merely that others may take and enjoy them.", "구속은 정치적 목적을 위한 것일 수 없소—통치자와 권세를 끌어내리고, 단지 다른 자들이 그 자리를 차지하여 누리게 하려고 자리를 비우는 것일 수 없소."], ["If that were all of it, the wisdom of God would cease to be surpassing.", "만일 그게 전부라면, 하느님의 지혜는 더 이상 견줄 데 없는 것이기를 그칠 것이오."], ["I tell you, though it be but the saying of blind to blind, he that comes is to be a Savior of souls; and the Redemption means God once more on earth, and righteousness, that his stay here may be tolerable to himself.”", "내 그대에게 이르노니, 비록 이것이 눈먼 자가 눈먼 자에게 하는 말일지라도, 오시는 그분은 영혼의 구세주가 되실 것이오. 그리고 구속이란 하느님께서 다시 한 번 이 땅에 오심을 뜻하고, 의로움을 뜻하니, 그분이 여기 머무심을 그분 스스로 견디실 만하게 하기 위함이오.\""]], [["Disappointment showed plainly on Ben-Hur’s face—his head drooped; and if he was not convinced, he yet felt himself incapable that moment of disputing the opinion of the Egyptian.", "벤허의 얼굴에는 실망이 또렷이 드러났다—그의 고개가 떨구어졌다. 설득되지는 않았더라도, 그는 그 순간 이집트인의 견해에 맞서 논박할 기력이 없음을 느꼈다."], ["Not so Ilderim.", "일데림은 그렇지 않았다."]], [["“By the splendor of God!”", "\"하느님의 영광에 두고 말하건대!\""], ["he cried, impulsively, “the judgment does away with all custom.", "그가 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"그 판단은 모든 관습을 뒤엎는 것이오."], ["The ways of the world are fixed, and cannot be changed.", "세상의 이치는 정해져 있어 바뀔 수 없소."], ["There must be a leader in every community clothed with power, else there is no reform.”", "어느 공동체에나 권력을 입은 지도자가 있어야 하오. 그렇지 않으면 개혁이란 없소.\""]], [["Balthasar received the burst gravely.", "발타사르는 그 격한 말을 진중하게 받았다."]], [["“Thy wisdom, good sheik, is of the world; and thou dost forget that it is from the ways of the world we are to be redeemed.", "\"선한 족장이여, 그대의 지혜는 세상의 것이오. 그리고 그대는 우리가 구속받아야 할 바가 바로 세상의 이치임을 잊고 있소."], ["Man as a subject is the ambition of a king; the soul of a man for its salvation is the desire of a God.”", "사람을 신민으로 삼는 것은 왕의 야심이오. 사람의 영혼을 그 구원으로 이끄는 것은 하느님의 바람이오.\""]], [["Ilderim, though silenced, shook his head, unwilling to believe.", "일데림은 입을 다물었으나, 믿으려 하지 않고 고개를 저었다."], ["Ben-Hur took up the argument for him.", "벤허가 그를 대신해 그 논의를 이어받았다."]], [["“Father—I call thee such by permission,”", "\"아버지여—허락하신 대로 그렇게 부르겠습니다.\""], ["he said—“for whom wert thou required to ask at the gates of Jerusalem?”", "그가 말했다. \"어르신께서는 예루살렘 성문에서 누구를 찾으라는 명을 받으셨습니까?\""]], [["The sheik threw him a grateful look.", "족장이 그에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“I was to ask of the people,”", "\"나는 사람들에게 물으라는 명을 받았소.\""], ["said Balthasar, quietly, “‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’”", "발타사르가 조용히 말했다. \"'유대인의 왕으로 나신 이가 어디 계시냐?'고 말이오.\""]], [["“And you saw him in the cave by Bethlehem?”", "\"그리고 베들레헴 곁 동굴에서 그를 보셨고요?\""]], [["“We saw and worshipped him, and gave him presents—Melchior, gold; Gaspar, frankincense; and I, myrrh.”", "\"우리는 그를 보고 경배하고, 그에게 예물을 드렸소—멜키오르는 황금을, 가스파르는 유향을, 그리고 나는 몰약을 드렸소.\""]], [["“When thou dost speak of fact, O father, to hear thee is to believe,”", "\"어르신께서 사실을 말씀하실 때는, 듣는 것이 곧 믿는 것입니다, 아버지여.\""], ["said Ben-Hur; “but in the matter of opinion, I cannot understand the kind of king thou wouldst make of the Child—I cannot separate the ruler from his powers and duties.”", "벤허가 말했다. \"하지만 견해의 문제에서는, 어르신께서 그 아기를 어떤 종류의 왕으로 삼으려 하시는지 저로서는 이해할 수가 없습니다. 저는 그 통치자를 그의 권세와 의무에서 떼어 놓고 생각할 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Son,”", "\"아들아,\""], ["said Balthasar, “we have the habit of studying closely the things which chance to lie at our feet, giving but a look at the greater objects in the distance.", "발타사르가 말했다. \"우리는 우연히 제 발치에 놓인 것들은 가까이 들여다보면서, 멀리 있는 더 큰 것들에는 흘끗 눈길만 던지는 버릇이 있소."], ["Thou seest now but the title—KING OF THE JEWS; wilt thou lift thine eyes to the mystery beyond it, the stumbling-block will disappear.", "그대는 지금 그 칭호만을—유대인의 왕이라는 것만을 보고 있소. 그러나 그 너머의 신비로 눈을 들어 올린다면, 그 걸림돌은 사라질 것이오."], ["Of the title, a word.", "그 칭호에 대해 한마디 하리다."], ["Thy Israel hath seen better days—days in which God called thy people endearingly his people, and dealt with them through prophets.", "그대의 이스라엘은 더 나은 시절을 보았소—하느님께서 그대의 백성을 사랑스레 자신의 백성이라 부르시고, 예언자들을 통해 그들을 다루시던 시절을 말이오."], ["Now, if in those days he promised them the Savior I saw—promised him as KING OF THE JEWS—the appearance must be according to the promise, if only for the word’s sake.", "자, 만일 그 시절에 그분이 그들에게 내가 본 구세주를 약속하셨다면—유대인의 왕으로 약속하셨다면—그 나타남은 오직 그 말씀을 위해서라도 그 약속에 따른 것이어야 하오."], ["Ah, thou seest the reason of my question at the gate!—thou seest, and I will no more of it, but pass on.", "아, 그대는 내가 성문에서 던진 그 물음의 까닭을 알겠구려!—그대가 알았으니 나는 더 말하지 않고 넘어가겠소."], ["It may be, next, thou art regarding the dignity of the Child; if so, bethink thee—what is it to be a successor of Herod?—by the world’s standard of honor, what?", "다음으로, 그대는 어쩌면 그 아기의 위엄을 헤아리고 있을지도 모르겠소. 그렇다면 생각해 보시오—헤롯의 후계자가 된다는 것이 무엇이오? 세상의 영예 기준으로 보면, 그것이 무엇이오?"], ["Could not God better by his beloved? If thou canst think of the Almighty Father in want of a title, and stooping to borrow the inventions of men, why was I not bidden ask for a Cæsar at once?", "하느님께서 당신의 사랑하는 이를 그보다 더 낫게 하실 수 없으셨겠소? 만일 그대가 전능하신 아버지께서 칭호가 아쉬워 몸을 굽혀 인간의 발명품을 빌리신다고 생각할 수 있다면, 어찌하여 나는 곧장 카이사르를 찾으라는 명을 받지 않았겠소?"], ["Oh, for the substance of that whereof we speak, look higher, I pray thee!", "오, 우리가 말하는 것의 본질을 위해서라도, 청컨대 더 높은 곳을 보시오!"], ["Ask rather of what he whom we await shall be king; for I do tell, my son, that is the key to the mystery, which no man shall understand without the key.”", "차라리 우리가 기다리는 그분이 무엇의 왕이 되실지를 물으시오. 내 아들아, 그것이 바로 그 신비의 열쇠이며, 어떤 사람도 그 열쇠 없이는 그것을 이해하지 못하리라고 나는 말하오.\""]], [["Balthasar raised his eyes devoutly.", "발타사르는 경건하게 두 눈을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“There is a kingdom on the earth, though it is not of it—a kingdom of wider bounds than the earth—wider than the sea and the earth, though they were rolled together as finest gold and spread by the beating of hammers.", "\"이 땅 위에 한 왕국이 있소, 비록 이 땅에 속한 것은 아니지만 말이오—땅보다 더 넓은 경계를 지닌 왕국, 바다와 땅을 한데 말아 가장 고운 황금처럼 만들어 망치질로 두드려 펼친다 한들 그보다 더 넓은 왕국이오."], ["Its existence is a fact as our hearts are facts, and we journey through it from birth to death without seeing it; nor shall any man see it until he hath first known his own soul; for the kingdom is not for him, but for his soul.", "그 존재는 우리 마음이 사실인 것처럼 하나의 사실이오. 우리는 태어나서 죽기까지 그것을 지나가면서도 그것을 보지 못하오. 또한 어떤 사람도 먼저 제 영혼을 알기 전에는 그것을 보지 못하리니, 그 왕국은 그를 위한 것이 아니라 그의 영혼을 위한 것이기 때문이오."], ["And in its dominion there is glory such as hath not entered imagination—original, incomparable, impossible of increase.”", "그리고 그 다스림 안에는 상상에도 들어 본 적 없는 영광이 있으니—본디 그러하고, 견줄 데 없으며, 더는 보탤 수 없는 영광이오.\""]], [["“What thou sayest, father, is a riddle to me,”", "\"어르신께서 하시는 말씀은 제게 수수께끼입니다, 아버지여.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“I never heard of such a kingdom.”", "\"저는 그런 왕국에 대해 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Nor did I,” said Ilderim.", "\"나도 들어 본 적이 없소.\" 일데림이 말했다."]], [["“And I may not tell more of it,”", "\"그리고 나도 그에 대해 더는 말할 수 없소.\""], ["Balthasar added, humbly dropping his eyes.", "발타사르가 겸손히 눈을 떨구며 덧붙였다."], ["“What it is, what it is for, how it may be reached, none can know until the Child comes to take possession of it as his own.", "\"그것이 무엇인지, 무엇을 위한 것인지, 어떻게 거기에 이를 수 있는지는, 그 아기가 그것을 자신의 것으로 차지하러 올 때까지 아무도 알 수 없소."], ["He brings the key of the viewless gate, which he will open for his beloved, among whom will be all who love him, for of such only the redeemed will be.”", "그는 보이지 않는 문의 열쇠를 가져오니, 자신의 사랑하는 이들을 위해 그 문을 열어 줄 것이오. 그들 가운데에는 그를 사랑하는 모든 이가 있으리니, 오직 그러한 이들만이 구속받은 자가 될 것이기 때문이오.\""]], [["After that there was a long silence, which Balthasar accepted as the end of the conversation.", "그 뒤로 긴 침묵이 흘렀고, 발타사르는 그것을 대화가 끝났다는 뜻으로 받아들였다."]], [["“Good sheik,”", "\"선한 족장이여,\""], ["he said, in his placid way, “to-morrow or the next day I will go up to the city for a time.", "그가 그 평온한 어조로 말했다. \"내일이나 모레쯤 나는 한동안 도시로 올라가려 하오."], ["My daughter wishes to see the preparations for the games.", "내 딸이 경기 준비하는 모습을 보고 싶어 하오."], ["I will speak further about the time of our going.", "우리가 떠날 때에 대해서는 다시 이야기하리다."], ["And, my son, I will see you again.", "그리고 내 아들아, 그대를 다시 보겠소."], ["To you both, peace and good-night.”", "두 분께, 평안과 안녕히 주무시기를 비오.\""]], [["They all arose from the table.", "그들은 모두 자리에서 일어났다."], ["The sheik and Ben-Hur remained looking after the Egyptian until he was conducted out of the tent.", "족장과 벤허는 이집트인이 천막 밖으로 안내되어 나갈 때까지 그 뒷모습을 바라보며 남아 있었다."]], [["“Sheik Ilderim,”", "\"일데림 족장이여,\""], ["said Ben-Hur then, “I have heard strange things tonight.", "그제야 벤허가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 저는 기이한 이야기들을 들었습니다."], ["Give me leave, I pray, to walk by the lake that I may think of them.”", "청컨대, 그것들을 곰곰이 생각할 수 있도록 호숫가를 거닐게 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Go; and I will come after you.”", "\"가시오. 나도 뒤따라 가리다.\""]], [["They washed their hands again; after which, at a sign from the master, a servant brought Ben-Hur his shoes, and directly he went out.", "그들은 다시 손을 씻었다. 그러고 나서 주인의 신호에 하인이 벤허에게 신을 가져다주었고, 그는 곧장 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVII", "# CHAPTER XVII"]], [["Up a little way from the dower there was a cluster of palms, which threw its shade half in the water, half on the land.", "둑에서 조금 위쪽에 야자나무 한 무리가 있어, 그 그늘을 반쯤은 물 위에, 반쯤은 뭍에 드리우고 있었다."], ["A bulbul sang from the branches a song of invitation.", "가지에서는 불불새 한 마리가 부르는 듯한 노래를 불렀다."], ["Ben-Hur stopped beneath to listen.", "벤허는 그 아래 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["At any other time the notes of the bird would have driven thought away; but the story of the Egyptian was a burden of wonder, and he was a laborer carrying it, and, like other laborers, there was to him no music in the sweetest music until mind and body were happily attuned by rest.", "다른 때였다면 그 새의 가락이 온갖 상념을 몰아냈으리라. 그러나 이집트인의 이야기는 경이로 가득한 짐이었고, 그는 그 짐을 진 일꾼이었다. 그리고 여느 일꾼이 그러하듯, 그에게는 마음과 몸이 휴식으로 흐뭇하게 어우러지기 전까지는 그 어떤 감미로운 음악도 음악이 아니었다."]], [["The night was quiet.", "밤은 고요했다."], ["Not a ripple broke upon the shore.", "물가에는 잔물결 하나 일지 않았다."], ["The old stars of the old East were all out, each in its accustomed place; and there was summer everywhere—on land, on lake, in the sky.", "옛 동방의 오래된 별들이 저마다 제자리에 떠올라 있었다. 그리고 온 세상에 여름이 깃들어 있었으니—뭍에도, 호수에도, 하늘에도."]], [["Ben-Hur’s imagination was heated, his feelings aroused, his will all unsettled.", "벤허의 상상은 달아올랐고, 그의 감정은 들끓었으며, 그의 의지는 온통 흔들렸다."]], [["So the palms, the sky, the air, seemed to him of the far south zone into which Balthasar had been driven by despair for men; the lake, with its motionless surface, was a suggestion of the Nilotic mother by which the good man stood praying when the Spirit made its radiant appearance.", "그리하여 야자나무도, 하늘도, 대기도 그에게는 사람에 대한 절망에 떠밀려 발타사르가 흘러든 저 머나먼 남쪽 지대의 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그 잔잔한 수면을 지닌 호수는, 성령이 빛나는 모습으로 나타났을 때 그 선한 사람이 곁에 서서 기도하던 나일의 어머니 강을 떠올리게 했다."], ["Had all these accessories of the miracle come to Ben-Hur?", "그 기적에 딸린 이 모든 정경이 벤허에게 찾아온 것인가?"], ["or had he been transferred to them?", "아니면 그가 그 정경 속으로 옮겨진 것인가?"], ["And what if the miracle should be repeated—and to him?", "그리고 만일 그 기적이 되풀이된다면—그것도 그에게 일어난다면?"], ["He feared, yet wished, and even waited for the vision.", "그는 두려워하면서도 바랐고, 그 환영을 기다리기까지 했다."], ["When at last his feverish mood was cooled, permitting him to become himself, he was able to think.", "마침내 그 열에 들뜬 마음이 가라앉아 제정신으로 돌아오게 되었을 때, 비로소 그는 생각할 수 있었다."]], [["His scheme of life has been explained.", "그가 세운 삶의 계획은 이미 설명한 바 있다."], ["In all reflection about it heretofore there had been one hiatus which he had not been able to bridge or fill up—one so broad he could see but vaguely to the other side of it.", "지금까지 그것을 곱씹을 때마다 그가 끝내 잇거나 메울 수 없었던 하나의 틈이 있었으니—어찌나 넓은지 건너편이 어렴풋이 보일 뿐이었다."], ["When, finally, he was graduated a captain as well as a soldier, to what object should he address his efforts?", "마침내 그가 군인일 뿐만 아니라 지휘관으로까지 다듬어졌을 때, 그는 어떤 목표를 향해 자기의 힘을 쏟아야 하는가?"], ["Revolution he contemplated, of course; but the processes of revolution have always been the same, and to lead men into them there have always been required, first, a cause or presence to enlist adherents; second, an end, or something as a practical achievement.", "물론 그는 혁명을 꿈꾸고 있었다. 그러나 혁명의 과정이란 예나 지금이나 한결같으니, 사람들을 그 속으로 이끌려면 언제나 먼저 추종자를 모을 명분 혹은 구심점이 필요하고, 다음으로는 목적, 곧 실제로 이룰 무언가가 필요했다."], ["As a rule he fights well who has wrongs to redress; but vastly better fights he who, with wrongs as a spur, has also steadily before him a glorious result in prospect—a result in which he can discern balm for wounds, compensation for valor, remembrance and gratitude in the event of death.", "대체로 바로잡을 부당함을 지닌 자는 잘 싸운다. 그러나 부당함을 채찍으로 삼는 동시에, 눈앞에 영광스러운 결과를 한결같이 그려 보는 자—상처를 어루만질 위안과, 용맹에 대한 보상과, 죽음을 맞을 때의 추억과 감사를 그 결과 속에서 읽어 낼 수 있는 자야말로 한층 더 잘 싸운다."]], [["To determine the sufficiency of either the cause or the end, it was needful that Ben-Hur should study the adherents to whom he looked when all was ready for action.", "명분이든 목적이든 그것이 충분한지를 가늠하려면, 벤허는 모든 준비가 갖추어져 행동에 나설 때 의지하게 될 추종자들을 살펴보아야 했다."], ["Very naturally, they were his countrymen.", "지극히 당연하게도, 그들은 그의 동족이었다."], ["The wrongs of Israel were to every son of Abraham, and each one was a cause vastly holy, vastly inspiring.", "이스라엘의 부당함은 아브라함의 모든 자손에게 닥친 일이었고, 저마다 그것은 더없이 거룩하고 더없이 가슴을 뒤흔드는 명분이었다."]], [["Ay, the cause was there; but the end—what should it be?", "그렇다, 명분은 거기 있었다. 그러나 목적은—그것은 무엇이어야 하는가?"]], [["The hours and days he had given this branch of his scheme were past calculation—all with the same conclusion—a dim, uncertain, general idea of national liberty.", "이 계획의 이 갈래에 그가 바친 시간과 나날은 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많았으나—그 모두가 똑같은 결론에 이르렀으니—민족의 자유라는 흐릿하고 막연하고 두루뭉술한 관념이었다."], ["Was it sufficient?", "그것으로 충분한가?"], ["He could not say no, for that would have been the death of his hope; he shrank from saying yes, because his judgment taught him better.", "그는 아니라고 말할 수 없었으니, 그리 말하면 그의 희망은 죽고 말 터였다. 또한 그는 그렇다고 말하기를 주저했으니, 그의 판단이 더 나은 것을 가르쳐 주었기 때문이다."], ["He could not assure himself even that Israel was able single-handed to successfully combat Rome.", "이스라엘이 홀로 로마와 맞서 싸워 이겨 낼 수 있으리라는 것조차 그는 스스로 장담할 수 없었다."], ["He knew the resources of that great enemy; he knew her art was superior to her resources.", "그는 그 거대한 적의 자원을 알았고, 그 자원보다 그 기술이 더 뛰어남을 알았다."], ["A universal alliance might suffice, but, alas!", "온 세상이 동맹을 맺는다면 충분하겠으나, 아아!"], ["that was impossible, except—and upon the exception how long and earnestly he had dwelt!—except a hero would come from one of the suffering nations, and by martial successes accomplish a renown to fill the whole earth.", "그것은 불가능했다—다만 한 가지 경우는 예외였다. 그리고 그는 그 예외를 두고 얼마나 오래, 얼마나 간절히 생각에 잠겼던가!—고난받는 민족들 가운데 하나에서 한 영웅이 나타나, 전쟁의 승리로 온 땅을 가득 채울 명성을 이룩하는 경우만은 예외였다."], ["What glory to Judea could she prove the Macedonia of the new Alexander!", "유대가 새로운 알렉산드로스의 마케도니아임을 증명할 수 있다면, 그 영광이 얼마나 크겠는가!"], ["Alas, again! Under the rabbis valor was possible, but not discipline.", "그러나 아아, 다시금! 랍비들 아래서는 용맹은 가능했으나 규율은 가능하지 않았다."], ["And then the taunt of Messala in the garden of Herod—“All you conquer in the six days, you lose on the seventh.”", "그리고 헤롯의 정원에서 메살라가 던진 비웃음—\"네가 엿새 동안 정복한 것은 이레째에 모조리 잃는다.\""]], [["So it happened he never approached the chasm thinking to surmount it, but he was beaten back; and so incessantly had he failed in the object that he had about given it over, except as a thing of chance.", "그리하여 그는 그 틈을 뛰어넘으리라 마음먹고 다가설 때마다 번번이 물러나야 했다. 그리고 그 목표에서 어찌나 끊임없이 좌절했던지, 그는 그것을 거의 운에 맡길 일로 여기고 단념하다시피 했다."], ["The hero might be discovered in his day, or he might not.", "그 영웅이 자기 시대에 나타날 수도, 나타나지 않을 수도 있었다."], ["God only knew. Such his state of mind, there need be no lingering upon the effect of Malluch’s skeleton recital of the story of Balthasar.", "오직 하느님만이 아셨다. 그의 마음이 이러했으니, 발타사르의 이야기를 말루크가 뼈대만 간추려 들려준 것이 그에게 미친 영향에 대해서는 굳이 길게 머무를 필요가 없다."], ["He heard it with a bewildering satisfaction—a feeling that here was the solution of the trouble—here was the requisite hero found at last; and he a son of the Lion tribe, and King of the Jews!", "그는 어리둥절할 만큼 흡족한 마음으로 그 이야기를 들었다—여기에 그 고민의 해답이 있다는 느낌, 그토록 필요했던 영웅이 마침내 여기서 발견되었다는 느낌이었다. 더구나 그는 사자 지파의 아들이요, 유대인의 왕이었다!"], ["Behind the hero, lo!", "그 영웅의 뒤에는, 보라!"], ["the world in arms.", "무장한 온 세상이 있었다."]], [["The king implied a kingdom; he was to be a warrior glorious as David, a ruler wise and magnificent as Solomon; the kingdom was to be a power against which Rome was to dash itself to pieces.", "왕이라 하면 곧 왕국을 뜻했다. 그는 다윗처럼 영광된 전사가 될 것이요, 솔로몬처럼 지혜롭고 위풍당당한 통치자가 될 것이었다. 그 왕국은 로마가 부딪쳐 산산조각 날 하나의 권능이 될 것이었다."], ["There would be colossal war, and the agonies of death and birth—then peace, meaning, of course, Judean dominion forever.", "거대한 전쟁이 있을 것이요, 죽음의 고통과 탄생의 진통이 따를 것이며—그런 다음에는 평화, 곧 두말할 것 없이 유대의 영원한 지배가 올 것이었다."]], [["Ben-Hur’s heart beat hard as for an instant he had a vision of Jerusalem the capital of the world, and Zion, the site of the throne of the Universal Master.", "벤허의 가슴은 세차게 뛰었으니, 한순간 그는 세계의 수도가 된 예루살렘과, 만유의 주재자의 보좌가 놓인 시온의 환영을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["It seemed to the enthusiast rare fortune that the man who had seen the king was at the tent to which he was going.", "이 열광에 빠진 사람에게는, 그 왕을 직접 본 사람이 바로 자기가 찾아가는 천막에 있다는 것이 더없는 행운으로 여겨졌다."], ["He could see him there, and hear him, and learn of him what all he knew of the coming change, especially all he knew of the time of its happening.", "거기서 그를 만나고, 그의 말을 듣고, 다가올 변혁에 관하여 그가 아는 모든 것을, 특히 그 일이 일어날 시기에 관하여 그가 아는 모든 것을 알아낼 수 있을 터였다."], ["If it were at hand, the campaign with Maxentius should be abandoned; and he would go and set about organizing and arming the tribes, that Israel might be ready when the great day of the restoration began to break.", "그것이 임박했다면, 막센티우스와의 원정은 포기할 것이며, 그는 가서 지파들을 조직하고 무장시키는 일에 착수하여, 회복의 위대한 날이 동터 오기 시작할 때 이스라엘이 채비를 갖추고 있도록 할 것이었다."]], [["Now, as we have seen, from Balthasar himself Ben-Hur had the marvelous story.", "그런데 우리가 이미 보았듯이, 벤허는 발타사르 본인에게서 그 놀라운 이야기를 들었다."], ["Was he satisfied?", "그는 만족했는가?"]], [["There was a shadow upon him deeper than that of the cluster of palms—the shadow of a great uncertainty, which—take note, O reader! which pertained more to the kingdom than the king.", "그의 위에는 야자나무 무리의 그늘보다 더 짙은 그늘이 드리워 있었으니—커다란 불확실성의 그늘이었다. 그리고 그것은—오, 독자여, 새겨 두라!—그것은 왕보다도 왕국에 더 깊이 닿아 있는 그늘이었다."]], [["“What of this kingdom?", "\"이 왕국이란 어떤 것인가?"], ["And what is it to be?”", "또 그것은 무엇이 될 것인가?\""], ["Ben-Hur asked himself in thought.", "벤허는 마음속으로 자신에게 물었다."]], [["Thus early arose the questions which were to follow the Child to his end, and survive him on earth—incomprehensible in his day, a dispute in this—an enigma to all who do not or cannot understand that every man is two in one—a deathless Soul and a mortal Body.", "이렇게 일찍부터, 그 아기를 그의 마지막까지 따라다니고 또 그가 떠난 뒤에도 이 땅에 남게 될 물음들이 솟아났다—그의 시대에는 헤아릴 수 없었고, 우리 시대에는 논쟁거리이며—사람이란 누구나 하나 속에 둘, 곧 죽지 않는 영혼과 죽어야 할 육체임을 깨닫지 못하거나 깨달을 수 없는 모든 이에게는 수수께끼인 물음들이었다."]], [["“What is it to be?”", "\"그것은 무엇이 될 것인가?\""], ["he asked.", "그는 물었다."]], [["For us, O reader, the Child himself has answered; but for Ben-Hur there were only the words of Balthasar, “On the earth, yet not of it—not for men, but for their souls—a dominion, nevertheless, of unimaginable glory.”", "오, 독자여, 우리에게는 그 아기 자신이 답해 주었다. 그러나 벤허에게는 오직 발타사르의 말만이 있었을 뿐이니, \"이 땅 위에 있되 이 땅에 속하지 않는 것—사람들을 위함이 아니라 그들의 영혼을 위한 것—그러면서도 상상할 수 없는 영광의 지배\"라는 말이었다."]], [["What wonder the hapless youth found the phrases but the darkening of a riddle?", "이 가엾은 젊은이가 그 말들을 한낱 수수께끼를 더 어둡게 만드는 것으로 받아들였다 한들 무엇이 이상하랴?"]], [["“The hand of man is not in it,”", "\"거기에는 사람의 손이 들어설 자리가 없다.\""], ["he said, despairingly.", "그는 절망하여 말했다."], ["“Nor has the king of such a kingdom use for men; neither toilers, nor councillors, nor soldiers.", "\"그런 왕국의 왕에게는 사람이 필요치 않다. 일꾼도, 의논할 신하도, 군사도 필요치 않다."], ["The earth must die or be made anew, and for government new principles must be discovered—something besides armed hands—something in place of Force. But what?”", "땅은 멸하거나 새로 지어져야 하고, 다스림에는 새로운 원리가 발견되어야 한다—무장한 손이 아닌 무언가, 힘을 대신할 무언가가. 그런데 그것이 무엇인가?\""]], [["Again, O reader!", "다시금, 오 독자여!"]], [["That which we will not see, he could not.", "우리가 보려 하지 않는 것을 그는 볼 수 없었다."], ["The power there is in Love had not yet occurred to any man; much less had one come saying directly that for government and its objects—peace and order—Love is better and mightier than Force.", "사랑 속에 깃든 권능은 아직 어떤 사람의 머릿속에도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 하물며 다스림과 그 목적—평화와 질서—을 위해서는 사랑이 힘보다 더 낫고 더 강하다고 곧바로 말하러 온 이는 더더욱 없었다."]], [["In the midst of his reverie a hand was laid upon his shoulder.", "그가 한창 상념에 잠겨 있을 때, 한 손이 그의 어깨 위에 얹혔다."]], [["“I have a word to say, O son of Arrius,”", "\"한마디 할 말이 있네, 오, 아리우스의 아들이여.\""], ["said Ilderim, stopping by his side—“a word, and then I must return, for the night is going.”", "일데림이 그의 곁에 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"한마디만 하고 나는 돌아가야 하네. 밤이 깊어 가니 말일세.\""]], [["“I give you welcome, sheik.”", "\"잘 오셨습니다, 족장님.\""]], [["“As to the things you have heard but now,”", "\"방금 자네가 들은 것들에 대해 말하자면,\""], ["said Ilderim, almost without pause, “take in belief all save that relating to the kind of kingdom the Child will set up when he comes; as to so much keep virgin mind until you hear Simonides the merchant—a good man here in Antioch, to whom I will make you known.", "일데림이 거의 쉬지도 않고 말을 이었다. \"그 아기가 와서 세울 왕국이 어떤 것인가에 관한 부분만 빼고는 모두 믿어 두게나. 그 부분만큼은 시모니데스 상인의 말을 들을 때까지 마음을 비워 두게—여기 안티오크의 선량한 사람이니, 내가 자네에게 소개해 주겠네."], ["The Egyptian gives you coinage of his dreams which are too good for the earth; Simonides is wiser; he will ring you the sayings of your prophets, giving book and page, so you cannot deny that the Child will be King of the Jews in fact—ay, by the splendor of God!", "이집트인은 자네에게 제 꿈을 주조하여 내미는데, 그 꿈이란 이 땅에 두기에는 너무 좋은 것일세. 시모니데스는 더 지혜로워, 자네 예언자들의 말씀을 책과 쪽수까지 짚어 가며 또렷이 울려 줄 것이니, 그 아기가 실제로 유대인의 왕이 되리라는 것을—그렇다, 하느님의 광휘에 걸고!"], ["a king as Herod was, only better and far more magnificent. And then, see you, we will taste the sweetness of vengeance.", "헤롯이 그러했던 것 같은 왕이로되, 다만 더 훌륭하고 훨씬 더 위풍당당한 왕이 되리라는 것을 자네는 부인하지 못할 걸세. 그러고 나면, 두고 보게나, 우리는 복수의 단맛을 맛보게 될 걸세."], ["I have said. Peace to you!”", "할 말은 다 했네. 평안하시게!\""]], [["“Stay—sheik!”", "\"잠깐만요—족장님!\""]], [["If Ilderim heard his call, he did not stay.", "일데림이 그 부름을 들었다 해도, 그는 멈추지 않았다."]], [["“Simonides again!”", "\"또 시모니데스라!\""], ["said Ben-Hur, bitterly.", "벤허는 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["“Simonides here, Simonides there; from this one now, then from that!", "\"여기서도 시모니데스, 저기서도 시모니데스. 이쪽에서도 그 사람, 저쪽에서도 그 사람이라니!"], ["I am like to be well ridden by my father’s servant, who knows at least to hold fast that which is mine; wherefore he is richer, if indeed he be not wiser, than the Egyptian.", "나는 아무래도 내 아버지의 종에게 단단히 휘둘리게 생겼구나. 적어도 내 것을 단단히 움켜쥘 줄은 아는 사람이니, 그래서 그가 이집트인보다 더 부유한 것이리라—설령 더 지혜롭지는 않다 하더라도 말이다."], ["By the covenant!", "언약에 걸고!"], ["it is not to the faithless a man should go to find a faith to keep—and I will not. But, hark!", "사람이 지킬 만한 믿음을 찾으러 갈 곳은 믿음 없는 자에게가 아니다—나는 가지 않겠다. 그런데, 가만히!"], ["singing—and the voice a woman’s—or an angel’s!", "노래로구나—그것도 여인의 목소리—아니면 천사의 목소리인가!"], ["It comes this way.”", "이쪽으로 다가오는구나.\""]], [["Down the lake towards the dower came a woman singing.", "호수를 따라 둑 쪽으로 노래를 부르며 한 여인이 다가오고 있었다."], ["Her voice floated along the hushed water melodious as a flute, and louder growing each instant.", "그녀의 목소리는 고요한 물 위를 피리처럼 가락 고르게 떠다니며, 시시각각 점점 커져 갔다."], ["Directly the dipping of oars was heard in slow measure; a little later the words were distinguishable—words in purest Greek, best fitted of all the tongues of the day for the expression of passionate grief.", "곧이어 노가 느린 박자로 물에 잠겼다 나오는 소리가 들려왔다. 조금 뒤에는 노랫말이 또렷이 들렸으니—당대의 모든 언어 가운데 격정에 찬 슬픔을 나타내기에 가장 알맞은, 더없이 순수한 그리스어로 된 노랫말이었다."]], [["THE LAMENT.", "비탄의 노래."], ["(Egyptian.)", "(이집트의 노래.)"]], [["I sigh as I sing for the story land Across the Syrian sea.", "나는 노래하며 한숨짓네, 저 옛이야기의 땅을 그리며 시리아의 바다 건너편 그 땅을."], ["The odorous winds from the musky sand Were breaths of life to me.", "사향내 어린 모래에서 불어오던 향기로운 바람은 내게 생명의 숨결이었네."], ["They play with the plumes of the whispering palm For me, alas!", "바람은 속삭이는 야자나무의 깃털을 어루만지건만 아아, 내게는 이제 없네."], ["no more; Nor more does the Nile in the moonlit calm Moan past the Memphian shore.", "달빛 어린 고요 속에 나일강이 멤피스의 기슭을 지나며 흐느끼던 일도 이제 없네."]], [["O Nilus! thou god of my fainting soul!", "오, 닐루스여! 스러져 가는 내 영혼의 신이여!"], ["In dreams thou comest to me; And, dreaming, I play with the lotus bowl, And sing old songs to thee; And hear from afar the Memnonian strain, And calls from dear Simbel; And wake to a passion of grief and pain That e’er I said—Farewell!", "꿈속에서 그대 내게 찾아오니, 나는 꿈을 꾸며 연꽃 잔을 가지고 놀고, 그대에게 옛 노래를 불러 주네. 멀리서 멤논의 가락을 들으며, 그리운 심벨의 부름을 들으며, 잠에서 깨어나 슬픔과 아픔에 사무치네— 내가 일찍이 작별을 고했음에!"]], [["At the conclusion of the song the singer was past the cluster of palms.", "노래가 끝났을 때 노래하던 이는 이미 야자나무 무리를 지나간 뒤였다."], ["The last word—farewell—floated past Ben-Hur weighted with all the sweet sorrow of parting.", "마지막 한마디—작별—이 이별의 온갖 감미로운 슬픔을 가득 싣고 벤허의 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["The passing of the boat was as the passing of a deeper shadow into the deeper night.", "배가 지나가는 것은 마치 더 짙은 그늘 하나가 더 깊은 밤 속으로 지나가는 것과도 같았다."]], [["Ben-Hur drew a long breath hardly distinguishable from a sigh.", "벤허는 한숨과 거의 분간할 수 없는 긴 숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“I know her by the song—the daughter of Balthasar. How beautiful it was!", "\"노래를 듣고 그녀임을 알겠구나—발타사르의 딸이다. 그 노래는 어찌 그리 아름다웠던가!"], ["And how beautiful is she!”", "또 그녀는 어찌 그리 아름다운가!\""]], [["He recalled her large eyes curtained slightly by the drooping lids, the cheeks oval and rosy rich, the lips full and deep with dimpling in the corners, and all the grace of the tall lithe figure.", "그는 살짝 내리깐 눈꺼풀에 가려진 그녀의 큰 눈을, 갸름하고 발그레하니 윤기 도는 뺨을, 도톰하고 깊으며 입꼬리에 보조개가 옴폭 패는 입술을, 그리고 늘씬하고 나긋한 자태의 온갖 우아함을 떠올렸다."]], [["“How beautiful she is!”", "\"그녀는 어찌 그리 아름다운가!\""], ["he repeated.", "그는 거듭 말했다."]], [["And his heart made answer by a quickening of its movement.", "그러자 그의 심장이 한층 빨라진 고동으로 화답했다."]], [["Then, almost the same instant, another face, younger and quite as beautiful—more childlike and tender, if not so passionate—appeared as if held up to him out of the lake.", "그러다가 거의 같은 순간에, 또 다른 얼굴 하나가—더 앳되면서도 그에 못지않게 아름답고—그토록 격정적이지는 않더라도 더 어린아이답고 다정한 얼굴이, 마치 호수 밖에서 그에게 들어 올려진 듯 떠올랐다."]], [["“Esther!”", "\"에스델!\""], ["he said, smiling.", "그는 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“As I wished, a star has been sent to me.”", "\"내가 바라던 대로, 별 하나가 내게 보내졌구나.\""]], [["He turned, and passed slowly back to the tent.", "그는 몸을 돌려 천천히 천막으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["His life had been crowded with griefs and with vengeful preparations—too much crowded for love.", "그의 삶은 슬픔과 복수의 준비로 빼곡했다—사랑이 들어설 틈조차 없을 만큼 빼곡했다."], ["Was this the beginning of a happy change?", "이것이 행복한 변화의 시작인가?"]], [["And if the influence went with him into the tent, whose was it?", "그리고 그 영향이 그와 더불어 천막 안으로 들어갔다면, 그것은 누구의 것이었던가?"], ["Esther had given him a cup.", "에스델이 그에게 잔을 건넸다."], ["So had the Egyptian.", "이집트 여인도 그러했다."], ["And both had come to him at the same time under the palms.", "그리고 두 사람 모두 야자나무 아래서 같은 시각에 그에게 찾아왔다."]], [["Which?", "어느 쪽인가?"]], [["# BOOK FIFTH", "# BOOK FIFTH"]], [["“Only the actions of the just Smell sweet and blossom in the dust.” SHIRLEY.", "셜리. “다툼의 열기 속에서도 율법을 지키며,"]], [["“And, through the heat of conflict, keeps the law, In calmness made, and sees what he foresaw.” WORDSWORTH. CHAPTER I", "고요함 속에 이루어, 미리 본 바를 끝내 보느니라.” 워즈워스."]], [["The morning after the bacchanalia in the saloon of the palace, the divan was covered with young patricians.", "궁전 연회장에서 흥청거린 다음 날 아침, 긴 의자는 젊은 귀족들로 뒤덮여 있었다. 막센티우스가 오고, 도시가 그를 맞으러 몰려들지도 모른다. 군단이 무기와 갑옷의 위세를 떨치며 술피우스산에서 내려올지도 모른다."], ["Maxentius might come, and the city throng to receive him; the legion might descend from Mount Sulpius in glory of arms and armor; from Nymphaeum to Omphalus there might be ceremonial splendors to shame the most notable ever before seen or heard of in the gorgeous East; yet would the many continue to sleep ignominiously on the divan where they had fallen or been carelessly tumbled by the indifferent slaves; that they would be able to take part in the reception that day was about as possible as for the lay-figures in the studio of a modern artist to rise and go bonneted and plumed through the one, two, three of a waltz.", "님파이움에서 옴팔로스까지, 일찍이 이 화려한 동방에서 보거나 들은 그 어떤 광경도 무색하게 할 의식의 장관이 펼쳐질지도 모른다. 그래도 이들 가운데 많은 수는 자기가 쓰러진 자리, 혹은 무심한 노예들이 아무렇게나 굴려 놓은 그 긴 의자 위에서 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 계속 잠들어 있을 터였다. 그들이 그날 영접 행렬에 참여하기를 바라는 것은, 현대 화가의 화실에 놓인 인형이 일어나 모자를 쓰고 깃털을 꽂은 채 왈츠의 하나둘셋 박자를 밟으며 걸어가기를 바라는 것이나 다름없었다."]], [["Not all, however, who participated in the orgy were in the shameful condition.", "그러나 그 난잡한 잔치에 끼었던 자가 모두 그토록 수치스러운 꼴은 아니었다."], ["When dawn began to peer through the skylights of the saloon, Messala arose, and took the chaplet from his head, in sign that the revel was at end; then he gathered his robe about him, gave a last look at the scene, and, without a word, departed for his quarters.", "새벽빛이 연회장 천창으로 스며들기 시작하자, 메살라가 몸을 일으켜 머리에서 화환을 벗었으니, 흥청거림이 끝났다는 표시였다. 그러고는 옷자락을 여미고, 그 자리를 마지막으로 한 번 돌아본 뒤, 한마디 말도 없이 제 거처로 떠났다."], ["Cicero could not have retired with more gravity from a night-long senatorial debate.", "키케로라도 밤새 이어진 원로원 토론에서 그보다 더 무게 있게 물러나지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["Three hours afterwards two couriers entered his room, and from his own hand received each a despatch, sealed and in duplicate, and consisting chiefly of a letter to Valerius Gratus, the procurator, still resident in Cæsarea.", "세 시간 뒤, 두 명의 전령이 그의 방으로 들어와, 각각 그의 손에서 봉인된 사본 하나씩을 받았다. 그것은 주로 아직 카이사레아에 머물고 있는 총독 발레리우스 그라투스에게 보내는 편지였다."], ["The importance attached to the speedy and certain delivery of the paper may be inferred.", "그 문서를 빠르고 확실하게 전달하는 일에 얼마나 큰 비중이 실려 있었는지는 미루어 짐작할 만하다."], ["One courier was to proceed overland, the other by sea; both were to make the utmost haste.", "한 전령은 육로로, 다른 한 전령은 바닷길로 가게 되어 있었고, 둘 다 최대한 서둘러야 했다."]], [["It is of great concern now that the reader should be fully informed of the contents of the letter thus forwarded, and it is accordingly given:", "이제 독자가 그렇게 보내진 편지의 내용을 빠짐없이 알아 두는 것이 매우 중요하다. 그래서 여기에 그 전문을 싣는다. “안티오크, 7월 초하루 열이틀 전."]], [["“Messala to Gratus.", "“메살라가 그라투스에게."]], [["“O my Midas!", "“오, 나의 미다스여!"]], [["“I pray thou take no offense at the address, seeing it is one of love and gratitude, and an admission that thou art most fortunate among men; seeing, also, that thy ears are as they were derived from thy mother, only proportionate to thy matured condition.", "“이렇게 부르는 것을 부디 언짢게 여기지 마시오. 이는 사랑과 감사의 부름이며, 그대가 사람들 가운데 가장 복받은 자임을 인정하는 것이외다. 또한 그대의 두 귀가 어머니에게서 물려받은 그대로이되, 다만 그대의 무르익은 형편에 걸맞게 자랐음을 인정하는 것이기도 하오."]], [["“O my Midas!", "“오, 나의 미다스여!"]], [["“I have to relate to thee an astonishing event, which, though as yet somewhat in the field of conjecture, will, I doubt not, justify thy instant consideration.", "“그대에게 놀라운 사건 하나를 전하려 하오. 아직은 다소 추측의 영역에 머물러 있으나, 그대가 당장 깊이 헤아려 마땅한 일임을 나는 의심치 않소."]], [["“Allow me first to revive thy recollection.", "“먼저 그대의 기억을 되살리게 해 주시오."], ["Remember, a good many years ago, a family of a prince of Jerusalem, incredibly ancient and vastly rich—by name Ben-Hur.", "여러 해 전, 예루살렘의 어느 귀족 가문을 기억하시오. 믿기 어려울 만큼 유서 깊고 어마어마하게 부유했던 가문, 그 이름은 벤허였소."], ["If thy memory have a limp or ailment of any kind, there is, if I mistake not, a wound on thy head which may help thee to a revival of the circumstance.", "만일 그대의 기억에 어떤 절뚝거림이나 병통이 있다면, 내가 잘못 안 것이 아닌 한, 그 일을 되살리는 데 도움이 될 상처 하나가 그대의 머리에 남아 있을 것이오."]], [["“Next, to arouse thy interest. In punishment of the attempt upon thy life—for dear repose of conscience, may all the gods forbid it should ever prove to have been an accident!—the family were seized and summarily disposed of, and their property confiscated.", "“다음으로 그대의 흥미를 일깨워 보겠소. 그대의 목숨을 노린 시도에 대한 징벌로—양심의 안식을 위하여, 그것이 한낱 사고였음이 끝내 밝혀지는 일이 없도록 모든 신들이 막아 주시기를!—그 가문은 붙잡혀 즉각 처리되었고, 그 재산은 몰수되었소."], ["And inasmuch, O my Midas! as the action had the approval of our Cæsar, who was as just as he was wise—be there flowers upon his altars forever!—there should be no shame in referring to the sums which were realized to us respectively from that source, for which it is not possible I can ever cease to be grateful to thee, certainly not while I continue, as at present, in the uninterrupted enjoyment of the part which fell to me.", "그리고, 오 나의 미다스여, 그 조치가 슬기로우면서도 공정하셨던 우리 카이사르의 재가를 받았으니—그분의 제단에 영원토록 꽃이 피어나기를!—그 일에서 우리가 각자 거둬들인 액수를 언급하는 데에는 아무런 부끄러움이 없을 것이오. 나로서는 그 일로 그대에게 감사하기를 영영 멈출 수 없으리니, 적어도 지금처럼 내 몫으로 떨어진 것을 끊임없이 누리는 동안에는 분명히 그러할 것이오."]], [["“In vindication of thy wisdom—a quality for which, as I am now advised, the son of Gordius, to whom I have boldly likened thee, was never distinguished among men or gods—I recall further that thou didst make disposition of the family of Hur, both of us at the time supposing the plan hit upon to be the most effective possible for the purposes in view, which were silence and delivery over to inevitable but natural death.", "“그대의 지혜를 변호하건대—내가 지금 듣기로는, 내가 그대를 대담하게 견준 저 고르디우스의 아들은 사람들 사이에서도 신들 사이에서도 결코 그런 자질로 이름난 적이 없었다 하오—나는 그대가 허 가문을 어떻게 처분했는지 다시금 떠올리오. 그때 우리 둘 다 그 방책이 우리가 노린 목적, 곧 침묵과 피할 길 없되 자연스러운 죽음으로 넘겨 버리는 데 가장 효과적이라 여겼지요."], ["Thou wilt remember what thou didst with the mother and sister of the malefactor; yet, if now I yield to a desire to learn whether they be living or dead, I know, from knowing the amiability of thy nature, O my Gratus, that thou wilt pardon me as one scarcely less amiable than thyself.", "그대가 그 죄인의 어미와 누이를 어떻게 했는지 그대는 기억할 것이오. 허나 지금 내가 그들이 살았는지 죽었는지 알고 싶은 마음에 굴복한다 해도, 오 나의 그라투스여, 그대의 온화한 천성을 아는 터라, 그대 못지않게 온화하다 할 만한 이 사람을 그대가 너그러이 용서하리라 믿소."]], [["“As more immediately essential to the present business, however, I take the liberty of inviting to thy remembrance that the actual criminal was sent to the galleys a slave for life—so the precept ran; and it may serve to make the event which I am about to relate the more astonishing by saying here that I saw and read the receipt for his body delivered in course to the tribune commanding a galley.", "“그러나 지금의 일에 더 직결되는 것으로서, 나는 그 진짜 죄인이 종신 노예로 갤리선에 보내졌음을 그대의 기억에 불러일으키는 무례를 무릅쓰겠소. 명령이 그러했지요. 그리고 내가 이제 전하려는 사건을 한층 더 놀랍게 만들기 위하여 여기서 말해 두건대, 그자의 몸이 절차에 따라 어느 갤리선을 지휘하는 호민관에게 인도되었다는 영수증을 내가 직접 보고 읽었소."]], [["“Thou mayst begin now to give me more especial heed, O my most excellent Phrygian!", "“이제 그대는 내게 좀 더 각별한 주의를 기울이기 시작해도 좋소, 오 더없이 훌륭한 프리기아 사람이여!"]], [["“Referring to the limit of life at the oar, the outlaw thus justly disposed of should be dead, or, better speaking, some one of the three thousand Oceanides should have taken him to husband at least five years ago.", "“노 젓는 자의 수명의 한계로 미루어 볼 때, 그렇게 정당하게 처리된 무법자는 이미 죽었어야 마땅하오. 더 잘 말하자면, 삼천의 오케아니데스 가운데 하나가 적어도 다섯 해 전에 그를 지아비로 삼았어야 했소."], ["And if thou wilt excuse a momentary weakness, O most virtuous and tender of men! inasmuch as I loved him in childhood, and also because he was very handsome—I used in much admiration to call him my Ganymede—he ought in right to have fallen into the arms of the most beautiful daughter of the family.", "그리고, 오 사람들 가운데 가장 덕스럽고 다정한 이여, 한순간의 약한 마음을 부디 너그러이 봐주시오—내가 어린 시절 그를 사랑했고, 또한 그가 무척 잘생긴 까닭에—나는 크게 흠모하며 그를 내 가니메데스라 부르곤 했소—그는 마땅히 그 가문의 가장 아름다운 딸의 품에 안겼어야 옳았소."], ["Of opinion, however, that he was certainly dead, I have lived quite five years in calm and innocent enjoyment of the fortune for which I am in a degree indebted to him.", "그러나 그가 분명 죽었으리라 여겨, 나는 어느 정도 그의 덕에 얻은 재산을 다섯 해 동안 고요하고 떳떳하게 누리며 살아왔소."], ["I make the admission of indebtedness without intending it to diminish my obligation to thee.", "내가 빚을 졌음을 인정하되, 그렇다고 그대에 대한 내 의무를 줄이려는 뜻은 아니오."]], [["“Now I am at the very point of interest.", "“이제 바로 그 흥미로운 대목에 이르렀소."]], [["“Last night, while acting as master of the feast for a party just from Rome—their extreme youth and inexperience appealed to my compassion—I heard a singular story.", "“간밤에, 로마에서 갓 온 한 무리를 위해 내가 연회의 주관자 노릇을 하던 차에—그들의 지나친 젊음과 미숙함이 내 측은지심을 자아냈소—나는 기이한 이야기 하나를 들었소."], ["Maxentius, the consul, as you know, comes to-day to conduct a campaign against the Parthians.", "그대도 알다시피, 집정관 막센티우스가 오늘 와서 파르티아인을 칠 원정을 지휘할 것이오."], ["Of the ambitious who are to accompany him there is one, a son of the late duumvir Quintus Arrius.", "그를 따를 야심가들 가운데 하나가 고(故) 이두정관 퀸투스 아리우스의 아들이오."], ["I had occasion to inquire about him particularly.", "나는 그에 대해 특히 자세히 물어볼 일이 있었소."], ["When Arrius set out in pursuit of the pirates, whose defeat gained him his final honors, he had no family; when he returned from the expedition, he brought back with him an heir.", "아리우스가 해적을 추격하러 나섰을 때—그 해적들을 무찌른 일로 그는 마지막 영예를 얻었소—그에게는 가족이 없었소. 그러나 원정에서 돌아왔을 때, 그는 상속자를 하나 데려왔소."], ["Now be thou composed as becomes the owner of so many talents in ready sestertii!", "자, 그 많은 세스테르티우스를 현금으로 가진 자답게 부디 침착하시오!"], ["The son and heir of whom I speak is he whom thou didst send to the galleys—the very Ben-Hur who should have died at his oar five years ago—returned now with fortune and rank, and possibly as a Roman citizen, to— Well, thou art too firmly seated to be alarmed, but I, O my Midas! I am in danger—no need to tell thee of what.", "내가 말하는 그 아들이자 상속자가, 바로 그대가 갤리선으로 보낸 자—다섯 해 전에 자기 노 옆에서 죽었어야 마땅한 바로 그 벤허란 말이오—이제 재산과 신분을 갖추고, 어쩌면 로마 시민이 되어 돌아온 것이오, 그리하여— 흠, 그대야 자리가 워낙 굳건하니 놀랄 것이 없겠으나, 나는, 오 나의 미다스여, 나는 위태롭소—무엇 때문인지는 굳이 그대에게 말할 필요가 없을 것이오."], ["Who should know, if thou dost not?", "그대가 모른다면 누가 알겠소?"]], [["“Sayst thou to all this, tut-tut?", "“이 모든 것에 대해 그대는 ‘쯧쯧’이라 하겠소?"]], [["“When Arrius, the father, by adoption, of this apparition from the arms of the most beautiful of the Oceanides (see above my opinion of what she should be), joined battle with the pirates, his vessel was sunk, and but two of all her crew escaped drowning—Arrius himself and this one, his heir.", "“아리우스가, 그러니까 가장 아름다운 오케아니데스의 품에서 나타난 이 환영의 양아버지인 그자가(그녀가 어떠해야 했는지에 대한 내 견해는 위를 보시오) 해적과 맞붙어 싸웠을 때, 그의 배는 가라앉았고, 온 선원 가운데 단 둘만이 익사를 면했소—아리우스 본인과 그의 상속자인 이 한 사람이오."]], [["“The officers who took them from the plank on which they were floating say the associate of the fortunate tribune was a young man who, when lifted to the deck, was in the dress of a galley slave.", "“그들이 떠다니던 널빤지에서 그 둘을 건져 올린 장교들의 말로는, 운 좋은 호민관의 동료는 갑판에 끌어올렸을 때 갤리선 노예의 차림을 한 젊은이였다 하오."]], [["“This should be convincing, to say least; but lest thou say tut-tut again, I tell thee, O my Midas!", "“이쯤이면 적어도 설득력이 있을 것이오."], ["that yesterday, by good chance—I have a vow to Fortune in consequence—I met the mysterious son of Arrius face to face; and I declare now that, though I did not then recognize him, he is the very Ben-Hur who was for years my playmate; the very Ben-Hur who, if he be a man, though of the commonest grade, must this very moment of my writing be thinking of vengeance—for so would I were I he—vengeance not to be satisfied short of life; vengeance for country, mother, sister, self, and—I say it last, though thou mayst think it would be first—for fortune lost.", "그러나 그대가 또다시 ‘쯧쯧’ 할까 하여 말하는데, 오 나의 미다스여, 어제, 다행스러운 우연으로—그 덕에 나는 운명의 여신에게 한 가지 서원을 했소—나는 그 수수께끼 같은 아리우스의 아들과 얼굴을 맞대고 마주쳤소. 그리고 이제 분명히 말하건대, 비록 그때는 알아보지 못했으나 그자는 여러 해 동안 나의 놀이 동무였던 바로 그 벤허였소. 만약 그가 사내라면, 비록 가장 천한 부류일지라도, 내가 이 글을 쓰는 바로 이 순간에도 틀림없이 복수를 골똘히 생각하고 있을 그 벤허란 말이오—내가 그였다면 분명 그러했을 테니—목숨을 거두기 전에는 결코 채워지지 않을 복수, 조국을 위한, 어미를 위한, 누이를 위한, 자기 자신을 위한, 그리고—맨 마지막에 말하지만 그대는 첫째라 여길지도 모를—잃어버린 재산을 위한 복수를 말이오."]], [["“By this time, O good my benefactor and friend! my Gratus! in consideration of thy sestertii in peril, their loss being the worst which could befall one of thy high estate—I quit calling thee after the foolish old King of Phrygia—by this time, I say (meaning after having read me so far), I have faith to believe thou hast ceased saying tut-tut, and art ready to think what ought to be done in such emergency.", "“이쯤이면, 오 나의 선한 은인이자 벗인 그라투스여, 위태로워진 그대의 세스테르티우스를 생각해 보면—그것을 잃는 것이야말로 그대처럼 높은 신분을 가진 자에게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 일일 테니—나는 그대를 저 어리석은 프리기아의 늙은 왕에 빗대 부르기를 그만두겠소—이쯤이면, 다시 말해(여기까지 나를 읽고 나면) 그대가 ‘쯧쯧’ 소리를 멈추고, 이런 위급한 상황에서 무엇을 해야 할지 헤아릴 채비를 마쳤으리라 나는 믿어 의심치 않소."]], [["“It were vulgar to ask thee now what shall be done.", "“이제 그대에게 어찌해야 하느냐고 묻는 것은 천박한 일일 것이오."], ["Rather let me say I am thy client; or, better yet, thou art my Ulysses whose part it is to give me sound direction.", "차라리 내가 그대의 의뢰인이라 말하겠소. 아니, 더 낫게 말하자면, 그대는 나에게 옳은 길을 일러 주는 것이 소임인 나의 오디세우스요."]], [["“And I please myself thinking I see thee when this letter is put into thy hand.", "“이 편지가 그대의 손에 쥐어질 때의 그대를 떠올리며 나는 즐거워하오. 그대가 그것을 한 번 읽는 것을 나는 보오."], ["I see thee read it once; thy countenance all gravity, and then again with a smile; then, hesitation ended, and thy judgment formed, it is this, or it is that; wisdom like Mercury’s, promptitude like Cæsar’s.", "그대의 얼굴이 온통 진지함으로 가득하다가, 다시 한 번 읽을 때는 미소를 띠는 것을 보오. 그러고는 망설임이 끝나고 판단이 서면, 이리하라, 혹은 저리하라 하겠지요. 메르쿠리우스 같은 지혜와 카이사르 같은 결단으로 말이오."]], [["“The sun is now fairly risen.", "“이제 해가 제법 솟았소."], ["An hour hence two messengers will depart from my door, each with a sealed copy hereof; one of them will go by land, the other by sea, so important do I regard it that thou shouldst be early and particularly informed of the appearance of our enemy in this part of our Roman world.", "한 시간 뒤면 두 전령이 각자 이것의 봉인된 사본을 들고 내 문을 나설 것이오. 하나는 육로로, 다른 하나는 바닷길로 갈 것이니, 우리의 이 로마 세계 한구석에 우리의 적이 나타났음을 그대가 일찍, 그리고 각별히 알아야 한다고 내가 그만큼 중대하게 여기는 까닭이오."]], [["“I will await thy answer here.", "“나는 여기서 그대의 답을 기다리겠소."]], [["“Ben-Hur’s going and coming will of course be regulated by his master, the consul, who, though he exert himself without rest day and night, cannot get away under a month.", "“벤허의 오고 감은 물론 그의 주인인 집정관이 좌우할 것이오. 집정관은 밤낮없이 쉬지 않고 애를 쓴다 해도 한 달 안에는 떠날 수 없을 것이오."], ["Thou knowest what work it is to assemble and provide for an army destined to operate in a desolate, townless country.", "황량하고 도시 하나 없는 땅에서 작전을 펼칠 군대를 모으고 채비를 갖추는 일이 얼마나 큰 작업인지는 그대도 알 것이오."]], [["“I saw the Jew yesterday in the Grove of Daphne; and if he be not there now, he is certainly in the neighborhood, making it easy for me to keep him in eye.", "“나는 어제 다프네 숲에서 그 유대인을 보았소. 그가 지금 거기에 없다 해도, 분명 그 근방에 있어 내가 그를 눈에 담아 두기 쉬울 것이오."], ["Indeed, wert thou to ask me where he is now, I should say, with the most positive assurance, he is to be found at the old Orchard of Palms, under the tent of the traitor Sheik Ilderim, who cannot long escape our strong hand.", "정녕 그대가 지금 그가 어디 있느냐고 내게 묻는다면, 나는 더없이 확신에 차 말하겠소. 그는 저 오래된 종려 과수원에서, 우리의 강한 손길을 오래 피할 수 없을 반역자 셰이크 일데림의 천막 아래에 있다고 말이오."], ["Be not surprised if Maxentius, as his first measure, places the Arab on ship for forwarding to Rome.", "막센티우스가 첫 조치로 그 아랍인을 배에 실어 로마로 보낸다 한들 놀라지 마시오."]], [["“I am so particular about the whereabouts of the Jew because it will be important to thee, O illustrious! when thou comest to consider what is to be done; for already I know, and by the knowledge I flatter myself I am growing in wisdom, that in every scheme involving human action there are three elements always to be taken into account—time, place, and agency.", "“내가 그 유대인의 행방을 이토록 자세히 따지는 까닭은, 오 빛나는 이여, 그대가 무엇을 해야 할지 헤아릴 때 그것이 그대에게 긴요할 것이기 때문이오. 이미 나는 알고 있고, 그 앎으로써 내 지혜가 자라고 있다고 자부하건대, 사람의 행위가 얽힌 모든 책략에는 언제나 고려해야 할 세 가지 요소가 있소—때와 장소와 수단이 그것이오."]], [["“If thou sayest this is the place, have thou then no hesitancy in trusting the business to thy most loving friend, who would be thy aptest scholar as well.", "“만약 그대가 여기가 바로 그 장소라 한다면, 그 일을 그대를 더없이 아끼는 벗에게 맡기는 데 조금도 주저하지 마시오. 그 벗은 또한 그대의 가장 영민한 제자가 될 것이오. 메살라.”"]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["About the time the couriers departed from Messala’s door with the despatches (it being yet the early morning hour), Ben-Hur entered Ilderim’s tent.", "메살라의 집 앞에서 급사들이 전갈을 들고 떠날 무렵—아직 이른 아침이었다—벤허는 일데림의 천막으로 들어섰다."], ["He had taken a plunge into the lake, and breakfasted, and appeared now in an under-tunic, sleeveless, and with skirt scarcely reaching to the knee.", "그는 호수에 몸을 담갔다가 아침을 먹은 뒤였고, 지금은 소매 없는 속옷 차림에 자락이 무릎에도 닿지 않는 옷을 걸치고 있었다."]], [["The sheik saluted him from the divan.", "족장은 긴 의자에 앉아 그를 맞이했다."]], [["“I give thee peace, son of Arrius,”", "\"그대에게 평안이 있기를, 아리우스의 아들이여.\""], ["he said, with admiration, for, in truth, he had never seen a more perfect illustration of glowing, powerful, confident manhood.", "그는 감탄을 담아 말했다. 사실 그는 이토록 완벽하게 빛나고 강건하며 자신만만한 사내다움의 본보기를 본 적이 없었다."], ["“I give thee peace and good-will.", "\"그대에게 평안과 호의를 베푸노라."], ["The horses are ready, I am ready. And thou?”", "말들도 준비되었고, 나도 준비되었다. 그대는 어떤가?\""]], [["“The peace thou givest me, good sheik, I give thee in return.", "\"그대가 베푼 평안을, 어진 족장이여, 나도 그대에게 돌려드리겠소."], ["I thank thee for so much good-will. I am ready.”", "그토록 큰 호의에 감사하오. 나도 준비되었소.\""]], [["Ilderim clapped his hands.", "일데림이 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["“I will have the horses brought. Be seated.”", "\"말들을 데려오게 하겠다. 앉으라.\""]], [["“Are they yoked?”", "\"멍에는 메웠소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니다.\""]], [["“Then suffer me to serve myself,”", "\"그러면 내 손으로 직접 하게 해 주시오.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“It is needful that I make the acquaintance of thy Arabs.", "\"내가 그대의 아랍 말들과 친해질 필요가 있소."], ["I must know them by name, O sheik, that I may speak to them singly; nor less must I know their temper, for they are like men: if bold, the better of scolding; if timid, the better of praise and flattery.", "한 마리 한 마리에게 말을 걸 수 있도록 이름으로 알아 두어야 하오, 족장이여. 또한 그 못지않게 그들의 성미도 알아야 하니, 말도 사람과 같기 때문이오. 대담한 말이면 꾸짖는 편이 낫고, 겁이 많은 말이면 칭찬하고 추어 주는 편이 낫소."], ["Let the servants bring me the harness.”", "하인들에게 마구를 가져오라 해 주시오.\""]], [["“And the chariot?” asked the sheik.", "\"전차는?\" 족장이 물었다."]], [["“I will let the chariot alone to-day.", "\"오늘은 전차를 그냥 두겠소."], ["In its place, let them bring me a fifth horse, if thou hast it; he should be barebacked, and fleet as the others.”", "그 대신, 가지고 있다면 다섯째 말을 데려오게 해 주시오. 안장을 얹지 않은 채, 다른 말들 못지않게 날쌘 놈이어야 하오.\""]], [["Ilderim’s wonder was aroused, and he summoned a servant immediately.", "일데림의 호기심이 일었고, 그는 곧장 하인을 불렀다."]], [["“Bid them bring the harness for the four,”", "\"네 마리분 마구를 가져오라 일러라.\""], ["he said—“the harness for the four, and the bridle for Sirius.”", "그가 말했다. \"네 마리분 마구와, 시리우스의 굴레를 말이다.\""]], [["Ilderim then arose.", "그러고 나서 일데림이 일어섰다."]], [["“Sirius is my love, and I am his, O son of Arrius. We have been comrades for twenty years—in tent, in battle, in all stages of the desert we have been comrades.", "\"시리우스는 나의 사랑이요, 나는 그의 사랑이라네, 아리우스의 아들이여. 우리는 스무 해 동안 동지였지—천막에서, 전투에서, 사막의 온갖 고비마다 우리는 동지였다네."], ["I will show him to you.”", "그를 그대에게 보여 주겠다.\""]], [["Going to the division curtain, he held it, while Ben-Hur passed under.", "칸막이 휘장으로 가더니 그는 그것을 들어 올렸고, 벤허는 그 아래로 지나갔다."], ["The horses came to him in a body.", "말들이 한꺼번에 그에게 다가왔다."], ["One with a small head, luminous eyes, neck like the segment of a bended bow, and mighty chest, curtained thickly by a profusion of mane soft and wavy as a damsel’s locks, nickered low and gladly at sight of him.", "그중 한 마리는 작은 머리에 빛나는 눈, 휘어진 활의 한 도막 같은 목, 그리고 처녀의 머릿단처럼 부드럽고 물결치는 갈기가 두텁게 드리운 강건한 가슴을 지녔는데, 그를 보자 나직하고 반가운 울음소리를 냈다."]], [["“Good horse,”", "\"착한 말이다.\""], ["said the sheik, patting the dark-brown cheek.", "족장은 짙은 갈색 뺨을 토닥이며 말했다."], ["“Good horse, good-morning.”", "\"착한 말, 잘 잤느냐.\""], ["Turning then to Ben-Hur, he added, “This is Sirius, father of the four here.", "그러고는 벤허를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"이놈이 시리우스, 여기 네 마리의 아비라네."], ["Mira, the mother, awaits our return, being too precious to be hazarded in a region where there is a stronger hand than mine.", "어미인 미라는 우리가 돌아오기를 기다리고 있으니, 너무 귀해서 나보다 강한 손이 있는 곳에 위험을 무릅쓰게 할 수 없기 때문이지. 그리고 나는 의심스럽다네.\""], ["And much I doubt,”", "그는 말하면서 웃었다."], ["he laughed as he spoke—“much I doubt, O son of Arrius, if the tribe could endure her absence.", "\"참으로 의심스럽다네, 아리우스의 아들이여, 부족이 그녀의 부재를 견딜 수 있을지 말이야. 그녀는 그들의 자랑이요, 그들은 그녀를 떠받든다네."], ["She is their glory; they worship her; did she gallop over them, they would laugh.", "그녀가 그들 위로 내달린다 해도 그들은 웃을 걸세. 오늘 만 명의 기마병, 사막의 아들들이 물을 거야."], ["Ten thousand horsemen, sons of the desert, will ask to-day, ‘Have you heard of Mira?’ And to the answer, ‘She is well,’ they will say, ‘God is good!", "'미라의 소식을 들었는가?' 그리고 '잘 있다네'라는 대답에 그들은 '하느님은 선하시도다! 하느님께 복이 있기를!'"], ["blessed be God!’”", "하고 말할 거라네.\""]], [["“Mira—Sirius—names of stars, are they not, O sheik?”", "\"미라—시리우스—별 이름들이지요, 그렇지 않소, 족장이여?\""], ["asked Ben-Hur, going to each of the four, and to the sire, offering his hand.", "벤허가 네 마리 말과 그 아비에게 차례로 다가가 손을 내밀며 물었다."]], [["“And why not?” replied Ilderim.", "\"아니 될 것이 무엇인가?\" 일데림이 대답했다."], ["“Wert thou ever abroad on the desert at night?”", "\"그대는 밤에 사막에 나가 본 적이 있는가?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"없소.\""]], [["“Then thou canst not know how much we Arabs depend upon the stars.", "\"그러면 우리 아랍인이 별에 얼마나 기대고 사는지 모를 걸세."], ["We borrow their names in gratitude, and give them in love.", "우리는 고마운 마음으로 그 이름을 빌려다가, 사랑으로 그것을 붙여 준다네."], ["My fathers all had their Miras, as I have mine; and these children are stars no less.", "내 선조들도 모두 저마다의 미라를 두었으니, 내가 나의 미라를 둔 것과 같고, 이 자식들 또한 별이나 다름없지."], ["There, see thou, is Rigel, and there Antares; that one is Atair, and he whom thou goest to now is Aldebaran, the youngest of the brood, but none the worse of that—no, not he!", "저기, 보게나, 저놈이 리겔이고, 저놈이 안타레스라네. 저것은 아타이르, 그리고 그대가 지금 다가가는 놈은 알데바란, 한배에서 가장 어린 놈이지만, 그렇다고 못한 것은 아니야—아니, 결코 아니지!"], ["Against the wind he will carry thee till it roar in thy ears like Akaba; and he will go where thou sayest, son of Arrius—ay, by the glory of Solomon!", "바람을 거슬러도 그놈은 그대 귀에 아카바처럼 울부짖을 때까지 그대를 실어 갈 걸세. 그리고 그대가 이르는 곳이면 어디든 가리라, 아리우스의 아들이여—그렇다네, 솔로몬의 영광에 걸고!"], ["he will take thee to the lion’s jaws, if thou darest so much.”", "그대가 그만한 용기만 있다면, 그놈은 그대를 사자의 아가리로도 데려갈 걸세.\""]], [["The harness was brought.", "마구가 들려 왔다."], ["With his own hands Ben-Hur equipped the horses; with his own hands he led them out of the tent, and there attached the reins.", "벤허는 제 손으로 말들에게 마구를 채웠고, 제 손으로 그들을 천막 밖으로 끌어내어 거기서 고삐를 매었다."]], [["“Bring me Sirius,”", "\"시리우스를 데려와 주시오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["An Arab could not have better sprung to seat on the courser’s back.", "아랍인이라도 그보다 더 능숙하게 준마의 등에 뛰어오르지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["“And now the reins.”", "\"이제 고삐를 주시오.\""]], [["They were given him, and carefully separated.", "고삐가 그에게 건네졌고, 그는 조심스레 그것들을 갈라 잡았다."]], [["“Good sheik,”", "\"어진 족장이여.\""], ["he said, “I am ready.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 준비되었소."], ["Let a guide go before me to the field, and send some of thy men with water.”", "길잡이 하나를 앞세워 벌판으로 보내 주시고, 그대의 사람 몇을 시켜 물을 가져오게 해 주시오.\""]], [["There was no trouble at starting.", "출발에는 아무런 어려움이 없었다."], ["The horses were not afraid.", "말들은 두려워하지 않았다."], ["Already there seemed a tacit understanding between them and the new driver, who had performed his part calmly, and with the confidence which always begets confidence.", "벌써 그들과 새 마부 사이에는 무언의 이해가 자리 잡은 듯했다. 마부는 침착하게, 언제나 신뢰를 낳는 그 신뢰로 제 몫을 해낸 터였다."], ["The order of going was precisely that of driving, except that Ben-Hur sat upon Sirius instead of standing in the chariot.", "나아가는 방식은 벤허가 전차에 서는 대신 시리우스에 올라탔다는 것만 다를 뿐, 전차를 모는 것과 똑같았다."], ["Ilderim’s spirit arose.", "일데림의 기운이 솟구쳤다."], ["He combed his beard, and smiled with satisfaction as he muttered, “He is not a Roman, no, by the splendor of God!”", "그는 수염을 빗어 내리며 흡족하게 미소 짓고는 중얼거렸다. \"저자는 로마인이 아니야, 아니지, 하느님의 광휘에 걸고!\""], ["He followed on foot, the entire tenantry of the dowar—men, women, and children—pouring after him, participants all in his solicitude, if not in his confidence.", "그는 걸어서 뒤를 따랐고, 두와르의 온 식솔들—남자와 여자와 아이들—이 그의 뒤로 쏟아져 나오며, 신뢰까지는 아니어도 그 노심초사만큼은 다 함께 나누었다."]], [["The field, when reached, proved ample and well fitted for the training, which Ben-Hur began immediately by driving the four at first slowly, and in perpendicular lines, and then in wide circles.", "벌판에 이르고 보니, 그곳은 넓고도 훈련에 알맞은 곳이었다. 벤허는 곧장 훈련을 시작하여, 처음에는 네 마리를 천천히, 일직선으로 몰았다가, 이어 넓은 원을 그리며 몰았다."], ["Advancing a step in the course, he put them next into a trot; again progressing, he pushed into a gallop; at length he contracted the circles, and yet later drove eccentrically here and there, right, left, forward, and without a break.", "한 단계 더 나아가 그는 다음으로 그들을 속보로 몰았고, 다시 한 걸음 나아가 구보로 밀어붙였다. 마침내 그는 원을 좁혔고, 더 나중에는 이리저리, 오른쪽으로, 왼쪽으로, 앞으로, 끊임없이 변화무쌍하게 몰았다."], ["An hour was thus occupied.", "그렇게 한 시간이 흘렀다."], ["Slowing the gait to a walk, he drove up to Ilderim.", "걸음을 늦추어 보통 걸음으로 만든 뒤, 그는 일데림에게로 몰고 갔다."]], [["“The work is done, nothing now but practice,”", "\"훈련은 끝났소. 이제 연습만 남았소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “I give you joy, Sheik Ilderim, that you have such servants as these. See,”", "\"기쁘게 여기시오, 일데림 족장이여, 그대가 이런 종복들을 거느렸으니. 보시오.\" 그는 말에서 내려 말들에게 다가가며 말을 이었다."], ["he continued, dismounting and going to the horses, “see, the gloss of their red coats is without spot; they breathe lightly as when I began.", "\"보시오, 붉은 털의 윤기에는 티 하나 없소. 내가 시작했을 때처럼 숨을 가볍게 쉬고 있소. 그대에게 크나큰 기쁨을 안기겠소."], ["I give thee great joy, and it will go hard if”—he turned his flashing eyes upon the old man’s face—“if we have not the victory and our—”", "그리고 어려운 일이 있다면 다만—\" 그는 번득이는 눈을 노인의 얼굴로 돌렸다. \"—우리가 승리를 거두지 못하고 우리의—\""]], [["He stopped, colored, bowed. At the sheik’s side he observed, for the first time, Balthasar, leaning upon his staff, and two women closely veiled.", "그는 멈추었고, 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 숙였다. 그제야 그는 족장의 곁에, 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 발타사르와, 베일을 깊이 두른 두 여인이 있는 것을 처음으로 알아차렸다."], ["At one of the latter he looked a second time, saying to himself, with a flutter about his heart, “’Tis she—’tis the Egyptian!”", "그 가운데 한 여인을 그는 다시 한번 바라보며, 가슴이 두근거리는 가운데 속으로 말했다. \"그녀다—이집트 여인이다!\""], ["Ilderim picked up his broken sentence—", "일데림이 끊긴 그의 말을 이어받았다."]], [["“The victory, and our revenge!”", "\"승리, 그리고 우리의 복수 말이지!\""], ["Then he said aloud, “I am not afraid; I am glad.", "그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"나는 두렵지 않다. 나는 기쁘다."], ["Son of Arrius, thou art the man.", "아리우스의 아들이여, 그대야말로 적임자다."], ["Be the end like the beginning, and thou shalt see of what stuff is the lining of the hand of an Arab who is able to give.”", "끝맺음이 시작과 같다면, 그대는 베풀 줄 아는 아랍인의 손바닥 안쪽이 어떤 것으로 채워져 있는지 보게 될 것이다.\""]], [["“I thank thee, good sheik,”", "\"감사하오, 어진 족장이여.\""], ["Ben-Hur returned, modestly.", "벤허는 겸손하게 대답했다."], ["“Let the servants bring drink for the horses.”", "\"하인들에게 말들이 마실 물을 가져오게 해 주시오.\""]], [["With his own hands he gave the water.", "그는 제 손으로 물을 먹였다."]], [["Remounting Sirius, he renewed the training, going as before from walk to trot, from trot to gallop; finally, he pushed the steady racers into the run, gradually quickening it to full speed.", "다시 시리우스에 올라타고 그는 훈련을 새로 시작하여, 앞서처럼 보통 걸음에서 속보로, 속보에서 구보로 나아갔다. 마침내 그는 한결같은 준마들을 질주로 밀어붙이며, 차츰 속도를 최고로 끌어올렸다."], ["The performance then became exciting; and there were applause for the dainty handling of the reins, and admiration for the four, which were the same, whether they flew forward or wheeled in varying curvature.", "그러자 그 광경은 흥미진진해졌다. 고삐를 다루는 섬세한 솜씨에 갈채가 쏟아졌고, 앞으로 날아가든 갖가지 곡선으로 돌아가든 한결같은 네 마리에 감탄이 일었다."], ["In their action there were unity, power, grace, pleasure, all without effort or sign of labor.", "그들의 움직임에는 통일과 힘과 우아함과 즐거움이 있었으니, 그 모두가 애쓰는 기색이나 노고의 흔적 하나 없었다."], ["The admiration was unmixed with pity or reproach, which would have been as well bestowed upon swallows in their evening flight.", "그 감탄에는 연민도 책망도 섞이지 않았으니, 그런 것은 차라리 저녁 하늘을 나는 제비들에게나 바칠 것이었다."]], [["In the midst of the exercises, and the attention they received from all the bystanders, Malluch came upon the ground, seeking the sheik.", "이 훈련이 한창이고 둘러선 모든 이가 그것에 마음을 빼앗긴 그때, 말루크가 족장을 찾아 그 자리에 나타났다."]], [["“I have a message for you, O sheik,”", "\"그대에게 전할 말이 있소, 족장이여.\""], ["he said, availing himself of a moment he supposed favorable for the speech—“a message from Simonides, the merchant.”", "그는 말을 꺼내기에 알맞다 여긴 순간을 틈타 말했다. \"상인 시모니데스가 보낸 전갈이오.\""]], [["“Simonides!”", "\"시모니데스라!\""], ["ejaculated the Arab. “Ah!", "아랍인이 외쳤다. \"아!"], ["’tis well.", "잘되었구나."], ["May Abaddon take all his enemies!”", "아바돈이 그의 적들을 모조리 잡아가기를!\""]], [["“He bade me give thee first the holy peace of God,”", "\"그분께서는 먼저 하느님의 거룩한 평안을 그대에게 전하라 하셨소.\""], ["Malluch continued; “and then this despatch, with prayer that thou read it the instant of receipt.”", "말루크가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 이 전갈을 드리니, 받는 즉시 읽으시기를 청하셨소.\""]], [["Ilderim, standing in his place, broke the sealing of the package delivered to him, and from a wrapping of fine linen took two letters, which he proceeded to read.", "일데림은 제자리에 선 채, 건네받은 꾸러미의 봉인을 뜯고, 고운 아마포 싸개에서 편지 두 통을 꺼내어 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["[No. 1.]", "[첫째 편지]"]], [["“Simonides to Sheik Ilderim.", "\"시모니데스가 일데림 족장에게."]], [["“O friend!", "오, 벗이여!"]], [["“Assure thyself first of a place in my inner heart.", "먼저 그대가 내 마음 깊은 곳에 한자리를 차지하고 있음을 믿으시오."]], [["“Then—", "그리고—"]], [["“There is in thy dowar a youth of fair presence, calling himself the son of Arrius; and such he is by adoption.", "그대의 두와르에 풍채가 훌륭한 한 젊은이가 있으니, 스스로를 아리우스의 아들이라 부르오. 양자로서 과연 그러하오."]], [["“He is very dear to me.", "그는 내게 더없이 소중한 사람이오."]], [["“He hath a wonderful history, which I will tell thee; come thou to-day or to-morrow, that I may tell thee the history, and have thy counsel.", "그에게는 놀라운 내력이 있어, 내가 그대에게 들려주려 하오. 오늘이든 내일이든 내게 오시오, 그 내력을 들려주고 그대의 조언을 구하고자 하오."]], [["“Meantime, favor all his requests, so they be not against honor.", "그동안에는 그의 모든 청을 들어주시오, 다만 명예에 어긋나지 않는 한에서요."], ["Should there be need of reparation, I am bound to thee for it.", "보상이 필요할 일이 생기거든, 그것은 내가 그대에게 갚을 책임이 있소."]], [["“That I have interest in this youth, keep thou private.", "내가 이 젊은이에게 관심을 두고 있다는 것은, 그대만 알고 비밀로 해 주시오."]], [["“Remember me to thy other guest. He, his daughter, thyself, and all whom thou mayst choose to be of thy company, must depend upon me at the Circus the day of the games.", "그대의 다른 손님에게도 안부를 전해 주시오. 그와 그의 딸, 그대 자신, 그리고 그대가 일행으로 택할 모든 이는 경기가 열리는 날 원형경기장에서 나를 믿고 의지하시오."], ["I have seats already engaged.", "좌석은 이미 잡아 두었소."]], [["“To thee and all thine, peace.", "그대와 그대의 모든 사람에게 평안이 있기를."]], [["“What should I be, O my friend, but thy friend?", "오, 나의 벗이여, 내가 그대의 벗이 아니고 무엇이겠소? 시모니데스.\""]], [["[No. 2.]", "[둘째 편지]"]], [["“Simonides to Sheik Ilderim.", "\"시모니데스가 일데림 족장에게."]], [["“O friend!", "오, 벗이여!"]], [["“Out of the abundance of my experience, I send you a word.", "내 풍부한 경험에서 한마디 보내오."]], [["“There is a sign which all persons not Romans, and who have moneys or goods subject to despoilment, accept as warning—that is, the arrival at a seat of power of some high Roman official charged with authority.", "로마인이 아니면서 재물이나 물건을 약탈당할 처지에 있는 모든 사람이 경고로 받아들이는 징조가 하나 있으니, 곧 권력의 자리에 권한을 위임받은 어느 고위 로마 관리가 도착하는 일이오."]], [["“To-day comes the Consul Maxentius.", "오늘 막센티우스 집정관이 오오."]], [["“Be thou warned!", "경계하시오!"]], [["“Another word of advice.", "또 한 가지 충고가 있소."]], [["“A conspiracy, to be of effect against thee, O friend, must include the Herods as parties; thou hast great properties in their dominions.", "그대를 노린 음모가 효력을 거두려면, 오, 벗이여, 반드시 헤롯 일족이 끼어 있어야 하오. 그대는 그들의 영지에 막대한 재산을 두고 있기 때문이오."]], [["“Wherefore keep thou watch.", "그러니 경계를 늦추지 마시오."]], [["“Send this morning to thy trusty keepers of the roads leading south from Antioch, and bid them search every courier going and coming; if they find private despatches relating to thee or thine affairs, THOU SHOULDST SEE THEM.", "오늘 아침 안티오크에서 남쪽으로 뻗은 길들을 지키는 그대의 믿음직한 자들에게 사람을 보내, 오가는 모든 급사를 샅샅이 살피라 이르시오. 그대나 그대의 일에 관한 사사로운 전갈을 그들이 찾아내거든, 그대가 그것을 보아야 하오."]], [["“You should have received this yesterday, though it is not too late, if you act promptly.", "그대는 이것을 어제 받았어야 했으나, 신속히 움직인다면 아직 늦지는 않았소."]], [["“If couriers left Antioch this morning, your messengers know the byways, and can get before them with your orders.", "오늘 아침 안티오크에서 급사들이 떠났더라도, 그대의 전령들은 샛길을 알 터이니, 그대의 명령을 들고 그들을 앞지를 수 있소."]], [["“Do not hesitate.", "망설이지 마시오."]], [["“Burn this after reading.", "읽은 뒤에는 이것을 태우시오."]], [["“O my friend!", "오, 나의 벗이여!"], ["thy friend,", "그대의 벗,"]], [["Ilderim read the letters a second time, and refolded them in the linen wrap, and put the package under his girdle.", "시모니데스.\" 일데림은 편지를 다시 한번 읽고, 아마포 싸개에 도로 접어 넣은 뒤, 그 꾸러미를 허리띠 밑에 끼웠다."]], [["The exercises in the field continued but a little longer—in all about two hours.", "벌판에서의 훈련은 잠시 더 이어졌으니—통틀어 두 시간쯤이었다."], ["At their conclusion, Ben-Hur brought the four to a walk, and drove to Ilderim.", "끝낼 무렵 벤허는 네 마리를 보통 걸음으로 늦추어 일데림에게로 몰고 갔다."]], [["“With leave, O sheik,”", "\"허락하신다면, 족장이여.\""], ["he said, “I will return thy Arabs to the tent, and bring them out again this afternoon.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대의 아랍 말들을 천막으로 돌려보내고, 오늘 오후에 다시 끌어내겠소.\""]], [["Ilderim walked to him as he sat on Sirius, and said, “I give them to you, son of Arrius, to do with as you will until after the games.", "일데림은 시리우스에 올라앉은 그에게 걸어와 말했다. \"그들을 그대에게 맡기겠다, 아리우스의 아들이여. 경기가 끝날 때까지 그대 뜻대로 다루라."], ["You have done with them in two hours what the Roman—may jackals gnaw his bones fleshless!—could not in as many weeks.", "그대는 그 로마인—그놈의 뼈를 자칼들이 살점 하나 없이 갉아 먹기를!—이 그만한 주(週)를 들여도 못 한 일을 두 시간 만에 해냈다."], ["We will win—by the splendor of God, we will win!”", "우리는 이길 것이다—하느님의 광휘에 걸고, 우리는 이길 것이다!\""]], [["At the tent Ben-Hur remained with the horses while they were being cared for; then, after a plunge in the lake and a cup of arrack with the sheik, whose flow of spirits was royally exuberant, he dressed himself in his Jewish garb again, and walked with Malluch on into the Orchard.", "천막에 이르러 벤허는 말들이 보살핌을 받는 동안 곁에 머물렀다. 그런 뒤 호수에 몸을 담그고, 기운이 임금처럼 한껏 넘쳐흐르는 족장과 더불어 아라크 한 잔을 들고는, 다시 유대인의 옷차림으로 갈아입고서 말루크와 함께 과수원 안으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["There was much conversation between the two, not all of it important.", "두 사람 사이에는 많은 이야기가 오갔으나, 모두가 중요한 것은 아니었다."], ["One part, however, must not be overlooked.", "그러나 그 가운데 한 대목만은 놓쳐서는 안 된다."], ["Ben-Hur was speaking.", "벤허가 말하고 있었다."]], [["“I will give you,”", "\"그대에게 증서를 하나 드리겠소.\""], ["he said, “an order for my property stored in the khan this side the river by the Seleucian Bridge.", "그가 말했다. \"셀레우키아 다리 이쪽, 강가 여관에 보관해 둔 내 물건에 대한 증서요. 가능하다면 오늘 내게 가져다주시오."], ["Bring it to me to-day, if you can. And, good Malluch—if I do not overtask you—”", "그리고, 어진 말루크여—내가 그대에게 너무 무거운 짐을 지우는 것이 아니라면—\""]], [["Malluch protested heartily his willingness to be of service.", "말루크는 기꺼이 봉사하겠노라고 진심으로 다짐했다."]], [["“Thank you, Malluch, thank you,”", "\"고맙소, 말루크, 고맙소.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“I will take you at your word, remembering that we are brethren of the old tribe, and that the enemy is a Roman.", "\"그 말을 믿고 그대에게 부탁하겠소. 우리가 같은 옛 부족의 형제이고, 적은 로마인임을 떠올리면서 말이오."], ["First, then—as you are a man of business, which I much fear Sheik Ilderim is not—”", "그러면 먼저—그대는 사업하는 사람이니, 일데림 족장은 그렇지 못한 듯해 몹시 걱정스럽소만—\""]], [["“Arabs seldom are,” said Malluch, gravely.", "\"아랍인은 좀처럼 그렇지 못하지요.\" 말루크가 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“Nay, I do not impeach their shrewdness, Malluch.", "\"아니, 그들의 영민함을 탓하는 것은 아니오, 말루크."], ["It is well, however, to look after them.", "그래도 그들을 살펴 주는 편이 좋소."], ["To save all forfeit or hindrance in connection with the race, you would put me perfectly at rest by going to the office of the Circus, and seeing that he has complied with every preliminary rule; and if you can get a copy of the rules, the service may be of great avail to me.", "경주와 관련하여 위약금이나 차질이 생길 일을 모두 막으려면, 그대가 원형경기장 사무소에 가서 그가 모든 예비 규정을 따랐는지 확인해 준다면 나는 더없이 마음을 놓을 수 있겠소. 그리고 규정 사본을 한 부 얻어 줄 수 있다면, 그 도움은 내게 크게 쓸모가 있을 것이오."], ["I would like to know the colors I am to wear, and particularly the number of the crypt I am to occupy at the starting; if it be next Messala’s on the right or left, it is well; if not, and you can have it changed so as to bring me next the Roman, do so.", "내가 어떤 색깔을 입어야 하는지, 그리고 특히 출발할 때 내가 차지할 칸이 몇 번인지 알고 싶소. 그것이 메살라의 칸 오른쪽이나 왼쪽 바로 옆이라면 좋겠고, 그렇지 않다면, 그대가 그것을 바꾸어 나를 그 로마인 바로 옆에 오게 할 수 있다면 그리해 주시오."], ["Have you good memory, Malluch?”", "그대는 기억력이 좋소, 말루크?\""]], [["“It has failed me, but never, son of Arrius, where the heart helped it as now.”", "\"예전엔 흐려진 적도 있으나, 아리우스의 아들이여, 지금처럼 마음이 받쳐 줄 때는 결코 그렇지 않습니다.\""]], [["“I will venture, then, to charge you with one further service.", "\"그러면 그대에게 한 가지 더 부탁해 보겠소."], ["I saw yesterday that Messala was proud of his chariot, as he might be, for the best of Cæsar’s scarcely surpass it.", "어제 보니 메살라가 제 전차를 자랑스러워하더이다. 그럴 만도 했소, 카이사르의 최고급 전차로도 그것을 넘어서기 어려울 테니."], ["Can you not make its display an excuse which will enable you to find if it be light or heavy?", "그 자랑거리를 구실 삼아 그것이 가벼운지 무거운지 알아낼 수 없겠소?"], ["I would like to have its exact weight and measurements—and, Malluch, though you fail in all else, bring me exactly the height his axle stands above the ground.", "그 정확한 무게와 치수를 알고 싶소—그리고, 말루크, 다른 것은 다 놓치더라도, 그 차축이 땅 위로 얼마나 높이 서 있는지만은 정확히 알아 와 주시오."], ["You understand, Malluch? I do not wish him to have any actual advantage of me.", "알겠소, 말루크? 나는 그가 나보다 실제로 유리한 점을 가지는 것을 원치 않소."], ["I do not care for his splendor; if I beat him, it will make his fall the harder, and my triumph the more complete.", "그의 호화로움에는 관심 없소. 내가 그를 이긴다면, 그의 몰락은 더욱 호되고, 나의 승리는 더욱 완전해질 것이오."], ["If there are advantages really important, I want them.”", "정말로 중요한 이점들이 있다면, 그것을 나도 갖고 싶소.\""]], [["“I see, I see!”", "\"알겠소, 알겠소!\""], ["said Malluch.", "말루크가 말했다."], ["“A line dropped from the centre of the axle is what you want.”", "\"차축 한가운데서 내려뜨린 수직선, 그것이 그대가 원하는 것이군요.\""]], [["“Thou hast it; and be glad, Malluch—it is the last of my commissions.", "\"바로 그것이오. 그리고 기뻐하시오, 말루크—이것이 내 마지막 부탁이오."], ["Let us return to the dowar.”", "두와르로 돌아갑시다.\""]], [["At the door of the tent they found a servant replenishing the smoke-stained bottles of leben freshly made, and stopped to refresh themselves.", "천막 문 앞에 이르니 한 하인이 갓 만든 레벤을 연기에 그을린 병에 채우고 있어, 그들은 걸음을 멈추고 목을 축였다."], ["Shortly afterwards Malluch returned to the city.", "얼마 뒤 말루크는 도시로 돌아갔다."]], [["During their absence, a messenger well mounted had been despatched with orders as suggested by Simonides.", "그들이 자리를 비운 사이, 시모니데스가 일러 준 대로 명령을 받은 사자 하나가 좋은 말에 올라 떠난 뒤였다."], ["He was an Arab, and carried nothing written.", "그는 아랍인이었고, 문서로 쓴 것은 아무것도 지니지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["“Iras, the daughter of Balthasar, sends me with salutation and a message,”", "“발타사르의 딸 이라스 님이 인사와 함께 전언을 전하라 하셨습니다.”"], ["said a servant to Ben-Hur, who was taking his ease in the tent.", "천막에서 한가로이 쉬고 있던 벤허에게 하인이 말했다."]], [["“Give me the message.”", "“전언을 말해 보아라.”"]], [["“Would it please you to accompany her upon the lake?”", "“호수에 함께 나가 주시겠는지 여쭈라 하셨습니다.”"]], [["“I will carry the answer myself. Tell her so.”", "“답은 내가 직접 전하겠다. 그렇게 일러라.”"]], [["His shoes were brought him, and in a few minutes Ben-Hur sallied out to find the fair Egyptian.", "신발을 가져오게 하고, 벤허는 몇 분 뒤 아름다운 이집트 여인을 찾아 나섰다."], ["The shadow of the mountains was creeping over the Orchard of Palms in advance of night.", "산그늘이 밤보다 앞서 종려나무 동산 위로 기어들고 있었다."], ["Afar through the trees came the tinkling of sheep bells, the lowing of cattle, and the voices of the herdsmen bringing their charges home.", "멀리 나무들 사이로 양 떼의 방울 소리, 소들의 울음소리, 그리고 가축을 집으로 몰아오는 목동들의 목소리가 딸랑딸랑 들려왔다."], ["Life at the Orchard, it should be remembered, was in all respects as pastoral as life on the scantier meadows of the desert.", "기억해 둘 것은, 이 동산의 삶이 모든 면에서 사막의 메마른 목초지에서의 삶 못지않게 목가적이었다는 사실이다."]], [["Sheik Ilderim had witnessed the exercises of the afternoon, being a repetition of those of the morning; after which he had gone to the city in answer to the invitation of Simonides; he might return in the night; but, considering the immensity of the field to be talked over with his friend, it was hardly possible.", "일데림 족장은 오후의 훈련을 지켜보았는데, 그것은 아침에 했던 것을 되풀이한 것이었다. 그 뒤 그는 시모니데스의 초대에 응하여 도시로 떠났다. 밤에 돌아올 수도 있었으나, 벗과 나눌 이야기가 워낙 방대한 것을 생각하면 그러기는 어려웠다."], ["Ben-Hur, thus left alone, had seen his horses cared for; cooled and purified himself in the lake; exchanged the field garb for his customary vestments, all white, as became a Sadducean of the pure blood; supped early; and, thanks to the strength of youth, was well recovered from the violent exertion he had undergone.", "그리하여 홀로 남은 벤허는 말들을 돌보게 하고, 호수에서 몸을 식히고 씻은 뒤, 순수한 혈통의 사두개인답게 들판에서 입던 옷을 평소의 온통 흰 옷으로 갈아입고, 일찍 저녁을 먹었다. 그리고 젊음의 힘 덕분에, 격렬하게 치른 노고에서 거뜬히 회복되어 있었다."]], [["It is neither wise nor honest to detract from beauty as a quality.", "아름다움을 하나의 성질로 보아 깎아내리는 것은 슬기롭지도 정직하지도 않은 일이다."], ["There cannot be a refined soul insensible to its influence.", "그 영향에 무감각한 세련된 영혼이란 있을 수 없다."], ["The story of Pygmalion and his statue is as natural as it is poetical.", "피그말리온과 그의 조각상 이야기는 시적인 만큼이나 자연스럽다."], ["Beauty is of itself a power; and it was now drawing Ben-Hur.", "아름다움은 그 자체로 하나의 힘이다. 그리고 그 힘이 지금 벤허를 끌어당기고 있었다."]], [["The Egyptian was to him a wonderfully beautiful woman—beautiful of face, beautiful of form.", "이집트 여인은 그에게 놀랍도록 아름다운 여자였으니, 얼굴도 아름답고 자태도 아름다웠다. 그의 생각 속에서 그녀는 언제나 샘가에서 보았던 모습 그대로 나타났다. 그는 그녀의 목소리가 풍기는 힘을 느꼈으니, 자신에게 눈물 어린 감사를 표하던 까닭에 그 목소리는 더욱 감미로웠다."], ["In his thought she always appeared to him as he saw her at the fountain; and he felt the influence of her voice, sweeter because in tearful expression of gratitude to him, and of her eyes—the large, soft, black, almond-shaped eyes declarative of her race—eyes which looked more than lies in the supremest wealth of words to utter; and recurrences of the thought of her were returns just so frequent of a figure tall, slender, graceful, refined, wrapped in rich and floating drapery, wanting nothing but a fitting mind to make her, like the Shulamite, and in the same sense, terrible as an army with banners.", "또한 그녀의 눈이 풍기는 힘도 느꼈으니, 그 종족을 드러내는 크고 부드러운 검은 아몬드 모양의 눈은 어떤 풍성한 말로도 다 담을 수 없는 것을 말하고 있었다. 그녀에 대한 생각이 거듭 떠오를 때마다, 크고 가냘프며 우아하고 세련된, 화려하고 하늘하늘 흘러내리는 옷자락에 감싸인 그 자태가 그만큼 자주 되살아났다. 그 자태에 모자란 것은 오직 어울리는 마음씨 하나뿐이었으니, 그것만 갖추면 그녀는 술람미 여인처럼, 그리고 그와 똑같은 뜻에서, 깃발을 든 군대처럼 두려운 존재가 되었을 것이다."], ["In other words, as she returned to his fancy, the whole passionate Song of Solomon came with her, inspired by her presence.", "다시 말해, 그녀가 그의 공상 속으로 되돌아올 때면, 솔로몬의 정열적인 노래 전부가 그녀와 함께, 그녀의 존재에 고무되어 따라왔다."], ["With this sentiment and that feeling, he was going to see if she actually justified them.", "이런 감정과 이런 느낌을 품은 채, 그는 그녀가 과연 그것을 정당화해 주는지 보러 가는 길이었다."], ["It was not love that was taking him, but admiration and curiosity, which might be the heralds of love.", "그를 데려가는 것은 사랑이 아니라, 사랑의 선구자일지도 모를 찬탄과 호기심이었다."]], [["The landing was a simple affair, consisting of a short stairway, and a platform garnished by some lamp-posts; yet at the top of the steps he paused, arrested by what he beheld.", "선착장은 짧은 계단과 등잔 기둥 몇 개로 꾸며진 단(壇)으로 이루어진, 단출한 것이었다. 그런데도 계단 꼭대기에서 그는 눈에 들어온 광경에 사로잡혀 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["There was a shallop resting upon the clear water lightly as an egg-shell.", "맑은 물 위에 작은 거룻배 한 척이 달걀 껍데기처럼 가볍게 떠 있었다."], ["An Ethiop—the camel-driver at the Castalian fount—occupied the rower’s place, his blackness intensified by a livery of shining white.", "카스탈리아 샘가에서 보았던 그 낙타몰이꾼 에티오피아인이 노 젓는 자리를 차지하고 있었는데, 번쩍이는 흰 제복 때문에 그 검은 빛깔이 한층 짙어 보였다."], ["All the boat aft was cushioned and carpeted with stuffs brilliant with Tyrian red.", "배의 고물 쪽은 온통 티로스 진홍빛으로 눈부신 천을 덧대어 방석과 양탄자를 깔아 두었다."], ["On the rudder seat sat the Egyptian herself, sunk in Indian shawls and a very vapor of most delicate veils and scarfs.", "키잡이 자리에는 이집트 여인이 몸소 앉아, 인도산 숄과 더없이 섬세한 너울과 목도리의 안개 속에 묻혀 있었다."], ["Her arms were bare to the shoulders; and, not merely faultless in shape, they had the effect of compelling attention to them—their pose, their action, their expression; the hands, the fingers even, seemed endowed with graces and meaning; each was an object of beauty.", "그녀의 두 팔은 어깨까지 드러나 있었는데, 그저 흠 없는 모양일 뿐 아니라, 그 자태와 그 움직임과 그 표정으로 시선을 사로잡는 힘이 있었다. 손도, 심지어 손가락마저도 우아함과 의미를 부여받은 듯했다. 그 하나하나가 아름다움의 대상이었다."], ["The shoulders and neck were protected from the evening air by an ample scarf, which yet did not hide them.", "어깨와 목은 넉넉한 목도리로 저녁 공기를 막았으나, 그렇다고 그것을 가리지는 못했다."]], [["In the glance he gave her, Ben-Hur paid no attention to these details.", "그녀에게 던진 눈길 속에서 벤허는 이런 세세한 것들에 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["There was simply an impression made upon him; and, like strong light, it was a sensation, not a thing of sight or enumeration.", "그저 하나의 인상이 그에게 새겨졌을 뿐이었다. 강한 빛이 그러하듯, 그것은 하나의 감각이었지, 보거나 헤아릴 수 있는 것이 아니었다."], ["Thy lips are like a thread of scarlet; thy temples are like a piece of pomegranate within thy locks. Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away; for, lo!", "네 입술은 홍색 실 같고, 네 머리채 속 두 뺨은 석류 한 쪽 같구나. 일어나라, 나의 사랑, 나의 어여쁜 자여, 어서 오라. 보라!"], ["the winter is past, the rain is over and gone; the flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing of birds is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in the land—such was the impression she made upon him translated into words.", "겨울도 지나고, 비도 그쳐 갔으며, 땅에는 꽃이 피고, 새들의 노래할 때가 이르렀으니, 산비둘기의 소리가 우리 땅에 들리는구나. 그녀가 그에게 새긴 인상은 말로 옮기면 그러했다."]], [["“Come,”", "“오세요.”"], ["she said, observing him stop, “come, or I shall think you a poor sailor.”", "그가 멈춰 선 것을 보고 그녀가 말했다. “오세요, 그러지 않으면 당신을 형편없는 뱃사람으로 여기겠어요.”"]], [["The red of his cheek deepened.", "그의 뺨에 붉은 기운이 짙어졌다."], ["Did she know anything of his life upon the sea? He descended to the platform at once.", "그녀가 그의 바다 위에서의 삶을 무엇이든 알고 있는 걸까? 그는 곧장 단으로 내려섰다."]], [["“I was afraid,”", "“두려웠소.”"], ["he said, as he took the vacant seat before her.", "그녀 앞의 빈자리에 앉으며 그가 말했다."]], [["“Of what?”", "“무엇이요?”"]], [["“Of sinking the boat,”", "“배를 가라앉힐까 봐.”"], ["he replied, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“Wait until we are in deeper water,”", "“더 깊은 물에 닿을 때까지 기다리세요.”"], ["she said, giving a signal to the black, who dipped the oars, and they were off.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며 흑인에게 신호를 보냈고, 그가 노를 담그자 배는 출발했다."]], [["If love and Ben-Hur were enemies, the latter was never more at mercy.", "사랑과 벤허가 서로 적이었다면, 벤허는 그 어느 때보다 사랑의 처분에 내맡겨져 있었다."], ["The Egyptian sat where he could not but see her; she, whom he had already engrossed in memory as his ideal of the Shulamite.", "이집트 여인은 그가 보지 않을 수 없는 자리에 앉아 있었다. 이미 기억 속에서 그가 술람미 여인의 이상으로 몰두해 둔 바로 그 여인이었다."], ["With her eyes giving light to his, the stars might come out, and he not see them; and so they did.", "그녀의 눈이 그의 눈에 빛을 주니, 별들이 떠오른들 그는 보지 못했을 것이다. 실제로 별들이 떠올랐다."], ["The night might fall with unrelieved darkness everywhere else; her look would make illumination for him.", "다른 모든 곳에는 가시지 않는 어둠과 함께 밤이 내릴지 몰라도, 그녀의 눈길이 그를 위해 빛을 밝혀 줄 터였다."], ["And then, as everybody knows, given youth and such companionship, there is no situation in which the fancy takes such complete control as upon tranquil waters under a calm night sky, warm with summer.", "그리고 누구나 알다시피, 젊음과 이런 동행이 주어졌을 때, 여름의 온기를 머금은 고요한 밤하늘 아래 잔잔한 물 위만큼 공상이 온전히 사람을 사로잡는 상황은 없다."], ["It is so easy at such time to glide imperceptibly out of the commonplace into the ideal.", "그런 때면 평범함에서 이상(理想)으로 자기도 모르게 미끄러져 들기란 그토록 쉬운 일이다."]], [["“Give me the rudder,” he said.", "“키를 내게 주시오.” 그가 말했다."]], [["“No,” she replied, “that were to reverse the relation.", "“아니에요.” 그녀가 대답했다."], ["Did I not ask you to ride with me?", "“그러면 관계가 뒤바뀌어요."], ["I am indebted to you, and would begin payment.", "제가 함께 타자고 청하지 않았나요?"], ["You may talk and I will listen, or I will talk and you will listen: that choice is yours; but it shall be mine to choose where we go, and the way thither.”", "저는 당신께 빚이 있고, 갚기 시작하려는 거예요. 당신이 말하고 제가 듣거나, 제가 말하고 당신이 듣거나, 그 선택은 당신 몫이에요. 하지만 우리가 어디로 가는지, 또 그리로 가는 길은 제가 정할 거예요.”"]], [["“And where may that be?”", "“그럼 그곳이 어디요?”"]], [["“You are alarmed again.”", "“또 놀라시는군요.”"]], [["“O fair Egyptian, I but asked you the first question of every captive.”", "“오, 아름다운 이집트 여인이여, 나는 그저 모든 포로가 던지는 첫 질문을 했을 뿐이오.”"]], [["“Call me Egypt.”", "“저를 이집트라고 부르세요.”"]], [["“I would rather call you Iras.”", "“차라리 이라스라고 부르고 싶소.”"]], [["“You may think of me by that name, but call me Egypt.”", "“그 이름으로 저를 생각하셔도 좋아요, 하지만 부르기는 이집트라고 하세요.”"]], [["“Egypt is a country, and means many people.”", "“이집트는 한 나라이고, 수많은 백성을 뜻하는데.”"]], [["“Yes, yes! And such a country!”", "“그래요, 그래요! 그것도 그런 나라죠!”"]], [["“I see; it is to Egypt we are going.”", "“알겠소. 우리가 가는 곳이 이집트로군.”"]], [["“Would we were!", "“그랬으면 좋겠네요!"], ["I would be so glad.”", "그러면 정말 기쁠 텐데.”"]], [["She sighed as she spoke.", "그녀는 말하며 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“You have no care for me, then,”", "“그럼 당신은 내게 마음을 쓰지 않는구려.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, by that I know you were never there.” “I never was.”", "“아, 그 말로 당신이 거기 한 번도 가 본 적 없다는 걸 알겠어요.”"]], [["“Oh, it is the land where there are no unhappy people, the desired of all the rest of the earth, the mother of all the gods, and therefore supremely blest.", "“한 번도 가 본 적 없소.” “오, 그곳은 불행한 사람이 하나도 없는 땅, 세상 모든 다른 곳이 부러워하는 땅, 모든 신의 어머니, 그래서 더없이 복받은 땅이에요."], ["There, O son of Arrius, there the happy find increase of happiness, and the wretched, going, drink once of the sweet water of the sacred river, and laugh and sing, rejoicing like children.”", "거기서는, 오 아리우스의 아들이여, 행복한 자는 행복이 더해지고, 비참한 자는 거기 가서 거룩한 강의 단물을 한 번 마시기만 하면 아이처럼 기뻐하며 웃고 노래해요.”"]], [["“Are not the very poor with you there as elsewhere?”", "“거기라고 더없이 가난한 자들이 다른 곳처럼 없는 건 아니지 않소?”"]], [["“The very poor in Egypt are the very simple in wants and ways,”", "“이집트에서 더없이 가난한 자들은 바라는 것도 사는 모양도 더없이 소박한 사람들이에요.”"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“They have no wish beyond enough, and how little that is, a Greek or a Roman cannot know.”", "“그들은 충분한 것 이상을 바라지 않는데, 그게 얼마나 작은 것인지는 그리스인이나 로마인은 알 수 없어요.”"]], [["“But I am neither Greek nor Roman.”", "“하지만 나는 그리스인도 로마인도 아니오.”"]], [["She laughed.", "그녀가 웃었다."]], [["“I have a garden of roses, and in the midst of it is a tree, and its bloom is the richest of all.", "“제게 장미 정원이 하나 있는데, 그 한가운데 나무 한 그루가 있고, 그 꽃이 모든 것 가운데 가장 화사해요."], ["Whence came it, think you?”", "그게 어디서 왔을 것 같으세요?”"]], [["“From Persia, the home of the rose.”", "“장미의 고향, 페르시아에서.” “아니에요.”"]], [["“From India, then.”", "“그럼 인도에서.”"]], [["“Ah! one of the isles of Greece.”", "“아니에요.” “아! 그리스의 어느 섬에서.”"]], [["“I will tell you,”", "“말씀드리죠.”"], ["she said: “a traveller found it perishing by the roadside on the plain of Rephaim.”", "그녀가 말했다. “한 나그네가 르바임 평원의 길가에서 시들어 가는 그것을 발견했어요.”"]], [["“Oh, in Judea!”", "“오, 유대 땅에서!”"]], [["“I put it in the earth left bare by the receding Nile, and the soft south wind blew over the desert and nursed it, and the sun kissed it in pity; after which it could not else than grow and flourish.", "“저는 나일강이 물러나며 드러난 맨땅에 그것을 심었고, 보드라운 남풍이 사막 너머로 불어와 그것을 보살피고, 해가 가엾이 여겨 입 맞추었어요. 그러니 어찌 자라고 무성해지지 않을 수 있었겠어요."], ["I stand in its shade now, and it thanks me with much perfume.", "저는 이제 그 그늘에 서 있고, 그것은 짙은 향기로 제게 감사해요."], ["As with the roses, so with the men of Israel.", "장미가 그러하듯, 이스라엘 사람들도 그래요."], ["Where shall they reach perfection but in Egypt?”", "이집트가 아니면 어디서 그들이 완성에 이르겠어요?”"]], [["“Moses was but one of millions.”", "“모세는 수백만 가운데 하나였을 뿐이오.”"]], [["“Nay, there was a reader of dreams.", "“아니요, 꿈을 풀이하는 자가 있었지요."], ["Will you forget him?”", "그분을 잊으시려고요?”"]], [["“The friendly Pharaohs are dead.”", "“우호적인 파라오들은 죽었소.”"]], [["“Ah, yes!", "“아, 그래요!"], ["The river by which they dwelt sings to them in their tombs; yet the same sun tempers the same air to the same people.”", "그들이 곁에 두고 살던 강이 그들의 무덤에서 그들에게 노래해 줘요. 그래도 같은 해가 같은 공기를 같은 백성에게 데워 주죠.”"]], [["“Alexandria is but a Roman town.”", "“알렉산드리아는 로마의 도시일 뿐이오.”"]], [["“She has but exchanged sceptres.", "“그저 왕홀(王笏)을 바꿨을 뿐이에요."], ["Cæsar took from her that of the sword, and in its place left that of learning.", "카이사르가 그 도시에서 칼의 왕홀을 거두고, 그 자리에 학문의 왕홀을 남겼죠. 저와 함께 브루케이움으로 가요, 그러면 만국의 학당을 보여 드릴게요."], ["Go with me to the Brucheium, and I will show you the college of nations; to the Serapeion, and see the perfection of architecture; to the Library, and read the immortals; to the theatre, and hear the heroics of the Greeks and Hindoos; to the quay, and count the triumphs of commerce; descend with me into the streets, O son of Arrius, and, when the philosophers have dispersed, and taken with them the masters of all the arts, and all the gods have home their votaries, and nothing remains of the day but its pleasures, you shall hear the stories that have amused men from the beginning, and the songs which will never, never die.”", "세라페이온으로 가서 건축의 완성을 보고, 도서관으로 가서 불멸의 작가들을 읽고, 극장으로 가서 그리스인과 힌두인의 영웅담을 듣고, 부두로 가서 상업의 승리를 헤아려요. 저와 함께 거리로 내려가요, 오 아리우스의 아들이여. 그리고 철학자들이 흩어지며 온갖 기예의 대가들을 데려가고, 모든 신이 자기 숭배자들을 거느려 돌아가고, 그날에 남은 것이라곤 그 즐거움뿐일 때, 당신은 태초부터 사람들을 즐겁게 해 온 이야기들과, 영영 죽지 않을 노래들을 듣게 될 거예요.”"]], [["As he listened, Ben-Hur was carried back to the night when, in the summer-house in Jerusalem, his mother, in much the same poetry of patriotism, declaimed the departed glories of Israel.", "그 말을 들으며, 벤허는 예루살렘의 정자에서 어머니가 이와 거의 같은 애국의 시정(詩情)으로 사라진 이스라엘의 영광을 읊던 그 밤으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“I see now why you wish to be called Egypt.", "“이제 알겠소, 당신이 어째서 이집트라고 불리고 싶어 하는지."], ["Will you sing me a song if I call you by that name?", "내가 그 이름으로 부르면 노래를 한 곡 불러 주겠소?"], ["I heard you last night.”", "어젯밤에 당신 노래를 들었소.”"]], [["“That was a hymn of the Nile,”", "“그건 나일강의 찬가였어요.”"], ["she answered, “a lament which I sing when I would fancy I smell the breath of the desert, and hear the surge of the dear old river; let me rather give you a piece of the Indian mind.", "그녀가 대답했다. “사막의 숨결을 맡고, 그리운 옛 강의 물결 소리를 듣는다고 상상하고 싶을 때 부르는 만가(輓歌)지요. 차라리 인도의 마음을 담은 한 곡을 들려 드릴게요."], ["When we get to Alexandria, I will take you to the corner of the street where you can hear it from the daughter of the Ganga, who taught it to me.", "알렉산드리아에 가면, 거리 모퉁이로 당신을 데려가, 제게 그 노래를 가르쳐 준 갠지스의 딸에게서 그것을 들을 수 있게 해 드리죠."], ["Kapila, you should know, was one of the most revered of the Hindoo sages.”", "카필라는, 아셔야 할 텐데, 힌두 현자들 가운데 가장 존경받던 분이었어요.”"]], [["Then, as if it were a natural mode of expression, she began the song.", "그러고는 마치 그것이 본디의 표현 방식인 양, 그녀는 노래를 시작했다. 카필라"]], [["# I.", "# I."]], [["“Kapila, Kapila, so young and true, I yearn for a glory like thine, And hail thee from battle to ask anew, Can ever thy Valor be mine?", "하나\n“카필라, 카필라, 그토록 젊고 참된 이여, 나는 그대 같은 영광을 갈망하여, 싸움터에서 그대를 불러 다시 묻노니, 그대의 용기가 언젠가 내 것이 될 수 있으리까?"]], [["“Kapila sat on his charger dun, A hero never so grave: ‘Who loveth all things hath fear of none, ’Tis love that maketh me brave.", "“카필라는 잿빛 군마에 올라앉아, 그토록 진지한 영웅은 없었으니, ‘모든 것을 사랑하는 자는 두려울 게 없으니, 나를 용감케 하는 것은 사랑이라."], ["A woman gave me her soul one day, The soul of my soul to be alway; Thence came my Valor to me, Go try it—try it—and see.’", "어느 날 한 여인이 내게 제 영혼을 주어, 영영 내 영혼의 영혼이 되었으니, 거기서 내 용기가 내게 왔노라, 가서 시험해 보라—시험해 보라—그리고 보라.’"]], [["# II.", "# II."]], [["“Kapila, Kapila, so old and gray, The queen is calling for me; But ere I go hence, I wish thou wouldst say, How Wisdom first came to thee.", "“카필라, 카필라, 그토록 늙고 백발이 된 이여, 왕비께서 나를 부르고 계시나, 이곳을 떠나기 전에, 부디 그대가 말해 주오, 지혜가 어떻게 처음 그대에게 왔는지를."]], [["“Kapila stood in his temple door, A priest in eremite guise: ‘It did not come as men get their lore, ’Tis faith that maketh me wise.", "“카필라는 제 신전 문간에 서서, 은수자(隱修者)의 차림을 한 사제로서, ‘그것은 사람이 학문을 얻듯 온 것이 아니니, 나를 슬기롭게 하는 것은 믿음이라."], ["A woman gave me her heart one day, The heart of my heart to be alway; Thence came my Wisdom to me, Go try it—try it—and see.’”", "어느 날 한 여인이 내게 제 마음을 주어, 영영 내 마음의 마음이 되었으니, 거기서 내 지혜가 내게 왔노라, 가서 시험해 보라—시험해 보라—그리고 보라.’”"]], [["Ben-Hur had not time to express his thanks for the song before the keel of the boat grated upon the underlying sand, and, next moment, the bow ran upon the shore.", "벤허가 미처 노래에 고마움을 표하기도 전에, 배의 용골이 밑에 깔린 모래에 긁히더니, 이내 뱃머리가 기슭에 닿았다."]], [["“A quick voyage, O Egypt!”", "“참으로 빠른 항해였소, 오 이집트여!”"], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["“And a briefer stay!” she replied, as, with a strong push, the black sent them shooting into the open water again.", "“그리고 더없이 짧은 머무름이었죠!” 그녀가 대답하자, 흑인이 힘껏 밀어 배를 다시 트인 물로 쏘아 보냈다."]], [["“You will give me the rudder now.”", "“이제 키를 내게 주겠소.”"]], [["“Oh no,” said she, laughing.", "“오, 안 돼요.” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“To you, the chariot; to me, the boat.", "“당신에겐 전차, 제겐 배예요."], ["We are merely at the lake’s end, and the lesson is that I must not sing any more.", "우리는 그저 호수 끝에 닿았을 뿐이고, 교훈인즉슨 제가 더는 노래해선 안 된다는 거예요."], ["Having been to Egypt, let us now to the Grove of Daphne.”", "이집트에 다녀왔으니, 이제 다프네 숲으로 가요.”"]], [["“Without a song on the way?”", "“가는 길에 노래도 없이?”"], ["he said, in deprecation.", "그가 아쉬운 듯 말했다."]], [["“Tell me something of the Roman from whom you saved us to-day,” she asked.", "“오늘 우리를 구해 주신 그 로마인 이야기나 들려주세요.” 그녀가 청했다."]], [["The request struck Ben-Hur unpleasantly.", "그 청은 벤허의 마음을 언짢게 했다."]], [["“I wish this were the Nile,”", "“여기가 나일강이었으면.”"], ["he said, evasively. “The kings and queens, having slept so long, might come down from their tombs, and ride with us.”", "그가 말을 돌리며 말했다. “그토록 오래 잠들어 있던 왕들과 왕비들이 무덤에서 내려와 우리와 함께 배를 탈 텐데.”"]], [["“They were of the colossi, and would sink our boat.", "“그들은 거상(巨像)이라 우리 배를 가라앉힐 거예요."], ["The pygmies would be preferable. But tell me of the Roman.", "차라리 난쟁이가 나을 텐데. 하지만 그 로마인 이야기를 해 주세요."], ["He is very wicked, is he not?”", "그 사람, 무척 사악하지 않나요?”"]], [["“I cannot say.”", "“뭐라 말할 수 없소.”"]], [["“Is he of noble family, and rich?”", "“귀족 가문에 부자인가요?”"]], [["“I cannot speak of his riches.”", "“그 사람의 재산에 대해서는 말할 수 없소.”"]], [["“How beautiful his horses were!", "“그 사람의 말들은 어찌나 아름답던지!"], ["and the bed of his chariot was gold, and the wheels ivory. And his audacity!", "전차의 바닥은 황금이고, 바퀴는 상아였어요. 그리고 그 대담함이라니!"], ["The bystanders laughed as he rode away; they, who were so nearly under his wheels!”", "그가 달려가 버릴 때 구경꾼들이 웃었어요. 하마터면 그 바퀴에 깔릴 뻔했던 그들이 말이에요!”"]], [["She laughed at the recollection.", "그녀는 그 일을 떠올리며 웃었다."]], [["“They were rabble,”", "“그자들은 천한 무리였소.”"], ["said Ben-Hur, bitterly.", "벤허가 쓰디쓰게 말했다."]], [["“He must be one of the monsters who are said to be growing up in Rome—Apollos ravenous as Cerberus.", "“그는 로마에서 자라고 있다는 그 괴물들 가운데 하나임에 틀림없어요. 케르베로스처럼 게걸스러운 아폴론들 말이에요."], ["Does he reside in Antioch?”", "그 사람이 안티오크에 사나요?”"]], [["“He is of the East somewhere.”", "“동방 어딘가 출신이오.”"]], [["“Egypt would suit him better than Syria.”", "“그라면 시리아보다 이집트가 더 어울리겠어요.”"]], [["“Hardly,”", "“글쎄.”"], ["Ben-Hur replied.", "벤허가 대답했다."], ["“Cleopatra is dead.”", "“클레오파트라는 죽었소.”"]], [["That instant the lamps burning before the door of the tent came into view.", "그 순간 천막 문 앞에 켜 둔 등잔불이 시야에 들어왔다."]], [["“The dowar!”", "“다와르예요!”"], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah, then, we have not been to Egypt.", "“아, 그럼 우리는 이집트에 가지 못했군요."], ["I have not seen Karnak or Philae or Abydos.", "나는 카르나크도 필라이도 아비도스도 보지 못했소."], ["This is not the Nile.", "여기는 나일강이 아니오."], ["I have but heard a song of India, and been boating in a dream.”", "나는 그저 인도의 노래 한 곡을 들었고, 꿈속에서 배를 탔을 뿐이오.”"]], [["“Philae—Karnak.", "“필라이—카르나크."], ["Mourn rather that you have not seen the Rameses at Aboo Simbel, looking at which makes it so easy to think of God, the maker of the heavens and earth.", "차라리 당신이 아부심벨의 람세스를 보지 못한 것을 슬퍼하세요. 그것을 바라보면 하늘과 땅을 지으신 하느님을 떠올리기가 그토록 쉬워져요."], ["Or why should you mourn at all?", "아니, 어째서 슬퍼해야 하나요?"], ["Let us go on to the river; and if I cannot sing”—she laughed—“because I have said I would not, yet I can tell you stories of Egypt.”", "강으로 계속 나아가요. 제가 노래는 못 하더라도”—그녀가 웃었다—“하지 않겠다고 말했으니까요, 그래도 이집트 이야기는 들려드릴 수 있어요.”"]], [["“Go on! Ay, till morning comes, and the evening, and the next morning!”", "“계속하시오! 그래, 아침이 오고, 저녁이 오고, 다음 날 아침이 올 때까지!”"], ["he said, vehemently.", "그가 격하게 말했다."]], [["“Of what shall my stories be? Of the mathematicians?”", "“제 이야기는 무엇에 대한 게 좋을까요?"], ["“Oh no.”", "수학자들?”"]], [["“Of the philosophers?”", "“오, 아니오.”"]], [["“No, no.”", "“철학자들?”"]], [["“Of the magicians and genii?”", "“아니, 아니오.” “마법사와 정령들?”"]], [["“If you will.”", "“그게 좋다면.”"]], [["“Of war?”", "“전쟁?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“좋소.”"]], [["“Of love?”", "“사랑?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“좋소.”"]], [["“I will tell you a cure for love.", "“사랑을 고치는 처방을 들려드릴게요."], ["It is the story of a queen.", "어느 왕비의 이야기예요."], ["Listen reverently.", "경건히 들으세요."], ["The papyrus from which it was taken by the priests of Philae was wrested from the hand of the heroine herself. It is correct in form, and must be true:", "이것을 적은 파피루스는 필라이의 사제들이 바로 그 여주인공의 손에서 빼앗아 온 것이에요. 형식이 올바르니, 틀림없는 사실일 거예요. 네네호프라"]], [["“There is no parallelism in human lives.", "하나\n“인간의 삶에는 나란한 평행이란 없다."]], [["“No life runs a straight line.", "“어떤 삶도 곧은 선을 그리지 않는다."]], [["“The most perfect life develops as a circle, and terminates in its beginning, making it impossible to say, This is the commencement, that the end.", "“가장 완전한 삶은 원으로 펼쳐져, 그 시작에서 끝나니, 여기가 시작이고 저기가 끝이라고 말할 수 없게 한다."]], [["“Perfect lives are the treasures of God; of great days he wears them on the ring-finger of his heart hand.”", "“완전한 삶은 하느님의 보물이다. 위대한 날에 그분은 그것들을 자기 심장 쪽 손의 약손가락에 끼신다."]], [["# II.", "# II."]], [["“Ne-ne-hofra dwelt in a house close by Essouan, yet closer to the first cataract—so close, indeed, that the sound of the eternal battle waged there between river and rocks was of the place a part.", "“네네호프라는 에수안 가까이, 그러나 첫 번째 폭포에 더 가까운 집에 살았다. 어찌나 가까웠던지, 거기서 강과 바위 사이에 영원히 벌어지는 싸움의 소리가 그 집의 일부였다."]], [["“She grew in beauty day by day, so that it was said of her, as of the poppies in her father’s garden, What will she not be in the time of blooming?", "“그녀는 날마다 아름답게 자라나, 그녀를 두고 마치 아버지의 정원에 핀 양귀비를 두고 하듯 이런 말이 오갔다. 활짝 피어날 때면 그녀가 어떤 모습이 될까?"]], [["“Each year of her life was the beginning of a new song more delightful than any of those which went before.", "“그녀의 한 해 한 해가, 앞서간 그 어떤 것보다 더 황홀한 새 노래의 시작이었다."]], [["“Child was she of a marriage between the North, bounded by the sea, and the South, bounded by the desert beyond the Luna mountains; and one gave her its passion, the other its genius; so when they beheld her, both laughed, saying, not meanly, ‘She is mine,’ but generously, ‘Ha, ha!", "“그녀는 바다에 막힌 북녘과, 루나산맥 너머 사막에 막힌 남녘 사이의 혼인에서 난 아이였다. 하나는 그녀에게 제 정열을 주고, 다른 하나는 제 천재를 주었다. 그래서 둘은 그녀를 바라보며, 옹졸하게 ‘저 아이는 내 것’이라 하지 않고, 너그럽게 ‘하, 하!"], ["she is ours.’", "저 아이는 우리 것’이라 하며 웃었다."]], [["“All excellences in nature contributed to her perfection and rejoiced in her presence.", "“자연의 온갖 빼어남이 그녀의 완성에 이바지하고 그녀의 존재를 기뻐했다. 그녀가 오고 가면 새들은 인사하며 날개를 떨었고, 사나운 바람은 가라앉아 서늘한 미풍이 되었으며, 흰 연꽃은 그녀를 보려고 물 깊은 곳에서 떠올랐고, 엄숙한 강은 제 갈 길에서 머뭇거렸으며, 종려나무는 고개를 끄덕이며 제 깃털을 다 흔들었다."], ["Did she come or go, the birds ruffled their wings in greeting; the unruly winds sank to cooling zephyrs; the white lotus rose from the water’s depth to look at her; the solemn river loitered on its way; the palm-trees, nodding, shook all their plumes; and they seemed to say, this one, I gave her of my grace; that, I gave her of my brightness; the other, I gave her of my purity: and so each as it had a virtue to give.", "그것들은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. 이것은, 내가 그녀에게 내 우아함을 주었고, 저것은, 내가 그녀에게 내 광채를 주었고, 또 다른 것은, 내가 그녀에게 내 순결을 주었노라. 그리하여 저마다 줄 미덕이 있는 만큼 주었다."]], [["“At twelve, Ne-ne-hofra was the delight of Essouan; at sixteen, the fame of her beauty was universal; at twenty, there was never a day which did not bring to her door princes of the desert on swift camels, and lords of Egypt in gilded barges; and, going away disconsolate, they reported everywhere, ‘I have seen her, and she is not a woman, but Athor herself.’”", "“열두 살에 네네호프라는 에수안의 기쁨이었고, 열여섯 살에는 그 아름다움의 명성이 두루 퍼졌으며, 스무 살에는 빠른 낙타를 탄 사막의 군주들과 황금 거룻배를 탄 이집트의 영주들이 그녀의 문 앞에 찾아오지 않는 날이 없었다. 그들은 낙담하여 돌아가서는 어디서나 이렇게 전했다. ‘나는 그녀를 보았는데, 그녀는 여자가 아니라 아토르 여신 그 자체였다.’"]], [["# III.", "# III."]], [["“Now of the three hundred and thirty successors of good King Menes, eighteen were Ethiopians, of whom Oraetes was one hundred and ten years old.", "“선왕 메네스의 삼백서른 후계자 가운데 열여덟은 에티오피아인이었고, 그 가운데 오라이테스는 백열 살이었다."], ["He had reigned seventy-six years.", "그는 칠십육 년을 다스렸다."], ["Under him the people thrived, and the land groaned with fatness of plenty.", "그의 치세에 백성은 번성했고, 땅은 풍요의 기름짐으로 신음했다."], ["He practised wisdom because, having seen so much, he knew what it was.", "그는 슬기를 행했으니, 그토록 많은 것을 보았기에 슬기가 무엇인지 알았던 것이다."], ["He dwelt in Memphis, having there his principal palace, his arsenals, and his treasure-house.", "그는 멤피스에 살며, 그곳에 자신의 으뜸가는 궁전과 병기고와 보물 창고를 두었다."], ["Frequently he went down to Butos to talk with Latona.", "그는 자주 부토스로 내려가 라토나와 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["“The wife of the good king died.", "“선왕의 왕비가 죽었다."], ["Too old was she for perfect embalmment; yet he loved her, and mourned as the inconsolable; seeing which, a colchyte presumed one day to speak to him.", "그녀는 너무 늙어 완전한 방부 처리를 할 수 없었으나, 그래도 왕은 그녀를 사랑하여 위로받을 길 없는 사람처럼 슬퍼했다. 그것을 본 한 콜키테가 어느 날 감히 그에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“‘O Oraetes, I am astonished that one so wise and great should not know how to cure a sorrow like this.’", "“‘오 오라이테스시여, 그토록 슬기롭고 위대하신 분이 이런 슬픔을 고치는 법을 모르시다니 저는 놀랍습니다.’"]], [["“‘Tell me a cure,’ said the king.", "“‘그 고치는 법을 말해 보아라.’ 왕이 말했다."]], [["“Three times the colchyte kissed the floor, and then he replied, knowing the dead could not hear him, ‘At Essouan lives Ne-ne-hofra, beautiful as Athor the beautiful.", "“콜키테는 세 번 바닥에 입 맞춘 뒤, 죽은 이가 자기 말을 들을 수 없음을 알고 이렇게 대답했다. ‘에수안에 네네호프라가 사는데, 아름다운 아토르 여신처럼 아름답습니다."], ["Send for her.", "그녀를 부르소서."], ["She has refused all the lords and princes, and I know not how many kings; but who can say no to Oraetes?’”", "그녀는 모든 영주와 군주를, 또 얼마나 많은 왕인지 모를 이들을 물리쳤으나, 누가 오라이테스께 ‘아니오’라고 할 수 있겠습니까?’"]], [["# IV.", "# IV."]], [["“Ne-ne-hofra descended the Nile in a barge richer than any ever before seen, attended by an army in barges each but a little less fine.", "“네네호프라는 일찍이 본 적 없는 화려한 거룻배를 타고 나일강을 내려왔고, 저마다 조금 덜 화려한 거룻배에 탄 군세가 그녀를 호위했다."], ["All Nubia and Egypt, and a myriad from Libya, and a host of Troglodytes, and not a few Macrobii from beyond the Mountains of the Moon, lined the tented shores to see the cortege pass, wafted by perfumed winds and golden oars.", "누비아와 이집트 전역, 그리고 리비아에서 온 무수한 사람들, 트로글로디테스 무리, 또 달의 산맥 너머에서 온 적지 않은 마크로비이가 천막을 친 강기슭에 늘어서서, 향기로운 바람과 황금 노에 실려 지나가는 행렬을 구경했다."]], [["“Through a dromos of sphinxes and couchant double-winged lions she was borne, and set down before Oraetes sitting on a throne specially erected at the sculptured pylon of the palace.", "“스핑크스와 두 날개를 가진 채 엎드린 사자들이 늘어선 참배로를 지나 그녀가 옮겨져, 궁전의 조각된 탑문 앞에 특별히 세워진 옥좌에 앉은 오라이테스 앞에 내려졌다."], ["He raised her up, gave her place by his side, clasped the uraeus upon her arm, kissed her, and Ne-ne-hofra was queen of all queens.", "그는 그녀를 일으켜 제 곁에 자리를 주고, 그녀의 팔에 우라이우스를 채우고, 입 맞추니, 네네호프라는 모든 왕비 위의 왕비가 되었다."]], [["“That was not enough for the wise Oraetes; he wanted love, and a queen happy in his love.", "“그것으로는 슬기로운 오라이테스에게 충분치 않았다. 그는 사랑을, 그리고 자기 사랑 속에서 행복한 왕비를 원했다."], ["So he dealt with her tenderly, showing her his possessions, cities, palaces, people; his armies, his ships: and with his own hand he led her through his treasure-house, saying, ‘O.", "그래서 그는 그녀를 다정하게 대하며 자기 소유를, 도시들과 궁전들과 백성을, 자기 군대와 배들을 보여 주었고, 손수 그녀를 보물 창고로 이끌며 말했다. ‘오 네네호프라여!"], ["Ne-ne-hofra! but kiss me in love, and they are all thine.’", "사랑으로 내게 입 맞추기만 하면, 이 모든 것이 다 네 것이다.’"]], [["“And, thinking she could be happy, if she was not then, she kissed him once, twice, thrice—kissed him thrice, his hundred and ten years notwithstanding.", "“그래서 지금은 아니더라도 행복해질 수 있으리라 생각하며, 그녀는 그에게 한 번, 두 번, 세 번 입 맞추었으니—백열 살이라는 그의 나이에도 아랑곳없이 세 번 입 맞추었다."]], [["“The first year she was happy, and it was very short; the third year she was wretched, and it was very long; then she was enlightened: that which she thought love of Oraetes was only daze of his power.", "“첫해에 그녀는 행복했고, 그 해는 무척 짧았다. 셋째 해에 그녀는 비참했고, 그 해는 무척 길었다."], ["Well for her had the daze endured!", "그러다 그녀는 깨달았다."], ["Her spirits deserted her; she had long spells of tears, and her women could not remember when they heard her laugh; of the roses on her cheeks only ashes remained; she languished and faded gradually, but certainly.", "오라이테스를 향한 사랑이라 여겼던 것이 그저 그의 권세에 눈멀었던 것일 뿐임을. 그 눈멂이 그대로 이어졌더라면 차라리 다행이었을 것을! 기력이 그녀를 떠났고, 오랫동안 눈물에 잠겼으며, 그녀의 시녀들은 마지막으로 그녀의 웃음소리를 들은 때가 언제인지 떠올리지 못했다."], ["Some said she was haunted by the Erinnyes for cruelty to a lover; others, that she was stricken by some god envious of Oraetes.", "두 뺨의 장미는 잿빛만 남았다. 그녀는 차츰, 그러나 분명하게 시들고 야위어 갔다. 어떤 이는 그녀가 한 연인에게 모질게 군 탓에 복수의 여신들에게 시달리는 것이라 했고, 또 어떤 이는 오라이테스를 시샘하는 어느 신에게 화를 입은 것이라 했다."], ["Whatever the cause of her decline, the charms of the magicians availed not to restore her, and the prescript of the doctor was equally without virtue.", "그녀가 쇠하는 까닭이 무엇이든, 마법사들의 부적도 그녀를 되살리지 못했고, 의원의 처방 또한 똑같이 아무 효험이 없었다."], ["Ne-ne-hofra was given over to die.", "네네호프라는 죽음에 내맡겨졌다."]], [["“Oraetes chose a crypt for her up in the tombs of the queens; and, calling the master sculptors and painters to Memphis, he set them to work upon designs more elaborate than any even in the great galleries of the dead kings.", "“오라이테스는 왕비들의 무덤 위쪽에 그녀를 위한 묘실을 골랐다. 그리고 으뜸가는 조각가와 화가들을 멤피스로 불러, 죽은 왕들의 그 거대한 회랑에 있는 것보다도 더 공들인 도안에 매달리게 했다."]], [["“‘O thou beautiful as Athor herself, my queen!’ said the king, whose hundred and thirteen years did not lessen his ardor as a lover, ‘Tell me, I pray, the ailment of which, alas!", "“‘오 아토르 여신만큼이나 아름다운 그대, 나의 왕비여!’ 백열세 살이 되어서도 연인으로서의 열정이 사그라지지 않은 왕이 말했다. ‘부디 내게 말해 주오, 아아!"], ["thou art so certainly perishing before my eyes.’", "그대가 그토록 분명히 내 눈앞에서 스러져 가게 하는 그 병이 무엇인지를.’"]], [["“‘You will not love me any more if I tell you,’ she said, in doubt and fear.", "“‘말씀드리면 더는 저를 사랑하지 않으실 거예요.’ 그녀가 의심과 두려움에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“‘Not love you!", "“‘너를 사랑하지 않다니!"], ["I will love you the more.", "나는 더욱더 사랑할 것이다."], ["I swear it, by the genii of Amente!", "아멘테의 정령들에 걸고 맹세하노라!"], ["by the eye of Osiris, I swear it!", "오시리스의 눈에 걸고 맹세하노라!"], ["Speak!’ he cried, passionate as a lover, authoritative as a king.", "말하라!’ 그가 연인처럼 정열적으로, 왕처럼 위엄 있게 외쳤다."]], [["“‘Hear, then,’ she said.", "“‘그럼 들으세요.’ 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘There is an anchorite, the oldest and holiest of his class, in a cave near Essouan.", "‘에수안 가까운 어느 동굴에, 그 무리 가운데 가장 늙고 가장 거룩한 은수자가 살고 있어요."], ["His name is Menopha.", "이름은 메노파예요."], ["He was my teacher and guardian.", "그분은 제 스승이자 보호자였어요."], ["Send for him, O Oraetes, and he will tell you that you seek to know; he will also help you find the cure for my affliction.’", "그분을 부르세요, 오 오라이테스시여, 그러면 당신이 알고자 하는 것을 말씀드릴 거예요. 또 제 병을 고칠 방법을 찾도록 도와주실 거예요.’"]], [["“Oraetes arose rejoicing. He went away in spirit a hundred years younger than when he came.”", "“오라이테스는 기뻐하며 일어섰다. 올 때보다 영혼이 백 년은 젊어진 채 그는 떠났다."]], [["# V.", "# V."]], [["“‘Speak!’ said Oraetes to Menopha, in the palace at Memphis.", "“‘말하라!’ 멤피스 궁전에서 오라이테스가 메노파에게 말했다."]], [["“And Menopha replied, ‘Most mighty king, if you were young, I should not answer, because I am yet pleased with life; as it is, I will say the queen, like any other mortal, is paying the penalty of a crime.’", "“그러자 메노파가 대답했다. ‘더없이 강대하신 왕이시여, 만일 당신이 젊으셨다면 저는 대답하지 않았을 것입니다. 저는 아직 삶을 즐기고 있으니까요. 사정이 이러하니, 저는 말씀드리겠습니다. 왕비께서는 다른 모든 인간과 마찬가지로 어느 죄의 대가를 치르고 계십니다.’"]], [["“‘A crime!’ exclaimed Oraetes, angrily.", "“‘죄라니!’ 오라이테스가 노하여 외쳤다."]], [["“Menopha bowed very low.", "“메노파는 아주 낮게 절했다."]], [["“‘Yes; to herself.’", "“‘그렇습니다. 자기 자신에게 지은 죄지요.’"]], [["“‘I am not in mood for riddles,’ said the king.", "“‘나는 수수께끼를 들을 기분이 아니다.’ 왕이 말했다."]], [["“‘What I say is not a riddle, as you shall hear.", "“‘제가 드리는 말씀은 수수께끼가 아닙니다, 들으시면 아실 겁니다."], ["Ne-ne-hofra grew up under my eyes, and confided every incident of her life to me; among others, that she loved the son of her father’s gardener, Barbec by name.’", "네네호프라는 제 눈앞에서 자라났고, 자기 삶의 일을 하나하나 제게 털어놓았습니다. 그 가운데, 자기 아버지의 정원사 아들 바르베크라는 자를 사랑했다는 것도 있었지요.’"]], [["“Oraetes’s frown, strangely enough, began to dissipate.", "“오라이테스의 찌푸린 얼굴이, 참으로 이상하게도, 풀리기 시작했다."]], [["“‘With that love in her heart, O king, she came to you; of that love she is dying.’", "“‘그 사랑을 가슴에 품고서, 오 왕이시여, 그녀는 당신께 왔습니다. 바로 그 사랑 때문에 그녀는 죽어 가고 있습니다.’"]], [["“‘Where is the gardener’s son now?’ asked Oraetes.", "“‘그 정원사의 아들은 지금 어디 있느냐?’ 오라이테스가 물었다."]], [["“‘In Essouan.’", "“‘에수안에 있습니다.’"]], [["“The king went out and gave two orders.", "“왕은 밖으로 나가 두 가지 명령을 내렸다."], ["To one oeris he said, ‘Go to Essouan and bring hither a youth named Barbec.", "한 오에리스에게 그는 말했다. ‘에수안으로 가서 바르베크라는 청년을 데려오너라."], ["You will find him in the garden of the queen’s father;’ to another, ‘Assemble workmen and cattle and tools, and construct for me in Lake Chemmis an island, which, though laden with a temple, a palace, and a garden, and all manner of trees bearing fruit, and all manner of vines, shall nevertheless float about as the winds may blow it.", "왕비의 아버지의 정원에서 그를 찾을 것이다.’ 또 다른 자에게는 이렇게 일렀다. ‘일꾼과 가축과 연장을 모아, 켐미스 호수에 나를 위해 섬을 하나 만들어라. 신전과 궁전과 정원, 갖가지 열매 맺는 나무와 갖가지 포도 덩굴을 실었으되, 그러면서도 바람이 부는 대로 떠다닐 수 있는 섬이라야 한다."], ["Make the island, and let it be fully furnished by the time the moon begins to wane.’", "그 섬을 만들고, 달이 이지러지기 시작할 때까지 온전히 갖추어 두어라.’"]], [["“Then to the queen he said,", "“그러고는 왕비에게 말했다."]], [["“‘Be of cheer.", "“‘기운을 내라."], ["I know all, and have sent for Barbec.’", "나는 모든 것을 알며, 바르베크를 부르러 보냈다.’"]], [["“Ne-ne-hofra kissed his hands.", "“네네호프라는 그의 손에 입 맞추었다."]], [["“‘You shall have him to yourself, and he you to himself; nor shall any disturb your loves for a year.’", "“‘너는 그를 너만의 것으로 갖고, 그는 너를 자기만의 것으로 가질 것이다. 한 해 동안 누구도 너희 사랑을 방해하지 못하리라.’"]], [["“She kissed his feet; he raised her, and kissed her in return; and the rose came back to her cheek, the scarlet to her lips, and the laughter to her heart.”", "“그녀는 그의 발에 입 맞추었다. 그는 그녀를 일으켜 답례로 입 맞추었다. 그러자 두 뺨에 장미가 돌아오고, 입술에 진홍빛이 돌아오고, 가슴에 웃음이 돌아왔다."]], [["# VI.", "# VI."]], [["“For one year Ne-ne-hofra and Barbec the gardener floated as the winds blew on the island of Chemmis, which became one of the wonders of the world; never a home of love more beautiful; one year, seeing no one and existing for no one but themselves.", "“한 해 동안 네네호프라와 정원사 바르베크는 바람 부는 대로 켐미스의 섬을 떠다녔으니, 그 섬은 세상의 경이 가운데 하나가 되었다. 그보다 더 아름다운 사랑의 보금자리는 일찍이 없었다. 한 해 동안, 아무도 보지 않고 오직 자기들끼리만을 위해 존재하며 살았다."], ["Then she returned in state to the palace in Memphis.", "그러고는 그녀가 위풍당당하게 멤피스의 궁전으로 돌아왔다."]], [["“‘Now whom lovest thou best?’ asked the king.", "“‘이제 너는 누구를 가장 사랑하느냐?’ 왕이 물었다."]], [["“She kissed his cheek and said, ‘Take me back, O good king, for I am cured.’", "“그녀는 그의 뺨에 입 맞추며 말했다. ‘저를 다시 거두소서, 오 선하신 왕이여, 저는 다 나았습니다.’"]], [["“Oraetes laughed, none the worse, that moment, of his hundred and fourteen years.", "“오라이테스는 웃었으니, 그 순간 백열네 살이라 해서 조금도 못할 것이 없었다."]], [["“‘Then it is true, as Menopha said: ha, ha, ha!", "“‘그럼 메노파가 말한 대로 사실이로구나. 하, 하, 하!"], ["it is true, the cure of love is love.’", "사실이야, 사랑의 약은 사랑이라더니.’"]], [["“‘Even so,’ she replied.", "“‘그러하옵니다.’ 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Suddenly his manner changed, and his look became terrible.", "“돌연 그의 태도가 바뀌더니, 그 표정이 무시무시해졌다."]], [["“‘I did not find it so,’ he said.", "“‘나는 그렇지 않았다.’ 그가 말했다."]], [["“She shrank affrighted.", "“그녀는 겁에 질려 움츠러들었다."]], [["“‘Thou guilty!’ he continued.", "“‘너는 죄인이다!’ 그가 말을 이었다."], ["‘Thy offense to Oraetes the man he forgives; but thy offence to Oraetes the king remains to be punished.’", "‘인간 오라이테스에 대한 네 죄는 그가 용서한다. 그러나 왕 오라이테스에 대한 네 죄는 아직 벌해야 할 것으로 남아 있다.’"]], [["“She cast herself at his feet.", "“그녀는 그의 발치에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["“‘Hush!’ he cried.", "“‘조용히!’ 그가 외쳤다."], ["‘Thou art dead!’", "‘너는 죽었다!’"]], [["“He clapped his hands, and a terrible procession came in—a procession of parachistes, or embalmers, each with some implement or material of his loathsome art.", "“그가 두 손을 마주치자, 무시무시한 행렬이 들어왔다—파라키스테스, 곧 방부 처리하는 자들의 행렬이었으니, 저마다 그 역겨운 기술의 어떤 연장이나 재료를 들고 있었다."]], [["“The King pointed to Ne-ne-hofra.", "“왕은 네네호프라를 가리켰다."]], [["“‘She is dead.", "“‘저 여자는 죽었다."], ["Do thy work well.’”", "너희 일을 잘 해내라.’"]], [["# VII.", "# VII."]], [["“Ne-ne-hofra the beautiful, after seventy-two days, was carried to the crypt chosen for her the year before, and laid with her queenly predecessors; yet there was no funeral procession in her honor across the sacred lake.”", "“아름다운 네네호프라는 일흔두 날 뒤, 한 해 전에 그녀를 위해 골라 둔 묘실로 옮겨져, 그녀에 앞선 왕비들과 나란히 뉘었다. 그러나 그녀를 기리며 거룩한 호수를 건너는 장례 행렬은 없었다.”"]], [["At the conclusion of the story, Ben-Hur was sitting at the Egyptian’s feet, and her hand upon the tiller was covered by his hand.", "이야기가 끝났을 때, 벤허는 이집트 여인의 발치에 앉아 있었고, 키를 잡은 그녀의 손은 그의 손에 덮여 있었다. “메노파가 틀렸소.” 그가 말했다."]], [["“Menopha was wrong,” he said.", "“어째서요?”"]], [["“How?” “Love lives by loving.”", "“사랑은 사랑함으로써 살아 있는 거요.”"]], [["“Then there is no cure for it?”", "“그럼 그것에는 약이 없나요?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“있소."], ["Oraetes found the cure.”", "오라이테스가 그 약을 찾아냈소.”"]], [["“What was it?”", "“그게 무엇이었죠?”"]], [["“Death.”", "“죽음이오.”"]], [["“You are a good listener, O son of Arrius.”", "“당신은 잘 들어 주는 분이군요, 오 아리우스의 아들이여.”"]], [["And so with conversation and stories, they whiled the hours away.", "그렇게 대화와 이야기로 그들은 시간을 흘려보냈다."], ["As they stepped ashore, she said,", "기슭에 내려서며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“To-morrow we go to the city.”", "“내일 우리는 도시로 가요.”"]], [["“But you will be at the games?”", "“하지만 경기에는 오시겠지요?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh yes.”", "“오, 그럼요.”"]], [["“I will send you my colors.”", "“내 색깔을 보내 드리겠소.”"]], [["With that they separated.", "그 말과 함께 그들은 헤어졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["Ilderim returned to the dowar next day about the third hour.", "일데림은 이튿날 제3시경에 두아르로 돌아왔다."], ["As he dismounted, a man whom he recognized as of his own tribe came to him and said, “O sheik, I was bidden give thee this package, with request that thou read it at once.", "그가 말에서 내리자, 제 부족 사람임을 알아본 한 남자가 다가와 말했다. \"오, 족장이시여, 이 꾸러미를 전하라는 분부를 받았습니다. 즉시 읽어 주시기를 청하셨습니다."], ["If there be answer, I was to wait thy pleasure.”", "답신이 있으시면, 분부가 내릴 때까지 기다리라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["Ilderim gave the package immediate attention.", "일데림은 곧바로 꾸러미를 살폈다. 봉인은 이미 뜯겨 있었다."], ["The seal was already broken.", "겉봉의 수신인은 이러했다."], ["The address ran, To Valerius Gratus at Cæsarea.", "카이사레아의 발레리우스 그라투스에게."]], [["“Abaddon take him!” growled the sheik, at discovering a letter in Latin.", "\"아바돈이 저놈을 데려가기를!\" 라틴어로 쓰인 편지를 발견하고 족장은 으르렁거리듯 말했다."]], [["Had the missive been in Greek or Arabic, he could have read it; as it was, the utmost he could make out was the signature in bold Roman letters—MESSALA—whereat his eyes twinkled.", "만약 그 편지가 그리스어나 아랍어로 쓰였다면 그는 읽을 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 형편이 이러하니, 그가 알아볼 수 있는 것이라곤 굵직한 로마 글자로 쓰인 서명—메살라—뿐이었고, 그 순간 그의 눈이 번득였다."]], [["“Where is the young Jew?”", "\"그 젊은 유대인은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“In the field with the horses,”", "\"말들과 함께 들판에 있습니다.\""], ["a servant replied.", "하인 하나가 대답했다."]], [["The sheik replaced the papyrus in its envelopes, and, tucking the package under his girdle, remounted the horse.", "족장은 파피루스를 다시 봉투 속에 넣고, 그 꾸러미를 허리띠 안에 찔러 넣은 뒤, 다시 말에 올랐다."], ["That moment a stranger made his appearance, coming, apparently, from the city.", "바로 그때 낯선 사람 하나가 모습을 드러냈는데, 보아하니 도시 쪽에서 오는 길이었다."]], [["“I am looking for Sheik Ilderim, surnamed the Generous,”", "\"관대한 자라 불리는 일데림 족장을 찾고 있습니다.\""], ["the stranger said.", "낯선 사람이 말했다."]], [["His language and attire bespoke him a Roman.", "그의 말투와 차림새는 그가 로마인임을 드러냈다."]], [["What he could not read, he yet could speak; so the old Arab answered, with dignity, “I am Sheik Ilderim.”", "읽지는 못해도 말은 할 줄 알았으므로, 늙은 아랍인은 위엄을 갖추어 대답했다. \"내가 일데림 족장이오.\""]], [["The man’s eyes fell; he raised them again, and said, with forced composure, “I heard you had need of a driver for the games.”", "남자의 시선이 아래로 떨어졌다가 다시 들렸다. 그는 애써 침착을 꾸미며 말했다. \"경주에 쓸 마부가 필요하시다는 말을 들었습니다.\""]], [["Ilderim’s lip under the white mustache curled contemptuously.", "하얀 콧수염 아래로 일데림의 입술이 경멸하듯 비틀렸다."]], [["“Go thy way,”", "\"네 갈 길이나 가거라.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I have a driver.”", "\"마부는 이미 있다.\""]], [["He turned to ride away, but the man, lingering, spoke again.", "그는 말을 돌려 떠나려 했으나, 그 남자는 머뭇거리며 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Sheik, I am a lover of horses, and they say you have the most beautiful in the world.”", "\"족장이시여, 저는 말을 사랑하는 사람입니다. 세상에서 가장 아름다운 말들을 가지고 계시다고들 하더군요.\""]], [["The old man was touched; he drew rein, as if on the point of yielding to the flattery, but finally replied, “Not to-day, not to-day; some other time I will show them to you.", "늙은이의 마음이 움직였다. 그는 그 아첨에 넘어가려는 듯 고삐를 당겼으나, 끝내 이렇게 대답했다. \"오늘은 안 되네, 오늘은 안 돼."], ["I am too busy just now.”", "다른 날 보여 주겠네. 지금은 너무 바빠서 말이야.\""]], [["He rode to the field, while the stranger betook himself to town again with a smiling countenance.", "그는 들판으로 말을 몰았고, 그동안 낯선 사람은 흐뭇한 얼굴로 다시 도시를 향해 발길을 돌렸다."], ["He had accomplished his mission.", "그는 제 임무를 완수한 것이었다."]], [["And every day thereafter, down to the great day of the games, a man—sometimes two or three men—came to the sheik at the Orchard, pretending to seek an engagement as driver.", "그 뒤로 날마다, 경주가 열리는 대망의 날에 이르기까지, 한 사람이—때로는 두세 사람이—마부 자리를 구하는 척하며 그 과수원의 족장을 찾아왔다."]], [["In such manner Messala kept watch over Ben-Hur.", "이런 식으로 메살라는 벤허를 감시하고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["The sheik waited, well satisfied, until Ben-Hur drew his horses off the field for the forenoon—well satisfied, for he had seen them, after being put through all the other paces, run full speed in such manner that it did not seem there were one the slowest and another the fastest—run in other words, as if the four were one.", "족장은 흡족한 마음으로 기다렸다. 벤허가 오전 훈련을 마치고 말들을 경기장에서 끌어낼 때까지—흡족하게, 그도 그럴 것이 그는 말들이 온갖 걸음새를 다 거친 끝에 전속력으로 달리는 모습을 지켜보았는데, 그 가운데 가장 느린 놈도 가장 빠른 놈도 따로 있는 것 같지 않을 만큼—다시 말해, 네 마리가 마치 한 마리처럼 달렸던 것이다."]], [["“This afternoon, O sheik, I will give Sirius back to you.”", "\"오늘 오후에, 족장이시여, 시리우스를 돌려드리겠습니다.\""], ["Ben-Hur patted the neck of the old horse as he spoke.", "벤허가 말하면서 늙은 말의 목을 토닥였다."], ["“I will give him back, and take to the chariot.”", "\"이놈을 돌려드리고, 전차에 매달겠습니다.\""]], [["“So soon?”", "\"그리 빨리?\""], ["Ilderim asked.", "일데림이 물었다."]], [["“With such as these, good sheik, one day suffices.", "\"이런 말들이라면, 족장이시여, 하루면 충분합니다."], ["They are not afraid; they have a man’s intelligence, and they love the exercise.", "이놈들은 두려워하지 않습니다. 사람의 지혜를 지녔고, 훈련을 좋아합니다. 이놈은,\""], ["This one,” he shook a rein over the back of the youngest of the four—“you called him Aldebaran, I believe—is the swiftest; in once round a stadium he would lead the others thrice his length.”", "그는 네 마리 가운데 가장 어린 놈의 등 위로 고삐를 흔들었다—\"알데바란이라고 부르셨던 것 같은데—가장 빠릅니다. 경기장을 한 바퀴 돌면 제 몸길이의 세 배만큼 다른 놈들을 앞지를 겁니다.\""]], [["Ilderim pulled his beard, and said, with twinkling eyes, “Aldebaran is the swiftest; but what of the slowest?”", "일데림은 수염을 쓰다듬으며, 눈을 반짝이며 말했다. \"알데바란이 가장 빠르다면, 가장 느린 놈은 어느 놈인가?\""]], [["“This is he.”", "\"이놈입니다.\""], ["Ben-Hur shook the rein over Antares.", "벤허가 안타레스의 등 위로 고삐를 흔들었다. \"이놈입니다."], ["“This is he: but he will win, for, look you, sheik, he will run his utmost all day—all day; and, as the sun goes down, he will reach his swiftest.”", "하지만 이놈이 이길 겁니다. 보십시오, 족장이시여, 이놈은 온종일 제 힘을 다해 달릴 겁니다—온종일 말입니다. 그리고 해가 질 무렵이면 가장 빠른 속도에 이를 겁니다.\""]], [["“Right again,”", "\"또 맞는 말이군.\""], ["said Ilderim.", "일데림이 말했다."]], [["“I have but one fear, O sheik.”", "\"한 가지 두려운 것이 있습니다, 족장이시여.\""]], [["The sheik became doubly serious.", "족장은 갑절로 진지해졌다."]], [["“In his greed of triumph, a Roman cannot keep honor pure.", "\"승리에 눈이 먼 로마인은 명예를 깨끗이 지키지 못합니다."], ["In the games—all of them, mark you—their tricks are infinite; in chariot racing their knavery extends to everything—from horse to driver, from driver to master.", "경기에서는—모든 경기에서, 명심하십시오—그들의 술책이 끝이 없습니다. 전차 경주에서는 그 간계가 온갖 것에 미칩니다—말에서 마부에게로, 마부에서 주인에게로 말입니다."], ["Wherefore, good sheik, look well to all thou hast; from this till the trial is over, let no stranger so much as see the horses.", "그러니, 족장이시여, 가지신 모든 것을 잘 지키십시오. 지금부터 시합이 끝날 때까지, 낯선 자가 말들을 눈으로 보는 것조차 허락하지 마십시오."], ["Would you be perfectly safe, do more—keep watch over them with armed hand as well as sleepless eye; then I will have no fear of the end.”", "완전히 안전하기를 바라신다면, 그 이상을 하십시오—잠들지 않는 눈만이 아니라 무장한 손으로 지키십시오. 그러면 저는 결과를 두려워하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["At the door of the tent they dismounted.", "천막 문 앞에서 그들은 말에서 내렸다."]], [["“What you say shall be attended to. By the splendor of God, no hand shall come near them except it belong to one of the faithful.", "\"자네 말대로 하겠네. 하느님의 영광을 두고 맹세컨대, 충성스러운 자들의 손이 아니고서는 어떤 손도 말들 가까이 오지 못할 것일세."], ["To-night I will set watches.", "오늘 밤 보초를 세우겠네."], ["But, son of Arrius”—Ilderim drew forth the package, and opened it slowly, while they walked to the divan and seated themselves—“son of Arrius, see thou here, and help me with thy Latin.”", "그건 그렇고, 아리우스의 아들이여\"—일데림은 꾸러미를 꺼내 천천히 펼쳤다. 그들이 긴 의자로 걸어가 자리에 앉는 동안이었다—\"아리우스의 아들이여, 여기 좀 보고, 자네의 라틴어로 나를 도와주게.\""]], [["He passed the despatch to Ben-Hur.", "그는 그 문서를 벤허에게 건넸다."]], [["“There; read—and read aloud, rendering what thou findest into the tongue of thy fathers.", "\"자, 읽어 보게—소리 내어 읽되, 거기 적힌 것을 자네 조상의 말로 옮겨 주게."], ["Latin is an abomination.”", "라틴어는 끔찍한 것이거든.\""]], [["Ben-Hur was in good spirits, and began the reading carelessly. “‘MESSALA TO GRATUS!’”", "벤허는 기분이 좋았던 터라, 무심히 읽기 시작했다. \"'메살라가 그라투스에게!'\""], ["He paused. A premonition drove the blood to his heart.", "그는 멈추었다. 어떤 예감이 피를 가슴으로 몰아붙였다."], ["Ilderim observed his agitation.", "일데림이 그의 동요를 알아차렸다."]], [["“Well; I am waiting.”", "\"자, 기다리고 있네.\""]], [["Ben-Hur prayed pardon, and recommenced the paper, which, it is sufficient to say, was one of the duplicates of the letter despatched so carefully to Gratus by Messala the morning after the revel in the palace.", "벤허는 용서를 구하고, 다시 문서를 읽기 시작했다. 그 문서는 궁전에서 향연이 벌어진 다음 날 아침, 메살라가 그토록 조심스럽게 그라투스에게 보낸 편지의 사본 가운데 하나였다는 것만 밝혀 두면 충분하다."]], [["The paragraphs in the beginning were remarkable only as proof that the writer had not outgrown his habit of mockery; when they were passed, and the reader came to the parts intended to refresh the memory of Gratus, his voice trembled, and twice he stopped to regain his self-control.", "서두의 몇 단락은 글쓴이가 조롱하는 버릇을 아직 버리지 못했음을 보여 주는 증거로서만 주목할 만했다. 그 부분을 지나, 읽는 이가 그라투스의 기억을 되살리려는 대목에 이르자, 그의 목소리는 떨렸고, 두 번이나 멈추어 마음을 가다듬어야 했다. 그는 안간힘을 다해 계속 읽었다."], ["By a strong effort he continued. “‘I recall further,’”", "\"'또 한 가지 떠오르는 것은,'\" 그가 읽었다."], ["he read, “‘that thou didst make disposition of the family of Hur’”—there the reader again paused and drew a long breath—“‘both of us at the time supposing the plan hit upon to be the most effective possible for the purposes in view, which were silence and delivery over to inevitable but natural death.’”", "\"'그대가 후르 가문을 처리한 일이오'\"—여기서 읽는 이는 다시 멈추어 긴 숨을 들이쉬었다—\"'그때 우리 둘 다, 우리가 떠올린 그 방법이 목적을 이루는 데 가장 효과적이라 여겼소. 그 목적이란 침묵, 그리고 피할 수 없으나 자연스러운 죽음에 그들을 넘기는 것이었소.'\""]], [["Here Ben-Hur broke down utterly.", "여기서 벤허는 완전히 무너졌다."], ["The paper fell from his hands, and he covered his face.", "문서가 그의 손에서 떨어졌고, 그는 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“They are dead—dead.", "\"그들은 죽었구나—죽었어."], ["I alone am left.”", "나 혼자만 남았어.\""]], [["The sheik had been a silent, but not unsympathetic, witness of the young man’s suffering; now he arose and said, “Son of Arrius, it is for me to beg thy pardon.", "족장은 말없이, 그러나 동정 어린 마음으로 젊은이의 고통을 지켜보고 있었다. 이제 그는 일어서서 말했다. \"아리우스의 아들이여, 이번엔 내가 자네에게 용서를 구해야겠네."], ["Read the paper by thyself.", "그 문서는 자네 혼자 읽게."], ["When thou art strong enough to give the rest of it to me, send word, and I will return.”", "남은 부분을 나에게 들려줄 만큼 마음이 추슬러지면, 기별을 주게. 그러면 돌아오겠네.\""]], [["He went out of the tent, and nothing in all his life became him better.", "그는 천막을 나갔다. 그의 평생에 그보다 더 어울리는 행동은 없었다."]], [["Ben-Hur flung himself on the divan and gave way to his feelings.", "벤허는 긴 의자에 몸을 던지고 감정에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["When somewhat recovered, he recollected that a portion of the letter remained unread, and, taking it up, he resumed the reading.", "어느 정도 마음을 추스르자, 편지의 한 부분을 아직 읽지 않은 것이 떠올랐다. 그는 그것을 집어 들고 다시 읽기 시작했다."], ["“Thou wilt remember,”", "\"그대도 기억할 것이오,\""], ["the missive ran, “what thou didst with the mother and sister of the malefactor; yet, if now I yield to a desire to learn if they be living or dead”—Ben-Hur started, and read again, and then again, and at last broke into exclamation.", "편지는 이어졌다. \"그 죄인의 어미와 누이를 어찌 처리했는지를. 그렇지만, 지금 내가 그들이 살았는지 죽었는지 알고 싶은 마음에 굴복한다면\"—벤허는 흠칫하여, 다시 읽고, 또다시 읽다가, 마침내 탄성을 터뜨렸다."], ["“He does not know they are dead; he does not know it!", "\"이자는 그들이 죽은 줄 모르는구나. 모르고 있어!"], ["Blessed be the name of the Lord!", "주님의 이름에 복이 있기를!"], ["there is yet hope.”", "아직 희망이 있어.\""], ["He finished the sentence, and was strengthened by it, and went on bravely to the end of the letter.", "그는 그 문장을 끝까지 읽었고, 그것에 힘을 얻어, 용기를 내어 편지의 끝까지 읽어 나갔다."]], [["“They are not dead,”", "\"그들은 죽지 않았어,\""], ["he said, after reflection; “they are not dead, or he would have heard of it.”", "그는 생각에 잠긴 뒤 말했다. \"그들은 죽지 않았어. 안 그랬다면 이자가 그 소식을 들었을 테지.\""]], [["A second reading, more careful than the first, confirmed him in the opinion. Then he sent for the sheik.", "처음보다 더 신중하게 두 번째로 읽고 나니, 그 생각이 굳어졌다. 그러고 나서 그는 족장을 불러오게 했다."]], [["“In coming to your hospitable tent, O sheik,”", "\"환대해 주시는 천막에 들어오면서, 족장이시여,\""], ["he said, calmly, when the Arab was seated and they were alone, “it was not in my mind to speak of myself further than to assure you I had sufficient training to be intrusted with your horses.", "아랍인이 자리에 앉고 둘만 남게 되자, 그는 차분히 말했다. \"저는 제 말들을 맡길 만큼 충분한 훈련을 받았다는 것을 보여드리는 것 말고는, 제 자신에 대해 더 말씀드릴 생각이 없었습니다."], ["I declined to tell you my history.", "제 내력을 말씀드리기를 사양했지요."], ["But the chances which have sent this paper to my hand and given it to me to be read are so strange that I feel bidden to trust you with everything.", "그러나 이 문서를 제 손에 들어오게 하고 제가 읽도록 한 우연이 너무도 기이하여, 모든 것을 당신께 털어놓아야겠다는 마음이 듭니다."], ["And I am the more inclined to do so by knowledge here conveyed that we are both of us threatened by the same enemy, against whom it is needful that we make common cause.", "더욱이 이 편지가 알려 준 바, 우리 둘이 같은 적에게 위협받고 있으며, 그자에 맞서 우리가 힘을 합쳐야 한다는 사실을 알았기에, 더욱 그러고 싶어집니다."], ["I will read the letter and give you explanation; after which you will not wonder I was so moved.", "편지를 읽고 설명을 드리겠습니다. 그러고 나면 제가 왜 그토록 흔들렸는지 의아해하지 않으실 겁니다."], ["If you thought me weak or childish, you will then excuse me.”", "저를 나약하다거나 어린아이 같다고 여기셨다면, 그때 양해해 주실 겁니다.\""]], [["The sheik held his peace, listening closely, until Ben-Hur came to the paragraph in which he was particularly mentioned: “‘I saw the Jew yesterday in the Grove of Daphne;’”", "족장은 입을 다물고 귀를 기울여 듣다가, 벤허가 자신이 특별히 언급된 단락에 이르자 더욱 그러했다. \"'어제 다프네 숲에서 그 유대인을 보았소.'\" 그 부분은 이렇게 이어졌다."], ["so ran the part, “‘and if he be not there now, he is certainly in the neighborhood, making it easy for me to keep him in eye.", "\"'그리고 지금 거기에 없다 해도, 분명 그 근처에 있을 터이니, 내가 그를 눈여겨 살피기는 쉬운 일이오."], ["Indeed, wert thou to ask me where he is now, I should say, with the most positive assurance, he is to be found at the old Orchard of Palms.’”", "실로, 그대가 나에게 지금 그가 어디 있느냐고 묻는다면, 나는 더없는 확신을 가지고, 그가 옛 종려나무 과수원에 있을 거라고 말하겠소.'\""]], [["“A—h!”", "\"아—!\""], ["exclaimed Ilderim, in such a tone one might hardly say he was more surprised than angry; at the same time, he clutched his beard.", "일데림이 외쳤다. 놀랐다기보다 화가 났다고 하기 어려운 어조였다. 동시에 그는 수염을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“‘At the old Orchard of Palms,’”", "\"'옛 종려나무 과수원에,'\""], ["Ben-Hur repeated, “‘under the tent of the traitor Shiek Ilderim.’”", "벤허가 거듭 읽었다. \"'배신자 일데림 족장의 천막 아래에 말이오.'\""]], [["“Traitor!—I?”", "\"배신자라고!—내가?\""], ["the old man cried, in his shrillest tone, while lip and beard curled with ire, and on his forehead and neck the veins swelled and beat as they would burst.", "노인이 더없이 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. 입술과 수염이 분노로 일그러지고, 이마와 목에는 핏줄이 터질 듯 부풀어 올라 펄떡였다."]], [["“Yet a moment, sheik,”", "\"잠시만 더, 족장이시여,\""], ["said Ben-Hur, with a deprecatory gesture.", "벤허가 말리는 몸짓으로 말했다."], ["“Such is Messala’s opinion of you.", "\"이것은 당신에 대한 메살라의 생각입니다."], ["Hear his threat.” And he read on—“‘under the tent of the traitor Sheik Ilderim, who cannot long escape our strong hand.", "그의 위협을 들어 보십시오.\" 그러고 그는 계속 읽었다—\"'배신자 일데림 족장의 천막 아래에 있으니, 그자도 우리의 강한 손을 오래 피하지는 못할 것이오."], ["Be not surprised if Maxentius, as his first measure, places the Arab on ship for forwarding to Rome.’”", "막센티우스가 그 첫 조치로 그 아랍인을 배에 태워 로마로 보낸다 해도 놀라지 마시오.'\""]], [["“To Rome!", "\"로마로!"], ["Me—Ilderim—sheik of ten thousand horsemen with spears—me to Rome!”", "나를—이 일데림을—창을 든 기마병 일만 명의 족장인 나를—로마로 보낸다고!\""]], [["He leaped rather than rose to his feet, his arms outstretched, his fingers spread and curved like claws, his eyes glittering like a serpent’s.", "그는 일어선다기보다 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다. 두 팔을 쭉 뻗고, 손가락을 발톱처럼 펴서 구부리고, 두 눈은 뱀의 그것처럼 번득였다."]], [["“O God!—nay, by all the gods except of Rome!—when shall this insolence end?", "\"오 하느님!—아니, 로마의 신들을 뺀 모든 신을 두고 맹세컨대!—이 오만함이 언제나 끝이 날 것인가?"], ["A freeman am I; free are my people.", "나는 자유인이다. 내 백성도 자유롭다."], ["Must we die slaves?", "우리가 노예로 죽어야 한단 말인가?"], ["Or, worse, must I live a dog, crawling to a master’s feet?", "아니, 그보다 더 못하게, 내가 주인의 발치에 기어가는 개로 살아야 한단 말인가?"], ["Must I lick his hand, lest he lash me?", "매를 맞지 않으려고 그자의 손을 핥아야 한단 말인가?"], ["What is mine is not mine; I am not my own; for breath of body I must be beholden to a Roman.", "내 것이 내 것이 아니고, 나는 내 것이 아니구나. 몸뚱이의 숨결조차 로마인에게 빚져야 하다니."], ["Oh, if I were young again!", "오, 내가 다시 젊어질 수만 있다면!"], ["Oh, could I shake off twenty years—or ten—or five!”", "오, 스무 해를—아니 열 해를—아니 다섯 해라도 떨쳐 버릴 수 있다면!\""]], [["He ground his teeth and shook his hands overhead; then, under the impulse of another idea, he walked away and back again to Ben-Hur swiftly, and caught his shoulder with a strong grasp.", "그는 이를 갈며 머리 위로 두 손을 흔들었다. 그러더니 또 다른 생각에 사로잡혀, 성큼성큼 걸어갔다가 다시 벤허에게로 돌아와, 억센 손아귀로 그의 어깨를 붙들었다."]], [["“If I were as thou, son of Arrius—as young, as strong, as practised in arms; if I had a motive hissing me to revenge—a motive, like thine, great enough to make hate holy— Away with disguise on thy part and on mine!", "\"내가 자네 같다면, 아리우스의 아들이여—그토록 젊고, 그토록 강하고, 무예에 그토록 단련되어 있다면. 복수를 부추기는 동기가 있다면—자네의 것처럼, 증오를 거룩하게 만들 만큼 큰 동기가 있다면— 자네도 나도 위장은 그만두세!"], ["Son of Hur, son of Hur, I say—”", "후르의 아들, 후르의 아들이여, 내가 말하노니—\""]], [["At that name all the currents of Ben-Hur’s blood stopped; surprised, bewildered, he gazed into the Arab’s eyes, now close to his, and fiercely bright.", "그 이름에 벤허의 온몸의 피가 멎었다. 놀랍고 어리둥절하여, 그는 이제 제 얼굴에 바싹 다가온, 사납게 번득이는 아랍인의 두 눈을 응시했다."]], [["“Son of Hur, I say, were I as thou, with half thy wrongs, bearing about with me memories like thine, I would not, I could not, rest.”", "\"후르의 아들이여, 내가 말하노니, 내가 자네 같다면, 자네가 당한 부당함의 절반만이라도 당했다면, 자네 같은 기억을 안고 다닌다면, 나는 결코 가만히 있지 않을 것이며, 있을 수도 없을 것이네.\""], ["Never pausing, his words following each other torrent-like, the old man swept on.", "노인은 한 번도 멈추지 않고, 말들이 격류처럼 잇따라 쏟아져 나오며 거침없이 이어 갔다."], ["“To all my grievances, I would add those of the world, and devote myself to vengeance.", "\"내 모든 원한에다 온 세상의 원한을 보태어, 복수에 나 자신을 바치겠네."], ["From land to land I would go firing all mankind.", "이 땅에서 저 땅으로 다니며 온 인류에 불을 지르겠네."], ["No war for freedom but should find me engaged; no battle against Rome in which I would not bear a part.", "자유를 위한 전쟁에는 빠짐없이 끼어들겠고, 로마에 맞선 싸움에는 어느 것에든 한몫 거들겠네."], ["I would turn Parthian, if I could not better.", "달리 나을 길이 없다면 파르티아인이라도 되겠네."], ["If men failed me, still I would not give over the effort—ha, ha, ha!", "사람들이 나를 저버린다 해도, 나는 그 노력을 그만두지 않을 것이네—하, 하, 하!"], ["By the splendor of God!", "하느님의 영광을 두고 맹세컨대!"], ["I would herd with wolves, and make friends of lions and tigers, in hope of marshalling them against the common enemy.", "나는 이리 떼와 무리를 이루고, 사자와 호랑이를 벗 삼아, 공동의 적에 맞서 그것들을 부릴 수 있기를 바라겠네."], ["I would use every weapon.", "나는 모든 무기를 쓸 것이네."], ["So my victims were Romans, I would rejoice in slaughter.", "내 희생자가 로마인이기만 하다면, 나는 살육 속에서 기뻐하겠네."], ["Quarter I would not ask; quarter I would not give.", "자비를 구하지도 않을 것이며, 자비를 베풀지도 않을 것이네."], ["To the flames everything Roman; to the sword every Roman born.", "로마의 것이라면 무엇이든 불길 속에 던지고, 로마에서 태어난 자라면 누구든 칼에 부치겠네."], ["Of nights I would pray the gods, the good and the bad alike, to lend me their special terrors—tempests, drought, heat, cold, and all the nameless poisons they let loose in air, all the thousand things of which men die on sea and on land.", "밤이면 신들에게, 선한 신이든 악한 신이든 가리지 않고 기도하여, 그들의 특별한 공포를 빌려 달라 하겠네—폭풍, 가뭄, 더위, 추위, 그리고 그들이 공중에 풀어놓는 이름 없는 온갖 독, 사람들이 바다와 뭍에서 죽어 나가는 그 수천 가지 것들을 말이네."], ["Oh, I could not sleep. I—I—”", "오, 나는 잠을 이룰 수 없을 것이네. 나는—나는—\""]], [["The sheik stopped for want of breath, panting, wringing his hands.", "족장은 숨이 차서 멈추었다. 헐떡이며 두 손을 쥐어짰다."], ["And, sooth to say, of all the passionate burst Ben-Hur retained but a vague impression wrought by fiery eyes, a piercing voice, and a rage too intense for coherent expression.", "그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그 격정의 폭발 가운데 벤허에게 남은 것은 불타는 눈, 꿰뚫는 목소리, 조리 있게 풀어내기에는 너무도 격렬한 분노가 빚어낸 어렴풋한 인상뿐이었다."]], [["For the first time in years, the desolate youth heard himself addressed by his proper name.", "여러 해 만에 처음으로, 외로운 젊은이는 자신이 제 본명으로 불리는 것을 들었다."], ["One man at least knew him, and acknowledged it without demand of identity; and he an Arab fresh from the desert!", "적어도 한 사람은 그를 알아보았고, 신분을 캐묻지도 않고 그것을 인정했다. 더구나 그자는 사막에서 갓 나온 아랍인이었다!"]], [["How came the man by his knowledge?", "그자는 어떻게 그것을 알게 되었을까?"], ["The letter? No. It told the cruelties from which his family had suffered; it told the story of his own misfortunes, but it did not say he was the very victim whose escape from doom was the theme of the heartless narrative.", "편지를 통해서? 아니다. 편지는 그의 가족이 당한 잔혹함을 말해 주었고, 그 자신의 불행을 이야기해 주었으나, 그 무정한 이야기의 주제가 된, 파멸에서 벗어난 바로 그 희생자가 그라는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["That was the point of explanation he had notified the sheik would follow the reading of the letter.", "바로 그 점이, 편지를 다 읽은 뒤에 이어질 것이라고 그가 족장에게 알려 두었던 설명할 대목이었다."], ["He was pleased, and thrilled with hope restored, yet kept an air of calmness.", "그는 기뻤고, 되살아난 희망에 가슴이 떨렸으나, 짐짓 침착한 태도를 지켰다."]], [["“Good sheik, tell me how you came by this letter.”", "\"족장이시여, 이 편지를 어떻게 손에 넣으셨는지 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“My people keep the roads between cities,”", "\"내 사람들이 도시와 도시 사이의 길을 지킨다네,\""], ["Ilderim answered, bluntly.", "일데림이 퉁명스럽게 대답했다."], ["“They took it from a courier.”", "\"전령에게서 빼앗은 것일세.\""]], [["“Are they known to be thy people?”", "\"그들이 당신의 사람들로 알려져 있습니까?\""]], [["“No. To the world they are robbers, whom it is mine to catch and slay.”", "\"아닐세. 세상이 보기에 그들은 도둑이고, 내가 잡아서 죽여야 할 자들이지.\""]], [["“Again, sheik. You call me son of Hur—my father’s name.", "\"다시 여쭙겠습니다, 족장이시여. 당신은 저를 후르의 아들이라 부르셨습니다—제 아버지의 이름이지요."], ["I did not think myself known to a person on earth.", "저는 이 세상 누구도 저를 알지 못한다고 여겼습니다."], ["How came you by the knowledge?”", "어떻게 그것을 아셨습니까?\""]], [["Ilderim hesitated; but, rallying, he answered, “I know you, yet I am not free to tell you more.”", "일데림은 머뭇거렸다. 그러나 마음을 다잡고 대답했다. \"나는 자네를 아네. 그렇지만 그 이상은 자유로이 말할 수가 없네.\""]], [["“Some one holds you in restraint?”", "\"누군가가 당신을 묶어 두고 있습니까?\""]], [["The sheik closed his mouth, and walked away; but, observing Ben-Hur’s disappointment, he came back, and said, “Let us say no more about the matter now.", "족장은 입을 닫고 걸어가 버렸다. 그러나 벤허의 실망한 기색을 보고는 다시 돌아와 말했다. \"그 일은 지금 더 말하지 마세."], ["I will go to town; when I return, I may talk to you fully.", "나는 시내로 가겠네. 돌아오면 자네에게 다 이야기할 수 있을지도 모르지."], ["Give me the letter.”", "편지를 주게.\""]], [["Ilderim rolled the papyrus carefully, restored it to its envelopes, and became once more all energy.", "일데림은 파피루스를 조심스럽게 말아 봉투에 도로 넣고, 다시금 온통 활기에 넘쳤다."]], [["“What sayest thou?” he asked, while waiting for his horse and retinue.", "\"자네는 어찌 말하려는가?\" 그는 말과 수행원들을 기다리며 물었다."], ["“I told what I would do, were I thou, and thou hast made no answer.”", "\"내가 자네라면 어찌하겠다고 말했건만, 자네는 아무 대답이 없었네.\""]], [["“I intended to answer, sheik, and I will.”", "\"대답할 생각이었습니다, 족장이시여."], ["Ben-Hur’s countenance and voice changed with the feeling invoked.", "그리고 대답하겠습니다.\" 벤허의 얼굴과 목소리는 솟구치는 감정과 더불어 달라졌다."], ["“All thou hast said, I will do—all at least in the power of a man.", "\"당신이 말씀하신 모든 것을 저는 하겠습니다—적어도 한 사람의 힘이 미치는 한 모두 다 말입니다."], ["I devoted myself to vengeance long ago.", "저는 오래전에 복수에 제 자신을 바쳤습니다."], ["Every hour of the five years passed, I have lived with no other thought.", "지나간 다섯 해의 매 순간을, 저는 오로지 그 생각만으로 살아왔습니다."], ["I have taken no respite.", "한순간도 쉬지 않았습니다."], ["I have had no pleasures of youth.", "젊음의 즐거움이라고는 누리지 못했습니다."], ["The blandishments of Rome were not for me.", "로마의 온갖 유혹도 저에게는 아무것도 아니었습니다."], ["I wanted her to educate me for revenge.", "저는 로마가 저를 복수를 위해 길러 주기를 바랐습니다."], ["I resorted to her most famous masters and professors—not those of rhetoric or philosophy: alas!", "저는 로마의 가장 이름난 스승과 교사들을 찾아갔습니다—수사학이나 철학을 가르치는 자들이 아니라 말입니다. 아아!"], ["I had no time for them.", "그런 것에는 시간이 없었습니다."], ["The arts essential to a fighting-man were my desire.", "싸우는 자에게 꼭 필요한 기예가 제가 바라는 것이었지요."], ["I associated with gladiators, and with winners of prizes in the Circus; and they were my teachers.", "저는 검투사들과, 원형 경기장에서 상을 탄 자들과 어울렸고, 그들이 제 스승이었습니다."], ["The drill-masters in the great camp accepted me as a scholar, and were proud of my attainments in their line.", "대규모 병영의 훈련 교관들은 저를 제자로 받아들였고, 그들의 분야에서 제가 이룬 성취를 자랑스러워했습니다."], ["O sheik, I am a soldier; but the things of which I dream require me to be a captain.", "오 족장이시여, 저는 군인입니다. 그러나 제가 꿈꾸는 것들은 제가 지휘관이 되기를 요구합니다."], ["With that thought, I have taken part in the campaign against the Parthians; when it is over, then, if the Lord spare my life and strength—then”—he raised his clenched hands, and spoke vehemently—“then I will be an enemy Roman-taught in all things; then Rome shall account to me in Roman lives for her ills.", "그 생각으로, 저는 파르티아인에 맞선 원정에 참가했습니다. 그것이 끝나면, 그때, 주님께서 제 목숨과 힘을 살려 주신다면—그때\"—그는 움켜쥔 두 손을 들어 올리며 격렬하게 말했다—\"그때 저는 로마가 가르친 모든 것으로 무장한 적이 될 것입니다. 그때 로마는 저에게 그 죄악을 로마인의 목숨으로 갚을 것입니다."], ["You have my answer, sheik.”", "이것이 제 대답입니다, 족장이시여.\""]], [["Ilderim put an arm over his shoulder, and kissed him, saying, passionately, “If thy God favor thee not, son of Hur, it is because he is dead.", "일데림은 그의 어깨에 한 팔을 두르고 입을 맞추며, 격정에 차서 말했다. \"자네의 하느님이 자네를 돌보지 않는다면, 후르의 아들이여, 그것은 그분이 죽었기 때문일 것이네."], ["Take thou this from me—sworn to, if so thy preference run: thou shalt have my hands, and their fulness—men, horses, camels, and the desert for preparation.", "내게서 이것을 받아 두게—자네가 원한다면 맹세코 하리니, 자네는 내 두 손과 그 손이 가득 채운 것을 가지게 될 걸세—사람과 말과 낙타와, 그리고 준비를 위한 이 사막을 말이네."], ["I swear it!", "내 맹세하노라!"], ["For the present, enough.", "지금은 이것으로 충분하네."], ["Thou shalt see or hear from me before night.”", "밤이 되기 전에 자네는 나를 보거나 내 기별을 듣게 될 걸세.\""]], [["Turning abruptly off, the sheik was speedily on the road to the city.", "족장은 불쑥 돌아서더니, 어느새 시내로 가는 길에 올라 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["The intercepted letter was conclusive upon a number of points of great interest to Ben-Hur.", "가로챈 편지는 벤허에게 큰 관심사인 여러 사항을 명백히 밝혀 주었다."], ["It had all the effect of a confession that the writer was a party to the putting-away of the family with murderous intent; that he had sanctioned the plan adopted for the purpose; that he had received a portion of the proceeds of the confiscation, and was yet in enjoyment of his part; that he dreaded the unexpected appearance of what he was pleased to call the chief malefactor, and accepted it as a menace; that he contemplated such further action as would secure him in the future, and was ready to do whatever his accomplice in Cæsarea might advise.", "그것은 한낱 자백이나 다름없었다. 편지를 쓴 자가 살의를 품고 그 일가를 처치하는 데 가담했다는 것, 그 목적을 위해 채택된 계획을 승인했다는 것, 몰수한 재산의 일부를 받아 지금도 그 몫을 누리고 있다는 것, 그가 즐겨 일컫는 이른바 주범의 예기치 못한 출현을 두려워하며 그것을 위협으로 받아들였다는 것, 앞으로 자신을 안전하게 지켜 줄 다음 행동을 궁리하고 있으며, 카이사레아의 공범이 권하는 일이라면 무엇이든 할 준비가 되어 있다는 것—이 모든 것을 자백한 셈이었다."]], [["And, now that the letter had reached the hand of him really its subject, it was notice of danger to come, as well as a confession of guilt.", "그리고 이제 그 편지가 정작 그 글의 진짜 대상인 사람의 손에 들어왔으니, 그것은 죄의 고백인 동시에 다가올 위험을 알리는 통지였다."], ["So when Ilderim left the tent, Ben-Hur had much to think about, requiring immediate action.", "그래서 일데림이 천막을 나갔을 때, 벤허에게는 곰곰이 생각해야 할 일이 많았고, 그 일에는 즉각적인 행동이 필요했다."], ["His enemies were as adroit and powerful as any in the East.", "그의 적들은 동방의 누구 못지않게 교활하고 강력했다."], ["If they were afraid of him, he had greater reason to be afraid of them.", "그들이 그를 두려워한다면, 그가 그들을 두려워할 까닭은 더욱 컸다."], ["He strove earnestly to reflect upon the situation, but could not; his feelings constantly overwhelmed him.", "그는 처지를 진지하게 헤아려 보려 애썼으나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 감정이 끊임없이 그를 압도했기 때문이다."], ["There was a certain qualified pleasure in the assurance that his mother and sister were alive; and it mattered little that the foundation of the assurance was a mere inference.", "어머니와 누이가 살아 있다는 확신에는 어떤 절제된 기쁨이 있었다. 그 확신의 근거가 한낱 추론에 불과하다는 것은 그다지 중요하지 않았다."], ["That there was one person who could tell him where they were seemed to his hope so long deferred as if discovery were now close at hand.", "그들이 어디에 있는지 일러 줄 사람이 하나 있다는 사실은, 그토록 오래 미뤄진 그의 희망에는 마치 발견이 이제 코앞에 다가온 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["These were mere causes of feeling; underlying them, it must be confessed he had a superstitious fancy that God was about to make ordination in his behalf, in which event faith whispered him to stand still.", "이것들은 한낱 감정의 원인일 뿐이었다. 그 밑바닥에는, 고백건대, 하느님이 자신을 위해 무언가를 마련해 주시려 한다는 미신 같은 상상이 깔려 있었고, 그런 경우라면 가만히 기다리라고 신앙이 그에게 속삭이는 것이었다."]], [["Occasionally, referring to the words of Ilderim, he wondered whence the Arab derived his information about him; not from Malluch certainly; nor from Simonides, whose interests, all adverse, would hold him dumb.", "이따금 일데림의 말을 떠올리며, 그는 그 아랍인이 자신에 관한 정보를 어디서 얻었는지 궁금해했다. 멀룩에게서 얻은 것은 분명 아니었고, 시모니데스에게서도 아니었으니, 그는 이해관계가 온통 상반되어 입을 다물 수밖에 없는 사람이었다."], ["Could Messala have been the informant?", "메살라가 정보를 준 자일 수 있을까?"], ["No, no: disclosure might be dangerous in that quarter.", "아니, 아니다. 그쪽에서는 발설이 위험할 수 있었다."], ["Conjecture was vain; at the same time, often as Ben-Hur was beaten back from the solution, he was consoled with the thought that whoever the person with the knowledge might be, he was a friend, and, being such, would reveal himself in good time.", "추측은 부질없었다. 그러나 벤허는 해답에서 번번이 물러서면서도, 그 지식을 가진 사람이 누구든 친구일 것이며, 친구라면 때가 되면 스스로 모습을 드러내리라는 생각에 위안을 얻었다. 조금만 더 기다리면 되었다."], ["A little more waiting—a little more patience. Possibly the errand of the sheik was to see the worthy; possibly the letter might precipitate a full disclosure.", "조금만 더 참으면 되었다. 어쩌면 족장의 볼일은 그 가치 있는 사람을 만나는 일인지도 몰랐고, 어쩌면 그 편지가 모든 것의 완전한 폭로를 앞당길지도 몰랐다."]], [["And patient he would have been if only he could have believed Tirzah and his mother were waiting for him under circumstances permitting hope on their part strong as his; if, in other words, conscience had not stung him with accusations respecting them.", "티르자와 어머니가, 자신만큼이나 강한 희망을 품을 만한 처지에서 그를 기다리고 있다고 믿을 수만 있었다면, 다시 말해 그들을 두고 양심이 그를 가책으로 찌르지만 않았다면, 그는 능히 참을 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["To escape such accusations, he wandered far through the Orchard, pausing now where the date-gatherers were busy, yet not too busy to offer him of their fruit and talk with him; then, under the great trees, to watch the nesting birds, or hear the bees swarming about the berries bursting with honeyed sweetness, and filling all the green and golden spaces with the music of their beating wings.", "그런 가책에서 벗어나려고 그는 과수원을 멀리 헤매고 다녔다. 대추야자를 따는 일꾼들이 바삐 일하는 곳에 잠시 멈추었으나, 그들은 그에게 열매를 권하고 이야기를 나눌 만큼은 한가했다. 그러다 큰 나무 아래에서 둥지를 트는 새들을 바라보거나, 꿀같이 단 즙으로 터질 듯한 열매 주위로 벌들이 떼 지어 모여드는 소리를, 그리고 푸르고 황금빛으로 물든 온 공간을 날갯짓 소리로 채우는 소리를 듣곤 했다."]], [["By the lake, however, he lingered longest.", "그러나 호숫가에서 그는 가장 오래 머물렀다."], ["He might not look upon the water and its sparkling ripples, so like sensuous life, without thinking of the Egyptian and her marvellous beauty, and of floating with her here and there through the night, made brilliant by her songs and stories; he might not forget the charm of her manner, the lightness of her laugh, the flattery of her attention, the warmth of her little hand under his upon the tiller of the boat.", "관능적인 생명처럼 반짝이는 잔물결이 일렁이는 물을 바라보면서, 그 이집트 여인과 그녀의 경이로운 아름다움을, 그리고 그녀의 노래와 이야기로 찬란하게 빛나던 밤에 그녀와 함께 여기저기 떠다니던 일을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀의 몸짓이 지닌 매력을, 가벼운 웃음소리를, 자신에게 쏟던 관심의 달콤함을, 배의 키 위에 얹은 자신의 손 아래로 느껴지던 그녀의 작은 손의 온기를 잊을 수 없었다."], ["From her it was for his thought but a short way to Balthasar, and the strange things of which he had been witness, unaccountable by any law of nature; and from him, again, to the King of the Jews, whom the good man, with such pathos of patience, was holding in holy promise, the distance was even nearer.", "그녀에게서 발타사르에게로 생각이 옮겨 가는 것은 그저 짧은 걸음에 지나지 않았고, 자연의 어떤 법칙으로도 설명할 수 없는, 그가 목격한 기이한 일들로 이어지는 것 또한 마찬가지였다. 그리고 거기서 다시, 그 선량한 노인이 그토록 애틋한 인내로 거룩한 약속 속에 붙들고 있는 유대인의 왕에게로 이어지는 거리는 더욱 가까웠다."], ["And there his mind stayed, finding in the mysteries of that personage a satisfaction answering well for the rest he was seeking.", "그리고 그의 마음은 거기에 머물렀으니, 그 인물의 신비 속에서 그가 찾던 안식에 잘 답해 주는 만족을 얻었기 때문이다."], ["Because, it may have been, nothing is so easy as denial of an idea not agreeable to our wishes, he rejected the definition given by Balthasar of the kingdom the king was coming to establish.", "어쩌면 우리의 바람에 맞지 않는 생각을 부정하는 것만큼 쉬운 일은 없기에, 그는 그 왕이 세우러 온다는 나라에 대해 발타사르가 내린 정의를 거부했다."], ["A kingdom of souls, if not intolerable to his Sadducean faith, seemed to him but an abstraction drawn from the depths of a devotion too fond and dreamy.", "영혼의 나라란, 사두개인다운 그의 신앙에 견딜 수 없는 것까지는 아니더라도, 너무도 애틋하고 몽상적인 헌신의 깊은 곳에서 끌어낸 한낱 관념에 불과하게 여겨졌다."], ["A kingdom of Judea, on the other hand, was more than comprehensible: such had been, and, if only for that reason, might be again.", "반면에 유대의 나라란 충분히 이해할 만한 것이었다. 그런 나라는 일찍이 존재했고, 다만 그 이유만으로도 다시 존재할 수 있었다."], ["And it suited his pride to think of a new kingdom broader of domain, richer in power, and of a more unapproachable splendor than the old one; of a new king wiser and mightier than Solomon—a new king under whom, especially, he could find both service and revenge.", "옛 나라보다 영토가 더 넓고, 권세가 더 풍부하며, 더 범접할 수 없는 영화를 누리는 새로운 나라를 떠올리는 것, 솔로몬보다 더 지혜롭고 더 강성한 새 왕을, 무엇보다도 자신이 봉직할 곳과 복수할 길을 함께 찾을 수 있는 새 왕을 떠올리는 것은 그의 자존심에 맞아떨어졌다."], ["In that mood he resumed to the dowar.", "그런 심정으로 그는 천막촌으로 돌아왔다."]], [["The mid-day meal disposed of, still further to occupy himself, Ben-Hur had the chariot rolled out into the sunlight for inspection.", "점심을 마친 뒤에도 무언가에 더 몰두하려고, 벤허는 점검을 위해 전차를 햇빛 속으로 끌어내게 했다."], ["The word but poorly conveys the careful study the vehicle underwent. No point or part of it escaped him.", "점검이라는 말로는 그 수레가 받은 세심한 연구를 제대로 전하지 못한다. 그것의 어떤 점도, 어떤 부분도 그의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다."], ["With a pleasure which will be better understood hereafter, he saw the pattern was Greek, in his judgment preferable to the Roman in many respects; it was wider between the wheels, and lower and stronger, and the disadvantage of greater weight would be more than compensated by the greater endurance of his Arabs.", "뒤에 가서 더 잘 이해될 어떤 기쁨을 느끼며, 그는 그것이 그리스식 양식임을 알아보았으니, 그의 판단으로는 여러 면에서 로마식보다 나았다. 그것은 바퀴 사이가 더 넓고, 더 낮고 더 튼튼했으며, 무게가 더 나간다는 불리함은 자신의 아랍 말들이 지닌 더 큰 지구력으로 충분히 메워질 터였다."], ["Speaking generally, the carriage-makers of Rome built for the games almost solely, sacrificing safety to beauty, and durability to grace; while the chariots of Achilles and “the king of men,” designed for war and all its extreme tests, still ruled the tastes of those who met and struggled for the crowns Isthmian and Olympic.", "대체로 말해, 로마의 수레 장인들은 거의 오로지 경기를 위해서만 전차를 만들어, 아름다움을 위해 안전을 희생하고 우아함을 위해 내구성을 버렸다. 반면에 전쟁과 그 온갖 극한의 시험을 위해 고안된 아킬레우스와 \"인간들의 왕\"의 전차는, 이스트미아와 올림피아의 월계관을 두고 맞붙어 겨루는 자들의 취향을 여전히 지배하고 있었다."]], [["Next he brought the horses, and, hitching them to the chariot, drove to the field of exercise, where, hour after hour, he practised them in movement under the yoke.", "다음으로 그는 말들을 끌어내어 전차에 매고는, 운동장으로 몰고 가, 멍에를 메운 채 움직이는 연습을 몇 시간이고 시켰다."], ["When he came away in the evening, it was with restored spirit, and a fixed purpose to defer action in the matter of Messala until the race was won or lost.", "저녁에 그곳을 떠날 때, 그는 기운을 되찾았고, 경주에서 이기든 지든 그 결과가 날 때까지 메살라에 관한 일의 행동은 미루기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["He could not forego the pleasure of meeting his adversary under the eyes of the East; that there might be other competitors seemed not to enter his thought.", "그는 동방의 눈앞에서 적수와 맞서는 즐거움을 포기할 수 없었다. 다른 경쟁자들이 있을 수 있다는 생각은 그의 머릿속에 들어오지도 않는 듯했다."], ["His confidence in the result was absolute; no doubt of his own skill; and as to the four, they were his full partners in the glorious game.", "결과에 대한 그의 확신은 절대적이었으니, 자신의 기량에 대한 의심은 조금도 없었다. 그리고 네 마리 말로 말하자면, 그들은 그 영광스러운 경기에서 그의 온전한 동반자였다."]], [["“Let him look to it, let him look to it!", "\"그자더러 정신 차리라고 해, 정신 차리라고!"], ["Ha, Antares—Aldebaran!", "하, 안타레스, 알데바란!"], ["Shall he not, O honest Rigel?", "그렇지 않으냐, 오 정직한 리겔아?"], ["and thou, Atair, king among coursers, shall he not beware of us? Ha, ha!", "그리고 너, 준마들의 왕 아타이르야, 그자가 우리를 경계해야 하지 않겠느냐? 하, 하!"], ["good hearts!”", "착한 녀석들!\""]], [["So in rests he passed from horse to horse, speaking, not as a master, but the senior of as many brethren.", "그렇게 그는 짬짬이 말에서 말로 옮겨 다니며 말을 건넸으니, 주인으로서가 아니라 여러 형제 가운데 맏이로서였다."]], [["After nightfall, Ben-Hur sat by the door of the tent waiting for Ilderim, not yet returned from the city.", "날이 저문 뒤, 벤허는 아직 도시에서 돌아오지 않은 일데림을 기다리며 천막 문가에 앉아 있었다."], ["He was not impatient, or vexed, or doubtful.", "그는 조급하지도, 짜증스럽지도, 미심쩍어하지도 않았다."], ["The sheik would be heard from, at least.", "적어도 족장에게서는 소식이 올 터였다."], ["Indeed, whether it was from satisfaction with the performance of the four, or the refreshment there is in cold water succeeding bodily exercise, or supper partaken with royal appetite, or the reaction which, as a kindly provision of nature, always follows depression, the young man was in good-humor verging upon elation.", "사실, 네 마리 말의 성능에 대한 만족 때문이든, 몸을 쓴 뒤 찬물이 주는 상쾌함 때문이든, 임금 같은 식욕으로 든 저녁 식사 때문이든, 자연의 다정한 섭리로 침체 뒤에 늘 따라오는 반작용 때문이든, 그 젊은이는 의기양양함에 가까운 좋은 기분에 젖어 있었다."], ["He felt himself in the hands of Providence no longer his enemy.", "그는 자신이 더는 적이 아닌 섭리의 손안에 있다고 느꼈다."], ["At last there was a sound of horse’s feet coming rapidly, and Malluch rode up.", "마침내 말발굽이 빠르게 다가오는 소리가 났고, 멀룩이 말을 타고 다가왔다."]], [["“Son of Arrius,”", "\"아리우스의 아들이여,\""], ["he said, cheerily, after salutation, “I salute you for Sheik Ilderim, who requests you to mount and go to the city.", "그가 인사를 건넨 뒤 쾌활하게 말했다. \"일데림 족장을 대신해 인사드립니다. 족장께서는 그대가 말을 타고 도시로 와 주기를 청하십니다."], ["He is waiting for you.”", "그대를 기다리고 계십니다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur asked no questions, but went in where the horses were feeding.", "벤허는 아무것도 묻지 않고, 말들이 먹이를 먹고 있는 곳으로 들어갔다."], ["Aldebaran came to him, as if offering his service.", "알데바란이 그에게 다가왔으니, 마치 제 봉사를 자청하는 것 같았다."], ["He played with him lovingly, but passed on, and chose another, not of the four—they were sacred to the race.", "그는 그 말을 다정하게 어루만지다가 지나쳐, 네 마리 가운데 들지 않은 다른 말을 골랐다. 그 넷은 경주를 위해 신성하게 남겨 둔 것이었다."], ["Very shortly the two were on the road, going swiftly and in silence.", "곧 두 사람은 길에 올라, 빠르게, 그리고 말없이 나아갔다."]], [["Some distance below the Seleucian Bridge, they crossed the river by a ferry, and, riding far round on the right bank, and recrossing by another ferry, entered the city from the west.", "셀레우키아 다리에서 얼마쯤 아래에서 그들은 나룻배로 강을 건넌 다음, 오른편 강기슭을 멀리 돌아가, 또 다른 나룻배로 다시 건너서, 서쪽에서 도시로 들어섰다."], ["The detour was long, but Ben-Hur accepted it as a precaution for which there was good reason.", "우회로는 길었으나, 벤허는 그것을 충분한 까닭이 있는 예방책으로 받아들였다."]], [["Down to Simonides’ landing they rode, and in front of the great warehouse, under the bridge, Malluch drew rein.", "그들은 시모니데스의 부두까지 내려가, 다리 밑 큰 창고 앞에서 멀룩이 고삐를 당겼다."]], [["“We are come,”", "\"다 왔습니다,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Dismount.”", "\"내리시지요.\""]], [["Ben-Hur recognized the place.", "벤허는 그곳을 알아보았다."]], [["“Where is the sheik?”", "\"족장은 어디 계시오?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Come with me.", "\"저와 함께 가시지요."], ["I will show you.”", "제가 보여 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["A watchman took the horses, and almost before he realized it Ben-Hur stood once more at the door of the house up on the greater one, listening to the response from within—“In God’s name, enter.”", "야경꾼이 말들을 받아 갔고, 미처 깨닫기도 전에 벤허는 큰 건물 위에 얹힌 그 집의 문 앞에 다시 서서, 안에서 들려오는 대답에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. \"하느님의 이름으로, 어서 드십시오.\""]], [["Malluch stopped at the door; Ben-Hur entered alone.", "말루크는 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 벤허는 혼자 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["The room was the same in which he had formerly interviewed Simonides, and it had been in nowise changed, except now, close by the arm-chair, a polished brazen rod, set on a broad wooden pedestal, arose higher than a tall man, holding lamps of silver on sliding arms, half-a-dozen or more in number, and all burning.", "그 방은 전에 시모니데스를 만났던 바로 그 방이었고, 어디 하나 달라진 데가 없었다. 다만 안락의자 가까이에, 넓은 나무 받침대 위에 윤이 나는 놋쇠 막대가 키 큰 사람보다 높게 솟아, 미끄러지는 팔에 은 등잔을 여섯 개 남짓 매달고 있었으며, 모두 불을 밝히고 있었다."], ["The light was clear, bringing into view the panelling on the walls, the cornice with its row of gilded balls, and the dome dully tinted with violet mica.", "그 빛은 맑아서, 벽의 판자 장식과 도금한 구슬이 한 줄로 늘어선 처마 돌림띠와 보랏빛 운모로 은은하게 물든 둥근 천장까지 한눈에 드러나게 했다."]], [["Within a few steps, Ben-Hur stopped.", "몇 걸음 채 못 가서, 벤허는 멈춰 섰다."]], [["Three persons were present, looking at him—Simonides, Ilderim, and Esther.", "세 사람이 그곳에 있었으니, 모두 그를 바라보고 있었다. 시모니데스와 일데림과 에스더였다."]], [["He glanced hurriedly from one to another, as if to find answer to the question half formed in his mind, What business can these have with me?", "그는 마음속에 반쯤 떠오른 물음의 답을 찾기라도 하려는 듯, 한 사람씩 차례로 황급히 눈길을 옮겼다. 이들이 내게 무슨 볼일이 있단 말인가?"], ["He became calm, with every sense on the alert, for the question was succeeded by another, Are they friends or enemies?", "그는 곧 마음을 가라앉히고 온 감각을 곤두세웠으니, 그 물음 뒤에 또 다른 물음이 이어졌기 때문이다. 이들은 벗인가, 적인가?"]], [["At length, his eyes rested upon Esther.", "마침내 그의 눈길이 에스더에게 머물렀다."]], [["The men returned his look kindly; in her face there was something more than kindness—something too spirituel for definition, which yet went to his inner consciousness without definition.", "두 사내는 다정하게 그의 눈길을 받아 주었다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴에는 다정함 이상의 무엇이 있었으니, 정의 내리기에는 너무도 영적인 그것은 정의되지 않은 채로 그의 내면 깊은 의식에까지 닿았다."]], [["Shall it be said, good reader?", "말해도 좋을까, 선량한 독자여?"], ["Back of his gaze there was a comparison in which the Egyptian arose and set herself over against the gentle Jewess; but it lived an instant, and, as is the habit of such comparisons, passed away without a conclusion.", "그 눈길 뒤편에서는 한 비교가 일어나, 이집트 여인이 떠올라 그 온화한 유대 처녀와 맞세워졌다. 그러나 그것은 한순간 살아 있다가, 그런 비교가 으레 그러하듯, 어떤 결론도 없이 스러졌다."]], [["“Son of Hur—”", "\"후르의 아들이여—\""]], [["The guest turned to the speaker.", "손님은 말하는 이를 향해 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Son of Hur,”", "\"후르의 아들이여.\""], ["said Simonides, repeating the address slowly, and with distinct emphasis, as if to impress all its meaning upon him most interested in understanding it, “take thou the peace of the Lord God of our fathers—take it from me.”", "시모니데스는 그 부름을 천천히, 또렷한 강세를 실어 되풀이했으니, 마치 그 말의 온 뜻을, 그것을 알아듣는 데 가장 마음 쓰는 이의 가슴에 새겨 넣으려는 듯했다. \"우리 조상들의 주 하느님의 평화를 받으시오. 나로부터 그것을 받으시오.\""], ["He paused, then added, “From me and mine.”", "그는 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"나와 내 사람들로부터.\""]], [["The speaker sat in his chair; there were the royal head, the bloodless face, the masterful air, under the influence of which visitors forgot the broken limbs and distorted body of the man.", "말하는 이는 의자에 앉아 있었다. 그 위엄 있는 머리와 핏기 없는 얼굴과 당당한 기품 아래에서, 찾아온 이들은 이 사내의 부서진 사지와 뒤틀린 몸을 잊곤 했다."], ["The full black eyes gazed out under the white brows steadily, but not sternly.", "새까만 두 눈은 하얀 눈썹 아래에서 흔들림 없이, 그러나 매섭지 않게 바라보고 있었다."], ["A moment thus, then he crossed his hands upon his breast.", "그렇게 한순간이 흐른 뒤, 그는 두 손을 가슴 위에 포갰다."]], [["The action, taken with the salutation, could not be misunderstood, and was not.", "그 인사와 더불어 행해진 그 동작은 오해될 수 없는 것이었고, 또한 오해되지 않았다."]], [["“Simonides,”", "\"시모니데스,\""], ["Ben-Hur answered, much moved, “the holy peace you tender is accepted.", "벤허가 깊이 감동하여 대답했다. \"그대가 건네는 거룩한 평화를 받겠소."], ["As son to father, I return it to you. Only let there be perfect understanding between us.”", "아들이 아버지에게 하듯, 그것을 그대에게 되돌리겠소. 다만 우리 사이에 온전한 이해가 있게 합시다.\""]], [["Thus delicately he sought to put aside the submission of the merchant, and, in place of the relation of master and servant, substitute one higher and holier.", "그는 이토록 섬세하게, 상인의 복종을 물리치고, 주인과 종이라는 관계 대신 그보다 더 높고 더 거룩한 관계를 세우려 했다."]], [["Simonides let fall his hands, and, turning to Esther, said, “A seat for the master, daughter.”", "시모니데스는 두 손을 내려놓고, 에스더를 향해 말했다. \"주인님께 앉을 자리를, 딸아.\""]], [["She hastened, and brought a stool, and stood, with suffused face, looking from one to the other—from Ben-Hur to Simonides, from Simonides to Ben-Hur; and they waited, each declining the superiority direction would imply.", "그녀는 서둘러 등받이 없는 의자를 가져왔으나, 얼굴이 발갛게 물든 채 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라보며 서 있었다. 벤허에게서 시모니데스에게로, 시모니데스에게서 벤허에게로. 두 사람은 자리를 권하는 일이 내포할 우위를 서로 사양하며 기다렸다."], ["When at length the pause began to be embarrassing, Ben-Hur advanced, and gently took the stool from her, and, going to the chair, placed it at the merchant’s feet.", "마침내 그 침묵이 거북해지기 시작하자, 벤허가 앞으로 나서서 그녀에게서 부드럽게 의자를 받아 들고, 의자 쪽으로 가서 상인의 발치에 놓았다."]], [["“I will sit here,”", "\"나는 여기 앉겠소.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["His eyes met hers—an instant only; but both were better of the look.", "그의 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주쳤다. 단 한순간이었으나, 그 눈길로 두 사람 모두 마음이 한결 나아졌다."], ["He recognized her gratitude, she his generosity and forbearance.", "그는 그녀의 고마움을, 그녀는 그의 너그러움과 너른 아량을 알아보았다."]], [["Simonides bowed his acknowledgment.", "시모니데스가 고개를 숙여 고마움을 표했다."]], [["“Esther, child, bring me the paper,”", "\"에스더, 얘야, 그 문서를 가져오너라.\""], ["he said, with a breath of relief.", "그가 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 말했다."]], [["She went to a panel in the wall, opened it, took out a roll of papyri, and brought and gave it to him.", "그녀는 벽의 판자 한 곳으로 가서 그것을 열고, 파피루스 두루마리 하나를 꺼내, 그에게 가져다주었다."]], [["“Thou saidst well, son of Hur,”", "\"그대 말이 옳소, 후르의 아들이여.\""], ["Simonides began, while unrolling the sheets.", "시모니데스는 종이를 펼치며 입을 열었다."], ["“Let us understand each other.", "\"우리 서로를 이해하도록 합시다."], ["In anticipation of the demand—which I would have made hadst thou waived it—I have here a statement covering everything necessary to the understanding required.", "그대가 요구하지 않더라도 내가 먼저 내놓았을 그 요구를 미리 헤아려, 여기 우리에게 필요한 이해에 빠짐없이 닿는 명세를 마련해 두었소."], ["I could see but two points involved—the property first, and then our relation.", "내가 보기에 얽힌 문제는 두 가지뿐이었으니, 첫째는 재산이요, 다음은 우리의 관계요."], ["The statement is explicit as to both.", "이 명세는 그 둘 모두에 대해 분명하오."], ["Will it please thee to read it now?”", "지금 그것을 읽어 보시겠소?\""]], [["Ben-Hur received the papers, but glanced at Ilderim.", "벤허는 종이를 받아 들었으나, 일데림을 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Simonides, “the sheik shall not deter thee from reading.", "시모니데스가 말했다. \"족장이 읽기를 막지는 않을 것이오."], ["The account—such thou wilt find it—is of a nature requiring a witness.", "이 계산서는—그대가 보면 알겠지만—증인이 필요한 성격의 것이오."], ["In the attesting place at the end thou wilt find, when thou comest to it, the name—Ilderim, Sheik. He knows all.", "그대가 그곳에 이르면, 끝의 증명란에서 '일데림, 족장'이라는 이름을 보게 될 것이오. 그는 모든 것을 알고 있소."], ["He is thy friend.", "그는 그대의 벗이오."], ["All he has been to me, that will he be to thee also.”", "그가 내게 그러했던 모든 것을, 그대에게도 그리할 것이오.\""]], [["Simonides looked at the Arab, nodding pleasantly, and the latter gravely returned the nod, saying, “Thou hast said.”", "시모니데스는 아랍인을 바라보며 다정하게 고개를 끄덕였고, 상대는 진중하게 그 끄덕임을 되돌리며 말했다. \"그대 말 그대로요.\""]], [["Ben-Hur replied, “I know already the excellence of his friendship, and have yet to prove myself worthy of it.”", "벤허가 대답했다. \"나는 이미 그의 우정이 얼마나 훌륭한지 알고 있으며, 아직 그럴 만한 사람임을 스스로 증명해야 할 처지요.\""], ["Immediately he continued, “Later, O Simonides, I will read the papers carefully; for the present, do thou take them, and if thou be not too weary, give me their substance.”", "그는 곧바로 말을 이었다. \"시모니데스, 나중에 이 문서를 꼼꼼히 읽겠소. 지금은 그대가 그것을 받아 두었다가, 너무 지치지 않았다면 그 요지를 들려주시오.\""]], [["Simonides took back the roll.", "시모니데스는 두루마리를 도로 받았다."]], [["“Here, Esther, stand by me and receive the sheets, lest they fall into confusion.”", "\"자, 에스더, 내 곁에 서서 이 종이들을 받아 다오. 흐트러지지 않도록.\""]], [["She took place by his chair, letting her right arm fall lightly across his shoulder, so, when he spoke, the account seemed to have rendition from both of them jointly.", "그녀는 그의 의자 곁에 자리를 잡고, 오른팔을 그의 어깨 위에 가볍게 올렸다. 그리하여 그가 말할 때, 그 계산서는 두 사람이 함께 풀어내는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“This,”", "\"이것은,\""], ["said Simonides, drawing out the first leaf, “shows the money I had of thy father’s, being the amount saved from the Romans; there was no property saved, only money, and that the robbers would have secured but for our Jewish custom of bills of exchange.", "시모니데스가 첫 장을 빼내며 말했다. \"내가 그대 부친에게서 받은 돈을 보여 주는 것으로, 로마인들로부터 건져 낸 액수요. 건진 재산은 없었고 오직 돈뿐이었는데, 우리 유대인의 환어음 관습이 아니었다면 그 돈마저 강도들이 차지했을 것이오."], ["The amount saved, being sums I drew from Rome, Alexandria, Damascus, Carthage, Valentia, and elsewhere within the circle of trade, was one hundred and twenty talents Jewish money.”", "건져 낸 액수는, 내가 로마와 알렉산드리아와 다마스쿠스와 카르타고와 발렌티아와 그 밖에 교역망 안의 여러 곳에서 거두어들인 금액으로, 유대 화폐로 백이십 달란트였소.\""]], [["He gave the sheet to Esther, and took the next one.", "그는 그 장을 에스더에게 건네고, 다음 장을 집어 들었다."]], [["“With that amount—one hundred and twenty talents—I charged myself. Hear now my credits.", "\"그 액수, 곧 백이십 달란트를 내 책임 아래 두었소. 이제 내가 거둔 수익을 들으시오."], ["I use the word, as thou wilt see, with reference rather to the proceeds gained from the use of the money.”", "그대도 알게 되겠지만, 나는 이 말을 그 돈을 굴려 얻은 이익을 가리키는 뜻으로 쓰는 것이오.\""]], [["From separate sheets he then read footings, which, fractions omitted, were as follows:", "그러고는 따로따로 된 장들에서 합계를 읽어 내려갔으니, 끝수를 버리면 다음과 같았다."]], [["By ships 60 talents ” goods in store 110 ” ” cargoes in transit 75 ” ” camels, horses, etc. 20 ” ” warehouses 10 ” ” bills due\t54 ” ” money on hand and subject to draft\t224 ” —— Total\t553 ”", "선박으로 60달란트, 창고에 쌓인 물품으로 110달란트, 운송 중인 화물로 75달란트, 낙타와 말 따위로 20달란트, 창고 건물로 10달란트, 받을 어음으로 54달란트, 수중에 있고 언제든 인출할 수 있는 돈으로 224달란트—총계 553달란트."]], [["“To these now, to the five hundred and fifty-three talents gained, add the original capital I had from thy father, and thou hast SIX HUNDRED AND SEVENTY THREE TALENTS!—and all thine—making thee, O son of Hur, the richest subject in the world.”", "\"이제 이것에, 거두어들인 오백쉰세 달란트에, 그대 부친에게서 받은 처음의 자본을 더하면, 그대에게는 육백일흔세 달란트가 있게 되오! 그리고 모두 그대의 것이니—후르의 아들이여, 그대는 이로써 세상에서 가장 부유한 신민이 되는 것이오.\""]], [["He took the papyri from Esther, and, reserving one, rolled them and offered them to Ben-Hur.", "그는 에스더에게서 파피루스를 받아 들고, 한 장을 따로 남겨 둔 채 나머지를 둘둘 말아 벤허에게 내밀었다."], ["The pride perceptible in his manner was not offensive; it might have been from a sense of duty well done; it might have been for Ben-Hur without reference to himself.", "그의 태도에 어린 자랑스러움은 거슬리지 않았으니, 그것은 임무를 잘 완수했다는 마음에서 비롯된 것일 수도 있었고, 자기 자신과는 상관없이 오로지 벤허를 위한 것일 수도 있었다."]], [["“And there is nothing,”", "\"그리고 이제는,\""], ["he added, dropping his voice, but not his eyes—“there is nothing now thou mayst not do.”", "그는 목소리는 낮추되 눈길은 거두지 않으며 덧붙였다. \"이제는 그대가 못할 일이 아무것도 없소.\""]], [["The moment was one of absorbing interest to all present.", "그 순간은 그곳에 있는 모든 이에게 마음을 사로잡는 순간이었다."], ["Simonides crossed his hands upon his breast again; Esther was anxious; Ilderim nervous.", "시모니데스는 다시 두 손을 가슴 위에 포갰고, 에스더는 마음을 졸였으며, 일데림은 안절부절못했다."], ["A man is never so on trial as in the moment of excessive good-fortune.", "사람은 지나친 행운의 순간만큼 호되게 시험받는 때가 없다."]], [["Taking the roll, Ben-Hur arose, struggling with emotion.", "벤허는 두루마리를 받아 들고 일어섰으니, 북받치는 감정과 싸우고 있었다."]], [["“All this is to me as a light from heaven, sent to drive away a night which has been so long I feared it would never end, and so dark I had lost the hope of seeing,”", "\"이 모든 것은 내게 하늘에서 보내 준 빛과 같소. 너무도 길어서 결코 끝나지 않으리라 두려워했던 밤, 너무도 어두워서 다시 보리라는 희망마저 잃었던 밤을 몰아내려고 보내 주신 빛이오.\""], ["he said, with a husky voice.", "그가 잠긴 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I give first thanks to the Lord, who has not abandoned me, and my next to thee, O Simonides.", "\"먼저 나를 버리지 않으신 주님께 감사드리고, 그다음으로 그대에게 감사드리오, 시모니데스."], ["Thy faithfulness outweighs the cruelty of others, and redeems our human nature.", "그대의 충심은 다른 이들의 잔인함을 무겁게 누르고, 우리 인간 본성을 구원하오."], ["‘There is nothing I cannot do:’ be it so.", "'내가 못할 일이 아무것도 없다.' 그럼 그렇게 하리다."], ["Shall any man in this my hour of such mighty privilege be more generous than I?", "이토록 큰 특권이 주어진 이 시각에, 나보다 더 너그러운 자가 어디 있겠소?"], ["Serve me as a witness now, Sheik Ilderim.", "이제 내 증인이 되어 주시오, 일데림 족장."], ["Hear thou my words as I shall speak them—hear and remember.", "내가 말하는 대로 들으시오—듣고 기억해 주시오."], ["And thou, Esther, good angel of this good man!", "그리고 그대, 에스더, 이 선량한 분의 선한 천사여!"], ["hear thou also.”", "그대도 들으시오.\""]], [["He stretched his hand with the roll to Simonides.", "그는 두루마리를 든 손을 시모니데스에게 내밀었다."]], [["“The things these papers take into account—all of them: ships, houses, goods, camels, horses, money; the least as well as the greatest—give I back to thee, O Simonides, making them all thine, and sealing them to thee and thine forever.”", "\"이 문서가 셈해 놓은 것들을—그 모두를, 선박이며 가옥이며 물품이며 낙타며 말이며 돈이며, 가장 작은 것에서 가장 큰 것까지—그대에게 도로 돌려주겠소, 시모니데스. 그 모두를 그대의 것으로 삼아, 그대와 그대의 사람들에게 영원히 봉인하겠소.\""]], [["Esther smiled through her tears; Ilderim pulled his beard with rapid motion, his eyes glistening like beads of jet.", "에스더는 눈물 사이로 미소 지었고, 일데림은 빠른 손짓으로 수염을 잡아당겼으니, 그 두 눈은 흑옥 구슬처럼 반짝였다."], ["Simonides alone was calm.", "오직 시모니데스만이 평온했다."]], [["“Sealing them to thee and thine forever,”", "\"그대와 그대의 사람들에게 영원히 봉인하겠소.\""], ["Ben-Hur continued, with better control of himself, “with one exception, and upon one condition.”", "벤허는 한결 마음을 추스르고 말을 이었다. \"단 하나의 예외와, 단 하나의 조건을 두고서.\""]], [["The breath of the listeners waited upon his words.", "듣는 이들의 숨결이 그의 말에 매달렸다."]], [["“The hundred and twenty talents which were my father’s thou shalt return to me.”", "\"내 부친의 것이었던 백이십 달란트는 그대가 내게 돌려주시오.\""]], [["Ilderim’s countenance brightened.", "일데림의 얼굴이 환해졌다."]], [["“And thou shalt join me in search of my mother and sister, holding all thine subject to the expense of discovery, even as I will hold mine.”", "\"그리고 그대는 나와 함께 내 어머니와 누이를 찾는 일에 나서야 하오. 내가 내 몫을 그 수색 비용에 내놓듯, 그대도 그대의 모든 것을 그 비용에 내놓을 셈치고서 말이오.\""]], [["Simonides was much affected.", "시모니데스는 크게 감동했다."], ["Stretching out his hand, he said, “I see thy spirit, son of Hur, and I am grateful to the Lord that he hath sent thee to me such as thou art.", "그는 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"그대의 마음을 알겠소, 후르의 아들이여. 주님께서 그대를 지금 이 모습 그대로 내게 보내 주신 것을 감사하오."], ["If I served well thy father in life, and his memory afterwards, be not afraid of default to thee; yet must I say the exception cannot stand.”", "내가 살아 계실 적의 그대 부친을 잘 섬겼고 그 뒤로도 그 기억을 잘 받들었다면, 그대에게 소홀할까는 염려치 마시오. 허나 한 가지 말해 둘 것은, 그 예외는 받아들일 수 없다는 것이오.\""]], [["Exhibiting, then, the reserved sheet, he continued,", "그러고는 따로 남겨 두었던 장을 내보이며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Thou hast not all the account.", "\"그대는 아직 계산서를 다 보지 못했소."], ["Take this and read—read aloud.”", "이것을 받아 읽으시오—소리 내어 읽으시오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur took the supplement, and read it.", "벤허는 그 부속 문서를 받아 읽었다."]], [["“Statement of the servants of Hur, rendered by Simonides, steward of the estate.", "\"후르가의 종복 명세, 후르가 재산의 청지기 시모니데스가 작성함."]], [["1. Amrah, Egyptian, keeping the palace in Jerusalem. 2.", "1. 암라, 이집트인, 예루살렘의 저택을 지킴. 2."], ["Simonides, the steward, in Antioch. 3.", "시모니데스, 청지기, 안티오크에 있음. 3."], ["Esther, daughter of Simonides.”", "에스더, 시모니데스의 딸.\""]], [["Now, in all his thoughts of Simonides, not once had it entered Ben-Hur’s mind that, by the law, a daughter followed the parent’s condition.", "그런데 시모니데스를 두고 생각할 때, 벤허의 머릿속에는 율법에 따라 딸이 부모의 신분을 잇는다는 사실이 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["In all his visions of her, the sweet-faced Esther had figured as the rival of the Egyptian, and an object of possible love.", "그녀를 마음속에 그릴 때면, 그 다정한 얼굴의 에스더는 늘 이집트 여인의 맞수로, 그리고 사랑할 수도 있을 대상으로 떠올랐다."], ["He shrank from the revelation so suddenly brought him, and looked at her blushing; and, blushing, she dropped her eyes before him.", "그는 그토록 느닷없이 닥쳐온 사실 앞에서 움츠러들며, 얼굴을 붉히는 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉힌 채 그의 앞에서 눈을 떨구었다."], ["Then he said, while the papyrus rolled itself together,", "그러고서 그는, 파피루스가 저절로 둘둘 말리는 동안 말했다."]], [["“A man with six hundred talents is indeed rich, and may do what he pleases; but, rarer than the money, more priceless than the property, is the mind which amassed the wealth, and the heart it could not corrupt when amassed.", "\"육백 달란트를 지닌 사람이라면 참으로 부유하고, 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 할 수 있을 것이오. 그러나 그 돈보다 더 드물고 그 재산보다 더 값을 매길 수 없는 것은, 그 부를 쌓아 올린 정신이며, 쌓아 올린 뒤에도 그 부가 더럽힐 수 없었던 마음이오."], ["O Simonides—and thou, fair Esther—fear not.", "시모니데스—그리고 그대, 어여쁜 에스더—두려워 마시오."], ["Sheik Ilderim here shall be witness that in the same moment ye were declared my servants, that moment I declared ye free; and what I declare, that will I put in writing.", "여기 일데림 족장이 증인이 되어 줄 것이니, 그대들이 내 종복이라 선언된 바로 그 순간, 그 순간에 나는 그대들이 자유롭다고 선언하는 바요. 그리고 내가 선언하는 것을 문서로도 남기겠소. 이것으로 충분치 않소?"], ["Is it not enough? Can I do more?”", "내가 더 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Son of Hur,”", "\"후르의 아들이여,\""], ["said Simonides, “verily thou dost make servitude lightsome.", "시모니데스가 말했다. \"참으로 그대는 종살이를 가볍게 만들어 주는구려."], ["I was wrong; there are some things thou canst not do; thou canst not make us free in law.", "내가 틀렸소. 그대가 못할 일이 더러 있으니, 그대는 율법상으로 우리를 자유롭게 만들 수는 없소."], ["I am thy servant forever, because I went to the door with thy father one day, and in my ear the awl-marks yet abide.”", "나는 영원히 그대의 종이오. 어느 날 그대 부친과 함께 문으로 갔던 까닭이며, 내 귀에는 아직도 송곳 자국이 남아 있기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Did my father that?”", "\"내 부친이 그리하셨단 말이오?\""]], [["“Judge him not,”", "\"그분을 탓하지 마시오.\""], ["cried Simonides, quickly.", "시모니데스가 황급히 외쳤다."], ["“He accepted me a servant of that class because I prayed him to do so. I never repented the step.", "\"내가 간청하였기에 그분께서 나를 그런 부류의 종으로 받아 주신 것이오. 나는 그 일을 한 번도 후회한 적이 없소."], ["It was the price I paid for Rachel, the mother of my child here; for Rachel, who would not be my wife unless I became what she was.”", "그것은 라헬을, 여기 내 자식의 어미를 얻기 위해 내가 치른 값이었소. 자기와 같은 처지가 되지 않으면 내 아내가 되려 하지 않았던 그 라헬을 위해서 말이오.\""]], [["“Was she a servant forever?”", "\"그분도 영원한 종이었소?\""]], [["“Even so.”", "\"그러하였소.\""]], [["Ben-Hur walked the floor in pain of impotent wish.", "벤허는 이루지 못할 소망의 아픔 속에서 방 안을 거닐었다."]], [["“I was rich before,”", "\"나는 전부터 부유했소.\""], ["he said, stopping suddenly.", "그가 문득 걸음을 멈추고 말했다."], ["“I was rich with the gifts of the generous Arrius; now comes this greater fortune, and the mind which achieved it.", "\"너그러운 아리우스가 주신 선물로 부유했고, 이제는 이 더 큰 재산과, 그것을 이루어 낸 정신까지 더해졌소."], ["Is there not a purpose of God in it all?", "이 모든 일에 하느님의 뜻이 있는 것이 아니겠소?"], ["Counsel me, O Simonides!", "내게 조언해 주시오, 시모니데스!"], ["Help me to see the right and do it.", "옳은 것을 보고 행하도록 도와주시오."], ["Help me to be worthy my name, and what thou art in law to me, that will I be to thee in fact and deed.", "내 이름에 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 되도록 도와주시오. 그리고 그대가 율법상으로 내게 어떤 존재이든, 나도 그대에게 사실과 행실로 그러한 존재가 되겠소."], ["I will be thy servant forever.”", "나는 영원히 그대의 종이 되겠소.\""]], [["Simonides’ face actually glowed.", "시모니데스의 얼굴이 정말로 환히 빛났다."]], [["“O son of my dead master!", "\"오, 돌아가신 내 주인의 아드님이여!"], ["I will do better than help; I will serve thee with all my might of mind and heart.", "나는 돕는 것보다 더한 일을 하겠소. 내 온 마음과 정신을 다해 그대를 섬기겠소."], ["Body, I have not; it perished in thy cause; but with mind and heart I will serve thee.", "몸은 내게 없으니, 그것은 그대 부친의 일로 망가졌소. 허나 마음과 정신으로 그대를 섬기겠소."], ["I swear it, by the altar of our God, and the gifts upon the altar!", "우리 하느님의 제단과 그 위의 제물을 두고 맹세하오!"], ["Only make me formally what I have assumed to be.”", "다만 내가 자처해 온 것을 정식으로 내게 맡겨만 주시오.\""]], [["“Name it,” said Ben-Hur, eagerly.", "\"그것을 말해 보시오.\" 벤허가 간절히 말했다."]], [["“As steward the care of the property will be mine.”", "\"청지기로서 재산을 돌보는 일이 내 몫이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“Count thyself steward now; or wilt thou have it in writing?”", "\"지금부터 그대를 청지기로 여기겠소. 아니면 문서로 받기를 원하오?\""]], [["“Thy word simply is enough; it was so with the father, and I will not more from the son.", "\"그대의 말 한마디면 충분하오. 부친께도 그러하였으니, 아드님께 더 바라지는 않겠소."], ["And now, if the understanding be perfect”—Simonides paused.", "자, 이제 이해가 온전하다면—\" 시모니데스는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“It is with me,”", "\"내게는 온전하오.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["“And thou, daughter of Rachel, speak!”", "\"그리고 너, 라헬의 딸아, 말해 보아라!\""], ["said Simonides, lifting her arm from his shoulder.", "시모니데스가 자기 어깨에서 그녀의 팔을 들어 내리며 말했다."]], [["Esther, left thus alone, stood a moment abashed, her color coming and going; then she went to Ben-Hur, and said, with a womanliness singularly sweet, “I am not better than my mother was; and, as she is gone, I pray you, O my master, let me care for my father.”", "그리하여 홀로 남겨진 에스더는 한순간 머뭇거리며 서 있었으니, 얼굴빛이 붉어졌다 가셨다 했다. 이윽고 그녀는 벤허에게 다가가, 유달리 고운 여인다움으로 말했다. \"저는 제 어머니보다 나은 사람이 아닙니다. 어머니가 가셨으니, 청하옵건대, 주인님, 제가 제 아버지를 돌보게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur took her hand, and led her back to the chair, saying, “Thou art a good child.", "벤허는 그녀의 손을 잡고 의자로 도로 이끌며 말했다. \"그대는 착한 아이로구려."], ["Have thy will.”", "그대 뜻대로 하시오.\""]], [["Simonides replaced her arm upon his neck, and there was silence for a time in the room.", "시모니데스는 그녀의 팔을 다시 제 목에 올려놓았고, 방 안에는 한동안 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["Simonides looked up, none the less a master.", "시모니데스가 눈을 들었으니, 그래도 여전히 주인의 풍모였다."]], [["“Esther,”", "\"에스더야.\""], ["he said, quietly, “the night is going fast; and, lest we become too weary for that which is before us, let the refreshments be brought.”", "그가 조용히 말했다. \"밤이 빠르게 깊어 가는구나. 앞에 놓인 일을 감당하기에 너무 지쳐 버리지 않도록, 요기할 것을 가져오너라.\""]], [["She rang a bell. A servant answered with wine and bread, which she bore round.", "그녀가 종을 울렸다. 하인이 포도주와 빵을 내왔고, 그녀가 그것을 돌렸다."]], [["“The understanding, good my master,”", "\"좋은 나의 주인이시여.\""], ["continued Simonides, when all were served, “is not perfect in my sight.", "모두에게 음식이 돌아가자 시모니데스가 말을 이었다. \"제가 보기에 우리 사이의 이해는 아직 온전치 못합니다."], ["Henceforth our lives will run on together like rivers which have met and joined their waters.", "이제부터 우리의 삶은 만나서 서로의 물줄기를 합친 강물처럼 함께 흘러갈 것입니다."], ["I think their flowing will be better if every cloud is blown from the sky above them.", "그 흐름 위의 하늘에서 구름 한 점까지 모두 걷힌다면, 그 흐름은 더욱 나아질 것이라 생각합니다."], ["You left my door the other day with what seemed a denial of the claims which I have just allowed in the broadest terms; but it was not so, indeed it was not.", "도련님은 며칠 전 제 문을 떠나실 때, 제가 방금 가장 폭넓은 말로 인정한 그 권리를 거부당하기라도 한 것처럼 보였습니다. 그러나 결코 그런 것이 아니었습니다, 정말이지 그렇지 않았습니다."], ["Esther is witness that I recognized you; and that I did not abandon you, let Malluch say.”", "제가 도련님을 알아보았다는 것은 에스더가 증인이요, 제가 도련님을 저버리지 않았다는 것은 말루크가 말해 줄 것입니다.\""]], [["“Malluch!” exclaimed Ben-Hur.", "\"말루크라니!\" 벤허가 소리쳤다."]], [["“One bound to a chair, like me, must have many hands far-reaching, if he would move the world from which he is so cruelly barred.", "\"저처럼 의자에 묶인 몸이라면, 그토록 모질게 가로막힌 세상을 움직이려 할 때 멀리까지 뻗는 손이 여럿 있어야 합니다."], ["I have many such, and Malluch is one of the best of them.", "저에게는 그런 손이 많고, 말루크는 그 가운데 가장 뛰어난 자의 하나입니다."], ["And, sometimes”—he cast a grateful glance at the sheik—“sometimes I borrow from others good of heart, like Ilderim the Generous—good and brave.", "그리고 때로는\"—그는 족장에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다—\"때로는 너그러운 일데림처럼 마음이 어진 다른 분들에게서 도움을 빌리기도 합니다. 어질고도 용감한 분이지요."], ["Let him say if I either denied or forgot you.”", "제가 도련님을 거부했는지 잊었는지, 저분에게 물어보십시오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur looked at the Arab.", "벤허가 아랍인을 바라보았다."]], [["“This is he, good Ilderim, this is he who told you of me?”", "\"이분이로군요, 좋은 일데림이시여. 이분이 저에 관해 당신께 말씀드린 분이로군요?\""]], [["Ilderim’s eyes twinkled as he nodded his answer.", "일데림의 두 눈이 반짝였고, 그는 고개를 끄덕여 답했다."]], [["“How, O my master,”", "\"오, 나의 주인이시여.\""], ["said Simonides, “may we without trial tell what a man is?", "시모니데스가 말했다. \"시험해 보지 않고서 어찌 한 사람이 어떤 인물인지 알 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I knew you; I saw your father in you; but the kind of man you were I did not know.", "저는 도련님을 알았고, 도련님 안에서 부친을 보았습니다. 그러나 도련님이 어떤 부류의 사람인지는 알지 못했습니다."], ["There are people to whom fortune is a curse in disguise. Were you of them?", "어떤 이들에게 행운은 변장한 저주입니다. 도련님이 그런 부류였습니까?"], ["I sent Malluch to find out for me, and in the service he was my eyes and ears.", "저는 그것을 알아내고자 말루크를 보냈고, 그 일을 하는 동안 그는 저의 눈이요 귀였습니다."], ["Do not blame him.", "그를 탓하지 마십시오."], ["He brought me report of you which was all good.”", "그가 가져온 도련님에 관한 보고는 한결같이 좋은 것이었습니다.\""]], [["“I do not,” said Ben-Hur, heartily.", "\"탓하지 않습니다.\" 벤허가 진심으로 말했다."], ["“There was wisdom in your goodness.”", "\"당신의 선의에는 지혜가 깃들어 있었습니다.\""]], [["“The words are very pleasant to me,”", "\"그 말씀이 제게는 참으로 기쁩니다.\""], ["said the merchant, with feeling, “very pleasant.", "상인이 감격하여 말했다. \"참으로 기쁩니다."], ["My fear of misunderstanding is laid.", "오해에 대한 두려움이 가셨습니다."], ["Let the rivers run on now as God may give them direction.”", "이제 하느님께서 인도하시는 대로 강물이 흘러가게 하십시오.\""]], [["After an interval he continued:", "잠시 사이를 둔 뒤 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I am compelled now by truth.", "\"이제 저는 진실에 떠밀려 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["The weaver sits weaving, and, as the shuttle flies, the cloth increases, and the figures grow, and he dreams dreams meanwhile; so to my hands the fortune grew, and I wondered at the increase, and asked myself about it many times.", "직조공이 앉아서 베를 짜면, 북이 날아갈수록 천이 늘어나고 무늬가 자라나며, 그 사이 그는 꿈을 꿉니다. 그처럼 제 손에서 재산이 불어났고, 저는 그 불어남에 놀라 여러 번 스스로에게 까닭을 물었습니다."], ["I could see a care not my own went with the enterprises I set going.", "제가 벌인 사업마다 제 것이 아닌 어떤 보살핌이 함께함을 볼 수 있었습니다."], ["The simooms which smote others on the desert jumped over the things which were mine.", "사막에서 남들을 후려친 모래폭풍은 제 것을 뛰어넘어 갔습니다."], ["The storms which heaped the seashore with wrecks did but blow my ships the sooner into port.", "해안을 난파선으로 뒤덮은 폭풍우는 오히려 제 배들을 더 빨리 항구로 몰아넣을 뿐이었습니다."], ["Strangest of all, I, so dependent upon others, fixed to a place like a dead thing, had never a loss by an agent—never.", "무엇보다 기이한 것은, 그토록 남들에게 의지하며 죽은 물건처럼 한자리에 붙박인 제가, 대리인 때문에 손해를 본 적이 단 한 번도 없었다는 것입니다, 단 한 번도."], ["The elements stooped to serve me, and all my servants, in fact, were faithful.”", "천지만물이 몸을 굽혀 저를 섬겼고, 사실 제 모든 종이 충실했습니다.\""]], [["“It is very strange,”", "\"참으로 기이한 일이군요.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["“So I said, and kept saying.", "\"저도 그렇게 말했고, 거듭 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["Finally, O my master, finally I came to be of your opinion—God was in it—and, like you, I asked, What can his purpose be?", "마침내, 오, 나의 주인이시여, 마침내 저는 도련님과 같은 생각에 이르렀습니다. 하느님께서 그 안에 계셨던 것입니다."], ["Intelligence is never wasted; intelligence like God’s never stirs except with design.", "그리고 도련님처럼 저도 물었습니다, 그분의 뜻이 무엇일 수 있는가? 지혜는 결코 헛되이 쓰이지 않습니다."], ["I have held the question in heart, lo!", "하느님의 지혜 같은 것은 뜻 없이 움직이는 법이 없습니다."], ["these many years, watching for an answer. I felt sure, if God were in it, some day, in his own good time, in his own way, he would show me his purpose, making it clear as a whited house upon a hill.", "보십시오, 저는 이 여러 해 동안 그 물음을 가슴에 품고 답을 기다려 왔습니다. 만약 하느님께서 그 안에 계신다면, 언젠가 그분의 좋은 때에, 그분의 방식으로, 언덕 위에 회칠한 집처럼 환하게 드러내며 그 뜻을 제게 보여 주시리라 확신했습니다."], ["And I believe he has done so.”", "그리고 저는 그분께서 그리하셨다고 믿습니다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur listened with every faculty intent.", "벤허는 온 정신을 모아 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Many years ago, with my people—thy mother was with me, Esther, beautiful as morning over old Olivet—I sat by the wayside out north of Jerusalem, near the Tombs of the Kings, when three men passed by riding great white camels, such as had never been seen in the Holy City.", "\"여러 해 전, 제 식솔과 함께—네 어머니도 나와 함께였단다, 에스더야, 옛 올리벳 위로 떠오르는 아침처럼 아름다웠지—저는 예루살렘 북쪽 바깥, 왕들의 무덤 가까운 길가에 앉아 있었습니다. 그때 세 사람이 거대한 흰 낙타를 타고 지나갔는데, 거룩한 도성에서 한 번도 본 적 없는 낙타였습니다. 그들은 낯선 이들로, 먼 나라에서 온 사람들이었습니다."], ["The men were strangers, and from far countries.", "첫 번째 사람이 멈추어 서서 제게 물었습니다. '유대인의 왕으로 나신 분이 어디 계십니까?'"], ["The first one stopped and asked me a question.", "그러고는 제 놀라움을 달래기라도 하려는 듯 이렇게 덧붙였습니다."], ["‘Where is he that is born King of the Jews?’ As if to allay my wonder, he went on to say, ‘We have seen his star in the east, and have come to worship him.’ I could not understand, but followed them to the Damascus Gate; and of every person they met on the way—of the guard at the Gate, even—they asked the question.", "'우리는 동방에서 그분의 별을 보고, 그분께 경배하러 왔습니다.' 저는 영문을 알 수 없었으나 다마스쿠스 문까지 그들을 따라갔습니다. 그들은 길에서 마주치는 사람마다—심지어 문을 지키는 보초에게까지—그 물음을 던졌습니다."], ["All who heard it were amazed like me.", "그것을 들은 모든 이가 저처럼 놀랐습니다."], ["In time I forgot the circumstance, though there was much talk of it as a presage of the Messiah.", "그 일은 메시아의 전조라며 말이 많았으나, 세월이 흐르며 저는 그 일을 잊었습니다."], ["Alas, alas!", "아아, 아아!"], ["What children we are, even the wisest!", "가장 지혜로운 자라 한들 우리는 얼마나 어린아이 같은지요!"], ["When God walks the earth, his steps are often centuries apart.", "하느님께서 이 땅을 걸으실 때, 그분의 발걸음은 흔히 수백 년씩 떨어져 있습니다."], ["You have seen Balthasar?”", "도련님은 발타사르를 만나셨습니까?\""]], [["“And heard him tell his story,”", "\"그분이 자기 이야기를 들려주는 것을 들었습니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["“A miracle!—a very miracle!”", "\"기적입니다! 그야말로 기적입니다!\""], ["cried Simonides.", "시모니데스가 외쳤다."], ["“As he told it to me, good my master, I seemed to hear the answer I had so long waited; God’s purpose burst upon me.", "\"좋은 나의 주인이시여, 그분이 그 이야기를 들려주실 때, 저는 그토록 오래 기다려 온 그 답을 듣는 것만 같았습니다. 하느님의 뜻이 제게 활짝 열렸습니다."], ["Poor will the King be when he comes—poor and friendless; without following, without armies, without cities or castles; a kingdom to be set up, and Rome reduced and blotted out. See, see, O my master!", "그 왕이 오실 때 그분은 가난할 것입니다—가난하고 벗도 없이, 따르는 무리도 없고, 군대도 없고, 도성도 성채도 없이 말입니다. 세워야 할 왕국이 있고, 로마는 무너뜨려 자취를 지워야 합니다."], ["thou flushed with strength, thou trained to arms, thou burdened with riches; behold the opportunity the Lord hath sent thee!", "보십시오, 보십시오, 오, 나의 주인이시여! 도련님은 힘이 넘치고, 무예로 단련되었으며, 재물을 가득 짊어지셨습니다. 주께서 도련님께 보내신 이 기회를 보십시오!"], ["Shall not his purpose be thine?", "그분의 뜻이 곧 도련님의 뜻이 되어서는 안 되겠습니까?"], ["Could a man be born to a more perfect glory?”", "사람이 이보다 더 완전한 영광을 위해 태어날 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["Simonides put his whole force in the appeal.", "시모니데스는 그 호소에 온 힘을 쏟아부었다."]], [["“But the kingdom, the kingdom!”", "\"하지만 그 왕국, 그 왕국 말입니다!\""], ["Ben-Hur answered, eagerly. “Balthasar says it is to be of souls.”", "벤허가 간절하게 답했다. \"발타사르는 그것이 영혼의 왕국이 될 것이라 말합니다.\""]], [["The pride of the Jew was strong in Simonides, and therefore the slightly contemptuous curl of the lip with which he began his reply:", "시모니데스 안에는 유대인의 자긍심이 강하게 살아 있었으니, 그가 답을 시작하며 입술이 살짝 비웃음으로 일그러진 것도 그 때문이었다."]], [["“Balthasar has been a witness of wonderful things—of miracles, O my master; and when he speaks of them, I bow with belief, for they are of sight and sound personal to him.", "\"발타사르는 놀라운 일들의 증인이었습니다—기적의 증인 말입니다, 오, 나의 주인이시여. 그가 그 일들을 말할 때 저는 믿음으로 머리를 숙입니다. 그것은 그가 몸소 보고 들은 것이기 때문입니다."], ["But he is a son of Mizraim, and not even a proselyte.", "그러나 그는 미스라임의 자손이요, 개종자조차 아닙니다."], ["Hardly may he be supposed to have special knowledge by virtue of which we must bow to him in a matter of God’s dealing with our Israel.", "하느님께서 우리 이스라엘을 다루시는 일에 관하여 우리가 그에게 머리를 숙여야 할 만큼, 그가 특별한 지식을 지녔다고 보기는 어렵습니다."], ["The prophets had their light from Heaven directly, even as he had his—many to one, and Jehovah the same forever.", "예언자들은 그가 그러했듯 하늘에서 직접 빛을 받았습니다—여럿이 하나에게서, 그리고 여호와께서는 영원토록 한결같으십니다."], ["I must believe the prophets.—Bring me the Torah, Esther.”", "저는 예언자들을 믿어야 합니다.—토라를 가져오너라, 에스더야.\""]], [["He proceeded without waiting for her.", "그는 그녀를 기다리지 않고 말을 이었다."]], [["“May the testimony of a whole people be slighted, my master?", "\"한 민족 전체의 증언이 가벼이 여겨질 수 있겠습니까, 나의 주인이시여?"], ["Though you travel from Tyre, which is by the sea in the north, to the capital of Edom, which is in the desert south, you will not find a lisper of the Shema, an alms-giver in the Temple, or any one who has ever eaten of the lamb of the Passover, to tell you the kingdom the King is coming to build for us, the children of the covenant, is other than of this world, like our father David’s. Now where got they the faith, ask you!", "북쪽 바닷가에 있는 티레에서 남쪽 사막에 있는 에돔의 수도까지 두루 다니신다 해도, 셰마를 읊조리는 자, 성전에서 자선을 베푸는 자, 유월절 어린양을 먹어 본 자 가운데, 그 왕이 우리, 곧 언약의 자손들을 위해 세우러 오시는 왕국이 우리 조상 다윗의 왕국처럼 이 세상의 것이 아니라고 말할 사람을 단 하나도 찾지 못할 것입니다. 그렇다면 그들이 그 믿음을 어디서 얻었는지 물으시겠지요!"], ["We will see presently.”", "곧 알게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["Esther here returned, bringing a number of rolls carefully enveloped in dark-brown linen lettered quaintly in gold.", "그때 에스더가 돌아왔는데, 짙은 갈색 아마포에 정성껏 싸여 금빛으로 예스럽게 글자가 새겨진 두루마리를 여러 개 들고 왔다."]], [["“Keep them, daughter, to give to me as I call for them,”", "\"잘 간수하고 있다가, 얘야, 내가 청할 때 하나씩 다오.\""], ["the father said, in the tender voice he always used in speaking to her, and continued his argument:", "아버지가 그녀에게 말할 때면 늘 쓰던 다정한 목소리로 말하고는, 그 논변을 이어 갔다."]], [["“It were long, good my master—too long, indeed—for me to repeat to you the names of the holy men who, in the providence of God, succeeded the prophets, only a little less favored than they—the seers who have written and the preachers who have taught since the Captivity; the very wise who borrowed their lights from the lamp of Malachi, the last of his line, and whose great names Hillel and Shammai never tired of repeating in the colleges.", "\"좋은 나의 주인이시여, 하느님의 섭리 가운데 예언자들의 뒤를 이은 거룩한 사람들의 이름을 도련님께 일일이 되풀이하기에는 너무 길고—참으로 너무 길 것입니다—그들은 예언자들보다 조금 덜 은총을 입었을 뿐입니다. 바빌론 유수 이후 글을 쓴 선견자들과 가르침을 베푼 설교자들, 그리고 자기 계보의 마지막인 말라기의 등불에서 빛을 빌려 온 더없이 지혜로운 자들, 힐렐과 샴마이가 학당에서 그 위대한 이름들을 입에 올리기를 결코 싫증 내지 않던 그들 말입니다."], ["Will you ask them of the kingdom? Thus, the Lord of the sheep in the Book of Enoch—who is he?", "도련님은 그들에게 그 왕국에 관하여 물으시겠습니까? 자, 에녹서에 나오는 양들의 주님—그는 누구입니까?"], ["Who but the King of whom we are speaking?", "우리가 말하고 있는 그 왕이 아니면 누구이겠습니까?"], ["A throne is set up for him; he smites the earth, and the other kings are shaken from their thrones, and the scourges of Israel flung into a cavern of fire flaming with pillars of fire.", "그를 위해 보좌가 세워지고, 그가 땅을 치니 다른 왕들이 그 보좌에서 떨려 나며, 이스라엘을 매질하던 자들은 불기둥으로 타오르는 불의 동굴 속으로 던져집니다."], ["So also the singer of the Psalms of Solomon—‘Behold, O Lord, and raise up to Israel their king, the son of David, at the time thou knowest, O God, to rule Israel, thy children....", "솔로몬의 시편을 노래한 자도 마찬가지입니다—'보소서, 오 주여, 이스라엘에게 그들의 왕을, 다윗의 아들을 일으켜 세우소서, 당신께서 아시는 그때에, 오 하느님이시여, 당신의 자녀인 이스라엘을 다스리게 하소서.……"], ["And he will bring the peoples of the heathen under his yoke to serve him....", "그리고 그는 이방의 백성들을 그의 멍에 아래 끌어들여 그를 섬기게 하리이다.……"], ["And he shall be a righteous king taught of God, ...", "그리고 그는 하느님께 가르침을 받은 의로운 왕이 되리니, ……"], ["for he shall rule all the earth by the word of his mouth forever.’ And last, though not least, hear Ezra, the second Moses, in his visions of the night, and ask him who is the lion with human voice that says to the eagle—which is Rome—‘Thou hast loved liars, and overthrown the cities of the industrious, and razed their walls, though they did thee no harm.", "그는 그 입의 말씀으로 온 땅을 영원토록 다스릴 것이기 때문이니이다.' 그리고 마지막으로, 그러나 결코 못하지 않은, 제2의 모세인 에스라가 밤의 환상 속에서 한 말을 들으십시오. 그에게 물어보십시오, 독수리—이는 곧 로마입니다—에게 이렇게 말하는, 사람의 목소리를 가진 사자가 누구인지를."], ["Therefore, begone, that the earth may be refreshed, and recover itself, and hope in the justice and piety of him who made her.’ Whereat the eagle was seen no more.", "'너는 거짓말쟁이들을 사랑하였고, 부지런한 자들의 도성을 뒤엎었으며, 그들이 네게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았건만 그 성벽을 허물었다. 그러므로 물러가라, 땅이 새로워지고 스스로 회복하며, 그를 지으신 분의 공의와 경건을 바라게 하라.' 그러자 그 독수리는 더는 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Surely, O my master, the testimony of these should be enough!", "참으로, 오, 나의 주인이시여, 이들의 증언만으로도 넉넉할 것입니다!"], ["But the way to the fountain’s head is open.", "그러나 샘의 발원지로 가는 길은 열려 있습니다."], ["Let us go up to it at once.—Some wine, Esther, and then the Torah.”", "곧바로 그리로 올라가 봅시다.—포도주를 좀 다오, 에스더야. 그러고 나서 토라를 다오.\""]], [["“Dost thou believe the prophets, master?”", "\"도련님은 예언자들을 믿으십니까, 주인이시여?\""], ["he asked, after drinking.", "그는 포도주를 마신 뒤 물었다."], ["“I know thou dost, for of such was the faith of all thy kindred.—Give me, Esther, the book which hath in it the visions of Isaiah.”", "\"믿으시리라는 것을 압니다. 도련님의 온 일가가 그러한 믿음을 지녔으니까요.—에스더야, 이사야의 환상이 담긴 책을 다오.\""]], [["He took one of the rolls which she had unwrapped for him, and read, “‘The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined....", "그는 그녀가 펴서 풀어 준 두루마리 가운데 하나를 받아 들고 읽었다. \"'어둠 속을 걷던 백성이 큰 빛을 보았고, 죽음의 그늘진 땅에 거하던 자들 위에 빛이 비치었도다.……"], ["For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder....", "우리에게 한 아기가 태어났고, 우리에게 한 아들이 주어졌으니, 그 정권이 그의 어깨에 있으리로다.……"], ["Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it, and to establish it with judgment and with justice from henceforth even forever.’—Believest thou the prophets, O my master?—Now, Esther, the word of the Lord that came to Micah.”", "그 정권과 평강의 더함이 끝이 없을 것이며, 다윗의 보좌와 그의 나라 위에 군림하여, 이제부터 영원토록 공평과 정의로 그것을 굳게 세우리로다.'—예언자들을 믿으십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?—이제, 에스더야, 미가에게 임한 주님의 말씀을 다오.\""]], [["She gave him the roll he asked.", "그녀가 그가 청한 두루마리를 건넸다."]], [["“‘But thou,’”", "\"'그러나 너'.\""], ["he began reading—“‘but thou, Bethlehem Ephrath, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel.’—This was he, the very child Balthasar saw and worshipped in the cave.", "그가 읽기 시작했다. \"'그러나 너 베들레헴 에브라다야, 너는 유다의 수많은 무리 가운데 작을지라도, 이스라엘을 다스릴 자가 너에게서 내게로 나오리라.'—이가 바로 그분, 발타사르가 동굴에서 보고 경배한 바로 그 아기입니다."], ["Believest thou the prophets, O my master?—Give me, Esther, the words of Jeremiah.”", "예언자들을 믿으십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?—에스더야, 예레미야의 말씀을 다오.\""]], [["Receiving that roll, he read as before, “‘Behold, the days come, saith the Lord, that I will raise unto David a righteous branch, and a king shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth.", "그 두루마리를 받아 그는 앞서처럼 읽었다. \"'보라, 날이 이르리니, 주께서 말씀하시되, 내가 다윗에게 한 의로운 가지를 일으키리라, 한 왕이 다스려 형통하며, 세상에서 공평과 정의를 행하리라."], ["In his days Judah shall be saved, and Israel shall dwell safely.’ As a king he shall reign—as a king, O my master!", "그의 날에 유다가 구원을 받고, 이스라엘이 평안히 거하리라.' 그분은 왕으로서 다스릴 것입니다—왕으로서 말입니다, 오, 나의 주인이시여!"], ["Believest thou the prophets?—Now, daughter, the roll of the sayings of that son of Judah in whom there was no blemish.”", "예언자들을 믿으십니까?—이제, 얘야, 흠 없던 유다의 그 아들이 한 말이 담긴 두루마리를 다오.\""]], [["She gave him the Book of Daniel.", "그녀가 그에게 다니엘서를 건넸다."]], [["“Hear, my master,”", "\"들으십시오, 나의 주인이시여.\""], ["he said: “‘I saw in the night visions, and behold, one like the Son of man came with the clouds of heaven....", "그가 말했다. \"'내가 밤의 환상 가운데 보았는데, 보라, 인자 같은 이가 하늘 구름을 타고 오더라.……"], ["And there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom, that all people, nations, and languages should serve him; his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away, and his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed.’—Believest thou the prophets, O my master?”", "그에게 권세와 영광과 나라가 주어져, 모든 백성과 나라와 언어가 그를 섬기게 하니, 그의 권세는 영원한 권세라 사라지지 아니할 것이요, 그의 나라는 결코 멸망하지 아니하리라.'—예언자들을 믿으십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?\""]], [["“It is enough. I believe,”", "\"충분합니다. 저는 믿습니다.\""], ["cried Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 외쳤다."]], [["“What then?” asked Simonides.", "\"그렇다면 어떻게 하시겠습니까?\" 시모니데스가 물었다."], ["“If the King come poor, will not my master, of his abundance, give him help?”", "\"만약 그 왕이 가난하게 오신다면, 나의 주인은 그 풍족함으로 그분을 도와드리지 않으시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Help him? To the last shekel and the last breath.", "\"그분을 돕는다고요? 마지막 한 푼까지, 마지막 숨까지 바칠 것입니다."], ["But why speak of his coming poor?”", "그런데 어찌하여 그분이 가난하게 오신다고 말하십니까?\""]], [["“Give me, Esther, the word of the Lord as it came to Zechariah,” said Simonides.", "\"에스더야, 스가랴에게 임한 주님의 말씀을 다오.\" 시모니데스가 말했다."]], [["She gave him one of the rolls.", "그녀가 그에게 두루마리 하나를 건넸다."]], [["“Hear how the King will enter Jerusalem.”", "\"그 왕이 어떻게 예루살렘에 들어오실지 들으십시오.\""], ["Then he read, “‘Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion....", "그러고는 그가 읽었다. \"'크게 기뻐할지어다, 오 시온의 딸아.……"], ["Behold, thy King cometh unto thee with justice and salvation; lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt, the foal of an ass.’”", "보라, 네 왕이 공의와 구원을 가지고 네게 임하시나니, 그는 겸손하여 나귀를 타시며, 나귀의 새끼인 어린 나귀를 타셨도다.'\""]], [["Ben-Hur looked away.", "벤허가 시선을 돌렸다."]], [["“What see you, O my master?”", "\"무엇을 보십니까, 오, 나의 주인이시여?\""]], [["“Rome!”", "\"로마입니다!\""], ["he answered, gloomily—“Rome, and her legions.", "그가 침울하게 답했다. \"로마와 그 군단들 말입니다."], ["I have dwelt with them in their camps.", "저는 그들의 진영에서 그들과 함께 지냈습니다."], ["I know them.”", "저는 그들을 압니다.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Simonides.", "시모니데스가 말했다."], ["“Thou shalt be a master of legions for the King, with millions to choose from.”", "\"도련님은 그 왕을 위한 군단의 주인이 되실 것입니다, 수백만 가운데서 골라 거느리면서 말입니다.\""]], [["“Millions!” cried Ben-Hur.", "\"수백만이라고요!\" 벤허가 외쳤다."]], [["Simonides sat a moment thinking.", "시모니데스는 잠시 앉아 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“The question of power should not trouble you,”", "\"힘의 문제는 도련님을 괴롭히지 않을 것입니다.\""], ["he next said.", "그가 이어 말했다."]], [["Ben-Hur looked at him inquiringly.", "벤허가 의아한 듯 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“You were seeing the lowly King in the act of coming to his own,”", "\"도련님은 그 겸손한 왕이 자기 백성에게 임하시는 모습을 보고 계셨습니다.\""], ["Simonides answered—“seeing him on the right hand, as it were, and on the left the brassy legions of Cæsar, and you were asking, What can he do?”", "시모니데스가 답했다. \"이를테면 오른편에는 그분을, 왼편에는 카이사르의 놋쇠 같은 군단을 두고 보면서, 이렇게 물으셨겠지요, 그분이 무엇을 하실 수 있겠는가?\""]], [["“It was my very thought.”", "\"바로 제 생각이 그것이었습니다.\""]], [["“O my master!” Simonides continued.", "\"오, 나의 주인이시여!\" 시모니데스가 말을 이었다."], ["“You do not know how strong our Israel is.", "\"도련님은 우리 이스라엘이 얼마나 강한지 모르십니다."], ["You think of him as a sorrowful old man weeping by the rivers of Babylon.", "도련님은 그를 바빌론 강가에서 우는 슬픈 노인쯤으로 여기십니다. 그러나 다음 유월절에 예루살렘으로 올라가, 크시스토스나 흥정의 거리에 서서, 그가 어떤 모습인지 보십시오. 파단아람을 떠나오던 우리 조상 야곱에게 주신 주님의 약속은, 우리 백성이 번성하기를 그치지 않게 한 율법이었습니다—심지어 유수 중에도 그러했습니다."], ["But go up to Jerusalem next Passover, and stand on the Xystus or in the Street of Barter, and see him as he is.", "그들은 이집트인의 발밑에서 자라났고, 로마인의 움켜쥠도 그들에게는 오히려 건강한 양분이었습니다. 이제 그들은 참으로 '한 민족이요 많은 민족의 무리'입니다. 그뿐이 아닙니다, 나의 주인이시여."], ["The promise of the Lord to father Jacob coming out of Padan-Aram was a law under which our people have not ceased multiplying—not even in captivity; they grew under foot of the Egyptian; the clench of the Roman has been but wholesome nurture to them; now they are indeed ‘a nation and a company of nations.’ Nor that only, my master; in fact, to measure the strength of Israel—which is, in fact, measuring what the King can do—you shall not bide solely by the rule of natural increase, but add thereto the other—I mean the spread of the faith, which will carry you to the far and near of the whole known earth.", "사실 이스라엘의 힘을 가늠하는 일은—이는 곧 그 왕이 무엇을 하실 수 있는지를 가늠하는 일입니다—자연스러운 번성의 잣대에만 의지해서는 안 되고, 거기에 또 다른 것을 더해야 합니다. 곧 믿음의 퍼짐을 말합니다. 그것은 도련님을 알려진 온 땅의 멀고 가까운 곳으로 데려갈 것입니다."], ["Further, the habit is, I know, to think and speak of Jerusalem as Israel, which may be likened to our finding an embroidered shred, and holding it up as a magisterial robe of Cæsar’s.", "더 나아가, 사람들이 예루살렘을 이스라엘이라 여기고 말하는 버릇이 있음을 압니다. 그러나 이는 마치 수놓인 자투리 천 한 조각을 발견하고는 그것을 카이사르의 위엄 있는 옷이라며 치켜드는 것과 같습니다. 예루살렘은 성전의 돌 하나에 지나지 않고, 몸속의 심장에 지나지 않습니다."], ["Jerusalem is but a stone of the Temple, or the heart in the body.", "강하기는 하지만 그 군단들을 바라보던 눈을 돌려, '네 장막으로 돌아가라, 오 이스라엘아!'"], ["Turn from beholding the legions, strong though they be, and count the hosts of the faithful waiting the old alarm, ‘To your tents, O Israel!’—count the many in Persia, children of those who chose not to return with the returning; count the brethren who swarm the marts of Egypt and Farther Africa; count the Hebrew colonists eking profit in the West—in Lodinum and the trade-courts of Spain; count the pure of blood and the proselytes in Greece and in the isles of the sea, and over in Pontus, and here in Antioch, and, for that matter, those of that city lying accursed in the shadow of the unclean walls of Rome herself; count the worshippers of the Lord dwelling in tents along the deserts next us, as well as in the deserts beyond the Nile: and in the regions across the Caspian, and up in the old lands of Gog and Magog even, separate those who annually send gifts to the Holy Temple in acknowledgment of God—separate them, that they may be counted also.", "하는 옛 외침을 기다리는 신실한 무리를 헤아려 보십시오.—돌아오는 자들과 함께 돌아오기를 택하지 않은 자들의 자손인, 페르시아의 수많은 이들을 헤아려 보십시오. 이집트와 그 너머 아프리카의 저자에 들끓는 형제들을 헤아려 보십시오. 서쪽에서—로디눔과 에스파냐의 교역 마당에서—이문을 챙기는 히브리 이주민들을 헤아려 보십시오."], ["And when you have done counting, lo! my master, a census of the sword hands that await you; lo!", "그리스와 바다의 섬들, 저 멀리 폰토스, 그리고 이곳 안티오크에서, 또 그런 까닭에 로마 자신의 부정한 성벽 그늘 아래 저주받아 누운 그 도성의 사람들 가운데, 순수한 혈통의 자들과 개종자들을 헤아려 보십시오. 우리와 인접한 사막을 따라 장막에 거하는 주님의 경배자들을, 나일강 너머 사막에 거하는 자들과 더불어 헤아려 보십시오."], ["a kingdom ready fashioned for him who is to do ‘judgment and justice in the whole earth’—in Rome not less than in Zion.", "카스피해 건너편 지역, 그리고 곡과 마곡의 옛 땅에 이르기까지, 하느님을 인정하는 표시로 해마다 거룩한 성전에 예물을 보내는 자들을 가려내십시오—그들도 헤아릴 수 있도록 가려내십시오. 그리고 헤아리기를 마치면, 보십시오, 나의 주인이시여, 도련님을 기다리는 칼을 쥔 손들의 호적이 드러날 것입니다. 보십시오, '온 땅에서 공평과 정의를'—시온에서 못지않게 로마에서도—행하실 그분을 위해 이미 빚어진 왕국이 드러날 것입니다."], ["Have then the answer, What Israel can do, that can the King.”", "그러니 답을 가지십시오, 이스라엘이 할 수 있는 것, 그것을 그 왕도 하실 수 있습니다.\""]], [["The picture was fervently given.", "그 그림은 열렬하게 그려졌다."]], [["Upon Ilderim it operated like the blowing of a trumpet. “Oh that I had back my youth!”", "일데림에게는 그것이 나팔 소리처럼 작용했다. \"오, 내 젊음을 되찾을 수만 있다면!\""], ["he cried, starting to his feet.", "그가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."]], [["Ben-Hur sat still.", "벤허는 가만히 앉아 있었다."], ["The speech, he saw, was an invitation to devote his life and fortune to the mysterious Being who was palpably as much the centre of a great hope with Simonides as with the devout Egyptian.", "그 말은 자기 삶과 재산을 그 신비로운 존재에게 바치라는 권유임을 그는 알아차렸다. 그 존재는 경건한 이집트인에게 그러하듯 시모니데스에게도 분명 위대한 희망의 중심이었다."], ["The idea, as we have seen, was not a new one, but had come to him repeatedly; once while listening to Malluch in the Grove of Daphne; afterwards more distinctly while Balthasar was giving his conception of what the kingdom was to be; still later, in the walk through the old Orchard, it had risen almost, if not quite, into a resolve.", "우리가 보아 왔듯이, 그 생각은 새로운 것이 아니라 그에게 거듭 찾아왔던 것이다. 한 번은 다프네 숲에서 말루크의 말을 들을 때였고, 그 뒤에는 발타사르가 그 왕국이 어떤 것이 될지 자기 생각을 펼칠 때 더욱 또렷하게 찾아왔으며, 그보다 더 뒤, 옛 과수원을 거닐 때는 거의, 어쩌면 완전히, 하나의 결심으로까지 솟아오른 적도 있었다."], ["At such times it had come and gone only an idea, attended with feelings more or less acute. Not so now.", "그러나 그럴 때마다 그것은 다만 하나의 생각으로서 오갔을 뿐이며, 더하거나 덜한 절실한 감정을 거느리고 있었다. 그러나 지금은 달랐다."], ["A master had it in charge, a master was working it up; already he had exalted it into a cause brilliant with possibilities and infinitely holy.", "한 주인이 그 일을 떠맡았고, 한 주인이 그것을 빚어 가고 있었다. 이미 그는 그것을 무수한 가능성으로 찬란하고 한없이 거룩한 하나의 대의로 끌어올렸던 것이다."], ["The effect was as if a door theretofore unseen had suddenly opened flooding Ben-Hur with light, and admitting him to a service which had been his one perfect dream—a service reaching far into the future, and rich with the rewards of duty done, and prizes to sweeten and soothe his ambition.", "그 효과는 마치 그때껏 보이지 않던 문이 갑자기 열려, 벤허에게 빛이 쏟아져 들고, 그가 한결같이 꿈꾸어 온 그 완전한 일에 그를 들어서게 하는 것과 같았다—먼 미래까지 뻗어 가고, 다한 의무의 보상으로 풍성하며, 그의 야망을 달래고 어루만질 상급으로 가득한 일이었다."], ["One touch more was needed.", "한 번의 손길만 더 필요했다."]], [["“Let us concede all you say, O Simonides,”", "\"당신이 하신 모든 말씀을 인정하기로 합시다, 오, 시모니데스.\" 벤허가 말했다."], ["said Ben-Hur—“that the King will come, and his kingdom be as Solomon’s; say also I am ready to give myself and all I have to him and his cause; yet more, say that I should do as was God’s purpose in the ordering of my life and in your quick amassment of astonishing fortune; then what?", "\"그 왕이 오시고, 그 왕국이 솔로몬의 왕국과 같으리라는 것을. 또한 제가 저 자신과 제가 가진 모든 것을 그분과 그분의 대의에 바칠 각오가 되어 있다고 합시다. 더 나아가, 제 삶을 그렇게 빚으시고 당신의 놀라운 재산을 그토록 빠르게 쌓게 하신 것이 하느님의 뜻이었던 대로 제가 행해야 한다고 합시다."], ["Shall we proceed like blind men building?", "그렇다면 어떻게 하지요? 우리는 눈먼 자들이 집을 짓듯 나아가야 합니까?"], ["Shall we wait till the King comes?", "그 왕이 오실 때까지 기다려야 합니까?"], ["Or until he sends for me?", "아니면 그분이 저를 부르실 때까지요?"], ["You have age and experience on your side. Answer.”", "당신께는 연륜과 경험이 있습니다. 답해 주십시오.\""]], [["Simonides answered at once.", "시모니데스가 곧바로 답했다."]], [["“We have no choice; none. This letter”—he produced Messala’s despatch as he spoke—“this letter is the signal for action.", "\"우리에게 선택의 여지는 없습니다, 전혀 없습니다. 이 편지가\"—그는 말하면서 메살라의 급보를 꺼냈다—\"이 편지가 행동에 나서라는 신호입니다."], ["The alliance proposed between Messala and Gratus we are not strong enough to resist; we have not the influence at Rome nor the force here.", "메살라와 그라투스 사이에 제안된 동맹에 맞서기에 우리는 그만큼 강하지 못합니다. 로마에 영향력도 없고, 이곳에 무력도 없습니다."], ["They will kill you if we wait.", "우리가 기다린다면 그들은 도련님을 죽일 것입니다."], ["How merciful they are, look at me and judge.”", "그들이 얼마나 자비로운 자들인지, 저를 보고 헤아리십시오.\""]], [["He shuddered at the terrible recollection.", "그는 그 끔찍한 기억에 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“O good my master,”", "\"오, 좋은 나의 주인이시여.\""], ["he continued, recovering himself; “how strong are you—in purpose, I mean?”", "그는 마음을 추스르며 말을 이었다. \"도련님은 얼마나 강하십니까—제 말은, 의지에 있어서 말입니다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur did not understand him.", "벤허는 그의 말을 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["“I remember how pleasant the world was to me in my youth,”", "\"저는 젊은 시절 이 세상이 제게 얼마나 즐거웠는지 기억합니다.\""], ["Simonides proceeded.", "시모니데스가 이어 말했다."]], [["“Yet,”", "\"그런데도.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, “you were capable of a great sacrifice.”", "벤허가 말했다. \"당신은 위대한 희생을 감당할 수 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Yes; for love.”", "\"그렇습니다, 사랑을 위해서 말입니다.\""]], [["“Has not life other motives as strong?”", "\"삶에는 그만큼 강한 다른 동기가 없습니까?\""]], [["Simonides shook his head.", "시모니데스가 고개를 저었다."]], [["“There is ambition.”", "\"야망이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ambition is forbidden a son of Israel.”", "\"야망은 이스라엘의 자손에게 금지되어 있습니다.\""]], [["“What, then, of revenge?”", "\"그렇다면 복수는 어떻습니까?\""]], [["The spark dropped upon the inflammable passion; the man’s eyes gleamed; his hands shook; he answered, quickly, “Revenge is a Jew’s of right; it is the law.”", "그 불꽃이 불붙기 쉬운 열정 위로 떨어졌다. 그의 두 눈이 번득였고, 두 손이 떨렸다. 그가 재빨리 답했다. \"복수는 유대인의 당연한 권리입니다. 그것은 율법입니다.\""]], [["“A camel, even a dog, will remember a wrong,”", "\"낙타도, 개조차도, 당한 해코지를 기억합니다.\""], ["cried Ilderim.", "일데림이 외쳤다."]], [["Directly Simonides picked up the broken thread of his thought.", "곧이어 시모니데스는 끊어졌던 생각의 실타래를 다시 집어 들었다."]], [["“There is a work, a work for the King, which should be done in advance of his coming.", "\"한 가지 일이 있습니다, 그 왕을 위한 일이, 그분이 오시기에 앞서 마쳐 두어야 할 일이 말입니다."], ["We may not doubt that Israel is to be his right hand; but, alas!", "이스라엘이 그분의 오른팔이 되리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그러나, 아아!"], ["it is a hand of peace, without cunning in war.", "그것은 평화의 손이라, 싸움의 솜씨가 없습니다."], ["Of the millions, there is not one trained band, not a captain.", "그 수백만 가운데 훈련된 부대 하나, 지휘관 하나 없습니다."], ["The mercenaries of the Herods I do not count, for they are kept to crush us.", "헤롯 일가의 용병들은 헤아리지 않습니다. 그들은 우리를 짓밟기 위해 길러진 자들이니까요."], ["The condition is as the Roman would have it; his policy has fruited well for his tyranny; but the time of change is at hand, when the shepherd shall put on armor, and take to spear and sword, and the feeding flocks be turned to fighting lions.", "그 형편은 로마인이 바라는 그대로입니다. 그의 정책은 그의 폭정을 위해 좋은 열매를 맺었습니다. 그러나 변화의 때가 가까이 왔으니, 목자가 갑옷을 두르고 창과 칼을 잡으며, 풀을 뜯던 양 떼가 싸우는 사자로 바뀌는 때 말입니다."], ["Some one, my son, must have place next the King at his right hand.", "누군가는, 내 아들아, 그 왕의 오른편에서 그분 곁의 자리를 차지해야 합니다."], ["Who shall it be if not he who does this work well?”", "이 일을 잘 해내는 자가 아니라면 누가 그 자리에 앉겠습니까?\""]], [["Ben-Hur’s face flushed at the prospect, though he said, “I see; but speak plainly.", "벤허의 얼굴이 그 전망에 붉어졌으나, 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"알겠습니다. 그러나 분명하게 말씀해 주십시오."], ["A deed to be done is one thing; how to do it is another.”", "해야 할 일이 있다는 것은 하나요, 그것을 어떻게 하느냐는 또 다른 일입니다.\""]], [["Simonides sipped the wine Esther brought him, and replied,", "시모니데스는 에스더가 가져온 포도주를 한 모금 마시고 답했다."]], [["“The sheik, and thou, my master, shall be principals, each with a part.", "\"족장과 도련님이, 나의 주인이시여, 각기 한몫을 맡은 주역이 되실 것입니다."], ["I will remain here, carrying on as now, and watchful that the spring go not dry.", "저는 이곳에 남아 지금처럼 일을 꾸려 가며, 그 샘이 마르지 않도록 살피겠습니다."], ["Thou shalt betake thee to Jerusalem, and thence to the wilderness, and begin numbering the fighting-men of Israel, and telling them into tens and hundreds, and choosing captains and training them, and in secret places hoarding arms, for which I shall keep thee supplied.", "도련님은 예루살렘으로, 거기서 다시 광야로 가셔서, 이스라엘의 싸울 수 있는 자들을 헤아리기 시작하고, 그들을 열 명 단위, 백 명 단위로 셈하며, 지휘관을 뽑아 훈련시키고, 은밀한 곳에 무기를 쌓아 두십시오. 그 무기는 제가 계속 대어 드리겠습니다."], ["Commencing over in Perea, thou shalt go then to Galilee, whence it is but a step to Jerusalem.", "페레아에서 시작하여, 그다음 갈릴리로 가십시오. 거기서 예루살렘까지는 한 걸음에 지나지 않습니다."], ["In Perea, the desert will be at thy back, and Ilderim in reach of thy hand.", "페레아에서는 사막이 도련님의 등 뒤에 있고, 일데림이 도련님의 손이 닿는 거리에 있을 것입니다."], ["He will keep the roads, so that nothing shall pass without thy knowledge.", "그는 길을 지킬 것이니, 도련님이 모르는 채로 지나갈 것은 아무것도 없을 것입니다."], ["He will help thee in many ways.", "그는 여러모로 도련님을 도울 것입니다."], ["Until the ripening time no one shall know what is here contracted.", "무르익을 때가 오기까지는 여기서 맺은 약조를 아무도 모를 것입니다."], ["Mine is but a servant’s part.", "제 몫은 다만 종의 몫일 뿐입니다."], ["I have spoken to Ilderim. What sayest thou?”", "저는 일데림과 이야기를 나누었습니다. 도련님은 어떻게 말씀하시겠습니까?\""]], [["Ben-Hur looked at the sheik.", "벤허가 족장을 바라보았다."]], [["“It is as he says, son of Hur,”", "\"그의 말 그대로다, 후르의 아들이여.\""], ["the Arab responded.", "아랍인이 답했다."], ["“I have given my word, and he is content with it; but thou shalt have my oath, binding me, and the ready hands of my tribe, and whatever serviceable thing I have.”", "\"나는 이미 내 말을 주었고, 그는 그것으로 흡족해한다. 그러나 그대에게는 나를 묶는 맹세를 주겠다. 내 부족의 늘 준비된 손들과, 내게 쓸모 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 함께 말이다.\""]], [["The three—Simonides, Ilderim, Esther—gazed at Ben-Hur fixedly.", "세 사람—시모니데스, 일데림, 에스더—이 벤허를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“Every man,”", "\"사람은 누구나.\""], ["he answered, at first sadly, “has a cup of pleasure poured for him, and soon or late it comes to his hand, and he tastes and drinks—every man but me.", "그가 처음에는 슬프게 답했다. \"자기 몫으로 부어진 즐거움의 잔을 가지고 있어, 이르든 늦든 그것이 그의 손에 닿으면 맛보고 마십니다—저만 빼고 누구나 그렇습니다. 알겠습니다, 시모니데스, 그리고 그대, 오, 너그러운 족장이시여!—저는 이 제안이 어디로 향하는지 알겠습니다."], ["I see, Simonides, and thou, O generous sheik!—I see whither the proposal tends.", "제가 받아들여 그 길에 들어선다면, 평화도, 그것을 둘러싼 온갖 희망도 안녕입니다."], ["If I accept, and enter upon the course, farewell peace, and the hopes which cluster around it.", "제가 들어설 수 있었을 문들, 평온한 삶으로 통하는 그 문들은 제 뒤에서 닫혀 다시는 열리지 않을 것입니다."], ["The doors I might enter and the gates of quiet life will shut behind me, never to open again, for Rome keeps them all; and her outlawry will follow me, and her hunters; and in the tombs near cities and the dismal caverns of remotest hills, I must eat my crust and take my rest.”", "로마가 그 모든 문을 쥐고 있기 때문입니다. 로마의 추방령이 저를 따라다닐 것이고, 로마의 사냥꾼들도 그러할 것입니다. 그리고 도성 가까운 무덤 속과 가장 외진 언덕의 음산한 동굴 속에서, 저는 제 빵 부스러기를 먹고 쉬어야 할 것입니다.\""]], [["The speech was broken by a sob.", "그 말이 흐느낌에 끊겼다."], ["All turned to Esther, who hid her face upon her father’s shoulder.", "모두 에스더에게로 고개를 돌렸으니, 그녀가 아버지의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 있었다."]], [["“I did not think of you, Esther,”", "\"내 너를 미처 생각하지 못했구나, 에스더야.\""], ["said Simonides, gently, for he was himself deeply moved.", "시모니데스가 부드럽게 말했으니, 그 자신이 깊이 마음이 움직인 까닭이었다."]], [["“It is well enough, Simonides,”", "\"괜찮습니다, 시모니데스.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“A man bears a hard doom better, knowing there is pity for him.", "\"사람은 자기를 가엾이 여기는 마음이 있음을 알면, 모진 운명도 더 잘 견디는 법입니다."], ["Let me go on.”", "계속하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["They gave him ear again.", "그들이 다시 그에게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“I was about to say,”", "\"제가 말하려던 것은.\""], ["he continued, “I have no choice, but take the part you assign me; and as remaining here is to meet an ignoble death, I will to the work at once.”", "그가 이어 말했다. \"저에게 선택의 여지가 없으니, 당신이 제게 맡기는 몫을 받겠다는 것입니다. 그리고 이곳에 남는 것이 곧 비천한 죽음을 맞는 일이니, 저는 곧바로 그 일에 착수하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Shall we have writings?”", "\"우리가 문서를 작성할까요?\""], ["asked Simonides, moved by his habit of business.", "시모니데스가 사업가다운 버릇에 이끌려 물었다."]], [["“I rest upon your word,”", "\"저는 당신의 말씀에 의지하겠습니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["“And I,” Ilderim answered.", "\"저 또한 그렇습니다.\" 일데림이 답했다."]], [["Thus simply was effected the treaty which was to alter Ben-Hur’s life.", "이렇게 단출하게, 벤허의 삶을 바꾸어 놓을 그 맹약이 맺어졌다."], ["And almost immediately the latter added,", "그리고 거의 곧바로 벤허가 덧붙였다."]], [["“It is done, then.”", "\"그러면 다 된 것입니다.\""]], [["“May the God of Abraham help us!”", "\"아브라함의 하느님께서 우리를 도우시기를!\""], ["Simonides exclaimed.", "시모니데스가 외쳤다."]], [["“One word now, my friends,”", "\"이제 한마디만, 벗들이여.\""], ["Ben-Hur said, more cheerfully.", "벤허가 한결 밝게 말했다."], ["“By your leave, I will be my own until after the games.", "\"허락해 주신다면, 경기가 끝날 때까지는 제 몸을 제 뜻대로 두겠습니다."], ["It is not probable Messala will set peril on foot for me until he has given the procurator time to answer him; and that cannot be in less than seven days from the despatch of his letter.", "메살라가 자기 편지에 총독이 답할 시간을 주기 전에 저를 위태롭게 할 일을 꾸미지는 않을 것 같습니다. 그 답은 그가 편지를 보낸 지 이레 안에는 오지 못할 것입니다."], ["The meeting him in the Circus is a pleasure I would buy at whatever risk.”", "원형경기장에서 그를 만나는 것은, 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 사고 싶은 즐거움입니다.\""]], [["Ilderim, well pleased, assented readily, and Simonides, intent on business, added, “It is well; for look you, my master, the delay will give me time to do you a good part.", "일데림은 크게 흡족하여 선뜻 동의했고, 사업에 골몰한 시모니데스가 덧붙였다. \"잘 생각하셨습니다. 보십시오, 나의 주인이시여, 그 미룸은 제가 도련님께 한 가지 좋은 일을 해 드릴 시간을 줄 것입니다."], ["I understood you to speak of an inheritance derived from Arrius. Is it in property?”", "제가 알기로 도련님은 아리우스에게서 물려받은 유산을 말씀하셨지요. 그것이 재산으로 되어 있습니까?\""]], [["“A villa near Misenum, and houses in Rome.”", "\"미세눔 근처의 별장과, 로마에 있는 집들입니다.\""]], [["“I suggest, then, the sale of the property, and safe deposit of the proceeds.", "\"그렇다면 저는 그 재산을 팔아, 그 대금을 안전하게 맡겨 두기를 권합니다."], ["Give me an account of it, and I will have authorities drawn, and despatch an agent on the mission forthwith.", "제게 그 명세를 주시면, 위임장을 작성하고 곧바로 대리인을 그 일에 보내겠습니다."], ["We will forestall the imperial robbers at least this once.”", "적어도 이번 한 번은 제국의 도적들보다 한발 앞서겠습니다.\""]], [["“You shall have the account to-morrow.”", "\"내일 명세를 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then, if there be nothing more, the work of the night is done,”", "\"그러면, 더 할 말이 없다면, 오늘 밤의 일은 다 마쳤습니다.\""], ["said Simonides.", "시모니데스가 말했다."]], [["Ilderim combed his beard complacently, saying, “And well done.”", "일데림은 흡족하게 수염을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"그것도 잘 마쳤지요.\""]], [["“The bread and wine again, Esther.", "\"빵과 포도주를 다시 다오, 에스더야."], ["Sheik Ilderim will make us happy by staying with us till to-morrow, or at his pleasure; and thou, my master—”", "일데림 족장께서 내일까지, 혹은 마음 내키시는 만큼 우리와 함께 머물러 주신다면 우리는 기쁠 것입니다. 그리고 도련님은—\""]], [["“Let the horses be brought,”", "\"말을 끌어오게 해 주십시오.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“I will return to the Orchard.", "\"저는 과수원으로 돌아가겠습니다."], ["The enemy will not discover me if I go now, and”—he glanced at Ilderim—“the four will be glad to see me.”", "지금 가면 적이 저를 알아채지 못할 것이고, 그리고\"—그는 일데림을 흘긋 보았다—\"그 네 마리도 저를 보면 반가워할 것입니다.\""]], [["As the day dawned, he and Malluch dismounted at the door of the tent.", "날이 밝아 올 무렵, 그와 말루크는 천막 문 앞에서 말에서 내렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# CHAPTER IX"]], [["Next night, about the fourth hour, Ben-Hur stood on the terrace of the great warehouse with Esther.", "이튿날 밤, 네 번째 시각 무렵, 벤허는 거대한 창고의 옥상 테라스에 에스더와 함께 서 있었다."], ["Below them, on the landing, there was much running about, and shifting of packages and boxes, and shouting of men, whose figures, stooping, heaving, hauling, looked, in the light of the crackling torches kindled in their aid, like the laboring genii of the fantastic Eastern tales.", "그들 아래, 부두에서는 분주한 움직임이 한창이어서, 짐짝과 상자가 옮겨지고, 사람들이 고함을 질렀다. 허리를 구부리고, 들어 올리고, 끌어당기는 그들의 모습은, 그 일을 거들려고 피워 놓은 타닥거리는 횃불 빛 속에서, 동방의 환상적인 이야기에 나오는 일하는 정령들처럼 보였다."], ["A galley was being laden for instant departure.", "갤리선 한 척이 당장 떠날 채비로 짐을 싣고 있었다."], ["Simonides had not yet come from his office, in which, at the last moment, he would deliver to the captain of the vessel instructions to proceed without stop to Ostia, the seaport of Rome, and, after landing a passenger there, continue more leisurely to Valentia, on the coast of Spain.", "시모니데스는 아직 사무실에서 나오지 않았으니, 마지막 순간에 그 배의 선장에게 로마의 항구인 오스티아까지 쉬지 말고 나아가, 거기서 승객 한 사람을 내려놓은 뒤, 더 느긋하게 에스파냐 해안의 발렌티아까지 계속 가라는 지시를 내릴 참이었다."]], [["The passenger is the agent going to dispose of the estate derived from Arrius the duumvir.", "그 승객은 두움비르 아리우스에게서 물려받은 재산을 처분하러 가는 대리인이다."], ["When the lines of the vessel are cast off, and she is put about, and her voyage begun, Ben-Hur will be committed irrevocably to the work undertaken the night before.", "배의 밧줄이 풀리고, 뱃머리가 돌려져, 항해가 시작되면, 벤허는 전날 밤에 떠맡은 일에 돌이킬 수 없이 매이게 된다."], ["If he is disposed to repent the agreement with Ilderim, a little time is allowed him to give notice and break it off.", "만약 일데림과 맺은 약속을 후회할 마음이 있다면, 그것을 알리고 깨뜨릴 약간의 시간이 그에게 허락되어 있다."], ["He is master, and has only to say the word.", "그가 주인이니, 한마디 말만 하면 그만이다."]], [["Such may have been the thought at the moment in his mind.", "아마도 그 순간 그의 마음속에 든 생각이 그러했을 것이다."], ["He was standing with folded arms, looking upon the scene in the manner of a man debating with himself.", "그는 팔짱을 낀 채, 자기 자신과 논쟁하는 사람의 태도로 그 광경을 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Young, handsome, rich, but recently from the patrician circles of Roman society, it is easy to think of the world besetting him with appeals not to give more to onerous duty or ambition attended with outlawry and danger.", "젊고, 잘생기고, 부유하며, 최근까지 로마 사회의 귀족 계층에 속했던 사람이니, 세상이 그를 둘러싸고, 무법과 위험이 뒤따르는 짐스러운 의무나 야망에 더 이상 자신을 바치지 말라고 호소하는 모습을 떠올리기란 어렵지 않다."], ["We can even imagine the arguments with which he was pressed; the hopelessness of contention with Cæsar; the uncertainty veiling everything connected with the King and his coming; the ease, honors, state, purchasable like goods in market; and, strongest of all, the sense newly acquired of home, with friends to make it delightful.", "그를 짓누른 논리마저 우리는 상상할 수 있다. 카이사르와 다투어 보았자 가망이 없다는 것, 왕과 그의 강림에 얽힌 모든 것을 가리고 있는 불확실함, 시장에서 물건을 사듯 살 수 있는 안락과 명예와 지위, 그리고 무엇보다도 가장 강력한 것, 새로이 얻은 가정에 대한 감각, 그것을 즐겁게 해 줄 벗들."], ["Only those who have been wanderers long desolate can know the power there was in the latter appeal.", "오랫동안 황량하게 떠돈 자만이 그 마지막 호소에 깃든 힘을 알 수 있으리라."]], [["Let us add now, the world—always cunning enough of itself; always whispering to the weak, Stay, take thine ease; always presenting the sunny side of life—the world was in this instance helped by Ben-Hur’s companion.", "이제 덧붙이자면, 세상은—언제나 제 스스로 충분히 교활하고, 언제나 약한 자에게 머물러라, 편히 쉬어라 하고 속삭이며, 언제나 삶의 양지바른 면을 내보이는 세상은—이 경우 벤허의 길동무의 도움까지 받았다. \"로마에 가 본 적이 있소?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“Were you ever at Rome?” he asked.", "\"아니요.\" 에스더가 대답했다."]], [["“No,” Esther replied.", "\"가 보고 싶소?\""]], [["“Would you like to go?”", "\"그렇지 않은 것 같아요.\""]], [["“I think not.”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Why?” “I am afraid of Rome,”", "\"저는 로마가 두려워요.\""], ["she answered, with a perceptible tremor of the voice.", "그녀가 또렷이 떨리는 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["He looked at her then—or rather down upon her, for at his side she appeared little more than a child.", "그러자 그는 그녀를 바라보았다. 아니, 그녀를 내려다보았다고 해야 옳으니, 그의 곁에서 그녀는 어린아이나 다름없어 보였기 때문이다."], ["In the dim light he could not see her face distinctly; even the form was shadowy.", "어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 또렷이 볼 수 없었다. 그 모습조차 그림자 같았다."], ["But again he was reminded of Tirzah, and a sudden tenderness fell upon him—just so the lost sister stood with him on the house-top the calamitous morning of the accident to Gratus.", "그러나 다시금 그는 티르자를 떠올렸고, 문득 부드러운 정이 그에게 밀려왔다. 그라투스에게 사고가 일어났던 그 재앙의 아침에, 잃어버린 누이가 바로 그렇게 지붕 위에서 그와 함께 서 있었던 것이다."], ["Poor Tirzah!", "가엾은 티르자!"], ["Where was she now?", "그녀는 지금 어디에 있는가?"], ["Esther had the benefit of the feeling evoked.", "에스더는 그렇게 불러일으켜진 그 감정의 덕을 보았다."], ["If not his sister, he could never look upon her as his servant; and that she was his servant in fact would make him always the more considerate and gentle towards her.", "누이가 아니라 해도, 그는 결코 그녀를 자기 종으로 볼 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 실제로 그의 종이라는 사실은, 그가 늘 그녀에게 더욱 사려 깊고 다정하게 대하도록 만들 것이었다."]], [["“I cannot think of Rome,”", "\"저는 로마를 생각할 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 목소리를 가다듬고, 조용하고 여인다운 말투로 이어 갔다. \"저는 로마를 궁전과 신전이 가득하고 사람들이 북적이는 도시로는 생각할 수가 없어요."], ["she continued, recovering her voice, and speaking in her quiet womanly way—“I cannot think of Rome as a city of palaces and temples, and crowded with people; she is to me a monster which has possession of one of the beautiful lands, and lies there luring men to ruin and death—a monster which it is not possible to resist—a ravenous beast gorging with blood. Why—”", "제게 로마는 아름다운 땅 하나를 차지하고 들어앉아, 거기서 사람들을 파멸과 죽음으로 꾀어내는 괴물이에요. 도저히 맞설 수 없는 괴물, 피를 게걸스레 빨아먹는 굶주린 짐승이지요. 그런데 왜—\""]], [["She faltered, looked down, stopped.", "그녀는 말을 더듬다가, 시선을 떨구고, 멈추었다."]], [["“Go on,” said Ben-Hur, reassuringly.", "\"계속하시오.\" 벤허가 안심시키듯 말했다."]], [["She drew closer to him, looked up again, and said, “Why must you make her your enemy?", "그녀는 그에게 더 가까이 다가서서, 다시 고개를 들고 말했다. \"어째서 로마를 적으로 삼으셔야 하나요?"], ["Why not rather make peace with her, and be at rest?", "차라리 로마와 화평을 맺고 편히 쉬시는 것이 어떻겠어요?"], ["You have had many ills, and borne them; you have survived the snares laid for you by foes.", "당신은 숱한 고난을 겪고도 견뎌 내셨어요. 원수들이 놓은 덫에서도 살아남으셨고요."], ["Sorrow has consumed your youth; is it well to give it the remainder of your days?”", "슬픔이 당신의 젊음을 다 삼켜 버렸는데, 남은 날들마저 거기에 내주는 것이 옳은 일인가요?\""]], [["The girlish face under his eyes seemed to come nearer and get whiter as the pleading went on; he stooped towards it, and asked, softly, “What would you have me do, Esther?”", "호소가 이어질수록, 그의 눈 아래 어린 소녀 같은 얼굴은 더 가까이 다가오며 더 창백해지는 듯했다. 그는 그 얼굴을 향해 몸을 숙이고 부드럽게 물었다. \"내가 어찌하기를 바라오, 에스더?\""]], [["She hesitated a moment, then asked, in return, “Is the property near Rome a residence?”", "그녀는 잠시 머뭇거리더니, 도리어 이렇게 물었다. \"로마 근처의 그 재산은 살 만한 집인가요?\" \"그렇소.\""]], [["“Yes.” “And pretty?”", "\"그리고 아름다운가요?\""]], [["“It is beautiful—a palace in the midst of gardens and shell-strewn walks; fountains without and within; statuary in the shady nooks; hills around covered with vines, and so high that Neapolis and Vesuvius are in sight, and the sea an expanse of purpling blue dotted with restless sails.", "\"아름답소. 정원과 조개껍데기 깔린 산책로 한가운데 자리 잡은 궁전이지. 안에도 밖에도 분수가 있고, 그늘진 구석마다 조각상이 서 있소."], ["Cæsar has a country-seat near-by, but in Rome they say the old Arrian villa is the prettiest.”", "둘레의 언덕들은 포도 덩굴로 뒤덮여 있고, 어찌나 높은지 네아폴리스와 베수비우스가 다 보인다오. 바다는 자줏빛으로 물든 푸른 너른 들판처럼 펼쳐지고, 그 위로 쉼 없이 움직이는 돛들이 점점이 박혀 있소. 카이사르도 근처에 시골 별장을 두고 있지만, 로마 사람들은 옛 아리우스 별장이 가장 아름답다고들 하오.\""]], [["“And the life there, is it quiet?”", "\"그러면 그곳의 삶은 평온한가요?\""]], [["“There was never a summer day, never a moonlit night, more quiet, save when visitors come.", "\"손님이 찾아올 때를 빼면, 그보다 더 평온한 여름날도, 그보다 더 평온한 달밤도 없었소. 이제는 옛 주인도 떠나고 나도 이곳에 와 있으니, 그 고요를 깨뜨릴 것은 아무것도 없소."], ["Now that the old owner is gone, and I am here, there is nothing to break its silence—nothing, unless it be the whispering of servants, or the whistling of happy birds, or the noise of fountains at play; it is changeless, except as day by day old flowers fade and fall, and new ones bud and bloom, and the sunlight gives place to the shadow of a passing cloud.", "종들의 속삭임이나, 행복한 새들의 지저귐이나, 분수의 노니는 소리가 아니라면 말이오. 그것은 변함이 없소. 다만 날마다 옛 꽃들이 시들어 떨어지고 새 꽃들이 움트고 피어나며, 햇살이 지나가는 구름 그림자에 자리를 내주는 것 말고는."], ["The life, Esther, was all too quiet for me.", "에스더, 그 삶은 내게는 너무 평온했소."], ["It made me restless by keeping always present a feeling that I, who have so much to do, was dropping into idle habits, and tying myself with silken chains, and after a while—and not a long while either—would end with nothing done.”", "할 일이 그토록 많은 내가 게으른 습관에 빠져들고, 비단 사슬로 나 자신을 옭아매며, 얼마 지나지 않아—그것도 그리 긴 세월이 아니라—아무것도 이룬 것 없이 끝나 버리리라는 느낌을 끊임없이 일깨우며 나를 불안하게 만들었소.\""]], [["She looked off over the river.", "그녀는 강 건너편으로 시선을 돌렸다."]], [["“Why did you ask?”", "\"왜 물어본 거요?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Good my master—”", "\"주인님—\""]], [["“No, no, Esther—not that.", "\"아니, 아니오, 에스더, 그렇게 부르지 마시오."], ["Call me friend—brother, if you will; I am not your master, and will not be.", "친구라고 부르시오. 원한다면 오라비라 불러도 좋소. 나는 당신의 주인이 아니고, 주인이 되지도 않을 것이오."], ["Call me brother.”", "오라비라 부르시오.\""]], [["He could not see the flush of pleasure which reddened her face, and the glow of the eyes that went out lost in the void above the river.", "기쁨으로 그녀의 얼굴을 붉게 물들인 홍조도, 강물 위 허공 속으로 사라져 간 두 눈의 빛도, 그는 보지 못했다."]], [["“I cannot understand,”", "\"저는 이해할 수가 없어요.\""], ["she said, “the nature which prefers the life you are going to—a life of—”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 택하시려는 그런 삶을—그런—\""]], [["“Of violence, and it may be of blood,”", "\"폭력의 삶, 어쩌면 피의 삶이겠지.\""], ["he said, completing the sentence.", "그가 그녀의 말을 마무리 지었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she added, “the nature which could prefer that life to such as might be in the beautiful villa.”", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그 아름다운 별장에서 누릴 수 있는 삶보다 그런 삶을 더 좋아하실 수 있는 그런 본성을요.\""]], [["“Esther, you mistake.", "\"에스더, 당신이 잘못 알고 있소."], ["There is no preference. Alas!", "좋아하고 말고가 아니오. 아아!"], ["the Roman is not so kind.", "로마인은 그렇게 너그럽지 않다오."], ["I am going of necessity.", "나는 어쩔 수 없이 가는 것이오."], ["To stay here is to die; and if I go there, the end will be the same—a poisoned cup, a bravo’s blow, or a judge’s sentence obtained by perjury.", "여기 머무는 것은 곧 죽는 것이고, 거기로 간다 해도 끝은 마찬가지일 것이오. 독을 탄 잔, 자객의 칼, 아니면 위증으로 얻어 낸 재판관의 판결이겠지."], ["Messala and the procurator Gratus are rich with plunder of my father’s estate, and it is more important to them to keep their gains now than was their getting in the first instance.", "메살라와 충독 그라투스는 내 아버지의 재산을 약탈해 부유해졌으니, 그들에게는 처음 그것을 손에 넣었을 때보다 지금 그 이득을 지키는 것이 더 중요한 일이오."], ["A peaceable settlement is out of reach, because of the confession it would imply.", "화평한 합의란 손에 닿지 않는 것이니, 그것은 곧 자기들의 죄를 인정하는 일이 되기 때문이오."], ["And then—then— Ah, Esther, if I could buy them, I do not know that I would.", "그리고 또한—또한—아, 에스더, 설령 내가 그들을 돈으로 살 수 있다 해도, 그러고 싶을지 모르겠소."], ["I do not believe peace possible to me; no, not even in the sleepy shade and sweet air of the marble porches of the old villa—no matter who might be there to help me bear the burden of the days, nor by what patience of love she made the effort.", "나는 내게 평화가 가능하다고 믿지 않소. 그렇소, 옛 별장의 대리석 회랑의 졸음 겨운 그늘과 향긋한 공기 속에서도 그렇지 못할 것이오. 거기서 누가 내 곁에 있어 날들의 짐을 함께 져 주든, 그녀가 얼마나 사랑의 인내로 그 일에 힘쓰든 말이오."], ["Peace is not possible to me while my people are lost, for I must be watchful to find them.", "내 식구들이 사라진 채로 있는 한, 내게 평화란 가능하지 않소. 그들을 찾아내기 위해 나는 늘 경계하고 있어야 하기 때문이오."], ["If I find them, and they have suffered wrong, shall not the guilty suffer for it?", "만약 내가 그들을 찾아냈는데 그들이 부당한 일을 당했다면, 죄지은 자가 그 대가를 치러야 마땅하지 않겠소?"], ["If they are dead by violence, shall the murderers escape?", "만약 그들이 폭력으로 죽임을 당했다면, 살인자들이 빠져나가도 되겠소?"], ["Oh, I could not sleep for dreams!", "오, 나는 꿈자리가 사나워 잠을 이룰 수 없을 것이오!"], ["Nor could the holiest love, by any stratagem, lull me to a rest which conscience would not strangle.”", "아무리 거룩한 사랑이라도, 어떤 술수를 부린들, 양심이 졸라 죽이지 않을 안식으로 나를 달랠 수는 없을 것이오.\""]], [["“Is it so bad then?”", "\"그렇게나 나쁜 건가요?\""], ["she asked, her voice tremulous with feeling. “Can nothing, nothing, be done?”", "그녀가 감정으로 떨리는 목소리로 물었다. \"아무것도, 정말 아무것도 할 수 없는 건가요?\""]], [["Ben-Hur took her hand.", "벤허는 그녀의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Do you care so much for me?”", "\"나를 그토록 생각해 주는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered, simply.", "그녀가 꾸밈없이 대답했다."]], [["The hand was warm, and in the palm of his it was lost. He felt it tremble.", "그 손은 따뜻했고, 그의 손바닥 안에서 자취를 감추었다. 그는 그 손이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Then the Egyptian came, so the opposite of this little one; so tall, so audacious, with a flattery so cunning, a wit so ready, a beauty so wonderful, a manner so bewitching.", "그때 이 작은 여인과 그토록 대조적인 그 이집트 여인이 떠올랐다. 그토록 키가 크고, 그토록 대담하며, 그토록 교묘한 아첨과 그토록 재빠른 재치와 그토록 놀라운 아름다움과 그토록 사람을 호리는 태도를 지닌 여인이."], ["He carried the hand to his lips, and gave it back.", "그는 그 손을 들어 입술에 가져다 댔다가, 다시 돌려주었다."]], [["“You shall be another Tirzah to me, Esther.”", "\"당신은 내게 또 하나의 티르자가 되어 줄 거요, 에스더.\""]], [["“Who is Tirzah?”", "\"티르자가 누구예요?\""]], [["“The little sister the Roman stole from me, and whom I must find before I can rest or be happy.”", "\"로마인이 내게서 훔쳐 간 어린 누이요. 내가 쉬거나 행복해지기 전에 반드시 찾아내야 할 누이지.\""]], [["Just then a gleam of light flashed athwart the terrace and fell upon the two; and, looking round, they saw a servant roll Simonides in his chair out of the door.", "바로 그때 한 줄기 빛이 테라스를 가로질러 번쩍이며 두 사람 위로 떨어졌다. 돌아보니, 한 종이 의자에 앉은 시모니데스를 밀어 문밖으로 내오고 있었다."], ["They went to the merchant, and in the after-talk he was principal.", "그들은 그 상인에게로 갔고, 뒤이은 대화에서는 그가 주역이었다."]], [["Immediately the lines of the galley were cast off, and she swung round, and, midst the flashing of torches and the shouting of joyous sailors, hurried off to the sea—leaving Ben-Hur committed to the cause of the KING WHO WAS TO COME.", "곧이어 갤리선의 밧줄이 풀리고, 배가 빙 돌아섰다. 그리고 횃불의 번쩍임과 즐거워하는 뱃사람들의 고함 속에서, 배는 바다를 향해 서둘러 떠나갔다. 벤허를 장차 오실 왕의 대의에 매어 둔 채로."]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# CHAPTER X"]], [["The day before the games, in the afternoon, all Ilderim’s racing property was taken to the city, and put in quarters adjoining the Circus.", "경기 전날 오후, 일데림의 경주용 가축은 모두 도시로 옮겨져 원형경기장에 딸린 마구간에 들었다."], ["Along with it the good man carried a great deal of property not of that class; so with servants, retainers mounted and armed, horses in leading, cattle driven, camels laden with baggage, his outgoing from the Orchard was not unlike a tribal migration.", "그 일행과 더불어 이 선량한 사람은 경주와는 무관한 재산도 잔뜩 거느리고 갔다. 하인과 종자들은 말을 타고 무장했으며, 끌려가는 말, 몰려가는 가축, 짐을 실은 낙타까지 어우러진 그의 과수원 행렬은 부족 전체가 이주하는 모습과 다르지 않았다."], ["The people along the road failed not to laugh at his motley procession; on the other side, it was observed that, with all his irascibility, he was not in the least offended by their rudeness.", "길가의 사람들은 그 잡다한 행렬을 보고 웃어 댔으나, 다른 한편으로는 그토록 성마른 그가 그들의 무례에 조금도 언짢아하지 않는다는 사실이 눈에 띄었다."], ["If he was under surveillance, as he had reason to believe, the informer would describe the semi-barbarous show with which he came up to the races.", "그가 짐작할 만한 이유가 있듯이 만일 감시를 받고 있다면, 밀고자는 그가 경주를 향해 올라온 그 반쯤 야만스러운 행렬을 보고할 것이다."], ["The Romans would laugh; the city would be amused; but what cared he?", "로마인들은 비웃을 테고, 도시는 즐거워할 테지만, 그가 무엇을 마음에 두랴?"], ["Next morning the pageant would be far on the road to the desert, and going with it would be every movable thing of value belonging to the Orchard—everything save such as were essential to the success of his four.", "이튿날 아침이면 그 행렬은 사막으로 가는 길을 멀리 나아가 있을 테고, 함께 가는 것은 과수원에 속한 값나가는 물건 일체—오로지 그의 네 마리 말의 승리에 꼭 필요한 것만 빼고 모조리—였다. 사실 그는 이미 귀향길에 오른 셈이었다."], ["He was, in fact, started home; his tents were all folded; the dowar was no more; in twelve hours all would be out of reach, pursue who might.", "천막은 모두 접혔고, 다와르는 더 이상 없었다. 열두 시간이 지나면 누가 쫓아오든 모든 것이 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있을 터였다."], ["A man is never safer than when he is under the laugh; and the shrewd old Arab knew it.", "사람은 비웃음을 받고 있을 때만큼 안전한 때가 없는 법이니, 이 약삭빠른 늙은 아랍인은 그것을 알고 있었다."]], [["Neither he nor Ben-Hur overestimated the influence of Messala; it was their opinion, however, that he would not begin active measures against them until after the meeting in the Circus; if defeated there, especially if defeated by Ben-Hur, they might instantly look for the worst he could do; he might not even wait for advices from Gratus.", "* 다와르: 천막들을 둥글게 둘러친 아랍인의 야영지. 그도 벤허도 메살라의 영향력을 과대평가하지는 않았다. 다만 두 사람의 생각은, 그가 원형경기장에서의 대결이 끝나기 전에는 그들을 상대로 본격적인 조치를 시작하지 않으리라는 것이었다."], ["With this view, they shaped their course, and were prepared to betake themselves out of harm’s way.", "그곳에서 패한다면, 특히 벤허에게 패한다면, 그가 할 수 있는 최악의 짓을 당장 각오해야 할 것이다. 그는 그라투스의 지시를 기다리지도 않을지 모른다. 이런 관점에서 그들은 행로를 짰고, 위험을 벗어날 채비를 갖추어 두었다."], ["They rode together now in good spirits, calmly confident of success on the morrow.", "이제 두 사람은 좋은 기분으로 나란히 말을 타고 가며, 내일의 승리를 차분히 확신했다."]], [["On the way, they came upon Malluch in waiting for them.", "가는 길에 그들은 자기들을 기다리고 있던 말루크와 마주쳤다."], ["The faithful fellow gave no sign by which it was possible to infer any knowledge on his part of the relationship so recently admitted between Ben-Hur and Simonides, or of the treaty between them and Ilderim.", "충직한 그는 벤허와 시모니데스 사이에 얼마 전 인정된 관계나, 그들과 일데림 사이의 협약을 알고 있다고 짐작할 만한 기색을 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그는 여느 때처럼 인사를 나누고는 종이 한 장을 꺼내며 족장에게 말했다."], ["He exchanged salutations as usual, and produced a paper, saying to the sheik, “I have here the notice of the editor of the games, just issued, in which you will find your horses published for the race. You will find in it also the order of exercises.", "\"방금 발표된 경기 주최자의 공고문을 가지고 왔습니다. 여기에 어르신의 말들이 경주에 출전하는 것으로 실려 있고, 경기 순서도 적혀 있습니다."], ["Without waiting, good sheik, I congratulate you upon your victory.”", "미리 기다릴 것도 없이, 어르신, 어르신의 승리를 축하드립니다.\""]], [["He gave the paper over, and, leaving the worthy to master it, turned to Ben-Hur.", "그는 종이를 건네주고, 그것을 찬찬히 살피도록 그 어른에게 맡긴 채 벤허에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“To you also, son of Arrius, my congratulations.", "\"아리우스의 아들이신 당신께도 축하드립니다."], ["There is nothing now to prevent your meeting Messala.", "이제 메살라와 맞붙는 것을 막을 것은 아무것도 없습니다."], ["Every condition preliminary to the race is complied with.", "경주에 앞선 모든 조건이 충족되었습니다."], ["I have the assurance from the editor himself.”", "주최자 본인에게서 직접 확약을 받았습니다.\""]], [["“I thank you, Malluch,”", "\"고맙네, 말루크.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["Malluch proceeded:", "말루크는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Your color is white, and Messala’s mixed scarlet and gold.", "\"당신의 색은 흰색이고, 메살라의 색은 진홍과 황금이 섞인 색입니다."], ["The good effects of the choice are visible already.", "그 선택의 효과는 벌써 눈에 보입니다."], ["Boys are now hawking white ribbons along the streets; tomorrow every Arab and Jew in the city will wear them.", "지금 아이들이 거리에서 흰 리본을 외치며 팔고 있고, 내일이면 도시의 아랍인과 유대인이 모두 그것을 두를 겁니다."], ["In the Circus you will see the white fairly divide the galleries with the red.”", "원형경기장에서는 흰색이 붉은색과 관람석을 엇비슷하게 나누어 차지하는 광경을 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“The galleries—but not the tribunal over the Porta Pompae.”", "\"관람석은 그렇겠지—하지만 폼파이 문 위의 귀빈석은 아닐 테지.\""]], [["“No; the scarlet and gold will rule there.", "\"네, 그곳은 진홍과 황금이 다스릴 겁니다."], ["But if we win”—Malluch chuckled with the pleasure of the thought—“if we win, how the dignitaries will tremble!", "하지만 우리가 이긴다면\"—말루크는 그 생각의 즐거움에 빙긋 웃었다—\"우리가 이긴다면, 그 고관들이 얼마나 떨겠습니까!"], ["They will bet, of course, according to their scorn of everything not Roman—two, three, five to one on Messala, because he is Roman.”", "그들은 물론 로마 아닌 모든 것을 멸시하는 만큼 판돈을 걸 겁니다—메살라가 로마인이라는 이유로 그에게 두 배, 세 배, 다섯 배씩 말입니다.\""], ["Dropping his voice yet lower, he added, “It ill becomes a Jew of good standing in the Temple to put his money at such a hazard; yet, in confidence, I will have a friend next behind the consul’s seat to accept offers of three to one, or five, or ten—the madness may go to such height.", "목소리를 한층 더 낮추며 그는 덧붙였다. \"성전에서 신망 높은 유대인이 그런 위험에 돈을 거는 것은 어울리지 않는 일이지요. 그래도 은밀히 말씀드리면, 집정관 자리 바로 뒤에 친구를 하나 두어 세 배, 다섯 배, 열 배의 제안을 받게 할 작정입니다—광기가 그렇게까지 치솟을지도 모릅니다."], ["I have put to his order six thousand shekels for the purpose.”", "그 목적으로 그에게 육천 세겔을 맡겨 두었습니다.\""]], [["“Nay, Malluch,”", "\"아닐세, 말루크.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, “a Roman will wager only in his Roman coin.", "벤허가 말했다. \"로마인은 오직 로마 화폐로만 내기를 걸 걸세."], ["Suppose you find your friend to-night, and place to his order sestertii in such amount as you choose.", "오늘 밤에 자네의 친구를 찾아가, 자네가 원하는 만큼의 세스테르티우스를 그에게 맡기게."], ["And look you, Malluch—let him be instructed to seek wagers with Messala and his supporters; Ilderim’s four against Messala’s.”", "그리고 잘 듣게, 말루크—그에게 메살라와 그 지지자들과 내기를 하도록 일러 두게. 일데림의 네 마리 말이 메살라의 네 마리 말에 맞서는 것으로 말일세.\""]], [["Malluch reflected a moment.", "말루크는 잠시 생각했다."]], [["“The effect will be to centre interest upon your contest.”", "\"그렇게 하면 당신의 대결에 관심이 집중되는 효과가 있겠군요.\""]], [["“The very thing I seek, Malluch.”", "\"내가 노리는 바로 그것일세, 말루크.\""]], [["“I see, I see.”", "\"알겠습니다, 알겠습니다.\""]], [["“Ay, Malluch; would you serve me perfectly, help me to fix the public eye upon our race—Messala’s and mine.”", "\"그래, 말루크. 자네가 나를 완벽히 섬길 생각이라면, 대중의 눈을 우리 경주에—메살라와 나의 경주에 못 박도록 도와주게.\""]], [["Malluch spoke quickly—“It can be done.”", "말루크가 재빨리 말했다. \"할 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“Then let it be done,”", "\"그러면 그렇게 하게.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["“Enormous wagers offered will answer; if the offers are accepted, all the better.”", "\"엄청난 판돈을 제안하면 효과가 있을 겁니다. 그 제안이 받아들여진다면 더욱 좋고요.\""]], [["Malluch turned his eyes watchfully upon Ben-Hur.", "말루크는 경계하는 눈빛으로 벤허를 바라보았다."]], [["“Shall I not have back the equivalent of his robbery?”", "\"그가 빼앗아 간 것만큼 되찾아야 하지 않겠나?\""], ["said Ben-Hur, partly to himself.", "벤허가 반쯤 혼잣말로 말했다."], ["“Another opportunity may not come.", "\"이런 기회가 다시 오지 않을지도 모르네."], ["And if I could break him in fortune as well as in pride!", "그의 자존심뿐 아니라 재산까지 무너뜨릴 수 있다면!"], ["Our father Jacob could take no offence.”", "우리 조상 야곱도 노여워하지 않으실 걸세.\""]], [["A look of determined will knit his handsome face, giving emphasis to his further speech.", "굳은 의지의 빛이 그의 잘생긴 얼굴에 어리며, 뒤이은 말에 무게를 실었다."]], [["“Yes, it shall be.", "\"그래, 그렇게 하겠네."], ["Hark, Malluch!", "잘 듣게, 말루크!"], ["Stop not in thy offer of sestertii.", "세스테르티우스로 제안하는 데서 그치지 말게."], ["Advance them to talents, if any there be who dare so high.", "감히 그렇게 높이 거는 자가 있거든 탈렌트로 올리게."], ["Five, ten, twenty talents; ay, fifty, so the wager be with Messala himself.”", "다섯, 열, 스무 탈렌트, 아니 쉰까지라도—메살라 본인과의 내기라면 말일세.\""]], [["“It is a mighty sum,”", "\"엄청난 금액입니다.\""], ["said Malluch.", "말루크가 말했다."], ["“I must have security.”", "\"담보가 있어야 합니다.\""]], [["“So thou shalt.", "\"그리될 걸세."], ["Go to Simonides, and tell him I wish the matter arranged.", "시모니데스에게 가서 내가 이 일을 처리해 주기를 바란다고 전하게."], ["Tell him my heart is set on the ruin of my enemy, and that the opportunity hath such excellent promise that I choose such hazards.", "내 마음이 적의 파멸에 쏠려 있으며, 이번 기회가 더없이 좋은 전망을 품고 있어 이런 위험을 택한다고 전하게."], ["On our side be the God of our fathers.", "우리 편에는 우리 조상의 하나님이 계시네."], ["Go, good Malluch.", "가게, 충직한 말루크."], ["Let this not slip.”", "이 일을 놓치지 말게.\""]], [["And Malluch, greatly delighted, gave him parting salutation, and started to ride away, but returned presently.", "크게 기뻐한 말루크는 작별 인사를 하고 말을 몰아 떠나려다가 이내 되돌아왔다."]], [["“Your pardon,”", "\"송구합니다.\""], ["he said to Ben-Hur.", "그가 벤허에게 말했다."], ["“There was another matter.", "\"한 가지 더 있었습니다."], ["I could not get near Messala’s chariot myself, but I had another measure it; and, from his report, its hub stands quite a palm higher from the ground than yours.”", "저로서는 메살라의 전차에 가까이 갈 수 없었으나, 다른 사람을 시켜 그것을 재 보게 했습니다. 그 보고에 따르면, 그 전차의 바퀴통이 당신 것보다 땅에서 한 뼘쯤 더 높이 서 있다고 합니다.\""]], [["“A palm!", "\"한 뼘이라고?"], ["So much?”", "그렇게나?\""], ["cried Ben-Hur, joyfully.", "벤허가 기뻐하며 외쳤다."]], [["Then he leaned over to Malluch.", "그러더니 그는 말루크에게로 몸을 기울였다."]], [["“As thou art a son of Judah, Malluch, and faithful to thy kin, get thee a seat in the gallery over the Gate of Triumph, down close to the balcony in front of the pillars, and watch well when we make the turns there; watch well, for if I have favor at all, I will— Nay, Malluch, let it go unsaid!", "\"자네가 유다의 아들이고 동족에게 충실하다면, 말루크, 개선문 위 관람석에서 기둥 앞 발코니에 바싹 가까운 자리를 잡고, 우리가 거기서 회전할 때 잘 지켜보게. 잘 지켜보게. 내게 조금이라도 운이 따른다면, 나는— 아닐세, 말루크, 말하지 않은 채로 두겠네!"], ["Only get thee there, and watch well.”", "그저 거기 자리를 잡고, 잘 지켜보게.\""]], [["At that moment a cry burst from Ilderim.", "그 순간 일데림의 입에서 외침이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Ha!", "\"하!"], ["By the splendor of God!", "하나님의 영광에 맹세코!"], ["what is this?”", "이게 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["He drew near Ben-Hur with a finger pointing on the face of the notice.", "그는 공고문의 한 대목을 손가락으로 가리키며 벤허에게 다가갔다. \"읽어 보게.\" 벤허가 말했다."]], [["“Read,” said Ben-Hur. “No; better thou.”", "\"아니, 자네가 읽는 편이 낫겠네.\""]], [["Ben-Hur took the paper, which, signed by the prefect of the province as editor, performed the office of a modern programme, giving particularly the several divertisements provided for the occasion.", "벤허는 종이를 받아 들었다. 그것은 주최자인 속주 장관의 서명이 담긴, 오늘날의 식순과 같은 구실을 하는 문서로, 이번 행사를 위해 마련된 여러 여흥을 자세히 일러 주고 있었다."], ["It informed the public that there would be first a procession of extraordinary splendor; that the procession would be succeeded by the customary honors to the god Consus, whereupon the games would begin; running, leaping, wrestling, boxing, each in the order stated.", "거기에는 먼저 더없이 화려한 행진이 있을 것이며, 그 행진에 뒤이어 신 콘수스에게 바치는 관례의 의식이 거행되고, 그런 다음 경기가 시작된다고 적혀 있었다. 달리기, 뛰기, 씨름, 권투가 적힌 순서대로 이어진다고 했다."], ["The names of the competitors were given, with their several nationalities and schools of training, the trials in which they had been engaged, the prizes won, and the prizes now offered; under the latter head the sums of money were stated in illuminated letters, telling of the departure of the day when the simple chaplet of pine or laurel was fully enough for the victor, hungering for glory as something better than riches, and content with it.", "출전자들의 이름이 저마다의 국적과 훈련받은 학교, 그들이 참가했던 시합, 거머쥔 상, 그리고 이번에 걸린 상과 더불어 실려 있었다. 마지막 항목에는 상금의 액수가 화려한 글자로 적혀 있어, 영광을 부보다 나은 것으로 갈망하며 그것에 만족했던 승자에게 소박한 솔잎 관이나 월계관만으로 족했던 시절이 이미 지나갔음을 일러 주었다."]], [["Over these parts of the programme Ben-Hur sped with rapid eyes.", "이런 부분들을 벤허는 빠른 눈길로 훑어 내려갔다."], ["At last he came to the announcement of the race.", "마침내 그는 경주에 관한 발표에 이르렀다."], ["He read it slowly.", "그것은 천천히 읽었다."], ["Attending lovers of the heroic sports were assured they would certainly be gratified by an Orestean struggle unparalleled in Antioch.", "영웅적 경기를 사랑하여 모여든 이들은, 안티오크에서 일찍이 없던 오레스테스 같은 격돌에 반드시 흡족하리라는 약속을 받았다."], ["The city offered the spectacle in honor of the consul.", "도시가 집정관을 기리어 이 볼거리를 마련한 것이었다."], ["One hundred thousand sestertii and a crown of laurel were the prizes.", "십만 세스테르티우스와 월계관이 상으로 걸렸다."], ["Then followed the particulars.", "이어서 세부 사항이 따랐다."], ["The entries were six in all—fours only permitted; and, to further interest in the performance, the competitors would be turned into the course together.", "출전은 모두 여섯으로, 네 마리 전차만 허락되었다. 그리고 경기의 흥미를 더하기 위해, 경쟁자들은 한꺼번에 출발선에 세워질 것이었다."], ["Each four then received description.", "그런 다음 네 마리 말 각각에 대한 설명이 이어졌다."]], [["“I.", "\"제1번."], ["A four of Lysippus the Corinthian—two grays, a bay, and a black; entered at Alexandria last year, and again at Corinth, where they were winners.", "코린토스 사람 리시포스의 사두마차—회색 둘, 적갈색 하나, 검은색 하나. 지난해 알렉산드리아에서 출전했고, 코린토스에서 다시 출전하여 우승했다."], ["Lysippus, driver.", "마부는 리시포스."], ["Color, yellow.", "색은 노란색."]], [["“II.", "\"제2번."], ["A four of Messala of Rome—two white, two black; victors of the Circensian as exhibited in the Circus Maximus last year.", "로마 사람 메살라의 사두마차—흰색 둘, 검은색 둘. 지난해 마시무스 원형경기장에서 펼쳐진 키르켄세스의 우승마."], ["Messala, driver.", "마부는 메살라."], ["Colors, scarlet and gold.", "색은 진홍과 황금."]], [["“III.", "\"제3번."], ["A four of Cleanthes the Athenian—three gray, one bay; winners at the Isthmian last year.", "아테네 사람 클레안테스의 사두마차—회색 셋, 적갈색 하나. 지난해 이스트미아에서 우승했다."], ["Cleanthes, driver.", "마부는 클레안테스."], ["Color, green.", "색은 초록색."]], [["“IV.", "\"제4번."], ["A four of Dicaeus the Byzantine—two black, one gray, one bay; winners this year at Byzantium.", "비잔티움 사람 디카이오스의 사두마차—검은색 둘, 회색 하나, 적갈색 하나. 올해 비잔티움에서 우승했다."], ["Dicaeus, driver.", "마부는 디카이오스."], ["Color, black.", "색은 검은색."]], [["“V. A four of Admetus the Sidonian—all grays.", "\"제5번. 시돈 사람 아드메토스의 사두마차—모두 회색."], ["Thrice entered at Cæsarea, and thrice victors.", "카이사레아에서 세 차례 출전하여 세 번 모두 우승했다."], ["Admetus, driver.", "마부는 아드메토스."], ["Color, blue.", "색은 파란색."]], [["“VI. A four of Ilderim, sheik of the Desert.", "\"제6번. 사막의 족장 일데림의 사두마차."], ["All bays; first race.", "모두 적갈색. 첫 경주."], ["Ben-Hur, a Jew, driver.", "마부는 유대인 벤허. 색은 흰색.\""], ["Color, white.”", "마부는 유대인 벤허!"]], [["Why that name instead of Arrius?", "어찌하여 아리우스 대신 그 이름인가?"]], [["Ben-Hur raised his eyes to Ilderim.", "벤허는 눈을 들어 일데림을 바라보았다."], ["He had found the cause of the Arab’s outcry.", "그는 그 아랍인이 외친 까닭을 알아차렸다."], ["Both rushed to the same conclusion.", "두 사람은 똑같은 결론으로 치달았다."]], [["The hand was the hand of Messala!", "그 손은 메살라의 손이었다!"]], [["# CHAPTER XI", "# CHAPTER XI"]], [["Evening was hardly come upon Antioch, when the Omphalus, nearly in the centre of the city, became a troubled fountain from which in every direction, but chiefly down to the Nymphaeum and east and west along the Colonnade of Herod, flowed currents of people, for the time given up to Bacchus and Apollo.", "저녁이 안티오크에 막 내려앉자, 도시의 거의 한복판에 있는 옴팔로스는 사방으로 흘러넘치는 어수선한 샘이 되었다. 그 물줄기는 무엇보다 님파이움 쪽으로, 또 동서로 헤롯의 주랑(柱廊)을 따라 사람들의 흐름이 되어 쏟아졌으니, 그 시각 사람들은 바쿠스와 아폴로에게 몸을 내맡기고 있었다."]], [["For such indulgence anything more fitting cannot be imagined than the great roofed streets, which were literally miles on miles of porticos wrought of marble, polished to the last degree of finish, and all gifts to the voluptuous city by princes careless of expenditure where, as in this instance, they thought they were eternizing themselves.", "그런 방종에 이보다 더 어울리는 곳은 상상하기 어려우니, 지붕을 인 그 거대한 거리는 말 그대로 몇 킬로미터에 걸쳐 이어지는 대리석 주랑이었고, 마지막 한 점까지 닦아 광을 낸 그것은 모두 씀씀이에 개의치 않는 군주들이 음락(淫樂)의 도시에 바친 선물이었다. 이 경우처럼, 그들은 그렇게 함으로써 제 이름을 영원히 새긴다고 믿었던 것이다."], ["Darkness was not permitted anywhere; and the singing, the laughter, the shouting, were incessant, and in compound like the roar of waters dashing through hollow grots, confused by a multitude of echoes.", "어디에도 어둠은 허락되지 않았다. 노랫소리와 웃음소리와 외침이 끊이지 않았고, 한데 뒤섞여 텅 빈 동굴을 때리며 흐르는 물의 포효처럼, 수많은 메아리가 어지러이 얽혀들었다."]], [["The many nationalities represented, though they might have amazed a stranger, were not peculiar to Antioch.", "거기에 모인 온갖 민족은 낯선 이방인을 놀라게 했을지언정, 안티오크에만 있는 풍경은 아니었다."], ["Of the various missions of the great empire, one seems to have been the fusion of men and the introduction of strangers to each other; accordingly, whole peoples rose up and went at pleasure, taking with them their costumes, customs, speech, and gods; and where they chose, they stopped, engaged in business, built houses, erected altars, and were what they had been at home.", "이 거대한 제국이 떠맡은 여러 사명 가운데 하나는 사람들을 뒤섞고 낯선 이들을 서로에게 소개하는 것이었던 듯하다. 그리하여 온 민족이 일어나 제 마음 가는 대로 옮겨 다녔고, 제 의복과 풍습과 언어와 신들을 함께 가지고 다녔다. 그들은 마음에 드는 곳에서 멈추어, 생업에 종사하고, 집을 짓고, 제단을 세웠으니, 고향에서 살던 모습 그대로였다."]], [["There was a peculiarity, however, which could not have failed the notice of a looker-on this night in Antioch.", "그러나 그날 밤 안티오크에서 구경꾼이라면 누구도 놓칠 수 없는 한 가지 특이한 점이 있었다."], ["Nearly everybody wore the colors of one or other of the charioteers announced for the morrow’s race.", "거의 모든 사람이 이튿날 경주에 나설 어느 한 전차 기수의 색깔을 몸에 두르고 있었던 것이다."], ["Sometimes it was in form of a scarf, sometimes a badge; often a ribbon or a feather.", "어떤 이는 목도리로, 어떤 이는 휘장으로, 또 흔히는 리본이나 깃털로 그것을 드러냈다."], ["Whatever the form, it signified merely the wearer’s partiality; thus, green published a friend of Cleanthes the Athenian, and black an adherent of the Byzantine.", "형태가 무엇이든 그것은 단지 두른 자의 편애를 뜻할 뿐이었다. 초록은 아테네 사람 클레안테스의 벗임을 알렸고, 검정은 비잔티움 사람의 지지자임을 알렸다."], ["This was according to a custom, old probably as the day of the race of Orestes—a custom, by the way, worthy of study as a marvel of history, illustrative of the absurd yet appalling extremities to which men frequently suffer their follies to drag them.", "이는 아마도 오레스테스가 경주를 벌이던 시절만큼이나 오래된 관습에 따른 것이었다. 그것은 역사의 경이로서 연구할 만한 관습이니, 사람들이 제 어리석음에 끌려 얼마나 우스꽝스럽고도 끔찍한 극단에까지 자주 빠져드는지를 잘 보여 준다."]], [["The observer abroad on this occasion, once attracted to the wearing of colors, would have very shortly decided that there were three in predominance—green, white, and the mixed scarlet and gold.", "그날 밖으로 나선 관찰자라면, 한번 그 색깔에 마음이 끌린 순간 곧바로 세 가지 색깔이 우세하다는 것을 알아챘을 것이다. 초록과 흰색, 그리고 진홍과 황금이 뒤섞인 색이었다."]], [["But let us from the streets to the palace on the island.", "그러나 이제 거리를 떠나 섬에 있는 궁전으로 가 보자."]], [["The five great chandeliers in the saloon are freshly lighted.", "홀의 거대한 샹들리에 다섯 개에 갓 불이 밝혀졌다."], ["The assemblage is much the same as that already noticed in connection with the place.", "모인 사람들은 앞서 이 장소와 관련하여 살펴본 바로 그 무리와 크게 다르지 않았다."], ["The divan has its corps of sleepers and burden of garments, and the tables yet resound with the rattle and clash of dice.", "긴 의자에는 잠든 자들과 벗어 놓은 옷가지가 그득했고, 탁자에서는 여전히 주사위가 달그락거리고 부딪치는 소리가 울렸다."], ["Yet the greater part of the company are not doing anything.", "그러나 무리의 대부분은 아무것도 하지 않고 있었다."], ["They walk about, or yawn tremendously, or pause as they pass each other to exchange idle nothings.", "그들은 이리저리 거닐거나, 입이 찢어지게 하품하거나, 서로 스쳐 지나가다 멈추어 시답잖은 말을 주고받았다. 내일 날씨는 좋을까? 경기 준비는 다 되었을까?"], ["Will the weather be fair to-morrow?", "안티오크 원형 경기장의 규칙은 로마의 그것과 다를까?"], ["Are the preparations for the games complete?", "사실 이 젊은이들은 권태에 시달리고 있었다."], ["Do the laws of the Circus in Antioch differ from the laws of the Circus in Rome? Truth is, the young fellows are suffering from ennui.", "힘든 일은 이미 끝나 있었다. 다시 말해, 그들의 서판을 들여다볼 수 있다면 거기에 내기 메모가 빼곡히 적혀 있는 것을 보았으리라."], ["Their heavy work is done; that is, we would find their tablets, could we look at them, covered with memoranda of wagers—wagers on every contest; on the running, the wrestling, the boxing; on everything but the chariot-race.", "모든 시합에 건 내기였다. 달리기와 씨름과 권투에—전차 경주만 빼고 모든 것에 건 내기였다. 그런데 어찌하여 전차 경주에는 걸지 않았는가?"]], [["Good reader, they cannot find anybody who will hazard so much as a denarius with them against Messala.", "선량한 독자여, 그들은 메살라를 상대로 단 1데나리우스라도 걸려는 사람을 단 한 명도 찾을 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["There are no colors in the saloon but his.", "홀 안에는 그의 색깔 말고는 다른 색깔이 없었다."]], [["No one thinks of his defeat.", "누구도 그의 패배를 생각하지 않았다."]], [["Why, they say, is he not perfect in his training?", "그들은 말했다. 그가 어찌 완벽하게 훈련되지 않았겠는가?"], ["Did he not graduate from an imperial lanista?", "그가 황제의 검투사 양성소를 졸업하지 않았던가?"], ["Were not his horses winners at the Circensian in the Circus Maximus?", "그의 말들이 막시무스 원형 경기장의 키르켄세스 경주에서 우승하지 않았던가?"], ["And then—ah, yes!", "게다가—아, 그렇다!"], ["he is a Roman!", "그는 로마인이 아닌가!"]], [["In a corner, at ease on the divan, Messala himself may be seen.", "긴 의자 한 귀퉁이에 편히 기대앉은 메살라 자신의 모습이 보였다."], ["Around him, sitting or standing, are his courtierly admirers, plying him with questions.", "그를 둘러싸고 앉거나 선 채로, 아첨하는 숭배자들이 그에게 질문을 퍼붓고 있었다."], ["There is, of course, but one topic.", "화제는 물론 단 하나뿐이었다."]], [["Enter Drusus and Cecilius.", "드루수스와 케킬리우스가 들어왔다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["cries the young prince, throwing himself on the divan at Messala’s feet, “Ah, by Bacchus, I am tired!”", "젊은 귀공자는 메살라의 발치에 있는 긴 의자에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. \"아, 바쿠스에게 맹세코, 지쳤다!\""]], [["“Whither away?”", "\"어디를 다녀왔나?\""], ["asks Messala.", "메살라가 물었다."]], [["“Up the street; up to the Omphalus, and beyond—who shall say how far?", "\"거리를 따라 올라갔다가, 옴팔로스까지, 그 너머까지—얼마나 멀리 갔는지 누가 알겠나?"], ["Rivers of people; never so many in the city before.", "사람의 강물이었다. 일찍이 이 도시에서 그렇게 많은 사람을 본 적이 없다."], ["They say we will see the whole world at the Circus to-morrow.”", "내일 원형 경기장에서 온 세상을 다 보게 될 거라더군.\""]], [["Messala laughed scornfully.", "메살라는 비웃듯 껄껄 웃었다."]], [["“The idiots! Perpol!", "\"멍청이들! 페르폴(맙소사)!"], ["They never beheld a Circensian with Cæsar for editor.", "그들은 카이사르가 주최하는 키르켄세스를 본 적이 없는 거야."], ["But, my Drusus, what found you?”", "그런데 드루수스, 뭘 좀 보았나?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도.\""]], [["“O—ah!", "\"오—아!"], ["You forget,”", "자네 잊었군.\""], ["said Cecilius.", "케킬리우스가 말했다."]], [["“What?”", "\"무엇을?\""], ["asked Drusus.", "드루수스가 물었다."]], [["“The procession of whites.”", "\"흰색의 행렬 말일세.\""]], [["“Mirabile!” cried Drusus, half rising.", "\"미라빌레(놀라운 일이지)!\" 드루수스가 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다."], ["“We met a faction of whites, and they had a banner.", "\"우리는 흰색 패거리를 만났는데, 그들에게 깃발이 있었지."], ["But—ha, ha, ha!”", "그런데—하, 하, 하!\""]], [["He fell back indolently.", "그는 나른하게 다시 몸을 뉘었다."]], [["“Cruel Drusus—not to go on,”", "\"잔인하군, 드루수스, 말을 끝까지 안 하다니.\""], ["said Messala.", "메살라가 말했다."]], [["“Scum of the desert were they, my Messala, and garbage-eaters from the Jacob’s Temple in Jerusalem.", "\"사막의 쓰레기들이었네, 메살라. 예루살렘 야곱의 성전에서 온 음식 찌꺼기나 먹는 자들이지."], ["What had I to do with them!”", "내가 그자들과 무슨 상관이 있겠나!\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Cecilius, “Drusus is afraid of a laugh, but I am not, my Messala.”", "케킬리우스가 말했다. \"드루수스는 웃음거리가 될까 두려워하지만, 나는 그렇지 않네, 메살라.\""]], [["“Speak thou, then.”", "\"그럼 자네가 말해 보게.\""]], [["“Well, we stopped the faction, and—”", "\"좋아, 우리는 그 패거리를 멈춰 세우고는—\""]], [["“Offered them a wager,”", "\"내기를 걸자고 했지.\""], ["said Drusus, relenting, and taking the word from the shadow’s mouth.", "드루수스가 마음이 누그러져, 그림자 같은 친구의 입에서 말을 가로채며 끼어들었다."], ["“And—ha, ha, ha!—one fellow with not enough skin on his face to make a worm for a carp stepped forth, and—ha, ha, ha!—said yes.", "\"그랬더니—하, 하, 하!—잉어 미끼로 쓸 지렁이 한 마리 만들 가죽도 얼굴에 없는 녀석 하나가 나서더니—하, 하, 하!—그러겠다고 하는 거야."], ["I drew my tablets.", "내가 서판을 꺼냈지."], ["‘Who is your man?’ I asked. ‘Ben-Hur, the Jew,’ said he.", "'네 선수가 누구냐?' 하고 물었더니, '유대인 벤허'라더군."], ["Then I: ‘What shall it be?", "그래서 내가 '얼마를 걸겠느냐? 액수는?'"], ["How much?’ He answered, ‘A—a—’ Excuse me, Messala.", "하고 묻자, 그가 '한—한—' 미안하네, 메살라."], ["By Jove’s thunder, I cannot go on for laughter!", "유피테르의 천둥에 맹세코, 웃음 때문에 더는 못 잇겠어!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!”", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["The listeners leaned forward.", "듣는 이들이 몸을 앞으로 기울였다."]], [["Messala looked to Cecilius.", "메살라가 케킬리우스를 보았다."]], [["“A shekel,”", "\"세겔 한 닢.\""], ["said the latter.", "케킬리우스가 말했다."]], [["“A shekel!", "\"세겔 한 닢!"], ["A shekel!”", "세겔 한 닢이라니!\""]], [["A burst of scornful laughter ran fast upon the repetition.", "그 말이 되풀이되자마자 비웃는 웃음이 와르르 터져 나왔다."]], [["“And what did Drusus?”", "\"그래서 드루수스는 어떻게 했나?\""], ["asked Messala.", "메살라가 물었다."]], [["An outcry over about the door just then occasioned a rush to that quarter; and, as the noise there continued, and grew louder, even Cecilius betook himself off, pausing only to say, “The noble Drusus, my Messala, put up his tablets and—lost the shekel.”", "바로 그때 문 쪽에서 떠들썩한 소리가 일어나, 사람들이 그쪽으로 우르르 몰려갔다. 그곳의 소음이 계속되며 점점 더 커지자, 케킬리우스마저 자리를 떴는데, 떠나면서 이 말만 남겼다. \"고결한 드루수스는, 메살라, 서판을 도로 넣고는—세겔 한 닢을 잃었네.\""]], [["“A white!", "\"흰색이다!"], ["A white!”", "흰색이야!\""]], [["“Let him come!”", "\"이리 오라고 해!\""]], [["“This way, this way!”", "\"이쪽이오, 이쪽!\""]], [["These and like exclamations filled the saloon, to the stoppage of other speech.", "이런 외침들이 홀을 가득 채워 다른 말은 끊기고 말았다."], ["The dice-players quit their games; the sleepers awoke, rubbed their eyes, drew their tablets, and hurried to the common centre.", "주사위 놀이꾼들은 게임을 멈추었고, 잠자던 자들은 깨어나 눈을 비비고 서판을 꺼내 들고는 사람들이 모인 한복판으로 서둘러 달려갔다."]], [["“I offer you—”", "\"나는 당신에게 제안하노니—\""]], [["“And I—”", "\"나는—\""]], [["“I—” The person so warmly received was the respectable Jew, Ben-Hur’s fellow-voyager from Cyprus.", "그토록 열렬한 환영을 받은 사람은 다름 아닌, 키프로스에서 벤허와 같은 배를 탔던 점잖은 유대인이었다."], ["He entered grave, quiet, observant.", "그는 진지하고 조용하며 주의 깊은 태도로 들어섰다."], ["His robe was spotlessly white; so was the cloth of his turban.", "그의 겉옷은 티 한 점 없이 희었고, 터번의 천 또한 그러했다."], ["Bowing and smiling at the welcome, he moved slowly towards the central table.", "환대에 고개를 숙이고 미소 지으며, 그는 천천히 가운데 탁자로 다가갔다."], ["Arrived there, he drew his robe about him in a stately manner, took seat, and waved his hand.", "그곳에 이르자 그는 위엄 있는 몸짓으로 겉옷을 여미고 자리에 앉아 손을 흔들었다."], ["The gleam of a jewel on a finger helped him not a little to the silence which ensued.", "손가락에서 반짝이는 보석 하나가, 뒤이어 찾아온 침묵을 끌어내는 데 적잖이 한몫했다."]], [["“Romans—most noble Romans—I salute you!”", "\"로마인 여러분—더없이 고귀한 로마인 여러분, 인사드립니다!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Easy, by Jupiter!", "\"이런, 유피테르에게 맹세코!"], ["Who is he?”", "저자는 누구인가?\""], ["asked Drusus.", "드루수스가 물었다."]], [["“A dog of Israel—Sanballat by name—purveyor for the army; residence, Rome; vastly rich; grown so as a contractor of furnishments which he never furnishes.", "\"이스라엘의 개일세—이름은 산발랏이라 하지. 군대 납품업자고, 거주지는 로마, 엄청난 부자라네. 결코 납품하지도 않는 군수품 계약자로 그렇게 부자가 되었지."], ["He spins mischiefs, nevertheless, finer than spiders spin their webs.", "그래도 거미가 거미줄을 치는 것보다 더 정교하게 못된 술수를 꾸미는 자야."], ["Come—by the girdle of Venus!", "자—베누스의 허리띠에 맹세코!"], ["let us catch him!”", "저자를 한번 사로잡아 보세!\""]], [["Messala arose as he spoke, and, with Drusus, joined the mass crowded about the purveyor.", "메살라는 이렇게 말하며 일어나, 드루수스와 함께 납품업자를 둘러싼 무리 속으로 끼어들었다."]], [["“It came to me on the street,”", "\"거리에서 들은 이야기인데,\""], ["said that person, producing his tablets, and opening them on the table with an impressive air of business, “that there was great discomfort in the palace because offers on Messala were going without takers.", "그 사람은 서판을 꺼내어 인상적일 만큼 사무적인 태도로 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓으며 말했다. \"메살라에게 건 제안에 응하는 사람이 없어서 궁전 안이 무척 불편한 분위기라더군요."], ["The gods, you know, must have sacrifices; and here am I.", "아시다시피 신들은 제물을 받으셔야 하는 법, 그래서 제가 여기 있는 겁니다."], ["You see my color; let us to the matter.", "제 색깔이 보이시지요. 자, 본론으로 들어갑시다."], ["Odds first, amounts next.", "먼저 배당률, 그다음에 액수입니다."], ["What will you give me?”", "제게 얼마를 거시겠습니까?\""]], [["The audacity seemed to stun his hearers.", "그 대담함에 듣는 이들은 어안이 벙벙해진 듯했다."]], [["“Haste!” he said.", "\"서두르시오!\""], ["“I have an engagement with the consul.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 집정관과 약속이 있소.\""]], [["The spur was effective.", "그 채찍질이 효과가 있었다."]], [["“Two to one,”", "\"이 대 일!\""], ["cried half a dozen in a voice.", "대여섯 명이 한목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["exclaimed the purveyor, astonished. “Only two to one, and yours a Roman!”", "납품업자가 놀라 소리쳤다. \"겨우 이 대 일이라니, 그것도 여러분 쪽이 로마인인데!\""]], [["“Take three, then.”", "\"그럼 삼 대 일로 하지.\""]], [["“Three say you—only three—and mine but a dog of a Jew!", "\"삼이라고 하셨소—겨우 삼이라니—게다가 내 쪽은 유대인 개에 불과한데!"], ["Give me four.”", "사 대 일을 주시오.\""]], [["“Four it is,”", "\"사 대 일이다.\""], ["said a boy, stung by the taunt.", "그 비웃음에 발끈한 소년 하나가 말했다."]], [["“Five—give me five,” cried the purveyor, instantly.", "\"오—오 대 일을 주시오.\" 납품업자가 즉시 외쳤다."]], [["A profound stillness fell upon the assemblage.", "깊은 정적이 모인 사람들 위에 내려앉았다."]], [["“The consul—your master and mine—is waiting for me.”", "\"집정관께서—당신들과 나의 주인이신 그분이—나를 기다리고 계시오.\""]], [["The inaction became awkward to the many.", "여럿이 머뭇거리는 모양새가 어색해지기 시작했다."]], [["“Give me five—for the honor of Rome, five.”", "\"오 대 일을 주시오—로마의 명예를 위해, 오 대 일을.\""]], [["“Five let it be,”", "\"오 대 일로 하지.\""], ["said one in answer.", "한 사람이 대답하며 말했다."]], [["There was a sharp cheer—a commotion—and Messala himself appeared.", "날카로운 환호가 터지고—소동이 일더니—메살라 자신이 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“Five let it be,”", "\"오 대 일로 하지.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["And Sanballat smiled, and made ready to write.", "그러자 산발랏은 미소 지으며 적을 채비를 했다."]], [["“If Cæsar die to-morrow,”", "\"내일 카이사르께서 돌아가신다 해도,\""], ["he said, “Rome will not be all bereft.", "그가 말했다. \"로마가 텅 비지는 않겠군요."], ["There is at least one other with spirit to take his place. Give me six.”", "적어도 그분의 자리를 메울 기개를 지닌 분이 또 하나는 있으니까요. 육 대 일을 주시오.\""]], [["“Six be it,”", "\"육 대 일이다.\""], ["answered Messala.", "메살라가 대답했다."]], [["There was another shout louder than the first.", "처음보다 더 큰 함성이 또 한 번 일었다."]], [["“Six be it,”", "\"육 대 일이다.\""], ["repeated Messala.", "메살라가 되풀이했다."], ["“Six to one—the difference between a Roman and a Jew.", "\"육 대 일—로마인과 유대인의 차이가 그만큼이지."], ["And, having found it, now, O redemptor of the flesh of swine, let us on.", "자, 이제 그것을 찾았으니, 오, 돼지고기를 속량하는 자여, 어서 진행하자."], ["The amount—and quickly. The consul may send for thee, and I will then be bereft.”", "액수다—어서. 집정관께서 너를 부르러 사람을 보낼지도 모르니, 그러면 나야말로 텅 비고 말 테니까.\""]], [["Sanballat took the laugh against him coolly, and wrote, and offered the writing to Messala.", "산발랏은 자신을 겨냥한 그 웃음을 태연히 받아넘기고는, 적은 다음 그 글을 메살라에게 내밀었다."]], [["“Read, read!” everybody demanded.", "\"읽어라, 읽어!\" 모두가 요구했다."]], [["And Messala read:", "그리하여 메살라가 읽었다."]], [["“Mem.—Chariot-race.", "\"각서—전차 경주."], ["Messala of Rome, in wager with Sanballat, also of Rome, says he will beat Ben-Hur, the Jew.", "로마의 메살라는, 역시 로마의 산발랏과 내기를 하여, 유대인 벤허를 이기겠다고 말한다."], ["Amount of wager, twenty talents. Odds to Sanballat, six to one.", "내기 액수, 이십 탈렌트. 산발랏에게 주는 배당률, 육 대 일."]], [["“Witnesses: SANBALLAT.”", "증인: 산발랏.\""]], [["There was no noise, no motion.", "아무 소리도, 아무 움직임도 없었다."], ["Each person seemed held in the pose the reading found him.", "저마다 글을 다 읽었을 때의 자세 그대로 굳어 버린 듯했다."], ["Messala stared at the memorandum, while the eyes which had him in view opened wide, and stared at him.", "메살라는 그 각서를 뚫어지게 보았고, 그를 지켜보던 눈들은 휘둥그레져서 그를 응시했다."], ["He felt the gaze, and thought rapidly.", "그는 그 시선을 느끼며 빠르게 머리를 굴렸다."], ["So lately he stood in the same place, and in the same way hectored the countrymen around him.", "바로 얼마 전에 그는 같은 자리에 서서, 같은 방식으로 주위의 동향인들을 윽박질렀던 것이다."], ["They would remember it.", "그들은 그것을 기억할 것이다."], ["If he refused to sign, his hero-ship was lost.", "서명을 거부한다면 그의 영웅 자리는 사라진다."], ["And sign he could not; he was not worth one hundred talents, nor the fifth part of the sum.", "그렇다고 서명할 수도 없었다. 그는 백 탈렌트는커녕 그 오분의 일도 가진 사람이 아니었다."], ["Suddenly his mind became a blank; he stood speechless; the color fled his face.", "갑자기 그의 머릿속이 텅 비었다. 그는 말없이 서 있었고, 얼굴에서 핏기가 가셨다."], ["An idea at last came to his relief.", "마침내 한 가지 생각이 떠올라 그를 구원했다."]], [["“Thou Jew!”", "\"유대인 너!\""], ["he said, “where hast thou twenty talents?", "그가 말했다. \"이십 탈렌트가 어디에 있느냐?"], ["Show me.”", "내게 보여라.\""]], [["Sanballat’s provoking smile deepened.", "산발랏의 약 올리는 미소가 더 깊어졌다."]], [["“There,”", "\"여기 있소.\""], ["he replied, offering Messala a paper.", "그가 메살라에게 종이 한 장을 내밀며 대답했다."]], [["“Read, read!”", "\"읽어라, 읽어!\""], ["arose all around.", "사방에서 소리가 일었다."]], [["Again Messala read:", "메살라가 다시 읽었다."]], [["“AT ANTIOCH, Tammuz 16th day.", "\"안티오크에서, 담무스 16일."]], [["“The bearer, Sanballat of Rome, hath now to his order with me fifty talents, coin of Cæsar.", "이 증서를 지닌 자, 로마의 산발랏은 지금 나에게 카이사르의 화폐로 오십 탈렌트를 청구할 권리가 있다."]], [["“Fifty talents, fifty talents!”", "시모니데스.\" \"오십 탈렌트, 오십 탈렌트라니!\""], ["echoed the throng, in amazement.", "무리가 경악하여 따라 외쳤다."]], [["Then Drusus came to the rescue.", "그때 드루수스가 구원에 나섰다."]], [["“By Hercules!”", "\"헤르쿨레스에게 맹세코!\""], ["he shouted, “the paper lies, and the Jew is a liar.", "그가 소리쳤다. \"그 종이는 거짓말이고, 저 유대인은 거짓말쟁이다."], ["Who but Cæsar hath fifty talents at order?", "카이사르 말고 누가 오십 탈렌트를 청구할 권리가 있단 말인가?"], ["Down with the insolent white!”", "저 건방진 흰색을 끌어내려라!\""]], [["The cry was angry, and it was angrily repeated; yet Sanballat kept his seat, and his smile grew more exasperating the longer he waited.", "그 외침은 분노에 차 있었고, 분노에 차서 되풀이되었다. 그러나 산발랏은 자리를 지켰고, 오래 기다릴수록 그의 미소는 더욱 사람의 속을 긁었다."], ["At length Messala spoke.", "마침내 메살라가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"조용히!"], ["One to one, my countrymen—one to one, for love of our ancient Roman name.”", "일 대 일이다, 동향인들이여—일 대 일로, 우리 유서 깊은 로마라는 이름을 위하여.\""]], [["The timely action recovered him his ascendancy.", "그 시의적절한 처사가 그에게 우위를 되찾아 주었다."]], [["“O thou circumcised dog!”", "\"오, 할례받은 개여!\""], ["he continued, to Sanballat, “I gave thee six to one, did I not?”", "그가 산발랏을 향해 말을 이었다. \"내가 너에게 육 대 일을 주었지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“Yes,” said the Jew, quietly.", "\"그렇소.\" 유대인이 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Well, give me now the fixing of the amount.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 이제 액수를 정하는 것은 나에게 맡겨라.\""]], [["“With reserve, if the amount be trifling, have thy will,”", "\"액수가 보잘것없는 경우에 한해서라면, 마음대로 하시오.\""], ["answered Sanballat.", "산발랏이 대답했다."]], [["“Write, then, five in place of twenty.”", "\"그럼 적어라, 이십 대신 오 탈렌트라고.\""]], [["“Hast thou so much?”", "\"그만한 돈은 있소?\""]], [["“By the mother of the gods, I will show you receipts.”", "\"신들의 어머니께 맹세코, 영수증을 보여 주마.\""]], [["“Nay, the word of so brave a Roman must pass.", "\"아니오, 그토록 용감한 로마인의 말이라면 통하는 법이지요."], ["Only make the sum even—six make it, and I will write.”", "다만 액수를 떨어지게만 하시오—육으로 하면 적겠소.\""]], [["“Write it so.”", "\"그렇게 적어라.\""]], [["And forthwith they exchanged writings.", "그리하여 그들은 곧바로 글을 교환했다."]], [["Sanballat immediately arose and looked around him, a sneer in place of his smile.", "산발랏은 즉시 일어나 주위를 둘러보았는데, 미소 대신 냉소를 띠고 있었다."], ["No man better than he knew those with whom he was dealing.", "자신이 상대하는 자들을 그보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다."]], [["“Romans,” he said, “another wager, if you dare!", "\"로마인 여러분,\" 그가 말했다."], ["Five talents against five talents that the white will win.", "\"또 하나 내기를 하실까요, 만약 용기가 있다면! 흰색이 이긴다는 데 오 탈렌트 대 오 탈렌트로."], ["I challenge you collectively.”", "여러분 모두에게 도전합니다.\""]], [["They were again surprised.", "그들은 또다시 놀랐다."]], [["“What!” he cried, louder.", "\"뭐라고요!\" 그가 더 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Shall it be said in the Circus to-morrow that a dog of Israel went into the saloon of the palace full of Roman nobles—among them the scion of a Cæsar—and laid five talents before them in challenge, and they had not the courage to take it up?”", "\"이스라엘의 개 한 마리가 로마 귀족들로 가득 찬 궁전 홀에 들어와—그 가운데에는 카이사르의 후손도 있는데—그들 앞에 오 탈렌트를 내놓으며 도전했건만, 그들에게는 그것을 받아들일 용기조차 없더라고, 내일 원형 경기장에서 이런 말이 떠돌게 해도 좋단 말입니까?\""]], [["The sting was unendurable.", "그 가시는 견딜 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["“Have done, O insolent!”", "\"그만하라, 오, 건방진 자여!\""], ["said Drusus, “write the challenge, and leave it on the table; and to-morrow, if we find thou hast indeed so much money to put at such hopeless hazard, I, Drusus, promise it shall be taken.”", "드루수스가 말했다. \"그 도전을 적어 탁자 위에 두고 가라. 그러면 내일, 네가 정말로 그토록 가망 없는 모험에 걸 만한 돈을 가졌음이 확인된다면, 나 드루수스가 그것을 받아들이겠다고 약속하마.\""]], [["Sanballat wrote again, and, rising, said, unmoved as ever, “See, Drusus, I leave the offer with you.", "산발랏은 다시 글을 적고는, 일어서며 여전히 흔들림 없이 말했다. \"보시오, 드루수스, 이 제안을 당신에게 남겨 두겠소."], ["When it is signed, send it to me any time before the race begins.", "서명이 끝나거든, 경주가 시작되기 전 언제든 내게 보내시오."], ["I will be found with the consul in a seat over the Porta Pompae.", "나는 포르타 폼파이 위쪽 좌석에서 집정관과 함께 있을 것이오."], ["Peace to you; peace to all.”", "평안하시기를. 모두 평안하시기를.\""]], [["He bowed, and departed, careless of the shout of derision with which they pursued him out of the door.", "그는 고개를 숙이고 떠났는데, 사람들이 문밖까지 그를 쫓으며 퍼붓는 조롱의 함성에는 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["In the night the story of the prodigious wager flew along the streets and over the city; and Ben-Hur, lying with his four, was told of it, and also that Messala’s whole fortune was on the hazard.", "밤사이 그 엄청난 내기 이야기는 거리를 따라 도시 전역으로 퍼져 나갔다. 자신의 네 마리 말과 함께 누워 있던 벤허도 그 소식을 전해 들었고, 메살라의 전 재산이 그 모험에 걸려 있다는 것도 알게 되었다."]], [["And he slept never so soundly.", "그리고 그는 그 어느 때보다 깊이 잠들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII", "# CHAPTER XII"]], [["The Circus at Antioch stood on the south bank of the river, nearly opposite the island, differing in no respect from the plan of such buildings in general.", "안티오크의 원형경기장은 강 남쪽 기슭, 섬과 거의 마주 보는 자리에 서 있었으니, 그 설계는 이런 종류의 건물이 으레 따르는 양식과 조금도 다르지 않았다."]], [["In the purest sense, the games were a gift to the public; consequently, everybody was free to attend; and, vast as the holding capacity of the structure was, so fearful were the people, on this occasion, lest there should not be room for them, that, early the day before the opening of the exhibition, they took up all the vacant spaces in the vicinity, where their temporary shelter suggested an army in waiting.", "가장 순수한 의미에서 이 경기는 대중에게 베푸는 선물이었다. 따라서 누구나 자유로이 구경할 수 있었다. 건물이 받아들일 수 있는 규모가 그토록 방대했음에도, 이번만큼은 사람들이 자기 자리가 없을까 두려워한 나머지, 흥행이 열리기 하루 전 이른 시각부터 그 일대의 빈터를 죄다 차지해 버렸다. 그곳에 임시로 친 거처들은 마치 출진을 기다리는 군대를 떠올리게 했다."]], [["At midnight the entrances were thrown wide, and the rabble, surging in, occupied the quarters assigned to them, from which nothing less than an earthquake or an army with spears could have dislodged them.", "자정이 되자 입구가 활짝 열렸고, 밀려든 군중은 자기들에게 배정된 구역을 차지했으니, 지진이나 창을 든 군대가 아니고서는 그들을 그곳에서 몰아낼 수 없었다."], ["They dozed the night away on the benches, and breakfasted there; and there the close of the exercises found them, patient and sight-hungry as in the beginning.", "그들은 의자에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸며 밤을 새우고 거기서 아침을 먹었다. 행사가 끝날 때까지도 그들은 그 자리에 그대로 있었으니, 처음과 다름없이 끈기 있고 구경에 굶주린 모습이었다."]], [["The better people, their seats secured, began moving towards the Circus about the first hour of the morning, the noble and very rich among them distinguished by litters and retinues of liveried servants.", "신분이 높은 사람들은 자리를 확보해 둔 터라, 아침 첫 시각쯤에야 경기장을 향해 움직이기 시작했다. 그 가운데 귀족과 부호 들은 가마와 제복을 갖춰 입은 종자들의 행렬로 한눈에 구별되었다."]], [["By the second hour, the efflux from the city was a stream unbroken and innumerable.", "둘째 시각이 되자, 도시에서 흘러나오는 인파는 끊이지 않고 헤아릴 수 없는 물줄기를 이루었다."]], [["Exactly as the gnomon of the official dial up in the citadel pointed the second hour half gone, the legion, in full panoply, and with all its standards on exhibit, descended from Mount Sulpius; and when the rear of the last cohort disappeared in the bridge, Antioch was literally abandoned—not that the Circus could hold the multitude, but that the multitude was gone out to it, nevertheless.", "성채 위에 세워진 공식 해시계의 바늘이 둘째 시각의 절반이 지났음을 가리키는 바로 그 순간, 군단이 완전 무장을 하고 군기를 모두 내건 채 술피우스산에서 내려왔다. 마지막 보병대의 후미가 다리 위로 사라지자, 안티오크는 그야말로 텅 비고 말았다. 경기장이 그 많은 무리를 다 담을 수 있어서가 아니라, 그래도 그 무리가 모조리 경기장으로 빠져나갔기 때문이다."]], [["A great concourse on the river shore witnessed the consul come over from the island in a barge of state.", "강기슭에 모인 큰 무리가, 집정관이 성대한 거룻배를 타고 섬에서 건너오는 광경을 지켜보았다."], ["As the great man landed, and was received by the legion, the martial show for one brief moment transcended the attraction of the Circus.", "그 위인이 뭍에 올라 군단의 영접을 받자, 그 군사적 위용은 잠시나마 경기장의 매력마저 능가했다."]], [["At the third hour, the audience, if such it may be termed, was assembled; at last, a flourish of trumpets called for silence, and instantly the gaze of over a hundred thousand persons was directed towards a pile forming the eastern section of the building.", "셋째 시각이 되자 관중이라 부를 만한 사람들이 모두 모였다. 마침내 나팔 소리가 한 차례 울려 정숙을 명하니, 그 즉시 십만이 넘는 사람들의 시선이 건물 동쪽 구역을 이루는 한 건축물로 쏠렸다."]], [["There was a basement first, broken in the middle by a broad arched passage, called the Porta Pompae, over which, on an elevated tribunal magnificently decorated with insignia and legionary standards, the consul sat in the place of honor.", "그 아래쪽에는 먼저 기단이 있었는데, 가운데가 폭넓은 아치형 통로로 갈라져 있었고 그것을 포르타 폼파이라 불렀다. 그 위로는 휘장과 군단 깃발로 호화롭게 꾸민 높직한 단상이 있어, 집정관이 영예로운 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["On both sides of the passage the basement was divided into stalls termed carceres, each protected in front by massive gates swung to statuesque pilasters.", "통로 양쪽으로는 기단이 칸칸이 나뉘어 카르케레스라 불리는 마구간을 이루었는데, 칸마다 앞쪽이 조각상 같은 벽기둥에 걸린 육중한 문으로 막혀 있었다."], ["Over the stalls next was a cornice crowned by a low balustrade; back of which the seats arose in theatre arrangement, all occupied by a throng of dignitaries superbly attired.", "그 마구간 위로는 처마 돌림띠가 있고, 그 위에 낮은 난간이 얹혀 있었다. 난간 뒤로는 극장처럼 자리가 층층이 올라가 있었으니, 그 모두를 더없이 화려하게 차려입은 고관들의 무리가 차지하고 있었다."], ["The pile extended the width of the Circus, and was flanked on both sides by towers which, besides helping the architects give grace to their work, served the velaria, or purple awnings, stretched between them so as to throw the whole quarter in a shade that became exceedingly grateful as the day advanced.", "이 건축물은 경기장의 너비만큼 뻗어 있었고, 양옆에는 탑이 솟아 있었다. 그 탑들은 설계자가 작품에 우아함을 더하는 데 보탬이 되었을 뿐 아니라, 그 사이에 펼쳐 친 벨라리아, 곧 자줏빛 차일을 떠받치는 구실도 했다. 그리하여 그 일대를 온통 그늘로 덮으니, 날이 깊어 갈수록 그 그늘이 더없이 고맙게 여겨졌다."]], [["This structure, it is now thought, can be made useful in helping the reader to a sufficient understanding of the arrangement of the rest of the interior of the Circus.", "이 건축물을 설명해 두면, 경기장 안쪽의 나머지 배치를 독자가 충분히 이해하는 데 보탬이 될 것이다."], ["He has only to fancy himself seated on the tribunal with the consul, facing to the west, where everything is under his eye.", "독자는 다만 자신이 집정관과 함께 단상에 앉아 서쪽을 바라보고 있다고 상상하기만 하면 된다. 그러면 모든 것이 그의 눈 아래에 펼쳐진다."]], [["On the right and left, if he will look, he will see the main entrances, very ample, and guarded by gates hinged to the towers.", "좌우를 둘러보면, 매우 넓은 정문들이 보이는데, 그 문들은 탑에 경첩으로 달린 문짝으로 지켜지고 있다."]], [["Directly below him is the arena—a level plane of considerable extent, covered with fine white sand.", "그의 바로 아래에는 경기장이 있다—곱고 흰 모래로 덮인, 제법 넓은 평지다."], ["There all the trials will take place except the running.", "달리기를 제외한 모든 경기가 그곳에서 치러질 것이다."]], [["Looking across this sanded arena westwardly still, there is a pedestal of marble supporting three low conical pillars of gray stone, much carven.", "모래 깔린 이 경기장 너머로 여전히 서쪽을 바라보면, 대리석 받침대 하나가 있어 정교하게 새겨진 잿빛 돌 원뿔 기둥 세 개를 떠받치고 있다."], ["Many an eye will hunt for those pillars before the day is done, for they are the first goal, and mark the beginning and end of the race-course.", "날이 저물기 전에 수많은 눈이 그 기둥을 찾아 헤맬 것이니, 그것이 바로 첫 번째 반환점이요, 경주로의 시작이자 끝을 표시하기 때문이다."], ["Behind the pedestal, leaving a passage-way and space for an altar, commences a wall ten or twelve feet in breadth and five or six in height, extending thence exactly two hundred yards, or one Olympic stadium.", "받침대 뒤로는 통로와 제단을 놓을 공간을 남겨 두고, 폭이 3, 4미터에 높이가 1.5미터에서 2미터쯤 되는 벽이 시작되어, 거기서부터 정확히 약 180미터, 곧 올림픽 경기장 하나 길이만큼 뻗어 있다."], ["At the farther, or westward, extremity of the wall there is another pedestal, surmounted with pillars which mark the second goal.", "그 벽의 저쪽 끝, 곧 서쪽 끝에는 또 하나의 받침대가 있어, 두 번째 반환점을 표시하는 기둥들을 떠받치고 있다."]], [["The racers will enter the course on the right of the first goal, and keep the wall all the time to their left.", "경주마들은 첫 번째 반환점 오른쪽으로 들어와, 내내 그 벽을 왼편에 두고 달릴 것이다."], ["The beginning and ending points of the contest lie, consequently, directly in front of the consul across the arena; and for that reason his seat was admittedly the most desirable in the Circus.", "따라서 경기의 시작점과 끝점은 경기장을 사이에 두고 집정관 정면에 놓여 있다. 그런 까닭에 그의 자리가 경기장에서 가장 바람직한 자리로 인정받았던 것이다."]], [["Now if the reader, who is still supposed to be seated on the consular tribunal over the Porta Pompae, will look up from the ground arrangement of the interior, the first point to attract his notice will be the marking of the outer boundary-line of the course—that is, a plain-faced, solid wall, fifteen or twenty feet in height, with a balustrade on its cope, like that over the carceres, or stalls, in the east.", "이제 여전히 포르타 폼파이 위 집정관 단상에 앉아 있다고 여겨지는 독자가 안쪽의 바닥 배치에서 눈을 들어 올린다면, 가장 먼저 눈에 들어오는 것은 경주로 바깥 경계선의 표시일 것이다—곧 표면이 밋밋하고 단단한 벽으로, 높이가 4, 5미터쯤 되며, 그 마루에는 동쪽 카르케레스, 곧 마구간 위에 있는 것과 같은 난간이 둘려 있다."], ["This balcony, if followed round the course, will be found broken in three places to allow passages of exit and entrance, two in the north and one in the west; the latter very ornate, and called the Gate of Triumph, because, when all is over, the victors will pass out that way, crowned, and with triumphal escort and ceremonies.", "이 발코니는 경주로를 따라 빙 돌아가면 세 군데가 끊겨 있어 드나드는 통로 구실을 하는데, 둘은 북쪽에, 하나는 서쪽에 있다. 서쪽 것은 매우 화려하게 꾸며져 있어 개선문이라 불린다. 모든 것이 끝나면 승자들이 월계관을 쓰고 개선 행렬과 의식을 거느린 채 그곳으로 나가기 때문이다."]], [["At the west end the balcony encloses the course in the form of a half circle, and is made to uphold two great galleries.", "서쪽 끝에서 발코니는 반원 모양으로 경주로를 에워싸며, 두 개의 커다란 관람석을 떠받치도록 만들어졌다."]], [["Directly behind the balustrade on the coping of the balcony is the first seat, from which ascend the succeeding benches, each higher than the one in front of it; giving to view a spectacle of surpassing interest—the spectacle of a vast space ruddy and glistening with human faces, and rich with varicolored costumes.", "발코니 마루의 난간 바로 뒤에 첫 번째 자리가 있고, 거기서부터 이어지는 의자들이 층층이 올라가는데, 저마다 앞자리보다 한 단씩 높다. 그리하여 더없이 흥미로운 광경이 한눈에 펼쳐진다—드넓은 공간이 사람의 얼굴들로 발갛게 빛나고, 갖가지 빛깔의 의상으로 화려하게 물든 광경이다."]], [["The commonalty occupy quarters over in the west, beginning at the point of termination of an awning, stretched, it would seem, for the accommodation of the better classes exclusively.", "평민들은 서쪽 구역을 차지하는데, 차일이 끝나는 지점에서부터 시작된다. 그 차일은 오로지 상류층을 위해서만 펼쳐 친 듯했다."]], [["Having thus the whole interior of the Circus under view at the moment of the sounding of the trumpets, let the reader next imagine the multitude seated and sunk to sudden silence, and motionless in its intensity of interest.", "이렇게 나팔이 울리는 순간 경기장 안쪽 전체를 한눈에 두었으니, 이제 독자는 그 무리가 자리에 앉아 별안간 침묵에 잠긴 채, 흥미에 사로잡혀 미동도 없는 모습을 떠올려 보라."]], [["Out of the Porta Pompae over in the east rises a sound mixed of voices and instruments harmonized.", "동쪽 포르타 폼파이에서 사람의 목소리와 악기 소리가 어우러진 가락이 솟아오른다. 이윽고 축전을 여는 행렬의 합창대가 모습을 드러낸다."], ["Presently, forth issues the chorus of the procession with which the celebration begins; the editor and civic authorities of the city, givers of the games, follow in robes and garlands; then the gods, some on platforms borne by men, others in great four-wheel carriages gorgeously decorated; next them, again, the contestants of the day, each in costume exactly as he will run, wrestle, leap, box, or drive.", "경기를 베푸는 자인 흥행 주최자와 도시의 관헌들이 예복에 화관을 쓰고 그 뒤를 따른다. 다음으로는 신들이 나오는데, 더러는 사람이 짊어진 단 위에, 더러는 호화롭게 장식한 큼직한 네 바퀴 수레에 실려 있다. 그다음으로 다시, 그날의 출전자들이 저마다 달리거나 씨름하거나 뛰거나 권투하거나 전차를 몰 때 그대로의 차림으로 나온다."]], [["Slowly crossing the arena, the procession proceeds to make circuit of the course.", "행렬은 경기장을 천천히 가로질러, 경주로를 한 바퀴 도는 데로 나아간다."], ["The display is beautiful and imposing.", "그 행렬은 아름답고도 위풍당당하다."], ["Approval runs before it in a shout, as the water rises and swells in front of a boat in motion.", "마치 움직이는 배 앞에서 물이 솟아 부풀어 오르듯, 함성이 그 앞을 앞질러 달려간다."], ["If the dumb, figured gods make no sign of appreciation of the welcome, the editor and his associates are not so backward.", "형상으로 빚어진 말 없는 신들이야 그 환영에 고마워하는 기색을 보이지 않더라도, 흥행 주최자와 그 동료들은 그처럼 무덤덤하지만은 않다."]], [["The reception of the athletes is even more demonstrative, for there is not a man in the assemblage who has not something in wager upon them, though but a mite or farthing.", "선수들에 대한 환대는 한층 더 요란하다. 그도 그럴 것이, 비록 한 푼이나 반 푼일망정, 그들에게 무언가를 걸지 않은 사람이 모인 무리 가운데 하나도 없기 때문이다."], ["And it is noticeable, as the classes move by, that the favorites among them are speedily singled out: either their names are loudest in the uproar, or they are more profusely showered with wreaths and garlands tossed to them from the balcony.", "그리고 각 부류가 지나갈 때면, 그 가운데 인기 있는 자들이 금세 가려진다는 점이 눈에 띈다. 함성 속에서 그들의 이름이 가장 크게 울리거나, 발코니에서 던지는 화환과 화관이 그들에게 더욱 풍성하게 쏟아진다."]], [["If there is a question as to the popularity with the public of the several games, it is now put to rest.", "여러 경기 가운데 어느 것이 대중에게 인기 있느냐는 물음이 있었다면, 이제 그 답이 났다."], ["To the splendor of the chariots and the superexcellent beauty of the horses, the charioteers add the personality necessary to perfect the charm of their display.", "전차의 찬란함과 말들의 빼어난 아름다움에 더하여, 전차몰이꾼들은 그 볼거리의 매력을 완성하는 데 필요한 개성을 보탠다."], ["Their tunics, short, sleeveless, and of the finest woollen texture, are of the assigned colors.", "그들의 튜닉은 짧고 소매가 없으며 가장 고운 모직으로 짠 것인데, 저마다 배정받은 빛깔로 되어 있다."], ["A horseman accompanies each one of them except Ben-Hur, who, for some reason—possibly distrust—has chosen to go alone; so, too, they are all helmeted but him.", "벤허를 제외한 저마다에게는 기수 한 사람씩이 딸려 있었으니, 벤허는 무슨 까닭에선지—어쩌면 의심 때문에—혼자 가기를 택했다. 마찬가지로 그를 빼고는 모두 투구를 쓰고 있었다."], ["As they approach, the spectators stand upon the benches, and there is a sensible deepening of the clamor, in which a sharp listener may detect the shrill piping of women and children; at the same time, the things roseate flying from the balcony thicken into a storm, and, striking the men, drop into the chariot-beds, which are threatened with filling to the tops.", "그들이 다가오자 구경꾼들은 의자 위에 올라섰고, 함성은 눈에 띄게 깊어졌다. 귀가 밝은 자라면 그 속에서 여인과 아이 들의 새된 휘파람 같은 외침을 가려낼 수 있었다. 동시에 발코니에서 날아오는 장밋빛 물건들이 폭풍처럼 짙어져, 사내들을 때리고는 전차 칸 안으로 떨어졌으니, 칸은 곧 가득 채워질 듯했다."], ["Even the horses have a share in the ovation; nor may it be said they are less conscious than their masters of the honors they receive.", "말들조차 그 갈채에 한몫을 받았다. 그 말들이 자신들이 받는 영예를 주인보다 못하게 느낀다고는 말할 수 없었다."]], [["Very soon, as with the other contestants, it is made apparent that some of the drivers are more in favor than others; and then the discovery follows that nearly every individual on the benches, women and children as well as men, wears a color, most frequently a ribbon upon the breast or in the hair: now it is green, now yellow, now blue; but, searching the great body carefully, it is manifest that there is a preponderance of white, and scarlet and gold.", "이내, 다른 출전자들과 마찬가지로, 전차몰이꾼 가운데 더러는 다른 자들보다 더 인기 있다는 사실이 드러났다. 그러자 의자에 앉은 거의 모든 사람이, 여인과 아이도 사내와 마찬가지로, 빛깔 하나씩을 걸치고 있다는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 흔히는 가슴이나 머리에 단 리본이었다. 어떤 것은 초록, 어떤 것은 노랑, 어떤 것은 파랑이었다. 그러나 그 큰 무리를 꼼꼼히 살펴보면, 흰빛과 진홍과 금빛이 우세하다는 것이 분명했다."]], [["In a modern assemblage called together as this one is, particularly where there are sums at hazard upon the race, a preference would be decided by the qualities or performance of the horses; here, however, nationality was the rule.", "이처럼 모여든 현대의 집회였다면, 더구나 경주에 돈이 걸린 자리였다면, 누구를 편드느냐는 말의 됨됨이나 그 솜씨로 정해졌을 것이다. 그러나 이곳에서는 민족이 그 기준이었다."], ["If the Byzantine and Sidonian found small support, it was because their cities were scarcely represented on the benches.", "비잔티움 사람과 시돈 사람이 거의 지지를 받지 못한 것은, 그들의 도시가 의자에 변변히 대표되지 않았기 때문이다."], ["On their side, the Greeks, though very numerous, were divided between the Corinthian and the Athenian, leaving but a scant showing of green and yellow.", "한편 그리스인은 수가 매우 많았으나 코린토스 편과 아테네 편으로 갈라져, 초록과 노랑은 빈약하게 보일 뿐이었다."], ["Messala’s scarlet and gold would have been but little better had not the citizens of Antioch, proverbially a race of courtiers, joined the Romans by adopting the color of their favorite.", "메살라의 진홍과 금빛도, 으레 아첨꾼의 족속이라 일컬어지는 안티오크 시민들이 자기들이 좋아하는 자의 빛깔을 받아들여 로마인 편에 가담하지 않았더라면, 그보다 별로 나을 것이 없었을 것이다."], ["There were left then the country people, or Syrians, the Jews, and the Arabs; and they, from faith in the blood of the sheik’s four, blent largely with hate of the Romans, whom they desired, above all things, to see beaten and humbled, mounted the white, making the most noisy, and probably the most numerous, faction of all.", "그러고 나면 시골 사람들, 곧 시리아인과 유대인과 아랍인이 남았다. 그들은 족장의 네 마리 말의 혈통에 대한 믿음에, 무엇보다도 로마인이 패하고 굴욕당하는 꼴을 보고 싶어 하는 미움이 크게 뒤섞여, 흰빛을 택했다. 그리하여 가장 떠들썩하고, 아마도 가장 수가 많은 무리를 이루었다."]], [["As the charioteers move on in the circuit, the excitement increases; at the second goal, where, especially in the galleries, the white is the ruling color, the people exhaust their flowers and rive the air with screams.", "전차몰이꾼들이 한 바퀴를 돌며 나아가자, 흥분은 더욱 높아졌다. 특히 관람석에서 흰빛이 으뜸가는 빛깔인 두 번째 반환점에 이르자, 사람들은 꽃을 다 쏟아붓고 비명으로 허공을 갈랐다."]], [["“Messala! Messala!”", "\"메살라! 메살라!\""]], [["“Ben-Hur! Ben-Hur!”", "\"벤허! 벤허!\""]], [["Such are the cries.", "이런 외침들이었다."]], [["Upon the passage of the procession, the factionists take their seats and resume conversation.", "행렬이 지나가자 편을 가른 자들은 자리에 앉아 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Ah, by Bacchus! was he not handsome?”", "\"아, 바쿠스 신께 맹세코! 그이가 잘생기지 않았어?\""], ["exclaims a woman, whose Romanism is betrayed by the colors flying in her hair.", "머리에 휘날리는 빛깔로 로마 편임이 드러나는 한 여인이 소리쳤다."]], [["“And how splendid his chariot!”", "\"게다가 그이 전차가 얼마나 멋진지!\""], ["replies a neighbor, of the same proclivities.", "같은 성향을 지닌 옆 사람이 대꾸했다."], ["“It is all ivory and gold. Jupiter grant he wins!”", "\"온통 상아와 황금이잖아. 유피테르께서 그이를 이기게 해 주시기를!\""]], [["The notes on the bench behind them were entirely different.", "그들 뒤쪽 의자에서 흘러나오는 가락은 전혀 달랐다."]], [["“A hundred shekels on the Jew!”", "\"유대인에게 백 세겔!\""]], [["The voice is high and shrill.", "높고 새된 목소리였다."]], [["“Nay, be thou not rash,” whispers a moderating friend to the speaker.", "\"아니, 그렇게 경솔하지 말게.\" 함께 말리는 친구가 그자에게 속삭였다."], ["“The children of Jacob are not much given to Gentile sports, which are too often accursed in the sight of the Lord.”", "\"야곱의 자손은 이방인의 경기에 그다지 끼어들지 않는 법이지. 주님 보시기에 너무도 자주 저주받은 것이니 말일세.\""]], [["“True, but saw you ever one more cool and assured?", "\"맞는 말일세. 하지만 저보다 더 침착하고 자신만만한 자를 본 적이 있나?"], ["And what an arm he has!”", "게다가 저 팔뚝 좀 보게!\""]], [["“And what horses!”", "\"그리고 저 말들은 또 어떻고!\""], ["says a third.", "세 번째 사람이 말했다."]], [["“And for that,”", "\"게다가 말일세,\""], ["a fourth one adds, “they say he has all the tricks of the Romans.”", "네 번째 사람이 덧붙였다. \"사람들 말로는 저자가 로마인들의 온갖 솜씨를 죄다 익혔다더군.\""]], [["A woman completes the eulogium:", "한 여인이 그 칭송을 마무리지었다."]], [["“Yes, and he is even handsomer than the Roman.”", "\"그래요, 게다가 저이는 그 로마인보다도 잘생겼다고요.\""]], [["Thus encouraged, the enthusiast shrieks again, “A hundred shekels on the Jew!”", "이렇게 부추김을 받자, 그 열렬한 자는 다시 비명을 질렀다. \"유대인에게 백 세겔!\""]], [["“Thou fool!” answers an Antiochian, from a bench well forward on the balcony.", "\"이런 바보 같으니!\" 발코니 앞쪽 의자에 앉은 한 안티오크 사람이 대꾸했다."], ["“Knowest thou not there are fifty talents laid against him, six to one, on Messala?", "\"메살라에게 일 대 육으로 오십 탈란트가 걸린 것을 모른단 말인가? 자네 세겔이나 거두어들이게."], ["Put up thy shekels, lest Abraham rise and smite thee.”", "아브라함이 일어나 자네를 치기 전에 말일세.\""]], [["“Ha, ha!", "\"하, 하!"], ["thou ass of Antioch!", "이 안티오크의 멍청이야!"], ["Cease thy bray.", "그 울음소리 좀 그치게."], ["Knowest thou not it was Messala betting on himself?”", "그게 메살라가 제 자신에게 건 돈이라는 걸 모른단 말인가?\""]], [["Such the reply.", "이런 대꾸였다."]], [["And so ran the controversy, not always good-natured.", "이렇게 논쟁은 이어졌으니, 늘 좋게만 흘러가지는 않았다."]], [["When at length the march was ended and the Porta Pompae received back the procession, Ben-Hur knew he had his prayer.", "마침내 행진이 끝나고 포르타 폼파이가 행렬을 도로 받아들였을 때, 벤허는 자신의 기도가 이루어졌음을 알았다."]], [["The eyes of the East were upon his contest with Messala.", "동방의 눈길이 메살라와 겨루는 그의 대결에 쏠려 있었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII", "# CHAPTER XIII"]], [["About three o’clock, speaking in modern style, the program was concluded except the chariot-race.", "현대식으로 말하자면 오후 세 시쯤, 전차 경주를 제외한 순서는 모두 끝났다."], ["The editor, wisely considerate of the comfort of the people, chose that time for a recess.", "흥행 책임자는 관중의 편안함을 슬기롭게 헤아려 이때를 휴식 시간으로 잡았다."], ["At once the vomitoria were thrown open, and all who could hastened to the portico outside where the restaurateurs had their quarters.", "곧바로 출입구가 활짝 열렸고, 나갈 수 있는 사람은 모두 음식 장수들이 자리 잡은 바깥 회랑으로 서둘러 나갔다."], ["Those who remained yawned, talked, gossiped, consulted their tablets, and, all distinctions else forgotten, merged into but two classes—the winners, who were happy, and the losers, who were grum and captious.", "남은 사람들은 하품을 하고, 이야기를 나누고, 잡담을 떨고, 자기 서판을 들여다보았으니, 다른 모든 구별은 잊은 채 오직 두 부류로 나뉘었다—만족스러운 승자와, 뚱하고 까다로운 패자로."]], [["Now, however, a third class of spectators, composed of citizens who desired only to witness the chariot-race, availed themselves of the recess to come in and take their reserved seats; by so doing they thought to attract the least attention and give the least offence.", "그런데 이제 제삼의 관중, 곧 오직 전차 경주만을 보고 싶어 하는 시민들이 이 휴식 시간을 틈타 들어와 예약된 자리를 차지했다. 그렇게 함으로써 그들은 가장 적은 눈길을 끌고 가장 적은 거슬림을 주리라 여겼다."], ["Among these were Simonides and his party, whose places were in the vicinity of the main entrance on the north side, opposite the consul.", "그 가운데 시모니데스와 그 일행이 있었으니, 그들의 자리는 집정관 맞은편, 북쪽 정문 가까이였다."]], [["As the four stout servants carried the merchant in his chair up the aisle, curiosity was much excited.", "건장한 하인 넷이 의자에 앉은 상인을 들고 통로를 올라가자, 호기심이 크게 일었다."], ["Presently some one called his name.", "이윽고 누군가 그의 이름을 불렀다."], ["Those about caught it and passed it on along the benches to the west; and there was hurried climbing on seats to get sight of the man about whom common report had coined and put in circulation a romance so mixed of good fortune and bad that the like had never been known or heard of before.", "둘레에 있던 사람들이 그 소리를 받아 서쪽 의자들을 따라 옮겨 전했다. 사람들은 자리 위로 황급히 기어올라, 일찍이 들어 본 적도 본 적도 없을 만큼 행운과 불행이 뒤섞인 풍문이 만들어져 떠돌게 한 그 사내를 보려 했다."]], [["Ilderim was also recognized and warmly greeted; but nobody knew Balthasar or the two women who followed him closely veiled.", "일데림 또한 알아보고 따뜻한 인사를 받았다. 그러나 발타사르나, 그를 바싹 따라가며 너울로 얼굴을 가린 두 여인은 아무도 몰라보았다."]], [["The people made way for the party respectfully, and the ushers seated them in easy speaking distance of each other down by the balustrade overlooking the arena.", "사람들은 정중히 일행에게 길을 내주었고, 안내인들은 경기장을 내려다보는 난간 곁에, 서로 편히 말을 주고받을 만한 거리에 자리를 마련해 주었다."], ["In providence of comfort, they sat upon cushions and had stools for footrests.", "편안함을 헤아려 그들은 방석 위에 앉았고 발받침으로 걸상을 두었다."]], [["The women were Iras and Esther.", "두 여인은 이라스와 에스더였다."]], [["Upon being seated, the latter cast a frightened look over the Circus, and drew the veil closer about her face; while the Egyptian, letting her veil fall upon her shoulders, gave herself to view, and gazed at the scene with the seeming unconsciousness of being stared at, which, in a woman, is usually the result of long social habitude.", "자리에 앉자 에스더는 경기장을 한 차례 겁먹은 눈길로 둘러보고, 너울을 얼굴에 더 바싹 끌어당겼다. 한편 이집트 여인은 너울을 어깨로 떨어뜨려 제 모습을 드러내고는, 남들이 자기를 쳐다본다는 것을 의식하지 못하는 듯한 태도로 광경을 바라보았으니, 여인에게 그런 태도란 대개 오랜 사교 생활에서 몸에 밴 것이었다."]], [["The new-comers generally were yet making their first examination of the great spectacle, beginning with the consul and his attendants, when some workmen ran in and commenced to stretch a chalked rope across the arena from balcony to balcony in front of the pillars of the first goal.", "새로 들어온 사람들은 대체로 아직 그 거대한 구경거리를 처음 살펴보는 중이었으니, 집정관과 그 시종들부터 보기 시작하던 차에, 일꾼 몇이 뛰어들어 와 첫 번째 반환점 기둥 앞을 가로질러 발코니에서 발코니까지 분필 칠한 줄을 치기 시작했다."]], [["About the same time, also, six men came in through the Porta Pompae and took post, one in front of each occupied stall; whereat there was a prolonged hum of voices in every quarter.", "거의 같은 무렵, 여섯 사내가 폼파이 문을 통해 들어와, 사용 중인 칸막이 자리 앞마다 하나씩 늘어섰다. 그러자 사방에서 길게 끄는 목소리의 웅성거림이 일었다."]], [["“See, see!", "\"봐, 봐!"], ["The green goes to number four on the right; the Athenian is there.”", "초록이 오른쪽 네 번째로 가는군. 아테네 사람이 저기 있어.\""]], [["“And Messala—yes, he is in number two.”", "\"그리고 메살라—그래, 그는 두 번째에 있어.\""]], [["“The Corinthian—”", "\"코린토스 사람은—\""]], [["“Watch the white!", "\"흰색을 봐!"], ["See, he crosses over, he stops; number one it is—number one on the left.”", "봐라, 그가 건너간다, 멈춘다. 첫 번째다—왼쪽 첫 번째야.\""]], [["“No, the black stops there, and the white at number two.”", "\"아니, 검은색이 거기 멈추고, 흰색은 두 번째야.\""]], [["“So it is.”", "\"그렇군.\""]], [["These gate-keepers, it should be understood, were dressed in tunics colored like those of the competing charioteers; so, when they took their stations, everybody knew the particular stall in which his favorite was that moment waiting.", "알아 두어야 할 것은, 이 문지기들이 경쟁하는 전차 몰이꾼들과 같은 색의 짧은 옷을 입고 있었다는 점이다. 그래서 그들이 제자리에 서면, 모두가 자기가 미는 선수가 그 순간 어느 칸막이에서 기다리고 있는지 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Did you ever see Messala?”", "\"메살라를 본 적이 있느냐?\""], ["the Egyptian asked Esther.", "이집트 여인이 에스더에게 물었다."]], [["The Jewess shuddered as she answered no.", "유대 여인은 몸서리치며 아니라고 답했다."], ["If not her father’s enemy, the Roman was Ben-Hur’s.", "그 로마인이 제 아버지의 원수는 아닐지언정, 벤허의 원수였다."]], [["“He is beautiful as Apollo.”", "\"그는 아폴론처럼 아름답단다.\""]], [["As Iras spoke, her large eyes brightened and she shook her jeweled fan.", "이라스가 말할 때, 그 큰 눈이 빛났고 보석으로 장식한 부채를 흔들었다."], ["Esther looked at her with the thought, “Is he, then, so much handsomer than Ben-Hur?”", "에스더는 \"그렇다면 그가 벤허보다 그토록 잘생겼단 말인가?\" 하고 생각하며 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Next moment she heard Ilderim say to her father, “Yes, his stall is number two on the left of the Porta Pompae;”", "다음 순간 일데림이 제 아버지에게 \"그렇소, 그의 칸은 폼파이 문 왼쪽으로 두 번째요\" 하고 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["and, thinking it was of Ben-Hur he spoke, her eyes turned that way.", "벤허를 두고 하는 말이라 여긴 에스더는 그쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다."], ["Taking but the briefest glance at the wattled face of the gate, she drew the veil close and muttered a little prayer.", "엮어 짠 문의 얼굴을 잠깐 흘낏 보고는, 너울을 바싹 끌어당기며 짧은 기도를 중얼거렸다."]], [["Presently Sanballat came to the party.", "이윽고 산발라트가 일행에게로 왔다."]], [["“I am just from the stalls, O sheik,”", "\"방금 칸막이에서 오는 길입니다, 족장님.\""], ["he said, bowing gravely to Ilderim, who began combing his beard, while his eyes glittered with eager inquiry.", "그는 일데림에게 정중히 고개를 숙이며 말했고, 족장은 수염을 빗어 내리기 시작했으니, 그 눈은 간절한 물음으로 반짝였다."], ["“The horses are in perfect condition.”", "\"말들은 더없이 좋은 상태입니다.\""]], [["Ilderim replied simply, “If they are beaten, I pray it be by some other than Messala.”", "일데림은 짤막하게 대답했다. \"그것들이 진다면, 부디 메살라 아닌 다른 자에게 지기를 비오.\""]], [["Turning then to Simonides, Sanballat drew out a tablet, saying, “I bring you also something of interest.", "이어 시모니데스를 향해 산발라트는 서판을 꺼내며 말했다. \"당신에게도 흥미로운 것을 가져왔습니다."], ["I reported, you will remember, the wager concluded with Messala last night, and stated that I left another which, if taken, was to be delivered to me in writing to-day before the race began.", "기억하시겠지만, 어젯밤 메살라와 맺은 내기를 보고드렸고, 또 다른 내기 하나를 남겨 두었는데 그것이 받아들여진다면 경주가 시작되기 전 오늘 안에 서면으로 제게 전해지기로 했다고 말씀드렸지요."], ["Here it is.”", "여기 있습니다.\""]], [["Simonides took the tablet and read the memorandum carefully.", "시모니데스는 서판을 받아 그 적힌 내용을 꼼꼼히 읽었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he said, “their emissary came to ask me if you had so much money with me.", "그가 말했다. \"그들의 심부름꾼이 와서 자네가 그만한 돈을 내게 맡겨 두었는지 물었네."], ["Keep the tablet close.", "그 서판을 잘 간수하게."], ["If you lose, you know where to come; if you win”—his face knit hard—“if you win—ah, friend, see to it!", "진다면 어디로 와야 할지 알 테고, 이긴다면\"—그의 얼굴이 단단히 굳었다—\"이긴다면—아, 친구, 잘 살피게!"], ["See the signers escape not; hold them to the last shekel.", "서명한 자들이 빠져나가지 못하게 하게. 마지막 세겔까지 붙들어 두게."], ["That is what they would with us.”", "그자들이 우리에게 하려던 짓이 바로 그것이니.\""]], [["“Trust me,” replied the purveyor.", "\"믿으십시오.\" 조달인이 대답했다."]], [["“Will you not sit with us?”", "\"우리와 함께 앉지 않겠나?\""], ["asked Simonides.", "시모니데스가 물었다."]], [["“You are very good,”", "\"참으로 고마우신 말씀입니다.\""], ["the other returned; “but if I leave the consul, young Rome yonder will boil over.", "그가 답했다. \"허나 제가 집정관 곁을 떠나면 저쪽 젊은 로마가 끓어 넘칠 겁니다. 평안하시기를."], ["Peace to you; peace to all.”", "모두에게 평안이 있기를.\""]], [["At length the recess came to an end.", "마침내 휴식 시간이 끝났다."]], [["The trumpeters blew a call at which the absentees rushed back to their places.", "나팔수들이 신호를 불자 자리를 비웠던 이들이 제자리로 달려 돌아왔다."], ["At the same time, some attendants appeared in the arena, and, climbing upon the division wall, went to an entablature near the second goal at the west end, and placed upon it seven wooden balls; then returning to the first goal, upon an entablature there they set up seven other pieces of wood hewn to represent dolphins.", "동시에 시중꾼 몇이 경기장에 나타나, 가운데 칸막이 벽 위로 기어올라, 서쪽 끝 두 번째 반환점 가까이의 벽 장식대로 가서 그 위에 나무 공 일곱 개를 올려놓았다. 그러고는 첫 번째 반환점으로 돌아와, 그곳 장식대 위에 돌고래 모양으로 깎은 나무 일곱 개를 또 세워 놓았다."]], [["“What shall they do with the balls and fishes, O sheik?”", "\"저 공과 물고기로 무엇을 하려는 것이오, 족장?\""], ["asked Balthasar.", "발타사르가 물었다."]], [["“Hast thou never attended a race?”", "\"경주를 한 번도 본 적이 없소?\""]], [["“Never before; and hardly know I why I am here.”", "\"한 번도 없소. 게다가 내가 왜 여기 와 있는지조차 잘 모르겠소.\""]], [["“Well, they are to keep the count.", "\"음, 저것들은 횟수를 세는 데 쓰이오."], ["At the end of each round run thou shalt see one ball and one fish taken down.”", "한 바퀴 돌 때마다 공 하나와 물고기 하나씩 내려놓는 것을 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["The preparations were now complete, and presently a trumpeter in gaudy uniform arose by the editor, ready to blow the signal of commencement promptly at his order.", "이제 준비가 모두 끝났고, 이윽고 화려한 옷차림의 나팔수 하나가 흥행 책임자 곁에 일어나, 그 명령이 떨어지면 곧장 개시 신호를 불 채비를 갖추었다."], ["Straightway the stir of the people and the hum of their conversation died away.", "즉시 사람들의 술렁임과 이야기의 웅성거림이 잦아들었다."], ["Every face near-by, and every face in the lessening perspective, turned to the east, as all eyes settled upon the gates of the six stalls which shut in the competitors.", "가까이 있는 얼굴마다, 점점 작아져 가는 시야 속 얼굴마다 동쪽으로 돌아섰으니, 모든 눈이 경쟁자들을 가둔 여섯 칸막이의 문에 박혔다."]], [["The unusual flush upon his face gave proof that even Simonides had caught the universal excitement.", "얼굴에 떠오른 여느 때와 다른 홍조가, 시모니데스조차 그 보편적 흥분에 사로잡혔음을 증명했다."], ["Ilderim pulled his beard fast and furious.", "일데림은 수염을 빠르고 거세게 잡아당겼다."]], [["“Look now for the Roman,”", "\"이제 로마인을 보아라.\""], ["said the fair Egyptian to Esther, who did not hear her, for, with close-drawn veil and beating heart, she sat watching for Ben-Hur.", "어여쁜 이집트 여인이 에스더에게 말했으나, 에스더는 듣지 못했으니, 너울을 바싹 끌어당기고 가슴을 뛰게 하며 벤허를 지켜보느라 여념이 없었다."]], [["The structure containing the stalls, it should be observed, was in form of the segment of a circle, retired on the right so that its central point was projected forward, and midway the course, on the starting side of the first goal.", "알아 두어야 할 것은, 칸막이가 늘어선 건물이 원의 한 조각 모양을 하고 있었다는 점이다. 오른쪽으로 물러나 그 중심점이 앞으로 튀어나오게 하여, 첫 번째 반환점의 출발 쪽 주로 중간쯤에 자리하게 했다."], ["Every stall, consequently, was equally distant from the starting-line or chalked rope above mentioned.", "따라서 모든 칸막이는 출발선, 곧 앞서 말한 분필 칠한 줄에서 똑같은 거리에 있었다."]], [["The trumpet sounded short and sharp; whereupon the starters, one for each chariot, leaped down from behind the pillars of the goal, ready to give assistance if any of the fours proved unmanageable.", "나팔이 짧고 날카롭게 울렸다. 그러자 출발 도우미들이, 전차마다 하나씩 배정된 그들이, 반환점 기둥 뒤에서 뛰어내려, 어느 사두마차든 다루기 힘들어지면 도울 채비를 갖추었다."]], [["Again the trumpet blew, and simultaneously the gate-keepers threw the stalls open.", "다시 나팔이 울렸고, 동시에 문지기들이 칸막이를 활짝 열어젖혔다."]], [["First appeared the mounted attendants of the charioteers, five in all, Ben-Hur having rejected the service.", "먼저 전차 몰이꾼들의 기마 시중꾼들이 나타났으니, 모두 다섯이었다. 벤허는 그 시중을 마다했던 것이다."], ["The chalked line was lowered to let them pass, then raised again.", "분필 줄을 내려 그들을 지나가게 하고는 다시 올렸다."], ["They were beautifully mounted, yet scarcely observed as they rode forward; for all the time the trampling of eager horses, and the voices of drivers scarcely less eager, were heard behind in the stalls, so that one might not look away an instant from the gaping doors.", "그들은 아름다운 말을 타고 있었으나, 앞으로 나아가는 동안 거의 눈길을 끌지 못했다. 그동안 내내 칸막이 안에서 안달하는 말들의 발 구르는 소리와, 그에 못지않게 안달하는 몰이꾼들의 목소리가 들려왔으니, 누구도 잠시라도 그 떡 벌어진 문에서 눈을 뗄 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["The chalked line up again, the gate-keepers called their men; instantly the ushers on the balcony waved their hands, and shouted with all their strength, “Down! down!”", "분필 줄이 다시 올라가자, 문지기들이 자기 선수를 불렀다. 즉시 발코니의 안내인들이 손을 흔들며 있는 힘껏 외쳤다. \"내려라! 내려라!\""]], [["As well have whistled to stay a storm.", "폭풍을 멈추라고 휘파람을 부는 편이 차라리 나았을 것이다."]], [["Forth from each stall, like missiles in a volley from so many great guns, rushed the six fours; and up the vast assemblage arose, electrified and irrepressible, and, leaping upon the benches, filled the Circus and the air above it with yells and screams.", "여섯 사두마차가 각 칸막이에서, 마치 수많은 거포에서 일제히 쏘아 올린 포탄처럼 쏟아져 나왔다. 그러자 거대한 군중이 일어나, 전율하며 걷잡을 수 없이, 의자 위로 뛰어올라 경기장과 그 위 허공을 고함과 비명으로 가득 채웠다."], ["This was the time for which they had so patiently waited!—this the moment of supreme interest treasured up in talk and dreams since the proclamation of the games!", "바로 이때를 위해 그들은 그토록 참을성 있게 기다려 온 것이다!—경기가 선포된 뒤로 이야기와 꿈속에 간직해 온 더없는 흥분의 순간이었다!"]], [["“He is come—there—look!”", "\"그가 왔어—저기—봐!\""], ["cried Iras, pointing to Messala.", "이라스가 메살라를 가리키며 외쳤다."]], [["“I see him,” answered Esther, looking at Ben-Hur.", "\"보고 있어요.\" 에스더가 벤허를 바라보며 답했다."]], [["The veil was withdrawn.", "너울이 걷혔다."], ["For an instant the little Jewess was brave.", "한순간 이 작은 유대 여인은 용감했다."], ["An idea of the joy there is in doing an heroic deed under the eyes of a multitude came to her, and she understood ever after how, at such times, the souls of men, in the frenzy of performance, laugh at death or forget it utterly.", "군중의 눈앞에서 영웅적인 일을 해내는 데 깃든 기쁨이 어떤 것인지 그녀에게 떠올랐고, 그런 순간에 사람의 영혼이 어떻게 실행의 광란 속에서 죽음을 비웃거나 아예 잊어버리는지를 이후로 줄곧 깨닫게 되었다."]], [["The competitors were now under view from nearly every part of the Circus, yet the race was not begun; they had first to make the chalked line successfully.", "이제 경쟁자들은 경기장 거의 모든 곳에서 보였으나, 경주는 아직 시작되지 않았다. 그들은 먼저 분필 줄을 무사히 넘어야 했다."]], [["The line was stretched for the purpose of equalizing the start.", "그 줄은 출발을 고르게 하려고 쳐 둔 것이었다."], ["If it were dashed upon, discomfiture of man and horses might be apprehended; on the other hand, to approach it timidly was to incur the hazard of being thrown behind in the beginning of the race; and that was certain forfeit of the great advantage always striven for—the position next the division wall on the inner line of the course.", "거기에 달려들었다가는 사람과 말이 낭패를 볼 수 있었다. 반면, 머뭇거리며 다가가는 것은 경주 첫머리에 뒤처질 위험을 무릅쓰는 일이었으니, 그것은 늘 다투어 차지하려는 큰 이점—주로 안쪽 선, 곧 칸막이 벽에 바싹 붙은 자리—을 확실히 잃는 것을 뜻했다."]], [["This trial, its perils and consequences, the spectators knew thoroughly; and if the opinion of old Nestor, uttered that time he handed the reins to his son, were true—", "이 시험과 그 위험과 결과를, 관중은 속속들이 알고 있었다. 만약 늙은 네스토르가 아들에게 고삐를 건넬 그때 했던 말이 참이라면—"]], [["“It is not strength, but art, obtained the prize, And to be swift is less than to be wise”—", "\"상을 얻은 것은 힘이 아니라 기술이며,\n빠른 것은 슬기로운 것만 못하다\"—"]], [["all on the benches might well look for warning of the winner to be now given, justifying the interest with which they breathlessly watched for the result.", "의자에 앉은 모두가 이제 승자를 알리는 경고가 주어지기를 기대해 봄 직했으니, 그들이 숨죽이며 결과를 지켜보는 그 관심도 마땅한 것이었다."]], [["The arena swam in a dazzle of light; yet each driver looked first thing for the rope, then for the coveted inner line.", "경기장은 눈부신 빛 속에 일렁였다. 그러나 몰이꾼마다 가장 먼저 줄을 살피고, 다음으로 그토록 탐내는 안쪽 선을 살폈다."], ["So, all six aiming at the same point and speeding furiously, a collision seemed inevitable; nor that merely.", "그리하여 여섯 모두 같은 지점을 노리며 맹렬히 내달리니, 충돌은 피할 수 없어 보였다."], ["What if the editor, at the last moment, dissatisfied with the start, should withhold the signal to drop the rope?", "그뿐만이 아니었다. 만약 흥행 책임자가 마지막 순간에 출발이 마뜩잖아 줄을 떨어뜨리라는 신호를 내지 않는다면?"], ["Or if he should not give it in time?", "혹은 제때 내지 못한다면?"]], [["The crossing was about two hundred and fifty feet in width.", "그 교차 지점은 폭이 약 75미터였다."], ["Quick the eye, steady the hand, unerring the judgment required.", "빠른 눈, 흔들림 없는 손, 어김없는 판단이 요구되었다."], ["If now one look away!", "이제 누구든 한눈을 판다면!"], ["or his mind wander!", "혹은 마음이 흐트러진다면!"], ["or a rein slip! And what attraction in the ensemble of the thousands over the spreading balcony!", "혹은 고삐가 미끄러진다면! 그리고 펼쳐진 발코니 위 수천 명의 무리에는 어떤 끌림이 있던가!"], ["Calculating upon the natural impulse to give one glance—just one—in sooth of curiosity or vanity, malice might be there with an artifice; while friendship and love, did they serve the same result, might be as deadly as malice.", "한 번—단 한 번만이라도—호기심에서든 허영에서든 시선을 던지고 싶은 자연스러운 충동을 계산에 넣고, 악의가 거기에 책략을 부려 도사릴 수도 있었다. 한편 우정과 사랑도, 같은 결과를 빚는다면, 악의 못지않게 치명적일 수 있었다."]], [["The divine last touch in perfecting the beautiful is animation. Can we accept the saying, then these latter days, so tame in pastime and dull in sports, have scarcely anything to compare to the spectacle offered by the six contestants. Let the reader try to fancy it; let him first look down upon the arena, and see it glistening in its frame of dull-gray granite walls; let him then, in this perfect field, see the chariots, light of wheel, very graceful, and ornate as paint and burnishing can make them—Messala’s rich with ivory and gold; let him see the drivers, erect and statuesque, undisturbed by the motion of the cars, their limbs naked, and fresh and ruddy with the healthful polish of the baths—in their right hands goads, suggestive of torture dreadful to the thought—in their left hands, held in careful separation, and high, that they may not interfere with view of the steeds, the reins passing taut from the fore ends of the carriage-poles; let him see the fours, chosen for beauty as well as speed; let him see them in magnificent action, their masters not more conscious of the situation and all that is asked and hoped from them—their heads tossing, nostrils in play, now distent, now contracted—limbs too dainty for the sand which they touch but to spurn—limbs slender, yet with impact crushing as hammers—every muscle of the rounded bodies instinct with glorious life, swelling, diminishing, justifying the world in taking from them its ultimate measure of force; finally, along with chariots, drivers, horses, let the reader see the accompanying shadows fly; and, with such distinctness as the picture comes, he may share the satisfaction and deeper pleasure of those to whom it was a thrilling fact, not a feeble fancy. Every age has its plenty of sorrows; Heaven help where there are no pleasures!", "아름다움을 완성하는 신적인 마지막 손길은 생동감이다. 이 말을 받아들일 수 있다면, 오락은 그토록 맥없고 운동은 그토록 따분한 이 후세에는, 여섯 경쟁자가 보여 준 그 광경에 견줄 만한 것이 거의 없다. 독자는 그것을 상상해 보라. 먼저 경기장을 내려다보아, 칙칙한 잿빛 화강암 벽으로 둘러싸인 채 반짝이는 그곳을 보라. 그다음 이 완벽한 무대 위에서, 바퀴 가볍고 더없이 우아하며 칠과 광택이 낼 수 있는 한껏 장식된 전차들을—메살라의 것은 상아와 황금으로 호화로운 그 전차들을 보라. 몰이꾼들이 꼿꼿이, 조각상처럼, 수레의 흔들림에도 흔들리지 않고 선 모습을 보라. 그 팔다리는 벌거벗었고, 목욕으로 건강하게 윤이 나 싱싱하고 불그레했다—오른손에는 생각만으로도 끔찍한 고문을 떠올리게 하는 채찍을, 왼손에는 말들을 보는 데 방해가 되지 않도록 조심스레 떼어 높이 든 채, 수레 채의 앞 끝에서 팽팽히 뻗어 온 고삐를 쥐고 있었다. 빠르기 못지않게 아름다움으로 골라 뽑은 사두마들을 보라. 그것들이 장엄하게 내달리는 모습을 보라. 그 주인들조차 처지와, 자기들에게 요구되고 바라는 모든 것을 그보다 더 의식하지는 못할 만큼이었다. 머리를 치켜들고, 콧구멍을 벌름거려 넓혔다 좁혔다 하고, 디디는 모래마저도 뿌리치듯 차 버릴 만큼 그 다리가 너무도 곱고—가늘면서도 부딪치면 망치처럼 으스러뜨리는 다리였다. 둥근 몸의 근육 하나하나가 영광스러운 생명으로 가득 차 부풀었다 줄어들었다 하니, 세상이 그것들에게서 힘의 궁극적 잣대를 가져가는 것도 마땅했다. 끝으로, 전차와 몰이꾼과 말과 더불어, 그 곁을 따라 나는 그림자들을 독자는 보라. 그리하여 그 그림이 또렷이 떠오르는 만큼, 독자는 그것이 가냘픈 공상이 아니라 가슴 떨리는 사실이었던 이들의 만족과 더 깊은 기쁨을 함께 나눌 수 있으리라. 어느 시대에나 슬픔은 넘쳐 난다. 기쁨이 없는 곳에는 하늘이 도와주시기를!"]], [["The competitors having started each on the shortest line for the position next the wall, yielding would be like giving up the race; and who dared yield?", "경쟁자들은 저마다 벽에 바싹 붙은 자리를 향해 최단 선을 따라 출발했으니, 양보하는 것은 경주를 포기하는 것과 같았다. 한데 누가 감히 양보하겠는가?"], ["It is not in common nature to change a purpose in mid-career; and the cries of encouragement from the balcony were indistinguishable and indescribable: a roar which had the same effect upon all the drivers.", "길 한복판에서 마음먹은 바를 바꾸는 것은 사람의 본성에 없는 일이다. 발코니에서 쏟아지는 격려의 외침은 알아들을 수도, 말로 옮길 수도 없었다. 그것은 모든 몰이꾼에게 똑같은 효과를 미치는 함성이었다."]], [["The fours neared the rope together.", "사두마차들이 다 함께 줄에 다가섰다."], ["Then the trumpeter by the editor’s side blew a signal vigorously.", "그러자 흥행 책임자 곁의 나팔수가 힘차게 신호를 불었다."], ["Twenty feet away it was not heard.", "6미터 떨어진 곳에서는 들리지 않았다."], ["Seeing the action, however, the judges dropped the rope, and not an instant too soon, for the hoof of one of Messala’s horses struck it as it fell.", "그러나 그 동작을 본 심판들이 줄을 떨어뜨렸으니, 조금도 이르지 않은 때였다. 메살라의 말 한 마리의 발굽이 떨어지는 줄을 차고 지나갔던 것이다."], ["Nothing daunted, the Roman shook out his long lash, loosed the reins, leaned forward, and, with a triumphant shout, took the wall.", "조금도 겁먹지 않고 로마인은 긴 채찍을 휘둘러 풀고, 고삐를 늦추고, 앞으로 몸을 기울이며, 의기양양한 외침과 함께 벽 쪽을 차지했다."]], [["“Jove with us! Jove with us!”", "\"제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!"], ["yelled all the Roman faction, in a frenzy of delight.", "제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 모든 로마 패거리가 미칠 듯한 기쁨으로 외쳤다."]], [["As Messala turned in, the bronze lion’s head at the end of his axle caught the fore-leg of the Athenian’s right-hand trace-mate, flinging the brute over against its yoke-fellow.", "메살라가 안으로 돌아드는 순간, 그 굴대 끝의 청동 사자 머리가 아테네 선수의 오른쪽 곁말 앞다리를 걸어, 그 짐승을 멍에 짝에게로 내동댕이쳤다."], ["Both staggered, struggled, and lost their headway.", "둘 다 비틀거리고 버둥거리다 달리던 기세를 잃었다."], ["The ushers had their will at least in part.", "안내인들의 뜻이 적어도 얼마쯤은 이루어진 셈이었다."], ["The thousands held their breath with horror; only up where the consul sat was there shouting.", "수천 명이 공포에 숨을 죽였다. 오직 집정관이 앉은 저 위에서만 함성이 일었다."]], [["“Jove with us!” screamed Drusus, frantically.", "\"제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 드루수스가 미친 듯이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“He wins!", "\"그가 이긴다!"], ["Jove with us!” answered his associates, seeing Messala speed on.", "제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\" 메살라가 내달리는 것을 보고 그 일당이 화답했다."]], [["Tablet in hand, Sanballat turned to them; a crash from the course below stopped his speech, and he could not but look that way.", "서판을 손에 든 채 산발라트가 그들에게로 돌아섰다. 그러나 아래 주로에서 들려온 충돌 소리가 그의 말을 막았으니, 그도 그쪽을 보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Messala having passed, the Corinthian was the only contestant on the Athenian’s right, and to that side the latter tried to turn his broken four; and then; as ill-fortune would have it, the wheel of the Byzantine, who was next on the left, struck the tail-piece of his chariot, knocking his feet from under him.", "메살라가 지나가 버린 뒤, 아테네 선수의 오른쪽에는 코린토스 선수만 남아 있었고, 그쪽으로 아테네 선수는 망가진 사두마를 돌리려 했다. 그러자 운이 나쁘게도, 왼쪽으로 그 옆에 있던 비잔티움 선수의 바퀴가 그의 전차 뒤꽁무니를 들이받아 그의 발밑을 휩쓸어 버렸다."], ["There was a crash, a scream of rage and fear, and the unfortunate Cleanthes fell under the hoofs of his own steeds: a terrible sight, against which Esther covered her eyes.", "우지끈하는 소리, 분노와 두려움의 비명이 일었고, 불운한 클레안테스는 제 말들의 발굽 아래로 쓰러졌으니, 에스더가 두 눈을 가린 그 끔찍한 광경이었다."]], [["On swept the Corinthian, on the Byzantine, on the Sidonian.", "코린토스 선수가 내달렸고, 비잔티움 선수가, 시돈 선수가 내달렸다."]], [["Sanballat looked for Ben-Hur, and turned again to Drusus and his coterie.", "산발라트는 벤허를 살피고는, 다시 드루수스와 그 패거리를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“A hundred sestertii on the Jew!”", "\"유대인에게 백 세스테르티우스!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["“Taken!”", "\"받겠소!\""], ["answered Drusus.", "드루수스가 답했다."]], [["“Another hundred on the Jew!” shouted Sanballat.", "\"유대인에게 또 백!\" 산발라트가 소리쳤다."]], [["Nobody appeared to hear him.", "아무도 그의 말을 듣는 것 같지 않았다."], ["He called again; the situation below was too absorbing, and they were too busy shouting, “Messala!", "그가 다시 외쳤다. 아래 상황이 너무도 사람을 사로잡았고, 그들은 \"메살라! 메살라! 제우스가 우리와 함께하신다!\""], ["Messala! Jove with us!”", "하고 외치느라 너무 바빴다."]], [["When the Jewess ventured to look again, a party of workmen were removing the horses and broken car; another party were taking off the man himself; and every bench upon which there was a Greek was vocal with execrations and prayers for vengeance.", "유대 여인이 다시 보기로 마음을 다잡았을 때, 일꾼 한 무리는 말들과 부서진 수레를 치우고 있었고, 또 다른 무리는 사람 자체를 들어 내고 있었다. 그리고 그리스 사람이 앉은 의자마다 저주와 복수를 비는 기도가 울려 퍼졌다."], ["Suddenly she dropped her hands; Ben-Hur, unhurt, was to the front, coursing freely forward along with the Roman!", "문득 그녀가 두 손을 떨구었다. 다친 데 없는 벤허가 앞쪽에서, 로마인과 나란히 거침없이 앞으로 내달리고 있었던 것이다!"], ["Behind them, in a group, followed the Sidonian, the Corinthian, and the Byzantine.", "그들 뒤로는 시돈 선수와 코린토스 선수와 비잔티움 선수가 한 무리를 이루어 따라왔다."]], [["The race was on; the souls of the racers were in it; over them bent the myriads.", "경주가 진행되고 있었다. 경주자들의 영혼이 그 안에 들어 있었다. 그들 위로 수만 명이 몸을 굽혀 내려다보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV", "# CHAPTER XIV"]], [["When the dash for position began, Ben-Hur, as we have seen, was on the extreme left of the six.", "위치 다툼이 시작되었을 때, 우리가 이미 보았듯이 벤허는 여섯 명 가운데 맨 왼쪽에 있었다."], ["For a moment, like the others, he was half blinded by the light in the arena; yet he managed to catch sight of his antagonists and divine their purpose.", "다른 이들처럼 그도 한순간 경기장의 빛에 반쯤 눈이 멀었으나, 그래도 그는 가까스로 적수들의 모습을 포착하고 그들의 속셈을 헤아렸다. 단순한 적수 이상인 메살라에게 그는 한 번 꿰뚫는 시선을 던졌다."], ["At Messala, who was more than an antagonist to him, he gave one searching look.", "그 우아한 귀족의 얼굴에 깃든 무정하고 오만한 분위기는 예전 그대로였고, 투구가 오히려 돋보이게 하는 이탈리아풍의 아름다움도 여전했다."], ["The air of passionless hauteur characteristic of the fine patrician face was there as of old, and so was the Italian beauty, which the helmet rather increased; but more—it may have been a jealous fancy, or the effect of the brassy shadow in which the features were at the moment cast, still the Israelite thought he saw the soul of the man as through a glass, darkly: cruel, cunning, desperate; not so excited as determined—a soul in a tension of watchfulness and fierce resolve.", "그러나 그 이상이었으니—질투에서 비롯된 착각이었을 수도, 그 순간 이목구비 위에 드리운 놋빛 그림자의 효과였을 수도 있으나, 그래도 이 이스라엘 사람은 마치 유리를 통해 어렴풋이 보듯 그자의 영혼을 들여다보았다고 여겼다. 잔인하고, 교활하고, 필사적인 영혼. 흥분했다기보다는 단단히 결심한—경계와 사나운 결의로 팽팽히 당겨진 영혼이었다."]], [["In a time not longer than was required to turn to his four again, Ben-Hur felt his own resolution harden to a like temper.", "다시 자기의 네 마리에게로 눈을 돌리는 데 걸리는 시간도 채 안 되어, 벤허는 자기 자신의 결심이 그와 똑같은 강도로 단단해지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["At whatever cost, at all hazards, he would humble this enemy!", "어떤 대가를 치르더라도, 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰더라도, 이 원수를 무릎 꿇리리라!"], ["Prize, friends, wagers, honor—everything that can be thought of as a possible interest in the race was lost in the one deliberate purpose.", "상금이며, 친구들이며, 내기며, 명예며—경주에서 가질 법한 온갖 관심사가 그 하나의 단호한 목적 속에 모조리 사라졌다."], ["Regard for life even should not hold him back.", "목숨에 대한 미련조차 그를 붙들지 못할 터였다."], ["Yet there was no passion, on his part; no blinding rush of heated blood from heart to brain, and back again; no impulse to fling himself upon Fortune: he did not believe in Fortune; far otherwise.", "그러나 그에게 격정은 없었다. 심장에서 머리로, 다시 심장으로 치닫는 달뜬 피의 눈먼 격류도 없었고, 운명에 제 몸을 내던지려는 충동도 없었다. 그는 운명을 믿지 않았으니, 오히려 그 반대였다."], ["He had his plan, and, confiding in himself, he settled to the task never more observant, never more capable.", "그에게는 계획이 있었고, 자기 자신을 믿으며 그 어느 때보다 빈틈없이, 그 어느 때보다 능숙하게 일에 임할 채비를 갖추었다."], ["The air about him seemed aglow with a renewed and perfect transparency.", "그를 둘러싼 공기마저 새롭고도 완벽한 투명함으로 빛나는 듯했다."]], [["When not half-way across the arena, he saw that Messala’s rush would, if there was no collision, and the rope fell, give him the wall; that the rope would fall, he ceased as soon to doubt; and, further, it came to him, a sudden flash-like insight, that Messala knew it was to be let drop at the last moment (prearrangement with the editor could safely reach that point in the contest); and it suggested, what more Roman-like than for the official to lend himself to a countryman who, besides being so popular, had also so much at stake? There could be no other accounting for the confidence with which Messala pushed his four forward the instant his competitors were prudentially checking their fours in front of the obstruction—no other except madness.", "경기장을 가로질러 절반도 채 못 갔을 때, 그는 깨달았다. 충돌만 없고 밧줄이 떨어지기만 하면, 메살라의 돌진이 그에게 안쪽 벽을 내줄 것임을. 밧줄이 떨어지리라는 것은 곧 의심할 여지가 없었다. 게다가 번개처럼 갑작스러운 통찰이 그에게 찾아왔으니, 그것은 메살라가 밧줄이 마지막 순간에 떨어지도록 되어 있음을 알고 있다는 사실이었다(주최자와의 사전 모의가 경기에서 그 정도 지점까지는 충분히 미칠 수 있었다). 그리고 그것은 또 한 가지를 일깨워 주었다. 관리가 동족에게, 그것도 그토록 인기가 높을 뿐 아니라 걸린 것도 그토록 많은 동족에게 한몫 거들어 주는 것보다 더 로마인다운 일이 어디 있겠는가? 경쟁자들이 장애물 앞에서 신중하게 자기의 네 마리를 제어하고 있는 바로 그 순간 메살라가 자기 네 마리를 앞으로 밀어붙이는 그 자신감을 달리 설명할 길은 없었다—광기가 아니고서는 달리 설명할 수 없었다."]], [["It is one thing to see a necessity and another to act upon it.", "필연을 알아보는 것과 그에 따라 행동하는 것은 별개의 일이다."], ["Ben-Hur yielded the wall for the time.", "벤허는 일단 벽을 양보했다."]], [["The rope fell, and all the fours but his sprang into the course under urgency of voice and lash.", "밧줄이 떨어지자, 그의 네 마리를 뺀 모든 네 마리가 고함과 채찍의 재촉을 받으며 경주로로 튀어 나갔다."], ["He drew head to the right, and, with all the speed of his Arabs, darted across the trails of his opponents, the angle of movement being such as to lose the least time and gain the greatest possible advance.", "그는 머리를 오른쪽으로 돌려, 아랍 말들의 온 속력을 다해 적수들의 진로를 가로질러 내달렸다. 그 움직임의 각도는 시간을 가장 적게 잃고 가능한 한 가장 크게 전진하도록 맞춰져 있었다."], ["So, while the spectators were shivering at the Athenian’s mishap, and the Sidonian, Byzantine, and Corinthian were striving, with such skill as they possessed, to avoid involvement in the ruin, Ben-Hur swept around and took the course neck and neck with Messala, though on the outside.", "그리하여 관중이 아테네 사람의 사고에 몸서리치고, 시돈 사람과 비잔티움 사람과 코린토스 사람이 저마다의 솜씨로 그 난파에 휘말리지 않으려 안간힘을 쓰는 동안, 벤허는 휘돌아 나와 비록 바깥쪽이긴 했으나 메살라와 나란히 어깨를 견주며 경주로에 들어섰다."], ["The marvellous skill shown in making the change thus from the extreme left across to the right without appreciable loss did not fail the sharp eyes upon the benches; the Circus seemed to rock and rock again with prolonged applause.", "맨 왼쪽에서 오른쪽까지 이렇게 별다른 손실 없이 자리를 바꾸는 데 보인 놀라운 솜씨는 관람석의 날카로운 눈들을 놓치지 않았다. 경기장은 오래도록 이어지는 박수갈채에 흔들리고 또 흔들리는 듯했다."], ["Then Esther clasped her hands in glad surprise; then Sanballat, smiling, offered his hundred sestertii a second time without a taker; and then the Romans began to doubt, thinking Messala might have found an equal, if not a master, and that in an Israelite!", "그러자 에스더는 기쁜 놀라움에 두 손을 맞잡았고, 산발랏은 미소 지으며 다시 한번 100세스테르티우스를 걸었으나 받는 자가 없었으며, 로마인들은 의심을 품기 시작했으니, 메살라가 자기의 스승은 아닐지언정 호적수를 만난 것은 아닌가, 그것도 이스라엘 사람 중에서!"]], [["And now, racing together side by side, a narrow interval between them, the two neared the second goal.", "이제 둘은 좁은 간격을 사이에 두고 나란히 어깨를 견주며 달려, 두 번째 반환점에 다가갔다."]], [["The pedestal of the three pillars there, viewed from the west, was a stone wall in the form of a half-circle, around which the course and opposite balcony were bent in exact parallelism.", "거기 세워진 세 기둥의 받침대는 서쪽에서 보면 반원 모양의 돌벽으로, 경주로와 맞은편 발코니가 그 둘레를 정확히 평행으로 휘감고 있었다."], ["Making this turn was considered in all respects the most telling test of a charioteer; it was, in fact, the very feat in which Oraetes failed.", "이 반환점을 도는 것은 모든 면에서 전차 모는 자의 가장 결정적인 시험으로 여겨졌으니, 사실 오라이테스가 실패한 것이 바로 이 묘기였다."], ["As an involuntary admission of interest on the part of the spectators, a hush fell over all the Circus, so that for the first time in the race the rattle and clang of the cars plunging after the tugging steeds were distinctly heard.", "관중이 무심결에 드러내는 관심의 표시인 양, 온 경기장에 정적이 내려앉았다. 그리하여 경주가 시작된 이래 처음으로, 안간힘을 다하는 말들을 뒤쫓아 돌진하는 전차들의 덜그럭거리고 쟁그랑거리는 소리가 또렷이 들려왔다."], ["Then, it would seem, Messala observed Ben-Hur, and recognized him; and at once the audacity of the man flamed out in an astonishing manner.", "그때, 보아하니 메살라가 벤허를 알아본 모양이었다. 누구인지 알아차린 것이다. 그러자 그자의 대담함이 단번에 놀라운 방식으로 불타올랐다."]], [["“Down Eros, up Mars!” he shouted, whirling his lash with practised hand—“Down Eros, up Mars!”", "\"에로스는 내려가고, 마르스는 올라오라!\" 그는 노련한 손놀림으로 채찍을 휘두르며 외쳤다—\"에로스는 내려가고, 마르스는 올라오라!\""], ["he repeated, and caught the well-doing Arabs of Ben-Hur a cut the like of which they had never known.", "그는 거듭 외치며, 잘 달리고 있던 벤허의 아랍 말들에게 그들이 일찍이 겪어 본 적 없는 채찍질을 가했다."]], [["The blow was seen in every quarter, and the amazement was universal.", "그 일격은 사방에서 목격되었고, 놀라움은 누구에게나 한결같았다."], ["The silence deepened; up on the benches behind the consul the boldest held his breath, waiting for the outcome.", "정적은 더욱 깊어졌다. 집정관 뒤편 관람석 위에서는 가장 담대한 자조차 숨을 죽인 채 결과를 기다렸다."], ["Only a moment thus: then, involuntarily, down from the balcony, as thunder falls, burst the indignant cry of the people.", "그러나 그것도 한순간이었다. 곧 천둥이 떨어지듯, 발코니에서 백성의 분노에 찬 외침이 무심결에 터져 나왔다."]], [["The four sprang forward affrighted.", "네 마리는 겁에 질려 앞으로 튀어 나갔다."], ["No hand had ever been laid upon them except in love; they had been nurtured ever so tenderly; and as they grew, their confidence in man became a lesson to men beautiful to see.", "이제껏 사랑의 손길 말고는 누구의 손도 닿은 적이 없는 말들이었다. 더없이 다정하게 길러졌고, 자라면서 사람에게 보이는 그 신뢰는 사람들이 보기에도 아름다운 본보기가 되었다."], ["What should such dainty natures do under such indignity but leap as from death?", "그토록 곱게 자란 천성이 그런 모욕 앞에서 죽음에서 달아나듯 펄쩍 뛰는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴?"]], [["Forward they sprang as with one impulse, and forward leaped the car.", "마치 한 가지 충동에 사로잡힌 듯 네 마리가 앞으로 솟구쳤고, 전차도 앞으로 튀어 나갔다."], ["Past question, every experience is serviceable to us.", "두말할 것도 없이, 모든 경험은 우리에게 쓸모가 있다."], ["Where got Ben-Hur the large hand and mighty grip which helped him now so well?", "지금 그토록 큰 도움이 된 벤허의 그 큰 손과 억센 악력은 어디서 얻은 것인가?"], ["Where but from the oar with which so long he fought the sea?", "그토록 오랜 세월 바다와 싸우던 그 노가 아니고서 어디겠는가?"], ["And what was this spring of the floor under his feet to the dizzy eccentric lurch with which in the old time the trembling ship yielded to the beat of staggering billows, drunk with their power?", "그리고 발밑 바닥의 이 들썩임이, 비틀거리는 물결의 힘에 취해 떨리던 배가 그 너울의 박동에 못 이겨 어지러이 제멋대로 휘청거리던 옛 시절의 그것에 비하면 무엇이겠는가?"], ["So he kept his place, and gave the four free rein, and called to them in soothing voice, trying merely to guide them round the dangerous turn; and before the fever of the people began to abate, he had back the mastery.", "그리하여 그는 제자리를 지키며 네 마리에게 고삐를 늦추어 주고, 다독이는 목소리로 그들을 불렀으니, 그저 그들을 위험한 반환점 둘레로 이끌고자 할 뿐이었다. 그리고 백성의 열기가 가라앉기도 전에, 그는 다시 통제권을 되찾았다."], ["Nor that only: on approaching the first goal, he was again side by side with Messala, bearing with him the sympathy and admiration of every one not a Roman.", "그뿐이 아니었다. 첫 번째 반환점에 다가가면서 그는 다시 메살라와 어깨를 나란히 했으니, 로마인이 아닌 모든 이의 공감과 찬탄을 한 몸에 받으면서였다."], ["So clearly was the feeling shown, so vigorous its manifestation, that Messala, with all his boldness, felt it unsafe to trifle further.", "그 감정이 어찌나 또렷이 드러났던지, 그 표출이 어찌나 격렬했던지, 메살라는 그 모든 대담함에도 더는 함부로 굴기가 위험하다고 느꼈다."]], [["As the cars whirled round the goal, Esther caught sight of Ben-Hur’s face—a little pale, a little higher raised, otherwise calm, even placid.", "전차들이 반환점을 휘돌아 나갈 때, 에스더는 벤허의 얼굴을 흘끗 보았다. 조금 창백하고, 조금 더 높이 쳐들었으되, 그 밖에는 차분한, 심지어 평온한 얼굴이었다."]], [["Immediately a man climbed on the entablature at the west end of the division wall, and took down one of the conical wooden balls.", "곧바로 한 사람이 분리벽 서쪽 끝의 처마 장식 위로 올라가, 원뿔 모양의 나무 공 하나를 내렸다."], ["A dolphin on the east entablature was taken down at the same time.", "동쪽 처마 장식의 돌고래 하나도 같은 때에 내려졌다."]], [["In like manner, the second ball and second dolphin disappeared.", "같은 방식으로, 두 번째 공과 두 번째 돌고래가 사라졌다."]], [["And then the third ball and third dolphin.", "그러고는 세 번째 공과 세 번째 돌고래가 사라졌다."]], [["Three rounds concluded: still Messala held the inside position; still Ben-Hur moved with him side by side; still the other competitors followed as before.", "세 바퀴를 마쳤다. 여전히 메살라가 안쪽 자리를 지켰고, 여전히 벤허가 그와 어깨를 나란히 하며 움직였으며, 여전히 다른 경쟁자들이 전과 같이 뒤따랐다."], ["The contest began to have the appearance of one of the double races which became so popular in Rome during the later Cæsarean period—Messala and Ben-Hur in the first, the Corinthian, Sidonian, and Byzantine in the second.", "경주는 카이사르 후기 시절 로마에서 그토록 인기를 끌었던 이중 경주 가운데 하나와 같은 양상을 띠기 시작했다—첫 번째 무리에는 메살라와 벤허가, 두 번째 무리에는 코린토스 사람과 시돈 사람과 비잔티움 사람이 있었다."], ["Meantime the ushers succeeded in returning the multitude to their seats, though the clamor continued to run the rounds, keeping, as it were, even pace with the rivals in the course below.", "그동안 안내인들은 군중을 자리로 되돌려 앉히는 데 성공했으나, 함성은 계속 경주로를 돌며, 마치 저 아래 경주로의 맞수들과 발맞추기라도 하듯 이어졌다."]], [["In the fifth round the Sidonian succeeded in getting a place outside Ben-Hur, but lost it directly.", "다섯 번째 바퀴에서 시돈 사람이 벤허의 바깥쪽 자리를 차지하는 데 성공했으나, 곧 그 자리를 잃었다."]], [["The sixth round was entered upon without change of relative position.", "여섯 번째 바퀴는 서로의 위치에 변화 없이 시작되었다."]], [["Gradually the speed had been quickened—gradually the blood of the competitors warmed with the work.", "속력은 차츰 빨라졌고—경쟁자들의 피는 차츰 그 분투로 달아올랐다."], ["Men and beasts seemed to know alike that the final crisis was near, bringing the time for the winner to assert himself.", "사람도 짐승도 한결같이, 마지막 위기가 가까웠음을, 승자가 자기를 내세울 때가 다가오고 있음을 아는 듯했다."]], [["The interest which from the beginning had centred chiefly in the struggle between the Roman and the Jew, with an intense and general sympathy for the latter, was fast changing to anxiety on his account.", "처음부터 주로 로마인과 유대인의 다툼에 쏠려 있던, 그러면서 후자에게 강렬하고도 두루 퍼진 공감을 보내던 관심은, 이제 빠르게 그를 위한 근심으로 바뀌어 갔다."], ["On all the benches the spectators bent forward motionless, except as their faces turned following the contestants.", "모든 관람석에서 관중은 꼼짝 않고 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채, 다만 얼굴만 경쟁자들을 따라 돌렸다."], ["Ilderim quitted combing his beard, and Esther forgot her fears.", "일데림은 수염 빗질을 멈추었고, 에스더는 두려움도 잊었다."]], [["“A hundred sestertii on the Jew!”", "\"유대인에게 100세스테르티우스!\""], ["cried Sanballat to the Romans under the consul’s awning.", "산발랏이 집정관의 차일 아래 로마인들을 향해 외쳤다."]], [["There was no reply.", "아무런 대답이 없었다."]], [["“A talent—or five talents, or ten; choose ye!”", "\"한 탈렌트—아니 다섯 탈렌트, 아니 열 탈렌트라도, 골라 보시오!\""]], [["He shook his tablets at them defiantly.", "그는 도전하듯 그들에게 서판을 흔들어 보였다."]], [["“I will take thy sestertii,”", "\"내가 그대의 세스테르티우스를 받겠소.\""], ["answered a Roman youth, preparing to write.", "한 로마 청년이 적을 채비를 하며 대답했다."]], [["“Do not so,”", "\"그러지 마시게.\""], ["interposed a friend.", "한 친구가 가로막았다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Messala hath reached his utmost speed.", "\"메살라는 이미 제 속력을 다했네."], ["See him lean over his chariot rim, the reins loose as flying ribbons.", "보게, 전차 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 고삐를 날리는 리본처럼 늦추어 잡고 있지 않은가."], ["Look then at the Jew.”", "그럼 이제 저 유대인을 보게.\""]], [["The first one looked.", "처음의 청년이 바라보았다."]], [["“By Hercules!” he replied, his countenance falling.", "\"헤라클레스에 맹세코!\" 그는 낯빛이 어두워지며 대답했다."], ["“The dog throws all his weight on the bits.", "\"저 개 같은 자가 재갈에 제 온 무게를 싣고 있구나."], ["I see, I see!", "알겠다, 알겠어!"], ["If the gods help not our friend, he will be run away with by the Israelite.", "신들이 우리 벗을 돕지 않으면, 저자는 이스라엘 사람에게 추월당하고 말 게다. 아니, 아직은 아니다. 보게!"], ["No, not yet. Look! Jove with us, Jove with us!”", "유피테르께서 우리와 함께하시기를, 유피테르께서 우리와 함께하시기를!\""]], [["The cry, swelled by every Latin tongue, shook the velaria over the consul’s head.", "라틴어를 쓰는 모든 입에서 부풀어 오른 그 외침이, 집정관의 머리 위 차일을 뒤흔들었다."]], [["If it were true that Messala had attained his utmost speed, the effort was with effect; slowly but certainly he was beginning to forge ahead.", "메살라가 정말로 제 속력을 다한 것이 사실이라면, 그 분투에는 효과가 있었다. 더디지만 분명히 그는 앞서 나가기 시작했다."], ["His horses were running with their heads low down; from the balcony their bodies appeared actually to skim the earth; their nostrils showed blood red in expansion; their eyes seemed straining in their sockets.", "그의 말들은 머리를 낮게 숙인 채 달렸고, 발코니에서 보면 그 몸뚱이가 실제로 땅을 스치는 듯했으며, 벌름거리는 콧구멍은 핏빛으로 붉었고, 두 눈은 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯 부릅떠져 있었다."], ["Certainly the good steeds were doing their best!", "분명 그 훌륭한 말들은 온 힘을 다하고 있었다!"], ["How long could they keep the pace? It was but the commencement of the sixth round.", "그러나 그 속력을 얼마나 오래 유지할 수 있을 것인가? 이제 겨우 여섯 번째 바퀴의 시작이었다."], ["On they dashed. As they neared the second goal, Ben-Hur turned in behind the Roman’s car.", "그들은 계속 내달렸다. 두 번째 반환점에 다가가면서, 벤허는 로마인의 전차 뒤로 돌아섰다."]], [["The joy of the Messala faction reached its bound: they screamed and howled, and tossed their colors; and Sanballat filled his tablets with wagers of their tendering.", "메살라 편 사람들의 환희는 극에 달했다. 그들은 비명을 지르고 울부짖으며 자기네 깃발을 흔들어 댔고, 산발랏은 그들이 내미는 내기로 서판을 가득 채웠다."]], [["Malluch, in the lower gallery over the Gate of Triumph, found it hard to keep his cheer.", "개선문 위쪽 아래 회랑에 있던 말루크는 환호를 이어 가기가 힘들었다."], ["He had cherished the vague hint dropped to him by Ben-Hur of something to happen in the turning of the western pillars.", "그는 서쪽 기둥을 도는 길목에서 무슨 일이 벌어지리라던, 벤허가 그에게 흘린 막연한 암시를 가슴에 품어 왔던 것이다."], ["It was the fifth round, yet the something had not come; and he had said to himself, the sixth will bring it; but, lo!", "어느덧 다섯 번째 바퀴가 되었으나 그 무엇은 아직 오지 않았다. 그는 여섯 번째 바퀴가 그것을 가져오리라고 스스로 다독였으나, 보라!"], ["Ben-Hur was hardly holding a place at the tail of his enemy’s car.", "벤허는 원수의 전차 꽁무니에서 자리를 겨우 지키고 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["Over in the east end, Simonides’ party held their peace.", "저편 동쪽 끝에서, 시모니데스 일행은 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["The merchant’s head was bent low.", "상인은 고개를 깊이 떨구었다."], ["Ilderim tugged at his beard, and dropped his brows till there was nothing of his eyes but an occasional sparkle of light.", "일데림은 수염을 잡아당기며 눈썹을 내리깔아, 이따금 한 줄기 빛이 반짝이는 것 말고는 두 눈이 도무지 보이지 않았다."], ["Esther scarcely breathed.", "에스더는 숨도 제대로 쉬지 못했다."], ["Iras alone appeared glad.", "오직 이라스만이 기뻐 보였다."]], [["Along the home-stretch—sixth round—Messala leading, next him Ben-Hur, and so close it was the old story:", "홈스트레치를 따라—여섯 번째 바퀴—메살라가 앞서고, 그다음에 벤허, 그것도 어찌나 바짝 붙었던지 그것은 그 옛 이야기 그대로였다."]], [["“First flew Eumelus on Pheretian steeds; With those of Tros bold Diomed succeeds; Close on Eumelus’ back they puff the wind, And seem just mounting on his car behind; Full on his neck he feels the sultry breeze, And, hovering o’er, their stretching shadow sees.”", "\"맨 앞은 페레스의 준마를 몰며 에우멜로스가 날았고, 트로스의 말들로 용맹한 디오메데스가 그 뒤를 이었네. 에우멜로스의 등에 바짝 붙어 그들은 바람을 내뿜으니, 금방이라도 뒤에서 그의 전차에 올라탈 듯하구나. 그의 목덜미에 후끈한 바람이 그대로 끼쳐 오고, 머리 위를 맴도는 그들의 길게 뻗은 그림자가 보이네.\""]], [["Thus to the first goal, and round it.", "이렇게 첫 번째 반환점까지, 그리고 그것을 돌았다."], ["Messala, fearful of losing his place, hugged the stony wall with perilous clasp; a foot to the left, and he had been dashed to pieces; yet, when the turn was finished, no man, looking at the wheel-tracks of the two cars, could have said, here went Messala, there the Jew.", "메살라는 자리를 잃을까 두려워, 아슬아슬하게 돌벽을 끌어안았다. 왼쪽으로 한 발만 빗나갔어도 그는 산산조각이 났으리라. 그러나 반환을 마쳤을 때, 두 전차의 바퀴 자국을 본 그 누구도 여기는 메살라가, 저기는 유대인이 지나갔다고 말할 수 없었으리라."], ["They left but one trace behind them.", "그들은 단 하나의 자국만을 뒤에 남겼다."]], [["As they whirled by, Esther saw Ben-Hur’s face again, and it was whiter than before.", "그들이 휙 지나갈 때, 에스더는 다시 벤허의 얼굴을 보았는데, 그것은 전보다 더 창백했다."]], [["Simonides, shrewder than Esther, said to Ilderim, the moment the rivals turned into the course, “I am no judge, good sheik, if Ben-Hur be not about to execute some design.", "에스더보다 영민한 시모니데스는, 맞수들이 경주로로 접어드는 그 순간 일데림에게 말했다. \"내 눈이 틀리지 않다면, 선량한 족장이여, 벤허가 무언가 계책을 펼치려는 참이오."], ["His face hath that look.”", "그 얼굴에 그런 기색이 있소.\""]], [["To which Ilderim answered, “Saw you how clean they were and fresh?", "이에 일데림이 대답했다. \"저 말들이 얼마나 멀쩡하고 생생한지 보았소?"], ["By the splendor of God, friend, they have not been running!", "하느님의 영광에 맹세코, 친구여, 저놈들은 아직 달린 것도 아니오!"], ["But now watch!”", "자, 이제 두고 보시오!\""]], [["One ball and one dolphin remained on the entablatures; and all the people drew a long breath, for the beginning of the end was at hand.", "처마 장식 위에는 공 하나와 돌고래 하나만이 남았다. 그리고 모든 백성이 길게 숨을 들이쉬었으니, 마지막의 시작이 임박했기 때문이다."]], [["First, the Sidonian gave the scourge to his four, and, smarting with fear and pain, they dashed desperately forward, promising for a brief time to go to the front.", "먼저, 시돈 사람이 자기 네 마리에게 채찍을 휘둘렀고, 두려움과 고통에 따끔거리던 말들은 필사적으로 앞으로 내달려, 잠시 동안 선두로 나설 듯이 보였다."], ["The effort ended in promise. Next, the Byzantine and the Corinthian each made the trial with like result, after which they were practically out of the race.", "그러나 그 분투는 가능성에 그치고 말았다. 다음으로 비잔티움 사람과 코린토스 사람이 저마다 시도했으나 결과는 같았고, 그 뒤로 그들은 사실상 경주에서 밀려났다."], ["Thereupon, with a readiness perfectly explicable, all the factions except the Romans joined hope in Ben-Hur, and openly indulged their feeling.", "그러자 충분히 이해할 만한 거리낌 없음으로, 로마인을 뺀 모든 편이 벤허에게 희망을 모았고, 드러내 놓고 그 감정을 쏟아 냈다."]], [["“Ben-Hur! Ben-Hur!”", "\"벤허! 벤허!\""], ["they shouted, and the blent voices of the many rolled overwhelmingly against the consular stand.", "그들은 외쳤고, 수많은 사람의 어우러진 목소리가 집정관석을 향해 압도하듯 굽이쳐 갔다."]], [["From the benches above him as he passed, the favor descended in fierce injunctions.", "그가 지나갈 때, 그의 머리 위 관람석에서는 호의가 격렬한 당부의 말이 되어 쏟아져 내렸다."]], [["“Speed thee, Jew!”", "\"서둘러라, 유대인이여!\""]], [["“Take the wall now!”", "\"이제 안쪽 벽을 차지해라!\""]], [["“On!", "\"가라!"], ["loose the Arabs! Give them rein and scourge!”", "아랍 말들을 풀어 줘라! 고삐도 채찍도!\""]], [["“Let him not have the turn on thee again. Now or never!”", "\"저자에게 다시 반환점을 빼앗기지 마라. 지금이 아니면 영영 안 된다!\""]], [["Over the balustrade they stooped low, stretching their hands imploringly to him.", "그들은 난간 너머로 몸을 낮게 숙이고, 그를 향해 애원하듯 두 손을 뻗었다."]], [["Either he did not hear, or could not do better, for halfway round the course and he was still following; at the second goal even still no change!", "그가 듣지 못한 것인지, 아니면 그 이상 어쩔 수 없었던 것인지, 경주로를 절반이나 돌도록 그는 여전히 뒤따르고 있었다. 두 번째 반환점에 이르러서도 여전히 아무런 변화가 없었다!"]], [["And now, to make the turn, Messala began to draw in his left-hand steeds, an act which necessarily slackened their speed.", "그리고 이제, 반환점을 돌고자 메살라가 왼쪽 말들을 안으로 당기기 시작했으니, 이는 필연적으로 그들의 속력을 늦추는 행위였다."], ["His spirit was high; more than one altar was richer of his vows; the Roman genius was still president.", "그의 기세는 드높았다. 한둘이 아닌 제단이 그의 봉헌으로 풍성해졌고, 로마의 수호신은 여전히 그를 주재하고 있었다."], ["On the three pillars only six hundred feet away were fame, increase of fortune, promotions, and a triumph ineffably sweetened by hate, all in store for him!", "600피트밖에 떨어지지 않은 세 기둥 위에는 명성과, 재산의 증식과, 승진과, 증오로 형언할 수 없이 달콤해진 승리가, 모두 그를 위해 마련되어 있었다! 바로 그 순간, 회랑에 있던 말루크는 벤허가 자기의 아랍 말들 위로 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 고삐를 늦추어 주는 것을 보았다."], ["That moment Malluch, in the gallery, saw Ben-Hur lean forward over his Arabs, and give them the reins.", "그의 손에서 여러 겹으로 접힌 채찍이 튀어 나가, 놀란 말들의 등 위로 꿈틀거리며 쉭쉭 소리를 내고, 다시 쉭쉭거리며 거듭 꿈틀거렸다."], ["Out flew the many-folded lash in his hand; over the backs of the startled steeds it writhed and hissed, and hissed and writhed again and again; and though it fell not, there were both sting and menace in its quick report; and as the man passed thus from quiet to resistless action, his face suffused, his eyes gleaming, along the reins he seemed to flash his will; and instantly not one, but the four as one, answered with a leap that landed them alongside the Roman’s car.", "그것이 살에 닿지는 않았으나, 그 잽싼 울림에는 따끔함과 위협이 함께 담겨 있었다. 그리고 그 사람이 이렇게 고요함에서 거스를 수 없는 행동으로 옮겨 갈 때, 그의 얼굴은 붉게 달아오르고 두 눈은 번득이며, 고삐를 따라 자기의 의지를 번개처럼 내쏘는 듯했다. 그러자 한 마리가 아니라 네 마리가 한 몸처럼, 단번에 도약하여 로마인의 전차와 나란히 들어섰다."], ["Messala, on the perilous edge of the goal, heard, but dared not look to see what the awakening portended.", "반환점의 아슬아슬한 가장자리에 있던 메살라는 그 소리를 들었으나, 그 깨어남이 무엇을 예고하는지 감히 돌아볼 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["From the people he received no sign.", "백성에게서 그는 아무런 신호도 받지 못했다."], ["Above the noises of the race there was but one voice, and that was Ben-Hur’s.", "경주의 온갖 소음 위로 오직 하나의 목소리만이 들렸으니, 그것은 벤허의 목소리였다."], ["In the old Aramaic, as the sheik himself, he called to the Arabs,", "그 옛 아람어로, 마치 족장이 그러하듯 그는 아랍 말들에게 외쳤다."]], [["“On, Atair!", "\"가자, 알타이르!"], ["On, Rigel!", "가자, 리겔!"], ["What, Antares!", "뭐냐, 안타레스!"], ["dost thou linger now?", "이제 와서 머뭇거리느냐?"], ["Good horse—oho, Aldebaran!", "착하지—이랴, 알데바란!"], ["I hear them singing in the tents.", "천막에서 그들이 노래하는 소리가 들린다."], ["I hear the children singing and the women—singing of the stars, of Atair, Antares, Rigel, Aldebaran, victory!—and the song will never end. Well done!", "아이들이 노래하고 여인들이 노래하는 소리가 들린다—별들의 노래, 알타이르와 안타레스와 리겔과 알데바란의 노래, 승리의 노래!—그리고 그 노래는 영영 그치지 않으리라. 잘했다!"], ["Home to-morrow, under the black tent—home!", "내일이면 집으로, 검은 천막 아래 집으로—집으로 간다!"], ["On, Antares!", "가자, 안타레스!"], ["The tribe is waiting for us, and the master is waiting! ’Tis done! ’tis done!", "부족이 우리를 기다리고, 주인이 우리를 기다린다! 해냈다! 해냈어! 하, 하!"], ["Ha, ha! We have overthrown the proud.", "우리가 저 오만한 자를 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["The hand that smote us is in the dust.", "우리를 친 그 손이 흙먼지 속에 처박혔다."], ["Ours the glory!", "영광은 우리 것이다!"], ["Ha, ha!—steady!", "하, 하!—천천히!"], ["The work is done—soho! Rest!”", "일은 끝났다—워워! 쉬어라!\""]], [["There had never been anything of the kind more simple; seldom anything so instantaneous.", "이보다 더 단순한 일은 일찍이 없었고, 이토록 순식간에 벌어진 일도 좀처럼 없었다."]], [["At the moment chosen for the dash, Messala was moving in a circle round the goal.", "돌진을 위해 택한 그 순간, 메살라는 반환점 둘레로 원을 그리며 돌고 있었다."], ["To pass him, Ben-Hur had to cross the track, and good strategy required the movement to be in a forward direction; that is, on a like circle limited to the least possible increase.", "그를 추월하려면 벤허는 진로를 가로질러야 했고, 좋은 전략은 그 움직임을 앞으로 향하게 할 것을 요구했다. 다시 말해, 늘어남이 가능한 한 가장 적은, 비슷한 원을 그려야 했다."], ["The thousands on the benches understood it all: they saw the signal given—the magnificent response; the four close outside Messala’s outer wheel; Ben-Hur’s inner wheel behind the other’s car—all this they saw.", "관람석의 수천 명이 그 모든 것을 알아차렸다. 그들은 신호가 떨어지는 것을—그 장엄한 응답을, 메살라의 바깥 바퀴 바로 옆에 바짝 붙은 네 마리를, 메살라의 전차 뒤편에 놓인 벤허의 안쪽 바퀴를—그 모든 것을 보았다."], ["Then they heard a crash loud enough to send a thrill through the Circus, and, quicker than thought, out over the course a spray of shining white and yellow flinders flew.", "그러더니 경기장에 전율을 일으킬 만큼 요란한 충돌음이 들렸고, 생각보다 빠르게 경주로 위로 번쩍이는 흰빛과 노란빛 파편이 물보라처럼 흩날렸다."], ["Down on its right side toppled the bed of the Roman’s chariot.", "로마인의 전차 차대가 오른쪽으로 기우뚱 쓰러졌다."], ["There was a rebound as of the axle hitting the hard earth; another and another; then the car went to pieces; and Messala, entangled in the reins, pitched forward headlong.", "차축이 단단한 땅에 부딪히는 듯한 반동이 일었고, 또 한 번, 또 한 번 그러더니, 전차는 산산조각으로 부서졌다. 그리고 고삐에 뒤엉킨 메살라가 머리부터 앞으로 곤두박질쳤다."]], [["To increase the horror of the sight by making death certain, the Sidonian, who had the wall next behind, could not stop or turn out.", "죽음을 확실하게 만들어 그 광경의 참혹함을 더하기라도 하듯, 바로 뒤 안쪽 벽을 차지하고 있던 시돈 사람은 멈추지도 비켜 가지도 못했다."], ["Into the wreck full speed he drove; then over the Roman, and into the latter’s four, all mad with fear.", "그는 전속력으로 그 잔해 속으로 돌진했고, 이어 로마인을 타고 넘어, 두려움에 미쳐 날뛰는 그자의 네 마리 속으로 처박혔다."], ["Presently, out of the turmoil, the fighting of horses, the resound of blows, the murky cloud of dust and sand, he crawled, in time to see the Corinthian and Byzantine go on down the course after Ben-Hur, who had not been an instant delayed.", "이윽고 그 아수라장에서, 말들이 뒤엉켜 몸부림치고, 부딪치는 소리가 울려 퍼지고, 흙먼지와 모래가 자욱이 피어오르는 가운데에서, 그는 기어 나왔다. 코린토스 사람과 비잔티움 사람이 한순간도 지체하지 않은 벤허를 뒤쫓아 경주로를 따라 내달리는 것을 때맞춰 보면서였다."]], [["The people arose, and leaped upon the benches, and shouted and screamed.", "백성이 일어나, 관람석 위로 뛰어올라, 고함치고 비명을 질렀다."], ["Those who looked that way caught glimpses of Messala, now under the trampling of the fours, now under the abandoned cars.", "그쪽을 바라본 자들은 메살라를 흘끗 보았으니, 어느 때는 네 마리의 짓밟힘 아래, 어느 때는 버려진 전차들 아래 깔린 모습이었다."], ["He was still; they thought him dead; but far the greater number followed Ben-Hur in his career.", "그는 미동도 없었다. 사람들은 그가 죽었다고 여겼다. 그러나 훨씬 더 많은 수는 질주하는 벤허를 좇았다."], ["They had not seen the cunning touch of the reins by which, turning a little to the left, he caught Messala’s wheel with the iron-shod point of his axle, and crushed it; but they had seen the transformation of the man, and themselves felt the heat and glow of his spirit, the heroic resolution, the maddening energy of action with which, by look, word, and gesture, he so suddenly inspired his Arabs.", "그들은 벤허가 고삐를 살짝 왼쪽으로 트는 그 교묘한 손놀림으로, 쇠를 두른 차축 끝으로 메살라의 바퀴를 걸어 부숴 버린 것은 보지 못했다. 그러나 그 사람의 변모는 보았고, 그가 눈빛과 말과 몸짓으로 그토록 갑작스레 자기의 아랍 말들에게 불어넣은 그 영혼의 열기와 광채를, 그 영웅적인 결의를, 그 미칠 듯한 행동의 기운을 스스로도 느꼈다."], ["And such running!", "그리고 그 질주라니!"], ["It was rather the long leaping of lions in harness; but for the lumbering chariot, it seemed the four were flying.", "그것은 차라리 멍에를 멘 사자들의 길고 긴 도약과도 같았다. 굼뜬 전차만 아니었다면 네 마리는 날고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["When the Byzantine and Corinthian were halfway down the course, Ben-Hur turned the first goal.", "비잔티움 사람과 코린토스 사람이 경주로의 절반쯤에 이르렀을 때, 벤허는 첫 번째 반환점을 돌았다."]], [["# AND THE RACE WAS WON!", "# AND THE RACE WAS WON!"]], [["The consul arose; the people shouted themselves hoarse; the editor came down from his seat, and crowned the victors.", "경주는 이렇게 끝났다! 집정관이 일어섰다. 백성은 목이 쉬도록 외쳤다. 주최자는 자기 자리에서 내려와 승자들에게 관을 씌웠다."]], [["The fortunate man among the boxers was a low-browed, yellow-haired Saxon, of such brutalized face as to attract a second look from Ben-Hur, who recognized a teacher with whom he himself had been a favorite at Rome.", "권투 시합에서 운 좋게 이긴 자는 이마가 낮고 머리칼이 노란 색슨 사람으로, 그 짐승 같은 얼굴이 어찌나 험상궂은지 벤허가 두 번 돌아보게 만들었으니, 그는 자기가 로마에서 총애받던 시절의 한 스승을 알아보았다."], ["From him the young Jew looked up and beheld Simonides and his party on the balcony.", "그에게서 눈을 들어 젊은 유대인은 발코니 위의 시모니데스 일행을 바라보았다."], ["They waved their hands to him.", "그들은 그를 향해 손을 흔들었다."], ["Esther kept her seat; but Iras arose, and gave him a smile and a wave of her fan—favors not the less intoxicating to him because we know, O reader, they would have fallen to Messala had he been the victor.", "에스더는 자리를 지켰으나, 이라스는 일어서서 그에게 미소를 보내고 부채를 흔들어 보였다—그 호의는, 독자여, 메살라가 승자였다면 그에게로 돌아갔으리라는 것을 우리가 안다 한들, 그렇다고 그에게 덜 도취적인 것은 아니었다."]], [["The procession was then formed, and, midst the shouting of the multitude which had had its will, passed out of the Gate of Triumph.", "이윽고 행렬이 이루어졌고, 제 뜻을 다 이룬 군중의 함성 한가운데를 지나, 개선문 밖으로 빠져나갔다."]], [["And the day was over.", "그리고 그날은 저물었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV", "# CHAPTER XV"]], [["Ben-Hur tarried across the river with Ilderim; for at midnight, as previously determined, they would take the road which the caravan, then thirty hours out, had pursued.", "벤허는 일데림과 함께 강 건너에 머물렀다. 자정이 되면, 미리 정한 대로, 서른 시간 전에 떠난 대상(隊商)이 밟아 간 길을 따라나설 것이기 때문이었다."]], [["The sheik was happy; his offers of gifts had been royal; but Ben-Hur had refused everything, insisting that he was satisfied with the humiliation of his enemy.", "족장은 행복했다. 그가 내놓은 선물은 임금에게 어울릴 만큼 후했으나, 벤허는 모든 것을 사양하며, 원수를 욕보인 것만으로 만족한다고 거듭 말했다."], ["The generous dispute was long continued.", "너그러운 실랑이는 오래 이어졌다."]], [["“Think,”", "\"생각해 보게.\""], ["the sheik would say, “what thou hast done for me.", "족장이 말했다. \"자네가 나를 위해 무슨 일을 했는지를."], ["In every black tent down to the Akaba and to the ocean, and across to the Euphrates, and beyond to the sea of the Scythians, the renown of my Mira and her children will go; and they who sing of them will magnify me, and forget that I am in the wane of life; and all the spears now masterless will come to me, and my sword-hands multiply past counting.", "아카바와 대양에 이르기까지, 또 유프라테스를 건너 그 너머 스키타이인의 바다에 이르기까지, 검은 천막마다 나의 미라와 그 새끼들의 명성이 퍼져 나갈 걸세. 그들을 노래하는 자들이 나를 드높여, 내가 인생의 황혼에 접어들었음을 잊을 걸세. 이제 주인 없는 모든 창들이 나에게로 모여들 것이고, 내 칼잡이들은 셀 수 없을 만큼 불어날 걸세."], ["Thou dost not know what it is to have sway of the desert such as will now be mine.", "자네는 이제 내 것이 될 그런 사막의 지배가 어떤 것인지 모르네."], ["I tell thee it will bring tribute incalculable from commerce, and immunity from kings.", "그것이 교역에서 헤아릴 수 없는 공물을 가져다주고, 왕들로부터의 면제를 안겨 주리라고 내 장담하지."], ["Ay, by the sword of Solomon!", "그렇고말고, 솔로몬의 칼을 두고 맹세하건대!"], ["doth my messenger seek favor for me of Cæsar, that will he get. Yet nothing—nothing?”", "내 사자(使者)가 카이사르에게 나를 위한 호의를 구한다면, 그것마저 얻을 걸세. 그런데도 아무것도, 아무것도 받지 않겠다는 건가?\""]], [["And Ben-Hur would answer,", "그러면 벤허는 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Nay, sheik, have I not thy hand and heart?", "\"아닙니다, 족장님. 제가 족장님의 손과 마음을 얻지 않았습니까?"], ["Let thy increase of power and influence inure to the King who comes.", "족장님의 늘어난 권세와 영향력은 장차 오실 왕을 위해 쓰이게 하십시오."], ["Who shall say it was not allowed thee for him?", "그것이 그분을 위해 족장님께 허락된 것이 아니라고 누가 말하겠습니까?"], ["In the work I am going to, I may have great need.", "제가 이제부터 하려는 일에서, 저는 큰 도움이 필요할지도 모릅니다."], ["Saying no now will leave me to ask of thee with better grace hereafter.”", "지금 사양해 두면, 훗날 더 떳떳하게 족장님께 청할 수 있을 것입니다.\""]], [["In the midst of a controversy of the kind, two messengers arrived—Malluch and one unknown.", "이런 다툼이 한창일 때, 두 사람의 사자가 도착했다. 말루크와 알지 못하는 한 사람이었다."], ["The former was admitted first.", "먼저 말루크가 안으로 들었다."]], [["The good fellow did not attempt to hide his joy over the event of the day.", "이 선량한 사내는 그날의 사건을 두고 솟구치는 기쁨을 숨기려 하지 않았다."]], [["“But, coming to that with which I am charged,”", "\"하지만 제게 맡겨진 일로 말씀드리자면,\""], ["he said, “the master Simonides sends me to say that, upon the adjournment of the games, some of the Roman faction made haste to protest against payment of the money prize.”", "그가 말했다. \"시모니데스 어른께서 저를 보내어 전하시기를, 경기가 끝나자 로마 일파의 몇몇이 서둘러 상금 지급에 항의를 제기했다고 합니다.\""]], [["Ilderim started up, crying, in his shrillest tones,", "일데림이 벌떡 일어나, 더없이 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“By the splendor of God!", "\"하느님의 영광을 두고!"], ["the East shall decide whether the race was fairly won.”", "그 경주가 정정당당하게 이긴 것인지는 동방이 판가름할 것이다.\""]], [["“Nay, good sheik,”", "\"아닙니다, 족장님.\""], ["said Malluch, “the editor has paid the money.”", "말루크가 말했다. \"주최자가 이미 상금을 지급했습니다.\""]], [["“’Tis well.”", "\"그것 참 잘되었군.\""]], [["“When they said Ben-Hur struck Messala’s wheel, the editor laughed, and reminded them of the blow the Arabs had at the turn of the goal.”", "\"벤허가 메살라의 바퀴를 들이받았다고 그들이 말하자, 주최자는 웃으며 반환점에서 아랍 말들이 당한 충돌을 그들에게 일깨워 주었습니다.\""]], [["“And what of the Athenian?”", "\"그래, 그 아테네 사람은 어찌 되었나?\""]], [["“He is dead.”", "\"죽었습니다.\""]], [["“Dead!” cried Ben-Hur.", "\"죽다니!\" 벤허가 외쳤다."]], [["“Dead!”", "\"죽다니!\""], ["echoed Ilderim.", "일데림이 따라 외쳤다."], ["“What fortune these Roman monsters have!", "\"이 로마의 괴물들은 무슨 운을 타고났단 말인가!"], ["Messala escaped?”", "메살라는 살아남았느냐?\""]], [["“Escaped—yes, O sheik, with life; but it shall be a burden to him.", "\"살아남았습니다. 그렇습니다, 족장님, 목숨만은 부지했습니다. 그러나 그것이 그에게는 짐이 될 것입니다."], ["The physicians say he will live, but never walk again.”", "의원들은 그가 살기는 하겠으나 다시는 걷지 못하리라고 합니다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur looked silently up to heaven.", "벤허는 말없이 하늘을 우러러보았다."], ["He had a vision of Messala, chairbound like Simonides, and, like him, going abroad on the shoulders of servants.", "그의 눈앞에는 시모니데스처럼 의자에 묶인 채, 그처럼 하인들의 어깨에 실려 바깥을 다니는 메살라의 모습이 떠올랐다."], ["The good man had abode well; but what would this one with his pride and ambition?", "그 선량한 사람은 그 처지를 잘 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 자존심과 야망으로 가득한 이 사람은 어떻게 견딜 것인가?"]], [["“Simonides bade me say, further,”", "\"시모니데스 어른께서 더 전하라 하셨습니다.\""], ["Malluch continued, “Sanballat is having trouble.", "말루크가 말을 이었다. \"산발랏이 곤경에 처했습니다."], ["Drusus, and those who signed with him, referred the question of paying the five talents they lost to the Consul Maxentius, and he has referred it to Cæsar.", "드루수스와 그와 함께 서명한 자들은 자기들이 잃은 다섯 탈란트를 지급하는 문제를 집정관 막센티우스에게 넘겼고, 막센티우스는 그것을 카이사르에게 넘겼습니다."], ["Messala also refused his losses, and Sanballat, in imitation of Drusus, went to the consul, where the matter is still in advisement.", "메살라 역시 자기 패전금 지급을 거부했고, 산발랏은 드루수스를 본받아 집정관에게 갔으나, 그 문제는 아직 검토 중입니다."], ["The better Romans say the protestants shall not be excused; and all the adverse factions join with them.", "점잖은 로마인들은 항의한 자들을 봐줘서는 안 된다고 말하며, 적대하는 모든 일파가 그들과 한편이 되고 있습니다."], ["The city rings with the scandal.”", "도시가 그 추문으로 떠들썩합니다.\""]], [["“What says Simonides?” asked Ben-Hur.", "\"시모니데스 어른은 뭐라 하시던가?\" 벤허가 물었다."]], [["“The master laughs, and is well pleased.", "\"어른께서는 웃으시며 무척 흡족해하십니다."], ["If the Roman pays, he is ruined; if he refuses to pay, he is dishonored.", "로마인이 돈을 치르면 그는 파산이요, 치르기를 거부하면 그는 불명예입니다."], ["The imperial policy will decide the matter.", "황제의 정책이 그 문제를 판가름할 것입니다."], ["To offend the East would be a bad beginning with the Parthians; to offend Sheik Ilderim would be to antagonize the Desert, over which lie all Maxentius’s lines of operation.", "동방을 노엽게 하는 것은 파르티아인과의 관계에서 좋지 못한 시작이 될 것이요, 일데림 족장님을 노엽게 하는 것은 사막을 적으로 돌리는 일이니, 막센티우스의 모든 군사 작전선이 그 사막을 가로지르고 있습니다."], ["Wherefore Simonides bade me tell you to have no disquiet; Messala will pay.”", "그러므로 시모니데스 어른께서는 조금도 마음 졸이지 말라 전하라 하셨습니다. 메살라는 돈을 치를 것입니다.\""]], [["Ilderim was at once restored to his good-humor.", "일데림은 곧바로 명랑한 기분을 되찾았다."]], [["“Let us be off now,”", "\"이제 그만 떠나세.\""], ["he said, rubbing his hands.", "그가 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["“The business will do well with Simonides.", "\"그 일은 시모니데스가 잘 처리할 걸세."], ["The glory is ours.", "영광은 우리 것이지."], ["I will order the horses.”", "내 말들을 준비시키겠네.\""]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐만요.\""], ["said Malluch.", "말루크가 말했다."], ["“I left a messenger outside.", "\"제가 밖에 사자 하나를 남겨 두었습니다."], ["Will you see him?”", "만나 보시겠습니까?\""]], [["“By the splendor of God! I forgot him.”", "\"하느님의 영광을 두고! 내가 그를 깜빡 잊었구먼.\""]], [["Malluch retired, and was succeeded by a lad of gentle manners and delicate appearance, who knelt upon one knee, and said, winningly, “Iras, the daughter of Balthasar, well known to good Sheik Ilderim, hath intrusted me with a message to the sheik, who, she saith, will do her great favor so he receive her congratulations on account of the victory of his four.”", "말루크가 물러가고, 그 뒤를 이어 점잖은 몸가짐에 고운 용모를 지닌 한 소년이 들어와, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 상냥하게 말했다. \"발타사르의 따님이시며, 선량한 일데림 족장님께서 잘 아시는 이라스 님께서 저에게 족장님께 전할 말씀을 맡기셨습니다. 따님께서 말씀하시기를, 족장님께서 사두마차의 승리를 두고 따님의 축하를 받아 주신다면 따님에게 크나큰 은혜를 베푸시는 것이라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“The daughter of my friend is kind,”", "\"내 벗의 딸은 친절하구나.\""], ["said Ilderim, with sparkling eyes.", "일데림이 눈을 반짝이며 말했다."], ["“Do thou give her this jewel, in sign of the pleasure I have from her message.”", "\"이 보석을 그 아가씨에게 전해 다오. 그 말에 내가 느낀 기쁨의 표시로다.\""]], [["He took a ring from his finger as he spoke.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 손가락에서 반지를 빼냈다."]], [["“I will as thou sayest, O sheik,”", "\"분부대로 하겠습니다, 족장님.\""], ["the lad replied, and continued, “The daughter of the Egyptian charged me further.", "소년이 대답하고는 말을 이었다. \"이집트 여인의 따님께서 저에게 한 가지 더 당부하셨습니다."], ["She prays the good Sheik Ilderim to send word to the youth Ben-Hur that her father hath taken residence for a time in the palace of Idernee, where she will receive the youth after the fourth hour to-morrow.", "따님께서는 선량한 일데림 족장님께서 청년 벤허에게 전갈을 보내 주시기를 청하십니다. 따님의 아버님께서 한동안 이데르네 궁전에 거처를 정하셨으니, 따님께서 내일 넷째 시가 지나서 그 청년을 맞이하시겠다는 것입니다."], ["And if, with her congratulations, Sheik Ilderim will accept her gratitude for this other favor done, she will be ever so pleased.”", "그리고 따님의 축하와 더불어, 베풀어 주신 이 또 다른 은혜에 대한 감사를 일데림 족장님께서 받아 주신다면, 따님께서는 더없이 기뻐하실 것입니다.\""]], [["The sheik looked at Ben-Hur, whose face was suffused with pleasure.", "족장은 기쁨으로 얼굴이 환해진 벤허를 바라보았다."]], [["“What will you?” he asked.", "\"어찌하겠나?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“By your leave, O sheik, I will see the fair Egyptian.”", "\"허락해 주신다면, 족장님, 저는 그 아리따운 이집트 여인을 만나 보겠습니다.\""]], [["Ilderim laughed, and said, “Shall not a man enjoy his youth?”", "일데림이 웃으며 말했다. \"사내가 어찌 제 젊음을 누리지 않을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["Then Ben-Hur answered the messenger.", "그러자 벤허가 사자에게 대답했다."]], [["“Say to her who sent you that I, Ben-Hur, will see her at the palace of Idernee, wherever that may be, to-morrow at noon.”", "\"너를 보낸 분께 전하여라. 나, 벤허는 이데르네 궁전이 어디에 있든, 내일 정오에 그곳에서 그분을 뵙겠노라고.\""]], [["The lad arose, and, with silent salute, departed.", "소년이 일어나, 말없이 인사를 올리고 떠났다."]], [["At midnight Ilderim took the road, having arranged to leave a horse and a guide for Ben-Hur, who was to follow him.", "자정이 되자 일데림이 길에 올랐다. 자기를 뒤따라올 벤허를 위해 말 한 필과 길잡이 한 사람을 남겨 두기로 해 둔 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI", "# CHAPTER XVI"]], [["Going next day to fill his appointment with Iras, Ben-Hur turned from the Omphalus, which was in the heart of the city, into the Colonnade of Herod, and came shortly to the palace of Idernee.", "이튿날 이라스와의 약속을 지키러 가는 길에, 벤허는 도시 한복판에 있는 옴팔로스에서 헤롯의 주랑(柱廊)으로 접어들어, 이내 이데르네 궁에 다다랐다."]], [["From the street he passed first into a vestibule, on the sides of which were stairways under cover, leading up to a portico.", "거리에서 그는 먼저 현관으로 들어섰는데, 그 양옆에는 지붕이 덮인 계단이 있어 위쪽 주랑 현관으로 이어졌다."], ["Winged lions sat by the stairs; in the middle there was a gigantic ibis spouting water over the floor; the lions, ibis, walls, and floor were reminders of the Egyptians: everything, even the balustrading of the stairs, was of massive gray stone.", "계단 곁에는 날개 달린 사자들이 앉아 있었고, 한가운데에는 거대한 따오기 상이 바닥 위로 물을 뿜고 있었다. 사자, 따오기, 벽, 바닥은 모두 이집트를 떠올리게 했으니, 계단의 난간조차도 무엇 하나 빠짐없이 육중한 잿빛 돌로 되어 있었다."]], [["Above the vestibule, and covering the landing of the steps, arose the portico, a pillared grace, so light, so exquisitely proportioned, it was at that period hardly possible of conception except by a Greek.", "현관 위, 계단 층계참을 덮으며 주랑 현관이 솟아 있었다. 그것은 기둥으로 이루어진 우아한 구조물로, 어찌나 가볍고 어찌나 절묘하게 균형이 잡혀 있는지, 그 시대에는 그리스인이 아니고서는 좀처럼 떠올리기조차 어려운 것이었다."], ["Of marble snowy white, its effect was that of a lily dropped carelessly upon a great bare rock.", "눈처럼 새하얀 대리석으로 된 그것은, 마치 거대한 헐벗은 바위 위에 무심히 떨어진 백합 한 송이 같은 인상을 주었다."]], [["Ben-Hur paused in the shade of the portico to admire its tracery and finish, and the purity of its marble; then he passed on into the palace.", "벤허는 주랑 현관의 그늘에 멈춰 서서 그 무늬와 마감, 그리고 대리석의 순백을 감상했다. 그러고 나서 그는 궁 안으로 들어섰다."], ["Ample folding-doors stood open to receive him.", "널찍한 접이문이 활짝 열린 채 그를 맞이했다."], ["The passage into which he first entered was high, but somewhat narrow; red tiling formed the floor, and the walls were tinted to correspond.", "그가 처음 들어선 통로는 높았으나 다소 좁았다. 바닥은 붉은 타일로 깔려 있었고, 벽도 그에 맞추어 같은 빛으로 물들여져 있었다."], ["Yet this plainness was a warning of something beautiful to come.", "그러나 이 소박함은 곧 다가올 어떤 아름다움을 예고하는 것이었다."]], [["He moved on slowly, all his faculties in repose.", "그는 천천히 걸음을 옮겼고, 온갖 감각은 차분히 가라앉아 있었다."], ["Presently he would be in the presence of Iras; she was waiting for him; waiting with song and story and badinage, sparkling, fanciful, capricious—with smiles which glorified her glance, and glances which lent voluptuous suggestion to her whisper.", "머지않아 그는 이라스 앞에 서게 될 터였다. 그녀가 그를 기다리고 있었다. 노래와 이야기와 우스갯소리로, 반짝이고 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없이 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["She had sent for him the evening of the boat-ride on the lake in the Orchard of Palms; she had sent for him now; and he was going to her in the beautiful palace of Idernee.", "그녀의 눈길을 빛내는 미소와, 그 속삭임에 관능의 여운을 더해 주는 눈길로. 종려나무 과수원의 호수에서 뱃놀이를 하던 그날 저녁에도 그녀는 그를 불렀고, 지금도 그를 불렀다. 그리하여 그는 아름다운 이데르네 궁으로 그녀를 찾아가고 있었다."], ["He was happy and dreamful rather than thoughtless.", "그는 생각이 없다기보다, 행복하고 꿈결 같은 기분이었다."]], [["The passage brought him to a closed door, in front of which he paused; and, as he did so, the broad leaves began to open of themselves, without creak or sound of lock or latch, or touch of foot or finger.", "통로는 그를 닫힌 문 앞으로 데려갔고, 그는 그 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그러자 그 넓은 문짝들이 삐걱 소리도, 자물쇠나 빗장 소리도, 발이나 손가락의 닿음도 없이 저절로 열리기 시작했다."], ["The singularity was lost in the view that broke upon him.", "그 기이함은 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경 속에 묻혀 버렸다."]], [["Standing in the shade of the dull passage, and looking through the doorway, he beheld the atrium of a Roman house, roomy and rich to a fabulous degree of magnificence.", "침침한 통로의 그늘에 서서 문간 너머를 바라보며, 그는 로마식 저택의 아트리움을 보았다. 널찍하고, 호화롭기가 그야말로 꿈결 같은 장려함에 이르러 있었다."]], [["How large the chamber was cannot be stated, because of the deceit there is in exact proportions; its depth was vista-like, something never to be said of an equal interior.", "그 방이 얼마나 컸는지는 말할 수 없으니, 정확한 비례에는 사람을 속이는 데가 있기 때문이다. 그 깊이는 끝없이 펼쳐진 풍경 같았으니, 같은 넓이의 실내를 두고는 결코 할 수 없는 말이었다."], ["When he stopped to make survey, and looked down upon the floor, he was standing upon the breast of a Leda, represented as caressing a swan; and, looking farther, he saw the whole floor was similarly laid in mosaic pictures of mythological subjects.", "그가 걸음을 멈추고 둘러보며 바닥을 내려다보았을 때, 그는 백조를 어루만지는 모습으로 그려진 레다의 가슴 위에 서 있었다. 그리고 더 멀리 보니, 바닥 전체가 똑같이 신화를 소재로 한 모자이크 그림으로 깔려 있었다."], ["And there were stools and chairs, each a separate design, and a work of art exquisitely composed, and tables much carven, and here and there couches which were invitations of themselves.", "또 걸상과 의자가 있었는데, 저마다 다른 도안에, 절묘하게 짜인 예술품이었다. 정교하게 새긴 탁자들이 있었고, 여기저기에는 그 자체로 사람을 청하는 듯한 긴 의자들이 놓여 있었다."], ["The articles of furniture, which stood out from the walls, were duplicated on the floor distinctly as if they floated unrippled water; even the panelling of the walls, the figures upon them in painting and bas-relief, and the fresco of the ceiling were reflected on the floor.", "벽에서 도드라져 나온 가구들은, 마치 잔물결 하나 없는 물 위에 떠 있기라도 한 듯, 바닥에 또렷이 한 번 더 비쳤다. 벽의 판벽, 그 위에 그려지고 돋을새김된 형상들, 천장의 벽화까지도 바닥에 그대로 비쳤다."], ["The ceiling curved up towards the centre, where there was an opening through which the sunlight poured without hindrance, and the sky, ever so blue, seemed in hand-reach; the impluvium under the opening was guarded by bronzed rails; the gilded pillars supporting the roof at the edges of the opening shone like flame where the sun struck them, and their reflections beneath seemed to stretch to infinite depth.", "천장은 한가운데를 향해 휘어 올라갔고, 그곳에 난 구멍으로는 햇빛이 거침없이 쏟아져 들어왔으며, 더없이 푸른 하늘은 손에 닿을 듯했다. 그 구멍 아래의 임플루비움은 청동 난간으로 둘러쳐져 있었다. 구멍 가장자리에서 지붕을 떠받친 금빛 기둥들은 햇빛이 닿는 곳마다 불꽃처럼 빛났고, 그 아래 비친 모습은 끝없는 깊이로 뻗어 내려가는 듯했다."], ["And there were candelabra quaint and curious, and statuary and vases; the whole making an interior that would have befitted well the house on the Palatine Hill which Cicero bought of Crassus, or that other, yet more famous for extravagance, the Tusculan villa of Scaurus.", "진기하고 기묘한 촛대들이 있었고, 조각상과 꽃병들이 있었다. 그 모든 것이 어우러져, 키케로가 크라수스에게서 사들인 팔라티누스 언덕의 저택에도, 또는 사치로 그보다 더 이름난 스카우루스의 투스쿨룸 별장에도 능히 어울릴 만한 실내를 이루고 있었다."]], [["Still in his dreamful mood, Ben-Hur sauntered about, charmed by all he beheld, and waiting.", "여전히 꿈결 같은 기분에 잠긴 채, 벤허는 눈에 보이는 모든 것에 매혹되어 거닐며 기다렸다."], ["He did not mind a little delay; when Iras was ready, she would come or send a servant.", "조금 늦어지는 것쯤은 개의치 않았다. 이라스가 채비를 마치면, 그녀가 오거나 하인을 보낼 터였다."], ["In every well-regulated Roman house the atrium was the reception chamber for visitors.", "잘 갖추어진 로마식 저택이라면 어디서나 아트리움은 손님을 맞이하는 방이었다."]], [["Twice, thrice, he made the round.", "그는 두 번, 세 번 방을 빙 돌았다."], ["As often he stood under the opening in the roof, and pondered the sky and its azure depth; then, leaning against a pillar, he studied the distribution of light and shade, and its effects; here a veil diminishing objects, there a brilliance exaggerating others; yet nobody came.", "그때마다 지붕의 구멍 아래 서서, 하늘과 그 짙푸른 깊이를 헤아렸다. 그러고는 기둥에 몸을 기대어, 빛과 그늘이 어떻게 나뉘는지, 그 효과가 어떠한지 살폈다. 여기서는 베일처럼 사물을 작아 보이게 하고, 저기서는 광채로 다른 것들을 부풀려 보이게 했다."], ["Time, or rather the passage of time, began at length to impress itself upon him, and he wondered why Iras stayed so long.", "그런데도 아무도 오지 않았다. 시간이, 아니 그보다도 시간의 흐름이 마침내 그에게 와닿기 시작했고, 그는 이라스가 왜 이리 오래 머무는지 의아해졌다. 그는 다시 바닥의 형상들을 더듬어 보았으나, 처음 보았을 때의 만족감은 없었다."], ["Again he traced out the figures upon the floor, but not with the satisfaction the first inspection gave him.", "그는 자주 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다. 곧 조바심이 그의 마음에 약간 달뜬 입김을 불어넣었고, 다음번에는 더 세고 더 뜨겁게 불었다."], ["He paused often to listen: directly impatience blew a little fevered breath upon his spirit; next time it blew stronger and hotter; and at last he woke to a consciousness of the silence which held the house in thrall, and the thought of it made him uneasy and distrustful.", "그러다 마침내 그는 저택을 옭아맨 정적을 깨닫게 되었고, 그 생각이 그를 불안하고 미심쩍게 만들었다. 그래도 그는 미소와 다짐으로 그 느낌을 밀어냈다. \"아, 그녀가 눈가에 마지막 단장을 하고 있거나, 나를 위해 화관을 엮고 있는 게로군."], ["Still he put the feeling off with a smile and a promise.", "곧 올 거야, 늦어진 만큼 더 아름다워져서!\""], ["“Oh, she is giving the last touch to her eyelids, or she is arranging a chaplet for me; she will come presently, more beautiful of the delay!”", "그러고 나서 그는 자리에 앉아 촛대 하나를 감상했다. 굴림대 위에 얹힌 청동 받침대로, 옆면과 가장자리에 가는 세공이 새겨져 있었다. 한쪽 끝에는 기둥이, 그 맞은편 끝에는 제단과 여자 사제가 있었다."], ["He sat down then to admire a candelabrum—a bronze plinth on rollers, filigree on the sides and edges; the post at one end, and on the end opposite it an altar and a female celebrant; the lamp-rests swinging by delicate chains from the extremities of drooping palm-branches; altogether a wonder in its way.", "늘어진 종려나무 가지의 끝마다 가느다란 사슬로 등잔 받침이 매달려 흔들렸다. 그것은 그 나름으로 더없는 경이였다. 그러나 정적이 자꾸만 끼어들었다."], ["But the silence would obtrude itself: he listened even as he looked at the pretty object—he listened, but there was not a sound; the palace was still as a tomb.", "그는 그 어여쁜 물건을 바라보면서도 귀를 기울였다. 귀를 기울였으나, 아무 소리도 없었다. 궁은 무덤처럼 고요했다."]], [["There might be a mistake. No, the messenger had come from the Egyptian, and this was the palace of Idernee.", "무언가 착오가 있었을 수도 있었다. 아니, 이집트 여인이 보낸 사람이 왔었고, 이곳은 이데르네 궁이었다."], ["Then he remembered how mysteriously the door had opened so soundlessly, so of itself.", "그러자 그는 그 문이 얼마나 신비롭게, 소리도 없이, 저절로 열렸던가를 떠올렸다."], ["He would see!", "어디 한번 보자!"]], [["He went to the same door.", "그는 같은 문으로 갔다."], ["Though he walked ever so lightly the sound of his stepping was loud and harsh, and he shrank from it.", "아무리 가볍게 걸어도 그 발소리는 크고 거슬렸고, 그는 그 소리에 움찔했다."], ["He was getting nervous.", "그는 신경이 곤두서고 있었다."], ["The cumbrous Roman lock resisted his first effort to raise it; and the second—the blood chilled in his cheeks—he wrenched with all his might: in vain—the door was not even shaken.", "육중한 로마식 자물쇠는 그가 처음 들어 올리려는 시도를 버텨 냈다. 두 번째에는—그의 뺨에서 피가 싸늘하게 식었다—그가 온 힘을 다해 비틀어 보았으나, 헛일이었다. 문은 흔들리지도 않았다."], ["A sense of danger seized him, and for a moment he stood irresolute.", "위험에 대한 감각이 그를 사로잡았고, 그는 한순간 어찌할 바를 모르고 서 있었다."]], [["Who in Antioch had the motive to do him harm?", "안티오크에서 그를 해치려는 동기를 가진 자가 누구인가?"]], [["Messala!", "메살라!"]], [["And this palace of Idernee?", "그렇다면 이 이데르네 궁은?"], ["He had seen Egypt in the vestibule, Athens in the snowy portico; but here, in the atrium, was Rome; everything about him betrayed Roman ownership.", "그는 현관에서 이집트를 보았고, 새하얀 주랑 현관에서 아테네를 보았다. 그러나 이곳, 아트리움에서는 로마가 있었다. 그를 둘러싼 모든 것이 로마인의 소유임을 드러냈다."], ["True, the site was on the great thoroughfare of the city, a very public place in which to do him violence; but for that reason it was more accordant with the audacious genius of his enemy.", "과연 그 자리는 도시의 큰 길목에 있어, 그에게 폭력을 휘두르기에는 지나치게 사람들의 눈에 띄는 곳이었다. 그러나 바로 그렇기에, 그것은 그의 적이 지닌 대담무쌍한 기질에 더욱 들어맞았다."], ["The atrium underwent a change; with all its elegance and beauty, it was no more than a trap.", "아트리움은 모습을 바꾸었다. 그 모든 우아함과 아름다움에도, 그것은 함정에 지나지 않았다."], ["Apprehension always paints in black.", "불안은 언제나 모든 것을 검게 칠한다."]], [["The idea irritated Ben-Hur.", "그 생각이 벤허를 격앙시켰다."]], [["There were many doors on the right and left of the atrium, leading, doubtless, to sleeping-chambers; he tried them, but they were all firmly fastened.", "아트리움의 좌우에는 문이 여럿 있었는데, 틀림없이 침실로 통하는 것이었다. 그는 그것들을 밀어 보았으나, 모두 단단히 잠겨 있었다."], ["Knocking might bring response.", "두드리면 응답이 올지도 몰랐다."], ["Ashamed to make outcry, he betook himself to a couch, and, lying down, tried to reflect.", "소리 지르기가 부끄러워, 그는 긴 의자로 가서 누운 채 곰곰이 생각해 보려 했다."]], [["All too plainly he was a prisoner; but for what purpose?", "너무도 분명하게, 그는 갇힌 몸이었다. 그러나 무엇 때문에?"], ["and by whom?", "그리고 누구의 손에?"]], [["If the work were Messala’s!", "만일 이것이 메살라의 짓이라면!"], ["He sat up, looked about, and smiled defiantly. There were weapons in every table.", "그는 일어나 앉아 사방을 둘러보고는, 도전하듯 미소 지었다. 탁자마다 무기가 있었다."], ["But birds had been starved in golden cages; not so would he—the couches would serve him as battering-rams; and he was strong, and there was such increase of might in rage and despair!", "새들이 황금 새장 속에서 굶어 죽기도 했다지만, 그는 그렇게 되지 않을 터였다. 긴 의자들이 그에게는 성문을 부수는 망치가 되어 줄 것이었다. 그는 힘이 셌고, 분노와 절망 속에서 그 힘은 한껏 부풀어 올랐다!"]], [["Messala himself could not come.", "메살라가 직접 올 수는 없었다."], ["He would never walk again; he was a cripple like Simonides; still he could move others.", "그는 두 번 다시 걷지 못할 것이었다. 시모니데스처럼 불구가 된 몸이었다. 그래도 그는 다른 자들을 움직일 수 있었다."], ["And where were there not others to be moved by him?", "그가 움직일 수 있는 자들이 어디엔들 없겠는가?"], ["Ben-Hur arose, and tried the doors again.", "벤허는 일어나 다시 문들을 밀어 보았다."], ["Once he called out; the room echoed so that he was startled.", "한번은 소리쳐 불렀는데, 방이 어찌나 메아리치는지 그 자신이 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["With such calmness as he could assume, he made up his mind to wait a time before attempting to break a way out.", "그는 애써 침착함을 가장하며, 빠져나갈 길을 부수어 내기 전에 한동안 기다려 보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["In such a situation the mind has its ebb and flow of disquiet, with intervals of peace between.", "그런 처지에 놓이면 마음은 불안의 밀물과 썰물을 겪으며, 그 사이사이에 평온의 틈이 끼어든다."], ["At length—how long, though, he could not have said—he came to the conclusion that the affair was an accident or mistake.", "마침내—그것이 얼마나 오래였는지는 그 자신도 말할 수 없었으나—그는 이 일이 우연이거나 착오라는 결론에 이르렀다. 이 궁은 분명 누군가의 것이었고, 보살피고 지키는 사람이 있을 터였다."], ["The palace certainly belonged to somebody; it must have care and keeping: and the keeper would come; the evening or the night would bring him. Patience!", "그 관리인이 올 것이었다. 저녁이나 밤이 그를 데려올 것이었다. 인내하자!"]], [["So concluding, he waited.", "그렇게 결론을 내리고, 그는 기다렸다."]], [["Half an hour passed—a much longer period to Ben-Hur—when the door which had admitted him opened and closed noiselessly as before, and without attracting his attention.", "반 시간이 흘렀다—벤허에게는 훨씬 더 긴 시간이었다—그때 그를 들였던 문이 전과 같이 소리 없이 열렸다 닫혔으나, 그의 주의를 끌지는 못했다."]], [["The moment of the occurrence he was sitting at the farther end of the room.", "그 일이 일어난 순간, 그는 방의 가장 먼 끝에 앉아 있었다."], ["A footstep startled him.", "발소리에 그는 흠칫했다."]], [["“At last she has come!”", "\"드디어 그녀가 왔구나!\""], ["he thought, with a throb of relief and pleasure, and arose.", "그는 안도와 기쁨에 가슴이 뛰며 그렇게 생각하고 일어났다."]], [["The step was heavy, and accompanied with the gride and clang of coarse sandals.", "발걸음은 무거웠고, 거친 샌들이 긁히고 부딪치는 소리가 따랐다."], ["The gilded pillars were between him and the door; he advanced quietly, and leaned against one of them.", "금빛 기둥들이 그와 문 사이를 가로막고 있었다. 그는 조용히 다가가 그중 하나에 몸을 기댔다."], ["Presently he heard voices—the voices of men—one of them rough and guttural.", "이내 그는 목소리를 들었다—남자들의 목소리였고, 그중 하나는 거칠고 목구멍에서 나오는 소리였다."], ["What was said he could not understand, as the language was not of the East or South of Europe.", "무슨 말인지는 알아들을 수 없었으니, 그것은 동방의 말도, 남유럽의 말도 아니었다."]], [["After a general survey of the room, the strangers crossed to their left, and were brought into Ben-Hur’s view—two men, one very stout, both tall, and both in short tunics.", "방을 두루 둘러본 뒤, 낯선 자들은 왼쪽으로 가로질러 가더니, 벤허의 시야에 들어왔다. 두 남자였는데, 하나는 몹시 건장했고, 둘 다 키가 컸으며, 둘 다 짧은 튜닉을 걸치고 있었다."], ["They had not the air of masters of the house or domestics.", "그들은 이 집의 주인이나 하인의 분위기가 아니었다."], ["Everything they saw appeared wonderful to them; everything they stopped to examine they touched.", "그들 눈에는 보이는 것마다 다 신기한 모양이었고, 멈춰 서서 살펴보는 것마다 죄다 손을 댔다."], ["They were vulgarians.", "그들은 천박한 자들이었다."], ["The atrium seemed profaned by their presence.", "그들이 있는 것만으로도 아트리움이 더럽혀지는 것 같았다."], ["At the same time, their leisurely manner and the assurance with which they proceeded pointed to some right or business; if business, with whom?", "그러면서도 그 느긋한 태도와 거침없는 거동은 무언가 권리나 볼일이 있음을 가리켰다. 볼일이라면, 누구와의 볼일인가?"]], [["With much jargon they sauntered this way and that, all the time gradually approaching the pillar by which Ben-Hur was standing.", "그들은 알아들을 수 없는 말을 잔뜩 지껄이며 이리저리 거닐었고, 그러는 내내 벤허가 서 있는 기둥 쪽으로 조금씩 다가왔다."], ["Off a little way, where a slanted gleam of the sun fell with a glare upon the mosaic of the floor, there was a statue which attracted their notice.", "조금 떨어진 곳, 비스듬히 비쳐 든 햇살이 바닥의 모자이크 위에 환하게 떨어진 자리에, 그들의 눈길을 끄는 조각상 하나가 있었다."], ["In examining it, they stopped in the light.", "그것을 살피느라, 그들은 빛 속에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["The mystery surrounding his own presence in the palace tended, as we have seen, to make Ben-Hur nervous; so now, when in the tall stout stranger he recognized the Northman whom he had known in Rome, and seen crowned only the day before in the Circus as the winning pugilist; when he saw the man’s face, scarred with the wounds of many battles, and imbruted by ferocious passions; when he surveyed the fellow’s naked limbs, very marvels of exercise and training, and his shoulders of Herculean breadth, a thought of personal danger started a chill along every vein.", "이미 보았듯이, 이 궁에 자신이 있게 된 까닭을 둘러싼 수수께끼는 벤허의 신경을 곤두서게 했다. 그러던 차에, 키가 크고 건장한 낯선 자가 로마에서 알고 지내던, 그리고 바로 전날 원형 경기장에서 우승한 권투 선수로 월계관을 쓴 것을 보았던 바로 그 북방인임을 알아보았을 때, 그 사내의 얼굴이 숱한 싸움의 상처로 흉터투성이가 되고 사나운 격정으로 짐승처럼 거칠어진 것을 보았을 때, 그 사내의 드러난 사지가 그야말로 단련과 훈련의 경이이며 그 어깨가 헤라클레스처럼 떡 벌어진 것을 살펴보았을 때, 자신에게 닥친 위험에 대한 생각이 그의 온 핏줄에 한기를 일으켰다."], ["A sure instinct warned him that the opportunity for murder was too perfect to have come by chance; and here now were the myrmidons, and their business was with him.", "확실한 본능이 그에게 경고했으니, 살인을 위한 이 기회가 우연히 찾아왔다고 하기에는 너무도 완벽하다는 것이었다. 그리고 지금 여기 그 하수인들이 있었고, 그들의 볼일은 그를 상대로 한 것이었다."], ["He turned an anxious eye upon the Northman’s comrade—young, black-eyed, black-haired, and altogether Jewish in appearance; he observed, also, that both the men were in costume exactly such as professionals of their class were in the habit of wearing in the arena.", "그는 불안한 눈길을 북방인의 동료에게로 돌렸다. 젊고, 눈도 머리카락도 검었으며, 생김새가 영락없이 유대인이었다. 그는 또한 두 사내 모두 그들 부류의 직업인들이 경기장에서 즐겨 입던 차림 그대로임을 알아챘다."], ["Putting the several circumstances together, Ben-Hur could not be longer in doubt: he had been lured into the palace with design.", "여러 정황을 한데 꿰어 맞추자, 벤허는 더는 의심할 수 없었다. 그는 어떤 계략에 걸려 이 궁으로 꾀어 들여진 것이었다."], ["Out of reach of aid, in this splendid privacy, he was to die!", "도움의 손길이 닿지 않는 곳, 이 으리으리한 밀실에서, 그는 죽게 될 운명이었다!"]], [["At a loss what to do, he gazed from man to man, while there was enacted within him that miracle of mind by which life is passed before us in awful detail, to be looked at by ourselves as if it were another’s; and from the evolvement, from a hidden depth, cast up, as it were, by a hidden hand, he was given to see that he had entered upon a new life, different from the old one in this: whereas, in that, he had been the victim of violences done to him, henceforth he was to be the aggressor.", "어찌해야 할지 몰라, 그는 이 사내에게서 저 사내에게로 시선을 옮겼다. 그러는 사이 그의 안에서는 마음의 그 기적이 일어났으니, 그로 인해 삶이 무시무시할 만큼 세세하게 우리 앞에 펼쳐져, 마치 남의 것인 양 스스로 들여다보게 되는 것이었다. 그리고 그 펼쳐짐으로부터, 어떤 숨은 깊이로부터, 마치 보이지 않는 손에 의해 던져 올려지듯, 그는 자신이 새로운 삶에 들어섰음을 보게 되었다."], ["Only yesterday he had found his first victim!", "그 삶이 옛 삶과 다른 점은 이것이었다."], ["To the purely Christian nature the presentation would have brought the weakness of remorse.", "옛 삶에서 그는 자신에게 가해진 폭력의 희생자였으나, 이제부터 그는 공격하는 자가 되리라는 것이었다. 바로 어제 그는 자신의 첫 희생자를 보지 않았던가!"], ["Not so with Ben-Hur; his spirit had its emotions from the teachings of the first lawgiver, not the last and greatest one.", "순전히 그리스도교적인 천성이라면 이 깨달음이 회한이라는 나약함을 가져왔을 것이다. 벤허는 그렇지 않았다. 그의 영혼은 마지막의, 가장 위대한 율법 수여자가 아니라, 첫 번째 율법 수여자의 가르침에서 그 감정을 길어 왔다."], ["He had dealt punishment, not wrong, to Messala.", "그는 메살라에게 잘못이 아니라 응징을 내린 것이었다."], ["By permission of the Lord, he had triumphed; and he derived faith from the circumstance—faith the source of all rational strength, especially strength in peril.", "주님의 허락으로 그는 승리했다. 그리고 그는 그 사실에서 믿음을 길어 냈다—믿음이야말로 모든 이성적인 힘의 원천이요, 특히 위기 속에서의 힘의 원천이었다."]], [["Nor did the influence stop there.", "그 영향은 거기서 그치지 않았다."], ["The new life was made appear to him a mission just begun, and holy as the King to come was holy, and certain as the coming of the King was certain—a mission in which force was lawful if only because it was unavoidable.", "그 새로운 삶은 그에게 이제 막 시작된 사명으로 비쳤으니, 장차 오실 왕이 거룩한 만큼 거룩하고, 그 왕의 오심이 확실한 만큼 확실한 사명이었다—힘을 쓰는 것이 다만 피할 수 없는 일이라는 이유만으로도 정당한 사명이었다."], ["Should he, on the very threshold of such an errand, be afraid?", "그런 사명의 문턱에서, 그가 두려워해야 한단 말인가?"]], [["He undid the sash around his waist, and, baring his head and casting off his white Jewish gown, stood forth in an undertunic not unlike those of the enemy, and was ready, body and mind.", "그는 허리에 두른 띠를 풀고, 머리를 드러내고, 새하얀 유대식 겉옷을 벗어 던졌다. 그러자 적들의 것과 그리 다르지 않은 속튜닉 차림으로 나서게 되었고, 그는 몸도 마음도 만반의 채비가 되었다."], ["Folding his arms, he placed his back against the pillar, and calmly waited.", "팔짱을 끼고, 그는 기둥에 등을 댄 채, 차분히 기다렸다."]], [["The examination of the statue was brief.", "조각상을 살피는 일은 오래가지 않았다."], ["Directly the Northman turned, and said something in the unknown tongue; then both looked at Ben-Hur.", "곧 북방인이 돌아서서 알 수 없는 말로 무언가를 말했다. 그러자 둘 다 벤허를 바라보았다."], ["A few more words, and they advanced towards him.", "몇 마디를 더 주고받더니, 그들은 그를 향해 다가왔다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"너희는 누구냐?\""], ["he asked, in Latin.", "그가 라틴어로 물었다."]], [["The Northman fetched a smile which did not relieve his face of its brutalism, and answered,", "북방인이 미소를 지었으나, 그 얼굴에서 야수성을 걷어내지는 못했다. 그가 대답했다."]], [["“Barbarians.”", "\"야만인이다.\""]], [["“This is the palace of Idernee.", "\"이곳은 이데르네 궁이다."], ["Whom seek you?", "누구를 찾느냐?"], ["Stand and answer.”", "멈춰 서서 대답하라.\""]], [["The words were spoken with earnestness.", "그 말은 진지하게 던져졌다."], ["The strangers stopped; and in his turn the Northman asked, “Who are you?”", "낯선 자들이 멈춰 섰다. 그리고 이번에는 북방인이 물었다. \"너는 누구냐?\""]], [["“A Roman.”", "\"로마인이다.\""]], [["The giant laid his head back upon his shoulders.", "거인은 어깨 위로 고개를 젖혔다."]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["I have heard how a god once came from a cow licking a salted stone; but not even a god can make a Roman of a Jew.”", "옛날에 신 하나가 소금 바른 돌을 핥는 암소에게서 나왔다는 얘기는 들었다만, 아무리 신이라도 유대인을 로마인으로 만들 수는 없지.\""]], [["The laugh over, he spoke to his companion again, and they moved nearer.", "웃음이 끝나자 그는 다시 동료에게 무어라 말했고, 그들은 더 가까이 다가왔다."]], [["“Hold!”", "\"멈춰라!\""], ["said Ben-Hur, quitting the pillar.", "벤허가 기둥에서 떨어져 나오며 말했다."], ["“One word.”", "\"한마디만 하자.\""]], [["They stopped again.", "그들이 다시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“A word!”", "\"한마디라!\""], ["replied the Saxon, folding his immense arms across his breast, and relaxing the menace beginning to blacken his face.", "색슨인이 그 거대한 두 팔을 가슴께에 모아 끼고, 얼굴에 거뭇거뭇 떠오르던 위협을 누그러뜨리며 대꾸했다. \"한마디라!"], ["“A word! Speak.”", "말해 봐라.\""]], [["“You are Thord the Northman.”", "\"너는 북방인 토르드다.\""]], [["The giant opened his blue eyes.", "거인이 푸른 눈을 크게 떴다."]], [["“You were lanista in Rome.”", "“넌 로마에서 검투사 교관이었지.”"]], [["“I was your scholar.”", "\"나는 너의 제자였다.\""]], [["“No,” said Thord, shaking his head.", "\"아니지.\" 토르드가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“By the beard of Irmin, I had never a Jew to make a fighting-man of.”", "\"이르민의 수염을 두고 맹세컨대, 나는 유대인을 데려다 싸움꾼으로 만든 적이 단 한 번도 없다.\""]], [["“But I will prove my saying.”", "\"하지만 내 말이 참임을 증명해 보이지.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“You came here to kill me.”", "\"너는 나를 죽이러 여기 왔다.\""]], [["“That is true.”", "\"그건 사실이지.\""]], [["“Then let this man fight me singly, and I will make the proof on his body.”", "\"그렇다면 이 사내가 나와 단둘이 겨루게 하라. 그러면 내가 그자의 몸으로 증명해 보이겠다.\""]], [["A gleam of humor shone in the Northman’s face.", "북방인의 얼굴에 한 가닥 익살이 번졌다."], ["He spoke to his companion, who made answer; then he replied with the naivete of a diverted child,", "그는 동료에게 무어라 말했고, 동료가 대답하자, 그는 흥에 겨운 어린아이 같은 천진함으로 응답했다."]], [["“Wait till I say begin.”", "\"내가 시작하라고 할 때까지 기다려라.\""]], [["By repeated touches of his foot, he pushed a couch out on the floor, and proceeded leisurely to stretch his burly form upon it; when perfectly at ease, he said, simply, “Now begin.”", "그는 발끝으로 거듭 밀어, 긴 의자 하나를 바닥 가운데로 밀어내고는, 느긋하게 그 우람한 몸을 그 위에 뉘었다. 더없이 편안한 자세를 잡고 나서, 그는 간단히 말했다. \"자, 시작해라.\""]], [["Without ado, Ben-Hur walked to his antagonist.", "벤허는 군말 없이 자신의 적수에게로 걸어갔다."]], [["“Defend thyself,” he said.", "\"막아 봐라.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["The man, nothing loath, put up his hands.", "사내는 마다하지 않고 두 손을 들어 올렸다."]], [["As the two thus confronted each other in approved position, there was no discernible inequality between them; on the contrary, they were as like as brothers.", "이렇게 둘이 격투의 정석대로 자세를 잡고 마주 섰을 때, 둘 사이에는 이렇다 할 우열의 차이가 보이지 않았다. 오히려 그들은 형제처럼 닮아 있었다."], ["To the stranger’s confident smile, Ben-Hur opposed an earnestness which, had his skill been known, would have been accepted fair warning of danger.", "낯선 자의 자신만만한 미소에 맞서, 벤허는 진지함을 드러냈으니, 만일 그의 솜씨가 알려졌더라면 그 진지함은 위험을 알리는 정당한 경고로 받아들여졌을 것이다."], ["Both knew the combat was to be mortal.", "둘 다 이 싸움이 목숨을 건 것임을 알고 있었다."]], [["Ben-Hur feinted with his right hand.", "벤허가 오른손으로 거짓 동작을 했다."], ["The stranger warded, slightly advancing his left arm. Ere he could return to guard, Ben-Hur caught him by the wrist in a grip which years at the oar had made terrible as a vise.", "낯선 자가 왼팔을 살짝 내밀며 막았다. 그가 미처 방어 자세로 돌아오기도 전에, 벤허가 그의 손목을 움켜잡았으니, 여러 해를 노에 매달려 살아온 손아귀가 만들어 낸 그 악력은 바이스처럼 무시무시했다."], ["The surprise was complete, and no time given.", "기습은 완벽했고, 빈틈이라곤 주지 않았다."], ["To throw himself forward; to push the arm across the man’s throat and over his right shoulder, and turn him left side front; to strike surely with the ready left hand; to strike the bare neck under the ear—were but petty divisions of the same act.", "몸을 앞으로 던지는 것, 사내의 팔을 그 목을 가로질러 오른쪽 어깨 위로 밀어붙이며 그를 왼쪽 옆으로 돌려세우는 것, 준비된 왼손으로 어김없이 내리치는 것, 귀밑의 드러난 목을 후려치는 것—이 모든 것은 한 동작의 자잘한 마디에 지나지 않았다."], ["No need of a second blow.", "두 번째 일격은 필요 없었다."], ["The myrmidon fell heavily, and without a cry, and lay still.", "하수인은 비명 한 번 없이 묵직하게 쓰러져, 꼼짝 않고 누웠다."]], [["Ben-Hur turned to Thord.", "벤허가 토르드에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Ha! What!", "\"하! 이게 뭐냐!"], ["By the beard of Irmin!”", "이르민의 수염을 두고!\""], ["the latter cried, in astonishment, rising to a sitting posture. Then he laughed.", "토르드가 깜짝 놀라 소리치며 일어나 앉았다. 그러더니 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["I could not have done it better myself.”", "나라도 저보다 더 잘하지는 못했겠다.\""]], [["He viewed Ben-Hur coolly from head to foot, and, rising, faced him with undisguised admiration.", "그는 벤허를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 차분히 훑어보더니, 일어서서 감탄을 숨기지 않은 채 그를 마주 보았다."]], [["“It was my trick—the trick I have practised for ten years in the schools of Rome.", "\"저건 내 기술이다—내가 로마의 도장에서 십 년 동안 갈고닦은 기술이야."], ["You are not a Jew.", "너는 유대인이 아니다."], ["Who are you?”", "너는 누구냐?\""]], [["“You knew Arrius the duumvir.”", "\"너는 이인 집정관 아리우스를 알았지.\""]], [["“Quintus Arrius?", "\"퀸투스 아리우스?"], ["Yes, he was my patron.”", "그래, 그분은 내 후원자였다.\""]], [["“He had a son.”", "\"그분에게는 아들이 있었다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Thord, his battered features lighting dully, “I knew the boy; he would have made a king gladiator.", "토르드가 말했고, 두들겨 맞은 그의 얼굴이 흐릿하게나마 환해졌다. \"그 젊은이를 나도 알았지. 그는 검투사의 왕이 되고도 남았을 거다."], ["Cæsar offered him his patronage.", "카이사르께서 친히 후원하겠다고 하셨지."], ["I taught him the very trick you played on this one here—a trick impossible except to a hand and arm like mine.", "내가 그에게 바로 네가 이자에게 쓴 그 기술을 가르쳤다—나 같은 손과 팔이 아니고서는 도저히 불가능한 기술이야."], ["It has won me many a crown.”", "그 기술로 나는 숱한 월계관을 따냈지.\""]], [["“I am that son of Arrius.”", "\"내가 바로 그 아리우스의 아들이다.\""]], [["Thord drew nearer, and viewed him carefully; then his eyes brightened with genuine pleasure, and, laughing, he held out his hand.", "토르드가 더 가까이 다가와 그를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그러더니 그의 눈이 진정한 기쁨으로 빛났고, 그는 웃으며 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["He told me I would find a Jew here—a Jew—a dog of a Jew—killing whom was serving the gods.”", "그자가 나더러 여기서 유대인을 만나게 될 거라 했지—유대인을—유대인 개새끼를—그놈을 죽이는 게 신들을 섬기는 일이라고 했어.\""]], [["“Who told you so?”", "\"누가 그렇게 말하더냐?\""], ["asked Ben-Hur, taking the hand.", "벤허가 그 손을 잡으며 물었다."]], [["“He—Messala—ha, ha, ha!”", "\"그자가—메살라가—하, 하, 하!\""]], [["“When, Thord?”", "\"언제 그랬느냐, 토르드?\""]], [["“Last night.”", "\"어젯밤이다.\""]], [["“I thought he was hurt.”", "\"나는 그가 다친 줄로 알았는데.\""]], [["“He will never walk again.", "\"그자는 두 번 다시 걷지 못할 거다."], ["On his bed he told me between groans.”", "침상에 누워, 신음 사이사이로 내게 말하더군.\""]], [["A very vivid portrayal of hate in a few words; and Ben-Hur saw that the Roman, if he lived, would still be capable and dangerous, and follow him unrelentingly.", "몇 마디 말로 증오를 더없이 생생하게 그려 낸 셈이었다. 그리고 벤허는 보았다. 그 로마인이 살아남는다면 여전히 능력 있고 위험한 자로서, 끈질기게 자신을 쫓아오리라는 것을."], ["Revenge remained to sweeten the ruined life; therefore the clinging to fortune lost in the wager with Sanballat.", "무너진 삶에 단맛을 더해 줄 복수가 아직 남아 있었으니, 그래서 산발랏과의 내기에서 잃은 재산에 그토록 매달리는 것이었다."], ["Ben-Hur ran the ground over, with a distinct foresight of the many ways in which it would be possible for his enemy to interfere with him in the work he had undertaken for the King who was coming.", "벤허는 사태를 곰곰이 짚어 보며, 자신이 장차 오실 왕을 위해 떠맡은 일에서 그의 적이 그를 방해할 수 있는 갖가지 길을 또렷이 내다보았다."], ["Why not he resort to the Roman’s methods?", "그라고 로마인의 방식을 쓰지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가?"], ["The man hired to kill him could be hired to strike back.", "그를 죽이라고 고용된 자라면, 거꾸로 되받아치라고도 고용할 수 있었다."], ["It was in his power to offer higher wages.", "더 높은 삯을 내미는 것은 그의 능력 안에 있었다."], ["The temptation was strong; and, half yielding, he chanced to look down at his late antagonist lying still, with white upturned face, so like himself.", "유혹은 강했다. 그리고 거기에 반쯤 넘어가던 차에, 그는 우연히 좀 전의 적수를 내려다보게 되었으니, 그자는 하얗게 위를 향한 얼굴로 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었고, 그 모습이 자신과 어찌나 닮았던지."], ["A light came to him, and he asked, “Thord, what was Messala to give you for killing me?”", "한 줄기 빛이 그에게 떠올랐고, 그가 물었다. \"토르드, 메살라가 나를 죽이는 대가로 너에게 얼마를 주기로 했느냐?\""]], [["“A thousand sestertii.”", "\"천 세스테르티우스다.\""]], [["“You shall have them yet; and so you do now what I tell you, I will add three thousand more to the sum.”", "\"그건 그대로 받게 될 거다. 그리고 네가 지금 내 말대로 한다면, 거기에 삼천을 더 얹어 주겠다.\""]], [["The giant reflected aloud,", "거인이 소리 내어 셈을 했다."]], [["“I won five thousand yesterday; from the Roman one—six.", "\"어제 오천을 벌었고, 로마인 그자에게서—육천이지."], ["Give me four, good Arrius—four more—and I will stand firm for you, though old Thor, my namesake, strike me with his hammer.", "사천을 다오, 착한 아리우스—사천을 더 주면—내 이름과 같은 늙은 토르 신이 망치로 나를 내려친다 해도, 내 너를 위해 굳건히 서겠다."], ["Make it four, and I will kill the lying patrician, if you say so.", "사천으로 하자. 네가 시키기만 한다면 그 거짓말쟁이 귀족 놈도 죽여 주마."], ["I have only to cover his mouth with my hand—thus.”", "그저 그 입을 손으로 틀어막기만 하면 돼—이렇게.\""]], [["He illustrated the process by clapping his hand over his own mouth.", "그는 제 입을 손으로 틀어막아 보이며 그 방법을 시연했다."]], [["“I see,”", "\"알겠다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur; “ten thousand sestertii is a fortune.", "벤허가 말했다. \"만 세스테르티우스라면 한밑천이지."], ["It will enable you to return to Rome, and open a wine-shop near the Great Circus, and live as becomes the first of the lanistae.”", "그 돈이면 로마로 돌아가, 대전차 경기장 근처에 술집을 열고, 검투사 교관 중에서도 으뜸가는 자답게 살 수 있을 거다.\""]], [["The very scars on the giant’s face glowed afresh with the pleasure the picture gave him.", "그 그림이 거인에게 안겨 준 기쁨에, 그의 얼굴에 난 흉터들마저 새삼 화끈 달아올랐다."]], [["“I will make it four thousand,”", "\"사천으로 해 주마.\""], ["Ben-Hur continued; “and in what you shall do for the money there will be no blood on your hands, Thord.", "벤허가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그 돈을 받고 네가 할 일에는, 토르드, 네 손에 피 한 방울 묻지 않을 거다."], ["Hear me now.", "자, 들어 봐라."], ["Did not your friend here look like me?”", "여기 네 동료가 나와 닮지 않았느냐?\""]], [["“I would have said he was an apple from the same tree.”", "\"같은 나무에서 딴 사과라고 해도 믿겠더라.\""]], [["“Well, if I put on his tunic, and dress him in these clothes of mine, and you and I go away together, leaving him here, can you not get your sestertii from Messala all the same?", "\"좋다. 내가 그자의 튜닉을 입고, 그자에게는 내 이 옷을 입혀, 너와 내가 그자를 여기 남겨 두고 함께 떠난다면, 그래도 너는 메살라에게서 그 세스테르티우스를 똑같이 받아 낼 수 있지 않겠느냐?"], ["You have only to make him believe it me that is dead.”", "죽은 게 나라고 그자가 믿게 만들기만 하면 되는 거다.\""]], [["Thord laughed till the tears ran into his mouth.", "토르드는 눈물이 입가로 흘러내리도록 웃어 댔다."]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["Ten thousand sestertii were never won so easily.", "만 세스테르티우스를 이렇게 쉽게 번 적은 없었지."], ["And a wine-shop by the Great Circus!—all for a lie without blood in it!", "게다가 대전차 경기장 옆에 술집까지!—그것도 피 한 방울 안 묻은 거짓말 하나로!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["Give me thy hand, O son of Arrius.", "손을 다오, 오 아리우스의 아들이여."], ["Get on now, and—ha, ha, ha!—if ever you come to Rome, fail not to ask for the wine-shop of Thord the Northman.", "자, 이제 어서 가거라, 그리고—하, 하, 하!—언제고 로마에 오거든, 북방인 토르드의 술집을 꼭 물어보아라."], ["By the beard of Irmin, I will give you the best, though I borrow it from Cæsar!”", "이르민의 수염을 두고, 카이사르에게서 빌려서라도 너에게 제일 좋은 걸 내주마!\""]], [["They shook hands again; after which the exchange of clothes was effected.", "그들은 다시 손을 맞잡았다. 그러고 나서 옷을 바꿔 입는 일이 이루어졌다."], ["It was arranged then that a messenger should go at night to Thord’s lodging-place with the four thousand sestertii.", "그런 다음, 밤에 심부름꾼을 토르드의 거처로 보내 사천 세스테르티우스를 전하기로 약속이 정해졌다."], ["When they were done, the giant knocked at the front door; it opened to him; and, passing out of the atrium, he led Ben-Hur into a room adjoining, where the latter completed his attire from the coarse garments of the dead pugilist.", "일을 다 마치자, 거인은 정문을 두드렸다. 문이 그에게 열렸다. 그리고 아트리움을 나서며, 그는 벤허를 옆방으로 데려갔고, 거기서 벤허는 죽은 권투 선수의 거친 옷가지로 차림을 마저 갖추었다."], ["They separated directly in the Omphalus.", "그들은 곧장 옴팔로스에서 헤어졌다."]], [["“Fail not, O son of Arrius, fail not the wine-shop near the Great Circus!", "\"잊지 마라, 오 아리우스의 아들이여, 대전차 경기장 옆 그 술집을 잊지 마라!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["By the beard of Irmin, there was never fortune gained so cheap.", "이르민의 수염을 두고, 이렇게 헐값에 거머쥔 행운은 다시없을 게다."], ["The gods keep you!”", "신들이 너를 지켜 주시기를!\""]], [["Upon leaving the atrium, Ben-Hur gave a last look at the myrmidon as he lay in the Jewish vestments, and was satisfied.", "아트리움을 나서며, 벤허는 유대식 옷을 입고 누워 있는 그 하수인을 마지막으로 한 번 바라보았고, 흡족했다."], ["The likeness was striking.", "닮은 모습이 놀라울 정도였다."], ["If Thord kept faith, the cheat was a secret to endure forever.", "토르드가 신의를 지킨다면, 그 속임수는 영영 비밀로 묻힐 것이었다."]], [["At night, in the house of Simonides, Ben-Hur told the good man all that had taken place in the palace of Idernee; and it was agreed that, after a few days, public inquiry should be set afloat for the discovery of the whereabouts of the son of Arrius.", "밤이 되어, 시모니데스의 집에서 벤허는 그 선한 사람에게 이데르네 궁에서 일어난 일을 모두 들려주었다. 그리고 며칠 뒤에 아리우스의 아들의 행방을 찾는 공개 수색을 흘려보내기로 의견이 모아졌다."], ["Eventually the matter was to be carried boldly to Maxentius; then, if the mystery came not out, it was concluded that Messala and Gratus would be at rest and happy, and Ben-Hur free to betake himself to Jerusalem, to make search for his lost people.", "결국 그 일은 막센티우스에게까지 대담하게 가져갈 것이었다. 그렇게 해도 수수께끼가 풀리지 않으면, 메살라와 그라투스는 마음을 놓고 흡족해할 것이고, 벤허는 자유로이 예루살렘으로 떠나 잃어버린 가족을 찾아 나설 수 있으리라는 결론이었다."]], [["At the leave-taking, Simonides sat in his chair out on the terrace overlooking the river, and gave his farewell and the peace of the Lord with the impressment of a father.", "작별할 때, 시모니데스는 강이 내려다보이는 테라스에 의자를 내놓고 앉아, 아버지 같은 간곡함으로 작별 인사와 주님의 평안을 빌어 주었다."], ["Esther went with the young man to the head of the steps.", "에스더가 그 젊은이를 따라 계단 어귀까지 나왔다."]], [["“If I find my mother, Esther, thou shalt go to her at Jerusalem, and be a sister to Tirzah.”", "\"내가 어머니를 찾게 되면, 에스더, 그대는 예루살렘으로 어머니에게 가서, 티르자에게 자매가 되어 주오.\""]], [["And with the words he kissed her.", "그러고는 그 말과 함께 그가 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["Was it only a kiss of peace?", "그것은 다만 평안의 입맞춤이었을까?"]], [["He crossed the river next to the late quarters of Ilderim, where he found the Arab who was to serve him as guide.", "이튿날 그는 강을 건너 일데림이 머물던 곳으로 갔고, 거기서 자신의 길잡이가 되어 줄 아랍인을 만났다."], ["The horses were brought out.", "말들이 끌려 나왔다."]], [["“This one is thine,”", "\"이놈은 당신 것입니다.\""], ["said the Arab.", "아랍인이 말했다."]], [["Ben-Hur looked, and, lo! it was Aldebaran, the swiftest and brightest of the sons of Mira, and, next to Sirius, the beloved of the sheik; and he knew the old man’s heart came to him along with the gift.", "벤허가 보니, 아, 그것은 미라의 아들들 가운데 가장 빠르고 가장 빛나는 알데바란이었으니, 시리우스 다음으로 족장이 사랑하던 말이었다. 그리고 그는 알았다, 이 선물과 더불어 그 노인의 마음까지 자신에게 건너왔음을."]], [["The corpse in the atrium was taken up and buried by night; and, as part of Messala’s plan, a courier was sent off to Gratus to make him at rest by the announcement of Ben-Hur’s death—this time past question.", "아트리움의 시신은 밤중에 들려 나가 묻혔다. 그리고 메살라의 계획의 일부로, 벤허의 죽음을—이번에는 의심할 여지 없는 죽음을—알려 그라투스를 안심시키기 위해 한 전령이 그에게로 떠났다."]], [["Ere long a wine-shop was opened near the Circus Maximus, with inscription over the door:", "머지않아 대전차 경기장 근처에 술집 하나가 문을 열었고, 그 문 위에는 이런 글귀가 적혀 있었다. 북방인 토르드."]], [["# BOOK SIXTH", "# BOOK SIXTH"]], [["“Is that a Death?", "“저것은 죽음인가?"], ["and are there two?", "그리고 둘이 있는가?"], ["Is Death that woman’s mate?", "저 여인이 죽음의 짝인가?"]], [["Her skin was as white as leprosy, The Nightmare Life-in-Death was she, Who thicks man’s blood with cold.”", "그 살갗은 나병처럼 희었으니,\n‘삶 속의 죽음’이라는 악몽이 바로 그 여인이라,"]], [["# COLERIDGE. CHAPTER I", "# COLERIDGE. CHAPTER I"]], [["Our story moves forward now thirty days from the night Ben-Hur left Antioch to go out with Sheik Ilderim into the desert.", "사람의 피를 차갑게 엉기게 하는 자였다.” 콜리지."]], [["A great change has befallen—great at least as respects the fortunes of our hero. Valerius Gratus has been succeeded by Pontius Pilate!", "우리 이야기는 이제 벤허가 안티오크를 떠나 일데림 족장과 함께 사막으로 나간 그 밤으로부터 서른 날을 앞으로 나아간다."]], [["The removal, it may be remarked, cost Simonides exactly five talents Roman money in hand paid to Sejanus, who was then in height of power as imperial favorite; the object being to help Ben-Hur, by lessening his exposure while in and about Jerusalem attempting discovery of his people. To such pious use the faithful servant put the winnings from Drusus and his associates; all of whom, having paid their wagers, became at once and naturally the enemies of Messala, whose repudiation was yet an unsettled question in Rome.", "커다란 변화가 일어났다. 적어도 우리 주인공의 운명에 관한 한 큰 변화였다. 발레리우스 그라투스의 후임으로 본디오 빌라도가 부임한 것이다! 이 교체에는, 한마디 덧붙이자면, 시모니데스가 당시 황제의 총신으로서 권세의 정점에 있던 세야누스에게 직접 건넨 로마 화폐로 정확히 다섯 달란트의 비용이 들었다. 그 목적은 벤허가 예루살렘 안팎에서 가족을 찾으려 애쓰는 동안 노출을 줄여 그를 돕는 데 있었다. 충직한 종은 드루수스와 그 일당에게서 딴 돈을 이처럼 경건한 용도에 썼다. 내기에 진 그들은 빚을 갚자마자 당연하게도 일제히 메살라의 적이 되었으니, 메살라가 빚을 떼어먹은 일은 로마에서 아직도 결말이 나지 않은 문제로 남아 있었다."]], [["Brief as the time was, already the Jews knew the change of rulers was not for the better.", "짧은 시간이었으나 유대인들은 이미 통치자의 교체가 더 나은 쪽으로 이루어진 것이 아님을 알았다."]], [["The cohorts sent to relieve the garrison of Antonia made their entry into the city by night; next morning the first sight that greeted the people resident in the neighborhood was the walls of the old Tower decorated with military ensigns, which unfortunately consisted of busts of the emperor mixed with eagles and globes.", "안토니아 요새의 수비대를 교대하러 파견된 군대는 밤을 틈타 도성으로 들어왔다. 이튿날 아침, 그 근처에 사는 사람들의 눈에 가장 먼저 들어온 광경은 옛 요새의 성벽을 장식한 군기였는데, 불행하게도 그것은 독수리와 구체에 황제의 흉상이 뒤섞인 표지였다."], ["A multitude, in passion, marched to Cæsarea, where Pilate was lingering, and implored him to remove the detested images.", "격분한 군중이 빌라도가 머물고 있는 가이사랴로 행진하여, 그 가증스러운 형상들을 치워 달라고 간청했다."], ["Five days and nights they beset his palace gates; at last he appointed a meeting with them in the Circus.", "닷새 밤낮을 그들은 그의 궁전 문 앞에 죽치고 늘어섰다. 마침내 그는 원형 경기장에서 그들을 만나기로 약속했다."], ["When they were assembled, he encircled them with soldiers; instead of resisting, they offered him their lives, and conquered.", "그들이 모이자 그는 병사들로 그들을 에워쌌다. 그러나 그들은 저항하는 대신 제 목숨을 내놓았고, 그리하여 이겼다."], ["He recalled the images and ensigns to Cæsarea, where Gratus, with more consideration, had kept such abominations housed during the eleven years of his reign.", "그는 그 형상과 군기를 가이사랴로 다시 거두어 갔으니, 그곳은 그라투스가 좀 더 사려 깊게 그 혐오스러운 물건들을 십일 년 통치 기간 내내 가두어 두었던 곳이었다."]], [["The worst of men do once in a while vary their wickednesses by good acts; so with Pilate.", "아무리 악한 자라도 이따금은 선행으로 제 악행에 변화를 주는 법이니, 빌라도도 그러했다."], ["He ordered an inspection of all the prisons in Judea, and a return of the names of the persons in custody, with a statement of the crimes for which they had been committed.", "그는 유대 지방의 모든 감옥을 점검하라 명하고, 구금된 자들의 이름과 그들이 갇힌 죄목을 적은 보고를 올리게 했다."], ["Doubtless, the motive was the one so common with officials just installed—dread of entailed responsibility; the people, however, in thought of the good which might come of the measure, gave him credit, and, for a period, were comforted.", "그 동기는 분명, 갓 부임한 관리에게 흔한 것—떠넘겨진 책임에 대한 두려움이었다. 그러나 백성은 그 조치에서 비롯될 선을 헤아려 그를 좋게 여겼고, 한동안 위안을 얻었다."], ["The revelations were astonishing.", "드러난 사실들은 놀라웠다."], ["Hundreds of persons were released against whom there were no accusations; many others came to light who had long been accounted dead; yet more amazing, there was opening of dungeons not merely unknown at the time by the people, but actually forgotten by the prison authorities.", "아무런 고발도 없던 수백 명이 풀려났고, 오래전에 죽은 줄로만 알았던 많은 이들이 햇빛을 보게 되었다. 더욱 놀라운 것은, 당시 백성에게 알려지지 않았을 뿐 아니라 감옥 당국조차 까맣게 잊고 있던 지하 감방들이 열렸다는 사실이다."], ["With one instance of the latter kind we have now to deal; and, strange to say, it occurred in Jerusalem.", "그런 후자의 사례 가운데 하나를 우리는 지금 다루려 한다. 그리고 기이하게도, 그 일은 예루살렘에서 일어났다."]], [["The Tower of Antonia, which will be remembered as occupying two thirds of the sacred area on Mount Moriah, was originally a castle built by the Macedonians.", "안토니아 요새는 모리아산의 성스러운 구역 가운데 삼분의 이를 차지하고 있던 것으로 기억될 터인데, 본디 마케도니아 사람들이 세운 성채였다."], ["Afterwards, John Hyrcanus erected the castle into a fortress for the defence of the Temple, and in his day it was considered impregnable to assault; but when Herod came with his bolder genius, he strengthened its walls and extended them, leaving a vast pile which included every appurtenance necessary for the stronghold he intended it to be forever; such as offices, barracks, armories, magazines, cisterns, and last, though not least, prisons of all grades.", "훗날 요한 히르카누스가 그 성채를 성전 방어를 위한 요새로 키웠으니, 그의 시대에는 어떤 공격에도 함락되지 않으리라 여겨졌다. 그러나 헤롯이 더 대담한 기질을 품고 등장하여 성벽을 보강하고 넓혀, 거대한 건축물을 남겼다. 그것은 그가 영원토록 요새로 삼고자 한 시설에 꼭 필요한 모든 부속을 갖추고 있었으니, 곧 집무실, 막사, 무기고, 창고, 저수조, 그리고 마지막이되 결코 사소하지 않은 온갖 등급의 감옥이 그러했다."], ["He levelled the solid rock, and tapped it with deep excavations, and built over them; connecting the whole great mass with the Temple by a beautiful colonnade, from the roof of which one could look down over the courts of the sacred structure.", "그는 단단한 바위를 평평하게 깎고, 깊이 파 들어가 굴을 뚫었으며, 그 위에 건물을 올렸다. 그리고 그 거대한 덩어리 전체를 아름다운 주랑(柱廊)으로 성전과 이었으니, 그 주랑의 지붕에서는 성전 건물의 안뜰들을 내려다볼 수 있었다."], ["In such condition the Tower fell at last out of his hands into those of the Romans, who were quick to see its strength and advantages, and convert it to uses becoming such masters.", "이러한 상태로 요새는 마침내 그의 손에서 로마인의 손으로 넘어갔다. 그들은 그 견고함과 이점을 재빨리 알아보았고, 그런 주인에게 어울리는 용도로 그것을 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["All through the administration of Gratus it had been a garrisoned citadel and underground prison terrible to revolutionists. Woe when the cohorts poured from its gates to suppress disorder!", "그라투스의 통치 내내 그곳은 수비대가 주둔한 성채였고, 혁명가들에게 두려운 지하 감옥이었다. 무리를 이룬 군대가 소요를 진압하러 그 문에서 쏟아져 나올 때 화 있을진저!"], ["Woe not less when a Jew passed the same gates going in under arrest!", "유대인이 체포되어 그 같은 문을 지나 안으로 끌려들어 갈 때 그에 못지않게 화 있을진저!"]], [["With this explanation, we hasten to our story.", "이쯤 설명해 두고, 우리는 서둘러 이야기로 나아간다."]], [["The order of the new procurator requiring a report of the persons in custody was received at the Tower of Antonia, and promptly executed; and two days have gone since the last unfortunate was brought up for examination.", "구금된 자들의 보고를 요구한 새 총독의 명령은 안토니아 요새에 전달되었고, 곧바로 이행되었다. 마지막 불행한 자가 심문을 위해 끌려 올라온 지 이틀이 지났다."], ["The tabulated statement, ready for forwarding, lies on the table of the tribune in command; in five minutes more it will be on the way to Pilate, sojourning in the palace up on Mount Zion.", "발송 준비를 마친 그 표로 정리된 보고서는 지휘를 맡은 호민관의 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다. 앞으로 오 분이면 그것은 시온산 위의 궁전에 머물고 있는 빌라도에게로 떠날 참이었다."]], [["The tribune’s office is spacious and cool, and furnished in a style suitable to the dignity of the commandant of a post in every respect so important.", "호민관의 집무실은 널찍하고 시원했으며, 여러모로 그토록 중요한 초소 사령관의 위엄에 걸맞은 양식으로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["Looking in upon him about the seventh hour of the day, the officer appears weary and impatient; when the report is despatched, he will to the roof of the colonnade for air and exercise, and the amusement to be had watching the Jews over in the courts of the Temple.", "그날 일곱째 시각쯤 그를 들여다보면, 그 장교는 지치고 초조해 보였다. 보고서가 발송되고 나면 그는 주랑의 지붕으로 올라가 바람을 쐬고 운동을 하며, 저편 성전 안뜰의 유대인들을 구경하는 재미를 누릴 작정이었다."], ["His subordinates and clerks share his impatience.", "그의 부하들과 서기들도 그 초조함을 함께 나누고 있었다."]], [["In the spell of waiting a man appeared in a doorway leading to an adjoining apartment.", "기다림에 사로잡혀 있을 때, 한 사내가 옆방으로 통하는 문간에 나타났다."], ["He rattled a bunch of keys, each heavy as a hammer, and at once attracted the chief’s attention.", "그는 저마다 망치만큼 묵직한 열쇠 꾸러미를 절그럭거리며, 단번에 책임자의 주의를 끌었다."]], [["“Ah, Gesius!", "“아, 게시우스!"], ["come in,”", "들어오게.”"], ["the tribune said.", "호민관이 말했다."]], [["As the new-comer approached the table behind which the chief sat in an easy-chair, everybody present looked at him, and, observing a certain expression of alarm and mortification on his face, became silent that they might hear what he had to say.", "새로 들어온 자가 책임자가 안락의자에 앉아 있는 탁자 뒤편으로 다가가자, 그 자리에 있던 모두가 그를 바라보았다. 그리고 그의 얼굴에 어린 일종의 놀라움과 굴욕의 표정을 알아채고는, 그가 무슨 말을 하려는지 들으려 입을 다물었다."]], [["“O tribune!”", "“오, 호민관님!”"], ["he began, bending low, “I fear to tell what now I bring you.”", "그는 허리를 깊이 숙이며 입을 열었다. “지금 제가 가져온 소식을 어떻게 말씀드려야 할지 두렵습니다.”"]], [["“Another mistake—ha, Gesius?”", "“또 무슨 착오인가, 게시우스?”"]], [["“If I could persuade myself it is but a mistake, I would not be afraid.”", "“그저 착오일 뿐이라고 스스로를 납득시킬 수 있다면, 저는 두렵지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“A crime then—or, worse, a breach of duty.", "“그렇다면 범죄인가. 아니면, 더 나쁘게는 직무 위반인가."], ["Thou mayst laugh at Cæsar, or curse the gods, and live; but if the offence be to the eagles—ah, thou knowest, Gesius—go on!”", "자네는 카이사르를 비웃거나 신들을 저주하고도 살아남을 수 있네. 그러나 그 죄가 독수리 군기에 닿는 것이라면—아, 자네도 알지 않나, 게시우스—말해 보게!”"]], [["“It is now about eight years since Valerius Gratus selected me to be keeper of prisoners here in the Tower,”", "“발레리우스 그라투스가 저를 이 요새의 감옥지기로 뽑은 지 이제 여덟 해쯤 되었습니다.”"], ["said the man, deliberately.", "사내는 차분하게 말했다."], ["“I remember the morning I entered upon the duties of my office.", "“제가 직무를 시작하던 그날 아침이 기억납니다."], ["There had been a riot the day before, and fighting in the streets. We slew many Jews, and suffered on our side.", "그 전날 폭동이 있었고, 거리에서 싸움이 벌어졌습니다. 우리는 많은 유대인을 죽였고, 우리 쪽도 손실을 입었습니다."], ["The affair came, it was said, of an attempt to assassinate Gratus, who had been knocked from his horse by a tile thrown from a roof.", "그 일은 그라투스를 암살하려던 시도에서 비롯되었다고들 했는데, 그분은 지붕에서 날아온 기와에 맞아 말에서 떨어진 터였습니다."], ["I found him sitting where you now sit, O tribune, his head swathed in bandages.", "저는 그분이 지금 호민관님께서 앉아 계신 바로 그 자리에 머리에 붕대를 감고 앉아 계신 것을 보았습니다."], ["He told me of my selection, and gave me these keys, numbered to correspond with the numbers of the cells; they were the badges of my office, he said, and not to be parted with.", "그분은 저를 뽑았다고 일러 주시고, 감방 번호와 맞추어 번호가 매겨진 이 열쇠들을 주셨습니다. 이것이 제 직무의 표지이니 결코 손에서 놓아서는 안 된다고 하셨지요."], ["There was a roll of parchment on the table.", "탁자 위에는 양피지 두루마리가 있었습니다."], ["Calling me to him, he opened the roll.", "저를 가까이 부르신 그분은 그 두루마리를 펼치셨습니다."], ["‘Here are maps of the cells,’ said he.", "‘여기 감방들의 지도가 있다’고 하셨습니다."], ["There were three of them.", "지도는 세 장이었습니다."], ["‘This one,’ he went on, ‘shows the arrangement of the upper floor; this second one gives you the second floor; and this last is of the lower floor.", "‘이것은’ 하고 그분이 말을 이으셨습니다. ‘위층의 배치를 보여 준다. 이 둘째 것은 둘째 층을, 이 마지막 것은 아래층을 알려 준다."], ["I give them to you in trust.’ I took them from his hand, and he said, further, ‘Now you have the keys and the maps; go immediately, and acquaint yourself with the whole arrangement; visit each cell, and see to its condition.", "이것들을 너에게 맡긴다.’ 제가 그분 손에서 그것들을 받자, 그분은 다시 말씀하셨습니다. ‘이제 너에게 열쇠와 지도가 있으니, 곧장 가서 전체 배치를 익혀 두어라. 감방마다 들러 그 상태를 살펴라."], ["When anything is needed for the security of a prisoner, order it according to your judgment, for you are the master under me, and no other.’", "죄수의 보안을 위해 필요한 것이 있거든 네 판단에 따라 조치하라. 너는 내 밑에서 주인이며, 그 밖에 다른 자는 없으니.’"]], [["“I saluted him, and turned to go away; he called me back.", "“저는 그분께 경례를 올리고 물러나려 돌아섰습니다. 그분이 저를 다시 부르셨습니다."], ["‘Ah, I forgot,’ he said.", "‘아, 깜박했구나.’ 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["‘Give me the map of the third floor.’ I gave it to him, and he spread it upon the table.", "‘셋째 층의 지도를 다오.’ 제가 그것을 드리자, 그분은 탁자 위에 그것을 펼치셨습니다. ‘게시우스, 여기다.’ 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. ‘이 감방을 보아라.’ 그분은 5번이라 매겨진 감방에 손가락을 짚으셨습니다."], ["‘Here, Gesius,’ he said, ‘see this cell.’ He laid his finger on the one numbered V.", "‘저 감방에는 세 사람이 갇혀 있다. 흉악한 자들로, 어떤 경로로 나라의 기밀을 손에 넣었고, 그 호기심의 대가를 치르고 있는 것이다."], ["‘There are three men confined in that cell, desperate characters, who by some means got hold of a state secret, and suffer for their curiosity, which’—he looked at me severely—‘in such matters is worse than a crime.", "그 호기심이란’—그분은 저를 매섭게 바라보셨습니다—‘그런 일에 관해서라면 죄보다도 더 나쁜 것이다. 그래서 그들은 눈이 멀고 혀가 잘린 채 그곳에 종신토록 두어졌다. 그들에게는 먹을 것과 마실 것 외에는 아무것도 주지 않을 것이며, 그것은 벽에 난 구멍—덧문으로 가려진 그 구멍을 너는 보게 될 것이다—을 통해 넣어 줄 것이다."], ["Accordingly, they are blind and tongueless, and are placed there for life.", "알아들었느냐, 게시우스?’ 저는 그분께 대답을 올렸습니다. ‘좋다.’ 하고 그분이 말을 이으셨습니다."], ["They shall have nothing but food and drink, to be given them through a hole, which you will find in the wall covered by a slide. Do you hear, Gesius?’ I made him answer.", "‘한 가지 더, 너는 결코 잊어서는 안 될 것이 있다. 잊는다면’—그분은 저를 위협하듯 바라보셨습니다—‘그들의 감방 문—같은 층의 5번 감방—바로 이것이다, 게시우스’—그분은 제 기억에 새기려 그 특정한 감방에 손가락을 짚으셨습니다—‘어떤 목적으로도 결코 열려서는 안 된다."], ["‘It is well,’ he continued. ‘One thing more which you shall not forget, or’—he looked at me threateningly—‘The door of their cell—cell number V.", "누구를 들이려고도, 내보내려고도 안 되며, 너 자신도 마찬가지다.’ ‘그러나 그들이 죽으면 어찌합니까?’ 제가 여쭈었습니다. ‘그들이 죽으면’ 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["on the same floor—this one, Gesius’—he put his finger on the particular cell to impress my memory—‘shall never be opened for any purpose, neither to let one in nor out, not even yourself.’ ‘But if they die?’ I asked.", "‘그 감방이 그들의 무덤이 될 것이다. 그들은 죽어 사라지도록 그곳에 두어졌다. 그 감방은 나병이 든 곳이다."], ["‘If they die,’ he said, ‘the cell shall be their tomb. They were put there to die, and be lost.", "알아들었느냐?’ 그 말씀과 함께 그분은 저를 보내셨습니다.” 게시우스는 말을 멈추고, 겉옷 가슴께에서 양피지 세 장을 꺼냈는데, 모두 세월과 손때로 누렇게 바래 있었다."], ["The cell is leprous. Do you understand?’ With that he let me go.”", "그중 하나를 골라 호민관 앞 탁자 위에 펼치며, 그는 간단히 말했다. “이것이 아래층입니다.”"]], [["Gesius stopped, and from the breast of his tunic drew three parchments, all much yellowed by time and use; selecting one of them, he spread it upon the table before the tribune, saying, simply, “This is the lower floor.”", "사람들 모두가 그 지도를 바라보았다. __________"]], [["The whole company looked at", "| |\n| 통로 |"]], [["_________________________________________ | | | Passage | | | |--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--| | | | | | | | V | IV | III | II | I | |_____|______|______|______|______|", "| |\n|--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--|"]], [["“This is exactly, O tribune, as I had it from Gratus. See, there is cell number V.,” said Gesius.", "| | | | | |\n| 5 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 1 |"]], [["“I see,” the tribune replied. “Go on now.", "|_|__|__|__|___|\n“이것이, 오 호민관님, 제가 그라투스에게서 받은 그대로입니다."], ["The cell was leprous, he said.”", "보십시오, 5번 감방이 있습니다.” 게시우스가 말했다."]], [["“I would like to ask you a question,”", "“보이는군.” 호민관이 대답했다. “이제 계속하게."], ["remarked the keeper, modestly.", "그가 그 감방이 나병이 든 곳이라 했다지.” “한 가지 여쭙고 싶습니다.” 감옥지기가 조심스레 말했다."]], [["The tribune assented.", "호민관이 그러라고 했다."]], [["“Had I not a right, under the circumstances, to believe the map a true one?”", "“그런 상황에서, 저로서는 그 지도를 참된 것이라 믿을 권리가 있지 않았겠습니까?” “달리 어찌하겠는가?”"]], [["“What else couldst thou?”", "“그런데, 그것은 참된 것이 아닙니다.”"]], [["“Well, it is not a true one.”", "책임자가 놀라 고개를 들었다."]], [["The chief looked up surprised.", "“그것은 참된 것이 아닙니다.” 감옥지기가 거듭 말했다. “그것은 그 층에 감방이 다섯뿐이라 보여 주지만, 실은 여섯입니다.” “여섯이라고 했나?”"]], [["“It is not a true one,” the keeper repeated.", "“그 층을 있는 그대로—제가 그러하다고 믿는 그대로 보여 드리겠습니다.”"], ["“It shows but five cells upon that floor, while there are six.”", "게시우스는 서판의 한 면에 다음과 같은 도면을 그려 호민관에게 건넸다."]], [["“Six, sayest thou?”", "__________\n| |"]], [["“I will show you the floor as it is—or as I believe it to be.”", "|--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--|"]], [["Upon a page of his tablets, Gesius drew the following diagram, and gave it to the tribune:", "| | | | | |\n| 5 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 1 |"]], [["_________________________________________ | | |--][---+---][---+---][---+---][---+---][--| | | | | | | | V | IV | III | II | I | |--][---+--------+--------+--------+-------| | VI | |_________________________________________|", "|--][---+--------+--------+--------+-------|\n| 6 |"]], [["“Thou hast done well,” said the tribune, examining the drawing, and thinking the narrative at an end.", "|__________|\n“수고했네.” 호민관이 그 그림을 살피며, 이야기가 끝났으리라 여기고 말했다."], ["“I will have the map corrected, or, better, I will have a new one made, and given thee.", "“지도를 바로잡게 하겠네. 아니, 더 나은 방법으로, 새로 하나 만들어 자네에게 주겠네."], ["Come for it in the morning.”", "아침에 받으러 오게.”"]], [["So saying, he arose.", "이렇게 말하며 그가 일어섰다."]], [["“But hear me further, O tribune.”", "“그러나 더 들어 주십시오, 오 호민관님.”"]], [["“To-morrow, Gesius, to-morrow.”", "“내일 하세, 게시우스, 내일.”"]], [["“That which I have yet to tell will not wait.”", "“제가 아직 말씀드리지 못한 것은 기다릴 수 없는 일입니다.”"]], [["The tribune good-naturedly resumed his chair.", "호민관이 너그럽게 다시 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“I will hurry,”", "“서두르겠습니다.”"], ["said the keeper, humbly, “only let me ask another question.", "감옥지기가 겸손하게 말했다. “다만 한 가지 더 여쭙겠습니다."], ["Had I not a right to believe Gratus in what he further told me as to the prisoners in cell number V.?”", "5번 감방의 죄수들에 관하여 그라투스가 제게 더 일러 준 말을, 제가 믿을 권리가 있지 않았겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, it was thy duty to believe there were three prisoners in the cell—prisoners of state—blind and without tongues.”", "“그렇네. 그 감방에 세 죄수가 있다고—나라의 죄수로서 눈멀고 혀 없는 자들이라고 믿는 것이 자네의 의무였네.”"]], [["“Well,”", "“그런데,”"], ["said the keeper, “that was not true either.”", "감옥지기가 말했다. “그것 또한 사실이 아니었습니다.”"]], [["“No!” said the tribune, with returning interest.", "“아니라고!” 호민관이 다시 흥미를 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Hear, and judge for yourself, O tribune.", "“들어 보시고 친히 판단하십시오, 오 호민관님."], ["As required, I visited all the cells, beginning with those on the first floor, and ending with those on the lower.", "명령받은 대로 저는 모든 감방을 둘러보았으니, 첫 층의 것들에서 시작하여 아래층의 것들로 마쳤습니다."], ["The order that the door of number V. should not be opened had been respected; through all the eight years food and drink for three men had been passed through a hole in the wall.", "5번 감방의 문은 결코 열어서는 안 된다는 명령은 지켜졌습니다. 여덟 해 내내 세 사람 몫의 먹을 것과 마실 것이 벽의 구멍을 통해 들어갔습니다."], ["I went to the door yesterday, curious to see the wretches who, against all expectation, had lived so long.", "어제 저는 그 문으로 갔습니다. 어떤 예상도 뒤엎고 그토록 오래 살아남은 가련한 자들을 보고 싶은 호기심에서였습니다."], ["The locks refused the key.", "자물쇠가 열쇠를 받지 않았습니다."], ["We pulled a little, and the door fell down, rusted from its hinges.", "우리가 조금 잡아당기자 문은 경첩에서 녹슬어 떨어져 버렸습니다."], ["Going in, I found but one man, old, blind, tongueless, and naked.", "안으로 들어가 보니, 단 한 사람뿐이었습니다. 늙고, 눈멀고, 혀가 없고, 벌거벗은 자였습니다."], ["His hair dropped in stiffened mats below his waist.", "그의 머리카락은 굳어 엉긴 채 허리 아래로 늘어져 있었습니다."], ["His skin was like the parchment there.", "그의 살갗은 저기 저 양피지 같았습니다."], ["He held his hands out, and the finger-nails curled and twisted like the claws of a bird.", "그가 두 손을 내밀었는데, 손톱은 새의 발톱처럼 말려 비틀려 있었습니다."], ["I asked him where his companions were.", "저는 그의 동료들이 어디 있느냐고 물었습니다."], ["He shook his head in denial.", "그는 고개를 저어 없다고 했습니다."], ["Thinking to find the others, we searched the cell.", "나머지를 찾을까 하여 우리는 감방을 뒤졌습니다."], ["The floor was dry; so were the walls.", "바닥은 말라 있었고, 벽도 그러했습니다."], ["If three men had been shut in there, and two of them had died, at least their bones would have endured.”", "세 사람이 그곳에 갇혔고 그중 둘이 죽었다면, 적어도 그들의 뼈는 남아 있었을 것입니다.”"]], [["“Wherefore thou thinkest—”", "“그래서 자네 생각은—”"]], [["“I think, O tribune, there has been but one prisoner there in the eight years.”", "“제 생각으로는, 오 호민관님, 여덟 해 동안 그곳에는 단 한 명의 죄수만 있었습니다.”"]], [["The chief regarded the keeper sharply, and said, “Have a care; thou art more than saying Valerius lied.”", "책임자가 감옥지기를 날카롭게 바라보며 말했다. “말조심하게. 자네는 발레리우스가 거짓을 말했다는 것 이상을 말하고 있네.”"]], [["Gesius bowed, but said, “He might have been mistaken.”", "게시우스는 고개를 숙였으나, 이렇게 말했다. “그분이 착오를 하셨을 수도 있습니다.”"]], [["“No, he was right,”", "“아니, 그는 옳았네.”"], ["said the tribune, warmly.", "호민관이 열을 띠며 말했다."], ["“By thine own statement he was right.", "“자네 자신의 진술로 보아 그는 옳았어."], ["Didst thou not say but now that for eight years food and drink had been furnished three men?”", "방금 자네가 여덟 해 동안 세 사람 몫의 먹을 것과 마실 것이 들어갔다고 하지 않았나?”"]], [["The bystanders approved the shrewdness of their chief; yet Gesius did not seem discomfited.", "곁에 있던 자들이 책임자의 명민함을 칭찬했다. 그러나 게시우스는 당황한 기색이 없었다."]], [["“You have but half the story, O tribune. When you have it all, you will agree with me.", "“호민관님은 아직 이야기의 절반밖에 듣지 못하셨습니다. 다 들으시면 제 말에 동의하실 겁니다."], ["You know what I did with the man: that I sent him to the bath, and had him shorn and clothed, and then took him to the gate of the Tower, and bade him go free.", "제가 그 사내를 어찌했는지 아실 겁니다. 저는 그를 목욕탕에 보내고, 머리를 깎고 옷을 입힌 다음, 요새의 문으로 데려가 자유로이 가라 했습니다."], ["I washed my hands of him.", "그렇게 그에게서 손을 씻은 것이지요."], ["To-day he came back, and was brought to me.", "오늘 그가 돌아왔고, 제게로 끌려왔습니다."], ["By signs and tears he at last made me understand he wished to return to his cell, and I so ordered.", "손짓과 눈물로 그는 마침내 제게, 제 감방으로 돌아가고 싶다는 뜻을 알렸고, 저는 그리하라 명했습니다."], ["As they were leading him off, he broke away and kissed my feet, and, by piteous dumb imploration, insisted I should go with him; and I went.", "그를 데려가려는데, 그가 뿌리치고 제 발에 입을 맞추더니, 애처롭게 말 못 하는 간청으로 저더러 함께 가 달라 졸랐습니다. 그래서 저는 갔습니다."], ["The mystery of the three men stayed in my mind.", "세 사람의 수수께끼가 제 마음에 남아 있었거든요."], ["I was not satisfied about it.", "저는 그것이 못내 미심쩍었습니다."], ["Now I am glad I yielded to his entreaty.”", "이제 저는 그의 애원에 응한 것을 기쁘게 여깁니다.”"]], [["The whole company at this point became very still.", "이 대목에서 사람들 모두가 매우 조용해졌다."]], [["“When we were in the cell again, and the prisoner knew it, he caught my hand eagerly, and led me to a hole like that through which we were accustomed to pass him his food.", "“우리가 다시 감방 안에 들었고, 죄수가 그것을 알아채자, 그는 제 손을 간절히 붙잡고 우리가 그에게 음식을 넣어 주곤 하던 그런 구멍으로 저를 이끌었습니다."], ["Though large enough to push your helmet through, it escaped me yesterday.", "투구를 밀어 넣을 만큼 컸는데도, 어제는 제 눈을 비껴갔습니다."], ["Still holding my hand, he put his face to the hole and gave a beast-like cry.", "여전히 제 손을 붙든 채, 그는 얼굴을 구멍에 대고 짐승 같은 소리를 질렀습니다."], ["A sound came faintly back.", "희미하게 무슨 소리가 되돌아왔습니다."], ["I was astonished, and drew him away, and called out, ‘Ho, here!’ At first there was no answer.", "저는 놀라 그를 떼어 놓고 외쳤습니다. ‘여보시오, 거기!’ 처음엔 아무 대답이 없었습니다."], ["I called again, and received back these words, ‘Be thou praised, O Lord!’ Yet more astonishing, O tribune, the voice was a woman’s.", "다시 외치자, 이런 말이 돌아왔습니다. ‘주여, 찬양받으소서!’ 더욱 놀라운 것은, 오 호민관님, 그 목소리가 여인의 것이었다는 사실입니다. 제가 물었습니다."], ["And I asked, ‘Who are you?’ and had reply, ‘A woman of Israel, entombed here with her daughter.", "‘당신은 누구요?’ 그러자 이런 대답이 돌아왔습니다. ‘이스라엘의 한 여인으로, 딸과 함께 이곳에 산 채로 묻혀 있습니다."], ["Help us quickly, or we die.’ I told them to be of cheer, and hurried here to know your will.”", "어서 도와주십시오, 그러지 않으면 우리는 죽습니다.’ 저는 그들에게 기운을 내라 이르고, 호민관님의 뜻을 알고자 이리로 서둘러 왔습니다.”"]], [["The tribune arose hastily.", "호민관이 황급히 일어섰다."]], [["“Thou wert right, Gesius,”", "“자네가 옳았네, 게시우스.”"], ["he said, “and I see now.", "그가 말했다. “이제 알겠군."], ["The map was a lie, and so was the tale of the three men.", "그 지도는 거짓이었고, 세 사람 이야기도 그러했네."], ["There have been better Romans than Valerius Gratus.”", "발레리우스 그라투스보다 나은 로마인들이 있었던 것이지.”"]], [["“Yes,” said the keeper.", "“그렇습니다.” 감옥지기가 말했다."], ["“I gleaned from the prisoner that he had regularly given the women of the food and drink he had received.”", "“저는 그 죄수에게서, 그가 받은 먹을 것과 마실 것을 그 여인들에게 꼬박꼬박 나누어 주었다는 것을 알아냈습니다.”"]], [["“It is accounted for,”", "“이제 설명이 되는군.”"], ["replied the tribune, and observing the countenances of his friends, and reflecting how well it would be to have witnesses, he added, “Let us rescue the women.", "호민관이 대답했다. 그리고 친구들의 얼굴을 살피며, 증인을 두는 것이 얼마나 좋을지 헤아리고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. “그 여인들을 구해 내세."], ["Come all.”", "모두 따라오게.”"]], [["Gesuis was pleased.", "게시우스는 흡족해했다."]], [["“We will have to pierce the wall,” he said.", "“벽을 뚫어야 할 것입니다.” 그가 말했다."], ["“I found where a door had been, but it was filled solidly with stones and mortar.”", "“문이 있던 자리를 찾았는데, 돌과 모르타르로 빈틈없이 메워져 있었습니다.”"]], [["The tribune stayed to say to a clerk, “Send workmen after me with tools.", "호민관은 머무르며 한 서기에게 일렀다. “일꾼들을 연장과 함께 내 뒤로 보내게. 서둘러라."], ["Make haste; but hold the report, for I see it will have to be corrected.”", "그러나 보고서는 붙들어 두게. 보아하니 바로잡아야 할 듯하니.”"]], [["In a short time they were gone.", "얼마 지나지 않아 그들은 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["“A woman of Israel, entombed here with her daughter.", "“이스라엘의 한 여인이 딸과 함께 이곳에 갇혀 있습니다. 어서 도와주십시오."], ["Help us quickly, or we die.”", "그러지 않으면 우리는 죽습니다.”"]], [["Such was the reply Gesius, the keeper, had from the cell which appears on his amended map as VI.", "간수 게시우스가 새로 고쳐 그린 지도에 6번으로 표시된 감방에서 들려온 대답이 바로 이것이었다."], ["The reader, when he observed the answer, knew who the unfortunates were, and, doubtless, said to himself, “At last the mother of Ben-Hur, and Tirzah, his sister!”", "독자는 이 대답을 보는 순간 그 불행한 이들이 누구인지 알아차렸을 것이며, 틀림없이 속으로 이렇게 중얼거렸을 것이다. “마침내 벤허의 어머니와 누이동생 티르자로구나!”"]], [["And so it was.", "과연 그러했다."]], [["The morning of their seizure, eight years before, they had been carried to the Tower, where Gratus proposed to put them out of the way.", "여드레 해 전, 그들이 붙잡힌 그날 아침에 두 사람은 탑으로 끌려갔고, 그라투스는 그곳에서 그들을 영영 없애 버릴 작정이었다."], ["He had chosen the Tower for the purpose as more immediately in his own keeping, and cell VI. because, first, it could be better lost than any other; and, secondly, it was infected with leprosy; for these prisoners were not merely to be put in a safe place, but in a place to die.", "그가 이 일을 위해 탑을 고른 것은 그곳이 자기 손아귀 안에 가장 가까이 있었기 때문이며, 6번 감방을 고른 것은 첫째로 그곳이 다른 어떤 곳보다 잊히기 쉬웠기 때문이고, 둘째로 그곳이 나병에 오염되어 있었기 때문이다. 이 죄수들은 그저 안전한 곳에 가두기 위함이 아니라, 죽으라고 가두는 것이었으니 말이다."], ["They were, accordingly, taken down by slaves in the night-time, when there were no witnesses of the deed; then, in completion of the savage task, the same slaves walled up the door, after which they were themselves separated, and sent away never to be heard of more.", "그리하여 두 사람은 그 일을 목격할 사람이 아무도 없는 한밤중에 노예들의 손에 끌려 내려갔다. 그 야만스러운 일을 마무리하기 위해 그 노예들은 문을 벽돌로 막아 버렸고, 그러고 나서 그들 자신은 뿔뿔이 갈라져 멀리 보내져 다시는 소식이 들리지 않았다."], ["To save accusation, and, in the event of discovery, to leave himself such justification as might be allowed in a distinction between the infliction of a punishment and the commission of a double murder, Gratus preferred sinking his victims where natural death was certain, though slow.", "고발을 면하기 위해, 그리고 만에 하나 발각될 경우 형벌을 내린 것과 두 사람을 죽인 것 사이의 구별 속에서 자신에게 허락될 만한 정당화의 구실을 남겨 두기 위해, 그라투스는 비록 더디기는 하나 자연사가 확실한 곳에 희생자들을 가라앉히는 편을 택했다."], ["That they might linger along, he selected a convict who had been made blind and tongueless, and sank him in the only connecting cell, there to serve them with food and drink.", "그들이 더 오래 목숨을 부지하도록, 그는 눈이 멀고 혀가 잘린 죄수 하나를 골라 그들과 통하는 단 하나의 감방에 가두어, 그가 그들에게 먹을 것과 마실 것을 가져다주게 했다."], ["Under no circumstances could the poor wretch tell the tale or identify either the prisoners or their doomsman.", "그 불쌍한 자는 어떤 경우에도 이 일을 발설하거나, 죄수들이 누구인지 또 누가 그들을 죽음에 몰아넣었는지 알릴 수가 없었다."], ["So, with a cunning partly due to Messala, the Roman, under color of punishing a brood of assassins, smoothed a path to confiscation of the estate of the Hurs, of which no portion ever reached the imperial coffers.", "이렇게 하여, 얼마쯤은 로마인 메살라의 머리에서 나온 술책으로, 한 무리의 자객을 처벌한다는 구실 아래, 후르 가문의 재산을 몰수하는 길이 매끄럽게 닦였으니, 그 재산 가운데 단 한 몫도 황실 금고에 들어간 일이 없었다."]], [["As the last step in the scheme, Gratus summarily removed the old keeper of the prisons; not because he knew what had been done—for he did not—but because, knowing the underground floors as he did, it would be next to impossible to keep the transaction from him.", "계획의 마지막 단계로, 그라투스는 옛 간수를 별안간 자리에서 내쫓았다. 그가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 알았기 때문은 아니었으니—그는 알지 못했다—다만 지하층을 그토록 훤히 꿰고 있는 그에게 이 거래를 숨기기란 거의 불가능했기 때문이다."], ["Then, with masterly ingenuity, the procurator had new maps drawn for delivery to a new keeper, with the omission, as we have seen, of cell VI.", "그런 다음, 그 충독은 능란한 솜씨로 새 간수에게 넘겨줄 새 지도를 그리게 하면서, 우리가 이미 보았듯이 6번 감방을 빠뜨렸다."], ["The instructions given the latter, taken with the omission on the map, accomplished the design—the cell and its unhappy tenants were all alike lost.", "새 간수에게 내려진 지시와 지도에서의 누락이 한데 어우러져 그 계략은 이루어졌으니—감방도 그 가엾은 거주자들도 모조리 똑같이 잊혀 버린 것이다."]], [["What may be thought of the life of the mother and daughter during the eight years must have relation to their culture and previous habits.", "여드레 해 동안 그 어머니와 딸의 삶이 어떠했는지는 그들의 교양과 지난날의 습관에 비추어 생각해야 한다."], ["Conditions are pleasant or grievous to us according to our sensibilities.", "우리에게 어떤 처지가 즐거운가 괴로운가는 우리의 감수성에 달려 있다."], ["It is not extreme to say, if there was a sudden exit of all men from the world, heaven, as prefigured in the Christian idea, would not be a heaven to the majority; on the other hand, neither would all suffer equally in the so-called Tophet.", "만일 세상에서 모든 사람이 별안간 떠나간다면, 그리스도교의 관념 속에 그려진 천국이라 한들 대다수에게는 천국이 아닐 것이며, 다른 한편으로 이른바 토페트에서도 누구나 똑같이 고통받지는 않으리라고 말한다 해서 그것이 지나친 말은 아니다."], ["Cultivation has its balances.", "교양에는 그 나름의 저울이 있다."], ["As the mind is made intelligent, the capacity of the soul for pure enjoyment is proportionally increased. Well, therefore, if it be saved!", "정신이 총명해질수록 영혼이 순수한 기쁨을 누리는 능력도 그에 비례하여 커진다. 그러니 그것이 구원받는다면 얼마나 좋은 일인가!"], ["If lost, however, alas that it ever had cultivation!", "그러나 잃어버린다면, 아아, 교양을 지녔던 것이 오히려 한이 되리라!"], ["its capacity for enjoyment in the one case is the measure of its capacity to suffer in the other.", "한쪽 경우에 누리는 능력이 곧 다른 쪽 경우에 고통받는 능력의 척도가 되는 까닭이다."], ["Wherefore repentance must be something more than mere remorse for sins; it comprehends a change of nature befitting heaven.", "그러므로 회개란 죄에 대한 단순한 뉘우침 이상의 것이어야 하니, 그것은 천국에 어울리는 본성의 변화를 아우르는 것이다."]], [["We repeat, to form an adequate idea of the suffering endured by the mother of Ben-Hur, the reader must think of her spirit and its sensibilities as much as, if not more than, of the conditions of the immurement; the question being, not what the conditions were, but how she was affected by them.", "다시 한번 말하건대, 벤허의 어머니가 견뎌 낸 고통을 제대로 그려 보려면, 독자는 갇혀 있던 처지의 조건 못지않게, 아니 그보다 더, 그 영혼과 그 감수성을 헤아려야 한다. 문제는 그 조건이 어떠했는가가 아니라, 그것이 그녀에게 어떻게 작용했는가에 있기 때문이다."], ["And now we may be permitted to say it was in anticipation of this thought that the scene in the summer-house on the roof of the family palace was given so fully in the beginning of the Second Book of our story.", "이제 우리는, 가문의 궁전 옥상 정자에서 벌어진 장면을 우리 이야기 둘째 권 첫머리에서 그토록 자세히 그려 둔 것이 바로 이 생각을 미리 내다보았기 때문이라고 밝혀도 좋을 것이다."], ["So, too, to be helpful when the inquiry should come up, we ventured the elaborate description of the palace of the Hurs.", "마찬가지로, 이런 물음이 떠오를 때 도움이 되도록, 우리는 후르 가문의 궁전을 공들여 묘사하는 일도 마다하지 않았다."]], [["In other words, let the serene, happy, luxurious life in the princely house be recalled and contrasted with this existence in the lower dungeon of the Tower of Antonia; then if the reader, in his effort to realize the misery of the woman, persists in mere reference to conditions physical, he cannot go amiss; as he is a lover of his kind, tender of heart, he will be melted with much sympathy.", "다시 말해, 그 고귀한 집에서 누리던 평온하고 행복하며 호사스러운 삶을 떠올려, 안토니아 탑의 아래쪽 토굴에서 영위하는 이 생존과 견주어 보라. 그런 다음 독자가 그 여인의 비참함을 실감하려는 노력 속에서 한낱 물리적 조건만을 끝까지 들먹인다 해도 그것이 그릇된 일은 아니다. 그가 동족을 사랑하는 이요 마음이 여린 이라면 깊은 연민에 가슴이 녹아내릴 것이다."], ["But will he go further; will he more than sympathize with her; will he share her agony of mind and spirit; will he at least try to measure it—let him recall her as she discoursed to her son of God and nations and heroes; one moment a philosopher, the next a teacher, and all the time a mother.", "그러나 그가 거기서 더 나아가려 한다면, 그녀에게 연민을 느끼는 것 이상을 하려 한다면, 그녀의 마음과 영혼의 고뇌를 함께 나누려 한다면, 적어도 그것을 가늠해 보려 한다면—그녀가 아들에게 하느님과 민족들과 영웅들에 관해 들려주던 모습을, 한순간은 철학자였다가 다음 순간은 교사였으며 내내 어머니였던 그 모습을 떠올리게 하라."]], [["Would you hurt a man keenest, strike at his self-love; would you hurt a woman worst, aim at her affections.", "남자에게 가장 날카로운 상처를 입히려거든 그의 자존심을 치라. 여자에게 가장 모진 상처를 입히려거든 그녀의 정을 겨누라."]], [["With quickened remembrance of these unfortunates—remembrance of them as they were—let us go down and see them as they are.", "이 불행한 이들에 대한 기억을 새로이 떠올렸으니—그들이 예전에 어떠했는지를 떠올렸으니—이제 아래로 내려가 그들이 지금 어떠한지를 보기로 하자."]], [["The cell VI. was in form as Gesius drew it on his map.", "6번 감방은 게시우스가 지도에 그린 그대로의 모양이었다."], ["Of its dimensions but little idea can be had; enough that it was a roomy, roughened interior, with ledged and broken walls and floor.", "그 크기는 짐작하기 어려우나, 거칠고 널찍한 내부에 턱이 지고 부서진 벽과 바닥이 있었다는 것으로 족하다."]], [["In the beginning, the site of the Macedonian Castle was separated from the site of the Temple by a narrow but deep cliff somewhat in shape of a wedge.", "애초에 마케도니아 성채가 들어선 자리는 성전이 들어선 자리와 좁지만 깊은 절벽으로 나뉘어 있었으니, 그 모양은 다소 쐐기를 닮아 있었다."], ["The workmen, wishing to hew out a series of chambers, made their entry in the north face of the cleft, and worked in, leaving a ceiling of the natural stone; delving farther, they executed the cells V., IV., III., II., I., with no connection with number VI. except through number V.", "일꾼들은 여러 개의 방을 깎아 내고자 그 갈라진 틈의 북쪽 면에 입구를 내어 안쪽으로 파 들어가면서, 천장은 자연석 그대로 남겨 두었다. 더 깊이 파 들어가 5번, 4번, 3번, 2번, 1번 감방을 만들었는데, 이것들은 5번을 거치지 않고서는 6번과 통하는 길이 없었다."], ["In like manner, they constructed the passage and stairs to the floor above.", "같은 방식으로 그들은 위층으로 이어지는 통로와 계단을 만들었다."], ["The process of the work was precisely that resorted to in carving out the Tombs of the Kings, yet to be seen a short distance north of Jerusalem; only when the cutting was done, cell VI. was enclosed on its outer side by a wall of prodigious stones, in which, for ventilation, narrow apertures were left bevelled like modern port-holes.", "이 작업의 과정은 예루살렘 북쪽으로 조금 떨어진 곳에서 지금도 볼 수 있는 왕들의 무덤을 깎아 내던 그 방식과 똑같았다. 다만 깎는 일이 끝났을 때 6번 감방은 그 바깥쪽이 엄청나게 큰 돌들로 쌓은 벽으로 둘러막혔는데, 그 벽에는 환기를 위해 오늘날의 포안처럼 비스듬히 깎인 좁은 틈이 남겨졌다."], ["Herod, when he took hold of the Temple and Tower, put a facing yet more massive upon this outer wall, and shut up all the apertures but one, which yet admitted a little vitalizing air, and a ray of light not nearly strong enough to redeem the room from darkness.", "헤롯은 성전과 탑에 손을 댈 적에 이 바깥벽 위에 한층 더 육중한 외장을 입히고, 그 틈을 모두 막아 버리되 단 하나만 남겨 두었으니, 그 틈으로는 그래도 목숨을 부지할 만한 공기와, 그 방을 어둠에서 건져 내기에는 어림없이 약한 한 줄기 빛이 들어왔다."]], [["Such was cell VI.", "이것이 6번 감방이었다."]], [["Startle not now!", "지금 너무 놀라지는 말라!"]], [["The description of the blind and tongueless wretch just liberated from cell V. may be accepted to break the horror of what is coming.", "5번 감방에서 막 풀려난 그 눈멀고 혀 잘린 가엾은 자에 대한 묘사를, 이제 닥쳐올 끔찍한 광경의 충격을 누그러뜨리는 데 받아들여도 좋으리라."]], [["The two women are grouped close by the aperture; one is seated, the other is half reclining against her; there is nothing between them and the bare rock.", "두 여인은 그 틈 가까이에 함께 모여 있다. 하나는 앉아 있고, 다른 하나는 그녀에게 반쯤 기대어 누워 있으며, 그들과 맨바위 사이에는 아무것도 없다."], ["The light, slanting upwards, strikes them with ghastly effect, and we cannot avoid seeing they are without vesture or covering.", "비스듬히 위로 비쳐 드는 빛이 그들을 소름 끼치게 비추는데, 우리는 그들에게 옷도 덮을 것도 없다는 사실을 보지 않을 수 없다."], ["At the same time we are helped to the knowledge that love is there yet, for the two are in each other’s arms.", "동시에 우리는 그곳에 아직 사랑이 있음을 알게 되니, 두 사람이 서로를 끌어안고 있기 때문이다."], ["Riches take wings, comforts vanish, hope withers away, but love stays with us. Love is God.", "재물은 날개 돋쳐 날아가고 안락은 사라지며 희망은 시들어 가지만, 사랑은 우리 곁에 머문다. 사랑은 하느님이다."]], [["Where the two are thus grouped the stony floor is polished shining smooth.", "두 사람이 이렇게 모여 있는 자리의 돌바닥은 반들반들 윤이 나도록 닳아 있다."], ["Who shall say how much of the eight years they have spent in that space there in front of the aperture, nursing their hope of rescue by that timid yet friendly ray of light?", "그 틈에서 들어오는 수줍지만 정겨운 빛에 기대어 구조의 희망을 키우며, 여드레 해 가운데 얼마를 그 틈 앞의 그 자리에서 보냈는지 누가 말할 수 있으랴?"], ["When the brightness came creeping in, they knew it was dawn; when it began to fade, they knew the world was hushing for the night, which could not be anywhere so long and utterly dark as with them. The world!", "환한 빛이 슬며시 기어들면 그들은 동이 텄음을 알았고, 그 빛이 스러지기 시작하면 세상이 밤을 맞아 잠잠해짐을 알았으니, 그 밤은 어디에서도 그들에게만큼 그토록 길고 그토록 완전히 어둡지는 않았으리라. 세상이여!"], ["Through that crevice, as if it were broad and high as a king’s gate, they went to the world in thought, and passed the weary time going up and down as spirits go, looking and asking, the one for her son, the other for her brother.", "마치 그 틈이 임금의 성문처럼 넓고 높기라도 한 듯, 그들은 그 틈을 지나 마음으로 세상에 나아가, 영혼이 떠돌듯 오르내리며 그 고단한 시간을 보냈으니, 보고 또 물으며, 한 사람은 제 아들을, 다른 한 사람은 제 오빠를 찾았다."], ["On the seas they sought him, and on the islands of the seas; to-day he was in this city, to-morrow in that other; and everywhere, and at all times, he was a flitting sojourner; for, as they lived waiting for him, he lived looking for them.", "바다 위에서도 그를 찾았고 바다의 섬들에서도 그를 찾았으며, 오늘은 그가 이 도시에, 내일은 저 다른 도시에 있었으니, 어디에서나 또 언제나 그는 잠깐 머물다 떠나는 나그네였다. 그들이 그를 기다리며 살았듯이, 그도 그들을 찾으며 살았기 때문이다."], ["How often their thoughts passed each other in the endless search, his coming, theirs going!", "그 끝없는 찾음 속에서 그들의 생각이 얼마나 자주 엇갈렸던가—그는 오고 그들은 가니!"], ["It was such sweet flattery for them to say to each other, “While he lives, we shall not be forgotten; as long as he remembers us, there is hope!”", "“그가 살아 있는 한 우리는 잊히지 않으리라. 그가 우리를 기억하는 한 희망이 있으리라!” 하고 서로에게 말하는 것은 그들에게 얼마나 달콤한 위안이었던가!"], ["The strength one can eke from little, who knows till he has been subjected to the trial?", "시련을 겪어 보기 전에야, 사람이 그토록 보잘것없는 것에서 얼마만큼의 힘을 짜낼 수 있는지 누가 알겠는가?"]], [["Our recollections of them in former days enjoin us to be respectful; their sorrows clothe them with sanctity.", "지난날의 그들에 대한 우리의 기억은 우리에게 경의를 갖추라 명한다. 그들의 슬픔이 그들에게 거룩함을 입히고 있다."], ["Without going too near, across the dungeon, we see they have undergone a change of appearance not to be accounted for by time or long confinement.", "토굴을 가로질러 너무 가까이 다가가지 않더라도, 우리는 그들이 세월이나 오랜 감금만으로는 설명할 수 없는 외모의 변화를 겪었음을 알 수 있다."], ["The mother was beautiful as a woman, the daughter beautiful as a child; not even love could say so much now.", "어머니는 여인으로서 아름다웠고 딸은 아이로서 아름다웠으나, 이제는 사랑조차도 그렇게 말할 수 없다."], ["Their hair is long, unkempt, and strangely white; they make us shrink and shudder with an indefinable repulsion, though the effect may be from an illusory glozing of the light glimmering dismally through the unhealthy murk; or they may be enduring the tortures of hunger and thirst, not having had to eat or drink since their servant, the convict, was taken away—that is, since yesterday.", "그들의 머리카락은 길고 헝클어졌으며 기이하게도 새하얗다. 그 모습은 우리를 형언할 수 없는 혐오로 움츠리고 떨게 하나, 그 느낌은 어쩌면 병든 어둠을 음산하게 가물거리며 비추는 빛이 만들어 낸 착시 탓일지도 모른다. 또 어쩌면 그들은 굶주림과 목마름의 고통을 견디고 있는지도 모르니, 그들을 섬기던 죄수가 끌려간 뒤로—다시 말해 어제부터—먹지도 마시지도 못했기 때문이다."]], [["Tirzah, reclining against her mother in half embrace, moans piteously.", "티르자는 어머니에게 반쯤 안긴 채 기대어 가엾게 신음했다."]], [["“Be quiet, Tirzah.", "“조용히 하렴, 티르자."], ["They will come.", "그들이 올 거야."], ["God is good. We have been mindful of him, and forgotten not to pray at every sounding of the trumpets over in the Temple.", "하느님은 선하시단다. 우리는 그분을 잊지 않았고, 성전에서 나팔이 울릴 때마다 기도하기를 잊지 않았어."], ["The light, you see, is still bright; the sun is standing in the south sky yet, and it is hardly more than the seventh hour.", "보렴, 빛이 아직 환하구나. 해가 아직 남쪽 하늘에 떠 있으니, 일곱째 시가 겨우 지났을 뿐이야."], ["Somebody will come to us.", "누군가 우리에게 올 거야."], ["Let us have faith. God is good.”", "믿음을 가지자. 하느님은 선하시단다.”"]], [["Thus the mother.", "이렇게 어머니가 말했다."], ["The words were simple and effective, although, eight years being now to be added to the thirteen she had attained when last we saw her, Tirzah was no longer a child.", "그 말은 소박하면서도 힘이 있었다. 우리가 마지막으로 그녀를 보았을 때 열세 살이던 티르자에게 이제 여덟 해를 더해야 했으니, 그녀는 더 이상 아이가 아니었다."]], [["“I will try and be strong, mother,” she said.", "“힘을 내 볼게요, 어머니.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Your suffering must be as great as mine; and I do so want to live for you and my brother!", "“어머니의 고통도 제 고통만큼이나 크실 텐데, 저는 어머니와 오빠를 위해 살고 싶어요."], ["But my tongue burns, my lips scorch.", "그런데 혀가 타들어 가고 입술이 바싹 마르네요."], ["I wonder where he is, and if he will ever, ever find us!”", "오빠가 어디에 있을까, 그리고 언젠가, 정말 언젠가 우리를 찾아낼 수 있을까 궁금해요!”"]], [["There is something in the voices that strikes us singularly—an unexpected tone, sharp, dry, metallic, unnatural.", "그 목소리에는 우리를 묘하게 사로잡는 무엇이 있다—뜻밖의 음색, 날카롭고 메마르며 쇠붙이 같고 부자연스러운 음색이다."]], [["The mother draws the daughter closer to her breast, and says, “I dreamed about him last night, and saw him as plainly, Tirzah, as I see you.", "어머니는 딸을 제 가슴으로 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. “어젯밤에 그 애 꿈을 꾸었단다. 티르자, 지금 너를 보는 것만큼이나 또렷이 그 애를 보았어."], ["We must believe in dreams, you know, because our fathers did.", "우리는 꿈을 믿어야 한단다, 알지? 우리 조상들이 그러셨으니까."], ["The Lord spoke to them so often in that way.", "주님께서는 그렇게 자주 그들에게 말씀하셨지."], ["I thought we were in the Women’s Court just before the Gate Beautiful; there were many women with us; and he came and stood in the shade of the Gate, and looked here and there, at this one and that.", "우리가 아름다운 문 바로 앞 여인의 뜰에 있는 것 같았어. 우리와 함께 많은 여인들이 있었지. 그러자 그 애가 와서 그 문 그늘에 서서, 이쪽저쪽을, 이 사람 저 사람을 둘러보더구나."], ["My heart beat strong.", "내 가슴이 세차게 뛰었어."], ["I knew he was looking for us, and stretched my arms to him, and ran, calling him.", "그 애가 우리를 찾고 있다는 걸 알았으니까. 나는 그 애에게 두 팔을 뻗고, 그 애를 부르며 달려갔지."], ["He heard me and saw me, but he did not know me.", "그 애가 내 소리를 듣고 나를 보았지만, 나를 알아보지 못하더구나."], ["In a moment he was gone.”", "한순간에 그 애는 사라져 버렸어.”"]], [["“Would it not be so, mother, if we were to meet him in fact? We are so changed.”", "“정말로 그 애를 만난다 해도 그렇지 않을까요, 어머니? 우리는 너무나 변해 버렸잖아요.”"]], [["“It might be so; but—”", "“그럴지도 모르지. 하지만—”"], ["The mother’s head droops, and her face knits as with a wrench of pain; recovering, however, she goes on—“but we could make ourselves known to him.”", "어머니의 머리가 수그러지고 그 얼굴이 고통에 비틀리듯 일그러졌다. 그러나 곧 추스르고는 말을 이었다. “하지만 우리가 우리 자신을 그 애에게 알릴 수는 있을 거야.”"]], [["Tirzah tossed her arms, and moaned again.", "티르자는 두 팔을 내저으며 다시 신음했다."]], [["“Water, mother, water, though but a drop.”", "“물을, 어머니, 물을, 단 한 방울이라도.”"]], [["The mother stares around in blank helplessness.", "어머니는 망연한 무력감에 사로잡혀 사방을 둘러본다."], ["She has named God so often, and so often promised in his name, the repetition is beginning to have a mocking effect upon herself.", "그녀는 하느님의 이름을 너무도 자주 불렀고 그 이름으로 너무도 자주 약속했기에, 그 되풀이가 이제 그녀 자신에게 조롱처럼 느껴지기 시작한다."], ["A shadow passes before her dimming the dim light, and she is brought down to think of death as very near, waiting to come in as her faith goes out.", "그림자 하나가 그 어슴푸레한 빛을 더욱 흐리며 그녀 앞을 스쳐 지나가니, 그녀는 죽음이 아주 가까이 와서, 그녀의 믿음이 꺼져 갈 적에 들어오려고 기다리고 있음을 생각하게 된다."], ["Hardly knowing what she does, speaking aimlessly, because speak she must, she says again,", "자신이 무엇을 하는지도 거의 알지 못한 채, 그저 말을 해야 하기에 두서없이 그녀는 다시 말한다."]], [["“Patience, Tirzah; they are coming—they are almost here.”", "“참으렴, 티르자. 그들이 오고 있어—거의 다 왔단다.”"]], [["She thought she heard a sound over by the little trap in the partition-wall through which they held all their actual communication with the world.", "칸막이벽에 난 작은 쪽문—그들이 세상과 실제로 통하는 모든 일을 그것을 통해 해 온—쪽에서 무슨 소리가 들린 것 같았다."], ["And she was not mistaken. A moment, and the cry of the convict rang through the cell.", "그리고 그녀의 생각은 틀리지 않았다. 한순간, 그 죄수의 외침이 감방을 울렸다."], ["Tirzah heard it also; and they both arose, still keeping hold of each other.", "티르자도 그 소리를 들었고, 두 사람은 여전히 서로를 붙든 채 일어섰다."]], [["“Praised be the Lord forever!”", "“주님께 영원토록 찬미를!”"], ["exclaimed the mother, with the fervor of restored faith and hope.", "어머니가 되살아난 믿음과 희망의 열렬함으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Ho, there!”", "“여보시오, 거기!”"], ["they heard next; and then, “Who are you?”", "다음에 그들이 들은 것은 이것이었고, 이어서, “당신들은 누구요?”"]], [["The voice was strange. What matter?", "낯선 목소리였다. 그것이 무슨 상관이랴?"], ["Except from Tirzah, they were the first and only words the mother had heard in eight years.", "티르자를 빼고는, 그것은 여드레 해 동안 어머니가 들은 첫 번째이자 유일한 말이었다."], ["The revulsion was mighty—from death to life—and so instantly!", "그 격변은 엄청났으니—죽음에서 삶으로, 그것도 그토록 순식간에!"]], [["“A woman of Israel, entombed here with her daughter.", "“이스라엘의 한 여인이 딸과 함께 이곳에 갇혀 있습니다. 어서 도와주십시오."], ["Help us quickly, or we die.”", "그러지 않으면 우리는 죽습니다.”"]], [["“Be of cheer.", "“기운을 내시오."], ["I will return.”", "내 다시 돌아오리다.”"]], [["The women sobbed aloud.", "여인들은 소리 내어 흐느꼈다."], ["They were found; help was coming.", "그들은 발견되었고, 도움이 오고 있었다."], ["From wish to wish hope flew as the twittering swallows fly.", "바람에서 바람으로, 희망은 지저귀는 제비처럼 날아다녔다."], ["They were found; they would be released.", "그들은 발견되었으니, 풀려나리라."], ["And restoration would follow—restoration to all they had lost—home, society, property, son and brother!", "그리고 회복이 뒤따르리라—그들이 잃은 모든 것의 회복, 집과 사회와 재산과 아들과 오빠의 회복이!"], ["The scanty light glozed them with the glory of day, and, forgetful of pain and thirst and hunger, and of the menace of death, they sank upon the floor and cried, keeping fast hold of each other the while.", "그 보잘것없는 빛이 그들을 대낮의 영광으로 입혔으니, 고통과 목마름과 굶주림과 죽음의 위협마저 잊은 채, 그들은 내내 서로를 꼭 붙든 채로 바닥에 주저앉아 울었다."]], [["And this time they had not long to wait.", "그리고 이번에는 오래 기다릴 것도 없었다."], ["Gesius, the keeper, told his tale methodically, but finished it at last.", "간수 게시우스가 차근차근 자기 이야기를 늘어놓았으나, 마침내 그것을 끝냈다."], ["The tribune was prompt.", "그 호민관은 지체하지 않았다."]], [["“Within there!” he shouted through the trap.", "“거기 안에 있는 이들이여!” 그가 쪽문 너머로 외쳤다."]], [["“Here!” said the mother, rising.", "“여기요!” 어머니가 일어서며 말했다."]], [["Directly she heard another sound in another place, as of blows on the wall—blows quick, ringing, and delivered with iron tools.", "곧이어 그녀는 다른 곳에서 또 다른 소리를 들었으니, 마치 벽을 두드리는 소리 같았다—재빠르고 쟁쟁하며 쇠 연장으로 내리치는 타격 소리였다."], ["She did not speak, nor did Tirzah, but they listened, well knowing the meaning of it all—that a way to liberty was being made for them.", "그녀도 티르자도 말하지 않았으나, 둘은 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 잘 알기에 귀를 기울였다—자유로 이어지는 길이 그들을 위해 뚫리고 있다는 것을."], ["So men a long time buried in deep mines hear the coming of rescuers, heralded by thrust of bar and beat of pick, and answer gratefully with heart-throbs, their eyes fixed upon the spot whence the sounds proceed; and they cannot look away, lest the work should cease, and they be returned to despair.", "깊은 광산에 오래 묻혀 있던 사람들이 쇠지레를 찔러 넣고 곡괭이를 내리치는 소리로 미리 알려지는 구조자들의 다가옴을 듣고, 그 소리가 나는 자리에 눈을 못 박은 채 심장의 고동으로 고마이 화답하는 것처럼, 그리고 작업이 멈추어 다시 절망 속으로 돌려보내질까 봐 차마 눈을 돌리지 못하는 것처럼, 그들도 그러했다."]], [["The arms outside were strong, the hands skillful, the will good.", "바깥의 팔들은 억세었고, 손들은 능란했으며, 의지는 굳건했다."], ["Each instant the blows sounded more plainly; now and then a piece fell with a crash; and liberty came nearer and nearer.", "매 순간 그 타격 소리가 더욱 또렷이 울려왔다. 이따금 돌 한 조각이 우지끈 떨어졌고, 자유는 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["Presently the workmen could be heard speaking.", "이윽고 일꾼들이 말하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Then—O happiness!—through a crevice flashed a red ray of torches.", "그러자—오, 행복이여!—한 틈새로 횃불의 붉은 빛줄기가 번뜩였다."], ["Into the darkness it cut incisive as diamond brilliance, beautiful as if from a spear of the morning.", "그것은 다이아몬드의 광채처럼 예리하게, 아침의 창에서 쏟아진 듯 아름답게 어둠을 갈랐다."]], [["“It is he, mother, it is he!", "“그예요, 어머니, 그예요!"], ["He has found us at last!”", "마침내 우리를 찾아냈어요!”"], ["cried Tirzah, with the quickened fancy of youth.", "티르자가 젊음 특유의 들뜬 상상으로 외쳤다."]], [["But the mother answered meekly, “God is good!”", "그러나 어머니는 온순하게 대답했다. “하느님은 선하시단다!”"]], [["A block fell inside, and another—then a great mass, and the door was open.", "안쪽에서 돌덩이 하나가 떨어지고, 또 하나가 떨어지더니—이윽고 커다란 덩어리가 떨어지고, 문이 열렸다."], ["A man grimed with mortar and stone-dust stepped in, and stopped, holding a torch over his head.", "회반죽과 돌가루를 뒤집어쓴 사내 하나가 안으로 들어서서, 머리 위로 횃불을 치켜든 채 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["Two or three others followed with torches, and stood aside for the tribune to enter.", "두엇이 횃불을 들고 뒤따라 들어와, 그 호민관이 들어올 수 있도록 옆으로 비켜섰다."]], [["Respect for women is not all a conventionality, for it is the best proof of their proper nature.", "여인에 대한 존중은 한낱 관습만은 아니니, 그것은 그들의 올바른 본성을 보여 주는 가장 좋은 증거이기 때문이다."], ["The tribune stopped, because they fled from him—not with fear, be it said, but shame; nor yet, O reader, from shame alone!", "그 호민관이 걸음을 멈춘 것은, 그들이 그에게서 달아났기 때문이다—말해 두건대, 두려움 탓이 아니라 부끄러움 탓이었다. 아니, 독자여, 부끄러움 탓만도 아니었다!"], ["From the obscurity of their partial hiding he heard these words, the saddest, most dreadful, most utterly despairing of the human tongue:", "어슴푸레 반쯤 몸을 숨긴 그 자리에서 그는 이런 말을 들었으니, 사람의 혀가 내뱉을 수 있는 가장 슬프고, 가장 끔찍하며, 가장 철저히 절망적인 말이었다."]], [["“Come not near us—unclean, unclean!”", "“우리에게 가까이 오지 마세요—부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"]], [["The men flared their torches while they stared at each other.", "사내들은 서로를 멀거니 바라보면서 횃불을 흔들어 댔다."]], [["“Unclean, unclean!”", "“부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"], ["came from the corner again, a slow tremulous wail exceedingly sorrowful.", "그 구석에서 다시금 그 소리가 들려왔으니, 더없이 슬픈, 느릿하고 떨리는 통곡이었다."], ["With such a cry we can imagine a spirit vanishing from the gates of Paradise, looking back the while.", "그런 외침과 함께라면, 우리는 한 영혼이 낙원의 문에서 사라지면서 내내 뒤를 돌아보는 모습을 떠올릴 수 있으리라."]], [["So the widow and mother performed her duty, and in the moment realized that the freedom she had prayed for and dreamed of, fruit of scarlet and gold seen afar, was but an apple of Sodom in the hand.", "이렇게 하여 그 과부이자 어머니는 제 의무를 다했고, 그 순간 그녀가 기도하고 꿈꾸던 자유가, 멀리서 보이던 진홍빛과 황금빛의 열매가 손에 쥐고 보니 한낱 소돔의 사과에 지나지 않음을 깨달았다."]], [["# SHE AND TIRZAH WERE—LEPERS!", "# SHE AND TIRZAH WERE—LEPERS!"]], [["Possibly the reader does not know all the word means.", "그녀와 티르자는—나병 환자였던 것이다!"], ["Let him be told it with reference to the Law of that time, only a little modified in this.", "아마도 독자는 그 말이 지닌 뜻을 다 알지 못할 것이다. 당시의 율법에 비추어, 오늘날 조금 달라졌을 뿐인 그 뜻을 일러 주기로 하자."]], [["“These four are accounted as dead—the blind, the leper, the poor, and the childless.” Thus the Talmud.", "“이 넷은 죽은 자로 여겨진다—눈먼 자, 나병 환자, 가난한 자, 자식 없는 자.” 탈무드에 이렇게 적혀 있다."]], [["That is, to be a leper was to be treated as dead—to be excluded from the city as a corpse; to be spoken to by the best beloved and most loving only at a distance; to dwell with none but lepers; to be utterly unprivileged; to be denied the rites of the Temple and the synagogue; to go about in rent garments and with covered mouth, except when crying, “Unclean, unclean!”", "다시 말해, 나병 환자가 된다는 것은 죽은 자처럼 다루어지는 것이었다—주검처럼 도시 밖으로 내쳐지는 것이요, 가장 사랑하고 가장 사랑하는 이들조차 오직 멀리서만 말을 건넬 수 있는 것이요, 나병 환자 외에는 그 누구와도 함께 살 수 없는 것이요, 모든 권리를 철저히 빼앗기는 것이요, 성전과 회당의 예식을 거부당하는 것이요, “부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"], ["to find home in the wilderness or in abandoned tombs; to become a materialized specter of Hinnom and Gehenna; to be at all times less a living offence to others than a breathing torment to self; afraid to die, yet without hope except in death.", "하고 외칠 때를 빼고는 찢어진 옷차림에 입을 가린 채 돌아다니는 것이요, 광야나 버려진 무덤에서 거처를 찾는 것이요, 힌놈과 게헨나의 형상화된 망령이 되는 것이요, 늘 남에게 살아 있는 모욕거리라기보다 자기 자신에게 숨 쉬는 고통거리가 되는 것이요, 죽음을 두려워하면서도 죽음 외에는 아무런 희망이 없는 것이었다."]], [["Once—she might not tell the day or the year, for down in the haunted hell even time was lost—once the mother felt a dry scurf in the palm of her right hand, a trifle which she tried to wash away.", "언젠가—그날도 그해도 그녀는 말할 수 없었으니, 그 귀신 들린 지옥 아래에서는 시간마저 잃어버렸기에—언젠가 어머니는 오른손 바닥에서 마른 딱지를 느꼈으니, 그것은 그녀가 씻어 내려 한 하찮은 것이었다."], ["It clung to the member pertinaciously; yet she thought but little of the sign till Tirzah complained that she, too, was attacked in the same way.", "그것은 그 손에 끈질기게 달라붙었으나, 그녀는 티르자도 똑같은 식으로 침범당했다고 호소할 때까지 그 징조를 대수롭지 않게 여겼다."], ["The supply of water was scant, and they denied themselves drink that they might use it as a curative.", "물은 모자랐으나, 그들은 그것을 치료에 쓰고자 마실 것을 스스로 삼갔다."], ["At length the whole hand was attacked; the skin cracked open, the fingernails loosened from the flesh.", "마침내 손 전체가 침범당했으니, 살갗이 갈라져 터지고 손톱이 살에서 떨어져 나갔다."], ["There was not much pain withal, chiefly a steadily increasing discomfort.", "그렇다 해도 아픔은 그리 크지 않았으니, 주로 꾸준히 더해 가는 불편함뿐이었다."], ["Later their lips began to parch and seam. One day the mother, who was cleanly to godliness, and struggled against the impurities of the dungeon with all ingenuity, thinking the enemy was taking hold on Tirzah’s face, led her to the light, and, looking with the inspiration of a terrible dread, lo!", "그 뒤 그들의 입술이 바싹 마르고 갈라지기 시작했다. 어느 날, 청결함이 곧 경건함이라 여기며 온갖 슬기를 다해 토굴의 더러움과 맞서 싸우던 어머니가, 그 적이 티르자의 얼굴을 사로잡고 있다고 여겨 그녀를 빛 쪽으로 이끌고는, 끔찍한 두려움이 불어넣은 직감으로 들여다보니, 보라!"], ["the young girl’s eyebrows were white as snow.", "그 어린 소녀의 눈썹이 눈처럼 새하얗게 세어 있었다."]], [["Oh, the anguish of that assurance!", "오, 그 확인이 주는 고뇌여!"]], [["The mother sat awhile speechless, motionless, paralyzed of soul, and capable of but one thought—leprosy, leprosy!", "어머니는 한동안 말도 없이 미동도 없이 앉아 있었으니, 영혼이 마비된 채 단 하나의 생각만을 떠올릴 수 있었다—나병이다, 나병이다!"]], [["When she began to think, mother-like, it was not of herself, but her child, and, mother-like, her natural tenderness turned to courage, and she made ready for the last sacrifice of perfect heroism.", "그녀가 생각하기 시작했을 때, 어머니답게도 그것은 자기 자신이 아니라 제 아이에 관한 것이었으며, 어머니답게도 그녀의 타고난 다정함은 용기로 바뀌어, 그녀는 완벽한 영웅적 자기희생의 마지막 제물을 바칠 채비를 했다."], ["She buried her knowledge in her heart; hopeless herself, she redoubled her devotion to Tirzah, and with wonderful ingenuity—wonderful chiefly in its very inexhaustibility—continued to keep the daughter ignorant of what they were beset with, and even hopeful that it was nothing.", "그녀는 자신이 안 것을 가슴속에 묻었다. 스스로는 희망이 없으면서도 티르자에 대한 헌신을 곱절로 더했으며, 놀라운 슬기로—주로 그 끝없는 마르지 않음에서 놀라운 슬기로—그들에게 들이닥친 것이 무엇인지 딸이 알지 못하게, 나아가 그것이 아무것도 아니리라는 희망마저 품게 계속 지켜 주었다."], ["She repeated her little games, and retold her stories, and invented new ones, and listened with ever so much pleasure to the songs she would have from Tirzah, while on her own wasting lips the psalms of the singing king and their race served to bring soothing of forgetfulness, and keep alive in them both the recollection of the God who would seem to have abandoned them—the world not more lightly or utterly.", "그녀는 자신의 자그마한 놀이를 되풀이하고, 이야기를 되풀이해 들려주고, 새 이야기를 지어내고, 티르자에게 청해 듣는 노래에 더없는 기쁨으로 귀를 기울였다. 한편 자신의 시들어 가는 입술 위에서는 노래하는 임금과 그들 겨레의 시편이 망각의 위안을 가져다주고, 그들 둘 안에 그들을 버린 듯한 하느님—세상도 그보다 더 가벼이 또 철저히 버리지는 못했을 그 하느님—에 대한 기억을 살아 있게 했다."]], [["Slowly, steadily, with horrible certainty, the disease spread, after a while bleaching their heads white, eating holes in their lips and eyelids, and covering their bodies with scales; then it fell to their throats shrilling their voices, and to their joints, hardening the tissues and cartilages—slowly, and, as the mother well knew, past remedy, it was affecting their lungs and arteries and bones, at each advance making the sufferers more and more loathsome; and so it would continue till death, which might be years before them.", "천천히, 꾸준히, 끔찍한 확실함으로 그 병은 퍼져 나가, 얼마 뒤 그들의 머리를 새하얗게 표백하고, 입술과 눈꺼풀에 구멍을 파먹고, 그들의 몸을 비늘로 뒤덮었다. 그러고는 그들의 목구멍으로 내려가 목소리를 날카롭게 만들고, 관절로 내려가 조직과 연골을 굳게 만들었다—천천히, 그리고 어머니가 잘 알고 있었듯이 치유할 길 없이, 그것은 그들의 폐와 동맥과 뼈를 침범해, 한 걸음씩 나아갈 때마다 그 고통받는 이들을 더욱더 흉측하게 만들었다. 그리고 그것은 죽음에 이르기까지 그렇게 계속될 터였으니, 그 죽음은 여러 해 뒤에나 올지도 몰랐다."]], [["Another day of dread at length came—the day the mother, under impulsion of duty, at last told Tirzah the name of their ailment; and the two, in agony of despair, prayed that the end might come quickly.", "마침내 또 다른 두려운 날이 왔다—의무에 떠밀린 어머니가 끝내 티르자에게 그들 병의 이름을 일러 준 그날이었다. 그리고 두 사람은 절망의 고뇌 속에서, 그 끝이 어서 오기를 빌었다."]], [["Still, as is the force of habit, these so afflicted grew in time not merely to speak composedly of their disease; they beheld the hideous transformation of their persons as of course, and in despite clung to existence.", "그래도, 습관의 힘이란 그러하기에, 이토록 시달린 그들은 이윽고 제 병에 대해 차분히 말하게 되었을 뿐 아니라, 제 몸이 흉측하게 변해 가는 것을 당연한 일로 바라보면서도 그에 맞서 끈질기게 삶에 매달렸다."], ["One tie to earth remained to them; unmindful of their own loneliness, they kept up a certain spirit by talking and dreaming of Ben-Hur.", "그들에게는 이 땅과 잇는 끈 하나가 남아 있었다. 자신들의 외로움은 아랑곳하지 않고, 벤허에 관해 이야기하고 꿈꾸며 어떤 기개를 지켜 갔다."], ["The mother promised reunion with him to the sister, and she to the mother, not doubting, either of them, that he was equally faithful to them, and would be equally happy of the meeting.", "어머니는 누이동생에게 그와의 재회를 약속했고, 누이동생은 어머니에게 그것을 약속했으니, 둘 다 그가 자기들에게 똑같이 충실하며 그 만남을 똑같이 기뻐하리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다."], ["And with the spinning and respinning of this slender thread they found pleasure, and excused their not dying.", "그리고 이 가느다란 실을 잣고 또 다시 자으면서 그들은 기쁨을 찾았고, 죽지 않고 있는 것에 대한 핑계로 삼았다."], ["In such manner as we have seen, they were solacing themselves the moment Gesius called them, at the end of twelve hours’ fasting and thirst.", "우리가 본 것과 같은 식으로, 게시우스가 그들을 불렀던 그 순간에도, 열두 시간의 단식과 갈증 끝에 그들은 그렇게 스스로를 위로하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["The torches flashed redly through the dungeon, and liberty was come. “God is good,”", "횃불이 토굴을 가로질러 붉게 번뜩였고, 자유가 다가왔다. “하느님은 선하시다.”"], ["the widow cried—not for what had been, O reader, but for what was.", "과부가 외쳤으니—독자여, 지나간 일 때문이 아니라 지금 있는 일 때문이었다."], ["In thankfulness for present mercy, nothing so becomes us as losing sight of past ills.", "지금의 자비에 감사함에 있어, 지난날의 불행을 잊어버리는 것만큼 우리에게 어울리는 일은 없다."]], [["The tribune came directly; then in the corner to which she had fled, suddenly a sense of duty smote the elder of the women, and straightway the awful warning—", "그 호민관이 곧장 다가왔다. 그러자 그녀가 달아나 숨은 구석에서, 별안간 의무감이 그 나이 든 여인을 때렸으니, 곧바로 그 무시무시한 경고가 터져 나왔다—"]], [["“Unclean, unclean!”", "“부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"]], [["Ah, the pang the effort to acquit herself of that duty cost the mother!", "아아, 그 의무를 다하려는 노력이 어머니에게 안긴 고통이여!"], ["Not all the selfishness of joy over the prospect could keep her blind to the consequences of release, now that it was at hand.", "풀려나리라는 전망에 들뜬 그 어떤 이기적 기쁨도, 이제 그것이 손에 잡히려는 마당에 풀려남이 가져올 결과를 그녀가 보지 못하도록 눈멀게 할 수는 없었다."], ["The old happy life could never be again.", "옛날의 행복한 삶은 결코 다시 올 수 없었다."], ["If she went near the house called home, it would be to stop at the gate and cry, “Unclean, unclean!”", "그녀가 집이라 부르던 그 집 가까이 간다 해도, 그것은 대문 앞에 멈추어 서서 “부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"], ["She must go about with the yearnings of love alive in her breast strong as ever, and more sensitive even, because return in kind could not be.", "하고 외치기 위함이리라. 그녀는 가슴속에 사랑의 그리움을 여전히 변함없이 강하게, 아니 도리어 더 예민하게 살아 있는 채로 돌아다녀야 했으니, 같은 사랑으로 되갚음을 받을 수 없는 까닭이었다."], ["The boy of whom she had so constantly thought, and with all sweet promises such as mothers find their purest delight in, must, at meeting her, stand afar off.", "그녀가 그토록 끊임없이 생각해 온 아이, 어머니들이 가장 순수한 기쁨을 찾는 그 온갖 달콤한 약속들과 함께 생각해 온 그 아이는, 그녀를 만날 때 멀찍이 떨어져 서야만 했다."], ["If he held out his hands to her, and called “Mother, mother,” for very love of him she must answer, “Unclean, unclean!”", "그가 그녀에게 두 손을 내밀며 “어머니, 어머니” 하고 부른다 해도, 바로 그를 사랑하기에 그녀는 “부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"], ["And this other child, before whom, in want of other covering, she was spreading her long tangled locks, bleached unnaturally white—ah!", "하고 대답해야 했다. 그리고 달리 덮을 것이 없어 부자연스럽게 새하얗게 센 그 길고 헝클어진 머리카락을 펼쳐 가려 주고 있는 이 다른 아이—아아!"], ["that she was she must continue, sole partner of her blasted remainder of life.", "그 아이도 그렇게 남아 있어야 했으니, 그녀의 망가진 남은 삶의 유일한 동반자였다."], ["Yet, O reader, the brave woman accepted the lot, and took up the cry which had been its sign immemorially, and which thenceforward was to be her salutation without change—“Unclean, unclean!”", "그러나 독자여, 그 용감한 여인은 그 운명을 받아들였고, 까마득한 옛날부터 그 표시였으며 그때부터 변함없이 그녀의 인사가 될 그 외침을 입에 담았다—“부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"]], [["The tribune heard it with a tremor, but kept his place.", "그 호민관은 그 소리를 듣고 몸을 떨었으나, 제자리를 지켰다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "“당신들은 누구요?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Two women dying of hunger and thirst.", "“굶주림과 목마름으로 죽어 가는 두 여인입니다."], ["Yet”—the mother did not falter—“come not near us, nor touch the floor or the wall.", "하지만”—어머니는 머뭇거리지 않았다—“우리에게 가까이 오지 마시고, 바닥이나 벽도 만지지 마세요."], ["Unclean, unclean!”", "부정합니다, 부정합니다!”"]], [["“Give me thy story, woman—thy name, and when thou wert put here, and by whom, and for what.”", "“당신의 이야기를 내게 들려주시오, 여인이여—당신의 이름과, 언제 이곳에 갇혔으며, 누구의 손에, 무슨 까닭으로 갇혔는지.”"]], [["“There was once in this city of Jerusalem a Prince Ben-Hur, the friend of all generous Romans, and who had Cæsar for his friend.", "“언젠가 이 예루살렘 도시에 후르 가문의 한 영주가 있었으니, 그는 모든 너그러운 로마인의 벗이요 카이사르를 벗으로 둔 사람이었습니다."], ["I am his widow, and this one with me is his child.", "저는 그의 과부요, 저와 함께 있는 이는 그의 아이입니다."], ["How may I tell you for what we were sunk here, when I do not know, unless it was because we were rich?", "우리가 무슨 까닭으로 이곳에 가라앉혀졌는지 제가 어찌 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니까, 제 자신이 모르는데 말입니다. 다만 우리가 부유했던 탓이 아니라면요."], ["Valerius Gratus can tell you who our enemy was, and when our imprisonment began.", "누가 우리의 원수였으며 우리의 갇힘이 언제 시작되었는지는 발레리우스 그라투스가 말해 줄 수 있을 것입니다."], ["I cannot.", "저는 모릅니다."], ["See to what we have been reduced—oh, see, and have pity!”", "우리가 어떤 꼴로 전락했는지 보십시오—오, 보시고, 가엾이 여겨 주십시오!”"]], [["The air was heavy with the pest and the smoke of the torches, yet the Roman called one of the torch-bearers to his side, and wrote the answer nearly word for word.", "공기는 역병과 횃불 연기로 무거웠으나, 그 로마인은 횃불을 든 자 하나를 제 곁으로 불러, 그 대답을 거의 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 받아 적었다."], ["It was terse, and comprehensive, containing at once a history, an accusation, and a prayer.", "그것은 간결하면서도 두루 갖추어, 역사와 고발과 탄원을 한꺼번에 담고 있었다."], ["No common person could have made it, and he could not but pity and believe.", "여느 평범한 사람이 할 수 있는 말이 아니었으니, 그는 가엾이 여기고 믿지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Thou shalt have relief, woman,”", "“구제를 받을 것이오, 여인이여.”"], ["he said, closing the tablets.", "그가 서판을 덮으며 말했다."], ["“I will send thee food and drink.”", "“당신에게 먹을 것과 마실 것을 보내 주겠소.”"]], [["“And raiment, and purifying water, we pray you, O generous Roman!”", "“그리고 옷과, 몸을 정결케 할 물도, 부디 베풀어 주소서, 오 너그러운 로마인이여!”"]], [["“As thou wilt,”", "“당신 뜻대로 하리다.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“God is good,” said the widow, sobbing.", "“하느님은 선하시다.” 과부가 흐느끼며 말했다."], ["“May his peace abide with you!”", "“그분의 평안이 당신과 함께 머물기를!”"]], [["“And, further,” he added, “I cannot see thee again.", "“그리고, 한 가지 더,” 그가 덧붙였다."], ["Make preparation, and to-night I will have thee taken to the gate of the Tower, and set free.", "“나는 당신을 다시 볼 수 없소. 채비를 하시오. 오늘 밤 당신을 탑의 문까지 데려가 풀어 주게 하리다."], ["Thou knowest the law.", "당신은 율법을 알고 있을 것이오."], ["Farewell.”", "잘 있으시오.”"]], [["He spoke to the men, and went out the door.", "그는 사내들에게 말을 건네고는 문밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Very shortly some slaves came to the cell with a large gurglet of water, a basin and napkins, a platter with bread and meat, and some garments of women’s wear; and, setting them down within reach of the prisoners, they ran away.", "머잖아 노예 몇이 감방으로 와서, 물이 가득 든 커다란 항아리와 대야와 수건, 빵과 고기를 담은 접시, 그리고 여인이 입을 옷가지를 가져왔다. 그것들을 죄수들의 손이 닿는 곳에 내려놓고는 그들은 달아났다."]], [["About the middle of the first watch, the two were conducted to the gate, and turned into the street.", "첫 번째 경(更)의 한복판쯤에, 두 사람은 그 문까지 안내되어 거리로 내보내졌다."], ["So the Roman quit himself of them, and in the city of their fathers they were once more free.", "이렇게 하여 그 로마인은 그들에게서 손을 떼었고, 조상들의 도시에서 그들은 다시 한번 자유로운 몸이 되었다."]], [["Up to the stars, twinkling merrily as of old, they looked; then they asked themselves,", "그들은 예전처럼 즐거이 반짝이는 별들을 우러러보았다. 그러고는 스스로에게 물었다."]], [["“What next?", "“이제 어떻게 하나?"], ["and where to?”", "어디로 가야 하나?”"]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["About the hour Gesius, the keeper, made his appearance before the tribune in the Tower of Antonia, a footman was climbing the eastern face of Mount Olivet.", "「안토니아 탑」에서 간수 게시우스가 호민관 앞에 모습을 드러낸 그 무렵, 한 도보 여행자가 올리브 산의 동쪽 비탈을 오르고 있었다."], ["The road was rough and dusty, and vegetation on that side burned brown, for it was the dry season in Judea.", "길은 험하고 먼지투성이였으며, 그쪽 비탈의 초목은 누렇게 타들어 가 있었으니, 유대는 건기였던 것이다."], ["Well for the traveller that he had youth and strength, not to speak of the cool, flowing garments with which he was clothed.", "그 나그네에게는 젊음과 힘이 있었으니 다행이었고, 게다가 시원하게 흘러내리는 옷을 걸치고 있었음은 두말할 나위도 없다."]], [["He proceeded slowly, looking often to his right and left; not with the vexed, anxious expression which marks a man going forward uncertain of the way, but rather the air with which one approaches as old acquaintance after a long separation—half of pleasure, half of inquiry; as if he were saying, “I am glad to be with you again; let me see in what you are changed.”", "그는 천천히 나아가며 좌우를 자주 둘러보았다. 길을 확신하지 못한 채 앞으로 가는 사람에게서 드러나는 짜증스럽고 불안한 표정이 아니라, 오랜 이별 끝에 옛 친구에게 다가가는 사람의 분위기였다. 절반은 기쁨이요, 절반은 캐묻는 마음이었으니, 마치 \"다시 너와 함께하게 되어 기쁘다, 네가 어떻게 변했는지 어디 보자\" 하고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["As he arose higher, he sometimes paused to look behind him over the gradually widening view terminating in the mountains of Moab; but when at length he drew near the summit, he quickened his step, unmindful of fatigue, and hurried on without pause or turning of the face.", "더 높이 올라가면서 그는 이따금 걸음을 멈추고 뒤를 돌아보았으니, 점점 넓어지다 마침내 모압의 산들에서 끝나는 풍경을 바라보았다. 그러나 마침내 정상 가까이에 이르자 그는 피로도 잊은 채 걸음을 재촉했고, 멈추지도 얼굴을 돌리지도 않은 채 서둘러 나아갔다."], ["On the summit—to reach which he bent his steps somewhat right of the beaten path—he came to a dead stop, arrested as if by a strong hand.", "정상에서는—그곳에 닿기 위해 그는 다져진 길에서 다소 오른쪽으로 발걸음을 틀었다—마치 억센 손에 붙들리기라도 한 듯 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Then one might have seen his eyes dilate, his cheeks flush, his breath quicken, effects all of one bright sweeping glance at what lay before him.", "그러자 그의 눈동자가 커지고, 두 뺨이 붉어지며, 숨결이 빨라지는 것을 누군가는 보았을 법했으니, 이 모두가 앞에 펼쳐진 광경을 한 번에 환히 훑어본 데서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["The traveller, good reader, was no other than Ben-Hur; the spectacle, Jerusalem.", "선량한 독자여, 그 나그네는 다름 아닌 벤허였다. 그 광경은 예루살렘이었다."]], [["Not the Holy City of to-day, but the Holy City as left by Herod—the Holy City of the Christ.", "오늘날의 거룩한 도시가 아니라, 헤롯이 남겨 놓은 거룩한 도시, 곧 그리스도의 거룩한 도시였다."], ["Beautiful yet, as seen from old Olivet, what must it have been then?", "오래된 올리브 산에서 바라본 지금도 아름답거늘, 그때는 과연 어떠했겠는가?"]], [["Ben-Hur betook him to a stone and sat down, and, stripping his head of the close white handkerchief which served it for covering, made the survey at leisure.", "벤허는 바위 하나로 다가가 앉았다. 그러고는 머리를 덮어 주던 흰 천을 벗고, 느긋하게 그 광경을 살폈다."]], [["The same has been done often since by a great variety of persons, under circumstances surpassingly singular—by the son of Vespasian, by the Islamite, by the Crusader, conquerors all of them; by many a pilgrim from the great New World, which waited discovery nearly fifteen hundred years after the time of our story; but of the multitude probably not one has taken that view with sensations more keenly poignant, more sadly sweet, more proudly bitter, than Ben-Hur.", "그 뒤로 이와 똑같은 일을 더없이 기이한 상황 속에서 갖가지 사람들이 자주 행했다. 베스파시아누스의 아들이, 이슬람교도가, 십자군이 그러했으니, 모두가 정복자였다. 우리 이야기의 시대로부터 천오백 년 가까이 지나서야 발견될 그 위대한 신세계에서 온 수많은 순례자들도 그러했다."], ["He was stirred by recollections of his countrymen, their triumphs and vicissitudes, their history the history of God.", "그러나 그 무리 가운데 벤허보다 더 사무치게 가슴 아프고, 더 슬프도록 달콤하며, 더 자랑스럽도록 쓰라린 감정으로 그 풍경을 바라본 이는 아마 하나도 없었을 것이다."], ["The city was of their building, at once a lasting testimony of their crimes and devotion, their weakness and genius, their religion and their irreligion.", "그는 제 동족의 추억에, 그들의 승리와 흥망에, 곧 하느님의 역사이기도 한 그들의 역사에 마음이 흔들렸다. 그 도시는 그들이 세운 것으로, 그들의 죄악과 헌신, 약함과 천재성, 신앙과 불신앙을 한꺼번에 길이 증언하는 곳이었다."], ["Though he had seen Rome to familiarity, he was gratified.", "그는 로마를 익숙해질 만큼 보아 왔건만, 그래도 흐뭇했다."], ["The sight filled a measure of pride which would have made him drunk with vainglory but for the thought, princely as the property was, it did not any longer belong to his countrymen; the worship in the Temple was by permission of strangers; the hill where David dwelt was a marbled cheat—an office in which the chosen of the Lord were wrung and wrung for taxes, and scourged for very deathlessness of faith.", "그 광경은 그를 자긍심으로 가득 채워, 헛된 영광에 취하게 만들었을 법도 했다. 다만 한 가지 생각이 그를 붙들었으니, 그토록 왕가에 어울리는 재산이건만 그것이 더는 제 동족의 것이 아니라는 사실이었다. 성전에서 드리는 예배는 이방인의 허락에 따른 것이요, 다윗이 살던 언덕은 대리석으로 꾸민 기만이었으니, 그곳은 주께서 택하신 백성이 세금 때문에 쥐어짜이고 또 쥐어짜이며, 죽음으로도 꺾이지 않는 신앙 때문에 채찍질당하는 관청이 되어 있었다."], ["These, however, were pleasures and griefs of patriotism common to every Jew of the period; in addition, Ben-Hur brought with him a personal history which would not out of mind for other consideration whatever, which the spectacle served only to freshen and vivify.", "그러나 이런 것들은 그 시대 모든 유대인이 함께 느끼던 애국의 기쁨이요 슬픔이었다. 거기에 더하여 벤허는 그 무엇으로도 마음에서 지울 수 없는 개인의 사연을 품고 있었으니, 그 광경은 다만 그 사연을 더 새롭고 생생하게 되살려 줄 뿐이었다."]], [["A country of hills changes but little; where the hills are of rock, it changes not at all.", "언덕의 고장은 거의 변하지 않는다. 언덕이 바위로 이루어진 곳이라면 조금도 변하지 않는다."], ["The scene Ben-Hur beheld is the same now, except as respects the city.", "벤허가 바라본 풍경은 도시를 빼면 지금도 그대로다."], ["The failure is in the handiwork of man alone.", "무너진 것은 오직 사람의 솜씨뿐이다."]], [["The sun dealt more kindly by the west side of Olivet than by the east, and men were certainly more loving towards it.", "해는 올리브 산의 동쪽보다 서쪽을 더 다정하게 대해 주었고, 사람들 또한 분명 그쪽을 더 사랑했다."], ["The vines with which it was partially clad, and the sprinkling of trees, chiefly figs and old wild olives, were comparatively green.", "산을 군데군데 덮은 포도나무와 듬성듬성 박힌 나무들, 주로 무화과나무와 늙은 야생 올리브나무는 그래도 푸른 편이었다."], ["Down to the dry bed of the Cedron the verdure extended, a refreshment to the vision; there Olivet ceased and Moriah began—a wall of bluff boldness, white as snow, founded by Solomon, completed by Herod.", "그 푸르름은 메마른 케드론 골짜기의 바닥까지 뻗어 내려가, 보는 눈을 시원하게 했다. 그곳에서 올리브 산이 끝나고 모리아 산이 시작되었으니, 눈처럼 흰 절벽 같은 성벽이 우뚝 솟아 있었다. 솔로몬이 기초를 놓고 헤롯이 완성한 것이었다."], ["Up, up the wall the eye climbed course by course of the ponderous rocks composing it—up to Solomon’s Porch, which was as the pedestal of the monument, the hill being the plinth.", "눈길은 그 성벽을 이루는 육중한 바위들을 한 켜 한 켜 짚으며 위로, 위로 기어올라 솔로몬 행각에 이르렀으니, 언덕이 주춧돌이라면 그 행각은 기념비의 받침대였다."], ["Lingering there a moment, the eye resumed its climbing, going next to the Gentiles’ Court, then to the Israelites’ Court, then to the Women’s Court, then to the Court of the Priests, each a pillared tier of white marble, one above the other in terraced retrocession; over them all a crown of crowns infinitely sacred, infinitely beautiful, majestic in proportions, effulgent with beaten gold—lo! the Tent, the Tabernacle, the Holy of Holies.", "그곳에서 잠시 머물던 눈길은 다시 오르기 시작하여, 다음으로 이방인의 뜰로, 그다음으로 이스라엘인의 뜰로, 그다음으로 여인의 뜰로, 그다음으로 제사장의 뜰로 옮겨 갔으니, 저마다 흰 대리석 기둥이 늘어선 한 층이 되어, 계단 모양으로 뒤로 물러나며 차례차례 위로 솟아 있었다. 그 모두의 위에는 더없이 거룩하고 더없이 아름다우며, 그 비례가 장엄하고, 두드려 편 금으로 눈부신 으뜸가는 면류관이 얹혀 있었으니, 보라, 곧 천막이요, 성막이요, 지성소였다."], ["The Ark was not there, but Jehovah was—in the faith of every child of Israel he was there a personal Presence.", "언약궤는 그곳에 없었으나 여호와는 그곳에 계셨다. 이스라엘의 모든 자손의 믿음 속에서 그분은 그곳에 친히 임재하신 분이었다."], ["As a temple, as a monument, there was nowhere anything of man’s building to approach that superlative apparition.", "성전으로 보나 기념비로 보나, 사람의 손으로 세운 것 가운데 그 더없이 빼어난 모습에 견줄 만한 것은 어디에도 없었다."], ["Now, not a stone of it remains above another.", "이제는 그 돌 하나도 다른 돌 위에 남아 있지 않다."], ["Who shall rebuild that building?", "누가 그 건물을 다시 세우겠는가?"], ["When shall the rebuilding be begun?", "그 재건은 언제 시작되겠는가?"], ["So asks every pilgrim who has stood where Ben-Hur was—he asks, knowing the answer is in the bosom of God, whose secrets are not least marvellous in their well-keeping.", "벤허가 섰던 그 자리에 서 본 순례자라면 누구나 이렇게 묻는다. 그러면서도 그 답이 하느님의 품속에 있음을 안다. 그분의 비밀은 그 잘 간직됨에서 더없이 놀랍다."], ["And then the third question, What of him who foretold the ruin which has so certainly befallen? God?", "그리고 세 번째 물음이 따른다. 그토록 분명히 닥쳐온 그 멸망을 미리 일러 준 이는 과연 누구인가? 하느님이신가?"], ["Or man of God?", "하느님의 사람인가?"], ["Or—enough that the question is for us to answer.", "아니면—그 물음은 우리가 답할 몫이라는 것으로 충분하다."]], [["And still Ben-Hur’s eyes climbed on and up—up over the roof of the Temple, to the hill Zion, consecrated to sacred memories, inseparable from the anointed kings.", "그래도 벤허의 눈길은 위로, 위로 기어올라—성전 지붕을 넘어, 거룩한 추억에 바쳐진, 기름 부음 받은 왕들과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 시온 언덕에 이르렀다. 그는 알고 있었다."], ["He knew the Cheesemonger’s Valley dipped deep down between Moriah and Zion; that it was spanned by the Xystus; that there were gardens and palaces in its depths; but over them all his thoughts soared with his vision to the great grouping on the royal hill—the house of Caiaphas, the Central Synagogue, the Roman Praetorium, Hippicus the eternal, and the sad but mighty cenotaphs Phasaelus and Mariamne—all relieved against Gareb, purpling in the distance.", "치즈장수 골짜기가 모리아와 시온 사이로 깊이 파여 내려가 있고, 그 위로 크시스토스 다리가 걸쳐 있으며, 그 골짜기 깊은 곳에는 정원과 궁전이 있다는 것을. 그러나 그 모든 것을 넘어 그의 생각은 그의 시선과 더불어 왕가의 언덕 위에 모여 선 큰 무리로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 가야바의 집, 중앙 회당, 로마 총독 관저, 영원한 히피쿠스 탑, 그리고 슬프되 위풍당당한 기념탑 파사엘루스와 마리암네—이 모두가 멀리서 자줏빛으로 물들어 가는 가렙 언덕을 배경으로 또렷이 드러나 있었다."], ["And when midst them he singled out the palace of Herod, what could he but think of the King Who Was Coming, to whom he was himself devoted, whose path he had undertaken to smooth, whose empty hands he dreamed of filling?", "그 가운데서 헤롯 궁전을 가려내었을 때, 그가 어찌 장차 오실 왕을 떠올리지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 그 자신이 몸 바쳐 섬기는 분, 그 길을 평탄케 하기로 떠맡은 분, 그 빈손을 채워 드리는 꿈을 꾸어 온 분이었다."], ["And forward ran his fancy to the day the new King should come to claim his own and take possession of it—of Moriah and its Temple; of Zion and its towers and palaces; of Antonia, frowning darkly there just to the right of the Temple; of the new unwalled city of Bezetha; of the millions of Israel to assemble with palm-branches and banners, to sing rejoicing because the Lord had conquered and given them the world.", "그리고 그의 상상은 앞으로 내달려, 새 왕이 자기 것을 되찾으러 와서 그것을 차지할 그날에 다다랐다—모리아와 그 성전을, 시온과 그 망루와 궁전들을, 성전 바로 오른편에서 음울하게 노려보고 있는 안토니아를, 성벽 없는 새 도시 베제다를, 그리고 종려 가지와 깃발을 들고 모여들어 주께서 정복하시고 그들에게 세상을 주셨다며 기뻐 노래할 수백만 이스라엘 백성을 차지할 그날이었다."]], [["Men speak of dreaming as if it were a phenomenon of night and sleep.", "사람들은 꿈꾸는 일을 마치 밤과 잠의 현상인 양 말한다."], ["They should know better.", "그들은 더 잘 알아야 한다."], ["All results achieved by us are self-promised, and all self-promises are made in dreams awake.", "우리가 이룬 온갖 성과는 스스로에게 한 약속에서 비롯되며, 그 모든 자기 약속은 깨어 있는 채 꾸는 꿈속에서 맺어진다."], ["Dreaming is the relief of labor, the wine that sustains us in act.", "꿈은 노고의 위안이요, 행동 속에서 우리를 떠받치는 포도주다."], ["We learn to love labor, not for itself, but for the opportunity it furnishes for dreaming, which is the great under-monotone of real life, unheard, unnoticed, because of its constancy.", "우리는 노동을 사랑하기를 배우되, 노동 그 자체 때문이 아니라 그것이 베풀어 주는 꿈꿀 기회 때문에 사랑한다. 그 꿈이야말로 실제 삶의 밑바닥을 흐르는 거대한 저음이니, 한결같기에 들리지도 않고 알아차려지지도 않는다."], ["Living is dreaming. Only in the grave are there no dreams.", "산다는 것은 꿈꾸는 것이다. 오직 무덤 속에만 꿈이 없다."], ["Let no one smile at Ben-Hur for doing that which he himself would have done at that time and place under the same circumstances.", "그러므로 벤허가 그런 꿈을 꾸었다고 누구도 비웃지 말라. 같은 시간 같은 장소 같은 처지였다면 비웃는 자 자신도 그리했을 것이다."]], [["The sun stooped low in its course.", "해는 제 길을 따라 낮게 기울었다."], ["Awhile the flaring disk seemed to perch itself on the far summit of the mountains in the west, brazening all the sky above the city, and rimming the walls and towers with the brightness of gold.", "한동안 활활 타오르는 둥근 해는 서쪽 산의 먼 봉우리에 걸터앉은 듯 보였으니, 도시 위의 온 하늘을 놋빛으로 물들이고, 성벽과 망루를 금빛으로 둘러쳤다."], ["Then it disappeared as with a plunge.", "그러더니 곤두박질치듯 사라져 버렸다."], ["The quiet turned Ben-Hur’s thought homeward.", "그 고요함이 벤허의 생각을 집으로 돌려놓았다."], ["There was a point in the sky a little north of the peerless front of the Holy of Holies upon which he fixed his gaze: under it, straight as a leadline would have dropped, lay his father’s house, if yet the house endured.", "지성소의 비할 데 없는 정면에서 조금 북쪽 하늘에 한 점이 있었으니, 그는 그곳에 시선을 박았다. 그 아래로, 다림줄을 곧장 떨어뜨린 듯 그 자리에, 아버지의 집이 있었다. 그 집이 아직 남아 있다면 말이다."]], [["The mellowing influences of the evening mellowed his feelings, and, putting his ambitions aside, he thought of the duty that was bringing him to Jerusalem.", "저녁의 부드러운 기운이 그의 감정을 누그러뜨렸다. 그는 야망을 한쪽으로 밀어 두고, 자신을 예루살렘으로 이끈 의무를 생각했다."]], [["Out in the desert while with Ilderim, looking for strong places and acquainting himself with it generally, as a soldier studies a country in which he has projected a campaign, a messenger came one evening with the news that Gratus was removed, and Pontius Pilate sent to take his place.", "사막 한가운데서 일데림과 함께 있을 때, 마치 군인이 작전을 펼칠 고장을 살피듯 그는 요충지를 찾고 그 일대를 두루 익히고 있었다. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 한 전령이 와서 그라투스가 물러나고 그 자리에 본디오 빌라도가 부임했다는 소식을 전했다."]], [["Messala was disabled and believed him dead; Gratus was powerless and gone; why should Ben-Hur longer defer the search for his mother and sister?", "메살라는 불구가 되었으니 그는 메살라가 죽은 줄로 여겼고, 그라투스는 힘을 잃고 떠났다. 그러니 벤허가 어머니와 누이를 찾는 일을 더 미룰 까닭이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["There was nothing to fear now.", "이제는 두려워할 것이 없었다."], ["If he could not himself see into the prisons of Judea, he could examine them with the eyes of others.", "그 자신이 유대의 감옥들을 직접 들여다볼 수 없다면, 다른 이들의 눈으로 살필 수 있었다."], ["If the lost were found, Pilate could have no motive in holding them in custody—none, at least, which could not be overcome by purchase.", "잃어버린 이들을 찾아낸다면, 빌라도가 그들을 가두어 둘 동기는 없을 것이었다. 적어도 돈으로 풀지 못할 동기는 없었다."], ["If found, he would carry them to a place of safety, and then, in calmer mind, his conscience at rest, this one first duty done, he could give himself more entirely to the King Who Was Coming.", "찾아내기만 하면 안전한 곳으로 데려갈 것이요, 그런 다음에는 더 평온한 마음으로, 양심의 짐을 내려놓고, 이 첫 번째 의무를 다한 뒤에, 장차 오실 왕께 자신을 더 온전히 바칠 수 있을 것이었다."], ["He resolved at once.", "그는 곧장 마음을 정했다."], ["That night he counselled with Ilderim, and obtained his assent.", "그날 밤 그는 일데림과 의논하여 그의 동의를 얻었다."], ["Three Arabs came with him to Jericho, where he left them and the horses, and proceeded alone and on foot.", "아랍인 셋이 그와 함께 여리고까지 왔고, 그는 그곳에 그들과 말들을 남겨 둔 채 홀로 걸어서 길을 떠났다."], ["Malluch was to meet him in Jerusalem.", "말루크가 예루살렘에서 그를 만나기로 되어 있었다."]], [["Ben-Hur’s scheme, be it observed, was as yet a generality.", "여기서 밝혀 둘 것은, 벤허의 계획이 아직은 막연한 윤곽에 지나지 않았다는 점이다."]], [["In view of the future, it was advisable to keep himself in hiding from the authorities, particularly the Romans.", "앞날을 생각하면, 당국의 눈, 특히 로마인의 눈을 피해 몸을 숨기는 것이 마땅했다."], ["Malluch was shrewd and trusty; the very man to charge with the conduct of the investigation.", "말루크는 영민하고 믿음직했으니, 바로 이 조사를 맡기기에 알맞은 사람이었다."]], [["Where to begin was the first point.", "어디서 시작할 것인가가 첫 번째 문제였다."], ["He had no clear idea about it.", "그는 그 점에 대해 뚜렷한 생각이 없었다."], ["His wish was to commence with the Tower of Antonia.", "그의 바람은 안토니아 탑에서부터 시작하는 것이었다."], ["Tradition not of long standing planted the gloomy pile over a labyrinth of prison-cells, which, more even than the strong garrison, kept it a terror to the Jewish fancy.", "그리 오래되지 않은 전승에 따르면, 그 음침한 건물 더미는 미궁 같은 감방들 위에 세워져 있었으니, 그 감방들은 강력한 수비대보다도 더 유대인의 상상 속에 공포로 자리 잡고 있었다."], ["A burial, such as his people had been subjected to, might be possible there.", "그의 가족이 당했던 것과 같은 생매장이 그곳에서라면 있을 법한 일이었다."], ["Besides, in such a strait, the natural inclination is to start search at the place where the loss occurred, and he could not forget that his last sight of the loved ones was as the guard pushed them along the street in the direction to the Tower.", "게다가 그런 막막한 처지에서는 잃어버린 그 자리에서 수색을 시작하는 것이 자연스러운 마음이었으니, 그는 사랑하는 이들을 마지막으로 본 모습을 잊을 수 없었다. 경비병들이 그들을 탑 쪽 거리로 떠밀고 가던 광경이었다."], ["If they were not there now, but had been, some record of the fact must remain, a clew which had only to be followed faithfully to the end.", "만약 그들이 지금은 그곳에 없더라도 한때 있었다면, 그 사실의 어떤 기록은 남아 있을 터이니, 충실히 끝까지 따라가기만 하면 될 단서였다."]], [["Under this inclination, moreover, there was a hope which he could not forego.", "더욱이 이 마음 밑바닥에는 그가 차마 버릴 수 없는 희망 하나가 있었다."], ["From Simonides he knew Amrah, the Egyptian nurse, was living.", "시모니데스에게서 그는 이집트인 유모 암라가 살아 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["It will be remembered, doubtless, that the faithful creature, the morning the calamity overtook the Hurs, broke from the guard and ran back into the palace, where, along with other chattels, she had been sealed up.", "의심할 바 없이 기억하겠지만, 그 충직한 여인은 허 가문에 재앙이 닥치던 그날 아침 경비병에게서 벗어나 궁전 안으로 도로 뛰어 들어갔고, 그곳에서 다른 가재도구들과 함께 봉인되어 갇혔다."], ["During the years following, Simonides kept her supplied; so she was there now, sole occupant of the great house, which, with all his offers, Gratus had not been able to sell.", "그 뒤 여러 해 동안 시모니데스가 그녀에게 필요한 것을 대 주었으니, 그래서 그녀는 지금도 그곳에 있었다. 그라투스가 온갖 제안을 내놓고도 끝내 팔지 못한 그 큰 집의 유일한 거주자였다."], ["The story of its rightful owners sufficed to secure the property from strangers, whether purchasers or mere occupants.", "그 정당한 주인들의 사연이 알려진 탓에, 매수하려는 자든 그저 들어와 살려는 자든, 낯선 사람들로부터 그 재산은 안전했다."], ["People going to and fro passed it with whispers.", "오가는 사람들은 그 집을 지나칠 때면 수군거렸다."], ["Its reputation was that of a haunted house; derived probably from the infrequent glimpses of poor old Amrah, sometimes on the roof, sometimes in a latticed window.", "그 집은 귀신 들린 집이라는 평판이 나 있었으니, 아마도 가엾은 늙은 암라의 모습이 드문드문 눈에 띄었기 때문일 것이다. 어떤 때는 지붕 위에서, 어떤 때는 격자창 안에서였다."], ["Certainly no more constant spirit ever abided than she; nor was there ever a tenement so shunned and fitted for ghostly habitation.", "분명 그녀보다 더 한결같이 머무른 혼령은 일찍이 없었고, 그토록 사람들이 피하면서도 유령의 거처로 딱 어울리는 집 또한 없었다."], ["Now, if he could get to her, Ben-Hur fancied she could help him to knowledge which, though faint, might yet be serviceable.", "이제 그녀에게 다가갈 수만 있다면, 벤허는 그녀가 자신에게 비록 희미할지언정 쓸모 있을 지식을 줄 수 있으리라 여겼다."], ["Anyhow, sight of her in that place, so endeared by recollection, would be to him a pleasure next to finding the objects of his solicitude.", "어쨌든 추억으로 그토록 정든 그곳에서 그녀를 보는 것은, 그가 애타게 찾는 이들을 찾아내는 일에 버금가는 기쁨이 될 터였다."]], [["So, first of all things, he would go to the old house, and look for Amrah.", "그리하여 무엇보다 먼저 그는 옛집으로 가서 암라를 찾아볼 작정이었다."]], [["Thus resolved, he arose shortly after the going-down of the sun, and began descent of the Mount by the road which, from the summit, bends a little north of east.", "이렇게 마음을 정한 그는 해가 진 직후에 일어나, 정상에서 동쪽보다 조금 북쪽으로 굽어지는 길을 따라 산을 내려가기 시작했다."], ["Down nearly at the foot, close by the bed of the Cedron, he came to the intersection with the road leading south to the village of Siloam and the pool of that name.", "거의 산기슭에 이르러, 케드론 골짜기 바닥 가까이에서 그는 남쪽으로 실로암 마을과 그 이름을 딴 못으로 이어지는 길과 만나는 갈림길에 다다랐다."], ["There he fell in with a herdsman driving some sheep to market.", "그곳에서 그는 양 떼를 몰아 장터로 가는 목자와 마주쳤다."], ["He spoke to the man, and joined him, and in his company passed by Gethsemane on into the city through the Fish Gate.", "그는 그 사람에게 말을 건네 함께 어울렸고, 그와 동행하여 겟세마네를 지나 어문(魚門)을 통해 도시로 들어갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["It was dark when, parting with the drover inside the gate, Ben-Hur turned into a narrow lane leading to the south.", "벤허가 성문 안에서 가축 몰이꾼과 헤어져, 남쪽으로 난 좁은 골목으로 접어들었을 때는 이미 날이 저물어 있었다."], ["A few of the people whom he met saluted him. The bouldering of the pavement was rough.", "마주치는 사람들 중 몇몇이 그에게 인사를 건넸다. 포석은 울퉁불퉁했다."], ["The houses on both sides were low, dark, and cheerless; the doors all closed: from the roofs, occasionally, he heard women crooning to children.", "양쪽으로 늘어선 집들은 낮고 어둡고 적막했으며, 문이란 문은 모두 닫혀 있었다. 지붕에서는 이따금 여인들이 아이들을 달래며 흥얼거리는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["The loneliness of his situation, the night, the uncertainty cloaking the object of his coming, all affected him cheerlessly.", "그가 처한 처지의 외로움, 밤, 그리고 그가 이곳에 온 목적을 휘감은 불확실함이 모두 그의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["With feelings sinking lower and lower, he came directly to the deep reservoir now known as the Pool of Bethesda, in which the water reflected the over-pending sky.", "마음이 점점 더 가라앉는 채로, 그는 오늘날 베데스다 못이라 불리는 깊은 저수지에 곧장 다다랐다. 그곳에는 머리 위로 드리운 하늘이 물에 비치고 있었다."], ["Looking up, he beheld the northern wall of the Tower of Antonia, a black frowning heap reared into the dim steel-gray sky.", "위를 올려다보니, 안토니아 탑의 북쪽 성벽이 보였는데, 검고 음산한 덩어리가 어슴푸레한 강철빛 잿빛 하늘로 솟아 있었다."], ["He halted as if challenged by a threatening sentinel.", "그는 위협하는 보초에게 가로막히기라도 한 듯 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["The Tower stood up so high, and seemed so vast, resting apparently upon foundations so sure, that he was constrained to acknowledge its strength.", "그 탑은 어찌나 높이 솟았고 어찌나 거대해 보였는지, 그리고 더없이 든든한 기초 위에 떡 버티고 선 듯하여, 그는 그 견고함을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["If his mother were there in living burial, what could he do for her?", "어머니가 그 안에 산 채로 갇혀 있다면, 그가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["By the strong hand, nothing.", "힘으로는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["An army might beat the stony face with ballista and ram, and be laughed at.", "한 부대의 군대가 투석기와 공성퇴로 그 돌의 얼굴을 두드린다 해도 비웃음만 살 것이다."], ["Against him alone, the gigantic southeast turret looked down in the self-containment of a hill.", "그 거대한 남동쪽 망루는 그 혼자만을 상대로, 산처럼 태연자약하게 내려다보고 있었다. 그리고 그는 생각했다."], ["And he thought, cunning is so easily baffled; and God, always the last resort of the helpless—God is sometimes so slow to act!", "꾀란 이토록 쉽게 좌절되는구나. 그리고 언제나 무력한 자들의 마지막 의지처인 하느님—그 하느님께서는 때로 이토록 더디게 움직이시는구나!"]], [["In doubt and misgiving, he turned into the street in front of the Tower, and followed it slowly on to the west.", "의혹과 불안 속에서, 그는 탑 앞으로 난 거리로 접어들어 천천히 서쪽으로 그 길을 따라갔다."]], [["Over in Bezetha he knew there was a khan, where it was his intention to seek lodging while in the city; but just now he could not resist the impulse to go home.", "베제다 너머에는 카라반사라이가 하나 있어, 이 도시에 머무는 동안 그곳에서 묵을 작정이었다. 그러나 지금 이 순간만큼은 집으로 가고 싶은 충동을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["His heart drew him that way.", "그의 마음이 그를 그쪽으로 끌어당겼다."]], [["The old formal salutation which he received from the few people who passed him had never sounded so pleasantly.", "곁을 지나는 몇 안 되는 사람들에게서 받은 그 오래되고 격식 있는 인사가 이토록 정겹게 들린 적이 없었다."], ["Presently, all the eastern sky began to silver and shine, and objects before invisible in the west—chiefly the tall towers on Mount Zion—emerged as from a shadowy depth, and put on spectral distinctness, floating, as it were, above the yawning blackness of the valley below, very castles in the air.", "이윽고 동쪽 하늘 전체가 은빛으로 물들며 빛나기 시작했고, 그때까지 서쪽에서 보이지 않던 것들이—주로 시온산의 높은 탑들이—마치 어둑한 심연에서 솟아오르듯 드러나, 유령 같은 또렷함을 입은 채, 발치의 골짜기에 입을 벌린 검은 어둠 위로 둥둥 떠올라, 그야말로 허공의 성채를 이루었다."]], [["He came, at length, to his father’s house.", "그는 마침내 아버지의 집에 다다랐다."]], [["Of those who read this page, some there will be to divine his feelings without prompting.", "이 글을 읽는 이들 가운데에는 굳이 일러 주지 않아도 그의 심정을 헤아릴 사람이 있을 것이다."], ["They are such as had happy homes in their youth, no matter how far that may have been back in time—homes which are now the starting-points of all recollection; paradises from which they went forth in tears, and which they would now return to, if they could, as little children; places of laughter and singing, and associations dearer than any or all the triumphs of after-life.", "어린 시절 행복한 가정을 가졌던 이들이 그러하니—그 시절이 시간상 아무리 멀리 떨어져 있다 해도—그 가정은 이제 모든 회상의 출발점이 되는 곳이요, 눈물을 흘리며 떠나왔으나 할 수만 있다면 다시 어린아이가 되어 돌아가고 싶은 낙원이며, 웃음과 노래의 자리요, 훗날의 그 어떤 승리보다, 아니 그 모든 승리를 합한 것보다 더 소중한 추억이 깃든 곳이다."]], [["At the gate on the north side of the old house Ben-Hur stopped.", "벤허는 오래된 집의 북쪽에 난 대문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["In the corners the wax used in the sealing-up was still plainly seen, and across the valves was the board with the inscription—", "봉인할 때 쓴 밀랍이 모서리마다 아직 또렷이 남아 있었고, 문짝을 가로질러 다음과 같은 글귀가 적힌 판자가 붙어 있었다—\n\"이 재산은 황제의 소유다.\""]], [["Nobody had gone in or out the gate since the dreadful day of the separation.", "그 끔찍한 이별의 날 이후로, 누구도 그 대문을 드나든 적이 없었다."], ["Should he knock as of old?", "옛날처럼 두드려 볼까?"], ["It was useless, he knew; yet he could not resist the temptation.", "부질없는 짓임을 그도 알았다. 그러나 그 유혹을 떨칠 수가 없었다."], ["Amrah might hear, and look out of one of the windows on that side.", "암라가 듣고서 그쪽 창문 하나로 내다볼지도 몰랐다."], ["Taking a stone, he mounted the broad stone step, and tapped three times.", "돌멩이 하나를 집어 들고, 그는 너른 돌계단을 올라가 세 번 두드렸다."], ["A dull echo replied.", "둔탁한 메아리가 답했다."], ["He tried again, louder than before; and again, pausing each time to listen. The silence was mocking.", "다시, 아까보다 더 크게 두드려 보았다. 그리고 또 한 번, 그때마다 귀를 기울이려 잠시 멈추었다."], ["Retiring into the street, he watched the windows; but they, too, were lifeless.", "정적이 그를 조롱하고 있었다. 거리로 물러나 창문들을 살폈으나, 그것들 또한 생기가 없었다."], ["The parapet on the roof was defined sharply against the brightening sky; nothing could have stirred upon it unseen by him, and nothing did stir.", "지붕의 난간이 밝아 오는 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 윤곽을 드러냈으니, 그 위에서 무언가 움직였다면 그의 눈을 피할 수 없었을 텐데, 아무것도 움직이지 않았다."]], [["From the north side he passed to the west, where there were four windows which he watched long and anxiously, but with as little effect.", "북쪽에서 서쪽으로 옮겨 가니 그곳에는 창문이 넷 있었고, 그는 그것들을 오래도록 애타게 살폈으나, 역시 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["At times his heart swelled with impotent wishes; at others, he trembled at the deceptions of his own fancy.", "어떤 때는 가슴이 부질없는 소망으로 부풀어 올랐고, 또 어떤 때는 제 공상이 빚어내는 속임수에 몸을 떨었다."], ["Amrah made no sign—not even a ghost stirred.", "암라는 아무 기척도 보이지 않았으니—유령 하나조차 움직이지 않았다."]], [["Silently, then, he stole round to the south.", "그리하여 그는 소리 없이 남쪽으로 돌아 들어갔다."], ["There, too, the gate was sealed and inscribed.", "그곳에서도 대문은 봉인되고 글귀가 적혀 있었다."], ["The mellow splendor of the August moon, pouring over the crest of Olivet, since termed the Mount of Offence, brought the lettering boldly out; and he read, and was filled with rage.", "훗날 죄악의 산이라 불리게 된 올리벳 마루 너머로 쏟아져 내리는 팔월 달의 부드러운 광채가 그 글자들을 또렷이 드러냈다. 그는 그것을 읽고, 분노에 사로잡혔다."], ["All he could do was to wrench the board from its nailing, and hurl it into the ditch.", "그가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 못 박힌 판자를 비틀어 뜯어내, 도랑에 내던지는 것뿐이었다."], ["Then he sat upon the step, and prayed for the New King, and that his coming might be hastened.", "그러고 나서 그는 계단에 앉아, 새로운 왕을 위해 기도하며, 그분의 오심이 앞당겨지기를 빌었다."], ["As his blood cooled, insensibly he yielded to the fatigue of long travel in the summer heat, and sank down lower, and, at last, slept.", "피가 식어 가면서, 그는 어느새 여름 더위 속 긴 여행의 피로에 굴복하여 점점 더 낮게 가라앉다가, 마침내 잠이 들었다."]], [["About that time two women came down the street from the direction of the Tower of Antonia, approaching the palace of the Hurs.", "그 무렵, 두 여인이 안토니아 탑 쪽에서 거리를 따라 내려와, 벤허 가문의 저택으로 다가오고 있었다."], ["They advanced stealthily, with timid steps, pausing often to listen.", "그들은 겁먹은 걸음으로 몰래 나아가며, 귀를 기울이려 자주 멈추어 섰다."], ["At the corner of the rugged pile, one said to the other, in a low voice,", "그 울퉁불퉁한 건물의 모퉁이에서, 한 여인이 다른 여인에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“This is it, Tirzah!”", "\"여기로구나, 티르자!\""]], [["And Tirzah, after a look, caught her mother’s hand, and leaned upon her heavily, sobbing, but silent.", "티르자는 한번 바라보고는 어머니의 손을 잡고서, 흐느끼면서도 소리는 내지 않은 채, 어머니에게 무겁게 기대었다."]], [["“Let us go on, my child, because”—the mother hesitated and trembled; then, with an effort to be calm, continued—“because when morning comes they will put us out of the gate of the city to—return no more.”", "\"가자꾸나, 얘야, 왜냐하면\"—어머니는 머뭇거리며 떨었다. 그러더니 애써 차분해지려 하며 말을 이었다—\"왜냐하면 아침이 오면 저들이 우리를 성문 밖으로 내쫓을 테고, 그러면 다시는 돌아오지 못할 테니까.\""]], [["Tirzah sank almost to the stones.", "티르자는 거의 돌바닥에 주저앉을 뻔했다."]], [["“Ah, yes!”", "\"아, 그래요!\""], ["she said, between sobs; “I forgot.", "그녀가 흐느끼는 사이사이 말했다. \"잊고 있었어요."], ["I had the feeling of going home.", "집으로 돌아가는 기분이 들었거든요."], ["But we are lepers, and have no homes; we belong to the dead!”", "하지만 우리는 나병 환자고, 집도 없어요. 우리는 죽은 자들의 무리에 속한걸요!\""]], [["The mother stooped and raised her tenderly, saying, “We have nothing to fear.", "어머니는 몸을 숙여 다정하게 그녀를 일으키며 말했다. \"두려워할 것 없다."], ["Let us go on.”", "가자꾸나.\""]], [["Indeed, lifting their empty hands, they could have run upon a legion and put it to flight.", "실로, 빈손을 쳐들기만 해도 그들은 한 군단을 향해 달려들어 그것을 패주시킬 수도 있었으리라."]], [["And, creeping in close to the rough wall, they glided on, like two ghosts, till they came to the gate, before which they also paused.", "그리고 거친 벽에 바싹 붙어, 그들은 두 유령처럼 미끄러져 나아가다가, 대문에 이르자 그 앞에서 또한 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["Seeing the board, they stepped upon the stone in the scarce cold tracks of Ben-Hur, and read the inscription—“This is the Property of the Emperor.”", "판자를 보고서, 그들은 아직 채 식지도 않은 벤허의 발자국 위로 그 돌을 밟고 올라서서, 그 글귀를 읽었다—\"이 재산은 황제의 소유다.\""]], [["Then the mother clasped her hands, and, with upraised eyes, moaned in unutterable anguish.", "그러자 어머니는 두 손을 맞잡고, 눈을 치켜뜬 채, 형언할 수 없는 비통함 속에서 신음했다."]], [["“What now, mother? You scare me!”", "\"왜 그러세요, 어머니? 무서워요!\""]], [["And the answer was, presently, “Oh, Tirzah, the poor are dead!", "이윽고 돌아온 대답은 이러했다. \"오, 티르자, 가엾은 그 애가 죽었구나!"], ["He is dead!”", "그 애가 죽었어!\""]], [["“Who, mother?”", "\"누가요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Your brother!", "\"네 오라비 말이다!"], ["They took everything from him—everything—even this house!”", "저들이 그 애한테서 모든 것을 빼앗았어—모든 것을—이 집까지도!\""]], [["“Poor!” said Tirzah, vacantly.", "\"가엾어라!\" 티르자가 멍하니 말했다."]], [["“He will never be able to help us.”", "\"이제 그 애는 우리를 도와줄 수 없게 되었구나.\""]], [["“And then, mother?”", "\"그러면, 어머니?\""]], [["“To-morrow—to-morrow, my child, we must find a seat by the wayside, and beg alms as the lepers do; beg, or—”", "\"내일—내일이면, 얘야, 우리는 길가에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 나병 환자들이 그러하듯 동냥을 해야 한다. 동냥을 하든가, 아니면—\""]], [["Tirzah leaned upon her again, and said, whispering, “Let us—let us die!”", "티르자는 다시 어머니에게 기대어, 속삭이듯 말했다. \"우리—우리 차라리 죽어요!\""]], [["“No!” the mother said, firmly.", "\"안 된다!\" 어머니가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“The Lord has appointed our times, and we are believers in the Lord.", "\"주님께서 우리의 때를 정해 놓으셨고, 우리는 주님을 믿는 사람들이다."], ["We will wait on him even in this.", "이런 일에서도 우리는 주님을 기다릴 것이다."], ["Come away!”", "어서 가자!\""]], [["She caught Tirzah’s hand as she spoke, and hastened to the west corner of the house, keeping close to the wall.", "이렇게 말하며 그녀는 티르자의 손을 잡고서, 벽에 바싹 붙은 채 집의 서쪽 모퉁이로 서둘러 갔다."], ["No one being in sight there, they kept on to the next corner, and shrank from the moonlight, which lay exceedingly bright over the whole south front, and along a part of the street.", "그곳에 아무도 보이지 않자, 그들은 다음 모퉁이까지 계속 나아갔고, 남쪽 정면 전체와 거리 일부에 유난히 환하게 깔린 달빛을 피해 몸을 움츠렸다."], ["The mother’s will was strong.", "어머니의 의지는 굳세었다."], ["Casting one look back and up to the windows on the west side, she stepped out into the light, drawing Tirzah after her; and the extent of their affliction was then to be seen—on their lips and cheeks, in their bleared eyes, in their cracked hands; especially in the long, snaky locks, stiff with loathsome ichor, and, like their eyebrows, ghastly white.", "그녀는 서쪽의 창문들을 한 번 뒤돌아 올려다보고는, 티르자를 끌어당기며 빛 속으로 발을 내디뎠다. 그러자 그들이 받은 재앙의 깊이가 비로소 드러났으니—입술과 뺨에, 짓무른 눈에, 갈라진 손에, 무엇보다 길고 뱀처럼 늘어진 머리채에 그것이 드러났다. 그 머리채는 역겨운 진물로 뻣뻣했고, 눈썹과 마찬가지로 소름 끼치도록 하얗게 세어 있었다."], ["Nor was it possible to have told which was mother, which daughter; both alike seemed witch-like old.", "또한 누가 어머니이고 누가 딸인지조차 분간할 수 없었으니, 둘 다 마녀처럼 늙어 보였다."]], [["“Hist!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said the mother.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["“There is some one lying upon the step—a man.", "\"계단에 누가 누워 있구나—남자다."], ["Let us go round him.”", "돌아서 가자.\""]], [["They crossed to the opposite side of the street quickly, and, in the shade there, moved on till before the gate, where they stopped.", "그들은 재빨리 거리 건너편으로 가서, 그 그늘 속을 따라 나아가다가, 대문 앞에 이르자 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“He is asleep, Tirzah!”", "\"잠들었구나, 티르자!\""]], [["The man was very still.", "남자는 미동도 없었다."]], [["“Stay here, and I will try the gate.”", "\"여기 있거라, 내가 대문을 열어 보마.\""]], [["So saying, the mother stole noiselessly across, and ventured to touch the wicket; she never knew if it yielded, for that moment the man sighed, and, turning restlessly, shifted the handkerchief on his head in such manner that the face was left upturned and fair in the broad moonlight.", "그렇게 말하며 어머니는 소리 없이 건너가, 쪽문을 만져 보려 했다. 그 문이 열렸는지 아닌지는 그녀도 끝내 알지 못했으니, 바로 그 순간 남자가 한숨을 쉬며 몸을 뒤척였고, 머리에 둘렀던 수건이 흘러내려 얼굴이 위를 향한 채 너른 달빛 속에 환히 드러났기 때문이다."], ["She looked down at it and started; then looked again, stooping a little, and arose and clasped her hands and raised her eyes to heaven in mute appeal.", "그녀는 그 얼굴을 내려다보고 흠칫 놀랐다. 그러고는 몸을 조금 숙여 다시 보더니, 몸을 일으켜 두 손을 맞잡고, 말없이 호소하듯 하늘로 눈을 들었다."], ["An instant so, and she ran back to Tirzah.", "그렇게 한순간을 보내고, 그녀는 티르자에게 달려 돌아갔다."]], [["“As the Lord liveth, the man is my son—thy brother!”", "\"살아 계신 주님께 맹세코, 저 사람은 내 아들이다—네 오라비야!\""], ["she said, in an awe-inspiring whisper.", "그녀가 경외에 찬 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["“My brother?—Judah?”", "\"제 오라비요?—유다요?\""]], [["The mother caught her hand eagerly.", "어머니가 다급히 그녀의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"가자!\""], ["she said, in the same enforced whisper, “let us look at him together—once more—only once—then help thou thy servants, Lord!”", "그녀가 똑같이 억누른 속삭임으로 말했다. \"함께 그 애를 보자꾸나—한 번 더—단 한 번만—그런 다음 주님, 당신의 종들을 도와주소서!\""]], [["They crossed the street hand in hand ghostly-quick, ghostly-still.", "그들은 손을 맞잡고 거리를 건넜으니, 유령처럼 재빠르고 유령처럼 고요했다."], ["When their shadows fell upon him, they stopped.", "그들의 그림자가 그에게 드리워지자, 그들은 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["One of his hands was lying out upon the step palm up.", "그의 한 손이 손바닥을 위로 한 채 계단에 놓여 있었다."], ["Tirzah fell upon her knees, and would have kissed it; but the mother drew her back.", "티르자는 무릎을 꿇고 그 손에 입을 맞추려 했으나, 어머니가 그녀를 끌어당겼다."]], [["“Not for thy life; not for thy life!", "\"목숨이 아깝거든 안 된다. 절대 안 돼!"], ["Unclean, unclean!”", "부정하다, 우리는 부정해!\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."]], [["Tirzah shrank from him, as if he were the leprous one.", "티르자는 마치 그가 나병 환자이기라도 한 듯 그에게서 몸을 움츠렸다."]], [["Ben-Hur was handsome as the manly are.", "벤허는 사내다운 사람들이 그러하듯 준수했다."], ["His cheeks and forehead were swarthy from exposure to the desert sun and air; yet under the light mustache the lips were red, and the teeth shone white, and the soft beard did not hide the full roundness of chin and throat.", "사막의 햇볕과 바람에 노출되어 뺨과 이마는 가무잡잡했으나, 옅은 콧수염 아래 입술은 붉었고, 이는 희게 빛났으며, 부드러운 턱수염도 턱과 목의 둥근 윤곽을 다 가리지는 못했다."], ["How beautiful he appeared to the mother’s eyes!", "어머니의 눈에 그가 얼마나 아름다워 보였던가!"], ["How mightily she yearned to put her arms about him, and take his head upon her bosom and kiss him, as had been her wont in his happy childhood!", "행복했던 어린 시절에 늘 그러했듯, 그를 두 팔로 끌어안고 그 머리를 가슴에 품고 입 맞추고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 강렬했던가!"], ["Where got she the strength to resist the impulse?", "그 충동을 이겨 낼 힘을 그녀는 어디서 얻었던가?"], ["From her love, O, reader!—her mother-love, which, if thou wilt observe well, hath this unlikeness to any other love: tender to the object, it can be infinitely tyrannical to itself, and thence all its power of self-sacrifice.", "그녀의 사랑에서다, 오 독자여!—그 어머니의 사랑에서다. 잘 살펴보면, 이 사랑에는 다른 어떤 사랑과도 닮지 않은 점이 있으니, 사랑하는 대상에게는 더없이 다정하면서도, 자기 자신에게는 한없이 가혹할 수 있다는 점이요, 바로 거기서 모든 자기희생의 힘이 솟아난다."], ["Not for restoration to health and fortune, not for any blessing of life, not for life itself, would she have left her leprous kiss upon his cheek!", "건강과 재산을 되찾기 위해서도, 인생의 그 어떤 축복을 위해서도, 생명 그 자체를 위해서도, 그녀는 결코 그의 뺨에 나병에 걸린 입맞춤을 남기지 않으리라!"], ["Yet touch him she must; in that instant of finding him she must renounce him forever!", "그러나 그를 만지지 않을 수는 없었다. 그를 찾아낸 바로 그 순간에, 그녀는 영원히 그를 단념해야만 했던 것이다!"], ["How bitter, bitter hard it was, let some other mother say!", "그것이 얼마나 쓰디쓰고 쓰디쓰게 힘든 일이었는지는, 다른 어느 어머니에게 말하게 하라!"], ["She knelt down, and, crawling to his feet, touched the sole of one of his sandals with her lips, yellow though it was with the dust of the street—and touched it again and again; and her very soul was in the kisses.", "그녀는 무릎을 꿇고, 그의 발치로 기어가, 그의 신발 한 짝 바닥에 입술을 댔다. 비록 거리의 먼지로 누렇게 더럽혀져 있었으나—그리고 거듭거듭 입을 맞추었으니, 그 입맞춤에 그녀의 온 영혼이 담겨 있었다."]], [["He stirred, and tossed his hand.", "그가 몸을 뒤척이며 손을 휘저었다."], ["They moved back, but heard him mutter in his dream,", "그들은 뒤로 물러났으나, 그가 꿈결에 중얼거리는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Mother! Amrah! Where is—”", "\"어머니! 암라! 어디—\""]], [["He fell off into the deep sleep.", "그는 다시 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다."]], [["Tirzah stared wistfully.", "티르자는 애틋하게 바라보았다."], ["The mother put her face in the dust, struggling to suppress a sob so deep and strong it seemed her heart was bursting.", "어머니는 얼굴을 먼지 속에 파묻고, 가슴이 터질 것만 같은 그 깊고 거센 흐느낌을 억누르려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["Almost she wished he might waken.", "그녀는 차라리 그가 깨어나기를 바랄 뻔했다."]], [["He had asked for her; she was not forgotten; in his sleep he was thinking of her.", "그가 그녀를 찾았으니, 그녀는 잊히지 않은 것이다. 잠결에도 그는 그녀를 생각하고 있었다."], ["Was it not enough?", "이것으로 충분하지 않은가?"]], [["Presently mother beckoned to Tirzah, and they arose, and taking one more look, as if to print his image past fading, hand in hand they recrossed the street.", "이윽고 어머니가 티르자에게 손짓했다. 그들은 몸을 일으켜, 그 모습을 영원히 지워지지 않도록 마음에 새기려는 듯 한 번 더 바라보고는, 손을 맞잡고 거리를 다시 건넜다."], ["Back in the shade of the wall there, they retired and knelt, looking at him, waiting for him to wake—waiting some revelation, they knew not what.", "그곳 벽의 그늘로 돌아가, 그들은 물러나 무릎을 꿇은 채, 그를 바라보며, 그가 깨어나기를 기다렸다—무엇인지도 모를 어떤 계시를 기다렸다."], ["Nobody has yet given us a measure for the patience of a love like theirs.", "그들의 것과 같은 사랑의 인내심을 잴 척도를, 아직 누구도 우리에게 일러 준 적이 없다."]], [["By-and-by, the sleep being yet upon him, another woman appeared at the corner of the palace.", "이윽고, 그가 아직 잠들어 있는 동안, 또 다른 여인이 저택 모퉁이에 나타났다."], ["The two in the shade saw her plainly in the light; a small figure, much bent, dark-skinned, gray-haired, dressed neatly in servant’s garb, and carrying a basket full of vegetables.", "그늘에 있던 두 사람은 빛 속에서 그녀를 또렷이 보았다. 자그마하고, 허리가 많이 굽고, 피부가 거무스름하고, 머리가 희끗희끗한, 하인의 옷차림을 단정하게 갖춘, 채소가 가득 든 바구니를 든 여인이었다."]], [["At sight of the man upon the step the new-comer stopped; then, as if decided, she walked on—very lightly as she drew near the sleeper.", "계단 위의 남자를 보고서 그 새로 온 여인은 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러더니 마음을 정한 듯, 잠든 이에게 다가가면서 더없이 가벼운 걸음으로 계속 나아갔다."], ["Passing round him, she went to the gate, slid the wicket latch easily to one side, and put her hand in the opening.", "그를 빙 돌아, 그녀는 대문으로 가서, 쪽문 빗장을 손쉽게 한쪽으로 밀고, 그 틈으로 손을 넣었다."], ["One of the broad boards in the left valve swung ajar without noise.", "왼쪽 문짝의 너른 판자 하나가 소리 없이 빠끔히 열렸다."], ["She put the basket through, and was about to follow, when, yielding to curiosity, she lingered to have one look at the stranger whose face was below her in open view.", "그녀는 바구니를 그 안으로 들이밀고 자신도 뒤따라 들어가려다가, 호기심에 못 이겨, 발치에 얼굴을 훤히 드러낸 채 누운 낯선 이를 한 번 보려고 머뭇거렸다."]], [["The spectators across the street heard a low exclamation, and saw the woman rub her eyes as if to renew their power, bend closer down, clasp her hands, gaze wildly around, look at the sleeper, stoop and raise the outlying hand, and kiss it fondly—that which they wished so mightily to do, but dared not.", "거리 건너편의 구경꾼들은 낮은 탄성을 들었고, 그 여인이 눈의 힘을 되살리려는 듯 두 눈을 비비고, 더 가까이 몸을 숙이고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 사방을 미친 듯이 둘러보고, 잠든 이를 바라보고, 몸을 굽혀 밖으로 뻗은 손을 들어 올려, 애틋하게 입 맞추는 것을 보았다—그들이 그토록 간절히 하고 싶었으나 감히 하지 못한 바로 그 일이었다."]], [["Awakened by the action, Ben-Hur instinctively withdrew the hand; as he did so, his eyes met the woman’s.", "그 행동에 잠이 깬 벤허는 본능적으로 손을 거두어들였다. 그러면서 그의 눈이 그 여인의 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Amrah!", "\"암라!"], ["O Amrah, is it thou?”", "오 암라, 정말 너냐?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["The good heart made no answer in words, but fell upon his neck, crying for joy.", "그 착한 마음씨는 말로 답하지 않고, 그의 목덜미에 엎드려 기쁨에 겨워 울었다."]], [["Gently he put her arms away, and lifting the dark face wet with tears, kissed it, his joy only a little less than hers.", "그는 가만히 그녀의 팔을 풀고, 눈물에 젖은 거무스름한 얼굴을 들어 올려 거기에 입 맞추었으니, 그의 기쁨은 그녀의 기쁨에 조금 못 미칠 뿐이었다."], ["Then those across the way heard him say,", "그러자 길 건너편의 두 사람은 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“Mother—Tirzah—O Amrah, tell me of them!", "\"어머니—티르자—오 암라, 그분들 소식을 말해 다오!"], ["Speak, speak, I pray thee!”", "말해 봐, 어서 말해 봐, 제발!\""]], [["Amrah only cried afresh.", "암라는 그저 새삼스레 울기만 했다."]], [["“Thou has seen them, Amrah.", "\"네가 그분들을 뵈었구나, 암라."], ["Thou knowest where they are; tell me they are at home.”", "어디 계신지 너는 알고 있어. 집에 계신다고 말해 다오.\""]], [["Tirzah moved, but her mother, divining her purpose, caught her and whispered, “Do not go—not for life.", "티르자가 움직였으나, 어머니는 그 속셈을 알아채고 그녀를 붙잡으며 속삭였다. \"가지 마라—목숨이 아깝거든."], ["Unclean, unclean!”", "부정하다, 우리는 부정해!\""]], [["Her love was in tyrannical mood.", "그녀의 사랑은 가혹한 기세에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Though both their hearts broke, he should not become what they were; and she conquered.", "두 사람의 가슴이 다 미어진다 해도, 그가 자신들과 같은 신세가 되어서는 안 되었다. 그리하여 그녀는 이겨 냈다."]], [["Meantime, Amrah, so entreated, only wept the more.", "그러는 동안, 그토록 애원을 받은 암라는 그저 더욱 흐느끼기만 했다."]], [["“Wert thou going in?”", "\"들어가는 길이었느냐?\""], ["he asked, presently, seeing the board swung back.", "그가 이윽고, 판자가 젖혀진 것을 보고 물었다."], ["“Come, then.", "\"그럼 가자."], ["I will go with thee.”", "나도 너와 함께 들어가마.\""], ["He arose as he spoke.", "그는 말하면서 몸을 일으켰다."], ["“The Romans—be the curse of the Lord upon them!—the Romans lied.", "\"로마인들—주님의 저주가 그들에게 내리기를!—로마인들이 거짓말을 한 것이다."], ["The house is mine.", "이 집은 내 것이야."], ["Rise, Amrah, and let us go in.”", "일어나라, 암라, 어서 들어가자.\""], ["A moment and they were gone, leaving the two in the shade to behold the gate staring blankly at them—the gate which they might not ever enter more.", "잠시 후 그들은 사라졌고, 그늘 속의 두 사람만 남겨져, 자신들을 향해 멍하니 입을 벌린 대문을 바라보았다—다시는 결코 들어갈 수 없는 그 대문을."], ["They nestled together in the dust.", "그들은 먼지 속에서 서로 몸을 맞대고 웅크렸다."]], [["They had done their duty.", "그들은 자신들의 도리를 다했다."]], [["Their love was proven.", "그들의 사랑은 증명되었다."]], [["Next morning they were found, and driven out the city with stones.", "이튿날 아침 그들은 발각되어, 돌팔매질을 받으며 도시 밖으로 쫓겨났다."]], [["“Begone!", "\"꺼져라!"], ["Ye are of the dead; go to the dead!”", "너희는 죽은 자들의 무리니, 죽은 자들에게로 가라!\""]], [["With the doom ringing in their ears, they went forth.", "그 선고가 귓전에 울리는 가운데, 그들은 밖으로 나아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["Nowadays travellers in the Holy Land looking for the famous place with the beautiful name, the King’s Garden, descend the bed of the Cedron or the curve of Gihon and Hinnom as far as the old well En-rogel, take a drink of the sweet living water, and stop, having reached the limit of the interesting in that direction.", "「왕의 정원」이라는 아름다운 이름을 지닌 그 유명한 장소를 찾는 오늘날의 성지 순례자들은 기드론 골짜기의 바닥을 따라가거나, 기혼과 힌놈의 굽이를 거슬러 옛 우물 엔로겔까지 내려간다. 거기서 달콤한 생수를 한 모금 마시고는 걸음을 멈추니, 그 방향에서 흥미를 끄는 것은 그것이 끝이다."], ["They look at the great stones with which the well is curbed, ask its depth, smile at the primitive mode of drawing the purling treasure, and waste some pity on the ragged wretch who presides over it; then, facing about, they are enraptured with the mounts Moriah and Zion, both of which slope towards them from the north, one terminating in Ophel, the other in what used to be the site of the city of David.", "그들은 우물을 두른 커다란 돌을 바라보고, 그 깊이를 물으며, 졸졸 흐르는 보물을 길어 올리는 원시적인 방식에 미소 짓고, 그것을 지키는 누더기 차림의 가엾은 사내에게 약간의 연민을 흘린다. 그런 다음 몸을 돌려, 북쪽에서 그들을 향해 비탈져 내려오는 모리아산과 시온산에 황홀해하니, 하나는 오벨에서 끝나고 다른 하나는 한때 다윗 성의 터였던 곳에서 끝난다."], ["In the background, up far in the sky, the garniture of the sacred places is visible: here the Haram, with its graceful dome; yonder the stalward remains of Hippicus, defiant even in ruins.", "그 너머, 저 멀리 하늘 높이에는 성스러운 곳들의 장식이 보인다. 여기에는 우아한 둥근 지붕을 인 하람이 있고, 저기에는 폐허가 되어서도 당당한, 히피쿠스의 굳건한 잔해가 있다."], ["When that view has been enjoyed, and is sufficiently impressed upon the memory, the travellers glance at the Mount of Offence standing in rugged stateliness at their right hand, and then at the Hill of Evil Counsel over on the left, in which, if they be well up in Scriptural history and in the traditions rabbinical and monkish, they will find a certain interest not to be overcome by superstitious horror.", "그 풍경을 충분히 즐기고 기억에 또렷이 새기고 나면, 순례자들은 오른편에 험준한 위엄으로 서 있는 죄악의 산을 한 번 보고, 이어 왼편 너머에 있는 악한 모의의 언덕으로 눈길을 옮긴다. 성서의 역사와 랍비 및 수도사들의 전승에 밝은 이라면, 그 언덕에서 미신적인 공포로도 이겨 낼 수 없는 어떤 흥미를 발견하게 될 것이다."]], [["It were long to tell all the points of interest grouped around that hill; for the present purpose, enough that its feet are planted in the veritable orthodox Hell of the moderns—the Hell of brimstone and fire—in the old nomenclature Gehenna; and that now, as in the days of Christ, its bluff face opposite the city on the south and southeast is seamed and pitted with tombs which have been immemorially the dwelling-places of lepers, not singly, but collectively.", "그 언덕을 둘러싸고 무리 지은 흥미로운 지점들을 모두 이야기하자면 너무 길어진다. 지금의 목적으로는, 그 언덕의 발치가 현대인들의 진짜 정통 지옥—유황과 불의 지옥, 옛 이름으로는 게헨나—에 박혀 있다는 것, 그리고 그리스도 시대에도 그러했듯 지금도, 남쪽과 남동쪽으로 도시와 마주한 그 가파른 면이 무덤들로 갈라지고 패여 있다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하다. 그 무덤들은 아득한 옛날부터 나병 환자들의 거처였으니, 하나씩이 아니라 무리를 지어 살았다."], ["There they set up their government and established their society; there they founded a city and dwelt by themselves, avoided as the accursed of God.", "그곳에서 그들은 자기네 통치를 세우고 사회를 이루었으며, 그곳에 도시를 세우고 저희끼리 살았으니, 하느님의 저주받은 자로서 사람들의 외면을 받았다."]], [["The second morning after the incidents of the preceding chapter, Amrah drew near the well En-rogel, and seated herself upon a stone.", "앞 장의 사건들이 있은 지 이틀째 되는 날 아침, 암라는 엔로겔 우물 가까이 다가가, 돌 위에 앉았다."], ["One familiar with Jerusalem, looking at her, would have said she was the favorite servant of some well-to-do family.", "예루살렘을 잘 아는 이가 그녀를 보았다면, 어느 넉넉한 집안의 사랑받는 하녀라고 했을 것이다."], ["She brought with her a water-jar and a basket, the contents of the latter covered with a snow-white napkin.", "그녀는 물항아리와 바구니를 가져왔는데, 바구니 안의 것은 눈처럼 흰 보자기로 덮여 있었다."], ["Placing them on the ground at her side, she loosened the shawl which fell from her head, knit her fingers together in her lap, and gazed demurely up to where the hill drops steeply down into Aceldama and the Potter’s Field.", "그것들을 곁의 땅에 내려놓은 뒤, 머리에서 흘러내린 숄을 풀고, 두 손을 무릎 위에 깍지 낀 채, 언덕이 아켈다마와 토기장이의 밭으로 가파르게 떨어지는 그곳을 다소곳이 올려다보았다."]], [["It was very early, and she was the first to arrive at the well.", "아직 무척 이른 시각이라, 그녀가 우물에 처음 도착한 사람이었다."], ["Soon, however, a man came bringing a rope and a leathern bucket.", "그러나 곧 한 사내가 밧줄과 가죽 두레박을 가지고 왔다."], ["Saluting the little dark-faced woman, he undid the rope, fixed it to the bucket, and waited customers.", "그는 자그마하고 가무잡잡한 얼굴의 여인에게 인사한 뒤, 밧줄을 풀어 두레박에 매고는 손님을 기다렸다."], ["Others who chose to do so might draw water for themselves, he was a professional in the business, and would fill the largest jar the stoutest woman could carry for a gerah.", "원하는 이는 스스로 물을 길어도 좋았지만, 그는 그 일을 직업으로 삼은 자였으니, 게라 한 닢이면 아무리 건장한 여인이 들고 갈 수 있는 가장 큰 항아리라도 채워 주었다."]], [["Amrah sat still, and had nothing to say.", "암라는 가만히 앉아, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Seeing the jar, the man asked after a while if she wished it filled; she answered him civilly, “Not now;”", "사내는 항아리를 보고는 잠시 뒤 채워 주기를 바라느냐고 물었다. 그녀가 정중하게 \"지금은 됐어요\""], ["whereupon he gave her no more attention. When the dawn was fairly defined over Olivet, his patrons began to arrive, and he had all he could do to attend to them.", "하고 대답하자, 그는 더 이상 그녀에게 마음을 두지 않았다. 새벽빛이 올리벳 위로 또렷이 드러나자 그의 손님들이 모여들기 시작했고, 그는 그들을 챙기느라 손이 모자랄 지경이었다."], ["All the time she kept her seat, looking intently up at the hill.", "그동안 그녀는 줄곧 제자리를 지키며, 언덕 위를 뚫어지게 올려다보았다."]], [["The sun made its appearance, yet she sat watching and waiting; and while she thus waits, let us see what her purpose is.", "해가 모습을 드러냈건만, 그녀는 여전히 앉아 지켜보며 기다렸다. 그녀가 이렇게 기다리는 동안, 그녀의 목적이 무엇인지 살펴보기로 하자."]], [["Her custom had been to go to market after nightfall.", "그녀는 해가 진 뒤에 장을 보러 가는 것이 버릇이었다."], ["Stealing out unobserved, she would seek the shops in the Tyropoeon, or those over by the Fish Gate in the east, make her purchases of meat and vegetables, and return and shut herself up again.", "남의 눈에 띄지 않게 몰래 빠져나가, 티로포에온 골짜기의 가게들이나 동쪽 어문 근처의 가게들을 찾아, 고기와 채소를 사서, 돌아와 다시 안에 틀어박혔다."]], [["The pleasure she derived from the presence of Ben-Hur in the old house once more may be imagined.", "벤허가 옛집에 다시 와 있다는 사실에서 그녀가 얻은 기쁨은 가히 짐작할 만하다."], ["She had nothing to tell him of her mistress or Tirzah—nothing.", "그녀에게는 여주인이나 티르자에 관해 그에게 들려줄 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["He would have had her move to a place not so lonesome; she refused.", "정말 아무것도. 그는 그토록 외롭지 않은 곳으로 그녀가 옮겨 가기를 바랐지만, 그녀는 거절했다."], ["She would have had him take his own room again, which was just as he had left it; but the danger of discovery was too great, and he wished above all things to avoid inquiry.", "그녀는 그가 떠날 때 그대로 남아 있는 자기 방을 다시 쓰기를 바랐지만, 발각될 위험이 너무 컸고, 그는 무엇보다 사람들의 캐물음을 피하고 싶어 했다."], ["He would come and see her often as possible.", "그는 되도록 자주 그녀를 보러 오기로 했다."], ["Coming in the night, he would also go away in the night.", "밤에 와서 밤에 떠나기로 했다."], ["She was compelled to be satisfied, and at once occupied herself contriving ways to make him happy.", "그녀는 그것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었고, 곧장 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 방법을 궁리하는 데 몰두했다."], ["That he was a man now did not occur to her; nor did it enter her mind that he might have put by or lost his boyish tastes; to please him, she thought to go on her old round of services.", "그가 이제 어엿한 사내가 되었다는 생각은 그녀의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 그가 소년 시절의 취향을 버렸거나 잃었을지도 모른다는 생각 역시 들지 않았다."], ["He used to be fond of confections; she remembered the things in that line which delighted him most, and resolved to make them, and have a supply always ready when he came.", "그를 기쁘게 하려고, 그녀는 옛날에 하던 시중의 순서를 그대로 이어 갈 작정이었다. 그는 단것을 좋아했었다. 그녀는 그가 가장 좋아하던 종류를 떠올려, 그것을 만들어 그가 올 때마다 늘 준비해 두기로 마음먹었다."], ["Could anything be happier?", "이보다 더 행복한 일이 있을까?"], ["So next night, earlier than usual, she stole out with her basket, and went over to the Fish Gate Market.", "그래서 이튿날 밤, 여느 때보다 일찍, 그녀는 바구니를 들고 몰래 나가 어문 시장으로 갔다."], ["Wandering about, seeking the best honey, she chanced to hear a man telling a story.", "가장 좋은 꿀을 찾아 이리저리 거니는데, 우연히 한 사내가 이야기를 들려주는 것을 듣게 되었다."]], [["What the story was the reader can arrive at with sufficient certainty when told that the narrator was one of the men who had held torches for the commandant of the Tower of Antonia when, down in cell VI., the Hurs were found.", "그 이야기가 무엇이었는지는, 그 이야기꾼이 안토니아 탑의 사령관을 위해 횃불을 들었던 자들 가운데 하나, 곧 지하 제6호 감방에서 후르 집안 사람들이 발견되었을 때 그 자리에 있던 사람이었다고 말해 두면, 독자는 충분히 확실하게 짐작할 수 있을 것이다."], ["The particulars of the finding were all told, and she heard them, with the names of the prisoners, and the widow’s account of herself.", "발견의 모든 경위가 낱낱이 이야기되었고, 그녀는 그것을 들었으니, 죄수들의 이름과 그 미망인이 스스로에 관해 한 진술까지 들었다."]], [["The feelings with which Amrah listened to the recital were such as became the devoted creature she was.", "이 이야기를 들으며 암라가 느낀 감정은, 그녀가 본래 그러한 헌신적인 사람이었던 만큼 마땅한 것이었다."], ["She made her purchases, and returned home in a dream.", "그녀는 장을 다 보고, 꿈을 꾸는 듯한 마음으로 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["What a happiness she had in store for her boy!", "자기 도련님을 위해 얼마나 큰 행복을 마련해 두었는가!"], ["She had found his mother!", "그녀는 그의 어머니를 찾아낸 것이다!"]], [["She put the basket away, now laughing, now crying.", "그녀는 웃다가 울다가 하면서 바구니를 치웠다."], ["Suddenly she stopped and thought.", "그러다 문득 멈춰 서서 생각에 잠겼다."], ["It would kill him to be told that his mother and Tirzah were lepers.", "어머니와 티르자가 나병 환자가 되었다는 말을 들으면 그는 죽고 말 것이다."], ["He would go through the awful city over on the Hill of Evil Counsel—into each infected tomb he would go without rest, asking for them, and the disease would catch him, and their fate would be his. She wrung her hands.", "그는 악한 모의의 언덕 위에 있는 그 무서운 도시를 헤매며, 감염된 무덤마다 쉬지 않고 들어가 그들을 찾아 헤맬 것이고, 그러면 병이 그에게 옮아, 그들의 운명이 곧 그의 운명이 될 것이다. 그녀는 두 손을 비틀어 쥐었다."], ["What should she do?", "어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?"]], [["Like many a one before her, and many a one since, she derived inspiration, if not wisdom, from her affection, and came to a singular conclusion.", "그녀보다 앞선 많은 이들이 그러했고 그녀 이후의 많은 이들이 그러하듯, 그녀는 지혜는 아닐지언정 영감만은 자신의 애정에서 얻어, 하나의 기묘한 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["The lepers, she knew, were accustomed of mornings to come down from their sepulchral abodes in the hill, and take a supply of water for the day from the well En-rogel.", "그녀가 알기로, 나병 환자들은 아침이면 언덕의 무덤 거처에서 내려와, 엔로겔 우물에서 하루치 물을 길어 가는 것이 습관이었다."], ["Bringing their jars, they would set them on the ground and wait, standing afar until they were filled.", "항아리를 가져와 땅에 내려놓고는, 물이 채워질 때까지 멀찍이 서서 기다렸다."], ["To that the mistress and Tirzah must come; for the law was inexorable, and admitted no distinction. A rich leper was no better than a poor one.", "여주인과 티르자도 거기에 와야 했으니, 율법은 가차 없어 아무런 차별도 두지 않았기 때문이다. 부유한 나병 환자라 해서 가난한 자보다 나을 것이 없었다."]], [["So Amrah decided not to speak to Ben-Hur of the story she had heard, but go alone to the well and wait.", "그리하여 암라는 자기가 들은 이야기를 벤허에게 말하지 않고, 혼자 우물로 가서 기다리기로 결심했다."], ["Hunger and thirst would drive the unfortunates thither, and she believed she could recognize them at sight; if not, they might recognize her.", "굶주림과 목마름이 그 가엾은 이들을 그리로 내몰 것이고, 그녀는 그들을 보면 알아볼 수 있으리라 믿었다. 설령 알아보지 못하더라도, 그들이 그녀를 알아볼지도 몰랐다."]], [["Meantime Ben-Hur came, and they talked much.", "그러는 사이 벤허가 와서, 둘은 많은 이야기를 나누었다."], ["To-morrow Malluch would arrive; then the search should be immediately begun.", "내일이면 말루크가 도착할 터였다. 그러면 곧바로 수색을 시작할 참이었다."], ["He was impatient to be about it.", "그는 그 일을 시작하고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["To amuse himself he would visit the sacred places in the vicinity.", "그동안 무료함을 달래려고 그는 인근의 성스러운 곳들을 둘러볼 작정이었다."], ["The secret, we may be sure, weighed heavily on the woman, but she held her peace.", "비밀이 그 여인을 무겁게 짓눌렀으리라는 것은 충분히 짐작할 만한 일이지만, 그녀는 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["When he was gone she busied herself in the preparation of things good to eat, applying her utmost skill to the work.", "그가 떠나자 그녀는 먹기 좋은 음식을 마련하느라 바삐 움직이며, 가진 솜씨를 다해 그 일에 매달렸다."], ["At the approach of day, as signalled by the stars, she filled the basket, selected a jar, and took the road to En-rogel, going out by the Fish Gate which was earliest open, and arriving as we have seen.", "별들이 알려 주는 대로 날이 밝아 올 무렵, 그녀는 바구니를 채우고 항아리 하나를 골라, 가장 먼저 열리는 어문으로 빠져나가 엔로겔로 가는 길에 올랐으니, 우리가 본 그대로 그곳에 다다랐다."]], [["Shortly after sunrise, when business at the well was most pressing, and the drawer of water most hurried; when, in fact, half a dozen buckets were in use at the same time, everybody making haste to get away before the cool of the morning melted into the heat of the day, the tenantry of the hill began to appear and move about the doors of their tombs.", "해가 뜨고 얼마 지나지 않아, 우물의 일이 가장 분주하고 물 긷는 사내가 가장 부산할 때, 사실 여섯 개나 되는 두레박이 한꺼번에 쓰이고 모두들 아침의 서늘함이 한낮의 더위로 녹아들기 전에 자리를 뜨려고 서두를 때, 언덕의 주민들이 모습을 드러내며 자기네 무덤 문 앞을 오가기 시작했다."], ["Somewhat later they were discernible in groups, of which not a few were children so young that they suggested the holiest relation.", "좀 더 시간이 지나자 그들은 무리를 이루어 분간되었는데, 그 가운데 적지 않은 수가 가장 거룩한 관계를 떠올리게 할 만큼 어린아이들이었다."], ["Numbers came momentarily around the turn of the bluff—women with jars upon their shoulders, old and very feeble men hobbling along on staffs and crutches.", "가파른 면의 모퉁이를 돌아 잇따라 사람들이 나타났으니, 어깨에 항아리를 인 여인들이며, 지팡이와 목발에 의지해 비척비척 걸어오는 늙고 몹시 쇠약한 사내들이었다."], ["Some leaned upon the shoulders of others; a few—the utterly helpless—lay, like heaps of rags, upon litters.", "더러는 다른 이의 어깨에 기댔고, 몇몇은—아주 속수무책인 자들은—넝마 더미처럼 들것 위에 누워 있었다."], ["Even that community of superlative sorrow had its love-light to make life endurable and attractive.", "더없이 극심한 슬픔으로 이루어진 그 공동체에조차, 삶을 견딜 만하고 끌리는 것으로 만들어 주는 사랑의 빛은 있었다."], ["Distance softened without entirely veiling the misery of the outcasts.", "거리가 그 버림받은 이들의 비참함을 온전히 가리지는 못했으되 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸다."]], [["From her seat by the well Amrah kept watch upon the spectral groups. She scarcely moved.", "우물가의 자리에서 암라는 그 유령 같은 무리를 지켜보았다. 그녀는 거의 움직이지 않았다."], ["More than once she imagined she saw those she sought.", "한 번 이상, 그녀는 자기가 찾는 이들을 본 것 같은 생각이 들었다."], ["That they were there upon the hill she had no doubt; that they must come down and near she knew; when the people at the well were all served they would come.", "그들이 언덕 위에 있다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었고, 그들이 내려와 가까이 와야 한다는 것도 그녀는 알았다. 우물의 사람들이 모두 물을 받고 나면, 그들이 올 터였다."]], [["Now, quite at the base of the bluff there was a tomb which had more than once attracted Amrah by its wide gaping.", "그런데 가파른 면의 맨 밑자락에, 입을 크게 벌리고 있어 한 번 이상 암라의 눈길을 끌었던 무덤이 하나 있었다."], ["A stone of large dimensions stood near its mouth.", "그 입구 가까이에는 커다란 돌 하나가 서 있었다."], ["The sun looked into it through the hottest hours of the day, and altogether it seemed uninhabitable by anything living, unless, perchance, by some wild dogs returning from scavenger duty down in Gehenna.", "해는 하루 중 가장 뜨거운 시간 내내 그 안을 들여다보았고, 무엇 하나 살아 있는 것이 깃들 만한 곳으로는 도무지 보이지 않았으니, 어쩌면 게헨나 밑에서 청소부 노릇을 마치고 돌아온 들개들이나 들어갈 만했다."], ["Thence, however, and greatly to her surprise, the patient Egyptian beheld two women come, one half supporting, half leading, the other.", "그런데도 그곳에서, 참을성 있는 이집트 여인은 무척 놀랍게도, 두 여인이 나오는 것을 보았으니, 하나가 다른 하나를 반쯤 부축하고 반쯤 이끌고 있었다."], ["They were both white-haired; both looked old; but their garments were not rent, and they gazed about them as if the locality were new.", "둘 다 머리가 하얗게 셌고, 둘 다 늙어 보였다. 그러나 그들의 옷은 찢겨 있지 않았고, 마치 그곳이 낯선 곳이기라도 한 듯 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["The witness below thought she even saw them shrink terrified at the spectacle offered by the hideous assemblage of which they found themselves part.", "아래에서 지켜보던 이는, 자기들도 그 한 자락이 되어 버린 그 끔찍한 무리의 광경에 그들이 겁에 질려 움츠러드는 것까지 본 듯한 생각이 들었다."], ["Slight reasons, certainly, to make her heart beat faster, and draw her attention to them exclusively; but so they did.", "그녀의 심장이 더 빨리 뛰고 오로지 그들에게만 마음이 쏠리게 할 만한 까닭으로는 분명 보잘것없었으나, 어쨌든 그러했다."]], [["The two remained by the stone awhile; then they moved slowly, painfully, and with much fear towards the well, whereat several voices were raised to stop them; yet they kept on.", "두 사람은 잠시 돌 곁에 머물렀다. 그러더니 천천히, 고통스럽게, 무척 두려워하며 우물 쪽으로 움직였는데, 그러자 여러 목소리가 그들을 멈추게 하려고 높아졌다. 그래도 그들은 계속 다가왔다."], ["The drawer of water picked up some pebbles, and made ready to drive them back. The company cursed them.", "물 긷던 사내가 조약돌 몇 개를 집어 들어 그들을 쫓아 보낼 채비를 했다. 사람들은 그들에게 저주를 퍼부었다."], ["The greater company on the hill shouted shrilly, “Unclean, unclean!”", "언덕 위의 더 큰 무리는 날카롭게 소리쳤다. \"부정하다, 부정하다!\""]], [["“Surely,”", "\"틀림없이,\""], ["thought Amrah of the two, as they kept coming—“surely, they are strangers to the usage of lepers.”", "그들이 계속 다가오는 것을 보며 암라는 두 사람을 두고 생각했다. \"틀림없이, 저들은 나병 환자의 관습을 모르는 사람들이야.\""]], [["She arose, and went to meet them, taking the basket and jar.", "그녀는 일어나, 바구니와 항아리를 들고 그들을 맞으러 갔다."], ["The alarm at the well immediately subsided.", "우물가의 소동은 곧 가라앉았다."]], [["“What a fool,”", "\"어리석기도 하지,\""], ["said one, laughing, “what a fool to give good bread to the dead in that way!”", "한 사람이 웃으며 말했다. \"저렇게 죽은 자들에게 좋은 빵을 주다니, 어리석기도 하지!\""]], [["“And to think of her coming so far!”", "\"그것도 저렇게 멀리까지 오다니!\""], ["said another. “I would at least make them meet me at the gate.”", "또 다른 사람이 말했다. \"나라면 적어도 문간에서 만나게 하겠어.\""]], [["Amrah, with better impulse, proceeded.", "암라는 더 갸륵한 마음으로 나아갔다."], ["If she should be mistaken!", "혹시 잘못 본 것이라면!"], ["Her heart arose into her throat.", "그녀의 심장이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐다."], ["And the farther she went the more doubtful and confused she became.", "그리고 다가갈수록 그녀는 더욱 미심쩍고 어지러워졌다."], ["Four or five yards from where they stood waiting for her she stopped.", "그들이 그녀를 기다리며 서 있는 곳에서 너덧 걸음 떨어진 데서 그녀는 멈춰 섰다."]], [["That the mistress she loved!", "저 사람이 자기가 사랑하던 여주인이라니!"], ["whose hand she had so often kissed in gratitude!", "감사한 마음에 그토록 자주 손에 입 맞추던 그분이!"], ["whose image of matronly loveliness she had treasured in memory so faithfully!", "그토록 신실하게 기억 속에 간직해 온 그 부인다운 사랑스러운 모습의 주인이!"], ["And that the Tirzah she had nursed through babyhood!", "그리고 저 사람이 자기가 갓난아기 때부터 길러 온 티르자라니!"], ["whose pains she had soothed, whose sports she had shared!", "아픔을 달래 주고, 놀이를 함께 나누던 그 아이가!"], ["that the smiling, sweet-faced, songful Tirzah, the light of the great house, the promised blessing of her old age!", "미소 짓고, 사랑스러운 얼굴에, 노래하던 티르자, 그 큰 집의 빛이요, 자기 노년에 약속된 축복이었던 그 아이가!"], ["Her mistress, her darling—they? The soul of the woman sickened at the sight.", "자기 여주인, 자기 귀염둥이가—저들이라니? 그 광경에 여인의 영혼은 메스꺼워졌다."]], [["“These are old women,”", "\"이들은 늙은 여인들이야,\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 속으로 말했다."], ["“I never saw them before. I will go back.”", "\"나는 전에 이들을 본 적이 없어. 돌아가야지.\""]], [["She turned away.", "그녀는 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["“Amrah,”", "\"암라,\""], ["said one of the lepers.", "나병 환자 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["The Egyptian dropped the jar, and looked back, trembling.", "이집트 여인은 항아리를 떨어뜨리고, 떨면서 뒤를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Who called me?”", "\"누가 나를 불렀어요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Amrah.”", "\"암라.\""]], [["The servant’s wondering eyes settled upon the speaker’s face.", "하녀의 어리둥절한 눈이 말하는 이의 얼굴에 머물렀다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"당신들은 누구예요?\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["“We are they you are seeking.”", "\"우리가 바로 네가 찾는 사람들이다.\""]], [["Amrah fell upon her knees.", "암라는 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“O my mistress, my mistress! As I have made your God my God, be he praised that he has led me to you!”", "\"오, 마님, 마님! 마님의 하느님을 제 하느님으로 삼았듯이, 저를 마님께로 인도하신 그분께 찬양이 있기를!\""]], [["And upon her knees the poor overwhelmed creature began moving forward.", "그러고는 무릎을 꿇은 채, 그 가엾고 벅찬 사람은 앞으로 다가가기 시작했다."]], [["“Stay, Amrah!", "\"멈춰라, 암라!"], ["Come not nearer.", "더 가까이 오지 마라."], ["Unclean, unclean!”", "부정하다, 부정하다!\""]], [["The words sufficed.", "그 말로 충분했다."], ["Amrah fell upon her face, sobbing so loud the people at the well heard her.", "암라는 엎드려, 우물가의 사람들이 들을 만큼 큰 소리로 흐느꼈다."], ["Suddenly she arose upon her knees again.", "그러다 갑자기 다시 무릎을 세웠다."]], [["“O my mistress, where is Tirzah?”", "\"오, 마님, 티르자 아가씨는 어디 계세요?\""]], [["“Here I am, Amrah, here!", "\"여기 있어, 암라, 여기!"], ["Will you not bring me a little water?”", "물을 조금만 가져다주지 않겠니?\""]], [["The habit of the servant renewed itself.", "하녀의 몸에 밴 버릇이 되살아났다."], ["Putting back the coarse hair fallen over her face, Amrah arose and went to the basket and uncovered it.", "얼굴 위로 흘러내린 거친 머리칼을 쓸어 올리고, 암라는 일어나 바구니로 가서 보자기를 걷었다."]], [["“See,”", "\"보세요,\""], ["she said, “here are bread and meat.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"여기 빵과 고기가 있어요.\""]], [["She would have spread the napkin upon the ground, but the mistress spoke again,", "그녀는 보자기를 땅에 펼치려 했으나, 여주인이 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Do not so, Amrah.", "\"그러지 마라, 암라."], ["Those yonder may stone you, and refuse us drink.", "저기 있는 자들이 너에게 돌을 던지고, 우리에게 물을 주지 않을지도 모른다."], ["Leave the basket with me.", "바구니는 우리 곁에 두어라."], ["Take up the jar and fill it, and bring it here.", "항아리를 들고 가서 물을 채워 이리로 가져오너라."], ["We will carry them to the tomb with us.", "우리가 그것들을 무덤으로 가지고 가마."], ["For this day you will then have rendered all the service that is lawful.", "그러면 오늘은 네가 율법이 허락하는 모든 시중을 다 든 셈이 된다."], ["Haste, Amrah.”", "서둘러라, 암라.\""]], [["The people under whose eyes all this had passed made way for the servant, and even helped her fill the jar, so piteous was the grief her countenance showed.", "이 모든 일이 눈앞에서 벌어진 사람들은 하녀에게 길을 비켜 주었고, 항아리 채우는 것까지 거들어 주었으니, 그녀의 얼굴에 드러난 슬픔이 그토록 가엾었던 것이다."]], [["“Who are they?”", "\"저들은 누구요?\""], ["a woman asked.", "한 여인이 물었다."]], [["Amrah meekly answered, “They used to be good to me.”", "암라는 다소곳이 대답했다. \"저에게 잘해 주시던 분들이에요.\""]], [["Raising the jar upon her shoulder, she hurried back.", "항아리를 어깨에 들어 올리고, 그녀는 서둘러 돌아왔다."], ["In forgetfulness, she would have gone to them, but the cry “Unclean, unclean!", "무심결에 그녀는 그들에게로 가려 했으나, \"부정하다, 부정하다! 조심해!\""], ["Beware!” arrested her.", "하는 외침이 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다."], ["Placing the water by the basket, she stepped back, and stood off a little way.", "물을 바구니 곁에 내려놓고, 그녀는 뒤로 물러나, 조금 떨어진 곳에 섰다."]], [["“Thank you, Amrah,”", "\"고맙다, 암라,\""], ["said the mistress, taking the articles into possession.", "여주인이 그것들을 거두어들이며 말했다."], ["“This is very good of you.”", "\"참으로 갸륵하구나.\""]], [["“Is there nothing more I can do?”", "\"제가 더 할 수 있는 일은 없을까요?\""], ["asked Amrah.", "암라가 물었다."]], [["The mother’s hand was upon the jar, and she was fevered with thirst; yet she paused, and rising, said firmly, “Yes, I know that Judah has come home.", "어머니의 손은 항아리 위에 놓여 있었고, 그녀는 갈증으로 애가 탔다. 그런데도 그녀는 멈칫하더니, 몸을 일으켜 단호하게 말했다. \"있다. 나는 유다가 집에 돌아온 것을 안다."], ["I saw him at the gate night before last asleep on the step. I saw you wake him.”", "그저께 밤, 문간 계단에서 잠든 그 애를 보았다. 네가 그 애를 깨우는 것도 보았다.\""]], [["Amrah clasped her hands.", "암라는 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“O my mistress!", "\"오, 마님!"], ["You saw it, and did not come!”", "그것을 보시고도, 나오지 않으셨어요!\""]], [["“That would have been to kill him.", "\"그랬다면 그 애를 죽이는 일이 되었겠지."], ["I can never take him in my arms again.", "나는 다시는 그 애를 품에 안을 수 없다."], ["I can never kiss him more.", "다시는 그 애에게 입 맞출 수 없어."], ["O Amrah, Amrah, you love him, I know!”", "오, 암라, 암라, 네가 그 애를 사랑하는 걸 나는 안다!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said the true heart, bursting into tears again, and kneeling. “I would die for him.”", "그 진실한 마음은 다시 눈물을 터뜨리며 무릎을 꿇은 채 말했다. \"그분을 위해서라면 저는 죽을 수도 있어요.\""]], [["“Prove to me what you say, Amrah.”", "\"네 말이 사실임을 내게 증명해 보여라, 암라.\""]], [["“I am ready.”", "\"준비되어 있어요.\""]], [["“Then you shall not tell him where we are or that you have seen us—only that, Amrah.”", "\"그러면 우리가 어디 있는지, 우리를 보았다는 것을 그 애에게 말하지 마라. 그것만이다, 암라.\""]], [["“But he is looking for you.", "\"하지만 도련님은 마님을 찾고 계세요."], ["He has come from afar to find you.”", "마님을 찾으려고 먼 곳에서 오셨어요.\""]], [["“He must not find us. He shall not become what we are.", "\"그 애는 우리를 찾아서는 안 된다. 그 애가 우리처럼 되어서는 안 돼."], ["Hear, Amrah.", "들어라, 암라."], ["You shall serve us as you have this day.", "너는 오늘 그러했듯이 우리를 섬기게 될 것이다."], ["You shall bring us the little we need—not long now—not long.", "우리에게 필요한 얼마 안 되는 것을 가져다주어라—이제 오래지 않을 거다—오래지 않아."], ["You shall come every morning and evening thus, and—and”—the voice trembled, the strong will almost broke down—“and you shall tell us of him, Amrah; but to him you shall say nothing of us.", "너는 이렇게 아침저녁으로 와서, 그리고—그리고\"—목소리가 떨렸고, 굳센 의지가 거의 무너질 뻔했다—\"그리고 우리에게 그 애의 소식을 전해 다오, 암라. 그러나 그 애에게는 우리에 관해 아무 말도 하지 마라."], ["Hear you?”", "알아들었느냐?\""]], [["“Oh, it will be so hard to hear him speak of you, and see him going about looking for you—to see all his love, and not tell him so much as that you are alive!”", "\"오, 도련님이 마님 이야기를 하시는 것을 듣고, 마님을 찾아 헤매시는 것을 보면서—그 모든 사랑을 보면서도, 마님이 살아 계시다는 것조차 말씀드리지 못한다니, 너무도 견디기 힘들 거예요!\""]], [["“Can you tell him we are well, Amrah?”", "\"우리가 잘 지낸다고 그 애에게 말할 수 있겠느냐, 암라?\""]], [["The servant bowed her head in her arms.", "하녀는 두 팔에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“No,” the mistress continued; “wherefore to be silent altogether.", "\"그럴 수 없지,\" 여주인이 말을 이었다."], ["Go now, and come this evening.", "\"그러니 아예 아무 말도 하지 마라. 이제 가거라, 그리고 오늘 저녁에 오너라."], ["We will look for you.", "우리가 너를 기다리마."], ["Till then, farewell.”", "그때까지, 잘 가거라.\""]], [["“The burden will be heavy, O my mistress, and hard to bear,”", "\"그 짐은 무거울 거예요, 오, 마님, 견디기 힘들 거예요,\""], ["said Amrah, falling upon her face.", "암라가 엎드리며 말했다."]], [["“How much harder would it be to see him as we are,” the mother answered as she gave the basket to Tirzah.", "\"우리가 이런 꼴이 된 것을 그 애가 보는 것은 얼마나 더 힘들겠느냐,\" 어머니는 바구니를 티르자에게 건네며 대답했다."], ["“Come again this evening,”", "\"오늘 저녁에 다시 오너라,\""], ["she repeated, taking up the water, and starting for the tomb.", "그녀는 거듭 말하며, 물을 들고 무덤을 향해 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Amrah waited kneeling until they had disappeared; then she took the road sorrowfully home.", "암라는 그들이 사라질 때까지 무릎을 꿇은 채 기다렸다. 그러고는 슬픈 마음으로 집으로 가는 길에 올랐다."]], [["In the evening she returned; and thereafter it became her custom to serve them in the morning and evening, so that they wanted for nothing needful.", "저녁에 그녀는 다시 왔다. 그 뒤로 아침저녁으로 그들을 섬기는 것이 그녀의 일과가 되었으니, 그리하여 그들은 꼭 필요한 것에 부족함이 없었다."], ["The tomb, though ever so stony and desolate, was less cheerless than the cell in the Tower had been.", "무덤은 아무리 돌투성이에 황량하다 해도, 탑의 감방보다는 덜 음울했다."], ["Daylight gilded its door, and it was in the beautiful world.", "햇빛이 그 문을 금빛으로 물들였고, 그곳은 아름다운 세상 안에 있었다."], ["Then, one can wait death with so much more faith out under the open sky.", "그리고 탁 트인 하늘 아래에서는, 그만큼 더 큰 믿음으로 죽음을 기다릴 수 있는 법이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["The morning of the first day of the seventh month—Tishri in the Hebrew, October in English—Ben-Hur arose from his couch in the khan ill satisfied with the whole world.", "일곱째 달 초하루 아침—히브리어로는 티슈리, 영어로는 시월—벤허는 객사의 침상에서 일어났으나, 온 세상이 못마땅하기만 했다."]], [["Little time had been lost in consultation upon the arrival of Malluch.", "말루크가 도착했을 때 의논에 허비한 시간은 거의 없었다."], ["The latter began the search at the Tower of Antonia, and began it boldly, by a direct inquiry of the tribune commanding.", "그는 안토니아 탑에서 수색을 시작했는데, 그 첫걸음이 대담하게도 지휘관인 호민관에게 직접 묻는 것이었다."], ["He gave the officer a history of the Hurs, and all the particulars of the accident to Gratus, describing the affair as wholly without criminality.", "그는 그 장교에게 후르 가문의 내력과 그라투스가 당한 사고의 자초지종을 들려주며, 그 일에는 아무런 범죄성이 없었음을 설명했다."], ["The object of the quest now, he said, was if any of the unhappy family were discovered alive to carry a petition to the feet of Cæsar, praying restitution of the estate and return to their civil rights.", "이제 이 수색의 목적은, 그 불행한 가족 가운데 누구라도 살아 있는 이가 발견되거든 카이사르의 발 앞에 청원서를 올려 재산의 반환과 시민권의 회복을 간청하려는 데 있다고 그는 말했다."], ["Such a petition, he had no doubt, would result in an investigation by the imperial order, a proceeding of which the friends of the family had no fear.", "그러한 청원은 황제의 명령에 따른 조사로 이어질 것이 분명하나, 그 가족의 벗들은 그러한 절차를 조금도 두려워하지 않는다고 했다."]], [["In reply the tribune stated circumstantially the discovery of the women in the Tower, and permitted a reading of the memorandum he had taken of their account of themselves; when leave to copy it was prayed, he even permitted that.", "이에 호민관은 탑에서 두 여인을 발견한 정황을 소상히 진술하고, 그들이 스스로에 관해 진술한 내용을 적어 둔 비망록을 읽도록 허락했다. 그 사본을 베끼게 해 달라고 청하자, 호민관은 그것마저 허락해 주었다."]], [["Malluch thereupon hurried to Ben-Hur.", "말루크는 곧장 벤허에게 달려갔다."]], [["It were useless to attempt description of the effect the terrible story had upon the young man.", "그 끔찍한 이야기가 이 젊은이에게 미친 충격을 묘사하려는 시도는 부질없다."], ["The pain was not relieved by tears or passionate outcries; it was too deep for any expression.", "그 고통은 눈물로도 격정의 절규로도 가시지 않았으니, 어떤 표현으로도 담아낼 수 없을 만큼 깊었다."], ["He sat still a long time, with pallid face and laboring heart.", "그는 창백한 얼굴과 짓눌린 가슴으로 오랫동안 가만히 앉아 있었다."], ["Now and then, as if to show the thoughts which were most poignant, he muttered,", "이따금, 가장 사무치는 생각이 무엇인지 드러내기라도 하듯, 그는 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Lepers, lepers! They—my mother and Tirzah—they lepers!", "\"나병환자라니, 나병환자라니! 그분이—내 어머니와 티르자가—나병환자라니!"], ["How long, how long, O Lord!”", "언제까지입니까, 언제까지입니까, 주여!\""]], [["One moment he was torn by a virtuous rage of sorrow, next by a longing for vengeance which, it must be admitted, was scarcely less virtuous.", "한순간 그는 의로운 슬픔의 분노에 찢겼다가, 다음 순간에는 복수의 갈망에 사로잡혔으니, 솔직히 말해 그 복수의 갈망 또한 슬픔 못지않게 의로운 것이었다."]], [["At length he arose.", "마침내 그는 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“I must look for them. They may be dying.”", "\"그들을 찾아야 한다. 죽어 가고 있을지도 모른다.\""]], [["“Where will you look?”", "\"어디서 찾으시렵니까?\""], ["asked Malluch.", "말루크가 물었다."]], [["“There is but one place for them to go.”", "\"그들이 갈 곳은 단 한 군데뿐이다.\""]], [["Malluch interposed, and finally prevailed so far as to have the management of the further attempt intrusted to him.", "말루크가 끼어들었고, 마침내 이후의 시도를 자신이 맡아 처리하게 해 달라는 청을 관철시켰다."], ["Together they went to the gate over on the side opposite the Hill of Evil Counsel, immemorially the lepers’ begging-ground.", "두 사람은 함께 악한 의논의 언덕 맞은편, 아득한 옛적부터 나병환자들의 구걸터였던 성문으로 갔다."], ["There they stayed all day, giving alms, asking for the two women, and offering rich rewards for their discovery.", "그곳에서 그들은 온종일 머물며 적선을 베풀고, 두 여인의 행방을 묻고, 그들을 찾아 주는 이에게 후한 상을 내걸었다."], ["So they did in repetition day after day through the remainder of the fifth month, and all the sixth.", "그렇게 그들은 다섯째 달의 남은 날들과 여섯째 달 내내 날마다 거듭하여 그 일을 했다."], ["There was diligent scouring of the dread city on the hill by lepers to whom the rewards offered were mighty incentives, for they were only dead in law.", "내건 상이 강력한 유인이 되어, 언덕 위의 그 두려운 도시를 나병환자들이 부지런히 샅샅이 뒤졌으니, 그들은 법으로만 죽은 자들이었기 때문이다."], ["Over and over again the gaping tomb down by the well was invaded, and its tenants subjected to inquiry; but they kept their secret fast.", "우물가 아래 입을 벌린 무덤도 거듭거듭 들어가 살펴, 그 안에 든 자들에게 캐물었으나, 두 사람은 자신들의 비밀을 굳게 지켰다."], ["The result was failure.", "그 결과는 실패였다."], ["And now, the morning of the first day of the seventh month, the extent of the additional information gained was that not long before two leprous women had been stoned from the Fish Gate by the authorities.", "그리고 이제, 일곱째 달 초하루 아침에 추가로 얻은 정보라고는, 얼마 전 두 나병환자 여인이 관헌에 의해 어문에서 돌팔매질을 당해 쫓겨났다는 것뿐이었다."], ["A little pressing of the clew, together with some shrewd comparison of dates, led to the sad assurance that the sufferers were the Hurs, and left the old questions darker than ever. Where were they?", "그 실마리를 조금 캐고 날짜를 빈틈없이 견주어 보니, 그 고난을 당한 이들이 후르 가문 사람들이라는 슬픈 확신에 이르렀고, 해묵은 의문은 그 어느 때보다 어두워졌다. 그들은 어디에 있는가?"], ["And what had become of them?", "또 그들은 어떻게 되었는가?"]], [["“It was not enough that my people should be made lepers,”", "\"내 가족이 나병환자가 된 것으로도 모자랐단 말인가.\""], ["said the son, over and over again, with what intensity of bitterness the reader may imagine; “that was not enough.", "아들은 거듭거듭 말했는데, 그 쓰라림이 얼마나 격렬했는지는 독자의 상상에 맡긴다. \"그것으로도 모자랐어. 오, 아니지!"], ["Oh no! They must be stoned from their native city!", "그들을 고향 도시에서 돌로 쳐서 내쫓아야만 했어!"], ["My mother is dead! she has wandered to the wilderness!", "내 어머니는 돌아가셨다! 광야를 헤매다 돌아가셨어!"], ["she is dead!", "돌아가셨어!"], ["Tirzah is dead!", "티르자도 죽었어!"], ["I alone am left.", "나만 홀로 남았다."], ["And for what? How long, O God, thou Lord God of my fathers, how long shall this Rome endure?”", "그런데 무엇을 위해서인가? 언제까지입니까, 오 하느님, 내 조상의 주 하느님이여, 언제까지 이 로마가 버티겠습니까?\""]], [["Angry, hopeless, vengeful, he entered the court of the khan, and found it crowded with people come in during the night.", "분노에 차고, 절망에 빠지고, 복수심에 불타며 그는 객사의 안뜰로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 밤사이 들어온 사람들로 붐비고 있었다."], ["While he ate his breakfast, he listened to some of them.", "그는 아침을 먹으며 그들 가운데 몇몇의 이야기에 귀를 기울였다."], ["To one party he was specially attracted.", "한 무리에게 그는 유독 마음이 끌렸다."], ["They were mostly young, stout, active, hardy men, in manner and speech provincial.", "그들은 대개 젊고, 건장하고, 활기차고, 다부진 사내들로, 거동과 말씨가 시골풍이었다."], ["In their look, the certain indefinable air, the pose of the head, glance of the eye, there was a spirit which did not, as a rule, belong to the outward seeming of the lower orders of Jerusalem; the spirit thought by some to be a peculiarity of life in mountainous districts, but which may be more surely traced to a life of healthful freedom.", "그들의 눈빛, 무어라 꼬집어 말할 수 없는 어떤 기운, 머리를 두는 자세, 눈을 굴리는 모습에는, 으레 예루살렘 하층민의 겉모습에 깃들지 않는 어떤 기상이 서려 있었다. 이는 어떤 이들이 산악 지대 생활의 특색이라 여기는 기상이나, 더 확실히는 건강하고 자유로운 삶에서 비롯된 것이라 할 수 있다."], ["In a short time he ascertained they were Galileans, in the city for various purposes, but chiefly to take part in the Feast of Trumpets, set for that day.", "얼마 지나지 않아 그는 그들이 갈릴리 사람들임을 알아냈으니, 여러 가지 볼일로 도시에 와 있었으나 주된 목적은 바로 그날로 정해진 나팔절에 참여하려는 것이었다."], ["They became to him at once objects of interest, as hailing from the region in which he hoped to find readiest support in the work he was shortly to set about.", "그들은 단번에 그의 관심거리가 되었으니, 그가 머지않아 착수하려는 일에서 가장 손쉽게 지지를 얻으리라 바라던 바로 그 지방에서 온 이들이었기 때문이다."]], [["While observing them, his mind running ahead in thought of achievements possible to a legion of such spirits disciplined after the severe Roman style, a man came into the court, his face much flushed, his eyes bright with excitement.", "그들을 살피며, 그의 마음은 그러한 기상을 지닌 자들로 이루어진 군단을 엄혹한 로마식으로 훈련시킨다면 능히 이룰 수 있을 업적을 앞질러 그리고 있었는데, 그때 한 사내가 안뜰로 들어왔으니, 얼굴은 잔뜩 상기되고 두 눈은 흥분으로 번득이고 있었다."]], [["“Why are you here?”", "\"여기서 무얼 하고 있는 거요?\""], ["he said to the Galileans.", "그가 갈릴리 사람들에게 말했다."], ["“The rabbis and elders are going from the Temple to see Pilate.", "\"랍비들과 장로들이 성전에서 빌라도를 보러 가고 있소."], ["Come, make haste, and let us go with them.”", "자, 서두르시오, 우리도 그들과 함께 갑시다.\""]], [["They surrounded him in a moment.", "그들이 순식간에 그를 에워쌌다."]], [["“To see Pilate!", "\"빌라도를 보러?"], ["For what?”", "무엇 때문에?\""]], [["“They have discovered a conspiracy. Pilate’s new aqueduct is to be paid for with money of the Temple.”", "\"음모가 발각됐소. 빌라도가 새로 짓는 수로를 성전 돈으로 치르려 한다오.\""]], [["“What, with the sacred treasure?”", "\"뭐라고, 신성한 보물로?\""]], [["They repeated the question to each other with flashing eyes.", "그들은 번득이는 눈으로 서로에게 그 물음을 되풀이했다."]], [["“It is Corban—money of God.", "\"그것은 코르반—하느님의 돈이오."], ["Let him touch a shekel of it if he dare!”", "그가 감히 한 세겔이라도 손댄다면 어디 두고 보자!\""]], [["“Come,” cried the messenger.", "\"갑시다.\" 전령이 외쳤다."], ["“The procession is by this time across the bridge. The whole city is pouring after.", "\"행렬은 지금쯤 다리를 건넜을 거요. 온 도시가 그 뒤를 따라 쏟아져 나오고 있소."], ["We may be needed.", "우리가 필요할지도 모르오."], ["Make haste!”", "서두릅시다!\""]], [["As if the thought and the act were one, there was quick putting away of useless garments, and the party stood forth bareheaded, and in the short sleeveless under-tunics they were used to wearing as reapers in the field and boatmen on the lake—the garb in which they climbed the hills following the herds, and plucked the ripened vintage, careless of the sun.", "생각과 행동이 하나이기라도 하듯, 거추장스러운 옷들이 잽싸게 벗어 던져졌고, 그 무리는 맨머리에, 들판에서 곡식을 거두는 일꾼으로서, 또 호수에서 뱃사공으로서 늘 걸치던 짧은 민소매 속옷 차림으로 나섰다. 그것은 그들이 가축 떼를 따라 언덕을 오르고, 햇볕에도 아랑곳없이 익은 포도를 따던 차림이었다."], ["Lingering only to tighten their girdles, they said, “We are ready.”", "허리띠를 단단히 죄느라 잠시 머뭇거렸을 뿐, 그들은 말했다. \"준비됐소.\""]], [["Then Ben-Hur spoke to them.", "그러자 벤허가 그들에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Men of Galilee,”", "\"갈릴리 사람들이여.\""], ["he said, “I am a son of Judah.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 유다의 아들이오."], ["Will you take me in your company?”", "그대들의 무리에 나를 끼워 주겠소?\""]], [["“We may have to fight,”", "\"싸워야 할지도 모르오.\""], ["they replied.", "그들이 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, then, I will not be first to run away!”", "\"오, 그렇다면 나는 결코 먼저 달아나지 않겠소!\""]], [["They took the retort in good humor, and the messenger said, “You seem stout enough.", "그들은 그 응수를 흔쾌히 받아들였고, 전령이 말했다. \"보아하니 어지간히 다부지구려."], ["Come along.”", "함께 갑시다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur put off his outer garments.", "벤허는 겉옷을 벗었다."]], [["“You think there may be fighting?” he asked, quietly, as he tightened his girdle.", "\"싸움이 벌어질지도 모른다고 생각하오?\" 그가 허리띠를 죄며 조용히 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“With whom?”", "\"누구와?\""]], [["“The guard.”", "\"수비대와.\""]], [["“Legionaries?”", "\"군단병과?\""]], [["“Whom else can a Roman trust?”", "\"로마인이 그 밖에 누구를 믿을 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“What have you to fight with?”", "\"무엇으로 싸울 작정이오?\""]], [["They looked at him silently.", "그들은 말없이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"음.\""], ["he continued, “we will have to do the best we can; but had we not better choose a leader?", "그가 말을 이었다. \"우리는 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해야 할 거요. 그래도 우두머리를 하나 정하는 게 낫지 않겠소?"], ["The legionaries always have one, and so are able to act with one mind.”", "군단병들은 늘 우두머리가 있어서 한마음으로 움직일 수 있는 거요.\""]], [["The Galileans stared more curiously, as if the idea were new to them.", "갈릴리 사람들은, 처음 듣는 발상이기라도 한 듯, 더욱 궁금한 눈길로 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Let us at least agree to stay together,”", "\"적어도 함께 뭉쳐 있기로 합시다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Now I am ready, if you are.”", "\"자, 그대들이 준비됐다면 나도 준비됐소.\""]], [["“Yes, let us go.”", "\"좋소, 갑시다.\""]], [["The khan, it should not be forgotten, was in Bezetha, the new town; and to get to the Praetorium, as the Romans resonantly styled the palace of Herod on Mount Zion, the party had to cross the lowlands north and west of the Temple.", "잊지 말아야 할 것은, 그 객사가 새 시가지인 베제타에 있었다는 사실이다. 그래서 로마인들이 시온산 위의 헤롯 궁전을 거창하게 일컫던 프라이토리움에 이르려면, 그 무리는 성전 북쪽과 서쪽의 저지대를 가로질러야 했다."], ["By streets—if they may be so called—trending north and south, with intersections hardly up to the dignity of alleys, they passed rapidly round the Akra district to the Tower of Mariamne, from which the way was short to the grand gate of the walled heights.", "남북으로 뻗은 거리들을—그렇게 부를 수 있다면—골목이라 할 위엄도 못 되는 교차로들과 함께 지나며, 그들은 아크라 지구를 빠르게 돌아 마리암네 탑에 이르렀으니, 거기서부터 성벽으로 둘러싸인 높은 곳의 웅장한 성문까지는 길이 짧았다."], ["In going, they overtook, or were overtaken by, people like themselves stirred to wrath by news of the proposed desecration.", "가는 길에 그들은 자신들처럼 신성모독의 소식에 분노가 끓어오른 사람들을 앞지르기도 하고, 그들에게 추월당하기도 했다."], ["When, at length, they reached the gate of the Praetorium, the procession of elders and rabbis had passed in with a great following, leaving a greater crowd clamoring outside.", "마침내 프라이토리움 성문에 다다랐을 때, 장로들과 랍비들의 행렬은 이미 큰 무리를 거느리고 안으로 들어간 뒤였고, 그보다 더 큰 군중이 밖에서 떠들썩하게 아우성치고 있었다."]], [["A centurion kept the entrance with a guard drawn up full armed under the beautiful marble battlements.", "백인대장이 아름다운 대리석 흉벽 아래 완전 무장한 채 도열한 수비대를 거느리고 입구를 지키고 있었다."], ["The sun struck the soldiers fervidly on helm and shield; but they kept their ranks indifferent alike to its dazzle and to the mouthings of the rabble.", "햇살이 병사들의 투구와 방패에 뜨겁게 내리쬐었으나, 그들은 그 눈부신 빛에도, 천한 무리의 떠벌림에도 한결같이 무심한 채 대오를 지켰다."], ["Through the open bronze gates a current of citizens poured in, while a much lesser one poured out.", "활짝 열린 청동 문으로 시민들의 물결이 안으로 쏟아져 들어갔고, 그보다 훨씬 적은 물결이 밖으로 흘러나왔다."]], [["“What is going on?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["one of the Galileans asked an outcomer.", "갈릴리 사람 하나가 나오는 이에게 물었다."]], [["“Nothing,” was the reply.", "\"아무 일도 없소.\" 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["“The rabbis are before the door of the palace asking to see Pilate.", "\"랍비들이 궁전 문 앞에서 빌라도를 만나게 해 달라고 청하고 있소."], ["He has refused to come out.", "그가 나오기를 거부했소."], ["They have sent one to tell him they will not go away till he has heard them.", "그래서 한 사람을 보내, 그가 자기들의 말을 들어줄 때까지 물러가지 않겠다고 전했소."], ["They are waiting.”", "지금 기다리는 중이오.\""]], [["“Let us go in,”", "\"들어갑시다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, in his quiet way, seeing what his companions probably did not, that there was not only a disagreement between the suitors and the governor, but an issue joined, and a serious question as to who should have his will.", "벤허가 특유의 조용한 어조로 말했다. 그는 자기 동료들이 미처 보지 못한 것을 보았으니, 청원하는 자들과 총독 사이에 의견 차이가 있을 뿐 아니라, 쟁점이 맞부딪쳤고, 누구의 뜻이 관철될 것인가 하는 심각한 문제가 걸려 있다는 것이었다."]], [["Inside the gate there was a row of trees in leaf, with seats under them.", "성문 안쪽에는 잎이 무성한 나무들이 줄지어 서 있고, 그 아래 의자들이 놓여 있었다."], ["The people, whether going or coming, carefully avoided the shade cast gratefully upon the white, clean-swept pavement; for, strange as it may seem, a rabbinical ordinance, alleged to have been derived from the law, permitted no green thing to be grown within the walls of Jerusalem.", "들어가는 이든 나오는 이든, 사람들은 희고 깨끗이 쓸린 포석 위에 고맙게도 드리워진 그늘을 애써 피했다. 기이하게 들릴지 모르나, 율법에서 비롯되었다고 일컬어지는 랍비들의 율례가 예루살렘 성벽 안에서는 어떤 푸른 것도 기르지 못하게 했기 때문이다."], ["Even the wise king, it was said, wanting a garden for his Egyptian bride, was constrained to found it down in the meeting-place of the valleys above En-rogel.", "전하는 바로는, 저 지혜로운 왕조차도 이집트인 신부를 위해 정원을 만들고 싶었으나, 엔로겔 위쪽 골짜기들이 만나는 자리 아래에 그것을 두지 않을 수 없었다고 한다."]], [["Through the tree-tops shone the outer fronts of the palace.", "나무 우듬지 사이로 궁전의 바깥 정면이 빛났다."], ["Turning to the right, the party proceeded a short distance to a spacious square, on the west side of which stood the residence of the governor.", "오른쪽으로 돌아 그 무리는 잠시 나아가 널찍한 광장에 이르렀으니, 그 서쪽에 총독의 거처가 서 있었다."], ["An excited multitude filled the square.", "흥분한 군중이 광장을 가득 메우고 있었다."], ["Every face was directed towards a portico built over a broad doorway which was closed.", "모든 얼굴이 닫힌 넓은 출입문 위에 세워진 주랑을 향하고 있었다."], ["Under the portico there was another array of legionaries.", "그 주랑 아래에는 또 한 무리의 군단병이 도열해 있었다."]], [["The throng was so close the friends could not well have advanced if such had been their desire; they remained therefore in the rear, observers of what was going on.", "군중이 어찌나 빽빽한지 그 벗들은 설령 그러고 싶었더라도 앞으로 나아가기 어려웠을 것이다. 그래서 그들은 뒤쪽에 남아 벌어지는 일을 지켜보았다."], ["About the portico they could see the high turbans of the rabbis, whose impatience communicated at times to the mass behind them; a cry was frequent to the effect “Pilate, if thou be a governor, come forth, come forth!”", "주랑 둘레로 랍비들의 높다란 터번이 보였는데, 그들의 조바심이 이따금 뒤에 늘어선 무리에게 옮아갔다. \"빌라도여, 그대가 총독이라면 나오라, 나오라!\" 하는 외침이 거듭 터져 나왔다."]], [["Once a man coming out pushed through the crowd, his face red with anger.", "한번은 밖으로 나온 사내가 분노로 얼굴이 벌게진 채 군중을 헤치고 지나갔다."]], [["“Israel is of no account here,”", "\"여기서는 이스라엘이 아무것도 아니로구나.\""], ["he said, in a loud voice.", "그가 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["“On this holy ground we are no better than dogs of Rome.”", "\"이 거룩한 땅에서 우리는 로마의 개만도 못한 신세다.\""]], [["“Will he not come out, think you?”", "\"그가 나올 것 같지 않소?\""]], [["“Come? Has he not thrice refused?”", "\"나오다니? 세 번이나 거절하지 않았소?\""]], [["“What will the rabbis do?”", "\"랍비들은 어떻게 할 작정이오?\""]], [["“As at Cæsarea—camp here till he gives them ear.”", "\"카이사레아에서처럼—그가 귀를 기울일 때까지 여기서 진을 칠 거요.\""]], [["“He will not dare touch the treasure, will he?”", "\"설마 그가 감히 보물에 손대지는 않겠지요?\""], ["asked one of the Galileans.", "갈릴리 사람 하나가 물었다."]], [["“Who can say?", "\"누가 알겠소?"], ["Did not a Roman profane the Holy of Holies?", "로마인이 지성소를 더럽힌 적이 있지 않소?"], ["Is there anything sacred from Romans?”", "로마인에게 거룩한 것이 무엇 하나라도 있겠소?\""]], [["An hour passed, and though Pilate deigned them no answer, the rabbis and crowd remained.", "한 시간이 지났으나, 빌라도가 그들에게 아무런 대답도 내려 주지 않았는데도 랍비들과 군중은 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["Noon came, bringing a shower from the west, but no change in the situation, except that the multitude was larger and much noisier, and the feeling more decidedly angry.", "정오가 되자 서쪽에서 소나기가 몰려왔으나, 상황에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 다만 군중이 더 불어나고 훨씬 더 시끄러워졌으며, 그 감정이 한결 단호하게 분노로 기울었을 뿐이었다."], ["The shouting was almost continuous, Come forth, come forth!", "나오라, 나오라! 하는 고함이 거의 끊이지 않았다."], ["The cry was sometimes with disrespectful variations.", "그 외침에는 때때로 불경한 변주가 섞였다."], ["Meanwhile Ben-Hur held his Galilean friends together.", "그동안 벤허는 갈릴리 동지들을 한데 묶어 두었다."], ["He judged the pride of the Roman would eventually get the better of his discretion, and that the end could not be far off.", "그는 로마인의 자존심이 끝내 그 분별을 이기리라, 그리고 그 끝이 멀지 않으리라 판단했다."], ["Pilate was but waiting for the people to furnish him an excuse for resort to violence.", "빌라도는 백성이 자신에게 폭력에 호소할 구실을 줄 때까지 기다리고 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["And at last the end came.", "마침내 그 끝이 왔다."], ["In the midst of the assemblage there was heard the sound of blows, succeeded instantly by yells of pain and rage, and a most furious commotion.", "무리 한복판에서 매질하는 소리가 들렸고, 곧바로 고통과 분노의 비명, 그리고 더없이 격렬한 소동이 뒤따랐다."], ["The venerable men in front of the portico faced about aghast.", "주랑 앞에 선 노인들이 아연실색하여 돌아섰다."], ["The common people in the rear at first pushed forward; in the centre, the effort was to get out; and for a short time the pressure of opposing forces was terrible.", "뒤편의 보통 사람들은 처음에 앞으로 밀고 나갔다. 한복판에서는 빠져나가려는 안간힘이 일었다. 잠시 동안 맞부딪치는 두 힘의 압력이 끔찍했다."], ["A thousand voices made inquiry, raised all at once; as no one had time to answer, the surprise speedily became a panic.", "일천의 목소리가 한꺼번에 일어나 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 아무도 대답할 겨를이 없으니, 놀라움은 삽시간에 공황으로 번졌다."]], [["Ben-Hur kept his senses.", "벤허는 침착함을 잃지 않았다."]], [["“You cannot see?”", "\"안 보이오?\""], ["he said to one of the Galileans.", "그가 갈릴리 사람 하나에게 말했다. \"안 보이오.\""]], [["“No.” “I will raise you up.”", "\"내가 들어 올려 주겠소.\""]], [["He caught the man about the middle, and lifted him bodily.", "그는 그 사내의 허리를 붙들어 번쩍 들어 올렸다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["“I see now,”", "\"이제 보이오.\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“There are some armed with clubs, and they are beating the people.", "\"몽둥이로 무장한 자들이 있는데, 사람들을 두들겨 패고 있소."], ["They are dressed like Jews.”", "유대인처럼 차려입었소.\""]], [["“Who are they?”", "\"그들이 누구요?\""]], [["“Romans, as the Lord liveth! Romans in disguise.", "\"로마인이오, 주께서 살아 계심을 두고 맹세하건대! 변장한 로마인이오."], ["Their clubs fly like flails!", "그들의 몽둥이가 도리깨처럼 휘날리고 있소!"], ["There, I saw a rabbi struck down—an old man!", "저기, 랍비 하나가 쓰러지는 걸 보았소—노인인데!"], ["They spare nobody!”", "그들은 아무도 봐주지 않소!\""]], [["Ben-Hur let the man down.", "벤허는 사내를 내려놓았다."]], [["“Men of Galilee,”", "\"갈릴리 사람들이여.\""], ["he said, “it is a trick of Pilate’s.", "그가 말했다. \"이것은 빌라도의 술책이오."], ["Now, will you do what I say, we will get even with the club-men.”", "자, 그대들이 내가 말하는 대로 한다면, 우리는 그 몽둥이꾼들에게 앙갚음할 수 있소.\""]], [["The Galilean spirit arose.", "갈릴리 사람의 기상이 솟구쳤다."]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"좋소, 좋소!\""], ["they answered.", "그들이 대답했다."]], [["“Let us go back to the trees by the gate, and we may find the planting of Herod, though unlawful, has some good in it after all. Come!”", "\"성문 옆 나무들이 있는 데로 돌아갑시다. 헤롯이 율법을 어기고 심은 그 나무들이 결국 쓸모가 좀 있다는 걸 알게 될지도 모르오. 갑시다!\""]], [["They ran back all of them fast as they could; and, by throwing their united weight upon the limbs, tore them from the trunks.", "그들 모두는 있는 힘껏 달려 되돌아갔다. 그리고 가지에 힘을 합쳐 매달려 몸무게를 실어, 줄기에서 가지를 뜯어냈다."], ["In a brief time they, too, were armed.", "잠깐 사이에 그들 또한 무장을 갖추었다."], ["Returning, at the corner of the square they met the crowd rushing madly for the gate.", "되돌아가는 길에, 광장 모퉁이에서 그들은 미친 듯이 성문으로 몰려오는 군중과 마주쳤다."], ["Behind, the clamor continued—a medley of shrieks, groans, and execrations.", "뒤에서는 비명과 신음과 저주가 뒤섞인 아우성이 계속 이어지고 있었다."]], [["“To the wall!”", "\"벽으로!\""], ["Ben-Hur shouted.", "벤허가 외쳤다."], ["“To the wall!—and let the herd go by!”", "\"벽으로!—그리고 저 떼거리는 지나가게 두시오!\""]], [["So, clinging to the masonry at their right hand, they escaped the might of the rush, and little by little made headway until, at last, the square was reached.", "그리하여 그들은 오른편 석벽에 바싹 붙어 밀려드는 군중의 힘을 피했고, 조금씩 앞으로 나아가 마침내 광장에 이르렀다."]], [["“Keep together now, and follow me!”", "\"이제 함께 뭉쳐 나를 따르시오!\""]], [["By this time Ben-Hur’s leadership was perfect; and as he pushed into the seething mob his party closed after him in a body.", "이 무렵 벤허의 지휘는 완벽했다. 그가 들끓는 군중 속으로 밀고 들어가자, 그의 무리가 한 덩어리로 그 뒤를 따라 좁혀 들었다."], ["And when the Romans, clubbing the people and making merry as they struck them down, came hand to hand with the Galileans, lithe of limb, eager for the fray, and equally armed, they were in turn surprised.", "그리고 사람들을 몽둥이로 후려치며 쳐 쓰러뜨리는 것을 즐기던 로마인들이, 팔다리가 날렵하고 싸움에 굶주린 데다 똑같이 무장한 갈릴리 사람들과 맞붙게 되자, 이번에는 그들이 놀랄 차례였다."], ["Then the shouting was close and fierce; the crash of sticks rapid and deadly; the advance furious as hate could make it.", "그러자 함성이 바싹 다가들며 사나워졌고, 막대기 부딪치는 소리가 빠르고 치명적으로 울렸으며, 진격은 증오가 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 맹렬한 것이었다."], ["No one performed his part as well as Ben-Hur, whose training served him admirably; for, not merely he knew to strike and guard; his long arm, perfect action, and incomparable strength helped him, also, to success in every encounter.", "누구도 벤허만큼 제 몫을 해내지 못했으니, 그의 훈련이 그를 훌륭히 도왔다. 그는 단지 치고 막을 줄 아는 것만이 아니라, 긴 팔과 완벽한 동작과 견줄 데 없는 힘이 또한 매번의 격돌에서 그를 승리로 이끌었기 때문이다."], ["He was at the same time fighting-man and leader.", "그는 싸우는 자이자 동시에 우두머리였다."], ["The club he wielded was of goodly length and weighty, so he had need to strike a man but once.", "그가 휘두르는 몽둥이는 길이가 넉넉하고 묵직해서, 한 사내를 단 한 번 후려치는 것으로 충분했다."], ["He seemed, moreover, to have eyes for each combat of his friends, and the faculty of being at the right moment exactly where he was most needed.", "게다가 그는 동지들의 격투 하나하나에까지 눈길이 닿는 듯했고, 가장 필요한 바로 그 순간 정확히 그 자리에 있는 재간을 지닌 듯했다."], ["In his fighting cry there were inspiration for his party and alarm for his enemies.", "그의 싸움터 외침에는 제 무리에게는 격려가, 적에게는 두려움이 담겨 있었다."], ["Thus surprised and equally matched, the Romans at first retired, but finally turned their backs and fled to the portico.", "그리하여 불시에 당하고 힘으로도 대등하게 맞붙은 로마인들은 처음에는 물러서다가, 끝내 등을 돌려 주랑으로 달아났다."], ["The impetuous Galileans would have pursued them to the steps, but Ben-Hur wisely restrained them.", "혈기 넘치는 갈릴리 사람들은 그들을 계단까지 뒤쫓으려 했으나, 벤허가 슬기롭게 그들을 말렸다."]], [["“Stay, my men!”", "\"멈추시오, 형제들!\""], ["he said. “The centurion yonder is coming with the guard.", "그가 말했다. \"저기 백인대장이 수비대를 거느리고 오고 있소."], ["They have swords and shields; we cannot fight them.", "저들은 칼과 방패를 지녔으니, 우리는 그들과 싸울 수 없소."], ["We have done well; let us get back and out of the gate while we may.”", "우리는 잘 해냈소. 할 수 있을 때 되돌아가 성문 밖으로 빠져나갑시다.\""]], [["They obeyed him, though slowly; for they had frequently to step over their countrymen lying where they had been felled; some writhing and groaning, some praying help, others mute as the dead.", "그들은, 비록 더디기는 했으나, 그의 말에 따랐다. 쓰러진 자리에 그대로 누운 동포들을 자주 넘어가야 했기 때문이다. 더러는 몸부림치며 신음했고, 더러는 도움을 구하며 빌었으며, 또 더러는 죽은 듯 말이 없었다."], ["But the fallen were not all Jews. In that there was consolation.", "그러나 쓰러진 자들이 모두 유대인은 아니었다. 거기에 위안이 있었다."]], [["The centurion shouted to them as they went off; Ben-Hur laughed at him, and replied in his own tongue, “If we are dogs of Israel, you are jackals of Rome.", "백인대장이 그들이 떠나는 동안 그들에게 소리쳤다. 벤허는 그를 비웃으며 제 나라 말로 응수했다. \"우리가 이스라엘의 개라면, 그대들은 로마의 자칼이오."], ["Remain here, and we will come again.”", "여기 남아 있으시오, 우리가 다시 오겠소.\""]], [["The Galileans cheered, and laughing went on.", "갈릴리 사람들은 환호하며 웃음을 터뜨리고는 가던 길을 갔다."]], [["Outside the gate there was a multitude the like of which Ben-Hur had never seen, not even in the circus at Antioch.", "성문 밖에는 벤허가 일찍이, 안티오크의 원형 경기장에서조차 본 적 없는 엄청난 군중이 모여 있었다."], ["The house-tops, the streets, the slope of the hill, appeared densely covered with people wailing and praying.", "지붕들, 거리들, 언덕의 비탈이 통곡하며 기도하는 사람들로 빽빽이 뒤덮인 듯했다."], ["The air was filled with their cries and imprecations.", "그들의 외침과 저주가 대기를 가득 채웠다."]], [["The party were permitted to pass without challenge by the outer guard.", "그 무리는 바깥 수비대의 제지 없이 통과할 수 있었다."], ["But hardly were they out before the centurion in charge at the portico appeared, and in the gateway called to Ben-Hur,", "그러나 그들이 막 빠져나오자마자, 주랑을 지키던 백인대장이 나타나 성문에서 벤허에게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Ho, insolent!", "\"이봐, 건방진 놈!"], ["Art thou a Roman or a Jew?”", "너는 로마인이냐 유대인이냐?\""]], [["Ben-Hur answered, “I am a son of Judah, born here.", "벤허가 대답했다. \"나는 유다의 아들이며, 여기서 태어났다."], ["What wouldst thou with me?”", "나에게 무슨 볼일이냐?\""]], [["“Stay and fight.”", "\"남아서 싸워라.\""]], [["“Singly?”", "\"일대일로?\""]], [["“As thou wilt!”", "\"네 좋을 대로!\""]], [["Ben-Hur laughed derisively.", "벤허는 비웃듯 웃었다."]], [["“O brave Roman!", "\"오, 용감한 로마인이여!"], ["Worthy son of the bastard Roman Jove!", "사생아 로마 신 유피테르의 합당한 아들이로구나!"], ["I have no arms.”", "나는 무기가 없다.\""]], [["“Thou shalt have mine,”", "\"내 것을 주마.\""], ["the centurion answered.", "백인대장이 대답했다."], ["“I will borrow of the guard here.”", "\"여기 수비대에게서 빌리겠다.\""]], [["The people in hearing of the colloquy became silent; and from them the hush spread afar.", "그 문답을 듣고 있던 사람들이 조용해졌고, 그 침묵이 그들에게서 멀리 번져 나갔다."], ["But lately Ben-Hur had beaten a Roman under the eyes of Antioch and the Farther East; now, could he beat another one under the eyes of Jerusalem, the honor might be vastly profitable to the cause of the New King.", "바로 얼마 전 벤허는 안티오크와 동방 저편의 눈앞에서 로마인 하나를 꺾었다. 이제 예루살렘의 눈앞에서 또 한 사람을 꺾을 수 있다면, 그 영예는 새 왕의 대업에 더없이 유익할 터였다."], ["He did not hesitate.", "그는 망설이지 않았다."], ["Going frankly to the centurion, he said, “I am willing.", "거리낌 없이 백인대장에게 다가가며 그가 말했다. \"기꺼이 하겠다."], ["Lend me thy sword and shield.”", "그대의 칼과 방패를 빌려다오.\""]], [["“And the helm and breastplate?” asked the Roman.", "\"투구와 흉갑도?\" 로마인이 물었다."]], [["“Keep them. They might not fit me.”", "\"그건 그냥 둬라. 내게 맞지 않을 테니.\""]], [["The arms were as frankly delivered, and directly the centurion was ready.", "무기는 그만큼이나 거리낌 없이 건네졌고, 곧 백인대장도 채비를 갖추었다."], ["All this time the soldiers in rank close by the gate never moved; they simply listened.", "그동안 내내 성문 가까이 도열한 병사들은 꿈쩍도 하지 않았으니, 그저 듣고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["As to the multitude, only when the combatants advanced to begin the fight the question sped from mouth to mouth, “Who is he?”", "군중으로 말하자면, 두 결투자가 싸움을 시작하려 나아갔을 때에야 비로소 \"저자는 누구냐?\" 하는 물음이 이 입에서 저 입으로 번졌다."], ["And no one knew.", "그러나 아무도 알지 못했다."]], [["Now the Roman supremacy in arms lay in three things—submission to discipline, the legionary formation of battle, and a peculiar use of the short sword.", "자, 무기를 다루는 데 있어 로마인의 우월함은 세 가지에 있었다—규율에 대한 복종, 군단의 전투 대형, 그리고 단검의 독특한 사용법이었다."], ["In combat, they never struck or cut; from first to last they thrust—they advanced thrusting, they retired thrusting; and generally their aim was at the foeman’s face.", "싸움에서 그들은 결코 후려치거나 베지 않았으니, 처음부터 끝까지 찔렀다. 찌르며 나아가고 찌르며 물러섰다. 그리고 대개 그들의 겨냥은 적의 얼굴이었다."], ["All this was well known to Ben-Hur.", "이 모든 것을 벤허는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["As they were about to engage he said,", "그들이 맞붙으려 할 때 그가 말했다."]], [["“I told thee I was a son of Judah; but I did not tell that I am lanista-taught.", "\"내가 유다의 아들이라고 그대에게 말했지. 그러나 내가 검투사 사범에게 배웠다는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["Defend thyself!”", "막아 봐라!\""]], [["At the last word Ben-Hur closed with his antagonist.", "마지막 말과 함께 벤허는 적수에게 달려들었다."], ["A moment, standing foot to foot, they glared at each other over the rims of their embossed shields; then the Roman pushed forward and feinted an under-thrust.", "한순간, 두 사람은 발과 발을 맞댄 채 돋을새김한 방패의 테두리 너머로 서로를 노려보았다. 그러더니 로마인이 앞으로 밀고 들며 낮은 찌르기를 거짓 동작으로 시도했다."], ["The Jew laughed at him.", "유대인은 그를 비웃었다."], ["A thrust at the face followed.", "얼굴을 향한 찌르기가 뒤따랐다."], ["The Jew stepped lightly to the left; quick as the thrust was, the step was quicker.", "유대인은 가볍게 왼쪽으로 발을 옮겼다. 찌르기가 빨랐으나, 그 발놀림이 더 빨랐다."], ["Under the lifted arm of the foe he slid his shield, advancing it until the sword and sword-arm were both caught on its upper surface; another step, this time forward and left, and the man’s whole right side was offered to the point.", "적이 치켜든 팔 아래로 그는 방패를 밀어 넣어, 칼과 칼을 쥔 팔이 둘 다 그 윗면에 걸릴 때까지 들이밀었다. 또 한 걸음, 이번에는 앞으로, 그리고 왼쪽으로 내딛자, 그 사내의 오른쪽 옆구리 전체가 칼끝에 드러났다."], ["The centurion fell heavily on his breast, clanging the pavement, and Ben-Hur had won.", "백인대장은 가슴을 짓찧으며 포석을 쟁그랑 울리며 무겁게 쓰러졌고, 벤허가 이겼다."], ["With his foot upon his enemy’s back, he raised his shield overhead after a gladiatorial custom, and saluted the imperturbable soldiers by the gate.", "그는 적의 등을 발로 디딘 채, 검투사의 관습에 따라 방패를 머리 위로 치켜들고는, 성문 옆에 미동도 없는 병사들에게 경례를 보냈다."]], [["When the people realized the victory they behaved like mad.", "사람들이 그 승리를 깨닫자 미친 듯이 날뛰었다."], ["On the houses far as the Xystus, fast as the word could fly, they waved their shawls and handkerchiefs and shouted; and if he had consented, the Galileans would have carried Ben-Hur off upon their shoulders.", "크시스토스에 이르기까지 멀리 지붕마다, 말이 날아갈 수 있는 한 빠르게, 그들은 숄과 손수건을 흔들며 환호했다. 그리고 만약 그가 허락했더라면, 갈릴리 사람들은 벤허를 어깨에 메고 갔으리라."]], [["To a petty officer who then advanced from the gate he said, “Thy comrade died like a soldier.", "그때 성문에서 나선 하급 장교에게 그가 말했다. \"그대의 동료는 군인답게 죽었다."], ["I leave him undespoiled.", "나는 그를 약탈하지 않은 채 남겨 둔다."], ["Only his sword and shield are mine.”", "다만 그의 칼과 방패는 내 것이다.\""]], [["With that, he walked away.", "그 말과 함께 그는 걸어 나갔다."], ["Off a little he spoke to the Galileans.", "조금 떨어진 곳에서 그는 갈릴리 사람들에게 말했다."]], [["“Brethren, you have behaved well.", "\"형제들이여, 그대들은 잘 싸웠소."], ["Let us now separate, lest we be pursued.", "이제 헤어집시다, 추격당하지 않도록 말이오."], ["Meet me to-night at the khan in Bethany.", "오늘 밤 베다니의 객사에서 나를 만나 주시오."], ["I have something to propose to you of great interest to Israel.”", "이스라엘에 크게 이로운 일을 그대들에게 제안하고 싶소.\""]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"당신은 누구요?\""], ["they asked him.", "그들이 그에게 물었다."]], [["“A son of Judah,”", "\"유다의 아들이오.\""], ["he answered, simply.", "그가 간단히 대답했다."]], [["A throng eager to see him surged around the party.", "그를 보려고 안달하는 무리가 그 일행 둘레로 밀려들었다."]], [["“Will you come to Bethany?” he asked.", "\"베다니로 오겠소?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, we will come.”", "\"그렇소, 가겠소.\""]], [["“Then bring with you this sword and shield that I may know you.”", "\"그러면 이 칼과 방패를 가지고 오시오, 그래야 내가 그대들을 알아볼 수 있소.\""]], [["Pushing brusquely through the increasing crowd, he speedily disappeared.", "점점 불어나는 군중을 거칠게 헤치며, 그는 이내 자취를 감추었다."]], [["At the instance of Pilate, the people went up from the city, and carried off their dead and wounded, and there was much mourning for them; but the grief was greatly lightened by the victory of the unknown champion, who was everywhere sought, and by every one extolled.", "빌라도의 분부에 따라, 사람들은 도시에서 올라가 자기네 죽은 자와 다친 자를 거두어 갔고, 그들을 두고 통곡이 컸다. 그러나 그 슬픔은 알려지지 않은 투사의 승리로 크게 가벼워졌으니, 그 투사는 사방에서 수소문되었고 누구에게나 칭송받았다."], ["The fainting spirit of the nation was revived by the brave deed; insomuch that in the streets and up in the Temple even, amidst the solemnities of the feast, old tales of the Maccabees were told again, and thousands shook their heads whispering wisely,", "그 용맹한 행적으로 풀 죽었던 민족의 기상이 되살아났다. 그리하여 거리에서도, 또 성전 위에서도, 절기의 엄숙한 의식 한가운데서마저, 마카베오 시대의 옛이야기가 다시금 입에 올랐고, 수천의 사람들이 고개를 끄덕이며 슬기롭게 속삭였다."]], [["“A little longer, only a little longer, brethren, and Israel will come to her own. Let there be faith in the Lord, and patience.”", "\"조금만 더, 형제들이여, 아주 조금만 더 기다리면, 이스라엘은 제 권리를 되찾을 것이오. 주를 믿고, 참읍시다.\""]], [["In such manner Ben-Hur obtained hold on Galilee, and paved the way to greater services in the cause of the King Who Was Coming.", "이런 식으로 벤허는 갈릴리에 발판을 마련했고, 장차 오실 왕의 대업에 더 큰 봉사를 바칠 길을 닦았다."]], [["And with what result we shall see.", "그리하여 그 결과가 어떠했는지는 차차 보게 될 것이다."]], [["# BOOK SEVENTH", "# BOOK SEVENTH"]], [["“And, waking, I beheld her there Sea-dreaming in the moted air, A siren lithe and debonair, With wristlets woven of scarlet weeds, And oblong lucent amber beads Of sea-kelp shining in her hair.”", "“그리고 잠에서 깨어나, 나는 그곳에 있는 그녀를 보았네\n티끌 어린 대기 속에서 바다를 꿈꾸는 그녀를,"]], [["# THOMAS BAILEY ALDRICH. CHAPTER I", "# THOMAS BAILEY ALDRICH. CHAPTER I"]], [["The meeting took place in the khan of Bethany as appointed. Thence Ben-Hur went with the Galileans into their country, where his exploits up in the old Market-place gave him fame and influence. Before the winter was gone he raised three legions, and organized them after the Roman pattern. He could have had as many more, for the martial spirit of that gallant people never slept. The proceeding, however, required careful guarding as against both Rome and Herod Antipas. Contenting himself for the present with the three, he strove to train and educate them for systematic action. For that purpose he carried the officers over into the lava-beds of Trachonitis, and taught them the use of arms, particularly the javelin and sword, and the manoeuvering peculiar to the legionary formation; after which he sent them home as teachers. And soon the training became a pastime of the people.", "날렵하고 우아한 한 세이렌을,\n진홍빛 해초로 엮은 팔찌를 두르고,"]], [["As may be thought, the task called for patience, skill, zeal, faith, and devotion on his part—qualities into which the power of inspiring others in matters of difficulty is always resolvable; and never man possessed them in greater degree or used them to better effect. How he labored! And with utter denial of self! Yet withal he would have failed but for the support he had from Simonides, who furnished him arms and money, and from Ilderim, who kept watch and brought him supplies. And still he would have failed but for the genius of the Galileans.", "머리카락에는 바닷말의 길쭉하고 투명한\n호박빛 구슬을 빛내고 있는 그녀를.”"]], [["Under that name were comprehended the four tribes—Asher, Zebulon, Issachar, and Naphthali—and the districts originally set apart to them. The Jew born in sight of the Temple despised these brethren of the north; but the Talmud itself has said, “The Galilean loves honor, and the Jew money.”", "토머스 베일리 올드리치. 약속한 대로 만남은 베다니의 카라반사라이에서 이루어졌다. 그곳에서 벤허는 갈릴리 사람들과 함께 그들의 고향으로 들어갔으니, 옛 장터에서 떨친 그의 공적이 그에게 명성과 영향력을 안겨 주었다. 겨울이 다 가기 전에 그는 세 개의 군단을 일으켜 로마식으로 편성했다. 마음만 먹었다면 그보다 더 많이 거느릴 수도 있었으니, 그 용맹한 백성의 상무 정신은 결코 잠드는 법이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 일은 로마와 헤롯 안티파스 양쪽을 경계하며 조심스럽게 진행해야 했다. 그는 당분간 세 군단으로 만족하면서, 그들을 조직적인 행동에 맞게 훈련하고 가르치는 데 힘을 쏟았다. 그 목적을 위해 그는 장교들을 트라코니티스의 용암 지대로 데려가 무기 다루는 법, 특히 투창과 칼 쓰는 법, 그리고 군단 대형 특유의 기동을 가르쳤다. 그런 다음 교관으로 삼아 고향으로 돌려보냈다. 머지않아 그 훈련은 백성의 놀이가 되었다."]], [["Hating Rome fervidly as they loved their own country, in every revolt they were first in the field and last to leave it. One hundred and fifty thousand Galilean youths perished in the final war with Rome.", "짐작할 수 있듯이, 이 과업은 그에게 인내와 기술, 열성과 믿음과 헌신을 요구했다—어려운 일에서 남을 분발하게 만드는 힘은 언제나 이러한 자질로 환원되는 법인데, 일찍이 그 누구도 그보다 더 큰 정도로 이를 지녔거나 더 훌륭하게 발휘한 적이 없었다. 그가 얼마나 애를 썼던가!"], ["For the great festal days, they went up to Jerusalem marching and camping like armies; yet they were liberal in sentiment, and even tolerant to heathenism.", "그것도 자기를 철저히 버린 채로! 그러나 그 모든 노력에도 불구하고, 무기와 자금을 대 준 시모니데스와 망을 보며 보급품을 가져다준 일데림의 지원이 없었다면 그는 실패하고 말았을 것이다."], ["In Herod’s beautiful cities, which were Roman in all things, in Sepphoris and Tiberias especially, they took pride, and in the building them gave loyal support.", "그리고 갈릴리 사람들의 천성이 없었더라도 여전히 실패했을 것이다. 그 이름 아래에는 네 지파—아셀, 스불론, 잇사갈, 납달리—와 본래 그들에게 나뉘어 떨어진 지역들이 포괄되었다."], ["They had for fellow-citizens men from the outside world everywhere, and lived in peace with them.", "성전이 보이는 곳에서 태어난 유대인은 이 북방의 형제들을 멸시했다. 그러나 탈무드조차 이렇게 말했다."], ["To the glory of the Hebrew name they contributed poets like the singer of the Song of Songs and prophets like Hosea.", "“갈릴리 사람은 명예를 사랑하고, 유대인은 돈을 사랑한다.”"]], [["Upon such a people, so quick, so proud, so brave, so devoted, so imaginative, a tale like that of the coming of the King was all-powerful. That he was coming to put Rome down would have been sufficient to enlist them in the scheme proposed by Ben-Hur; but when, besides, they were assured he was to rule the world, more mighty than Cæsar, more magnificent than Solomon, and that the rule was to last forever, the appeal was irresistible, and they vowed themselves to the cause body and soul. They asked Ben-Hur his authority for the sayings, and he quoted the prophets, and told them of Balthasar in waiting over in Antioch; and they were satisfied, for it was the old much-loved legend of the Messiah, familiar to them almost as the name of the Lord; the long-cherished dream with a time fixed for its realization. The King was not merely coming now; he was at hand.", "자기 고향을 사랑하는 만큼 로마를 격렬히 미워했기에, 그들은 어떤 반란에서든 가장 먼저 싸움터에 나서고 가장 나중에 떠났다. 로마와의 마지막 전쟁에서 갈릴리 젊은이 십오만 명이 목숨을 잃었다. 큰 축제일이면 그들은 군대처럼 행군하고 진을 치며 예루살렘으로 올라갔다. 그러면서도 그들은 정서가 너그러웠고, 심지어 이교도에게도 관대했다. 모든 면에서 로마식이던 헤롯의 아름다운 도시들, 특히 세포리스와 티베리아스를 자랑스러워했고, 그것을 짓는 데 충실히 힘을 보탰다. 그들은 바깥세상 곳곳에서 온 사람들을 동료 시민으로 두고 그들과 평화롭게 살았다. 히브리라는 이름의 영광을 위해 그들은 아가를 노래한 시인 같은 이들과 호세아 같은 예언자들을 배출했다. 이처럼 재빠르고, 자긍심 높고, 용감하고, 헌신적이며, 상상력 풍부한 백성에게, 왕의 도래에 관한 이야기는 더없이 강력했다. 그가 로마를 무너뜨리러 온다는 것만으로도 벤허가 내놓은 계획에 그들을 끌어들이기에 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 그뿐만 아니라, 그가 카이사르보다 더 강하고 솔로몬보다 더 장엄하게 온 세상을 다스릴 것이며 그 통치가 영원히 이어지리라는 확언까지 들었으니, 그 호소는 거역할 수 없는 것이 되어, 그들은 몸과 영혼을 다해 그 대의에 스스로를 바쳤다. 그들은 벤허에게 그런 말들의 근거를 물었고, 그는 예언자들의 말을 인용하며 안티오크에서 기다리고 있는 발타사르에 대해 일러 주었다. 그러자 그들은 흡족해했으니, 그것은 메시아에 관한 오래되고 사랑받는 전설로, 그들에게는 주님의 이름만큼이나 친숙한 것이요, 실현될 때까지 정해진, 오래도록 품어 온 꿈이었기 때문이다. 이제 왕은 단지 오는 정도가 아니라, 바로 코앞에 와 있었다."]], [["So with Ben-Hur the winter months rolled by, and spring came, with gladdening showers blown over from the summering sea in the west; and by that time so earnestly and successfully had he toiled that he could say to himself and his followers, “Let the good King come.", "이렇게 벤허에게 겨울 몇 달이 흘러갔고, 서쪽 여름 바다에서 불어온 반가운 소나기와 함께 봄이 찾아왔다. 그 무렵 그는 너무도 진지하고 성공적으로 애쓴 끝에, 스스로에게도 추종자들에게도 이렇게 말할 수 있었다. “선하신 왕이여, 오십시오."], ["He has only to tell us where he will have his throne set up.", "어디에 왕좌를 세우려 하시는지 우리에게 말씀만 하시면 됩니다."], ["We have the sword-hands to keep it for him.”", "그것을 지켜 드릴 검의 손길은 우리에게 있습니다.”"]], [["And in all his dealings with the many men they knew him only as a son of Judah, and by that name.", "그리고 그 수많은 사람과의 모든 거래에서, 그들은 그를 오직 유다의 아들로만, 그 이름으로만 알고 있었다."]], [["One evening, over in Trachonitis, Ben-Hur was sitting with some of his Galileans at the mouth of the cave in which he quartered, when an Arab courier rode to him, and delivered a letter.", "어느 저녁, 트라코니티스에서 벤허는 자신이 머무는 동굴 어귀에 갈릴리 사람 몇과 함께 앉아 있었는데, 한 아랍인 전령이 말을 타고 그에게 다가와 편지 한 통을 건넸다."], ["Breaking the package, he read,", "봉함을 뜯고 그는 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“Jerusalem, Nisan IV.", "“예루살렘, 니산월 사일."]], [["“A prophet has appeared who men say is Elias.", "“한 예언자가 나타났는데, 사람들은 그가 엘리야라고 합니다."], ["He has been in the wilderness for years, and to our eyes he is a prophet; and such also is his speech, the burden of which is of one much greater than himself, who, he says, is to come presently, and for whom he is now waiting on the eastern shore of the River Jordan.", "그는 여러 해 동안 광야에 있었으니, 우리 눈에도 그는 예언자입니다. 그의 말씀 또한 그러하여, 그 핵심은 자기보다 훨씬 위대한 한 분에 관한 것입니다. 그분이 곧 오시리라고 그는 말하며, 지금 요르단강 동편 기슭에서 그분을 기다리고 있습니다."], ["I have been to see and hear him, and the one he is waiting for is certainly the King you are awaiting.", "나는 그를 보고 그 말을 들으러 다녀왔는데, 그가 기다리는 그분은 분명 그대가 기다리는 왕입니다."], ["Come and judge for yourself.", "직접 와서 판단해 보십시오."]], [["“All Jerusalem is going out to the prophet, and with many people else the shore on which he abides is like Mount Olivet in the last days of the Passover.", "“온 예루살렘이 그 예언자에게로 몰려가고 있으며, 그 밖에도 수많은 사람과 더불어, 그가 머무는 기슭은 마치 유월절 마지막 날들의 올리브산과 같습니다. “말루크.”"]], [["Ben-Hur’s face flushed with joy.", "벤허의 얼굴이 기쁨으로 붉게 달아올랐다."]], [["“By this word, O my friends,”", "“오, 나의 벗들이여, 이 소식으로,”"], ["he said—“by this word, our waiting is at end. The herald of the King has appeared and announced him.”", "그가 말했다—“이 소식으로 우리의 기다림은 끝이 났소. 왕의 선구자가 나타나 그분을 알렸소.”"]], [["Upon hearing the letter read, they also rejoiced at the promise it held out.", "편지를 읽어 주는 것을 듣자, 그들 또한 그것이 품은 약속에 기뻐했다."]], [["“Get ready now,”", "“이제 채비를 하시오,”"], ["he added, “and in the morning set your faces homeward; when arrived there, send word to those under you, and bid them be ready to assemble as I may direct.", "그가 덧붙였다. “그리고 아침이 되면 고향으로 발걸음을 돌리시오. 거기 이르거든, 휘하의 사람들에게 기별을 보내, 내가 지시하는 대로 모일 수 있도록 준비하라 이르시오."], ["For myself and you, I will go see if the King be indeed at hand, and send you report.", "나와 그대들을 위하여, 나는 가서 왕이 정말로 가까이 오셨는지 살펴보고, 그대들에게 보고를 보내겠소."], ["Let us, in the meantime, live in the pleasure of the promise.”", "그동안 우리, 그 약속의 기쁨 속에 살아갑시다.”"]], [["Going into the cave, he addressed a letter to Ilderim, and another to Simonides, giving notice of the news received, and of his purpose to go up immediately to Jerusalem.", "동굴로 들어간 그는 일데림에게 편지 한 통을, 시모니데스에게 또 한 통을 써서, 받은 소식과 곧장 예루살렘으로 올라가려는 자신의 뜻을 알렸다."], ["The letters he despatched by swift messengers.", "그 편지들은 빠른 전령들 편에 부쳤다."], ["When night fell, and the stars of direction came out, he mounted, and with an Arab guide set out for the Jordan, intending to strike the track of the caravans between Rabbath-Ammon and Damascus.", "밤이 깃들고 방향을 일러 주는 별들이 떠오르자, 그는 말에 올라 아랍인 길잡이와 함께 요르단강을 향해 길을 떠났으니, 라바스암몬과 다마스쿠스 사이를 잇는 대상로를 타려는 작정이었다."]], [["The guide was sure, and Aldebaran swift; so by midnight the two were out of the lava fastness speeding southward.", "길잡이는 미덥고 알데바란은 빨랐다. 그리하여 자정 무렵 두 사람은 용암 요새를 벗어나 남쪽으로 내달리고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["It was Ben-Hur’s purpose to turn aside at the break of day, and find a safe place in which to rest; but the dawn overtook him while out in the Desert, and he kept on, the guide promising to bring him afterwhile to a vale shut in by great rocks, where there were a spring, some mulberry-trees, and herbage in plenty for the horses.", "날이 밝으면 길을 벗어나 쉴 만한 안전한 곳을 찾는 것이 벤허의 생각이었다. 그러나 새벽은 그가 아직 사막 한가운데 있을 때 그를 따라잡았고, 그는 그대로 길을 이어 갔다. 안내인은 큰 바위들에 둘러싸인 골짜기로 곧 데려다주겠노라 약속했는데, 그곳에는 샘이 있고, 뽕나무가 몇 그루 있으며, 말들이 먹을 풀이 넉넉하다고 했다."]], [["As he rode thinking of the wondrous events so soon to happen, and of the changes they were to bring about in the affairs of men and nations, the guide, ever on the alert, called attention to an appearance of strangers behind them.", "곧 일어날 그 놀라운 일들과, 그 일들이 사람과 나라들의 운명에 가져올 변화를 생각하며 말을 몰고 가던 그에게, 늘 경계를 늦추지 않던 안내인이 뒤편에 낯선 자들이 나타났음을 일러 주었다."], ["Everywhere around the Desert stretched away in waves of sand, slowly yellowing in the growing light, and without any green thing visible.", "사방으로 사막이 모래 물결을 이루며 멀리 뻗어 있었고, 그 물결은 밝아 오는 빛 속에서 서서히 누렇게 물들었으며, 푸른 것이라곤 하나도 보이지 않았다."], ["Over on the left, but still far off, a range of low mountains extended, apparently interminable.", "왼편으로는 아직 멀긴 했으나 낮은 산줄기가 이어졌는데, 보아하니 끝이 없는 듯했다."], ["In the vacancy of such a waste an object in motion could not long continue a mystery.", "그처럼 텅 빈 황무지에서 움직이는 물체가 오래도록 수수께끼로 남을 수는 없었다."]], [["“It is a camel with riders,”", "\"사람들을 태운 낙타입니다.\""], ["the guide said, directly.", "안내인이 곧바로 말했다."]], [["“Are there others behind?”", "\"그 뒤에 다른 자들은 없느냐?\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 물었다."]], [["“It is alone.", "\"한 마리뿐입니다."], ["No, there is a man on horseback—the driver, probably.”", "아니, 말을 탄 사람이 하나 있군요—몰이꾼인 듯합니다.\""]], [["A little later Ben-Hur himself could see the camel was white and unusually large, reminding him of the wonderful animal he had seen bring Balthasar and Iras to the fountain in the Grove of Daphne.", "조금 뒤에는 벤허 자신도 그 낙타가 희고 유난히 크다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었으니, 다프네 숲의 샘으로 발타사르와 이라스를 데려오던 그 놀라운 짐승이 떠올랐다."], ["There could be no other like it.", "그런 낙타가 또 있을 리 없었다."], ["Thinking then of the fair Egyptian, insensibly his gait became slower, and at length fell into the merest loiter, until finally he could discern a curtained houdah, and two persons seated within it.", "그러자 그 아리따운 이집트 여인이 생각나, 자기도 모르게 걸음이 느려지더니, 마침내는 거의 어슬렁거리는 지경에 이르렀고, 끝내는 휘장을 드리운 가마와 그 안에 앉은 두 사람을 분간할 수 있었다."], ["If they were Balthasar and Iras!", "만일 발타사르와 이라스라면!"], ["Should he make himself known to them?", "자신을 그들에게 알려야 할까?"], ["But it could not be: this was the Desert—and they were alone.", "그러나 그럴 리는 없었다. 이곳은 사막이었고—그들은 단둘이었다."], ["But while he debated the question the long swinging stride of the camel brought its riders up to him.", "그러나 그가 이 물음을 두고 망설이는 사이, 낙타의 길고 흔들거리는 걸음이 탄 이들을 그에게로 데려왔다."], ["He heard the ringing of the tiny bells, and beheld the rich housings which had been so attractive to the crowd at the Castalian fount.", "그는 작은 방울들이 딸랑거리는 소리를 들었고, 카스탈리아 샘가의 군중을 그토록 끌어당기던 화려한 장구를 보았다."], ["He beheld also the Ethiopian, always attendant upon the Egyptians.", "또한 늘 이집트인들을 시중드는 에티오피아인도 보였다."], ["The tall brute stopped close by his horse, and Ben-Hur, looking up, lo!", "그 키 큰 짐승은 그의 말 바로 곁에 멈춰 섰고, 벤허가 고개를 들어 올려다보니, 이런!"], ["Iras herself under the raised curtain looking down at him, her great swimming eyes bright with astonishment and inquiry!", "이라스가 걷어 올린 휘장 아래에서 그를 내려다보고 있었으니, 크고 촉촉한 두 눈이 놀라움과 궁금증으로 빛나고 있었다!"]], [["“The blessing of the true God upon you!”", "\"참된 하느님의 축복이 그대에게 있기를!\""], ["said Balthasar, in his tremulous voice.", "발타사르가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“And to thee and thine be the peace of the Lord,”", "\"그대와 그대의 사람들에게도 주님의 평화가 깃들기를.\""], ["Ben-Hur replied.", "벤허가 대답했다."]], [["“My eyes are weak with years,”", "\"내 눈은 나이가 들어 흐릿하오.\""], ["said Balthasar; “but they approve you that son of Hur whom lately I knew an honored guest in the tent of Ilderim the Generous.”", "발타사르가 말했다. \"그러나 이 눈도 그대가, 얼마 전 내가 너그러운 일데림의 천막에서 귀한 손님으로 뵌 후르의 아들임을 알아보는구려.\""]], [["“And thou art that Balthasar, the wise Egyptian, whose speech concerning certain holy things in expectation is having so much to do with the finding me in this waste place.", "\"그러시면 어르신은 그 발타사르, 곧 지혜로운 이집트인이시군요. 기다려 마지않는 어떤 거룩한 일들에 관한 어르신의 말씀이, 저를 이 황무지에서 만나게 한 일과 그토록 깊이 얽혀 있습니다."], ["What dost thou here?”", "어르신은 여기서 무얼 하고 계십니까?\""]], [["“He is never alone who is where God is—and God is everywhere,”", "\"하느님이 계신 곳에 있는 자는 결코 혼자가 아니라오—그리고 하느님은 어디에나 계시지요.\""], ["Balthasar answered, gravely; “but in the sense of your asking, there is a caravan short way behind us going to Alexandria; and as it is to pass through Jerusalem, I thought best to avail myself of its company as far as the Holy City, whither I am journeying.", "발타사르가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그러나 그대가 묻는 뜻으로 말하자면, 우리 뒤로 멀지 않은 곳에 알렉산드리아로 가는 대상이 하나 있소. 그 대상이 예루살렘을 지나가기에, 내가 가는 성도(聖都)까지는 그 일행에 끼는 것이 좋겠다 싶었소."], ["This morning, however, in discontent with its slow movement—slower because of a Roman cohort in attendance upon it—we rose early, and ventured thus far in advance.", "그런데 오늘 아침, 그 더딘 걸음에—거기 딸린 로마 보병대 때문에 더더욱 더뎠지요—마음이 답답해, 우리는 일찍 일어나 이렇게 앞질러 나섰다오."], ["As to robbers along the way, we are not afraid, for I have here a signet of Sheik Ilderim; against beasts of prey, God is our sufficient trust.”", "길의 도둑들이라면 두렵지 않으니, 내게 일데림 족장의 인장이 있기 때문이오. 들짐승들에 대해서는 하느님이 우리의 넉넉한 의지가 되어 주신다오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur bowed and said, “The good sheik’s signet is a safeguard wherever the wilderness extends, and the lion shall be swift that overtakes this king of his kind.”", "벤허는 고개를 숙이고 말했다. \"그 어진 족장의 인장은 황야가 뻗은 곳이라면 어디서나 안전을 지켜 줄 것입니다. 또한 이 짐승, 그 종족의 왕을 따라잡을 사자라면 무던히도 날쌔야 할 것입니다.\""]], [["He patted the neck of the camel as he spoke.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 낙타의 목을 토닥였다."]], [["“Yet,” said Iras, with a smile which was not lost upon the youth, whose eyes, it must be admitted, had several times turned to her during the interchange of speeches with the elder—“Yet even he would be better if his fast were broken.", "\"그렇지만요,\" 이라스가 미소를 띠며 말했는데, 그 미소는 젊은이의 눈길을 놓치지 않았으니, 솔직히 말해 그의 눈은 노인과 말을 주고받는 동안 몇 번이나 그 여인에게로 향했던 것이다."], ["Kings have hunger and headaches.", "\"그렇지만 이 짐승도 끼니를 채운다면 한결 나아질 거예요."], ["If you be, indeed, the Ben-Hur of whom my father has spoken, and whom it was my pleasure to have known as well, you will be happy, I am sure, to show us some near path to living water, that with its sparkle we may grace a morning’s meal in the Desert.”", "왕에게도 시장기와 두통은 있는 법이지요. 그대가 정녕 제 아버지께서 말씀하신 그 벤허이고, 저 또한 알게 되어 기뻤던 바로 그분이라면, 사막에서 아침상을 차릴 수 있도록 그 반짝이는 물이 솟는 가까운 샘으로 우리를 데려다주시는 일이 분명 그대에게도 기쁨이 되리라 믿어요.\""]], [["Ben-Hur, nothing loath, hastened to answer.", "벤허는 마다하지 않고 서둘러 대답했다."]], [["“Fair Egyptian, I give you sympathy. Can you bear suffering a little longer, we will find the spring you ask for, and I promise that its draught shall be as sweet and cooling as that of the more famous Castalia.", "\"아리따운 이집트 여인이시여, 그 딱한 사정에 마음이 갑니다. 조금만 더 견디실 수 있다면 찾으시는 샘을 우리가 찾아낼 것이고, 그 한 모금이 저 이름난 카스탈리아의 물만큼이나 달고 시원하리라 약속드립니다."], ["With leave, we will make haste.”", "허락하신다면, 어서 서두르지요.\""]], [["“I give you the blessing of the thirsty,”", "\"목마른 자의 축복을 그대에게 드리지요.\""], ["she replied; “and offer you in return a bit of bread from the city ovens, dipped in fresh butter from the dewy meadows of Damascus.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그 답례로는 도시의 화덕에서 구운 빵 한 조각을, 이슬 맺힌 다마스쿠스 목초지에서 짜낸 신선한 버터에 적셔 드리겠어요.\""]], [["“A most rare favor!", "\"더없이 귀한 호의로군요!"], ["Let us go on.”", "자, 갑시다.\""]], [["So saying, Ben-Hur rode forward with the guide, one of the inconveniences of travelling with camels being that it is necessarily an interdiction of polite conversation.", "이렇게 말하며 벤허는 안내인과 더불어 앞으로 말을 몰았다. 낙타와 함께 다니는 일의 불편함 가운데 하나는, 정중한 대화를 어쩔 수 없이 가로막는다는 데 있었다."]], [["Afterwhile the party came to a shallow wady, down which, turning to the right hand, the guide led them.", "얼마 뒤 일행은 얕은 와디에 이르렀고, 안내인은 오른쪽으로 꺾어 그 골을 따라 그들을 이끌었다."], ["The bed of the cut was somewhat soft from recent rains, and quite bold in its descent.", "갈라진 바닥은 얼마 전 내린 비로 다소 무른 데다, 가파르게 내리쏠렸다."], ["Momentarily, however, it widened; and erelong the sides became bluffs ribbed with rocks much scarred by floods rushing to lower depths ahead.", "그러나 이내 폭이 넓어졌고, 머지않아 양옆은 절벽을 이루었으니, 앞쪽 더 낮은 곳으로 쏟아져 내리던 급류에 깊이 패인 바위들이 갈빗대처럼 박혀 있었다."], ["Finally, from a narrow passage, the travellers entered a spreading vale which was very delightful; but come upon suddenly from the yellow, unrelieved, verdureless plain, it had the effect of a freshly discovered Paradise.", "마침내 좁은 통로를 빠져나오자 나그네들은 넓게 펼쳐진 골짜기로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 무척이나 아름다웠다. 그러나 누렇고 단조롭고 푸른 기운이라곤 없는 평원에서 갑작스레 마주친 터라, 마치 새로 발견한 낙원과도 같은 느낌을 주었다."], ["The water-channels winding here and there, definable by crisp white shingling, appeared like threads tangled among islands green with grasses and fringed with reeds.", "여기저기 굽이치는 물줄기는 바삭바삭한 흰 자갈로 분간할 수 있었는데, 풀빛으로 푸르고 갈대로 가장자리를 두른 섬들 사이에 엉킨 실들처럼 보였다."], ["Up from the final depths of the valley of the Jordan some venturous oleanders had crept, and with their large bloom now starred the sunken place.", "요르단 골짜기의 깊디깊은 바닥에서부터 겁 없는 협죽도가 몇 그루 기어올라, 이제 그 큼직한 꽃송이로 이 움푹한 곳을 별처럼 수놓고 있었다."], ["One palm-tree arose in royal assertion.", "종려나무 한 그루가 왕처럼 당당히 솟아 있었다."], ["The bases of the boundary-walls were cloaked with clambering vines, and under a leaning cliff over on the left the mulberry grove had planted itself, proclaiming the spring which the party were seeking.", "경계를 이루는 벼랑의 밑동은 기어오르는 덩굴들로 뒤덮여 있었고, 왼편으로 비스듬히 기운 절벽 아래에는 뽕나무 숲이 자리를 잡아, 일행이 찾던 그 샘이 거기 있음을 알려 주었다."], ["And thither the guide conducted them, careless of whistling partridges and lesser birds of brighter hues roused whirring from the reedy coverts.", "안내인은 그곳으로 그들을 이끌었으니, 갈대 우거진 덤불에서 푸드덕 날아오르며 휘파람 같은 소리를 내는 자고새들이나, 더 고운 빛깔의 작은 새들에는 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["The water started from a crack in the cliff which some loving hand had enlarged into an arched cavity.", "물은 절벽의 갈라진 틈에서 솟아났는데, 어느 사랑 깊은 손길이 그것을 아치 모양의 우묵한 공간으로 넓혀 놓았다."], ["Graven over it in bold Hebraic letters was the word GOD.", "그 위에는 굵직한 히브리 글자로 \"하느님\"이라는 말이 새겨져 있었다."], ["The graver had no doubt drunk there, and tarried many days, and given thanks in that durable form.", "그것을 새긴 이는 분명 거기서 물을 마셨고, 여러 날 머물렀으며, 그 변치 않는 형태로 감사를 드렸으리라."], ["From the arch the stream ran merrily over a flag spotted with bright moss, and leaped into a pool glassy clear; thence it stole away between grassy banks, nursing the trees before it vanished in the thirsty sand.", "아치에서 흘러나온 물줄기는 산뜻한 이끼가 점점이 박힌 넓적한 돌 위를 경쾌하게 지나, 거울처럼 맑은 웅덩이로 뛰어들었다. 거기서 다시 풀 우거진 둑 사이로 슬며시 빠져나가, 목마른 모래 속으로 자취를 감추기 전에 나무들을 길러 냈다."], ["A few narrow paths were noticeable about the margin of the pool; otherwise the space around was untrodden turf, at sight of which the guide was assured of rest free from intrusion by men.", "웅덩이 가장자리로는 좁다란 길이 몇 가닥 보였으나, 그 밖의 둘레는 사람의 발길이 닿지 않은 잔디밭이었다. 그 모습을 본 안내인은 사람의 방해를 받지 않고 쉴 수 있으리라 확신했다."], ["The horses were presently turned loose, and from the kneeling camel the Ethiopian assisted Balthasar and Iras; whereupon the old man, turning his face to the east, crossed his hands reverently upon his breast and prayed.", "말들은 곧 풀어 놓았고, 꿇어앉은 낙타에서 에티오피아인이 발타사르와 이라스를 내려 주었다. 그러자 노인은 동쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 두 손을 경건하게 가슴 위에 포개어 기도했다."]], [["“Bring me a cup,”", "\"잔을 가져오너라.\""], ["Iras said, with some impatience.", "이라스가 다소 조급한 기색으로 말했다."]], [["From the houdah the slave brought her a crystal goblet; then she said to Ben-Hur,", "종이 가마에서 수정 잔을 가져오자, 그녀는 벤허에게 말했다."]], [["“I will be your servant at the fountain.”", "\"제가 샘가에서 그대의 시중을 들겠어요.\""]], [["They walked to the pool together.", "두 사람은 함께 웅덩이로 걸어갔다."], ["He would have dipped the water for her, but she refused his offer, and kneeling, held the cup to be filled by the stream itself; nor yet content, when it was cooled and overrunning, she tendered him the first draught.", "그가 물을 떠 주려 했으나 그녀는 그 호의를 마다하고, 무릎을 꿇은 채 물줄기 그 자체가 잔을 채우도록 받쳐 들었다. 그것으로도 모자라, 물이 차고 시원해져 넘쳐흐르자 그녀는 첫 모금을 그에게 권했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["he said, putting the graceful hand aside, and seeing only the large eyes half hidden beneath the arches of the upraised brows, “be the service mine, I pray.”", "그는 그 우아한 손을 옆으로 밀어내며 말했는데, 치켜올린 눈썹의 활 아래 반쯤 가려진 그 큰 두 눈만이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. \"부디 그 시중은 제 몫이 되게 해 주시지요.\""]], [["She persisted in having her way.", "그녀는 한사코 자기 뜻을 굽히지 않았다."]], [["“In my country, O son of Hur, we have a saying, ‘Better a cupbearer to the fortunate than minister to a king.’”", "\"제 나라에는, 오 후르의 아들이여, 이런 말이 있답니다. '왕을 섬기는 신하가 되느니 행운아의 술잔을 받드는 자가 되라'고요.\""]], [["“Fortunate!”", "\"행운아라니요!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["There were both surprise and inquiry in the tone of his voice and in his look, and she said quickly,", "그 목소리에도 그 표정에도 놀라움과 궁금증이 함께 어려 있었으니, 그녀가 얼른 말했다."]], [["“The gods give us success as a sign by which we may know them on our side.", "\"신들은 우리에게 성공을 내려, 그것을 표징 삼아 그분들이 우리 편임을 알게 하시지요."], ["Were you not winner in the Circus?”", "그대는 원형경기장에서 승리한 분이 아니던가요?\""]], [["His cheeks began to flush.", "그의 두 뺨이 붉어지기 시작했다."]], [["“That was one sign.", "\"그것이 하나의 표징이지요."], ["There is another.", "또 하나가 있어요."], ["In a combat with swords you slew a Roman.”", "칼을 맞댄 싸움에서 그대는 로마인을 베어 죽이셨지요.\""]], [["The flush deepened—not so much for the triumphs themselves as the flattery there was in the thought that she had followed his career with interest.", "홍조가 더욱 짙어졌다—그 승리들 자체 때문이라기보다, 그녀가 자신의 행적을 관심 있게 좇아왔다는 생각에 깃든 그 은근한 추어줌 때문이었다."], ["A moment, and the pleasure was succeeded by a reflection.", "잠시 뒤, 그 기쁨은 한 가닥 생각에 자리를 내주었다."], ["The combat, he knew, was matter of report throughout the East; but the name of the victor had been committed to a very few—Malluch, Ilderim, and Simonides.", "그 싸움은 동방 전역에 소문이 퍼졌음을 그도 알고 있었다. 그러나 승자의 이름은 극히 몇몇 사람—말루크, 일데림, 시모니데스에게만 맡겨져 있었다."], ["Could they have made a confidante of the woman?", "그들이 이 여인에게 속을 털어놓았을까?"], ["So with wonder and gratification he was confused; and seeing it, she arose and said, holding the cup over the pool,", "그리하여 그는 의아함과 흐뭇함이 뒤섞여 어쩔 줄 몰랐다. 그것을 알아챈 그녀가 자리에서 일어나, 잔을 웅덩이 위로 받쳐 들고 말했다."]], [["“O gods of Egypt!", "\"오 이집트의 신들이시여!"], ["I give thanks for a hero discovered—thanks that the victim in the Palace of Idernee was not my king of men. And so, O holy gods, I pour and drink.”", "한 영웅을 찾아낸 것에 감사드립니다—이데르네 궁에서 희생된 자가 제 사내들의 왕이 아니었음에 감사드립니다. 하여, 오 거룩한 신들이시여, 저는 이렇게 따라 마십니다.\""]], [["Part of the contents of the cup she returned to the stream, the rest she drank.", "그녀는 잔에 든 것을 일부는 다시 샘물로 돌려보내고, 나머지는 마셨다."], ["When she took the crystal from her lips, she laughed at him.", "입술에서 수정 잔을 떼며, 그녀는 그를 보고 웃었다."]], [["“O son of Hur, is it a fashion of the very brave to be so easily overcome by a woman?", "\"오, 후르의 아들이여, 한낱 여인에게 이토록 쉽게 무너지는 것이 참으로 용감한 자의 풍습인가요?"], ["Take the cup now, and see if you cannot find a happy word in it for me.”", "자, 이제 잔을 받으세요. 거기서 저를 위한 즐거운 한마디를 찾아낼 수 있는지 보세요.\""]], [["He took the cup, and stooped to refill it.", "그는 잔을 받아 들고, 다시 채우려 몸을 숙였다."]], [["“A son of Israel has no gods whom he can libate,”", "\"이스라엘의 아들에게는 헌주를 바칠 신이 없습니다.\""], ["he said, playing with the water to hide his amazement, now greater than before.", "그는 이렇게 말하며, 전보다 한층 커진 놀라움을 감추려 물장난을 쳤다."], ["What more did the Egyptian know about him? Had she been told of his relations with Simonides?", "이 이집트 여인은 자신에 관해 그밖에 또 무엇을 알고 있는 것일까? 시모니데스와의 관계도 들었을까?"], ["And there was the treaty with Ilderim—had she knowledge of that also?", "게다가 일데림과의 약조가 있었는데, 그것까지 알고 있는 것일까?"], ["He was struck with mistrust.", "그는 불신에 사로잡혔다."], ["Somebody had betrayed his secrets, and they were serious. And, besides, he was going to Jerusalem, just then of all the world the place where such intelligence possessed by an enemy might be most dangerous to him, his associates, and the cause.", "누군가 그의 비밀을 누설한 것이고, 그 비밀은 결코 가벼운 것이 아니었다. 더구나 그는 지금 예루살렘으로 가는 길이었으니, 적이 그런 정보를 쥐고 있을 때 자신과 동지들과 대의에 가장 위험할 수 있는 곳이 바로 그곳이었다."], ["But was she an enemy?", "그러나 그녀가 과연 적일까?"], ["It is well for us that, while writing is slow, thought is instantaneous.", "글쓰기는 더디나 생각은 순간이라는 것이 우리에게는 다행한 일이다."], ["When the cup was fairly cooled, he filled it and arose, saying, with indifference well affected,", "잔이 충분히 식자 그는 잔을 채우고 일어서며, 짐짓 무심한 척 말했다."]], [["“Most fair, were I an Egyptian or a Greek or a Roman, I would say”—he raised the goblet overhead as he spoke—“O ye better gods!", "\"더없이 아리따운 분이여, 제가 이집트인이거나 그리스인이거나 로마인이었다면 이렇게 말했겠지요.\" 그는 말하면서 잔을 머리 위로 들어 올렸다. \"오, 그대들 선한 신들이여!"], ["I give thanks that there are yet left to the world, despite its wrongs and sufferings, the charm of beauty and the solace of love, and I drink to her who best represents them—to Iras, loveliest of the daughters of the Nile!”", "이 세상이 온갖 부조리와 고통을 겪으면서도, 아직 아름다움의 매력과 사랑의 위안이 남아 있음에 감사드리나이다. 그리하여 나는 그것들을 가장 잘 드러내는 이를 위해 마시노라. 나일강의 딸들 가운데 가장 사랑스러운 이라스를 위하여!\""]], [["She laid her hand softly upon his shoulder.", "그녀는 그의 어깨 위에 살며시 손을 얹었다."]], [["“You have offended against the law.", "\"당신은 율법을 어겼어요."], ["The gods you have drunk to are false gods.", "당신이 위해 마신 신들은 거짓 신들이에요."], ["Why shall I not tell the rabbis on you?”", "제가 랍비들에게 당신을 일러바치면 어떡하시겠어요?\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["he replied, laughing, “that is very little to tell for one who knows so much else that is really important.”", "그는 웃으며 대답했다. \"정말로 중요한 다른 일들을 그토록 많이 아시는 분에게는, 그것쯤은 일러바칠 거리도 못 되지요.\""]], [["“I will go further—I will go to the little Jewess who makes the roses grow and the shadows flame in the house of the great merchant over in Antioch.", "\"한 걸음 더 나아가겠어요. 안티오크 건너편, 그 위대한 상인의 집에서 장미를 피우고 그림자를 불타오르게 하는 작은 유대인 처녀에게 가겠어요."], ["To the rabbis I will accuse you of impenitence; to her—”", "랍비들에게는 당신이 뉘우칠 줄 모른다고 고발하고, 그녀에게는……\""]], [["“Well, to her?”", "\"그래, 그녀에게는?\""]], [["“I will repeat what you have said to me under the lifted cup, with the gods for witnesses.”", "\"잔을 들어 올린 채, 신들을 증인 삼아 저에게 하신 말씀을 그대로 되풀이해 주겠어요.\""]], [["He was still a moment, as if waiting for the Egyptian to go on.", "그는 잠시 가만히 있었다. 마치 이집트 여인이 말을 잇기를 기다리는 듯했다."], ["With quickened fancy he saw Esther at her father’s side listening to the despatches he had forwarded—sometimes reading them.", "빨라진 상상 속에서 그는 아버지 곁에서 자신이 보낸 전갈에 귀 기울이는 에스더를, 때로는 그것을 읽는 에스더를 떠올렸다."], ["In her presence he had told Simonides the story of the affair in the Palace of Idernee.", "그녀가 있는 자리에서 그는 시모니데스에게 이데르네 궁에서 벌어진 사건의 전말을 들려준 적이 있었다."], ["She and Iras were acquainted; this one was shrewd and worldly; the other was simple and affectionate, and therefore easily won.", "그녀와 이라스는 서로 아는 사이였다. 이쪽은 영리하고 세상물정에 밝았고, 저쪽은 순박하고 정이 많아 그만큼 쉽게 마음을 얻을 수 있었다."], ["Simonides could not have broken faith—nor Ilderim—for if not held by honor, there was no one, unless it might be himself, to whom the consequences of exposure were more serious and certain.", "시모니데스가 신의를 저버렸을 리 없고, 일데림도 마찬가지였다. 명예에 매여 있지 않더라도, 비밀이 드러났을 때의 결과가 더 무겁고 확실하게 닥칠 사람은 자신 말고는 아무도 없었으니 말이다."], ["Could Esther have been the Egyptian’s informant?", "에스더가 이집트 여인에게 알려준 사람일 수 있을까?"], ["He did not accuse her; yet a suspicion was sown with the thought, and suspicions, as we all know, are weeds of the mind which grow of themselves, and most rapidly when least wanted.", "그는 그녀를 탓하지는 않았다. 그러나 그 생각과 더불어 의심의 씨가 뿌려졌고, 누구나 알다시피 의심이란 마음속에서 절로 자라나는 잡초이며, 가장 원치 않을 때 가장 빠르게 자라는 법이다."], ["Before he could answer the allusion to the little Jewess, Balthasar came to the pool.", "그가 작은 유대인 처녀에 대한 암시에 미처 대답하기도 전에, 발타사르가 샘가로 다가왔다."]], [["“We are greatly indebted to you, son of Hur,”", "\"후르의 아들이여, 우리는 그대에게 큰 신세를 졌소.\""], ["he said, in his grave manner.", "그는 진중한 태도로 말했다."], ["“This vale is very beautiful; the grass, the trees, the shade, invite us to stay and rest, and the spring here has the sparkle of diamonds in motion, and sings to me of a loving God.", "\"이 골짜기는 참으로 아름답구려. 풀과 나무와 그늘이 우리에게 머물러 쉬어 가라 권하고, 여기 이 샘은 움직이는 다이아몬드처럼 반짝이며 사랑이 넘치는 하느님을 내게 노래해 주는구려."], ["It is not enough to thank you for the enjoyment we find; come sit with us, and taste our bread.”", "우리가 누리는 이 즐거움에 대해 그저 감사하다는 말만으로는 부족하오. 와서 우리와 함께 앉아 우리의 빵을 맛보시오.\""]], [["“Suffer me first to serve you.”", "\"먼저 제가 모시게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["With that Ben-Hur filled the goblet, and gave it to Balthasar, who lifted his eyes in thanksgiving.", "그러면서 벤허는 잔을 채워 발타사르에게 건넸고, 발타사르는 감사의 뜻으로 눈을 들어 올렸다."]], [["Immediately the slave brought napkins; and after laving their hands and drying them, the three seated themselves in Eastern style under the tent which years before had served the Wise Men at the meeting in the Desert.", "곧 종이 수건을 가져왔다. 세 사람은 손을 씻고 닦은 뒤, 동방식으로 천막 아래 자리를 잡았으니, 그것은 여러 해 전 사막에서 만났던 동방박사들을 받들어 모셨던 바로 그 천막이었다."], ["And they ate heartily of the good things taken from the camel’s pack.", "그리고 그들은 낙타의 짐에서 꺼낸 좋은 음식들을 마음껏 먹었다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["The tent was cosily pitched beneath a tree where the gurgle of the stream was constantly in ear.", "천막은 시냇물 소리가 끊임없이 귀에 닿는 나무 아래 아늑하게 쳐져 있었다."], ["Overhead the broad leaves hung motionless on their stems; the delicate reed-stalks off in the pearly haze stood up arrowy-straight; occasionally a home-returning bee shot humming athwart the shade, and a partridge creeping from the sedge drank, whistled to his mate, and ran away.", "머리 위에서는 넓은 잎사귀들이 줄기에 매달린 채 미동도 없었고, 진줏빛 아지랑이 너머 가녀린 갈대 줄기들은 화살처럼 곧게 서 있었다. 이따금 집으로 돌아가는 벌 한 마리가 윙윙거리며 그늘을 가로질러 쏜살같이 지나갔고, 사초 사이에서 기어 나온 자고새 한 마리가 물을 마시고는 짝을 향해 휘파람을 불고 달아났다."], ["The restfulness of the vale, the freshness of the air, the garden beauty, the Sabbath stillness, seemed to have affected the spirits of the elder Egyptian; his voice, gestures, and whole manner were unusually gentle; and often as he bent his eyes upon Ben-Hur conversing with Iras, they softened with pity.", "골짜기의 안온함, 공기의 신선함, 정원의 아름다움, 안식일 같은 고요—이 모든 것이 나이 든 이집트인의 마음을 움직인 듯했다. 그의 목소리며 몸짓이며 태도가 유난히 부드러웠다. 그리고 이라스와 이야기를 나누는 벤허에게 눈길을 보낼 때면, 그 눈빛은 자주 연민으로 누그러졌다."]], [["“When we overtook you, son of Hur,”", "\"우리가 그대를 따라잡았을 때, 후르의 아들이여,\""], ["he said, at the conclusion of the repast, “it seemed your face was also turned towards Jerusalem.", "식사가 끝날 무렵 그가 말했다. \"그대의 얼굴 또한 예루살렘을 향하고 있는 듯했네."], ["May I ask, without offence, if you are going so far?”", "무례가 아니라면, 그토록 먼 곳까지 가는 길인지 물어도 되겠나?\""]], [["“I am going to the Holy City.”", "\"성도(聖都)로 가는 길입니다.\""]], [["“For the great need I have to spare myself prolonged toil, I will further ask you, Is there a shorter road than that by Rabbath-Ammon?”", "\"긴 여정의 수고를 덜어야 할 큰 사정이 있어 한 가지 더 묻겠네. 랍밧암몬을 거치는 길보다 더 가까운 길이 있는가?\""]], [["“A rougher route, but shorter, lies by Gerasa and Rabbath-Gilead.", "\"거친 길이긴 하나 더 가까운 길이 게라사와 랍밧길르앗을 지나 있습니다."], ["It is the one I design taking.”", "제가 가려는 길이 바로 그 길입니다.\""]], [["“I am impatient,”", "\"마음이 조급하다네,\""], ["said Balthasar.", "발타사르가 말했다."], ["“Latterly my sleep has been visited by dreams—or rather by the same dream in repetition.", "\"요사이 잠을 자면 꿈이 찾아드네—아니, 같은 꿈이 거듭 찾아든다고 해야겠지."], ["A voice—it is nothing more—comes and tells me, ‘Haste—arise!", "어떤 목소리가—그저 목소리일 뿐이네—찾아와 이르더군. '서둘러라, 일어나라!"], ["He whom thou hast so long awaited is at hand.’”", "네가 그토록 오래 기다려 온 분이 가까이 와 계신다.'\""]], [["“You mean he that is to be King of the Jews?”", "\"유대인의 왕이 되실 분을 말씀하시는 겁니까?\""], ["Ben-Hur asked, gazing at the Egyptian in wonder.", "벤허가 놀라움에 차서 이집트인을 바라보며 물었다. \"바로 그러하네.\""]], [["“Even so.” “Then you have heard nothing of him?”", "\"그렇다면 그분에 관해 아무 소식도 듣지 못하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Nothing, except the words of the voice in the dream.”", "\"아무것도 듣지 못했네. 꿈속 목소리의 말 외에는.\""]], [["“Here, then, are tidings to make you glad as they made me.”", "\"그렇다면 여기, 제가 그러했듯 어른께도 기쁨을 안겨 드릴 소식이 있습니다.\""]], [["From his gown Ben-Hur drew the letter received from Malluch.", "벤허는 자기 옷에서 말루크에게서 받은 편지를 꺼냈다."], ["The hand the Egyptian held out trembled violently.", "이집트인이 내민 손이 심하게 떨렸다."], ["He read aloud, and as he read his feelings increased; the limp veins in his neck swelled and throbbed.", "그는 소리 내어 읽었고, 읽어 가면서 감정이 북받쳤다. 목의 늘어진 핏줄이 부풀어 올라 고동쳤다."], ["At the conclusion he raised his suffused eyes in thanksgiving and prayer.", "다 읽고 나자 그는 눈물 어린 눈을 들어 감사와 기도를 올렸다."], ["He asked no questions, yet had no doubts.", "그는 아무것도 묻지 않았으나, 아무런 의심도 없었다."]], [["“Thou hast been very good to me, O God,” he said.", "\"주여, 당신은 제게 참으로 자비로우셨나이다,\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Give me, I pray thee, to see the Saviour again, and worship him, and thy servant will be ready to go in peace.”", "\"비오니, 구주를 다시 뵙고 그분을 경배하게 하소서. 그리하면 당신의 종은 평안히 떠날 채비가 되겠나이다.\""]], [["The words, the manner, the singular personality of the simple prayer, touched Ben-Hur with a sensation new and abiding.", "그 말, 그 태도, 그 소박한 기도의 남다른 인격이 벤허에게 새롭고도 오래 남을 느낌을 안겨 주었다."], ["God never seemed so actual and so near by; it was as if he were there bending over them or sitting at their side—a Friend whose favors were to be had by the most unceremonious asking—a Father to whom all his children were alike in love—Father, not more of the Jew than of the Gentile—the Universal Father, who needed no intermediates, no rabbis, no priests, no teachers.", "하느님이 이토록 실재하고 가까이 계신 듯 여겨진 적은 일찍이 없었다. 마치 그분이 그곳에서 그들 위로 몸을 굽히고 계시거나 곁에 앉아 계신 것만 같았다—더없이 격식 없는 청만으로도 은혜를 베푸시는 벗, 모든 자식을 똑같이 사랑하시는 아버지, 유대인의 아버지인 동시에 이방인의 아버지인, 중개자도 랍비도 사제도 교사도 필요로 하지 않으시는 만민의 아버지."], ["The idea that such a God might send mankind a Saviour instead of a king appeared to Ben-Hur in a light not merely new, but so plain that he could almost discern both the greater want of such a gift and its greater consistency with the nature of such a Deity.", "그런 하느님이 인류에게 왕이 아니라 구주를 보내실지도 모른다는 생각이, 단지 새로울 뿐 아니라 너무도 분명하게 벤허에게 다가왔다. 그래서 그는 그러한 선물에 대한 더 큰 갈망과, 그러한 신성에 그것이 더 잘 어울린다는 사실을 거의 또렷이 분별할 수 있을 정도였다."], ["So he could not resist asking,", "그리하여 그는 묻지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Now that he has come, O Balthasar, you still think he is to be a Saviour, and not a king?”", "\"이제 그분이 오셨는데도, 발타사르 어른, 어른께서는 여전히 그분이 왕이 아니라 구주가 되실 거라고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["Balthasar gave him a look thoughtful as it was tender.", "발타사르는 다정하면서도 사려 깊은 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“How shall I understand you?” he asked, in return.", "\"그대의 말을 어찌 알아들어야 할까?\" 그가 되물었다."], ["“The Spirit, which was the Star that was my guide of old, has not appeared to me since I met you in the tent of the good sheik; that is to say, I have not seen or heard it as formerly.", "\"옛적 나의 인도자였던 별, 곧 그 영(靈)은 내가 그 어진 족장의 천막에서 그대를 만난 이래로 다시는 내게 나타나지 않았네. 다시 말해, 예전처럼 그것을 보거나 듣지 못했다는 말일세."], ["I believe the voice that spoke to me in my dreams was it; but other than that I have no revelation.”", "꿈속에서 내게 이른 그 목소리가 바로 그것이라고 나는 믿네. 허나 그 외에는 어떤 계시도 받지 못했네.\""]], [["“I will recall the difference between us,”", "\"우리 사이의 차이를 다시 짚어 보겠습니다,\""], ["said Ben-Hur, with deference.", "벤허가 정중하게 말했다."], ["“You were of opinion that he would be a king, but not as Cæsar is; you thought his sovereignty would be spiritual, not of the world.”", "\"어른께서는 그분이 왕이 되시되 카이사르 같은 왕은 아닐 거라고 보셨지요. 그 통치권이 세속의 것이 아니라 영적인 것이리라 여기셨습니다.\""]], [["“Oh yes,”", "\"오, 그렇다네,\""], ["the Egyptian answered; “and I am of the same opinion now.", "이집트인이 대답했다. \"그리고 지금도 같은 생각일세."], ["I see the divergence in our faith.", "우리 믿음이 어디서 갈라지는지 나는 보네."], ["You are going to meet a king of men, I a Saviour of souls.”", "그대는 사람들의 왕을 만나러 가는 것이고, 나는 영혼들의 구주를 만나러 가는 것이지.\""]], [["He paused with the look often seen when people are struggling, with introverted effort, to disentangle a thought which is either too high for quick discernment or too subtle for simple expression.", "그는 사람들이 너무 높아 얼른 분별하기 어렵거나 너무 미묘해 단순히 표현하기 어려운 생각을 안으로 더듬어 풀어내려 애쓸 때 흔히 보이는 그런 표정으로 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Let me try, O son of Hur,”", "\"내가 한번 애써 보겠네, 후르의 아들이여,\""], ["he said, directly, “and help you to a clear understanding of my belief; then it may be, seeing how the spiritual kingdom I expect him to set up can be more excellent in every sense than anything of mere Cæsarean splendor, you will better understand the reason of the interest I take in the mysterious person we are going to welcome.", "그가 곧바로 말했다. \"그대가 내 믿음을 분명히 깨닫도록 도와주겠네. 그러면 내가 그분이 세우시리라 기대하는 영적 왕국이 어떤 의미로 보아도 한낱 카이사르식 화려함보다 더 뛰어날 수 있음을 알게 되어, 우리가 맞이하러 가는 그 신비로운 분에게 내가 품은 관심의 까닭을 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이네."]], [["“I cannot tell you when the idea of a Soul in every man had its origin.", "\"사람마다 영혼이 깃들어 있다는 생각이 언제 비롯되었는지는 나도 말할 수 없네."], ["Most likely the first parents brought it with them out of the garden in which they had their first dwelling. We all do know, however, that it has never perished entirely out of mind.", "아마도 최초의 부모가 처음 거하던 동산에서 그것을 지니고 나왔을 게야. 그러나 그 생각이 사람의 마음에서 완전히 사라진 적은 한 번도 없다는 것을 우리 모두 알고 있네."], ["By some peoples it was lost, but not by all; in some ages it dulled and faded, in others it was overwhelmed with doubts; but, in great goodness, God kept sending us at intervals mighty intellects to argue it back to faith and hope.", "어떤 민족은 그것을 잃었으나 모든 민족이 그런 것은 아니었네. 어떤 시대에는 그것이 흐려지고 바래었으며, 어떤 시대에는 의심에 휩싸였네. 그러나 큰 자비로, 하느님은 때마다 우리에게 위대한 지성을 보내시어 그것을 논하여 다시 믿음과 소망으로 이끄셨네."]], [["“Why should there be a Soul in every man?", "\"어찌하여 사람마다 영혼이 깃들어 있어야 하는가?"], ["Look, O son of Hur—for one moment look at the necessity of such a device.", "보게나, 후르의 아들이여—그러한 장치가 어찌하여 필요한지 잠시만 들여다보게."], ["To lie down and die, and be no more—no more forever—time never was when man wished for such an end; nor has the man ever been who did not in his heart promise himself something better.", "누워 죽어 더는 존재하지 않는 것—영영 사라지는 것—사람이 그런 종말을 바란 때는 일찍이 없었네. 또한 마음속으로 더 나은 무언가를 스스로 약속하지 않은 사람도 없었네. 여러 민족의 기념비는 모두 죽음 뒤의 허무에 대한 항거일세."], ["The monuments of the nations are all protests against nothingness after death; so are statues and inscriptions; so is history.", "조각상과 비문도 그러하고, 역사 또한 그러하네. 우리 이집트의 가장 위대한 왕은 단단한 바위 언덕을 깎아 자기 형상을 만들게 했네."], ["The greatest of our Egyptian kings had his effigy cut-out of a hill of solid rock.", "날마다 그는 수많은 전차를 거느리고 그 작업을 보러 갔지."], ["Day after day he went with a host in chariots to see the work; at last it was finished, never effigy so grand, so enduring: it looked like him—the features were his, faithful even in expression.", "마침내 그것이 완성되었으니, 그토록 웅장하고 그토록 오래갈 형상은 일찍이 없었네. 그것은 그를 빼닮았고—이목구비가 그의 것이었으며, 표정마저 충실했네. 자, 그 자랑스러운 순간에 그가 이렇게 말했으리라고 생각해 볼 수 있지 않겠나."], ["Now may we not think of him saying in that moment of pride, ‘Let Death come; there is an after-life for me!’ He had his wish.", "'죽음이여 오라. 내게는 사후의 삶이 있도다!' 그는 소원을 이루었네."], ["The statue is there yet.", "그 조각상은 지금도 그곳에 있으니."]], [["“But what is the after-life he thus secured?", "\"그러나 그가 그렇게 얻은 사후의 삶이란 무엇인가?"], ["Only a recollection by men—a glory unsubstantial as moonshine on the brow of the great bust; a story in stone—nothing more.", "그저 사람들의 기억일 뿐—그 거대한 흉상의 이마에 비친 달빛처럼 실체 없는 영광일 뿐. 돌에 새겨진 이야기—그 이상은 아무것도 아니네."], ["Meantime what has become of the king?", "그동안 왕 자신은 어찌 되었는가?"], ["There is an embalmed body up in the royal tombs which once was his—an effigy not so fair to look at as the other out in the Desert.", "왕릉에는 한때 그의 것이었던, 방부 처리된 몸뚱이 하나가 있네—사막에 있는 저 형상만큼 보기 좋지도 않은 형상이지."], ["But where, O son of Hur, where is the king himself?", "그러나, 후르의 아들이여, 왕 자신은 어디에 있는가?"], ["Is he fallen into nothingness?", "그는 허무 속으로 떨어졌는가?"], ["Two thousand years have gone since he was a man alive as you and I are.", "그가 그대와 나처럼 살아 있는 사람이었던 때로부터 이천 년이 흘렀네."], ["Was his last breath the end of him?", "그의 마지막 숨이 곧 그의 끝이었는가?"]], [["“To say yes would be to accuse God; let us rather accept his better plan of attaining life after death for us—actual life, I mean—the something more than a place in mortal memory; life with going and coming, with sensation, with knowledge, with power and all appreciation; life eternal in term though it may be with changes of condition.", "\"그렇다고 말하는 것은 하느님을 탓하는 일이 되리니, 차라리 우리에게 사후의 삶—내 말은 실제의 삶—을 얻게 하시려는 그분의 더 나은 계획을 받아들이세. 죽을 자들의 기억 속 한자리 그 이상의 것, 오감과 가고 옴이 있고, 지식이 있고, 능력과 온갖 향유가 있는 삶, 비록 상태의 변화는 있을지언정 그 기한은 영원한 삶 말일세."]], [["“Ask you what God’s plan is?", "\"하느님의 계획이 무엇이냐고 묻는가?"], ["The gift of a Soul to each of us at birth, with this simple law—there shall be no immortality except through the Soul.", "태어날 때 우리 각자에게 영혼을 주시되, 이 한 가지 단순한 법을 두신 것일세—영혼을 통하지 않고는 어떤 불멸도 없으리라는 법."], ["In that law see the necessity of which I spoke.", "그 법 안에 내가 말한 그 필요가 있네."]], [["“Let us turn from the necessity now.", "\"이제 그 필요에서 눈을 돌려 보세."], ["A word as to the pleasure there is in the thought of a Soul in each of us.", "우리 각자에게 영혼이 깃들어 있다는 생각 속에 있는 기쁨에 관해 한마디 하겠네."], ["In the first place, it robs death of its terrors by making dying a change for the better, and burial but the planting of a seed from which there will spring a new life.", "우선 그것은 죽음에서 그 공포를 앗아 가네. 죽는 것을 더 나은 쪽으로의 변화로, 묻히는 것을 새 생명이 움터 나올 씨앗을 심는 일로 만들기 때문이지."], ["In the next place, behold me as I am—weak, weary, old, shrunken in body, and graceless; look at my wrinkled face, think of my failing senses, listen to my shrilled voice. Ah!", "다음으로, 지금 내 모습을 보게나—약하고, 지치고, 늙고, 몸은 쪼그라들었으며, 볼품없네. 내 주름진 얼굴을 보게, 쇠해 가는 내 감각을 생각해 보게, 내 새된 목소리에 귀 기울여 보게. 아!"], ["what happiness to me in the promise that when the tomb opens, as soon it will, to receive the worn-out husk I call myself, the now viewless doors of the universe, which is but the palace of God, will swing wide ajar to receive me, a liberated immortal Soul!", "무덤이 곧 열려, 내가 나라고 부르는 이 닳아 버린 껍질을 받아들일 때, 곧 하느님의 궁전에 다름 아닌 우주의, 지금은 보이지 않는 문이 활짝 열려 해방된 불멸의 영혼인 나를 맞아들이리라는 약속, 그것이 내게 주는 행복이 어떠하겠는가!"]], [["“I would I could tell the ecstasy there must be in that life to come!", "\"다가올 그 삶 속에 있을 황홀을 내가 다 말할 수 있다면 좋으련만!"], ["Do not say I know nothing about it.", "내가 그에 관해 아무것도 모른다고는 말하지 말게."], ["This much I know, and it is enough for me—the being a Soul implies conditions of divine superiority.", "이만큼은 내가 아네. 그리고 내게는 그것으로 충분하네—영혼이 된다는 것은 신성한 우월함의 조건을 함의한다는 것을."], ["In such a being there is no dust, nor any gross thing; it must be finer than air, more impalpable than light, purer than essence—it is life in absolute purity.", "그러한 존재 안에는 티끌 한 점도, 어떤 거친 것도 없네. 그것은 공기보다 더 미세하고, 빛보다 더 만질 수 없으며, 정수보다 더 순수할 것일세—그것은 더없이 순수한 삶이라네."]], [["“What now, O son of Hur? Knowing so much, shall I dispute with myself or you about the unnecessaries—about the form of my soul?", "\"그렇다면, 후르의 아들이여, 이만큼 알고 있으면서 굳이 그 불필요한 것들을 두고 나 자신과, 또는 그대와 다투어야 하겠는가—내 영혼의 형태를 두고?"], ["Or where it is to abide?", "또는 그것이 어디에 머물 것인지를 두고?"], ["Or whether it eats and drinks?", "또는 그것이 먹고 마시는지를 두고?"], ["Or is winged, or wears this or that? No. It is more becoming to trust in God.", "또는 날개가 있는지, 이런저런 것을 걸치는지를 두고? 아닐세. 하느님을 믿는 것이 더 마땅하네."], ["The beautiful in this world is all from his hand declaring the perfection of taste; he is the author of all form; he clothes the lily, he colors the rose, he distils the dew-drop, he makes the music of nature; in a word, he organized us for this life, and imposed its conditions; and they are such guaranty to me that, trustful as a little child, I leave to him the organization of my Soul, and every arrangement for the life after death.", "이 세상의 아름다운 것은 모두 그분의 손에서 나와 그 취향의 완전함을 드러내네. 그분은 모든 형태의 창조자이시며, 백합을 입히시고, 장미를 물들이시고, 이슬방울을 빚으시고, 자연의 음악을 지으시네. 한마디로, 그분은 이 삶을 위해 우리를 빚으시고 그 조건을 정하셨으니, 그것이 내게는 너무도 든든한 보증이라, 나는 어린아이처럼 믿음을 품고 내 영혼의 빚음과 사후의 삶을 위한 온갖 마련을 그분께 맡기네."], ["I know he loves me.”", "나는 그분이 나를 사랑하심을 아네.\""]], [["The good man stopped and drank, and the hand carrying the cup to his lips trembled; and both Iras and Ben-Hur shared his emotion and remained silent.", "이 어진 사람은 말을 멈추고 물을 마셨는데, 잔을 입으로 가져가는 손이 떨렸다. 이라스와 벤허도 그의 감동을 함께 나누며 잠자코 있었다."], ["Upon the latter a light was breaking.", "벤허에게는 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐 들고 있었다."], ["He was beginning to see, as never before, that there might be a spiritual kingdom of more import to men than any earthly empire; and that after all a Saviour would indeed be a more godly gift than the greatest king.", "그는 일찍이 없었던 방식으로 깨닫기 시작했다. 어떤 지상의 제국보다도 사람들에게 더 중대한 영적 왕국이 있을 수 있으며, 결국 구주야말로 가장 위대한 왕보다 참으로 더 신성한 선물이리라는 것을."]], [["“I might ask you now,”", "\"이제 그대에게 물어볼 수도 있겠네,\""], ["said Balthasar, continuing, “whether this human life, so troubled and brief, is preferable to the perfect and everlasting life designed for the Soul?", "발타사르가 말을 이었다. \"이토록 고달프고 짧은 인간의 삶이, 영혼을 위해 마련된 완전하고 영원한 삶보다 더 나은가를 말일세."], ["But take the question, and think of it for yourself, formulating thus: Supposing both to be equally happy, is one hour more desirable than one year?", "허나 그 물음을 받아 들고, 이렇게 정리하여 스스로 생각해 보게. 두 삶이 똑같이 행복하다고 가정할 때, 한 시간이 한 해보다 더 바람직한가?"], ["From that then advance to the final inquiry, what are threescore and ten years on earth to all eternity with God?", "거기서 다시 마지막 물음으로 나아가 보게. 지상의 일흔 해가 하느님과 함께하는 영원에 비하면 무엇이겠는가?"], ["By-and-by, son of Hur, thinking in such manner, you will be filled with the meaning of the fact I present you next, to me the most amazing of all events, and in its effects the most sorrowful; it is that the very idea of life as a Soul is a light almost gone out in the world.", "머지않아, 후르의 아들이여, 그런 식으로 생각하다 보면, 내가 다음으로 제시하는 사실의 의미로 그대 마음이 가득 차게 될 걸세. 그것은 내게 모든 사건 가운데 가장 놀라운 것이요, 그 영향에서 가장 슬픈 것이네. 곧 영혼으로서의 삶이라는 생각 자체가 세상에서 거의 꺼져 버린 빛이라는 사실 말일세."], ["Here and there, to be sure, a philosopher may be found who will talk to you of a Soul, likening it to a principle; but because philosophers take nothing upon faith, they will not go the length of admitting a Soul to be a being, and on that account its purpose is compressed darkness to them.", "물론 여기저기서, 영혼을 두고 그것을 하나의 원리에 빗대어 그대에게 이야기할 철학자를 더러 만날 수도 있겠지. 그러나 철학자들은 어떤 것도 믿음으로 받아들이지 않기에, 영혼을 하나의 존재라고 인정하는 데까지는 나아가지 않을 것이네. 그런 까닭에 그들에게는 영혼의 목적이 빽빽한 어둠일 뿐이지."]], [["“Everything animate has a mind measurable by its wants. Is there to you no meaning in the singularity that power in full degree to speculate upon the future was given to man alone?", "\"숨 쉬는 모든 것은 제 필요만큼 헤아릴 수 있는 마음을 지니고 있네. 미래를 온전히 헤아릴 능력이 오직 사람에게만 주어졌다는 그 남다름이 그대에게는 아무 의미도 없는가?"], ["By the sign as I see it, God meant to make us know ourselves created for another and a better life, such being in fact the greatest need of our nature.", "내가 보기에 그 표징으로, 하느님은 우리가 또 다른 더 나은 삶을 위해 창조되었음을 알게 하려 하셨네. 사실 그것이야말로 우리 본성의 가장 큰 필요이기 때문이지."], ["But, alas!", "허나, 아아!"], ["into what a habit the nations have fallen!", "여러 민족이 얼마나 한심한 버릇에 빠져들었는가!"], ["They live for the day, as if the present were the all in all, and go about saying, ‘There is no to-morrow after death; or if there be, since we know nothing about it, be it a care unto itself.’ So when Death calls them, ‘Come,’ they may not enter into enjoyment of the glorious after-life because of their unfitness.", "그들은 마치 현재가 전부인 양 하루하루만 살며, 이렇게 떠들고 다니네. '죽음 뒤에 내일은 없다. 설령 있다 한들 우리가 그에 관해 아무것도 모르니, 그것은 그것대로 두라.'"], ["That is to say, the ultimate happiness of man was everlasting life in the society of God.", "그리하여 죽음이 그들에게 '오라'고 부를 때, 그들은 그 영광스러운 사후의 삶을 누리는 데 들지 못하네."], ["Alas, O son of Hur, that I should say it!", "거기에 합당하지 못한 까닭이지."], ["but as well yon sleeping camel constant in such society as the holiest priests this day serving the highest altars in the most renowned temples.", "다시 말해, 사람의 궁극의 행복은 하느님과 더불어 사는 영원한 삶이었네. 아아, 후르의 아들이여, 이런 말을 해야 하다니! 허나 오늘날 가장 이름난 신전에서 가장 높은 제단을 섬기는 더없이 거룩한 사제들이나, 저기 잠든 낙타나, 그러한 사귐에 한결같기는 매한가지일세."], ["So much are men given to this lower earthly life!", "사람들은 그토록 이 낮은 지상의 삶에 매여 있네!"], ["So nearly have they forgotten that other which is to come!", "다가올 저 삶을 그토록 거의 다 잊어버렸네!"]], [["“See now, I pray you, that which is to be saved to us.", "\"이제, 청컨대, 우리에게 구원되어야 할 것이 무엇인지 보게나."]], [["“For my part, speaking with the holiness of truth, I would not give one hour of life as a Soul for a thousand years of life as a man.”", "\"나로 말하자면, 진리의 거룩함을 걸고 말하건대, 영혼으로서의 한 시간을 사람으로서의 천 년과도 바꾸지 않겠네.\""]], [["Here the Egyptian seemed to become unconscious of companionship and fall away into abstraction.", "여기서 이집트인은 함께 있는 이들을 잊은 듯, 깊은 사색 속으로 빠져드는 것 같았다."]], [["“This life has its problems,”", "\"이 삶에도 문제들이 있네,\""], ["he said, “and there are men who spend their days trying to solve them; but what are they to the problems of the hereafter?", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 그것들을 풀어 보려 평생을 바치는 사람들도 있지. 허나 그것이 내세의 문제들에 비하면 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What is there like knowing God?", "하느님을 아는 것에 견줄 만한 것이 어디 있겠나?"], ["Not a scroll of the mysteries, but the mysteries themselves would for that hour at least lie before me revealed; even the innermost and most awful—the power which now we shrink from thought of—which rimmed the void with shores, and lighted the darkness, and out of nothing appointed the universe.", "신비의 두루마리가 아니라 신비 그 자체가, 적어도 그 한 시간만은 내 앞에 드러나 놓이게 될 것이네. 가장 깊고 가장 두려운 것마저도—지금 우리가 생각만으로도 움츠러드는 그 권능, 곧 허공에 기슭을 둘러치고, 어둠을 밝히고, 무에서 우주를 지으신 그 권능마저도."], ["All places would be opened.", "모든 곳이 열리리라."], ["I would be filled with divine knowledge; I would see all glories, taste all delights; I would revel in being.", "나는 신성한 지식으로 가득 차고, 온갖 영광을 보며, 온갖 기쁨을 맛보고, 존재함을 마음껏 누릴 것이네."], ["And if, at the end of the hour, it should please God to tell me, ‘I take thee into my service forever,’ the furthest limit of desire would be passed; after which the attainable ambitions of life, and its joys of whatever kind, would not be so much as the tinkling of little bells.”", "그리고 만일 그 한 시간이 끝날 무렵, 하느님이 기꺼이 내게 '내가 너를 영원히 내 종으로 삼겠노라' 하고 이르신다면, 욕망의 가장 먼 끝마저 넘어서게 되리니, 그 뒤로는 삶에서 이룰 수 있는 야망도, 어떤 종류의 즐거움도, 자그마한 방울들이 짤랑이는 소리만도 못하게 될 것이네.\""]], [["Balthasar paused as if to recover from very ecstasy of feeling; and to Ben-Hur it seemed the speech had been the delivery of a Soul speaking for itself.", "발타사르는 감정의 황홀에서 헤어나려는 듯 말을 멈추었다. 벤허에게는 그 말이 한 영혼이 스스로를 대변하여 전한 것처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“I pray pardon, son of Hur,”", "\"부디 용서하게, 후르의 아들이여,\""], ["the good man continued, with a bow the gravity of which was relieved by the tender look that followed it, “I meant to leave the life of a Soul, its conditions, pleasures, superiority, to your own reflection and finding out.", "어진 사람이 말을 이으며, 그 무게를 뒤따르는 다정한 눈빛으로 누그러뜨린 채 고개를 숙였다. \"나는 영혼의 삶과 그 조건, 그 기쁨, 그 우월함을 그대 자신의 성찰과 깨달음에 맡길 작정이었네."], ["The joy of the thought has betrayed me into much speech.", "그 생각이 주는 기쁨에 그만 끌려 말이 너무 많아졌구려."], ["I set out to show, though ever so faintly, the reason of my faith.", "나는 내 믿음의 까닭을, 아무리 희미할지라도, 보여 주려 했네."], ["It grieves me that words are so weak.", "말이 이토록 약한 것이 안타깝네."], ["But help yourself to truth.", "허나 진리는 그대 스스로 거두게나."], ["Consider first the excellence of the existence which was reserved for us after death, and give heed to the feelings and impulses the thought is sure to awaken in you—heed them, I say, because they are your own Soul astir, doing what it can to urge you in the right way.", "먼저 죽음 뒤에 우리를 위해 마련된 그 존재의 빼어남을 헤아려 보고, 그 생각이 그대 안에 불러일으킬 것이 분명한 감정과 충동에 마음을 기울이게—거기에 마음을 기울이라는 말일세. 그것들이야말로 그대 자신의 영혼이 깨어나, 그대를 옳은 길로 다그치려 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하고 있는 것이니까."], ["Consider next that the afterlife has become so obscured as to justify calling it a lost light.", "다음으로 사후의 삶이 너무도 흐려져 잃어버린 빛이라 불러도 마땅할 지경에 이르렀음을 헤아려 보게."], ["If you find it, rejoice, O son of Hur—rejoice as I do, though in beggary of words.", "만일 그것을 찾거든 기뻐하게, 후르의 아들이여—내가 그러하듯 기뻐하게, 비록 말이 이토록 궁색할지라도."], ["For then, besides the great gift which is to be saved to us, you will have found the need of a Saviour so infinitely greater than the need of a king; and he we are going to meet will not longer hold place in your hope a warrior with a sword or a monarch with a crown.", "그러면 우리에게 구원되어야 할 그 큰 선물 외에도, 그대는 왕에 대한 필요보다 한없이 더 큰 구주에 대한 필요를 발견하게 될 것이며, 우리가 만나러 가는 그분은 더 이상 그대의 소망 속에서 칼을 든 전사나 왕관을 쓴 군주의 자리를 차지하지 않게 될 것이네."]], [["“A practical question presents itself—How shall we know him at sight?", "\"한 가지 실제적인 물음이 떠오르네—우리가 어찌 그분을 한눈에 알아보겠는가?"], ["If you continue in your belief as to his character—that he is to be a king as Herod was—of course you will keep on until you meet a man clothed in purple and with a sceptre.", "만일 그대가 그분의 성격에 대한 믿음을 고수한다면—그분이 헤롯처럼 왕이 되시리라는 믿음 말일세—물론 그대는 자줏빛 옷을 걸치고 왕홀을 든 사람을 만날 때까지 계속 나아갈 것이네."], ["On the other hand, he I look for will be one poor, humble, undistinguished—a man in appearance as other men; and the sign by which I will know him will be never so simple.", "반면에 내가 찾는 분은 가난하고, 비천하고, 두드러지지 않은 분—겉모습은 다른 사람들과 다름없는 사람일 것이네. 그리고 내가 그분을 알아볼 표징은 더없이 단순할 것이네."], ["He will offer to show me and all mankind the way to the eternal life; the beautiful pure Life of the Soul.”", "그분은 나와 온 인류에게 영원한 삶에 이르는 길을, 곧 영혼의 아름답고 순수한 삶을 보여 주려 하실 것이네.\""]], [["The company sat a moment in silence which was broken by Balthasar.", "일행은 잠시 말없이 앉아 있었고, 그 침묵을 발타사르가 깨뜨렸다."]], [["“Let us arise now,”", "\"이제 일어나세,\""], ["he said—“let us arise and set forward again.", "그가 말했다. \"일어나 다시 길을 나서세."], ["What I have said has caused a return of impatience to see him who is ever in my thought; and if I seem to hurry you, O son of Hur—and you, my daughter—be that my excuse.”", "내가 한 말이 늘 내 마음속에 있는 그분을 뵙고 싶은 조바심을 되살려 놓았네. 그러니 내가 그대를 재촉하는 듯 보이거든, 후르의 아들이여—그리고 내 딸이여—그것을 내 변명으로 삼아 주게.\""]], [["At his signal the slave brought them wine in a skin bottle; and they poured and drank, and shaking the lap-cloths out arose.", "그의 신호에 종이 가죽 부대에 담은 포도주를 가져왔다. 그들은 그것을 따라 마시고, 무릎에 덮었던 천을 털어 내며 일어섰다."]], [["While the slave restored the tent and wares to the box under the houdah, and the Arab brought up the horses, the three principals laved themselves in the pool.", "종이 천막과 그릇들을 후다 밑의 상자에 도로 챙겨 넣고, 아랍인이 말들을 끌어 오는 동안, 세 주인공은 못에서 몸을 씻었다."]], [["In a little while they were retracing their steps back through the wady, intending to overtake the caravan if it had passed them by.", "이윽고 그들은 와디를 거슬러 발걸음을 되짚어 가고 있었으니, 대상이 자기들을 지나쳐 갔다면 따라잡으려는 셈이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["The caravan, stretched out upon the Desert, was very picturesque; in motion, however, it was like a lazy serpent.", "사막 위로 길게 늘어선 대상(隊商)의 모습은 무척이나 그림 같았다. 그러나 움직일 때는 게으른 뱀과 다름없었다."], ["By-and-by its stubborn dragging became intolerably irksome to Balthasar, patient as he was; so, at his suggestion, the party determined to go on by themselves.", "발타사르는 참을성이 많은 사람이었지만, 그 고집스러운 굼뜬 걸음은 머지않아 견디기 힘들 만큼 따분해졌다. 그리하여 그의 제안에 따라 일행은 자기들끼리 먼저 나아가기로 했다."]], [["If the reader be young, or if he has yet a sympathetic recollection of the romanticisms of his youth, he will relish the pleasure with which Ben-Hur, riding near the camel of the Egyptians, gave a last look at the head of the straggling column almost out of sight on the shimmering plain.", "만약 독자가 젊은이라면, 또는 젊은 날의 낭만에 대한 다정한 추억을 아직 간직하고 있다면, 이집트인들의 낙타 가까이에서 말을 몰던 벤허가 가물거리는 평원 위로 거의 시야에서 사라져 가는, 더디게 늘어선 행렬의 선두를 마지막으로 돌아보며 느낀 그 즐거움을 함께 음미할 수 있으리라."]], [["To be definite as may be, and perfectly confidential, Ben-Hur found a certain charm in Iras’s presence.", "되도록 분명하게, 또 솔직하게 말하자면, 벤허는 이라스가 곁에 있다는 사실에서 어떤 매혹을 느꼈다."], ["If she looked down upon him from her high place, he made haste to get near her; if she spoke to him, his heart beat out of its usual time.", "그녀가 높은 자리에서 그를 내려다보면, 그는 서둘러 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그녀가 그에게 말을 건네면, 그의 심장은 여느 때의 박자를 잃었다."], ["The desire to be agreeable to her was a constant impulse.", "그녀의 마음에 들고 싶은 욕망은 끊임없는 충동이었다."], ["Objects on the way, though ever so common, became interesting the moment she called attention to them; a black swallow in the air pursued by her pointing finger went off in a halo; if a bit of quartz or a flake of mica was seen to sparkle in the drab sand under kissing of the sun, at a word he turned aside and brought it to her; and if she threw it away in disappointment, far from thinking of the trouble he had been put to, he was sorry it proved so worthless, and kept a lookout for something better—a ruby, perchance a diamond.", "길가의 사물들은 아무리 흔한 것이라도 그녀가 눈길을 주는 순간 흥미로워졌다. 그녀가 가리키는 손가락을 좇아 날아가는 검은 제비 한 마리는 후광을 두르고 사라졌다. 햇살의 입맞춤을 받아 칙칙한 모래 속에서 석영이나 운모 조각이 반짝이는 것이 보이면, 그는 한마디 말에 길을 비켜 가 그것을 주워 그녀에게 가져다주었다."], ["So the purple of the far mountains became intensely deep and rich if she distinguished it with an exclamation of praise; and when, now and then, the curtain of the houdah fell down, it seemed a sudden dulness had dropped from the sky bedraggling all the landscape.", "그녀가 실망하여 그것을 던져 버려도, 그는 자기가 겪은 수고는 조금도 생각지 않고 다만 그것이 그토록 보잘것없는 것이었음을 안타까워하며, 더 나은 것 — 어쩌면 루비나 다이아몬드 — 을 찾아 두리번거렸다. 그래서 그녀가 감탄의 외침으로 알아보면 먼 산들의 자줏빛도 한층 깊고 짙어졌다. 그리고 이따금 가마의 휘장이 내려오면, 갑작스러운 침침함이 하늘에서 떨어져 온 풍경을 흐려 놓는 것만 같았다."], ["Thus disposed, yielding to the sweet influence, what shall save him from the dangers there are in days of the close companionship with the fair Egyptian incident to the solitary journey they were entered upon?", "이렇게 마음이 기울고, 그 달콤한 영향에 굴복한 채라면, 두 사람이 들어선 이 외로운 여정에 따르는, 아름다운 이집트 여인과의 가까운 동행이 며칠씩 이어지는 동안 그를 그 위험에서 무엇이 구해 낼 수 있겠는가?"]], [["For that there is no logic in love, nor the least mathematical element, it is simply natural that she shall fashion the result who has the wielding of the influence.", "사랑에는 논리가 없고 수학적 요소라곤 조금도 없으니, 영향력을 휘두르는 자가 그 결과를 빚어내는 것은 그저 자연스러운 일이다."]], [["To quicken the conclusion, there were signs, too, that she well knew the influence she was exercising over him.", "이 결말을 재촉하기라도 하듯, 그녀가 자신이 그에게 미치는 영향을 잘 알고 있다는 징표 또한 있었다."], ["From some place under hand she had since morning drawn a caul of golden coins, and adjusted it so the gleaming strings fell over her forehead and upon her cheeks, blending lustrously with the flowing of her blue-black hair.", "그녀는 아침나절부터 어딘가 손 가까운 곳에서 금화로 엮은 머리그물을 꺼내, 반짝이는 줄이 이마와 두 뺨 위로 흘러내려 그녀의 검푸른 머리채와 윤기 있게 어우러지도록 매만져 두었다."], ["From the same safe deposit she had also produced articles of jewelry—rings for finger and ear, bracelets, a necklace of pearls—also, a shawl embroidered with threads of fine gold—the effect of all which she softened with a scarf of Indian lace skillfully folded about her throat and shoulders.", "같은 안전한 보관처에서 그녀는 또 장신구들을 꺼냈으니 — 손가락과 귀에 끼우는 반지, 팔찌, 진주 목걸이 — 또한 고운 금실로 수놓은 숄이 있었다. 그 효과를 그녀는 목과 어깨에 솜씨 있게 두른 인도산 레이스 스카프로 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸다."], ["And so arrayed, she plied Ben-Hur with countless coquetries of speech and manner; showering him with smiles; laughing in flute-like tremolo—and all the while following him with glances, now melting-tender, now sparkling-bright.", "그렇게 차려입고서 그녀는 말과 몸짓으로 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 교태를 벤허에게 부렸다. 미소를 쏟아붓고, 피리 같은 떨림으로 웃었다 — 그러면서 줄곧 그를 눈길로 좇았으니, 그 눈빛은 어느 때는 녹아내릴 듯 부드럽고, 어느 때는 반짝반짝 빛났다."], ["By such play Antony was weaned from his glory; yet she who wrought his ruin was really not half so beautiful as this her countrywoman.", "이런 농간으로 안토니우스는 자기 영광에서 떨어져 나갔다. 그러나 그를 파멸로 이끈 여인은 사실 이 동향(同鄕) 여인의 절반만큼도 아름답지 못했다."]], [["And so to them the nooning came, and the evening.", "이리하여 그들에게 한낮이 왔고, 또 저녁이 왔다."]], [["The sun at its going down behind a spur of the old Bashan, left the party halted by a pool of clear water of the rains out in the Abilene Desert.", "해가 오래된 바산의 한 산자락 뒤로 넘어갈 무렵, 일행은 아빌레네 사막 들판에 빗물이 고여 맑게 된 못 곁에 멈추었다."], ["There the tent was pitched, the supper eaten, and preparations made for the night.", "거기에 천막을 치고, 저녁을 먹고, 밤을 보낼 채비를 했다."]], [["The second watch was Ben-Hur’s; and he was standing, spear in hand, within arm-reach of the dozing camel, looking awhile at the stars, then over the veiled land.", "두 번째 불침번은 벤허의 차례였다. 그는 창을 손에 쥐고, 졸고 있는 낙타에서 팔이 닿을 만한 거리에 서서, 한동안 별들을 바라보다가, 다시 너울에 덮인 듯한 대지를 둘러보았다. 적막은 깊었다."], ["The stillness was intense; only after long spells a warm breath of wind would sough past, but without disturbing him, for yet in thought he entertained the Egyptian, recounting her charms, and sometimes debating how she came by his secrets, the uses she might make of them, and the course he should pursue with her.", "다만 긴 사이를 두고 따스한 바람이 한숨처럼 스쳐 지나갈 뿐이었으나, 그것도 그를 흩뜨리지는 못했다. 아직도 그의 생각은 이집트 여인을 붙들고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀의 매력을 헤아리고, 때로는 그녀가 어떻게 자기 비밀을 알게 되었는지, 그것을 어디에 쓸지, 또 그녀를 어떻게 대해야 할지를 따져 보았다."], ["And through all the debate Love stood off but a little way—a strong temptation, the stronger of a gleam of policy behind.", "그러한 궁리가 이어지는 내내 사랑은 조금 떨어진 곳에 서 있었으니 — 강한 유혹이었고, 그 뒤에 어른거리는 책략의 빛 때문에 더욱 강했다."], ["At the very moment he was most inclined to yield to the allurement, a hand very fair even in the moonless gloaming was laid softly upon his shoulder.", "바로 그 미혹에 굴복하고 싶은 마음이 가장 컸던 순간, 달 없는 어스름 속에서도 무척 고운 손 하나가 살며시 그의 어깨에 얹혔다. 그 손길에 그는 전율했다."], ["The touch thrilled him; he started, turned—and she was there.", "깜짝 놀라 몸을 돌리니 — 그녀가 거기에 있었다."]], [["“I thought you asleep,”", "\"잠든 줄 알았는데요.\""], ["he said, presently.", "그가 이윽고 말했다."]], [["“Sleep is for old people and little children, and I came out to look at my friends, the stars in the south—those now holding the curtains of midnight over the Nile.", "\"잠은 늙은이와 어린아이의 것이지요. 저는 제 벗들을 보러 나왔답니다, 남쪽 하늘의 별들 말이에요 — 지금 나일강 위로 한밤의 휘장을 드리우고 있는 별들 말이에요."], ["But confess yourself surprised!”", "하지만 놀랐다고 인정하세요!\""]], [["He took the hand which had fallen from his shoulder, and said, “Well, was it by an enemy?”", "그는 자기 어깨에서 떨어진 그 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"그래요, 그것이 적의 짓이었던가요?\""]], [["“Oh no!", "\"아니에요!"], ["To be an enemy is to hate, and hating is a sickness which Isis will not suffer to come near me.", "적이 된다는 건 미워하는 것이고, 미움은 병이에요. 이시스 여신께서는 그 병이 제게 가까이 오는 것을 두고 보지 않으세요."], ["She kissed me, you should know, on the heart when I was a child.”", "아셔야 할 것은, 제가 어렸을 때 여신께서 제 가슴에 입을 맞춰 주셨다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Your speech does not sound in the least like your father’s. Are you not of his faith?”", "\"당신의 말투는 아버님과는 조금도 닮지 않았군요. 당신은 아버님과 같은 신앙을 갖고 있지 않은가요?\""]], [["“I might have been”—and she laughed low—“I might have been had I seen what he has.", "\"그랬을지도 모르지요\" — 그녀는 나직이 웃었다 — \"아버지가 본 것을 제가 보았더라면 그랬을지도 몰라요."], ["I may be when I get old like him.", "아버지처럼 늙으면 그렇게 될지도 모르고요."], ["There should be no religion for youth, only poetry and philosophy; and no poetry except such as is the inspiration of wine and mirth and love, and no philosophy that does not nod excuse for follies which cannot outlive a season.", "젊은이에게는 종교가 있어선 안 돼요, 오직 시와 철학만 있으면 되지요. 그리고 시라면 술과 환락과 사랑에서 우러나온 것 외에는 안 되고, 철학이라면 한 철도 못 넘기는 어리석음에 변명을 끄덕여 주지 않는 것이라면 안 되지요."], ["My father’s God is too awful for me.", "아버지의 하느님은 제게는 너무 두려운 분이에요."], ["I failed to find him in the Grove of Daphne.", "저는 다프네 숲에서 그분을 찾지 못했어요."], ["He was never heard of as present in the atria of Rome. But, son of Hur, I have a wish.”", "로마의 안뜰에 그분이 계신다는 말도 들어 본 적이 없고요. 하지만, 후르의 아들이여, 제게는 소원이 하나 있어요.\""]], [["“A wish!", "\"소원이라고요!"], ["Where is he who could say it no?”", "그 소원에 안 된다고 말할 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["“I will try you.”", "\"한번 시험해 보지요.\""]], [["“Tell it then.”", "\"그럼 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“It is very simple.", "\"아주 간단한 거예요."], ["I wish to help you.”", "저는 당신을 돕고 싶어요.\""]], [["She drew closer as she spoke.", "이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 더 가까이 다가왔다."]], [["He laughed, and replied, lightly, “O Egypt!—I came near saying dear Egypt!—does not the sphinx abide in your country?”", "그는 웃으며 가볍게 대꾸했다. \"오, 이집트여! — 사랑하는 이집트라고 할 뻔했군! — 당신네 나라에는 스핑크스가 살지 않소?\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“You are one of its riddles.", "\"당신은 그 수수께끼들 가운데 하나요."], ["Be merciful, and give me a little clew to help me understand you.", "자비를 베풀어, 당신을 이해할 수 있도록 작은 실마리라도 주시오."], ["In what do I need help?", "내가 무엇에서 도움이 필요하단 말이오?"], ["And how can you help me?”", "그리고 당신이 어떻게 나를 도울 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["She took her hand from him, and, turning to the camel, spoke to it endearingly, and patted its monstrous head as it were a thing of beauty.", "그녀는 그에게서 손을 거두더니, 낙타 쪽으로 돌아서서 다정하게 말을 걸고, 그 거대한 머리가 아름다운 것이라도 되는 양 쓰다듬었다."]], [["“O thou last and swiftest and stateliest of the herds of Job! Sometimes thou, too, goest stumbling, because the way is rough and stony and the burden grievous.", "\"오, 욥의 무리 가운데 가장 마지막에 나고 가장 빠르며 가장 위엄 있는 자여! 때로는 너 또한 비틀거리며 가는구나, 길은 험하고 돌투성이고 짐은 무거우니."], ["How is it thou knowest the kind intent by a word; and always makest answer gratefully, though the help offered is from a woman?", "어찌하여 너는 한마디 말에 따뜻한 뜻을 알아채고, 또 그 도움이 여인에게서 나온 것일지라도 늘 고맙게 응답하느냐?"], ["I will kiss thee, thou royal brute!”—she stooped and touched its broad forehead with her lips, saying immediately, “because in thy intelligence there is no suspicion!”", "내 너에게 입 맞추리라, 너 고귀한 짐승아!\" — 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그 넓은 이마에 입술을 대고는, 곧바로 말했다. \"네 슬기에는 의심이라는 것이 없으니까!\""]], [["And Ben-Hur, restraining himself, said calmly, “The reproach has not failed its mark, O Egypt!", "그러자 벤허는 스스로를 억누르며 차분히 말했다. \"그 책망이 과녁을 빗나가지는 않았소, 오 이집트여!"], ["I seem to say thee no; may it not be because I am under seal of honor, and by my silence cover the lives and fortunes of others?”", "내가 당신에게 안 된다고 말하는 것처럼 보인다면, 그것은 내가 명예의 봉인 아래 있고, 내 침묵으로 다른 이들의 목숨과 운명을 지키고 있기 때문은 아니겠소?\""]], [["“May be!” she said, quickly.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요!\" 그녀가 재빨리 말했다."], ["“It is so.”", "\"정말 그래요.\""]], [["He shrank a step, and asked, his voice sharp with amazement, “What all knowest thou?”", "그는 한 걸음 물러서며, 놀라움으로 날카로워진 목소리로 물었다. \"당신은 대체 무엇을 다 알고 있는 거요?\""]], [["She answered, after a laugh,", "그녀는 한바탕 웃고 나서 대답했다."]], [["“Why do men deny that the senses of women are sharper than theirs?", "\"왜 사내들은 여인의 감각이 자기들보다 더 예리하다는 것을 부정할까요?"], ["Your face has been under my eyes all day.", "당신 얼굴은 온종일 제 눈앞에 있었어요."], ["I had but to look at it to see you bore some weight in mind; and to find the weight, what had I to do more than recall your debates with my father?", "저는 그저 그것을 바라보기만 하면 당신이 마음에 어떤 무게를 지고 있다는 걸 알 수 있었지요. 그리고 그 무게가 무엇인지 알려면, 당신이 제 아버지와 나눈 토론을 떠올리는 것 말고 무얼 더 해야 했겠어요?"], ["Son of Hur!”—she lowered her voice with singular dexterity, and, going nearer, spoke so her breath was warm upon his cheek—“son of Hur!", "후르의 아들이여!\" — 그녀는 남다른 솜씨로 목소리를 낮추고, 더 가까이 다가가, 자기 숨결이 그의 뺨에 따뜻하게 닿도록 속삭였다 — \"후르의 아들이여!"], ["he thou art going to find is to be King of the Jews, is he not?”", "당신이 찾으러 가는 그분은 유대인의 왕이 되실 분이지요, 그렇지 않아요?\""]], [["His heart beat fast and hard.", "그의 심장이 빠르고 세차게 뛰었다."]], [["“A King of the Jews like Herod, only greater,”", "\"헤롯 같은 유대인의 왕, 다만 그보다 더 위대한 분이지요.\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."]], [["He looked away—into the night, up to the stars; then his eyes met hers, and lingered there; and her breath was on his lips, so near was she.", "그는 눈길을 돌려 — 밤 속을, 별들을 올려다보았다. 그러다 그의 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주쳐, 거기에 머물렀다. 그녀의 숨결이 그의 입술에 닿았으니, 그만큼 그녀는 가까이 있었다."]], [["“Since morning,”", "\"아침나절부터\""], ["she said, further, “we have been having visions.", "하고 그녀가 다시 말했다. \"우리는 환상을 보고 있었지요."], ["Now if I tell you mine, will you serve me as well? What!", "자, 제가 제 환상을 당신에게 말해 준다면, 당신도 제게 그만큼 해 주시겠어요? 어머!"], ["silent still?”", "아직도 말이 없으시군요?\""]], [["She pushed his hand away, and turned as if to go; but he caught her, and said, eagerly, “Stay—stay and speak!”", "그녀는 그의 손을 밀쳐 내고, 가려는 듯 돌아섰다. 그러나 그가 그녀를 붙잡으며 간절히 말했다. \"있어 줘요 — 있어 주고 말해 주오!\""]], [["She went back, and with her hand upon his shoulder, leaned against him; and he put his arm around her, and drew her close, very close; and in the caress was the promise she asked.", "그녀는 돌아와, 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 그에게 기댔다. 그는 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안고, 가까이, 아주 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그 포옹 속에 그녀가 구한 약속이 담겨 있었다."]], [["“Speak, and tell me thy visions, O Egypt, dear Egypt!", "\"말해 주오, 당신의 환상을 들려주오, 오 이집트여, 사랑하는 이집트여!"], ["A prophet—nay, not the Tishbite, not even the Lawgiver—could have refused an asking of thine.", "예언자라도 — 아니, 디셉 사람도, 율법을 준 그분조차도 — 당신의 청을 거절할 수는 없었을 거요."], ["I am at thy will.", "나는 당신 뜻대로 하겠소."], ["Be merciful—merciful, I pray.”", "자비를 베푸시오 — 자비를, 비오.\""]], [["The entreaty passed apparently unheard, for looking up and nestling in his embrace, she said, slowly, “The vision which followed me was of magnificent war—war on land and sea—with clashing of arms and rush of armies, as if Cæsar and Pompey were come again, and Octavius and Antony.", "그 애원은 들은 척 만 척 지나간 듯, 그녀는 그를 올려다보며 그의 품에 파고든 채 천천히 말했다. \"저를 따라온 환상은 어마어마한 전쟁이었어요 — 뭍과 바다에서 벌어지는 전쟁 — 병장기가 부딪치고 군대가 밀려드는, 마치 카이사르와 폼페이우스가 다시 살아 돌아오고, 옥타비아누스와 안토니우스가 되돌아온 것 같았어요."], ["A cloud of dust and ashes arose and covered the world, and Rome was not any more; all dominion returned to the East; out of the cloud issued another race of heroes; and there were vaster satrapies and brighter crowns for giving away than were ever known.", "먼지와 잿더미의 구름이 일어나 온 세상을 뒤덮었고, 로마는 더 이상 없었지요. 모든 지배권이 동방으로 돌아왔어요. 그 구름 속에서 또 다른 영웅의 족속이 나왔고, 일찍이 알려진 적 없던 더 광대한 태수령(太守領)과 더 빛나는 왕관들이 나눠 주기 위해 마련되어 있었어요."], ["And, son of Hur, while the vision was passing, and after it was gone, I kept asking myself, ‘What shall he not have who served the King earliest and best?’”", "그리고 후르의 아들이여, 그 환상이 지나가는 동안에도, 또 그것이 사라진 뒤에도, 저는 줄곧 자신에게 물었답니다. '왕을 가장 먼저, 가장 훌륭히 섬긴 자가 무엇인들 얻지 못하랴?'\""]], [["Again Ben-Hur recoiled.", "벤허는 또다시 움찔했다."], ["The question was the very question which had been with him all day.", "그 물음이야말로 온종일 그를 따라다닌 바로 그 물음이었다."], ["Presently he fancied he had the clew he wanted.", "이윽고 그는 원하던 실마리를 잡았다고 여겼다."]], [["“So,” he said, “I have you now.", "\"그렇군.\" 그가 말했다."], ["The satrapies and crowns are the things to which you would help me.", "\"이제 알겠소. 태수령과 왕관, 그것이 당신이 나를 돕고 싶다는 그것이로군."], ["I see, I see!", "알겠소, 알겠어!"], ["And there never was such queen as you would be, so shrewd, so beautiful, so royal—never!", "그리고 당신만큼 그토록 영리하고, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 위엄 있는 왕비는 일찍이 없었을 거요 — 결코!"], ["But, alas, dear Egypt!", "하지만, 아아, 사랑하는 이집트여!"], ["by the vision as you show it me the prizes are all of war, and you are but a woman, though Isis did kiss you on the heart.", "당신이 내게 보여 준 그 환상에서 상(賞)들은 모두 전쟁에서 나오는 것이고, 당신은 비록 이시스 여신께서 가슴에 입을 맞춰 주셨다 해도 한낱 여인일 뿐이오."], ["And crowns are starry gifts beyond your power of help, unless, indeed, you have a way to them more certain than that of the sword. If so, O Egypt, Egypt, show it me, and I will walk in it, if only for your sake.”", "왕관이란 당신의 힘으로는 도울 수 없는 별처럼 먼 선물이오, 칼보다 더 확실한 길이 정말로 당신에게 있지 않다면 말이오. 만약 그렇다면, 오 이집트여, 이집트여, 그 길을 내게 보여 주오, 그러면 오직 당신을 위해서라도 나는 그 길을 걷겠소.\""]], [["She removed his arm, and said, “Spread your cloak upon the sand—here, so I can rest against the camel.", "그녀는 그의 팔을 풀고 말했다. \"당신의 외투를 모래 위에 펼쳐 줘요 — 여기, 제가 낙타에 기댈 수 있도록요."], ["I will sit, and tell you a story which came down the Nile to Alexandria, where I had it.”", "저는 앉아서, 나일강을 따라 알렉산드리아까지 내려온 이야기를 들려 드릴게요. 저는 거기서 그 이야기를 들었거든요.\""]], [["He did as she said, first planting the spear in the ground near by.", "그는 그녀가 말한 대로 했다. 먼저 창을 가까운 땅에 꽂아 두었다."]], [["“And what shall I do?”", "\"그럼 나는 무얼 해야 하오?\""], ["he said, ruefully, when she was seated.", "그녀가 자리에 앉자 그가 처량하게 물었다."], ["“In Alexandria is it customary for the listeners to sit or stand?”", "\"알렉산드리아에서는 이야기를 듣는 사람이 앉는 것이 관례요, 아니면 서 있는 것이 관례요?\""]], [["From the comfortable place against the old domestic she answered, laughing, “The audiences of story-tellers are wilful, and sometimes they do as they please.”", "그 늙은 가축에 편안히 기댄 자리에서 그녀가 웃으며 대답했다. \"이야기꾼의 청중은 제멋대로지요, 때로는 마음 내키는 대로 한답니다.\""]], [["Without more ado he stretched himself upon the sand, and put her arm about his neck.", "그는 더 군말 없이 모래 위에 몸을 뉘고, 그녀의 팔을 자기 목에 둘렀다."]], [["“I am ready,” he said.", "\"준비됐소.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["And directly she began:", "그러자 그녀가 곧바로 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["# HOW THE BEAUTIFUL CAME TO THE EARTH.", "# HOW THE BEAUTIFUL CAME TO THE EARTH."]], [["“You must know, in the first place, that Isis was—and, for that matter, she may yet be—the most beautiful of deities; and Osiris, her husband, though wise and powerful, was sometimes stung with jealousy of her, for only in their loves are the gods like mortals.", "아름다움이 어떻게 땅에 왔는가. \"우선 아셔야 할 것은, 이시스 여신은 — 그리고 그건 지금도 그럴지 모르지만 — 신들 가운데 가장 아름다운 분이었다는 거예요. 그리고 그녀의 남편 오시리스는 지혜롭고 강대했지만, 때로는 그녀에 대한 질투에 마음이 찔렸지요. 신들도 사랑에 있어서만큼은 인간과 같으니까요."]], [["“The palace of the Divine Wife was of silver, crowning the tallest mountain in the moon, and thence she passed often to the sun, in the heart of which, a source of eternal light, Osiris kept his palace of gold too shining for men to look at.", "신성한 아내의 궁전은 은으로 지어졌고, 달에서 가장 높은 산을 왕관처럼 이고 있었어요. 그녀는 거기서 자주 해로 건너갔는데, 그 한가운데에는 영원한 빛의 원천이 있어, 오시리스가 황금 궁전을 두고 있었지요. 너무 눈부셔 사람은 차마 바라볼 수 없는 궁전이었답니다."]], [["“One time—there are no days with the gods—while she was full pleasantly with him on the roof of the golden palace, she chanced to look, and afar, just on the line of the universe, saw Indra passing with an army of simians, all borne upon the backs of flying eagles.", "한번은 — 신들에게는 날(日)이라는 게 없지요 — 그녀가 황금 궁전 지붕 위에서 그와 더없이 즐겁게 지내고 있을 때, 우연히 시선을 돌리다가, 저 멀리 우주의 경계선 바로 위에서 인드라가 원숭이들의 군대를 거느리고 지나가는 것을 보았어요. 모두 날아다니는 독수리들의 등에 실려 있었지요."], ["He, the Friend of Living Things—so with much love is Indra called—was returning from his final war with the hideous Rakshakas—returning victorious; and in his suite were Rama, the hero, and Sita, his bride, who, next to Isis herself, was the very most beautiful.", "그분, 살아 있는 것들의 벗 — 깊은 사랑을 담아 인드라를 그렇게 부르지요 — 은 끔찍한 락샤사들과의 마지막 전쟁에서 돌아오는 길이었어요, 승리를 거두고 돌아오는 길이었지요. 그 행렬에는 영웅 라마와, 그의 신부 시타가 있었는데, 시타는 이시스 여신 다음으로 가장 아름다웠어요."], ["And Isis arose, and took off her girdle of stars, and waved it to Sita—to Sita, mind you—waved it in glad salute.", "이시스는 일어나 별들로 엮은 허리띠를 풀어, 시타에게 흔들어 보였지요 — 시타에게요, 잘 들으세요 — 반가운 인사로 흔들어 보였답니다."], ["And instantly, between the marching host and the two on the golden roof, a something as of night fell, and shut out the view; but it was not night—only the frown of Osiris.", "그러자 즉시 행군하는 무리와 황금 지붕 위의 두 신 사이로, 마치 밤 같은 무언가가 내려와 그 광경을 가려 버렸어요. 그러나 그것은 밤이 아니었어요 — 그저 오시리스의 찌푸린 얼굴이었지요."]], [["“It happened the subject of his speech that moment was such as none else than they could think of; and he arose, and said, majestically, ‘Get thee home.", "마침 그 순간 그가 하던 이야기는 그들 둘 말고는 아무도 떠올릴 수 없는 그런 것이었어요. 그는 일어나 위엄 있게 말했지요. '집으로 돌아가라."], ["I will do the work myself.", "그 일은 내가 직접 하겠다."], ["To make a perfectly happy being I do not need thy help.", "완전히 행복한 존재를 만드는 데 그대의 도움은 필요 없다."], ["Get thee gone.’", "어서 가라.'"]], [["“Now Isis had eyes large as those of the white cow which in the temple eats sweet grasses from the hands of the faithful even while they say their prayers; and her eyes were the color of the cows, and quite as tender.", "그런데 이시스는 흰 암소처럼 큰 눈을 지녔어요. 신전에서 그 암소는 신도들이 기도를 올리는 동안에도 그들의 손에서 달콤한 풀을 받아먹지요. 그녀의 눈은 그 암소의 눈 빛깔이었고, 꼭 그만큼 부드러웠어요."], ["And she too arose and said, smiling as she spoke, so her look was little more than the glow of the moon in the hazy harvest-month, ‘Farewell, good my lord.", "그녀 또한 일어나, 말하면서 미소를 지었는데, 그 표정은 안개 낀 추수의 달밤에 비치는 달빛보다 조금 더 환할 뿐이었지요. '안녕히 계세요, 다정한 나의 주여."], ["You will call me presently, I know; for without me you cannot make the perfectly happy creature of which you were thinking, any more’—and she stopped to laugh, knowing well the truth of the saying—‘any more, my lord, than you yourself can be perfectly happy without me.’", "당신은 머지않아 저를 부르실 거예요, 저는 알아요. 저 없이는 당신이 생각하시던 완전히 행복한 피조물을 만드실 수 없으니까요, 더 이상은' — 그녀는 그 말이 참임을 잘 알기에 웃으려 잠시 멈추었어요 — '더 이상은요, 나의 주여, 당신 자신이 저 없이는 완전히 행복할 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로요.'"]], [["“‘We will see,’ he said.", "'두고 보면 알겠지.' 그가 말했어요."]], [["“And she went her way, and took her needles and her chair, and on the roof of the silver palace sat watching and knitting.", "그래서 그녀는 제 길을 갔고, 뜨개바늘과 의자를 가져다, 은 궁전 지붕 위에 앉아 지켜보며 뜨개질을 했지요."]], [["“And the will of Osiris, at labor in his mighty breast, was as the sound of the mills of all the other gods grinding at once, so loud that the near stars rattled like seeds in a parched pod; and some dropped out and were lost.", "그리고 오시리스의 의지가 그 위대한 가슴속에서 일하는 소리는, 다른 모든 신들의 방앗간이 한꺼번에 돌아가며 가는 소리 같았어요. 어찌나 컸던지 가까운 별들이 마른 꼬투리 속 씨앗처럼 달그락거렸고, 더러는 떨어져 나가 사라졌지요."], ["And while the sound kept on she waited and knit; nor lost she ever a stitch the while.", "그 소리가 이어지는 동안 그녀는 기다리며 뜨개질을 했어요. 그러면서도 코 하나 놓치는 법이 없었답니다."]], [["“Soon a spot appeared in the space over towards the sun; and it grew until it was great as the moon, and then she knew a world was intended; but when, growing and growing, at last it cast her planet in the shade, all save the little point lighted by her presence, she knew how very angry he was; yet she knit away, assured that the end would be as she had said.", "이윽고 해 쪽으로 향한 공간에 점 하나가 나타났어요. 그것은 달만큼 커질 때까지 자라났고, 그제야 그녀는 하나의 세계가 의도되고 있음을 알았지요. 그러나 그것이 자라고 또 자라, 마침내 그녀의 별을 그늘 속에 던져 넣고, 오직 그녀가 있어 빛나는 작은 점만 남기자, 그녀는 그가 얼마나 노했는지를 알았어요. 그래도 그녀는 자기가 말한 대로 끝이 나리라 확신하며 뜨개질을 계속했지요."]], [["“And so came the earth, at first but a cold gray mass hanging listless in the hollow void.", "이렇게 땅이 생겨났어요, 처음에는 그저 텅 빈 허공에 무기력하게 매달린 차갑고 잿빛인 덩어리였지요."], ["Later she saw it separate into divisions; here a plain, there a mountain, yonder a sea, all as yet without a sparkle.", "나중에 그녀는 그것이 여러 갈래로 나뉘는 것을 보았어요. 여기는 평원, 저기는 산, 그 너머는 바다, 그러나 아직 반짝임이라곤 하나도 없었지요."], ["And then, by a river-bank, something moved; and she stopped her knitting for wonder.", "그러다가 강기슭에서 무언가가 움직였어요. 그녀는 놀라워 뜨개질을 멈추었지요."], ["The something arose, and lifted its hands to the sun in sign of knowledge whence it had its being.", "그 무언가가 일어나, 자기 존재가 어디서 비롯되었는지를 아는 표시로 두 손을 해를 향해 들어 올렸어요."], ["And this First Man was beautiful to see.", "이 첫 사람은 보기에 아름다웠어요."], ["And about him were the creations we call nature—the grass, the trees, birds, beasts, even the insects and reptiles.", "그리고 그의 둘레에는 우리가 자연이라 부르는 창조물들이 있었지요 — 풀, 나무, 새, 짐승, 심지어 곤충과 파충류까지요."]], [["“And for a time the man went about happy in his life: it was easy to see how happy he was.", "한동안 그 사람은 자기 삶을 누리며 행복하게 돌아다녔어요. 그가 얼마나 행복한지는 한눈에 알 수 있었지요."], ["And in the lull of the sound of the laboring will Isis heard a scornful laugh, and presently the words, blown across from the sun,", "그리고 일하는 의지의 소리가 잦아든 틈에 이시스는 비웃는 웃음소리를, 곧이어 해 너머에서 불어오는 말소리를 들었어요."]], [["“‘Thy help, indeed!", "'그대의 도움이라고, 흥!"], ["Behold a creature perfectly happy!’", "보라, 완전히 행복한 피조물이로다!'"]], [["“And Isis fell to knitting again, for she was patient as Osiris was strong; and if he could work, she could wait; and wait she did, knowing that mere life is not enough to keep anything content.", "그러자 이시스는 다시 뜨개질에 빠져들었어요. 오시리스가 강한 만큼 그녀는 참을성이 많았으니까요. 그가 일할 수 있다면, 그녀는 기다릴 수 있었지요. 그리고 그녀는 기다렸어요, 그저 사는 것만으로는 무엇도 만족시킬 수 없음을 알기에."]], [["“And sure enough.", "그리고 정말 그랬지요."], ["Not long until the Divine Wife could see a change in the man.", "오래지 않아 신성한 아내는 그 사람에게 변화가 일어나는 것을 볼 수 있었어요."], ["He grew listless, and kept to one place prone by the river, and looked up but seldom, and then always with a moody face.", "그는 무기력해졌고, 강가 한자리에 엎드려 있기만 하면서, 좀처럼 눈을 들지 않았고, 든다 해도 늘 침울한 얼굴이었지요. 그의 마음에서 흥미가 죽어 가고 있었어요."], ["Interest was dying in him.", "그녀가 그것을 확신하고, '이 피조물이 자기 존재에 싫증이 났구나'"], ["And when she made sure of it, even while she was saying to herself, ‘The creature is sick of his being,’ there was a roar of the creative will at work again, and in a twinkling the earth, theretofore all a thing of coldest gray, flamed with colors; the mountains swam in purple, the plains bearing grass and trees turned green, the sea blue, and the clouds varied infinitely.”", "하고 혼잣말을 하던 바로 그때, 창조의 의지가 다시 일하는 굉음이 울렸어요. 그러자 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 그때까지 온통 가장 차가운 잿빛이던 땅이 색채로 타올랐지요. 산들은 자줏빛으로 일렁이고, 풀과 나무를 인 평원은 초록으로 변하고, 바다는 푸르게, 구름은 끝없이 변화무쌍하게 물들었어요.\""]], [["And the man sprang up and clapped his hands, for he was cured and happy again.", "그러자 그 사람은 벌떡 일어나 손뼉을 쳤어요, 다시 나아 행복해졌으니까요."]], [["“And Isis smiled, and knit away, saying to herself, ‘It was well thought, and will do a little while; but mere beauty in a world is not enough for such a being.", "\"이시스는 미소를 지으며 뜨개질을 계속했어요, 혼잣말을 하면서요. '잘 생각하셨어, 한동안은 효험이 있겠지. 하지만 세계의 한낱 아름다움만으로는 저런 존재에게 충분치 않아."], ["My lord must try again.’", "나의 주는 다시 한번 시도하셔야 할 거야.'"]], [["“With the last word, the thunder of the will at work shook the moon, and, looking, Isis dropped her knitting and clapped her hands; for theretofore everything on the earth but the man had been fixed to a given place; now all living, and much that was not living, received the gift of Motion. The birds took to wing joyously; beasts great and small went about, each in its way; the trees shook their verdurous branches, nodding to the enamoured winds; the rivers ran to the seas, and the seas tossed in their beds and rolled in crested waves, and with surging and ebbing painted the shores with glistening foam; and over all the clouds floated like sailed ships unanchored.", "그 마지막 말과 함께, 일하는 의지의 천둥소리가 달을 뒤흔들었어요. 이시스가 바라보다가 뜨개질을 떨어뜨리고 손뼉을 쳤지요. 그때까지 땅 위의 모든 것은 사람 말고는 정해진 자리에 붙박여 있었는데, 이제 살아 있는 모든 것이, 또 살아 있지 않은 많은 것까지 운동이라는 선물을 받았으니까요. 새들은 기쁘게 날개를 폈고, 크고 작은 짐승들이 저마다 제 방식으로 돌아다녔어요. 나무들은 푸르른 가지를 흔들며 그것을 사랑하는 바람에게 고개를 끄덕였지요. 강물은 바다로 흘러갔고, 바다는 제 자리에서 뒤척이며 마루를 인 물결로 굽이쳤고, 밀려왔다 밀려가며 반짝이는 거품으로 해안을 물들였어요. 그리고 그 모든 것 위로 구름이 닻을 내리지 않은 돛단배처럼 떠다녔지요."]], [["“And the man rose up happy as a child; whereat Osiris was pleased, so that he shouted, ‘Ha, ha!", "그러자 그 사람은 어린아이처럼 행복하게 일어났어요. 그것을 보고 오시리스는 흡족하여 외쳤지요. '하하!"], ["See how well I am doing without thee!’", "보아라, 그대 없이도 내가 얼마나 잘 해내고 있는지를!'"]], [["“The good wife took up her work, and answered ever so quietly, ‘It was well thought, my lord—ever so well thought—and will serve awhile.’", "착한 아내는 일감을 집어 들고, 더없이 조용히 대답했어요. '잘 생각하셨어요, 나의 주여 — 참으로 잘 생각하셨어요 — 한동안은 쓸모가 있을 거예요.'"]], [["“And as before, so again. The sight of things in motion became to the man as of course.", "그리고 전과 같이, 또 같은 일이 일어났지요. 움직이는 것들의 광경은 그 사람에게 당연한 것이 되어 버렸어요."], ["The birds in flight, the rivers running, the seas in tumult of action, ceased to amuse him, and he pined again even worse.", "나는 새도, 흐르는 강도, 활동의 소용돌이 속 바다도 더 이상 그를 즐겁게 하지 못했고, 그는 다시, 전보다 더 심하게 시들어 갔지요."]], [["“And Isis waited, saying to herself, ‘Poor creature!", "이시스는 기다리며 혼잣말을 했어요. '딱한 것!"], ["He is more wretched than ever.’", "그 어느 때보다 비참하구나.'"]], [["“And, as if he heard the thought, Osiris stirred, and the noise of his will shook the universe; the sun in its central seat alone stood firm.", "그러자 마치 그 생각을 듣기라도 한 듯, 오시리스가 움직였고, 그 의지의 소리가 우주를 뒤흔들었어요. 오직 한가운데 자리에 앉은 해만이 굳건히 서 있었지요."], ["And Isis looked, but saw no change; then while she was smiling, assured that her lord’s last invention was sped, suddenly the creature arose, and seemed to listen; and his face brightened, and he clapped his hands for joy, for Sounds were heard the first time on earth—sounds dissonant, sounds harmonious.", "이시스가 바라보았지만 아무런 변화도 보지 못했어요. 그러다가 그녀가 미소를 짓고, 주의 마지막 발명이 끝났다고 확신하던 그때, 갑자기 그 피조물이 일어나, 귀를 기울이는 듯했어요. 그의 얼굴이 환해지더니, 기뻐서 손뼉을 쳤지요."], ["The winds murmured in the trees; the birds sang, each kind a song of its own, or chattered in speech; the rivulets running to the rivers became so many harpers with harps of silver strings all tinkling together; and the rivers running to the seas surged on in solemn accord, while the seas beat the land to a tune of thunder.", "땅 위에 처음으로 소리가 들렸으니까요 — 어긋나는 소리, 어우러지는 소리. 바람은 나무들 사이에서 속삭였고, 새들은 노래했어요, 저마다 제 종류의 노래를, 아니면 말처럼 재잘거렸지요. 강으로 흘러드는 시냇물들은 은빛 줄을 매단 수많은 하프 연주자가 되어 다 함께 딸랑거렸고, 바다로 흘러드는 강들은 엄숙한 화음을 이루며 밀려갔고, 그동안 바다는 천둥의 가락으로 뭍을 두드렸어요."], ["There was music, music everywhere, and all the time; so the man could not but be happy.", "음악이, 음악이 어디에나 있었고, 언제나 있었지요. 그러니 그 사람은 행복하지 않을 수 없었답니다."]], [["“Then Isis mused, thinking how well, how wondrous well, her lord was doing; but presently she shook her head: Color, Motion, Sound—and she repeated them slowly—there was no element else of beauty except Form and Light, and to them the earth had been born.", "그러자 이시스는 골똘히 생각했어요, 나의 주가 얼마나 잘, 얼마나 놀랍도록 잘 해내고 있는지를. 그러나 이윽고 그녀는 고개를 가로저었지요. 색채, 운동, 소리 — 그녀는 그것들을 천천히 되뇌었어요 — 형상과 빛 말고는 아름다움의 요소가 더 없었고, 그 둘을 위해 땅이 태어났던 거예요."], ["Now, indeed, Osiris was done; and if the creature should again fall off into wretchedness, her help must be asked; and her fingers flew—two, three, five, even ten stitches she took at once.", "이제야말로 오시리스는 다 한 셈이었지요. 그리고 만약 그 피조물이 또다시 비참함에 빠진다면, 그녀의 도움을 청해야 할 터였어요. 그녀의 손가락이 날아다녔지요 — 한 번에 두 코, 세 코, 다섯 코, 심지어 열 코까지 떴어요."]], [["“And the man was happy a long time—longer than ever before; it seemed, indeed, he would never tire again.", "그리고 그 사람은 오랫동안 행복했어요 — 그 어느 때보다도 오래요. 정말이지 그는 다시는 싫증 내지 않을 것만 같았지요."], ["But Isis knew better; and she waited and waited, nor minded the many laughs flung at her from the sun; she waited and waited, and at last saw signs of the end.", "그러나 이시스는 더 잘 알았어요. 그녀는 기다리고 또 기다렸지요, 해 쪽에서 던져 오는 숱한 웃음에도 아랑곳하지 않고요. 기다리고 또 기다리던 끝에 마침내 끝이 다가오는 징조를 보았어요."], ["Sounds became familiar to him, and in their range, from the chirruping of the cricket under the roses to the roar of the seas and the bellow of the clouds in storm, there was not anything unusual.", "소리들이 그에게 익숙해졌고, 장미꽃 아래 귀뚜라미의 울음에서부터 바다의 포효와 폭풍 속 구름의 으르렁거림에 이르기까지, 그 범위 안에 새로운 것이라곤 하나도 없었지요."], ["And he pined and sickened, and sought his place of moping by the river, and at last fell down motionless.", "그는 시들고 병들어, 강가의 침울한 자리를 찾았고, 마침내 꼼짝없이 쓰러졌어요. 그러자 이시스가 가엾이 여겨 입을 열었어요."]], [["“Then Isis in pity spoke.", "'나의 주여,' 그녀가 말했지요."], ["“‘My lord,’ she said, ‘the creature is dying.’", "'저 피조물이 죽어 가고 있어요.'"]], [["“But Osiris, though seeing it all, held his peace; he could do no more.", "그러나 오시리스는 그 모든 것을 보면서도 잠자코 있었어요. 그는 더 이상 할 수 있는 것이 없었으니까요."]], [["“‘Shall I help him?’ she asked.", "'제가 그를 도울까요?' 그녀가 물었어요."]], [["“Osiris was too proud to speak.", "오시리스는 너무 자존심이 강해 입을 열지 않았지요."]], [["“Then Isis took the last stitch in her knitting, and gathering her work in a roll of brilliance flung it off—flung it so it fell close to the man.", "그러자 이시스는 뜨개질의 마지막 코를 떴어요. 그리고 자기 일감을 눈부신 두루마리로 거두어 던졌지요 — 그것이 그 사람 가까이에 떨어지도록 던졌어요."], ["And he, hearing the sound of the fall so near by, looked up, and lo!", "그러자 그는, 그토록 가까이에서 떨어지는 소리를 듣고 눈을 들었지요. 그런데 보세요!"], ["a Woman—the First Woman—was stooping to help him!", "한 여인이 — 첫 여인이 — 몸을 굽혀 그를 돕고 있었어요!"], ["She reached a hand to him; he caught it and arose; and nevermore was miserable, but evermore happy.”", "그녀가 그에게 손을 내밀자, 그는 그것을 잡고 일어났어요. 그리고 다시는 비참해지지 않았고, 영원토록 행복했답니다.\""]], [["“Such, O son of Hur!", "\"후르의 아들이여!"], ["is the genesis of the beautiful, as they tell it on the Nile.”", "이것이 바로 아름다움의 기원이에요, 나일강에서 전해 오는 이야기로는 그렇답니다.\""]], [["She paused.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“A pretty invention, and cunning,”", "\"예쁜 이야기로군, 게다가 영리하기도 하고.\""], ["he said, directly; “but it is imperfect.", "그가 곧바로 말했다. \"하지만 미완성이오."], ["What did Osiris afterwards?”", "오시리스는 그 뒤에 어떻게 했소?\""]], [["“Oh yes,” she replied.", "\"아, 그래요.\""], ["“He called the Divine Wife back to the sun, and they went on all pleasantly together, each helping the other.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그는 신성한 아내를 다시 해로 불러들였고, 둘은 서로를 도우며 더없이 즐겁게 함께 지냈답니다.\""]], [["“And shall I not do as the first man?”", "\"그럼 나도 그 첫 사람처럼 해야 하지 않겠소?\""]], [["He carried the hand resting upon his neck to his lips. “In love—in love!”", "그는 자기 목에 얹힌 그녀의 손을 입술로 가져갔다. \"사랑 속에서 — 사랑 속에서!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["His head dropped softly into her lap.", "그의 머리가 그녀의 무릎 위로 부드럽게 떨어졌다."]], [["“You will find the King,”", "\"당신은 왕을 찾아낼 거예요.\""], ["she said, placing her other hand caressingly upon his head.", "그녀가 다른 손을 그의 머리에 다정하게 얹으며 말했다."], ["“You will go on and find the King and serve him.", "\"당신은 나아가 왕을 찾아 그를 섬길 거예요."], ["With your sword you will earn his richest gifts; and his best soldier will be my hero.”", "당신의 칼로 그분의 가장 값진 선물들을 얻을 것이고, 그분의 가장 훌륭한 병사가 바로 나의 영웅일 거예요.\""]], [["He turned his face, and saw hers close above. In all the sky there was that moment nothing so bright to him as her eyes, enshadowed though they were.", "그는 얼굴을 돌렸고, 자기 위로 가까이 있는 그녀의 얼굴을 보았다. 온 하늘에서 그 순간 그에게 그녀의 두 눈만큼 빛나는 것은 없었다, 그늘에 가려져 있었는데도."], ["Presently he sat up, and put his arms about her, and kissed her passionately, saying, “O Egypt, Egypt!", "이윽고 그는 일어나 앉아, 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸고 격정에 차서 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"오 이집트여, 이집트여!"], ["If the King has crowns in gift, one shall be mine; and I will bring it and put it here over the place my lips have marked.", "왕이 나눠 줄 왕관을 가지고 있다면, 그중 하나는 내 것이 될 거요. 나는 그것을 가져다, 내 입술이 표를 새긴 바로 여기에 얹어 주겠소."], ["You shall be a queen—my queen—no one more beautiful!", "당신은 왕비가 될 거요 — 나의 왕비가 — 그보다 더 아름다운 이는 없으리!"], ["And we will be ever, ever so happy!”", "그리고 우리는 언제나, 언제까지나 더없이 행복할 거요!\""]], [["“And you will tell me everything, and let me help you in all?”", "\"그러면 당신은 제게 모든 것을 말해 주고, 제가 무엇이든 당신을 돕게 해 주실 거예요?\""], ["she said, kissing him in return.", "그녀가 답으로 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["The question chilled his fervor.", "그 물음이 그의 열정을 식혀 버렸다."]], [["“Is it not enough that I love you?”", "\"내가 당신을 사랑하는 것으로 충분치 않소?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Perfect love means perfect faith,”", "\"완전한 사랑은 완전한 믿음을 뜻하지요.\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“But never mind—you will know me better.”", "\"하지만 괜찮아요 — 당신은 저를 더 잘 알게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["She took her hand from him and arose.", "그녀는 그에게서 손을 거두고 일어섰다."]], [["“You are cruel,”", "\"당신은 잔인하군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Moving away, she stopped by the camel, and touched its front face with her lips.", "물러서던 그녀가 낙타 곁에 멈추어, 그 낯짝 앞쪽에 입술을 댔다."]], [["“O thou noblest of thy kind!—that, because there is no suspicion in thy love.”", "\"오, 네 종족 가운데 가장 고귀한 자여! — 네 사랑에는 의심이라곤 없으니까.\""]], [["An instant, and she was gone.", "그러고는 한순간, 그녀는 사라졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["The third day of the journey the party nooned by the river Jabbok, where there were a hundred or more men, mostly of Peraea, resting themselves and their beasts.", "여행 사흘째 되던 날, 일행은 얍복강 가에서 한낮의 휴식을 가졌다. 그곳에는 대부분 페레아 출신인 백 명이 넘는 사람들이 자기 몸과 짐승들을 쉬게 하고 있었다. 그들이 낙타에서 내리기가 무섭게 한 남자가 물 항아리와 대접을 들고 다가와 마실 것을 권했다."], ["Hardly had they dismounted, before a man came to them with a pitcher of water and a bowl, and offered them drink; as they received the attention with much courtesy, he said, looking at the camel, “I am returning from the Jordan, where just now there are many people from distant parts, travelling as you are, illustrious friend; but they had none of them the equal of your servant here.", "그들이 정중하게 그 호의를 받자, 남자는 낙타를 바라보며 말했다. \"저는 요르단강에서 돌아오는 길입니다. 지금 그곳에는 먼 곳에서 온 사람들이 많은데, 빛나는 벗이여, 그들도 당신처럼 길을 가는 이들이지요."], ["A very noble animal.", "그러나 그들 가운데 누구도 여기 당신의 종만 한 짐승은 없었습니다."], ["May I ask of what breed he is sprung?”", "참으로 고귀한 짐승이군요. 어떤 혈통에서 났는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["Balthasar answered, and sought his rest; but Ben-Hur, more curious, took up the remark.", "발타사르가 대답하고는 쉬려고 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 더 호기심이 동한 벤허가 그 말을 이어받았다."]], [["“At what place on the river are the people?”", "\"강의 어느 곳에 사람들이 모여 있습니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“At Bethabara.”", "\"베다바라입니다.\""]], [["“It used to be a lonesome ford,”", "\"거기는 본래 인적 드문 나루터였는데요.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“I cannot understand how it can have become of such interest.”", "\"어떻게 그토록 사람들의 관심을 끄는 곳이 되었는지 이해가 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“I see,”", "\"알겠습니다.\""], ["the stranger replied; “you, too, are from abroad, and have not heard the good tidings.”", "낯선 남자가 대답했다. \"당신도 외지에서 온 분이라 그 좋은 소식을 듣지 못했군요.\""]], [["“What tidings?”", "\"무슨 소식 말입니까?\""]], [["“Well, a man has appeared out of the wilderness—a very holy man—with his mouth full of strange words, which take hold of all who hear them.", "\"그게, 한 사람이 광야에서 나타났습니다. 매우 거룩한 사람인데, 입에는 듣는 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡는 기이한 말이 가득합니다."], ["He calls himself John the Nazarite, son of Zacharias, and says he is the messenger sent before the Messiah.”", "자기를 나사렛 사람 요한, 사가랴의 아들이라 부르며, 자기는 메시아보다 앞서 보내심을 받은 사자라고 말합니다.\""]], [["Even Iras listened closely while the man continued:", "이라스조차 귀를 기울이는 가운데 남자는 말을 이었다."]], [["“They say of this John that he has spent his life from childhood in a cave down by En-Gedi, praying and living more strictly than the Essenes.", "\"사람들이 이 요한에 대해 말하기를, 그는 어린 시절부터 엔게디 아래쪽 동굴에서 평생을 보내며 에세네파보다 더 엄격하게 기도하며 살았다고 합니다."], ["Crowds go to hear him preach.", "무리가 그의 설교를 들으러 갑니다."], ["I went to hear him with the rest.”", "저도 다른 사람들과 함께 들으러 갔지요.\""]], [["“Have all these, your friends, been there?”", "\"여기 당신의 벗들도 모두 그곳에 다녀왔습니까?\""]], [["“Most of them are going; a few are coming away.”", "\"대부분은 가는 길이고, 더러는 돌아오는 길입니다.\""]], [["“What does he preach?”", "\"그가 무엇을 설교합니까?\""]], [["“A new doctrine—one never before taught in Israel, as all say.", "\"새로운 교리입니다. 다들 말하기를, 이스라엘에서 일찍이 가르친 적이 없는 것이라 합니다."], ["He calls it repentance and baptism.", "그는 그것을 회개와 세례라 부릅니다."], ["The rabbis do not know what to make of him; nor do we.", "랍비들도 그를 어찌 보아야 할지 모르고, 우리도 모릅니다."], ["Some have asked him if he is the Christ, others if he is Elias; but to them all he has the answer, ‘I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness, Make straight the way of the Lord!’”", "어떤 이들은 그에게 그가 그리스도냐고 물었고, 또 어떤 이들은 그가 엘리야냐고 물었습니다. 그러나 그는 그들 모두에게 이렇게 대답합니다. '나는 광야에서 외치는 자의 소리니, 주의 길을 곧게 하라!'\""]], [["At this point the man was called away by his friends; as he was going, Balthasar spoke.", "이 대목에서 남자는 벗들에게 불려 갔다. 그가 떠나려 할 때 발타사르가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Good stranger!”", "\"착한 나그네여!\""], ["he said, tremulously, “tell us if we shall find the preacher at the place you left him.”", "그가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"당신이 떠나온 그곳에 가면 그 설교자를 만날 수 있을지 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“Yes, at Bethabara.”", "\"네, 베다바라에 있습니다.\""]], [["“Who should this Nazarite be?” said Ben-Hur to Iras, “if not the herald of our King?”", "\"이 나사렛 사람이 우리의 왕을 알리는 전령이 아니라면 도대체 누구겠습니까?\" 벤허가 이라스에게 말했다."]], [["In so short a time he had come to regard the daughter as more interested in the mysterious personage he was looking for than the aged father!", "이토록 짧은 시간에 그는 딸이 늙은 아버지보다 자기가 찾고 있는 그 신비로운 인물에게 더 관심이 많다고 여기게 되었다."], ["Nevertheless, the latter with a positive glow in his sunken eyes half arose, and said,", "그런데도 아버지는 움푹 꺼진 두 눈에 또렷한 빛을 띠며 몸을 반쯤 일으키고 말했다."]], [["“Let us make haste. I am not tired.”", "\"서두릅시다. 나는 피곤하지 않소.\""]], [["They turned away to help the slave.", "그들은 몸을 돌려 노인이 일어서도록 거들었다."]], [["There was little conversation between the three at the stopping-place for the night west of Ramoth-Gilead.", "라못 길르앗 서쪽에서 하룻밤을 묵으려 멈춘 곳에서 세 사람 사이에는 별다른 대화가 없었다."]], [["“Let us arise early, son of Hur,”", "\"일찍 일어납시다, 후르의 아들이여.\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["“The Saviour may come, and we not there.”", "\"구세주께서 오시는데 우리가 그곳에 없을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“The King cannot be far behind his herald,” Iras whispered, as she prepared to take her place on the camel.", "\"왕은 전령의 뒤를 멀리 떨어져 따라오실 리 없어요.\" 이라스가 낙타에 오를 채비를 하며 속삭였다."]], [["“To-morrow we will see!”", "\"내일이면 알게 되겠지요!\""], ["Ben-Hur replied, kissing her hand.", "벤허가 그녀의 손에 입맞추며 대답했다."]], [["Next day about the third hour, out of the pass through which, skirting the base of Mount Gilead, they had journeyed since leaving Ramoth, the party came upon the barren steppe east of the sacred river.", "이튿날 제삼시쯤, 라못을 떠난 뒤로 길르앗산 기슭을 끼고 지나온 산길을 빠져나오자, 일행은 거룩한 강 동쪽의 메마른 초원에 다다랐다."], ["Opposite them they saw the upper limit of the old palm lands of Jericho, stretching off to the hill-country of Judea.", "맞은편으로는 여리고의 오래된 종려나무 땅의 위쪽 경계가 보였는데, 그것은 유대의 구릉지대까지 뻗어 있었다."], ["Ben-Hur’s blood ran quickly, for he knew the ford was close at hand.", "벤허는 나루터가 가까이 있음을 알았기에 피가 빠르게 뛰었다."]], [["“Content you, good Balthasar,”", "\"마음을 놓으시오, 착한 발타사르.\""], ["he said; “we are almost there.”", "그가 말했다. \"이제 거의 다 왔습니다.\""]], [["The driver quickened the camel’s pace.", "낙타 모는 자가 낙타의 걸음을 재촉했다."], ["Soon they caught sight of booths and tents and tethered animals; and then of the river, and a multitude collected down close by the bank, and yet another multitude on the western shore.", "곧 그들은 임시 막사와 천막, 매어 둔 짐승들을 보았다. 그러고는 강과, 강기슭 가까이 모여든 한 무리, 그리고 서쪽 기슭의 또 다른 무리를 보았다."], ["Knowing that the preacher was preaching, they made greater haste; yet, as they were drawing near, suddenly there was a commotion in the mass, and it began to break up and disperse.", "설교자가 설교하고 있음을 알고 그들은 더욱 서둘렀다. 그러나 그들이 가까이 다가가는 사이에, 갑자기 그 무리에 동요가 일더니 흩어지며 헤어지기 시작했다."]], [["They were too late!", "너무 늦었던 것이다!"]], [["“Let us stay here,”", "\"여기 머무릅시다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur to Balthasar, who was wringing his hands.", "벤허가 두 손을 비틀고 있는 발타사르에게 말했다."], ["“The Nazarite may come this way.”", "\"나사렛 사람이 이쪽으로 올지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["The people were too intent upon what they had heard, and too busy in discussion, to notice the new-comers.", "사람들은 자기들이 들은 것에 너무 골몰해 있었고, 논의에 너무 바빠 새로 온 이들을 알아채지 못했다."], ["When some hundreds were gone by, and it seemed the opportunity to so much as see the Nazarite was lost to the latter, up the river not far away they beheld a person coming towards them of such singular appearance they forgot all else.", "수백 명이 지나가고, 이제 나사렛 사람을 보는 일조차 이 일행에게는 놓쳐 버린 기회처럼 여겨질 무렵, 강 위쪽 멀지 않은 곳에서 그들을 향해 다가오는 한 사람이 보였는데, 그 모습이 어찌나 기이한지 그들은 다른 모든 것을 잊었다."]], [["Outwardly the man was rude and uncouth, even savage.", "겉모습으로 보아 그 사람은 거칠고 투박했으며, 야만스럽기까지 했다."], ["Over a thin, gaunt visage of the hue of brown parchment, over his shoulders and down his back below the middle, in witch-like locks, fell a covering of sun-scorched hair.", "갈색 양피지 빛깔의 야위고 수척한 얼굴 위로, 어깨와 등을 타고 허리 아래까지, 마법사 같은 텁수룩한 머리채로, 햇볕에 그을린 머리카락이 덮개처럼 늘어져 있었다."], ["His eyes were burning-bright.", "두 눈은 불타듯 빛났다."], ["All his right side was naked, and of the color of his face, and quite as meagre; a shirt of the coarsest camel’s-hair—coarse as Bedouin tent-cloth—clothed the rest of his person to the knees, being gathered at the waist by a broad girdle of untanned leather.", "오른쪽 몸은 온통 드러나 있었는데, 얼굴과 같은 빛깔이었고 똑같이 비쩍 말라 있었다. 베두인의 천막 천처럼 거친, 가장 거친 낙타털로 지은 옷이 무릎까지 그의 나머지 몸을 가렸고, 허리에는 무두질하지 않은 가죽으로 된 넓은 띠를 둘렀다."], ["His feet were bare.", "발은 맨발이었다."], ["A scrip, also of untanned leather, was fastened to the girdle.", "역시 무두질하지 않은 가죽으로 된 자루가 띠에 매여 있었다."], ["He used a knotted staff to help him forward.", "그는 마디진 지팡이를 짚어 걸음을 도왔다."], ["His movement was quick, decided, and strangely watchful.", "그의 움직임은 빠르고 단호하며 기이하리만치 경계하는 듯했다."], ["Every little while he tossed the unruly hair from his eyes, and peered round as if searching for somebody.", "이따금 그는 흐트러진 머리카락을 눈에서 떨쳐 내고는, 누군가를 찾기라도 하는 듯 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["The fair Egyptian surveyed the son of the Desert with surprise, not to say disgust.", "아리따운 이집트 여인은 놀라움을, 아니 혐오에 가까운 마음으로 그 사막의 아들을 바라보았다."], ["Presently, raising the curtain of the houdah, she spoke to Ben-Hur, who sat his horse near by.", "이윽고 그녀는 가마의 휘장을 들어 올리고, 가까이 말을 타고 있는 벤허에게 말했다."]], [["“Is that the herald of thy King?”", "\"저 사람이 당신 왕의 전령인가요?\""]], [["“It is the Nazarite,”", "\"나사렛 사람입니다.\""], ["he replied, without looking up.", "그가 올려다보지도 않고 대답했다."]], [["In truth, he was himself more than disappointed.", "사실 그 자신도 실망 이상의 감정을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Despite his familiarity with the ascetic colonists in En-Gedi—their dress, their indifference to all worldly opinion, their constancy to vows which gave them over to every imaginable suffering of body, and separated them from others of their kind as absolutely as if they had not been born like them—and notwithstanding he had been notified on the way to look for a Nazarite whose simple description of himself was a Voice from the Wilderness—still Ben-Hur’s dream of the King who was to be so great and do so much had colored all his thought of him, so that he never doubted to find in the forerunner some sign or token of the goodliness and royalty he was announcing.", "엔게디의 금욕적인 정착민들에 대해 잘 알고 있었음에도—그들의 복장, 세상의 모든 평판에 대한 무관심, 상상할 수 있는 온갖 육신의 고통에 몸을 내맡기고 동족의 다른 이들과는 마치 같은 모양으로 태어나지 않은 양 철저히 갈라서게 만드는 그 서원에 대한 한결같음을 알고 있었음에도—그리고 오는 길에 자신을 광야의 소리라고 소박하게 일컫는 나사렛 사람을 찾으라는 일러둠을 받았음에도—그토록 위대하고 그토록 많은 일을 하실 왕에 대한 벤허의 꿈은 그에 관한 모든 생각을 물들여 놓았기에, 그는 그 선구자에게서 그가 알리는 분의 훌륭함과 왕다움을 보여 주는 어떤 표징이나 징표를 찾으리라고 의심치 않았다."], ["Gazing at the savage figure before him, the long trains of courtiers whom he had been used to see in the thermae and imperial corridors at Rome arose before him, forcing a comparison. Shocked, shamed, bewildered, he could only answer,", "눈앞의 그 거친 모습을 바라보자, 로마의 온천장과 황궁 회랑에서 늘 보아 온 신하들의 긴 행렬이 떠올라 자꾸만 견주게 되었다. 충격을 받고, 부끄럽고, 어리둥절한 그는 이렇게밖에 대답할 수 없었다."]], [["“It is the Nazarite.”", "\"나사렛 사람입니다.\""]], [["With Balthasar it was very different.", "발타사르에게는 사정이 사뭇 달랐다."], ["The ways of God, he knew, were not as men would have them.", "그는 하느님의 길이 사람들이 바라는 대로가 아님을 알고 있었다."], ["He had seen the Saviour a child in a manger, and was prepared by his faith for the rude and simple in connection with the Divine reappearance.", "그는 구세주가 구유에 누인 아기였을 때 보았고, 그 신성한 재림과 관련하여 거칠고 소박한 것을 자신의 믿음으로 미리 받아들일 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["So he kept his seat, his hands crossed upon his breast, his lips moving in prayer.", "그래서 그는 자리에 앉은 채 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 입술로 기도를 읊조렸다."], ["He was not expecting a king.", "그는 왕을 기대하고 있지 않았다."]], [["In this time of such interest to the new-comers, and in which they were so differently moved, another man had been sitting by himself on a stone at the edge of the river, thinking yet, probably, of the sermon he had been hearing.", "새로 온 이들에게 이토록 흥미로운, 그리고 그들이 저마다 다르게 마음이 움직이던 이 무렵, 또 한 사람이 강가의 돌 위에 홀로 앉아, 아마도 자기가 들은 설교를 여전히 곱씹으며 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Now, however, he arose, and walked slowly up from the shore, in a course to take him across the line the Nazarite was pursuing and bring him near the camel.", "그러나 이제 그가 일어나 기슭에서 천천히 걸어 올라왔는데, 그 길은 나사렛 사람이 가는 방향을 가로질러 그를 낙타 가까이로 데려올 진로였다."]], [["And the two—the preacher and the stranger—kept on until they came, the former within twenty yards of the animal, the latter within ten feet.", "그리고 그 두 사람—설교자와 낯선 이—은 계속 나아가, 마침내 앞사람은 짐승에게서 스무 걸음 안쪽에, 뒷사람은 열 발짝 안쪽에 이르렀다."], ["Then the preacher stopped, and flung the hair from his eyes, looked at the stranger, threw his hands up as a signal to all the people in sight; and they also stopped, each in the pose of a listener; and when the hush was perfect, slowly the staff in the Nazarite’s right hand came down and pointed to the stranger.", "그러자 설교자가 걸음을 멈추고 눈에서 머리카락을 떨쳐 내고는, 낯선 이를 바라보며, 보이는 모든 사람에게 신호를 보내듯 두 손을 치켜들었다. 그러자 사람들도 저마다 귀 기울이는 자세로 걸음을 멈추었다. 그리고 정적이 완전해지자, 나사렛 사람의 오른손에 든 지팡이가 천천히 내려와 낯선 이를 가리켰다."]], [["All those who before were but listeners became watchers also.", "조금 전까지 그저 듣기만 하던 모든 이들이 이제 지켜보는 자들도 되었다."]], [["At the same instant, under the same impulse, Balthasar and Ben-Hur fixed their gaze upon the man pointed out, and both took the same impression, only in different degree.", "같은 순간, 같은 충동에 이끌려 발타사르와 벤허는 지목된 그 사람에게 시선을 못 박았고, 둘 다 같은 인상을, 다만 그 정도만 다르게 받았다."], ["He was moving slowly towards them in a clear space a little to their front, a form slightly above the average in stature, and slender, even delicate.", "그는 그들의 조금 앞쪽 트인 공간에서 천천히 그들을 향해 다가오고 있었는데, 키는 보통보다 조금 크고, 호리호리하며, 가냘프기까지 한 몸이었다."], ["His action was calm and deliberate, like that habitual to men much given to serious thought upon grave subjects; and it well became his costume, which was an undergarment full-sleeved and reaching to the ankles, and an outer robe called the talith; on his left arm he carried the usual handkerchief for the head, the red fillet swinging loose down his side.", "그의 거동은 차분하고 신중했으니, 진중한 문제를 두고 깊이 사색하는 데 익숙한 사람들에게 몸에 밴 그런 태도였다. 그것은 그의 복장과도 잘 어울렸는데, 그 옷은 소매가 길고 발목까지 닿는 속옷과, 탈리트라 불리는 겉옷이었다. 그의 왼팔에는 머리에 두르는 흔한 수건이 걸려 있었고, 붉은 끈이 옆구리로 느슨하게 늘어져 있었다."], ["Except the fillet and a narrow border of blue at the lower edge of the talith, his attire was of linen yellowed with dust and road stains.", "그 끈과 탈리트 아랫단의 좁은 파란 가두리를 빼면, 그의 옷은 먼지와 길의 얼룩으로 누렇게 바랜 아마포였다."], ["Possibly the exception should be extended to the tassels, which were blue and white, as prescribed by law for rabbis.", "어쩌면 그 예외에는 술도 더해야 할 터인데, 그것은 랍비를 위해 율법이 정한 대로 파랑과 하양이었다."], ["His sandals were of the simplest kind.", "그의 샌들은 더없이 소박한 것이었다."], ["He was without scrip or girdle or staff.", "그는 자루도 띠도 지팡이도 지니지 않았다."]], [["These points of appearance, however, the three beholders observed briefly, and rather as accessories to the head and face of the man, which—especially the latter—were the real sources of the spell they caught in common with all who stood looking at him.", "그러나 세 구경꾼은 이런 외양의 세부들을 잠깐만 살폈을 뿐, 오히려 그것을 그 사람의 머리와 얼굴에 딸린 부속물쯤으로 여겼다. 그 머리와 얼굴이—특히 얼굴이—그를 바라보고 선 모든 이들과 더불어 그들이 함께 사로잡힌 그 마력의 진짜 원천이었던 것이다."]], [["The head was open to the cloudless light, except as it was draped with hair long and slightly waved, and parted in the middle, and auburn in tint, with a tendency to reddish golden where most strongly touched by the sun.", "그 머리는 구름 한 점 없는 빛에 활짝 드러나 있었으나, 길고 살짝 물결치며 한가운데서 갈라진 적갈색 머리카락이 그것을 덮고 있었는데, 햇볕이 가장 세게 닿은 곳은 불그스레한 금빛을 띠는 경향이 있었다."], ["Under a broad, low forehead, under black well arched brows, beamed eyes dark-blue and large, and softened to exceeding tenderness by lashes of the great length sometimes seen on children, but seldom, if ever, on men.", "넓고 낮은 이마 아래, 검고 잘 휘어진 눈썹 아래로, 짙푸르고 큰 두 눈이 빛났으니, 아이들에게서나 이따금 볼 수 있을 뿐 어른에게서는 좀처럼, 아니 거의 볼 수 없는 그 긴 속눈썹 덕에 더없이 부드러워져 있었다."], ["As to the other features, it would have been difficult to decide whether they were Greek or Jewish.", "다른 이목구비로 말하면, 그것이 그리스적인지 유대적인지 판단하기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["The delicacy of the nostrils and mouth was unusual to the latter type; and when it was taken into account with the gentleness of the eyes, the pallor of the complexion, the fine texture of the hair, and the softness of the beard, which fell in waves over his throat to his breast, never a soldier but would have laughed at him in encounter, never a woman who would not have confided in him at sight, never a child that would not, with quick instinct, have given him its hand and whole artless trust; nor might any one have said he was not beautiful.", "콧방울과 입의 섬세함은 유대인의 모습에서는 드문 것이었다. 그리고 그 눈의 온화함, 안색의 창백함, 머리카락의 고운 결, 그리고 목을 타고 가슴까지 물결치며 흘러내린 수염의 부드러움까지 함께 헤아리면, 어떤 병사도 맞붙어 싸우는 자리에서 그를 비웃지 않을 수 없었을 것이요, 어떤 여인도 한눈에 그를 믿지 않을 수 없었을 것이며, 어떤 아이도 재빠른 본능으로 그에게 제 손을 내밀어 천진한 신뢰를 송두리째 맡기지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 또한 누구도 그가 아름답지 않다고는 말하지 못했으리라."]], [["The features, it should be further said, were ruled by a certain expression which, as the viewer chose, might with equal correctness have been called the effect of intelligence, love, pity, or sorrow; though, in better speech, it was a blending of them all—a look easy to fancy as the mark of a sinless soul doomed to the sight and understanding of the utter sinfulness of those among whom it was passing; yet withal no one could have observed the face with a thought of weakness in the man; so, at least, would not they who know that the qualities mentioned—love, sorrow, pity—are the results of a consciousness of strength to bear suffering oftener than strength to do; such has been the might of martyrs and devotees and the myriads written down in saintly calendars.", "덧붙여 말해 두자면, 그 이목구비는 어떤 표정에 다스려지고 있었으니, 보는 이에 따라 지성이라 불러도, 사랑이라 불러도, 연민이라 불러도, 슬픔이라 불러도 똑같이 옳았을 표정이었다. 그러나 더 나은 말로 하자면 그것은 그 모두가 한데 어우러진 것이었다—죄 없는 영혼이 자기가 지나가는 무리의 더할 나위 없는 죄악을 보고 깨달을 운명에 처한 듯한, 그런 표정으로 떠올리기 쉬운 모습이었다. 그러면서도 그 누구도 그 얼굴을 보며 그 사람에게 나약함이 있다고는 생각하지 못했을 것이다."], ["And such, indeed, was the air of this one.", "적어도, 앞서 말한 그 자질들—사랑, 슬픔, 연민—이 무언가를 행할 힘보다는 고난을 견딜 힘을 의식하는 데서 비롯되는 경우가 더 많다는 것을 아는 이들은 그러지 않았으리라. 순교자들과 신앙의 헌신자들, 그리고 성인의 명부에 적힌 무수한 이들의 위력이 그러했던 것이다. 그리고 이 사람의 분위기 또한 참으로 그러했다."]], [["Slowly he drew near—nearer the three.", "그는 천천히 다가왔다—세 사람에게 더 가까이."]], [["Now Ben-Hur, mounted and spear in hand, was an object to claim the glance of a king; yet the eyes of the man approaching were all the time raised above him—and not to Iras, whose loveliness has been so often remarked, but to Balthasar, the old and unserviceable.", "그런데 말을 타고 창을 든 벤허는 왕의 눈길을 끌 만한 대상이었다. 그러나 다가오는 사람의 눈은 줄곧 그의 위쪽으로 들려 있었으니—그 아름다움이 그토록 자주 언급된 이라스에게가 아니라, 늙고 쓸모없어진 발타사르에게 향해 있었다."]], [["The hush was profound.", "정적이 깊었다."]], [["Presently the Nazarite, still pointing with his staff, cried, in a loud voice,", "이윽고 나사렛 사람이 여전히 지팡이로 가리키며 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world!”", "\"보라, 세상 죄를 지고 가는 하느님의 어린양이로다!\""]], [["The many standing still, arrested by the action of the speaker, and listening for what might follow, were struck with awe by words so strange and past their understanding; upon Balthasar they were overpowering.", "말하는 이의 동작에 붙들려 멈춰 선 채 다음에 무슨 일이 이어질까 귀 기울이던 많은 이들은, 너무도 기이하고 자기네 이해를 넘어선 그 말에 경외에 사로잡혔다. 발타사르에게 그 말은 압도적이었다."], ["He was there to see once more the Redeemer of men.", "그는 사람들의 구속자를 다시 한번 보기 위해 그곳에 있었던 것이다."], ["The faith which had brought him the singular privileges of the time long gone abode yet in his heart; and if now it gave him a power of vision above that of his fellows—a power to see and know him for whom he was looking—better than calling the power a miracle, let it be thought of as the faculty of a soul not yet entirely released from the divine relations to which it had been formerly admitted, or as the fitting reward of a life in that age so without examples of holiness—a life itself a miracle.", "오래전 그에게 그 보기 드문 특권을 가져다준 그 믿음이 여전히 그의 가슴에 머물러 있었다. 그리고 만약 그 믿음이 이제 그에게 동료들의 것을 뛰어넘는 봄의 능력을—자기가 찾고 있던 분을 보고 알아보는 능력을—주었다면, 그 능력을 기적이라 부르기보다는, 일찍이 받아들여진 신성한 관계에서 아직 온전히 풀려나지 않은 영혼의 기능이라 여기거나, 혹은 거룩함의 본보기가 그토록 없던 시대에 그 자체가 하나의 기적인 한 생애에 주어진 합당한 보답이라 여기는 편이 나을 것이다."], ["The ideal of his faith was before him, perfect in face, form, dress, action, age; and he was in its view, and the view was recognition.", "그 믿음의 이상이 그의 눈앞에 있었으니, 얼굴도, 모습도, 옷차림도, 거동도, 나이도 완벽했다. 그리고 그는 그 이상의 시야 안에 있었고, 그 시선은 알아봄이었다."], ["Ah, now if something should happen to identify the stranger beyond all doubt!", "아, 이제 무언가 일이 일어나 이 낯선 이를 한 점 의심도 없이 확인해 준다면!"]], [["And that was what did happen.", "그리고 바로 그 일이 일어났다."]], [["Exactly at the fitting moment, as if to assure the trembling Egyptian, the Nazarite repeated the outcry,", "꼭 알맞은 순간에, 떨고 있는 이집트인을 안심시키려는 듯, 나사렛 사람이 그 외침을 되풀이했다."]], [["“Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world!”", "\"보라, 세상 죄를 지고 가는 하느님의 어린양이로다!\""]], [["Balthasar fell upon his knees.", "발타사르가 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["For him there was no need of explanation; and as if the Nazarite knew it, he turned to those more immediately about him staring in wonder, and continued:", "그에게는 더 이상의 설명이 필요 없었다. 그리고 나사렛 사람도 그것을 안다는 듯, 자기 가까이서 놀라움에 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고 바라보는 이들을 향해 돌아서서 말을 이었다."]], [["“This is he of whom I said, After me cometh a man which is preferred before me, for he was before me.", "\"이분은 내가 전에 말하기를, 내 뒤에 오는 사람이 나보다 앞선 것은 나보다 먼저 계셨음이라 한 바로 그분이라."], ["And I knew him not: but that he should be manifest to Israel, therefore am I come baptizing with water.", "나도 그를 알지 못하였으나, 그가 이스라엘에 나타나시게 하려고 내가 와서 물로 세례를 베푸노라."], ["I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove, and it abode upon him.", "나는 성령이 하늘로부터 비둘기같이 내려와 그 위에 머무는 것을 보았노라."], ["And I knew him not: but he that sent me to baptize with water, the same said unto me, Upon whom thou shalt see the Spirit descending and remaining on him, the same is he which baptizeth with the Holy Ghost.", "나도 그를 알지 못하였으나, 나를 보내어 물로 세례를 베풀게 하신 그분이 내게 말씀하시기를, 성령이 내려서 누구 위에 머무는 것을 보거든 그가 곧 성령으로 세례를 베푸는 이라 하셨느니라."], ["And I saw and bare record, that this”—he paused, his staff still pointing at the stranger in the white garments, as if to give a more absolute certainty to both his words and the conclusions intended—“I bare record, THAT THIS IS THE SON OF GOD!”", "내가 보고 증언하노니, 이는\"—그가 말을 멈추었으나, 지팡이는 여전히 흰옷 입은 낯선 이를 가리킨 채였으니, 자기 말과 거기에 담긴 결론 모두에 더 절대적인 확실함을 부여하려는 듯했다—\"내가 증언하노니, 이는 하느님의 아들이시니라!\""]], [["“It is he, it is he!”", "\"그분이다, 그분이야!\""], ["Balthasar cried, with upraised tearful eyes.", "발타사르가 눈물 어린 눈을 치켜뜨며 외쳤다."], ["Next moment he sank down insensible.", "다음 순간 그는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."]], [["In this time, it should be remembered, Ben-Hur was studying the face of the stranger, though with an interest entirely different.", "기억해 둘 것은, 이 무렵 벤허도 그 낯선 이의 얼굴을 살피고 있었다는 사실이다. 다만 그 관심은 전혀 다른 것이었다."], ["He was not insensible to its purity of feature, and its thoughtfulness, tenderness, humility, and holiness; but just then there was room in his mind for but one thought—Who is this man? And what?", "그도 그 이목구비의 깨끗함과, 그 사색적인 면모, 부드러움, 겸손함, 거룩함에 무감각하지는 않았다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그의 마음에는 단 하나의 생각만이 들어설 자리가 있었다—이 사람은 누구인가?"], ["Messiah or king?", "그리고 무엇인가?"], ["Never was apparition more unroyal.", "메시아인가, 왕인가?"], ["Nay, looking at that calm, benignant countenance, the very idea of war and conquest, and lust of dominion, smote him like a profanation.", "이토록 왕답지 않은 형상은 일찍이 없었다. 아니, 그 차분하고 자애로운 얼굴을 바라보자, 전쟁과 정복, 지배를 향한 탐욕이라는 생각 자체가 신성모독처럼 그를 후려쳤다."], ["He said, as if speaking to his own heart, Balthasar must be right and Simonides wrong.", "그는 마치 자기 마음에 대고 말하듯 이렇게 되뇌었다. 발타사르가 옳고 시모니데스가 틀린 것이 분명하다."], ["This man has not come to rebuild the throne of Solomon; he has neither the nature nor the genius of Herod; king he may be, but not of another and greater than Rome.", "이 사람은 솔로몬의 왕좌를 다시 세우러 온 것이 아니다. 그에게는 헤롯의 본성도, 헤롯의 수완도 없다. 왕일지는 모르나, 로마보다 또 다른 더 위대한 나라의 왕은 아니다."]], [["It should be understood now that this was not a conclusion with Ben-Hur, but an impression merely; and while it was forming, while yet he gazed at the wonderful countenance, his memory began to throe and struggle.", "이제 알아 두어야 할 것은, 이것이 벤허에게 하나의 결론이 아니라 그저 인상일 뿐이었다는 점이다. 그리고 그 인상이 형성되어 가는 동안, 그가 여전히 그 경이로운 얼굴을 응시하는 동안, 그의 기억이 꿈틀거리며 몸부림치기 시작했다."], ["“Surely,”", "\"분명히,\""], ["he said to himself, “I have seen the man; but where and when?”", "그는 속으로 말했다. \"나는 저 사람을 본 적이 있다. 그런데 어디서, 언제였던가?\""], ["That the look, so calm, so pitiful, so loving, had somewhere in a past time beamed upon him as that moment it was beaming upon Balthasar became an assurance.", "그토록 차분하고, 그토록 가엾이 여기고, 그토록 사랑이 가득한 그 눈길이, 지금 발타사르에게 비치는 것처럼 지난 어느 때 어딘가에서 자기에게도 비쳤었다는 것이 하나의 확신으로 다가왔다."], ["Faintly at first, at last a clear light, a burst of sunshine, the scene by the well at Nazareth what time the Roman guard was dragging him to the galleys returned, and all his being thrilled.", "처음에는 희미하게, 마침내는 또렷한 빛으로, 햇살이 터지듯, 로마 호위병이 그를 갤리선으로 끌고 가던 그때 나사렛 우물가의 그 장면이 되살아났고, 그의 온 존재가 전율했다."], ["Those hands had helped him when he was perishing.", "그 손이 그가 스러져 가던 때에 그를 도와주었던 것이다."], ["The face was one of the pictures he had carried in mind ever since.", "그 얼굴은 그가 그 후로 줄곧 마음에 품어 온 그림들 가운데 하나였다."], ["In the effusion of feeling excited, the explanation of the preacher was lost by him, all but the last words—words so marvellous that the world yet rings with them:", "북받쳐 오른 감정의 격류 속에서, 그는 설교자의 말을 놓치고 말았으니, 다만 마지막 말만은—세상이 아직도 그 울림을 간직하고 있는 그토록 놀라운 그 말만은 들었다."]], [["“—this is the SON OF GOD!”", "\"—이는 하느님의 아들이시니라!\""]], [["Ben-Hur leaped from his horse to render homage to his benefactor; but Iras cried to him, “Help, son of Hur, help, or my father will die!”", "벤허는 자기 은인에게 경의를 표하려고 말에서 뛰어내렸다. 그러나 이라스가 그에게 외쳤다. \"도와줘요, 후르의 아들이여, 도와줘요. 안 그러면 아버지가 돌아가시겠어요!\""]], [["He stopped, looked back, then hurried to her assistance.", "그는 멈춰 서서 돌아보고는, 서둘러 그녀를 도우러 갔다."], ["She gave him a cup; and leaving the slave to bring the camel to its knees, he ran to the river for water.", "그녀가 그에게 잔을 건넸다. 그리고 종에게 낙타를 꿇어앉히는 일을 맡긴 채, 그는 물을 뜨러 강으로 달려갔다."], ["The stranger was gone when he came back.", "그가 돌아왔을 때 그 낯선 이는 떠나고 없었다."]], [["At last Balthasar was restored to consciousness.", "마침내 발타사르가 의식을 되찾았다."], ["Stretching forth his hands, he asked, feebly, “Where is he?”", "두 손을 앞으로 뻗으며 그는 힘없이 물었다. \"그분은 어디 계시오?\""]], [["“Who?” asked Iras.", "\"누구 말씀이세요?\" 이라스가 물었다."]], [["An intense instant interest shone upon the good man’s face, as if a last wish had been gratified, and he answered,", "마치 마지막 소원이 이루어지기라도 한 듯, 그 선량한 노인의 얼굴에 강렬하고도 순간적인 관심이 빛났다. 그가 대답했다."]], [["“He—the Redeemer—the Son of God, whom I have seen again.”", "\"그분 말이오—구속자—내가 다시 뵌 하느님의 아들 말이오.\""]], [["“Believest thou so?”", "\"정말 그렇게 믿으세요?\""], ["Iras asked in a low voice of Ben-Hur.", "이라스가 낮은 목소리로 벤허에게 물었다."]], [["“The time is full of wonders; let us wait,”", "\"이 시대는 경이로 가득하오. 기다려 봅시다.\""], ["was all he said.", "그가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["And next day while the three were listening to him, the Nazarite broke off in mid-speech, saying reverently, “Behold the Lamb of God!”", "그리고 이튿날 세 사람이 그의 말을 듣고 있을 때, 나사렛 사람이 말을 하다 말고 멈추더니 경건하게 말했다. \"보라, 하느님의 어린양이로다!\""]], [["Looking to where he pointed, they beheld the stranger again.", "그가 가리키는 곳을 보자, 그들은 그 낯선 이를 다시 보았다."], ["As Ben-Hur surveyed the slender figure, and holy beautiful countenance compassionate to sadness, a new idea broke upon him.", "벤허가 그 호리호리한 모습과, 슬픔에 잠길 만큼 가엾이 여기는 거룩하고 아름다운 얼굴을 살피는 사이, 새로운 생각 하나가 그에게 떠올랐다."]], [["“Balthasar is right—so is Simonides.", "\"발타사르가 옳다—시모니데스도 옳다."], ["May not the Redeemer be a king also?”", "구속자가 왕이기도 하지 말란 법이 어디 있는가?\""]], [["And he asked one at his side, “Who is the man walking yonder?”", "그리고 그는 곁에 있는 한 사람에게 물었다. \"저기 걸어가는 사람이 누구입니까?\""]], [["The other laughed mockingly, and replied,", "상대는 비웃듯 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["“He is the son of a carpenter over in Nazareth.”", "\"저 너머 나사렛에 사는 목수의 아들이지요.\""]], [["# BOOK EIGHTH", "# BOOK EIGHTH"]], [["“Who could resist? Who in this universe? She did so breathe ambrosia, so immerse My fine existence in a golden clime.", "“누가 능히 거역할 수 있으랴?"], ["She took me like a child of suckling-time, And cradled me in roses.", "이 우주에 그 누가?"], ["Thus condemn’d, The current of my former life was stemm’d, And to this arbitrary queen of sense I bow’d a tranced vassal.”—KEATS, Endymion.", "그녀는 신의 음식을 내쉬듯 숨 쉬며, 나의 고귀한 존재를"]], [["“I am the resurrection and the life.” CHAPTER I", "황금빛 하늘에 그토록 깊이 잠기게 하였다."]], [["“Esther—Esther! Speak to the servant below that he may bring me a cup of water.”", "그녀는 나를 젖먹이 어린아이처럼 안아\n장미꽃 요람에 뉘었다. 이리하여 정죄받아,"]], [["“Would you not rather have wine, father?”", "내 지난 삶의 물줄기는 막히고 말았으니,"]], [["“Let him bring both.”", "이 제멋대로인 감각의 여왕에게"]], [["This was in the summer-house upon the roof of the old palace of the Hurs in Jerusalem. From the parapet overlooking the court-yard Esther called to a man in waiting there; at the same moment another man-servant came up the steps and saluted respectfully.", "나는 황홀에 사로잡힌 신하로서 머리를 조아렸다.”—키츠, 「엔디미온」."]], [["“A package for the master,”", "“나는 부활이요 생명이니.”"], ["he said, giving her a letter enclosed in linen cloth, tied and sealed.", "“에스더야—에스더야! 아래에 있는 하인에게 일러 물 한 잔만 가져오라고 하여라.”"]], [["For the satisfaction of the reader, we stop to say that it is the twenty-first day of March, nearly three years after the annunciation of the Christ at Bethabara.", "“포도주가 낫지 않으시겠어요, 아버지?” “둘 다 가져오라고 하려무나.”"]], [["In the meanwhile, Malluch, acting for Ben-Hur, who could not longer endure the emptiness and decay of his father’s house, had bought it from Pontius Pilate; and, in process of repair, gates, courts, lewens, stairways, terraces, rooms, and roof had been cleansed and thoroughly restored; not only was there no reminder left of the tragic circumstances so ruinous to the family, but the refurnishment was in a style richer than before.", "이곳은 예루살렘에 있는 후르 가문의 옛 저택, 그 지붕 위에 마련된 정자였다. 안마당이 내려다보이는 난간에서 에스더는 그곳에 대기하고 있던 한 사람을 불렀다. 바로 그때 또 다른 하인 하나가 계단을 올라와 공손히 인사했다."], ["At every point, indeed, a visitor was met by evidences of the higher tastes acquired by the young proprietor during his years of residence in the villa by Misenum and in the Roman capital.", "“주인님께 온 소포입니다.” 그는 아마포에 싸여 끈으로 묶이고 봉인된 편지 하나를 그녀에게 건네며 말했다."]], [["Now it should not be inferred from this explanation that Ben-Hur had publicly assumed ownership of the property.", "독자의 이해를 돕기 위해 잠시 멈추어 말해 두자면, 지금은 3월 21일로, 베다바라에서 그리스도가 선포된 지 거의 3년이 지난 때다."], ["In his opinion, the hour for that was not yet come. Neither had he yet taken his proper name.", "그동안 아버지의 집이 텅 비고 허물어져 가는 모습을 더는 견딜 수 없었던 벤허를 대신해 말루크가 본디오 빌라도에게서 그 집을 사들였다."], ["Passing the time in the labors of preparation in Galilee, he waited patiently the action of the Nazarene, who became daily more and more a mystery to him, and by prodigies done, often before his eyes, kept him in a state of anxious doubt both as to his character and mission.", "그리고 수리를 하는 과정에서 대문과 안마당, 회랑과 계단, 테라스와 방과 지붕까지 모두 깨끗이 손질되고 철저히 복구되었다. 가문을 파멸로 몰아넣은 그 비극적 사연을 떠올리게 하는 자취가 하나도 남지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 새로 갖춘 살림은 예전보다도 더 호화로운 양식이었다."], ["Occasionally he came up to the Holy City, stopping at the paternal house; always, however, as a stranger and a guest.", "실로 어디를 둘러보든, 찾아온 이는 젊은 주인이 미세눔의 별장과 로마의 수도에서 여러 해 머무는 동안 익힌 한층 높은 안목의 증거와 마주치게 되었다."]], [["These visits of Ben-Hur, it should also be observed, were for more than mere rest from labor.", "그렇다고 이 설명에서 벤허가 그 재산의 소유권을 공공연히 떠맡았다고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 그의 생각으로는 아직 그럴 때가 오지 않았다. 또한 그는 아직 자신의 본래 이름을 쓰지도 않았다."], ["Balthasar and Iras made their home in the palace; and the charm of the daughter was still upon him with all its original freshness, while the father, though feebler in body, held him an unflagging listener to speeches of astonishing power, urging the divinity of the wandering miracle-worker of whom they were all so expectant.", "갈릴리에서 준비의 노고로 시간을 보내며, 그는 나사렛 사람의 움직임을 끈기 있게 기다렸다. 그 사람은 날이 갈수록 그에게 더욱더 수수께끼가 되어 갔으니, 흔히 그의 눈앞에서 행해지는 이적들로 말미암아 그의 본성과 사명 양쪽 모두에 대해 그를 불안한 의심의 상태에 머물게 했다. 그는 이따금 거룩한 도성으로 올라와 아버지의 집에 묵었으나, 언제나 한낱 낯선 손님으로서였다."]], [["As to Simonides and Esther, they had arrived from Antioch only a few days before this their reappearance—a wearisome journey to the merchant, borne, as he had been, in a palanquin swung between two camels, which, in their careening, did not always keep the same step.", "벤허의 이런 방문이 단지 노동에서 벗어나 쉬기 위한 것만은 아니었음도 일러두어야겠다. 발타사르와 이라스가 저택에 거처를 두고 있었던 것이다. 그 딸의 매력은 처음 그대로의 신선함을 간직한 채 여전히 그를 사로잡고 있었고, 한편 아버지는 몸은 더 쇠약해졌으나, 그들 모두가 그토록 기다리는 저 떠도는 기적의 행자(行者)가 신성을 지녔음을 역설하는, 놀라운 힘이 깃든 그 이야기에 그를 지칠 줄 모르는 청자로 붙들어 두었다."], ["But now that he was come, the good man, it seemed, could not see enough of his native land.", "시모니데스와 에스더로 말하자면, 이번에 이렇게 다시 모습을 보이기 불과 며칠 전 안디옥에서 막 도착한 참이었다."], ["He delighted in the perch upon the roof, and spent most of his day hours there seated in an arm-chair, the duplicate of that one kept for him in the cabinet over the store-house by the Orontes.", "그것은 상인에게 고된 여정이었으니, 그는 두 마리 낙타 사이에 매달린 가마에 실려 왔는데, 낙타들은 이리저리 기우뚱거리며 늘 발걸음을 맞추지는 못했다. 그러나 막상 와 보니, 이 선량한 사람은 고향 땅을 아무리 보아도 성에 차지 않는 듯했다. 그는 지붕 위 높은 자리를 즐겨, 하루의 낮 시간 대부분을 그곳에서 안락의자에 앉아 보냈는데, 그것은 오론테스 강가 창고 위 사실(私室)에 마련된 그 의자와 똑같은 것이었다."], ["In the shade of the summer-house he could drink fully of the inspiring air lying lightly upon the familiar hills; he could better watch the sun rise, run its course, and set as it used to in the far-gone, not a habit lost; and with Esther by him it was so much easier up there close to the sky, to bring back the other Esther, his love in youth, his wife, dearer growing with the passage of years.", "정자의 그늘에서 그는 낯익은 언덕들 위에 가볍게 깔린, 영감을 불어넣는 공기를 한껏 들이마실 수 있었다. 그곳에서는 해가 떠올라 제 길을 달려 저무는 모습을, 지난날 그러했던 대로 더 잘 바라볼 수 있었으니, 한 가지 습관도 잃지 않았다. 그리고 에스더가 곁에 있으면, 하늘 가까운 그 높은 곳에서는 또 다른 에스더, 곧 젊은 날의 연인이요 그의 아내이며 세월이 흐를수록 더욱 소중해지는 그 사람을 떠올리기가 한결 수월했다."], ["And yet he was not unmindful of business.", "그러면서도 그는 일을 소홀히 하지 않았다."], ["Every day a messenger brought him a despatch from Sanballat, in charge of the big commerce behind; and every day a despatch left him for Sanballat with directions of such minuteness of detail as to exclude all judgment save his own, and all chances except those the Almighty has refused to submit to the most mindful of men.", "날마다 전령 하나가 뒤에 남겨 둔 큰 상업을 맡아보는 산발랏에게서 전갈을 가져왔고, 날마다 전갈 하나가 그에게서 산발랏에게로 떠났는데, 그 지시는 세세하기 이를 데 없어 그 자신의 판단 말고는 다른 어떤 판단도, 그리고 전능하신 분이 가장 사려 깊은 사람에게조차 맡기기를 거부하신 우연 말고는 다른 어떤 우연도 끼어들 여지가 없었다."]], [["As Esther started in return to the summer-house, the sunlight fell softly upon the dustless roof, showing her a woman now—small, graceful in form, of regular features, rosy with youth and health, bright with intelligence, beautiful with the outshining of a devoted nature—a woman to be loved because loving was a habit of life irrepressible with her.", "에스더가 정자로 돌아서려는데, 햇빛이 티끌 하나 없는 지붕 위로 부드럽게 내려앉아 그녀의 모습을 비추었다. 이제 그녀는 어엿한 여인이었다—몸집은 작으나 자태가 우아하고, 이목구비가 단정하며, 젊음과 건강으로 발그레하고, 지성으로 빛나며, 헌신적인 천성이 밖으로 비쳐 나와 아름다운—사랑하는 일이 도무지 억누를 수 없는 삶의 습관이었기에 누구에게나 사랑받을 만한 여인이었다."]], [["She looked at the package as she turned, paused, looked at it a second time more closely than at first; and the blood rose reddening her cheeks—the seal was Ben-Hur’s.", "돌아서며 그녀는 그 소포를 보았다. 걸음을 멈추고 처음보다 더 가까이 다시 한번 들여다보았다. 그러자 피가 솟구쳐 두 뺨을 붉게 물들였다—봉인이 벤허의 것이었다."], ["With quickened steps she hastened on.", "그녀는 발걸음을 재촉해 서둘러 걸어갔다."]], [["Simonides held the package a moment while he also inspected the seal.", "시모니데스는 그 역시 봉인을 살피느라 소포를 잠시 손에 들고 있었다."], ["Breaking it open, he gave her the roll it contained.", "그것을 뜯어, 안에 든 두루마리를 그녀에게 건넸다."]], [["“Read,”", "“읽어 보아라.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["His eyes were upon her as he spoke, and instantly a troubled expression fell upon his own face.", "그가 말하는 동안 그의 눈은 그녀에게 가 있었는데, 이내 그의 얼굴에도 근심스러운 빛이 어렸다."]], [["“You know who it is from, I see, Esther.”", "“누구한테서 온 것인지 알고 있구나, 에스더. 보아하니 그렇구나.”"]], [["“Yes—from—our master.”", "“네—저—주인님한테서요.”"]], [["Though the manner was halting, she met his gaze with modest sincerity.", "말투는 더듬거렸으나, 그녀는 정숙한 진솔함으로 그의 시선을 마주했다."], ["Slowly his chin sank into the roll of flesh puffed out under it like a cushion.", "그의 턱이 천천히 그 아래 방석처럼 부풀어 오른 살의 두툼한 주름 속으로 묻혔다."]], [["“You love him, Esther,”", "“너는 그를 사랑하는구나, 에스더.”"], ["he said, quietly.", "그가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Have you thought well of what you do?”", "“네가 하는 일에 대해 깊이 생각해 보았느냐?”"]], [["“I have tried not to think of him, father, except as the master to whom I am dutifully bound.", "“그분을 생각하지 않으려고 애썼어요, 아버지. 제가 도리로써 매여 있는 주인 이상으로는요."], ["The effort has not helped me to strength.”", "하지만 그 애씀이 저를 강하게 만들어 주지는 못했어요.”"]], [["“A good girl, a good girl, even as thy mother was,”", "“착한 아이로구나, 착한 아이야, 꼭 네 어미 같구나.”"], ["he said, dropping into reverie, from which she roused him by unrolling the paper.", "그가 이렇게 말하며 회상에 잠겼는데, 그녀가 종이를 펼치는 바람에 그는 거기서 깨어났다."]], [["“The Lord forgive me, but—but thy love might not have been vainly given had I kept fast hold of all I had, as I might have done—such power is there in money!”", "“주님께서 나를 용서하시기를. 하지만—하지만 내가 가졌던 모든 것을, 능히 그럴 수 있었던 대로 단단히 붙들고 있었더라면 네 사랑이 헛되이 바쳐지지는 않았을지도 모른다—돈에는 그만한 힘이 있는 법이니!”"]], [["“It would have been worse for me had you done so, father; for then I had been unworthy a look from him, and without pride in you.", "“그러셨다면 저로서는 더 나빴을 거예요, 아버지. 그랬다면 저는 그분의 눈길 한 번 받을 자격도 없었을 테고, 아버지를 자랑스러워할 수도 없었을 테니까요."], ["Shall I not read now?”", "이제 읽어도 될까요?”"]], [["“In a moment,” he said.", "“잠깐만.” 그가 말했다."], ["“Let me, for your sake, my child, show you the worst.", "“내 아이야, 너를 위해 가장 나쁜 것을 먼저 보여 주마."], ["Seeing it with me may make it less terrible to you.", "나와 함께 그것을 보면 네게 덜 끔찍해질지도 모르겠구나."], ["His love, Esther, is all bestowed.”", "그의 사랑은, 에스더, 이미 온전히 다른 데 바쳐졌단다.”"]], [["“I know it,”", "“알고 있어요.”"], ["she said, calmly.", "그녀가 차분히 말했다."]], [["“The Egyptian has him in her net,”", "“그 이집트 여자가 그를 제 그물에 가두어 놓았다.”"], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“She has the cunning of her race, with beauty to help her—much beauty, great cunning; but, like her race again, no heart.", "“그 여자는 제 핏줄다운 교활함을 지녔고, 거기에 미모까지 더했으니—대단한 미모에, 대단한 교활함이지. 하지만 또한 제 핏줄답게 가슴은 없는 여자다."], ["The daughter who despises her father will bring her husband to grief.”", "제 아비를 업신여기는 딸은 제 남편을 비탄에 빠뜨리고 말 것이다.”"]], [["“Does she that?”", "“그 여자가 그래요?”"]], [["Simonides went on:", "시모니데스가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Balthasar is a wise man who has been wonderfully favored for a Gentile, and his faith becomes him; yet she makes a jest of it.", "“발타사르는 슬기로운 사람으로, 이방인치고는 놀랍도록 은총을 입었고, 그 신앙이 그에게 잘 어울린다. 그런데도 그 여자는 그것을 비웃음거리로 삼는구나."], ["I heard her say, speaking of him yesterday, ‘The follies of youth are excusable; nothing is admirable in the aged except wisdom, and when that goes from them, they should die.’ A cruel speech, fit for a Roman.", "어제 그 여자가 아비 이야기를 하며 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. ‘젊은 날의 어리석음은 용서받을 만하지요. 늙은이에게 칭송할 만한 것이라곤 지혜밖에 없는데, 그것마저 떠나 버리면 죽는 편이 마땅해요.’ 잔인한 말이다, 로마인에게나 어울릴 말이지."], ["I applied it to myself, knowing a feebleness like her father’s will come to me also—nay, it is not far off.", "나는 그 말을 내게도 적용해 보았다. 그 아비 같은 쇠약함이 내게도 닥쳐오리라는 것을 알기에—아니, 그것은 멀지 않았다."], ["But you, Esther, will never say of me—no, never—‘It were better he were dead.’ No, your mother was a daughter of Judah.”", "하지만 너는, 에스더야, 결코 나를 두고 그런 말을 하지 않겠지—그래, 결코—‘차라리 돌아가시는 편이 나아.’ 그래, 네 어미는 유다의 딸이었다.”"]], [["With half-formed tears, she kissed him, and said, “I am my mother’s child.”", "눈물이 반쯤 고인 채 그녀는 그에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. “저는 제 어머니의 자식이에요.”"]], [["“Yes, and my daughter—my daughter, who is to me all the Temple was to Solomon.”", "“그래, 그리고 내 딸이지—내 딸, 솔로몬에게 성전이 그러했듯이 내게 전부인 내 딸.”"]], [["After a silence, he laid his hand upon her shoulder, and resumed: “When he has taken the Egyptian to wife, Esther, he will think of you with repentance and much calling of the spirit; for at last he will awake to find himself but the minister of her bad ambition.", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그는 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹고 다시 말을 이었다. “그가 그 이집트 여자를 아내로 맞이하고 나면, 에스더야, 그는 후회하며 너를 떠올리고 영혼으로 너를 부르게 될 것이다. 마침내 그는 깨어나, 자신이 한낱 그 여자의 못된 야망을 받드는 종에 지나지 않음을 알게 될 테니까."], ["Rome is the centre of all her dreams.", "로마야말로 그 여자의 온갖 꿈의 중심이다."], ["To her he is the son of Arrius the duumvir, not the son of Hur, Prince of Jerusalem.”", "그 여자에게 그는 예루살렘의 군주 후르의 아들이 아니라, 두 집정관 가운데 하나였던 아리우스의 아들일 뿐이다.”"]], [["Esther made no attempt to conceal the effect of these words.", "에스더는 이 말이 미친 영향을 굳이 감추려 들지 않았다."]], [["“Save him, father!", "“그분을 구해 주세요, 아버지!"], ["It is not too late!”", "아직 너무 늦지 않았어요!”"], ["she said, entreatingly.", "그녀가 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["He answered, with a dubious smile, “A man drowning may be saved; not so a man in love.”", "그는 미심쩍은 미소를 지으며 대답했다. “물에 빠진 사람은 구할 수 있어도, 사랑에 빠진 사람은 그렇지 못한 법이란다.”"]], [["“But you have influence with him.", "“하지만 아버지는 그분에게 영향력이 있으시잖아요."], ["He is alone in the world.", "그분은 이 세상에 홀로예요."], ["Show him his danger.", "그분께 위험을 일깨워 주세요."], ["Tell him what a woman she is.”", "그 여자가 어떤 여자인지 말씀해 주세요.”"]], [["“That might save him from her.", "“그러면 그를 그 여자한테서 구할 수는 있을지도 모르지."], ["Would it give him to you, Esther? No,”", "그렇다고 그를 네게 줄 수 있겠느냐, 에스더? 아니다.”"], ["and his brows fell darkly over his eyes.", "그러면서 그의 눈썹이 어둡게 두 눈 위로 내려앉았다."], ["“I am a servant, as my fathers were for generations; yet I could not say to him, ‘Lo, master, my daughter!", "“나는 종이다, 내 조상들이 여러 대에 걸쳐 그러했듯이. 그러니 그에게 ‘보십시오, 주인님, 제 딸입니다!"], ["She is fairer than the Egyptian, and loves thee better!’ I have caught too much from years of liberty and direction.", "이 아이는 그 이집트 여자보다 더 곱고, 당신을 더 깊이 사랑합니다!’ 하고 말할 수는 없다. 나는 자유와 결정권을 누린 세월에서 너무 많은 것을 물려받았다."], ["The words would blister my tongue.", "그런 말은 내 혀를 데게 할 것이다."], ["The stones upon the old hills yonder would turn in their beds for shame when I go out to them.", "내가 저기 저 오래된 언덕들로 나아가면, 그 위의 돌들이 부끄러움에 제자리에서 돌아누울 것이다."], ["No, by the patriarchs, Esther, I would rather lay us both with your mother to sleep as she sleeps!”", "아니다, 조상들의 이름을 걸고, 에스더야, 차라리 나는 우리 둘을 네 어미 곁에, 그가 잠든 그 잠 속에 함께 뉘이고 말겠다!”"]], [["A blush burned Esther’s whole face.", "에스더의 온 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐다."]], [["“I did not mean you to tell him so, father.", "“그렇게 말씀해 달라는 뜻이 아니었어요, 아버지."], ["I was concerned for him alone—for his happiness, not mine.", "저는 그분만을 생각했어요—제 행복이 아니라 그분의 행복을요."], ["Because I have dared love him, I shall keep myself worthy his respect; so only can I excuse my folly.", "제가 감히 그분을 사랑한 이상, 저는 그분의 존경을 받을 만한 사람으로 저를 지킬 거예요. 그래야만 제 어리석음을 변명할 수 있으니까요."], ["Let me read his letter now.”", "이제 그분의 편지를 읽게 해 주세요.”"]], [["“Yes, read it.”", "“그래, 읽어 보아라.”"]], [["She began at once, in haste to conclude the distasteful subject.", "그녀는 이 달갑잖은 이야기를 끝내고 싶은 마음에 곧장 읽기 시작했다."]], [["“Nisan, 8th day.", "“니산월 여드렛날."]], [["“On the road from Galilee to Jerusalem.", "“갈릴리에서 예루살렘으로 가는 길에서."]], [["“The Nazarene is on the way also.", "“나사렛 사람도 같은 길을 가고 있습니다."], ["With him, though without his knowledge, I am bringing a full legion of mine. A second legion follows.", "나는 그가 모르게, 내 군단 하나를 온전히 거느리고 그와 함께 가고 있습니다. 또 다른 군단 하나가 뒤따르고 있습니다."], ["The Passover will excuse the multitude.", "유월절이 그 많은 무리를 너끈히 변명해 줄 것입니다."], ["He said upon setting out, ‘We will go up to Jerusalem, and all things that are written by the prophets concerning me shall be accomplished.’", "그는 길을 나서며 이렇게 말했습니다. ‘우리가 예루살렘으로 올라가노니, 선지자들로 나를 두고 기록한 모든 것이 다 이루어지리라.’"]], [["“Our waiting draws to an end.", "“우리의 기다림이 끝을 향해 가고 있습니다."]], [["“In haste.", "“총총."]], [["“Peace to thee, Simonides.", "“그대에게 평화가 있기를, 시모니데스."]], [["Esther returned the letter to her father, while a choking sensation gathered in her throat.", "“벤허.” 에스더는 편지를 아버지에게 돌려주었는데, 그러는 사이 목구멍에 무언가 메는 것이 차올랐다."], ["There was not a word in the missive for her—not even in the salutation had she a share—and it would have been so easy to have written “and to thine, peace.”", "그 편지에는 그녀를 위한 말이 한마디도 없었다—인사말 속에서조차 그녀의 몫은 없었으니—“그대의 가족에게도 평화가 있기를” 한 줄 적기가 그토록 쉬웠을 텐데도."], ["For the first time in her life she felt the smart of a jealous sting.", "평생 처음으로 그녀는 질투의 따끔한 가시를 느꼈다."]], [["“The eighth day,”", "“여드렛날이라.”"], ["said Simonides, “the eighth day; and this, Esther, this is the—”", "시모니데스가 말했다. “여드렛날이라. 그러면, 에스더야, 오늘이—”"]], [["“The ninth,”", "“아흐렛날이에요.”"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Ah, then, they may be in Bethany now.”", "“아, 그렇다면 지금쯤 베다니에 와 있겠구나.”"]], [["“And possibly we may see him to-night,”", "“그러면 어쩌면 오늘 밤에 그분을 뵐 수도 있겠어요.”"], ["she added, pleased into momentary forgetfulness.", "그녀는 잠시 모든 것을 잊고 기뻐하며 덧붙였다."]], [["“It may be, it may be!", "“그럴지도, 그럴지도 모르지!"], ["To-morrow is the Feast of Unleavened Bread, and he may wish to celebrate it; so may the Nazarene; and we may see him—we may see both of them, Esther.”", "내일이 무교절(無酵節)이니, 그가 그것을 지키고 싶어 할지도 모른다. 나사렛 사람도 그러할 테고. 그러면 우리는 그를 볼 수 있을 게다—우리는 둘 다 볼 수 있을 게야, 에스더.”"]], [["At this point the servant appeared with the wine and water.", "이때 하인이 포도주와 물을 가지고 나타났다."], ["Esther helped her father, and in the midst of the service Iras came upon the roof.", "에스더가 아버지의 시중을 드는데, 그 와중에 이라스가 지붕 위로 올라왔다."]], [["To the Jewess the Egyptian never appeared so very, very beautiful as at that moment.", "이 유대 처녀가 보기에 그 이집트 여자가 그 순간만큼 그토록, 그토록 아름다워 보인 적은 없었다."], ["Her gauzy garments fluttered about her like a little cloud of mist; her forehead, neck, and arms glittered with the massive jewelry so affected by her people.", "얇은 비단옷이 자그마한 안개구름처럼 그녀의 몸을 감싸고 나풀거렸다. 이마와 목과 두 팔은 그 민족이 즐겨 다는 묵직한 패물로 번쩍였다."], ["Her countenance was suffused with pleasure.", "그녀의 얼굴은 기쁨으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["She moved with buoyant steps, and self-conscious, though without affectation.", "그녀는 들뜬 걸음으로, 스스로를 의식하면서도 꾸밈은 없이 움직였다."], ["Esther at the sight shrank within herself, and nestled closer to her father.", "그 모습에 에스더는 제 속으로 움츠러들며 아버지에게 더 바짝 붙었다."]], [["“Peace to you, Simonides, and to the pretty Esther peace,”", "“그대에게 평화가 있기를, 시모니데스. 그리고 어여쁜 에스더에게도 평화를.”"], ["said Iras, inclining her head to the latter.", "이라스가 에스더에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“You remind me, good master—if I may say it without offence-you remind me of the priests in Persia who climb their temples at the decline of day to send prayers after the departing sun.", "“선량한 어르신, 무례가 아니라면 말씀드리건대—어르신을 뵈니 페르시아의 사제들이 떠오르는군요. 그들은 날이 저물 무렵 신전 위로 올라가, 떠나가는 해를 뒤쫓아 기도를 보내지요."], ["Is there anything in the worship you do not know, let me call my father. He is Magian-bred.”", "그 예배에 대해 모르시는 것이 있거든, 제 아버지를 부르게 해 주세요. 그분은 마기교(敎)의 가르침을 받고 자라셨으니까요.”"]], [["“Fair Egyptian,”", "“아리따운 이집트 처녀여,”"], ["the merchant replied, nodding with grave politeness, “your father is a good man who would not be offended if he knew I told you his Persian lore is the least part of his wisdom.”", "상인은 진중한 예의로 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. “그대의 아버지는 선량한 분으로, 내가 그대에게 그분의 페르시아 학문은 그분 지혜의 가장 작은 부분일 뿐이라고 말했다는 것을 아셔도 결코 언짢아하지 않으실 게요.”"]], [["Iras’s lip curled slightly.", "이라스의 입술이 살짝 비틀어졌다."]], [["“To speak like a philosopher, as you invite me,”", "“어르신께서 그리 하라 하시니 철학자처럼 말해 보자면,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “the least part always implies a greater.", "“가장 작은 부분이 있다는 것은 언제나 더 큰 부분이 있다는 뜻이지요."], ["Let me ask what you esteem the greater part of the rare quality you are pleased to attribute to him.”", "어르신께서 그분께 기꺼이 돌리시는 그 드문 자질 가운데, 더 큰 부분이라 여기시는 것이 무엇인지 여쭤도 될까요?”"]], [["Simonides turned upon her somewhat sternly.", "시모니데스는 다소 엄한 얼굴로 그녀를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Pure wisdom always directs itself towards God; the purest wisdom is knowledge of God; and no man of my acquaintance has it in higher degree, or makes it more manifest in speech and act, than the good Balthasar.”", "“순수한 지혜는 언제나 하느님을 향한다오. 가장 순수한 지혜는 하느님을 아는 것이지. 그리고 내가 아는 사람 가운데 그것을 선량한 발타사르보다 더 높이 지닌 이도, 말과 행실로 그것을 더 뚜렷이 드러내는 이도 없소.”"]], [["To end the parley, he raised the cup and drank.", "입씨름을 끝내려고 그는 잔을 들어 마셨다."]], [["The Egyptian turned to Esther a little testily.", "이집트 여자는 다소 짜증스럽게 에스더 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“A man who has millions in store, and fleets of ships at sea, cannot discern in what simple women like us find amusement.", "“창고에 수백만 금을 쌓아 두고 바다에 함대를 띄워 둔 사람은, 우리 같은 순박한 여인들이 무엇에서 재미를 찾는지 알아차리지 못하는 법이지."], ["Let us leave him.", "우리는 어르신을 두고 갑시다."], ["By the wall yonder we can talk.”", "저기 담장 옆에 가면 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠어요.”"]], [["They went to the parapet then, stopping at the place where, years before, Ben-Hur loosed the broken tile upon the head of Gratus.", "그리하여 그들은 난간으로 갔는데, 여러 해 전 벤허가 깨진 기와 조각을 그라투스의 머리 위로 떨어뜨린 바로 그 자리에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“You have not been to Rome?”", "“로마에 가 본 적 없나요?”"], ["Iras began, toying the while with one of her unclasped bracelets.", "이라스가 끌러 둔 팔찌 하나를 만지작거리며 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“No,” said Esther, demurely.", "“없어요.” 에스더가 얌전히 말했다."]], [["“Have you not wished to go?”", "“가 보고 싶지도 않았나요?”"]], [["“No.”", "“네.”"]], [["“Ah, how little there has been of your life!”", "“아, 그대의 삶에는 어쩌면 그토록 가진 것이 없을까!”"]], [["The sigh that succeeded the exclamation could not have been more piteously expressive had the loss been the Egyptian’s own.", "그 탄식 뒤에 이어진 한숨은, 잃은 것이 이집트 여자 자신의 것이었다 해도 그보다 더 가련하게 들릴 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Next moment her laugh might have been heard in the street below; and she said “Oh, oh, my pretty simpleton!", "다음 순간 그녀의 웃음소리는 아래 거리에서도 들릴 만했다. 그러고는 그녀가 말했다. “오, 오, 가엾은 내 숙맥!"], ["The half-fledged birds nested in the ear of the great bust out on the Memphian sands know nearly as much as you.”", "멤피스의 모래밭에 솟은 저 거대한 흉상의 귓속에 둥지를 튼, 깃도 채 나지 않은 새 새끼들도 그대만큼은 알 거예요.”"]], [["Then, seeing Esther’s confusion, she changed her manner, and said in a confiding tone, “You must not take offence. Oh no!", "그러다 에스더가 당황하는 것을 보고는 태도를 바꾸어, 속을 터놓는 어조로 말했다. “언짢게 여기면 안 돼요."], ["I was playing.", "오, 그러지 마요!"], ["Let me kiss the hurt, and tell you what I would not to any other—not if Simbel himself asked it of me, offering a lotus-cup of the spray of the Nile!”", "장난친 거예요. 내가 입맞춤으로 그 아픈 데를 달래 주고, 다른 누구에게도—심벨 신이 친히 청하며 나일강 물보라를 담은 연꽃 잔을 내민다 해도—하지 않을 이야기를 들려줄게요!”"]], [["Another laugh, masking excellently the look she turned sharply upon the Jewess, and she said, “The King is coming.”", "또 한 번 웃음을 터뜨려, 유대 처녀에게 날카롭게 던진 눈길을 감쪽같이 가리고는, 그녀가 말했다. “왕이 오신답니다.”"]], [["Esther gazed at her in innocent surprise.", "에스더는 순진한 놀라움으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“The Nazarene,”", "“나사렛 사람 말이에요.” 이라스가 말을 이었다. “우리 아버지들이 그토록 많이 입에 올리던 그 사람, 벤허가 그토록 오래 섬기고 애써 온 그 사람.”"], ["Iras continued—“he whom our fathers have been talking about so much, whom Ben-Hur has been serving and toiling for so long”—her voice dropped several tones lower—“the Nazarene will be here to-morrow, and Ben-Hur to-night.”", "그녀의 목소리가 몇 음 더 낮아졌다. “나사렛 사람은 내일 이곳에 올 거예요. 그리고 벤허는 오늘 밤에요.”"]], [["Esther struggled to maintain her composure, but failed: her eyes fell, the tell-tale blood surged to her cheek and forehead, and she was saved sight of the triumphant smile that passed, like a gleam, over the face of the Egyptian.", "에스더는 침착함을 지키려 애썼으나 그러지 못했다. 그녀의 눈이 내리깔리고, 마음을 들켜 버린 피가 두 뺨과 이마로 솟구쳤다. 그래서 그녀는 이집트 여자의 얼굴 위로 한 줄기 빛처럼 스쳐 간 그 의기양양한 미소를 보지 못했다."]], [["“See, here is his promise.”", "“보세요, 여기 그분의 약속이 있어요.”"]], [["And from her girdle she took a roll.", "그러고는 허리띠에서 두루마리 하나를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Rejoice with me, O my friend!", "“나와 함께 기뻐해 줘요, 오 나의 벗이여!"], ["He will be here tonight!", "그분은 오늘 밤 이곳에 올 거예요!"], ["On the Tiber there is a house, a royal property, which he has pledged to me; and to be its mistress is to be—”", "티베르 강가에 집이 한 채 있는데, 왕가의 재산이지요. 그분이 그것을 내게 약속하셨어요. 그 집의 안주인이 된다는 것은 곧—”"]], [["A sound of some one walking swiftly along the street below interrupted the speech, and she leaned over the parapet to see.", "아래 거리를 누군가 빠른 걸음으로 지나는 소리가 그 말을 끊었고, 그녀는 보려고 난간 너머로 몸을 기울였다."], ["Then she drew back, and cried, with hands clasped above her head, “Now blessed be Isis!", "그러다 다시 물러서며, 두 손을 머리 위로 맞잡고 외쳤다. “이제 이시스 여신께 축복 있으라!"], ["’Tis he—Ben-Hur himself!", "그분이에요—벤허 본인이에요!"], ["That he should appear while I had such thought of him!", "내가 그분을 이토록 생각하고 있는데 그분이 나타나다니!"], ["There are no gods if it be not a good omen.", "이것이 길조가 아니라면 신은 없는 거예요."], ["Put your arms about me, Esther—and a kiss!”", "나를 안아 줘요, 에스더—그리고 입맞춤도!”"]], [["The Jewess looked up.", "유대 처녀가 고개를 들었다."], ["Upon each cheek there was a glow; her eyes sparkled with a light more nearly of anger than ever her nature emitted before.", "두 뺨에는 저마다 발그레한 기운이 어렸고, 그녀의 눈은 일찍이 그 천성이 내뿜은 적 없던, 분노에 가까운 빛으로 번득였다."], ["Her gentleness had been too roughly overridden.", "그녀의 온순함이 너무도 거칠게 짓밟힌 것이었다."], ["It was not enough for her to be forbidden more than fugitive dreams of the man she loved; a boastful rival must tell her in confidence of her better success, and of the brilliant promises which were its rewards.", "사랑하는 사람을 두고 덧없는 꿈조차 마음껏 꾸지 못하도록 금지당한 것만으로도 모자란데, 으스대는 연적이 자신의 더 큰 성공을, 그리고 그 보답으로 받은 빛나는 약속들을 은밀히 일러 주기까지 한 것이다."], ["Of her, the servant of a servant, there had been no hint of remembrance; this other could show his letter, leaving her to imagine all it breathed. So she said,", "종의 종에 지나지 않는 그녀에게는 기억해 주는 기색 한 점 없었건만, 이 다른 여자는 제 편지를 보여 줄 수 있었고, 그 편지에 깃든 모든 것을 그녀더러 상상하게 내버려 두었다. 그래서 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Dost thou love him so much, then, or Rome so much better?”", "“그러면 그대는 그분을 그토록 사랑하나요, 아니면 로마를 그보다 더 사랑하나요?”"]], [["The Egyptian drew back a step; then she bent her haughty head quite near her questioner.", "이집트 여자는 한 걸음 물러섰다. 그러더니 거만한 고개를 숙여 질문한 이에게 바짝 들이댔다."]], [["“What is he to thee, daughter of Simonides?”", "“그분이 그대에게 무엇이지요, 시모니데스의 딸이여?”"]], [["Esther, all thrilling, began, “He is my—”", "에스더는 온몸을 떨며 입을 열었다. “그분은 제—”"]], [["A thought blasting as lightning stayed the words: she paled, trembled, recovered, and answered,", "번개처럼 모든 것을 태워 버릴 듯한 한 생각이 그 말을 멈춰 세웠다. 그녀는 핏기를 잃고 떨다가, 다시 정신을 차려 대답했다."]], [["“He is my father’s friend.”", "“그분은 제 아버지의 벗이에요.”"]], [["Her tongue had refused to admit her servile condition.", "그녀의 혀가 자신의 종된 처지를 인정하기를 거부한 것이다."]], [["Iras laughed more lightly than before.", "이라스는 아까보다 더 가볍게 웃었다."]], [["“Not more than that?” she said.", "“그뿐이에요?” 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Ah, by the lover-gods of Egypt, thou mayst keep thy kisses—keep them.", "“아, 이집트의 사랑의 신들을 걸고, 그대의 입맞춤은 그대가 간직해도 좋아요—간직해 둬요."], ["Thou hast taught me but now that there are others vastly more estimable waiting me here in Judea; and”—she turned away, looking back over her shoulder—“I will go get them.", "그대는 방금, 여기 유대 땅에서 나를 기다리는, 비할 데 없이 더 값진 다른 이들이 있다는 것을 일러 주었으니까요. 그리고”—그녀는 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 돌아섰다—“나는 가서 그 입맞춤들을 받겠어요."], ["Peace to thee.”", "그대에게 평화가 있기를.”"]], [["Esther saw her disappear down the steps, when, putting her hands over her face, she burst into tears so they ran scalding through her fingers—tears of shame and choking passion.", "에스더는 그녀가 계단 아래로 사라지는 것을 지켜보다가, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 눈물이 손가락 사이로 뜨겁게 흘러내렸다—수치와, 목이 메도록 북받치는 격정의 눈물이었다."], ["And, to deepen the paroxysm to her even temper so strange, up with a new meaning of withering force rose her father’s words—“Thy love might not have been vainly given had I kept fast hold of all I had, as I might have done.”", "그리고 그토록 차분하던 그녀의 성정에는 이상하리만치 그 발작을 더욱 깊게 하느라, 아버지의 말이 사람을 시들게 하는 새로운 힘의 의미를 띠고 되살아났다—“내가 가졌던 모든 것을, 능히 그럴 수 있었던 대로 단단히 붙들고 있었더라면 네 사랑이 헛되이 바쳐지지는 않았을지도 모른다.”"]], [["And all the stars were out, burning low above the city and the dark wall of mountains about it, before she recovered enough to go back to the summer-house, and in silence take her accustomed place at her father’s side, humbly waiting his pleasure.", "그녀가 가까스로 마음을 추슬러 정자로 돌아가, 말없이 아버지 곁 제 자리에 앉아 그의 분부를 겸허히 기다릴 수 있게 되었을 무렵에는, 도성과 그것을 에워싼 어두운 산줄기 위로 별들이 죄다 나와 낮게 타오르고 있었다."], ["To such duty it seemed her youth, if not her life, must be given.", "그런 도리에 그녀의 젊음이, 아니 어쩌면 그녀의 삶이 바쳐져야 할 것만 같았다."], ["And, let the truth be said, now that the pang was spent, she went not unwillingly back to the duty.", "그리고 진실을 말하자면, 이제 아픔이 가신 터라, 그녀는 그 도리로 돌아가는 것이 그리 내키지 않는 일만은 아니었다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["An hour or thereabouts after the scene upon the roof, Balthasar and Simonides, the latter attended by Esther, met in the great chamber of the palace; and while they were talking, Ben-Hur and Iras came in together.", "지붕 위에서 있었던 장면이 끝나고 한 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 발타사르와 시모니데스가 궁전의 큰 방에서 만났다. 시모니데스 곁에는 에스더가 시중을 들고 있었다. 두 사람이 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 벤허와 이라스가 함께 들어왔다."]], [["The young Jew, advancing in front of his companion, walked first to Balthasar, and saluted him, and received his reply; then he turned to Simonides, but paused at sight of Esther.", "젊은 유대인은 길동무보다 앞서 나아가, 먼저 발타사르에게 다가가 인사를 건네고 답례를 받았다. 그런 다음 시모니데스에게로 몸을 돌렸으나, 에스더를 보고는 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["It is not often we have hearts roomy enough for more than one of the absorbing passions at the same time; in its blaze the others may continue to live, but only as lesser lights.", "우리 마음은 한꺼번에 두 가지 넘는 정념을 한껏 품을 만큼 넉넉하지 못할 때가 많다. 어느 하나가 활활 타오르면 나머지는 살아남을지언정 더 작은 불빛으로 머물 뿐이다."], ["So with Ben-Hur, much study of possibilities, indulgence of hopes and dreams, influences born of the condition of his country, influences more direct—that of Iras, for example—had made him in the broadest worldly sense ambitious; and as he had given the passion place, allowing it to become a rule, and finally an imperious governor, the resolves and impulses of former days faded imperceptibly out of being, and at last almost out of recollection.", "벤허도 그러했다. 온갖 가능성을 거듭 따져 보고, 희망과 꿈에 마음을 내맡기고, 조국의 처지에서 비롯된 영향과 더 직접적인 영향—이를테면 이라스가 끼친 영향—을 받으며, 그는 세속의 가장 넓은 의미에서 야심가가 되어 갔다. 그 정념에게 자리를 내주어 그것이 하나의 법칙이 되도록, 마침내 거역할 수 없는 지배자가 되도록 내버려 두는 사이, 지난날의 결의와 충동은 알게 모르게 스러져 가다가 끝내 거의 기억에서조차 사라졌다."], ["It is at best so easy to forget our youth; in his case it was but natural that his own sufferings and the mystery darkening the fate of his family should move him less and less as, in hope at least, he approached nearer and nearer the goals which occupied all his visions.", "누구든 제 젊은 날을 잊기란 너무도 쉬운 법이다. 그의 경우, 자신을 둘러싼 모든 환상을 채우는 목표에 적어도 희망 속에서나마 점점 더 가까이 다가갈수록, 제가 겪은 고통과 가족의 운명을 어둠으로 덮은 그 수수께끼가 점점 덜 마음을 흔든 것도 그저 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["Only let us not judge him too harshly.", "다만 그를 너무 가혹하게 판단하지는 말자."]], [["He paused in surprise at seeing Esther a woman now, and so beautiful; and as he stood looking at her a still voice reminded him of broken vows and duties undone: almost his old self returned.", "그는 에스더가 이제 어엿한 여인이 되었고, 또 그토록 아름다워진 것을 보고 놀라 걸음을 멈췄다. 그렇게 서서 그녀를 바라보고 있노라니, 어떤 고요한 목소리가 깨어진 맹세와 다하지 못한 의무를 일깨워 주었다. 거의 옛 자신이 되돌아온 듯했다."]], [["For an instant he was startled; but recovering, he went to Esther, and said, “Peace to thee, sweet Esther—peace; and thou, Simonides”—he looked to the merchant as he spoke—“the blessing of the Lord be thine, if only because thou hast been a good father to the fatherless.”", "한순간 그는 흠칫했으나, 곧 마음을 가다듬고 에스더에게 다가가 말했다. \"그대에게 평안이 있기를, 어여쁜 에스더여—평안이 있기를. 그리고 그대, 시모니데스여\"—그는 말하면서 상인을 바라보았다—\"주님의 축복이 그대에게 함께하기를. 다른 까닭이 없더라도, 그대가 아비 없는 자에게 좋은 아비가 되어 주었으니 말이오.\""]], [["Esther heard him with downcast face; Simonides answered,", "에스더는 고개를 떨군 채 그 말을 들었다. 시모니데스가 대답했다."]], [["“I repeat the welcome of the good Balthasar, son of Hur—welcome to thy father’s house; and sit, and tell us of thy travels, and of thy work, and of the wonderful Nazarene—who he is, and what.", "\"훌륭한 발타사르께서 베푸신 환대를 나도 거듭 전하리다, 후르의 아들이여—그대 아버지의 집에 온 것을 환영하오. 자, 앉아서 그대의 여행과 그대가 한 일, 그리고 그 놀라운 나사렛 사람에 대해 들려주오—그가 누구이며 무엇인지를."], ["If thou art not at ease here, who shall be?", "그대가 여기서 편안하지 못하다면, 누가 편안하겠소?"], ["Sit, I pray—there, between us, that we may all hear.”", "부디 앉으시오—거기, 우리 사이에 앉아 우리 모두가 들을 수 있게 말이오.\""]], [["Esther stepped out quickly and brought a covered stool, and set it for him.", "에스더가 잰걸음으로 나가 덮개 씌운 걸상을 가져와 그를 위해 놓아 주었다."]], [["“Thanks,”", "\"고맙소.\""], ["he said to her, gratefully.", "그가 그녀에게 고마운 마음으로 말했다."]], [["When seated, after some other conversation, he addressed himself to the men.", "자리에 앉은 그는 다른 이야기를 조금 나눈 뒤, 두 남자를 향해 말했다."]], [["“I have come to tell you of the Nazarene.”", "\"나는 나사렛 사람에 대해 말씀드리려고 왔습니다.\""]], [["The two became instantly attentive.", "두 사람은 곧바로 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“For many days now I have followed him with such watchfulness as one may give another upon whom he is waiting so anxiously.", "\"여러 날 동안 나는 그를 따라다녔습니다. 누군가를 그토록 애타게 기다리는 사람이 그 대상에게 기울일 만한 그런 주의를 다해서 말입니다."], ["I have seen him under all circumstances said to be trials and tests of men; and while I am certain he is a man as I am, not less certain am I that he is something more.”", "사람을 시험하고 떠본다고들 하는 온갖 상황에서 그를 지켜보았습니다. 그가 나와 같은 사람이라는 것은 분명하지만, 동시에 그가 그 이상의 무언가라는 것 또한 못지않게 분명합니다.\""]], [["“What more?” asked Simonides.", "\"무엇 이상이란 말이오?\" 시모니데스가 물었다."]], [["“I will tell you—”", "\"말씀드리지요—\""]], [["Some one coming into the room interrupted him; he turned, and arose with extended hands.", "누군가 방으로 들어오는 바람에 그의 말이 끊겼다. 그가 몸을 돌리며 두 손을 내밀고 일어섰다."]], [["“Amrah!", "\"암라!"], ["Dear old Amrah!”", "정다운 암라 할멈!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["She came forward; and they, seeing the joy in her face, thought not once how wrinkled and tawny it was.", "그녀가 앞으로 나왔다. 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 기쁨을 본 사람들은, 그 얼굴이 얼마나 주름지고 거무튀튀한지는 한 번도 생각하지 않았다."], ["She knelt at his feet, clasped his knees, and kissed his hands over and over; and when he could he put the lank gray hair from her cheeks, and kissed them, saying, “Good Amrah, have you nothing, nothing of them—not a word—not one little sign?”", "그녀는 그의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 그의 무릎을 끌어안았으며, 그의 두 손에 거듭 입을 맞추었다. 그는 짬을 내어 그녀의 뺨에서 가느다란 잿빛 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기고, 그 뺨에 입 맞추며 말했다. \"착한 암라여, 그들에 대해 아무것도, 정말 아무것도 모르는가—단 한마디도—아주 작은 기별 하나도?\""]], [["Then she broke into sobbing which made him answer plainer even than the spoken word.", "그러자 그녀는 흐느낌을 터뜨렸으니, 그것은 어떤 말보다도 더 분명하게 그에게 대답해 주었다."]], [["“God’s will has been done,”", "\"하나님의 뜻이 이루어졌구나.\""], ["he next said, solemnly, in a tone to make each listener know he had no hope more of finding his people.", "이어 그가 엄숙하게 말했으니, 그 어조는 듣는 이마다 그가 이제 제 가족을 찾을 희망을 더는 품지 않음을 알게 했다."], ["In his eyes there were tears which he would not have them see, because he was a man.", "그의 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었으나, 그는 사내였으므로 남들에게 그것을 보이려 하지 않았다."]], [["When he could again, he took seat, and said, “Come, sit by me, Amrah—here. No?", "다시 추스를 수 있게 되자, 그는 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"자, 내 곁에 앉게, 암라여—여기에. 싫은가?"], ["then at my feet; for I have much to say to these good friends of a wonderful man come into the world.”", "그럼 내 발치에라도 앉게. 세상에 오신 한 놀라운 분에 대해 이 좋은 벗들에게 들려줄 말이 많으니 말일세.\""], ["But she went off, and stooping with her back to the wall, joined her hands before her knees, content, they all thought, with seeing him.", "그러나 그녀는 자리를 떠나, 벽에 등을 대고 웅크려 앉아 무릎 앞에 두 손을 모았다. 모두가 보기에, 그녀는 그를 바라보는 것만으로도 만족하는 듯했다."], ["Then Ben-Hur, bowing to the old men, began again:", "그러자 벤허는 두 노인에게 고개를 숙여 보이고 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“I fear to answer the question asked me about the Nazarene without first telling you some of the things I have seen him do; and to that I am the more inclined, my friends, because to-morrow he will come to the city, and go up into the Temple, which he calls his father’s house, where, it is further said, he will proclaim himself.", "\"나사렛 사람에 대해 받은 물음에 답하기 전에, 내가 그가 하는 일을 직접 본 몇 가지를 먼저 말씀드리지 않으면 두려울 것 같습니다. 더욱 그러고 싶은 까닭은, 벗들이여, 내일 그가 도성으로 와서 그가 제 아버지의 집이라 부르는 성전으로 올라갈 것이기 때문입니다. 게다가 거기서 그가 스스로를 드러내 보이리라는 말도 있습니다."], ["So, whether you are right, O Balthasar, or you, Simonides, we and Israel shall know to-morrow.”", "그러니 발타사르여, 그대가 옳든, 시모니데스여, 그대가 옳든, 우리와 이스라엘은 내일이면 알게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["Balthasar rubbed his hands tremulously together, and asked, “Where shall I go to see him?”", "발타사르가 두 손을 떨리는 듯 비비며 물었다. \"그를 보려면 어디로 가야 하겠소?\""]], [["“The pressure of the crowd will be very great.", "\"군중에 밀려 몹시 혼잡할 것입니다."], ["Better, I think, that you all go upon the roof above the cloisters—say upon the Porch of Solomon.”", "회랑 위 지붕으로—이를테면 솔로몬 행각으로 다들 올라가시는 편이 나으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, “my friends will require me, perhaps, in the procession.”", "벤허가 말했다. \"내 벗들이 어쩌면 행렬에서 나를 필요로 할 것입니다.\""]], [["“Procession!”", "\"행렬이라니!\""], ["exclaimed Simonides.", "시모니데스가 소리쳤다."], ["“Does he travel in state?”", "\"그가 위엄을 갖추어 다닌단 말이오?\""]], [["Ben-Hur saw the argument in mind.", "벤허는 그 마음속에 깃든 속뜻을 알아챘다."]], [["“He brings twelve men with him, fishermen, tillers of the soil, one a publican, all of the humbler class; and he and they make their journeys on foot, careless of wind, cold, rain, or sun.", "\"그는 열두 사람을 데리고 다닙니다. 어부와 농부, 그 가운데 하나는 세리이며, 모두 비천한 계층의 사람들입니다. 그와 그들은 바람도 추위도 비도 햇볕도 아랑곳하지 않고 걸어서 길을 갑니다."], ["Seeing them stop by the wayside at nightfall to break bread or lie down to sleep, I have been reminded of a party of shepherds going back to their flocks from market, not of nobles and kings.", "해 질 녘 길가에 멈추어 빵을 떼거나 누워 잠을 청하는 그들을 보고 있으면, 나는 귀족이나 왕이 아니라 장에서 제 양 떼에게로 돌아가는 한 무리의 목자들을 떠올리곤 합니다."], ["Only when he lifts the corners of his handkerchief to look at some one or shake the dust from his head, I am made known he is their teacher as well as their companion—their superior not less than their friend.", "다만 그가 누군가를 보려고, 혹은 머리에서 먼지를 털려고 머릿수건 자락을 들어 올릴 때에야, 나는 그가 그들의 동무일 뿐 아니라 스승이며—벗인 동시에 그들보다 윗사람임을 깨닫게 됩니다."]], [["“You are shrewd men,” Ben-Hur resumed, after a pause.", "\"두 분은 명민한 분들이십니다.\" 벤허가 잠시 멈추었다가 다시 말을 이었다."], ["“You know what creatures of certain master motives we are, and that it has become little less than a law of our nature to spend life in eager pursuit of certain objects; now, appealing to that law as something by which we may know ourselves, what would you say of a man who could be rich by making gold of the stones under his feet, yet is poor of choice?”", "\"우리가 어떤 으뜸가는 동기에 사로잡힌 존재인지, 또 어떤 목적을 열심히 좇으며 한평생을 보내는 것이 우리 본성의 법칙이나 다름없이 되었다는 것을 두 분은 아십니다. 그러니 우리 자신을 가늠할 잣대로서 그 법칙에 호소하건대, 발밑의 돌을 황금으로 바꾸어 부자가 될 수 있는데도 스스로 가난을 택한 사람을 두고 무어라 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“The Greeks would call him a philosopher,” said Iras.", "\"그리스 사람이라면 철학자라 부르겠지요.\" 이라스가 말했다."]], [["“Nay, daughter,”", "\"아니다, 딸아.\""], ["said Balthasar, “the philosophers had never the power to do such thing.”", "발타사르가 말했다. \"철학자들은 그런 일을 할 힘을 가진 적이 없었느니라.\""]], [["“How know you this man has?”", "\"이 사람이 그런 힘을 지녔다는 것을 어찌 아시는지요?\""]], [["Ben-Hur answered quickly, “I saw him turn water into wine.”", "벤허가 곧바로 대답했다. \"나는 그가 물을 포도주로 바꾸는 것을 보았습니다.\""]], [["“Very strange, very strange,”", "\"참으로 기이하고, 참으로 기이하오.\""], ["said Simonides; “but it is not so strange to me as that he should prefer to live poor when he could be so rich.", "시모니데스가 말했다. \"허나 내게는 그것이 그토록 부유해질 수 있는데도 가난하게 살기를 택했다는 사실만큼 기이하지는 않소."], ["Is he so poor?”", "그가 그토록 가난하단 말이오?\""]], [["“He owns nothing, and envies nobody his owning. He pities the rich.", "\"그는 아무것도 가진 것이 없고, 누가 무엇을 가졌든 부러워하지 않습니다."], ["But passing that, what would you say to see a man multiply seven loaves and two fishes, all his store, into enough to feed five thousand people, and have full baskets over?", "그는 도리어 부자를 가엾이 여깁니다. 그런데 그 일은 그만두고라도, 한 사람이 빵 일곱 덩이와 물고기 두 마리, 곧 가진 것 전부를 불려서 오천 명을 먹이고도 광주리에 가득 남기는 것을 보았다면 무어라 하시겠습니까?"], ["That I saw the Nazarene do.”", "나는 나사렛 사람이 그렇게 하는 것을 보았습니다.\""]], [["“You saw it?” exclaimed Simonides.", "\"그대가 그것을 보았단 말이오?\" 시모니데스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Ay, and ate of the bread and fish.”", "\"그렇습니다. 게다가 그 빵과 물고기를 먹기도 했습니다.\""]], [["“More marvellous still,”", "\"더욱 놀라운 일은,\""], ["Ben-Hur continued, “what would you say of a man in whom there is such healing virtue that the sick have but to touch the hem of his garment to be cured, or cry to him afar?", "벤허가 말을 이었다. \"병자가 그저 그 옷자락에 손을 대기만 해도, 혹은 멀리서 그에게 부르짖기만 해도 낫게 하는 그런 치유의 힘을 지닌 사람을 두고는 무어라 하시겠습니까?"], ["That, too, I witnessed, not once, but many times.", "그것 또한 나는 한 번이 아니라 여러 번 목격했습니다."], ["As we came out of Jericho two blind men by the wayside called to the Nazarene, and he touched their eyes, and they saw.", "우리가 여리고를 나설 때 길가의 눈먼 두 사람이 나사렛 사람을 불렀는데, 그가 그들의 눈에 손을 대니 그들이 보게 되었습니다."], ["So they brought a palsied man to him, and he said merely, ‘Go unto thy house,’ and the man went away well.", "또 사람들이 중풍 든 자를 그에게 데려오자, 그는 그저 '네 집으로 가라'고만 했는데, 그 사람은 멀쩡해져서 떠나갔습니다."], ["What say you to these things?”", "이런 일들을 두고 무어라 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["The merchant had no answer.", "상인은 아무런 대답이 없었다."]], [["“Think you now, as I have heard others argue, that what I have told you are tricks of jugglery?", "\"이제 두 분께서는, 다른 이들이 따지는 것을 내가 들었던 대로, 내가 들려드린 일들이 요술쟁이의 속임수라 여기십니까?"], ["Let me answer by recalling greater things which I have seen him do.", "그렇다면 내가 본 더 큰 일들을 떠올려 답해 드리겠습니다."], ["Look first to that curse of God—comfortless, as you all know, except by death—leprosy.”", "먼저 하나님의 저주, 곧 두 분 모두 아시다시피 죽음 말고는 달랠 길이 없는 그 나병을 보십시오.\""]], [["At these words Amrah dropped her hands to the floor, and in her eagerness to hear him half arose.", "이 말에 암라가 두 손을 바닥에 내려놓고는, 그의 말을 듣고 싶은 간절함에 반쯤 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“What would you say,”", "\"무어라 하시겠습니까,\" 벤허가 한층 진지하게 말했다. \"내가 이제 들려드리는 것을 직접 보셨다면 무어라 하시겠습니까?"], ["said Ben-Hur, with increased earnestness—“what would you say to have seen that I now tell you?", "내가 갈릴리에 그와 함께 내려가 있을 때, 한 나병환자가 나사렛 사람에게 와서 말했습니다. '주여, 원하시면 저를 깨끗하게 하실 수 있나이다.' 그가 그 부르짖음을 듣고 손으로 그 버림받은 자를 어루만지며 말했습니다."], ["A leper came to the Nazarene while I was with him down in Galilee, and said, ‘Lord, if thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.’ He heard the cry, and touched the outcast with his hand, saying, ‘Be thou clean;’ and forthwith the man was himself again, healthful as any of us who beheld the cure, and we were a multitude.”", "'깨끗함을 받으라.' 그러자 그 사람은 곧바로 본래의 자신으로 돌아와, 그 치유를 지켜본 우리 가운데 누구 못지않게 건강해졌습니다. 그리고 우리는 한 무리의 군중이었습니다.\""]], [["Here Amrah arose, and with her gaunt fingers held the wiry locks from her eyes.", "이때 암라가 몸을 일으켜, 앙상한 손가락으로 눈을 가린 뻣뻣한 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겼다."], ["The brain of the poor creature had long since gone to heart, and she was troubled to follow the speech.", "이 가엾은 여인의 머릿속 생각은 이미 오래전에 가슴으로 옮겨 가 버린 터라, 그녀는 그 이야기를 따라가기가 힘겨웠다."]], [["“Then, again,”", "\"그리고 또,\" 벤허가 멈추지 않고 말했다. \"어느 날 나병환자 열 사람이 한꺼번에 그에게 와서 발치에 엎드려 부르짖었습니다—나는 그 모든 것을 보고 들었습니다—부르짖기를, '선생님, 선생님, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!'"], ["said Ben-Hur, without stop, “ten lepers came to him one day in a body, and falling at his feet, called out—I saw and heard it all—called out, ‘Master, Master, have mercy upon us!’ He told them, ‘Go, show yourselves to the priest, as the law requires; and before you are come there ye shall be healed.’”", "그가 그들에게 일렀습니다. '가서 율법이 정한 대로 제사장에게 너희 몸을 보이라. 거기에 이르기 전에 너희가 나으리라.'\""]], [["“And were they?”", "\"그래서 나았소?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["On the road going their infirmity left them, so that there was nothing to remind us of it except their polluted clothes.”", "가는 길에 그들의 병이 떠나가, 더럽혀진 옷 말고는 그 병을 떠올리게 할 것이 아무것도 남지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Such thing was never heard before—never in all Israel!”", "\"그런 일은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없소—온 이스라엘을 통틀어 한 번도 없었소!\""], ["said Simonides, in undertone.", "시모니데스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["And then, while he was speaking, Amrah turned away, and walked noiselessly to the door, and went out; and none of the company saw her go.", "그러고 나서 그가 말하는 동안, 암라는 몸을 돌려 소리 없이 문으로 걸어가 밖으로 나갔다. 그러나 그녀가 나가는 것을 일행 가운데 누구도 보지 못했다."]], [["“The thoughts stirred by such things done under my eyes I leave you to imagine,”", "\"내 눈앞에서 벌어진 그런 일들이 불러일으킨 생각이 어떠했을지는 두 분의 상상에 맡기겠습니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, continuing; “but my doubts, my misgivings, my amazement, were not yet at the full.", "벤허가 말을 이었다. \"허나 나의 의혹과 불안과 경탄은 아직 그 끝에 이르지 않았습니다."], ["The people of Galilee are, as you know, impetuous and rash; after years of waiting their swords burned their hands; nothing would do them but action.", "두 분도 아시다시피 갈릴리 사람들은 성미가 급하고 무모합니다. 여러 해를 기다린 끝에 그들의 손은 칼자루를 쥐고 싶어 달아올랐고, 행동 말고는 아무것도 그들을 만족시키지 못했습니다."], ["‘He is slow to declare himself; let us force him,’ they cried to me.", "'그분은 좀처럼 스스로를 드러내지 않으니, 우리가 밀어붙입시다.' 그들이 내게 외쳤습니다."], ["And I too became impatient.", "나 또한 조바심이 났습니다."], ["If he is to be king, why not now?", "그가 왕이 되려 한다면 어찌하여 지금이 아닌가?"], ["The legions are ready.", "군단은 준비되어 있는데."], ["So as he was once teaching by the seaside we would have crowned him whether or not; but he disappeared, and was next seen on a ship departing from the shore.", "그래서 그가 한번 바닷가에서 가르치고 있을 때, 우리는 그가 원하든 원치 않든 그에게 왕관을 씌우려 했습니다. 그러나 그는 사라졌고, 이내 기슭을 떠나는 배 위에서 모습을 보였습니다."], ["Good Simonides, the desires that make other men mad—riches, power, even kingships offered out of great love by a great people—move this one not at all.", "훌륭한 시모니데스여, 다른 사람들을 미치게 하는 욕망들—부와 권력, 심지어 위대한 백성이 큰 사랑으로 바치는 왕위마저도—이 사람만은 조금도 움직이지 못합니다."], ["What say you?”", "무어라 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["The merchant’s chin was low upon his breast; raising his head, he replied, resolutely, “The Lord liveth, and so do the words of the prophets.", "상인의 턱은 가슴께로 낮게 떨구어져 있었다. 그가 고개를 들고 단호하게 대답했다. \"주께서 살아 계시고, 예언자들의 말씀 또한 살아 있소."], ["Time is in the green yet; let to-morrow answer.”", "때는 아직 무르익지 않았으니, 내일이 답하게 합시다.\""]], [["“Be it so,”", "\"그렇게 합시다.\""], ["said Balthasar, smiling.", "발타사르가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["And Ben-Hur said, “Be it so.”", "벤허도 \"그렇게 합시다\" 하고 말했다."], ["Then he went on: “But I have not yet done.", "그런 다음 그가 말을 이었다. \"허나 아직 끝나지 않았습니다."], ["From these things, not too great to be above suspicion by such as did not see them in performance as I did, let me carry you now to others infinitely greater, acknowledged since the world began to be past the power of man.", "나처럼 그 행함을 직접 보지 못한 이라면 의심해 봄 직도 한, 그러나 의심하기에는 너무도 큰 이 일들로부터, 이제 두 분을 한없이 더 큰 일들로 이끌고자 합니다. 세상이 시작된 이래로 사람의 힘을 넘어선 것이라 누구나 인정해 온 일들 말입니다."], ["Tell me, has any one to your knowledge ever reached out and taken from Death what Death has made his own?", "말씀해 보십시오, 두 분이 아시는 한, 죽음이 제 것으로 삼은 것을 손을 뻗어 죽음에게서 도로 빼앗은 사람이 일찍이 있었습니까?"], ["Who ever gave again the breath of a life lost? Who but—”", "잃어버린 생명에 다시 숨을 불어넣은 이가 누구입니까? 오직—\""]], [["“God!” said Balthasar, reverently.", "\"하나님이시지요!\" 발타사르가 경건하게 말했다."]], [["Ben-Hur bowed.", "벤허가 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“O wise Egyptian!", "\"오, 지혜로운 이집트인이여!"], ["I may not refuse the name you lend me.", "그대가 일러 주신 그 이름을 나도 마다하지 못하겠습니다."], ["What would you—or you, Simonides—what would you either or both have said had you seen as I did, a man, with few words and no ceremony, without effort more than a mother’s when she speaks to wake her child asleep, undo the work of Death?", "두 분께서는—아니, 시모니데스여, 그대는—내가 본 것을, 곧 한 사람이 몇 마디 말뿐 아무 의식도 없이, 잠든 아이를 깨우려 말 건네는 어머니의 수고보다도 더 힘들이지 않고 죽음이 한 일을 되돌려 놓는 것을 보셨다면, 둘 중 하나든 두 분 다든 무어라 하셨겠습니까?"], ["It was down at Nain.", "그것은 나인에서였습니다."], ["We were about going into the gate, when a company came out bearing a dead man.", "우리가 막 성문으로 들어가려 할 때, 한 무리가 죽은 사람을 메고 나왔습니다."], ["The Nazarene stopped to let the train pass.", "나사렛 사람은 그 행렬이 지나가도록 멈춰 섰습니다."], ["There was a woman among them crying.", "그들 가운데 한 여인이 울고 있었습니다."], ["I saw his face soften with pity.", "그의 얼굴이 연민으로 부드러워지는 것을 나는 보았습니다."], ["He spoke to her, then went and touched the bier, and said to him who lay upon it dressed for burial, ‘Young man, I say unto thee, Arise!’ And instantly the dead sat up and talked.”", "그가 그녀에게 말을 건넨 뒤, 다가가 상여에 손을 대고, 장례 차림으로 그 위에 누운 자에게 말했습니다. '청년아, 내가 네게 이르노니, 일어나라!' 그러자 곧바로 죽었던 자가 일어나 앉아 말을 했습니다.\""]], [["“God only is so great,”", "\"오직 하나님만이 그토록 위대하시오.\""], ["said Balthasar to Simonides.", "발타사르가 시모니데스에게 말했다."]], [["“Mark you,”", "\"새겨들으십시오.\""], ["Ben-Hur proceeded, “I do but tell you things of which I was a witness, together with a cloud of other men.", "벤허가 계속했다. \"나는 다른 수많은 사람들과 함께 내가 목격한 일들만을 말씀드리는 것입니다. 이리로 오는 길에 나는 더욱 강력한 또 한 가지 일을 보았습니다."], ["On the way hither I saw another act still more mighty.", "베다니에 나사로라는 사람이 있었는데, 그가 죽어 묻혔습니다."], ["In Bethany there was a man named Lazarus, who died and was buried; and after he had lain four days in a tomb, shut in by a great stone, the Nazarene was shown to the place.", "그가 큰 돌로 막힌 무덤 속에 나흘 동안이나 누워 있은 뒤, 나사렛 사람이 그곳으로 안내되었습니다. 돌을 굴려 치우자, 우리는 그 사람이 안에서 묶인 채 썩어 가며 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Upon rolling the stone away, we beheld the man lying inside bound and rotting.", "곁에 서 있는 사람이 많았는데, 나사렛 사람이 큰 소리로 말했으므로 우리 모두가 그 말을 들었습니다."], ["There were many people standing by, and we all heard what the Nazarene said, for he spoke in a loud voice: ‘Lazarus, come forth!’ I cannot tell you my feelings when in answer, as it were, the man arose and came out to us with all his cerements about him.", "'나사로야, 나오너라!' 그 부름에 답하기라도 하듯 그 사람이 온몸에 수의를 두른 채 일어나 우리에게로 걸어 나왔을 때, 내 심정이 어떠했는지는 차마 다 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 나사렛 사람이 다음으로 말했습니다."], ["‘Loose him,’ said the Nazarene next, ‘loose him, and let him go.’ And when the napkin was taken from the face of the resurrected, lo, my friends!", "'풀어 주라. 풀어 주어 가게 하라.' 그리고 그 되살아난 자의 얼굴에서 수건을 벗기니, 보십시오, 벗들이여!"], ["the blood ran anew through the wasted body, and he was exactly as he had been in life before the sickness that took him off. He lives yet, and is hourly seen and spoken to.", "시들어 버린 몸으로 피가 새로이 흘렀고, 그는 그를 데려간 병이 들기 전 살아 있을 때와 꼭 같았습니다. 그는 지금도 살아 있어, 시시각각 사람들이 그를 보고 그와 더불어 말합니다."], ["You may go see him to-morrow.", "두 분도 내일 가서 그를 보실 수 있습니다."], ["And now, as nothing more is needed for the purpose, I ask you that which I came to ask, it being but a repetition of what you asked me, O Simonides, What more than a man is this Nazarene?”", "자, 이제 이 목적을 위해 더 필요한 것이 없으니, 내가 여쭈러 온 것을 여쭙겠습니다. 그것은 시모니데스여, 그대가 내게 물으신 것을 되풀이하는 것에 지나지 않습니다. 이 나사렛 사람은 사람 이상의 무엇입니까?\""]], [["The question was put solemnly, and long after midnight the company sat and debated it; Simonides being yet unwilling to give up his understanding of the sayings of the prophets, and Ben-Hur contending that the elder disputants were both right—that the Nazarene was the Redeemer, as claimed by Balthasar, and also the destined king the merchant would have.", "그 물음은 엄숙하게 던져졌고, 자정이 한참 지나도록 일행은 앉아서 그것을 두고 토론했다. 시모니데스는 여전히 예언자들의 말씀에 대한 제 나름의 이해를 버리려 하지 않았고, 벤허는 두 연로한 논객이 둘 다 옳다고—발타사르가 주장하는 대로 나사렛 사람이 구속자이며, 동시에 상인이 바라는 정해진 왕이기도 하다고 우겼다."]], [["“To-morrow we will see. Peace to you all.”", "\"내일이면 알게 되겠지요. 두 분 모두에게 평안이 있기를.\""]], [["So saying, Ben-Hur took his leave, intending to return to Bethany.", "이렇게 말하고 벤허는 작별을 고했으니, 베다니로 돌아갈 작정이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["The first person to go out of the city upon the opening of the Sheep’s Gate next morning was Amrah, basket on arm.", "이튿날 아침, 양의 문이 열리자 가장 먼저 성을 나선 사람은 팔에 바구니를 건 암라였다."], ["No questions were asked her by the keepers, since the morning itself had not been more regular in coming than she; they knew her somebody’s faithful servant, and that was enough for them.", "문지기들은 그녀에게 아무것도 묻지 않았으니, 아침이 어김없이 찾아오듯 그녀 또한 어김없이 찾아왔기 때문이다. 그들은 그녀가 누군가의 충실한 하인이라는 것을 알았고, 그것으로 충분했다."]], [["Down the eastern valley she took her way.", "그녀는 동쪽 골짜기로 길을 잡았다."], ["The side of Olivet, darkly green, was spotted with white tents recently put up by people attending the feasts; the hour, however, was too early for the strangers to be abroad; still, had it not been so, no one would have troubled her.", "짙푸른 올리벳 산비탈에는 명절을 지키러 온 사람들이 최근에 세운 흰 천막들이 점점이 박혀 있었다. 그러나 이방인들이 나돌아 다니기에는 너무 이른 시각이었다. 설령 그렇지 않았더라도 아무도 그녀를 성가시게 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["Past Gethsemane; past the tombs at the meeting of the Bethany roads; past the sepulchral village of Siloam she went.", "그녀는 겟세마네를 지나고, 베다니로 가는 길이 만나는 곳의 무덤들을 지나고, 무덤이 많은 마을 실로암을 지나갔다. 이따금 노쇠한 작은 몸이 비틀거렸다."], ["Occasionally the decrepit little body staggered; once she sat down to get her breath; rising shortly, she struggled on with renewed haste.", "한 번은 숨을 고르려고 주저앉았다가, 곧 다시 일어나 새로운 조급함으로 발걸음을 재촉했다. 길 양옆의 큰 바위들에 귀가 있었다면, 그녀가 혼자 중얼거리는 소리를 들었을지도 모른다. 눈이 있었다면, 그녀가 얼마나 자주 산 너머를 올려다보며 새벽이 너무 일찍 온 것을 나무라는지 보았을 것이다."], ["The great rocks on either hand, if they had had ears, might have heard her mutter to herself; could they have seen, it would have been to observe how frequently she looked up over the Mount, reproving the dawn for its promptness; if it had been possible for them to gossip, not improbably they would have said to each other, “Our friend is in a hurry this morning; the mouths she goes to feed must be very hungry.”", "만약 바위들이 수다를 떨 수 있었다면, 서로 이렇게 말했을지도 모른다. \"우리 친구가 오늘 아침엔 급한가 보군. 먹여 살릴 입들이 어지간히 굶주린 모양이야.\""]], [["When at last she reached the King’s Garden she slackened her gait; for then the grim city of the lepers was in view, extending far round the pitted south hill of Hinnom.", "마침내 왕의 정원에 이르자 그녀는 걸음을 늦추었다. 이제 한놈의 골짜기 남쪽 언덕을 멀리 두르고 펼쳐진 음산한 나병 환자들의 도시가 눈에 들어왔기 때문이다."]], [["As the reader must by this time have surmised, she was going to her mistress, whose tomb, it will be remembered, overlooked the well En-Rogel.", "독자도 이쯤이면 짐작했겠지만, 그녀는 자기 여주인을 찾아가는 길이었다. 기억하겠지만, 그 여주인의 무덤은 엔로겔 우물을 내려다보는 자리에 있었다."]], [["Early as it was, the unhappy woman was up and sitting outside, leaving Tirzah asleep within.", "이른 시각이었으나, 그 불행한 여인은 일어나 밖에 앉아 있었고, 안에는 티르자가 잠들어 있었다."], ["The course of the malady had been terribly swift in the three years.", "지난 삼 년 동안 병세는 무섭도록 빠르게 진행되었다."], ["Conscious of her appearance, with the refined instincts of her nature, she kept her whole person habitually covered.", "자기 모습을 의식한 그녀는 본디 섬세한 천성으로, 늘 온몸을 가린 채 지냈다."], ["Seldom as possible she permitted even Tirzah to see her.", "티르자에게조차도 되도록 자기 모습을 보이지 않았다."]], [["This morning she was taking the air with bared head, knowing there was no one to be shocked by the exposure.", "이날 아침 그녀는 맨머리로 바람을 쐬고 있었으니, 그 드러난 모습에 충격받을 사람이 아무도 없음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["The light was not full, but enough to show the ravages to which she had been subject.", "빛은 충분치 않았으나, 그녀가 겪어 온 참혹한 변화를 드러내기에는 모자라지 않았다."], ["Her hair was snow-white and unmanageably coarse, falling over her back and shoulders like so much silver wire.", "머리카락은 눈처럼 희고 손쓸 수 없이 거칠어, 수많은 은실처럼 등과 어깨로 흘러내렸다."], ["The eyelids, the lips, the nostrils, the flesh of the cheeks, were either gone or reduced to fetid rawness.", "눈꺼풀과 입술과 콧구멍과 두 뺨의 살은 사라졌거나, 악취 나는 짓무른 살로 변해 있었다."], ["The neck was a mass of ash-colored scales.", "목은 잿빛 비늘 덩어리였다."], ["One hand lay outside the folds of her habit rigid as that of a skeleton; the nails had been eaten away; the joints of the fingers, if not bare to the bone, were swollen knots crusted with red secretion.", "한쪽 손은 옷자락 밖으로 나와 해골처럼 뻣뻣하게 놓여 있었다. 손톱은 다 파먹혔고, 손가락 마디는 뼈까지 드러나지는 않았더라도, 붉은 분비물로 딱지가 앉은 부어오른 혹이었다."], ["Head, face, neck, and hand indicated all too plainly the condition of the whole body.", "머리와 얼굴과 목과 손은 온몸의 상태가 어떠한지를 너무도 또렷이 일러 주었다."], ["Seeing her thus, it was easy to understand how the once fair widow of the princely Hur had been able to maintain her incognito so well through such a period of years.", "이러한 모습을 보면, 한때 어여뻤던 후르 가문의 미망인이 그토록 여러 해 동안 어떻게 그렇게 잘 정체를 숨길 수 있었는지 쉬이 이해할 수 있었다."]], [["When the sun would gild the crest of Olivet and the Mount of Offence with light sharper and more brilliant in that old land than in the West, she knew Amrah would come, first to the well, then to a stone midway the well and the foot of the hill on which she had her abode, and that the good servant would there deposit the food she carried in the basket, and fill the water-jar afresh for the day.", "해가 그 옛 땅에서 서방보다 한결 날카롭고 눈부신 빛으로 올리벳 산마루와 멸시의 산마루를 금빛으로 물들일 무렵이면, 그녀는 암라가 오리라는 것을 알았다. 먼저 우물로, 다음에는 우물과 그녀가 거처하는 언덕 기슭 중간의 돌로 와서, 그 착한 하인이 바구니에 담아 온 음식을 그곳에 내려놓고 그날 쓸 물항아리를 새로 채워 두리라는 것을. 한때 넘치도록 누렸던 행복 가운데, 이 불행한 여인에게 남은 것은 그 짧은 방문뿐이었다."], ["Of her former plentitude of happiness, that brief visit was all that remained to the unfortunate.", "그때면 그녀는 아들의 소식을 물을 수 있었고, 그가 잘 지낸다는 말과, 심부름꾼이 주워 모을 수 있는 만큼의 소식을 들을 수 있었다."], ["She could then ask about her son, and be told of his welfare, with such bits of news concerning him as the messenger could glean.", "대개 그 소식은 매우 빈약했으나, 그래도 위안이 되었다. 때때로 그녀는 아들이 집에 와 있다는 말을 들었다."], ["Usually the information was meagre enough, yet comforting; at times she heard he was at home; then she would issue from her dreary cell at break of day, and sit till noon, and from noon to set of sun, a motionless figure draped in white, looking, statue-like, invariably to one point—over the Temple to the spot under the rounded sky where the old house stood, dear in memory, and dearer because he was there.", "그러면 그녀는 동틀 녘에 음산한 굴에서 나와 정오까지, 다시 정오부터 해질녘까지, 흰옷을 두른 채 미동도 없이 앉아, 마치 조각상처럼 늘 한 곳만을 바라보았다. 성전 너머, 둥근 하늘 아래, 기억 속에 정겨운, 그리고 아들이 그곳에 있기에 더욱 정겨운 옛집이 서 있던 그 자리를. 그녀에게 남은 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 없었다."], ["Nothing else was left her.", "티르자는 죽은 자로 쳤다."], ["Tirzah she counted of the dead; and as for herself, she simply waited the end, knowing every hour of life was an hour of dying—happily, of painless dying.", "그리고 자기 자신으로 말하자면, 삶의 매 순간이 죽어 가는 순간임을, 다행히도 고통 없이 죽어 가는 순간임을 알면서, 그저 끝을 기다릴 뿐이었다."]], [["The things of nature about the hill to keep her sensitive to the world’s attractions were wretchedly scant; beasts and birds avoided the place as if they knew its history and present use; every green thing perished in its first season; the winds warred upon the shrubs and venturous grasses, leaving to drought such as they could not uproot.", "언덕 둘레에서 그녀를 세상의 매력에 마음 쓰게 할 만한 자연의 것들은 비참하리만치 모자랐다. 짐승과 새들은 마치 그 내력과 지금의 쓰임새를 알기라도 하듯 그곳을 피했다. 푸른 것은 무엇이든 첫 철에 시들어 버렸다."], ["Look where she would, the view was made depressingly suggestive by tombs—tombs above her, tombs below, tombs opposite her own tomb—all now freshly whitened in warning to visiting pilgrims.", "바람은 관목과 위태로이 돋은 풀들과 싸워, 뽑아내지 못한 것은 가뭄에 내맡겼다. 어디를 바라보든, 무덤들이 그 풍경을 우울하게 물들였다. 그녀의 위에도 무덤, 아래에도 무덤, 그녀의 무덤 맞은편에도 무덤이었으니, 이제 모두 순례객들에게 경고하느라 새로 하얗게 칠해져 있었다."], ["In the sky—clear, fair, inviting—one would think she might have found some relief to her ache of mind; but, alas!", "맑고 고와 마음을 끄는 하늘에서는 마음의 아픔을 덜어 줄 무언가를 찾을 수 있으리라 여길 법도 했다."], ["in making the beautiful elsewhere the sun served her never so unfriendly—it did but disclose her growing hideousness.", "그러나 아아, 다른 곳을 아름답게 만들면서도 해는 그녀에게는 결코 그토록 매정하게 굴었으니, 다만 점점 더해 가는 그녀의 흉측함을 드러낼 뿐이었다."], ["But for the sun she would not have been the horror she was to herself, nor been waked so cruelly from dreams of Tirzah as she used to be.", "해만 없었더라면, 그녀는 스스로에게 그토록 끔찍한 존재가 되지도 않았을 것이며, 예전처럼 티르자의 꿈에서 그토록 잔인하게 깨어나지도 않았을 것이다."], ["The gift of seeing can be sometimes a dreadful curse.", "본다는 재능이 때로는 무서운 저주가 될 수도 있는 것이다."]], [["Does one ask why she did not make an end to her sufferings?", "어찌하여 그녀가 자기 고통을 끝내지 않았는지 묻는 이가 있을까? 율법이 그것을 금했기 때문이다!"]], [["# THE LAW FORBADE HER!", "# THE LAW FORBADE HER!"]], [["A Gentile may smile at the answer; but so will not a son of Israel.", "이방인이라면 이 대답에 웃을지도 모른다. 그러나 이스라엘의 자손이라면 결코 웃지 않으리라."]], [["While she sat there peopling the dusky solitude with thoughts even more cheerless, suddenly a woman came up the hill staggering and spent with exertion.", "그녀가 그곳에 앉아, 한층 더 음울한 생각들로 그 어스레한 적막을 채우고 있을 때, 갑자기 한 여인이 비틀거리며, 안간힘을 다 쓴 듯 지친 모습으로 언덕을 올라왔다."]], [["The widow arose hastily, and covering her head, cried, in a voice unnaturally harsh, “Unclean, unclean!”", "미망인은 황급히 일어나, 머리를 가리고는, 부자연스레 거친 목소리로 외쳤다. \"부정한 몸이다, 부정한 몸이야!\""]], [["In a moment, heedless of the notice, Amrah was at her feet.", "그러나 암라는 그 경고에 아랑곳없이 한순간에 그녀의 발치에 엎드렸다."], ["All the long-pent love of the simple creature burst forth: with tears and passionate exclamations she kissed her mistress’s garments, and for a while the latter strove to escape from her; then, seeing she could not, she waited till the violence of the paroxysm was over.", "그 순박한 여인이 오래 가두어 둔 사랑이 한꺼번에 터져 나왔다. 그녀는 눈물과 격정 어린 부르짖음으로 여주인의 옷에 입을 맞추었고, 여주인은 한동안 그녀에게서 벗어나려 애썼으나, 그럴 수 없음을 깨닫고는 그 격발의 격정이 가라앉기를 기다렸다."]], [["“What have you done, Amrah?”", "\"무슨 짓을 한 거냐, 암라?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Is it by such disobedience you prove your love for us? Wicked woman!", "\"이런 불순종으로 우리를 향한 네 사랑을 증명하겠다는 거냐? 어리석은 여인 같으니!"], ["You are lost; and he—your master—you can never, never go back to him.”", "너는 이제 끝이다. 그리고 그분, 네 주인에게로 너는 결코, 결코 돌아갈 수 없게 되었어.\""]], [["Amrah grovelled sobbing in the dust.", "암라는 흐느끼며 흙바닥에 엎드렸다."]], [["“The ban of the Law is upon you, too; you cannot return to Jerusalem.", "\"율법의 금령이 이제 네게도 내린 거야. 너는 예루살렘으로 돌아갈 수 없어."], ["What will become of us?", "우리는 어찌 되겠느냐?"], ["Who will bring us bread?", "누가 우리에게 빵을 가져다주겠어?"], ["O wicked, wicked Amrah!", "오, 어리석고 어리석은 암라야!"], ["We are all, all undone alike!”", "우리는 모두, 모두 똑같이 끝장나고 말았구나!\""]], [["“Mercy, mercy!” Amrah answered from the ground.", "\"용서를, 용서를 해 주세요!\" 암라가 땅바닥에서 대답했다."]], [["“You should have been merciful to yourself, and by so doing been most merciful to us.", "\"너 자신에게 자비를 베풀었어야지. 그랬다면 우리에게 가장 큰 자비를 베푸는 것이 되었을 텐데."], ["Now where can we fly?", "이제 우리가 어디로 달아나겠느냐?"], ["There is no one to help us.", "우리를 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없어."], ["O false servant!", "오, 못난 하인아!"], ["The wrath of the Lord was already too heavy upon us.”", "주님의 진노가 이미 우리에게 너무도 무겁게 내려 있거늘.\""]], [["Here Tirzah, awakened by the noise, appeared at the door of the tomb.", "이때 그 소리에 잠을 깬 티르자가 무덤 입구에 나타났다."], ["The pen shrinks from the picture she presented.", "붓으로는 그녀가 드러낸 모습을 차마 그릴 수가 없다."], ["In the half-clad apparition, patched with scales, lividly seamed, nearly blind, its limbs and extremities swollen to grotesque largeness, familiar eyes however sharpened by love could not have recognized the creature of childish grace and purity we first beheld her.", "반쯤 옷을 걸친 그 허깨비 같은 모습은 비늘로 덮이고, 검푸르게 갈라지고, 거의 앞을 보지 못했으며, 팔다리와 손발은 흉측하게 커다랗도록 부어 있었으니, 사랑으로 아무리 눈이 밝아진들 낯익은 눈으로도, 우리가 처음 보았던 그 어린아이다운 우아함과 순결함을 지닌 존재를 알아볼 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Is it Amrah, mother?”", "\"암라예요, 어머니?\""]], [["The servant tried to crawl to her also.", "하인은 그녀에게도 기어가려 했다."]], [["“Stay, Amrah!”", "\"멈춰라, 암라!\""], ["the widow cried, imperiously.", "미망인이 위엄 있게 외쳤다."], ["“I forbid you touching her.", "\"저 아이를 만지는 것을 금한다."], ["Rise, and get you gone before any at the well see you here.", "일어나서, 우물가의 누구든 너를 보기 전에 어서 가거라. 아니, 잊고 있었구나."], ["Nay, I forgot—it is too late! You must remain now and share our doom.", "이제 너무 늦었어! 너는 이제 남아서 우리의 운명을 함께 나눌 수밖에 없다."], ["Rise, I say!”", "일어나라니까!\""]], [["Amrah rose to her knees, and said, brokenly and with clasped hands, “O good mistress!", "암라가 무릎을 꿇고 일어나, 두 손을 맞잡은 채 더듬거리며 말했다. \"오, 착하신 마님!"], ["I am not false—I am not wicked.", "저는 거짓되지도, 사악하지도 않습니다."], ["I bring you good tidings.”", "좋은 소식을 가져왔어요.\""]], [["“Of Judah?” and as she spoke, the widow half withdrew the cloth from her head.", "\"유다 소식이냐?\" 그녀가 말하며, 머리에서 천을 반쯤 걷어 냈다."]], [["“There is a wonderful man,”", "\"놀라운 분이 있는데,\""], ["Amrah continued, “who has power to cure you.", "암라가 말을 이었다. \"마님을 고칠 능력이 있답니다."], ["He speaks a word, and the sick are made well, and even the dead come to life.", "그분이 한마디 말씀을 하시면, 병든 자가 낫고, 죽은 자까지도 살아난답니다."], ["I have come to take you to him.”", "제가 마님을 그분에게 모셔 가려고 왔어요.\""]], [["“Poor Amrah!”", "\"가엾은 암라야!\""], ["said Tirzah, compassionately.", "티르자가 가엾이 여기며 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요,\""], ["cried Amrah, detecting the doubt underlying the expression—“no, as the Lord lives, even the Lord of Israel, my God as well as yours, I speak the truth.", "암라가 그 말 아래 깔린 의심을 알아채고 외쳤다. \"아니에요, 주님께서, 곧 이스라엘의 주님이시며 마님의 하느님이자 제 하느님이신 그분께서 살아 계심을 두고, 저는 진실을 말하고 있어요."], ["Go with me, I pray, and lose no time.", "저와 함께 가세요, 부디, 머뭇거리지 마시고요."], ["This morning he will pass by on his way to the city. See!", "오늘 아침 그분이 성으로 가시는 길에 이리로 지나가십니다. 보세요!"], ["the day is at hand.", "날이 밝아 옵니다."], ["Take the food here—eat, and let us go.”", "여기 음식을 드세요. 잡수시고, 어서 가십시다.\""]], [["The mother listened eagerly.", "어머니는 귀를 곤두세우고 들었다."], ["Not unlikely she had heard of the wonderful man, for by this time his fame had penetrated every nook in the land.", "그녀도 그 놀라운 사람의 소문을 들었을 법했으니, 이미 그의 명성이 온 땅 구석구석까지 스며들었기 때문이다."]], [["“Who is he?”", "\"그가 누구냐?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“A Nazarene.”", "\"나사렛 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Who told you about him?”", "\"누가 그에 대해 말해 주더냐?\""]], [["“Judah.”", "\"유다 도련님이요.\""]], [["“Judah told you?", "\"유다가 네게 말했다고?"], ["Is he at home?”", "그 아이가 집에 있느냐?\""]], [["“He came last night.”", "\"어젯밤에 오셨어요.\""]], [["The widow, trying to still the beating of her heart, was silent awhile.", "미망인은 두근거리는 가슴을 가라앉히려 애쓰며 한동안 말이 없었다."]], [["“Did Judah send you to tell us this?”", "\"유다가 우리에게 이것을 알리라고 너를 보냈느냐?\""], ["she next asked.", "그녀가 이윽고 물었다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니에요."], ["He believes you dead.”", "도련님은 마님이 돌아가신 줄로 알고 계세요.\""]], [["“There was a prophet once who cured a leper,”", "\"옛적에 나병 환자를 고친 예언자가 있었지.\""], ["the mother said thoughtfully to Tirzah; “but he had his power from God.”", "어머니가 생각에 잠겨 티르자에게 말했다. \"하지만 그는 하느님께로부터 그 능력을 받았던 거란다.\""], ["Then addressing Amrah, she asked, “How does my son know this man so possessed?”", "그러고는 암라를 향해 물었다. \"내 아들은 그 사람이 그런 능력을 지녔다는 것을 어찌 알았다더냐?\""]], [["“He was travelling with him, and heard the lepers call, and saw them go away well.", "\"그분과 함께 길을 가시다가, 나병 환자들이 부르짖는 소리를 듣고, 그들이 다 나아 떠나가는 것을 보셨답니다."], ["First there was one man; then there were ten; and they were all made whole.”", "처음엔 한 사람이었고, 다음엔 열 사람이었는데, 모두 온전해졌답니다.\""]], [["The elder listener was silent again.", "연배 든 이는 다시 입을 다물었다."], ["The skeleton hand shook.", "해골 같은 손이 떨렸다."], ["We may believe she was struggling to give the story the sanction of faith, which is always an absolutist in demand, and that it was with her as with the men of the day, eye-witnesses of what was done by the Christ, as well as the myriads who have succeeded them.", "그녀가 그 이야기에 믿음의 인정을 부여하려고 애쓰고 있었다고 우리는 믿어도 좋으리라. 믿음이란 언제나 절대적인 것을 요구하는 법이다. 그녀도 그 시대 사람들, 곧 그리스도가 행한 일을 직접 목격한 자들이나, 그 뒤를 이은 수많은 무리와 다를 바 없었으리라."], ["She did not question the performance, for her own son was the witness testifying through the servant; but she strove to comprehend the power by which work so astonishing could be done by a man.", "그녀는 그 일이 행해졌다는 것을 의심하지는 않았으니, 다름 아닌 자기 아들이 하인을 통해 증언하는 증인이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀는 한낱 사람이 어떻게 그토록 놀라운 일을 해낼 수 있는지, 그 능력의 정체를 헤아리려 애썼다."], ["Well enough to make inquiry as to the fact; to comprehend the power, on the other hand, it is first necessary to comprehend God; and he who waits for that will die waiting.", "사실 여부를 캐묻는 것은 좋은 일이다. 그러나 그 능력을 헤아리려면, 먼저 하느님을 헤아려야 하는 법이니, 그것을 기다리는 자는 기다리다가 죽고 말 것이다."], ["With her, however, the hesitation was brief. To Tirzah she said,", "그러나 그녀의 망설임은 잠시였다. 그녀가 티르자에게 말했다."]], [["“This must be the Messiah!”", "\"이분은 틀림없이 메시아일 거야!\""]], [["She spoke not coldly, like one reasoning a doubt away, but as a woman of Israel familiar with the promises of God to her race—a woman of understanding, ready to be glad over the least sign of the realization of the promises.", "그녀는 의심을 논리로 떨쳐 내려는 사람처럼 차갑게 말한 것이 아니라, 자기 민족을 향한 하느님의 약속에 익숙한 이스라엘의 여인으로서, 그 약속이 이루어지는 가장 작은 표징에도 기꺼이 기뻐할 준비가 된, 깨우친 여인으로서 말했다."]], [["“There was a time when Jerusalem and all Judea were filled with a story that he was born.", "\"한때 예루살렘과 온 유대가 그분이 태어났다는 이야기로 가득 찼던 때가 있었지. 나는 기억한단다."], ["I remember it. By this time he should be a man.", "지금쯤이면 그분도 어른이 되셨을 거야. 틀림없어."], ["It must be—it is he. Yes,”", "바로 그분이야. 그래,\""], ["she said to Amrah, “we will go with you.", "그녀가 암라에게 말했다. \"우리가 너와 함께 가마."], ["Bring the water which you will find in the tomb in a jar, and set the food for us.", "무덤 안에 항아리에 든 물을 가져오고, 우리에게 음식을 차려 주거라."], ["We will eat and be gone.”", "먹고 나서 떠나자꾸나.\""]], [["The breakfast, partaken under excitement, was soon despatched, and the three women set out on their extraordinary journey.", "흥분 속에 든 아침 식사는 이내 끝났고, 세 여인은 그 예사롭지 않은 여정에 올랐다."], ["As Tirzah had caught the confident spirit of the others, there was but one fear that troubled the party.", "티르자도 다른 두 사람의 자신에 찬 마음을 받아들였으니, 일행을 괴롭히는 두려움은 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["Bethany, Amrah said, was the town the man was coming from; now from that to Jerusalem there were three roads, or rather paths—one over the first summit of Olivet, a second at its base, a third between the second summit and the Mount of Offence.", "암라의 말로는 그 사람이 베다니에서 오고 있다는 것이었다. 그런데 그곳에서 예루살렘으로 가는 길은 셋, 더 정확히 말하면 세 갈래의 작은 길이 있었다. 하나는 올리벳의 첫째 봉우리를 넘는 길이요, 둘째는 그 기슭을 지나는 길이며, 셋째는 둘째 봉우리와 멸시의 산 사이로 난 길이었다."], ["The three were not far apart; far enough, however, to make it possible for the unfortunates to miss the Nazarene if they failed the one he chose to come by.", "세 길은 서로 멀지 않았으나, 그 불행한 이들이 나사렛 사람이 택해 오는 길을 놓치면 그를 만나지 못할 만큼은 떨어져 있었다."]], [["A little questioning satisfied the mother that Amrah knew nothing of the country beyond the Cedron, and even less of the intentions of the man they were going to see, if they could.", "조금 캐물어 본 어머니는 암라가 키드론 너머의 땅에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 것을, 그리고 그들이 만나려는 그 사람의 뜻에 대해서는 더더욱 모른다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["She discerned, also, that both Amrah and Tirzah—the one from confirmed habits of servitude, the other from natural dependency—looked to her for guidance; and she accepted the charge.", "또한 그녀는 암라와 티르자 둘 다, 하나는 몸에 밴 종살이 습성 탓에, 다른 하나는 타고난 의존심 탓에, 자신에게 길잡이 노릇을 기대하고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그래서 그 책임을 떠맡았다."]], [["“We will go first to Bethphage,”", "\"우리는 먼저 벳바게로 가자.\""], ["she said to them.", "그녀가 그들에게 말했다."], ["“There, if the Lord favor us, we may learn what else to do.”", "\"거기서 주님께서 우리를 도우시면, 그다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 알게 될 거다.\""]], [["They descended the hill to Tophet and the King’s Garden, and paused in the deep trail furrowed through them by centuries of wayfaring.", "그들은 언덕을 내려가 도벳과 왕의 정원에 이르러, 수백 년 동안 길손들이 밟아 깊이 파인 자취 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“I am afraid of the road,”", "\"나는 저 길이 두렵구나.\""], ["the matron said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["“Better that we keep to the country among the rocks and trees.", "\"차라리 바위와 나무들 사이로 들판을 따라가는 편이 낫겠어."], ["This is feast-day, and on the hill-sides yonder I see signs of a great multitude in attendance.", "오늘은 명절날이라, 저 산비탈에 큰 무리가 모여든 기색이 보이는구나."], ["By going across the Mount of Offence here we may avoid them.”", "여기 멸시의 산을 가로질러 가면 그들을 피할 수 있을 게다.\""]], [["Tirzah had been walking with great difficulty; upon hearing this her heart began to fail her.", "티르자는 무척 힘겹게 걷고 있었다. 이 말을 듣자 그녀의 마음이 약해지기 시작했다."]], [["“The mount is steep, mother; I cannot climb it.”", "\"산이 가파른걸요, 어머니. 저는 못 오르겠어요.\""]], [["“Remember, we are going to find health and life.", "\"명심하렴, 우리는 건강과 생명을 찾으러 가는 길이란다."], ["See, my child, how the day brightens around us!", "보아라, 얘야, 우리 둘레로 날이 얼마나 환히 밝아 오는지!"], ["And yonder are women coming this way to the well. They will stone us if we stay here.", "그리고 저기 여인들이 우물로 이리 오고 있구나. 우리가 여기 머물면 저들이 우리에게 돌을 던질 거야."], ["Come, be strong this once.”", "자, 이번 한 번만 굳세어지렴.\""]], [["Thus the mother, not less tortured herself, sought to inspire the daughter; and Amrah came to her aid.", "이렇게 어머니는, 자기 자신도 못지않게 괴로우면서도 딸에게 용기를 북돋우려 했고, 암라가 그녀를 거들었다."], ["To this time the latter had not touched the persons of the afflicted, nor they her; now, in disregard of consequences as well as of command, the faithful creature went to Tirzah, and put her arm over her shoulder, and whispered, “Lean on me.", "이때까지 암라는 병든 두 사람의 몸에 손을 대지 않았고, 그들도 암라에게 손을 대지 않았다. 그러나 이제 그 충실한 여인은 그 결과도, 명령도 아랑곳하지 않고 티르자에게 다가가, 그 어깨에 팔을 두르고 속삭였다. \"제게 기대세요."], ["I am strong, though I am old; and it is but a little way off.", "저는 늙었어도 튼튼해요. 그리고 이제 얼마 안 남았어요."], ["There—now we can go.”", "자, 이제 갈 수 있어요.\""]], [["The face of the hill they essayed to cross was somewhat broken with pits, and ruins of old structures; but when at last they stood upon the top to rest, and looked at the spectacle presented them over in the northwest—at the Temple and its courtly terraces, at Zion, at the enduring towers white beetling into the sky beyond—the mother was strengthened with a love of life for life’s sake.", "그들이 넘으려는 언덕 비탈은 구덩이며 옛 건물의 잔해로 다소 험했다. 그러나 마침내 꼭대기에 올라 쉬며, 북서쪽 너머로 펼쳐진 광경을 바라보았을 때, 곧 성전과 그 화려한 층계를, 시온을, 그리고 그 너머 하늘로 희게 솟아오른 든든한 탑들을 바라보았을 때, 어머니는 삶 그 자체를 향한 사랑으로 새 힘을 얻었다."]], [["“Look, Tirzah,”", "\"보아라, 티르자,\""], ["she said—“look at the plates of gold on the Gate Beautiful.", "그녀가 말했다. \"아름다운 문에 입힌 금판들을 보렴."], ["How they give back the flames of the sun, brightness for brightness!", "저것들이 햇살을 어떻게 되비추는지, 빛에 빛으로 화답하는구나!"], ["Do you remember we used to go up there?", "우리가 저기 올라가곤 했던 것을 기억하느냐?"], ["Will it not be pleasant to do so again?", "다시 그리할 수 있다면 얼마나 즐겁겠니?"], ["And think—home is but a little way off.", "그리고 생각해 보렴, 집이 바로 코앞이야."], ["I can almost see it over the roof of the Holy of Holies; and Judah will be there to receive us!”", "지성소 지붕 너머로 거의 보일 듯하구나. 그리고 유다가 거기서 우리를 맞아 줄 게다!\""]], [["From the side of the middle summit garnished green with myrtle and olive trees, they saw, upon looking that way next, thin columns of smoke rising lightly and straight up into the pulseless morning, each a warning of restless pilgrims astir, and of the flight of the pitiless hours, and the need of haste.", "도금양과 올리브 나무로 푸르게 단장한 가운데 봉우리 쪽에서, 그들이 그쪽을 바라보자, 맥박 없이 고요한 아침 공기 속으로 가느다란 연기 기둥들이 가볍고 곧게 피어오르고 있었으니, 저마다 분주히 움직이는 순례객들과, 무정하게 흘러가는 시간과, 서둘러야 할 까닭을 일러 주는 경고였다."]], [["Though the good servant toiled faithfully to lighten the labor in descending the hill-side, not sparing herself in the least, the girl moaned at every step; sometimes in extremity of anguish she cried out.", "착한 하인이 언덕 비탈을 내려가는 수고를 덜어 주려고 자신을 조금도 아끼지 않고 충실히 애썼으나, 소녀는 걸음마다 신음했고, 때로는 견딜 수 없는 고통에 부르짖었다."], ["Upon reaching the road—that is, the road between the Mount of Offence and the middle or second summit of Olivet—she fell down exhausted.", "길에, 곧 멸시의 산과 올리벳의 가운데 봉우리, 곧 둘째 봉우리 사이로 난 길에 다다르자, 그녀는 기진하여 쓰러졌다."]], [["“Go on with Amrah, mother, and leave me here,”", "\"어머니, 암라와 함께 가세요. 저는 여기 두고요.\""], ["she said, faintly.", "그녀가 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["“No, no, Tirzah. What would the gain be to me if I were healed and you not?", "\"아니, 안 된다, 티르자. 내가 낫고 네가 낫지 못한다면, 그게 내게 무슨 소용이 있겠니?"], ["When Judah asks for you, as he will, what would I have to say to him were I to leave you?”", "유다가, 틀림없이 그럴 텐데, 너를 찾을 때, 내가 너를 두고 왔다면 그 아이에게 무슨 말을 하겠느냐?\""]], [["“Tell him I loved him.”", "\"제가 도련님을 사랑했다고 전해 주세요.\""]], [["The elder leper arose from bending over the fainting sufferer, and gazed about her with that sensation of hope perishing which is more nearly like annihilation of the soul than anything else.", "연배 든 나병 환자는 까무러친 가엾은 딸 위로 굽혔던 몸을 일으켜, 무엇보다 영혼이 사멸되는 것에 가장 가까운 그 절망의 느낌으로 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["The supremest joy of the thought of cure was inseparable from Tirzah, who was not too old to forget, in the happiness of healthful life to come, the years of misery by which she had been so reduced in body and broken in spirit.", "치유의 생각이 주는 더없는 기쁨은 티르자와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 것이었으니, 그 아이는 앞으로 누릴 건강한 삶의 행복 속에서 몸이 그토록 쇠하고 마음이 그토록 꺾인 그 비참했던 세월을 잊기에 너무 나이 든 것도 아니었다."], ["Even as the brave woman was about leaving the venture they were engaged in to the determination of God, she saw a man on foot coming rapidly up the road from the east.", "그 의연한 여인이 자신들이 나선 그 모험을 하느님의 뜻에 막 내맡기려던 바로 그때, 그녀는 한 남자가 동쪽에서 길을 따라 빠르게 걸어 올라오는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Courage, Tirzah!", "\"용기를 내렴, 티르자!"], ["Be of cheer,”", "기운을 내거라.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Yonder I know is one to tell us of the Nazarene.”", "\"저기 오는 이가 나사렛 사람에 대해 우리에게 일러 줄 사람인 게 틀림없구나.\""]], [["Amrah helped the girl to a sitting posture, and supported her while the man advanced.", "암라가 소녀를 부축해 앉히고, 사내가 다가오는 동안 그녀를 받쳐 주었다."]], [["“In your goodness, mother, you forget what we are.", "\"어머니, 어머니는 마음이 고우셔서 우리가 어떤 처지인지 잊으시는군요."], ["The stranger will go around us; his best gift to us will be a curse, if not a stone.”", "저 나그네는 우리를 빙 둘러 갈 거예요. 그가 우리에게 베풀 가장 큰 선물이라야 저주일 테고, 아니면 돌멩이겠지요.\""]], [["“We will see.”", "\"두고 보자꾸나.\""]], [["There was no other answer to be given, since the mother was too well and sadly acquainted with the treatment outcasts of the class to which she belonged were accustomed to at the hands of her countrymen.", "이 밖에 달리 해 줄 대답은 없었으니, 어머니는 자기가 속한 그 부류의 버림받은 자들이 동족의 손에서 으레 받아 온 대접을 너무도 잘, 그리고 슬프게도 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["As has been said, the road at the edge of which the group was posted was little more than a worn path or trail, winding crookedly through tumuli of limestone.", "앞서 말했듯, 이 무리가 자리 잡은 길가는, 석회암 둔덕들 사이로 구불구불 뻗은, 닳고 닳은 오솔길, 곧 자취에 지나지 않았다."], ["If the stranger kept it, he must meet them face to face; and he did so, until near enough to hear the cry she was bound to give.", "나그네가 그 길을 계속 간다면, 그들과 얼굴을 마주칠 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 그는 그렇게 다가왔으니, 그녀가 마땅히 외쳐야 할 소리를 들을 만큼 가까워질 때까지였다."], ["Then, uncovering her head, a further demand of the law, she shouted shrilly,", "그러자 그녀는 율법이 또한 요구하는 대로 머리를 드러내고, 날카롭게 외쳤다."]], [["“Unclean, unclean!”", "\"부정한 몸이다, 부정한 몸이야!\""]], [["To her surprise, the man came steadily on.", "그녀가 놀랍게도, 사내는 망설임 없이 다가왔다."]], [["“What would you have?”", "\"무엇을 원하시오?\""], ["he asked, stopping opposite them not four yards off.", "그가 그들 맞은편 네 걸음도 채 안 되는 곳에 멈춰 서서 물었다."]], [["“Thou seest us.", "\"우리를 보고 계시오."], ["Have a care,”", "조심하시오.\""], ["the mother said, with dignity.", "어머니가 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“Woman, I am the courier of him who speaketh but once to such as thou and they are healed.", "\"여인이여, 나는 그대 같은 이에게 단 한마디만 하시면 그들이 낫게 되는 분의 전령이오."], ["I am not afraid.”", "나는 두렵지 않소.\""]], [["“The Nazarene?”", "\"나사렛 사람 말이오?\""]], [["“The Messiah,” he said.", "\"메시아요.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Is it true that he cometh to the city to-day?”", "\"그분이 오늘 성으로 오신다는 게 참말이오?\""]], [["“He is now at Bethphage.”", "\"그분은 지금 벳바게에 계시오.\""]], [["“On what road, master?”", "\"어느 길로 오신단 말이오, 선생?\""]], [["“This one.”", "\"이 길이오.\""]], [["She clasped her hands, and looked up thankfully.", "그녀는 두 손을 맞잡고, 감사하며 위를 올려다보았다."]], [["“For whom takest thou him?”", "\"그대는 그분을 누구로 여기시오?\""], ["the man asked, with pity.", "사내가 가엾이 여기며 물었다."]], [["“The Son of God,”", "\"하느님의 아들이지요.\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Stay thou here then; or, as there is a multitude with him, take thy stand by the rock yonder, the white one under the tree; and as he goeth by fail not to call to him; call, and fear not.", "\"그렇다면 여기 머물러 계시오. 아니면, 그분과 함께 무리가 있으니, 저기 저 바위 곁에, 나무 아래 저 흰 바위 곁에 서 계시오. 그리고 그분이 지나가시거든 잊지 말고 그분을 부르시오."], ["If thy faith but equal thy knowledge, he will hear thee though all the heavens thunder.", "부르되, 두려워 마시오. 그대의 믿음이 그대의 앎과 같기만 하다면, 온 하늘이 천둥 친다 해도 그분은 그대의 소리를 들으실 거요."], ["I go to tell Israel, assembled in and about the city, that he is at hand, and to make ready to receive him. Peace to thee and thine, woman.”", "나는 성 안팎에 모인 이스라엘에게 그분이 가까이 오셨음을 알리고, 그분을 맞을 채비를 시키러 가오. 그대와 그대의 식구들에게 평화가 있기를, 여인이여.\""]], [["The stranger moved on.", "나그네는 길을 떠났다."]], [["“Did you hear, Tirzah?", "\"들었느냐, 티르자? 들었어?"], ["Did you hear? The Nazarene is on the road, on this one, and he will hear us.", "나사렛 사람이 이 길로, 바로 이 길로 오고 계신다는구나. 그리고 그분이 우리 소리를 들으실 거래."], ["Once more, my child—oh, only once!", "한 번만 더, 얘야, 오, 단 한 번만 더!"], ["and let us to the rock.", "저 바위로 가자꾸나."], ["It is but a step.”", "한 걸음이면 된다.\""]], [["Thus encouraged Tirzah took Amrah’s hand and arose; but as they were going, Amrah said, “Stay; the man is returning.”", "이렇게 용기를 얻은 티르자는 암라의 손을 잡고 일어섰다. 그러나 그들이 막 가려 할 때, 암라가 말했다. \"잠깐만요."], ["And they waited for him.", "그 사람이 되돌아오고 있어요.\" 그래서 그들은 그를 기다렸다."]], [["“I pray your grace, woman,”", "\"그대의 양해를 구하오, 여인이여.\""], ["he said, upon overtaking them.", "그가 그들을 따라잡고 말했다."], ["“Remembering that the sun will be hot before the Nazarene arrives, and that the city is near by to give me refreshment should I need it, I thought this water would do thee better than it will me.", "\"나사렛 사람이 오시기 전에 해가 뜨거워질 것이고, 내게 마실 것이 필요하면 성이 가까이 있어 마련해 주리라는 생각이 들어, 이 물이 내게보다 그대에게 더 요긴하겠다 싶었소."], ["Take it and be of good cheer.", "받으시고 기운을 내시오."], ["Call to him as he passes.”", "그분이 지나가시거든 부르시오.\""]], [["He followed the words by offering her a gourd full of water, such as foot-travellers sometimes carried with them in their journeys across the hills; and instead of placing the gift on the ground for her to take up when he was at a safe distance, he gave it into her hand.", "그는 말끝에 호리병박에 가득 담은 물을 그녀에게 내밀었으니, 길손들이 더러 산을 넘는 여정에 지니고 다니는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 안전한 거리로 물러난 뒤 그녀가 집어 들도록 땅바닥에 놓아두는 대신, 그것을 그녀의 손에 직접 건네주었다."]], [["“Art thou a Jew?”", "\"그대는 유대인이오?\""], ["she asked, surprised.", "그녀가 놀라며 물었다."]], [["“I am that, and better; I am a disciple of the Christ who teacheth daily by word and example this thing which I have done unto you.", "\"그렇소, 그 이상이오. 나는 그리스도의 제자요. 그분은 내가 그대에게 행한 이 일을 날마다 말씀과 본보기로 가르치시오."], ["The world hath long known the word charity without understanding it.", "세상은 오래도록 자비라는 말을 알면서도 그 뜻을 깨닫지 못했소."], ["Again I say peace and good cheer to thee and thine.”", "다시 한번 말하니, 그대와 그대의 식구들에게 평화와 기쁨이 있기를.\""]], [["He went on, and they went slowly to the rock he had pointed out to them, high as their heads, and scarcely thirty yards from the road on the right.", "그는 계속 길을 갔고, 그들은 그가 가리켜 준 바위로 천천히 갔으니, 그것은 그들의 키만큼 높았고, 오른편 길에서 채 서른 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 있었다."], ["Standing in front of it, the mother satisfied herself they could be seen and heard plainly by passers-by whose notice they desired to attract.", "그 앞에 선 어머니는, 자기들이 눈길을 끌고자 하는 길손들에게 또렷이 보이고 들릴 수 있음을 확인하고 흡족해했다."], ["There they cast themselves under the tree in its shade, and drank of the gourd, and rested refreshed.", "그곳, 나무 그늘 아래 그들은 몸을 누이고, 호리병박의 물을 마시며, 기운을 되찾아 쉬었다."], ["Ere long Tirzah slept, and fearing to disturb her, the others held their peace.", "이윽고 티르자가 잠들었고, 다른 이들은 그녀를 깨울까 저어하여 입을 다물었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["During the third hour the road in front of the resting-place of the lepers became gradually more and more frequented by people going in the direction of Bethphage and Bethany; now, however, about the commencement of the fourth hour, a great crowd appeared over the crest of Olivet, and as it defiled down the road thousands in number, the two watchers noticed with wonder that every one in it carried a palm-branch freshly cut.", "셋째 시간 동안 나병환자들이 쉬던 곳 앞의 길에는 베다니와 벳바게 쪽으로 가는 사람들이 차츰 늘어 갔다. 그러나 넷째 시간이 시작될 무렵, 올리벳 산마루 위로 거대한 무리가 나타났다. 수천 명이 길을 따라 줄지어 내려오는데, 두 사람은 무리 가운데 누구나 갓 잘라 낸 종려나무 가지를 손에 들고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["As they sat absorbed by the novelty, the noise of another multitude approaching from the east drew their eyes that way.", "두 사람이 이 새로운 광경에 넋을 잃고 앉아 있을 때, 동쪽에서 다가오는 또 다른 무리의 소리가 그들의 눈길을 그쪽으로 끌었다."], ["Then the mother awoke Tirzah.", "그러자 어머니가 티르자를 깨웠다."]], [["“What is the meaning of it all?”", "\"이게 다 무슨 일이에요?\""], ["the latter asked.", "딸이 물었다."]], [["“He is coming,”", "\"그분이 오신단다.\""], ["answered the mother.", "어머니가 대답했다."], ["“These we see are from the city going to meet him; those we hear in the east are his friends bearing him company; and it will not be strange if the processions meet here before us.", "\"우리가 보는 저들은 그분을 맞으러 성에서 나온 사람들이고, 동쪽에서 소리가 들리는 저들은 그분을 모시고 오는 동무들이란다. 두 행렬이 우리 앞에서 만나게 되더라도 이상할 게 없겠지.\""]], [["“I fear, if they do, we cannot be heard.”", "\"그리되면, 우리 소리가 들리지 않을까 봐 두려워요.\""]], [["The same thought was in the elder’s mind.", "같은 생각이 어머니의 마음속에도 들어 있었다."]], [["“Amrah,”", "\"암라야.\""], ["she asked, “when Judah spoke of the healing of the ten, in what words did he say they called to the Nazarene?”", "어머니가 물었다. \"유다가 열 사람을 고친 이야기를 했을 때, 그들이 나사렛 사람을 어떤 말로 불렀다더냐?\""]], [["“Either they said, ‘Lord, have mercy upon us,’ or ‘Master, have mercy.’”", "\"'주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서' 아니면 '선생님, 불쌍히 여기소서' 둘 중 하나였습니다.\""]], [["“Only that?”", "\"그것뿐이더냐?\""]], [["“No more that I heard.”", "\"제가 들은 건 그게 다입니다.\""]], [["“Yet it was enough,” the mother added, to herself.", "\"하지만 그것으로 충분했겠지.\" 어머니가 혼잣말처럼 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Amrah, “Judah said he saw them go away well.”", "암라가 말했다. \"유다는 그들이 멀쩡해져서 떠나는 걸 보았다고 했습니다.\""]], [["Meantime the people in the east came up slowly.", "그러는 동안 동쪽의 사람들은 천천히 다가왔다."], ["When at length the foremost of them were in sight, the gaze of the lepers fixed upon a man riding in the midst of what seemed a chosen company which sang and danced about him in extravagance of joy.", "마침내 맨 앞에 선 자들이 시야에 들어왔을 때, 나병환자들의 시선은 한 무리 가운데서 말을 탄 한 사람에게 못 박혔다. 무리는 마치 가려 뽑힌 듯 보였고, 그를 둘러싸고 넘치는 기쁨으로 노래하고 춤추었다."], ["The rider was bareheaded and clad all in white.", "말 탄 이는 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않았고, 온몸에 흰옷을 걸치고 있었다."], ["When he was in distance to be more clearly observed, these, looking anxiously, saw an olive-hued face shaded by long chestnut hair slightly sunburned and parted in the middle.", "그가 더 또렷이 볼 만한 거리에 이르렀을 때, 이들은 애타게 바라보다가, 올리브빛 얼굴이 길고 밤색인 머리카락에 가려진 것을 보았다. 그 머리카락은 햇볕에 살짝 그을렸고 한가운데서 가르마를 타고 있었다."], ["He looked neither to the right nor left.", "그는 좌로도 우로도 돌아보지 않았다."], ["In the noisy abandon of his followers he appeared to have no part; nor did their favor disturb him in the least, or raise him out of the profound melancholy into which, as his countenance showed, he was plunged.", "떠들썩하게 들떠 있는 추종자들의 모습에 그는 아무런 몫도 없는 듯했다. 그들의 환호도 그를 조금도 흔들지 못했고, 그 얼굴에 드러나듯 그가 빠져 있는 깊은 우수에서 그를 끌어올리지도 못했다."], ["The sun beat upon the back of his head, and lighting up the floating hair gave it a delicate likeness to a golden nimbus.", "해가 그의 뒤통수를 내리쬐어 흩날리는 머리카락을 빛나게 하니, 그것이 황금빛 후광과 어렴풋이 닮아 보였다."], ["Behind him the irregular procession, pouring forward with continuous singing and shouting, extended out of view.", "그의 뒤로는 들쭉날쭉한 행렬이 끊임없이 노래하고 외치며 앞으로 쏟아져 나오는데, 끝이 보이지 않을 만큼 길게 뻗어 있었다."], ["There was no need of any one to tell the lepers that this was he—the wonderful Nazarene!", "나병환자들에게 이가 바로 그분이라고, 그 놀라운 나사렛 사람이라고 일러 줄 사람은 아무도 필요 없었다."]], [["“He is here, Tirzah,”", "\"그분이 여기 오셨다, 티르자야.\""], ["the mother said; “he is here.", "어머니가 말했다. \"그분이 오셨어."], ["Come, my child.”", "가자, 얘야.\""]], [["As she spoke she glided in front of the white rock and fell upon her knees.", "이렇게 말하며 어머니는 흰 바위 앞으로 미끄러지듯 나아가 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["Directly the daughter and servant were by her side.", "곧바로 딸과 하녀도 그 곁에 섰다."], ["Then at sight of the procession in the west, the thousands from the city halted, and began to wave their green branches, shouting, or rather chanting (for it was all in one voice),", "그때 서쪽에서 오는 행렬을 보고, 성에서 나온 수천 명이 걸음을 멈추고는 푸른 가지를 흔들며 외쳤다. 아니, 차라리 노래했다고 해야 옳았으니, 모두가 한목소리였기 때문이다."]], [["“Blessed is the King of Israel that cometh in the name of the Lord!”", "\"주의 이름으로 오시는 이스라엘의 왕이여 찬송하리로다!\""]], [["And all the thousands who were of the rider’s company, both those near and those afar, replied so the air shook with the sound, which was as a great wind threshing the side of the hill.", "그러자 말 탄 이의 무리에 속한 수천 명이, 가까이 있는 자도 멀리 있는 자도 모두 화답하니, 그 소리에 공기가 진동했다. 그것은 마치 산허리를 후려치는 큰 바람과도 같았다."], ["Amidst the din, the cries of the poor lepers were not more than the twittering of dazed sparrows.", "그 소란 속에서 가련한 나병환자들의 외침은 정신이 멍해진 참새들의 지저귐만도 못했다."]], [["The moment of the meeting of the hosts was come, and with it the opportunity the sufferers were seeking; if not taken, it would be lost forever, and they would be lost as well.", "두 무리가 만나는 순간이 다가왔으니, 그와 더불어 고통받는 자들이 찾던 기회도 다가왔다. 잡지 못하면 영영 사라질 기회였고, 그들 또한 함께 사라질 터였다."]], [["“Nearer, my child—let us get nearer.", "\"더 가까이, 얘야. 더 가까이 가자."], ["He cannot hear us,” said the mother.", "그분이 우리 소리를 듣지 못하시는구나.\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["She arose, and staggered forward.", "어머니는 일어나 비틀비틀 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Her ghastly hands were up, and she screamed with horrible shrillness.", "그 소름 끼치는 두 손을 치켜들고, 어머니는 끔찍하리만큼 날카롭게 비명을 질렀다."], ["The people saw her—saw her hideous face, and stopped awe-struck—an effect for which extreme human misery, visible as in this instance, is as potent as majesty in purple and gold.", "사람들이 그녀를 보았다. 그 흉측한 얼굴을 보고는 두려움에 사로잡혀 걸음을 멈추었다. 이 경우처럼 눈에 보이는 극한의 인간적 비참은, 자줏빛과 황금으로 치장한 위엄에 못지않은 힘을 지니는 법이다."], ["Tirzah, behind her a little way, fell down too faint and frightened to follow farther.", "티르자는 어머니 뒤로 조금 떨어진 자리에서 너무 기진하고 겁에 질려 더는 따라가지 못한 채 쓰러졌다."]], [["“The lepers!", "\"나병환자다!"], ["the lepers!”", "나병환자야!\""]], [["“Stone them!”", "\"돌로 쳐라!\""]], [["“The accursed of God! Kill them!”", "\"하느님의 저주받은 것들이다! 죽여라!\""]], [["These, with other yells of like import, broke in upon the hosannas of the part of the multitude too far removed to see and understand the cause of the interruption.", "이런 외침과 비슷한 뜻의 다른 고함들이, 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어 소동의 원인을 보지도 알지도 못하는 무리 일부의 호산나 사이로 끼어들었다."], ["Some there were, however, near by familiar with the nature of the man to whom the unfortunates were appealing—some who, by long intercourse with him, had caught somewhat of his divine compassion: they gazed at him, and were silent while, in fair view, he rode up and stopped in front of the woman.", "그러나 가까이 있는 자들 가운데 더러는, 이 불행한 이들이 호소하는 그 사람의 본성을 잘 아는 자들이 있었다. 그와 오래 사귀면서 그의 신성한 연민을 얼마간 받아 지닌 자들이었다. 그들은 그를 바라보며 잠잠했고, 그동안 그는 환히 보이는 곳에서 말을 몰아 그 여인 앞에 와 멈추었다."], ["She also beheld his face—calm, pitiful, and of exceeding beauty, the large eyes tender with benignant purpose.", "여인도 그의 얼굴을 보았다. 고요하고, 자비로우며, 더없이 아름다운 얼굴이었고, 그 큰 눈은 너그러운 뜻으로 부드러웠다."]], [["And this was the colloquy that ensued:", "이어서 다음과 같은 대화가 오갔다."]], [["“O Master, Master!", "\"오 선생님, 선생님!"], ["Thou seest our need; thou canst make us clean.", "저희의 곤궁을 보고 계시지요. 선생님은 저희를 깨끗하게 하실 수 있습니다."], ["Have mercy upon us—mercy!”", "저희를 불쌍히 여기소서, 불쌍히 여기소서!\""]], [["“Believest thou I am able to do this?”", "\"내가 이 일을 할 수 있다고 믿느냐?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Thou art he of whom the prophets spake—thou art the Messiah!”", "\"선생님은 예언자들이 말한 바로 그분이십니다. 선생님은 메시아이십니다!\""], ["she replied.", "여인이 대답했다."]], [["His eyes grew radiant, his manner confident.", "그의 눈이 빛나고, 그 태도가 확신에 찼다."]], [["“Woman,”", "\"여인아.\""], ["he said, “great is thy faith; be it unto thee even as thou wilt.”", "그가 말했다. \"네 믿음이 크도다. 네 뜻대로 이루어질지어다.\""]], [["He lingered an instant after, apparently unconscious of the presence of the throng—an instant—then he rode away.", "그는 그 뒤로도 한순간 머뭇거렸는데, 군중이 거기 있다는 것을 알아차리지 못하는 듯했다. 한순간이었다. 그러고는 말을 몰아 떠났다."]], [["To the heart divinely original, yet so human in all the better elements of humanity, going with sure prevision to a death of all the inventions of men the foulest and most cruel, breathing even then in the forecast shadow of the awful event, and still as hungry and thirsty for love and faith as in the beginning, how precious and ineffably soothing the farewell exclamation of the grateful woman:", "신성하게 독창적이면서도 인간성의 더 나은 요소들에서는 더없이 인간다운 그 마음, 사람이 고안한 가장 더럽고 잔혹한 죽음을 향해 확실한 예지로 나아가는 그 마음, 그 무서운 사건의 미리 드리운 그림자 속에서 이미 숨 쉬면서도 처음과 다름없이 사랑과 믿음에 굶주리고 목마른 그 마음에게, 감사하는 여인의 작별 외침은 얼마나 귀하고 이루 말할 수 없이 위안이 되었을까."]], [["“To God in the highest, glory!", "\"지극히 높은 곳에 계신 하느님께 영광을!"], ["Blessed, thrice blessed, the Son whom he hath given us!”", "그분이 우리에게 주신 아드님께 복이, 세 곱절의 복이 있을지어다!\""]], [["Immediately both the hosts, that from the city and that from Bethphage, closed around him with their joyous demonstrations, with hosannas and waving of palms, and so he passed from the lepers forever.", "곧바로 두 무리가, 성에서 온 무리와 벳바게에서 온 무리가 기쁨에 찬 환호와 호산나와 종려나무 흔들기로 그를 에워쌌고, 그렇게 그는 나병환자들에게서 영영 멀어졌다."], ["Covering her head, the elder hastened to Tirzah, and folded her in her arms, crying, “Daughter, look up!", "어머니는 머리를 가리고 서둘러 티르자에게 달려가, 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 외쳤다. \"딸아, 고개를 들어라!"], ["I have his promise; he is indeed the Messiah.", "나는 그분의 약속을 받았다. 그분은 참으로 메시아시다."], ["We are saved—saved!”", "우리는 구원받았다."], ["And the two remained kneeling while the procession, slowly going, disappeared over the mount.", "구원받았어!\" 두 사람은 무릎을 꿇은 채 그대로 있었고, 그동안 행렬은 천천히 가다가 산 너머로 사라졌다."], ["When the noise of its singing afar was a sound scarcely heard the miracle began.", "그 노랫소리가 멀리서 가까스로 들릴 만큼 잦아들었을 때 기적이 시작되었다."]], [["There was first in the hearts of the lepers a freshening of the blood; then it flowed faster and stronger, thrilling their wasted bodies with an infinitely sweet sense of painless healing.", "먼저 나병환자들의 가슴속에서 피가 새로워졌다. 이어 피가 더 빠르고 힘차게 흐르며, 쇠약해진 몸을 통증 없는 치유의 한없이 달콤한 감각으로 떨리게 했다."], ["Each felt the scourge going from her; their strength revived; they were returning to be themselves.", "저마다 채찍 같은 병이 제 몸에서 빠져나가는 것을 느꼈다. 기운이 되살아났다."], ["Directly, as if to make the purification complete, from body to spirit the quickening ran, exalting them to a very fervor of ecstasy.", "그들은 본래의 자신으로 돌아가고 있었다. 곧, 정화를 완전하게 하려는 듯, 그 생기가 몸에서 정신으로 번져 그들을 황홀경의 열기로까지 끌어올렸다."], ["The power possessing them to this good end was most nearly that of a draught of swift and happy effect; yet it was unlike and superior in that its healing and cleansing were absolute, and not merely a delicious consciousness while in progress, but the planting, growing, and maturing all at once of a recollection so singular and so holy that the simple thought of it should be of itself ever after a formless yet perfect thanksgiving.", "이 선한 목적을 위해 그들을 사로잡은 힘은 빠르고 행복한 효력을 지닌 한 모금의 음료에 가장 가까웠다. 그러나 그것과는 다르고 또 그보다 뛰어났으니, 그 치유와 정화가 절대적이었기 때문이고, 진행되는 동안의 황홀한 의식에 그치지 않고, 단지 그것을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그 뒤로 언제까지나 형태 없으되 온전한 감사가 되리만큼 유별나고 거룩한 기억을 한꺼번에 심고 키우고 무르익게 했기 때문이다."]], [["To this transformation—for such it may be called quite as properly as a cure—there was a witness other than Amrah.", "이 변화에는, 그것은 치유라고 부르는 것만큼이나 마땅하게 변화라고 부를 수 있는데, 암라 말고도 또 다른 증인이 있었다."], ["The reader will remember the constancy with which Ben-Hur had followed the Nazarene throughout his wanderings; and now, recalling the conversation of the night before, there will be little surprise at learning that the young Jew was present when the leprous woman appeared in the path of the pilgrims.", "독자는 벤허가 나사렛 사람의 유랑길을 줄곧 한결같이 따라다닌 것을 기억할 것이다. 이제 전날 밤의 대화를 떠올린다면, 나병에 걸린 여인이 순례자들의 길에 나타났을 때 그 젊은 유대인이 그 자리에 있었다는 사실을 알게 되어도 그리 놀라지 않을 것이다."], ["He heard her prayer, and saw her disfigured face; he heard the answer also, and was not so accustomed to incidents of the kind, frequent as they had been, as to have lost interest in them.", "그는 여인의 기도를 들었고, 그 일그러진 얼굴을 보았다. 그 대답도 들었는데, 그런 일이 잦았다고는 해도, 그런 종류의 사건에 익숙해져 흥미를 잃을 만큼은 아니었다."], ["Had such thing been possible with him, still the bitter disputation always excited by the simplest display of the Master’s curative gift would have sufficed to keep his curiosity alive.", "설령 그가 그럴 수 있었다 해도, 선생의 치유 능력을 가장 단순하게 드러낼 때마다 늘 일어나는 신랄한 논쟁만으로도 그의 호기심을 살려 두기에 충분했을 것이다."], ["Besides that, if not above it as an incentive, his hope to satisfy himself upon the vexed question of the mission of the mysterious man was still upon him strong as in the beginning; we might indeed say even stronger, because of a belief that now quickly, before the sun went down, the man himself would make all known by public proclamation.", "게다가, 그 동기로서 그에 못지않게, 아니 어쩌면 그 이상으로, 이 신비로운 사람의 사명이라는 골치 아픈 문제를 스스로 풀어 보려는 희망이 처음과 다름없이 강하게 그를 붙들고 있었다. 사실 더 강해졌다고도 할 수 있었으니, 이제 곧, 해가 지기 전에, 그 사람이 공개 선포로써 모든 것을 알리리라는 믿음 때문이었다."], ["At the close of the scene, consequently, Ben-Hur had withdrawn from the procession, and seated himself upon a stone to wait its passage.", "그리하여 그 장면이 끝나자 벤허는 행렬에서 빠져나와 돌 위에 앉아 행렬이 지나가기를 기다렸다."]], [["From his place he nodded recognition to many of the people—Galileans in his league, carrying short swords under their long abbas.", "그는 제자리에서 많은 사람들에게 알아본다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 긴 아바 자락 아래 짧은 칼을 감춘, 그와 한패인 갈릴리 사람들이었다."], ["After a little a swarthy Arab came up leading two horses; at a sign from Ben-Hur he also drew out.", "잠시 후 거무스름한 아랍인 하나가 말 두 필을 끌고 다가왔다. 벤허의 신호에 그도 길에서 물러났다."]], [["“Stay here,” the young master said, when all were gone by, even the laggards.", "\"여기서 기다려라.\" 모두가, 심지어 뒤처진 자들까지 다 지나간 뒤에 젊은 주인이 말했다."], ["“I wish to be at the city early, and Aldebaran must do me service.”", "\"나는 일찍 성에 가야 하니, 알데바란이 내게 수고를 해 주어야겠다.\""]], [["He stroked the broad forehead of the horse, now in his prime of strength and beauty, then crossed the road towards the two women.", "그는 이제 한창 힘과 아름다움이 무르익은 말의 넓은 이마를 쓰다듬고는, 두 여인을 향해 길을 건넜다."]], [["They were to him, it should be borne in mind, strangers in whom he felt interest only as they were subjects of a superhuman experiment, the result of which might possibly help him to solution of the mystery that had so long engaged him.", "명심해 둘 것은, 그에게 이들은 낯선 사람들이었고, 오직 초인적인 실험의 대상으로서만 관심이 가는 존재였다는 점이다. 그 실험의 결과가 어쩌면 그를 오래도록 사로잡아 온 신비의 해답으로 이끌어 줄지도 모르는 까닭이었다."], ["As he proceeded, he glanced casually at the figure of the little woman over by the white rock, standing there, her face hidden in her hands.", "걸어가면서 그는 흰 바위 곁에 서 있는 작은 여인의 모습을 무심코 흘긋 보았다. 여인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 그곳에 서 있었다."]], [["“As the Lord liveth, it is Amrah!” he said to himself.", "\"주께서 살아 계심을 두고 맹세컨대, 저건 암라가 아닌가!\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["He hurried on, and passing by the mother and daughter, still without recognizing them, he stopped before the servant.", "그는 걸음을 재촉했고, 어머니와 딸을 지나치면서도 여전히 그들을 알아보지 못한 채, 하녀 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Amrah,”", "\"암라.\""], ["he said to her, “Amrah, what do you here?”", "그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"암라, 여기서 무얼 하는 게냐?\""]], [["She rushed forward, and fell upon her knees before him, blinded by her tears, nigh speechless with contending joy and fear.", "암라는 앞으로 달려 나와 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다. 눈물에 앞이 가려지고, 뒤엉킨 기쁨과 두려움에 거의 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["“O master, master!", "\"오 도련님, 도련님!"], ["Thy God and mine, how good he is!”", "도련님의 하느님이자 저의 하느님이, 얼마나 선하신지요!\""]], [["The knowledge we gain from much sympathy with others passing through trials is but vaguely understood; strangely enough, it enables us, among other things, to merge our identity into theirs often so completely that their sorrows and their delights become our own.", "남들이 시련을 겪는 것을 깊이 공감하면서 얻는 깨달음은 어렴풋이밖에 이해되지 못한다. 묘하게도 그 깨달음은, 다른 무엇보다도, 우리의 자아를 그들의 자아에 녹여 넣어 그들의 슬픔과 기쁨이 곧 우리 것이 되게 할 만큼 자주 우리를 그들과 온전히 하나로 만든다."], ["So poor Amrah, aloof and hiding her face, knew the transformation the lepers were undergoing without a word spoken to her—knew it, and shared all their feeling to the full.", "그래서 가련한 암라는, 멀찍이 떨어져 얼굴을 가리고 있으면서도, 한마디 말을 듣지 않고도 나병환자들이 겪는 변화를 알았다. 알았을 뿐 아니라 그들의 모든 감정을 남김없이 함께 나누었다."], ["Her countenance, her words, her whole manner, betrayed her condition; and with swift presentiment he connected it with the women he had just passed: he felt her presence there at that time was in some way associated with them, and turned hastily as they arose to their feet.", "그녀의 표정, 그녀의 말, 그녀의 온 태도가 그 상태를 드러냈다. 그러자 벤허는 재빠른 예감으로 그것을 방금 지나쳐 온 여인들과 연결 지었다. 그녀가 그때 거기 있는 것이 어떤 식으로든 그들과 관련되어 있음을 느낀 그는, 그들이 몸을 일으키자 황급히 돌아보았다."], ["His heart stood still, he became rooted in his tracks—dumb past outcry—awe-struck.", "그의 심장이 멎었고, 그는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 굳어졌다. 소리조차 지르지 못한 채, 두려움에 사로잡혔다."]], [["The woman he had seen before the Nazarene was standing with her hands clasped and eyes streaming, looking towards heaven.", "그가 나사렛 사람 앞에서 보았던 그 여인이 두 손을 맞잡고 눈물을 흘리며 하늘을 향해 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["The mere transformation would have been a sufficient surprise; but it was the least of the causes of his emotion.", "그 변화만으로도 충분히 놀랄 일이었을 것이다. 그러나 그것은 그의 감정을 일으킨 원인 중 가장 작은 것이었다."], ["Could he be mistaken?", "그가 잘못 본 것일까?"], ["Never was there in life a stranger so like his mother; and like her as she was the day the Roman snatched her from him.", "살아오면서 이토록 그의 어머니를 닮은 낯선 사람은 없었다. 게다가 로마인이 어머니를 그에게서 앗아 가던 그날의 모습 그대로 닮아 있었다."], ["There was but one difference to mar the identity—the hair of this person was a little streaked with gray; yet that was not impossible of reconcilement, since the intelligence which had directed the miracle might have taken into consideration the natural effects of the passage of years.", "그 동일함을 흐리는 차이는 단 하나뿐이었으니, 이 사람의 머리카락이 군데군데 희끗하다는 것이었다. 그러나 그것도 들어맞지 않는 것은 아니었으니, 기적을 주관한 그 지혜가 세월의 흐름이 가져오는 자연스러운 결과를 헤아렸을 수도 있는 까닭이었다."], ["And who was it by her side, if not Tirzah?—fair, beautiful, perfect, more mature, but in all other respects exactly the same in appearance as when she looked with him over the parapet the morning of the accident to Gratus.", "그리고 그 곁에 있는 이는, 티르자가 아니라면 누구란 말인가? 곱고 아름답고 흠 없으며, 더 성숙해졌으되, 그 밖의 모든 점에서는 그라투스에게 사고가 일어나던 그날 아침 그와 함께 난간 너머를 내려다보던 때와 똑같은 모습이었다."], ["He had given them over as dead, and time had accustomed him to the bereavement; he had not ceased mourning for them, yet, as something distinguishable, they had simply dropped out of his plans and dreams.", "그는 그들을 죽은 사람으로 단념했고, 세월은 그를 그 상실에 익숙해지게 했다. 그들을 위한 애도를 그친 것은 아니었으나, 또렷이 구분되는 무엇으로서의 그들은 그저 그의 계획과 꿈에서 떨어져 나가 있었다."], ["Scarcely believing his senses, he laid his hand upon the servant’s head, and asked, tremulously,", "자신의 감각을 좀처럼 믿지 못한 채, 그는 하녀의 머리에 손을 얹고 떨리는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Amrah, Amrah—my mother!", "\"암라, 암라야. 내 어머니! 티르자!"], ["Tirzah! tell me if I see aright.”", "내가 제대로 보고 있는 건지 말해 다오.\""]], [["“Speak to them, O master, speak to them!”", "\"저분들께 말씀하세요, 오 도련님, 저분들께 말씀하세요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He waited no longer, but ran, with outstretched arms, crying, “Mother! mother! Tirzah! Here I am!”", "그는 더 기다리지 않고, 두 팔을 벌린 채 달려가며 외쳤다. \"어머니! 어머니! 티르자! 저예요!\""]], [["They heard his call, and with a cry as loving started to meet him.", "그들은 그의 부름을 듣고, 못지않게 다정한 외침과 함께 그를 맞으러 나섰다."], ["Suddenly the mother stopped, drew back, and uttered the old alarm,", "그런데 갑자기 어머니가 걸음을 멈추고 뒤로 물러서며, 옛 경고를 외쳤다."]], [["“Stay, Judah, my son; come not nearer.", "\"멈춰라, 유다, 내 아들아. 더 가까이 오지 마라."], ["Unclean, unclean!”", "부정하다, 부정해!\""]], [["The utterance was not from habit, grown since the dread disease struck her, as much as fear; and the fear was but another form of the ever-thoughtful maternal love.", "그 외침은 그 무서운 병이 어머니를 덮친 뒤로 몸에 밴 습관에서 나온 것이라기보다는 두려움에서 나온 것이었다. 그리고 그 두려움은 언제나 사려 깊은 모성애의 또 다른 형태일 뿐이었다."], ["Though they were healed in person, the taint of the scourge might be in their garments ready for communication.", "비록 몸은 나았어도, 그 채찍 같은 병의 독기가 옷에 남아 옮길 채비를 하고 있을지도 몰랐다. 그에게는 그런 생각이 없었다."], ["He had no such thought.", "그들이 그의 앞에 있었다."], ["They were before him; he had called them, they had answered. Who or what should keep them from him now?", "그가 그들을 불렀고, 그들이 대답했다. 이제 누가, 무엇이 그들을 그에게서 떼어 놓을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Next moment the three, so long separated, were mingling their tears in each other’s arms.", "다음 순간, 그토록 오래 떨어져 있던 세 사람은 서로의 품에 안겨 눈물을 뒤섞고 있었다."]], [["The first ecstasy over, the mother said, “In this happiness, O my children, let us not be ungrateful.", "첫 황홀경이 지나가자 어머니가 말했다. \"이 행복 속에서, 오 내 자식들아, 우리 감사할 줄 모르는 자가 되지는 말자."], ["Let us begin life anew by acknowledgment of him to whom we are all so indebted.”", "우리 모두가 그토록 큰 빚을 진 그분을 받들며 새 삶을 시작하자.\""]], [["They fell upon their knees, Amrah with the rest; and the prayer of the elder outspoken was as a psalm.", "그들은 무릎을 꿇었고, 암라도 함께 꿇었다. 소리 높여 올리는 어머니의 기도는 한 편의 시편과도 같았다."]], [["Tirzah repeated it word for word; so did Ben-Hur, but not with the same clear mind and questionless faith; for when they were risen, he asked,", "티르자가 한마디 한마디 그대로 따라 했다. 벤허도 그리했으나, 같은 맑은 마음과 의심 없는 믿음으로 한 것은 아니었다. 다들 일어났을 때 그가 물은 까닭이다."]], [["“In Nazareth, where the man was born, mother, they call him the son of a carpenter. What is he?”", "\"그 사람이 태어난 나사렛에서는, 어머니, 사람들이 그를 목수의 아들이라고 부릅니다. 그는 누구입니까?\""]], [["Her eyes rested upon him with all their old tenderness, and she answered as she had answered the Nazarene himself—", "어머니의 눈길이 옛날 그대로의 다정함을 가득 담아 그에게 머물렀고, 어머니는 나사렛 사람에게 대답했던 그대로 대답했다."]], [["“He is the Messiah.”", "\"그분은 메시아시다.\""]], [["“And whence has he his power?”", "\"그러면 그 힘은 어디서 나온 것입니까?\""]], [["“We may know by the use he makes of it.", "\"그분이 그 힘을 어떻게 쓰는가를 보면 알 수 있다."], ["Can you tell me any ill he has done?”", "그분이 행한 악을 하나라도 말할 수 있겠느냐?\" \"없습니다.\""]], [["“No.” “By that sign then I answer, He has his power from God.”", "\"그러면 그 표징으로 답하마. 그분은 그 힘을 하느님에게서 받으셨다.\""]], [["It is not an easy thing to shake off in a moment the expectations nurtured through years until they have become essentially a part of us; and though Ben-Hur asked himself what the vanities of the world were to such a one, his ambition was obdurate and would not down.", "여러 해 동안 길러 온 기대가 마침내 우리의 본질적인 일부가 되어 버렸을 때, 그것을 한순간에 떨쳐 버리기란 쉬운 일이 아니다. 벤허는 그런 분에게 세상의 헛된 것들이 다 무엇이겠느냐고 스스로 물어보았지만, 그의 야망은 완고하여 수그러들려 하지 않았다."], ["He persisted as men do yet every day in measuring the Christ by himself.", "그는 오늘날에도 사람들이 날마다 그러하듯, 그리스도를 자신의 잣대로 재기를 고집했다."], ["How much better if we measured ourselves by the Christ!", "우리가 우리 자신을 그리스도의 잣대로 잰다면 얼마나 더 나을 것인가!"]], [["Naturally, the mother was the first to think of the cares of life.", "자연히 살림 걱정을 먼저 떠올린 것은 어머니였다."]], [["“What shall we do now, my son?", "\"이제 우리 어떻게 하면 좋겠느냐, 아들아?"], ["Where shall we go?”", "어디로 가야 하지?\""]], [["Then Ben-Hur, recalled to duty, observed how completely every trace of the scourge had disappeared from his restored people; that each had back her perfection of person; that, as with Naaman when he came up out of the water, their flesh had come again like unto the flesh of a little child; and he took off his cloak, and threw it over Tirzah.", "그러자 의무를 다시 떠올린 벤허는, 회복된 식구들에게서 그 채찍 같은 병의 흔적이 얼마나 깨끗이 사라졌는지를 살펴보았다. 저마다 몸의 온전함을 되찾았고, 나아만이 물에서 올라왔을 때처럼 그들의 살갗이 어린아이의 살갗처럼 다시 돌아와 있었다. 그는 자기 겉옷을 벗어 티르자에게 둘러 주었다."]], [["“Take it,”", "\"받으세요.\""], ["he said, smiling; “the eye of the stranger would have shunned you before, now it shall not offend you.”", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"전에는 낯선 사람의 눈이 누나를 피했지만, 이제는 누나를 욕보이지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["The act exposed a sword belted to his side.", "이 동작에 옆구리에 찬 칼이 드러났다."]], [["“Is it a time of war?”", "\"전쟁 때라도 되었느냐?\""], ["asked the mother, anxiously.", "어머니가 걱정스레 물었다. \"아닙니다.\""]], [["“No.” “Why, then, are you armed?”", "\"그런데 어찌하여 무기를 지녔느냐?\""]], [["“It may be necessary to defend the Nazarene.”", "\"나사렛 사람을 지켜야 할지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Thus Ben-Hur evaded the whole truth.", "이렇게 벤허는 온전한 진실을 비껴갔다."]], [["“Has he enemies?", "\"그분에게 적이 있느냐?"], ["Who are they?”", "그들이 누구냐?\""]], [["“Alas, mother, they are not all Romans!”", "\"아아, 어머니, 그들이 다 로마인은 아닙니다!\""]], [["“Is he not of Israel, and a man of peace?”", "\"그분은 이스라엘 사람이며 평화의 사람이 아니냐?\""]], [["“There was never one more so; but in the opinion of the rabbis and teachers he is guilty of a great crime.”", "\"그보다 더한 사람은 없었지요. 그러나 랍비들과 율법학자들의 생각으로는, 그분이 큰 죄를 지은 셈입니다.\""]], [["“What crime?”", "\"무슨 죄냐?\""]], [["“In his eyes the uncircumcised Gentile is as worthy favor as a Jew of the strictest habit.", "\"그분의 눈에는 할례받지 않은 이방인도 가장 엄격한 율법을 지키는 유대인 못지않게 은혜를 받을 만합니다."], ["He preaches a new dispensation.”", "그분은 새로운 가르침을 설파하십니다.\""]], [["The mother was silent, and they moved to the shade of the tree by the rock.", "어머니는 잠잠했고, 그들은 바위 곁 나무 그늘로 자리를 옮겼다."], ["Calming his impatience to have them home again and hear their story, he showed them the necessity of obedience to the law governing in cases like theirs, and in conclusion called the Arab, bidding him take the horses to the gate by Bethesda and await him there; whereupon they set out by the way of the Mount of Offence.", "그들을 어서 집으로 데려가 그동안의 이야기를 듣고 싶은 조바심을 가라앉히고, 그는 그들과 같은 처지에 적용되는 율법에 따라야 할 필요성을 일러 주었다. 끝으로 아랍인을 불러, 말들을 베데스다 곁 성문으로 끌고 가 거기서 자기를 기다리라고 일렀다. 그러고서 그들은 멸시의 산 쪽 길로 출발했다."], ["The return was very different from the coming; they walked rapidly and with ease, and in good time reached a tomb newly made near that of Absalom, overlooking the depths of Cedron.", "돌아가는 길은 올 때와는 사뭇 달랐다. 그들은 빠르고 가뿐하게 걸었고, 알맞은 때에 압살롬의 무덤 가까이 새로 만든 무덤에 이르렀는데, 그곳은 기드론의 깊은 골짜기를 내려다보고 있었다."], ["Finding it unoccupied, the women took possession, while he went on hastily to make the preparations required for their new condition.", "비어 있는 것을 알고 여인들이 그곳을 차지했고, 그동안 그는 그들의 새로운 처지에 필요한 채비를 갖추려고 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["Ben-Hur pitched two tents out on the Upper Cedron east a short space of the Tombs of the Kings, and furnished them with every comfort at his command; and thither, without loss of time, he conducted his mother and sister, to remain until the examining priest could certify their perfect cleansing.", "벤허는 키드론강 상류, 왕들의 무덤에서 동쪽으로 조금 떨어진 곳에 천막 두 개를 치고, 그가 마련할 수 있는 온갖 편의를 갖추어 놓았다. 그리고 한시도 지체하지 않고 어머니와 누이를 그리로 데려가, 검사를 맡은 제사장이 두 사람이 완전히 정결해졌음을 증명해 줄 때까지 머물게 했다."]], [["In course of the duty, the young man had subjected himself to such serious defilement as to debar him from participation in the ceremonies of the great feast, then near at hand.", "이 의무를 치르는 동안 젊은이는 큰 명절의 의식에 참여하지 못할 만큼 심각한 부정(不淨)을 스스로 뒤집어쓰고 말았으니, 명절은 이제 코앞에 다가와 있었다."], ["He could not enter the least sacred of the courts of the Temple.", "그는 성전의 가장 덜 거룩한 뜰에조차 들어갈 수 없었다."], ["Of necessity, not less than choice, therefore, he stayed at the tents with his beloved people.", "그러므로 그는 선택이라기보다 어쩔 수 없이, 사랑하는 가족과 함께 천막에 머물렀다."], ["There was a great deal to hear from them, and a great deal to tell them of himself.", "그들에게서 들을 이야기도 많았고, 자신에 대해 들려줄 이야기도 많았다."]], [["Stories such as theirs—sad experiences extending through a lapse of years, sufferings of body, acuter sufferings of mind—are usually long in the telling, the incidents seldom following each other in threaded connection.", "그들이 겪은 것과 같은 사연—여러 해에 걸친 슬픈 경험, 육신의 고통, 그보다 더 날카로운 마음의 고통—은 이야기하는 데 으레 오랜 시간이 걸리고, 사건들이 실에 꿰이듯 차례로 이어지는 일도 드물다. 그는 그 이야기와 그들이 들려주는 모든 말에 귀를 기울이며, 겉으로는 인내심을 가장하여 속의 감정을 가렸다."], ["He listened to the narrative and all they told him, with outward patience masking inward feeling.", "사실 로마와 로마인을 향한 그의 증오는 그 어느 때보다 높은 곳에 다다라 있었다."], ["In fact, his hatred of Rome and Romans reached a higher mark than ever; his desire for vengeance became a thirst which attempts at reflection only intensified.", "복수를 향한 그의 열망은 갈증이 되어, 곰곰이 생각해 보려는 시도가 도리어 그것을 더욱 거세게 만들었다. 거의 야수와 같은 쓰라린 마음속에서 수많은 광기 어린 충동이 그를 사로잡았다."], ["In the almost savage bitterness of his humor many mad impulses took hold of him.", "큰길이 주는 기회들이 유달리 강한 유혹의 힘으로 그 앞에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["The opportunities of the highways presented themselves with singular force of temptation; he thought seriously of insurrection in Galilee; even the sea, ordinarily a retrospective horror to him, stretched itself map-like before his fancy, laced and interlaced with lines of passage crowded with imperial plunder and imperial travellers; but the better judgment matured in calmer hours was happily too firmly fixed to be supplanted by present passion however strong.", "그는 갈릴리에서의 봉기를 진지하게 생각했다. 평소에는 떠올리기만 해도 끔찍하던 바다조차 그의 상상 앞에 지도처럼 펼쳐졌으니, 황제의 약탈품과 황제의 길손들로 붐비는 항로가 거기에 얼기설기 그려져 있었다. 그러나 더 차분한 시간에 무르익은 그의 더 나은 판단은 다행히도 너무나 단단히 자리 잡고 있어서, 아무리 강렬한 지금의 격정으로도 그것을 밀어낼 수 없었다."], ["Each mental venture in reach of new expedients brought him back to the old conclusion—that there could be no sound success except in a war involving all Israel in solid union; and all musing upon the subject, all inquiry, all hope, ended where they began—in the Nazarene and his purposes.", "새로운 방책을 향한 그의 모든 정신적 모험은 그를 옛 결론으로 되돌려 놓았다—온 이스라엘이 굳게 하나로 뭉친 전쟁이 아니고서는 온전한 성공이 있을 수 없다는 것이었다. 그리고 이 문제를 둘러싼 모든 사색, 모든 탐구, 모든 희망은 시작한 자리에서 끝났으니—나사렛 사람과 그의 목적에서였다."]], [["At odd moments the excited schemer found a pleasure in fashioning a speech for that person:", "흥분한 이 책략가는 이따금 그 사람을 위한 연설을 지어 보는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다."]], [["“Hear, O Israel!", "\"들으라, 오 이스라엘이여!"], ["I am he, the promised of God, born King of the Jews—come to you with the dominion spoken of by the prophets.", "내가 바로 그다. 하느님께서 약속하신 자, 나면서부터 유대인의 왕인 자—예언자들이 말한 그 권세를 가지고 너희에게 왔노라."], ["Rise now, and lay hold on the world!”", "이제 일어나, 세상을 거머쥐어라!\""]], [["Would the Nazarene but speak these few words, what a tumult would follow!", "나사렛 사람이 이 몇 마디만 말한다면, 얼마나 큰 소용돌이가 뒤따를 것인가!"], ["How many mouths performing the office of trumpets would take them up and blow them abroad for the massing of armies!", "나팔 노릇을 하는 입이 얼마나 많이 그 말을 받아 사방으로 불어, 군대를 불러 모을 것인가!"]], [["Would he speak them?", "그가 과연 그 말을 할 것인가?"]], [["And eager to begin the work, and answering in the worldly way, Ben-Hur lost sight of the double nature of the man, and of the other possibility, that the divine in him might transcend the human.", "그리고 그 일을 어서 시작하고 싶은 마음에, 세속의 방식으로 헤아리느라 벤허는 그 사람의 이중적 본성과, 그 안의 신성(神性)이 인간성을 뛰어넘을 수 있다는 또 다른 가능성을 놓치고 말았다."], ["In the miracle of which Tirzah and his mother were the witnesses even more nearly than himself, he saw and set apart and dwelt upon a power ample enough to raise and support a Jewish crown over the wrecks of the Italian, and more than ample to remodel society, and convert mankind into one purified happy family; and when that work was done, could any one say the peace which might then be ordered without hindrance was not a mission worthy a son of God?", "자신보다 오히려 더 가까이서 티르자와 어머니가 목격한 그 기적 속에서, 그는 한 가지 권능을 보고 따로 떼어 거기에 마음을 쏟았다. 이탈리아 왕관의 잔해 위에 유대인의 왕관을 세우고 떠받치기에 충분하고도 남는, 사회를 다시 빚고 인류를 정결한 하나의 행복한 가족으로 바꾸어 놓기에도 차고 넘치는 권능이었다. 그리고 그 일이 이루어지고 나면, 그때 거침없이 베풀어질 평화가 하느님의 아들에게 합당한 사명이 아니라고 말할 자가 있겠는가?"], ["Could any one then deny the Redeemership of the Christ?", "그때 그리스도의 구속자 되심을 부인할 자가 있겠는가?"], ["And discarding all consideration of political consequences, what unspeakable personal glory there would then be to him as a man?", "정치적 결과에 대한 온갖 고려를 다 제쳐 두더라도, 한 인간으로서 그에게 돌아올 개인적 영광은 또 얼마나 형언할 수 없을 것인가?"], ["It was not in the nature of any mere mortal to refuse such a career.", "그런 앞길을 마다하는 것은 한낱 인간의 본성에 있을 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["Meantime down the Cedron, and in towards Bezetha, especially on the roadsides quite up to the Damascus Gate, the country filled rapidly with all kinds of temporary shelters for pilgrims to the Passover.", "그러는 동안 키드론을 따라 내려가며, 또 베세다 쪽으로 들어가는 길, 특히 다마스쿠스 문에 이르기까지 길가에는 유월절 순례자들을 위한 온갖 임시 거처가 빠르게 들어찼다."], ["Ben-Hur visited the strangers, and talked with them; and returning to his tents, he was each time more and more astonished at the vastness of their numbers.", "벤허는 그 나그네들을 찾아가 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 천막으로 돌아올 때마다 그들의 어마어마한 수에 갈수록 더 놀랐다."], ["And when he further discovered that every part of the world was represented among them—cities upon both shores of the Mediterranean far off as the Pillars of the West, river-towns in distant India, provinces in northernmost Europe; and that, though they frequently saluted him with tongues unacquainted with a syllable of the old Hebrew of the fathers, these representatives had all the same object—celebration of the notable feast—an idea tinged mistily with superstitious fancy forced itself upon him.", "그뿐 아니라 그들 가운데 세계의 모든 곳이 다 대표되어 있음을—지중해 양쪽 기슭의 도시들로, 멀리 서쪽 끝의 헤라클레스 기둥에 이르는 곳까지, 머나먼 인도의 강변 마을들, 유럽 최북단의 속주들까지—그리고 그들이 더러는 조상들의 옛 히브리어 한 음절도 모르는 혀로 그에게 인사를 건네면서도, 이 대표자들이 모두 같은 목적, 곧 그 이름난 명절을 기념하려는 한 가지 목적을 가지고 있음을 알게 되었을 때, 미신적인 환상으로 어렴풋이 물든 한 가지 생각이 그에게 밀려들었다."], ["Might he not after all have misunderstood the Nazarene?", "어쩌면 자신이 결국 나사렛 사람을 잘못 이해한 것이 아닐까?"], ["Might not that person by patient waiting be covering silent preparation, and proving his fitness for the glorious task before him?", "그 사람이 참고 기다림으로써 조용한 준비를 감추고 있는 것은 아닐까? 자기 앞에 놓인 영광스러운 과업에 자신이 합당함을 증명하고 있는 것은 아닐까?"], ["How much better this time for the movement than that other when, by Gennesaret, the Galileans would have forced assumption of the crown?", "갈릴리 사람들이 게네사렛 호숫가에서 그에게 왕관을 억지로 씌우려 했던 그때보다, 지금이 그 운동에 얼마나 더 좋은 때인가?"], ["Then the support would have been limited to a few thousands; now his proclamation would be responded to by millions—who could say how many?", "그때라면 지지하는 무리가 고작 몇천에 그쳤겠지만, 지금은 그의 선포에 수백만이 호응할 터이니—누가 그 수를 헤아릴 수 있겠는가?"], ["Pursuing this theory to its conclusions, Ben-Hur moved amidst brilliant promises, and glowed with the thought that the melancholy man, under gentle seeming and wondrous self-denial, was in fact carrying in disguise the subtlety of a politician and the genius of a soldier.", "이 가설을 그 결론까지 좇아가며, 벤허는 눈부신 약속들 사이를 거닐었다. 그 우울한 사람이 온화한 겉모습과 놀라운 자기 절제 아래, 실은 정치가의 빈틈없음과 군인의 천재성을 변장하여 품고 다니는 것이라는 생각에 가슴이 뜨거워졌다."]], [["Several times also, in the meanwhile, low-set, brawny men, bareheaded and black-bearded, came and asked for Ben-Hur at the tent; his interviews with them were always apart; and to his mother’s question who they were he answered,", "또한 그러는 동안 몇 차례, 키가 작고 우람한 사내들이—머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않고 검은 수염을 기른 자들이—천막으로 찾아와 벤허를 찾았다. 그들과의 면담은 늘 따로 이루어졌다. 그들이 누구냐는 어머니의 물음에 그는 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Some good friends of mine from Galilee.”", "\"갈릴리에서 온 제 좋은 친구들입니다.\""]], [["Through them he kept informed of the movements of the Nazarene, and of the schemes of the Nazarene’s enemies, Rabbinical and Roman.", "그들을 통해 그는 나사렛 사람의 동향과, 율법학자와 로마인을 비롯한 나사렛 사람의 적들이 꾸미는 음모를 계속 전해 들었다."], ["That the good man’s life was in danger, he knew; but that there were any bold enough to attempt to take it at that time, he could not believe.", "그 선한 사람의 목숨이 위태롭다는 것은 그도 알고 있었다. 그러나 이 시점에 감히 그 목숨을 노릴 만큼 대담한 자가 있으리라고는 믿을 수 없었다."], ["It seemed too securely intrenched in a great fame and an assured popularity.", "그 목숨은 드높은 명성과 확고한 인기 속에 너무도 단단히 보호받고 있는 듯했다."], ["The very vastness of the attendance in and about the city brought with it a seeming guaranty of safety.", "도성 안팎에 몰려든 군중의 그 방대함 자체가 안전을 보증해 주는 것 같았다."], ["And yet, to say truth, Ben-Hur’s confidence rested most certainly upon the miraculous power of the Christ.", "그렇지만 진실을 말하자면, 벤허의 확신이 가장 확실하게 기대고 있는 것은 그리스도의 기적적인 권능이었다."], ["Pondering the subject in the purely human view, that the master of such authority over life and death, used so frequently for the good of others, would not exert it in care of himself was simply as much past belief as it was past understanding.", "순전히 인간적인 관점에서 이 문제를 곰곰이 헤아려 볼 때, 생사를 다스리는 그런 권세를 지닌 분이—그 권세를 그토록 자주 남을 위해 써 온 분이—정작 자신을 지키는 데는 쓰지 않으리라는 것은 이해할 수 없는 만큼이나 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["Nor should it be forgotten that all these were incidents of occurrence between the twenty-first day of March—counting by the modern calendar—and the twenty-fifth.", "또한 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 이 모든 일이 현대의 달력으로 셈하여 삼월 이십일일에서 이십오일 사이에 일어난 사건이라는 사실이다."], ["The evening of the latter day Ben-Hur yielded to his impatience, and rode to the city, leaving behind him a promise to return in the night.", "이십오일 저녁, 벤허는 조바심을 이기지 못하고 말을 타고 도성으로 향했으니, 밤중에 돌아오겠다는 약속을 뒤에 남긴 채였다."]], [["The horse was fresh, and choosing his own gait, sped swiftly.", "말은 기운이 넘쳤고, 제 걸음을 골라 잡아 쏜살같이 달렸다."], ["The eyes of the clambering vines winked at the rider from the garden fences on the way; there was nothing else to see him, nor child nor woman nor man.", "길가 정원 울타리에서는 기어오른 덩굴의 눈들이 말 탄 이를 향해 눈짓했다. 그를 지켜보는 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 없었으니, 아이도 여자도 남자도 없었다."], ["Through the rocky float in the hollows of the road the agate hoofs drummed, ringing like cups of steel; but without notice from any stranger.", "길 우묵한 곳에 깔린 돌 위로 마노 같은 발굽이 둥둥 울리며, 강철 잔이 부딪치듯 쩌렁거렸다. 그러나 어떤 나그네도 그것을 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["In the houses passed there were no tenants; the fires by the tent-doors were out; the road was deserted; for this was the first Passover eve, and the hour “between the evenings”", "지나치는 집집마다 사람이 없었고, 천막 문 앞의 불은 꺼져 있었다. 길은 텅 비어 있었다. 이날이 첫 유월절 전야였기 때문이다."], ["when the visiting millions crowded the city, and the slaughter of lambs in offering reeked the fore-courts of the Temple, and the priests in ordered lines caught the flowing blood and carried it swiftly to the dripping altars—when all was haste and hurry, racing with the stars fast coming with the signal after which the roasting and the eating and the singing might go on, but not the preparation more.", "\"저녁과 저녁 사이\"라 불리는 그 시각, 찾아온 수백만이 도성을 가득 메우고, 제물로 바칠 어린양을 잡는 피비린내가 성전 앞뜰에 진동하며, 제사장들이 줄지어 서서 흘러나오는 피를 받아 뚝뚝 떨어지는 제단으로 재빨리 날라 가던 때였다. 모든 것이 서두름과 부산함이었으니, 신호와 함께 빠르게 떠오르는 별들과 다투어 달음질치고 있었다. 그 신호가 떨어진 뒤라야 굽고 먹고 노래하는 일은 이어질 수 있어도, 준비는 더 이상 할 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Through the great northern gate the rider rode, and lo!", "말 탄 이는 큰 북쪽 문을 지나 안으로 들어섰다. 그러자 보라!"], ["Jerusalem before the fall, in ripeness of glory, illuminated for the Lord.", "멸망을 앞둔 예루살렘이, 무르익은 영광 속에서, 주님을 위해 환히 밝혀진 채 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["Ben-Hur alighted at the gate of the khan from which the three Wise Men more than thirty years before departed, going down to Bethlehem.", "벤허는 카라반사라이의 문 앞에서 말에서 내렸다. 삼십여 년 전 세 명의 동방박사가 베들레헴으로 내려가려고 떠난 바로 그 문이었다."], ["There, in keeping of his Arab followers, he left the horse, and shortly after was at the wicket of his father’s house, and in a yet briefer space in the great chamber.", "그는 아랍인 수행원들에게 말을 맡기고, 곧 아버지 집의 쪽문에 이르렀으며, 그보다 더 짧은 사이에 큰 방에 들어섰다."], ["He called for Malluch first; that worthy being out, he sent a salutation to his friends the merchant and the Egyptian.", "그는 먼저 말루크를 찾았으나, 그 충직한 사람은 자리에 없었다. 그래서 그는 벗인 상인과 이집트인에게 안부 인사를 전하게 했다."], ["They were being carried abroad to see the celebration.", "두 사람은 축제 구경을 위해 밖으로 실려 나간 참이었다."], ["The latter, he was informed, was very feeble, and in a state of deep dejection.", "후자는, 그가 전해 들은 바로는, 몹시 쇠약하고 깊은 실의에 빠져 있다고 했다."]], [["Young people of that time who were supposed hardly to know their own hearts indulged the habit of politic indirection quite as much as young people in the same condition indulge it in this time; so when Ben-Hur inquired for the good Balthasar, and with grave courtesy desired to know if he would be pleased to see him, he really addressed the daughter a notice of his arrival.", "당시의 젊은이들은 제 마음조차 잘 모를 것이라 여겨졌지만, 실은 오늘날 같은 처지의 젊은이들이 그러하듯 빙 둘러 에두르는 술책을 곧잘 부렸다. 그래서 벤허가 선량한 발타사르의 안부를 묻고, 정중한 예를 갖추어 자기를 만나 줄 뜻이 있는지 알고자 했을 때, 그는 사실 그 딸에게 자기가 왔다는 기별을 보낸 셈이었다."], ["While the servant was answering for the elder, the curtain of the doorway was drawn aside, and the younger Egyptian came in, and walked—or floated, upborne in a white cloud of the gauzy raiment she so loved and lived in—to the centre of the chamber, where the light cast by lamps from the seven-armed brazen stick planted upon the floor was the strongest.", "하인이 어른을 대신해 대답하는 동안, 문간의 휘장이 옆으로 젖혀지고 젊은 이집트 여자가 들어와—아니, 그녀가 그토록 좋아하며 그 속에서 살아가는 얇은 비단옷의 흰 구름에 떠받쳐 떠가듯이—방 한가운데로 걸어갔다. 그곳은 바닥에 세워진 일곱 가지 놋쇠 촛대의 등잔에서 쏟아지는 빛이 가장 강한 자리였다."], ["With her there was no fear of light.", "그녀에게 빛은 두려움의 대상이 아니었다."]], [["The servant left the two alone.", "하인은 두 사람만 남겨 두고 물러갔다."]], [["In the excitement occasioned by the events of the few days past Ben-Hur had scarcely given a thought to the fair Egyptian.", "지난 며칠간의 사건들이 불러일으킨 흥분 속에서 벤허는 그 어여쁜 이집트 여자를 거의 떠올리지 못했다."], ["If she came to his mind at all, it was merely as a briefest pleasure, a suggestion of a delight which could wait for him, and was waiting.", "설령 마음에 떠오른다 해도, 그것은 한순간의 가벼운 즐거움, 자기를 기다려 줄 수 있으며 또 기다리고 있는 어떤 기쁨의 암시일 뿐이었다."]], [["But now the influence of the woman revived with all its force the instant Ben-Hur beheld her.", "그러나 이제 벤허가 그녀를 본 순간, 그 여자의 영향력은 온 힘을 되찾아 되살아났다."], ["He advanced to her eagerly, but stopped and gazed.", "그는 반가운 마음에 다가서다가, 멈춰 서서 바라보았다."], ["Such a change he had never seen!", "그토록 큰 변화는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다!"]], [["Theretofore she had been a lover studious to win him—in manner all warmth, each glance an admission, each action an avowal.", "그때까지 그녀는 그의 마음을 얻으려고 애쓰는 연인이었으니, 몸짓 하나하나가 온통 따스함이요, 눈길 하나하나가 마음을 내보이는 고백이며, 행동 하나하나가 사랑의 맹세였다."], ["She had showered him with incense of flattery.", "그녀는 아첨의 향기를 그에게 뿌려 댔다."], ["While he was present, she had impressed him with her admiration; going away, he carried the impression with him to remain a delicious expectancy hastening his return.", "그가 곁에 있을 때면 그녀는 자신의 흠모로 그를 사로잡았다. 그가 떠나갈 때면 그 인상을 가슴에 품고 가게 하여, 그것이 그의 귀환을 재촉하는 달콤한 기대로 남게 했다."], ["It was for him the painted eyelids drooped lowest over the lustrous almond eyes; for him the love-stories caught from the professionals abounding in the streets of Alexandria were repeated with emphasis and lavishment of poetry; for him endless exclamations of sympathy, and smiles, and little privileges with hand and hair and cheek and lips, and songs of the Nile, and displays of jewelry, and subtleties of lace in veils and scarfs, and other subtleties not less exquisite in flosses of Indian silk.", "윤기 흐르는 살구씨 같은 눈 위로 화장한 눈꺼풀을 가장 낮게 드리운 것도 그를 위해서였고, 알렉산드리아의 거리에 넘쳐나는 이야기꾼들에게서 주워들은 사랑 이야기를 힘주어, 또 시정(詩情)을 아낌없이 쏟아부으며 거듭 들려준 것도 그를 위해서였다. 그를 위해 끝없는 공감의 탄성과 미소, 손과 머리카락과 뺨과 입술로 베푸는 자잘한 호의, 나일강의 노래, 보석의 자랑, 베일과 스카프에 담긴 레이스의 정교함, 그에 못지않게 빼어난 인도 명주실 보풀의 또 다른 정교함이 있었다."], ["The idea, old as the oldest of peoples, that beauty is the reward of the hero had never such realism as she contrived for his pleasure; insomuch that he could not doubt he was her hero; she avouched it in a thousand artful ways as natural with her as her beauty—winsome ways reserved, it would seem, by the passionate genius of old Egypt for its daughters.", "가장 오래된 민족만큼이나 오래된 생각, 곧 아름다움은 영웅에게 주어지는 보상이라는 그 생각이, 그녀가 그의 즐거움을 위해 꾸며 낸 만큼 생생한 현실로 다가온 적은 없었다. 그리하여 그는 자기가 그녀의 영웅임을 의심할 수 없었으니, 그녀는 제 아름다움처럼 자연스러운 천 가지 교묘한 방식으로 그것을 단언했다—옛 이집트의 정열적인 천재가 제 딸들을 위해 따로 간직해 둔 듯한, 사람을 호리는 그 방식들로."]], [["Such the Egyptian had been to Ben-Hur from the night of the boat-ride on the lake in the Orchard of Palms. But now!", "야자 동산의 호숫가에서 배를 타던 그날 밤 이후로, 그 이집트 여자는 벤허에게 그런 존재였다. 그러나 지금은!"]], [["Elsewhere in this volume the reader may have observed a term of somewhat indefinite meaning used reverently in a sacred connection; we repeat it now with a general application.", "이 책의 다른 대목에서 독자는 거룩한 맥락에서 경건하게 쓰인, 다소 막연한 뜻의 어떤 말을 보았을 것이다. 우리는 이제 그것을 일반적인 의미로 다시 쓰려 한다."], ["There are few persons who have not a double nature, the real and the acquired; the latter a kind of addendum resulting from education, which in time often perfects it into a part of the being as unquestionable as the first.", "이중의 본성, 곧 타고난 본성과 후천적인 본성을 지니지 않은 사람은 거의 없다. 후자는 교육에서 비롯된 일종의 덧붙임이지만, 세월이 흐르면 흔히 그것이 다듬어져 전자만큼이나 의심할 수 없는 존재의 일부가 된다."], ["Leaving the thought to the thoughtful, we proceed to say that now the real nature of the Egyptian made itself manifest.", "이 생각은 사색을 즐기는 이들에게 맡겨 두고, 우리는 다만 이제 그 이집트 여자의 타고난 본성이 모습을 드러냈음을 말해 두려 한다."]], [["It was not possible for her to have received a stranger with repulsion more incisive; yet she was apparently as passionless as a statue, only the small head was a little tilted, the nostrils a little drawn, and the sensuous lower lip pushed the upper the least bit out of its natural curvature.", "그녀가 낯선 이를 더 날카로운 혐오로 맞이하기란 불가능했을 것이다. 그러면서도 그녀는 겉보기에 조각상처럼 무정해 보였으니, 다만 작은 머리를 조금 갸웃하고, 콧구멍을 살짝 오므렸으며, 관능적인 아랫입술이 윗입술을 본래의 곡선에서 아주 조금 밀어낼 뿐이었다."]], [["She was the first to speak.", "먼저 입을 연 것은 그녀였다."]], [["“Your coming is timely, O son of Hur,”", "\"오, 후르의 아들이여, 마침맞게 오셨군요.\""], ["she said, in a voice sharply distinct.", "그녀는 또렷하고 날카로운 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I wish to thank you for hospitality; after to-morrow I may not have the opportunity to do so.”", "\"베풀어 주신 환대에 감사하고 싶어요. 내일이 지나면 그럴 기회가 없을지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["Ben-Hur bowed slightly without taking his eyes from her.", "벤허는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 가볍게 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“I have heard of a custom which the dice-players observe with good result among themselves,” she continued.", "\"주사위 노름꾼들이 저희끼리 좋은 결과를 보려고 지키는 풍습이 있다고 들었어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“When the game is over, they refer to their tablets and cast up their accounts; then they libate the gods and put a crown upon the happy winner.", "\"노름이 끝나면 그들은 서판을 들여다보며 셈을 맞추지요. 그러고는 신들에게 술을 따라 바치고, 운 좋은 승자에게 화관을 씌워 줘요."], ["We have had a game—it has lasted through many days and nights.", "우리도 한 판 노름을 했어요—여러 날 밤낮에 걸쳐 이어진 노름이었지요."], ["Why, now that it is at an end, shall not we see to which the chaplet belongs?”", "자, 이제 그것이 끝났으니, 화관이 누구의 것인지 가려 보지 않으시겠어요?\""]], [["Yet very watchful, Ben-Hur answered, lightly, “A man may not balk a woman bent on having her way.”", "여전히 빈틈없이 경계하면서도 벤허는 가볍게 대답했다. \"제 뜻을 굽히지 않으려는 여자를 막을 수 있는 남자는 없지요.\""]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말해 보세요.\""], ["she continued, inclining her head, and permitting the sneer to become positive—“tell me, O prince of Jerusalem, where is he, that son of the carpenter of Nazareth, and son not less of God, from whom so lately such mighty things were expected?”", "그녀는 고개를 기울이며, 비웃음이 또렷이 드러나도록 내버려 두고 말을 이었다—\"말해 보세요, 오, 예루살렘의 왕자여, 그가 어디 있나요? 그 나사렛 목수의 아들, 또한 못지않게 하느님의 아들이라던, 얼마 전까지 그토록 엄청난 일을 기대받던 그 사람 말이에요.\""]], [["He waved his hand impatiently, and replied, “I am not his keeper.”", "그는 짜증스럽게 손을 내저으며 대꾸했다. \"나는 그의 파수꾼이 아니오.\""]], [["The beautiful head sank forward yet lower.", "아름다운 머리가 더욱 앞으로 수그러들었다."]], [["“Has he broken Rome to pieces?”", "\"그가 로마를 산산조각 냈던가요?\""]], [["Again, but with anger, Ben-Hur raised his hand in deprecation.", "다시, 그러나 이번에는 노여움을 담아, 벤허는 그만두라는 듯 손을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Where has he seated his capital?”", "\"그는 어디에 도읍을 정했나요?\""], ["she proceeded.", "그녀가 계속했다."], ["“Cannot I go see his throne and its lions of bronze?", "\"가서 그의 옥좌와 청동 사자들을 구경할 수는 없을까요?"], ["And his palace—he raised the dead; and to such a one, what is it to raise a golden house?", "그리고 그의 궁전은요—그는 죽은 자를 살려 냈다지요. 그런 사람에게 황금 집 하나 짓는 게 무슨 대수겠어요?"], ["He has but to stamp his foot and say the word, and the house is, pillared like Karnak, and wanting nothing.”", "발을 한 번 구르며 한마디 하기만 하면, 카르나크처럼 기둥이 늘어선 집이 부족함 없이 들어서겠지요.\""]], [["There was by this time slight ground left to believe her playing; the questions were offensive, and her manner pointed with unfriendliness; seeing which, he on his side became more wary, and said, with good humor, “O Egypt, let us wait another day, even another week, for him, the lions, and the palace.”", "이쯤 되니 그녀가 농담을 하고 있다고 믿을 만한 근거는 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 그 물음들은 모욕적이었고, 태도에는 적의가 날카롭게 서려 있었다. 그것을 알아챈 그는 그 나름대로 더욱 경계하며, 좋은 낯빛으로 말했다. \"오, 이집트여, 그와 사자들과 궁전을 위해서라면, 하루만 더, 아니 한 주일만 더 기다려 봅시다.\""]], [["She went on without noticing the suggestion.", "그녀는 그 제안을 못 들은 척 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“And how is it I see you in that garb?", "\"그런데 어찌하여 당신은 그런 차림새인가요?"], ["Such is not the habit of governors in India or vice-kings elsewhere.", "그건 인도의 총독이나 다른 곳의 부왕(副王)이 하는 차림이 아니지요."], ["I saw the satrap of Teheran once, and he wore a turban of silk and a cloak of cloth of gold, and the hilt and scabbard of his sword made me dizzy with their splendor of precious stones.", "나는 한 번 테헤란의 태수를 본 적이 있는데, 그는 비단 터번을 두르고 금실로 짠 천의 망토를 걸쳤으며, 검의 자루와 칼집은 보석의 광채로 내 눈을 어지럽게 했어요."], ["I thought Osiris had lent him a glory from the sun.", "나는 오시리스가 해에서 광휘를 빌려 그에게 준 줄 알았답니다."], ["I fear you have not entered upon your kingdom—the kingdom I was to share with you.”", "아무래도 당신은 아직 당신의 왕국에—나와 함께 나누기로 했던 그 왕국에 들어서지 못한 모양이군요.\""]], [["“The daughter of my wise guest is kinder than she imagines herself; she is teaching me that Isis may kiss a heart without making it better.”", "\"내 슬기로운 손님의 따님은 자기가 생각하는 것보다 친절하시군요. 이시스가 마음에 입을 맞춘다 해서 그 마음이 더 나아지는 건 아님을 내게 가르쳐 주고 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur spoke with cold courtesy, and Iras, after playing with the pendent solitaire of her necklace of coins, rejoined, “For a Jew, the son of Hur is clever.", "벤허가 차가운 예의를 갖추어 말하자, 이라스는 동전 목걸이에 매달린 외알 보석을 만지작거리다가 받아쳤다. \"유대인치고는, 후르의 아들은 영리하군요."], ["I saw your dreaming Cæsar make his entry into Jerusalem.", "나는 당신의 꿈꾸는 카이사르가 예루살렘에 입성하는 걸 보았어요."], ["You told us he would that day proclaim himself King of the Jews from the steps of the Temple.", "당신은 그날 그가 성전 계단에서 유대인의 왕임을 선포하리라 했지요."], ["I beheld the procession descend the mountain bringing him.", "나는 행렬이 그를 모시고 산을 내려오는 걸 지켜보았어요."], ["I heard their singing.", "그들이 노래하는 걸 들었지요."], ["They were beautiful with palms in motion.", "그들은 종려나무 가지를 흔들며 아름다웠어요."], ["I looked everywhere among them for a figure with a promise of royalty—a horseman in purple, a chariot with a driver in shining brass, a stately warrior behind an orbed shield, rivalling his spear in stature.", "나는 그 무리 가운데서 왕다운 기상을 약속하는 인물을 찾아 사방을 둘러보았어요—자줏빛 옷을 입은 기마병, 번쩍이는 놋쇠 갑옷의 마부가 모는 전차, 둥근 방패 뒤에 선, 키가 제 창에 견줄 만한 당당한 무사를요."], ["I looked for his guard. It would have been pleasant to have seen a prince of Jerusalem and a cohort of the legions of Galilee.”", "나는 그의 호위대를 찾았어요. 예루살렘의 왕자와 갈릴리 군단의 한 보병대를 보았다면 기뻤을 텐데요.\""]], [["She flung her listener a glance of provoking disdain, then laughed heartily, as if the ludicrousness of the picture in her mind were too strong for contempt.", "그녀는 듣는 이에게 사람을 약 올리는 멸시의 눈길을 던지고는, 마음속 그림의 우스꽝스러움이 경멸을 누를 만큼 강렬했다는 듯, 한바탕 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["“Instead of a Sesostris returning in triumph or a Cæsar helmed and sworded—ha, ha, ha!—I saw a man with a woman’s face and hair, riding an ass’s colt, and in tears. The King!", "\"개선하여 돌아오는 세소스트리스나 투구를 쓰고 칼을 찬 카이사르 대신에—하, 하, 하!—나는 여자의 얼굴과 머리를 한 사내가 나귀 새끼를 타고 눈물 흘리는 걸 보았어요. 그 왕!"], ["the Son of God!", "하느님의 아들!"], ["the Redeemer of the world! Ha, ha, ha!”", "세상의 구원자! 하, 하, 하!\""]], [["In spite of himself, Ben-Hur winced.", "벤허는 자기도 모르게 움찔했다."]], [["“I did not quit my place, O prince of Jerusalem,”", "\"나는 자리를 뜨지 않았어요, 오, 예루살렘의 왕자여.\""], ["she said, before he could recover.", "그가 미처 정신을 추스르기도 전에 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I did not laugh.", "\"나는 웃지 않았어요."], ["I said to myself, ‘Wait.", "나는 속으로 말했지요."], ["In the Temple he will glorify himself as becomes a hero about to take possession of the world.’ I saw him enter the Gate of Shushan and the Court of the Women.", "'기다려라. 성전에서 그는 세상을 차지하려는 영웅답게 자기를 영광스럽게 드러낼 것이다.' 나는 그가 수산 문과 여인의 뜰로 들어가는 걸 보았어요."], ["I saw him stop and stand before the Gate Beautiful.", "그가 아름다운 문 앞에 멈춰 서는 걸 보았지요."], ["There were people with me on the porch and in the courts, and on the cloisters and on the steps of the three sides of the Temple there were other people—I will say a million of people, all waiting breathlessly to hear his proclamation.", "회랑과 뜰에는 나와 함께 있던 사람들이 있었고, 성전 세 면의 주랑과 계단에는 또 다른 사람들이 있었어요—백만 명은 되었다고 해 두지요. 모두 숨죽이며 그의 선포를 들으려 기다리고 있었어요. 기둥들도 우리보다 더 고요하진 못했어요."], ["The pillars were not more still than we. Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하! 나는 그 거대한 로마 기계의 굴대가 삐걱거리기 시작하는 소리를 들은 것만 같았어요."], ["I fancied I heard the axles of the mighty Roman machine begin to crack. Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하! 오, 왕자여, 솔로몬의 영혼을 두고 맹세컨대, 당신의 세상의 왕은 옷자락을 추슬러 걸어가 버렸어요."], ["O prince, by the soul of Solomon, your King of the World drew his gown about him and walked away, and out by the farthest gate, nor opened his mouth to say a word; and—the Roman machine is running yet!”", "가장 먼 문으로 나가 버렸지요. 한마디 하려고 입을 열지도 않았어요. 그리고—로마 기계는 아직도 잘만 돌아가고 있답니다!\""]], [["In simple homage to a hope that instant lost—a hope which, as it began to fall and while it was falling, he unconsciously followed with a parting look down to its disappearance—Ben-Hur lowered his eyes.", "그 순간 잃어버린 한 가닥 희망—무너지기 시작하여 무너져 가는 동안, 그가 무심코 그 사라짐을 끝까지 작별의 눈길로 좇았던 그 희망—에 대한 순수한 경의로, 벤허는 눈을 내리깔았다."]], [["At no previous time, whether when Balthasar was plying him with arguments, or when miracles were being done before his face, had the disputed nature of the Nazarene been so plainly set before him.", "발타사르가 그에게 논증을 펼치던 때든, 그의 눈앞에서 기적이 일어나던 때든, 그 어느 때에도 나사렛 사람의 논란 많은 본성이 이토록 또렷하게 그의 앞에 펼쳐진 적은 없었다."], ["The best way, after all, to reach an understanding of the divine is by study of the human.", "따지고 보면 신적인 것을 이해하는 가장 좋은 길은 인간적인 것을 살피는 데 있다."], ["In the things superior to men we may always look to find God.", "사람보다 뛰어난 것들 속에서 우리는 언제나 하느님을 찾아볼 수 있다."], ["So with the picture given by the Egyptian of the scene when the Nazarene turned from the Gate Beautiful; its central theme was an act utterly beyond performance by a man under control of merely human inspirations.", "나사렛 사람이 아름다운 문에서 돌아서던 장면을 그 이집트 여자가 그려 낸 그림도 그러했다. 그 중심 주제는 한낱 인간의 영감에 좌우되는 사람으로서는 도저히 해낼 수 없는 행동이었다."], ["A parable to a parable-loving people, it taught what the Christ had so often asserted—that his mission was not political.", "비유를 좋아하는 백성에게 던진 한 편의 비유로서, 그것은 그리스도가 그토록 자주 단언한 바—자기의 사명은 정치적인 것이 아니라는 것을 가르쳐 주었다."], ["There was not much more time for thought of all this than that allowed for a common respiration; yet the idea took fast hold of Ben-Hur, and in the same instant he followed his hope of vengeance out of sight, and the man with the woman’s face and hair, and in tears, came near to him—near enough to leave something of his spirit behind.", "이 모든 것을 생각할 시간은 보통의 한 호흡에 허락된 만큼밖에 되지 않았다. 그런데도 그 생각은 벤허를 단단히 사로잡았고, 같은 순간에 그는 복수의 희망이 시야에서 사라지는 것을 끝까지 좇았으며, 여자의 얼굴과 머리를 하고 눈물 흘리던 그 사람이 그에게 가까이 다가왔다—제 영혼의 무언가를 그에게 남기고 갈 만큼 가까이."]], [["“Daughter of Balthasar,”", "\"발타사르의 따님이여.\""], ["he said, with dignity, “if this be the game of which you spoke to me, take the chaplet—I accord it yours.", "그가 위엄을 갖추어 말했다. \"이것이 당신이 내게 말한 그 노름이라면, 화관을 가지시오—그대의 것임을 인정하리다."], ["Only let us make an end of words.", "다만 이제 말은 그만둡시다."], ["That you have a purpose I am sure.", "당신에게 어떤 목적이 있음을 나는 확신하오."], ["To it, I pray, and I will answer you; then let us go our several ways, and forget we ever met.", "부디 그 본론으로 들어가시오, 그러면 답하리다. 그런 다음 우리는 각자의 길로 가서, 서로 만났던 일을 잊읍시다. 말하시오."], ["Say on; I will listen, but not to more of that which you have given me.”", "들어 드리리다. 다만 당신이 지금까지 내게 들려준 그런 말은 더 이상 듣지 않겠소.\""]], [["She regarded him intently a moment, as if determining what to do—possibly she might have been measuring his will—then she said, coldly, “You have my leave—go.”", "그녀는 무엇을 할지 작정하려는 듯—어쩌면 그의 의지를 가늠하고 있었는지도 모른다—잠시 그를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 차갑게 말했다. \"허락하지요—가세요.\""]], [["“Peace to you,” he responded, and walked away.", "\"그대에게 평안이 있기를.\" 그는 그렇게 답하고 걸어 나갔다."]], [["As he was about passing out of the door, she called to him.", "문을 막 나서려는데, 그녀가 그를 불러 세웠다."]], [["“A word.”", "\"한마디만.\""]], [["He stopped where he was, and looked back.", "그는 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 돌아보았다."]], [["“Consider all I know about you.”", "\"내가 당신에 대해 아는 모든 걸 헤아려 보세요.\""]], [["“O most fair Egyptian,”", "\"오, 더없이 아리따운 이집트 여인이여.\""], ["he said, returning, “what all do you know about me?”", "그는 되돌아오며 말했다. \"당신이 나에 대해 안다는 그 모든 게 대체 무엇이오?\""]], [["She looked at him absently.", "그녀는 멍하니 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“You are more of a Roman, son of Hur, then any of your Hebrew brethren.”", "\"당신은, 후르의 아들이여, 당신의 어느 히브리 형제보다도 로마인에 가까운 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Am I so unlike my countrymen?” he asked, indifferently.", "\"내가 그렇게도 내 동족과 다르단 말이오?\" 그가 무심히 물었다."]], [["“The demi-gods are all Roman now,”", "\"반신반인(半神半人)은 이제 모두 로마인이지요.\""], ["she rejoined.", "그녀가 받아쳤다."]], [["“And therefore you will tell me what more you know about me?”", "\"그래서 당신은 나에 대해 더 아는 걸 내게 말해 주려는 거요?\""]], [["“The likeness is not lost upon me. It might induce me to save you.”", "\"그 닮음이 내 눈을 비껴가지는 않았어요. 그것이 어쩌면 당신을 구해 주고 싶은 마음을 내게 일으킬지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Save me!”", "\"날 구한다고!\""]], [["The pink-stained fingers toyed daintily with the lustrous pendant at the throat, and her voice was exceeding low and soft; only a tapping on the floor with her silken sandal admonished him to have a care.", "연분홍빛으로 물든 손가락이 목에 걸린 윤기 흐르는 장식을 곱게 만지작거렸고, 그녀의 목소리는 더없이 낮고 부드러웠다. 다만 비단 신발로 바닥을 톡톡 두드리는 소리만이 조심하라고 그에게 일러 주었다."]], [["“There was a Jew, an escaped galley-slave, who killed a man in the Palace of Idernee,”", "\"한 유대인이 있었어요. 갤리선에서 도망친 노예였는데, 이데르네이 궁전에서 한 사내를 죽였지요.\""], ["she began, slowly.", "그녀가 천천히 말문을 열었다."]], [["Ben-Hur was startled.", "벤허는 흠칫했다."]], [["“The same Jew slew a Roman soldier before the Market-place here in Jerusalem; the same Jew has three trained legions from Galilee to seize the Roman governor to-night; the same Jew has alliances perfected for war upon Rome, and Ilderim the Sheik is one of his partners.”", "\"같은 그 유대인이 여기 예루살렘의 시장 앞에서 로마 병사 하나를 죽였어요. 같은 그 유대인이 오늘 밤 로마 총독을 사로잡으려고 갈릴리에서 훈련된 세 개 군단을 데려왔지요. 같은 그 유대인이 로마와 싸우려고 동맹을 완벽하게 맺어 두었는데, 일데림 족장이 그 동맹자 가운데 하나예요.\""]], [["Drawing nearer him, she almost whispered,", "그에게 더 가까이 다가서며, 그녀는 거의 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“You have lived in Rome.", "\"당신은 로마에서 살아 보았지요."], ["Suppose these things repeated in ears we know of. Ah!", "이 일들이 우리 둘 다 아는 어떤 귀에 들어간다고 해 봐요. 아!"], ["you change color.”", "당신, 안색이 변하는군요.\""]], [["He drew back from her with somewhat of the look which may be imagined upon the face of a man who, thinking to play with a kitten, has run upon a tiger; and she proceeded:", "그는 그녀에게서 물러섰으니, 그 얼굴에는 새끼 고양이와 장난치려다 호랑이에게 달려든 사람의 얼굴에 떠올랐을 법한 빛이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“You are acquainted in the antechamber, and know the Lord Sejanus.", "\"당신은 궁정 대기실 사람들과 안면이 있고, 세야누스 공도 알지요."], ["Suppose it were told him with the proofs in hand—or without the proofs—that the same Jew is the richest man in the East—nay, in all the empire.", "증거를 손에 쥐고서—아니, 증거 없이라도—그 같은 유대인이 동방에서 가장 부유한 사람이라고, 아니 온 제국에서 가장 부유한 사람이라고 그에게 일러바친다고 해 봐요."], ["The fishes of the Tiber would have fattening other than that they dig out of its ooze, would they not?", "티베르강의 물고기들은 강바닥 진흙에서 파내는 것 말고 다른 살찌울 거리를 얻게 되지 않겠어요?"], ["And while they were feeding—ha!", "그리고 그것들이 배를 불리는 동안—하!"], ["son of Hur!—what splendor there would be on exhibition in the Circus!", "후르의 아들이여!—원형경기장에는 얼마나 화려한 구경거리가 펼쳐지겠어요!"], ["Amusing the Roman people is a fine art; getting the money to keep them amused is another art even finer; and was there ever an artist the equal of the Lord Sejanus?”", "로마 백성을 즐겁게 하는 건 훌륭한 기술이지요. 그들을 계속 즐겁게 해 줄 돈을 마련하는 건 그보다 더 훌륭한 기술이고요. 한데 세야누스 공에 견줄 만한 예술가가 일찍이 있었던가요?\""]], [["Ben-Hur was not too much stirred by the evident baseness of the woman for recollection.", "벤허는 그 여자의 명백한 비열함에 너무 마음이 흔들린 나머지 도리어 기억을 잃을 지경은 아니었다."], ["Not unfrequently when all the other faculties are numb and failing memory does its offices with the greatest fidelity.", "다른 모든 능력이 마비되어 스러져 갈 때에도, 흔히 기억은 더없이 충실하게 제 소임을 다하는 법이다."], ["The scene at the spring on the way to the Jordan reproduced itself; and he remembered thinking then that Esther had betrayed him, and thinking so now, he said calmly as he could,", "요르단으로 가는 길의 샘가 장면이 되살아났다. 그때 그는 에스델이 자기를 배신했다고 생각했음을 떠올렸고, 지금도 그렇게 생각하며, 될 수 있는 한 침착하게 말했다."]], [["“To give you pleasure, daughter of Egypt, I acknowledge your cunning, and that I am at your mercy.", "\"당신을 즐겁게 해 드리고자, 이집트의 따님이여, 나는 당신의 교활함을 인정하고, 내가 당신의 처분에 달려 있음을 인정하리다."], ["It may also please you to hear me acknowledge I have no hope of your favor.", "또한 당신의 호의 따위는 바라지 않음을 내가 인정하는 것을 듣는 것도 당신을 즐겁게 할지 모르겠소."], ["I could kill you, but you are a woman.", "나는 당신을 죽일 수도 있으나, 당신은 여자요."], ["The Desert is open to receive me; and though Rome is a good hunter of men, there she would follow long and far before she caught me, for in its heart there are wildernesses of spears as well as wildernesses of sand, and it is not unlovely to the unconquered Parthian.", "사막은 나를 받아들이려 열려 있소. 비록 로마가 사람을 잘 사냥하는 자라 하나, 나를 붙잡기까지는 거기서 오래도록 멀리 쫓아야 할 것이오. 그 사막의 한복판에는 모래의 광야뿐 아니라 창의 광야도 있고, 정복되지 않은 파르티아인에게 그곳은 그리 정 떨어지는 곳도 아니니 말이오."], ["In the toils as I am—dupe that I have been—yet there is one thing my due: who told you all you know about me?", "이렇게 덫에 걸려—얼마나 어리석은 멍청이였던가—그래도 내게 마땅히 주어져야 할 것이 하나 있소."], ["In flight or captivity, dying even, there will be consolation in leaving the traitor the curse of a man who has lived knowing nothing but wretchedness.", "나에 대해 당신이 아는 그 모든 걸 누가 말해 주었소? 도망치든 사로잡히든, 설령 죽는다 해도, 비참함 말고는 아무것도 모르고 살아온 사내의 저주를 그 배신자에게 남기는 것이 위안이 될 거요."], ["Who told you all you know about me?”", "나에 대해 당신이 아는 그 모든 걸 누가 말해 주었소?\""]], [["It might have been a touch of art, or might have been sincere—that as it may—the expression of the Egyptian’s face became sympathetic.", "기교의 한 자락이었는지, 아니면 진심이었는지—그야 어쨌든—그 이집트 여자의 얼굴에는 공감의 빛이 떠올랐다."]], [["“There are in my country, O son of Hur,”", "\"내 나라에는요, 오, 후르의 아들이여.\""], ["she said, presently, “workmen who make pictures by gathering vari-colored shells here and there on the sea-shore after storms, and cutting them up, and patching the pieces as inlaying on marble slabs.", "그녀가 이윽고 말했다. \"폭풍이 지나간 뒤 바닷가 여기저기서 빛깔 고운 조개껍데기를 주워 모아 그걸 잘라서, 그 조각들을 대리석판에 박아 넣어 그림을 만드는 장인들이 있어요. 비밀을 캐러 다니는 이들에게 그 솜씨가 주는 암시가 보이지 않으세요?"], ["Can you not see the hint there is in the practice to such as go searching for secrets?", "이 사람에게서 자잘한 사정 한 줌을 모으고, 저 사람에게서 또 한 줌을 모아서, 한참 뒤에 그것들을 짜 맞춘 끝에, 한 사내의 재산과 목숨을 마음대로 할 수 있게 된 여자가 누릴 수 있는 만큼 행복했다고 해 두면 충분하겠지요. 그 사내를—\""], ["Enough that from this person I gathered a handful of little circumstances, and from that other yet another handful, and that afterwhile I put them together, and was happy as a woman can be who has at disposal the fortune and life of a man whom”—she stopped, and beat the floor with her foot, and looked away as if to hide a sudden emotion from him; with an air of even painful resolution she presently finished the sentence—“whom she is at loss what to do with.”", "그녀는 말을 멈추고 발로 바닥을 두드리며, 별안간 솟구친 감정을 그에게 감추려는 듯 눈길을 돌렸다. 이윽고 고통스럽다 할 만한 결연한 태도로 그 문장을 끝맺었다. \"—그 사내를 어찌해야 할지 몰라 막막한 여자가요.\""]], [["“No, it is not enough,”", "\"아니, 그것으로는 충분치 않소.\""], ["Ben-Hur said, unmoved by the play—“it is not enough.", "벤허는 그 연극에 흔들리지 않고 말했다—\"충분치 않소."], ["To-morrow you will determine what to do with me. I may die.”", "내일 당신은 나를 어찌할지 정하겠지. 나는 죽을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["she rejoined quickly and with emphasis, “I had something from Sheik Ilderim as he lay with my father in a grove out in the Desert.", "그녀는 재빨리, 힘주어 받아쳤다. \"일데림 족장에게서 무언가를 얻었지요. 그가 사막의 한 숲에서 내 아버지와 함께 누워 있을 때였어요."], ["The night was still, very still, and the walls of the tent, sooth to say, were poor ward against ears outside listening to—birds and beetles flying through the air.”", "밤은 고요했어요, 아주 고요했지요. 그리고, 솔직히 말하면, 천막의 벽이란 게 밖에서 엿듣는 귀를 막는 데는 변변치 못한 방패더군요—공중을 날아다니는 새와 풍뎅이의 귀 말이에요.\""]], [["She smiled at the conceit, but proceeded:", "그녀는 제 익살에 미소 짓고는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Some other things—bits of shell for the picture—I had from—”", "\"또 다른 것들—그림에 박을 조개껍데기 몇 조각은—이런 이에게서 얻었지요—\" \"누구한테서?\""]], [["“Whom?” “The son of Hur himself.”", "\"바로 후르의 아들 자신에게서요.\""]], [["“Was there no other who contributed?”", "\"달리 거든 사람은 없었소?\""]], [["“No, not one.”", "\"없어요, 하나도.\""]], [["Hur drew a breath of relief, and said, lightly, “Thanks.", "후르는 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 가볍게 말했다. \"고맙소."], ["It were not well to keep the Lord Sejanus waiting for you.", "세야누스 공을 기다리게 해서야 당신에게 좋지 않겠지."], ["The Desert is not so sensitive. Again, O Egypt, peace!”", "사막은 그렇게 까다롭지 않다오. 다시 한번, 오, 이집트여, 평안하시오!\""]], [["To this time he had been standing uncovered; now he took the handkerchief from his arm where it had been hanging, and adjusting it upon his head, turned to depart.", "이때까지 그는 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않은 채 서 있었다. 이제 그는 팔에 걸쳐 두었던 머릿수건을 집어 머리에 매만져 두르고는, 떠나려고 몸을 돌렸다."], ["But she arrested him; in her eagerness, she even reached a hand to him.", "그러나 그녀가 그를 붙잡았다. 다급한 마음에 그녀는 그에게 손까지 내밀었다."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"가지 마세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He looked back at her, but without taking the hand, though it was very noticeable for its sparkling of jewels; and he knew by her manner that the reserved point of the scene which was so surprising to him was now to come.", "그는 그녀를 돌아보았으나, 보석으로 눈부시게 빛나 유난히 눈에 띄는 그 손은 잡지 않았다. 그리고 그녀의 태도로 미루어, 자기를 그토록 놀라게 한 이 장면의 아껴 둔 핵심이 이제 나오리라는 것을 그는 알았다."]], [["“Stay, and do not distrust me, O son of Hur, if I declare I know why the noble Arrius took you for his heir. And, by Isis!", "\"가지 말고, 나를 의심하지 마세요, 오, 후르의 아들이여. 고귀한 아리우스가 어찌하여 당신을 상속자로 삼았는지 내가 안다고 말한다 해도요."], ["by all the gods of Egypt!", "그리고, 이시스를 두고!"], ["I swear I tremble to think of you, so brave and generous, under the hand of the remorseless minister.", "이집트의 모든 신을 두고 맹세컨대! 그토록 용감하고 너그러운 당신이 그 무자비한 대신의 손아귀에 들어간다고 생각하면 나는 몸이 떨려요."], ["You have left a portion of your youth in the atria of the great capital; consider, as I do, what the Desert will be to you in contrast of life.", "당신은 위대한 수도의 안마당에 청춘의 한 자락을 두고 왔지요. 나처럼 헤아려 보세요. 그런 삶에 견주어 사막이 당신에게 어떤 것일지를요."], ["Oh, I give you pity—pity!", "오, 당신이 가여워요—가여워!"], ["And if you but do what I say, I will save you.", "당신이 내 말대로만 하면, 내가 당신을 구해 드리겠어요."], ["That, also, I swear, by our holy Isis!”", "그것도, 우리의 거룩한 이시스를 두고 맹세하지요!\""]], [["Words of entreaty and prayer these, poured forth volubly and with earnestness and the mighty sanction of beauty.", "이 모든 애원과 간청의 말이 거침없이, 진지하게, 그리고 아름다움이라는 강력한 권위에 실려 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["“Almost—almost I believe you,”", "\"하마터면—하마터면 당신을 믿을 뻔했소.\""], ["Ben-Hur said, yet hesitatingly, and in a voice low and indistinct; for a doubt remained with him grumbling against the yielding tendency of the man—a good sturdy doubt, such a one as has saved many a life and fortune.", "벤허는 그래도 망설이며, 낮고 또렷하지 않은 목소리로 말했다. 그의 안에는 사내의 누그러지려는 성향에 맞서 으르렁대는 의심이 남아 있었기 때문이다—수많은 목숨과 재산을 구해 낸, 든든하고 굳건한 그런 의심이었다."]], [["“The perfect life for a woman is to live in love; the greatest happiness for a man is the conquest of himself; and that, O prince, is what I have to ask of you.”", "\"여자에게 완전한 삶은 사랑 속에 사는 것이고, 남자에게 가장 큰 행복은 자기 자신을 이기는 것이지요. 그리고, 오, 왕자여, 그것이 바로 내가 당신에게 청하려는 것이랍니다.\""]], [["She spoke rapidly, and with animation; indeed, she had never appeared to him so fascinating.", "그녀는 빠르게, 생기 넘치게 말했다. 정말이지 그녀가 그토록 매혹적으로 보인 적은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["“You had once a friend,”", "\"당신에게는 한때 벗이 있었지요.\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 이어 갔다."], ["“It was in your boyhood.", "\"당신의 소년 시절이었어요."], ["There was a quarrel, and you and he became enemies. He did you wrong.", "다툼이 있었고, 당신과 그는 원수가 되었지요. 그가 당신에게 못된 짓을 했어요."], ["After many years you met him again in the Circus at Antioch.”", "여러 해 뒤, 당신은 안티오크의 원형경기장에서 그를 다시 만났지요.\""]], [["“Messala!”", "\"메살라!\""]], [["“Yes, Messala.", "\"그래요, 메살라."], ["You are his creditor.", "당신은 그의 채권자예요. 지난 일을 용서하세요. 그를 다시 벗으로 받아들이세요."], ["Forgive the past; admit him to friendship again; restore the fortune he lost in the great wager; rescue him.", "그가 그 큰 내기에서 잃은 재산을 되돌려 주세요. 그를 구해 주세요. 당신에게 여섯 탈렌트는 아무것도 아니에요."], ["The six talents are as nothing to you; not so much as a bud lost upon a tree already in full leaf; but to him— Ah, he must go about with a broken body; wherever you meet him he must look up to you from the ground.", "이미 잎이 무성한 나무에서 봉오리 하나 잃는 것만도 못하지요. 하지만 그에게는—아, 그는 부서진 몸을 이끌고 살아가야 해요. 당신을 만나는 곳마다 그는 땅바닥에서 당신을 우러러보아야 하지요."], ["O Ben-Hur, noble prince!", "오, 벤허, 고귀한 왕자여!"], ["to a Roman descended as he is beggary is the other most odious name for death.", "그처럼 혈통 있는 로마인에게 거지 신세란 죽음의 또 다른, 가장 끔찍한 이름이에요."], ["Save him from beggary!”", "그를 거지 신세에서 구해 주세요!\""]], [["If the rapidity with which she spoke was a cunning invention to keep him from thinking, either she never knew or else had forgotten that there are convictions which derive nothing from thought, but drop into place without leave or notice.", "만일 그녀가 그토록 빠르게 말한 것이 그를 생각하지 못하게 하려는 교묘한 술책이었다면, 그녀는 생각에서 비롯되지 않고 허락도 기별도 없이 제자리에 떨어지는 어떤 확신이 있다는 것을 아예 몰랐거나, 아니면 잊고 있었던 셈이다."], ["It seemed to him, when at last she paused to have his answer, that he could see Messala himself peering at him over her shoulder; and in its expression the countenance of the Roman was not that of a mendicant or a friend; the sneer was as patrician as ever, and the fine edge of the hauteur as flawless and irritating.", "마침내 그녀가 그의 대답을 들으려고 말을 멈추었을 때, 그에게는 메살라 자신이 그녀의 어깨 너머로 자기를 들여다보는 것이 보이는 듯했다. 그리고 그 표정에 어린 로마인의 얼굴은 구걸하는 자의 것도, 벗의 것도 아니었다. 그 비웃음은 여전히 귀족다웠고, 거만함의 그 날카로운 날은 흠 하나 없이 사람을 거슬렸다."]], [["“The appeal has been decided then, and for once a Messala takes nothing.", "\"그렇다면 청은 판가름이 났고, 메살라가 단 한 번 아무것도 얻지 못하게 되었군."], ["I must go and write it in my book of great occurrences—a judgment by a Roman against a Roman!", "가서 내 큰일들의 기록부에 적어 두어야겠소—로마인에 맞서 로마인이 내린 판결이라고!"], ["But did he—did Messala send you to me with this request, O Egypt?”", "한데 그가—메살라가 이 청을 가지고 당신을 내게 보냈소, 오, 이집트여?\""]], [["“He has a noble nature, and judged you by it.”", "\"그는 고귀한 천성을 지녔기에, 당신도 그러하리라 여겼지요.\""]], [["Ben-Hur took the hand upon arm.", "벤허는 그녀의 팔에 얹힌 손을 잡았다."]], [["“As you know him in such friendly way, fair Egyptian, tell me, would he do for me, there being a reversal of the conditions, that he asks of me?", "\"당신이 그를 그토록 다정한 사이로 안다 하니, 어여쁜 이집트 여인이여, 말해 주시오. 처지가 뒤바뀌어, 그가 내게 청하는 것을 내가 그에게 청한다면, 그가 나를 위해 그리해 주겠소?"], ["Answer, by Isis! Answer, for the truth’s sake!”", "이시스를 두고 답하시오! 진실을 위해 답하시오!\""]], [["There was insistence in the touch of his hand, and in his look also.", "그의 손길에도, 눈길에도 다그침이 서려 있었다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["she began, “he is—”", "그녀가 입을 열었다. \"그는—\""]], [["“A Roman, you were about to say; meaning that I, a Jew, must not determine dues from me to him by any measure of dues from him to me; being a Jew, I must forgive him my winnings because he is a Roman.", "\"로마인이다, 그렇게 말하려던 거지. 곧 나, 유대인은 그가 내게 진 빚의 잣대로 내가 그에게 받을 빚을 가늠해서는 안 된다는 거요. 유대인이니, 나는 그가 로마인이라는 이유로 내가 딴 것을 그에게 용서해 주어야 한다는 거지."], ["If you have more to tell me, daughter of Balthasar, speak quickly, quickly; for by the Lord God of Israel, when this heat of blood, hotter waxing, attains its highest, I may not be able longer to see that you are a woman, and beautiful!", "더 할 말이 있거든, 발타사르의 따님이여, 어서, 어서 말하시오. 이스라엘의 주 하느님을 두고 맹세컨대, 이 끓는 피가 더욱 뜨거워져 가장 높은 곳에 이르면, 나는 더 이상 당신이 여자이며 아름답다는 것을 보지 못할지도 모르오!"], ["I may see but the spy of a master the more hateful because the master is a Roman.", "다만 한 주인의 첩자만이 보일 뿐이며, 그 주인이 로마인이기에 더욱 가증스러울 따름이오."], ["Say on, and quickly.”", "말하시오, 그것도 어서.\""]], [["She threw his hand off and stepped back into the full light, with all the evil of her nature collected in her eyes and voice.", "그녀는 그의 손을 뿌리치고 환한 빛 속으로 물러서며, 제 본성의 온갖 악을 두 눈과 목소리에 모았다."]], [["“Thou drinker of lees, feeder upon husks!", "\"이 찌꺼기나 들이켜고 쭉정이나 먹는 자야!"], ["To think I could love thee, having seen Messala!", "메살라를 보고서도 내가 너를 사랑할 수 있었으리라 생각하다니!"], ["Such as thou were born to serve him.", "너 같은 자는 그를 섬기려고 태어난 것이다."], ["He would have been satisfied with release of the six talents; but I say to the six thou shalt add twenty—twenty, dost thou hear?", "그는 여섯 탈렌트를 풀어 주는 것으로 만족했을 테지만, 나는 말한다. 그 여섯에 스무 탈렌트를 더하라—스물, 듣고 있느냐?"], ["The kissings of my little finger which thou hast taken from him, though with my consent, shall be paid for; and that I have followed thee with affection of sympathy, and endured thee so long, enter into the account not less because I was serving him.", "비록 내 동의로 그에게서 가져갔다 하나, 네가 가져간 내 새끼손가락의 입맞춤도 값을 치러야 한다. 또한 내가 공감의 애정으로 너를 좇으며 그토록 오래 너를 견뎌 온 것도, 그를 섬기느라 그리한 것이라 해서 셈에서 빼지 못한다."], ["The merchant here is thy keeper of moneys.", "여기 이 상인이 네 돈을 맡아 둔 자이니라."], ["If by to-morrow at noon he has not thy order acted upon in favor of my Messala for six-and-twenty talents—mark the sum!—thou shalt settle with the Lord Sejanus. Be wise and—farewell.”", "내일 정오까지 그가 내 메살라에게 스물여섯 탈렌트—그 액수를 똑똑히 새겨라!—를 치르라는 네 명령을 실행하지 않는다면, 너는 세야누스 공과 셈을 치르게 될 것이다. 슬기롭게 굴어라, 그리고—잘 있거라.\""]], [["As she was going to the door, he put himself in her way.", "그녀가 문으로 가려 하자, 그가 그 앞을 가로막았다."]], [["“The old Egypt lives in you,” he said. “Whether you see Messala to-morrow or the next day, here or in Rome, give him this message. Tell him I have back the money, even the six talents, he robbed me of by robbing my father’s estate; tell him I survived the galleys to which he had me sent, and in my strength rejoice in his beggary and dishonor; tell him I think the affliction of body which he has from my hand is the curse of our Lord God of Israel upon him more fit than death for his crimes against the helpless; tell him my mother and sister whom he had sent to a cell in Antonia that they might die of leprosy, are alive and well, thanks to the power of the Nazarene whom you so despise; tell him that, to fill my measure of happiness, they are restored to me, and that I will go hence to their love, and find in it more than compensation for the impure passions which you leave me to take to him; tell him—this for your comfort, O cunning incarnate, as much as his—tell him that when the Lord Sejanus comes to despoil me he will find nothing; for the inheritance I had from the duumvir, including the villa by Misenum, has been sold, and the money from the sale is out of reach, afloat in the marts of the world as bills of exchange; and that this house and the goods and merchandise and the ships and caravans with which Simonides plies his commerce with such princely profits are covered by imperial safeguards—a wise head having found the price of the favor, and the Lord Sejanus preferring a reasonable gain in the way of gift to much gain fished from pools of blood and wrong; tell him if all this were not so, if the money and property were all mine, yet should he not have the least part of it, for when he finds our Jewish bills, and forces them to give up their values, there is yet another resort left me—a deed of gift to Cæsar—so much, O Egypt, I found out in the atria of the great capital; tell him that along with my defiance I do not send him a curse in words, but, as a better expression of my undying hate, I send him one who will prove to him the sum of all curses; and when he looks at you repeating this my message, daughter of Balthasar, his Roman shrewdness will tell him all I mean. Go now—and I will go.”", "\"옛 이집트가 당신 안에 살아 있구려.\" 그가 말했다. \"내일이든 모레든, 여기서든 로마에서든 메살라를 만나거든, 그에게 이 전갈을 전하시오. 그가 내 아버지의 재산을 강탈하여 내게서 빼앗은 돈을, 그 여섯 탈렌트마저 도로 찾았다고 그에게 전하시오. 그가 나를 보낸 갤리선에서 살아남아, 이제 내 힘으로 그의 거지 신세와 불명예를 기뻐한다고 전하시오. 내 손으로 그에게 입힌 몸의 고통이, 그가 무력한 자들에게 저지른 죄에 대해 죽음보다 더 합당한, 이스라엘의 주 하느님께서 그에게 내리신 저주라고 내가 여긴다고 전하시오. 그가 나병으로 죽으라고 안토니아의 감방에 보냈던 내 어머니와 누이가, 당신이 그토록 업신여기는 나사렛 사람의 권능 덕분에 살아 건강하다고 전하시오. 내 행복을 가득 채우려고 그들이 내게 돌아왔으며, 나는 이제 그들의 사랑을 향해 떠나, 당신이 내게 그에게로 가져가라고 떠넘긴 그 더러운 정욕에 대한 보상보다 더한 것을 그 사랑 속에서 찾으리라고 전하시오. 그에게 전하시오—오, 교활함의 화신이여, 이건 그를 위해서만큼이나 당신을 위한 위안이오—세야누스 공이 나를 약탈하러 와도 아무것도 찾지 못하리라고 전하시오. 미세눔의 별장을 비롯해 그 두움비르에게서 물려받은 유산은 이미 팔아넘겼고, 그 판 돈은 환어음이 되어 세상의 시장들을 떠다니며 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있으니 말이오. 그리고 이 집과 재물과 상품, 시모니데스가 그토록 어마어마한 이문을 남기며 장사를 부리는 배와 대상(隊商)들은 황제의 보호를 받고 있다고 전하시오—슬기로운 머리가 그 호의의 값을 알아냈고, 세야누스 공은 피와 불의의 웅덩이에서 낚아 올린 큰 이익보다 선물의 형식으로 들어오는 적당한 이득을 더 좋아하니 말이오. 설령 이 모든 게 그렇지 않다 해도, 그 돈과 재산이 모조리 내 것이라 해도, 그는 그 가운데 티끌만큼도 가지지 못하리라고 전하시오. 그가 우리의 유대 어음들을 찾아내어 억지로 그 값을 토해 내게 한다 해도, 내게는 아직 마지막 수단이 하나 남아 있으니—카이사르께 바치는 증여 증서가 그것이오—이만큼은, 오, 이집트여, 내가 위대한 수도의 안마당에서 알아낸 것이라오. 내 도전과 더불어 말로 된 저주는 보내지 않으나, 사그라지지 않는 내 증오를 더 잘 드러내는 표시로, 그에게 온갖 저주의 총합임을 증명해 줄 사람 하나를 보낸다고 전하시오. 그리고 발타사르의 따님이여, 당신이 이 전갈을 되풀이하는 것을 그가 바라볼 때, 그 로마인의 약삭빠름이 내 말뜻을 모조리 그에게 일러 줄 것이오. 이제 가시오—나도 가리다.\""]], [["He conducted her to the door, and, with ceremonious politeness, held back the curtain while she passed out.", "그는 그녀를 문까지 바래다주었고, 격식을 갖춘 정중함으로 그녀가 나갈 때까지 휘장을 걷어 잡고 있었다."]], [["“Peace to you,” he said, as she disappeared.", "\"그대에게 평안이 있기를.\" 그녀가 사라질 때, 그가 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["When Ben-Hur left the guest-chamber, there was not nearly so much life in his action as when he entered it; his steps were slower, and he went along with his head quite upon his breast.", "벤허가 객실을 나설 때, 그의 거동에는 들어갈 때만큼의 활기가 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 걸음은 느려졌고, 고개는 가슴께까지 푹 떨군 채였다."], ["Having made discovery that a man with a broken back may yet have a sound brain, he was reflecting upon the discovery.", "등이 부러진 사람이라도 멀쩡한 머리는 지닐 수 있음을 깨달은 그는, 그 깨달음을 곱씹고 있었다."]], [["Forasmuch as it is easy after a calamity has befallen to look back and see the proofs of its coming strewn along the way, the thought that he had not even suspected the Egyptian as in Messala’s interest, but had gone blindly on through whole years putting himself and his friends more and more at her mercy, was a sore wound to the young man’s vanity.", "재앙이 닥친 뒤에 돌아보면 그것이 다가오던 흔적이 길 위에 흩뿌려져 있었음을 알아채기란 쉬운 법이다. 그러나 그는 이집트 여인이 메살라의 편이라는 것을 의심조차 하지 않았고, 여러 해 동안 눈먼 채로 나아가며 자기 자신과 벗들을 점점 더 그녀의 손아귀에 내맡겨 왔으니, 그 생각은 이 젊은이의 자존심에 아픈 상처가 되었다. \"이제 와 떠올려 보니,\""], ["“I remember,” he said to himself, “she had no word of indignation for the perfidious Roman at the Fountain of Castalia!", "그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"그녀는 카스탈리아의 샘에서 그 신의 없는 로마인을 두고 한마디 분노도 내비치지 않았다!"], ["I remember she extolled him at the boat-ride on the lake in the Orchard of Palms!", "떠올려 보니, 종려나무 정원의 호수에서 뱃놀이를 할 때 그녀는 오히려 그를 추켜세웠다!"], ["And, ah!”—he stopped, and beat his left hand violently with his right—“ah!", "그리고, 아!\" 그는 걸음을 멈추고 오른손으로 왼손을 사납게 내리쳤다. \"아!"], ["that mystery about the appointment she made with me at the Palace of Idernee is no mystery now!”", "이데르네 궁전에서 나와 약속을 잡은 그 수수께끼도 이제는 더 이상 수수께끼가 아니다!\""]], [["The wound, it should be observed, was to his vanity; and fortunately it is not often that people die of such hurts, or even continue a long time sick.", "여기서 눈여겨봐야 할 것은, 그 상처가 자존심에 난 것이라는 사실이다. 다행히도 사람이 그런 상처로 죽는 일은 드물고, 오래도록 앓아눕는 일조차 흔하지 않다."], ["In Ben-Hur’s case, moreover, there was a compensation; for presently he exclaimed aloud, “Praised be the Lord God that the woman took not a more lasting hold on me!", "더욱이 벤허의 경우에는 보상이 따랐으니, 그가 곧 소리 내어 외쳤기 때문이다. \"주 하느님께 찬미를 드리노라, 그 여인이 나를 더 깊이 사로잡지 못한 것을!"], ["I see I did not love her.”", "나는 그녀를 사랑하지 않았음을 알겠다.\""]], [["Then, as if he had already parted with not a little of the weight on his mind, he stepped forward more lightly; and, coming to the place on the terrace where one stairway led down to the court-yard below, and another ascended to the roof, he took the latter and began to climb.", "그러더니, 마음을 짓누르던 무게를 적잖이 덜어 낸 듯, 그의 발걸음은 한결 가벼워졌다. 테라스에서 한쪽 계단은 아래 안뜰로 내려가고 다른 한쪽은 지붕으로 올라가는 곳에 이르자, 그는 후자를 택해 오르기 시작했다."], ["As he made the last step in the flight he stopped again.", "그러다 마지막 계단을 밟으며 그는 다시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Can Balthasar have been her partner in the long mask she has been playing? No, no.", "\"발타사르가 그녀가 그토록 오래 써 온 가면극의 동반자였을 수 있을까? 아니, 아니다."], ["Hypocrisy seldom goes with wrinkled age like that.", "위선은 그렇게 주름진 노년과는 좀처럼 어울리지 않는다."], ["Balthasar is a good man.”", "발타사르는 선한 사람이다.\""]], [["With this decided opinion he stepped upon the roof.", "이렇게 마음을 정하고 그는 지붕 위로 올라섰다."], ["There was a full moon overhead, yet the vault of the sky at the moment was lurid with light cast up from the fires burning in the streets and open places of the city, and the chanting and chorusing of the old psalmody of Israel filled it with plaintive harmonies to which he could not but listen.", "머리 위에는 보름달이 떠 있었으나, 바로 그 순간 하늘의 둥근 천장은 도시의 거리와 빈터에서 타오르는 불빛이 솟아올라 불그레하게 물들어 있었고, 이스라엘의 오래된 시편을 노래하고 합창하는 소리가 구슬픈 가락으로 그 하늘을 가득 채우니, 그는 귀 기울이지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The countless voices bearing the burden seemed to say, “Thus, O son of Judah, we prove our worshipfulness of the Lord God, and our loyalty to the land he gave us.", "그 가락을 떠받치는 수없이 많은 목소리들은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"유다의 아들이여, 이렇게 우리는 주 하느님을 향한 우리의 경배와, 그분이 주신 이 땅에 대한 우리의 충성을 증명하나이다."], ["Let a Gideon appear, or a David, or a Maccabaeus, and we are ready.”", "기드온이 나타나든, 다윗이 나타나든, 마카베오가 나타나든, 우리는 준비되어 있나이다.\""]], [["That seemed an introduction; for next he saw the man of Nazareth.", "그것은 서막처럼 들렸으니, 다음 순간 그는 나사렛 사람을 떠올렸다."]], [["In certain moods the mind is disposed to mock itself with inapposite fancies.", "어떤 기분에 잠겨 있을 때면 마음은 어울리지 않는 환상으로 스스로를 희롱하기도 한다."]], [["The tearful woman-like face of the Christ stayed with him while he crossed the roof to the parapet above the street on the north side of the house, and there was in it no sign of war; but rather as the heavens of calm evenings look peace upon everything, so it looked, provoking the old question, What manner of man is he?", "집의 북쪽, 거리 위로 솟은 난간을 향해 지붕을 가로질러 가는 동안, 눈물 어린 여인 같은 그리스도의 얼굴이 그에게서 떠나지 않았다. 그 얼굴에는 싸움의 기색이라곤 없었다. 오히려 고요한 저녁의 하늘이 만물에 평화를 비추듯, 그 얼굴이 그렇게 보였으니, 해묵은 물음을 다시 불러일으켰다. 저 사람은 대체 어떤 부류의 사람인가?"]], [["Ben-Hur permitted himself one glance over the parapet, then turned and walked mechanically towards the summer-house.", "벤허는 난간 너머로 한 번 눈길을 던지더니, 몸을 돌려 정자를 향해 무심히 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“Let them do their worst,”", "\"마음껏 해 보라지.\""], ["he said, as he went slowly on.", "그는 천천히 걸으며 말했다."], ["“I will not forgive the Roman.", "\"나는 그 로마인을 용서하지 않으리라."], ["I will not divide my fortune with him, nor will I fly from this city of my fathers.", "그와 내 재산을 나누지도 않을 것이며, 조상들의 이 도시에서 달아나지도 않으리라."], ["I will call on Galilee first, and here make the fight.", "먼저 갈릴리에 호소하여 이곳에서 싸움을 벌이리라."], ["By brave deeds I will bring the tribes to our side.", "용감한 행동으로 여러 지파를 우리 편으로 끌어오리라."], ["He who raised up Moses will find us a leader, if I fail.", "모세를 일으키신 분께서, 내가 실패한다면 우리에게 또 다른 지도자를 보내 주시리라."], ["If not the Nazarene, then some other of the many ready to die for freedom.”", "나사렛 사람이 아니라면, 자유를 위해 죽기를 마다하지 않는 수많은 이들 가운데 누군가가 나타나리라.\""]], [["The interior of the summer-house, when Ben-Hur, slow sauntering, came to it, was murkily lighted.", "벤허가 느릿느릿 거닐다 정자에 이르렀을 때, 그 안은 어슴푸레하게 밝혀져 있었다."], ["The faintest of shadows lay along the floor from the pillars on the north and west sides.", "북쪽과 서쪽 기둥들이 던지는 희미하기 짝이 없는 그림자가 바닥을 따라 누워 있었다."], ["Looking in, he saw the arm-chair usually occupied by Simonides drawn to a spot from which a view of the city over towards the Market-place could be best had.", "안을 들여다보니, 평소 시모니데스가 차지하던 안락의자가 시장 쪽으로 도시를 가장 잘 내려다볼 수 있는 자리로 끌어당겨져 있었다."]], [["“The good man is returned.", "\"그 선한 어른이 돌아오셨군."], ["I will speak with him, unless he be asleep.”", "잠들지 않으셨다면 말씀을 나눠야겠다.\""]], [["He walked in, and with a quiet step approached the chair.", "그는 안으로 들어가 조용한 걸음으로 의자에 다가갔다."], ["Peering over the high back, he beheld Esther nestled in the seat asleep—a small figure snugged away under her father’s lap-robe.", "높은 등받이 너머로 들여다보니, 에스더가 잠든 채 의자에 파묻혀 있었다. 아버지의 무릎담요 아래 포근히 몸을 웅크린 작은 모습이었다."], ["The hair dishevelled fell over her face.", "헝클어진 머리카락이 얼굴 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["Her breathing was low and irregular.", "그녀의 숨결은 낮고 고르지 않았다."], ["Once it was broken by a long sigh, ending in a sob.", "한번은 긴 한숨에 끊어졌다가 흐느낌으로 끝나기도 했다."], ["Something—it might have been the sigh or the loneliness in which he found her—imparted to him the idea that the sleep was a rest from sorrow rather than fatigue.", "무언가가—그 한숨이었는지, 그녀가 홀로 있는 그 외로움이었는지—그에게 그 잠이 고단함에서 오는 휴식이라기보다 슬픔에서 벗어난 쉼이라는 생각을 일깨웠다."], ["Nature kindly sends such relief to children, and he was used to thinking Esther scarcely more than a child.", "자연은 이런 위안을 아이들에게 너그러이 베푸는 법이고, 그는 에스더를 아이나 다름없다고 늘 여겨 왔다."], ["He put his arms upon the back of the chair, and thought.", "그는 의자 등받이에 두 팔을 얹고 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“I will not wake her.", "\"깨우지 말아야겠다."], ["I have nothing to tell her—nothing unless—unless it be my love....", "그녀에게 들려줄 이야기가 없으니—없다, 만약 그것이—만약 그것이 내 사랑이 아니라면……"], ["She is a daughter of Judah, and beautiful, and so unlike the Egyptian; for there it is all vanity, here all truth; there ambition, here duty; there selfishness, here self-sacrifice....", "그녀는 유다의 딸이고, 아름다우며, 그 이집트 여인과는 어찌나 다른가. 거기에는 온통 허영뿐이지만, 여기에는 온통 진실이 있다. 거기에는 야심이, 여기에는 책무가 있다."], ["Nay, the question is not do I love her, but does she love me? She was my friend from the beginning.", "거기에는 이기심이, 여기에는 자기희생이 있다…… 아니, 물음은 내가 그녀를 사랑하느냐가 아니라, 그녀가 나를 사랑하느냐다."], ["The night on the terrace at Antioch, how child-like she begged me not to make Rome my enemy, and had me tell her of the villa by Misenum, and of the life there!", "그녀는 처음부터 내 벗이었다. 안티오크의 테라스에서 보낸 그날 밤, 그녀는 얼마나 아이처럼 로마를 적으로 삼지 말라고 내게 간청하며, 미세눔의 별장과 그곳의 삶에 대해 들려 달라고 졸랐던가!"], ["That she should not see I saw her cunning drift I kissed her.", "나는 그녀의 그 영리한 속내를 알아챘다는 것을 들키지 않으려고 입을 맞추었다."], ["Can she have forgotten the kiss!", "그녀가 그 입맞춤을 잊었을 리 있을까!"], ["I have not.", "나는 잊지 않았다."], ["I love her.... They do not know in the city that I have back my people.", "나는 그녀를 사랑한다…… 도시 사람들은 내가 가족을 되찾았다는 것을 모른다."], ["I shrank from telling it to the Egyptian; but this little one will rejoice with me over their restoration, and welcome them with love and sweet services of hand and heart.", "그것을 그 이집트 여인에게 말하기는 꺼려졌다. 그러나 이 어린 사람은 그들이 돌아온 것을 나와 함께 기뻐할 것이고, 사랑과 손과 마음의 다정한 보살핌으로 그들을 맞이할 것이다."], ["She will be to my mother another daughter; in Tirzah she will find her other self.", "그녀는 내 어머니에게 또 한 명의 딸이 되어 줄 것이고, 티르자는 그녀에게서 또 하나의 자기 자신을 찾으리라."], ["I would wake her and tell her these things, but—out on the sorceress of Egypt!", "그녀를 깨워 이 이야기들을 들려주고 싶지만—그 이집트의 마녀는 꺼지라지!"], ["Of that folly I could not command myself to speak.", "그 어리석은 일에 대해서는 도무지 입을 뗄 마음이 들지 않는구나."], ["I will go away, and wait another and a better time.", "나는 물러가, 더 나은 다른 때를 기다리리라."], ["I will wait.", "기다리겠다."], ["Fair Esther, dutiful child, daughter of Judah!”", "아리따운 에스더, 효성스러운 아이, 유다의 딸이여!\""]], [["He retired silently as he came.", "그는 들어올 때처럼 소리 없이 물러갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# CHAPTER VIII"]], [["The streets were full of people going and coming, or grouped about the fires roasting meat, and feasting and singing, and happy.", "거리는 오가는 사람들로 가득했고, 더러는 불가에 모여 고기를 굽고, 잔치를 벌이고, 노래하며 즐거워하고 있었다."], ["The odor of scorching flesh mixed with the odor of cedar-wood aflame and smoking loaded the air; and as this was the occasion when every son of Israel was full brother to every other son of Israel, and hospitality was without bounds, Ben-Hur was saluted at every step, while the groups by the fires insisted, “Stay and partake with us.", "살이 타는 냄새가 향나무 장작이 타며 피우는 연기 냄새와 뒤섞여 공기를 채웠다. 이날만큼은 이스라엘의 모든 아들이 다른 모든 이스라엘의 아들과 한 형제가 되고, 환대에는 끝이 없었으므로, 벤허는 걸음을 옮길 때마다 인사를 받았고, 불가에 모인 사람들은 한사코 권했다. \"머물러 우리와 함께 드시오."], ["We are brethren in the love of the Lord.”", "우리는 주님의 사랑 안에서 형제이외다.\""], ["But with thanks to them he hurried on, intending to take horse at the khan and return to the tents on the Cedron.", "그러나 그는 고맙다는 말을 남기고 발걸음을 재촉했으니, 객사에서 말을 타고 케드론 가의 천막으로 돌아갈 작정이었다."]], [["To make the place, it was necessary for him to cross the thoroughfare so soon to receive sorrowful Christian perpetuation.", "그곳에 이르자면, 머지않아 그리스도교에 슬픈 기념으로 남게 될 거리를 가로질러야 했다."], ["There also the pious celebration was at its height.", "그곳에서도 경건한 축제가 절정에 이르러 있었다."], ["Looking up the street, he noticed the flames of torches in motion streaming out like pennons; then he observed that the singing ceased where the torches came.", "거리 위쪽을 바라보던 그는 횃불의 불길이 깃발처럼 펄럭이며 흘러나오는 것을 알아차렸다. 이어 횃불이 닿는 곳마다 노래가 그친다는 것을 보았다."], ["His wonder rose to its highest, however, when he became certain that amidst the smoke and dancing sparks he saw the keener sparkling of burnished spear-tips, arguing the presence of Roman soldiers.", "그러나 그의 놀라움이 절정에 달한 것은, 연기와 춤추는 불티 속에서 잘 닦인 창끝이 더 날카롭게 번득이는 것을 분명히 보았을 때였으니, 그것은 로마 병사들이 있다는 증거였다."], ["What were they, the scoffing legionaries, doing in a Jewish religious procession?", "저 비웃기 좋아하는 군단병들이 유대인의 종교 행렬 속에서 무엇을 하고 있단 말인가?"], ["The circumstance was unheard of, and he stayed to see the meaning of it.", "일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 일이라, 그는 그 까닭을 알아보려고 멈춰 섰다."]], [["The moon was shining its best; yet, as if the moon and the torches, and the fires in the street, and the rays streaming from windows and open doors were not enough to make the way clear, some of the processionists carried lighted lanterns; and fancying he discovered a special purpose in the use of such equipments, Ben-Hur stepped into the street so close to the line of march as to bring every one of the company under view while passing.", "달이 한껏 밝게 빛나고 있었다. 그런데도 달과 횃불과 거리의 불, 창과 열린 문에서 흘러나오는 빛만으로는 길을 밝히기에 모자라기라도 한 듯, 행렬에 낀 몇몇은 불 켠 등을 들고 있었다. 그런 장비를 쓰는 데 무언가 특별한 목적이 있으리라 짐작한 벤허는, 지나가는 일행을 하나하나 살펴볼 수 있을 만큼 행렬의 줄에 바싹 붙어 거리로 들어섰다."], ["The torches and the lanterns were being borne by servants, each of whom was armed with a bludgeon or a sharpened stave.", "횃불과 등은 하인들이 들고 있었고, 그들은 저마다 몽둥이나 끝을 뾰족하게 깎은 막대로 무장하고 있었다."], ["Their present duty seemed to be to pick out the smoothest paths among the rocks in the street for certain dignitaries among them—elders and priests; rabbis with long beards, heavy brows, and beaked noses; men of the class potential in the councils of Caiaphas and Hannas.", "그들의 지금 임무는 일행 가운데 몇몇 높은 이들—장로와 제사장들, 긴 수염에 굵은 눈썹과 매부리코를 한 랍비들, 가야바와 한나스의 의회에서 세력을 떨치는 부류의 사람들—을 위해 거리의 바위 사이로 가장 평탄한 길을 골라 주는 것인 듯했다."], ["Where could they be going?", "그들은 어디로 가는 것일까?"], ["Not to the Temple, certainly, for the route to the sacred house from Zion, whence these appeared to be coming, was by the Xystus. And their business—if peaceful, why the soldiers?", "성전은 분명 아니었으니, 이들이 떠나온 듯한 시온에서 성전으로 가는 길은 크시스투스를 거치기 때문이다. 그리고 그들의 볼일이—평화로운 것이라면, 어찌하여 병사들이 있단 말인가?"]], [["As the procession began to go by Ben-Hur, his attention was particularly called to three persons walking together.", "행렬이 벤허 앞을 지나가기 시작할 때, 함께 걷는 세 사람에게 특히 그의 눈길이 쏠렸다."], ["They were well towards the front, and the servants who went before them with lanterns appeared unusually careful in the service.", "그들은 앞쪽에 가까이 서 있었고, 등을 들고 그들 앞에 선 하인들은 시중을 드는 데 유난히 조심스러워 보였다."], ["In the person moving on the left of this group he recognized a chief policeman of the Temple; the one on the right was a priest; the middle man was not at first so easily placed, as he walked leaning heavily upon the arms of the others, and carried his head so low upon his breast as to hide his face.", "이 무리의 왼편에서 움직이는 사람에게서 그는 성전의 경비 책임자를 알아보았다. 오른편 사람은 제사장이었다. 가운데 사람은 처음에는 그리 쉽게 가려낼 수 없었으니, 그는 양옆 사람의 팔에 무겁게 기대어 걸으며, 얼굴을 감추려는 듯 머리를 가슴께로 푹 숙이고 있었다."], ["His appearance was that of a prisoner not yet recovered from the fright of arrest, or being taken to something dreadful—to torture or death.", "그 모습은 체포의 공포에서 아직 헤어나지 못한 죄수, 혹은 무언가 끔찍한 것—고문이나 죽음—으로 끌려가는 자의 것이었다."], ["The dignitaries helping him on the right and left, and the attention they gave him, made it clear that if he were not himself the object moving the party, he was at least in some way connected with the object—a witness or a guide, possibly an informer.", "좌우에서 그를 부축하는 높은 이들과 그에게 쏟는 관심으로 미루어, 설령 그 자신이 이 무리를 움직이게 한 목표가 아니더라도, 적어도 그 목표와 어떤 식으로든 얽혀 있음이—증인이거나 길잡이, 어쩌면 밀고자임이—분명했다."], ["So if it could be found who he was the business in hand might be shrewdly guessed.", "그러니 그가 누구인지만 알아낸다면 지금 벌어지는 일을 영리하게 짐작할 수 있을 터였다."], ["With great assurance, Ben-Hur fell in on the right of the priest, and walked along with him. Now if the man would lift his head!", "벤허는 한껏 대담하게 제사장의 오른편에 끼어들어 그와 나란히 걸었다. 이제 저 사람이 머리를 들어 주기만 한다면!"], ["And presently he did so, letting the light of the lanterns strike full in his face, pale, dazed, pinched with dread; the beard roughed; the eyes filmy, sunken, and despairing.", "이윽고 그가 머리를 들어, 등불의 빛이 그의 얼굴에 정면으로 비치게 했다. 창백하고 멍하니 두려움에 일그러진 얼굴, 헝클어진 수염, 흐릿하고 움푹 들어가 절망에 잠긴 두 눈이었다."], ["In much going about following the Nazarene, Ben-Hur had come to know his disciples as well as the Master; and now, at sight of the dismal countenance, he cried out,", "나사렛 사람을 따라 두루 다니는 동안 벤허는 그 스승만큼이나 제자들도 알게 되었던 터라, 그 침울한 얼굴을 보자 그는 외쳤다."]], [["“The ’Scariot!”", "\"가룟 사람이로구나!\""]], [["Slowly the head of the man turned until his eyes settled upon Ben-Hur, and his lips moved as if he were about to speak; but the priest interfered.", "천천히 그 사람의 머리가 돌아가더니, 그 두 눈이 벤허에게 머물렀고, 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 입술이 움직였다. 그러나 제사장이 가로막았다."]], [["“Who art thou? Begone!”", "\"너는 누구냐? 썩 물러가라!\""], ["he said to Ben-Hur, pushing him away.", "제사장은 그를 밀쳐 내며 벤허에게 말했다."]], [["The young man took the push good-naturedly, and, waiting an opportunity, fell into the procession again.", "젊은이는 그 밀침을 너그럽게 받아넘기고, 기회를 엿보다가 다시 행렬에 끼어들었다."], ["Thus he was carried passively along down the street, through the crowded lowlands between the hill Bezetha and the Castle of Antonia, and on by the Bethesda reservoir to the Sheep Gate.", "그렇게 그는 거리를 따라 떠밀리듯 끌려 내려가, 베세다 언덕과 안토니아 요새 사이에 붐비는 저지대를 지나, 베데스다 저수지를 거쳐 양 문에 이르렀다."], ["There were people everywhere, and everywhere the people were engaged in sacred observances.", "어디에나 사람들이 있었고, 어디에서나 사람들은 거룩한 의식에 몰두해 있었다."]], [["It being Passover night, the valves of the Gate stood open.", "유월절 밤이었으므로, 성문의 두 짝은 열려 있었다."], ["The keepers were off somewhere feasting.", "문지기들은 어디론가 잔치를 즐기러 가고 없었다."], ["In front of the procession as it passed out unchallenged was the deep gorge of the Cedron, with Olivet beyond, its dressing of cedar and olive trees darker of the moonlight silvering all the heavens.", "행렬이 아무런 제지도 받지 않고 빠져나갈 때, 그 앞에는 케드론의 깊은 골짜기가 펼쳐졌고, 그 너머로는 올리벳이 솟아 있었다. 향나무와 올리브나무로 단장한 그 산은, 온 하늘을 은빛으로 물들이는 달빛 속에서 더욱 어둡게 보였다."], ["Two roads met and merged into the street at the gate—one from the northeast, the other from Bethany.", "성문에서 두 길이 만나 거리로 합쳐졌으니, 하나는 북동쪽에서, 다른 하나는 베다니에서 오는 길이었다."], ["Ere Ben-Hur could finish wondering whether he were to go farther, and if so, which road was to be taken, he was led off down into the gorge.", "더 멀리 가야 하는지, 만일 그렇다면 어느 길로 가야 하는지 벤허가 미처 다 헤아리기도 전에, 그는 골짜기 아래로 이끌려 내려갔다."], ["And still no hint of the purpose of the midnight march.", "그런데도 이 한밤중 행군의 목적에 대해서는 여전히 아무런 실마리도 없었다."]], [["Down the gorge and over the bridge at the bottom of it.", "골짜기를 따라 내려가, 그 바닥의 다리를 건넜다."], ["There was a great clatter on the floor as the crowd, now a straggling rabble, passed over beating and pounding with their clubs and staves.", "이제 흩어진 군중이 된 무리가 몽둥이와 막대로 두드리고 짓찧으며 건너가니, 다리 바닥에서 요란한 소리가 났다."], ["A little farther, and they turned off to the left in the direction of an olive orchard enclosed by a stone wall in view from the road.", "조금 더 가서 그들은 길에서 보이는, 돌담으로 둘러싸인 올리브 과수원 쪽으로 왼편으로 꺾어 들었다."], ["Ben-Hur knew there was nothing in the place but old gnarled trees, the grass, and a trough hewn out of a rock for the treading of oil after the fashion of the country.", "벤허는 그곳에 늙고 옹이진 나무들과 풀, 그리고 그 고장의 방식대로 기름을 짜기 위해 바위를 파서 만든 통 말고는 아무것도 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["While, yet more wonder-struck, he was thinking what could bring such a company at such an hour to a quarter so lonesome, they were all brought to a standstill.", "무엇이 이런 무리를 이런 시각에 이토록 적막한 곳으로 끌고 왔을까 하고 더욱 놀라 생각에 잠겨 있는데, 일행이 모두 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Voices called out excitedly in front; a chill sensation ran from man to man; there was a rapid falling-back, and a blind stumbling over each other.", "앞쪽에서 사람들이 흥분하여 외치는 소리가 들렸다. 오싹한 느낌이 이 사람에게서 저 사람에게로 번졌다. 황급히 물러서며 서로 부딪쳐 비틀거렸다."], ["The soldiers alone kept their order.", "오직 병사들만이 대오를 지켰다."]], [["It took Ben-Hur but a moment to disengage himself from the mob and run forward.", "벤허가 무리에서 빠져나와 앞으로 달려 나가는 데에는 한순간이면 충분했다."], ["There he found a gateway without a gate admitting to the orchard, and he halted to take in the scene.", "그곳에서 그는 문짝 없는 문간이 과수원으로 통하는 것을 발견하고, 광경을 가늠하려고 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["A man in white clothes, and bareheaded, was standing outside the entrance, his hands crossed before him—a slender, stooping figure, with long hair and thin face—in an attitude of resignation and waiting.", "흰옷을 입고 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않은 한 사람이 입구 밖에 서서 두 손을 앞으로 모으고 있었다. 호리호리하고 구부정한 몸매에, 긴 머리와 야윈 얼굴을 한, 체념과 기다림의 자세였다."]], [["It was the Nazarene!", "나사렛 사람이었다!"]], [["Behind him, next the gateway, were the disciples in a group; they were excited, but no man was ever calmer than he.", "그의 뒤, 문간 가까이에는 제자들이 무리 지어 있었다. 그들은 흥분해 있었으나, 그보다 더 차분한 사람은 일찍이 없었다."], ["The torchlight beat redly upon him, giving his hair a tint ruddier than was natural to it; yet the expression of the countenance was as usual all gentleness and pity.", "횃불의 빛이 그에게 붉게 쏟아져, 그의 머리카락에 본래의 빛보다 더 불그스름한 빛을 입혔다. 그러나 그 얼굴의 표정은 여느 때와 다름없이 온통 온화함과 연민이었다."]], [["Opposite this most unmartial figure stood the rabble, gaping, silent, awed, cowering—ready at a sign of anger from him to break and run.", "이 더없이 비전투적인 모습 맞은편에는 군중이 입을 벌린 채 말없이, 경외에 잠겨 움츠리며 서 있었으니, 그에게서 노여움의 기색이라도 비치면 곧 흩어져 달아날 채비였다."], ["And from him to them—then at Judas, conspicuous in their midst—Ben-Hur looked—one quick glance, and the object of the visit lay open to his understanding.", "그에게서 그들에게로—이어 그들 한가운데 두드러져 보이는 유다에게로—벤허는 눈길을 돌렸다. 한 번의 재빠른 일별에, 이 방문의 목적이 그의 이해 앞에 환히 드러났다."], ["Here was the betrayer, there the betrayed; and these with clubs and staves, and the legionaries, were brought to take him.", "여기 배신자가 있고, 저기 배신당한 이가 있었다. 그리고 몽둥이와 막대를 든 이자들과 군단병들은 그를 잡으러 온 것이었다."]], [["A man may not always tell what he will do until the trial is upon him.", "사람은 시련이 닥치기 전까지는 자기가 무엇을 할지 늘 알지는 못한다."], ["This was the emergency for which Ben-Hur had been for years preparing.", "이것이야말로 벤허가 오랜 세월 대비해 온 바로 그 위급한 순간이었다."], ["The man to whose security he had devoted himself, and upon whose life he had been building so largely, was in personal peril; yet he stood still.", "자신이 그 안전을 위해 헌신해 왔고, 그 목숨 위에 그토록 많은 것을 쌓아 올려 온 그 사람이 몸소 위험에 처했건만, 그는 가만히 서 있었다."], ["Such contradictions are there in human nature!", "사람의 본성에는 이러한 모순이 있는 것이다!"], ["To say truth, O reader, he was not entirely recovered from the picture of the Christ before the Gate Beautiful as it had been given by the Egyptian; and, besides that, the very calmness with which the mysterious person confronted the mob held him in restraint by suggesting the possession of a power in reserve more than sufficient for the peril.", "사실대로 말하자면, 오 독자여, 그는 이집트 여인이 들려준, 아름다운 문 앞에 선 그리스도의 그림에서 아직 완전히 헤어나지 못한 상태였다. 게다가 그 신비로운 인물이 군중을 마주하는 그 침착함 자체가, 이 위험을 감당하고도 남을 힘을 비축하고 있음을 시사함으로써 그를 자제하게 했다."], ["Peace and good-will, and love and non-resistance, had been the burden of the Nazarene’s teaching; would he put his preaching into practice?", "평화와 선의, 사랑과 무저항이 나사렛 사람이 가르친 핵심이었다. 그는 자신의 설교를 실천에 옮길 것인가? 그는 생명의 주인이었다."], ["He was master of life; he could restore it when lost; he could take it at pleasure.", "그는 잃어버린 생명을 되살릴 수 있었고, 마음대로 거두어들일 수도 있었다."], ["What use would he make of the power now?", "이제 그 힘을 어떻게 쓸 것인가? 자신을 지킬 것인가?"], ["Defend himself?", "그러면 어떻게?"], ["And how? A word—a breath—a thought were sufficient.", "한마디 말로—한 번의 숨으로—한 가닥 생각만으로도 충분했다."], ["That there would be some signal exhibition of astonishing force beyond the natural Ben-Hur believed, and in that faith waited.", "자연을 뛰어넘는 놀라운 힘의 신호가 어떤 식으로든 나타나리라고 벤허는 믿었고, 그 믿음 속에서 기다렸다."], ["And in all this he was still measuring the Nazarene by himself—by the human standard.", "그리고 이 모든 동안에도 그는 여전히 나사렛 사람을 자기 자신의 잣대로—인간의 기준으로 재고 있었다."]], [["Presently the clear voice of the Christ arose.", "이윽고 그리스도의 맑은 목소리가 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["“Whom seek ye?”", "\"너희가 누구를 찾느냐?\""]], [["“Jesus of Nazareth,”", "\"나사렛 예수요.\""], ["the priest replied.", "제사장이 대답했다."]], [["“I am he.”", "\"내가 그로다.\""]], [["At these simplest of words, spoken without passion or alarm, the assailants fell back several steps, the timid among them cowering to the ground; and they might have let him alone and gone away had not Judas walked over to him.", "격정도 놀람도 없이 내뱉은 이 더없이 단순한 말에, 습격자들은 몇 걸음 물러섰고, 그중 겁 많은 자들은 땅바닥에 움츠렸다. 유다가 그에게로 걸어가지만 않았더라면, 그들은 그를 내버려 두고 떠나갔을지도 모른다."]], [["“Hail, master!”", "\"안녕하시옵니까, 선생님!\""]], [["With this friendly speech, he kissed him.", "이 다정한 인사와 함께 그는 그에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Judas,”", "\"유다야,\""], ["said the Nazarene, mildly, “betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss?", "나사렛 사람이 부드럽게 말했다. \"네가 입맞춤으로 인자를 파느냐?"], ["Wherefore art thou come?”", "무엇 하러 왔느냐?\""]], [["Receiving no reply, the Master spoke to the crowd again.", "대답이 없자, 스승은 다시 군중에게 말했다. \"너희가 누구를 찾느냐?\""]], [["“Whom seek ye?”", "\"나사렛 예수요.\""]], [["“Jesus of Nazareth.” “I have told you that I am he.", "\"내가 그라고 너희에게 일렀느니라."], ["If, therefore, you seek me, let these go their way.”", "그러니 너희가 나를 찾거든, 이 사람들은 제 갈 길로 가게 두라.\""]], [["At these words of entreaty the rabbis advanced upon him; and, seeing their intent, some of the disciples for whom he interceded drew nearer; one of them cut off a man’s ear, but without saving the Master from being taken.", "이 간청의 말에 랍비들이 그에게 다가들었다. 그 의도를 알아챈, 그가 두둔해 준 제자 몇이 더 가까이 다가들었다. 그중 하나가 한 사람의 귀를 베었으나, 그렇다고 스승이 붙잡히는 것을 막지는 못했다."], ["And yet Ben-Hur stood still!", "그런데도 벤허는 가만히 서 있었다!"], ["Nay, while the officers were making ready with their ropes the Nazarene was doing his greatest charity—not the greatest in deed, but the very greatest in illustration of his forbearance, so far surpassing that of men.", "아니, 관헌들이 밧줄을 준비하는 동안, 나사렛 사람은 그의 가장 큰 자비를 베풀고 있었다—행위로 따져 가장 큰 것이 아니라, 사람의 그것을 그토록 뛰어넘는 그의 인내를 보여 주는 데에 더없이 큰 자비였다."]], [["“Suffer ye thus far,”", "\"이것까지 참으라.\""], ["he said to the wounded man, and healed him with a touch.", "그는 다친 사람에게 이렇게 말하고, 손길 한 번으로 그를 고쳤다."]], [["Both friends and enemies were confounded—one side that he could do such a thing, the other that he would do it under the circumstances.", "벗도 적도 모두 어리둥절했으니, 한쪽은 그가 그런 일을 할 수 있다는 데에, 다른 쪽은 그가 이런 상황에서 그렇게 하려 한다는 데에 그러했다."]], [["“Surely he will not allow them to bind him!”", "\"분명 그는 저들이 자기를 결박하도록 내버려 두지는 않으리라!\""]], [["Thus thought Ben-Hur.", "벤허는 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["“Put up thy sword into the sheath; the cup which my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?”", "\"네 칼을 칼집에 도로 꽂아라. 아버지께서 내게 주신 잔을 내가 마시지 않겠느냐?\""], ["From the offending follower, the Nazarene turned to his captors.", "나사렛 사람은 잘못을 저지른 추종자에게서 자신을 사로잡으러 온 자들에게로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["“Are you come out as against a thief, with swords and staves to take me?", "\"너희가 강도를 잡으려는 것처럼 칼과 막대를 들고 나를 잡으러 나왔느냐?"], ["I was daily with you in the Temple, and you took me not; but this is your hour, and the power of darkness.”", "내가 날마다 성전에서 너희와 함께 있었으되 너희가 나를 잡지 아니하였도다. 그러나 이는 너희 때요, 어둠의 권세로다.\""]], [["The posse plucked up courage and closed about him; and when Ben-Hur looked for the faithful they were gone—not one of them remained.", "무리는 용기를 내어 그를 에워쌌다. 그리고 벤허가 충직한 이들을 찾았을 때, 그들은 사라지고 없었다—한 사람도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["The crowd about the deserted man seemed very busy with tongue, hand, and foot.", "버림받은 사람을 둘러싼 군중은 혀와 손과 발을 몹시 부산스레 놀리는 듯했다."], ["Over their heads, between the torch-sticks, through the smoke, sometimes in openings between the restless men, Ben-Hur caught momentary glimpses of the prisoner.", "그들의 머리 너머로, 횃불 막대 사이로, 연기 사이로, 때로는 들썩이는 사람들 사이의 틈으로, 벤허는 죄수의 모습을 순간순간 흘긋 보았다."], ["Never had anything struck him as so piteous, so unfriended, so forsaken!", "그토록 가엾고, 그토록 벗 없고, 그토록 버림받은 것은 일찍이 그의 마음을 친 적이 없었다!"], ["Yet, he thought, the man could have defended himself—he could have slain his enemies with a breath, but he would not.", "그러나 그는 생각했다. 저 사람은 자신을 지킬 수 있었으리라—숨 한 번으로 적들을 죽일 수 있었으련만, 그러려 하지 않았다."], ["What was the cup his father had given him to drink?", "그의 아버지가 그에게 마시라고 준 잔이란 무엇인가?"], ["And who was the father to be so obeyed?", "그리고 그토록 순종해야 할 아버지란 누구인가?"], ["Mystery upon mystery—not one, but many.", "신비 위에 신비라—하나가 아니라, 여럿이었다."]], [["Directly the mob started in return to the city, the soldiers in the lead.", "이내 무리는 병사들을 앞세우고 도성으로 돌아가기 시작했다."], ["Ben-Hur became anxious; he was not satisfied with himself.", "벤허는 마음이 불안해졌다. 그는 자신이 만족스럽지 않았다."], ["Where the torches were in the midst of the rabble he knew the Nazarene was to be found.", "무리 한가운데 횃불이 있는 곳에 나사렛 사람이 있으리라는 것을 그는 알았다."], ["Suddenly he resolved to see him again.", "문득 그는 그를 다시 보기로 결심했다."], ["He would ask him one question.", "그에게 한 가지를 물어보리라."]], [["Taking off his long outer garment and the handkerchief from his head, he threw them upon the orchard wall, and started after the posse, which he boldly joined.", "그는 긴 겉옷과 머리에 쓴 두건을 벗어 과수원 담장 위에 던져 놓고, 무리를 뒤쫓아 대담하게 그 속에 끼어들었다."], ["Through the stragglers he made way, and by littles at length reached the man who carried the ends of the rope with which the prisoner was bound.", "뒤처진 자들 사이를 헤치고, 조금씩조금씩 나아가 마침내 죄수를 묶은 밧줄 끝을 쥔 사람에게 이르렀다."]], [["The Nazarene was walking slowly, his head down, his hands bound behind him; the hair fell thickly over his face, and he stooped more than usual; apparently he was oblivious to all going on around him.", "나사렛 사람은 머리를 떨군 채 두 손을 등 뒤로 묶이고 천천히 걷고 있었다. 머리카락이 그의 얼굴 위로 수북이 흘러내렸고, 그는 여느 때보다 더 구부정했다. 그는 주위에서 벌어지는 일을 모두 잊은 듯 보였다."], ["In advance a few steps were priests and elders talking and occasionally looking back.", "몇 걸음 앞에서는 제사장들과 장로들이 이야기를 나누며 이따금 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["When, at length, they were all near the bridge in the gorge, Ben-Hur took the rope from the servant who had it, and stepped past him.", "마침내 그들이 모두 골짜기의 다리 가까이에 이르렀을 때, 벤허는 밧줄을 쥔 하인에게서 그것을 받아 들고 그를 지나쳐 갔다."]], [["“Master, master!”", "\"선생님, 선생님!\""], ["he said, hurriedly, speaking close to the Nazarene’s ear.", "그는 나사렛 사람의 귀에 바싹 대고 황급히 말했다. \"제 말이 들리십니까, 선생님?"], ["“Dost thou hear, master?", "한마디만—한마디만 하여 주십시오."], ["A word—one word. Tell me—”", "제게 말씀해 주십시오—\""]], [["The fellow from whom he had taken the rope now claimed it.", "밧줄을 그에게서 넘겨받았던 그 사내가 이제 그것을 도로 내놓으라 했다."]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말씀해 주십시오.\""], ["Ben-Hur continued, “goest thou with these of thine own accord?”", "벤허는 계속했다. \"스스로 원하셔서 이들과 함께 가시는 것입니까?\""]], [["The people were come up now, and in his own ears asking angrily, “Who art thou, man?”", "이제 사람들이 다가들어, 그의 귓전에다 성난 목소리로 물었다. \"이자야, 너는 누구냐?\""]], [["“O master,”", "\"오 선생님,\""], ["Ben-Hur made haste to say, his voice sharp with anxiety, “I am thy friend and lover.", "벤허는 불안에 날카로워진 목소리로 다급히 말했다. \"저는 당신의 벗이요 당신을 사랑하는 자입니다."], ["Tell me, I pray thee, if I bring rescue, wilt thou accept it?”", "비오니 말씀해 주십시오, 제가 구원을 가져온다면, 받아들이시겠습니까?\""]], [["The Nazarene never so much as looked up or allowed the slightest sign of recognition; yet the something which when we are suffering is always telling it to such as look at us, though they be strangers, failed not now.", "나사렛 사람은 고개를 들어 보기는커녕 알아본다는 기색을 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그러나 우리가 고통받을 때, 비록 낯선 이라 할지라도 우리를 바라보는 자에게 늘 그것을 일러 주는 그 무언가가, 이번에도 어김없이 전해졌다. \"그를 내버려 두라.\""], ["“Let him alone,”", "그것은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"그는 벗들에게 버림받았다."], ["it seemed to say; “he has been abandoned by his friends; the world has denied him; in bitterness of spirit, he has taken farewell of men; he is going he knows not where, and he cares not.", "세상은 그를 부인했다. 마음의 쓰라림 속에서 그는 사람들에게 작별을 고했다. 그는 자기가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 가고 있으며, 마음 쓰지도 않는다."], ["Let him alone.”", "그를 내버려 두라.\""]], [["And to that Ben-Hur was now driven.", "그리고 이제 벤허는 그리로 떠밀렸다."], ["A dozen hands were upon him, and from all sides there was shouting, “He is one of them.", "열 개도 넘는 손이 그를 붙들었고, 사방에서 외치는 소리가 들렸다. \"이자도 그 패거리다."], ["Bring him along; club him—kill him!”", "끌고 오라. 몽둥이로 쳐라—죽여 버려라!\""]], [["With a gust of passion which gave him many times his ordinary force, Ben-Hur raised himself, turned once about with arms outstretched, shook the hands off, and rushed through the circle which was fast hemming him in.", "벤허는 평소의 몇 배에 달하는 힘을 솟구치게 하는 격정으로 몸을 일으켜, 두 팔을 활짝 뻗고 한 번 빙 돌며 붙든 손들을 떨쳐 냈다. 그러고는 빠르게 자기를 에워싸 오는 원을 뚫고 내달렸다."], ["The hands snatching at him as he passed tore his garments from his back, so he ran off the road naked; and the gorge, in keeping of the friendly darkness, darker there than elsewhere, received him safe.", "그가 지나갈 때 그를 움켜쥐려던 손들이 등에서 옷을 찢어 냈으므로, 그는 발가벗은 채 길에서 달아났다. 그리고 다른 어느 곳보다 더 어두운, 정다운 어둠이 깃든 골짜기가 그를 무사히 받아들였다."]], [["Reclaiming his handkerchief and outer garments from the orchard wall, he followed back to the city gate; thence he went to the khan, and on the good horse rode to the tents of his people out by the Tombs of the Kings.", "그는 과수원 담장에서 두건과 겉옷을 되찾아 입고, 도성 문까지 되짚어 갔다. 거기서 객사로 가, 좋은 말을 타고 왕들의 무덤 곁에 있는 제 식구들의 천막으로 달려갔다."]], [["As he rode, he promised himself to see the Nazarene on the morrow—promised it, not knowing that the unfriended man was taken straightway to the house of Hannas to be tried that night.", "말을 달리며 그는 이튿날 나사렛 사람을 보리라고 스스로에게 다짐했다—다짐하면서도, 그 벗 없는 사람이 그날 밤 재판을 받으러 곧장 한나스의 집으로 끌려갔다는 것은 알지 못했다."]], [["The heart the young man carried to his couch beat so heavily he could not sleep; for now clearly his renewed Judean kingdom resolved itself into what it was—only a dream.", "침상에 누운 젊은이의 가슴은 어찌나 무겁게 뛰는지 잠을 이룰 수 없었다. 이제 그가 되살리려던 유대 왕국이 본래 무엇이었는지 분명해졌기 때문이다—한낱 꿈에 지나지 않았다."], ["It is bad enough to see our castles overthrown one after another with an interval between in which to recover from the shock, or at least let the echoes of the fall die away; but when they go altogether—go as ships sink, as houses tumble in earthquakes—the spirits which endure it calmly are made of stuffs sterner than common, and Ben-Hur’s was not of them.", "우리의 성채가 충격에서 회복할, 적어도 무너지는 메아리가 잦아들 만한 사이를 두고 하나씩 차례로 쓰러지는 것을 보는 일도 충분히 괴롭다. 그러나 그것들이 한꺼번에 무너질 때—배가 가라앉듯, 집이 지진에 무너지듯 무너질 때—그것을 차분히 견디는 영혼은 여느 사람보다 더 굳센 재료로 빚어진 법이거니와, 벤허의 영혼은 그런 부류가 아니었다."], ["Through vistas in the future, he began to catch glimpses of a life serenely beautiful, with a home instead of a palace of state, and Esther its mistress.", "미래로 트인 길목들 사이로, 그는 고요히 아름다운 삶을 어렴풋이 보기 시작했으니, 국정의 궁전 대신 가정이 있고, 에스더가 그 안주인인 삶이었다."], ["Again and again through the leaden-footed hours of the night he saw the villa by Misenum, and with his little countrywoman strolled through the garden, and rested in the panelled atrium; overhead the Neapolitan sky, at their feet the sunniest of sun-lands and the bluest of bays.", "납덩이처럼 더디 흐르는 밤의 시간을 지나는 동안, 그는 거듭거듭 미세눔 곁의 별장을 보았고, 어린 동향 여인과 함께 정원을 거닐었으며, 널을 댄 안뜰에서 쉬었다. 머리 위에는 나폴리의 하늘이, 발치에는 더없이 볕 좋은 땅과 더없이 푸른 만이 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["In plainest speech, he was entering upon a crisis with which to-morrow and the Nazarene will have everything to do.", "가장 쉬운 말로 하자면, 그는 어떤 위기로 접어들고 있었으니, 그 위기와는 이튿날과 나사렛 사람이 모든 것을 좌우하게 될 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# CHAPTER IX"]], [["Next morning, about the second hour, two men rode full speed to the doors of Ben-Hur’s tents, and dismounting, asked to see him.", "아침나절 두 시간쯤 되었을 때, 두 사내가 전속력으로 말을 달려 벤허의 천막 앞에 이르렀다. 그들은 말에서 내려 그를 만나게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He was not yet risen, but gave directions for their admission.", "그는 아직 일어나지 않았으나, 들이라고 일렀다."]], [["“Peace to you, brethren,”", "\"평안하시오, 형제들.\""], ["he said, for they were of his Galileans, and trusted officers.", "그가 말했다. 그들은 그의 갈릴리 사람들로, 믿음직한 부하들이었다."], ["“Will you be seated?”", "\"앉으시겠소?\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["the senior replied, bluntly, “to sit and be at ease is to let the Nazarene die.", "연장자가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다. \"이렇게 편히 앉아 있다가는 나사렛 사람이 죽고 맙니다."], ["Rise, son of Judah, and go with us. The judgment has been given.", "일어나십시오, 유다의 아들이여, 우리와 함께 가십시오. 판결이 내려졌습니다."], ["The tree of the cross is already at Golgotha.”", "십자가의 나무는 벌써 골고다에 세워졌습니다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur stared at them.", "벤허가 그들을 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“The cross!”", "\"십자가라니!\""], ["was all he could for the moment say.", "그 순간 그가 할 수 있는 말은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“They took him last night, and tried him,”", "\"어젯밤 그를 잡아다 재판에 부쳤습니다.\""], ["the man continued.", "사내가 말을 이었다."], ["“At dawn they led him before Pilate.", "\"날이 밝자 그들은 그를 빌라도 앞으로 끌고 갔습니다."], ["Twice the Roman denied his guilt; twice he refused to give him over.", "그 로마인은 두 번이나 그의 죄를 부인했고, 두 번이나 그를 넘겨주기를 거부했습니다."], ["At last he washed his hands, and said, ‘Be it upon you then;’ and they answered—”", "마침내 그는 손을 씻으며 말했습니다. '그렇다면 너희가 책임지라.' 그러자 그들이 대답했습니다—\""]], [["“Who answered?”", "\"누가 대답했단 말이오?\""]], [["“They—the priests and people—‘His blood be upon us and our children.’”", "\"그들이—제사장들과 백성이—'그 피를 우리와 우리 자손에게 돌리라'고 했습니다.\""]], [["“Holy father Abraham!”", "\"거룩하신 조상 아브라함이여!\""], ["cried Ben-Hur; “a Roman kinder to an Israelite than his own kin!", "벤허가 부르짖었다. \"로마인이 자기네 동족보다 이스라엘 사람에게 더 자비롭다니!"], ["And if—ah, if he should indeed be the son of God, what shall ever wash his blood from their children?", "그리고 만일—아, 만일 그가 정말 하나님의 아들이라면, 무엇이 그 피를 그들의 자손에게서 씻어 줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["It must not be—’tis time to fight!”", "이래서는 안 된다—이제 싸울 때다!\""]], [["His face brightened with resolution, and he clapped his hands.", "그의 얼굴이 결의로 환해졌고, 그는 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["“The horses—and quickly!”", "\"말을—빨리!\""], ["he said to the Arab who answered the signal.", "신호에 응해 나온 아랍인에게 그가 말했다."], ["“And bid Amrah send me fresh garments, and bring my sword!", "\"그리고 암라에게 깨끗한 옷을 보내고 내 검을 가져오라고 일러라!"], ["It is time to die for Israel, my friends.", "이스라엘을 위해 죽을 때가 왔소, 벗들이여."], ["Tarry without till I come.”", "내가 나올 때까지 밖에서 기다리시오.\""]], [["He ate a crust, drank a cup of wine, and was soon upon the road.", "그는 빵 한 조각을 먹고 포도주 한 잔을 마신 뒤, 곧 길에 나섰다."]], [["“Whither would you go first?”", "\"어디로 먼저 가시렵니까?\""], ["asked the Galilean.", "갈릴리 사람이 물었다."]], [["“To collect the legions.”", "\"군단을 모아야지.\""]], [["“Alas!” the man replied, throwing up his hands.", "\"아아!\" 사내가 두 손을 치켜들며 대답했다."]], [["“Why alas?”", "\"어찌 아아라 하는가?\""]], [["“Master”—the man spoke with shame—“master, I and my friend here are all that are faithful.", "\"주인님\"—사내가 부끄러운 듯 말했다—\"주인님, 저와 여기 이 벗만이 충실한 자들의 전부입니다."], ["The rest do follow the priests.”", "나머지는 제사장들을 따르고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Seeking what?”", "\"무엇을 노리고?\""], ["and Ben-Hur drew rein.", "벤허가 고삐를 당겼다."]], [["“To kill him.”", "\"그를 죽이려는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Not the Nazarene?” “You have said it.”", "\"나사렛 사람을 죽인단 말이 아니겠지?\" \"네 입으로 말했다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur looked slowly from one man to the other.", "\"바로 그를 말합니다.\" 벤허는 두 사내를 천천히 번갈아 바라보았다."], ["He was hearing again the question of the night before: “The cup my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?”", "그는 어젯밤의 그 물음을 다시 듣고 있었다. \"아버지께서 내게 주신 잔을 내가 마시지 않겠느냐?\""], ["In the ear of the Nazarene he was putting his own question, “If I bring thee rescue, wilt thou accept it?”", "그는 나사렛 사람의 귀에 자신의 물음을 던지고 있었다. \"내가 당신을 구해 드린다면, 당신은 그것을 받아들이시겠습니까?\""], ["He was saying to himself, “This death may not be averted.", "그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"이 죽음은 막을 수 없을지도 모른다."], ["The man has been travelling towards it with full knowledge from the day he began his mission: it is imposed by a will higher than his; whose but the Lord’s!", "이 사람은 사명을 시작한 날부터 모든 것을 알면서도 그 죽음을 향해 걸어왔다. 그것은 그 자신보다 높은 뜻에 의해 정해진 것이니, 주님의 뜻이 아니고 누구의 뜻이겠는가!"], ["If he is consenting, if he goes to it voluntarily, what shall another do?”", "그가 동의한다면, 그가 스스로 그리로 나아간다면, 다른 누가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?\""], ["Nor less did Ben-Hur see the failure of the scheme he had built upon the fidelity of the Galileans; their desertion, in fact, left nothing more of it.", "그러면서도 벤허는 갈릴리 사람들의 충성 위에 세웠던 계획이 무너지는 것을 똑똑히 보았다. 사실 그들의 이탈로 그 계획에는 아무것도 남지 않았다."], ["But how singular it should happen that morning of all others! A dread seized him.", "그러나 하필이면 다른 날도 아닌 바로 그날 아침에 이런 일이 일어나다니, 이 얼마나 기이한가!"], ["It was possible his scheming, and labor, and expenditure of treasure might have been but blasphemous contention with God.", "두려움이 그를 사로잡았다. 그의 책략과 노고와 재물의 소모가 어쩌면 하나님을 거스르는 불경한 다툼에 지나지 않았을지도 몰랐다."], ["When he picked up the reins and said, “Let us go, brethren,” all before him was uncertainty.", "그가 고삐를 집어 들고 \"갑시다, 형제들\" 하고 말했을 때, 그의 앞에 놓인 모든 것은 불확실했다."], ["The faculty of resolving quickly, without which one cannot be a hero in the midst of stirring scenes, was numb within him.", "들끓는 장면 한복판에서 영웅이 되려면 없어서는 안 될, 빠르게 결단하는 능력이 그의 안에서 마비되어 있었다."]], [["“Let us go, brethren; let us to Golgotha.”", "\"갑시다, 형제들. 골고다로 갑시다.\""]], [["They passed through excited crowds of people going south, like themselves.", "그들은 자신들처럼 남쪽으로 가는, 들뜬 인파를 헤치고 나아갔다."], ["All the country north of the city seemed aroused and in motion.", "도성 북쪽의 온 고장이 깨어나 움직이는 듯했다."]], [["Hearing that the procession with the condemned might be met with somewhere near the great white towers left by Herod, the three friends rode thither, passing round southeast of Akra.", "사형수를 끌고 가는 행렬을 헤롯이 남긴 거대한 흰 망루 근처 어디쯤에서 마주칠 수 있으리라는 말을 듣고, 세 벗은 아크라의 남동쪽을 돌아 그리로 말을 달렸다."], ["In the valley below the Pool of Hezekiah, passage-way against the multitude became impossible, and they were compelled to dismount, and take shelter behind the corner of a house and wait.", "히스기야 못 아래 골짜기에서는 군중을 거슬러 나아가기가 불가능해졌으므로, 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 말에서 내려 어느 집 모퉁이 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 기다려야 했다."]], [["The waiting was as if they were on a river bank, watching a flood go by, for such the people seemed.", "기다림은 마치 강둑에 서서 홍수가 지나가는 것을 지켜보는 것과도 같았으니, 사람들이 꼭 그렇게 보였던 것이다."]], [["There are certain chapters in the First Book of this story which were written to give the reader an idea of the composition of the Jewish nationality as it was in the time of Christ.", "이 이야기의 제1부에는 그리스도 시대의 유대 민족 구성에 관해 독자에게 어떤 그림을 보여 주려고 쓴 몇몇 장이 있다. 그 장들은 또한 바로 이 시각과 이 장면을 내다보며 쓴 것이기도 하다."], ["They were also written in anticipation of this hour and scene; so that he who has read them with attention can now see all Ben-Hur saw of the going to the crucifixion—a rare and wonderful sight!", "그래서 그것을 주의 깊게 읽은 사람이라면 이제 벤허가 본 십자가형으로 가는 행렬을 모두 볼 수 있을 것이니—그것은 보기 드물고 놀라운 광경이었다!"]], [["Half an hour—an hour—the flood surged by Ben-Hur and his companions, within arm’s reach, incessant, undiminished.", "반 시간—한 시간—홍수는 벤허와 그 일행 곁을, 팔이 닿을 만한 거리에서, 끊임없이, 줄어들지 않고 밀려갔다. 그 시간이 다할 무렵 그는 이렇게 말할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["At the end of that time he could have said, “I have seen all the castes of Jerusalem, all the sects of Judea, all the tribes of Israel, and all the nationalities of earth represented by them.”", "\"나는 예루살렘의 모든 계급을, 유대의 모든 분파를, 이스라엘의 모든 지파를, 그리고 그들이 대표하는 땅 위의 모든 민족을 보았다.\" 리비아의 유대인이 지나갔고, 이집트의 유대인이, 라인강 가의 유대인이 지나갔다. 한마디로 모든 동방 나라와 모든 서방 나라, 그리고 교역으로 이어진 모든 섬에서 온 유대인들이었다."], ["The Libyan Jew went by, and the Jew of Egypt, and the Jew from the Rhine; in short, Jews from all East countries and all West countries, and all islands within commercial connection; they went by on foot, on horseback, on camels, in litters and chariots, and with an infinite variety of costumes, yet with the same marvellous similitude of features which to-day particularizes the children of Israel, tried as they have been by climates and modes of life; they went by speaking all known tongues, for by that means only were they distinguishable group from group; they went by in haste—eager, anxious, crowding—all to behold one poor Nazarene die, a felon between felons.", "그들은 걸어서, 말을 타고, 낙타를 타고, 가마와 마차에 실려, 헤아릴 수 없이 다양한 의복을 걸치고 지나갔으나, 풍토와 생활 방식으로 그토록 시달려 왔으면서도 오늘날까지 이스라엘 자손을 두드러지게 하는 그 놀라운 용모의 닮음을 한결같이 지니고 있었다. 그들은 알려진 모든 언어를 말하며 지나갔으니, 오직 그 수단으로만 무리와 무리를 가려낼 수 있었던 것이다. 그들은 황급히 지나갔다—애타게, 조마조마하게, 서로 밀치며—하나같이 가련한 나사렛 사람 하나가 죄인들 틈에 끼여 죄인으로 죽는 것을 보려고."]], [["These were the many, but they were not all.", "이들이 다수였으나, 전부는 아니었다."]], [["Borne along with the stream were thousands not Jews—thousands hating and despising them—Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Syrians, Africans, Egyptians, Easterns.", "그 물결에 실려 가는 이들 가운데는 유대인이 아닌 수천 명이—그들을 미워하고 멸시하는 수천 명이—있었으니, 그리스인, 로마인, 아랍인, 시리아인, 아프리카인, 이집트인, 동방 사람들이었다."], ["So that, studying the mass, it seemed the whole world was to be represented, and, in that sense, present at the crucifixion.", "그리하여 그 무리를 살펴보면, 마치 온 세상이 거기 대표되어, 그런 의미에서 십자가형 자리에 함께한 듯이 보였다."]], [["The going was singularly quiet.", "그 행렬은 유달리 조용했다."], ["A hoof-stroke upon a rock, the glide and rattle of revolving wheels, voices in conversation, and now and then a calling voice, were all the sounds heard above the rustle of the mighty movement.", "바위에 부딪히는 말발굽 소리, 돌아가는 바퀴가 미끄러지고 덜그럭거리는 소리, 이야기하는 목소리, 그리고 이따금 외치는 소리, 이런 것들이 그 거대한 움직임의 술렁임 위로 들리는 소리의 전부였다."], ["Yet was there upon every countenance the look with which men make haste to see some dreadful sight, some sudden wreck, or ruin, or calamity of war.", "그런데도 사람들의 얼굴마다 어떤 무서운 광경을, 갑작스러운 파선이나 폐허나 전쟁의 참화 같은 것을 황급히 보러 갈 때의 그런 표정이 어려 있었다."], ["And by such signs Ben-Hur judged that these were the strangers in the city come up to the Passover, who had had no part in the trial of the Nazarene, and might be his friends.", "그런 기색으로 미루어 벤허는 이들이 유월절을 지키러 도성에 올라온 이방 사람들이며, 나사렛 사람의 재판에는 끼지 않았으니 그의 벗이 될 수도 있는 자들이라고 판단했다."]], [["At length, from the direction of the great towers, Ben-Hur heard, at first faint in the distance, a shouting of many men.", "마침내 거대한 망루 쪽에서, 벤허는 처음에는 멀리서 희미하게, 수많은 사람의 외침을 들었다."]], [["“Hark! they are coming now,”", "\"들으십시오! 이제 오고 있습니다.\""], ["said one of his friends.", "그의 벗 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["The people in the street halted to hear; but as the cry rang on over their heads, they looked at each other, and in shuddering silence moved along.", "거리의 사람들은 그 소리를 들으려고 걸음을 멈췄다. 그러나 그 외침이 머리 위로 울려 퍼지자, 그들은 서로 마주 보고는 몸서리치는 침묵 속에 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["The shouting drew nearer each moment; and the air was already full of it and trembling, when Ben-Hur saw the servants of Simonides coming with their master in his chair, and Esther walking by his side; a covered litter was next behind them.", "외침은 순간순간 가까워졌다. 어느새 대기가 그 소리로 가득 차 떨릴 무렵, 벤허는 시모니데스의 종들이 가마에 주인을 모시고 오는 것을 보았으니, 에스더가 그 곁에서 걷고 있었다. 그 바로 뒤에는 휘장을 두른 가마가 따라왔다."]], [["“Peace to you, O Simonides—and to you, Esther,”", "\"평안하시오, 시모니데스여—그대도 평안하시오, 에스더.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, meeting them.", "그들을 맞으며 벤허가 말했다."], ["“If you are for Golgotha, stay until the procession passes; I will then go with you.", "\"골고다로 가시는 길이라면, 행렬이 지나갈 때까지 기다리십시오. 그러면 함께 가겠습니다."], ["There is room to turn in by the house here.”", "여기 이 집 옆으로 들어가 돌아설 만한 자리가 있습니다.\""]], [["The merchant’s large head rested heavily upon his breast; rousing himself, he answered, “Speak to Balthasar; his pleasure will be mine.", "상인의 큰 머리가 가슴 위로 무겁게 떨구어져 있었다. 그는 몸을 추스르며 대답했다. \"발타사르에게 말해 보게."], ["He is in the litter.”", "그분의 뜻이 곧 내 뜻일세. 그분은 저 가마 안에 계시네.\""]], [["Ben-Hur hastened to draw aside the curtain.", "벤허는 서둘러 휘장을 젖혔다."], ["The Egyptian was lying within, his wan face so pinched as to appear like a dead man’s.", "이집트인이 그 안에 누워 있었는데, 핼쑥한 얼굴이 어찌나 야위었는지 죽은 사람처럼 보였다."], ["The proposal was submitted to him.", "그에게 제안이 전해졌다."]], [["“Can we see him?” he inquired, faintly.", "\"우리가 그를 볼 수 있겠소?\" 그가 가냘프게 물었다."]], [["“The Nazarene?", "\"나사렛 사람 말씀입니까?"], ["yes; he must pass within a few feet of us.”", "그렇습니다. 그는 우리에게서 몇 걸음 안 되는 곳을 지나갈 것입니다.\""]], [["“Dear Lord!” the old man cried, fervently.", "\"사랑하는 주님이시여!\" 노인이 간절하게 부르짖었다."], ["“Once more, once more! Oh, it is a dreadful day for the world!”", "\"다시 한 번, 다시 한 번! 오, 세상에 무서운 날이로다!\""]], [["Shortly the whole party were in waiting under shelter of the house.", "이내 온 일행은 그 집 그늘 아래에서 기다리게 되었다."], ["They said but little, afraid, probably, to trust their thoughts to each other; everything was uncertain, and nothing so much so as opinions.", "그들은 말을 거의 하지 않았으니, 아마도 서로에게 제 생각을 털어놓기가 두려웠으리라. 모든 것이 불확실했고, 그 무엇보다도 견해가 그러했다."], ["Balthasar drew himself feebly from the litter, and stood supported by a servant; Esther and Ben-Hur kept Simonides company.", "발타사르는 가마에서 힘없이 몸을 일으켜, 종의 부축을 받으며 섰다. 에스더와 벤허는 시모니데스 곁을 지켰다."]], [["Meantime the flood poured along, if anything, more densely than before; and the shouting came nearer, shrill up in the air, hoarse along the earth, and cruel.", "그러는 동안에도 홍수는 도리어 전보다 더 빽빽하게 밀려갔다. 외침은 더 가까워져, 공중에서는 날카롭게, 땅을 따라서는 거칠게, 잔인하게 들려왔다."], ["At last the procession was up.", "마침내 행렬이 다가왔다."]], [["“See!”", "\"보십시오!\""], ["said Ben-Hur, bitterly; “that which cometh now is Jerusalem.”", "벤허가 쓰라리게 말했다. \"이제 오는 저것이 예루살렘입니다.\""]], [["The advance was in possession of an army of boys, hooting and screaming, “The King of the Jews!", "행렬의 선두는 한 무리의 소년들이 차지하고 있었으니, 그들은 \"유대인의 왕이다! 길을 비켜라, 유대인의 왕에게 길을 비켜라!\""], ["Room, room for the King of the Jews!”", "하고 야유하며 소리쳐 댔다."]], [["Simonides watched them as they whirled and danced along, like a cloud of summer insects, and said, gravely, “When these come to their inheritance, son of Hur, alas for the city of Solomon!”", "시모니데스는 여름 벌레 떼처럼 빙빙 돌며 춤추듯 지나가는 그들을 지켜보다가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"허의 아들이여, 저 아이들이 제 유산을 물려받을 때면, 아아, 솔로몬의 도성이 가엾어질 걸세!\""]], [["A band of legionaries fully armed followed next, marching in sturdy indifference, the glory of burnished brass about them the while.", "다음으로 완전 무장한 한 떼의 군단병들이 따라왔으니, 그들은 우직한 무관심 속에 행진했고, 그동안 광나는 놋쇠의 광채가 그들을 두르고 있었다."]], [["Then came the NAZARENE!", "그러고 나서 나사렛 사람이 왔다!"]], [["He was nearly dead. Every few steps he staggered as if he would fall.", "그는 거의 죽어 가고 있었다. 몇 걸음마다 그는 쓰러질 듯 비틀거렸다."], ["A stained gown badly torn hung from his shoulders over a seamless undertunic.", "심하게 찢긴 얼룩진 겉옷이 솔기 없는 속옷 위로 어깨에서 흘러내려 있었다."], ["His bare feet left red splotches upon the stones.", "그의 맨발은 돌마다 붉은 자국을 남겼다."], ["An inscription on a board was tied to his neck.", "죄패를 적은 판자 하나가 그의 목에 매달려 있었다."], ["A crown of thorns had been crushed hard down upon his head, making cruel wounds from which streams of blood, now dry and blackened, had run over his face and neck.", "가시관이 그의 머리에 모질게 눌려 박혀 잔혹한 상처를 냈고, 거기서 흘러내린 피가 이제는 말라 검게 엉긴 채 그의 얼굴과 목을 덮고 있었다."], ["The long hair, tangled in the thorns, was clotted thick.", "가시에 엉킨 긴 머리카락은 두텁게 들러붙어 있었다."], ["The skin, where it could be seen, was ghastly white.", "살갗은 보이는 곳마다 소름 끼치도록 희었다."], ["His hands were tied before him.", "그의 두 손은 앞으로 묶여 있었다."], ["Back somewhere in the city he had fallen exhausted under the transverse beam of his cross, which, as a condemned person, custom required him to bear to the place of execution; now a countryman carried the burden in his stead.", "도성 어딘가 뒤편에서 그는 십자가의 가로 막대 아래 지쳐 쓰러졌으니, 사형수로서 관습에 따라 그것을 형장까지 메고 가야 했던 것이다. 이제는 한 시골 사람이 그를 대신해 그 짐을 지고 있었다."], ["Four soldiers went with him as a guard against the mob, who sometimes, nevertheless, broke through, and struck him with sticks, and spit upon him.", "병사 넷이 그를 따르며 군중을 막았으나, 그래도 군중은 이따금 뚫고 들어와 막대기로 그를 치고 침을 뱉었다."], ["Yet no sound escaped him, neither remonstrance nor groan; nor did he look up until he was nearly in front of the house sheltering Ben-Hur and his friends, all of whom were moved with quick compassion.", "그런데도 그에게서는 아무 소리도 새어 나오지 않았으니, 항변도 신음도 없었다. 그는 벤허와 그 벗들이 몸을 숨긴 집 거의 앞에 이르기까지 눈을 들지 않았는데, 그들은 모두 순식간에 연민에 사로잡혔다."], ["Esther clung to her father; and he, strong of will as he was, trembled.", "에스더는 아버지에게 매달렸고, 의지가 굳센 그도 떨었다."], ["Balthasar fell down speechless.", "발타사르는 말없이 주저앉았다."], ["Even Ben-Hur cried out, “O my God! my God!”", "벤허마저 \"오 나의 하나님! 나의 하나님!\""], ["Then, as if he divined their feelings or heard the exclamation, the Nazarene turned his wan face towards the party, and looked at them each one, so they carried the look in memory through life.", "하고 부르짖었다. 그러자 마치 그들의 심정을 알아챘거나 그 외침을 들은 듯이, 나사렛 사람은 핼쑥한 얼굴을 그 일행 쪽으로 돌려 그들을 하나하나 바라보았으니, 그들은 평생토록 그 눈길을 기억 속에 간직했다."], ["They could see he was thinking of them, not himself, and the dying eyes gave them the blessing he was not permitted to speak.", "그가 자신이 아니라 그들을 생각하고 있음을 그들은 볼 수 있었고, 죽어 가는 그 눈은 그가 입으로 말할 수 없는 축복을 그들에게 내려 주었다. \"그대의 군단은 어디 있는가, 허의 아들이여?\" 시모니데스가 정신을 차리고 물었다."]], [["“Where are thy legions, son of Hur?” asked Simonides, aroused.", "\"한나스가 저보다 더 잘 일러 드릴 것입니다.\""]], [["“Hannas can tell thee better than I.”", "\"무엇이라, 배신하였다는 말인가?\""]], [["“What, faithless?”", "\"이 두 사람을 빼고는 모두.\""]], [["“All but these two.” “Then all is lost, and this good man must die!”", "\"그렇다면 모든 것이 끝났구나. 그리고 이 선한 사람은 죽어야 하는구나!\""]], [["The face of the merchant knit convulsively as he spoke, and his head sank upon his breast.", "상인은 말하면서 얼굴을 발작하듯 일그러뜨렸고, 그의 머리는 가슴 위로 떨구어졌다."], ["He had borne his part in Ben-Hur’s labors well, and he had been inspired by the same hopes, now blown out never to be rekindled.", "그는 벤허의 노고에 맡은 몫을 잘 감당해 왔고, 같은 희망에 고무되어 있었으나, 그 희망은 이제 다시는 되살아날 수 없도록 꺼져 버렸다."]], [["Two other men succeeded the Nazarene bearing cross-beams.", "다른 두 사람이 가로 막대를 메고 나사렛 사람의 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Who are these?”", "\"이들은 누구요?\""], ["Ben-Hur asked of the Galileans.", "벤허가 갈릴리 사람들에게 물었다."]], [["“Thieves appointed to die with the Nazarene,”", "\"나사렛 사람과 함께 죽도록 정해진 도둑들입니다.\""], ["they replied.", "그들이 대답했다."]], [["Next in the procession stalked a mitred figure clad all in the golden vestments of the high-priest.", "행렬에서 그다음으로는 대제사장의 금빛 예복을 온전히 갖춰 입고 관을 쓴 인물이 위풍당당하게 걸어왔다."], ["Policemen from the Temple curtained him round about; and after him, in order, strode the sanhedrim, and a long array of priests, the latter in their plain white garments, overwrapped by abnets of many folds and gorgeous colors.", "성전의 경비병들이 그를 둘러싸 가렸고, 그 뒤로는 산헤드린이 차례로 성큼성큼 걸었으며, 길게 늘어선 제사장들이 따랐으니, 이들은 수수한 흰옷 위에 주름이 많고 화려한 빛깔의 띠를 두르고 있었다."]], [["“The son-in-law of Hannas,”", "\"한나스의 사위입니다.\""], ["said Ben-Hur, in a low voice.", "벤허가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Caiaphas!", "\"가야바로구나!"], ["I have seen him,” Simonides replied, adding, after a pause during which he thoughtfully watched the haughty pontiff, “And now am I convinced.", "나는 그를 본 적이 있네.\" 시모니데스가 대답하고는, 거만한 대제사장을 사색에 잠겨 지켜보더니 잠시 뒤 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["With such assurance as proceeds from clear enlightenment of the spirit—with absolute assurance—now know I that he who first goes yonder with the inscription about his neck is what the inscription proclaims him—KING OF THE JEWS.", "\"이제 나는 확신하네. 영혼의 맑은 깨우침에서 비롯된 그런 확신으로—절대적인 확신으로—이제 나는 아노라, 저 앞에서 목에 죄패를 걸고 먼저 가는 이가 바로 그 죄패가 선포하는 그자임을—유대인의 왕임을."], ["A common man, an impostor, a felon, was never thus waited upon. For look!", "한낱 평범한 사람이, 사기꾼이, 죄인이 이렇게 떠받들리는 일은 결코 없었네. 보게나!"], ["Here are the nations—Jerusalem, Israel.", "여기 만방이—예루살렘이, 이스라엘이 있네."], ["Here is the ephod, here the blue robe with its fringe, and purple pomegranates, and golden bells, not seen in the street since the day Jaddua went out to meet the Macedonian—proofs all that this Nazarene is King.", "여기 에봇이, 여기 단을 두르고 자줏빛 석류와 금방울을 단 청색 겉옷이 있으니, 얏두아가 마케도니아 사람을 맞으러 나간 그날 이후로 거리에서 본 적 없는 것일세—이 모든 것이 이 나사렛 사람이 왕임을 증명하는 것이라네."], ["Would I could rise and go after him!”", "일어나 그를 따라갈 수만 있다면!\""]], [["Ben-Hur listened surprised; and directly, as if himself awakening to his unusual display of feeling, Simonides said, impatiently,", "벤허는 놀라며 귀를 기울였다. 그러자 곧 시모니데스는 마치 제 유별난 감정의 발로를 스스로 깨달은 듯, 조바심을 내며 말했다."]], [["“Speak to Balthasar, I pray you, and let us begone.", "\"부디 발타사르에게 말씀하시게. 어서 떠나세."], ["The vomit of Jerusalem is coming.”", "예루살렘의 게운 찌꺼기가 오고 있네.\""]], [["Then Esther spoke.", "그때 에스더가 입을 열었다."]], [["“I see some women there, and they are weeping.", "\"저기 여인들이 보이는데, 울고 있어요."], ["Who are they?”", "누구인가요?\""]], [["Following the pointing of her hand, the party beheld four women in tears; one of them leaned upon the arm of a man of aspect not unlike the Nazarene’s.", "그녀가 가리키는 손길을 따라가니, 일행은 눈물을 흘리는 네 여인을 보았다. 그 가운데 하나는 나사렛 사람과 닮은 데가 있는 어떤 남자의 팔에 기대어 있었다."], ["Presently Ben-Hur answered,", "이내 벤허가 대답했다."]], [["“The man is the disciple whom the Nazarene loves the best of all; she who leans upon his arm is Mary, the Master’s mother; the others are friendly women of Galilee.”", "\"저 남자는 나사렛 사람이 누구보다 가장 사랑하는 제자입니다. 그의 팔에 기댄 여인은 마리아로, 그분의 어머니입니다. 나머지는 갈릴리의 다정한 여인들입니다.\""]], [["Esther pursued the mourners with glistening eyes until the multitude received them out of sight.", "에스더는 빛나는 눈으로 그 슬퍼하는 이들을 좇았으나, 군중이 이내 그들을 시야에서 거두어 가 버렸다."]], [["It may be the reader will fancy the foregoing snatches of conversation were had in quiet; but it was not so.", "독자는 앞서 띄엄띄엄 오간 대화들이 조용한 가운데 이루어졌으리라 여길지 모르나, 그렇지 않았다."], ["The talking was, for the most part, like that indulged by people at the seaside under the sound of the surf; for to nothing else can the clamor of this division of the mob be so well likened.", "그 이야기는 대개 사람들이 파도 소리 아래 바닷가에서 주고받는 것과 같았으니, 이 무리의 외침은 다른 무엇에도 그토록 잘 견줄 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["The demonstration was the forerunner of those in which, scarce thirty years later, under rule of the factions, the Holy City was torn to pieces; it was quite as great in numbers, as fanatical and bloodthirsty; boiled and raved, and had in it exactly the same elements—servants, camel-drivers, marketmen, gate-keepers, gardeners, dealers in fruits and wines, proselytes, and foreigners not proselytes, watchmen and menials from the Temple, thieves, robbers, and the myriad not assignable to any class, but who, on such occasions as this, appeared no one could say whence, hungry and smelling of caves and old tombs—bareheaded wretches with naked arms and legs, hair and beard in uncombed mats, and each with one garment the color of clay; beasts with abysmal mouths, in outcry effective as lions calling each other across desert spaces.", "그 소동은 그로부터 채 삼십 년도 지나지 않아 당파들의 지배 아래 거룩한 도성이 갈가리 찢기게 될 때의 그것들을 미리 보여 주는 것이었다. 그것은 머릿수도 그에 못지않았고, 그만큼이나 광신적이고 피에 굶주려 있었으며, 끓어오르고 미쳐 날뛰었고, 그 안에 똑같은 무리가 들어 있었다—종들, 낙타몰이꾼, 장사치, 문지기, 정원사, 과일과 포도주 장수, 개종자들, 개종하지 않은 이방인들, 성전의 파수꾼과 하인들, 도둑들, 강도들, 그리고 어느 부류로도 헤아릴 수 없으나 이런 때면 어디서 나타났는지 아무도 말할 수 없게 모습을 드러내는, 동굴과 오래된 무덤 냄새를 풍기며 굶주린 무수한 자들이었다—맨머리에 팔다리를 드러내고, 머리와 수염은 빗질하지 않아 엉겨 붙고, 저마다 진흙 빛깔의 옷 한 벌만 걸친 가련한 자들이었으니, 사막 너머로 서로를 부르는 사자들처럼 그 외침이 우렁찬, 입이 아득히 벌어진 짐승들이었다."], ["Some of them had swords; a greater number flourished spears and javelins; though the weapons of the many were staves and knotted clubs, and slings, for which latter selected stones were stored in scrips, and sometimes in sacks improvised from the foreskirts of their dirty tunics.", "그들 가운데 더러는 검을 지녔고, 더 많은 자가 창과 투창을 휘둘렀으나, 대다수의 무기는 막대기와 옹이진 몽둥이와 무릿매였으니, 무릿매에 쓸 골라낸 돌은 전대에, 더러는 더러운 속옷 자락으로 즉석에서 만든 자루에 담아 두었다."], ["Among the mass here and there appeared persons of high degree—scribes, elders, rabbis, Pharisees with broad fringing, Sadducees in fine cloaks—serving for the time as prompters and directors.", "그 무리 가운데 여기저기 높은 신분의 인물들이 보였으니—서기관, 장로, 랍비, 술을 넓게 단 바리새인, 고운 망토를 걸친 사두개인이었다—이들은 한동안 부추기는 자요 이끄는 자 노릇을 하고 있었다."], ["If a throat tired of one cry, they invented another for it; if brassy lungs showed signs of collapse, they set them going again; and yet the clamor, loud and continuous as it was, could have been reduced to a few syllables—King of the Jews!", "한 목구멍이 한 외침에 지치면, 그들은 그를 위해 새 외침을 지어냈다. 놋쇠 같던 허파가 주저앉을 기색을 보이면, 그들은 그것을 다시 돌아가게 했다. 그러면서도 그토록 크고 끊임없던 그 소동은 몇 마디 음절로 간추릴 수 있었으니—유대인의 왕!"], ["Room for the King of the Jews!—Defiler of the Temple!—Blasphemer of God!—Crucify him, crucify him!", "유대인의 왕에게 길을 비켜라!—성전을 더럽힌 자!—하나님을 모독한 자!—그를 십자가에 못 박아라, 십자가에 못 박아라!"], ["And of these cries the last one seemed in greatest favor, because, doubtless, it was more directly expressive of the wish of the mob, and helped to better articulate its hatred of the Nazarene.", "이런 외침들 가운데 마지막 것이 가장 인기를 끄는 듯했으니, 의심할 바 없이 그것이 군중의 바람을 가장 직접적으로 드러냈고, 나사렛 사람을 향한 증오를 더 또렷이 표현하는 데 보탬이 되었기 때문이다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"갑시다.\""], ["said Simonides, when Balthasar was ready to proceed—“come, let us forward.”", "발타사르가 떠날 채비를 마치자 시모니데스가 말했다. \"갑시다, 어서 나아갑시다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur did not hear the call.", "벤허는 그 부름을 듣지 못했다."], ["The appearance of the part of the procession then passing, its brutality and hunger for life, were reminding him of the Nazarene—his gentleness, and the many charities he had seen him do for suffering men.", "그때 지나가던 행렬의 그 부분이 보여 주는 모습이, 그 잔인함과 목숨을 향한 굶주림이, 그에게 나사렛 사람을 떠올리게 했던 것이다—그의 온유함과, 고통받는 사람들에게 베푸는 것을 그가 숱하게 보았던 그 많은 자비를. 떠오름은 또 다른 떠오름을 낳았다."], ["Suggestions beget suggestions; so he remembered suddenly his own great indebtedness to the man; the time he himself was in the hands of a Roman guard going, as was supposed, to a death as certain and almost as terrible as this one of the cross; the cooling drink he had at the well by Nazareth, and the divine expression of the face of him who gave it; the later goodness, the miracle of Palm-Sunday; and with these recollections, the thought of his present powerlessness to give back help for help or make return in kind stung him keenly, and he accused himself.", "그리하여 그는 문득 그 사람에게 진 자신의 크나큰 빚을 기억해 냈다. 그 자신이 로마 호위병의 손에 붙들려, 짐작하건대 이 십자가의 죽음만큼이나 확실하고 거의 그만큼이나 끔찍한 죽음을 향해 끌려가던 때를, 나사렛 곁의 우물가에서 마셨던 시원한 물을, 그리고 그것을 건네준 이의 얼굴에 어린 거룩한 표정을, 그 뒤의 선함을, 종려주일의 기적을. 이런 기억과 더불어, 도움에 도움으로 갚거나 같은 식으로 보답할 힘이 지금 자신에게 없다는 생각이 그를 매섭게 찔렀고, 그는 스스로를 책망했다."], ["He had not done all he might; he could have watched with the Galileans, and kept them true and ready; and this—ah!", "그는 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다하지 않았다. 갈릴리 사람들과 함께 망을 보며 그들을 충실하고 채비된 채로 지킬 수 있었을 텐데. 그리고 이때야말로—아!"], ["this was the moment to strike!", "이때야말로 칠 순간이었다!"], ["A blow well given now would not merely disperse the mob and set the Nazarene free; it would be a trumpet-call to Israel, and precipitate the long-dreamt-of war for freedom.", "지금 제대로 한 방을 먹인다면 군중을 흩뜨리고 나사렛 사람을 풀어 주는 데 그치지 않을 것이었다. 그것은 이스라엘을 향한 나팔 소리가 되어, 오래도록 꿈꿔 온 자유의 전쟁을 앞당길 것이었다. 기회가 가고 있었다."], ["The opportunity was going; the minutes were bearing it away; and if lost!", "시간이 그것을 실어 가고 있었다. 그러니 만일 놓친다면! 아브라함의 하나님이여!"], ["God of Abraham! Was there nothing to be done—nothing?", "정녕 할 수 있는 일이 아무것도—아무것도 없단 말인가?"]], [["That instant a party of Galileans caught his eye.", "바로 그 순간 한 무리의 갈릴리 사람들이 그의 눈에 띄었다."], ["He rushed through the press and overtook them.", "그는 인파를 뚫고 달려가 그들을 따라잡았다."]], [["“Follow me,” he said.", "\"나를 따라오시오.\""], ["“I would have speech with you.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대들과 할 말이 있소.\""]], [["The men obeyed him, and when they were under shelter of the house, he spoke again:", "사람들이 그를 따랐고, 그들이 집 그늘 아래 이르자 그는 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“You are of those who took my swords, and agreed with me to strike for freedom and the King who was coming.", "\"그대들은 내 검을 받고, 자유를 위해, 그리고 오시리라던 왕을 위해 떨쳐 일어서기로 나와 약조한 이들이오."], ["You have the swords now, and now is the time to strike with them.", "그대들에게 지금 그 검이 있고, 지금이 바로 그것으로 칠 때요."], ["Go, look everywhere, and find our brethren, and tell them to meet me at the tree of the cross making ready for the Nazarene.", "가서, 사방을 살펴 우리 형제들을 찾고, 나사렛 사람을 위해 채비하고 있는 십자가 나무 곁에서 나를 만나자고 이르시오."], ["Haste all of you! Nay, stand not so!", "모두 서두르시오! 아니, 그렇게 서 있지 마시오!"], ["The Nazarene is the King, and freedom dies with him.”", "나사렛 사람이 왕이오. 그리고 그가 죽으면 자유도 죽는 것이오.\""]], [["They looked at him respectfully, but did not move.", "그들은 그를 정중하게 바라보았으나, 움직이지 않았다."]], [["“Hear you?” he asked.", "\"내 말이 들리지 않소?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["Then one of them replied,", "그러자 그들 가운데 하나가 대답했다."]], [["“Son of Judah”—by that name they knew him—“son of Judah, it is you who are deceived, not we or our brethren who have your swords.", "\"유다의 아들이여\"—그들은 그 이름으로 그를 알고 있었다—\"유다의 아들이여, 속고 있는 것은 바로 당신이지, 당신의 검을 지닌 우리나 우리 형제들이 아닙니다."], ["The Nazarene is not the King; neither has he the spirit of a king.", "나사렛 사람은 왕이 아닙니다. 그에게는 왕의 기개도 없습니다."], ["We were with him when he came into Jerusalem; we saw him in the Temple; he failed himself, and us, and Israel; at the Gate Beautiful he turned his back upon God and refused the throne of David.", "우리는 그가 예루살렘에 들어올 때 함께 있었고, 성전에서 그를 보았습니다. 그는 자신을, 우리를, 이스라엘을 저버렸습니다. 미문에서 그는 하나님께 등을 돌리고 다윗의 왕좌를 마다했습니다."], ["He is not King, and Galilee is not with him.", "그는 왕이 아니며, 갈릴리도 그와 함께하지 않습니다."], ["He shall die the death.", "그는 죽음을 맞을 것입니다."], ["But hear you, son of Judah.", "그러나 들으십시오, 유다의 아들이여."], ["We have your swords, and we are ready now to draw them and strike for freedom; and so is Galilee.", "우리에게는 당신의 검이 있고, 우리는 지금이라도 그것을 뽑아 자유를 위해 칠 채비가 되어 있습니다. 갈릴리도 그러합니다."], ["Be it for freedom, O son of Judah, for freedom!", "자유를 위해서라면, 오 유다의 아들이여, 자유를 위해서라면!"], ["and we will meet you at the tree of the cross.”", "우리는 십자가 나무 곁에서 당신을 만나겠습니다.\""]], [["The sovereign moment of his life was upon Ben-Hur.", "벤허의 생애에서 가장 결정적인 순간이 그에게 닥쳐 있었다."], ["Could he have taken the offer and said the word, history might have been other than it is; but then it would have been history ordered by men, not God—something that never was, and never will be.", "그가 그 제안을 받아들여 한마디만 했더라면, 역사는 지금과 달라졌을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇게 되었다면 그것은 하나님이 아니라 사람이 정한 역사였을 것이니—결코 있었던 적 없고, 앞으로도 결코 없을 것이었다."], ["A confusion fell upon him; he knew not how, though afterwards he attributed it to the Nazarene; for when the Nazarene was risen, he understood the death was necessary to faith in the resurrection, without which Christianity would be an empty husk.", "혼란이 그를 덮쳤다. 그 까닭을 그는 알지 못했으나, 훗날 그는 그것을 나사렛 사람의 탓으로 돌렸다. 나사렛 사람이 부활했을 때, 그는 그 죽음이 부활에 대한 믿음을 위해 꼭 필요했음을, 그 믿음이 없다면 기독교란 속이 빈 껍데기에 지나지 않으리라는 것을 깨달았기 때문이다."], ["The confusion, as has been said, left him without the faculty of decision; he stood helpless—wordless even.", "앞서 말했듯, 그 혼란은 그에게서 결단의 능력을 앗아 갔다. 그는 무력하게—말조차 잃은 채 서 있었다."], ["Covering his face with his hand, he shook with the conflict between his wish, which was what he would have ordered, and the power that was upon him.", "그는 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 자신이 명하고자 했던 바람과, 자신을 짓누르는 힘 사이의 다툼으로 떨었다."]], [["“Come; we are waiting for you,”", "\"갑시다, 우리가 기다리고 있소.\""], ["said Simonides, the fourth time.", "시모니데스가 네 번째로 말했다."]], [["Thereupon he walked mechanically after the chair and the litter. Esther walked with him.", "그러자 그는 기계처럼 가마와 들것을 뒤따라 걸었다. 에스더가 그와 함께 걸었다."], ["Like Balthasar and his friends, the Wise Men, the day they went to the meeting in the desert, he was being led along the way.", "발타사르와 그의 벗들인 동방 박사들이 사막의 만남으로 가던 그날처럼, 그 역시 그 길로 이끌려 가고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# CHAPTER X"]], [["When the party—Balthasar, Simonides, Ben-Hur, Esther, and the two faithful Galileans—reached the place of crucifixion, Ben-Hur was in advance leading them.", "일행이—발타사르, 시모니데스, 벤허, 에스더, 그리고 두 충직한 갈릴리 사람이—처형장에 다다랐을 때, 벤허가 앞장서서 그들을 이끌고 있었다."], ["How they had been able to make way through the great press of excited people, he never knew; no more did he know the road by which they came or the time it took them to come.", "흥분한 군중의 엄청난 인파를 어떻게 헤치고 나아갈 수 있었는지, 그는 끝내 알지 못했다. 어느 길로 왔는지도, 오는 데 얼마나 걸렸는지도 알지 못했다."], ["He had walked in total unconsciousness, neither hearing nor seeing anybody or anything, and without a thought of where he was going, or the ghostliest semblance of a purpose in his mind.", "그는 아무것도 의식하지 못한 채 걸었으니, 누구의 말도 듣지 못했고, 무엇도 보지 못했으며, 어디로 가는지 생각조차 없었고, 마음속에 어떤 목적의 희미한 그림자조차 떠올리지 못했다."], ["In such condition a little child could have done as much as he to prevent the awful crime he was about to witness.", "이런 상태에서라면, 그가 곧 목격하게 될 끔찍한 범죄를 막는 일에는 어린아이라도 그만큼은 할 수 있었으리라."], ["The intentions of God are always strange to us; but not more so than the means by which they are wrought out, and at last made plain to our belief.", "하느님의 뜻은 언제나 우리에게 낯설다. 그러나 그 뜻이 이루어지는 방편, 그리하여 마침내 우리의 믿음 앞에 분명히 드러나는 그 방편 또한 그에 못지않게 낯설다."]], [["Ben-Hur came to a stop; those following him also stopped.", "벤허는 걸음을 멈추었다. 그를 뒤따르던 이들도 멈추어 섰다."], ["As a curtain rises before an audience, the spell holding him in its sleep-awake rose, and he saw with a clear understanding.", "관객 앞에서 막이 오르듯, 그를 자다 깬 듯한 상태에 붙들어 두던 주문이 걷히고, 그는 또렷한 깨달음으로 바라보았다."]], [["There was a space upon the top of a low knoll rounded like a skull, and dry, dusty, and without vegetation, except some scrubby hyssop.", "해골처럼 둥글게 솟은 나지막한 언덕 꼭대기에 빈터가 있었다. 메마르고 먼지투성이에 풀 한 포기 없었으니, 다만 우슬초 덤불 몇 무더기가 있을 뿐이었다."], ["The boundary of the space was a living wall of men, with men behind struggling, some to look over, others to look through it.", "그 빈터의 경계는 사람들로 이루어진 살아 있는 벽이었고, 그 뒤로 또 사람들이 안간힘을 쓰고 있었으니, 더러는 넘겨다보려고, 더러는 그 틈으로 들여다보려고 애썼다."], ["An inner wall of Roman soldiery held the dense outer wall rigidly to its place.", "안쪽으로는 로마 군병들의 벽이 그 빽빽한 바깥 벽을 제자리에 단단히 붙들어 두었다."], ["A centurion kept eye upon the soldiers.", "백부장 하나가 군병들을 지켜보았다."], ["Up to the very line so vigilantly guarded Ben-Hur had been led; at the line he now stood, his face to the northwest.", "벤허는 그토록 삼엄하게 지켜지는 경계선 바로 앞까지 인도되어, 이제 그 선에 서서 북서쪽을 향하고 있었다."], ["The knoll was the old Aramaic Golgotha—in Latin, Calvaria; anglicized, Calvary; translated, The Skull.", "그 언덕은 옛 아람말로 골고다였으니—라틴말로는 칼바리아, 영어로는 캘버리, 풀이하면 해골이다."]], [["On its slopes, in the low places, on the swells and higher hills, the earth sparkled with a strange enamelling.", "그 비탈에서, 낮은 자리에서, 융기한 곳과 더 높은 언덕들에서, 대지는 기이한 칠보처럼 반짝였다."], ["Look where he would outside the walled space, he saw no patch of brown soil, no rock, no green thing; he saw only thousands of eyes in ruddy faces; off a little way in the perspective only ruddy faces without eyes; off a little farther only a broad, broad circle, which the nearer view instructed him was also of faces.", "벽으로 둘러쳐진 빈터 바깥의 어디를 바라보아도, 그는 갈색 흙 한 조각도, 바위 하나도, 푸른 것 하나도 보지 못했다. 그가 본 것은 오직 불그레한 얼굴들 속의 수천 개의 눈동자뿐이었고, 조금 떨어진 곳에서는 눈동자 없는 불그레한 얼굴들뿐이었으며, 거기서 조금 더 떨어진 곳에서는 넓고도 넓은 둥근 띠뿐이었으니, 가까이서 보면 그것 또한 얼굴들임을 알 수 있었다."], ["And this was the ensemble of three millions of people; under it three millions of hearts throbbing with passionate interest in what was taking place upon the knoll; indifferent as to the thieves, caring only for the Nazarene, and for him only as he was an object of hate or fear or curiosity—he who loved them all, and was about to die for them.", "그리고 이것이 곧 삼백만 사람이 한데 모인 광경이었다. 그 아래에서는 삼백만 개의 심장이 언덕 위에서 벌어지는 일에 격정 어린 관심으로 두근거리고 있었다. 강도들에게는 무심한 채, 오직 그 나사렛 사람만을 마음에 두었으니, 그것도 오로지 그가 미움이나 두려움이나 호기심의 대상인 까닭이었다—그들 모두를 사랑하고, 그들을 위해 곧 죽으려 하는 바로 그 사람을."]], [["In the spectacle of a great assemblage of people there are always the bewilderment and fascination one feels while looking over a stretch of sea in agitation, and never had this one been exceeded; yet Ben-Hur gave it but a passing glance, for that which was going on in the space described would permit no division of his interest.", "큰 무리의 사람들이 모인 광경에는, 일렁이는 바다의 펼쳐진 면을 바라볼 때 느끼는 그 어리둥절함과 매혹이 언제나 깃들어 있는 법이며, 이번만큼 그것을 능가한 적은 없었다. 그러나 벤허는 그것에 스쳐 가는 눈길 한 번만을 던졌을 뿐이니, 앞서 말한 빈터에서 벌어지고 있는 일이 그의 관심을 다른 데로 나누는 것을 허락하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Up on the knoll so high as to be above the living wall, and visible over the heads of an attending company of notables, conspicuous because of his mitre and vestments and his haughty air, stood the high priest.", "언덕 위, 살아 있는 벽보다 높이 솟아 둘러선 명사들의 무리 머리 위로 보이는 곳, 주교관과 제의(祭衣)와 거만한 태도로 단연 눈에 띄는 자리에 대제사장이 서 있었다."], ["Up the knoll still higher, up quite to the round summit, so as to be seen far and near, was the Nazarene, stooped and suffering, but silent.", "언덕을 더 높이 올라가, 둥근 정상 바로 그곳에 이르러, 멀리서도 가까이서도 보이는 자리에 그 나사렛 사람이 있었으니, 구부정하니 고통받으면서도 잠잠했다."], ["The wit among the guard had complemented the crown upon his head by putting a reed in his hand for a sceptre.", "경비병 가운데 한 익살꾼이 그의 머리에 씌운 가시관을 보태어, 그의 손에 갈대 하나를 홀(笏) 삼아 쥐여 주었다."], ["Clamors blew upon him like blasts—laughter—execrations—sometimes both together indistinguishably.", "외침이 돌풍처럼 그에게 몰아쳤다—웃음소리—저주—때로는 둘이 한데 뒤엉켜 분간할 수 없었다."], ["A man—ONLY a man, O reader, would have charged the blasts with the remainder of his love for the race, and let it go forever.", "한 사람이—한낱 사람이라면, 오 독자여, 그 돌풍에 인류를 향한 사랑의 남은 몫까지 모조리 실어, 영영 흘려보냈으리라."]], [["All the eyes then looking were fixed upon the Nazarene.", "그때 바라보던 모든 눈은 그 나사렛 사람에게 못 박혀 있었다."], ["It may have been pity with which he was moved; whatever the cause, Ben-Hur was conscious of a change in his feelings.", "그를 움직인 것은 어쩌면 연민이었는지도 모른다. 그 까닭이 무엇이든, 벤허는 제 마음속에 일어나는 변화를 의식했다."], ["A conception of something better than the best of this life—something so much better that it could serve a weak man with strength to endure agonies of spirit as well as of body; something to make death welcome—perhaps another life purer than this one—perhaps the spirit-life which Balthasar held to so fast, began to dawn upon his mind clearer and clearer, bringing to him a certain sense that, after all, the mission of the Nazarene was that of guide across the boundary for such as loved him; across the boundary to where his kingdom was set up and waiting for him.", "이 삶의 가장 좋은 것보다 더 나은 무엇—연약한 사람에게 육신의 고통뿐 아니라 영혼의 고통까지 견딜 힘을 줄 만큼 그토록 더 나은 무엇, 죽음을 기꺼이 맞이하게 만드는 무엇—어쩌면 이 삶보다 더 순결한 또 다른 삶, 어쩌면 발타사르가 그토록 굳게 붙들었던 영혼의 삶에 대한 깨달음이 그의 마음속에 점점 또렷이 밝아 오기 시작하더니, 결국 그 나사렛 사람의 사명이 자기를 사랑하는 이들을 경계 너머로 인도하는 것이었다는 어떤 확신을 그에게 안겨 주었다—그의 나라가 세워져 그를 기다리고 있는 그 경계 너머로 말이다."], ["Then, as something borne through the air out of the almost forgotten, he heard again, or seemed to hear, the saying of the Nazarene,", "그러자 거의 잊혔던 것 가운데서 공기를 타고 실려 오듯, 그는 그 나사렛 사람의 말을 다시 들었으니, 적어도 들은 것만 같았다."]], [["And the words repeated themselves over and over, and took form, and the dawn touched them with its light, and filled them with a new meaning.", "\"나는 부활이요 생명이니.\" 그 말이 거듭거듭 되풀이되어 형태를 갖추고, 새벽빛이 그것을 어루만져 새로운 뜻으로 채웠다."], ["And as men repeat a question to grasp and fix the meaning, he asked, gazing at the figure on the hill fainting under its crown, Who the Resurrection?", "그리하여 사람들이 뜻을 붙들어 새기려고 물음을 되묻듯, 그는 가시관 아래 기진해 가는 언덕 위의 형상을 바라보며 물었다. 부활은 누구인가?"], ["and who the Life?", "그리고 생명은 누구인가?"]], [["the figure seemed to say—and say it for him; for instantly he was sensible of a peace such as he had never known—the peace which is the end of doubt and mystery, and the beginning of faith and love and clear understanding.", "\"나니라.\" 그 형상이 그렇게 말하는 듯했다—그를 위해 말하는 듯했다. 그 순간 그는 일찍이 알지 못했던 평안을 느꼈으니—의심과 신비의 끝이며 믿음과 사랑과 또렷한 깨달음의 시작인 그 평안이었다."]], [["From this dreamy state Ben-Hur was aroused by the sound of hammering.", "이 꿈결 같은 상태에서 벤허는 망치질 소리에 깨어났다."], ["On the summit of the knoll he observed then what had escaped him before—some soldiers and workmen preparing the crosses.", "그때 그는 언덕 정상에서 미처 보지 못했던 것을 알아차렸다—군병 몇과 일꾼들이 십자가를 마련하고 있었던 것이다."], ["The holes for planting the trees were ready, and now the transverse beams were being fitted to their places.", "나무를 박을 구덩이는 이미 준비되어 있었고, 이제 가로대를 제자리에 맞추는 중이었다."]], [["“Bid the men make haste,” said the high-priest to the centurion.", "\"사람들에게 서두르라 일러라.\" 대제사장이 백부장에게 말했다."], ["“These”—and he pointed to the Nazarene—“must be dead by the going-down of the sun, and buried that the land may not be defiled.", "\"이자들은\"—그러며 그는 나사렛 사람을 가리켰다—\"해가 지기 전에 죽어야 하고, 이 땅이 더럽혀지지 않도록 묻혀야 한다."], ["Such is the Law.”", "율법이 그러하다.\""]], [["With a better mind, a soldier went to the Nazarene and offered him something to drink, but he refused the cup.", "한 군병이 갸륵한 마음으로 나사렛 사람에게 다가가 마실 것을 권했으나, 그는 그 잔을 물리쳤다."], ["Then another went to him and took from his neck the board with the inscription upon it, which he nailed to the tree of the cross—and the preparation was complete.", "그러자 다른 군병이 다가가 그의 목에서 죄패가 적힌 판을 떼어, 그것을 십자가 나무에 못으로 박았다—그리하여 채비가 끝났다."]], [["“The crosses are ready,”", "\"십자가가 준비되었습니다.\""], ["said the centurion to the pontiff, who received the report with a wave of the hand and the reply,", "백부장이 제사장에게 말하자, 제사장은 손을 한 번 내저으며 그 보고를 받고 대답했다."]], [["“Let the blasphemer go first.", "\"저 신성모독자부터 먼저 처리하라."], ["The Son of God should be able to save himself.", "하느님의 아들이라면 제 한 몸쯤은 구할 수 있으렷다."], ["We will see.”", "어디 두고 보자.\""]], [["The people to whom the preparation in its several stages was visible, and who to this time had assailed the hill with incessant cries of impatience, permitted a lull which directly became a universal hush.", "채비가 단계마다 눈에 보이던 사람들, 그때껏 그치지 않는 조바심의 외침으로 언덕을 들볶던 사람들이 잠잠해지더니, 그 잠잠함이 곧장 온통의 침묵으로 번졌다."], ["The part of the infliction most shocking, at least to the thought, was reached—the men were to be nailed to their crosses.", "적어도 생각하기에 가장 충격적인 형벌의 대목에 이른 것이었다—사람들이 십자가에 못 박히게 된 것이다."], ["When for that purpose the soldiers laid their hands upon the Nazarene first, a shudder passed through the great concourse; the most brutalized shrank with dread.", "그 일을 위해 군병들이 먼저 나사렛 사람에게 손을 댔을 때, 거대한 군중 사이로 전율이 지나갔다. 가장 모질게 무뎌진 자들마저 두려움에 움츠러들었다."], ["Afterwards there were those who said the air suddenly chilled and made them shiver.", "나중에 더러는 별안간 공기가 차가워져 몸이 떨렸다고 말하기도 했다."]], [["“How very still it is!” Esther said, as she put her arm about her father’s neck.", "\"어쩜 이리도 고요할까요!\" 에스더가 아버지의 목에 팔을 두르며 말했다."]], [["And remembering the torture he himself had suffered, he drew her face down upon his breast, and sat trembling.", "그러자 그는 자신이 겪었던 고문을 떠올리며 딸의 얼굴을 제 가슴 위로 끌어당기고는, 떨면서 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Avoid it, Esther, avoid it!”", "\"보지 마라, 에스더, 보지 마라!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I know not but all who stand and see it—the innocent as well as the guilty—may be cursed from this hour.”", "\"여기 서서 이 일을 보는 모든 이가—죄 있는 자뿐 아니라 죄 없는 자까지도—이 시각부터 저주받을지 누가 알겠느냐.\""]], [["Balthasar sank upon his knees.", "발타사르는 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다."]], [["“Son of Hur,”", "\"후르의 아들이여,\""], ["said Simonides, with increasing excitement—“son of Hur, if Jehovah stretch not forth his hand, and quickly, Israel is lost—and we are lost.”", "시모니데스가 점점 격해지며 말했다—\"후르의 아들이여, 여호와께서 손을 뻗으시지 않는다면, 그것도 어서 뻗으시지 않는다면, 이스라엘은 끝장이오—우리도 끝장이오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur answered, calmly, “I have been in a dream, Simonides, and heard in it why all this should be, and why it should go on.", "벤허가 차분히 대답했다. \"나는 꿈을 꾸었소, 시모니데스. 그 꿈속에서 어찌하여 이 모든 일이 일어나야 하는지, 어찌하여 이대로 진행되어야 하는지 들었소."], ["It is the will of the Nazarene—it is God’s will.", "이것은 저 나사렛 사람의 뜻이오—하느님의 뜻이오."], ["Let us do as the Egyptian here—let us hold our peace and pray.”", "여기 이 이집트 사람처럼 합시다—잠잠히 기도합시다.\""]], [["As he looked up on the knoll again, the words were wafted to him through the awful stillness—", "그가 다시 언덕 위를 올려다보았을 때, 그 두려운 고요를 뚫고 말씀이 그에게 실려 왔다. \"나는 부활이요 생명이니.\""]], [["He bowed reverently as to a person speaking.", "그는 곁에서 말하는 사람을 대하듯 경건히 머리를 숙였다."]], [["Up on the summit meantime the work went on.", "그동안 정상에서는 일이 진행되었다."], ["The guard took the Nazarene’s clothes from him; so that he stood before the millions naked.", "경비병들이 나사렛 사람의 옷을 벗기니, 그는 수백만 사람 앞에 벌거벗은 채로 섰다."], ["The stripes of the scourging he had received in the early morning were still bloody upon his back; yet he was laid pitilessly down, and stretched upon the cross—first, the arms upon the transverse beam; the spikes were sharp—a few blows, and they were driven through the tender palms; next, they drew his knees up until the soles of the feet rested flat upon the tree; then they placed one foot upon the other, and one spike fixed both of them fast.", "이른 아침에 받은 채찍질 자국이 아직도 그의 등에 핏자국으로 남아 있었다. 그런데도 그는 무자비하게 눕혀져 십자가 위에 펼쳐졌다—먼저 두 팔이 가로대 위에 놓였다. 못은 날카로웠다—몇 번 두드리자, 못은 그 여린 손바닥을 꿰뚫었다."], ["The dulled sound of the hammering was heard outside the guarded space; and such as could not hear, yet saw the hammer as it fell, shivered with fear.", "다음으로 그들은 발바닥이 나무에 평평하게 닿도록 그의 무릎을 끌어올렸다. 그러고는 한쪽 발을 다른 발 위에 포개고, 못 하나로 두 발을 한꺼번에 단단히 박았다. 둔탁한 망치질 소리가 지켜지는 빈터 밖으로 새어 나왔고, 그 소리를 듣지 못하는 이들도 망치가 떨어지는 모습을 보고는 두려움에 떨었다."], ["And withal not a groan, or cry, or word of remonstrance from the sufferer: nothing at which an enemy could laugh; nothing a lover could regret.", "그러면서도 그 고통받는 이에게서는 신음 한 번, 외침 한 번, 항변의 말 한마디도 없었으니, 원수가 비웃을 만한 것도 없었고, 사랑하는 이가 안타까워할 만한 것도 없었다."]], [["“Which way wilt thou have him faced?”", "\"이자를 어느 쪽으로 향하게 할까요?\""], ["asked a soldier, bluntly.", "한 군병이 무뚝뚝하게 물었다."]], [["“Towards the Temple,”", "\"성전 쪽으로.\""], ["the pontiff replied.", "제사장이 대답했다."], ["“In dying I would have him see the holy house hath not suffered by him.”", "\"죽어 가면서 거룩한 집이 자기 때문에 해를 입지 않았음을 보게 하리라.\""]], [["The workmen put their hands to the cross, and carried it, burden and all, to the place of planting.", "일꾼들이 십자가에 손을 대어, 짐과 함께 통째로 박을 자리로 옮겼다."], ["At a word, they dropped the tree into the hole; and the body of the Nazarene also dropped heavily, and hung by the bleeding hands.", "한마디 신호에 그들은 나무를 구덩이에 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 나사렛 사람의 몸 또한 묵직하게 떨어지면서, 피 흐르는 두 손에 매달렸다."], ["Still no cry of pain—only the exclamation divinest of all recorded exclamations,", "그래도 고통의 외침은 없었다—다만 기록된 모든 탄성 가운데 가장 거룩한 탄성이 흘러나왔을 뿐이다."]], [["“Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do.”", "\"아버지, 저들을 용서하소서. 저들은 자기가 하는 일을 알지 못하나이다.\""]], [["The cross, reared now above all other objects, and standing singly out against the sky, was greeted with a burst of delight; and all who could see and read the writing upon the board over the Nazarene’s head made haste to decipher it.", "이제 다른 모든 것 위로 높이 세워져 하늘을 배경으로 홀로 우뚝 선 십자가는 환호의 폭발로 맞이되었다. 나사렛 사람의 머리 위 판자에 적힌 글을 보고 읽을 수 있는 이들은 모두 서둘러 그것을 풀이했다."], ["Soon as read, the legend was adopted by them and communicated, and presently the whole mighty concourse was ringing the salutation from side to side, and repeating it with laughter and groans,", "읽자마자 그 글귀는 받아들여져 입에서 입으로 전해졌고, 곧 그 거대한 군중 전체가 이쪽저쪽에서 인사말을 울려 대며 웃음과 신음 속에 그것을 되뇌었다."]], [["“King of the Jews!", "\"유대인의 왕이여!"], ["Hail, King of the Jews!”", "만세, 유대인의 왕이여!\""]], [["The pontiff, with a clearer idea of the import of the inscription, protested against it, but in vain; so the titled King, looking from the knoll with dying eyes, must have had the city of his fathers at rest below him—she who had so ignominiously cast him out.", "그 글귀의 의미를 더 분명히 알아챈 제사장이 그에 항의했으나, 부질없었다. 그리하여 왕이라 불린 그분은 죽어 가는 눈으로 언덕에서 바라보며, 자기를 그토록 욕되게 내쫓았던 조상들의 도성이 자기 발치 아래 잠잠히 놓여 있는 것을 틀림없이 보았으리라."]], [["The sun was rising rapidly to noon; the hills bared their brown breasts lovingly to it; the more distant mountains rejoiced in the purple with which it so regally dressed them.", "해는 빠르게 정오를 향해 떠올랐다. 언덕들은 그 갈색 가슴을 정겹게 해에게 드러냈고, 더 멀리 있는 산들은 해가 그토록 왕답게 입혀 준 자줏빛에 기뻐했다."], ["In the city, the temples, palaces, towers, pinnacles, and all points of beauty and prominence seemed to lift themselves into the unrivalled brilliance, as if they knew the pride they were giving the many who from time to time turned to look at them.", "도성에서는 성전과 궁전과 탑과 첨탑, 그리고 아름답고 두드러진 모든 지점이 그 견줄 데 없는 광채 속으로 스스로를 들어 올리는 듯했으니, 마치 이따금 그것들을 돌아보는 많은 이에게 자기가 안겨 주는 긍지를 알고 있기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Suddenly a dimness began to fill the sky and cover the earth—at first no more than a scarce perceptible fading of the day; a twilight out of time; an evening gliding in upon the splendors of noon.", "그런데 별안간 어둑함이 하늘을 채우고 대지를 덮기 시작했다—처음에는 좀처럼 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 낮이 희미해지는 정도였으니, 때아닌 어스름, 정오의 광휘 위로 슬며시 미끄러져 드는 저녁이었다. 그러나 그것은 점점 짙어져 곧장 주의를 끌었다."], ["But it deepened, and directly drew attention; whereat the noise of the shouting and laughter fell off, and men, doubting their senses, gazed at each other curiously: then they looked to the sun again; then at the mountains, getting farther away; at the sky and the near landscape, sinking in shadow; at the hill upon which the tragedy was enacting; and from all these they gazed at each other again, and turned pale, and held their peace.", "그러자 외침과 웃음의 소란이 잦아들었고, 사람들은 제 감각을 의심하며 야릇하게 서로를 바라보았다. 그러고는 다시 해를 올려다보았고, 점점 멀어져 가는 산들을 보았으며, 그늘에 잠겨 드는 하늘과 가까운 들녘을 보았고, 비극이 펼쳐지고 있는 언덕을 보았다. 그러고는 이 모든 것에서 다시 서로를 바라보고는, 얼굴이 새파래져 입을 다물었다."]], [["“It is only a mist or passing cloud,”", "\"안개나 지나가는 구름일 뿐이란다.\""], ["Simonides said soothingly to Esther, who was alarmed.", "시모니데스가 겁에 질린 에스더를 달래며 말했다."], ["“It will brighten presently.”", "\"곧 다시 밝아질 게다.\""]], [["Ben-Hur did not think so.", "벤허는 그렇게 생각하지 않았다."]], [["“It is not a mist or a cloud,” he said.", "\"안개나 구름이 아닙니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“The spirits who live in the air—the prophets and saints—are at work in mercy to themselves and nature. I say to you, O Simonides, truly as God lives, he who hangs yonder is the Son of God.”", "\"공중에 사는 영들이—예언자들과 성도들이—자기 자신과 자연을 위해 자비롭게 일하고 있는 것입니다. 그대에게 이르노니, 오 시모니데스여, 하느님이 살아 계심이 진실인 것만큼이나 진실로, 저기 매달린 분은 하느님의 아들입니다.\""]], [["And leaving Simonides lost in wonder at such a speech from him, he went where Balthasar was kneeling near by, and laid his hand upon the good man’s shoulder.", "그러고는 그런 말을 한 그에게 놀라 어쩔 줄 모르는 시모니데스를 두고, 그는 발타사르가 가까이서 무릎 꿇고 있는 곳으로 가, 그 선한 사람의 어깨에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“O wise Egyptian, hearken! Thou alone wert right—the Nazarene is indeed the Son of God.”", "\"오 슬기로운 이집트 사람이여, 들으시오! 그대만이 옳았소—저 나사렛 사람은 참으로 하느님의 아들이오.\""]], [["Balthasar drew him down to him, and replied, feebly, “I saw him a child in the manger where he was first laid; it is not strange that I knew him sooner than thou; but oh that I should live to see this day!", "발타사르는 그를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기고는 힘없이 대답했다. \"나는 그가 처음 눕혀진 구유 속의 갓난아기일 적에 그를 보았다네. 그러니 내가 자네보다 먼저 그를 알아본 것이 이상할 게 없지."], ["Would I had died with my brethren!", "하지만 아, 이 날을 보도록 내가 살아 있다니! 차라리 내 형제들과 함께 죽었더라면!"], ["Happy Melchior!", "복된 멜키오르여!"], ["Happy, happy Gaspar!”", "복되고 복된 가스파르여!\""]], [["“Comfort thee!”", "\"위안을 삼으시오!\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."], ["“Doubtless they too are here.”", "\"분명 그들도 여기 함께 있을 것이오.\""]], [["The dimness went on deepening into obscurity, and that into positive darkness, but without deterring the bolder spirits upon the knoll.", "어둑함은 점점 짙어져 어슴푸레함이 되고, 그것이 다시 완연한 어둠이 되었으나, 언덕 위의 더 대담한 자들을 막지는 못했다."], ["One after the other the thieves were raised on their crosses, and the crosses planted.", "강도들이 차례로 제 십자가에 들어 올려지고, 십자가가 박혔다."], ["The guard was then withdrawn, and the people set free closed in upon the height, and surged up it, like a converging wave.", "그러자 경비가 거두어지고, 풀려난 사람들이 그 높은 곳을 향해 밀려들어, 한곳으로 모이는 물결처럼 그 위로 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["A man might take a look, when a new-comer would push him on, and take his place, to be in turn pushed on—and there were laughter and ribaldry and revilements, all for the Nazarene.", "한 사람이 한 번 들여다볼라치면, 새로 온 자가 그를 밀쳐 내고 그 자리를 차지했다가 다시 떠밀리곤 했다—그리고 웃음과 음담과 욕설이 있었으니, 모두 나사렛 사람을 향한 것이었다."]], [["“Ha, ha!", "\"하, 하!"], ["If thou be King of the Jews, save thyself,”", "네가 유대인의 왕이거든, 네 한 몸이나 구해 보아라.\""], ["a soldier shouted.", "한 군병이 외쳤다."]], [["“Ay,”", "\"옳소,\""], ["said a priest, “if he will come down to us now, we will believe in him.”", "한 제사장이 말했다. \"저자가 지금 우리에게 내려온다면, 우리도 그를 믿어 주마.\""]], [["Others wagged their heads wisely, saying, “He would destroy the Temple, and rebuild it in three days, but cannot save himself.”", "또 다른 자들은 짐짓 똑똑한 척 머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"성전을 헐고 사흘 만에 다시 짓겠다더니, 제 한 몸도 못 구하는구나.\""]], [["Others still: “He called himself the Son of God; let us see if God will have him.”", "또 다른 자들은 이렇게 말했다. \"자기를 하느님의 아들이라 일컬었으니, 하느님이 그를 거두시는지 어디 보자.\""]], [["What all there is in prejudice no one has ever said.", "편견 속에 무엇이 다 들어 있는지는 아무도 말한 적이 없다."], ["The Nazarene had never harmed the people; far the greater part of them had never seen him except in this his hour of calamity; yet—singular contrariety!—they loaded him with their curses, and gave their sympathy to the thieves.", "그 나사렛 사람은 이 사람들을 결코 해친 적이 없었다. 그들 가운데 대부분은 이 재난의 시각이 아니고서는 그를 본 적조차 없었다. 그런데도—기이한 모순이여!—그들은 그에게 저주를 퍼붓고, 강도들에게 동정을 보냈다."]], [["The supernatural night, dropped thus from the heavens, affected Esther as it began to affect thousands of others braver and stronger.", "하늘에서 그렇게 떨어진 초자연의 밤은, 그것이 더 용감하고 강한 수천 사람에게 영향을 미치기 시작했듯이, 에스더에게도 영향을 미쳤다."]], [["“Let us go home,”", "\"집으로 가요.\""], ["she prayed—twice, three times—saying, “It is the frown of God, father.", "그녀가 두 번, 세 번 애원했다. \"이건 하느님의 진노예요, 아버지."], ["What other dreadful things may happen, who can tell?", "또 무슨 무서운 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠어요?"], ["I am afraid.”", "저는 무서워요.\""]], [["Simonides was obstinate.", "시모니데스는 고집을 부렸다."], ["He said little, but was plainly under great excitement.", "그는 말은 거의 없었으나, 분명 큰 흥분에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Observing, about the end of the first hour, that the violence of the crowding up on the knoll was somewhat abated, at his suggestion the party advanced to take position nearer the crosses.", "첫 시각이 끝나갈 무렵, 언덕 위로 밀려드는 사람들의 기세가 다소 누그러진 것을 보고는, 그의 제안에 따라 일행은 십자가에 더 가까운 자리로 나아갔다."], ["Ben-Hur gave his arm to Balthasar; yet the Egyptian made the ascent with difficulty.", "벤허가 발타사르에게 팔을 내주었으나, 그 이집트 사람은 어렵게야 오를 수 있었다."], ["From their new stand, the Nazarene was imperfectly visible, appearing to them not more than a dark suspended figure.", "새로 잡은 자리에서는 나사렛 사람이 흐릿하게 보였으니, 그들에게는 매달린 어두운 형상에 지나지 않았다."], ["They could hear him, however—hear his sighing, which showed an endurance or exhaustion greater than that of his fellow-sufferers; for they filled every lull in the noises with their groans and entreaties.", "그래도 그들은 그의 소리를 들을 수 있었으니—그의 한숨 소리를 들을 수 있었는데, 그 한숨은 함께 고통받는 자들보다 더 큰 인내, 혹은 더 큰 탈진을 드러냈다. 그 두 사람은 소음이 잦아드는 틈마다 신음과 애원으로 채웠기 때문이다."]], [["The second hour after the suspension passed like the first one.", "십자가에 매달린 지 두 시간째도 첫 시간처럼 지나갔다."], ["To the Nazarene they were hours of insult, provocation, and slow dying.", "나사렛 사람에게 그 시간은 모욕과 도발과 더딘 죽음의 시간이었다."], ["He spoke but once in the time.", "그는 그 사이에 단 한 번 입을 열었다."], ["Some women came and knelt at the foot of his cross.", "몇몇 여인이 와서 그의 십자가 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["Among them he recognized his mother with the beloved disciple.", "그들 가운데서 그는 자기 어머니와 사랑하는 제자를 알아보았다."]], [["“Woman,”", "\"여인이여,\""], ["he said, raising his voice, “behold thy son!”", "그가 목소리를 높여 말했다. \"당신의 아들을 보소서!\""], ["And to the disciple, “Behold thy mother!”", "그리고 제자에게는 \"네 어머니를 보아라!\" 하고 말했다."]], [["The third hour came, and still the people surged round the hill, held to it by some strange attraction, with which, in probability, the night in midday had much to do.", "세 시간째가 되었으나, 사람들은 여전히 언덕 둘레로 밀려들었으니, 어떤 기이한 이끌림에 붙들려 있었고, 그 이끌림에는 십중팔구 한낮의 밤이 큰 몫을 했을 것이다."], ["They were quieter than in the preceding hour; yet at intervals they could be heard off in the darkness shouting to each other, multitude calling unto multitude.", "그들은 앞선 시각보다 잠잠했다. 그래도 이따금 어둠 속 멀리서 서로 외쳐 대는 소리가 들렸으니, 무리가 무리를 부르는 소리였다."], ["It was noticeable, also, that coming now to the Nazarene, they approached his cross in silence, took the look in silence, and so departed.", "또한 눈에 띈 것은, 이제 나사렛 사람에게 다가올 때면 사람들이 잠잠히 그의 십자가에 이르러, 잠잠히 바라보고는, 그렇게 잠잠히 떠나갔다는 점이다."], ["This change extended even to the guard, who so shortly before had cast lots for the clothes of the crucified; they stood with their officers a little apart, more watchful of the one convict than of the throngs coming and going.", "이 변화는 경비병들에게까지 미쳤으니, 그토록 조금 전에 처형된 자의 옷을 두고 제비를 뽑던 자들이었다. 그들은 장교들과 함께 조금 떨어져 서서, 오가는 군중보다 그 한 죄수를 더 눈여겨 지켰다."], ["If he but breathed heavily, or tossed his head in a paroxysm of pain, they were instantly on the alert.", "그가 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬거나 고통의 발작으로 머리를 젖히기만 해도, 그들은 즉시 경계 태세에 들어갔다."], ["Most marvellous of all, however, was the altered behavior of the high-priest and his following, the wise men who had assisted him in the trial in the night, and, in the victim’s face, kept place by him with zealous approval.", "그러나 무엇보다 놀라운 것은 대제사장과 그를 따르는 자들, 곧 밤의 재판에서 그를 거들고 희생자의 면전에서 열성껏 동조하며 그 곁을 지켰던 현자들의 달라진 행동이었다."], ["When the darkness began to fall, they began to lose their confidence.", "어둠이 내리기 시작하자, 그들은 자신감을 잃기 시작했다."], ["There were among them many learned in astronomy, and familiar with the apparitions so terrible in those days to the masses; much of the knowledge was descended to them from their fathers far back; some of it had been brought away at the end of the Captivity; and the necessities of the Temple service kept it all bright.", "그들 가운데는 천문학에 밝아, 그 시절 대중에게 그토록 무서웠던 천체 현상에 익숙한 자가 많았다. 그 지식의 상당 부분은 먼 조상에게서 물려받은 것이었고, 일부는 포로 생활이 끝날 무렵 가지고 나온 것이었으며, 성전 봉사의 필요가 그 모든 지식을 늘 또렷하게 지켜 주었다."], ["These closed together when the sun commenced to fade before their eyes, and the mountains and hills to recede; they drew together in a group around their pontiff, and debated what they saw.", "이들은 해가 자기네 눈앞에서 희미해지고 산과 언덕이 물러가기 시작하자 한데 모여들었다. 그들은 제사장을 둘러싸고 무리 지어, 자기네가 본 것을 두고 논의했다."], ["“The moon is at its full,”", "\"달이 만월이니,\""], ["they said, with truth, “and this cannot be an eclipse.”", "그들은 사실대로 말했다. \"이것은 일식일 수 없다.\""], ["Then, as no one could answer the question common with them all—as no one could account for the darkness, or for its occurrence at that particular time, in their secret hearts they associated it with the Nazarene, and yielded to an alarm which the long continuance of the phenomenon steadily increased.", "그러고는, 그들 모두에게 공통된 그 물음에 아무도 답할 수 없자—아무도 그 어둠을, 또는 하필 그 특정한 시각에 일어난 그 어둠을 설명할 수 없자, 그들은 속마음으로 그것을 나사렛 사람과 연결 지었고, 그 현상이 오래 이어질수록 점점 커지는 불안에 굴복했다."], ["In their place behind the soldiers, they noted every word and motion of the Nazarene, and hung with fear upon his sighs, and talked in whispers.", "군병들 뒤 제자리에서 그들은 나사렛 사람의 말 한마디 몸짓 하나하나를 살피며, 그의 한숨에 두려워 매달리고, 소곤소곤 말을 나누었다."], ["The man might be the Messiah, and then— But they would wait and see!", "저 사람이 메시아일지도 모르고, 그렇다면— 그러나 그들은 두고 보기로 했다!"]], [["In the meantime Ben-Hur was not once visited by the old spirit.", "그동안 벤허에게는 그 옛날의 영(靈)이 한 번도 찾아오지 않았다."], ["The perfect peace abode with him.", "그 완전한 평안이 그에게 머물러 있었다."], ["He prayed simply that the end might be hastened.", "그는 다만 그 끝이 어서 앞당겨지기를 간구했다."], ["He knew the condition of Simonides’ mind—that he was hesitating on the verge of belief.", "그는 시모니데스의 마음 상태를 알고 있었으니—그가 믿음의 문턱에서 망설이고 있음을 알았다."], ["He could see the massive face weighed down by solemn reflection.", "그 묵직한 얼굴이 엄숙한 사색에 짓눌려 있는 것이 그의 눈에 보였다."], ["He noticed him casting inquiring glances at the sun, as seeking the cause of the darkness.", "그가 어둠의 원인을 찾기라도 하듯 해를 향해 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던지는 것이 그의 눈에 띄었다."], ["Nor did he fail to notice the solicitude with which Esther clung to him, smothering her fears to accommodate his wishes.", "또한 에스더가 제 두려움을 억누르고 아버지의 뜻에 맞추며 그에게 매달리는 그 애틋함도 그는 놓치지 않았다."]], [["“Be not afraid,”", "\"두려워 말아라.\""], ["he heard him say to her; “but stay and watch with me.", "그가 딸에게 말하는 것을 그는 들었다. \"다만 머물러 나와 함께 지켜보아라."], ["Thou mayst live twice the span of my life, and see nothing of human interest equal to this; and there may be revelations more.", "너는 내 평생의 두 곱절을 산다 해도, 이만한 인간사를 보지는 못할 것이다. 그리고 더 많은 계시가 있을지도 모른다."], ["Let us stay to the close.”", "끝까지 머물자꾸나.\""]], [["When the third hour was about half gone, some men of the rudest class—wretches from the tombs about the city—came and stopped in front of the centre cross.", "세 시간째가 절반쯤 지났을 때, 가장 천한 부류의 사람들 몇이—도성 둘레 무덤가에서 나온 비참한 자들이—와서 한가운데 십자가 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“This is he, the new King of the Jews,”", "\"이자가 바로 그자다, 유대인의 새 왕이라지.\""], ["said one of them.", "그들 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["The others cried, with laughter, “Hail, all hail, King of the Jews!”", "다른 자들이 웃으며 외쳤다. \"만세, 만만세, 유대인의 왕이시여!\""]], [["Receiving no reply, they went closer.", "아무 대답이 없자, 그들은 더 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["“If thou be King of the Jews, or Son of God, come down,”", "\"네가 유대인의 왕이거나 하느님의 아들이거든, 어디 내려와 보아라.\""], ["they said, loudly.", "그들이 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["At this, one of the thieves quit groaning, and called to the Nazarene, “Yes, if thou be Christ, save thyself and us.”", "이 말에, 강도 가운데 하나가 신음을 그치고 나사렛 사람에게 외쳤다. \"그래, 네가 그리스도거든, 너 자신과 우리를 구원해 보아라.\""]], [["The people laughed and applauded; then, while they were listening for a reply, the other felon was heard to say to the first one, “Dost thou not fear God?", "사람들이 웃으며 박수를 쳤다. 그러고는 그들이 대답을 들으려고 귀를 기울이는데, 다른 죄수가 첫째 죄수에게 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. \"너는 하느님이 두렵지도 않으냐?"], ["We receive the due rewards of our deeds; but this man hath done nothing amiss.”", "우리는 우리가 행한 일에 마땅한 갚음을 받는 것이지만, 이분은 아무런 잘못도 하지 않으셨다.\""]], [["The bystanders were astonished; in the midst of the hush which ensued, the second felon spoke again, but this time to the Nazarene:", "곁에 선 자들이 놀랐다. 뒤이은 침묵 가운데 둘째 죄수가 다시 입을 열었으나, 이번에는 나사렛 사람을 향해서였다."]], [["“Lord,”", "\"주여,\""], ["he said, “remember me when thou comest into thy kingdom.”", "그가 말했다. \"당신의 나라에 들어가실 때 저를 기억하소서.\""]], [["Simonides gave a great start.", "시모니데스가 크게 움찔했다."], ["“When thou comest into thy kingdom!”", "\"당신의 나라에 들어가실 때!\""], ["It was the very point of doubt in his mind; the point he had so often debated with Balthasar.", "그것은 바로 그의 마음속 의심의 핵심이었으니, 그가 발타사르와 그토록 자주 논쟁했던 바로 그 지점이었다."]], [["“Didst thou hear?”", "\"들었소?\""], ["said Ben-Hur to him.", "벤허가 그에게 말했다."], ["“The kingdom cannot be of this world.", "\"그 나라는 이 세상에 속한 것일 수 없소."], ["Yon witness saith the King is but going to his kingdom; and, in effect, I heard the same in my dream.”", "저 증인이 말하기를, 왕은 다만 자기 나라로 가는 길이라 하오. 그리고 사실, 나도 내 꿈속에서 똑같은 것을 들었소.\""]], [["“Hush!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said Simonides, more imperiously than ever before in speech to Ben-Hur.", "시모니데스가 일찍이 벤허에게 한 어떤 말보다도 단호하게 말했다."], ["“Hush, I pray thee! If the Nazarene should answer—”", "\"쉿, 제발! 만일 저 나사렛 사람이 대답한다면—\""]], [["And as he spoke the Nazarene did answer, in a clear voice, full of confidence:", "그가 말하는 사이에 그 나사렛 사람이 정말로 대답했으니, 확신이 가득한 또렷한 목소리였다."]], [["“Verily I say unto thee, To-day shalt thou be with me in Paradise!”", "\"진실로 네게 이르노니, 오늘 네가 나와 함께 낙원에 있으리라!\""]], [["Simonides waited to hear if that were all; then he folded his hands and said, “No more, no more, Lord!", "시모니데스는 그것이 전부인지 잠시 기다렸다. 그러고는 두 손을 모으고 말했다. \"더는 필요 없습니다, 더는, 주여!"], ["The darkness is gone; I see with other eyes—even as Balthasar, I see with eyes of perfect faith.”", "어둠이 걷혔습니다. 저는 다른 눈으로 봅니다—발타사르처럼, 저는 완전한 믿음의 눈으로 봅니다.\""]], [["The faithful servant had at last his fitting reward.", "그 충직한 종이 마침내 합당한 보상을 받았다."], ["His broken body might never be restored; nor was there riddance of the recollection of his sufferings, or recall of the years embittered by them; but suddenly a new life was shown him, with assurance that it was for him—a new life lying just beyond this one—and its name was Paradise.", "그의 망가진 몸은 결코 회복되지 못할 것이며, 그가 겪은 고초의 기억에서 벗어날 길도, 그 고초로 쓰라렸던 세월을 되돌릴 길도 없었다. 그러나 별안간 그에게 새로운 삶이 보였으니, 그것이 그를 위한 것이라는 확신과 더불어였다—이 삶 바로 너머에 놓인 새로운 삶, 그 이름은 낙원이었다."], ["There he would find the Kingdom of which he had been dreaming, and the King.", "거기서 그는 그토록 꿈꾸어 온 그 나라와 그 왕을 만나리라."], ["A perfect peace fell upon him.", "완전한 평안이 그에게 임했다."]], [["Over the way, in front of the cross, however, there were surprise and consternation.", "그러나 길 건너편, 십자가 앞에서는 놀람과 경악이 일었다."], ["The cunning casuists there put the assumption underlying the question and the admission underlying the answer together.", "거기 교활한 궤변가들은 그 물음의 밑바탕에 깔린 전제와 그 대답의 밑바탕에 깔린 시인(是認)을 한데 짜 맞추었다."], ["For saying through the land that he was the Messiah, they had brought the Nazarene to the cross; and, lo!", "그가 메시아라고 온 땅에 떠들고 다닌 까닭에 그들은 나사렛 사람을 십자가에 매달았던 것이다. 그런데, 보라!"], ["on the cross, more confidently than ever, he had not only reasserted himself, but promised enjoyment of his Paradise to a malefactor.", "십자가 위에서 그는 그 어느 때보다 확신에 차서 자기를 다시 내세웠을 뿐 아니라, 한 죄인에게 자기 낙원을 누리게 해 주겠다고 약속한 것이다."], ["They trembled at what they were doing.", "그들은 자기가 하고 있는 일에 몸을 떨었다."], ["The pontiff, with all his pride, was afraid.", "그토록 거만하던 제사장도 두려워졌다."], ["Where got the man his confidence except from Truth?", "저 사람이 그 확신을 진리가 아니고서야 어디서 얻었겠는가?"], ["And what should the Truth be but God?", "그리고 진리란 곧 하느님이 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["A very little now would put them all to flight.", "이제 아주 작은 것 하나면 그들 모두를 달아나게 하기에 족할 터였다."]], [["The breathing of the Nazarene grew harder, his sighs became great gasps.", "나사렛 사람의 숨결이 점점 거칠어지더니, 그의 한숨이 큰 헐떡임으로 변했다."], ["Only three hours upon the cross, and he was dying!", "십자가에 매달린 지 겨우 세 시간 만에, 그가 죽어 가고 있었다!"]], [["The intelligence was carried from man to man, until every one knew it; and then everything hushed; the breeze faltered and died; a stifling vapor loaded the air; heat was superadded to darkness; nor might any one unknowing the fact have thought that off the hill, out under the overhanging pall, there were three millions of people waiting awe-struck what should happen next—they were so still!", "그 소식이 이 사람에게서 저 사람에게로 전해져, 마침내 모두가 그것을 알게 되었다. 그러자 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다. 미풍이 비틀거리다 잦아들었다. 숨 막히는 수증기가 공기를 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 어둠에 열기까지 더해졌다. 그 사실을 모르는 자라면 누구도, 저 언덕 너머 드리운 장막 아래에 삼백만 사람이 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질까 외경에 사로잡혀 기다리고 있다고는 생각하지 못했으리라—그토록 그들은 잠잠했다!"]], [["Then there went out through the gloom, over the heads of such as were on the hill within hearing of the dying man, a cry of despair, if not reproach:", "그러자 어둠을 뚫고, 죽어 가는 사람의 목소리가 들리는 거리에 있던 자들의 머리 위로, 절망의, 어쩌면 책망의 외침이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“My God! my God! why hast thou forsaken me?”", "\"나의 하느님, 나의 하느님, 어찌하여 저를 버리셨나이까?\""]], [["The voice startled all who heard it.", "그 목소리는 들은 모든 이를 소스라치게 했다."], ["One it touched uncontrollably.", "한 사람은 걷잡을 수 없이 그것에 사로잡혔다."]], [["The soldiers in coming had brought with them a vessel of wine and water, and set it down a little way from Ben-Hur.", "군병들은 올 때 포도주와 물을 담은 그릇을 가지고 와, 벤허에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에 내려놓았다."], ["With a sponge dipped into the liquor, and put on the end of a stick, they could moisten the tongue of a sufferer at their pleasure.", "그 액체에 적신 해면을 막대 끝에 꽂으면, 고통받는 자의 혀를 제 마음대로 적셔 줄 수 있었다."], ["Ben-Hur thought of the draught he had had at the well near Nazareth; an impulse seized him; catching up the sponge, he dipped it into the vessel, and started for the cross.", "벤허는 나사렛 부근 우물에서 자기가 마셨던 한 모금을 떠올렸다. 어떤 충동이 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 해면을 집어 들어 그릇에 적시고는 십자가를 향해 달려갔다."]], [["“Let him be!”", "\"내버려 두어라!\""], ["the people in the way shouted, angrily.", "길에 선 사람들이 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Let him be!”", "\"내버려 두어!\""]], [["Without minding them, he ran on, and put the sponge to the Nazarene’s lips.", "그는 아랑곳하지 않고 달려가, 그 해면을 나사렛 사람의 입술에 가져다 댔다."]], [["Too late, too late!", "너무 늦었다, 너무 늦었다!"]], [["The face then plainly seen by Ben-Hur, bruised and black with blood and dust as it was, lighted nevertheless with a sudden glow; the eyes opened wide, and fixed upon some one visible to them alone in the far heavens; and there were content and relief, even triumph, in the shout the victim gave.", "그때 벤허에게 또렷이 보인 그 얼굴은, 피와 먼지로 멍들고 검게 그을렸으나, 그래도 별안간 환한 빛으로 밝아졌다. 두 눈이 활짝 열려, 오직 그들만이 볼 수 있는 누군가를 저 먼 하늘에서 지그시 바라보았다. 그리고 그 희생자가 내지른 외침에는 만족과 안도가, 심지어 승리가 깃들어 있었다."]], [["“It is finished!", "\"다 이루었다!"], ["It is finished!”", "다 이루었다!\""]], [["So a hero, dying in the doing a great deed, celebrates his success with a last cheer.", "위대한 일을 이루다가 죽어 가는 영웅이 마지막 환호로 제 성취를 기리듯이."]], [["The light in the eyes went out; slowly the crowned head sank upon the laboring breast.", "그 두 눈의 빛이 꺼져 갔다. 가시관 쓴 머리가 천천히 가쁜 가슴 위로 떨어졌다."], ["Ben-Hur thought the struggle over; but the fainting soul recollected itself, so that he and those around him caught the other and last words, spoken in a low voice, as if to one listening close by:", "벤허는 그 싸움이 끝났다고 생각했다. 그러나 기진해 가던 영혼이 정신을 가다듬어, 그와 그 둘레의 사람들은 마치 가까이서 듣는 이에게 건네듯 나직한 목소리로 말하는 또 한마디, 마지막 말을 들었다."]], [["“Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit.”", "\"아버지, 제 영혼을 아버지 손에 맡기나이다.\""]], [["A tremor shook the tortured body; there was a scream of fiercest anguish, and the mission and the earthly life were over at once.", "전율이 그 고문당한 몸을 뒤흔들었다. 가장 사나운 고통의 비명이 울렸고, 그 사명과 지상의 삶이 일순간에 끝났다."], ["The heart, with all its love, was broken; for of that, O reader, the man died!", "그 온갖 사랑을 품은 심장이 터졌으니, 오 독자여, 그 때문에 그분은 죽으신 것이다!"]], [["Ben-Hur went back to his friends, saying, simply, “It is over; he is dead.”", "벤허는 친구들에게 돌아가, 그저 이렇게 말했다. \"끝났습니다. 그분은 돌아가셨습니다.\""]], [["In a space incredibly short the multitude was informed of the circumstance.", "믿기 어려울 만큼 짧은 사이에 군중에게 그 사실이 알려졌다."], ["No one repeated it aloud; there was a murmur which spread from the knoll in every direction; a murmur that was little more than a whispering, “He is dead!", "누구도 그것을 큰 소리로 되뇌지 않았다. 언덕에서 사방으로 번져 가는 웅성거림이 있었을 뿐이니, 소곤거림에 지나지 않는 웅성거림이었다. \"그가 죽었다!"], ["he is dead!”", "그가 죽었어!\""], ["and that was all.", "그것이 전부였다."], ["The people had their wish; the Nazarene was dead; yet they stared at each other aghast.", "사람들은 제 바라던 바를 이루었다. 그 나사렛 사람이 죽은 것이다. 그러나 그들은 아연실색하여 서로를 빤히 바라보았다."], ["His blood was upon them!", "그의 피가 그들 위에 떨어진 것이다!"], ["And while they stood staring at each other, the ground commenced to shake; each man took hold of his neighbor to support himself; in a twinkling the darkness disappeared, and the sun came out; and everybody, as with the same glance, beheld the crosses upon the hill all reeling drunken-like in the earthquake.", "그렇게 서로를 응시하며 서 있는데, 땅이 흔들리기 시작했다. 저마다 몸을 가누려고 곁 사람을 붙들었다. 눈 깜짝할 새 어둠이 사라지고 해가 나왔다."], ["They beheld all three of them; but the one in the centre was arbitrary; it alone would be seen; and for that it seemed to extend itself upwards, and lift its burden, and swing it to and fro higher and higher in the blue of the sky.", "그리고 모두가 한순간에 같은 눈길로 보기라도 하듯, 언덕 위 십자가들이 지진에 술 취한 듯 죄다 휘청거리는 것을 보았다. 그들은 셋을 다 보았으나, 한가운데 것이 유독 두드러졌다. 그것만이 보였다."], ["And every man among them who had jeered at the Nazarene; every one who had struck him; every one who had voted to crucify him; every one who had marched in the procession from the city; every one who had in his heart wished him dead, and they were as ten to one, felt that he was in some way individually singled out from the many, and that if he would live he must get away quickly as possible from that menace in the sky.", "그리고 그것은 위로 스스로를 뻗어, 제 짐을 들어 올리고, 푸른 하늘 속에서 점점 더 높이 그것을 이리저리 흔드는 듯했다. 그리고 그들 가운데 나사렛 사람을 조롱했던 자, 그를 때렸던 자, 그를 십자가에 못 박자고 표결했던 자, 도성에서부터 행렬을 지어 걸어왔던 자, 마음속으로 그가 죽기를 바랐던 자—이들이 열에 아홉이었다—모두가 자기가 어떤 식으로든 그 많은 무리 가운데서 따로 지목되었다고, 그래서 살려거든 저 하늘의 위협으로부터 되도록 빨리 달아나야 한다고 느꼈다. 그들은 달리기 시작했다."], ["They started to run; they ran with all their might; on horseback, and camels, and in chariots they ran, as well as on foot; but then as if it were mad at them for what they had done, and had taken up the cause of the unoffending and friendless dead, the earthquake pursued them, and tossed them about, and flung them down, and terrified them yet more by the horrible noise of great rocks grinding and rending beneath them.", "그들은 있는 힘을 다해 달렸다. 말을 타고, 낙타를 타고, 전차를 타고, 또 두 발로 달렸다. 그러나 그러자 지진은, 마치 그들이 저지른 일에 노하여 죄 없고 의지할 데 없는 죽은 이의 편을 들기라도 한 듯, 그들을 뒤쫓아 이리저리 내던지고 거꾸러뜨렸으며, 발아래에서 거대한 바위들이 갈리고 찢기는 무시무시한 굉음으로 그들을 한층 더 떨게 했다."], ["They beat their breasts and shrieked with fear.", "그들은 제 가슴을 치며 두려움에 비명을 질렀다."], ["His blood was upon them! The home-bred and the foreign, priest and layman, beggar, Sadducee, Pharisee, were overtaken in the race, and tumbled about indiscriminately.", "그의 피가 그들 위에 떨어진 것이다! 본토박이든 이방인이든, 제사장이든 평신도든, 거지든, 사두개인이든, 바리새인이든, 달아나다 따라잡혀 분별없이 나뒹굴었다."], ["If they called on the Lord, the outraged earth answered for him in fury, and dealt them all alike.", "그들이 주를 부르면, 능욕당한 대지가 그를 대신해 격노로 답하며 그들 모두를 한결같이 다루었다."], ["It did not even know wherein the high-priest was better than his guilty brethren; overtaking him, it tripped him up also, and smirched the fringing of his robe, and filled the golden bells with sand, and his mouth with dust.", "대지는 대제사장이 그 죄 많은 형제들보다 어디가 나은지조차 알지 못했다. 그를 따라잡아 그 또한 거꾸러뜨리고, 그 옷자락에 흙칠을 하고, 금방울 속을 모래로 채우고, 그 입을 먼지로 채웠다."], ["He and his people were alike in the one thing at least—the blood of the Nazarene was upon them all!", "그와 그의 백성은 적어도 한 가지에서는 똑같았으니—그 나사렛 사람의 피가 그들 모두 위에 떨어진 것이다!"]], [["When the sunlight broke upon the crucifixion, the mother of the Nazarene, the disciple, and the faithful women of Galilee, the centurion and his soldiers, and Ben-Hur and his party, were all who remained upon the hill.", "햇빛이 처형장에 비쳐 들었을 때, 나사렛 사람의 어머니와 제자와 갈릴리의 충실한 여인들, 백부장과 그의 군병들, 그리고 벤허와 그 일행만이 언덕 위에 남아 있었다."], ["These had not time to observe the flight of the multitude; they were too loudly called upon to take care of themselves.", "이들에게는 군중의 도피를 살필 겨를이 없었으니, 제 한 몸 건사하기에도 너무나 다급하게 내몰렸던 것이다."]], [["“Seat thyself here,”", "\"여기 앉아라.\""], ["said Ben-Hur to Esther, making a place for her at her father’s feet.", "벤허가 에스더에게 아버지의 발치에 자리를 만들어 주며 말했다."], ["“Now cover thine eyes and look not up; but put thy trust in God, and the spirit of yon just man so foully slain.”", "\"자, 두 눈을 가리고 위를 보지 마라. 다만 하느님과, 저토록 끔찍하게 죽임당한 의로운 분의 영을 믿어라.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아닙니다,\""], ["said Simonides, reverently, “let us henceforth speak of him as the Christ.”", "시모니데스가 경건하게 말했다. \"이제부터 그분을 그리스도라 부릅시다.\""]], [["“Be it so,”", "\"그러지요.\""], ["said Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 말했다."]], [["Presently a wave of the earthquake struck the hill.", "이윽고 지진의 물결이 언덕을 쳤다."], ["The shrieks of the thieves upon the reeling crosses were terrible to hear.", "휘청대는 십자가 위 강도들의 비명은 차마 듣기 끔찍했다."], ["Though giddy with the movements of the ground, Ben-Hur had time to look at Balthasar, and beheld him prostrate and still.", "땅의 움직임에 어지러우면서도, 벤허는 발타사르를 살필 겨를이 있었으니, 그가 엎어진 채 미동도 없는 것을 보았다."], ["He ran to him and called—there was no reply.", "그는 그에게 달려가 불렀다—아무 대답이 없었다."], ["The good man was dead!", "그 선한 사람이 죽은 것이다!"], ["Then Ben-Hur remembered to have heard a cry in answer, as it were, to the scream of the Nazarene in his last moment; but he had not looked to see from whom it had proceeded; and ever after he believed the spirit of the Egyptian accompanied that of his Master over the boundary into the kingdom of Paradise.", "그제야 벤허는, 나사렛 사람이 마지막 순간에 지른 비명에 화답하듯 외침 하나가 들렸던 것을 떠올렸다. 그러나 그것이 누구에게서 나온 것인지 살펴보지는 않았던 터라, 그 뒤로 줄곧 그는 그 이집트 사람의 영이 그 주님의 영과 함께 경계를 넘어 낙원의 나라로 들어갔다고 믿었다."], ["The idea rested not only upon the cry heard, but upon the exceeding fitness of the distinction.", "그 생각은 들었던 그 외침에 근거했을 뿐 아니라, 그러한 영예가 그에게 더없이 합당하다는 데에도 근거했다."], ["If faith were worthy reward in the person of Gaspar, and love in that of Melchior, surely he should have some special meed who through a long life and so excellently illustrated the three virtues in combination—Faith, Love, and Good Works.", "믿음이 가스파르라는 인물 안에서 보상받을 만하고, 사랑이 멜키오르라는 인물 안에서 보상받을 만하다면, 긴 일생을 통하여 믿음과 사랑과 선행이라는 세 가지 덕을 하나로 어우러 그토록 훌륭하게 보여 준 그가 어떤 특별한 갚음을 받는 것은 마땅한 일이었다."]], [["The servants of Balthasar had deserted their master; but when all was over, the two Galileans bore the old man in his litter back to the city.", "발타사르의 종들은 주인을 버리고 달아났다. 그러나 모든 것이 끝나자, 두 갈릴리 사람이 그 노인을 가마에 실어 도성으로 옮겼다."]], [["It was a sorrowful procession that entered the south gate of the palace of the Hurs about the set of sun that memorable day.", "그 잊을 수 없는 날 해 질 무렵, 후르 가의 궁전 남문으로 들어선 것은 슬픔에 잠긴 행렬이었다."], ["About the same hour the body of the Christ was taken down from the cross.", "그와 거의 같은 시각에 그리스도의 몸이 십자가에서 내려졌다."]], [["The remains of Balthasar were carried to the guest-chamber.", "발타사르의 유해는 객실로 옮겨졌다."], ["All the servants hastened weeping to see him; for he had the love of every living thing with which he had in anywise to do; but when they beheld his face, and the smile upon it, they dried their tears, saying, “It is well.", "모든 종이 울며 그를 보러 달려왔으니, 그는 자기가 어떤 식으로든 관계 맺은 살아 있는 모든 것의 사랑을 받았기 때문이다. 그러나 그들이 그의 얼굴과 그 위에 어린 미소를 보고는, 눈물을 거두며 말했다. \"잘된 일이다."], ["He is happier this evening than when he went out in the morning.”", "그분은 아침에 나가실 때보다 이 저녁에 더 행복하시구나.\""]], [["Ben-Hur would not trust a servant to inform Iras what had befallen her father.", "벤허는 아버지에게 닥친 일을 이라스에게 알리는 일을 종에게 맡기려 하지 않았다."], ["He went himself to see her and bring her to the body.", "그는 몸소 그녀를 만나러 가, 그녀를 그 유해 곁으로 데려오려 했다."], ["He imagined her grief; she would now be alone in the world; it was a time to forgive and pity her.", "그는 그녀의 슬픔을 헤아려 보았다. 이제 그녀는 세상에 홀로 남을 것이었다. 용서하고 가엾이 여길 때였다."], ["He remembered he had not asked why she was not of the party in the morning, or where she was; he remembered he had not thought of her; and, from shame, he was ready to make any amends, the more so as he was about to plunge her into such acute grief.", "그는 자기가 아침에 어찌하여 그녀가 일행에 없었는지, 그녀가 어디 있는지 묻지 않았던 것을 떠올렸다. 그녀를 생각조차 하지 않았던 것을 떠올렸다. 그래서 부끄러움에, 그는 어떤 식으로든 보상할 마음이 들었으니, 그녀를 그토록 사무치는 슬픔에 빠뜨리려는 참이었기에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["He shook the curtains of her door; and though he heard the ringing of the little bells echoing within, he had no response; he called her name, and again he called—still no answer.", "그는 그녀 방의 휘장을 흔들었다. 안에서 작은 방울들이 울리는 소리가 들렸으나, 아무 응답이 없었다. 그는 그녀의 이름을 불렀고, 다시 불렀다—여전히 대답이 없었다."], ["He drew the curtain aside and went into the room; she was not there.", "그는 휘장을 걷고 방 안으로 들어갔다. 그녀는 거기 없었다."], ["He ascended hastily to the roof in search of her; nor was she there.", "그는 그녀를 찾아 황급히 지붕으로 올라갔다. 거기에도 그녀는 없었다."], ["He questioned the servants; none of them had seen her during the day.", "그는 종들에게 물었다. 그날 하루 종일 그녀를 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["After a long quest everywhere through the house, Ben-Hur returned to the guest-chamber, and took the place by the dead which should have been hers; and he bethought him there how merciful the Christ had been to his aged servant.", "집 안 곳곳을 오래도록 찾아 헤맨 끝에, 벤허는 객실로 돌아와, 마땅히 그녀의 자리였어야 할 그 죽은 이의 곁자리에 앉았다. 그러고는 거기서 그리스도께서 그 늙은 종에게 얼마나 자비로우셨는가를 생각했다."], ["At the gate of the kingdom of Paradise happily the afflictions of this life, even its desertions, are left behind and forgotten by those who go in and rest.", "낙원의 나라 문턱에서는, 다행히도 이 삶의 고난이, 심지어 그 버림받음마저도, 그 안으로 들어가 안식하는 이들에게는 뒤에 남겨져 잊히는 것이다."]], [["When the gloom of the burial was nigh gone, on the ninth day after the healing, the law being fulfilled, Ben-Hur brought his mother and Tirzah home; and from that day, in that house the most sacred names possible of utterance by men were always coupled worshipfully together,", "매장의 침통함이 거의 가셨을 무렵, 치유가 있은 지 아흐레째 되는 날, 율법이 다 이루어진 뒤에, 벤허는 어머니와 티르자를 집으로 데려왔다. 그리고 그날부터 그 집에서는 사람의 입으로 일컬을 수 있는 가장 거룩한 이름이 언제나 경배 속에 나란히 짝지어 불렸으니,\n성부 하느님과 성자 그리스도였다."]], [["————\nAbout five years after the crucifixion, Esther, the wife of Ben-Hur, sat in her room in the beautiful villa by Misenum.", "처형이 있은 지 다섯 해쯤 뒤, 벤허의 아내 에스더는 미세눔 곁의 아름다운 별장 안 제 방에 앉아 있었다."], ["It was noon, with a warm Italian sun making summer for the roses and vines outside.", "정오였고, 따스한 이탈리아의 해가 바깥의 장미와 포도덩굴에 여름을 펼쳐 주고 있었다."], ["Everything in the apartment was Roman, except that Esther wore the garments of a Jewish matron.", "그 방의 모든 것이 로마풍이었으나, 다만 에스더가 유대 부인의 옷을 걸치고 있는 것만은 달랐다."], ["Tirzah and two children at play upon a lion skin on the floor were her companions; and one had only to observe how carefully she watched them to know that the little ones were hers.", "티르자와, 바닥의 사자 가죽 위에서 놀고 있는 두 아이가 그녀의 벗이었다. 그녀가 얼마나 정성스레 그들을 지켜보는지 보기만 해도, 그 어린것들이 그녀의 아이임을 알 수 있었다."]], [["Time had treated her generously.", "세월은 그녀에게 너그러웠다."], ["She was more than ever beautiful, and in becoming mistress of the villa, she had realized one of her cherished dreams.", "그녀는 어느 때보다 아름다웠고, 이 별장의 안주인이 됨으로써 그녀는 오래 품어 온 꿈 하나를 이룬 것이었다."]], [["In the midst of this simple, home-like scene, a servant appeared in the doorway, and spoke to her.", "이 소박하고 단란한 정경 한가운데로 한 종이 문간에 나타나 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“A woman in the atrium to speak with the mistress.”", "\"안마당에 한 여인이 와서 안주인을 뵙겠다 합니다.\""]], [["“Let her come.", "\"들라 하라."], ["I will receive her here.”", "여기서 맞이하겠다.\""]], [["Presently the stranger entered.", "이윽고 그 낯선 여인이 들어왔다. 그녀를 보자 유대 여인은 일어서서 막 말을 건네려 했다."], ["At sight of her the Jewess arose, and was about to speak; then she hesitated, changed color, and finally drew back, saying, “I have known you, good woman. You are—”", "그러다 머뭇거리며 낯빛이 변하더니, 마침내 뒤로 물러서며 말했다. \"당신을 알 듯합니다, 부인. 당신은—\""]], [["“I was Iras, the daughter of Balthasar.”", "\"저는 발타사르의 딸 이라스였습니다.\""]], [["Esther conquered her surprise, and bade the servant bring the Egyptian a seat.", "에스더는 놀라움을 가라앉히고, 종에게 그 이집트 여인의 자리를 가져오라 일렀다."]], [["“No,” said Iras, coldly.", "\"아니에요.\" 이라스가 쌀쌀하게 말했다."], ["“I will retire directly.”", "\"곧 물러갈 겁니다.\""]], [["The two gazed at each other.", "두 여인은 서로를 바라보았다."], ["We know what Esther presented—a beautiful woman, a happy mother, a contented wife.", "에스더가 어떤 모습인지는 우리가 안다—아름다운 여인, 행복한 어머니, 만족한 아내였다."], ["On the other side, it was very plain that fortune had not dealt so gently with her former rival.", "다른 한쪽은, 운명이 한때의 연적에게는 그토록 너그럽지 못했음이 너무도 분명했다."], ["The tall figure remained with some of its grace; but an evil life had tainted the whole person.", "그 큰 키에는 우아함이 얼마간 남아 있었으나, 사악한 삶이 온몸을 더럽혀 놓았다."], ["The face was coarse; the large eyes were red and pursed beneath the lower lids; there was no color in her cheeks.", "얼굴은 거칠었고, 큰 두 눈은 붉었으며 아래 눈꺼풀 밑이 부어 있었다. 두 뺨에는 핏기가 없었다."], ["The lips were cynical and hard, and general neglect was leading rapidly to premature old age.", "입술은 냉소적이고 굳어 있었으며, 전반적인 방치가 빠르게 때 이른 노쇠로 이끌고 있었다."], ["Her attire was ill chosen and draggled.", "차림새는 형편없이 골라 입은 데다 너절했다."], ["The mud of the road clung to her sandals.", "길의 진흙이 그녀의 샌들에 들러붙어 있었다."], ["Iras broke the painful silence.", "이라스가 그 괴로운 침묵을 깼다."]], [["“These are thy children?”", "\"이 아이들이 당신 아이들인가요?\""]], [["Esther looked at them, and smiled.", "에스더는 아이들을 바라보며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Will you not speak to them?”", "이 아이들에게 말을 건네지 않겠어요?\""]], [["“I would scare them,”", "\"제가 아이들을 겁먹게 할 거예요.\""], ["Iras replied.", "이라스가 대답했다."], ["Then she drew closer to Esther, and seeing her shrink, said, “Be not afraid.", "그러고는 에스더에게 더 가까이 다가갔다가, 그녀가 움찔하는 것을 보고 말했다. \"두려워 마세요."], ["Give thy husband a message for me.", "당신 남편에게 내 말을 전해 주세요."], ["Tell him his enemy is dead, and that for the much misery he brought me I slew him.”", "그의 원수가 죽었고, 그가 내게 안긴 그 숱한 비참함의 갚음으로 내가 그를 죽였다고.\""]], [["“His enemy!”", "\"그의 원수라니요!\""]], [["“The Messala.", "\"메살라 말이에요."], ["Further, tell thy husband that for the harm I sought to do him I have been punished until even he would pity me.”", "또한 당신 남편에게 전해 주세요. 그에게 해를 끼치려 했던 그 죄로, 나는 그조차 가엾이 여길 만큼 벌을 받아 왔다고.\""]], [["Tears arose in Esther’s eyes, and she was about to speak.", "에스더의 눈에 눈물이 고였고, 그녀는 막 입을 열려 했다."]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Iras, “I do not want pity or tears.", "이라스가 말했다. \"나는 동정도 눈물도 바라지 않아요."], ["Tell him, finally, I have found that to be a Roman is to be a brute. Farewell.”", "마지막으로 그에게 전해 주세요. 로마인이 된다는 것은 곧 짐승이 된다는 것임을 내가 깨달았다고. 안녕히.\""]], [["She moved to go.", "그녀는 가려고 몸을 움직였다."], ["Esther followed her.", "에스더가 그녀를 뒤따랐다."]], [["“Stay, and see my husband.", "\"머물러 제 남편을 만나세요."], ["He has no feeling against you.", "그는 당신에게 아무런 악감정이 없어요."], ["He sought for you everywhere.", "그는 당신을 사방으로 찾았어요."], ["He will be your friend.", "그가 당신의 벗이 되어 줄 거예요."], ["I will be your friend.", "저도 당신의 벗이 되겠어요."], ["We are Christians.”", "우리는 그리스도인이에요.\""]], [["The other was firm.", "상대는 단호했다."]], [["“No; I am what I am of choice.", "\"아니에요. 나는 스스로 택해 지금의 내가 된 거예요."], ["It will be over shortly.”", "곧 다 끝날 거예요.\""]], [["“But”—Esther hesitated—“have we nothing you would wish; nothing to—to—”", "\"하지만\"—에스더가 머뭇거렸다—\"우리에게 당신이 바라는 것이, 무언가 그—그—\""]], [["The countenance of the Egyptian softened; something like a smile played about her lips.", "이집트 여인의 표정이 누그러졌다. 미소 비슷한 것이 그녀의 입가에 어렸다."], ["She looked at the children upon the floor.", "그녀는 바닥의 아이들을 바라보았다."]], [["“There is something,” she said.", "\"한 가지 있어요.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["Esther followed her eyes, and with quick perception answered, “It is yours.”", "에스더가 그 눈길을 좇고는, 재빠른 헤아림으로 대답했다. \"그건 당신 거예요.\""]], [["Iras went to them, and knelt on the lion’s skin, and kissed them both.", "이라스는 아이들에게 다가가, 사자 가죽 위에 무릎을 꿇고 둘 다에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Rising slowly, she looked at them; then passed to the door and out of it without a parting word.", "천천히 일어서서 그녀는 아이들을 바라보았다. 그러고는 작별의 말 한마디 없이 문 쪽으로 가 밖으로 나섰다."], ["She walked rapidly, and was gone before Esther could decide what to do.", "그녀는 빠르게 걸어, 에스더가 어찌할지 미처 정하기도 전에 가 버리고 없었다."]], [["Ben-Hur, when he was told of the visit, knew certainly what he had long surmised—that on the day of the crucifixion Iras had deserted her father for Messala.", "벤허는 그 방문에 대해 전해 들었을 때, 자기가 오래도록 짐작해 온 것을 확실히 알게 되었다—처형의 날에 이라스가 메살라를 위해 제 아버지를 버렸다는 것을."], ["Nevertheless, he set out immediately and hunted for her vainly; they never saw her more, or heard of her.", "그런데도 그는 곧장 길을 나서 그녀를 찾았으나 헛수고였다. 그들은 다시는 그녀를 보지도, 그녀의 소식을 듣지도 못했다."], ["The blue bay, with all its laughing under the sun, has yet its dark secrets.", "그 푸른 만(灣)은, 햇빛 아래 온통 웃음 짓고 있으면서도, 여전히 어두운 비밀들을 품고 있다."], ["Had it a tongue, it might tell us of the Egyptian.", "만일 그것에 혀가 있다면, 그 이집트 여인의 이야기를 우리에게 들려줄 수 있으리라."]], [["Simonides lived to be a very old man.", "시모니데스는 아주 늙도록 살았다."], ["In the tenth year of Nero’s reign, he gave up the business so long centred in the warehouse at Antioch.", "네로 치세 열째 해에, 그는 안디옥의 창고에 그토록 오래 자리 잡고 있던 사업에서 손을 뗐다."], ["To the last he kept a clear head and a good heart, and was successful.", "마지막까지 그는 맑은 머리와 선한 마음을 지녔고, 성공을 거두었다."]], [["One evening, in the year named, he sat in his arm-chair on the terrace of the warehouse.", "말한 그 해의 어느 저녁, 그는 창고 옥상에 안락의자를 놓고 앉아 있었다."], ["Ben-Hur and Esther, and their three children, were with him.", "벤허와 에스더, 그리고 그들의 세 아이가 그와 함께 있었다."], ["The last of the ships swung at mooring in the current of the river; all the rest had been sold.", "마지막 남은 배 한 척이 강물의 흐름 속에 매여 일렁이고 있었다. 나머지는 모두 팔려 나간 뒤였다."], ["In the long interval between this and the day of the crucifixion but one sorrow had befallen them: that was when the mother of Ben-Hur died; and then and now their grief would have been greater but for their Christian faith.", "처형의 날부터 이때에 이르는 긴 세월 동안 그들에게 닥친 슬픔은 단 하나였으니, 벤허의 어머니가 세상을 떠난 일이었다. 그때나 지금이나 그들의 그리스도 신앙이 아니었다면 그 슬픔은 한층 더 컸으리라."]], [["The ship spoken of had arrived only the day before, bringing intelligence of the persecution of Christians begun by Nero in Rome, and the party on the terrace were talking of the news when Malluch, who was still in their service, approached and delivered a package to Ben-Hur.", "방금 말한 그 배는 바로 전날에야 도착하여, 네로가 로마에서 시작한 그리스도인 박해의 소식을 가져왔다. 옥상의 일행이 그 소식을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있는데, 아직도 그들을 섬기고 있던 말루크가 다가와 벤허에게 꾸러미 하나를 전했다."]], [["“Who brings this?” the latter asked, after reading.", "\"누가 이걸 가져왔는가?\" 벤허가 읽고 나서 물었다."]], [["“An Arab.” “Where is he?”", "\"한 아랍 사람입니다.\""]], [["“He left immediately.”", "\"그는 어디 있는가?\""]], [["“Listen,” said Ben-Hur to Simonides.", "\"곧바로 떠났습니다.\""]], [["He read then the following letter:", "\"들어 보시오.\" 벤허가 시모니데스에게 말했다."]], [["“I, Ilderim, the son of Ilderim the Generous, and sheik of the tribe of Ilderim, to Judah, son of Hur.", "그러고는 다음 편지를 읽었다. \"나, 너그러운 일데림의 아들 일데림이며 일데림 부족의 족장인 내가, 후르의 아들 유다에게."]], [["“Know, O friend of my father’s, how my father loved you.", "알아 두시오, 오 내 아버지의 벗이여, 내 아버지가 그대를 얼마나 사랑하셨는지를."], ["Read what is herewith sent, and you will know.", "여기 함께 보내는 글을 읽으면 알게 될 것이오."], ["His will is my will; therefore what he gave is thine.", "아버지의 뜻이 곧 내 뜻이니, 그러므로 아버지가 주신 것은 그대의 것이오."]], [["“All the Parthians took from him in the great battle in which they slew him I have retaken—this writing, with other things, and vengeance, and all the brood of that Mira who in his time was mother of so many stars.", "파르티아 사람들이 내 아버지를 살해한 그 큰 전투에서 그분에게서 빼앗아 간 모든 것을 나는 되찾았소—이 글과, 다른 것들과, 복수와, 그리고 그 시절 그토록 많은 별의 어미였던 그 미라의 온 핏줄까지."]], [["“Peace be to you and all yours.", "그대와 그대의 모든 이에게 평안이 있기를."]], [["“This voice out of the desert is the voice of", "사막에서 들려오는 이 목소리는"]], [["“Ilderim, Shiek.”", "일데림 족장의 목소리요.\""]], [["Ben-Hur next unrolled a scrap of papyrus yellow as a withered mulberry leaf.", "벤허는 다음으로, 시든 뽕잎처럼 누런 파피루스 조각을 펼쳤다."], ["It required the daintiest handling.", "그것은 더없이 조심스러운 손길을 요했다."], ["Proceeding, he read:", "그는 이어서 읽어 나갔다."]], [["“Ilderim, surnamed the Generous, sheik of the tribe of Ilderim, to the son who succeeds me.", "\"너그러운 이라 불리는, 일데림 부족의 족장 일데림이, 나를 이을 아들에게."]], [["“All I have, O son, shall be thine in the day of thy succession, except that property by Antioch known as the Orchard of Palms; and it shall be to the son of Hur who brought us such glory in the Circus—to him and his forever.", "내가 가진 모든 것은, 오 아들아, 네가 뒤를 잇는 날에 네 것이 되리라. 다만 안디옥 곁에 있는 종려나무 과수원이라 알려진 그 재산만은 예외이니, 그것은 원형 경기장에서 우리에게 그토록 큰 영광을 안겨 준 후르의 아들의 것이 되리라—그와 그의 자손에게 영원히."]], [["“Dishonor not thy father. ILDERIM THE GENEROUS, Sheik.”", "네 아버지를 욕되게 하지 말라. 너그러운 일데림 족장.\""]], [["“What say you?”", "\"어찌 생각하시오?\""], ["asked Ben-Hur, of Simonides.", "벤허가 시모니데스에게 물었다."]], [["Esther took the papers pleased, and read them to herself.", "에스더는 그 글들을 기뻐하며 받아, 혼자 속으로 읽었다."], ["Simonides remained silent.", "시모니데스는 잠잠히 있었다."], ["His eyes were upon the ship; but he was thinking.", "그의 눈은 배를 향하고 있었으나, 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["At length he spoke.", "이윽고 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Son of Hur,”", "\"후르의 아들이여,\""], ["he said, gravely, “the Lord has been good to you in these later years.", "그가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"주께서는 이 근래의 세월에 그대에게 자애로우셨소."], ["You have much to be thankful for.", "그대는 감사할 일이 많소."], ["Is it not time to decide finally the meaning of the gift of the great fortune now all in your hand, and growing?”", "이제 그대 손안에 다 들어와 자라고 있는 그 큰 재물의 선물이 무엇을 뜻하는지를 마침내 결정할 때가 되지 않았소?\""]], [["“I decided that long ago.", "\"나는 오래전에 그것을 결정했소."], ["The fortune was meant for the service of the Giver; not a part, Simonides, but all of it.", "그 재물은 주신 분을 섬기는 데에 쓰라고 주어진 것이오. 그 일부가 아니라, 시모니데스, 그 전부 말이오."], ["The question with me has been, How can I make it most useful in his cause?", "나의 물음은 다만, 어찌하면 그것을 그분의 대의에 가장 쓸모 있게 만들 수 있을까 하는 것이었소."], ["And of that tell me, I pray you.”", "그 점을 내게 일러 주시오, 청하건대.\""]], [["Simonides answered,", "시모니데스가 대답했다."]], [["“The great sums you have given to the Church here in Antioch, I am witness to.", "\"그대가 여기 안디옥의 교회에 내놓은 큰돈을, 나는 똑똑히 보아 왔소."], ["Now, instantly almost with this gift of the generous sheik’s, comes the news of the persecution of the brethren in Rome.", "그런데 이제, 너그러운 족장의 이 선물과 거의 때를 같이하여, 로마의 형제들이 박해받는다는 소식이 왔소."], ["It is the opening of a new field.", "이것은 새로운 일터가 열리는 것이오."], ["The light must not go out in the capital.”", "수도에서 빛이 꺼져서는 안 되오.\""]], [["“Tell me how I can keep it alive.”", "\"어찌하면 그 빛을 살려 둘 수 있는지 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“I will tell you.", "\"내 일러 주리다."], ["The Romans, even this Nero, hold two things sacred—I know of no others they so hold—they are the ashes of the dead and all places of burial.", "로마인들은, 이 네로조차도, 두 가지를 거룩히 여기오—그들이 그토록 거룩히 여기는 다른 것을 나는 알지 못하오—그것은 죽은 자의 유골과 모든 매장지요."], ["If you cannot build temples for the worship of the Lord above ground, then build them below the ground; and to keep them from profanation, carry to them the bodies of all who die in the faith.”", "만일 그대가 주를 예배할 성전을 땅 위에 지을 수 없거든, 땅 밑에 지으시오. 그리고 그곳이 더럽혀지지 않도록, 신앙 안에서 죽은 모든 이의 시신을 그리로 옮기시오.\""]], [["Ben-Hur arose excitedly.", "벤허가 흥분하여 일어섰다."]], [["“It is a great idea,” he said.", "\"위대한 생각이오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“I will not wait to begin it. Time forbids waiting.", "\"그 일을 시작하는 데에 지체하지 않겠소."], ["The ship that brought the news of the suffering of our brethren shall take me to Rome.", "시간이 지체를 허락하지 않소. 우리 형제들의 고난을 전해 준 그 배가 나를 로마로 데려갈 것이오."], ["I will sail to-morrow.”", "나는 내일 떠나겠소.\""]], [["He turned to Malluch.", "그는 말루크에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Get the ship ready, Malluch, and be thou ready to go with me.”", "\"배를 준비하게, 말루크. 그리고 자네도 나와 함께 갈 채비를 하게.\""]], [["“It is well,”", "\"잘된 일이오.\""], ["said Simonides.", "시모니데스가 말했다."]], [["“And thou, Esther, what sayest thou?”", "\"그리고 그대, 에스더는, 어찌 생각하오?\""], ["asked Ben-Hur.", "벤허가 물었다."]], [["Esther came to his side, and put her hand on his arm, and answered,", "에스더가 그의 곁으로 와 그의 팔에 손을 얹고 대답했다."]], [["“So wilt thou best serve the Christ.", "\"그렇게 하셔야 그리스도를 가장 잘 섬기시는 거예요."], ["O my husband, let me not hinder, but go with thee and help.”", "오 여보, 제가 막지 않게 해 주세요. 그저 당신과 함께 가서 돕게 해 주세요.\""]], [["If any of my readers, visiting Rome, will make the short journey to the Catacomb of San Calixto, which is more ancient than that of San Sebastiano, he will see what became of the fortune of Ben-Hur, and give him thanks.", "내 독자 가운데 누구라도 로마를 찾거든, 산 세바스티아노보다 더 오래된 산 칼리스토 카타콤으로 잠깐 발걸음을 옮겨 보라. 그러면 그는 벤허의 재물이 어찌 되었는지를 보게 될 것이며, 그에게 감사를 드릴 것이다."], ["Out of that vast tomb Christianity issued to supersede the Cæsars.", "그 광대한 무덤에서 그리스도교가 솟아 나와, 카이사르들의 자리를 대신했던 것이다."]]];
